if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-2554"]="A few words about Dostoevsky himself may help the English reader to understand his work.\n\nDostoevsky was the son of a doctor. His parents were very hard-working and deeply religious people, but so poor that they lived with their five children in only two rooms. The father and mother spent their evenings in reading aloud to their children, generally from books of a serious character.\nThough always sickly and delicate Dostoevsky came out third in the final examination of the Petersburg school of Engineering. There he had already begun his first work, “Poor Folk.”\n\nThis story was published by the poet Nekrassov in his review and was received with acclamations. The shy, unknown youth found himself instantly something of a celebrity. A brilliant and successful career seemed to open before him, but those hopes were soon dashed. In 1849 he was arrested.\nThough neither by temperament nor conviction a revolutionist, Dostoevsky was one of a little group of young men who met together to read Fourier and Proudhon. He was accused of “taking part in conversations against the censorship, of reading a letter from Byelinsky to Gogol, and of knowing of the intention to set up a printing press.” Under Nicholas I. (that “stern and just man,” as Maurice Baring calls him) this was enough, and he was condemned to death. After eight months’ imprisonment he was with twenty-one others taken out to the Semyonovsky Square to be shot. Writing to his brother Mihail, Dostoevsky says: “They snapped words over our heads, and they made us put on the white shirts worn by persons condemned to death. Thereupon we were bound in threes to stakes, to suffer execution. Being the third in the row, I concluded I had only a few minutes of life before me. I thought of you and your dear ones and I contrived to kiss Plestcheiev and Dourov, who were next to me, and to bid them farewell. Suddenly the troops beat a tattoo, we were unbound, brought back upon the scaffold, and informed that his Majesty had spared us our lives.” The sentence was commuted to hard labour.\n\nOne of the prisoners, Grigoryev, went mad as soon as he was untied, and never regained his sanity.\n\nThe intense suffering of this experience left a lasting stamp on Dostoevsky’s mind. Though his religious temper led him in the end to accept every suffering with resignation and to regard it as a blessing in his own case, he constantly recurs to the subject in his writings. He describes the awful agony of the condemned man and insists on the cruelty of inflicting such torture. Then followed four years of penal servitude, spent in the company of common criminals in Siberia, where he began the “Dead House,” and some years of service in a disciplinary battalion.\n\nHe had shown signs of some obscure nervous disease before his arrest and this now developed into violent attacks of epilepsy, from which he suffered for the rest of his life. The fits occurred three or four times a year and were more frequent in periods of great strain. In 1859 he was allowed to return to Russia. He started a journal--“Vremya,” which was forbidden by the Censorship through a misunderstanding. In 1864 he lost his first wife and his brother Mihail. He was in terrible poverty, yet he took upon himself the payment of his brother’s debts. He started another journal--“The Epoch,” which within a few months was also prohibited. He was weighed down by debt, his brother’s family was dependent on him, he was forced to write at heart-breaking speed, and is said never to have corrected his work. The later years of his life were much softened by the tenderness and devotion of his second wife.\nIn June 1880 he made his famous speech at the unveiling of the monument to Pushkin in Moscow and he was received with extraordinary demonstrations of love and honour.\n\nA few months later Dostoevsky died. He was followed to the grave by a vast multitude of mourners, who “gave the hapless man the funeral of a king.” He is still probably the most widely read writer in Russia.\nIn the words of a Russian critic, who seeks to explain the feeling inspired by Dostoevsky: “He was one of ourselves, a man of our blood and our bone, but one who has suffered and has seen so much more deeply than we have his insight impresses us as wisdom... that wisdom of the heart which we seek that we may learn from it how to live. All his other gifts came to him from nature, this he won for himself and through it he became great.”\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nOn an exceptionally hot evening early in July a young man came out of the garret in which he lodged in S. Place and walked slowly, as though in hesitation, towards K. bridge.\nHe had successfully avoided meeting his landlady on the staircase. His garret was under the roof of a high, five-storied house and was more like a cupboard than a room. The landlady who provided him with garret, dinners, and attendance, lived on the floor below, and every time he went out he was obliged to pass her kitchen, the door of which invariably stood open. And each time he passed, the young man had a sick, frightened feeling, which made him scowl and feel ashamed. He was hopelessly in debt to his landlady, and was afraid of meeting her.\n\nThis was not because he was cowardly and abject, quite the contrary; but for some time past he had been in an overstrained irritable condition, verging on hypochondria. He had become so completely absorbed in himself, and isolated from his fellows that he dreaded meeting, not only his landlady, but anyone at all. He was crushed by poverty, but the anxieties of his position had of late ceased to weigh upon him. He had given up attending to matters of practical importance; he had lost all desire to do so. Nothing that any landlady could do had a real terror for him. But to be stopped on the stairs, to be forced to listen to her trivial, irrelevant gossip, to pestering demands for payment, threats and complaints, and to rack his brains for excuses, to prevaricate, to lie--no, rather than that, he would creep down the stairs like a cat and slip out unseen.\nThis evening, however, on coming out into the street, he became acutely aware of his fears.\n\n“I want to attempt a thing _like that_ and am frightened by these trifles,” he thought, with an odd smile. “Hm... yes, all is in a man’s hands and he lets it all slip from cowardice, that’s an axiom. It would be interesting to know what it is men are most afraid of. Taking a new step, uttering a new word is what they fear most.... But I am talking too much. It’s because I chatter that I do nothing. Or perhaps it is that I chatter because I do nothing. I’ve learned to chatter this last month, lying for days together in my den thinking... of Jack the Giant-killer. Why am I going there now? Am I capable of _that_? Is _that_ serious? It is not serious at all. It’s simply a fantasy to amuse myself; a plaything! Yes, maybe it is a plaything.”\n\nThe heat in the street was terrible: and the airlessness, the bustle and the plaster, scaffolding, bricks, and dust all about him, and that special Petersburg stench, so familiar to all who are unable to get out of town in summer--all worked painfully upon the young man’s already overwrought nerves. The insufferable stench from the pot-houses, which are particularly numerous in that part of the town, and the drunken men whom he met continually, although it was a working day, completed the revolting misery of the picture. An expression of the profoundest disgust gleamed for a moment in the young man’s refined face. He was, by the way, exceptionally handsome, above the average in height, slim, well-built, with beautiful dark eyes and dark brown hair. Soon he sank into deep thought, or more accurately speaking into a complete blankness of mind; he walked along not observing what was about him and not caring to observe it. From time to time, he would mutter something, from the habit of talking to himself, to which he had just confessed. At these moments he would become conscious that his ideas were sometimes in a tangle and that he was very weak; for two days he had scarcely tasted food.\nHe was so badly dressed that even a man accustomed to shabbiness would have been ashamed to be seen in the street in such rags. In that quarter of the town, however, scarcely any shortcoming in dress would have created surprise. Owing to the proximity of the Hay Market, the number of establishments of bad character, the preponderance of the trading and working class population crowded in these streets and alleys in the heart of Petersburg, types so various were to be seen in the streets that no figure, however queer, would have caused surprise. But there was such accumulated bitterness and contempt in the young man’s heart, that, in spite of all the fastidiousness of youth, he minded his rags least of all in the street. It was a different matter when he met with acquaintances or with former fellow students, whom, indeed, he disliked meeting at any time. And yet when a drunken man who, for some unknown reason, was being taken somewhere in a huge waggon dragged by a heavy dray horse, suddenly shouted at him as he drove past: “Hey there, German hatter” bawling at the top of his voice and pointing at him--the young man stopped suddenly and clutched tremulously at his hat. It was a tall round hat from Zimmerman’s, but completely worn out, rusty with age, all torn and bespattered, brimless and bent on one side in a most unseemly fashion. Not shame, however, but quite another feeling akin to terror had overtaken him.\n\n“I knew it,” he muttered in confusion, “I thought so! That’s the worst of all! Why, a stupid thing like this, the most trivial detail might spoil the whole plan. Yes, my hat is too noticeable.... It looks absurd and that makes it noticeable.... With my rags I ought to wear a cap, any sort of old pancake, but not this grotesque thing. Nobody wears such a hat, it would be noticed a mile off, it would be remembered.... What matters is that people would remember it, and that would give them a clue. For this business one should be as little conspicuous as possible.... Trifles, trifles are what matter! Why, it’s just such trifles that always ruin everything....”\nHe had not far to go; he knew indeed how many steps it was from the gate of his lodging house: exactly seven hundred and thirty. He had counted them once when he had been lost in dreams. At the time he had put no faith in those dreams and was only tantalising himself by their hideous but daring recklessness. Now, a month later, he had begun to look upon them differently, and, in spite of the monologues in which he jeered at his own impotence and indecision, he had involuntarily come to regard this “hideous” dream as an exploit to be attempted, although he still did not realise this himself. He was positively going now for a “rehearsal” of his project, and at every step his excitement grew more and more violent.\n\nWith a sinking heart and a nervous tremor, he went up to a huge house which on one side looked on to the canal, and on the other into the street. This house was let out in tiny tenements and was inhabited by working people of all kinds--tailors, locksmiths, cooks, Germans of sorts, girls picking up a living as best they could, petty clerks, etc. There was a continual coming and going through the two gates and in the two courtyards of the house. Three or four door-keepers were employed on the building. The young man was very glad to meet none of them, and at once slipped unnoticed through the door on the right, and up the staircase. It was a back staircase, dark and narrow, but he was familiar with it already, and knew his way, and he liked all these surroundings: in such darkness even the most inquisitive eyes were not to be dreaded.\n“If I am so scared now, what would it be if it somehow came to pass that I were really going to do it?” he could not help asking himself as he reached the fourth storey. There his progress was barred by some porters who were engaged in moving furniture out of a flat. He knew that the flat had been occupied by a German clerk in the civil service, and his family. This German was moving out then, and so the fourth floor on this staircase would be untenanted except by the old woman. “That’s a good thing anyway,” he thought to himself, as he rang the bell of the old woman’s flat. The bell gave a faint tinkle as though it were made of tin and not of copper. The little flats in such houses always have bells that ring like that. He had forgotten the note of that bell, and now its peculiar tinkle seemed to remind him of something and to bring it clearly before him.... He started, his nerves were terribly overstrained by now. In a little while, the door was opened a tiny crack: the old woman eyed her visitor with evident distrust through the crack, and nothing could be seen but her little eyes, glittering in the darkness. But, seeing a number of people on the landing, she grew bolder, and opened the door wide. The young man stepped into the dark entry, which was partitioned off from the tiny kitchen. The old woman stood facing him in silence and looking inquiringly at him. She was a diminutive, withered up old woman of sixty, with sharp malignant eyes and a sharp little nose. Her colourless, somewhat grizzled hair was thickly smeared with oil, and she wore no kerchief over it. Round her thin long neck, which looked like a hen’s leg, was knotted some sort of flannel rag, and, in spite of the heat, there hung flapping on her shoulders, a mangy fur cape, yellow with age. The old woman coughed and groaned at every instant. The young man must have looked at her with a rather peculiar expression, for a gleam of mistrust came into her eyes again.\n\n“Raskolnikov, a student, I came here a month ago,” the young man made haste to mutter, with a half bow, remembering that he ought to be more polite.\n“I remember, my good sir, I remember quite well your coming here,” the old woman said distinctly, still keeping her inquiring eyes on his face.\n\n“And here... I am again on the same errand,” Raskolnikov continued, a little disconcerted and surprised at the old woman’s mistrust. “Perhaps she is always like that though, only I did not notice it the other time,” he thought with an uneasy feeling.\nThe old woman paused, as though hesitating; then stepped on one side, and pointing to the door of the room, she said, letting her visitor pass in front of her:\n\n“Step in, my good sir.”\n\nThe little room into which the young man walked, with yellow paper on the walls, geraniums and muslin curtains in the windows, was brightly lighted up at that moment by the setting sun.\n\n“So the sun will shine like this _then_ too!” flashed as it were by chance through Raskolnikov’s mind, and with a rapid glance he scanned everything in the room, trying as far as possible to notice and remember its arrangement. But there was nothing special in the room. The furniture, all very old and of yellow wood, consisted of a sofa with a huge bent wooden back, an oval table in front of the sofa, a dressing-table with a looking-glass fixed on it between the windows, chairs along the walls and two or three half-penny prints in yellow frames, representing German damsels with birds in their hands--that was all. In the corner a light was burning before a small ikon. Everything was very clean; the floor and the furniture were brightly polished; everything shone.\n\n“Lizaveta’s work,” thought the young man. There was not a speck of dust to be seen in the whole flat.\n\n“It’s in the houses of spiteful old widows that one finds such cleanliness,” Raskolnikov thought again, and he stole a curious glance at the cotton curtain over the door leading into another tiny room, in which stood the old woman’s bed and chest of drawers and into which he had never looked before. These two rooms made up the whole flat.\n\n“What do you want?” the old woman said severely, coming into the room and, as before, standing in front of him so as to look him straight in the face.\n\n“I’ve brought something to pawn here,” and he drew out of his pocket an old-fashioned flat silver watch, on the back of which was engraved a globe; the chain was of steel.\n\n“But the time is up for your last pledge. The month was up the day before yesterday.”\n\n“I will bring you the interest for another month; wait a little.”\n\n“But that’s for me to do as I please, my good sir, to wait or to sell your pledge at once.”\n\n“How much will you give me for the watch, Alyona Ivanovna?”\n\n“You come with such trifles, my good sir, it’s scarcely worth anything. I gave you two roubles last time for your ring and one could buy it quite new at a jeweler’s for a rouble and a half.”\n\n“Give me four roubles for it, I shall redeem it, it was my father’s. I shall be getting some money soon.”\n\n“A rouble and a half, and interest in advance, if you like!”\n\n“A rouble and a half!” cried the young man.\n\n“Please yourself”--and the old woman handed him back the watch. The young man took it, and was so angry that he was on the point of going away; but checked himself at once, remembering that there was nowhere else he could go, and that he had had another object also in coming.\n\n“Hand it over,” he said roughly.\n\nThe old woman fumbled in her pocket for her keys, and disappeared behind the curtain into the other room. The young man, left standing alone in the middle of the room, listened inquisitively, thinking. He could hear her unlocking the chest of drawers.\n\n“It must be the top drawer,” he reflected. “So she carries the keys in a pocket on the right. All in one bunch on a steel ring.... And there’s one key there, three times as big as all the others, with deep notches; that can’t be the key of the chest of drawers... then there must be some other chest or strong-box... that’s worth knowing. Strong-boxes always have keys like that... but how degrading it all is.”\n\nThe old woman came back.\n\n“Here, sir: as we say ten copecks the rouble a month, so I must take fifteen copecks from a rouble and a half for the month in advance. But for the two roubles I lent you before, you owe me now twenty copecks on the same reckoning in advance. That makes thirty-five copecks altogether. So I must give you a rouble and fifteen copecks for the watch. Here it is.”\n\n“What! only a rouble and fifteen copecks now!”\n\n“Just so.”\n\nThe young man did not dispute it and took the money. He looked at the old woman, and was in no hurry to get away, as though there was still something he wanted to say or to do, but he did not himself quite know what.\n\n“I may be bringing you something else in a day or two, Alyona Ivanovna--a valuable thing--silver--a cigarette-box, as soon as I get it back from a friend...” he broke off in confusion.\n\n“Well, we will talk about it then, sir.”\n\n“Good-bye--are you always at home alone, your sister is not here with you?” He asked her as casually as possible as he went out into the passage.\n\n“What business is she of yours, my good sir?”\n\n“Oh, nothing particular, I simply asked. You are too quick.... Good-day, Alyona Ivanovna.”\n\nRaskolnikov went out in complete confusion. This confusion became more and more intense. As he went down the stairs, he even stopped short, two or three times, as though suddenly struck by some thought. When he was in the street he cried out, “Oh, God, how loathsome it all is! and can I, can I possibly.... No, it’s nonsense, it’s rubbish!” he added resolutely. “And how could such an atrocious thing come into my head? What filthy things my heart is capable of. Yes, filthy above all, disgusting, loathsome, loathsome!--and for a whole month I’ve been....” But no words, no exclamations, could express his agitation. The feeling of intense repulsion, which had begun to oppress and torture his heart while he was on his way to the old woman, had by now reached such a pitch and had taken such a definite form that he did not know what to do with himself to escape from his wretchedness. He walked along the pavement like a drunken man, regardless of the passers-by, and jostling against them, and only came to his senses when he was in the next street. Looking round, he noticed that he was standing close to a tavern which was entered by steps leading from the pavement to the basement. At that instant two drunken men came out at the door, and abusing and supporting one another, they mounted the steps. Without stopping to think, Raskolnikov went down the steps at once. Till that moment he had never been into a tavern, but now he felt giddy and was tormented by a burning thirst. He longed for a drink of cold beer, and attributed his sudden weakness to the want of food. He sat down at a sticky little table in a dark and dirty corner; ordered some beer, and eagerly drank off the first glassful. At once he felt easier; and his thoughts became clear.\n\n“All that’s nonsense,” he said hopefully, “and there is nothing in it all to worry about! It’s simply physical derangement. Just a glass of beer, a piece of dry bread--and in one moment the brain is stronger, the mind is clearer and the will is firm! Phew, how utterly petty it all is!”\n\nBut in spite of this scornful reflection, he was by now looking cheerful as though he were suddenly set free from a terrible burden: and he gazed round in a friendly way at the people in the room. But even at that moment he had a dim foreboding that this happier frame of mind was also not normal.\n\nThere were few people at the time in the tavern. Besides the two drunken men he had met on the steps, a group consisting of about five men and a girl with a concertina had gone out at the same time. Their departure left the room quiet and rather empty. The persons still in the tavern were a man who appeared to be an artisan, drunk, but not extremely so, sitting before a pot of beer, and his companion, a huge, stout man with a grey beard, in a short full-skirted coat. He was very drunk: and had dropped asleep on the bench; every now and then, he began as though in his sleep, cracking his fingers, with his arms wide apart and the upper part of his body bounding about on the bench, while he hummed some meaningless refrain, trying to recall some such lines as these:\n\n“His wife a year he fondly loved His wife a--a year he--fondly loved.”\n\nOr suddenly waking up again:\n\n“Walking along the crowded row He met the one he used to know.”\n\nBut no one shared his enjoyment: his silent companion looked with positive hostility and mistrust at all these manifestations. There was another man in the room who looked somewhat like a retired government clerk. He was sitting apart, now and then sipping from his pot and looking round at the company. He, too, appeared to be in some agitation.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nRaskolnikov was not used to crowds, and, as we said before, he avoided society of every sort, more especially of late. But now all at once he felt a desire to be with other people. Something new seemed to be taking place within him, and with it he felt a sort of thirst for company. He was so weary after a whole month of concentrated wretchedness and gloomy excitement that he longed to rest, if only for a moment, in some other world, whatever it might be; and, in spite of the filthiness of the surroundings, he was glad now to stay in the tavern.\n\nThe master of the establishment was in another room, but he frequently came down some steps into the main room, his jaunty, tarred boots with red turn-over tops coming into view each time before the rest of his person. He wore a full coat and a horribly greasy black satin waistcoat, with no cravat, and his whole face seemed smeared with oil like an iron lock. At the counter stood a boy of about fourteen, and there was another boy somewhat younger who handed whatever was wanted. On the counter lay some sliced cucumber, some pieces of dried black bread, and some fish, chopped up small, all smelling very bad. It was insufferably close, and so heavy with the fumes of spirits that five minutes in such an atmosphere might well make a man drunk.\n\nThere are chance meetings with strangers that interest us from the first moment, before a word is spoken. Such was the impression made on Raskolnikov by the person sitting a little distance from him, who looked like a retired clerk. The young man often recalled this impression afterwards, and even ascribed it to presentiment. He looked repeatedly at the clerk, partly no doubt because the latter was staring persistently at him, obviously anxious to enter into conversation. At the other persons in the room, including the tavern-keeper, the clerk looked as though he were used to their company, and weary of it, showing a shade of condescending contempt for them as persons of station and culture inferior to his own, with whom it would be useless for him to converse. He was a man over fifty, bald and grizzled, of medium height, and stoutly built. His face, bloated from continual drinking, was of a yellow, even greenish, tinge, with swollen eyelids out of which keen reddish eyes gleamed like little chinks. But there was something very strange in him; there was a light in his eyes as though of intense feeling--perhaps there were even thought and intelligence, but at the same time there was a gleam of something like madness. He was wearing an old and hopelessly ragged black dress coat, with all its buttons missing except one, and that one he had buttoned, evidently clinging to this last trace of respectability. A crumpled shirt front, covered with spots and stains, protruded from his canvas waistcoat. Like a clerk, he wore no beard, nor moustache, but had been so long unshaven that his chin looked like a stiff greyish brush. And there was something respectable and like an official about his manner too. But he was restless; he ruffled up his hair and from time to time let his head drop into his hands dejectedly resting his ragged elbows on the stained and sticky table. At last he looked straight at Raskolnikov, and said loudly and resolutely:\n\n“May I venture, honoured sir, to engage you in polite conversation? Forasmuch as, though your exterior would not command respect, my experience admonishes me that you are a man of education and not accustomed to drinking. I have always respected education when in conjunction with genuine sentiments, and I am besides a titular counsellor in rank. Marmeladov--such is my name; titular counsellor. I make bold to inquire--have you been in the service?”\n\n“No, I am studying,” answered the young man, somewhat surprised at the grandiloquent style of the speaker and also at being so directly addressed. In spite of the momentary desire he had just been feeling for company of any sort, on being actually spoken to he felt immediately his habitual irritable and uneasy aversion for any stranger who approached or attempted to approach him.\n\n“A student then, or formerly a student,” cried the clerk. “Just what I thought! I’m a man of experience, immense experience, sir,” and he tapped his forehead with his fingers in self-approval. “You’ve been a student or have attended some learned institution!... But allow me....” He got up, staggered, took up his jug and glass, and sat down beside the young man, facing him a little sideways. He was drunk, but spoke fluently and boldly, only occasionally losing the thread of his sentences and drawling his words. He pounced upon Raskolnikov as greedily as though he too had not spoken to a soul for a month.\n\n“Honoured sir,” he began almost with solemnity, “poverty is not a vice, that’s a true saying. Yet I know too that drunkenness is not a virtue, and that that’s even truer. But beggary, honoured sir, beggary is a vice. In poverty you may still retain your innate nobility of soul, but in beggary--never--no one. For beggary a man is not chased out of human society with a stick, he is swept out with a broom, so as to make it as humiliating as possible; and quite right, too, forasmuch as in beggary I am ready to be the first to humiliate myself. Hence the pot-house! Honoured sir, a month ago Mr. Lebeziatnikov gave my wife a beating, and my wife is a very different matter from me! Do you understand? Allow me to ask you another question out of simple curiosity: have you ever spent a night on a hay barge, on the Neva?”\n\n“No, I have not happened to,” answered Raskolnikov. “What do you mean?”\n\n“Well, I’ve just come from one and it’s the fifth night I’ve slept so....” He filled his glass, emptied it and paused. Bits of hay were in fact clinging to his clothes and sticking to his hair. It seemed quite probable that he had not undressed or washed for the last five days. His hands, particularly, were filthy. They were fat and red, with black nails.\n\nHis conversation seemed to excite a general though languid interest. The boys at the counter fell to sniggering. The innkeeper came down from the upper room, apparently on purpose to listen to the “funny fellow” and sat down at a little distance, yawning lazily, but with dignity. Evidently Marmeladov was a familiar figure here, and he had most likely acquired his weakness for high-flown speeches from the habit of frequently entering into conversation with strangers of all sorts in the tavern. This habit develops into a necessity in some drunkards, and especially in those who are looked after sharply and kept in order at home. Hence in the company of other drinkers they try to justify themselves and even if possible obtain consideration.\n\n“Funny fellow!” pronounced the innkeeper. “And why don’t you work, why aren’t you at your duty, if you are in the service?”\n\n“Why am I not at my duty, honoured sir,” Marmeladov went on, addressing himself exclusively to Raskolnikov, as though it had been he who put that question to him. “Why am I not at my duty? Does not my heart ache to think what a useless worm I am? A month ago when Mr. Lebeziatnikov beat my wife with his own hands, and I lay drunk, didn’t I suffer? Excuse me, young man, has it ever happened to you... hm... well, to petition hopelessly for a loan?”\n\n“Yes, it has. But what do you mean by hopelessly?”\n\n“Hopelessly in the fullest sense, when you know beforehand that you will get nothing by it. You know, for instance, beforehand with positive certainty that this man, this most reputable and exemplary citizen, will on no consideration give you money; and indeed I ask you why should he? For he knows of course that I shan’t pay it back. From compassion? But Mr. Lebeziatnikov who keeps up with modern ideas explained the other day that compassion is forbidden nowadays by science itself, and that that’s what is done now in England, where there is political economy. Why, I ask you, should he give it to me? And yet though I know beforehand that he won’t, I set off to him and...”\n\n“Why do you go?” put in Raskolnikov.\n\n“Well, when one has no one, nowhere else one can go! For every man must have somewhere to go. Since there are times when one absolutely must go somewhere! When my own daughter first went out with a yellow ticket, then I had to go... (for my daughter has a yellow passport),” he added in parenthesis, looking with a certain uneasiness at the young man. “No matter, sir, no matter!” he went on hurriedly and with apparent composure when both the boys at the counter guffawed and even the innkeeper smiled--“No matter, I am not confounded by the wagging of their heads; for everyone knows everything about it already, and all that is secret is made open. And I accept it all, not with contempt, but with humility. So be it! So be it! ‘Behold the man!’ Excuse me, young man, can you.... No, to put it more strongly and more distinctly; not _can_ you but _dare_ you, looking upon me, assert that I am not a pig?”\n\nThe young man did not answer a word.\n\n“Well,” the orator began again stolidly and with even increased dignity, after waiting for the laughter in the room to subside. “Well, so be it, I am a pig, but she is a lady! I have the semblance of a beast, but Katerina Ivanovna, my spouse, is a person of education and an officer’s daughter. Granted, granted, I am a scoundrel, but she is a woman of a noble heart, full of sentiments, refined by education. And yet... oh, if only she felt for me! Honoured sir, honoured sir, you know every man ought to have at least one place where people feel for him! But Katerina Ivanovna, though she is magnanimous, she is unjust.... And yet, although I realise that when she pulls my hair she only does it out of pity--for I repeat without being ashamed, she pulls my hair, young man,” he declared with redoubled dignity, hearing the sniggering again--“but, my God, if she would but once.... But no, no! It’s all in vain and it’s no use talking! No use talking! For more than once, my wish did come true and more than once she has felt for me but... such is my fate and I am a beast by nature!”\n\n“Rather!” assented the innkeeper yawning. Marmeladov struck his fist resolutely on the table.\n\n“Such is my fate! Do you know, sir, do you know, I have sold her very stockings for drink? Not her shoes--that would be more or less in the order of things, but her stockings, her stockings I have sold for drink! Her mohair shawl I sold for drink, a present to her long ago, her own property, not mine; and we live in a cold room and she caught cold this winter and has begun coughing and spitting blood too. We have three little children and Katerina Ivanovna is at work from morning till night; she is scrubbing and cleaning and washing the children, for she’s been used to cleanliness from a child. But her chest is weak and she has a tendency to consumption and I feel it! Do you suppose I don’t feel it? And the more I drink the more I feel it. That’s why I drink too. I try to find sympathy and feeling in drink.... I drink so that I may suffer twice as much!” And as though in despair he laid his head down on the table.\n\n“Young man,” he went on, raising his head again, “in your face I seem to read some trouble of mind. When you came in I read it, and that was why I addressed you at once. For in unfolding to you the story of my life, I do not wish to make myself a laughing-stock before these idle listeners, who indeed know all about it already, but I am looking for a man of feeling and education. Know then that my wife was educated in a high-class school for the daughters of noblemen, and on leaving she danced the shawl dance before the governor and other personages for which she was presented with a gold medal and a certificate of merit. The medal... well, the medal of course was sold--long ago, hm... but the certificate of merit is in her trunk still and not long ago she showed it to our landlady. And although she is most continually on bad terms with the landlady, yet she wanted to tell someone or other of her past honours and of the happy days that are gone. I don’t condemn her for it, I don’t blame her, for the one thing left her is recollection of the past, and all the rest is dust and ashes. Yes, yes, she is a lady of spirit, proud and determined. She scrubs the floors herself and has nothing but black bread to eat, but won’t allow herself to be treated with disrespect. That’s why she would not overlook Mr. Lebeziatnikov’s rudeness to her, and so when he gave her a beating for it, she took to her bed more from the hurt to her feelings than from the blows. She was a widow when I married her, with three children, one smaller than the other. She married her first husband, an infantry officer, for love, and ran away with him from her father’s house. She was exceedingly fond of her husband; but he gave way to cards, got into trouble and with that he died. He used to beat her at the end: and although she paid him back, of which I have authentic documentary evidence, to this day she speaks of him with tears and she throws him up to me; and I am glad, I am glad that, though only in imagination, she should think of herself as having once been happy.... And she was left at his death with three children in a wild and remote district where I happened to be at the time; and she was left in such hopeless poverty that, although I have seen many ups and downs of all sort, I don’t feel equal to describing it even. Her relations had all thrown her off. And she was proud, too, excessively proud.... And then, honoured sir, and then, I, being at the time a widower, with a daughter of fourteen left me by my first wife, offered her my hand, for I could not bear the sight of such suffering. You can judge the extremity of her calamities, that she, a woman of education and culture and distinguished family, should have consented to be my wife. But she did! Weeping and sobbing and wringing her hands, she married me! For she had nowhere to turn! Do you understand, sir, do you understand what it means when you have absolutely nowhere to turn? No, that you don’t understand yet.... And for a whole year, I performed my duties conscientiously and faithfully, and did not touch this” (he tapped the jug with his finger), “for I have feelings. But even so, I could not please her; and then I lost my place too, and that through no fault of mine but through changes in the office; and then I did touch it!... It will be a year and a half ago soon since we found ourselves at last after many wanderings and numerous calamities in this magnificent capital, adorned with innumerable monuments. Here I obtained a situation.... I obtained it and I lost it again. Do you understand? This time it was through my own fault I lost it: for my weakness had come out.... We have now part of a room at Amalia Fyodorovna Lippevechsel’s; and what we live upon and what we pay our rent with, I could not say. There are a lot of people living there besides ourselves. Dirt and disorder, a perfect Bedlam... hm... yes... And meanwhile my daughter by my first wife has grown up; and what my daughter has had to put up with from her step-mother whilst she was growing up, I won’t speak of. For, though Katerina Ivanovna is full of generous feelings, she is a spirited lady, irritable and short-tempered.... Yes. But it’s no use going over that! Sonia, as you may well fancy, has had no education. I did make an effort four years ago to give her a course of geography and universal history, but as I was not very well up in those subjects myself and we had no suitable books, and what books we had... hm, anyway we have not even those now, so all our instruction came to an end. We stopped at Cyrus of Persia. Since she has attained years of maturity, she has read other books of romantic tendency and of late she had read with great interest a book she got through Mr. Lebeziatnikov, Lewes’ Physiology--do you know it?--and even recounted extracts from it to us: and that’s the whole of her education. And now may I venture to address you, honoured sir, on my own account with a private question. Do you suppose that a respectable poor girl can earn much by honest work? Not fifteen farthings a day can she earn, if she is respectable and has no special talent and that without putting her work down for an instant! And what’s more, Ivan Ivanitch Klopstock the civil counsellor--have you heard of him?--has not to this day paid her for the half-dozen linen shirts she made him and drove her roughly away, stamping and reviling her, on the pretext that the shirt collars were not made like the pattern and were put in askew. And there are the little ones hungry.... And Katerina Ivanovna walking up and down and wringing her hands, her cheeks flushed red, as they always are in that disease: ‘Here you live with us,’ says she, ‘you eat and drink and are kept warm and you do nothing to help.’ And much she gets to eat and drink when there is not a crust for the little ones for three days! I was lying at the time... well, what of it! I was lying drunk and I heard my Sonia speaking (she is a gentle creature with a soft little voice... fair hair and such a pale, thin little face). She said: ‘Katerina Ivanovna, am I really to do a thing like that?’ And Darya Frantsovna, a woman of evil character and very well known to the police, had two or three times tried to get at her through the landlady. ‘And why not?’ said Katerina Ivanovna with a jeer, ‘you are something mighty precious to be so careful of!’ But don’t blame her, don’t blame her, honoured sir, don’t blame her! She was not herself when she spoke, but driven to distraction by her illness and the crying of the hungry children; and it was said more to wound her than anything else.... For that’s Katerina Ivanovna’s character, and when children cry, even from hunger, she falls to beating them at once. At six o’clock I saw Sonia get up, put on her kerchief and her cape, and go out of the room and about nine o’clock she came back. She walked straight up to Katerina Ivanovna and she laid thirty roubles on the table before her in silence. She did not utter a word, she did not even look at her, she simply picked up our big green _drap de dames_ shawl (we have a shawl, made of _drap de dames_), put it over her head and face and lay down on the bed with her face to the wall; only her little shoulders and her body kept shuddering.... And I went on lying there, just as before.... And then I saw, young man, I saw Katerina Ivanovna, in the same silence go up to Sonia’s little bed; she was on her knees all the evening kissing Sonia’s feet, and would not get up, and then they both fell asleep in each other’s arms... together, together... yes... and I... lay drunk.”\n\nMarmeladov stopped short, as though his voice had failed him. Then he hurriedly filled his glass, drank, and cleared his throat.\n\n“Since then, sir,” he went on after a brief pause--“Since then, owing to an unfortunate occurrence and through information given by evil-intentioned persons--in all which Darya Frantsovna took a leading part on the pretext that she had been treated with want of respect--since then my daughter Sofya Semyonovna has been forced to take a yellow ticket, and owing to that she is unable to go on living with us. For our landlady, Amalia Fyodorovna would not hear of it (though she had backed up Darya Frantsovna before) and Mr. Lebeziatnikov too... hm.... All the trouble between him and Katerina Ivanovna was on Sonia’s account. At first he was for making up to Sonia himself and then all of a sudden he stood on his dignity: ‘how,’ said he, ‘can a highly educated man like me live in the same rooms with a girl like that?’ And Katerina Ivanovna would not let it pass, she stood up for her... and so that’s how it happened. And Sonia comes to us now, mostly after dark; she comforts Katerina Ivanovna and gives her all she can.... She has a room at the Kapernaumovs’ the tailors, she lodges with them; Kapernaumov is a lame man with a cleft palate and all of his numerous family have cleft palates too. And his wife, too, has a cleft palate. They all live in one room, but Sonia has her own, partitioned off.... Hm... yes... very poor people and all with cleft palates... yes. Then I got up in the morning, and put on my rags, lifted up my hands to heaven and set off to his excellency Ivan Afanasyvitch. His excellency Ivan Afanasyvitch, do you know him? No? Well, then, it’s a man of God you don’t know. He is wax... wax before the face of the Lord; even as wax melteth!... His eyes were dim when he heard my story. ‘Marmeladov, once already you have deceived my expectations... I’ll take you once more on my own responsibility’--that’s what he said, ‘remember,’ he said, ‘and now you can go.’ I kissed the dust at his feet--in thought only, for in reality he would not have allowed me to do it, being a statesman and a man of modern political and enlightened ideas. I returned home, and when I announced that I’d been taken back into the service and should receive a salary, heavens, what a to-do there was!...”\n\nMarmeladov stopped again in violent excitement. At that moment a whole party of revellers already drunk came in from the street, and the sounds of a hired concertina and the cracked piping voice of a child of seven singing “The Hamlet” were heard in the entry. The room was filled with noise. The tavern-keeper and the boys were busy with the new-comers. Marmeladov paying no attention to the new arrivals continued his story. He appeared by now to be extremely weak, but as he became more and more drunk, he became more and more talkative. The recollection of his recent success in getting the situation seemed to revive him, and was positively reflected in a sort of radiance on his face. Raskolnikov listened attentively.\n\n“That was five weeks ago, sir. Yes.... As soon as Katerina Ivanovna and Sonia heard of it, mercy on us, it was as though I stepped into the kingdom of Heaven. It used to be: you can lie like a beast, nothing but abuse. Now they were walking on tiptoe, hushing the children. ‘Semyon Zaharovitch is tired with his work at the office, he is resting, shh!’ They made me coffee before I went to work and boiled cream for me! They began to get real cream for me, do you hear that? And how they managed to get together the money for a decent outfit--eleven roubles, fifty copecks, I can’t guess. Boots, cotton shirt-fronts--most magnificent, a uniform, they got up all in splendid style, for eleven roubles and a half. The first morning I came back from the office I found Katerina Ivanovna had cooked two courses for dinner--soup and salt meat with horse radish--which we had never dreamed of till then. She had not any dresses... none at all, but she got herself up as though she were going on a visit; and not that she’d anything to do it with, she smartened herself up with nothing at all, she’d done her hair nicely, put on a clean collar of some sort, cuffs, and there she was, quite a different person, she was younger and better looking. Sonia, my little darling, had only helped with money ‘for the time,’ she said, ‘it won’t do for me to come and see you too often. After dark maybe when no one can see.’ Do you hear, do you hear? I lay down for a nap after dinner and what do you think: though Katerina Ivanovna had quarrelled to the last degree with our landlady Amalia Fyodorovna only a week before, she could not resist then asking her in to coffee. For two hours they were sitting, whispering together. ‘Semyon Zaharovitch is in the service again, now, and receiving a salary,’ says she, ‘and he went himself to his excellency and his excellency himself came out to him, made all the others wait and led Semyon Zaharovitch by the hand before everybody into his study.’ Do you hear, do you hear? ‘To be sure,’ says he, ‘Semyon Zaharovitch, remembering your past services,’ says he, ‘and in spite of your propensity to that foolish weakness, since you promise now and since moreover we’ve got on badly without you,’ (do you hear, do you hear;) ‘and so,’ says he, ‘I rely now on your word as a gentleman.’ And all that, let me tell you, she has simply made up for herself, and not simply out of wantonness, for the sake of bragging; no, she believes it all herself, she amuses herself with her own fancies, upon my word she does! And I don’t blame her for it, no, I don’t blame her!... Six days ago when I brought her my first earnings in full--twenty-three roubles forty copecks altogether--she called me her poppet: ‘poppet,’ said she, ‘my little poppet.’ And when we were by ourselves, you understand? You would not think me a beauty, you would not think much of me as a husband, would you?... Well, she pinched my cheek, ‘my little poppet,’ said she.”\n\nMarmeladov broke off, tried to smile, but suddenly his chin began to twitch. He controlled himself however. The tavern, the degraded appearance of the man, the five nights in the hay barge, and the pot of spirits, and yet this poignant love for his wife and children bewildered his listener. Raskolnikov listened intently but with a sick sensation. He felt vexed that he had come here.\n\n“Honoured sir, honoured sir,” cried Marmeladov recovering himself--“Oh, sir, perhaps all this seems a laughing matter to you, as it does to others, and perhaps I am only worrying you with the stupidity of all the trivial details of my home life, but it is not a laughing matter to me. For I can feel it all.... And the whole of that heavenly day of my life and the whole of that evening I passed in fleeting dreams of how I would arrange it all, and how I would dress all the children, and how I should give her rest, and how I should rescue my own daughter from dishonour and restore her to the bosom of her family.... And a great deal more.... Quite excusable, sir. Well, then, sir” (Marmeladov suddenly gave a sort of start, raised his head and gazed intently at his listener) “well, on the very next day after all those dreams, that is to say, exactly five days ago, in the evening, by a cunning trick, like a thief in the night, I stole from Katerina Ivanovna the key of her box, took out what was left of my earnings, how much it was I have forgotten, and now look at me, all of you! It’s the fifth day since I left home, and they are looking for me there and it’s the end of my employment, and my uniform is lying in a tavern on the Egyptian bridge. I exchanged it for the garments I have on... and it’s the end of everything!”\n\nMarmeladov struck his forehead with his fist, clenched his teeth, closed his eyes and leaned heavily with his elbow on the table. But a minute later his face suddenly changed and with a certain assumed slyness and affectation of bravado, he glanced at Raskolnikov, laughed and said:\n\n“This morning I went to see Sonia, I went to ask her for a pick-me-up! He-he-he!”\n\n“You don’t say she gave it to you?” cried one of the new-comers; he shouted the words and went off into a guffaw.\n\n“This very quart was bought with her money,” Marmeladov declared, addressing himself exclusively to Raskolnikov. “Thirty copecks she gave me with her own hands, her last, all she had, as I saw.... She said nothing, she only looked at me without a word.... Not on earth, but up yonder... they grieve over men, they weep, but they don’t blame them, they don’t blame them! But it hurts more, it hurts more when they don’t blame! Thirty copecks yes! And maybe she needs them now, eh? What do you think, my dear sir? For now she’s got to keep up her appearance. It costs money, that smartness, that special smartness, you know? Do you understand? And there’s pomatum, too, you see, she must have things; petticoats, starched ones, shoes, too, real jaunty ones to show off her foot when she has to step over a puddle. Do you understand, sir, do you understand what all that smartness means? And here I, her own father, here I took thirty copecks of that money for a drink! And I am drinking it! And I have already drunk it! Come, who will have pity on a man like me, eh? Are you sorry for me, sir, or not? Tell me, sir, are you sorry or not? He-he-he!”\n\nHe would have filled his glass, but there was no drink left. The pot was empty.\n\n“What are you to be pitied for?” shouted the tavern-keeper who was again near them.\n\nShouts of laughter and even oaths followed. The laughter and the oaths came from those who were listening and also from those who had heard nothing but were simply looking at the figure of the discharged government clerk.\n\n“To be pitied! Why am I to be pitied?” Marmeladov suddenly declaimed, standing up with his arm outstretched, as though he had been only waiting for that question.\n\n“Why am I to be pitied, you say? Yes! there’s nothing to pity me for! I ought to be crucified, crucified on a cross, not pitied! Crucify me, oh judge, crucify me but pity me! And then I will go of myself to be crucified, for it’s not merry-making I seek but tears and tribulation!... Do you suppose, you that sell, that this pint of yours has been sweet to me? It was tribulation I sought at the bottom of it, tears and tribulation, and have found it, and I have tasted it; but He will pity us Who has had pity on all men, Who has understood all men and all things, He is the One, He too is the judge. He will come in that day and He will ask: ‘Where is the daughter who gave herself for her cross, consumptive step-mother and for the little children of another? Where is the daughter who had pity upon the filthy drunkard, her earthly father, undismayed by his beastliness?’ And He will say, ‘Come to me! I have already forgiven thee once.... I have forgiven thee once.... Thy sins which are many are forgiven thee for thou hast loved much....’ And he will forgive my Sonia, He will forgive, I know it... I felt it in my heart when I was with her just now! And He will judge and will forgive all, the good and the evil, the wise and the meek.... And when He has done with all of them, then He will summon us. ‘You too come forth,’ He will say, ‘Come forth ye drunkards, come forth, ye weak ones, come forth, ye children of shame!’ And we shall all come forth, without shame and shall stand before him. And He will say unto us, ‘Ye are swine, made in the Image of the Beast and with his mark; but come ye also!’ And the wise ones and those of understanding will say, ‘Oh Lord, why dost Thou receive these men?’ And He will say, ‘This is why I receive them, oh ye wise, this is why I receive them, oh ye of understanding, that not one of them believed himself to be worthy of this.’ And He will hold out His hands to us and we shall fall down before him... and we shall weep... and we shall understand all things! Then we shall understand all!... and all will understand, Katerina Ivanovna even... she will understand.... Lord, Thy kingdom come!” And he sank down on the bench exhausted, and helpless, looking at no one, apparently oblivious of his surroundings and plunged in deep thought. His words had created a certain impression; there was a moment of silence; but soon laughter and oaths were heard again.\n\n“That’s his notion!”\n\n“Talked himself silly!”\n\n“A fine clerk he is!”\n\nAnd so on, and so on.\n\n“Let us go, sir,” said Marmeladov all at once, raising his head and addressing Raskolnikov--“come along with me... Kozel’s house, looking into the yard. I’m going to Katerina Ivanovna--time I did.”\n\nRaskolnikov had for some time been wanting to go and he had meant to help him. Marmeladov was much unsteadier on his legs than in his speech and leaned heavily on the young man. They had two or three hundred paces to go. The drunken man was more and more overcome by dismay and confusion as they drew nearer the house.\n\n“It’s not Katerina Ivanovna I am afraid of now,” he muttered in agitation--“and that she will begin pulling my hair. What does my hair matter! Bother my hair! That’s what I say! Indeed it will be better if she does begin pulling it, that’s not what I am afraid of... it’s her eyes I am afraid of... yes, her eyes... the red on her cheeks, too, frightens me... and her breathing too.... Have you noticed how people in that disease breathe... when they are excited? I am frightened of the children’s crying, too.... For if Sonia has not taken them food... I don’t know what’s happened! I don’t know! But blows I am not afraid of.... Know, sir, that such blows are not a pain to me, but even an enjoyment. In fact I can’t get on without it.... It’s better so. Let her strike me, it relieves her heart... it’s better so... There is the house. The house of Kozel, the cabinet-maker... a German, well-to-do. Lead the way!”\n\nThey went in from the yard and up to the fourth storey. The staircase got darker and darker as they went up. It was nearly eleven o’clock and although in summer in Petersburg there is no real night, yet it was quite dark at the top of the stairs.\n\nA grimy little door at the very top of the stairs stood ajar. A very poor-looking room about ten paces long was lighted up by a candle-end; the whole of it was visible from the entrance. It was all in disorder, littered up with rags of all sorts, especially children’s garments. Across the furthest corner was stretched a ragged sheet. Behind it probably was the bed. There was nothing in the room except two chairs and a sofa covered with American leather, full of holes, before which stood an old deal kitchen-table, unpainted and uncovered. At the edge of the table stood a smoldering tallow-candle in an iron candlestick. It appeared that the family had a room to themselves, not part of a room, but their room was practically a passage. The door leading to the other rooms, or rather cupboards, into which Amalia Lippevechsel’s flat was divided stood half open, and there was shouting, uproar and laughter within. People seemed to be playing cards and drinking tea there. Words of the most unceremonious kind flew out from time to time.\n\nRaskolnikov recognised Katerina Ivanovna at once. She was a rather tall, slim and graceful woman, terribly emaciated, with magnificent dark brown hair and with a hectic flush in her cheeks. She was pacing up and down in her little room, pressing her hands against her chest; her lips were parched and her breathing came in nervous broken gasps. Her eyes glittered as in fever and looked about with a harsh immovable stare. And that consumptive and excited face with the last flickering light of the candle-end playing upon it made a sickening impression. She seemed to Raskolnikov about thirty years old and was certainly a strange wife for Marmeladov.... She had not heard them and did not notice them coming in. She seemed to be lost in thought, hearing and seeing nothing. The room was close, but she had not opened the window; a stench rose from the staircase, but the door on to the stairs was not closed. From the inner rooms clouds of tobacco smoke floated in, she kept coughing, but did not close the door. The youngest child, a girl of six, was asleep, sitting curled up on the floor with her head on the sofa. A boy a year older stood crying and shaking in the corner, probably he had just had a beating. Beside him stood a girl of nine years old, tall and thin, wearing a thin and ragged chemise with an ancient cashmere pelisse flung over her bare shoulders, long outgrown and barely reaching her knees. Her arm, as thin as a stick, was round her brother’s neck. She was trying to comfort him, whispering something to him, and doing all she could to keep him from whimpering again. At the same time her large dark eyes, which looked larger still from the thinness of her frightened face, were watching her mother with alarm. Marmeladov did not enter the door, but dropped on his knees in the very doorway, pushing Raskolnikov in front of him. The woman seeing a stranger stopped indifferently facing him, coming to herself for a moment and apparently wondering what he had come for. But evidently she decided that he was going into the next room, as he had to pass through hers to get there. Taking no further notice of him, she walked towards the outer door to close it and uttered a sudden scream on seeing her husband on his knees in the doorway.\n\n“Ah!” she cried out in a frenzy, “he has come back! The criminal! the monster!... And where is the money? What’s in your pocket, show me! And your clothes are all different! Where are your clothes? Where is the money! Speak!”\n\nAnd she fell to searching him. Marmeladov submissively and obediently held up both arms to facilitate the search. Not a farthing was there.\n\n“Where is the money?” she cried--“Mercy on us, can he have drunk it all? There were twelve silver roubles left in the chest!” and in a fury she seized him by the hair and dragged him into the room. Marmeladov seconded her efforts by meekly crawling along on his knees.\n\n“And this is a consolation to me! This does not hurt me, but is a positive con-so-la-tion, ho-nou-red sir,” he called out, shaken to and fro by his hair and even once striking the ground with his forehead. The child asleep on the floor woke up, and began to cry. The boy in the corner losing all control began trembling and screaming and rushed to his sister in violent terror, almost in a fit. The eldest girl was shaking like a leaf.\n\n“He’s drunk it! he’s drunk it all,” the poor woman screamed in despair--“and his clothes are gone! And they are hungry, hungry!”--and wringing her hands she pointed to the children. “Oh, accursed life! And you, are you not ashamed?”--she pounced all at once upon Raskolnikov--“from the tavern! Have you been drinking with him? You have been drinking with him, too! Go away!”\n\nThe young man was hastening away without uttering a word. The inner door was thrown wide open and inquisitive faces were peering in at it. Coarse laughing faces with pipes and cigarettes and heads wearing caps thrust themselves in at the doorway. Further in could be seen figures in dressing gowns flung open, in costumes of unseemly scantiness, some of them with cards in their hands. They were particularly diverted, when Marmeladov, dragged about by his hair, shouted that it was a consolation to him. They even began to come into the room; at last a sinister shrill outcry was heard: this came from Amalia Lippevechsel herself pushing her way amongst them and trying to restore order after her own fashion and for the hundredth time to frighten the poor woman by ordering her with coarse abuse to clear out of the room next day. As he went out, Raskolnikov had time to put his hand into his pocket, to snatch up the coppers he had received in exchange for his rouble in the tavern and to lay them unnoticed on the window. Afterwards on the stairs, he changed his mind and would have gone back.\n\n“What a stupid thing I’ve done,” he thought to himself, “they have Sonia and I want it myself.” But reflecting that it would be impossible to take it back now and that in any case he would not have taken it, he dismissed it with a wave of his hand and went back to his lodging. “Sonia wants pomatum too,” he said as he walked along the street, and he laughed malignantly--“such smartness costs money.... Hm! And maybe Sonia herself will be bankrupt to-day, for there is always a risk, hunting big game... digging for gold... then they would all be without a crust to-morrow except for my money. Hurrah for Sonia! What a mine they’ve dug there! And they’re making the most of it! Yes, they are making the most of it! They’ve wept over it and grown used to it. Man grows used to everything, the scoundrel!”\n\nHe sank into thought.\n\n“And what if I am wrong,” he cried suddenly after a moment’s thought. “What if man is not really a scoundrel, man in general, I mean, the whole race of mankind--then all the rest is prejudice, simply artificial terrors and there are no barriers and it’s all as it should be.”\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nHe waked up late next day after a broken sleep. But his sleep had not refreshed him; he waked up bilious, irritable, ill-tempered, and looked with hatred at his room. It was a tiny cupboard of a room about six paces in length. It had a poverty-stricken appearance with its dusty yellow paper peeling off the walls, and it was so low-pitched that a man of more than average height was ill at ease in it and felt every moment that he would knock his head against the ceiling. The furniture was in keeping with the room: there were three old chairs, rather rickety; a painted table in the corner on which lay a few manuscripts and books; the dust that lay thick upon them showed that they had been long untouched. A big clumsy sofa occupied almost the whole of one wall and half the floor space of the room; it was once covered with chintz, but was now in rags and served Raskolnikov as a bed. Often he went to sleep on it, as he was, without undressing, without sheets, wrapped in his old student’s overcoat, with his head on one little pillow, under which he heaped up all the linen he had, clean and dirty, by way of a bolster. A little table stood in front of the sofa.\n\nIt would have been difficult to sink to a lower ebb of disorder, but to Raskolnikov in his present state of mind this was positively agreeable. He had got completely away from everyone, like a tortoise in its shell, and even the sight of a servant girl who had to wait upon him and looked sometimes into his room made him writhe with nervous irritation. He was in the condition that overtakes some monomaniacs entirely concentrated upon one thing. His landlady had for the last fortnight given up sending him in meals, and he had not yet thought of expostulating with her, though he went without his dinner. Nastasya, the cook and only servant, was rather pleased at the lodger’s mood and had entirely given up sweeping and doing his room, only once a week or so she would stray into his room with a broom. She waked him up that day.\n\n“Get up, why are you asleep?” she called to him. “It’s past nine, I have brought you some tea; will you have a cup? I should think you’re fairly starving?”\n\nRaskolnikov opened his eyes, started and recognised Nastasya.\n\n“From the landlady, eh?” he asked, slowly and with a sickly face sitting up on the sofa.\n\n“From the landlady, indeed!”\n\nShe set before him her own cracked teapot full of weak and stale tea and laid two yellow lumps of sugar by the side of it.\n\n“Here, Nastasya, take it please,” he said, fumbling in his pocket (for he had slept in his clothes) and taking out a handful of coppers--“run and buy me a loaf. And get me a little sausage, the cheapest, at the pork-butcher’s.”\n\n“The loaf I’ll fetch you this very minute, but wouldn’t you rather have some cabbage soup instead of sausage? It’s capital soup, yesterday’s. I saved it for you yesterday, but you came in late. It’s fine soup.”\n\nWhen the soup had been brought, and he had begun upon it, Nastasya sat down beside him on the sofa and began chatting. She was a country peasant-woman and a very talkative one.\n\n“Praskovya Pavlovna means to complain to the police about you,” she said.\n\nHe scowled.\n\n“To the police? What does she want?”\n\n“You don’t pay her money and you won’t turn out of the room. That’s what she wants, to be sure.”\n\n“The devil, that’s the last straw,” he muttered, grinding his teeth, “no, that would not suit me... just now. She is a fool,” he added aloud. “I’ll go and talk to her to-day.”\n\n“Fool she is and no mistake, just as I am. But why, if you are so clever, do you lie here like a sack and have nothing to show for it? One time you used to go out, you say, to teach children. But why is it you do nothing now?”\n\n“I am doing...” Raskolnikov began sullenly and reluctantly.\n\n“What are you doing?”\n\n“Work...”\n\n“What sort of work?”\n\n“I am thinking,” he answered seriously after a pause.\n\nNastasya was overcome with a fit of laughter. She was given to laughter and when anything amused her, she laughed inaudibly, quivering and shaking all over till she felt ill.\n\n“And have you made much money by your thinking?” she managed to articulate at last.\n\n“One can’t go out to give lessons without boots. And I’m sick of it.”\n\n“Don’t quarrel with your bread and butter.”\n\n“They pay so little for lessons. What’s the use of a few coppers?” he answered, reluctantly, as though replying to his own thought.\n\n“And you want to get a fortune all at once?”\n\nHe looked at her strangely.\n\n“Yes, I want a fortune,” he answered firmly, after a brief pause.\n\n“Don’t be in such a hurry, you quite frighten me! Shall I get you the loaf or not?”\n\n“As you please.”\n\n“Ah, I forgot! A letter came for you yesterday when you were out.”\n\n“A letter? for me! from whom?”\n\n“I can’t say. I gave three copecks of my own to the postman for it. Will you pay me back?”\n\n“Then bring it to me, for God’s sake, bring it,” cried Raskolnikov greatly excited--“good God!”\n\nA minute later the letter was brought him. That was it: from his mother, from the province of R----. He turned pale when he took it. It was a long while since he had received a letter, but another feeling also suddenly stabbed his heart.\n\n“Nastasya, leave me alone, for goodness’ sake; here are your three copecks, but for goodness’ sake, make haste and go!”\n\nThe letter was quivering in his hand; he did not want to open it in her presence; he wanted to be left _alone_ with this letter. When Nastasya had gone out, he lifted it quickly to his lips and kissed it; then he gazed intently at the address, the small, sloping handwriting, so dear and familiar, of the mother who had once taught him to read and write. He delayed; he seemed almost afraid of something. At last he opened it; it was a thick heavy letter, weighing over two ounces, two large sheets of note paper were covered with very small handwriting.\n\n“My dear Rodya,” wrote his mother--“it’s two months since I last had a talk with you by letter which has distressed me and even kept me awake at night, thinking. But I am sure you will not blame me for my inevitable silence. You know how I love you; you are all we have to look to, Dounia and I, you are our all, our one hope, our one stay. What a grief it was to me when I heard that you had given up the university some months ago, for want of means to keep yourself and that you had lost your lessons and your other work! How could I help you out of my hundred and twenty roubles a year pension? The fifteen roubles I sent you four months ago I borrowed, as you know, on security of my pension, from Vassily Ivanovitch Vahrushin a merchant of this town. He is a kind-hearted man and was a friend of your father’s too. But having given him the right to receive the pension, I had to wait till the debt was paid off and that is only just done, so that I’ve been unable to send you anything all this time. But now, thank God, I believe I shall be able to send you something more and in fact we may congratulate ourselves on our good fortune now, of which I hasten to inform you. In the first place, would you have guessed, dear Rodya, that your sister has been living with me for the last six weeks and we shall not be separated in the future. Thank God, her sufferings are over, but I will tell you everything in order, so that you may know just how everything has happened and all that we have hitherto concealed from you. When you wrote to me two months ago that you had heard that Dounia had a great deal to put up with in the Svidrigaïlovs’ house, when you wrote that and asked me to tell you all about it--what could I write in answer to you? If I had written the whole truth to you, I dare say you would have thrown up everything and have come to us, even if you had to walk all the way, for I know your character and your feelings, and you would not let your sister be insulted. I was in despair myself, but what could I do? And, besides, I did not know the whole truth myself then. What made it all so difficult was that Dounia received a hundred roubles in advance when she took the place as governess in their family, on condition of part of her salary being deducted every month, and so it was impossible to throw up the situation without repaying the debt. This sum (now I can explain it all to you, my precious Rodya) she took chiefly in order to send you sixty roubles, which you needed so terribly then and which you received from us last year. We deceived you then, writing that this money came from Dounia’s savings, but that was not so, and now I tell you all about it, because, thank God, things have suddenly changed for the better, and that you may know how Dounia loves you and what a heart she has. At first indeed Mr. Svidrigaïlov treated her very rudely and used to make disrespectful and jeering remarks at table.... But I don’t want to go into all those painful details, so as not to worry you for nothing when it is now all over. In short, in spite of the kind and generous behaviour of Marfa Petrovna, Mr. Svidrigaïlov’s wife, and all the rest of the household, Dounia had a very hard time, especially when Mr. Svidrigaïlov, relapsing into his old regimental habits, was under the influence of Bacchus. And how do you think it was all explained later on? Would you believe that the crazy fellow had conceived a passion for Dounia from the beginning, but had concealed it under a show of rudeness and contempt. Possibly he was ashamed and horrified himself at his own flighty hopes, considering his years and his being the father of a family; and that made him angry with Dounia. And possibly, too, he hoped by his rude and sneering behaviour to hide the truth from others. But at last he lost all control and had the face to make Dounia an open and shameful proposal, promising her all sorts of inducements and offering, besides, to throw up everything and take her to another estate of his, or even abroad. You can imagine all she went through! To leave her situation at once was impossible not only on account of the money debt, but also to spare the feelings of Marfa Petrovna, whose suspicions would have been aroused: and then Dounia would have been the cause of a rupture in the family. And it would have meant a terrible scandal for Dounia too; that would have been inevitable. There were various other reasons owing to which Dounia could not hope to escape from that awful house for another six weeks. You know Dounia, of course; you know how clever she is and what a strong will she has. Dounia can endure a great deal and even in the most difficult cases she has the fortitude to maintain her firmness. She did not even write to me about everything for fear of upsetting me, although we were constantly in communication. It all ended very unexpectedly. Marfa Petrovna accidentally overheard her husband imploring Dounia in the garden, and, putting quite a wrong interpretation on the position, threw the blame upon her, believing her to be the cause of it all. An awful scene took place between them on the spot in the garden; Marfa Petrovna went so far as to strike Dounia, refused to hear anything and was shouting at her for a whole hour and then gave orders that Dounia should be packed off at once to me in a plain peasant’s cart, into which they flung all her things, her linen and her clothes, all pell-mell, without folding it up and packing it. And a heavy shower of rain came on, too, and Dounia, insulted and put to shame, had to drive with a peasant in an open cart all the seventeen versts into town. Only think now what answer could I have sent to the letter I received from you two months ago and what could I have written? I was in despair; I dared not write to you the truth because you would have been very unhappy, mortified and indignant, and yet what could you do? You could only perhaps ruin yourself, and, besides, Dounia would not allow it; and fill up my letter with trifles when my heart was so full of sorrow, I could not. For a whole month the town was full of gossip about this scandal, and it came to such a pass that Dounia and I dared not even go to church on account of the contemptuous looks, whispers, and even remarks made aloud about us. All our acquaintances avoided us, nobody even bowed to us in the street, and I learnt that some shopmen and clerks were intending to insult us in a shameful way, smearing the gates of our house with pitch, so that the landlord began to tell us we must leave. All this was set going by Marfa Petrovna who managed to slander Dounia and throw dirt at her in every family. She knows everyone in the neighbourhood, and that month she was continually coming into the town, and as she is rather talkative and fond of gossiping about her family affairs and particularly of complaining to all and each of her husband--which is not at all right--so in a short time she had spread her story not only in the town, but over the whole surrounding district. It made me ill, but Dounia bore it better than I did, and if only you could have seen how she endured it all and tried to comfort me and cheer me up! She is an angel! But by God’s mercy, our sufferings were cut short: Mr. Svidrigaïlov returned to his senses and repented and, probably feeling sorry for Dounia, he laid before Marfa Petrovna a complete and unmistakable proof of Dounia’s innocence, in the form of a letter Dounia had been forced to write and give to him, before Marfa Petrovna came upon them in the garden. This letter, which remained in Mr. Svidrigaïlov’s hands after her departure, she had written to refuse personal explanations and secret interviews, for which he was entreating her. In that letter she reproached him with great heat and indignation for the baseness of his behaviour in regard to Marfa Petrovna, reminding him that he was the father and head of a family and telling him how infamous it was of him to torment and make unhappy a defenceless girl, unhappy enough already. Indeed, dear Rodya, the letter was so nobly and touchingly written that I sobbed when I read it and to this day I cannot read it without tears. Moreover, the evidence of the servants, too, cleared Dounia’s reputation; they had seen and known a great deal more than Mr. Svidrigaïlov had himself supposed--as indeed is always the case with servants. Marfa Petrovna was completely taken aback, and ‘again crushed’ as she said herself to us, but she was completely convinced of Dounia’s innocence. The very next day, being Sunday, she went straight to the Cathedral, knelt down and prayed with tears to Our Lady to give her strength to bear this new trial and to do her duty. Then she came straight from the Cathedral to us, told us the whole story, wept bitterly and, fully penitent, she embraced Dounia and besought her to forgive her. The same morning without any delay, she went round to all the houses in the town and everywhere, shedding tears, she asserted in the most flattering terms Dounia’s innocence and the nobility of her feelings and her behavior. What was more, she showed and read to everyone the letter in Dounia’s own handwriting to Mr. Svidrigaïlov and even allowed them to take copies of it--which I must say I think was superfluous. In this way she was busy for several days in driving about the whole town, because some people had taken offence through precedence having been given to others. And therefore they had to take turns, so that in every house she was expected before she arrived, and everyone knew that on such and such a day Marfa Petrovna would be reading the letter in such and such a place and people assembled for every reading of it, even many who had heard it several times already both in their own houses and in other people’s. In my opinion a great deal, a very great deal of all this was unnecessary; but that’s Marfa Petrovna’s character. Anyway she succeeded in completely re-establishing Dounia’s reputation and the whole ignominy of this affair rested as an indelible disgrace upon her husband, as the only person to blame, so that I really began to feel sorry for him; it was really treating the crazy fellow too harshly. Dounia was at once asked to give lessons in several families, but she refused. All of a sudden everyone began to treat her with marked respect and all this did much to bring about the event by which, one may say, our whole fortunes are now transformed. You must know, dear Rodya, that Dounia has a suitor and that she has already consented to marry him. I hasten to tell you all about the matter, and though it has been arranged without asking your consent, I think you will not be aggrieved with me or with your sister on that account, for you will see that we could not wait and put off our decision till we heard from you. And you could not have judged all the facts without being on the spot. This was how it happened. He is already of the rank of a counsellor, Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin, and is distantly related to Marfa Petrovna, who has been very active in bringing the match about. It began with his expressing through her his desire to make our acquaintance. He was properly received, drank coffee with us and the very next day he sent us a letter in which he very courteously made an offer and begged for a speedy and decided answer. He is a very busy man and is in a great hurry to get to Petersburg, so that every moment is precious to him. At first, of course, we were greatly surprised, as it had all happened so quickly and unexpectedly. We thought and talked it over the whole day. He is a well-to-do man, to be depended upon, he has two posts in the government and has already made his fortune. It is true that he is forty-five years old, but he is of a fairly prepossessing appearance and might still be thought attractive by women, and he is altogether a very respectable and presentable man, only he seems a little morose and somewhat conceited. But possibly that may only be the impression he makes at first sight. And beware, dear Rodya, when he comes to Petersburg, as he shortly will do, beware of judging him too hastily and severely, as your way is, if there is anything you do not like in him at first sight. I give you this warning, although I feel sure that he will make a favourable impression upon you. Moreover, in order to understand any man one must be deliberate and careful to avoid forming prejudices and mistaken ideas, which are very difficult to correct and get over afterwards. And Pyotr Petrovitch, judging by many indications, is a thoroughly estimable man. At his first visit, indeed, he told us that he was a practical man, but still he shares, as he expressed it, many of the convictions ‘of our most rising generation’ and he is an opponent of all prejudices. He said a good deal more, for he seems a little conceited and likes to be listened to, but this is scarcely a vice. I, of course, understood very little of it, but Dounia explained to me that, though he is not a man of great education, he is clever and seems to be good-natured. You know your sister’s character, Rodya. She is a resolute, sensible, patient and generous girl, but she has a passionate heart, as I know very well. Of course, there is no great love either on his side, or on hers, but Dounia is a clever girl and has the heart of an angel, and will make it her duty to make her husband happy who on his side will make her happiness his care. Of that we have no good reason to doubt, though it must be admitted the matter has been arranged in great haste. Besides he is a man of great prudence and he will see, to be sure, of himself, that his own happiness will be the more secure, the happier Dounia is with him. And as for some defects of character, for some habits and even certain differences of opinion--which indeed are inevitable even in the happiest marriages--Dounia has said that, as regards all that, she relies on herself, that there is nothing to be uneasy about, and that she is ready to put up with a great deal, if only their future relationship can be an honourable and straightforward one. He struck me, for instance, at first, as rather abrupt, but that may well come from his being an outspoken man, and that is no doubt how it is. For instance, at his second visit, after he had received Dounia’s consent, in the course of conversation, he declared that before making Dounia’s acquaintance, he had made up his mind to marry a girl of good reputation, without dowry and, above all, one who had experienced poverty, because, as he explained, a man ought not to be indebted to his wife, but that it is better for a wife to look upon her husband as her benefactor. I must add that he expressed it more nicely and politely than I have done, for I have forgotten his actual phrases and only remember the meaning. And, besides, it was obviously not said of design, but slipped out in the heat of conversation, so that he tried afterwards to correct himself and smooth it over, but all the same it did strike me as somewhat rude, and I said so afterwards to Dounia. But Dounia was vexed, and answered that ‘words are not deeds,’ and that, of course, is perfectly true. Dounia did not sleep all night before she made up her mind, and, thinking that I was asleep, she got out of bed and was walking up and down the room all night; at last she knelt down before the ikon and prayed long and fervently and in the morning she told me that she had decided.\n\n“I have mentioned already that Pyotr Petrovitch is just setting off for Petersburg, where he has a great deal of business, and he wants to open a legal bureau. He has been occupied for many years in conducting civil and commercial litigation, and only the other day he won an important case. He has to be in Petersburg because he has an important case before the Senate. So, Rodya dear, he may be of the greatest use to you, in every way indeed, and Dounia and I have agreed that from this very day you could definitely enter upon your career and might consider that your future is marked out and assured for you. Oh, if only this comes to pass! This would be such a benefit that we could only look upon it as a providential blessing. Dounia is dreaming of nothing else. We have even ventured already to drop a few words on the subject to Pyotr Petrovitch. He was cautious in his answer, and said that, of course, as he could not get on without a secretary, it would be better to be paying a salary to a relation than to a stranger, if only the former were fitted for the duties (as though there could be doubt of your being fitted!) but then he expressed doubts whether your studies at the university would leave you time for work at his office. The matter dropped for the time, but Dounia is thinking of nothing else now. She has been in a sort of fever for the last few days, and has already made a regular plan for your becoming in the end an associate and even a partner in Pyotr Petrovitch’s business, which might well be, seeing that you are a student of law. I am in complete agreement with her, Rodya, and share all her plans and hopes, and think there is every probability of realising them. And in spite of Pyotr Petrovitch’s evasiveness, very natural at present (since he does not know you), Dounia is firmly persuaded that she will gain everything by her good influence over her future husband; this she is reckoning upon. Of course we are careful not to talk of any of these more remote plans to Pyotr Petrovitch, especially of your becoming his partner. He is a practical man and might take this very coldly, it might all seem to him simply a day-dream. Nor has either Dounia or I breathed a word to him of the great hopes we have of his helping us to pay for your university studies; we have not spoken of it in the first place, because it will come to pass of itself, later on, and he will no doubt without wasting words offer to do it of himself, (as though he could refuse Dounia that) the more readily since you may by your own efforts become his right hand in the office, and receive this assistance not as a charity, but as a salary earned by your own work. Dounia wants to arrange it all like this and I quite agree with her. And we have not spoken of our plans for another reason, that is, because I particularly wanted you to feel on an equal footing when you first meet him. When Dounia spoke to him with enthusiasm about you, he answered that one could never judge of a man without seeing him close, for oneself, and that he looked forward to forming his own opinion when he makes your acquaintance. Do you know, my precious Rodya, I think that perhaps for some reasons (nothing to do with Pyotr Petrovitch though, simply for my own personal, perhaps old-womanish, fancies) I should do better to go on living by myself, apart, than with them, after the wedding. I am convinced that he will be generous and delicate enough to invite me and to urge me to remain with my daughter for the future, and if he has said nothing about it hitherto, it is simply because it has been taken for granted; but I shall refuse. I have noticed more than once in my life that husbands don’t quite get on with their mothers-in-law, and I don’t want to be the least bit in anyone’s way, and for my own sake, too, would rather be quite independent, so long as I have a crust of bread of my own, and such children as you and Dounia. If possible, I would settle somewhere near you, for the most joyful piece of news, dear Rodya, I have kept for the end of my letter: know then, my dear boy, that we may, perhaps, be all together in a very short time and may embrace one another again after a separation of almost three years! It is settled _for certain_ that Dounia and I are to set off for Petersburg, exactly when I don’t know, but very, very soon, possibly in a week. It all depends on Pyotr Petrovitch who will let us know when he has had time to look round him in Petersburg. To suit his own arrangements he is anxious to have the ceremony as soon as possible, even before the fast of Our Lady, if it could be managed, or if that is too soon to be ready, immediately after. Oh, with what happiness I shall press you to my heart! Dounia is all excitement at the joyful thought of seeing you, she said one day in joke that she would be ready to marry Pyotr Petrovitch for that alone. She is an angel! She is not writing anything to you now, and has only told me to write that she has so much, so much to tell you that she is not going to take up her pen now, for a few lines would tell you nothing, and it would only mean upsetting herself; she bids me send you her love and innumerable kisses. But although we shall be meeting so soon, perhaps I shall send you as much money as I can in a day or two. Now that everyone has heard that Dounia is to marry Pyotr Petrovitch, my credit has suddenly improved and I know that Afanasy Ivanovitch will trust me now even to seventy-five roubles on the security of my pension, so that perhaps I shall be able to send you twenty-five or even thirty roubles. I would send you more, but I am uneasy about our travelling expenses; for though Pyotr Petrovitch has been so kind as to undertake part of the expenses of the journey, that is to say, he has taken upon himself the conveyance of our bags and big trunk (which will be conveyed through some acquaintances of his), we must reckon upon some expense on our arrival in Petersburg, where we can’t be left without a halfpenny, at least for the first few days. But we have calculated it all, Dounia and I, to the last penny, and we see that the journey will not cost very much. It is only ninety versts from us to the railway and we have come to an agreement with a driver we know, so as to be in readiness; and from there Dounia and I can travel quite comfortably third class. So that I may very likely be able to send to you not twenty-five, but thirty roubles. But enough; I have covered two sheets already and there is no space left for more; our whole history, but so many events have happened! And now, my precious Rodya, I embrace you and send you a mother’s blessing till we meet. Love Dounia your sister, Rodya; love her as she loves you and understand that she loves you beyond everything, more than herself. She is an angel and you, Rodya, you are everything to us--our one hope, our one consolation. If only you are happy, we shall be happy. Do you still say your prayers, Rodya, and believe in the mercy of our Creator and our Redeemer? I am afraid in my heart that you may have been visited by the new spirit of infidelity that is abroad to-day; If it is so, I pray for you. Remember, dear boy, how in your childhood, when your father was living, you used to lisp your prayers at my knee, and how happy we all were in those days. Good-bye, till we meet then--I embrace you warmly, warmly, with many kisses.\n\n“Yours till death,\n\nAlmost from the first, while he read the letter, Raskolnikov’s face was wet with tears; but when he finished it, his face was pale and distorted and a bitter, wrathful and malignant smile was on his lips. He laid his head down on his threadbare dirty pillow and pondered, pondered a long time. His heart was beating violently, and his brain was in a turmoil. At last he felt cramped and stifled in the little yellow room that was like a cupboard or a box. His eyes and his mind craved for space. He took up his hat and went out, this time without dread of meeting anyone; he had forgotten his dread. He turned in the direction of the Vassilyevsky Ostrov, walking along Vassilyevsky Prospect, as though hastening on some business, but he walked, as his habit was, without noticing his way, muttering and even speaking aloud to himself, to the astonishment of the passers-by. Many of them took him to be drunk.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nHis mother’s letter had been a torture to him, but as regards the chief fact in it, he had felt not one moment’s hesitation, even whilst he was reading the letter. The essential question was settled, and irrevocably settled, in his mind: “Never such a marriage while I am alive and Mr. Luzhin be damned!” “The thing is perfectly clear,” he muttered to himself, with a malignant smile anticipating the triumph of his decision. “No, mother, no, Dounia, you won’t deceive me! and then they apologise for not asking my advice and for taking the decision without me! I dare say! They imagine it is arranged now and can’t be broken off; but we will see whether it can or not! A magnificent excuse: ‘Pyotr Petrovitch is such a busy man that even his wedding has to be in post-haste, almost by express.’ No, Dounia, I see it all and I know what you want to say to me; and I know too what you were thinking about, when you walked up and down all night, and what your prayers were like before the Holy Mother of Kazan who stands in mother’s bedroom. Bitter is the ascent to Golgotha.... Hm... so it is finally settled; you have determined to marry a sensible business man, Avdotya Romanovna, one who has a fortune (has _already_ made his fortune, that is so much more solid and impressive), a man who holds two government posts and who shares the ideas of our most rising generation, as mother writes, and who _seems_ to be kind, as Dounia herself observes. That _seems_ beats everything! And that very Dounia for that very ‘_seems_’ is marrying him! Splendid! splendid!\n\n“... But I should like to know why mother has written to me about ‘our most rising generation’? Simply as a descriptive touch, or with the idea of prepossessing me in favour of Mr. Luzhin? Oh, the cunning of them! I should like to know one thing more: how far they were open with one another that day and night and all this time since? Was it all put into _words_, or did both understand that they had the same thing at heart and in their minds, so that there was no need to speak of it aloud, and better not to speak of it. Most likely it was partly like that, from mother’s letter it’s evident: he struck her as rude _a little_, and mother in her simplicity took her observations to Dounia. And she was sure to be vexed and ‘answered her angrily.’ I should think so! Who would not be angered when it was quite clear without any naïve questions and when it was understood that it was useless to discuss it. And why does she write to me, ‘love Dounia, Rodya, and she loves you more than herself’? Has she a secret conscience-prick at sacrificing her daughter to her son? ‘You are our one comfort, you are everything to us.’ Oh, mother!”\n\nHis bitterness grew more and more intense, and if he had happened to meet Mr. Luzhin at the moment, he might have murdered him.\n\n“Hm... yes, that’s true,” he continued, pursuing the whirling ideas that chased each other in his brain, “it is true that ‘it needs time and care to get to know a man,’ but there is no mistake about Mr. Luzhin. The chief thing is he is ‘a man of business and _seems_ kind,’ that was something, wasn’t it, to send the bags and big box for them! A kind man, no doubt after that! But his _bride_ and her mother are to drive in a peasant’s cart covered with sacking (I know, I have been driven in it). No matter! It is only ninety versts and then they can ‘travel very comfortably, third class,’ for a thousand versts! Quite right, too. One must cut one’s coat according to one’s cloth, but what about you, Mr. Luzhin? She is your bride.... And you must be aware that her mother has to raise money on her pension for the journey. To be sure it’s a matter of business, a partnership for mutual benefit, with equal shares and expenses;--food and drink provided, but pay for your tobacco. The business man has got the better of them, too. The luggage will cost less than their fares and very likely go for nothing. How is it that they don’t both see all that, or is it that they don’t want to see? And they are pleased, pleased! And to think that this is only the first blossoming, and that the real fruits are to come! But what really matters is not the stinginess, is not the meanness, but the _tone_ of the whole thing. For that will be the tone after marriage, it’s a foretaste of it. And mother too, why should she be so lavish? What will she have by the time she gets to Petersburg? Three silver roubles or two ‘paper ones’ as _she_ says.... that old woman... hm. What does she expect to live upon in Petersburg afterwards? She has her reasons already for guessing that she _could not_ live with Dounia after the marriage, even for the first few months. The good man has no doubt let slip something on that subject also, though mother would deny it: ‘I shall refuse,’ says she. On whom is she reckoning then? Is she counting on what is left of her hundred and twenty roubles of pension when Afanasy Ivanovitch’s debt is paid? She knits woollen shawls and embroiders cuffs, ruining her old eyes. And all her shawls don’t add more than twenty roubles a year to her hundred and twenty, I know that. So she is building all her hopes all the time on Mr. Luzhin’s generosity; ‘he will offer it of himself, he will press it on me.’ You may wait a long time for that! That’s how it always is with these Schilleresque noble hearts; till the last moment every goose is a swan with them, till the last moment, they hope for the best and will see nothing wrong, and although they have an inkling of the other side of the picture, yet they won’t face the truth till they are forced to; the very thought of it makes them shiver; they thrust the truth away with both hands, until the man they deck out in false colours puts a fool’s cap on them with his own hands. I should like to know whether Mr. Luzhin has any orders of merit; I bet he has the Anna in his buttonhole and that he puts it on when he goes to dine with contractors or merchants. He will be sure to have it for his wedding, too! Enough of him, confound him!\n\n“Well,... mother I don’t wonder at, it’s like her, God bless her, but how could Dounia? Dounia darling, as though I did not know you! You were nearly twenty when I saw you last: I understood you then. Mother writes that ‘Dounia can put up with a great deal.’ I know that very well. I knew that two years and a half ago, and for the last two and a half years I have been thinking about it, thinking of just that, that ‘Dounia can put up with a great deal.’ If she could put up with Mr. Svidrigaïlov and all the rest of it, she certainly can put up with a great deal. And now mother and she have taken it into their heads that she can put up with Mr. Luzhin, who propounds the theory of the superiority of wives raised from destitution and owing everything to their husband’s bounty--who propounds it, too, almost at the first interview. Granted that he ‘let it slip,’ though he is a sensible man, (yet maybe it was not a slip at all, but he meant to make himself clear as soon as possible) but Dounia, Dounia? She understands the man, of course, but she will have to live with the man. Why! she’d live on black bread and water, she would not sell her soul, she would not barter her moral freedom for comfort; she would not barter it for all Schleswig-Holstein, much less Mr. Luzhin’s money. No, Dounia was not that sort when I knew her and... she is still the same, of course! Yes, there’s no denying, the Svidrigaïlovs are a bitter pill! It’s a bitter thing to spend one’s life a governess in the provinces for two hundred roubles, but I know she would rather be a nigger on a plantation or a Lett with a German master than degrade her soul, and her moral dignity, by binding herself for ever to a man whom she does not respect and with whom she has nothing in common--for her own advantage. And if Mr. Luzhin had been of unalloyed gold, or one huge diamond, she would never have consented to become his legal concubine. Why is she consenting then? What’s the point of it? What’s the answer? It’s clear enough: for herself, for her comfort, to save her life she would not sell herself, but for someone else she is doing it! For one she loves, for one she adores, she will sell herself! That’s what it all amounts to; for her brother, for her mother, she will sell herself! She will sell everything! In such cases, ‘we overcome our moral feeling if necessary,’ freedom, peace, conscience even, all, all are brought into the market. Let my life go, if only my dear ones may be happy! More than that, we become casuists, we learn to be Jesuitical and for a time maybe we can soothe ourselves, we can persuade ourselves that it is one’s duty for a good object. That’s just like us, it’s as clear as daylight. It’s clear that Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov is the central figure in the business, and no one else. Oh, yes, she can ensure his happiness, keep him in the university, make him a partner in the office, make his whole future secure; perhaps he may even be a rich man later on, prosperous, respected, and may even end his life a famous man! But my mother? It’s all Rodya, precious Rodya, her first born! For such a son who would not sacrifice such a daughter! Oh, loving, over-partial hearts! Why, for his sake we would not shrink even from Sonia’s fate. Sonia, Sonia Marmeladov, the eternal victim so long as the world lasts. Have you taken the measure of your sacrifice, both of you? Is it right? Can you bear it? Is it any use? Is there sense in it? And let me tell you, Dounia, Sonia’s life is no worse than life with Mr. Luzhin. ‘There can be no question of love,’ mother writes. And what if there can be no respect either, if on the contrary there is aversion, contempt, repulsion, what then? So you will have to ‘keep up your appearance,’ too. Is not that so? Do you understand what that smartness means? Do you understand that the Luzhin smartness is just the same thing as Sonia’s and may be worse, viler, baser, because in your case, Dounia, it’s a bargain for luxuries, after all, but with Sonia it’s simply a question of starvation. It has to be paid for, it has to be paid for, Dounia, this smartness. And what if it’s more than you can bear afterwards, if you regret it? The bitterness, the misery, the curses, the tears hidden from all the world, for you are not a Marfa Petrovna. And how will your mother feel then? Even now she is uneasy, she is worried, but then, when she sees it all clearly? And I? Yes, indeed, what have you taken me for? I won’t have your sacrifice, Dounia, I won’t have it, mother! It shall not be, so long as I am alive, it shall not, it shall not! I won’t accept it!”\n\nHe suddenly paused in his reflection and stood still.\n\n“It shall not be? But what are you going to do to prevent it? You’ll forbid it? And what right have you? What can you promise them on your side to give you such a right? Your whole life, your whole future, you will devote to them _when you have finished your studies and obtained a post_? Yes, we have heard all that before, and that’s all _words_, but now? Now something must be done, now, do you understand that? And what are you doing now? You are living upon them. They borrow on their hundred roubles pension. They borrow from the Svidrigaïlovs. How are you going to save them from Svidrigaïlovs, from Afanasy Ivanovitch Vahrushin, oh, future millionaire Zeus who would arrange their lives for them? In another ten years? In another ten years, mother will be blind with knitting shawls, maybe with weeping too. She will be worn to a shadow with fasting; and my sister? Imagine for a moment what may have become of your sister in ten years? What may happen to her during those ten years? Can you fancy?”\n\nSo he tortured himself, fretting himself with such questions, and finding a kind of enjoyment in it. And yet all these questions were not new ones suddenly confronting him, they were old familiar aches. It was long since they had first begun to grip and rend his heart. Long, long ago his present anguish had its first beginnings; it had waxed and gathered strength, it had matured and concentrated, until it had taken the form of a fearful, frenzied and fantastic question, which tortured his heart and mind, clamouring insistently for an answer. Now his mother’s letter had burst on him like a thunderclap. It was clear that he must not now suffer passively, worrying himself over unsolved questions, but that he must do something, do it at once, and do it quickly. Anyway he must decide on something, or else...\n\n“Or throw up life altogether!” he cried suddenly, in a frenzy--“accept one’s lot humbly as it is, once for all and stifle everything in oneself, giving up all claim to activity, life and love!”\n\n“Do you understand, sir, do you understand what it means when you have absolutely nowhere to turn?” Marmeladov’s question came suddenly into his mind, “for every man must have somewhere to turn....”\n\nHe gave a sudden start; another thought, that he had had yesterday, slipped back into his mind. But he did not start at the thought recurring to him, for he knew, he had _felt beforehand_, that it must come back, he was expecting it; besides it was not only yesterday’s thought. The difference was that a month ago, yesterday even, the thought was a mere dream: but now... now it appeared not a dream at all, it had taken a new menacing and quite unfamiliar shape, and he suddenly became aware of this himself.... He felt a hammering in his head, and there was a darkness before his eyes.\n\nHe looked round hurriedly, he was searching for something. He wanted to sit down and was looking for a seat; he was walking along the K---- Boulevard. There was a seat about a hundred paces in front of him. He walked towards it as fast he could; but on the way he met with a little adventure which absorbed all his attention. Looking for the seat, he had noticed a woman walking some twenty paces in front of him, but at first he took no more notice of her than of other objects that crossed his path. It had happened to him many times going home not to notice the road by which he was going, and he was accustomed to walk like that. But there was at first sight something so strange about the woman in front of him, that gradually his attention was riveted upon her, at first reluctantly and, as it were, resentfully, and then more and more intently. He felt a sudden desire to find out what it was that was so strange about the woman. In the first place, she appeared to be a girl quite young, and she was walking in the great heat bareheaded and with no parasol or gloves, waving her arms about in an absurd way. She had on a dress of some light silky material, but put on strangely awry, not properly hooked up, and torn open at the top of the skirt, close to the waist: a great piece was rent and hanging loose. A little kerchief was flung about her bare throat, but lay slanting on one side. The girl was walking unsteadily, too, stumbling and staggering from side to side. She drew Raskolnikov’s whole attention at last. He overtook the girl at the seat, but, on reaching it, she dropped down on it, in the corner; she let her head sink on the back of the seat and closed her eyes, apparently in extreme exhaustion. Looking at her closely, he saw at once that she was completely drunk. It was a strange and shocking sight. He could hardly believe that he was not mistaken. He saw before him the face of a quite young, fair-haired girl--sixteen, perhaps not more than fifteen, years old, pretty little face, but flushed and heavy looking and, as it were, swollen. The girl seemed hardly to know what she was doing; she crossed one leg over the other, lifting it indecorously, and showed every sign of being unconscious that she was in the street.\n\nRaskolnikov did not sit down, but he felt unwilling to leave her, and stood facing her in perplexity. This boulevard was never much frequented; and now, at two o’clock, in the stifling heat, it was quite deserted. And yet on the further side of the boulevard, about fifteen paces away, a gentleman was standing on the edge of the pavement. He, too, would apparently have liked to approach the girl with some object of his own. He, too, had probably seen her in the distance and had followed her, but found Raskolnikov in his way. He looked angrily at him, though he tried to escape his notice, and stood impatiently biding his time, till the unwelcome man in rags should have moved away. His intentions were unmistakable. The gentleman was a plump, thickly-set man, about thirty, fashionably dressed, with a high colour, red lips and moustaches. Raskolnikov felt furious; he had a sudden longing to insult this fat dandy in some way. He left the girl for a moment and walked towards the gentleman.\n\n“Hey! You Svidrigaïlov! What do you want here?” he shouted, clenching his fists and laughing, spluttering with rage.\n\n“What do you mean?” the gentleman asked sternly, scowling in haughty astonishment.\n\n“Get away, that’s what I mean.”\n\n“How dare you, you low fellow!”\n\nHe raised his cane. Raskolnikov rushed at him with his fists, without reflecting that the stout gentleman was a match for two men like himself. But at that instant someone seized him from behind, and a police constable stood between them.\n\n“That’s enough, gentlemen, no fighting, please, in a public place. What do you want? Who are you?” he asked Raskolnikov sternly, noticing his rags.\n\nRaskolnikov looked at him intently. He had a straight-forward, sensible, soldierly face, with grey moustaches and whiskers.\n\n“You are just the man I want,” Raskolnikov cried, catching at his arm. “I am a student, Raskolnikov.... You may as well know that too,” he added, addressing the gentleman, “come along, I have something to show you.”\n\nAnd taking the policeman by the hand he drew him towards the seat.\n\n“Look here, hopelessly drunk, and she has just come down the boulevard. There is no telling who and what she is, she does not look like a professional. It’s more likely she has been given drink and deceived somewhere... for the first time... you understand? and they’ve put her out into the street like that. Look at the way her dress is torn, and the way it has been put on: she has been dressed by somebody, she has not dressed herself, and dressed by unpractised hands, by a man’s hands; that’s evident. And now look there: I don’t know that dandy with whom I was going to fight, I see him for the first time, but he, too, has seen her on the road, just now, drunk, not knowing what she is doing, and now he is very eager to get hold of her, to get her away somewhere while she is in this state... that’s certain, believe me, I am not wrong. I saw him myself watching her and following her, but I prevented him, and he is just waiting for me to go away. Now he has walked away a little, and is standing still, pretending to make a cigarette.... Think how can we keep her out of his hands, and how are we to get her home?”\n\nThe policeman saw it all in a flash. The stout gentleman was easy to understand, he turned to consider the girl. The policeman bent over to examine her more closely, and his face worked with genuine compassion.\n\n“Ah, what a pity!” he said, shaking his head--“why, she is quite a child! She has been deceived, you can see that at once. Listen, lady,” he began addressing her, “where do you live?” The girl opened her weary and sleepy-looking eyes, gazed blankly at the speaker and waved her hand.\n\n“Here,” said Raskolnikov feeling in his pocket and finding twenty copecks, “here, call a cab and tell him to drive her to her address. The only thing is to find out her address!”\n\n“Missy, missy!” the policeman began again, taking the money. “I’ll fetch you a cab and take you home myself. Where shall I take you, eh? Where do you live?”\n\n“Go away! They won’t let me alone,” the girl muttered, and once more waved her hand.\n\n“Ach, ach, how shocking! It’s shameful, missy, it’s a shame!” He shook his head again, shocked, sympathetic and indignant.\n\n“It’s a difficult job,” the policeman said to Raskolnikov, and as he did so, he looked him up and down in a rapid glance. He, too, must have seemed a strange figure to him: dressed in rags and handing him money!\n\n“Did you meet her far from here?” he asked him.\n\n“I tell you she was walking in front of me, staggering, just here, in the boulevard. She only just reached the seat and sank down on it.”\n\n“Ah, the shameful things that are done in the world nowadays, God have mercy on us! An innocent creature like that, drunk already! She has been deceived, that’s a sure thing. See how her dress has been torn too.... Ah, the vice one sees nowadays! And as likely as not she belongs to gentlefolk too, poor ones maybe.... There are many like that nowadays. She looks refined, too, as though she were a lady,” and he bent over her once more.\n\nPerhaps he had daughters growing up like that, “looking like ladies and refined” with pretensions to gentility and smartness....\n\n“The chief thing is,” Raskolnikov persisted, “to keep her out of this scoundrel’s hands! Why should he outrage her? It’s as clear as day what he is after; ah, the brute, he is not moving off!”\n\nRaskolnikov spoke aloud and pointed to him. The gentleman heard him, and seemed about to fly into a rage again, but thought better of it, and confined himself to a contemptuous look. He then walked slowly another ten paces away and again halted.\n\n“Keep her out of his hands we can,” said the constable thoughtfully, “if only she’d tell us where to take her, but as it is.... Missy, hey, missy!” he bent over her once more.\n\nShe opened her eyes fully all of a sudden, looked at him intently, as though realising something, got up from the seat and walked away in the direction from which she had come. “Oh shameful wretches, they won’t let me alone!” she said, waving her hand again. She walked quickly, though staggering as before. The dandy followed her, but along another avenue, keeping his eye on her.\n\n“Don’t be anxious, I won’t let him have her,” the policeman said resolutely, and he set off after them.\n\n“Ah, the vice one sees nowadays!” he repeated aloud, sighing.\n\nAt that moment something seemed to sting Raskolnikov; in an instant a complete revulsion of feeling came over him.\n\n“Hey, here!” he shouted after the policeman.\n\nThe latter turned round.\n\n“Let them be! What is it to do with you? Let her go! Let him amuse himself.” He pointed at the dandy, “What is it to do with you?”\n\nThe policeman was bewildered, and stared at him open-eyed. Raskolnikov laughed.\n\n“Well!” ejaculated the policeman, with a gesture of contempt, and he walked after the dandy and the girl, probably taking Raskolnikov for a madman or something even worse.\n\n“He has carried off my twenty copecks,” Raskolnikov murmured angrily when he was left alone. “Well, let him take as much from the other fellow to allow him to have the girl and so let it end. And why did I want to interfere? Is it for me to help? Have I any right to help? Let them devour each other alive--what is it to me? How did I dare to give him twenty copecks? Were they mine?”\n\nIn spite of those strange words he felt very wretched. He sat down on the deserted seat. His thoughts strayed aimlessly.... He found it hard to fix his mind on anything at that moment. He longed to forget himself altogether, to forget everything, and then to wake up and begin life anew....\n\n“Poor girl!” he said, looking at the empty corner where she had sat--“She will come to herself and weep, and then her mother will find out.... She will give her a beating, a horrible, shameful beating and then maybe, turn her out of doors.... And even if she does not, the Darya Frantsovnas will get wind of it, and the girl will soon be slipping out on the sly here and there. Then there will be the hospital directly (that’s always the luck of those girls with respectable mothers, who go wrong on the sly) and then... again the hospital... drink... the taverns... and more hospital, in two or three years--a wreck, and her life over at eighteen or nineteen.... Have not I seen cases like that? And how have they been brought to it? Why, they’ve all come to it like that. Ugh! But what does it matter? That’s as it should be, they tell us. A certain percentage, they tell us, must every year go... that way... to the devil, I suppose, so that the rest may remain chaste, and not be interfered with. A percentage! What splendid words they have; they are so scientific, so consolatory.... Once you’ve said ‘percentage’ there’s nothing more to worry about. If we had any other word... maybe we might feel more uneasy.... But what if Dounia were one of the percentage! Of another one if not that one?\n\n“But where am I going?” he thought suddenly. “Strange, I came out for something. As soon as I had read the letter I came out.... I was going to Vassilyevsky Ostrov, to Razumihin. That’s what it was... now I remember. What for, though? And what put the idea of going to Razumihin into my head just now? That’s curious.”\n\nHe wondered at himself. Razumihin was one of his old comrades at the university. It was remarkable that Raskolnikov had hardly any friends at the university; he kept aloof from everyone, went to see no one, and did not welcome anyone who came to see him, and indeed everyone soon gave him up. He took no part in the students’ gatherings, amusements or conversations. He worked with great intensity without sparing himself, and he was respected for this, but no one liked him. He was very poor, and there was a sort of haughty pride and reserve about him, as though he were keeping something to himself. He seemed to some of his comrades to look down upon them all as children, as though he were superior in development, knowledge and convictions, as though their beliefs and interests were beneath him.\n\nWith Razumihin he had got on, or, at least, he was more unreserved and communicative with him. Indeed it was impossible to be on any other terms with Razumihin. He was an exceptionally good-humoured and candid youth, good-natured to the point of simplicity, though both depth and dignity lay concealed under that simplicity. The better of his comrades understood this, and all were fond of him. He was extremely intelligent, though he was certainly rather a simpleton at times. He was of striking appearance--tall, thin, black-haired and always badly shaved. He was sometimes uproarious and was reputed to be of great physical strength. One night, when out in a festive company, he had with one blow laid a gigantic policeman on his back. There was no limit to his drinking powers, but he could abstain from drink altogether; he sometimes went too far in his pranks; but he could do without pranks altogether. Another thing striking about Razumihin, no failure distressed him, and it seemed as though no unfavourable circumstances could crush him. He could lodge anywhere, and bear the extremes of cold and hunger. He was very poor, and kept himself entirely on what he could earn by work of one sort or another. He knew of no end of resources by which to earn money. He spent one whole winter without lighting his stove, and used to declare that he liked it better, because one slept more soundly in the cold. For the present he, too, had been obliged to give up the university, but it was only for a time, and he was working with all his might to save enough to return to his studies again. Raskolnikov had not been to see him for the last four months, and Razumihin did not even know his address. About two months before, they had met in the street, but Raskolnikov had turned away and even crossed to the other side that he might not be observed. And though Razumihin noticed him, he passed him by, as he did not want to annoy him.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n“Of course, I’ve been meaning lately to go to Razumihin’s to ask for work, to ask him to get me lessons or something...” Raskolnikov thought, “but what help can he be to me now? Suppose he gets me lessons, suppose he shares his last farthing with me, if he has any farthings, so that I could get some boots and make myself tidy enough to give lessons... hm... Well and what then? What shall I do with the few coppers I earn? That’s not what I want now. It’s really absurd for me to go to Razumihin....”\n\nThe question why he was now going to Razumihin agitated him even more than he was himself aware; he kept uneasily seeking for some sinister significance in this apparently ordinary action.\n\n“Could I have expected to set it all straight and to find a way out by means of Razumihin alone?” he asked himself in perplexity.\n\nHe pondered and rubbed his forehead, and, strange to say, after long musing, suddenly, as if it were spontaneously and by chance, a fantastic thought came into his head.\n\n“Hm... to Razumihin’s,” he said all at once, calmly, as though he had reached a final determination. “I shall go to Razumihin’s of course, but... not now. I shall go to him... on the next day after It, when It will be over and everything will begin afresh....”\n\nAnd suddenly he realised what he was thinking.\n\n“After It,” he shouted, jumping up from the seat, “but is It really going to happen? Is it possible it really will happen?” He left the seat, and went off almost at a run; he meant to turn back, homewards, but the thought of going home suddenly filled him with intense loathing; in that hole, in that awful little cupboard of his, all _this_ had for a month past been growing up in him; and he walked on at random.\n\nHis nervous shudder had passed into a fever that made him feel shivering; in spite of the heat he felt cold. With a kind of effort he began almost unconsciously, from some inner craving, to stare at all the objects before him, as though looking for something to distract his attention; but he did not succeed, and kept dropping every moment into brooding. When with a start he lifted his head again and looked round, he forgot at once what he had just been thinking about and even where he was going. In this way he walked right across Vassilyevsky Ostrov, came out on to the Lesser Neva, crossed the bridge and turned towards the islands. The greenness and freshness were at first restful to his weary eyes after the dust of the town and the huge houses that hemmed him in and weighed upon him. Here there were no taverns, no stifling closeness, no stench. But soon these new pleasant sensations passed into morbid irritability. Sometimes he stood still before a brightly painted summer villa standing among green foliage, he gazed through the fence, he saw in the distance smartly dressed women on the verandahs and balconies, and children running in the gardens. The flowers especially caught his attention; he gazed at them longer than at anything. He was met, too, by luxurious carriages and by men and women on horseback; he watched them with curious eyes and forgot about them before they had vanished from his sight. Once he stood still and counted his money; he found he had thirty copecks. “Twenty to the policeman, three to Nastasya for the letter, so I must have given forty-seven or fifty to the Marmeladovs yesterday,” he thought, reckoning it up for some unknown reason, but he soon forgot with what object he had taken the money out of his pocket. He recalled it on passing an eating-house or tavern, and felt that he was hungry.... Going into the tavern he drank a glass of vodka and ate a pie of some sort. He finished eating it as he walked away. It was a long while since he had taken vodka and it had an effect upon him at once, though he only drank a wineglassful. His legs felt suddenly heavy and a great drowsiness came upon him. He turned homewards, but reaching Petrovsky Ostrov he stopped completely exhausted, turned off the road into the bushes, sank down upon the grass and instantly fell asleep.\n\nIn a morbid condition of the brain, dreams often have a singular actuality, vividness, and extraordinary semblance of reality. At times monstrous images are created, but the setting and the whole picture are so truth-like and filled with details so delicate, so unexpectedly, but so artistically consistent, that the dreamer, were he an artist like Pushkin or Turgenev even, could never have invented them in the waking state. Such sick dreams always remain long in the memory and make a powerful impression on the overwrought and deranged nervous system.\n\nRaskolnikov had a fearful dream. He dreamt he was back in his childhood in the little town of his birth. He was a child about seven years old, walking into the country with his father on the evening of a holiday. It was a grey and heavy day, the country was exactly as he remembered it; indeed he recalled it far more vividly in his dream than he had done in memory. The little town stood on a level flat as bare as the hand, not even a willow near it; only in the far distance, a copse lay, a dark blur on the very edge of the horizon. A few paces beyond the last market garden stood a tavern, a big tavern, which had always aroused in him a feeling of aversion, even of fear, when he walked by it with his father. There was always a crowd there, always shouting, laughter and abuse, hideous hoarse singing and often fighting. Drunken and horrible-looking figures were hanging about the tavern. He used to cling close to his father, trembling all over when he met them. Near the tavern the road became a dusty track, the dust of which was always black. It was a winding road, and about a hundred paces further on, it turned to the right to the graveyard. In the middle of the graveyard stood a stone church with a green cupola where he used to go to mass two or three times a year with his father and mother, when a service was held in memory of his grandmother, who had long been dead, and whom he had never seen. On these occasions they used to take on a white dish tied up in a table napkin a special sort of rice pudding with raisins stuck in it in the shape of a cross. He loved that church, the old-fashioned, unadorned ikons and the old priest with the shaking head. Near his grandmother’s grave, which was marked by a stone, was the little grave of his younger brother who had died at six months old. He did not remember him at all, but he had been told about his little brother, and whenever he visited the graveyard he used religiously and reverently to cross himself and to bow down and kiss the little grave. And now he dreamt that he was walking with his father past the tavern on the way to the graveyard; he was holding his father’s hand and looking with dread at the tavern. A peculiar circumstance attracted his attention: there seemed to be some kind of festivity going on, there were crowds of gaily dressed townspeople, peasant women, their husbands, and riff-raff of all sorts, all singing and all more or less drunk. Near the entrance of the tavern stood a cart, but a strange cart. It was one of those big carts usually drawn by heavy cart-horses and laden with casks of wine or other heavy goods. He always liked looking at those great cart-horses, with their long manes, thick legs, and slow even pace, drawing along a perfect mountain with no appearance of effort, as though it were easier going with a load than without it. But now, strange to say, in the shafts of such a cart he saw a thin little sorrel beast, one of those peasants’ nags which he had often seen straining their utmost under a heavy load of wood or hay, especially when the wheels were stuck in the mud or in a rut. And the peasants would beat them so cruelly, sometimes even about the nose and eyes, and he felt so sorry, so sorry for them that he almost cried, and his mother always used to take him away from the window. All of a sudden there was a great uproar of shouting, singing and the balalaïka, and from the tavern a number of big and very drunken peasants came out, wearing red and blue shirts and coats thrown over their shoulders.\n\n“Get in, get in!” shouted one of them, a young thick-necked peasant with a fleshy face red as a carrot. “I’ll take you all, get in!”\n\nBut at once there was an outbreak of laughter and exclamations in the crowd.\n\n“Take us all with a beast like that!”\n\n“Why, Mikolka, are you crazy to put a nag like that in such a cart?”\n\n“And this mare is twenty if she is a day, mates!”\n\n“Get in, I’ll take you all,” Mikolka shouted again, leaping first into the cart, seizing the reins and standing straight up in front. “The bay has gone with Matvey,” he shouted from the cart--“and this brute, mates, is just breaking my heart, I feel as if I could kill her. She’s just eating her head off. Get in, I tell you! I’ll make her gallop! She’ll gallop!” and he picked up the whip, preparing himself with relish to flog the little mare.\n\n“Get in! Come along!” The crowd laughed. “D’you hear, she’ll gallop!”\n\n“Gallop indeed! She has not had a gallop in her for the last ten years!”\n\n“She’ll jog along!”\n\n“Don’t you mind her, mates, bring a whip each of you, get ready!”\n\n“All right! Give it to her!”\n\nThey all clambered into Mikolka’s cart, laughing and making jokes. Six men got in and there was still room for more. They hauled in a fat, rosy-cheeked woman. She was dressed in red cotton, in a pointed, beaded headdress and thick leather shoes; she was cracking nuts and laughing. The crowd round them was laughing too and indeed, how could they help laughing? That wretched nag was to drag all the cartload of them at a gallop! Two young fellows in the cart were just getting whips ready to help Mikolka. With the cry of “now,” the mare tugged with all her might, but far from galloping, could scarcely move forward; she struggled with her legs, gasping and shrinking from the blows of the three whips which were showered upon her like hail. The laughter in the cart and in the crowd was redoubled, but Mikolka flew into a rage and furiously thrashed the mare, as though he supposed she really could gallop.\n\n“Let me get in, too, mates,” shouted a young man in the crowd whose appetite was aroused.\n\n“Get in, all get in,” cried Mikolka, “she will draw you all. I’ll beat her to death!” And he thrashed and thrashed at the mare, beside himself with fury.\n\n“Father, father,” he cried, “father, what are they doing? Father, they are beating the poor horse!”\n\n“Come along, come along!” said his father. “They are drunken and foolish, they are in fun; come away, don’t look!” and he tried to draw him away, but he tore himself away from his hand, and, beside himself with horror, ran to the horse. The poor beast was in a bad way. She was gasping, standing still, then tugging again and almost falling.\n\n“Beat her to death,” cried Mikolka, “it’s come to that. I’ll do for her!”\n\n“What are you about, are you a Christian, you devil?” shouted an old man in the crowd.\n\n“Did anyone ever see the like? A wretched nag like that pulling such a cartload,” said another.\n\n“You’ll kill her,” shouted the third.\n\n“Don’t meddle! It’s my property, I’ll do what I choose. Get in, more of you! Get in, all of you! I will have her go at a gallop!...”\n\nAll at once laughter broke into a roar and covered everything: the mare, roused by the shower of blows, began feebly kicking. Even the old man could not help smiling. To think of a wretched little beast like that trying to kick!\n\nTwo lads in the crowd snatched up whips and ran to the mare to beat her about the ribs. One ran each side.\n\n“Hit her in the face, in the eyes, in the eyes,” cried Mikolka.\n\n“Give us a song, mates,” shouted someone in the cart and everyone in the cart joined in a riotous song, jingling a tambourine and whistling. The woman went on cracking nuts and laughing.\n\n... He ran beside the mare, ran in front of her, saw her being whipped across the eyes, right in the eyes! He was crying, he felt choking, his tears were streaming. One of the men gave him a cut with the whip across the face, he did not feel it. Wringing his hands and screaming, he rushed up to the grey-headed old man with the grey beard, who was shaking his head in disapproval. One woman seized him by the hand and would have taken him away, but he tore himself from her and ran back to the mare. She was almost at the last gasp, but began kicking once more.\n\n“I’ll teach you to kick,” Mikolka shouted ferociously. He threw down the whip, bent forward and picked up from the bottom of the cart a long, thick shaft, he took hold of one end with both hands and with an effort brandished it over the mare.\n\n“He’ll crush her,” was shouted round him. “He’ll kill her!”\n\n“It’s my property,” shouted Mikolka and brought the shaft down with a swinging blow. There was a sound of a heavy thud.\n\n“Thrash her, thrash her! Why have you stopped?” shouted voices in the crowd.\n\nAnd Mikolka swung the shaft a second time and it fell a second time on the spine of the luckless mare. She sank back on her haunches, but lurched forward and tugged forward with all her force, tugged first on one side and then on the other, trying to move the cart. But the six whips were attacking her in all directions, and the shaft was raised again and fell upon her a third time, then a fourth, with heavy measured blows. Mikolka was in a fury that he could not kill her at one blow.\n\n“She’s a tough one,” was shouted in the crowd.\n\n“She’ll fall in a minute, mates, there will soon be an end of her,” said an admiring spectator in the crowd.\n\n“Fetch an axe to her! Finish her off,” shouted a third.\n\n“I’ll show you! Stand off,” Mikolka screamed frantically; he threw down the shaft, stooped down in the cart and picked up an iron crowbar. “Look out,” he shouted, and with all his might he dealt a stunning blow at the poor mare. The blow fell; the mare staggered, sank back, tried to pull, but the bar fell again with a swinging blow on her back and she fell on the ground like a log.\n\n“Finish her off,” shouted Mikolka and he leapt beside himself, out of the cart. Several young men, also flushed with drink, seized anything they could come across--whips, sticks, poles, and ran to the dying mare. Mikolka stood on one side and began dealing random blows with the crowbar. The mare stretched out her head, drew a long breath and died.\n\n“You butchered her,” someone shouted in the crowd.\n\n“Why wouldn’t she gallop then?”\n\n“My property!” shouted Mikolka, with bloodshot eyes, brandishing the bar in his hands. He stood as though regretting that he had nothing more to beat.\n\n“No mistake about it, you are not a Christian,” many voices were shouting in the crowd.\n\nBut the poor boy, beside himself, made his way, screaming, through the crowd to the sorrel nag, put his arms round her bleeding dead head and kissed it, kissed the eyes and kissed the lips.... Then he jumped up and flew in a frenzy with his little fists out at Mikolka. At that instant his father, who had been running after him, snatched him up and carried him out of the crowd.\n\n“Come along, come! Let us go home,” he said to him.\n\n“Father! Why did they... kill... the poor horse!” he sobbed, but his voice broke and the words came in shrieks from his panting chest.\n\n“They are drunk.... They are brutal... it’s not our business!” said his father. He put his arms round his father but he felt choked, choked. He tried to draw a breath, to cry out--and woke up.\n\nHe waked up, gasping for breath, his hair soaked with perspiration, and stood up in terror.\n\n“Thank God, that was only a dream,” he said, sitting down under a tree and drawing deep breaths. “But what is it? Is it some fever coming on? Such a hideous dream!”\n\nHe felt utterly broken: darkness and confusion were in his soul. He rested his elbows on his knees and leaned his head on his hands.\n\n“Good God!” he cried, “can it be, can it be, that I shall really take an axe, that I shall strike her on the head, split her skull open... that I shall tread in the sticky warm blood, break the lock, steal and tremble; hide, all spattered in the blood... with the axe.... Good God, can it be?”\n\nHe was shaking like a leaf as he said this.\n\n“But why am I going on like this?” he continued, sitting up again, as it were in profound amazement. “I knew that I could never bring myself to it, so what have I been torturing myself for till now? Yesterday, yesterday, when I went to make that... _experiment_, yesterday I realised completely that I could never bear to do it.... Why am I going over it again, then? Why am I hesitating? As I came down the stairs yesterday, I said myself that it was base, loathsome, vile, vile... the very thought of it made me feel sick and filled me with horror.\n\n“No, I couldn’t do it, I couldn’t do it! Granted, granted that there is no flaw in all that reasoning, that all that I have concluded this last month is clear as day, true as arithmetic.... My God! Anyway I couldn’t bring myself to it! I couldn’t do it, I couldn’t do it! Why, why then am I still...?”\n\nHe rose to his feet, looked round in wonder as though surprised at finding himself in this place, and went towards the bridge. He was pale, his eyes glowed, he was exhausted in every limb, but he seemed suddenly to breathe more easily. He felt he had cast off that fearful burden that had so long been weighing upon him, and all at once there was a sense of relief and peace in his soul. “Lord,” he prayed, “show me my path--I renounce that accursed... dream of mine.”\n\nCrossing the bridge, he gazed quietly and calmly at the Neva, at the glowing red sun setting in the glowing sky. In spite of his weakness he was not conscious of fatigue. It was as though an abscess that had been forming for a month past in his heart had suddenly broken. Freedom, freedom! He was free from that spell, that sorcery, that obsession!\n\nLater on, when he recalled that time and all that happened to him during those days, minute by minute, point by point, he was superstitiously impressed by one circumstance, which, though in itself not very exceptional, always seemed to him afterwards the predestined turning-point of his fate. He could never understand and explain to himself why, when he was tired and worn out, when it would have been more convenient for him to go home by the shortest and most direct way, he had returned by the Hay Market where he had no need to go. It was obviously and quite unnecessarily out of his way, though not much so. It is true that it happened to him dozens of times to return home without noticing what streets he passed through. But why, he was always asking himself, why had such an important, such a decisive and at the same time such an absolutely chance meeting happened in the Hay Market (where he had moreover no reason to go) at the very hour, the very minute of his life when he was just in the very mood and in the very circumstances in which that meeting was able to exert the gravest and most decisive influence on his whole destiny? As though it had been lying in wait for him on purpose!\n\nIt was about nine o’clock when he crossed the Hay Market. At the tables and the barrows, at the booths and the shops, all the market people were closing their establishments or clearing away and packing up their wares and, like their customers, were going home. Rag pickers and costermongers of all kinds were crowding round the taverns in the dirty and stinking courtyards of the Hay Market. Raskolnikov particularly liked this place and the neighbouring alleys, when he wandered aimlessly in the streets. Here his rags did not attract contemptuous attention, and one could walk about in any attire without scandalising people. At the corner of an alley a huckster and his wife had two tables set out with tapes, thread, cotton handkerchiefs, etc. They, too, had got up to go home, but were lingering in conversation with a friend, who had just come up to them. This friend was Lizaveta Ivanovna, or, as everyone called her, Lizaveta, the younger sister of the old pawnbroker, Alyona Ivanovna, whom Raskolnikov had visited the previous day to pawn his watch and make his _experiment_.... He already knew all about Lizaveta and she knew him a little too. She was a single woman of about thirty-five, tall, clumsy, timid, submissive and almost idiotic. She was a complete slave and went in fear and trembling of her sister, who made her work day and night, and even beat her. She was standing with a bundle before the huckster and his wife, listening earnestly and doubtfully. They were talking of something with special warmth. The moment Raskolnikov caught sight of her, he was overcome by a strange sensation as it were of intense astonishment, though there was nothing astonishing about this meeting.\n\n“You could make up your mind for yourself, Lizaveta Ivanovna,” the huckster was saying aloud. “Come round to-morrow about seven. They will be here too.”\n\n“To-morrow?” said Lizaveta slowly and thoughtfully, as though unable to make up her mind.\n\n“Upon my word, what a fright you are in of Alyona Ivanovna,” gabbled the huckster’s wife, a lively little woman. “I look at you, you are like some little babe. And she is not your own sister either--nothing but a step-sister and what a hand she keeps over you!”\n\n“But this time don’t say a word to Alyona Ivanovna,” her husband interrupted; “that’s my advice, but come round to us without asking. It will be worth your while. Later on your sister herself may have a notion.”\n\n“Am I to come?”\n\n“About seven o’clock to-morrow. And they will be here. You will be able to decide for yourself.”\n\n“And we’ll have a cup of tea,” added his wife.\n\n“All right, I’ll come,” said Lizaveta, still pondering, and she began slowly moving away.\n\nRaskolnikov had just passed and heard no more. He passed softly, unnoticed, trying not to miss a word. His first amazement was followed by a thrill of horror, like a shiver running down his spine. He had learnt, he had suddenly quite unexpectedly learnt, that the next day at seven o’clock Lizaveta, the old woman’s sister and only companion, would be away from home and that therefore at seven o’clock precisely the old woman _would be left alone_.\n\nHe was only a few steps from his lodging. He went in like a man condemned to death. He thought of nothing and was incapable of thinking; but he felt suddenly in his whole being that he had no more freedom of thought, no will, and that everything was suddenly and irrevocably decided.\n\nCertainly, if he had to wait whole years for a suitable opportunity, he could not reckon on a more certain step towards the success of the plan than that which had just presented itself. In any case, it would have been difficult to find out beforehand and with certainty, with greater exactness and less risk, and without dangerous inquiries and investigations, that next day at a certain time an old woman, on whose life an attempt was contemplated, would be at home and entirely alone.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nLater on Raskolnikov happened to find out why the huckster and his wife had invited Lizaveta. It was a very ordinary matter and there was nothing exceptional about it. A family who had come to the town and been reduced to poverty were selling their household goods and clothes, all women’s things. As the things would have fetched little in the market, they were looking for a dealer. This was Lizaveta’s business. She undertook such jobs and was frequently employed, as she was very honest and always fixed a fair price and stuck to it. She spoke as a rule little and, as we have said already, she was very submissive and timid.\n\nBut Raskolnikov had become superstitious of late. The traces of superstition remained in him long after, and were almost ineradicable. And in all this he was always afterwards disposed to see something strange and mysterious, as it were, the presence of some peculiar influences and coincidences. In the previous winter a student he knew called Pokorev, who had left for Harkov, had chanced in conversation to give him the address of Alyona Ivanovna, the old pawnbroker, in case he might want to pawn anything. For a long while he did not go to her, for he had lessons and managed to get along somehow. Six weeks ago he had remembered the address; he had two articles that could be pawned: his father’s old silver watch and a little gold ring with three red stones, a present from his sister at parting. He decided to take the ring. When he found the old woman he had felt an insurmountable repulsion for her at the first glance, though he knew nothing special about her. He got two roubles from her and went into a miserable little tavern on his way home. He asked for tea, sat down and sank into deep thought. A strange idea was pecking at his brain like a chicken in the egg, and very, very much absorbed him.\n\nAlmost beside him at the next table there was sitting a student, whom he did not know and had never seen, and with him a young officer. They had played a game of billiards and began drinking tea. All at once he heard the student mention to the officer the pawnbroker Alyona Ivanovna and give him her address. This of itself seemed strange to Raskolnikov; he had just come from her and here at once he heard her name. Of course it was a chance, but he could not shake off a very extraordinary impression, and here someone seemed to be speaking expressly for him; the student began telling his friend various details about Alyona Ivanovna.\n\n“She is first-rate,” he said. “You can always get money from her. She is as rich as a Jew, she can give you five thousand roubles at a time and she is not above taking a pledge for a rouble. Lots of our fellows have had dealings with her. But she is an awful old harpy....”\n\nAnd he began describing how spiteful and uncertain she was, how if you were only a day late with your interest the pledge was lost; how she gave a quarter of the value of an article and took five and even seven percent a month on it and so on. The student chattered on, saying that she had a sister Lizaveta, whom the wretched little creature was continually beating, and kept in complete bondage like a small child, though Lizaveta was at least six feet high.\n\n“There’s a phenomenon for you,” cried the student and he laughed.\n\nThey began talking about Lizaveta. The student spoke about her with a peculiar relish and was continually laughing and the officer listened with great interest and asked him to send Lizaveta to do some mending for him. Raskolnikov did not miss a word and learned everything about her. Lizaveta was younger than the old woman and was her half-sister, being the child of a different mother. She was thirty-five. She worked day and night for her sister, and besides doing the cooking and the washing, she did sewing and worked as a charwoman and gave her sister all she earned. She did not dare to accept an order or job of any kind without her sister’s permission. The old woman had already made her will, and Lizaveta knew of it, and by this will she would not get a farthing; nothing but the movables, chairs and so on; all the money was left to a monastery in the province of N----, that prayers might be said for her in perpetuity. Lizaveta was of lower rank than her sister, unmarried and awfully uncouth in appearance, remarkably tall with long feet that looked as if they were bent outwards. She always wore battered goatskin shoes, and was clean in her person. What the student expressed most surprise and amusement about was the fact that Lizaveta was continually with child.\n\n“But you say she is hideous?” observed the officer.\n\n“Yes, she is so dark-skinned and looks like a soldier dressed up, but you know she is not at all hideous. She has such a good-natured face and eyes. Strikingly so. And the proof of it is that lots of people are attracted by her. She is such a soft, gentle creature, ready to put up with anything, always willing, willing to do anything. And her smile is really very sweet.”\n\n“You seem to find her attractive yourself,” laughed the officer.\n\n“From her queerness. No, I’ll tell you what. I could kill that damned old woman and make off with her money, I assure you, without the faintest conscience-prick,” the student added with warmth. The officer laughed again while Raskolnikov shuddered. How strange it was!\n\n“Listen, I want to ask you a serious question,” the student said hotly. “I was joking of course, but look here; on one side we have a stupid, senseless, worthless, spiteful, ailing, horrid old woman, not simply useless but doing actual mischief, who has not an idea what she is living for herself, and who will die in a day or two in any case. You understand? You understand?”\n\n“Yes, yes, I understand,” answered the officer, watching his excited companion attentively.\n\n“Well, listen then. On the other side, fresh young lives thrown away for want of help and by thousands, on every side! A hundred thousand good deeds could be done and helped, on that old woman’s money which will be buried in a monastery! Hundreds, thousands perhaps, might be set on the right path; dozens of families saved from destitution, from ruin, from vice, from the Lock hospitals--and all with her money. Kill her, take her money and with the help of it devote oneself to the service of humanity and the good of all. What do you think, would not one tiny crime be wiped out by thousands of good deeds? For one life thousands would be saved from corruption and decay. One death, and a hundred lives in exchange--it’s simple arithmetic! Besides, what value has the life of that sickly, stupid, ill-natured old woman in the balance of existence! No more than the life of a louse, of a black-beetle, less in fact because the old woman is doing harm. She is wearing out the lives of others; the other day she bit Lizaveta’s finger out of spite; it almost had to be amputated.”\n\n“Of course she does not deserve to live,” remarked the officer, “but there it is, it’s nature.”\n\n“Oh, well, brother, but we have to correct and direct nature, and, but for that, we should drown in an ocean of prejudice. But for that, there would never have been a single great man. They talk of duty, conscience--I don’t want to say anything against duty and conscience;--but the point is, what do we mean by them? Stay, I have another question to ask you. Listen!”\n\n“No, you stay, I’ll ask you a question. Listen!”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“You are talking and speechifying away, but tell me, would you kill the old woman _yourself_?”\n\n“Of course not! I was only arguing the justice of it.... It’s nothing to do with me....”\n\n“But I think, if you would not do it yourself, there’s no justice about it.... Let us have another game.”\n\nRaskolnikov was violently agitated. Of course, it was all ordinary youthful talk and thought, such as he had often heard before in different forms and on different themes. But why had he happened to hear such a discussion and such ideas at the very moment when his own brain was just conceiving... _the very same ideas_? And why, just at the moment when he had brought away the embryo of his idea from the old woman had he dropped at once upon a conversation about her? This coincidence always seemed strange to him. This trivial talk in a tavern had an immense influence on him in his later action; as though there had really been in it something preordained, some guiding hint....\n*****\n\nOn returning from the Hay Market he flung himself on the sofa and sat for a whole hour without stirring. Meanwhile it got dark; he had no candle and, indeed, it did not occur to him to light up. He could never recollect whether he had been thinking about anything at that time. At last he was conscious of his former fever and shivering, and he realised with relief that he could lie down on the sofa. Soon heavy, leaden sleep came over him, as it were crushing him.\n\nHe slept an extraordinarily long time and without dreaming. Nastasya, coming into his room at ten o’clock the next morning, had difficulty in rousing him. She brought him in tea and bread. The tea was again the second brew and again in her own tea-pot.\n\n“My goodness, how he sleeps!” she cried indignantly. “And he is always asleep.”\n\nHe got up with an effort. His head ached, he stood up, took a turn in his garret and sank back on the sofa again.\n\n“Going to sleep again,” cried Nastasya. “Are you ill, eh?”\nHe made no reply.\n\n“Do you want some tea?”\n\n“Afterwards,” he said with an effort, closing his eyes again and turning to the wall.\n\nNastasya stood over him.\n\n“Perhaps he really is ill,” she said, turned and went out. She came in again at two o’clock with soup. He was lying as before. The tea stood untouched. Nastasya felt positively offended and began wrathfully rousing him.\n\n“Why are you lying like a log?” she shouted, looking at him with repulsion.\n\nHe got up, and sat down again, but said nothing and stared at the floor.\n\n“Are you ill or not?” asked Nastasya and again received no answer. “You’d better go out and get a breath of air,” she said after a pause. “Will you eat it or not?”\n\n“Afterwards,” he said weakly. “You can go.”\n\nAnd he motioned her out.\n\nShe remained a little longer, looked at him with compassion and went out.\n\nA few minutes afterwards, he raised his eyes and looked for a long while at the tea and the soup. Then he took the bread, took up a spoon and began to eat.\n\nHe ate a little, three or four spoonfuls, without appetite, as it were mechanically. His head ached less. After his meal he stretched himself on the sofa again, but now he could not sleep; he lay without stirring, with his face in the pillow. He was haunted by day-dreams and such strange day-dreams; in one, that kept recurring, he fancied that he was in Africa, in Egypt, in some sort of oasis. The caravan was resting, the camels were peacefully lying down; the palms stood all around in a complete circle; all the party were at dinner. But he was drinking water from a spring which flowed gurgling close by. And it was so cool, it was wonderful, wonderful, blue, cold water running among the parti-coloured stones and over the clean sand which glistened here and there like gold.... Suddenly he heard a clock strike. He started, roused himself, raised his head, looked out of the window, and seeing how late it was, suddenly jumped up wide awake as though someone had pulled him off the sofa. He crept on tiptoe to the door, stealthily opened it and began listening on the staircase. His heart beat terribly. But all was quiet on the stairs as if everyone was asleep.... It seemed to him strange and monstrous that he could have slept in such forgetfulness from the previous day and had done nothing, had prepared nothing yet.... And meanwhile perhaps it had struck six. And his drowsiness and stupefaction were followed by an extraordinary, feverish, as it were distracted haste. But the preparations to be made were few. He concentrated all his energies on thinking of everything and forgetting nothing; and his heart kept beating and thumping so that he could hardly breathe. First he had to make a noose and sew it into his overcoat--a work of a moment. He rummaged under his pillow and picked out amongst the linen stuffed away under it, a worn out, old unwashed shirt. From its rags he tore a long strip, a couple of inches wide and about sixteen inches long. He folded this strip in two, took off his wide, strong summer overcoat of some stout cotton material (his only outer garment) and began sewing the two ends of the rag on the inside, under the left armhole. His hands shook as he sewed, but he did it successfully so that nothing showed outside when he put the coat on again. The needle and thread he had got ready long before and they lay on his table in a piece of paper. As for the noose, it was a very ingenious device of his own; the noose was intended for the axe. It was impossible for him to carry the axe through the street in his hands. And if hidden under his coat he would still have had to support it with his hand, which would have been noticeable. Now he had only to put the head of the axe in the noose, and it would hang quietly under his arm on the inside. Putting his hand in his coat pocket, he could hold the end of the handle all the way, so that it did not swing; and as the coat was very full, a regular sack in fact, it could not be seen from outside that he was holding something with the hand that was in the pocket. This noose, too, he had designed a fortnight before.\n\nWhen he had finished with this, he thrust his hand into a little opening between his sofa and the floor, fumbled in the left corner and drew out the _pledge_, which he had got ready long before and hidden there. This pledge was, however, only a smoothly planed piece of wood the size and thickness of a silver cigarette case. He picked up this piece of wood in one of his wanderings in a courtyard where there was some sort of a workshop. Afterwards he had added to the wood a thin smooth piece of iron, which he had also picked up at the same time in the street. Putting the iron which was a little the smaller on the piece of wood, he fastened them very firmly, crossing and re-crossing the thread round them; then wrapped them carefully and daintily in clean white paper and tied up the parcel so that it would be very difficult to untie it. This was in order to divert the attention of the old woman for a time, while she was trying to undo the knot, and so to gain a moment. The iron strip was added to give weight, so that the woman might not guess the first minute that the “thing” was made of wood. All this had been stored by him beforehand under the sofa. He had only just got the pledge out when he heard someone suddenly about in the yard.\n\n“It struck six long ago.”\n\n“Long ago! My God!”\n\nHe rushed to the door, listened, caught up his hat and began to descend his thirteen steps cautiously, noiselessly, like a cat. He had still the most important thing to do--to steal the axe from the kitchen. That the deed must be done with an axe he had decided long ago. He had also a pocket pruning-knife, but he could not rely on the knife and still less on his own strength, and so resolved finally on the axe. We may note in passing, one peculiarity in regard to all the final resolutions taken by him in the matter; they had one strange characteristic: the more final they were, the more hideous and the more absurd they at once became in his eyes. In spite of all his agonising inward struggle, he never for a single instant all that time could believe in the carrying out of his plans.\n\nAnd, indeed, if it had ever happened that everything to the least point could have been considered and finally settled, and no uncertainty of any kind had remained, he would, it seems, have renounced it all as something absurd, monstrous and impossible. But a whole mass of unsettled points and uncertainties remained. As for getting the axe, that trifling business cost him no anxiety, for nothing could be easier. Nastasya was continually out of the house, especially in the evenings; she would run in to the neighbours or to a shop, and always left the door ajar. It was the one thing the landlady was always scolding her about. And so, when the time came, he would only have to go quietly into the kitchen and to take the axe, and an hour later (when everything was over) go in and put it back again. But these were doubtful points. Supposing he returned an hour later to put it back, and Nastasya had come back and was on the spot. He would of course have to go by and wait till she went out again. But supposing she were in the meantime to miss the axe, look for it, make an outcry--that would mean suspicion or at least grounds for suspicion.\n\nBut those were all trifles which he had not even begun to consider, and indeed he had no time. He was thinking of the chief point, and put off trifling details, until _he could believe in it all_. But that seemed utterly unattainable. So it seemed to himself at least. He could not imagine, for instance, that he would sometime leave off thinking, get up and simply go there.... Even his late experiment (i.e. his visit with the object of a final survey of the place) was simply an attempt at an experiment, far from being the real thing, as though one should say “come, let us go and try it--why dream about it!”--and at once he had broken down and had run away cursing, in a frenzy with himself. Meanwhile it would seem, as regards the moral question, that his analysis was complete; his casuistry had become keen as a razor, and he could not find rational objections in himself. But in the last resort he simply ceased to believe in himself, and doggedly, slavishly sought arguments in all directions, fumbling for them, as though someone were forcing and drawing him to it.\n\nAt first--long before indeed--he had been much occupied with one question; why almost all crimes are so badly concealed and so easily detected, and why almost all criminals leave such obvious traces? He had come gradually to many different and curious conclusions, and in his opinion the chief reason lay not so much in the material impossibility of concealing the crime, as in the criminal himself. Almost every criminal is subject to a failure of will and reasoning power by a childish and phenomenal heedlessness, at the very instant when prudence and caution are most essential. It was his conviction that this eclipse of reason and failure of will power attacked a man like a disease, developed gradually and reached its highest point just before the perpetration of the crime, continued with equal violence at the moment of the crime and for longer or shorter time after, according to the individual case, and then passed off like any other disease. The question whether the disease gives rise to the crime, or whether the crime from its own peculiar nature is always accompanied by something of the nature of disease, he did not yet feel able to decide.\n\nWhen he reached these conclusions, he decided that in his own case there could not be such a morbid reaction, that his reason and will would remain unimpaired at the time of carrying out his design, for the simple reason that his design was “not a crime....” We will omit all the process by means of which he arrived at this last conclusion; we have run too far ahead already.... We may add only that the practical, purely material difficulties of the affair occupied a secondary position in his mind. “One has but to keep all one’s will-power and reason to deal with them, and they will all be overcome at the time when once one has familiarised oneself with the minutest details of the business....” But this preparation had never been begun. His final decisions were what he came to trust least, and when the hour struck, it all came to pass quite differently, as it were accidentally and unexpectedly.\n\nOne trifling circumstance upset his calculations, before he had even left the staircase. When he reached the landlady’s kitchen, the door of which was open as usual, he glanced cautiously in to see whether, in Nastasya’s absence, the landlady herself was there, or if not, whether the door to her own room was closed, so that she might not peep out when he went in for the axe. But what was his amazement when he suddenly saw that Nastasya was not only at home in the kitchen, but was occupied there, taking linen out of a basket and hanging it on a line. Seeing him, she left off hanging the clothes, turned to him and stared at him all the time he was passing. He turned away his eyes, and walked past as though he noticed nothing. But it was the end of everything; he had not the axe! He was overwhelmed.\n\n“What made me think,” he reflected, as he went under the gateway, “what made me think that she would be sure not to be at home at that moment! Why, why, why did I assume this so certainly?”\n\nHe was crushed and even humiliated. He could have laughed at himself in his anger.... A dull animal rage boiled within him.\n\nHe stood hesitating in the gateway. To go into the street, to go a walk for appearance’ sake was revolting; to go back to his room, even more revolting. “And what a chance I have lost for ever!” he muttered, standing aimlessly in the gateway, just opposite the porter’s little dark room, which was also open. Suddenly he started. From the porter’s room, two paces away from him, something shining under the bench to the right caught his eye.... He looked about him--nobody. He approached the room on tiptoe, went down two steps into it and in a faint voice called the porter. “Yes, not at home! Somewhere near though, in the yard, for the door is wide open.” He dashed to the axe (it was an axe) and pulled it out from under the bench, where it lay between two chunks of wood; at once, before going out, he made it fast in the noose, he thrust both hands into his pockets and went out of the room; no one had noticed him! “When reason fails, the devil helps!” he thought with a strange grin. This chance raised his spirits extraordinarily.\n\nHe walked along quietly and sedately, without hurry, to avoid awakening suspicion. He scarcely looked at the passers-by, tried to escape looking at their faces at all, and to be as little noticeable as possible. Suddenly he thought of his hat. “Good heavens! I had the money the day before yesterday and did not get a cap to wear instead!” A curse rose from the bottom of his soul.\n\nGlancing out of the corner of his eye into a shop, he saw by a clock on the wall that it was ten minutes past seven. He had to make haste and at the same time to go someway round, so as to approach the house from the other side....\n\nWhen he had happened to imagine all this beforehand, he had sometimes thought that he would be very much afraid. But he was not very much afraid now, was not afraid at all, indeed. His mind was even occupied by irrelevant matters, but by nothing for long. As he passed the Yusupov garden, he was deeply absorbed in considering the building of great fountains, and of their refreshing effect on the atmosphere in all the squares. By degrees he passed to the conviction that if the summer garden were extended to the field of Mars, and perhaps joined to the garden of the Mihailovsky Palace, it would be a splendid thing and a great benefit to the town. Then he was interested by the question why in all great towns men are not simply driven by necessity, but in some peculiar way inclined to live in those parts of the town where there are no gardens nor fountains; where there is most dirt and smell and all sorts of nastiness. Then his own walks through the Hay Market came back to his mind, and for a moment he waked up to reality. “What nonsense!” he thought, “better think of nothing at all!”\n\n“So probably men led to execution clutch mentally at every object that meets them on the way,” flashed through his mind, but simply flashed, like lightning; he made haste to dismiss this thought.... And by now he was near; here was the house, here was the gate. Suddenly a clock somewhere struck once. “What! can it be half-past seven? Impossible, it must be fast!”\n\nLuckily for him, everything went well again at the gates. At that very moment, as though expressly for his benefit, a huge waggon of hay had just driven in at the gate, completely screening him as he passed under the gateway, and the waggon had scarcely had time to drive through into the yard, before he had slipped in a flash to the right. On the other side of the waggon he could hear shouting and quarrelling; but no one noticed him and no one met him. Many windows looking into that huge quadrangular yard were open at that moment, but he did not raise his head--he had not the strength to. The staircase leading to the old woman’s room was close by, just on the right of the gateway. He was already on the stairs....\n\nDrawing a breath, pressing his hand against his throbbing heart, and once more feeling for the axe and setting it straight, he began softly and cautiously ascending the stairs, listening every minute. But the stairs, too, were quite deserted; all the doors were shut; he met no one. One flat indeed on the first floor was wide open and painters were at work in it, but they did not glance at him. He stood still, thought a minute and went on. “Of course it would be better if they had not been here, but... it’s two storeys above them.”\n\nAnd there was the fourth storey, here was the door, here was the flat opposite, the empty one. The flat underneath the old woman’s was apparently empty also; the visiting card nailed on the door had been torn off--they had gone away!... He was out of breath. For one instant the thought floated through his mind, “Shall I go back?” But he made no answer and began listening at the old woman’s door, a dead silence. Then he listened again on the staircase, listened long and intently... then looked about him for the last time, pulled himself together, drew himself up, and once more tried the axe in the noose. “Am I very pale?” he wondered. “Am I not evidently agitated? She is mistrustful.... Had I better wait a little longer... till my heart leaves off thumping?”\n\nBut his heart did not leave off. On the contrary, as though to spite him, it throbbed more and more violently. He could stand it no longer, he slowly put out his hand to the bell and rang. Half a minute later he rang again, more loudly.\n\nNo answer. To go on ringing was useless and out of place. The old woman was, of course, at home, but she was suspicious and alone. He had some knowledge of her habits... and once more he put his ear to the door. Either his senses were peculiarly keen (which it is difficult to suppose), or the sound was really very distinct. Anyway, he suddenly heard something like the cautious touch of a hand on the lock and the rustle of a skirt at the very door. Someone was standing stealthily close to the lock and just as he was doing on the outside was secretly listening within, and seemed to have her ear to the door.... He moved a little on purpose and muttered something aloud that he might not have the appearance of hiding, then rang a third time, but quietly, soberly, and without impatience, Recalling it afterwards, that moment stood out in his mind vividly, distinctly, for ever; he could not make out how he had had such cunning, for his mind was as it were clouded at moments and he was almost unconscious of his body.... An instant later he heard the latch unfastened.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nThe door was as before opened a tiny crack, and again two sharp and suspicious eyes stared at him out of the darkness. Then Raskolnikov lost his head and nearly made a great mistake.\n\nFearing the old woman would be frightened by their being alone, and not hoping that the sight of him would disarm her suspicions, he took hold of the door and drew it towards him to prevent the old woman from attempting to shut it again. Seeing this she did not pull the door back, but she did not let go the handle so that he almost dragged her out with it on to the stairs. Seeing that she was standing in the doorway not allowing him to pass, he advanced straight upon her. She stepped back in alarm, tried to say something, but seemed unable to speak and stared with open eyes at him.\n\n“Good evening, Alyona Ivanovna,” he began, trying to speak easily, but his voice would not obey him, it broke and shook. “I have come... I have brought something... but we’d better come in... to the light....”\n\nAnd leaving her, he passed straight into the room uninvited. The old woman ran after him; her tongue was unloosed.\n\n“Good heavens! What it is? Who is it? What do you want?”\n\n“Why, Alyona Ivanovna, you know me... Raskolnikov... here, I brought you the pledge I promised the other day...” And he held out the pledge.\n\nThe old woman glanced for a moment at the pledge, but at once stared in the eyes of her uninvited visitor. She looked intently, maliciously and mistrustfully. A minute passed; he even fancied something like a sneer in her eyes, as though she had already guessed everything. He felt that he was losing his head, that he was almost frightened, so frightened that if she were to look like that and not say a word for another half minute, he thought he would have run away from her.\n\n“Why do you look at me as though you did not know me?” he said suddenly, also with malice. “Take it if you like, if not I’ll go elsewhere, I am in a hurry.”\n\nHe had not even thought of saying this, but it was suddenly said of itself. The old woman recovered herself, and her visitor’s resolute tone evidently restored her confidence.\n\n“But why, my good sir, all of a minute.... What is it?” she asked, looking at the pledge.\n\n“The silver cigarette case; I spoke of it last time, you know.”\n\nShe held out her hand.\n\n“But how pale you are, to be sure... and your hands are trembling too? Have you been bathing, or what?”\n\n“Fever,” he answered abruptly. “You can’t help getting pale... if you’ve nothing to eat,” he added, with difficulty articulating the words.\n\nHis strength was failing him again. But his answer sounded like the truth; the old woman took the pledge.\n\n“What is it?” she asked once more, scanning Raskolnikov intently, and weighing the pledge in her hand.\n\n“A thing... cigarette case.... Silver.... Look at it.”\n\n“It does not seem somehow like silver.... How he has wrapped it up!”\n\nTrying to untie the string and turning to the window, to the light (all her windows were shut, in spite of the stifling heat), she left him altogether for some seconds and stood with her back to him. He unbuttoned his coat and freed the axe from the noose, but did not yet take it out altogether, simply holding it in his right hand under the coat. His hands were fearfully weak, he felt them every moment growing more numb and more wooden. He was afraid he would let the axe slip and fall.... A sudden giddiness came over him.\n\n“But what has he tied it up like this for?” the old woman cried with vexation and moved towards him.\n\nHe had not a minute more to lose. He pulled the axe quite out, swung it with both arms, scarcely conscious of himself, and almost without effort, almost mechanically, brought the blunt side down on her head. He seemed not to use his own strength in this. But as soon as he had once brought the axe down, his strength returned to him.\n\nThe old woman was as always bareheaded. Her thin, light hair, streaked with grey, thickly smeared with grease, was plaited in a rat’s tail and fastened by a broken horn comb which stood out on the nape of her neck. As she was so short, the blow fell on the very top of her skull. She cried out, but very faintly, and suddenly sank all of a heap on the floor, raising her hands to her head. In one hand she still held “the pledge.” Then he dealt her another and another blow with the blunt side and on the same spot. The blood gushed as from an overturned glass, the body fell back. He stepped back, let it fall, and at once bent over her face; she was dead. Her eyes seemed to be starting out of their sockets, the brow and the whole face were drawn and contorted convulsively.\n\nHe laid the axe on the ground near the dead body and felt at once in her pocket (trying to avoid the streaming body)--the same right-hand pocket from which she had taken the key on his last visit. He was in full possession of his faculties, free from confusion or giddiness, but his hands were still trembling. He remembered afterwards that he had been particularly collected and careful, trying all the time not to get smeared with blood.... He pulled out the keys at once, they were all, as before, in one bunch on a steel ring. He ran at once into the bedroom with them. It was a very small room with a whole shrine of holy images. Against the other wall stood a big bed, very clean and covered with a silk patchwork wadded quilt. Against a third wall was a chest of drawers. Strange to say, so soon as he began to fit the keys into the chest, so soon as he heard their jingling, a convulsive shudder passed over him. He suddenly felt tempted again to give it all up and go away. But that was only for an instant; it was too late to go back. He positively smiled at himself, when suddenly another terrifying idea occurred to his mind. He suddenly fancied that the old woman might be still alive and might recover her senses. Leaving the keys in the chest, he ran back to the body, snatched up the axe and lifted it once more over the old woman, but did not bring it down. There was no doubt that she was dead. Bending down and examining her again more closely, he saw clearly that the skull was broken and even battered in on one side. He was about to feel it with his finger, but drew back his hand and indeed it was evident without that. Meanwhile there was a perfect pool of blood. All at once he noticed a string on her neck; he tugged at it, but the string was strong and did not snap and besides, it was soaked with blood. He tried to pull it out from the front of the dress, but something held it and prevented its coming. In his impatience he raised the axe again to cut the string from above on the body, but did not dare, and with difficulty, smearing his hand and the axe in the blood, after two minutes’ hurried effort, he cut the string and took it off without touching the body with the axe; he was not mistaken--it was a purse. On the string were two crosses, one of Cyprus wood and one of copper, and an image in silver filigree, and with them a small greasy chamois leather purse with a steel rim and ring. The purse was stuffed very full; Raskolnikov thrust it in his pocket without looking at it, flung the crosses on the old woman’s body and rushed back into the bedroom, this time taking the axe with him.\n\nHe was in terrible haste, he snatched the keys, and began trying them again. But he was unsuccessful. They would not fit in the locks. It was not so much that his hands were shaking, but that he kept making mistakes; though he saw for instance that a key was not the right one and would not fit, still he tried to put it in. Suddenly he remembered and realised that the big key with the deep notches, which was hanging there with the small keys could not possibly belong to the chest of drawers (on his last visit this had struck him), but to some strong box, and that everything perhaps was hidden in that box. He left the chest of drawers, and at once felt under the bedstead, knowing that old women usually keep boxes under their beds. And so it was; there was a good-sized box under the bed, at least a yard in length, with an arched lid covered with red leather and studded with steel nails. The notched key fitted at once and unlocked it. At the top, under a white sheet, was a coat of red brocade lined with hareskin; under it was a silk dress, then a shawl and it seemed as though there was nothing below but clothes. The first thing he did was to wipe his blood-stained hands on the red brocade. “It’s red, and on red blood will be less noticeable,” the thought passed through his mind; then he suddenly came to himself. “Good God, am I going out of my senses?” he thought with terror.\n\nBut no sooner did he touch the clothes than a gold watch slipped from under the fur coat. He made haste to turn them all over. There turned out to be various articles made of gold among the clothes--probably all pledges, unredeemed or waiting to be redeemed--bracelets, chains, ear-rings, pins and such things. Some were in cases, others simply wrapped in newspaper, carefully and exactly folded, and tied round with tape. Without any delay, he began filling up the pockets of his trousers and overcoat without examining or undoing the parcels and cases; but he had not time to take many....\n\nHe suddenly heard steps in the room where the old woman lay. He stopped short and was still as death. But all was quiet, so it must have been his fancy. All at once he heard distinctly a faint cry, as though someone had uttered a low broken moan. Then again dead silence for a minute or two. He sat squatting on his heels by the box and waited holding his breath. Suddenly he jumped up, seized the axe and ran out of the bedroom.\n\nIn the middle of the room stood Lizaveta with a big bundle in her arms. She was gazing in stupefaction at her murdered sister, white as a sheet and seeming not to have the strength to cry out. Seeing him run out of the bedroom, she began faintly quivering all over, like a leaf, a shudder ran down her face; she lifted her hand, opened her mouth, but still did not scream. She began slowly backing away from him into the corner, staring intently, persistently at him, but still uttered no sound, as though she could not get breath to scream. He rushed at her with the axe; her mouth twitched piteously, as one sees babies’ mouths, when they begin to be frightened, stare intently at what frightens them and are on the point of screaming. And this hapless Lizaveta was so simple and had been so thoroughly crushed and scared that she did not even raise a hand to guard her face, though that was the most necessary and natural action at the moment, for the axe was raised over her face. She only put up her empty left hand, but not to her face, slowly holding it out before her as though motioning him away. The axe fell with the sharp edge just on the skull and split at one blow all the top of the head. She fell heavily at once. Raskolnikov completely lost his head, snatching up her bundle, dropped it again and ran into the entry.\n\nFear gained more and more mastery over him, especially after this second, quite unexpected murder. He longed to run away from the place as fast as possible. And if at that moment he had been capable of seeing and reasoning more correctly, if he had been able to realise all the difficulties of his position, the hopelessness, the hideousness and the absurdity of it, if he could have understood how many obstacles and, perhaps, crimes he had still to overcome or to commit, to get out of that place and to make his way home, it is very possible that he would have flung up everything, and would have gone to give himself up, and not from fear, but from simple horror and loathing of what he had done. The feeling of loathing especially surged up within him and grew stronger every minute. He would not now have gone to the box or even into the room for anything in the world.\n\nBut a sort of blankness, even dreaminess, had begun by degrees to take possession of him; at moments he forgot himself, or rather, forgot what was of importance, and caught at trifles. Glancing, however, into the kitchen and seeing a bucket half full of water on a bench, he bethought him of washing his hands and the axe. His hands were sticky with blood. He dropped the axe with the blade in the water, snatched a piece of soap that lay in a broken saucer on the window, and began washing his hands in the bucket. When they were clean, he took out the axe, washed the blade and spent a long time, about three minutes, washing the wood where there were spots of blood rubbing them with soap. Then he wiped it all with some linen that was hanging to dry on a line in the kitchen and then he was a long while attentively examining the axe at the window. There was no trace left on it, only the wood was still damp. He carefully hung the axe in the noose under his coat. Then as far as was possible, in the dim light in the kitchen, he looked over his overcoat, his trousers and his boots. At the first glance there seemed to be nothing but stains on the boots. He wetted the rag and rubbed the boots. But he knew he was not looking thoroughly, that there might be something quite noticeable that he was overlooking. He stood in the middle of the room, lost in thought. Dark agonising ideas rose in his mind--the idea that he was mad and that at that moment he was incapable of reasoning, of protecting himself, that he ought perhaps to be doing something utterly different from what he was now doing. “Good God!” he muttered, “I must fly, fly,” and he rushed into the entry. But here a shock of terror awaited him such as he had never known before.\n\nHe stood and gazed and could not believe his eyes: the door, the outer door from the stairs, at which he had not long before waited and rung, was standing unfastened and at least six inches open. No lock, no bolt, all the time, all that time! The old woman had not shut it after him perhaps as a precaution. But, good God! Why, he had seen Lizaveta afterwards! And how could he, how could he have failed to reflect that she must have come in somehow! She could not have come through the wall!\n\nHe dashed to the door and fastened the latch.\n\n“But no, the wrong thing again! I must get away, get away....”\n\nHe unfastened the latch, opened the door and began listening on the staircase.\n\nHe listened a long time. Somewhere far away, it might be in the gateway, two voices were loudly and shrilly shouting, quarrelling and scolding. “What are they about?” He waited patiently. At last all was still, as though suddenly cut off; they had separated. He was meaning to go out, but suddenly, on the floor below, a door was noisily opened and someone began going downstairs humming a tune. “How is it they all make such a noise?” flashed through his mind. Once more he closed the door and waited. At last all was still, not a soul stirring. He was just taking a step towards the stairs when he heard fresh footsteps.\n\nThe steps sounded very far off, at the very bottom of the stairs, but he remembered quite clearly and distinctly that from the first sound he began for some reason to suspect that this was someone coming _there_, to the fourth floor, to the old woman. Why? Were the sounds somehow peculiar, significant? The steps were heavy, even and unhurried. Now _he_ had passed the first floor, now he was mounting higher, it was growing more and more distinct! He could hear his heavy breathing. And now the third storey had been reached. Coming here! And it seemed to him all at once that he was turned to stone, that it was like a dream in which one is being pursued, nearly caught and will be killed, and is rooted to the spot and cannot even move one’s arms.\n\nAt last when the unknown was mounting to the fourth floor, he suddenly started, and succeeded in slipping neatly and quickly back into the flat and closing the door behind him. Then he took the hook and softly, noiselessly, fixed it in the catch. Instinct helped him. When he had done this, he crouched holding his breath, by the door. The unknown visitor was by now also at the door. They were now standing opposite one another, as he had just before been standing with the old woman, when the door divided them and he was listening.\n\nThe visitor panted several times. “He must be a big, fat man,” thought Raskolnikov, squeezing the axe in his hand. It seemed like a dream indeed. The visitor took hold of the bell and rang it loudly.\n\nAs soon as the tin bell tinkled, Raskolnikov seemed to be aware of something moving in the room. For some seconds he listened quite seriously. The unknown rang again, waited and suddenly tugged violently and impatiently at the handle of the door. Raskolnikov gazed in horror at the hook shaking in its fastening, and in blank terror expected every minute that the fastening would be pulled out. It certainly did seem possible, so violently was he shaking it. He was tempted to hold the fastening, but _he_ might be aware of it. A giddiness came over him again. “I shall fall down!” flashed through his mind, but the unknown began to speak and he recovered himself at once.\n\n“What’s up? Are they asleep or murdered? D-damn them!” he bawled in a thick voice, “Hey, Alyona Ivanovna, old witch! Lizaveta Ivanovna, hey, my beauty! open the door! Oh, damn them! Are they asleep or what?”\n\nAnd again, enraged, he tugged with all his might a dozen times at the bell. He must certainly be a man of authority and an intimate acquaintance.\n\nAt this moment light hurried steps were heard not far off, on the stairs. Someone else was approaching. Raskolnikov had not heard them at first.\n\n“You don’t say there’s no one at home,” the new-comer cried in a cheerful, ringing voice, addressing the first visitor, who still went on pulling the bell. “Good evening, Koch.”\n\n“From his voice he must be quite young,” thought Raskolnikov.\n\n“Who the devil can tell? I’ve almost broken the lock,” answered Koch. “But how do you come to know me?”\n\n“Why! The day before yesterday I beat you three times running at billiards at Gambrinus’.”\n\n“Oh!”\n\n“So they are not at home? That’s queer. It’s awfully stupid though. Where could the old woman have gone? I’ve come on business.”\n\n“Yes; and I have business with her, too.”\n\n“Well, what can we do? Go back, I suppose, Aie--aie! And I was hoping to get some money!” cried the young man.\n\n“We must give it up, of course, but what did she fix this time for? The old witch fixed the time for me to come herself. It’s out of my way. And where the devil she can have got to, I can’t make out. She sits here from year’s end to year’s end, the old hag; her legs are bad and yet here all of a sudden she is out for a walk!”\n\n“Hadn’t we better ask the porter?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Where she’s gone and when she’ll be back.”\n\n“Hm.... Damn it all!... We might ask.... But you know she never does go anywhere.”\n\nAnd he once more tugged at the door-handle.\n\n“Damn it all. There’s nothing to be done, we must go!”\n\n“Stay!” cried the young man suddenly. “Do you see how the door shakes if you pull it?”\n\n“That shows it’s not locked, but fastened with the hook! Do you hear how the hook clanks?”\n“Well?”\n\n“Why, don’t you see? That proves that one of them is at home. If they were all out, they would have locked the door from the outside with the key and not with the hook from inside. There, do you hear how the hook is clanking? To fasten the hook on the inside they must be at home, don’t you see? So there they are sitting inside and don’t open the door!”\n\n“Well! And so they must be!” cried Koch, astonished. “What are they about in there?” And he began furiously shaking the door.\n\n“Stay!” cried the young man again. “Don’t pull at it! There must be something wrong.... Here, you’ve been ringing and pulling at the door and still they don’t open! So either they’ve both fainted or...”\n\n“What?”\n“I tell you what. Let’s go fetch the porter, let him wake them up.”\n\n“All right.”\n\nBoth were going down.\n\n“Stay. You stop here while I run down for the porter.”\n\n“What for?”\n\n“Well, you’d better.”\n\n“All right.”\n\n“I’m studying the law you see! It’s evident, e-vi-dent there’s something wrong here!” the young man cried hotly, and he ran downstairs.\n\nKoch remained. Once more he softly touched the bell which gave one tinkle, then gently, as though reflecting and looking about him, began touching the door-handle pulling it and letting it go to make sure once more that it was only fastened by the hook. Then puffing and panting he bent down and began looking at the keyhole: but the key was in the lock on the inside and so nothing could be seen.\n\nRaskolnikov stood keeping tight hold of the axe. He was in a sort of delirium. He was even making ready to fight when they should come in. While they were knocking and talking together, the idea several times occurred to him to end it all at once and shout to them through the door. Now and then he was tempted to swear at them, to jeer at them, while they could not open the door! “Only make haste!” was the thought that flashed through his mind.\n\n“But what the devil is he about?...” Time was passing, one minute, and another--no one came. Koch began to be restless.\n\n“What the devil?” he cried suddenly and in impatience deserting his sentry duty, he, too, went down, hurrying and thumping with his heavy boots on the stairs. The steps died away.\n\n“Good heavens! What am I to do?”\n\nRaskolnikov unfastened the hook, opened the door--there was no sound. Abruptly, without any thought at all, he went out, closing the door as thoroughly as he could, and went downstairs.\n\nHe had gone down three flights when he suddenly heard a loud voice below--where could he go! There was nowhere to hide. He was just going back to the flat.\n\n“Hey there! Catch the brute!”\n\nSomebody dashed out of a flat below, shouting, and rather fell than ran down the stairs, bawling at the top of his voice.\n\n“Mitka! Mitka! Mitka! Mitka! Mitka! Blast him!”\n\nThe shout ended in a shriek; the last sounds came from the yard; all was still. But at the same instant several men talking loud and fast began noisily mounting the stairs. There were three or four of them. He distinguished the ringing voice of the young man. “Hey!”\n\nFilled with despair he went straight to meet them, feeling “come what must!” If they stopped him--all was lost; if they let him pass--all was lost too; they would remember him. They were approaching; they were only a flight from him--and suddenly deliverance! A few steps from him on the right, there was an empty flat with the door wide open, the flat on the second floor where the painters had been at work, and which, as though for his benefit, they had just left. It was they, no doubt, who had just run down, shouting. The floor had only just been painted, in the middle of the room stood a pail and a broken pot with paint and brushes. In one instant he had whisked in at the open door and hidden behind the wall and only in the nick of time; they had already reached the landing. Then they turned and went on up to the fourth floor, talking loudly. He waited, went out on tiptoe and ran down the stairs.\n\nNo one was on the stairs, nor in the gateway. He passed quickly through the gateway and turned to the left in the street.\n\nHe knew, he knew perfectly well that at that moment they were at the flat, that they were greatly astonished at finding it unlocked, as the door had just been fastened, that by now they were looking at the bodies, that before another minute had passed they would guess and completely realise that the murderer had just been there, and had succeeded in hiding somewhere, slipping by them and escaping. They would guess most likely that he had been in the empty flat, while they were going upstairs. And meanwhile he dared not quicken his pace much, though the next turning was still nearly a hundred yards away. “Should he slip through some gateway and wait somewhere in an unknown street? No, hopeless! Should he fling away the axe? Should he take a cab? Hopeless, hopeless!”\n\nAt last he reached the turning. He turned down it more dead than alive. Here he was half way to safety, and he understood it; it was less risky because there was a great crowd of people, and he was lost in it like a grain of sand. But all he had suffered had so weakened him that he could scarcely move. Perspiration ran down him in drops, his neck was all wet. “My word, he has been going it!” someone shouted at him when he came out on the canal bank.\n\nHe was only dimly conscious of himself now, and the farther he went the worse it was. He remembered however, that on coming out on to the canal bank, he was alarmed at finding few people there and so being more conspicuous, and he had thought of turning back. Though he was almost falling from fatigue, he went a long way round so as to get home from quite a different direction.\n\nHe was not fully conscious when he passed through the gateway of his house! He was already on the staircase before he recollected the axe. And yet he had a very grave problem before him, to put it back and to escape observation as far as possible in doing so. He was of course incapable of reflecting that it might perhaps be far better not to restore the axe at all, but to drop it later on in somebody’s yard. But it all happened fortunately, the door of the porter’s room was closed but not locked, so that it seemed most likely that the porter was at home. But he had so completely lost all power of reflection that he walked straight to the door and opened it. If the porter had asked him, “What do you want?” he would perhaps have simply handed him the axe. But again the porter was not at home, and he succeeded in putting the axe back under the bench, and even covering it with the chunk of wood as before. He met no one, not a soul, afterwards on the way to his room; the landlady’s door was shut. When he was in his room, he flung himself on the sofa just as he was--he did not sleep, but sank into blank forgetfulness. If anyone had come into his room then, he would have jumped up at once and screamed. Scraps and shreds of thoughts were simply swarming in his brain, but he could not catch at one, he could not rest on one, in spite of all his efforts....\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nSo he lay a very long while. Now and then he seemed to wake up, and at such moments he noticed that it was far into the night, but it did not occur to him to get up. At last he noticed that it was beginning to get light. He was lying on his back, still dazed from his recent oblivion. Fearful, despairing cries rose shrilly from the street, sounds which he heard every night, indeed, under his window after two o’clock. They woke him up now.\n\n“Ah! the drunken men are coming out of the taverns,” he thought, “it’s past two o’clock,” and at once he leaped up, as though someone had pulled him from the sofa.\n\n“What! Past two o’clock!”\n\nHe sat down on the sofa--and instantly recollected everything! All at once, in one flash, he recollected everything.\n\nFor the first moment he thought he was going mad. A dreadful chill came over him; but the chill was from the fever that had begun long before in his sleep. Now he was suddenly taken with violent shivering, so that his teeth chattered and all his limbs were shaking. He opened the door and began listening--everything in the house was asleep. With amazement he gazed at himself and everything in the room around him, wondering how he could have come in the night before without fastening the door, and have flung himself on the sofa without undressing, without even taking his hat off. It had fallen off and was lying on the floor near his pillow.\n\n“If anyone had come in, what would he have thought? That I’m drunk but...”\n\nHe rushed to the window. There was light enough, and he began hurriedly looking himself all over from head to foot, all his clothes; were there no traces? But there was no doing it like that; shivering with cold, he began taking off everything and looking over again. He turned everything over to the last threads and rags, and mistrusting himself, went through his search three times.\n\nBut there seemed to be nothing, no trace, except in one place, where some thick drops of congealed blood were clinging to the frayed edge of his trousers. He picked up a big claspknife and cut off the frayed threads. There seemed to be nothing more.\n\nSuddenly he remembered that the purse and the things he had taken out of the old woman’s box were still in his pockets! He had not thought till then of taking them out and hiding them! He had not even thought of them while he was examining his clothes! What next? Instantly he rushed to take them out and fling them on the table. When he had pulled out everything, and turned the pocket inside out to be sure there was nothing left, he carried the whole heap to the corner. The paper had come off the bottom of the wall and hung there in tatters. He began stuffing all the things into the hole under the paper: “They’re in! All out of sight, and the purse too!” he thought gleefully, getting up and gazing blankly at the hole which bulged out more than ever. Suddenly he shuddered all over with horror; “My God!” he whispered in despair: “what’s the matter with me? Is that hidden? Is that the way to hide things?”\n\nHe had not reckoned on having trinkets to hide. He had only thought of money, and so had not prepared a hiding-place.\n\n“But now, now, what am I glad of?” he thought, “Is that hiding things? My reason’s deserting me--simply!”\n\nHe sat down on the sofa in exhaustion and was at once shaken by another unbearable fit of shivering. Mechanically he drew from a chair beside him his old student’s winter coat, which was still warm though almost in rags, covered himself up with it and once more sank into drowsiness and delirium. He lost consciousness.\n\nNot more than five minutes had passed when he jumped up a second time, and at once pounced in a frenzy on his clothes again.\n\n“How could I go to sleep again with nothing done? Yes, yes; I have not taken the loop off the armhole! I forgot it, forgot a thing like that! Such a piece of evidence!”\n\nHe pulled off the noose, hurriedly cut it to pieces and threw the bits among his linen under the pillow.\n\n“Pieces of torn linen couldn’t rouse suspicion, whatever happened; I think not, I think not, any way!” he repeated, standing in the middle of the room, and with painful concentration he fell to gazing about him again, at the floor and everywhere, trying to make sure he had not forgotten anything. The conviction that all his faculties, even memory, and the simplest power of reflection were failing him, began to be an insufferable torture.\n\n“Surely it isn’t beginning already! Surely it isn’t my punishment coming upon me? It is!”\n\nThe frayed rags he had cut off his trousers were actually lying on the floor in the middle of the room, where anyone coming in would see them!\n\n“What is the matter with me!” he cried again, like one distraught.\n\nThen a strange idea entered his head; that, perhaps, all his clothes were covered with blood, that, perhaps, there were a great many stains, but that he did not see them, did not notice them because his perceptions were failing, were going to pieces... his reason was clouded.... Suddenly he remembered that there had been blood on the purse too. “Ah! Then there must be blood on the pocket too, for I put the wet purse in my pocket!”\n\nIn a flash he had turned the pocket inside out and, yes!--there were traces, stains on the lining of the pocket!\n\n“So my reason has not quite deserted me, so I still have some sense and memory, since I guessed it of myself,” he thought triumphantly, with a deep sigh of relief; “it’s simply the weakness of fever, a moment’s delirium,” and he tore the whole lining out of the left pocket of his trousers. At that instant the sunlight fell on his left boot; on the sock which poked out from the boot, he fancied there were traces! He flung off his boots; “traces indeed! The tip of the sock was soaked with blood;” he must have unwarily stepped into that pool.... “But what am I to do with this now? Where am I to put the sock and rags and pocket?”\n\nHe gathered them all up in his hands and stood in the middle of the room.\n\n“In the stove? But they would ransack the stove first of all. Burn them? But what can I burn them with? There are no matches even. No, better go out and throw it all away somewhere. Yes, better throw it away,” he repeated, sitting down on the sofa again, “and at once, this minute, without lingering...”\n\nBut his head sank on the pillow instead. Again the unbearable icy shivering came over him; again he drew his coat over him.\n\nAnd for a long while, for some hours, he was haunted by the impulse to “go off somewhere at once, this moment, and fling it all away, so that it may be out of sight and done with, at once, at once!” Several times he tried to rise from the sofa, but could not.\n\nHe was thoroughly waked up at last by a violent knocking at his door.\n\n“Open, do, are you dead or alive? He keeps sleeping here!” shouted Nastasya, banging with her fist on the door. “For whole days together he’s snoring here like a dog! A dog he is too. Open, I tell you. It’s past ten.”\n\n“Maybe he’s not at home,” said a man’s voice.\n\n“Ha! that’s the porter’s voice.... What does he want?”\n\nHe jumped up and sat on the sofa. The beating of his heart was a positive pain.\n\n“Then who can have latched the door?” retorted Nastasya. “He’s taken to bolting himself in! As if he were worth stealing! Open, you stupid, wake up!”\n\n“What do they want? Why the porter? All’s discovered. Resist or open? Come what may!...”\n\nHe half rose, stooped forward and unlatched the door.\n\nHis room was so small that he could undo the latch without leaving the bed. Yes; the porter and Nastasya were standing there.\n\nNastasya stared at him in a strange way. He glanced with a defiant and desperate air at the porter, who without a word held out a grey folded paper sealed with bottle-wax.\n\n“A notice from the office,” he announced, as he gave him the paper.\n\n“From what office?”\n\n“A summons to the police office, of course. You know which office.”\n\n“To the police?... What for?...”\n\n“How can I tell? You’re sent for, so you go.”\n\nThe man looked at him attentively, looked round the room and turned to go away.\n\n“He’s downright ill!” observed Nastasya, not taking her eyes off him. The porter turned his head for a moment. “He’s been in a fever since yesterday,” she added.\n\nRaskolnikov made no response and held the paper in his hands, without opening it. “Don’t you get up then,” Nastasya went on compassionately, seeing that he was letting his feet down from the sofa. “You’re ill, and so don’t go; there’s no such hurry. What have you got there?”\n\nHe looked; in his right hand he held the shreds he had cut from his trousers, the sock, and the rags of the pocket. So he had been asleep with them in his hand. Afterwards reflecting upon it, he remembered that half waking up in his fever, he had grasped all this tightly in his hand and so fallen asleep again.\n\n“Look at the rags he’s collected and sleeps with them, as though he has got hold of a treasure...”\n\nAnd Nastasya went off into her hysterical giggle.\n\nInstantly he thrust them all under his great coat and fixed his eyes intently upon her. Far as he was from being capable of rational reflection at that moment, he felt that no one would behave like that with a person who was going to be arrested. “But... the police?”\n\n“You’d better have some tea! Yes? I’ll bring it, there’s some left.”\n\n“No... I’m going; I’ll go at once,” he muttered, getting on to his feet.\n\n“Why, you’ll never get downstairs!”\n\n“Yes, I’ll go.”\n\n“As you please.”\n\nShe followed the porter out.\n\nAt once he rushed to the light to examine the sock and the rags.\n\n“There are stains, but not very noticeable; all covered with dirt, and rubbed and already discoloured. No one who had no suspicion could distinguish anything. Nastasya from a distance could not have noticed, thank God!” Then with a tremor he broke the seal of the notice and began reading; he was a long while reading, before he understood. It was an ordinary summons from the district police-station to appear that day at half-past nine at the office of the district superintendent.\n\n“But when has such a thing happened? I never have anything to do with the police! And why just to-day?” he thought in agonising bewilderment. “Good God, only get it over soon!”\n\nHe was flinging himself on his knees to pray, but broke into laughter--not at the idea of prayer, but at himself.\n\nHe began, hurriedly dressing. “If I’m lost, I am lost, I don’t care! Shall I put the sock on?” he suddenly wondered, “it will get dustier still and the traces will be gone.”\n\nBut no sooner had he put it on than he pulled it off again in loathing and horror. He pulled it off, but reflecting that he had no other socks, he picked it up and put it on again--and again he laughed.\n\n“That’s all conventional, that’s all relative, merely a way of looking at it,” he thought in a flash, but only on the top surface of his mind, while he was shuddering all over, “there, I’ve got it on! I have finished by getting it on!”\n\nBut his laughter was quickly followed by despair.\n\n“No, it’s too much for me...” he thought. His legs shook. “From fear,” he muttered. His head swam and ached with fever. “It’s a trick! They want to decoy me there and confound me over everything,” he mused, as he went out on to the stairs--“the worst of it is I’m almost light-headed... I may blurt out something stupid...”\n\nOn the stairs he remembered that he was leaving all the things just as they were in the hole in the wall, “and very likely, it’s on purpose to search when I’m out,” he thought, and stopped short. But he was possessed by such despair, such cynicism of misery, if one may so call it, that with a wave of his hand he went on. “Only to get it over!”\n\nIn the street the heat was insufferable again; not a drop of rain had fallen all those days. Again dust, bricks and mortar, again the stench from the shops and pot-houses, again the drunken men, the Finnish pedlars and half-broken-down cabs. The sun shone straight in his eyes, so that it hurt him to look out of them, and he felt his head going round--as a man in a fever is apt to feel when he comes out into the street on a bright sunny day.\n\nWhen he reached the turning into _the_ street, in an agony of trepidation he looked down it... at _the_ house... and at once averted his eyes.\n\n“If they question me, perhaps I’ll simply tell,” he thought, as he drew near the police-station.\n\nThe police-station was about a quarter of a mile off. It had lately been moved to new rooms on the fourth floor of a new house. He had been once for a moment in the old office but long ago. Turning in at the gateway, he saw on the right a flight of stairs which a peasant was mounting with a book in his hand. “A house-porter, no doubt; so then, the office is here,” and he began ascending the stairs on the chance. He did not want to ask questions of anyone.\n\n“I’ll go in, fall on my knees, and confess everything...” he thought, as he reached the fourth floor.\n\nThe staircase was steep, narrow and all sloppy with dirty water. The kitchens of the flats opened on to the stairs and stood open almost the whole day. So there was a fearful smell and heat. The staircase was crowded with porters going up and down with their books under their arms, policemen, and persons of all sorts and both sexes. The door of the office, too, stood wide open. Peasants stood waiting within. There, too, the heat was stifling and there was a sickening smell of fresh paint and stale oil from the newly decorated rooms.\n\nAfter waiting a little, he decided to move forward into the next room. All the rooms were small and low-pitched. A fearful impatience drew him on and on. No one paid attention to him. In the second room some clerks sat writing, dressed hardly better than he was, and rather a queer-looking set. He went up to one of them.\n\n“What is it?”\n\nHe showed the notice he had received.\n\n“You are a student?” the man asked, glancing at the notice.\n\n“Yes, formerly a student.”\n\nThe clerk looked at him, but without the slightest interest. He was a particularly unkempt person with the look of a fixed idea in his eye.\n\n“There would be no getting anything out of him, because he has no interest in anything,” thought Raskolnikov.\n\n“Go in there to the head clerk,” said the clerk, pointing towards the furthest room.\n\nHe went into that room--the fourth in order; it was a small room and packed full of people, rather better dressed than in the outer rooms. Among them were two ladies. One, poorly dressed in mourning, sat at the table opposite the chief clerk, writing something at his dictation. The other, a very stout, buxom woman with a purplish-red, blotchy face, excessively smartly dressed with a brooch on her bosom as big as a saucer, was standing on one side, apparently waiting for something. Raskolnikov thrust his notice upon the head clerk. The latter glanced at it, said: “Wait a minute,” and went on attending to the lady in mourning.\n\nHe breathed more freely. “It can’t be that!”\n\nBy degrees he began to regain confidence, he kept urging himself to have courage and be calm.\n\n“Some foolishness, some trifling carelessness, and I may betray myself! Hm... it’s a pity there’s no air here,” he added, “it’s stifling.... It makes one’s head dizzier than ever... and one’s mind too...”\n\nHe was conscious of a terrible inner turmoil. He was afraid of losing his self-control; he tried to catch at something and fix his mind on it, something quite irrelevant, but he could not succeed in this at all. Yet the head clerk greatly interested him, he kept hoping to see through him and guess something from his face.\n\nHe was a very young man, about two and twenty, with a dark mobile face that looked older than his years. He was fashionably dressed and foppish, with his hair parted in the middle, well combed and pomaded, and wore a number of rings on his well-scrubbed fingers and a gold chain on his waistcoat. He said a couple of words in French to a foreigner who was in the room, and said them fairly correctly.\n\n“Luise Ivanovna, you can sit down,” he said casually to the gaily-dressed, purple-faced lady, who was still standing as though not venturing to sit down, though there was a chair beside her.\n\n“Ich danke,” said the latter, and softly, with a rustle of silk she sank into the chair. Her light blue dress trimmed with white lace floated about the table like an air-balloon and filled almost half the room. She smelt of scent. But she was obviously embarrassed at filling half the room and smelling so strongly of scent; and though her smile was impudent as well as cringing, it betrayed evident uneasiness.\n\nThe lady in mourning had done at last, and got up. All at once, with some noise, an officer walked in very jauntily, with a peculiar swing of his shoulders at each step. He tossed his cockaded cap on the table and sat down in an easy-chair. The small lady positively skipped from her seat on seeing him, and fell to curtsying in a sort of ecstasy; but the officer took not the smallest notice of her, and she did not venture to sit down again in his presence. He was the assistant superintendent. He had a reddish moustache that stood out horizontally on each side of his face, and extremely small features, expressive of nothing much except a certain insolence. He looked askance and rather indignantly at Raskolnikov; he was so very badly dressed, and in spite of his humiliating position, his bearing was by no means in keeping with his clothes. Raskolnikov had unwarily fixed a very long and direct look on him, so that he felt positively affronted.\n\n“What do you want?” he shouted, apparently astonished that such a ragged fellow was not annihilated by the majesty of his glance.\n\n“I was summoned... by a notice...” Raskolnikov faltered.\n\n“For the recovery of money due, from _the student_,” the head clerk interfered hurriedly, tearing himself from his papers. “Here!” and he flung Raskolnikov a document and pointed out the place. “Read that!”\n\n“Money? What money?” thought Raskolnikov, “but... then... it’s certainly not _that_.”\n\nAnd he trembled with joy. He felt sudden intense indescribable relief. A load was lifted from his back.\n\n“And pray, what time were you directed to appear, sir?” shouted the assistant superintendent, seeming for some unknown reason more and more aggrieved. “You are told to come at nine, and now it’s twelve!”\n\n“The notice was only brought me a quarter of an hour ago,” Raskolnikov answered loudly over his shoulder. To his own surprise he, too, grew suddenly angry and found a certain pleasure in it. “And it’s enough that I have come here ill with fever.”\n\n“Kindly refrain from shouting!”\n\n“I’m not shouting, I’m speaking very quietly, it’s you who are shouting at me. I’m a student, and allow no one to shout at me.”\n\nThe assistant superintendent was so furious that for the first minute he could only splutter inarticulately. He leaped up from his seat.\n\n“Be silent! You are in a government office. Don’t be impudent, sir!”\n\n“You’re in a government office, too,” cried Raskolnikov, “and you’re smoking a cigarette as well as shouting, so you are showing disrespect to all of us.”\n\nHe felt an indescribable satisfaction at having said this.\n\nThe head clerk looked at him with a smile. The angry assistant superintendent was obviously disconcerted.\n\n“That’s not your business!” he shouted at last with unnatural loudness. “Kindly make the declaration demanded of you. Show him. Alexandr Grigorievitch. There is a complaint against you! You don’t pay your debts! You’re a fine bird!”\n\nBut Raskolnikov was not listening now; he had eagerly clutched at the paper, in haste to find an explanation. He read it once, and a second time, and still did not understand.\n\n“What is this?” he asked the head clerk.\n\n“It is for the recovery of money on an I O U, a writ. You must either pay it, with all expenses, costs and so on, or give a written declaration when you can pay it, and at the same time an undertaking not to leave the capital without payment, and nor to sell or conceal your property. The creditor is at liberty to sell your property, and proceed against you according to the law.”\n\n“But I... am not in debt to anyone!”\n\n“That’s not our business. Here, an I O U for a hundred and fifteen roubles, legally attested, and due for payment, has been brought us for recovery, given by you to the widow of the assessor Zarnitsyn, nine months ago, and paid over by the widow Zarnitsyn to one Mr. Tchebarov. We therefore summon you, hereupon.”\n\n“But she is my landlady!”\n\n“And what if she is your landlady?”\n\nThe head clerk looked at him with a condescending smile of compassion, and at the same time with a certain triumph, as at a novice under fire for the first time--as though he would say: “Well, how do you feel now?” But what did he care now for an I O U, for a writ of recovery! Was that worth worrying about now, was it worth attention even! He stood, he read, he listened, he answered, he even asked questions himself, but all mechanically. The triumphant sense of security, of deliverance from overwhelming danger, that was what filled his whole soul that moment without thought for the future, without analysis, without suppositions or surmises, without doubts and without questioning. It was an instant of full, direct, purely instinctive joy. But at that very moment something like a thunderstorm took place in the office. The assistant superintendent, still shaken by Raskolnikov’s disrespect, still fuming and obviously anxious to keep up his wounded dignity, pounced on the unfortunate smart lady, who had been gazing at him ever since he came in with an exceedingly silly smile.\n\n“You shameful hussy!” he shouted suddenly at the top of his voice. (The lady in mourning had left the office.) “What was going on at your house last night? Eh! A disgrace again, you’re a scandal to the whole street. Fighting and drinking again. Do you want the house of correction? Why, I have warned you ten times over that I would not let you off the eleventh! And here you are again, again, you... you...!”\n\nThe paper fell out of Raskolnikov’s hands, and he looked wildly at the smart lady who was so unceremoniously treated. But he soon saw what it meant, and at once began to find positive amusement in the scandal. He listened with pleasure, so that he longed to laugh and laugh... all his nerves were on edge.\n\n“Ilya Petrovitch!” the head clerk was beginning anxiously, but stopped short, for he knew from experience that the enraged assistant could not be stopped except by force.\n\nAs for the smart lady, at first she positively trembled before the storm. But, strange to say, the more numerous and violent the terms of abuse became, the more amiable she looked, and the more seductive the smiles she lavished on the terrible assistant. She moved uneasily, and curtsied incessantly, waiting impatiently for a chance of putting in her word: and at last she found it.\n\n“There was no sort of noise or fighting in my house, Mr. Captain,” she pattered all at once, like peas dropping, speaking Russian confidently, though with a strong German accent, “and no sort of scandal, and his honour came drunk, and it’s the whole truth I am telling, Mr. Captain, and I am not to blame.... Mine is an honourable house, Mr. Captain, and honourable behaviour, Mr. Captain, and I always, always dislike any scandal myself. But he came quite tipsy, and asked for three bottles again, and then he lifted up one leg, and began playing the pianoforte with one foot, and that is not at all right in an honourable house, and he _ganz_ broke the piano, and it was very bad manners indeed and I said so. And he took up a bottle and began hitting everyone with it. And then I called the porter, and Karl came, and he took Karl and hit him in the eye; and he hit Henriette in the eye, too, and gave me five slaps on the cheek. And it was so ungentlemanly in an honourable house, Mr. Captain, and I screamed. And he opened the window over the canal, and stood in the window, squealing like a little pig; it was a disgrace. The idea of squealing like a little pig at the window into the street! Fie upon him! And Karl pulled him away from the window by his coat, and it is true, Mr. Captain, he tore _sein rock_. And then he shouted that _man muss_ pay him fifteen roubles damages. And I did pay him, Mr. Captain, five roubles for _sein rock_. And he is an ungentlemanly visitor and caused all the scandal. ‘I will show you up,’ he said, ‘for I can write to all the papers about you.’”\n\n“Then he was an author?”\n\n“Yes, Mr. Captain, and what an ungentlemanly visitor in an honourable house....”\n\n“Now then! Enough! I have told you already...”\n\n“Ilya Petrovitch!” the head clerk repeated significantly.\n\nThe assistant glanced rapidly at him; the head clerk slightly shook his head.\n\n“... So I tell you this, most respectable Luise Ivanovna, and I tell it you for the last time,” the assistant went on. “If there is a scandal in your honourable house once again, I will put you yourself in the lock-up, as it is called in polite society. Do you hear? So a literary man, an author took five roubles for his coat-tail in an ‘honourable house’? A nice set, these authors!”\n\nAnd he cast a contemptuous glance at Raskolnikov. “There was a scandal the other day in a restaurant, too. An author had eaten his dinner and would not pay; ‘I’ll write a satire on you,’ says he. And there was another of them on a steamer last week used the most disgraceful language to the respectable family of a civil councillor, his wife and daughter. And there was one of them turned out of a confectioner’s shop the other day. They are like that, authors, literary men, students, town-criers.... Pfoo! You get along! I shall look in upon you myself one day. Then you had better be careful! Do you hear?”\n\nWith hurried deference, Luise Ivanovna fell to curtsying in all directions, and so curtsied herself to the door. But at the door, she stumbled backwards against a good-looking officer with a fresh, open face and splendid thick fair whiskers. This was the superintendent of the district himself, Nikodim Fomitch. Luise Ivanovna made haste to curtsy almost to the ground, and with mincing little steps, she fluttered out of the office.\n\n“Again thunder and lightning--a hurricane!” said Nikodim Fomitch to Ilya Petrovitch in a civil and friendly tone. “You are aroused again, you are fuming again! I heard it on the stairs!”\n\n“Well, what then!” Ilya Petrovitch drawled with gentlemanly nonchalance; and he walked with some papers to another table, with a jaunty swing of his shoulders at each step. “Here, if you will kindly look: an author, or a student, has been one at least, does not pay his debts, has given an I O U, won’t clear out of his room, and complaints are constantly being lodged against him, and here he has been pleased to make a protest against my smoking in his presence! He behaves like a cad himself, and just look at him, please. Here’s the gentleman, and very attractive he is!”\n\n“Poverty is not a vice, my friend, but we know you go off like powder, you can’t bear a slight, I daresay you took offence at something and went too far yourself,” continued Nikodim Fomitch, turning affably to Raskolnikov. “But you were wrong there; he is a capital fellow, I assure you, but explosive, explosive! He gets hot, fires up, boils over, and no stopping him! And then it’s all over! And at the bottom he’s a heart of gold! His nickname in the regiment was the Explosive Lieutenant....”\n\n“And what a regiment it was, too,” cried Ilya Petrovitch, much gratified at this agreeable banter, though still sulky.\n\nRaskolnikov had a sudden desire to say something exceptionally pleasant to them all. “Excuse me, Captain,” he began easily, suddenly addressing Nikodim Fomitch, “will you enter into my position?... I am ready to ask pardon, if I have been ill-mannered. I am a poor student, sick and shattered (shattered was the word he used) by poverty. I am not studying, because I cannot keep myself now, but I shall get money.... I have a mother and sister in the province of X. They will send it to me, and I will pay. My landlady is a good-hearted woman, but she is so exasperated at my having lost my lessons, and not paying her for the last four months, that she does not even send up my dinner... and I don’t understand this I O U at all. She is asking me to pay her on this I O U. How am I to pay her? Judge for yourselves!...”\n\n“But that is not our business, you know,” the head clerk was observing.\n\n“Yes, yes. I perfectly agree with you. But allow me to explain...” Raskolnikov put in again, still addressing Nikodim Fomitch, but trying his best to address Ilya Petrovitch also, though the latter persistently appeared to be rummaging among his papers and to be contemptuously oblivious of him. “Allow me to explain that I have been living with her for nearly three years and at first... at first... for why should I not confess it, at the very beginning I promised to marry her daughter, it was a verbal promise, freely given... she was a girl... indeed, I liked her, though I was not in love with her... a youthful affair in fact... that is, I mean to say, that my landlady gave me credit freely in those days, and I led a life of... I was very heedless...”\n\n“Nobody asks you for these personal details, sir, we’ve no time to waste,” Ilya Petrovitch interposed roughly and with a note of triumph; but Raskolnikov stopped him hotly, though he suddenly found it exceedingly difficult to speak.\n\n“But excuse me, excuse me. It is for me to explain... how it all happened... In my turn... though I agree with you... it is unnecessary. But a year ago, the girl died of typhus. I remained lodging there as before, and when my landlady moved into her present quarters, she said to me... and in a friendly way... that she had complete trust in me, but still, would I not give her an I O U for one hundred and fifteen roubles, all the debt I owed her. She said if only I gave her that, she would trust me again, as much as I liked, and that she would never, never--those were her own words--make use of that I O U till I could pay of myself... and now, when I have lost my lessons and have nothing to eat, she takes action against me. What am I to say to that?”\n\n“All these affecting details are no business of ours.” Ilya Petrovitch interrupted rudely. “You must give a written undertaking but as for your love affairs and all these tragic events, we have nothing to do with that.”\n\n“Come now... you are harsh,” muttered Nikodim Fomitch, sitting down at the table and also beginning to write. He looked a little ashamed.\n\n“Write!” said the head clerk to Raskolnikov.\n\n“Write what?” the latter asked, gruffly.\n\n“I will dictate to you.”\n\nRaskolnikov fancied that the head clerk treated him more casually and contemptuously after his speech, but strange to say he suddenly felt completely indifferent to anyone’s opinion, and this revulsion took place in a flash, in one instant. If he had cared to think a little, he would have been amazed indeed that he could have talked to them like that a minute before, forcing his feelings upon them. And where had those feelings come from? Now if the whole room had been filled, not with police officers, but with those nearest and dearest to him, he would not have found one human word for them, so empty was his heart. A gloomy sensation of agonising, everlasting solitude and remoteness, took conscious form in his soul. It was not the meanness of his sentimental effusions before Ilya Petrovitch, nor the meanness of the latter’s triumph over him that had caused this sudden revulsion in his heart. Oh, what had he to do now with his own baseness, with all these petty vanities, officers, German women, debts, police-offices? If he had been sentenced to be burnt at that moment, he would not have stirred, would hardly have heard the sentence to the end. Something was happening to him entirely new, sudden and unknown. It was not that he understood, but he felt clearly with all the intensity of sensation that he could never more appeal to these people in the police-office with sentimental effusions like his recent outburst, or with anything whatever; and that if they had been his own brothers and sisters and not police-officers, it would have been utterly out of the question to appeal to them in any circumstance of life. He had never experienced such a strange and awful sensation. And what was most agonising--it was more a sensation than a conception or idea, a direct sensation, the most agonising of all the sensations he had known in his life.\n\nThe head clerk began dictating to him the usual form of declaration, that he could not pay, that he undertook to do so at a future date, that he would not leave the town, nor sell his property, and so on.\n\n“But you can’t write, you can hardly hold the pen,” observed the head clerk, looking with curiosity at Raskolnikov. “Are you ill?”\n\n“Yes, I am giddy. Go on!”\n\n“That’s all. Sign it.”\n\nThe head clerk took the paper, and turned to attend to others.\n\nRaskolnikov gave back the pen; but instead of getting up and going away, he put his elbows on the table and pressed his head in his hands. He felt as if a nail were being driven into his skull. A strange idea suddenly occurred to him, to get up at once, to go up to Nikodim Fomitch, and tell him everything that had happened yesterday, and then to go with him to his lodgings and to show him the things in the hole in the corner. The impulse was so strong that he got up from his seat to carry it out. “Hadn’t I better think a minute?” flashed through his mind. “No, better cast off the burden without thinking.” But all at once he stood still, rooted to the spot. Nikodim Fomitch was talking eagerly with Ilya Petrovitch, and the words reached him:\n\n“It’s impossible, they’ll both be released. To begin with, the whole story contradicts itself. Why should they have called the porter, if it had been their doing? To inform against themselves? Or as a blind? No, that would be too cunning! Besides, Pestryakov, the student, was seen at the gate by both the porters and a woman as he went in. He was walking with three friends, who left him only at the gate, and he asked the porters to direct him, in the presence of the friends. Now, would he have asked his way if he had been going with such an object? As for Koch, he spent half an hour at the silversmith’s below, before he went up to the old woman and he left him at exactly a quarter to eight. Now just consider...”\n\n“But excuse me, how do you explain this contradiction? They state themselves that they knocked and the door was locked; yet three minutes later when they went up with the porter, it turned out the door was unfastened.”\n\n“That’s just it; the murderer must have been there and bolted himself in; and they’d have caught him for a certainty if Koch had not been an ass and gone to look for the porter too. _He_ must have seized the interval to get downstairs and slip by them somehow. Koch keeps crossing himself and saying: ‘If I had been there, he would have jumped out and killed me with his axe.’ He is going to have a thanksgiving service--ha, ha!”\n\n“And no one saw the murderer?”\n\n“They might well not see him; the house is a regular Noah’s Ark,” said the head clerk, who was listening.\n\n“It’s clear, quite clear,” Nikodim Fomitch repeated warmly.\n\n“No, it is anything but clear,” Ilya Petrovitch maintained.\n\nRaskolnikov picked up his hat and walked towards the door, but he did not reach it....\n\nWhen he recovered consciousness, he found himself sitting in a chair, supported by someone on the right side, while someone else was standing on the left, holding a yellowish glass filled with yellow water, and Nikodim Fomitch standing before him, looking intently at him. He got up from the chair.\n\n“What’s this? Are you ill?” Nikodim Fomitch asked, rather sharply.\n\n“He could hardly hold his pen when he was signing,” said the head clerk, settling back in his place, and taking up his work again.\n\n“Have you been ill long?” cried Ilya Petrovitch from his place, where he, too, was looking through papers. He had, of course, come to look at the sick man when he fainted, but retired at once when he recovered.\n\n“Since yesterday,” muttered Raskolnikov in reply.\n\n“Did you go out yesterday?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Though you were ill?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“At what time?”\n\n“About seven.”\n\n“And where did you go, may I ask?”\n\n“Along the street.”\n\n“Short and clear.”\n\nRaskolnikov, white as a handkerchief, had answered sharply, jerkily, without dropping his black feverish eyes before Ilya Petrovitch’s stare.\n\n“He can scarcely stand upright. And you...” Nikodim Fomitch was beginning.\n\n“No matter,” Ilya Petrovitch pronounced rather peculiarly.\n\nNikodim Fomitch would have made some further protest, but glancing at the head clerk who was looking very hard at him, he did not speak. There was a sudden silence. It was strange.\n\n“Very well, then,” concluded Ilya Petrovitch, “we will not detain you.”\n\nRaskolnikov went out. He caught the sound of eager conversation on his departure, and above the rest rose the questioning voice of Nikodim Fomitch. In the street, his faintness passed off completely.\n\n“A search--there will be a search at once,” he repeated to himself, hurrying home. “The brutes! they suspect.”\n\nHis former terror mastered him completely again.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“And what if there has been a search already? What if I find them in my room?”\n\nBut here was his room. Nothing and no one in it. No one had peeped in. Even Nastasya had not touched it. But heavens! how could he have left all those things in the hole?\n\nHe rushed to the corner, slipped his hand under the paper, pulled the things out and lined his pockets with them. There were eight articles in all: two little boxes with ear-rings or something of the sort, he hardly looked to see; then four small leather cases. There was a chain, too, merely wrapped in newspaper and something else in newspaper, that looked like a decoration.... He put them all in the different pockets of his overcoat, and the remaining pocket of his trousers, trying to conceal them as much as possible. He took the purse, too. Then he went out of his room, leaving the door open. He walked quickly and resolutely, and though he felt shattered, he had his senses about him. He was afraid of pursuit, he was afraid that in another half-hour, another quarter of an hour perhaps, instructions would be issued for his pursuit, and so at all costs, he must hide all traces before then. He must clear everything up while he still had some strength, some reasoning power left him.... Where was he to go?\n\nThat had long been settled: “Fling them into the canal, and all traces hidden in the water, the thing would be at an end.” So he had decided in the night of his delirium when several times he had had the impulse to get up and go away, to make haste, and get rid of it all. But to get rid of it, turned out to be a very difficult task. He wandered along the bank of the Ekaterininsky Canal for half an hour or more and looked several times at the steps running down to the water, but he could not think of carrying out his plan; either rafts stood at the steps’ edge, and women were washing clothes on them, or boats were moored there, and people were swarming everywhere. Moreover he could be seen and noticed from the banks on all sides; it would look suspicious for a man to go down on purpose, stop, and throw something into the water. And what if the boxes were to float instead of sinking? And of course they would. Even as it was, everyone he met seemed to stare and look round, as if they had nothing to do but to watch him. “Why is it, or can it be my fancy?” he thought.\n\nAt last the thought struck him that it might be better to go to the Neva. There were not so many people there, he would be less observed, and it would be more convenient in every way, above all it was further off. He wondered how he could have been wandering for a good half-hour, worried and anxious in this dangerous past without thinking of it before. And that half-hour he had lost over an irrational plan, simply because he had thought of it in delirium! He had become extremely absent and forgetful and he was aware of it. He certainly must make haste.\n\nHe walked towards the Neva along V---- Prospect, but on the way another idea struck him. “Why to the Neva? Would it not be better to go somewhere far off, to the Islands again, and there hide the things in some solitary place, in a wood or under a bush, and mark the spot perhaps?” And though he felt incapable of clear judgment, the idea seemed to him a sound one. But he was not destined to go there. For coming out of V---- Prospect towards the square, he saw on the left a passage leading between two blank walls to a courtyard. On the right hand, the blank unwhitewashed wall of a four-storied house stretched far into the court; on the left, a wooden hoarding ran parallel with it for twenty paces into the court, and then turned sharply to the left. Here was a deserted fenced-off place where rubbish of different sorts was lying. At the end of the court, the corner of a low, smutty, stone shed, apparently part of some workshop, peeped from behind the hoarding. It was probably a carriage builder’s or carpenter’s shed; the whole place from the entrance was black with coal dust. Here would be the place to throw it, he thought. Not seeing anyone in the yard, he slipped in, and at once saw near the gate a sink, such as is often put in yards where there are many workmen or cab-drivers; and on the hoarding above had been scribbled in chalk the time-honoured witticism, “Standing here strictly forbidden.” This was all the better, for there would be nothing suspicious about his going in. “Here I could throw it all in a heap and get away!”\n\nLooking round once more, with his hand already in his pocket, he noticed against the outer wall, between the entrance and the sink, a big unhewn stone, weighing perhaps sixty pounds. The other side of the wall was a street. He could hear passers-by, always numerous in that part, but he could not be seen from the entrance, unless someone came in from the street, which might well happen indeed, so there was need of haste.\n\nHe bent down over the stone, seized the top of it firmly in both hands, and using all his strength turned it over. Under the stone was a small hollow in the ground, and he immediately emptied his pocket into it. The purse lay at the top, and yet the hollow was not filled up. Then he seized the stone again and with one twist turned it back, so that it was in the same position again, though it stood a very little higher. But he scraped the earth about it and pressed it at the edges with his foot. Nothing could be noticed.\n\nThen he went out, and turned into the square. Again an intense, almost unbearable joy overwhelmed him for an instant, as it had in the police-office. “I have buried my tracks! And who, who can think of looking under that stone? It has been lying there most likely ever since the house was built, and will lie as many years more. And if it were found, who would think of me? It is all over! No clue!” And he laughed. Yes, he remembered that he began laughing a thin, nervous noiseless laugh, and went on laughing all the time he was crossing the square. But when he reached the K---- Boulevard where two days before he had come upon that girl, his laughter suddenly ceased. Other ideas crept into his mind. He felt all at once that it would be loathsome to pass that seat on which after the girl was gone, he had sat and pondered, and that it would be hateful, too, to meet that whiskered policeman to whom he had given the twenty copecks: “Damn him!”\n\nHe walked, looking about him angrily and distractedly. All his ideas now seemed to be circling round some single point, and he felt that there really was such a point, and that now, now, he was left facing that point--and for the first time, indeed, during the last two months.\n\n“Damn it all!” he thought suddenly, in a fit of ungovernable fury. “If it has begun, then it has begun. Hang the new life! Good Lord, how stupid it is!... And what lies I told to-day! How despicably I fawned upon that wretched Ilya Petrovitch! But that is all folly! What do I care for them all, and my fawning upon them! It is not that at all! It is not that at all!”\n\nSuddenly he stopped; a new utterly unexpected and exceedingly simple question perplexed and bitterly confounded him.\n\n“If it all has really been done deliberately and not idiotically, if I really had a certain and definite object, how is it I did not even glance into the purse and don’t know what I had there, for which I have undergone these agonies, and have deliberately undertaken this base, filthy degrading business? And here I wanted at once to throw into the water the purse together with all the things which I had not seen either... how’s that?”\n\nYes, that was so, that was all so. Yet he had known it all before, and it was not a new question for him, even when it was decided in the night without hesitation and consideration, as though so it must be, as though it could not possibly be otherwise.... Yes, he had known it all, and understood it all; it surely had all been settled even yesterday at the moment when he was bending over the box and pulling the jewel-cases out of it.... Yes, so it was.\n\n“It is because I am very ill,” he decided grimly at last, “I have been worrying and fretting myself, and I don’t know what I am doing.... Yesterday and the day before yesterday and all this time I have been worrying myself.... I shall get well and I shall not worry.... But what if I don’t get well at all? Good God, how sick I am of it all!”\n\nHe walked on without resting. He had a terrible longing for some distraction, but he did not know what to do, what to attempt. A new overwhelming sensation was gaining more and more mastery over him every moment; this was an immeasurable, almost physical, repulsion for everything surrounding him, an obstinate, malignant feeling of hatred. All who met him were loathsome to him--he loathed their faces, their movements, their gestures. If anyone had addressed him, he felt that he might have spat at him or bitten him....\n\nHe stopped suddenly, on coming out on the bank of the Little Neva, near the bridge to Vassilyevsky Ostrov. “Why, he lives here, in that house,” he thought, “why, I have not come to Razumihin of my own accord! Here it’s the same thing over again.... Very interesting to know, though; have I come on purpose or have I simply walked here by chance? Never mind, I said the day before yesterday that I would go and see him the day _after_; well, and so I will! Besides I really cannot go further now.”\n\nHe went up to Razumihin’s room on the fifth floor.\n\nThe latter was at home in his garret, busily writing at the moment, and he opened the door himself. It was four months since they had seen each other. Razumihin was sitting in a ragged dressing-gown, with slippers on his bare feet, unkempt, unshaven and unwashed. His face showed surprise.\n\n“Is it you?” he cried. He looked his comrade up and down; then after a brief pause, he whistled. “As hard up as all that! Why, brother, you’ve cut me out!” he added, looking at Raskolnikov’s rags. “Come sit down, you are tired, I’ll be bound.”\n\nAnd when he had sunk down on the American leather sofa, which was in even worse condition than his own, Razumihin saw at once that his visitor was ill.\n\n“Why, you are seriously ill, do you know that?” He began feeling his pulse. Raskolnikov pulled away his hand.\n\n“Never mind,” he said, “I have come for this: I have no lessons.... I wanted,... but I don’t really want lessons....”\n\n“But I say! You are delirious, you know!” Razumihin observed, watching him carefully.\n\n“No, I am not.”\n\nRaskolnikov got up from the sofa. As he had mounted the stairs to Razumihin’s, he had not realised that he would be meeting his friend face to face. Now, in a flash, he knew, that what he was least of all disposed for at that moment was to be face to face with anyone in the wide world. His spleen rose within him. He almost choked with rage at himself as soon as he crossed Razumihin’s threshold.\n\n“Good-bye,” he said abruptly, and walked to the door.\n\n“Stop, stop! You queer fish.”\n\n“I don’t want to,” said the other, again pulling away his hand.\n\n“Then why the devil have you come? Are you mad, or what? Why, this is... almost insulting! I won’t let you go like that.”\n\n“Well, then, I came to you because I know no one but you who could help... to begin... because you are kinder than anyone--cleverer, I mean, and can judge... and now I see that I want nothing. Do you hear? Nothing at all... no one’s services... no one’s sympathy. I am by myself... alone. Come, that’s enough. Leave me alone.”\n\n“Stay a minute, you sweep! You are a perfect madman. As you like for all I care. I have no lessons, do you see, and I don’t care about that, but there’s a bookseller, Heruvimov--and he takes the place of a lesson. I would not exchange him for five lessons. He’s doing publishing of a kind, and issuing natural science manuals and what a circulation they have! The very titles are worth the money! You always maintained that I was a fool, but by Jove, my boy, there are greater fools than I am! Now he is setting up for being advanced, not that he has an inkling of anything, but, of course, I encourage him. Here are two signatures of the German text--in my opinion, the crudest charlatanism; it discusses the question, ‘Is woman a human being?’ And, of course, triumphantly proves that she is. Heruvimov is going to bring out this work as a contribution to the woman question; I am translating it; he will expand these two and a half signatures into six, we shall make up a gorgeous title half a page long and bring it out at half a rouble. It will do! He pays me six roubles the signature, it works out to about fifteen roubles for the job, and I’ve had six already in advance. When we have finished this, we are going to begin a translation about whales, and then some of the dullest scandals out of the second part of _Les Confessions_ we have marked for translation; somebody has told Heruvimov, that Rousseau was a kind of Radishchev. You may be sure I don’t contradict him, hang him! Well, would you like to do the second signature of ‘_Is woman a human being?_’ If you would, take the German and pens and paper--all those are provided, and take three roubles; for as I have had six roubles in advance on the whole thing, three roubles come to you for your share. And when you have finished the signature there will be another three roubles for you. And please don’t think I am doing you a service; quite the contrary, as soon as you came in, I saw how you could help me; to begin with, I am weak in spelling, and secondly, I am sometimes utterly adrift in German, so that I make it up as I go along for the most part. The only comfort is, that it’s bound to be a change for the better. Though who can tell, maybe it’s sometimes for the worse. Will you take it?”\n\nRaskolnikov took the German sheets in silence, took the three roubles and without a word went out. Razumihin gazed after him in astonishment. But when Raskolnikov was in the next street, he turned back, mounted the stairs to Razumihin’s again and laying on the table the German article and the three roubles, went out again, still without uttering a word.\n\n“Are you raving, or what?” Razumihin shouted, roused to fury at last. “What farce is this? You’ll drive me crazy too... what did you come to see me for, damn you?”\n\n“I don’t want... translation,” muttered Raskolnikov from the stairs.\n\n“Then what the devil do you want?” shouted Razumihin from above. Raskolnikov continued descending the staircase in silence.\n\n“Hey, there! Where are you living?”\n\nNo answer.\n\n“Well, confound you then!”\n\nBut Raskolnikov was already stepping into the street. On the Nikolaevsky Bridge he was roused to full consciousness again by an unpleasant incident. A coachman, after shouting at him two or three times, gave him a violent lash on the back with his whip, for having almost fallen under his horses’ hoofs. The lash so infuriated him that he dashed away to the railing (for some unknown reason he had been walking in the very middle of the bridge in the traffic). He angrily clenched and ground his teeth. He heard laughter, of course.\n\n“Serves him right!”\n\n“A pickpocket I dare say.”\n\n“Pretending to be drunk, for sure, and getting under the wheels on purpose; and you have to answer for him.”\n\n“It’s a regular profession, that’s what it is.”\n\nBut while he stood at the railing, still looking angry and bewildered after the retreating carriage, and rubbing his back, he suddenly felt someone thrust money into his hand. He looked. It was an elderly woman in a kerchief and goatskin shoes, with a girl, probably her daughter, wearing a hat, and carrying a green parasol.\n\n“Take it, my good man, in Christ’s name.”\n\nHe took it and they passed on. It was a piece of twenty copecks. From his dress and appearance they might well have taken him for a beggar asking alms in the streets, and the gift of the twenty copecks he doubtless owed to the blow, which made them feel sorry for him.\n\nHe closed his hand on the twenty copecks, walked on for ten paces, and turned facing the Neva, looking towards the palace. The sky was without a cloud and the water was almost bright blue, which is so rare in the Neva. The cupola of the cathedral, which is seen at its best from the bridge about twenty paces from the chapel, glittered in the sunlight, and in the pure air every ornament on it could be clearly distinguished. The pain from the lash went off, and Raskolnikov forgot about it; one uneasy and not quite definite idea occupied him now completely. He stood still, and gazed long and intently into the distance; this spot was especially familiar to him. When he was attending the university, he had hundreds of times--generally on his way home--stood still on this spot, gazed at this truly magnificent spectacle and almost always marvelled at a vague and mysterious emotion it roused in him. It left him strangely cold; this gorgeous picture was for him blank and lifeless. He wondered every time at his sombre and enigmatic impression and, mistrusting himself, put off finding the explanation of it. He vividly recalled those old doubts and perplexities, and it seemed to him that it was no mere chance that he recalled them now. It struck him as strange and grotesque, that he should have stopped at the same spot as before, as though he actually imagined he could think the same thoughts, be interested in the same theories and pictures that had interested him... so short a time ago. He felt it almost amusing, and yet it wrung his heart. Deep down, hidden far away out of sight all that seemed to him now--all his old past, his old thoughts, his old problems and theories, his old impressions and that picture and himself and all, all.... He felt as though he were flying upwards, and everything were vanishing from his sight. Making an unconscious movement with his hand, he suddenly became aware of the piece of money in his fist. He opened his hand, stared at the coin, and with a sweep of his arm flung it into the water; then he turned and went home. It seemed to him, he had cut himself off from everyone and from everything at that moment.\n\nEvening was coming on when he reached home, so that he must have been walking about six hours. How and where he came back he did not remember. Undressing, and quivering like an overdriven horse, he lay down on the sofa, drew his greatcoat over him, and at once sank into oblivion....\n\nIt was dusk when he was waked up by a fearful scream. Good God, what a scream! Such unnatural sounds, such howling, wailing, grinding, tears, blows and curses he had never heard.\n\nHe could never have imagined such brutality, such frenzy. In terror he sat up in bed, almost swooning with agony. But the fighting, wailing and cursing grew louder and louder. And then to his intense amazement he caught the voice of his landlady. She was howling, shrieking and wailing, rapidly, hurriedly, incoherently, so that he could not make out what she was talking about; she was beseeching, no doubt, not to be beaten, for she was being mercilessly beaten on the stairs. The voice of her assailant was so horrible from spite and rage that it was almost a croak; but he, too, was saying something, and just as quickly and indistinctly, hurrying and spluttering. All at once Raskolnikov trembled; he recognised the voice--it was the voice of Ilya Petrovitch. Ilya Petrovitch here and beating the landlady! He is kicking her, banging her head against the steps--that’s clear, that can be told from the sounds, from the cries and the thuds. How is it, is the world topsy-turvy? He could hear people running in crowds from all the storeys and all the staircases; he heard voices, exclamations, knocking, doors banging. “But why, why, and how could it be?” he repeated, thinking seriously that he had gone mad. But no, he heard too distinctly! And they would come to him then next, “for no doubt... it’s all about that... about yesterday.... Good God!” He would have fastened his door with the latch, but he could not lift his hand... besides, it would be useless. Terror gripped his heart like ice, tortured him and numbed him.... But at last all this uproar, after continuing about ten minutes, began gradually to subside. The landlady was moaning and groaning; Ilya Petrovitch was still uttering threats and curses.... But at last he, too, seemed to be silent, and now he could not be heard. “Can he have gone away? Good Lord!” Yes, and now the landlady is going too, still weeping and moaning... and then her door slammed.... Now the crowd was going from the stairs to their rooms, exclaiming, disputing, calling to one another, raising their voices to a shout, dropping them to a whisper. There must have been numbers of them--almost all the inmates of the block. “But, good God, how could it be! And why, why had he come here!”\n\nRaskolnikov sank worn out on the sofa, but could not close his eyes. He lay for half an hour in such anguish, such an intolerable sensation of infinite terror as he had never experienced before. Suddenly a bright light flashed into his room. Nastasya came in with a candle and a plate of soup. Looking at him carefully and ascertaining that he was not asleep, she set the candle on the table and began to lay out what she had brought--bread, salt, a plate, a spoon.\n\n“You’ve eaten nothing since yesterday, I warrant. You’ve been trudging about all day, and you’re shaking with fever.”\n\n“Nastasya... what were they beating the landlady for?”\n\nShe looked intently at him.\n\n“Who beat the landlady?”\n\n“Just now... half an hour ago, Ilya Petrovitch, the assistant superintendent, on the stairs.... Why was he ill-treating her like that, and... why was he here?”\n\nNastasya scrutinised him, silent and frowning, and her scrutiny lasted a long time. He felt uneasy, even frightened at her searching eyes.\n\n“Nastasya, why don’t you speak?” he said timidly at last in a weak voice.\n\n“It’s the blood,” she answered at last softly, as though speaking to herself.\n\n“Blood? What blood?” he muttered, growing white and turning towards the wall.\n\nNastasya still looked at him without speaking.\n\n“Nobody has been beating the landlady,” she declared at last in a firm, resolute voice.\n\nHe gazed at her, hardly able to breathe.\n\n“I heard it myself.... I was not asleep... I was sitting up,” he said still more timidly. “I listened a long while. The assistant superintendent came.... Everyone ran out on to the stairs from all the flats.”\n\n“No one has been here. That’s the blood crying in your ears. When there’s no outlet for it and it gets clotted, you begin fancying things.... Will you eat something?”\n\nHe made no answer. Nastasya still stood over him, watching him.\n\n“Give me something to drink... Nastasya.”\n\nShe went downstairs and returned with a white earthenware jug of water. He remembered only swallowing one sip of the cold water and spilling some on his neck. Then followed forgetfulness.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nHe was not completely unconscious, however, all the time he was ill; he was in a feverish state, sometimes delirious, sometimes half conscious. He remembered a great deal afterwards. Sometimes it seemed as though there were a number of people round him; they wanted to take him away somewhere, there was a great deal of squabbling and discussing about him. Then he would be alone in the room; they had all gone away afraid of him, and only now and then opened the door a crack to look at him; they threatened him, plotted something together, laughed, and mocked at him. He remembered Nastasya often at his bedside; he distinguished another person, too, whom he seemed to know very well, though he could not remember who he was, and this fretted him, even made him cry. Sometimes he fancied he had been lying there a month; at other times it all seemed part of the same day. But of _that_--of _that_ he had no recollection, and yet every minute he felt that he had forgotten something he ought to remember. He worried and tormented himself trying to remember, moaned, flew into a rage, or sank into awful, intolerable terror. Then he struggled to get up, would have run away, but someone always prevented him by force, and he sank back into impotence and forgetfulness. At last he returned to complete consciousness.\n\nIt happened at ten o’clock in the morning. On fine days the sun shone into the room at that hour, throwing a streak of light on the right wall and the corner near the door. Nastasya was standing beside him with another person, a complete stranger, who was looking at him very inquisitively. He was a young man with a beard, wearing a full, short-waisted coat, and looked like a messenger. The landlady was peeping in at the half-opened door. Raskolnikov sat up.\n\n“Who is this, Nastasya?” he asked, pointing to the young man.\n\n“I say, he’s himself again!” she said.\n\n“He is himself,” echoed the man.\n\nConcluding that he had returned to his senses, the landlady closed the door and disappeared. She was always shy and dreaded conversations or discussions. She was a woman of forty, not at all bad-looking, fat and buxom, with black eyes and eyebrows, good-natured from fatness and laziness, and absurdly bashful.\n\n“Who... are you?” he went on, addressing the man. But at that moment the door was flung open, and, stooping a little, as he was so tall, Razumihin came in.\n\n“What a cabin it is!” he cried. “I am always knocking my head. You call this a lodging! So you are conscious, brother? I’ve just heard the news from Pashenka.”\n\n“He has just come to,” said Nastasya.\n\n“Just come to,” echoed the man again, with a smile.\n\n“And who are you?” Razumihin asked, suddenly addressing him. “My name is Vrazumihin, at your service; not Razumihin, as I am always called, but Vrazumihin, a student and gentleman; and he is my friend. And who are you?”\n\n“I am the messenger from our office, from the merchant Shelopaev, and I’ve come on business.”\n\n“Please sit down.” Razumihin seated himself on the other side of the table. “It’s a good thing you’ve come to, brother,” he went on to Raskolnikov. “For the last four days you have scarcely eaten or drunk anything. We had to give you tea in spoonfuls. I brought Zossimov to see you twice. You remember Zossimov? He examined you carefully and said at once it was nothing serious--something seemed to have gone to your head. Some nervous nonsense, the result of bad feeding, he says you have not had enough beer and radish, but it’s nothing much, it will pass and you will be all right. Zossimov is a first-rate fellow! He is making quite a name. Come, I won’t keep you,” he said, addressing the man again. “Will you explain what you want? You must know, Rodya, this is the second time they have sent from the office; but it was another man last time, and I talked to him. Who was it came before?”\n\n“That was the day before yesterday, I venture to say, if you please, sir. That was Alexey Semyonovitch; he is in our office, too.”\n\n“He was more intelligent than you, don’t you think so?”\n\n“Yes, indeed, sir, he is of more weight than I am.”\n\n“Quite so; go on.”\n\n“At your mamma’s request, through Afanasy Ivanovitch Vahrushin, of whom I presume you have heard more than once, a remittance is sent to you from our office,” the man began, addressing Raskolnikov. “If you are in an intelligible condition, I’ve thirty-five roubles to remit to you, as Semyon Semyonovitch has received from Afanasy Ivanovitch at your mamma’s request instructions to that effect, as on previous occasions. Do you know him, sir?”\n\n“Yes, I remember... Vahrushin,” Raskolnikov said dreamily.\n\n“You hear, he knows Vahrushin,” cried Razumihin. “He is in ‘an intelligible condition’! And I see you are an intelligent man too. Well, it’s always pleasant to hear words of wisdom.”\n\n“That’s the gentleman, Vahrushin, Afanasy Ivanovitch. And at the request of your mamma, who has sent you a remittance once before in the same manner through him, he did not refuse this time also, and sent instructions to Semyon Semyonovitch some days since to hand you thirty-five roubles in the hope of better to come.”\n\n“That ‘hoping for better to come’ is the best thing you’ve said, though ‘your mamma’ is not bad either. Come then, what do you say? Is he fully conscious, eh?”\n\n“That’s all right. If only he can sign this little paper.”\n\n“He can scrawl his name. Have you got the book?”\n\n“Yes, here’s the book.”\n\n“Give it to me. Here, Rodya, sit up. I’ll hold you. Take the pen and scribble ‘Raskolnikov’ for him. For just now, brother, money is sweeter to us than treacle.”\n\n“I don’t want it,” said Raskolnikov, pushing away the pen.\n\n“Not want it?”\n\n“I won’t sign it.”\n\n“How the devil can you do without signing it?”\n\n“I don’t want... the money.”\n\n“Don’t want the money! Come, brother, that’s nonsense, I bear witness. Don’t trouble, please, it’s only that he is on his travels again. But that’s pretty common with him at all times though.... You are a man of judgment and we will take him in hand, that is, more simply, take his hand and he will sign it. Here.”\n\n“But I can come another time.”\n\n“No, no. Why should we trouble you? You are a man of judgment.... Now, Rodya, don’t keep your visitor, you see he is waiting,” and he made ready to hold Raskolnikov’s hand in earnest.\n\n“Stop, I’ll do it alone,” said the latter, taking the pen and signing his name.\n\nThe messenger took out the money and went away.\n\n“Bravo! And now, brother, are you hungry?”\n\n“Yes,” answered Raskolnikov.\n\n“Is there any soup?”\n\n“Some of yesterday’s,” answered Nastasya, who was still standing there.\n\n“With potatoes and rice in it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I know it by heart. Bring soup and give us some tea.”\n\n“Very well.”\n\nRaskolnikov looked at all this with profound astonishment and a dull, unreasoning terror. He made up his mind to keep quiet and see what would happen. “I believe I am not wandering. I believe it’s reality,” he thought.\n\nIn a couple of minutes Nastasya returned with the soup, and announced that the tea would be ready directly. With the soup she brought two spoons, two plates, salt, pepper, mustard for the beef, and so on. The table was set as it had not been for a long time. The cloth was clean.\n\n“It would not be amiss, Nastasya, if Praskovya Pavlovna were to send us up a couple of bottles of beer. We could empty them.”\n\n“Well, you are a cool hand,” muttered Nastasya, and she departed to carry out his orders.\n\nRaskolnikov still gazed wildly with strained attention. Meanwhile Razumihin sat down on the sofa beside him, as clumsily as a bear put his left arm round Raskolnikov’s head, although he was able to sit up, and with his right hand gave him a spoonful of soup, blowing on it that it might not burn him. But the soup was only just warm. Raskolnikov swallowed one spoonful greedily, then a second, then a third. But after giving him a few more spoonfuls of soup, Razumihin suddenly stopped, and said that he must ask Zossimov whether he ought to have more.\n\nNastasya came in with two bottles of beer.\n\n“And will you have tea?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Cut along, Nastasya, and bring some tea, for tea we may venture on without the faculty. But here is the beer!” He moved back to his chair, pulled the soup and meat in front of him, and began eating as though he had not touched food for three days.\n\n“I must tell you, Rodya, I dine like this here every day now,” he mumbled with his mouth full of beef, “and it’s all Pashenka, your dear little landlady, who sees to that; she loves to do anything for me. I don’t ask for it, but, of course, I don’t object. And here’s Nastasya with the tea. She is a quick girl. Nastasya, my dear, won’t you have some beer?”\n\n“Get along with your nonsense!”\n\n“A cup of tea, then?”\n\n“A cup of tea, maybe.”\n\n“Pour it out. Stay, I’ll pour it out myself. Sit down.”\n\nHe poured out two cups, left his dinner, and sat on the sofa again. As before, he put his left arm round the sick man’s head, raised him up and gave him tea in spoonfuls, again blowing each spoonful steadily and earnestly, as though this process was the principal and most effective means towards his friend’s recovery. Raskolnikov said nothing and made no resistance, though he felt quite strong enough to sit up on the sofa without support and could not merely have held a cup or a spoon, but even perhaps could have walked about. But from some queer, almost animal, cunning he conceived the idea of hiding his strength and lying low for a time, pretending if necessary not to be yet in full possession of his faculties, and meanwhile listening to find out what was going on. Yet he could not overcome his sense of repugnance. After sipping a dozen spoonfuls of tea, he suddenly released his head, pushed the spoon away capriciously, and sank back on the pillow. There were actually real pillows under his head now, down pillows in clean cases, he observed that, too, and took note of it.\n\n“Pashenka must give us some raspberry jam to-day to make him some raspberry tea,” said Razumihin, going back to his chair and attacking his soup and beer again.\n\n“And where is she to get raspberries for you?” asked Nastasya, balancing a saucer on her five outspread fingers and sipping tea through a lump of sugar.\n\n“She’ll get it at the shop, my dear. You see, Rodya, all sorts of things have been happening while you have been laid up. When you decamped in that rascally way without leaving your address, I felt so angry that I resolved to find you out and punish you. I set to work that very day. How I ran about making inquiries for you! This lodging of yours I had forgotten, though I never remembered it, indeed, because I did not know it; and as for your old lodgings, I could only remember it was at the Five Corners, Harlamov’s house. I kept trying to find that Harlamov’s house, and afterwards it turned out that it was not Harlamov’s, but Buch’s. How one muddles up sound sometimes! So I lost my temper, and I went on the chance to the address bureau next day, and only fancy, in two minutes they looked you up! Your name is down there.”\n\n“My name!”\n\n“I should think so; and yet a General Kobelev they could not find while I was there. Well, it’s a long story. But as soon as I did land on this place, I soon got to know all your affairs--all, all, brother, I know everything; Nastasya here will tell you. I made the acquaintance of Nikodim Fomitch and Ilya Petrovitch, and the house-porter and Mr. Zametov, Alexandr Grigorievitch, the head clerk in the police office, and, last, but not least, of Pashenka; Nastasya here knows....”\n\n“He’s got round her,” Nastasya murmured, smiling slyly.\n\n“Why don’t you put the sugar in your tea, Nastasya Nikiforovna?”\n\n“You are a one!” Nastasya cried suddenly, going off into a giggle. “I am not Nikiforovna, but Petrovna,” she added suddenly, recovering from her mirth.\n\n“I’ll make a note of it. Well, brother, to make a long story short, I was going in for a regular explosion here to uproot all malignant influences in the locality, but Pashenka won the day. I had not expected, brother, to find her so... prepossessing. Eh, what do you think?”\n\nRaskolnikov did not speak, but he still kept his eyes fixed upon him, full of alarm.\n\n“And all that could be wished, indeed, in every respect,” Razumihin went on, not at all embarrassed by his silence.\n\n“Ah, the sly dog!” Nastasya shrieked again. This conversation afforded her unspeakable delight.\n\n“It’s a pity, brother, that you did not set to work in the right way at first. You ought to have approached her differently. She is, so to speak, a most unaccountable character. But we will talk about her character later.... How could you let things come to such a pass that she gave up sending you your dinner? And that I O U? You must have been mad to sign an I O U. And that promise of marriage when her daughter, Natalya Yegorovna, was alive?... I know all about it! But I see that’s a delicate matter and I am an ass; forgive me. But, talking of foolishness, do you know Praskovya Pavlovna is not nearly so foolish as you would think at first sight?”\n\n“No,” mumbled Raskolnikov, looking away, but feeling that it was better to keep up the conversation.\n\n“She isn’t, is she?” cried Razumihin, delighted to get an answer out of him. “But she is not very clever either, eh? She is essentially, essentially an unaccountable character! I am sometimes quite at a loss, I assure you.... She must be forty; she says she is thirty-six, and of course she has every right to say so. But I swear I judge her intellectually, simply from the metaphysical point of view; there is a sort of symbolism sprung up between us, a sort of algebra or what not! I don’t understand it! Well, that’s all nonsense. Only, seeing that you are not a student now and have lost your lessons and your clothes, and that through the young lady’s death she has no need to treat you as a relation, she suddenly took fright; and as you hid in your den and dropped all your old relations with her, she planned to get rid of you. And she’s been cherishing that design a long time, but was sorry to lose the I O U, for you assured her yourself that your mother would pay.”\n\n“It was base of me to say that.... My mother herself is almost a beggar... and I told a lie to keep my lodging... and be fed,” Raskolnikov said loudly and distinctly.\n\n“Yes, you did very sensibly. But the worst of it is that at that point Mr. Tchebarov turns up, a business man. Pashenka would never have thought of doing anything on her own account, she is too retiring; but the business man is by no means retiring, and first thing he puts the question, ‘Is there any hope of realising the I O U?’ Answer: there is, because he has a mother who would save her Rodya with her hundred and twenty-five roubles pension, if she has to starve herself; and a sister, too, who would go into bondage for his sake. That’s what he was building upon.... Why do you start? I know all the ins and outs of your affairs now, my dear boy--it’s not for nothing that you were so open with Pashenka when you were her prospective son-in-law, and I say all this as a friend.... But I tell you what it is; an honest and sensitive man is open; and a business man ‘listens and goes on eating’ you up. Well, then she gave the I O U by way of payment to this Tchebarov, and without hesitation he made a formal demand for payment. When I heard of all this I wanted to blow him up, too, to clear my conscience, but by that time harmony reigned between me and Pashenka, and I insisted on stopping the whole affair, engaging that you would pay. I went security for you, brother. Do you understand? We called Tchebarov, flung him ten roubles and got the I O U back from him, and here I have the honour of presenting it to you. She trusts your word now. Here, take it, you see I have torn it.”\n\nRazumihin put the note on the table. Raskolnikov looked at him and turned to the wall without uttering a word. Even Razumihin felt a twinge.\n\n“I see, brother,” he said a moment later, “that I have been playing the fool again. I thought I should amuse you with my chatter, and I believe I have only made you cross.”\n\n“Was it you I did not recognise when I was delirious?” Raskolnikov asked, after a moment’s pause without turning his head.\n\n“Yes, and you flew into a rage about it, especially when I brought Zametov one day.”\n\n“Zametov? The head clerk? What for?” Raskolnikov turned round quickly and fixed his eyes on Razumihin.\n\n“What’s the matter with you?... What are you upset about? He wanted to make your acquaintance because I talked to him a lot about you.... How could I have found out so much except from him? He is a capital fellow, brother, first-rate... in his own way, of course. Now we are friends--see each other almost every day. I have moved into this part, you know. I have only just moved. I’ve been with him to Luise Ivanovna once or twice.... Do you remember Luise, Luise Ivanovna?”\n\n“Did I say anything in delirium?”\n\n“I should think so! You were beside yourself.”\n\n“What did I rave about?”\n\n“What next? What did you rave about? What people do rave about.... Well, brother, now I must not lose time. To work.” He got up from the table and took up his cap.\n\n“What did I rave about?”\n\n“How he keeps on! Are you afraid of having let out some secret? Don’t worry yourself; you said nothing about a countess. But you said a lot about a bulldog, and about ear-rings and chains, and about Krestovsky Island, and some porter, and Nikodim Fomitch and Ilya Petrovitch, the assistant superintendent. And another thing that was of special interest to you was your own sock. You whined, ‘Give me my sock.’ Zametov hunted all about your room for your socks, and with his own scented, ring-bedecked fingers he gave you the rag. And only then were you comforted, and for the next twenty-four hours you held the wretched thing in your hand; we could not get it from you. It is most likely somewhere under your quilt at this moment. And then you asked so piteously for fringe for your trousers. We tried to find out what sort of fringe, but we could not make it out. Now to business! Here are thirty-five roubles; I take ten of them, and shall give you an account of them in an hour or two. I will let Zossimov know at the same time, though he ought to have been here long ago, for it is nearly twelve. And you, Nastasya, look in pretty often while I am away, to see whether he wants a drink or anything else. And I will tell Pashenka what is wanted myself. Good-bye!”\n\n“He calls her Pashenka! Ah, he’s a deep one!” said Nastasya as he went out; then she opened the door and stood listening, but could not resist running downstairs after him. She was very eager to hear what he would say to the landlady. She was evidently quite fascinated by Razumihin.\n\nNo sooner had she left the room than the sick man flung off the bedclothes and leapt out of bed like a madman. With burning, twitching impatience he had waited for them to be gone so that he might set to work. But to what work? Now, as though to spite him, it eluded him.\n\n“Good God, only tell me one thing: do they know of it yet or not? What if they know it and are only pretending, mocking me while I am laid up, and then they will come in and tell me that it’s been discovered long ago and that they have only... What am I to do now? That’s what I’ve forgotten, as though on purpose; forgotten it all at once, I remembered a minute ago.”\n\nHe stood in the middle of the room and gazed in miserable bewilderment about him; he walked to the door, opened it, listened; but that was not what he wanted. Suddenly, as though recalling something, he rushed to the corner where there was a hole under the paper, began examining it, put his hand into the hole, fumbled--but that was not it. He went to the stove, opened it and began rummaging in the ashes; the frayed edges of his trousers and the rags cut off his pocket were lying there just as he had thrown them. No one had looked, then! Then he remembered the sock about which Razumihin had just been telling him. Yes, there it lay on the sofa under the quilt, but it was so covered with dust and grime that Zametov could not have seen anything on it.\n\n“Bah, Zametov! The police office! And why am I sent for to the police office? Where’s the notice? Bah! I am mixing it up; that was then. I looked at my sock then, too, but now... now I have been ill. But what did Zametov come for? Why did Razumihin bring him?” he muttered, helplessly sitting on the sofa again. “What does it mean? Am I still in delirium, or is it real? I believe it is real.... Ah, I remember; I must escape! Make haste to escape. Yes, I must, I must escape! Yes... but where? And where are my clothes? I’ve no boots. They’ve taken them away! They’ve hidden them! I understand! Ah, here is my coat--they passed that over! And here is money on the table, thank God! And here’s the I O U... I’ll take the money and go and take another lodging. They won’t find me!... Yes, but the address bureau? They’ll find me, Razumihin will find me. Better escape altogether... far away... to America, and let them do their worst! And take the I O U... it would be of use there.... What else shall I take? They think I am ill! They don’t know that I can walk, ha-ha-ha! I could see by their eyes that they know all about it! If only I could get downstairs! And what if they have set a watch there--policemen! What’s this tea? Ah, and here is beer left, half a bottle, cold!”\n\nHe snatched up the bottle, which still contained a glassful of beer, and gulped it down with relish, as though quenching a flame in his breast. But in another minute the beer had gone to his head, and a faint and even pleasant shiver ran down his spine. He lay down and pulled the quilt over him. His sick and incoherent thoughts grew more and more disconnected, and soon a light, pleasant drowsiness came upon him. With a sense of comfort he nestled his head into the pillow, wrapped more closely about him the soft, wadded quilt which had replaced the old, ragged greatcoat, sighed softly and sank into a deep, sound, refreshing sleep.\n\nHe woke up, hearing someone come in. He opened his eyes and saw Razumihin standing in the doorway, uncertain whether to come in or not. Raskolnikov sat up quickly on the sofa and gazed at him, as though trying to recall something.\n\n“Ah, you are not asleep! Here I am! Nastasya, bring in the parcel!” Razumihin shouted down the stairs. “You shall have the account directly.”\n\n“What time is it?” asked Raskolnikov, looking round uneasily.\n\n“Yes, you had a fine sleep, brother, it’s almost evening, it will be six o’clock directly. You have slept more than six hours.”\n\n“Good heavens! Have I?”\n\n“And why not? It will do you good. What’s the hurry? A tryst, is it? We’ve all time before us. I’ve been waiting for the last three hours for you; I’ve been up twice and found you asleep. I’ve called on Zossimov twice; not at home, only fancy! But no matter, he will turn up. And I’ve been out on my own business, too. You know I’ve been moving to-day, moving with my uncle. I have an uncle living with me now. But that’s no matter, to business. Give me the parcel, Nastasya. We will open it directly. And how do you feel now, brother?”\n\n“I am quite well, I am not ill. Razumihin, have you been here long?”\n\n“I tell you I’ve been waiting for the last three hours.”\n\n“No, before.”\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“How long have you been coming here?”\n\n“Why I told you all about it this morning. Don’t you remember?”\n\nRaskolnikov pondered. The morning seemed like a dream to him. He could not remember alone, and looked inquiringly at Razumihin.\n\n“Hm!” said the latter, “he has forgotten. I fancied then that you were not quite yourself. Now you are better for your sleep.... You really look much better. First-rate! Well, to business. Look here, my dear boy.”\n\nHe began untying the bundle, which evidently interested him.\n\n“Believe me, brother, this is something specially near my heart. For we must make a man of you. Let’s begin from the top. Do you see this cap?” he said, taking out of the bundle a fairly good though cheap and ordinary cap. “Let me try it on.”\n\n“Presently, afterwards,” said Raskolnikov, waving it off pettishly.\n\n“Come, Rodya, my boy, don’t oppose it, afterwards will be too late; and I shan’t sleep all night, for I bought it by guess, without measure. Just right!” he cried triumphantly, fitting it on, “just your size! A proper head-covering is the first thing in dress and a recommendation in its own way. Tolstyakov, a friend of mine, is always obliged to take off his pudding basin when he goes into any public place where other people wear their hats or caps. People think he does it from slavish politeness, but it’s simply because he is ashamed of his bird’s nest; he is such a boastful fellow! Look, Nastasya, here are two specimens of headgear: this Palmerston”--he took from the corner Raskolnikov’s old, battered hat, which for some unknown reason, he called a Palmerston--“or this jewel! Guess the price, Rodya, what do you suppose I paid for it, Nastasya!” he said, turning to her, seeing that Raskolnikov did not speak.\n\n“Twenty copecks, no more, I dare say,” answered Nastasya.\n\n“Twenty copecks, silly!” he cried, offended. “Why, nowadays you would cost more than that--eighty copecks! And that only because it has been worn. And it’s bought on condition that when’s it’s worn out, they will give you another next year. Yes, on my word! Well, now let us pass to the United States of America, as they called them at school. I assure you I am proud of these breeches,” and he exhibited to Raskolnikov a pair of light, summer trousers of grey woollen material. “No holes, no spots, and quite respectable, although a little worn; and a waistcoat to match, quite in the fashion. And its being worn really is an improvement, it’s softer, smoother.... You see, Rodya, to my thinking, the great thing for getting on in the world is always to keep to the seasons; if you don’t insist on having asparagus in January, you keep your money in your purse; and it’s the same with this purchase. It’s summer now, so I’ve been buying summer things--warmer materials will be wanted for autumn, so you will have to throw these away in any case... especially as they will be done for by then from their own lack of coherence if not your higher standard of luxury. Come, price them! What do you say? Two roubles twenty-five copecks! And remember the condition: if you wear these out, you will have another suit for nothing! They only do business on that system at Fedyaev’s; if you’ve bought a thing once, you are satisfied for life, for you will never go there again of your own free will. Now for the boots. What do you say? You see that they are a bit worn, but they’ll last a couple of months, for it’s foreign work and foreign leather; the secretary of the English Embassy sold them last week--he had only worn them six days, but he was very short of cash. Price--a rouble and a half. A bargain?”\n\n“But perhaps they won’t fit,” observed Nastasya.\n\n“Not fit? Just look!” and he pulled out of his pocket Raskolnikov’s old, broken boot, stiffly coated with dry mud. “I did not go empty-handed--they took the size from this monster. We all did our best. And as to your linen, your landlady has seen to that. Here, to begin with are three shirts, hempen but with a fashionable front.... Well now then, eighty copecks the cap, two roubles twenty-five copecks the suit--together three roubles five copecks--a rouble and a half for the boots--for, you see, they are very good--and that makes four roubles fifty-five copecks; five roubles for the underclothes--they were bought in the lot--which makes exactly nine roubles fifty-five copecks. Forty-five copecks change in coppers. Will you take it? And so, Rodya, you are set up with a complete new rig-out, for your overcoat will serve, and even has a style of its own. That comes from getting one’s clothes from Sharmer’s! As for your socks and other things, I leave them to you; we’ve twenty-five roubles left. And as for Pashenka and paying for your lodging, don’t you worry. I tell you she’ll trust you for anything. And now, brother, let me change your linen, for I daresay you will throw off your illness with your shirt.”\n\n“Let me be! I don’t want to!” Raskolnikov waved him off. He had listened with disgust to Razumihin’s efforts to be playful about his purchases.\n\n“Come, brother, don’t tell me I’ve been trudging around for nothing,” Razumihin insisted. “Nastasya, don’t be bashful, but help me--that’s it,” and in spite of Raskolnikov’s resistance he changed his linen. The latter sank back on the pillows and for a minute or two said nothing.\n\n“It will be long before I get rid of them,” he thought. “What money was all that bought with?” he asked at last, gazing at the wall.\n\n“Money? Why, your own, what the messenger brought from Vahrushin, your mother sent it. Have you forgotten that, too?”\n\n“I remember now,” said Raskolnikov after a long, sullen silence. Razumihin looked at him, frowning and uneasy.\n\nThe door opened and a tall, stout man whose appearance seemed familiar to Raskolnikov came in.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nZossimov was a tall, fat man with a puffy, colourless, clean-shaven face and straight flaxen hair. He wore spectacles, and a big gold ring on his fat finger. He was twenty-seven. He had on a light grey fashionable loose coat, light summer trousers, and everything about him loose, fashionable and spick and span; his linen was irreproachable, his watch-chain was massive. In manner he was slow and, as it were, nonchalant, and at the same time studiously free and easy; he made efforts to conceal his self-importance, but it was apparent at every instant. All his acquaintances found him tedious, but said he was clever at his work.\n\n“I’ve been to you twice to-day, brother. You see, he’s come to himself,” cried Razumihin.\n\n“I see, I see; and how do we feel now, eh?” said Zossimov to Raskolnikov, watching him carefully and, sitting down at the foot of the sofa, he settled himself as comfortably as he could.\n\n“He is still depressed,” Razumihin went on. “We’ve just changed his linen and he almost cried.”\n\n“That’s very natural; you might have put it off if he did not wish it.... His pulse is first-rate. Is your head still aching, eh?”\n\n“I am well, I am perfectly well!” Raskolnikov declared positively and irritably. He raised himself on the sofa and looked at them with glittering eyes, but sank back on to the pillow at once and turned to the wall. Zossimov watched him intently.\n\n“Very good.... Going on all right,” he said lazily. “Has he eaten anything?”\n\nThey told him, and asked what he might have.\n\n“He may have anything... soup, tea... mushrooms and cucumbers, of course, you must not give him; he’d better not have meat either, and... but no need to tell you that!” Razumihin and he looked at each other. “No more medicine or anything. I’ll look at him again to-morrow. Perhaps, to-day even... but never mind...”\n\n“To-morrow evening I shall take him for a walk,” said Razumihin. “We are going to the Yusupov garden and then to the Palais de Cristal.”\n\n“I would not disturb him to-morrow at all, but I don’t know... a little, maybe... but we’ll see.”\n\n“Ach, what a nuisance! I’ve got a house-warming party to-night; it’s only a step from here. Couldn’t he come? He could lie on the sofa. You are coming?” Razumihin said to Zossimov. “Don’t forget, you promised.”\n\n“All right, only rather later. What are you going to do?”\n\n“Oh, nothing--tea, vodka, herrings. There will be a pie... just our friends.”\n“And who?”\n\n“All neighbours here, almost all new friends, except my old uncle, and he is new too--he only arrived in Petersburg yesterday to see to some business of his. We meet once in five years.”\n\n“He’s been stagnating all his life as a district postmaster; gets a little pension. He is sixty-five--not worth talking about.... But I am fond of him. Porfiry Petrovitch, the head of the Investigation Department here... But you know him.”\n\n“Is he a relation of yours, too?”\n\n“A very distant one. But why are you scowling? Because you quarrelled once, won’t you come then?”\n\n“I don’t care a damn for him.”\n\n“So much the better. Well, there will be some students, a teacher, a government clerk, a musician, an officer and Zametov.”\n\n“Do tell me, please, what you or he”--Zossimov nodded at Raskolnikov--“can have in common with this Zametov?”\n\n“Oh, you particular gentleman! Principles! You are worked by principles, as it were by springs; you won’t venture to turn round on your own account. If a man is a nice fellow, that’s the only principle I go upon. Zametov is a delightful person.”\n\n“Though he does take bribes.”\n\n“Well, he does! and what of it? I don’t care if he does take bribes,” Razumihin cried with unnatural irritability. “I don’t praise him for taking bribes. I only say he is a nice man in his own way! But if one looks at men in all ways--are there many good ones left? Why, I am sure I shouldn’t be worth a baked onion myself... perhaps with you thrown in.”\n\n“That’s too little; I’d give two for you.”\n\n“And I wouldn’t give more than one for you. No more of your jokes! Zametov is no more than a boy. I can pull his hair and one must draw him not repel him. You’ll never improve a man by repelling him, especially a boy. One has to be twice as careful with a boy. Oh, you progressive dullards! You don’t understand. You harm yourselves running another man down.... But if you want to know, we really have something in common.”\n\n“I should like to know what.”\n\n“Why, it’s all about a house-painter.... We are getting him out of a mess! Though indeed there’s nothing to fear now. The matter is absolutely self-evident. We only put on steam.”\n\n“A painter?”\n\n“Why, haven’t I told you about it? I only told you the beginning then about the murder of the old pawnbroker-woman. Well, the painter is mixed up in it...”\n\n“Oh, I heard about that murder before and was rather interested in it... partly... for one reason.... I read about it in the papers, too....”\n\n“Lizaveta was murdered, too,” Nastasya blurted out, suddenly addressing Raskolnikov. She remained in the room all the time, standing by the door listening.\n\n“Lizaveta,” murmured Raskolnikov hardly audibly.\n\n“Lizaveta, who sold old clothes. Didn’t you know her? She used to come here. She mended a shirt for you, too.”\n\nRaskolnikov turned to the wall where in the dirty, yellow paper he picked out one clumsy, white flower with brown lines on it and began examining how many petals there were in it, how many scallops in the petals and how many lines on them. He felt his arms and legs as lifeless as though they had been cut off. He did not attempt to move, but stared obstinately at the flower.\n\n“But what about the painter?” Zossimov interrupted Nastasya’s chatter with marked displeasure. She sighed and was silent.\n\n“Why, he was accused of the murder,” Razumihin went on hotly.\n\n“Was there evidence against him then?”\n\n“Evidence, indeed! Evidence that was no evidence, and that’s what we have to prove. It was just as they pitched on those fellows, Koch and Pestryakov, at first. Foo! how stupidly it’s all done, it makes one sick, though it’s not one’s business! Pestryakov may be coming to-night.... By the way, Rodya, you’ve heard about the business already; it happened before you were ill, the day before you fainted at the police office while they were talking about it.”\n\nZossimov looked curiously at Raskolnikov. He did not stir.\n\n“But I say, Razumihin, I wonder at you. What a busybody you are!” Zossimov observed.\n\n“Maybe I am, but we will get him off anyway,” shouted Razumihin, bringing his fist down on the table. “What’s the most offensive is not their lying--one can always forgive lying--lying is a delightful thing, for it leads to truth--what is offensive is that they lie and worship their own lying.... I respect Porfiry, but... What threw them out at first? The door was locked, and when they came back with the porter it was open. So it followed that Koch and Pestryakov were the murderers--that was their logic!”\n\n“But don’t excite yourself; they simply detained them, they could not help that.... And, by the way, I’ve met that man Koch. He used to buy unredeemed pledges from the old woman? Eh?”\n\n“Yes, he is a swindler. He buys up bad debts, too. He makes a profession of it. But enough of him! Do you know what makes me angry? It’s their sickening rotten, petrified routine.... And this case might be the means of introducing a new method. One can show from the psychological data alone how to get on the track of the real man. ‘We have facts,’ they say. But facts are not everything--at least half the business lies in how you interpret them!”\n\n“Can you interpret them, then?”\n\n“Anyway, one can’t hold one’s tongue when one has a feeling, a tangible feeling, that one might be a help if only.... Eh! Do you know the details of the case?”\n\n“I am waiting to hear about the painter.”\n\n“Oh, yes! Well, here’s the story. Early on the third day after the murder, when they were still dandling Koch and Pestryakov--though they accounted for every step they took and it was as plain as a pikestaff--an unexpected fact turned up. A peasant called Dushkin, who keeps a dram-shop facing the house, brought to the police office a jeweller’s case containing some gold ear-rings, and told a long rigamarole. ‘The day before yesterday, just after eight o’clock’--mark the day and the hour!--‘a journeyman house-painter, Nikolay, who had been in to see me already that day, brought me this box of gold ear-rings and stones, and asked me to give him two roubles for them. When I asked him where he got them, he said that he picked them up in the street. I did not ask him anything more.’ I am telling you Dushkin’s story. ‘I gave him a note’--a rouble that is--‘for I thought if he did not pawn it with me he would with another. It would all come to the same thing--he’d spend it on drink, so the thing had better be with me. The further you hide it the quicker you will find it, and if anything turns up, if I hear any rumours, I’ll take it to the police.’ Of course, that’s all taradiddle; he lies like a horse, for I know this Dushkin, he is a pawnbroker and a receiver of stolen goods, and he did not cheat Nikolay out of a thirty-rouble trinket in order to give it to the police. He was simply afraid. But no matter, to return to Dushkin’s story. ‘I’ve known this peasant, Nikolay Dementyev, from a child; he comes from the same province and district of Zaraïsk, we are both Ryazan men. And though Nikolay is not a drunkard, he drinks, and I knew he had a job in that house, painting work with Dmitri, who comes from the same village, too. As soon as he got the rouble he changed it, had a couple of glasses, took his change and went out. But I did not see Dmitri with him then. And the next day I heard that someone had murdered Alyona Ivanovna and her sister, Lizaveta Ivanovna, with an axe. I knew them, and I felt suspicious about the ear-rings at once, for I knew the murdered woman lent money on pledges. I went to the house, and began to make careful inquiries without saying a word to anyone. First of all I asked, “Is Nikolay here?” Dmitri told me that Nikolay had gone off on the spree; he had come home at daybreak drunk, stayed in the house about ten minutes, and went out again. Dmitri didn’t see him again and is finishing the job alone. And their job is on the same staircase as the murder, on the second floor. When I heard all that I did not say a word to anyone’--that’s Dushkin’s tale--‘but I found out what I could about the murder, and went home feeling as suspicious as ever. And at eight o’clock this morning’--that was the third day, you understand--‘I saw Nikolay coming in, not sober, though not to say very drunk--he could understand what was said to him. He sat down on the bench and did not speak. There was only one stranger in the bar and a man I knew asleep on a bench and our two boys. “Have you seen Dmitri?” said I. “No, I haven’t,” said he. “And you’ve not been here either?” “Not since the day before yesterday,” said he. “And where did you sleep last night?” “In Peski, with the Kolomensky men.” “And where did you get those ear-rings?” I asked. “I found them in the street,” and the way he said it was a bit queer; he did not look at me. “Did you hear what happened that very evening, at that very hour, on that same staircase?” said I. “No,” said he, “I had not heard,” and all the while he was listening, his eyes were staring out of his head and he turned as white as chalk. I told him all about it and he took his hat and began getting up. I wanted to keep him. “Wait a bit, Nikolay,” said I, “won’t you have a drink?” And I signed to the boy to hold the door, and I came out from behind the bar; but he darted out and down the street to the turning at a run. I have not seen him since. Then my doubts were at an end--it was his doing, as clear as could be....’”\n\n“I should think so,” said Zossimov.\n\n“Wait! Hear the end. Of course they sought high and low for Nikolay; they detained Dushkin and searched his house; Dmitri, too, was arrested; the Kolomensky men also were turned inside out. And the day before yesterday they arrested Nikolay in a tavern at the end of the town. He had gone there, taken the silver cross off his neck and asked for a dram for it. They gave it to him. A few minutes afterwards the woman went to the cowshed, and through a crack in the wall she saw in the stable adjoining he had made a noose of his sash from the beam, stood on a block of wood, and was trying to put his neck in the noose. The woman screeched her hardest; people ran in. ‘So that’s what you are up to!’ ‘Take me,’ he says, ‘to such-and-such a police officer; I’ll confess everything.’ Well, they took him to that police station--that is here--with a suitable escort. So they asked him this and that, how old he is, ‘twenty-two,’ and so on. At the question, ‘When you were working with Dmitri, didn’t you see anyone on the staircase at such-and-such a time?’--answer: ‘To be sure folks may have gone up and down, but I did not notice them.’ ‘And didn’t you hear anything, any noise, and so on?’ ‘We heard nothing special.’ ‘And did you hear, Nikolay, that on the same day Widow So-and-so and her sister were murdered and robbed?’ ‘I never knew a thing about it. The first I heard of it was from Afanasy Pavlovitch the day before yesterday.’ ‘And where did you find the ear-rings?’ ‘I found them on the pavement.’ ‘Why didn’t you go to work with Dmitri the other day?’ ‘Because I was drinking.’ ‘And where were you drinking?’ ‘Oh, in such-and-such a place.’ ‘Why did you run away from Dushkin’s?’ ‘Because I was awfully frightened.’ ‘What were you frightened of?’ ‘That I should be accused.’ ‘How could you be frightened, if you felt free from guilt?’ Now, Zossimov, you may not believe me, that question was put literally in those words. I know it for a fact, it was repeated to me exactly! What do you say to that?”\n\n“Well, anyway, there’s the evidence.”\n\n“I am not talking of the evidence now, I am talking about that question, of their own idea of themselves. Well, so they squeezed and squeezed him and he confessed: ‘I did not find it in the street, but in the flat where I was painting with Dmitri.’ ‘And how was that?’ ‘Why, Dmitri and I were painting there all day, and we were just getting ready to go, and Dmitri took a brush and painted my face, and he ran off and I after him. I ran after him, shouting my hardest, and at the bottom of the stairs I ran right against the porter and some gentlemen--and how many gentlemen were there I don’t remember. And the porter swore at me, and the other porter swore, too, and the porter’s wife came out, and swore at us, too; and a gentleman came into the entry with a lady, and he swore at us, too, for Dmitri and I lay right across the way. I got hold of Dmitri’s hair and knocked him down and began beating him. And Dmitri, too, caught me by the hair and began beating me. But we did it all not for temper but in a friendly way, for sport. And then Dmitri escaped and ran into the street, and I ran after him; but I did not catch him, and went back to the flat alone; I had to clear up my things. I began putting them together, expecting Dmitri to come, and there in the passage, in the corner by the door, I stepped on the box. I saw it lying there wrapped up in paper. I took off the paper, saw some little hooks, undid them, and in the box were the ear-rings....’”\n\n“Behind the door? Lying behind the door? Behind the door?” Raskolnikov cried suddenly, staring with a blank look of terror at Razumihin, and he slowly sat up on the sofa, leaning on his hand.\n\n“Yes... why? What’s the matter? What’s wrong?” Razumihin, too, got up from his seat.\n\n“Nothing,” Raskolnikov answered faintly, turning to the wall. All were silent for a while.\n\n“He must have waked from a dream,” Razumihin said at last, looking inquiringly at Zossimov. The latter slightly shook his head.\n\n“Well, go on,” said Zossimov. “What next?”\n\n“What next? As soon as he saw the ear-rings, forgetting Dmitri and everything, he took up his cap and ran to Dushkin and, as we know, got a rouble from him. He told a lie saying he found them in the street, and went off drinking. He keeps repeating his old story about the murder: ‘I know nothing of it, never heard of it till the day before yesterday.’ ‘And why didn’t you come to the police till now?’ ‘I was frightened.’ ‘And why did you try to hang yourself?’ ‘From anxiety.’ ‘What anxiety?’ ‘That I should be accused of it.’ Well, that’s the whole story. And now what do you suppose they deduced from that?”\n\n“Why, there’s no supposing. There’s a clue, such as it is, a fact. You wouldn’t have your painter set free?”\n\n“Now they’ve simply taken him for the murderer. They haven’t a shadow of doubt.”\n\n“That’s nonsense. You are excited. But what about the ear-rings? You must admit that, if on the very same day and hour ear-rings from the old woman’s box have come into Nikolay’s hands, they must have come there somehow. That’s a good deal in such a case.”\n\n“How did they get there? How did they get there?” cried Razumihin. “How can you, a doctor, whose duty it is to study man and who has more opportunity than anyone else for studying human nature--how can you fail to see the character of the man in the whole story? Don’t you see at once that the answers he has given in the examination are the holy truth? They came into his hand precisely as he has told us--he stepped on the box and picked it up.”\n\n“The holy truth! But didn’t he own himself that he told a lie at first?”\n\n“Listen to me, listen attentively. The porter and Koch and Pestryakov and the other porter and the wife of the first porter and the woman who was sitting in the porter’s lodge and the man Kryukov, who had just got out of a cab at that minute and went in at the entry with a lady on his arm, that is eight or ten witnesses, agree that Nikolay had Dmitri on the ground, was lying on him beating him, while Dmitri hung on to his hair, beating him, too. They lay right across the way, blocking the thoroughfare. They were sworn at on all sides while they ‘like children’ (the very words of the witnesses) were falling over one another, squealing, fighting and laughing with the funniest faces, and, chasing one another like children, they ran into the street. Now take careful note. The bodies upstairs were warm, you understand, warm when they found them! If they, or Nikolay alone, had murdered them and broken open the boxes, or simply taken part in the robbery, allow me to ask you one question: do their state of mind, their squeals and giggles and childish scuffling at the gate fit in with axes, bloodshed, fiendish cunning, robbery? They’d just killed them, not five or ten minutes before, for the bodies were still warm, and at once, leaving the flat open, knowing that people would go there at once, flinging away their booty, they rolled about like children, laughing and attracting general attention. And there are a dozen witnesses to swear to that!”\n\n“Of course it is strange! It’s impossible, indeed, but...”\n\n“No, brother, no _buts_. And if the ear-rings being found in Nikolay’s hands at the very day and hour of the murder constitutes an important piece of circumstantial evidence against him--although the explanation given by him accounts for it, and therefore it does not tell seriously against him--one must take into consideration the facts which prove him innocent, especially as they are facts that _cannot be denied_. And do you suppose, from the character of our legal system, that they will accept, or that they are in a position to accept, this fact--resting simply on a psychological impossibility--as irrefutable and conclusively breaking down the circumstantial evidence for the prosecution? No, they won’t accept it, they certainly won’t, because they found the jewel-case and the man tried to hang himself, ‘which he could not have done if he hadn’t felt guilty.’ That’s the point, that’s what excites me, you must understand!”\n\n“Oh, I see you are excited! Wait a bit. I forgot to ask you; what proof is there that the box came from the old woman?”\n\n“That’s been proved,” said Razumihin with apparent reluctance, frowning. “Koch recognised the jewel-case and gave the name of the owner, who proved conclusively that it was his.”\n\n“That’s bad. Now another point. Did anyone see Nikolay at the time that Koch and Pestryakov were going upstairs at first, and is there no evidence about that?”\n\n“Nobody did see him,” Razumihin answered with vexation. “That’s the worst of it. Even Koch and Pestryakov did not notice them on their way upstairs, though, indeed, their evidence could not have been worth much. They said they saw the flat was open, and that there must be work going on in it, but they took no special notice and could not remember whether there actually were men at work in it.”\n\n“Hm!... So the only evidence for the defence is that they were beating one another and laughing. That constitutes a strong presumption, but... How do you explain the facts yourself?”\n\n“How do I explain them? What is there to explain? It’s clear. At any rate, the direction in which explanation is to be sought is clear, and the jewel-case points to it. The real murderer dropped those ear-rings. The murderer was upstairs, locked in, when Koch and Pestryakov knocked at the door. Koch, like an ass, did not stay at the door; so the murderer popped out and ran down, too; for he had no other way of escape. He hid from Koch, Pestryakov and the porter in the flat when Nikolay and Dmitri had just run out of it. He stopped there while the porter and others were going upstairs, waited till they were out of hearing, and then went calmly downstairs at the very minute when Dmitri and Nikolay ran out into the street and there was no one in the entry; possibly he was seen, but not noticed. There are lots of people going in and out. He must have dropped the ear-rings out of his pocket when he stood behind the door, and did not notice he dropped them, because he had other things to think of. The jewel-case is a conclusive proof that he did stand there.... That’s how I explain it.”\n\n“Too clever! No, my boy, you’re too clever. That beats everything.”\n\n“But, why, why?”\n\n“Why, because everything fits too well... it’s too melodramatic.”\n\n“A-ach!” Razumihin was exclaiming, but at that moment the door opened and a personage came in who was a stranger to all present.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThis was a gentleman no longer young, of a stiff and portly appearance, and a cautious and sour countenance. He began by stopping short in the doorway, staring about him with offensive and undisguised astonishment, as though asking himself what sort of place he had come to. Mistrustfully and with an affectation of being alarmed and almost affronted, he scanned Raskolnikov’s low and narrow “cabin.” With the same amazement he stared at Raskolnikov, who lay undressed, dishevelled, unwashed, on his miserable dirty sofa, looking fixedly at him. Then with the same deliberation he scrutinised the uncouth, unkempt figure and unshaven face of Razumihin, who looked him boldly and inquiringly in the face without rising from his seat. A constrained silence lasted for a couple of minutes, and then, as might be expected, some scene-shifting took place. Reflecting, probably from certain fairly unmistakable signs, that he would get nothing in this “cabin” by attempting to overawe them, the gentleman softened somewhat, and civilly, though with some severity, emphasising every syllable of his question, addressed Zossimov:\n\n“Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov, a student, or formerly a student?”\n\nZossimov made a slight movement, and would have answered, had not Razumihin anticipated him.\n\n“Here he is lying on the sofa! What do you want?”\n\nThis familiar “what do you want” seemed to cut the ground from the feet of the pompous gentleman. He was turning to Razumihin, but checked himself in time and turned to Zossimov again.\n\n“This is Raskolnikov,” mumbled Zossimov, nodding towards him. Then he gave a prolonged yawn, opening his mouth as wide as possible. Then he lazily put his hand into his waistcoat-pocket, pulled out a huge gold watch in a round hunter’s case, opened it, looked at it and as slowly and lazily proceeded to put it back.\n\nRaskolnikov himself lay without speaking, on his back, gazing persistently, though without understanding, at the stranger. Now that his face was turned away from the strange flower on the paper, it was extremely pale and wore a look of anguish, as though he had just undergone an agonising operation or just been taken from the rack. But the new-comer gradually began to arouse his attention, then his wonder, then suspicion and even alarm. When Zossimov said “This is Raskolnikov” he jumped up quickly, sat on the sofa and with an almost defiant, but weak and breaking, voice articulated:\n\n“Yes, I am Raskolnikov! What do you want?”\n\nThe visitor scrutinised him and pronounced impressively:\n\n“Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin. I believe I have reason to hope that my name is not wholly unknown to you?”\n\nBut Raskolnikov, who had expected something quite different, gazed blankly and dreamily at him, making no reply, as though he heard the name of Pyotr Petrovitch for the first time.\n\n“Is it possible that you can up to the present have received no information?” asked Pyotr Petrovitch, somewhat disconcerted.\n\nIn reply Raskolnikov sank languidly back on the pillow, put his hands behind his head and gazed at the ceiling. A look of dismay came into Luzhin’s face. Zossimov and Razumihin stared at him more inquisitively than ever, and at last he showed unmistakable signs of embarrassment.\n\n“I had presumed and calculated,” he faltered, “that a letter posted more than ten days, if not a fortnight ago...”\n\n“I say, why are you standing in the doorway?” Razumihin interrupted suddenly. “If you’ve something to say, sit down. Nastasya and you are so crowded. Nastasya, make room. Here’s a chair, thread your way in!”\n\nHe moved his chair back from the table, made a little space between the table and his knees, and waited in a rather cramped position for the visitor to “thread his way in.” The minute was so chosen that it was impossible to refuse, and the visitor squeezed his way through, hurrying and stumbling. Reaching the chair, he sat down, looking suspiciously at Razumihin.\n\n“No need to be nervous,” the latter blurted out. “Rodya has been ill for the last five days and delirious for three, but now he is recovering and has got an appetite. This is his doctor, who has just had a look at him. I am a comrade of Rodya’s, like him, formerly a student, and now I am nursing him; so don’t you take any notice of us, but go on with your business.”\n\n“Thank you. But shall I not disturb the invalid by my presence and conversation?” Pyotr Petrovitch asked of Zossimov.\n\n“N-no,” mumbled Zossimov; “you may amuse him.” He yawned again.\n\n“He has been conscious a long time, since the morning,” went on Razumihin, whose familiarity seemed so much like unaffected good-nature that Pyotr Petrovitch began to be more cheerful, partly, perhaps, because this shabby and impudent person had introduced himself as a student.\n\n“Your mamma,” began Luzhin.\n\n“Hm!” Razumihin cleared his throat loudly. Luzhin looked at him inquiringly.\n\n“That’s all right, go on.”\n\nLuzhin shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“Your mamma had commenced a letter to you while I was sojourning in her neighbourhood. On my arrival here I purposely allowed a few days to elapse before coming to see you, in order that I might be fully assured that you were in full possession of the tidings; but now, to my astonishment...”\n\n“I know, I know!” Raskolnikov cried suddenly with impatient vexation. “So you are the _fiancé_? I know, and that’s enough!”\n\nThere was no doubt about Pyotr Petrovitch’s being offended this time, but he said nothing. He made a violent effort to understand what it all meant. There was a moment’s silence.\n\nMeanwhile Raskolnikov, who had turned a little towards him when he answered, began suddenly staring at him again with marked curiosity, as though he had not had a good look at him yet, or as though something new had struck him; he rose from his pillow on purpose to stare at him. There certainly was something peculiar in Pyotr Petrovitch’s whole appearance, something which seemed to justify the title of “fiancé” so unceremoniously applied to him. In the first place, it was evident, far too much so indeed, that Pyotr Petrovitch had made eager use of his few days in the capital to get himself up and rig himself out in expectation of his betrothed--a perfectly innocent and permissible proceeding, indeed. Even his own, perhaps too complacent, consciousness of the agreeable improvement in his appearance might have been forgiven in such circumstances, seeing that Pyotr Petrovitch had taken up the rôle of fiancé. All his clothes were fresh from the tailor’s and were all right, except for being too new and too distinctly appropriate. Even the stylish new round hat had the same significance. Pyotr Petrovitch treated it too respectfully and held it too carefully in his hands. The exquisite pair of lavender gloves, real Louvain, told the same tale, if only from the fact of his not wearing them, but carrying them in his hand for show. Light and youthful colours predominated in Pyotr Petrovitch’s attire. He wore a charming summer jacket of a fawn shade, light thin trousers, a waistcoat of the same, new and fine linen, a cravat of the lightest cambric with pink stripes on it, and the best of it was, this all suited Pyotr Petrovitch. His very fresh and even handsome face looked younger than his forty-five years at all times. His dark, mutton-chop whiskers made an agreeable setting on both sides, growing thickly upon his shining, clean-shaven chin. Even his hair, touched here and there with grey, though it had been combed and curled at a hairdresser’s, did not give him a stupid appearance, as curled hair usually does, by inevitably suggesting a German on his wedding-day. If there really was something unpleasing and repulsive in his rather good-looking and imposing countenance, it was due to quite other causes. After scanning Mr. Luzhin unceremoniously, Raskolnikov smiled malignantly, sank back on the pillow and stared at the ceiling as before.\n\nBut Mr. Luzhin hardened his heart and seemed to determine to take no notice of their oddities.\n\n“I feel the greatest regret at finding you in this situation,” he began, again breaking the silence with an effort. “If I had been aware of your illness I should have come earlier. But you know what business is. I have, too, a very important legal affair in the Senate, not to mention other preoccupations which you may well conjecture. I am expecting your mamma and sister any minute.”\n\nRaskolnikov made a movement and seemed about to speak; his face showed some excitement. Pyotr Petrovitch paused, waited, but as nothing followed, he went on:\n\n“... Any minute. I have found a lodging for them on their arrival.”\n\n“Where?” asked Raskolnikov weakly.\n\n“Very near here, in Bakaleyev’s house.”\n\n“That’s in Voskresensky,” put in Razumihin. “There are two storeys of rooms, let by a merchant called Yushin; I’ve been there.”\n\n“Yes, rooms...”\n\n“A disgusting place--filthy, stinking and, what’s more, of doubtful character. Things have happened there, and there are all sorts of queer people living there. And I went there about a scandalous business. It’s cheap, though...”\n\n“I could not, of course, find out so much about it, for I am a stranger in Petersburg myself,” Pyotr Petrovitch replied huffily. “However, the two rooms are exceedingly clean, and as it is for so short a time... I have already taken a permanent, that is, our future flat,” he said, addressing Raskolnikov, “and I am having it done up. And meanwhile I am myself cramped for room in a lodging with my friend Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, in the flat of Madame Lippevechsel; it was he who told me of Bakaleyev’s house, too...”\n\n“Lebeziatnikov?” said Raskolnikov slowly, as if recalling something.\n\n“Yes, Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, a clerk in the Ministry. Do you know him?”\n\n“Yes... no,” Raskolnikov answered.\n\n“Excuse me, I fancied so from your inquiry. I was once his guardian.... A very nice young man and advanced. I like to meet young people: one learns new things from them.” Luzhin looked round hopefully at them all.\n\n“How do you mean?” asked Razumihin.\n\n“In the most serious and essential matters,” Pyotr Petrovitch replied, as though delighted at the question. “You see, it’s ten years since I visited Petersburg. All the novelties, reforms, ideas have reached us in the provinces, but to see it all more clearly one must be in Petersburg. And it’s my notion that you observe and learn most by watching the younger generation. And I confess I am delighted...”\n\n“At what?”\n\n“Your question is a wide one. I may be mistaken, but I fancy I find clearer views, more, so to say, criticism, more practicality...”\n\n“That’s true,” Zossimov let drop.\n\n“Nonsense! There’s no practicality.” Razumihin flew at him. “Practicality is a difficult thing to find; it does not drop down from heaven. And for the last two hundred years we have been divorced from all practical life. Ideas, if you like, are fermenting,” he said to Pyotr Petrovitch, “and desire for good exists, though it’s in a childish form, and honesty you may find, although there are crowds of brigands. Anyway, there’s no practicality. Practicality goes well shod.”\n\n“I don’t agree with you,” Pyotr Petrovitch replied, with evident enjoyment. “Of course, people do get carried away and make mistakes, but one must have indulgence; those mistakes are merely evidence of enthusiasm for the cause and of abnormal external environment. If little has been done, the time has been but short; of means I will not speak. It’s my personal view, if you care to know, that something has been accomplished already. New valuable ideas, new valuable works are circulating in the place of our old dreamy and romantic authors. Literature is taking a maturer form, many injurious prejudices have been rooted up and turned into ridicule.... In a word, we have cut ourselves off irrevocably from the past, and that, to my thinking, is a great thing...”\n\n“He’s learnt it by heart to show off!” Raskolnikov pronounced suddenly.\n\n“What?” asked Pyotr Petrovitch, not catching his words; but he received no reply.\n\n“That’s all true,” Zossimov hastened to interpose.\n\n“Isn’t it so?” Pyotr Petrovitch went on, glancing affably at Zossimov. “You must admit,” he went on, addressing Razumihin with a shade of triumph and superciliousness--he almost added “young man”--“that there is an advance, or, as they say now, progress in the name of science and economic truth...”\n\n“A commonplace.”\n\n“No, not a commonplace! Hitherto, for instance, if I were told, ‘love thy neighbour,’ what came of it?” Pyotr Petrovitch went on, perhaps with excessive haste. “It came to my tearing my coat in half to share with my neighbour and we both were left half naked. As a Russian proverb has it, ‘Catch several hares and you won’t catch one.’ Science now tells us, love yourself before all men, for everything in the world rests on self-interest. You love yourself and manage your own affairs properly and your coat remains whole. Economic truth adds that the better private affairs are organised in society--the more whole coats, so to say--the firmer are its foundations and the better is the common welfare organised too. Therefore, in acquiring wealth solely and exclusively for myself, I am acquiring, so to speak, for all, and helping to bring to pass my neighbour’s getting a little more than a torn coat; and that not from private, personal liberality, but as a consequence of the general advance. The idea is simple, but unhappily it has been a long time reaching us, being hindered by idealism and sentimentality. And yet it would seem to want very little wit to perceive it...”\n\n“Excuse me, I’ve very little wit myself,” Razumihin cut in sharply, “and so let us drop it. I began this discussion with an object, but I’ve grown so sick during the last three years of this chattering to amuse oneself, of this incessant flow of commonplaces, always the same, that, by Jove, I blush even when other people talk like that. You are in a hurry, no doubt, to exhibit your acquirements; and I don’t blame you, that’s quite pardonable. I only wanted to find out what sort of man you are, for so many unscrupulous people have got hold of the progressive cause of late and have so distorted in their own interests everything they touched, that the whole cause has been dragged in the mire. That’s enough!”\n\n“Excuse me, sir,” said Luzhin, affronted, and speaking with excessive dignity. “Do you mean to suggest so unceremoniously that I too...”\n\n“Oh, my dear sir... how could I?... Come, that’s enough,” Razumihin concluded, and he turned abruptly to Zossimov to continue their previous conversation.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch had the good sense to accept the disavowal. He made up his mind to take leave in another minute or two.\n\n“I trust our acquaintance,” he said, addressing Raskolnikov, “may, upon your recovery and in view of the circumstances of which you are aware, become closer... Above all, I hope for your return to health...”\n\nRaskolnikov did not even turn his head. Pyotr Petrovitch began getting up from his chair.\n\n“One of her customers must have killed her,” Zossimov declared positively.\n\n“Not a doubt of it,” replied Razumihin. “Porfiry doesn’t give his opinion, but is examining all who have left pledges with her there.”\n\n“Examining them?” Raskolnikov asked aloud.\n\n“Yes. What then?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“How does he get hold of them?” asked Zossimov.\n\n“Koch has given the names of some of them, other names are on the wrappers of the pledges and some have come forward of themselves.”\n\n“It must have been a cunning and practised ruffian! The boldness of it! The coolness!”\n\n“That’s just what it wasn’t!” interposed Razumihin. “That’s what throws you all off the scent. But I maintain that he is not cunning, not practised, and probably this was his first crime! The supposition that it was a calculated crime and a cunning criminal doesn’t work. Suppose him to have been inexperienced, and it’s clear that it was only a chance that saved him--and chance may do anything. Why, he did not foresee obstacles, perhaps! And how did he set to work? He took jewels worth ten or twenty roubles, stuffing his pockets with them, ransacked the old woman’s trunks, her rags--and they found fifteen hundred roubles, besides notes, in a box in the top drawer of the chest! He did not know how to rob; he could only murder. It was his first crime, I assure you, his first crime; he lost his head. And he got off more by luck than good counsel!”\n\n“You are talking of the murder of the old pawnbroker, I believe?” Pyotr Petrovitch put in, addressing Zossimov. He was standing, hat and gloves in hand, but before departing he felt disposed to throw off a few more intellectual phrases. He was evidently anxious to make a favourable impression and his vanity overcame his prudence.\n\n“Yes. You’ve heard of it?”\n\n“Oh, yes, being in the neighbourhood.”\n\n“Do you know the details?”\n\n“I can’t say that; but another circumstance interests me in the case--the whole question, so to say. Not to speak of the fact that crime has been greatly on the increase among the lower classes during the last five years, not to speak of the cases of robbery and arson everywhere, what strikes me as the strangest thing is that in the higher classes, too, crime is increasing proportionately. In one place one hears of a student’s robbing the mail on the high road; in another place people of good social position forge false banknotes; in Moscow of late a whole gang has been captured who used to forge lottery tickets, and one of the ringleaders was a lecturer in universal history; then our secretary abroad was murdered from some obscure motive of gain.... And if this old woman, the pawnbroker, has been murdered by someone of a higher class in society--for peasants don’t pawn gold trinkets--how are we to explain this demoralisation of the civilised part of our society?”\n\n“There are many economic changes,” put in Zossimov.\n\n“How are we to explain it?” Razumihin caught him up. “It might be explained by our inveterate impracticality.”\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“What answer had your lecturer in Moscow to make to the question why he was forging notes? ‘Everybody is getting rich one way or another, so I want to make haste to get rich too.’ I don’t remember the exact words, but the upshot was that he wants money for nothing, without waiting or working! We’ve grown used to having everything ready-made, to walking on crutches, to having our food chewed for us. Then the great hour struck,[*] and every man showed himself in his true colours.”\n\n[*] The emancipation of the serfs in 1861 is meant. --TRANSLATOR’S NOTE.\n\n“But morality? And so to speak, principles...”\n\n“But why do you worry about it?” Raskolnikov interposed suddenly. “It’s in accordance with your theory!”\n\n“In accordance with my theory?”\n\n“Why, carry out logically the theory you were advocating just now, and it follows that people may be killed...”\n\n“Upon my word!” cried Luzhin.\n\n“No, that’s not so,” put in Zossimov.\n\nRaskolnikov lay with a white face and twitching upper lip, breathing painfully.\n\n“There’s a measure in all things,” Luzhin went on superciliously. “Economic ideas are not an incitement to murder, and one has but to suppose...”\n\n“And is it true,” Raskolnikov interposed once more suddenly, again in a voice quivering with fury and delight in insulting him, “is it true that you told your _fiancée_... within an hour of her acceptance, that what pleased you most... was that she was a beggar... because it was better to raise a wife from poverty, so that you may have complete control over her, and reproach her with your being her benefactor?”\n\n“Upon my word,” Luzhin cried wrathfully and irritably, crimson with confusion, “to distort my words in this way! Excuse me, allow me to assure you that the report which has reached you, or rather, let me say, has been conveyed to you, has no foundation in truth, and I... suspect who... in a word... this arrow... in a word, your mamma... She seemed to me in other things, with all her excellent qualities, of a somewhat high-flown and romantic way of thinking.... But I was a thousand miles from supposing that she would misunderstand and misrepresent things in so fanciful a way.... And indeed... indeed...”\n\n“I tell you what,” cried Raskolnikov, raising himself on his pillow and fixing his piercing, glittering eyes upon him, “I tell you what.”\n\n“What?” Luzhin stood still, waiting with a defiant and offended face. Silence lasted for some seconds.\n\n“Why, if ever again... you dare to mention a single word... about my mother... I shall send you flying downstairs!”\n\n“What’s the matter with you?” cried Razumihin.\n\n“So that’s how it is?” Luzhin turned pale and bit his lip. “Let me tell you, sir,” he began deliberately, doing his utmost to restrain himself but breathing hard, “at the first moment I saw you you were ill-disposed to me, but I remained here on purpose to find out more. I could forgive a great deal in a sick man and a connection, but you... never after this...”\n\n“I am not ill,” cried Raskolnikov.\n\n“So much the worse...”\n\n“Go to hell!”\n\nBut Luzhin was already leaving without finishing his speech, squeezing between the table and the chair; Razumihin got up this time to let him pass. Without glancing at anyone, and not even nodding to Zossimov, who had for some time been making signs to him to let the sick man alone, he went out, lifting his hat to the level of his shoulders to avoid crushing it as he stooped to go out of the door. And even the curve of his spine was expressive of the horrible insult he had received.\n\n“How could you--how could you!” Razumihin said, shaking his head in perplexity.\n\n“Let me alone--let me alone all of you!” Raskolnikov cried in a frenzy. “Will you ever leave off tormenting me? I am not afraid of you! I am not afraid of anyone, anyone now! Get away from me! I want to be alone, alone, alone!”\n\n“Come along,” said Zossimov, nodding to Razumihin.\n\n“But we can’t leave him like this!”\n\n“Come along,” Zossimov repeated insistently, and he went out. Razumihin thought a minute and ran to overtake him.\n\n“It might be worse not to obey him,” said Zossimov on the stairs. “He mustn’t be irritated.”\n\n“What’s the matter with him?”\n\n“If only he could get some favourable shock, that’s what would do it! At first he was better.... You know he has got something on his mind! Some fixed idea weighing on him.... I am very much afraid so; he must have!”\n\n“Perhaps it’s that gentleman, Pyotr Petrovitch. From his conversation I gather he is going to marry his sister, and that he had received a letter about it just before his illness....”\n\n“Yes, confound the man! he may have upset the case altogether. But have you noticed, he takes no interest in anything, he does not respond to anything except one point on which he seems excited--that’s the murder?”\n\n“Yes, yes,” Razumihin agreed, “I noticed that, too. He is interested, frightened. It gave him a shock on the day he was ill in the police office; he fainted.”\n\n“Tell me more about that this evening and I’ll tell you something afterwards. He interests me very much! In half an hour I’ll go and see him again.... There’ll be no inflammation though.”\n\n“Thanks! And I’ll wait with Pashenka meantime and will keep watch on him through Nastasya....”\n\nRaskolnikov, left alone, looked with impatience and misery at Nastasya, but she still lingered.\n\n“Won’t you have some tea now?” she asked.\n\n“Later! I am sleepy! Leave me.”\n\nHe turned abruptly to the wall; Nastasya went out.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nBut as soon as she went out, he got up, latched the door, undid the parcel which Razumihin had brought in that evening and had tied up again and began dressing. Strange to say, he seemed immediately to have become perfectly calm; not a trace of his recent delirium nor of the panic fear that had haunted him of late. It was the first moment of a strange sudden calm. His movements were precise and definite; a firm purpose was evident in them. “To-day, to-day,” he muttered to himself. He understood that he was still weak, but his intense spiritual concentration gave him strength and self-confidence. He hoped, moreover, that he would not fall down in the street. When he had dressed in entirely new clothes, he looked at the money lying on the table, and after a moment’s thought put it in his pocket. It was twenty-five roubles. He took also all the copper change from the ten roubles spent by Razumihin on the clothes. Then he softly unlatched the door, went out, slipped downstairs and glanced in at the open kitchen door. Nastasya was standing with her back to him, blowing up the landlady’s samovar. She heard nothing. Who would have dreamed of his going out, indeed? A minute later he was in the street.\n\nIt was nearly eight o’clock, the sun was setting. It was as stifling as before, but he eagerly drank in the stinking, dusty town air. His head felt rather dizzy; a sort of savage energy gleamed suddenly in his feverish eyes and his wasted, pale and yellow face. He did not know and did not think where he was going, he had one thought only: “that all _this_ must be ended to-day, once for all, immediately; that he would not return home without it, because he _would not go on living like that_.” How, with what to make an end? He had not an idea about it, he did not even want to think of it. He drove away thought; thought tortured him. All he knew, all he felt was that everything must be changed “one way or another,” he repeated with desperate and immovable self-confidence and determination.\n\nFrom old habit he took his usual walk in the direction of the Hay Market. A dark-haired young man with a barrel organ was standing in the road in front of a little general shop and was grinding out a very sentimental song. He was accompanying a girl of fifteen, who stood on the pavement in front of him. She was dressed up in a crinoline, a mantle and a straw hat with a flame-coloured feather in it, all very old and shabby. In a strong and rather agreeable voice, cracked and coarsened by street singing, she sang in hope of getting a copper from the shop. Raskolnikov joined two or three listeners, took out a five copeck piece and put it in the girl’s hand. She broke off abruptly on a sentimental high note, shouted sharply to the organ grinder “Come on,” and both moved on to the next shop.\n\n“Do you like street music?” said Raskolnikov, addressing a middle-aged man standing idly by him. The man looked at him, startled and wondering.\n\n“I love to hear singing to a street organ,” said Raskolnikov, and his manner seemed strangely out of keeping with the subject--“I like it on cold, dark, damp autumn evenings--they must be damp--when all the passers-by have pale green, sickly faces, or better still when wet snow is falling straight down, when there’s no wind--you know what I mean?--and the street lamps shine through it...”\n\n“I don’t know.... Excuse me...” muttered the stranger, frightened by the question and Raskolnikov’s strange manner, and he crossed over to the other side of the street.\n\nRaskolnikov walked straight on and came out at the corner of the Hay Market, where the huckster and his wife had talked with Lizaveta; but they were not there now. Recognising the place, he stopped, looked round and addressed a young fellow in a red shirt who stood gaping before a corn chandler’s shop.\n\n“Isn’t there a man who keeps a booth with his wife at this corner?”\n\n“All sorts of people keep booths here,” answered the young man, glancing superciliously at Raskolnikov.\n\n“What’s his name?”\n\n“What he was christened.”\n\n“Aren’t you a Zaraïsky man, too? Which province?”\n\nThe young man looked at Raskolnikov again.\n\n“It’s not a province, your excellency, but a district. Graciously forgive me, your excellency!”\n\n“Is that a tavern at the top there?”\n\n“Yes, it’s an eating-house and there’s a billiard-room and you’ll find princesses there too.... La-la!”\n\nRaskolnikov crossed the square. In that corner there was a dense crowd of peasants. He pushed his way into the thickest part of it, looking at the faces. He felt an unaccountable inclination to enter into conversation with people. But the peasants took no notice of him; they were all shouting in groups together. He stood and thought a little and took a turning to the right in the direction of V.\n\nHe had often crossed that little street which turns at an angle, leading from the market-place to Sadovy Street. Of late he had often felt drawn to wander about this district, when he felt depressed, that he might feel more so.\n\nNow he walked along, thinking of nothing. At that point there is a great block of buildings, entirely let out in dram shops and eating-houses; women were continually running in and out, bare-headed and in their indoor clothes. Here and there they gathered in groups, on the pavement, especially about the entrances to various festive establishments in the lower storeys. From one of these a loud din, sounds of singing, the tinkling of a guitar and shouts of merriment, floated into the street. A crowd of women were thronging round the door; some were sitting on the steps, others on the pavement, others were standing talking. A drunken soldier, smoking a cigarette, was walking near them in the road, swearing; he seemed to be trying to find his way somewhere, but had forgotten where. One beggar was quarrelling with another, and a man dead drunk was lying right across the road. Raskolnikov joined the throng of women, who were talking in husky voices. They were bare-headed and wore cotton dresses and goatskin shoes. There were women of forty and some not more than seventeen; almost all had blackened eyes.\n\nHe felt strangely attracted by the singing and all the noise and uproar in the saloon below.... someone could be heard within dancing frantically, marking time with his heels to the sounds of the guitar and of a thin falsetto voice singing a jaunty air. He listened intently, gloomily and dreamily, bending down at the entrance and peeping inquisitively in from the pavement.\n\n“Oh, my handsome soldier Don’t beat me for nothing,”\n\ntrilled the thin voice of the singer. Raskolnikov felt a great desire to make out what he was singing, as though everything depended on that.\n\n“Shall I go in?” he thought. “They are laughing. From drink. Shall I get drunk?”\n\n“Won’t you come in?” one of the women asked him. Her voice was still musical and less thick than the others, she was young and not repulsive--the only one of the group.\n\n“Why, she’s pretty,” he said, drawing himself up and looking at her.\n\nShe smiled, much pleased at the compliment.\n\n“You’re very nice looking yourself,” she said.\n\n“Isn’t he thin though!” observed another woman in a deep bass. “Have you just come out of a hospital?”\n\n“They’re all generals’ daughters, it seems, but they have all snub noses,” interposed a tipsy peasant with a sly smile on his face, wearing a loose coat. “See how jolly they are.”\n\n“Go along with you!”\n\n“I’ll go, sweetie!”\n\nAnd he darted down into the saloon below. Raskolnikov moved on.\n\n“I say, sir,” the girl shouted after him.\n\n“What is it?”\n\nShe hesitated.\n\n“I’ll always be pleased to spend an hour with you, kind gentleman, but now I feel shy. Give me six copecks for a drink, there’s a nice young man!”\n\nRaskolnikov gave her what came first--fifteen copecks.\n\n“Ah, what a good-natured gentleman!”\n\n“What’s your name?”\n\n“Ask for Duclida.”\n\n“Well, that’s too much,” one of the women observed, shaking her head at Duclida. “I don’t know how you can ask like that. I believe I should drop with shame....”\n\nRaskolnikov looked curiously at the speaker. She was a pock-marked wench of thirty, covered with bruises, with her upper lip swollen. She made her criticism quietly and earnestly. “Where is it,” thought Raskolnikov. “Where is it I’ve read that someone condemned to death says or thinks, an hour before his death, that if he had to live on some high rock, on such a narrow ledge that he’d only room to stand, and the ocean, everlasting darkness, everlasting solitude, everlasting tempest around him, if he had to remain standing on a square yard of space all his life, a thousand years, eternity, it were better to live so than to die at once! Only to live, to live and live! Life, whatever it may be!... How true it is! Good God, how true! Man is a vile creature!... And vile is he who calls him vile for that,” he added a moment later.\n\nHe went into another street. “Bah, the Palais de Cristal! Razumihin was just talking of the Palais de Cristal. But what on earth was it I wanted? Yes, the newspapers.... Zossimov said he’d read it in the papers. Have you the papers?” he asked, going into a very spacious and positively clean restaurant, consisting of several rooms, which were, however, rather empty. Two or three people were drinking tea, and in a room further away were sitting four men drinking champagne. Raskolnikov fancied that Zametov was one of them, but he could not be sure at that distance. “What if it is?” he thought.\n\n“Will you have vodka?” asked the waiter.\n\n“Give me some tea and bring me the papers, the old ones for the last five days, and I’ll give you something.”\n\n“Yes, sir, here’s to-day’s. No vodka?”\n\nThe old newspapers and the tea were brought. Raskolnikov sat down and began to look through them.\n\n“Oh, damn... these are the items of intelligence. An accident on a staircase, spontaneous combustion of a shopkeeper from alcohol, a fire in Peski... a fire in the Petersburg quarter... another fire in the Petersburg quarter... and another fire in the Petersburg quarter.... Ah, here it is!” He found at last what he was seeking and began to read it. The lines danced before his eyes, but he read it all and began eagerly seeking later additions in the following numbers. His hands shook with nervous impatience as he turned the sheets. Suddenly someone sat down beside him at his table. He looked up, it was the head clerk Zametov, looking just the same, with the rings on his fingers and the watch-chain, with the curly, black hair, parted and pomaded, with the smart waistcoat, rather shabby coat and doubtful linen. He was in a good humour, at least he was smiling very gaily and good-humouredly. His dark face was rather flushed from the champagne he had drunk.\n\n“What, you here?” he began in surprise, speaking as though he’d known him all his life. “Why, Razumihin told me only yesterday you were unconscious. How strange! And do you know I’ve been to see you?”\n\nRaskolnikov knew he would come up to him. He laid aside the papers and turned to Zametov. There was a smile on his lips, and a new shade of irritable impatience was apparent in that smile.\n\n“I know you have,” he answered. “I’ve heard it. You looked for my sock.... And you know Razumihin has lost his heart to you? He says you’ve been with him to Luise Ivanovna’s--you know, the woman you tried to befriend, for whom you winked to the Explosive Lieutenant and he would not understand. Do you remember? How could he fail to understand--it was quite clear, wasn’t it?”\n\n“What a hot head he is!”\n\n“The explosive one?”\n\n“No, your friend Razumihin.”\n\n“You must have a jolly life, Mr. Zametov; entrance free to the most agreeable places. Who’s been pouring champagne into you just now?”\n\n“We’ve just been... having a drink together.... You talk about pouring it into me!”\n\n“By way of a fee! You profit by everything!” Raskolnikov laughed, “it’s all right, my dear boy,” he added, slapping Zametov on the shoulder. “I am not speaking from temper, but in a friendly way, for sport, as that workman of yours said when he was scuffling with Dmitri, in the case of the old woman....”\n\n“How do you know about it?”\n\n“Perhaps I know more about it than you do.”\n\n“How strange you are.... I am sure you are still very unwell. You oughtn’t to have come out.”\n\n“Oh, do I seem strange to you?”\n\n“Yes. What are you doing, reading the papers?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“There’s a lot about the fires.”\n\n“No, I am not reading about the fires.” Here he looked mysteriously at Zametov; his lips were twisted again in a mocking smile. “No, I am not reading about the fires,” he went on, winking at Zametov. “But confess now, my dear fellow, you’re awfully anxious to know what I am reading about?”\n\n“I am not in the least. Mayn’t I ask a question? Why do you keep on...?”\n\n“Listen, you are a man of culture and education?”\n\n“I was in the sixth class at the gymnasium,” said Zametov with some dignity.\n\n“Sixth class! Ah, my cock-sparrow! With your parting and your rings--you are a gentleman of fortune. Foo! what a charming boy!” Here Raskolnikov broke into a nervous laugh right in Zametov’s face. The latter drew back, more amazed than offended.\n\n“Foo! how strange you are!” Zametov repeated very seriously. “I can’t help thinking you are still delirious.”\n\n“I am delirious? You are fibbing, my cock-sparrow! So I am strange? You find me curious, do you?”\n\n“Yes, curious.”\n\n“Shall I tell you what I was reading about, what I was looking for? See what a lot of papers I’ve made them bring me. Suspicious, eh?”\n\n“Well, what is it?”\n\n“You prick up your ears?”\n\n“How do you mean--‘prick up my ears’?”\n\n“I’ll explain that afterwards, but now, my boy, I declare to you... no, better ‘I confess’... No, that’s not right either; ‘I make a deposition and you take it.’ I depose that I was reading, that I was looking and searching....” he screwed up his eyes and paused. “I was searching--and came here on purpose to do it--for news of the murder of the old pawnbroker woman,” he articulated at last, almost in a whisper, bringing his face exceedingly close to the face of Zametov. Zametov looked at him steadily, without moving or drawing his face away. What struck Zametov afterwards as the strangest part of it all was that silence followed for exactly a minute, and that they gazed at one another all the while.\n\n“What if you have been reading about it?” he cried at last, perplexed and impatient. “That’s no business of mine! What of it?”\n\n“The same old woman,” Raskolnikov went on in the same whisper, not heeding Zametov’s explanation, “about whom you were talking in the police-office, you remember, when I fainted. Well, do you understand now?”\n\n“What do you mean? Understand... what?” Zametov brought out, almost alarmed.\n\nRaskolnikov’s set and earnest face was suddenly transformed, and he suddenly went off into the same nervous laugh as before, as though utterly unable to restrain himself. And in one flash he recalled with extraordinary vividness of sensation a moment in the recent past, that moment when he stood with the axe behind the door, while the latch trembled and the men outside swore and shook it, and he had a sudden desire to shout at them, to swear at them, to put out his tongue at them, to mock them, to laugh, and laugh, and laugh!\n\n“You are either mad, or...” began Zametov, and he broke off, as though stunned by the idea that had suddenly flashed into his mind.\n\n“Or? Or what? What? Come, tell me!”\n\n“Nothing,” said Zametov, getting angry, “it’s all nonsense!”\n\nBoth were silent. After his sudden fit of laughter Raskolnikov became suddenly thoughtful and melancholy. He put his elbow on the table and leaned his head on his hand. He seemed to have completely forgotten Zametov. The silence lasted for some time.\n\n“Why don’t you drink your tea? It’s getting cold,” said Zametov.\n\n“What! Tea? Oh, yes....” Raskolnikov sipped the glass, put a morsel of bread in his mouth and, suddenly looking at Zametov, seemed to remember everything and pulled himself together. At the same moment his face resumed its original mocking expression. He went on drinking tea.\n\n“There have been a great many of these crimes lately,” said Zametov. “Only the other day I read in the _Moscow News_ that a whole gang of false coiners had been caught in Moscow. It was a regular society. They used to forge tickets!”\n\n“Oh, but it was a long time ago! I read about it a month ago,” Raskolnikov answered calmly. “So you consider them criminals?” he added, smiling.\n\n“Of course they are criminals.”\n\n“They? They are children, simpletons, not criminals! Why, half a hundred people meeting for such an object--what an idea! Three would be too many, and then they want to have more faith in one another than in themselves! One has only to blab in his cups and it all collapses. Simpletons! They engaged untrustworthy people to change the notes--what a thing to trust to a casual stranger! Well, let us suppose that these simpletons succeed and each makes a million, and what follows for the rest of their lives? Each is dependent on the others for the rest of his life! Better hang oneself at once! And they did not know how to change the notes either; the man who changed the notes took five thousand roubles, and his hands trembled. He counted the first four thousand, but did not count the fifth thousand--he was in such a hurry to get the money into his pocket and run away. Of course he roused suspicion. And the whole thing came to a crash through one fool! Is it possible?”\n\n“That his hands trembled?” observed Zametov, “yes, that’s quite possible. That, I feel quite sure, is possible. Sometimes one can’t stand things.”\n\n“Can’t stand that?”\n\n“Why, could you stand it then? No, I couldn’t. For the sake of a hundred roubles to face such a terrible experience? To go with false notes into a bank where it’s their business to spot that sort of thing! No, I should not have the face to do it. Would you?”\n\nRaskolnikov had an intense desire again “to put his tongue out.” Shivers kept running down his spine.\n\n“I should do it quite differently,” Raskolnikov began. “This is how I would change the notes: I’d count the first thousand three or four times backwards and forwards, looking at every note and then I’d set to the second thousand; I’d count that half-way through and then hold some fifty-rouble note to the light, then turn it, then hold it to the light again--to see whether it was a good one. ‘I am afraid,’ I would say, ‘a relation of mine lost twenty-five roubles the other day through a false note,’ and then I’d tell them the whole story. And after I began counting the third, ‘No, excuse me,’ I would say, ‘I fancy I made a mistake in the seventh hundred in that second thousand, I am not sure.’ And so I would give up the third thousand and go back to the second and so on to the end. And when I had finished, I’d pick out one from the fifth and one from the second thousand and take them again to the light and ask again, ‘Change them, please,’ and put the clerk into such a stew that he would not know how to get rid of me. When I’d finished and had gone out, I’d come back, ‘No, excuse me,’ and ask for some explanation. That’s how I’d do it.”\n\n“Foo! what terrible things you say!” said Zametov, laughing. “But all that is only talk. I dare say when it came to deeds you’d make a slip. I believe that even a practised, desperate man cannot always reckon on himself, much less you and I. To take an example near home--that old woman murdered in our district. The murderer seems to have been a desperate fellow, he risked everything in open daylight, was saved by a miracle--but his hands shook, too. He did not succeed in robbing the place, he couldn’t stand it. That was clear from the...”\n\nRaskolnikov seemed offended.\n\n“Clear? Why don’t you catch him then?” he cried, maliciously gibing at Zametov.\n\n“Well, they will catch him.”\n\n“Who? You? Do you suppose you could catch him? You’ve a tough job! A great point for you is whether a man is spending money or not. If he had no money and suddenly begins spending, he must be the man. So that any child can mislead you.”\n\n“The fact is they always do that, though,” answered Zametov. “A man will commit a clever murder at the risk of his life and then at once he goes drinking in a tavern. They are caught spending money, they are not all as cunning as you are. You wouldn’t go to a tavern, of course?”\n\nRaskolnikov frowned and looked steadily at Zametov.\n\n“You seem to enjoy the subject and would like to know how I should behave in that case, too?” he asked with displeasure.\n\n“I should like to,” Zametov answered firmly and seriously. Somewhat too much earnestness began to appear in his words and looks.\n\n“Very much?”\n\n“Very much!”\n\n“All right then. This is how I should behave,” Raskolnikov began, again bringing his face close to Zametov’s, again staring at him and speaking in a whisper, so that the latter positively shuddered. “This is what I should have done. I should have taken the money and jewels, I should have walked out of there and have gone straight to some deserted place with fences round it and scarcely anyone to be seen, some kitchen garden or place of that sort. I should have looked out beforehand some stone weighing a hundredweight or more which had been lying in the corner from the time the house was built. I would lift that stone--there would sure to be a hollow under it, and I would put the jewels and money in that hole. Then I’d roll the stone back so that it would look as before, would press it down with my foot and walk away. And for a year or two, three maybe, I would not touch it. And, well, they could search! There’d be no trace.”\n\n“You are a madman,” said Zametov, and for some reason he too spoke in a whisper, and moved away from Raskolnikov, whose eyes were glittering. He had turned fearfully pale and his upper lip was twitching and quivering. He bent down as close as possible to Zametov, and his lips began to move without uttering a word. This lasted for half a minute; he knew what he was doing, but could not restrain himself. The terrible word trembled on his lips, like the latch on that door; in another moment it will break out, in another moment he will let it go, he will speak out.\n\n“And what if it was I who murdered the old woman and Lizaveta?” he said suddenly and--realised what he had done.\n\nZametov looked wildly at him and turned white as the tablecloth. His face wore a contorted smile.\n\n“But is it possible?” he brought out faintly. Raskolnikov looked wrathfully at him.\n\n“Own up that you believed it, yes, you did?”\n\n“Not a bit of it, I believe it less than ever now,” Zametov cried hastily.\n\n“I’ve caught my cock-sparrow! So you did believe it before, if now you believe less than ever?”\n\n“Not at all,” cried Zametov, obviously embarrassed. “Have you been frightening me so as to lead up to this?”\n\n“You don’t believe it then? What were you talking about behind my back when I went out of the police-office? And why did the explosive lieutenant question me after I fainted? Hey, there,” he shouted to the waiter, getting up and taking his cap, “how much?”\n\n“Thirty copecks,” the latter replied, running up.\n\n“And there is twenty copecks for vodka. See what a lot of money!” he held out his shaking hand to Zametov with notes in it. “Red notes and blue, twenty-five roubles. Where did I get them? And where did my new clothes come from? You know I had not a copeck. You’ve cross-examined my landlady, I’ll be bound.... Well, that’s enough! _Assez causé!_ Till we meet again!”\n\nHe went out, trembling all over from a sort of wild hysterical sensation, in which there was an element of insufferable rapture. Yet he was gloomy and terribly tired. His face was twisted as after a fit. His fatigue increased rapidly. Any shock, any irritating sensation stimulated and revived his energies at once, but his strength failed as quickly when the stimulus was removed.\n\nZametov, left alone, sat for a long time in the same place, plunged in thought. Raskolnikov had unwittingly worked a revolution in his brain on a certain point and had made up his mind for him conclusively.\n\n“Ilya Petrovitch is a blockhead,” he decided.\n\nRaskolnikov had hardly opened the door of the restaurant when he stumbled against Razumihin on the steps. They did not see each other till they almost knocked against each other. For a moment they stood looking each other up and down. Razumihin was greatly astounded, then anger, real anger gleamed fiercely in his eyes.\n\n“So here you are!” he shouted at the top of his voice--“you ran away from your bed! And here I’ve been looking for you under the sofa! We went up to the garret. I almost beat Nastasya on your account. And here he is after all. Rodya! What is the meaning of it? Tell me the whole truth! Confess! Do you hear?”\n\n“It means that I’m sick to death of you all and I want to be alone,” Raskolnikov answered calmly.\n\n“Alone? When you are not able to walk, when your face is as white as a sheet and you are gasping for breath! Idiot!... What have you been doing in the Palais de Cristal? Own up at once!”\n\n“Let me go!” said Raskolnikov and tried to pass him. This was too much for Razumihin; he gripped him firmly by the shoulder.\n\n“Let you go? You dare tell me to let you go? Do you know what I’ll do with you directly? I’ll pick you up, tie you up in a bundle, carry you home under my arm and lock you up!”\n\n“Listen, Razumihin,” Raskolnikov began quietly, apparently calm--“can’t you see that I don’t want your benevolence? A strange desire you have to shower benefits on a man who... curses them, who feels them a burden in fact! Why did you seek me out at the beginning of my illness? Maybe I was very glad to die. Didn’t I tell you plainly enough to-day that you were torturing me, that I was... sick of you! You seem to want to torture people! I assure you that all that is seriously hindering my recovery, because it’s continually irritating me. You saw Zossimov went away just now to avoid irritating me. You leave me alone too, for goodness’ sake! What right have you, indeed, to keep me by force? Don’t you see that I am in possession of all my faculties now? How, how can I persuade you not to persecute me with your kindness? I may be ungrateful, I may be mean, only let me be, for God’s sake, let me be! Let me be, let me be!”\n\nHe began calmly, gloating beforehand over the venomous phrases he was about to utter, but finished, panting for breath, in a frenzy, as he had been with Luzhin.\n\nRazumihin stood a moment, thought and let his hand drop.\n\n“Well, go to hell then,” he said gently and thoughtfully. “Stay,” he roared, as Raskolnikov was about to move. “Listen to me. Let me tell you, that you are all a set of babbling, posing idiots! If you’ve any little trouble you brood over it like a hen over an egg. And you are plagiarists even in that! There isn’t a sign of independent life in you! You are made of spermaceti ointment and you’ve lymph in your veins instead of blood. I don’t believe in anyone of you! In any circumstances the first thing for all of you is to be unlike a human being! Stop!” he cried with redoubled fury, noticing that Raskolnikov was again making a movement--“hear me out! You know I’m having a house-warming this evening, I dare say they’ve arrived by now, but I left my uncle there--I just ran in--to receive the guests. And if you weren’t a fool, a common fool, a perfect fool, if you were an original instead of a translation... you see, Rodya, I recognise you’re a clever fellow, but you’re a fool!--and if you weren’t a fool you’d come round to me this evening instead of wearing out your boots in the street! Since you have gone out, there’s no help for it! I’d give you a snug easy chair, my landlady has one... a cup of tea, company.... Or you could lie on the sofa--any way you would be with us.... Zossimov will be there too. Will you come?”\n“No.”\n\n“R-rubbish!” Razumihin shouted, out of patience. “How do you know? You can’t answer for yourself! You don’t know anything about it.... Thousands of times I’ve fought tooth and nail with people and run back to them afterwards.... One feels ashamed and goes back to a man! So remember, Potchinkov’s house on the third storey....”\n\n“Why, Mr. Razumihin, I do believe you’d let anybody beat you from sheer benevolence.”\n\n“Beat? Whom? Me? I’d twist his nose off at the mere idea! Potchinkov’s house, 47, Babushkin’s flat....”\n\n“I shall not come, Razumihin.” Raskolnikov turned and walked away.\n\n“I bet you will,” Razumihin shouted after him. “I refuse to know you if you don’t! Stay, hey, is Zametov in there?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did you see him?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Talked to him?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What about? Confound you, don’t tell me then. Potchinkov’s house, 47, Babushkin’s flat, remember!”\n\nRaskolnikov walked on and turned the corner into Sadovy Street. Razumihin looked after him thoughtfully. Then with a wave of his hand he went into the house but stopped short of the stairs.\n\n“Confound it,” he went on almost aloud. “He talked sensibly but yet... I am a fool! As if madmen didn’t talk sensibly! And this was just what Zossimov seemed afraid of.” He struck his finger on his forehead. “What if... how could I let him go off alone? He may drown himself.... Ach, what a blunder! I can’t.” And he ran back to overtake Raskolnikov, but there was no trace of him. With a curse he returned with rapid steps to the Palais de Cristal to question Zametov.\n\nRaskolnikov walked straight to X---- Bridge, stood in the middle, and leaning both elbows on the rail stared into the distance. On parting with Razumihin, he felt so much weaker that he could scarcely reach this place. He longed to sit or lie down somewhere in the street. Bending over the water, he gazed mechanically at the last pink flush of the sunset, at the row of houses growing dark in the gathering twilight, at one distant attic window on the left bank, flashing as though on fire in the last rays of the setting sun, at the darkening water of the canal, and the water seemed to catch his attention. At last red circles flashed before his eyes, the houses seemed moving, the passers-by, the canal banks, the carriages, all danced before his eyes. Suddenly he started, saved again perhaps from swooning by an uncanny and hideous sight. He became aware of someone standing on the right side of him; he looked and saw a tall woman with a kerchief on her head, with a long, yellow, wasted face and red sunken eyes. She was looking straight at him, but obviously she saw nothing and recognised no one. Suddenly she leaned her right hand on the parapet, lifted her right leg over the railing, then her left and threw herself into the canal. The filthy water parted and swallowed up its victim for a moment, but an instant later the drowning woman floated to the surface, moving slowly with the current, her head and legs in the water, her skirt inflated like a balloon over her back.\n\n“A woman drowning! A woman drowning!” shouted dozens of voices; people ran up, both banks were thronged with spectators, on the bridge people crowded about Raskolnikov, pressing up behind him.\n\n“Mercy on it! it’s our Afrosinya!” a woman cried tearfully close by. “Mercy! save her! kind people, pull her out!”\n\n“A boat, a boat!” was shouted in the crowd. But there was no need of a boat; a policeman ran down the steps to the canal, threw off his great coat and his boots and rushed into the water. It was easy to reach her: she floated within a couple of yards from the steps, he caught hold of her clothes with his right hand and with his left seized a pole which a comrade held out to him; the drowning woman was pulled out at once. They laid her on the granite pavement of the embankment. She soon recovered consciousness, raised her head, sat up and began sneezing and coughing, stupidly wiping her wet dress with her hands. She said nothing.\n\n“She’s drunk herself out of her senses,” the same woman’s voice wailed at her side. “Out of her senses. The other day she tried to hang herself, we cut her down. I ran out to the shop just now, left my little girl to look after her--and here she’s in trouble again! A neighbour, gentleman, a neighbour, we live close by, the second house from the end, see yonder....”\n\nThe crowd broke up. The police still remained round the woman, someone mentioned the police station.... Raskolnikov looked on with a strange sensation of indifference and apathy. He felt disgusted. “No, that’s loathsome... water... it’s not good enough,” he muttered to himself. “Nothing will come of it,” he added, “no use to wait. What about the police office...? And why isn’t Zametov at the police office? The police office is open till ten o’clock....” He turned his back to the railing and looked about him.\n\n“Very well then!” he said resolutely; he moved from the bridge and walked in the direction of the police office. His heart felt hollow and empty. He did not want to think. Even his depression had passed, there was not a trace now of the energy with which he had set out “to make an end of it all.” Complete apathy had succeeded to it.\n\n“Well, it’s a way out of it,” he thought, walking slowly and listlessly along the canal bank. “Anyway, I’ll make an end, for I want to.... But is it a way out? What does it matter! There’ll be the square yard of space--ha! But what an end! Is it really the end? Shall I tell them or not? Ah... damn! How tired I am! If I could find somewhere to sit or lie down soon! What I am most ashamed of is its being so stupid. But I don’t care about that either! What idiotic ideas come into one’s head.”\n\nTo reach the police office he had to go straight forward and take the second turning to the left. It was only a few paces away. But at the first turning he stopped and, after a minute’s thought, turned into a side street and went two streets out of his way, possibly without any object, or possibly to delay a minute and gain time. He walked, looking at the ground; suddenly someone seemed to whisper in his ear; he lifted his head and saw that he was standing at the very gate of _the_ house. He had not passed it, he had not been near it since _that_ evening. An overwhelming, unaccountable prompting drew him on. He went into the house, passed through the gateway, then into the first entrance on the right, and began mounting the familiar staircase to the fourth storey. The narrow, steep staircase was very dark. He stopped at each landing and looked round him with curiosity; on the first landing the framework of the window had been taken out. “That wasn’t so then,” he thought. Here was the flat on the second storey where Nikolay and Dmitri had been working. “It’s shut up and the door newly painted. So it’s to let.” Then the third storey and the fourth. “Here!” He was perplexed to find the door of the flat wide open. There were men there, he could hear voices; he had not expected that. After brief hesitation he mounted the last stairs and went into the flat. It, too, was being done up; there were workmen in it. This seemed to amaze him; he somehow fancied that he would find everything as he left it, even perhaps the corpses in the same places on the floor. And now, bare walls, no furniture; it seemed strange. He walked to the window and sat down on the window-sill. There were two workmen, both young fellows, but one much younger than the other. They were papering the walls with a new white paper covered with lilac flowers, instead of the old, dirty, yellow one. Raskolnikov for some reason felt horribly annoyed by this. He looked at the new paper with dislike, as though he felt sorry to have it all so changed. The workmen had obviously stayed beyond their time and now they were hurriedly rolling up their paper and getting ready to go home. They took no notice of Raskolnikov’s coming in; they were talking. Raskolnikov folded his arms and listened.\n\n“She comes to me in the morning,” said the elder to the younger, “very early, all dressed up. ‘Why are you preening and prinking?’ says I. ‘I am ready to do anything to please you, Tit Vassilitch!’ That’s a way of going on! And she dressed up like a regular fashion book!”\n\n“And what is a fashion book?” the younger one asked. He obviously regarded the other as an authority.\n\n“A fashion book is a lot of pictures, coloured, and they come to the tailors here every Saturday, by post from abroad, to show folks how to dress, the male sex as well as the female. They’re pictures. The gentlemen are generally wearing fur coats and for the ladies’ fluffles, they’re beyond anything you can fancy.”\n\n“There’s nothing you can’t find in Petersburg,” the younger cried enthusiastically, “except father and mother, there’s everything!”\n\n“Except them, there’s everything to be found, my boy,” the elder declared sententiously.\n\nRaskolnikov got up and walked into the other room where the strong box, the bed, and the chest of drawers had been; the room seemed to him very tiny without furniture in it. The paper was the same; the paper in the corner showed where the case of ikons had stood. He looked at it and went to the window. The elder workman looked at him askance.\n\n“What do you want?” he asked suddenly.\n\nInstead of answering Raskolnikov went into the passage and pulled the bell. The same bell, the same cracked note. He rang it a second and a third time; he listened and remembered. The hideous and agonisingly fearful sensation he had felt then began to come back more and more vividly. He shuddered at every ring and it gave him more and more satisfaction.\n\n“Well, what do you want? Who are you?” the workman shouted, going out to him. Raskolnikov went inside again.\n\n“I want to take a flat,” he said. “I am looking round.”\n\n“It’s not the time to look at rooms at night! and you ought to come up with the porter.”\n\n“The floors have been washed, will they be painted?” Raskolnikov went on. “Is there no blood?”\n\n“What blood?”\n\n“Why, the old woman and her sister were murdered here. There was a perfect pool there.”\n\n“But who are you?” the workman cried, uneasy.\n\n“Who am I?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“You want to know? Come to the police station, I’ll tell you.”\n\nThe workmen looked at him in amazement.\n\n“It’s time for us to go, we are late. Come along, Alyoshka. We must lock up,” said the elder workman.\n\n“Very well, come along,” said Raskolnikov indifferently, and going out first, he went slowly downstairs. “Hey, porter,” he cried in the gateway.\n\nAt the entrance several people were standing, staring at the passers-by; the two porters, a peasant woman, a man in a long coat and a few others. Raskolnikov went straight up to them.\n\n“What do you want?” asked one of the porters.\n\n“Have you been to the police office?”\n\n“I’ve just been there. What do you want?”\n\n“Is it open?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“Is the assistant there?”\n\n“He was there for a time. What do you want?”\n\nRaskolnikov made no reply, but stood beside them lost in thought.\n\n“He’s been to look at the flat,” said the elder workman, coming forward.\n\n“Which flat?”\n\n“Where we are at work. ‘Why have you washed away the blood?’ says he. ‘There has been a murder here,’ says he, ‘and I’ve come to take it.’ And he began ringing at the bell, all but broke it. ‘Come to the police station,’ says he. ‘I’ll tell you everything there.’ He wouldn’t leave us.”\n\nThe porter looked at Raskolnikov, frowning and perplexed.\n\n“Who are you?” he shouted as impressively as he could.\n\n“I am Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov, formerly a student, I live in Shil’s house, not far from here, flat Number 14, ask the porter, he knows me.” Raskolnikov said all this in a lazy, dreamy voice, not turning round, but looking intently into the darkening street.\n\n“Why have you been to the flat?”\n\n“To look at it.”\n\n“What is there to look at?”\n\n“Take him straight to the police station,” the man in the long coat jerked in abruptly.\n\nRaskolnikov looked intently at him over his shoulder and said in the same slow, lazy tones:\n\n“Come along.”\n\n“Yes, take him,” the man went on more confidently. “Why was he going into _that_, what’s in his mind, eh?”\n\n“He’s not drunk, but God knows what’s the matter with him,” muttered the workman.\n\n“But what do you want?” the porter shouted again, beginning to get angry in earnest--“Why are you hanging about?”\n\n“You funk the police station then?” said Raskolnikov jeeringly.\n\n“How funk it? Why are you hanging about?”\n\n“He’s a rogue!” shouted the peasant woman.\n\n“Why waste time talking to him?” cried the other porter, a huge peasant in a full open coat and with keys on his belt. “Get along! He is a rogue and no mistake. Get along!”\n\nAnd seizing Raskolnikov by the shoulder he flung him into the street. He lurched forward, but recovered his footing, looked at the spectators in silence and walked away.\n\n“Strange man!” observed the workman.\n\n“There are strange folks about nowadays,” said the woman.\n\n“You should have taken him to the police station all the same,” said the man in the long coat.\n\n“Better have nothing to do with him,” decided the big porter. “A regular rogue! Just what he wants, you may be sure, but once take him up, you won’t get rid of him.... We know the sort!”\n\n“Shall I go there or not?” thought Raskolnikov, standing in the middle of the thoroughfare at the cross-roads, and he looked about him, as though expecting from someone a decisive word. But no sound came, all was dead and silent like the stones on which he walked, dead to him, to him alone.... All at once at the end of the street, two hundred yards away, in the gathering dusk he saw a crowd and heard talk and shouts. In the middle of the crowd stood a carriage.... A light gleamed in the middle of the street. “What is it?” Raskolnikov turned to the right and went up to the crowd. He seemed to clutch at everything and smiled coldly when he recognised it, for he had fully made up his mind to go to the police station and knew that it would all soon be over.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nAn elegant carriage stood in the middle of the road with a pair of spirited grey horses; there was no one in it, and the coachman had got off his box and stood by; the horses were being held by the bridle.... A mass of people had gathered round, the police standing in front. One of them held a lighted lantern which he was turning on something lying close to the wheels. Everyone was talking, shouting, exclaiming; the coachman seemed at a loss and kept repeating:\n\n“What a misfortune! Good Lord, what a misfortune!”\n\nRaskolnikov pushed his way in as far as he could, and succeeded at last in seeing the object of the commotion and interest. On the ground a man who had been run over lay apparently unconscious, and covered with blood; he was very badly dressed, but not like a workman. Blood was flowing from his head and face; his face was crushed, mutilated and disfigured. He was evidently badly injured.\n\n“Merciful heaven!” wailed the coachman, “what more could I do? If I’d been driving fast or had not shouted to him, but I was going quietly, not in a hurry. Everyone could see I was going along just like everybody else. A drunken man can’t walk straight, we all know.... I saw him crossing the street, staggering and almost falling. I shouted again and a second and a third time, then I held the horses in, but he fell straight under their feet! Either he did it on purpose or he was very tipsy.... The horses are young and ready to take fright... they started, he screamed... that made them worse. That’s how it happened!”\n\n“That’s just how it was,” a voice in the crowd confirmed.\n\n“He shouted, that’s true, he shouted three times,” another voice declared.\n\n“Three times it was, we all heard it,” shouted a third.\n\nBut the coachman was not very much distressed and frightened. It was evident that the carriage belonged to a rich and important person who was awaiting it somewhere; the police, of course, were in no little anxiety to avoid upsetting his arrangements. All they had to do was to take the injured man to the police station and the hospital. No one knew his name.\n\nMeanwhile Raskolnikov had squeezed in and stooped closer over him. The lantern suddenly lighted up the unfortunate man’s face. He recognised him.\n\n“I know him! I know him!” he shouted, pushing to the front. “It’s a government clerk retired from the service, Marmeladov. He lives close by in Kozel’s house.... Make haste for a doctor! I will pay, see?” He pulled money out of his pocket and showed it to the policeman. He was in violent agitation.\n\nThe police were glad that they had found out who the man was. Raskolnikov gave his own name and address, and, as earnestly as if it had been his father, he besought the police to carry the unconscious Marmeladov to his lodging at once.\n\n“Just here, three houses away,” he said eagerly, “the house belongs to Kozel, a rich German. He was going home, no doubt drunk. I know him, he is a drunkard. He has a family there, a wife, children, he has one daughter.... It will take time to take him to the hospital, and there is sure to be a doctor in the house. I’ll pay, I’ll pay! At least he will be looked after at home... they will help him at once. But he’ll die before you get him to the hospital.” He managed to slip something unseen into the policeman’s hand. But the thing was straightforward and legitimate, and in any case help was closer here. They raised the injured man; people volunteered to help.\n\nKozel’s house was thirty yards away. Raskolnikov walked behind, carefully holding Marmeladov’s head and showing the way.\n\n“This way, this way! We must take him upstairs head foremost. Turn round! I’ll pay, I’ll make it worth your while,” he muttered.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna had just begun, as she always did at every free moment, walking to and fro in her little room from window to stove and back again, with her arms folded across her chest, talking to herself and coughing. Of late she had begun to talk more than ever to her eldest girl, Polenka, a child of ten, who, though there was much she did not understand, understood very well that her mother needed her, and so always watched her with her big clever eyes and strove her utmost to appear to understand. This time Polenka was undressing her little brother, who had been unwell all day and was going to bed. The boy was waiting for her to take off his shirt, which had to be washed at night. He was sitting straight and motionless on a chair, with a silent, serious face, with his legs stretched out straight before him--heels together and toes turned out.\n\nHe was listening to what his mother was saying to his sister, sitting perfectly still with pouting lips and wide-open eyes, just as all good little boys have to sit when they are undressed to go to bed. A little girl, still younger, dressed literally in rags, stood at the screen, waiting for her turn. The door on to the stairs was open to relieve them a little from the clouds of tobacco smoke which floated in from the other rooms and brought on long terrible fits of coughing in the poor, consumptive woman. Katerina Ivanovna seemed to have grown even thinner during that week and the hectic flush on her face was brighter than ever.\n\n“You wouldn’t believe, you can’t imagine, Polenka,” she said, walking about the room, “what a happy luxurious life we had in my papa’s house and how this drunkard has brought me, and will bring you all, to ruin! Papa was a civil colonel and only a step from being a governor; so that everyone who came to see him said, ‘We look upon you, Ivan Mihailovitch, as our governor!’ When I... when...” she coughed violently, “oh, cursed life,” she cried, clearing her throat and pressing her hands to her breast, “when I... when at the last ball... at the marshal’s... Princess Bezzemelny saw me--who gave me the blessing when your father and I were married, Polenka--she asked at once ‘Isn’t that the pretty girl who danced the shawl dance at the breaking-up?’ (You must mend that tear, you must take your needle and darn it as I showed you, or to-morrow--cough, cough, cough--he will make the hole bigger,” she articulated with effort.) “Prince Schegolskoy, a kammerjunker, had just come from Petersburg then... he danced the mazurka with me and wanted to make me an offer next day; but I thanked him in flattering expressions and told him that my heart had long been another’s. That other was your father, Polya; papa was fearfully angry.... Is the water ready? Give me the shirt, and the stockings! Lida,” said she to the youngest one, “you must manage without your chemise to-night... and lay your stockings out with it... I’ll wash them together.... How is it that drunken vagabond doesn’t come in? He has worn his shirt till it looks like a dish-clout, he has torn it to rags! I’d do it all together, so as not to have to work two nights running! Oh, dear! (Cough, cough, cough, cough!) Again! What’s this?” she cried, noticing a crowd in the passage and the men, who were pushing into her room, carrying a burden. “What is it? What are they bringing? Mercy on us!”\n\n“Where are we to put him?” asked the policeman, looking round when Marmeladov, unconscious and covered with blood, had been carried in.\n\n“On the sofa! Put him straight on the sofa, with his head this way,” Raskolnikov showed him.\n\n“Run over in the road! Drunk!” someone shouted in the passage.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna stood, turning white and gasping for breath. The children were terrified. Little Lida screamed, rushed to Polenka and clutched at her, trembling all over.\n\nHaving laid Marmeladov down, Raskolnikov flew to Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“For God’s sake be calm, don’t be frightened!” he said, speaking quickly, “he was crossing the road and was run over by a carriage, don’t be frightened, he will come to, I told them bring him here... I’ve been here already, you remember? He will come to; I’ll pay!”\n\n“He’s done it this time!” Katerina Ivanovna cried despairingly and she rushed to her husband.\n\nRaskolnikov noticed at once that she was not one of those women who swoon easily. She instantly placed under the luckless man’s head a pillow, which no one had thought of and began undressing and examining him. She kept her head, forgetting herself, biting her trembling lips and stifling the screams which were ready to break from her.\n\nRaskolnikov meanwhile induced someone to run for a doctor. There was a doctor, it appeared, next door but one.\n\n“I’ve sent for a doctor,” he kept assuring Katerina Ivanovna, “don’t be uneasy, I’ll pay. Haven’t you water?... and give me a napkin or a towel, anything, as quick as you can.... He is injured, but not killed, believe me.... We shall see what the doctor says!”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna ran to the window; there, on a broken chair in the corner, a large earthenware basin full of water had been stood, in readiness for washing her children’s and husband’s linen that night. This washing was done by Katerina Ivanovna at night at least twice a week, if not oftener. For the family had come to such a pass that they were practically without change of linen, and Katerina Ivanovna could not endure uncleanliness and, rather than see dirt in the house, she preferred to wear herself out at night, working beyond her strength when the rest were asleep, so as to get the wet linen hung on a line and dry by the morning. She took up the basin of water at Raskolnikov’s request, but almost fell down with her burden. But the latter had already succeeded in finding a towel, wetted it and began washing the blood off Marmeladov’s face.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna stood by, breathing painfully and pressing her hands to her breast. She was in need of attention herself. Raskolnikov began to realise that he might have made a mistake in having the injured man brought here. The policeman, too, stood in hesitation.\n\n“Polenka,” cried Katerina Ivanovna, “run to Sonia, make haste. If you don’t find her at home, leave word that her father has been run over and that she is to come here at once... when she comes in. Run, Polenka! there, put on the shawl.”\n\n“Run your fastest!” cried the little boy on the chair suddenly, after which he relapsed into the same dumb rigidity, with round eyes, his heels thrust forward and his toes spread out.\n\nMeanwhile the room had become so full of people that you couldn’t have dropped a pin. The policemen left, all except one, who remained for a time, trying to drive out the people who came in from the stairs. Almost all Madame Lippevechsel’s lodgers had streamed in from the inner rooms of the flat; at first they were squeezed together in the doorway, but afterwards they overflowed into the room. Katerina Ivanovna flew into a fury.\n\n“You might let him die in peace, at least,” she shouted at the crowd, “is it a spectacle for you to gape at? With cigarettes! (Cough, cough, cough!) You might as well keep your hats on.... And there is one in his hat!... Get away! You should respect the dead, at least!”\n\nHer cough choked her--but her reproaches were not without result. They evidently stood in some awe of Katerina Ivanovna. The lodgers, one after another, squeezed back into the doorway with that strange inner feeling of satisfaction which may be observed in the presence of a sudden accident, even in those nearest and dearest to the victim, from which no living man is exempt, even in spite of the sincerest sympathy and compassion.\n\nVoices outside were heard, however, speaking of the hospital and saying that they’d no business to make a disturbance here.\n\n“No business to die!” cried Katerina Ivanovna, and she was rushing to the door to vent her wrath upon them, but in the doorway came face to face with Madame Lippevechsel who had only just heard of the accident and ran in to restore order. She was a particularly quarrelsome and irresponsible German.\n\n“Ah, my God!” she cried, clasping her hands, “your husband drunken horses have trampled! To the hospital with him! I am the landlady!”\n\n“Amalia Ludwigovna, I beg you to recollect what you are saying,” Katerina Ivanovna began haughtily (she always took a haughty tone with the landlady that she might “remember her place” and even now could not deny herself this satisfaction). “Amalia Ludwigovna...”\n\n“I have you once before told that you to call me Amalia Ludwigovna may not dare; I am Amalia Ivanovna.”\n\n“You are not Amalia Ivanovna, but Amalia Ludwigovna, and as I am not one of your despicable flatterers like Mr. Lebeziatnikov, who’s laughing behind the door at this moment (a laugh and a cry of ‘they are at it again’ was in fact audible at the door) so I shall always call you Amalia Ludwigovna, though I fail to understand why you dislike that name. You can see for yourself what has happened to Semyon Zaharovitch; he is dying. I beg you to close that door at once and to admit no one. Let him at least die in peace! Or I warn you the Governor-General, himself, shall be informed of your conduct to-morrow. The prince knew me as a girl; he remembers Semyon Zaharovitch well and has often been a benefactor to him. Everyone knows that Semyon Zaharovitch had many friends and protectors, whom he abandoned himself from an honourable pride, knowing his unhappy weakness, but now (she pointed to Raskolnikov) a generous young man has come to our assistance, who has wealth and connections and whom Semyon Zaharovitch has known from a child. You may rest assured, Amalia Ludwigovna...”\n\nAll this was uttered with extreme rapidity, getting quicker and quicker, but a cough suddenly cut short Katerina Ivanovna’s eloquence. At that instant the dying man recovered consciousness and uttered a groan; she ran to him. The injured man opened his eyes and without recognition or understanding gazed at Raskolnikov who was bending over him. He drew deep, slow, painful breaths; blood oozed at the corners of his mouth and drops of perspiration came out on his forehead. Not recognising Raskolnikov, he began looking round uneasily. Katerina Ivanovna looked at him with a sad but stern face, and tears trickled from her eyes.\n\n“My God! His whole chest is crushed! How he is bleeding,” she said in despair. “We must take off his clothes. Turn a little, Semyon Zaharovitch, if you can,” she cried to him.\n\nMarmeladov recognised her.\n\n“A priest,” he articulated huskily.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna walked to the window, laid her head against the window frame and exclaimed in despair:\n\n“Oh, cursed life!”\n\n“A priest,” the dying man said again after a moment’s silence.\n\n“They’ve gone for him,” Katerina Ivanovna shouted to him, he obeyed her shout and was silent. With sad and timid eyes he looked for her; she returned and stood by his pillow. He seemed a little easier but not for long.\n\nSoon his eyes rested on little Lida, his favourite, who was shaking in the corner, as though she were in a fit, and staring at him with her wondering childish eyes.\n\n“A-ah,” he signed towards her uneasily. He wanted to say something.\n\n“What now?” cried Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“Barefoot, barefoot!” he muttered, indicating with frenzied eyes the child’s bare feet.\n\n“Be silent,” Katerina Ivanovna cried irritably, “you know why she is barefooted.”\n\n“Thank God, the doctor,” exclaimed Raskolnikov, relieved.\n\nThe doctor came in, a precise little old man, a German, looking about him mistrustfully; he went up to the sick man, took his pulse, carefully felt his head and with the help of Katerina Ivanovna he unbuttoned the blood-stained shirt, and bared the injured man’s chest. It was gashed, crushed and fractured, several ribs on the right side were broken. On the left side, just over the heart, was a large, sinister-looking yellowish-black bruise--a cruel kick from the horse’s hoof. The doctor frowned. The policeman told him that he was caught in the wheel and turned round with it for thirty yards on the road.\n\n“It’s wonderful that he has recovered consciousness,” the doctor whispered softly to Raskolnikov.\n\n“What do you think of him?” he asked.\n\n“He will die immediately.”\n\n“Is there really no hope?”\n\n“Not the faintest! He is at the last gasp.... His head is badly injured, too... Hm... I could bleed him if you like, but... it would be useless. He is bound to die within the next five or ten minutes.”\n\n“Better bleed him then.”\n\n“If you like.... But I warn you it will be perfectly useless.”\n\nAt that moment other steps were heard; the crowd in the passage parted, and the priest, a little, grey old man, appeared in the doorway bearing the sacrament. A policeman had gone for him at the time of the accident. The doctor changed places with him, exchanging glances with him. Raskolnikov begged the doctor to remain a little while. He shrugged his shoulders and remained.\n\nAll stepped back. The confession was soon over. The dying man probably understood little; he could only utter indistinct broken sounds. Katerina Ivanovna took little Lida, lifted the boy from the chair, knelt down in the corner by the stove and made the children kneel in front of her. The little girl was still trembling; but the boy, kneeling on his little bare knees, lifted his hand rhythmically, crossing himself with precision and bowed down, touching the floor with his forehead, which seemed to afford him especial satisfaction. Katerina Ivanovna bit her lips and held back her tears; she prayed, too, now and then pulling straight the boy’s shirt, and managed to cover the girl’s bare shoulders with a kerchief, which she took from the chest without rising from her knees or ceasing to pray. Meanwhile the door from the inner rooms was opened inquisitively again. In the passage the crowd of spectators from all the flats on the staircase grew denser and denser, but they did not venture beyond the threshold. A single candle-end lighted up the scene.\n\nAt that moment Polenka forced her way through the crowd at the door. She came in panting from running so fast, took off her kerchief, looked for her mother, went up to her and said, “She’s coming, I met her in the street.” Her mother made her kneel beside her.\n\nTimidly and noiselessly a young girl made her way through the crowd, and strange was her appearance in that room, in the midst of want, rags, death and despair. She, too, was in rags, her attire was all of the cheapest, but decked out in gutter finery of a special stamp, unmistakably betraying its shameful purpose. Sonia stopped short in the doorway and looked about her bewildered, unconscious of everything. She forgot her fourth-hand, gaudy silk dress, so unseemly here with its ridiculous long train, and her immense crinoline that filled up the whole doorway, and her light-coloured shoes, and the parasol she brought with her, though it was no use at night, and the absurd round straw hat with its flaring flame-coloured feather. Under this rakishly-tilted hat was a pale, frightened little face with lips parted and eyes staring in terror. Sonia was a small thin girl of eighteen with fair hair, rather pretty, with wonderful blue eyes. She looked intently at the bed and the priest; she too was out of breath with running. At last whispers, some words in the crowd probably, reached her. She looked down and took a step forward into the room, still keeping close to the door.\n\nThe service was over. Katerina Ivanovna went up to her husband again. The priest stepped back and turned to say a few words of admonition and consolation to Katerina Ivanovna on leaving.\n\n“What am I to do with these?” she interrupted sharply and irritably, pointing to the little ones.\n\n“God is merciful; look to the Most High for succour,” the priest began.\n\n“Ach! He is merciful, but not to us.”\n\n“That’s a sin, a sin, madam,” observed the priest, shaking his head.\n\n“And isn’t that a sin?” cried Katerina Ivanovna, pointing to the dying man.\n\n“Perhaps those who have involuntarily caused the accident will agree to compensate you, at least for the loss of his earnings.”\n\n“You don’t understand!” cried Katerina Ivanovna angrily waving her hand. “And why should they compensate me? Why, he was drunk and threw himself under the horses! What earnings? He brought us in nothing but misery. He drank everything away, the drunkard! He robbed us to get drink, he wasted their lives and mine for drink! And thank God he’s dying! One less to keep!”\n\n“You must forgive in the hour of death, that’s a sin, madam, such feelings are a great sin.”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna was busy with the dying man; she was giving him water, wiping the blood and sweat from his head, setting his pillow straight, and had only turned now and then for a moment to address the priest. Now she flew at him almost in a frenzy.\n\n“Ah, father! That’s words and only words! Forgive! If he’d not been run over, he’d have come home to-day drunk and his only shirt dirty and in rags and he’d have fallen asleep like a log, and I should have been sousing and rinsing till daybreak, washing his rags and the children’s and then drying them by the window and as soon as it was daylight I should have been darning them. That’s how I spend my nights!... What’s the use of talking of forgiveness! I have forgiven as it is!”\n\nA terrible hollow cough interrupted her words. She put her handkerchief to her lips and showed it to the priest, pressing her other hand to her aching chest. The handkerchief was covered with blood. The priest bowed his head and said nothing.\n\nMarmeladov was in the last agony; he did not take his eyes off the face of Katerina Ivanovna, who was bending over him again. He kept trying to say something to her; he began moving his tongue with difficulty and articulating indistinctly, but Katerina Ivanovna, understanding that he wanted to ask her forgiveness, called peremptorily to him:\n\n“Be silent! No need! I know what you want to say!” And the sick man was silent, but at the same instant his wandering eyes strayed to the doorway and he saw Sonia.\n\nTill then he had not noticed her: she was standing in the shadow in a corner.\n\n“Who’s that? Who’s that?” he said suddenly in a thick gasping voice, in agitation, turning his eyes in horror towards the door where his daughter was standing, and trying to sit up.\n\n“Lie down! Lie do-own!” cried Katerina Ivanovna.\n\nWith unnatural strength he had succeeded in propping himself on his elbow. He looked wildly and fixedly for some time on his daughter, as though not recognising her. He had never seen her before in such attire. Suddenly he recognised her, crushed and ashamed in her humiliation and gaudy finery, meekly awaiting her turn to say good-bye to her dying father. His face showed intense suffering.\n\n“Sonia! Daughter! Forgive!” he cried, and he tried to hold out his hand to her, but losing his balance, he fell off the sofa, face downwards on the floor. They rushed to pick him up, they put him on the sofa; but he was dying. Sonia with a faint cry ran up, embraced him and remained so without moving. He died in her arms.\n\n“He’s got what he wanted,” Katerina Ivanovna cried, seeing her husband’s dead body. “Well, what’s to be done now? How am I to bury him! What can I give them to-morrow to eat?”\n\nRaskolnikov went up to Katerina Ivanovna.\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna,” he began, “last week your husband told me all his life and circumstances.... Believe me, he spoke of you with passionate reverence. From that evening, when I learnt how devoted he was to you all and how he loved and respected you especially, Katerina Ivanovna, in spite of his unfortunate weakness, from that evening we became friends.... Allow me now... to do something... to repay my debt to my dead friend. Here are twenty roubles, I think--and if that can be of any assistance to you, then... I... in short, I will come again, I will be sure to come again... I shall, perhaps, come again to-morrow.... Good-bye!”\n\nAnd he went quickly out of the room, squeezing his way through the crowd to the stairs. But in the crowd he suddenly jostled against Nikodim Fomitch, who had heard of the accident and had come to give instructions in person. They had not met since the scene at the police station, but Nikodim Fomitch knew him instantly.\n\n“Ah, is that you?” he asked him.\n\n“He’s dead,” answered Raskolnikov. “The doctor and the priest have been, all as it should have been. Don’t worry the poor woman too much, she is in consumption as it is. Try and cheer her up, if possible... you are a kind-hearted man, I know...” he added with a smile, looking straight in his face.\n\n“But you are spattered with blood,” observed Nikodim Fomitch, noticing in the lamplight some fresh stains on Raskolnikov’s waistcoat.\n\n“Yes... I’m covered with blood,” Raskolnikov said with a peculiar air; then he smiled, nodded and went downstairs.\n\nHe walked down slowly and deliberately, feverish but not conscious of it, entirely absorbed in a new overwhelming sensation of life and strength that surged up suddenly within him. This sensation might be compared to that of a man condemned to death who has suddenly been pardoned. Halfway down the staircase he was overtaken by the priest on his way home; Raskolnikov let him pass, exchanging a silent greeting with him. He was just descending the last steps when he heard rapid footsteps behind him. Someone overtook him; it was Polenka. She was running after him, calling “Wait! wait!”\n\nHe turned round. She was at the bottom of the staircase and stopped short a step above him. A dim light came in from the yard. Raskolnikov could distinguish the child’s thin but pretty little face, looking at him with a bright childish smile. She had run after him with a message which she was evidently glad to give.\n\n“Tell me, what is your name?... and where do you live?” she said hurriedly in a breathless voice.\n\nHe laid both hands on her shoulders and looked at her with a sort of rapture. It was such a joy to him to look at her, he could not have said why.\n\n“Who sent you?”\n\n“Sister Sonia sent me,” answered the girl, smiling still more brightly.\n\n“I knew it was sister Sonia sent you.”\n\n“Mamma sent me, too... when sister Sonia was sending me, mamma came up, too, and said ‘Run fast, Polenka.’”\n\n“Do you love sister Sonia?”\n\n“I love her more than anyone,” Polenka answered with a peculiar earnestness, and her smile became graver.\n\n“And will you love me?”\n\nBy way of answer he saw the little girl’s face approaching him, her full lips naïvely held out to kiss him. Suddenly her arms as thin as sticks held him tightly, her head rested on his shoulder and the little girl wept softly, pressing her face against him.\n\n“I am sorry for father,” she said a moment later, raising her tear-stained face and brushing away the tears with her hands. “It’s nothing but misfortunes now,” she added suddenly with that peculiarly sedate air which children try hard to assume when they want to speak like grown-up people.\n\n“Did your father love you?”\n\n“He loved Lida most,” she went on very seriously without a smile, exactly like grown-up people, “he loved her because she is little and because she is ill, too. And he always used to bring her presents. But he taught us to read and me grammar and scripture, too,” she added with dignity. “And mother never used to say anything, but we knew that she liked it and father knew it, too. And mother wants to teach me French, for it’s time my education began.”\n\n“And do you know your prayers?”\n\n“Of course, we do! We knew them long ago. I say my prayers to myself as I am a big girl now, but Kolya and Lida say them aloud with mother. First they repeat the ‘Ave Maria’ and then another prayer: ‘Lord, forgive and bless sister Sonia,’ and then another, ‘Lord, forgive and bless our second father.’ For our elder father is dead and this is another one, but we do pray for the other as well.”\n\n“Polenka, my name is Rodion. Pray sometimes for me, too. ‘And Thy servant Rodion,’ nothing more.”\n\n“I’ll pray for you all the rest of my life,” the little girl declared hotly, and suddenly smiling again she rushed at him and hugged him warmly once more.\n\nRaskolnikov told her his name and address and promised to be sure to come next day. The child went away quite enchanted with him. It was past ten when he came out into the street. In five minutes he was standing on the bridge at the spot where the woman had jumped in.\n\n“Enough,” he pronounced resolutely and triumphantly. “I’ve done with fancies, imaginary terrors and phantoms! Life is real! haven’t I lived just now? My life has not yet died with that old woman! The Kingdom of Heaven to her--and now enough, madam, leave me in peace! Now for the reign of reason and light... and of will, and of strength... and now we will see! We will try our strength!” he added defiantly, as though challenging some power of darkness. “And I was ready to consent to live in a square of space!\n\n“I am very weak at this moment, but... I believe my illness is all over. I knew it would be over when I went out. By the way, Potchinkov’s house is only a few steps away. I certainly must go to Razumihin even if it were not close by... let him win his bet! Let us give him some satisfaction, too--no matter! Strength, strength is what one wants, you can get nothing without it, and strength must be won by strength--that’s what they don’t know,” he added proudly and self-confidently and he walked with flagging footsteps from the bridge. Pride and self-confidence grew continually stronger in him; he was becoming a different man every moment. What was it had happened to work this revolution in him? He did not know himself; like a man catching at a straw, he suddenly felt that he, too, ‘could live, that there was still life for him, that his life had not died with the old woman.’ Perhaps he was in too great a hurry with his conclusions, but he did not think of that.\n\n“But I did ask her to remember ‘Thy servant Rodion’ in her prayers,” the idea struck him. “Well, that was... in case of emergency,” he added and laughed himself at his boyish sally. He was in the best of spirits.\n\nHe easily found Razumihin; the new lodger was already known at Potchinkov’s and the porter at once showed him the way. Half-way upstairs he could hear the noise and animated conversation of a big gathering of people. The door was wide open on the stairs; he could hear exclamations and discussion. Razumihin’s room was fairly large; the company consisted of fifteen people. Raskolnikov stopped in the entry, where two of the landlady’s servants were busy behind a screen with two samovars, bottles, plates and dishes of pie and savouries, brought up from the landlady’s kitchen. Raskolnikov sent in for Razumihin. He ran out delighted. At the first glance it was apparent that he had had a great deal to drink and, though no amount of liquor made Razumihin quite drunk, this time he was perceptibly affected by it.\n\n“Listen,” Raskolnikov hastened to say, “I’ve only just come to tell you you’ve won your bet and that no one really knows what may not happen to him. I can’t come in; I am so weak that I shall fall down directly. And so good evening and good-bye! Come and see me to-morrow.”\n\n“Do you know what? I’ll see you home. If you say you’re weak yourself, you must...”\n\n“And your visitors? Who is the curly-headed one who has just peeped out?”\n\n“He? Goodness only knows! Some friend of uncle’s, I expect, or perhaps he has come without being invited... I’ll leave uncle with them, he is an invaluable person, pity I can’t introduce you to him now. But confound them all now! They won’t notice me, and I need a little fresh air, for you’ve come just in the nick of time--another two minutes and I should have come to blows! They are talking such a lot of wild stuff... you simply can’t imagine what men will say! Though why shouldn’t you imagine? Don’t we talk nonsense ourselves? And let them... that’s the way to learn not to!... Wait a minute, I’ll fetch Zossimov.”\n\nZossimov pounced upon Raskolnikov almost greedily; he showed a special interest in him; soon his face brightened.\n\n“You must go to bed at once,” he pronounced, examining the patient as far as he could, “and take something for the night. Will you take it? I got it ready some time ago... a powder.”\n\n“Two, if you like,” answered Raskolnikov. The powder was taken at once.\n\n“It’s a good thing you are taking him home,” observed Zossimov to Razumihin--“we shall see how he is to-morrow, to-day he’s not at all amiss--a considerable change since the afternoon. Live and learn...”\n\n“Do you know what Zossimov whispered to me when we were coming out?” Razumihin blurted out, as soon as they were in the street. “I won’t tell you everything, brother, because they are such fools. Zossimov told me to talk freely to you on the way and get you to talk freely to me, and afterwards I am to tell him about it, for he’s got a notion in his head that you are... mad or close on it. Only fancy! In the first place, you’ve three times the brains he has; in the second, if you are not mad, you needn’t care a hang that he has got such a wild idea; and thirdly, that piece of beef whose specialty is surgery has gone mad on mental diseases, and what’s brought him to this conclusion about you was your conversation to-day with Zametov.”\n\n“Zametov told you all about it?”\n\n“Yes, and he did well. Now I understand what it all means and so does Zametov.... Well, the fact is, Rodya... the point is... I am a little drunk now.... But that’s... no matter... the point is that this idea... you understand? was just being hatched in their brains... you understand? That is, no one ventured to say it aloud, because the idea is too absurd and especially since the arrest of that painter, that bubble’s burst and gone for ever. But why are they such fools? I gave Zametov a bit of a thrashing at the time--that’s between ourselves, brother; please don’t let out a hint that you know of it; I’ve noticed he is a ticklish subject; it was at Luise Ivanovna’s. But to-day, to-day it’s all cleared up. That Ilya Petrovitch is at the bottom of it! He took advantage of your fainting at the police station, but he is ashamed of it himself now; I know that...”\n\nRaskolnikov listened greedily. Razumihin was drunk enough to talk too freely.\n\n“I fainted then because it was so close and the smell of paint,” said Raskolnikov.\n\n“No need to explain that! And it wasn’t the paint only: the fever had been coming on for a month; Zossimov testifies to that! But how crushed that boy is now, you wouldn’t believe! ‘I am not worth his little finger,’ he says. Yours, he means. He has good feelings at times, brother. But the lesson, the lesson you gave him to-day in the Palais de Cristal, that was too good for anything! You frightened him at first, you know, he nearly went into convulsions! You almost convinced him again of the truth of all that hideous nonsense, and then you suddenly--put out your tongue at him: ‘There now, what do you make of it?’ It was perfect! He is crushed, annihilated now! It was masterly, by Jove, it’s what they deserve! Ah, that I wasn’t there! He was hoping to see you awfully. Porfiry, too, wants to make your acquaintance...”\n\n“Ah!... he too... but why did they put me down as mad?”\n\n“Oh, not mad. I must have said too much, brother.... What struck him, you see, was that only that subject seemed to interest you; now it’s clear why it did interest you; knowing all the circumstances... and how that irritated you and worked in with your illness... I am a little drunk, brother, only, confound him, he has some idea of his own... I tell you, he’s mad on mental diseases. But don’t you mind him...”\n\nFor half a minute both were silent.\n\n“Listen, Razumihin,” began Raskolnikov, “I want to tell you plainly: I’ve just been at a death-bed, a clerk who died... I gave them all my money... and besides I’ve just been kissed by someone who, if I had killed anyone, would just the same... in fact I saw someone else there... with a flame-coloured feather... but I am talking nonsense; I am very weak, support me... we shall be at the stairs directly...”\n\n“What’s the matter? What’s the matter with you?” Razumihin asked anxiously.\n\n“I am a little giddy, but that’s not the point, I am so sad, so sad... like a woman. Look, what’s that? Look, look!”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Don’t you see? A light in my room, you see? Through the crack...”\n\nThey were already at the foot of the last flight of stairs, at the level of the landlady’s door, and they could, as a fact, see from below that there was a light in Raskolnikov’s garret.\n\n“Queer! Nastasya, perhaps,” observed Razumihin.\n\n“She is never in my room at this time and she must be in bed long ago, but... I don’t care! Good-bye!”\n\n“What do you mean? I am coming with you, we’ll come in together!”\n\n“I know we are going in together, but I want to shake hands here and say good-bye to you here. So give me your hand, good-bye!”\n\n“What’s the matter with you, Rodya?”\n\n“Nothing... come along... you shall be witness.”\n\nThey began mounting the stairs, and the idea struck Razumihin that perhaps Zossimov might be right after all. “Ah, I’ve upset him with my chatter!” he muttered to himself.\n\nWhen they reached the door they heard voices in the room.\n\n“What is it?” cried Razumihin. Raskolnikov was the first to open the door; he flung it wide and stood still in the doorway, dumbfoundered.\n\nHis mother and sister were sitting on his sofa and had been waiting an hour and a half for him. Why had he never expected, never thought of them, though the news that they had started, were on their way and would arrive immediately, had been repeated to him only that day? They had spent that hour and a half plying Nastasya with questions. She was standing before them and had told them everything by now. They were beside themselves with alarm when they heard of his “running away” to-day, ill and, as they understood from her story, delirious! “Good Heavens, what had become of him?” Both had been weeping, both had been in anguish for that hour and a half.\n\nA cry of joy, of ecstasy, greeted Raskolnikov’s entrance. Both rushed to him. But he stood like one dead; a sudden intolerable sensation struck him like a thunderbolt. He did not lift his arms to embrace them, he could not. His mother and sister clasped him in their arms, kissed him, laughed and cried. He took a step, tottered and fell to the ground, fainting.\n\nAnxiety, cries of horror, moans... Razumihin who was standing in the doorway flew into the room, seized the sick man in his strong arms and in a moment had him on the sofa.\n\n“It’s nothing, nothing!” he cried to the mother and sister--“it’s only a faint, a mere trifle! Only just now the doctor said he was much better, that he is perfectly well! Water! See, he is coming to himself, he is all right again!”\n\nAnd seizing Dounia by the arm so that he almost dislocated it, he made her bend down to see that “he is all right again.” The mother and sister looked on him with emotion and gratitude, as their Providence. They had heard already from Nastasya all that had been done for their Rodya during his illness, by this “very competent young man,” as Pulcheria Alexandrovna Raskolnikov called him that evening in conversation with Dounia.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nRaskolnikov got up, and sat down on the sofa. He waved his hand weakly to Razumihin to cut short the flow of warm and incoherent consolations he was addressing to his mother and sister, took them both by the hand and for a minute or two gazed from one to the other without speaking. His mother was alarmed by his expression. It revealed an emotion agonisingly poignant, and at the same time something immovable, almost insane. Pulcheria Alexandrovna began to cry.\n\nAvdotya Romanovna was pale; her hand trembled in her brother’s.\n\n“Go home... with him,” he said in a broken voice, pointing to Razumihin, “good-bye till to-morrow; to-morrow everything... Is it long since you arrived?”\n\n“This evening, Rodya,” answered Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “the train was awfully late. But, Rodya, nothing would induce me to leave you now! I will spend the night here, near you...”\n\n“Don’t torture me!” he said with a gesture of irritation.\n\n“I will stay with him,” cried Razumihin, “I won’t leave him for a moment. Bother all my visitors! Let them rage to their hearts’ content! My uncle is presiding there.”\n\n“How, how can I thank you!” Pulcheria Alexandrovna was beginning, once more pressing Razumihin’s hands, but Raskolnikov interrupted her again.\n\n“I can’t have it! I can’t have it!” he repeated irritably, “don’t worry me! Enough, go away... I can’t stand it!”\n\n“Come, mamma, come out of the room at least for a minute,” Dounia whispered in dismay; “we are distressing him, that’s evident.”\n\n“Mayn’t I look at him after three years?” wept Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Stay,” he stopped them again, “you keep interrupting me, and my ideas get muddled.... Have you seen Luzhin?”\n\n“No, Rodya, but he knows already of our arrival. We have heard, Rodya, that Pyotr Petrovitch was so kind as to visit you today,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna added somewhat timidly.\n\n“Yes... he was so kind... Dounia, I promised Luzhin I’d throw him downstairs and told him to go to hell....”\n\n“Rodya, what are you saying! Surely, you don’t mean to tell us...” Pulcheria Alexandrovna began in alarm, but she stopped, looking at Dounia.\n\nAvdotya Romanovna was looking attentively at her brother, waiting for what would come next. Both of them had heard of the quarrel from Nastasya, so far as she had succeeded in understanding and reporting it, and were in painful perplexity and suspense.\n\n“Dounia,” Raskolnikov continued with an effort, “I don’t want that marriage, so at the first opportunity to-morrow you must refuse Luzhin, so that we may never hear his name again.”\n\n“Good Heavens!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Brother, think what you are saying!” Avdotya Romanovna began impetuously, but immediately checked herself. “You are not fit to talk now, perhaps; you are tired,” she added gently.\n\n“You think I am delirious? No... You are marrying Luzhin for _my_ sake. But I won’t accept the sacrifice. And so write a letter before to-morrow, to refuse him... Let me read it in the morning and that will be the end of it!”\n\n“That I can’t do!” the girl cried, offended, “what right have you...”\n\n“Dounia, you are hasty, too, be quiet, to-morrow... Don’t you see...” the mother interposed in dismay. “Better come away!”\n\n“He is raving,” Razumihin cried tipsily, “or how would he dare! To-morrow all this nonsense will be over... to-day he certainly did drive him away. That was so. And Luzhin got angry, too.... He made speeches here, wanted to show off his learning and he went out crest-fallen....”\n\n“Then it’s true?” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Good-bye till to-morrow, brother,” said Dounia compassionately--“let us go, mother... Good-bye, Rodya.”\n\n“Do you hear, sister,” he repeated after them, making a last effort, “I am not delirious; this marriage is--an infamy. Let me act like a scoundrel, but you mustn’t... one is enough... and though I am a scoundrel, I wouldn’t own such a sister. It’s me or Luzhin! Go now....”\n\n“But you’re out of your mind! Despot!” roared Razumihin; but Raskolnikov did not and perhaps could not answer. He lay down on the sofa, and turned to the wall, utterly exhausted. Avdotya Romanovna looked with interest at Razumihin; her black eyes flashed; Razumihin positively started at her glance.\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna stood overwhelmed.\n\n“Nothing would induce me to go,” she whispered in despair to Razumihin. “I will stay somewhere here... escort Dounia home.”\n\n“You’ll spoil everything,” Razumihin answered in the same whisper, losing patience--“come out on to the stairs, anyway. Nastasya, show a light! I assure you,” he went on in a half whisper on the stairs--“that he was almost beating the doctor and me this afternoon! Do you understand? The doctor himself! Even he gave way and left him, so as not to irritate him. I remained downstairs on guard, but he dressed at once and slipped off. And he will slip off again if you irritate him, at this time of night, and will do himself some mischief....”\n\n“What are you saying?”\n\n“And Avdotya Romanovna can’t possibly be left in those lodgings without you. Just think where you are staying! That blackguard Pyotr Petrovitch couldn’t find you better lodgings... But you know I’ve had a little to drink, and that’s what makes me... swear; don’t mind it....”\n\n“But I’ll go to the landlady here,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna insisted, “I’ll beseech her to find some corner for Dounia and me for the night. I can’t leave him like that, I cannot!”\n\nThis conversation took place on the landing just before the landlady’s door. Nastasya lighted them from a step below. Razumihin was in extraordinary excitement. Half an hour earlier, while he was bringing Raskolnikov home, he had indeed talked too freely, but he was aware of it himself, and his head was clear in spite of the vast quantities he had imbibed. Now he was in a state bordering on ecstasy, and all that he had drunk seemed to fly to his head with redoubled effect. He stood with the two ladies, seizing both by their hands, persuading them, and giving them reasons with astonishing plainness of speech, and at almost every word he uttered, probably to emphasise his arguments, he squeezed their hands painfully as in a vise. He stared at Avdotya Romanovna without the least regard for good manners. They sometimes pulled their hands out of his huge bony paws, but far from noticing what was the matter, he drew them all the closer to him. If they’d told him to jump head foremost from the staircase, he would have done it without thought or hesitation in their service. Though Pulcheria Alexandrovna felt that the young man was really too eccentric and pinched her hand too much, in her anxiety over her Rodya she looked on his presence as providential, and was unwilling to notice all his peculiarities. But though Avdotya Romanovna shared her anxiety, and was not of timorous disposition, she could not see the glowing light in his eyes without wonder and almost alarm. It was only the unbounded confidence inspired by Nastasya’s account of her brother’s queer friend, which prevented her from trying to run away from him, and to persuade her mother to do the same. She realised, too, that even running away was perhaps impossible now. Ten minutes later, however, she was considerably reassured; it was characteristic of Razumihin that he showed his true nature at once, whatever mood he might be in, so that people quickly saw the sort of man they had to deal with.\n\n“You can’t go to the landlady, that’s perfect nonsense!” he cried. “If you stay, though you are his mother, you’ll drive him to a frenzy, and then goodness knows what will happen! Listen, I’ll tell you what I’ll do: Nastasya will stay with him now, and I’ll conduct you both home, you can’t be in the streets alone; Petersburg is an awful place in that way.... But no matter! Then I’ll run straight back here and a quarter of an hour later, on my word of honour, I’ll bring you news how he is, whether he is asleep, and all that. Then, listen! Then I’ll run home in a twinkling--I’ve a lot of friends there, all drunk--I’ll fetch Zossimov--that’s the doctor who is looking after him, he is there, too, but he is not drunk; he is not drunk, he is never drunk! I’ll drag him to Rodya, and then to you, so that you’ll get two reports in the hour--from the doctor, you understand, from the doctor himself, that’s a very different thing from my account of him! If there’s anything wrong, I swear I’ll bring you here myself, but, if it’s all right, you go to bed. And I’ll spend the night here, in the passage, he won’t hear me, and I’ll tell Zossimov to sleep at the landlady’s, to be at hand. Which is better for him: you or the doctor? So come home then! But the landlady is out of the question; it’s all right for me, but it’s out of the question for you: she wouldn’t take you, for she’s... for she’s a fool... She’d be jealous on my account of Avdotya Romanovna and of you, too, if you want to know... of Avdotya Romanovna certainly. She is an absolutely, absolutely unaccountable character! But I am a fool, too!... No matter! Come along! Do you trust me? Come, do you trust me or not?”\n\n“Let us go, mother,” said Avdotya Romanovna, “he will certainly do what he has promised. He has saved Rodya already, and if the doctor really will consent to spend the night here, what could be better?”\n\n“You see, you... you... understand me, because you are an angel!” Razumihin cried in ecstasy, “let us go! Nastasya! Fly upstairs and sit with him with a light; I’ll come in a quarter of an hour.”\n\nThough Pulcheria Alexandrovna was not perfectly convinced, she made no further resistance. Razumihin gave an arm to each and drew them down the stairs. He still made her uneasy, as though he was competent and good-natured, was he capable of carrying out his promise? He seemed in such a condition....\n\n“Ah, I see you think I am in such a condition!” Razumihin broke in upon her thoughts, guessing them, as he strolled along the pavement with huge steps, so that the two ladies could hardly keep up with him, a fact he did not observe, however. “Nonsense! That is... I am drunk like a fool, but that’s not it; I am not drunk from wine. It’s seeing you has turned my head... But don’t mind me! Don’t take any notice: I am talking nonsense, I am not worthy of you.... I am utterly unworthy of you! The minute I’ve taken you home, I’ll pour a couple of pailfuls of water over my head in the gutter here, and then I shall be all right.... If only you knew how I love you both! Don’t laugh, and don’t be angry! You may be angry with anyone, but not with me! I am his friend, and therefore I am your friend, too, I want to be... I had a presentiment... Last year there was a moment... though it wasn’t a presentiment really, for you seem to have fallen from heaven. And I expect I shan’t sleep all night... Zossimov was afraid a little time ago that he would go mad... that’s why he mustn’t be irritated.”\n\n“What do you say?” cried the mother.\n\n“Did the doctor really say that?” asked Avdotya Romanovna, alarmed.\n\n“Yes, but it’s not so, not a bit of it. He gave him some medicine, a powder, I saw it, and then your coming here.... Ah! It would have been better if you had come to-morrow. It’s a good thing we went away. And in an hour Zossimov himself will report to you about everything. He is not drunk! And I shan’t be drunk.... And what made me get so tight? Because they got me into an argument, damn them! I’ve sworn never to argue! They talk such trash! I almost came to blows! I’ve left my uncle to preside. Would you believe, they insist on complete absence of individualism and that’s just what they relish! Not to be themselves, to be as unlike themselves as they can. That’s what they regard as the highest point of progress. If only their nonsense were their own, but as it is...”\n\n“Listen!” Pulcheria Alexandrovna interrupted timidly, but it only added fuel to the flames.\n\n“What do you think?” shouted Razumihin, louder than ever, “you think I am attacking them for talking nonsense? Not a bit! I like them to talk nonsense. That’s man’s one privilege over all creation. Through error you come to the truth! I am a man because I err! You never reach any truth without making fourteen mistakes and very likely a hundred and fourteen. And a fine thing, too, in its way; but we can’t even make mistakes on our own account! Talk nonsense, but talk your own nonsense, and I’ll kiss you for it. To go wrong in one’s own way is better than to go right in someone else’s. In the first case you are a man, in the second you’re no better than a bird. Truth won’t escape you, but life can be cramped. There have been examples. And what are we doing now? In science, development, thought, invention, ideals, aims, liberalism, judgment, experience and everything, everything, everything, we are still in the preparatory class at school. We prefer to live on other people’s ideas, it’s what we are used to! Am I right, am I right?” cried Razumihin, pressing and shaking the two ladies’ hands.\n\n“Oh, mercy, I do not know,” cried poor Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Yes, yes... though I don’t agree with you in everything,” added Avdotya Romanovna earnestly and at once uttered a cry, for he squeezed her hand so painfully.\n\n“Yes, you say yes... well after that you... you...” he cried in a transport, “you are a fount of goodness, purity, sense... and perfection. Give me your hand... you give me yours, too! I want to kiss your hands here at once, on my knees...” and he fell on his knees on the pavement, fortunately at that time deserted.\n\n“Leave off, I entreat you, what are you doing?” Pulcheria Alexandrovna cried, greatly distressed.\n\n“Get up, get up!” said Dounia laughing, though she, too, was upset.\n\n“Not for anything till you let me kiss your hands! That’s it! Enough! I get up and we’ll go on! I am a luckless fool, I am unworthy of you and drunk... and I am ashamed.... I am not worthy to love you, but to do homage to you is the duty of every man who is not a perfect beast! And I’ve done homage.... Here are your lodgings, and for that alone Rodya was right in driving your Pyotr Petrovitch away.... How dare he! how dare he put you in such lodgings! It’s a scandal! Do you know the sort of people they take in here? And you his betrothed! You are his betrothed? Yes? Well, then, I’ll tell you, your _fiancé_ is a scoundrel.”\n\n“Excuse me, Mr. Razumihin, you are forgetting...” Pulcheria Alexandrovna was beginning.\n\n“Yes, yes, you are right, I did forget myself, I am ashamed of it,” Razumihin made haste to apologise. “But... but you can’t be angry with me for speaking so! For I speak sincerely and not because... hm, hm! That would be disgraceful; in fact not because I’m in... hm! Well, anyway, I won’t say why, I daren’t.... But we all saw to-day when he came in that that man is not of our sort. Not because he had his hair curled at the barber’s, not because he was in such a hurry to show his wit, but because he is a spy, a speculator, because he is a skin-flint and a buffoon. That’s evident. Do you think him clever? No, he is a fool, a fool. And is he a match for you? Good heavens! Do you see, ladies?” he stopped suddenly on the way upstairs to their rooms, “though all my friends there are drunk, yet they are all honest, and though we do talk a lot of trash, and I do, too, yet we shall talk our way to the truth at last, for we are on the right path, while Pyotr Petrovitch... is not on the right path. Though I’ve been calling them all sorts of names just now, I do respect them all... though I don’t respect Zametov, I like him, for he is a puppy, and that bullock Zossimov, because he is an honest man and knows his work. But enough, it’s all said and forgiven. Is it forgiven? Well, then, let’s go on. I know this corridor, I’ve been here, there was a scandal here at Number 3.... Where are you here? Which number? eight? Well, lock yourselves in for the night, then. Don’t let anybody in. In a quarter of an hour I’ll come back with news, and half an hour later I’ll bring Zossimov, you’ll see! Good-bye, I’ll run.”\n\n“Good heavens, Dounia, what is going to happen?” said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, addressing her daughter with anxiety and dismay.\n\n“Don’t worry yourself, mother,” said Dounia, taking off her hat and cape. “God has sent this gentleman to our aid, though he has come from a drinking party. We can depend on him, I assure you. And all that he has done for Rodya....”\n\n“Ah. Dounia, goodness knows whether he will come! How could I bring myself to leave Rodya?... And how different, how different I had fancied our meeting! How sullen he was, as though not pleased to see us....”\n\nTears came into her eyes.\n\n“No, it’s not that, mother. You didn’t see, you were crying all the time. He is quite unhinged by serious illness--that’s the reason.”\n\n“Ah, that illness! What will happen, what will happen? And how he talked to you, Dounia!” said the mother, looking timidly at her daughter, trying to read her thoughts and, already half consoled by Dounia’s standing up for her brother, which meant that she had already forgiven him. “I am sure he will think better of it to-morrow,” she added, probing her further.\n\n“And I am sure that he will say the same to-morrow... about that,” Avdotya Romanovna said finally. And, of course, there was no going beyond that, for this was a point which Pulcheria Alexandrovna was afraid to discuss. Dounia went up and kissed her mother. The latter warmly embraced her without speaking. Then she sat down to wait anxiously for Razumihin’s return, timidly watching her daughter who walked up and down the room with her arms folded, lost in thought. This walking up and down when she was thinking was a habit of Avdotya Romanovna’s and the mother was always afraid to break in on her daughter’s mood at such moments.\n\nRazumihin, of course, was ridiculous in his sudden drunken infatuation for Avdotya Romanovna. Yet apart from his eccentric condition, many people would have thought it justified if they had seen Avdotya Romanovna, especially at that moment when she was walking to and fro with folded arms, pensive and melancholy. Avdotya Romanovna was remarkably good-looking; she was tall, strikingly well-proportioned, strong and self-reliant--the latter quality was apparent in every gesture, though it did not in the least detract from the grace and softness of her movements. In face she resembled her brother, but she might be described as really beautiful. Her hair was dark brown, a little lighter than her brother’s; there was a proud light in her almost black eyes and yet at times a look of extraordinary kindness. She was pale, but it was a healthy pallor; her face was radiant with freshness and vigour. Her mouth was rather small; the full red lower lip projected a little as did her chin; it was the only irregularity in her beautiful face, but it gave it a peculiarly individual and almost haughty expression. Her face was always more serious and thoughtful than gay; but how well smiles, how well youthful, lighthearted, irresponsible, laughter suited her face! It was natural enough that a warm, open, simple-hearted, honest giant like Razumihin, who had never seen anyone like her and was not quite sober at the time, should lose his head immediately. Besides, as chance would have it, he saw Dounia for the first time transfigured by her love for her brother and her joy at meeting him. Afterwards he saw her lower lip quiver with indignation at her brother’s insolent, cruel and ungrateful words--and his fate was sealed.\n\nHe had spoken the truth, moreover, when he blurted out in his drunken talk on the stairs that Praskovya Pavlovna, Raskolnikov’s eccentric landlady, would be jealous of Pulcheria Alexandrovna as well as of Avdotya Romanovna on his account. Although Pulcheria Alexandrovna was forty-three, her face still retained traces of her former beauty; she looked much younger than her age, indeed, which is almost always the case with women who retain serenity of spirit, sensitiveness and pure sincere warmth of heart to old age. We may add in parenthesis that to preserve all this is the only means of retaining beauty to old age. Her hair had begun to grow grey and thin, there had long been little crow’s foot wrinkles round her eyes, her cheeks were hollow and sunken from anxiety and grief, and yet it was a handsome face. She was Dounia over again, twenty years older, but without the projecting underlip. Pulcheria Alexandrovna was emotional, but not sentimental, timid and yielding, but only to a certain point. She could give way and accept a great deal even of what was contrary to her convictions, but there was a certain barrier fixed by honesty, principle and the deepest convictions which nothing would induce her to cross.\n\nExactly twenty minutes after Razumihin’s departure, there came two subdued but hurried knocks at the door: he had come back.\n\n“I won’t come in, I haven’t time,” he hastened to say when the door was opened. “He sleeps like a top, soundly, quietly, and God grant he may sleep ten hours. Nastasya’s with him; I told her not to leave till I came. Now I am fetching Zossimov, he will report to you and then you’d better turn in; I can see you are too tired to do anything....”\n\nAnd he ran off down the corridor.\n\n“What a very competent and... devoted young man!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna exceedingly delighted.\n\n“He seems a splendid person!” Avdotya Romanovna replied with some warmth, resuming her walk up and down the room.\n\nIt was nearly an hour later when they heard footsteps in the corridor and another knock at the door. Both women waited this time completely relying on Razumihin’s promise; he actually had succeeded in bringing Zossimov. Zossimov had agreed at once to desert the drinking party to go to Raskolnikov’s, but he came reluctantly and with the greatest suspicion to see the ladies, mistrusting Razumihin in his exhilarated condition. But his vanity was at once reassured and flattered; he saw that they were really expecting him as an oracle. He stayed just ten minutes and succeeded in completely convincing and comforting Pulcheria Alexandrovna. He spoke with marked sympathy, but with the reserve and extreme seriousness of a young doctor at an important consultation. He did not utter a word on any other subject and did not display the slightest desire to enter into more personal relations with the two ladies. Remarking at his first entrance the dazzling beauty of Avdotya Romanovna, he endeavoured not to notice her at all during his visit and addressed himself solely to Pulcheria Alexandrovna. All this gave him extraordinary inward satisfaction. He declared that he thought the invalid at this moment going on very satisfactorily. According to his observations the patient’s illness was due partly to his unfortunate material surroundings during the last few months, but it had partly also a moral origin, “was, so to speak, the product of several material and moral influences, anxieties, apprehensions, troubles, certain ideas... and so on.” Noticing stealthily that Avdotya Romanovna was following his words with close attention, Zossimov allowed himself to enlarge on this theme. On Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s anxiously and timidly inquiring as to “some suspicion of insanity,” he replied with a composed and candid smile that his words had been exaggerated; that certainly the patient had some fixed idea, something approaching a monomania--he, Zossimov, was now particularly studying this interesting branch of medicine--but that it must be recollected that until to-day the patient had been in delirium and... and that no doubt the presence of his family would have a favourable effect on his recovery and distract his mind, “if only all fresh shocks can be avoided,” he added significantly. Then he got up, took leave with an impressive and affable bow, while blessings, warm gratitude, and entreaties were showered upon him, and Avdotya Romanovna spontaneously offered her hand to him. He went out exceedingly pleased with his visit and still more so with himself.\n\n“We’ll talk to-morrow; go to bed at once!” Razumihin said in conclusion, following Zossimov out. “I’ll be with you to-morrow morning as early as possible with my report.”\n\n“That’s a fetching little girl, Avdotya Romanovna,” remarked Zossimov, almost licking his lips as they both came out into the street.\n\n“Fetching? You said fetching?” roared Razumihin and he flew at Zossimov and seized him by the throat. “If you ever dare.... Do you understand? Do you understand?” he shouted, shaking him by the collar and squeezing him against the wall. “Do you hear?”\n\n“Let me go, you drunken devil,” said Zossimov, struggling and when he had let him go, he stared at him and went off into a sudden guffaw. Razumihin stood facing him in gloomy and earnest reflection.\n\n“Of course, I am an ass,” he observed, sombre as a storm cloud, “but still... you are another.”\n\n“No, brother, not at all such another. I am not dreaming of any folly.”\n\nThey walked along in silence and only when they were close to Raskolnikov’s lodgings, Razumihin broke the silence in considerable anxiety.\n\n“Listen,” he said, “you’re a first-rate fellow, but among your other failings, you’re a loose fish, that I know, and a dirty one, too. You are a feeble, nervous wretch, and a mass of whims, you’re getting fat and lazy and can’t deny yourself anything--and I call that dirty because it leads one straight into the dirt. You’ve let yourself get so slack that I don’t know how it is you are still a good, even a devoted doctor. You--a doctor--sleep on a feather bed and get up at night to your patients! In another three or four years you won’t get up for your patients... But hang it all, that’s not the point!... You are going to spend to-night in the landlady’s flat here. (Hard work I’ve had to persuade her!) And I’ll be in the kitchen. So here’s a chance for you to get to know her better.... It’s not as you think! There’s not a trace of anything of the sort, brother...!”\n\n“But I don’t think!”\n\n“Here you have modesty, brother, silence, bashfulness, a savage virtue... and yet she’s sighing and melting like wax, simply melting! Save me from her, by all that’s unholy! She’s most prepossessing... I’ll repay you, I’ll do anything....”\n\nZossimov laughed more violently than ever.\n\n“Well, you are smitten! But what am I to do with her?”\n\n“It won’t be much trouble, I assure you. Talk any rot you like to her, as long as you sit by her and talk. You’re a doctor, too; try curing her of something. I swear you won’t regret it. She has a piano, and you know, I strum a little. I have a song there, a genuine Russian one: ‘I shed hot tears.’ She likes the genuine article--and well, it all began with that song; Now you’re a regular performer, a _maître_, a Rubinstein.... I assure you, you won’t regret it!”\n\n“But have you made her some promise? Something signed? A promise of marriage, perhaps?”\n\n“Nothing, nothing, absolutely nothing of the kind! Besides she is not that sort at all.... Tchebarov tried that....”\n\n“Well then, drop her!”\n\n“But I can’t drop her like that!”\n\n“Why can’t you?”\n\n“Well, I can’t, that’s all about it! There’s an element of attraction here, brother.”\n\n“Then why have you fascinated her?”\n\n“I haven’t fascinated her; perhaps I was fascinated myself in my folly. But she won’t care a straw whether it’s you or I, so long as somebody sits beside her, sighing.... I can’t explain the position, brother... look here, you are good at mathematics, and working at it now... begin teaching her the integral calculus; upon my soul, I’m not joking, I’m in earnest, it’ll be just the same to her. She will gaze at you and sigh for a whole year together. I talked to her once for two days at a time about the Prussian House of Lords (for one must talk of something)--she just sighed and perspired! And you mustn’t talk of love--she’s bashful to hysterics--but just let her see you can’t tear yourself away--that’s enough. It’s fearfully comfortable; you’re quite at home, you can read, sit, lie about, write. You may even venture on a kiss, if you’re careful.”\n\n“But what do I want with her?”\n\n“Ach, I can’t make you understand! You see, you are made for each other! I have often been reminded of you!... You’ll come to it in the end! So does it matter whether it’s sooner or later? There’s the feather-bed element here, brother--ach! and not only that! There’s an attraction here--here you have the end of the world, an anchorage, a quiet haven, the navel of the earth, the three fishes that are the foundation of the world, the essence of pancakes, of savoury fish-pies, of the evening samovar, of soft sighs and warm shawls, and hot stoves to sleep on--as snug as though you were dead, and yet you’re alive--the advantages of both at once! Well, hang it, brother, what stuff I’m talking, it’s bedtime! Listen. I sometimes wake up at night; so I’ll go in and look at him. But there’s no need, it’s all right. Don’t you worry yourself, yet if you like, you might just look in once, too. But if you notice anything--delirium or fever--wake me at once. But there can’t be....”\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nRazumihin waked up next morning at eight o’clock, troubled and serious. He found himself confronted with many new and unlooked-for perplexities. He had never expected that he would ever wake up feeling like that. He remembered every detail of the previous day and he knew that a perfectly novel experience had befallen him, that he had received an impression unlike anything he had known before. At the same time he recognised clearly that the dream which had fired his imagination was hopelessly unattainable--so unattainable that he felt positively ashamed of it, and he hastened to pass to the other more practical cares and difficulties bequeathed him by that “thrice accursed yesterday.”\n\nThe most awful recollection of the previous day was the way he had shown himself “base and mean,” not only because he had been drunk, but because he had taken advantage of the young girl’s position to abuse her _fiancé_ in his stupid jealousy, knowing nothing of their mutual relations and obligations and next to nothing of the man himself. And what right had he to criticise him in that hasty and unguarded manner? Who had asked for his opinion? Was it thinkable that such a creature as Avdotya Romanovna would be marrying an unworthy man for money? So there must be something in him. The lodgings? But after all how could he know the character of the lodgings? He was furnishing a flat... Foo! how despicable it all was! And what justification was it that he was drunk? Such a stupid excuse was even more degrading! In wine is truth, and the truth had all come out, “that is, all the uncleanness of his coarse and envious heart”! And would such a dream ever be permissible to him, Razumihin? What was he beside such a girl--he, the drunken noisy braggart of last night? Was it possible to imagine so absurd and cynical a juxtaposition? Razumihin blushed desperately at the very idea and suddenly the recollection forced itself vividly upon him of how he had said last night on the stairs that the landlady would be jealous of Avdotya Romanovna... that was simply intolerable. He brought his fist down heavily on the kitchen stove, hurt his hand and sent one of the bricks flying.\n\n“Of course,” he muttered to himself a minute later with a feeling of self-abasement, “of course, all these infamies can never be wiped out or smoothed over... and so it’s useless even to think of it, and I must go to them in silence and do my duty... in silence, too... and not ask forgiveness, and say nothing... for all is lost now!”\n\nAnd yet as he dressed he examined his attire more carefully than usual. He hadn’t another suit--if he had had, perhaps he wouldn’t have put it on. “I would have made a point of not putting it on.” But in any case he could not remain a cynic and a dirty sloven; he had no right to offend the feelings of others, especially when they were in need of his assistance and asking him to see them. He brushed his clothes carefully. His linen was always decent; in that respect he was especially clean.\n\nHe washed that morning scrupulously--he got some soap from Nastasya--he washed his hair, his neck and especially his hands. When it came to the question whether to shave his stubbly chin or not (Praskovya Pavlovna had capital razors that had been left by her late husband), the question was angrily answered in the negative. “Let it stay as it is! What if they think that I shaved on purpose to...? They certainly would think so! Not on any account!”\n\n“And... the worst of it was he was so coarse, so dirty, he had the manners of a pothouse; and... and even admitting that he knew he had some of the essentials of a gentleman... what was there in that to be proud of? Everyone ought to be a gentleman and more than that... and all the same (he remembered) he, too, had done little things... not exactly dishonest, and yet.... And what thoughts he sometimes had; hm... and to set all that beside Avdotya Romanovna! Confound it! So be it! Well, he’d make a point then of being dirty, greasy, pothouse in his manners and he wouldn’t care! He’d be worse!”\n\nHe was engaged in such monologues when Zossimov, who had spent the night in Praskovya Pavlovna’s parlour, came in.\n\nHe was going home and was in a hurry to look at the invalid first. Razumihin informed him that Raskolnikov was sleeping like a dormouse. Zossimov gave orders that they shouldn’t wake him and promised to see him again about eleven.\n\n“If he is still at home,” he added. “Damn it all! If one can’t control one’s patients, how is one to cure them? Do you know whether _he_ will go to them, or whether _they_ are coming here?”\n\n“They are coming, I think,” said Razumihin, understanding the object of the question, “and they will discuss their family affairs, no doubt. I’ll be off. You, as the doctor, have more right to be here than I.”\n\n“But I am not a father confessor; I shall come and go away; I’ve plenty to do besides looking after them.”\n\n“One thing worries me,” interposed Razumihin, frowning. “On the way home I talked a lot of drunken nonsense to him... all sorts of things... and amongst them that you were afraid that he... might become insane.”\n\n“You told the ladies so, too.”\n\n“I know it was stupid! You may beat me if you like! Did you think so seriously?”\n\n“That’s nonsense, I tell you, how could I think it seriously? You, yourself, described him as a monomaniac when you fetched me to him... and we added fuel to the fire yesterday, you did, that is, with your story about the painter; it was a nice conversation, when he was, perhaps, mad on that very point! If only I’d known what happened then at the police station and that some wretch... had insulted him with this suspicion! Hm... I would not have allowed that conversation yesterday. These monomaniacs will make a mountain out of a mole-hill... and see their fancies as solid realities.... As far as I remember, it was Zametov’s story that cleared up half the mystery, to my mind. Why, I know one case in which a hypochondriac, a man of forty, cut the throat of a little boy of eight, because he couldn’t endure the jokes he made every day at table! And in this case his rags, the insolent police officer, the fever and this suspicion! All that working upon a man half frantic with hypochondria, and with his morbid exceptional vanity! That may well have been the starting-point of illness. Well, bother it all!... And, by the way, that Zametov certainly is a nice fellow, but hm... he shouldn’t have told all that last night. He is an awful chatterbox!”\n\n“But whom did he tell it to? You and me?”\n\n“And Porfiry.”\n\n“What does that matter?”\n\n“And, by the way, have you any influence on them, his mother and sister? Tell them to be more careful with him to-day....”\n\n“They’ll get on all right!” Razumihin answered reluctantly.\n\n“Why is he so set against this Luzhin? A man with money and she doesn’t seem to dislike him... and they haven’t a farthing, I suppose? eh?”\n\n“But what business is it of yours?” Razumihin cried with annoyance. “How can I tell whether they’ve a farthing? Ask them yourself and perhaps you’ll find out....”\n\n“Foo! what an ass you are sometimes! Last night’s wine has not gone off yet.... Good-bye; thank your Praskovya Pavlovna from me for my night’s lodging. She locked herself in, made no reply to my _bonjour_ through the door; she was up at seven o’clock, the samovar was taken into her from the kitchen. I was not vouchsafed a personal interview....”\n\nAt nine o’clock precisely Razumihin reached the lodgings at Bakaleyev’s house. Both ladies were waiting for him with nervous impatience. They had risen at seven o’clock or earlier. He entered looking as black as night, bowed awkwardly and was at once furious with himself for it. He had reckoned without his host: Pulcheria Alexandrovna fairly rushed at him, seized him by both hands and was almost kissing them. He glanced timidly at Avdotya Romanovna, but her proud countenance wore at that moment an expression of such gratitude and friendliness, such complete and unlooked-for respect (in place of the sneering looks and ill-disguised contempt he had expected), that it threw him into greater confusion than if he had been met with abuse. Fortunately there was a subject for conversation, and he made haste to snatch at it.\n\nHearing that everything was going well and that Rodya had not yet waked, Pulcheria Alexandrovna declared that she was glad to hear it, because “she had something which it was very, very necessary to talk over beforehand.” Then followed an inquiry about breakfast and an invitation to have it with them; they had waited to have it with him. Avdotya Romanovna rang the bell: it was answered by a ragged dirty waiter, and they asked him to bring tea which was served at last, but in such a dirty and disorderly way that the ladies were ashamed. Razumihin vigorously attacked the lodgings, but, remembering Luzhin, stopped in embarrassment and was greatly relieved by Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s questions, which showered in a continual stream upon him.\n\nHe talked for three quarters of an hour, being constantly interrupted by their questions, and succeeded in describing to them all the most important facts he knew of the last year of Raskolnikov’s life, concluding with a circumstantial account of his illness. He omitted, however, many things, which were better omitted, including the scene at the police station with all its consequences. They listened eagerly to his story, and, when he thought he had finished and satisfied his listeners, he found that they considered he had hardly begun.\n\n“Tell me, tell me! What do you think...? Excuse me, I still don’t know your name!” Pulcheria Alexandrovna put in hastily.\n\n“Dmitri Prokofitch.”\n\n“I should like very, very much to know, Dmitri Prokofitch... how he looks... on things in general now, that is, how can I explain, what are his likes and dislikes? Is he always so irritable? Tell me, if you can, what are his hopes and, so to say, his dreams? Under what influences is he now? In a word, I should like...”\n\n“Ah, mother, how can he answer all that at once?” observed Dounia.\n\n“Good heavens, I had not expected to find him in the least like this, Dmitri Prokofitch!”\n\n“Naturally,” answered Razumihin. “I have no mother, but my uncle comes every year and almost every time he can scarcely recognise me, even in appearance, though he is a clever man; and your three years’ separation means a great deal. What am I to tell you? I have known Rodion for a year and a half; he is morose, gloomy, proud and haughty, and of late--and perhaps for a long time before--he has been suspicious and fanciful. He has a noble nature and a kind heart. He does not like showing his feelings and would rather do a cruel thing than open his heart freely. Sometimes, though, he is not at all morbid, but simply cold and inhumanly callous; it’s as though he were alternating between two characters. Sometimes he is fearfully reserved! He says he is so busy that everything is a hindrance, and yet he lies in bed doing nothing. He doesn’t jeer at things, not because he hasn’t the wit, but as though he hadn’t time to waste on such trifles. He never listens to what is said to him. He is never interested in what interests other people at any given moment. He thinks very highly of himself and perhaps he is right. Well, what more? I think your arrival will have a most beneficial influence upon him.”\n\n“God grant it may,” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna, distressed by Razumihin’s account of her Rodya.\n\nAnd Razumihin ventured to look more boldly at Avdotya Romanovna at last. He glanced at her often while he was talking, but only for a moment and looked away again at once. Avdotya Romanovna sat at the table, listening attentively, then got up again and began walking to and fro with her arms folded and her lips compressed, occasionally putting in a question, without stopping her walk. She had the same habit of not listening to what was said. She was wearing a dress of thin dark stuff and she had a white transparent scarf round her neck. Razumihin soon detected signs of extreme poverty in their belongings. Had Avdotya Romanovna been dressed like a queen, he felt that he would not be afraid of her, but perhaps just because she was poorly dressed and that he noticed all the misery of her surroundings, his heart was filled with dread and he began to be afraid of every word he uttered, every gesture he made, which was very trying for a man who already felt diffident.\n\n“You’ve told us a great deal that is interesting about my brother’s character... and have told it impartially. I am glad. I thought that you were too uncritically devoted to him,” observed Avdotya Romanovna with a smile. “I think you are right that he needs a woman’s care,” she added thoughtfully.\n\n“I didn’t say so; but I daresay you are right, only...”\n“What?”\n\n“He loves no one and perhaps he never will,” Razumihin declared decisively.\n\n“You mean he is not capable of love?”\n\n“Do you know, Avdotya Romanovna, you are awfully like your brother, in everything, indeed!” he blurted out suddenly to his own surprise, but remembering at once what he had just before said of her brother, he turned as red as a crab and was overcome with confusion. Avdotya Romanovna couldn’t help laughing when she looked at him.\n\n“You may both be mistaken about Rodya,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna remarked, slightly piqued. “I am not talking of our present difficulty, Dounia. What Pyotr Petrovitch writes in this letter and what you and I have supposed may be mistaken, but you can’t imagine, Dmitri Prokofitch, how moody and, so to say, capricious he is. I never could depend on what he would do when he was only fifteen. And I am sure that he might do something now that nobody else would think of doing... Well, for instance, do you know how a year and a half ago he astounded me and gave me a shock that nearly killed me, when he had the idea of marrying that girl--what was her name--his landlady’s daughter?”\n\n“Did you hear about that affair?” asked Avdotya Romanovna.\n\n“Do you suppose----” Pulcheria Alexandrovna continued warmly. “Do you suppose that my tears, my entreaties, my illness, my possible death from grief, our poverty would have made him pause? No, he would calmly have disregarded all obstacles. And yet it isn’t that he doesn’t love us!”\n\n“He has never spoken a word of that affair to me,” Razumihin answered cautiously. “But I did hear something from Praskovya Pavlovna herself, though she is by no means a gossip. And what I heard certainly was rather strange.”\n\n“And what did you hear?” both the ladies asked at once.\n\n“Well, nothing very special. I only learned that the marriage, which only failed to take place through the girl’s death, was not at all to Praskovya Pavlovna’s liking. They say, too, the girl was not at all pretty, in fact I am told positively ugly... and such an invalid... and queer. But she seems to have had some good qualities. She must have had some good qualities or it’s quite inexplicable.... She had no money either and he wouldn’t have considered her money.... But it’s always difficult to judge in such matters.”\n\n“I am sure she was a good girl,” Avdotya Romanovna observed briefly.\n\n“God forgive me, I simply rejoiced at her death. Though I don’t know which of them would have caused most misery to the other--he to her or she to him,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna concluded. Then she began tentatively questioning him about the scene on the previous day with Luzhin, hesitating and continually glancing at Dounia, obviously to the latter’s annoyance. This incident more than all the rest evidently caused her uneasiness, even consternation. Razumihin described it in detail again, but this time he added his own conclusions: he openly blamed Raskolnikov for intentionally insulting Pyotr Petrovitch, not seeking to excuse him on the score of his illness.\n\n“He had planned it before his illness,” he added.\n\n“I think so, too,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna agreed with a dejected air. But she was very much surprised at hearing Razumihin express himself so carefully and even with a certain respect about Pyotr Petrovitch. Avdotya Romanovna, too, was struck by it.\n\n“So this is your opinion of Pyotr Petrovitch?” Pulcheria Alexandrovna could not resist asking.\n\n“I can have no other opinion of your daughter’s future husband,” Razumihin answered firmly and with warmth, “and I don’t say it simply from vulgar politeness, but because... simply because Avdotya Romanovna has of her own free will deigned to accept this man. If I spoke so rudely of him last night, it was because I was disgustingly drunk and... mad besides; yes, mad, crazy, I lost my head completely... and this morning I am ashamed of it.”\n\nHe crimsoned and ceased speaking. Avdotya Romanovna flushed, but did not break the silence. She had not uttered a word from the moment they began to speak of Luzhin.\n\nWithout her support Pulcheria Alexandrovna obviously did not know what to do. At last, faltering and continually glancing at her daughter, she confessed that she was exceedingly worried by one circumstance.\n\n“You see, Dmitri Prokofitch,” she began. “I’ll be perfectly open with Dmitri Prokofitch, Dounia?”\n\n“Of course, mother,” said Avdotya Romanovna emphatically.\n\n“This is what it is,” she began in haste, as though the permission to speak of her trouble lifted a weight off her mind. “Very early this morning we got a note from Pyotr Petrovitch in reply to our letter announcing our arrival. He promised to meet us at the station, you know; instead of that he sent a servant to bring us the address of these lodgings and to show us the way; and he sent a message that he would be here himself this morning. But this morning this note came from him. You’d better read it yourself; there is one point in it which worries me very much... you will soon see what that is, and... tell me your candid opinion, Dmitri Prokofitch! You know Rodya’s character better than anyone and no one can advise us better than you can. Dounia, I must tell you, made her decision at once, but I still don’t feel sure how to act and I... I’ve been waiting for your opinion.”\n\nRazumihin opened the note which was dated the previous evening and read as follows:\n\n“Dear Madam, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, I have the honour to inform you that owing to unforeseen obstacles I was rendered unable to meet you at the railway station; I sent a very competent person with the same object in view. I likewise shall be deprived of the honour of an interview with you to-morrow morning by business in the Senate that does not admit of delay, and also that I may not intrude on your family circle while you are meeting your son, and Avdotya Romanovna her brother. I shall have the honour of visiting you and paying you my respects at your lodgings not later than to-morrow evening at eight o’clock precisely, and herewith I venture to present my earnest and, I may add, imperative request that Rodion Romanovitch may not be present at our interview--as he offered me a gross and unprecedented affront on the occasion of my visit to him in his illness yesterday, and, moreover, since I desire from you personally an indispensable and circumstantial explanation upon a certain point, in regard to which I wish to learn your own interpretation. I have the honour to inform you, in anticipation, that if, in spite of my request, I meet Rodion Romanovitch, I shall be compelled to withdraw immediately and then you have only yourself to blame. I write on the assumption that Rodion Romanovitch who appeared so ill at my visit, suddenly recovered two hours later and so, being able to leave the house, may visit you also. I was confirmed in that belief by the testimony of my own eyes in the lodging of a drunken man who was run over and has since died, to whose daughter, a young woman of notorious behaviour, he gave twenty-five roubles on the pretext of the funeral, which gravely surprised me knowing what pains you were at to raise that sum. Herewith expressing my special respect to your estimable daughter, Avdotya Romanovna, I beg you to accept the respectful homage of\n\n“Your humble servant,\n\n“What am I to do now, Dmitri Prokofitch?” began Pulcheria Alexandrovna, almost weeping. “How can I ask Rodya not to come? Yesterday he insisted so earnestly on our refusing Pyotr Petrovitch and now we are ordered not to receive Rodya! He will come on purpose if he knows, and... what will happen then?”\n\n“Act on Avdotya Romanovna’s decision,” Razumihin answered calmly at once.\n\n“Oh, dear me! She says... goodness knows what she says, she doesn’t explain her object! She says that it would be best, at least, not that it would be best, but that it’s absolutely necessary that Rodya should make a point of being here at eight o’clock and that they must meet.... I didn’t want even to show him the letter, but to prevent him from coming by some stratagem with your help... because he is so irritable.... Besides I don’t understand about that drunkard who died and that daughter, and how he could have given the daughter all the money... which...”\n\n“Which cost you such sacrifice, mother,” put in Avdotya Romanovna.\n\n“He was not himself yesterday,” Razumihin said thoughtfully, “if you only knew what he was up to in a restaurant yesterday, though there was sense in it too.... Hm! He did say something, as we were going home yesterday evening, about a dead man and a girl, but I didn’t understand a word.... But last night, I myself...”\n\n“The best thing, mother, will be for us to go to him ourselves and there I assure you we shall see at once what’s to be done. Besides, it’s getting late--good heavens, it’s past ten,” she cried looking at a splendid gold enamelled watch which hung round her neck on a thin Venetian chain, and looked entirely out of keeping with the rest of her dress. “A present from her _fiancé_,” thought Razumihin.\n\n“We must start, Dounia, we must start,” her mother cried in a flutter. “He will be thinking we are still angry after yesterday, from our coming so late. Merciful heavens!”\n\nWhile she said this she was hurriedly putting on her hat and mantle; Dounia, too, put on her things. Her gloves, as Razumihin noticed, were not merely shabby but had holes in them, and yet this evident poverty gave the two ladies an air of special dignity, which is always found in people who know how to wear poor clothes. Razumihin looked reverently at Dounia and felt proud of escorting her. “The queen who mended her stockings in prison,” he thought, “must have looked then every inch a queen and even more a queen than at sumptuous banquets and levées.”\n\n“My God!” exclaimed Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “little did I think that I should ever fear seeing my son, my darling, darling Rodya! I am afraid, Dmitri Prokofitch,” she added, glancing at him timidly.\n\n“Don’t be afraid, mother,” said Dounia, kissing her, “better have faith in him.”\n\n“Oh, dear, I have faith in him, but I haven’t slept all night,” exclaimed the poor woman.\n\nThey came out into the street.\n\n“Do you know, Dounia, when I dozed a little this morning I dreamed of Marfa Petrovna... she was all in white... she came up to me, took my hand, and shook her head at me, but so sternly as though she were blaming me.... Is that a good omen? Oh, dear me! You don’t know, Dmitri Prokofitch, that Marfa Petrovna’s dead!”\n\n“No, I didn’t know; who is Marfa Petrovna?”\n\n“She died suddenly; and only fancy...”\n\n“Afterwards, mamma,” put in Dounia. “He doesn’t know who Marfa Petrovna is.”\n\n“Ah, you don’t know? And I was thinking that you knew all about us. Forgive me, Dmitri Prokofitch, I don’t know what I am thinking about these last few days. I look upon you really as a providence for us, and so I took it for granted that you knew all about us. I look on you as a relation.... Don’t be angry with me for saying so. Dear me, what’s the matter with your right hand? Have you knocked it?”\n\n“Yes, I bruised it,” muttered Razumihin overjoyed.\n\n“I sometimes speak too much from the heart, so that Dounia finds fault with me.... But, dear me, what a cupboard he lives in! I wonder whether he is awake? Does this woman, his landlady, consider it a room? Listen, you say he does not like to show his feelings, so perhaps I shall annoy him with my... weaknesses? Do advise me, Dmitri Prokofitch, how am I to treat him? I feel quite distracted, you know.”\n\n“Don’t question him too much about anything if you see him frown; don’t ask him too much about his health; he doesn’t like that.”\n\n“Ah, Dmitri Prokofitch, how hard it is to be a mother! But here are the stairs.... What an awful staircase!”\n\n“Mother, you are quite pale, don’t distress yourself, darling,” said Dounia caressing her, then with flashing eyes she added: “He ought to be happy at seeing you, and you are tormenting yourself so.”\n\n“Wait, I’ll peep in and see whether he has waked up.”\n\nThe ladies slowly followed Razumihin, who went on before, and when they reached the landlady’s door on the fourth storey, they noticed that her door was a tiny crack open and that two keen black eyes were watching them from the darkness within. When their eyes met, the door was suddenly shut with such a slam that Pulcheria Alexandrovna almost cried out.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n“He is well, quite well!” Zossimov cried cheerfully as they entered.\n\nHe had come in ten minutes earlier and was sitting in the same place as before, on the sofa. Raskolnikov was sitting in the opposite corner, fully dressed and carefully washed and combed, as he had not been for some time past. The room was immediately crowded, yet Nastasya managed to follow the visitors in and stayed to listen.\n\nRaskolnikov really was almost well, as compared with his condition the day before, but he was still pale, listless, and sombre. He looked like a wounded man or one who has undergone some terrible physical suffering. His brows were knitted, his lips compressed, his eyes feverish. He spoke little and reluctantly, as though performing a duty, and there was a restlessness in his movements.\n\nHe only wanted a sling on his arm or a bandage on his finger to complete the impression of a man with a painful abscess or a broken arm. The pale, sombre face lighted up for a moment when his mother and sister entered, but this only gave it a look of more intense suffering, in place of its listless dejection. The light soon died away, but the look of suffering remained, and Zossimov, watching and studying his patient with all the zest of a young doctor beginning to practise, noticed in him no joy at the arrival of his mother and sister, but a sort of bitter, hidden determination to bear another hour or two of inevitable torture. He saw later that almost every word of the following conversation seemed to touch on some sore place and irritate it. But at the same time he marvelled at the power of controlling himself and hiding his feelings in a patient who the previous day had, like a monomaniac, fallen into a frenzy at the slightest word.\n\n“Yes, I see myself now that I am almost well,” said Raskolnikov, giving his mother and sister a kiss of welcome which made Pulcheria Alexandrovna radiant at once. “And I don’t say this _as I did yesterday_,” he said, addressing Razumihin, with a friendly pressure of his hand.\n\n“Yes, indeed, I am quite surprised at him to-day,” began Zossimov, much delighted at the ladies’ entrance, for he had not succeeded in keeping up a conversation with his patient for ten minutes. “In another three or four days, if he goes on like this, he will be just as before, that is, as he was a month ago, or two... or perhaps even three. This has been coming on for a long while.... eh? Confess, now, that it has been perhaps your own fault?” he added, with a tentative smile, as though still afraid of irritating him.\n\n“It is very possible,” answered Raskolnikov coldly.\n\n“I should say, too,” continued Zossimov with zest, “that your complete recovery depends solely on yourself. Now that one can talk to you, I should like to impress upon you that it is essential to avoid the elementary, so to speak, fundamental causes tending to produce your morbid condition: in that case you will be cured, if not, it will go from bad to worse. These fundamental causes I don’t know, but they must be known to you. You are an intelligent man, and must have observed yourself, of course. I fancy the first stage of your derangement coincides with your leaving the university. You must not be left without occupation, and so, work and a definite aim set before you might, I fancy, be very beneficial.”\n\n“Yes, yes; you are perfectly right.... I will make haste and return to the university: and then everything will go smoothly....”\n\nZossimov, who had begun his sage advice partly to make an effect before the ladies, was certainly somewhat mystified, when, glancing at his patient, he observed unmistakable mockery on his face. This lasted an instant, however. Pulcheria Alexandrovna began at once thanking Zossimov, especially for his visit to their lodging the previous night.\n\n“What! he saw you last night?” Raskolnikov asked, as though startled. “Then you have not slept either after your journey.”\n\n“Ach, Rodya, that was only till two o’clock. Dounia and I never go to bed before two at home.”\n\n“I don’t know how to thank him either,” Raskolnikov went on, suddenly frowning and looking down. “Setting aside the question of payment--forgive me for referring to it (he turned to Zossimov)--I really don’t know what I have done to deserve such special attention from you! I simply don’t understand it... and... and... it weighs upon me, indeed, because I don’t understand it. I tell you so candidly.”\n\n“Don’t be irritated.” Zossimov forced himself to laugh. “Assume that you are my first patient--well--we fellows just beginning to practise love our first patients as if they were our children, and some almost fall in love with them. And, of course, I am not rich in patients.”\n\n“I say nothing about him,” added Raskolnikov, pointing to Razumihin, “though he has had nothing from me either but insult and trouble.”\n\n“What nonsense he is talking! Why, you are in a sentimental mood to-day, are you?” shouted Razumihin.\n\nIf he had had more penetration he would have seen that there was no trace of sentimentality in him, but something indeed quite the opposite. But Avdotya Romanovna noticed it. She was intently and uneasily watching her brother.\n\n“As for you, mother, I don’t dare to speak,” he went on, as though repeating a lesson learned by heart. “It is only to-day that I have been able to realise a little how distressed you must have been here yesterday, waiting for me to come back.”\n\nWhen he had said this, he suddenly held out his hand to his sister, smiling without a word. But in this smile there was a flash of real unfeigned feeling. Dounia caught it at once, and warmly pressed his hand, overjoyed and thankful. It was the first time he had addressed her since their dispute the previous day. The mother’s face lighted up with ecstatic happiness at the sight of this conclusive unspoken reconciliation. “Yes, that is what I love him for,” Razumihin, exaggerating it all, muttered to himself, with a vigorous turn in his chair. “He has these movements.”\n\n“And how well he does it all,” the mother was thinking to herself. “What generous impulses he has, and how simply, how delicately he put an end to all the misunderstanding with his sister--simply by holding out his hand at the right minute and looking at her like that.... And what fine eyes he has, and how fine his whole face is!... He is even better looking than Dounia.... But, good heavens, what a suit--how terribly he’s dressed!... Vasya, the messenger boy in Afanasy Ivanitch’s shop, is better dressed! I could rush at him and hug him... weep over him--but I am afraid.... Oh, dear, he’s so strange! He’s talking kindly, but I’m afraid! Why, what am I afraid of?...”\n\n“Oh, Rodya, you wouldn’t believe,” she began suddenly, in haste to answer his words to her, “how unhappy Dounia and I were yesterday! Now that it’s all over and done with and we are quite happy again--I can tell you. Fancy, we ran here almost straight from the train to embrace you and that woman--ah, here she is! Good morning, Nastasya!... She told us at once that you were lying in a high fever and had just run away from the doctor in delirium, and they were looking for you in the streets. You can’t imagine how we felt! I couldn’t help thinking of the tragic end of Lieutenant Potanchikov, a friend of your father’s--you can’t remember him, Rodya--who ran out in the same way in a high fever and fell into the well in the court-yard and they couldn’t pull him out till next day. Of course, we exaggerated things. We were on the point of rushing to find Pyotr Petrovitch to ask him to help.... Because we were alone, utterly alone,” she said plaintively and stopped short, suddenly, recollecting it was still somewhat dangerous to speak of Pyotr Petrovitch, although “we are quite happy again.”\n\n“Yes, yes.... Of course it’s very annoying....” Raskolnikov muttered in reply, but with such a preoccupied and inattentive air that Dounia gazed at him in perplexity.\n\n“What else was it I wanted to say?” He went on trying to recollect. “Oh, yes; mother, and you too, Dounia, please don’t think that I didn’t mean to come and see you to-day and was waiting for you to come first.”\n\n“What are you saying, Rodya?” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna. She, too, was surprised.\n\n“Is he answering us as a duty?” Dounia wondered. “Is he being reconciled and asking forgiveness as though he were performing a rite or repeating a lesson?”\n\n“I’ve only just waked up, and wanted to go to you, but was delayed owing to my clothes; I forgot yesterday to ask her... Nastasya... to wash out the blood... I’ve only just dressed.”\n\n“Blood! What blood?” Pulcheria Alexandrovna asked in alarm.\n\n“Oh, nothing--don’t be uneasy. It was when I was wandering about yesterday, rather delirious, I chanced upon a man who had been run over... a clerk...”\n\n“Delirious? But you remember everything!” Razumihin interrupted.\n\n“That’s true,” Raskolnikov answered with special carefulness. “I remember everything even to the slightest detail, and yet--why I did that and went there and said that, I can’t clearly explain now.”\n\n“A familiar phenomenon,” interposed Zossimov, “actions are sometimes performed in a masterly and most cunning way, while the direction of the actions is deranged and dependent on various morbid impressions--it’s like a dream.”\n\n“Perhaps it’s a good thing really that he should think me almost a madman,” thought Raskolnikov.\n\n“Why, people in perfect health act in the same way too,” observed Dounia, looking uneasily at Zossimov.\n\n“There is some truth in your observation,” the latter replied. “In that sense we are certainly all not infrequently like madmen, but with the slight difference that the deranged are somewhat madder, for we must draw a line. A normal man, it is true, hardly exists. Among dozens--perhaps hundreds of thousands--hardly one is to be met with.”\n\nAt the word “madman,” carelessly dropped by Zossimov in his chatter on his favourite subject, everyone frowned.\n\nRaskolnikov sat seeming not to pay attention, plunged in thought with a strange smile on his pale lips. He was still meditating on something.\n\n“Well, what about the man who was run over? I interrupted you!” Razumihin cried hastily.\n\n“What?” Raskolnikov seemed to wake up. “Oh... I got spattered with blood helping to carry him to his lodging. By the way, mamma, I did an unpardonable thing yesterday. I was literally out of my mind. I gave away all the money you sent me... to his wife for the funeral. She’s a widow now, in consumption, a poor creature... three little children, starving... nothing in the house... there’s a daughter, too... perhaps you’d have given it yourself if you’d seen them. But I had no right to do it I admit, especially as I knew how you needed the money yourself. To help others one must have the right to do it, or else _Crevez, chiens, si vous n’êtes pas contents_.” He laughed, “That’s right, isn’t it, Dounia?”\n\n“No, it’s not,” answered Dounia firmly.\n\n“Bah! you, too, have ideals,” he muttered, looking at her almost with hatred, and smiling sarcastically. “I ought to have considered that.... Well, that’s praiseworthy, and it’s better for you... and if you reach a line you won’t overstep, you will be unhappy... and if you overstep it, maybe you will be still unhappier.... But all that’s nonsense,” he added irritably, vexed at being carried away. “I only meant to say that I beg your forgiveness, mother,” he concluded, shortly and abruptly.\n\n“That’s enough, Rodya, I am sure that everything you do is very good,” said his mother, delighted.\n\n“Don’t be too sure,” he answered, twisting his mouth into a smile.\n\nA silence followed. There was a certain constraint in all this conversation, and in the silence, and in the reconciliation, and in the forgiveness, and all were feeling it.\n\n“It is as though they were afraid of me,” Raskolnikov was thinking to himself, looking askance at his mother and sister. Pulcheria Alexandrovna was indeed growing more timid the longer she kept silent.\n\n“Yet in their absence I seemed to love them so much,” flashed through his mind.\n\n“Do you know, Rodya, Marfa Petrovna is dead,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna suddenly blurted out.\n\n“What Marfa Petrovna?”\n\n“Oh, mercy on us--Marfa Petrovna Svidrigaïlov. I wrote you so much about her.”\n\n“A-a-h! Yes, I remember.... So she’s dead! Oh, really?” he roused himself suddenly, as if waking up. “What did she die of?”\n\n“Only imagine, quite suddenly,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna answered hurriedly, encouraged by his curiosity. “On the very day I was sending you that letter! Would you believe it, that awful man seems to have been the cause of her death. They say he beat her dreadfully.”\n\n“Why, were they on such bad terms?” he asked, addressing his sister.\n\n“Not at all. Quite the contrary indeed. With her, he was always very patient, considerate even. In fact, all those seven years of their married life he gave way to her, too much so indeed, in many cases. All of a sudden he seems to have lost patience.”\n\n“Then he could not have been so awful if he controlled himself for seven years? You seem to be defending him, Dounia?”\n\n“No, no, he’s an awful man! I can imagine nothing more awful!” Dounia answered, almost with a shudder, knitting her brows, and sinking into thought.\n\n“That had happened in the morning,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna went on hurriedly. “And directly afterwards she ordered the horses to be harnessed to drive to the town immediately after dinner. She always used to drive to the town in such cases. She ate a very good dinner, I am told....”\n\n“After the beating?”\n\n“That was always her... habit; and immediately after dinner, so as not to be late in starting, she went to the bath-house.... You see, she was undergoing some treatment with baths. They have a cold spring there, and she used to bathe in it regularly every day, and no sooner had she got into the water when she suddenly had a stroke!”\n\n“I should think so,” said Zossimov.\n\n“And did he beat her badly?”\n\n“What does that matter!” put in Dounia.\n\n“H’m! But I don’t know why you want to tell us such gossip, mother,” said Raskolnikov irritably, as it were in spite of himself.\n\n“Ah, my dear, I don’t know what to talk about,” broke from Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Why, are you all afraid of me?” he asked, with a constrained smile.\n\n“That’s certainly true,” said Dounia, looking directly and sternly at her brother. “Mother was crossing herself with terror as she came up the stairs.”\n\nHis face worked, as though in convulsion.\n\n“Ach, what are you saying, Dounia! Don’t be angry, please, Rodya.... Why did you say that, Dounia?” Pulcheria Alexandrovna began, overwhelmed--“You see, coming here, I was dreaming all the way, in the train, how we should meet, how we should talk over everything together.... And I was so happy, I did not notice the journey! But what am I saying? I am happy now.... You should not, Dounia.... I am happy now--simply in seeing you, Rodya....”\n\n“Hush, mother,” he muttered in confusion, not looking at her, but pressing her hand. “We shall have time to speak freely of everything!”\n\nAs he said this, he was suddenly overwhelmed with confusion and turned pale. Again that awful sensation he had known of late passed with deadly chill over his soul. Again it became suddenly plain and perceptible to him that he had just told a fearful lie--that he would never now be able to speak freely of everything--that he would never again be able to _speak_ of anything to anyone. The anguish of this thought was such that for a moment he almost forgot himself. He got up from his seat, and not looking at anyone walked towards the door.\n\n“What are you about?” cried Razumihin, clutching him by the arm.\n\nHe sat down again, and began looking about him, in silence. They were all looking at him in perplexity.\n\n“But what are you all so dull for?” he shouted, suddenly and quite unexpectedly. “Do say something! What’s the use of sitting like this? Come, do speak. Let us talk.... We meet together and sit in silence.... Come, anything!”\n\n“Thank God; I was afraid the same thing as yesterday was beginning again,” said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, crossing herself.\n\n“What is the matter, Rodya?” asked Avdotya Romanovna, distrustfully.\n\n“Oh, nothing! I remembered something,” he answered, and suddenly laughed.\n\n“Well, if you remembered something; that’s all right!... I was beginning to think...” muttered Zossimov, getting up from the sofa. “It is time for me to be off. I will look in again perhaps... if I can...” He made his bows, and went out.\n\n“What an excellent man!” observed Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Yes, excellent, splendid, well-educated, intelligent,” Raskolnikov began, suddenly speaking with surprising rapidity, and a liveliness he had not shown till then. “I can’t remember where I met him before my illness.... I believe I have met him somewhere----... And this is a good man, too,” he nodded at Razumihin. “Do you like him, Dounia?” he asked her; and suddenly, for some unknown reason, laughed.\n\n“Very much,” answered Dounia.\n\n“Foo!--what a pig you are!” Razumihin protested, blushing in terrible confusion, and he got up from his chair. Pulcheria Alexandrovna smiled faintly, but Raskolnikov laughed aloud.\n\n“Where are you off to?”\n\n“I must go.”\n\n“You need not at all. Stay. Zossimov has gone, so you must. Don’t go. What’s the time? Is it twelve o’clock? What a pretty watch you have got, Dounia. But why are you all silent again? I do all the talking.”\n\n“It was a present from Marfa Petrovna,” answered Dounia.\n\n“And a very expensive one!” added Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“A-ah! What a big one! Hardly like a lady’s.”\n\n“I like that sort,” said Dounia.\n\n“So it is not a present from her _fiancé_,” thought Razumihin, and was unreasonably delighted.\n\n“I thought it was Luzhin’s present,” observed Raskolnikov.\n\n“No, he has not made Dounia any presents yet.”\n\n“A-ah! And do you remember, mother, I was in love and wanted to get married?” he said suddenly, looking at his mother, who was disconcerted by the sudden change of subject and the way he spoke of it.\n\n“Oh, yes, my dear.”\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna exchanged glances with Dounia and Razumihin.\n\n“H’m, yes. What shall I tell you? I don’t remember much indeed. She was such a sickly girl,” he went on, growing dreamy and looking down again. “Quite an invalid. She was fond of giving alms to the poor, and was always dreaming of a nunnery, and once she burst into tears when she began talking to me about it. Yes, yes, I remember. I remember very well. She was an ugly little thing. I really don’t know what drew me to her then--I think it was because she was always ill. If she had been lame or hunchback, I believe I should have liked her better still,” he smiled dreamily. “Yes, it was a sort of spring delirium.”\n\n“No, it was not only spring delirium,” said Dounia, with warm feeling.\n\nHe fixed a strained intent look on his sister, but did not hear or did not understand her words. Then, completely lost in thought, he got up, went up to his mother, kissed her, went back to his place and sat down.\n\n“You love her even now?” said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, touched.\n\n“Her? Now? Oh, yes.... You ask about her? No... that’s all now, as it were, in another world... and so long ago. And indeed everything happening here seems somehow far away.” He looked attentively at them. “You, now... I seem to be looking at you from a thousand miles away... but, goodness knows why we are talking of that! And what’s the use of asking about it?” he added with annoyance, and biting his nails, fell into dreamy silence again.\n\n“What a wretched lodging you have, Rodya! It’s like a tomb,” said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, suddenly breaking the oppressive silence. “I am sure it’s quite half through your lodging you have become so melancholy.”\n\n“My lodging,” he answered, listlessly. “Yes, the lodging had a great deal to do with it.... I thought that, too.... If only you knew, though, what a strange thing you said just now, mother,” he said, laughing strangely.\n\nA little more, and their companionship, this mother and this sister, with him after three years’ absence, this intimate tone of conversation, in face of the utter impossibility of really speaking about anything, would have been beyond his power of endurance. But there was one urgent matter which must be settled one way or the other that day--so he had decided when he woke. Now he was glad to remember it, as a means of escape.\n\n“Listen, Dounia,” he began, gravely and drily, “of course I beg your pardon for yesterday, but I consider it my duty to tell you again that I do not withdraw from my chief point. It is me or Luzhin. If I am a scoundrel, you must not be. One is enough. If you marry Luzhin, I cease at once to look on you as a sister.”\n\n“Rodya, Rodya! It is the same as yesterday again,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna cried, mournfully. “And why do you call yourself a scoundrel? I can’t bear it. You said the same yesterday.”\n\n“Brother,” Dounia answered firmly and with the same dryness. “In all this there is a mistake on your part. I thought it over at night, and found out the mistake. It is all because you seem to fancy I am sacrificing myself to someone and for someone. That is not the case at all. I am simply marrying for my own sake, because things are hard for me. Though, of course, I shall be glad if I succeed in being useful to my family. But that is not the chief motive for my decision....”\n\n“She is lying,” he thought to himself, biting his nails vindictively. “Proud creature! She won’t admit she wants to do it out of charity! Too haughty! Oh, base characters! They even love as though they hate.... Oh, how I... hate them all!”\n\n“In fact,” continued Dounia, “I am marrying Pyotr Petrovitch because of two evils I choose the less. I intend to do honestly all he expects of me, so I am not deceiving him.... Why did you smile just now?” She, too, flushed, and there was a gleam of anger in her eyes.\n\n“All?” he asked, with a malignant grin.\n\n“Within certain limits. Both the manner and form of Pyotr Petrovitch’s courtship showed me at once what he wanted. He may, of course, think too well of himself, but I hope he esteems me, too.... Why are you laughing again?”\n\n“And why are you blushing again? You are lying, sister. You are intentionally lying, simply from feminine obstinacy, simply to hold your own against me.... You cannot respect Luzhin. I have seen him and talked with him. So you are selling yourself for money, and so in any case you are acting basely, and I am glad at least that you can blush for it.”\n\n“It is not true. I am not lying,” cried Dounia, losing her composure. “I would not marry him if I were not convinced that he esteems me and thinks highly of me. I would not marry him if I were not firmly convinced that I can respect him. Fortunately, I can have convincing proof of it this very day... and such a marriage is not a vileness, as you say! And even if you were right, if I really had determined on a vile action, is it not merciless on your part to speak to me like that? Why do you demand of me a heroism that perhaps you have not either? It is despotism; it is tyranny. If I ruin anyone, it is only myself.... I am not committing a murder. Why do you look at me like that? Why are you so pale? Rodya, darling, what’s the matter?”\n\n“Good heavens! You have made him faint,” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“No, no, nonsense! It’s nothing. A little giddiness--not fainting. You have fainting on the brain. H’m, yes, what was I saying? Oh, yes. In what way will you get convincing proof to-day that you can respect him, and that he... esteems you, as you said. I think you said to-day?”\n\n“Mother, show Rodya Pyotr Petrovitch’s letter,” said Dounia.\n\nWith trembling hands, Pulcheria Alexandrovna gave him the letter. He took it with great interest, but, before opening it, he suddenly looked with a sort of wonder at Dounia.\n\n“It is strange,” he said, slowly, as though struck by a new idea. “What am I making such a fuss for? What is it all about? Marry whom you like!”\n\nHe said this as though to himself, but said it aloud, and looked for some time at his sister, as though puzzled. He opened the letter at last, still with the same look of strange wonder on his face. Then, slowly and attentively, he began reading, and read it through twice. Pulcheria Alexandrovna showed marked anxiety, and all indeed expected something particular.\n\n“What surprises me,” he began, after a short pause, handing the letter to his mother, but not addressing anyone in particular, “is that he is a business man, a lawyer, and his conversation is pretentious indeed, and yet he writes such an uneducated letter.”\n\nThey all started. They had expected something quite different.\n\n“But they all write like that, you know,” Razumihin observed, abruptly.\n\n“Have you read it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“We showed him, Rodya. We... consulted him just now,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna began, embarrassed.\n\n“That’s just the jargon of the courts,” Razumihin put in. “Legal documents are written like that to this day.”\n\n“Legal? Yes, it’s just legal--business language--not so very uneducated, and not quite educated--business language!”\n\n“Pyotr Petrovitch makes no secret of the fact that he had a cheap education, he is proud indeed of having made his own way,” Avdotya Romanovna observed, somewhat offended by her brother’s tone.\n\n“Well, if he’s proud of it, he has reason, I don’t deny it. You seem to be offended, sister, at my making only such a frivolous criticism on the letter, and to think that I speak of such trifling matters on purpose to annoy you. It is quite the contrary, an observation apropos of the style occurred to me that is by no means irrelevant as things stand. There is one expression, ‘blame yourselves’, put in very significantly and plainly, and there is besides a threat that he will go away at once if I am present. That threat to go away is equivalent to a threat to abandon you both if you are disobedient, and to abandon you now after summoning you to Petersburg. Well, what do you think? Can one resent such an expression from Luzhin, as we should if he (he pointed to Razumihin) had written it, or Zossimov, or one of us?”\n\n“N-no,” answered Dounia, with more animation. “I saw clearly that it was too naïvely expressed, and that perhaps he simply has no skill in writing... that is a true criticism, brother. I did not expect, indeed...”\n\n“It is expressed in legal style, and sounds coarser than perhaps he intended. But I must disillusion you a little. There is one expression in the letter, one slander about me, and rather a contemptible one. I gave the money last night to the widow, a woman in consumption, crushed with trouble, and not ‘on the pretext of the funeral,’ but simply to pay for the funeral, and not to the daughter--a young woman, as he writes, of notorious behaviour (whom I saw last night for the first time in my life)--but to the widow. In all this I see a too hasty desire to slander me and to raise dissension between us. It is expressed again in legal jargon, that is to say, with a too obvious display of the aim, and with a very naïve eagerness. He is a man of intelligence, but to act sensibly, intelligence is not enough. It all shows the man and... I don’t think he has a great esteem for you. I tell you this simply to warn you, because I sincerely wish for your good...”\n\nDounia did not reply. Her resolution had been taken. She was only awaiting the evening.\n\n“Then what is your decision, Rodya?” asked Pulcheria Alexandrovna, who was more uneasy than ever at the sudden, new businesslike tone of his talk.\n\n“What decision?”\n\n“You see Pyotr Petrovitch writes that you are not to be with us this evening, and that he will go away if you come. So will you... come?”\n\n“That, of course, is not for me to decide, but for you first, if you are not offended by such a request; and secondly, by Dounia, if she, too, is not offended. I will do what you think best,” he added, drily.\n\n“Dounia has already decided, and I fully agree with her,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna hastened to declare.\n\n“I decided to ask you, Rodya, to urge you not to fail to be with us at this interview,” said Dounia. “Will you come?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I will ask you, too, to be with us at eight o’clock,” she said, addressing Razumihin. “Mother, I am inviting him, too.”\n\n“Quite right, Dounia. Well, since you have decided,” added Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “so be it. I shall feel easier myself. I do not like concealment and deception. Better let us have the whole truth.... Pyotr Petrovitch may be angry or not, now!”\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nAt that moment the door was softly opened, and a young girl walked into the room, looking timidly about her. Everyone turned towards her with surprise and curiosity. At first sight, Raskolnikov did not recognise her. It was Sofya Semyonovna Marmeladov. He had seen her yesterday for the first time, but at such a moment, in such surroundings and in such a dress, that his memory retained a very different image of her. Now she was a modestly and poorly-dressed young girl, very young, indeed, almost like a child, with a modest and refined manner, with a candid but somewhat frightened-looking face. She was wearing a very plain indoor dress, and had on a shabby old-fashioned hat, but she still carried a parasol. Unexpectedly finding the room full of people, she was not so much embarrassed as completely overwhelmed with shyness, like a little child. She was even about to retreat. “Oh... it’s you!” said Raskolnikov, extremely astonished, and he, too, was confused. He at once recollected that his mother and sister knew through Luzhin’s letter of “some young woman of notorious behaviour.” He had only just been protesting against Luzhin’s calumny and declaring that he had seen the girl last night for the first time, and suddenly she had walked in. He remembered, too, that he had not protested against the expression “of notorious behaviour.” All this passed vaguely and fleetingly through his brain, but looking at her more intently, he saw that the humiliated creature was so humiliated that he felt suddenly sorry for her. When she made a movement to retreat in terror, it sent a pang to his heart.\n\n“I did not expect you,” he said, hurriedly, with a look that made her stop. “Please sit down. You come, no doubt, from Katerina Ivanovna. Allow me--not there. Sit here....”\n\nAt Sonia’s entrance, Razumihin, who had been sitting on one of Raskolnikov’s three chairs, close to the door, got up to allow her to enter. Raskolnikov had at first shown her the place on the sofa where Zossimov had been sitting, but feeling that the sofa which served him as a bed, was too _familiar_ a place, he hurriedly motioned her to Razumihin’s chair.\n\n“You sit here,” he said to Razumihin, putting him on the sofa.\n\nSonia sat down, almost shaking with terror, and looked timidly at the two ladies. It was evidently almost inconceivable to herself that she could sit down beside them. At the thought of it, she was so frightened that she hurriedly got up again, and in utter confusion addressed Raskolnikov.\n\n“I... I... have come for one minute. Forgive me for disturbing you,” she began falteringly. “I come from Katerina Ivanovna, and she had no one to send. Katerina Ivanovna told me to beg you... to be at the service... in the morning... at Mitrofanievsky... and then... to us... to her... to do her the honour... she told me to beg you...” Sonia stammered and ceased speaking.\n\n“I will try, certainly, most certainly,” answered Raskolnikov. He, too, stood up, and he, too, faltered and could not finish his sentence. “Please sit down,” he said, suddenly. “I want to talk to you. You are perhaps in a hurry, but please, be so kind, spare me two minutes,” and he drew up a chair for her.\n\nSonia sat down again, and again timidly she took a hurried, frightened look at the two ladies, and dropped her eyes. Raskolnikov’s pale face flushed, a shudder passed over him, his eyes glowed.\n\n“Mother,” he said, firmly and insistently, “this is Sofya Semyonovna Marmeladov, the daughter of that unfortunate Mr. Marmeladov, who was run over yesterday before my eyes, and of whom I was just telling you.”\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna glanced at Sonia, and slightly screwed up her eyes. In spite of her embarrassment before Rodya’s urgent and challenging look, she could not deny herself that satisfaction. Dounia gazed gravely and intently into the poor girl’s face, and scrutinised her with perplexity. Sonia, hearing herself introduced, tried to raise her eyes again, but was more embarrassed than ever.\n\n“I wanted to ask you,” said Raskolnikov, hastily, “how things were arranged yesterday. You were not worried by the police, for instance?”\n\n“No, that was all right... it was too evident, the cause of death... they did not worry us... only the lodgers are angry.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“At the body’s remaining so long. You see it is hot now. So that, to-day, they will carry it to the cemetery, into the chapel, until to-morrow. At first Katerina Ivanovna was unwilling, but now she sees herself that it’s necessary...”\n\n“To-day, then?”\n\n“She begs you to do us the honour to be in the church to-morrow for the service, and then to be present at the funeral lunch.”\n\n“She is giving a funeral lunch?”\n\n“Yes... just a little.... She told me to thank you very much for helping us yesterday. But for you, we should have had nothing for the funeral.”\n\nAll at once her lips and chin began trembling, but, with an effort, she controlled herself, looking down again.\n\nDuring the conversation, Raskolnikov watched her carefully. She had a thin, very thin, pale little face, rather irregular and angular, with a sharp little nose and chin. She could not have been called pretty, but her blue eyes were so clear, and when they lighted up, there was such a kindliness and simplicity in her expression that one could not help being attracted. Her face, and her whole figure indeed, had another peculiar characteristic. In spite of her eighteen years, she looked almost a little girl--almost a child. And in some of her gestures, this childishness seemed almost absurd.\n\n“But has Katerina Ivanovna been able to manage with such small means? Does she even mean to have a funeral lunch?” Raskolnikov asked, persistently keeping up the conversation.\n\n“The coffin will be plain, of course... and everything will be plain, so it won’t cost much. Katerina Ivanovna and I have reckoned it all out, so that there will be enough left... and Katerina Ivanovna was very anxious it should be so. You know one can’t... it’s a comfort to her... she is like that, you know....”\n\n“I understand, I understand... of course... why do you look at my room like that? My mother has just said it is like a tomb.”\n\n“You gave us everything yesterday,” Sonia said suddenly, in reply, in a loud rapid whisper; and again she looked down in confusion. Her lips and chin were trembling once more. She had been struck at once by Raskolnikov’s poor surroundings, and now these words broke out spontaneously. A silence followed. There was a light in Dounia’s eyes, and even Pulcheria Alexandrovna looked kindly at Sonia.\n\n“Rodya,” she said, getting up, “we shall have dinner together, of course. Come, Dounia.... And you, Rodya, had better go for a little walk, and then rest and lie down before you come to see us.... I am afraid we have exhausted you....”\n\n“Yes, yes, I’ll come,” he answered, getting up fussily. “But I have something to see to.”\n\n“But surely you will have dinner together?” cried Razumihin, looking in surprise at Raskolnikov. “What do you mean?”\n\n“Yes, yes, I am coming... of course, of course! And you stay a minute. You do not want him just now, do you, mother? Or perhaps I am taking him from you?”\n\n“Oh, no, no. And will you, Dmitri Prokofitch, do us the favour of dining with us?”\n\n“Please do,” added Dounia.\n\nRazumihin bowed, positively radiant. For one moment, they were all strangely embarrassed.\n\n“Good-bye, Rodya, that is till we meet. I do not like saying good-bye. Good-bye, Nastasya. Ah, I have said good-bye again.”\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna meant to greet Sonia, too; but it somehow failed to come off, and she went in a flutter out of the room.\n\nBut Avdotya Romanovna seemed to await her turn, and following her mother out, gave Sonia an attentive, courteous bow. Sonia, in confusion, gave a hurried, frightened curtsy. There was a look of poignant discomfort in her face, as though Avdotya Romanovna’s courtesy and attention were oppressive and painful to her.\n\n“Dounia, good-bye,” called Raskolnikov, in the passage. “Give me your hand.”\n\n“Why, I did give it to you. Have you forgotten?” said Dounia, turning warmly and awkwardly to him.\n\n“Never mind, give it to me again.” And he squeezed her fingers warmly.\n\nDounia smiled, flushed, pulled her hand away, and went off quite happy.\n\n“Come, that’s capital,” he said to Sonia, going back and looking brightly at her. “God give peace to the dead, the living have still to live. That is right, isn’t it?”\n\nSonia looked surprised at the sudden brightness of his face. He looked at her for some moments in silence. The whole history of the dead father floated before his memory in those moments....\n\n*****\n“Heavens, Dounia,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna began, as soon as they were in the street, “I really feel relieved myself at coming away--more at ease. How little did I think yesterday in the train that I could ever be glad of that.”\n\n“I tell you again, mother, he is still very ill. Don’t you see it? Perhaps worrying about us upset him. We must be patient, and much, much can be forgiven.”\n\n“Well, you were not very patient!” Pulcheria Alexandrovna caught her up, hotly and jealously. “Do you know, Dounia, I was looking at you two. You are the very portrait of him, and not so much in face as in soul. You are both melancholy, both morose and hot-tempered, both haughty and both generous.... Surely he can’t be an egoist, Dounia. Eh? When I think of what is in store for us this evening, my heart sinks!”\n\n“Don’t be uneasy, mother. What must be, will be.”\n\n“Dounia, only think what a position we are in! What if Pyotr Petrovitch breaks it off?” poor Pulcheria Alexandrovna blurted out, incautiously.\n\n“He won’t be worth much if he does,” answered Dounia, sharply and contemptuously.\n\n“We did well to come away,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna hurriedly broke in. “He was in a hurry about some business or other. If he gets out and has a breath of air... it is fearfully close in his room.... But where is one to get a breath of air here? The very streets here feel like shut-up rooms. Good heavens! what a town!... stay... this side... they will crush you--carrying something. Why, it is a piano they have got, I declare... how they push!... I am very much afraid of that young woman, too.”\n\n“What young woman, mother?”\n\n“Why, that Sofya Semyonovna, who was there just now.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“I have a presentiment, Dounia. Well, you may believe it or not, but as soon as she came in, that very minute, I felt that she was the chief cause of the trouble....”\n\n“Nothing of the sort!” cried Dounia, in vexation. “What nonsense, with your presentiments, mother! He only made her acquaintance the evening before, and he did not know her when she came in.”\n\n“Well, you will see.... She worries me; but you will see, you will see! I was so frightened. She was gazing at me with those eyes. I could scarcely sit still in my chair when he began introducing her, do you remember? It seems so strange, but Pyotr Petrovitch writes like that about her, and he introduces her to us--to you! So he must think a great deal of her.”\n\n“People will write anything. We were talked about and written about, too. Have you forgotten? I am sure that she is a good girl, and that it is all nonsense.”\n\n“God grant it may be!”\n\n“And Pyotr Petrovitch is a contemptible slanderer,” Dounia snapped out, suddenly.\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna was crushed; the conversation was not resumed.\n\n*****\n“I will tell you what I want with you,” said Raskolnikov, drawing Razumihin to the window.\n\n“Then I will tell Katerina Ivanovna that you are coming,” Sonia said hurriedly, preparing to depart.\n\n“One minute, Sofya Semyonovna. We have no secrets. You are not in our way. I want to have another word or two with you. Listen!” he turned suddenly to Razumihin again. “You know that... what’s his name... Porfiry Petrovitch?”\n\n“I should think so! He is a relation. Why?” added the latter, with interest.\n\n“Is not he managing that case... you know, about that murder?... You were speaking about it yesterday.”\n\n“Yes... well?” Razumihin’s eyes opened wide.\n\n“He was inquiring for people who had pawned things, and I have some pledges there, too--trifles--a ring my sister gave me as a keepsake when I left home, and my father’s silver watch--they are only worth five or six roubles altogether... but I value them. So what am I to do now? I do not want to lose the things, especially the watch. I was quaking just now, for fear mother would ask to look at it, when we spoke of Dounia’s watch. It is the only thing of father’s left us. She would be ill if it were lost. You know what women are. So tell me what to do. I know I ought to have given notice at the police station, but would it not be better to go straight to Porfiry? Eh? What do you think? The matter might be settled more quickly. You see, mother may ask for it before dinner.”\n\n“Certainly not to the police station. Certainly to Porfiry,” Razumihin shouted in extraordinary excitement. “Well, how glad I am. Let us go at once. It is a couple of steps. We shall be sure to find him.”\n\n“Very well, let us go.”\n\n“And he will be very, very glad to make your acquaintance. I have often talked to him of you at different times. I was speaking of you yesterday. Let us go. So you knew the old woman? So that’s it! It is all turning out splendidly.... Oh, yes, Sofya Ivanovna...”\n\n“Sofya Semyonovna,” corrected Raskolnikov. “Sofya Semyonovna, this is my friend Razumihin, and he is a good man.”\n\n“If you have to go now,” Sonia was beginning, not looking at Razumihin at all, and still more embarrassed.\n\n“Let us go,” decided Raskolnikov. “I will come to you to-day, Sofya Semyonovna. Only tell me where you live.”\n\nHe was not exactly ill at ease, but seemed hurried, and avoided her eyes. Sonia gave her address, and flushed as she did so. They all went out together.\n\n“Don’t you lock up?” asked Razumihin, following him on to the stairs.\n\n“Never,” answered Raskolnikov. “I have been meaning to buy a lock for these two years. People are happy who have no need of locks,” he said, laughing, to Sonia. They stood still in the gateway.\n\n“Do you go to the right, Sofya Semyonovna? How did you find me, by the way?” he added, as though he wanted to say something quite different. He wanted to look at her soft clear eyes, but this was not easy.\n\n“Why, you gave your address to Polenka yesterday.”\n\n“Polenka? Oh, yes; Polenka, that is the little girl. She is your sister? Did I give her the address?”\n\n“Why, had you forgotten?”\n\n“No, I remember.”\n\n“I had heard my father speak of you... only I did not know your name, and he did not know it. And now I came... and as I had learnt your name, I asked to-day, ‘Where does Mr. Raskolnikov live?’ I did not know you had only a room too.... Good-bye, I will tell Katerina Ivanovna.”\n\nShe was extremely glad to escape at last; she went away looking down, hurrying to get out of sight as soon as possible, to walk the twenty steps to the turning on the right and to be at last alone, and then moving rapidly along, looking at no one, noticing nothing, to think, to remember, to meditate on every word, every detail. Never, never had she felt anything like this. Dimly and unconsciously a whole new world was opening before her. She remembered suddenly that Raskolnikov meant to come to her that day, perhaps at once!\n\n“Only not to-day, please, not to-day!” she kept muttering with a sinking heart, as though entreating someone, like a frightened child. “Mercy! to me... to that room... he will see... oh, dear!”\n\nShe was not capable at that instant of noticing an unknown gentleman who was watching her and following at her heels. He had accompanied her from the gateway. At the moment when Razumihin, Raskolnikov, and she stood still at parting on the pavement, this gentleman, who was just passing, started on hearing Sonia’s words: “and I asked where Mr. Raskolnikov lived?” He turned a rapid but attentive look upon all three, especially upon Raskolnikov, to whom Sonia was speaking; then looked back and noted the house. All this was done in an instant as he passed, and trying not to betray his interest, he walked on more slowly as though waiting for something. He was waiting for Sonia; he saw that they were parting, and that Sonia was going home.\n\n“Home? Where? I’ve seen that face somewhere,” he thought. “I must find out.”\n\nAt the turning he crossed over, looked round, and saw Sonia coming the same way, noticing nothing. She turned the corner. He followed her on the other side. After about fifty paces he crossed over again, overtook her and kept two or three yards behind her.\n\nHe was a man about fifty, rather tall and thickly set, with broad high shoulders which made him look as though he stooped a little. He wore good and fashionable clothes, and looked like a gentleman of position. He carried a handsome cane, which he tapped on the pavement at each step; his gloves were spotless. He had a broad, rather pleasant face with high cheek-bones and a fresh colour, not often seen in Petersburg. His flaxen hair was still abundant, and only touched here and there with grey, and his thick square beard was even lighter than his hair. His eyes were blue and had a cold and thoughtful look; his lips were crimson. He was a remarkedly well-preserved man and looked much younger than his years.\n\nWhen Sonia came out on the canal bank, they were the only two persons on the pavement. He observed her dreaminess and preoccupation. On reaching the house where she lodged, Sonia turned in at the gate; he followed her, seeming rather surprised. In the courtyard she turned to the right corner. “Bah!” muttered the unknown gentleman, and mounted the stairs behind her. Only then Sonia noticed him. She reached the third storey, turned down the passage, and rang at No. 9. On the door was inscribed in chalk, “Kapernaumov, Tailor.” “Bah!” the stranger repeated again, wondering at the strange coincidence, and he rang next door, at No. 8. The doors were two or three yards apart.\n\n“You lodge at Kapernaumov’s,” he said, looking at Sonia and laughing. “He altered a waistcoat for me yesterday. I am staying close here at Madame Resslich’s. How odd!” Sonia looked at him attentively.\n\n“We are neighbours,” he went on gaily. “I only came to town the day before yesterday. Good-bye for the present.”\n\nSonia made no reply; the door opened and she slipped in. She felt for some reason ashamed and uneasy.\n*****\n\nOn the way to Porfiry’s, Razumihin was obviously excited.\n\n“That’s capital, brother,” he repeated several times, “and I am glad! I am glad!”\n\n“What are you glad about?” Raskolnikov thought to himself.\n\n“I didn’t know that you pledged things at the old woman’s, too. And... was it long ago? I mean, was it long since you were there?”\n\n“What a simple-hearted fool he is!”\n\n“When was it?” Raskolnikov stopped still to recollect. “Two or three days before her death it must have been. But I am not going to redeem the things now,” he put in with a sort of hurried and conspicuous solicitude about the things. “I’ve not more than a silver rouble left... after last night’s accursed delirium!”\n\nHe laid special emphasis on the delirium.\n\n“Yes, yes,” Razumihin hastened to agree--with what was not clear. “Then that’s why you... were stuck... partly... you know in your delirium you were continually mentioning some rings or chains! Yes, yes... that’s clear, it’s all clear now.”\n\n“Hullo! How that idea must have got about among them. Here this man will go to the stake for me, and I find him delighted at having it _cleared up_ why I spoke of rings in my delirium! What a hold the idea must have on all of them!”\n\n“Shall we find him?” he asked suddenly.\n\n“Oh, yes,” Razumihin answered quickly. “He is a nice fellow, you will see, brother. Rather clumsy, that is to say, he is a man of polished manners, but I mean clumsy in a different sense. He is an intelligent fellow, very much so indeed, but he has his own range of ideas.... He is incredulous, sceptical, cynical... he likes to impose on people, or rather to make fun of them. His is the old, circumstantial method.... But he understands his work... thoroughly.... Last year he cleared up a case of murder in which the police had hardly a clue. He is very, very anxious to make your acquaintance!”\n\n“On what grounds is he so anxious?”\n\n“Oh, it’s not exactly... you see, since you’ve been ill I happen to have mentioned you several times.... So, when he heard about you... about your being a law student and not able to finish your studies, he said, ‘What a pity!’ And so I concluded... from everything together, not only that; yesterday Zametov... you know, Rodya, I talked some nonsense on the way home to you yesterday, when I was drunk... I am afraid, brother, of your exaggerating it, you see.”\n\n“What? That they think I am a madman? Maybe they are right,” he said with a constrained smile.\n\n“Yes, yes.... That is, pooh, no!... But all that I said (and there was something else too) it was all nonsense, drunken nonsense.”\n\n“But why are you apologising? I am so sick of it all!” Raskolnikov cried with exaggerated irritability. It was partly assumed, however.\n\n“I know, I know, I understand. Believe me, I understand. One’s ashamed to speak of it.”\n\n“If you are ashamed, then don’t speak of it.”\n\nBoth were silent. Razumihin was more than ecstatic and Raskolnikov perceived it with repulsion. He was alarmed, too, by what Razumihin had just said about Porfiry.\n\n“I shall have to pull a long face with him too,” he thought, with a beating heart, and he turned white, “and do it naturally, too. But the most natural thing would be to do nothing at all. Carefully do nothing at all! No, _carefully_ would not be natural again.... Oh, well, we shall see how it turns out.... We shall see... directly. Is it a good thing to go or not? The butterfly flies to the light. My heart is beating, that’s what’s bad!”\n\n“In this grey house,” said Razumihin.\n\n“The most important thing, does Porfiry know that I was at the old hag’s flat yesterday... and asked about the blood? I must find that out instantly, as soon as I go in, find out from his face; otherwise... I’ll find out, if it’s my ruin.”\n\n“I say, brother,” he said suddenly, addressing Razumihin, with a sly smile, “I have been noticing all day that you seem to be curiously excited. Isn’t it so?”\n\n“Excited? Not a bit of it,” said Razumihin, stung to the quick.\n\n“Yes, brother, I assure you it’s noticeable. Why, you sat on your chair in a way you never do sit, on the edge somehow, and you seemed to be writhing all the time. You kept jumping up for nothing. One moment you were angry, and the next your face looked like a sweetmeat. You even blushed; especially when you were invited to dinner, you blushed awfully.”\n\n“Nothing of the sort, nonsense! What do you mean?”\n\n“But why are you wriggling out of it, like a schoolboy? By Jove, there he’s blushing again.”\n\n“What a pig you are!”\n\n“But why are you so shamefaced about it? Romeo! Stay, I’ll tell of you to-day. Ha-ha-ha! I’ll make mother laugh, and someone else, too...”\n\n“Listen, listen, listen, this is serious.... What next, you fiend!” Razumihin was utterly overwhelmed, turning cold with horror. “What will you tell them? Come, brother... foo! what a pig you are!”\n\n“You are like a summer rose. And if only you knew how it suits you; a Romeo over six foot high! And how you’ve washed to-day--you cleaned your nails, I declare. Eh? That’s something unheard of! Why, I do believe you’ve got pomatum on your hair! Bend down.”\n“Pig!”\n\nRaskolnikov laughed as though he could not restrain himself. So laughing, they entered Porfiry Petrovitch’s flat. This is what Raskolnikov wanted: from within they could be heard laughing as they came in, still guffawing in the passage.\n\n“Not a word here or I’ll... brain you!” Razumihin whispered furiously, seizing Raskolnikov by the shoulder.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nRaskolnikov was already entering the room. He came in looking as though he had the utmost difficulty not to burst out laughing again. Behind him Razumihin strode in gawky and awkward, shamefaced and red as a peony, with an utterly crestfallen and ferocious expression. His face and whole figure really were ridiculous at that moment and amply justified Raskolnikov’s laughter. Raskolnikov, not waiting for an introduction, bowed to Porfiry Petrovitch, who stood in the middle of the room looking inquiringly at them. He held out his hand and shook hands, still apparently making desperate efforts to subdue his mirth and utter a few words to introduce himself. But he had no sooner succeeded in assuming a serious air and muttering something when he suddenly glanced again as though accidentally at Razumihin, and could no longer control himself: his stifled laughter broke out the more irresistibly the more he tried to restrain it. The extraordinary ferocity with which Razumihin received this “spontaneous” mirth gave the whole scene the appearance of most genuine fun and naturalness. Razumihin strengthened this impression as though on purpose.\n\n“Fool! You fiend,” he roared, waving his arm which at once struck a little round table with an empty tea-glass on it. Everything was sent flying and crashing.\n\n“But why break chairs, gentlemen? You know it’s a loss to the Crown,” Porfiry Petrovitch quoted gaily.\n\nRaskolnikov was still laughing, with his hand in Porfiry Petrovitch’s, but anxious not to overdo it, awaited the right moment to put a natural end to it. Razumihin, completely put to confusion by upsetting the table and smashing the glass, gazed gloomily at the fragments, cursed and turned sharply to the window where he stood looking out with his back to the company with a fiercely scowling countenance, seeing nothing. Porfiry Petrovitch laughed and was ready to go on laughing, but obviously looked for explanations. Zametov had been sitting in the corner, but he rose at the visitors’ entrance and was standing in expectation with a smile on his lips, though he looked with surprise and even it seemed incredulity at the whole scene and at Raskolnikov with a certain embarrassment. Zametov’s unexpected presence struck Raskolnikov unpleasantly.\n\n“I’ve got to think of that,” he thought. “Excuse me, please,” he began, affecting extreme embarrassment. “Raskolnikov.”\n\n“Not at all, very pleasant to see you... and how pleasantly you’ve come in.... Why, won’t he even say good-morning?” Porfiry Petrovitch nodded at Razumihin.\n\n“Upon my honour I don’t know why he is in such a rage with me. I only told him as we came along that he was like Romeo... and proved it. And that was all, I think!”\n\n“Pig!” ejaculated Razumihin, without turning round.\n\n“There must have been very grave grounds for it, if he is so furious at the word,” Porfiry laughed.\n\n“Oh, you sharp lawyer!... Damn you all!” snapped Razumihin, and suddenly bursting out laughing himself, he went up to Porfiry with a more cheerful face as though nothing had happened. “That’ll do! We are all fools. To come to business. This is my friend Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov; in the first place he has heard of you and wants to make your acquaintance, and secondly, he has a little matter of business with you. Bah! Zametov, what brought you here? Have you met before? Have you known each other long?”\n\n“What does this mean?” thought Raskolnikov uneasily.\n\nZametov seemed taken aback, but not very much so.\n\n“Why, it was at your rooms we met yesterday,” he said easily.\n\n“Then I have been spared the trouble. All last week he was begging me to introduce him to you. Porfiry and you have sniffed each other out without me. Where is your tobacco?”\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch was wearing a dressing-gown, very clean linen, and trodden-down slippers. He was a man of about five and thirty, short, stout even to corpulence, and clean shaven. He wore his hair cut short and had a large round head, particularly prominent at the back. His soft, round, rather snub-nosed face was of a sickly yellowish colour, but had a vigorous and rather ironical expression. It would have been good-natured except for a look in the eyes, which shone with a watery, mawkish light under almost white, blinking eyelashes. The expression of those eyes was strangely out of keeping with his somewhat womanish figure, and gave it something far more serious than could be guessed at first sight.\n\nAs soon as Porfiry Petrovitch heard that his visitor had a little matter of business with him, he begged him to sit down on the sofa and sat down himself on the other end, waiting for him to explain his business, with that careful and over-serious attention which is at once oppressive and embarrassing, especially to a stranger, and especially if what you are discussing is in your opinion of far too little importance for such exceptional solemnity. But in brief and coherent phrases Raskolnikov explained his business clearly and exactly, and was so well satisfied with himself that he even succeeded in taking a good look at Porfiry. Porfiry Petrovitch did not once take his eyes off him. Razumihin, sitting opposite at the same table, listened warmly and impatiently, looking from one to the other every moment with rather excessive interest.\n\n“Fool,” Raskolnikov swore to himself.\n\n“You have to give information to the police,” Porfiry replied, with a most businesslike air, “that having learnt of this incident, that is of the murder, you beg to inform the lawyer in charge of the case that such and such things belong to you, and that you desire to redeem them... or... but they will write to you.”\n\n“That’s just the point, that at the present moment,” Raskolnikov tried his utmost to feign embarrassment, “I am not quite in funds... and even this trifling sum is beyond me... I only wanted, you see, for the present to declare that the things are mine, and that when I have money....”\n\n“That’s no matter,” answered Porfiry Petrovitch, receiving his explanation of his pecuniary position coldly, “but you can, if you prefer, write straight to me, to say, that having been informed of the matter, and claiming such and such as your property, you beg...”\n\n“On an ordinary sheet of paper?” Raskolnikov interrupted eagerly, again interested in the financial side of the question.\n\n“Oh, the most ordinary,” and suddenly Porfiry Petrovitch looked with obvious irony at him, screwing up his eyes and, as it were, winking at him. But perhaps it was Raskolnikov’s fancy, for it all lasted but a moment. There was certainly something of the sort, Raskolnikov could have sworn he winked at him, goodness knows why.\n\n“He knows,” flashed through his mind like lightning.\n\n“Forgive my troubling you about such trifles,” he went on, a little disconcerted, “the things are only worth five roubles, but I prize them particularly for the sake of those from whom they came to me, and I must confess that I was alarmed when I heard...”\n\n“That’s why you were so much struck when I mentioned to Zossimov that Porfiry was inquiring for everyone who had pledges!” Razumihin put in with obvious intention.\n\nThis was really unbearable. Raskolnikov could not help glancing at him with a flash of vindictive anger in his black eyes, but immediately recollected himself.\n\n“You seem to be jeering at me, brother?” he said to him, with a well-feigned irritability. “I dare say I do seem to you absurdly anxious about such trash; but you mustn’t think me selfish or grasping for that, and these two things may be anything but trash in my eyes. I told you just now that the silver watch, though it’s not worth a cent, is the only thing left us of my father’s. You may laugh at me, but my mother is here,” he turned suddenly to Porfiry, “and if she knew,” he turned again hurriedly to Razumihin, carefully making his voice tremble, “that the watch was lost, she would be in despair! You know what women are!”\n\n“Not a bit of it! I didn’t mean that at all! Quite the contrary!” shouted Razumihin distressed.\n\n“Was it right? Was it natural? Did I overdo it?” Raskolnikov asked himself in a tremor. “Why did I say that about women?”\n\n“Oh, your mother is with you?” Porfiry Petrovitch inquired.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“When did she come?”\n\n“Last night.”\n\nPorfiry paused as though reflecting.\n\n“Your things would not in any case be lost,” he went on calmly and coldly. “I have been expecting you here for some time.”\n\nAnd as though that was a matter of no importance, he carefully offered the ash-tray to Razumihin, who was ruthlessly scattering cigarette ash over the carpet. Raskolnikov shuddered, but Porfiry did not seem to be looking at him, and was still concerned with Razumihin’s cigarette.\n\n“What? Expecting him? Why, did you know that he had pledges _there_?” cried Razumihin.\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch addressed himself to Raskolnikov.\n\n“Your things, the ring and the watch, were wrapped up together, and on the paper your name was legibly written in pencil, together with the date on which you left them with her...”\n\n“How observant you are!” Raskolnikov smiled awkwardly, doing his very utmost to look him straight in the face, but he failed, and suddenly added:\n\n“I say that because I suppose there were a great many pledges... that it must be difficult to remember them all.... But you remember them all so clearly, and... and...”\n\n“Stupid! Feeble!” he thought. “Why did I add that?”\n\n“But we know all who had pledges, and you are the only one who hasn’t come forward,” Porfiry answered with hardly perceptible irony.\n\n“I haven’t been quite well.”\n\n“I heard that too. I heard, indeed, that you were in great distress about something. You look pale still.”\n\n“I am not pale at all.... No, I am quite well,” Raskolnikov snapped out rudely and angrily, completely changing his tone. His anger was mounting, he could not repress it. “And in my anger I shall betray myself,” flashed through his mind again. “Why are they torturing me?”\n\n“Not quite well!” Razumihin caught him up. “What next! He was unconscious and delirious all yesterday. Would you believe, Porfiry, as soon as our backs were turned, he dressed, though he could hardly stand, and gave us the slip and went off on a spree somewhere till midnight, delirious all the time! Would you believe it! Extraordinary!”\n\n“Really delirious? You don’t say so!” Porfiry shook his head in a womanish way.\n\n“Nonsense! Don’t you believe it! But you don’t believe it anyway,” Raskolnikov let slip in his anger. But Porfiry Petrovitch did not seem to catch those strange words.\n\n“But how could you have gone out if you hadn’t been delirious?” Razumihin got hot suddenly. “What did you go out for? What was the object of it? And why on the sly? Were you in your senses when you did it? Now that all danger is over I can speak plainly.”\n\n“I was awfully sick of them yesterday.” Raskolnikov addressed Porfiry suddenly with a smile of insolent defiance, “I ran away from them to take lodgings where they wouldn’t find me, and took a lot of money with me. Mr. Zametov there saw it. I say, Mr. Zametov, was I sensible or delirious yesterday; settle our dispute.”\n\nHe could have strangled Zametov at that moment, so hateful were his expression and his silence to him.\n\n“In my opinion you talked sensibly and even artfully, but you were extremely irritable,” Zametov pronounced dryly.\n\n“And Nikodim Fomitch was telling me to-day,” put in Porfiry Petrovitch, “that he met you very late last night in the lodging of a man who had been run over.”\n\n“And there,” said Razumihin, “weren’t you mad then? You gave your last penny to the widow for the funeral. If you wanted to help, give fifteen or twenty even, but keep three roubles for yourself at least, but he flung away all the twenty-five at once!”\n\n“Maybe I found a treasure somewhere and you know nothing of it? So that’s why I was liberal yesterday.... Mr. Zametov knows I’ve found a treasure! Excuse us, please, for disturbing you for half an hour with such trivialities,” he said, turning to Porfiry Petrovitch, with trembling lips. “We are boring you, aren’t we?”\n\n“Oh no, quite the contrary, quite the contrary! If only you knew how you interest me! It’s interesting to look on and listen... and I am really glad you have come forward at last.”\n\n“But you might give us some tea! My throat’s dry,” cried Razumihin.\n\n“Capital idea! Perhaps we will all keep you company. Wouldn’t you like... something more essential before tea?”\n\n“Get along with you!”\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch went out to order tea.\n\nRaskolnikov’s thoughts were in a whirl. He was in terrible exasperation.\n\n“The worst of it is they don’t disguise it; they don’t care to stand on ceremony! And how if you didn’t know me at all, did you come to talk to Nikodim Fomitch about me? So they don’t care to hide that they are tracking me like a pack of dogs. They simply spit in my face.” He was shaking with rage. “Come, strike me openly, don’t play with me like a cat with a mouse. It’s hardly civil, Porfiry Petrovitch, but perhaps I won’t allow it! I shall get up and throw the whole truth in your ugly faces, and you’ll see how I despise you.” He could hardly breathe. “And what if it’s only my fancy? What if I am mistaken, and through inexperience I get angry and don’t keep up my nasty part? Perhaps it’s all unintentional. All their phrases are the usual ones, but there is something about them.... It all might be said, but there is something. Why did he say bluntly, ‘With her’? Why did Zametov add that I spoke artfully? Why do they speak in that tone? Yes, the tone.... Razumihin is sitting here, why does he see nothing? That innocent blockhead never does see anything! Feverish again! Did Porfiry wink at me just now? Of course it’s nonsense! What could he wink for? Are they trying to upset my nerves or are they teasing me? Either it’s ill fancy or they know! Even Zametov is rude.... Is Zametov rude? Zametov has changed his mind. I foresaw he would change his mind! He is at home here, while it’s my first visit. Porfiry does not consider him a visitor; sits with his back to him. They’re as thick as thieves, no doubt, over me! Not a doubt they were talking about me before we came. Do they know about the flat? If only they’d make haste! When I said that I ran away to take a flat he let it pass.... I put that in cleverly about a flat, it may be of use afterwards.... Delirious, indeed... ha-ha-ha! He knows all about last night! He didn’t know of my mother’s arrival! The hag had written the date on in pencil! You are wrong, you won’t catch me! There are no facts... it’s all supposition! You produce facts! The flat even isn’t a fact but delirium. I know what to say to them.... Do they know about the flat? I won’t go without finding out. What did I come for? But my being angry now, maybe is a fact! Fool, how irritable I am! Perhaps that’s right; to play the invalid.... He is feeling me. He will try to catch me. Why did I come?”\n\nAll this flashed like lightning through his mind.\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch returned quickly. He became suddenly more jovial.\n\n“Your party yesterday, brother, has left my head rather.... And I am out of sorts altogether,” he began in quite a different tone, laughing to Razumihin.\n\n“Was it interesting? I left you yesterday at the most interesting point. Who got the best of it?”\n\n“Oh, no one, of course. They got on to everlasting questions, floated off into space.”\n\n“Only fancy, Rodya, what we got on to yesterday. Whether there is such a thing as crime. I told you that we talked our heads off.”\n\n“What is there strange? It’s an everyday social question,” Raskolnikov answered casually.\n\n“The question wasn’t put quite like that,” observed Porfiry.\n\n“Not quite, that’s true,” Razumihin agreed at once, getting warm and hurried as usual. “Listen, Rodion, and tell us your opinion, I want to hear it. I was fighting tooth and nail with them and wanted you to help me. I told them you were coming.... It began with the socialist doctrine. You know their doctrine; crime is a protest against the abnormality of the social organisation and nothing more, and nothing more; no other causes admitted!...”\n\n“You are wrong there,” cried Porfiry Petrovitch; he was noticeably animated and kept laughing as he looked at Razumihin, which made him more excited than ever.\n\n“Nothing is admitted,” Razumihin interrupted with heat.\n\n“I am not wrong. I’ll show you their pamphlets. Everything with them is ‘the influence of environment,’ and nothing else. Their favourite phrase! From which it follows that, if society is normally organised, all crime will cease at once, since there will be nothing to protest against and all men will become righteous in one instant. Human nature is not taken into account, it is excluded, it’s not supposed to exist! They don’t recognise that humanity, developing by a historical living process, will become at last a normal society, but they believe that a social system that has come out of some mathematical brain is going to organise all humanity at once and make it just and sinless in an instant, quicker than any living process! That’s why they instinctively dislike history, ‘nothing but ugliness and stupidity in it,’ and they explain it all as stupidity! That’s why they so dislike the _living_ process of life; they don’t want a _living soul_! The living soul demands life, the soul won’t obey the rules of mechanics, the soul is an object of suspicion, the soul is retrograde! But what they want though it smells of death and can be made of India-rubber, at least is not alive, has no will, is servile and won’t revolt! And it comes in the end to their reducing everything to the building of walls and the planning of rooms and passages in a phalanstery! The phalanstery is ready, indeed, but your human nature is not ready for the phalanstery--it wants life, it hasn’t completed its vital process, it’s too soon for the graveyard! You can’t skip over nature by logic. Logic presupposes three possibilities, but there are millions! Cut away a million, and reduce it all to the question of comfort! That’s the easiest solution of the problem! It’s seductively clear and you musn’t think about it. That’s the great thing, you mustn’t think! The whole secret of life in two pages of print!”\n\n“Now he is off, beating the drum! Catch hold of him, do!” laughed Porfiry. “Can you imagine,” he turned to Raskolnikov, “six people holding forth like that last night, in one room, with punch as a preliminary! No, brother, you are wrong, environment accounts for a great deal in crime; I can assure you of that.”\n\n“Oh, I know it does, but just tell me: a man of forty violates a child of ten; was it environment drove him to it?”\n\n“Well, strictly speaking, it did,” Porfiry observed with noteworthy gravity; “a crime of that nature may be very well ascribed to the influence of environment.”\n\nRazumihin was almost in a frenzy. “Oh, if you like,” he roared. “I’ll prove to you that your white eyelashes may very well be ascribed to the Church of Ivan the Great’s being two hundred and fifty feet high, and I will prove it clearly, exactly, progressively, and even with a Liberal tendency! I undertake to! Will you bet on it?”\n\n“Done! Let’s hear, please, how he will prove it!”\n\n“He is always humbugging, confound him,” cried Razumihin, jumping up and gesticulating. “What’s the use of talking to you? He does all that on purpose; you don’t know him, Rodion! He took their side yesterday, simply to make fools of them. And the things he said yesterday! And they were delighted! He can keep it up for a fortnight together. Last year he persuaded us that he was going into a monastery: he stuck to it for two months. Not long ago he took it into his head to declare he was going to get married, that he had everything ready for the wedding. He ordered new clothes indeed. We all began to congratulate him. There was no bride, nothing, all pure fantasy!”\n\n“Ah, you are wrong! I got the clothes before. It was the new clothes in fact that made me think of taking you in.”\n\n“Are you such a good dissembler?” Raskolnikov asked carelessly.\n\n“You wouldn’t have supposed it, eh? Wait a bit, I shall take you in, too. Ha-ha-ha! No, I’ll tell you the truth. All these questions about crime, environment, children, recall to my mind an article of yours which interested me at the time. ‘On Crime’... or something of the sort, I forget the title, I read it with pleasure two months ago in the _Periodical Review_.”\n\n“My article? In the _Periodical Review_?” Raskolnikov asked in astonishment. “I certainly did write an article upon a book six months ago when I left the university, but I sent it to the _Weekly Review_.”\n\n“But it came out in the _Periodical_.”\n\n“And the _Weekly Review_ ceased to exist, so that’s why it wasn’t printed at the time.”\n\n“That’s true; but when it ceased to exist, the _Weekly Review_ was amalgamated with the _Periodical_, and so your article appeared two months ago in the latter. Didn’t you know?”\n\nRaskolnikov had not known.\n\n“Why, you might get some money out of them for the article! What a strange person you are! You lead such a solitary life that you know nothing of matters that concern you directly. It’s a fact, I assure you.”\n\n“Bravo, Rodya! I knew nothing about it either!” cried Razumihin. “I’ll run to-day to the reading-room and ask for the number. Two months ago? What was the date? It doesn’t matter though, I will find it. Think of not telling us!”\n\n“How did you find out that the article was mine? It’s only signed with an initial.”\n\n“I only learnt it by chance, the other day. Through the editor; I know him.... I was very much interested.”\n\n“I analysed, if I remember, the psychology of a criminal before and after the crime.”\n\n“Yes, and you maintained that the perpetration of a crime is always accompanied by illness. Very, very original, but... it was not that part of your article that interested me so much, but an idea at the end of the article which I regret to say you merely suggested without working it out clearly. There is, if you recollect, a suggestion that there are certain persons who can... that is, not precisely are able to, but have a perfect right to commit breaches of morality and crimes, and that the law is not for them.”\n\nRaskolnikov smiled at the exaggerated and intentional distortion of his idea.\n\n“What? What do you mean? A right to crime? But not because of the influence of environment?” Razumihin inquired with some alarm even.\n\n“No, not exactly because of it,” answered Porfiry. “In his article all men are divided into ‘ordinary’ and ‘extraordinary.’ Ordinary men have to live in submission, have no right to transgress the law, because, don’t you see, they are ordinary. But extraordinary men have a right to commit any crime and to transgress the law in any way, just because they are extraordinary. That was your idea, if I am not mistaken?”\n\n“What do you mean? That can’t be right?” Razumihin muttered in bewilderment.\n\nRaskolnikov smiled again. He saw the point at once, and knew where they wanted to drive him. He decided to take up the challenge.\n\n“That wasn’t quite my contention,” he began simply and modestly. “Yet I admit that you have stated it almost correctly; perhaps, if you like, perfectly so.” (It almost gave him pleasure to admit this.) “The only difference is that I don’t contend that extraordinary people are always bound to commit breaches of morals, as you call it. In fact, I doubt whether such an argument could be published. I simply hinted that an ‘extraordinary’ man has the right... that is not an official right, but an inner right to decide in his own conscience to overstep... certain obstacles, and only in case it is essential for the practical fulfilment of his idea (sometimes, perhaps, of benefit to the whole of humanity). You say that my article isn’t definite; I am ready to make it as clear as I can. Perhaps I am right in thinking you want me to; very well. I maintain that if the discoveries of Kepler and Newton could not have been made known except by sacrificing the lives of one, a dozen, a hundred, or more men, Newton would have had the right, would indeed have been in duty-bound... to _eliminate_ the dozen or the hundred men for the sake of making his discoveries known to the whole of humanity. But it does not follow from that that Newton had a right to murder people right and left and to steal every day in the market. Then, I remember, I maintain in my article that all... well, legislators and leaders of men, such as Lycurgus, Solon, Mahomet, Napoleon, and so on, were all without exception criminals, from the very fact that, making a new law, they transgressed the ancient one, handed down from their ancestors and held sacred by the people, and they did not stop short at bloodshed either, if that bloodshed--often of innocent persons fighting bravely in defence of ancient law--were of use to their cause. It’s remarkable, in fact, that the majority, indeed, of these benefactors and leaders of humanity were guilty of terrible carnage. In short, I maintain that all great men or even men a little out of the common, that is to say capable of giving some new word, must from their very nature be criminals--more or less, of course. Otherwise it’s hard for them to get out of the common rut; and to remain in the common rut is what they can’t submit to, from their very nature again, and to my mind they ought not, indeed, to submit to it. You see that there is nothing particularly new in all that. The same thing has been printed and read a thousand times before. As for my division of people into ordinary and extraordinary, I acknowledge that it’s somewhat arbitrary, but I don’t insist upon exact numbers. I only believe in my leading idea that men are _in general_ divided by a law of nature into two categories, inferior (ordinary), that is, so to say, material that serves only to reproduce its kind, and men who have the gift or the talent to utter _a new word_. There are, of course, innumerable sub-divisions, but the distinguishing features of both categories are fairly well marked. The first category, generally speaking, are men conservative in temperament and law-abiding; they live under control and love to be controlled. To my thinking it is their duty to be controlled, because that’s their vocation, and there is nothing humiliating in it for them. The second category all transgress the law; they are destroyers or disposed to destruction according to their capacities. The crimes of these men are of course relative and varied; for the most part they seek in very varied ways the destruction of the present for the sake of the better. But if such a one is forced for the sake of his idea to step over a corpse or wade through blood, he can, I maintain, find within himself, in his conscience, a sanction for wading through blood--that depends on the idea and its dimensions, note that. It’s only in that sense I speak of their right to crime in my article (you remember it began with the legal question). There’s no need for such anxiety, however; the masses will scarcely ever admit this right, they punish them or hang them (more or less), and in doing so fulfil quite justly their conservative vocation. But the same masses set these criminals on a pedestal in the next generation and worship them (more or less). The first category is always the man of the present, the second the man of the future. The first preserve the world and people it, the second move the world and lead it to its goal. Each class has an equal right to exist. In fact, all have equal rights with me--and _vive la guerre éternelle_--till the New Jerusalem, of course!”\n\n“Then you believe in the New Jerusalem, do you?”\n\n“I do,” Raskolnikov answered firmly; as he said these words and during the whole preceding tirade he kept his eyes on one spot on the carpet.\n\n“And... and do you believe in God? Excuse my curiosity.”\n\n“I do,” repeated Raskolnikov, raising his eyes to Porfiry.\n\n“And... do you believe in Lazarus’ rising from the dead?”\n\n“I... I do. Why do you ask all this?”\n\n“You believe it literally?”\n\n“Literally.”\n\n“You don’t say so.... I asked from curiosity. Excuse me. But let us go back to the question; they are not always executed. Some, on the contrary...”\n\n“Triumph in their lifetime? Oh, yes, some attain their ends in this life, and then...”\n\n“They begin executing other people?”\n\n“If it’s necessary; indeed, for the most part they do. Your remark is very witty.”\n\n“Thank you. But tell me this: how do you distinguish those extraordinary people from the ordinary ones? Are there signs at their birth? I feel there ought to be more exactitude, more external definition. Excuse the natural anxiety of a practical law-abiding citizen, but couldn’t they adopt a special uniform, for instance, couldn’t they wear something, be branded in some way? For you know if confusion arises and a member of one category imagines that he belongs to the other, begins to ‘eliminate obstacles’ as you so happily expressed it, then...”\n\n“Oh, that very often happens! That remark is wittier than the other.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“No reason to; but take note that the mistake can only arise in the first category, that is among the ordinary people (as I perhaps unfortunately called them). In spite of their predisposition to obedience very many of them, through a playfulness of nature, sometimes vouchsafed even to the cow, like to imagine themselves advanced people, ‘destroyers,’ and to push themselves into the ‘new movement,’ and this quite sincerely. Meanwhile the really _new_ people are very often unobserved by them, or even despised as reactionaries of grovelling tendencies. But I don’t think there is any considerable danger here, and you really need not be uneasy for they never go very far. Of course, they might have a thrashing sometimes for letting their fancy run away with them and to teach them their place, but no more; in fact, even this isn’t necessary as they castigate themselves, for they are very conscientious: some perform this service for one another and others chastise themselves with their own hands.... They will impose various public acts of penitence upon themselves with a beautiful and edifying effect; in fact you’ve nothing to be uneasy about.... It’s a law of nature.”\n\n“Well, you have certainly set my mind more at rest on that score; but there’s another thing worries me. Tell me, please, are there many people who have the right to kill others, these extraordinary people? I am ready to bow down to them, of course, but you must admit it’s alarming if there are a great many of them, eh?”\n\n“Oh, you needn’t worry about that either,” Raskolnikov went on in the same tone. “People with new ideas, people with the faintest capacity for saying something _new_, are extremely few in number, extraordinarily so in fact. One thing only is clear, that the appearance of all these grades and sub-divisions of men must follow with unfailing regularity some law of nature. That law, of course, is unknown at present, but I am convinced that it exists, and one day may become known. The vast mass of mankind is mere material, and only exists in order by some great effort, by some mysterious process, by means of some crossing of races and stocks, to bring into the world at last perhaps one man out of a thousand with a spark of independence. One in ten thousand perhaps--I speak roughly, approximately--is born with some independence, and with still greater independence one in a hundred thousand. The man of genius is one of millions, and the great geniuses, the crown of humanity, appear on earth perhaps one in many thousand millions. In fact I have not peeped into the retort in which all this takes place. But there certainly is and must be a definite law, it cannot be a matter of chance.”\n\n“Why, are you both joking?” Razumihin cried at last. “There you sit, making fun of one another. Are you serious, Rodya?”\n\nRaskolnikov raised his pale and almost mournful face and made no reply. And the unconcealed, persistent, nervous, and _discourteous_ sarcasm of Porfiry seemed strange to Razumihin beside that quiet and mournful face.\n\n“Well, brother, if you are really serious... You are right, of course, in saying that it’s not new, that it’s like what we’ve read and heard a thousand times already; but what is really original in all this, and is exclusively your own, to my horror, is that you sanction bloodshed _in the name of conscience_, and, excuse my saying so, with such fanaticism.... That, I take it, is the point of your article. But that sanction of bloodshed _by conscience_ is to my mind... more terrible than the official, legal sanction of bloodshed....”\n\n“You are quite right, it is more terrible,” Porfiry agreed.\n\n“Yes, you must have exaggerated! There is some mistake, I shall read it. You can’t think that! I shall read it.”\n\n“All that is not in the article, there’s only a hint of it,” said Raskolnikov.\n\n“Yes, yes.” Porfiry couldn’t sit still. “Your attitude to crime is pretty clear to me now, but... excuse me for my impertinence (I am really ashamed to be worrying you like this), you see, you’ve removed my anxiety as to the two grades getting mixed, but... there are various practical possibilities that make me uneasy! What if some man or youth imagines that he is a Lycurgus or Mahomet--a future one of course--and suppose he begins to remove all obstacles.... He has some great enterprise before him and needs money for it... and tries to get it... do you see?”\n\nZametov gave a sudden guffaw in his corner. Raskolnikov did not even raise his eyes to him.\n\n“I must admit,” he went on calmly, “that such cases certainly must arise. The vain and foolish are particularly apt to fall into that snare; young people especially.”\n\n“Yes, you see. Well then?”\n\n“What then?” Raskolnikov smiled in reply; “that’s not my fault. So it is and so it always will be. He said just now (he nodded at Razumihin) that I sanction bloodshed. Society is too well protected by prisons, banishment, criminal investigators, penal servitude. There’s no need to be uneasy. You have but to catch the thief.”\n\n“And what if we do catch him?”\n\n“Then he gets what he deserves.”\n\n“You are certainly logical. But what of his conscience?”\n\n“Why do you care about that?”\n\n“Simply from humanity.”\n\n“If he has a conscience he will suffer for his mistake. That will be his punishment--as well as the prison.”\n\n“But the real geniuses,” asked Razumihin frowning, “those who have the right to murder? Oughtn’t they to suffer at all even for the blood they’ve shed?”\n\n“Why the word _ought_? It’s not a matter of permission or prohibition. He will suffer if he is sorry for his victim. Pain and suffering are always inevitable for a large intelligence and a deep heart. The really great men must, I think, have great sadness on earth,” he added dreamily, not in the tone of the conversation.\n\nHe raised his eyes, looked earnestly at them all, smiled, and took his cap. He was too quiet by comparison with his manner at his entrance, and he felt this. Everyone got up.\n\n“Well, you may abuse me, be angry with me if you like,” Porfiry Petrovitch began again, “but I can’t resist. Allow me one little question (I know I am troubling you). There is just one little notion I want to express, simply that I may not forget it.”\n\n“Very good, tell me your little notion,” Raskolnikov stood waiting, pale and grave before him.\n\n“Well, you see... I really don’t know how to express it properly.... It’s a playful, psychological idea.... When you were writing your article, surely you couldn’t have helped, he-he! fancying yourself... just a little, an ‘extraordinary’ man, uttering a _new word_ in your sense.... That’s so, isn’t it?”\n\n“Quite possibly,” Raskolnikov answered contemptuously.\n\nRazumihin made a movement.\n\n“And, if so, could you bring yourself in case of worldly difficulties and hardship or for some service to humanity--to overstep obstacles?... For instance, to rob and murder?”\n\nAnd again he winked with his left eye, and laughed noiselessly just as before.\n\n“If I did I certainly should not tell you,” Raskolnikov answered with defiant and haughty contempt.\n\n“No, I was only interested on account of your article, from a literary point of view...”\n\n“Foo! how obvious and insolent that is!” Raskolnikov thought with repulsion.\n\n“Allow me to observe,” he answered dryly, “that I don’t consider myself a Mahomet or a Napoleon, nor any personage of that kind, and not being one of them I cannot tell you how I should act.”\n\n“Oh, come, don’t we all think ourselves Napoleons now in Russia?” Porfiry Petrovitch said with alarming familiarity.\n\nSomething peculiar betrayed itself in the very intonation of his voice.\n\n“Perhaps it was one of these future Napoleons who did for Alyona Ivanovna last week?” Zametov blurted out from the corner.\n\nRaskolnikov did not speak, but looked firmly and intently at Porfiry. Razumihin was scowling gloomily. He seemed before this to be noticing something. He looked angrily around. There was a minute of gloomy silence. Raskolnikov turned to go.\n\n“Are you going already?” Porfiry said amiably, holding out his hand with excessive politeness. “Very, very glad of your acquaintance. As for your request, have no uneasiness, write just as I told you, or, better still, come to me there yourself in a day or two... to-morrow, indeed. I shall be there at eleven o’clock for certain. We’ll arrange it all; we’ll have a talk. As one of the last to be _there_, you might perhaps be able to tell us something,” he added with a most good-natured expression.\n\n“You want to cross-examine me officially in due form?” Raskolnikov asked sharply.\n\n“Oh, why? That’s not necessary for the present. You misunderstand me. I lose no opportunity, you see, and... I’ve talked with all who had pledges.... I obtained evidence from some of them, and you are the last.... Yes, by the way,” he cried, seemingly suddenly delighted, “I just remember, what was I thinking of?” he turned to Razumihin, “you were talking my ears off about that Nikolay... of course, I know, I know very well,” he turned to Raskolnikov, “that the fellow is innocent, but what is one to do? We had to trouble Dmitri too.... This is the point, this is all: when you went up the stairs it was past seven, wasn’t it?”\n\n“Yes,” answered Raskolnikov, with an unpleasant sensation at the very moment he spoke that he need not have said it.\n\n“Then when you went upstairs between seven and eight, didn’t you see in a flat that stood open on a second storey, do you remember? two workmen or at least one of them? They were painting there, didn’t you notice them? It’s very, very important for them.”\n\n“Painters? No, I didn’t see them,” Raskolnikov answered slowly, as though ransacking his memory, while at the same instant he was racking every nerve, almost swooning with anxiety to conjecture as quickly as possible where the trap lay and not to overlook anything. “No, I didn’t see them, and I don’t think I noticed a flat like that open.... But on the fourth storey” (he had mastered the trap now and was triumphant) “I remember now that someone was moving out of the flat opposite Alyona Ivanovna’s.... I remember... I remember it clearly. Some porters were carrying out a sofa and they squeezed me against the wall. But painters... no, I don’t remember that there were any painters, and I don’t think that there was a flat open anywhere, no, there wasn’t.”\n\n“What do you mean?” Razumihin shouted suddenly, as though he had reflected and realised. “Why, it was on the day of the murder the painters were at work, and he was there three days before? What are you asking?”\n\n“Foo! I have muddled it!” Porfiry slapped himself on the forehead. “Deuce take it! This business is turning my brain!” he addressed Raskolnikov somewhat apologetically. “It would be such a great thing for us to find out whether anyone had seen them between seven and eight at the flat, so I fancied you could perhaps have told us something.... I quite muddled it.”\n\n“Then you should be more careful,” Razumihin observed grimly.\n\nThe last words were uttered in the passage. Porfiry Petrovitch saw them to the door with excessive politeness.\n\nThey went out into the street gloomy and sullen, and for some steps they did not say a word. Raskolnikov drew a deep breath.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n“I don’t believe it, I can’t believe it!” repeated Razumihin, trying in perplexity to refute Raskolnikov’s arguments.\n\nThey were by now approaching Bakaleyev’s lodgings, where Pulcheria Alexandrovna and Dounia had been expecting them a long while. Razumihin kept stopping on the way in the heat of discussion, confused and excited by the very fact that they were for the first time speaking openly about _it_.\n\n“Don’t believe it, then!” answered Raskolnikov, with a cold, careless smile. “You were noticing nothing as usual, but I was weighing every word.”\n\n“You are suspicious. That is why you weighed their words... h’m... certainly, I agree, Porfiry’s tone was rather strange, and still more that wretch Zametov!... You are right, there was something about him--but why? Why?”\n\n“He has changed his mind since last night.”\n\n“Quite the contrary! If they had that brainless idea, they would do their utmost to hide it, and conceal their cards, so as to catch you afterwards.... But it was all impudent and careless.”\n\n“If they had had facts--I mean, real facts--or at least grounds for suspicion, then they would certainly have tried to hide their game, in the hope of getting more (they would have made a search long ago besides). But they have no facts, not one. It is all mirage--all ambiguous. Simply a floating idea. So they try to throw me out by impudence. And perhaps, he was irritated at having no facts, and blurted it out in his vexation--or perhaps he has some plan... he seems an intelligent man. Perhaps he wanted to frighten me by pretending to know. They have a psychology of their own, brother. But it is loathsome explaining it all. Stop!”\n\n“And it’s insulting, insulting! I understand you. But... since we have spoken openly now (and it is an excellent thing that we have at last--I am glad) I will own now frankly that I noticed it in them long ago, this idea. Of course the merest hint only--an insinuation--but why an insinuation even? How dare they? What foundation have they? If only you knew how furious I have been. Think only! Simply because a poor student, unhinged by poverty and hypochondria, on the eve of a severe delirious illness (note that), suspicious, vain, proud, who has not seen a soul to speak to for six months, in rags and in boots without soles, has to face some wretched policemen and put up with their insolence; and the unexpected debt thrust under his nose, the I.O.U. presented by Tchebarov, the new paint, thirty degrees Reaumur and a stifling atmosphere, a crowd of people, the talk about the murder of a person where he had been just before, and all that on an empty stomach--he might well have a fainting fit! And that, that is what they found it all on! Damn them! I understand how annoying it is, but in your place, Rodya, I would laugh at them, or better still, spit in their ugly faces, and spit a dozen times in all directions. I’d hit out in all directions, neatly too, and so I’d put an end to it. Damn them! Don’t be downhearted. It’s a shame!”\n\n“He really has put it well, though,” Raskolnikov thought.\n\n“Damn them? But the cross-examination again, to-morrow?” he said with bitterness. “Must I really enter into explanations with them? I feel vexed as it is, that I condescended to speak to Zametov yesterday in the restaurant....”\n\n“Damn it! I will go myself to Porfiry. I will squeeze it out of him, as one of the family: he must let me know the ins and outs of it all! And as for Zametov...”\n\n“At last he sees through him!” thought Raskolnikov.\n\n“Stay!” cried Razumihin, seizing him by the shoulder again. “Stay! you were wrong. I have thought it out. You are wrong! How was that a trap? You say that the question about the workmen was a trap. But if you had done _that_, could you have said you had seen them painting the flat... and the workmen? On the contrary, you would have seen nothing, even if you had seen it. Who would own it against himself?”\n\n“If I had done _that thing_, I should certainly have said that I had seen the workmen and the flat,” Raskolnikov answered, with reluctance and obvious disgust.\n\n“But why speak against yourself?”\n\n“Because only peasants, or the most inexperienced novices deny everything flatly at examinations. If a man is ever so little developed and experienced, he will certainly try to admit all the external facts that can’t be avoided, but will seek other explanations of them, will introduce some special, unexpected turn, that will give them another significance and put them in another light. Porfiry might well reckon that I should be sure to answer so, and say I had seen them to give an air of truth, and then make some explanation.”\n\n“But he would have told you at once that the workmen could not have been there two days before, and that therefore you must have been there on the day of the murder at eight o’clock. And so he would have caught you over a detail.”\n\n“Yes, that is what he was reckoning on, that I should not have time to reflect, and should be in a hurry to make the most likely answer, and so would forget that the workmen could not have been there two days before.”\n\n“But how could you forget it?”\n\n“Nothing easier. It is in just such stupid things clever people are most easily caught. The more cunning a man is, the less he suspects that he will be caught in a simple thing. The more cunning a man is, the simpler the trap he must be caught in. Porfiry is not such a fool as you think....”\n\n“He is a knave then, if that is so!”\n\nRaskolnikov could not help laughing. But at the very moment, he was struck by the strangeness of his own frankness, and the eagerness with which he had made this explanation, though he had kept up all the preceding conversation with gloomy repulsion, obviously with a motive, from necessity.\n\n“I am getting a relish for certain aspects!” he thought to himself. But almost at the same instant he became suddenly uneasy, as though an unexpected and alarming idea had occurred to him. His uneasiness kept on increasing. They had just reached the entrance to Bakaleyev’s.\n\n“Go in alone!” said Raskolnikov suddenly. “I will be back directly.”\n\n“Where are you going? Why, we are just here.”\n\n“I can’t help it.... I will come in half an hour. Tell them.”\n\n“Say what you like, I will come with you.”\n\n“You, too, want to torture me!” he screamed, with such bitter irritation, such despair in his eyes that Razumihin’s hands dropped. He stood for some time on the steps, looking gloomily at Raskolnikov striding rapidly away in the direction of his lodging. At last, gritting his teeth and clenching his fist, he swore he would squeeze Porfiry like a lemon that very day, and went up the stairs to reassure Pulcheria Alexandrovna, who was by now alarmed at their long absence.\n\nWhen Raskolnikov got home, his hair was soaked with sweat and he was breathing heavily. He went rapidly up the stairs, walked into his unlocked room and at once fastened the latch. Then in senseless terror he rushed to the corner, to that hole under the paper where he had put the things; put his hand in, and for some minutes felt carefully in the hole, in every crack and fold of the paper. Finding nothing, he got up and drew a deep breath. As he was reaching the steps of Bakaleyev’s, he suddenly fancied that something, a chain, a stud or even a bit of paper in which they had been wrapped with the old woman’s handwriting on it, might somehow have slipped out and been lost in some crack, and then might suddenly turn up as unexpected, conclusive evidence against him.\n\nHe stood as though lost in thought, and a strange, humiliated, half senseless smile strayed on his lips. He took his cap at last and went quietly out of the room. His ideas were all tangled. He went dreamily through the gateway.\n\n“Here he is himself,” shouted a loud voice.\n\nHe raised his head.\n\nThe porter was standing at the door of his little room and was pointing him out to a short man who looked like an artisan, wearing a long coat and a waistcoat, and looking at a distance remarkably like a woman. He stooped, and his head in a greasy cap hung forward. From his wrinkled flabby face he looked over fifty; his little eyes were lost in fat and they looked out grimly, sternly and discontentedly.\n\n“What is it?” Raskolnikov asked, going up to the porter.\n\nThe man stole a look at him from under his brows and he looked at him attentively, deliberately; then he turned slowly and went out of the gate into the street without saying a word.\n\n“What is it?” cried Raskolnikov.\n\n“Why, he there was asking whether a student lived here, mentioned your name and whom you lodged with. I saw you coming and pointed you out and he went away. It’s funny.”\n\nThe porter too seemed rather puzzled, but not much so, and after wondering for a moment he turned and went back to his room.\n\nRaskolnikov ran after the stranger, and at once caught sight of him walking along the other side of the street with the same even, deliberate step with his eyes fixed on the ground, as though in meditation. He soon overtook him, but for some time walked behind him. At last, moving on to a level with him, he looked at his face. The man noticed him at once, looked at him quickly, but dropped his eyes again; and so they walked for a minute side by side without uttering a word.\n\n“You were inquiring for me... of the porter?” Raskolnikov said at last, but in a curiously quiet voice.\n\nThe man made no answer; he didn’t even look at him. Again they were both silent.\n\n“Why do you... come and ask for me... and say nothing.... What’s the meaning of it?”\n\nRaskolnikov’s voice broke and he seemed unable to articulate the words clearly.\n\nThe man raised his eyes this time and turned a gloomy sinister look at Raskolnikov.\n\n“Murderer!” he said suddenly in a quiet but clear and distinct voice.\n\nRaskolnikov went on walking beside him. His legs felt suddenly weak, a cold shiver ran down his spine, and his heart seemed to stand still for a moment, then suddenly began throbbing as though it were set free. So they walked for about a hundred paces, side by side in silence.\n\nThe man did not look at him.\n\n“What do you mean... what is.... Who is a murderer?” muttered Raskolnikov hardly audibly.\n\n“_You_ are a murderer,” the man answered still more articulately and emphatically, with a smile of triumphant hatred, and again he looked straight into Raskolnikov’s pale face and stricken eyes.\n\nThey had just reached the cross-roads. The man turned to the left without looking behind him. Raskolnikov remained standing, gazing after him. He saw him turn round fifty paces away and look back at him still standing there. Raskolnikov could not see clearly, but he fancied that he was again smiling the same smile of cold hatred and triumph.\n\nWith slow faltering steps, with shaking knees, Raskolnikov made his way back to his little garret, feeling chilled all over. He took off his cap and put it on the table, and for ten minutes he stood without moving. Then he sank exhausted on the sofa and with a weak moan of pain he stretched himself on it. So he lay for half an hour.\n\nHe thought of nothing. Some thoughts or fragments of thoughts, some images without order or coherence floated before his mind--faces of people he had seen in his childhood or met somewhere once, whom he would never have recalled, the belfry of the church at V., the billiard table in a restaurant and some officers playing billiards, the smell of cigars in some underground tobacco shop, a tavern room, a back staircase quite dark, all sloppy with dirty water and strewn with egg-shells, and the Sunday bells floating in from somewhere.... The images followed one another, whirling like a hurricane. Some of them he liked and tried to clutch at, but they faded and all the while there was an oppression within him, but it was not overwhelming, sometimes it was even pleasant.... The slight shivering still persisted, but that too was an almost pleasant sensation.\n\nHe heard the hurried footsteps of Razumihin; he closed his eyes and pretended to be asleep. Razumihin opened the door and stood for some time in the doorway as though hesitating, then he stepped softly into the room and went cautiously to the sofa. Raskolnikov heard Nastasya’s whisper:\n\n“Don’t disturb him! Let him sleep. He can have his dinner later.”\n\n“Quite so,” answered Razumihin. Both withdrew carefully and closed the door. Another half-hour passed. Raskolnikov opened his eyes, turned on his back again, clasping his hands behind his head.\n\n“Who is he? Who is that man who sprang out of the earth? Where was he, what did he see? He has seen it all, that’s clear. Where was he then? And from where did he see? Why has he only now sprung out of the earth? And how could he see? Is it possible? Hm...” continued Raskolnikov, turning cold and shivering, “and the jewel case Nikolay found behind the door--was that possible? A clue? You miss an infinitesimal line and you can build it into a pyramid of evidence! A fly flew by and saw it! Is it possible?” He felt with sudden loathing how weak, how physically weak he had become. “I ought to have known it,” he thought with a bitter smile. “And how dared I, knowing myself, knowing how I should be, take up an axe and shed blood! I ought to have known beforehand.... Ah, but I did know!” he whispered in despair. At times he came to a standstill at some thought.\n\n“No, those men are not made so. The real _Master_ to whom all is permitted storms Toulon, makes a massacre in Paris, _forgets_ an army in Egypt, _wastes_ half a million men in the Moscow expedition and gets off with a jest at Vilna. And altars are set up to him after his death, and so _all_ is permitted. No, such people, it seems, are not of flesh but of bronze!”\n\nOne sudden irrelevant idea almost made him laugh. Napoleon, the pyramids, Waterloo, and a wretched skinny old woman, a pawnbroker with a red trunk under her bed--it’s a nice hash for Porfiry Petrovitch to digest! How can they digest it! It’s too inartistic. “A Napoleon creep under an old woman’s bed! Ugh, how loathsome!”\n\nAt moments he felt he was raving. He sank into a state of feverish excitement. “The old woman is of no consequence,” he thought, hotly and incoherently. “The old woman was a mistake perhaps, but she is not what matters! The old woman was only an illness.... I was in a hurry to overstep.... I didn’t kill a human being, but a principle! I killed the principle, but I didn’t overstep, I stopped on this side.... I was only capable of killing. And it seems I wasn’t even capable of that... Principle? Why was that fool Razumihin abusing the socialists? They are industrious, commercial people; ‘the happiness of all’ is their case. No, life is only given to me once and I shall never have it again; I don’t want to wait for ‘the happiness of all.’ I want to live myself, or else better not live at all. I simply couldn’t pass by my mother starving, keeping my rouble in my pocket while I waited for the ‘happiness of all.’ I am putting my little brick into the happiness of all and so my heart is at peace. Ha-ha! Why have you let me slip? I only live once, I too want.... Ech, I am an æsthetic louse and nothing more,” he added suddenly, laughing like a madman. “Yes, I am certainly a louse,” he went on, clutching at the idea, gloating over it and playing with it with vindictive pleasure. “In the first place, because I can reason that I am one, and secondly, because for a month past I have been troubling benevolent Providence, calling it to witness that not for my own fleshly lusts did I undertake it, but with a grand and noble object--ha-ha! Thirdly, because I aimed at carrying it out as justly as possible, weighing, measuring and calculating. Of all the lice I picked out the most useless one and proposed to take from her only as much as I needed for the first step, no more nor less (so the rest would have gone to a monastery, according to her will, ha-ha!). And what shows that I am utterly a louse,” he added, grinding his teeth, “is that I am perhaps viler and more loathsome than the louse I killed, and _I felt beforehand_ that I should tell myself so _after_ killing her. Can anything be compared with the horror of that? The vulgarity! The abjectness! I understand the ‘prophet’ with his sabre, on his steed: Allah commands and ‘trembling’ creation must obey! The ‘prophet’ is right, he is right when he sets a battery across the street and blows up the innocent and the guilty without deigning to explain! It’s for you to obey, trembling creation, and not _to have desires_, for that’s not for you!... I shall never, never forgive the old woman!”\n\nHis hair was soaked with sweat, his quivering lips were parched, his eyes were fixed on the ceiling.\n\n“Mother, sister--how I loved them! Why do I hate them now? Yes, I hate them, I feel a physical hatred for them, I can’t bear them near me.... I went up to my mother and kissed her, I remember.... To embrace her and think if she only knew... shall I tell her then? That’s just what I might do.... _She_ must be the same as I am,” he added, straining himself to think, as it were struggling with delirium. “Ah, how I hate the old woman now! I feel I should kill her again if she came to life! Poor Lizaveta! Why did she come in?... It’s strange though, why is it I scarcely ever think of her, as though I hadn’t killed her? Lizaveta! Sonia! Poor gentle things, with gentle eyes.... Dear women! Why don’t they weep? Why don’t they moan? They give up everything... their eyes are soft and gentle.... Sonia, Sonia! Gentle Sonia!”\n\nHe lost consciousness; it seemed strange to him that he didn’t remember how he got into the street. It was late evening. The twilight had fallen and the full moon was shining more and more brightly; but there was a peculiar breathlessness in the air. There were crowds of people in the street; workmen and business people were making their way home; other people had come out for a walk; there was a smell of mortar, dust and stagnant water. Raskolnikov walked along, mournful and anxious; he was distinctly aware of having come out with a purpose, of having to do something in a hurry, but what it was he had forgotten. Suddenly he stood still and saw a man standing on the other side of the street, beckoning to him. He crossed over to him, but at once the man turned and walked away with his head hanging, as though he had made no sign to him. “Stay, did he really beckon?” Raskolnikov wondered, but he tried to overtake him. When he was within ten paces he recognised him and was frightened; it was the same man with stooping shoulders in the long coat. Raskolnikov followed him at a distance; his heart was beating; they went down a turning; the man still did not look round. “Does he know I am following him?” thought Raskolnikov. The man went into the gateway of a big house. Raskolnikov hastened to the gate and looked in to see whether he would look round and sign to him. In the court-yard the man did turn round and again seemed to beckon him. Raskolnikov at once followed him into the yard, but the man was gone. He must have gone up the first staircase. Raskolnikov rushed after him. He heard slow measured steps two flights above. The staircase seemed strangely familiar. He reached the window on the first floor; the moon shone through the panes with a melancholy and mysterious light; then he reached the second floor. Bah! this is the flat where the painters were at work... but how was it he did not recognise it at once? The steps of the man above had died away. “So he must have stopped or hidden somewhere.” He reached the third storey, should he go on? There was a stillness that was dreadful.... But he went on. The sound of his own footsteps scared and frightened him. How dark it was! The man must be hiding in some corner here. Ah! the flat was standing wide open, he hesitated and went in. It was very dark and empty in the passage, as though everything had been removed; he crept on tiptoe into the parlour which was flooded with moonlight. Everything there was as before, the chairs, the looking-glass, the yellow sofa and the pictures in the frames. A huge, round, copper-red moon looked in at the windows. “It’s the moon that makes it so still, weaving some mystery,” thought Raskolnikov. He stood and waited, waited a long while, and the more silent the moonlight, the more violently his heart beat, till it was painful. And still the same hush. Suddenly he heard a momentary sharp crack like the snapping of a splinter and all was still again. A fly flew up suddenly and struck the window pane with a plaintive buzz. At that moment he noticed in the corner between the window and the little cupboard something like a cloak hanging on the wall. “Why is that cloak here?” he thought, “it wasn’t there before....” He went up to it quietly and felt that there was someone hiding behind it. He cautiously moved the cloak and saw, sitting on a chair in the corner, the old woman bent double so that he couldn’t see her face; but it was she. He stood over her. “She is afraid,” he thought. He stealthily took the axe from the noose and struck her one blow, then another on the skull. But strange to say she did not stir, as though she were made of wood. He was frightened, bent down nearer and tried to look at her; but she, too, bent her head lower. He bent right down to the ground and peeped up into her face from below, he peeped and turned cold with horror: the old woman was sitting and laughing, shaking with noiseless laughter, doing her utmost that he should not hear it. Suddenly he fancied that the door from the bedroom was opened a little and that there was laughter and whispering within. He was overcome with frenzy and he began hitting the old woman on the head with all his force, but at every blow of the axe the laughter and whispering from the bedroom grew louder and the old woman was simply shaking with mirth. He was rushing away, but the passage was full of people, the doors of the flats stood open and on the landing, on the stairs and everywhere below there were people, rows of heads, all looking, but huddled together in silence and expectation. Something gripped his heart, his legs were rooted to the spot, they would not move.... He tried to scream and woke up.\n\nHe drew a deep breath--but his dream seemed strangely to persist: his door was flung open and a man whom he had never seen stood in the doorway watching him intently.\n\nRaskolnikov had hardly opened his eyes and he instantly closed them again. He lay on his back without stirring.\n\n“Is it still a dream?” he wondered and again raised his eyelids hardly perceptibly; the stranger was standing in the same place, still watching him.\n\nHe stepped cautiously into the room, carefully closing the door after him, went up to the table, paused a moment, still keeping his eyes on Raskolnikov, and noiselessly seated himself on the chair by the sofa; he put his hat on the floor beside him and leaned his hands on his cane and his chin on his hands. It was evident that he was prepared to wait indefinitely. As far as Raskolnikov could make out from his stolen glances, he was a man no longer young, stout, with a full, fair, almost whitish beard.\n\nTen minutes passed. It was still light, but beginning to get dusk. There was complete stillness in the room. Not a sound came from the stairs. Only a big fly buzzed and fluttered against the window pane. It was unbearable at last. Raskolnikov suddenly got up and sat on the sofa.\n\n“Come, tell me what you want.”\n\n“I knew you were not asleep, but only pretending,” the stranger answered oddly, laughing calmly. “Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov, allow me to introduce myself....”\n\n“Can this be still a dream?” Raskolnikov thought once more.\n\nHe looked carefully and suspiciously at the unexpected visitor.\n\n“Svidrigaïlov! What nonsense! It can’t be!” he said at last aloud in bewilderment.\n\nHis visitor did not seem at all surprised at this exclamation.\n\n“I’ve come to you for two reasons. In the first place, I wanted to make your personal acquaintance, as I have already heard a great deal about you that is interesting and flattering; secondly, I cherish the hope that you may not refuse to assist me in a matter directly concerning the welfare of your sister, Avdotya Romanovna. For without your support she might not let me come near her now, for she is prejudiced against me, but with your assistance I reckon on...”\n\n“You reckon wrongly,” interrupted Raskolnikov.\n\n“They only arrived yesterday, may I ask you?”\n\nRaskolnikov made no reply.\n\n“It was yesterday, I know. I only arrived myself the day before. Well, let me tell you this, Rodion Romanovitch, I don’t consider it necessary to justify myself, but kindly tell me what was there particularly criminal on my part in all this business, speaking without prejudice, with common sense?”\n\nRaskolnikov continued to look at him in silence.\n\n“That in my own house I persecuted a defenceless girl and ‘insulted her with my infamous proposals’--is that it? (I am anticipating you.) But you’ve only to assume that I, too, am a man _et nihil humanum_... in a word, that I am capable of being attracted and falling in love (which does not depend on our will), then everything can be explained in the most natural manner. The question is, am I a monster, or am I myself a victim? And what if I am a victim? In proposing to the object of my passion to elope with me to America or Switzerland, I may have cherished the deepest respect for her and may have thought that I was promoting our mutual happiness! Reason is the slave of passion, you know; why, probably, I was doing more harm to myself than anyone!”\n\n“But that’s not the point,” Raskolnikov interrupted with disgust. “It’s simply that whether you are right or wrong, we dislike you. We don’t want to have anything to do with you. We show you the door. Go out!”\n\nSvidrigaïlov broke into a sudden laugh.\n\n“But you’re... but there’s no getting round you,” he said, laughing in the frankest way. “I hoped to get round you, but you took up the right line at once!”\n\n“But you are trying to get round me still!”\n\n“What of it? What of it?” cried Svidrigaïlov, laughing openly. “But this is what the French call _bonne guerre_, and the most innocent form of deception!... But still you have interrupted me; one way or another, I repeat again: there would never have been any unpleasantness except for what happened in the garden. Marfa Petrovna...”\n\n“You have got rid of Marfa Petrovna, too, so they say?” Raskolnikov interrupted rudely.\n\n“Oh, you’ve heard that, too, then? You’d be sure to, though.... But as for your question, I really don’t know what to say, though my own conscience is quite at rest on that score. Don’t suppose that I am in any apprehension about it. All was regular and in order; the medical inquiry diagnosed apoplexy due to bathing immediately after a heavy dinner and a bottle of wine, and indeed it could have proved nothing else. But I’ll tell you what I have been thinking to myself of late, on my way here in the train, especially: didn’t I contribute to all that... calamity, morally, in a way, by irritation or something of the sort. But I came to the conclusion that that, too, was quite out of the question.”\n\nRaskolnikov laughed.\n\n“I wonder you trouble yourself about it!”\n\n“But what are you laughing at? Only consider, I struck her just twice with a switch--there were no marks even... don’t regard me as a cynic, please; I am perfectly aware how atrocious it was of me and all that; but I know for certain, too, that Marfa Petrovna was very likely pleased at my, so to say, warmth. The story of your sister had been wrung out to the last drop; for the last three days Marfa Petrovna had been forced to sit at home; she had nothing to show herself with in the town. Besides, she had bored them so with that letter (you heard about her reading the letter). And all of a sudden those two switches fell from heaven! Her first act was to order the carriage to be got out.... Not to speak of the fact that there are cases when women are very, very glad to be insulted in spite of all their show of indignation. There are instances of it with everyone; human beings in general, indeed, greatly love to be insulted, have you noticed that? But it’s particularly so with women. One might even say it’s their only amusement.”\n\nAt one time Raskolnikov thought of getting up and walking out and so finishing the interview. But some curiosity and even a sort of prudence made him linger for a moment.\n\n“You are fond of fighting?” he asked carelessly.\n\n“No, not very,” Svidrigaïlov answered, calmly. “And Marfa Petrovna and I scarcely ever fought. We lived very harmoniously, and she was always pleased with me. I only used the whip twice in all our seven years (not counting a third occasion of a very ambiguous character). The first time, two months after our marriage, immediately after we arrived in the country, and the last time was that of which we are speaking. Did you suppose I was such a monster, such a reactionary, such a slave driver? Ha, ha! By the way, do you remember, Rodion Romanovitch, how a few years ago, in those days of beneficent publicity, a nobleman, I’ve forgotten his name, was put to shame everywhere, in all the papers, for having thrashed a German woman in the railway train. You remember? It was in those days, that very year I believe, the ‘disgraceful action of the _Age_’ took place (you know, ‘The Egyptian Nights,’ that public reading, you remember? The dark eyes, you know! Ah, the golden days of our youth, where are they?). Well, as for the gentleman who thrashed the German, I feel no sympathy with him, because after all what need is there for sympathy? But I must say that there are sometimes such provoking ‘Germans’ that I don’t believe there is a progressive who could quite answer for himself. No one looked at the subject from that point of view then, but that’s the truly humane point of view, I assure you.”\n\nAfter saying this, Svidrigaïlov broke into a sudden laugh again. Raskolnikov saw clearly that this was a man with a firm purpose in his mind and able to keep it to himself.\n\n“I expect you’ve not talked to anyone for some days?” he asked.\n\n“Scarcely anyone. I suppose you are wondering at my being such an adaptable man?”\n\n“No, I am only wondering at your being too adaptable a man.”\n\n“Because I am not offended at the rudeness of your questions? Is that it? But why take offence? As you asked, so I answered,” he replied, with a surprising expression of simplicity. “You know, there’s hardly anything I take interest in,” he went on, as it were dreamily, “especially now, I’ve nothing to do.... You are quite at liberty to imagine though that I am making up to you with a motive, particularly as I told you I want to see your sister about something. But I’ll confess frankly, I am very much bored. The last three days especially, so I am delighted to see you.... Don’t be angry, Rodion Romanovitch, but you seem to be somehow awfully strange yourself. Say what you like, there’s something wrong with you, and now, too... not this very minute, I mean, but now, generally.... Well, well, I won’t, I won’t, don’t scowl! I am not such a bear, you know, as you think.”\n\nRaskolnikov looked gloomily at him.\n\n“You are not a bear, perhaps, at all,” he said. “I fancy indeed that you are a man of very good breeding, or at least know how on occasion to behave like one.”\n\n“I am not particularly interested in anyone’s opinion,” Svidrigaïlov answered, dryly and even with a shade of haughtiness, “and therefore why not be vulgar at times when vulgarity is such a convenient cloak for our climate... and especially if one has a natural propensity that way,” he added, laughing again.\n\n“But I’ve heard you have many friends here. You are, as they say, ‘not without connections.’ What can you want with me, then, unless you’ve some special object?”\n\n“That’s true that I have friends here,” Svidrigaïlov admitted, not replying to the chief point. “I’ve met some already. I’ve been lounging about for the last three days, and I’ve seen them, or they’ve seen me. That’s a matter of course. I am well dressed and reckoned not a poor man; the emancipation of the serfs hasn’t affected me; my property consists chiefly of forests and water meadows. The revenue has not fallen off; but... I am not going to see them, I was sick of them long ago. I’ve been here three days and have called on no one.... What a town it is! How has it come into existence among us, tell me that? A town of officials and students of all sorts. Yes, there’s a great deal I didn’t notice when I was here eight years ago, kicking up my heels.... My only hope now is in anatomy, by Jove, it is!”\n\n“Anatomy?”\n\n“But as for these clubs, Dussauts, parades, or progress, indeed, maybe--well, all that can go on without me,” he went on, again without noticing the question. “Besides, who wants to be a card-sharper?”\n\n“Why, have you been a card-sharper then?”\n\n“How could I help being? There was a regular set of us, men of the best society, eight years ago; we had a fine time. And all men of breeding, you know, poets, men of property. And indeed as a rule in our Russian society the best manners are found among those who’ve been thrashed, have you noticed that? I’ve deteriorated in the country. But I did get into prison for debt, through a low Greek who came from Nezhin. Then Marfa Petrovna turned up; she bargained with him and bought me off for thirty thousand silver pieces (I owed seventy thousand). We were united in lawful wedlock and she bore me off into the country like a treasure. You know she was five years older than I. She was very fond of me. For seven years I never left the country. And, take note, that all my life she held a document over me, the IOU for thirty thousand roubles, so if I were to elect to be restive about anything I should be trapped at once! And she would have done it! Women find nothing incompatible in that.”\n\n“If it hadn’t been for that, would you have given her the slip?”\n\n“I don’t know what to say. It was scarcely the document restrained me. I didn’t want to go anywhere else. Marfa Petrovna herself invited me to go abroad, seeing I was bored, but I’ve been abroad before, and always felt sick there. For no reason, but the sunrise, the bay of Naples, the sea--you look at them and it makes you sad. What’s most revolting is that one is really sad! No, it’s better at home. Here at least one blames others for everything and excuses oneself. I should have gone perhaps on an expedition to the North Pole, because _j’ai le vin mauvais_ and hate drinking, and there’s nothing left but wine. I have tried it. But, I say, I’ve been told Berg is going up in a great balloon next Sunday from the Yusupov Garden and will take up passengers at a fee. Is it true?”\n\n“Why, would you go up?”\n“I... No, oh, no,” muttered Svidrigaïlov really seeming to be deep in thought.\n\n“What does he mean? Is he in earnest?” Raskolnikov wondered.\n\n“No, the document didn’t restrain me,” Svidrigaïlov went on, meditatively. “It was my own doing, not leaving the country, and nearly a year ago Marfa Petrovna gave me back the document on my name-day and made me a present of a considerable sum of money, too. She had a fortune, you know. ‘You see how I trust you, Arkady Ivanovitch’--that was actually her expression. You don’t believe she used it? But do you know I managed the estate quite decently, they know me in the neighbourhood. I ordered books, too. Marfa Petrovna at first approved, but afterwards she was afraid of my over-studying.”\n\n“You seem to be missing Marfa Petrovna very much?”\n\n“Missing her? Perhaps. Really, perhaps I am. And, by the way, do you believe in ghosts?”\n\n“What ghosts?”\n\n“Why, ordinary ghosts.”\n\n“Do you believe in them?”\n\n“Perhaps not, _pour vous plaire_.... I wouldn’t say no exactly.”\n\n“Do you see them, then?”\n\nSvidrigaïlov looked at him rather oddly.\n\n“Marfa Petrovna is pleased to visit me,” he said, twisting his mouth into a strange smile.\n\n“How do you mean ‘she is pleased to visit you’?”\n\n“She has been three times. I saw her first on the very day of the funeral, an hour after she was buried. It was the day before I left to come here. The second time was the day before yesterday, at daybreak, on the journey at the station of Malaya Vishera, and the third time was two hours ago in the room where I am staying. I was alone.”\n\n“Were you awake?”\n\n“Quite awake. I was wide awake every time. She comes, speaks to me for a minute and goes out at the door--always at the door. I can almost hear her.”\n\n“What made me think that something of the sort must be happening to you?” Raskolnikov said suddenly.\n\nAt the same moment he was surprised at having said it. He was much excited.\n\n“What! Did you think so?” Svidrigaïlov asked in astonishment. “Did you really? Didn’t I say that there was something in common between us, eh?”\n\n“You never said so!” Raskolnikov cried sharply and with heat.\n\n“Didn’t I?”\n\n“No!”\n“I thought I did. When I came in and saw you lying with your eyes shut, pretending, I said to myself at once, ‘Here’s the man.’”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘the man?’ What are you talking about?” cried Raskolnikov.\n\n“What do I mean? I really don’t know....” Svidrigaïlov muttered ingenuously, as though he, too, were puzzled.\n\nFor a minute they were silent. They stared in each other’s faces.\n\n“That’s all nonsense!” Raskolnikov shouted with vexation. “What does she say when she comes to you?”\n\n“She! Would you believe it, she talks of the silliest trifles and--man is a strange creature--it makes me angry. The first time she came in (I was tired you know: the funeral service, the funeral ceremony, the lunch afterwards. At last I was left alone in my study. I lighted a cigar and began to think), she came in at the door. ‘You’ve been so busy to-day, Arkady Ivanovitch, you have forgotten to wind the dining-room clock,’ she said. All those seven years I’ve wound that clock every week, and if I forgot it she would always remind me. The next day I set off on my way here. I got out at the station at daybreak; I’d been asleep, tired out, with my eyes half open, I was drinking some coffee. I looked up and there was suddenly Marfa Petrovna sitting beside me with a pack of cards in her hands. ‘Shall I tell your fortune for the journey, Arkady Ivanovitch?’ She was a great hand at telling fortunes. I shall never forgive myself for not asking her to. I ran away in a fright, and, besides, the bell rang. I was sitting to-day, feeling very heavy after a miserable dinner from a cookshop; I was sitting smoking, all of a sudden Marfa Petrovna again. She came in very smart in a new green silk dress with a long train. ‘Good day, Arkady Ivanovitch! How do you like my dress? Aniska can’t make like this.’ (Aniska was a dressmaker in the country, one of our former serf girls who had been trained in Moscow, a pretty wench.) She stood turning round before me. I looked at the dress, and then I looked carefully, very carefully, at her face. ‘I wonder you trouble to come to me about such trifles, Marfa Petrovna.’ ‘Good gracious, you won’t let one disturb you about anything!’ To tease her I said, ‘I want to get married, Marfa Petrovna.’ ‘That’s just like you, Arkady Ivanovitch; it does you very little credit to come looking for a bride when you’ve hardly buried your wife. And if you could make a good choice, at least, but I know it won’t be for your happiness or hers, you will only be a laughing-stock to all good people.’ Then she went out and her train seemed to rustle. Isn’t it nonsense, eh?”\n\n“But perhaps you are telling lies?” Raskolnikov put in.\n\n“I rarely lie,” answered Svidrigaïlov thoughtfully, apparently not noticing the rudeness of the question.\n\n“And in the past, have you ever seen ghosts before?”\n\n“Y-yes, I have seen them, but only once in my life, six years ago. I had a serf, Filka; just after his burial I called out forgetting ‘Filka, my pipe!’ He came in and went to the cupboard where my pipes were. I sat still and thought ‘he is doing it out of revenge,’ because we had a violent quarrel just before his death. ‘How dare you come in with a hole in your elbow?’ I said. ‘Go away, you scamp!’ He turned and went out, and never came again. I didn’t tell Marfa Petrovna at the time. I wanted to have a service sung for him, but I was ashamed.”\n\n“You should go to a doctor.”\n\n“I know I am not well, without your telling me, though I don’t know what’s wrong; I believe I am five times as strong as you are. I didn’t ask you whether you believe that ghosts are seen, but whether you believe that they exist.”\n\n“No, I won’t believe it!” Raskolnikov cried, with positive anger.\n\n“What do people generally say?” muttered Svidrigaïlov, as though speaking to himself, looking aside and bowing his head. “They say, ‘You are ill, so what appears to you is only unreal fantasy.’ But that’s not strictly logical. I agree that ghosts only appear to the sick, but that only proves that they are unable to appear except to the sick, not that they don’t exist.”\n\n“Nothing of the sort,” Raskolnikov insisted irritably.\n\n“No? You don’t think so?” Svidrigaïlov went on, looking at him deliberately. “But what do you say to this argument (help me with it): ghosts are, as it were, shreds and fragments of other worlds, the beginning of them. A man in health has, of course, no reason to see them, because he is above all a man of this earth and is bound for the sake of completeness and order to live only in this life. But as soon as one is ill, as soon as the normal earthly order of the organism is broken, one begins to realise the possibility of another world; and the more seriously ill one is, the closer becomes one’s contact with that other world, so that as soon as the man dies he steps straight into that world. I thought of that long ago. If you believe in a future life, you could believe in that, too.”\n\n“I don’t believe in a future life,” said Raskolnikov.\n\nSvidrigaïlov sat lost in thought.\n\n“And what if there are only spiders there, or something of that sort,” he said suddenly.\n\n“He is a madman,” thought Raskolnikov.\n\n“We always imagine eternity as something beyond our conception, something vast, vast! But why must it be vast? Instead of all that, what if it’s one little room, like a bath house in the country, black and grimy and spiders in every corner, and that’s all eternity is? I sometimes fancy it like that.”\n\n“Can it be you can imagine nothing juster and more comforting than that?” Raskolnikov cried, with a feeling of anguish.\n\n“Juster? And how can we tell, perhaps that is just, and do you know it’s what I would certainly have made it,” answered Svidrigaïlov, with a vague smile.\n\nThis horrible answer sent a cold chill through Raskolnikov. Svidrigaïlov raised his head, looked at him, and suddenly began laughing.\n\n“Only think,” he cried, “half an hour ago we had never seen each other, we regarded each other as enemies; there is a matter unsettled between us; we’ve thrown it aside, and away we’ve gone into the abstract! Wasn’t I right in saying that we were birds of a feather?”\n\n“Kindly allow me,” Raskolnikov went on irritably, “to ask you to explain why you have honoured me with your visit... and... and I am in a hurry, I have no time to waste. I want to go out.”\n\n“By all means, by all means. Your sister, Avdotya Romanovna, is going to be married to Mr. Luzhin, Pyotr Petrovitch?”\n\n“Can you refrain from any question about my sister and from mentioning her name? I can’t understand how you dare utter her name in my presence, if you really are Svidrigaïlov.”\n\n“Why, but I’ve come here to speak about her; how can I avoid mentioning her?”\n\n“Very good, speak, but make haste.”\n\n“I am sure that you must have formed your own opinion of this Mr. Luzhin, who is a connection of mine through my wife, if you have only seen him for half an hour, or heard any facts about him. He is no match for Avdotya Romanovna. I believe Avdotya Romanovna is sacrificing herself generously and imprudently for the sake of... for the sake of her family. I fancied from all I had heard of you that you would be very glad if the match could be broken off without the sacrifice of worldly advantages. Now I know you personally, I am convinced of it.”\n\n“All this is very naïve... excuse me, I should have said impudent on your part,” said Raskolnikov.\n\n“You mean to say that I am seeking my own ends. Don’t be uneasy, Rodion Romanovitch, if I were working for my own advantage, I would not have spoken out so directly. I am not quite a fool. I will confess something psychologically curious about that: just now, defending my love for Avdotya Romanovna, I said I was myself the victim. Well, let me tell you that I’ve no feeling of love now, not the slightest, so that I wonder myself indeed, for I really did feel something...”\n\n“Through idleness and depravity,” Raskolnikov put in.\n\n“I certainly am idle and depraved, but your sister has such qualities that even I could not help being impressed by them. But that’s all nonsense, as I see myself now.”\n\n“Have you seen that long?”\n\n“I began to be aware of it before, but was only perfectly sure of it the day before yesterday, almost at the moment I arrived in Petersburg. I still fancied in Moscow, though, that I was coming to try to get Avdotya Romanovna’s hand and to cut out Mr. Luzhin.”\n\n“Excuse me for interrupting you; kindly be brief, and come to the object of your visit. I am in a hurry, I want to go out...”\n\n“With the greatest pleasure. On arriving here and determining on a certain... journey, I should like to make some necessary preliminary arrangements. I left my children with an aunt; they are well provided for; and they have no need of me personally. And a nice father I should make, too! I have taken nothing but what Marfa Petrovna gave me a year ago. That’s enough for me. Excuse me, I am just coming to the point. Before the journey which may come off, I want to settle Mr. Luzhin, too. It’s not that I detest him so much, but it was through him I quarrelled with Marfa Petrovna when I learned that she had dished up this marriage. I want now to see Avdotya Romanovna through your mediation, and if you like in your presence, to explain to her that in the first place she will never gain anything but harm from Mr. Luzhin. Then, begging her pardon for all past unpleasantness, to make her a present of ten thousand roubles and so assist the rupture with Mr. Luzhin, a rupture to which I believe she is herself not disinclined, if she could see the way to it.”\n\n“You are certainly mad,” cried Raskolnikov not so much angered as astonished. “How dare you talk like that!”\n\n“I knew you would scream at me; but in the first place, though I am not rich, this ten thousand roubles is perfectly free; I have absolutely no need for it. If Avdotya Romanovna does not accept it, I shall waste it in some more foolish way. That’s the first thing. Secondly, my conscience is perfectly easy; I make the offer with no ulterior motive. You may not believe it, but in the end Avdotya Romanovna and you will know. The point is, that I did actually cause your sister, whom I greatly respect, some trouble and unpleasantness, and so, sincerely regretting it, I want--not to compensate, not to repay her for the unpleasantness, but simply to do something to her advantage, to show that I am not, after all, privileged to do nothing but harm. If there were a millionth fraction of self-interest in my offer, I should not have made it so openly; and I should not have offered her ten thousand only, when five weeks ago I offered her more, Besides, I may, perhaps, very soon marry a young lady, and that alone ought to prevent suspicion of any design on Avdotya Romanovna. In conclusion, let me say that in marrying Mr. Luzhin, she is taking money just the same, only from another man. Don’t be angry, Rodion Romanovitch, think it over coolly and quietly.”\n\nSvidrigaïlov himself was exceedingly cool and quiet as he was saying this.\n\n“I beg you to say no more,” said Raskolnikov. “In any case this is unpardonable impertinence.”\n\n“Not in the least. Then a man may do nothing but harm to his neighbour in this world, and is prevented from doing the tiniest bit of good by trivial conventional formalities. That’s absurd. If I died, for instance, and left that sum to your sister in my will, surely she wouldn’t refuse it?”\n\n“Very likely she would.”\n\n“Oh, no, indeed. However, if you refuse it, so be it, though ten thousand roubles is a capital thing to have on occasion. In any case I beg you to repeat what I have said to Avdotya Romanovna.”\n\n“No, I won’t.”\n\n“In that case, Rodion Romanovitch, I shall be obliged to try and see her myself and worry her by doing so.”\n\n“And if I do tell her, will you not try to see her?”\n\n“I don’t know really what to say. I should like very much to see her once more.”\n\n“Don’t hope for it.”\n\n“I’m sorry. But you don’t know me. Perhaps we may become better friends.”\n\n“You think we may become friends?”\n\n“And why not?” Svidrigaïlov said, smiling. He stood up and took his hat. “I didn’t quite intend to disturb you and I came here without reckoning on it... though I was very much struck by your face this morning.”\n\n“Where did you see me this morning?” Raskolnikov asked uneasily.\n\n“I saw you by chance.... I kept fancying there is something about you like me.... But don’t be uneasy. I am not intrusive; I used to get on all right with card-sharpers, and I never bored Prince Svirbey, a great personage who is a distant relation of mine, and I could write about Raphael’s _Madonna_ in Madam Prilukov’s album, and I never left Marfa Petrovna’s side for seven years, and I used to stay the night at Viazemsky’s house in the Hay Market in the old days, and I may go up in a balloon with Berg, perhaps.”\n\n“Oh, all right. Are you starting soon on your travels, may I ask?”\n\n“What travels?”\n\n“Why, on that ‘journey’; you spoke of it yourself.”\n\n“A journey? Oh, yes. I did speak of a journey. Well, that’s a wide subject.... if only you knew what you are asking,” he added, and gave a sudden, loud, short laugh. “Perhaps I’ll get married instead of the journey. They’re making a match for me.”\n\n“Here?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“How have you had time for that?”\n\n“But I am very anxious to see Avdotya Romanovna once. I earnestly beg it. Well, good-bye for the present. Oh, yes. I have forgotten something. Tell your sister, Rodion Romanovitch, that Marfa Petrovna remembered her in her will and left her three thousand roubles. That’s absolutely certain. Marfa Petrovna arranged it a week before her death, and it was done in my presence. Avdotya Romanovna will be able to receive the money in two or three weeks.”\n\n“Are you telling the truth?”\n\n“Yes, tell her. Well, your servant. I am staying very near you.”\n\nAs he went out, Svidrigaïlov ran up against Razumihin in the doorway.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nIt was nearly eight o’clock. The two young men hurried to Bakaleyev’s, to arrive before Luzhin.\n\n“Why, who was that?” asked Razumihin, as soon as they were in the street.\n\n“It was Svidrigaïlov, that landowner in whose house my sister was insulted when she was their governess. Through his persecuting her with his attentions, she was turned out by his wife, Marfa Petrovna. This Marfa Petrovna begged Dounia’s forgiveness afterwards, and she’s just died suddenly. It was of her we were talking this morning. I don’t know why I’m afraid of that man. He came here at once after his wife’s funeral. He is very strange, and is determined on doing something.... We must guard Dounia from him... that’s what I wanted to tell you, do you hear?”\n\n“Guard her! What can he do to harm Avdotya Romanovna? Thank you, Rodya, for speaking to me like that.... We will, we will guard her. Where does he live?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Why didn’t you ask? What a pity! I’ll find out, though.”\n\n“Did you see him?” asked Raskolnikov after a pause.\n\n“Yes, I noticed him, I noticed him well.”\n\n“You did really see him? You saw him clearly?” Raskolnikov insisted.\n\n“Yes, I remember him perfectly, I should know him in a thousand; I have a good memory for faces.”\n\nThey were silent again.\n\n“Hm!... that’s all right,” muttered Raskolnikov. “Do you know, I fancied... I keep thinking that it may have been an hallucination.”\n\n“What do you mean? I don’t understand you.”\n\n“Well, you all say,” Raskolnikov went on, twisting his mouth into a smile, “that I am mad. I thought just now that perhaps I really am mad, and have only seen a phantom.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Why, who can tell? Perhaps I am really mad, and perhaps everything that happened all these days may be only imagination.”\n\n“Ach, Rodya, you have been upset again!... But what did he say, what did he come for?”\n\nRaskolnikov did not answer. Razumihin thought a minute.\n\n“Now let me tell you my story,” he began, “I came to you, you were asleep. Then we had dinner and then I went to Porfiry’s, Zametov was still with him. I tried to begin, but it was no use. I couldn’t speak in the right way. They don’t seem to understand and can’t understand, but are not a bit ashamed. I drew Porfiry to the window, and began talking to him, but it was still no use. He looked away and I looked away. At last I shook my fist in his ugly face, and told him as a cousin I’d brain him. He merely looked at me, I cursed and came away. That was all. It was very stupid. To Zametov I didn’t say a word. But, you see, I thought I’d made a mess of it, but as I went downstairs a brilliant idea struck me: why should we trouble? Of course if you were in any danger or anything, but why need you care? You needn’t care a hang for them. We shall have a laugh at them afterwards, and if I were in your place I’d mystify them more than ever. How ashamed they’ll be afterwards! Hang them! We can thrash them afterwards, but let’s laugh at them now!”\n\n“To be sure,” answered Raskolnikov. “But what will you say to-morrow?” he thought to himself. Strange to say, till that moment it had never occurred to him to wonder what Razumihin would think when he knew. As he thought it, Raskolnikov looked at him. Razumihin’s account of his visit to Porfiry had very little interest for him, so much had come and gone since then.\n\nIn the corridor they came upon Luzhin; he had arrived punctually at eight, and was looking for the number, so that all three went in together without greeting or looking at one another. The young men walked in first, while Pyotr Petrovitch, for good manners, lingered a little in the passage, taking off his coat. Pulcheria Alexandrovna came forward at once to greet him in the doorway, Dounia was welcoming her brother. Pyotr Petrovitch walked in and quite amiably, though with redoubled dignity, bowed to the ladies. He looked, however, as though he were a little put out and could not yet recover himself. Pulcheria Alexandrovna, who seemed also a little embarrassed, hastened to make them all sit down at the round table where a samovar was boiling. Dounia and Luzhin were facing one another on opposite sides of the table. Razumihin and Raskolnikov were facing Pulcheria Alexandrovna, Razumihin was next to Luzhin and Raskolnikov was beside his sister.\n\nA moment’s silence followed. Pyotr Petrovitch deliberately drew out a cambric handkerchief reeking of scent and blew his nose with an air of a benevolent man who felt himself slighted, and was firmly resolved to insist on an explanation. In the passage the idea had occurred to him to keep on his overcoat and walk away, and so give the two ladies a sharp and emphatic lesson and make them feel the gravity of the position. But he could not bring himself to do this. Besides, he could not endure uncertainty, and he wanted an explanation: if his request had been so openly disobeyed, there was something behind it, and in that case it was better to find it out beforehand; it rested with him to punish them and there would always be time for that.\n\n“I trust you had a favourable journey,” he inquired officially of Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“Oh, very, Pyotr Petrovitch.”\n\n“I am gratified to hear it. And Avdotya Romanovna is not over-fatigued either?”\n\n“I am young and strong, I don’t get tired, but it was a great strain for mother,” answered Dounia.\n\n“That’s unavoidable! our national railways are of terrible length. ‘Mother Russia,’ as they say, is a vast country.... In spite of all my desire to do so, I was unable to meet you yesterday. But I trust all passed off without inconvenience?”\n\n“Oh, no, Pyotr Petrovitch, it was all terribly disheartening,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna hastened to declare with peculiar intonation, “and if Dmitri Prokofitch had not been sent us, I really believe by God Himself, we should have been utterly lost. Here, he is! Dmitri Prokofitch Razumihin,” she added, introducing him to Luzhin.\n\n“I had the pleasure... yesterday,” muttered Pyotr Petrovitch with a hostile glance sidelong at Razumihin; then he scowled and was silent.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch belonged to that class of persons, on the surface very polite in society, who make a great point of punctiliousness, but who, directly they are crossed in anything, are completely disconcerted, and become more like sacks of flour than elegant and lively men of society. Again all was silent; Raskolnikov was obstinately mute, Avdotya Romanovna was unwilling to open the conversation too soon. Razumihin had nothing to say, so Pulcheria Alexandrovna was anxious again.\n\n“Marfa Petrovna is dead, have you heard?” she began having recourse to her leading item of conversation.\n\n“To be sure, I heard so. I was immediately informed, and I have come to make you acquainted with the fact that Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov set off in haste for Petersburg immediately after his wife’s funeral. So at least I have excellent authority for believing.”\n\n“To Petersburg? here?” Dounia asked in alarm and looked at her mother.\n\n“Yes, indeed, and doubtless not without some design, having in view the rapidity of his departure, and all the circumstances preceding it.”\n\n“Good heavens! won’t he leave Dounia in peace even here?” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\n“I imagine that neither you nor Avdotya Romanovna have any grounds for uneasiness, unless, of course, you are yourselves desirous of getting into communication with him. For my part I am on my guard, and am now discovering where he is lodging.”\n\n“Oh, Pyotr Petrovitch, you would not believe what a fright you have given me,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna went on: “I’ve only seen him twice, but I thought him terrible, terrible! I am convinced that he was the cause of Marfa Petrovna’s death.”\n\n“It’s impossible to be certain about that. I have precise information. I do not dispute that he may have contributed to accelerate the course of events by the moral influence, so to say, of the affront; but as to the general conduct and moral characteristics of that personage, I am in agreement with you. I do not know whether he is well off now, and precisely what Marfa Petrovna left him; this will be known to me within a very short period; but no doubt here in Petersburg, if he has any pecuniary resources, he will relapse at once into his old ways. He is the most depraved, and abjectly vicious specimen of that class of men. I have considerable reason to believe that Marfa Petrovna, who was so unfortunate as to fall in love with him and to pay his debts eight years ago, was of service to him also in another way. Solely by her exertions and sacrifices, a criminal charge, involving an element of fantastic and homicidal brutality for which he might well have been sentenced to Siberia, was hushed up. That’s the sort of man he is, if you care to know.”\n\n“Good heavens!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna. Raskolnikov listened attentively.\n\n“Are you speaking the truth when you say that you have good evidence of this?” Dounia asked sternly and emphatically.\n\n“I only repeat what I was told in secret by Marfa Petrovna. I must observe that from the legal point of view the case was far from clear. There was, and I believe still is, living here a woman called Resslich, a foreigner, who lent small sums of money at interest, and did other commissions, and with this woman Svidrigaïlov had for a long while close and mysterious relations. She had a relation, a niece I believe, living with her, a deaf and dumb girl of fifteen, or perhaps not more than fourteen. Resslich hated this girl, and grudged her every crust; she used to beat her mercilessly. One day the girl was found hanging in the garret. At the inquest the verdict was suicide. After the usual proceedings the matter ended, but, later on, information was given that the child had been... cruelly outraged by Svidrigaïlov. It is true, this was not clearly established, the information was given by another German woman of loose character whose word could not be trusted; no statement was actually made to the police, thanks to Marfa Petrovna’s money and exertions; it did not get beyond gossip. And yet the story is a very significant one. You heard, no doubt, Avdotya Romanovna, when you were with them the story of the servant Philip who died of ill treatment he received six years ago, before the abolition of serfdom.”\n\n“I heard, on the contrary, that this Philip hanged himself.”\n\n“Quite so, but what drove him, or rather perhaps disposed him, to suicide was the systematic persecution and severity of Mr. Svidrigaïlov.”\n\n“I don’t know that,” answered Dounia, dryly. “I only heard a queer story that Philip was a sort of hypochondriac, a sort of domestic philosopher, the servants used to say, ‘he read himself silly,’ and that he hanged himself partly on account of Mr. Svidrigaïlov’s mockery of him and not his blows. When I was there he behaved well to the servants, and they were actually fond of him, though they certainly did blame him for Philip’s death.”\n\n“I perceive, Avdotya Romanovna, that you seem disposed to undertake his defence all of a sudden,” Luzhin observed, twisting his lips into an ambiguous smile, “there’s no doubt that he is an astute man, and insinuating where ladies are concerned, of which Marfa Petrovna, who has died so strangely, is a terrible instance. My only desire has been to be of service to you and your mother with my advice, in view of the renewed efforts which may certainly be anticipated from him. For my part it’s my firm conviction, that he will end in a debtor’s prison again. Marfa Petrovna had not the slightest intention of settling anything substantial on him, having regard for his children’s interests, and, if she left him anything, it would only be the merest sufficiency, something insignificant and ephemeral, which would not last a year for a man of his habits.”\n\n“Pyotr Petrovitch, I beg you,” said Dounia, “say no more of Mr. Svidrigaïlov. It makes me miserable.”\n\n“He has just been to see me,” said Raskolnikov, breaking his silence for the first time.\n\nThere were exclamations from all, and they all turned to him. Even Pyotr Petrovitch was roused.\n\n“An hour and a half ago, he came in when I was asleep, waked me, and introduced himself,” Raskolnikov continued. “He was fairly cheerful and at ease, and quite hopes that we shall become friends. He is particularly anxious, by the way, Dounia, for an interview with you, at which he asked me to assist. He has a proposition to make to you, and he told me about it. He told me, too, that a week before her death Marfa Petrovna left you three thousand roubles in her will, Dounia, and that you can receive the money very shortly.”\n\n“Thank God!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna, crossing herself. “Pray for her soul, Dounia!”\n\n“It’s a fact!” broke from Luzhin.\n\n“Tell us, what more?” Dounia urged Raskolnikov.\n\n“Then he said that he wasn’t rich and all the estate was left to his children who are now with an aunt, then that he was staying somewhere not far from me, but where, I don’t know, I didn’t ask....”\n\n“But what, what does he want to propose to Dounia?” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna in a fright. “Did he tell you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What was it?”\n\n“I’ll tell you afterwards.”\n\nRaskolnikov ceased speaking and turned his attention to his tea.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch looked at his watch.\n\n“I am compelled to keep a business engagement, and so I shall not be in your way,” he added with an air of some pique and he began getting up.\n\n“Don’t go, Pyotr Petrovitch,” said Dounia, “you intended to spend the evening. Besides, you wrote yourself that you wanted to have an explanation with mother.”\n\n“Precisely so, Avdotya Romanovna,” Pyotr Petrovitch answered impressively, sitting down again, but still holding his hat. “I certainly desired an explanation with you and your honoured mother upon a very important point indeed. But as your brother cannot speak openly in my presence of some proposals of Mr. Svidrigaïlov, I, too, do not desire and am not able to speak openly... in the presence of others... of certain matters of the greatest gravity. Moreover, my most weighty and urgent request has been disregarded....”\n\nAssuming an aggrieved air, Luzhin relapsed into dignified silence.\n\n“Your request that my brother should not be present at our meeting was disregarded solely at my insistence,” said Dounia. “You wrote that you had been insulted by my brother; I think that this must be explained at once, and you must be reconciled. And if Rodya really has insulted you, then he _should_ and _will_ apologise.”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch took a stronger line.\n\n“There are insults, Avdotya Romanovna, which no goodwill can make us forget. There is a line in everything which it is dangerous to overstep; and when it has been overstepped, there is no return.”\n\n“That wasn’t what I was speaking of exactly, Pyotr Petrovitch,” Dounia interrupted with some impatience. “Please understand that our whole future depends now on whether all this is explained and set right as soon as possible. I tell you frankly at the start that I cannot look at it in any other light, and if you have the least regard for me, all this business must be ended to-day, however hard that may be. I repeat that if my brother is to blame he will ask your forgiveness.”\n\n“I am surprised at your putting the question like that,” said Luzhin, getting more and more irritated. “Esteeming, and so to say, adoring you, I may at the same time, very well indeed, be able to dislike some member of your family. Though I lay claim to the happiness of your hand, I cannot accept duties incompatible with...”\n\n“Ah, don’t be so ready to take offence, Pyotr Petrovitch,” Dounia interrupted with feeling, “and be the sensible and generous man I have always considered, and wish to consider, you to be. I’ve given you a great promise, I am your betrothed. Trust me in this matter and, believe me, I shall be capable of judging impartially. My assuming the part of judge is as much a surprise for my brother as for you. When I insisted on his coming to our interview to-day after your letter, I told him nothing of what I meant to do. Understand that, if you are not reconciled, I must choose between you--it must be either you or he. That is how the question rests on your side and on his. I don’t want to be mistaken in my choice, and I must not be. For your sake I must break off with my brother, for my brother’s sake I must break off with you. I can find out for certain now whether he is a brother to me, and I want to know it; and of you, whether I am dear to you, whether you esteem me, whether you are the husband for me.”\n\n“Avdotya Romanovna,” Luzhin declared huffily, “your words are of too much consequence to me; I will say more, they are offensive in view of the position I have the honour to occupy in relation to you. To say nothing of your strange and offensive setting me on a level with an impertinent boy, you admit the possibility of breaking your promise to me. You say ‘you or he,’ showing thereby of how little consequence I am in your eyes... I cannot let this pass considering the relationship and... the obligations existing between us.”\n\n“What!” cried Dounia, flushing. “I set your interest beside all that has hitherto been most precious in my life, what has made up the _whole_ of my life, and here you are offended at my making too _little_ account of you.”\n\nRaskolnikov smiled sarcastically, Razumihin fidgeted, but Pyotr Petrovitch did not accept the reproof; on the contrary, at every word he became more persistent and irritable, as though he relished it.\n\n“Love for the future partner of your life, for your husband, ought to outweigh your love for your brother,” he pronounced sententiously, “and in any case I cannot be put on the same level.... Although I said so emphatically that I would not speak openly in your brother’s presence, nevertheless, I intend now to ask your honoured mother for a necessary explanation on a point of great importance closely affecting my dignity. Your son,” he turned to Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “yesterday in the presence of Mr. Razsudkin (or... I think that’s it? excuse me, I have forgotten your surname”) (he bowed politely to Razumihin), “insulted me by misrepresenting the idea I expressed to you in a private conversation, drinking coffee, that is, that marriage with a poor girl who has had experience of trouble is more advantageous from the conjugal point of view than with one who has lived in luxury, since it is more profitable for the moral character. Your son intentionally exaggerated the significance of my words and made them ridiculous, accusing me of malicious intentions, and, as far as I could see, relied upon your correspondence with him. I shall consider myself happy, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, if it is possible for you to convince me of an opposite conclusion, and thereby considerately reassure me. Kindly let me know in what terms precisely you repeated my words in your letter to Rodion Romanovitch.”\n\n“I don’t remember,” faltered Pulcheria Alexandrovna. “I repeated them as I understood them. I don’t know how Rodya repeated them to you, perhaps he exaggerated.”\n\n“He could not have exaggerated them, except at your instigation.”\n\n“Pyotr Petrovitch,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna declared with dignity, “the proof that Dounia and I did not take your words in a very bad sense is the fact that we are here.”\n\n“Good, mother,” said Dounia approvingly.\n\n“Then this is my fault again,” said Luzhin, aggrieved.\n\n“Well, Pyotr Petrovitch, you keep blaming Rodion, but you yourself have just written what was false about him,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna added, gaining courage.\n\n“I don’t remember writing anything false.”\n\n“You wrote,” Raskolnikov said sharply, not turning to Luzhin, “that I gave money yesterday not to the widow of the man who was killed, as was the fact, but to his daughter (whom I had never seen till yesterday). You wrote this to make dissension between me and my family, and for that object added coarse expressions about the conduct of a girl whom you don’t know. All that is mean slander.”\n\n“Excuse me, sir,” said Luzhin, quivering with fury. “I enlarged upon your qualities and conduct in my letter solely in response to your sister’s and mother’s inquiries, how I found you, and what impression you made on me. As for what you’ve alluded to in my letter, be so good as to point out one word of falsehood, show, that is, that you didn’t throw away your money, and that there are not worthless persons in that family, however unfortunate.”\n\n“To my thinking, you, with all your virtues, are not worth the little finger of that unfortunate girl at whom you throw stones.”\n\n“Would you go so far then as to let her associate with your mother and sister?”\n\n“I have done so already, if you care to know. I made her sit down to-day with mother and Dounia.”\n\n“Rodya!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna. Dounia crimsoned, Razumihin knitted his brows. Luzhin smiled with lofty sarcasm.\n\n“You may see for yourself, Avdotya Romanovna,” he said, “whether it is possible for us to agree. I hope now that this question is at an end, once and for all. I will withdraw, that I may not hinder the pleasures of family intimacy, and the discussion of secrets.” He got up from his chair and took his hat. “But in withdrawing, I venture to request that for the future I may be spared similar meetings, and, so to say, compromises. I appeal particularly to you, honoured Pulcheria Alexandrovna, on this subject, the more as my letter was addressed to you and to no one else.”\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna was a little offended.\n\n“You seem to think we are completely under your authority, Pyotr Petrovitch. Dounia has told you the reason your desire was disregarded, she had the best intentions. And indeed you write as though you were laying commands upon me. Are we to consider every desire of yours as a command? Let me tell you on the contrary that you ought to show particular delicacy and consideration for us now, because we have thrown up everything, and have come here relying on you, and so we are in any case in a sense in your hands.”\n\n“That is not quite true, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, especially at the present moment, when the news has come of Marfa Petrovna’s legacy, which seems indeed very apropos, judging from the new tone you take to me,” he added sarcastically.\n\n“Judging from that remark, we may certainly presume that you were reckoning on our helplessness,” Dounia observed irritably.\n\n“But now in any case I cannot reckon on it, and I particularly desire not to hinder your discussion of the secret proposals of Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov, which he has entrusted to your brother and which have, I perceive, a great and possibly a very agreeable interest for you.”\n\n“Good heavens!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\nRazumihin could not sit still on his chair.\n\n“Aren’t you ashamed now, sister?” asked Raskolnikov.\n\n“I am ashamed, Rodya,” said Dounia. “Pyotr Petrovitch, go away,” she turned to him, white with anger.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch had apparently not at all expected such a conclusion. He had too much confidence in himself, in his power and in the helplessness of his victims. He could not believe it even now. He turned pale, and his lips quivered.\n\n“Avdotya Romanovna, if I go out of this door now, after such a dismissal, then, you may reckon on it, I will never come back. Consider what you are doing. My word is not to be shaken.”\n\n“What insolence!” cried Dounia, springing up from her seat. “I don’t want you to come back again.”\n\n“What! So that’s how it stands!” cried Luzhin, utterly unable to the last moment to believe in the rupture and so completely thrown out of his reckoning now. “So that’s how it stands! But do you know, Avdotya Romanovna, that I might protest?”\n\n“What right have you to speak to her like that?” Pulcheria Alexandrovna intervened hotly. “And what can you protest about? What rights have you? Am I to give my Dounia to a man like you? Go away, leave us altogether! We are to blame for having agreed to a wrong action, and I above all....”\n\n“But you have bound me, Pulcheria Alexandrovna,” Luzhin stormed in a frenzy, “by your promise, and now you deny it and... besides... I have been led on account of that into expenses....”\n\nThis last complaint was so characteristic of Pyotr Petrovitch, that Raskolnikov, pale with anger and with the effort of restraining it, could not help breaking into laughter. But Pulcheria Alexandrovna was furious.\n\n“Expenses? What expenses? Are you speaking of our trunk? But the conductor brought it for nothing for you. Mercy on us, we have bound you! What are you thinking about, Pyotr Petrovitch, it was you bound us, hand and foot, not we!”\n\n“Enough, mother, no more please,” Avdotya Romanovna implored. “Pyotr Petrovitch, do be kind and go!”\n\n“I am going, but one last word,” he said, quite unable to control himself. “Your mamma seems to have entirely forgotten that I made up my mind to take you, so to speak, after the gossip of the town had spread all over the district in regard to your reputation. Disregarding public opinion for your sake and reinstating your reputation, I certainly might very well reckon on a fitting return, and might indeed look for gratitude on your part. And my eyes have only now been opened! I see myself that I may have acted very, very recklessly in disregarding the universal verdict....”\n\n“Does the fellow want his head smashed?” cried Razumihin, jumping up.\n\n“You are a mean and spiteful man!” cried Dounia.\n\n“Not a word! Not a movement!” cried Raskolnikov, holding Razumihin back; then going close up to Luzhin, “Kindly leave the room!” he said quietly and distinctly, “and not a word more or...”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch gazed at him for some seconds with a pale face that worked with anger, then he turned, went out, and rarely has any man carried away in his heart such vindictive hatred as he felt against Raskolnikov. Him, and him alone, he blamed for everything. It is noteworthy that as he went downstairs he still imagined that his case was perhaps not utterly lost, and that, so far as the ladies were concerned, all might “very well indeed” be set right again.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe fact was that up to the last moment he had never expected such an ending; he had been overbearing to the last degree, never dreaming that two destitute and defenceless women could escape from his control. This conviction was strengthened by his vanity and conceit, a conceit to the point of fatuity. Pyotr Petrovitch, who had made his way up from insignificance, was morbidly given to self-admiration, had the highest opinion of his intelligence and capacities, and sometimes even gloated in solitude over his image in the glass. But what he loved and valued above all was the money he had amassed by his labour, and by all sorts of devices: that money made him the equal of all who had been his superiors.\n\nWhen he had bitterly reminded Dounia that he had decided to take her in spite of evil report, Pyotr Petrovitch had spoken with perfect sincerity and had, indeed, felt genuinely indignant at such “black ingratitude.” And yet, when he made Dounia his offer, he was fully aware of the groundlessness of all the gossip. The story had been everywhere contradicted by Marfa Petrovna, and was by then disbelieved by all the townspeople, who were warm in Dounia’s defence. And he would not have denied that he knew all that at the time. Yet he still thought highly of his own resolution in lifting Dounia to his level and regarded it as something heroic. In speaking of it to Dounia, he had let out the secret feeling he cherished and admired, and he could not understand that others should fail to admire it too. He had called on Raskolnikov with the feelings of a benefactor who is about to reap the fruits of his good deeds and to hear agreeable flattery. And as he went downstairs now, he considered himself most undeservedly injured and unrecognised.\n\nDounia was simply essential to him; to do without her was unthinkable. For many years he had had voluptuous dreams of marriage, but he had gone on waiting and amassing money. He brooded with relish, in profound secret, over the image of a girl--virtuous, poor (she must be poor), very young, very pretty, of good birth and education, very timid, one who had suffered much, and was completely humbled before him, one who would all her life look on him as her saviour, worship him, admire him and only him. How many scenes, how many amorous episodes he had imagined on this seductive and playful theme, when his work was over! And, behold, the dream of so many years was all but realised; the beauty and education of Avdotya Romanovna had impressed him; her helpless position had been a great allurement; in her he had found even more than he dreamed of. Here was a girl of pride, character, virtue, of education and breeding superior to his own (he felt that), and this creature would be slavishly grateful all her life for his heroic condescension, and would humble herself in the dust before him, and he would have absolute, unbounded power over her!... Not long before, he had, too, after long reflection and hesitation, made an important change in his career and was now entering on a wider circle of business. With this change his cherished dreams of rising into a higher class of society seemed likely to be realised.... He was, in fact, determined to try his fortune in Petersburg. He knew that women could do a very great deal. The fascination of a charming, virtuous, highly educated woman might make his way easier, might do wonders in attracting people to him, throwing an aureole round him, and now everything was in ruins! This sudden horrible rupture affected him like a clap of thunder; it was like a hideous joke, an absurdity. He had only been a tiny bit masterful, had not even time to speak out, had simply made a joke, been carried away--and it had ended so seriously. And, of course, too, he did love Dounia in his own way; he already possessed her in his dreams--and all at once! No! The next day, the very next day, it must all be set right, smoothed over, settled. Above all he must crush that conceited milksop who was the cause of it all. With a sick feeling he could not help recalling Razumihin too, but, he soon reassured himself on that score; as though a fellow like that could be put on a level with him! The man he really dreaded in earnest was Svidrigaïlov.... He had, in short, a great deal to attend to....\n\n*****\n“No, I, I am more to blame than anyone!” said Dounia, kissing and embracing her mother. “I was tempted by his money, but on my honour, brother, I had no idea he was such a base man. If I had seen through him before, nothing would have tempted me! Don’t blame me, brother!”\n\n“God has delivered us! God has delivered us!” Pulcheria Alexandrovna muttered, but half consciously, as though scarcely able to realise what had happened.\n\nThey were all relieved, and in five minutes they were laughing. Only now and then Dounia turned white and frowned, remembering what had passed. Pulcheria Alexandrovna was surprised to find that she, too, was glad: she had only that morning thought rupture with Luzhin a terrible misfortune. Razumihin was delighted. He did not yet dare to express his joy fully, but he was in a fever of excitement as though a ton-weight had fallen off his heart. Now he had the right to devote his life to them, to serve them.... Anything might happen now! But he felt afraid to think of further possibilities and dared not let his imagination range. But Raskolnikov sat still in the same place, almost sullen and indifferent. Though he had been the most insistent on getting rid of Luzhin, he seemed now the least concerned at what had happened. Dounia could not help thinking that he was still angry with her, and Pulcheria Alexandrovna watched him timidly.\n\n“What did Svidrigaïlov say to you?” said Dounia, approaching him.\n\n“Yes, yes!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.\n\nRaskolnikov raised his head.\n\n“He wants to make you a present of ten thousand roubles and he desires to see you once in my presence.”\n\n“See her! On no account!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna. “And how dare he offer her money!”\n\nThen Raskolnikov repeated (rather dryly) his conversation with Svidrigaïlov, omitting his account of the ghostly visitations of Marfa Petrovna, wishing to avoid all unnecessary talk.\n\n“What answer did you give him?” asked Dounia.\n\n“At first I said I would not take any message to you. Then he said that he would do his utmost to obtain an interview with you without my help. He assured me that his passion for you was a passing infatuation, now he has no feeling for you. He doesn’t want you to marry Luzhin.... His talk was altogether rather muddled.”\n\n“How do you explain him to yourself, Rodya? How did he strike you?”\n\n“I must confess I don’t quite understand him. He offers you ten thousand, and yet says he is not well off. He says he is going away, and in ten minutes he forgets he has said it. Then he says he is going to be married and has already fixed on the girl.... No doubt he has a motive, and probably a bad one. But it’s odd that he should be so clumsy about it if he had any designs against you.... Of course, I refused this money on your account, once for all. Altogether, I thought him very strange.... One might almost think he was mad. But I may be mistaken; that may only be the part he assumes. The death of Marfa Petrovna seems to have made a great impression on him.”\n\n“God rest her soul,” exclaimed Pulcheria Alexandrovna. “I shall always, always pray for her! Where should we be now, Dounia, without this three thousand! It’s as though it had fallen from heaven! Why, Rodya, this morning we had only three roubles in our pocket and Dounia and I were just planning to pawn her watch, so as to avoid borrowing from that man until he offered help.”\n\nDounia seemed strangely impressed by Svidrigaïlov’s offer. She still stood meditating.\n\n“He has got some terrible plan,” she said in a half whisper to herself, almost shuddering.\n\nRaskolnikov noticed this disproportionate terror.\n\n“I fancy I shall have to see him more than once again,” he said to Dounia.\n\n“We will watch him! I will track him out!” cried Razumihin, vigorously. “I won’t lose sight of him. Rodya has given me leave. He said to me himself just now. ‘Take care of my sister.’ Will you give me leave, too, Avdotya Romanovna?”\n\nDounia smiled and held out her hand, but the look of anxiety did not leave her face. Pulcheria Alexandrovna gazed at her timidly, but the three thousand roubles had obviously a soothing effect on her.\n\nA quarter of an hour later, they were all engaged in a lively conversation. Even Raskolnikov listened attentively for some time, though he did not talk. Razumihin was the speaker.\n\n“And why, why should you go away?” he flowed on ecstatically. “And what are you to do in a little town? The great thing is, you are all here together and you need one another--you do need one another, believe me. For a time, anyway.... Take me into partnership, and I assure you we’ll plan a capital enterprise. Listen! I’ll explain it all in detail to you, the whole project! It all flashed into my head this morning, before anything had happened... I tell you what; I have an uncle, I must introduce him to you (a most accommodating and respectable old man). This uncle has got a capital of a thousand roubles, and he lives on his pension and has no need of that money. For the last two years he has been bothering me to borrow it from him and pay him six per cent interest. I know what that means; he simply wants to help me. Last year I had no need of it, but this year I resolved to borrow it as soon as he arrived. Then you lend me another thousand of your three and we have enough for a start, so we’ll go into partnership, and what are we going to do?”\n\nThen Razumihin began to unfold his project, and he explained at length that almost all our publishers and booksellers know nothing at all of what they are selling, and for that reason they are usually bad publishers, and that any decent publications pay as a rule and give a profit, sometimes a considerable one. Razumihin had, indeed, been dreaming of setting up as a publisher. For the last two years he had been working in publishers’ offices, and knew three European languages well, though he had told Raskolnikov six days before that he was “schwach” in German with an object of persuading him to take half his translation and half the payment for it. He had told a lie then, and Raskolnikov knew he was lying.\n\n“Why, why should we let our chance slip when we have one of the chief means of success--money of our own!” cried Razumihin warmly. “Of course there will be a lot of work, but we will work, you, Avdotya Romanovna, I, Rodion.... You get a splendid profit on some books nowadays! And the great point of the business is that we shall know just what wants translating, and we shall be translating, publishing, learning all at once. I can be of use because I have experience. For nearly two years I’ve been scuttling about among the publishers, and now I know every detail of their business. You need not be a saint to make pots, believe me! And why, why should we let our chance slip! Why, I know--and I kept the secret--two or three books which one might get a hundred roubles simply for thinking of translating and publishing. Indeed, and I would not take five hundred for the very idea of one of them. And what do you think? If I were to tell a publisher, I dare say he’d hesitate--they are such blockheads! And as for the business side, printing, paper, selling, you trust to me, I know my way about. We’ll begin in a small way and go on to a large. In any case it will get us our living and we shall get back our capital.”\n\nDounia’s eyes shone.\n\n“I like what you are saying, Dmitri Prokofitch!” she said.\n\n“I know nothing about it, of course,” put in Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “it may be a good idea, but again God knows. It’s new and untried. Of course, we must remain here at least for a time.” She looked at Rodya.\n\n“What do you think, brother?” said Dounia.\n\n“I think he’s got a very good idea,” he answered. “Of course, it’s too soon to dream of a publishing firm, but we certainly might bring out five or six books and be sure of success. I know of one book myself which would be sure to go well. And as for his being able to manage it, there’s no doubt about that either. He knows the business.... But we can talk it over later....”\n\n“Hurrah!” cried Razumihin. “Now, stay, there’s a flat here in this house, belonging to the same owner. It’s a special flat apart, not communicating with these lodgings. It’s furnished, rent moderate, three rooms. Suppose you take them to begin with. I’ll pawn your watch to-morrow and bring you the money, and everything can be arranged then. You can all three live together, and Rodya will be with you. But where are you off to, Rodya?”\n\n“What, Rodya, you are going already?” Pulcheria Alexandrovna asked in dismay.\n\n“At such a minute?” cried Razumihin.\n\nDounia looked at her brother with incredulous wonder. He held his cap in his hand, he was preparing to leave them.\n\n“One would think you were burying me or saying good-bye for ever,” he said somewhat oddly. He attempted to smile, but it did not turn out a smile. “But who knows, perhaps it is the last time we shall see each other...” he let slip accidentally. It was what he was thinking, and it somehow was uttered aloud.\n\n“What is the matter with you?” cried his mother.\n\n“Where are you going, Rodya?” asked Dounia rather strangely.\n\n“Oh, I’m quite obliged to...” he answered vaguely, as though hesitating what he would say. But there was a look of sharp determination in his white face.\n\n“I meant to say... as I was coming here... I meant to tell you, mother, and you, Dounia, that it would be better for us to part for a time. I feel ill, I am not at peace.... I will come afterwards, I will come of myself... when it’s possible. I remember you and love you.... Leave me, leave me alone. I decided this even before... I’m absolutely resolved on it. Whatever may come to me, whether I come to ruin or not, I want to be alone. Forget me altogether, it’s better. Don’t inquire about me. When I can, I’ll come of myself or... I’ll send for you. Perhaps it will all come back, but now if you love me, give me up... else I shall begin to hate you, I feel it.... Good-bye!”\n\n“Good God!” cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna. Both his mother and his sister were terribly alarmed. Razumihin was also.\n\n“Rodya, Rodya, be reconciled with us! Let us be as before!” cried his poor mother.\n\nHe turned slowly to the door and slowly went out of the room. Dounia overtook him.\n\n“Brother, what are you doing to mother?” she whispered, her eyes flashing with indignation.\n\nHe looked dully at her.\n\n“No matter, I shall come.... I’m coming,” he muttered in an undertone, as though not fully conscious of what he was saying, and he went out of the room.\n\n“Wicked, heartless egoist!” cried Dounia.\n\n“He is insane, but not heartless. He is mad! Don’t you see it? You’re heartless after that!” Razumihin whispered in her ear, squeezing her hand tightly. “I shall be back directly,” he shouted to the horror-stricken mother, and he ran out of the room.\n\nRaskolnikov was waiting for him at the end of the passage.\n\n“I knew you would run after me,” he said. “Go back to them--be with them... be with them to-morrow and always.... I... perhaps I shall come... if I can. Good-bye.”\n\nAnd without holding out his hand he walked away.\n\n“But where are you going? What are you doing? What’s the matter with you? How can you go on like this?” Razumihin muttered, at his wits’ end.\n\nRaskolnikov stopped once more.\n\n“Once for all, never ask me about anything. I have nothing to tell you. Don’t come to see me. Maybe I’ll come here.... Leave me, but _don’t leave_ them. Do you understand me?”\n\nIt was dark in the corridor, they were standing near the lamp. For a minute they were looking at one another in silence. Razumihin remembered that minute all his life. Raskolnikov’s burning and intent eyes grew more penetrating every moment, piercing into his soul, into his consciousness. Suddenly Razumihin started. Something strange, as it were, passed between them.... Some idea, some hint, as it were, slipped, something awful, hideous, and suddenly understood on both sides.... Razumihin turned pale.\n\n“Do you understand now?” said Raskolnikov, his face twitching nervously. “Go back, go to them,” he said suddenly, and turning quickly, he went out of the house.\n\nI will not attempt to describe how Razumihin went back to the ladies, how he soothed them, how he protested that Rodya needed rest in his illness, protested that Rodya was sure to come, that he would come every day, that he was very, very much upset, that he must not be irritated, that he, Razumihin, would watch over him, would get him a doctor, the best doctor, a consultation.... In fact from that evening Razumihin took his place with them as a son and a brother.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nRaskolnikov went straight to the house on the canal bank where Sonia lived. It was an old green house of three storeys. He found the porter and obtained from him vague directions as to the whereabouts of Kapernaumov, the tailor. Having found in the corner of the courtyard the entrance to the dark and narrow staircase, he mounted to the second floor and came out into a gallery that ran round the whole second storey over the yard. While he was wandering in the darkness, uncertain where to turn for Kapernaumov’s door, a door opened three paces from him; he mechanically took hold of it.\n\n“Who is there?” a woman’s voice asked uneasily.\n\n“It’s I... come to see you,” answered Raskolnikov, and he walked into the tiny entry.\n\nOn a broken chair stood a candle in a battered copper candlestick.\n\n“It’s you! Good heavens!” cried Sonia weakly, and she stood rooted to the spot.\n\n“Which is your room? This way?” and Raskolnikov, trying not to look at her, hastened in.\n\nA minute later Sonia, too, came in with the candle, set down the candlestick and, completely disconcerted, stood before him inexpressibly agitated and apparently frightened by his unexpected visit. The colour rushed suddenly to her pale face and tears came into her eyes... She felt sick and ashamed and happy, too.... Raskolnikov turned away quickly and sat on a chair by the table. He scanned the room in a rapid glance.\n\nIt was a large but exceedingly low-pitched room, the only one let by the Kapernaumovs, to whose rooms a closed door led in the wall on the left. In the opposite side on the right hand wall was another door, always kept locked. That led to the next flat, which formed a separate lodging. Sonia’s room looked like a barn; it was a very irregular quadrangle and this gave it a grotesque appearance. A wall with three windows looking out on to the canal ran aslant so that one corner formed a very acute angle, and it was difficult to see in it without very strong light. The other corner was disproportionately obtuse. There was scarcely any furniture in the big room: in the corner on the right was a bedstead, beside it, nearest the door, a chair. A plain, deal table covered by a blue cloth stood against the same wall, close to the door into the other flat. Two rush-bottom chairs stood by the table. On the opposite wall near the acute angle stood a small plain wooden chest of drawers looking, as it were, lost in a desert. That was all there was in the room. The yellow, scratched and shabby wall-paper was black in the corners. It must have been damp and full of fumes in the winter. There was every sign of poverty; even the bedstead had no curtain.\n\nSonia looked in silence at her visitor, who was so attentively and unceremoniously scrutinising her room, and even began at last to tremble with terror, as though she was standing before her judge and the arbiter of her destinies.\n\n“I am late.... It’s eleven, isn’t it?” he asked, still not lifting his eyes.\n\n“Yes,” muttered Sonia, “oh yes, it is,” she added, hastily, as though in that lay her means of escape. “My landlady’s clock has just struck... I heard it myself....”\n\n“I’ve come to you for the last time,” Raskolnikov went on gloomily, although this was the first time. “I may perhaps not see you again...”\n\n“Are you... going away?”\n\n“I don’t know... to-morrow....”\n\n“Then you are not coming to Katerina Ivanovna to-morrow?” Sonia’s voice shook.\n\n“I don’t know. I shall know to-morrow morning.... Never mind that: I’ve come to say one word....”\n\nHe raised his brooding eyes to her and suddenly noticed that he was sitting down while she was all the while standing before him.\n\n“Why are you standing? Sit down,” he said in a changed voice, gentle and friendly.\n\nShe sat down. He looked kindly and almost compassionately at her.\n\n“How thin you are! What a hand! Quite transparent, like a dead hand.”\n\nHe took her hand. Sonia smiled faintly.\n\n“I have always been like that,” she said.\n\n“Even when you lived at home?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Of course, you were,” he added abruptly and the expression of his face and the sound of his voice changed again suddenly.\n\nHe looked round him once more.\n\n“You rent this room from the Kapernaumovs?”\n\n“Yes....”\n\n“They live there, through that door?”\n\n“Yes.... They have another room like this.”\n\n“All in one room?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I should be afraid in your room at night,” he observed gloomily.\n\n“They are very good people, very kind,” answered Sonia, who still seemed bewildered, “and all the furniture, everything... everything is theirs. And they are very kind and the children, too, often come to see me.”\n\n“They all stammer, don’t they?”\n\n“Yes.... He stammers and he’s lame. And his wife, too.... It’s not exactly that she stammers, but she can’t speak plainly. She is a very kind woman. And he used to be a house serf. And there are seven children... and it’s only the eldest one that stammers and the others are simply ill... but they don’t stammer.... But where did you hear about them?” she added with some surprise.\n\n“Your father told me, then. He told me all about you.... And how you went out at six o’clock and came back at nine and how Katerina Ivanovna knelt down by your bed.”\n\nSonia was confused.\n\n“I fancied I saw him to-day,” she whispered hesitatingly.\n\n“Whom?”\n\n“Father. I was walking in the street, out there at the corner, about ten o’clock and he seemed to be walking in front. It looked just like him. I wanted to go to Katerina Ivanovna....”\n\n“You were walking in the streets?”\n\n“Yes,” Sonia whispered abruptly, again overcome with confusion and looking down.\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna used to beat you, I dare say?”\n\n“Oh no, what are you saying? No!” Sonia looked at him almost with dismay.\n\n“You love her, then?”\n\n“Love her? Of course!” said Sonia with plaintive emphasis, and she clasped her hands in distress. “Ah, you don’t.... If you only knew! You see, she is quite like a child.... Her mind is quite unhinged, you see... from sorrow. And how clever she used to be... how generous... how kind! Ah, you don’t understand, you don’t understand!”\n\nSonia said this as though in despair, wringing her hands in excitement and distress. Her pale cheeks flushed, there was a look of anguish in her eyes. It was clear that she was stirred to the very depths, that she was longing to speak, to champion, to express something. A sort of _insatiable_ compassion, if one may so express it, was reflected in every feature of her face.\n\n“Beat me! how can you? Good heavens, beat me! And if she did beat me, what then? What of it? You know nothing, nothing about it.... She is so unhappy... ah, how unhappy! And ill.... She is seeking righteousness, she is pure. She has such faith that there must be righteousness everywhere and she expects it.... And if you were to torture her, she wouldn’t do wrong. She doesn’t see that it’s impossible for people to be righteous and she is angry at it. Like a child, like a child. She is good!”\n\n“And what will happen to you?”\n\nSonia looked at him inquiringly.\n\n“They are left on your hands, you see. They were all on your hands before, though.... And your father came to you to beg for drink. Well, how will it be now?”\n\n“I don’t know,” Sonia articulated mournfully.\n\n“Will they stay there?”\n\n“I don’t know.... They are in debt for the lodging, but the landlady, I hear, said to-day that she wanted to get rid of them, and Katerina Ivanovna says that she won’t stay another minute.”\n\n“How is it she is so bold? She relies upon you?”\n\n“Oh, no, don’t talk like that.... We are one, we live like one.” Sonia was agitated again and even angry, as though a canary or some other little bird were to be angry. “And what could she do? What, what could she do?” she persisted, getting hot and excited. “And how she cried to-day! Her mind is unhinged, haven’t you noticed it? At one minute she is worrying like a child that everything should be right to-morrow, the lunch and all that.... Then she is wringing her hands, spitting blood, weeping, and all at once she will begin knocking her head against the wall, in despair. Then she will be comforted again. She builds all her hopes on you; she says that you will help her now and that she will borrow a little money somewhere and go to her native town with me and set up a boarding school for the daughters of gentlemen and take me to superintend it, and we will begin a new splendid life. And she kisses and hugs me, comforts me, and you know she has such faith, such faith in her fancies! One can’t contradict her. And all the day long she has been washing, cleaning, mending. She dragged the wash tub into the room with her feeble hands and sank on the bed, gasping for breath. We went this morning to the shops to buy shoes for Polenka and Lida for theirs are quite worn out. Only the money we’d reckoned wasn’t enough, not nearly enough. And she picked out such dear little boots, for she has taste, you don’t know. And there in the shop she burst out crying before the shopmen because she hadn’t enough.... Ah, it was sad to see her....”\n\n“Well, after that I can understand your living like this,” Raskolnikov said with a bitter smile.\n\n“And aren’t you sorry for them? Aren’t you sorry?” Sonia flew at him again. “Why, I know, you gave your last penny yourself, though you’d seen nothing of it, and if you’d seen everything, oh dear! And how often, how often I’ve brought her to tears! Only last week! Yes, I! Only a week before his death. I was cruel! And how often I’ve done it! Ah, I’ve been wretched at the thought of it all day!”\n\nSonia wrung her hands as she spoke at the pain of remembering it.\n\n“You were cruel?”\n\n“Yes, I--I. I went to see them,” she went on, weeping, “and father said, ‘read me something, Sonia, my head aches, read to me, here’s a book.’ He had a book he had got from Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, he lives there, he always used to get hold of such funny books. And I said, ‘I can’t stay,’ as I didn’t want to read, and I’d gone in chiefly to show Katerina Ivanovna some collars. Lizaveta, the pedlar, sold me some collars and cuffs cheap, pretty, new, embroidered ones. Katerina Ivanovna liked them very much; she put them on and looked at herself in the glass and was delighted with them. ‘Make me a present of them, Sonia,’ she said, ‘please do.’ ‘_Please do_,’ she said, she wanted them so much. And when could she wear them? They just reminded her of her old happy days. She looked at herself in the glass, admired herself, and she has no clothes at all, no things of her own, hasn’t had all these years! And she never asks anyone for anything; she is proud, she’d sooner give away everything. And these she asked for, she liked them so much. And I was sorry to give them. ‘What use are they to you, Katerina Ivanovna?’ I said. I spoke like that to her, I ought not to have said that! She gave me such a look. And she was so grieved, so grieved at my refusing her. And it was so sad to see.... And she was not grieved for the collars, but for my refusing, I saw that. Ah, if only I could bring it all back, change it, take back those words! Ah, if I... but it’s nothing to you!”\n\n“Did you know Lizaveta, the pedlar?”\n\n“Yes.... Did you know her?” Sonia asked with some surprise.\n\n“Katerina Ivanovna is in consumption, rapid consumption; she will soon die,” said Raskolnikov after a pause, without answering her question.\n“Oh, no, no, no!”\n\nAnd Sonia unconsciously clutched both his hands, as though imploring that she should not.\n\n“But it will be better if she does die.”\n\n“No, not better, not at all better!” Sonia unconsciously repeated in dismay.\n\n“And the children? What can you do except take them to live with you?”\n\n“Oh, I don’t know,” cried Sonia, almost in despair, and she put her hands to her head.\n\nIt was evident that that idea had very often occurred to her before and he had only roused it again.\n\n“And, what, if even now, while Katerina Ivanovna is alive, you get ill and are taken to the hospital, what will happen then?” he persisted pitilessly.\n\n“How can you? That cannot be!”\n\nAnd Sonia’s face worked with awful terror.\n\n“Cannot be?” Raskolnikov went on with a harsh smile. “You are not insured against it, are you? What will happen to them then? They will be in the street, all of them, she will cough and beg and knock her head against some wall, as she did to-day, and the children will cry.... Then she will fall down, be taken to the police station and to the hospital, she will die, and the children...”\n\n“Oh, no.... God will not let it be!” broke at last from Sonia’s overburdened bosom.\n\nShe listened, looking imploringly at him, clasping her hands in dumb entreaty, as though it all depended upon him.\n\nRaskolnikov got up and began to walk about the room. A minute passed. Sonia was standing with her hands and her head hanging in terrible dejection.\n\n“And can’t you save? Put by for a rainy day?” he asked, stopping suddenly before her.\n\n“No,” whispered Sonia.\n\n“Of course not. Have you tried?” he added almost ironically.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And it didn’t come off! Of course not! No need to ask.”\n\nAnd again he paced the room. Another minute passed.\n\n“You don’t get money every day?”\n\nSonia was more confused than ever and colour rushed into her face again.\n\n“No,” she whispered with a painful effort.\n\n“It will be the same with Polenka, no doubt,” he said suddenly.\n\n“No, no! It can’t be, no!” Sonia cried aloud in desperation, as though she had been stabbed. “God would not allow anything so awful!”\n“He lets others come to it.”\n\n“No, no! God will protect her, God!” she repeated beside herself.\n\n“But, perhaps, there is no God at all,” Raskolnikov answered with a sort of malignance, laughed and looked at her.\n\nSonia’s face suddenly changed; a tremor passed over it. She looked at him with unutterable reproach, tried to say something, but could not speak and broke into bitter, bitter sobs, hiding her face in her hands.\n\n“You say Katerina Ivanovna’s mind is unhinged; your own mind is unhinged,” he said after a brief silence.\n\nFive minutes passed. He still paced up and down the room in silence, not looking at her. At last he went up to her; his eyes glittered. He put his two hands on her shoulders and looked straight into her tearful face. His eyes were hard, feverish and piercing, his lips were twitching. All at once he bent down quickly and dropping to the ground, kissed her foot. Sonia drew back from him as from a madman. And certainly he looked like a madman.\n\n“What are you doing to me?” she muttered, turning pale, and a sudden anguish clutched at her heart.\n\nHe stood up at once.\n\n“I did not bow down to you, I bowed down to all the suffering of humanity,” he said wildly and walked away to the window. “Listen,” he added, turning to her a minute later. “I said just now to an insolent man that he was not worth your little finger... and that I did my sister honour making her sit beside you.”\n\n“Ach, you said that to them! And in her presence?” cried Sonia, frightened. “Sit down with me! An honour! Why, I’m... dishonourable.... Ah, why did you say that?”\n\n“It was not because of your dishonour and your sin I said that of you, but because of your great suffering. But you are a great sinner, that’s true,” he added almost solemnly, “and your worst sin is that you have destroyed and betrayed yourself _for nothing_. Isn’t that fearful? Isn’t it fearful that you are living in this filth which you loathe so, and at the same time you know yourself (you’ve only to open your eyes) that you are not helping anyone by it, not saving anyone from anything? Tell me,” he went on almost in a frenzy, “how this shame and degradation can exist in you side by side with other, opposite, holy feelings? It would be better, a thousand times better and wiser to leap into the water and end it all!”\n\n“But what would become of them?” Sonia asked faintly, gazing at him with eyes of anguish, but not seeming surprised at his suggestion.\n\nRaskolnikov looked strangely at her. He read it all in her face; so she must have had that thought already, perhaps many times, and earnestly she had thought out in her despair how to end it and so earnestly, that now she scarcely wondered at his suggestion. She had not even noticed the cruelty of his words. (The significance of his reproaches and his peculiar attitude to her shame she had, of course, not noticed either, and that, too, was clear to him.) But he saw how monstrously the thought of her disgraceful, shameful position was torturing her and had long tortured her. “What, what,” he thought, “could hitherto have hindered her from putting an end to it?” Only then he realised what those poor little orphan children and that pitiful half-crazy Katerina Ivanovna, knocking her head against the wall in her consumption, meant for Sonia.\n\nBut, nevertheless, it was clear to him again that with her character and the amount of education she had after all received, she could not in any case remain so. He was still confronted by the question, how could she have remained so long in that position without going out of her mind, since she could not bring herself to jump into the water? Of course he knew that Sonia’s position was an exceptional case, though unhappily not unique and not infrequent, indeed; but that very exceptionalness, her tinge of education, her previous life might, one would have thought, have killed her at the first step on that revolting path. What held her up--surely not depravity? All that infamy had obviously only touched her mechanically, not one drop of real depravity had penetrated to her heart; he saw that. He saw through her as she stood before him....\n\n“There are three ways before her,” he thought, “the canal, the madhouse, or... at last to sink into depravity which obscures the mind and turns the heart to stone.”\n\nThe last idea was the most revolting, but he was a sceptic, he was young, abstract, and therefore cruel, and so he could not help believing that the last end was the most likely.\n\n“But can that be true?” he cried to himself. “Can that creature who has still preserved the purity of her spirit be consciously drawn at last into that sink of filth and iniquity? Can the process already have begun? Can it be that she has only been able to bear it till now, because vice has begun to be less loathsome to her? No, no, that cannot be!” he cried, as Sonia had just before. “No, what has kept her from the canal till now is the idea of sin and they, the children.... And if she has not gone out of her mind... but who says she has not gone out of her mind? Is she in her senses? Can one talk, can one reason as she does? How can she sit on the edge of the abyss of loathsomeness into which she is slipping and refuse to listen when she is told of danger? Does she expect a miracle? No doubt she does. Doesn’t that all mean madness?”\n\nHe stayed obstinately at that thought. He liked that explanation indeed better than any other. He began looking more intently at her.\n\n“So you pray to God a great deal, Sonia?” he asked her.\n\nSonia did not speak; he stood beside her waiting for an answer.\n\n“What should I be without God?” she whispered rapidly, forcibly, glancing at him with suddenly flashing eyes, and squeezing his hand.\n\n“Ah, so that is it!” he thought.\n\n“And what does God do for you?” he asked, probing her further.\n\nSonia was silent a long while, as though she could not answer. Her weak chest kept heaving with emotion.\n\n“Be silent! Don’t ask! You don’t deserve!” she cried suddenly, looking sternly and wrathfully at him.\n\n“That’s it, that’s it,” he repeated to himself.\n\n“He does everything,” she whispered quickly, looking down again.\n\n“That’s the way out! That’s the explanation,” he decided, scrutinising her with eager curiosity, with a new, strange, almost morbid feeling. He gazed at that pale, thin, irregular, angular little face, those soft blue eyes, which could flash with such fire, such stern energy, that little body still shaking with indignation and anger--and it all seemed to him more and more strange, almost impossible. “She is a religious maniac!” he repeated to himself.\n\nThere was a book lying on the chest of drawers. He had noticed it every time he paced up and down the room. Now he took it up and looked at it. It was the New Testament in the Russian translation. It was bound in leather, old and worn.\n\n“Where did you get that?” he called to her across the room.\n\nShe was still standing in the same place, three steps from the table.\n\n“It was brought me,” she answered, as it were unwillingly, not looking at him.\n\n“Who brought it?”\n\n“Lizaveta, I asked her for it.”\n\n“Lizaveta! strange!” he thought.\n\nEverything about Sonia seemed to him stranger and more wonderful every moment. He carried the book to the candle and began to turn over the pages.\n\n“Where is the story of Lazarus?” he asked suddenly.\n\nSonia looked obstinately at the ground and would not answer. She was standing sideways to the table.\n\n“Where is the raising of Lazarus? Find it for me, Sonia.”\n\nShe stole a glance at him.\n\n“You are not looking in the right place.... It’s in the fourth gospel,” she whispered sternly, without looking at him.\n\n“Find it and read it to me,” he said. He sat down with his elbow on the table, leaned his head on his hand and looked away sullenly, prepared to listen.\n\n“In three weeks’ time they’ll welcome me in the madhouse! I shall be there if I am not in a worse place,” he muttered to himself.\n\nSonia heard Raskolnikov’s request distrustfully and moved hesitatingly to the table. She took the book however.\n\n“Haven’t you read it?” she asked, looking up at him across the table.\n\nHer voice became sterner and sterner.\n\n“Long ago.... When I was at school. Read!”\n\n“And haven’t you heard it in church?”\n\n“I... haven’t been. Do you often go?”\n\n“N-no,” whispered Sonia.\n\nRaskolnikov smiled.\n\n“I understand.... And you won’t go to your father’s funeral to-morrow?”\n\n“Yes, I shall. I was at church last week, too... I had a requiem service.”\n\n“For whom?”\n\n“For Lizaveta. She was killed with an axe.”\n\nHis nerves were more and more strained. His head began to go round.\n\n“Were you friends with Lizaveta?”\n\n“Yes.... She was good... she used to come... not often... she couldn’t.... We used to read together and... talk. She will see God.”\n\nThe last phrase sounded strange in his ears. And here was something new again: the mysterious meetings with Lizaveta and both of them--religious maniacs.\n\n“I shall be a religious maniac myself soon! It’s infectious!”\n\n“Read!” he cried irritably and insistently.\n\nSonia still hesitated. Her heart was throbbing. She hardly dared to read to him. He looked almost with exasperation at the “unhappy lunatic.”\n\n“What for? You don’t believe?...” she whispered softly and as it were breathlessly.\n\n“Read! I want you to,” he persisted. “You used to read to Lizaveta.”\n\nSonia opened the book and found the place. Her hands were shaking, her voice failed her. Twice she tried to begin and could not bring out the first syllable.\n\n“Now a certain man was sick named Lazarus of Bethany...” she forced herself at last to read, but at the third word her voice broke like an overstrained string. There was a catch in her breath.\n\nRaskolnikov saw in part why Sonia could not bring herself to read to him and the more he saw this, the more roughly and irritably he insisted on her doing so. He understood only too well how painful it was for her to betray and unveil all that was her _own_. He understood that these feelings really were her _secret treasure_, which she had kept perhaps for years, perhaps from childhood, while she lived with an unhappy father and a distracted stepmother crazed by grief, in the midst of starving children and unseemly abuse and reproaches. But at the same time he knew now and knew for certain that, although it filled her with dread and suffering, yet she had a tormenting desire to read and to read to _him_ that he might hear it, and to read _now_ whatever might come of it!... He read this in her eyes, he could see it in her intense emotion. She mastered herself, controlled the spasm in her throat and went on reading the eleventh chapter of St. John. She went on to the nineteenth verse:\n\n“And many of the Jews came to Martha and Mary to comfort them concerning their brother.\n\n“Then Martha as soon as she heard that Jesus was coming went and met Him: but Mary sat still in the house.\n\n“Then said Martha unto Jesus, Lord, if Thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.\n\n“But I know that even now whatsoever Thou wilt ask of God, God will give it Thee....”\n\nThen she stopped again with a shamefaced feeling that her voice would quiver and break again.\n\n“Jesus said unto her, thy brother shall rise again.\n\n“Martha saith unto Him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection, at the last day.\n\n“Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection and the life: he that believeth in Me though he were dead, yet shall he live.\n\n“And whosoever liveth and believeth in Me shall never die. Believest thou this?\n\n“She saith unto Him,”\n\n(And drawing a painful breath, Sonia read distinctly and forcibly as though she were making a public confession of faith.)\n\n“Yea, Lord: I believe that Thou art the Christ, the Son of God Which should come into the world.”\n\nShe stopped and looked up quickly at him, but controlling herself went on reading. Raskolnikov sat without moving, his elbows on the table and his eyes turned away. She read to the thirty-second verse.\n\n“Then when Mary was come where Jesus was and saw Him, she fell down at His feet, saying unto Him, Lord if Thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.\n\n“When Jesus therefore saw her weeping, and the Jews also weeping which came with her, He groaned in the spirit and was troubled,\n\n“And said, Where have ye laid him? They said unto Him, Lord, come and see.\n\n“Jesus wept.\n“Then said the Jews, behold how He loved him!\n\n“And some of them said, could not this Man which opened the eyes of the blind, have caused that even this man should not have died?”\n\nRaskolnikov turned and looked at her with emotion. Yes, he had known it! She was trembling in a real physical fever. He had expected it. She was getting near the story of the greatest miracle and a feeling of immense triumph came over her. Her voice rang out like a bell; triumph and joy gave it power. The lines danced before her eyes, but she knew what she was reading by heart. At the last verse “Could not this Man which opened the eyes of the blind...” dropping her voice she passionately reproduced the doubt, the reproach and censure of the blind disbelieving Jews, who in another moment would fall at His feet as though struck by thunder, sobbing and believing.... “And _he, he_--too, is blinded and unbelieving, he, too, will hear, he, too, will believe, yes, yes! At once, now,” was what she was dreaming, and she was quivering with happy anticipation.\n\n“Jesus therefore again groaning in Himself cometh to the grave. It was a cave, and a stone lay upon it.\n\n“Jesus said, Take ye away the stone. Martha, the sister of him that was dead, saith unto Him, Lord by this time he stinketh: for he hath been dead four days.”\n\nShe laid emphasis on the word _four_.\n\n“Jesus saith unto her, Said I not unto thee that if thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see the glory of God?\n\n“Then they took away the stone from the place where the dead was laid. And Jesus lifted up His eyes and said, Father, I thank Thee that Thou hast heard Me.\n\n“And I knew that Thou hearest Me always; but because of the people which stand by I said it, that they may believe that Thou hast sent Me.\n\n“And when He thus had spoken, He cried with a loud voice, Lazarus, come forth.\n\n“And he that was dead came forth.”\n\n(She read loudly, cold and trembling with ecstasy, as though she were seeing it before her eyes.)\n\n“Bound hand and foot with graveclothes; and his face was bound about with a napkin. Jesus saith unto them, Loose him and let him go.\n\n“Then many of the Jews which came to Mary and had seen the things which Jesus did believed on Him.”\n\nShe could read no more, closed the book and got up from her chair quickly.\n\n“That is all about the raising of Lazarus,” she whispered severely and abruptly, and turning away she stood motionless, not daring to raise her eyes to him. She still trembled feverishly. The candle-end was flickering out in the battered candlestick, dimly lighting up in the poverty-stricken room the murderer and the harlot who had so strangely been reading together the eternal book. Five minutes or more passed.\n\n“I came to speak of something,” Raskolnikov said aloud, frowning. He got up and went to Sonia. She lifted her eyes to him in silence. His face was particularly stern and there was a sort of savage determination in it.\n\n“I have abandoned my family to-day,” he said, “my mother and sister. I am not going to see them. I’ve broken with them completely.”\n\n“What for?” asked Sonia amazed. Her recent meeting with his mother and sister had left a great impression which she could not analyse. She heard his news almost with horror.\n\n“I have only you now,” he added. “Let us go together.... I’ve come to you, we are both accursed, let us go our way together!”\n\nHis eyes glittered “as though he were mad,” Sonia thought, in her turn.\n\n“Go where?” she asked in alarm and she involuntarily stepped back.\n\n“How do I know? I only know it’s the same road, I know that and nothing more. It’s the same goal!”\n\nShe looked at him and understood nothing. She knew only that he was terribly, infinitely unhappy.\n\n“No one of them will understand, if you tell them, but I have understood. I need you, that is why I have come to you.”\n\n“I don’t understand,” whispered Sonia.\n\n“You’ll understand later. Haven’t you done the same? You, too, have transgressed... have had the strength to transgress. You have laid hands on yourself, you have destroyed a life... _your own_ (it’s all the same!). You might have lived in spirit and understanding, but you’ll end in the Hay Market.... But you won’t be able to stand it, and if you remain alone you’ll go out of your mind like me. You are like a mad creature already. So we must go together on the same road! Let us go!”\n\n“What for? What’s all this for?” said Sonia, strangely and violently agitated by his words.\n\n“What for? Because you can’t remain like this, that’s why! You must look things straight in the face at last, and not weep like a child and cry that God won’t allow it. What will happen, if you should really be taken to the hospital to-morrow? She is mad and in consumption, she’ll soon die and the children? Do you mean to tell me Polenka won’t come to grief? Haven’t you seen children here at the street corners sent out by their mothers to beg? I’ve found out where those mothers live and in what surroundings. Children can’t remain children there! At seven the child is vicious and a thief. Yet children, you know, are the image of Christ: ‘theirs is the kingdom of Heaven.’ He bade us honour and love them, they are the humanity of the future....”\n\n“What’s to be done, what’s to be done?” repeated Sonia, weeping hysterically and wringing her hands.\n\n“What’s to be done? Break what must be broken, once for all, that’s all, and take the suffering on oneself. What, you don’t understand? You’ll understand later.... Freedom and power, and above all, power! Over all trembling creation and all the ant-heap!... That’s the goal, remember that! That’s my farewell message. Perhaps it’s the last time I shall speak to you. If I don’t come to-morrow, you’ll hear of it all, and then remember these words. And some day later on, in years to come, you’ll understand perhaps what they meant. If I come to-morrow, I’ll tell you who killed Lizaveta.... Good-bye.”\n\nSonia started with terror.\n\n“Why, do you know who killed her?” she asked, chilled with horror, looking wildly at him.\n\n“I know and will tell... you, only you. I have chosen you out. I’m not coming to you to ask forgiveness, but simply to tell you. I chose you out long ago to hear this, when your father talked of you and when Lizaveta was alive, I thought of it. Good-bye, don’t shake hands. To-morrow!”\n\nHe went out. Sonia gazed at him as at a madman. But she herself was like one insane and felt it. Her head was going round.\n\n“Good heavens, how does he know who killed Lizaveta? What did those words mean? It’s awful!” But at the same time _the idea_ did not enter her head, not for a moment! “Oh, he must be terribly unhappy!... He has abandoned his mother and sister.... What for? What has happened? And what had he in his mind? What did he say to her? He had kissed her foot and said... said (yes, he had said it clearly) that he could not live without her.... Oh, merciful heavens!”\n\nSonia spent the whole night feverish and delirious. She jumped up from time to time, wept and wrung her hands, then sank again into feverish sleep and dreamt of Polenka, Katerina Ivanovna and Lizaveta, of reading the gospel and him... him with pale face, with burning eyes... kissing her feet, weeping.\n\nOn the other side of the door on the right, which divided Sonia’s room from Madame Resslich’s flat, was a room which had long stood empty. A card was fixed on the gate and a notice stuck in the windows over the canal advertising it to let. Sonia had long been accustomed to the room’s being uninhabited. But all that time Mr. Svidrigaïlov had been standing, listening at the door of the empty room. When Raskolnikov went out he stood still, thought a moment, went on tiptoe to his own room which adjoined the empty one, brought a chair and noiselessly carried it to the door that led to Sonia’s room. The conversation had struck him as interesting and remarkable, and he had greatly enjoyed it--so much so that he brought a chair that he might not in the future, to-morrow, for instance, have to endure the inconvenience of standing a whole hour, but might listen in comfort.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nWhen next morning at eleven o’clock punctually Raskolnikov went into the department of the investigation of criminal causes and sent his name in to Porfiry Petrovitch, he was surprised at being kept waiting so long: it was at least ten minutes before he was summoned. He had expected that they would pounce upon him. But he stood in the waiting-room, and people, who apparently had nothing to do with him, were continually passing to and fro before him. In the next room which looked like an office, several clerks were sitting writing and obviously they had no notion who or what Raskolnikov might be. He looked uneasily and suspiciously about him to see whether there was not some guard, some mysterious watch being kept on him to prevent his escape. But there was nothing of the sort: he saw only the faces of clerks absorbed in petty details, then other people, no one seemed to have any concern with him. He might go where he liked for them. The conviction grew stronger in him that if that enigmatic man of yesterday, that phantom sprung out of the earth, had seen everything, they would not have let him stand and wait like that. And would they have waited till he elected to appear at eleven? Either the man had not yet given information, or... or simply he knew nothing, had seen nothing (and how could he have seen anything?) and so all that had happened to him the day before was again a phantom exaggerated by his sick and overstrained imagination. This conjecture had begun to grow strong the day before, in the midst of all his alarm and despair. Thinking it all over now and preparing for a fresh conflict, he was suddenly aware that he was trembling--and he felt a rush of indignation at the thought that he was trembling with fear at facing that hateful Porfiry Petrovitch. What he dreaded above all was meeting that man again; he hated him with an intense, unmitigated hatred and was afraid his hatred might betray him. His indignation was such that he ceased trembling at once; he made ready to go in with a cold and arrogant bearing and vowed to himself to keep as silent as possible, to watch and listen and for once at least to control his overstrained nerves. At that moment he was summoned to Porfiry Petrovitch.\n\nHe found Porfiry Petrovitch alone in his study. His study was a room neither large nor small, furnished with a large writing-table, that stood before a sofa, upholstered in checked material, a bureau, a bookcase in the corner and several chairs--all government furniture, of polished yellow wood. In the further wall there was a closed door, beyond it there were no doubt other rooms. On Raskolnikov’s entrance Porfiry Petrovitch had at once closed the door by which he had come in and they remained alone. He met his visitor with an apparently genial and good-tempered air, and it was only after a few minutes that Raskolnikov saw signs of a certain awkwardness in him, as though he had been thrown out of his reckoning or caught in something very secret.\n\n“Ah, my dear fellow! Here you are... in our domain”... began Porfiry, holding out both hands to him. “Come, sit down, old man... or perhaps you don’t like to be called ‘my dear fellow’ and ‘old man!’--_tout court_? Please don’t think it too familiar.... Here, on the sofa.”\n\nRaskolnikov sat down, keeping his eyes fixed on him. “In our domain,” the apologies for familiarity, the French phrase _tout court_, were all characteristic signs.\n\n“He held out both hands to me, but he did not give me one--he drew it back in time,” struck him suspiciously. Both were watching each other, but when their eyes met, quick as lightning they looked away.\n\n“I brought you this paper... about the watch. Here it is. Is it all right or shall I copy it again?”\n\n“What? A paper? Yes, yes, don’t be uneasy, it’s all right,” Porfiry Petrovitch said as though in haste, and after he had said it he took the paper and looked at it. “Yes, it’s all right. Nothing more is needed,” he declared with the same rapidity and he laid the paper on the table.\n\nA minute later when he was talking of something else he took it from the table and put it on his bureau.\n\n“I believe you said yesterday you would like to question me... formally... about my acquaintance with the murdered woman?” Raskolnikov was beginning again. “Why did I put in ‘I believe’” passed through his mind in a flash. “Why am I so uneasy at having put in that ‘_I believe_’?” came in a second flash. And he suddenly felt that his uneasiness at the mere contact with Porfiry, at the first words, at the first looks, had grown in an instant to monstrous proportions, and that this was fearfully dangerous. His nerves were quivering, his emotion was increasing. “It’s bad, it’s bad! I shall say too much again.”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes! There’s no hurry, there’s no hurry,” muttered Porfiry Petrovitch, moving to and fro about the table without any apparent aim, as it were making dashes towards the window, the bureau and the table, at one moment avoiding Raskolnikov’s suspicious glance, then again standing still and looking him straight in the face.\n\nHis fat round little figure looked very strange, like a ball rolling from one side to the other and rebounding back.\n\n“We’ve plenty of time. Do you smoke? have you your own? Here, a cigarette!” he went on, offering his visitor a cigarette. “You know I am receiving you here, but my own quarters are through there, you know, my government quarters. But I am living outside for the time, I had to have some repairs done here. It’s almost finished now.... Government quarters, you know, are a capital thing. Eh, what do you think?”\n\n“Yes, a capital thing,” answered Raskolnikov, looking at him almost ironically.\n\n“A capital thing, a capital thing,” repeated Porfiry Petrovitch, as though he had just thought of something quite different. “Yes, a capital thing,” he almost shouted at last, suddenly staring at Raskolnikov and stopping short two steps from him.\n\nThis stupid repetition was too incongruous in its ineptitude with the serious, brooding and enigmatic glance he turned upon his visitor.\n\nBut this stirred Raskolnikov’s spleen more than ever and he could not resist an ironical and rather incautious challenge.\n\n“Tell me, please,” he asked suddenly, looking almost insolently at him and taking a kind of pleasure in his own insolence. “I believe it’s a sort of legal rule, a sort of legal tradition--for all investigating lawyers--to begin their attack from afar, with a trivial, or at least an irrelevant subject, so as to encourage, or rather, to divert the man they are cross-examining, to disarm his caution and then all at once to give him an unexpected knock-down blow with some fatal question. Isn’t that so? It’s a sacred tradition, mentioned, I fancy, in all the manuals of the art?”\n\n“Yes, yes.... Why, do you imagine that was why I spoke about government quarters... eh?”\n\nAnd as he said this Porfiry Petrovitch screwed up his eyes and winked; a good-humoured, crafty look passed over his face. The wrinkles on his forehead were smoothed out, his eyes contracted, his features broadened and he suddenly went off into a nervous prolonged laugh, shaking all over and looking Raskolnikov straight in the face. The latter forced himself to laugh, too, but when Porfiry, seeing that he was laughing, broke into such a guffaw that he turned almost crimson, Raskolnikov’s repulsion overcame all precaution; he left off laughing, scowled and stared with hatred at Porfiry, keeping his eyes fixed on him while his intentionally prolonged laughter lasted. There was lack of precaution on both sides, however, for Porfiry Petrovitch seemed to be laughing in his visitor’s face and to be very little disturbed at the annoyance with which the visitor received it. The latter fact was very significant in Raskolnikov’s eyes: he saw that Porfiry Petrovitch had not been embarrassed just before either, but that he, Raskolnikov, had perhaps fallen into a trap; that there must be something, some motive here unknown to him; that, perhaps, everything was in readiness and in another moment would break upon him...\n\nHe went straight to the point at once, rose from his seat and took his cap.\n\n“Porfiry Petrovitch,” he began resolutely, though with considerable irritation, “yesterday you expressed a desire that I should come to you for some inquiries” (he laid special stress on the word “inquiries”). “I have come and if you have anything to ask me, ask it, and if not, allow me to withdraw. I have no time to spare.... I have to be at the funeral of that man who was run over, of whom you... know also,” he added, feeling angry at once at having made this addition and more irritated at his anger. “I am sick of it all, do you hear? and have long been. It’s partly what made me ill. In short,” he shouted, feeling that the phrase about his illness was still more out of place, “in short, kindly examine me or let me go, at once. And if you must examine me, do so in the proper form! I will not allow you to do so otherwise, and so meanwhile, good-bye, as we have evidently nothing to keep us now.”\n\n“Good heavens! What do you mean? What shall I question you about?” cackled Porfiry Petrovitch with a change of tone, instantly leaving off laughing. “Please don’t disturb yourself,” he began fidgeting from place to place and fussily making Raskolnikov sit down. “There’s no hurry, there’s no hurry, it’s all nonsense. Oh, no, I’m very glad you’ve come to see me at last... I look upon you simply as a visitor. And as for my confounded laughter, please excuse it, Rodion Romanovitch. Rodion Romanovitch? That is your name?... It’s my nerves, you tickled me so with your witty observation; I assure you, sometimes I shake with laughter like an india-rubber ball for half an hour at a time.... I’m often afraid of an attack of paralysis. Do sit down. Please do, or I shall think you are angry...”\n\nRaskolnikov did not speak; he listened, watching him, still frowning angrily. He did sit down, but still held his cap.\n\n“I must tell you one thing about myself, my dear Rodion Romanovitch,” Porfiry Petrovitch continued, moving about the room and again avoiding his visitor’s eyes. “You see, I’m a bachelor, a man of no consequence and not used to society; besides, I have nothing before me, I’m set, I’m running to seed and... and have you noticed, Rodion Romanovitch, that in our Petersburg circles, if two clever men meet who are not intimate, but respect each other, like you and me, it takes them half an hour before they can find a subject for conversation--they are dumb, they sit opposite each other and feel awkward. Everyone has subjects of conversation, ladies for instance... people in high society always have their subjects of conversation, _c’est de rigueur_, but people of the middle sort like us, thinking people that is, are always tongue-tied and awkward. What is the reason of it? Whether it is the lack of public interest, or whether it is we are so honest we don’t want to deceive one another, I don’t know. What do you think? Do put down your cap, it looks as if you were just going, it makes me uncomfortable... I am so delighted...”\n\nRaskolnikov put down his cap and continued listening in silence with a serious frowning face to the vague and empty chatter of Porfiry Petrovitch. “Does he really want to distract my attention with his silly babble?”\n\n“I can’t offer you coffee here; but why not spend five minutes with a friend?” Porfiry pattered on, “and you know all these official duties... please don’t mind my running up and down, excuse it, my dear fellow, I am very much afraid of offending you, but exercise is absolutely indispensable for me. I’m always sitting and so glad to be moving about for five minutes... I suffer from my sedentary life... I always intend to join a gymnasium; they say that officials of all ranks, even Privy Councillors, may be seen skipping gaily there; there you have it, modern science... yes, yes.... But as for my duties here, inquiries and all such formalities... you mentioned inquiries yourself just now... I assure you these interrogations are sometimes more embarrassing for the interrogator than for the interrogated.... You made the observation yourself just now very aptly and wittily.” (Raskolnikov had made no observation of the kind.) “One gets into a muddle! A regular muddle! One keeps harping on the same note, like a drum! There is to be a reform and we shall be called by a different name, at least, he-he-he! And as for our legal tradition, as you so wittily called it, I thoroughly agree with you. Every prisoner on trial, even the rudest peasant, knows that they begin by disarming him with irrelevant questions (as you so happily put it) and then deal him a knock-down blow, he-he-he!--your felicitous comparison, he-he! So you really imagined that I meant by ‘government quarters’... he-he! You are an ironical person. Come. I won’t go on! Ah, by the way, yes! One word leads to another. You spoke of formality just now, apropos of the inquiry, you know. But what’s the use of formality? In many cases it’s nonsense. Sometimes one has a friendly chat and gets a good deal more out of it. One can always fall back on formality, allow me to assure you. And after all, what does it amount to? An examining lawyer cannot be bounded by formality at every step. The work of investigation is, so to speak, a free art in its own way, he-he-he!”\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch took breath a moment. He had simply babbled on uttering empty phrases, letting slip a few enigmatic words and again reverting to incoherence. He was almost running about the room, moving his fat little legs quicker and quicker, looking at the ground, with his right hand behind his back, while with his left making gesticulations that were extraordinarily incongruous with his words. Raskolnikov suddenly noticed that as he ran about the room he seemed twice to stop for a moment near the door, as though he were listening.\n\n“Is he expecting anything?”\n\n“You are certainly quite right about it,” Porfiry began gaily, looking with extraordinary simplicity at Raskolnikov (which startled him and instantly put him on his guard); “certainly quite right in laughing so wittily at our legal forms, he-he! Some of these elaborate psychological methods are exceedingly ridiculous and perhaps useless, if one adheres too closely to the forms. Yes... I am talking of forms again. Well, if I recognise, or more strictly speaking, if I suspect someone or other to be a criminal in any case entrusted to me... you’re reading for the law, of course, Rodion Romanovitch?”\n\n“Yes, I was...”\n\n“Well, then it is a precedent for you for the future--though don’t suppose I should venture to instruct you after the articles you publish about crime! No, I simply make bold to state it by way of fact, if I took this man or that for a criminal, why, I ask, should I worry him prematurely, even though I had evidence against him? In one case I may be bound, for instance, to arrest a man at once, but another may be in quite a different position, you know, so why shouldn’t I let him walk about the town a bit? he-he-he! But I see you don’t quite understand, so I’ll give you a clearer example. If I put him in prison too soon, I may very likely give him, so to speak, moral support, he-he! You’re laughing?”\n\nRaskolnikov had no idea of laughing. He was sitting with compressed lips, his feverish eyes fixed on Porfiry Petrovitch’s.\n\n“Yet that is the case, with some types especially, for men are so different. You say ‘evidence’. Well, there may be evidence. But evidence, you know, can generally be taken two ways. I am an examining lawyer and a weak man, I confess it. I should like to make a proof, so to say, mathematically clear. I should like to make a chain of evidence such as twice two are four, it ought to be a direct, irrefutable proof! And if I shut him up too soon--even though I might be convinced _he_ was the man, I should very likely be depriving myself of the means of getting further evidence against him. And how? By giving him, so to speak, a definite position, I shall put him out of suspense and set his mind at rest, so that he will retreat into his shell. They say that at Sevastopol, soon after Alma, the clever people were in a terrible fright that the enemy would attack openly and take Sevastopol at once. But when they saw that the enemy preferred a regular siege, they were delighted, I am told and reassured, for the thing would drag on for two months at least. You’re laughing, you don’t believe me again? Of course, you’re right, too. You’re right, you’re right. These are special cases, I admit. But you must observe this, my dear Rodion Romanovitch, the general case, the case for which all legal forms and rules are intended, for which they are calculated and laid down in books, does not exist at all, for the reason that every case, every crime, for instance, so soon as it actually occurs, at once becomes a thoroughly special case and sometimes a case unlike any that’s gone before. Very comic cases of that sort sometimes occur. If I leave one man quite alone, if I don’t touch him and don’t worry him, but let him know or at least suspect every moment that I know all about it and am watching him day and night, and if he is in continual suspicion and terror, he’ll be bound to lose his head. He’ll come of himself, or maybe do something which will make it as plain as twice two are four--it’s delightful. It may be so with a simple peasant, but with one of our sort, an intelligent man cultivated on a certain side, it’s a dead certainty. For, my dear fellow, it’s a very important matter to know on what side a man is cultivated. And then there are nerves, there are nerves, you have overlooked them! Why, they are all sick, nervous and irritable!... And then how they all suffer from spleen! That I assure you is a regular gold-mine for us. And it’s no anxiety to me, his running about the town free! Let him, let him walk about for a bit! I know well enough that I’ve caught him and that he won’t escape me. Where could he escape to, he-he? Abroad, perhaps? A Pole will escape abroad, but not here, especially as I am watching and have taken measures. Will he escape into the depths of the country perhaps? But you know, peasants live there, real rude Russian peasants. A modern cultivated man would prefer prison to living with such strangers as our peasants. He-he! But that’s all nonsense, and on the surface. It’s not merely that he has nowhere to run to, he is _psychologically_ unable to escape me, he-he! What an expression! Through a law of nature he can’t escape me if he had anywhere to go. Have you seen a butterfly round a candle? That’s how he will keep circling and circling round me. Freedom will lose its attractions. He’ll begin to brood, he’ll weave a tangle round himself, he’ll worry himself to death! What’s more, he will provide me with a mathematical proof--if I only give him long enough interval.... And he’ll keep circling round me, getting nearer and nearer and then--flop! He’ll fly straight into my mouth and I’ll swallow him, and that will be very amusing, he-he-he! You don’t believe me?”\n\nRaskolnikov made no reply; he sat pale and motionless, still gazing with the same intensity into Porfiry’s face.\n\n“It’s a lesson,” he thought, turning cold. “This is beyond the cat playing with a mouse, like yesterday. He can’t be showing off his power with no motive... prompting me; he is far too clever for that... he must have another object. What is it? It’s all nonsense, my friend, you are pretending, to scare me! You’ve no proofs and the man I saw had no real existence. You simply want to make me lose my head, to work me up beforehand and so to crush me. But you are wrong, you won’t do it! But why give me such a hint? Is he reckoning on my shattered nerves? No, my friend, you are wrong, you won’t do it even though you have some trap for me... let us see what you have in store for me.”\n\nAnd he braced himself to face a terrible and unknown ordeal. At times he longed to fall on Porfiry and strangle him. This anger was what he dreaded from the beginning. He felt that his parched lips were flecked with foam, his heart was throbbing. But he was still determined not to speak till the right moment. He realised that this was the best policy in his position, because instead of saying too much he would be irritating his enemy by his silence and provoking him into speaking too freely. Anyhow, this was what he hoped for.\n\n“No, I see you don’t believe me, you think I am playing a harmless joke on you,” Porfiry began again, getting more and more lively, chuckling at every instant and again pacing round the room. “And to be sure you’re right: God has given me a figure that can awaken none but comic ideas in other people; a buffoon; but let me tell you, and I repeat it, excuse an old man, my dear Rodion Romanovitch, you are a man still young, so to say, in your first youth and so you put intellect above everything, like all young people. Playful wit and abstract arguments fascinate you and that’s for all the world like the old Austrian _Hof-kriegsrath_, as far as I can judge of military matters, that is: on paper they’d beaten Napoleon and taken him prisoner, and there in their study they worked it all out in the cleverest fashion, but look you, General Mack surrendered with all his army, he-he-he! I see, I see, Rodion Romanovitch, you are laughing at a civilian like me, taking examples out of military history! But I can’t help it, it’s my weakness. I am fond of military science. And I’m ever so fond of reading all military histories. I’ve certainly missed my proper career. I ought to have been in the army, upon my word I ought. I shouldn’t have been a Napoleon, but I might have been a major, he-he! Well, I’ll tell you the whole truth, my dear fellow, about this _special case_, I mean: actual fact and a man’s temperament, my dear sir, are weighty matters and it’s astonishing how they sometimes deceive the sharpest calculation! I--listen to an old man--am speaking seriously, Rodion Romanovitch” (as he said this Porfiry Petrovitch, who was scarcely five-and-thirty, actually seemed to have grown old; even his voice changed and he seemed to shrink together) “Moreover, I’m a candid man... am I a candid man or not? What do you say? I fancy I really am: I tell you these things for nothing and don’t even expect a reward for it, he-he! Well, to proceed, wit in my opinion is a splendid thing, it is, so to say, an adornment of nature and a consolation of life, and what tricks it can play! So that it sometimes is hard for a poor examining lawyer to know where he is, especially when he’s liable to be carried away by his own fancy, too, for you know he is a man after all! But the poor fellow is saved by the criminal’s temperament, worse luck for him! But young people carried away by their own wit don’t think of that ‘when they overstep all obstacles,’ as you wittily and cleverly expressed it yesterday. He will lie--that is, the man who is a _special case_, the incognito, and he will lie well, in the cleverest fashion; you might think he would triumph and enjoy the fruits of his wit, but at the most interesting, the most flagrant moment he will faint. Of course there may be illness and a stuffy room as well, but anyway! Anyway he’s given us the idea! He lied incomparably, but he didn’t reckon on his temperament. That’s what betrays him! Another time he will be carried away by his playful wit into making fun of the man who suspects him, he will turn pale as it were on purpose to mislead, but his paleness will be _too natural_, too much like the real thing, again he has given us an idea! Though his questioner may be deceived at first, he will think differently next day if he is not a fool, and, of course, it is like that at every step! He puts himself forward where he is not wanted, speaks continually when he ought to keep silent, brings in all sorts of allegorical allusions, he-he! Comes and asks why didn’t you take me long ago? he-he-he! And that can happen, you know, with the cleverest man, the psychologist, the literary man. The temperament reflects everything like a mirror! Gaze into it and admire what you see! But why are you so pale, Rodion Romanovitch? Is the room stuffy? Shall I open the window?”\n\n“Oh, don’t trouble, please,” cried Raskolnikov and he suddenly broke into a laugh. “Please don’t trouble.”\n\nPorfiry stood facing him, paused a moment and suddenly he too laughed. Raskolnikov got up from the sofa, abruptly checking his hysterical laughter.\n\n“Porfiry Petrovitch,” he began, speaking loudly and distinctly, though his legs trembled and he could scarcely stand. “I see clearly at last that you actually suspect me of murdering that old woman and her sister Lizaveta. Let me tell you for my part that I am sick of this. If you find that you have a right to prosecute me legally, to arrest me, then prosecute me, arrest me. But I will not let myself be jeered at to my face and worried...”\n\nHis lips trembled, his eyes glowed with fury and he could not restrain his voice.\n\n“I won’t allow it!” he shouted, bringing his fist down on the table. “Do you hear that, Porfiry Petrovitch? I won’t allow it.”\n\n“Good heavens! What does it mean?” cried Porfiry Petrovitch, apparently quite frightened. “Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow, what is the matter with you?”\n\n“I won’t allow it,” Raskolnikov shouted again.\n\n“Hush, my dear man! They’ll hear and come in. Just think, what could we say to them?” Porfiry Petrovitch whispered in horror, bringing his face close to Raskolnikov’s.\n\n“I won’t allow it, I won’t allow it,” Raskolnikov repeated mechanically, but he too spoke in a sudden whisper.\n\nPorfiry turned quickly and ran to open the window.\n\n“Some fresh air! And you must have some water, my dear fellow. You’re ill!” and he was running to the door to call for some when he found a decanter of water in the corner. “Come, drink a little,” he whispered, rushing up to him with the decanter. “It will be sure to do you good.”\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch’s alarm and sympathy were so natural that Raskolnikov was silent and began looking at him with wild curiosity. He did not take the water, however.\n\n“Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow, you’ll drive yourself out of your mind, I assure you, ach, ach! Have some water, do drink a little.”\n\nHe forced him to take the glass. Raskolnikov raised it mechanically to his lips, but set it on the table again with disgust.\n\n“Yes, you’ve had a little attack! You’ll bring back your illness again, my dear fellow,” Porfiry Petrovitch cackled with friendly sympathy, though he still looked rather disconcerted. “Good heavens, you must take more care of yourself! Dmitri Prokofitch was here, came to see me yesterday--I know, I know, I’ve a nasty, ironical temper, but what they made of it!... Good heavens, he came yesterday after you’d been. We dined and he talked and talked away, and I could only throw up my hands in despair! Did he come from you? But do sit down, for mercy’s sake, sit down!”\n\n“No, not from me, but I knew he went to you and why he went,” Raskolnikov answered sharply.\n“You knew?”\n\n“I knew. What of it?”\n\n“Why this, Rodion Romanovitch, that I know more than that about you; I know about everything. I know how you went _to take a flat_ at night when it was dark and how you rang the bell and asked about the blood, so that the workmen and the porter did not know what to make of it. Yes, I understand your state of mind at that time... but you’ll drive yourself mad like that, upon my word! You’ll lose your head! You’re full of generous indignation at the wrongs you’ve received, first from destiny, and then from the police officers, and so you rush from one thing to another to force them to speak out and make an end of it all, because you are sick of all this suspicion and foolishness. That’s so, isn’t it? I have guessed how you feel, haven’t I? Only in that way you’ll lose your head and Razumihin’s, too; he’s too _good_ a man for such a position, you must know that. You are ill and he is good and your illness is infectious for him... I’ll tell you about it when you are more yourself.... But do sit down, for goodness’ sake. Please rest, you look shocking, do sit down.”\n\nRaskolnikov sat down; he no longer shivered, he was hot all over. In amazement he listened with strained attention to Porfiry Petrovitch who still seemed frightened as he looked after him with friendly solicitude. But he did not believe a word he said, though he felt a strange inclination to believe. Porfiry’s unexpected words about the flat had utterly overwhelmed him. “How can it be, he knows about the flat then,” he thought suddenly, “and he tells it me himself!”\n\n“Yes, in our legal practice there was a case almost exactly similar, a case of morbid psychology,” Porfiry went on quickly. “A man confessed to murder and how he kept it up! It was a regular hallucination; he brought forward facts, he imposed upon everyone and why? He had been partly, but only partly, unintentionally the cause of a murder and when he knew that he had given the murderers the opportunity, he sank into dejection, it got on his mind and turned his brain, he began imagining things and he persuaded himself that he was the murderer. But at last the High Court of Appeal went into it and the poor fellow was acquitted and put under proper care. Thanks to the Court of Appeal! Tut-tut-tut! Why, my dear fellow, you may drive yourself into delirium if you have the impulse to work upon your nerves, to go ringing bells at night and asking about blood! I’ve studied all this morbid psychology in my practice. A man is sometimes tempted to jump out of a window or from a belfry. Just the same with bell-ringing.... It’s all illness, Rodion Romanovitch! You have begun to neglect your illness. You should consult an experienced doctor, what’s the good of that fat fellow? You are lightheaded! You were delirious when you did all this!”\n\nFor a moment Raskolnikov felt everything going round.\n\n“Is it possible, is it possible,” flashed through his mind, “that he is still lying? He can’t be, he can’t be.” He rejected that idea, feeling to what a degree of fury it might drive him, feeling that that fury might drive him mad.\n\n“I was not delirious. I knew what I was doing,” he cried, straining every faculty to penetrate Porfiry’s game, “I was quite myself, do you hear?”\n\n“Yes, I hear and understand. You said yesterday you were not delirious, you were particularly emphatic about it! I understand all you can tell me! A-ach!... Listen, Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow. If you were actually a criminal, or were somehow mixed up in this damnable business, would you insist that you were not delirious but in full possession of your faculties? And so emphatically and persistently? Would it be possible? Quite impossible, to my thinking. If you had anything on your conscience, you certainly ought to insist that you were delirious. That’s so, isn’t it?”\n\nThere was a note of slyness in this inquiry. Raskolnikov drew back on the sofa as Porfiry bent over him and stared in silent perplexity at him.\n\n“Another thing about Razumihin--you certainly ought to have said that he came of his own accord, to have concealed your part in it! But you don’t conceal it! You lay stress on his coming at your instigation.”\n\nRaskolnikov had not done so. A chill went down his back.\n\n“You keep telling lies,” he said slowly and weakly, twisting his lips into a sickly smile, “you are trying again to show that you know all my game, that you know all I shall say beforehand,” he said, conscious himself that he was not weighing his words as he ought. “You want to frighten me... or you are simply laughing at me...”\n\nHe still stared at him as he said this and again there was a light of intense hatred in his eyes.\n\n“You keep lying,” he said. “You know perfectly well that the best policy for the criminal is to tell the truth as nearly as possible... to conceal as little as possible. I don’t believe you!”\n\n“What a wily person you are!” Porfiry tittered, “there’s no catching you; you’ve a perfect monomania. So you don’t believe me? But still you do believe me, you believe a quarter; I’ll soon make you believe the whole, because I have a sincere liking for you and genuinely wish you good.”\n\nRaskolnikov’s lips trembled.\n\n“Yes, I do,” went on Porfiry, touching Raskolnikov’s arm genially, “you must take care of your illness. Besides, your mother and sister are here now; you must think of them. You must soothe and comfort them and you do nothing but frighten them...”\n\n“What has that to do with you? How do you know it? What concern is it of yours? You are keeping watch on me and want to let me know it?”\n\n“Good heavens! Why, I learnt it all from you yourself! You don’t notice that in your excitement you tell me and others everything. From Razumihin, too, I learnt a number of interesting details yesterday. No, you interrupted me, but I must tell you that, for all your wit, your suspiciousness makes you lose the common-sense view of things. To return to bell-ringing, for instance. I, an examining lawyer, have betrayed a precious thing like that, a real fact (for it is a fact worth having), and you see nothing in it! Why, if I had the slightest suspicion of you, should I have acted like that? No, I should first have disarmed your suspicions and not let you see I knew of that fact, should have diverted your attention and suddenly have dealt you a knock-down blow (your expression) saying: ‘And what were you doing, sir, pray, at ten or nearly eleven at the murdered woman’s flat and why did you ring the bell and why did you ask about blood? And why did you invite the porters to go with you to the police station, to the lieutenant?’ That’s how I ought to have acted if I had a grain of suspicion of you. I ought to have taken your evidence in due form, searched your lodging and perhaps have arrested you, too... so I have no suspicion of you, since I have not done that! But you can’t look at it normally and you see nothing, I say again.”\n\nRaskolnikov started so that Porfiry Petrovitch could not fail to perceive it.\n\n“You are lying all the while,” he cried, “I don’t know your object, but you are lying. You did not speak like that just now and I cannot be mistaken!”\n\n“I am lying?” Porfiry repeated, apparently incensed, but preserving a good-humoured and ironical face, as though he were not in the least concerned at Raskolnikov’s opinion of him. “I am lying... but how did I treat you just now, I, the examining lawyer? Prompting you and giving you every means for your defence; illness, I said, delirium, injury, melancholy and the police officers and all the rest of it? Ah! He-he-he! Though, indeed, all those psychological means of defence are not very reliable and cut both ways: illness, delirium, I don’t remember--that’s all right, but why, my good sir, in your illness and in your delirium were you haunted by just those delusions and not by any others? There may have been others, eh? He-he-he!”\n\nRaskolnikov looked haughtily and contemptuously at him.\n\n“Briefly,” he said loudly and imperiously, rising to his feet and in so doing pushing Porfiry back a little, “briefly, I want to know, do you acknowledge me perfectly free from suspicion or not? Tell me, Porfiry Petrovitch, tell me once for all and make haste!”\n\n“What a business I’m having with you!” cried Porfiry with a perfectly good-humoured, sly and composed face. “And why do you want to know, why do you want to know so much, since they haven’t begun to worry you? Why, you are like a child asking for matches! And why are you so uneasy? Why do you force yourself upon us, eh? He-he-he!”\n\n“I repeat,” Raskolnikov cried furiously, “that I can’t put up with it!”\n\n“With what? Uncertainty?” interrupted Porfiry.\n\n“Don’t jeer at me! I won’t have it! I tell you I won’t have it. I can’t and I won’t, do you hear, do you hear?” he shouted, bringing his fist down on the table again.\n\n“Hush! Hush! They’ll overhear! I warn you seriously, take care of yourself. I am not joking,” Porfiry whispered, but this time there was not the look of old womanish good nature and alarm in his face. Now he was peremptory, stern, frowning and for once laying aside all mystification.\n\nBut this was only for an instant. Raskolnikov, bewildered, suddenly fell into actual frenzy, but, strange to say, he again obeyed the command to speak quietly, though he was in a perfect paroxysm of fury.\n\n“I will not allow myself to be tortured,” he whispered, instantly recognising with hatred that he could not help obeying the command and driven to even greater fury by the thought. “Arrest me, search me, but kindly act in due form and don’t play with me! Don’t dare!”\n\n“Don’t worry about the form,” Porfiry interrupted with the same sly smile, as it were, gloating with enjoyment over Raskolnikov. “I invited you to see me quite in a friendly way.”\n\n“I don’t want your friendship and I spit on it! Do you hear? And, here, I take my cap and go. What will you say now if you mean to arrest me?”\n\nHe took up his cap and went to the door.\n\n“And won’t you see my little surprise?” chuckled Porfiry, again taking him by the arm and stopping him at the door.\n\nHe seemed to become more playful and good-humoured, which maddened Raskolnikov.\n\n“What surprise?” he asked, standing still and looking at Porfiry in alarm.\n\n“My little surprise, it’s sitting there behind the door, he-he-he!” (He pointed to the locked door.) “I locked him in that he should not escape.”\n\n“What is it? Where? What?...”\n\nRaskolnikov walked to the door and would have opened it, but it was locked.\n\n“It’s locked, here is the key!”\n\nAnd he brought a key out of his pocket.\n\n“You are lying,” roared Raskolnikov without restraint, “you lie, you damned punchinello!” and he rushed at Porfiry who retreated to the other door, not at all alarmed.\n\n“I understand it all! You are lying and mocking so that I may betray myself to you...”\n\n“Why, you could not betray yourself any further, my dear Rodion Romanovitch. You are in a passion. Don’t shout, I shall call the clerks.”\n\n“You are lying! Call the clerks! You knew I was ill and tried to work me into a frenzy to make me betray myself, that was your object! Produce your facts! I understand it all. You’ve no evidence, you have only wretched rubbishly suspicions like Zametov’s! You knew my character, you wanted to drive me to fury and then to knock me down with priests and deputies.... Are you waiting for them? eh! What are you waiting for? Where are they? Produce them?”\n\n“Why deputies, my good man? What things people will imagine! And to do so would not be acting in form as you say, you don’t know the business, my dear fellow.... And there’s no escaping form, as you see,” Porfiry muttered, listening at the door through which a noise could be heard.\n\n“Ah, they’re coming,” cried Raskolnikov. “You’ve sent for them! You expected them! Well, produce them all: your deputies, your witnesses, what you like!... I am ready!”\n\nBut at this moment a strange incident occurred, something so unexpected that neither Raskolnikov nor Porfiry Petrovitch could have looked for such a conclusion to their interview.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nWhen he remembered the scene afterwards, this is how Raskolnikov saw it.\n\nThe noise behind the door increased, and suddenly the door was opened a little.\n\n“What is it?” cried Porfiry Petrovitch, annoyed. “Why, I gave orders...”\n\nFor an instant there was no answer, but it was evident that there were several persons at the door, and that they were apparently pushing somebody back.\n\n“What is it?” Porfiry Petrovitch repeated, uneasily.\n\n“The prisoner Nikolay has been brought,” someone answered.\n\n“He is not wanted! Take him away! Let him wait! What’s he doing here? How irregular!” cried Porfiry, rushing to the door.\n\n“But he...” began the same voice, and suddenly ceased.\n\nTwo seconds, not more, were spent in actual struggle, then someone gave a violent shove, and then a man, very pale, strode into the room.\n\nThis man’s appearance was at first sight very strange. He stared straight before him, as though seeing nothing. There was a determined gleam in his eyes; at the same time there was a deathly pallor in his face, as though he were being led to the scaffold. His white lips were faintly twitching.\n\nHe was dressed like a workman and was of medium height, very young, slim, his hair cut in round crop, with thin spare features. The man whom he had thrust back followed him into the room and succeeded in seizing him by the shoulder; he was a warder; but Nikolay pulled his arm away.\n\nSeveral persons crowded inquisitively into the doorway. Some of them tried to get in. All this took place almost instantaneously.\n\n“Go away, it’s too soon! Wait till you are sent for!... Why have you brought him so soon?” Porfiry Petrovitch muttered, extremely annoyed, and as it were thrown out of his reckoning.\n\nBut Nikolay suddenly knelt down.\n\n“What’s the matter?” cried Porfiry, surprised.\n\n“I am guilty! Mine is the sin! I am the murderer,” Nikolay articulated suddenly, rather breathless, but speaking fairly loudly.\n\nFor ten seconds there was silence as though all had been struck dumb; even the warder stepped back, mechanically retreated to the door, and stood immovable.\n\n“What is it?” cried Porfiry Petrovitch, recovering from his momentary stupefaction.\n\n“I... am the murderer,” repeated Nikolay, after a brief pause.\n\n“What... you... what... whom did you kill?” Porfiry Petrovitch was obviously bewildered.\n\nNikolay again was silent for a moment.\n\n“Alyona Ivanovna and her sister Lizaveta Ivanovna, I... killed... with an axe. Darkness came over me,” he added suddenly, and was again silent.\n\nHe still remained on his knees. Porfiry Petrovitch stood for some moments as though meditating, but suddenly roused himself and waved back the uninvited spectators. They instantly vanished and closed the door. Then he looked towards Raskolnikov, who was standing in the corner, staring wildly at Nikolay and moved towards him, but stopped short, looked from Nikolay to Raskolnikov and then again at Nikolay, and seeming unable to restrain himself darted at the latter.\n\n“You’re in too great a hurry,” he shouted at him, almost angrily. “I didn’t ask you what came over you.... Speak, did you kill them?”\n\n“I am the murderer.... I want to give evidence,” Nikolay pronounced.\n\n“Ach! What did you kill them with?”\n\n“An axe. I had it ready.”\n\n“Ach, he is in a hurry! Alone?”\n\nNikolay did not understand the question.\n\n“Did you do it alone?”\n\n“Yes, alone. And Mitka is not guilty and had no share in it.”\n\n“Don’t be in a hurry about Mitka! A-ach! How was it you ran downstairs like that at the time? The porters met you both!”\n\n“It was to put them off the scent... I ran after Mitka,” Nikolay replied hurriedly, as though he had prepared the answer.\n\n“I knew it!” cried Porfiry, with vexation. “It’s not his own tale he is telling,” he muttered as though to himself, and suddenly his eyes rested on Raskolnikov again.\n\nHe was apparently so taken up with Nikolay that for a moment he had forgotten Raskolnikov. He was a little taken aback.\n\n“My dear Rodion Romanovitch, excuse me!” he flew up to him, “this won’t do; I’m afraid you must go... it’s no good your staying... I will... you see, what a surprise!... Good-bye!”\n\nAnd taking him by the arm, he showed him to the door.\n\n“I suppose you didn’t expect it?” said Raskolnikov who, though he had not yet fully grasped the situation, had regained his courage.\n\n“You did not expect it either, my friend. See how your hand is trembling! He-he!”\n\n“You’re trembling, too, Porfiry Petrovitch!”\n\n“Yes, I am; I didn’t expect it.”\n\nThey were already at the door; Porfiry was impatient for Raskolnikov to be gone.\n\n“And your little surprise, aren’t you going to show it to me?” Raskolnikov said, sarcastically.\n\n“Why, his teeth are chattering as he asks, he-he! You are an ironical person! Come, till we meet!”\n\n“I believe we can say _good-bye_!”\n\n“That’s in God’s hands,” muttered Porfiry, with an unnatural smile.\n\nAs he walked through the office, Raskolnikov noticed that many people were looking at him. Among them he saw the two porters from _the_ house, whom he had invited that night to the police station. They stood there waiting. But he was no sooner on the stairs than he heard the voice of Porfiry Petrovitch behind him. Turning round, he saw the latter running after him, out of breath.\n\n“One word, Rodion Romanovitch; as to all the rest, it’s in God’s hands, but as a matter of form there are some questions I shall have to ask you... so we shall meet again, shan’t we?”\n\nAnd Porfiry stood still, facing him with a smile.\n\n“Shan’t we?” he added again.\n\nHe seemed to want to say something more, but could not speak out.\n\n“You must forgive me, Porfiry Petrovitch, for what has just passed... I lost my temper,” began Raskolnikov, who had so far regained his courage that he felt irresistibly inclined to display his coolness.\n\n“Don’t mention it, don’t mention it,” Porfiry replied, almost gleefully. “I myself, too... I have a wicked temper, I admit it! But we shall meet again. If it’s God’s will, we may see a great deal of one another.”\n\n“And will get to know each other through and through?” added Raskolnikov.\n\n“Yes; know each other through and through,” assented Porfiry Petrovitch, and he screwed up his eyes, looking earnestly at Raskolnikov. “Now you’re going to a birthday party?”\n\n“To a funeral.”\n\n“Of course, the funeral! Take care of yourself, and get well.”\n\n“I don’t know what to wish you,” said Raskolnikov, who had begun to descend the stairs, but looked back again. “I should like to wish you success, but your office is such a comical one.”\n\n“Why comical?” Porfiry Petrovitch had turned to go, but he seemed to prick up his ears at this.\n\n“Why, how you must have been torturing and harassing that poor Nikolay psychologically, after your fashion, till he confessed! You must have been at him day and night, proving to him that he was the murderer, and now that he has confessed, you’ll begin vivisecting him again. ‘You are lying,’ you’ll say. ‘You are not the murderer! You can’t be! It’s not your own tale you are telling!’ You must admit it’s a comical business!”\n\n“He-he-he! You noticed then that I said to Nikolay just now that it was not his own tale he was telling?”\n\n“How could I help noticing it!”\n\n“He-he! You are quick-witted. You notice everything! You’ve really a playful mind! And you always fasten on the comic side... he-he! They say that was the marked characteristic of Gogol, among the writers.”\n\n“Yes, of Gogol.”\n\n“Yes, of Gogol.... I shall look forward to meeting you.”\n\n“So shall I.”\n\nRaskolnikov walked straight home. He was so muddled and bewildered that on getting home he sat for a quarter of an hour on the sofa, trying to collect his thoughts. He did not attempt to think about Nikolay; he was stupefied; he felt that his confession was something inexplicable, amazing--something beyond his understanding. But Nikolay’s confession was an actual fact. The consequences of this fact were clear to him at once, its falsehood could not fail to be discovered, and then they would be after him again. Till then, at least, he was free and must do something for himself, for the danger was imminent.\n\nBut how imminent? His position gradually became clear to him. Remembering, sketchily, the main outlines of his recent scene with Porfiry, he could not help shuddering again with horror. Of course, he did not yet know all Porfiry’s aims, he could not see into all his calculations. But he had already partly shown his hand, and no one knew better than Raskolnikov how terrible Porfiry’s “lead” had been for him. A little more and he _might_ have given himself away completely, circumstantially. Knowing his nervous temperament and from the first glance seeing through him, Porfiry, though playing a bold game, was bound to win. There’s no denying that Raskolnikov had compromised himself seriously, but no _facts_ had come to light as yet; there was nothing positive. But was he taking a true view of the position? Wasn’t he mistaken? What had Porfiry been trying to get at? Had he really some surprise prepared for him? And what was it? Had he really been expecting something or not? How would they have parted if it had not been for the unexpected appearance of Nikolay?\n\nPorfiry had shown almost all his cards--of course, he had risked something in showing them--and if he had really had anything up his sleeve (Raskolnikov reflected), he would have shown that, too. What was that “surprise”? Was it a joke? Had it meant anything? Could it have concealed anything like a fact, a piece of positive evidence? His yesterday’s visitor? What had become of him? Where was he to-day? If Porfiry really had any evidence, it must be connected with him....\n\nHe sat on the sofa with his elbows on his knees and his face hidden in his hands. He was still shivering nervously. At last he got up, took his cap, thought a minute, and went to the door.\n\nHe had a sort of presentiment that for to-day, at least, he might consider himself out of danger. He had a sudden sense almost of joy; he wanted to make haste to Katerina Ivanovna’s. He would be too late for the funeral, of course, but he would be in time for the memorial dinner, and there at once he would see Sonia.\n\nHe stood still, thought a moment, and a suffering smile came for a moment on to his lips.\n\n“To-day! To-day,” he repeated to himself. “Yes, to-day! So it must be....”\n\nBut as he was about to open the door, it began opening of itself. He started and moved back. The door opened gently and slowly, and there suddenly appeared a figure--yesterday’s visitor _from underground_.\n\nThe man stood in the doorway, looked at Raskolnikov without speaking, and took a step forward into the room. He was exactly the same as yesterday; the same figure, the same dress, but there was a great change in his face; he looked dejected and sighed deeply. If he had only put his hand up to his cheek and leaned his head on one side he would have looked exactly like a peasant woman.\n\n“What do you want?” asked Raskolnikov, numb with terror. The man was still silent, but suddenly he bowed down almost to the ground, touching it with his finger.\n\n“What is it?” cried Raskolnikov.\n\n“I have sinned,” the man articulated softly.\n\n“How?”\n\n“By evil thoughts.”\n\nThey looked at one another.\n\n“I was vexed. When you came, perhaps in drink, and bade the porters go to the police station and asked about the blood, I was vexed that they let you go and took you for drunken. I was so vexed that I lost my sleep. And remembering the address we came here yesterday and asked for you....”\n\n“Who came?” Raskolnikov interrupted, instantly beginning to recollect.\n\n“I did, I’ve wronged you.”\n\n“Then you come from that house?”\n\n“I was standing at the gate with them... don’t you remember? We have carried on our trade in that house for years past. We cure and prepare hides, we take work home... most of all I was vexed....”\n\nAnd the whole scene of the day before yesterday in the gateway came clearly before Raskolnikov’s mind; he recollected that there had been several people there besides the porters, women among them. He remembered one voice had suggested taking him straight to the police-station. He could not recall the face of the speaker, and even now he did not recognise it, but he remembered that he had turned round and made him some answer....\n\nSo this was the solution of yesterday’s horror. The most awful thought was that he had been actually almost lost, had almost done for himself on account of such a _trivial_ circumstance. So this man could tell nothing except his asking about the flat and the blood stains. So Porfiry, too, had nothing but that _delirium_, no facts but this _psychology_ which _cuts both ways_, nothing positive. So if no more facts come to light (and they must not, they must not!) then... then what can they do to him? How can they convict him, even if they arrest him? And Porfiry then had only just heard about the flat and had not known about it before.\n\n“Was it you who told Porfiry... that I’d been there?” he cried, struck by a sudden idea.\n\n“What Porfiry?”\n\n“The head of the detective department?”\n\n“Yes. The porters did not go there, but I went.”\n\n“To-day?”\n\n“I got there two minutes before you. And I heard, I heard it all, how he worried you.”\n\n“Where? What? When?”\n\n“Why, in the next room. I was sitting there all the time.”\n\n“What? Why, then you were the surprise? But how could it happen? Upon my word!”\n\n“I saw that the porters did not want to do what I said,” began the man; “for it’s too late, said they, and maybe he’ll be angry that we did not come at the time. I was vexed and I lost my sleep, and I began making inquiries. And finding out yesterday where to go, I went to-day. The first time I went he wasn’t there, when I came an hour later he couldn’t see me. I went the third time, and they showed me in. I informed him of everything, just as it happened, and he began skipping about the room and punching himself on the chest. ‘What do you scoundrels mean by it? If I’d known about it I should have arrested him!’ Then he ran out, called somebody and began talking to him in the corner, then he turned to me, scolding and questioning me. He scolded me a great deal; and I told him everything, and I told him that you didn’t dare to say a word in answer to me yesterday and that you didn’t recognise me. And he fell to running about again and kept hitting himself on the chest, and getting angry and running about, and when you were announced he told me to go into the next room. ‘Sit there a bit,’ he said. ‘Don’t move, whatever you may hear.’ And he set a chair there for me and locked me in. ‘Perhaps,’ he said, ‘I may call you.’ And when Nikolay’d been brought he let me out as soon as you were gone. ‘I shall send for you again and question you,’ he said.”\n\n“And did he question Nikolay while you were there?”\n\n“He got rid of me as he did of you, before he spoke to Nikolay.”\n\nThe man stood still, and again suddenly bowed down, touching the ground with his finger.\n\n“Forgive me for my evil thoughts, and my slander.”\n\n“May God forgive you,” answered Raskolnikov.\n\nAnd as he said this, the man bowed down again, but not to the ground, turned slowly and went out of the room.\n\n“It all cuts both ways, now it all cuts both ways,” repeated Raskolnikov, and he went out more confident than ever.\n\n“Now we’ll make a fight for it,” he said, with a malicious smile, as he went down the stairs. His malice was aimed at himself; with shame and contempt he recollected his “cowardice.”\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nThe morning that followed the fateful interview with Dounia and her mother brought sobering influences to bear on Pyotr Petrovitch. Intensely unpleasant as it was, he was forced little by little to accept as a fact beyond recall what had seemed to him only the day before fantastic and incredible. The black snake of wounded vanity had been gnawing at his heart all night. When he got out of bed, Pyotr Petrovitch immediately looked in the looking-glass. He was afraid that he had jaundice. However his health seemed unimpaired so far, and looking at his noble, clear-skinned countenance which had grown fattish of late, Pyotr Petrovitch for an instant was positively comforted in the conviction that he would find another bride and, perhaps, even a better one. But coming back to the sense of his present position, he turned aside and spat vigorously, which excited a sarcastic smile in Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, the young friend with whom he was staying. That smile Pyotr Petrovitch noticed, and at once set it down against his young friend’s account. He had set down a good many points against him of late. His anger was redoubled when he reflected that he ought not to have told Andrey Semyonovitch about the result of yesterday’s interview. That was the second mistake he had made in temper, through impulsiveness and irritability.... Moreover, all that morning one unpleasantness followed another. He even found a hitch awaiting him in his legal case in the senate. He was particularly irritated by the owner of the flat which had been taken in view of his approaching marriage and was being redecorated at his own expense; the owner, a rich German tradesman, would not entertain the idea of breaking the contract which had just been signed and insisted on the full forfeit money, though Pyotr Petrovitch would be giving him back the flat practically redecorated. In the same way the upholsterers refused to return a single rouble of the instalment paid for the furniture purchased but not yet removed to the flat.\n\n“Am I to get married simply for the sake of the furniture?” Pyotr Petrovitch ground his teeth and at the same time once more he had a gleam of desperate hope. “Can all that be really so irrevocably over? Is it no use to make another effort?” The thought of Dounia sent a voluptuous pang through his heart. He endured anguish at that moment, and if it had been possible to slay Raskolnikov instantly by wishing it, Pyotr Petrovitch would promptly have uttered the wish.\n\n“It was my mistake, too, not to have given them money,” he thought, as he returned dejectedly to Lebeziatnikov’s room, “and why on earth was I such a Jew? It was false economy! I meant to keep them without a penny so that they should turn to me as their providence, and look at them! foo! If I’d spent some fifteen hundred roubles on them for the trousseau and presents, on knick-knacks, dressing-cases, jewellery, materials, and all that sort of trash from Knopp’s and the English shop, my position would have been better and... stronger! They could not have refused me so easily! They are the sort of people that would feel bound to return money and presents if they broke it off; and they would find it hard to do it! And their conscience would prick them: how can we dismiss a man who has hitherto been so generous and delicate?.... H’m! I’ve made a blunder.”\n\nAnd grinding his teeth again, Pyotr Petrovitch called himself a fool--but not aloud, of course.\n\nHe returned home, twice as irritated and angry as before. The preparations for the funeral dinner at Katerina Ivanovna’s excited his curiosity as he passed. He had heard about it the day before; he fancied, indeed, that he had been invited, but absorbed in his own cares he had paid no attention. Inquiring of Madame Lippevechsel who was busy laying the table while Katerina Ivanovna was away at the cemetery, he heard that the entertainment was to be a great affair, that all the lodgers had been invited, among them some who had not known the dead man, that even Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov was invited in spite of his previous quarrel with Katerina Ivanovna, that he, Pyotr Petrovitch, was not only invited, but was eagerly expected as he was the most important of the lodgers. Amalia Ivanovna herself had been invited with great ceremony in spite of the recent unpleasantness, and so she was very busy with preparations and was taking a positive pleasure in them; she was moreover dressed up to the nines, all in new black silk, and she was proud of it. All this suggested an idea to Pyotr Petrovitch and he went into his room, or rather Lebeziatnikov’s, somewhat thoughtful. He had learnt that Raskolnikov was to be one of the guests.\n\nAndrey Semyonovitch had been at home all the morning. The attitude of Pyotr Petrovitch to this gentleman was strange, though perhaps natural. Pyotr Petrovitch had despised and hated him from the day he came to stay with him and at the same time he seemed somewhat afraid of him. He had not come to stay with him on his arrival in Petersburg simply from parsimony, though that had been perhaps his chief object. He had heard of Andrey Semyonovitch, who had once been his ward, as a leading young progressive who was taking an important part in certain interesting circles, the doings of which were a legend in the provinces. It had impressed Pyotr Petrovitch. These powerful omniscient circles who despised everyone and showed everyone up had long inspired in him a peculiar but quite vague alarm. He had not, of course, been able to form even an approximate notion of what they meant. He, like everyone, had heard that there were, especially in Petersburg, progressives of some sort, nihilists and so on, and, like many people, he exaggerated and distorted the significance of those words to an absurd degree. What for many years past he had feared more than anything was _being shown up_ and this was the chief ground for his continual uneasiness at the thought of transferring his business to Petersburg. He was afraid of this as little children are sometimes panic-stricken. Some years before, when he was just entering on his own career, he had come upon two cases in which rather important personages in the province, patrons of his, had been cruelly shown up. One instance had ended in great scandal for the person attacked and the other had very nearly ended in serious trouble. For this reason Pyotr Petrovitch intended to go into the subject as soon as he reached Petersburg and, if necessary, to anticipate contingencies by seeking the favour of “our younger generation.” He relied on Andrey Semyonovitch for this and before his visit to Raskolnikov he had succeeded in picking up some current phrases. He soon discovered that Andrey Semyonovitch was a commonplace simpleton, but that by no means reassured Pyotr Petrovitch. Even if he had been certain that all the progressives were fools like him, it would not have allayed his uneasiness. All the doctrines, the ideas, the systems, with which Andrey Semyonovitch pestered him had no interest for him. He had his own object--he simply wanted to find out at once what was happening _here_. Had these people any power or not? Had he anything to fear from them? Would they expose any enterprise of his? And what precisely was now the object of their attacks? Could he somehow make up to them and get round them if they really were powerful? Was this the thing to do or not? Couldn’t he gain something through them? In fact hundreds of questions presented themselves.\n\nAndrey Semyonovitch was an anæmic, scrofulous little man, with strangely flaxen mutton-chop whiskers of which he was very proud. He was a clerk and had almost always something wrong with his eyes. He was rather soft-hearted, but self-confident and sometimes extremely conceited in speech, which had an absurd effect, incongruous with his little figure. He was one of the lodgers most respected by Amalia Ivanovna, for he did not get drunk and paid regularly for his lodgings. Andrey Semyonovitch really was rather stupid; he attached himself to the cause of progress and “our younger generation” from enthusiasm. He was one of the numerous and varied legion of dullards, of half-animate abortions, conceited, half-educated coxcombs, who attach themselves to the idea most in fashion only to vulgarise it and who caricature every cause they serve, however sincerely.\n\nThough Lebeziatnikov was so good-natured, he, too, was beginning to dislike Pyotr Petrovitch. This happened on both sides unconsciously. However simple Andrey Semyonovitch might be, he began to see that Pyotr Petrovitch was duping him and secretly despising him, and that “he was not the right sort of man.” He had tried expounding to him the system of Fourier and the Darwinian theory, but of late Pyotr Petrovitch began to listen too sarcastically and even to be rude. The fact was he had begun instinctively to guess that Lebeziatnikov was not merely a commonplace simpleton, but, perhaps, a liar, too, and that he had no connections of any consequence even in his own circle, but had simply picked things up third-hand; and that very likely he did not even know much about his own work of propaganda, for he was in too great a muddle. A fine person he would be to show anyone up! It must be noted, by the way, that Pyotr Petrovitch had during those ten days eagerly accepted the strangest praise from Andrey Semyonovitch; he had not protested, for instance, when Andrey Semyonovitch belauded him for being ready to contribute to the establishment of the new “commune,” or to abstain from christening his future children, or to acquiesce if Dounia were to take a lover a month after marriage, and so on. Pyotr Petrovitch so enjoyed hearing his own praises that he did not disdain even such virtues when they were attributed to him.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch had had occasion that morning to realise some five-per-cent bonds and now he sat down to the table and counted over bundles of notes. Andrey Semyonovitch who hardly ever had any money walked about the room pretending to himself to look at all those bank notes with indifference and even contempt. Nothing would have convinced Pyotr Petrovitch that Andrey Semyonovitch could really look on the money unmoved, and the latter, on his side, kept thinking bitterly that Pyotr Petrovitch was capable of entertaining such an idea about him and was, perhaps, glad of the opportunity of teasing his young friend by reminding him of his inferiority and the great difference between them.\n\nHe found him incredibly inattentive and irritable, though he, Andrey Semyonovitch, began enlarging on his favourite subject, the foundation of a new special “commune.” The brief remarks that dropped from Pyotr Petrovitch between the clicking of the beads on the reckoning frame betrayed unmistakable and discourteous irony. But the “humane” Andrey Semyonovitch ascribed Pyotr Petrovitch’s ill-humour to his recent breach with Dounia and he was burning with impatience to discourse on that theme. He had something progressive to say on the subject which might console his worthy friend and “could not fail” to promote his development.\n\n“There is some sort of festivity being prepared at that... at the widow’s, isn’t there?” Pyotr Petrovitch asked suddenly, interrupting Andrey Semyonovitch at the most interesting passage.\n\n“Why, don’t you know? Why, I was telling you last night what I think about all such ceremonies. And she invited you too, I heard. You were talking to her yesterday...”\n\n“I should never have expected that beggarly fool would have spent on this feast all the money she got from that other fool, Raskolnikov. I was surprised just now as I came through at the preparations there, the wines! Several people are invited. It’s beyond everything!” continued Pyotr Petrovitch, who seemed to have some object in pursuing the conversation. “What? You say I am asked too? When was that? I don’t remember. But I shan’t go. Why should I? I only said a word to her in passing yesterday of the possibility of her obtaining a year’s salary as a destitute widow of a government clerk. I suppose she has invited me on that account, hasn’t she? He-he-he!”\n\n“I don’t intend to go either,” said Lebeziatnikov.\n\n“I should think not, after giving her a thrashing! You might well hesitate, he-he!”\n\n“Who thrashed? Whom?” cried Lebeziatnikov, flustered and blushing.\n\n“Why, you thrashed Katerina Ivanovna a month ago. I heard so yesterday... so that’s what your convictions amount to... and the woman question, too, wasn’t quite sound, he-he-he!” and Pyotr Petrovitch, as though comforted, went back to clicking his beads.\n\n“It’s all slander and nonsense!” cried Lebeziatnikov, who was always afraid of allusions to the subject. “It was not like that at all, it was quite different. You’ve heard it wrong; it’s a libel. I was simply defending myself. She rushed at me first with her nails, she pulled out all my whiskers.... It’s permissable for anyone, I should hope, to defend himself and I never allow anyone to use violence to me on principle, for it’s an act of despotism. What was I to do? I simply pushed her back.”\n\n“He-he-he!” Luzhin went on laughing maliciously.\n\n“You keep on like that because you are out of humour yourself.... But that’s nonsense and it has nothing, nothing whatever to do with the woman question! You don’t understand; I used to think, indeed, that if women are equal to men in all respects, even in strength (as is maintained now) there ought to be equality in that, too. Of course, I reflected afterwards that such a question ought not really to arise, for there ought not to be fighting and in the future society fighting is unthinkable... and that it would be a queer thing to seek for equality in fighting. I am not so stupid... though, of course, there is fighting... there won’t be later, but at present there is... confound it! How muddled one gets with you! It’s not on that account that I am not going. I am not going on principle, not to take part in the revolting convention of memorial dinners, that’s why! Though, of course, one might go to laugh at it.... I am sorry there won’t be any priests at it. I should certainly go if there were.”\n\n“Then you would sit down at another man’s table and insult it and those who invited you. Eh?”\n\n“Certainly not insult, but protest. I should do it with a good object. I might indirectly assist the cause of enlightenment and propaganda. It’s a duty of every man to work for enlightenment and propaganda and the more harshly, perhaps, the better. I might drop a seed, an idea.... And something might grow up from that seed. How should I be insulting them? They might be offended at first, but afterwards they’d see I’d done them a service. You know, Terebyeva (who is in the community now) was blamed because when she left her family and... devoted... herself, she wrote to her father and mother that she wouldn’t go on living conventionally and was entering on a free marriage and it was said that that was too harsh, that she might have spared them and have written more kindly. I think that’s all nonsense and there’s no need of softness; on the contrary, what’s wanted is protest. Varents had been married seven years, she abandoned her two children, she told her husband straight out in a letter: ‘I have realised that I cannot be happy with you. I can never forgive you that you have deceived me by concealing from me that there is another organisation of society by means of the communities. I have only lately learned it from a great-hearted man to whom I have given myself and with whom I am establishing a community. I speak plainly because I consider it dishonest to deceive you. Do as you think best. Do not hope to get me back, you are too late. I hope you will be happy.’ That’s how letters like that ought to be written!”\n\n“Is that Terebyeva the one you said had made a third free marriage?”\n\n“No, it’s only the second, really! But what if it were the fourth, what if it were the fifteenth, that’s all nonsense! And if ever I regretted the death of my father and mother, it is now, and I sometimes think if my parents were living what a protest I would have aimed at them! I would have done something on purpose... I would have shown them! I would have astonished them! I am really sorry there is no one!”\n\n“To surprise! He-he! Well, be that as you will,” Pyotr Petrovitch interrupted, “but tell me this; do you know the dead man’s daughter, the delicate-looking little thing? It’s true what they say about her, isn’t it?”\n\n“What of it? I think, that is, it is my own personal conviction that this is the normal condition of women. Why not? I mean, _distinguons_. In our present society it is not altogether normal, because it is compulsory, but in the future society it will be perfectly normal, because it will be voluntary. Even as it is, she was quite right: she was suffering and that was her asset, so to speak, her capital which she had a perfect right to dispose of. Of course, in the future society there will be no need of assets, but her part will have another significance, rational and in harmony with her environment. As to Sofya Semyonovna personally, I regard her action as a vigorous protest against the organisation of society, and I respect her deeply for it; I rejoice indeed when I look at her!”\n\n“I was told that you got her turned out of these lodgings.”\n\nLebeziatnikov was enraged.\n\n“That’s another slander,” he yelled. “It was not so at all! That was all Katerina Ivanovna’s invention, for she did not understand! And I never made love to Sofya Semyonovna! I was simply developing her, entirely disinterestedly, trying to rouse her to protest.... All I wanted was her protest and Sofya Semyonovna could not have remained here anyway!”\n\n“Have you asked her to join your community?”\n\n“You keep on laughing and very inappropriately, allow me to tell you. You don’t understand! There is no such rôle in a community. The community is established that there should be no such rôles. In a community, such a rôle is essentially transformed and what is stupid here is sensible there, what, under present conditions, is unnatural becomes perfectly natural in the community. It all depends on the environment. It’s all the environment and man himself is nothing. And I am on good terms with Sofya Semyonovna to this day, which is a proof that she never regarded me as having wronged her. I am trying now to attract her to the community, but on quite, quite a different footing. What are you laughing at? We are trying to establish a community of our own, a special one, on a broader basis. We have gone further in our convictions. We reject more! And meanwhile I’m still developing Sofya Semyonovna. She has a beautiful, beautiful character!”\n\n“And you take advantage of her fine character, eh? He-he!”\n\n“No, no! Oh, no! On the contrary.”\n\n“Oh, on the contrary! He-he-he! A queer thing to say!”\n\n“Believe me! Why should I disguise it? In fact, I feel it strange myself how timid, chaste and modern she is with me!”\n\n“And you, of course, are developing her... he-he! trying to prove to her that all that modesty is nonsense?”\n\n“Not at all, not at all! How coarsely, how stupidly--excuse me saying so--you misunderstand the word development! Good heavens, how... crude you still are! We are striving for the freedom of women and you have only one idea in your head.... Setting aside the general question of chastity and feminine modesty as useless in themselves and indeed prejudices, I fully accept her chastity with me, because that’s for her to decide. Of course if she were to tell me herself that she wanted me, I should think myself very lucky, because I like the girl very much; but as it is, no one has ever treated her more courteously than I, with more respect for her dignity... I wait in hopes, that’s all!”\n\n“You had much better make her a present of something. I bet you never thought of that.”\n\n“You don’t understand, as I’ve told you already! Of course, she is in such a position, but it’s another question. Quite another question! You simply despise her. Seeing a fact which you mistakenly consider deserving of contempt, you refuse to take a humane view of a fellow creature. You don’t know what a character she is! I am only sorry that of late she has quite given up reading and borrowing books. I used to lend them to her. I am sorry, too, that with all the energy and resolution in protesting--which she has already shown once--she has little self-reliance, little, so to say, independence, so as to break free from certain prejudices and certain foolish ideas. Yet she thoroughly understands some questions, for instance about kissing of hands, that is, that it’s an insult to a woman for a man to kiss her hand, because it’s a sign of inequality. We had a debate about it and I described it to her. She listened attentively to an account of the workmen’s associations in France, too. Now I am explaining the question of coming into the room in the future society.”\n\n“And what’s that, pray?”\n\n“We had a debate lately on the question: Has a member of the community the right to enter another member’s room, whether man or woman, at any time... and we decided that he has!”\n\n“It might be at an inconvenient moment, he-he!”\n\nLebeziatnikov was really angry.\n\n“You are always thinking of something unpleasant,” he cried with aversion. “Tfoo! How vexed I am that when I was expounding our system, I referred prematurely to the question of personal privacy! It’s always a stumbling-block to people like you, they turn it into ridicule before they understand it. And how proud they are of it, too! Tfoo! I’ve often maintained that that question should not be approached by a novice till he has a firm faith in the system. And tell me, please, what do you find so shameful even in cesspools? I should be the first to be ready to clean out any cesspool you like. And it’s not a question of self-sacrifice, it’s simply work, honourable, useful work which is as good as any other and much better than the work of a Raphael and a Pushkin, because it is more useful.”\n\n“And more honourable, more honourable, he-he-he!”\n\n“What do you mean by ‘more honourable’? I don’t understand such expressions to describe human activity. ‘More honourable,’ ‘nobler’--all those are old-fashioned prejudices which I reject. Everything which is _of use_ to mankind is honourable. I only understand one word: _useful_! You can snigger as much as you like, but that’s so!”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch laughed heartily. He had finished counting the money and was putting it away. But some of the notes he left on the table. The “cesspool question” had already been a subject of dispute between them. What was absurd was that it made Lebeziatnikov really angry, while it amused Luzhin and at that moment he particularly wanted to anger his young friend.\n\n“It’s your ill-luck yesterday that makes you so ill-humoured and annoying,” blurted out Lebeziatnikov, who in spite of his “independence” and his “protests” did not venture to oppose Pyotr Petrovitch and still behaved to him with some of the respect habitual in earlier years.\n\n“You’d better tell me this,” Pyotr Petrovitch interrupted with haughty displeasure, “can you... or rather are you really friendly enough with that young person to ask her to step in here for a minute? I think they’ve all come back from the cemetery... I heard the sound of steps... I want to see her, that young person.”\n\n“What for?” Lebeziatnikov asked with surprise.\n\n“Oh, I want to. I am leaving here to-day or to-morrow and therefore I wanted to speak to her about... However, you may be present during the interview. It’s better you should be, indeed. For there’s no knowing what you might imagine.”\n\n“I shan’t imagine anything. I only asked and, if you’ve anything to say to her, nothing is easier than to call her in. I’ll go directly and you may be sure I won’t be in your way.”\n\nFive minutes later Lebeziatnikov came in with Sonia. She came in very much surprised and overcome with shyness as usual. She was always shy in such circumstances and was always afraid of new people, she had been as a child and was even more so now.... Pyotr Petrovitch met her “politely and affably,” but with a certain shade of bantering familiarity which in his opinion was suitable for a man of his respectability and weight in dealing with a creature so young and so _interesting_ as she. He hastened to “reassure” her and made her sit down facing him at the table. Sonia sat down, looked about her--at Lebeziatnikov, at the notes lying on the table and then again at Pyotr Petrovitch and her eyes remained riveted on him. Lebeziatnikov was moving to the door. Pyotr Petrovitch signed to Sonia to remain seated and stopped Lebeziatnikov.\n\n“Is Raskolnikov in there? Has he come?” he asked him in a whisper.\n\n“Raskolnikov? Yes. Why? Yes, he is there. I saw him just come in.... Why?”\n\n“Well, I particularly beg you to remain here with us and not to leave me alone with this... young woman. I only want a few words with her, but God knows what they may make of it. I shouldn’t like Raskolnikov to repeat anything.... You understand what I mean?”\n\n“I understand!” Lebeziatnikov saw the point. “Yes, you are right.... Of course, I am convinced personally that you have no reason to be uneasy, but... still, you are right. Certainly I’ll stay. I’ll stand here at the window and not be in your way... I think you are right...”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch returned to the sofa, sat down opposite Sonia, looked attentively at her and assumed an extremely dignified, even severe expression, as much as to say, “don’t you make any mistake, madam.” Sonia was overwhelmed with embarrassment.\n\n“In the first place, Sofya Semyonovna, will you make my excuses to your respected mamma.... That’s right, isn’t it? Katerina Ivanovna stands in the place of a mother to you?” Pyotr Petrovitch began with great dignity, though affably.\n\nIt was evident that his intentions were friendly.\n\n“Quite so, yes; the place of a mother,” Sonia answered, timidly and hurriedly.\n\n“Then will you make my apologies to her? Through inevitable circumstances I am forced to be absent and shall not be at the dinner in spite of your mamma’s kind invitation.”\n\n“Yes... I’ll tell her... at once.”\n\nAnd Sonia hastily jumped up from her seat.\n\n“Wait, that’s not all,” Pyotr Petrovitch detained her, smiling at her simplicity and ignorance of good manners, “and you know me little, my dear Sofya Semyonovna, if you suppose I would have ventured to trouble a person like you for a matter of so little consequence affecting myself only. I have another object.”\n\nSonia sat down hurriedly. Her eyes rested again for an instant on the grey-and-rainbow-coloured notes that remained on the table, but she quickly looked away and fixed her eyes on Pyotr Petrovitch. She felt it horribly indecorous, especially for _her_, to look at another person’s money. She stared at the gold eye-glass which Pyotr Petrovitch held in his left hand and at the massive and extremely handsome ring with a yellow stone on his middle finger. But suddenly she looked away and, not knowing where to turn, ended by staring Pyotr Petrovitch again straight in the face. After a pause of still greater dignity he continued.\n\n“I chanced yesterday in passing to exchange a couple of words with Katerina Ivanovna, poor woman. That was sufficient to enable me to ascertain that she is in a position--preternatural, if one may so express it.”\n\n“Yes... preternatural...” Sonia hurriedly assented.\n\n“Or it would be simpler and more comprehensible to say, ill.”\n\n“Yes, simpler and more comprehen... yes, ill.”\n\n“Quite so. So then from a feeling of humanity and so to speak compassion, I should be glad to be of service to her in any way, foreseeing her unfortunate position. I believe the whole of this poverty-stricken family depends now entirely on you?”\n\n“Allow me to ask,” Sonia rose to her feet, “did you say something to her yesterday of the possibility of a pension? Because she told me you had undertaken to get her one. Was that true?”\n\n“Not in the slightest, and indeed it’s an absurdity! I merely hinted at her obtaining temporary assistance as the widow of an official who had died in the service--if only she has patronage... but apparently your late parent had not served his full term and had not indeed been in the service at all of late. In fact, if there could be any hope, it would be very ephemeral, because there would be no claim for assistance in that case, far from it.... And she is dreaming of a pension already, he-he-he!... A go-ahead lady!”\n\n“Yes, she is. For she is credulous and good-hearted, and she believes everything from the goodness of her heart and... and... and she is like that... yes... You must excuse her,” said Sonia, and again she got up to go.\n\n“But you haven’t heard what I have to say.”\n\n“No, I haven’t heard,” muttered Sonia.\n\n“Then sit down.” She was terribly confused; she sat down again a third time.\n\n“Seeing her position with her unfortunate little ones, I should be glad, as I have said before, so far as lies in my power, to be of service, that is, so far as is in my power, not more. One might for instance get up a subscription for her, or a lottery, something of the sort, such as is always arranged in such cases by friends or even outsiders desirous of assisting people. It was of that I intended to speak to you; it might be done.”\n\n“Yes, yes... God will repay you for it,” faltered Sonia, gazing intently at Pyotr Petrovitch.\n\n“It might be, but we will talk of it later. We might begin it to-day, we will talk it over this evening and lay the foundation so to speak. Come to me at seven o’clock. Mr. Lebeziatnikov, I hope, will assist us. But there is one circumstance of which I ought to warn you beforehand and for which I venture to trouble you, Sofya Semyonovna, to come here. In my opinion money cannot be, indeed it’s unsafe to put it into Katerina Ivanovna’s own hands. The dinner to-day is a proof of that. Though she has not, so to speak, a crust of bread for to-morrow and... well, boots or shoes, or anything; she has bought to-day Jamaica rum, and even, I believe, Madeira and... and coffee. I saw it as I passed through. To-morrow it will all fall upon you again, they won’t have a crust of bread. It’s absurd, really, and so, to my thinking, a subscription ought to be raised so that the unhappy widow should not know of the money, but only you, for instance. Am I right?”\n\n“I don’t know... this is only to-day, once in her life.... She was so anxious to do honour, to celebrate the memory.... And she is very sensible... but just as you think and I shall be very, very... they will all be... and God will reward... and the orphans...”\n\nSonia burst into tears.\n\n“Very well, then, keep it in mind; and now will you accept for the benefit of your relation the small sum that I am able to spare, from me personally. I am very anxious that my name should not be mentioned in connection with it. Here... having so to speak anxieties of my own, I cannot do more...”\n\nAnd Pyotr Petrovitch held out to Sonia a ten-rouble note carefully unfolded. Sonia took it, flushed crimson, jumped up, muttered something and began taking leave. Pyotr Petrovitch accompanied her ceremoniously to the door. She got out of the room at last, agitated and distressed, and returned to Katerina Ivanovna, overwhelmed with confusion.\n\nAll this time Lebeziatnikov had stood at the window or walked about the room, anxious not to interrupt the conversation; when Sonia had gone he walked up to Pyotr Petrovitch and solemnly held out his hand.\n\n“I heard and _saw_ everything,” he said, laying stress on the last verb. “That is honourable, I mean to say, it’s humane! You wanted to avoid gratitude, I saw! And although I cannot, I confess, in principle sympathise with private charity, for it not only fails to eradicate the evil but even promotes it, yet I must admit that I saw your action with pleasure--yes, yes, I like it.”\n\n“That’s all nonsense,” muttered Pyotr Petrovitch, somewhat disconcerted, looking carefully at Lebeziatnikov.\n\n“No, it’s not nonsense! A man who has suffered distress and annoyance as you did yesterday and who yet can sympathise with the misery of others, such a man... even though he is making a social mistake--is still deserving of respect! I did not expect it indeed of you, Pyotr Petrovitch, especially as according to your ideas... oh, what a drawback your ideas are to you! How distressed you are for instance by your ill-luck yesterday,” cried the simple-hearted Lebeziatnikov, who felt a return of affection for Pyotr Petrovitch. “And, what do you want with marriage, with _legal_ marriage, my dear, noble Pyotr Petrovitch? Why do you cling to this _legality_ of marriage? Well, you may beat me if you like, but I am glad, positively glad it hasn’t come off, that you are free, that you are not quite lost for humanity.... you see, I’ve spoken my mind!”\n\n“Because I don’t want in your free marriage to be made a fool of and to bring up another man’s children, that’s why I want legal marriage,” Luzhin replied in order to make some answer.\n\nHe seemed preoccupied by something.\n\n“Children? You referred to children,” Lebeziatnikov started off like a warhorse at the trumpet call. “Children are a social question and a question of first importance, I agree; but the question of children has another solution. Some refuse to have children altogether, because they suggest the institution of the family. We’ll speak of children later, but now as to the question of honour, I confess that’s my weak point. That horrid, military, Pushkin expression is unthinkable in the dictionary of the future. What does it mean indeed? It’s nonsense, there will be no deception in a free marriage! That is only the natural consequence of a legal marriage, so to say, its corrective, a protest. So that indeed it’s not humiliating... and if I ever, to suppose an absurdity, were to be legally married, I should be positively glad of it. I should say to my wife: ‘My dear, hitherto I have loved you, now I respect you, for you’ve shown you can protest!’ You laugh! That’s because you are incapable of getting away from prejudices. Confound it all! I understand now where the unpleasantness is of being deceived in a legal marriage, but it’s simply a despicable consequence of a despicable position in which both are humiliated. When the deception is open, as in a free marriage, then it does not exist, it’s unthinkable. Your wife will only prove how she respects you by considering you incapable of opposing her happiness and avenging yourself on her for her new husband. Damn it all! I sometimes dream if I were to be married, pfoo! I mean if I were to marry, legally or not, it’s just the same, I should present my wife with a lover if she had not found one for herself. ‘My dear,’ I should say, ‘I love you, but even more than that I desire you to respect me. See!’ Am I not right?”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch sniggered as he listened, but without much merriment. He hardly heard it indeed. He was preoccupied with something else and even Lebeziatnikov at last noticed it. Pyotr Petrovitch seemed excited and rubbed his hands. Lebeziatnikov remembered all this and reflected upon it afterwards.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nIt would be difficult to explain exactly what could have originated the idea of that senseless dinner in Katerina Ivanovna’s disordered brain. Nearly ten of the twenty roubles, given by Raskolnikov for Marmeladov’s funeral, were wasted upon it. Possibly Katerina Ivanovna felt obliged to honour the memory of the deceased “suitably,” that all the lodgers, and still more Amalia Ivanovna, might know “that he was in no way their inferior, and perhaps very much their superior,” and that no one had the right “to turn up his nose at him.” Perhaps the chief element was that peculiar “poor man’s pride,” which compels many poor people to spend their last savings on some traditional social ceremony, simply in order to do “like other people,” and not to “be looked down upon.” It is very probable, too, that Katerina Ivanovna longed on this occasion, at the moment when she seemed to be abandoned by everyone, to show those “wretched contemptible lodgers” that she knew “how to do things, how to entertain” and that she had been brought up “in a genteel, she might almost say aristocratic colonel’s family” and had not been meant for sweeping floors and washing the children’s rags at night. Even the poorest and most broken-spirited people are sometimes liable to these paroxysms of pride and vanity which take the form of an irresistible nervous craving. And Katerina Ivanovna was not broken-spirited; she might have been killed by circumstance, but her spirit could not have been broken, that is, she could not have been intimidated, her will could not be crushed. Moreover Sonia had said with good reason that her mind was unhinged. She could not be said to be insane, but for a year past she had been so harassed that her mind might well be overstrained. The later stages of consumption are apt, doctors tell us, to affect the intellect.\n\nThere was no great variety of wines, nor was there Madeira; but wine there was. There was vodka, rum and Lisbon wine, all of the poorest quality but in sufficient quantity. Besides the traditional rice and honey, there were three or four dishes, one of which consisted of pancakes, all prepared in Amalia Ivanovna’s kitchen. Two samovars were boiling, that tea and punch might be offered after dinner. Katerina Ivanovna had herself seen to purchasing the provisions, with the help of one of the lodgers, an unfortunate little Pole who had somehow been stranded at Madame Lippevechsel’s. He promptly put himself at Katerina Ivanovna’s disposal and had been all that morning and all the day before running about as fast as his legs could carry him, and very anxious that everyone should be aware of it. For every trifle he ran to Katerina Ivanovna, even hunting her out at the bazaar, at every instant called her “_Pani_.” She was heartily sick of him before the end, though she had declared at first that she could not have got on without this “serviceable and magnanimous man.” It was one of Katerina Ivanovna’s characteristics to paint everyone she met in the most glowing colours. Her praises were so exaggerated as sometimes to be embarrassing; she would invent various circumstances to the credit of her new acquaintance and quite genuinely believe in their reality. Then all of a sudden she would be disillusioned and would rudely and contemptuously repulse the person she had only a few hours before been literally adoring. She was naturally of a gay, lively and peace-loving disposition, but from continual failures and misfortunes she had come to desire so _keenly_ that all should live in peace and joy and should not _dare_ to break the peace, that the slightest jar, the smallest disaster reduced her almost to frenzy, and she would pass in an instant from the brightest hopes and fancies to cursing her fate and raving, and knocking her head against the wall.\n\nAmalia Ivanovna, too, suddenly acquired extraordinary importance in Katerina Ivanovna’s eyes and was treated by her with extraordinary respect, probably only because Amalia Ivanovna had thrown herself heart and soul into the preparations. She had undertaken to lay the table, to provide the linen, crockery, etc., and to cook the dishes in her kitchen, and Katerina Ivanovna had left it all in her hands and gone herself to the cemetery. Everything had been well done. Even the table-cloth was nearly clean; the crockery, knives, forks and glasses were, of course, of all shapes and patterns, lent by different lodgers, but the table was properly laid at the time fixed, and Amalia Ivanovna, feeling she had done her work well, had put on a black silk dress and a cap with new mourning ribbons and met the returning party with some pride. This pride, though justifiable, displeased Katerina Ivanovna for some reason: “as though the table could not have been laid except by Amalia Ivanovna!” She disliked the cap with new ribbons, too. “Could she be stuck up, the stupid German, because she was mistress of the house, and had consented as a favour to help her poor lodgers! As a favour! Fancy that! Katerina Ivanovna’s father who had been a colonel and almost a governor had sometimes had the table set for forty persons, and then anyone like Amalia Ivanovna, or rather Ludwigovna, would not have been allowed into the kitchen.”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna, however, put off expressing her feelings for the time and contented herself with treating her coldly, though she decided inwardly that she would certainly have to put Amalia Ivanovna down and set her in her proper place, for goodness only knew what she was fancying herself. Katerina Ivanovna was irritated too by the fact that hardly any of the lodgers invited had come to the funeral, except the Pole who had just managed to run into the cemetery, while to the memorial dinner the poorest and most insignificant of them had turned up, the wretched creatures, many of them not quite sober. The older and more respectable of them all, as if by common consent, stayed away. Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin, for instance, who might be said to be the most respectable of all the lodgers, did not appear, though Katerina Ivanovna had the evening before told all the world, that is Amalia Ivanovna, Polenka, Sonia and the Pole, that he was the most generous, noble-hearted man with a large property and vast connections, who had been a friend of her first husband’s, and a guest in her father’s house, and that he had promised to use all his influence to secure her a considerable pension. It must be noted that when Katerina Ivanovna exalted anyone’s connections and fortune, it was without any ulterior motive, quite disinterestedly, for the mere pleasure of adding to the consequence of the person praised. Probably “taking his cue” from Luzhin, “that contemptible wretch Lebeziatnikov had not turned up either. What did he fancy himself? He was only asked out of kindness and because he was sharing the same room with Pyotr Petrovitch and was a friend of his, so that it would have been awkward not to invite him.”\n\nAmong those who failed to appear were “the genteel lady and her old-maidish daughter,” who had only been lodgers in the house for the last fortnight, but had several times complained of the noise and uproar in Katerina Ivanovna’s room, especially when Marmeladov had come back drunk. Katerina Ivanovna heard this from Amalia Ivanovna who, quarrelling with Katerina Ivanovna, and threatening to turn the whole family out of doors, had shouted at her that they “were not worth the foot” of the honourable lodgers whom they were disturbing. Katerina Ivanovna determined now to invite this lady and her daughter, “whose foot she was not worth,” and who had turned away haughtily when she casually met them, so that they might know that “she was more noble in her thoughts and feelings and did not harbour malice,” and might see that she was not accustomed to her way of living. She had proposed to make this clear to them at dinner with allusions to her late father’s governorship, and also at the same time to hint that it was exceedingly stupid of them to turn away on meeting her. The fat colonel-major (he was really a discharged officer of low rank) was also absent, but it appeared that he had been “not himself” for the last two days. The party consisted of the Pole, a wretched looking clerk with a spotty face and a greasy coat, who had not a word to say for himself, and smelt abominably, a deaf and almost blind old man who had once been in the post office and who had been from immemorial ages maintained by someone at Amalia Ivanovna’s.\n\nA retired clerk of the commissariat department came, too; he was drunk, had a loud and most unseemly laugh and only fancy--was without a waistcoat! One of the visitors sat straight down to the table without even greeting Katerina Ivanovna. Finally one person having no suit appeared in his dressing-gown, but this was too much, and the efforts of Amalia Ivanovna and the Pole succeeded in removing him. The Pole brought with him, however, two other Poles who did not live at Amalia Ivanovna’s and whom no one had seen here before. All this irritated Katerina Ivanovna intensely. “For whom had they made all these preparations then?” To make room for the visitors the children had not even been laid for at the table; but the two little ones were sitting on a bench in the furthest corner with their dinner laid on a box, while Polenka as a big girl had to look after them, feed them, and keep their noses wiped like well-bred children’s.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna, in fact, could hardly help meeting her guests with increased dignity, and even haughtiness. She stared at some of them with special severity, and loftily invited them to take their seats. Rushing to the conclusion that Amalia Ivanovna must be responsible for those who were absent, she began treating her with extreme nonchalance, which the latter promptly observed and resented. Such a beginning was no good omen for the end. All were seated at last.\n\nRaskolnikov came in almost at the moment of their return from the cemetery. Katerina Ivanovna was greatly delighted to see him, in the first place, because he was the one “educated visitor, and, as everyone knew, was in two years to take a professorship in the university,” and secondly because he immediately and respectfully apologised for having been unable to be at the funeral. She positively pounced upon him, and made him sit on her left hand (Amalia Ivanovna was on her right). In spite of her continual anxiety that the dishes should be passed round correctly and that everyone should taste them, in spite of the agonising cough which interrupted her every minute and seemed to have grown worse during the last few days, she hastened to pour out in a half whisper to Raskolnikov all her suppressed feelings and her just indignation at the failure of the dinner, interspersing her remarks with lively and uncontrollable laughter at the expense of her visitors and especially of her landlady.\n\n“It’s all that cuckoo’s fault! You know whom I mean? Her, her!” Katerina Ivanovna nodded towards the landlady. “Look at her, she’s making round eyes, she feels that we are talking about her and can’t understand. Pfoo, the owl! Ha-ha! (Cough-cough-cough.) And what does she put on that cap for? (Cough-cough-cough.) Have you noticed that she wants everyone to consider that she is patronising me and doing me an honour by being here? I asked her like a sensible woman to invite people, especially those who knew my late husband, and look at the set of fools she has brought! The sweeps! Look at that one with the spotty face. And those wretched Poles, ha-ha-ha! (Cough-cough-cough.) Not one of them has ever poked his nose in here, I’ve never set eyes on them. What have they come here for, I ask you? There they sit in a row. Hey, _pan_!” she cried suddenly to one of them, “have you tasted the pancakes? Take some more! Have some beer! Won’t you have some vodka? Look, he’s jumped up and is making his bows, they must be quite starved, poor things. Never mind, let them eat! They don’t make a noise, anyway, though I’m really afraid for our landlady’s silver spoons... Amalia Ivanovna!” she addressed her suddenly, almost aloud, “if your spoons should happen to be stolen, I won’t be responsible, I warn you! Ha-ha-ha!” She laughed turning to Raskolnikov, and again nodding towards the landlady, in high glee at her sally. “She didn’t understand, she didn’t understand again! Look how she sits with her mouth open! An owl, a real owl! An owl in new ribbons, ha-ha-ha!”\n\nHere her laugh turned again to an insufferable fit of coughing that lasted five minutes. Drops of perspiration stood out on her forehead and her handkerchief was stained with blood. She showed Raskolnikov the blood in silence, and as soon as she could get her breath began whispering to him again with extreme animation and a hectic flush on her cheeks.\n\n“Do you know, I gave her the most delicate instructions, so to speak, for inviting that lady and her daughter, you understand of whom I am speaking? It needed the utmost delicacy, the greatest nicety, but she has managed things so that that fool, that conceited baggage, that provincial nonentity, simply because she is the widow of a major, and has come to try and get a pension and to fray out her skirts in the government offices, because at fifty she paints her face (everybody knows it)... a creature like that did not think fit to come, and has not even answered the invitation, which the most ordinary good manners required! I can’t understand why Pyotr Petrovitch has not come? But where’s Sonia? Where has she gone? Ah, there she is at last! what is it, Sonia, where have you been? It’s odd that even at your father’s funeral you should be so unpunctual. Rodion Romanovitch, make room for her beside you. That’s your place, Sonia... take what you like. Have some of the cold entrée with jelly, that’s the best. They’ll bring the pancakes directly. Have they given the children some? Polenka, have you got everything? (Cough-cough-cough.) That’s all right. Be a good girl, Lida, and, Kolya, don’t fidget with your feet; sit like a little gentleman. What are you saying, Sonia?”\n\nSonia hastened to give her Pyotr Petrovitch’s apologies, trying to speak loud enough for everyone to hear and carefully choosing the most respectful phrases which she attributed to Pyotr Petrovitch. She added that Pyotr Petrovitch had particularly told her to say that, as soon as he possibly could, he would come immediately to discuss _business_ alone with her and to consider what could be done for her, etc., etc.\n\nSonia knew that this would comfort Katerina Ivanovna, would flatter her and gratify her pride. She sat down beside Raskolnikov; she made him a hurried bow, glancing curiously at him. But for the rest of the time she seemed to avoid looking at him or speaking to him. She seemed absent-minded, though she kept looking at Katerina Ivanovna, trying to please her. Neither she nor Katerina Ivanovna had been able to get mourning; Sonia was wearing dark brown, and Katerina Ivanovna had on her only dress, a dark striped cotton one.\n\nThe message from Pyotr Petrovitch was very successful. Listening to Sonia with dignity, Katerina Ivanovna inquired with equal dignity how Pyotr Petrovitch was, then at once whispered almost aloud to Raskolnikov that it certainly would have been strange for a man of Pyotr Petrovitch’s position and standing to find himself in such “extraordinary company,” in spite of his devotion to her family and his old friendship with her father.\n\n“That’s why I am so grateful to you, Rodion Romanovitch, that you have not disdained my hospitality, even in such surroundings,” she added almost aloud. “But I am sure that it was only your special affection for my poor husband that has made you keep your promise.”\n\nThen once more with pride and dignity she scanned her visitors, and suddenly inquired aloud across the table of the deaf man: “Wouldn’t he have some more meat, and had he been given some wine?” The old man made no answer and for a long while could not understand what he was asked, though his neighbours amused themselves by poking and shaking him. He simply gazed about him with his mouth open, which only increased the general mirth.\n\n“What an imbecile! Look, look! Why was he brought? But as to Pyotr Petrovitch, I always had confidence in him,” Katerina Ivanovna continued, “and, of course, he is not like...” with an extremely stern face she addressed Amalia Ivanovna so sharply and loudly that the latter was quite disconcerted, “not like your dressed up draggletails whom my father would not have taken as cooks into his kitchen, and my late husband would have done them honour if he had invited them in the goodness of his heart.”\n\n“Yes, he was fond of drink, he was fond of it, he did drink!” cried the commissariat clerk, gulping down his twelfth glass of vodka.\n\n“My late husband certainly had that weakness, and everyone knows it,” Katerina Ivanovna attacked him at once, “but he was a kind and honourable man, who loved and respected his family. The worst of it was his good nature made him trust all sorts of disreputable people, and he drank with fellows who were not worth the sole of his shoe. Would you believe it, Rodion Romanovitch, they found a gingerbread cock in his pocket; he was dead drunk, but he did not forget the children!”\n\n“A cock? Did you say a cock?” shouted the commissariat clerk.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna did not vouchsafe a reply. She sighed, lost in thought.\n\n“No doubt you think, like everyone, that I was too severe with him,” she went on, addressing Raskolnikov. “But that’s not so! He respected me, he respected me very much! He was a kind-hearted man! And how sorry I was for him sometimes! He would sit in a corner and look at me, I used to feel so sorry for him, I used to want to be kind to him and then would think to myself: ‘Be kind to him and he will drink again,’ it was only by severity that you could keep him within bounds.”\n\n“Yes, he used to get his hair pulled pretty often,” roared the commissariat clerk again, swallowing another glass of vodka.\n\n“Some fools would be the better for a good drubbing, as well as having their hair pulled. I am not talking of my late husband now!” Katerina Ivanovna snapped at him.\n\nThe flush on her cheeks grew more and more marked, her chest heaved. In another minute she would have been ready to make a scene. Many of the visitors were sniggering, evidently delighted. They began poking the commissariat clerk and whispering something to him. They were evidently trying to egg him on.\n\n“Allow me to ask what are you alluding to,” began the clerk, “that is to say, whose... about whom... did you say just now... But I don’t care! That’s nonsense! Widow! I forgive you.... Pass!”\n\nAnd he took another drink of vodka.\n\nRaskolnikov sat in silence, listening with disgust. He only ate from politeness, just tasting the food that Katerina Ivanovna was continually putting on his plate, to avoid hurting her feelings. He watched Sonia intently. But Sonia became more and more anxious and distressed; she, too, foresaw that the dinner would not end peaceably, and saw with terror Katerina Ivanovna’s growing irritation. She knew that she, Sonia, was the chief reason for the ‘genteel’ ladies’ contemptuous treatment of Katerina Ivanovna’s invitation. She had heard from Amalia Ivanovna that the mother was positively offended at the invitation and had asked the question: “How could she let her daughter sit down beside _that young person_?” Sonia had a feeling that Katerina Ivanovna had already heard this and an insult to Sonia meant more to Katerina Ivanovna than an insult to herself, her children, or her father. Sonia knew that Katerina Ivanovna would not be satisfied now, “till she had shown those draggletails that they were both...” To make matters worse someone passed Sonia, from the other end of the table, a plate with two hearts pierced with an arrow, cut out of black bread. Katerina Ivanovna flushed crimson and at once said aloud across the table that the man who sent it was “a drunken ass!”\n\nAmalia Ivanovna was foreseeing something amiss, and at the same time deeply wounded by Katerina Ivanovna’s haughtiness, and to restore the good-humour of the company and raise herself in their esteem she began, apropos of nothing, telling a story about an acquaintance of hers “Karl from the chemist’s,” who was driving one night in a cab, and that “the cabman wanted him to kill, and Karl very much begged him not to kill, and wept and clasped hands, and frightened and from fear pierced his heart.” Though Katerina Ivanovna smiled, she observed at once that Amalia Ivanovna ought not to tell anecdotes in Russian; the latter was still more offended, and she retorted that her “_Vater aus Berlin_ was a very important man, and always went with his hands in pockets.” Katerina Ivanovna could not restrain herself and laughed so much that Amalia Ivanovna lost patience and could scarcely control herself.\n\n“Listen to the owl!” Katerina Ivanovna whispered at once, her good-humour almost restored, “she meant to say he kept his hands in his pockets, but she said he put his hands in people’s pockets. (Cough-cough.) And have you noticed, Rodion Romanovitch, that all these Petersburg foreigners, the Germans especially, are all stupider than we! Can you fancy anyone of us telling how ‘Karl from the chemist’s’ ‘pierced his heart from fear’ and that the idiot, instead of punishing the cabman, ‘clasped his hands and wept, and much begged.’ Ah, the fool! And you know she fancies it’s very touching and does not suspect how stupid she is! To my thinking that drunken commissariat clerk is a great deal cleverer, anyway one can see that he has addled his brains with drink, but you know, these foreigners are always so well behaved and serious.... Look how she sits glaring! She is angry, ha-ha! (Cough-cough-cough.)”\n\nRegaining her good-humour, Katerina Ivanovna began at once telling Raskolnikov that when she had obtained her pension, she intended to open a school for the daughters of gentlemen in her native town T----. This was the first time she had spoken to him of the project, and she launched out into the most alluring details. It suddenly appeared that Katerina Ivanovna had in her hands the very certificate of honour of which Marmeladov had spoken to Raskolnikov in the tavern, when he told him that Katerina Ivanovna, his wife, had danced the shawl dance before the governor and other great personages on leaving school. This certificate of honour was obviously intended now to prove Katerina Ivanovna’s right to open a boarding-school; but she had armed herself with it chiefly with the object of overwhelming “those two stuck-up draggletails” if they came to the dinner, and proving incontestably that Katerina Ivanovna was of the most noble, “she might even say aristocratic family, a colonel’s daughter and was far superior to certain adventuresses who have been so much to the fore of late.” The certificate of honour immediately passed into the hands of the drunken guests, and Katerina Ivanovna did not try to retain it, for it actually contained the statement _en toutes lettres_, that her father was of the rank of a major, and also a companion of an order, so that she really was almost the daughter of a colonel.\n\nWarming up, Katerina Ivanovna proceeded to enlarge on the peaceful and happy life they would lead in T----, on the gymnasium teachers whom she would engage to give lessons in her boarding-school, one a most respectable old Frenchman, one Mangot, who had taught Katerina Ivanovna herself in old days and was still living in T----, and would no doubt teach in her school on moderate terms. Next she spoke of Sonia who would go with her to T---- and help her in all her plans. At this someone at the further end of the table gave a sudden guffaw.\n\nThough Katerina Ivanovna tried to appear to be disdainfully unaware of it, she raised her voice and began at once speaking with conviction of Sonia’s undoubted ability to assist her, of “her gentleness, patience, devotion, generosity and good education,” tapping Sonia on the cheek and kissing her warmly twice. Sonia flushed crimson, and Katerina Ivanovna suddenly burst into tears, immediately observing that she was “nervous and silly, that she was too much upset, that it was time to finish, and as the dinner was over, it was time to hand round the tea.”\n\nAt that moment, Amalia Ivanovna, deeply aggrieved at taking no part in the conversation, and not being listened to, made one last effort, and with secret misgivings ventured on an exceedingly deep and weighty observation, that “in the future boarding-school she would have to pay particular attention to _die Wäsche_, and that there certainly must be a good _dame_ to look after the linen, and secondly that the young ladies must not novels at night read.”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna, who certainly was upset and very tired, as well as heartily sick of the dinner, at once cut short Amalia Ivanovna, saying “she knew nothing about it and was talking nonsense, that it was the business of the laundry maid, and not of the directress of a high-class boarding-school to look after _die Wäsche_, and as for novel-reading, that was simply rudeness, and she begged her to be silent.” Amalia Ivanovna fired up and getting angry observed that she only “meant her good,” and that “she had meant her very good,” and that “it was long since she had paid her _gold_ for the lodgings.”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna at once “set her down,” saying that it was a lie to say she wished her good, because only yesterday when her dead husband was lying on the table, she had worried her about the lodgings. To this Amalia Ivanovna very appropriately observed that she had invited those ladies, but “those ladies had not come, because those ladies _are_ ladies and cannot come to a lady who is not a lady.” Katerina Ivanovna at once pointed out to her, that as she was a slut she could not judge what made one really a lady. Amalia Ivanovna at once declared that her “_Vater aus Berlin_ was a very, very important man, and both hands in pockets went, and always used to say: ‘Poof! poof!’” and she leapt up from the table to represent her father, sticking her hands in her pockets, puffing her cheeks, and uttering vague sounds resembling “poof! poof!” amid loud laughter from all the lodgers, who purposely encouraged Amalia Ivanovna, hoping for a fight.\n\nBut this was too much for Katerina Ivanovna, and she at once declared, so that all could hear, that Amalia Ivanovna probably never had a father, but was simply a drunken Petersburg Finn, and had certainly once been a cook and probably something worse. Amalia Ivanovna turned as red as a lobster and squealed that perhaps Katerina Ivanovna never had a father, “but she had a _Vater aus Berlin_ and that he wore a long coat and always said poof-poof-poof!”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna observed contemptuously that all knew what her family was and that on that very certificate of honour it was stated in print that her father was a colonel, while Amalia Ivanovna’s father--if she really had one--was probably some Finnish milkman, but that probably she never had a father at all, since it was still uncertain whether her name was Amalia Ivanovna or Amalia Ludwigovna.\n\nAt this Amalia Ivanovna, lashed to fury, struck the table with her fist, and shrieked that she was Amalia Ivanovna, and not Ludwigovna, “that her _Vater_ was named Johann and that he was a burgomeister, and that Katerina Ivanovna’s _Vater_ was quite never a burgomeister.” Katerina Ivanovna rose from her chair, and with a stern and apparently calm voice (though she was pale and her chest was heaving) observed that “if she dared for one moment to set her contemptible wretch of a father on a level with her papa, she, Katerina Ivanovna, would tear her cap off her head and trample it under foot.” Amalia Ivanovna ran about the room, shouting at the top of her voice, that she was mistress of the house and that Katerina Ivanovna should leave the lodgings that minute; then she rushed for some reason to collect the silver spoons from the table. There was a great outcry and uproar, the children began crying. Sonia ran to restrain Katerina Ivanovna, but when Amalia Ivanovna shouted something about “the yellow ticket,” Katerina Ivanovna pushed Sonia away, and rushed at the landlady to carry out her threat.\n\nAt that minute the door opened, and Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin appeared on the threshold. He stood scanning the party with severe and vigilant eyes. Katerina Ivanovna rushed to him.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n“Pyotr Petrovitch,” she cried, “protect me... you at least! Make this foolish woman understand that she can’t behave like this to a lady in misfortune... that there is a law for such things.... I’ll go to the governor-general himself.... She shall answer for it.... Remembering my father’s hospitality protect these orphans.”\n\n“Allow me, madam.... Allow me.” Pyotr Petrovitch waved her off. “Your papa as you are well aware I had not the honour of knowing” (someone laughed aloud) “and I do not intend to take part in your everlasting squabbles with Amalia Ivanovna.... I have come here to speak of my own affairs... and I want to have a word with your stepdaughter, Sofya... Ivanovna, I think it is? Allow me to pass.”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch, edging by her, went to the opposite corner where Sonia was.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna remained standing where she was, as though thunderstruck. She could not understand how Pyotr Petrovitch could deny having enjoyed her father’s hospitality. Though she had invented it herself, she believed in it firmly by this time. She was struck too by the businesslike, dry and even contemptuous menacing tone of Pyotr Petrovitch. All the clamour gradually died away at his entrance. Not only was this “serious business man” strikingly incongruous with the rest of the party, but it was evident, too, that he had come upon some matter of consequence, that some exceptional cause must have brought him and that therefore something was going to happen. Raskolnikov, standing beside Sonia, moved aside to let him pass; Pyotr Petrovitch did not seem to notice him. A minute later Lebeziatnikov, too, appeared in the doorway; he did not come in, but stood still, listening with marked interest, almost wonder, and seemed for a time perplexed.\n\n“Excuse me for possibly interrupting you, but it’s a matter of some importance,” Pyotr Petrovitch observed, addressing the company generally. “I am glad indeed to find other persons present. Amalia Ivanovna, I humbly beg you as mistress of the house to pay careful attention to what I have to say to Sofya Ivanovna. Sofya Ivanovna,” he went on, addressing Sonia, who was very much surprised and already alarmed, “immediately after your visit I found that a hundred-rouble note was missing from my table, in the room of my friend Mr. Lebeziatnikov. If in any way whatever you know and will tell us where it is now, I assure you on my word of honour and call all present to witness that the matter shall end there. In the opposite case I shall be compelled to have recourse to very serious measures and then... you must blame yourself.”\n\nComplete silence reigned in the room. Even the crying children were still. Sonia stood deadly pale, staring at Luzhin and unable to say a word. She seemed not to understand. Some seconds passed.\n\n“Well, how is it to be then?” asked Luzhin, looking intently at her.\n\n“I don’t know.... I know nothing about it,” Sonia articulated faintly at last.\n\n“No, you know nothing?” Luzhin repeated and again he paused for some seconds. “Think a moment, mademoiselle,” he began severely, but still, as it were, admonishing her. “Reflect, I am prepared to give you time for consideration. Kindly observe this: if I were not so entirely convinced I should not, you may be sure, with my experience venture to accuse you so directly. Seeing that for such direct accusation before witnesses, if false or even mistaken, I should myself in a certain sense be made responsible, I am aware of that. This morning I changed for my own purposes several five-per-cent securities for the sum of approximately three thousand roubles. The account is noted down in my pocket-book. On my return home I proceeded to count the money--as Mr. Lebeziatnikov will bear witness--and after counting two thousand three hundred roubles I put the rest in my pocket-book in my coat pocket. About five hundred roubles remained on the table and among them three notes of a hundred roubles each. At that moment you entered (at my invitation)--and all the time you were present you were exceedingly embarrassed; so that three times you jumped up in the middle of the conversation and tried to make off. Mr. Lebeziatnikov can bear witness to this. You yourself, mademoiselle, probably will not refuse to confirm my statement that I invited you through Mr. Lebeziatnikov, solely in order to discuss with you the hopeless and destitute position of your relative, Katerina Ivanovna (whose dinner I was unable to attend), and the advisability of getting up something of the nature of a subscription, lottery or the like, for her benefit. You thanked me and even shed tears. I describe all this as it took place, primarily to recall it to your mind and secondly to show you that not the slightest detail has escaped my recollection. Then I took a ten-rouble note from the table and handed it to you by way of first instalment on my part for the benefit of your relative. Mr. Lebeziatnikov saw all this. Then I accompanied you to the door--you being still in the same state of embarrassment--after which, being left alone with Mr. Lebeziatnikov I talked to him for ten minutes--then Mr. Lebeziatnikov went out and I returned to the table with the money lying on it, intending to count it and to put it aside, as I proposed doing before. To my surprise one hundred-rouble note had disappeared. Kindly consider the position. Mr. Lebeziatnikov I cannot suspect. I am ashamed to allude to such a supposition. I cannot have made a mistake in my reckoning, for the minute before your entrance I had finished my accounts and found the total correct. You will admit that recollecting your embarrassment, your eagerness to get away and the fact that you kept your hands for some time on the table, and taking into consideration your social position and the habits associated with it, I was, so to say, with horror and positively against my will, _compelled_ to entertain a suspicion--a cruel, but justifiable suspicion! I will add further and repeat that in spite of my positive conviction, I realise that I run a certain risk in making this accusation, but as you see, I could not let it pass. I have taken action and I will tell you why: solely, madam, solely owing to your black ingratitude! Why! I invite you for the benefit of your destitute relative, I present you with my donation of ten roubles and you, on the spot, repay me for all that with such an action. It is too bad! You need a lesson. Reflect! Moreover, like a true friend I beg you--and you could have no better friend at this moment--think what you are doing, otherwise I shall be immovable! Well, what do you say?”\n\n“I have taken nothing,” Sonia whispered in terror, “you gave me ten roubles, here it is, take it.”\n\nSonia pulled her handkerchief out of her pocket, untied a corner of it, took out the ten-rouble note and gave it to Luzhin.\n\n“And the hundred roubles you do not confess to taking?” he insisted reproachfully, not taking the note.\n\nSonia looked about her. All were looking at her with such awful, stern, ironical, hostile eyes. She looked at Raskolnikov... he stood against the wall, with his arms crossed, looking at her with glowing eyes.\n\n“Good God!” broke from Sonia.\n\n“Amalia Ivanovna, we shall have to send word to the police and therefore I humbly beg you meanwhile to send for the house porter,” Luzhin said softly and even kindly.\n\n“_Gott der Barmherzige!_ I knew she was the thief,” cried Amalia Ivanovna, throwing up her hands.\n\n“You knew it?” Luzhin caught her up, “then I suppose you had some reason before this for thinking so. I beg you, worthy Amalia Ivanovna, to remember your words which have been uttered before witnesses.”\n\nThere was a buzz of loud conversation on all sides. All were in movement.\n\n“What!” cried Katerina Ivanovna, suddenly realising the position, and she rushed at Luzhin. “What! You accuse her of stealing? Sonia? Ah, the wretches, the wretches!”\n\nAnd running to Sonia she flung her wasted arms round her and held her as in a vise.\n\n“Sonia! how dared you take ten roubles from him? Foolish girl! Give it to me! Give me the ten roubles at once--here!”\n\nAnd snatching the note from Sonia, Katerina Ivanovna crumpled it up and flung it straight into Luzhin’s face. It hit him in the eye and fell on the ground. Amalia Ivanovna hastened to pick it up. Pyotr Petrovitch lost his temper.\n\n“Hold that mad woman!” he shouted.\n\nAt that moment several other persons, besides Lebeziatnikov, appeared in the doorway, among them the two ladies.\n\n“What! Mad? Am I mad? Idiot!” shrieked Katerina Ivanovna. “You are an idiot yourself, pettifogging lawyer, base man! Sonia, Sonia take his money! Sonia a thief! Why, she’d give away her last penny!” and Katerina Ivanovna broke into hysterical laughter. “Did you ever see such an idiot?” she turned from side to side. “And you too?” she suddenly saw the landlady, “and you too, sausage eater, you declare that she is a thief, you trashy Prussian hen’s leg in a crinoline! She hasn’t been out of this room: she came straight from you, you wretch, and sat down beside me, everyone saw her. She sat here, by Rodion Romanovitch. Search her! Since she’s not left the room, the money would have to be on her! Search her, search her! But if you don’t find it, then excuse me, my dear fellow, you’ll answer for it! I’ll go to our Sovereign, to our Sovereign, to our gracious Tsar himself, and throw myself at his feet, to-day, this minute! I am alone in the world! They would let me in! Do you think they wouldn’t? You’re wrong, I will get in! I will get in! You reckoned on her meekness! You relied upon that! But I am not so submissive, let me tell you! You’ve gone too far yourself. Search her, search her!”\n\nAnd Katerina Ivanovna in a frenzy shook Luzhin and dragged him towards Sonia.\n\n“I am ready, I’ll be responsible... but calm yourself, madam, calm yourself. I see that you are not so submissive!... Well, well, but as to that...” Luzhin muttered, “that ought to be before the police... though indeed there are witnesses enough as it is.... I am ready.... But in any case it’s difficult for a man... on account of her sex.... But with the help of Amalia Ivanovna... though, of course, it’s not the way to do things.... How is it to be done?”\n\n“As you will! Let anyone who likes search her!” cried Katerina Ivanovna. “Sonia, turn out your pockets! See! Look, monster, the pocket is empty, here was her handkerchief! Here is the other pocket, look! D’you see, d’you see?”\n\nAnd Katerina Ivanovna turned--or rather snatched--both pockets inside out. But from the right pocket a piece of paper flew out and describing a parabola in the air fell at Luzhin’s feet. Everyone saw it, several cried out. Pyotr Petrovitch stooped down, picked up the paper in two fingers, lifted it where all could see it and opened it. It was a hundred-rouble note folded in eight. Pyotr Petrovitch held up the note showing it to everyone.\n\n“Thief! Out of my lodging. Police, police!” yelled Amalia Ivanovna. “They must to Siberia be sent! Away!”\n\nExclamations arose on all sides. Raskolnikov was silent, keeping his eyes fixed on Sonia, except for an occasional rapid glance at Luzhin. Sonia stood still, as though unconscious. She was hardly able to feel surprise. Suddenly the colour rushed to her cheeks; she uttered a cry and hid her face in her hands.\n\n“No, it wasn’t I! I didn’t take it! I know nothing about it,” she cried with a heartrending wail, and she ran to Katerina Ivanovna, who clasped her tightly in her arms, as though she would shelter her from all the world.\n\n“Sonia! Sonia! I don’t believe it! You see, I don’t believe it!” she cried in the face of the obvious fact, swaying her to and fro in her arms like a baby, kissing her face continually, then snatching at her hands and kissing them, too, “you took it! How stupid these people are! Oh dear! You are fools, fools,” she cried, addressing the whole room, “you don’t know, you don’t know what a heart she has, what a girl she is! She take it, she? She’d sell her last rag, she’d go barefoot to help you if you needed it, that’s what she is! She has the yellow passport because my children were starving, she sold herself for us! Ah, husband, husband! Do you see? Do you see? What a memorial dinner for you! Merciful heavens! Defend her, why are you all standing still? Rodion Romanovitch, why don’t you stand up for her? Do you believe it, too? You are not worth her little finger, all of you together! Good God! Defend her now, at least!”\n\nThe wail of the poor, consumptive, helpless woman seemed to produce a great effect on her audience. The agonised, wasted, consumptive face, the parched blood-stained lips, the hoarse voice, the tears unrestrained as a child’s, the trustful, childish and yet despairing prayer for help were so piteous that everyone seemed to feel for her. Pyotr Petrovitch at any rate was at once moved to _compassion_.\n\n“Madam, madam, this incident does not reflect upon you!” he cried impressively, “no one would take upon himself to accuse you of being an instigator or even an accomplice in it, especially as you have proved her guilt by turning out her pockets, showing that you had no previous idea of it. I am most ready, most ready to show compassion, if poverty, so to speak, drove Sofya Semyonovna to it, but why did you refuse to confess, mademoiselle? Were you afraid of the disgrace? The first step? You lost your head, perhaps? One can quite understand it.... But how could you have lowered yourself to such an action? Gentlemen,” he addressed the whole company, “gentlemen! Compassionate and, so to say, commiserating these people, I am ready to overlook it even now in spite of the personal insult lavished upon me! And may this disgrace be a lesson to you for the future,” he said, addressing Sonia, “and I will carry the matter no further. Enough!”\n\nPyotr Petrovitch stole a glance at Raskolnikov. Their eyes met, and the fire in Raskolnikov’s seemed ready to reduce him to ashes. Meanwhile Katerina Ivanovna apparently heard nothing. She was kissing and hugging Sonia like a madwoman. The children, too, were embracing Sonia on all sides, and Polenka--though she did not fully understand what was wrong--was drowned in tears and shaking with sobs, as she hid her pretty little face, swollen with weeping, on Sonia’s shoulder.\n\n“How vile!” a loud voice cried suddenly in the doorway.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch looked round quickly.\n\n“What vileness!” Lebeziatnikov repeated, staring him straight in the face.\n\nPyotr Petrovitch gave a positive start--all noticed it and recalled it afterwards. Lebeziatnikov strode into the room.\n\n“And you dared to call me as witness?” he said, going up to Pyotr Petrovitch.\n\n“What do you mean? What are you talking about?” muttered Luzhin.\n\n“I mean that you... are a slanderer, that’s what my words mean!” Lebeziatnikov said hotly, looking sternly at him with his short-sighted eyes.\n\nHe was extremely angry. Raskolnikov gazed intently at him, as though seizing and weighing each word. Again there was a silence. Pyotr Petrovitch indeed seemed almost dumbfounded for the first moment.\n\n“If you mean that for me,...” he began, stammering. “But what’s the matter with you? Are you out of your mind?”\n\n“I’m in my mind, but you are a scoundrel! Ah, how vile! I have heard everything. I kept waiting on purpose to understand it, for I must own even now it is not quite logical.... What you have done it all for I can’t understand.”\n\n“Why, what have I done then? Give over talking in your nonsensical riddles! Or maybe you are drunk!”\n\n“You may be a drunkard, perhaps, vile man, but I am not! I never touch vodka, for it’s against my convictions. Would you believe it, he, he himself, with his own hands gave Sofya Semyonovna that hundred-rouble note--I saw it, I was a witness, I’ll take my oath! He did it, he!” repeated Lebeziatnikov, addressing all.\n\n“Are you crazy, milksop?” squealed Luzhin. “She is herself before you--she herself here declared just now before everyone that I gave her only ten roubles. How could I have given it to her?”\n\n“I saw it, I saw it,” Lebeziatnikov repeated, “and though it is against my principles, I am ready this very minute to take any oath you like before the court, for I saw how you slipped it in her pocket. Only like a fool I thought you did it out of kindness! When you were saying good-bye to her at the door, while you held her hand in one hand, with the other, the left, you slipped the note into her pocket. I saw it, I saw it!”\n\nLuzhin turned pale.\n\n“What lies!” he cried impudently, “why, how could you, standing by the window, see the note? You fancied it with your short-sighted eyes. You are raving!”\n\n“No, I didn’t fancy it. And though I was standing some way off, I saw it all. And though it certainly would be hard to distinguish a note from the window--that’s true--I knew for certain that it was a hundred-rouble note, because, when you were going to give Sofya Semyonovna ten roubles, you took up from the table a hundred-rouble note (I saw it because I was standing near then, and an idea struck me at once, so that I did not forget you had it in your hand). You folded it and kept it in your hand all the time. I didn’t think of it again until, when you were getting up, you changed it from your right hand to your left and nearly dropped it! I noticed it because the same idea struck me again, that you meant to do her a kindness without my seeing. You can fancy how I watched you and I saw how you succeeded in slipping it into her pocket. I saw it, I saw it, I’ll take my oath.”\n\nLebeziatnikov was almost breathless. Exclamations arose on all hands chiefly expressive of wonder, but some were menacing in tone. They all crowded round Pyotr Petrovitch. Katerina Ivanovna flew to Lebeziatnikov.\n\n“I was mistaken in you! Protect her! You are the only one to take her part! She is an orphan. God has sent you!”\n\nKaterina Ivanovna, hardly knowing what she was doing, sank on her knees before him.\n\n“A pack of nonsense!” yelled Luzhin, roused to fury, “it’s all nonsense you’ve been talking! ‘An idea struck you, you didn’t think, you noticed’--what does it amount to? So I gave it to her on the sly on purpose? What for? With what object? What have I to do with this...?”\n\n“What for? That’s what I can’t understand, but that what I am telling you is the fact, that’s certain! So far from my being mistaken, you infamous criminal man, I remember how, on account of it, a question occurred to me at once, just when I was thanking you and pressing your hand. What made you put it secretly in her pocket? Why you did it secretly, I mean? Could it be simply to conceal it from me, knowing that my convictions are opposed to yours and that I do not approve of private benevolence, which effects no radical cure? Well, I decided that you really were ashamed of giving such a large sum before me. Perhaps, too, I thought, he wants to give her a surprise, when she finds a whole hundred-rouble note in her pocket. (For I know, some benevolent people are very fond of decking out their charitable actions in that way.) Then the idea struck me, too, that you wanted to test her, to see whether, when she found it, she would come to thank you. Then, too, that you wanted to avoid thanks and that, as the saying is, your right hand should not know... something of that sort, in fact. I thought of so many possibilities that I put off considering it, but still thought it indelicate to show you that I knew your secret. But another idea struck me again that Sofya Semyonovna might easily lose the money before she noticed it, that was why I decided to come in here to call her out of the room and to tell her that you put a hundred roubles in her pocket. But on my way I went first to Madame Kobilatnikov’s to take them the ‘General Treatise on the Positive Method’ and especially to recommend Piderit’s article (and also Wagner’s); then I come on here and what a state of things I find! Now could I, could I, have all these ideas and reflections if I had not seen you put the hundred-rouble note in her pocket?”\n\nWhen Lebeziatnikov finished his long-winded harangue with the logical deduction at the end, he was quite tired, and the perspiration streamed from his face. He could not, alas, even express himself correctly in Russian, though he knew no other language, so that he was quite exhausted, almost emaciated after this heroic exploit. But his speech produced a powerful effect. He had spoken with such vehemence, with such conviction that everyone obviously believed him. Pyotr Petrovitch felt that things were going badly with him.\n\n“What is it to do with me if silly ideas did occur to you?” he shouted, “that’s no evidence. You may have dreamt it, that’s all! And I tell you, you are lying, sir. You are lying and slandering from some spite against me, simply from pique, because I did not agree with your free-thinking, godless, social propositions!”\n\nBut this retort did not benefit Pyotr Petrovitch. Murmurs of disapproval were heard on all sides.\n\n“Ah, that’s your line now, is it!” cried Lebeziatnikov, “that’s nonsense! Call the police and I’ll take my oath! There’s only one thing I can’t understand: what made him risk such a contemptible action. Oh, pitiful, despicable man!”\n\n“I can explain why he risked such an action, and if necessary, I, too, will swear to it,” Raskolnikov said at last in a firm voice, and he stepped forward.\n\nHe appeared to be firm and composed. Everyone felt clearly, from the very look of him that he really knew about it and that the mystery would be solved.\n\n“Now I can explain it all to myself,” said Raskolnikov, addressing Lebeziatnikov. “From the very beginning of the business, I suspected that there was some scoundrelly intrigue at the bottom of it. I began to suspect it from some special circumstances known to me only, which I will explain at once to everyone: they account for everything. Your valuable evidence has finally made everything clear to me. I beg all, all to listen. This gentleman (he pointed to Luzhin) was recently engaged to be married to a young lady--my sister, Avdotya Romanovna Raskolnikov. But coming to Petersburg he quarrelled with me, the day before yesterday, at our first meeting and I drove him out of my room--I have two witnesses to prove it. He is a very spiteful man.... The day before yesterday I did not know that he was staying here, in your room, and that consequently on the very day we quarrelled--the day before yesterday--he saw me give Katerina Ivanovna some money for the funeral, as a friend of the late Mr. Marmeladov. He at once wrote a note to my mother and informed her that I had given away all my money, not to Katerina Ivanovna but to Sofya Semyonovna, and referred in a most contemptible way to the... character of Sofya Semyonovna, that is, hinted at the character of my attitude to Sofya Semyonovna. All this you understand was with the object of dividing me from my mother and sister, by insinuating that I was squandering on unworthy objects the money which they had sent me and which was all they had. Yesterday evening, before my mother and sister and in his presence, I declared that I had given the money to Katerina Ivanovna for the funeral and not to Sofya Semyonovna and that I had no acquaintance with Sofya Semyonovna and had never seen her before, indeed. At the same time I added that he, Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin, with all his virtues, was not worth Sofya Semyonovna’s little finger, though he spoke so ill of her. To his question--would I let Sofya Semyonovna sit down beside my sister, I answered that I had already done so that day. Irritated that my mother and sister were unwilling to quarrel with me at his insinuations, he gradually began being unpardonably rude to them. A final rupture took place and he was turned out of the house. All this happened yesterday evening. Now I beg your special attention: consider: if he had now succeeded in proving that Sofya Semyonovna was a thief, he would have shown to my mother and sister that he was almost right in his suspicions, that he had reason to be angry at my putting my sister on a level with Sofya Semyonovna, that, in attacking me, he was protecting and preserving the honour of my sister, his betrothed. In fact he might even, through all this, have been able to estrange me from my family, and no doubt he hoped to be restored to favour with them; to say nothing of revenging himself on me personally, for he has grounds for supposing that the honour and happiness of Sofya Semyonovna are very precious to me. That was what he was working for! That’s how I understand it. That’s the whole reason for it and there can be no other!”\n\nIt was like this, or somewhat like this, that Raskolnikov wound up his speech which was followed very attentively, though often interrupted by exclamations from his audience. But in spite of interruptions he spoke clearly, calmly, exactly, firmly. His decisive voice, his tone of conviction and his stern face made a great impression on everyone.\n\n“Yes, yes, that’s it,” Lebeziatnikov assented gleefully, “that must be it, for he asked me, as soon as Sofya Semyonovna came into our room, whether you were here, whether I had seen you among Katerina Ivanovna’s guests. He called me aside to the window and asked me in secret. It was essential for him that you should be here! That’s it, that’s it!”\n\nLuzhin smiled contemptuously and did not speak. But he was very pale. He seemed to be deliberating on some means of escape. Perhaps he would have been glad to give up everything and get away, but at the moment this was scarcely possible. It would have implied admitting the truth of the accusations brought against him. Moreover, the company, which had already been excited by drink, was now too much stirred to allow it. The commissariat clerk, though indeed he had not grasped the whole position, was shouting louder than anyone and was making some suggestions very unpleasant to Luzhin. But not all those present were drunk; lodgers came in from all the rooms. The three Poles were tremendously excited and were continually shouting at him: “The _pan_ is a _lajdak_!” and muttering threats in Polish. Sonia had been listening with strained attention, though she too seemed unable to grasp it all; she seemed as though she had just returned to consciousness. She did not take her eyes off Raskolnikov, feeling that all her safety lay in him. Katerina Ivanovna breathed hard and painfully and seemed fearfully exhausted. Amalia Ivanovna stood looking more stupid than anyone, with her mouth wide open, unable to make out what had happened. She only saw that Pyotr Petrovitch had somehow come to grief.\n\nRaskolnikov was attempting to speak again, but they did not let him. Everyone was crowding round Luzhin with threats and shouts of abuse. But Pyotr Petrovitch was not intimidated. Seeing that his accusation of Sonia had completely failed, he had recourse to insolence:\n\n“Allow me, gentlemen, allow me! Don’t squeeze, let me pass!” he said, making his way through the crowd. “And no threats, if you please! I assure you it will be useless, you will gain nothing by it. On the contrary, you’ll have to answer, gentlemen, for violently obstructing the course of justice. The thief has been more than unmasked, and I shall prosecute. Our judges are not so blind and... not so drunk, and will not believe the testimony of two notorious infidels, agitators, and atheists, who accuse me from motives of personal revenge which they are foolish enough to admit.... Yes, allow me to pass!”\n\n“Don’t let me find a trace of you in my room! Kindly leave at once, and everything is at an end between us! When I think of the trouble I’ve been taking, the way I’ve been expounding... all this fortnight!”\n\n“I told you myself to-day that I was going, when you tried to keep me; now I will simply add that you are a fool. I advise you to see a doctor for your brains and your short sight. Let me pass, gentlemen!”\n\nHe forced his way through. But the commissariat clerk was unwilling to let him off so easily: he picked up a glass from the table, brandished it in the air and flung it at Pyotr Petrovitch; but the glass flew straight at Amalia Ivanovna. She screamed, and the clerk, overbalancing, fell heavily under the table. Pyotr Petrovitch made his way to his room and half an hour later had left the house. Sonia, timid by nature, had felt before that day that she could be ill-treated more easily than anyone, and that she could be wronged with impunity. Yet till that moment she had fancied that she might escape misfortune by care, gentleness and submissiveness before everyone. Her disappointment was too great. She could, of course, bear with patience and almost without murmur anything, even this. But for the first minute she felt it too bitter. In spite of her triumph and her justification--when her first terror and stupefaction had passed and she could understand it all clearly--the feeling of her helplessness and of the wrong done to her made her heart throb with anguish and she was overcome with hysterical weeping. At last, unable to bear any more, she rushed out of the room and ran home, almost immediately after Luzhin’s departure. When amidst loud laughter the glass flew at Amalia Ivanovna, it was more than the landlady could endure. With a shriek she rushed like a fury at Katerina Ivanovna, considering her to blame for everything.\n\n“Out of my lodgings! At once! Quick march!”\n\nAnd with these words she began snatching up everything she could lay her hands on that belonged to Katerina Ivanovna, and throwing it on the floor. Katerina Ivanovna, pale, almost fainting, and gasping for breath, jumped up from the bed where she had sunk in exhaustion and darted at Amalia Ivanovna. But the battle was too unequal: the landlady waved her away like a feather.\n\n“What! As though that godless calumny was not enough--this vile creature attacks me! What! On the day of my husband’s funeral I am turned out of my lodging! After eating my bread and salt she turns me into the street, with my orphans! Where am I to go?” wailed the poor woman, sobbing and gasping. “Good God!” she cried with flashing eyes, “is there no justice upon earth? Whom should you protect if not us orphans? We shall see! There is law and justice on earth, there is, I will find it! Wait a bit, godless creature! Polenka, stay with the children, I’ll come back. Wait for me, if you have to wait in the street. We will see whether there is justice on earth!”\n\nAnd throwing over her head that green shawl which Marmeladov had mentioned to Raskolnikov, Katerina Ivanovna squeezed her way through the disorderly and drunken crowd of lodgers who still filled the room, and, wailing and tearful, she ran into the street--with a vague intention of going at once somewhere to find justice. Polenka with the two little ones in her arms crouched, terrified, on the trunk in the corner of the room, where she waited trembling for her mother to come back. Amalia Ivanovna raged about the room, shrieking, lamenting and throwing everything she came across on the floor. The lodgers talked incoherently, some commented to the best of their ability on what had happened, others quarrelled and swore at one another, while others struck up a song....\n\n“Now it’s time for me to go,” thought Raskolnikov. “Well, Sofya Semyonovna, we shall see what you’ll say now!”\n\nAnd he set off in the direction of Sonia’s lodgings.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nRaskolnikov had been a vigorous and active champion of Sonia against Luzhin, although he had such a load of horror and anguish in his own heart. But having gone through so much in the morning, he found a sort of relief in a change of sensations, apart from the strong personal feeling which impelled him to defend Sonia. He was agitated too, especially at some moments, by the thought of his approaching interview with Sonia: he _had_ to tell her who had killed Lizaveta. He knew the terrible suffering it would be to him and, as it were, brushed away the thought of it. So when he cried as he left Katerina Ivanovna’s, “Well, Sofya Semyonovna, we shall see what you’ll say now!” he was still superficially excited, still vigorous and defiant from his triumph over Luzhin. But, strange to say, by the time he reached Sonia’s lodging, he felt a sudden impotence and fear. He stood still in hesitation at the door, asking himself the strange question: “Must he tell her who killed Lizaveta?” It was a strange question because he felt at the very time not only that he could not help telling her, but also that he could not put off the telling. He did not yet know why it must be so, he only _felt_ it, and the agonising sense of his impotence before the inevitable almost crushed him. To cut short his hesitation and suffering, he quickly opened the door and looked at Sonia from the doorway. She was sitting with her elbows on the table and her face in her hands, but seeing Raskolnikov she got up at once and came to meet him as though she were expecting him.\n\n“What would have become of me but for you?” she said quickly, meeting him in the middle of the room.\n\nEvidently she was in haste to say this to him. It was what she had been waiting for.\n\nRaskolnikov went to the table and sat down on the chair from which she had only just risen. She stood facing him, two steps away, just as she had done the day before.\n\n“Well, Sonia?” he said, and felt that his voice was trembling, “it was all due to ‘your social position and the habits associated with it.’ Did you understand that just now?”\n\nHer face showed her distress.\n\n“Only don’t talk to me as you did yesterday,” she interrupted him. “Please don’t begin it. There is misery enough without that.”\n\nShe made haste to smile, afraid that he might not like the reproach.\n\n“I was silly to come away from there. What is happening there now? I wanted to go back directly, but I kept thinking that... you would come.”\n\nHe told her that Amalia Ivanovna was turning them out of their lodging and that Katerina Ivanovna had run off somewhere “to seek justice.”\n\n“My God!” cried Sonia, “let’s go at once....”\n\nAnd she snatched up her cape.\n\n“It’s everlastingly the same thing!” said Raskolnikov, irritably. “You’ve no thought except for them! Stay a little with me.”\n\n“But... Katerina Ivanovna?”\n\n“You won’t lose Katerina Ivanovna, you may be sure, she’ll come to you herself since she has run out,” he added peevishly. “If she doesn’t find you here, you’ll be blamed for it....”\n\nSonia sat down in painful suspense. Raskolnikov was silent, gazing at the floor and deliberating.\n\n“This time Luzhin did not want to prosecute you,” he began, not looking at Sonia, “but if he had wanted to, if it had suited his plans, he would have sent you to prison if it had not been for Lebeziatnikov and me. Ah?”\n\n“Yes,” she assented in a faint voice. “Yes,” she repeated, preoccupied and distressed.\n\n“But I might easily not have been there. And it was quite an accident Lebeziatnikov’s turning up.”\n\nSonia was silent.\n\n“And if you’d gone to prison, what then? Do you remember what I said yesterday?”\n\nAgain she did not answer. He waited.\n\n“I thought you would cry out again ‘don’t speak of it, leave off.’” Raskolnikov gave a laugh, but rather a forced one. “What, silence again?” he asked a minute later. “We must talk about something, you know. It would be interesting for me to know how you would decide a certain ‘problem’ as Lebeziatnikov would say.” (He was beginning to lose the thread.) “No, really, I am serious. Imagine, Sonia, that you had known all Luzhin’s intentions beforehand. Known, that is, for a fact, that they would be the ruin of Katerina Ivanovna and the children and yourself thrown in--since you don’t count yourself for anything--Polenka too... for she’ll go the same way. Well, if suddenly it all depended on your decision whether he or they should go on living, that is whether Luzhin should go on living and doing wicked things, or Katerina Ivanovna should die? How would you decide which of them was to die? I ask you?”\n\nSonia looked uneasily at him. There was something peculiar in this hesitating question, which seemed approaching something in a roundabout way.\n\n“I felt that you were going to ask some question like that,” she said, looking inquisitively at him.\n\n“I dare say you did. But how is it to be answered?”\n\n“Why do you ask about what could not happen?” said Sonia reluctantly.\n\n“Then it would be better for Luzhin to go on living and doing wicked things? You haven’t dared to decide even that!”\n\n“But I can’t know the Divine Providence.... And why do you ask what can’t be answered? What’s the use of such foolish questions? How could it happen that it should depend on my decision--who has made me a judge to decide who is to live and who is not to live?”\n\n“Oh, if the Divine Providence is to be mixed up in it, there is no doing anything,” Raskolnikov grumbled morosely.\n\n“You’d better say straight out what you want!” Sonia cried in distress. “You are leading up to something again.... Can you have come simply to torture me?”\n\nShe could not control herself and began crying bitterly. He looked at her in gloomy misery. Five minutes passed.\n\n“Of course you’re right, Sonia,” he said softly at last. He was suddenly changed. His tone of assumed arrogance and helpless defiance was gone. Even his voice was suddenly weak. “I told you yesterday that I was not coming to ask forgiveness and almost the first thing I’ve said is to ask forgiveness.... I said that about Luzhin and Providence for my own sake. I was asking forgiveness, Sonia....”\n\nHe tried to smile, but there was something helpless and incomplete in his pale smile. He bowed his head and hid his face in his hands.\n\nAnd suddenly a strange, surprising sensation of a sort of bitter hatred for Sonia passed through his heart. As it were wondering and frightened of this sensation, he raised his head and looked intently at her; but he met her uneasy and painfully anxious eyes fixed on him; there was love in them; his hatred vanished like a phantom. It was not the real feeling; he had taken the one feeling for the other. It only meant that _that_ minute had come.\n\nHe hid his face in his hands again and bowed his head. Suddenly he turned pale, got up from his chair, looked at Sonia, and without uttering a word sat down mechanically on her bed.\n\nHis sensations that moment were terribly like the moment when he had stood over the old woman with the axe in his hand and felt that “he must not lose another minute.”\n\n“What’s the matter?” asked Sonia, dreadfully frightened.\n\nHe could not utter a word. This was not at all, not at all the way he had intended to “tell” and he did not understand what was happening to him now. She went up to him, softly, sat down on the bed beside him and waited, not taking her eyes off him. Her heart throbbed and sank. It was unendurable; he turned his deadly pale face to her. His lips worked, helplessly struggling to utter something. A pang of terror passed through Sonia’s heart.\n\n“What’s the matter?” she repeated, drawing a little away from him.\n\n“Nothing, Sonia, don’t be frightened.... It’s nonsense. It really is nonsense, if you think of it,” he muttered, like a man in delirium. “Why have I come to torture you?” he added suddenly, looking at her. “Why, really? I keep asking myself that question, Sonia....”\n\nHe had perhaps been asking himself that question a quarter of an hour before, but now he spoke helplessly, hardly knowing what he said and feeling a continual tremor all over.\n\n“Oh, how you are suffering!” she muttered in distress, looking intently at him.\n\n“It’s all nonsense.... Listen, Sonia.” He suddenly smiled, a pale helpless smile for two seconds. “You remember what I meant to tell you yesterday?”\n\nSonia waited uneasily.\n\n“I said as I went away that perhaps I was saying good-bye for ever, but that if I came to-day I would tell you who... who killed Lizaveta.”\n\nShe began trembling all over.\n\n“Well, here I’ve come to tell you.”\n\n“Then you really meant it yesterday?” she whispered with difficulty. “How do you know?” she asked quickly, as though suddenly regaining her reason.\n\nSonia’s face grew paler and paler, and she breathed painfully.\n\n“I know.”\n\nShe paused a minute.\n\n“Have they found him?” she asked timidly.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then how do you know about _it_?” she asked again, hardly audibly and again after a minute’s pause.\n\nHe turned to her and looked very intently at her.\n\n“Guess,” he said, with the same distorted helpless smile.\n\nA shudder passed over her.\n\n“But you... why do you frighten me like this?” she said, smiling like a child.\n\n“I must be a great friend of _his_... since I know,” Raskolnikov went on, still gazing into her face, as though he could not turn his eyes away. “He... did not mean to kill that Lizaveta... he... killed her accidentally.... He meant to kill the old woman when she was alone and he went there... and then Lizaveta came in... he killed her too.”\n\nAnother awful moment passed. Both still gazed at one another.\n\n“You can’t guess, then?” he asked suddenly, feeling as though he were flinging himself down from a steeple.\n\n“N-no...” whispered Sonia.\n\n“Take a good look.”\n\nAs soon as he had said this again, the same familiar sensation froze his heart. He looked at her and all at once seemed to see in her face the face of Lizaveta. He remembered clearly the expression in Lizaveta’s face, when he approached her with the axe and she stepped back to the wall, putting out her hand, with childish terror in her face, looking as little children do when they begin to be frightened of something, looking intently and uneasily at what frightens them, shrinking back and holding out their little hands on the point of crying. Almost the same thing happened now to Sonia. With the same helplessness and the same terror, she looked at him for a while and, suddenly putting out her left hand, pressed her fingers faintly against his breast and slowly began to get up from the bed, moving further from him and keeping her eyes fixed even more immovably on him. Her terror infected him. The same fear showed itself on his face. In the same way he stared at her and almost with the same _childish_ smile.\n\n“Have you guessed?” he whispered at last.\n\n“Good God!” broke in an awful wail from her bosom.\n\nShe sank helplessly on the bed with her face in the pillows, but a moment later she got up, moved quickly to him, seized both his hands and, gripping them tight in her thin fingers, began looking into his face again with the same intent stare. In this last desperate look she tried to look into him and catch some last hope. But there was no hope; there was no doubt remaining; it was all true! Later on, indeed, when she recalled that moment, she thought it strange and wondered why she had seen at once that there was no doubt. She could not have said, for instance, that she had foreseen something of the sort--and yet now, as soon as he told her, she suddenly fancied that she had really foreseen this very thing.\n\n“Stop, Sonia, enough! don’t torture me,” he begged her miserably.\n\nIt was not at all, not at all like this he had thought of telling her, but this is how it happened.\n\nShe jumped up, seeming not to know what she was doing, and, wringing her hands, walked into the middle of the room; but quickly went back and sat down again beside him, her shoulder almost touching his. All of a sudden she started as though she had been stabbed, uttered a cry and fell on her knees before him, she did not know why.\n\n“What have you done--what have you done to yourself?” she said in despair, and, jumping up, she flung herself on his neck, threw her arms round him, and held him tightly.\n\nRaskolnikov drew back and looked at her with a mournful smile.\n\n“You are a strange girl, Sonia--you kiss me and hug me when I tell you about that.... You don’t think what you are doing.”\n\n“There is no one--no one in the whole world now so unhappy as you!” she cried in a frenzy, not hearing what he said, and she suddenly broke into violent hysterical weeping.\n\nA feeling long unfamiliar to him flooded his heart and softened it at once. He did not struggle against it. Two tears started into his eyes and hung on his eyelashes.\n\n“Then you won’t leave me, Sonia?” he said, looking at her almost with hope.\n\n“No, no, never, nowhere!” cried Sonia. “I will follow you, I will follow you everywhere. Oh, my God! Oh, how miserable I am!... Why, why didn’t I know you before! Why didn’t you come before? Oh, dear!”\n\n“Here I have come.”\n\n“Yes, now! What’s to be done now?... Together, together!” she repeated as it were unconsciously, and she hugged him again. “I’ll follow you to Siberia!”\n\nHe recoiled at this, and the same hostile, almost haughty smile came to his lips.\n\n“Perhaps I don’t want to go to Siberia yet, Sonia,” he said.\n\nSonia looked at him quickly.\n\nAgain after her first passionate, agonising sympathy for the unhappy man the terrible idea of the murder overwhelmed her. In his changed tone she seemed to hear the murderer speaking. She looked at him bewildered. She knew nothing as yet, why, how, with what object it had been. Now all these questions rushed at once into her mind. And again she could not believe it: “He, he is a murderer! Could it be true?”\n\n“What’s the meaning of it? Where am I?” she said in complete bewilderment, as though still unable to recover herself. “How could you, you, a man like you.... How could you bring yourself to it?... What does it mean?”\n\n“Oh, well--to plunder. Leave off, Sonia,” he answered wearily, almost with vexation.\n\nSonia stood as though struck dumb, but suddenly she cried:\n\n“You were hungry! It was... to help your mother? Yes?”\n\n“No, Sonia, no,” he muttered, turning away and hanging his head. “I was not so hungry.... I certainly did want to help my mother, but... that’s not the real thing either.... Don’t torture me, Sonia.”\n\nSonia clasped her hands.\n\n“Could it, could it all be true? Good God, what a truth! Who could believe it? And how could you give away your last farthing and yet rob and murder! Ah,” she cried suddenly, “that money you gave Katerina Ivanovna... that money.... Can that money...”\n\n“No, Sonia,” he broke in hurriedly, “that money was not it. Don’t worry yourself! That money my mother sent me and it came when I was ill, the day I gave it to you.... Razumihin saw it... he received it for me.... That money was mine--my own.”\n\nSonia listened to him in bewilderment and did her utmost to comprehend.\n\n“And _that_ money.... I don’t even know really whether there was any money,” he added softly, as though reflecting. “I took a purse off her neck, made of chamois leather... a purse stuffed full of something... but I didn’t look in it; I suppose I hadn’t time.... And the things--chains and trinkets--I buried under a stone with the purse next morning in a yard off the V---- Prospect. They are all there now....”\n\nSonia strained every nerve to listen.\n\n“Then why... why, you said you did it to rob, but you took nothing?” she asked quickly, catching at a straw.\n\n“I don’t know.... I haven’t yet decided whether to take that money or not,” he said, musing again; and, seeming to wake up with a start, he gave a brief ironical smile. “Ach, what silly stuff I am talking, eh?”\n\nThe thought flashed through Sonia’s mind, wasn’t he mad? But she dismissed it at once. “No, it was something else.” She could make nothing of it, nothing.\n\n“Do you know, Sonia,” he said suddenly with conviction, “let me tell you: if I’d simply killed because I was hungry,” laying stress on every word and looking enigmatically but sincerely at her, “I should be _happy_ now. You must believe that! What would it matter to you,” he cried a moment later with a sort of despair, “what would it matter to you if I were to confess that I did wrong? What do you gain by such a stupid triumph over me? Ah, Sonia, was it for that I’ve come to you to-day?”\n\nAgain Sonia tried to say something, but did not speak.\n\n“I asked you to go with me yesterday because you are all I have left.”\n\n“Go where?” asked Sonia timidly.\n\n“Not to steal and not to murder, don’t be anxious,” he smiled bitterly. “We are so different.... And you know, Sonia, it’s only now, only this moment that I understand _where_ I asked you to go with me yesterday! Yesterday when I said it I did not know where. I asked you for one thing, I came to you for one thing--not to leave me. You won’t leave me, Sonia?”\n\nShe squeezed his hand.\n\n“And why, why did I tell her? Why did I let her know?” he cried a minute later in despair, looking with infinite anguish at her. “Here you expect an explanation from me, Sonia; you are sitting and waiting for it, I see that. But what can I tell you? You won’t understand and will only suffer misery... on my account! Well, you are crying and embracing me again. Why do you do it? Because I couldn’t bear my burden and have come to throw it on another: you suffer too, and I shall feel better! And can you love such a mean wretch?”\n\n“But aren’t you suffering, too?” cried Sonia.\n\nAgain a wave of the same feeling surged into his heart, and again for an instant softened it.\n\n“Sonia, I have a bad heart, take note of that. It may explain a great deal. I have come because I am bad. There are men who wouldn’t have come. But I am a coward and... a mean wretch. But... never mind! That’s not the point. I must speak now, but I don’t know how to begin.”\n\nHe paused and sank into thought.\n\n“Ach, we are so different,” he cried again, “we are not alike. And why, why did I come? I shall never forgive myself that.”\n\n“No, no, it was a good thing you came,” cried Sonia. “It’s better I should know, far better!”\n\nHe looked at her with anguish.\n\n“What if it were really that?” he said, as though reaching a conclusion. “Yes, that’s what it was! I wanted to become a Napoleon, that is why I killed her.... Do you understand now?”\n\n“N-no,” Sonia whispered naïvely and timidly. “Only speak, speak, I shall understand, I shall understand _in myself_!” she kept begging him.\n\n“You’ll understand? Very well, we shall see!” He paused and was for some time lost in meditation.\n\n“It was like this: I asked myself one day this question--what if Napoleon, for instance, had happened to be in my place, and if he had not had Toulon nor Egypt nor the passage of Mont Blanc to begin his career with, but instead of all those picturesque and monumental things, there had simply been some ridiculous old hag, a pawnbroker, who had to be murdered too to get money from her trunk (for his career, you understand). Well, would he have brought himself to that if there had been no other means? Wouldn’t he have felt a pang at its being so far from monumental and... and sinful, too? Well, I must tell you that I worried myself fearfully over that ‘question’ so that I was awfully ashamed when I guessed at last (all of a sudden, somehow) that it would not have given him the least pang, that it would not even have struck him that it was not monumental... that he would not have seen that there was anything in it to pause over, and that, if he had had no other way, he would have strangled her in a minute without thinking about it! Well, I too... left off thinking about it... murdered her, following his example. And that’s exactly how it was! Do you think it funny? Yes, Sonia, the funniest thing of all is that perhaps that’s just how it was.”\n\nSonia did not think it at all funny.\n\n“You had better tell me straight out... without examples,” she begged, still more timidly and scarcely audibly.\n\nHe turned to her, looked sadly at her and took her hands.\n\n“You are right again, Sonia. Of course that’s all nonsense, it’s almost all talk! You see, you know of course that my mother has scarcely anything, my sister happened to have a good education and was condemned to drudge as a governess. All their hopes were centered on me. I was a student, but I couldn’t keep myself at the university and was forced for a time to leave it. Even if I had lingered on like that, in ten or twelve years I might (with luck) hope to be some sort of teacher or clerk with a salary of a thousand roubles” (he repeated it as though it were a lesson) “and by that time my mother would be worn out with grief and anxiety and I could not succeed in keeping her in comfort while my sister... well, my sister might well have fared worse! And it’s a hard thing to pass everything by all one’s life, to turn one’s back upon everything, to forget one’s mother and decorously accept the insults inflicted on one’s sister. Why should one? When one has buried them to burden oneself with others--wife and children--and to leave them again without a farthing? So I resolved to gain possession of the old woman’s money and to use it for my first years without worrying my mother, to keep myself at the university and for a little while after leaving it--and to do this all on a broad, thorough scale, so as to build up a completely new career and enter upon a new life of independence.... Well... that’s all.... Well, of course in killing the old woman I did wrong.... Well, that’s enough.”\n\nHe struggled to the end of his speech in exhaustion and let his head sink.\n\n“Oh, that’s not it, that’s not it,” Sonia cried in distress. “How could one... no, that’s not right, not right.”\n\n“You see yourself that it’s not right. But I’ve spoken truly, it’s the truth.”\n\n“As though that could be the truth! Good God!”\n\n“I’ve only killed a louse, Sonia, a useless, loathsome, harmful creature.”\n\n“A human being--a louse!”\n\n“I too know it wasn’t a louse,” he answered, looking strangely at her. “But I am talking nonsense, Sonia,” he added. “I’ve been talking nonsense a long time.... That’s not it, you are right there. There were quite, quite other causes for it! I haven’t talked to anyone for so long, Sonia.... My head aches dreadfully now.”\n\nHis eyes shone with feverish brilliance. He was almost delirious; an uneasy smile strayed on his lips. His terrible exhaustion could be seen through his excitement. Sonia saw how he was suffering. She too was growing dizzy. And he talked so strangely; it seemed somehow comprehensible, but yet... “But how, how! Good God!” And she wrung her hands in despair.\n\n“No, Sonia, that’s not it,” he began again suddenly, raising his head, as though a new and sudden train of thought had struck and as it were roused him--“that’s not it! Better... imagine--yes, it’s certainly better--imagine that I am vain, envious, malicious, base, vindictive and... well, perhaps with a tendency to insanity. (Let’s have it all out at once! They’ve talked of madness already, I noticed.) I told you just now I could not keep myself at the university. But do you know that perhaps I might have done? My mother would have sent me what I needed for the fees and I could have earned enough for clothes, boots and food, no doubt. Lessons had turned up at half a rouble. Razumihin works! But I turned sulky and wouldn’t. (Yes, sulkiness, that’s the right word for it!) I sat in my room like a spider. You’ve been in my den, you’ve seen it.... And do you know, Sonia, that low ceilings and tiny rooms cramp the soul and the mind? Ah, how I hated that garret! And yet I wouldn’t go out of it! I wouldn’t on purpose! I didn’t go out for days together, and I wouldn’t work, I wouldn’t even eat, I just lay there doing nothing. If Nastasya brought me anything, I ate it, if she didn’t, I went all day without; I wouldn’t ask, on purpose, from sulkiness! At night I had no light, I lay in the dark and I wouldn’t earn money for candles. I ought to have studied, but I sold my books; and the dust lies an inch thick on the notebooks on my table. I preferred lying still and thinking. And I kept thinking.... And I had dreams all the time, strange dreams of all sorts, no need to describe! Only then I began to fancy that... No, that’s not it! Again I am telling you wrong! You see I kept asking myself then: why am I so stupid that if others are stupid--and I know they are--yet I won’t be wiser? Then I saw, Sonia, that if one waits for everyone to get wiser it will take too long.... Afterwards I understood that that would never come to pass, that men won’t change and that nobody can alter it and that it’s not worth wasting effort over it. Yes, that’s so. That’s the law of their nature, Sonia,... that’s so!... And I know now, Sonia, that whoever is strong in mind and spirit will have power over them. Anyone who is greatly daring is right in their eyes. He who despises most things will be a lawgiver among them and he who dares most of all will be most in the right! So it has been till now and so it will always be. A man must be blind not to see it!”\n\nThough Raskolnikov looked at Sonia as he said this, he no longer cared whether she understood or not. The fever had complete hold of him; he was in a sort of gloomy ecstasy (he certainly had been too long without talking to anyone). Sonia felt that his gloomy creed had become his faith and code.\n\n“I divined then, Sonia,” he went on eagerly, “that power is only vouchsafed to the man who dares to stoop and pick it up. There is only one thing, one thing needful: one has only to dare! Then for the first time in my life an idea took shape in my mind which no one had ever thought of before me, no one! I saw clear as daylight how strange it is that not a single person living in this mad world has had the daring to go straight for it all and send it flying to the devil! I... I wanted _to have the daring_... and I killed her. I only wanted to have the daring, Sonia! That was the whole cause of it!”\n\n“Oh hush, hush,” cried Sonia, clasping her hands. “You turned away from God and God has smitten you, has given you over to the devil!”\n\n“Then Sonia, when I used to lie there in the dark and all this became clear to me, was it a temptation of the devil, eh?”\n\n“Hush, don’t laugh, blasphemer! You don’t understand, you don’t understand! Oh God! He won’t understand!”\n\n“Hush, Sonia! I am not laughing. I know myself that it was the devil leading me. Hush, Sonia, hush!” he repeated with gloomy insistence. “I know it all, I have thought it all over and over and whispered it all over to myself, lying there in the dark.... I’ve argued it all over with myself, every point of it, and I know it all, all! And how sick, how sick I was then of going over it all! I have kept wanting to forget it and make a new beginning, Sonia, and leave off thinking. And you don’t suppose that I went into it headlong like a fool? I went into it like a wise man, and that was just my destruction. And you mustn’t suppose that I didn’t know, for instance, that if I began to question myself whether I had the right to gain power--I certainly hadn’t the right--or that if I asked myself whether a human being is a louse it proved that it wasn’t so for me, though it might be for a man who would go straight to his goal without asking questions.... If I worried myself all those days, wondering whether Napoleon would have done it or not, I felt clearly of course that I wasn’t Napoleon. I had to endure all the agony of that battle of ideas, Sonia, and I longed to throw it off: I wanted to murder without casuistry, to murder for my own sake, for myself alone! I didn’t want to lie about it even to myself. It wasn’t to help my mother I did the murder--that’s nonsense--I didn’t do the murder to gain wealth and power and to become a benefactor of mankind. Nonsense! I simply did it; I did the murder for myself, for myself alone, and whether I became a benefactor to others, or spent my life like a spider catching men in my web and sucking the life out of men, I couldn’t have cared at that moment.... And it was not the money I wanted, Sonia, when I did it. It was not so much the money I wanted, but something else.... I know it all now.... Understand me! Perhaps I should never have committed a murder again. I wanted to find out something else; it was something else led me on. I wanted to find out then and quickly whether I was a louse like everybody else or a man. Whether I can step over barriers or not, whether I dare stoop to pick up or not, whether I am a trembling creature or whether I have the _right_...”\n\n“To kill? Have the right to kill?” Sonia clasped her hands.\n\n“Ach, Sonia!” he cried irritably and seemed about to make some retort, but was contemptuously silent. “Don’t interrupt me, Sonia. I want to prove one thing only, that the devil led me on then and he has shown me since that I had not the right to take that path, because I am just such a louse as all the rest. He was mocking me and here I’ve come to you now! Welcome your guest! If I were not a louse, should I have come to you? Listen: when I went then to the old woman’s I only went to _try_.... You may be sure of that!”\n\n“And you murdered her!”\n\n“But how did I murder her? Is that how men do murders? Do men go to commit a murder as I went then? I will tell you some day how I went! Did I murder the old woman? I murdered myself, not her! I crushed myself once for all, for ever.... But it was the devil that killed that old woman, not I. Enough, enough, Sonia, enough! Let me be!” he cried in a sudden spasm of agony, “let me be!”\n\nHe leaned his elbows on his knees and squeezed his head in his hands as in a vise.\n\n“What suffering!” A wail of anguish broke from Sonia.\n\n“Well, what am I to do now?” he asked, suddenly raising his head and looking at her with a face hideously distorted by despair.\n\n“What are you to do?” she cried, jumping up, and her eyes that had been full of tears suddenly began to shine. “Stand up!” (She seized him by the shoulder, he got up, looking at her almost bewildered.) “Go at once, this very minute, stand at the cross-roads, bow down, first kiss the earth which you have defiled and then bow down to all the world and say to all men aloud, ‘I am a murderer!’ Then God will send you life again. Will you go, will you go?” she asked him, trembling all over, snatching his two hands, squeezing them tight in hers and gazing at him with eyes full of fire.\n\nHe was amazed at her sudden ecstasy.\n\n“You mean Siberia, Sonia? I must give myself up?” he asked gloomily.\n\n“Suffer and expiate your sin by it, that’s what you must do.”\n\n“No! I am not going to them, Sonia!”\n\n“But how will you go on living? What will you live for?” cried Sonia, “how is it possible now? Why, how can you talk to your mother? (Oh, what will become of them now?) But what am I saying? You have abandoned your mother and your sister already. He has abandoned them already! Oh, God!” she cried, “why, he knows it all himself. How, how can he live by himself! What will become of you now?”\n\n“Don’t be a child, Sonia,” he said softly. “What wrong have I done them? Why should I go to them? What should I say to them? That’s only a phantom.... They destroy men by millions themselves and look on it as a virtue. They are knaves and scoundrels, Sonia! I am not going to them. And what should I say to them--that I murdered her, but did not dare to take the money and hid it under a stone?” he added with a bitter smile. “Why, they would laugh at me, and would call me a fool for not getting it. A coward and a fool! They wouldn’t understand and they don’t deserve to understand. Why should I go to them? I won’t. Don’t be a child, Sonia....”\n\n“It will be too much for you to bear, too much!” she repeated, holding out her hands in despairing supplication.\n\n“Perhaps I’ve been unfair to myself,” he observed gloomily, pondering, “perhaps after all I am a man and not a louse and I’ve been in too great a hurry to condemn myself. I’ll make another fight for it.”\n\nA haughty smile appeared on his lips.\n\n“What a burden to bear! And your whole life, your whole life!”\n\n“I shall get used to it,” he said grimly and thoughtfully. “Listen,” he began a minute later, “stop crying, it’s time to talk of the facts: I’ve come to tell you that the police are after me, on my track....”\n\n“Ach!” Sonia cried in terror.\n\n“Well, why do you cry out? You want me to go to Siberia and now you are frightened? But let me tell you: I shall not give myself up. I shall make a struggle for it and they won’t do anything to me. They’ve no real evidence. Yesterday I was in great danger and believed I was lost; but to-day things are going better. All the facts they know can be explained two ways, that’s to say I can turn their accusations to my credit, do you understand? And I shall, for I’ve learnt my lesson. But they will certainly arrest me. If it had not been for something that happened, they would have done so to-day for certain; perhaps even now they will arrest me to-day.... But that’s no matter, Sonia; they’ll let me out again... for there isn’t any real proof against me, and there won’t be, I give you my word for it. And they can’t convict a man on what they have against me. Enough.... I only tell you that you may know.... I will try to manage somehow to put it to my mother and sister so that they won’t be frightened.... My sister’s future is secure, however, now, I believe... and my mother’s must be too.... Well, that’s all. Be careful, though. Will you come and see me in prison when I am there?”\n\n“Oh, I will, I will.”\n\nThey sat side by side, both mournful and dejected, as though they had been cast up by the tempest alone on some deserted shore. He looked at Sonia and felt how great was her love for him, and strange to say he felt it suddenly burdensome and painful to be so loved. Yes, it was a strange and awful sensation! On his way to see Sonia he had felt that all his hopes rested on her; he expected to be rid of at least part of his suffering, and now, when all her heart turned towards him, he suddenly felt that he was immeasurably unhappier than before.\n\n“Sonia,” he said, “you’d better not come and see me when I am in prison.”\n\nSonia did not answer, she was crying. Several minutes passed.\n\n“Have you a cross on you?” she asked, as though suddenly thinking of it.\n\nHe did not at first understand the question.\n\n“No, of course not. Here, take this one, of cypress wood. I have another, a copper one that belonged to Lizaveta. I changed with Lizaveta: she gave me her cross and I gave her my little ikon. I will wear Lizaveta’s now and give you this. Take it... it’s mine! It’s mine, you know,” she begged him. “We will go to suffer together, and together we will bear our cross!”\n\n“Give it me,” said Raskolnikov.\n\nHe did not want to hurt her feelings. But immediately he drew back the hand he held out for the cross.\n\n“Not now, Sonia. Better later,” he added to comfort her.\n\n“Yes, yes, better,” she repeated with conviction, “when you go to meet your suffering, then put it on. You will come to me, I’ll put it on you, we will pray and go together.”\n\nAt that moment someone knocked three times at the door.\n\n“Sofya Semyonovna, may I come in?” they heard in a very familiar and polite voice.\n\nSonia rushed to the door in a fright. The flaxen head of Mr. Lebeziatnikov appeared at the door.\n\nLebeziatnikov looked perturbed.\n\n“I’ve come to you, Sofya Semyonovna,” he began. “Excuse me... I thought I should find you,” he said, addressing Raskolnikov suddenly, “that is, I didn’t mean anything... of that sort... But I just thought... Katerina Ivanovna has gone out of her mind,” he blurted out suddenly, turning from Raskolnikov to Sonia.\n\nSonia screamed.\n\n“At least it seems so. But... we don’t know what to do, you see! She came back--she seems to have been turned out somewhere, perhaps beaten.... So it seems at least.... She had run to your father’s former chief, she didn’t find him at home: he was dining at some other general’s.... Only fancy, she rushed off there, to the other general’s, and, imagine, she was so persistent that she managed to get the chief to see her, had him fetched out from dinner, it seems. You can imagine what happened. She was turned out, of course; but, according to her own story, she abused him and threw something at him. One may well believe it.... How it is she wasn’t taken up, I can’t understand! Now she is telling everyone, including Amalia Ivanovna; but it’s difficult to understand her, she is screaming and flinging herself about.... Oh yes, she shouts that since everyone has abandoned her, she will take the children and go into the street with a barrel-organ, and the children will sing and dance, and she too, and collect money, and will go every day under the general’s window... ‘to let everyone see well-born children, whose father was an official, begging in the street.’ She keeps beating the children and they are all crying. She is teaching Lida to sing ‘My Village,’ the boy to dance, Polenka the same. She is tearing up all the clothes, and making them little caps like actors; she means to carry a tin basin and make it tinkle, instead of music.... She won’t listen to anything.... Imagine the state of things! It’s beyond anything!”\n\nLebeziatnikov would have gone on, but Sonia, who had heard him almost breathless, snatched up her cloak and hat, and ran out of the room, putting on her things as she went. Raskolnikov followed her and Lebeziatnikov came after him.\n\n“She has certainly gone mad!” he said to Raskolnikov, as they went out into the street. “I didn’t want to frighten Sofya Semyonovna, so I said ‘it seemed like it,’ but there isn’t a doubt of it. They say that in consumption the tubercles sometimes occur in the brain; it’s a pity I know nothing of medicine. I did try to persuade her, but she wouldn’t listen.”\n\n“Did you talk to her about the tubercles?”\n\n“Not precisely of the tubercles. Besides, she wouldn’t have understood! But what I say is, that if you convince a person logically that he has nothing to cry about, he’ll stop crying. That’s clear. Is it your conviction that he won’t?”\n\n“Life would be too easy if it were so,” answered Raskolnikov.\n\n“Excuse me, excuse me; of course it would be rather difficult for Katerina Ivanovna to understand, but do you know that in Paris they have been conducting serious experiments as to the possibility of curing the insane, simply by logical argument? One professor there, a scientific man of standing, lately dead, believed in the possibility of such treatment. His idea was that there’s nothing really wrong with the physical organism of the insane, and that insanity is, so to say, a logical mistake, an error of judgment, an incorrect view of things. He gradually showed the madman his error and, would you believe it, they say he was successful? But as he made use of douches too, how far success was due to that treatment remains uncertain.... So it seems at least.”\n\nRaskolnikov had long ceased to listen. Reaching the house where he lived, he nodded to Lebeziatnikov and went in at the gate. Lebeziatnikov woke up with a start, looked about him and hurried on.\n\nRaskolnikov went into his little room and stood still in the middle of it. Why had he come back here? He looked at the yellow and tattered paper, at the dust, at his sofa.... From the yard came a loud continuous knocking; someone seemed to be hammering... He went to the window, rose on tiptoe and looked out into the yard for a long time with an air of absorbed attention. But the yard was empty and he could not see who was hammering. In the house on the left he saw some open windows; on the window-sills were pots of sickly-looking geraniums. Linen was hung out of the windows... He knew it all by heart. He turned away and sat down on the sofa.\n\nNever, never had he felt himself so fearfully alone!\n\nYes, he felt once more that he would perhaps come to hate Sonia, now that he had made her more miserable.\n\n“Why had he gone to her to beg for her tears? What need had he to poison her life? Oh, the meanness of it!”\n\n“I will remain alone,” he said resolutely, “and she shall not come to the prison!”\n\nFive minutes later he raised his head with a strange smile. That was a strange thought.\n\n“Perhaps it really would be better in Siberia,” he thought suddenly.\n\nHe could not have said how long he sat there with vague thoughts surging through his mind. All at once the door opened and Dounia came in. At first she stood still and looked at him from the doorway, just as he had done at Sonia; then she came in and sat down in the same place as yesterday, on the chair facing him. He looked silently and almost vacantly at her.\n\n“Don’t be angry, brother; I’ve only come for one minute,” said Dounia.\n\nHer face looked thoughtful but not stern. Her eyes were bright and soft. He saw that she too had come to him with love.\n\n“Brother, now I know all, _all_. Dmitri Prokofitch has explained and told me everything. They are worrying and persecuting you through a stupid and contemptible suspicion.... Dmitri Prokofitch told me that there is no danger, and that you are wrong in looking upon it with such horror. I don’t think so, and I fully understand how indignant you must be, and that that indignation may have a permanent effect on you. That’s what I am afraid of. As for your cutting yourself off from us, I don’t judge you, I don’t venture to judge you, and forgive me for having blamed you for it. I feel that I too, if I had so great a trouble, should keep away from everyone. I shall tell mother nothing _of this_, but I shall talk about you continually and shall tell her from you that you will come very soon. Don’t worry about her; _I_ will set her mind at rest; but don’t you try her too much--come once at least; remember that she is your mother. And now I have come simply to say” (Dounia began to get up) “that if you should need me or should need... all my life or anything... call me, and I’ll come. Good-bye!”\n\nShe turned abruptly and went towards the door.\n\n“Dounia!” Raskolnikov stopped her and went towards her. “That Razumihin, Dmitri Prokofitch, is a very good fellow.”\n\nDounia flushed slightly.\n\n“Well?” she asked, waiting a moment.\n\n“He is competent, hardworking, honest and capable of real love.... Good-bye, Dounia.”\n\nDounia flushed crimson, then suddenly she took alarm.\n\n“But what does it mean, brother? Are we really parting for ever that you... give me such a parting message?”\n\n“Never mind.... Good-bye.”\n\nHe turned away, and walked to the window. She stood a moment, looked at him uneasily, and went out troubled.\n\nNo, he was not cold to her. There was an instant (the very last one) when he had longed to take her in his arms and _say good-bye_ to her, and even _to tell_ her, but he had not dared even to touch her hand.\n\n“Afterwards she may shudder when she remembers that I embraced her, and will feel that I stole her kiss.”\n\n“And would _she_ stand that test?” he went on a few minutes later to himself. “No, she wouldn’t; girls like that can’t stand things! They never do.”\n\nAnd he thought of Sonia.\n\nThere was a breath of fresh air from the window. The daylight was fading. He took up his cap and went out.\n\nHe could not, of course, and would not consider how ill he was. But all this continual anxiety and agony of mind could not but affect him. And if he were not lying in high fever it was perhaps just because this continual inner strain helped to keep him on his legs and in possession of his faculties. But this artificial excitement could not last long.\n\nHe wandered aimlessly. The sun was setting. A special form of misery had begun to oppress him of late. There was nothing poignant, nothing acute about it; but there was a feeling of permanence, of eternity about it; it brought a foretaste of hopeless years of this cold leaden misery, a foretaste of an eternity “on a square yard of space.” Towards evening this sensation usually began to weigh on him more heavily.\n\n“With this idiotic, purely physical weakness, depending on the sunset or something, one can’t help doing something stupid! You’ll go to Dounia, as well as to Sonia,” he muttered bitterly.\n\nHe heard his name called. He looked round. Lebeziatnikov rushed up to him.\n\n“Only fancy, I’ve been to your room looking for you. Only fancy, she’s carried out her plan, and taken away the children. Sofya Semyonovna and I have had a job to find them. She is rapping on a frying-pan and making the children dance. The children are crying. They keep stopping at the cross-roads and in front of shops; there’s a crowd of fools running after them. Come along!”\n\n“And Sonia?” Raskolnikov asked anxiously, hurrying after Lebeziatnikov.\n\n“Simply frantic. That is, it’s not Sofya Semyonovna’s frantic, but Katerina Ivanovna, though Sofya Semyonova’s frantic too. But Katerina Ivanovna is absolutely frantic. I tell you she is quite mad. They’ll be taken to the police. You can fancy what an effect that will have.... They are on the canal bank, near the bridge now, not far from Sofya Semyonovna’s, quite close.”\n\nOn the canal bank near the bridge and not two houses away from the one where Sonia lodged, there was a crowd of people, consisting principally of gutter children. The hoarse broken voice of Katerina Ivanovna could be heard from the bridge, and it certainly was a strange spectacle likely to attract a street crowd. Katerina Ivanovna in her old dress with the green shawl, wearing a torn straw hat, crushed in a hideous way on one side, was really frantic. She was exhausted and breathless. Her wasted consumptive face looked more suffering than ever, and indeed out of doors in the sunshine a consumptive always looks worse than at home. But her excitement did not flag, and every moment her irritation grew more intense. She rushed at the children, shouted at them, coaxed them, told them before the crowd how to dance and what to sing, began explaining to them why it was necessary, and driven to desperation by their not understanding, beat them.... Then she would make a rush at the crowd; if she noticed any decently dressed person stopping to look, she immediately appealed to him to see what these children “from a genteel, one may say aristocratic, house” had been brought to. If she heard laughter or jeering in the crowd, she would rush at once at the scoffers and begin squabbling with them. Some people laughed, others shook their heads, but everyone felt curious at the sight of the madwoman with the frightened children. The frying-pan of which Lebeziatnikov had spoken was not there, at least Raskolnikov did not see it. But instead of rapping on the pan, Katerina Ivanovna began clapping her wasted hands, when she made Lida and Kolya dance and Polenka sing. She too joined in the singing, but broke down at the second note with a fearful cough, which made her curse in despair and even shed tears. What made her most furious was the weeping and terror of Kolya and Lida. Some effort had been made to dress the children up as street singers are dressed. The boy had on a turban made of something red and white to look like a Turk. There had been no costume for Lida; she simply had a red knitted cap, or rather a night cap that had belonged to Marmeladov, decorated with a broken piece of white ostrich feather, which had been Katerina Ivanovna’s grandmother’s and had been preserved as a family possession. Polenka was in her everyday dress; she looked in timid perplexity at her mother, and kept at her side, hiding her tears. She dimly realised her mother’s condition, and looked uneasily about her. She was terribly frightened of the street and the crowd. Sonia followed Katerina Ivanovna, weeping and beseeching her to return home, but Katerina Ivanovna was not to be persuaded.\n\n“Leave off, Sonia, leave off,” she shouted, speaking fast, panting and coughing. “You don’t know what you ask; you are like a child! I’ve told you before that I am not coming back to that drunken German. Let everyone, let all Petersburg see the children begging in the streets, though their father was an honourable man who served all his life in truth and fidelity, and one may say died in the service.” (Katerina Ivanovna had by now invented this fantastic story and thoroughly believed it.) “Let that wretch of a general see it! And you are silly, Sonia: what have we to eat? Tell me that. We have worried you enough, I won’t go on so! Ah, Rodion Romanovitch, is that you?” she cried, seeing Raskolnikov and rushing up to him. “Explain to this silly girl, please, that nothing better could be done! Even organ-grinders earn their living, and everyone will see at once that we are different, that we are an honourable and bereaved family reduced to beggary. And that general will lose his post, you’ll see! We shall perform under his windows every day, and if the Tsar drives by, I’ll fall on my knees, put the children before me, show them to him, and say ‘Defend us father.’ He is the father of the fatherless, he is merciful, he’ll protect us, you’ll see, and that wretch of a general.... Lida, _tenez vous droite_! Kolya, you’ll dance again. Why are you whimpering? Whimpering again! What are you afraid of, stupid? Goodness, what am I to do with them, Rodion Romanovitch? If you only knew how stupid they are! What’s one to do with such children?”\n\nAnd she, almost crying herself--which did not stop her uninterrupted, rapid flow of talk--pointed to the crying children. Raskolnikov tried to persuade her to go home, and even said, hoping to work on her vanity, that it was unseemly for her to be wandering about the streets like an organ-grinder, as she was intending to become the principal of a boarding-school.\n\n“A boarding-school, ha-ha-ha! A castle in the air,” cried Katerina Ivanovna, her laugh ending in a cough. “No, Rodion Romanovitch, that dream is over! All have forsaken us!... And that general.... You know, Rodion Romanovitch, I threw an inkpot at him--it happened to be standing in the waiting-room by the paper where you sign your name. I wrote my name, threw it at him and ran away. Oh, the scoundrels, the scoundrels! But enough of them, now I’ll provide for the children myself, I won’t bow down to anybody! She has had to bear enough for us!” she pointed to Sonia. “Polenka, how much have you got? Show me! What, only two farthings! Oh, the mean wretches! They give us nothing, only run after us, putting their tongues out. There, what is that blockhead laughing at?” (She pointed to a man in the crowd.) “It’s all because Kolya here is so stupid; I have such a bother with him. What do you want, Polenka? Tell me in French, _parlez-moi français_. Why, I’ve taught you, you know some phrases. Else how are you to show that you are of good family, well brought-up children, and not at all like other organ-grinders? We aren’t going to have a Punch and Judy show in the street, but to sing a genteel song.... Ah, yes,... What are we to sing? You keep putting me out, but we... you see, we are standing here, Rodion Romanovitch, to find something to sing and get money, something Kolya can dance to.... For, as you can fancy, our performance is all impromptu.... We must talk it over and rehearse it all thoroughly, and then we shall go to Nevsky, where there are far more people of good society, and we shall be noticed at once. Lida knows ‘My Village’ only, nothing but ‘My Village,’ and everyone sings that. We must sing something far more genteel.... Well, have you thought of anything, Polenka? If only you’d help your mother! My memory’s quite gone, or I should have thought of something. We really can’t sing ‘An Hussar.’ Ah, let us sing in French, ‘Cinq sous,’ I have taught it you, I have taught it you. And as it is in French, people will see at once that you are children of good family, and that will be much more touching.... You might sing ‘Marlborough s’en va-t-en guerre,’ for that’s quite a child’s song and is sung as a lullaby in all the aristocratic houses.\n\n“_Marlborough s’en va-t-en guerre Ne sait quand reviendra_...” she began singing. “But no, better sing ‘Cinq sous.’ Now, Kolya, your hands on your hips, make haste, and you, Lida, keep turning the other way, and Polenka and I will sing and clap our hands!\n\n“_Cinq sous, cinq sous Pour monter notre menage_.”\n\n(Cough-cough-cough!) “Set your dress straight, Polenka, it’s slipped down on your shoulders,” she observed, panting from coughing. “Now it’s particularly necessary to behave nicely and genteelly, that all may see that you are well-born children. I said at the time that the bodice should be cut longer, and made of two widths. It was your fault, Sonia, with your advice to make it shorter, and now you see the child is quite deformed by it.... Why, you’re all crying again! What’s the matter, stupids? Come, Kolya, begin. Make haste, make haste! Oh, what an unbearable child!\n\n“Cinq sous, cinq sous.\n\n“A policeman again! What do you want?”\n\nA policeman was indeed forcing his way through the crowd. But at that moment a gentleman in civilian uniform and an overcoat--a solid-looking official of about fifty with a decoration on his neck (which delighted Katerina Ivanovna and had its effect on the policeman)--approached and without a word handed her a green three-rouble note. His face wore a look of genuine sympathy. Katerina Ivanovna took it and gave him a polite, even ceremonious, bow.\n\n“I thank you, honoured sir,” she began loftily. “The causes that have induced us (take the money, Polenka: you see there are generous and honourable people who are ready to help a poor gentlewoman in distress). You see, honoured sir, these orphans of good family--I might even say of aristocratic connections--and that wretch of a general sat eating grouse... and stamped at my disturbing him. ‘Your excellency,’ I said, ‘protect the orphans, for you knew my late husband, Semyon Zaharovitch, and on the very day of his death the basest of scoundrels slandered his only daughter.’... That policeman again! Protect me,” she cried to the official. “Why is that policeman edging up to me? We have only just run away from one of them. What do you want, fool?”\n\n“It’s forbidden in the streets. You mustn’t make a disturbance.”\n\n“It’s you’re making a disturbance. It’s just the same as if I were grinding an organ. What business is it of yours?”\n\n“You have to get a licence for an organ, and you haven’t got one, and in that way you collect a crowd. Where do you lodge?”\n\n“What, a license?” wailed Katerina Ivanovna. “I buried my husband to-day. What need of a license?”\n\n“Calm yourself, madam, calm yourself,” began the official. “Come along; I will escort you.... This is no place for you in the crowd. You are ill.”\n\n“Honoured sir, honoured sir, you don’t know,” screamed Katerina Ivanovna. “We are going to the Nevsky.... Sonia, Sonia! Where is she? She is crying too! What’s the matter with you all? Kolya, Lida, where are you going?” she cried suddenly in alarm. “Oh, silly children! Kolya, Lida, where are they off to?...”\n\nKolya and Lida, scared out of their wits by the crowd, and their mother’s mad pranks, suddenly seized each other by the hand, and ran off at the sight of the policeman who wanted to take them away somewhere. Weeping and wailing, poor Katerina Ivanovna ran after them. She was a piteous and unseemly spectacle, as she ran, weeping and panting for breath. Sonia and Polenka rushed after them.\n\n“Bring them back, bring them back, Sonia! Oh stupid, ungrateful children!... Polenka! catch them.... It’s for your sakes I...”\n\nShe stumbled as she ran and fell down.\n\n“She’s cut herself, she’s bleeding! Oh, dear!” cried Sonia, bending over her.\n\nAll ran up and crowded around. Raskolnikov and Lebeziatnikov were the first at her side, the official too hastened up, and behind him the policeman who muttered, “Bother!” with a gesture of impatience, feeling that the job was going to be a troublesome one.\n\n“Pass on! Pass on!” he said to the crowd that pressed forward.\n\n“She’s dying,” someone shouted.\n\n“She’s gone out of her mind,” said another.\n\n“Lord have mercy upon us,” said a woman, crossing herself. “Have they caught the little girl and the boy? They’re being brought back, the elder one’s got them.... Ah, the naughty imps!”\n\nWhen they examined Katerina Ivanovna carefully, they saw that she had not cut herself against a stone, as Sonia thought, but that the blood that stained the pavement red was from her chest.\n\n“I’ve seen that before,” muttered the official to Raskolnikov and Lebeziatnikov; “that’s consumption; the blood flows and chokes the patient. I saw the same thing with a relative of my own not long ago... nearly a pint of blood, all in a minute.... What’s to be done though? She is dying.”\n\n“This way, this way, to my room!” Sonia implored. “I live here!... See, that house, the second from here.... Come to me, make haste,” she turned from one to the other. “Send for the doctor! Oh, dear!”\n\nThanks to the official’s efforts, this plan was adopted, the policeman even helping to carry Katerina Ivanovna. She was carried to Sonia’s room, almost unconscious, and laid on the bed. The blood was still flowing, but she seemed to be coming to herself. Raskolnikov, Lebeziatnikov, and the official accompanied Sonia into the room and were followed by the policeman, who first drove back the crowd which followed to the very door. Polenka came in holding Kolya and Lida, who were trembling and weeping. Several persons came in too from the Kapernaumovs’ room; the landlord, a lame one-eyed man of strange appearance with whiskers and hair that stood up like a brush, his wife, a woman with an everlastingly scared expression, and several open-mouthed children with wonder-struck faces. Among these, Svidrigaïlov suddenly made his appearance. Raskolnikov looked at him with surprise, not understanding where he had come from and not having noticed him in the crowd. A doctor and priest were spoken of. The official whispered to Raskolnikov that he thought it was too late now for the doctor, but he ordered him to be sent for. Kapernaumov ran himself.\n\nMeanwhile Katerina Ivanovna had regained her breath. The bleeding ceased for a time. She looked with sick but intent and penetrating eyes at Sonia, who stood pale and trembling, wiping the sweat from her brow with a handkerchief. At last she asked to be raised. They sat her up on the bed, supporting her on both sides.\n\n“Where are the children?” she said in a faint voice. “You’ve brought them, Polenka? Oh the sillies! Why did you run away.... Och!”\n\nOnce more her parched lips were covered with blood. She moved her eyes, looking about her.\n\n“So that’s how you live, Sonia! Never once have I been in your room.”\n\nShe looked at her with a face of suffering.\n\n“We have been your ruin, Sonia. Polenka, Lida, Kolya, come here! Well, here they are, Sonia, take them all! I hand them over to you, I’ve had enough! The ball is over.” (Cough!) “Lay me down, let me die in peace.”\n\nThey laid her back on the pillow.\n\n“What, the priest? I don’t want him. You haven’t got a rouble to spare. I have no sins. God must forgive me without that. He knows how I have suffered.... And if He won’t forgive me, I don’t care!”\n\nShe sank more and more into uneasy delirium. At times she shuddered, turned her eyes from side to side, recognised everyone for a minute, but at once sank into delirium again. Her breathing was hoarse and difficult, there was a sort of rattle in her throat.\n\n“I said to him, your excellency,” she ejaculated, gasping after each word. “That Amalia Ludwigovna, ah! Lida, Kolya, hands on your hips, make haste! _Glissez, glissez! pas de basque!_ Tap with your heels, be a graceful child!\n\n“_Du hast Diamanten und Perlen_\n\n“What next? That’s the thing to sing.\n“_Du hast die schönsten Augen Mädchen, was willst du mehr?_\n\n“What an idea! _Was willst du mehr?_ What things the fool invents! Ah, yes!\n\n“In the heat of midday in the vale of Dagestan.\n\n“Ah, how I loved it! I loved that song to distraction, Polenka! Your father, you know, used to sing it when we were engaged.... Oh, those days! Oh, that’s the thing for us to sing! How does it go? I’ve forgotten. Remind me! How was it?”\n\nShe was violently excited and tried to sit up. At last, in a horribly hoarse, broken voice, she began, shrieking and gasping at every word, with a look of growing terror.\n\n“In the heat of midday!... in the vale!... of Dagestan!... With lead in my breast!...”\n\n“Your excellency!” she wailed suddenly with a heart-rending scream and a flood of tears, “protect the orphans! You have been their father’s guest... one may say aristocratic....” She started, regaining consciousness, and gazed at all with a sort of terror, but at once recognised Sonia.\n\n“Sonia, Sonia!” she articulated softly and caressingly, as though surprised to find her there. “Sonia darling, are you here, too?”\n\nThey lifted her up again.\n\n“Enough! It’s over! Farewell, poor thing! I am done for! I am broken!” she cried with vindictive despair, and her head fell heavily back on the pillow.\n\nShe sank into unconsciousness again, but this time it did not last long. Her pale, yellow, wasted face dropped back, her mouth fell open, her leg moved convulsively, she gave a deep, deep sigh and died.\n\nSonia fell upon her, flung her arms about her, and remained motionless with her head pressed to the dead woman’s wasted bosom. Polenka threw herself at her mother’s feet, kissing them and weeping violently. Though Kolya and Lida did not understand what had happened, they had a feeling that it was something terrible; they put their hands on each other’s little shoulders, stared straight at one another and both at once opened their mouths and began screaming. They were both still in their fancy dress; one in a turban, the other in the cap with the ostrich feather.\n\nAnd how did “the certificate of merit” come to be on the bed beside Katerina Ivanovna? It lay there by the pillow; Raskolnikov saw it.\n\nHe walked away to the window. Lebeziatnikov skipped up to him.\n\n“She is dead,” he said.\n\n“Rodion Romanovitch, I must have two words with you,” said Svidrigaïlov, coming up to them.\n\nLebeziatnikov at once made room for him and delicately withdrew. Svidrigaïlov drew Raskolnikov further away.\n\n“I will undertake all the arrangements, the funeral and that. You know it’s a question of money and, as I told you, I have plenty to spare. I will put those two little ones and Polenka into some good orphan asylum, and I will settle fifteen hundred roubles to be paid to each on coming of age, so that Sofya Semyonovna need have no anxiety about them. And I will pull her out of the mud too, for she is a good girl, isn’t she? So tell Avdotya Romanovna that that is how I am spending her ten thousand.”\n\n“What is your motive for such benevolence?” asked Raskolnikov.\n\n“Ah! you sceptical person!” laughed Svidrigaïlov. “I told you I had no need of that money. Won’t you admit that it’s simply done from humanity? She wasn’t ‘a louse,’ you know” (he pointed to the corner where the dead woman lay), “was she, like some old pawnbroker woman? Come, you’ll agree, is Luzhin to go on living, and doing wicked things or is she to die? And if I didn’t help them, Polenka would go the same way.”\n\nHe said this with an air of a sort of gay winking slyness, keeping his eyes fixed on Raskolnikov, who turned white and cold, hearing his own phrases, spoken to Sonia. He quickly stepped back and looked wildly at Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“How do you know?” he whispered, hardly able to breathe.\n\n“Why, I lodge here at Madame Resslich’s, the other side of the wall. Here is Kapernaumov, and there lives Madame Resslich, an old and devoted friend of mine. I am a neighbour.”\n\n“You?”\n\n“Yes,” continued Svidrigaïlov, shaking with laughter. “I assure you on my honour, dear Rodion Romanovitch, that you have interested me enormously. I told you we should become friends, I foretold it. Well, here we have. And you will see what an accommodating person I am. You’ll see that you can get on with me!”\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nA strange period began for Raskolnikov: it was as though a fog had fallen upon him and wrapped him in a dreary solitude from which there was no escape. Recalling that period long after, he believed that his mind had been clouded at times, and that it had continued so, with intervals, till the final catastrophe. He was convinced that he had been mistaken about many things at that time, for instance as to the date of certain events. Anyway, when he tried later on to piece his recollections together, he learnt a great deal about himself from what other people told him. He had mixed up incidents and had explained events as due to circumstances which existed only in his imagination. At times he was a prey to agonies of morbid uneasiness, amounting sometimes to panic. But he remembered, too, moments, hours, perhaps whole days, of complete apathy, which came upon him as a reaction from his previous terror and might be compared with the abnormal insensibility, sometimes seen in the dying. He seemed to be trying in that latter stage to escape from a full and clear understanding of his position. Certain essential facts which required immediate consideration were particularly irksome to him. How glad he would have been to be free from some cares, the neglect of which would have threatened him with complete, inevitable ruin.\n\nHe was particularly worried about Svidrigaïlov, he might be said to be permanently thinking of Svidrigaïlov. From the time of Svidrigaïlov’s too menacing and unmistakable words in Sonia’s room at the moment of Katerina Ivanovna’s death, the normal working of his mind seemed to break down. But although this new fact caused him extreme uneasiness, Raskolnikov was in no hurry for an explanation of it. At times, finding himself in a solitary and remote part of the town, in some wretched eating-house, sitting alone lost in thought, hardly knowing how he had come there, he suddenly thought of Svidrigaïlov. He recognised suddenly, clearly, and with dismay that he ought at once to come to an understanding with that man and to make what terms he could. Walking outside the city gates one day, he positively fancied that they had fixed a meeting there, that he was waiting for Svidrigaïlov. Another time he woke up before daybreak lying on the ground under some bushes and could not at first understand how he had come there.\n\nBut during the two or three days after Katerina Ivanovna’s death, he had two or three times met Svidrigaïlov at Sonia’s lodging, where he had gone aimlessly for a moment. They exchanged a few words and made no reference to the vital subject, as though they were tacitly agreed not to speak of it for a time.\n\nKaterina Ivanovna’s body was still lying in the coffin, Svidrigaïlov was busy making arrangements for the funeral. Sonia too was very busy. At their last meeting Svidrigaïlov informed Raskolnikov that he had made an arrangement, and a very satisfactory one, for Katerina Ivanovna’s children; that he had, through certain connections, succeeded in getting hold of certain personages by whose help the three orphans could be at once placed in very suitable institutions; that the money he had settled on them had been of great assistance, as it is much easier to place orphans with some property than destitute ones. He said something too about Sonia and promised to come himself in a day or two to see Raskolnikov, mentioning that “he would like to consult with him, that there were things they must talk over....”\n\nThis conversation took place in the passage on the stairs. Svidrigaïlov looked intently at Raskolnikov and suddenly, after a brief pause, dropping his voice, asked: “But how is it, Rodion Romanovitch; you don’t seem yourself? You look and you listen, but you don’t seem to understand. Cheer up! We’ll talk things over; I am only sorry, I’ve so much to do of my own business and other people’s. Ah, Rodion Romanovitch,” he added suddenly, “what all men need is fresh air, fresh air... more than anything!”\n\nHe moved to one side to make way for the priest and server, who were coming up the stairs. They had come for the requiem service. By Svidrigaïlov’s orders it was sung twice a day punctually. Svidrigaïlov went his way. Raskolnikov stood still a moment, thought, and followed the priest into Sonia’s room. He stood at the door. They began quietly, slowly and mournfully singing the service. From his childhood the thought of death and the presence of death had something oppressive and mysteriously awful; and it was long since he had heard the requiem service. And there was something else here as well, too awful and disturbing. He looked at the children: they were all kneeling by the coffin; Polenka was weeping. Behind them Sonia prayed, softly and, as it were, timidly weeping.\n\n“These last two days she hasn’t said a word to me, she hasn’t glanced at me,” Raskolnikov thought suddenly. The sunlight was bright in the room; the incense rose in clouds; the priest read, “Give rest, oh Lord....” Raskolnikov stayed all through the service. As he blessed them and took his leave, the priest looked round strangely. After the service, Raskolnikov went up to Sonia. She took both his hands and let her head sink on his shoulder. This slight friendly gesture bewildered Raskolnikov. It seemed strange to him that there was no trace of repugnance, no trace of disgust, no tremor in her hand. It was the furthest limit of self-abnegation, at least so he interpreted it.\n\nSonia said nothing. Raskolnikov pressed her hand and went out. He felt very miserable. If it had been possible to escape to some solitude, he would have thought himself lucky, even if he had to spend his whole life there. But although he had almost always been by himself of late, he had never been able to feel alone. Sometimes he walked out of the town on to the high road, once he had even reached a little wood, but the lonelier the place was, the more he seemed to be aware of an uneasy presence near him. It did not frighten him, but greatly annoyed him, so that he made haste to return to the town, to mingle with the crowd, to enter restaurants and taverns, to walk in busy thoroughfares. There he felt easier and even more solitary. One day at dusk he sat for an hour listening to songs in a tavern and he remembered that he positively enjoyed it. But at last he had suddenly felt the same uneasiness again, as though his conscience smote him. “Here I sit listening to singing, is that what I ought to be doing?” he thought. Yet he felt at once that that was not the only cause of his uneasiness; there was something requiring immediate decision, but it was something he could not clearly understand or put into words. It was a hopeless tangle. “No, better the struggle again! Better Porfiry again... or Svidrigaïlov.... Better some challenge again... some attack. Yes, yes!” he thought. He went out of the tavern and rushed away almost at a run. The thought of Dounia and his mother suddenly reduced him almost to a panic. That night he woke up before morning among some bushes in Krestovsky Island, trembling all over with fever; he walked home, and it was early morning when he arrived. After some hours’ sleep the fever left him, but he woke up late, two o’clock in the afternoon.\n\nHe remembered that Katerina Ivanovna’s funeral had been fixed for that day, and was glad that he was not present at it. Nastasya brought him some food; he ate and drank with appetite, almost with greediness. His head was fresher and he was calmer than he had been for the last three days. He even felt a passing wonder at his previous attacks of panic.\n\nThe door opened and Razumihin came in.\n\n“Ah, he’s eating, then he’s not ill,” said Razumihin. He took a chair and sat down at the table opposite Raskolnikov.\n\nHe was troubled and did not attempt to conceal it. He spoke with evident annoyance, but without hurry or raising his voice. He looked as though he had some special fixed determination.\n\n“Listen,” he began resolutely. “As far as I am concerned, you may all go to hell, but from what I see, it’s clear to me that I can’t make head or tail of it; please don’t think I’ve come to ask you questions. I don’t want to know, hang it! If you begin telling me your secrets, I dare say I shouldn’t stay to listen, I should go away cursing. I have only come to find out once for all whether it’s a fact that you are mad? There is a conviction in the air that you are mad or very nearly so. I admit I’ve been disposed to that opinion myself, judging from your stupid, repulsive and quite inexplicable actions, and from your recent behavior to your mother and sister. Only a monster or a madman could treat them as you have; so you must be mad.”\n\n“When did you see them last?”\n\n“Just now. Haven’t you seen them since then? What have you been doing with yourself? Tell me, please. I’ve been to you three times already. Your mother has been seriously ill since yesterday. She had made up her mind to come to you; Avdotya Romanovna tried to prevent her; she wouldn’t hear a word. ‘If he is ill, if his mind is giving way, who can look after him like his mother?’ she said. We all came here together, we couldn’t let her come alone all the way. We kept begging her to be calm. We came in, you weren’t here; she sat down, and stayed ten minutes, while we stood waiting in silence. She got up and said: ‘If he’s gone out, that is, if he is well, and has forgotten his mother, it’s humiliating and unseemly for his mother to stand at his door begging for kindness.’ She returned home and took to her bed; now she is in a fever. ‘I see,’ she said, ‘that he has time for _his girl_.’ She means by _your girl_ Sofya Semyonovna, your betrothed or your mistress, I don’t know. I went at once to Sofya Semyonovna’s, for I wanted to know what was going on. I looked round, I saw the coffin, the children crying, and Sofya Semyonovna trying them on mourning dresses. No sign of you. I apologised, came away, and reported to Avdotya Romanovna. So that’s all nonsense and you haven’t got a girl; the most likely thing is that you are mad. But here you sit, guzzling boiled beef as though you’d not had a bite for three days. Though as far as that goes, madmen eat too, but though you have not said a word to me yet... you are not mad! That I’d swear! Above all, you are not mad! So you may go to hell, all of you, for there’s some mystery, some secret about it, and I don’t intend to worry my brains over your secrets. So I’ve simply come to swear at you,” he finished, getting up, “to relieve my mind. And I know what to do now.”\n\n“What do you mean to do now?”\n\n“What business is it of yours what I mean to do?”\n\n“You are going in for a drinking bout.”\n\n“How... how did you know?”\n\n“Why, it’s pretty plain.”\n\nRazumihin paused for a minute.\n\n“You always have been a very rational person and you’ve never been mad, never,” he observed suddenly with warmth. “You’re right: I shall drink. Good-bye!”\n\nAnd he moved to go out.\n\n“I was talking with my sister--the day before yesterday, I think it was--about you, Razumihin.”\n\n“About me! But... where can you have seen her the day before yesterday?” Razumihin stopped short and even turned a little pale.\n\nOne could see that his heart was throbbing slowly and violently.\n\n“She came here by herself, sat there and talked to me.”\n\n“She did!”\n“Yes.”\n\n“What did you say to her... I mean, about me?”\n\n“I told her you were a very good, honest, and industrious man. I didn’t tell her you love her, because she knows that herself.”\n\n“She knows that herself?”\n\n“Well, it’s pretty plain. Wherever I might go, whatever happened to me, you would remain to look after them. I, so to speak, give them into your keeping, Razumihin. I say this because I know quite well how you love her, and am convinced of the purity of your heart. I know that she too may love you and perhaps does love you already. Now decide for yourself, as you know best, whether you need go in for a drinking bout or not.”\n\n“Rodya! You see... well.... Ach, damn it! But where do you mean to go? Of course, if it’s all a secret, never mind.... But I... I shall find out the secret... and I am sure that it must be some ridiculous nonsense and that you’ve made it all up. Anyway you are a capital fellow, a capital fellow!...”\n\n“That was just what I wanted to add, only you interrupted, that that was a very good decision of yours not to find out these secrets. Leave it to time, don’t worry about it. You’ll know it all in time when it must be. Yesterday a man said to me that what a man needs is fresh air, fresh air, fresh air. I mean to go to him directly to find out what he meant by that.”\n\nRazumihin stood lost in thought and excitement, making a silent conclusion.\n\n“He’s a political conspirator! He must be. And he’s on the eve of some desperate step, that’s certain. It can only be that! And... and Dounia knows,” he thought suddenly.\n\n“So Avdotya Romanovna comes to see you,” he said, weighing each syllable, “and you’re going to see a man who says we need more air, and so of course that letter... that too must have something to do with it,” he concluded to himself.\n\n“What letter?”\n\n“She got a letter to-day. It upset her very much--very much indeed. Too much so. I began speaking of you, she begged me not to. Then... then she said that perhaps we should very soon have to part... then she began warmly thanking me for something; then she went to her room and locked herself in.”\n\n“She got a letter?” Raskolnikov asked thoughtfully.\n\n“Yes, and you didn’t know? hm...”\n\nThey were both silent.\n\n“Good-bye, Rodion. There was a time, brother, when I.... Never mind, good-bye. You see, there was a time.... Well, good-bye! I must be off too. I am not going to drink. There’s no need now.... That’s all stuff!”\n\nHe hurried out; but when he had almost closed the door behind him, he suddenly opened it again, and said, looking away:\n\n“Oh, by the way, do you remember that murder, you know Porfiry’s, that old woman? Do you know the murderer has been found, he has confessed and given the proofs. It’s one of those very workmen, the painter, only fancy! Do you remember I defended them here? Would you believe it, all that scene of fighting and laughing with his companions on the stairs while the porter and the two witnesses were going up, he got up on purpose to disarm suspicion. The cunning, the presence of mind of the young dog! One can hardly credit it; but it’s his own explanation, he has confessed it all. And what a fool I was about it! Well, he’s simply a genius of hypocrisy and resourcefulness in disarming the suspicions of the lawyers--so there’s nothing much to wonder at, I suppose! Of course people like that are always possible. And the fact that he couldn’t keep up the character, but confessed, makes him easier to believe in. But what a fool I was! I was frantic on their side!”\n\n“Tell me, please, from whom did you hear that, and why does it interest you so?” Raskolnikov asked with unmistakable agitation.\n\n“What next? You ask me why it interests me!... Well, I heard it from Porfiry, among others... It was from him I heard almost all about it.”\n\n“From Porfiry?”\n\n“From Porfiry.”\n\n“What... what did he say?” Raskolnikov asked in dismay.\n\n“He gave me a capital explanation of it. Psychologically, after his fashion.”\n\n“He explained it? Explained it himself?”\n\n“Yes, yes; good-bye. I’ll tell you all about it another time, but now I’m busy. There was a time when I fancied... But no matter, another time!... What need is there for me to drink now? You have made me drunk without wine. I am drunk, Rodya! Good-bye, I’m going. I’ll come again very soon.”\n\nHe went out.\n\n“He’s a political conspirator, there’s not a doubt about it,” Razumihin decided, as he slowly descended the stairs. “And he’s drawn his sister in; that’s quite, quite in keeping with Avdotya Romanovna’s character. There are interviews between them!... She hinted at it too... So many of her words.... and hints... bear that meaning! And how else can all this tangle be explained? Hm! And I was almost thinking... Good heavens, what I thought! Yes, I took leave of my senses and I wronged him! It was his doing, under the lamp in the corridor that day. Pfoo! What a crude, nasty, vile idea on my part! Nikolay is a brick, for confessing.... And how clear it all is now! His illness then, all his strange actions... before this, in the university, how morose he used to be, how gloomy.... But what’s the meaning now of that letter? There’s something in that, too, perhaps. Whom was it from? I suspect...! No, I must find out!”\n\nHe thought of Dounia, realising all he had heard and his heart throbbed, and he suddenly broke into a run.\n\nAs soon as Razumihin went out, Raskolnikov got up, turned to the window, walked into one corner and then into another, as though forgetting the smallness of his room, and sat down again on the sofa. He felt, so to speak, renewed; again the struggle, so a means of escape had come.\n\n“Yes, a means of escape had come! It had been too stifling, too cramping, the burden had been too agonising. A lethargy had come upon him at times. From the moment of the scene with Nikolay at Porfiry’s he had been suffocating, penned in without hope of escape. After Nikolay’s confession, on that very day had come the scene with Sonia; his behaviour and his last words had been utterly unlike anything he could have imagined beforehand; he had grown feebler, instantly and fundamentally! And he had agreed at the time with Sonia, he had agreed in his heart he could not go on living alone with such a thing on his mind!\n\n“And Svidrigaïlov was a riddle... He worried him, that was true, but somehow not on the same point. He might still have a struggle to come with Svidrigaïlov. Svidrigaïlov, too, might be a means of escape; but Porfiry was a different matter.\n\n“And so Porfiry himself had explained it to Razumihin, had explained it _psychologically_. He had begun bringing in his damned psychology again! Porfiry? But to think that Porfiry should for one moment believe that Nikolay was guilty, after what had passed between them before Nikolay’s appearance, after that tête-à-tête interview, which could have only _one_ explanation? (During those days Raskolnikov had often recalled passages in that scene with Porfiry; he could not bear to let his mind rest on it.) Such words, such gestures had passed between them, they had exchanged such glances, things had been said in such a tone and had reached such a pass, that Nikolay, whom Porfiry had seen through at the first word, at the first gesture, could not have shaken his conviction.\n\n“And to think that even Razumihin had begun to suspect! The scene in the corridor under the lamp had produced its effect then. He had rushed to Porfiry.... But what had induced the latter to receive him like that? What had been his object in putting Razumihin off with Nikolay? He must have some plan; there was some design, but what was it? It was true that a long time had passed since that morning--too long a time--and no sight nor sound of Porfiry. Well, that was a bad sign....”\n\nRaskolnikov took his cap and went out of the room, still pondering. It was the first time for a long while that he had felt clear in his mind, at least. “I must settle Svidrigaïlov,” he thought, “and as soon as possible; he, too, seems to be waiting for me to come to him of my own accord.” And at that moment there was such a rush of hate in his weary heart that he might have killed either of those two--Porfiry or Svidrigaïlov. At least he felt that he would be capable of doing it later, if not now.\n\n“We shall see, we shall see,” he repeated to himself.\n\nBut no sooner had he opened the door than he stumbled upon Porfiry himself in the passage. He was coming in to see him. Raskolnikov was dumbfounded for a minute, but only for one minute. Strange to say, he was not very much astonished at seeing Porfiry and scarcely afraid of him. He was simply startled, but was quickly, instantly, on his guard. “Perhaps this will mean the end? But how could Porfiry have approached so quietly, like a cat, so that he had heard nothing? Could he have been listening at the door?”\n\n“You didn’t expect a visitor, Rodion Romanovitch,” Porfiry explained, laughing. “I’ve been meaning to look in a long time; I was passing by and thought why not go in for five minutes. Are you going out? I won’t keep you long. Just let me have one cigarette.”\n\n“Sit down, Porfiry Petrovitch, sit down.” Raskolnikov gave his visitor a seat with so pleased and friendly an expression that he would have marvelled at himself, if he could have seen it.\n\nThe last moment had come, the last drops had to be drained! So a man will sometimes go through half an hour of mortal terror with a brigand, yet when the knife is at his throat at last, he feels no fear.\n\nRaskolnikov seated himself directly facing Porfiry, and looked at him without flinching. Porfiry screwed up his eyes and began lighting a cigarette.\n\n“Speak, speak,” seemed as though it would burst from Raskolnikov’s heart. “Come, why don’t you speak?”\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“Ah these cigarettes!” Porfiry Petrovitch ejaculated at last, having lighted one. “They are pernicious, positively pernicious, and yet I can’t give them up! I cough, I begin to have tickling in my throat and a difficulty in breathing. You know I am a coward, I went lately to Dr. B----n; he always gives at least half an hour to each patient. He positively laughed looking at me; he sounded me: ‘Tobacco’s bad for you,’ he said, ‘your lungs are affected.’ But how am I to give it up? What is there to take its place? I don’t drink, that’s the mischief, he-he-he, that I don’t. Everything is relative, Rodion Romanovitch, everything is relative!”\n\n“Why, he’s playing his professional tricks again,” Raskolnikov thought with disgust. All the circumstances of their last interview suddenly came back to him, and he felt a rush of the feeling that had come upon him then.\n\n“I came to see you the day before yesterday, in the evening; you didn’t know?” Porfiry Petrovitch went on, looking round the room. “I came into this very room. I was passing by, just as I did to-day, and I thought I’d return your call. I walked in as your door was wide open, I looked round, waited and went out without leaving my name with your servant. Don’t you lock your door?”\n\nRaskolnikov’s face grew more and more gloomy. Porfiry seemed to guess his state of mind.\n\n“I’ve come to have it out with you, Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow! I owe you an explanation and must give it to you,” he continued with a slight smile, just patting Raskolnikov’s knee.\n\nBut almost at the same instant a serious and careworn look came into his face; to his surprise Raskolnikov saw a touch of sadness in it. He had never seen and never suspected such an expression in his face.\n\n“A strange scene passed between us last time we met, Rodion Romanovitch. Our first interview, too, was a strange one; but then... and one thing after another! This is the point: I have perhaps acted unfairly to you; I feel it. Do you remember how we parted? Your nerves were unhinged and your knees were shaking and so were mine. And, you know, our behaviour was unseemly, even ungentlemanly. And yet we are gentlemen, above all, in any case, gentlemen; that must be understood. Do you remember what we came to?... and it was quite indecorous.”\n\n“What is he up to, what does he take me for?” Raskolnikov asked himself in amazement, raising his head and looking with open eyes on Porfiry.\n\n“I’ve decided openness is better between us,” Porfiry Petrovitch went on, turning his head away and dropping his eyes, as though unwilling to disconcert his former victim and as though disdaining his former wiles. “Yes, such suspicions and such scenes cannot continue for long. Nikolay put a stop to it, or I don’t know what we might not have come to. That damned workman was sitting at the time in the next room--can you realise that? You know that, of course; and I am aware that he came to you afterwards. But what you supposed then was not true: I had not sent for anyone, I had made no kind of arrangements. You ask why I hadn’t? What shall I say to you? it had all come upon me so suddenly. I had scarcely sent for the porters (you noticed them as you went out, I dare say). An idea flashed upon me; I was firmly convinced at the time, you see, Rodion Romanovitch. Come, I thought--even if I let one thing slip for a time, I shall get hold of something else--I shan’t lose what I want, anyway. You are nervously irritable, Rodion Romanovitch, by temperament; it’s out of proportion with other qualities of your heart and character, which I flatter myself I have to some extent divined. Of course I did reflect even then that it does not always happen that a man gets up and blurts out his whole story. It does happen sometimes, if you make a man lose all patience, though even then it’s rare. I was capable of realising that. If I only had a fact, I thought, the least little fact to go upon, something I could lay hold of, something tangible, not merely psychological. For if a man is guilty, you must be able to get something substantial out of him; one may reckon upon most surprising results indeed. I was reckoning on your temperament, Rodion Romanovitch, on your temperament above all things! I had great hopes of you at that time.”\n\n“But what are you driving at now?” Raskolnikov muttered at last, asking the question without thinking.\n\n“What is he talking about?” he wondered distractedly, “does he really take me to be innocent?”\n\n“What am I driving at? I’ve come to explain myself, I consider it my duty, so to speak. I want to make clear to you how the whole business, the whole misunderstanding arose. I’ve caused you a great deal of suffering, Rodion Romanovitch. I am not a monster. I understand what it must mean for a man who has been unfortunate, but who is proud, imperious and above all, impatient, to have to bear such treatment! I regard you in any case as a man of noble character and not without elements of magnanimity, though I don’t agree with all your convictions. I wanted to tell you this first, frankly and quite sincerely, for above all I don’t want to deceive you. When I made your acquaintance, I felt attracted by you. Perhaps you will laugh at my saying so. You have a right to. I know you disliked me from the first and indeed you’ve no reason to like me. You may think what you like, but I desire now to do all I can to efface that impression and to show that I am a man of heart and conscience. I speak sincerely.”\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch made a dignified pause. Raskolnikov felt a rush of renewed alarm. The thought that Porfiry believed him to be innocent began to make him uneasy.\n\n“It’s scarcely necessary to go over everything in detail,” Porfiry Petrovitch went on. “Indeed, I could scarcely attempt it. To begin with there were rumours. Through whom, how, and when those rumours came to me... and how they affected you, I need not go into. My suspicions were aroused by a complete accident, which might just as easily not have happened. What was it? Hm! I believe there is no need to go into that either. Those rumours and that accident led to one idea in my mind. I admit it openly--for one may as well make a clean breast of it--I was the first to pitch on you. The old woman’s notes on the pledges and the rest of it--that all came to nothing. Yours was one of a hundred. I happened, too, to hear of the scene at the office, from a man who described it capitally, unconsciously reproducing the scene with great vividness. It was just one thing after another, Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow! How could I avoid being brought to certain ideas? From a hundred rabbits you can’t make a horse, a hundred suspicions don’t make a proof, as the English proverb says, but that’s only from the rational point of view--you can’t help being partial, for after all a lawyer is only human. I thought, too, of your article in that journal, do you remember, on your first visit we talked of it? I jeered at you at the time, but that was only to lead you on. I repeat, Rodion Romanovitch, you are ill and impatient. That you were bold, headstrong, in earnest and... had felt a great deal I recognised long before. I, too, have felt the same, so that your article seemed familiar to me. It was conceived on sleepless nights, with a throbbing heart, in ecstasy and suppressed enthusiasm. And that proud suppressed enthusiasm in young people is dangerous! I jeered at you then, but let me tell you that, as a literary amateur, I am awfully fond of such first essays, full of the heat of youth. There is a mistiness and a chord vibrating in the mist. Your article is absurd and fantastic, but there’s a transparent sincerity, a youthful incorruptible pride and the daring of despair in it. It’s a gloomy article, but that’s what’s fine in it. I read your article and put it aside, thinking as I did so ‘that man won’t go the common way.’ Well, I ask you, after that as a preliminary, how could I help being carried away by what followed? Oh, dear, I am not saying anything, I am not making any statement now. I simply noted it at the time. What is there in it? I reflected. There’s nothing in it, that is really nothing and perhaps absolutely nothing. And it’s not at all the thing for the prosecutor to let himself be carried away by notions: here I have Nikolay on my hands with actual evidence against him--you may think what you like of it, but it’s evidence. He brings in his psychology, too; one has to consider him, too, for it’s a matter of life and death. Why am I explaining this to you? That you may understand, and not blame my malicious behaviour on that occasion. It was not malicious, I assure you, he-he! Do you suppose I didn’t come to search your room at the time? I did, I did, he-he! I was here when you were lying ill in bed, not officially, not in my own person, but I was here. Your room was searched to the last thread at the first suspicion; but _umsonst_! I thought to myself, now that man will come, will come of himself and quickly, too; if he’s guilty, he’s sure to come. Another man wouldn’t, but he will. And you remember how Mr. Razumihin began discussing the subject with you? We arranged that to excite you, so we purposely spread rumours, that he might discuss the case with you, and Razumihin is not a man to restrain his indignation. Mr. Zametov was tremendously struck by your anger and your open daring. Think of blurting out in a restaurant ‘I killed her.’ It was too daring, too reckless. I thought so myself, if he is guilty he will be a formidable opponent. That was what I thought at the time. I was expecting you. But you simply bowled Zametov over and... well, you see, it all lies in this--that this damnable psychology can be taken two ways! Well, I kept expecting you, and so it was, you came! My heart was fairly throbbing. Ach!\n\n“Now, why need you have come? Your laughter, too, as you came in, do you remember? I saw it all plain as daylight, but if I hadn’t expected you so specially, I should not have noticed anything in your laughter. You see what influence a mood has! Mr. Razumihin then--ah, that stone, that stone under which the things were hidden! I seem to see it somewhere in a kitchen garden. It was in a kitchen garden, you told Zametov and afterwards you repeated that in my office? And when we began picking your article to pieces, how you explained it! One could take every word of yours in two senses, as though there were another meaning hidden.\n\n“So in this way, Rodion Romanovitch, I reached the furthest limit, and knocking my head against a post, I pulled myself up, asking myself what I was about. After all, I said, you can take it all in another sense if you like, and it’s more natural so, indeed. I couldn’t help admitting it was more natural. I was bothered! ‘No, I’d better get hold of some little fact’ I said. So when I heard of the bell-ringing, I held my breath and was all in a tremor. ‘Here is my little fact,’ thought I, and I didn’t think it over, I simply wouldn’t. I would have given a thousand roubles at that minute to have seen you with my own eyes, when you walked a hundred paces beside that workman, after he had called you murderer to your face, and you did not dare to ask him a question all the way. And then what about your trembling, what about your bell-ringing in your illness, in semi-delirium?\n\n“And so, Rodion Romanovitch, can you wonder that I played such pranks on you? And what made you come at that very minute? Someone seemed to have sent you, by Jove! And if Nikolay had not parted us... and do you remember Nikolay at the time? Do you remember him clearly? It was a thunderbolt, a regular thunderbolt! And how I met him! I didn’t believe in the thunderbolt, not for a minute. You could see it for yourself; and how could I? Even afterwards, when you had gone and he began making very, very plausible answers on certain points, so that I was surprised at him myself, even then I didn’t believe his story! You see what it is to be as firm as a rock! No, thought I, _Morgenfrüh_. What has Nikolay got to do with it!”\n\n“Razumihin told me just now that you think Nikolay guilty and had yourself assured him of it....”\n\nHis voice failed him, and he broke off. He had been listening in indescribable agitation, as this man who had seen through and through him, went back upon himself. He was afraid of believing it and did not believe it. In those still ambiguous words he kept eagerly looking for something more definite and conclusive.\n\n“Mr. Razumihin!” cried Porfiry Petrovitch, seeming glad of a question from Raskolnikov, who had till then been silent. “He-he-he! But I had to put Mr. Razumihin off; two is company, three is none. Mr. Razumihin is not the right man, besides he is an outsider. He came running to me with a pale face.... But never mind him, why bring him in? To return to Nikolay, would you like to know what sort of a type he is, how I understand him, that is? To begin with, he is still a child and not exactly a coward, but something by way of an artist. Really, don’t laugh at my describing him so. He is innocent and responsive to influence. He has a heart, and is a fantastic fellow. He sings and dances, he tells stories, they say, so that people come from other villages to hear him. He attends school too, and laughs till he cries if you hold up a finger to him; he will drink himself senseless--not as a regular vice, but at times, when people treat him, like a child. And he stole, too, then, without knowing it himself, for ‘How can it be stealing, if one picks it up?’ And do you know he is an Old Believer, or rather a dissenter? There have been Wanderers[*] in his family, and he was for two years in his village under the spiritual guidance of a certain elder. I learnt all this from Nikolay and from his fellow villagers. And what’s more, he wanted to run into the wilderness! He was full of fervour, prayed at night, read the old books, ‘the true’ ones, and read himself crazy.\n\n[*] A religious sect.--TRANSLATOR’S NOTE.\n\n“Petersburg had a great effect upon him, especially the women and the wine. He responds to everything and he forgot the elder and all that. I learnt that an artist here took a fancy to him, and used to go and see him, and now this business came upon him.\n\n“Well, he was frightened, he tried to hang himself! He ran away! How can one get over the idea the people have of Russian legal proceedings? The very word ‘trial’ frightens some of them. Whose fault is it? We shall see what the new juries will do. God grant they do good! Well, in prison, it seems, he remembered the venerable elder; the Bible, too, made its appearance again. Do you know, Rodion Romanovitch, the force of the word ‘suffering’ among some of these people! It’s not a question of suffering for someone’s benefit, but simply, ‘one must suffer.’ If they suffer at the hands of the authorities, so much the better. In my time there was a very meek and mild prisoner who spent a whole year in prison always reading his Bible on the stove at night and he read himself crazy, and so crazy, do you know, that one day, apropos of nothing, he seized a brick and flung it at the governor; though he had done him no harm. And the way he threw it too: aimed it a yard on one side on purpose, for fear of hurting him. Well, we know what happens to a prisoner who assaults an officer with a weapon. So ‘he took his suffering.’\n\n“So I suspect now that Nikolay wants to take his suffering or something of the sort. I know it for certain from facts, indeed. Only he doesn’t know that I know. What, you don’t admit that there are such fantastic people among the peasants? Lots of them. The elder now has begun influencing him, especially since he tried to hang himself. But he’ll come and tell me all himself. You think he’ll hold out? Wait a bit, he’ll take his words back. I am waiting from hour to hour for him to come and abjure his evidence. I have come to like that Nikolay and am studying him in detail. And what do you think? He-he! He answered me very plausibly on some points, he obviously had collected some evidence and prepared himself cleverly. But on other points he is simply at sea, knows nothing and doesn’t even suspect that he doesn’t know!\n\n“No, Rodion Romanovitch, Nikolay doesn’t come in! This is a fantastic, gloomy business, a modern case, an incident of to-day when the heart of man is troubled, when the phrase is quoted that blood ‘renews,’ when comfort is preached as the aim of life. Here we have bookish dreams, a heart unhinged by theories. Here we see resolution in the first stage, but resolution of a special kind: he resolved to do it like jumping over a precipice or from a bell tower and his legs shook as he went to the crime. He forgot to shut the door after him, and murdered two people for a theory. He committed the murder and couldn’t take the money, and what he did manage to snatch up he hid under a stone. It wasn’t enough for him to suffer agony behind the door while they battered at the door and rung the bell, no, he had to go to the empty lodging, half delirious, to recall the bell-ringing, he wanted to feel the cold shiver over again.... Well, that we grant, was through illness, but consider this: he is a murderer, but looks upon himself as an honest man, despises others, poses as injured innocence. No, that’s not the work of a Nikolay, my dear Rodion Romanovitch!”\n\nAll that had been said before had sounded so like a recantation that these words were too great a shock. Raskolnikov shuddered as though he had been stabbed.\n\n“Then... who then... is the murderer?” he asked in a breathless voice, unable to restrain himself.\n\nPorfiry Petrovitch sank back in his chair, as though he were amazed at the question.\n\n“Who is the murderer?” he repeated, as though unable to believe his ears. “Why, _you_, Rodion Romanovitch! You are the murderer,” he added, almost in a whisper, in a voice of genuine conviction.\n\nRaskolnikov leapt from the sofa, stood up for a few seconds and sat down again without uttering a word. His face twitched convulsively.\n\n“Your lip is twitching just as it did before,” Porfiry Petrovitch observed almost sympathetically. “You’ve been misunderstanding me, I think, Rodion Romanovitch,” he added after a brief pause, “that’s why you are so surprised. I came on purpose to tell you everything and deal openly with you.”\n\n“It was not I murdered her,” Raskolnikov whispered like a frightened child caught in the act.\n\n“No, it was you, you Rodion Romanovitch, and no one else,” Porfiry whispered sternly, with conviction.\n\nThey were both silent and the silence lasted strangely long, about ten minutes. Raskolnikov put his elbow on the table and passed his fingers through his hair. Porfiry Petrovitch sat quietly waiting. Suddenly Raskolnikov looked scornfully at Porfiry.\n\n“You are at your old tricks again, Porfiry Petrovitch! Your old method again. I wonder you don’t get sick of it!”\n\n“Oh, stop that, what does that matter now? It would be a different matter if there were witnesses present, but we are whispering alone. You see yourself that I have not come to chase and capture you like a hare. Whether you confess it or not is nothing to me now; for myself, I am convinced without it.”\n\n“If so, what did you come for?” Raskolnikov asked irritably. “I ask you the same question again: if you consider me guilty, why don’t you take me to prison?”\n\n“Oh, that’s your question! I will answer you, point for point. In the first place, to arrest you so directly is not to my interest.”\n\n“How so? If you are convinced you ought....”\n\n“Ach, what if I am convinced? That’s only my dream for the time. Why should I put you in safety? You know that’s it, since you ask me to do it. If I confront you with that workman for instance and you say to him ‘were you drunk or not? Who saw me with you? I simply took you to be drunk, and you were drunk, too.’ Well, what could I answer, especially as your story is a more likely one than his? for there’s nothing but psychology to support his evidence--that’s almost unseemly with his ugly mug, while you hit the mark exactly, for the rascal is an inveterate drunkard and notoriously so. And I have myself admitted candidly several times already that that psychology can be taken in two ways and that the second way is stronger and looks far more probable, and that apart from that I have as yet nothing against you. And though I shall put you in prison and indeed have come--quite contrary to etiquette--to inform you of it beforehand, yet I tell you frankly, also contrary to etiquette, that it won’t be to my advantage. Well, secondly, I’ve come to you because...”\n\n“Yes, yes, secondly?” Raskolnikov was listening breathless.\n\n“Because, as I told you just now, I consider I owe you an explanation. I don’t want you to look upon me as a monster, as I have a genuine liking for you, you may believe me or not. And in the third place I’ve come to you with a direct and open proposition--that you should surrender and confess. It will be infinitely more to your advantage and to my advantage too, for my task will be done. Well, is this open on my part or not?”\n\nRaskolnikov thought a minute.\n\n“Listen, Porfiry Petrovitch. You said just now you have nothing but psychology to go on, yet now you’ve gone on mathematics. Well, what if you are mistaken yourself, now?”\n\n“No, Rodion Romanovitch, I am not mistaken. I have a little fact even then, Providence sent it me.”\n\n“What little fact?”\n\n“I won’t tell you what, Rodion Romanovitch. And in any case, I haven’t the right to put it off any longer, I must arrest you. So think it over: it makes no difference to me _now_ and so I speak only for your sake. Believe me, it will be better, Rodion Romanovitch.”\n\nRaskolnikov smiled malignantly.\n\n“That’s not simply ridiculous, it’s positively shameless. Why, even if I were guilty, which I don’t admit, what reason should I have to confess, when you tell me yourself that I shall be in greater safety in prison?”\n\n“Ah, Rodion Romanovitch, don’t put too much faith in words, perhaps prison will not be altogether a restful place. That’s only theory and my theory, and what authority am I for you? Perhaps, too, even now I am hiding something from you? I can’t lay bare everything, he-he! And how can you ask what advantage? Don’t you know how it would lessen your sentence? You would be confessing at a moment when another man has taken the crime on himself and so has muddled the whole case. Consider that! I swear before God that I will so arrange that your confession shall come as a complete surprise. We will make a clean sweep of all these psychological points, of a suspicion against you, so that your crime will appear to have been something like an aberration, for in truth it was an aberration. I am an honest man, Rodion Romanovitch, and will keep my word.”\n\nRaskolnikov maintained a mournful silence and let his head sink dejectedly. He pondered a long while and at last smiled again, but his smile was sad and gentle.\n\n“No!” he said, apparently abandoning all attempt to keep up appearances with Porfiry, “it’s not worth it, I don’t care about lessening the sentence!”\n\n“That’s just what I was afraid of!” Porfiry cried warmly and, as it seemed, involuntarily. “That’s just what I feared, that you wouldn’t care about the mitigation of sentence.”\n\nRaskolnikov looked sadly and expressively at him.\n\n“Ah, don’t disdain life!” Porfiry went on. “You have a great deal of it still before you. How can you say you don’t want a mitigation of sentence? You are an impatient fellow!”\n\n“A great deal of what lies before me?”\n\n“Of life. What sort of prophet are you, do you know much about it? Seek and ye shall find. This may be God’s means for bringing you to Him. And it’s not for ever, the bondage....”\n\n“The time will be shortened,” laughed Raskolnikov.\n\n“Why, is it the bourgeois disgrace you are afraid of? It may be that you are afraid of it without knowing it, because you are young! But anyway _you_ shouldn’t be afraid of giving yourself up and confessing.”\n\n“Ach, hang it!” Raskolnikov whispered with loathing and contempt, as though he did not want to speak aloud.\n\nHe got up again as though he meant to go away, but sat down again in evident despair.\n\n“Hang it, if you like! You’ve lost faith and you think that I am grossly flattering you; but how long has your life been? How much do you understand? You made up a theory and then were ashamed that it broke down and turned out to be not at all original! It turned out something base, that’s true, but you are not hopelessly base. By no means so base! At least you didn’t deceive yourself for long, you went straight to the furthest point at one bound. How do I regard you? I regard you as one of those men who would stand and smile at their torturer while he cuts their entrails out, if only they have found faith or God. Find it and you will live. You have long needed a change of air. Suffering, too, is a good thing. Suffer! Maybe Nikolay is right in wanting to suffer. I know you don’t believe in it--but don’t be over-wise; fling yourself straight into life, without deliberation; don’t be afraid--the flood will bear you to the bank and set you safe on your feet again. What bank? How can I tell? I only believe that you have long life before you. I know that you take all my words now for a set speech prepared beforehand, but maybe you will remember them after. They may be of use some time. That’s why I speak. It’s as well that you only killed the old woman. If you’d invented another theory you might perhaps have done something a thousand times more hideous. You ought to thank God, perhaps. How do you know? Perhaps God is saving you for something. But keep a good heart and have less fear! Are you afraid of the great expiation before you? No, it would be shameful to be afraid of it. Since you have taken such a step, you must harden your heart. There is justice in it. You must fulfil the demands of justice. I know that you don’t believe it, but indeed, life will bring you through. You will live it down in time. What you need now is fresh air, fresh air, fresh air!”\n\nRaskolnikov positively started.\n\n“But who are you? what prophet are you? From the height of what majestic calm do you proclaim these words of wisdom?”\n\n“Who am I? I am a man with nothing to hope for, that’s all. A man perhaps of feeling and sympathy, maybe of some knowledge too, but my day is over. But you are a different matter, there is life waiting for you. Though, who knows? maybe your life, too, will pass off in smoke and come to nothing. Come, what does it matter, that you will pass into another class of men? It’s not comfort you regret, with your heart! What of it that perhaps no one will see you for so long? It’s not time, but yourself that will decide that. Be the sun and all will see you. The sun has before all to be the sun. Why are you smiling again? At my being such a Schiller? I bet you’re imagining that I am trying to get round you by flattery. Well, perhaps I am, he-he-he! Perhaps you’d better not believe my word, perhaps you’d better never believe it altogether--I’m made that way, I confess it. But let me add, you can judge for yourself, I think, how far I am a base sort of man and how far I am honest.”\n\n“When do you mean to arrest me?”\n\n“Well, I can let you walk about another day or two. Think it over, my dear fellow, and pray to God. It’s more in your interest, believe me.”\n\n“And what if I run away?” asked Raskolnikov with a strange smile.\n\n“No, you won’t run away. A peasant would run away, a fashionable dissenter would run away, the flunkey of another man’s thought, for you’ve only to show him the end of your little finger and he’ll be ready to believe in anything for the rest of his life. But you’ve ceased to believe in your theory already, what will you run away with? And what would you do in hiding? It would be hateful and difficult for you, and what you need more than anything in life is a definite position, an atmosphere to suit you. And what sort of atmosphere would you have? If you ran away, you’d come back of yourself. _You can’t get on without us._ And if I put you in prison--say you’ve been there a month, or two, or three--remember my word, you’ll confess of yourself and perhaps to your own surprise. You won’t know an hour beforehand that you are coming with a confession. I am convinced that you will decide, ‘to take your suffering.’ You don’t believe my words now, but you’ll come to it of yourself. For suffering, Rodion Romanovitch, is a great thing. Never mind my having grown fat, I know all the same. Don’t laugh at it, there’s an idea in suffering, Nikolay is right. No, you won’t run away, Rodion Romanovitch.”\n\nRaskolnikov got up and took his cap. Porfiry Petrovitch also rose.\n\n“Are you going for a walk? The evening will be fine, if only we don’t have a storm. Though it would be a good thing to freshen the air.”\n\nHe, too, took his cap.\n\n“Porfiry Petrovitch, please don’t take up the notion that I have confessed to you to-day,” Raskolnikov pronounced with sullen insistence. “You’re a strange man and I have listened to you from simple curiosity. But I have admitted nothing, remember that!”\n\n“Oh, I know that, I’ll remember. Look at him, he’s trembling! Don’t be uneasy, my dear fellow, have it your own way. Walk about a bit, you won’t be able to walk too far. If anything happens, I have one request to make of you,” he added, dropping his voice. “It’s an awkward one, but important. If anything were to happen (though indeed I don’t believe in it and think you quite incapable of it), yet in case you were taken during these forty or fifty hours with the notion of putting an end to the business in some other way, in some fantastic fashion--laying hands on yourself--(it’s an absurd proposition, but you must forgive me for it) do leave a brief but precise note, only two lines, and mention the stone. It will be more generous. Come, till we meet! Good thoughts and sound decisions to you!”\n\nPorfiry went out, stooping and avoiding looking at Raskolnikov. The latter went to the window and waited with irritable impatience till he calculated that Porfiry had reached the street and moved away. Then he too went hurriedly out of the room.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nHe hurried to Svidrigaïlov’s. What he had to hope from that man he did not know. But that man had some hidden power over him. Having once recognised this, he could not rest, and now the time had come.\n\nOn the way, one question particularly worried him: had Svidrigaïlov been to Porfiry’s?\n\nAs far as he could judge, he would swear to it, that he had not. He pondered again and again, went over Porfiry’s visit; no, he hadn’t been, of course he hadn’t.\n\nBut if he had not been yet, would he go? Meanwhile, for the present he fancied he couldn’t. Why? He could not have explained, but if he could, he would not have wasted much thought over it at the moment. It all worried him and at the same time he could not attend to it. Strange to say, none would have believed it perhaps, but he only felt a faint vague anxiety about his immediate future. Another, much more important anxiety tormented him--it concerned himself, but in a different, more vital way. Moreover, he was conscious of immense moral fatigue, though his mind was working better that morning than it had done of late.\n\nAnd was it worth while, after all that had happened, to contend with these new trivial difficulties? Was it worth while, for instance, to manoeuvre that Svidrigaïlov should not go to Porfiry’s? Was it worth while to investigate, to ascertain the facts, to waste time over anyone like Svidrigaïlov?\n\nOh, how sick he was of it all!\n\nAnd yet he was hastening to Svidrigaïlov; could he be expecting something _new_ from him, information, or means of escape? Men will catch at straws! Was it destiny or some instinct bringing them together? Perhaps it was only fatigue, despair; perhaps it was not Svidrigaïlov but some other whom he needed, and Svidrigaïlov had simply presented himself by chance. Sonia? But what should he go to Sonia for now? To beg her tears again? He was afraid of Sonia, too. Sonia stood before him as an irrevocable sentence. He must go his own way or hers. At that moment especially he did not feel equal to seeing her. No, would it not be better to try Svidrigaïlov? And he could not help inwardly owning that he had long felt that he must see him for some reason.\n\nBut what could they have in common? Their very evil-doing could not be of the same kind. The man, moreover, was very unpleasant, evidently depraved, undoubtedly cunning and deceitful, possibly malignant. Such stories were told about him. It is true he was befriending Katerina Ivanovna’s children, but who could tell with what motive and what it meant? The man always had some design, some project.\n\nThere was another thought which had been continually hovering of late about Raskolnikov’s mind, and causing him great uneasiness. It was so painful that he made distinct efforts to get rid of it. He sometimes thought that Svidrigaïlov was dogging his footsteps. Svidrigaïlov had found out his secret and had had designs on Dounia. What if he had them still? Wasn’t it practically certain that he had? And what if, having learnt his secret and so having gained power over him, he were to use it as a weapon against Dounia?\n\nThis idea sometimes even tormented his dreams, but it had never presented itself so vividly to him as on his way to Svidrigaïlov. The very thought moved him to gloomy rage. To begin with, this would transform everything, even his own position; he would have at once to confess his secret to Dounia. Would he have to give himself up perhaps to prevent Dounia from taking some rash step? The letter? This morning Dounia had received a letter. From whom could she get letters in Petersburg? Luzhin, perhaps? It’s true Razumihin was there to protect her, but Razumihin knew nothing of the position. Perhaps it was his duty to tell Razumihin? He thought of it with repugnance.\n\nIn any case he must see Svidrigaïlov as soon as possible, he decided finally. Thank God, the details of the interview were of little consequence, if only he could get at the root of the matter; but if Svidrigaïlov were capable... if he were intriguing against Dounia--then...\n\nRaskolnikov was so exhausted by what he had passed through that month that he could only decide such questions in one way; “then I shall kill him,” he thought in cold despair.\n\nA sudden anguish oppressed his heart, he stood still in the middle of the street and began looking about to see where he was and which way he was going. He found himself in X. Prospect, thirty or forty paces from the Hay Market, through which he had come. The whole second storey of the house on the left was used as a tavern. All the windows were wide open; judging from the figures moving at the windows, the rooms were full to overflowing. There were sounds of singing, of clarionet and violin, and the boom of a Turkish drum. He could hear women shrieking. He was about to turn back wondering why he had come to the X. Prospect, when suddenly at one of the end windows he saw Svidrigaïlov, sitting at a tea-table right in the open window with a pipe in his mouth. Raskolnikov was dreadfully taken aback, almost terrified. Svidrigaïlov was silently watching and scrutinising him and, what struck Raskolnikov at once, seemed to be meaning to get up and slip away unobserved. Raskolnikov at once pretended not to have seen him, but to be looking absent-mindedly away, while he watched him out of the corner of his eye. His heart was beating violently. Yet, it was evident that Svidrigaïlov did not want to be seen. He took the pipe out of his mouth and was on the point of concealing himself, but as he got up and moved back his chair, he seemed to have become suddenly aware that Raskolnikov had seen him, and was watching him. What had passed between them was much the same as what happened at their first meeting in Raskolnikov’s room. A sly smile came into Svidrigaïlov’s face and grew broader and broader. Each knew that he was seen and watched by the other. At last Svidrigaïlov broke into a loud laugh.\n\n“Well, well, come in if you want me; I am here!” he shouted from the window.\n\nRaskolnikov went up into the tavern. He found Svidrigaïlov in a tiny back room, adjoining the saloon in which merchants, clerks and numbers of people of all sorts were drinking tea at twenty little tables to the desperate bawling of a chorus of singers. The click of billiard balls could be heard in the distance. On the table before Svidrigaïlov stood an open bottle and a glass half full of champagne. In the room he found also a boy with a little hand organ, a healthy-looking red-cheeked girl of eighteen, wearing a tucked-up striped skirt, and a Tyrolese hat with ribbons. In spite of the chorus in the other room, she was singing some servants’ hall song in a rather husky contralto, to the accompaniment of the organ.\n\n“Come, that’s enough,” Svidrigaïlov stopped her at Raskolnikov’s entrance. The girl at once broke off and stood waiting respectfully. She had sung her guttural rhymes, too, with a serious and respectful expression in her face.\n\n“Hey, Philip, a glass!” shouted Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“I won’t drink anything,” said Raskolnikov.\n\n“As you like, I didn’t mean it for you. Drink, Katia! I don’t want anything more to-day, you can go.” He poured her out a full glass, and laid down a yellow note.\n\nKatia drank off her glass of wine, as women do, without putting it down, in twenty gulps, took the note and kissed Svidrigaïlov’s hand, which he allowed quite seriously. She went out of the room and the boy trailed after her with the organ. Both had been brought in from the street. Svidrigaïlov had not been a week in Petersburg, but everything about him was already, so to speak, on a patriarchal footing; the waiter, Philip, was by now an old friend and very obsequious.\n\nThe door leading to the saloon had a lock on it. Svidrigaïlov was at home in this room and perhaps spent whole days in it. The tavern was dirty and wretched, not even second-rate.\n\n“I was going to see you and looking for you,” Raskolnikov began, “but I don’t know what made me turn from the Hay Market into the X. Prospect just now. I never take this turning. I turn to the right from the Hay Market. And this isn’t the way to you. I simply turned and here you are. It is strange!”\n\n“Why don’t you say at once ‘it’s a miracle’?”\n\n“Because it may be only chance.”\n\n“Oh, that’s the way with all you folk,” laughed Svidrigaïlov. “You won’t admit it, even if you do inwardly believe it a miracle! Here you say that it may be only chance. And what cowards they all are here, about having an opinion of their own, you can’t fancy, Rodion Romanovitch. I don’t mean you, you have an opinion of your own and are not afraid to have it. That’s how it was you attracted my curiosity.”\n\n“Nothing else?”\n\n“Well, that’s enough, you know,” Svidrigaïlov was obviously exhilarated, but only slightly so, he had not had more than half a glass of wine.\n\n“I fancy you came to see me before you knew that I was capable of having what you call an opinion of my own,” observed Raskolnikov.\n\n“Oh, well, it was a different matter. Everyone has his own plans. And apropos of the miracle let me tell you that I think you have been asleep for the last two or three days. I told you of this tavern myself, there is no miracle in your coming straight here. I explained the way myself, told you where it was, and the hours you could find me here. Do you remember?”\n\n“I don’t remember,” answered Raskolnikov with surprise.\n\n“I believe you. I told you twice. The address has been stamped mechanically on your memory. You turned this way mechanically and yet precisely according to the direction, though you are not aware of it. When I told you then, I hardly hoped you understood me. You give yourself away too much, Rodion Romanovitch. And another thing, I’m convinced there are lots of people in Petersburg who talk to themselves as they walk. This is a town of crazy people. If only we had scientific men, doctors, lawyers and philosophers might make most valuable investigations in Petersburg each in his own line. There are few places where there are so many gloomy, strong and queer influences on the soul of man as in Petersburg. The mere influences of climate mean so much. And it’s the administrative centre of all Russia and its character must be reflected on the whole country. But that is neither here nor there now. The point is that I have several times watched you. You walk out of your house--holding your head high--twenty paces from home you let it sink, and fold your hands behind your back. You look and evidently see nothing before nor beside you. At last you begin moving your lips and talking to yourself, and sometimes you wave one hand and declaim, and at last stand still in the middle of the road. That’s not at all the thing. Someone may be watching you besides me, and it won’t do you any good. It’s nothing really to do with me and I can’t cure you, but, of course, you understand me.”\n\n“Do you know that I am being followed?” asked Raskolnikov, looking inquisitively at him.\n\n“No, I know nothing about it,” said Svidrigaïlov, seeming surprised.\n\n“Well, then, let us leave me alone,” Raskolnikov muttered, frowning.\n\n“Very good, let us leave you alone.”\n\n“You had better tell me, if you come here to drink, and directed me twice to come here to you, why did you hide, and try to get away just now when I looked at the window from the street? I saw it.”\n\n“He-he! And why was it you lay on your sofa with closed eyes and pretended to be asleep, though you were wide awake while I stood in your doorway? I saw it.”\n\n“I may have had... reasons. You know that yourself.”\n\n“And I may have had my reasons, though you don’t know them.”\n\nRaskolnikov dropped his right elbow on the table, leaned his chin in the fingers of his right hand, and stared intently at Svidrigaïlov. For a full minute he scrutinised his face, which had impressed him before. It was a strange face, like a mask; white and red, with bright red lips, with a flaxen beard, and still thick flaxen hair. His eyes were somehow too blue and their expression somehow too heavy and fixed. There was something awfully unpleasant in that handsome face, which looked so wonderfully young for his age. Svidrigaïlov was smartly dressed in light summer clothes and was particularly dainty in his linen. He wore a huge ring with a precious stone in it.\n\n“Have I got to bother myself about you, too, now?” said Raskolnikov suddenly, coming with nervous impatience straight to the point. “Even though perhaps you are the most dangerous man if you care to injure me, I don’t want to put myself out any more. I will show you at once that I don’t prize myself as you probably think I do. I’ve come to tell you at once that if you keep to your former intentions with regard to my sister and if you think to derive any benefit in that direction from what has been discovered of late, I will kill you before you get me locked up. You can reckon on my word. You know that I can keep it. And in the second place if you want to tell me anything--for I keep fancying all this time that you have something to tell me--make haste and tell it, for time is precious and very likely it will soon be too late.”\n\n“Why in such haste?” asked Svidrigaïlov, looking at him curiously.\n\n“Everyone has his plans,” Raskolnikov answered gloomily and impatiently.\n\n“You urged me yourself to frankness just now, and at the first question you refuse to answer,” Svidrigaïlov observed with a smile. “You keep fancying that I have aims of my own and so you look at me with suspicion. Of course it’s perfectly natural in your position. But though I should like to be friends with you, I shan’t trouble myself to convince you of the contrary. The game isn’t worth the candle and I wasn’t intending to talk to you about anything special.”\n\n“What did you want me for, then? It was you who came hanging about me.”\n\n“Why, simply as an interesting subject for observation. I liked the fantastic nature of your position--that’s what it was! Besides, you are the brother of a person who greatly interested me, and from that person I had in the past heard a very great deal about you, from which I gathered that you had a great influence over her; isn’t that enough? Ha-ha-ha! Still I must admit that your question is rather complex, and is difficult for me to answer. Here, you, for instance, have come to me not only for a definite object, but for the sake of hearing something new. Isn’t that so? Isn’t that so?” persisted Svidrigaïlov with a sly smile. “Well, can’t you fancy then that I, too, on my way here in the train was reckoning on you, on your telling me something new, and on my making some profit out of you! You see what rich men we are!”\n\n“What profit could you make?”\n\n“How can I tell you? How do I know? You see in what a tavern I spend all my time and it’s my enjoyment, that’s to say it’s no great enjoyment, but one must sit somewhere; that poor Katia now--you saw her?... If only I had been a glutton now, a club gourmand, but you see I can eat this.”\n\nHe pointed to a little table in the corner where the remnants of a terrible-looking beef-steak and potatoes lay on a tin dish.\n\n“Have you dined, by the way? I’ve had something and want nothing more. I don’t drink, for instance, at all. Except for champagne I never touch anything, and not more than a glass of that all the evening, and even that is enough to make my head ache. I ordered it just now to wind myself up, for I am just going off somewhere and you see me in a peculiar state of mind. That was why I hid myself just now like a schoolboy, for I was afraid you would hinder me. But I believe,” he pulled out his watch, “I can spend an hour with you. It’s half-past four now. If only I’d been something, a landowner, a father, a cavalry officer, a photographer, a journalist... I am nothing, no specialty, and sometimes I am positively bored. I really thought you would tell me something new.”\n\n“But what are you, and why have you come here?”\n\n“What am I? You know, a gentleman, I served for two years in the cavalry, then I knocked about here in Petersburg, then I married Marfa Petrovna and lived in the country. There you have my biography!”\n\n“You are a gambler, I believe?”\n\n“No, a poor sort of gambler. A card-sharper--not a gambler.”\n\n“You have been a card-sharper then?”\n\n“Yes, I’ve been a card-sharper too.”\n\n“Didn’t you get thrashed sometimes?”\n\n“It did happen. Why?”\n\n“Why, you might have challenged them... altogether it must have been lively.”\n\n“I won’t contradict you, and besides I am no hand at philosophy. I confess that I hastened here for the sake of the women.”\n\n“As soon as you buried Marfa Petrovna?”\n\n“Quite so,” Svidrigaïlov smiled with engaging candour. “What of it? You seem to find something wrong in my speaking like that about women?”\n\n“You ask whether I find anything wrong in vice?”\n\n“Vice! Oh, that’s what you are after! But I’ll answer you in order, first about women in general; you know I am fond of talking. Tell me, what should I restrain myself for? Why should I give up women, since I have a passion for them? It’s an occupation, anyway.”\n\n“So you hope for nothing here but vice?”\n\n“Oh, very well, for vice then. You insist on its being vice. But anyway I like a direct question. In this vice at least there is something permanent, founded indeed upon nature and not dependent on fantasy, something present in the blood like an ever-burning ember, for ever setting one on fire and, maybe, not to be quickly extinguished, even with years. You’ll agree it’s an occupation of a sort.”\n\n“That’s nothing to rejoice at, it’s a disease and a dangerous one.”\n\n“Oh, that’s what you think, is it! I agree, that it is a disease like everything that exceeds moderation. And, of course, in this one must exceed moderation. But in the first place, everybody does so in one way or another, and in the second place, of course, one ought to be moderate and prudent, however mean it may be, but what am I to do? If I hadn’t this, I might have to shoot myself. I am ready to admit that a decent man ought to put up with being bored, but yet...”\n\n“And could you shoot yourself?”\n\n“Oh, come!” Svidrigaïlov parried with disgust. “Please don’t speak of it,” he added hurriedly and with none of the bragging tone he had shown in all the previous conversation. His face quite changed. “I admit it’s an unpardonable weakness, but I can’t help it. I am afraid of death and I dislike its being talked of. Do you know that I am to a certain extent a mystic?”\n\n“Ah, the apparitions of Marfa Petrovna! Do they still go on visiting you?”\n\n“Oh, don’t talk of them; there have been no more in Petersburg, confound them!” he cried with an air of irritation. “Let’s rather talk of that... though... H’m! I have not much time, and can’t stay long with you, it’s a pity! I should have found plenty to tell you.”\n\n“What’s your engagement, a woman?”\n\n“Yes, a woman, a casual incident.... No, that’s not what I want to talk of.”\n\n“And the hideousness, the filthiness of all your surroundings, doesn’t that affect you? Have you lost the strength to stop yourself?”\n\n“And do you pretend to strength, too? He-he-he! You surprised me just now, Rodion Romanovitch, though I knew beforehand it would be so. You preach to me about vice and æsthetics! You--a Schiller, you--an idealist! Of course that’s all as it should be and it would be surprising if it were not so, yet it is strange in reality.... Ah, what a pity I have no time, for you’re a most interesting type! And, by-the-way, are you fond of Schiller? I am awfully fond of him.”\n\n“But what a braggart you are,” Raskolnikov said with some disgust.\n\n“Upon my word, I am not,” answered Svidrigaïlov laughing. “However, I won’t dispute it, let me be a braggart, why not brag, if it hurts no one? I spent seven years in the country with Marfa Petrovna, so now when I come across an intelligent person like you--intelligent and highly interesting--I am simply glad to talk and, besides, I’ve drunk that half-glass of champagne and it’s gone to my head a little. And besides, there’s a certain fact that has wound me up tremendously, but about that I... will keep quiet. Where are you off to?” he asked in alarm.\n\nRaskolnikov had begun getting up. He felt oppressed and stifled and, as it were, ill at ease at having come here. He felt convinced that Svidrigaïlov was the most worthless scoundrel on the face of the earth.\n\n“A-ach! Sit down, stay a little!” Svidrigaïlov begged. “Let them bring you some tea, anyway. Stay a little, I won’t talk nonsense, about myself, I mean. I’ll tell you something. If you like I’ll tell you how a woman tried ‘to save’ me, as you would call it? It will be an answer to your first question indeed, for the woman was your sister. May I tell you? It will help to spend the time.”\n\n“Tell me, but I trust that you...”\n\n“Oh, don’t be uneasy. Besides, even in a worthless low fellow like me, Avdotya Romanovna can only excite the deepest respect.”\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n“You know perhaps--yes, I told you myself,” began Svidrigaïlov, “that I was in the debtors’ prison here, for an immense sum, and had not any expectation of being able to pay it. There’s no need to go into particulars how Marfa Petrovna bought me out; do you know to what a point of insanity a woman can sometimes love? She was an honest woman, and very sensible, although completely uneducated. Would you believe that this honest and jealous woman, after many scenes of hysterics and reproaches, condescended to enter into a kind of contract with me which she kept throughout our married life? She was considerably older than I, and besides, she always kept a clove or something in her mouth. There was so much swinishness in my soul and honesty too, of a sort, as to tell her straight out that I couldn’t be absolutely faithful to her. This confession drove her to frenzy, but yet she seems in a way to have liked my brutal frankness. She thought it showed I was unwilling to deceive her if I warned her like this beforehand and for a jealous woman, you know, that’s the first consideration. After many tears an unwritten contract was drawn up between us: first, that I would never leave Marfa Petrovna and would always be her husband; secondly, that I would never absent myself without her permission; thirdly, that I would never set up a permanent mistress; fourthly, in return for this, Marfa Petrovna gave me a free hand with the maidservants, but only with her secret knowledge; fifthly, God forbid my falling in love with a woman of our class; sixthly, in case I--which God forbid--should be visited by a great serious passion I was bound to reveal it to Marfa Petrovna. On this last score, however, Marfa Petrovna was fairly at ease. She was a sensible woman and so she could not help looking upon me as a dissolute profligate incapable of real love. But a sensible woman and a jealous woman are two very different things, and that’s where the trouble came in. But to judge some people impartially we must renounce certain preconceived opinions and our habitual attitude to the ordinary people about us. I have reason to have faith in your judgment rather than in anyone’s. Perhaps you have already heard a great deal that was ridiculous and absurd about Marfa Petrovna. She certainly had some very ridiculous ways, but I tell you frankly that I feel really sorry for the innumerable woes of which I was the cause. Well, and that’s enough, I think, by way of a decorous _oraison funèbre_ for the most tender wife of a most tender husband. When we quarrelled, I usually held my tongue and did not irritate her and that gentlemanly conduct rarely failed to attain its object, it influenced her, it pleased her, indeed. These were times when she was positively proud of me. But your sister she couldn’t put up with, anyway. And however she came to risk taking such a beautiful creature into her house as a governess. My explanation is that Marfa Petrovna was an ardent and impressionable woman and simply fell in love herself--literally fell in love--with your sister. Well, little wonder--look at Avdotya Romanovna! I saw the danger at the first glance and what do you think, I resolved not to look at her even. But Avdotya Romanovna herself made the first step, would you believe it? Would you believe it too that Marfa Petrovna was positively angry with me at first for my persistent silence about your sister, for my careless reception of her continual adoring praises of Avdotya Romanovna. I don’t know what it was she wanted! Well, of course, Marfa Petrovna told Avdotya Romanovna every detail about me. She had the unfortunate habit of telling literally everyone all our family secrets and continually complaining of me; how could she fail to confide in such a delightful new friend? I expect they talked of nothing else but me and no doubt Avdotya Romanovna heard all those dark mysterious rumours that were current about me.... I don’t mind betting that you too have heard something of the sort already?”\n\n“I have. Luzhin charged you with having caused the death of a child. Is that true?”\n\n“Don’t refer to those vulgar tales, I beg,” said Svidrigaïlov with disgust and annoyance. “If you insist on wanting to know about all that idiocy, I will tell you one day, but now...”\n\n“I was told too about some footman of yours in the country whom you treated badly.”\n\n“I beg you to drop the subject,” Svidrigaïlov interrupted again with obvious impatience.\n\n“Was that the footman who came to you after death to fill your pipe?... you told me about it yourself.” Raskolnikov felt more and more irritated.\n\nSvidrigaïlov looked at him attentively and Raskolnikov fancied he caught a flash of spiteful mockery in that look. But Svidrigaïlov restrained himself and answered very civilly:\n\n“Yes, it was. I see that you, too, are extremely interested and shall feel it my duty to satisfy your curiosity at the first opportunity. Upon my soul! I see that I really might pass for a romantic figure with some people. Judge how grateful I must be to Marfa Petrovna for having repeated to Avdotya Romanovna such mysterious and interesting gossip about me. I dare not guess what impression it made on her, but in any case it worked in my interests. With all Avdotya Romanovna’s natural aversion and in spite of my invariably gloomy and repellent aspect--she did at least feel pity for me, pity for a lost soul. And if once a girl’s heart is moved to _pity_, it’s more dangerous than anything. She is bound to want to ‘save him,’ to bring him to his senses, and lift him up and draw him to nobler aims, and restore him to new life and usefulness--well, we all know how far such dreams can go. I saw at once that the bird was flying into the cage of herself. And I too made ready. I think you are frowning, Rodion Romanovitch? There’s no need. As you know, it all ended in smoke. (Hang it all, what a lot I am drinking!) Do you know, I always, from the very beginning, regretted that it wasn’t your sister’s fate to be born in the second or third century A.D., as the daughter of a reigning prince or some governor or pro-consul in Asia Minor. She would undoubtedly have been one of those who would endure martyrdom and would have smiled when they branded her bosom with hot pincers. And she would have gone to it of herself. And in the fourth or fifth century she would have walked away into the Egyptian desert and would have stayed there thirty years living on roots and ecstasies and visions. She is simply thirsting to face some torture for someone, and if she can’t get her torture, she’ll throw herself out of a window. I’ve heard something of a Mr. Razumihin--he’s said to be a sensible fellow; his surname suggests it, indeed. He’s probably a divinity student. Well, he’d better look after your sister! I believe I understand her, and I am proud of it. But at the beginning of an acquaintance, as you know, one is apt to be more heedless and stupid. One doesn’t see clearly. Hang it all, why is she so handsome? It’s not my fault. In fact, it began on my side with a most irresistible physical desire. Avdotya Romanovna is awfully chaste, incredibly and phenomenally so. Take note, I tell you this about your sister as a fact. She is almost morbidly chaste, in spite of her broad intelligence, and it will stand in her way. There happened to be a girl in the house then, Parasha, a black-eyed wench, whom I had never seen before--she had just come from another village--very pretty, but incredibly stupid: she burst into tears, wailed so that she could be heard all over the place and caused scandal. One day after dinner Avdotya Romanovna followed me into an avenue in the garden and with flashing eyes _insisted_ on my leaving poor Parasha alone. It was almost our first conversation by ourselves. I, of course, was only too pleased to obey her wishes, tried to appear disconcerted, embarrassed, in fact played my part not badly. Then came interviews, mysterious conversations, exhortations, entreaties, supplications, even tears--would you believe it, even tears? Think what the passion for propaganda will bring some girls to! I, of course, threw it all on my destiny, posed as hungering and thirsting for light, and finally resorted to the most powerful weapon in the subjection of the female heart, a weapon which never fails one. It’s the well-known resource--flattery. Nothing in the world is harder than speaking the truth and nothing easier than flattery. If there’s the hundredth part of a false note in speaking the truth, it leads to a discord, and that leads to trouble. But if all, to the last note, is false in flattery, it is just as agreeable, and is heard not without satisfaction. It may be a coarse satisfaction, but still a satisfaction. And however coarse the flattery, at least half will be sure to seem true. That’s so for all stages of development and classes of society. A vestal virgin might be seduced by flattery. I can never remember without laughter how I once seduced a lady who was devoted to her husband, her children, and her principles. What fun it was and how little trouble! And the lady really had principles--of her own, anyway. All my tactics lay in simply being utterly annihilated and prostrate before her purity. I flattered her shamelessly, and as soon as I succeeded in getting a pressure of the hand, even a glance from her, I would reproach myself for having snatched it by force, and would declare that she had resisted, so that I could never have gained anything but for my being so unprincipled. I maintained that she was so innocent that she could not foresee my treachery, and yielded to me unconsciously, unawares, and so on. In fact, I triumphed, while my lady remained firmly convinced that she was innocent, chaste, and faithful to all her duties and obligations and had succumbed quite by accident. And how angry she was with me when I explained to her at last that it was my sincere conviction that she was just as eager as I. Poor Marfa Petrovna was awfully weak on the side of flattery, and if I had only cared to, I might have had all her property settled on me during her lifetime. (I am drinking an awful lot of wine now and talking too much.) I hope you won’t be angry if I mention now that I was beginning to produce the same effect on Avdotya Romanovna. But I was stupid and impatient and spoiled it all. Avdotya Romanovna had several times--and one time in particular--been greatly displeased by the expression of my eyes, would you believe it? There was sometimes a light in them which frightened her and grew stronger and stronger and more unguarded till it was hateful to her. No need to go into detail, but we parted. There I acted stupidly again. I fell to jeering in the coarsest way at all such propaganda and efforts to convert me; Parasha came on to the scene again, and not she alone; in fact there was a tremendous to-do. Ah, Rodion Romanovitch, if you could only see how your sister’s eyes can flash sometimes! Never mind my being drunk at this moment and having had a whole glass of wine. I am speaking the truth. I assure you that this glance has haunted my dreams; the very rustle of her dress was more than I could stand at last. I really began to think that I might become epileptic. I could never have believed that I could be moved to such a frenzy. It was essential, indeed, to be reconciled, but by then it was impossible. And imagine what I did then! To what a pitch of stupidity a man can be brought by frenzy! Never undertake anything in a frenzy, Rodion Romanovitch. I reflected that Avdotya Romanovna was after all a beggar (ach, excuse me, that’s not the word... but does it matter if it expresses the meaning?), that she lived by her work, that she had her mother and you to keep (ach, hang it, you are frowning again), and I resolved to offer her all my money--thirty thousand roubles I could have realised then--if she would run away with me here, to Petersburg. Of course I should have vowed eternal love, rapture, and so on. Do you know, I was so wild about her at that time that if she had told me to poison Marfa Petrovna or to cut her throat and to marry herself, it would have been done at once! But it ended in the catastrophe of which you know already. You can fancy how frantic I was when I heard that Marfa Petrovna had got hold of that scoundrelly attorney, Luzhin, and had almost made a match between them--which would really have been just the same thing as I was proposing. Wouldn’t it? Wouldn’t it? I notice that you’ve begun to be very attentive... you interesting young man....”\n\nSvidrigaïlov struck the table with his fist impatiently. He was flushed. Raskolnikov saw clearly that the glass or glass and a half of champagne that he had sipped almost unconsciously was affecting him--and he resolved to take advantage of the opportunity. He felt very suspicious of Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“Well, after what you have said, I am fully convinced that you have come to Petersburg with designs on my sister,” he said directly to Svidrigaïlov, in order to irritate him further.\n\n“Oh, nonsense,” said Svidrigaïlov, seeming to rouse himself. “Why, I told you... besides your sister can’t endure me.”\n\n“Yes, I am certain that she can’t, but that’s not the point.”\n\n“Are you so sure that she can’t?” Svidrigaïlov screwed up his eyes and smiled mockingly. “You are right, she doesn’t love me, but you can never be sure of what has passed between husband and wife or lover and mistress. There’s always a little corner which remains a secret to the world and is only known to those two. Will you answer for it that Avdotya Romanovna regarded me with aversion?”\n\n“From some words you’ve dropped, I notice that you still have designs--and of course evil ones--on Dounia and mean to carry them out promptly.”\n\n“What, have I dropped words like that?” Svidrigaïlov asked in naïve dismay, taking not the slightest notice of the epithet bestowed on his designs.\n\n“Why, you are dropping them even now. Why are you so frightened? What are you so afraid of now?”\n\n“Me--afraid? Afraid of you? You have rather to be afraid of me, _cher ami_. But what nonsense.... I’ve drunk too much though, I see that. I was almost saying too much again. Damn the wine! Hi! there, water!”\n\nHe snatched up the champagne bottle and flung it without ceremony out of the window. Philip brought the water.\n\n“That’s all nonsense!” said Svidrigaïlov, wetting a towel and putting it to his head. “But I can answer you in one word and annihilate all your suspicions. Do you know that I am going to get married?”\n\n“You told me so before.”\n\n“Did I? I’ve forgotten. But I couldn’t have told you so for certain for I had not even seen my betrothed; I only meant to. But now I really have a betrothed and it’s a settled thing, and if it weren’t that I have business that can’t be put off, I would have taken you to see them at once, for I should like to ask your advice. Ach, hang it, only ten minutes left! See, look at the watch. But I must tell you, for it’s an interesting story, my marriage, in its own way. Where are you off to? Going again?”\n\n“No, I’m not going away now.”\n\n“Not at all? We shall see. I’ll take you there, I’ll show you my betrothed, only not now. For you’ll soon have to be off. You have to go to the right and I to the left. Do you know that Madame Resslich, the woman I am lodging with now, eh? I know what you’re thinking, that she’s the woman whose girl they say drowned herself in the winter. Come, are you listening? She arranged it all for me. You’re bored, she said, you want something to fill up your time. For, you know, I am a gloomy, depressed person. Do you think I’m light-hearted? No, I’m gloomy. I do no harm, but sit in a corner without speaking a word for three days at a time. And that Resslich is a sly hussy, I tell you. I know what she has got in her mind; she thinks I shall get sick of it, abandon my wife and depart, and she’ll get hold of her and make a profit out of her--in our class, of course, or higher. She told me the father was a broken-down retired official, who has been sitting in a chair for the last three years with his legs paralysed. The mamma, she said, was a sensible woman. There is a son serving in the provinces, but he doesn’t help; there is a daughter, who is married, but she doesn’t visit them. And they’ve two little nephews on their hands, as though their own children were not enough, and they’ve taken from school their youngest daughter, a girl who’ll be sixteen in another month, so that then she can be married. She was for me. We went there. How funny it was! I present myself--a landowner, a widower, of a well-known name, with connections, with a fortune. What if I am fifty and she is not sixteen? Who thinks of that? But it’s fascinating, isn’t it? It is fascinating, ha-ha! You should have seen how I talked to the papa and mamma. It was worth paying to have seen me at that moment. She comes in, curtseys, you can fancy, still in a short frock--an unopened bud! Flushing like a sunset--she had been told, no doubt. I don’t know how you feel about female faces, but to my mind these sixteen years, these childish eyes, shyness and tears of bashfulness are better than beauty; and she is a perfect little picture, too. Fair hair in little curls, like a lamb’s, full little rosy lips, tiny feet, a charmer!... Well, we made friends. I told them I was in a hurry owing to domestic circumstances, and the next day, that is the day before yesterday, we were betrothed. When I go now I take her on my knee at once and keep her there.... Well, she flushes like a sunset and I kiss her every minute. Her mamma of course impresses on her that this is her husband and that this must be so. It’s simply delicious! The present betrothed condition is perhaps better than marriage. Here you have what is called _la nature et la vérité_, ha-ha! I’ve talked to her twice, she is far from a fool. Sometimes she steals a look at me that positively scorches me. Her face is like Raphael’s Madonna. You know, the Sistine Madonna’s face has something fantastic in it, the face of mournful religious ecstasy. Haven’t you noticed it? Well, she’s something in that line. The day after we’d been betrothed, I bought her presents to the value of fifteen hundred roubles--a set of diamonds and another of pearls and a silver dressing-case as large as this, with all sorts of things in it, so that even my Madonna’s face glowed. I sat her on my knee, yesterday, and I suppose rather too unceremoniously--she flushed crimson and the tears started, but she didn’t want to show it. We were left alone, she suddenly flung herself on my neck (for the first time of her own accord), put her little arms round me, kissed me, and vowed that she would be an obedient, faithful, and good wife, would make me happy, would devote all her life, every minute of her life, would sacrifice everything, everything, and that all she asks in return is my _respect_, and that she wants ‘nothing, nothing more from me, no presents.’ You’ll admit that to hear such a confession, alone, from an angel of sixteen in a muslin frock, with little curls, with a flush of maiden shyness in her cheeks and tears of enthusiasm in her eyes is rather fascinating! Isn’t it fascinating? It’s worth paying for, isn’t it? Well... listen, we’ll go to see my betrothed, only not just now!”\n\n“The fact is this monstrous difference in age and development excites your sensuality! Will you really make such a marriage?”\n\n“Why, of course. Everyone thinks of himself, and he lives most gaily who knows best how to deceive himself. Ha-ha! But why are you so keen about virtue? Have mercy on me, my good friend. I am a sinful man. Ha-ha-ha!”\n\n“But you have provided for the children of Katerina Ivanovna. Though... though you had your own reasons.... I understand it all now.”\n\n“I am always fond of children, very fond of them,” laughed Svidrigaïlov. “I can tell you one curious instance of it. The first day I came here I visited various haunts, after seven years I simply rushed at them. You probably notice that I am not in a hurry to renew acquaintance with my old friends. I shall do without them as long as I can. Do you know, when I was with Marfa Petrovna in the country, I was haunted by the thought of these places where anyone who knows his way about can find a great deal. Yes, upon my soul! The peasants have vodka, the educated young people, shut out from activity, waste themselves in impossible dreams and visions and are crippled by theories; Jews have sprung up and are amassing money, and all the rest give themselves up to debauchery. From the first hour the town reeked of its familiar odours. I chanced to be in a frightful den--I like my dens dirty--it was a dance, so called, and there was a _cancan_ such as I never saw in my day. Yes, there you have progress. All of a sudden I saw a little girl of thirteen, nicely dressed, dancing with a specialist in that line, with another one _vis-à-vis_. Her mother was sitting on a chair by the wall. You can’t fancy what a _cancan_ that was! The girl was ashamed, blushed, at last felt insulted, and began to cry. Her partner seized her and began whirling her round and performing before her; everyone laughed and--I like your public, even the _cancan_ public--they laughed and shouted, ‘Serves her right--serves her right! Shouldn’t bring children!’ Well, it’s not my business whether that consoling reflection was logical or not. I at once fixed on my plan, sat down by the mother, and began by saying that I too was a stranger and that people here were ill-bred and that they couldn’t distinguish decent folks and treat them with respect, gave her to understand that I had plenty of money, offered to take them home in my carriage. I took them home and got to know them. They were lodging in a miserable little hole and had only just arrived from the country. She told me that she and her daughter could only regard my acquaintance as an honour. I found out that they had nothing of their own and had come to town upon some legal business. I proffered my services and money. I learnt that they had gone to the dancing saloon by mistake, believing that it was a genuine dancing class. I offered to assist in the young girl’s education in French and dancing. My offer was accepted with enthusiasm as an honour--and we are still friendly.... If you like, we’ll go and see them, only not just now.”\n\n“Stop! Enough of your vile, nasty anecdotes, depraved vile, sensual man!”\n\n“Schiller, you are a regular Schiller! _O la vertu va-t-elle se nicher?_ But you know I shall tell you these things on purpose, for the pleasure of hearing your outcries!”\n\n“I dare say. I can see I am ridiculous myself,” muttered Raskolnikov angrily.\n\nSvidrigaïlov laughed heartily; finally he called Philip, paid his bill, and began getting up.\n\n“I say, but I am drunk, _assez causé_,” he said. “It’s been a pleasure.”\n\n“I should rather think it must be a pleasure!” cried Raskolnikov, getting up. “No doubt it is a pleasure for a worn-out profligate to describe such adventures with a monstrous project of the same sort in his mind--especially under such circumstances and to such a man as me.... It’s stimulating!”\n\n“Well, if you come to that,” Svidrigaïlov answered, scrutinising Raskolnikov with some surprise, “if you come to that, you are a thorough cynic yourself. You’ve plenty to make you so, anyway. You can understand a great deal... and you can do a great deal too. But enough. I sincerely regret not having had more talk with you, but I shan’t lose sight of you.... Only wait a bit.”\n\nSvidrigaïlov walked out of the restaurant. Raskolnikov walked out after him. Svidrigaïlov was not however very drunk, the wine had affected him for a moment, but it was passing off every minute. He was preoccupied with something of importance and was frowning. He was apparently excited and uneasy in anticipation of something. His manner to Raskolnikov had changed during the last few minutes, and he was ruder and more sneering every moment. Raskolnikov noticed all this, and he too was uneasy. He became very suspicious of Svidrigaïlov and resolved to follow him.\n\nThey came out on to the pavement.\n\n“You go to the right, and I to the left, or if you like, the other way. Only _adieu, mon plaisir_, may we meet again.”\n\nAnd he walked to the right towards the Hay Market.\n\nRaskolnikov walked after him.\n\n“What’s this?” cried Svidrigaïlov turning round, “I thought I said...”\n\n“It means that I am not going to lose sight of you now.”\n\n“What?”\n\nBoth stood still and gazed at one another, as though measuring their strength.\n\n“From all your half tipsy stories,” Raskolnikov observed harshly, “I am _positive_ that you have not given up your designs on my sister, but are pursuing them more actively than ever. I have learnt that my sister received a letter this morning. You have hardly been able to sit still all this time.... You may have unearthed a wife on the way, but that means nothing. I should like to make certain myself.”\n\nRaskolnikov could hardly have said himself what he wanted and of what he wished to make certain.\n\n“Upon my word! I’ll call the police!”\n\n“Call away!”\n\nAgain they stood for a minute facing each other. At last Svidrigaïlov’s face changed. Having satisfied himself that Raskolnikov was not frightened at his threat, he assumed a mirthful and friendly air.\n\n“What a fellow! I purposely refrained from referring to your affair, though I am devoured by curiosity. It’s a fantastic affair. I’ve put it off till another time, but you’re enough to rouse the dead.... Well, let us go, only I warn you beforehand I am only going home for a moment, to get some money; then I shall lock up the flat, take a cab and go to spend the evening at the Islands. Now, now are you going to follow me?”\n\n“I’m coming to your lodgings, not to see you but Sofya Semyonovna, to say I’m sorry not to have been at the funeral.”\n\n“That’s as you like, but Sofya Semyonovna is not at home. She has taken the three children to an old lady of high rank, the patroness of some orphan asylums, whom I used to know years ago. I charmed the old lady by depositing a sum of money with her to provide for the three children of Katerina Ivanovna and subscribing to the institution as well. I told her too the story of Sofya Semyonovna in full detail, suppressing nothing. It produced an indescribable effect on her. That’s why Sofya Semyonovna has been invited to call to-day at the X. Hotel where the lady is staying for the time.”\n\n“No matter, I’ll come all the same.”\n\n“As you like, it’s nothing to me, but I won’t come with you; here we are at home. By the way, I am convinced that you regard me with suspicion just because I have shown such delicacy and have not so far troubled you with questions... you understand? It struck you as extraordinary; I don’t mind betting it’s that. Well, it teaches one to show delicacy!”\n“And to listen at doors!”\n\n“Ah, that’s it, is it?” laughed Svidrigaïlov. “Yes, I should have been surprised if you had let that pass after all that has happened. Ha-ha! Though I did understand something of the pranks you had been up to and were telling Sofya Semyonovna about, what was the meaning of it? Perhaps I am quite behind the times and can’t understand. For goodness’ sake, explain it, my dear boy. Expound the latest theories!”\n\n“You couldn’t have heard anything. You’re making it all up!”\n\n“But I’m not talking about that (though I did hear something). No, I’m talking of the way you keep sighing and groaning now. The Schiller in you is in revolt every moment, and now you tell me not to listen at doors. If that’s how you feel, go and inform the police that you had this mischance: you made a little mistake in your theory. But if you are convinced that one mustn’t listen at doors, but one may murder old women at one’s pleasure, you’d better be off to America and make haste. Run, young man! There may still be time. I’m speaking sincerely. Haven’t you the money? I’ll give you the fare.”\n\n“I’m not thinking of that at all,” Raskolnikov interrupted with disgust.\n\n“I understand (but don’t put yourself out, don’t discuss it if you don’t want to). I understand the questions you are worrying over--moral ones, aren’t they? Duties of citizen and man? Lay them all aside. They are nothing to you now, ha-ha! You’ll say you are still a man and a citizen. If so you ought not to have got into this coil. It’s no use taking up a job you are not fit for. Well, you’d better shoot yourself, or don’t you want to?”\n\n“You seem trying to enrage me, to make me leave you.”\n\n“What a queer fellow! But here we are. Welcome to the staircase. You see, that’s the way to Sofya Semyonovna. Look, there is no one at home. Don’t you believe me? Ask Kapernaumov. She leaves the key with him. Here is Madame de Kapernaumov herself. Hey, what? She is rather deaf. Has she gone out? Where? Did you hear? She is not in and won’t be till late in the evening probably. Well, come to my room; you wanted to come and see me, didn’t you? Here we are. Madame Resslich’s not at home. She is a woman who is always busy, an excellent woman I assure you.... She might have been of use to you if you had been a little more sensible. Now, see! I take this five-per-cent bond out of the bureau--see what a lot I’ve got of them still--this one will be turned into cash to-day. I mustn’t waste any more time. The bureau is locked, the flat is locked, and here we are again on the stairs. Shall we take a cab? I’m going to the Islands. Would you like a lift? I’ll take this carriage. Ah, you refuse? You are tired of it! Come for a drive! I believe it will come on to rain. Never mind, we’ll put down the hood....”\n\nSvidrigaïlov was already in the carriage. Raskolnikov decided that his suspicions were at least for that moment unjust. Without answering a word he turned and walked back towards the Hay Market. If he had only turned round on his way he might have seen Svidrigaïlov get out not a hundred paces off, dismiss the cab and walk along the pavement. But he had turned the corner and could see nothing. Intense disgust drew him away from Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“To think that I could for one instant have looked for help from that coarse brute, that depraved sensualist and blackguard!” he cried.\n\nRaskolnikov’s judgment was uttered too lightly and hastily: there was something about Svidrigaïlov which gave him a certain original, even a mysterious character. As concerned his sister, Raskolnikov was convinced that Svidrigaïlov would not leave her in peace. But it was too tiresome and unbearable to go on thinking and thinking about this.\n\nWhen he was alone, he had not gone twenty paces before he sank, as usual, into deep thought. On the bridge he stood by the railing and began gazing at the water. And his sister was standing close by him.\n\nHe met her at the entrance to the bridge, but passed by without seeing her. Dounia had never met him like this in the street before and was struck with dismay. She stood still and did not know whether to call to him or not. Suddenly she saw Svidrigaïlov coming quickly from the direction of the Hay Market.\n\nHe seemed to be approaching cautiously. He did not go on to the bridge, but stood aside on the pavement, doing all he could to avoid Raskolnikov’s seeing him. He had observed Dounia for some time and had been making signs to her. She fancied he was signalling to beg her not to speak to her brother, but to come to him.\n\nThat was what Dounia did. She stole by her brother and went up to Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“Let us make haste away,” Svidrigaïlov whispered to her, “I don’t want Rodion Romanovitch to know of our meeting. I must tell you I’ve been sitting with him in the restaurant close by, where he looked me up and I had great difficulty in getting rid of him. He has somehow heard of my letter to you and suspects something. It wasn’t you who told him, of course, but if not you, who then?”\n\n“Well, we’ve turned the corner now,” Dounia interrupted, “and my brother won’t see us. I have to tell you that I am going no further with you. Speak to me here. You can tell it all in the street.”\n\n“In the first place, I can’t say it in the street; secondly, you must hear Sofya Semyonovna too; and, thirdly, I will show you some papers.... Oh well, if you won’t agree to come with me, I shall refuse to give any explanation and go away at once. But I beg you not to forget that a very curious secret of your beloved brother’s is entirely in my keeping.”\n\nDounia stood still, hesitating, and looked at Svidrigaïlov with searching eyes.\n\n“What are you afraid of?” he observed quietly. “The town is not the country. And even in the country you did me more harm than I did you.”\n\n“Have you prepared Sofya Semyonovna?”\n\n“No, I have not said a word to her and am not quite certain whether she is at home now. But most likely she is. She has buried her stepmother to-day: she is not likely to go visiting on such a day. For the time I don’t want to speak to anyone about it and I half regret having spoken to you. The slightest indiscretion is as bad as betrayal in a thing like this. I live there in that house, we are coming to it. That’s the porter of our house--he knows me very well; you see, he’s bowing; he sees I’m coming with a lady and no doubt he has noticed your face already and you will be glad of that if you are afraid of me and suspicious. Excuse my putting things so coarsely. I haven’t a flat to myself; Sofya Semyonovna’s room is next to mine--she lodges in the next flat. The whole floor is let out in lodgings. Why are you frightened like a child? Am I really so terrible?”\n\nSvidrigaïlov’s lips were twisted in a condescending smile; but he was in no smiling mood. His heart was throbbing and he could scarcely breathe. He spoke rather loud to cover his growing excitement. But Dounia did not notice this peculiar excitement, she was so irritated by his remark that she was frightened of him like a child and that he was so terrible to her.\n\n“Though I know that you are not a man... of honour, I am not in the least afraid of you. Lead the way,” she said with apparent composure, but her face was very pale.\n\nSvidrigaïlov stopped at Sonia’s room.\n\n“Allow me to inquire whether she is at home.... She is not. How unfortunate! But I know she may come quite soon. If she’s gone out, it can only be to see a lady about the orphans. Their mother is dead.... I’ve been meddling and making arrangements for them. If Sofya Semyonovna does not come back in ten minutes, I will send her to you, to-day if you like. This is my flat. These are my two rooms. Madame Resslich, my landlady, has the next room. Now, look this way. I will show you my chief piece of evidence: this door from my bedroom leads into two perfectly empty rooms, which are to let. Here they are... You must look into them with some attention.”\n\nSvidrigaïlov occupied two fairly large furnished rooms. Dounia was looking about her mistrustfully, but saw nothing special in the furniture or position of the rooms. Yet there was something to observe, for instance, that Svidrigaïlov’s flat was exactly between two sets of almost uninhabited apartments. His rooms were not entered directly from the passage, but through the landlady’s two almost empty rooms. Unlocking a door leading out of his bedroom, Svidrigaïlov showed Dounia the two empty rooms that were to let. Dounia stopped in the doorway, not knowing what she was called to look upon, but Svidrigaïlov hastened to explain.\n\n“Look here, at this second large room. Notice that door, it’s locked. By the door stands a chair, the only one in the two rooms. I brought it from my rooms so as to listen more conveniently. Just the other side of the door is Sofya Semyonovna’s table; she sat there talking to Rodion Romanovitch. And I sat here listening on two successive evenings, for two hours each time--and of course I was able to learn something, what do you think?”\n\n“You listened?”\n\n“Yes, I did. Now come back to my room; we can’t sit down here.”\n\nHe brought Avdotya Romanovna back into his sitting-room and offered her a chair. He sat down at the opposite side of the table, at least seven feet from her, but probably there was the same glow in his eyes which had once frightened Dounia so much. She shuddered and once more looked about her distrustfully. It was an involuntary gesture; she evidently did not wish to betray her uneasiness. But the secluded position of Svidrigaïlov’s lodging had suddenly struck her. She wanted to ask whether his landlady at least were at home, but pride kept her from asking. Moreover, she had another trouble in her heart incomparably greater than fear for herself. She was in great distress.\n\n“Here is your letter,” she said, laying it on the table. “Can it be true what you write? You hint at a crime committed, you say, by my brother. You hint at it too clearly; you daren’t deny it now. I must tell you that I’d heard of this stupid story before you wrote and don’t believe a word of it. It’s a disgusting and ridiculous suspicion. I know the story and why and how it was invented. You can have no proofs. You promised to prove it. Speak! But let me warn you that I don’t believe you! I don’t believe you!”\n\nDounia said this, speaking hurriedly, and for an instant the colour rushed to her face.\n\n“If you didn’t believe it, how could you risk coming alone to my rooms? Why have you come? Simply from curiosity?”\n\n“Don’t torment me. Speak, speak!”\n\n“There’s no denying that you are a brave girl. Upon my word, I thought you would have asked Mr. Razumihin to escort you here. But he was not with you nor anywhere near. I was on the look-out. It’s spirited of you, it proves you wanted to spare Rodion Romanovitch. But everything is divine in you.... About your brother, what am I to say to you? You’ve just seen him yourself. What did you think of him?”\n\n“Surely that’s not the only thing you are building on?”\n\n“No, not on that, but on his own words. He came here on two successive evenings to see Sofya Semyonovna. I’ve shown you where they sat. He made a full confession to her. He is a murderer. He killed an old woman, a pawnbroker, with whom he had pawned things himself. He killed her sister too, a pedlar woman called Lizaveta, who happened to come in while he was murdering her sister. He killed them with an axe he brought with him. He murdered them to rob them and he did rob them. He took money and various things.... He told all this, word for word, to Sofya Semyonovna, the only person who knows his secret. But she has had no share by word or deed in the murder; she was as horrified at it as you are now. Don’t be anxious, she won’t betray him.”\n\n“It cannot be,” muttered Dounia, with white lips. She gasped for breath. “It cannot be. There was not the slightest cause, no sort of ground.... It’s a lie, a lie!”\n\n“He robbed her, that was the cause, he took money and things. It’s true that by his own admission he made no use of the money or things, but hid them under a stone, where they are now. But that was because he dared not make use of them.”\n\n“But how could he steal, rob? How could he dream of it?” cried Dounia, and she jumped up from the chair. “Why, you know him, and you’ve seen him, can he be a thief?”\n\nShe seemed to be imploring Svidrigaïlov; she had entirely forgotten her fear.\n\n“There are thousands and millions of combinations and possibilities, Avdotya Romanovna. A thief steals and knows he is a scoundrel, but I’ve heard of a gentleman who broke open the mail. Who knows, very likely he thought he was doing a gentlemanly thing! Of course I should not have believed it myself if I’d been told of it as you have, but I believe my own ears. He explained all the causes of it to Sofya Semyonovna too, but she did not believe her ears at first, yet she believed her own eyes at last.”\n\n“What... were the causes?”\n\n“It’s a long story, Avdotya Romanovna. Here’s... how shall I tell you?--A theory of a sort, the same one by which I for instance consider that a single misdeed is permissible if the principal aim is right, a solitary wrongdoing and hundreds of good deeds! It’s galling too, of course, for a young man of gifts and overweening pride to know that if he had, for instance, a paltry three thousand, his whole career, his whole future would be differently shaped and yet not to have that three thousand. Add to that, nervous irritability from hunger, from lodging in a hole, from rags, from a vivid sense of the charm of his social position and his sister’s and mother’s position too. Above all, vanity, pride and vanity, though goodness knows he may have good qualities too.... I am not blaming him, please don’t think it; besides, it’s not my business. A special little theory came in too--a theory of a sort--dividing mankind, you see, into material and superior persons, that is persons to whom the law does not apply owing to their superiority, who make laws for the rest of mankind, the material, that is. It’s all right as a theory, _une théorie comme une autre_. Napoleon attracted him tremendously, that is, what affected him was that a great many men of genius have not hesitated at wrongdoing, but have overstepped the law without thinking about it. He seems to have fancied that he was a genius too--that is, he was convinced of it for a time. He has suffered a great deal and is still suffering from the idea that he could make a theory, but was incapable of boldly overstepping the law, and so he is not a man of genius. And that’s humiliating for a young man of any pride, in our day especially....”\n\n“But remorse? You deny him any moral feeling then? Is he like that?”\n\n“Ah, Avdotya Romanovna, everything is in a muddle now; not that it was ever in very good order. Russians in general are broad in their ideas, Avdotya Romanovna, broad like their land and exceedingly disposed to the fantastic, the chaotic. But it’s a misfortune to be broad without a special genius. Do you remember what a lot of talk we had together on this subject, sitting in the evenings on the terrace after supper? Why, you used to reproach me with breadth! Who knows, perhaps we were talking at the very time when he was lying here thinking over his plan. There are no sacred traditions amongst us, especially in the educated class, Avdotya Romanovna. At the best someone will make them up somehow for himself out of books or from some old chronicle. But those are for the most part the learned and all old fogeys, so that it would be almost ill-bred in a man of society. You know my opinions in general, though. I never blame anyone. I do nothing at all, I persevere in that. But we’ve talked of this more than once before. I was so happy indeed as to interest you in my opinions.... You are very pale, Avdotya Romanovna.”\n\n“I know his theory. I read that article of his about men to whom all is permitted. Razumihin brought it to me.”\n\n“Mr. Razumihin? Your brother’s article? In a magazine? Is there such an article? I didn’t know. It must be interesting. But where are you going, Avdotya Romanovna?”\n\n“I want to see Sofya Semyonovna,” Dounia articulated faintly. “How do I go to her? She has come in, perhaps. I must see her at once. Perhaps she...”\n\nAvdotya Romanovna could not finish. Her breath literally failed her.\n\n“Sofya Semyonovna will not be back till night, at least I believe not. She was to have been back at once, but if not, then she will not be in till quite late.”\n\n“Ah, then you are lying! I see... you were lying... lying all the time.... I don’t believe you! I don’t believe you!” cried Dounia, completely losing her head.\n\nAlmost fainting, she sank on to a chair which Svidrigaïlov made haste to give her.\n\n“Avdotya Romanovna, what is it? Control yourself! Here is some water. Drink a little....”\n\nHe sprinkled some water over her. Dounia shuddered and came to herself.\n\n“It has acted violently,” Svidrigaïlov muttered to himself, frowning. “Avdotya Romanovna, calm yourself! Believe me, he has friends. We will save him. Would you like me to take him abroad? I have money, I can get a ticket in three days. And as for the murder, he will do all sorts of good deeds yet, to atone for it. Calm yourself. He may become a great man yet. Well, how are you? How do you feel?”\n\n“Cruel man! To be able to jeer at it! Let me go...”\n\n“Where are you going?”\n\n“To him. Where is he? Do you know? Why is this door locked? We came in at that door and now it is locked. When did you manage to lock it?”\n\n“We couldn’t be shouting all over the flat on such a subject. I am far from jeering; it’s simply that I’m sick of talking like this. But how can you go in such a state? Do you want to betray him? You will drive him to fury, and he will give himself up. Let me tell you, he is already being watched; they are already on his track. You will simply be giving him away. Wait a little: I saw him and was talking to him just now. He can still be saved. Wait a bit, sit down; let us think it over together. I asked you to come in order to discuss it alone with you and to consider it thoroughly. But do sit down!”\n\n“How can you save him? Can he really be saved?”\n\nDounia sat down. Svidrigaïlov sat down beside her.\n\n“It all depends on you, on you, on you alone,” he began with glowing eyes, almost in a whisper and hardly able to utter the words for emotion.\n\nDounia drew back from him in alarm. He too was trembling all over.\n\n“You... one word from you, and he is saved. I... I’ll save him. I have money and friends. I’ll send him away at once. I’ll get a passport, two passports, one for him and one for me. I have friends... capable people.... If you like, I’ll take a passport for you... for your mother.... What do you want with Razumihin? I love you too.... I love you beyond everything.... Let me kiss the hem of your dress, let me, let me.... The very rustle of it is too much for me. Tell me, ‘do that,’ and I’ll do it. I’ll do everything. I will do the impossible. What you believe, I will believe. I’ll do anything--anything! Don’t, don’t look at me like that. Do you know that you are killing me?...”\n\nHe was almost beginning to rave.... Something seemed suddenly to go to his head. Dounia jumped up and rushed to the door.\n\n“Open it! Open it!” she called, shaking the door. “Open it! Is there no one there?”\n\nSvidrigaïlov got up and came to himself. His still trembling lips slowly broke into an angry mocking smile.\n\n“There is no one at home,” he said quietly and emphatically. “The landlady has gone out, and it’s waste of time to shout like that. You are only exciting yourself uselessly.”\n\n“Where is the key? Open the door at once, at once, base man!”\n\n“I have lost the key and cannot find it.”\n\n“This is an outrage,” cried Dounia, turning pale as death. She rushed to the furthest corner, where she made haste to barricade herself with a little table.\n\nShe did not scream, but she fixed her eyes on her tormentor and watched every movement he made.\n\nSvidrigaïlov remained standing at the other end of the room facing her. He was positively composed, at least in appearance, but his face was pale as before. The mocking smile did not leave his face.\n\n“You spoke of outrage just now, Avdotya Romanovna. In that case you may be sure I’ve taken measures. Sofya Semyonovna is not at home. The Kapernaumovs are far away--there are five locked rooms between. I am at least twice as strong as you are and I have nothing to fear, besides. For you could not complain afterwards. You surely would not be willing actually to betray your brother? Besides, no one would believe you. How should a girl have come alone to visit a solitary man in his lodgings? So that even if you do sacrifice your brother, you could prove nothing. It is very difficult to prove an assault, Avdotya Romanovna.”\n\n“Scoundrel!” whispered Dounia indignantly.\n\n“As you like, but observe I was only speaking by way of a general proposition. It’s my personal conviction that you are perfectly right--violence is hateful. I only spoke to show you that you need have no remorse even if... you were willing to save your brother of your own accord, as I suggest to you. You would be simply submitting to circumstances, to violence, in fact, if we must use that word. Think about it. Your brother’s and your mother’s fate are in your hands. I will be your slave... all my life... I will wait here.”\n\nSvidrigaïlov sat down on the sofa about eight steps from Dounia. She had not the slightest doubt now of his unbending determination. Besides, she knew him. Suddenly she pulled out of her pocket a revolver, cocked it and laid it in her hand on the table. Svidrigaïlov jumped up.\n\n“Aha! So that’s it, is it?” he cried, surprised but smiling maliciously. “Well, that completely alters the aspect of affairs. You’ve made things wonderfully easier for me, Avdotya Romanovna. But where did you get the revolver? Was it Mr. Razumihin? Why, it’s my revolver, an old friend! And how I’ve hunted for it! The shooting lessons I’ve given you in the country have not been thrown away.”\n\n“It’s not your revolver, it belonged to Marfa Petrovna, whom you killed, wretch! There was nothing of yours in her house. I took it when I began to suspect what you were capable of. If you dare to advance one step, I swear I’ll kill you.” She was frantic.\n\n“But your brother? I ask from curiosity,” said Svidrigaïlov, still standing where he was.\n\n“Inform, if you want to! Don’t stir! Don’t come nearer! I’ll shoot! You poisoned your wife, I know; you are a murderer yourself!” She held the revolver ready.\n\n“Are you so positive I poisoned Marfa Petrovna?”\n\n“You did! You hinted it yourself; you talked to me of poison.... I know you went to get it... you had it in readiness.... It was your doing.... It must have been your doing.... Scoundrel!”\n\n“Even if that were true, it would have been for your sake... you would have been the cause.”\n\n“You are lying! I hated you always, always....”\n\n“Oho, Avdotya Romanovna! You seem to have forgotten how you softened to me in the heat of propaganda. I saw it in your eyes. Do you remember that moonlight night, when the nightingale was singing?”\n\n“That’s a lie,” there was a flash of fury in Dounia’s eyes, “that’s a lie and a libel!”\n\n“A lie? Well, if you like, it’s a lie. I made it up. Women ought not to be reminded of such things,” he smiled. “I know you will shoot, you pretty wild creature. Well, shoot away!”\n\nDounia raised the revolver, and deadly pale, gazed at him, measuring the distance and awaiting the first movement on his part. Her lower lip was white and quivering and her big black eyes flashed like fire. He had never seen her so handsome. The fire glowing in her eyes at the moment she raised the revolver seemed to kindle him and there was a pang of anguish in his heart. He took a step forward and a shot rang out. The bullet grazed his hair and flew into the wall behind. He stood still and laughed softly.\n\n“The wasp has stung me. She aimed straight at my head. What’s this? Blood?” he pulled out his handkerchief to wipe the blood, which flowed in a thin stream down his right temple. The bullet seemed to have just grazed the skin.\n\nDounia lowered the revolver and looked at Svidrigaïlov not so much in terror as in a sort of wild amazement. She seemed not to understand what she was doing and what was going on.\n\n“Well, you missed! Fire again, I’ll wait,” said Svidrigaïlov softly, still smiling, but gloomily. “If you go on like that, I shall have time to seize you before you cock again.”\n\nDounia started, quickly cocked the pistol and again raised it.\n\n“Let me be,” she cried in despair. “I swear I’ll shoot again. I... I’ll kill you.”\n\n“Well... at three paces you can hardly help it. But if you don’t... then.” His eyes flashed and he took two steps forward. Dounia shot again: it missed fire.\n\n“You haven’t loaded it properly. Never mind, you have another charge there. Get it ready, I’ll wait.”\n\nHe stood facing her, two paces away, waiting and gazing at her with wild determination, with feverishly passionate, stubborn, set eyes. Dounia saw that he would sooner die than let her go. “And... now, of course she would kill him, at two paces!” Suddenly she flung away the revolver.\n\n“She’s dropped it!” said Svidrigaïlov with surprise, and he drew a deep breath. A weight seemed to have rolled from his heart--perhaps not only the fear of death; indeed he may scarcely have felt it at that moment. It was the deliverance from another feeling, darker and more bitter, which he could not himself have defined.\n\nHe went to Dounia and gently put his arm round her waist. She did not resist, but, trembling like a leaf, looked at him with suppliant eyes. He tried to say something, but his lips moved without being able to utter a sound.\n\n“Let me go,” Dounia implored. Svidrigaïlov shuddered. Her voice now was quite different.\n\n“Then you don’t love me?” he asked softly. Dounia shook her head.\n\n“And... and you can’t? Never?” he whispered in despair.\n\n“Never!”\n\nThere followed a moment of terrible, dumb struggle in the heart of Svidrigaïlov. He looked at her with an indescribable gaze. Suddenly he withdrew his arm, turned quickly to the window and stood facing it. Another moment passed.\n\n“Here’s the key.”\n\nHe took it out of the left pocket of his coat and laid it on the table behind him, without turning or looking at Dounia.\n\n“Take it! Make haste!”\n\nHe looked stubbornly out of the window. Dounia went up to the table to take the key.\n\n“Make haste! Make haste!” repeated Svidrigaïlov, still without turning or moving. But there seemed a terrible significance in the tone of that “make haste.”\n\nDounia understood it, snatched up the key, flew to the door, unlocked it quickly and rushed out of the room. A minute later, beside herself, she ran out on to the canal bank in the direction of X. Bridge.\n\nSvidrigaïlov remained three minutes standing at the window. At last he slowly turned, looked about him and passed his hand over his forehead. A strange smile contorted his face, a pitiful, sad, weak smile, a smile of despair. The blood, which was already getting dry, smeared his hand. He looked angrily at it, then wetted a towel and washed his temple. The revolver which Dounia had flung away lay near the door and suddenly caught his eye. He picked it up and examined it. It was a little pocket three-barrel revolver of old-fashioned construction. There were still two charges and one capsule left in it. It could be fired again. He thought a little, put the revolver in his pocket, took his hat and went out.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nHe spent that evening till ten o’clock going from one low haunt to another. Katia too turned up and sang another gutter song, how a certain “villain and tyrant”\n\n“began kissing Katia.”\nSvidrigaïlov treated Katia and the organ-grinder and some singers and the waiters and two little clerks. He was particularly drawn to these clerks by the fact that they both had crooked noses, one bent to the left and the other to the right. They took him finally to a pleasure garden, where he paid for their entrance. There was one lanky three-year-old pine-tree and three bushes in the garden, besides a “Vauxhall,” which was in reality a drinking-bar where tea too was served, and there were a few green tables and chairs standing round it. A chorus of wretched singers and a drunken but exceedingly depressed German clown from Munich with a red nose entertained the public. The clerks quarrelled with some other clerks and a fight seemed imminent. Svidrigaïlov was chosen to decide the dispute. He listened to them for a quarter of an hour, but they shouted so loud that there was no possibility of understanding them. The only fact that seemed certain was that one of them had stolen something and had even succeeded in selling it on the spot to a Jew, but would not share the spoil with his companion. Finally it appeared that the stolen object was a teaspoon belonging to the Vauxhall. It was missed and the affair began to seem troublesome. Svidrigaïlov paid for the spoon, got up, and walked out of the garden. It was about six o’clock. He had not drunk a drop of wine all this time and had ordered tea more for the sake of appearances than anything.\n\nIt was a dark and stifling evening. Threatening storm-clouds came over the sky about ten o’clock. There was a clap of thunder, and the rain came down like a waterfall. The water fell not in drops, but beat on the earth in streams. There were flashes of lightning every minute and each flash lasted while one could count five.\n\nDrenched to the skin, he went home, locked himself in, opened the bureau, took out all his money and tore up two or three papers. Then, putting the money in his pocket, he was about to change his clothes, but, looking out of the window and listening to the thunder and the rain, he gave up the idea, took up his hat and went out of the room without locking the door. He went straight to Sonia. She was at home.\n\nShe was not alone: the four Kapernaumov children were with her. She was giving them tea. She received Svidrigaïlov in respectful silence, looking wonderingly at his soaking clothes. The children all ran away at once in indescribable terror.\n\nSvidrigaïlov sat down at the table and asked Sonia to sit beside him. She timidly prepared to listen.\n\n“I may be going to America, Sofya Semyonovna,” said Svidrigaïlov, “and as I am probably seeing you for the last time, I have come to make some arrangements. Well, did you see the lady to-day? I know what she said to you, you need not tell me.” (Sonia made a movement and blushed.) “Those people have their own way of doing things. As to your sisters and your brother, they are really provided for and the money assigned to them I’ve put into safe keeping and have received acknowledgments. You had better take charge of the receipts, in case anything happens. Here, take them! Well now, that’s settled. Here are three 5-per-cent bonds to the value of three thousand roubles. Take those for yourself, entirely for yourself, and let that be strictly between ourselves, so that no one knows of it, whatever you hear. You will need the money, for to go on living in the old way, Sofya Semyonovna, is bad, and besides there is no need for it now.”\n\n“I am so much indebted to you, and so are the children and my stepmother,” said Sonia hurriedly, “and if I’ve said so little... please don’t consider...”\n\n“That’s enough! that’s enough!”\n\n“But as for the money, Arkady Ivanovitch, I am very grateful to you, but I don’t need it now. I can always earn my own living. Don’t think me ungrateful. If you are so charitable, that money....”\n\n“It’s for you, for you, Sofya Semyonovna, and please don’t waste words over it. I haven’t time for it. You will want it. Rodion Romanovitch has two alternatives: a bullet in the brain or Siberia.” (Sonia looked wildly at him, and started.) “Don’t be uneasy, I know all about it from himself and I am not a gossip; I won’t tell anyone. It was good advice when you told him to give himself up and confess. It would be much better for him. Well, if it turns out to be Siberia, he will go and you will follow him. That’s so, isn’t it? And if so, you’ll need money. You’ll need it for him, do you understand? Giving it to you is the same as my giving it to him. Besides, you promised Amalia Ivanovna to pay what’s owing. I heard you. How can you undertake such obligations so heedlessly, Sofya Semyonovna? It was Katerina Ivanovna’s debt and not yours, so you ought not to have taken any notice of the German woman. You can’t get through the world like that. If you are ever questioned about me--to-morrow or the day after you will be asked--don’t say anything about my coming to see you now and don’t show the money to anyone or say a word about it. Well, now good-bye.” (He got up.) “My greetings to Rodion Romanovitch. By the way, you’d better put the money for the present in Mr. Razumihin’s keeping. You know Mr. Razumihin? Of course you do. He’s not a bad fellow. Take it to him to-morrow or... when the time comes. And till then, hide it carefully.”\n\nSonia too jumped up from her chair and looked in dismay at Svidrigaïlov. She longed to speak, to ask a question, but for the first moments she did not dare and did not know how to begin.\n\n“How can you... how can you be going now, in such rain?”\n\n“Why, be starting for America, and be stopped by rain! Ha, ha! Good-bye, Sofya Semyonovna, my dear! Live and live long, you will be of use to others. By the way... tell Mr. Razumihin I send my greetings to him. Tell him Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov sends his greetings. Be sure to.”\n\nHe went out, leaving Sonia in a state of wondering anxiety and vague apprehension.\n\nIt appeared afterwards that on the same evening, at twenty past eleven, he made another very eccentric and unexpected visit. The rain still persisted. Drenched to the skin, he walked into the little flat where the parents of his betrothed lived, in Third Street in Vassilyevsky Island. He knocked some time before he was admitted, and his visit at first caused great perturbation; but Svidrigaïlov could be very fascinating when he liked, so that the first, and indeed very intelligent surmise of the sensible parents that Svidrigaïlov had probably had so much to drink that he did not know what he was doing vanished immediately. The decrepit father was wheeled in to see Svidrigaïlov by the tender and sensible mother, who as usual began the conversation with various irrelevant questions. She never asked a direct question, but began by smiling and rubbing her hands and then, if she were obliged to ascertain something--for instance, when Svidrigaïlov would like to have the wedding--she would begin by interested and almost eager questions about Paris and the court life there, and only by degrees brought the conversation round to Third Street. On other occasions this had of course been very impressive, but this time Arkady Ivanovitch seemed particularly impatient, and insisted on seeing his betrothed at once, though he had been informed, to begin with, that she had already gone to bed. The girl of course appeared.\n\nSvidrigaïlov informed her at once that he was obliged by very important affairs to leave Petersburg for a time, and therefore brought her fifteen thousand roubles and begged her accept them as a present from him, as he had long been intending to make her this trifling present before their wedding. The logical connection of the present with his immediate departure and the absolute necessity of visiting them for that purpose in pouring rain at midnight was not made clear. But it all went off very well; even the inevitable ejaculations of wonder and regret, the inevitable questions were extraordinarily few and restrained. On the other hand, the gratitude expressed was most glowing and was reinforced by tears from the most sensible of mothers. Svidrigaïlov got up, laughed, kissed his betrothed, patted her cheek, declared he would soon come back, and noticing in her eyes, together with childish curiosity, a sort of earnest dumb inquiry, reflected and kissed her again, though he felt sincere anger inwardly at the thought that his present would be immediately locked up in the keeping of the most sensible of mothers. He went away, leaving them all in a state of extraordinary excitement, but the tender mamma, speaking quietly in a half whisper, settled some of the most important of their doubts, concluding that Svidrigaïlov was a great man, a man of great affairs and connections and of great wealth--there was no knowing what he had in his mind. He would start off on a journey and give away money just as the fancy took him, so that there was nothing surprising about it. Of course it was strange that he was wet through, but Englishmen, for instance, are even more eccentric, and all these people of high society didn’t think of what was said of them and didn’t stand on ceremony. Possibly, indeed, he came like that on purpose to show that he was not afraid of anyone. Above all, not a word should be said about it, for God knows what might come of it, and the money must be locked up, and it was most fortunate that Fedosya, the cook, had not left the kitchen. And above all not a word must be said to that old cat, Madame Resslich, and so on and so on. They sat up whispering till two o’clock, but the girl went to bed much earlier, amazed and rather sorrowful.\n\nSvidrigaïlov meanwhile, exactly at midnight, crossed the bridge on the way back to the mainland. The rain had ceased and there was a roaring wind. He began shivering, and for one moment he gazed at the black waters of the Little Neva with a look of special interest, even inquiry. But he soon felt it very cold, standing by the water; he turned and went towards Y. Prospect. He walked along that endless street for a long time, almost half an hour, more than once stumbling in the dark on the wooden pavement, but continually looking for something on the right side of the street. He had noticed passing through this street lately that there was a hotel somewhere towards the end, built of wood, but fairly large, and its name he remembered was something like Adrianople. He was not mistaken: the hotel was so conspicuous in that God-forsaken place that he could not fail to see it even in the dark. It was a long, blackened wooden building, and in spite of the late hour there were lights in the windows and signs of life within. He went in and asked a ragged fellow who met him in the corridor for a room. The latter, scanning Svidrigaïlov, pulled himself together and led him at once to a close and tiny room in the distance, at the end of the corridor, under the stairs. There was no other, all were occupied. The ragged fellow looked inquiringly.\n\n“Is there tea?” asked Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“What else is there?”\n\n“Veal, vodka, savouries.”\n\n“Bring me tea and veal.”\n\n“And you want nothing else?” he asked with apparent surprise.\n\n“Nothing, nothing.”\n\nThe ragged man went away, completely disillusioned.\n\n“It must be a nice place,” thought Svidrigaïlov. “How was it I didn’t know it? I expect I look as if I came from a café chantant and have had some adventure on the way. It would be interesting to know who stayed here?”\n\nHe lighted the candle and looked at the room more carefully. It was a room so low-pitched that Svidrigaïlov could only just stand up in it; it had one window; the bed, which was very dirty, and the plain-stained chair and table almost filled it up. The walls looked as though they were made of planks, covered with shabby paper, so torn and dusty that the pattern was indistinguishable, though the general colour--yellow--could still be made out. One of the walls was cut short by the sloping ceiling, though the room was not an attic but just under the stairs.\n\nSvidrigaïlov set down the candle, sat down on the bed and sank into thought. But a strange persistent murmur which sometimes rose to a shout in the next room attracted his attention. The murmur had not ceased from the moment he entered the room. He listened: someone was upbraiding and almost tearfully scolding, but he heard only one voice.\n\nSvidrigaïlov got up, shaded the light with his hand and at once he saw light through a crack in the wall; he went up and peeped through. The room, which was somewhat larger than his, had two occupants. One of them, a very curly-headed man with a red inflamed face, was standing in the pose of an orator, without his coat, with his legs wide apart to preserve his balance, and smiting himself on the breast. He reproached the other with being a beggar, with having no standing whatever. He declared that he had taken the other out of the gutter and he could turn him out when he liked, and that only the finger of Providence sees it all. The object of his reproaches was sitting in a chair, and had the air of a man who wants dreadfully to sneeze, but can’t. He sometimes turned sheepish and befogged eyes on the speaker, but obviously had not the slightest idea what he was talking about and scarcely heard it. A candle was burning down on the table; there were wine-glasses, a nearly empty bottle of vodka, bread and cucumber, and glasses with the dregs of stale tea. After gazing attentively at this, Svidrigaïlov turned away indifferently and sat down on the bed.\n\nThe ragged attendant, returning with the tea, could not resist asking him again whether he didn’t want anything more, and again receiving a negative reply, finally withdrew. Svidrigaïlov made haste to drink a glass of tea to warm himself, but could not eat anything. He began to feel feverish. He took off his coat and, wrapping himself in the blanket, lay down on the bed. He was annoyed. “It would have been better to be well for the occasion,” he thought with a smile. The room was close, the candle burnt dimly, the wind was roaring outside, he heard a mouse scratching in the corner and the room smelt of mice and of leather. He lay in a sort of reverie: one thought followed another. He felt a longing to fix his imagination on something. “It must be a garden under the window,” he thought. “There’s a sound of trees. How I dislike the sound of trees on a stormy night, in the dark! They give one a horrid feeling.” He remembered how he had disliked it when he passed Petrovsky Park just now. This reminded him of the bridge over the Little Neva and he felt cold again as he had when standing there. “I never have liked water,” he thought, “even in a landscape,” and he suddenly smiled again at a strange idea: “Surely now all these questions of taste and comfort ought not to matter, but I’ve become more particular, like an animal that picks out a special place... for such an occasion. I ought to have gone into the Petrovsky Park! I suppose it seemed dark, cold, ha-ha! As though I were seeking pleasant sensations!... By the way, why haven’t I put out the candle?” he blew it out. “They’ve gone to bed next door,” he thought, not seeing the light at the crack. “Well, now, Marfa Petrovna, now is the time for you to turn up; it’s dark, and the very time and place for you. But now you won’t come!”\n\nHe suddenly recalled how, an hour before carrying out his design on Dounia, he had recommended Raskolnikov to trust her to Razumihin’s keeping. “I suppose I really did say it, as Raskolnikov guessed, to tease myself. But what a rogue that Raskolnikov is! He’s gone through a good deal. He may be a successful rogue in time when he’s got over his nonsense. But now he’s _too_ eager for life. These young men are contemptible on that point. But, hang the fellow! Let him please himself, it’s nothing to do with me.”\n\nHe could not get to sleep. By degrees Dounia’s image rose before him, and a shudder ran over him. “No, I must give up all that now,” he thought, rousing himself. “I must think of something else. It’s queer and funny. I never had a great hatred for anyone, I never particularly desired to avenge myself even, and that’s a bad sign, a bad sign, a bad sign. I never liked quarrelling either, and never lost my temper--that’s a bad sign too. And the promises I made her just now, too--Damnation! But--who knows?--perhaps she would have made a new man of me somehow....”\n\nHe ground his teeth and sank into silence again. Again Dounia’s image rose before him, just as she was when, after shooting the first time, she had lowered the revolver in terror and gazed blankly at him, so that he might have seized her twice over and she would not have lifted a hand to defend herself if he had not reminded her. He recalled how at that instant he felt almost sorry for her, how he had felt a pang at his heart...\n\n“Aïe! Damnation, these thoughts again! I must put it away!”\n\nHe was dozing off; the feverish shiver had ceased, when suddenly something seemed to run over his arm and leg under the bedclothes. He started. “Ugh! hang it! I believe it’s a mouse,” he thought, “that’s the veal I left on the table.” He felt fearfully disinclined to pull off the blanket, get up, get cold, but all at once something unpleasant ran over his leg again. He pulled off the blanket and lighted the candle. Shaking with feverish chill he bent down to examine the bed: there was nothing. He shook the blanket and suddenly a mouse jumped out on the sheet. He tried to catch it, but the mouse ran to and fro in zigzags without leaving the bed, slipped between his fingers, ran over his hand and suddenly darted under the pillow. He threw down the pillow, but in one instant felt something leap on his chest and dart over his body and down his back under his shirt. He trembled nervously and woke up.\n\nThe room was dark. He was lying on the bed and wrapped up in the blanket as before. The wind was howling under the window. “How disgusting,” he thought with annoyance.\n\nHe got up and sat on the edge of the bedstead with his back to the window. “It’s better not to sleep at all,” he decided. There was a cold damp draught from the window, however; without getting up he drew the blanket over him and wrapped himself in it. He was not thinking of anything and did not want to think. But one image rose after another, incoherent scraps of thought without beginning or end passed through his mind. He sank into drowsiness. Perhaps the cold, or the dampness, or the dark, or the wind that howled under the window and tossed the trees roused a sort of persistent craving for the fantastic. He kept dwelling on images of flowers, he fancied a charming flower garden, a bright, warm, almost hot day, a holiday--Trinity day. A fine, sumptuous country cottage in the English taste overgrown with fragrant flowers, with flower beds going round the house; the porch, wreathed in climbers, was surrounded with beds of roses. A light, cool staircase, carpeted with rich rugs, was decorated with rare plants in china pots. He noticed particularly in the windows nosegays of tender, white, heavily fragrant narcissus bending over their bright, green, thick long stalks. He was reluctant to move away from them, but he went up the stairs and came into a large, high drawing-room and again everywhere--at the windows, the doors on to the balcony, and on the balcony itself--were flowers. The floors were strewn with freshly-cut fragrant hay, the windows were open, a fresh, cool, light air came into the room. The birds were chirruping under the window, and in the middle of the room, on a table covered with a white satin shroud, stood a coffin. The coffin was covered with white silk and edged with a thick white frill; wreaths of flowers surrounded it on all sides. Among the flowers lay a girl in a white muslin dress, with her arms crossed and pressed on her bosom, as though carved out of marble. But her loose fair hair was wet; there was a wreath of roses on her head. The stern and already rigid profile of her face looked as though chiselled of marble too, and the smile on her pale lips was full of an immense unchildish misery and sorrowful appeal. Svidrigaïlov knew that girl; there was no holy image, no burning candle beside the coffin; no sound of prayers: the girl had drowned herself. She was only fourteen, but her heart was broken. And she had destroyed herself, crushed by an insult that had appalled and amazed that childish soul, had smirched that angel purity with unmerited disgrace and torn from her a last scream of despair, unheeded and brutally disregarded, on a dark night in the cold and wet while the wind howled....\n\nSvidrigaïlov came to himself, got up from the bed and went to the window. He felt for the latch and opened it. The wind lashed furiously into the little room and stung his face and his chest, only covered with his shirt, as though with frost. Under the window there must have been something like a garden, and apparently a pleasure garden. There, too, probably there were tea-tables and singing in the daytime. Now drops of rain flew in at the window from the trees and bushes; it was dark as in a cellar, so that he could only just make out some dark blurs of objects. Svidrigaïlov, bending down with elbows on the window-sill, gazed for five minutes into the darkness; the boom of a cannon, followed by a second one, resounded in the darkness of the night. “Ah, the signal! The river is overflowing,” he thought. “By morning it will be swirling down the street in the lower parts, flooding the basements and cellars. The cellar rats will swim out, and men will curse in the rain and wind as they drag their rubbish to their upper storeys. What time is it now?” And he had hardly thought it when, somewhere near, a clock on the wall, ticking away hurriedly, struck three.\n\n“Aha! It will be light in an hour! Why wait? I’ll go out at once straight to the park. I’ll choose a great bush there drenched with rain, so that as soon as one’s shoulder touches it, millions of drops drip on one’s head.”\n\nHe moved away from the window, shut it, lighted the candle, put on his waistcoat, his overcoat and his hat and went out, carrying the candle, into the passage to look for the ragged attendant who would be asleep somewhere in the midst of candle-ends and all sorts of rubbish, to pay him for the room and leave the hotel. “It’s the best minute; I couldn’t choose a better.”\n\nHe walked for some time through a long narrow corridor without finding anyone and was just going to call out, when suddenly in a dark corner between an old cupboard and the door he caught sight of a strange object which seemed to be alive. He bent down with the candle and saw a little girl, not more than five years old, shivering and crying, with her clothes as wet as a soaking house-flannel. She did not seem afraid of Svidrigaïlov, but looked at him with blank amazement out of her big black eyes. Now and then she sobbed as children do when they have been crying a long time, but are beginning to be comforted. The child’s face was pale and tired, she was numb with cold. “How can she have come here? She must have hidden here and not slept all night.” He began questioning her. The child suddenly becoming animated, chattered away in her baby language, something about “mammy” and that “mammy would beat her,” and about some cup that she had “bwoken.” The child chattered on without stopping. He could only guess from what she said that she was a neglected child, whose mother, probably a drunken cook, in the service of the hotel, whipped and frightened her; that the child had broken a cup of her mother’s and was so frightened that she had run away the evening before, had hidden for a long while somewhere outside in the rain, at last had made her way in here, hidden behind the cupboard and spent the night there, crying and trembling from the damp, the darkness and the fear that she would be badly beaten for it. He took her in his arms, went back to his room, sat her on the bed, and began undressing her. The torn shoes which she had on her stockingless feet were as wet as if they had been standing in a puddle all night. When he had undressed her, he put her on the bed, covered her up and wrapped her in the blanket from her head downwards. She fell asleep at once. Then he sank into dreary musing again.\n\n“What folly to trouble myself,” he decided suddenly with an oppressive feeling of annoyance. “What idiocy!” In vexation he took up the candle to go and look for the ragged attendant again and make haste to go away. “Damn the child!” he thought as he opened the door, but he turned again to see whether the child was asleep. He raised the blanket carefully. The child was sleeping soundly, she had got warm under the blanket, and her pale cheeks were flushed. But strange to say that flush seemed brighter and coarser than the rosy cheeks of childhood. “It’s a flush of fever,” thought Svidrigaïlov. It was like the flush from drinking, as though she had been given a full glass to drink. Her crimson lips were hot and glowing; but what was this? He suddenly fancied that her long black eyelashes were quivering, as though the lids were opening and a sly crafty eye peeped out with an unchildlike wink, as though the little girl were not asleep, but pretending. Yes, it was so. Her lips parted in a smile. The corners of her mouth quivered, as though she were trying to control them. But now she quite gave up all effort, now it was a grin, a broad grin; there was something shameless, provocative in that quite unchildish face; it was depravity, it was the face of a harlot, the shameless face of a French harlot. Now both eyes opened wide; they turned a glowing, shameless glance upon him; they laughed, invited him.... There was something infinitely hideous and shocking in that laugh, in those eyes, in such nastiness in the face of a child. “What, at five years old?” Svidrigaïlov muttered in genuine horror. “What does it mean?” And now she turned to him, her little face all aglow, holding out her arms.... “Accursed child!” Svidrigaïlov cried, raising his hand to strike her, but at that moment he woke up.\n\nHe was in the same bed, still wrapped in the blanket. The candle had not been lighted, and daylight was streaming in at the windows.\n\n“I’ve had nightmare all night!” He got up angrily, feeling utterly shattered; his bones ached. There was a thick mist outside and he could see nothing. It was nearly five. He had overslept himself! He got up, put on his still damp jacket and overcoat. Feeling the revolver in his pocket, he took it out and then he sat down, took a notebook out of his pocket and in the most conspicuous place on the title page wrote a few lines in large letters. Reading them over, he sank into thought with his elbows on the table. The revolver and the notebook lay beside him. Some flies woke up and settled on the untouched veal, which was still on the table. He stared at them and at last with his free right hand began trying to catch one. He tried till he was tired, but could not catch it. At last, realising that he was engaged in this interesting pursuit, he started, got up and walked resolutely out of the room. A minute later he was in the street.\n\nA thick milky mist hung over the town. Svidrigaïlov walked along the slippery dirty wooden pavement towards the Little Neva. He was picturing the waters of the Little Neva swollen in the night, Petrovsky Island, the wet paths, the wet grass, the wet trees and bushes and at last the bush.... He began ill-humouredly staring at the houses, trying to think of something else. There was not a cabman or a passer-by in the street. The bright yellow, wooden, little houses looked dirty and dejected with their closed shutters. The cold and damp penetrated his whole body and he began to shiver. From time to time he came across shop signs and read each carefully. At last he reached the end of the wooden pavement and came to a big stone house. A dirty, shivering dog crossed his path with its tail between its legs. A man in a greatcoat lay face downwards, dead drunk, across the pavement. He looked at him and went on. A high tower stood up on the left. “Bah!” he shouted, “here is a place. Why should it be Petrovsky? It will be in the presence of an official witness anyway....”\n\nHe almost smiled at this new thought and turned into the street where there was the big house with the tower. At the great closed gates of the house, a little man stood with his shoulder leaning against them, wrapped in a grey soldier’s coat, with a copper Achilles helmet on his head. He cast a drowsy and indifferent glance at Svidrigaïlov. His face wore that perpetual look of peevish dejection, which is so sourly printed on all faces of Jewish race without exception. They both, Svidrigaïlov and Achilles, stared at each other for a few minutes without speaking. At last it struck Achilles as irregular for a man not drunk to be standing three steps from him, staring and not saying a word.\n\n“What do you want here?” he said, without moving or changing his position.\n\n“Nothing, brother, good morning,” answered Svidrigaïlov.\n\n“This isn’t the place.”\n\n“I am going to foreign parts, brother.”\n\n“To foreign parts?”\n\n“To America.”\n\n“America.”\n\nSvidrigaïlov took out the revolver and cocked it. Achilles raised his eyebrows.\n\n“I say, this is not the place for such jokes!”\n\n“Why shouldn’t it be the place?”\n\n“Because it isn’t.”\n\n“Well, brother, I don’t mind that. It’s a good place. When you are asked, you just say he was going, he said, to America.”\n\nHe put the revolver to his right temple.\n\n“You can’t do it here, it’s not the place,” cried Achilles, rousing himself, his eyes growing bigger and bigger.\n\nSvidrigaïlov pulled the trigger.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nThe same day, about seven o’clock in the evening, Raskolnikov was on his way to his mother’s and sister’s lodging--the lodging in Bakaleyev’s house which Razumihin had found for them. The stairs went up from the street. Raskolnikov walked with lagging steps, as though still hesitating whether to go or not. But nothing would have turned him back: his decision was taken.\n\n“Besides, it doesn’t matter, they still know nothing,” he thought, “and they are used to thinking of me as eccentric.”\n\nHe was appallingly dressed: his clothes torn and dirty, soaked with a night’s rain. His face was almost distorted from fatigue, exposure, the inward conflict that had lasted for twenty-four hours. He had spent all the previous night alone, God knows where. But anyway he had reached a decision.\n\nHe knocked at the door which was opened by his mother. Dounia was not at home. Even the servant happened to be out. At first Pulcheria Alexandrovna was speechless with joy and surprise; then she took him by the hand and drew him into the room.\n\n“Here you are!” she began, faltering with joy. “Don’t be angry with me, Rodya, for welcoming you so foolishly with tears: I am laughing, not crying. Did you think I was crying? No, I am delighted, but I’ve got into such a stupid habit of shedding tears. I’ve been like that ever since your father’s death. I cry for anything. Sit down, dear boy, you must be tired; I see you are. Ah, how muddy you are.”\n\n“I was in the rain yesterday, mother....” Raskolnikov began.\n\n“No, no,” Pulcheria Alexandrovna hurriedly interrupted, “you thought I was going to cross-question you in the womanish way I used to; don’t be anxious, I understand, I understand it all: now I’ve learned the ways here and truly I see for myself that they are better. I’ve made up my mind once for all: how could I understand your plans and expect you to give an account of them? God knows what concerns and plans you may have, or what ideas you are hatching; so it’s not for me to keep nudging your elbow, asking you what you are thinking about? But, my goodness! why am I running to and fro as though I were crazy...? I am reading your article in the magazine for the third time, Rodya. Dmitri Prokofitch brought it to me. Directly I saw it I cried out to myself: ‘There, foolish one,’ I thought, ‘that’s what he is busy about; that’s the solution of the mystery! Learned people are always like that. He may have some new ideas in his head just now; he is thinking them over and I worry him and upset him.’ I read it, my dear, and of course there was a great deal I did not understand; but that’s only natural--how should I?”\n\n“Show me, mother.”\n\nRaskolnikov took the magazine and glanced at his article. Incongruous as it was with his mood and his circumstances, he felt that strange and bitter sweet sensation that every author experiences the first time he sees himself in print; besides, he was only twenty-three. It lasted only a moment. After reading a few lines he frowned and his heart throbbed with anguish. He recalled all the inward conflict of the preceding months. He flung the article on the table with disgust and anger.\n\n“But, however foolish I may be, Rodya, I can see for myself that you will very soon be one of the leading--if not the leading man--in the world of Russian thought. And they dared to think you were mad! You don’t know, but they really thought that. Ah, the despicable creatures, how could they understand genius! And Dounia, Dounia was all but believing it--what do you say to that? Your father sent twice to magazines--the first time poems (I’ve got the manuscript and will show you) and the second time a whole novel (I begged him to let me copy it out) and how we prayed that they should be taken--they weren’t! I was breaking my heart, Rodya, six or seven days ago over your food and your clothes and the way you are living. But now I see again how foolish I was, for you can attain any position you like by your intellect and talent. No doubt you don’t care about that for the present and you are occupied with much more important matters....”\n\n“Dounia’s not at home, mother?”\n\n“No, Rodya. I often don’t see her; she leaves me alone. Dmitri Prokofitch comes to see me, it’s so good of him, and he always talks about you. He loves you and respects you, my dear. I don’t say that Dounia is very wanting in consideration. I am not complaining. She has her ways and I have mine; she seems to have got some secrets of late and I never have any secrets from you two. Of course, I am sure that Dounia has far too much sense, and besides she loves you and me... but I don’t know what it will all lead to. You’ve made me so happy by coming now, Rodya, but she has missed you by going out; when she comes in I’ll tell her: ‘Your brother came in while you were out. Where have you been all this time?’ You mustn’t spoil me, Rodya, you know; come when you can, but if you can’t, it doesn’t matter, I can wait. I shall know, anyway, that you are fond of me, that will be enough for me. I shall read what you write, I shall hear about you from everyone, and sometimes you’ll come yourself to see me. What could be better? Here you’ve come now to comfort your mother, I see that.”\n\nHere Pulcheria Alexandrovna began to cry.\n\n“Here I am again! Don’t mind my foolishness. My goodness, why am I sitting here?” she cried, jumping up. “There is coffee and I don’t offer you any. Ah, that’s the selfishness of old age. I’ll get it at once!”\n\n“Mother, don’t trouble, I am going at once. I haven’t come for that. Please listen to me.”\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna went up to him timidly.\n\n“Mother, whatever happens, whatever you hear about me, whatever you are told about me, will you always love me as you do now?” he asked suddenly from the fullness of his heart, as though not thinking of his words and not weighing them.\n\n“Rodya, Rodya, what is the matter? How can you ask me such a question? Why, who will tell me anything about you? Besides, I shouldn’t believe anyone, I should refuse to listen.”\n\n“I’ve come to assure you that I’ve always loved you and I am glad that we are alone, even glad Dounia is out,” he went on with the same impulse. “I have come to tell you that though you will be unhappy, you must believe that your son loves you now more than himself, and that all you thought about me, that I was cruel and didn’t care about you, was all a mistake. I shall never cease to love you.... Well, that’s enough: I thought I must do this and begin with this....”\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna embraced him in silence, pressing him to her bosom and weeping gently.\n\n“I don’t know what is wrong with you, Rodya,” she said at last. “I’ve been thinking all this time that we were simply boring you and now I see that there is a great sorrow in store for you, and that’s why you are miserable. I’ve foreseen it a long time, Rodya. Forgive me for speaking about it. I keep thinking about it and lie awake at nights. Your sister lay talking in her sleep all last night, talking of nothing but you. I caught something, but I couldn’t make it out. I felt all the morning as though I were going to be hanged, waiting for something, expecting something, and now it has come! Rodya, Rodya, where are you going? You are going away somewhere?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That’s what I thought! I can come with you, you know, if you need me. And Dounia, too; she loves you, she loves you dearly--and Sofya Semyonovna may come with us if you like. You see, I am glad to look upon her as a daughter even... Dmitri Prokofitch will help us to go together. But... where... are you going?”\n\n“Good-bye, mother.”\n\n“What, to-day?” she cried, as though losing him for ever.\n\n“I can’t stay, I must go now....”\n\n“And can’t I come with you?”\n\n“No, but kneel down and pray to God for me. Your prayer perhaps will reach Him.”\n\n“Let me bless you and sign you with the cross. That’s right, that’s right. Oh, God, what are we doing?”\n\nYes, he was glad, he was very glad that there was no one there, that he was alone with his mother. For the first time after all those awful months his heart was softened. He fell down before her, he kissed her feet and both wept, embracing. And she was not surprised and did not question him this time. For some days she had realised that something awful was happening to her son and that now some terrible minute had come for him.\n\n“Rodya, my darling, my first born,” she said sobbing, “now you are just as when you were little. You would run like this to me and hug me and kiss me. When your father was living and we were poor, you comforted us simply by being with us and when I buried your father, how often we wept together at his grave and embraced, as now. And if I’ve been crying lately, it’s that my mother’s heart had a foreboding of trouble. The first time I saw you, that evening, you remember, as soon as we arrived here, I guessed simply from your eyes. My heart sank at once, and to-day when I opened the door and looked at you, I thought the fatal hour had come. Rodya, Rodya, you are not going away to-day?”\n“No!”\n\n“You’ll come again?”\n\n“Yes... I’ll come.”\n\n“Rodya, don’t be angry, I don’t dare to question you. I know I mustn’t. Only say two words to me--is it far where you are going?”\n\n“Very far.”\n\n“What is awaiting you there? Some post or career for you?”\n\n“What God sends... only pray for me.” Raskolnikov went to the door, but she clutched him and gazed despairingly into his eyes. Her face worked with terror.\n\n“Enough, mother,” said Raskolnikov, deeply regretting that he had come.\n\n“Not for ever, it’s not yet for ever? You’ll come, you’ll come to-morrow?”\n\n“I will, I will, good-bye.” He tore himself away at last.\n\nIt was a warm, fresh, bright evening; it had cleared up in the morning. Raskolnikov went to his lodgings; he made haste. He wanted to finish all before sunset. He did not want to meet anyone till then. Going up the stairs he noticed that Nastasya rushed from the samovar to watch him intently. “Can anyone have come to see me?” he wondered. He had a disgusted vision of Porfiry. But opening his door he saw Dounia. She was sitting alone, plunged in deep thought, and looked as though she had been waiting a long time. He stopped short in the doorway. She rose from the sofa in dismay and stood up facing him. Her eyes, fixed upon him, betrayed horror and infinite grief. And from those eyes alone he saw at once that she knew.\n\n“Am I to come in or go away?” he asked uncertainly.\n\n“I’ve been all day with Sofya Semyonovna. We were both waiting for you. We thought that you would be sure to come there.”\n\nRaskolnikov went into the room and sank exhausted on a chair.\n\n“I feel weak, Dounia, I am very tired; and I should have liked at this moment to be able to control myself.”\n\nHe glanced at her mistrustfully.\n\n“Where were you all night?”\n\n“I don’t remember clearly. You see, sister, I wanted to make up my mind once for all, and several times I walked by the Neva, I remember that I wanted to end it all there, but... I couldn’t make up my mind,” he whispered, looking at her mistrustfully again.\n\n“Thank God! That was just what we were afraid of, Sofya Semyonovna and I. Then you still have faith in life? Thank God, thank God!”\n\nRaskolnikov smiled bitterly.\n\n“I haven’t faith, but I have just been weeping in mother’s arms; I haven’t faith, but I have just asked her to pray for me. I don’t know how it is, Dounia, I don’t understand it.”\n\n“Have you been at mother’s? Have you told her?” cried Dounia, horror-stricken. “Surely you haven’t done that?”\n\n“No, I didn’t tell her... in words; but she understood a great deal. She heard you talking in your sleep. I am sure she half understands it already. Perhaps I did wrong in going to see her. I don’t know why I did go. I am a contemptible person, Dounia.”\n\n“A contemptible person, but ready to face suffering! You are, aren’t you?”\n\n“Yes, I am going. At once. Yes, to escape the disgrace I thought of drowning myself, Dounia, but as I looked into the water, I thought that if I had considered myself strong till now I’d better not be afraid of disgrace,” he said, hurrying on. “It’s pride, Dounia.”\n\n“Pride, Rodya.”\n\nThere was a gleam of fire in his lustreless eyes; he seemed to be glad to think that he was still proud.\n\n“You don’t think, sister, that I was simply afraid of the water?” he asked, looking into her face with a sinister smile.\n\n“Oh, Rodya, hush!” cried Dounia bitterly. Silence lasted for two minutes. He sat with his eyes fixed on the floor; Dounia stood at the other end of the table and looked at him with anguish. Suddenly he got up.\n\n“It’s late, it’s time to go! I am going at once to give myself up. But I don’t know why I am going to give myself up.”\n\nBig tears fell down her cheeks.\n\n“You are crying, sister, but can you hold out your hand to me?”\n\n“You doubted it?”\n\nShe threw her arms round him.\n\n“Aren’t you half expiating your crime by facing the suffering?” she cried, holding him close and kissing him.\n\n“Crime? What crime?” he cried in sudden fury. “That I killed a vile noxious insect, an old pawnbroker woman, of use to no one!... Killing her was atonement for forty sins. She was sucking the life out of poor people. Was that a crime? I am not thinking of it and I am not thinking of expiating it, and why are you all rubbing it in on all sides? ‘A crime! a crime!’ Only now I see clearly the imbecility of my cowardice, now that I have decided to face this superfluous disgrace. It’s simply because I am contemptible and have nothing in me that I have decided to, perhaps too for my advantage, as that... Porfiry... suggested!”\n\n“Brother, brother, what are you saying? Why, you have shed blood?” cried Dounia in despair.\n\n“Which all men shed,” he put in almost frantically, “which flows and has always flowed in streams, which is spilt like champagne, and for which men are crowned in the Capitol and are called afterwards benefactors of mankind. Look into it more carefully and understand it! I too wanted to do good to men and would have done hundreds, thousands of good deeds to make up for that one piece of stupidity, not stupidity even, simply clumsiness, for the idea was by no means so stupid as it seems now that it has failed.... (Everything seems stupid when it fails.) By that stupidity I only wanted to put myself into an independent position, to take the first step, to obtain means, and then everything would have been smoothed over by benefits immeasurable in comparison.... But I... I couldn’t carry out even the first step, because I am contemptible, that’s what’s the matter! And yet I won’t look at it as you do. If I had succeeded I should have been crowned with glory, but now I’m trapped.”\n\n“But that’s not so, not so! Brother, what are you saying?”\n\n“Ah, it’s not picturesque, not æsthetically attractive! I fail to understand why bombarding people by regular siege is more honourable. The fear of appearances is the first symptom of impotence. I’ve never, never recognised this more clearly than now, and I am further than ever from seeing that what I did was a crime. I’ve never, never been stronger and more convinced than now.”\n\nThe colour had rushed into his pale exhausted face, but as he uttered his last explanation, he happened to meet Dounia’s eyes and he saw such anguish in them that he could not help being checked. He felt that he had, anyway, made these two poor women miserable, that he was, anyway, the cause...\n\n“Dounia darling, if I am guilty forgive me (though I cannot be forgiven if I am guilty). Good-bye! We won’t dispute. It’s time, high time to go. Don’t follow me, I beseech you, I have somewhere else to go.... But you go at once and sit with mother. I entreat you to! It’s my last request of you. Don’t leave her at all; I left her in a state of anxiety, that she is not fit to bear; she will die or go out of her mind. Be with her! Razumihin will be with you. I’ve been talking to him.... Don’t cry about me: I’ll try to be honest and manly all my life, even if I am a murderer. Perhaps I shall some day make a name. I won’t disgrace you, you will see; I’ll still show.... Now good-bye for the present,” he concluded hurriedly, noticing again a strange expression in Dounia’s eyes at his last words and promises. “Why are you crying? Don’t cry, don’t cry: we are not parting for ever! Ah, yes! Wait a minute, I’d forgotten!”\n\nHe went to the table, took up a thick dusty book, opened it and took from between the pages a little water-colour portrait on ivory. It was the portrait of his landlady’s daughter, who had died of fever, that strange girl who had wanted to be a nun. For a minute he gazed at the delicate expressive face of his betrothed, kissed the portrait and gave it to Dounia.\n\n“I used to talk a great deal about it to her, only to her,” he said thoughtfully. “To her heart I confided much of what has since been so hideously realised. Don’t be uneasy,” he returned to Dounia, “she was as much opposed to it as you, and I am glad that she is gone. The great point is that everything now is going to be different, is going to be broken in two,” he cried, suddenly returning to his dejection. “Everything, everything, and am I prepared for it? Do I want it myself? They say it is necessary for me to suffer! What’s the object of these senseless sufferings? shall I know any better what they are for, when I am crushed by hardships and idiocy, and weak as an old man after twenty years’ penal servitude? And what shall I have to live for then? Why am I consenting to that life now? Oh, I knew I was contemptible when I stood looking at the Neva at daybreak to-day!”\n\nAt last they both went out. It was hard for Dounia, but she loved him. She walked away, but after going fifty paces she turned round to look at him again. He was still in sight. At the corner he too turned and for the last time their eyes met; but noticing that she was looking at him, he motioned her away with impatience and even vexation, and turned the corner abruptly.\n\n“I am wicked, I see that,” he thought to himself, feeling ashamed a moment later of his angry gesture to Dounia. “But why are they so fond of me if I don’t deserve it? Oh, if only I were alone and no one loved me and I too had never loved anyone! _Nothing of all this would have happened._ But I wonder shall I in those fifteen or twenty years grow so meek that I shall humble myself before people and whimper at every word that I am a criminal? Yes, that’s it, that’s it, that’s what they are sending me there for, that’s what they want. Look at them running to and fro about the streets, every one of them a scoundrel and a criminal at heart and, worse still, an idiot. But try to get me off and they’d be wild with righteous indignation. Oh, how I hate them all!”\n\nHe fell to musing by what process it could come to pass, that he could be humbled before all of them, indiscriminately--humbled by conviction. And yet why not? It must be so. Would not twenty years of continual bondage crush him utterly? Water wears out a stone. And why, why should he live after that? Why should he go now when he knew that it would be so? It was the hundredth time perhaps that he had asked himself that question since the previous evening, but still he went.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\nWhen he went into Sonia’s room, it was already getting dark. All day Sonia had been waiting for him in terrible anxiety. Dounia had been waiting with her. She had come to her that morning, remembering Svidrigaïlov’s words that Sonia knew. We will not describe the conversation and tears of the two girls, and how friendly they became. Dounia gained one comfort at least from that interview, that her brother would not be alone. He had gone to her, Sonia, first with his confession; he had gone to her for human fellowship when he needed it; she would go with him wherever fate might send him. Dounia did not ask, but she knew it was so. She looked at Sonia almost with reverence and at first almost embarrassed her by it. Sonia was almost on the point of tears. She felt herself, on the contrary, hardly worthy to look at Dounia. Dounia’s gracious image when she had bowed to her so attentively and respectfully at their first meeting in Raskolnikov’s room had remained in her mind as one of the fairest visions of her life.\n\nDounia at last became impatient and, leaving Sonia, went to her brother’s room to await him there; she kept thinking that he would come there first. When she had gone, Sonia began to be tortured by the dread of his committing suicide, and Dounia too feared it. But they had spent the day trying to persuade each other that that could not be, and both were less anxious while they were together. As soon as they parted, each thought of nothing else. Sonia remembered how Svidrigaïlov had said to her the day before that Raskolnikov had two alternatives--Siberia or... Besides she knew his vanity, his pride and his lack of faith.\n\n“Is it possible that he has nothing but cowardice and fear of death to make him live?” she thought at last in despair.\n\nMeanwhile the sun was setting. Sonia was standing in dejection, looking intently out of the window, but from it she could see nothing but the unwhitewashed blank wall of the next house. At last when she began to feel sure of his death--he walked into the room.\n\nShe gave a cry of joy, but looking carefully into his face she turned pale.\n\n“Yes,” said Raskolnikov, smiling. “I have come for your cross, Sonia. It was you told me to go to the cross-roads; why is it you are frightened now it’s come to that?”\n\nSonia gazed at him astonished. His tone seemed strange to her; a cold shiver ran over her, but in a moment she guessed that the tone and the words were a mask. He spoke to her looking away, as though to avoid meeting her eyes.\n\n“You see, Sonia, I’ve decided that it will be better so. There is one fact.... But it’s a long story and there’s no need to discuss it. But do you know what angers me? It annoys me that all those stupid brutish faces will be gaping at me directly, pestering me with their stupid questions, which I shall have to answer--they’ll point their fingers at me.... Tfoo! You know I am not going to Porfiry, I am sick of him. I’d rather go to my friend, the Explosive Lieutenant; how I shall surprise him, what a sensation I shall make! But I must be cooler; I’ve become too irritable of late. You know I was nearly shaking my fist at my sister just now, because she turned to take a last look at me. It’s a brutal state to be in! Ah! what am I coming to! Well, where are the crosses?”\n\nHe seemed hardly to know what he was doing. He could not stay still or concentrate his attention on anything; his ideas seemed to gallop after one another, he talked incoherently, his hands trembled slightly.\n\nWithout a word Sonia took out of the drawer two crosses, one of cypress wood and one of copper. She made the sign of the cross over herself and over him, and put the wooden cross on his neck.\n\n“It’s the symbol of my taking up the cross,” he laughed. “As though I had not suffered much till now! The wooden cross, that is the peasant one; the copper one, that is Lizaveta’s--you will wear yourself, show me! So she had it on... at that moment? I remember two things like these too, a silver one and a little ikon. I threw them back on the old woman’s neck. Those would be appropriate now, really, those are what I ought to put on now.... But I am talking nonsense and forgetting what matters; I’m somehow forgetful.... You see I have come to warn you, Sonia, so that you might know... that’s all--that’s all I came for. But I thought I had more to say. You wanted me to go yourself. Well, now I am going to prison and you’ll have your wish. Well, what are you crying for? You too? Don’t. Leave off! Oh, how I hate it all!”\n\nBut his feeling was stirred; his heart ached, as he looked at her. “Why is she grieving too?” he thought to himself. “What am I to her? Why does she weep? Why is she looking after me, like my mother or Dounia? She’ll be my nurse.”\n\n“Cross yourself, say at least one prayer,” Sonia begged in a timid broken voice.\n\n“Oh certainly, as much as you like! And sincerely, Sonia, sincerely....”\nBut he wanted to say something quite different.\n\nHe crossed himself several times. Sonia took up her shawl and put it over her head. It was the green _drap de dames_ shawl of which Marmeladov had spoken, “the family shawl.” Raskolnikov thought of that looking at it, but he did not ask. He began to feel himself that he was certainly forgetting things and was disgustingly agitated. He was frightened at this. He was suddenly struck too by the thought that Sonia meant to go with him.\n\n“What are you doing? Where are you going? Stay here, stay! I’ll go alone,” he cried in cowardly vexation, and almost resentful, he moved towards the door. “What’s the use of going in procession?” he muttered going out.\n\nSonia remained standing in the middle of the room. He had not even said good-bye to her; he had forgotten her. A poignant and rebellious doubt surged in his heart.\n\n“Was it right, was it right, all this?” he thought again as he went down the stairs. “Couldn’t he stop and retract it all... and not go?”\n\nBut still he went. He felt suddenly once for all that he mustn’t ask himself questions. As he turned into the street he remembered that he had not said good-bye to Sonia, that he had left her in the middle of the room in her green shawl, not daring to stir after he had shouted at her, and he stopped short for a moment. At the same instant, another thought dawned upon him, as though it had been lying in wait to strike him then.\n\n“Why, with what object did I go to her just now? I told her--on business; on what business? I had no sort of business! To tell her I was _going_; but where was the need? Do I love her? No, no, I drove her away just now like a dog. Did I want her crosses? Oh, how low I’ve sunk! No, I wanted her tears, I wanted to see her terror, to see how her heart ached! I had to have something to cling to, something to delay me, some friendly face to see! And I dared to believe in myself, to dream of what I would do! I am a beggarly contemptible wretch, contemptible!”\n\nHe walked along the canal bank, and he had not much further to go. But on reaching the bridge he stopped and turning out of his way along it went to the Hay Market.\n\nHe looked eagerly to right and left, gazed intently at every object and could not fix his attention on anything; everything slipped away. “In another week, another month I shall be driven in a prison van over this bridge, how shall I look at the canal then? I should like to remember this!” slipped into his mind. “Look at this sign! How shall I read those letters then? It’s written here ‘Campany,’ that’s a thing to remember, that letter _a_, and to look at it again in a month--how shall I look at it then? What shall I be feeling and thinking then?... How trivial it all must be, what I am fretting about now! Of course it must all be interesting... in its way... (Ha-ha-ha! What am I thinking about?) I am becoming a baby, I am showing off to myself; why am I ashamed? Foo! how people shove! that fat man--a German he must be--who pushed against me, does he know whom he pushed? There’s a peasant woman with a baby, begging. It’s curious that she thinks me happier than she is. I might give her something, for the incongruity of it. Here’s a five copeck piece left in my pocket, where did I get it? Here, here... take it, my good woman!”\n\n“God bless you,” the beggar chanted in a lachrymose voice.\n\nHe went into the Hay Market. It was distasteful, very distasteful to be in a crowd, but he walked just where he saw most people. He would have given anything in the world to be alone; but he knew himself that he would not have remained alone for a moment. There was a man drunk and disorderly in the crowd; he kept trying to dance and falling down. There was a ring round him. Raskolnikov squeezed his way through the crowd, stared for some minutes at the drunken man and suddenly gave a short jerky laugh. A minute later he had forgotten him and did not see him, though he still stared. He moved away at last, not remembering where he was; but when he got into the middle of the square an emotion suddenly came over him, overwhelming him body and mind.\n\nHe suddenly recalled Sonia’s words, “Go to the cross-roads, bow down to the people, kiss the earth, for you have sinned against it too, and say aloud to the whole world, ‘I am a murderer.’” He trembled, remembering that. And the hopeless misery and anxiety of all that time, especially of the last hours, had weighed so heavily upon him that he positively clutched at the chance of this new unmixed, complete sensation. It came over him like a fit; it was like a single spark kindled in his soul and spreading fire through him. Everything in him softened at once and the tears started into his eyes. He fell to the earth on the spot....\n\nHe knelt down in the middle of the square, bowed down to the earth, and kissed that filthy earth with bliss and rapture. He got up and bowed down a second time.\n\n“He’s boozed,” a youth near him observed.\n\nThere was a roar of laughter.\n\n“He’s going to Jerusalem, brothers, and saying good-bye to his children and his country. He’s bowing down to all the world and kissing the great city of St. Petersburg and its pavement,” added a workman who was a little drunk.\n\n“Quite a young man, too!” observed a third.\n\n“And a gentleman,” someone observed soberly.\n\n“There’s no knowing who’s a gentleman and who isn’t nowadays.”\n\nThese exclamations and remarks checked Raskolnikov, and the words, “I am a murderer,” which were perhaps on the point of dropping from his lips, died away. He bore these remarks quietly, however, and, without looking round, he turned down a street leading to the police office. He had a glimpse of something on the way which did not surprise him; he had felt that it must be so. The second time he bowed down in the Hay Market he saw, standing fifty paces from him on the left, Sonia. She was hiding from him behind one of the wooden shanties in the market-place. She had followed him then on his painful way! Raskolnikov at that moment felt and knew once for all that Sonia was with him for ever and would follow him to the ends of the earth, wherever fate might take him. It wrung his heart... but he was just reaching the fatal place.\n\nHe went into the yard fairly resolutely. He had to mount to the third storey. “I shall be some time going up,” he thought. He felt as though the fateful moment was still far off, as though he had plenty of time left for consideration.\n\nAgain the same rubbish, the same eggshells lying about on the spiral stairs, again the open doors of the flats, again the same kitchens and the same fumes and stench coming from them. Raskolnikov had not been here since that day. His legs were numb and gave way under him, but still they moved forward. He stopped for a moment to take breath, to collect himself, so as to enter _like a man_. “But why? what for?” he wondered, reflecting. “If I must drink the cup what difference does it make? The more revolting the better.” He imagined for an instant the figure of the “explosive lieutenant,” Ilya Petrovitch. Was he actually going to him? Couldn’t he go to someone else? To Nikodim Fomitch? Couldn’t he turn back and go straight to Nikodim Fomitch’s lodgings? At least then it would be done privately.... No, no! To the “explosive lieutenant”! If he must drink it, drink it off at once.\n\nTurning cold and hardly conscious, he opened the door of the office. There were very few people in it this time--only a house porter and a peasant. The doorkeeper did not even peep out from behind his screen. Raskolnikov walked into the next room. “Perhaps I still need not speak,” passed through his mind. Some sort of clerk not wearing a uniform was settling himself at a bureau to write. In a corner another clerk was seating himself. Zametov was not there, nor, of course, Nikodim Fomitch.\n\n“No one in?” Raskolnikov asked, addressing the person at the bureau.\n\n“Whom do you want?”\n\n“A-ah! Not a sound was heard, not a sight was seen, but I scent the Russian... how does it go on in the fairy tale... I’ve forgotten! ‘At your service!’” a familiar voice cried suddenly.\n\nRaskolnikov shuddered. The Explosive Lieutenant stood before him. He had just come in from the third room. “It is the hand of fate,” thought Raskolnikov. “Why is he here?”\n\n“You’ve come to see us? What about?” cried Ilya Petrovitch. He was obviously in an exceedingly good humour and perhaps a trifle exhilarated. “If it’s on business you are rather early.[*] It’s only a chance that I am here... however I’ll do what I can. I must admit, I... what is it, what is it? Excuse me....”\n\n[*] Dostoevsky appears to have forgotten that it is after sunset, and that the last time Raskolnikov visited the police office at two in the afternoon he was reproached for coming too late.--TRANSLATOR.\n\n“Raskolnikov.”\n\n“Of course, Raskolnikov. You didn’t imagine I’d forgotten? Don’t think I am like that... Rodion Ro--Ro--Rodionovitch, that’s it, isn’t it?”\n\n“Rodion Romanovitch.”\n\n“Yes, yes, of course, Rodion Romanovitch! I was just getting at it. I made many inquiries about you. I assure you I’ve been genuinely grieved since that... since I behaved like that... it was explained to me afterwards that you were a literary man... and a learned one too... and so to say the first steps... Mercy on us! What literary or scientific man does not begin by some originality of conduct! My wife and I have the greatest respect for literature, in my wife it’s a genuine passion! Literature and art! If only a man is a gentleman, all the rest can be gained by talents, learning, good sense, genius. As for a hat--well, what does a hat matter? I can buy a hat as easily as I can a bun; but what’s under the hat, what the hat covers, I can’t buy that! I was even meaning to come and apologise to you, but thought maybe you’d... But I am forgetting to ask you, is there anything you want really? I hear your family have come?”\n\n“Yes, my mother and sister.”\n\n“I’ve even had the honour and happiness of meeting your sister--a highly cultivated and charming person. I confess I was sorry I got so hot with you. There it is! But as for my looking suspiciously at your fainting fit--that affair has been cleared up splendidly! Bigotry and fanaticism! I understand your indignation. Perhaps you are changing your lodging on account of your family’s arriving?”\n\n“No, I only looked in... I came to ask... I thought that I should find Zametov here.”\n\n“Oh, yes! Of course, you’ve made friends, I heard. Well, no, Zametov is not here. Yes, we’ve lost Zametov. He’s not been here since yesterday... he quarrelled with everyone on leaving... in the rudest way. He is a feather-headed youngster, that’s all; one might have expected something from him, but there, you know what they are, our brilliant young men. He wanted to go in for some examination, but it’s only to talk and boast about it, it will go no further than that. Of course it’s a very different matter with you or Mr. Razumihin there, your friend. Your career is an intellectual one and you won’t be deterred by failure. For you, one may say, all the attractions of life _nihil est_--you are an ascetic, a monk, a hermit!... A book, a pen behind your ear, a learned research--that’s where your spirit soars! I am the same way myself.... Have you read Livingstone’s Travels?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Oh, I have. There are a great many Nihilists about nowadays, you know, and indeed it is not to be wondered at. What sort of days are they? I ask you. But we thought... you are not a Nihilist of course? Answer me openly, openly!”\n\n“N-no...”\n“Believe me, you can speak openly to me as you would to yourself! Official duty is one thing but... you are thinking I meant to say _friendship_ is quite another? No, you’re wrong! It’s not friendship, but the feeling of a man and a citizen, the feeling of humanity and of love for the Almighty. I may be an official, but I am always bound to feel myself a man and a citizen.... You were asking about Zametov. Zametov will make a scandal in the French style in a house of bad reputation, over a glass of champagne... that’s all your Zametov is good for! While I’m perhaps, so to speak, burning with devotion and lofty feelings, and besides I have rank, consequence, a post! I am married and have children, I fulfil the duties of a man and a citizen, but who is he, may I ask? I appeal to you as a man ennobled by education... Then these midwives, too, have become extraordinarily numerous.”\n\nRaskolnikov raised his eyebrows inquiringly. The words of Ilya Petrovitch, who had obviously been dining, were for the most part a stream of empty sounds for him. But some of them he understood. He looked at him inquiringly, not knowing how it would end.\n\n“I mean those crop-headed wenches,” the talkative Ilya Petrovitch continued. “Midwives is my name for them. I think it a very satisfactory one, ha-ha! They go to the Academy, study anatomy. If I fall ill, am I to send for a young lady to treat me? What do you say? Ha-ha!” Ilya Petrovitch laughed, quite pleased with his own wit. “It’s an immoderate zeal for education, but once you’re educated, that’s enough. Why abuse it? Why insult honourable people, as that scoundrel Zametov does? Why did he insult me, I ask you? Look at these suicides, too, how common they are, you can’t fancy! People spend their last halfpenny and kill themselves, boys and girls and old people. Only this morning we heard about a gentleman who had just come to town. Nil Pavlitch, I say, what was the name of that gentleman who shot himself?”\n\n“Svidrigaïlov,” someone answered from the other room with drowsy listlessness.\n\nRaskolnikov started.\n\n“Svidrigaïlov! Svidrigaïlov has shot himself!” he cried.\n\n“What, do you know Svidrigaïlov?”\n\n“Yes... I knew him.... He hadn’t been here long.”\n\n“Yes, that’s so. He had lost his wife, was a man of reckless habits and all of a sudden shot himself, and in such a shocking way.... He left in his notebook a few words: that he dies in full possession of his faculties and that no one is to blame for his death. He had money, they say. How did you come to know him?”\n\n“I... was acquainted... my sister was governess in his family.”\n\n“Bah-bah-bah! Then no doubt you can tell us something about him. You had no suspicion?”\n\n“I saw him yesterday... he... was drinking wine; I knew nothing.”\nRaskolnikov felt as though something had fallen on him and was stifling him.\n\n“You’ve turned pale again. It’s so stuffy here...”\n\n“Yes, I must go,” muttered Raskolnikov. “Excuse my troubling you....”\n\n“Oh, not at all, as often as you like. It’s a pleasure to see you and I am glad to say so.”\n\nIlya Petrovitch held out his hand.\n\n“I only wanted... I came to see Zametov.”\n\n“I understand, I understand, and it’s a pleasure to see you.”\n\n“I... am very glad... good-bye,” Raskolnikov smiled.\n\nHe went out; he reeled, he was overtaken with giddiness and did not know what he was doing. He began going down the stairs, supporting himself with his right hand against the wall. He fancied that a porter pushed past him on his way upstairs to the police office, that a dog in the lower storey kept up a shrill barking and that a woman flung a rolling-pin at it and shouted. He went down and out into the yard. There, not far from the entrance, stood Sonia, pale and horror-stricken. She looked wildly at him. He stood still before her. There was a look of poignant agony, of despair, in her face. She clasped her hands. His lips worked in an ugly, meaningless smile. He stood still a minute, grinned and went back to the police office.\n\nIlya Petrovitch had sat down and was rummaging among some papers. Before him stood the same peasant who had pushed by on the stairs.\n\n“Hulloa! Back again! have you left something behind? What’s the matter?”\n\nRaskolnikov, with white lips and staring eyes, came slowly nearer. He walked right to the table, leaned his hand on it, tried to say something, but could not; only incoherent sounds were audible.\n\n“You are feeling ill, a chair! Here, sit down! Some water!”\n\nRaskolnikov dropped on to a chair, but he kept his eyes fixed on the face of Ilya Petrovitch, which expressed unpleasant surprise. Both looked at one another for a minute and waited. Water was brought.\n\n“It was I...” began Raskolnikov.\n\n“Drink some water.”\n\nRaskolnikov refused the water with his hand, and softly and brokenly, but distinctly said:\n\n“_It was I killed the old pawnbroker woman and her sister Lizaveta with an axe and robbed them._”\n\nIlya Petrovitch opened his mouth. People ran up on all sides.\nRaskolnikov repeated his statement.\n\n# I\n\nSiberia. On the banks of a broad solitary river stands a town, one of the administrative centres of Russia; in the town there is a fortress, in the fortress there is a prison. In the prison the second-class convict Rodion Raskolnikov has been confined for nine months. Almost a year and a half has passed since his crime.\n\nThere had been little difficulty about his trial. The criminal adhered exactly, firmly, and clearly to his statement. He did not confuse nor misrepresent the facts, nor soften them in his own interest, nor omit the smallest detail. He explained every incident of the murder, the secret of _the pledge_ (the piece of wood with a strip of metal) which was found in the murdered woman’s hand. He described minutely how he had taken her keys, what they were like, as well as the chest and its contents; he explained the mystery of Lizaveta’s murder; described how Koch and, after him, the student knocked, and repeated all they had said to one another; how he afterwards had run downstairs and heard Nikolay and Dmitri shouting; how he had hidden in the empty flat and afterwards gone home. He ended by indicating the stone in the yard off the Voznesensky Prospect under which the purse and the trinkets were found. The whole thing, in fact, was perfectly clear. The lawyers and the judges were very much struck, among other things, by the fact that he had hidden the trinkets and the purse under a stone, without making use of them, and that, what was more, he did not now remember what the trinkets were like, or even how many there were. The fact that he had never opened the purse and did not even know how much was in it seemed incredible. There turned out to be in the purse three hundred and seventeen roubles and sixty copecks. From being so long under the stone, some of the most valuable notes lying uppermost had suffered from the damp. They were a long while trying to discover why the accused man should tell a lie about this, when about everything else he had made a truthful and straightforward confession. Finally some of the lawyers more versed in psychology admitted that it was possible he had really not looked into the purse, and so didn’t know what was in it when he hid it under the stone. But they immediately drew the deduction that the crime could only have been committed through temporary mental derangement, through homicidal mania, without object or the pursuit of gain. This fell in with the most recent fashionable theory of temporary insanity, so often applied in our days in criminal cases. Moreover Raskolnikov’s hypochondriacal condition was proved by many witnesses, by Dr. Zossimov, his former fellow students, his landlady and her servant. All this pointed strongly to the conclusion that Raskolnikov was not quite like an ordinary murderer and robber, but that there was another element in the case.\n\nTo the intense annoyance of those who maintained this opinion, the criminal scarcely attempted to defend himself. To the decisive question as to what motive impelled him to the murder and the robbery, he answered very clearly with the coarsest frankness that the cause was his miserable position, his poverty and helplessness, and his desire to provide for his first steps in life by the help of the three thousand roubles he had reckoned on finding. He had been led to the murder through his shallow and cowardly nature, exasperated moreover by privation and failure. To the question what led him to confess, he answered that it was his heartfelt repentance. All this was almost coarse....\n\nThe sentence however was more merciful than could have been expected, perhaps partly because the criminal had not tried to justify himself, but had rather shown a desire to exaggerate his guilt. All the strange and peculiar circumstances of the crime were taken into consideration. There could be no doubt of the abnormal and poverty-stricken condition of the criminal at the time. The fact that he had made no use of what he had stolen was put down partly to the effect of remorse, partly to his abnormal mental condition at the time of the crime. Incidentally the murder of Lizaveta served indeed to confirm the last hypothesis: a man commits two murders and forgets that the door is open! Finally, the confession, at the very moment when the case was hopelessly muddled by the false evidence given by Nikolay through melancholy and fanaticism, and when, moreover, there were no proofs against the real criminal, no suspicions even (Porfiry Petrovitch fully kept his word)--all this did much to soften the sentence. Other circumstances, too, in the prisoner’s favour came out quite unexpectedly. Razumihin somehow discovered and proved that while Raskolnikov was at the university he had helped a poor consumptive fellow student and had spent his last penny on supporting him for six months, and when this student died, leaving a decrepit old father whom he had maintained almost from his thirteenth year, Raskolnikov had got the old man into a hospital and paid for his funeral when he died. Raskolnikov’s landlady bore witness, too, that when they had lived in another house at Five Corners, Raskolnikov had rescued two little children from a house on fire and was burnt in doing so. This was investigated and fairly well confirmed by many witnesses. These facts made an impression in his favour.\n\nAnd in the end the criminal was, in consideration of extenuating circumstances, condemned to penal servitude in the second class for a term of eight years only.\n\nAt the very beginning of the trial Raskolnikov’s mother fell ill. Dounia and Razumihin found it possible to get her out of Petersburg during the trial. Razumihin chose a town on the railway not far from Petersburg, so as to be able to follow every step of the trial and at the same time to see Avdotya Romanovna as often as possible. Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s illness was a strange nervous one and was accompanied by a partial derangement of her intellect.\n\nWhen Dounia returned from her last interview with her brother, she had found her mother already ill, in feverish delirium. That evening Razumihin and she agreed what answers they must make to her mother’s questions about Raskolnikov and made up a complete story for her mother’s benefit of his having to go away to a distant part of Russia on a business commission, which would bring him in the end money and reputation.\n\nBut they were struck by the fact that Pulcheria Alexandrovna never asked them anything on the subject, neither then nor thereafter. On the contrary, she had her own version of her son’s sudden departure; she told them with tears how he had come to say good-bye to her, hinting that she alone knew many mysterious and important facts, and that Rodya had many very powerful enemies, so that it was necessary for him to be in hiding. As for his future career, she had no doubt that it would be brilliant when certain sinister influences could be removed. She assured Razumihin that her son would be one day a great statesman, that his article and brilliant literary talent proved it. This article she was continually reading, she even read it aloud, almost took it to bed with her, but scarcely asked where Rodya was, though the subject was obviously avoided by the others, which might have been enough to awaken her suspicions.\n\nThey began to be frightened at last at Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s strange silence on certain subjects. She did not, for instance, complain of getting no letters from him, though in previous years she had only lived on the hope of letters from her beloved Rodya. This was the cause of great uneasiness to Dounia; the idea occurred to her that her mother suspected that there was something terrible in her son’s fate and was afraid to ask, for fear of hearing something still more awful. In any case, Dounia saw clearly that her mother was not in full possession of her faculties.\n\nIt happened once or twice, however, that Pulcheria Alexandrovna gave such a turn to the conversation that it was impossible to answer her without mentioning where Rodya was, and on receiving unsatisfactory and suspicious answers she became at once gloomy and silent, and this mood lasted for a long time. Dounia saw at last that it was hard to deceive her and came to the conclusion that it was better to be absolutely silent on certain points; but it became more and more evident that the poor mother suspected something terrible. Dounia remembered her brother’s telling her that her mother had overheard her talking in her sleep on the night after her interview with Svidrigaïlov and before the fatal day of the confession: had not she made out something from that? Sometimes days and even weeks of gloomy silence and tears would be succeeded by a period of hysterical animation, and the invalid would begin to talk almost incessantly of her son, of her hopes of his future.... Her fancies were sometimes very strange. They humoured her, pretended to agree with her (she saw perhaps that they were pretending), but she still went on talking.\n\nFive months after Raskolnikov’s confession, he was sentenced. Razumihin and Sonia saw him in prison as often as it was possible. At last the moment of separation came. Dounia swore to her brother that the separation should not be for ever, Razumihin did the same. Razumihin, in his youthful ardour, had firmly resolved to lay the foundations at least of a secure livelihood during the next three or four years, and saving up a certain sum, to emigrate to Siberia, a country rich in every natural resource and in need of workers, active men and capital. There they would settle in the town where Rodya was and all together would begin a new life. They all wept at parting.\n\nRaskolnikov had been very dreamy for a few days before. He asked a great deal about his mother and was constantly anxious about her. He worried so much about her that it alarmed Dounia. When he heard about his mother’s illness he became very gloomy. With Sonia he was particularly reserved all the time. With the help of the money left to her by Svidrigaïlov, Sonia had long ago made her preparations to follow the party of convicts in which he was despatched to Siberia. Not a word passed between Raskolnikov and her on the subject, but both knew it would be so. At the final leave-taking he smiled strangely at his sister’s and Razumihin’s fervent anticipations of their happy future together when he should come out of prison. He predicted that their mother’s illness would soon have a fatal ending. Sonia and he at last set off.\n\nTwo months later Dounia was married to Razumihin. It was a quiet and sorrowful wedding; Porfiry Petrovitch and Zossimov were invited however. During all this period Razumihin wore an air of resolute determination. Dounia put implicit faith in his carrying out his plans and indeed she could not but believe in him. He displayed a rare strength of will. Among other things he began attending university lectures again in order to take his degree. They were continually making plans for the future; both counted on settling in Siberia within five years at least. Till then they rested their hopes on Sonia.\n\nPulcheria Alexandrovna was delighted to give her blessing to Dounia’s marriage with Razumihin; but after the marriage she became even more melancholy and anxious. To give her pleasure Razumihin told her how Raskolnikov had looked after the poor student and his decrepit father and how a year ago he had been burnt and injured in rescuing two little children from a fire. These two pieces of news excited Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s disordered imagination almost to ecstasy. She was continually talking about them, even entering into conversation with strangers in the street, though Dounia always accompanied her. In public conveyances and shops, wherever she could capture a listener, she would begin the discourse about her son, his article, how he had helped the student, how he had been burnt at the fire, and so on! Dounia did not know how to restrain her. Apart from the danger of her morbid excitement, there was the risk of someone’s recalling Raskolnikov’s name and speaking of the recent trial. Pulcheria Alexandrovna found out the address of the mother of the two children her son had saved and insisted on going to see her.\n\nAt last her restlessness reached an extreme point. She would sometimes begin to cry suddenly and was often ill and feverishly delirious. One morning she declared that by her reckoning Rodya ought soon to be home, that she remembered when he said good-bye to her he said that they must expect him back in nine months. She began to prepare for his coming, began to do up her room for him, to clean the furniture, to wash and put up new hangings and so on. Dounia was anxious, but said nothing and helped her to arrange the room. After a fatiguing day spent in continual fancies, in joyful day-dreams and tears, Pulcheria Alexandrovna was taken ill in the night and by morning she was feverish and delirious. It was brain fever. She died within a fortnight. In her delirium she dropped words which showed that she knew a great deal more about her son’s terrible fate than they had supposed.\n\nFor a long time Raskolnikov did not know of his mother’s death, though a regular correspondence had been maintained from the time he reached Siberia. It was carried on by means of Sonia, who wrote every month to the Razumihins and received an answer with unfailing regularity. At first they found Sonia’s letters dry and unsatisfactory, but later on they came to the conclusion that the letters could not be better, for from these letters they received a complete picture of their unfortunate brother’s life. Sonia’s letters were full of the most matter-of-fact detail, the simplest and clearest description of all Raskolnikov’s surroundings as a convict. There was no word of her own hopes, no conjecture as to the future, no description of her feelings. Instead of any attempt to interpret his state of mind and inner life, she gave the simple facts--that is, his own words, an exact account of his health, what he asked for at their interviews, what commission he gave her and so on. All these facts she gave with extraordinary minuteness. The picture of their unhappy brother stood out at last with great clearness and precision. There could be no mistake, because nothing was given but facts.\n\nBut Dounia and her husband could get little comfort out of the news, especially at first. Sonia wrote that he was constantly sullen and not ready to talk, that he scarcely seemed interested in the news she gave him from their letters, that he sometimes asked after his mother and that when, seeing that he had guessed the truth, she told him at last of her death, she was surprised to find that he did not seem greatly affected by it, not externally at any rate. She told them that, although he seemed so wrapped up in himself and, as it were, shut himself off from everyone--he took a very direct and simple view of his new life; that he understood his position, expected nothing better for the time, had no ill-founded hopes (as is so common in his position) and scarcely seemed surprised at anything in his surroundings, so unlike anything he had known before. She wrote that his health was satisfactory; he did his work without shirking or seeking to do more; he was almost indifferent about food, but except on Sundays and holidays the food was so bad that at last he had been glad to accept some money from her, Sonia, to have his own tea every day. He begged her not to trouble about anything else, declaring that all this fuss about him only annoyed him. Sonia wrote further that in prison he shared the same room with the rest, that she had not seen the inside of their barracks, but concluded that they were crowded, miserable and unhealthy; that he slept on a plank bed with a rug under him and was unwilling to make any other arrangement. But that he lived so poorly and roughly, not from any plan or design, but simply from inattention and indifference.\n\nSonia wrote simply that he had at first shown no interest in her visits, had almost been vexed with her indeed for coming, unwilling to talk and rude to her. But that in the end these visits had become a habit and almost a necessity for him, so that he was positively distressed when she was ill for some days and could not visit him. She used to see him on holidays at the prison gates or in the guard-room, to which he was brought for a few minutes to see her. On working days she would go to see him at work either at the workshops or at the brick kilns, or at the sheds on the banks of the Irtish.\n\nAbout herself, Sonia wrote that she had succeeded in making some acquaintances in the town, that she did sewing, and, as there was scarcely a dressmaker in the town, she was looked upon as an indispensable person in many houses. But she did not mention that the authorities were, through her, interested in Raskolnikov; that his task was lightened and so on.\n\nAt last the news came (Dounia had indeed noticed signs of alarm and uneasiness in the preceding letters) that he held aloof from everyone, that his fellow prisoners did not like him, that he kept silent for days at a time and was becoming very pale. In the last letter Sonia wrote that he had been taken very seriously ill and was in the convict ward of the hospital.\n\n# II\n\nHe was ill a long time. But it was not the horrors of prison life, not the hard labour, the bad food, the shaven head, or the patched clothes that crushed him. What did he care for all those trials and hardships! he was even glad of the hard work. Physically exhausted, he could at least reckon on a few hours of quiet sleep. And what was the food to him--the thin cabbage soup with beetles floating in it? In the past as a student he had often not had even that. His clothes were warm and suited to his manner of life. He did not even feel the fetters. Was he ashamed of his shaven head and parti-coloured coat? Before whom? Before Sonia? Sonia was afraid of him, how could he be ashamed before her? And yet he was ashamed even before Sonia, whom he tortured because of it with his contemptuous rough manner. But it was not his shaven head and his fetters he was ashamed of: his pride had been stung to the quick. It was wounded pride that made him ill. Oh, how happy he would have been if he could have blamed himself! He could have borne anything then, even shame and disgrace. But he judged himself severely, and his exasperated conscience found no particularly terrible fault in his past, except a simple _blunder_ which might happen to anyone. He was ashamed just because he, Raskolnikov, had so hopelessly, stupidly come to grief through some decree of blind fate, and must humble himself and submit to “the idiocy” of a sentence, if he were anyhow to be at peace.\n\nVague and objectless anxiety in the present, and in the future a continual sacrifice leading to nothing--that was all that lay before him. And what comfort was it to him that at the end of eight years he would only be thirty-two and able to begin a new life! What had he to live for? What had he to look forward to? Why should he strive? To live in order to exist? Why, he had been ready a thousand times before to give up existence for the sake of an idea, for a hope, even for a fancy. Mere existence had always been too little for him; he had always wanted more. Perhaps it was just because of the strength of his desires that he had thought himself a man to whom more was permissible than to others.\nAnd if only fate would have sent him repentance--burning repentance that would have torn his heart and robbed him of sleep, that repentance, the awful agony of which brings visions of hanging or drowning! Oh, he would have been glad of it! Tears and agonies would at least have been life. But he did not repent of his crime.\n\nAt least he might have found relief in raging at his stupidity, as he had raged at the grotesque blunders that had brought him to prison. But now in prison, _in freedom_, he thought over and criticised all his actions again and by no means found them so blundering and so grotesque as they had seemed at the fatal time.\n\n“In what way,” he asked himself, “was my theory stupider than others that have swarmed and clashed from the beginning of the world? One has only to look at the thing quite independently, broadly, and uninfluenced by commonplace ideas, and my idea will by no means seem so... strange. Oh, sceptics and halfpenny philosophers, why do you halt half-way!\n\n“Why does my action strike them as so horrible?” he said to himself. “Is it because it was a crime? What is meant by crime? My conscience is at rest. Of course, it was a legal crime, of course, the letter of the law was broken and blood was shed. Well, punish me for the letter of the law... and that’s enough. Of course, in that case many of the benefactors of mankind who snatched power for themselves instead of inheriting it ought to have been punished at their first steps. But those men succeeded and so _they were right_, and I didn’t, and so I had no right to have taken that step.”\n\nIt was only in that that he recognised his criminality, only in the fact that he had been unsuccessful and had confessed it.\n\nHe suffered too from the question: why had he not killed himself? Why had he stood looking at the river and preferred to confess? Was the desire to live so strong and was it so hard to overcome it? Had not Svidrigaïlov overcome it, although he was afraid of death?\n\nIn misery he asked himself this question, and could not understand that, at the very time he had been standing looking into the river, he had perhaps been dimly conscious of the fundamental falsity in himself and his convictions. He didn’t understand that that consciousness might be the promise of a future crisis, of a new view of life and of his future resurrection.\n\nHe preferred to attribute it to the dead weight of instinct which he could not step over, again through weakness and meanness. He looked at his fellow prisoners and was amazed to see how they all loved life and prized it. It seemed to him that they loved and valued life more in prison than in freedom. What terrible agonies and privations some of them, the tramps for instance, had endured! Could they care so much for a ray of sunshine, for the primeval forest, the cold spring hidden away in some unseen spot, which the tramp had marked three years before, and longed to see again, as he might to see his sweetheart, dreaming of the green grass round it and the bird singing in the bush? As he went on he saw still more inexplicable examples.\n\nIn prison, of course, there was a great deal he did not see and did not want to see; he lived as it were with downcast eyes. It was loathsome and unbearable for him to look. But in the end there was much that surprised him and he began, as it were involuntarily, to notice much that he had not suspected before. What surprised him most of all was the terrible impossible gulf that lay between him and all the rest. They seemed to be a different species, and he looked at them and they at him with distrust and hostility. He felt and knew the reasons of his isolation, but he would never have admitted till then that those reasons were so deep and strong. There were some Polish exiles, political prisoners, among them. They simply looked down upon all the rest as ignorant churls; but Raskolnikov could not look upon them like that. He saw that these ignorant men were in many respects far wiser than the Poles. There were some Russians who were just as contemptuous, a former officer and two seminarists. Raskolnikov saw their mistake as clearly. He was disliked and avoided by everyone; they even began to hate him at last--why, he could not tell. Men who had been far more guilty despised and laughed at his crime.\n\n“You’re a gentleman,” they used to say. “You shouldn’t hack about with an axe; that’s not a gentleman’s work.”\n\nThe second week in Lent, his turn came to take the sacrament with his gang. He went to church and prayed with the others. A quarrel broke out one day, he did not know how. All fell on him at once in a fury.\n\n“You’re an infidel! You don’t believe in God,” they shouted. “You ought to be killed.”\n\nHe had never talked to them about God nor his belief, but they wanted to kill him as an infidel. He said nothing. One of the prisoners rushed at him in a perfect frenzy. Raskolnikov awaited him calmly and silently; his eyebrows did not quiver, his face did not flinch. The guard succeeded in intervening between him and his assailant, or there would have been bloodshed.\n\nThere was another question he could not decide: why were they all so fond of Sonia? She did not try to win their favour; she rarely met them, sometimes only she came to see him at work for a moment. And yet everybody knew her, they knew that she had come out to follow _him_, knew how and where she lived. She never gave them money, did them no particular services. Only once at Christmas she sent them all presents of pies and rolls. But by degrees closer relations sprang up between them and Sonia. She would write and post letters for them to their relations. Relations of the prisoners who visited the town, at their instructions, left with Sonia presents and money for them. Their wives and sweethearts knew her and used to visit her. And when she visited Raskolnikov at work, or met a party of the prisoners on the road, they all took off their hats to her. “Little mother Sofya Semyonovna, you are our dear, good little mother,” coarse branded criminals said to that frail little creature. She would smile and bow to them and everyone was delighted when she smiled. They even admired her gait and turned round to watch her walking; they admired her too for being so little, and, in fact, did not know what to admire her most for. They even came to her for help in their illnesses.\n\nHe was in the hospital from the middle of Lent till after Easter. When he was better, he remembered the dreams he had had while he was feverish and delirious. He dreamt that the whole world was condemned to a terrible new strange plague that had come to Europe from the depths of Asia. All were to be destroyed except a very few chosen. Some new sorts of microbes were attacking the bodies of men, but these microbes were endowed with intelligence and will. Men attacked by them became at once mad and furious. But never had men considered themselves so intellectual and so completely in possession of the truth as these sufferers, never had they considered their decisions, their scientific conclusions, their moral convictions so infallible. Whole villages, whole towns and peoples went mad from the infection. All were excited and did not understand one another. Each thought that he alone had the truth and was wretched looking at the others, beat himself on the breast, wept, and wrung his hands. They did not know how to judge and could not agree what to consider evil and what good; they did not know whom to blame, whom to justify. Men killed each other in a sort of senseless spite. They gathered together in armies against one another, but even on the march the armies would begin attacking each other, the ranks would be broken and the soldiers would fall on each other, stabbing and cutting, biting and devouring each other. The alarm bell was ringing all day long in the towns; men rushed together, but why they were summoned and who was summoning them no one knew. The most ordinary trades were abandoned, because everyone proposed his own ideas, his own improvements, and they could not agree. The land too was abandoned. Men met in groups, agreed on something, swore to keep together, but at once began on something quite different from what they had proposed. They accused one another, fought and killed each other. There were conflagrations and famine. All men and all things were involved in destruction. The plague spread and moved further and further. Only a few men could be saved in the whole world. They were a pure chosen people, destined to found a new race and a new life, to renew and purify the earth, but no one had seen these men, no one had heard their words and their voices.\n\nRaskolnikov was worried that this senseless dream haunted his memory so miserably, the impression of this feverish delirium persisted so long. The second week after Easter had come. There were warm bright spring days; in the prison ward the grating windows under which the sentinel paced were opened. Sonia had only been able to visit him twice during his illness; each time she had to obtain permission, and it was difficult. But she often used to come to the hospital yard, especially in the evening, sometimes only to stand a minute and look up at the windows of the ward.\n\nOne evening, when he was almost well again, Raskolnikov fell asleep. On waking up he chanced to go to the window, and at once saw Sonia in the distance at the hospital gate. She seemed to be waiting for someone. Something stabbed him to the heart at that minute. He shuddered and moved away from the window. Next day Sonia did not come, nor the day after; he noticed that he was expecting her uneasily. At last he was discharged. On reaching the prison he learnt from the convicts that Sofya Semyonovna was lying ill at home and was unable to go out.\n\nHe was very uneasy and sent to inquire after her; he soon learnt that her illness was not dangerous. Hearing that he was anxious about her, Sonia sent him a pencilled note, telling him that she was much better, that she had a slight cold and that she would soon, very soon come and see him at his work. His heart throbbed painfully as he read it.\n\nAgain it was a warm bright day. Early in the morning, at six o’clock, he went off to work on the river bank, where they used to pound alabaster and where there was a kiln for baking it in a shed. There were only three of them sent. One of the convicts went with the guard to the fortress to fetch a tool; the other began getting the wood ready and laying it in the kiln. Raskolnikov came out of the shed on to the river bank, sat down on a heap of logs by the shed and began gazing at the wide deserted river. From the high bank a broad landscape opened before him, the sound of singing floated faintly audible from the other bank. In the vast steppe, bathed in sunshine, he could just see, like black specks, the nomads’ tents. There there was freedom, there other men were living, utterly unlike those here; there time itself seemed to stand still, as though the age of Abraham and his flocks had not passed. Raskolnikov sat gazing, his thoughts passed into day-dreams, into contemplation; he thought of nothing, but a vague restlessness excited and troubled him. Suddenly he found Sonia beside him; she had come up noiselessly and sat down at his side. It was still quite early; the morning chill was still keen. She wore her poor old burnous and the green shawl; her face still showed signs of illness, it was thinner and paler. She gave him a joyful smile of welcome, but held out her hand with her usual timidity. She was always timid of holding out her hand to him and sometimes did not offer it at all, as though afraid he would repel it. He always took her hand as though with repugnance, always seemed vexed to meet her and was sometimes obstinately silent throughout her visit. Sometimes she trembled before him and went away deeply grieved. But now their hands did not part. He stole a rapid glance at her and dropped his eyes on the ground without speaking. They were alone, no one had seen them. The guard had turned away for the time.\n\nHow it happened he did not know. But all at once something seemed to seize him and fling him at her feet. He wept and threw his arms round her knees. For the first instant she was terribly frightened and she turned pale. She jumped up and looked at him trembling. But at the same moment she understood, and a light of infinite happiness came into her eyes. She knew and had no doubt that he loved her beyond everything and that at last the moment had come....\n\nThey wanted to speak, but could not; tears stood in their eyes. They were both pale and thin; but those sick pale faces were bright with the dawn of a new future, of a full resurrection into a new life. They were renewed by love; the heart of each held infinite sources of life for the heart of the other.\n\nThey resolved to wait and be patient. They had another seven years to wait, and what terrible suffering and what infinite happiness before them! But he had risen again and he knew it and felt it in all his being, while she--she only lived in his life.\n\nOn the evening of the same day, when the barracks were locked, Raskolnikov lay on his plank bed and thought of her. He had even fancied that day that all the convicts who had been his enemies looked at him differently; he had even entered into talk with them and they answered him in a friendly way. He remembered that now, and thought it was bound to be so. Wasn’t everything now bound to be changed?\n\nHe thought of her. He remembered how continually he had tormented her and wounded her heart. He remembered her pale and thin little face. But these recollections scarcely troubled him now; he knew with what infinite love he would now repay all her sufferings. And what were all, _all_ the agonies of the past! Everything, even his crime, his sentence and imprisonment, seemed to him now in the first rush of feeling an external, strange fact with which he had no concern. But he could not think for long together of anything that evening, and he could not have analysed anything consciously; he was simply feeling. Life had stepped into the place of theory and something quite different would work itself out in his mind.\n\nUnder his pillow lay the New Testament. He took it up mechanically. The book belonged to Sonia; it was the one from which she had read the raising of Lazarus to him. At first he was afraid that she would worry him about religion, would talk about the gospel and pester him with books. But to his great surprise she had not once approached the subject and had not even offered him the Testament. He had asked her for it himself not long before his illness and she brought him the book without a word. Till now he had not opened it.\n\nHe did not open it now, but one thought passed through his mind: “Can her convictions not be mine now? Her feelings, her aspirations at least....”\n\nShe too had been greatly agitated that day, and at night she was taken ill again. But she was so happy--and so unexpectedly happy--that she was almost frightened of her happiness. Seven years, _only_ seven years! At the beginning of their happiness at some moments they were both ready to look on those seven years as though they were seven days. He did not know that the new life would not be given him for nothing, that he would have to pay dearly for it, that it would cost him great striving, great suffering.\n\nBut that is the beginning of a new story--the story of the gradual renewal of a man, the story of his gradual regeneration, of his passing from one world into another, of his initiation into a new unknown life. That might be the subject of a new story, but our present story is ended.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-2554"]="7월 초의 유난히 무더운 어느 저녁, 한 청년이 S 골목에 세 들어 살던 다락방에서 나와, 망설이듯 천천히 K 다리 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n그는 계단에서 집주인 여자와 마주치는 일을 용케 피했다. 그의 다락방은 오 층짜리 높은 건물의 지붕 바로 밑에 있었는데, 방이라기보다는 차라리 벽장에 가까웠다. 다락방과 식사, 잔심부름까지 챙겨 주는 집주인 여자는 아래층에 살았고, 그래서 그가 밖으로 나갈 때면 매번 그녀의 부엌을 지나가야만 했는데, 그 부엌문은 언제나 열려 있었다. 그리고 그 앞을 지날 때마다 청년은 메스껍고 두려운 기분에 휩싸여 얼굴을 찌푸리고 부끄러움을 느꼈다. 그는 집주인 여자에게 절망적일 만큼 빚을 지고 있었고, 그녀와 마주치는 것이 두려웠다.\n\n이는 그가 비겁하고 비굴해서가 아니었다. 오히려 정반대였다. 그러나 얼마 전부터 그는 우울증에 가까운, 과도하게 긴장되고 신경질적인 상태에 빠져 있었다. 자기 자신에게 너무도 완전히 몰두한 나머지 주변 사람들로부터 고립되어, 집주인 여자뿐 아니라 그 누구와 마주치는 것조차 끔찍해했다. 그는 가난에 짓눌려 있었지만, 최근에는 자기 처지에 대한 근심마저 그를 더는 짓누르지 못했다. 실질적으로 중요한 일들에는 신경 쓰기를 그만두었고, 그러고 싶은 마음조차 깡그리 사라져 버렸다. 집주인 여자가 무슨 짓을 한들 그에게는 진짜 두려움이 되지 못했다. 하지만 계단에서 붙잡혀, 그녀의 시시하고 부질없는 잔소리며 빚 독촉, 협박과 불평을 억지로 듣고 있어야 하고, 변명거리를 짜내느라 머리를 쥐어짜고, 얼버무리고, 거짓말을 해야 한다……, 아니, 그럴 바엔 차라리 고양이처럼 계단을 살금살금 내려가 아무도 모르게 빠져나가는 편이 나았다.\n\n그러나 이날 저녁, 거리로 나서면서 그는 자신의 두려움을 예리하게 의식하게 되었다.\n\n'나는 그런 일을 저지르려 하면서, 이런 하찮은 것들에 겁을 먹고 있군.' 그는 묘한 미소를 지으며 생각했다. '흠……, 그래, 모든 것은 인간의 손에 달려 있는데, 인간은 그 모든 걸 비겁함 때문에 놓쳐 버리지, 이건 공리야. 인간이 가장 두려워하는 게 뭔지 안다면 흥미로울 텐데. 새로운 발걸음을 내딛는 것, 새로운 말을 입에 올리는 것, 바로 그것을 가장 두려워하지……. 하지만 내가 말이 너무 많군. 내가 떠벌리기만 하니까 아무것도 하지 못하는 거야. 아니 어쩌면 아무것도 하지 않으니까 떠벌리는 건지도. 지난 한 달 동안 나는 떠벌리는 법을 배웠어, 며칠이고 내 굴속에 누워서…… 동화 같은 생각이나 하면서. 그런데 내가 지금 왜 거기로 가고 있는 거지? 내가 정말 그런 일을 할 수 있을까? 그게 진심이긴 한가? 전혀 진심이 아니야. 그저 나를 즐겁게 하려는 공상일 뿐이지, 장난감이야! 그래, 어쩌면 장난감인지도.'\n\n거리의 더위는 끔찍했다. 게다가 숨 막히는 공기와 북적임, 사방에 널린 회반죽과 비계며 벽돌, 먼지, 그리고 여름에 도시를 빠져나가지 못하는 모든 이들에게 너무도 익숙한 페테르부르크 특유의 악취 — 이 모든 것이 이미 과도하게 곤두선 청년의 신경을 고통스럽게 자극했다. 이 동네에 유난히 많은 선술집에서 풍겨 나오는 견딜 수 없는 악취, 그리고 평일인데도 끊임없이 마주치는 술 취한 사내들이 이 혐오스러운 풍경을 더없이 비참하게 완성시켰다. 청년의 섬세한 얼굴에 한순간 깊디깊은 혐오의 표정이 스쳤다. 그건 그렇고, 그는 유난히 잘생긴 청년으로, 평균보다 키가 크고 호리호리하면서도 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 아름다운 검은 눈과 짙은 갈색 머리칼을 지니고 있었다. 곧 그는 깊은 생각에, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 완전한 정신의 공허 속에 빠져들었다. 그는 주변의 무엇도 보지 않고, 보려고도 하지 않은 채 걸어갔다. 가끔씩 그는 무언가를 중얼거렸는데, 방금 스스로 고백했던 그 혼잣말하는 버릇 탓이었다. 그런 순간이면 그는 자신의 생각이 때때로 뒤엉켜 있고 자신이 몹시 쇠약해져 있음을 자각하곤 했다. 이틀 동안 그는 거의 아무것도 먹지 못했던 것이다.\n\n그는 옷차림이 너무도 형편없어서, 누더기에 익숙한 사람이라 해도 그런 차림으로 거리에 나서기는 부끄러워했을 정도였다. 하지만 이 동네에서는 옷차림이 아무리 변변찮아도 놀랄 일이 거의 없었다. 건초 시장이 가까운 데다, 평판 나쁜 업소가 즐비하고, 페테르부르크 한복판의 이 거리와 골목에 장사치며 노동자 계층이 빽빽이 몰려든 탓에, 거리에는 워낙 별의별 인간들이 다 보여서 아무리 괴상한 행색이라도 놀라움을 자아내지 못했다. 그러나 청년의 마음속에는 쌓이고 쌓인 쓰라림과 경멸이 가득해서, 젊은이 특유의 까다로움에도 불구하고 거리에서만큼은 자신의 누더기를 가장 개의치 않았다. 아는 사람이나 옛 동창들과 마주칠 때는 사정이 달랐는데, 사실 그는 언제든 그들과 마주치는 것을 싫어했다. 그런데 어쩐 영문인지 거대한 짐마차에 실려 어디론가 끌려가던 술 취한 사내 하나가 — 무거운 짐말이 끄는 마차였다 — 그의 곁을 지나치며 갑자기 목청껏 소리를 지르고 그를 손가락질하며 \"야, 거기, 독일 모자쟁이야!\" 하고 악을 써 댔을 때, 청년은 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고 부들부들 떨며 자기 모자를 움켜쥐었다. 그것은 치머만 가게에서 산 키 높은 둥근 모자였는데, 이제는 완전히 닳아 빠지고 세월에 누렇게 절고, 온통 찢기고 얼룩진 데다, 챙도 없이 한쪽으로 보기 흉하게 꺾여 있었다. 그러나 그를 사로잡은 것은 부끄러움이 아니라, 차라리 공포에 가까운 전혀 다른 감정이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n'그럴 줄 알았어.' 그는 당황한 채 중얼거렸다. '그럴 거라고 생각했다고! 이게 무엇보다 최악이야! 아니, 이런 멍청한 것, 이렇게 사소한 것 하나가 계획 전체를 망쳐 버릴 수도 있어. 그래, 내 모자가 너무 눈에 띄어……. 우스꽝스러우니까 눈에 띄는 거지……. 이런 누더기 차림이면 차라리 챙 달린 모자를, 아무 낡아빠진 납작모자라도 써야지, 이런 괴상한 물건이 아니라. 아무도 이런 모자를 쓰지 않으니, 일 마일 밖에서도 눈에 띄고 기억에 남을 거야……. 중요한 건 사람들이 이걸 기억할 거라는 점이고, 그게 그들에게 단서가 되겠지. 이런 일에서는 가능한 한 눈에 띄지 않아야 하는데……. 사소한 것들, 사소한 것들이 중요하단 말이야! 아니, 바로 그런 사소한 것들이 늘 모든 걸 망쳐 놓지…….'\n\n갈 길은 멀지 않았다. 사실 그는 자기 하숙집 대문에서 거기까지가 몇 걸음인지도 알고 있었다. 정확히 칠백서른 걸음이었다. 언젠가 공상에 푹 빠져 있을 때 한번 세어 본 적이 있었다. 그때만 해도 그는 그 공상을 믿지 않았고, 그 끔찍하면서도 대담한 무모함으로 그저 스스로를 안달하게 했을 뿐이었다. 그런데 한 달이 지난 지금, 그는 그것을 다르게 바라보기 시작했고, 자신의 무력함과 우유부단함을 비웃는 독백을 늘어놓으면서도, 어느새 자신도 모르게 이 '끔찍한' 공상을 한번 시도해 볼 만한 위업으로 여기게 되었던 것이다. 비록 그 자신은 아직 이를 깨닫지 못하고 있었지만. 그는 지금 분명 자기 계획의 '예행연습'을 하러 가는 길이었고, 한 걸음 한 걸음 옮길 때마다 그의 흥분은 점점 더 격렬해졌다.\n\n가슴이 철렁 내려앉고 신경이 떨려 오는 가운데, 그는 한쪽으로는 운하를, 다른 한쪽으로는 거리를 향해 있는 어느 거대한 건물로 다가갔다. 이 건물은 작디작은 셋방들로 쪼개져 세 놓여 있었고, 온갖 부류의 노동자들이 살고 있었다 — 재단사, 자물쇠공, 요리사, 갖가지 독일인들, 닥치는 대로 입에 풀칠하는 처녀들, 말단 관리들 등등. 두 개의 출입문과 두 개의 안뜰로는 사람들이 끊임없이 드나들었다. 건물에는 서너 명의 문지기가 고용되어 있었다. 청년은 그들 중 누구와도 마주치지 않은 것이 무척 다행스러웠고, 곧장 눈에 띄지 않게 오른쪽 문으로 빠져나가 계단을 올랐다. 그것은 어둡고 좁은 뒷계단이었지만, 그는 이미 그곳에 익숙해 길을 잘 알았고, 이 모든 환경이 마음에 들었다. 이런 어둠 속에서라면 아무리 캐묻기 좋아하는 눈길이라도 두려워할 필요가 없었으니까.\n\n'지금도 이렇게 겁을 먹는데, 만일 어쩌다 내가 정말로 그 일을 하러 가게 된다면 어떨까?' 사 층에 다다르며 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 거기서 그의 발걸음은, 어느 셋집에서 가구를 들어내고 있던 인부 몇 사람에게 가로막혔다. 그는 그 셋집에 관청에 다니는 독일인 관리와 그 가족이 살고 있었다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러니까 그 독일인이 이사를 나가고 있었던 것이고, 따라서 이 계단의 사 층은 그 노파를 빼고는 텅 비게 될 터였다. '어쨌든 잘된 일이야.' 그는 노파의 셋집 초인종을 울리며 속으로 생각했다. 초인종은 구리가 아니라 양철로 만든 것인 양 희미하게 딸랑거렸다. 이런 건물의 작은 셋집들은 늘 그렇게 울리는 초인종을 달고 있었다. 그는 그 초인종 소리를 잊고 있었는데, 이제 그 독특한 딸랑임이 무언가를 일깨우며 또렷이 눈앞에 떠올려 주는 것 같았다……. 그는 흠칫했다. 이제 그의 신경은 끔찍하게 곤두서 있었다. 잠시 후, 문이 아주 조금 빠끔히 열렸다. 노파는 그 틈으로 방문객을 노골적인 불신의 눈초리로 살펴보았는데, 어둠 속에서 반짝이는 그녀의 작은 두 눈 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 층계참에 여러 사람이 있는 것을 보자 그녀는 좀 더 대담해져서 문을 활짝 열었다. 청년은 작디작은 부엌과 칸막이로 나뉜 어두운 현관으로 발을 들여놓았다. 노파는 말없이 그와 마주 서서 캐묻듯 그를 바라보았다. 그녀는 예순쯤 된, 자그마하고 바싹 야윈 노파로, 날카롭고 표독스러운 눈과 뾰족한 작은 코를 지니고 있었다. 윤기 없이 희끗희끗 센 머리칼은 기름을 잔뜩 발라 떡이 졌고, 그 위에 두건 따위는 쓰지 않았다. 닭다리처럼 가늘고 긴 목에는 플란넬 누더기 같은 것이 묶여 있었고, 더위에도 불구하고 어깨에는 세월에 누렇게 바랜 다 해진 모피 망토가 펄럭이며 늘어져 있었다. 노파는 시도 때도 없이 기침을 하고 끙끙거렸다. 청년이 어지간히 묘한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았던 모양인지, 그녀의 눈에는 또다시 불신의 빛이 어렸다.\n\n\"라스콜니코프입니다, 대학생이고요, 한 달 전에 여기 왔었지요.\" 청년은 좀 더 공손해야 한다는 것을 떠올리고는 반쯤 고개를 숙이며 서둘러 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"기억하고말고요, 선생, 잘 기억하고 있어요, 댁이 여기 왔던 거.\" 노파는 또박또박 말하면서도 여전히 캐묻는 눈길을 그의 얼굴에서 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"그래서…… 또 같은 일로 왔습니다.\" 라스콜니코프는 노파의 불신에 다소 당황하고 놀라며 말을 이었다. '하지만 늘 저런 식인지도 모르지, 지난번에는 미처 알아차리지 못했을 뿐이고.' 그는 께름칙한 기분으로 생각했다.\n\n노파는 망설이는 듯 잠시 가만히 있다가, 한쪽으로 비켜서더니 방문을 가리키며 방문객을 앞세우고 말했다.\n\n\"들어오시구려, 선생.\"\n\n청년이 들어선 작은 방은, 벽에 노란 벽지가 발려 있고 창가에는 제라늄 화분과 모슬린 커튼이 드리워져 있었는데, 그 순간 지는 해에 환하게 물들어 있었다.\n\n'그렇다면 그때도 해가 이렇게 비치겠군!' 어쩐지 우연인 듯 라스콜니코프의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔고, 그는 재빠른 눈길로 방 안의 모든 것을 훑으며, 가능한 한 그 배치를 눈여겨보고 기억해 두려 애썼다. 하지만 방 안에 특별한 것은 없었다. 가구는 모두 아주 낡고 노란 목재로 된 것들로, 거대하게 휘어진 나무 등받이가 달린 소파, 소파 앞의 타원형 탁자, 창과 창 사이에 거울이 붙은 화장대, 벽을 따라 늘어선 의자들, 그리고 노란 액자에 끼운 두서너 점의 싸구려 판화 — 손에 새를 든 독일 처녀들을 그린 — 가 전부였다. 한쪽 구석에는 작은 성상 앞에 등불이 켜져 있었다. 모든 것이 무척 깨끗했다. 바닥도 가구도 반들반들 닦여, 모든 것이 반짝였다.\n\n'리자베타가 한 일이군.' 청년은 생각했다. 셋집 전체에 먼지 한 점 보이지 않았다.\n\n'이렇게 깔끔한 건 심술궂은 늙은 과부들 집에서나 볼 수 있는 거지.' 라스콜니코프는 다시 생각하며, 다른 작은 방으로 통하는 문에 드리운 무명 커튼을 호기심 어린 눈길로 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다. 그 방에는 노파의 침대와 서랍장이 놓여 있었는데, 그는 지금껏 한 번도 그 안을 들여다본 적이 없었다. 이 두 개의 방이 셋집 전부였다.\n\n\"뭘 원하시나?\" 노파는 방으로 들어와 아까처럼 그의 정면에 서서 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"여기에 저당 잡힐 물건을 가져왔습니다.\" 그러고는 주머니에서 구식의 납작한 은시계를 꺼냈는데, 뒷면에는 지구본이 새겨져 있었고, 줄은 강철이었다.\n\n\"하지만 지난번 저당물 기한이 다 됐는데. 그저께가 한 달째였어.\"\n\n\"한 달 치 이자를 더 가져다드리겠습니다, 조금만 기다려 주세요.\"\n\n\"기다리든 댁의 저당물을 당장 팔아 버리든, 그건 내 맘대로 할 일이지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"이 시계는 얼마나 쳐주시겠습니까, 알료나 이바노브나?\"\n\n\"이런 하찮은 걸 가져오니, 선생, 거의 값어치가 없구려. 지난번엔 반지에 이 루블이나 쳐줬지만, 그건 보석상에서 한 루블 반이면 새것으로 살 수 있는 거였어요.\"\n\n\"이걸로 사 루블만 주십시오, 다시 찾으러 오겠습니다, 아버지 유품이거든요. 곧 돈이 좀 들어올 겁니다.\"\n\n\"한 루블 반에, 이자는 선불이오, 그게 싫으면 그만두시고!\"\n\n\"한 루블 반이라니요!\" 청년이 외쳤다.\n\n\"맘대로 하시구려.\" 그러고는 노파가 시계를 그에게 도로 건넸다. 청년은 시계를 받아 들었는데, 어찌나 화가 치미는지 그대로 나가 버릴 뻔했다. 그러나 곧 마음을 다잡았으니, 달리 갈 데도 없거니와, 여기 온 데에는 또 다른 목적이 있었음을 떠올렸기 때문이다.\n\n\"이리 주십시오.\" 그가 퉁명스레 말했다.\n\n노파는 주머니를 뒤적여 열쇠를 찾더니, 커튼 뒤 다른 방으로 사라졌다. 방 한가운데 홀로 남겨진 청년은 생각에 잠긴 채 귀를 기울이며 호기심 어린 표정으로 들었다. 그는 그녀가 서랍장을 여는 소리를 들을 수 있었다.\n\n'분명 맨 위 서랍이겠지.' 그는 곰곰이 생각했다. '그러니까 열쇠를 오른쪽 주머니에 넣고 다니는군. 강철 고리에 한꺼번에 꿰어서……. 그런데 거기 다른 열쇠들보다 세 배는 큰 게 하나 있어, 홈이 깊게 팬……, 그건 서랍장 열쇠일 리가 없는데……, 그렇다면 다른 궤짝이나 금고가 있다는 거지……, 알아 둘 만한 일이야. 금고에는 늘 그런 열쇠가 달려 있으니까……. 하지만 이 모든 게 얼마나 치사한 짓인지.'\n\n노파가 돌아왔다.\n\n\"여기요, 선생. 우리야 한 달에 한 루블당 십 코페이카로 치니까, 한 루블 반에서 한 달 선불로 십오 코페이카를 떼야겠구려. 한데 전에 빌려준 이 루블에 대해서도, 같은 셈으로 선불 이십 코페이카를 빚진 셈이오. 그러니 다 합쳐 삼십오 코페이카요. 그래서 시계 값으로는 한 루블 십오 코페이카를 줘야겠소. 자, 여기 있어요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 이제 와서 고작 한 루블 십오 코페이카라니!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\"\n\n청년은 따지지 않고 돈을 받았다. 그는 노파를 바라보았고, 떠날 생각도 않은 채 머뭇거렸으니, 마치 아직 무언가 하고 싶거나 말하고 싶은 것이 남은 듯했으나, 정작 그 자신도 그게 무엇인지 잘 알지 못했다.\n\n\"하루 이틀 안에 다른 물건을 또 가져올지도 모릅니다, 알료나 이바노브나 — 값나가는 물건이지요 — 은으로 된 — 담뱃갑인데, 친구한테 돌려받는 대로…….\" 그는 당황하여 말끝을 흐렸다.\n\n\"뭐, 그땐 그때 얘기하지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요 — 늘 혼자 집에 계시나요, 동생분은 같이 안 계시고요?\" 그는 통로로 나가면서 가능한 한 아무렇지 않은 듯 물었다.\n\n\"그애가 댁이랑 무슨 상관이 있다고 그러시나, 선생?\"\n\n\"아, 별것 아닙니다, 그냥 물어본 거예요. 너무 성급하시군요……. 안녕히 계세요, 알료나 이바노브나.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 완전히 당황한 채 밖으로 나왔다. 이 당혹감은 점점 더 격렬해졌다. 계단을 내려오면서 그는 두세 번이나 우뚝 멈춰 서기까지 했는데, 마치 어떤 생각에 불현듯 부딪힌 것 같았다. 거리에 나섰을 때 그는 외쳤다. \"오, 하느님, 이 모든 게 어찌나 역겨운지! 그리고 내가, 정말 내가 그럴 수 있을까……. 아니, 말도 안 돼, 헛소리야!\" 그는 단호하게 덧붙였다. \"도대체 어떻게 이런 끔찍한 일이 내 머릿속에 떠올랐단 말인가? 내 마음이 이토록 추잡한 짓을 저지를 수 있다니. 그래, 무엇보다 추잡하고, 역겹고, 혐오스럽고, 또 혐오스러워! — 그런데 꼬박 한 달이나 나는…….\" 그러나 그 어떤 말로도, 그 어떤 외침으로도 그의 동요를 다 표현할 수는 없었다. 노파에게 가는 길에 이미 그의 마음을 짓누르고 괴롭히기 시작했던 그 강렬한 혐오의 감정은, 이제 너무도 극에 달하고 너무도 뚜렷한 형태를 갖춘 나머지, 그는 이 비참함에서 벗어나려고 어찌해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그는 행인들도 아랑곳없이, 그들과 부딪치면서, 술 취한 사람처럼 보도를 걸었고, 옆 거리에 이르러서야 비로소 정신을 차렸다. 둘러보니 그는 어느 선술집 바로 곁에 서 있었는데, 그곳은 보도에서 지하로 내려가는 계단을 통해 들어가게 되어 있었다. 바로 그 순간 술 취한 사내 둘이 문에서 나와, 서로 욕설을 퍼붓고 부축하며 계단을 올라왔다. 라스콜니코프는 생각할 겨를도 없이 곧장 계단을 내려갔다. 그 순간까지 그는 한 번도 선술집에 들어가 본 적이 없었지만, 지금은 어지럼증이 일고 타는 듯한 갈증에 시달리고 있었다. 그는 차가운 맥주가 간절했고, 자신의 갑작스러운 쇠약을 음식을 먹지 못한 탓으로 돌렸다. 그는 어둡고 더러운 구석의 끈적끈적한 작은 탁자에 앉아, 맥주를 시키고는 첫 잔을 게걸스레 들이켰다. 곧 한결 편안해졌고, 생각도 또렷해졌다.\n\n\"이 모든 게 다 헛소리야.\" 그는 희망에 차서 말했다. \"걱정할 거라곤 아무것도 없어! 그저 신체의 이상일 뿐이라고. 맥주 한 잔, 마른 빵 한 조각이면 — 한순간에 머리가 단단해지고, 정신이 또렷해지고, 의지가 굳건해지는걸! 흥, 이 모든 게 얼마나 하찮은지!\"\n\n그러나 이런 경멸 어린 생각에도 불구하고, 그는 이제 마치 끔찍한 짐에서 갑자기 풀려난 듯 명랑한 표정이 되어, 방 안의 사람들을 다정한 눈길로 둘러보았다. 하지만 바로 그 순간에도 그는 이 한결 행복해진 기분 역시 정상이 아니라는 것을 어렴풋이 예감했다.\n\n그때 선술집에는 사람이 별로 없었다. 계단에서 마주친 술 취한 두 사내 말고도, 사내 다섯쯤과 콘서티나를 든 처녀 하나로 이루어진 무리가 같은 때에 나가 버렸다. 그들이 떠나자 방 안은 조용하고 꽤 텅 비었다. 아직 선술집에 남아 있는 이들은, 직공으로 보이는 사내 하나로, 맥주 단지를 앞에 두고 술에 취해 있었지만 그리 심하지는 않았고, 또 그 일행인, 회색 수염을 기르고 짧고 품이 넉넉한 외투를 걸친 거대하고 뚱뚱한 사내가 있었다. 그는 몹시 취해서 긴 의자에 쓰러져 잠들어 있었는데, 이따금 잠결인 듯 화들짝 놀라며 손가락을 딱딱 튕기고, 두 팔을 활짝 벌린 채 상체를 의자 위에서 들썩거리면서, 무슨 이런 시구 같은 것을 떠올리려 애쓰며 의미도 없는 후렴을 흥얼거리곤 했다.\n\n\"그는 일 년 동안 아내를 애틋이 사랑했네 그의 아내를 — 일 년 동안 그는 — 애틋이 사랑했네.\"\n\n또는 갑자기 다시 잠에서 깨어나서는,\n\n\"북적이는 거리를 걸어가다가 그는 옛정 든 그 여인을 만났다네.\"\n\n그러나 누구도 그의 흥겨움에 함께하지 않았다. 말없는 그의 일행은 이 모든 행태를 노골적인 적의와 불신의 눈으로 바라보고 있었다. 방 안에는 또 다른 사내 하나가 있었는데, 어쩐지 퇴직한 관청 서기처럼 보였다. 그는 한쪽에 떨어져 앉아 이따금 단지째 홀짝이며 좌중을 둘러보고 있었다. 그 역시 어떤 동요에 사로잡혀 있는 듯했다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n라스콜니코프는 사람들 틈에 끼는 일에 익숙하지 않았고, 앞서 말했듯이 모든 종류의 사교를 피해 왔는데, 특히 요즈음 들어 더욱 그러했다. 그런데 지금 그는 갑자기 다른 사람들과 함께 있고 싶다는 욕망을 느꼈다. 그의 내면에서 무언가 새로운 것이 일어나고 있는 듯했고, 그와 더불어 일종의 동석에 대한 갈증 같은 것이 느껴졌다. 꼬박 한 달 동안 응축된 비참함과 음울한 흥분에 너무도 지쳐 버린 나머지, 그는 단 한순간만이라도 어떤 다른 세계에서 — 그것이 어떤 세계든 — 쉬고 싶었다. 그래서 주변이 더럽기 그지없는데도 그는 지금 이 선술집에 머물게 된 것이 반가웠다.\n\n가게 주인은 다른 방에 있었지만, 자주 계단 몇 개를 내려와 큰 방으로 들어오곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 빨간 접단이 달린, 콜타르를 칠한 멋들어진 부츠가 늘 그의 몸뚱이보다 먼저 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 긴 외투에 끔찍하게 기름때가 낀 검은 새틴 조끼를 걸쳤는데, 넥타이는 매지 않았고, 그의 얼굴 전체가 쇠 자물쇠처럼 기름에 절어 번들거리는 듯했다. 계산대에는 열네 살쯤 된 소년이 서 있었고, 좀 더 어린 또 다른 소년이 손님이 원하는 것이면 무엇이든 가져다주었다. 계산대 위에는 얇게 썬 오이 몇 조각, 말린 흑빵 몇 쪽, 그리고 잘게 다진 생선이 놓여 있었는데, 모두 지독한 악취를 풍겼다. 공기는 견딜 수 없이 답답했고, 독한 술 냄새가 어찌나 짙게 배어 있는지 이런 공기 속에서는 오 분만 있어도 능히 사람을 취하게 만들 만했다.\n\n낯선 사람과의 우연한 만남 가운데는, 한마디 말이 오가기도 전에 첫 순간부터 우리의 흥미를 끄는 것들이 있다. 라스콜니코프에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 앉아 있던, 퇴직한 관리처럼 보이는 사람이 바로 그런 인상을 주었다. 청년은 나중에도 이 인상을 자주 떠올렸고, 심지어 그것을 일종의 예감 탓으로 돌리기까지 했다. 그는 그 관리를 거듭 쳐다보았는데, 의심할 바 없이 그 관리 쪽에서도 그를 집요하게 빤히 바라보면서 분명 대화를 트고 싶어 안달하는 듯했기 때문이기도 했다. 술집 주인을 포함해 방 안의 다른 사람들을 대하는 그 관리의 태도는, 마치 그들과 어울리는 데 익숙하면서도 이제는 진력이 난 듯했고, 자기보다 신분과 교양이 낮아 함께 이야기를 나눠 봐야 소용없다고 여기는 사람들에 대한 약간의 너그러운 경멸 같은 것을 내비치고 있었다. 그는 쉰이 넘은 사내로, 머리가 벗어졌고 희끗희끗했으며, 중간 키에 다부진 몸집이었다. 끊임없는 음주로 부어오른 그의 얼굴은 누렇다 못해 푸르스름한 빛마저 띠었고, 부은 눈꺼풀 사이로 작은 틈처럼 가느다란 두 눈이 날카로운 불그스름한 빛을 발했다. 그러나 그에게는 무언가 무척 기이한 데가 있었다. 그의 두 눈에는 격렬한 감정에서 비롯된 듯한 빛이 어려 있었으며 — 어쩌면 사색과 지성마저 깃들어 있는 듯했으나 — 동시에 광기 비슷한 것이 번뜩였다. 그는 낡고 손쓸 수 없을 만큼 너덜너덜한 검은 연미복을 걸치고 있었는데, 단추는 단 하나만 남고 모조리 떨어져 나갔으며, 그는 그 단 하나의 단추를 채우고 있었으니, 분명 이 마지막 체면의 흔적에 매달리고 있는 셈이었다. 무명 조끼 밖으로는 얼룩투성이의 구겨진 셔츠 앞섶이 비어져 나와 있었다. 관리답게 그는 턱수염도 콧수염도 기르지 않았으나, 너무 오래 면도를 하지 않은 탓에 그의 턱은 뻣뻣한 잿빛 솔처럼 보였다. 그리고 그의 태도에도 무언가 점잖고 관리다운 데가 있었다. 그러나 그는 안절부절못했다. 머리칼을 헝클어뜨리는가 하면, 이따금 너덜너덜한 팔꿈치를 얼룩지고 끈적이는 탁자에 괴고는 절망한 듯 두 손에 머리를 파묻기도 했다. 마침내 그는 라스콜니코프를 똑바로 바라보더니, 큰 소리로 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"실례지만 선생, 외람되게도 정중한 담소를 좀 청해도 되겠습니까? 비록 선생의 행색은 존경을 자아낼 만한 것은 못 되나, 제 경험으로 미루어 선생이 교양 있는 분이며 음주에는 익숙하지 않은 분임을 알겠기 때문입니다. 저는 진실한 감정과 어우러진 교양이라면 늘 존경해 왔거니와, 게다가 직급으로 말하자면 9등 문관입니다. 마르멜라도프 — 제 이름이 그렇습니다, 9등 문관이지요. 외람되나 여쭙겠는데 — 선생은 관청에 몸담은 적이 있으신지요?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 저는 공부하는 학생입니다.\" 청년은 그 거창한 말투에, 또 자기에게 그토록 대놓고 말을 거는 것에 다소 놀라며 대답했다. 방금 전까지만 해도 어떤 동석이든 마다하지 않겠다는 순간적인 욕망을 느꼈건만, 막상 누군가가 실제로 말을 걸어오자 그는 자기에게 다가오거나 다가오려는 낯선 이라면 누구에게나 품게 마련인, 늘 그렇듯 신경질적이고 거북한 혐오감을 곧바로 느꼈다.\n\n\"그렇다면 학생이거나, 아니면 예전에 학생이었군요!\" 관리가 외쳤다. \"내 그럴 줄 알았지요! 나는 경험이 많은 사람이올시다, 어마어마한 경험을요, 선생.\" 그러고는 자화자찬하듯 손가락으로 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드렸다. \"선생은 학생이었거나, 아니면 무슨 학문 기관에 다닌 적이 있으시군요!…… 한데 잠깐만 실례를…….\" 그는 일어나서 비틀거리며 자기 술병과 잔을 집어 들고는 청년 곁으로 와, 비스듬히 마주 보는 자세로 앉았다. 그는 취해 있었지만 말은 막힘없이 대담하게 이어 갔고, 다만 이따금 문장의 갈피를 잃거나 말을 길게 끄는 정도였다. 그는 마치 자기 역시 한 달 동안 그 누구와도 말 한마디 나누지 못했다는 듯, 게걸스레 라스콜니코프에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"존경하는 선생,\" 그는 거의 엄숙하다 할 만큼 입을 열었다. \"가난은 죄가 아니다, 이건 참된 격언이지요. 한데 술주정 또한 미덕이 아니라는 것도 나는 잘 알고, 그건 더더욱 참되지요. 하지만 거지 신세는, 존경하는 선생, 거지 신세는 죄악이올시다. 가난 속에서도 사람은 타고난 영혼의 고귀함을 그래도 지킬 수 있지만, 거지 신세에서는 — 결코 — 그 누구도 못 합니다. 거지 신세가 되면 사람은 몽둥이로 인간 사회에서 쫓겨나는 게 아니라, 빗자루로 쓸려 나가지요, 그래야 최대한 모욕적이게 되니까요. 그리고 그게 또 지당하기도 한 것이, 거지 신세가 되면 나야말로 누구보다 먼저 스스로를 모욕할 각오가 되어 있거든요. 그래서 이 선술집이올시다! 존경하는 선생, 한 달 전에 레베지아트니코프 씨가 내 아내를 두들겨 팼는데, 내 아내는 나하고는 영 딴판인 사람이란 말입니다! 아시겠습니까? 단순한 호기심에서 한 가지 더 여쭙겠는데, 선생은 네바 강의 건초 거룻배에서 하룻밤을 지새워 본 적이 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 그런 적은 없습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다. \"그게 무슨 말씀이신지요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 나는 방금 거기서 오는 길이고, 그렇게 잔 게 벌써 닷새째올시다…….\" 그는 잔을 채워 단숨에 비우고는 잠시 입을 다물었다. 실제로 그의 옷에는 건초 부스러기가 들러붙어 있었고, 머리칼에도 엉겨 있었다. 지난 닷새 동안 옷을 벗거나 몸을 씻은 적이 없는 게 분명해 보였다. 특히 그의 두 손은 더러웠다. 살이 찌고 벌건 손에 손톱은 새카맸다.\n\n그의 이야기는 전반적으로 미적지근하나마 사람들의 관심을 자아내는 듯했다. 계산대의 소년들이 킥킥거리기 시작했다. 술집 주인은 이 \"익살꾼\"의 말을 들으려는 게 분명한 양 위층에서 내려와, 조금 떨어진 곳에 앉아서는 게으르게, 그러나 점잖게 하품을 했다. 마르멜라도프는 이곳에서 낯익은 인물인 게 분명했고, 그의 이 거창한 연설벽도 십중팔구 선술집에서 온갖 부류의 낯선 이들과 자주 대화를 트는 버릇에서 비롯된 것이리라. 이런 버릇은 어떤 주정뱅이들에게는, 특히 집에서 매섭게 감시당하며 단속받는 자들에게는 하나의 필요로 자라난다. 그래서 다른 술꾼들과 어울릴 때면 그들은 자기 자신을 변호하려 애쓰고, 가능하다면 인정마저 얻어 내려 드는 것이다.\n\n\"익살꾼 같으니!\" 술집 주인이 한마디 했다. \"한데 자넨 왜 일을 안 하나, 관청에 몸담았다면서 왜 근무처에 안 가고 이러나?\"\n\n\"내가 왜 근무처에 안 가느냐고요, 존경하는 선생,\" 마르멜라도프는 마치 그 질문을 던진 게 라스콜니코프이기라도 한 양, 오로지 그에게만 대고 말을 이어 갔다. \"내가 왜 근무처에 안 가느냐? 내가 얼마나 쓸모없는 벌레인지를 생각하면 내 가슴이 미어지지 않겠습니까? 한 달 전에 레베지아트니코프 씨가 제 손으로 내 아내를 두들겨 팰 때, 내가 곤드레만드레 취해 누워 있을 때, 내가 괴로워하지 않았겠습니까? 실례지만 젊은이, 자네는 혹시 이런 적이 있었나…… 흠…… 그러니까, 가망 없이 돈을 좀 꿔 달라고 애걸해 본 적이?\"\n\n\"네, 있습니다. 한데 가망 없이라는 게 무슨 뜻이신지요?\"\n\n\"가망 없이라 함은 가장 온전한 의미에서, 그래 봐야 아무것도 못 얻으리라는 걸 미리부터 아는 경우올시다. 가령 이 사람, 이 더없이 평판 좋고 모범적인 시민이 무슨 일이 있어도 자네에게 돈을 내주지 않으리라는 걸 미리부터 분명하게 확신하는 거지요. 하기야 내가 자네에게 묻겠는데, 그가 왜 주겠습니까? 내가 갚지 않으리란 걸 그도 물론 알고 있으니 말입니다. 동정심에서요? 하지만 요즘 사상에 밝은 레베지아트니코프 씨가 일전에 설명해 주기를, 동정심이란 건 요즘 과학 그 자체에서 금하는 바이며, 정치 경제학이 있는 영국에서는 이미 그렇게들 한다고 하더이다. 그러니 내가 자네에게 묻겠는데, 그가 무엇 때문에 내게 주겠습니까? 그런데도 그가 주지 않으리란 걸 미리 알면서도, 나는 그에게 가서…….\"\n\n\"왜 가시는 겁니까?\" 라스콜니코프가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 아무도 없고, 달리 갈 데가 한 군데도 없을 땐 말입니다! 사람이란 모름지기 어딘가 갈 데가 있어야 하는 법이니까요. 어쨌든 반드시 어딘가로 가야만 하는 때가 있는 법이거든요! 내 친딸이 처음으로 노란 딱지를 들고 나갔을 때, 그때 나도 가야만 했지요…… (내 딸은 노란 신분증을 갖고 있으니까요).\" 그가 청년을 다소 불안하게 바라보며 괄호를 치듯 덧붙였다. \"괜찮습니다, 선생, 괜찮아요!\" 계산대의 두 소년이 와락 폭소를 터뜨리고 술집 주인마저 빙긋 웃자, 그는 짐짓 태연한 척 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"괜찮아요, 저들이 고개를 절레절레 흔든다 해도 나는 당황하지 않습니다. 다들 이미 그 일에 대해 죄다 알고 있고, 비밀이란 비밀은 죄다 까발려졌으니까요. 나는 그 모든 걸 받아들입니다, 경멸로써가 아니라 겸허함으로써요. 될 대로 되라지! 될 대로 되라지! '이 사람을 보라!' 실례지만 젊은이, 자네는…… 아니, 좀 더 강하고 좀 더 또렷하게 말하지요. 자네가 할 수 있느냐가 아니라, 감히 나를 보면서 내가 돼지가 아니라고 단언할 수 있겠나?\"\n\n청년은 한마디도 대꾸하지 않았다.\n\n\"뭐,\" 연설가는 방 안의 웃음이 잦아들기를 기다렸다가, 무덤덤하게, 한층 더 늘어난 위엄을 갖추고 다시 입을 열었다. \"그래, 좋소, 나는 돼지요, 하지만 그녀는 귀부인이오! 나는 짐승의 형상을 하고 있으나, 내 아내 카테리나 이바노브나는 교양 있는 사람이며 장교의 딸이올시다. 좋소, 그래요, 나는 비열한이오, 하지만 그녀는 고결한 마음을 지닌 여인이며, 갖가지 감정으로 가득 차 있고, 교양으로 다듬어진 사람이지요. 그런데도…… 오, 그녀가 나를 가엾게만 여겨 준다면! 존경하는 선생, 존경하는 선생, 사람이란 적어도 누군가가 자기를 가엾게 여겨 주는 곳이 한 군데는 있어야 하는 법 아닙니까! 한데 카테리나 이바노브나는 도량은 넓은 사람이지만, 공정하지가 못해서요…… 게다가, 내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯는 것도 실은 가여워서 그러는 줄 알면서도 — 다시 말하지만 부끄러워하지 않고 말하건대, 그녀는 내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯거든요, 젊은이,\" 그는 다시 킥킥대는 소리를 듣고는 갑절로 위엄을 갖추며 단언했다. \"하지만, 맙소사, 그녀가 단 한 번만이라도…… 아니, 아니! 다 부질없는 짓이고, 말해 봐야 소용없어요! 말해 봐야 소용없다고요! 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 내 소원이 정말로 이루어져 그녀가 나를 가엾게 여긴 적도 있지만…… 이게 내 운명이고, 나는 천성이 짐승인 게지요!\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지!\" 술집 주인이 하품을 하며 맞장구쳤다. 마르멜라도프는 주먹으로 탁자를 단호히 내리쳤다.\n\n\"이게 내 운명이올시다! 아시겠습니까, 선생, 아시겠어요, 나는 술 마시려고 그녀의 양말까지 팔아 치웠습니다! 신발이 아니라 — 그거야 그래도 어느 정도 순리에 맞는 일이겠지만 — 양말을, 그녀의 양말을 술값으로 팔아 치웠다 이 말입니다! 그녀의 모헤어 숄도 술값으로 팔았지요, 오래전에 누가 선물한 것이고, 내 것이 아니라 그녀 소유였는데도 말입니다. 우리는 추운 방에서 사는데, 그녀는 이번 겨울에 감기가 들어 기침을 하기 시작하더니 이젠 피까지 토하기 시작했어요. 우리에겐 어린 자식이 셋 있는데, 카테리나 이바노브나는 아침부터 밤까지 일을 합니다. 닦고 청소하고 아이들을 씻기지요, 어릴 적부터 청결이 몸에 밴 사람이라서요. 한데 그녀는 가슴이 약하고 폐병에 걸리기 쉬운 체질이라, 나도 그걸 느낍니다! 내가 그걸 못 느낄 것 같습니까? 술을 마시면 마실수록 나는 그걸 더 절절히 느낍니다. 그래서 술을 마시는 것이기도 해요. 술 속에서 동정과 연민을 찾으려는 거지요…… 두 배로 더 괴로워하려고 마시는 것이올시다!\" 그러고는 절망한 듯 탁자에 머리를 떨궜다.\n\n\"젊은이,\" 그가 다시 고개를 들며 말을 이었다. \"자네 얼굴에서 무언가 마음의 괴로움이 읽히는 것 같소. 자네가 들어왔을 때 그걸 읽었고, 그래서 곧바로 자네에게 말을 건 것이올시다. 내가 자네에게 내 인생 이야기를 풀어놓는 것은, 사실 다 알고 있는 저 빈둥거리는 구경꾼들 앞에서 나 자신을 웃음거리로 만들려는 게 아니라, 감정과 교양을 갖춘 사람을 찾고 있기 때문이지요. 그러니 알아 두시오, 내 아내는 귀족 자제들을 위한 명문 학교에서 교육받았고, 졸업할 때는 지사를 비롯한 여러 고관 앞에서 숄 춤을 추어 금메달과 표창장을 받았소. 메달은…… 뭐, 메달이야 물론 팔아 치웠지요, 오래전에, 흠…… 하지만 표창장은 아직도 그녀의 트렁크에 들어 있어서, 얼마 전에도 우리 집주인 여자에게 그걸 보여 주더이다. 그녀는 집주인 여자와 노상 사이가 틀어져 있는데도, 누군가에게 자기의 지난 영예와 흘러가 버린 행복한 시절을 이야기하고 싶었던 거지요. 나는 그걸 두고 그녀를 탓하지 않습니다, 나무라지 않아요, 그녀에게 남은 유일한 것이 지난날의 추억뿐이고 나머지는 죄다 티끌과 잿더미니까요. 그래요, 그래, 그녀는 기개 있는 귀부인이고, 자존심 강하고 의지가 굳은 사람입니다. 손수 방바닥을 닦고 흑빵밖에 먹을 게 없어도, 자신이 무례한 대접을 받는 것은 결코 용납하지 않지요. 그래서 레베지아트니코프 씨의 무례를 그냥 넘기지 못한 거고, 그가 그 일로 그녀를 두들겨 팼을 때 그녀는 매를 맞아서라기보다 자존심에 입은 상처 때문에 자리에 드러눕고 말았던 겁니다. 내가 그녀와 결혼할 때 그녀는 아이 셋 딸린 과부였는데, 하나같이 줄줄이 어렸지요. 그녀는 첫 남편인 보병 장교와 사랑에 빠져 결혼했고, 그 사람과 함께 친정집에서 도망쳤소. 남편을 끔찍이도 사랑했지만, 그는 도박에 빠져 곤경에 처했고 그러다 죽고 말았지요. 끝에 가서는 그녀를 두들겨 패곤 했답니다. 그녀도 그에게 되갚아 주긴 했지만 — 나한테 그걸 입증할 진짜 문서 증거까지 있소만 — 그녀는 오늘날까지도 눈물을 흘리며 그를 이야기하고, 그를 들먹이며 나를 닦달하지요. 한데 나는 기쁩니다, 비록 상상 속에서나마 그녀가 자신이 한때 행복했었노라 여길 수 있다는 게 기뻐요…… 그렇게 그녀는 남편이 죽고 나서 마침 그때 내가 머물던 어느 황량하고 외진 고장에 아이 셋을 데리고 남겨졌는데, 어찌나 가망 없는 가난에 빠져 있었던지, 나도 온갖 부침을 숱하게 겪어 봤지만 차마 그걸 묘사할 엄두조차 나지 않을 지경이올시다. 친척들은 죄다 그녀를 내쳤지요. 게다가 그녀는 자존심도 셌고, 지나치다 싶을 만큼 자존심이 강했소…… 그래서, 존경하는 선생, 그래서, 마침 그때 첫 아내가 남긴 열네 살 난 딸을 거느린 홀아비였던 내가 그녀에게 청혼을 했지요, 그런 고통을 차마 두고 볼 수가 없었으니까요. 교양 있고 명문가 출신인 그녀가 나 같은 사람의 아내가 되는 데 동의했다는 것만 봐도, 그녀가 처한 재앙이 얼마나 극에 달했는지 짐작이 가실 거요. 한데 그녀는 동의했습니다! 울며불며 두 손을 부르쥐고 흐느끼면서, 그녀는 나와 결혼했어요! 달리 갈 데가 한 군데도 없었으니까요! 아시겠습니까, 선생, 정말이지 갈 데가 한 군데도 없다는 게 무슨 뜻인지 아시겠어요? 아니, 자네는 아직 그걸 모를 거요…… 그리고 꼬박 일 년 동안 나는 양심껏 충실히 직무를 다했고, 이것에는\" (그가 손가락으로 술병을 톡톡 두드렸다) \"손도 대지 않았지요, 나도 감정이란 게 있는 사람이니까요. 한데 그렇게 했는데도 그녀를 만족시킬 수가 없었고, 그러다 일자리마저 잃었는데, 그게 내 잘못이 아니라 관청의 인사 개편 때문이었습니다. 그러고 나서 나는 정말 이것에 손을 대고 말았지요!…… 우리가 숱한 방랑과 무수한 재난 끝에 마침내 이 무수한 기념비로 장식된 웅장한 수도에 정착한 지도 곧 일 년 반이 됩니다. 여기서 나는 자리를 하나 얻었지요…… 얻었다가 다시 잃었습니다. 아시겠어요? 이번에는 내 잘못으로 잃은 것이올시다, 내 약점이 도졌으니까요…… 지금 우리는 아말리야 표도로브나 리페베흐젤네 집에서 방 한 칸의 일부를 빌려 살고 있는데, 우리가 무얼로 먹고살고 무얼로 집세를 치르는지는 나도 차마 말 못 하겠소. 거기엔 우리 말고도 많은 사람들이 살고 있지요. 더럽고 무질서한, 그야말로 아수라장이올시다…… 흠…… 그래요…… 그러는 사이 첫 아내가 남긴 내 딸은 다 컸는데, 그 애가 자라는 동안 계모한테서 무슨 일을 겪었는지는 입에 올리지 않겠소. 카테리나 이바노브나는 너그러운 감정으로 가득 찬 사람이긴 해도, 기개 있는 귀부인인 데다 신경질적이고 성미가 급하니까요…… 그래요. 한데 그 얘긴 들춰 봐야 소용없지요! 짐작하시겠지만 소냐는 교육을 받지 못했습니다. 사 년 전에 내가 그 애에게 지리와 세계사를 좀 가르쳐 보려고 애를 써 봤지만, 나 자신이 그 과목들에 그리 밝지 못한 데다 마땅한 책도 없었고, 있는 책이라곤…… 흠, 어쨌든 지금은 그 책마저 없으니, 우리의 모든 교육은 거기서 끝나고 말았지요. 우리는 페르시아의 키루스에서 멈추고 말았소. 다 큰 뒤로 그 애는 연애물에 가까운 다른 책들을 읽었고, 요즘은 레베지아트니코프 씨를 통해 구한 루이스의 『생리학』이라는 책을 — 아시오? — 무척 흥미롭게 읽고는 그 발췌문을 우리에게 들려주기까지 했지요. 그게 그 애 교육의 전부올시다. 그러면 이제, 존경하는 선생, 외람되나 제 개인적인 일로 한 가지 사사로운 질문을 여쭤도 되겠습니까. 선생은 점잖은 가난한 처녀가 정직한 노동으로 많이 벌 수 있다고 보십니까? 정직하고 별다른 재주가 없다면, 한순간도 손을 놓지 않고 일해도 하루에 십오 파딩도 못 법니다! 게다가 더 기막힌 건, 클롭슈토크라는 5등 문관 — 이반 이바니치 클롭슈토크 말이오, 들어 보셨소? — 이 그 애가 만들어 준 아마포 셔츠 여섯 벌의 삯을 오늘날까지 안 주고 있을뿐더러, 셔츠 깃이 본과 다르게 비뚜로 박혔다는 핑계로 발을 동동 구르며 욕설을 퍼붓고 그 애를 거칠게 쫓아냈다는 거지요. 그런데 집에는 어린것들이 굶주리고 있고…… 카테리나 이바노브나는 두 손을 부르쥐고 방 안을 서성이는데, 그 병에 걸린 사람들이 늘 그렇듯 두 뺨이 벌겋게 상기되어서는, '여기서 우리하고 빌붙어 살면서,' 그러는 거요, '먹고 마시고 따뜻하게 지내면서, 거드는 게 아무것도 없잖니' 하고요. 한데 사흘째 어린것들 입에 빵 부스러기 하나 들어가지 못하는 판국에 무얼 그리 먹고 마시겠소! 나는 그때 누워 있었는데…… 뭐, 어쨌든! 나는 곤드레만드레 취해 누워서, 우리 소냐가 하는 말을 들었지요 (그 애는 부드러운 목소리를 가진 온순한 아이올시다…… 머리칼은 금빛이고, 얼굴은 그렇게 창백하고 야위었지요). 그 애가 그러더이다. '카테리나 이바노브나, 제가 정말 그런 짓을 해야 하나요?' 다리야 프란초브나라는, 행실이 나쁘고 경찰에도 잘 알려진 여자가 두세 번이나 집주인 여자를 통해 그 애에게 수작을 걸려 한 적이 있었거든요. '안 될 게 또 뭐냐?' 카테리나 이바노브나가 비웃듯이 그러더이다, '뭐 그리 대단한 보물이라고 그렇게 애지중지하니!' 하지만 그녀를 탓하지 마시오, 그녀를 탓하지 마세요, 존경하는 선생, 그녀를 탓하지 말아요! 그녀는 그 말을 할 때 제정신이 아니었습니다, 병과 굶주린 아이들의 울음소리에 미칠 지경으로 내몰린 거였지요. 그리고 그건 무슨 뜻이 있어서라기보다 그저 그 애 마음을 아프게 하려고 한 말이었소…… 그게 카테리나 이바노브나의 성미라서요, 아이들이 울면, 굶주려서 우는 거라도 당장 두들겨 패기 시작하지요. 여섯 시에 나는 소냐가 일어나 머릿수건을 두르고 망토를 걸치고 방을 나가는 걸 보았고, 아홉 시쯤 그 애가 돌아왔지요. 그 애는 곧장 카테리나 이바노브나에게로 걸어가더니, 말없이 그녀 앞 탁자에 삼십 루블을 내려놓았소. 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않았어요, 그저 우리의 그 커다란 초록빛 드라드담 숄을 (우리한테 드라드담으로 만든 숄이 하나 있거든요) 집어서 머리와 얼굴을 뒤덮고는, 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌린 채 침대에 누웠지요. 다만 그 가냘픈 어깨와 몸뚱이가 계속 떨리고 있을 뿐이었소…… 그리고 나는 아까처럼 그대로 누워 있었지요…… 그러고 나서 나는 보았소, 젊은이, 나는 카테리나 이바노브나가 마찬가지로 말없이 소냐의 작은 침대로 다가가는 걸 보았습니다. 그녀는 저녁 내내 무릎을 꿇고 소냐의 발에 입을 맞추며 도무지 일어나려 들지 않다가, 마침내 둘은 서로 부둥켜안은 채 잠이 들고 말았지요…… 함께, 함께요…… 그래요…… 그런데 나는…… 곤드레만드레 취해 누워 있었습니다.\"\n\n마르멜라도프는 목소리가 잠겨 버리기라도 한 듯 뚝 말을 멈췄다. 그러더니 황급히 잔을 채워 들이켜고는 목을 가다듬었다.\n\n\"그때 이후로, 선생,\" 그는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말을 이었다. \"그때 이후로, 어떤 불행한 사건과 악의를 품은 자들의 밀고 탓에 — 자신이 무례한 대접을 받았다는 핑계를 댄 다리야 프란초브나가 거기서 주도적인 역할을 했지요 — 그때 이후로 내 딸 소피야 세묘노브나는 노란 딱지를 받지 않을 수 없게 되었고, 그 탓에 우리와 함께 살 수도 없게 되고 말았소. 우리 집주인 여자인 아말리야 표도로브나가 그건 들으려고도 하지 않았으니까요 (전에는 다리야 프란초브나를 거들어 주더니 말이오), 레베지아트니코프 씨 역시…… 흠…… 그 사람과 카테리나 이바노브나 사이의 모든 분란은 소냐 때문에 일어난 거올시다. 처음엔 그 사람이 소냐에게 직접 수작을 걸더니, 그러다 갑자기 점잔을 빼면서 '나처럼 고등 교육을 받은 사람이 어떻게 저런 계집애와 한방에 살 수 있겠소?' 하고 그러더란 말이지요. 그러자 카테리나 이바노브나가 그냥 넘어가지 않고 그 애 편을 들었고…… 그래서 일이 그렇게 된 겁니다. 그래서 이제 소냐는, 주로 날이 어두워진 뒤에 우리한테 옵니다. 카테리나 이바노브나를 위로해 주고, 자기가 할 수 있는 건 다 갖다주지요…… 그 애는 재단사 카페르나우모프네 집에 방을 하나 얻어 그 집에 세 들어 살고 있습니다. 카페르나우모프는 절름발이에다 언청이인데, 그 많은 식구들도 죄다 언청이지요. 그 사람 아내 또한 언청이고요. 다들 한방에서 사는데, 소냐만은 칸막이로 막아 놓은 제 방을 따로 쓰고 있소…… 흠…… 그래요…… 무척 가난한 사람들이고 죄다 언청이지요…… 그래요. 그러고 나서 나는 아침에 일어나 누더기를 걸치고, 두 손을 하늘로 쳐들고는 이반 아파나시예비치 각하를 찾아갔습니다. 이반 아파나시예비치 각하를 아시오? 모른다고요? 그럼 선생은 하느님 같은 사람을 모르는 거외다. 그분은 밀랍이지요…… 주님 앞의 밀랍이올시다, 밀랍이 녹듯이 말이지요!…… 내 이야기를 듣고 그분의 두 눈이 흐려지더이다. '마르멜라도프, 자네는 이미 한 번 내 기대를 저버렸지…… 내 책임으로 한 번 더 자네를 받아 주겠네' — 그분이 그렇게 말씀하셨소, '명심하게,' 그렇게 말씀하시고는, '이제 가 보게' 하셨지요. 나는 그분 발치의 먼지에 입을 맞췄소 — 마음속으로만요, 실제로는 그분이 그렇게 하도록 두지 않으셨을 테니까요, 국정을 맡으신 분이자 근대적이고 계몽된 정치사상을 지닌 분이라서요. 나는 집으로 돌아왔고, 내가 다시 관청에 복직해서 봉급을 받게 되었다고 알리자, 맙소사, 얼마나 야단법석이 났던지!……\"\n\n마르멜라도프는 격한 흥분에 휩싸여 다시 말을 멈췄다. 바로 그때, 이미 거나하게 취한 한 무리의 술꾼들이 거리에서 우르르 들어왔고, 입구 쪽에서는 빌린 손풍금 소리와 「소촌(小村)」을 부르는 일곱 살배기 아이의 갈라진 새된 목소리가 들려왔다. 방 안은 소음으로 가득 찼다. 술집 주인과 소년들은 새로 온 손님들을 치르느라 분주했다. 마르멜라도프는 새로 온 이들에게는 아랑곳하지 않고 자기 이야기를 계속했다. 이제 그는 몹시 쇠약해진 듯 보였으나, 점점 더 취해 갈수록 점점 더 수다스러워졌다. 일자리를 얻은 최근의 성공을 떠올리자 그는 되살아나는 듯했고, 그것이 그의 얼굴에 일종의 광채가 되어 또렷이 비치기까지 했다. 라스콜니코프는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그게 다섯 주 전이올시다, 선생. 그래요…… 카테리나 이바노브나와 소냐가 그 소식을 듣자마자, 맙소사, 나는 마치 천국에 발을 들여놓은 것만 같았지요. 전에는 이랬소. 짐승처럼 누워 있어 봐, 욕설밖에 안 돌아왔지요. 그런데 이젠 둘이 발끝으로 살금살금 걸으며 아이들을 쉬쉬 달래더이다. '세묜 자하로비치가 관청 일로 피곤하셔서 쉬고 계셔, 쉿!' 그러는 거지요. 내가 일하러 나가기 전에 커피를 끓여 주고, 크림을 데워 주었습니다! 나를 위해 진짜 크림을 구해 오기 시작했단 말이오, 들립니까? 게다가 그 둘이 어떻게 변변한 옷차림 한 벌 값을 — 십일 루블 오십 코페이카를 — 마련했는지, 나는 도무지 짐작도 못 하겠소. 부츠며 면 셔츠 앞섶이며 — 더없이 훌륭한 것들로요 — 제복까지, 십일 루블 반으로 죄다 멋들어지게 갖춰 주었지요. 관청에서 처음 돌아온 날 아침에 보니 카테리나 이바노브나가 점심으로 두 가지 요리를 — 수프와 고추냉이를 곁들인 소금에 절인 고기를 — 만들어 놓았더이다, 그때까지는 꿈도 못 꾸던 거였지요. 그녀에겐 변변한 드레스가 한 벌도…… 단 한 벌도 없었지만, 마치 손님을 맞으러 가기라도 하는 양 차려입었더이다. 한데 차려입을 거리가 있어서 그런 게 아니라, 아무것도 없는 채로 멋을 낸 거였지요, 머리를 곱게 매만지고, 무슨 깨끗한 깃을 달고, 소맷동을 달고 하니, 거기 전혀 딴사람이 서 있더란 말입니다, 더 젊고 더 고와 보였지요. 내 어여쁜 소냐는 그저 돈만 좀 보탰을 뿐, '잠시 동안만요,' 그러더이다, '제가 아버지를 너무 자주 뵈러 와선 안 되니까요. 아마 날이 어두워진 뒤에, 아무도 못 볼 때라면 몰라도요' 하고요. 들립니까, 들려요? 나는 점심 후에 한숨 자려고 누웠는데, 글쎄 어찌 된 줄 아시오. 카테리나 이바노브나가 불과 일주일 전에 집주인 여자 아말리야 표도로브나와 더할 나위 없이 심하게 다퉜는데도, 그때만큼은 못 참고 그 여자를 커피에 초대하더란 말이오. 둘이 두 시간 동안 마주 앉아 소곤소곤 이야기를 나눴지요. '세묜 자하로비치가 이제 다시 관청에 나가게 됐고, 봉급도 받는답니다,' 그녀가 그러더이다, '그이가 직접 각하를 찾아갔는데, 각하께서 친히 그이에게 나오셔서, 다른 사람들은 죄다 기다리게 하시고는, 세묜 자하로비치의 손을 잡고 모두가 보는 앞에서 당신 집무실로 데리고 들어가셨다지 뭐예요' 하고요. 들립니까, 들려요? '암,' 각하께서 그러셨다지요, '세묜 자하로비치, 자네의 지난 공로를 기억하고,' 그러시고는, '자네가 그 어리석은 약점에 빠지는 버릇이 있긴 하지만, 이제 자네가 약속을 했고, 더구나 자네가 없으니 우리도 일이 영 안 풀렸던 터라,' (들립니까, 들려요) '그러니,' 각하께서 그러셨다지요, '이제 나는 신사로서의 자네 말을 믿겠네' 하고요. 한데 말이오, 그 모든 게 그녀가 그냥 혼자 지어낸 것이올시다, 그저 허영심에서, 떠벌리고 싶어서 그런 게 아니라요. 아니, 그녀는 그걸 전부 스스로 믿고 있어요, 제가 지어낸 공상으로 스스로 즐거워하는 거지요, 정말이지 그렇다니까요! 그리고 나는 그걸 두고 그녀를 탓하지 않습니다, 아니, 나는 그녀를 탓하지 않아요!…… 엿새 전에 내가 첫 봉급을 고스란히 — 다 합쳐 이십삼 루블 사십 코페이카를 — 그녀에게 가져다주었을 때, 그녀는 나를 자기 귀염둥이라고 부르더이다. '귀염둥이,' 그러는 거예요, '내 귀여운 귀염둥이' 하고요. 그것도 우리 둘만 있을 때였지요, 아시겠소? 자네는 나를 미남이라고도, 변변한 남편이라고도 여기지 않겠지요?…… 한데 그녀가 내 뺨을 꼬집으며, '내 귀여운 귀염둥이' 하고 그러더란 말입니다.\"\n\n마르멜라도프는 말을 뚝 끊고는 미소를 지으려 애썼으나, 갑자기 그의 턱이 실룩거리기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 가까스로 자제했다. 이 선술집, 이 사내의 추락한 행색, 건초 거룻배에서 보낸 닷새 밤, 술 단지, 그러면서도 아내와 자식들을 향한 이 가슴 저미는 사랑이 듣는 이를 어리둥절하게 했다. 라스콜니코프는 골똘히 귀를 기울였으나, 속이 메스꺼웠다. 그는 여기 온 것이 짜증스러웠다.\n\n\"존경하는 선생, 존경하는 선생,\" 마르멜라도프가 정신을 추스르며 외쳤다. \"오, 선생, 어쩌면 이 모든 게 다른 이들에게 그렇듯 선생에게도 웃음거리로 보일지 모르고, 어쩌면 나는 그저 우리 집안 살림의 자질구레하고 하찮은 일들을 시시콜콜 늘어놓아 선생을 성가시게 하고 있는지도 모르지요, 하지만 나에게는 그게 결코 웃음거리가 아니올시다. 나는 그 모든 걸 절절히 느낄 수 있으니까요…… 내 인생의 그 천국 같은 하루와 그날 저녁 내내, 나는 이 모든 걸 어떻게 꾸려 나갈까, 아이들을 죄다 어떻게 입힐까, 그녀를 어떻게 쉬게 해 줄까, 내 친딸을 치욕에서 어떻게 구해 내어 가족의 품으로 되돌릴까 하는 덧없는 꿈에 잠겨 보냈소…… 그 밖에도 숱한 꿈을요…… 충분히 그럴 만하지 않겠습니까, 선생. 자, 그런데, 선생,\" (마르멜라도프가 갑자기 흠칫하더니, 고개를 들고 듣는 이를 뚫어지게 바라보았다) \"자, 그 모든 꿈을 꾼 바로 다음 날, 그러니까 정확히 닷새 전 저녁에, 나는 교활한 수작으로, 한밤의 도둑처럼, 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 그녀의 궤짝 열쇠를 슬쩍해서는 봉급에서 남은 걸 꺼냈는데, 그게 얼마였는지는 이젠 잊어버렸소, 자, 이제 나를 좀 보시오, 여러분 모두! 내가 집을 나온 지 닷새째이고, 그쪽에서는 나를 찾고 있을 테고, 내 일자리도 이걸로 끝장이고, 내 제복은 이집트 다리 옆 어느 선술집에 잡혀 있소. 나는 그걸 지금 입고 있는 이 옷가지와 바꿔치기했지요…… 그러니 이제 만사가 끝장이올시다!\"\n\n마르멜라도프는 주먹으로 자기 이마를 치고는, 이를 악물고 두 눈을 감은 채, 팔꿈치로 탁자를 무겁게 짚었다. 그러나 잠시 후 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 변하더니, 짐짓 꾸민 듯한 능청과 허세를 부리며 라스콜니코프를 흘끗 바라보고는, 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 나는 소냐를 찾아갔소, 해장술 값 좀 달라고 갔지요! 헤헤헤!\"\n\n\"설마 그 애가 줬다는 건 아니겠지?\" 새로 온 손님 하나가 외쳤다. 그는 그 말을 큰 소리로 내뱉고는 폭소를 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"바로 이 한 쿼트가 그 애 돈으로 산 거올시다.\" 마르멜라도프가 오로지 라스콜니코프에게만 대고 단언했다. \"그 애가 제 손으로 내게 삼십 코페이카를 주었지요, 마지막 남은 돈을, 가진 전부를, 내가 본 그대로요…… 그 애는 아무 말도 하지 않았소, 그저 말없이 나를 바라보기만 했지요…… 이 땅에서가 아니라, 저 높은 곳에서…… 그분들은 사람들을 가엾어하며 슬퍼하고 눈물 흘리지만, 그들을 탓하지는 않습니다, 탓하지 않아요! 하지만 탓하지 않으면 더 아프지요, 탓하지 않으면 더 아파요! 삼십 코페이카, 그래요! 한데 어쩌면 그 애한텐 지금 그 돈이 필요했을지도 모르지요, 안 그렇소? 어떻게 생각하시오, 선생? 이제 그 애는 겉모양을 갖춰야 하니까요. 그게 돈이 든단 말이오, 그 맵시, 그 특별한 맵시 말이오, 아시겠소? 알아듣겠소? 그리고 포마드도 있어야 하고, 알다시피, 그 애한테는 이것저것 필요한 게 있어요. 풀 먹인 페티코트도, 신발도, 웅덩이를 건널 때 발을 내보일 수 있는 진짜 멋들어진 신발 말이오. 아시겠소, 선생, 그 모든 맵시가 무얼 뜻하는지 아시겠어요? 그런데 나는, 그 애의 친아비인 나는, 그 돈에서 삼십 코페이카를 술값으로 챙겼단 말이오! 그리고 그걸 마시고 있어요! 게다가 이미 다 마셔 버렸지요! 자, 나 같은 인간을 누가 가엾어해 주겠소, 응? 선생, 나를 가엾게 여기시오, 아니면 안 그러시오? 말해 보시오, 선생, 가엾소, 안 가엾소? 헤헤헤!\"\n\n그는 잔을 채우려 했으나, 마실 게 남아 있지 않았다. 단지가 비어 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"자네가 가엾을 게 뭐가 있나?\" 다시 그들 곁으로 다가온 술집 주인이 외쳤다.\n\n폭소와 욕설마저 뒤따랐다. 그 웃음과 욕설은 이야기를 듣고 있던 자들에게서도, 그리고 아무것도 듣지 못한 채 그저 그 해고당한 관리의 꼴을 바라보고 있던 자들에게서도 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"가엾다니! 내가 왜 가엾어야 하느냐고?\" 마르멜라도프가 마치 바로 그 질문을 기다리고 있었다는 듯, 팔을 쭉 뻗고 벌떡 일어서며 갑자기 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"내가 왜 가엾어야 하느냐, 그 말이오? 그래! 나를 가엾어할 거리라곤 하나도 없소! 나는 십자가에 못 박혀야 마땅하지, 십자가에 못 박혀야 해, 가엾어할 게 아니라! 나를 못 박으시오, 오 심판자여, 나를 못 박되 또한 나를 가엾어해 주시오! 그러면 나는 제 발로 못 박히러 가리다, 내가 찾는 건 흥겨움이 아니라 눈물과 고난이니까요!…… 술 파는 자네는 이 술 한 파인트가 내게 달콤했을 거라 생각하시오? 나는 그 술잔 밑바닥에서 고난을 찾았소, 눈물과 고난을 찾았고, 그걸 찾아냈고, 그걸 맛보았지요. 하지만 모든 사람을 가엾어하신 분, 모든 사람과 모든 것을 헤아리신 분, 그분께서 우리를 가엾어하실 것이오. 그분은 유일한 분이고, 그분이야말로 심판자시니까요. 그분께서 그날에 오시어 물으실 거요. '폐병 든 계모와 남의 어린것들을 위해 제 몸을 십자가에 바친 그 딸은 어디 있느냐? 짐승 같은 짓에도 굴하지 않고 더럽고 술에 절은 제 지상의 아비를 가엾어한 그 딸은 어디 있느냐?' 그러고는 이렇게 말씀하시겠지요. '내게로 오너라! 나는 이미 한 번 너를 용서하였노라…… 이미 한 번 너를 용서하였노라…… 네 죄가 많으나 다 사함을 받았으니, 이는 네가 많이 사랑하였음이라……' 그리고 그분은 내 소냐를 용서하실 거요, 용서하실 거예요, 나는 그걸 압니다…… 방금 그 애와 함께 있을 때 내 가슴으로 그걸 느꼈지요! 그리고 그분은 만인을 심판하시고 용서하시리다, 선한 자도 악한 자도, 지혜로운 자도 온순한 자도요…… 그리고 그들 모두를 다 처리하시고 난 뒤, 그때 우리를 부르시겠지요. '너희도 나오너라,' 그분께서 그러실 거요, '나오너라, 너희 주정뱅이들아, 나오너라, 너희 나약한 자들아, 나오너라, 너희 치욕의 자식들아!' 그러면 우리는 모두 부끄러움 없이 나아가 그분 앞에 서리다. 그러면 그분께서 우리에게 이르시겠지요. '너희는 돼지로다, 짐승의 형상으로 빚어졌고 그 낙인이 찍혔도다. 하지만 너희도 오너라!' 그러면 지혜로운 자들과 분별 있는 자들이 말하겠지요. '오 주여, 어찌하여 이런 자들을 받아 주시나이까?' 그러면 그분께서 이르시리다. '내가 이들을 받아 주는 까닭은, 오 지혜로운 자들아, 내가 이들을 받아 주는 까닭은, 오 분별 있는 자들아, 이들 가운데 단 하나도 스스로 이럴 자격이 있다고 여기지 않았기 때문이니라.' 그러고는 우리에게 두 손을 내미실 것이고, 우리는 그분 앞에 엎드리리다…… 그리고 우리는 울 것이고…… 그리고 우리는 모든 것을 깨달으리다! 그때 우리는 모든 것을 깨달으리다!…… 그리고 모두가 깨달으리다, 카테리나 이바노브나마저도…… 그녀도 깨달으리다…… 주여, 당신의 나라가 임하소서!\" 그러고는 기진맥진하여 어쩔 줄 모른 채 의자에 털썩 주저앉았는데, 아무도 쳐다보지 않고, 주위를 까맣게 잊은 듯 깊은 상념에 잠겨 들었다. 그의 말은 어떤 인상을 자아냈다. 한순간 침묵이 흘렀다. 그러나 곧 다시 웃음소리와 욕설이 들려왔다.\n\n\"저게 저 사람 생각이라고!\"\n\n\"떠벌리다 제풀에 정신 줄을 놓았구먼!\"\n\n\"관리 났네, 관리 났어!\"\n\n이런 식으로, 계속해서 이어졌다.\n\n\"갑시다, 선생,\" 마르멜라도프가 갑자기 고개를 들고 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건넸다. \"나하고 같이 갑시다…… 코젤네 집인데, 안뜰 쪽으로 나 있지요. 카테리나 이바노브나한테 가야겠소 — 갈 때도 됐고요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 얼마 전부터 떠나고 싶던 차였고, 그를 거들어 줄 생각이었다. 마르멜라도프는 말보다도 다리가 훨씬 더 불안정해서, 청년에게 몸을 무겁게 기댔다. 가야 할 거리는 이삼백 걸음쯤이었다. 그 술 취한 사내는 집에 가까워질수록 점점 더 두려움과 혼란에 사로잡혔다.\n\n\"내가 지금 두려운 건 카테리나 이바노브나가 아니올시다.\" 그가 동요하며 중얼거렸다. \"그녀가 내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯기 시작할까 봐 두려운 것도 아니고요. 내 머리칼이 무슨 대수겠소! 머리칼 따위 될 대로 되라지요! 내 말이 그 말이외다! 차라리 그녀가 머리칼을 쥐어뜯기 시작하는 편이 나을 거요, 그게 두려운 게 아니니까요…… 내가 두려운 건 그녀의 눈이올시다…… 그래요, 그녀의 눈…… 두 뺨에 어린 그 붉은빛도 나를 겁먹게 하고요…… 그리고 그녀의 숨소리도요…… 그 병에 걸린 사람들이 어떻게 숨 쉬는지 눈여겨본 적 있소…… 흥분했을 때 말이오? 아이들의 울음소리도 두렵소…… 혹시 소냐가 그 애들에게 먹을 걸 갖다주지 않았다면…… 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 모르겠소! 모르겠어요! 하지만 매질은 두렵지 않소이다…… 알아 두시오, 선생, 그런 매질은 내게 고통이 아니라 오히려 즐거움이라는 걸요. 사실 나는 그게 없으면 못 견디겠소…… 그게 차라리 나아요. 그녀가 나를 때리게 두시오, 그러면 그녀의 가슴이 후련해지니까요…… 그게 차라리 나아요…… 저기 그 집이 있군. 가구장이 코젤네 집이오…… 독일 사람인데, 형편이 넉넉하지요. 앞장서시오!\"\n\n그들은 안뜰로 들어가 사 층으로 올라갔다. 위로 올라갈수록 계단은 점점 더 어두워졌다. 거의 열한 시였고, 페테르부르크의 여름에는 진짜 밤이라는 게 없다지만, 그래도 계단 꼭대기는 캄캄했다.\n\n계단 맨 꼭대기에 있는 때 묻은 작은 문이 빠끔히 열려 있었다. 열 걸음 남짓 되는 무척 초라한 방이 양초 토막 하나로 밝혀져 있었는데, 입구에서 방 전체가 다 들여다보였다. 방은 온통 어수선했고, 온갖 누더기, 특히 아이들 옷가지가 너저분하게 널려 있었다. 가장 안쪽 구석에는 너덜너덜한 홑이불 한 장이 가로질러 쳐져 있었다. 아마 그 뒤에 침대가 있는 모양이었다. 방 안에는 의자 두 개와, 구멍이 숭숭 뚫린 아메리카산 가죽을 씌운 소파 하나밖에 없었고, 그 앞에는 칠도 안 하고 덮개도 없는 낡은 전나무 부엌 탁자가 놓여 있었다. 탁자 가장자리에는 쇠 촛대에 꽂힌 우지 양초 하나가 가물가물 타고 있었다. 보아하니 이 가족은 방의 일부가 아니라 방 한 칸을 통째로 쓰고 있는 듯했으나, 그 방은 사실상 통로나 다름없었다. 아말리야 리페베흐젤의 셋집을 나눈 다른 방들, 아니 차라리 벽장들로 통하는 문이 반쯤 열려 있었고, 그 안에서는 고함과 소란, 웃음소리가 들려왔다. 거기서는 사람들이 카드놀이를 하며 차를 마시고 있는 듯했다. 이따금 더없이 무람없는 말들이 튀어나왔다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 카테리나 이바노브나를 단번에 알아보았다. 그녀는 꽤 키가 크고 호리호리하며 우아한 여인이었는데, 끔찍하게 야위었고, 짙은 갈색의 탐스러운 머리칼에 두 뺨은 병적인 홍조를 띠고 있었다. 그녀는 두 손을 가슴에 꼭 누른 채 그 작은 방 안을 서성이고 있었다. 입술은 바싹 말랐고, 숨은 신경질적으로 끊어지듯 헐떡이며 새어 나왔다. 그녀의 두 눈은 열병에라도 걸린 듯 번뜩였고, 매섭게 굳은 시선으로 주위를 두리번거렸다. 그 폐병 들고 흥분한 얼굴에 다 타들어 가는 양초 토막의 마지막 가물거리는 불빛이 어른거리니, 메스꺼운 인상을 자아냈다. 라스콜니코프가 보기에 그녀는 서른쯤 되어 보였고, 마르멜라도프와는 분명 어울리지 않는 아내였다…… 그녀는 그들이 오는 소리를 듣지도 못했고 알아차리지도 못했다. 그녀는 무언가에 골몰한 듯, 아무것도 듣지도 보지도 못하는 것 같았다. 방 안은 답답했지만, 그녀는 창문을 열지 않았다. 계단에서는 악취가 올라왔지만, 계단으로 통하는 문을 닫지 않았다. 안쪽 방들에서는 담배 연기가 뭉게뭉게 흘러들었고, 그녀는 연신 기침을 하면서도 문을 닫지 않았다. 막내인 여섯 살배기 계집아이는 바닥에 웅크려 앉아 소파에 머리를 기댄 채 잠들어 있었다. 한 살쯤 위인 사내아이는 구석에서 울며 떨고 있었는데, 아마 방금 매를 맞은 모양이었다. 그 곁에는 아홉 살 난 계집아이가 서 있었는데, 키가 크고 야위었으며, 얇고 너덜너덜한 속옷 위에 맨 어깨에는 오래되어 케케묵은 캐시미어 외투를 걸치고 있었으나, 너무 오래되어 작아진 그 옷은 무릎에도 채 닿지 않았다. 막대기처럼 가느다란 그 아이의 팔이 동생의 목을 감싸고 있었다. 아이는 동생을 달래며 무언가 소곤소곤 속삭였고, 동생이 다시 칭얼대지 않도록 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다. 그러면서도 겁먹은 야윈 얼굴 탓에 한층 더 커 보이는 큼직한 검은 눈으로, 두려운 듯 어머니를 지켜보고 있었다. 마르멜라도프는 문 안으로 들어서지 않고, 바로 그 문간에서 무릎을 꿇으며 라스콜니코프를 앞으로 떠밀었다. 그 여인은 낯선 사람을 보자 무심하게 멈춰 서서 그를 마주 보았는데, 잠시 정신이 든 듯 그가 무슨 일로 왔는지 의아해하는 것 같았다. 그러나 그녀는 그가 옆방으로 가려는 것이려니 결론을 내린 게 분명했으니, 거기로 가려면 자기네 방을 지나가야 했기 때문이다. 그녀는 더는 그에게 신경 쓰지 않고 바깥문을 닫으려 그쪽으로 걸어가다가, 문간에 무릎 꿇고 있는 남편을 보고는 별안간 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"앗!\" 그녀가 광란하듯 외쳤다. \"이 인간이 돌아왔어! 이 죄인이! 이 괴물이!…… 그래, 돈은 어디 있어? 주머니에 든 게 뭐야, 보여 봐! 옷도 죄다 딴것이잖아! 당신 옷은 어디 갔어? 돈은 어디 있느냐고! 말해!\"\n\n그러고는 그를 뒤지기 시작했다. 마르멜라도프는 고분고분 순순히 두 팔을 들어 올려 수색을 도왔다. 그러나 한 파딩도 나오지 않았다.\n\n\"돈은 어디 있어?\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 그걸 죄다 마셔 버린 거야? 궤짝에 은화로 십이 루블이 남아 있었는데!\" 그러고는 격분하여 그의 머리칼을 움켜쥐고 그를 방 안으로 끌고 들어갔다. 마르멜라도프는 온순하게 무릎으로 기어가며 그녀의 수고를 거들었다.\n\n\"이게 내겐 위안이올시다! 이게 내겐 고통이 아니라 진정한 위, 안, 이, 라, 고요, 존, 경, 하, 는, 선생,\" 그가 머리칼을 잡힌 채 이리저리 흔들리며, 심지어 한 번은 이마로 바닥을 쿵 찧기까지 하면서 외쳤다. 바닥에서 잠들어 있던 아이가 깨어나 울기 시작했다. 구석에 있던 사내아이는 자제력을 완전히 잃고 부들부들 떨며 비명을 지르더니, 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 듯 격렬한 공포에 사로잡혀 누나에게 달려갔다. 맏이인 계집아이는 사시나무처럼 떨고 있었다.\n\n\"마셔 버렸어! 죄다 마셔 버렸다고,\" 가엾은 여인이 절망하여 비명을 질렀다. \"옷마저 없어졌어! 그리고 이 애들은 굶주리고 있다고, 굶주리고 있어!\" 그러고는 두 손을 부르쥐고 아이들을 가리켰다. \"오, 이 저주받은 인생! 그리고 당신, 부끄럽지도 않아?\" 그녀가 별안간 라스콜니코프에게 달려들었다. \"선술집에서 왔구먼! 이 인간이랑 같이 마셨지? 당신도 이 인간이랑 같이 마신 거지! 썩 나가!\"\n\n청년은 한마디도 하지 않고 황급히 자리를 떴다. 그 사이 안쪽 문이 활짝 열리며, 호기심 어린 얼굴들이 그리로 기웃거렸다. 파이프며 담배를 문 거칠고 히죽거리는 얼굴들, 챙모자를 쓴 머리들이 문간으로 불쑥불쑥 들이밀어졌다. 좀 더 안쪽에는 앞섶을 풀어헤친 실내복 차림에, 꼴사납게 헐벗다시피 한 차림새의 사람들이 보였고, 더러는 손에 카드를 쥐고 있었다. 마르멜라도프가 머리칼을 잡힌 채 이리저리 끌려다니면서 이게 자기에겐 위안이라고 외쳐 대자, 그들은 유난히 흥겨워했다. 그들은 급기야 방 안으로 들어오기 시작했다. 마침내 불길하게 새된 고함 소리가 들려왔으니, 바로 아말리야 리페베흐젤 본인이 그들 틈을 비집고 나오며 제 나름의 방식으로 질서를 잡으려 하면서, 백 번째로 그 가엾은 여인에게 거친 욕설을 퍼부으며 내일 당장 방을 비우라 윽박질러 겁을 주려는 것이었다. 라스콜니코프는 나가는 길에, 주머니에 손을 넣어 선술집에서 자기 루블을 헐어 받은 동전들을 움켜쥐고는, 아무도 모르게 창턱에 내려놓을 짬을 낼 수 있었다. 그러나 나중에 계단에서 그는 마음이 바뀌어 도로 들어가려 했다.\n\n'내가 어쩌자고 이런 멍청한 짓을,' 그는 속으로 생각했다. '저들에겐 소냐가 있지만, 나는 내가 그 돈이 필요한데.' 그러나 이제 와서 도로 가져오기란 불가능하고, 어차피 가져오지도 못하리라는 데 생각이 미치자, 그는 손을 한 번 내젓고는 자기 하숙집으로 돌아갔다. '소냐도 포마드가 필요하겠지.' 그는 거리를 걸으며 이렇게 말하고는, 표독스럽게 웃었다. '그런 맵시에도 돈이 드는 법이니까…… 흠! 게다가 어쩌면 소냐 본인도 오늘 빈털터리가 될지 모르지, 큰 사냥감을 쫓는 데는…… 금맥을 캐는 데는…… 늘 위험이 따르는 법이니까. 그렇게 되면 내 돈이 아니고서야 저들은 내일 빵 부스러기 하나 없을 테고. 소냐 만세! 거 참 굉장한 금광을 파 놓으셨군! 게다가 그걸 알뜰히도 우려먹고 있어! 그래, 알뜰히도 우려먹고 있지! 다들 그 일로 눈물깨나 흘리다가도 이젠 익숙해져 버렸어. 인간이란 무엇에든 익숙해지는 법이니까, 비열한 작자 같으니!'\n\n그는 깊은 상념에 잠겨 들었다.\n\n'한데 만일 내가 틀렸다면?' 그는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 갑자기 외쳤다. '만일 인간이 정말로 비열한 게 아니라면, 인간 일반이, 그러니까 인류 전체가 말이야 — 그렇다면 나머지는 죄다 편견에 불과하고, 그저 인위적으로 지어낸 공포일 뿐이며, 아무런 장벽도 없는 것이고, 모든 게 마땅히 그래야 할 대로인 셈이지.'\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n그는 이튿날 늦게야 끊긴 잠에서 깨어났다. 그러나 잠은 그를 개운하게 해 주지 못했다. 그는 담즙이 끓는 듯 신경질적이고 심술궂은 기분으로 깨어나, 증오에 찬 눈길로 자기 방을 둘러보았다. 그것은 길이가 여섯 걸음쯤 되는, 벽장만 한 작디작은 방이었다. 벽에서 벗겨져 가는 먼지투성이 노란 벽지 탓에 방은 궁색해 보였고, 천장이 어찌나 낮은지 키가 보통보다 조금만 큰 사람도 그 안에서는 불편하기 짝이 없어, 매 순간 머리를 천장에 부딪칠 것만 같았다. 가구도 방과 어울렸다. 다리가 흔들거리는 낡은 의자 세 개, 구석에 놓인 칠한 탁자 — 그 위에는 원고 몇 장과 책 몇 권이 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에 두껍게 쌓인 먼지는 그것들이 오랫동안 손길이 닿지 않았음을 보여 주었다. 한쪽 벽 거의 전부와 방바닥의 절반을 큼직하고 볼품없는 소파가 차지하고 있었다. 한때는 친츠 천으로 싸여 있었으나 지금은 누더기가 된 그것이 라스콜니코프의 침대 노릇을 했다. 그는 흔히 그 위에서, 옷도 벗지 않고 시트도 깔지 않은 채, 낡은 학생 외투를 둘러쓰고, 작은 베개 하나에 머리를 얹고 잠들곤 했는데, 그 베개 밑에는 깨끗한 것이든 더러운 것이든 가진 속옷가지를 죄다 쌓아 올려 긴 베개 대용으로 삼았다. 소파 앞에는 작은 탁자가 하나 놓여 있었다.\n\n이보다 더 낮은 무질서의 밑바닥으로 가라앉기란 어려웠을 테지만, 지금 같은 마음 상태의 라스콜니코프에게 이것은 도리어 흡족한 일이었다. 그는 등딱지 속의 거북이처럼 모든 사람에게서 완전히 멀어져 있었고, 시중을 들어 주느라 이따금 그의 방을 들여다보아야 하는 하녀의 모습마저도 신경질적인 짜증으로 그를 몸부림치게 했다. 그는 한 가지 일에만 온통 집중하는 어떤 편집광들을 사로잡는 그런 상태에 빠져 있었다. 집주인 여자는 지난 두 주 동안 그에게 식사 보내는 일을 그만두었으나, 그는 끼니를 거르면서도 그녀에게 따져 볼 생각조차 하지 않았다. 요리사이자 유일한 하녀인 나스타시야는 이 하숙인의 기분을 차라리 반기는 편이어서, 그의 방을 쓸고 치우는 일을 완전히 그만두었고, 그저 일주일에 한 번쯤 빗자루를 들고 그의 방에 들어오는 게 고작이었다. 그날도 그를 깨운 것은 그녀였다.\n\n\"일어나요, 왜 아직 자고 있어요?\" 그녀가 그를 불렀다. \"벌써 아홉 시가 넘었어요, 차를 좀 가져왔는데, 한 잔 들겠어요? 어지간히 굶주렸을 것 같은데요?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 눈을 떴고, 흠칫 놀라 나스타시야를 알아보았다.\n\n\"집주인 여자가 보냈나, 응?\" 그는 병색이 도는 얼굴로 천천히 소파에 일어나 앉으며 물었다.\n\n\"집주인 여자가 보내긴요!\"\n\n그녀는 그의 앞에, 묽고 김빠진 차가 가득한 자기 것 금 간 찻주전자를 내려놓고, 그 옆에 누런 설탕 덩어리 두 개를 놓았다.\n\n\"여기, 나스타시야, 이것 좀 받아 줘.\" 그는 (옷을 입은 채 잤기에) 주머니를 뒤져 동전 한 줌을 꺼내며 말했다. \"달려가서 빵 한 덩이만 사다 줘. 그리고 돼지고기 가게에서 제일 싼 걸로 소시지도 조금만 사다 주고.\"\n\n\"빵은 당장 가져다드릴게요, 근데 소시지 대신 양배추 수프를 좀 들지 않겠어요? 아주 훌륭한 수프예요, 어제 거고요. 어제 댁한테 남겨 뒀는데 늦게 들어왔잖아요. 정말 맛 좋은 수프예요.\"\n\n수프가 들어오고 그가 먹기 시작하자, 나스타시야는 소파에 그의 곁에 앉아 수다를 떨기 시작했다. 그녀는 시골 출신의 농사꾼 아낙으로, 무척이나 말이 많았다.\n\n\"프라스코비야 파블로브나가 댁을 경찰에 고발하려고 해요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그는 얼굴을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"경찰에? 그 여자가 뭘 원하는 거야?\"\n\n\"돈은 안 갚지, 방은 안 비우지. 그러니 원하는 게 뻔하죠.\"\n\n\"제기랄, 이젠 갈 데까지 갔군.\" 그는 이를 갈며 중얼거렸다. \"안 돼, 그건 나한테 안 맞아…… 지금은. 멍청한 여자야.\" 그가 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"오늘 가서 얘기를 좀 해야겠어.\"\n\n\"멍청하긴 멍청하죠, 틀림없이, 나처럼요. 근데 댁은 그렇게 똑똑하다면서 왜 여기 자루처럼 누워서 아무것도 내보이는 게 없어요? 한때는 애들 가르치러 나갔다면서요. 근데 지금은 왜 아무것도 안 해요?\"\n\n\"나는 하고 있어…….\" 라스콜니코프가 시무룩하고 마지못한 투로 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"뭘 하고 있는데요?\"\n\n\"일을…….\"\n\n\"무슨 일을요?\"\n\n\"생각하고 있어.\" 그는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 진지하게 대답했다.\n\n나스타시야는 웃음보가 터졌다. 그녀는 잘 웃는 편이었는데, 무언가 우스운 일이 있으면 소리 없이 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 흔들며 속이 거북해질 지경까지 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"그래, 그 생각으로 돈은 많이 벌었어요?\" 그녀가 가까스로 한마디 입에 올렸다.\n\n\"장화 없이는 애들 가르치러 나갈 수가 없어. 게다가 지긋지긋하고.\"\n\n\"밥줄하고는 싸우지 마요.\"\n\n\"수업료를 너무 쥐꼬리만큼 준단 말이야. 동전 몇 푼이 무슨 소용이 있어?\" 그는 마치 자기 자신의 생각에 답하듯 마지못해 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그래서 댁은 한꺼번에 한밑천 잡고 싶다는 거예요?\"\n\n그는 묘한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 한밑천을 원해.\" 그가 잠깐 사이를 두었다가 단호하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇게 서두르지 좀 마요, 사람 정말 무섭게 하네! 빵은 가져올까요, 말까요?\"\n\n\"맘대로 해.\"\n\n\"아, 깜박했네! 어제 댁이 나갔을 때 편지가 한 통 왔어요.\"\n\n\"편지? 나한테! 누구한테서?\"\n\n\"몰라요. 우편배달부한테 내 돈으로 삼 코페이카를 줬어요. 갚을 거죠?\"\n\n\"그럼 그걸 이리 가져와, 제발, 가져오라고.\" 라스콜니코프가 몹시 흥분하여 외쳤다. \"맙소사!\"\n\n잠시 후 편지가 그에게 전해졌다. 바로 그것이었다. 어머니에게서 온, R 현(縣)에서 온 편지였다. 그는 그것을 받아 들면서 얼굴이 새파래졌다. 편지를 받아 본 지 오래되었지만, 또 다른 감정이 갑자기 그의 가슴을 찔러 왔다.\n\n\"나스타시야, 제발 나를 혼자 좀 내버려 둬. 여기 삼 코페이카 받아, 그러니 제발 서둘러서 좀 나가 줘!\"\n\n편지가 그의 손안에서 떨렸다. 그녀 앞에서 그것을 뜯고 싶지 않았다. 그는 이 편지와 단둘이 남겨지기를 바랐다. 나스타시야가 나가자 그는 편지를 재빨리 입술로 가져가 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 한때 그에게 읽고 쓰기를 가르쳐 준 어머니의, 그토록 정겹고 낯익은 작고 비스듬한 필체로 쓰인 수신인을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그는 머뭇거렸다. 무언가가 두려운 것 같았다. 마침내 그는 편지를 뜯었다. 두툼하고 묵직한 편지로, 무게가 두 온스가 넘었고, 큼직한 편지지 두 장에 아주 작은 글씨가 빼곡히 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 나의 로쟈.\" 어머니는 이렇게 썼다. \"내가 너와 편지로 이야기를 나눈 지 두 달이 지났구나, 그게 나를 얼마나 괴롭히던지, 밤에도 잠 못 이루며 골똘히 생각하곤 했단다. 하지만 내가 어쩔 수 없이 침묵했던 것을 너는 탓하지 않으리라 믿는다. 내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지 너도 알지. 두냐와 나에게는 너밖에 의지할 데가 없단다, 너는 우리의 전부, 우리의 단 하나뿐인 희망, 단 하나뿐인 버팀목이야. 몇 달 전에 네가 자기 생활을 꾸려 갈 돈이 없어서 대학을 그만두었고, 수업도 다른 일거리도 다 잃었다는 소식을 들었을 때 내가 얼마나 슬펐는지! 일 년에 연금 백이십 루블을 받는 내가 어떻게 너를 도울 수 있었겠니? 넉 달 전에 네게 보낸 십오 루블도, 너도 알다시피, 이 고장 상인 바실리 이바노비치 바흐루신에게서 내 연금을 담보로 빌린 것이었단다. 그분은 마음씨 고운 사람이고, 또 네 아버지의 친구이기도 했지. 그러나 그분에게 연금을 받을 권리를 넘겼기 때문에 빚이 다 청산될 때까지 기다려야 했고, 이제야 겨우 그것이 끝났구나, 그래서 그동안 너에게 아무것도 보내 줄 수가 없었단다. 하지만 이제는, 다행히도, 무언가 좀 더 보낼 수 있을 것 같고, 사실 지금 우리는 우리의 행운을 스스로 축하해도 좋을 듯해서 서둘러 네게 알리는 거란다. 우선, 사랑하는 로쟈, 네가 짐작이나 했겠니, 네 누이가 지난 여섯 주 동안 나와 함께 지내고 있고 앞으로도 우리는 떨어지지 않으리라는 것을. 다행히도 그애의 고생은 끝났지만, 모든 일을 차근차근 다 말해 주마, 그래야 네가 모든 일이 어떻게 된 것인지, 그리고 우리가 지금껏 네게 숨겨 온 것들을 다 알 수 있을 테니까. 두 달 전에 네가 나에게, 두냐가 스비드리가일로프 댁에서 무척 힘든 일을 많이 겪고 있다는 소문을 들었다고 쓰면서, 그 일을 다 이야기해 달라고 했을 때 — 내가 무어라 답을 쓸 수 있었겠니? 만일 내가 너에게 모든 진실을 다 써 보냈다면, 너는 분명 모든 걸 내팽개치고 우리에게 달려왔을 거다, 설령 내내 걸어서라도 말이야, 나는 네 성격과 감정을 잘 아니까, 너는 네 누이가 모욕당하도록 내버려 두지 않았을 테니. 나 자신도 절망에 빠져 있었지만, 내가 무얼 할 수 있었겠니? 게다가 나도 그때는 진실을 다 알지 못했단다. 모든 걸 더 어렵게 만든 건, 두냐가 그 집의 가정교사 자리를 맡으면서 백 루블을 선불로 받았고, 매달 봉급에서 그 일부를 제하기로 한 조건이었다는 점이었다. 그러니 빚을 다 갚기 전에는 그 자리를 내팽개칠 수가 없었던 거야. 이 돈은 (이제야 너에게 다 설명할 수 있겠구나, 소중한 로쟈), 그애가 무엇보다 그때 네가 그토록 절실히 필요로 했던, 그래서 작년에 우리에게서 받은 그 육십 루블을 너에게 보내 주려고 받은 것이었단다. 그때 우리는 이 돈이 두냐의 저축에서 나온 것이라고 써서 너를 속였지만, 그건 사실이 아니었어, 이제 너에게 다 말하는 건, 다행히도 사정이 갑자기 좋은 쪽으로 바뀌었고, 또 두냐가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그애가 얼마나 따뜻한 마음을 지녔는지 네가 알았으면 해서란다. 처음에 사실 스비드리가일로프 씨는 그애를 아주 무례하게 대했고, 식탁에서 모욕적이고 비웃는 듯한 말을 하곤 했단다…… 하지만 이제 다 끝난 마당에 괜히 너를 걱정시키지 않으려고, 그 고통스러운 자질구레한 일들까지 다 들춰내고 싶지는 않구나. 한마디로, 스비드리가일로프 씨의 부인인 마르파 페트로브나와 나머지 식구들이 친절하고 너그럽게 대해 주었음에도, 두냐는 무척 힘든 시간을 보냈단다, 특히 스비드리가일로프 씨가 옛 연대 시절의 버릇이 도져서 바쿠스의 영향 아래 있을 때면 더욱 그랬지. 그런데 나중에 이 모든 게 어떻게 밝혀졌는지 아니? 그 미친 사람이 처음부터 두냐에게 욕정을 품고 있었으면서도, 그것을 무례하고 경멸하는 태도로 감춰 왔다면 너는 믿겠니? 어쩌면 그는 자기 나이며 한 집안의 가장이라는 처지를 생각하면서, 자기 자신의 그 경솔한 욕망에 스스로 부끄러워하고 소스라쳤는지도 모른다. 그래서 두냐에게 화풀이를 했던 거지. 또 어쩌면 그는 무례하고 비웃는 듯한 태도로 다른 사람들에게 진실을 감추려 했는지도 모르겠다. 그러나 마침내 그는 자제력을 완전히 잃고서 뻔뻔스럽게도 두냐에게 노골적이고 수치스러운 제안을 하기에 이르렀단다, 온갖 것들로 그애를 꾀면서, 게다가 모든 걸 다 내던지고 그애를 자기의 다른 영지로, 심지어 외국으로 데려가겠다고까지 했다는구나. 그애가 어떤 일을 겪었을지 너도 짐작이 갈 게다! 그 자리를 당장 그만두는 것은, 돈 빚 때문만이 아니라, 마르파 페트로브나의 마음을 다치지 않기 위해서도 불가능했단다, 그랬다간 그분의 의심을 사게 될 테고, 그러면 두냐가 집안에 불화를 일으킨 원인이 되었을 테니까. 그리고 그건 두냐에게도 끔찍한 추문이 되었을 거다, 그건 피할 수 없는 일이었지. 그 밖에도 여러 다른 이유가 있어서 두냐는 그 끔찍한 집에서 여섯 주를 더 견디는 수밖에 도리가 없었단다. 너도 물론 두냐를 잘 알지. 그애가 얼마나 영리하고 또 얼마나 의지가 굳센지 알 테고. 두냐는 많은 것을 견뎌 낼 수 있고, 아무리 어려운 상황에서도 꿋꿋함을 잃지 않는 의연함을 지녔지. 우리가 끊임없이 연락을 주고받았는데도, 그애는 나를 속상하게 할까 봐 모든 걸 다 써 보내지도 않았단다. 그런데 그 모든 일이 아주 뜻밖에 끝나 버렸어. 마르파 페트로브나가 우연히 정원에서 자기 남편이 두냐에게 애원하는 것을 엿듣고는, 상황을 완전히 잘못 해석해서 모든 책임을 그애에게 돌리고, 그애가 모든 일의 원인이라고 믿어 버렸단다. 그 자리에서, 정원에서 그들 사이에 끔찍한 소동이 벌어졌어. 마르파 페트로브나는 두냐를 때리기까지 했고, 아무 말도 들으려 하지 않은 채 꼬박 한 시간 동안 그애에게 소리를 질러 댔단다. 그러고는 두냐를 당장 허름한 농사꾼 짐마차에 태워 나에게 보내라고 명령했지, 그 마차에 그애의 짐이며 속옷이며 옷가지를 죄다, 개거나 챙기지도 않고 마구잡이로 뒤죽박죽 던져 넣었단다. 게다가 억수같이 소나기까지 쏟아졌는데, 모욕당하고 수치를 당한 두냐는 덮개도 없는 마차에 농사꾼과 함께 타고 읍내까지 십칠 베르스타를 내내 달려와야 했단다. 자, 이제 생각해 보렴, 두 달 전에 네게서 받은 그 편지에 내가 무어라 답을 보낼 수 있었겠으며, 무어라 쓸 수 있었겠니? 나는 절망에 빠져 있었단다. 너에게 진실을 차마 써 보낼 수 없었던 건, 그랬다간 네가 몹시 불행하고 비통하고 분개했을 테니까, 그렇다고 네가 무얼 할 수 있었겠니? 기껏해야 너 자신을 망쳐 버릴 수 있었을 뿐이지, 게다가 두냐가 그걸 허락하지도 않았을 테고. 그렇다고 내 마음이 그토록 슬픔으로 가득 차 있는데 자질구레한 이야기로 편지를 채울 수도 없었단다. 꼬박 한 달 동안 읍내는 이 추문에 관한 소문으로 들끓었고, 급기야 두냐와 나는 경멸 어린 시선과 수군거림, 심지어 우리에 대한 노골적인 험담 때문에 교회에조차 갈 엄두를 못 낼 지경에 이르렀단다. 아는 사람들은 모두 우리를 피했고, 길에서 인사를 건네는 이조차 없었으며, 어떤 가게 점원들과 사무원들이 우리 집 대문에 콜타르를 칠하는 그런 치욕적인 방법으로 우리를 모욕하려 한다는 말까지 들렸단다, 그래서 집주인이 우리더러 집을 비우라고 말하기 시작했지. 이 모든 일은 마르파 페트로브나가 꾸민 짓이었어, 그분이 집집마다 다니며 두냐를 헐뜯고 진흙탕에 처박았던 거야. 그분은 이웃 사람들을 죄다 알고, 그달에는 끊임없이 읍내로 나왔는데, 워낙 말이 많은 데다 자기 집안일을 떠벌리기를 좋아하고, 특히 자기 남편에 대한 불평을 만나는 사람마다 늘어놓는 — 도무지 옳지 못한 일이지 — 버릇이 있어서, 순식간에 자기 이야기를 읍내뿐 아니라 인근 일대에 죄다 퍼뜨려 버렸단다. 그 때문에 나는 앓아눕고 말았지만, 두냐는 나보다 더 잘 견뎌 냈단다, 그애가 그 모든 걸 어떻게 견디며 나를 위로하고 북돋아 주려 애썼는지 네가 보았더라면! 그애는 천사란다! 하지만 하느님의 자비로 우리의 고통은 짧게 끝났어. 스비드리가일로프 씨가 제정신을 차리고 뉘우치고는, 아마도 두냐가 가엾어서였겠지, 마르파 페트로브나 앞에 두냐의 결백을 완전하고도 명백하게 증명하는 것을 내놓았단다, 마르파 페트로브나가 정원에서 그들을 맞닥뜨리기 전에 두냐가 어쩔 수 없이 써서 그에게 건넸던 편지의 형태로 말이야. 그애가 떠난 뒤 스비드리가일로프 씨의 손에 남아 있던 이 편지에서, 그애는 그가 애원하던 직접적인 해명이나 은밀한 만남을 거절했던 거야. 그 편지에서 그애는, 마르파 페트로브나에 대한 그의 비열한 행동을 두고 격렬한 분노로 그를 책망하면서, 그가 한 집안의 아버지요 가장임을 일깨우고, 이미 충분히 불행한 무력한 처녀를 괴롭히고 불행하게 만드는 것이 얼마나 파렴치한 짓인지 말했단다. 정말이지, 사랑하는 로쟈, 그 편지는 어찌나 고결하고 가슴 뭉클하게 쓰였던지, 나는 그것을 읽으며 흐느꼈고 지금까지도 눈물 없이는 읽지를 못한단다. 게다가 하인들의 증언도 두냐의 명예를 회복시켜 주었어. 그들은 스비드리가일로프 씨 자신이 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을 보고 알고 있었거든 — 하인들이란 늘 그런 법이지. 마르파 페트로브나는 완전히 허를 찔려, 자기 입으로 우리에게 말했듯이 '또다시 무너져 버렸지만', 두냐의 결백을 온전히 확신하게 되었단다. 바로 다음 날, 일요일이었는데, 그분은 곧장 대성당으로 가서 무릎을 꿇고 눈물로 성모님께 기도드렸어, 이 새로운 시련을 견디고 자기 도리를 다할 힘을 달라고 말이야. 그러고는 대성당에서 곧장 우리에게 와서 모든 이야기를 털어놓고, 비통하게 울며, 완전히 뉘우치고는 두냐를 끌어안고 용서해 달라고 빌었단다. 같은 날 아침, 그분은 지체 없이 읍내의 집집마다 돌아다니며, 가는 곳마다 눈물을 흘리면서 더없이 칭송하는 말로 두냐의 결백과 그애 감정의 고결함, 그애 행동의 고귀함을 단언했단다. 그뿐만 아니라 그분은 두냐가 스비드리가일로프 씨에게 직접 쓴 그 편지를 모두에게 보여 주고 읽어 주었으며, 심지어 그것을 베껴 가도록까지 허락했단다 — 솔직히 나로서는 그것까지는 지나친 일이었다고 생각하지만 말이야. 이런 식으로 그분은 며칠 동안이나 읍내 전체를 마차로 돌아다니느라 바빴는데, 어떤 사람들은 자기보다 다른 사람들이 먼저 들었다고 기분 상해했기 때문이지. 그래서 차례를 정해야 했고, 그분이 도착하기도 전에 집집마다 그분을 기다렸으며, 아무 날 아무 데서 마르파 페트로브나가 그 편지를 읽으리라는 것을 다들 알고서 그것을 읽을 때마다 사람들이 모여들었단다, 이미 자기 집에서며 남의 집에서 여러 번 들은 사람들까지 많이 말이야. 내 생각에는 이 모든 게 상당히, 아주 상당히 불필요한 일이었지만, 그게 마르파 페트로브나의 성격인 걸 어쩌겠니. 어쨌든 그분은 두냐의 명예를 완전히 회복시키는 데 성공했고, 이 일의 온갖 치욕은 유일한 죄인인 그분 남편에게 지울 수 없는 오명으로 고스란히 남게 되었단다, 그래서 나는 도리어 그가 가엾어지기 시작할 정도였지. 그 미친 사람을 너무 가혹하게 대하는 게 아닌가 싶어서 말이야. 두냐는 곧바로 여러 집안에서 가정교사를 해 달라는 청을 받았지만 거절했단다. 갑자기 모두가 그애를 눈에 띄게 존중하며 대하기 시작했고, 이 모든 것이, 말하자면 지금 우리의 운명 전체를 뒤바꿔 놓은 그 일을 이루는 데 크게 한몫을 했단다. 사랑하는 로쟈, 너는 알아야 한다, 두냐에게 청혼자가 생겼고 그애가 이미 그와 결혼하기로 승낙했다는 것을. 서둘러 이 일을 죄다 너에게 알리려 한다만, 비록 너의 동의를 구하지 않고 정해진 일이라 해도, 너는 그 일로 나나 네 누이를 원망하지 않으리라 생각한다, 우리가 너의 소식을 들을 때까지 결정을 미루며 기다릴 수 없었다는 것을 너도 알 테니까. 게다가 너는 현장에 있지 않고서는 모든 사정을 다 헤아릴 수 없었을 거다. 일이 이렇게 된 거란다. 그분은 이미 고문관 직위에 있는 표트르 페트로비치 루진이라는 분으로, 마르파 페트로브나와 먼 친척뻘인데, 그분이 이 혼사를 맺는 데 무척 적극적으로 나서 주었단다. 그분이 마르파 페트로브나를 통해 우리와 알고 지내고 싶다는 뜻을 표한 데서 시작되었어. 그분은 정중하게 영접을 받았고, 우리와 함께 커피를 마셨으며, 바로 그다음 날 우리에게 편지를 보내 무척 공손하게 청혼하면서 빠르고 분명한 답을 청했단다. 그분은 무척 바쁜 사람이고, 페테르부르크로 가야 해서 몹시 서두르고 있어, 그래서 그분에게는 한순간 한순간이 소중하단다. 물론 처음에 우리는 모든 일이 너무도 빠르고 뜻밖에 일어나서 몹시 놀랐지. 하루 종일 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하고 의논했단다. 그분은 살림이 넉넉하고 믿을 만한 사람으로, 관청에 두 자리를 맡고 있고 이미 재산도 모았단다. 마흔다섯이라는 게 사실이긴 하지만, 그분은 꽤 호감 가는 풍모에 아직도 여자들이 매력을 느낄 만하고, 대체로 무척 점잖고 풍채 좋은 사람이야, 다만 조금 음울하고 다소 거만해 보이긴 한다만. 하지만 어쩌면 그건 첫인상에 불과할지도 모르지. 그리고 사랑하는 로쟈, 그분이 곧 그러겠지만 페테르부르크에 오거든, 첫눈에 그분에게서 무언가 마음에 들지 않는 점이 있더라도, 네 버릇대로 너무 성급하고 가혹하게 그를 판단하지 않도록 조심하렴. 그분이 너에게 좋은 인상을 주리라 확신하기는 하지만, 그래도 이렇게 미리 일러두는 거란다. 게다가 어떤 사람이든 이해하려면 신중하고 조심스러워야 하고, 나중에 바로잡고 떨쳐 내기가 무척 어려운 편견과 그릇된 생각을 갖지 않도록 해야 하는 법이지. 그리고 표트르 페트로비치는 여러 정황으로 미루어 보건대 진실로 존경할 만한 사람이란다. 사실 첫 방문 때 그분은 자기가 현실적인 사람이라고 말하면서도, 그분 표현으로는 '우리의 가장 성장하는 세대'의 신념 가운데 많은 것을 공유하며 온갖 편견에 반대한다고 했단다. 그분은 그 밖에도 말을 많이 했는데, 조금 거만해서 남들이 자기 말에 귀 기울여 주는 걸 좋아하는 듯했지만, 그게 무슨 큰 흠이야 되겠니. 나는 물론 그 말을 거의 이해하지 못했지만, 두냐가 설명해 주기를, 그분은 대단히 교육받은 사람은 아니어도 영리하고 마음씨도 좋아 보인다고 하더구나. 너도 네 누이의 성격을 알지, 로쟈. 그애는 단호하고 분별 있고 인내심 강하고 너그러운 아이지만, 내가 잘 알다시피 정열적인 가슴을 지녔단다. 물론 그분 쪽에도 그애 쪽에도 대단한 사랑이 있는 건 아니지만, 두냐는 영리한 아이고 천사 같은 마음을 지녔으니, 남편을 행복하게 해 주는 것을 자기 도리로 삼을 게다, 그러면 그분도 그분대로 그애의 행복을 보살피는 것을 자기 일로 삼겠지. 그 점은 의심할 만한 이유가 없단다, 비록 일이 무척 서둘러 정해진 건 인정해야겠지만 말이야. 게다가 그분은 매우 신중한 사람이니, 분명 스스로 알 거다, 두냐가 그와 함께 행복할수록 자기 자신의 행복도 더욱 든든해지리라는 것을. 그리고 성격상의 몇 가지 결함이나 어떤 버릇, 심지어 의견의 차이 같은 것은 — 사실 그런 거야 더없이 행복한 결혼에서도 피할 수 없는 거지만 — 그 모든 것에 관해서는 두냐가 자기 자신을 믿으니 불안해할 것이 없고, 두 사람의 앞날의 관계가 정직하고 떳떳하기만 하다면 많은 것을 참아 낼 각오가 되어 있다고 하더구나. 예컨대 그분은 처음에 나에게 다소 퉁명스러운 사람으로 비쳤지만, 그건 충분히 솔직한 사람이기 때문일 수도 있고, 틀림없이 그래서일 게다. 예를 들면, 두냐의 승낙을 받은 뒤 두 번째 방문 때, 이야기를 나누던 중에 그분이 이런 말을 하더구나, 두냐와 알게 되기 전부터 평판 좋고 지참금 없는, 무엇보다 가난을 겪어 본 처녀와 결혼하기로 마음먹었다고 말이야, 왜냐하면 그분 설명으로는, 남편이 아내에게 신세를 져서는 안 되고, 아내가 남편을 자기 은인으로 우러러보는 편이 낫기 때문이라는 거였단다. 덧붙여 두자면 그분은 내가 옮긴 것보다 훨씬 더 점잖고 정중하게 표현했단다, 나는 그분이 실제로 한 말은 잊어버리고 그저 그 뜻만 기억하고 있으니까. 게다가 그건 분명 일부러 한 말이 아니라 이야기에 열을 올리다 무심코 튀어나온 거였어, 그래서 나중에는 그분도 그것을 바로잡고 무마하려 애쓰긴 했지만, 그래도 나에게는 어쩐지 좀 무례하게 들렸고, 나중에 두냐에게 그렇게 말했단다. 하지만 두냐는 언짢아하면서, '말은 행동이 아니'라고 대꾸하더구나, 물론 그건 더없이 옳은 말이지. 두냐는 마음을 정하기 전 밤새도록 잠을 이루지 못했단다, 내가 잠든 줄 알고 침대에서 일어나 밤새도록 방 안을 오락가락하더니, 마침내 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 오래도록 간절히 기도드렸고, 아침에 나에게 결심했다고 말했단다.\n\n\"이미 말했다시피 표트르 페트로비치는 막 페테르부르크로 떠나려는 참이고, 그곳에 처리할 일이 무척 많아서 법률 사무소를 열려고 한단다. 그분은 여러 해 동안 민사 및 상사 소송을 맡아 왔고, 바로 며칠 전에도 중요한 소송 하나를 이겼지. 그분이 페테르부르크에 가야 하는 것은, 원로원에 걸려 있는 중요한 사건이 있어서란다. 그러니, 사랑하는 로쟈, 그분이 너에게 더없이 큰 도움이 될 수 있을 거다, 정말이지 온갖 면에서 말이야, 그래서 두냐와 나는 바로 오늘부터 네가 분명히 너의 앞길에 발을 들여놓을 수 있고, 너의 장래가 너를 위해 정해지고 보장되었다고 여겨도 된다고 의견을 모았단다. 오, 부디 이 일이 이루어진다면! 이것은 우리가 그저 하늘의 축복으로밖에 여길 수 없을 만큼 크나큰 은혜가 될 거다. 두냐는 다른 건 아무것도 꿈꾸지 않는단다. 우리는 이미 그 일에 관해 표트르 페트로비치에게 몇 마디 슬쩍 꺼내 보기까지 했어. 그분은 신중하게 대답하기를, 물론 자기는 비서 없이는 일을 해 나갈 수 없으니, 그 직무에 적합하기만 하다면 낯선 사람보다는 친척에게 봉급을 주는 편이 낫다고 하더구나(마치 네가 그 일에 적합한지 의심할 여지라도 있다는 듯이 말이야!), 하지만 그러고는 너의 대학 공부 때문에 자기 사무소에서 일할 시간이 나겠느냐는 의문을 내비쳤단다. 그 이야기는 일단 그쯤에서 그쳤지만, 두냐는 지금 다른 건 아무것도 생각하지 않는단다. 그애는 요 며칠 동안 일종의 열병에 들뜬 상태로, 네가 끝내는 표트르 페트로비치 사업의 동업자, 심지어 공동 경영자가 되도록 하는 정식 계획까지 벌써 세워 놓았어, 네가 법학도이니 충분히 가능한 일이지. 나도 그애와 전적으로 같은 생각이란다, 로쟈, 그애의 모든 계획과 희망을 함께 나누며, 그것을 실현할 가능성이 충분하다고 본다. 그리고 표트르 페트로비치의 회피하는 태도에도 불구하고 — 지금으로서는 (그분이 너를 모르니) 지극히 당연한 일이지 — 두냐는 자기가 미래의 남편에게 미칠 좋은 영향력으로 모든 것을 얻어 내리라 굳게 믿고 있단다, 그애는 그것을 믿고 있어. 물론 우리는 이런 좀 더 먼 계획들, 특히 네가 그분의 동업자가 되는 일은 표트르 페트로비치에게 입 밖에 내지 않도록 조심하고 있어. 그분은 현실적인 사람이라 이 일을 아주 냉담하게 받아들일 수도 있고, 그 모든 게 그분에게는 그저 백일몽처럼 비칠지도 모르니까. 또 두냐도 나도, 그분이 너의 대학 학비를 대 주는 일을 도와주리라는 우리의 큰 희망에 대해서도 그분에게 한마디도 비치지 않았단다. 그 이야기를 하지 않은 것은, 우선 그 일이 나중에 저절로 이루어질 것이고, 그분도 틀림없이 군말 없이 스스로 그렇게 해 주겠다고 나설 테니까(마치 그분이 두냐에게 그것을 거절할 수 있기라도 하겠니), 더구나 네가 너 자신의 노력으로 사무소에서 그분의 오른팔이 될 수 있으니, 이 도움을 자선이 아니라 네가 일해서 번 봉급으로 받게 될 테니 더욱 그렇지. 두냐는 이 모든 걸 이렇게 짜 놓고 싶어 하고, 나도 그애와 전적으로 같은 생각이란다. 우리가 이 계획들을 말하지 않은 데는 또 다른 이유도 있는데, 그건 네가 그분을 처음 만날 때 대등한 처지에 있다고 느끼기를 내가 특히 바랐기 때문이야. 두냐가 그분에게 너에 대해 열을 올리며 이야기했을 때, 그분은 어떤 사람이든 직접 가까이서 자기 눈으로 보지 않고서는 결코 판단할 수 없다고, 너와 알게 되면 자기 나름의 견해를 갖게 되기를 기대한다고 대답했단다. 그런데 있잖니, 소중한 로쟈, 나는 어쩌면 어떤 이유들로(표트르 페트로비치와는 아무 상관 없는, 그저 나 개인의, 어쩌면 늙은이다운 부질없는 생각이긴 하다만), 결혼식 후에 그들과 함께 사는 것보다 따로 떨어져 혼자 사는 편이 나을 것 같구나. 나는 그분이 너그럽고 세심한 마음으로 나를 청해서, 앞으로 딸과 함께 지내라고 권하리라 믿고, 만일 그분이 지금껏 그 말을 하지 않았다면 그저 그것이 당연한 일로 여겨졌기 때문이겠지만, 나는 사양할 작정이다. 나는 살아오면서 사위들이 장모와는 그리 잘 지내지 못하는 것을 한두 번 본 게 아니고, 나는 누구에게도 조금이라도 거치적거리는 사람이 되고 싶지 않으며, 나 자신을 위해서도, 내 빵 한 조각과 너와 두냐 같은 자식들이 있는 한, 차라리 완전히 독립해 있고 싶구나. 가능하다면 너희 가까이에 자리를 잡고 싶다, 그런데 사랑하는 로쟈, 가장 기쁜 소식은 편지 끝까지 아껴 두었단다. 그러니 알아 두렴, 사랑하는 내 아들아, 우리는 어쩌면 머지않아 다 함께 모여, 거의 삼 년 만의 이별 끝에 다시 서로를 끌어안게 될지도 모른단다! 두냐와 내가 페테르부르크로 떠나기로 분명히 정해졌단다, 정확히 언제일지는 모르지만, 아주, 아주 곧, 어쩌면 일주일 안에 말이야. 모든 것은 표트르 페트로비치에게 달렸는데, 그분이 페테르부르크에서 둘러볼 짬이 생기는 대로 우리에게 알려 줄 거란다. 그분은 자기 사정에 맞추려고 가능한 한 빨리 식을 올리고 싶어 해, 될 수만 있다면 성모 축일 단식 전이라도, 혹은 그게 준비하기에 너무 이르다면 단식이 끝나는 즉시 말이야. 오, 내가 얼마나 행복하게 너를 가슴에 끌어안게 될지! 두냐는 너를 만난다는 기쁜 생각에 온통 들떠서, 언젠가 농담 삼아 그것만을 위해서라도 표트르 페트로비치와 결혼할 수 있겠다고 하더구나. 그애는 천사란다! 그애는 지금 너에게 아무것도 써 보내지 않으면서, 그저 나더러 이렇게 전하라고만 하는구나, 너에게 할 이야기가 너무도, 너무도 많아서 지금은 펜을 들지 않겠다고, 몇 줄로는 아무것도 전할 수 없을 테고 괜히 자기 마음만 어지럽힐 뿐이라고 말이야. 그애가 너에게 사랑과 수없는 입맞춤을 보내라고 하는구나. 하지만 우리가 곧 만나게 될 텐데도, 어쩌면 하루 이틀 안에 내가 보낼 수 있는 만큼 돈을 부쳐 줄지도 모르겠다. 두냐가 표트르 페트로비치와 결혼한다는 소식을 다들 듣고 나니 내 신용이 갑자기 좋아져서, 아파나시 이바노비치가 이제는 내 연금을 담보로 칠십오 루블까지도 빌려줄 거라는 걸 알겠구나, 그러니 어쩌면 너에게 이십오 루블, 아니 삼십 루블까지도 보낼 수 있을 게다. 더 보내고 싶다만 우리 여비가 걱정이구나. 표트르 페트로비치가 친절히도 여행 경비의 일부를, 그러니까 우리의 가방과 큰 트렁크 운반을(이건 그분이 아는 사람들 편으로 보내질 거란다) 맡아 주긴 했지만, 페테르부르크에 도착하면 어느 정도 비용이 들 것을 셈해 둬야지, 적어도 처음 며칠은 한 푼도 없이 지낼 수는 없을 테니까. 하지만 두냐와 나는 마지막 한 푼까지 다 계산해 보았고, 여행에 그리 큰돈이 들지는 않으리라는 걸 알았단다. 우리 집에서 철도까지는 구십 베르스타밖에 안 되고, 우리가 아는 마부와 미리 이야기를 맞춰 두었으니까. 그리고 거기서부터는 두냐와 내가 삼등칸으로 아주 편안히 갈 수 있단다. 그러니 어쩌면 너에게 이십오 루블이 아니라 삼십 루블을 보낼 수 있을 것 같구나. 하지만 이만 줄여야겠다. 벌써 두 장을 다 채워서 더 쓸 자리가 없구나. 우리의 모든 이야기를 다 적었다만, 어쩌면 이리도 많은 일이 일어났는지! 그럼, 소중한 로쟈, 나는 너를 끌어안으며 다시 만날 때까지 어미의 축복을 보낸다. 네 누이 두냐를 사랑하렴, 로쟈, 그애가 너를 사랑하는 만큼 그애를 사랑하고, 그애가 그 무엇보다도, 자기 자신보다도 더 너를 사랑한다는 것을 헤아려 주렴. 그애는 천사고, 너는, 로쟈, 너는 우리의 전부란다 — 우리의 단 하나뿐인 희망, 단 하나뿐인 위안이지. 네가 행복하기만 하다면, 우리도 행복할 거란다. 너는 아직도 기도를 드리니, 로쟈, 그리고 우리의 창조주이자 구원자이신 분의 자비를 믿니? 나는 마음속으로 두렵구나, 요즘 세상에 퍼져 있는 그 새로운 불신의 정신이 행여 너에게 찾아오지나 않았을까. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 너를 위해 기도하마. 기억하렴, 사랑하는 아들아, 네 아버지가 살아 계시던 어린 시절에, 네가 내 무릎에 기대 혀 짧은 소리로 기도를 외던 것을, 그리고 그 시절 우리 모두가 얼마나 행복했는지를. 잘 있으렴, 그럼 다시 만날 때까지 — 나는 너를 따뜻이, 따뜻이 끌어안고, 수없이 입을 맞추마.\n\n\"죽는 날까지 너의,\n\n\"풀헤리야 라스콜니코바.\"\n편지를 읽는 거의 처음부터 라스콜니코프의 얼굴은 눈물로 젖어 있었다. 그러나 다 읽고 나자 그의 얼굴은 창백하게 일그러졌고, 입술에는 쓰디쓰고 분노에 차고 악의에 찬 미소가 어렸다. 그는 닳아빠진 더러운 베개에 머리를 누이고 골똘히, 오래도록 생각에 잠겼다. 심장이 격렬하게 뛰었고, 머릿속은 온통 소용돌이쳤다. 마침내 그는 벽장이나 상자 같은 그 작은 노란 방 안에서 갑갑하고 숨이 막혀 왔다. 그의 눈도 정신도 공간을 갈망했다. 그는 모자를 집어 들고 밖으로 나갔는데, 이번에는 누군가와 마주칠까 두려워하지도 않았다. 그는 그 두려움을 잊고 있었다. 그는 바실리옙스키 섬 쪽으로 방향을 잡아 바실리옙스키 대로를 따라 걸었다, 마치 무슨 급한 볼일이라도 보러 가듯이. 그러나 그는 늘 그렇듯 자기가 가는 길도 의식하지 못한 채, 무언가를 중얼거리고 심지어 소리 내어 혼잣말까지 하면서 걸었고, 지나가는 사람들을 어리둥절하게 했다. 그들 중 많은 이들이 그를 술 취한 사람으로 여겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n어머니의 편지는 그에게 고문이었다. 그러나 거기에 담긴 핵심적인 사실에 관해서는, 편지를 읽고 있는 동안조차 단 한순간도 망설이지 않았다. 본질적인 문제는 그의 마음속에서 이미 결판이 났고, 그것도 돌이킬 수 없게 결판이 났다. \"내가 살아 있는 한 그런 결혼은 절대 안 돼, 루진 씨 따위는 지옥에나 떨어지라지!\" \"이건 너무도 명백한 일이야.\" 그는 자기 결정의 승리를 미리 음미하듯 악의에 찬 미소를 지으며 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"안 돼, 어머니, 안 돼요, 두냐, 당신들은 나를 속일 수 없어! 게다가 내게 의논도 없이, 나를 빼놓고 결정해 버린 것을 사과하다니! 참 잘들 하셨군! 그들은 이미 다 정해졌으니 깨뜨릴 수 없다고 여기겠지. 하지만 깨뜨릴 수 있는지 없는지는 두고 봐야지! 근사한 핑계로군. '표트르 페트로비치는 워낙 바쁜 분이라 결혼식조차 부랴부랴, 거의 급행으로 치러야 할 정도다.' 아니, 두냐, 나는 다 알아, 네가 내게 무슨 말을 하고 싶은지도 알고. 그리고 밤새도록 방 안을 서성였을 때 네가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 어머니 침실에 놓인 카잔의 성모 앞에서 무슨 기도를 올렸는지도 알지. 골고다로 오르는 길은 쓰라린 법이니까……. 흠……, 그래, 결국 다 정해졌단 말이지. 너는 분별 있는 사업가와 결혼하기로 마음을 굳혔구나, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 재산이 있는 자(그것도 이미 재산을 모은 자이니, 그게 훨씬 더 견실하고 인상적이지), 두 개의 관직을 차지하고, 어머니가 쓴 대로 우리의 가장 떠오르는 세대의 사상을 공유하며, 두냐 자신이 관찰한 대로 친절해 보이는 자와. 그 보인다는 게 무엇보다 압권이로군! 게다가 바로 그 두냐가 바로 그 보인다라는 것 때문에 그와 결혼한다니! 훌륭하군! 훌륭해!\n\n\"……하지만 나는 어머니가 왜 '우리의 가장 떠오르는 세대'에 관해 편지에 써 보냈는지 알고 싶군. 그저 묘사적인 곁들임일까, 아니면 나를 루진 씨 편으로 호감을 갖게 하려는 속셈일까? 오, 저들의 교활함이라니! 한 가지 더 알고 싶은 게 있어. 그날 밤낮 동안, 그리고 그 후로 지금껏 그들이 서로에게 얼마나 솔직했을까? 그 모든 게 말로 표현되었을까, 아니면 두 사람 다 마음속에 같은 것을 품고 있음을 알아채고, 그래서 굳이 입 밖에 낼 필요가 없었고, 오히려 입 밖에 내지 않는 편이 나았던 걸까. 십중팔구 어느 정도는 그랬을 거야, 어머니 편지를 보면 분명해. 그는 어머니에게 약간 무례하게 느껴졌고, 어머니는 그 순박함에 자기 생각을 두냐에게 털어놓은 거지. 그러자 두냐는 분명 언짢아져서 '화를 내며 대답했을' 거야. 당연하지! 천진한 질문 따위 없이도 모든 게 명백한데, 그걸 따져 봐야 소용없다는 걸 다 알고 있는데, 누군들 화가 나지 않겠어. 그런데 어머니는 왜 내게 '두냐를 사랑해 다오, 로쟈, 그애는 너를 자기 자신보다 더 사랑한단다'라고 쓴 걸까? 아들을 위해 딸을 희생시키는 것에 대해 양심의 가책이라도 은근히 느끼는 걸까? '너는 우리의 유일한 위안이고, 너는 우리의 전부란다.' 오, 어머니!\"\n\n그의 쓰라림은 점점 더 격렬해졌고, 만약 그 순간 루진 씨와 마주쳤더라면, 그를 죽여 버렸을지도 모른다.\n\n\"흠……, 그래, 맞는 말이지.\" 그는 머릿속에서 꼬리를 물고 빙빙 도는 생각들을 좇으며 말을 이었다. \"'사람을 제대로 알려면 시간과 정성이 필요하다'는 건 맞는 말이지만, 루진 씨에 관해서라면 오해의 여지가 없어. 가장 중요한 건 그가 '사업가이고 친절해 보인다'는 건데, 그들을 위해 가방과 큰 짐 상자를 보냈으니, 그것도 대단한 일 아닌가! 그러고 보니 친절한 사람이 틀림없겠군! 하지만 그의 신부와 신부의 어머니는 거적때기를 덮은 농부의 짐마차를 타고 가야 하지(나도 알아, 나도 그걸 타 봤으니까). 상관없어! 고작 구십 베르스타니까, 그다음엔 천 베르스타를 '아주 편안하게, 삼등칸으로' 여행할 수 있을 테니. 그것도 지당한 말이지. 형편에 맞춰 살아야 하는 법이니까. 하지만 당신은 어떻소, 루진 씨? 그녀는 당신의 신부 아니오……. 그리고 당신은 그녀의 어머니가 여행 경비를 마련하려고 연금을 담보로 돈을 빌려야 한다는 걸 알고 있을 텐데. 하긴 그건 사업의 문제, 서로의 이익을 위한 동업이지, 똑같은 지분에 똑같은 비용으로. 먹고 마시는 건 제공되지만 담배는 각자 부담하시오. 그 사업가는 그들에게서도 잇속을 챙긴 거야. 짐 운반비가 그들의 차비보다 적을 테고, 어쩌면 공짜로 갈지도 모르지. 어째서 그들은 둘 다 그걸 못 보는 걸까, 아니면 보고 싶지 않은 걸까? 게다가 만족하고 있어, 만족하고 있다고! 그런데 이건 겨우 첫 봉오리에 불과하고, 진짜 열매는 아직 오지 않았다는 걸 생각하면! 하지만 정말로 문제가 되는 건 인색함도, 쩨쩨함도 아니야. 그 모든 것의 어조야. 그게 바로 결혼 후의 어조가 될 테니까, 그건 그 맛보기인 거지. 어머니도 그래, 어째서 그렇게 헤퍼야 하지? 페테르부르크에 도착할 무렵엔 무엇이 남을까? 은화 삼 루블, 아니면 어머니 말마따나 '지폐로' 두 장……, 그 늙은 어머니가……, 흠. 그 후에 페테르부르크에서 무엇으로 살아갈 작정인 거지? 어머니는 이미, 결혼 후에는 단 몇 달이라도 두냐와 함께 살 수 없으리라는 걸 짐작할 만한 이유가 있어. 그 훌륭한 양반이 그 문제에 관해서도 분명 무슨 말을 흘렸을 테지, 어머니야 부인하겠지만. '나는 사양하마.' 어머니는 그렇게 말하지. 그렇다면 누구를 믿고 있는 걸까? 아파나시 이바노비치의 빚을 갚고 나서 백이십 루블의 연금에서 남은 것에 기대고 있는 걸까? 어머니는 늙은 눈을 망쳐 가며 모직 숄을 뜨고 토시에 수를 놓지. 그런데 그 모든 숄을 다 합쳐도 백이십 루블에 일 년 이십 루블 이상은 보태지 못한다는 걸, 나는 알아. 그러니까 어머니는 줄곧 루진 씨의 너그러움에 모든 희망을 걸고 있는 거야. '그가 스스로 내놓을 거다, 그가 내게 권할 거다'라면서. 그걸 기다리려면 오래 기다려야 할걸! 그 실러풍의 고결한 마음들은 늘 그런 식이지. 마지막 순간까지 모든 거위가 백조로 보이고, 마지막 순간까지 최선을 바라며 아무 잘못도 보려 하지 않아. 그림의 다른 면을 어렴풋이 눈치채면서도, 억지로 떠밀리기 전까지는 결코 진실을 마주하려 하지 않지. 그 생각만 해도 몸서리를 치고, 두 손으로 진실을 밀어내. 거짓 빛깔로 치장해 준 그 남자가 제 손으로 그들 머리에 바보의 고깔모자를 씌워 줄 때까지. 루진 씨에게 무슨 훈장이라도 있는지 알고 싶군. 단춧구멍에 안나 훈장을 달고 있을 거라고 장담하지, 그리고 도급업자나 상인들과 식사하러 갈 때면 그걸 달고 가겠지. 결혼식에도 틀림없이 그걸 달 거야! 그 작자 얘긴 이쯤 하지, 빌어먹을!\n\n\"뭐……, 어머니야 놀랄 것도 없어, 어머니다운 일이니까, 신의 가호가 있기를. 하지만 두냐가 어떻게? 두냐, 사랑하는 두냐, 마치 내가 너를 모르는 것처럼! 마지막으로 너를 봤을 때 너는 스물이 다 되어 가고 있었지. 그때 나는 너를 이해했어. 어머니는 '두냐는 많은 걸 견딜 수 있다'고 쓰셨지. 나도 그건 아주 잘 알아. 이 년 반 전에도 알고 있었고, 지난 이 년 반 동안 줄곧 그것에 대해, 바로 그것에 대해 생각해 왔어. '두냐는 많은 걸 견딜 수 있다'는 것을. 스비드리가일로프 씨와 그 모든 일을 견뎌 낼 수 있었다면, 분명 많은 걸 견딜 수 있겠지. 그런데 지금 어머니와 그애는, 그애가 루진 씨를 견딜 수 있다고 머릿속에 단단히 박아 넣은 거야. 빈곤에서 끌어올려져 남편의 은혜에 모든 걸 빚진 아내가 우월하다는 이론을 내세우는 자를. 그것도 거의 첫 만남에서부터 그 이론을 늘어놓는 자를. 그가 그걸 '무심코 흘렸다'고 치자, 분별 있는 사람이긴 하지만(어쩌면 무심코 흘린 게 전혀 아니라, 가능한 한 빨리 제 뜻을 분명히 해 두려는 거였는지도 몰라). 하지만 두냐는, 두냐는? 물론 그애는 그 인간을 꿰뚫어 보지, 그러나 그 인간과 함께 살아야 하잖아. 아니! 그애라면 검은 빵과 물만 먹고 살지언정 제 영혼을 팔지는 않을 거야, 안락을 위해 제 도덕적 자유를 거래하지는 않을 거라고. 슐레스비히홀슈타인 전부를 준대도 그애는 그걸 팔지 않을 텐데, 하물며 루진 씨의 돈 따위로야. 아니, 내가 알던 두냐는 그런 부류가 아니었고……, 물론 지금도 여전히 그대로일 테지! 그래, 부정할 수 없어, 스비드리가일로프 집안은 쓰디쓴 알약이지! 시골에서 이백 루블에 가정교사로 평생을 보내는 건 쓰라린 일이지만, 그래도 나는 알아. 그애는 존경하지도 않고 아무 공통점도 없는 남자에게 제 이익을 위해 영원히 매이느니, 차라리 농장의 흑인 노예가 되거나 독일인 주인을 둔 라트비아인이 되는 편을 택할 거야, 제 영혼과 도덕적 존엄을 더럽히기보다는. 설령 루진 씨가 순금 덩어리였다 해도, 거대한 다이아몬드 하나였다 해도, 그애는 결코 그의 합법적인 첩이 되는 데 동의하지 않았을 거야. 그렇다면 왜 동의하는 거지? 그 의미가 뭐야? 그 답이 뭐냐고? 충분히 명백해. 자기 자신을 위해서라면, 자기 안락을 위해서라면 그애는 목숨을 구하기 위해서라도 자신을 팔지 않을 거야. 그러나 다른 누군가를 위해서라면 그렇게 하는 거지! 사랑하는 사람을 위해, 흠모하는 사람을 위해서라면, 그애는 자신을 팔 거야! 그게 결국 전부야. 제 오라비를 위해, 제 어머니를 위해, 그애는 자신을 파는 거지! 그애는 모든 걸 팔 거야! 그런 경우엔 '필요하다면 우리의 도덕적 감정도 극복한다'면서, 자유도, 평안도, 양심마저도, 전부, 전부 시장에 내놓는 거야. 사랑하는 이들이 행복할 수만 있다면 내 인생쯤 내던져도 좋아! 그뿐인가, 우리는 궤변가가 되고, 예수회 신자처럼 둘러대는 법을 배우고, 한동안은 어쩌면 스스로를 달랠 수 있을지도 모르지, 좋은 목적을 위한 의무라고 자신을 설득할 수 있을지도. 그게 딱 우리다운 거야, 대낮처럼 명백한 일이지. 이 일의 중심인물이 로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프이고 다른 누구도 아니라는 게 명백해. 오, 그래, 그애는 그의 행복을 보장하고, 그를 대학에 계속 다니게 하고, 그를 사무소의 동업자로 만들고, 그의 미래 전체를 안전하게 해 줄 수 있겠지. 어쩌면 그는 나중에 부자가 될지도 몰라, 번창하고, 존경받고, 어쩌면 유명인사로 생을 마칠지도 모르지! 그런데 우리 어머니는? 다 로쟈를 위해서야, 소중한 로쟈, 첫아들을! 그런 아들을 위해서라면 누군들 그런 딸을 희생시키지 않겠어! 오, 사랑이 넘치다 못해 편애하는 마음들이여! 아무렴, 그를 위해서라면 우리는 소냐의 운명조차 마다하지 않을 거야. 소냐, 소냐 마르멜라도프, 세상이 존속하는 한 영원한 희생자. 너희 둘 다, 너희의 희생을 가늠해 보기는 한 거니? 그게 옳은 일이야? 너희가 견딜 수 있어? 무슨 소용이 있어? 거기에 분별이 있긴 한 거니? 그리고 내가 말해 두마, 두냐, 소냐의 삶이 루진 씨와의 삶보다 나쁠 게 없어. '사랑은 문제가 될 수 없다'고 어머니는 쓰셨지. 그런데 만약 존경마저 있을 수 없다면, 오히려 혐오와 경멸과 거부감만 있다면, 그땐 어쩔 거야? 그러니 너 역시 '겉모습을 유지해야' 할 거야. 그렇지 않아? 그 말쑥함이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알겠어? 루진식 말쑥함이 소냐의 것과 똑같은 것이며, 어쩌면 더 나쁘고, 더 비열하고, 더 천박할 수 있다는 걸 알겠어? 왜냐하면, 두냐, 네 경우엔 결국 사치를 위한 거래지만, 소냐의 경우엔 그저 굶주림의 문제일 뿐이니까. 대가를 치러야 해, 대가를 치러야 한다고, 두냐, 그 말쑥함의. 그리고 만약 나중에 그게 네가 견딜 수 있는 것 이상이라면, 만약 후회하게 된다면 어쩌지? 그 쓰라림, 그 비참함, 그 저주, 온 세상에 숨긴 눈물, 너는 마르파 페트로브나가 아니니까. 그럼 그때 어머니는 어떤 심정일까? 지금조차도 불안해하고, 걱정하는데, 그때, 모든 걸 또렷이 보게 된다면? 그리고 나는? 그래, 정말로, 너희는 나를 어떤 인간으로 본 거야? 나는 네 희생을 받아들이지 않겠어, 두냐, 받아들이지 않겠어요, 어머니! 그렇게는 안 돼, 내가 살아 있는 한 그렇게는 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 된다고! 나는 그걸 받아들이지 않겠어!\"\n\n그는 갑자기 사색을 멈추고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"그렇게는 안 된다고? 그런데 그걸 막으려고 네가 뭘 할 건데? 금지하기라도 할 거야? 무슨 권리로? 그런 권리를 갖게 하려고 네가 그들에게 무엇을 약속할 수 있는데? 네 인생 전부, 네 미래 전부를, 학업을 마치고 일자리를 얻은 다음에 그들에게 바치겠다고? 그래, 그런 말은 전에도 다 들어 봤어, 그건 죄다 말뿐이고, 그럼 지금은? 지금 당장 뭔가를 해야 해, 지금, 그걸 알겠어? 그런데 지금 너는 뭘 하고 있지? 너는 그들에게 얹혀살고 있어. 그들은 백 루블 연금을 담보로 돈을 빌리고 있어. 스비드리가일로프 집안에서 빌리고 있다고. 너는 그들을 스비드리가일로프 집안으로부터, 아파나시 이바노비치 바흐루신으로부터 어떻게 구해 낼 작정이지, 오, 그들의 삶을 대신 꾸려 줄 미래의 백만장자 제우스여? 앞으로 십 년 뒤에? 십 년 뒤면 어머니는 숄을 뜨다가 눈이 멀어 있을 거야, 어쩌면 울다가도. 단식하느라 그림자처럼 말라 있겠지. 그럼 내 누이는? 잠깐 상상해 봐, 십 년 후에 네 누이가 어떻게 되어 있을지를? 그 십 년 동안 그애에게 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있을지를? 짐작이나 할 수 있어?\"\n\n그리하여 그는 그런 질문들로 자신을 안달하게 하며 스스로를 고문했고, 거기서 일종의 쾌감마저 느꼈다. 그러면서도 이 모든 질문은 갑자기 그 앞에 들이닥친 새로운 것들이 아니라, 오래되고 낯익은 쓰라림이었다. 그것들이 처음으로 그의 마음을 움켜쥐고 찢기 시작한 것은 오래전부터였다. 오래, 아주 오래전에 지금의 이 고뇌는 처음 싹텄고, 점점 자라 힘을 모으고, 무르익고 응축되어, 마침내 그의 마음과 머리를 고문하며 집요하게 답을 요구하는, 무섭고 광적이며 환상적인 질문의 형태를 띠게 된 것이었다. 그런데 이제 어머니의 편지가 청천벽력처럼 그에게 들이닥쳤다. 분명한 건, 그가 이제 풀리지 않은 질문들에 안달하며 수동적으로 괴로워해서는 안 되고, 무언가를 해야 한다는 것, 그것도 당장, 빨리 해야 한다는 것이었다. 어쨌든 그는 무언가를 결정해야 했다, 그렇지 않으면…….\n\n\"아니면 인생을 통째로 내던지든가!\" 그는 광기에 휩싸여 불쑥 외쳤다. \"있는 그대로의 운명을 단번에, 영원히 겸허하게 받아들이고, 자기 안의 모든 걸 짓눌러 죽이고, 활동도 삶도 사랑도 모든 권리를 단념하든가!\"\n\n\"당신은 아십니까, 선생, 정말 갈 곳이 아무 데도 없다는 게 무슨 뜻인지 아십니까?\" 마르멜라도프의 질문이 불현듯 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. \"왜냐하면 모든 인간은 어딘가 갈 곳이 있어야 하니까요…….\"\n\n그는 갑자기 흠칫했다. 어제 했던 또 다른 생각 하나가 다시 그의 머릿속으로 슬며시 미끄러져 들어왔다. 그러나 그 생각이 되살아난 것에 흠칫한 것은 아니었으니, 그것이 반드시 돌아오리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 미리 느끼고 있었으며, 그것을 기다리고 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 그건 단지 어제의 생각만도 아니었다. 다른 점이 있다면, 한 달 전만 해도, 아니 바로 어제만 해도, 그 생각은 한낱 공상에 지나지 않았다는 것이다. 그러나 지금……, 지금 그것은 전혀 공상이 아닌 것처럼 보였고, 새롭고 위협적이며 너무도 낯선 형태를 띠게 되었으며, 그는 갑자기 그것을 스스로 자각하게 되었다……. 그는 머릿속에서 쾅쾅 두드리는 소리를 느꼈고, 눈앞이 캄캄해졌다.\n\n그는 황급히 주위를 둘러보았다. 무언가를 찾고 있었다. 앉고 싶어서 자리를 찾고 있었던 것이다. 그는 K 가로수 길을 따라 걷고 있었다. 그의 앞 백 걸음쯤 되는 곳에 벤치 하나가 있었다. 그는 할 수 있는 한 빨리 그쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그러나 가는 도중에 그의 온 주의를 사로잡는 작은 사건과 마주쳤다. 벤치를 찾던 중에 그는 자기 앞 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳을 걷고 있는 한 여자를 알아챘는데, 처음에는 길을 가로지르는 다른 물체들보다 더 눈여겨보지는 않았다. 집으로 돌아가면서 자신이 가고 있는 길을 알아채지 못한 적이 그에게는 여러 번 있었고, 그는 그런 식으로 걷는 데 익숙했다. 그러나 그의 앞에 있는 여자에게는 첫눈에 무언가 너무도 이상한 데가 있어서, 차츰 그의 주의가 그녀에게 못 박혔으니, 처음에는 마지못해, 말하자면 못마땅한 심정으로, 그러다 점점 더 골똘히. 그는 그 여자에게 그토록 이상한 점이 무엇인지 알아내고 싶은 욕구를 불현듯 느꼈다. 우선 그녀는 아주 젊은 처녀로 보였는데, 이 무더위 속에 모자도 쓰지 않고 양산도 장갑도 없이, 우스꽝스럽게 팔을 휘저으며 걷고 있었다. 그녀는 얇고 비단 같은 천으로 만든 드레스를 입었으나, 이상하게 비뚤어지게 걸쳐 입어 단추도 제대로 채워지지 않았고, 허리께 치맛자락 윗부분이 찢어져 벌어져 있었다. 커다란 천 조각이 뜯겨 너덜너덜 늘어져 있었다. 드러난 목에는 작은 손수건이 둘러져 있었으나 한쪽으로 비뚜름하게 걸쳐져 있었다. 게다가 처녀는 비틀비틀, 발을 헛디디고 좌우로 휘청거리며 걷고 있었다. 마침내 그녀는 라스콜니코프의 온 주의를 끌었다. 그는 벤치 근처에서 그녀를 따라잡았는데, 거기에 다다르자 그녀는 벤치 구석에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그녀는 고개를 등받이에 떨어뜨리고 눈을 감았는데, 보아하니 극도로 지친 모양이었다. 가까이서 그녀를 들여다본 그는, 그녀가 완전히 취해 있다는 것을 단번에 알아챘다. 그것은 이상하고도 충격적인 광경이었다. 그는 자신이 잘못 본 게 아니라는 사실을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 그의 눈앞에는 아주 젊고 금발인 처녀의 얼굴이 있었으니, 열여섯, 어쩌면 열다섯밖에 안 되어 보였고, 예쁘장한 작은 얼굴이었으나 발갛게 상기되고 둔해 보이며, 말하자면 부어 있었다. 처녀는 자신이 무얼 하고 있는지 거의 모르는 듯했다. 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 무례하게 들어 올려 포개고는, 자신이 거리에 있다는 것을 전혀 의식하지 못하는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 앉지 않았으나, 그녀를 떠날 마음도 내키지 않아, 당혹스러운 채로 그녀를 마주하고 서 있었다. 이 가로수 길은 늘 사람이 그리 많지 않았는데, 지금, 두 시, 숨 막히는 더위 속에서는 거의 인적이 끊겨 있었다. 그런데도 가로수 길 저편, 한 열다섯 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에 한 신사가 보도 가장자리에 서 있었다. 그 역시 보아하니 무언가 제 나름의 속셈을 품고 그 처녀에게 다가가고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 그 역시 멀리서 그녀를 보고 뒤따라왔을 테지만, 라스콜니코프가 길을 가로막고 있는 걸 알게 된 것이다. 그는 라스콜니코프의 눈에 띄지 않으려 애쓰면서도 화가 난 눈으로 그를 노려보았고, 이 달갑잖은 누더기 차림의 사내가 비켜 주기만을 초조하게 때를 기다리며 서 있었다. 그의 의도는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그 신사는 서른쯤 된, 통통하고 다부진 체격의 사내로, 유행에 맞춰 차려입었으며, 혈색이 좋고 입술이 붉으며 콧수염을 길렀다. 라스콜니코프는 격분했다. 그는 이 뚱뚱한 멋쟁이를 어떻게든 모욕해 주고 싶은 갑작스러운 충동을 느꼈다. 그는 잠시 처녀를 두고 그 신사에게로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"이봐! 당신 스비드리가일로프! 여기서 뭘 원하는 거요?\" 그는 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 웃어 대며, 분노에 침을 튀기며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 소리요?\" 신사가 거만한 놀라움에 얼굴을 찌푸리며 엄하게 물었다.\n\n\"꺼지란 말이오, 그게 내 말이오.\"\n\n\"감히, 이 천한 놈이!\"\n\n그는 지팡이를 치켜들었다. 라스콜니코프는, 그 건장한 신사가 자기 같은 사내 둘은 너끈히 상대할 만하다는 것도 헤아리지 못한 채 주먹을 휘두르며 그에게 달려들었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 누군가가 뒤에서 그를 붙들었고, 한 순경이 두 사람 사이에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"이만하면 됐소, 신사 양반들, 공공장소에서 싸움질은 하지 마시오, 제발. 무슨 일이오? 당신은 누구요?\" 그는 라스콜니코프의 누더기를 눈여겨보며 엄하게 물었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 그를 골똘히 바라보았다. 그는 솔직하고 분별 있는 군인다운 얼굴에, 희끗한 콧수염과 구레나룻을 기르고 있었다.\n\n\"당신이야말로 내가 찾던 사람이오.\" 라스콜니코프가 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. \"나는 대학생이오, 라스콜니코프라고 하지요……. 당신도 알아 두는 게 좋겠소.\" 그가 신사를 향해 덧붙였다. \"이리 와 보시오, 당신에게 보여 줄 게 있소.\"\n\n그러고는 순경의 손을 잡아 벤치 쪽으로 끌고 갔다.\n\n\"여기 좀 보시오, 가망 없이 취해 있고, 방금 가로수 길을 걸어 내려왔소. 이 여자가 누구인지, 어떤 사람인지는 알 길이 없지만, 직업적인 여자로는 보이지 않소. 어딘가에서 술을 먹이고 속였을 가능성이 더 크지……, 처음으로……, 무슨 말인지 알겠소? 그러고는 이렇게 거리로 내쫓은 거요. 드레스가 어떻게 찢겨 있는지, 어떻게 입혀져 있는지 좀 보시오. 누군가가 그녀에게 옷을 입힌 거지, 스스로 입은 게 아니오. 그것도 서툰 손으로, 남자의 손으로 입힌 거요. 그게 분명하오. 그리고 이제 저쪽을 보시오. 내가 싸우려던 저 멋쟁이는 모르는 자고, 처음 보는 작자지만, 그도 방금 길에서, 취해서 자기가 뭘 하는지도 모르는 이 여자를 본 거요. 그러고는 이런 상태의 그녀를 어떻게든 붙잡아 어딘가로 데려가려고 안달이 난 거지……. 그건 확실하오, 내 말을 믿으시오, 나는 틀리지 않았소. 그가 그녀를 지켜보고 뒤따르는 걸 내 눈으로 봤고, 내가 막은 거요. 그래서 그는 내가 비켜 주기만을 기다리고 있는 거지. 지금 조금 물러나서 가만히 서서, 담배라도 마는 척하고 있군……. 어떻게 하면 그녀를 그의 손아귀에서 지키고, 또 어떻게 집으로 데려갈 수 있을지 생각해 봅시다.\"\n\n순경은 단번에 모든 걸 알아챘다. 그 뚱뚱한 신사야 이해하기 쉬웠고, 그는 처녀를 살피려 몸을 돌렸다. 순경은 그녀를 좀 더 가까이 들여다보려 허리를 굽혔고, 그의 얼굴이 진정한 연민으로 일그러졌다.\n\n\"아, 가엾어라!\" 그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아니, 아직 애나 다름없잖아! 속은 거야, 한눈에 알 수 있군. 이봐요, 아가씨.\" 그가 그녀에게 말을 걸기 시작했다. \"어디 사시오?\" 처녀는 지치고 졸린 듯한 눈을 떠서 말하는 이를 멍하니 바라보고는 손을 내저었다.\n\n\"여기.\" 라스콜니코프가 주머니를 뒤져 이십 코페이카를 찾아내며 말했다. \"여기, 삯마차를 불러서 이 여자를 집까지 데려다 달라고 하시오. 다만 그녀의 주소를 알아내는 게 문제로군!\"\n\n\"아가씨, 아가씨!\" 순경이 돈을 받아 들고 다시 말을 걸기 시작했다. \"마차를 불러서 내가 직접 집까지 데려다주리다. 어디로 데려가면 되겠소, 응? 어디 사시오?\"\n\n\"저리 가요! 나를 가만 안 둬.\" 처녀가 중얼거리며 또 한 번 손을 내저었다.\n\n\"아아, 아아, 어찌나 망측한지! 부끄러운 일이오, 아가씨, 부끄러운 일이야!\" 그는 충격을 받고, 가엾어하면서도 분개하여 다시금 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"이건 어려운 일이군.\" 순경이 라스콜니코프에게 말하면서, 그러는 동안 재빠른 눈길로 그를 위아래로 훑어보았다. 그에게도 라스콜니코프는 분명 이상한 인물로 보였을 것이다. 누더기를 걸친 채 자신에게 돈을 건네다니!\n\n\"이 여자를 여기서 먼 데서 만났소?\" 그가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"내 말했잖소, 내 앞에서 걷고 있었다고, 비틀거리면서, 바로 여기, 가로수 길에서. 그녀는 겨우 이 벤치까지 와서는 여기 털썩 주저앉았소.\"\n\n\"아, 요즘 세상에 벌어지는 망측한 일들이라니, 하느님, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서! 저런 순진한 것이 벌써 취하다니! 속은 거야, 그건 틀림없어. 드레스가 어떻게 찢겼는지도 좀 보게……. 아, 요즘 보이는 이 타락이라니! 게다가 십중팔구 양갓집 출신일 것 같단 말이야, 어쩌면 가난한 집의……. 요즘엔 그런 경우가 많지. 보아하니 기품도 있어, 마치 귀한 집 아가씨 같단 말이야.\" 그러고는 다시 한 번 그녀에게 허리를 굽혔다.\n\n어쩌면 그에게도 그렇게 자라나는 딸들이, \"귀한 집 아가씨처럼 기품 있어 보이는\", 고상함과 말쑥함에 대한 허세를 품은 딸들이 있는지도 몰랐다…….\n\n\"중요한 건 말이오,\" 라스콜니코프가 고집스레 말했다. \"저 악당의 손아귀에서 그녀를 지키는 거요! 어째서 그가 그녀를 능욕하게 둔단 말이오? 그가 무얼 노리는지는 대낮처럼 명백하지. 아, 저 짐승 같은 놈, 떠날 생각을 안 하는군!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 큰 소리로 말하며 그를 가리켰다. 신사는 그 말을 듣고 또다시 발끈 화를 낼 듯하더니, 마음을 고쳐먹고는 경멸하는 눈길 하나로 그쳤다. 그러고는 천천히 열 걸음쯤 더 멀어졌다가 다시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"그의 손아귀에서 그녀를 지킬 수는 있소.\" 순경이 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"그녀가 어디로 데려가야 할지 말해 주기만 한다면 말이오, 하지만 이대로는……. 아가씨, 이봐요, 아가씨!\" 그는 다시 한 번 그녀에게 허리를 굽혔다.\n\n그녀는 갑자기 눈을 활짝 뜨더니, 무언가를 깨달은 듯 그를 골똘히 바라보다가, 벤치에서 일어나 자기가 왔던 방향으로 걸어가 버렸다. \"오, 부끄러운 줄도 모르는 작자들, 나를 가만 안 둬!\" 그녀가 다시 손을 내저으며 말했다. 그녀는 여전히 비틀거리면서도 빠르게 걸었다. 멋쟁이는 그녀를 뒤따랐으나, 다른 길을 따라가며 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"걱정 마시오, 그자가 저 여자를 차지하게 두진 않을 테니.\" 순경이 단호하게 말하고는 그들을 뒤쫓아 출발했다.\n\n\"아, 요즘 보이는 이 타락이라니!\" 그가 한숨을 쉬며 큰 소리로 되뇌었다.\n\n바로 그 순간 무언가가 라스콜니코프를 콕 찌르는 것 같았다. 일순간 그에게 완전한 감정의 역전이 밀려왔다.\n\n\"이봐, 거기!\" 그가 순경의 등 뒤로 소리쳤다.\n\n순경이 돌아보았다.\n\n\"내버려 두시오! 그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오? 그녀를 가게 두라고! 그가 즐기게 내버려 둬.\" 그가 멋쟁이를 가리켰다. \"그게 당신과 무슨 상관이냔 말이오?\"\n\n순경은 어리둥절하여 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 그를 빤히 바라보았다. 라스콜니코프는 웃었다.\n\n\"원, 참!\" 순경은 경멸하는 몸짓과 함께 내뱉고는, 멋쟁이와 처녀를 뒤쫓아 걸어갔다. 아마도 라스콜니코프를 미친 사람이거나, 어쩌면 그보다 더한 무엇으로 여기는 모양이었다.\n\n\"내 이십 코페이카를 가져가 버렸군.\" 혼자 남게 되자 라스콜니코프가 화가 나서 중얼거렸다. \"흥, 저쪽 작자한테서도 그만큼 받아 챙기고서 그자가 처녀를 차지하게 두라지, 그렇게 끝내 버리라고. 그런데 내가 왜 끼어들고 싶어 했지? 내가 도울 처지인가? 내게 도울 권리가 있기나 해? 저들끼리 산 채로 서로 잡아먹게 두지, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이야? 어떻게 감히 그에게 이십 코페이카를 줬을까? 그게 내 돈이었던가?\"\n\n그 이상한 말들에도 불구하고 그는 몹시 비참한 기분이 들었다. 그는 텅 빈 벤치에 앉았다. 그의 생각은 정처 없이 헤매었다……. 그 순간 그는 무엇에든 마음을 붙들어 매기가 힘들었다. 그는 자기 자신을 완전히 잊고, 모든 걸 잊은 다음, 깨어나 새로운 삶을 시작하고 싶었다…….\n\n\"가엾은 처녀!\" 그녀가 앉아 있던 텅 빈 구석을 바라보며 그가 말했다. \"정신을 차리면 울겠지, 그러면 어머니가 알게 될 테고……. 어머니는 그애를 때릴 거야, 끔찍하고 망측하게 두들겨 패겠지, 그러고는 어쩌면 문밖으로 내쫓을지도 몰라……. 설령 내쫓지 않더라도, 다리야 프란초브나 같은 여자들이 낌새를 챌 테고, 그러면 그 처녀는 곧 이리저리 몰래 빠져나다니기 시작하겠지. 그러고는 곧장 병원행이고(점잖은 어머니를 둔, 몰래 빗나가는 그런 처녀들의 운명이란 늘 그렇지), 그러고는……, 또 병원……, 술……, 선술집……, 그리고 또 병원, 이삼 년 안에 폐인이 되어, 열여덟이나 열아홉에 인생이 끝나 버리는 거야……. 내가 그런 경우를 못 봤던가? 그애들이 어쩌다 그 지경이 됐냐고? 그래, 다들 그런 식으로 그 지경이 된 거지. 윽! 하지만 그게 무슨 상관이야? 그게 마땅한 거라고들 하지. 매년 일정한 퍼센트가, 그렇게들 말하더군, 그쪽으로……, 악마에게로 가야 한다고, 아마 그래야 나머지가 순결하게 남아, 방해받지 않을 수 있다는 거겠지. 퍼센트라! 저들은 참 근사한 말들을 가졌단 말이야. 어찌나 과학적이고, 어찌나 위안이 되는지……. 일단 '퍼센트'라고 말해 버리면 더는 걱정할 게 없어지지. 만약 다른 말이었다면……, 어쩌면 우리는 좀 더 불안했을지도 몰라……. 그런데 만약 두냐가 그 퍼센트의 하나라면! 그 하나가 아니라면 또 다른 하나라면?\"\n\n\"그런데 나는 어디로 가고 있는 거지?\" 그가 문득 생각했다. \"이상하군, 나는 무언가 때문에 나왔는데. 편지를 읽자마자 나는 밖으로 나왔어……. 바실리옙스키 섬으로, 라주미힌에게 가려던 거였지. 그래, 바로 그거였어……, 이제야 기억나는군. 그런데 무엇 때문이지? 게다가 하필 지금 라주미힌에게 가야겠다는 생각이 어쩌다 내 머릿속에 떠오른 거지? 그것참 묘하군.\"\n\n그는 스스로에게 놀랐다. 라주미힌은 대학 시절의 옛 동료들 가운데 하나였다. 라스콜니코프가 대학에 친구라고는 거의 없었다는 점은 주목할 만했다. 그는 모두에게서 거리를 두었고, 아무도 찾아가지 않았으며, 누가 자기를 찾아와도 반기지 않았으니, 사실 모두가 곧 그를 단념해 버렸다. 그는 학생들의 모임이나 오락, 대화에 끼지 않았다. 그는 자신을 아끼지 않고 대단한 열정으로 공부했고, 그 점에서 존경을 받았으나, 아무도 그를 좋아하지는 않았다. 그는 몹시 가난했고, 무언가를 혼자 간직하고 있기라도 한 듯, 일종의 오만한 자존심과 과묵함을 지니고 있었다. 몇몇 동료에게 그는, 발달이나 지식, 신념에서 자신이 우월하기라도 한 듯, 그들의 믿음과 관심사가 자기보다 한참 아래에 있기라도 한 듯, 그들 모두를 어린애처럼 내려다보는 사람으로 비쳤다.\n\n라주미힌과는 사이가 잘 맞았다, 아니, 적어도 그에게만큼은 더 거리낌 없고 더 흉금을 털어놓았다. 사실 라주미힌과는 다른 식의 관계를 맺는 것이 불가능했다. 그는 유난히 쾌활하고 솔직한 청년으로, 순박할 만큼 선량했는데, 그 순박함 아래에는 깊이와 위엄이 감추어져 있었다. 동료들 중 나은 자들은 이 점을 알았고, 모두가 그를 좋아했다. 그는 대단히 영리했지만, 때로는 확실히 다소 어수룩하기도 했다. 그는 눈에 띄는 외모를 지녔으니, 키가 크고 마른 데다 머리칼이 검고 늘 면도가 엉성했다. 그는 이따금 떠들썩하게 굴었고, 굉장한 완력의 소유자로 정평이 나 있었다. 어느 날 밤, 흥겨운 무리와 어울려 나갔다가, 그는 단 한 방에 거인 같은 순경 하나를 나가떨어지게 한 적도 있었다. 그의 주량에는 한계가 없었으나, 술을 아예 끊을 수도 있었다. 이따금 장난이 지나칠 때도 있었으나, 장난 따위 아예 안 할 수도 있었다. 라주미힌의 또 한 가지 두드러진 점은, 어떤 실패도 그를 괴롭히지 못했고, 어떤 불리한 처지도 그를 짓누르지 못할 듯하다는 것이었다. 그는 어디서든 묵을 수 있었고, 극심한 추위와 굶주림도 견딜 수 있었다. 그는 몹시 가난했고, 이런저런 일로 벌어들이는 것만으로 온전히 자기 생계를 꾸렸다. 그는 돈을 벌 수단이라면 끝도 없이 알고 있었다. 한번은 겨우내 난로에 불 한 번 때지 않고 지냈는데, 추운 데서 잠이 더 잘 온다며 그편이 더 좋다고 단언하곤 했다. 지금으로서는 그 역시 대학을 그만둘 수밖에 없었으나, 그건 잠시일 뿐이었고, 그는 다시 학업으로 돌아가기 위한 돈을 마련하려 온 힘을 다해 일하고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 지난 넉 달 동안 그를 찾아간 적이 없었고, 라주미힌은 그의 주소조차 몰랐다. 두 달쯤 전에 두 사람은 거리에서 마주쳤으나, 라스콜니코프는 눈에 띄지 않으려고 몸을 돌려 길 건너편으로 건너가기까지 했다. 그리고 라주미힌은 그를 알아보았으면서도, 그를 성가시게 하고 싶지 않아 그냥 지나쳐 주었다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n“물론 요즘 나는 라주미힌한테 가서 일거리를 부탁할까, 가정교사 자리라도 얻어 달라고 부탁할까, 그런 생각을 했지…….” 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. “하지만 그가 지금 나에게 무슨 도움이 될 수 있단 말인가? 가정교사 자리를 구해 준다 치자, 푼돈이라도 있다면 마지막 한 푼까지 나눠 준다 치자, 그래서 내가 장화라도 사고 가정교사를 할 만큼 말끔히 차려입을 수 있다 치자……. 흠……. 그래, 그러고 나서는? 내가 벌어들인 몇 푼의 동전으로 무엇을 한단 말인가? 지금 내가 원하는 건 그게 아니야. 라주미힌한테 간다는 건 정말이지 터무니없는 일이지…….”\n\n자신이 지금 왜 라주미힌에게 가고 있는가 하는 의문은, 그 자신이 의식하는 것보다 훨씬 더 그를 동요시켰다. 그는 이 겉보기에 평범한 행동 속에서 어떤 불길한 의미를 불안하게 찾고 또 찾았다.\n\n“정말로 라주미힌 하나만으로 이 모든 걸 바로잡고 빠져나갈 길을 찾을 수 있다고 기대했던 걸까?” 그는 당혹스러워하며 자신에게 물었다.\n\n그는 곰곰이 생각하며 이마를 문질렀다. 그런데 이상하게도, 오랜 사색 끝에 갑자기, 마치 저절로 우연히 떠오른 듯, 한 가지 기이한 생각이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다.\n\n“흠……, 라주미힌한테로.” 그는 마치 최종 결심에 다다른 사람처럼 침착하게 단번에 말했다. “라주미힌한테 가는 거야, 물론, 하지만……, 지금은 아니지. 그에게 가는 거야……, 그 일 다음 날에, 그 일이 다 끝나고 모든 것이 새롭게 시작될 때…….”\n\n그러다 그는 문득 자신이 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지를 깨달았다.\n\n“그 일 다음에라니!” 그는 벤치에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다. “하지만 정말로 그 일이 일어난단 말인가? 정말로 그게 일어날 수 있단 말인가?” 그는 벤치를 떠나 거의 뛰다시피 걸어갔다. 집으로, 발길을 돌리려 했으나 집으로 돌아간다는 생각이 갑자기 격렬한 혐오로 그를 가득 채웠다. 바로 그 구멍 속에서, 그 끔찍하고 작은 벽장 같은 방에서, 이 모든 일이 지난 한 달 동안 그의 안에서 자라나고 있었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 발길 닿는 대로 걸어갔다.\n\n신경의 떨림은 어느새 그를 오슬오슬 떨게 하는 열기로 변해 있었다. 더위에도 불구하고 그는 한기를 느꼈다. 그는 일종의 안간힘으로, 거의 무의식적으로, 어떤 내면의 갈망에 떠밀려, 마치 주의를 다른 데로 돌릴 무언가를 찾는 사람처럼 눈앞의 모든 사물을 뚫어지게 바라보기 시작했다. 그러나 뜻대로 되지 않았고, 매 순간 다시 골똘한 생각 속으로 빠져들고 말았다. 흠칫하며 고개를 들어 다시 둘러볼 때면, 그는 방금 무엇을 생각하고 있었는지, 심지어 자신이 어디로 가고 있었는지조차 곧바로 잊어버렸다. 이런 식으로 그는 바실리옙스키 섬을 가로질러, 작은 네바 강가로 나와 다리를 건너 여러 섬 쪽으로 방향을 틀었다. 도시의 먼지와, 그를 둘러싸고 짓누르던 거대한 건물들 뒤에 마주한 녹음과 신선함은 처음에는 그의 지친 눈에 안식이 되어 주었다. 여기에는 선술집도, 숨 막히는 답답함도, 악취도 없었다. 그러나 곧 이 새롭고 기분 좋은 감각들은 병적인 신경질로 바뀌었다. 이따금 그는 푸른 나뭇잎 사이에 자리한, 밝게 칠한 별장 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 울타리 너머를 들여다보았고, 멀리 베란다와 발코니에 말쑥하게 차려입은 여인들과, 정원에서 뛰노는 아이들을 보았다. 특히 꽃들이 그의 눈길을 사로잡았다. 그는 그 무엇보다도 오래도록 꽃들을 바라보았다. 그는 또한 호화로운 마차들과 말 탄 남녀들과도 마주쳤다. 그는 호기심 어린 눈으로 그들을 바라보다가, 그들이 시야에서 사라지기도 전에 그들에 관해 잊어버렸다. 한번은 그가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 자기 돈을 세어 보았다. 삼십 코페이카가 있었다. “경찰한테 이십, 편지 때문에 나스타시야한테 삼, 그러니까 어제 마르멜라도프네 사람들에게 사십칠이나 오십을 준 셈이군.” 무슨 영문인지 그는 그렇게 셈을 따져 보며 생각했지만, 곧 자신이 무슨 목적으로 주머니에서 돈을 꺼냈는지조차 잊어버렸다. 어느 음식점인지 선술집인지 앞을 지나면서 그는 그것을 떠올렸고, 자신이 배가 고프다는 것을 느꼈다……. 선술집으로 들어가 그는 보드카 한 잔을 마시고 무슨 파이 같은 것을 먹었다. 걸어 나오면서 그는 파이를 다 먹었다. 보드카를 입에 댄 지 오래된지라 그것은 비록 한 잔뿐이었는데도 즉시 그에게 작용했다. 그의 두 다리가 갑자기 무거워지고 굉장한 졸음이 밀려왔다. 그는 집 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸으나, 페트롭스키 섬에 이르렀을 때 완전히 기진맥진하여 멈춰 섰고, 길에서 벗어나 덤불 속으로 들어가 풀밭에 주저앉더니 그 자리에서 곧바로 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n병적인 상태의 뇌에서는 꿈이 종종 기이한 현실감과 생생함, 그리고 더없이 실제와 흡사한 모습을 띠곤 한다. 때로는 기괴한 형상들이 만들어지지만, 그 배경과 전체 정경이 너무도 진실에 가깝고, 너무도 섬세하면서도 의외이면서도 예술적으로 일관된 세부로 가득해서, 꿈꾸는 자가 설령 푸시킨이나 투르게네프 같은 예술가라 할지라도 깨어 있는 상태에서는 결코 만들어 낼 수 없을 정도다. 그런 병든 꿈들은 언제나 오래도록 기억에 남아, 과도하게 곤두서고 어지러워진 신경 계통에 강렬한 인상을 남긴다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 무서운 꿈을 꾸었다. 꿈속에서 그는 다시 어린 시절로 돌아가, 자신이 태어난 작은 마을에 있었다. 그는 일곱 살쯤 된 어린아이였고, 어느 축일 저녁 아버지와 함께 시골길을 걷고 있었다. 흐리고 무거운 날이었고, 시골 풍경은 그가 기억하던 그대로였다. 아니, 사실 그는 기억 속에서보다 꿈속에서 그것을 훨씬 더 생생하게 떠올렸다. 작은 마을은 손바닥처럼 헐벗은 평평한 들판 위에 서 있었고, 근처에는 버드나무 한 그루조차 없었다. 다만 저 멀리 지평선의 맨 가장자리에 잡목림이 하나, 거뭇한 얼룩처럼 누워 있을 뿐이었다. 마지막 채마밭에서 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에 선술집이 하나 서 있었는데, 큼직한 선술집이었다. 아버지와 함께 그 앞을 지날 때면 그것은 늘 그의 마음속에 혐오감을, 심지어 두려움까지 불러일으켰다. 거기에는 늘 사람들이 북적였고, 늘 고함과 웃음과 욕설, 흉측하고 쉰 목소리의 노래, 그리고 종종 싸움이 벌어졌다. 술 취한, 끔찍한 몰골의 사내들이 선술집 주변을 어슬렁거렸다. 그들과 마주치면 그는 온몸을 떨며 아버지에게 바짝 매달리곤 했다. 선술집 근처에서 길은 먼지투성이 오솔길로 바뀌었는데, 그 먼지는 늘 검었다. 구불구불한 길이었고, 백 걸음쯤 더 가면 오른쪽으로 꺾여 묘지로 이어졌다. 묘지 한가운데에는 초록색 둥근 지붕을 인 돌로 지은 교회가 서 있었는데, 그는 일 년에 두세 번씩 아버지 어머니와 함께 그곳으로 미사를 드리러 가곤 했다. 오래전에 돌아가셔서 그가 한 번도 본 적 없는 할머니를 추모하는 예배가 열릴 때였다. 그럴 때면 그들은 식탁용 냅킨에 싸 매단 하얀 접시에, 십자가 모양으로 건포도를 박아 넣은 특별한 쌀 푸딩을 담아 가져가곤 했다. 그는 그 교회를, 그 구식의, 꾸밈없는 성상들을, 고개를 흔드는 늙은 사제를 사랑했다. 돌로 표시된 할머니의 무덤 곁에는, 생후 여섯 달 만에 죽은 그의 남동생의 작은 무덤이 있었다. 그는 그 동생을 전혀 기억하지 못했으나, 어린 동생에 관한 이야기를 들어 왔던지라, 묘지를 찾을 때면 경건하고 정성스럽게 성호를 긋고 절을 하며 그 작은 무덤에 입을 맞추곤 했다. 그런데 지금 그는 꿈속에서, 묘지로 가는 길에 아버지와 함께 선술집 앞을 지나고 있었다. 그는 아버지의 손을 잡고 두려움 어린 눈으로 선술집을 바라보았다. 한 가지 특이한 광경이 그의 주의를 끌었다. 무슨 축제 같은 것이 벌어지고 있는 듯, 화려하게 차려입은 마을 사람들과 농촌 아낙들, 그 남편들, 온갖 잡것들이 무리 지어 있었는데, 모두가 노래를 부르며 너나없이 어지간히 취해 있었다. 선술집 입구 근처에는 짐마차 한 대가 서 있었는데, 이상한 짐마차였다. 그것은 흔히 무거운 짐말이 끌고, 포도주 통이나 다른 무거운 짐을 싣는 그런 커다란 짐마차들 중 하나였다. 그는 그 거대한 짐말들을, 긴 갈기와 굵은 다리와 느릿느릿 한결같은 걸음걸이로, 마치 짐을 진 편이 진 게 없는 편보다 오히려 더 수월하다는 듯 힘들이는 기색 하나 없이 산더미 같은 짐을 끌어가는 그 모습을 늘 바라보기 좋아했다. 그런데 지금, 이상하게도, 그런 짐마차의 끌채에 그는 깡마르고 작은 밤색 짐승 한 마리를 보았다. 그 짐승은 그가 흔히 보아 온, 무거운 장작이나 건초 짐 아래서, 특히 바퀴가 진창이나 바큇자국에 빠졌을 때 죽을힘을 다해 버둥거리던 그런 농사꾼의 늙은 말이었다. 그럴 때면 농사꾼들은 그 말을 어찌나 잔인하게 두들겨 패는지, 때로는 콧등이며 눈까지 후려쳤고, 그는 그 짐승들이 너무도, 너무도 가여워서 거의 울 지경이 되곤 했으며, 그러면 어머니는 늘 그를 창가에서 데리고 나가곤 했다. 그런데 별안간 고함과 노래, 발랄라이카 소리로 요란한 소동이 일더니, 선술집에서 덩치 크고 잔뜩 취한 농사꾼 여럿이, 빨갛고 파란 셔츠를 입고 외투를 어깨에 걸친 채 우르르 몰려나왔다.\n\n“타라, 타!” 그들 중 하나가 소리쳤다. 목이 굵고 젊은 농사꾼으로, 당근처럼 시뻘건 살집 좋은 얼굴이었다. “다들 태워 주마, 타라!”\n\n그러자 곧장 군중 속에서 웃음과 외침이 터져 나왔다.\n\n“저런 짐승을 가지고 우리를 다 태우겠다고!”\n\n“이봐, 미콜카, 저런 비루먹은 말을 이런 짐마차에 매다니 미친 거 아냐?”\n\n“게다가 이 암말은 적어도 스무 살은 됐다고, 친구들!”\n\n“타라, 다들 태워 주마.” 미콜카가 다시 소리치며 맨 먼저 짐마차에 펄쩍 뛰어올라 고삐를 움켜쥐고 앞쪽에 똑바로 버티고 섰다. “밤색 말은 마트베이가 끌고 가 버렸어.” 그가 짐마차 위에서 소리쳤다. “그래서 이 못된 것이, 친구들, 내 속을 어찌나 썩이는지, 죽여 버리고 싶을 지경이라고. 그저 처먹기만 하잖아. 타라니까! 내가 이놈을 달리게 만들 거야! 달리고말고!” 그러고는 채찍을 집어 들더니, 신이 나서 작은 암말을 후려칠 채비를 했다.\n\n“타! 어서 와!” 군중이 웃어 댔다. “들었지, 이놈이 달린다잖아!”\n\n“달린다고! 저놈은 지난 십 년 동안 한 번도 달려 본 적이 없을 텐데!”\n\n“그냥 터덜터덜 가겠지!”\n\n“신경 쓰지 마, 친구들, 다들 채찍을 하나씩 들고 준비해!”\n\n“좋았어! 한 대 갈겨!”\n\n그들은 모두 웃고 농지거리하며 미콜카의 짐마차에 기어올랐다. 여섯 사내가 올라탔는데도 자리가 더 남아 있었다. 그들은 살집 좋고 발그레한 뺨의 여자 하나를 끌어 올렸다. 그녀는 빨간 무명옷을 입고, 끝이 뾰족하고 구슬을 단 머리쓰개를 쓰고, 두꺼운 가죽신을 신고 있었다. 그녀는 호두를 까먹으며 웃고 있었다. 그들을 둘러싼 군중도 웃고 있었으니, 사실 어찌 웃지 않을 수 있겠는가? 저 가련한 늙은 말이 그 짐마차에 탄 사람들을 몽땅 끌고 달려야 하는 판이니! 짐마차 위의 두 젊은 녀석은 미콜카를 거들려고 막 채찍을 챙기고 있었다. “이랴!” 하는 외침과 함께 암말은 죽을힘을 다해 끌었지만, 달리기는커녕 앞으로 거의 움직이지도 못했다. 그것은 우박처럼 쏟아지는 세 개의 채찍질에 헐떡이며 움츠러든 채 다리를 버둥거렸다. 짐마차 위와 군중 속의 웃음이 곱절로 커졌으나, 미콜카는 격분하여, 마치 정말로 이놈이 달릴 수 있다고 믿기라도 하는 양 미친 듯이 암말을 후려쳤다.\n\n“나도 좀 태워 줘, 친구들.” 군중 속에서 한 젊은이가 흥이 동했는지 소리쳤다.\n\n“타, 다들 타.” 미콜카가 외쳤다. “이놈이 너희 다 끌고 갈 거야. 내가 이놈을 죽도록 패 줄 테니!” 그러고는 분노에 제정신을 잃고 암말을 후려치고 또 후려쳤다.\n\n“아버지, 아버지.” 그가 울먹였다. “아버지, 저 사람들이 뭐 하는 거예요? 아버지, 저 사람들이 불쌍한 말을 때리고 있어요!”\n\n“가자, 가자!” 아버지가 말했다. “저들은 취해서 어리석은 짓을 하는 거란다, 장난질이야. 가자, 보지 마라!” 그러고는 그를 끌어당기려 했지만, 그는 아버지의 손을 뿌리치고는, 공포에 제정신을 잃은 채 말에게로 달려갔다. 가련한 짐승은 처참한 꼴이었다. 그것은 헐떡이며 멈춰 섰다가, 다시 끌어 보려 안간힘을 쓰다가 거의 쓰러질 지경이었다.\n\n“죽도록 패 버려.” 미콜카가 소리쳤다. “이젠 그 수밖에 없어. 내가 이놈을 끝장내 주마!”\n\n“대체 무슨 짓이야, 너 그리스도교인 맞아, 이 악마 같은 놈아?” 군중 속에서 한 노인이 소리쳤다.\n\n“이런 꼴을 누가 본 적이나 있나? 저런 비루먹은 말이 저런 짐을 끌다니.” 또 다른 사람이 말했다.\n\n“넌 저놈을 죽일 거야.” 세 번째 사람이 소리쳤다.\n\n“참견 마! 내 거니까, 내 맘대로 한다고. 타, 더 타라고! 다들 타! 내가 이놈을 기어이 달리게 만들 테니까……!”\n\n별안간 웃음이 와르르 터져 나와 모든 것을 뒤덮었다. 빗발치는 채찍질에 발끈한 암말이 힘없이 뒷발질을 하기 시작한 것이다. 노인조차 웃음을 참지 못했다. 저런 비루먹은 작은 짐승이 뒷발질을 하려 들다니!\n\n군중 속의 두 사내가 채찍을 낚아채더니 옆구리를 후려치려고 암말에게 달려갔다. 각자 한쪽씩 맡았다.\n\n“얼굴을 갈겨, 눈을, 눈을 갈겨.” 미콜카가 소리쳤다.\n\n“노래나 한 곡 뽑자고, 친구들.” 짐마차 위에서 누군가 소리쳤고, 짐마차 위의 모두가 탬버린을 흔들고 휘파람을 불며 떠들썩한 노래에 함께 어울렸다. 여자는 여전히 호두를 까먹으며 웃고 있었다.\n\n……그는 암말 곁에서 달렸고, 그 앞에서 달리며, 채찍이 눈을 가로질러, 바로 그 눈을 후려치는 것을 보았다! 그는 울고 있었고, 목이 메었으며, 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내렸다. 사내들 중 하나가 채찍으로 그의 얼굴을 후려쳤지만, 그는 그것을 느끼지도 못했다. 두 손을 비틀며 비명을 지르면서, 그는 못마땅하다는 듯 고개를 가로젓고 있던, 잿빛 머리에 잿빛 수염의 노인에게로 달려갔다. 한 여자가 그의 손을 잡고 데려가려 했으나, 그는 그녀에게서 몸을 뿌리치고 다시 암말에게로 달려갔다. 그것은 거의 마지막 숨을 헐떡이고 있었으나, 또다시 뒷발질을 하기 시작했다.\n\n“네놈에게 뒷발질하는 법을 가르쳐 주마.” 미콜카가 사납게 소리쳤다. 그는 채찍을 내던지고 몸을 숙여 짐마차 바닥에서 길고 굵은 끌채 하나를 집어 들더니, 두 손으로 한쪽 끝을 움켜쥐고 안간힘을 다해 암말 위로 휘둘렀다.\n\n“저놈이 말을 으스러뜨리겠어.” 그의 주위에서 사람들이 외쳤다. “저놈이 말을 죽이겠어!”\n\n“내 거야.” 미콜카가 소리치며 끌채를 힘껏 휘둘러 내리쳤다. 둔중한 타격 소리가 났다.\n\n“패, 패라고! 왜 멈춰 섰어?” 군중 속에서 여러 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n그러자 미콜카가 두 번째로 끌채를 휘둘렀고, 그것은 다시금 불운한 암말의 등뼈 위로 떨어졌다. 암말은 뒷다리를 꺾고 주저앉았으나, 다시 앞으로 비틀거리며 온 힘을 다해 끌었다. 한쪽으로 끌었다가 또 다른 쪽으로 끌며, 짐마차를 움직여 보려 안간힘을 썼다. 그러나 여섯 개의 채찍이 사방에서 그것을 공격했고, 끌채가 다시 들려 올라가 세 번째로, 다음엔 네 번째로 묵직하고 규칙적인 타격으로 그것 위에 떨어졌다. 미콜카는 단번에 그것을 죽이지 못한 것에 격분했다.\n\n“질긴 놈이군.” 군중 속에서 누군가 외쳤다.\n\n“곧 쓰러질 거야, 친구들, 이제 곧 끝장날 거라고.” 군중 속의 한 구경꾼이 감탄하듯 말했다.\n\n“도끼를 가져다 줘! 끝장을 내 버려.” 세 번째 사람이 외쳤다.\n\n“내가 본때를 보여 주지! 비켜.” 미콜카가 미친 듯이 비명을 질렀다. 그는 끌채를 내던지고 짐마차 안에서 몸을 굽혀 쇠 지렛대를 집어 들었다. “비켜.” 그가 외치며, 있는 힘을 다해 가련한 암말에게 정신이 아찔할 만큼 무서운 일격을 가했다. 타격이 떨어졌다. 암말은 휘청거리며 주저앉았다가 다시 끌어 보려 안간힘을 썼으나, 지렛대가 또다시 그것의 등 위로 힘껏 휘둘려 떨어졌고, 그것은 통나무처럼 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n“끝장을 내라고.” 미콜카가 소리치며 제정신을 잃고 짐마차에서 펄쩍 뛰어내렸다. 역시 술기운에 얼굴이 벌게진 젊은이 몇이 닥치는 대로 손에 잡히는 것을 — 채찍이며 막대기며 장대를 — 움켜쥐고 죽어 가는 암말에게로 달려갔다. 미콜카는 한쪽에 서서 지렛대로 아무렇게나 후려치기 시작했다. 암말은 목을 쭉 뻗고 길게 한 번 숨을 들이쉬더니 죽어 버렸다.\n\n“네놈이 도륙해 버렸어.” 군중 속에서 누군가 소리쳤다.\n\n“그럼 어째서 달리지 않은 거야?”\n\n“내 거라고!” 미콜카가 핏발 선 눈으로 소리치며 두 손에 지렛대를 휘둘렀다. 그는 마치 더 이상 때릴 것이 없는 게 아쉽다는 듯 서 있었다.\n\n“틀림없어, 넌 그리스도교인이 아니야.” 군중 속에서 많은 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 가련한 소년은 제정신을 잃고 비명을 지르며 군중을 헤치고 밤색 늙은 말에게로 다가가, 피투성이가 된 죽은 머리를 두 팔로 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다. 그 눈에 입을 맞추고, 그 입술에 입을 맞추었다……. 그러더니 벌떡 일어나 작은 두 주먹을 내지르며 광란하듯 미콜카에게 달려들었다. 바로 그 순간, 줄곧 그를 뒤쫓던 아버지가 그를 낚아채어 군중 밖으로 데리고 나갔다.\n\n“가자, 어서! 집으로 가자.” 아버지가 그에게 말했다.\n\n“아버지! 왜 저 사람들이……, 불쌍한 말을……, 죽인 거예요!” 그는 흐느꼈으나, 목소리가 갈라지며 헐떡이는 가슴에서 말이 비명처럼 터져 나왔다.\n\n“저들은 취했단다……. 짐승 같은 놈들이지……, 우리가 상관할 바 아니야!” 아버지가 말했다. 그는 두 팔로 아버지를 끌어안았으나, 숨이 막히고 또 막혔다. 그는 숨을 한 번 들이쉬려, 소리를 지르려 애썼고 — 그러다 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n그는 숨을 헐떡이며 깨어났다. 머리카락은 땀에 흠뻑 젖어 있었고, 그는 공포에 질려 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n“오, 하느님, 감사합니다, 그저 꿈이었어.” 그는 나무 아래 주저앉아 깊은 숨을 들이쉬며 말했다. “하지만 이게 뭐지? 무슨 열병이라도 오는 건가? 이토록 흉측한 꿈을 꾸다니!”\n\n그는 온몸이 완전히 부서진 듯한 기분이었다. 그의 영혼 속에는 어둠과 혼란이 깃들어 있었다. 그는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 머리를 떨구었다.\n\n“오, 하느님!” 그가 부르짖었다. “정말로, 정말로 내가 진짜 도끼를 들고, 그녀의 머리를 내리쳐, 그 두개골을 쪼개 버린다는 게……, 내가 끈적하고 따뜻한 피를 밟고, 자물쇠를 부수고, 훔치고 떨면서, 온통 피를 뒤집어쓴 채 숨는다는 게……, 도끼를 들고서……. 오, 하느님, 정말 그럴 수 있단 말인가?”\n\n이 말을 하면서 그는 나뭇잎처럼 떨고 있었다.\n\n“그런데 내가 왜 이러고 있는 거지?” 그는 깊은 경악에 빠진 듯 다시 몸을 일으키며 말을 이었다. “나는 결코 그 일을 해내지 못하리라는 걸 알고 있었는데, 그렇다면 지금까지 무엇 때문에 나 자신을 괴롭혀 온 거지? 어제, 어제, 내가 그……, 예행연습을 하러 갔을 때, 어제 나는 결코 그런 짓을 견뎌 낼 수 없으리라는 걸 완전히 깨달았어……. 그런데 무엇 때문에 또 이걸 되풀이하고 있는 거지? 무엇 때문에 망설이고 있는 거지? 어제 계단을 내려오면서, 나 스스로 그건 비열하고, 역겹고, 추잡하고, 추잡한 짓이라고 말했잖아……, 그걸 생각하는 것만으로도 메스껍고 공포에 휩싸였잖아.\n\n“아니, 나는 그 일을 할 수 없어, 할 수 없어! 그래, 인정한다, 그 모든 추론에 한 점 결함도 없다 치고, 지난 한 달 동안 내가 내린 모든 결론이 대낮처럼 명백하고 산수처럼 옳다 치자……. 오, 하느님! 어쨌든 나는 그 일을 해낼 수 없어! 나는 할 수 없어, 할 수 없다고! 그런데, 그런데 왜 나는 아직도……?”\n\n그는 자리에서 일어나, 마치 자신이 이런 곳에 있다는 것에 놀란 듯 의아한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보고는 다리 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그는 창백했고, 두 눈은 이글거렸으며, 온몸이 사지마다 기진맥진해 있었으나, 갑자기 한결 숨쉬기가 편해진 것 같았다. 그는 그토록 오래도록 자신을 짓눌러 온 그 무서운 짐을 벗어 던진 듯한 기분이었고, 별안간 그의 영혼 속에 안도와 평온의 느낌이 깃들었다. “주여,” 그는 기도했다. “저에게 제 길을 보여 주소서 — 저는 저 저주받은……, 저의 그 꿈을 단념하겠나이다.”\n\n다리를 건너며 그는 네바 강을, 이글거리는 하늘로 지고 있는 새빨갛게 타오르는 해를 조용하고 차분하게 바라보았다. 쇠약한 몸에도 불구하고 그는 피로를 의식하지 못했다. 마치 지난 한 달 동안 그의 가슴속에서 곪아 가던 종기가 별안간 터져 버린 것만 같았다. 자유, 자유다! 그는 그 주술에서, 그 마법에서, 그 망상에서 벗어난 것이다!\n\n훗날 그 시절을, 그 며칠 동안 자신에게 일어난 모든 일을 시시각각, 하나하나 떠올릴 때면, 그는 한 가지 정황에 미신적인 전율을 느끼곤 했다. 그것은 그 자체로는 그다지 특별할 것이 없었으나, 그에게는 언제나 자신의 운명이 예정된 전환점처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 도무지 이해할 수도, 스스로에게 설명할 수도 없었다. 어째서 자신이 지치고 기진맥진했을 때, 가장 짧고 가장 곧은 길로 집에 돌아가는 편이 더 편했을 텐데, 굳이 갈 필요도 없는 건초 시장을 거쳐 돌아왔는가를. 그리 많이는 아니었지만, 그것은 명백히, 그리고 전혀 불필요하게 길을 돌아가는 것이었다. 물론 그가 어느 거리를 지나는지도 모른 채 집으로 돌아온 일은 수십 번이나 있었다. 그러나 그는 늘 자문하곤 했다. 어째서 그토록 중요하고, 그토록 결정적이면서 동시에 그토록 완전히 우연한 만남이, 하필 건초 시장(게다가 그가 갈 이유도 없던 곳)에서, 자신의 일생에서 바로 그 시각에, 바로 그 순간에, 그 만남이 자신의 운명 전체에 가장 중대하고 가장 결정적인 영향을 미칠 수 있는 바로 그런 기분과 바로 그런 상황에 처해 있던 바로 그때 일어났는가를. 마치 그 만남이 일부러 그를 노리고 숨어서 기다리고 있기라도 했던 것처럼!\n\n그가 건초 시장을 가로지른 것은 아홉 시쯤이었다. 탁자며 손수레며 노점이며 가게에서, 장사꾼들은 모두 점포를 닫거나 물건을 치우고 꾸려 넣으며, 손님들과 마찬가지로 집으로 돌아가는 중이었다. 건초 시장의 더럽고 악취 나는 안뜰에서는 온갖 부류의 넝마주이와 행상들이 선술집 주변에 북적이고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 거리를 정처 없이 헤맬 때면 특히 이곳과 이웃한 골목들을 좋아했다. 여기서는 그의 누더기가 경멸 어린 시선을 끌지 않았고, 어떤 차림새로 돌아다녀도 사람들의 빈축을 사지 않았다. 어느 골목 모퉁이에서는 한 행상과 그의 아내가 탁자 두 개에 끈이며 실이며 무명 손수건 따위를 늘어놓고 있었다. 그들 역시 집에 가려고 일어선 참이었으나, 막 다가온 한 친구와 이야기를 나누느라 우물쭈물 머뭇거리고 있었다. 이 친구는 리자베타 이바노브나, 모두가 그저 리자베타라 부르는, 늙은 전당포 노파 알료나 이바노브나의 동생이었다. 라스콜니코프는 바로 전날 그 노파를 찾아가 시계를 저당 잡히고 자신의 예행연습을 했던 것이다……. 그는 이미 리자베타에 관해 모든 것을 알고 있었고, 그녀 또한 그를 조금은 알고 있었다. 그녀는 서른다섯쯤 된 독신녀로, 키가 크고 굼뜨며 소심하고 고분고분한, 거의 백치에 가까운 여자였다. 그녀는 완전히 노예나 다름없어서, 밤낮없이 일을 시키고 심지어 매질까지 하는 언니를 두려움에 떨며 받들었다. 그녀는 보따리를 든 채 행상과 그의 아내 앞에 서서, 진지하면서도 미심쩍은 표정으로 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그들은 무언가에 관해 유독 열을 올리며 이야기하는 중이었다. 라스콜니코프는 그녀를 알아본 순간, 비록 이 만남에 놀랄 만한 구석은 전혀 없었음에도, 일종의 강렬한 경악에 가까운 기이한 감각에 사로잡혔다.\n\n“알아서 결정하시구려, 리자베타 이바노브나.” 행상이 큰 소리로 말하고 있었다. “내일 일곱 시쯤 오시오. 그쪽 사람들도 여기 와 있을 거요.”\n\n“내일이요?” 리자베타는 마음을 정하지 못하는 듯, 천천히 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n“원, 댁은 알료나 이바노브나를 어쩜 그리도 무서워하는지.” 행상의 아내가, 기운차고 자그마한 여자가 재잘거렸다. “댁을 보고 있자니, 꼭 갓난아기 같구려. 게다가 그 여자가 친언니도 아니잖소 — 그저 의붓언니일 뿐인데, 어쩜 그리 댁을 쥐고 흔드는지!”\n\n“하지만 이번엔 알료나 이바노브나한테 한마디도 하지 마시오.” 남편이 말을 가로챘다. “내 조언이오, 묻지도 말고 그냥 우리한테 오시구려. 그럴 만한 값어치가 있을 테니까. 나중엔 언니도 알아서 짐작하겠지.”\n\n“가야 하나요?”\n\n“내일 일곱 시쯤이오. 그쪽 사람들도 여기 와 있을 거고. 댁이 직접 결정할 수 있을 거요.”\n\n“그리고 차도 한 잔 합시다.” 그의 아내가 덧붙였다.\n\n“좋아요, 갈게요.” 리자베타는 여전히 곰곰이 생각하며 말하고는, 천천히 자리를 뜨기 시작했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 막 그 곁을 지나친 참이라 더는 듣지 못했다. 그는 한마디도 놓치지 않으려 애쓰며, 들키지 않게 살그머니 지나갔다. 처음의 경악에 뒤이어, 마치 등줄기를 타고 내리는 한기처럼 공포의 전율이 그를 휩쓸었다. 그는 알게 된 것이다, 갑자기 너무도 뜻밖에 알게 된 것이다, 내일 일곱 시에 그 노파의 동생이자 유일한 동거인인 리자베타가 집을 비울 것이며, 따라서 정확히 일곱 시에 그 노파가 혼자 남게 되리라는 것을.\n\n그의 하숙집까지는 불과 몇 걸음이었다. 그는 사형 선고를 받은 사람처럼 안으로 들어갔다. 그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았고, 생각할 수도 없었다. 다만 그는 자기 존재 전체로 갑자기 느꼈다, 자신에게 더는 생각의 자유도, 의지도 없으며, 모든 것이 갑작스럽게 그리고 돌이킬 수 없이 결정되어 버렸음을.\n\n분명, 설령 적당한 기회를 얻기 위해 몇 년을 통째로 기다려야 했다 한들, 그는 방금 자신 앞에 나타난 것보다 그 계획의 성공을 향한 더 확실한 발판을 기대할 수는 없었을 것이다. 어찌 됐든, 위험한 캐물음이나 탐색 없이, 더 정확하게 그리고 더 적은 위험으로, 다음 날 어느 시각에 목숨을 노리는 그 노파가 집에 완전히 혼자 있으리라는 것을 미리, 그리고 확실하게 알아낸다는 것은 어려운 일이었을 터였다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n나중에 라스콜니코프는 그 잡화상과 그의 아내가 무슨 일로 리자베타를 불렀는지 우연히 알게 되었다. 그것은 지극히 평범한 일이었고, 별다른 게 없었다. 이 도시로 와서 가난에 쪼들리게 된 어느 가족이 살림살이며 옷가지를 팔고 있었는데, 죄다 여자들 물건이었다. 그 물건들은 시장에 내다 팔아 봤자 얼마 받지 못할 터라, 그들은 중개상을 찾고 있었다. 그게 바로 리자베타가 하는 일이었다. 그녀는 그런 일을 맡아 했고, 자주 불려 다녔는데, 워낙 정직하고 늘 공정한 값을 정해 그대로 지켰기 때문이다. 그녀는 대개 말수가 적었고, 앞서 말했듯이 무척 순종적이고 소심했다.\n\n그러나 라스콜니코프는 요즘 들어 미신에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 미신의 흔적은 그 후로도 오래도록 그에게 남아, 거의 지워지지 않을 정도였다. 그리고 이 모든 일에서 그는 훗날 늘 무언가 기이하고 신비로운 것, 말하자면 어떤 독특한 영향력과 우연의 일치 같은 것을 보려는 경향이 있었다. 지난겨울, 그가 아는 포코레프라는 대학생이 — 하리코프로 떠난 — 대화 중에 우연히, 혹시 무언가를 저당 잡힐 일이 생길 경우를 대비해 늙은 전당포 노파 알료나 이바노브나의 주소를 그에게 알려 주었다. 그는 오랫동안 그녀를 찾아가지 않았으니, 과외 수업이 있어 그럭저럭 살아갈 수 있었기 때문이다. 여섯 주 전에 그는 그 주소를 떠올렸다. 그에게는 저당 잡힐 만한 물건이 두 개 있었다. 아버지의 낡은 은시계와, 빨간 보석 세 개가 박힌 작은 금반지였는데, 후자는 헤어질 때 여동생이 준 선물이었다. 그는 반지를 가져가기로 했다. 그 노파를 찾아갔을 때, 그는 그녀에 대해 특별히 아는 것이 없었음에도 첫눈에 도저히 억누를 수 없는 혐오감을 느꼈다. 그는 그녀에게서 이 루블을 받아 들고, 집으로 가는 길에 어느 초라하고 작은 선술집에 들어갔다. 그는 차를 주문하고 자리에 앉아 깊은 생각에 잠겼다. 어떤 기이한 생각이 마치 알 속의 병아리처럼 그의 뇌리를 톡톡 쪼아 대며, 그를 너무도, 너무도 깊이 사로잡았다.\n\n거의 그의 곁, 옆 탁자에는 그가 알지도 못하고 한 번도 본 적 없는 어느 대학생이 앉아 있었고, 그와 함께 젊은 장교 하나가 있었다. 그들은 당구를 한 판 친 뒤 차를 마시기 시작했다. 그때 갑자기 그는 그 대학생이 장교에게 전당포 노파 알료나 이바노브나의 얘기를 하며 그녀의 주소를 일러 주는 것을 들었다. 이것만으로도 라스콜니코프에게는 기이하게 느껴졌다. 그는 방금 그녀에게서 왔는데, 여기서 곧장 그녀의 이름을 듣게 된 것이다. 물론 우연이었지만, 그는 어떤 너무도 별난 인상을 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었으니, 마치 누군가가 일부러 그를 위해 말해 주는 것 같았다. 대학생은 친구에게 알료나 이바노브나에 관한 갖가지 세세한 얘기를 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"그 여자는 일류야.\" 그가 말했다. \"언제든 거기서 돈을 빌릴 수 있지. 유대인만큼이나 부자라서, 한 번에 오천 루블도 내줄 수 있는데, 그러면서도 일 루블짜리 저당물을 받는 것도 마다하지 않는다고. 우리 패거리 중에도 그 여자랑 거래한 놈이 수두룩해. 하지만 끔찍한 늙은 흡혈귀야…….\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 얼마나 심술궂고 변덕스러운지, 이자를 하루만 늦게 내도 저당물이 날아가 버린다는 것, 물건 값의 사분의 일밖에 쳐주지 않으면서 한 달에 오 퍼센트, 심지어 칠 퍼센트나 이자를 받는다는 것 따위를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 대학생은 계속 떠들어 댔는데, 그녀에게 리자베타라는 여동생이 있다는 것, 그 비참하고 자그마한 노파가 끊임없이 그녀를 두들겨 패며, 리자베타가 적어도 육 피트는 되는데도 어린애처럼 완전히 종처럼 부려 먹는다는 얘기였다.\n\n\"그런 별난 존재가 또 어디 있겠어.\" 대학생은 이렇게 외치고는 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n그들은 리자베타에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다. 대학생은 유독 즐거운 듯 그녀 얘기를 하며 연신 웃어 댔고, 장교는 대단한 흥미를 가지고 듣더니 자기 옷을 수선하게 리자베타를 보내 달라고 부탁했다. 라스콜니코프는 한 마디도 놓치지 않았고, 그녀에 관한 모든 것을 알게 되었다. 리자베타는 노파보다 어렸고, 어머니가 다른 이복 여동생이었다. 그녀는 서른다섯이었다. 그녀는 언니를 위해 밤낮없이 일했고, 요리와 빨래를 하는 것 말고도 바느질을 하고 허드렛일까지 하면서 번 돈을 죄다 언니에게 갖다 바쳤다. 그녀는 언니의 허락 없이는 그 어떤 주문이나 일거리도 감히 받지 못했다. 노파는 이미 유언장을 써 두었는데, 리자베타도 그것을 알고 있었으니, 그 유언에 따르면 그녀는 한 푼도 받지 못하고, 오직 동산(動産)인 의자 따위만 받게 될 뿐이었다. 모든 돈은 N현(縣)의 어느 수도원에 남겨져, 자기 영혼을 위한 기도가 영원히 올려지도록 했다. 리자베타는 언니보다 신분이 낮았고, 미혼인 데다 외모는 끔찍하게 우락부락했으며, 유난히 키가 크고 긴 발은 마치 바깥쪽으로 굽은 것처럼 보였다. 그녀는 늘 닳아 빠진 염소 가죽 신발을 신고 다녔으며, 몸가짐은 깔끔했다. 대학생이 가장 놀라워하고 재미있어한 것은, 리자베타가 끊임없이 임신을 한다는 사실이었다.\n\n\"그런데 그 여자가 흉측하다며?\" 장교가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 살갗이 어찌나 거무튀튀한지 변장한 병사처럼 보이지만, 사실 전혀 흉측하지 않아. 얼굴하고 눈이 어찌나 선량한지. 놀랄 만큼 그렇다고. 그 증거로, 그 여자한테 끌리는 사람이 한둘이 아냐. 어찌나 부드럽고 순한 존재인지, 뭐든 다 참아 내고, 늘 기꺼이, 뭐든 다 하려 들지. 게다가 그 미소는 정말이지 무척 사랑스러워.\"\n\n\"자네 자신이 그 여자한테 끌린 모양이군.\" 장교가 웃었다.\n\n\"별나서 그렇지. 아니, 자네한테 이런 얘기를 해 주겠어. 나라면 그 빌어먹을 늙은이를 죽이고 돈을 털어 달아날 수 있어, 장담하는데, 양심의 가책 따위는 눈곱만큼도 없이 말이야.\" 대학생이 열을 올리며 덧붙였다. 장교가 다시 웃었고, 그때 라스콜니코프는 몸서리를 쳤다. 이 얼마나 기이한 일인가!\n\n\"들어 봐, 진지한 질문을 하나 하고 싶은데.\" 대학생이 열띤 어조로 말했다. \"물론 농담이긴 했지만, 봐, 한편에는 멍청하고, 무의미하고, 무가치하고, 심술궂고, 병들고, 끔찍한 늙은이가 있어, 단지 쓸모없을 뿐 아니라 실제로 해악을 끼치는, 자기가 무엇 때문에 사는지조차 모르는, 어차피 하루 이틀이면 죽을 늙은이가 말이야. 무슨 말인지 알겠어? 알겠냐고?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 알겠어.\" 장교는 흥분한 동료를 유심히 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 들어 봐. 다른 한편에는, 도움이 없어 헛되이 버려지는 싱싱한 젊은 목숨들이, 그것도 수천씩, 사방에 널려 있다고! 수도원에 묻혀 버릴 그 늙은이의 돈으로 십만 가지 선행을 베풀고 도울 수 있어! 수백, 어쩌면 수천의 사람들을 올바른 길로 이끌 수 있고, 수십 가구를 궁핍에서, 파멸에서, 타락에서, 성병 병원에서 구해 낼 수 있단 말이야 — 그 모든 게 그 여자 돈으로 말이지. 그 여자를 죽이고, 돈을 빼앗아, 그 돈으로 인류를 위해, 만인의 행복을 위해 헌신하는 거야. 어떻게 생각해, 단 하나의 자그마한 범죄가 수천의 선행으로 씻기지 않겠어? 한 목숨 대신 수천이 타락과 부패에서 구원받는 거야. 죽음 하나에, 그 대가로 백 개의 목숨이라니 — 이건 단순한 산수라고! 게다가 그 병들고, 멍청하고, 심술궂은 늙은이의 목숨이 존재의 저울 위에서 무슨 가치가 있겠어! 이[蝨]나 검정 풍뎅이의 목숨만도 못해, 사실은 그보다 못하지, 그 늙은이는 해악을 끼치니까. 그 여자는 남들의 삶을 갉아먹고 있어. 며칠 전에는 심술이 나서 리자베타의 손가락을 깨물었는데, 하마터면 잘라 내야 할 뻔했다고.\"\n\n\"물론 그 여자는 살 자격이 없지.\" 장교가 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌겠어, 그게 자연인걸.\"\n\n\"아, 그래, 형제, 하지만 우리는 자연을 바로잡고 이끌어야 하고, 그러지 않으면 편견의 바다에 빠져 죽고 말 거야. 그러지 않았다면 단 한 명의 위대한 인물도 없었을 테고. 사람들은 의무니, 양심이니 떠들어 대지 — 나는 의무와 양심에 반대하려는 게 아니야 — 하지만 문제는, 우리가 그것들을 무슨 뜻으로 쓰느냐는 거지. 잠깐, 자네한테 또 하나 물어볼 게 있어. 들어 봐!\"\n\n\"아니, 자네가 잠깐 기다려, 내가 자네한테 질문을 하나 하지. 들어 봐!\"\n\n\"뭔데?\"\n\n\"자네는 입을 놀리며 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 있지만, 말해 봐, 자네라면 그 늙은이를 자네 손으로 직접 죽일 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"물론 못하지! 난 그저 그 일의 정당함을 따져 본 것뿐이야……. 나하고는 아무 상관 없는 일이고…….\"\n\n\"하지만 내 생각엔, 자네가 직접 못한다면, 거기엔 아무런 정당함도 없는 거야……. 한 판 더 치자고.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 격렬하게 동요했다. 물론 그것은 죄다 평범한 젊은이들의 얘기와 사고였고, 그가 전에도 갖가지 형태와 갖가지 주제로 자주 들어 온 것이었다. 하지만 어째서 그는 하필 자기 자신의 머릿속이 바로 그…… 똑같은 생각을 막 잉태하던 바로 그 순간에 그런 토론과 그런 사상을 듣게 되었을까? 그리고 어째서, 노파에게서 자기 생각의 싹을 막 가져 나온 바로 그 순간에, 그녀에 관한 대화에 곧장 맞닥뜨린 것일까? 이 우연의 일치는 늘 그에게 기이하게 느껴졌다. 선술집에서 오간 이 사소한 대화는 훗날 그의 행동에 엄청난 영향을 끼쳤으니, 마치 그 안에 정말로 무언가 예정된 것, 어떤 인도하는 암시 같은 것이 들어 있기라도 했던 것처럼…….\n\n건초 시장에서 돌아온 그는 소파에 몸을 내던지고, 꼬박 한 시간을 꼼짝도 하지 않은 채 앉아 있었다. 그러는 사이 날이 어두워졌다. 그에게는 양초가 없었고, 사실 불을 켤 생각조차 들지 않았다. 그때 자기가 무언가를 생각하고 있었는지조차 그는 끝내 기억해 낼 수 없었다. 마침내 그는 예의 그 열과 오한을 의식했고, 소파에 누울 수 있다는 사실에 안도감을 느꼈다. 곧 무겁고 납덩이 같은 잠이, 마치 그를 짓누르듯이 덮쳐 왔다.\n\n그는 유난히 오랫동안, 꿈도 꾸지 않고 잤다. 이튿날 아침 열 시에 그의 방에 들어온 나스타시야는 그를 깨우느라 애를 먹었다. 그녀는 그에게 차와 빵을 가져왔다. 차는 이번에도 두 번 우려낸 것이었고, 이번에도 그녀 자신의 찻주전자에 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"세상에, 어쩜 이렇게 잠을 자담!\" 그녀가 분개해 소리쳤다. \"노상 잠만 잔다니까.\"\n\n그는 힘겹게 몸을 일으켰다. 머리가 아팠다. 그는 일어서서 다락방을 한 바퀴 돌더니 다시 소파에 풀썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"또 자려고 그러네.\" 나스타시야가 외쳤다. \"어디 아픈 거예요, 응?\"\n\n그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"차 좀 마실래요?\"\n\"이따가.\" 그는 다시 눈을 감고 벽 쪽으로 돌아누우며 힘겹게 말했다.\n\n나스타시야는 그를 내려다보며 서 있었다.\n\n\"정말로 아픈 모양이네.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 돌아서서 나갔다. 그녀는 두 시에 수프를 들고 다시 들어왔다. 그는 여전히 아까처럼 누워 있었다. 차는 손도 대지 않은 채 그대로였다. 나스타시야는 노골적으로 기분이 상해 화를 내며 그를 흔들어 깨우기 시작했다.\n\n\"어쩜 이렇게 통나무처럼 누워만 있어요?\" 그녀가 혐오스러운 듯 그를 바라보며 소리쳤다.\n\n그는 일어났다가 다시 주저앉았으나, 아무 말 없이 바닥만 뚫어지게 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"아픈 거예요, 아닌 거예요?\" 나스타시야가 물었지만, 이번에도 아무 대답을 듣지 못했다. \"차라리 밖에 나가서 바람이라도 좀 쐬는 게 낫겠어요.\" 그녀는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다. \"이거 먹을 거예요, 말 거예요?\"\n\n\"이따가.\" 그가 힘없이 말했다. \"그만 가 봐.\"\n\n그러고는 그녀에게 나가라는 손짓을 했다.\n\n그녀는 잠시 더 머무르며 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보다가 나갔다.\n\n몇 분 후, 그는 눈을 들어 차와 수프를 한참 동안 바라보았다. 그러고는 빵을 집고, 숟가락을 들어 먹기 시작했다.\n\n그는 조금, 서너 숟가락쯤 먹었는데, 식욕도 없이, 마치 기계적으로 먹는 것 같았다. 머리는 덜 아팠다. 식사를 마친 그는 다시 소파에 몸을 뻗었으나, 이번에는 잠이 오지 않았다. 그는 베개에 얼굴을 묻은 채 꼼짝도 않고 누워 있었다. 그는 백일몽에, 그것도 너무도 기이한 백일몽에 시달렸다. 그중 자꾸만 되풀이되는 하나에서, 그는 자기가 아프리카에, 이집트에, 어떤 오아시스에 있는 것 같았다. 대상(隊商)은 쉬고 있었고, 낙타들은 평온히 누워 있었다. 야자수들이 사방에 빙 둘러 완전한 원을 이루고 서 있었다. 일행은 모두 식사 중이었다. 그러나 그는 가까이에서 콸콸 흐르는 샘물을 마시고 있었다. 그것은 어찌나 시원한지, 너무도, 너무도 경이로운, 푸르고 차가운 물이 알록달록한 돌들 사이로, 군데군데 황금처럼 반짝이는 깨끗한 모래 위로 흘러갔다……. 갑자기 그는 시계 치는 소리를 들었다. 그는 흠칫 놀라 정신을 차렸고, 고개를 들어 창밖을 내다보더니, 시간이 얼마나 늦었는지 깨닫고는, 갑자기 누군가가 그를 소파에서 끌어내리기라도 한 것처럼 완전히 잠이 깨어 벌떡 일어났다. 그는 발끝으로 살금살금 문가로 다가가, 살며시 문을 열고 계단의 동정에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 심장이 끔찍하게 두근거렸다. 그러나 계단은 마치 모두가 잠든 듯 온통 고요했다……. 전날부터 그렇게 정신없이 잠들어, 아무것도 하지 않고, 아직 아무것도 준비해 두지 않았다는 것이 그에게는 기이하고 끔찍하게 느껴졌다……. 그러는 사이 어쩌면 여섯 시를 쳤는지도 몰랐다. 그리고 졸음과 멍한 상태에 뒤이어, 유난히 열에 들뜬, 말하자면 정신 나간 듯한 다급함이 찾아왔다. 그러나 해 둘 준비라야 별로 없었다. 그는 모든 것을 빠짐없이 생각하고 아무것도 잊지 않는 데 온 정신을 집중했다. 그러는 내내 심장이 어찌나 두근거리고 쿵쿵 뛰는지 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없었다. 우선 그는 올가미를 만들어 외투에 꿰매 넣어야 했는데 — 이건 순식간에 끝낼 일이었다. 그는 베개 밑을 뒤져, 그 아래 쑤셔 넣어 둔 속옷가지 사이에서 닳아 빠진, 빨지 않은 낡은 셔츠를 골라냈다. 그 누더기에서 그는 폭이 두 인치쯤, 길이가 열여섯 인치쯤 되는 기다란 천 조각을 찢어 냈다. 그는 이 천 조각을 둘로 접고, 두툼한 무명천으로 된 헐렁하고 튼튼한 여름 외투를 (그의 유일한 겉옷이었다) 벗어, 안쪽 왼쪽 겨드랑이 밑에 그 천의 양 끝을 꿰매기 시작했다. 바느질을 하는 동안 손이 떨렸지만, 그는 그것을 무사히 해냈고, 다시 외투를 입었을 때 겉으로는 아무것도 드러나지 않았다. 바늘과 실은 오래전에 준비해 두어 종이에 싸인 채 그의 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다. 올가미로 말하자면, 그것은 그 자신이 고안한 무척 기발한 장치였다. 그 올가미는 도끼를 매달기 위한 것이었다. 도끼를 손에 들고 거리를 지나갈 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 외투 안에 숨긴다 해도 손으로 받쳐 들어야 할 텐데, 그러면 눈에 띄고 말 것이었다. 그러나 이제 그는 도끼의 날 부분을 올가미에 끼우기만 하면 되었고, 그러면 도끼가 안쪽 겨드랑이 밑에 얌전히 매달릴 터였다. 외투 주머니에 손을 넣어 자루 끝을 줄곧 붙잡고 있으면 도끼가 흔들리지 않을 것이고, 게다가 외투가 워낙 헐렁해서 사실상 자루나 다름없었으므로, 주머니에 든 손으로 무언가를 붙잡고 있다는 것이 겉으로는 보이지 않을 터였다. 이 올가미 역시 그가 두 주 전에 고안해 둔 것이었다.\n\n이 일을 끝내자, 그는 소파와 바닥 사이의 작은 틈으로 손을 밀어 넣어 왼쪽 구석을 더듬었고, 오래전에 준비해 거기 숨겨 둔 저당물을 끄집어냈다. 그러나 이 저당물이라는 것은 사실 은제 담뱃갑 크기에 그만한 두께로 매끈하게 대패질한 나뭇조각에 불과했다. 그는 어느 작업장 비슷한 것이 있던 안뜰을 헤매던 중에 이 나뭇조각을 주웠다. 나중에 그는 그 나뭇조각에 얇고 매끈한 쇳조각을 덧붙였는데, 그것 역시 같은 때에 거리에서 주운 것이었다. 나뭇조각보다 조금 작은 그 쇳조각을 나뭇조각 위에 얹고, 실을 그 둘레로 가로세로 교차시켜 가며 단단히 묶었다. 그러고는 그것을 깨끗한 흰 종이로 조심스럽고 정갈하게 싸서, 풀기가 무척 어렵도록 꾸러미를 묶었다. 이것은 노파가 매듭을 풀려고 애쓰는 동안 잠시 그녀의 주의를 딴 데로 돌려, 그렇게 한순간을 벌기 위한 것이었다. 쇳조각을 덧붙인 것은 무게를 주어, 노파가 처음 한순간에 그 '물건'이 나무로 되어 있다는 것을 눈치채지 못하도록 하기 위해서였다. 이 모든 것을 그는 미리 소파 밑에 간직해 두었다. 저당물을 막 끄집어냈을 때, 그는 안뜰에서 갑자기 누군가가 움직이는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"여섯 시는 친 지 한참 됐어.\"\n\n\"한참 됐다고! 맙소사!\"\n\n그는 문가로 달려가 귀를 기울였고, 모자를 집어 들고는 열세 개의 계단을 조심스럽게, 소리 없이, 고양이처럼 내려가기 시작했다. 그에게는 아직 가장 중요한 일이 남아 있었으니 — 부엌에서 도끼를 훔쳐 내는 일이었다. 도끼로 그 일을 해야 한다는 것은 오래전에 결정해 둔 바였다. 그에게는 접이식 전지용 칼도 있었지만, 칼을 믿을 수 없었고 자기 힘은 더더욱 믿을 수 없었기에, 결국 도끼로 마음을 굳힌 것이었다. 한 가지 짚고 넘어가자면, 이 일과 관련해 그가 내린 모든 최종 결심에는 한 가지 기이한 특징이 있었다. 그것들이 더 최종적인 것이 될수록, 그 즉시 그의 눈에는 더 끔찍하고 더 터무니없는 것으로 비쳤다는 점이다. 그 모든 괴로운 내적 투쟁에도 불구하고, 그는 그동안 단 한순간도 자기 계획이 실행되리라고는 믿을 수 없었다.\n\n그리고 정말이지, 만일 모든 것이 사소한 점 하나까지 다 검토되어 최종적으로 매듭지어지고, 어떤 종류의 불확실함도 남지 않게 되는 일이 일어났더라면, 그는 아마도 그 모든 것을 터무니없고, 끔찍하며, 불가능한 일로 여겨 포기해 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 정리되지 않은 점들과 불확실함이 산더미처럼 남아 있었다. 도끼를 구하는 일로 말하자면, 그 하찮은 일은 그에게 아무런 근심도 끼치지 않았으니, 그보다 더 쉬운 일도 없었기 때문이다. 나스타시야는 늘 집을 비웠는데, 특히 저녁이면 그랬다. 그녀는 이웃집이나 가게에 들르려고 뛰쳐나가곤 했고, 늘 문을 빠끔히 열어 두었다. 그것은 집주인 여자가 노상 그녀를 나무라는 단 한 가지 일이었다. 그러니 때가 되면 그는 그저 부엌으로 슬며시 들어가 도끼를 집어 들기만 하면 되었고, 한 시간 후에 (모든 일이 끝난 뒤) 들어가 다시 제자리에 갖다 놓기만 하면 되었다. 그러나 여기엔 미심쩍은 점들이 있었다. 한 시간 후에 그것을 도로 갖다 놓으러 돌아왔는데, 나스타시야가 돌아와 그 자리에 있다고 치자. 물론 그는 그냥 지나쳐 그녀가 다시 나갈 때까지 기다려야 할 것이다. 하지만 그러는 사이 그녀가 도끼가 없어진 것을 알아채고, 그것을 찾으며 소동을 벌인다고 치면 — 그것은 의심을, 적어도 의심할 근거를 의미하게 될 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그런 것들은 죄다 사소한 일들이라 그는 아직 고려하기 시작하지도 않았고, 사실 그럴 겨를도 없었다. 그는 가장 중요한 점을 생각하고 있었고, 사소한 세부 사항들은 이 모든 것을 믿을 수 있게 될 때까지 미뤄 두었다. 그러나 그것은 도저히 다다를 수 없는 일처럼 보였다. 적어도 그 자신에게는 그렇게 느껴졌다. 이를테면 그는 자기가 언젠가 생각을 멈추고, 일어나서, 그저 그곳으로 가게 되리라는 것을 도무지 상상할 수 없었다……. 심지어 요전의 시도조차 (즉, 현장을 최종적으로 둘러보려는 목적의 그 방문조차) 그저 시도를 위한 시도였을 뿐, 진짜와는 거리가 멀었으니, 마치 \"자, 가서 한번 해 보자고 — 뭐 하러 공상만 하고 있어!\" 하고 말해 놓고는, 그 즉시 무너져 자기 자신에게 화가 치밀어 욕설을 퍼부으며 달아나 버린 것과 같았다. 그러는 한편, 도덕적 문제에 관한 한, 그의 분석은 완결된 듯 보였다. 그의 궤변은 면도날처럼 날카로워졌고, 그는 자기 안에서 이성적인 반론을 찾아낼 수 없었다. 그러나 최후의 순간에 그는 그저 자기 자신을 믿지 못하게 되었고, 마치 누군가가 그를 억지로 떠밀어 그쪽으로 끌고 가기라도 하는 것처럼, 끈질기게, 노예처럼, 사방을 더듬으며 온갖 방향에서 논거를 구했다.\n\n처음에는 — 사실 한참 전부터 — 그는 한 가지 문제에 몰두해 있었다. 어째서 거의 모든 범죄가 그토록 서투르게 은폐되어 그토록 쉽게 발각되는가, 그리고 어째서 거의 모든 범죄자가 그토록 명백한 흔적을 남기는가 하는 것이었다. 그는 차츰 갖가지 다양하고 기이한 결론에 다다랐는데, 그의 생각으로는 그 주된 원인이 범죄를 은폐하는 일의 물리적 불가능성에 있다기보다는 범죄자 자신에게 있었다. 거의 모든 범죄자는 신중함과 조심성이 가장 필수적인 바로 그 순간에, 어린애 같고 경이로울 정도의 부주의로 말미암아 의지력과 판단력의 상실에 빠진다. 그의 확신에 따르면, 이러한 이성의 마비와 의지력의 상실은 마치 질병처럼 인간을 덮쳐, 차츰 발전하여 범행 직전에 그 정점에 이르고, 범행의 순간에도 똑같은 격렬함으로 지속되며, 그 후로도 개별적인 경우에 따라 더 길거나 짧은 시간 동안 이어지다가, 이윽고 여느 질병처럼 사라진다는 것이었다. 질병이 범죄를 일으키는 것인지, 아니면 범죄가 그 고유한 본성으로 말미암아 늘 질병 비슷한 무언가를 동반하는 것인지, 그 문제에 대해서는 그는 아직 단정할 수 없다고 느꼈다.\n\n이러한 결론에 다다르자, 그는 자기 자신의 경우에는 그런 병적인 반응이 있을 리 없다고, 자기 계획을 실행하는 순간에도 자신의 이성과 의지는 온전히 유지될 것이라고 결론지었는데, 그 단순한 이유는 자기 계획이 \"범죄가 아니기\" 때문이라는 것이었다……. 그가 이 마지막 결론에 이르게 된 과정은 모두 생략하기로 하자. 우리는 이미 너무 앞서 나가 버렸으니까……. 다만 이 일의 실제적이고 순전히 물질적인 어려움들이 그의 머릿속에서 부차적인 위치를 차지하고 있었다는 점만 덧붙여 두자. \"그저 자신의 의지력과 이성을 온전히 지니고 그것들에 대처하기만 하면, 일단 이 일의 가장 사소한 세부 사항까지 익숙해지고 나면, 때가 됐을 때 그것들은 죄다 극복될 거야…….\" 그러나 이런 준비는 결코 시작된 적이 없었다. 그가 가장 믿지 못하게 된 것은 다름 아닌 자신의 최종 결심들이었고, 막상 시간이 닥쳤을 때, 모든 것은 전혀 다르게, 말하자면 우발적이고 예기치 못하게 벌어졌다.\n\n한 가지 사소한 사정이, 그가 미처 계단을 벗어나기도 전에 그의 계산을 뒤엎어 버렸다. 그가 집주인 여자의 부엌에 다다랐을 때, 그 문은 여느 때처럼 열려 있었는데, 그는 나스타시야가 없는 사이에 혹시 집주인 여자 본인이 거기 있는지, 그렇지 않다면 그가 도끼를 가지러 들어갈 때 그녀가 내다보지 못하도록 그녀 방문이 닫혀 있는지 살펴보려고 조심스레 안을 들여다보았다. 그러나 나스타시야가 부엌에 집에 있을 뿐 아니라, 거기서 바구니에서 빨랫감을 꺼내 빨랫줄에 널며 일까지 하고 있는 것을 갑자기 보았을 때 그가 얼마나 경악했던지! 그를 보자 그녀는 빨래 너는 일을 멈추고 그에게 몸을 돌려, 그가 지나가는 내내 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그는 눈길을 돌려, 아무것도 알아채지 못한 척하며 지나갔다. 그러나 이것으로 모든 게 끝장이었다. 그에게는 도끼가 없었으니! 그는 망연자실했다.\n\n'대체 무슨 생각으로,' 그는 대문 밑을 지나며 곱씹었다. '대체 무슨 생각으로 그 여자가 그 순간에 틀림없이 집에 없을 거라고 여겼던 걸까! 왜, 왜, 어째서 나는 그걸 그렇게 확신했지?'\n\n그는 짓눌렸고, 심지어 굴욕감마저 느꼈다. 화가 치밀어 자기 자신을 비웃을 지경이었다……. 둔중하고 짐승 같은 분노가 그의 안에서 부글부글 끓어올랐다.\n\n그는 대문간에 서서 망설였다. 거리로 나가, 남의 눈을 의식해 산책이라도 하는 것은 역겨웠고, 자기 방으로 돌아가는 것은 더더욱 역겨웠다. '이렇게 영영 다시없을 기회를 놓쳐 버리다니!' 그는 목적도 없이 대문간에, 마침 열려 있던 문지기의 작고 어두운 방 바로 맞은편에 서서 중얼거렸다. 갑자기 그는 흠칫 놀랐다. 그에게서 두 걸음쯤 떨어진 문지기 방 안, 오른쪽 의자 밑에서 무언가 번쩍이는 것이 그의 눈을 사로잡았다……. 그는 주위를 둘러보았다 — 아무도 없었다. 그는 발끝으로 살금살금 그 방으로 다가가, 두 계단을 내려가 안으로 들어가서는 가냘픈 목소리로 문지기를 불렀다. '그래, 집에 없군! 하지만 어딘가 근처에, 안뜰에 있는 거야, 문이 활짝 열려 있으니.' 그는 도끼로 (그것은 도끼였다) 달려들어, 장작 두 토막 사이에 놓여 있던 그것을 의자 밑에서 끄집어냈다. 그는 방을 나가기에 앞서 즉시 그것을 올가미에 단단히 끼우고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 방을 나섰다. 아무도 그를 보지 못했다! '이성이 말을 듣지 않을 땐, 악마가 도와주는 법이지!' 그는 묘한 쓴웃음을 지으며 생각했다. 이 우연은 그의 기운을 유난히 북돋워 주었다.\n\n그는 의심을 사지 않으려고 서두르지 않고, 조용하고 차분하게 걸어갔다. 그는 행인들을 거의 쳐다보지 않았고, 아예 그들의 얼굴을 보지 않으려, 가능한 한 눈에 띄지 않으려 애썼다. 갑자기 그는 자기 모자가 떠올랐다. '맙소사! 그저께 돈이 있었는데도 대신 쓸 납작모자 하나를 사 두지 않았다니!' 영혼 깊은 곳에서 저주가 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n곁눈질로 어느 가게 안을 흘끗 들여다본 그는 벽에 걸린 시계로 일곱 시 십 분이라는 것을 알았다. 서둘러야 했고, 동시에 다른 쪽에서 그 집으로 다가가기 위해 빙 돌아가야 했다…….\n\n전에 이 모든 것을 미리 상상했을 때, 그는 이따금 자기가 몹시 무서워하리라고 생각했었다. 그러나 지금 그는 그리 무섭지 않았으니, 사실 전혀 무섭지 않았다. 그의 머릿속은 심지어 엉뚱한 일들로 가득 차 있었지만, 어느 것도 오래가지는 않았다. 유수포프 정원을 지날 때, 그는 거대한 분수를 세우는 일과, 그것이 모든 광장의 대기에 가져다줄 상쾌한 효과에 대한 생각에 깊이 빠져들었다. 차츰 그는, 만일 여름 정원을 마르스 광장까지 넓히고, 어쩌면 미하일롭스키 궁전의 정원과 잇는다면, 그것은 멋진 일이 될 것이며 이 도시에 큰 이로움이 되리라는 확신에 이르렀다. 그러다가 그는 어째서 모든 대도시에서 사람들이 단지 필요에 떠밀려서가 아니라, 무언가 별난 방식으로, 정원도 분수도 없고, 흙먼지와 악취와 온갖 더러움이 가장 심한 도시의 그런 구역에 살려는 경향을 보이는가 하는 문제에 흥미를 느꼈다. 그러자 건초 시장을 거닐던 자신의 산책이 떠올랐고, 한순간 그는 현실로 깨어났다. '이게 다 무슨 헛소리야!' 그는 생각했다. '차라리 아무 생각도 하지 않는 게 낫겠어!'\n\n'사형장으로 끌려가는 사람도 아마 가는 길에 마주치는 온갖 것에 정신이 매달리겠지.' 이런 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔으나, 그저 번쩍, 번개처럼 스쳤을 뿐이었다. 그는 서둘러 이 생각을 떨쳐 냈다……. 그리고 이제 그는 가까이 와 있었다. 여기 그 집이, 여기 그 대문이 있었다. 갑자기 어디선가 시계가 한 번 울렸다. '뭐라고! 일곱 시 반이라고? 그럴 리가, 시계가 빠른 게 분명해!'\n\n그에게는 다행스럽게도, 대문에서 또다시 모든 일이 잘 풀렸다. 바로 그 순간, 마치 일부러 그를 위해서인 양, 거대한 건초 짐마차 한 대가 막 대문으로 들어와, 그가 대문 밑을 지나는 동안 그를 완전히 가려 주었고, 그 짐마차가 미처 안뜰로 다 들어가기도 전에 그는 번개같이 오른쪽으로 슬쩍 빠져 들어갔다. 짐마차 반대편에서는 고함 소리와 다투는 소리가 들려왔지만, 아무도 그를 알아채지 못했고 아무도 그와 마주치지 않았다. 그 거대한 사각형 안뜰을 향한 많은 창문이 그 순간 열려 있었지만, 그는 고개를 들지 않았다 — 그럴 힘이 없었다. 노파의 방으로 통하는 계단은 바로 가까이, 대문 바로 오른쪽에 있었다. 그는 어느새 계단을 오르고 있었다…….\n\n숨을 들이쉬고, 두근거리는 심장에 손을 갖다 대고, 다시 한번 도끼를 더듬어 바로잡으며, 그는 매 순간 귀를 기울이면서 부드럽게, 조심스럽게 계단을 오르기 시작했다. 그러나 계단 역시 텅 비어 있었다. 문들은 죄다 닫혀 있었고, 그는 아무와도 마주치지 않았다. 다만 이 층의 한 셋집이 활짝 열려 있고 그 안에서 도장공들이 일하고 있었지만, 그들은 그에게 눈길조차 주지 않았다. 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 잠시 생각하더니 다시 걸음을 옮겼다. '물론 저들이 여기 없었다면 더 좋았겠지만…… 저긴 그녀 집보다 두 층 아래잖아.'\n\n그리고 어느새 사 층이었다. 여기 그 문이 있었고, 여기 맞은편의, 비어 있는 셋집이 있었다. 노파 집 바로 아래층 셋집도 보아하니 비어 있는 모양이었다. 문에 박아 두었던 명함이 떼어져 있었으니 — 그들은 떠나 버린 것이다!…… 그는 숨이 가빴다. 한순간 '돌아갈까?' 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그러나 그는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 노파의 방문에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 죽음 같은 정적뿐이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 다시 계단의 동정에 귀를 기울였으니, 오래도록 골똘히 귀를 기울였다……. 그런 다음 마지막으로 한 번 주위를 둘러보고, 정신을 가다듬고, 몸을 곧추세우고, 다시 한번 올가미에 든 도끼를 점검해 보았다. '내 얼굴이 너무 창백한가?' 그는 마음속으로 물었다. '내가 동요한 게 빤히 보이는 건 아닐까? 저 여자는 의심이 많은데……. 차라리 좀 더 기다리는 게 나을까…… 심장이 쿵쿵거리는 게 멎을 때까지?'\n\n그러나 그의 심장은 멎지 않았다. 오히려, 마치 그를 약 올리기라도 하듯, 점점 더 격렬하게 두근거렸다. 그는 더는 견딜 수 없어, 천천히 손을 뻗어 초인종을 울렸다. 삼십 초쯤 후, 그는 다시, 좀 더 크게 울렸다.\n\n대답이 없었다. 계속 초인종을 울려 봐야 소용없는 데다 어울리지도 않는 짓이었다. 노파는 물론 집에 있었지만, 의심이 많은 데다 혼자였다. 그는 그녀의 습관을 좀 알고 있었다……. 그래서 그는 다시 한번 문에 귀를 갖다 댔다. 그의 감각이 유난히 예민했던 것이거나 (이렇게 보기는 어렵지만), 아니면 그 소리가 정말로 또렷했던 것이거나, 어쨌든 그는 갑자기 자물쇠에 조심스레 손을 갖다 대는 것 같은 소리와, 바로 문 앞에서 치맛자락이 스치는 소리를 들었다. 누군가가 자물쇠 가까이 살그머니 서서, 그가 바깥에서 하고 있는 것과 똑같이, 안에서 몰래 귀를 기울이며, 문에 귀를 갖다 댄 듯했다……. 그는 숨어 있는 것처럼 보이지 않으려고 일부러 조금 몸을 움직이며 무언가를 소리 내어 중얼거렸고, 그러고는 세 번째로 초인종을 울렸는데, 이번에는 조용히, 차분하게, 조급한 기색 없이 울렸다. 훗날 그것을 회상할 때면, 그 순간이 그의 머릿속에 생생하고 또렷하게, 영원히 남았다. 그는 자기에게 어떻게 그런 교활함이 있었는지 도무지 알 수 없었으니, 그 순간 그의 정신은 이를테면 흐릿해져 있었고, 자기 몸뚱이조차 거의 의식하지 못할 지경이었기 때문이다……. 잠시 후, 그는 빗장이 풀리는 소리를 들었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n문은 아까처럼 아주 조금 빠끔히 열렸고, 또다시 날카롭고 의심에 찬 두 눈이 어둠 속에서 그를 노려보았다. 그 순간 라스콜니코프는 정신을 잃고 하마터면 큰 실수를 저지를 뻔했다.\n\n단둘이 있다는 사실에 노파가 겁을 먹을까 봐 두렵고, 또 자기 모습을 보더라도 그녀의 의심이 풀리리라는 기대도 들지 않았기에, 그는 문을 붙잡고 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 노파가 다시 문을 닫지 못하게 했다. 이를 보고 노파는 문을 도로 잡아당기지는 않았으나 손잡이를 놓지도 않았으므로, 그는 그녀를 문째 계단으로 거의 끌어낼 뻔했다. 노파가 문간에 버티고 서서 자신을 들여보내지 않으려 하는 것을 보고, 그는 곧장 그녀를 향해 다가섰다. 그녀는 놀라서 뒷걸음질 치며 무언가 말하려 했으나, 말이 나오지 않는 듯 눈을 크게 뜨고 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 알료나 이바노브나.\" 그는 태연하게 말하려 애썼으나, 목소리가 말을 듣지 않아 갈라지고 떨려 나왔다. \"가져왔습니다……, 물건을 가져왔어요……, 그런데 안으로 들어가는 게 낫겠습니다……, 밝은 데로요…….\"\n\n그러고는 그녀를 내버려 둔 채, 청하지도 않았건만 곧장 방 안으로 들어갔다. 노파는 그를 뒤쫓아 달려왔다. 그녀의 혀가 풀렸다.\n\n\"맙소사! 이게 뭐요? 누구야? 뭘 원하는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니, 알료나 이바노브나, 저를 아시잖아요……, 라스콜니코프입니다……, 자, 며칠 전에 약속한 저당물을 가져왔어요…….\" 그러고는 저당물을 내밀었다.\n\n노파는 잠시 저당물을 흘긋 쳐다보았으나, 곧장 청하지도 않은 방문객의 눈을 빤히 들여다보았다. 그녀는 뚫어지게, 악의에 차서, 불신에 가득 차 바라보았다. 일 분이 지났다. 그는 심지어 그녀의 눈에서 마치 모든 것을 이미 알아챈 듯한 비웃음 같은 것까지 본 것만 같았다. 그는 자신이 정신을 잃어 가고 있음을, 거의 겁에 질려 있음을 느꼈는데, 만일 그녀가 그렇게 바라보며 또 반 분쯤 말 한마디 없이 있는다면 그는 그녀에게서 달아나 버리고 말 것 같다는 생각이 들 만큼 겁을 먹고 있었다.\n\n\"왜 저를 모르는 사람 보듯 쳐다보시는 겁니까?\" 그가 갑자기, 역시 악의에 차서 말했다. \"원하시면 받으시고, 싫으면 다른 데로 가겠습니다, 저는 바쁘거든요.\"\n\n이런 말을 하려고 생각조차 하지 않았는데, 갑자기 저절로 튀어나온 것이었다. 노파는 정신을 차렸고, 방문객의 단호한 어조에 분명 다시 마음을 놓는 듯했다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌자고, 선생, 이렇게 다짜고짜로……. 그게 뭐요?\" 그녀가 저당물을 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"은 담뱃갑입니다, 지난번에 말씀드렸잖아요, 아시면서.\"\n\n그녀가 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"한데 얼굴이 어쩜 이리도 창백하시나……, 손까지 떨고 있구려? 목욕이라도 하다 왔소, 어쨌소?\"\n\n\"열이 나서요.\" 그가 퉁명스레 대답했다. \"창백해질 수밖에 없지요……, 먹은 게 없으면.\" 그는 가까스로 말을 또박또박 내뱉었다.\n\n다시금 기운이 빠져나가고 있었다. 그러나 그의 대답은 진실처럼 들렸으니, 노파는 저당물을 받아 들었다.\n\n\"이게 뭐요?\" 그녀가 라스콜니코프를 뚫어지게 살피고 손으로 저당물의 무게를 가늠하며 다시 한번 물었다.\n\n\"물건입니다……, 담뱃갑이에요……. 은으로 된……. 보세요.\"\n\n\"어쩐지 은 같지가 않은데……. 어쩜 이리도 칭칭 싸 놨담!\"\n\n끈을 풀려고 애쓰며 그녀가 창문 쪽으로, 빛이 드는 쪽으로 돌아섰을 때(숨 막히는 더위에도 불구하고 그녀의 창문은 죄다 닫혀 있었다), 그녀는 몇 초 동안 그에게서 완전히 등을 돌리고 그를 등진 채 서 있게 되었다. 그는 외투의 단추를 풀고 도끼를 고리에서 빼냈으나, 아직 완전히 꺼내지는 않은 채 그저 외투 밑 오른손에 쥐고 있었다. 그의 손은 끔찍하게 힘이 없어, 매 순간 점점 더 마비되고 나무토막처럼 굳어 가는 것이 느껴졌다. 그는 도끼가 미끄러져 떨어질까 봐 두려웠다……. 갑자기 현기증이 그를 덮쳤다.\n\n\"한데 어쩌자고 이걸 이렇게 칭칭 싸 놨담?\" 노파가 짜증스레 외치며 그에게로 몸을 움직였다.\n\n이제 더는 단 일 분도 허비할 수 없었다. 그는 도끼를 완전히 꺼내 두 팔로 휘둘렀는데, 거의 자기 자신을 의식하지 못한 채, 거의 힘도 들이지 않고, 거의 기계적으로 도끼의 무딘 등을 그녀의 머리에 내리쳤다. 이때 그는 자기 힘을 쓰는 것 같지도 않았다. 그러나 일단 도끼를 한 번 내리치고 나자 그에게 기운이 돌아왔다.\n\n노파는 늘 그렇듯 맨머리였다. 희끗희끗 센 가닥이 섞인 그 가늘고 옅은 머리칼은 기름을 잔뜩 발라 떡이 진 채 쥐꼬리처럼 땋여, 부러진 뿔 빗으로 고정되어 뒤통수에 삐죽 솟아 있었다. 그녀가 워낙 키가 작아서 일격은 정수리 한복판에 떨어졌다. 그녀는 비명을 질렀으나 아주 희미했고, 갑자기 두 손을 머리로 가져가며 풀썩 바닥에 주저앉았다. 한 손에는 여전히 \"저당물\"을 쥔 채였다. 그러자 그는 또 한 번, 또 한 번 무딘 등으로 같은 자리를 내리쳤다. 피가 뒤집힌 잔에서처럼 쏟아져 나오고, 몸뚱이가 뒤로 넘어갔다. 그는 한 발짝 물러나 시체가 쓰러지게 두고는, 곧장 그녀의 얼굴 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그녀는 죽어 있었다. 두 눈은 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 이마와 온 얼굴은 경련을 일으키며 일그러지고 뒤틀려 있었다.\n\n그는 시체 곁 바닥에 도끼를 내려놓고는 곧장 그녀의 주머니를 뒤졌다(흘러나오는 피를 피하려 애쓰면서). 지난번 방문 때 그녀가 열쇠를 꺼냈던 바로 그 오른쪽 주머니였다. 그는 정신을 온전히 가다듬은 상태였고, 혼란도 현기증도 없었으나, 손은 여전히 떨리고 있었다. 그는 훗날, 자신이 유난히 침착하고 조심스러웠으며 줄곧 피를 묻히지 않으려 애썼던 것을 기억해 냈다……. 그는 곧 열쇠들을 꺼냈는데, 전과 마찬가지로 모두 강철 고리 하나에 한 다발로 꿰여 있었다. 그는 그것들을 들고 곧장 침실로 달려갔다. 그곳은 아주 작은 방으로, 성상이 한가득 모셔진 사당 같은 것이 있었다. 맞은편 벽에는 큼직한 침대가 놓여 있었는데, 무척 깨끗했고 비단 조각보를 이어 만든 누비이불이 덮여 있었다. 또 다른 벽에는 서랍장이 있었다. 이상하게도, 그가 열쇠를 서랍장에 끼우기 시작하자마자, 그 열쇠들이 절그럭거리는 소리를 듣자마자 경련하듯 떨림이 그를 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 갑자기 또다시 모든 걸 포기하고 떠나 버리고 싶은 유혹에 휩싸였다. 그러나 그것도 한순간뿐, 되돌리기에는 너무 늦었다. 그는 자기 자신을 보며 분명 미소까지 지었는데, 그때 갑자기 또 다른 끔찍한 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그는 갑자기 노파가 아직 살아 있어 정신을 차릴지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 그는 열쇠를 서랍장에 꽂아 둔 채 시체로 달려가 도끼를 움켜쥐고 다시 한번 노파 위로 쳐들었으나, 내리치지는 않았다. 그녀가 죽었다는 데는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 몸을 숙여 다시 한번 좀 더 가까이 살펴보니, 두개골이 깨지고 한쪽은 심지어 짓이겨져 있는 것이 또렷이 보였다. 그는 손가락으로 만져 보려 했으나 손을 도로 거두었으니, 사실 그러지 않아도 분명했다. 그러는 사이 완연한 핏웅덩이가 고였다. 갑자기 그는 그녀의 목에 끈이 걸려 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 그것을 잡아당겼으나 끈은 질겨서 끊어지지 않았고, 게다가 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 그는 옷 앞섶에서 그것을 빼내려 했으나, 무언가가 걸려 빠져나오질 않았다. 조바심이 나서 그는 다시 도끼를 쳐들어 끈을 몸 위에서 위쪽부터 끊으려 했으나 감히 그러지는 못하고, 가까스로 자기 손과 도끼에 피를 묻혀 가며 이 분간 허둥지둥 애쓴 끝에, 시체에 도끼를 대지 않고 끈을 끊어 벗겨 냈다. 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다 — 그것은 돈주머니였다. 끈에는 십자가 두 개가 달려 있었는데, 하나는 키프로스 나무로, 다른 하나는 구리로 된 것이었고, 또 은 세공으로 만든 성상 하나, 그리고 그것들과 함께 강철 테와 고리가 달린 작고 기름때 묻은 섀미 가죽 돈주머니가 매달려 있었다. 돈주머니는 빵빵하게 채워져 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그것을 들여다보지도 않고 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고, 십자가들은 노파의 몸뚱이 위로 내던지고는, 이번에는 도끼를 챙겨 들고 침실로 달려 들어갔다.\n\n그는 끔찍하게 서둘렀다. 그는 열쇠들을 움켜쥐고 다시 끼워 보기 시작했다. 그러나 잘되지 않았다. 열쇠가 자물쇠에 맞지 않았다. 손이 떨려서라기보다는 자꾸 헛손질을 했던 탓이었다. 이를테면 그는 어떤 열쇠가 맞지 않는다는 것을 빤히 보면서도 여전히 그것을 끼워 보려 했다. 갑자기 그는, 작은 열쇠들과 함께 거기 매달려 있던 홈이 깊이 팬 그 큰 열쇠가(지난번 방문 때 이것이 그의 눈에 띄었다) 서랍장 것일 리 없고, 어떤 튼튼한 궤짝의 것이며, 어쩌면 모든 것이 그 궤짝 안에 숨겨져 있을지도 모른다는 사실을 떠올리고 깨달았다. 그는 서랍장을 내버려 두고 곧장 침대 밑을 더듬었으니, 노파들이 대개 궤짝을 침대 밑에 둔다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 과연 그러했다. 침대 밑에는 길이가 적어도 일 아르신은 되는 꽤 큼직한 궤짝이 있었는데, 아치형 뚜껑에 붉은 가죽이 씌워지고 강철 못이 촘촘히 박혀 있었다. 홈이 팬 열쇠가 곧장 들어맞아 궤짝이 열렸다. 맨 위에는 흰 천 아래로 토끼 가죽을 댄 붉은 비단 외투가 있었고, 그 아래에는 비단 드레스, 그다음에는 숄이 있었는데, 그 밑에는 옷가지밖에 없는 것 같았다. 그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 피 묻은 손을 그 붉은 비단에 닦는 것이었다. '붉은색이니까, 붉은 데서는 피가 덜 눈에 띄겠지.' 이런 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그러다 그는 갑자기 정신이 들었다. '맙소사, 내가 미쳐 가는 건가?' 그는 공포에 질려 생각했다.\n\n그러나 그가 옷가지를 건드리자마자 모피 외투 밑에서 금시계 하나가 미끄러져 나왔다. 그는 서둘러 모든 것을 뒤집어 보았다. 옷가지들 사이에서 금으로 된 갖가지 물건들이 나왔는데 — 아마도 찾아가지 못했거나 찾아가기를 기다리는 저당물들이리라 — 팔찌, 사슬, 귀걸이, 핀 따위였다. 어떤 것들은 갑에 들어 있고, 어떤 것들은 그저 신문지에 정성스럽고 가지런히 접어 싸여 끈으로 동여매여 있었다. 그는 지체 없이, 꾸러미와 갑들을 살피거나 풀어 보지도 않은 채 바지와 외투 주머니를 채우기 시작했다. 그러나 미처 많이 챙길 새도 없었다…….\n\n그는 갑자기 노파가 누워 있는 방에서 발소리를 들었다. 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 죽은 듯이 가만히 있었다. 그러나 모든 것이 고요했으니, 분명 그의 착각이었으리라. 그러다 갑자기 그는 누군가가 나직이 끊어지는 신음을 내뱉은 듯, 희미한 비명을 또렷이 들었다. 그러고 나서 다시 일이 분간 죽은 듯한 정적이 흘렀다. 그는 궤짝 옆에 쪼그려 앉아 숨을 죽인 채 기다렸다. 갑자기 그는 벌떡 일어나 도끼를 움켜쥐고 침실에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n방 한가운데에 리자베타가 큼직한 보따리를 품에 안고 서 있었다. 그녀는 살해된 언니를 멍하니 바라보고 있었는데, 백지장처럼 하얗게 질려 비명을 지를 힘조차 없는 듯했다. 그가 침실에서 뛰쳐나오는 것을 보자 그녀는 나뭇잎처럼 온몸을 희미하게 떨기 시작했고, 얼굴에 경련이 스쳤다. 그녀는 손을 들어 입을 벌렸으나, 여전히 비명을 지르지는 못했다. 그녀는 그에게서 천천히 뒷걸음질 치며 구석으로 물러나면서, 그를 뚫어지게, 집요하게 바라보았으나, 여전히 아무 소리도 내지 못했으니, 마치 비명을 지를 숨조차 들이쉬지 못하는 듯했다. 그는 도끼를 들고 그녀에게 달려들었다. 그녀의 입이 가련하게 씰룩거렸는데, 마치 갓난아기들이 겁을 먹기 시작할 때, 자기를 겁먹게 하는 것을 뚫어지게 바라보며 막 울음을 터뜨리려 할 때 짓는 그런 입 모양이었다. 그리고 이 가엾은 리자베타는 어찌나 천진하고, 또 어찌나 철저하게 짓눌리고 겁에 질려 있었던지, 그 순간 무엇보다 절실하고 자연스러운 행동이었을 텐데도 자기 얼굴을 막으려고 손조차 들지 않았으니, 도끼가 그녀의 얼굴 위로 쳐들려 있었는데도 말이다. 그녀는 그저 빈 왼손을 들었을 뿐인데, 그것도 얼굴 쪽이 아니라, 마치 그를 밀어내려는 듯 천천히 앞으로 뻗었을 따름이었다. 도끼는 날카로운 날이 정통으로 정수리에 떨어져, 단 한 번에 머리 윗부분을 쪼개 놓았다. 그녀는 곧장 무겁게 쓰러졌다. 라스콜니코프는 완전히 정신을 잃은 채, 그녀의 보따리를 움켜쥐었다가 다시 떨어뜨리고 현관으로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n공포가 점점 더 그를 사로잡았으니, 특히 이 두 번째의, 전혀 예기치 못한 살인 이후로는 더더욱 그랬다. 그는 한시바삐 그곳에서 달아나고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다. 그리고 만일 그 순간 그가 좀 더 올바르게 보고 헤아릴 수 있었더라면, 만일 그가 자기 처지의 온갖 곤란함과 절망, 그 끔찍함과 부조리를 깨달을 수 있었더라면, 만일 그곳을 빠져나가 집으로 돌아가기까지 자신이 아직 얼마나 많은 장애를, 어쩌면 또 얼마나 많은 범죄를 극복하거나 저질러야 하는지를 이해할 수 있었더라면, 그는 십중팔구 모든 것을 내팽개치고 자수하러 갔을지도 모른다. 그것도 두려움 때문이 아니라, 그저 자신이 저지른 일에 대한 공포와 혐오 때문에. 특히 혐오의 감정이 그의 내면에서 솟구쳐 매 순간 점점 더 강해졌다. 그는 이제 세상 그 무엇을 준다 해도 궤짝으로는, 아니 그 방으로조차 다시 들어가지 않았으리라.\n\n그러나 일종의 멍한 상태가, 심지어 꿈결 같은 느낌마저 차츰 그를 사로잡기 시작했다. 어떤 순간이면 그는 자기 자신을 잊어버렸으니, 아니 더 정확히 말하면, 중요한 것을 잊어버린 채 사소한 것들에 매달렸다. 그러나 부엌을 흘긋 들여다보고 의자 위에 물이 반쯤 찬 양동이가 놓인 것을 보자, 그는 손과 도끼를 씻을 생각이 떠올랐다. 그의 손은 피로 끈적거렸다. 그는 도끼를 날이 물에 잠기도록 떨어뜨려 넣고, 창가의 깨진 접시에 놓인 비누 한 조각을 집어 들고 양동이에 손을 씻기 시작했다. 손이 깨끗해지자 그는 도끼를 꺼내 날을 씻고, 핏자국이 있는 나무 자루를 비누로 문지르며 오랫동안, 한 삼 분쯤 씻었다. 그런 다음 부엌에 줄을 매어 말리고 있던 무명천으로 모두 닦아 내고는, 또 한참을 창가에서 도끼를 꼼꼼히 살펴보았다. 도끼에는 아무런 흔적도 남아 있지 않았고, 다만 나무 자루가 아직 축축할 뿐이었다. 그는 조심스레 외투 밑 고리에 도끼를 걸었다. 그러고 나서 그는 부엌의 희미한 빛 속에서 가능한 한 자기 외투와 바지, 장화를 살펴보았다. 언뜻 보기에는 장화에 묻은 얼룩 말고는 아무것도 없는 듯했다. 그는 헝겊을 적셔 장화를 문질렀다. 그러나 그는 자신이 제대로 살피지 못하고 있다는 것을, 자신이 놓치고 있는 아주 눈에 띄는 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 방 한가운데 서서 생각에 잠겼다. 어둡고 괴로운 상념들이 마음속에 떠올랐다 — 자신이 미쳤다는 생각, 그리고 그 순간 자신이 헤아릴 능력도, 스스로를 지킬 능력도 없으며, 어쩌면 지금 하고 있는 것과는 전혀 다른 무언가를 해야 마땅하다는 생각이었다. \"맙소사!\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"달아나야 해, 달아나야 해.\" 그러고는 현관으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 그러나 여기서 그는 일찍이 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 공포의 충격과 맞닥뜨렸다.\n\n그는 멈춰 서서 바라보았으나 자기 눈을 믿을 수 없었다. 문이, 그가 조금 전 기다리며 초인종을 울렸던 계단 쪽 바깥문이 잠기지 않은 채 적어도 여섯 인치는 열려 있었다. 자물쇠도, 빗장도 없이, 그동안 줄곧, 그 모든 시간 동안 말이다! 노파가 어쩌면 조심하느라 그의 뒤로 문을 닫지 않았던 것이다. 그러나, 맙소사! 아니, 그는 그 뒤에 리자베타를 보지 않았던가! 그런데 어떻게, 어떻게 그녀가 어떻게든 들어왔으리라는 데 생각이 미치지 못했단 말인가! 그녀가 벽을 뚫고 들어왔을 리는 없지 않은가!\n\n그는 문으로 달려가 빗장을 걸었다.\n\n\"아니, 또 잘못했어! 빠져나가야 해, 빠져나가야 한다고…….\"\n\n그는 빗장을 풀고 문을 열어 계단의 소리에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다.\n\n그는 오랫동안 귀를 기울였다. 어딘가 멀리서, 어쩌면 대문께에서 두 목소리가 크고 날카롭게 외치며 다투고 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었다. \"저들은 무슨 일이지?\" 그는 참을성 있게 기다렸다. 마침내 모든 것이 마치 갑자기 끊긴 듯 잠잠해졌다. 그들이 헤어진 것이다. 그가 막 나가려는 참인데, 갑자기 한 층 아래에서 문이 요란하게 열리더니 누군가가 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 계단을 내려가기 시작했다. '어째서 다들 이렇게 시끄럽게 군담?' 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그는 다시 문을 닫고 기다렸다. 마침내 모든 것이 잠잠해졌고, 단 한 사람도 움직이지 않았다. 그가 막 계단 쪽으로 한 발짝 내디디려는데, 또다시 새로운 발소리가 들려왔다.\n\n발소리는 아주 멀리서, 계단의 맨 아래에서 들려왔으나, 그는 첫 소리부터 어쩐 영문인지 이것이 누군가가 이리로, 사 층으로, 노파에게로 올라오는 소리라고 의심하기 시작했음을 아주 또렷하고 분명하게 기억했다. 어째서일까? 그 소리들이 어쩐지 유별나고 심상찮았던 것일까? 발소리는 무겁고, 고르고, 서두르지 않았다. 자, 그가 이제 이 층을 지났다, 자, 더 높이 올라오고 있다, 점점 더 또렷해지고 있었다! 그의 거친 숨소리까지 들렸다. 그리고 이제 삼 층에 다다랐다. 이리로 오고 있다! 그러자 갑자기 그는 자신이 돌로 변해 버린 것만 같았고, 마치 누군가에게 쫓기다 거의 붙잡혀 살해당할 처지인데 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고 팔조차 움직일 수 없는 그런 꿈속 같았다.\n\n마침내 그 낯선 자가 사 층으로 올라오자, 그는 갑자기 흠칫하더니, 잽싸고 날쌔게 셋집 안으로 도로 미끄러져 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 닫는 데 성공했다. 그러고는 걸쇠를 잡아 부드럽게, 소리 없이 고리에 끼웠다. 본능이 그를 도왔다. 그러고 나서 그는 숨을 죽인 채 문 옆에 웅크렸다. 낯선 방문객도 이제 문 앞에 와 있었다. 그들은 이제 서로 마주 보고 서 있었는데, 마치 조금 전 그가 노파와 마주 서 있을 때, 문이 둘 사이를 가르고 그가 귀를 기울이고 있던 그때처럼.\n\n방문객은 여러 차례 헐떡거렸다. '덩치 크고 뚱뚱한 사내가 분명해.' 라스콜니코프는 손에 쥔 도끼를 움켜쥐며 생각했다. 정말이지 꿈만 같았다. 방문객은 초인종을 잡고 요란하게 울렸다.\n\n양철 초인종이 딸랑거리자마자, 라스콜니코프는 방 안에서 무언가 움직이는 것을 알아차린 듯했다. 몇 초 동안 그는 자못 진지하게 귀를 기울였다. 낯선 자는 다시 초인종을 울리고 기다리다가, 갑자기 문손잡이를 거칠고 조급하게 잡아당겼다. 라스콜니코프는 고리에서 흔들리는 걸쇠를 공포에 질려 바라보며, 매 순간 그 걸쇠가 뽑혀 나오지 않을까 망연한 두려움 속에서 기다렸다. 사내가 어찌나 거칠게 흔들어 대는지 정말 그럴 수도 있을 것만 같았다. 걸쇠를 붙잡고 싶었으나, 그러면 그자가 눈치챌지도 몰랐다. 다시금 현기증이 그를 덮쳤다. '쓰러지고 말겠어!' 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤으나, 낯선 자가 말을 하기 시작하자 그는 곧 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야? 잠든 거야, 아니면 뒈진 거야? 빌어먹을 것들!\" 그가 걸걸한 목소리로 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"이봐, 알료나 이바노브나, 이 늙은 마녀야! 리자베타 이바노브나, 이봐, 내 미인 아가씨! 문 열어! 아, 빌어먹을 것들! 잠든 거야, 뭐야?\"\n\n그러고는 또다시 화가 치밀어 있는 힘껏 열두 번이나 초인종을 잡아당겼다. 분명 권세깨나 있고 친하게 드나드는 자임이 틀림없었다.\n\n이 순간 멀지 않은 계단에서 가볍고 다급한 발소리가 들려왔다. 또 누군가가 다가오고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 처음에는 그 소리를 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"설마 집에 아무도 없다는 건 아니겠죠.\" 새로 온 자가 여전히 초인종을 잡아당기고 있던 첫 번째 방문객에게 명랑하고 낭랑한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"안녕하십니까, 코흐.\"\n\n'목소리를 들어 보니 꽤 젊은 자로군.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다.\n\n\"누가 알겠나, 빌어먹을? 자물쇠를 거의 부술 뻔했어.\" 코흐가 대답했다. \"그런데 자네가 어떻게 나를 아나?\"\n\n\"아니! 그저께 감브리누스에서 당구로 당신을 내리 세 판이나 이겼잖아요.\"\n\n\"아!\"\n\n\"그래서 집에 없다는 거예요? 거참 이상하네. 하지만 정말 어처구니없는 일이군요. 그 노파가 대체 어딜 갔을까요? 일이 있어 왔는데.\"\n\n\"그래, 나도 그 여자한테 볼일이 있단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩌죠? 돌아가는 수밖에 없겠네요. 아이고, 아이고! 돈 좀 받을까 했더니!\" 젊은이가 외쳤다.\n\n\"물론 포기하는 수밖에 없지, 한데 이번엔 어쩌자고 시간을 잡아 놓은 거야? 그 늙은 마녀가 나더러 오라고 직접 시간을 정해 놓고선. 여기 오는 게 내 길에서 한참 벗어나는데도 말이야. 게다가 그 여자가 대체 어딜 갔는지 도무지 알 수가 없군. 일 년 내내 여기 죽치고 앉아 있는 늙은 할망구가, 다리도 성치 않으면서, 한데 별안간 산책이라도 나갔단 말인가!\"\n\n\"문지기한테 물어보는 게 낫지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\"\n\n\"그 여자가 어디 갔는지, 언제 돌아올지 말이에요.\"\n\n\"흠……. 빌어먹을!……. 물어볼 수야 있겠지……. 하지만 그 여자는 어디 나가는 법이 없는데 말이야.\"\n\n그러고는 다시 한번 문손잡이를 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"빌어먹을. 어쩔 도리가 없군, 가야겠어!\"\n\n\"잠깐만요!\" 젊은이가 갑자기 외쳤다. \"당겨 보면 문이 어떻게 흔들리는지 보이세요?\"\n\"그게 어쨌다고?\"\n\n\"이건 문이 잠긴 게 아니라 걸쇠로 걸려 있다는 뜻이에요! 걸쇠가 절그럭거리는 소리 들리세요?\"\n\n\"그게 어쨌다고?\"\n\"아니, 모르시겠어요? 이건 둘 중 하나가 집에 있다는 증거잖아요. 만일 다들 나갔다면 바깥에서 열쇠로 문을 잠갔을 거예요, 안에서 걸쇠로 거는 게 아니라. 자, 걸쇠가 절그럭거리는 소리 들리시죠? 안에서 걸쇠를 걸려면 집 안에 있어야 하잖아요, 모르시겠어요? 그러니까 저들은 안에 들어앉아 있으면서 문을 안 여는 거라고요!\"\n\n\"이런! 그러고 보니 정말 그렇겠군!\" 코흐가 놀라 외쳤다. \"대체 안에서 뭘 하고 있는 거야?\" 그러고는 미친 듯이 문을 흔들기 시작했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요!\" 젊은이가 다시 외쳤다. \"당기지 마세요! 분명 뭔가 잘못된 거예요……. 자, 당신이 초인종을 울리고 문을 잡아당겨도 저들이 안 열잖아요! 그러니 둘 다 기절했거나, 아니면…….\"\n\"아니면 뭐?\"\n\n\"내 말 들어 봐요. 가서 문지기를 데려옵시다, 그 사람더러 저들을 깨우게 하자고요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\"\n\n둘은 내려가려 했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요. 당신은 여기 계세요, 내가 뛰어가서 문지기를 데려올 테니.\"\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"아, 그러는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"좋아.\"\n\n\"내가 법학을 공부하고 있거든요! 분명해요, 분-명-히 여기 뭔가 잘못된 거라고요!\" 젊은이가 흥분해 외치고는 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n코흐는 남았다. 그는 다시 한번 초인종을 부드럽게 건드려 한 번 딸랑 울리게 하고는, 가만히, 마치 곰곰이 생각하고 주위를 둘러보듯, 문손잡이를 만지작거리며 당겼다 놓았다 하면서, 그것이 그저 걸쇠로만 걸려 있다는 것을 다시 한번 확인했다. 그런 다음 씩씩거리고 헐떡거리며 몸을 숙여 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보기 시작했다. 그러나 열쇠가 안쪽에서 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있어 아무것도 보이지 않았다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 도끼를 꽉 움켜쥐고 서 있었다. 그는 일종의 착란 상태였다. 그는 그들이 들어오면 맞서 싸울 채비까지 하고 있었다. 그들이 문을 두드리며 서로 이야기를 나누는 동안, 한꺼번에 모든 것을 끝장내고 문 너머로 그들에게 소리쳐 버릴까 하는 생각이 몇 번이나 떠올랐다. 이따금 그는 그들이 문을 열지 못하는 사이에 그들에게 욕설을 퍼붓고, 비웃어 주고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔다! \"제발 좀 서둘러라!\" 하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다.\n\n\"한데 저놈은 대체 뭘 하고 있는 거야?……\" 시간이 흘러갔다, 일 분, 또 일 분 — 아무도 오지 않았다. 코흐가 안절부절못하기 시작했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 대체!\" 그가 갑자기 외치더니, 조바심을 못 이겨 보초 임무를 팽개치고 그 역시 무거운 장화로 계단을 쿵쿵 울리며 서둘러 내려갔다. 발소리가 잦아들었다.\n\n\"맙소사! 어떻게 해야 하나?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 걸쇠를 풀고 문을 열었다 — 아무 소리도 없었다. 그는 불쑥, 아무 생각도 없이 밖으로 나가 가능한 한 꼼꼼하게 문을 닫고 계단을 내려가기 시작했다.\n\n그가 세 층을 내려왔을 때 갑자기 아래쪽에서 큰 목소리가 들려왔다 — 어디로 가야 하나! 숨을 곳이 없었다. 그는 막 셋집으로 되돌아가려 했다.\n\n\"이봐, 거기! 저 짐승 같은 놈을 잡아!\"\n\n누군가가 아래층 셋집에서 고함을 지르며 뛰쳐나와, 목청껏 악을 쓰면서 계단을 뛰어 내려간다기보다 차라리 굴러떨어졌다.\n\n\"미트카! 미트카! 미트카! 미트카! 미트카! 빌어먹을 놈!\"\n\n고함은 비명으로 끝났다. 마지막 소리들은 안뜰에서 들려왔다. 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 여럿이 크고 빠르게 떠들어 대며 요란하게 계단을 올라오기 시작했다. 서너 명이었다. 그는 그 젊은이의 낭랑한 목소리를 알아들었다. \"이봐!\"\n\n절망에 사로잡혀 그는 곧장 그들을 마주하러 갔으니, '될 대로 되라!' 하는 심정이었다. 그들이 그를 멈춰 세운다면 — 모든 것이 끝장이고, 그를 지나가게 둔다 해도 — 역시 모든 것이 끝장이었으니, 그들이 그를 기억할 테니까. 그들이 다가오고 있었다. 그들은 그에게서 한 층밖에 떨어져 있지 않았다 — 그런데 갑자기 구원이 나타났다! 그에게서 오른쪽으로 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에, 문이 활짝 열린 빈 셋집이 있었다. 페인트공들이 일하고 있던 이 층 셋집이었는데, 마치 그를 위해서인 듯 그들이 방금 비워 둔 것이었다. 방금 고함을 지르며 뛰어 내려간 것이 바로 그들임에 틀림없었다. 바닥은 막 칠을 한 참이었고, 방 한가운데에는 양동이 하나와 페인트와 붓이 담긴 깨진 단지가 놓여 있었다. 그는 순식간에 열린 문 안으로 휙 들어가 벽 뒤에 숨었고, 그것도 아슬아슬한 순간이었다. 그들은 이미 층계참에 다다라 있었다. 그러더니 그들은 방향을 돌려 큰 소리로 떠들며 사 층으로 올라갔다. 그는 기다렸다가 발끝으로 살금살금 나와 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n계단에도, 대문께에도 아무도 없었다. 그는 재빨리 대문을 지나 거리에서 왼쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n그는 알았다, 그 순간 그들이 셋집에 있다는 것을, 방금 문이 걸려 있던 셋집이 잠겨 있지 않은 것을 발견하고 몹시 놀라리라는 것을, 이제 그들이 시체를 들여다보고 있으리라는 것을, 그리고 또 일 분이 채 지나기 전에 그들이 살인자가 방금 거기 있었으며 어딘가에 숨어 그들 곁을 빠져나가 달아나는 데 성공했다는 것을 짐작하고 완전히 깨달으리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다. 그들은 십중팔구, 자기들이 계단을 올라가는 동안 그가 그 빈 셋집에 있었으리라고 짐작하리라. 그러는 사이에도 그는 감히 걸음을 그리 빨리할 수가 없었으니, 다음 모퉁이까지는 아직 거의 백 야드나 남아 있었다. '어느 대문으로든 슬쩍 들어가 낯선 거리 어딘가에서 기다려 볼까? 아니야, 가망 없어! 도끼를 내던져 버릴까? 마차를 잡아탈까? 가망 없어, 가망 없다고!'\n\n마침내 그는 모퉁이에 다다랐다. 그는 산 것도 죽은 것도 아닌 꼴로 그 모퉁이를 돌아 내려갔다. 여기까지 오니 그는 안전한 곳까지 절반은 온 셈이었고, 그도 그것을 알고 있었다. 사람들이 잔뜩 붐벼 그가 모래알처럼 그 속에 묻혀 버렸기에 위험이 덜했다. 그러나 그가 겪은 그 모든 일이 그를 어찌나 쇠약하게 만들었던지 그는 거의 몸을 움직일 수조차 없었다. 땀이 방울방울 그를 타고 흘러내려, 목덜미가 온통 젖어 있었다. \"이런, 저 친구 어지간히 마셔 댔구먼!\" 그가 운하 강둑으로 나섰을 때 누군가가 그에게 소리쳤다.\n\n그는 이제 자기 자신을 어렴풋이밖에 의식하지 못했고, 멀리 갈수록 더 심해졌다. 그러나 그는 운하 강둑으로 나섰을 때, 거기에 사람이 거의 없어 자신이 더 눈에 띈다는 데 놀라 되돌아갈 생각을 했던 것을 기억했다. 그는 피로로 거의 쓰러질 지경이었으나, 전혀 다른 방향에서 집으로 가려고 한참을 빙 둘러서 갔다.\n\n자기 집 대문을 지날 때 그는 온전히 정신이 들어 있지 않았다! 그는 이미 계단에 올라서고 나서야 도끼를 떠올렸다. 그런데 그에게는 도끼를 제자리에 돌려놓되 그러는 동안 가능한 한 남의 눈에 띄지 않아야 한다는 자못 중대한 문제가 놓여 있었다. 그는 물론 도끼를 아예 돌려놓지 않고 나중에 누군가의 안뜰에 떨어뜨려 두는 편이 훨씬 나을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미칠 능력이 없었다. 그러나 모든 것이 다행스레 풀렸으니, 문지기 방의 문이 닫혀 있긴 했으나 잠겨 있지는 않아서, 십중팔구 문지기가 집에 있는 것 같았다. 그러나 그는 헤아릴 능력을 어찌나 완전히 잃어버렸던지 곧장 그 문으로 걸어가 문을 열었다. 만일 문지기가 그에게 \"뭘 원하시오?\" 하고 물었더라면, 그는 어쩌면 그냥 도끼를 건네주었을지도 모른다. 그러나 다행히 문지기는 또 집에 없었고, 그는 도끼를 의자 밑 제자리에 돌려놓고 전처럼 장작 토막으로 덮어 두는 데까지 성공했다. 그 후 방으로 가는 길에 그는 아무도, 단 한 사람도 마주치지 않았다. 집주인 여자의 문은 닫혀 있었다. 자기 방에 들어서자 그는 입은 그대로 소파에 몸을 내던졌다 — 그는 잠든 것이 아니라, 망연한 망각 속으로 가라앉았다. 만일 그때 누군가 그의 방에 들어왔더라면, 그는 곧장 벌떡 일어나 비명을 질렀으리라. 토막 나고 너덜너덜한 생각들이 그의 머릿속에 그저 우글거렸으나, 아무리 애를 써도 그는 그중 어느 하나도 붙잡을 수 없었고, 어느 하나에도 머물 수 없었다…….\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n그렇게 그는 아주 오랫동안 누워 있었다. 이따금 잠에서 깨어나는 듯했고, 그런 순간이면 밤이 깊었다는 것을 알아차렸지만, 일어나야겠다는 생각은 들지 않았다. 마침내 그는 날이 밝아 오기 시작하는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 등을 대고 누운 채, 조금 전의 망각으로부터 여전히 멍한 상태였다. 거리에서 무섭고 절망적인 비명 소리가 째지듯 솟아올랐는데, 사실 그가 매일 밤 두 시가 지나면 자기 창문 아래로 듣던 소리였다. 이제 그 소리가 그를 깨웠다.\n\n\"아! 술 취한 사내들이 선술집에서 쏟아져 나오는군.\" 그는 생각했다. \"두 시가 지났어.\" 그러고는 누군가 소파에서 끌어 내리기라도 한 듯 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 두 시가 지났다고!\"\n\n그는 소파에 앉았다 — 그리고 순간 모든 것이 떠올랐다! 갑자기, 단번에, 번개처럼 그는 모든 것을 기억해 냈다.\n\n처음 한순간 그는 자신이 미쳐 가는 줄 알았다. 끔찍한 한기가 그를 덮쳤다. 그러나 그 한기는 이미 잠 속에서 한참 전부터 시작된 열병에서 비롯된 것이었다. 이제 그는 갑자기 격렬한 오한에 사로잡혀, 이가 딱딱 부딪치고 온 사지가 떨려 왔다. 그는 문을 열고 귀를 기울이기 시작했다 — 집 안의 모든 것이 잠들어 있었다. 그는 자기 자신과 방 안의 모든 것을 놀란 눈으로 바라보며, 어떻게 어젯밤에 문도 잠그지 않고 들어와서는, 옷도 벗지 않고, 심지어 모자조차 벗지 않은 채로 소파에 몸을 던졌는지 의아해했다. 모자는 벗겨져 베개 곁 바닥에 떨어져 있었다.\n\n\"누가 들어왔더라면 어떻게 생각했을까? 내가 취했다고 — 하지만…….\"\n\n그는 창가로 달려갔다. 빛은 충분했고, 그는 서둘러 머리끝부터 발끝까지, 옷이란 옷은 죄다 살펴보기 시작했다. 흔적은 없는가? 그러나 그런 식으로는 안 될 일이었다. 추위에 떨면서 그는 모든 것을 벗어 다시 살펴보기 시작했다. 그는 마지막 실오라기와 누더기 한 조각까지 죄다 뒤집어 보았고, 자기 자신조차 믿지 못해 세 번이나 거듭 뒤졌다.\n\n그러나 아무것도 없는 듯했다, 흔적 하나 없었다, 다만 한 군데, 바지의 해진 가장자리에 엉겨 붙은 검붉은 핏방울 몇 개가 들러붙어 있을 뿐이었다. 그는 큼직한 접이식 칼을 집어 들어 해진 실밥들을 잘라 냈다. 그 밖에는 더 이상 아무것도 없는 듯했다.\n\n문득 그는 노파의 궤짝에서 꺼낸 지갑과 물건들이 아직 주머니 속에 그대로 들어 있다는 것을 떠올렸다! 그때까지 그것들을 꺼내 숨길 생각조차 하지 못했던 것이다! 옷을 살펴보는 동안에도 그것들을 떠올리지조차 못했다! 이게 무슨 일이람? 그는 즉시 달려가 그것들을 꺼내 탁자 위에 내던졌다. 모든 것을 꺼내고, 주머니를 뒤집어 아무것도 남지 않았는지 확인한 다음, 그는 그 무더기 전부를 구석으로 가져갔다. 벽 아래쪽 벽지가 떨어져 너덜너덜 늘어져 있었다. 그는 벽지 밑 그 구멍 속으로 물건들을 죄다 쑤셔 넣기 시작했다. \"들어갔어! 다 보이지 않게 됐어, 지갑까지도!\" 그는 신이 나서 생각하며 일어나, 어느 때보다 불룩 튀어나온 그 구멍을 멍하니 바라보았다. 갑자기 그는 공포로 온몸을 부르르 떨었다. \"맙소사!\" 그는 절망 속에서 속삭였다. \"내가 도대체 왜 이러지? 이게 숨긴 건가? 이게 물건을 숨기는 방식인가?\"\n\n그는 자질구레한 패물들을 숨겨야 하리라고는 미처 계산하지 못했었다. 그저 돈만 생각했기에, 숨길 장소를 마련해 두지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 이제, 이제 내가 뭘 기뻐하는 거지?\" 그는 생각했다. \"이게 물건을 숨기는 건가? 내 이성이 나를 버리는군 — 그저 그뿐이야!\"\n\n그는 기진맥진하여 소파에 주저앉았고, 곧바로 또 한 차례 견딜 수 없는 오한이 그를 뒤흔들었다. 무심결에 그는 곁의 의자에서 낡은 대학생 시절 겨울 외투를 끌어당겼는데, 거의 누더기가 다 됐어도 아직은 따뜻한 것이었다. 그는 그것을 덮고 다시 한번 졸음과 헛소리 속으로 가라앉았다. 그는 의식을 잃었다.\n\n오 분도 채 지나지 않아 그는 두 번째로 벌떡 일어났고, 즉시 미친 듯이 또다시 자기 옷에 달려들었다.\n\n\"어떻게 아무것도 안 해 놓고 또 잠들 수 있었지? 그래, 그래, 겨드랑이 안쪽에서 고리를 떼어 내지 않았잖아! 깜빡했어, 그런 걸 깜빡하다니! 이런 결정적인 증거를!\"\n\n그는 그 매듭을 뜯어내, 황급히 잘게 잘라서는 그 조각들을 베개 밑 속옷 사이에 던져 넣었다.\n\n\"찢어진 속옷 조각들이야 무슨 일이 생겨도 의심을 살 리 없지. 그렇고말고, 그렇고말고, 어쨌든!\" 그는 방 한가운데 서서 거듭 되뇌었고, 고통스러우리만치 정신을 집중하며 또다시 주위를, 바닥을, 사방을 둘러보기 시작했다, 무언가 빠뜨린 것은 없는지 확인하려고 애쓰면서. 자기의 모든 능력이, 기억마저, 그리고 가장 단순한 사고력조차 자신을 저버리고 있다는 확신이 견딜 수 없는 고문이 되어 가기 시작했다.\n\n\"정말 벌써 시작된 건 아니겠지! 정말 내 형벌이 내게 닥쳐오는 건 아니겠지? 그래, 바로 그거야!\"\n\n바지에서 잘라 낸 해진 누더기 조각들이 정말로 방 한가운데 바닥에, 들어오는 사람이라면 누구나 볼 수 있는 곳에 떨어져 있었던 것이다!\n\n\"내가 도대체 왜 이러지!\" 그는 넋이 나간 사람처럼 또다시 외쳤다.\n\n그때 이상한 생각이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 어쩌면 자기 옷이 죄다 피로 뒤덮여 있을지도, 어쩌면 수많은 얼룩이 있는데 다만 그것을 보지 못하고, 알아차리지 못하는 것일 뿐인지도 모른다고, 자신의 지각이 무너지고 산산조각 나고 있어서…… 이성이 흐려지고 있어서……. 문득 그는 지갑에도 피가 묻어 있었다는 것을 떠올렸다. \"아! 그렇다면 주머니에도 피가 묻어 있을 게 분명해, 젖은 지갑을 주머니에 넣었으니까!\"\n\n번개처럼 그는 주머니를 뒤집어 보았고, 그렇다! — 흔적이 있었다, 주머니 안감에 얼룩이 있었다!\n\n\"그러니 내 이성이 아주 나를 버린 건 아니야, 아직 분별력과 기억이 남아 있어, 내 스스로 이걸 알아차렸으니까.\" 그는 깊은 안도의 한숨을 내쉬며 의기양양하게 생각했다. \"이건 그저 열병으로 인한 쇠약일 뿐, 한순간의 헛소리일 뿐이야.\" 그러고는 바지 왼쪽 주머니의 안감을 통째로 뜯어냈다. 그 순간 햇빛이 그의 왼쪽 장화 위로 떨어졌다. 장화에서 삐져나온 양말에, 흔적이 있는 것만 같았다! 그는 장화를 벗어 던졌다. \"정말 흔적이군! 양말 끝이 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있어.\" 무심결에 그 핏웅덩이를 밟았던 게 분명했다……. \"하지만 이제 이걸 어떻게 하지? 이 양말이며 누더기며 주머니를 어디에 둔단 말인가?\"\n\n그는 그것들을 죄다 손에 그러모은 채 방 한가운데 섰다.\n\n\"난로 속에? 하지만 무엇보다 먼저 난로부터 샅샅이 뒤지겠지. 태워 버릴까? 하지만 무엇으로 태운담? 성냥조차 없는데. 아니야, 차라리 나가서 어디든 죄다 던져 버리는 게 나아. 그래, 던져 버리는 게 낫겠어.\" 그는 다시 소파에 앉으며 되뇌었다. \"그것도 당장, 바로 지금, 꾸물대지 말고…….\"\n\n그러나 그의 머리는 도리어 베개 위로 떨어졌다. 다시 견딜 수 없는 얼음장 같은 오한이 그를 덮쳤다. 그는 다시 외투를 끌어다 덮었다.\n\n그리고 오랫동안, 몇 시간이나, 그는 \"지금 당장, 이 순간에 어디로든 나가서 그것들을 죄다 던져 버려, 눈앞에서 사라지게 하고 끝장을 내자, 당장, 당장!\" 하는 충동에 시달렸다. 그는 몇 번이나 소파에서 일어나려 했지만 일어날 수가 없었다.\n\n마침내 그는 문을 사납게 두드리는 소리에 완전히 잠이 깼다.\n\n\"열어요, 좀, 죽었어요, 살았어요? 여기서 내내 잠만 자는군!\" 나스타시야가 주먹으로 문을 쾅쾅 두드리며 소리쳤다. \"며칠씩 내내 여기서 개처럼 코나 골고 말이야! 정말 개라니까. 열라고요. 열 시가 지났어요.\"\n\n\"집에 없을지도 모르지.\" 한 남자의 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"하! 저건 문지기 목소리잖아……. 저 사람이 뭘 원하는 거지?\"\n\n그는 벌떡 일어나 소파에 앉았다. 심장이 뛰는 것이 그야말로 고통스러웠다.\n\n\"그럼 누가 문을 걸어 잠갔겠어요?\" 나스타시야가 받아쳤다. \"이젠 문을 안에서 걸어 잠그기까지 하네! 훔쳐 갈 게 있기라도 한 것처럼! 열어요, 이 멍청아, 일어나라고요!\"\n\n\"저들이 뭘 원하는 거지? 왜 문지기가? 다 발각된 거야. 버틸까, 열까? 될 대로 되라지!……\"\n\n그는 반쯤 몸을 일으켜, 앞으로 구부려 문고리를 벗겼다.\n\n그의 방은 워낙 작아서 침대에서 내려오지 않고도 문고리를 풀 수 있었다. 그렇다, 문지기와 나스타시야가 거기 서 있었다.\n\n나스타시야가 묘한 눈길로 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그는 도전적이면서도 절망적인 태도로 문지기를 흘끗 보았는데, 문지기는 아무 말 없이 병마개 밀랍으로 봉인된 회색 접지 한 장을 내밀었다.\n\n\"관청에서 온 통지요.\" 그가 종이를 건네며 알렸다.\n\n\"무슨 관청에서?\"\n\n\"경찰서 출두 명령이지요, 당연히. 어느 관청인지 댁이 잘 알 거요.\"\n\n\"경찰서로?…… 무슨 일로?……\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 알겠소? 부르니까 가는 거지.\"\n\n사내는 그를 유심히 바라보고, 방 안을 둘러보더니, 돌아서 가려 했다.\n\n\"이 양반, 완전히 앓고 있네!\" 나스타시야가 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. 문지기가 잠깐 고개를 돌렸다. \"어제부터 열병에 걸렸어요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 아무 대꾸도 없이 종이를 손에 든 채 펴 보지도 않았다. \"그럼 일어나지 말아요.\" 나스타시야가 그가 소파에서 발을 내리는 것을 보고 측은한 듯 말을 이었다. \"앓고 있으니 가지 말아요. 그렇게 서두를 것도 없는데. 그건 또 뭐예요?\"\n\n그는 들여다보았다. 오른손에 그는 바지에서 잘라 낸 조각들과 양말, 그리고 주머니 누더기를 쥐고 있었다. 그러니까 그것들을 손에 쥔 채 잠들었던 것이다. 나중에 곰곰이 따져 보고서 그는, 열병 속에서 반쯤 깨어났을 때 이 모든 것을 손에 꽉 움켜쥐고는 그대로 다시 잠들었던 것을 기억해 냈다.\n\n\"좀 봐, 이 양반 누더기를 그러모아서는, 무슨 보물이라도 잡은 것처럼 끌어안고 잔다니까…….\"\n\n그러고는 나스타시야가 신경질적인 키득거림을 터뜨렸다.\n\n순간 그는 그것들을 죄다 외투 밑으로 쑤셔 넣고 그녀에게 뚫어지게 시선을 박았다. 그 순간 이성적으로 사고할 능력과는 한참 거리가 멀었지만, 그는 곧 체포될 사람을 두고 저런 식으로 행동하지는 않으리라는 것을 느꼈다. \"하지만…… 경찰서는?\"\n\n\"차라리 차나 좀 들어요! 어때요? 가져다줄게요, 남은 게 좀 있으니.\"\n\n\"아니…… 가야 해, 당장 가겠어.\" 그는 발을 디디고 일어서며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 댁은 아래층까지도 못 내려갈걸요!\"\n\n\"아니, 가야 해.\"\n\n\"맘대로 해요.\"\n\n그녀는 문지기를 따라 나갔다.\n\n곧바로 그는 밝은 데로 달려가 양말과 누더기를 살펴보았다.\n\n\"얼룩이 있긴 한데, 그리 눈에 띄지 않아. 죄다 흙먼지로 덮이고, 문질려서 벌써 변색됐어. 의심을 품지 않은 사람이라면 누구도 아무것도 분간하지 못할 거야. 나스타시야도 멀리서는 알아차리지 못했어, 다행이야!\" 그러고 나서 그는 떨리는 손으로 통지의 봉인을 뜯고 읽기 시작했다. 그는 한참 동안 읽고서야 비로소 내용을 이해했다. 그것은 그날 아홉 시 반에 관할 경찰서장 사무실로 출두하라는, 관할 경찰서의 평범한 출두 명령서였다.\n\n\"하지만 이런 일이 언제 있었던가? 난 경찰과는 아무 상관도 없는데! 그런데 왜 하필 오늘이지?\" 그는 괴로운 당혹감 속에서 생각했다. \"맙소사, 그저 어서 끝나기나 했으면!\"\n\n그는 기도하려고 무릎을 꿇으려다가, 웃음을 터뜨렸다 — 기도라는 생각 때문이 아니라, 자기 자신을 향한 웃음이었다.\n\n그는 서둘러 옷을 입기 시작했다. \"끝장이라면, 끝장인 거지, 상관없어! 양말을 신을까?\" 그는 문득 의아해했다. \"더 먼지투성이가 될 테고 흔적도 사라질 테니까.\"\n\n그러나 그것을 신자마자 그는 혐오와 공포에 사로잡혀 다시 벗어 버렸다. 벗긴 했지만, 다른 양말이 없다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 그는 그것을 집어 들어 다시 신었다 — 그러고는 또다시 웃었다.\n\n\"이건 죄다 관습적인 거야, 죄다 상대적인 거지, 그저 보기 나름인 거라고.\" 그는 번개처럼 생각했다, 다만 마음의 표면에서만, 온몸을 부르르 떨면서. \"자, 신었어! 결국 신고야 말았군!\"\n\n그러나 그의 웃음은 이내 절망으로 바뀌었다.\n\n\"아니, 내 힘으로는 감당이 안 돼…….\" 그는 생각했다. 그의 다리가 후들거렸다. \"두려움 때문이지.\" 그는 중얼거렸다. 그의 머리가 빙빙 돌고 열로 지끈거렸다. \"이건 함정이야! 날 거기로 꾀어내서는 모든 일을 두고 혼란에 빠뜨리려는 거지.\" 그는 계단으로 나서면서 곰곰이 생각했다. \"무엇보다 곤란한 건 내가 거의 정신이 나가 있다는 거야……. 멍청한 소리를 불쑥 내뱉을지도 몰라…….\"\n\n계단에서 그는 물건들을 죄다 벽 구멍 속에 그대로 둔 채 나왔다는 것을 떠올렸다. \"그리고 십중팔구는, 내가 나간 사이에 수색하려고 일부러 그런 거겠지.\" 그는 생각하며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그러나 그는 너무도 깊은 절망에, 굳이 그렇게 부를 수 있다면, 비참함이 빚어낸 냉소에 사로잡혀, 손을 한번 내젓고는 계속 걸어갔다. \"그저 끝내 버리기만 하자!\"\n\n거리는 또다시 견딜 수 없이 무더웠다. 그 며칠 동안 비라곤 한 방울도 내리지 않았다. 또다시 먼지, 벽돌과 회반죽, 또다시 가게와 선술집에서 풍기는 악취, 또다시 술 취한 사내들, 핀란드 행상들, 그리고 다 망가져 가는 삯마차들. 햇빛이 곧장 그의 눈을 찔러서 밖을 내다보는 것조차 고통스러웠고, 그는 머리가 핑 도는 것을 느꼈다 — 열병에 걸린 사람이 화창하게 해가 비치는 날 거리로 나섰을 때 으레 느끼게 되는 그 기분이었다.\n\n그가 바로 그 거리로 꺾어 드는 모퉁이에 다다랐을 때, 그는 두려움의 고통 속에서 그 거리를 내려다보았다…… 그 집을…… 그러고는 즉시 눈길을 돌려 버렸다.\n\n\"저들이 내게 캐물으면, 어쩌면 그냥 다 털어놓을지도 몰라.\" 그는 경찰서로 다가가며 생각했다.\n\n경찰서는 사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 있었다. 최근에 어느 새 건물 사 층의 새 사무실로 옮겨 온 참이었다. 그는 예전 사무실에 잠깐 들렀던 적이 있었지만 그건 한참 전의 일이었다. 대문 안으로 들어서면서 그는 오른쪽에 계단이 하나 있고, 어떤 농부 하나가 손에 장부를 든 채 그 계단을 올라가는 것을 보았다. \"필시 건물 문지기겠지, 그렇다면 사무실은 여기야.\" 그러고는 그는 어림짐작으로 계단을 오르기 시작했다. 그는 누구에게도 묻고 싶지 않았다.\n\n\"들어가서, 무릎을 꿇고, 모든 것을 자백해야지…….\" 사 층에 다다르며 그는 생각했다.\n\n계단은 가파르고 좁았으며, 온통 더러운 물로 질척거렸다. 셋방마다 부엌이 계단 쪽으로 나 있었고, 거의 온종일 활짝 열려 있었다. 그래서 끔찍한 악취와 열기가 가득했다. 계단에는 장부를 옆구리에 낀 채 오르내리는 문지기들과 경찰들, 온갖 부류의 남녀로 붐볐다. 사무실 문 역시 활짝 열려 있었다. 안에서는 농부들이 서서 기다리고 있었다. 거기에도 열기가 숨 막힐 듯했고, 새로 단장한 방에서 나는 새 페인트와 묵은 기름의 역겨운 냄새가 진동했다.\n\n잠시 기다린 뒤, 그는 다음 방으로 들어가기로 마음먹었다. 방들은 죄다 작고 천장이 낮았다. 끔찍한 조바심이 그를 자꾸만 앞으로 떠밀었다. 아무도 그에게 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 두 번째 방에는 서기 몇이 앉아서 글을 쓰고 있었는데, 그보다 별로 나을 것 없는 옷차림에다, 어쩐지 괴상해 보이는 무리였다. 그는 그들 중 하나에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n그는 자기가 받은 통지를 내보였다.\n\n\"대학생이오?\" 사내가 통지를 흘끗 보며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 예전엔 대학생이었습니다.\"\n\n서기는 그를 바라보았지만, 조금의 흥미도 없었다. 그는 유난히 부스스한 사람으로, 눈에는 어떤 고정관념에 사로잡힌 듯한 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이 사람한테서는 아무것도 얻어 낼 게 없겠어, 무엇에도 관심이 없으니까.\" 라스콜니코프는 생각했다.\n\n\"저기 수석 서기한테 가 보시오.\" 서기가 가장 먼 방을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n그는 그 방으로 들어갔다 — 순서로는 네 번째 방이었다. 작은 방인데도 사람들로 빽빽이 들어차 있었고, 바깥 방들보다는 다소 나은 옷차림이었다. 그들 가운데 부인 둘이 있었다. 하나는 상복 차림의 초라한 행색으로, 탁자 앞 수석 서기 맞은편에 앉아 그가 불러 주는 대로 무언가를 받아쓰고 있었다. 다른 하나는 몹시 살집 좋고 풍만한 여자로, 불그죽죽한 자줏빛에 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴을 하고, 가슴에 받침접시만 한 브로치를 단 지나치게 화려한 차림새로, 한쪽에 서서 무언가를 기다리는 듯했다. 라스콜니코프는 수석 서기에게 자기 통지를 들이밀었다. 그는 그것을 흘끗 보고는 \"잠깐 기다리시오\"라고 말하고는 다시 상복 입은 부인에게 주의를 돌렸다.\n\n그는 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었다. \"그 일일 리가 없어!\"\n\n차츰차츰 그는 자신감을 되찾기 시작했고, 용기를 내라고, 침착하라고 끊임없이 스스로를 다그쳤다.\n\n\"어떤 어리석은 짓, 사소한 부주의 하나로도 나는 나 자신을 드러내고 말지도 몰라! 흠…… 여기 공기가 없는 게 유감이군.\" 그는 덧붙였다. \"숨이 막혀……. 머리가 어느 때보다 더 어지러워지고…… 정신도 마찬가지야…….\"\n\n그는 끔찍한 내면의 동요를 의식하고 있었다. 그는 자제력을 잃을까 두려웠다. 무언가를 붙잡아 거기에 정신을 붙들어 매려 애썼다, 전혀 무관한 무언가를. 그러나 그것은 도무지 되지 않았다. 그래도 수석 서기가 그의 관심을 크게 끌었고, 그는 그를 꿰뚫어 보고 그의 얼굴에서 무언가를 알아채기를 줄곧 바랐다.\n\n그는 스물두 살쯤 된 아주 젊은 사내로, 가무잡잡하고 변화무쌍한 얼굴은 나이보다 늙어 보였다. 그는 멋쟁이답게 세련되게 차려입고서, 머리를 한가운데로 가르고 곱게 빗어 포마드를 발랐으며, 깨끗이 씻은 손가락마다 여러 개의 반지를 끼고, 조끼에는 금줄을 늘어뜨리고 있었다. 그는 방 안에 있던 어느 외국인에게 프랑스어로 두어 마디를 건넸는데, 제법 정확하게 말했다.\n\n\"루이자 이바노브나, 앉으셔도 됩니다.\" 그는 화려하게 차려입은 자줏빛 얼굴의 부인에게 무심히 말했는데, 그녀는 곁에 의자가 있는데도 감히 앉지 못하겠다는 듯 여전히 서 있었다.\n\n\"이히 단케.*\" 그녀가 말하고는, 비단 옷이 사락거리는 소리와 함께 사뿐히 의자에 내려앉았다. 흰 레이스로 장식한 그녀의 연푸른 드레스가 기구처럼 탁자 둘레로 부풀어 올라 방의 거의 절반을 채웠다. 그녀에게서는 향수 냄새가 났다. 하지만 그녀는 방의 절반을 차지하고 그토록 향수 냄새를 강하게 풍기는 것이 분명 거북한 듯했다. 그녀의 미소는 뻔뻔스러우면서도 굽실거리는 것이었으나, 역력한 불안을 드러내고 있었다.\n* 이히 단케(Ich danke): 독일어로 \"고맙습니다.\"\n\n상복 입은 부인이 마침내 일을 마치고 일어섰다. 갑자기 무언가 소란스러운 소리와 함께 한 장교가 걸음마다 어깨를 묘하게 흔들면서 무척이나 경쾌하게 걸어 들어왔다. 그는 모표 달린 모자를 탁자 위로 휙 던지고는 안락의자에 앉았다. 그 자그마한 부인은 그를 보자마자 자리에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 일종의 황홀경에 빠진 듯 굽실굽실 절을 해 댔다. 그러나 장교는 그녀에게 조금의 주의도 기울이지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 있는 앞에서는 감히 다시 앉지 못했다. 그는 부서장 보좌관이었다. 그는 얼굴 양옆으로 수평으로 뻗친 불그스름한 콧수염을 기르고 있었고, 이목구비는 지극히 작아서 어떤 거만함 말고는 별다른 것을 드러내지 않았다. 그는 라스콜니코프를 곁눈으로, 다소 못마땅하게 바라보았다. 라스콜니코프의 옷차림이 너무도 형편없는 데다, 그 굴욕적인 처지에도 불구하고 그의 태도가 결코 옷차림과 어울리지 않았던 것이다. 라스콜니코프가 무심결에 그에게 아주 길고 똑바른 시선을 박았던 탓에, 그는 그야말로 모욕을 당한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 그가 소리쳤는데, 자기 시선의 위엄으로도 저런 누더기 차림의 사내가 박살 나지 않은 것이 자못 놀랍다는 투였다.\n\n\"통지를 받고…… 출두했습니다…….\" 라스콜니코프가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"밀린 빚을 받으려는 건이지요, 그 대학생한테서.\" 수석 서기가 서류에서 몸을 떼면서 황급히 끼어들었다. \"여기요!\" 그러고는 라스콜니코프에게 문서 한 장을 던지며 그 자리를 가리켰다. \"이걸 읽어 보시오!\"\n\n\"돈? 무슨 돈?\" 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. \"하지만…… 그렇다면…… 분명 그 일은 아니로군.\"\n\n그러고는 그는 기쁨에 떨었다. 그는 갑작스럽고 강렬한, 형언할 수 없는 안도를 느꼈다. 등에서 무거운 짐이 내려졌다.\n\n\"그래, 도대체 몇 시까지 출두하라고 지시받았소, 선생?\" 부서장 보좌관이 무슨 영문인지 점점 더 못마땅해하는 기색으로 소리쳤다. \"아홉 시까지 오라고 했는데, 지금이 열두 시잖소!\"\n\n\"통지를 십오 분 전에야 받았습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 어깨 너머로 큰 소리로 대꾸했다. 자기도 놀랍게, 그 역시 갑자기 화가 치밀었고, 거기서 어떤 쾌감마저 느꼈다. \"게다가 열병으로 앓는 몸을 끌고 여기 온 것만 해도 충분하지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"제발 소리 좀 지르지 마시오!\"\n\n\"전 소리 지르는 게 아니라 아주 차분하게 말하고 있습니다, 도리어 당신이 제게 소리를 지르고 있지요. 전 대학생이고, 누구도 제게 소리 지르는 걸 용납하지 않습니다.\"\n\n부서장 보좌관은 어찌나 격분했는지 처음 한순간은 알아들을 수 없는 소리만 푸푸 내뱉을 뿐이었다. 그가 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"조용히 하시오! 여긴 관청이오. 무례하게 굴지 마시오, 선생!\"\n\n\"당신도 관청에 있는 거지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 외쳤다. \"게다가 당신은 소리를 지르면서 담배까지 피우고 있으니, 우리 모두에게 무례를 범하고 있는 거 아닙니까.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나니 그는 형언할 수 없는 만족감을 느꼈다.\n\n수석 서기가 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보았다. 성난 부서장 보좌관은 분명 당황한 기색이었다.\n\n\"그건 당신이 상관할 일이 아니오!\" 그가 마침내 부자연스러울 만큼 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"당신에게 요구된 진술서나 작성하시오. 그에게 보여 주게, 알렉산드르 그리고리예비치. 당신에 대한 고소가 들어왔소! 당신은 빚을 갚지 않고 있소! 참 잘난 양반이군!\"\n\n그러나 라스콜니코프는 이제 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 다급히 설명을 찾으려고 그 종이를 열렬히 거머쥐었다. 그는 한 번 읽고, 두 번째로 읽었으나, 여전히 이해하지 못했다.\n\n\"이게 뭡니까?\" 그가 수석 서기에게 물었다.\n\n\"차용증에 따른 채무 변제 청구, 그러니까 영장이오. 당신은 비용이며 소송 경비 등 일체와 함께 그것을 변제하든지, 아니면 언제 갚을 수 있는지 서면으로 진술하고, 동시에 변제 전까지 수도를 떠나지 않으며 재산을 매각하거나 은닉하지 않겠다는 서약서를 제출해야 하오. 채권자는 당신의 재산을 매각하고 법에 따라 당신을 상대로 소송을 제기할 자유가 있소.\"\n\n\"하지만 전…… 누구에게도 빚을 지지 않았습니다!\"\n\n\"그건 우리가 알 바 아니오. 자, 법적으로 공증되고 변제 기일이 지난 백십오 루블짜리 차용증이 변제 청구를 위해 우리에게 접수됐는데, 이건 당신이 구 개월 전에 평정관 자르니친의 미망인에게 써 준 것이고, 미망인 자르니친이 어떤 체바로프 씨에게 넘긴 것이오. 그래서 우리가 이 건으로 당신을 소환한 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분은 제 집주인입니다!\"\n\n\"집주인이면 또 어떻소?\"\n\n수석 서기는 너그러운 연민의 미소를 띠고, 동시에 어떤 승리감마저 어린 표정으로 그를 바라보았는데, 마치 처음으로 포화 속에 놓인 신참을 보듯, \"자, 이제 기분이 어떤가?\" 하고 말하는 듯했다. 그러나 이제 그가 차용증이며 변제 영장 따위에 무슨 관심이 있겠는가! 그게 지금 걱정할 만한 일이던가, 주의를 기울일 가치라도 있던가! 그는 서 있었고, 읽었고, 들었고, 대답했고, 심지어 스스로 질문까지 던졌지만, 그 모든 것이 기계적이었다. 안전하다는, 압도적인 위험에서 벗어났다는 의기양양한 감각, 바로 그것이 그 순간 그의 온 영혼을 가득 채웠다, 미래에 대한 생각도 없이, 분석도 없이, 가정이나 추측도 없이, 의심도 없이, 자문도 없이. 그것은 충만하고 직접적이며 순수하게 본능적인 기쁨의 한순간이었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간, 사무실에서 마치 뇌우 같은 일이 벌어졌다. 라스콜니코프의 무례함에 여전히 부르르 떨며, 여전히 씩씩대면서 상처 입은 자존심을 지키려고 안달하던 부서장 보좌관이, 그가 들어선 뒤로 줄곧 더없이 멍청한 미소를 띤 채 그를 바라보고 있던 그 가련한 멋쟁이 부인에게 달려들었던 것이다.\n\n\"이 파렴치한 계집!\" 그가 갑자기 목청껏 소리쳤다. (상복 입은 부인은 이미 사무실을 떠난 뒤였다.) \"어젯밤 네 집에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌어? 엉! 또 추태를 부렸지, 넌 온 거리의 골칫거리야. 또 싸움질에 술판이지. 감화원에 들어가고 싶어? 아니, 내가 열 번이나 경고했잖아, 열한 번째는 그냥 넘어가지 않겠다고! 그런데 또, 또 이러는군, 네가…… 네가……!\"\n\n라스콜니코프의 손에서 종이가 떨어졌고, 그는 그토록 무례하게 다뤄지는 멋쟁이 부인을 멍하니 바라보았다. 그러나 곧 그는 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알아차렸고, 즉시 그 소란에서 진짜 재미를 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 즐겁게 귀를 기울였고, 어찌나 웃고 또 웃고 싶었는지 모른다…… 그의 신경이 죄다 곤두서 있었다.\n\n\"일리야 페트로비치!\" 수석 서기가 걱정스럽게 입을 떼다가, 도중에 멈추었다. 격분한 보좌관은 힘으로 막지 않고서는 멈출 수 없다는 것을 경험으로 알았기 때문이다.\n\n멋쟁이 부인으로 말하자면, 처음에는 그 폭풍 앞에서 그야말로 벌벌 떨었다. 그러나 이상하게도, 욕설이 늘어나고 거세질수록 그녀의 표정은 더욱 상냥해졌고, 그 무서운 보좌관에게 쏟아붓는 미소는 더욱 유혹적으로 변해 갔다. 그녀는 안절부절못하며 끊임없이 굽실거렸고, 자기 말을 끼워 넣을 기회를 초조하게 기다렸다. 그리고 마침내 그 기회를 잡았다.\n\n\"제 집에서는 어떤 소란도 싸움도 없었어요, 대장님.\" 그녀가 갑자기 콩알 굴러가듯 또르르 지껄이기 시작했는데, 러시아어를 자신만만하게 구사하긴 했으나 독일어 억양이 강했다. \"어떤 추태도 없었고, 그분이 취해서 왔던 거예요, 이게 다 진실이에요, 대장님, 전 잘못이 없어요……. 제 집은 점잖은 집이에요, 대장님, 점잖게 처신하는 집이고요, 대장님, 그리고 저는 늘, 늘 소란 같은 건 질색이라고요. 그런데 그분이 잔뜩 취해서 와서는 또 세 병을 달라고 하더니, 그러고 나서 한쪽 다리를 들어 올려서는 한 발로 피아노를 치기 시작했어요, 점잖은 집에서 그건 도무지 옳지 않은 짓이잖아요, 그리고 피아노를 완전히 간츠* 부숴 버렸어요, 정말이지 너무도 무례한 짓이라 제가 그렇게 말했죠. 그랬더니 그분이 병 하나를 집어 들고 모두를 그걸로 때리기 시작했어요. 그래서 제가 문지기를 불렀고, 카를이 왔는데, 그분이 카를을 붙잡아 눈을 쳤어요. 헨리에테의 눈도 때렸고요, 제 뺨도 다섯 대나 갈겼어요. 점잖은 집에서 그건 너무도 신사답지 못한 짓이었어요, 대장님, 그래서 제가 비명을 질렀죠. 그러자 그분이 운하 쪽으로 난 창문을 열고, 창가에 서서, 새끼 돼지처럼 꽥꽥거렸어요. 정말 망신스러웠죠. 거리로 난 창가에서 새끼 돼지처럼 꽥꽥거리다니, 원! 망측해라! 그래서 카를이 그분의 외투를 잡아 창가에서 끌어 내렸는데, 정말이에요, 대장님, 그분이 자인 로크,* 그러니까 자기 외투를 찢어 버렸어요. 그러고는 그분이 손해 배상으로 만 무스,* 그러니까 자기한테 십오 루블을 물어내야 한다고 소리쳤어요. 그래서 제가 물어 줬어요, 대장님, 자인 로크 값으로 오 루블을요. 그분은 신사답지 못한 손님이고 이 모든 소란을 일으킨 장본인이에요. '내가 당신 본때를 보여 주겠어, 신문이란 신문에 죄다 당신 얘길 쓸 수 있으니까'라고 그분이 말했다고요.\"\n* 간츠(ganz), 자인 로크(sein Rock), 만 무스(man muss): 각각 독일어로 \"완전히\", \"자기 외투\", \"사람은 ~해야 한다\"는 뜻. 화자가 러시아어에 독일어를 섞어 말하고 있다.\n\n\"그럼 그자가 작가였단 말이오?\"\n\n\"네, 대장님, 그런데 점잖은 집에서 그렇게 신사답지 못한 손님이라니…….\"\n\n\"자, 됐소! 그만하면 충분해! 내가 이미 말했잖소…….\"\n\n\"일리야 페트로비치!\" 수석 서기가 의미심장하게 거듭 불렀다.\n\n보좌관이 그를 재빨리 흘끗 보았다. 수석 서기가 가볍게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"……그러니 내가 이 말을 하지, 더없이 존경스러운 루이자 이바노브나, 그리고 이건 마지막으로 하는 말이오.\" 보좌관이 말을 이었다. \"당신의 그 점잖은 집에서 또 한 번 소란이 벌어지면, 점잖게 말해서 당신을 직접 유치장에 처넣겠소. 알아들었소? 그래, 문필가라는 작자가, 작가라는 자가 '점잖은 집'에서 자기 외투 자락 값으로 오 루블을 받아 챙겼다? 참 잘난 족속들이야, 이 작가라는 것들은!\"\n\n그러고는 그는 라스콜니코프에게 경멸 어린 눈길을 던졌다. \"요전에도 어느 식당에서 소란이 있었지. 작가라는 자가 저녁을 먹고는 돈을 안 내려 하더래. '당신을 풍자하는 글을 써 주겠어'라고 했다지. 또 지난주에는 그런 작자 하나가 증기선에서 어느 점잖은 5등 문관 가족한테, 그 부인이며 딸한테 입에 담지 못할 욕을 퍼부었어. 또 요전에는 그런 작자 하나가 어느 과자점에서 쫓겨났고. 다들 그런 식이라니까, 작가니, 문필가니, 대학생이니, 거리의 떠벌이들이……. 풰! 가 보시오! 내가 직접 당신네 집을 한번 들여다보겠소. 그땐 조심하는 게 좋을 거요! 알아들었소?\"\n\n루이자 이바노브나는 황급히 굽실거리며 사방에 절을 해 댔고, 그렇게 절을 하면서 문 쪽으로 물러갔다. 그러나 문간에서 그녀는 뒷걸음치다가, 싱싱하고 시원한 얼굴에 멋들어지게 숱 많은 금빛 구레나룻을 기른 잘생긴 장교와 부딪혔다. 이 사람이 바로 그 관할 부서장, 니코딤 포미치였다. 루이자 이바노브나는 허둥지둥 거의 땅에 닿도록 절을 하고는, 종종걸음으로 사무실에서 팔랑팔랑 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"또 천둥에 번개로군 — 태풍이야!\" 니코딤 포미치가 일리야 페트로비치에게 점잖고 다정한 어조로 말했다. \"자넨 또 흥분했군, 또 씩씩대고 있어! 계단에서부터 다 들리던걸!\"\n\n\"흥, 그러면 또 어떤가!\" 일리야 페트로비치가 신사다운 무심함으로 느릿느릿 내뱉고는, 서류 몇 장을 들고 다른 탁자로, 걸음마다 어깨를 경쾌하게 흔들면서 걸어갔다. \"여기, 한번 봐 주게나. 작가인지, 적어도 한때는 대학생이었다는 자가, 빚을 갚지 않고, 차용증까지 써 줘 놓고, 방에서 나가려 하지도 않아서, 끊임없이 고소가 들어오는 판인데, 게다가 이자가 자기 앞에서 내가 담배 피우는 것에 황송하게도 항의까지 하셨다네! 자기가 막돼먹은 놈처럼 굴면서, 자, 이자를 좀 보게나. 여기 이 신사 양반이야, 아주 매력적이지!\"\n\n\"가난은 죄가 아니라네, 친구. 하지만 자네가 화약처럼 폭발하는 거야 우리도 다 알지, 자넨 모욕을 못 견디니까. 짐작건대 무언가에 발끈해서는 자네 쪽에서도 도가 지나쳤을 게야.\" 니코딤 포미치가 라스콜니코프를 향해 상냥하게 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"하지만 자네가 거기서 잘못한 거라네. 단언컨대 이 사람은 더없이 훌륭한 친구지만, 다혈질이야, 다혈질이라고! 욱하고, 발끈하고, 펄펄 끓어오르면 아무도 못 말려! 그러고 나면 다 끝나는 거지! 그리고 속을 들여다보면 황금 같은 마음씨를 지녔다네! 연대에서 별명이 '화약 중위'였지…….\"\n\n\"그런데 그 연대 또한 어떤 곳이었는데!\" 일리야 페트로비치가 외쳤는데, 이 유쾌한 농담에 무척 흡족해하면서도 여전히 뾰로통한 기색이었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 문득 그들 모두에게 유달리 기분 좋은 무언가를 말하고 싶은 마음이 들었다. \"죄송합니다, 대장님.\" 그가 느닷없이 니코딤 포미치를 향해 가볍게 입을 열었다. \"제 처지를 좀 헤아려 주시겠습니까?…… 제가 무례했다면 기꺼이 용서를 빌겠습니다. 저는 가난한 대학생으로, 병들고 가난에 짓눌려 만신창이가 됐습니다(만신창이라는 말을 그는 썼다). 지금은 제 한 몸 건사할 수 없어 학업도 중단했지만, 곧 돈이 들어올 겁니다……. X 도에 어머니와 누이가 있습니다. 그들이 돈을 보내 줄 거고, 그러면 갚겠습니다. 제 집주인은 마음씨 좋은 여자지만, 제가 과외 자리를 잃은 데다 넉 달째 방세를 못 내고 있는 것에 어찌나 화가 났는지, 이젠 제 밥상조차 올려 보내 주지 않습니다……. 그리고 저는 이 차용증이라는 게 도무지 이해가 안 됩니다. 그녀가 이 차용증으로 제게 갚으라고 요구하고 있는 건데, 제가 어떻게 갚는단 말입니까? 여러분이 한번 판단해 보십시오!……\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 우리가 알 바 아니잖소.\" 수석 서기가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"네, 네. 전적으로 동의합니다. 하지만 설명을 좀 드리게 해 주십시오…….\" 라스콜니코프가 다시 끼어들었는데, 여전히 니코딤 포미치를 향해 말하면서도 일리야 페트로비치에게도 한껏 말을 건네려 애썼다. 비록 후자는 줄곧 서류를 뒤적이며 그를 경멸하듯 안중에도 두지 않는 척했지만 말이다. \"설명을 좀 드리자면, 저는 거의 삼 년 가까이 그녀와 함께 살았고, 처음엔…… 처음엔…… 뭐, 고백 못 할 것도 없지요, 맨 처음에 저는 그녀의 딸과 결혼하겠다고 약속했었습니다. 말로 한 약속이었고, 자발적으로 한 거였지요…… 그녀는 처녀였고…… 사실 저는 그녀가 마음에 들긴 했지만, 사랑에 빠진 건 아니었어요…… 한마디로 젊은 날의 치기였지요…… 그러니까, 제 말은, 그 시절에 집주인이 제게 외상을 후하게 줬다는 거고, 저는 그런 생활을…… 저는 무척 경솔했습니다…….\"\n\n\"누가 당신더러 그런 사사로운 얘기를 늘어놓으라고 했소, 선생, 우리는 허비할 시간이 없소.\" 일리야 페트로비치가 거칠게, 그리고 어딘가 의기양양한 어조로 가로막았다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 그를 격하게 제지했다, 비록 갑자기 말하기가 몹시 힘겨워졌지만.\n\n\"하지만 죄송합니다, 죄송하지만. 제가 설명해야 할 일입니다…… 이 모든 게 어떻게 된 일인지를…… 제 차례니까요…… 저도 당신 말에 동의하긴 합니다만…… 그게 불필요한 일이라는 데에요. 하지만 일 년 전에, 그 처녀가 티푸스로 죽었습니다. 저는 전과 다름없이 거기에 세 들어 살았고, 집주인이 지금의 거처로 옮겼을 때, 그녀가 제게 말했습니다…… 그것도 다정하게…… 자기는 저를 완전히 믿지만, 그래도 자기한테 진 빚 전부, 백십오 루블에 대해 차용증을 하나 써 주지 않겠느냐고요. 그것만 써 주면 다시 얼마든지 저를 믿어 주겠다고, 그리고 절대로, 절대로 — 이게 그녀가 한 말 그대로입니다 — 제가 스스로 갚을 수 있을 때까지는 그 차용증을 쓰지 않겠다고 했습니다…… 그런데 지금, 제가 과외 자리를 잃고 먹을 것조차 없는 마당에, 그녀가 저를 상대로 소송을 거는 겁니다. 이걸 두고 제가 무슨 말을 하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그 모든 가슴 아픈 사연이야 우리가 알 바 아니오.\" 일리야 페트로비치가 무례하게 가로막았다. \"당신은 서면 서약서를 제출해야 하오. 하지만 당신의 연애 사건이며 이 모든 비극적인 일들로 말할 것 같으면, 우리하고는 아무 상관 없는 일이오.\"\n\n\"이봐, 자네…… 너무 박정하군.\" 니코딤 포미치가 탁자에 앉아 자기도 무언가를 쓰기 시작하면서 중얼거렸다. 그는 다소 멋쩍은 기색이었다.\n\n\"쓰시오!\" 수석 서기가 라스콜니코프에게 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 쓰란 말입니까?\" 그가 무뚝뚝하게 물었다.\n\n\"내가 불러 주겠소.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 수석 서기가 그 연설을 듣고 난 뒤로 자기를 더 무심하고 경멸적으로 대한다고 느꼈지만, 이상하게도 그는 문득 누구의 평가에도 완전히 무관심해졌다. 그리고 이 반전은 번개처럼, 한순간에 일어났다. 그가 조금이라도 생각해 보려 했다면, 불과 일 분 전에 자기가 그들에게 그런 식으로 말을 걸며 자기감정을 떠안기려 했다는 사실에 정말이지 놀랐으리라. 그런데 그 감정들은 도대체 어디서 나온 것이었을까? 이제 이 방이 경찰관들이 아니라 그에게 가장 가깝고 소중한 사람들로 가득 찼다 한들, 그는 그들에게 건넬 인간적인 말 한마디조차 찾지 못했으리라. 그만큼 그의 마음은 텅 비어 있었다. 고통스럽고 영원한 고독과 격리의 음울한 감각이 그의 영혼 속에서 또렷한 형태를 갖추었다. 그의 마음속에 이 갑작스러운 반전을 일으킨 것은, 일리야 페트로비치 앞에서 보인 자기 감상적 토로의 비루함도, 그를 누르고 거둔 후자의 승리의 비루함도 아니었다. 오, 이제 그가 자기 자신의 비열함이며, 이 모든 하찮은 허영이며, 장교들이며, 독일 여자들이며, 빚이며, 경찰서 따위와 무슨 상관이 있겠는가? 만일 그 순간 그에게 화형이 선고되었다 해도, 그는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았을 것이고, 그 판결을 끝까지 듣지도 않았으리라. 무언가 전혀 새롭고, 갑작스럽고, 알 수 없는 일이 그에게 일어나고 있었다. 그가 이해한 것은 아니었지만, 그는 감각의 온 강렬함으로 또렷이 느꼈다. 자기는 이제 다시는 이 경찰서 사람들에게 조금 전의 그 폭발 같은 감상적 토로로도, 그 무엇으로도 결코 호소할 수 없으리라는 것을. 그리고 설령 이들이 경찰관이 아니라 자기 친형제자매라 해도, 인생의 그 어떤 상황에서도 이들에게 호소한다는 것은 전혀 있을 수 없는 일이리라는 것을. 그는 이토록 기이하고 끔찍한 감각을 일찍이 경험한 적이 없었다. 그리고 무엇보다 고통스러운 것은 — 그것이 개념이나 관념이라기보다는 하나의 감각이라는 점, 직접적인 감각이라는 점, 그가 평생 알아 온 모든 감각 중에 가장 고통스러운 감각이라는 점이었다.\n\n수석 서기가 그에게, 변제할 수 없다는 것, 후일 변제할 것을 약속한다는 것, 도시를 떠나지 않고 재산을 매각하지 않겠다는 것 등을 적은 통상적인 서식의 진술서를 불러 주기 시작했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은 쓸 수가 없겠군요, 펜조차 제대로 못 쥐는데.\" 수석 서기가 라스콜니코프를 호기심 어린 눈으로 바라보며 말했다. \"어디 아프시오?\"\n\n\"네, 어지럽습니다. 계속하시지요!\"\n\n\"이게 다요. 서명하시오.\"\n\n수석 서기는 종이를 받아 들고 다른 사람들을 응대하러 돌아섰다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 펜을 돌려주었다. 그러나 일어나서 나가는 대신, 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 머리를 짓눌렀다. 두개골에 못이 박히는 것만 같았다. 문득 이상한 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다, 지금 당장 일어나 니코딤 포미치에게 가서 어제 일어난 모든 일을 다 털어놓고, 그러고는 그와 함께 자기 하숙으로 가서 구석 구멍 속의 물건들을 보여 주자는. 그 충동이 어찌나 강했던지 그는 그것을 실행하려고 자리에서 일어났다. \"잠깐 생각이라도 좀 해 보는 게 낫지 않을까?\" 하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. \"아니, 생각하지 말고 차라리 짐을 벗어 던지는 게 나아.\" 그러나 갑자기 그는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 니코딤 포미치가 일리야 페트로비치와 열심히 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 그 말이 그에게 들려왔던 것이다.\n\n\"그건 불가능해, 둘 다 풀려날 거야. 우선, 이야기 전체가 앞뒤가 안 맞아. 자기들이 한 짓이라면 뭐하러 문지기를 불렀겠나? 스스로를 고발하려고? 아니면 눈속임으로? 아니, 그건 너무 교활한 짓이지! 게다가 그 대학생 페스트랴코프는 들어갈 때 문지기 둘과 한 여자한테 대문에서 목격됐어. 친구 셋과 함께 걷고 있었는데, 그들이 바로 그 대문에서 헤어졌고, 그자가 친구들이 보는 앞에서 문지기들에게 길을 물었단 말이야. 그런 목적을 품고 가는 자라면 길을 물었겠나? 코흐로 말하면, 그자는 노파한테 올라가기 전에 아래층 은세공인 가게에서 삼십 분을 보냈고, 정확히 여덟 시 십오 분 전에 거기서 나왔어. 자, 한번 생각해 보게…….\"\n\n\"하지만 잠깐, 그 모순은 어떻게 설명하나? 그들 자신이 진술하기를, 문을 두드렸는데 잠겨 있었다고 했어. 그런데 삼 분 뒤에 문지기와 함께 올라가 보니 문이 잠겨 있지 않았다는 거 아닌가.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야. 살인자가 거기 있다가 안에서 빗장을 걸어 잠갔던 게 분명해. 코흐가 멍청이처럼 굴어서 자기도 문지기를 찾으러 가지만 않았더라면 틀림없이 그자를 잡았을 거야. 그자는 그 틈을 타서 아래층으로 내려가 어떻게든 그들 곁을 빠져나간 게 분명해. 코흐는 연신 성호를 그으며 말하지. '내가 거기 있었더라면, 그자가 뛰쳐나와 도끼로 날 죽였을 거요'라고. 감사 예배를 드리겠다는군 — 하, 하!\"\n\n\"그래서 아무도 그 살인자를 못 봤단 말인가?\"\n\n\"못 볼 만도 하지. 그 집은 영락없는 노아의 방주니까.\" 듣고 있던 수석 서기가 말했다.\n\n\"명백해, 아주 명백하다고.\" 니코딤 포미치가 열을 올리며 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 명백하긴커녕 전혀 아니야.\" 일리야 페트로비치가 우겼다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 모자를 집어 들고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔지만, 거기에 이르지는 못했다……\n\n정신을 차렸을 때, 그는 의자에 앉아 있는 자신을 발견했다. 오른쪽에서 누군가가 그를 부축하고 있었고, 왼쪽에서는 또 다른 누군가가 누르스름한 물이 담긴 누런 유리잔을 들고 서 있었으며, 니코딤 포미치가 그의 앞에 서서 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다. 그는 의자에서 일어났다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이오? 어디 아프시오?\" 니코딤 포미치가 다소 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"서명할 때도 펜을 제대로 못 쥐던걸요.\" 수석 서기가 자기 자리로 돌아가 다시 일을 시작하면서 말했다.\n\n\"앓은 지 오래됐소?\" 일리야 페트로비치가 자기 자리에서 외쳤는데, 그 역시 서류를 들여다보고 있었다. 그는 물론 라스콜니코프가 기절했을 때 환자를 보러 다가왔다가, 그가 정신을 차리자 곧장 물러난 참이었다.\n\n\"어제부터입니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 중얼거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"어제 외출했었소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"앓는 몸으로 말이오?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"몇 시에?\"\n\n\"일곱 시쯤요.\"\n\n\"그래, 어디를 갔는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"거리를 따라서요.\"\n\n\"짤막하고 분명하군.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 손수건처럼 새하얗게 질린 채, 일리야 페트로비치의 응시 앞에서 자신의 검고 열에 들뜬 눈을 떨어뜨리지 않은 채로 날카롭게, 뚝뚝 끊어 가며 대답했다.\n\n\"이 사람 똑바로 서 있기도 힘들어하는데. 그런데 자넨…….\" 니코딤 포미치가 입을 열려 했다.\n\n\"상관없어.\" 일리야 페트로비치가 어딘가 묘한 어조로 내뱉었다.\n\n니코딤 포미치는 무언가 더 항변하려 했지만, 자기를 아주 뚫어지게 바라보고 있는 수석 서기를 흘끗 보고는 입을 다물었다. 갑자기 침묵이 흘렀다. 기묘한 일이었다.\n\n\"그럼, 좋소.\" 일리야 페트로비치가 결론을 내렸다. \"더 붙잡아 두지 않겠소.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 밖으로 나갔다. 그가 나가자마자 활기찬 대화 소리가 들려왔고, 그 가운데서도 니코딤 포미치의 캐묻는 목소리가 두드러졌다. 거리에 나서자 그의 어지럼증은 말끔히 가셨다.\n\n\"수색 — 당장 수색이 있을 거야.\" 그는 집으로 서둘러 가면서 거듭 되뇌었다. \"짐승 같은 놈들! 의심하고 있어.\"\n\n조금 전의 그 공포가 다시금 그를 완전히 사로잡았다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n\"그런데 만약 벌써 수색이 있었다면? 만약 그들이 내 방에서 기다리고 있다면?\"\n\n그러나 여기 그의 방이 있었다. 방 안에는 아무것도, 아무도 없었다. 누구도 들여다보지 않았다. 나스타시야조차 손대지 않았다. 하지만 맙소사! 어떻게 그 모든 물건을 저 구멍 속에 그대로 둘 수 있었단 말인가?\n\n그는 구석으로 달려가 벽지 밑으로 손을 밀어 넣고는 물건들을 끄집어내어 주머니마다 쑤셔 넣었다. 전부 여덟 점이었다. 작은 상자 두 개에는 귀고리인지 뭔지가 들어 있었으나, 그는 제대로 들여다보지도 않았다. 그다음엔 가죽으로 된 작은 갑 네 개. 사슬도 하나 있었는데, 그저 신문지에 둘둘 말려 있었고, 또 신문지에 싸인 다른 무언가는 훈장처럼 보였다……. 그는 그것들을 모두 외투의 여러 주머니와 바지의 남은 한쪽 주머니에 나눠 넣으며, 되도록 눈에 띄지 않게 감추려 애썼다. 지갑도 챙겼다. 그러고는 문을 열어 둔 채 방을 나섰다. 그는 빠르고 단호하게 걸었고, 비록 만신창이가 된 듯했으나 정신만은 또렷했다. 그는 추적을 두려워했고, 앞으로 삼십 분, 어쩌면 십오 분 뒤면 그를 쫓으라는 지령이 떨어지리라는 것이 두려웠다. 그러니 무슨 일이 있어도 그전에 모든 흔적을 감춰야 했다. 아직 얼마간의 기력이, 얼마간의 판단력이 남아 있는 동안 모든 것을 정리해 두어야만 했다……. 어디로 가야 한단 말인가?\n\n그것은 이미 오래전에 정해 두었다. \"운하에 던져 버리는 거야, 그러면 모든 흔적이 물속에 묻히고, 이 일도 끝나는 거지.\" 그렇게 그는 헛소리에 시달리던 그날 밤에 결심했었다. 그날 밤 그는 몇 번이고 벌떡 일어나 나가서, 서둘러 이 모든 것을 없애 버리고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔던 것이다. 그러나 그것을 없애 버리기란 막상 무척 어려운 일임이 드러났다. 그는 예카테리닌스키 운하의 둑을 따라 삼십 분 남짓 헤매며 물가로 내려가는 계단을 몇 번이나 바라보았으나, 도무지 계획을 실행할 엄두가 나지 않았다. 계단 끝에는 뗏목이 떠 있고 그 위에서 여자들이 빨래를 하고 있거나, 보트들이 매여 있고 사방에 사람들이 우글거렸기 때문이다. 게다가 그는 양쪽 둑 어디에서든 눈에 띄어 사람들의 주목을 받을 수 있었다. 한 사내가 일부러 물가로 내려가 멈춰 서서 무언가를 물에 던진다면 의심을 살 만한 일이었다. 그리고 만약 상자들이 가라앉지 않고 떠오르기라도 하면? 당연히 떠오를 것이었다. 지금만 해도 마주치는 사람마다 그를 빤히 쳐다보고 돌아보는 것 같았으니, 마치 그를 지켜보는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없는 듯했다. \"왜 이러지, 아니면 그저 내 착각인가?\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n마침내 차라리 네바강으로 가는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 그를 스쳤다. 거기엔 사람이 그리 많지 않아 덜 눈에 띌 것이고, 모든 면에서 더 편할 터였으며, 무엇보다 더 멀리 떨어져 있었다. 그는 이 위험한 시간을 어떻게 꼬박 삼십 분이나 노심초사하며 헤매고 다니면서도 진작 그 생각을 떠올리지 못했는지 의아했다. 게다가 그 삼십 분을 한낱 터무니없는 계획에 허비한 것이, 단지 헛소리에 시달리던 중에 그 계획을 떠올렸다는 이유만으로 그러했던 것이다! 그는 극도로 멍해지고 건망증이 심해졌으며, 스스로도 그것을 자각하고 있었다. 정말이지 서둘러야만 했다.\n\n그는 V 거리를 따라 네바강 쪽으로 걸었으나, 가는 길에 또 다른 생각이 떠올랐다. \"왜 네바강이지? 차라리 어딘가 멀리, 다시 섬들로 가서 인적 없는 어느 곳에, 숲속이나 덤불 밑에 물건을 숨기고 그 자리를 표시해 두는 편이 낫지 않을까?\" 그리고 비록 명료하게 판단할 능력은 없다고 느꼈지만, 그 생각은 그럴듯하게 여겨졌다. 그러나 그는 그곳까지 갈 운명이 아니었다. V 거리에서 광장 쪽으로 나오던 그는, 왼편에 휑한 두 벽 사이로 안뜰로 이어지는 통로 하나를 보았던 것이다. 오른편으로는 회칠하지 않은 사 층 건물의 휑한 벽이 안뜰 깊숙이 뻗어 있었고, 왼편으로는 나무 울타리가 그것과 나란히 안뜰 안쪽으로 스무 걸음쯤 이어지다가, 다시 왼쪽으로 급히 꺾여 있었다. 거기엔 온갖 잡동사니가 널브러진, 울타리로 막힌 인적 없는 공터가 있었다. 안뜰 끝에는 낮고 거뭇한 돌 헛간의 모서리가 울타리 뒤로 빠끔히 내다보였는데, 어느 작업장의 일부인 듯했다. 아마 마차 제작소나 목공소의 헛간인 모양이었다. 입구에서부터 그 일대 전체가 석탄 먼지로 시커멨다. 여기야말로 던져 버리기에 딱 좋은 곳이라고 그는 생각했다. 안뜰에 아무도 없는 것을 확인하고 그는 슬그머니 들어섰고, 곧 대문 가까이에 하수구 하나가 있는 것을 보았다. 인부나 마부가 많이 드나드는 안뜰에 흔히 마련되어 있는 그런 것이었다. 그 위 울타리에는 분필로, \"여기서 볼일 보는 것을 엄금함\"이라는 케케묵은 농지거리가 휘갈겨져 있었다. 이것은 오히려 잘된 일이었으니, 그가 들어선 데에 의심스러울 것이 전혀 없었기 때문이다. \"여기다 한꺼번에 무더기로 던져 버리고 내빼면 되겠군!\"\n\n다시 한번 주위를 둘러보며, 이미 손을 주머니에 넣은 채, 그는 입구와 하수구 사이의 바깥벽에 기대어 놓인, 어쩌면 육십 파운드는 됨 직한 다듬지 않은 큼직한 돌 하나를 발견했다. 벽 너머는 거리였다. 그쪽 동네에는 늘 그렇듯 행인이 많아 그들의 발소리가 들렸으나, 누군가 거리에서 들어오지 않는 한 입구에서 그가 보이지는 않았다. 물론 그런 일이 충분히 일어날 수도 있었으니, 서둘러야 했다.\n\n그는 돌 위로 몸을 굽혀 두 손으로 그 윗부분을 단단히 움켜쥐고는, 온 힘을 다해 뒤집었다. 돌 밑 땅바닥에는 작은 구멍이 패여 있었고, 그는 즉시 주머니 속 물건들을 그 안에 쏟아부었다. 지갑이 맨 위에 놓였는데도 구멍은 다 차지 않았다. 그런 다음 그는 다시 돌을 붙잡아 한 번 비틀어 제자리로 돌려놓았고, 돌은 아주 약간 높아지긴 했으나 본래 자리에 다시 놓였다. 하지만 그는 돌 주위로 흙을 긁어모으고 발로 가장자리를 꾹꾹 눌러 다졌다. 아무것도 눈에 띌 게 없었다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 밖으로 나와 광장으로 접어들었다. 또다시 경찰서에서 그랬듯이, 강렬하고 거의 견디기 힘든 기쁨이 한순간 그를 휩쌌다. \"흔적을 묻어 버렸어! 그 누가, 도대체 누가 저 돌 밑을 들여다볼 생각을 하겠어? 저건 아마 이 집이 지어졌을 때부터 거기 있었을 테고, 앞으로도 그만큼 더 오래 그대로 있을 거야. 설령 발견된다 한들, 누가 나를 떠올리겠어? 다 끝났어! 단서는 없어!\" 그러고는 그가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그렇다, 그는 자신이 가늘고 신경질적이며 소리 없는 웃음을 터뜨리기 시작했고, 광장을 가로지르는 내내 줄곧 웃었다는 것을 기억했다. 그러나 이틀 전 그 소녀와 마주쳤던 K 대로에 이르자, 그의 웃음은 돌연 멎었다. 다른 생각들이 그의 머릿속으로 기어들었다. 그는 문득, 소녀가 떠난 뒤 자신이 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겼던 그 벤치를 지나치는 것이 혐오스럽게 느껴졌고, 이십 코페이카를 건네주었던 그 구레나룻 난 순경과 마주치는 것 또한 끔찍하게 여겨졌다. \"빌어먹을 놈!\"\n\n그는 성난, 그리고 넋이 나간 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 걸었다. 이제 그의 모든 생각이 어떤 단 하나의 점을 맴도는 듯했고, 그는 실제로 그런 점이 존재함을, 그리고 지금, 바로 지금 자신이 그 점과 정면으로 마주하게 되었음을 느꼈다 — 게다가 사실 지난 두 달 동안 처음으로.\n\n\"다 빌어먹을!\" 그는 걷잡을 수 없는 격분에 휩싸여 불현듯 생각했다. \"이미 시작된 일이면, 시작된 거야. 새로운 삶 따위 집어치워! 맙소사, 이 얼마나 어리석은가!…… 오늘 내가 얼마나 거짓말을 늘어놓았던가! 저 한심한 일리야 페트로비치한테 얼마나 비굴하게 알랑댔던가! 하지만 그딴 건 다 부질없어! 그자들이, 또 그자들한테 알랑댄 게 나한테 무슨 대수라고! 그게 문제가 아니야! 전혀 그게 문제가 아니라고!\"\n\n갑자기 그는 걸음을 멈추었다. 전혀 예기치 못한, 더없이 단순한 새로운 물음 하나가 그를 당혹스럽게, 그리고 쓰라리게 혼란에 빠뜨렸다.\n\n\"만약 이 모든 일이 정말로 멍청하게가 아니라 의도적으로 이루어진 거라면, 만약 내게 정말로 분명하고 확실한 목적이 있었다면, 어째서 나는 지갑 속을 들여다보지도 않았고, 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지도 모른단 말인가? 바로 그것을 위해 이런 고통을 겪고, 이렇게 비열하고 추잡하고 천한 짓을 의도적으로 저질렀으면서도? 게다가 나는 그 지갑을, 들여다보지도 않은 물건들과 함께 곧장 물에 던져 버리려 하지 않았던가…… 이게 어찌 된 일이지?\"\n\n그래, 그건 그랬다, 모든 게 그랬다. 그렇지만 그는 그 모든 것을 이미 전부터 알고 있었으며, 그것은 그에게 새로운 물음도 아니었다. 그날 밤, 망설임도 숙고도 없이, 마치 그래야만 하는 듯이, 마치 달리는 도저히 있을 수 없는 듯이 결정을 내렸을 때조차도……. 그래, 그는 그 모든 것을 알고 있었고, 그 모든 것을 이해하고 있었다. 분명 그것은 어제, 그가 궤짝 위로 몸을 굽혀 보석갑들을 끄집어내던 그 순간에 이미 모두 정해져 있었던 것이다……. 그래, 그랬던 것이다.\n\n\"이건 내가 몹시 아프기 때문이야.\" 마침내 그는 침울하게 결론지었다. \"나는 줄곧 노심초사하며 안달복달했고, 내가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 몰라……. 어제도, 그저께도, 이 모든 시간 내내 나는 스스로를 들볶아 왔어……. 나으면, 나는 안달하지 않을 거야……. 그런데 만약 영영 낫지 않으면? 맙소사, 이 모든 게 정말 지긋지긋해!\"\n\n그는 쉬지 않고 계속 걸었다. 그는 무언가 기분을 돌릴 거리가 절실히 그리웠으나, 무엇을 해야 할지, 무엇을 시도해야 할지 알지 못했다. 새로운, 압도적인 감각 하나가 매 순간 점점 더 그를 사로잡아 갔다. 그것은 자기를 둘러싼 모든 것에 대한 가늠할 수 없는, 거의 육체적인 혐오, 끈질기고 악의에 찬 증오의 감정이었다. 마주치는 모든 이가 그에게 역겨웠다 — 그들의 얼굴이, 그들의 움직임이, 그들의 몸짓이 역겨웠다. 만약 누군가 그에게 말을 걸었다면, 그는 그자에게 침을 뱉거나 물어뜯을 것만 같았다…….\n\n바실리옙스키 섬으로 건너가는 다리 근처, 작은 네바강 둑으로 나서다가 그는 문득 걸음을 멈추었다. \"참, 그가 여기 살지, 저 집에.\" 그는 생각했다. \"참, 내가 라주미힌한테 제 발로 온 게 아닌데! 또 똑같은 일이 되풀이되는군……. 그래도 알아 두면 흥미롭겠어. 일부러 온 건가, 아니면 그냥 우연히 여기까지 걸어온 건가? 아무려면 어때, 그저께 내가 모레쯤 그를 보러 가겠다고 했었지. 그러니, 그래, 가는 거다! 게다가 지금 더는 정말 걸을 수도 없으니까.\"\n\n그는 오 층에 있는 라주미힌의 방으로 올라갔다.\n\n라주미힌은 자기 다락방에 있었고, 마침 무언가를 부지런히 쓰던 중이었는데, 직접 문을 열어 주었다. 두 사람이 서로 만나지 못한 지 넉 달째였다. 라주미힌은 다 해진 실내복 차림에 맨발로 슬리퍼를 신고 있었고, 머리는 헝클어지고 수염도 깎지 않았으며 세수도 하지 않은 모습이었다. 그의 얼굴에 놀라움이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"자네였나?\" 그가 소리쳤다. 그는 동무를 위아래로 훑어보더니, 잠시 말이 없다가 휘파람을 불었다. \"그렇게까지 쪼들리나! 아니, 이 친구야, 나를 완전히 따돌렸군그래!\" 그는 라스콜니코프의 누더기를 보며 덧붙였다. \"자, 앉게, 피곤하겠지, 틀림없이.\"\n\n그리고 라스콜니코프가, 라주미힌 자신의 것보다도 한층 더 너덜너덜한 인조 가죽 소파에 털썩 주저앉자, 라주미힌은 단번에 손님이 아프다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"아니, 자네 중병이로군, 알고 있나?\" 그가 그의 맥을 짚기 시작했다. 라스콜니코프는 손을 홱 뿌리쳤다.\n\n\"괜찮아.\" 그가 말했다. \"이래서 온 거야. 일자리가 없어서…… 그러니까 가정교사 자리를 원했는데…… 아니, 사실 가정교사 자리는 필요 없어…….\"\n\n\"이봐! 자네 헛소리를 하고 있군, 알아!\" 라주미힌이 그를 유심히 지켜보며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그렇지 않아.\"\n\n라스콜니코프가 소파에서 일어섰다. 라주미힌의 집으로 계단을 올라올 때만 해도 그는 친구와 얼굴을 맞대게 되리라는 것을 미처 깨닫지 못했다. 그런데 이제, 순식간에, 그 순간 자기가 이 너른 세상에서 무엇보다도 원치 않는 것이 바로 누군가와 얼굴을 맞대는 일임을 깨달았다. 그의 속에서 울화가 치밀었다. 라주미힌의 문턱을 넘어서기가 무섭게 그는 자기 자신에 대한 분노로 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다.\n\n\"잘 있게.\" 그가 불쑥 말하고는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"멈춰, 멈추라고! 이 별난 친구야.\"\n\n\"가고 싶지 않아.\" 라스콜니코프가 다시 손을 뿌리치며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 도대체 뭣 하러 온 건가? 미쳤나, 어쩐 일이야? 아니, 이건…… 거의 모욕이잖아! 그렇게는 못 보내.\"\n\n\"그럼, 말하지, 내가 자네한테 온 건, 일을 시작하는 데에…… 도와줄 만한 사람이 자네밖에 없어서야…… 자네가 누구보다 친절하니까 — 아니, 똑똑하다는 거지, 그러니까 판단을 할 줄 아니까…… 그런데 이제 보니 난 아무것도 필요 없어. 알겠나? 아무것도…… 누구의 도움도…… 누구의 동정도. 난 혼자야…… 혼자라고. 자, 됐어. 날 좀 내버려 둬.\"\n\n\"잠깐만, 이 망나니야! 자넨 완전히 미치광이로군. 자네 좋을 대로 하게, 난 상관없으니까. 사실 나도 가정교사 자리가 없네, 알겠나, 그런데 그딴 건 아무래도 좋아. 헤루비모프라는 책 장수가 있는데 — 그자가 가정교사 자리만큼은 해 준단 말이지. 난 그자를 가정교사 자리 다섯 개랑도 안 바꿔. 그자가 일종의 출판이란 걸 하는데, 자연과학 입문서 같은 걸 찍어 내거든. 그게 얼마나 잘 팔리는지 몰라! 제목만 봐도 돈값을 한다니까! 자넨 늘 나를 바보라고 우겨댔지만, 이 친구야, 정말이지 나보다 더한 바보들이 수두룩해! 지금 그자가 진보적인 척하고 나서는데, 뭘 쥐뿔도 모르면서 그런 거지, 하지만 물론 나야 부추겨 주지. 자, 여기 독일어 원고 두 장이 있는데 — 내 생각엔 더없이 조잡한 사기야. '여자는 인간인가?'라는 문제를 다루는 거지. 그러고는 물론 여자가 인간임을 의기양양하게 증명해 보이지. 헤루비모프가 이걸 여성 문제에 기여하는 저작으로 내놓을 참이야. 내가 그걸 번역하고 있어. 그자는 이 두 장 반짜리를 여섯 장으로 늘리고, 우리는 반 페이지나 되는 거창한 제목을 지어 붙여서 반 루블에 내놓을 거야. 그럼 잘 팔리겠지! 그자가 한 장당 육 루블을 주니까, 이 일로 한 십오 루블쯤 떨어지는 셈이고, 난 벌써 육 루블을 선금으로 받았어. 이걸 끝내면, 우린 고래에 관한 번역을 시작할 거고, 그다음엔 『고백록』 2부에서 가장 따분한 추문 몇 개를 골라 번역하기로 점찍어 뒀지. 누가 헤루비모프한테 루소가 일종의 라디셰프 같은 자라고 일러 줬거든. 내가 반박할 리야 없지, 빌어먹을 자식! 그래, 자네 '여자는 인간인가?' 두 번째 장을 좀 해 보겠나? 하겠다면, 독일어 원고랑 펜이랑 종이를 가져가게 — 그건 다 마련돼 있으니까 — 그리고 삼 루블을 받아 가. 내가 일 전체에 대해 육 루블을 선금으로 받았으니, 자네 몫으로 삼 루블이 가는 셈이야. 그리고 그 장을 끝내면 자네한테 또 삼 루블이 갈 거고. 그리고 제발 내가 자네한테 호의를 베푸는 거라곤 생각 말게. 그 반대야. 자네가 들어오자마자, 난 자네가 날 어떻게 도와줄 수 있을지 알아봤거든. 우선, 난 철자에 약하고, 둘째로, 난 가끔 독일어에서 완전히 헤매서, 대개는 그냥 멋대로 지어내곤 해. 한 가지 위안이라면, 이게 분명 더 나아질 거라는 점이지. 하긴 누가 알겠어, 어쩌면 가끔은 더 나빠질지도. 자, 맡겠나?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 말없이 독일어 원고를 받아 들고, 삼 루블을 챙긴 뒤, 한마디도 없이 나가 버렸다. 라주미힌은 어리둥절하여 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 다음 거리에 이르렀을 때 발길을 돌려, 다시 라주미힌의 집으로 계단을 올라가, 탁자 위에 독일어 글과 삼 루블을 내려놓고는, 여전히 한마디도 내뱉지 않은 채 다시 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"자네 헛소리를 하는 건가, 어쩐 일이야?\" 라주미힌이 마침내 분통이 터져 고함쳤다. \"이게 무슨 광대 짓이야? 자네 나까지 미치게 만들 셈이군…… 도대체 뭣 하러 날 보러 온 거냐고, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"번역은…… 필요 없어.\" 라스콜니코프가 계단에서 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그럼 도대체 뭘 원하는 거야?\" 라주미힌이 위에서 소리쳤다. 라스콜니코프는 말없이 계단을 계속 내려갔다.\n\n\"이봐, 거기! 어디 사는 거야?\"\n\n대답이 없었다.\n\n\"그래, 그럼 빌어먹어라!\"\n\n그러나 라스콜니코프는 이미 거리로 발을 내딛고 있었다. 니콜라옙스키 다리 위에서 그는 어떤 불쾌한 사건으로 다시금 완전히 정신이 들었다. 한 마부가 그에게 두세 번 소리를 지른 끝에, 그가 하마터면 말발굽 밑에 깔릴 뻔하자, 채찍으로 그의 등을 세차게 후려쳤던 것이다. 그 채찍질에 그는 격분하여 난간으로 달려갔다(어쩐 영문인지 그는 마차들이 오가는 다리 한복판을 걷고 있었던 것이다). 그는 화가 치밀어 이를 악물고 빠득빠득 갈았다. 물론 사람들의 웃음소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"꼴좋다!\"\n\n\"소매치기겠지, 아마.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 취한 척하다가, 일부러 바퀴 밑으로 기어든 거야. 그러면 자네가 책임을 져야 하잖아.\"\n\n\"저게 아주 직업이라니까, 그렇다고.\"\n\n그러나 그가 멀어져 가는 마차 뒤로 여전히 성나고 어리둥절한 표정을 지으며 등을 문지른 채 난간 곁에 서 있을 때, 문득 누군가가 그의 손에 돈을 쥐여 주는 것이 느껴졌다. 그는 바라보았다. 두건을 쓰고 염소가죽 신을 신은 어느 나이 든 여자였는데, 모자를 쓰고 초록색 양산을 든, 아마 그녀의 딸인 듯한 소녀와 함께였다.\n\n\"받으세요, 선량한 양반, 그리스도의 이름으로.\"\n\n그가 그것을 받아 들자 그들은 지나갔다. 이십 코페이카짜리 동전이었다. 그의 옷차림과 행색으로 보아, 그들은 그를 거리에서 동냥하는 거지로 여겼던 모양이고, 이십 코페이카를 적선받은 것은 분명 그가 맞은 채찍질 덕분이었으니, 그것이 그들의 측은한 마음을 자아냈던 것이다.\n\n그는 이십 코페이카를 손에 꼭 쥔 채 열 걸음쯤 걷다가, 몸을 돌려 네바강을 향해 궁전 쪽을 바라보았다. 하늘에는 구름 한 점 없었고, 네바강에서는 좀처럼 보기 드문, 거의 새파란 빛으로 물이 빛나고 있었다. 다리 위에서, 예배당으로부터 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 가장 멋지게 보이는 대성당의 둥근 지붕이 햇빛 속에 반짝였고, 맑은 공기 속에 그 위의 장식 하나하나까지 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다. 채찍질의 통증은 가셨고, 라스콜니코프는 그것을 잊어버렸다. 이제 불안하고 그리 분명치 않은 하나의 생각이 그를 온통 사로잡았다. 그는 가만히 멈춰 서서, 오랫동안 뚫어지게 먼 곳을 응시했다. 이 자리는 그에게 유난히 낯익은 곳이었다. 대학에 다니던 시절, 그는 수백 번이나 — 대개 집으로 돌아가는 길에 — 바로 이 자리에 가만히 서서, 이 참으로 장엄한 광경을 바라보며, 그것이 자기 안에 불러일으키는 막연하고 신비로운 감정에 거의 언제나 의아해하곤 했다. 그것은 그를 묘하게 냉담하게 만들었다. 이 화려한 풍경이 그에게는 공허하고 생기 없는 것이었다. 그는 매번 자신의 침울하고 알 수 없는 인상에 의아해하면서도, 스스로를 믿지 못한 채 그 까닭을 밝히기를 미루곤 했다. 그는 그 옛날의 의혹과 곤혹을 생생히 떠올렸고, 지금 자신이 그것들을 떠올린 것이 한낱 우연만은 아닌 듯 여겨졌다. 자기가 예전과 똑같은 자리에 멈춰 섰다는 것이, 마치 자기가 정말로 똑같은 생각을 할 수 있고, 그토록…… 얼마 전까지만 해도 자신의 관심을 끌었던 똑같은 이론과 풍경에 흥미를 느낄 수 있다고 상상이라도 하는 듯하여, 그에게는 기이하고 그로테스크하게 느껴졌다. 그것이 거의 우습게까지 여겨졌고, 그러면서도 그의 가슴을 쥐어짰다. 깊은 곳, 시야에서 까마득히 멀리 감추어진 채, 이제 그 모든 것이 그에게는 그렇게 여겨졌다 — 그의 온 지난날, 그의 옛 생각들, 그의 옛 문제들과 이론들, 그의 옛 인상들, 그리고 저 풍경과 자기 자신, 그 모든 것, 그 모든 것이……. 그는 마치 자기가 위로 날아오르고, 모든 것이 시야에서 사라지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 무심코 손을 움직이다가, 그는 문득 주먹 속에 든 그 동전을 의식했다. 그는 손을 펴서 동전을 물끄러미 바라보다가, 팔을 휘둘러 그것을 물속에 내던졌다. 그러고는 몸을 돌려 집으로 향했다. 그 순간 그는 자신이 모든 이로부터, 모든 것으로부터 자신을 끊어 낸 듯한 느낌이 들었다.\n\n집에 다다랐을 때는 저녁이 다가오고 있었으니, 그는 여섯 시간쯤 돌아다녔던 셈이다. 어떻게, 어느 길로 돌아왔는지 그는 기억하지 못했다. 옷을 벗고, 혹사당한 말처럼 부들부들 떨면서, 그는 소파에 드러누워 외투를 끌어다 덮고는, 이내 망각 속으로 빠져들었다…….\n\n무시무시한 비명에 그가 잠에서 깼을 때는 땅거미가 지고 있었다. 맙소사, 그 무슨 비명이란 말인가! 그토록 부자연스러운 소리들, 그토록 울부짖고 통곡하고 이를 갈고 눈물을 쏟고 후려치고 욕설을 퍼붓는 소리를 그는 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다.\n\n그런 잔혹함, 그런 광란을 그는 도무지 상상조차 할 수 없었을 것이다. 공포에 질려 그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켰는데, 고통에 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다. 그러나 싸우고 통곡하고 욕설하는 소리는 점점 더 커져 갔다. 그러다 더없는 경악 속에서 그는 집주인 여자의 목소리를 알아들었다. 그녀는 빠르게, 다급하게, 두서없이 울부짖고 비명을 지르고 통곡하고 있어서, 그는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아들을 수 없었다. 그녀는 분명 때리지 말아 달라고 애원하고 있었으니, 계단에서 무자비하게 얻어맞고 있었던 것이다. 그녀를 때리는 자의 목소리는 악의와 분노로 어찌나 끔찍한지 거의 쉰 소리를 내고 있었으나, 그자 역시 무언가를 지껄이고 있었는데, 마찬가지로 빠르고 알아들을 수 없게, 서두르며 침을 튀기고 있었다. 별안간 라스콜니코프는 부르르 떨었다. 그 목소리를 알아챘던 것이다 — 그것은 일리야 페트로비치의 목소리였다. 일리야 페트로비치가 여기서 집주인 여자를 때리고 있다니! 그자가 그녀를 발로 차고, 그녀의 머리를 계단에 짓찧고 있어 — 그건 분명해, 그 소리로, 그 비명과 둔탁한 쿵쿵 소리로 알 수 있어. 어찌 된 일인가, 세상이 뒤집히기라도 했단 말인가? 그는 모든 층, 모든 계단에서 사람들이 떼 지어 달려 나오는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 목소리들, 외침들, 두드리는 소리, 문이 쾅쾅 닫히는 소리가 들렸다. \"그런데 왜, 왜, 어째서 이런 일이?\" 그는 자기가 정말 미쳐 버린 것이라고 진지하게 생각하며 되뇌었다. 하지만 아니야, 너무도 또렷이 들리잖아! 그러면 다음엔 그자들이 그에게 올 것이었다, \"왜냐하면 분명…… 모두 그 일 때문이야…… 어제 일 때문이라고…… 맙소사!\" 그는 빗장을 걸어 문을 잠그고 싶었으나, 손을 들 수가 없었다…… 게다가 그래 봐야 소용없을 터였다. 공포가 얼음처럼 그의 심장을 옥죄어, 그를 괴롭히고 마비시켰다……. 그러나 마침내 이 모든 소동은, 십 분쯤 이어진 끝에 차츰 가라앉기 시작했다. 집주인 여자는 끙끙 신음하고 있었다. 일리야 페트로비치는 여전히 위협과 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었다……. 그러나 마침내 그자도 잠잠해진 듯했고, 이제 그의 목소리는 들리지 않았다. \"가 버린 걸까? 오, 하느님!\" 그래, 이제 집주인 여자도 가는군, 여전히 흐느끼고 신음하면서…… 그러더니 그녀의 방문이 쾅 닫혔다……. 이제 사람들이 계단에서 저마다 자기 방으로 돌아가며, 소리치고, 다투고, 서로를 부르고, 목소리를 고함으로 높였다가 속삭임으로 낮추곤 했다. 분명 수가 많았을 것이다 — 이 건물에 사는 거의 모든 사람들이었으리라. \"그런데, 맙소사, 어떻게 이런 일이! 그리고 왜, 왜 그자가 여기 왔단 말인가!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 기진맥진하여 소파에 쓰러졌으나, 눈을 감을 수가 없었다. 그는 일찍이 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 그런 고뇌 속에서, 끝 모를 공포의 그토록 견딜 수 없는 감각 속에서 삼십 분가량 누워 있었다. 갑자기 밝은 빛이 그의 방으로 확 비쳐 들었다. 나스타시야가 촛불과 수프 한 접시를 들고 들어왔다. 그녀는 그를 유심히 살펴 그가 잠들지 않은 것을 확인하고는, 촛불을 탁자 위에 내려놓고 가져온 것들을 차려 놓기 시작했다 — 빵, 소금, 접시, 숟가락.\n\n\"어제부터 아무것도 안 먹었지, 틀림없이. 온종일 쏘다니더니, 열까지 나서 덜덜 떨고 있구먼.\"\n\n\"나스타시야…… 사람들이 왜 집주인 여자를 때린 거지?\"\n\n그녀가 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"누가 집주인 여자를 때렸다고?\"\n\n\"방금…… 삼십 분 전에, 일리야 페트로비치, 부서장 보좌가, 계단에서……. 왜 그자가 그녀를 그렇게 학대한 거지, 그리고…… 왜 그자가 여기 왔지?\"\n\n나스타시야는 말없이 이맛살을 찌푸린 채 그를 뜯어보았고, 그 뜯어보는 눈길은 오래도록 이어졌다. 그는 그녀의 캐묻는 듯한 눈초리에 불안해졌고, 심지어 겁이 났다.\n\n\"나스타시야, 왜 말이 없어?\" 마침내 그가 기어드는 목소리로 머뭇머뭇 말했다.\n\n\"피야.\" 마침내 그녀가, 마치 혼잣말을 하듯 나직이 대답했다.\n\n\"피? 무슨 피?\" 그가 새파랗게 질려 벽 쪽으로 돌아누우며 중얼거렸다.\n\n나스타시야는 여전히 말없이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"아무도 집주인 여자를 안 때렸어.\" 마침내 그녀가 단호하고 결연한 목소리로 잘라 말했다.\n\n그는 거의 숨도 쉬지 못한 채 그녀를 응시했다.\n\n\"내가 직접 들었는걸…… 난 잠들지 않았어…… 일어나 앉아 있었다고.\" 그가 한층 더 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"오랫동안 귀를 기울였어. 부서장 보좌가 왔어……. 모든 집에서 사람들이 계단으로 뛰쳐나왔다고.\"\n\n\"여긴 아무도 안 왔어. 그건 댁의 귓속에서 피가 울부짖는 거야. 피가 빠져나갈 데가 없어서 엉겨 붙으면, 자꾸 헛것이 보이기 시작하는 거지……. 뭐 좀 먹겠어?\"\n\n그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 나스타시야는 여전히 그를 굽어보며 지켜보고 서 있었다.\n\n\"마실 것 좀 줘…… 나스타시야.\"\n\n그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 흰 질그릇 물병에 물을 담아 돌아왔다. 그는 차가운 물을 한 모금 삼키고 목에 조금 흘렸던 것만을 기억했다. 그다음엔 망각이 뒤따랐다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n그렇지만 그는 앓는 내내 완전히 의식을 잃고 있었던 것은 아니었다. 열에 들떠 있었고, 때로는 헛소리를 했으며, 때로는 반쯤 정신이 들어 있었다. 그는 나중에 많은 것을 기억해 냈다. 어떤 때는 여러 사람이 자기 주위에 둘러서 있는 것 같았다. 그들은 그를 어딘가로 데려가려 했고, 그를 두고 한바탕 옥신각신하며 논쟁을 벌이고 있었다. 그러다가 그는 방 안에 홀로 남겨졌다. 다들 그를 무서워하며 가 버렸고, 이따금 문틈을 빠끔히 열어 그를 들여다볼 뿐이었다. 그들은 그를 위협하고, 무언가 함께 모의하고, 웃어 대며, 그를 비웃었다. 그는 나스타시야가 자주 자기 머리맡에 있었던 것을 기억했고, 또 다른 사람 하나도 분간해 냈는데, 아주 잘 아는 사람인 것 같으면서도 그가 누구인지 도무지 기억나지 않아 마음이 안달났고, 심지어 울음이 나올 지경이었다. 어떤 때는 자신이 거기 한 달이나 누워 있었던 것 같았고, 또 어떤 때는 그 모든 일이 같은 하루의 일부분처럼 느껴졌다. 그러나 그것에 대해서는 — 그것에 대해서는 — 아무런 기억이 없었지만, 그럼에도 매 순간 그는 자신이 마땅히 기억해야 할 무언가를 잊어버렸다는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 기억해 내려 애쓰며 안달하고 스스로를 괴롭혔고, 신음하고, 격분에 휩싸이거나, 끔찍하고 견딜 수 없는 공포 속으로 가라앉았다. 그러다가 일어나려고 발버둥 쳐 달아나려 했지만, 늘 누군가가 힘으로 그를 가로막았고, 그는 다시 무기력과 망각 속으로 주저앉았다. 마침내 그는 완전히 의식을 되찾았다.\n\n그 일은 아침 열 시에 일어났다. 날이 맑은 날이면 그 시각에 해가 방 안으로 비쳐 들어와, 오른쪽 벽과 문 가까운 구석에 한 줄기 빛을 던지곤 했다. 나스타시야가 그의 곁에, 또 다른 사람 하나와 함께 서 있었는데, 그 사람은 전혀 모르는 자로, 무척이나 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그는 수염이 난 젊은 사내로, 품이 넉넉하고 허리가 짧은 외투를 걸치고 있었고, 심부름꾼처럼 보였다. 집주인 여자는 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 안을 들여다보고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 몸을 일으켜 앉았다.\n\n\"이 사람은 누구야, 나스타시야?\" 그는 젊은 사내를 가리키며 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 정신이 돌아왔네!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"정신이 돌아왔습니다.\" 사내가 따라 말했다.\n\n그가 제정신을 되찾았다고 결론짓자, 집주인 여자는 문을 닫고 사라졌다. 그녀는 늘 수줍어했고 대화나 논쟁을 두려워했다. 마흔쯤 된 여자로, 결코 못생기지 않았으며, 살집이 있고 풍만했고, 검은 눈과 검은 눈썹을 지녔는데, 살이 찌고 게을러서 마음이 좋았고, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 수줍음을 탔다.\n\n\"당신은…… 누구요?\" 그가 사내를 향해 말을 이었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 문이 활짝 열리며, 키가 워낙 커서 몸을 약간 숙인 채로 라주미힌이 들어왔다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 오두막이야!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"나는 늘 여기서 머리를 박는다니까. 자네는 이런 데를 하숙이라고 부르나! 그래, 정신이 들었나, 이 사람아? 방금 파셴카한테서 소식을 들었네.\"\n\n\"방금 막 정신이 돌아왔어요.\" 나스타시야가 말했다.\n\n\"방금 정신이 돌아왔습니다.\" 사내가 미소를 지으며 또 따라 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 당신은 누구요?\" 라주미힌이 불쑥 그에게 말을 걸었다. \"내 이름은 브라주미힌이오, 잘 부탁하오. 다들 나를 라주미힌이라 부르지만, 브라주미힌이오, 대학생이자 신사지. 그리고 이 사람은 내 친구요. 그래, 당신은 누구요?\"\n\n\"저는 저희 사무소에서 온 심부름꾼입니다, 셸로파예프 상인 어른 댁에서요, 볼일이 있어 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"앉으시오.\" 라주미힌은 탁자 맞은편에 자리를 잡았다. \"자네가 정신을 차린 건 잘된 일이야, 이 사람아.\" 그는 라스콜니코프를 향해 말을 이었다. \"지난 나흘 동안 자네는 거의 먹지도 마시지도 못했다네. 차를 숟가락으로 떠 넣어 줘야 했어. 조시모프를 두 번이나 데려와 자네를 봐 달라고 했네. 조시모프 기억나나? 그가 자네를 꼼꼼히 진찰하더니 대뜸 별일 아니라고, 머리에 뭔가 좀 올라간 것 같다고 하더군. 잘 못 먹어서 생긴 신경성 헛소리라는 거야. 맥주랑 무를 충분히 못 먹어서 그렇다더군. 하지만 대단한 건 아니고, 곧 지나가서 멀쩡해질 거라는 거야. 조시모프는 일류 의사라네! 이름깨나 날리고 있지. 자, 더는 붙잡지 않으리다.\" 그는 다시 사내를 향해 말했다. \"당신이 원하는 게 뭔지 설명해 주겠소? 자네도 알아 둬야 하는데, 로쟈, 사무소에서 사람을 보낸 게 이번이 두 번째라네. 다만 지난번엔 다른 사람이었고, 내가 그 사람과 얘기를 나눴지. 지난번에 온 게 누구였소?\"\n\n\"그저께였습니다, 외람되지만 말씀드리자면요, 나리. 알렉세이 세묘노비치였습니다. 그 사람도 저희 사무소에 있지요.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 당신보다 더 똑똑하던데, 그렇게 생각하지 않소?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 나리, 그 사람이 저보다 더 무게가 있지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 자, 계속하시오.\"\n\n\"댁의 어머님 부탁으로, 아파나시 이바노비치 바흐루신 씨를 통해서요 — 그분에 대해선 한두 번쯤 들으셨으리라 생각합니다만 — 저희 사무소에서 송금이 보내졌습니다.\" 사내가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말을 꺼냈다. \"알아들으실 만한 상태이시라면, 댁에게 전해 드릴 삼십오 루블이 있습니다. 세묜 세묘노비치가 댁의 어머님 부탁에 따라 아파나시 이바노비치한테서 그런 지시를 받았기 때문이지요, 지난번에도 그랬듯이 말입니다. 그분을 아십니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"네, 기억합니다…… 바흐루신.\" 라스콜니코프가 꿈결처럼 말했다.\n\n\"들었나, 바흐루신을 안다는군!\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다. \"'알아들을 만한 상태'라는 거지! 그리고 보아하니 당신도 똑똑한 사람이로군. 음, 지혜로운 말은 언제 들어도 기분이 좋단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그분이 바로 그 어른이십니다, 바흐루신, 아파나시 이바노비치요. 그리고 댁의 어머님 부탁으로 — 어머님은 전에도 같은 방식으로 그분을 통해 한 번 송금을 보내셨지요 — 이번에도 거절하지 않으시고, 며칠 전 세묜 세묘노비치에게 삼십오 루블을 댁에게 전해 드리라는 지시를 보내셨습니다, 앞으로 더 나아지기를 바라며 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그 '앞으로 더 나아지기를 바란다'는 게 당신이 한 말 중에 최고로군, 하긴 '댁의 어머님'도 나쁘지 않았어. 자, 그래서, 당신 의견은 어떻소? 이 사람이 완전히 정신이 든 것 같소, 응?\"\n\n\"괜찮습니다. 이 작은 서류에 서명만 하실 수 있으면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"제 이름 정도는 휘갈길 수 있소. 장부 가져왔소?\"\n\n\"네, 여기 장부 있습니다.\"\n\n\"이리 주시오. 자, 로쟈, 일어나 앉게. 내가 잡아 주지. 펜을 들고 '라스콜니코프'라고 휘갈겨 주게. 지금 우리한테는, 이 사람아, 돈이 당밀보다도 더 달콤하다네.\"\n\n\"그건 필요 없어요.\" 라스콜니코프가 펜을 밀쳐 내며 말했다.\n\n\"필요 없다고?\"\n\n\"서명 안 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체 서명을 안 하면 어쩌란 말인가?\"\n\n\"그 돈은…… 필요 없어요.\"\n\n\"돈이 필요 없다니! 이봐, 이 사람아, 그건 말도 안 되는 소리야, 내가 증인이라네. 부디 신경 쓰지 마시오, 그저 또 헛것을 보고 있을 뿐이니까. 하긴 이 친구한테는 늘 흔한 일이오…… 당신은 분별 있는 사람이니, 우리가 이 친구를 다뤄 봅시다. 그러니까, 더 간단히 말해, 손을 잡아 주면 서명을 할 거요. 자.\"\n\n\"하지만 다음에 다시 와도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니. 뭐하러 당신을 또 번거롭게 하겠소? 당신은 분별 있는 사람이니…… 자, 로쟈, 손님을 기다리게 하지 마, 봐, 기다리고 있잖나.\" 그러고는 정말로 라스콜니코프의 손을 잡으려고 채비했다.\n\n\"그만, 내가 혼자 하겠어요.\" 라스콜니코프는 펜을 받아 들며 자기 이름을 서명했다.\n\n심부름꾼은 돈을 꺼내 놓고 가 버렸다.\n\n\"훌륭해! 자, 이제, 이 사람아, 배고픈가?\"\n\n\"네.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다.\n\n\"수프 좀 있나?\"\n\n\"어제 끓인 게 좀 있어요.\" 여전히 거기 서 있던 나스타시야가 대답했다.\n\n\"감자랑 쌀이 든 거?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"내가 그 맛을 훤히 알지. 수프 가져오고, 우리한테 차도 좀 주게.\"\n\n\"알겠어요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 이 모든 것을 깊은 놀라움과 무겁고 까닭 모를 공포에 휩싸여 바라보았다. 그는 잠자코 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 지켜보기로 마음먹었다. '내가 헛것을 보는 게 아닌 것 같아. 이건 현실인 것 같아.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n이삼 분 만에 나스타시야가 수프를 들고 돌아와, 차도 곧 준비될 거라고 알렸다. 수프와 함께 그녀는 숟가락 두 개, 접시 두 개, 소금, 후추, 쇠고기에 곁들일 겨자 따위를 가져왔다. 식탁이 오랜만에 제대로 차려졌다. 식탁보도 깨끗했다.\n\n\"나스타시야, 프라스코비야 파블로브나가 우리한테 맥주 두어 병만 올려 보내 주면 나쁘지 않을 텐데. 우리가 비워 줄 수 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"흥, 참 뻔뻔하기도 하네.\" 나스타시야가 중얼거리더니, 그의 분부를 따르러 자리를 떴다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 여전히 잔뜩 긴장한 채로 정신없이 응시하고 있었다. 그러는 동안 라주미힌이 그의 곁 소파에 앉았는데, 곰처럼 어설프게 왼팔로 라스콜니코프의 머리를 감싸 안았다. 라스콜니코프는 혼자 일어나 앉을 수 있었는데도 말이다. 그러고는 오른손으로 수프를 한 숟가락 떠서, 데지 않도록 후후 불어 가며 먹여 주었다. 하지만 수프는 겨우 미지근할 뿐이었다. 라스콜니코프는 한 숟가락을 게걸스럽게 삼켰고, 이어 두 숟가락, 세 숟가락을 삼켰다. 그러나 몇 숟가락을 더 떠먹여 준 뒤, 라주미힌은 갑자기 멈추더니, 더 먹여도 되는지 조시모프에게 물어봐야겠다고 말했다.\n\n나스타시야가 맥주 두 병을 들고 들어왔다.\n\n\"차도 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"빨리 가서, 나스타시야, 차 좀 가져오게. 차야 의사 양반 없이도 마셔 봄직하니까. 그런데 맥주가 여기 있군!\" 그는 자기 의자로 돌아가 수프와 고기를 앞으로 끌어당기고는, 사흘은 굶은 사람처럼 먹기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 말해 둘 게 있는데, 로쟈, 요즘 나는 매일 여기서 이렇게 식사를 한다네.\" 그는 쇠고기를 입에 가득 문 채 우물거렸다. \"그게 다 파셴카, 자네의 사랑스러운 집주인 아주머니가 챙겨 주는 덕분이지. 그 여자는 날 위해 뭐든 해 주는 걸 좋아해. 내가 부탁하는 것도 아닌데, 물론 마다하지도 않지. 그리고 여기 나스타시야가 차를 가져왔군. 손이 빠른 처녀야. 나스타시야, 우리 아가씨, 맥주 한잔 안 하겠나?\"\n\n\"그런 헛소리는 집어치워요!\"\n\n\"그럼 차 한 잔은?\"\n\n\"차 한 잔은 뭐, 좋아요.\"\n\n\"따라 주게. 가만, 내가 직접 따르지. 앉게나.\"\n\n그는 두 잔을 따르고는, 식사를 내버려 둔 채 다시 소파에 앉았다. 아까처럼 왼팔로 병자의 머리를 감싸 안아 일으키고는, 한 숟가락씩 차를 먹여 주었는데, 또다시 매 숟가락마다 한결같이 진지하게 후후 불어 주었으니, 마치 이 과정이 친구를 회복시키는 가장 중요하고 효과적인 수단이라도 되는 듯했다. 라스콜니코프는 아무 말도 하지 않았고 저항도 하지 않았지만, 사실은 받쳐 주지 않아도 혼자서 소파에 충분히 일어나 앉을 만큼, 단지 잔이나 숟가락을 쥘 수 있을 뿐 아니라 어쩌면 돌아다닐 수도 있을 만큼 기운이 있었다. 그러나 어떤 묘한, 거의 짐승 같은 교활함에서 그는 잠시 자기 힘을 숨기고 가만히 엎드려 있자는, 필요하다면 아직 제정신을 온전히 차리지 못한 척하면서, 그러는 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 귀 기울여 알아내자는 생각을 품었다. 그렇지만 그는 자신을 사로잡은 혐오감을 극복할 수 없었다. 차를 열 숟가락쯤 홀짝인 뒤, 그는 갑자기 머리를 빼내고는, 변덕스레 숟가락을 밀쳐 내고, 베개에 도로 쓰러졌다. 이제 그의 머리 밑에는 진짜 베개가, 깨끗한 잇을 씌운 깃털 베개가 놓여 있었으니, 그는 그것도 알아챘고 눈여겨보았다.\n\n\"파셴카가 오늘 우리한테 산딸기 잼을 좀 줘야겠어, 산딸기 차를 만들어 먹이게.\" 라주미힌은 자기 의자로 돌아가 다시 수프와 맥주에 달려들며 말했다.\n\n\"그 여자가 당신 줄 산딸기를 어디서 구한대요?\" 나스타시야가 다섯 손가락을 쫙 펴서 그 위에 받침 접시를 받쳐 들고, 각설탕을 입에 문 채 차를 홀짝이며 물었다.\n\n\"가게에서 구하겠지, 이 사람아. 그게, 로쟈, 자네가 앓아누워 있는 동안 별의별 일이 다 있었다네. 자네가 그렇게 못된 식으로 주소도 안 남기고 줄행랑을 쳤을 때, 내가 어찌나 화가 나던지 자넬 기어이 찾아내서 혼쭐을 내 주기로 결심했지. 바로 그날부터 나섰어. 자넬 찾아 수소문하느라 얼마나 쏘다녔던지! 이 하숙집은 까맣게 잊고 있었네, 하긴 애초에 몰랐으니 기억할 것도 없었지. 게다가 자네 옛날 하숙집은, 다섯 모퉁이에 있는 하를라모프네 집이었다는 것밖에 기억이 안 나더군. 그 하를라모프네 집을 줄곧 찾아다녔는데, 나중에 보니 그게 하를라모프네가 아니라 부흐네 집이지 뭔가! 사람이 가끔 소리를 어찌나 헷갈리는지! 그래서 나는 화가 나서, 이튿날 혹시나 하고 주소 관리소에 갔는데, 글쎄, 단 이 분 만에 자넬 찾아내지 뭔가! 자네 이름이 거기 올라 있더라고.\"\n\n\"내 이름이!\"\n\n\"그렇고말고. 그런데 내가 거기 있는 동안 코벨레프 장군은 못 찾아내더군. 뭐, 긴 얘기지. 어쨌든 이곳을 찾아내자마자, 나는 곧 자네 일을 죄다 알게 됐어 — 전부, 전부 다, 이 사람아, 내가 다 안다네. 여기 나스타시야가 자네한테 말해 줄 거야. 나는 니코딤 포미치와 일리야 페트로비치, 그리고 문지기와, 자메토프 씨, 알렉산드르 그리고리예비치 — 경찰서 서기장 말이야 — 와도 알게 됐고, 마지막으로 결코 빼놓을 수 없는 파셴카와도 알게 됐지. 여기 나스타시야가 알아…….\"\n\n\"이이가 그 여자를 구워삶았다니까요.\" 나스타시야가 능청맞게 웃으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"자네는 어째서 차에 설탕을 안 넣나, 나스타시야 니키포로브나?\"\n\n\"별사람 다 보겠네!\" 나스타시야가 갑자기 까르르 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"나는 니키포로브나가 아니라 페트로브나예요.\" 그녀는 웃음을 가라앉히며 불쑥 덧붙였다.\n\n\"잘 새겨 두지. 자, 이 사람아, 짧게 줄여 말하자면, 나는 여기서 한바탕 폭발이라도 일으켜 이 동네의 온갖 못된 기운을 죄다 뿌리 뽑을 작정이었는데, 파셴카가 이기더란 말이지. 나는, 이 사람아, 그 여자가 그렇게…… 사람을 끄는 데가 있을 줄은 미처 몰랐다네. 응, 자네는 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 말이 없었지만, 여전히 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 두려움이 가득한 눈길로 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"정말이지, 어느 모로 보나 바랄 게 없는 여자야.\" 라주미힌은 그의 침묵에 조금도 당황하지 않고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아유, 이 능청맞은 양반!\" 나스타시야가 또 한번 비명을 질렀다. 이 대화는 그녀에게 이루 말할 수 없는 즐거움을 안겨 주고 있었다.\n\n\"안타까운 건, 이 사람아, 자네가 처음부터 일을 제대로 풀어 가지 못했다는 거야. 자네는 그 여자한테 다르게 다가갔어야 했어. 그 여자는, 말하자면, 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 성격이거든. 하긴 그 여자 성격 얘기는 나중에 하기로 하고…… 자네는 어쩌다 일을 그 지경으로 만들어서, 그 여자가 자네 식사를 보내 주는 걸 그만두게 했나? 그리고 그 차용증은 또 뭐고? 차용증에 서명을 하다니 자네 미쳤던 게 분명해. 게다가 그 여자 딸 나탈리야 예고로브나가 살아 있을 때 했다는 그 결혼 약속은?…… 나는 다 안다네! 하지만 보아하니 이건 민감한 문제이고, 내가 멍청이로군. 미안하네. 그건 그렇고, 어리석은 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 자네 아나, 프라스코비야 파블로브나가 처음 보기처럼 그렇게 어리석은 여자가 결코 아니라는 거?\"\n\n\"그래.\" 라스콜니코프가 시선을 돌리며 우물거렸지만, 대화를 이어 가는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"안 그렇지?\" 라주미힌이 그에게서 대답을 얻어 낸 것이 기뻐 외쳤다. \"하지만 그렇다고 아주 영리한 것도 아니야, 응? 그 여자는 본질적으로, 본질적으로 종잡을 수 없는 성격이라고! 나도 가끔은 정말 어찌할 바를 모르겠다네, 정말일세…… 마흔은 됐을 거야. 본인은 서른여섯이라고 하지만, 물론 그렇게 말할 권리야 충분히 있지. 하지만 맹세코 나는 그 여자를 지적으로, 순전히 형이상학적인 관점에서 판단하는 거야. 우리 사이에는 일종의 상징 같은 게 싹터서, 일종의 대수학이라고 할까 뭐라고 할까! 나도 도무지 모르겠어! 뭐, 다 헛소리지. 다만, 자네가 이제 학생도 아니고, 가정교사 자리도 잃고 옷가지도 잃은 데다, 그 아가씨가 죽는 바람에 그 여자가 자넬 친척처럼 대할 까닭도 없어지니까, 그 여자가 별안간 겁을 집어먹은 거야. 게다가 자네가 자네 굴속에 틀어박혀서 그 여자와 옛정을 죄다 끊어 버리니까, 자넬 떼어 낼 궁리를 한 거지. 그리고 그 궁리를 오래도록 품어 왔는데, 차용증을 포기하기가 아까웠던 거야. 자네가 직접 자네 어머니가 갚아 줄 거라고 장담했으니까.\"\n\n\"그런 말을 하다니 비열한 짓이었어…… 우리 어머니는 거의 거지나 다름없는데…… 나는 하숙을 지키려고…… 그리고 밥을 얻어먹으려고 거짓말을 했어.\" 라스콜니코프가 크고 또렷하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 자넨 아주 분별 있게 처신했지. 다만 가장 고약한 건 거기서 체바로프 씨라는 작자가 나타났다는 거야, 사업가 말이지. 파셴카 혼자서야 무슨 일을 벌일 생각조차 못 했을 거야, 너무 내성적이니까. 하지만 그 사업가는 결코 내성적이지가 않아서, 대뜸 이렇게 묻는 거지. '이 차용증을 받아 낼 가망이 좀 있소?' 대답인즉슨, 가망이 있다, 왜냐하면 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 백이십오 루블 연금으로 제 아들 로쟈를 구할 어머니가 있고, 또 그를 위해서라면 몸이라도 팔 누이가 있으니까. 그자가 노린 게 바로 그거였어…… 왜 흠칫하나? 이제 나는 자네 일의 속사정을 다 안다네, 친구. 자네가 파셴카의 예비 사위였을 때 그 여자한테 그렇게 속을 다 털어놓은 게 괜한 일이 아니었지. 그리고 이 모든 얘기를 나는 친구로서 하는 걸세…… 하지만 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 아나. 정직하고 예민한 사람은 속을 다 털어놓고, 사업가는 '귀 기울여 듣는 척하면서 자넬 야금야금 잡아먹는다'는 거야. 그래서, 그 여자가 빚 대신으로 그 체바로프한테 차용증을 넘겼고, 그자는 망설임도 없이 정식으로 변제를 요구한 거야. 이 모든 얘기를 듣고서 나도 양심을 풀려고 그자를 한바탕 날려 버리고 싶었지만, 그때쯤엔 나와 파셴카 사이에 화목이 깃들어서, 자네가 갚겠다고 내가 보증을 서면서 이 일을 통째로 중단시키자고 우겼다네. 자네를 위해 내가 보증을 섰어, 이 사람아. 알겠나? 우리는 체바로프를 불러서, 십 루블을 던져 주고 그자한테서 차용증을 되찾아 왔지. 자, 그래서 영광스럽게도 그걸 자네한테 돌려주는 바일세. 이제 그 여자는 자네 말을 믿는다네. 자, 받게. 봐, 내가 찢어 놨어.\"\n\n라주미힌은 차용증을 탁자 위에 놓았다. 라스콜니코프는 그를 바라보더니, 한마디 말도 없이 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 라주미힌조차 가슴이 뜨끔했다.\n\n\"내가 보니, 이 사람아,\" 잠시 후 그가 말했다. \"또 멍청한 짓을 한 모양이군. 내 수다로 자넬 즐겁게 해 주려던 건데, 아무래도 화만 돋운 것 같아.\"\n\n\"내가 헛소리할 때 알아보지 못했던 게 자네였나?\" 라스콜니코프가 잠시 사이를 두고, 고개도 돌리지 않은 채 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 자네는 그 일로 잔뜩 화를 냈지, 특히 어느 날 내가 자메토프를 데려왔을 때 말이야.\"\n\n\"자메토프? 그 서기장? 뭐하러?\" 라스콜니코프가 홱 돌아누우며 라주미힌에게 눈길을 박았다.\n\n\"왜 그러나?…… 뭐 때문에 그렇게 흥분하나? 그 사람이 자네랑 사귀고 싶어 했어, 내가 자네 얘기를 그 사람한테 많이 했거든…… 그 사람한테서가 아니면 내가 어떻게 그 많은 걸 알아냈겠나? 그 사람은 대단한 친구라네, 이 사람아, 일류지…… 물론 제 나름대로 말이지만. 이제 우리는 친구가 돼서 — 거의 매일 만나. 내가 이 동네로 이사 왔거든, 알지. 막 이사 왔어. 그 사람이랑 같이 루이자 이바노브나한테도 한두 번 갔었지…… 루이자 기억나나, 루이자 이바노브나?\"\n\n\"내가 헛소리하면서 무슨 말을 했나?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이라니! 자넨 제정신이 아니었어.\"\n\n\"내가 무슨 헛소리를 했냐고?\"\n\n\"또 그 소린가? 무슨 헛소리를 했냐고? 사람들이 헛소리할 때 하는 그런 소리들이지…… 자, 이 사람아, 이제 시간을 더 허비하면 안 되겠어. 일하러 가야지.\" 그는 탁자에서 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 헛소리를 했냐니까?\"\n\n\"어찌나 끈질긴지! 무슨 비밀이라도 흘렸을까 봐 겁나나? 걱정 말게, 백작 부인 얘기 같은 건 안 했어. 다만 불도그 얘기는 잔뜩 했고, 또 귀고리며 목걸이 얘기, 크레스토프스키 섬 얘기, 어떤 문지기 얘기, 그리고 니코딤 포미치와 일리야 페트로비치 부서장 얘기를 했지. 그리고 또 한 가지 자네가 유난히 관심을 보인 건 자네 양말 한 짝이었어. 자네는 '내 양말 내놔' 하고 칭얼댔지. 자메토프가 자네 양말을 찾으려고 방 안을 온통 뒤졌고, 향수를 뿌리고 반지를 낀 제 손가락으로 그 누더기 같은 걸 자네한테 건네줬어. 그제야 자네는 안심이 됐는지, 그 후로 꼬박 하루 동안 그 너절한 걸 손에 꼭 쥐고 있었어. 우리가 그걸 도무지 자네한테서 못 떼어 냈다네. 지금도 십중팔구 자네 이불 밑 어딘가에 있을걸. 그러고 나서 자네는 바짓단에 술 장식을 달아 달라고 어찌나 애처롭게 조르던지. 우리는 어떤 술 장식인지 알아내려 했지만, 도무지 알 수가 없었어. 자, 이제 일 얘기로 돌아가지! 여기 삼십오 루블일세. 내가 그중에서 십 루블을 가져가고, 한두 시간 안에 셈을 해 주겠네. 그러는 김에 조시모프한테도 알리겠어, 진작에 왔어야 하는 사람인데, 벌써 열두 시가 다 됐으니 말이야. 그리고 나스타시야, 자네는 내가 없는 동안 이 사람이 마실 거나 다른 게 필요하지 않은지 자주 들여다봐 주게. 그리고 뭐가 필요한지는 내가 직접 파셴카한테 말하겠네. 잘 있게!\"\n\n\"파셴카라고 부르네! 아유, 보통내기가 아니라니까!\" 나스타시야는 그가 나가자 이렇게 말하더니, 문을 열고 서서 귀를 기울이다가, 끝내 참지 못하고 그를 따라 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다. 그녀는 그가 집주인 여자에게 무슨 말을 하는지 무척이나 듣고 싶었다. 그녀는 분명 라주미힌에게 단단히 홀려 있었다.\n\n그녀가 방을 나서기가 무섭게 병자는 이불을 걷어차고 미친 사람처럼 침대에서 벌떡 뛰어나왔다. 그는 일에 착수하려고, 그들이 가 버리기를 타는 듯한, 경련하듯 떨리는 조바심으로 기다려 왔던 것이다. 그런데 무슨 일에? 이제, 마치 그를 약 올리기라도 하듯, 그것은 그의 머릿속에서 빠져나가 버렸다.\n\n'오, 하느님, 한 가지만 말해 주세요. 그들이 벌써 그걸 알고 있는 겁니까, 아닙니까? 만일 그들이 알면서도 그저 모르는 척, 내가 누워 있는 동안 나를 비웃다가, 들이닥쳐서는 그게 진작에 발각됐다고, 그저 자기들이…… 라고 말하면 어쩌지? 이제 난 어떻게 해야 하지? 바로 그게 잊혀 버렸어, 마치 일부러 그런 것처럼. 한꺼번에 잊어버렸어, 방금 전엔 기억했는데.'\n\n그는 방 한가운데 서서 비참한 당혹감에 휩싸여 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 문으로 걸어가 문을 열고 귀를 기울였다. 하지만 그건 그가 원하던 게 아니었다. 갑자기, 무언가 생각난 듯, 그는 벽지 아래 구멍이 있는 구석으로 달려가 그것을 살피기 시작했고, 구멍 속에 손을 넣어 더듬었다. 하지만 그것도 아니었다. 그는 난로로 가서 뚜껑을 열고 잿더미를 뒤지기 시작했다. 거기엔 닳아 해진 바짓단과 주머니에서 잘라 낸 누더기 조각들이, 그가 던져 둔 그대로 놓여 있었다. 그렇다면 아무도 들여다보지 않았던 것이다! 그제야 그는 라주미힌이 방금 들려준 그 양말이 떠올랐다. 그래, 그건 이불 밑 소파 위에 놓여 있었는데, 어찌나 먼지와 때투성이인지 자메토프가 거기서 무엇 하나 알아챘을 리 없었다.\n\n'쳇, 자메토프! 경찰서! 그런데 왜 나더러 경찰서로 오라는 거지? 그 소환장은 어디 있지? 쳇! 내가 헷갈리고 있군. 그건 그때 일이야. 그때도 나는 내 양말을 들여다봤지만, 지금은…… 지금은 내가 앓았으니까. 그런데 자메토프는 뭐하러 온 거지? 라주미힌은 왜 그를 데려왔지?' 그는 맥없이 다시 소파에 주저앉으며 중얼거렸다. '이게 무슨 뜻일까? 내가 아직 헛것을 보는 건가, 아니면 현실인가? 현실인 것 같은데…… 아, 생각났어. 달아나야 해! 어서 달아나야 해. 그래, 달아나야 해, 달아나야 해! 그래…… 하지만 어디로? 게다가 내 옷은 어디 있지? 장화가 없어. 그들이 가져가 버렸어! 숨겨 버렸다고! 알겠어! 아, 여기 외투는 있군 — 이건 그냥 둔 거야! 그리고 탁자 위에 돈이 있어, 다행이야! 그리고 여기 차용증도…… 돈을 챙겨서 다른 하숙으로 가자. 그러면 그들이 날 못 찾을 거야!…… 그래, 하지만 주소 관리소는? 그들이 날 찾아낼 거야, 라주미힌이 날 찾아낼 거야. 차라리 아예 멀리 달아나는 게 낫겠어…… 아주 멀리…… 미국으로, 그래서 마음대로 하라고 내버려 두자! 차용증도 챙기고…… 거기서 쓸모가 있을 거야…… 또 뭘 챙기지? 그들은 내가 아픈 줄 알아! 내가 걸을 수 있는 줄은 모르지, 하하하! 그들 눈빛을 보니 다 알고 있더군! 어떻게든 아래층까지만 내려갈 수 있다면! 그런데 만일 거기 감시를 세워 뒀다면 — 경찰을! 이 차는 뭐지? 아, 여기 맥주가 남았네, 반 병, 차갑게!'\n\n그는 한 잔 분량의 맥주가 아직 남아 있는 병을 낚아채서, 마치 가슴속 불길을 끄기라도 하듯 흡족하게 들이켰다. 그러나 또 한순간이 지나자 맥주가 머리로 올라왔고, 등줄기를 따라 희미하면서도 오히려 기분 좋은 전율이 흘러내렸다. 그는 자리에 누워 이불을 끌어 덮었다. 병들고 두서없던 그의 생각은 점점 더 갈피를 잃어 갔고, 곧 가볍고 기분 좋은 졸음이 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 그는 안락한 기분으로 머리를 베개에 파묻고, 낡고 해진 외투를 대신한 부드럽고 솜이 든 이불로 몸을 더욱 단단히 감싼 채, 조용히 한숨을 내쉬고는 깊고 든든하며 개운한 잠 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n그는 누군가 들어오는 소리에 잠에서 깼다. 눈을 떠 보니 라주미힌이 문간에 서서, 들어갈지 말지 망설이고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 소파에서 얼른 몸을 일으켜 앉아, 무언가 기억해 내려는 듯 그를 응시했다.\n\n\"아, 안 자고 있었군! 자, 내가 왔네! 나스타시야, 꾸러미 가져오게!\" 라주미힌이 계단 아래로 소리쳤다. \"셈은 곧 해 주겠네.\"\n\n\"지금 몇 시야?\" 라스콜니코프가 불안하게 주위를 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 잘도 잤군, 이 사람아, 거의 저녁때야, 곧 여섯 시가 될 걸세. 자네는 여섯 시간도 넘게 잤어.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 내가 그랬다고?\"\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐 있나? 자네한테 도움이 될 거야. 뭐가 그리 급한가? 밀회라도 있나? 우리한텐 시간이 얼마든지 있어. 지난 세 시간 동안 자넬 기다렸다네. 두 번이나 올라왔는데 자네가 자고 있더군. 조시모프한테도 두 번이나 들렀어. 집에 없더라고, 글쎄! 하지만 상관없어, 곧 나타나겠지. 나도 내 볼일을 좀 보러 다녔어. 알다시피 나는 오늘 이사를 했거든, 삼촌이랑 같이 이사했지. 이젠 삼촌이 나랑 같이 사신다네. 하지만 그건 상관없는 얘기고, 일 얘기로 돌아가지. 꾸러미 이리 주게, 나스타시야. 곧 풀어 보세. 그래, 이제 좀 어떤가, 이 사람아?\"\n\n\"멀쩡해, 난 아프지 않아. 라주미힌, 자네 여기 온 지 오래됐나?\"\n\n\"내가 지난 세 시간 동안 자넬 기다렸다고 하잖나.\"\n\n\"아니, 그 전에.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말인가?\"\n\n\"여기 드나든 지 얼마나 됐냐고.\"\n\n\"아니, 오늘 아침에 내가 다 얘기했잖나. 기억 안 나?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 곰곰이 생각했다. 아침 일이 그에게는 꿈처럼 느껴졌다. 혼자서는 기억해 낼 수가 없어, 묻는 듯한 눈길로 라주미힌을 바라보았다.\n\n\"흠!\" 라주미힌이 말했다. \"잊어버렸군. 그때도 자네가 제정신이 아닌 것 같다고 생각했지. 이제 한숨 자고 나니 좀 나아졌군…… 정말 안색이 훨씬 좋아졌어. 일류야! 자, 일 얘기로 돌아가지. 이걸 좀 보게, 친구.\"\n\n그는 보따리를 풀기 시작했는데, 그 일에 자못 흥미를 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n\"믿어 주게, 이 사람아, 이건 내가 유난히 마음을 쓴 일이라네. 우리가 자넬 어엿한 사람으로 만들어야 하니까. 위에서부터 시작하지. 이 모자 보이나?\" 그는 보따리에서 제법 괜찮은, 비록 값싸고 평범한 것이긴 했지만, 모자 하나를 꺼내며 말했다. \"한번 씌워 보세.\"\n\n\"이따, 나중에.\" 라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 손을 내저으며 말했다.\n\n\"이러지 말게, 로쟈, 이 사람아, 마다하지 마. 나중엔 너무 늦어. 게다가 나는 밤새 한숨도 못 잘 거야, 치수도 안 재고 어림짐작으로 산 거니까. 딱 맞는군!\" 그가 모자를 씌워 보고는 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"꼭 자네 머리에 맞아! 제대로 된 모자야말로 옷차림에서 으뜸가는 것이고, 그 나름대로 추천장 노릇을 하지. 내 친구 톨스탸코프는 모자나 챙모자를 쓰는 사람들이 모이는 공공장소에 갈 때마다 늘 자기 푸딩 그릇 같은 모자를 벗어야만 한다네. 사람들은 그가 노예처럼 굽실거려서 그러는 줄 알지만, 사실은 그저 자기 까치집 같은 머리가 부끄러워서 그러는 거야. 그렇게 허세 부리기 좋아하는 친구거든! 보게, 나스타시야, 여기 모자 두 종류가 있네. 이 파머스턴이냐\" — 그는 무슨 영문인지 라스콜니코프의 낡고 찌그러진 모자를 파머스턴이라 부르며 구석에서 집어 들었다 — \"아니면 이 보물이냐! 값을 맞혀 보게, 로쟈, 내가 이걸 얼마 주고 샀을 것 같나, 나스타시야!\" 그는 라스콜니코프가 말이 없는 것을 보고 그녀를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"이십 코페이카, 그 이상은 아닐걸요, 아마.\" 나스타시야가 대답했다.\n\n\"이십 코페이카라니, 멍청하긴!\" 그가 발끈하며 외쳤다. \"아니, 요즘 같으면 자네 같은 사람도 그것보단 비싸겠다 — 팔십 코페이카는 줘야 한다고! 그것도 한 번 쓴 거라 그렇지. 게다가 조건까지 붙어서 산 거야. 이게 다 닳으면 내년에 새것 하나를 더 준다는 거지. 그래, 정말이라니까! 자, 이제 미국 합중국으로 넘어가 볼까, 학교에서 그렇게들 불렀듯이 말이야. 분명히 말하는데, 나는 이 바지가 자랑스럽다네.\" 그러고는 회색 모직으로 된 가볍고 시원한 여름 바지 한 벌을 라스콜니코프에게 펼쳐 보였다. \"구멍도 없고, 얼룩도 없고, 아주 멀쩡해, 조금 닳긴 했지만. 게다가 거기 어울리는 조끼도 있어, 아주 최신 유행이지. 그리고 좀 닳은 게 사실 더 좋아, 한결 부드럽고 매끈하거든…… 자, 로쟈, 내 생각엔 말이야, 세상을 살아가는 데 가장 중요한 건 늘 계절에 맞춰 사는 거야. 일월에 아스파라거스를 꼭 먹겠다고 고집부리지 않으면, 지갑 속에 돈을 지킬 수 있지. 이 물건도 마찬가지야. 지금은 여름이니 여름옷을 산 거고 — 가을엔 더 따뜻한 옷감이 필요할 테니, 어차피 이건 버려야 할 거야…… 더구나 그때쯤이면 자네의 고상한 사치 기준 때문이 아니더라도 옷감 자체가 제풀에 다 해어질 테니까. 자, 값을 매겨 보게! 얼마라고 하겠나? 이 루블 이십오 코페이카야! 그리고 그 조건을 잊지 말게. 이걸 다 닳도록 입으면, 다음 옷 한 벌을 공짜로 받는 거라고! 그런 식으로 장사하는 데는 페댜예프네뿐이야. 거기선 한번 물건을 사면 평생 만족이지, 다시는 제 발로 거길 안 가게 되니까. 자, 이제 장화 차례야. 어떤가? 보다시피 좀 닳긴 했지만, 두어 달은 갈 거야, 외제 솜씨에 외제 가죽이거든. 지난주에 영국 대사관 비서가 판 거야 — 엿새밖에 안 신었는데, 돈이 몹시 궁했던 모양이야. 값은 — 일 루블 반. 싸지?\"\n\n\"하지만 안 맞을지도 몰라요.\" 나스타시야가 말했다.\n\n\"안 맞다니? 이걸 좀 봐!\" 그러고는 그가 주머니에서 라스콜니코프의 낡고 터진 장화를 꺼냈는데, 마른 진흙이 뻣뻣하게 들러붙어 있었다. \"나도 빈손으로 간 게 아니야 — 이 괴물을 가지고 치수를 쟀다고. 우리 모두 최선을 다했지. 그리고 자네 속옷은 집주인 아주머니가 챙겨 주셨어. 자, 우선 셔츠가 세 벌인데, 삼베지만 유행하는 가슴 장식이 달렸어…… 자, 그럼, 모자가 팔십 코페이카, 옷 한 벌이 이 루블 이십오 코페이카 — 합쳐서 삼 루블 오 코페이카 — 장화가 일 루블 반인데, 보다시피 아주 좋은 거고 — 그러면 사 루블 오십오 코페이카, 속옷이 오 루블 — 한꺼번에 산 거야 — 그러니 딱 구 루블 오십오 코페이카로군. 잔돈 사십오 코페이카는 동전으로. 받겠나? 그래서, 로쟈, 자네는 완전히 새 차림으로 갖춰 입게 됐어. 외투야 쓸 만하고, 게다가 나름대로 멋도 있으니까. 그게 다 샤르메르네에서 옷을 맞춘 덕분이지! 양말이나 다른 것들은 자네한테 맡기겠네. 우리한텐 아직 이십오 루블이 남았어. 그리고 파셴카랑 하숙비 문제는 걱정 말게. 내가 장담하는데, 그 여자는 자네한테 뭐든 외상으로 줄 거야. 자, 이제, 이 사람아, 속옷을 갈아입혀 주지, 아마 셔츠와 함께 자네 병도 떨쳐 버릴 테니까.\"\n\n\"날 내버려 둬! 싫어!\" 라스콜니코프가 그를 밀쳐 냈다. 그는 자기가 산 물건들을 두고 익살을 떨려는 라주미힌의 애씀을 혐오스럽게 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"이러지 말게, 이 사람아, 내가 헛걸음만 하고 다녔다는 소린 말게나.\" 라주미힌이 우겼다. \"나스타시야, 부끄러워 말고 좀 도와주게 — 옳지, 그렇지.\" 그러고는 라스콜니코프의 저항에도 불구하고 그의 속옷을 갈아입혔다. 라스콜니코프는 베개에 도로 쓰러져 한두 마디도 하지 않고 잠자코 있었다.\n\n'이것들을 떨쳐 버리려면 한참 걸리겠군.' 그는 생각했다. \"그게 다 무슨 돈으로 산 거야?\" 그가 마침내 벽을 응시하며 물었다.\n\n\"돈? 아니, 자네 돈이지, 심부름꾼이 바흐루신한테서 가져온 거 말이야, 자네 어머니가 보내셨잖나. 그것도 잊어버렸나?\"\n\n\"이제 기억나.\" 라스콜니코프가 오래도록 시무룩한 침묵 끝에 말했다. 라주미힌은 인상을 찌푸린 채 불안하게 그를 바라보았다.\n\n문이 열리고, 라스콜니코프에게 낯이 익은 듯한, 키가 크고 뚱뚱한 사내가 들어왔다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n조시모프는 키가 크고 뚱뚱한 사내로, 부석부석하고 핏기 없는 말끔히 면도한 얼굴에 곧은 아마빛 머리칼을 가지고 있었다. 그는 안경을 쓰고 있었고, 살찐 손가락에는 커다란 금반지를 끼고 있었다. 나이는 스물일곱이었다. 밝은 회색의 유행하는 헐렁한 외투를 걸치고, 가벼운 여름 바지를 입었으며, 몸에 걸친 모든 것이 헐렁하고 유행을 따랐으며 티 하나 없이 말끔했다. 속옷은 흠잡을 데가 없었고, 시곗줄은 묵직했다. 행동거지는 굼떴고, 말하자면 무심한 듯했으며, 동시에 일부러 거리낌 없고 격의 없는 척했다. 자신의 거만함을 감추려 애썼지만, 그것은 매 순간 드러났다. 그를 아는 사람들은 하나같이 그를 지루한 사람이라 여겼지만, 일에 있어서는 영리하다고들 했다.\n\n\"오늘 두 번이나 자네한테 들렀었다네, 이 친구야. 보게나, 이 친구가 정신을 차렸어.\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다.\n\n\"보이는군, 보여. 그래, 지금은 기분이 어떤가, 응?\" 조시모프가 라스콜니코프에게 말하며 그를 주의 깊게 살피더니, 소파 발치에 앉아 가능한 한 편안하게 자리를 잡았다.\n\n\"아직 우울해하고 있어.\" 라주미힌이 말을 이었다. \"방금 속옷을 갈아입혔는데 거의 울 뻔했다네.\"\n\n\"그야 아주 자연스러운 일이지. 본인이 원치 않으면 나중으로 미뤘어도 됐을 텐데……. 맥박은 더할 나위 없이 좋군. 머리는 아직도 아픈가, 응?\"\n\n\"멀쩡합니다, 아주 멀쩡해요!\" 라스콜니코프가 단호하게, 그리고 짜증스럽게 잘라 말했다. 그는 소파에서 몸을 일으켜 반짝이는 눈으로 그들을 바라보았으나, 곧 다시 베개에 몸을 누이고 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 조시모프가 그를 유심히 지켜보았다.\n\n\"아주 좋아……. 잘 회복되고 있군.\" 그가 나른하게 말했다. \"뭘 좀 먹었나?\"\n\n그들이 그에게 말해 주고는, 무엇을 먹여도 되는지 물었다.\n\n\"뭐든 먹어도 돼……. 수프, 차……, 물론 버섯이나 오이는 주면 안 되지. 고기도 안 먹는 게 낫고, 그리고……, 뭐 자네한테 굳이 일러 줄 필요도 없겠지!\" 라주미힌과 그가 서로를 마주 보았다. \"약 같은 건 더 이상 필요 없네. 내일 다시 들여다보지. 어쩌면 오늘이라도……, 아니, 됐어…….\"\n\n\"내일 저녁에는 저 친구를 데리고 산책하러 갈 거야.\" 라주미힌이 말했다. \"유수포프 정원에 갔다가 그다음엔 팔레 드 크리스탈에 가려고.\"\n\n\"내일은 절대 저 친구를 들쑤셔 놓지 않겠는데, 글쎄……, 조금이라면 모르지……, 두고 보자고.\"\n\n\"아, 정말 골치 아프군! 오늘 밤 집들이 모임이 있는데, 여기서 코앞이야. 이 친구가 올 수는 없을까? 소파에 누워 있으면 되잖아. 자네도 오는 거지?\" 라주미힌이 조시모프에게 말했다. \"잊지 말게, 약속했으니까.\"\n\n\"좋아, 다만 좀 늦게 가겠네. 뭘 차릴 건가?\"\n\n\"오, 별거 없어 — 차, 보드카, 청어. 파이도 나올 거고……, 그냥 우리 친구들 모임이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 누가 오는데?\"\n\"여기 이웃들 전부, 거의 다 새로 사귄 친구들이지, 우리 늙은 삼촌만 빼면. 그분도 사실 처음이긴 해 — 어제야 무슨 볼일이 있어서 페테르부르크에 막 도착했거든. 우린 오 년에 한 번 보는 사이야.\"\n\n\"그분은 뭐 하는 분인데?\"\n\"평생을 지방 우체국장으로 빌빌대며 지내셨지. 쥐꼬리만 한 연금이나 받고. 예순다섯이야 — 입에 올릴 거리도 없지……. 그래도 난 그분을 좋아해. 포르피리 페트로비치는, 여기 수사과 과장인데……. 뭐 자네도 알겠지.\"\n\n\"그 사람도 자네 친척인가?\"\n\n\"아주 먼 친척이야. 한데 왜 인상을 찌푸리는 건가? 한 번 다툰 적 있다고 그럼 안 오겠다는 거야?\"\n\n\"그 사람 따위 알 게 뭐야.\"\n\n\"그럼 더 잘됐군. 자, 그리고 학생 몇 명에다 교사 하나, 관청 서기 하나, 음악가 하나, 장교 하나, 그리고 자메토프가 올 거야.\"\n\n\"그런데 말이지, 제발 좀 말해 주게, 자네나 저 친구가\" — 조시모프가 라스콜니코프 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다 — \"그 자메토프란 자와 무슨 공통점이 있을 수 있다는 건가?\"\n\n\"오, 까다로우신 양반 같으니! 원칙이라! 자넨 원칙에 휘둘려, 마치 태엽에 감긴 것처럼. 제 멋대로 한 바퀴 돌아볼 엄두도 못 내지. 사람이 괜찮은 녀석이면, 내가 따르는 원칙은 그것 하나뿐이야. 자메토프는 유쾌한 친구라네.\"\n\n\"뇌물을 받기는 하지만 말이지.\"\n\n\"흠, 그래, 받지! 그게 어쨌단 말인가? 그가 뇌물을 받든 말든 난 상관 안 해.\" 라주미힌이 부자연스러울 만큼 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"뇌물을 받는다고 그를 칭찬하는 게 아니야. 그저 그가 나름대로 괜찮은 사람이라는 거지! 한데 사람을 모든 면에서 따지고 들면 — 멀쩡한 자가 몇이나 남겠나? 아니, 나 자신부터가 구운 양파 한 알 값어치도 못 될 거라고 장담하네……. 어쩌면 자네까지 끼워 넣어도 그럴걸.\"\n\n\"그건 너무 박하군. 난 자네한테 양파 두 알은 쳐주겠는데.\"\n\n\"난 자네한테 한 알도 더는 안 쳐주겠어. 농담은 그만하자고! 자메토프는 애송이에 지나지 않아. 머리채를 휘어잡을 만한 녀석이지만, 그런 자는 밀어내지 말고 끌어당겨야 하는 법이야. 사람을 밀어내서야 절대 나아지게 만들 수 없어, 더구나 애송이는. 애송이는 두 배로 조심해서 다뤄야 한다고. 오, 이 진보적인 멍청이들 같으니! 자넨 이해를 못 해. 남을 깎아내리면서 제 발등을 찍는 거야……. 한데 알고 싶다면 말해 주지, 우린 정말로 공통점이 좀 있다네.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔지 알고 싶군.\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 다 어떤 칠장이에 관한 일이라네……. 우리가 그 친구를 곤경에서 빼내 주고 있거든! 하긴 이제 두려워할 건 전혀 없지만. 사건이야 더없이 자명하니까. 우린 그저 박차를 가하고 있을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"칠장이라고?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 얘기 안 했던가? 그때 자네한텐 늙은 전당포 노파가 살해된 일의 시작만 말해 줬었지. 그런데 그 칠장이가 거기 얽혀 있다는 거야…….\"\n\n\"오, 그 살인 사건이라면 전에 들었지, 그리고 좀 흥미를 느꼈어……, 부분적으로……, 어떤 이유 때문에……. 신문에서도 그 일을 읽었고…….\"\n\n\"리자베타도 살해됐어요.\" 나스타시야가 갑자기 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건네며 불쑥 끼어들었다. 그녀는 줄곧 방 안에 머물러 문가에 서서 듣고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"리자베타라니.\" 라스콜니코프가 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 작게 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"리자베타요, 헌 옷 팔던 여자 말이에요. 모르셨어요? 여기도 자주 왔었는데. 당신 셔츠도 한 번 기워 줬잖아요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 더러운 노란 벽지에서 그는 갈색 줄무늬가 들어간 볼품없는 흰 꽃 한 송이를 골라내어, 거기에 꽃잎이 몇 개나 되는지, 꽃잎마다 가장자리 굴곡이 몇 개인지, 그 위에 줄무늬가 몇 개인지 하나하나 헤아리기 시작했다. 그는 자기 팔다리가 마치 잘려 나간 것처럼 생기 없이 굳어 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 움직이려 들지도 않은 채 고집스레 그 꽃만 노려보았다.\n\n\"그래서 그 칠장이는 어떻게 됐는데?\" 조시모프가 노골적인 못마땅함으로 나스타시야의 수다를 가로막았다. 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"아니, 그 친구가 살인범으로 몰렸다는 거야.\" 라주미힌이 흥분해서 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그럼 그를 옭아맬 증거가 있었나?\"\n\n\"증거라니, 흥! 증거랄 것도 못 되는 증거지, 바로 그걸 우리가 입증해야 하는 거고. 처음에 코흐와 페스트랴코프, 그자들한테 누명을 씌웠던 거랑 똑같아. 흥! 죄다 어찌나 멍청하게 처리하는지, 내 일이 아닌데도 구역질이 날 지경이라니까! 페스트랴코프가 오늘 밤 올지도 몰라……. 그건 그렇고, 로쟈, 자넨 이미 이 일을 들어서 알지. 자네가 앓아눕기 전에 벌어진 일이니까. 경찰서에서 사람들이 그 얘기를 하고 있을 때 자네가 기절했던 그 전날이지.\"\n\n조시모프가 호기심 어린 눈으로 라스콜니코프를 바라보았다. 그는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"한데 말이야, 라주미힌, 난 자네가 신기해. 어쩜 그리 오지랖이 넓은가!\" 조시모프가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지, 어쨌든 우린 그 친구를 빼낼 거야.\" 라주미힌이 주먹으로 탁자를 내리치며 소리쳤다. \"무엇보다 거슬리는 건 그들이 거짓말을 한다는 게 아니야 — 거짓말이야 언제든 용서할 수 있지 — 거짓말이란 즐거운 거라고, 진실로 이어지니까 — 거슬리는 건 그들이 거짓말을 하면서 제 거짓말을 떠받든다는 거지……. 난 포르피리를 존경하네, 하지만……. 처음에 뭐가 그들을 헷갈리게 했는지 아나? 문이 잠겨 있었는데, 문지기를 데리고 다시 와 보니 열려 있었거든. 그러니 코흐와 페스트랴코프가 살인범이라는 거였어 — 그게 그들의 논리지!\"\n\n\"하지만 흥분하지 말게. 그들은 그저 두 사람을 붙잡아 둔 것뿐이야, 그럴 수밖에 없었던 거고……. 그건 그렇고, 나도 그 코흐란 사람을 만난 적이 있어. 그자가 노파한테서 기한 지난 저당물을 사들이곤 했다지? 응?\"\n\n\"맞아, 사기꾼이지. 악성 채권도 사들이고. 그걸 업으로 삼는 자야. 한데 그 작자 얘긴 그만하지! 내가 뭣 때문에 화가 나는지 아나? 그들의 진절머리 나고 썩어 빠지고 화석처럼 굳어 버린 관행 때문이야……. 그런데 이 사건이야말로 새로운 방법을 도입하는 계기가 될 수 있었어. 심리적 자료만으로도 진범의 자취를 어떻게 쫓아가는지 보여 줄 수 있단 말이지. '우리한텐 사실이 있다'고들 하지. 하지만 사실이 전부는 아니야 — 적어도 일의 절반은 그것을 어떻게 해석하느냐에 달려 있다고!\"\n\n\"그럼 자넨 그것들을 해석할 수 있다는 건가?\"\n\n\"어쨌든, 만약에 자기가 도움이 될 수도 있겠다는 느낌이, 손에 잡힐 듯 생생한 느낌이 들면, 입을 다물고 있을 수는 없는 거야……. 에이! 자넨 이 사건의 세세한 내막을 아나?\"\n\n\"난 그 칠장이 얘기를 기다리고 있다네.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지! 자, 그럼 얘기는 이렇네. 살인이 일어나고 사흘째 되던 날 아침 일찍, 그들이 아직 코흐와 페스트랴코프를 어르고 있을 때 — 하긴 그 둘은 자기 행적을 한 걸음 한 걸음 다 해명했고, 그게 대낮처럼 명백했지만 — 예기치 못한 사실 하나가 튀어나왔어. 그 집 맞은편에서 술집을 하는 두시킨이란 농사꾼이 금귀고리가 든 보석함을 경찰서로 가져와서는 장황한 사설을 늘어놓았다네. '그저께, 여덟 시가 막 지났을 무렵에' — 날짜와 시각을 잘 새겨 두게! — '그날 이미 한 번 절 찾아왔던 칠장이 견습공 니콜라이가 이 금귀고리와 보석이 든 상자를 가져와선, 이 루블만 빌려 달라고 했습니다. 어디서 났느냐고 물으니, 길에서 주웠다고 하더군요. 더는 캐묻지 않았습니다.' 이게 두시킨이 한 얘기야. '한 장짜리 지폐를' — 그러니까 일 루블이지 — '그에게 줬습니다. 저한테 저당 잡히지 않으면 다른 데 가서 잡힐 테고, 어차피 술값으로 날려 버릴 게 뻔하니, 차라리 제가 갖고 있는 게 낫겠다 싶었거든요. 깊이 감출수록 빨리 찾는 법이고, 무슨 일이 생기거나 소문이라도 들리면 경찰에 갖다주려고요.' 물론 죄다 허튼소리지. 거짓말을 입에 침도 안 바르고 하는 거야. 나도 이 두시킨이란 자를 아는데, 전당포 주인에다 장물아비거든. 그자가 니콜라이한테서 삼십 루블짜리 패물을 가로채고선 그걸 경찰에 갖다 바칠 위인이 아니야. 그냥 겁이 났던 거지. 어쨌든 그건 됐고, 두시킨 얘기로 돌아가지. '저는 이 니콜라이 데멘티예프란 농사꾼을 어릴 적부터 알았습니다. 자라이스크의 같은 군, 같은 면 출신이라, 둘 다 랴잔 사람이지요. 니콜라이가 술꾼은 아니어도 술을 마시긴 하는데, 그가 그 집에서 일하는 걸 알고 있었습니다, 역시 같은 마을 출신인 드미트리랑 칠일을 하고 있었지요. 일 루블을 받자마자 그걸 헐어서 두어 잔 들이켜고는, 거스름돈을 챙겨 나가더군요. 그땐 드미트리가 같이 있는 걸 보진 못했습니다. 그런데 이튿날, 누군가 도끼로 알료나 이바노브나와 그 동생 리자베타 이바노브나를 죽였다는 얘기를 들었지요. 전 그 두 사람을 알았고, 그 귀고리에 대해 곧장 의심이 들었습니다, 죽은 그 여자가 저당 잡고 돈을 빌려준다는 걸 알았으니까요. 그래서 그 집에 가서, 아무한테도 말 한마디 없이 조심스레 알아보기 시작했습니다. 우선 \"니콜라이가 여기 있소?\" 하고 물었지요. 드미트리가 말하기를, 니콜라이는 놀러 나갔다는 겁니다. 동틀 녘에 술에 취해 들어와선 십 분쯤 집에 있다가 다시 나갔다고요. 드미트리는 그 후로 그를 못 봤고 혼자 일을 마무리하고 있었습니다. 그리고 그들이 일하는 곳이 살인이 난 그 계단, 이 층이었지요. 그 얘길 다 듣고도 전 아무한테도 한마디 안 했습니다' — 이게 두시킨이 한 말이야 — '하지만 살인에 대해 알아낼 수 있는 건 다 알아내고, 여전히 의심을 품은 채 집으로 돌아왔습니다. 그리고 오늘 아침 여덟 시에' — 그러니까 사흘째 되는 날이지, 알겠나 — '니콜라이가 들어오는 걸 봤습니다, 멀쩡하진 않았지만 그렇다고 곤드레만드레도 아니어서 — 무슨 말을 하면 알아들을 정도는 됐지요. 그가 의자에 앉더니 말이 없었습니다. 술집엔 낯선 손님 하나에 제가 아는 사내 하나가 의자에서 자고 있었고, 우리 집 아이 둘이 있었지요. \"드미트리 봤나?\" 하고 제가 물었습니다. \"아뇨, 못 봤습니다\" 하더군요. \"그럼 자네도 여기 안 왔었나?\" \"그저께 이후로는요.\" \"어젯밤엔 어디서 잤나?\" \"페스키에서, 콜로멘스키 사람들이랑요.\" \"그 귀고린 어디서 났고?\" 하고 물었지요. \"길에서 주웠습니다\" 하는데, 말하는 품새가 어딘가 좀 이상했어요. 절 쳐다보지도 않더군요. \"바로 그날 저녁, 바로 그 시각에, 바로 그 계단에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 들었나?\" 하고 제가 물었습니다. \"아뇨, 못 들었는데요\" 하면서도, 내내 귀를 기울이고 있었고, 눈은 튀어나올 듯하더니 백묵처럼 하얗게 질려 가더군요. 제가 다 말해 주니까 그가 모자를 집어 들고 일어서려는 겁니다. 붙들고 싶어서 \"잠깐만, 니콜라이\" 하고 말했지요, \"한잔 안 할 텐가?\" 그러면서 아이한테 문을 막으라고 눈짓을 하고 바 뒤에서 나왔습니다. 한데 그가 휙 뛰쳐나가더니 길을 따라 모퉁이까지 냅다 달아나는 거예요. 그 후론 못 봤습니다. 그러니 제 의심도 끝난 거지요 — 그자 짓이었던 겁니다, 더할 나위 없이 명백하게…….'\"\n\n\"내 생각도 그렇네.\" 조시모프가 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐! 끝까지 들어 보게. 물론 그들은 사방팔방으로 니콜라이를 찾아 헤맸지. 두시킨을 붙잡아 두고 그 집을 수색했고, 드미트리도 체포했어. 콜로멘스키 사람들도 탈탈 털렸고. 그러다 그저께 도시 변두리의 어느 선술집에서 니콜라이를 붙잡았다네. 거기에 가서, 목에 걸고 있던 은십자가를 풀어 술 한 잔과 바꿔 달라고 했다는 거야. 사람들이 술을 줬지. 몇 분 뒤에 술집 여자가 외양간에 갔다가 벽 틈으로 들여다보니, 옆에 붙은 마구간에서 그가 들보에 허리띠로 올가미를 매달아 놓고, 나무토막 위에 올라서서 목을 그 올가미에 넣으려 하고 있더라는 거야. 여자가 있는 힘껏 비명을 질렀고, 사람들이 달려갔지. '아니, 이게 무슨 짓이야!' '날 데려가 주시오' 하고 그가 말했어, '아무개 경관한테 말이오. 다 자백하겠소.' 자, 그래서 적당한 호송을 붙여 그를 경찰서로 — 그러니까 여기로 — 데려갔지. 그래서 이것저것 물었어, 나이가 몇이냐, '스물둘입니다', 뭐 그런 식으로. '드미트리와 함께 일하고 있을 때, 아무 시각에 계단에서 누굴 못 봤느냐?'는 물음에 — 대답이, '뭐 사람들이야 오르내렸겠지만, 눈여겨보진 않았습니다.' '그럼 아무 소리도, 무슨 소음 같은 것도 못 들었느냐?' '별다른 건 못 들었습니다.' '그럼 니콜라이, 같은 날 아무개 과부와 그 동생이 살해당하고 강도를 당했다는 걸 들었느냐?' '그 일은 전혀 몰랐습니다. 그저께 아파나시 파블로비치한테 처음 들었습니다.' '그럼 그 귀고린 어디서 났느냐?' '길바닥에서 주웠습니다.' '그날 왜 드미트리랑 같이 일하러 안 갔느냐?' '술을 마시느라요.' '어디서 마셨느냐?' '아, 아무 데서요.' '왜 두시킨네서 달아났느냐?' '몹시 겁이 나서요.' '뭐가 무서웠느냐?' '제가 누명을 쓸까 봐요.' '죄가 없다고 느꼈다면 어째서 무서웠겠느냐?' 자, 조시모프, 자넨 못 믿을지도 모르지만, 그 질문은 글자 그대로 그렇게 던져진 거야. 난 그게 사실인 걸 알아, 토씨 하나 안 틀리고 내게 전해 줬으니까! 자넨 이걸 어떻게 생각하나?\"\n\n\"뭐, 어쨌든, 증거는 거기 있는 셈이군.\"\n\n\"난 지금 증거 얘길 하는 게 아니야, 그 질문 얘길 하는 거지, 자기 자신들에 대한 그들의 생각 말이야. 자, 그래서 그를 들들 볶고 또 볶으니까 자백을 했어. '길에서 주운 게 아니라, 드미트리랑 칠을 하던 그 셋집에서 주웠습니다.' '그럼 어떻게 된 거냐?' '아니, 드미트리랑 제가 거기서 온종일 칠을 하다가, 막 나가려던 참이었는데, 드미트리가 솔을 집어 들고 제 얼굴에 칠을 하고선 달아나는 거예요. 그래서 제가 쫓아갔지요. 있는 힘껏 소리치며 뒤쫓아 내려가다가, 계단 맨 아래서 문지기랑 신사 몇 분이랑 정면으로 부딪쳤습니다 — 신사가 몇이었는지는 기억이 안 나요. 문지기가 욕을 퍼붓고, 다른 문지기도 욕을 하고, 문지기 마누라도 나와서 우리한테 욕을 했지요. 그리고 어떤 신사가 부인이랑 입구로 들어오다가, 우리한테 또 욕을 했습니다, 드미트리랑 제가 길을 가로막고 누워 있었으니까요. 제가 드미트리의 머리채를 휘어잡아 넘어뜨리고 두들겨 패기 시작했지요. 그러자 드미트리도 제 머리채를 붙잡고 두들겨 패는 겁니다. 한데 그게 다 홧김이 아니라, 그저 장난삼아, 친한 사이라서 그런 거였어요. 그러고는 드미트리가 빠져나가 길로 달아났고, 제가 뒤를 쫓았지요. 한데 못 잡고 혼자 셋집으로 돌아갔습니다, 짐을 챙겨야 했거든요. 드미트리가 오려니 하며 짐을 한데 모으기 시작했는데, 거기 통로 문 옆 구석에서 그 상자를 밟았지 뭡니까. 그게 종이에 싸여 거기 놓여 있는 게 보이더군요. 종이를 벗기니 작은 갈고리들이 있어서 그걸 끄르니까, 상자 안에 귀고리가…….'\"\n\n\"문 뒤에라고? 문 뒤에 놓여 있었다고? 문 뒤에?\" 라스콜니코프가 느닷없이 외치며, 공포로 멍해진 눈길로 라주미힌을 노려보더니, 한 손으로 몸을 짚으며 천천히 소파에서 일어나 앉았다.\n\n\"그래……, 왜? 무슨 일이야? 뭐가 잘못됐나?\" 라주미힌도 자기 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"아무것도 아냐.\" 라스콜니코프가 힘없이 대답하며 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 잠시 모두가 말이 없었다.\n\n\"꿈에서 막 깬 모양이군.\" 마침내 라주미힌이 조시모프를 캐묻듯 바라보며 말했다. 조시모프는 가볍게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"자, 계속하게.\" 조시모프가 말했다. \"그다음은?\"\n\n\"그다음? 그가 귀고리를 보자마자, 드미트리고 뭐고 다 잊어버리고 모자를 집어 들고 두시킨한테 달려갔지, 그래서 우리도 알다시피 그한테서 일 루블을 받았고. 길에서 주웠다고 거짓말을 하고는 술을 마시러 갔지. 그리고 살인에 대해선 줄곧 같은 얘길 되풀이해. '그건 전혀 모릅니다, 그저께까지 들어 본 적도 없어요.' '그럼 왜 이제껏 경찰에 안 왔느냐?' '겁이 나서요.' '그럼 왜 목을 매려 했느냐?' '불안해서요.' '무슨 불안?' '그 일로 누명을 쓸까 봐서요.' 자, 이게 전부야. 그래, 자넨 그들이 거기서 무얼 추론해 냈을 것 같나?\"\n\n\"아니, 추론할 것도 없지. 단서가, 그런대로 단서가, 사실 하나가 있잖나. 자네라고 그 칠장이를 풀어 주자고는 못 할걸?\"\n\n\"그들은 이제 그냥 그를 살인범으로 단정해 버렸어. 한 점 의심도 없이.\"\n\n\"그건 말도 안 돼. 자넨 흥분하고 있어. 한데 그 귀고리는 어떻게 된 건가? 바로 그날 그 시각에 노파의 상자에서 나온 귀고리가 니콜라이의 손에 들어왔다면, 어떻게든 거기로 들어간 게 분명하다는 건 자네도 인정해야지. 이런 사건에선 그게 상당한 무게를 지니거든.\"\n\n\"어떻게 거기로 들어갔느냐고? 어떻게 거기로 들어갔겠나?\" 라주미힌이 소리쳤다. \"자네 같은 의사가, 인간을 연구하는 게 본분이고 그 누구보다 인간 본성을 연구할 기회가 많은 자네가 — 어떻게 그 모든 이야기 속에서 그 사내의 됨됨이를 못 본단 말인가? 그가 심문에서 한 대답이 신성한 진실이란 걸 단박에 모르겠나? 귀고리는 정확히 그가 우리한테 말한 그대로 그의 손에 들어간 거야 — 상자를 밟고 그걸 집어 든 거라고.\"\n\n\"신성한 진실이라! 하지만 그자 스스로 처음엔 거짓말을 했다고 인정하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"내 말을 듣게, 잘 들어 보라고. 문지기와 코흐와 페스트랴코프, 그리고 다른 문지기, 첫 번째 문지기의 마누라, 문지기 방에 앉아 있던 여자, 그리고 바로 그 순간 마차에서 내려 부인을 팔에 끼고 입구로 들어온 크류코프라는 사내, 그러니까 여덟에서 열 명쯤 되는 증인들이 입을 모아, 니콜라이가 드미트리를 바닥에 깔고 올라타 두들겨 패고 있었고, 한편 드미트리는 그의 머리채에 매달려 역시 그를 패고 있었다고 증언했어. 둘이 길을 정면으로 가로막고 통로를 틀어막은 채 누워 있었던 거지. 사방에서 욕을 먹는 동안에도 그 둘은 '어린애들처럼'(증인들이 한 말 그대로야) 서로 뒤엉켜 나뒹굴며, 꽥꽥대고 치고받고 더없이 우스꽝스러운 표정으로 웃어 대다가, 어린애들처럼 서로를 쫓으며 길로 달려 나갔다는 거야. 자, 잘 새겨듣게. 위층의 시신들은 아직 따뜻했어, 알겠나, 발견됐을 때 따뜻했단 말이야! 만약 그 둘이, 아니면 니콜라이 혼자서 그들을 죽이고 상자를 부숴 열었거나, 그저 강도질에 가담만 했다 치더라도, 자네한테 한 가지만 묻겠네. 그들의 정신 상태가, 그 꽥꽥거림과 낄낄거림과 대문간에서의 어린애 같은 몸싸움이, 도끼며 유혈이며 악마 같은 교활함이며 강도질과 들어맞는가? 그들은 막 사람을 죽인 거야, 채 오 분도 십 분도 안 됐어, 시신이 아직 따뜻했으니까. 그런데 곧장 셋집 문을 열어 둔 채로, 사람들이 당장 거기로 갈 걸 뻔히 알면서, 훔친 물건을 내팽개치고는, 어린애들처럼 나뒹굴며 웃어 대고 뭇사람의 눈길을 끌었단 말이지. 게다가 그걸 증언할 증인이 열두 명이나 된다고!\"\n\n\"물론 이상하긴 하군! 정말이지 있을 수 없는 일이야, 하지만…….\"\n\n\"아니, 이 사람아, 하지만은 없어. 그리고 만약 살인이 일어난 바로 그날 그 시각에 니콜라이의 손에서 귀고리가 발견된 것이 그에게 불리한 중요한 정황 증거가 된다 해도 — 그가 한 해명이 그걸 설명해 주고, 따라서 그에게 결정적으로 불리하게 작용하진 못하지만 — 그가 무죄임을 입증하는 사실들도 고려해야 하는 거야, 더구나 그건 부인할 수 없는 사실들이거든. 그런데 자넨 우리 사법 제도의 성격으로 미루어, 그들이 이 사실을 — 순전히 심리적 불가능성에 근거한 이 사실을 — 반박할 수 없는 것으로, 검찰 측의 정황 증거를 결정적으로 무너뜨리는 것으로 받아들일 거라고, 또는 받아들일 입장이라고 보나? 아니, 그들은 받아들이지 않을 거야, 결코 받아들이지 않아. 보석함을 찾아냈고 그 사내가 목을 매려 했으니까, '죄가 없었다면 그러지 않았을 것'이라는 거지. 바로 그게 핵심이야, 바로 그게 날 흥분하게 만든다고, 자네가 좀 알아줘야 해!\"\n\n\"오, 자네가 흥분한 건 알겠네! 잠깐만. 한 가지 물어보는 걸 깜빡했군. 그 상자가 노파한테서 나온 거라는 증거는 뭔가?\"\n\n\"그건 입증됐어.\" 라주미힌이 인상을 찌푸리며 마지못한 듯 대답했다. \"코흐가 그 보석함을 알아보고 주인 이름을 댔는데, 그 주인이 자기 것임을 확실하게 입증했지.\"\n\n\"그거 안 좋은데. 자, 또 한 가지. 코흐와 페스트랴코프가 처음 위층으로 올라가던 그 시각에 니콜라이를 본 사람이 있나, 그에 관한 증언은 없고?\"\n\n\"본 사람은 아무도 없어.\" 라주미힌이 분한 듯 대답했다. \"그게 가장 골치 아픈 점이야. 코흐와 페스트랴코프조차 위층으로 올라가는 길에 그들을 못 봤다는군, 하긴 그자들 증언이야 큰 값어치도 없겠지만. 그들은 셋집 문이 열려 있는 걸 봤고, 그 안에서 무슨 작업이 진행 중인 게 분명하다고 봤다지만, 특별히 눈여겨보진 않아서 실제로 거기 일하는 사람들이 있었는지는 기억하지 못한다더군.\"\n\n\"흠!……, 그렇다면 변호에 쓸 유일한 증거는 그들이 서로 치고받으며 웃고 있었다는 것뿐이군. 그게 강력한 추정 근거가 되긴 하지만……. 자네는 그 사실들을 어떻게 설명하나?\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 설명하느냐고? 설명할 게 뭐 있나? 명백하잖아. 적어도 설명을 찾아야 할 방향만큼은 명백해, 그리고 그 보석함이 그 방향을 가리키고 있지. 진범이 그 귀고리를 떨어뜨린 거야. 코흐와 페스트랴코프가 문을 두드렸을 때, 살인범은 위층에서 안에 갇혀 있었던 거지. 코흐가 멍청하게도 문 앞에 머물러 있지 않았으니, 살인범은 톡 튀어나와 역시 아래로 달아난 거야. 달리 빠져나갈 길이 없었으니까. 니콜라이와 드미트리가 막 뛰쳐나간 그 셋집에서, 그는 코흐와 페스트랴코프와 문지기를 피해 몸을 숨긴 거지. 문지기와 다른 사람들이 위층으로 올라가는 동안 거기 머물러 있다가, 그들의 인기척이 들리지 않을 때까지 기다린 다음, 드미트리와 니콜라이가 길로 뛰쳐나가 입구에 아무도 없는 바로 그 순간에 태연히 아래층으로 내려간 거야. 어쩌면 누군가 그를 봤겠지만, 눈여겨보진 않았겠지. 드나드는 사람이 워낙 많으니까. 그는 문 뒤에 서 있을 때 주머니에서 귀고리를 떨어뜨린 게 분명해, 그리고 떨어뜨린 줄도 몰랐던 거지, 신경 쓸 다른 일들이 있었으니까. 그 보석함이 바로 그가 거기 서 있었다는 결정적인 증거야……. 난 그렇게 설명하네.\"\n\n\"너무 영리하군! 아니, 이 친구야, 자넨 너무 영리해. 그건 정말 압권이야.\"\n\n\"한데 왜, 왜 그러나?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 모든 게 너무 딱 들어맞으니까……, 너무 신파조라고.\"\n\n\"아-아!\" 라주미힌이 막 외치려던 참에, 바로 그 순간 문이 열리며 그 자리에 있던 모두에게 낯선 한 인물이 들어왔다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n이자는 더 이상 젊지 않은, 뻣뻣하고 풍채 좋은 신사로, 조심스럽고 떨떠름한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그는 우선 문간에 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 마치 자기가 도대체 어떤 곳에 들어선 것인가를 스스로에게 묻기라도 하듯 무례하고 노골적인 놀라움으로 주변을 둘러보았다. 불신에 차서, 그리고 짐짓 놀란 듯, 거의 모욕이라도 당한 듯한 태도로, 그는 라스콜니코프의 나지막하고 좁은 '선실'을 훑어보았다. 그 똑같은 경악의 표정으로 그는 라스콜니코프를 빤히 바라보았는데, 그는 옷도 제대로 걸치지 않고 헝클어진 채 씻지도 않은 모습으로 그 초라하고 더러운 소파에 누워 그를 뚫어지게 쳐다보고 있었다. 그러고는 또 그 똑같은 신중한 태도로, 자리에서 일어나지도 않은 채 대담하고 캐묻듯 그의 얼굴을 마주 보고 있던 라주미힌의 우락부락하고 헝클어진 행색과 면도하지 않은 얼굴을 자세히 뜯어보았다. 어색한 침묵이 한 이 분쯤 이어졌고, 이윽고 으레 그렇듯 무대 전환 같은 것이 일어났다. 아마도 어지간히 분명한 몇 가지 징후로 미루어, 이 '선실'에서 위세를 부려 본들 아무것도 얻을 게 없으리라는 것을 깨달았는지, 신사는 다소 누그러져서, 비록 약간의 엄격함은 남아 있었지만 정중하게, 자기 질문의 음절 하나하나에 힘을 주며 조시모프를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프, 대학생, 아니면 한때 대학생이었던 분 맞소?\"\n\n조시모프는 가볍게 몸을 움직이며 대답하려 했으나, 라주미힌이 한발 앞섰다.\n\n\"여기 소파에 누워 있는 사람이오! 무슨 일이시오?\"\n\n이 허물없는 \"무슨 일이시오\"는 거드름 피우는 신사의 발밑을 단번에 허물어 버리는 것 같았다. 그는 라주미힌 쪽으로 몸을 돌리려다가, 제때 마음을 고쳐먹고 다시 조시모프를 향했다.\n\n\"이쪽이 라스콜니코프요.\" 조시모프가 그를 향해 고갯짓하며 우물거렸다. 그러고는 길게 하품을 하며 입을 한껏 크게 벌렸다. 이어 그는 나른하게 조끼 주머니에 손을 넣더니, 둥근 사냥꾼용 뚜껑이 달린 커다란 금시계를 꺼내어 뚜껑을 열고 들여다본 다음, 똑같이 느리고 나른한 동작으로 다시 집어넣었다.\n\n라스콜니코프 자신은 말없이 반듯이 누운 채, 영문도 모르면서 그 낯선 사람을 집요하게 바라보고 있었다. 벽지의 그 기묘한 꽃무늬에서 얼굴을 돌리고 나니, 그의 얼굴은 극도로 창백했고, 마치 방금 끔찍한 수술을 받았거나 고문대에서 막 끌려 내려오기라도 한 듯 고뇌의 빛을 띠고 있었다. 그러나 이 새로운 방문객은 차츰 그의 주의를 끌더니, 이어 호기심을, 그다음에는 의혹을, 심지어 두려움까지 불러일으키기 시작했다. 조시모프가 \"이쪽이 라스콜니코프요\"라고 말했을 때, 그는 벌떡 일어나 소파에 앉더니, 거의 도전적이지만 힘없이 갈라지는 목소리로 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 내가 라스콜니코프요! 무슨 일이시오?\"\n\n방문객은 그를 유심히 살펴보고는 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"표트르 페트로비치 루진이오. 내 이름이 댁에게 아주 생소하지는 않으리라 기대할 만한 이유가 있다고 생각하오만?\"\n\n그러나 전혀 다른 무언가를 예상하고 있던 라스콜니코프는, 마치 표트르 페트로비치라는 이름을 난생처음 듣기라도 한 듯, 아무 대답도 없이 멍하니, 꿈꾸듯 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"설마 지금까지 아무런 소식도 받지 못했단 말이오?\" 표트르 페트로비치가 다소 당황하여 물었다.\n\n대답 대신 라스콜니코프는 맥없이 베개에 도로 몸을 묻고, 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 낀 채 천장을 바라보았다. 루진의 얼굴에 낭패의 빛이 떠올랐다. 조시모프와 라주미힌이 그 어느 때보다 캐묻듯 그를 바라보자, 마침내 그는 영락없이 당황한 기색을 드러냈다.\n\n\"내 짐작에는, 또 계산으로는,\" 그가 더듬거렸다. \"보름은 아니더라도 열흘은 더 전에 부친 편지가…….\"\n\n\"이보시오, 왜 문간에 서 계시오?\" 라주미힌이 불쑥 말을 가로챘다. \"할 말이 있으면 앉으시오. 거기선 나스타시야하고 너무 비좁잖소. 나스타시야, 자리 좀 비켜. 여기 의자 있소, 비집고 들어오시오!\"\n\n그는 탁자에서 의자를 뒤로 빼어 탁자와 자기 무릎 사이에 작은 공간을 만들고는, 다소 옹색한 자세로 방문객이 \"비집고 들어오기를\" 기다렸다. 거절할 수 없는 절묘한 순간이었던지라, 방문객은 서두르고 비틀거리며 그 틈을 비집고 들어왔다. 의자에 다다른 그는 의심스러운 눈초리로 라주미힌을 보며 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"긴장하실 것 없소.\" 라주미힌이 불쑥 내뱉었다. \"로쟈는 닷새째 앓고 있고 사흘은 헛소리를 했지만, 이제 회복 중이고 입맛도 돌아왔소. 이쪽은 방금 진찰을 한 그의 의사요. 나는 로쟈의 동료인데, 그처럼 한때 대학생이었고 지금은 그를 간병하고 있소. 그러니 우리는 신경 쓰지 마시고 댁 용건이나 보시오.\"\n\n\"고맙소. 그런데 내가 있고 또 이야기를 나누면 환자에게 방해가 되지 않겠소?\" 표트르 페트로비치가 조시모프에게 물었다.\n\n\"아-니오.\" 조시모프가 우물거렸다. \"오히려 기분 전환이 될 거요.\" 그는 다시 하품을 했다.\n\n\"이 친구는 진작부터, 아침부터 의식이 또렷하오.\" 라주미힌이 말을 이었는데, 그의 허물없는 태도가 어찌나 꾸밈없는 호의처럼 보였던지, 표트르 페트로비치는 좀 더 기분이 밝아지기 시작했다. 어쩌면 이 남루하고 뻔뻔한 자가 자신을 대학생이라고 소개한 까닭도 한몫했을 것이다.\n\n\"댁의 어머님께서는,\" 루진이 입을 열었다.\n\n\"흠!\" 라주미힌이 큰 소리로 헛기침을 했다. 루진이 캐묻듯 그를 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니오, 계속하시오.\"\n\n루진이 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"댁의 어머님께서는 내가 그분 근처에 머무는 동안 댁에게 보낼 편지를 쓰기 시작하셨소. 나는 이곳에 도착해서도 일부러 며칠을 흘려보낸 뒤에 댁을 찾아왔는데, 댁이 그 소식을 온전히 전해 들었는지 확실히 해 두기 위해서였소. 그런데 이제 와서, 놀랍게도…….\"\n\n\"알고 있소, 알고 있다고요!\" 라스콜니코프가 갑자기 못 참겠다는 듯 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"그러니까 당신이 그 약혼자란 말이죠? 알고 있으니, 그걸로 됐소!\"\n\n이번에는 표트르 페트로비치가 모욕을 느낀 것이 분명했지만, 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 이 모든 게 무슨 영문인지 이해하려고 안간힘을 썼다. 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n그러는 사이 대답을 할 때 그를 향해 몸을 약간 돌렸던 라스콜니코프가, 마치 아직 그를 제대로 뜯어보지 못했거나 무언가 새로운 점이 눈에 띄기라도 한 듯, 갑자기 또렷한 호기심을 가지고 다시 그를 빤히 바라보기 시작했다. 그는 일부러 베개에서 몸을 일으켜 그를 응시했다. 과연 표트르 페트로비치의 전반적인 외양에는 무언가 독특한 데가 있었으니, 그토록 격의 없이 그에게 붙여진 '약혼자'라는 칭호를 정당화해 주는 듯한 무엇이었다. 우선, 표트르 페트로비치가 수도에서의 그 며칠을, 약혼녀를 맞이할 기대 속에 자신을 단장하고 차려입는 데 열심히 썼다는 것이 너무도, 실로 지나칠 만큼 분명했다. 사실 그 자체로는 전혀 흠잡을 데 없고 허용될 만한 처사였다. 자기 외모가 보기 좋게 나아진 데 대한, 어쩌면 지나치게 흡족해하는 자의식조차도, 표트르 페트로비치가 약혼자의 역할을 떠맡은 마당이니 이런 상황에서는 용서받을 만했다. 그의 옷은 모두 양복점에서 갓 나온 것으로 나무랄 데 없었지만, 다만 너무 새것이고 너무 노골적으로 용도에 들어맞는다는 게 흠이었다. 멋스러운 새 둥근 모자조차 같은 의미를 띠고 있었다. 표트르 페트로비치는 그 모자를 지나치게 정중하게 다루며 너무도 조심스럽게 손에 들고 있었다. 진짜 루뱅산인 그 고급스러운 라벤더색 장갑 한 켤레도 같은 이야기를 들려주었으니, 그가 그것을 끼지 않고 과시하듯 손에 들고만 있다는 사실만 보아도 그러했다. 표트르 페트로비치의 옷차림에는 밝고 젊은 색조가 두드러졌다. 그는 담황색의 멋스러운 여름 재킷에, 가볍고 얇은 바지를 입고, 같은 색 조끼를 받쳐 입었으며, 새것의 고운 셔츠에, 분홍색 줄무늬가 있는 더없이 가벼운 케임브릭 천 넥타이를 매고 있었다. 그리고 무엇보다 좋은 점은, 이 모든 게 표트르 페트로비치에게 잘 어울린다는 것이었다. 그의 매우 생기 있고 심지어 잘생기기까지 한 얼굴은 언제나 마흔다섯이라는 나이보다 젊어 보였다. 검은 구레나룻이 양 볼에 보기 좋은 테를 둘러, 윤기 흐르는 말끔히 면도한 턱 위로 무성하게 자라 있었다. 군데군데 희끗희끗하게 센 머리칼조차도, 미용사에게서 빗어 곱슬곱슬하게 다듬은 것이었지만, 보통 곱슬머리가 그러하듯 결혼식 날의 독일인을 떠올리게 하여 그를 멍청해 보이게 하지는 않았다. 만일 제법 잘생기고 위풍당당한 그의 얼굴에 정말로 불쾌하고 혐오스러운 무언가가 있었다면, 그것은 전혀 다른 원인에서 비롯된 것이었다. 격의 없이 루진 씨를 훑어본 뒤, 라스콜니코프는 짓궂게 웃고는 다시 베개에 몸을 묻고 아까처럼 천장을 응시했다.\n\n그러나 루진 씨는 마음을 다잡고, 그들의 괴상한 행동거지에는 일절 신경 쓰지 않기로 작정한 듯했다.\n\n\"이런 처지에 놓인 댁을 보게 되어 더없이 유감이오.\" 그는 애써 다시 침묵을 깨며 입을 열었다. \"댁이 앓고 있는 줄 알았더라면 더 일찍 왔을 거요. 하지만 일이라는 게 어떤 건지 댁도 알 거요. 게다가 나는 원로원에 걸린 아주 중요한 법적 소송도 있고, 댁도 능히 짐작할 다른 용무들도 있다는 건 말할 것도 없소. 댁의 어머님과 누이가 곧 도착하실 예정이오.\"\n\n라스콜니코프가 몸을 움직이며 무슨 말을 하려는 듯했다. 그의 얼굴에는 약간의 동요가 비쳤다. 표트르 페트로비치는 말을 멈추고 기다렸으나, 아무 말도 이어지지 않자 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"……곧 도착하실 거요. 그분들이 오시면 묵을 숙소도 마련해 두었소.\"\n\n\"어디에 말이오?\" 라스콜니코프가 힘없이 물었다.\n\n\"바로 이 근처, 바칼레예프의 집이오.\"\n\n\"보스크레센스키에 있는 곳이군요.\" 라주미힌이 끼어들었다. \"유신이라는 상인이 세놓는 방이 두 층 있는데, 나도 거기 가 본 적 있소.\"\n\n\"그래요, 방들이…….\"\n\n\"역겨운 곳이오. 더럽고 악취가 진동하는 데다, 게다가 평판도 수상쩍은 곳이지. 거기서 별일이 다 있었고, 온갖 괴상한 인간들이 살고 있소. 나도 어떤 추잡한 사건 때문에 거길 갔었지. 하긴 값은 싸지만…….\"\n\n\"나야 물론 그렇게까지 자세히는 알아볼 수 없었소. 나 역시 페테르부르크에는 낯선 사람이니까.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 못마땅한 듯 대꾸했다. \"어쨌든 그 두 방은 무척 깨끗하고, 또 아주 잠깐 머무를 거니까……. 나는 이미 영구적인, 그러니까 우리가 앞으로 살 집은 따로 구해 두었소.\" 그가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말했다. \"지금 손을 보게 하는 중이오. 그동안 나 자신은 친구 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프와 함께, 리페베흐젤 부인의 셋집에 비좁게 끼어 지내고 있소. 바칼레예프의 집을 일러 준 것도 그 사람이오…….\"\n\n\"레베쟈트니코프라고요?\" 라스콜니코프가 무언가를 떠올리는 듯 천천히 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프, 부처에 다니는 관리요. 그를 아시오?\"\n\n\"네…… 아니오.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다.\n\n\"미안하오, 댁이 물어보기에 아는 줄로 알았소. 한때 내가 그의 후견인이었지……. 아주 괜찮은 청년이고 진보적이오. 나는 젊은이들 만나는 걸 좋아하오. 그들에게서 새로운 것을 배우게 되니까.\" 루진이 기대에 차서 모두를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이오?\" 라주미힌이 물었다.\n\n\"가장 중대하고 본질적인 문제들에서 말이오.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 그 질문이 반갑기라도 한 듯 대답했다. \"보다시피 내가 페테르부르크에 들른 게 십 년 만이오. 온갖 새로운 것들, 개혁, 사상이 우리 지방까지도 다 전해지긴 했지만, 그 모든 걸 더 또렷이 보려면 페테르부르크에 있어야 하는 법이오. 그리고 내 생각에는, 젊은 세대를 지켜봄으로써 가장 많이 관찰하고 배울 수 있는 것 같소. 솔직히 고백하건대 나는 기쁘오…….\"\n\n\"무엇이 말이오?\"\n\n\"댁의 질문은 폭이 넓군. 내 착각일지도 모르지만, 내가 보기엔 더 명료한 견해, 말하자면 더 많은 비판, 더 많은 실천성을 발견하게 되는 것 같소…….\"\n\n\"그건 사실이지요.\" 조시모프가 한마디 흘렸다.\n\n\"헛소리! 실천성이라니 어디 있단 말이오.\" 라주미힌이 그에게 달려들었다. \"실천성이란 찾기 어려운 거요. 하늘에서 뚝 떨어지는 게 아니란 말이오. 지난 이백 년 동안 우리는 온갖 실천적 삶에서 동떨어져 있었소. 사상이라면, 좋소, 들끓고 있지.\" 그가 표트르 페트로비치에게 말했다. \"선을 향한 갈망도 존재하긴 하지만 유치한 형태일 뿐이고, 정직함도 찾을 수야 있지만 도둑놈들이 우글거리지. 어쨌거나 실천성이란 없소. 실천성은 제대로 된 신발을 신고 다니는 법이오.\"\n\n\"나는 댁의 의견에 동의하지 않소.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 눈에 띄게 즐거워하며 대꾸했다. \"물론 사람들이 도가 지나쳐 실수를 저지르기도 하지만, 너그럽게 보아야 하오. 그런 실수들은 그저 대의에 대한 열정과 비정상적인 외부 환경의 증거일 뿐이오. 이루어진 게 별로 없다면, 그건 시간이 짧았던 탓이지. 수단에 관해서는 말하지 않겠소. 알고 싶다면 말하건대, 이미 무언가는 이루어졌다는 게 내 개인적 견해요. 우리의 낡고 몽상적이고 낭만적인 작가들 대신에 새롭고 값진 사상, 새롭고 값진 저작들이 유통되고 있소. 문학은 더 성숙한 형태를 갖추어 가고, 해로운 편견들이 숱하게 뿌리 뽑혀 조롱거리가 되었지……. 한마디로, 우리는 과거와 돌이킬 수 없이 결별했고, 내 생각에 그건 대단한 일이오…….\"\n\n\"외워서 잘난 척하는군!\" 라스콜니코프가 갑자기 내뱉었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 표트르 페트로비치가 그 말을 알아듣지 못하고 물었다. 그러나 아무 대답도 돌아오지 않았다.\n\n\"다 옳은 말씀이오.\" 조시모프가 서둘러 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그렇지 않소?\" 표트르 페트로비치가 조시모프를 상냥하게 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다. \"인정해야 하오.\" 그가 약간의 승리감과 거만함을 띠고 라주미힌을 향해 말을 이었다. 하마터면 \"젊은이\"라는 말까지 덧붙일 뻔했다. \"과학과 경제적 진리의 이름으로 진전, 아니 요즘 말로 하면 진보가 있다는 것을…….\"\n\n\"진부한 소리요.\"\n\n\"아니, 진부한 소리가 아니오! 이를테면 지금까지는 '네 이웃을 사랑하라'는 말을 들으면, 그게 어떤 결과를 가져왔소?\" 표트르 페트로비치가, 어쩌면 지나치게 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"내가 내 외투를 반으로 찢어 이웃과 나눠 입고, 우리 둘 다 반쯤 헐벗은 채로 남는 거였소. 러시아 속담에도 있듯이, '여러 마리 토끼를 쫓다가는 한 마리도 못 잡는다'는 거지. 이제 과학은 우리에게 이렇게 말하오. 모든 사람보다 먼저 너 자신을 사랑하라, 세상의 모든 것은 사리사욕에 기초해 있으니. 자기 자신을 사랑하고 자기 일을 제대로 꾸려 나가면 외투는 멀쩡히 남는다는 거지. 경제적 진리는 여기에 덧붙이오. 사회에서 사적인 일들이 잘 조직될수록 — 말하자면 멀쩡한 외투가 많을수록 — 그 토대는 더 견고해지고 공공의 복리도 더 잘 조직된다고. 따라서 오로지 나만을 위해 부를 쌓을 때, 나는 말하자면 모두를 위해 쌓는 셈이며, 내 이웃이 찢어진 외투보다는 조금 더 나은 걸 얻게 되도록 돕는 셈이오. 그것도 사적이고 개인적인 자선에서가 아니라, 전반적인 진보의 결과로서 말이오. 단순한 생각이지만, 불행히도 이상주의와 감상주의에 가로막혀 우리에게 닿기까지 오랜 시간이 걸렸소. 게다가 그걸 알아차리는 데는 머리가 거의 필요 없을 것 같은데도 말이오…….\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 나는 머리가 영 없는 사람이오.\" 라주미힌이 날카롭게 말을 잘랐다. \"그러니 그 얘긴 그만둡시다. 나도 어떤 목적이 있어서 이 논쟁을 시작한 거지만, 지난 삼 년 동안 이렇게 자기 좋자고 지껄여 대는 것, 늘 똑같은 이 진부한 소리의 끝없는 흐름에 어찌나 신물이 났는지, 맹세코 다른 사람들이 그렇게 떠들어도 내가 다 낯이 뜨거울 지경이오. 댁은 분명 자기 학식을 뽐내고 싶어 안달일 테고, 나도 그걸 탓하진 않소, 충분히 용서할 만한 일이니까. 나는 그저 댁이 어떤 사람인지 알아보고 싶었을 뿐이오. 요즘 들어 양심 없는 인간들이 어찌나 진보의 대의를 거머쥐고서, 손대는 것마다 제 잇속에 맞게 왜곡해 놓는지, 그 대의 전체가 진흙탕에 끌려 들어가고 말았단 말이오. 그만하면 됐소!\"\n\n\"미안하오만, 선생.\" 루진이 모욕을 느끼고 지나치게 위엄을 부리며 말했다. \"이렇게 격의 없이, 나까지도 그렇다고 넌지시 말씀하시는 거요…….\"\n\n\"오, 이런, 선생……. 내가 어찌 감히?……. 자, 그만합시다.\" 라주미힌은 그렇게 말을 맺고는 홱 조시모프 쪽으로 돌아서서 아까 나누던 대화를 이어 갔다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 그 부인을 받아들일 만한 분별은 있었다. 그는 일 이 분 안에 자리를 뜨기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"우리의 교분이,\" 그가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말했다. \"댁이 회복되고 나면, 또 댁도 알고 있는 사정들에 비추어, 더 가까워지기를 바라오……. 무엇보다도, 댁이 건강을 되찾기를 바라오…….\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 고개조차 돌리지 않았다. 표트르 페트로비치는 의자에서 일어서기 시작했다.\n\n\"그 노파의 고객 중 누군가가 죽인 게 틀림없소.\" 조시모프가 단정 짓듯 말했다.\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없지.\" 라주미힌이 대답했다. \"포르피리는 자기 의견을 밝히지 않지만, 거기에 저당물을 맡긴 사람들을 모조리 조사하고 있소.\"\n\n\"조사를 한다고?\" 라스콜니코프가 큰 소리로 물었다.\n\n\"그래. 그게 어쨌다고?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니야.\"\n\n\"그가 어떻게 그들을 찾아내는데?\" 조시모프가 물었다.\n\n\"코흐가 그중 몇 사람의 이름을 댔고, 다른 이름들은 저당물 포장지에 적혀 있고, 또 몇몇은 제 발로 나섰다더군.\"\n\n\"교활하고 노련한 악당이 틀림없소! 그 대담함이라니! 그 침착함이라니!\"\n\n\"바로 그게 아니란 말이오!\" 라주미힌이 끼어들었다. \"다들 그것 때문에 헛다리를 짚고 있는 거요. 하지만 내 단언컨대 그자는 교활하지도 노련하지도 않고, 아마 이게 첫 범행이었을 거요! 계산된 범죄에 교활한 범인이라는 가정은 들어맞지 않소. 그자가 미숙했다고 쳐 보시오, 그럼 오로지 우연만이 그를 구해 줬다는 게 분명해지오 — 그리고 우연이란 무슨 일이든 다 해낼 수 있는 거지. 아니, 그자는 어쩌면 장애물조차 예상하지 못했을 거요! 그런데 일은 어떻게 시작했나? 십 루블, 이십 루블어치 보석을 집어 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고, 노파의 궤짝이며 누더기를 마구 뒤졌는데 — 정작 사람들이 서랍장 맨 위 칸 상자 안에서 어음 말고도 천오백 루블이나 찾아냈단 말이오! 그자는 훔칠 줄을 몰랐던 거요. 죽일 줄만 알았던 거지. 그게 첫 범행이었소, 내 장담하건대, 첫 범행이었고, 그자는 정신을 놓았던 거요. 그러니 머리를 잘 써서가 아니라 운이 좋아서 빠져나간 거란 말이오!\"\n\n\"노파, 그 전당포 노파의 살인 사건을 말하는 것 같은데, 맞소?\" 표트르 페트로비치가 조시모프를 향해 끼어들었다. 그는 모자와 장갑을 손에 든 채 서 있었지만, 떠나기에 앞서 지적인 말을 몇 마디 더 던지고 싶은 마음이 들었던 것이다. 그는 분명 좋은 인상을 남기고 싶어 안달이었고, 그의 허영심이 신중함을 이기고 있었다.\n\n\"그렇소. 그 얘길 들으셨소?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 근처에 있다 보니.\"\n\n\"자세한 내막을 아시오?\"\n\n\"그렇다고는 못 하겠소. 하지만 이 사건에서 나의 흥미를 끄는 건 다른 점이오 — 말하자면 문제 전체란 말이오. 지난 오 년 동안 하층 계급에서 범죄가 크게 늘었다는 사실은 차치하고, 도처에서 일어나는 강도와 방화 사건들도 차치하더라도, 내가 보기에 가장 기이한 점은, 상류 계급에서도 마찬가지로 범죄가 비례해서 늘고 있다는 것이오. 어느 곳에서는 대학생이 큰길에서 우편 마차를 털었다는 소리가 들리고, 또 어느 곳에서는 사회적 지위가 번듯한 사람들이 위조지폐를 만들고, 최근 모스크바에서는 복권을 위조하던 일당이 통째로 붙잡혔는데 그 주모자 중 하나가 세계사 강사였다더군. 그런가 하면 외국에 나가 있던 우리 서기관이 무슨 모호한 사리사욕 때문에 살해당했고……. 그리고 이 노파, 이 전당포 주인이 사회 상류층의 누군가에게 살해당한 거라면 — 농민들은 금붙이를 저당 잡히지 않으니까 — 우리 사회의 문명화된 부분의 이 도덕적 타락을 대체 어떻게 설명해야 하겠소?\"\n\n\"경제적 변화가 많지요.\" 조시모프가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 설명하느냐고요?\" 라주미힌이 그 말을 받아챘다. \"그건 우리의 고질적인 무실천성으로 설명할 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"모스크바의 그 강사가 왜 지폐를 위조했느냐는 물음에 뭐라고 대답했소? '다들 이런저런 방법으로 부자가 되고 있으니, 나도 어서 부자가 되고 싶었다'는 거였소. 정확한 말은 기억나지 않지만, 요지는 그자가 기다리지도 일하지도 않고 거저 돈을 원했다는 거요! 우리는 만사를 다 차려진 채로 받는 데, 목발을 짚고 걷는 데, 음식까지 씹어서 떠먹여 주는 데 익숙해져 버렸소. 그러다 그 위대한 시간이 닥치니, 너 나 할 것 없이 제 본색을 드러낸 거지.\"\n\n* 1861년 농노 해방을 가리킨다. ─옮긴이 주.\n\n\"하지만 도덕은? 또 말하자면, 원칙은…….\"\n\n\"그런데 댁은 왜 그런 걸 걱정하시오?\" 라스콜니코프가 갑자기 끼어들었다. \"그건 당신 이론에 들어맞는 거잖소!\"\n\n\"내 이론에 들어맞는다고?\"\n\n\"아니, 방금 당신이 주장하던 그 이론을 논리적으로 끝까지 밀고 나가면, 사람을 죽여도 된다는 결론이 나오잖소…….\"\n\n\"세상에 이럴 수가!\" 루진이 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 그렇지 않소.\" 조시모프가 끼어들었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 윗입술을 실룩이며 괴로운 듯 숨을 쉬고 누워 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일에든 정도라는 게 있는 법이오.\" 루진이 거만하게 말을 이었다. \"경제 사상이 살인을 부추기는 게 아니고, 다만 가정해 보면…….\"\n\n\"그리고 그게 사실이오?\" 라스콜니코프가 또다시 갑자기 끼어들었는데, 이번에도 분노와 그를 모욕하는 데서 오는 쾌감으로 떨리는 목소리였다. \"당신이 약혼녀에게…… 그녀가 청혼을 받아들인 지 한 시간도 안 돼서, 당신을 가장 기쁘게 한 게…… 그녀가 거지라는 사실이었다고 말한 게 사실이오? 가난에서 아내를 끌어올리는 편이 더 낫기 때문에, 그래야 그녀를 완전히 손아귀에 넣고, 자기가 은인이라는 걸 빌미로 그녀를 책망할 수 있으니까 말이오?\"\n\n\"세상에 이럴 수가.\" 루진이 당혹감에 새빨개진 얼굴로 분노하여 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"내 말을 이런 식으로 왜곡하다니! 미안하오만, 분명히 말해 두겠소. 댁의 귀에 들어간, 아니 차라리 누가 댁에게 전한 그 이야기는 사실에 아무런 근거도 없으며, 나는…… 누군지 짐작이 가오만…… 한마디로…… 이 화살은…… 한마디로, 댁의 어머님이…… 그분은 다른 일들에서도, 그 온갖 훌륭한 자질에도 불구하고, 다소 과장되고 낭만적인 사고방식을 지니신 듯 보였소……. 하지만 그분이 일을 이토록 공상적으로 오해하고 잘못 전하시리라고는 천 리 밖에서도 짐작 못 했소……. 더구나…… 더구나…….\"\n\n\"내 말해 두겠는데,\" 라스콜니코프가 베개에서 몸을 일으켜 날카롭게 번뜩이는 눈을 그에게 못 박으며 외쳤다. \"내 말해 두겠는데.\"\n\n\"뭐를 말이오?\" 루진이 도전적이고 모욕당한 얼굴로 기다리며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 침묵이 몇 초간 이어졌다.\n\n\"앞으로 또 한 번이라도…… 감히 단 한 마디라도…… 내 어머니에 관해 입에 올린다면…… 당신을 계단 아래로 날려 버리겠소!\"\n\n\"아니, 자네 왜 그러나?\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 이런 식이란 말이오?\" 루진이 새파랗게 질려 입술을 깨물었다. \"내 말해 두겠소, 선생.\" 그가 안간힘을 다해 자제하면서도 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬며 또박또박 말하기 시작했다. \"처음 댁을 본 순간부터 댁이 내게 적의를 품고 있다는 걸 알았지만, 나는 좀 더 알아보려고 일부러 여기 남아 있었던 거요. 병든 사람에다 인척이 될 사람이니 많은 것을 용서할 수 있었소만, 댁은…… 이 일이 있은 뒤로는 결코…….\"\n\n\"나는 병들지 않았소!\" 라스콜니코프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그렇다면 더 나쁘군…….\"\n\n\"썩 꺼지시오!\"\n\n그러나 루진은 이미 말을 끝맺지도 않고 탁자와 의자 사이를 비집으며 나가고 있었다. 이번에는 라주미힌이 그가 지나가도록 일어섰다. 그는 누구에게도 눈길 한번 주지 않고, 한동안 환자를 내버려 두라고 신호를 보내던 조시모프에게 고갯짓조차 하지 않은 채, 문을 나서며 몸을 숙일 때 모자가 찌부러지지 않도록 모자를 어깨 높이까지 들어 올리고는 밖으로 나갔다. 그리고 굽은 등의 곡선마저 그가 받은 끔찍한 모욕을 고스란히 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"자네 어떻게 그럴 수가 — 어떻게 그럴 수가 있나!\" 라주미힌이 어리둥절하여 고개를 내저으며 말했다.\n\n\"날 내버려 둬 — 다들 날 내버려 두란 말이야!\" 라스콜니코프가 미친 듯이 외쳤다. \"언제까지 날 못살게 굴 거야? 난 자네들이 무섭지 않아! 이제 난 누구도, 누구도 무섭지 않다고! 다들 내게서 떨어져! 혼자 있고 싶어, 혼자, 혼자!\"\n\n\"가세.\" 조시모프가 라주미힌에게 고갯짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이대로 내버려 둘 순 없잖나!\"\n\n\"가세.\" 조시모프가 다그치듯 거듭 말하고는 밖으로 나갔다. 라주미힌은 잠시 생각하다가 그를 따라잡으려고 뛰어나갔다.\n\n\"저 친구 말을 안 듣는 게 더 나쁠 수도 있네.\" 조시모프가 계단에서 말했다. \"자극하면 안 돼.\"\n\n\"대체 저 친구한테 무슨 일이 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"무언가 좋은 충격이라도 받을 수 있다면, 바로 그게 효험이 있을 텐데! 처음엔 좀 나아지더니만……. 자네도 알다시피 저 친구 마음에 뭔가 걸린 게 있어! 무슨 고정관념이 그를 짓누르고 있는 거지……. 정말 그런 것 같아 몹시 걱정스럽네. 틀림없이 그래!\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그 신사, 표트르 페트로비치 때문인지도 모르지. 그 사람 얘기로 미루어 보면 로쟈의 누이와 결혼하려는 모양이고, 로쟈가 앓아눕기 바로 직전에 그 일에 관한 편지를 받았던 것 같던데…….\"\n\n\"그래, 그 작자가 망쳐 놓은 거지! 일을 통째로 그르쳤을지도 몰라. 그런데 자네 눈치챘나, 저 친구는 무엇에도 관심이 없고 무엇에도 반응하지 않는데, 딱 한 가지, 흥분하는 듯한 점이 있단 말이지 — 바로 그 살인 사건이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 라주미힌이 동의했다. \"나도 그건 눈치챘네. 관심을 보이고, 또 겁을 먹고 있어. 경찰서에서 앓아누웠던 그날도 그것 때문에 충격을 받았던 거지. 기절까지 했으니까.\"\n\n\"그 얘기를 오늘 저녁에 좀 더 자세히 해 주게, 그럼 나중에 내가 자네한테 들려줄 게 있네. 저 친구한테 무척 흥미가 동하는군! 삼십 분 뒤에 다시 가서 들여다보겠네……. 하긴 염증은 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"고맙네! 그동안 나는 파셴카하고 같이 기다리면서 나스타시야를 통해 저 친구를 지켜보겠네…….\"\n\n홀로 남겨진 라스콜니코프는 초조하고 비참한 심정으로 나스타시야를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 여전히 우물쭈물 머물러 있었다.\n\n\"이제 차 좀 드시겠어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"나중에! 졸려. 날 내버려 둬.\"\n\n그는 홱 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 나스타시야는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n그러나 그녀가 나가자마자, 그는 일어나 문에 빗장을 걸고, 라주미힌이 그날 저녁 가져와 다시 묶어 두었던 보따리를 풀고 옷을 입기 시작했다. 이상한 일이지만, 그는 순식간에 완전히 평온해진 것 같았다. 조금 전의 헛소리도, 최근 그를 따라다니던 공황의 공포도 흔적조차 남지 않았다. 그것은 어떤 기묘하고 돌연한 평온의 첫 순간이었다. 그의 동작은 정확하고 분명했으며, 굳은 결의가 거기서 엿보였다. \"오늘이야, 오늘.\" 그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. 그는 자신이 여전히 쇠약하다는 것을 알고 있었지만, 격렬한 정신적 집중이 그에게 힘과 자신감을 주었다. 더욱이 그는 길거리에서 쓰러지지 않으리라 기대했다. 완전히 새 옷을 입고 나서, 그는 탁자 위에 놓인 돈을 바라보다가, 잠시 생각한 뒤 그것을 주머니에 넣었다. 이십오 루블이었다. 그는 라주미힌이 옷에 쓴 십 루블에서 남은 동전 잔돈도 모두 챙겼다. 그런 다음 살그머니 문빗장을 풀고 나가, 계단을 미끄러지듯 내려가 열린 부엌문 안을 들여다보았다. 나스타시야가 등을 돌린 채 서서 집주인 여자의 사모바르에 불을 지피고 있었다. 그녀는 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다. 사실 그가 밖으로 나가리라고 누가 꿈엔들 생각했겠는가? 잠시 후 그는 거리에 나와 있었다.\n\n여덟 시가 다 되어 가고, 해가 지고 있었다. 전과 다름없이 숨이 막혔지만, 그는 악취 나고 먼지투성이인 도시의 공기를 게걸스럽게 들이마셨다. 머리가 다소 어지러웠다. 일종의 야성적인 에너지가 그의 열에 들뜬 눈과 수척하고 창백하고 누런 얼굴에서 별안간 번뜩였다. 그는 자신이 어디로 가는지 알지도 못했고 생각하지도 않았다. 그에게는 단 하나의 생각뿐이었다. \"이 모든 것을 오늘, 단번에, 당장 끝내야 한다, 그러기 전에는 집으로 돌아가지 않으리라, 왜냐하면 그렇게 더는 살아갈 수 없으니까.\" 어떻게, 무엇으로 끝장을 낸단 말인가? 그는 그에 대해 아무런 생각도 없었고, 생각하고 싶지도 않았다. 그는 생각을 떨쳐 냈다. 생각이 그를 괴롭혔다. 그가 아는 것, 그가 느끼는 것은 단지 모든 것이 \"어떻게든\" 바뀌어야 한다는 것뿐이었다. 그는 절망적이면서도 흔들림 없는 자신감과 결의로 그렇게 되뇌었다.\n\n오랜 습관대로 그는 늘 가던 대로 건초 시장 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다. 검은 머리의 젊은 사내가 손풍금을 들고 작은 잡화점 앞 길거리에 서서 몹시 감상적인 노래를 켜고 있었다. 그는 자기 앞 보도에 선 열다섯 살쯤 된 처녀의 노래에 반주를 넣어 주고 있었다. 처녀는 크리놀린에 망토를 걸치고 불꽃 빛깔의 깃털이 꽂힌 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 죄다 무척 낡고 초라했다. 거리에서 노래하느라 갈라지고 거칠어졌지만 힘 있고 제법 듣기 좋은 목소리로, 처녀는 가게에서 동전 한 닢이라도 받을까 하는 기대에 노래를 불렀다. 라스콜니코프는 두세 명의 구경꾼 틈에 끼어, 오 코페이카짜리 동전을 꺼내 처녀의 손에 쥐여 주었다. 처녀는 감상적인 고음에서 뚝 노래를 끊더니, 손풍금쟁이에게 날카롭게 \"가자\" 하고 외쳤고, 둘은 다음 가게로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"거리의 음악을 좋아하십니까?\" 라스콜니코프가 곁에 멀거니 서 있던 중년 사내에게 말을 걸었다. 사내는 깜짝 놀라 의아한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저는 손풍금에 맞춰 부르는 노래를 듣는 게 좋아요.\" 라스콜니코프는 말했는데, 그의 태도는 화제와는 이상하게 어울리지 않는 듯했다. \"춥고 어둡고 축축한 가을 저녁에 듣는 게 좋지요 — 반드시 축축해야 합니다 — 지나가는 사람들 얼굴이 죄다 파리하고 병색이 짙을 때, 아니면 더 좋게는 바람 한 점 없이 진눈깨비가 곧장 내리쏟아질 때 말이지요 — 무슨 말인지 아시겠어요? — 그리고 가로등이 그 사이로 비칠 때…….\"\n\n\"모르겠는데요……. 실례합니다…….\" 낯선 사내는 그 질문과 라스콜니코프의 기이한 태도에 겁을 먹고 중얼거리더니, 길 건너편으로 가 버렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 곧장 걸어가 건초 시장 모퉁이로 나왔는데, 바로 그곳에서 행상과 그 아내가 리자베타와 이야기를 나누었던 것이다. 하지만 지금 그들은 거기 없었다. 그 장소를 알아본 그는 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보다가, 곡물상 가게 앞에서 입을 헤벌리고 서 있던 붉은 셔츠 차림의 젊은이에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"이 모퉁이에서 아내랑 같이 노점을 하는 사람이 있지 않나?\"\n\n\"여기선 별의별 사람이 다 노점을 합니다요.\" 젊은이가 라스콜니코프를 깔보듯 흘끗 보며 대답했다.\n\n\"그 사람 이름이 뭐지?\"\n\n\"세례받은 이름이지요.\"\n\n\"자네도 자라이스크 사람 아닌가? 어느 현(縣) 출신이지?\"\n\n젊은이는 다시 라스콜니코프를 바라보았다.\n\n\"현이 아니라 군(郡)입죠, 나리. 너그러이 용서하십쇼, 나리!\"\n\n\"저기 위쪽에 있는 게 선술집인가?\"\n\n\"예, 음식점인데 당구장도 있고, 공주님들도 거기서 만나실 수 있습죠……. 랄라!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 광장을 가로질렀다. 그 모퉁이에는 농부들이 빽빽이 몰려 있었다. 그는 사람들 얼굴을 살피며 그 가장 빽빽한 무리 속으로 비집고 들어갔다. 그는 까닭 모를 충동에 사람들과 이야기를 나누고 싶어졌다. 하지만 농부들은 그에게 아무 관심도 두지 않았다. 그들은 무리 지어 다 같이 떠들어 대고 있었다. 그는 잠시 서서 생각하다가, 오른쪽으로 꺾어 V 거리 쪽으로 발길을 옮겼다.\n\n그는 시장 광장에서 사도바야 거리로 비스듬히 꺾어지며 이어지는 그 작은 골목을 자주 지나다녔다. 최근 들어 그는 우울할 때면 더욱 우울해지려고 이 동네를 자주 배회하고 싶은 충동을 느끼곤 했다.\n\n지금 그는 아무 생각 없이 걸어가고 있었다. 그곳에는 거대한 건물 한 채가 있었는데, 통째로 술집과 음식점에 세 놓여 있었다. 여자들이 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않고 실내복 차림으로 끊임없이 들락날락했다. 여기저기 보도 위에, 특히 아래층의 갖가지 유흥업소 입구 근처에 그들이 무리 지어 모여 있었다. 그중 한 곳에서 요란한 소음과 노랫소리, 기타의 딩동거림, 흥겨운 고함이 거리로 흘러나왔다. 한 무리의 여자들이 문간을 둘러싸고 북적였다. 더러는 계단에, 더러는 보도에 앉아 있었고, 더러는 선 채로 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 술 취한 군인 하나가 담배를 피우며 욕설을 퍼부으면서 그들 근처 길을 어슬렁거렸는데, 어딘가로 가는 길을 찾으려는 듯하면서도 그곳이 어딘지 잊어버린 것 같았다. 거지 하나가 또 다른 거지와 다투고 있었고, 곤드레만드레 취한 사내 하나가 길 한복판에 가로로 뻗어 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 쉰 목소리로 떠들어 대는 여자들 무리에 끼어들었다. 그들은 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않고 무명옷에 염소 가죽 신발을 신고 있었다. 마흔 살쯤 된 여자도, 열일곱밖에 안 돼 보이는 여자도 있었는데, 거의 모두가 눈이 시퍼렇게 멍들어 있었다.\n\n그는 아래쪽 술집의 노랫소리와 온갖 소음, 떠들썩한 소동에 이상하게 끌렸다……. 안에서 누군가가 기타 소리와, 경쾌한 곡조를 부르는 가느다란 가성에 맞춰 발뒤꿈치로 박자를 맞추며 미친 듯이 춤추는 소리가 들렸다. 그는 입구에 몸을 숙이고 보도에서 호기심 어린 눈으로 안을 들여다보며, 음울하고 몽롱하게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"오, 나의 잘생긴 병사여 까닭 없이 날 때리지 말아요,\"\n\n가수의 가느다란 목소리가 떨리듯 흘러나왔다. 라스콜니코프는 마치 모든 것이 거기에 달려 있기라도 한 듯, 그가 무슨 노래를 부르는지 알아내고 싶은 강렬한 욕망에 사로잡혔다.\n\n'들어가 볼까?' 그는 생각했다. '저들은 웃고 있군. 술 때문이지. 나도 취해 볼까?'\n\n\"안 들어오시겠어요?\" 여자들 중 하나가 그에게 물었다. 그녀의 목소리는 아직 곱고 다른 여자들보다 덜 탁했다. 그녀는 젊고 혐오스럽지 않았다 — 그 무리에서 유일하게.\n\n\"아니, 예쁘네요.\" 그는 몸을 일으키며 그녀를 바라보고 말했다.\n\n그녀는 그 칭찬에 무척 기뻐하며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"손님도 아주 잘생기셨는걸요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"근데 어쩜 저렇게 비쩍 말랐담!\" 다른 여자가 굵은 저음으로 한마디 했다. \"병원에서 막 나왔수?\"\n\n\"보아하니 죄다 장군님 따님들인 모양인데, 하나같이 들창코구먼.\" 헐렁한 외투를 걸친 거나하게 취한 농부가 얼굴에 능청맞은 미소를 띠며 끼어들었다. \"거참 흥겹기도 하지.\"\n\n\"썩 꺼져!\"\n\n\"가지, 귀여운 것!\"\n\n그러더니 그는 아래쪽 술집으로 쏜살같이 뛰어들었다. 라스콜니코프는 발길을 옮겼다.\n\n\"저기요, 손님.\" 처녀가 그의 뒤에다 대고 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐요?\"\n\n그녀는 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"언제든 손님이랑 한 시간 즐겁게 보내 드릴게요, 친절한 양반. 근데 지금은 좀 부끄러워서요. 한잔 마시게 육 코페이카만 주세요, 착한 도련님!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 손에 먼저 잡히는 대로 — 십오 코페이카를 주었다.\n\n\"어머, 정말 마음씨 고운 양반이네!\"\n\n\"이름이 뭐요?\"\n\n\"두클리다라고 찾으세요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 너무하잖아.\" 여자들 중 하나가 두클리다를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 한마디 했다. \"어떻게 그렇게 손을 벌릴 수 있담. 나 같으면 창피해서 쓰러질 텐데…….\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 호기심 어린 눈으로 그 말을 한 여자를 바라보았다. 그녀는 서른 살쯤 된, 얼굴이 곰보투성이에 온몸이 멍투성이고 윗입술이 부어오른 계집이었다. 그녀는 조용하고 진지하게 핀잔을 주었다. '어디서였더라.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. '어디서 읽었더라, 사형을 선고받은 누군가가 죽기 한 시간 전에 이렇게 말하거나 생각했다는 것을. 만일 어느 높은 바위 위, 겨우 발 디딜 만큼 좁은 턱 위에서 살아야 한다 해도, 그리고 사방에 대양과 영원한 어둠, 영원한 고독, 영원한 폭풍이 둘러싸고 있다 해도, 평생을, 천 년을, 영원토록 그 한 평짜리 공간에 서 있어야 한다 해도, 당장 죽는 것보다는 그렇게라도 사는 게 낫다고! 오직 사는 것, 살고 또 사는 것! 그것이 어떤 삶이든 간에!……. 이 얼마나 진실한가! 오 하느님, 얼마나 진실한가! 인간이란 비열한 피조물이다!……. 그리고 그런 이유로 인간을 비열하다 부르는 자 또한 비열하다.' 그는 잠시 후 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n그는 다른 거리로 들어섰다. '아하, 팔레 드 크리스탈*! 라주미힌이 방금 팔레 드 크리스탈 얘기를 했었지. 그런데 내가 도대체 뭘 원했더라? 그래, 신문이지……. 조시모프가 신문에서 읽었다고 했어. 신문 있나요?' 그는 여러 개의 방으로 이루어진, 무척 널찍하고 정말이지 깨끗한 음식점으로 들어가며 물었는데, 방들은 그래도 다소 비어 있었다. 두세 사람이 차를 마시고 있었고, 더 안쪽 방에는 네 명의 사내가 앉아 샴페인을 마시고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그중 하나가 자메토프인 것 같았으나, 그 거리에서는 확신할 수 없었다. '그래 본들 어떤가?' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"보드카 드시겠습니까?\" 종업원이 물었다.\n\n\"차를 주게, 그리고 신문을 가져다줘, 지난 닷새치 묵은 걸로, 그러면 내 뭔가 주지.\"\n\n\"예, 손님, 여기 오늘 신문입니다. 보드카는 안 하시고요?\"\n\n묵은 신문과 차가 나왔다. 라스콜니코프는 자리에 앉아 그것들을 훑어보기 시작했다.\n\n'오, 제기랄……. 이게 사건 기사로군. 계단에서의 사고, 술 때문에 가게 주인이 저절로 불타 죽음, 페스키에서의 화재……, 페테르부르크 지구의 화재……, 또 페테르부르크 지구의 화재……, 또 페테르부르크 지구의 화재……. 아, 여기 있군!' 그는 마침내 찾던 것을 발견하고 읽기 시작했다. 글자들이 눈앞에서 춤을 췄지만, 그는 끝까지 다 읽고는 이어지는 신문들에서 그 후의 속보를 게걸스럽게 찾기 시작했다. 신문장을 넘기는 그의 손이 초조한 신경질로 떨렸다. 갑자기 누군가가 그의 탁자 옆에 와 앉았다. 그가 고개를 들어 보니, 그 서기장 자메토프였다. 손가락에 반지를 끼고 시곗줄을 늘어뜨린 채, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 가르마 타서 포마드를 바르고, 맵시 있는 조끼에 다소 낡은 외투, 미심쩍은 셔츠를 걸친 여전한 모습이었다. 그는 기분이 좋았다. 적어도 무척 명랑하고 유쾌하게 웃고 있었다. 마신 샴페인 탓에 그의 가무잡잡한 얼굴이 다소 발그레했다.\n\n\"아니, 여긴 웬일이오?\" 그는 마치 평생 알고 지낸 사이인 양 말을 걸며 놀라서 입을 열었다. \"라주미힌이 바로 어제 당신이 의식이 없다고 그러던데. 참 희한한 일이군! 그리고 말이오, 내가 당신을 보러 갔던 거 아시오?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 그가 다가오리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 신문을 옆으로 치우고 자메토프 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 그의 입가에 미소가 떠올랐고, 그 미소에는 짜증스러운 초조함의 새로운 기색이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"왔다는 거 압니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"들었어요. 내 양말을 찾았다지요……. 그리고 라주미힌이 당신한테 홀딱 반했다는 것도 아시오? 그가 그러더군요, 당신이 같이 루이자 이바노브나 집에 갔었다고 — 왜, 당신이 도와주려고 했던 그 여자 말이오, 그 여자 때문에 당신이 그 화약 같은 중위한테 눈짓을 했는데 그가 알아채지 못했다지요. 기억나오? 어떻게 그가 못 알아챌 수가 있나요 — 아주 분명했는데, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그 양반 정말 다혈질이라니까!\"\n\n\"그 화약 같은 양반 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니, 당신 친구 라주미힌 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 즐겁게 사는군요, 자메토프 씨. 가장 기분 좋은 곳에 공짜로 드나드니 말이오. 방금 누가 당신한테 샴페인을 부어 줬소?\"\n\n\"우린 그냥…… 같이 한잔하고 있었던 거요……. 부어 줬다니, 무슨 말씀을!\"\n\n\"보수 삼아 받았겠지! 당신은 매사에서 이득을 보는구먼!\" 라스콜니코프가 웃었다. \"괜찮소, 이 친구야.\" 그는 자메토프의 어깨를 툭 치며 덧붙였다. \"악의가 있어 하는 말이 아니라, 친근하게, 장난삼아 하는 거요, 그 노파 사건에서 당신네 직공이 드미트리랑 드잡이하면서 말한 것처럼 말이지…….\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아시오?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 당신보다 내가 더 잘 알고 있을지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"당신 참 이상하군……. 분명 아직 몸이 많이 안 좋은 게요. 나오지 말았어야 했는데.\"\n\n\"오, 내가 당신한테 이상해 보이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 뭘 하고 있는 거요, 신문 읽는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\"화재 기사가 많지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 난 화재 기사를 읽는 게 아니오.\" 여기서 그는 의미심장하게 자메토프를 바라보았다. 그의 입술이 다시 조롱하는 미소로 비틀렸다. \"아니, 난 화재 기사를 읽는 게 아니오.\" 그는 자메토프에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말을 이었다. \"그런데 솔직히 말해 보시오, 이 친구야, 당신 내가 뭘 읽는지 끔찍이도 알고 싶지 않소?\"\n\n\"전혀 아니오. 질문 하나 못 하오? 왜 자꾸……?\"\n\n\"이봐요, 당신은 교양 있고 학식 있는 사람이지요?\"\n\n\"김나지움 육 학년까지 다녔소.\" 자메토프가 다소 위엄을 부리며 말했다.\n\n\"육 학년이라! 아, 이 참새 같은 양반아! 그 가르마에 반지까지 — 당신은 팔자 좋은 신사로구먼. 이런! 정말 매력적인 도련님이야!\" 여기서 라스콜니코프는 자메토프의 얼굴에 대고 신경질적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 상대는 모욕감보다 놀라움이 더해 몸을 뒤로 물렸다.\n\n\"어휴! 당신 정말 이상하군!\" 자메토프가 무척 진지하게 되풀이했다. \"아직도 헛소리를 하는 것 같다는 생각이 자꾸 드는구려.\"\n\n\"내가 헛소리를 한다고? 거짓말 말아요, 이 참새 양반아! 그래, 내가 이상하다고? 내가 흥미롭소, 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 흥미롭소.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 읽고 있었는지, 뭘 찾고 있었는지 말해 줄까요? 보시오, 내가 얼마나 많은 신문을 가져다 달라고 했는지. 수상하지, 응?\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"귀가 솔깃해지지?\"\n\n\"'귀가 솔깃해진다'니, 무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"그건 나중에 설명하리다, 한데 지금은, 이 친구야, 당신한테 분명히 말하겠소……. 아니, '고백하겠소'가 낫겠군……. 아니, 그것도 맞지 않아. '내가 진술할 테니 당신이 받아 적으시오.' 나는 진술하노니, 내가 읽고 있었던 것은, 내가 보고 찾고 있었던 것은…….\" 그는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 잠시 멈추었다. \"내가 찾고 있었던 것은 — 그리고 일부러 그러려고 여기 온 것은 — 그 늙은 전당포 노파를 살해한 사건에 관한 소식이었소.\" 그는 마침내, 거의 속삭이듯 또박또박 말하며, 자기 얼굴을 자메토프의 얼굴에 바싹 갖다 댔다. 자메토프는 움직이지도, 얼굴을 떼지도 않은 채 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 나중에 자메토프에게 이 모든 것 중 가장 기이하게 느껴진 것은, 정확히 일 분 동안 침묵이 이어졌고, 그 내내 둘이 서로를 응시했다는 점이었다.\n\n\"당신이 그걸 읽고 있었던들 어떻단 말이오?\" 그가 당혹스럽고 초조한 듯 마침내 외쳤다. \"그게 나랑 무슨 상관이오! 그래서 뭐 어쨌다는 거요?\"\n\n\"바로 그 노파 말이오.\" 라스콜니코프는 자메토프의 해명에 아랑곳 않고 여전히 속삭이며 말을 이었다. \"당신이 경찰서에서 얘기했던, 내가 기절했을 때 말이오, 기억하시오? 자, 이제 알겠소?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이오? 알다니…… 뭘 말이오?\" 자메토프가 거의 겁에 질려 내뱉었다.\n\n라스콜니코프의 굳고 진지하던 얼굴이 별안간 일변하더니, 그는 마치 도저히 자제할 수 없다는 듯 갑자기 아까와 똑같은 신경질적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 한순간, 그는 가까운 과거의 어느 순간을 비상하리만큼 생생한 감각으로 떠올렸다. 그가 도끼를 들고 문 뒤에 서 있는 동안 빗장이 덜컹거리고 밖에 있던 사내들이 욕설을 퍼부으며 문을 흔들어 대던 그 순간, 그때 그는 별안간 그들에게 소리치고, 욕하고, 혀를 내밀고, 비웃고, 웃고, 웃고, 또 웃고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔던 것이다!\n\n\"당신은 미쳤거나, 아니면…….\" 자메토프가 입을 열다가, 별안간 머릿속에 번뜩 떠오른 생각에 망연자실한 듯 말을 뚝 끊었다.\n\n\"아니면? 아니면 뭐요? 뭐? 자, 말해 보시오!\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니오.\" 자메토프가 화를 내며 말했다. \"다 헛소리요!\"\n\n둘 다 입을 다물었다. 갑작스러운 웃음의 발작이 지나간 뒤 라스콜니코프는 별안간 생각에 잠기며 우울해졌다. 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 머리를 기댔다. 그는 자메토프를 완전히 잊어버린 듯했다. 침묵이 한동안 이어졌다.\n\n\"차를 왜 안 마시오? 식어 가는데.\" 자메토프가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 차? 아, 그래…….\" 라스콜니코프는 잔을 홀짝이고 빵 한 조각을 입에 넣었다가, 갑자기 자메토프를 바라보더니 모든 것을 떠올린 듯 정신을 차렸다. 동시에 그의 얼굴은 본래의 조롱하는 표정을 되찾았다. 그는 계속 차를 마셨다.\n\n\"요즘 이런 범죄가 굉장히 많아졌어요.\" 자메토프가 말했다. \"바로 며칠 전에도 '모스크바 통신'에서 읽었는데, 모스크바에서 위조 화폐범 일당이 통째로 잡혔다더군요. 아주 정식 조직이었답니다. 채권을 위조했다지 뭐요!\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 그건 오래전 일이오! 난 한 달 전에 읽었소.\" 라스콜니코프가 차분하게 대답했다. \"그래, 당신은 그들을 범죄자로 여기오?\" 그가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"물론 범죄자지요.\"\n\n\"그들이? 그들은 어린애요, 얼간이지, 범죄자가 아니오! 아니, 그런 목적으로 쉰 명이나 되는 사람이 모이다니 — 무슨 발상이람! 세 명도 너무 많은데, 게다가 그들은 자기 자신보다 서로를 더 믿어야 한단 말이지! 한 놈이 취해서 입을 놀리기만 하면 죄다 무너지는 거요. 얼간이들! 그들은 못 믿을 자들을 시켜 지폐를 바꾸게 했어 — 그런 일을 어쩌다 만난 낯선 자한테 맡기다니! 자, 그럼 이 얼간이들이 성공해서 저마다 백만 루블씩 벌었다고 칩시다, 그러면 남은 평생이 어떻게 되겠소? 저마다 남은 평생 동안 서로에게 매여 있어야 하는 거요! 차라리 당장 목을 매는 게 낫지! 게다가 그들은 지폐를 바꿀 줄도 몰랐어. 지폐를 바꾸러 간 자가 오천 루블을 받았는데 손을 벌벌 떨더라는 거요. 처음 사천 루블은 세었는데, 다섯 번째 천 루블은 세지도 않았소 — 돈을 주머니에 넣고 달아나기에 급급했던 거지. 당연히 의심을 샀고. 그래서 그 모든 게 멍청이 하나 때문에 와르르 무너진 거요! 어떻게 그럴 수가 있소?\"\n\n\"손을 떨었다는 거 말이오?\" 자메토프가 한마디 했다. \"그래, 그건 충분히 있을 법한 일이오. 그건, 분명 그럴 수 있다고 나는 확신하오. 때로는 사람이 견뎌 내지 못하는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"그걸 못 견딘다고?\"\n\n\"왜요, 당신이라면 그걸 견디겠소? 아니, 나라면 못 견디오. 고작 백 루블 때문에 그런 끔찍한 일을 겪다니? 그런 일을 적발하는 게 본업인 은행에 위조지폐를 들고 들어가다니! 아니, 난 그럴 낯짝이 없소. 당신은 어떻소?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 다시금 \"혀를 내밀고 싶은\" 강렬한 욕망에 사로잡혔다. 등줄기를 따라 자꾸만 오한이 흘렀다.\n\n\"나라면 전혀 다르게 했을 거요.\" 라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다. \"내가 지폐를 바꾼다면 이렇게 했겠지. 처음 천 루블을 서너 번씩 앞뒤로 세면서 한 장 한 장 들여다본 다음, 두 번째 천 루블로 넘어가는 거요. 그것도 절반쯤 세다가 오십 루블짜리 한 장을 불빛에 비춰 보고, 뒤집어 보고, 다시 불빛에 비춰 보고 — 진짜인지 확인하는 거지. '걱정이 돼서요,' 하고 내가 말하겠소, '며칠 전에 우리 친척이 위조지폐 때문에 이십오 루블을 날렸거든요,' 그러고는 그 이야기를 죄다 늘어놓는 거요. 그리고 세 번째 천 루블을 세기 시작한 뒤에, '아니, 죄송합니다,' 하고 내가 말하지, '아무래도 두 번째 천 루블의 칠백 번째에서 제가 실수한 것 같은데, 확실치가 않네요.' 그렇게 세 번째 천 루블을 그만두고 두 번째로 되돌아가고, 그런 식으로 끝까지 가는 거요. 그러고 나서 다 끝나면, 다섯 번째 천 루블에서 한 장, 두 번째 천 루블에서 한 장을 골라 다시 불빛에 비춰 보고 또 묻는 거지, '이것 좀 바꿔 주세요,' 하고. 그래서 그 직원을 어쩔 줄 모를 정도로 진땀 빼게 만드는 거요. 다 끝내고 나갔다가, 다시 돌아와서, '아니, 죄송합니다,' 하고 무슨 설명을 청하는 거지. 나라면 이렇게 하겠소.\"\n\n\"어휴! 당신 정말 끔찍한 소릴 하는군!\" 자메토프가 웃으며 말했다. \"하지만 그 모든 게 말뿐이지요. 막상 실행에 옮기면 당신도 실수할걸요. 단언컨대, 노련하고 필사적인 자도 늘 자기 자신을 믿을 수 있는 건 아니오, 하물며 당신이나 나야 말할 것도 없지. 가까운 예를 하나 들자면 — 우리 구역에서 살해된 그 노파 말이오. 살인자는 필사적인 자였던 모양인데, 백주 대낮에 모든 걸 걸었고, 기적적으로 모면했지만 — 그자도 손을 떨었어요. 그자는 그곳을 다 털어 가지 못했소, 견뎌 내질 못한 거지. 그건 분명했어요, 왜냐하면…….\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 모욕감을 느낀 듯했다.\n\n\"분명하다고? 그럼 왜 그자를 잡지 않는 거요?\" 그가 자메토프를 악의적으로 비웃으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"뭐, 곧 잡힐 거요.\"\n\n\"누가? 당신이? 당신이 그자를 잡을 수 있다고 생각하오? 만만찮은 일일 텐데! 당신네한테 중요한 건 어떤 자가 돈을 쓰느냐 안 쓰느냐 하는 거지. 돈이 없던 자가 갑자기 돈을 쓰기 시작하면, 그자가 범인이 틀림없다, 이거 아니오. 그러니 어린애라도 당신네를 속여 넘길 수 있소.\"\n\n\"사실 그자들은 늘 그렇게 하니까요.\" 자메토프가 대답했다. \"어떤 자가 목숨을 걸고 교묘하게 살인을 저지르고는, 곧장 선술집에 가서 마셔 댄단 말이오. 돈을 쓰다가 잡히는 거지요, 그들 모두가 당신처럼 교활한 건 아니거든. 당신이라면 물론 선술집에 가지 않겠지요?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 얼굴을 찌푸리며 자메토프를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은 이 화제가 꽤 즐거운가 보군. 그래서 그런 경우에 내가 어떻게 행동할지도 알고 싶은 거요?\" 그가 불쾌한 듯 물었다.\n\n\"알고 싶소.\" 자메토프가 단호하고 진지하게 대답했다. 그의 말과 표정에 다소 지나친 진지함이 어리기 시작했다.\n\n\"몹시?\"\n\n\"몹시!\"\n\n\"좋소, 그럼. 나라면 이렇게 행동하겠소.\" 라스콜니코프는 다시금 자기 얼굴을 자메토프의 얼굴에 바싹 갖다 대고, 다시 그를 빤히 응시하며 속삭이듯 말하기 시작했는데, 상대가 정말이지 몸서리를 칠 정도였다. \"나라면 이렇게 했을 거요. 돈과 보석을 챙겨서, 거기서 나와 곧장 어딘가 인적 없는, 울타리가 둘러쳐지고 사람이라곤 거의 보이지 않는 곳으로 갔겠지, 무슨 채마밭이나 그 비슷한 데로 말이오. 미리 봐 두는 거요, 집이 지어질 때부터 구석에 놓여 있던, 백 푸드*쯤 혹은 그 이상 나가는 돌덩이를. 그 돌을 들어 올리면 — 분명 그 밑에 움푹한 데가 있을 거고, 그 구덩이에 보석과 돈을 넣는 거지. 그런 다음 돌을 다시 굴려서 전처럼 보이게 해 놓고, 발로 꾹 눌러 놓고 가 버리는 거요. 그러고는 일 년이고 이 년이고, 어쩌면 삼 년이고 손도 대지 않는 거지. 그러니 자, 마음껏 수색해 보라지! 아무런 흔적도 없을 테니.\"\n\n\"당신은 미친 사람이오.\" 자메토프가 말했는데, 어찌 된 영문인지 그 역시 속삭이듯 말하며, 눈을 번뜩이는 라스콜니코프에게서 몸을 물렸다. 라스콜니코프는 무섭도록 창백해져 있었고, 그의 윗입술이 씰룩씰룩 떨리고 있었다. 그는 자메토프에게 가능한 한 바싹 몸을 기울였고, 그의 입술이 한마디 말도 내뱉지 못한 채 움직이기 시작했다. 이것이 반 분 동안 이어졌다. 그는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 알면서도 자제할 수가 없었다. 그 끔찍한 말이 그의 입술 위에서 떨고 있었으니, 마치 그때 그 문의 빗장처럼. 다음 순간이면 그 말이 터져 나올 것이고, 다음 순간이면 그가 그것을 놓아 버리고 입 밖에 내고 말 것이었다.\n\n\"그런데 만일 그 노파와 리자베타를 죽인 게 바로 나라면 어떻겠소?\" 그가 별안간 말했고 — 자신이 무슨 짓을 했는지 깨달았다.\n\n자메토프는 미친 듯이 그를 바라보았고 식탁보처럼 새하얗게 질렸다. 그의 얼굴에 일그러진 미소가 어렸다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 가능한 일이오?\" 그가 가냘프게 내뱉었다. 라스콜니코프는 노기 띤 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"솔직히 인정하시오, 당신 그 말을 믿었지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"전혀 아니오, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 안 믿게 됐소.\" 자메토프가 황급히 외쳤다.\n\n\"드디어 내 참새를 잡았군! 그러니까 전엔 믿었던 거구먼, 지금 그 어느 때보다 덜 믿는다면 말이지?\"\n\n\"전혀 아니라니까.\" 자메토프가 외쳤는데, 분명 당황한 기색이었다. \"당신은 이 말을 끌어내려고 날 그렇게 겁준 거요?\"\n\n\"그럼 안 믿는단 말이오? 그럼 내가 경찰서에서 나갔을 때 당신들은 등 뒤에서 무슨 얘기를 했던 거요? 그리고 내가 기절한 뒤에 그 화약 같은 중위는 왜 날 심문했던 거요? 어이, 거기.\" 그는 일어나 모자를 집어 들며 종업원에게 소리쳤다. \"얼마요?\"\n\n\"삼십 코페이카입니다.\" 종업원이 달려오며 대답했다.\n\n\"여기 보드카 값으로 이십 코페이카 더. 봐요, 이게 얼마나 많은 돈인지!\" 그는 떨리는 손에 지폐를 쥐고 자메토프에게 내밀었다. \"빨간 지폐에 파란 지폐, 이십오 루블이오. 이걸 내가 어디서 났겠소? 그리고 이 새 옷은 어디서 났겠소? 내가 한 푼도 없었던 거 당신도 알잖소. 분명 우리 집주인 여자도 캐물어 봤겠지……. 자, 이제 됐소! Assez causé*! 또 봅시다!\"\n\n그는 일종의 광적인 히스테리 감각에 온몸을 떨며 밖으로 나갔는데, 그 속에는 견딜 수 없는 황홀감의 요소도 섞여 있었다. 그러면서도 그는 음울하고 끔찍하게 지쳐 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 발작이 지난 뒤처럼 일그러져 있었다. 피로가 급속히 더해 갔다. 어떤 충격이든, 어떤 자극적인 감각이든 그의 기력을 단번에 자극하고 되살렸지만, 그 자극이 사라지면 힘도 그만큼 빠르게 빠져나갔다.\n\n홀로 남겨진 자메토프는 오랫동안 같은 자리에 앉아 생각에 잠겼다. 라스콜니코프는 어떤 한 가지 점에 대해 자기도 모르게 그의 머릿속에 일대 변혁을 일으켰고, 그를 대신해 결정적으로 마음을 정해 준 셈이었다.\n\n\"일리야 페트로비치는 멍청이야.\" 그는 결론지었다.\n\n라스콜니코프가 음식점 문을 열기가 무섭게 그는 계단에서 라주미힌과 부딪쳤다. 둘은 거의 충돌할 뻔할 때까지 서로를 보지 못했다. 한순간 그들은 서서 서로를 위아래로 훑어보았다. 라주미힌은 깜짝 놀랐다가, 이내 분노가, 진짜 분노가 그의 눈에서 사납게 번뜩였다.\n\n\"여기 있었군!\" 그가 목청껏 소리쳤다. \"침대에서 도망쳐 나왔단 말이지! 그런데 난 자넬 소파 밑에서 찾고 있었다고! 우린 다락방까지 올라갔어. 자네 때문에 나스타시야를 하마터면 두들겨 팰 뻔했네. 그런데 결국 여기 있었군. 로쟈! 이게 도대체 무슨 영문이야? 죄다 사실대로 말해! 털어놔! 듣고 있나?\"\n\n\"자네들 모두가 죽도록 지긋지긋해서 혼자 있고 싶다는 뜻이야.\" 라스콜니코프가 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"혼자라니? 제대로 걷지도 못하고, 얼굴은 백지장처럼 하얗고, 숨도 헐떡이면서! 이 멍청한 것!……. 자넨 팔레 드 크리스탈에서 뭘 하고 있었던 거야? 당장 털어놔!\"\n\n\"날 좀 내버려 둬!\" 라스콜니코프는 이렇게 말하며 그를 지나치려 했다. 이건 라주미힌에게 너무한 일이었다. 그는 라스콜니코프의 어깨를 단단히 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"널 내버려 두라고? 감히 나더러 널 내버려 두라는 거야? 내가 지금 당장 자네한테 무슨 짓을 할지 알아? 자넬 번쩍 들어 올려서 보따리처럼 꽁꽁 묶어 옆구리에 끼고 집으로 데려가 가둬 버릴 거야!\"\n\n\"이봐, 라주미힌.\" 라스콜니코프가 겉보기엔 차분하게, 조용히 입을 열었다. \"내가 자네 호의를 원하지 않는다는 게 안 보이나? 자기를 저주하는 사람, 사실 그걸 짐스럽게 여기는 사람한테…… 은혜를 퍼붓고 싶어 하다니, 참 이상한 욕망이로군! 내 병이 시작될 때 자넨 왜 날 찾아냈나? 어쩌면 난 죽고 싶어서 무척 기뻤는지도 몰라. 오늘 내가 충분히 분명하게 말하지 않았나, 자네가 날 괴롭히고 있다고, 자네가…… 지긋지긋하다고! 자넨 사람들을 괴롭히고 싶어 안달이 난 것 같아! 단언컨대 그 모든 게 내 회복을 심각하게 방해하고 있어, 끊임없이 날 자극하니까 말이야. 봤지, 조시모프가 방금 날 자극하지 않으려고 가 버린 거. 자네도 제발 좀 날 내버려 둬! 도대체 무슨 권리로 날 억지로 붙들어 두는 거야? 내가 지금 온전히 제정신이라는 게 안 보여? 어떻게, 어떻게 해야 자네 친절로 날 괴롭히지 말라고 설득할 수 있겠나? 내가 배은망덕할 수도 있지, 내가 비열할 수도 있어, 그러니 제발 날 좀 내버려 둬, 제발! 날 내버려 둬, 날 내버려 두라고!\"\n\n그는 차분하게, 자기가 막 내뱉으려는 독설을 미리 음미하며 입을 열었지만, 끝에 가서는 루진과 그랬던 것처럼 숨을 헐떡이며 광란 속에서 말을 맺었다.\n\n라주미힌은 잠시 서서 생각하다가, 손을 떨구었다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 지옥에나 가 버려.\" 그가 부드럽고 사려 깊게 말했다. \"잠깐.\" 라스콜니코프가 막 발을 떼려 하자 그가 고함쳤다. \"내 말 좀 들어. 한마디 하겠는데, 자네들은 죄다 떠들어 대고 거드름이나 피우는 바보들의 무리야! 무슨 사소한 고민거리라도 생기면 암탉이 알을 품듯 그걸 끼고 끙끙대지. 그것마저도 자넨 남의 걸 표절한 거고! 자네 안엔 독자적인 삶의 기미라곤 한 점도 없어! 자넨 경뇌유(鯨腦油) 연고로 만들어졌고, 혈관엔 피 대신 림프액이 흐른단 말이야! 난 자네들 중 누구도 안 믿어! 어떤 상황에서든 자네들 모두에게 가장 중요한 건 인간답지 않게 구는 거야! 잠깐!\" 라스콜니코프가 다시 움직이려는 것을 알아채고 그가 곱절의 분노로 외쳤다. \"내 말 끝까지 들어! 알다시피 오늘 저녁에 내가 집들이를 하잖아, 손님들이 지금쯤 다 왔을 게야, 한데 난 삼촌더러 거기서 손님을 맞으라고 하고는 — 잠깐 뛰쳐나온 거라고. 그런데 자네가 바보가 아니라면, 흔해 빠진 바보, 완전한 바보가 아니라면, 자네가 번역이 아니라 원작이라면……. 보게, 로쟈, 난 자네가 영리한 친구라는 걸 인정해, 하지만 자넨 바보야! — 자네가 바보가 아니라면 거리에서 신발이나 닳게 할 게 아니라 오늘 저녁 내 집에 들렀을 거야! 이왕 나왔으니 어쩔 수 없지! 자네한테 아늑하고 편안한 안락의자를 내주겠네, 우리 집주인 여자한테 하나 있거든……. 차 한잔에, 친구들도 있고……. 아니면 소파에 누워도 돼 — 어쨌든 우리랑 함께 있는 거지……. 조시모프도 거기 올 거야. 올 텐가?\"\n\n\"안 가.\"\n\"허, 헛소리!\" 라주미힌이 참다못해 소리쳤다. \"그걸 자네가 어떻게 알아? 자넨 자기 자신도 장담 못 해! 자넨 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라……. 나도 수천 번이나 사람들이랑 죽기 살기로 싸우고선 나중에 다시 그들한테 달려갔다고……. 부끄러워져서 사람한테 다시 돌아가게 되는 거야! 그러니 기억해 둬, 포친코프의 집 삼 층이야…….\"\n\n\"이봐, 라주미힌, 자넨 순전히 선의 때문에 누구한테든 얻어맞고도 가만히 있을 사람이야.\"\n\n\"얻어맞아? 누구한테? 내가? 그런 생각만 해도 그놈 코를 비틀어 떼어 버릴걸! 포친코프의 집, 47번지, 바부시킨의 셋방이야…….\"\n\n\"안 갈 거야, 라주미힌.\" 라스콜니코프는 돌아서서 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n\"올 거라고 장담하네.\" 라주미힌이 그의 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다. \"안 오면 자넬 아는 척도 안 할 테야! 잠깐, 어이, 자메토프가 거기 안에 있나?\"\n\n\"있어.\"\n\n\"그를 봤나?\"\n\n\"봤어.\"\n\n\"얘기도 했고?\"\n\n\"했어.\"\n\n\"무슨 얘길? 에이, 빌어먹을, 그럼 말 안 해도 돼. 포친코프의 집, 47번지, 바부시킨의 셋방, 기억해!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 걸음을 옮겨 모퉁이를 돌아 사도바야 거리로 들어섰다. 라주미힌은 생각에 잠겨 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다. 그러더니 손을 한번 내젓고는 집 안으로 들어갔으나, 계단 앞에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"빌어먹을.\" 그는 거의 소리 내어 말을 이었다. \"녀석이 분별 있게 말하긴 했지만, 그래도……. 난 바보야! 미친 사람이라고 분별 있게 말 못 하란 법 있나! 그리고 바로 이걸 조시모프가 두려워하는 것 같았는데.\" 그는 손가락으로 자기 이마를 탁 쳤다. \"만일……, 어떻게 녀석을 혼자 가게 내버려 둔 거지? 녀석이 물에 빠져 죽을지도 몰라……. 아, 이런 큰 실수를! 안 되겠어.\" 그러고는 라스콜니코프를 따라잡으려 되돌아 뛰어갔지만, 그의 흔적은 어디에도 없었다. 그는 욕설을 내뱉으며 빠른 걸음으로 자메토프에게 물어보려고 팔레 드 크리스탈로 되돌아갔다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 곧장 X 다리로 걸어가 한복판에 서서, 두 팔꿈치를 난간에 괴고 먼 곳을 응시했다. 라주미힌과 헤어지면서 그는 어찌나 기운이 빠졌는지 이곳까지 겨우 다다랐을 정도였다. 그는 거리 어딘가에 앉거나 눕고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다. 물 위로 몸을 숙인 채, 그는 노을의 마지막 분홍빛 잔영을, 짙어 가는 어스름 속에 어두워져 가는 줄지어 선 집들을, 지는 해의 마지막 햇살에 불붙은 듯 번쩍이는 왼편 강기슭의 멀리 있는 다락방 창문 하나를, 그리고 어두워져 가는 운하의 물을 무심히 바라보았는데, 그 물이 그의 시선을 사로잡는 듯했다. 마침내 그의 눈앞에 붉은 원들이 어른거렸고, 집들이 움직이는 것 같았으며, 지나가는 사람들도, 운하의 둑도, 마차들도 죄다 그의 눈앞에서 춤추는 것 같았다. 갑자기 그는 흠칫했는데, 어쩌면 어떤 섬뜩하고 흉측한 광경 덕분에 다시금 실신을 면한 것인지도 몰랐다. 그는 자기 오른쪽에 누군가 서 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 바라보니, 머리에 수건을 두른 키 큰 여자였는데, 길쭉하고 누렇게 뜬 수척한 얼굴에 붉게 충혈된 움푹한 눈을 하고 있었다. 그녀는 그를 똑바로 바라보고 있었지만, 분명 아무것도 보지 못하고 누구도 알아보지 못했다. 갑자기 그녀는 오른손으로 난간을 짚더니 오른쪽 다리를, 이어 왼쪽 다리를 난간 위로 들어 올려, 운하 속으로 몸을 던졌다. 더러운 물이 갈라지며 한순간 제물을 집어삼켰으나, 다음 순간 물에 빠진 여자가 수면 위로 떠올라, 머리와 두 다리는 물속에 잠긴 채 치맛자락이 등 위로 풍선처럼 부풀어 오른 채 물살을 따라 천천히 떠내려갔다.\n\n\"여자가 빠졌다! 여자가 빠졌어!\" 수십 개의 목소리가 외쳤다. 사람들이 달려왔고, 양쪽 기슭이 구경꾼들로 빽빽이 들어찼으며, 다리 위에서는 사람들이 라스콜니코프 주위로 몰려들어 그의 등 뒤에서 밀어 댔다.\n\n\"맙소사! 우리 아프로시니야잖아!\" 바로 곁에서 한 여자가 눈물 어린 목소리로 외쳤다. \"맙소사! 저 사람 좀 구해 줘요! 착한 분들, 끌어내 줘요!\"\n\n\"배다, 배!\" 군중 속에서 누군가 외쳤다. 하지만 배는 필요 없었다. 경찰관 하나가 운하로 내려가는 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 외투와 장화를 벗어 던지고 물속으로 뛰어들었다. 그녀에게 다가가기는 쉬웠다. 그녀가 계단에서 두어 야드 안쪽에 떠 있었으므로, 그는 오른손으로 그녀의 옷을 붙잡고 왼손으로는 동료가 내민 막대를 움켜잡았다. 물에 빠진 여자는 곧바로 끌어올려졌다. 사람들은 그녀를 둑의 화강암 포석 위에 눕혔다. 그녀는 곧 의식을 되찾아 고개를 들고 일어나 앉더니, 재채기와 기침을 하며 멍하니 손으로 젖은 옷을 닦기 시작했다. 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"술을 퍼마시고 제정신이 아니었던 게야.\" 아까 그 여자의 목소리가 그녀 곁에서 한탄했다. \"제정신이 아니었어. 며칠 전에도 목을 매려는 걸 우리가 끊어 내렸지. 방금 내가 가게에 잠깐 다녀오느라 우리 딸애더러 저 사람을 봐 달라고 했는데 — 그새 또 이 사달이 났네! 이웃이에요, 나리, 이웃이라고요, 바로 근처에 살아요, 저 끝에서 두 번째 집인데, 저기 보세요…….\"\n\n군중이 흩어졌다. 경찰관들은 여전히 그 여자 곁에 남아 있었고, 누군가가 경찰서를 입에 올렸다……. 라스콜니코프는 기이한 무관심과 냉담의 감각에 휩싸인 채 바라보았다. 그는 역겨움을 느꼈다. '아니야, 저건 혐오스러워……, 물은……, 그걸로는 안 돼.' 그는 혼자 중얼거렸다. '아무 소용 없어.' 그가 덧붙였다. '기다려 봤자 헛수고지. 경찰서는 어떨까……? 그런데 자메토프는 왜 경찰서에 없는 거지? 경찰서는 열 시까지 문을 여는데…….' 그는 난간에 등을 돌리고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"그래, 좋아!\" 그는 단호하게 말했다. 그는 다리에서 발길을 옮겨 경찰서 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그의 가슴은 텅 비고 공허하게 느껴졌다. 그는 아무 생각도 하고 싶지 않았다. 우울함마저 가시고, \"이 모든 것을 끝장내려고\" 길을 나섰을 때의 그 에너지는 이제 흔적조차 없었다. 그 자리에 완전한 냉담이 들어선 것이다.\n\n'뭐, 이것도 하나의 출구지.' 그는 운하 둑을 따라 천천히 맥없이 걸으며 생각했다. '어쨌든 끝을 내겠어, 내가 그러고 싶으니까……. 하지만 이게 출구이긴 한가? 그게 무슨 상관이람! 한 평짜리 공간이 있겠지 — 하! 하지만 이 무슨 끝이람! 정말 이게 끝인가? 그들에게 말할까, 말까? 아……, 빌어먹을! 어쩜 이렇게 피곤할까! 어디든 앉거나 누울 데를 빨리 찾을 수만 있다면! 가장 부끄러운 건 이게 이토록 어리석다는 거야. 하지만 그것도 아무래도 상관없어! 별 멍청한 생각이 다 머릿속에 떠오르는군.'\n\n경찰서에 가려면 곧장 앞으로 가다가 두 번째 길에서 왼쪽으로 꺾어야 했다. 겨우 몇 걸음 거리였다. 하지만 첫 번째 모퉁이에서 그는 멈춰 섰고, 잠시 생각하더니, 옆길로 접어들어 가던 길에서 두 거리나 벗어났는데, 어쩌면 아무 목적도 없이, 아니 어쩌면 잠깐이라도 미루며 시간을 벌려는 것이었는지도 몰랐다. 그는 땅을 바라보며 걸었다. 갑자기 누군가 그의 귀에 속삭이는 것 같았다. 그가 고개를 들어 보니, 자신이 바로 그 집의 대문 앞에 서 있는 것이었다. 그날 저녁 이후로 그는 그 앞을 지난 적도, 그 근처에 간 적도 없었다. 압도적이고 설명할 길 없는 충동이 그를 끌어당겼다. 그는 집 안으로 들어가 대문을 지나, 오른쪽 첫 번째 입구로 들어서서, 사 층으로 이어지는 낯익은 계단을 오르기 시작했다. 좁고 가파른 계단은 무척 어두웠다. 그는 층계참마다 멈춰 서서 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 첫 번째 층계참에서는 창틀이 뜯겨 나가 있었다. '그땐 이렇지 않았는데.' 그는 생각했다. 여기 이 층에는 니콜라이와 드미트리가 일하고 있던 셋집이 있었다. '문이 잠겨 있고 새로 칠을 했군. 세를 놓으려는 거야.' 그다음 삼 층, 그리고 사 층. '여기다!' 그는 그 셋집 문이 활짝 열려 있는 것을 보고 당황했다. 거기엔 사람들이 있었다. 말소리가 들렸다. 그는 그것을 예상하지 못했다. 잠시 망설인 뒤 그는 마지막 계단을 올라 셋집 안으로 들어섰다. 그곳 역시 수리 중이었다. 안에 일꾼들이 있었다. 이것이 그를 놀라게 한 듯했다. 그는 어쩐지 모든 것이 자기가 떠났을 때 그대로일 거라고, 어쩌면 시체들마저 바닥의 그 자리에 그대로 있을 거라고 상상했던 것이다. 그런데 이제 벽은 휑하니 비고 가구도 없으니, 기이하게 느껴졌다. 그는 창가로 걸어가 창턱에 걸터앉았다. 일꾼은 둘이었는데, 둘 다 젊은 친구였지만 하나가 다른 하나보다 훨씬 어렸다. 그들은 낡고 더러운 누런 벽지 대신, 라일락 꽃무늬가 그려진 새 흰 벽지를 바르고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 어쩐 일인지 이것이 끔찍하게 거슬렸다. 그는 모든 것이 이렇게 죄다 바뀐 것이 안타깝기라도 한 듯, 못마땅한 눈으로 새 벽지를 바라보았다. 일꾼들은 분명 정해진 시간을 넘기고 있었던 터라, 이제 서둘러 벽지를 둘둘 말아 집에 갈 채비를 하고 있었다. 그들은 라스콜니코프가 들어온 것에 아무 관심도 두지 않은 채 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 팔짱을 끼고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그 여자가 아침에 나한테 오는데,\" 나이 든 쪽이 어린 쪽에게 말했다. \"아주 일찍, 잔뜩 차려입고 말이야. '뭘 그렇게 빼입고 멋을 부렸어?' 하고 내가 그러니까, '당신 마음에 들기 위해서라면 뭐든 할 수 있어요, 티트 바실리치!' 이러는 거야. 이건 뭐 한참을 그러는 식이지! 게다가 영락없이 패션 책에서 막 튀어나온 것처럼 빼입었더라니까!\"\n\n\"그래, 패션 책이 뭔데?\" 어린 쪽이 물었다. 그는 분명 상대를 권위자로 여기고 있었다.\n\n\"패션 책이란 건 그림이 잔뜩 든 거야, 색깔도 들어가 있고, 매주 토요일마다 외국에서 우편으로 여기 재단사들한테 오지, 사람들한테 어떻게 차려입는지 보여 주려고 말이야, 남자고 여자고. 그게 그림이라고. 신사들은 대개 모피 외투를 입고 있고, 부인네들 옷의 그 보드라운 장식이란 건, 자네가 상상도 못 할 정도야.\"\n\n\"페테르부르크엔 없는 게 없어,\" 어린 쪽이 신이 나서 외쳤다. \"아버지하고 어머니만 빼고는 뭐든 다 있다니까!\"\n\n\"그것들만 빼고는 뭐든 다 찾을 수 있지, 이 친구야.\" 나이 든 쪽이 점잔을 빼며 단언했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 일어나 다른 방으로 걸어 들어갔는데, 거기엔 금고와 침대, 서랍장이 놓여 있던 곳이었다. 가구가 없으니 그 방이 그에게는 무척 작아 보였다. 벽지는 그대로였다. 한쪽 구석의 벽지는 성상 함이 놓여 있던 자리를 보여 주고 있었다. 그는 그것을 바라보다가 창가로 갔다. 나이 든 일꾼이 그를 곁눈질로 흘끔거렸다.\n\n\"뭐가 필요하슈?\" 그가 갑자기 물었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 대답 대신 통로로 가서 초인종 줄을 잡아당겼다. 똑같은 초인종, 똑같이 갈라진 소리였다. 그는 두 번, 세 번 그것을 울렸다. 그는 귀를 기울이며 기억을 떠올렸다. 그때 느꼈던 그 흉측하고 견딜 수 없이 두려운 감각이 점점 더 생생하게 되살아나기 시작했다. 그는 초인종이 울릴 때마다 몸서리쳤고, 그것이 점점 더 큰 만족감을 주었다.\n\n\"아니, 뭐가 필요하냐고? 댁은 누구슈?\" 일꾼이 그에게 다가서며 소리쳤다. 라스콜니코프는 다시 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"셋방을 하나 얻으려고요.\" 그가 말했다. \"둘러보는 중입니다.\"\n\n\"밤에 방을 보러 다닐 때가 아니지! 그리고 문지기랑 같이 와야 한단 말이오.\"\n\n\"바닥은 다 씻어 냈고, 칠을 할 건가요?\" 라스콜니코프가 계속 말했다. \"피는 없나요?\"\n\n\"무슨 피?\"\n\n\"아니, 여기서 노파랑 그 여동생이 살해당했잖소. 저기 피가 흥건히 고여 있었는데.\"\n\n\"한데 댁은 누구슈?\" 일꾼이 불안한 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 누구냐고?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\"알고 싶소? 경찰서로 갑시다, 거기서 말해 주리다.\"\n\n일꾼들은 어리둥절한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"우린 갈 시간이오, 늦었어. 가자, 알료시카. 문을 잠가야겠어.\" 나이 든 일꾼이 말했다.\n\n\"좋소, 갑시다.\" 라스콜니코프가 무심하게 말하고는, 먼저 나가 천천히 계단을 내려갔다. \"어이, 문지기.\" 그가 대문께에서 소리쳤다.\n\n입구에는 몇 사람이 서서 지나가는 행인들을 빤히 바라보고 있었다. 문지기 둘, 농가의 아낙 하나, 긴 외투를 걸친 사내 하나, 그리고 몇몇이 더 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 곧장 그들에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"뭐가 필요하슈?\" 문지기 중 하나가 물었다.\n\n\"경찰서에 다녀왔소?\"\n\n\"방금 다녀왔는데. 뭐가 필요하냐니까?\"\n\n\"문은 열려 있소?\"\n\n\"그럼요.\"\n\n\"담당자가 거기 있던가요?\"\n\n\"잠깐 있었지요. 뭐가 필요하슈?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 아무 대답도 않고 그들 곁에 서서 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"이 양반이 셋방을 보러 왔었지요.\" 나이 든 일꾼이 앞으로 나서며 말했다.\n\n\"어느 셋방?\"\n\n\"우리가 일하던 데요. '왜 피를 다 씻어 냈소?' 하고 이 양반이 그러더라고요. '여기서 살인이 있었는데, 내가 이 방을 얻으러 왔다.' 이러면서요. 그러더니 초인종을 울려 대지 뭡니까, 하마터면 부숴 버릴 뻔했어요. '경찰서로 갑시다,' 하고 그러더라고요. '거기서 죄다 말해 주겠다.' 우리한테서 떨어지질 않더라니까요.\"\n\n문지기는 얼굴을 찌푸리고 어리둥절한 채 라스콜니코프를 바라보았다.\n\n\"댁은 누구슈?\" 그가 가능한 한 위압적으로 소리쳤다.\n\n\"나는 로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프, 전직 대학생이고, 여기서 멀지 않은 실의 집, 14호 셋방에 살고 있소, 문지기한테 물어보시오, 그가 날 아니까.\" 라스콜니코프는 돌아보지도 않고 어두워져 가는 거리를 뚫어지게 바라보며, 나른하고 꿈결 같은 목소리로 이 모든 것을 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그 셋방엘 갔던 거요?\"\n\n\"보러 갔지요.\"\n\n\"거기 볼 게 뭐가 있다고?\"\n\n\"이 작자를 곧장 경찰서로 끌고 갑시다.\" 긴 외투를 걸친 사내가 불쑥 끼어들었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 어깨너머로 그를 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 여전히 느리고 나른한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"갑시다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 데려가요.\" 사내가 더 자신감 있게 말을 이었다. \"왜 그런 데를 들어갔겠소, 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있는 거지, 응?\"\n\n\"술에 취한 건 아닌데, 무슨 영문인지 알 게 뭐람.\" 일꾼이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아니, 도대체 뭐가 필요하냐니까?\" 문지기가 이제 정말로 화가 나기 시작하며 다시 소리쳤다. \"왜 여기서 얼쩡거리는 거요?\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 경찰서가 무서워서 못 가는 거요?\" 라스콜니코프가 비웃듯 말했다.\n\n\"무섭긴 뭐가 무서워? 왜 여기서 얼쩡거리느냐고?\"\n\n\"저자는 악당이야!\" 농가의 아낙이 외쳤다.\n\n\"저런 자랑 무슨 시간 낭비요?\" 다른 문지기가 외쳤는데, 그는 활짝 풀어 헤친 외투를 걸치고 허리띠에 열쇠를 매단 거구의 농부였다. \"썩 꺼져! 영락없는 악당이야, 틀림없어. 썩 꺼지라고!\"\n\n그러고는 라스콜니코프의 어깨를 움켜쥐고 그를 거리로 내던졌다. 그는 앞으로 비틀거렸으나 다시 발을 디뎌 균형을 잡고는, 말없이 구경꾼들을 바라보고 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n\"이상한 작자야!\" 일꾼이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"요즘엔 이상한 인간들이 다 돌아다닌다니까.\" 아낙이 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 경찰서로 데려갔어야 했는데.\" 긴 외투를 걸친 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"저런 작자랑은 아예 엮이지 않는 게 상책이지.\" 거구의 문지기가 단정 지었다. \"영락없는 악당이라고! 분명 노리는 게 있을 거야, 틀림없어, 한데 한번 붙잡았다 하면 떼어 낼 수가 없거든……. 우리가 그런 부류를 잘 알지!\"\n\n'갈까, 말까?' 라스콜니코프는 네거리 한복판에 서서 생각하며, 마치 누군가에게서 결정적인 한마디를 기다리기라도 하듯 주위를 둘러보았다. 하지만 아무 소리도 들려오지 않았고, 모든 것이 그가 밟고 선 돌멩이들처럼 죽은 듯 고요했으니, 그에게는, 오직 그에게만은 죽어 있었다……. 별안간 거리 끝, 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 짙어 가는 어스름 속에 그는 군중을 보았고 말소리와 고함을 들었다. 군중 한가운데에 마차 한 대가 서 있었다……. 거리 한복판에서 불빛 하나가 어른거렸다. '뭐지?' 라스콜니코프는 오른쪽으로 꺾어 군중에게 다가갔다. 그는 무엇에든 매달리려는 듯했고, 그것이 무엇인지 알아보고는 차갑게 미소 지었으니, 이미 경찰서로 가기로 단단히 마음을 정했고 머지않아 이 모든 것이 끝나리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n* 팔레 드 크리스탈: '수정궁'이라는 뜻의 음식점 이름. * 푸드: 러시아의 무게 단위. 1푸드는 약 16.38킬로그램. * Assez causé: 프랑스어로 \"이야기는 충분하다\"라는 뜻.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n길 한복판에 우아한 마차 한 대가 기운 넘치는 잿빛 말 두 필을 매단 채 서 있었다. 마차 안에는 아무도 없었고, 마부는 마부석에서 내려와 곁에 서 있었으며, 말들은 누군가가 고삐를 붙들고 있었다……. 한 무리의 사람들이 그 주위로 몰려들었고, 그 앞에는 경찰들이 서 있었다. 그중 하나가 불 켜진 등을 들고 바퀴 가까이에 놓인 무언가를 비추고 있었다. 모두가 이야기하고, 소리치고, 탄식했다. 마부는 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 같은 말만 되풀이했다.\n\n\"이런 변고가! 맙소사, 이런 변고가!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 비집고 들어갈 수 있는 데까지 파고들어, 마침내 이 소동과 관심의 대상을 보는 데 성공했다. 땅바닥에는 마차에 치인 한 사내가 의식을 잃은 듯 피투성이가 된 채 누워 있었다. 그는 무척 형편없는 옷차림이었지만, 노동자 같지는 않았다. 머리와 얼굴에서 피가 흘러내리고 있었고, 얼굴은 으스러지고 짓이겨져 알아볼 수 없게 일그러져 있었다. 그가 심하게 다친 것은 분명했다.\n\n\"하느님 맙소사!\" 마부가 울부짖었다. \"내가 더 어쩔 수 있었겠소? 내가 빨리 몰았거나 저 사람한테 소리치지 않았다면 모를까, 난 천천히, 서두르지 않고 가고 있었다고요. 누구나 다 봤다시피 난 다른 사람들처럼 그냥 가고 있었어요. 취한 사람이 똑바로 못 걷는다는 건 다들 알지 않습니까……. 저 사람이 비틀거리면서, 거의 쓰러질 듯이 길을 건너는 걸 봤지요. 한 번 소리치고, 두 번, 세 번 소리쳤어요, 그러고는 말을 멈춰 세웠는데, 저 사람이 곧장 말발굽 밑으로 쓰러진 거예요! 일부러 그랬거나, 아니면 잔뜩 취했거나……. 말들은 어려서 잘 놀라는 놈들이라……, 펄쩍 뛰었고, 저 사람이 비명을 질렀고……, 그래서 더 날뛴 거지요. 그렇게 된 일이에요!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇게 된 일이오.\" 군중 속에서 누군가의 목소리가 거들었다.\n\n\"그래요, 저 사람이 소리쳤어, 그건 사실이야, 세 번이나 소리쳤다고.\" 또 다른 목소리가 단언했다.\n\n\"세 번이었어요, 우리 다 들었다고.\" 세 번째 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 마부는 그다지 괴로워하거나 겁먹지는 않았다. 그 마차가 어딘가에서 기다리고 있을 부유하고 지체 높은 인물의 것임은 분명했고, 경찰들은 당연히 그분의 일정을 어그러뜨리지 않으려 적잖이 마음을 졸였다. 그들이 해야 할 일이라곤 다친 사람을 경찰서와 병원으로 옮기는 것뿐이었다. 그의 이름은 아무도 몰랐다.\n\n그러는 동안 라스콜니코프는 비집고 들어가 그 위로 더 가까이 몸을 숙였다. 등불이 불현듯 그 불운한 사내의 얼굴을 환히 비추었다. 그는 그를 알아보았다.\n\n\"이 사람을 압니다! 이 사람을 알아요!\" 그는 앞으로 밀고 나가며 외쳤다. \"퇴직한 관리, 마르멜라도프입니다. 바로 근처 코젤의 집에 살아요……. 어서 의사를 불러요! 내가 내겠습니다, 보세요?\" 그는 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내 경찰에게 보여 주었다. 그는 격렬하게 동요하고 있었다.\n\n경찰들은 그 사내가 누구인지 알아낸 것을 다행으로 여겼다. 라스콜니코프는 자신의 이름과 주소를 댔고, 마치 친아버지를 위한 일이라도 되는 양 간절하게, 의식을 잃은 마르멜라도프를 당장 그의 집으로 옮겨 달라고 경찰에게 애원했다.\n\n\"바로 여기, 세 집 건너입니다.\" 그가 다급하게 말했다. \"그 집은 코젤의 것인데, 부유한 독일인이에요. 이 사람은 집으로 가던 길이었어요, 틀림없이 취해서요. 내가 이 사람을 압니다, 술꾼이에요. 거기 가족이 있어요, 아내, 아이들, 딸이 하나 있고……. 병원으로 데려가려면 시간이 걸리지만, 그 집에는 분명 의사가 있을 겁니다. 내가 내겠습니다, 내가 낼게요! 적어도 집에서는 보살핌을 받을 테니……, 곧바로 도와줄 거예요. 하지만 병원까지 데려가기 전에 죽고 말 겁니다.\" 그는 아무도 모르게 무언가를 경찰의 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주는 데 성공했다. 그러나 일이라는 게 명백하고 정당한 데다, 어차피 도움이 더 가까운 곳에 있었다. 사람들이 다친 사내를 들어 올렸고, 자청해서 거드는 이들도 있었다.\n\n코젤의 집은 서른 걸음쯤 떨어져 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 마르멜라도프의 머리를 조심스레 받쳐 든 채 길을 안내하며 뒤따라 걸었다.\n\n\"이쪽이요, 이쪽! 머리부터 위로 올려야 합니다. 돌려요! 내가 내겠습니다, 후하게 사례하지요.\" 그가 중얼거렸다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는, 늘 그렇듯 짬이 날 때마다 하던 대로, 그 작은 방을 창가에서 난로까지, 다시 그 반대로 오가며 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 안은 채 혼잣말을 하고 기침을 하기 시작한 참이었다. 요즘 들어 그녀는 어느 때보다 더 자주 맏딸 폴렌카에게 말을 건넸는데, 폴렌카는 열 살배기 아이로, 알아듣지 못하는 것이 많았지만 엄마가 자기를 필요로 한다는 것만은 아주 잘 알았기에, 늘 그 크고 영리한 눈으로 엄마를 지켜보며 알아듣는 척하려 안간힘을 썼다. 이번에는 폴렌카가 하루 종일 몸이 좋지 않아 잠자리에 들려는 남동생의 옷을 벗기고 있었다. 사내아이는 누나가 셔츠를 벗겨 주기를 기다리고 있었는데, 그 셔츠는 밤에 빨아야 했다. 아이는 의자에 꼿꼿이, 미동도 없이 앉아 있었고, 말없는 진지한 얼굴로 두 다리를 앞으로 곧게 뻗은 채 — 발뒤꿈치는 모으고 발끝은 바깥으로 벌린 자세였다.\n\n아이는 엄마가 누나에게 하는 말에 귀를 기울이며, 입술을 삐죽 내밀고 두 눈을 동그랗게 뜬 채 완벽하게 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 잠자리에 들려고 옷을 벗을 때면 모든 착한 사내아이들이 으레 그러는 모습 그대로였다. 그보다 더 어린 여자아이 하나는, 말 그대로 누더기를 걸친 채, 칸막이 옆에 서서 제 차례를 기다리고 있었다. 계단으로 통하는 문은 열려 있었는데, 다른 방들에서 흘러들어와 가엾고 폐병을 앓는 여인에게 길고 끔찍한 기침 발작을 일으키는 담배 연기를 조금이나마 덜어 보려는 것이었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 그 한 주 사이에 더욱 야위어 버린 듯했고, 얼굴에 핀 병적인 홍조는 어느 때보다 짙었다.\n\n\"넌 믿지 못할 거야, 상상도 못 할 거야, 폴렌카.\" 그녀가 방 안을 오가며 말했다. \"우리가 친정아버지 집에서 얼마나 행복하고 호사스럽게 살았는지, 그리고 이 술꾼이 날 어떻게 파멸로 몰아넣었는지, 그리고 앞으로 너희들 모두를 어떻게 파멸시킬지! 아버지는 문관 대령이셨고, 한 발짝만 더 가면 지사가 될 분이셨어. 그래서 아버지를 뵈러 오는 사람마다 이렇게 말했단다, '저희는 어른을 지사님처럼 우러릅니다, 이반 미하일로비치 어른!' 내가……, 내가…….\" 그녀가 격렬하게 기침을 했다. \"오, 저주받은 인생.\" 그녀는 목을 가다듬고 두 손을 가슴에 누르며 외쳤다. \"내가……, 마지막 무도회에서……, 귀족 회장 댁에서……, 베즈제멜니 공작 부인이 날 보셨어 — 폴렌카, 네 아빠와 내가 결혼할 때 축복해 주신 분이지 — 그분이 곧바로 물으셨단다, '저 애가 졸업식 때 숄 댄스를 추던 그 예쁜 아가씨 아닌가?' (저기 찢어진 데 좀 꿰매라, 바늘을 들고 내가 가르쳐 준 대로 기워야지, 안 그러면 내일은 — 콜록, 콜록, 콜록 — 저이가 더 크게 찢어 놓을 거야.\" 그녀가 힘겹게 또박또박 말했다.) \"그때 시종보였던 셰골스코이 공작이 마침 페테르부르크에서 막 오셨는데……, 나하고 마주르카를 추고는 이튿날 청혼을 하려 했지. 하지만 난 듣기 좋은 말로 고마움을 전하면서, 내 마음은 이미 오래전부터 다른 사람의 것이라고 말했단다. 그 다른 사람이 바로 네 아빠였어, 폴랴. 아버지는 무섭게 화를 내셨지……. 물은 준비됐니? 셔츠를 다오, 양말도! 리다.\" 그녀가 막내에게 말했다. \"오늘 밤엔 속옷 없이 자야겠다……, 그리고 양말도 같이 벗어 놓으렴……, 한꺼번에 빨게. 저 술 취한 부랑자는 도대체 왜 안 들어오는 거야? 셔츠를 행주처럼 될 때까지 입어 대고, 너덜너덜 다 찢어 놓았으니! 한꺼번에 다 해치워야지, 이틀 밤 내리 일하지 않으려면! 아이고, 맙소사! (콜록, 콜록, 콜록, 콜록!) 또 시작이군! 이게 뭐야?\" 그녀는 통로에 몰려든 사람들과, 무언가를 들고 방으로 밀고 들어오는 사내들을 알아채고 소리쳤다. \"이게 뭐예요? 뭘 들고 오는 거예요? 맙소사!\"\n\n\"이 사람을 어디에 둘까요?\" 의식을 잃고 피투성이가 된 마르멜라도프가 옮겨져 들어왔을 때, 경찰이 둘러보며 물었다.\n\n\"소파에! 소파에 곧장 눕혀요, 머리는 이쪽으로.\" 라스콜니코프가 가리켰다.\n\n\"길에서 치였대! 취해서!\" 통로에서 누군가 소리쳤다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 새파랗게 질려 숨을 헐떡이며 서 있었다. 아이들은 겁에 질렸다. 어린 리다는 비명을 지르며 폴렌카에게 달려가 온몸을 떨며 매달렸다.\n\n마르멜라도프를 눕히고 나서, 라스콜니코프는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 날 듯이 달려갔다.\n\n\"제발 진정하세요, 무서워하지 마세요!\" 그가 빠르게 말했다. \"이분이 길을 건너다 마차에 치였습니다, 무서워하지 마세요, 곧 정신이 들 겁니다, 제가 이리로 옮기라고 했어요……, 제가 전에 여기 왔었지요, 기억하시죠? 곧 정신이 들 거예요, 제가 내겠습니다!\"\n\n\"이번엔 끝장이구나!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 절망하여 외치며 남편에게 달려들었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 그녀가 쉬이 기절하는 부류의 여자가 아님을 곧바로 알아차렸다. 그녀는 곧장 그 불운한 사내의 머리 밑에 베개를 받쳤는데 — 아무도 거기까지 생각하지 못했던 것이다 — 이내 옷을 벗기며 그를 살피기 시작했다. 그녀는 자신을 잊은 채 정신을 차리고서, 떨리는 입술을 깨물며 터져 나오려는 비명을 억눌렀다.\n\n그러는 동안 라스콜니코프는 누군가에게 의사를 부르러 가도록 시켰다. 알고 보니 한 집 건너에 의사가 있었다.\n\n\"의사를 부르러 보냈어요.\" 그는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 거듭 안심시켰다. \"걱정하지 마세요, 제가 내겠습니다. 물 없습니까?……, 그리고 냅킨이든 수건이든, 뭐든 좋으니 어서 좀 주세요……. 다치긴 했지만 죽진 않았어요, 믿으세요……. 의사가 뭐라고 하는지 봅시다!\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 창가로 달려갔다. 그곳, 구석의 망가진 의자 위에는 커다란 질그릇 대야에 물이 가득 담겨 있었는데, 그날 밤 아이들과 남편의 속옷을 빨려고 준비해 둔 것이었다. 이 빨래를 카테리나 이바노브나는 밤마다, 적어도 일주일에 두 번은, 아니 그보다 자주 했다. 이 집 형편이 갈아입을 속옷조차 거의 없을 지경에 이른 데다, 카테리나 이바노브나는 불결함을 견디지 못해서, 집 안에 더러운 꼴을 보느니 차라리 밤에 식구들이 다 잠든 사이 제 힘에 부치도록 일해 가며 몸을 축내는 편을 택했던 것이다. 그래야 젖은 속옷을 줄에 널어 아침까지 말릴 수 있었으니까. 그녀는 라스콜니코프가 청한 대로 물 대야를 집어 들었지만, 그 무게에 거의 쓰러질 뻔했다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 이미 수건을 찾아내, 그것을 적셔 마르멜라도프의 얼굴에서 피를 닦아 내기 시작했다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 곁에 서서 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬며 두 손을 가슴에 누르고 있었다. 그녀 자신이 보살핌을 받아야 할 처지였다. 라스콜니코프는 다친 사내를 이리로 옮긴 것이 어쩌면 실수였을지도 모른다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다. 경찰 역시 망설이며 서 있었다.\n\n\"폴렌카.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다. \"소냐한테 뛰어가렴, 어서. 집에 없거든 아빠가 마차에 치였으니 당장 이리로 오라고 말을 남겨 두렴……, 들어오는 대로 말이야. 뛰어가, 폴렌카! 자, 숄을 두르고.\"\n\n\"있는 힘껏 뛰어!\" 의자에 앉아 있던 사내아이가 갑자기 소리치더니, 그러고는 다시 그 말없는 굳은 자세로 돌아가, 동그란 눈에 발뒤꿈치는 앞으로 내밀고 발끝은 벌린 채였다.\n\n그러는 사이 방 안은 사람들로 가득 차서 바늘 하나 떨어뜨릴 틈도 없었다. 경찰들은 떠났고, 다만 한 명이 한동안 남아 계단에서 들어오는 사람들을 몰아내려 애썼다. 리페베흐젤 부인의 세입자란 세입자는 거의 다 집 안쪽 방에서 쏟아져 나왔는데, 처음에는 문간에 끼어 있다가 나중에는 방 안으로까지 넘쳐 들었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 격노했다.\n\n\"적어도 편히 죽게는 해 줘야 할 거 아녜요.\" 그녀가 군중을 향해 소리쳤다. \"이게 당신들 입 벌리고 구경할 거리예요? 담배까지 물고서! (콜록, 콜록, 콜록!) 모자라도 쓰고 계시지 그래요……. 저기 하나는 모자까지 쓰고 있군!……, 썩 나가요! 적어도 죽은 사람한테는 예의를 지켜야지!\"\n\n기침이 그녀의 목을 틀어막았지만 — 그녀의 질책은 효과가 없지 않았다. 사람들은 분명 카테리나 이바노브나를 다소 두려워하고 있었다. 세입자들은 하나둘 다시 문간으로 밀려 나갔는데, 그것은 갑작스러운 사고를 마주할 때 사람들 마음속에 일어나는 그 묘한 내면의 만족감, 희생자와 가장 가깝고 가장 아끼는 이들에게서조차 엿보이는, 아무리 진실한 동정과 연민을 품었다 한들 살아 있는 그 누구도 면치 못하는 그런 감정 탓이었다.\n\n그러나 바깥에서는 병원 이야기를 하며, 여기서 소동을 피울 일이 아니라고 떠드는 목소리들이 들려왔다.\n\n\"죽을 일도 아니라니!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외치며 분풀이를 하려고 문 쪽으로 달려들었으나, 문간에서 리페베흐젤 부인과 정면으로 마주쳤다. 사고 소식을 막 듣고 질서를 잡으러 달려온 참이었다. 그녀는 유달리 다투기 좋아하고 무책임한 독일 여자였다.\n\n\"아이고, 맙소사!\" 그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"당신 남편을 취한 말이 짓밟았구먼! 병원으로 데려가요! 내가 이 집 주인이야!\"\n\n\"아말리야 루드비고브나, 부디 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 좀 새겨 보시지요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 거만하게 입을 열었다(그녀는 집주인에게 늘 거만한 어조를 썼는데, 그녀가 '제 분수를 깨닫게' 하려는 것이었고, 지금 이 순간에도 그 만족을 스스로 마다할 수 없었다). \"아말리야 루드비고브나…….\"\n\n\"내가 전에 한 번 말했잖아, 날 아말리야 루드비고브나라고 부를 생각은 꿈도 꾸지 말라고. 난 아말리야 이바노브나야.\"\n\n\"당신은 아말리야 이바노브나가 아니라 아말리야 루드비고브나예요, 그리고 나는 지금 문 뒤에서 웃고 있는 레베쟈트니코프 씨 같은 당신의 천박한 아첨꾼이 아니니까 (실제로 문에서 웃음소리와 '또 시작이군'이라는 외침이 들려왔다) 나는 늘 당신을 아말리야 루드비고브나라고 부르겠어요, 도대체 왜 그 이름을 싫어하는지는 이해할 수 없지만요. 세묜 자하로비치에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 직접 보세요. 죽어 가고 있다고요. 부탁이니 당장 저 문을 닫고 아무도 들이지 마세요. 적어도 편히 죽게는 해 줘야지! 안 그러면 경고하건대, 내일 당장 총독 각하께 당신의 행실을 알릴 거예요. 공작께서는 제가 처녀였을 때부터 저를 아셨고, 세묜 자하로비치도 잘 기억하시며 그분께 여러 차례 은혜를 베푸셨어요. 세묜 자하로비치에게 친구와 후원자가 많았다는 건 누구나 다 아는 사실이지요, 그분이 자신의 불행한 약점을 알기에 고결한 자존심에서 스스로 그들을 멀리했을 뿐이지만요. 하지만 이제 (그녀가 라스콜니코프를 가리켰다) 너그러운 젊은이 한 분이 우리를 도우러 오셨는데, 재산도 연줄도 있는 분이고 세묜 자하로비치가 어릴 때부터 알던 분이에요. 안심하셔도 좋아요, 아말리야 루드비고브나…….\"\n\n이 모든 말이 극도로 빠르게, 점점 더 빨라지며 쏟아져 나왔지만, 갑작스러운 기침이 카테리나 이바노브나의 달변을 뚝 끊어 놓았다. 바로 그 순간 죽어 가던 사내가 의식을 되찾고 신음을 토했다. 그녀가 그에게 달려갔다. 다친 사내는 눈을 떴으나, 알아보지도 이해하지도 못한 채 자기 위로 몸을 숙인 라스콜니코프를 멍하니 바라보았다. 그는 깊고 느리고 괴로운 숨을 들이쉬었다. 입가에는 피가 배어 나오고 이마에는 땀방울이 맺혔다. 라스콜니코프를 알아보지 못한 채, 그는 불안하게 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 슬프지만 엄한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 주르륵 흘렀다.\n\n\"맙소사! 가슴이 통째로 으스러졌어! 어쩌면 이렇게 피를 흘리는지.\" 그녀가 절망하여 말했다. \"옷을 벗겨야 해요. 조금만 돌아누워 봐요, 세묜 자하로비치, 할 수 있겠거든.\" 그녀가 그에게 외쳤다.\n\n마르멜라도프가 그녀를 알아보았다.\n\n\"신부님을.\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 또박또박 말했다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 창가로 걸어가, 창틀에 머리를 기댄 채 절망하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 저주받은 인생!\"\n\n\"신부님을.\" 죽어 가던 사내가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 다시 말했다.\n\n\"부르러 갔어요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 소리쳤고, 그는 그 외침에 따르듯 입을 다물었다. 슬프고 겁먹은 눈으로 그가 그녀를 찾았다. 그녀가 돌아와 그의 베개 곁에 섰다. 그는 다소 편안해진 듯했으나, 오래가지는 않았다.\n\n이윽고 그의 눈길이, 구석에서 발작이라도 일으킨 듯 떨면서 의아해하는 어린아이의 눈으로 그를 빤히 바라보고 있는, 그가 가장 아끼는 어린 리다에게 멈췄다.\n\n\"아-아.\" 그가 불안하게 그쪽을 가리키며 신음했다. 그는 무언가 말하고 싶어 했다.\n\n\"또 뭐예요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다.\n\n\"맨발이야, 맨발!\" 그가 미친 듯한 눈으로 아이의 맨발을 가리키며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"조용히 해요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"저 애가 왜 맨발인지 당신도 알잖아요.\"\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다, 의사가.\" 라스콜니코프가 안도하며 외쳤다.\n\n의사가 들어왔는데, 꼼꼼해 보이는 자그마한 독일인 노인으로, 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 환자에게 다가가 맥을 짚고, 조심스레 머리를 만져 보고는, 카테리나 이바노브나의 도움을 받아 피로 물든 셔츠의 단추를 풀고 다친 사내의 가슴을 드러냈다. 가슴은 찢기고 으스러지고 부서져 있었으며, 오른쪽 갈비뼈가 여러 대 부러져 있었다. 왼쪽, 바로 심장 위에는 크고 불길해 보이는 누르스름한 검은 멍이 있었는데 — 말발굽에 모질게 채인 자국이었다. 의사가 미간을 찌푸렸다. 경찰이 그에게, 이 사람이 바퀴에 끼여 길에서 서른 걸음이나 함께 굴러갔다고 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람이 의식을 되찾았다는 게 놀랍군요.\" 의사가 라스콜니코프에게 나직이 속삭였다.\n\n\"이 사람은 어떻습니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"곧 죽을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말 가망이 없습니까?\"\n\n\"손톱만큼도 없어요! 마지막 숨을 몰아쉬고 있어요……. 머리도 심하게 다쳤고……. 흠……, 원하신다면 피를 뽑아 줄 수는 있지만……, 소용없을 겁니다. 앞으로 오 분이나 십 분 안에 틀림없이 죽을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼 피라도 뽑아 주는 게 낫겠지요.\"\n\n\"원하신다면야……. 하지만 미리 말해 두는데, 완전히 부질없는 짓일 겁니다.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 또 다른 발소리가 들렸다. 통로의 군중이 갈라지더니, 자그마한 백발노인인 신부가 성체를 받쳐 들고 문간에 나타났다. 사고가 났을 때 경찰 하나가 그를 부르러 갔던 것이다. 의사는 신부와 자리를 바꾸며 그와 눈짓을 주고받았다. 라스콜니코프는 의사에게 잠시만 더 머물러 달라고 청했다. 의사는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 남았다.\n\n모두가 뒤로 물러섰다. 고해는 금세 끝났다. 죽어 가는 사내는 아마 거의 알아듣지 못했을 것이다. 그는 그저 또렷하지 않은 끊긴 소리만 낼 수 있을 뿐이었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 어린 리다를 데려오고, 사내아이를 의자에서 들어 올린 뒤, 난로 옆 구석에 무릎을 꿇고 아이들을 자기 앞에 나란히 꿇어앉혔다. 여자아이는 여전히 떨고 있었지만, 사내아이는 작은 맨무릎을 꿇은 채 또박또박 손을 들어 정확하게 성호를 긋고는, 이마가 바닥에 닿도록 절을 했는데, 그것이 그 아이에게는 각별한 만족을 주는 듯했다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 입술을 깨물고 눈물을 참았다. 그녀도 기도를 했고, 이따금 사내아이의 셔츠를 똑바로 잡아당기기도 하면서, 무릎을 꿇은 채 기도를 멈추지 않고 궤에서 꺼낸 손수건으로 여자아이의 드러난 어깨를 가까스로 덮어 주었다. 그러는 동안 안쪽 방들로 통하는 문이 또다시 호기심에 못 이겨 열렸다. 통로에는 계단의 온 셋집에서 몰려든 구경꾼들이 점점 더 빽빽하게 들어찼지만, 그들은 문지방을 넘어설 엄두는 내지 못했다. 짤막한 양초 토막 하나가 이 광경을 비추고 있었다.\n\n바로 그 순간 폴렌카가 문간의 군중을 헤치고 비집고 들어왔다. 그녀는 너무 빨리 달려온 탓에 숨을 헐떡이며 들어와, 머릿수건을 벗고, 엄마를 찾아 다가가서는 말했다. \"언니가 와요, 길에서 만났어요.\" 엄마는 그녀를 자기 곁에 무릎 꿇렸다.\n\n겁먹은 듯 소리 없이 한 젊은 처녀가 군중을 헤치고 들어왔는데, 궁핍과 누더기와 죽음과 절망 한가운데 놓인 그 방에서 그녀의 모습은 기이했다. 그녀 역시 누더기를 걸쳤고, 차림새는 죄다 가장 값싼 것들이었지만, 거리에서 몸 파는 여자 특유의 천박한 치장으로 꾸며져 있어, 그 부끄러운 목적을 틀림없이 드러내고 있었다. 소냐는 문간에 멈춰 서서 어쩔 줄 모른 채 주위를 둘러보았는데, 아무것도 의식하지 못하는 듯했다. 그녀는 자신의 네 번째 손을 거친 야한 비단 드레스를, 여기서는 우스꽝스러울 만큼 긴 자락이 도무지 어울리지 않는 그 옷을 잊었고, 문간을 가득 메운 거대한 크리놀린도, 밝은 색 구두도, 밤에는 쓸데도 없건만 들고 온 양산도, 불꽃처럼 새빨간 깃털이 나풀거리는 우스꽝스러운 둥근 밀짚모자도 잊고 있었다. 멋들어지게 비스듬히 기울인 그 모자 아래로는, 입술을 벌리고 두 눈을 공포에 질려 부릅뜬, 창백하고 겁에 질린 자그마한 얼굴이 있었다. 소냐는 열여덟 살의 자그맣고 야윈 처녀로, 금발에, 제법 예뻤으며, 놀라운 푸른 눈을 지니고 있었다. 그녀는 침대와 신부를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그녀 역시 달려오느라 숨이 차 있었다. 마침내 속삭임이, 아마도 군중 속에서 들려온 몇 마디 말이 그녀의 귀에 닿았다. 그녀는 눈을 내리깔고, 여전히 문 가까이에 붙은 채로 방 안으로 한 걸음 내디뎠다.\n\n예식이 끝났다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 다시 남편에게 다가갔다. 신부는 뒤로 물러서며, 떠나기 전에 카테리나 이바노브나에게 몇 마디 훈계와 위로의 말을 건네려고 돌아섰다.\n\n\"이 애들을 어쩌란 말이에요?\" 그녀가 어린것들을 가리키며 날카롭고 짜증스럽게 그의 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"하느님은 자비로우시니, 지존하신 분께 도움을 구하십시오.\" 신부가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"흥! 그분은 자비로우시겠지요, 우리한테는 아니지만.\"\n\n\"그건 죄입니다, 죄예요, 부인.\" 신부가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 저건 죄가 아니고요?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 죽어 가는 사내를 가리키며 외쳤다.\n\n\"본의 아니게 사고를 낸 자들이, 적어도 그가 벌어들이던 몫의 손실에 대해서는 부인께 보상하기로 할지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"신부님은 이해를 못 하시는군요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 성을 내며 손을 내저었다. \"그리고 그들이 왜 나한테 보상을 해요? 아니, 저이는 취해서 제 발로 말 아래 뛰어든 거예요! 무슨 벌이요? 저이가 우리한테 가져다준 거라곤 비참함뿐이었어요. 다 마셔 없앴다고요, 저 술꾼이! 술 마시려고 우릴 등쳐 먹고, 술 때문에 저 애들 인생도 내 인생도 다 망쳐 놨어요! 그러니 죽으니 차라리 다행이지! 먹여 살릴 입 하나 줄었으니!\"\n\n\"죽음의 순간에는 용서해야 합니다, 그건 죄예요, 부인, 그런 마음은 큰 죄입니다.\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 죽어 가는 사내를 돌보느라 바빴다. 그에게 물을 먹이고, 머리에서 피와 땀을 닦아 내고, 베개를 똑바로 고쳐 주면서, 이따금 잠깐씩만 몸을 돌려 신부에게 말을 건넸을 뿐이다. 이제 그녀는 거의 광기에 사로잡힌 듯 그에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"아, 신부님! 그건 말, 말뿐이라고요! 용서하라니! 저이가 치이지 않았다면, 오늘도 술에 취해 집에 들어와서는, 하나뿐인 셔츠를 더럽고 너덜너덜하게 만들어 놓고는, 통나무처럼 곯아떨어졌겠지요, 그럼 나는 동틀 때까지 물에 담그고 헹구면서 저이의 누더기랑 애들 옷을 빨고, 그걸 창가에서 말리다가, 날이 밝자마자 기워야 했겠지요. 내가 밤을 그렇게 보낸다고요!……, 용서가 다 무슨 소용이람! 난 이미 용서했어요!\"\n\n끔찍하게 텅 빈 기침이 그녀의 말을 끊었다. 그녀는 손수건을 입에 갖다 대더니, 다른 손으로 쑤시는 가슴을 누른 채 그것을 신부에게 내보였다. 손수건은 피로 흥건했다. 신부는 고개를 숙인 채 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n마르멜라도프는 마지막 단말마의 고통 속에 있었다. 그는 다시 자기 위로 몸을 숙인 카테리나 이바노브나의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 못했다. 그는 그녀에게 무언가 말하려고 자꾸만 애썼다. 힘겹게 혀를 놀려 또렷하지 않게 말하기 시작했지만, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그가 용서를 빌고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아채고 단호하게 그를 향해 외쳤다.\n\n\"조용히 해요! 그럴 것 없어요! 당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 다 알아요!\" 그러자 환자는 입을 다물었으나, 바로 그 순간 그의 떠도는 눈길이 문간으로 흘러가 소냐를 보았다.\n\n그때까지 그는 그녀를 알아채지 못하고 있었다. 그녀는 구석의 그늘 속에 서 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"저게 누구야? 저게 누구야?\" 그가 갑자기 동요하며, 걸걸하게 헐떡이는 목소리로, 딸이 서 있는 문 쪽으로 공포에 질려 눈을 돌리며 말했다. 그러고는 몸을 일으키려 했다.\n\n\"누워 있어요! 누워 있으라니까요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다.\n\n그는 부자연스러운 힘으로 팔꿈치를 짚고 몸을 일으키는 데 성공했다. 그는 한동안 딸을 알아보지 못하는 듯, 거칠고 빤히 그녀를 바라보았다. 그는 이제껏 그녀의 그런 차림새를 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 문득 그는 그녀를 알아보았다 — 모욕과 야한 치장 속에 짓눌리고 부끄러워하며, 죽어 가는 아버지에게 작별을 고할 제 차례를 온순히 기다리고 있는 딸을. 그의 얼굴에 극심한 고통이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"소냐! 딸아! 용서해 다오!\" 그가 외치며 그녀에게 손을 내밀려 했으나, 균형을 잃고 소파에서 굴러떨어져 얼굴을 바닥에 박고 엎어졌다. 사람들이 그를 일으키려 달려들어 소파에 다시 눕혔다. 그러나 그는 죽어 가고 있었다. 소냐는 가냘프게 비명을 지르며 달려와 그를 끌어안고는, 그대로 미동도 없이 있었다. 그는 그녀의 품 안에서 숨을 거두었다.\n\n\"바라던 대로 됐구나.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 남편의 시신을 보며 외쳤다. \"그래, 이제 어쩌면 좋아? 무슨 수로 묻어 줘? 내일 저 애들한테 뭘 먹여?\"\n\n라스콜니코프가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"지난주에 부군께서 제게 당신의 살아온 이야기와 사정을 다 들려주셨습니다……. 믿어 주세요, 그분은 당신을 열렬한 경외심으로 말씀하셨어요. 그분이 당신들 모두에게 얼마나 헌신적이었는지, 그리고 그 불행한 약점에도 불구하고 특히 당신을 얼마나 사랑하고 존경했는지 알게 된 그날 저녁부터, 카테리나 이바노브나, 그날 저녁부터 우리는 친구가 됐습니다……. 이제 제가……, 무언가를 하도록 허락해 주십시오……, 죽은 친구에게 진 빚을 갚도록 말입니다. 여기 이십 루블쯤 있습니다 — 이게 당신께 조금이나마 보탬이 된다면, 그럼……, 저는……, 요컨대, 다시 오겠습니다, 꼭 다시 오겠습니다……, 어쩌면 내일 또 올지도 모르고요……. 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n그러고는 그는 군중을 헤치며 계단 쪽으로 비집고 나가, 재빨리 방을 나섰다. 그런데 군중 속에서 그는 불현듯 니코딤 포미치와 부딪혔다. 그는 사고 소식을 듣고 몸소 지시를 내리러 온 참이었다. 두 사람은 경찰서에서의 그 소동 이후로 만난 적이 없었지만, 니코딤 포미치는 그를 단번에 알아보았다.\n\n\"아, 당신이오?\" 그가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"그분은 돌아가셨습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다. \"의사도 신부도 다녀갔고, 모든 게 마땅한 대로 되었습니다. 저 가엾은 부인을 너무 괴롭히지 마세요, 안 그래도 폐병을 앓고 있으니까요. 되도록 기운을 북돋워 주세요……, 당신은 마음씨 고운 분이니까요, 제가 알지요…….\" 그가 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 미소를 띤 채 덧붙였다.\n\n\"한데 당신, 피가 튀어 있구려.\" 니코딤 포미치가 등불 빛에 라스콜니코프의 조끼에 묻은 신선한 핏자국 몇 개를 알아채고 말했다.\n\n\"네……, 온통 피투성이입니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 묘한 태도로 말했다. 그러고는 미소 짓고, 고개를 끄덕이고는 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n그는 천천히, 신중하게 내려갔다, 열에 들떠 있었으나 스스로는 그것을 의식하지 못한 채, 불현듯 마음속에 솟구쳐 오른 삶과 힘에 대한 새롭고도 압도적인 감각에 온통 사로잡혀서. 이 감각은 사형 선고를 받았다가 별안간 사면된 사람의 감각에 견줄 만했다. 계단을 반쯤 내려왔을 때 집으로 돌아가던 신부가 그를 앞질렀다. 라스콜니코프는 그와 말없이 인사를 주고받으며 그를 먼저 보냈다. 그가 막 마지막 계단을 내려서던 참에, 등 뒤에서 빠른 발소리가 들렸다. 누군가 그를 따라잡았다. 폴렌카였다. 그녀는 \"기다려요! 기다려요!\" 하고 부르며 그를 뒤쫓아 달려오고 있었다.\n\n그는 돌아보았다. 그녀는 계단 맨 아래에 서서, 그보다 한 단 위에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 안뜰에서 희미한 빛이 새어 들었다. 라스콜니코프는 그 아이의 야위었지만 예쁘장한 자그마한 얼굴이, 환한 어린아이의 미소를 띠고 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 무언가 전갈을 가지고 그를 뒤쫓아 온 것이었는데, 그것을 전하게 된 것이 분명 기쁜 듯했다.\n\n\"이름이 뭐예요?……, 그리고 어디 사세요?\" 그녀가 숨 가쁜 목소리로 다급하게 물었다.\n\n그는 두 손을 그녀의 어깨에 얹고, 일종의 황홀감에 젖어 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀를 바라보는 것이 그에게는 어찌나 기쁜지, 그 까닭은 그 자신도 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"누가 보냈니?\"\n\n\"소냐 언니가 보냈어요.\" 여자아이가 더욱 환하게 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"소냐 언니가 보냈을 줄 알았다.\"\n\n\"엄마도 보냈어요……, 소냐 언니가 날 보내려고 할 때, 엄마도 다가와서 '얼른 뛰어가, 폴렌카' 하고 말했거든요.\"\n\n\"소냐 언니를 사랑하니?\"\n\n\"누구보다도 사랑해요.\" 폴렌카가 묘하게 진지한 태도로 대답했고, 그녀의 미소는 더 엄숙해졌다.\n\n\"그럼 나도 사랑해 주겠니?\"\n\n대답 대신, 그는 그 여자아이의 얼굴이 자기에게 다가오는 것을, 입맞추려고 통통한 입술을 천진하게 내미는 것을 보았다. 별안간 막대기처럼 가는 그녀의 두 팔이 그를 꼭 끌어안았고, 머리가 그의 어깨에 기대어졌으며, 어린 여자아이는 그에게 얼굴을 묻은 채 가만히 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"아빠가 불쌍해요.\" 잠시 후 그녀가 눈물범벅이 된 얼굴을 들고 손으로 눈물을 훔치며 말했다. \"이제 온통 불행한 일들뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 갑자기, 아이들이 어른처럼 말하고 싶을 때 애써 꾸며 내는 그 유난히 점잖은 태도로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아빠가 너를 사랑하셨니?\"\n\n\"아빠는 리다를 제일 사랑하셨어요.\" 그녀는 미소도 없이, 어른들과 똑같이 아주 진지하게 말을 이었다. \"리다가 어리고, 또 아프기도 해서 사랑하셨어요. 그리고 늘 그 애한테 선물을 가져다주셨어요. 하지만 아빠는 우리한테 읽기를 가르쳐 주셨고, 나한테는 문법이랑 성경도 가르쳐 주셨어요.\" 그녀가 의젓하게 덧붙였다. \"그리고 엄마는 한 번도 아무 말 안 하셨지만, 우린 엄마가 그걸 좋아한다는 걸 알았고, 아빠도 그걸 아셨어요. 그리고 엄마는 나한테 프랑스어를 가르쳐 주고 싶어 하세요, 이제 내 교육을 시작할 때가 됐으니까요.\"\n\n\"넌 기도문도 아니?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 알지요! 오래전부터 알았어요. 난 이제 다 큰 애니까 기도를 혼자 마음속으로 외워요, 콜랴랑 리다는 엄마하고 소리 내어 외우고요. 먼저 '성모송'을 외우고, 그다음에 또 다른 기도를 해요, '주여, 소냐 언니를 용서하시고 축복하소서', 그리고 또 하나, '주여, 우리의 둘째 아버지를 용서하시고 축복하소서.' 우리의 첫째 아버지는 돌아가셨고 이분은 또 다른 아버지거든요, 하지만 우린 그 첫째 아버지를 위해서도 기도해요.\"\n\n\"폴렌카, 내 이름은 로지온이란다. 가끔 나를 위해서도 기도해 주렴. '그리고 당신의 종 로지온을', 그것뿐이면 돼.\"\n\n\"평생토록 아저씨를 위해 기도할게요.\" 여자아이가 열렬하게 단언했고, 갑자기 다시 미소 지으며 그에게 달려들어 또 한 번 따뜻하게 끌어안았다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 그녀에게 자기 이름과 주소를 일러 주고, 이튿날 꼭 오겠다고 약속했다. 아이는 그에게 완전히 매혹되어 떠나갔다. 그가 거리로 나왔을 때는 열 시가 지나 있었다. 오 분 뒤 그는 그 여인이 뛰어들었던 바로 그 자리, 다리 위에 서 있었다.\n\n\"이제 됐어.\" 그가 단호하고 의기양양하게 말했다. \"공상도, 헛된 공포도, 망령도 다 끝장이다! 인생은 현실이야! 내가 방금 살지 않았던가? 나의 삶은 저 노파와 함께 죽어 버린 게 아니야! 그녀에게는 천국을 — 그러니 이제 됐소, 부인, 날 좀 편히 내버려두시오! 이제부터는 이성과 빛의 시대다……, 그리고 의지의, 힘의 시대……, 자, 이제 두고 보자! 우리의 힘을 시험해 보자!\" 그는 마치 어떤 어둠의 힘에 도전하기라도 하듯 도전적으로 덧붙였다. \"그런데도 나는 한 평짜리 공간에서 사는 것에 동의할 작정이었다니!\n\n이 순간 나는 무척 쇠약하지만……, 내 병은 이제 다 끝난 것 같다. 밖에 나올 때부터 끝나리라는 걸 알았어. 그건 그렇고, 포친코프의 집이 몇 걸음밖에 안 떨어져 있군. 가까이 있지 않더라도 라주미힌한테는 꼭 가야겠어……, 그가 내기에서 이기게 해 주자! 그에게도 좀 만족을 주자고 — 상관없어! 힘이야, 사람한테 필요한 건 힘이지, 힘 없이는 아무것도 얻을 수 없어, 그리고 힘은 힘으로 얻어 내야 하는 거야 — 그게 바로 저들이 모르는 거지.\" 그가 자랑스럽고 자신만만하게 덧붙이고는, 맥 빠진 발걸음으로 다리에서 걸어 나왔다. 자긍심과 자신감이 그의 안에서 끊임없이 더 강해졌다. 그는 매 순간 다른 사람이 되어 가고 있었다. 도대체 무슨 일이 일어났기에 그의 안에서 이런 변혁이 일어난 것일까? 그 자신도 알지 못했다. 지푸라기라도 붙드는 사람처럼, 그는 문득 자기 역시 '살 수 있다, 아직 자신에게 삶이 남아 있다, 자신의 삶은 저 노파와 함께 죽어 버린 게 아니다' 하고 느꼈던 것이다. 어쩌면 그는 너무 성급하게 결론을 내린 것인지도 몰랐으나, 그는 그것을 생각하지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 그 애한테 기도할 때 '당신의 종 로지온'을 기억해 달라고 부탁했지.\" 문득 이런 생각이 그를 스쳤다. \"뭐, 그건……, 만일을 위해서였어.\" 그가 덧붙이고는, 자신의 소년 같은 객기에 스스로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그는 더없이 좋은 기분이었다.\n\n그는 라주미힌을 쉽게 찾아냈다. 새 세입자는 이미 포친코프의 집에 알려져 있어서 문지기가 곧바로 길을 일러 주었다. 계단을 반쯤 올라갔을 때, 그는 많은 사람이 모여 떠들썩하게 활기차게 이야기하는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 계단 쪽으로 난 문은 활짝 열려 있었고, 탄성과 논쟁 소리가 들려왔다. 라주미힌의 방은 제법 넓었다. 모인 사람은 열다섯 명쯤이었다. 라스콜니코프는 현관에 멈춰 섰는데, 그곳에서는 집주인의 하녀 둘이 칸막이 뒤에서 사모바르 두 개며 술병, 접시, 그리고 집주인의 부엌에서 가져온 파이와 안주 접시들을 가지고 분주히 움직이고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 사람을 들여보내 라주미힌을 불렀다. 그가 반색하며 뛰어나왔다. 한눈에 보기에도 그는 술을 잔뜩 마신 게 분명했고, 아무리 마셔도 라주미힌이 완전히 취하는 법은 없었지만, 이번에는 눈에 띄게 술기운이 올라 있었다.\n\n\"들어 봐.\" 라스콜니코프가 서둘러 말했다. \"난 그저 네가 내기에서 이겼다는 것, 그리고 사람한테 무슨 일이 안 일어날지는 정말 아무도 모른다는 걸 말하러 온 거야. 난 못 들어가, 너무 쇠약해서 금방 쓰러질 것 같아. 그러니 잘 있어, 안녕! 내일 나한테 와.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야? 내가 집까지 바래다줄게. 네 입으로 쇠약하다면서, 그럼 더더욱…….\"\n\n\"그럼 네 손님들은? 방금 빠끔히 내다본 그 곱슬머리는 누구야?\"\n\n\"그 사람? 누군지 누가 알겠어! 아마 삼촌 친구겠지, 아니면 초대도 안 했는데 온 건지도……. 삼촌은 저 사람들한테 맡겨 두지, 그분은 둘도 없이 귀한 양반이야, 지금 너한테 소개 못 시켜 주는 게 아쉽군. 하지만 이젠 다들 될 대로 되라지! 저들은 날 신경도 안 쓸 테고, 난 바람 좀 쐬야겠어, 마침 네가 딱 맞춰 와 줬어 — 이 분만 더 있었으면 주먹다짐이 났을 거야! 어찌나 허튼소리들을 늘어놓는지……, 사람들이 무슨 말을 지껄여 댈지 넌 상상도 못 할 거야! 하긴 왜 상상을 못 하겠어? 우리도 헛소리를 지껄이지 않나? 그러니 떠들게 내버려둬……, 그래야 안 떠드는 법을 배우지!……, 잠깐만, 조시모프를 데려올게.\"\n\n조시모프는 거의 게걸스럽다시피 라스콜니코프에게 달려들었다. 그는 그에게 각별한 관심을 보였다. 이내 그의 얼굴이 밝아졌다.\n\n\"당장 잠자리에 들어야 합니다.\" 그가 가능한 한 환자를 살펴보고는 말했다. \"그리고 밤에 먹을 걸 좀 들어요. 드시겠어요? 진작에 준비해 뒀거든요……, 가루약입니다.\"\n\n\"두 봉지라도 드리지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다. 가루약을 그 자리에서 먹었다.\n\n\"자네가 그를 집에 데려가는 건 잘된 일이야.\" 조시모프가 라주미힌에게 말했다. \"내일 그가 어떤지 봅시다, 오늘은 전혀 나쁘지 않아 — 낮보다 상당히 좋아졌어. 살다 보면 배우는 법이니…….\"\n\n\"우리가 나올 때 조시모프가 나한테 뭐라고 속삭였는지 알아?\" 둘이 거리로 나서자마자 라주미힌이 불쑥 내뱉었다. \"다는 얘기 안 할게, 형제, 워낙 바보 같은 소리라서. 조시모프가 그러는데, 가는 길에 너랑 허물없이 얘기를 나누면서 너도 나한테 허물없이 털어놓게 만든 다음, 나중에 그걸 자기한테 얘기해 달라는 거야, 글쎄 그자 머릿속에 네가……, 미쳤거나 미치기 직전이라는 생각이 박혀 있거든. 상상이나 되냐! 첫째로, 넌 그자보다 머리가 세 배는 좋고, 둘째로, 네가 미친 게 아니라면 그자가 그런 엉뚱한 생각을 품든 말든 콧방귀도 안 뀌면 그만이고, 셋째로, 전공이 외과인 그 고깃덩어리가 정신병에 푹 빠져 버린 거지, 그리고 그자가 너에 대해 그런 결론을 내리게 된 건 오늘 네가 자메토프랑 나눈 대화 때문이야.\"\n\n\"자메토프가 너한테 다 얘기했어?\"\n\n\"응, 잘했지. 이제 나도 이게 다 무슨 영문인지 알겠고, 자메토프도 알아……. 그러니까, 사실은 말이야, 로쟈……, 요점은……, 나 지금 좀 취했어……. 하지만 그건……, 상관없고……, 요점은 이 생각이……, 알겠어? 저들 머릿속에서 막 부화하고 있었다는 거야……, 알겠어? 그러니까, 아무도 감히 소리 내어 말하진 못했어, 그 생각이 너무 터무니없는 데다, 특히 그 페인트공이 체포된 뒤로는 그 거품이 터져 영영 사라져 버렸으니까. 그런데 저들은 왜 그렇게 바보 같은 거지? 그때 내가 자메토프를 좀 두들겨 줬어 — 이건 우리끼리 얘기야, 형제, 제발 내가 알고 있다는 티는 내지 마, 보니까 그자가 민감한 인물이더라고, 루이자 이바노브나 가게에서였어. 하지만 오늘, 오늘로 다 밝혀졌어. 그 일리야 페트로비치가 죄다 꾸민 거라고! 그자가 네가 경찰서에서 기절한 걸 이용해 먹었지, 하지만 이제 자기도 그걸 부끄러워하고 있어, 나도 알아…….\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 게걸스레 귀를 기울였다. 라주미힌은 입이 너무 헤퍼질 만큼 취해 있었다.\n\n\"그때 내가 기절한 건 너무 답답한 데다 페인트 냄새 때문이었어.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n\"그걸 굳이 설명할 필요 없어! 게다가 페인트 때문만도 아니었어, 열이 한 달째 오르고 있었으니까, 조시모프가 그 증인이야! 근데 그 친구가 지금 얼마나 풀이 죽었는지 넌 믿지 못할 거야! '난 그 사람 새끼손가락만도 못해' 하고 말한다니까. 너 말이야. 그 친구도 가끔은 마음씨가 괜찮아, 형제. 하지만 그 교훈, 오늘 네가 팔레 드 크리스탈에서 그자한테 준 그 교훈, 그건 정말이지 둘도 없이 멋졌어! 처음엔 네가 그자를 겁먹게 했지, 알잖아, 그자가 거의 경련을 일으킬 뻔했다고! 넌 그 끔찍한 허튼소리가 죄다 사실이라고 그자를 다시금 거의 믿게 만들어 놓고는, 별안간 — 그자한테 혀를 쑥 내밀었지. '자, 어때, 이걸 어떻게 생각하나?' 완벽했어! 그자는 이제 짓뭉개지고 박살이 났어! 정말이지 명수다웠어, 맙소사, 저들한테는 그게 딱이지! 아, 내가 그 자리에 없었다니! 그자는 너를 무지하게 보고 싶어 했어. 포르피리도 너랑 사귀고 싶어 하고…….\"\n\n\"아!……, 그 사람도……, 한데 왜 날 미친 사람 취급한 거야?\"\n\n\"아, 미쳤다는 게 아니라. 내가 말이 좀 지나쳤나 봐, 형제……. 그자한테 충격이었던 건 말이야, 알겠지만, 오직 그 주제 하나에만 네가 관심 있어 보였다는 거였어, 이제는 왜 거기에 관심을 가졌는지 분명해졌지, 모든 정황을 알고 보니……, 그게 어떻게 너를 자극했고, 또 네 병하고 맞물려 들어갔는지 말이야……, 나 좀 취했어, 형제, 다만, 젠장, 그자한테는 자기 나름의 생각이 있어서……, 글쎄 그자가 정신병에 미쳐 있다니까. 하지만 넌 그자 신경 쓰지 마…….\"\n\n삼십 초쯤 둘 다 말이 없었다.\n\n\"들어 봐, 라주미힌.\" 라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다. \"너한테 솔직히 말하고 싶어. 난 방금 임종 자리에 있었어, 어떤 관리가 죽었거든……, 거기에 내 돈을 다 줬어……, 게다가 방금 누군가한테 입맞춤을 받았는데, 내가 만일 누구를 죽였다 해도 그 사람은 똑같이……, 사실 거기서 또 다른 누군가를 봤어……, 불꽃 같은 깃털을 단……, 아니 내가 헛소리를 하는군. 난 무척 쇠약해, 좀 부축해 줘……, 곧 계단에 도착할 거야…….\"\n\n\"왜 그래? 도대체 무슨 일이야?\" 라주미힌이 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n\"좀 어지러워, 하지만 그게 문제가 아니야, 너무 슬퍼, 너무 슬프다고……, 여자처럼 말이야. 봐, 저게 뭐지? 봐, 저것 좀 봐!\"\n\n\"뭔데?\"\n\n\"안 보여? 내 방에 불빛이 있잖아, 보이지? 저 틈으로…….\"\n\n그들은 어느새 마지막 계단 층계 밑, 집주인의 문이 있는 높이에 이르러 있었고, 실제로 아래에서도 라스콜니코프의 다락방에 불빛이 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"이상하군! 나스타시야인지도 모르지.\" 라주미힌이 말했다.\n\n\"그 여자는 이 시간에 절대 내 방에 안 와, 진작에 잠자리에 들었을 텐데, 하지만……, 아무래도 상관없어! 잘 있어!\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야? 나도 너랑 같이 들어가, 우리 같이 들어가자고!\"\n\n\"같이 들어갈 거라는 거 알아, 하지만 난 여기서 악수하고 너한테 작별 인사를 하고 싶어. 그러니 손을 줘, 잘 있어!\"\n\n\"너 도대체 왜 그래, 로쟈?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니야……, 가자……, 네가 증인이 돼 줘.\"\n\n그들은 계단을 오르기 시작했고, 라주미힌은 어쩌면 조시모프의 말이 결국 옳을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. \"아, 내 수다로 저 친구를 들쑤셔 놨군!\" 그가 혼자 중얼거렸다.\n\n문 앞에 이르렀을 때 그들은 방 안에서 사람 목소리를 들었다.\n\n\"이게 뭐지?\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다. 라스콜니코프가 먼저 문을 열었다. 그는 문을 활짝 젖히고는, 어안이 벙벙하여 문간에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n그의 어머니와 누이가 그의 소파에 앉아, 한 시간 반째 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그들이 출발했고, 오는 중이며, 곧 도착하리라는 소식을 바로 그날 거듭 들었건만, 어째서 그는 한 번도 그들을 예상하지 못하고, 생각조차 하지 않았던 것일까? 그들은 그 한 시간 반 동안 나스타시야를 붙들고 이것저것 캐물으며 보냈다. 그녀는 그들 앞에 서서 이미 모든 것을 다 이야기한 터였다. 그가 오늘 병든 몸으로, 그녀의 이야기로 미루어 보건대 헛소리까지 하면서 '뛰쳐나갔다'는 말을 듣고 그들은 불안에 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다. \"맙소사, 그애한테 무슨 일이 생긴 거지?\" 두 사람 다 울었고, 두 사람 다 그 한 시간 반 동안 비탄에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n기쁨에 찬, 환희에 찬 외침이 라스콜니코프의 등장을 맞이했다. 두 사람이 그에게 달려들었다. 그러나 그는 죽은 사람처럼 서 있었다. 별안간 견딜 수 없는 감각이 벼락처럼 그를 내리쳤다. 그는 그들을 끌어안으려고 팔을 들지 못했다, 그럴 수가 없었다. 어머니와 누이가 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고, 입맞추고, 웃고 울었다. 그는 한 걸음 내딛더니, 휘청거리다가 바닥에 쓰러져 기절하고 말았다.\n\n불안, 공포의 외침, 신음 소리……. 문간에 서 있던 라주미힌이 방 안으로 날 듯이 뛰어들어, 억센 두 팔로 환자를 붙잡아 순식간에 소파에 눕혔다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 아무것도 아니에요!\" 그가 어머니와 누이에게 외쳤다. \"그저 기절일 뿐, 별것 아닙니다! 방금도 의사가 그가 훨씬 좋아졌다고, 완전히 건강하다고 했는걸요! 물! 보세요, 정신이 돌아오고 있어요, 다시 멀쩡해졌어요!\"\n\n그러고는 두냐의 팔을 어찌나 세게 붙잡았는지 거의 탈구시킬 뻔하면서, 그는 그녀에게 '그가 다시 멀쩡해졌다'는 것을 보라고 몸을 굽히게 했다. 어머니와 누이는 그를 자신들의 섭리처럼 여기며 감격과 감사의 눈길로 바라보았다. 그들은 이미 나스타시야에게서, 그들의 로쟈가 병을 앓는 동안 이 '무척 유능한 젊은이'가 그를 위해 해 준 모든 일을 들어 알고 있었으니, 그날 저녁 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나 라스콜니코바는 두냐와 이야기하면서 그를 그렇게 불렀던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n라스콜니코프는 일어나 소파에 걸터앉았다. 그는 라주미힌이 자기 어머니와 누이에게 쏟아 내던 따뜻하지만 두서없는 위로의 말들을 끊으려는 듯 힘없이 손을 내저었고, 두 사람의 손을 한 손씩 잡고는 일이 분 동안 아무 말 없이 한 사람씩 번갈아 바라보았다. 어머니는 그의 표정에 놀랐다. 거기에는 고통스러우리만치 절절한 감정이, 그러면서도 동시에 무언가 미동도 없는, 거의 광기에 가까운 무언가가 드러나 있었다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 울기 시작했다.\n\n아브도티야 로마노브나는 창백했다. 그녀의 손은 오라비의 손안에서 떨리고 있었다.\n\n\"집에 가세요…… 저 사람이랑.\" 그는 라주미힌을 가리키며 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"내일까지 안녕히…… 내일 모든 것을……. 도착하신 지 오래되셨어요?\"\n\n\"오늘 저녁이란다, 로쟈.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 대답했다. \"기차가 끔찍하게 연착했어. 하지만 로쟈, 그 무엇으로도 지금 너를 두고 갈 수는 없구나! 여기서, 네 곁에서 밤을 새우마…….\"\n\n\"저를 괴롭히지 마세요!\" 그는 짜증스레 손짓하며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 이 친구랑 있을게요.\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다. \"한순간도 곁을 떠나지 않겠습니다. 저쪽 손님들이야 다 집어치우라죠! 실컷 화내라고 내버려 두세요! 거긴 제 삼촌이 주재하고 계시니까요.\"\n\n\"어떻게, 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 다시 한번 라주미힌의 두 손을 꼭 쥐며 말을 꺼냈으나, 라스콜니코프가 또다시 그녀의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"이건 못 견디겠어요! 못 견디겠다고요!\" 그는 신경질적으로 되풀이했다. \"저를 들볶지 마세요! 그만하면 됐어요, 가세요……. 도저히 못 참겠어요!\"\n\n\"가요, 엄마, 잠깐만이라도 방에서 나가요.\" 두냐가 당황하여 속삭였다. \"우리가 오빠를 힘들게 하고 있어요, 그게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"삼 년 만에 아들 얼굴도 못 본단 말이냐?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 울먹였다.\n\n\"잠깐만요.\" 그가 다시 그들을 멈춰 세웠다. \"자꾸 말을 끊으시니까 제 생각이 뒤죽박죽이 돼서요……. 루진은 만나셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니다, 로쟈, 하지만 그분은 벌써 우리가 도착한 걸 알고 계셔. 로쟈, 우리가 들었는데, 표트르 페트로비치가 친절하게도 오늘 너를 찾아왔다더구나.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 다소 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"네…… 그렇게나 친절하셨죠……. 두냐, 나는 루진한테 계단 아래로 던져 버리겠다고 약속하고는 지옥에나 가라고 했어…….\"\n\n\"로쟈, 무슨 소리를 하는 거니! 설마, 우리한테 그런 말을 하려는 건…….\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 놀라서 입을 떼다가, 두냐를 바라보며 말을 멈췄다.\n\n아브도티야 로마노브나는 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 기다리며 오라비를 유심히 바라보았다. 두 사람은 나스타시야에게서 그 다툼에 관한 이야기를 — 그녀가 알아듣고 전할 수 있었던 한도 내에서 — 이미 들은 터라, 괴로운 당혹감과 불안 속에 있었다.\n\n\"두냐.\" 라스콜니코프가 안간힘을 쓰며 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 결혼이 싫어, 그러니 내일 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 루진을 거절해 버려, 다시는 그자 이름을 듣지 않아도 되게.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 부르짖었다.\n\n\"오빠, 무슨 말을 하는지 좀 생각해 봐!\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 발끈해서 말을 시작했으나, 곧바로 스스로를 다잡았다. \"지금은 말할 상태가 아닐지도 몰라, 피곤한 거겠지.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"내가 헛소리를 하는 줄 알아? 아니야……. 너는 나를 위해서 루진과 결혼하려는 거잖아. 하지만 나는 그 희생을 받아들이지 않겠어. 그러니 내일 전에 편지를 써서 그자를 거절해……. 내일 아침에 내가 읽어 보게 해 줘, 그럼 그걸로 끝이야!\"\n\n\"난 그렇게는 못 해!\" 처녀가 모욕감에 외쳤다. \"오빠가 무슨 권리로…….\"\n\n\"두냐, 너도 너무 성급하구나, 조용히 하렴, 내일……. 너도 알다시피…….\" 어머니가 당황하여 끼어들었다. \"차라리 나가는 게 낫겠다!\"\n\n\"오빠가 헛소리를 하는 겁니다.\" 라주미힌이 취기 어린 목소리로 외쳤다. \"안 그러면 어떻게 감히 그런 소릴! 내일이면 이 모든 헛소리가 다 사라질 겁니다……. 오늘 그가 확실히 그자를 쫓아낸 건 맞아요. 그건 그래요. 그래서 루진도 화가 났죠……. 여기서 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며 제 학식을 자랑하려 들더니, 풀이 죽어서 나가 버렸지요…….\"\n\n\"그럼 사실이란 말인가요?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다.\n\n\"내일까지 안녕히, 오빠.\" 두냐가 가엾다는 듯 말했다. \"가요, 엄마……. 잘 있어, 로쟈.\"\n\n\"내 말 들었니, 누이야.\" 그가 마지막 힘을 쥐어짜며 그들의 등 뒤에 대고 되풀이했다. \"나는 헛소리를 하는 게 아니야. 이 결혼은 — 치욕이야. 나야 비열한 놈처럼 굴어도 되지만, 너는 안 돼……. 한 사람으로 충분해……. 그리고 내가 비열한 놈이라 해도, 그런 누이는 인정하지 않겠어. 나냐, 루진이냐 둘 중 하나야! 이제 가…….\"\n\n\"하지만 자넨 제정신이 아니야! 폭군 같으니!\" 라주미힌이 고함쳤다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 대답하지 않았고, 어쩌면 대답할 수도 없었을 것이다. 그는 완전히 기진맥진한 채 소파에 누워 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 흥미로운 눈길로 라주미힌을 바라보았다. 그녀의 검은 눈이 번득였고, 라주미힌은 그 눈길에 그만 움찔하고 말았다.\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 망연자실한 채 서 있었다.\n\n\"그 무엇으로도 저는 갈 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 절망에 빠져 라주미힌에게 속삭였다. \"여기 어디서라도 머물겠어요……. 두냐만 집까지 데려다주세요.\"\n\n\"그러시면 모든 걸 망치게 됩니다.\" 라주미힌이 인내심을 잃고 똑같이 속삭이며 대답했다. \"어쨌든 계단으로 좀 나가시죠. 나스타시야, 불 좀 비춰 줘! 정말입니다.\" 그는 계단에서 반쯤 속삭이는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"오늘 오후엔 그가 의사와 저를 거의 두들겨 팰 뻔했다니까요! 아시겠어요? 의사를요, 의사를! 그 양반도 그를 자극하지 않으려고 한발 물러나 나가 버렸어요. 저는 아래층에서 망을 보고 있었는데, 그가 곧장 옷을 챙겨 입고는 빠져나가 버렸지 뭡니까. 어머님이 그를 자극하시면 이 야밤에 또 빠져나가 무슨 일을 저지를지 모릅니다…….\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"게다가 아브도티야 로마노브나를 어머님 없이 그런 하숙집에 둘 수는 없어요. 두 분이 묵으시는 데가 어떤 곳인지 생각해 보세요! 그 못된 표트르 페트로비치가 그보다 더 나은 숙소를 못 구해 줬다니……. 하지만 아시다시피 제가 술을 좀 걸쳤거든요, 그래서 이렇게…… 욕지거리를 하는 겁니다. 괘념치 마세요…….\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 여기 집주인 여자한테 가 보겠어요.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 고집스레 말했다. \"두냐와 제가 오늘 밤 묵을 자리 한구석이라도 마련해 달라고 사정해 보겠어요. 저는 저 애를 저렇게 두고 갈 수가 없어요, 그럴 수가 없다고요!\"\n\n이 대화는 집주인 여자의 방문 바로 앞 층계참에서 이루어졌다. 나스타시야가 한 계단 아래에서 그들에게 불을 비추어 주었다. 라주미힌은 유난히 흥분해 있었다. 반 시간 전, 라스콜니코프를 집으로 데려올 때만 해도 그는 확실히 말이 지나치게 많기는 했으나, 그 자신도 그걸 알고 있었고, 엄청난 양을 들이켰음에도 머리만은 멀쩡했다. 그런데 지금은 거의 황홀경에 가까운 상태에 빠져, 마신 술이 죄다 갑절의 효력으로 머리로 솟구치는 듯했다. 그는 두 부인 곁에 서서 양쪽의 손을 다 움켜쥐고는 설득하며 놀랍도록 거침없는 말투로 이런저런 이유를 늘어놓았는데, 한마디 한마디 할 때마다, 아마도 자기주장에 힘을 싣기 위해서인지, 두 사람의 손을 바이스로 죄듯 아프게 짓눌렀다. 그는 예의범절 따위는 조금도 아랑곳없이 아브도티야 로마노브나를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 두 사람은 이따금 그의 크고 앙상한 손아귀에서 손을 빼냈지만, 그는 무슨 영문인지 알아차리기는커녕 오히려 그들을 더 바싹 끌어당겼다. 만일 그들이 그에게 계단에서 머리부터 거꾸로 뛰어내리라고 했다면, 그는 그들을 위해 한 치의 생각이나 망설임도 없이 그렇게 했으리라. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 이 청년이 정말이지 너무 별나고 자기 손을 너무 아프게 쥔다고 느꼈으나, 로쟈에 대한 근심으로 그의 존재를 하늘이 내려 준 도움처럼 여겼던 터라, 그의 온갖 유별난 점을 짐짓 모른 체하려 했다. 한편 아브도티야 로마노브나도 그 근심을 함께 나누고 있었고 겁이 많은 성품도 아니었지만, 그의 눈에 타오르는 빛을 놀라움과 거의 두려움 없이는 바라볼 수 없었다. 나스타시야가 들려준, 오라비의 괴짜 친구에 관한 이야기가 안겨 준 무한한 신뢰만이 아니었다면, 그녀는 그에게서 달아나려 했을 것이고, 어머니에게도 그러자고 설득했을 것이다. 게다가 그녀는 지금 와서는 달아나는 것조차 어쩌면 불가능하리라는 것도 알아차렸다. 그러나 십 분쯤 지나자 그녀는 상당히 안심하게 되었으니, 어떤 기분에 사로잡혀 있든 자기 본성을 단번에 드러내 보여 사람들로 하여금 자기가 상대하는 인물이 어떤 부류인지 금세 알게 하는 것이 라주미힌의 특징이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"집주인 여자한테 가시면 안 됩니다, 그건 정말 말도 안 되는 소리예요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"어머님이 여기 머무르시면, 비록 그의 어머니이시라 해도, 그를 광란에 빠뜨리실 거고, 그러면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 누가 알겠습니까! 들어 보세요, 제가 어떻게 할지 말씀드리죠. 지금은 나스타시야가 그와 함께 있을 테고, 제가 두 분을 집까지 모셔다드리겠습니다, 두 분이 길거리에 혼자 다니실 수는 없으니까요. 페테르부르크는 그런 점에선 끔찍한 곳이거든요……. 하지만 그건 됐고요! 그런 다음 제가 곧장 여기로 달려와서, 명예를 걸고 십오 분 뒤엔 그가 어떤지, 자고 있는지 어떤지 죄다 소식을 전해 드리겠습니다. 그러고 나선, 들어 보세요! 그러고 나선 눈 깜짝할 새에 제 집으로 달려가겠어요 — 거기 친구들이 한 무리 있는데, 죄다 취해 있죠 — 조시모프를 데려오겠습니다 — 그가 바로 그를 돌보는 의사인데, 그 자리에도 있긴 하지만 취하진 않았어요. 그는 취하지 않았어요, 절대 취하는 법이 없죠! 그를 끌고 로쟈한테 갔다가 다시 두 분께 데려오겠습니다, 그러면 한 시간 안에 두 가지 보고를 받으시는 거예요 — 의사한테서, 아시겠죠, 의사 본인한테서요, 그건 제가 그에 대해 전하는 것하곤 전혀 다른 얘기죠! 만약 뭔가 잘못된 게 있으면, 맹세코 제가 직접 어머님을 여기로 모셔 오겠습니다. 하지만 다 괜찮으면 주무세요. 저는 여기 복도에서 밤을 새우겠습니다, 그는 제가 있는 줄도 모를 거예요, 그리고 조시모프한테는 가까이 있도록 집주인 여자 방에서 자라고 하겠습니다. 그에게 어느 쪽이 더 낫겠습니까, 어머님입니까 의사입니까? 그러니 집으로 가시죠! 하지만 집주인 여자한테 가시는 건 말도 안 됩니다. 저야 괜찮지만 두 분께는 어림없는 일이에요. 그 여자가 두 분을 받아 주지 않을 겁니다, 왜냐면 그 여자는…… 바보거든요……. 저 때문에 아브도티야 로마노브나를 질투할 거고, 알고 싶으시다면, 어머님까지도요……. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 확실히 그럴 거예요. 그 여자는 정말이지, 정말이지 종잡을 수 없는 인간이거든요! 하지만 저도 바보고요!…… 됐어요! 가시죠! 저를 믿으십니까? 자, 저를 믿으십니까, 안 믿으십니까?\"\n\n\"가요, 엄마.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 말했다. \"이분은 분명 약속한 대로 하실 거예요. 이미 로쟈를 구해 주셨고, 의사 선생님이 정말로 여기서 밤을 새우는 데 동의하신다면, 그보다 더 좋은 게 어디 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"그것 보세요, 당신은…… 당신은…… 저를 이해하시는군요, 당신은 천사니까요!\" 라주미힌이 황홀해하며 외쳤다. \"가시죠! 나스타시야! 위층으로 날아가서 불을 켜고 그 친구 곁에 앉아 있어, 십오 분 뒤에 갈 테니까.\"\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 완전히 납득한 것은 아니었으나, 더는 버티지 않았다. 라주미힌은 두 사람에게 한 팔씩 내주고는 계단 아래로 이끌었다. 그녀는 여전히 그가 마음에 걸렸으니, 비록 그가 유능하고 마음씨가 좋다 한들 과연 자기 약속을 지킬 수 있을까? 그가 저런 상태이고 보면…….\n\n\"아, 어머님이 제가 저런 상태라고 여기시는 게 보이네요!\" 라주미힌이 그녀의 생각을 알아채고는 끼어들며, 보도를 성큼성큼 걸어가는 바람에 두 부인은 그를 따라잡기가 버거울 지경이었으나, 정작 그는 그 사실을 알아차리지 못했다. \"당치도 않습니다! 그러니까…… 제가 바보처럼 취하긴 했지만, 그게 아니라요. 저는 술 때문에 취한 게 아닙니다. 두 분을 뵌 게 제 정신을 홀딱 빼앗아 가서……. 하지만 저한테 신경 쓰지 마세요! 괘념치 마세요. 저는 허튼소리를 지껄이고 있고, 두 분께 어울릴 자격이 없어요……. 저는 두 분께 도무지 어울리지 않는 인간입니다! 두 분을 집에 모셔다드리는 그 순간, 저는 여기 도랑에서 머리에 물을 두어 양동이 끼얹을 거고, 그러면 멀쩡해질 겁니다……. 제가 두 분을 얼마나 사랑하는지 아신다면! 웃지도, 화내지도 마세요! 누구한테든 화를 내셔도 좋지만, 저한테만은 안 됩니다! 저는 그의 친구고, 그러니 두 분의 친구이기도 합니다, 그러고 싶어요……. 저는 예감이 있었어요……. 작년에 어떤 순간이…… 하긴 사실 예감이랄 것도 아니었죠, 두 분은 마치 하늘에서 떨어진 것 같으니까요. 그리고 저는 아마 밤새 한숨도 못 잘 겁니다……. 조시모프가 조금 전에 그가 미쳐 버릴까 봐 걱정하더군요……. 그래서 그를 자극해선 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 어머니가 외쳤다.\n\n\"정말 의사 선생님이 그렇게 말했나요?\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"네, 하지만 그렇진 않아요, 전혀 그렇지 않습니다. 그가 그에게 약을, 가루약을 줬는데, 제가 봤어요, 그러고 나서 두 분이 여기 오신 거고요……. 아! 두 분이 내일 오셨더라면 더 좋았을 텐데. 우리가 나온 게 잘된 일이에요. 그리고 한 시간 뒤면 조시모프가 직접 모든 걸 보고드릴 겁니다. 그는 취하지 않았어요! 그리고 저도 취하지 않을 거고요……. 그런데 제가 왜 이렇게 곤드레가 됐을까요? 그자들이 저를 논쟁에 끌어들였거든요, 빌어먹을 놈들! 다시는 논쟁 따위 안 하겠다고 맹세했는데! 그자들은 어찌나 헛소리를 지껄이는지! 하마터면 주먹다짐을 할 뻔했어요! 그래서 삼촌한테 주재를 맡기고 와 버렸죠. 믿으시겠어요, 그자들은 개성을 완전히 없애야 한다고 우기는데, 바로 그걸 즐긴단 말입니다! 자기 자신이 되지 않는 것, 될 수 있는 한 자기 자신과 닮지 않는 것 말이에요. 그자들은 그걸 진보의 최고 경지로 여깁니다. 그 헛소리가 그자들 자신의 것이기라도 하면 모를까, 그런데 실상은…….\"\n\n\"저기요!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 머뭇거리며 끼어들었으나, 그것은 도리어 불에 기름을 붓는 격이었다.\n\n\"어떻게 생각하세요?\" 라주미힌이 그 어느 때보다 큰 소리로 고함쳤다. \"제가 그자들이 헛소리를 한다고 공격하는 줄 아세요? 천만에요! 저는 그자들이 헛소리하는 게 좋습니다. 그건 인간이 만물에 대해 지닌 유일한 특권이니까요. 오류를 거쳐서 진리에 이르는 겁니다! 저는 오류를 범하니까 인간인 거예요! 열네 번, 어쩌면 백열네 번쯤 실수를 저지르지 않고서는 그 어떤 진리에도 다다르지 못합니다. 그것도 그 나름으로 멋진 일이죠. 그런데 우리는 우리 힘으로 실수조차 저지르질 못해요! 헛소리를 하되, 자기 자신의 헛소리를 하라, 그러면 그것 때문에 입을 맞춰 주겠어요. 자기 식대로 잘못 가는 것이 남의 식대로 옳게 가는 것보다 낫습니다. 전자의 경우엔 인간이지만, 후자의 경우엔 새만도 못한 거예요. 진리는 달아나지 않지만, 삶은 옴짝달싹 못 하게 옥죄일 수 있습니다. 그런 예가 있었죠. 그런데 우리가 지금 뭘 하고 있습니까? 학문, 발전, 사상, 발명, 이상, 목표, 자유주의, 판단, 경험, 그 모든 것, 모든 것, 모든 것에서 우리는 여전히 학교 예비반 신세란 말입니다! 우리는 남의 사상에 얹혀 사는 걸 더 좋아해요, 그게 우리한테 익숙하니까요! 제 말이 맞죠, 맞죠?\" 라주미힌이 두 부인의 손을 짓누르고 흔들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 저런, 저는 잘 모르겠네요.\" 가엾은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 부르짖었다.\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아요…… 모든 면에서 동의하는 건 아니지만요.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 진지하게 덧붙였다가, 그가 손을 어찌나 아프게 쥐었던지 그만 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"그렇죠, 당신이 그렇다고 하시니…… 자, 그러고 나면 당신은…… 당신은…….\" 그가 도취되어 외쳤다. \"당신은 선함과 순결함과 분별력의…… 그리고 완벽함의 샘이에요. 손을 주세요…… 당신도 손을 주세요! 저는 지금 여기서, 무릎을 꿇고 두 분의 손에 입을 맞추고 싶습니다…….\" 그러고는 보도 위에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었는데, 다행히도 그 시각엔 인적이 없었다.\n\n\"그만하세요, 부탁이에요, 지금 뭐 하시는 거예요?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 몹시 당황하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"일어나세요, 일어나요!\" 두냐가 웃으며 말했으나, 그녀 역시 당혹스러워했다.\n\n\"두 분 손에 입 맞추게 해 주시기 전엔 무슨 일이 있어도 안 됩니다! 됐어요! 그만하죠! 일어나서 다시 갑시다! 저는 운도 지지리 없는 바보고, 두 분께 어울리지 않는데다 취하기까지 했죠…… 그래서 부끄럽습니다……. 저는 두 분을 사랑할 자격이 없지만, 두 분께 경의를 바치는 건 완전한 짐승이 아닌 한 모든 사내의 의무예요! 그래서 저도 경의를 바친 거고요……. 여기가 두 분 숙소군요, 그리고 그 한 가지만으로도 로쟈가 두 분의 표트르 페트로비치를 쫓아낸 건 옳았어요……. 그자가 어떻게 감히! 어떻게 감히 두 분을 이런 숙소에 두냔 말입니다! 이건 망신이에요! 여기서 어떤 부류의 인간들을 받아 주는지 아세요? 그런데 두 분이 그자의 약혼녀라니! 당신이 그의 약혼녀이신가요? 그렇죠? 그렇다면, 말씀드리는데, 당신의 약혼자는 비열한 놈입니다.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 라주미힌 씨, 잊으신 모양인데…….\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"네, 네, 맞습니다, 제가 그만 도를 넘었네요, 부끄럽습니다.\" 라주미힌이 황급히 사과했다. \"하지만…… 하지만 제가 이렇게 말한다고 화내실 순 없어요! 저는 진심으로 말하는 거지, 그게…… 흠, 흠! 그건 수치스러운 일일 테니까요. 사실 그게 제가…… 흠! 뭐, 어쨌든 이유는 말하지 않겠습니다, 감히 그럴 수 없으니……. 하지만 오늘 그가 들어왔을 때 우리 모두 봤죠, 저 사람은 우리 부류가 아니란 걸. 이발소에서 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 지지고 와서가 아니라, 제 재치를 그렇게 서둘러 뽐내려 들어서가 아니라, 그가 첩자이고 투기꾼이고, 구두쇠에다 어릿광대이기 때문이에요. 그건 명백합니다. 그가 영리하다고 여기세요? 아니요, 그자는 바보예요, 바보. 그런데 그자가 당신께 어울리는 짝이라고요? 맙소사! 보세요, 부인들.\" 그가 두 사람의 방으로 올라가는 도중에 별안간 멈춰 섰다. \"저쪽에 있는 제 친구들은 죄다 취했어도 죄다 정직한 인간들이고, 우리가 허튼소리를 잔뜩 지껄이고 저도 그러긴 하지만, 그래도 우리는 마침내 우리 말로써 진리에 다다를 겁니다, 우리는 옳은 길 위에 있으니까요. 반면에 표트르 페트로비치는…… 옳은 길 위에 있지 않아요. 제가 방금 그자들한테 온갖 욕을 퍼붓긴 했지만, 저는 그자들 모두를 존경합니다……. 자메토프는 존경하진 않아도 좋아하긴 해요, 강아지 같은 녀석이니까요, 그리고 저 황소 같은 조시모프는 정직한 인간이고 제 일을 잘 알기 때문에 좋아하고요. 하지만 됐어요, 다 말했고 다 용서됐죠. 용서됐나요? 자, 그럼 마저 갑시다. 저는 이 복도를 압니다, 와 본 적이 있거든요, 여기 3호실에서 한바탕 소동이 있었죠……. 두 분은 여기 어디시죠? 몇 호인가요? 8호? 자, 그럼 오늘 밤은 안에서 문을 잠그세요. 아무도 들이지 마시고요. 십오 분 뒤에 소식을 갖고 돌아오겠습니다, 그리고 반 시간 뒤엔 조시모프를 데려오겠어요, 두고 보세요! 안녕히, 저는 뛰어가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 두냐, 무슨 일이 벌어질까?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 근심과 당혹감에 싸여 딸에게 말했다.\n\n\"걱정 마세요, 엄마.\" 두냐가 모자와 망토를 벗으며 말했다. \"비록 술자리에서 오긴 했지만, 하느님이 우리를 도우라고 이분을 보내신 거예요. 우린 저분을 믿어도 돼요, 정말이에요. 게다가 저분이 로쟈를 위해 해 준 그 모든 일을 보면…….\"\n\n\"아, 두냐, 저분이 정말 올지는 하느님만 아실 거다! 내가 어떻게 로쟈를 두고 올 수 있었을까?…… 그리고 이 만남이 얼마나, 얼마나 다를 거라고 상상했던지! 그 애는 어찌나 시무룩했는지, 우리를 보고도 반갑지 않은 것처럼…….\"\n\n그녀의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다.\n\n\"아니에요, 그런 게 아니에요, 엄마. 엄마는 못 보셨죠, 내내 울고 계셨으니까요. 오빠는 심각한 병으로 완전히 정신이 무너진 거예요 — 그게 이유라고요.\"\n\n\"아, 그 병! 무슨 일이 벌어질까, 무슨 일이 벌어질까? 그리고 그 애가 너한테 어떻게 말했는지, 두냐!\" 어머니는 딸의 속내를 읽어 내려 머뭇머뭇 그녀를 바라보며 말했으나, 두냐가 오라비를 두둔하고 나선 것에 — 그건 그녀가 이미 그를 용서했다는 뜻이었으므로 — 벌써 반쯤은 위안을 얻고 있었다. \"그 앤 내일이면 분명 다시 생각을 고쳐먹을 거다.\" 그녀가 딸의 속을 더 떠보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그리고 저는 오빠가 내일도 똑같이 말할 거라고 확신해요…… 그 일에 대해선.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 마침내 말했다. 그리고 물론, 그 이상으로 나아갈 수는 없었으니, 그것은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 입에 올리기 두려워하던 화제였기 때문이다. 두냐는 다가가 어머니에게 입을 맞췄다. 어머니는 아무 말 없이 그녀를 따뜻하게 끌어안았다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 라주미힌이 돌아오기를 애타게 기다리며 앉아, 팔짱을 낀 채 생각에 잠겨 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있는 딸을 머뭇머뭇 바라보았다. 생각에 잠길 때면 이렇게 방 안을 거니는 것이 아브도티야 로마노브나의 버릇이었고, 어머니는 그런 순간에 딸의 기분을 깨뜨릴까 늘 두려워했다.\n\n물론 라주미힌이 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 별안간 취중에 빠져든 그 연정은 우스꽝스러웠다. 그러나 그의 별난 상태를 차치하더라도, 많은 이들은 아브도티야 로마노브나를, 특히 그녀가 팔짱을 낀 채 수심에 잠겨 우울하게 이리저리 거니는 그 순간의 모습을 보았다면, 그럴 만도 하다고 여겼을 것이다. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 눈에 띄게 아름다웠다. 키가 크고, 놀랍도록 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 강인하고 자신만만했는데 — 마지막의 그 자질은 모든 몸짓에서 드러났으나, 그렇다고 해서 그녀 동작의 우아함과 부드러움을 조금도 해치지는 않았다. 얼굴은 오라비를 닮았으나, 정말로 아름답다고 할 만했다. 머리칼은 짙은 갈색으로 오라비보다 조금 더 밝았고, 거의 검은 두 눈에는 도도한 빛이 어렸으면서도 때로는 유난한 다정함이 깃들곤 했다. 창백했지만, 그것은 건강한 창백함이었다. 얼굴은 싱그러움과 생기로 환히 빛났다. 입은 다소 작은 편이었고, 도톰한 붉은 아랫입술이 턱과 더불어 조금 앞으로 도드라져 있었는데, 그것이 그 아름다운 얼굴에서 유일하게 고르지 못한 점이었으나, 도리어 그 얼굴에 유난히 개성 있고 거의 오만하기까지 한 표정을 부여했다. 그녀의 얼굴은 언제나 즐겁다기보다는 더 진지하고 사색적이었으나, 그 얼굴에 미소가, 젊고 가뿐하고 거리낌 없는 웃음이 얼마나 잘 어울리던지! 라주미힌처럼 따뜻하고 솔직하고 순박하고 정직한 거인이, 일찍이 그녀 같은 사람을 본 적이 없는데다 그 당시 술까지 얼근하게 취해 있었으니, 단번에 정신을 잃어버린 것도 무리는 아니었다. 게다가 우연찮게도, 그는 오라비를 향한 사랑과 그를 만난 기쁨으로 환히 변모한 두냐의 모습을 처음으로 보았던 것이다. 이어서 그는 오라비의 무례하고 잔인하고 배은망덕한 말에 그녀의 아랫입술이 분노로 떨리는 것을 보았으니 — 그의 운명은 그것으로 결판이 났다.\n\n게다가 그가 계단에서 취중에 불쑥 내뱉었던 말, 즉 라스콜니코프의 별난 집주인 여자 프라스코비야 파블로브나가 자기 때문에 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나뿐 아니라 아브도티야 로마노브나까지 질투하리라는 그 말은 진실이었다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 마흔셋이었지만, 그 얼굴에는 여전히 한때의 미모의 흔적이 남아 있었고, 사실 나이보다 훨씬 젊어 보였는데, 늙도록 마음의 평온과 감수성, 순수하고 진실한 가슴의 온기를 간직한 여인들이 거의 언제나 그렇듯 말이다. 덧붙여 말하자면, 이 모든 것을 지키는 것이야말로 늙도록 아름다움을 간직하는 유일한 방법이다. 그녀의 머리칼은 희끗희끗 세고 숱이 적어지기 시작했고, 눈가에는 잔주름이 진 지 오래였으며, 두 뺨은 근심과 슬픔으로 홀쭉하게 꺼졌으나, 그래도 그것은 고운 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 스무 살 더 먹은 두냐, 그러나 도드라진 아랫입술만은 없는 두냐, 바로 그 자체였다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 다정다감했으나 감상적이지는 않았고, 소심하고 양보를 잘했으나 그것도 어느 선까지였다. 그녀는 자기 신념에 어긋나는 일조차 상당 부분 양보하고 받아들일 수 있었지만, 정직과 원칙과 가장 깊은 신념이 그어 놓은 어떤 경계만은 그 무엇으로도 넘게 할 수 없었다.\n\n라주미힌이 떠난 지 정확히 이십 분 뒤, 문을 두드리는 나직하지만 다급한 소리가 두 번 들렸다. 그가 돌아온 것이다.\n\n\"안 들어가겠습니다, 시간이 없어서요.\" 문이 열리자 그가 황급히 말했다. \"그는 통나무처럼 곤히, 조용히 잠들었어요, 부디 열 시간은 자기를 하느님께 빕니다. 나스타시야가 그와 함께 있고요, 제가 올 때까지 떠나지 말라고 해 뒀습니다. 이제 조시모프를 데리러 가는데, 그가 두 분께 보고할 거고, 그러고 나면 두 분도 주무시는 게 좋겠어요. 보아하니 두 분 다 너무 지치셔서 아무것도 못 하실 것 같으니…….\"\n\n그러고는 복도를 따라 달려가 버렸다.\n\n\"정말 유능하고…… 헌신적인 청년이로구나!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 더없이 기뻐하며 외쳤다.\n\n\"훌륭한 분 같아요!\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 다소 온기를 담아 대답하고는, 다시 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다.\n\n거의 한 시간이 지나서야 복도에서 발소리가 들리고 또 한 번 문 두드리는 소리가 났다. 이번에는 두 여인 모두 라주미힌의 약속을 온전히 믿으며 기다렸고, 그는 정말로 조시모프를 데려오는 데 성공했다. 조시모프는 라스콜니코프에게 가기 위해 술자리를 버리는 데는 단번에 동의했으나, 들떠 있는 라주미힌을 믿지 못한 탓에 마지못해, 그것도 잔뜩 미심쩍어하며 두 부인을 만나러 왔다. 그러나 그의 허영심은 곧바로 안심하고 흡족해졌다. 그녀들이 정말로 그를 신탁이라도 되는 양 기다리고 있었음을 알아차린 것이다. 그는 딱 십 분만 머물렀으나 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나를 완전히 납득시키고 안심시키는 데 성공했다. 그는 두드러진 동정을 담아 말하면서도, 중대한 진찰에 임한 젊은 의사다운 절제와 극도의 진지함을 유지했다. 그는 다른 어떤 화제에 대해서도 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았고, 두 부인과 좀 더 사사로운 관계를 맺으려는 기색도 조금도 드러내지 않았다. 처음 들어설 때 아브도티야 로마노브나의 눈부신 미모를 알아챘으면서도, 방문하는 동안 그녀에게는 도무지 눈길을 주지 않으려 애쓰며 오로지 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나에게만 말을 건넸다. 이 모든 것이 그에게 유별난 내적 만족을 안겨 주었다. 그는 환자가 지금으로선 아주 만족스럽게 회복되어 가고 있다고 보인다고 단언했다. 그의 관찰에 따르면 환자의 병은 한편으로는 지난 몇 달간의 불운한 물질적 환경 탓이었으나, 다른 한편으로는 정신적인 기원도 있어서, \"이를테면, 여러 물질적·정신적 영향과 근심, 불안, 고민, 어떤 관념들…… 등등의 산물\"이라고 했다. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 자기 말을 바싹 주의를 기울여 따라오고 있음을 슬그머니 알아챈 조시모프는 이 주제에 대해 좀 더 늘어놓는 것을 자신에게 허락했다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 근심스럽고 머뭇거리는 어조로 \"혹시 광기가 의심되지는 않는지\" 묻자, 그는 침착하고 솔직한 미소를 띠며 자기 말이 과장되었다고, 물론 환자가 어떤 고정관념을, 편집광에 가까운 무언가를 — 그, 조시모프는 지금 의학의 바로 이 흥미로운 분야를 특히 연구하는 중이었다 — 지니고 있긴 하지만, 환자가 오늘까지 섬망 상태에 있었음을 잊어선 안 되며…… 또한 의심할 여지 없이 가족의 존재가 그의 회복에 호의적인 영향을 미쳐 그의 마음을 흐트러뜨려 줄 거라고, \"다만 모든 새로운 충격을 피할 수만 있다면\" 하고 의미심장하게 덧붙였다. 그러고는 일어나 인상적이고 다정한 인사로 작별을 고했고, 그 위로 축복과 따뜻한 감사와 간청이 쏟아졌으며, 아브도티야 로마노브나는 자청해서 그에게 손을 내밀었다. 그는 자기 방문에 더없이 흡족해하며, 자기 자신에 대해서는 그보다 더 흡족해하며 나갔다.\n\n\"내일 얘기하죠, 지금은 곧장 주무세요!\" 라주미힌이 조시모프를 따라 나가며 마무리로 말했다. \"내일 아침 되도록 일찍 보고를 들고 두 분께 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"매력적인 아가씨로군, 아브도티야 로마노브나 말이야.\" 둘이 거리로 나서자 조시모프가 거의 입맛을 다시듯 한마디 했다.\n\n\"매력적이라고? 매력적이라고 했어?\" 라주미힌이 고함을 지르며 조시모프에게 달려들어 멱살을 움켜쥐었다. \"네가 감히 한 번만 더……. 알아들었어? 알아들었냐고?\" 그가 멱살을 흔들며 벽으로 밀어붙이고 소리쳤다. \"들었어?\"\n\n\"이거 놔, 이 술 취한 악마야.\" 조시모프가 버둥거리며 말했고, 라주미힌이 손을 놓자 그를 빤히 쳐다보더니 별안간 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다. 라주미힌은 침울하고 진지한 상념에 잠긴 채 그를 마주하고 서 있었다.\n\n\"물론, 나야 멍청한 놈이지.\" 그가 먹구름처럼 어두운 얼굴로 말했다. \"하지만 그래도…… 너도 마찬가지야.\"\n\n\"아니, 형제, 전혀 그런 부류가 아니지. 나는 그런 어리석은 꿈은 꾸지도 않으니까.\"\n\n그들은 말없이 걸었고, 라스콜니코프의 숙소에 가까워졌을 때에야 비로소 라주미힌이 적잖은 근심에 싸여 침묵을 깼다.\n\n\"들어 봐.\" 그가 말했다. \"넌 일류 사내지만, 다른 결점들 가운데서도 넌 방탕한 놈이야, 그건 내가 알지, 게다가 추잡한 놈이고. 넌 나약하고 신경질적인 한심한 작자에다 변덕덩어리야, 살이 오르고 게을러져서 뭐 하나 절제할 줄을 모르지 — 그리고 나는 그걸 추잡하다고 부르는데, 그게 사람을 곧장 진흙탕으로 끌고 가기 때문이야. 넌 자신을 어찌나 풀어 놓았는지, 네가 아직도 어떻게 훌륭한, 심지어 헌신적인 의사로 남아 있는지 모르겠다니까. 너 같은 의사가 — 깃털 침대에서 자다가도 밤중에 환자한테 가려고 일어나잖아! 앞으로 삼사 년만 더 지나면 넌 환자를 위해 일어나지도 않을 거야……. 하지만 빌어먹을, 그게 요점이 아니지!…… 넌 오늘 밤 여기 집주인 여자 방에서 자게 될 거야. (그 여자를 설득하느라 얼마나 애를 먹었는지 몰라!) 그리고 나는 부엌에 있을 거고. 그러니 너한테 그 여자를 좀 더 잘 알 기회가 생긴 거지……. 네가 생각하는 그런 게 아니야! 그런 종류의 일 따위는 흔적도 없어, 형제……!\"\n\n\"하지만 난 그런 생각 안 한다고!\"\n\n\"여기엔 정숙함이 있어, 형제, 침묵, 수줍음, 거친 미덕이……. 그런데도 한숨을 쉬고 밀랍처럼 녹아내린다니까, 그저 녹아내려! 그 여자한테서 날 좀 구해 줘, 세상의 온갖 불경한 것에 걸고 빌건대! 그 여잔 어찌나 사람 마음을 끄는지……. 보답할게, 뭐든 해 줄 테니까…….\"\n\n조시모프는 그 어느 때보다 격렬하게 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"이런, 너 단단히 반했구나! 그런데 내가 그 여자를 어쩌란 말이냐?\"\n\n\"별 수고도 안 들어, 정말이야. 그 여자 곁에 앉아서 얘기만 하면 되니까, 네 멋대로 무슨 허튼소리든 지껄여도 돼. 너도 의사잖아, 무슨 병이든 하나 고쳐 보라고. 맹세코 후회하진 않을 거야. 그 여잔 피아노가 있는데, 너도 알다시피 나도 좀 칠 줄 알거든. 내가 거기서 부르는 노래가 하나 있어, 진짜배기 러시아 노래로, '나는 뜨거운 눈물을 흘리네' 하는 거지. 그 여잔 진짜배기를 좋아해 — 그래, 그게 다 그 노래로 시작된 거야. 그런데 넌 어엿한 연주자에, 거장에, 루빈시테인이잖아……. 정말이지, 후회 안 할 거야!\"\n\n\"그런데 너 그 여자한테 무슨 약속이라도 했냐? 뭔가 서명한 거라도? 혹시 결혼 약속이라도?\"\n\n\"아무것도, 아무것도, 그런 건 전혀 없어! 게다가 그 여잔 그런 부류가 전혀 아니야……. 체바로프가 그걸 시도했다가…….\"\n\n\"그럼 그 여잘 그냥 버려!\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇게 버릴 순 없어!\"\n\n\"왜 못 버려?\"\n\n\"뭐, 못 버려, 그게 다야! 여기엔 끌리는 요소가 있단 말이야, 형제.\"\n\n\"그럼 넌 왜 그 여잘 홀린 거야?\"\n\n\"내가 그 여잘 홀린 게 아니라, 어쩌면 내가 어리석게도 스스로 홀린 건지도 모르지. 하지만 그 여잔 너든 나든 눈곱만큼도 개의치 않아, 누군가 곁에 앉아서 한숨만 쉬어 준다면 말이야……. 이 상황을 설명할 수가 없네, 형제……. 이봐, 넌 수학을 잘하고 지금도 그걸 연구하고 있잖아……. 그 여자한테 적분학을 가르치기 시작해 봐. 정말이지, 농담 아니야, 진심이라고, 그 여자한테야 매한가지일 테니까. 그 여잔 일 년 내내 널 빤히 바라보며 한숨이나 쉴 거야. 내가 한번은 그 여자한테 프로이센 상원에 대해 이틀 동안 줄곧 얘기한 적이 있는데(뭐라도 얘기는 해야 하니까) — 그 여잔 그저 한숨을 쉬며 땀을 흘리더라니까! 그리고 사랑 얘긴 꺼내지 마 — 그 여잔 발작이 일 만큼 수줍음을 타니까 — 그냥 네가 그 여자한테서 도무지 떨어지지 못하겠다는 걸 보여 주기만 해 — 그거면 충분해. 끔찍하게 편안하다니까. 완전히 제집처럼 지내면서 책도 읽고, 앉아 있고, 드러눕고, 글도 쓰고. 조심만 하면 입맞춤까지도 시도해 볼 만하지.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 그 여자한테 뭘 바라겠냐?\"\n\n\"아, 도무지 너한테 이해를 못 시키겠네! 봐, 너희 둘은 천생연분이라니까! 난 너를 보면 자주 그 여자가 떠올랐다고!…… 넌 결국 그렇게 될 거야! 그러니 그게 더 이르냐 늦냐가 무슨 상관이야? 여기엔 깃털 침대의 요소가 있단 말이야, 형제 — 아! 게다가 그것만이 아니지! 여기엔 끌어당기는 게 있어 — 여기엔 세상의 끝, 정박지, 고요한 안식처, 대지의 배꼽, 세상의 토대가 되는 세 마리 물고기, 블린의 정수, 고소한 생선 파이의 정수, 저녁 사모바르의 정수, 부드러운 한숨과 따스한 숄과 잠들기 좋은 뜨거운 페치카의 정수가 있단 말이야 — 죽은 듯이 아늑한데, 그러면서도 넌 살아 있는 거지 — 두 가지 이점을 한꺼번에 누리는 거야! 자, 빌어먹을, 형제, 내가 무슨 헛소릴 지껄이는 거람, 잘 시간이야! 들어 봐. 난 가끔 밤에 깨거든. 그러니 내가 들어가서 그를 살펴볼게. 하지만 그럴 필요도 없어, 다 괜찮으니까. 너무 걱정 말라고, 그래도 원한다면 너도 한 번쯤 들여다봐도 돼. 그런데 만약 뭔가 눈에 띄거든 — 섬망이든 열이든 — 즉시 날 깨워. 하지만 그럴 리는 없을 거야…….\"\n라주미힌은 이튿날 아침 여덟 시에 깨어났는데, 마음이 어지럽고 심각했다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n라주미힌은 이튿날 아침 여덟 시에 깨어났는데, 마음이 어지럽고 심각했다. 그는 새롭고 예기치 못한 갖가지 곤혹스러운 일들과 맞닥뜨려 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그는 자신이 언젠가 이런 기분으로 깨어나리라고는 한 번도 예상하지 못했다. 그는 어제 일을 하나하나 빠짐없이 기억하고 있었고, 자신에게 전혀 색다른 경험이 일어났다는 것, 지금껏 알던 그 무엇과도 다른 인상을 받았다는 것을 알고 있었다. 동시에 그는 자신의 상상에 불을 지폈던 그 꿈이 가망 없이 도달할 수 없는 것임을 똑똑히 깨달았다. 어찌나 도달할 수 없는지 그는 그것이 정말로 부끄럽게 느껴졌고, 그래서 그 '세 곱절로 저주받은 어제'가 그에게 물려준 다른, 더 실질적인 근심과 어려움 쪽으로 서둘러 넘어갔다.\n\n어제 일 중에서 가장 끔찍한 기억은 자신이 '비열하고 치사하게' 굴었다는 것이었는데, 그것은 단지 그가 취해 있었기 때문만이 아니라, 어리석은 질투에 사로잡혀 그 젊은 처녀의 처지를 이용해 그녀의 약혼자를 헐뜯었기 때문이었다. 그는 그들 사이의 관계와 의무에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그 남자 자체에 대해서도 거의 아는 바가 없었으면서 말이다. 도대체 무슨 권리로 그가 그토록 성급하고 경솔한 태도로 그 사람을 비판했단 말인가? 누가 그의 의견을 물었던가? 아브도티야 로마노브나 같은 사람이 돈 때문에 자격 없는 남자와 결혼하리라는 것이 생각이나 할 수 있는 일인가? 그러니 그 남자에게는 무언가 있는 게 분명했다. 그 셋집은? 하지만 따지고 보면 그가 어떻게 그 셋집의 됨됨이를 알 수 있단 말인가? 그는 신혼집을 꾸미고 있었던 거지……. 쳇! 이 모든 게 얼마나 한심한 일인가! 그리고 취해 있었다는 것이 무슨 변명이 되겠는가? 그런 어리석은 핑계는 오히려 더 굴욕적인 것이었다! 술 속에 진실이 있는 법, 그리하여 그 진실이 죄다 튀어나와 버린 것이다, '말하자면, 그의 거칠고 시기심 많은 마음속 온갖 더러움이' 말이다! 그리고 그런 꿈이 그에게, 라주미힌에게 도대체 가당키나 한 것이겠는가? 그런 처녀 곁에서 그는 무엇이란 말인가 — 어젯밤의 그 술 취해 떠들어 대던 허풍쟁이가? 그토록 터무니없고 파렴치한 짝짓기를 상상이나 할 수 있겠는가? 라주미힌은 그 생각만으로도 절망적으로 얼굴을 붉혔고, 갑자기 어젯밤 계단에서 집주인 여자가 아브도티야 로마노브나를 질투하게 될 거라고 지껄였던 기억이 생생하게 그를 덮쳤다……. 그것은 정말이지 견딜 수 없는 것이었다. 그는 주먹으로 부엌 화덕을 세게 내리쳤고, 손을 다치고 벽돌 하나를 튕겨 내고 말았다.\n\n\"물론이지.\" 잠시 후 그는 자기 비하의 감정에 휩싸여 중얼거렸다. \"물론, 이 모든 추잡한 짓은 결코 씻어 내거나 얼버무릴 수 없어……. 그러니 그걸 생각해 봤자 소용없고, 나는 잠자코 그들에게 가서 내 의무를 다해야 해……. 역시 잠자코……, 용서를 구하지도 말고, 아무 말도 하지 말고……. 이제 모든 게 끝장났으니까!\"\n\n그러면서도 옷을 입을 때 그는 평소보다 더 꼼꼼하게 자기 옷차림을 살폈다. 다른 옷이 없었다 — 만약 있었다면, 어쩌면 그는 그걸 입지 않았을지도 모른다. \"일부러 안 입었을 거야.\" 그러나 어쨌든 그는 냉소적이고 지저분한 추레한 꼴로 있을 수는 없었다. 그에게는 남들의 감정을 상하게 할 권리가 없었으니, 더구나 그들이 그의 도움을 필요로 하며 자기들을 보러 와 달라고 청하고 있는 마당에야. 그는 정성껏 옷을 솔질했다. 그의 속옷은 늘 멀쩡했다. 그 점에서 그는 유난히 청결했다.\n\n그날 아침 그는 빈틈없이 몸을 씻었다 — 나스타시야한테서 비누를 좀 얻어 — 머리며, 목이며, 특히 손을 씻었다. 까칠한 턱수염을 깎을지 말지 하는 문제에 이르자(프라스코비야 파블로브나에게는 죽은 남편이 남긴 훌륭한 면도칼이 있었다), 그 문제는 성난 듯 부정적으로 결론지어졌다. \"그냥 이대로 두자! 만일 내가 일부러……, 하려고 면도를 했다고 생각하면 어쩌지? 분명히 그렇게 생각할 거야! 절대 안 돼!\"\n\n\"그리고……, 무엇보다 최악인 건 그가 너무 거칠고, 너무 지저분하고, 선술집 사람 같은 매너를 지녔다는 것이고……, 또……, 자기에게 신사의 본질적인 요소가 좀 있다는 걸 안다 치더라도……, 거기에 무슨 뽐낼 거리가 있단 말인가? 누구나 신사여야 하고, 그 이상이어야 하지……, 게다가 (그는 떠올렸다) 그 자신도 자잘한 짓들을 저질러 왔지……, 딱히 부정직하달 건 아니지만, 그래도……. 그리고 가끔 어떤 생각들을 했던가. 흠……, 그런데 그 모든 걸 아브도티야 로마노브나 곁에 갖다 놓다니! 빌어먹을! 될 대로 되라지! 그래, 그럼 일부러 지저분하고, 기름때 절고, 선술집 같은 매너로 굴어 주지, 그러든 말든 상관 안 해! 더 험하게 굴어 줄 테다!\"\n\n그가 이런 독백에 빠져 있을 때, 프라스코비야 파블로브나의 거실에서 밤을 보낸 조시모프가 들어왔다.\n\n그는 집에 가려던 참이었는데, 먼저 환자를 들여다보려고 서두르고 있었다. 라주미힌은 그에게 라스콜니코프가 잠꾸러기 쥐처럼 곤히 자고 있다고 알려 주었다. 조시모프는 그를 깨우지 말라고 지시하고, 열한 시쯤 다시 들르겠다고 약속했다.\n\n\"만일 그가 아직 집에 있다면 말이지.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"젠장할! 자기 환자를 통제하지 못한다면, 어떻게 그를 고친단 말인가? 자네 혹시 아나, 그가 그들에게 갈지, 아니면 그들이 이리로 올지?\"\n\n\"올 것 같습니다.\" 라주미힌이 그 물음의 의도를 알아채고 말했다. \"그리고 분명 자기네 집안일을 의논하겠지요. 저는 가겠습니다. 의사로서 당신이 저보다 여기 있을 권리가 더 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 고해 신부가 아닐세. 잠깐 들렀다 갈 거야. 그들을 돌보는 것 말고도 할 일이 많거든.\"\n\n\"한 가지 마음에 걸리는 게 있습니다.\" 라주미힌이 얼굴을 찌푸리며 끼어들었다. \"집으로 오는 길에 제가 그에게 취해서 헛소리를 잔뜩 늘어놓았거든요……, 별의별 얘기를……, 그중에는 당신이 그가……, 미치지 않을까 걱정한다는 얘기도 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그 부인들한테도 그렇게 말했잖나.\"\n\n\"멍청한 짓이었다는 거 압니다! 원하시면 저를 때리셔도 됩니다! 정말 그렇게 진지하게 생각하셨던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"그건 헛소리라니까, 내가 어떻게 그걸 진지하게 생각하겠나? 자네가 직접, 그에게 날 데려갔을 때 그를 편집광이라고 묘사했지……, 그리고 어제 우리가 거기에 기름을 부은 거야, 그러니까 자네가 말일세, 화가 얘기로 말이야. 어쩌면 그가 바로 그 점에 미쳐 있었을지도 모르는 판에, 그건 참 멋진 대화였지! 그때 경찰서에서 무슨 일이 있었는지, 어떤 못된 작자가……, 그런 의심으로 그를 모욕했다는 걸 내가 알았더라면! 흠……, 어제 그런 대화를 하게 두진 않았을 텐데. 이런 편집광들은 두더지가 파 놓은 흙더미를 가지고 산을 만들고……, 자기 망상을 단단한 현실로 본단 말이지……. 내 기억으로는, 제 생각엔 자메토프의 이야기가 수수께끼의 절반을 풀어 줬어. 아, 내가 아는 어떤 사례가 있는데, 마흔 살 먹은 우울증 환자가 여덟 살짜리 사내아이의 목을 베었지, 매일 식탁에서 그 애가 하는 농담을 견딜 수 없다는 이유로! 그런데 이 경우에는 그의 누더기에다, 무례한 경찰관에다, 열병에다, 게다가 이 의심까지! 그 모든 게 우울증으로 반쯤 미쳐 버린 인간한테, 게다가 그 병적이고 유별난 자존심까지 지닌 인간한테 작용한 거지! 그게 병의 발단이 됐을 만도 해. 에이, 다 집어치우자!……. 그건 그렇고, 그 자메토프란 자는 분명 괜찮은 친구긴 한데, 흠……, 어젯밤에 그 모든 걸 떠벌리지는 말았어야 했어. 정말 지독한 수다쟁이라니까!\"\n\n\"그런데 그가 누구한테 말한 거지요? 당신과 저한테요?\"\n\n\"포르피리한테도.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 문제가 됩니까?\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 자네 그들한테, 그러니까 그의 어머니와 누이한테 무슨 영향력이 좀 있나? 오늘은 그를 좀 더 조심스럽게 대하라고 말해 주게…….\"\n\n\"잘들 해낼 겁니다!\" 라주미힌이 마지못해 대답했다.\n\n\"그는 왜 그렇게 그 루진이란 자한테 반감을 갖는 거지? 돈도 있는 사람이고, 그녀도 딱히 그를 싫어하는 것 같진 않은데……, 그리고 그들은 한 푼도 없는 것 같던데, 안 그래? 어?\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 당신과 무슨 상관입니까?\" 라주미힌이 짜증스레 소리쳤다. \"그들에게 한 푼이라도 있는지 없는지 제가 어떻게 압니까? 직접 물어보시면 어쩌면 알아내실 수도 있겠지요…….\"\n\n\"쳇! 자넨 가끔 정말 멍청이라니까! 어젯밤 마신 술이 아직 안 깼군……. 잘 있게. 자네의 프라스코비야 파블로브나한테 하룻밤 재워 줘서 고맙다고 전해 주게. 그녀는 문을 잠가 버리고, 문 너머로 내가 보낸 인사에도 대답이 없더군. 일곱 시에 일어나서, 부엌에서 사모바르를 자기 방으로 가져가게 했고. 나한테는 직접 만나 주는 영광을 베풀지 않았다네…….\"\n\n정확히 아홉 시에 라주미힌은 바칼레예프의 집에 있는 셋방에 도착했다. 두 부인은 초조하게 안달하며 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그들은 일곱 시나 그 전에 일어났던 것이다. 그는 밤처럼 어두운 표정으로 들어와 어색하게 인사를 했고, 곧바로 그런 자신에게 화가 치밀었다. 그의 예상은 빗나갔다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 그에게 그야말로 달려들어 두 손을 붙잡고는 거의 입을 맞추려 했다. 그는 머뭇거리며 아브도티야 로마노브나를 흘긋 보았는데, 그 도도한 얼굴에는 그 순간 그런 감사와 다정함, 그토록 온전하고 뜻밖의 존경의 빛이 어려 있어서(그가 예상했던 비웃는 눈길과 미처 감추지 못한 경멸 대신에) 차라리 욕설로 맞이했을 때보다 그를 더 큰 혼란에 빠뜨렸다. 다행히 대화거리가 있었고, 그는 서둘러 그것을 붙잡았다.\n\n모든 일이 잘 되어 가고 있으며 로댜가 아직 깨어나지 않았다는 말을 듣자, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 그 소식이 기쁘다고 밝혔는데, \"미리 아주, 아주 긴히 의논해야 할 일이 있기\" 때문이라고 했다. 그러고는 아침 식사에 대해 묻고는 함께 들자고 청했다. 그들은 그와 함께 먹으려고 기다리고 있었던 것이다. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 종을 울리자, 누더기 차림의 지저분한 종업원이 응답해 왔고, 그들은 그에게 차를 가져다 달라고 했다. 차는 마침내 나왔지만 어찌나 더럽고 어수선하게 차려져 나왔는지 부인들이 부끄러워할 정도였다. 라주미힌은 이 셋방을 격하게 비난하려다가, 루진을 떠올리고는 당황해 입을 다물었고, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 끊임없이 쏟아 내는 질문 덕분에 크게 안도했다.\n\n그는 사십오 분 동안 이야기했는데, 그들의 질문에 끊임없이 가로막히면서도, 라스콜니코프의 지난 한 해 삶에 대해 자신이 아는 가장 중요한 사실들을 죄다 그들에게 들려주는 데 성공했고, 그의 병에 관한 상세한 설명으로 이야기를 맺었다. 그러나 그는 차라리 빼는 편이 나은 많은 것을, 경찰서에서 있었던 장면과 그 모든 여파를 포함해 생략했다. 그들은 그의 이야기를 열심히 들었고, 그가 이야기를 마쳤고 듣는 이들을 만족시켰다고 생각했을 때, 그는 그들이 자기가 이제 겨우 시작했을 뿐이라고 여긴다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"말씀해 주세요, 말씀을! 당신 생각엔……? 죄송해요, 아직도 성함을 모르네요!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 황급히 끼어들었다.\n\n\"드미트리 프로코피치입니다.\"\n\n\"정말, 정말 알고 싶어요, 드미트리 프로코피치……, 그 애가 지금……, 전반적으로 모든 것에 대해 어떻게 보고 있는지, 그러니까, 뭐랄까, 어떻게 설명해야 하나, 그 애가 무얼 좋아하고 무얼 싫어하는지요? 늘 그렇게 신경질적인가요? 할 수 있으시면 말씀해 주세요, 그 애의 희망은, 말하자면, 그 애의 꿈은 뭔가요? 지금 어떤 영향 아래 있나요? 한마디로, 알고 싶은 게…….\"\n\n\"아, 어머니, 그분이 그걸 어떻게 한꺼번에 다 대답하시겠어요?\" 두냐가 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사, 그 애가 이런 모습일 줄은 조금도 예상 못 했어요, 드미트리 프로코피치!\"\n\n\"당연하지요.\" 라주미힌이 대답했다. \"저는 어머니가 안 계시지만, 제 삼촌은 해마다 오시는데, 거의 매번 저를 외양만으로도 알아보지 못하세요, 영리한 분이신데도 말이지요. 그러니 3년이나 떨어져 계셨다는 건 정말 큰 의미가 있습니다. 제가 뭐라고 말씀드려야 할까요? 저는 로디온을 1년 반 동안 알고 지냈습니다. 그는 침울하고, 음울하고, 자존심 세고, 오만합니다. 그리고 최근에는 — 어쩌면 그 전부터 오랫동안 — 의심이 많고 공상에 잘 빠져 있었지요. 그는 고결한 본성과 따뜻한 마음을 지녔습니다. 그는 자기 감정을 드러내기를 좋아하지 않고, 차라리 마음을 자유로이 여는 것보다 잔인한 짓을 하려 들지요. 하지만 가끔은 전혀 병적이지 않고, 그저 차갑고 비인간적으로 무정할 때도 있어요. 마치 두 인격 사이를 오가는 것 같습니다. 때로는 무섭도록 말이 없지요! 그는 자기가 너무 바빠서 모든 게 방해된다고 말하면서도, 정작 침대에 누워 아무것도 안 합니다. 그는 세상일을 비웃지 않는데, 그건 재치가 없어서가 아니라 마치 그런 사소한 것들에 허비할 시간이 없다는 듯한 태도지요. 그는 자기에게 하는 말을 결코 귀담아듣지 않아요. 어느 순간이든 남들이 관심을 갖는 일에는 결코 흥미를 느끼지 못하고요. 그는 자기 자신을 아주 높이 평가하는데, 어쩌면 그게 옳을지도 모르지요. 자, 또 뭐가 있을까요? 제 생각엔 두 분의 도착이 그에게 더없이 유익한 영향을 미칠 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하느님, 부디 그렇게 되게 해 주소서.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 자기 로댜에 대한 라주미힌의 이야기에 가슴 아파하며 외쳤다.\n\n그리하여 라주미힌은 마침내 좀 더 대담하게 아브도티야 로마노브나를 바라볼 용기를 냈다. 그는 이야기하는 동안 그녀를 자주 흘긋거렸지만, 매번 한순간뿐이었고 곧바로 다시 눈길을 돌리곤 했다. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 탁자에 앉아 주의 깊게 듣고 있다가, 다시 일어나서 팔짱을 끼고 입술을 꼭 다문 채 이리저리 거닐기 시작했고, 가끔씩 걸음을 멈추지 않고 질문을 던지곤 했다. 그녀에게도 자기에게 하는 말을 귀담아듣지 않는 똑같은 버릇이 있었다. 그녀는 얇은 짙은 색 천으로 지은 옷을 입고, 목에는 하얗고 투명한 스카프를 두르고 있었다. 라주미힌은 곧 그들의 소지품에서 극심한 가난의 흔적을 알아챘다. 만일 아브도티야 로마노브나가 여왕처럼 차려입었더라면 그는 그녀가 두렵지 않았을 거라고 느꼈지만, 어쩌면 바로 그녀가 초라하게 차려입었기 때문에, 그리고 그녀를 둘러싼 온갖 비참함을 그가 알아챘기 때문에 그의 가슴은 두려움으로 가득 찼고, 자기가 내뱉는 한마디 한마디, 자기가 하는 몸짓 하나하나가 두려워지기 시작했는데, 이는 이미 주눅 들어 있던 남자에게는 무척이나 힘든 일이었다.\n\n\"제 오빠의 성격에 관해 흥미로운 이야기를 많이 해 주셨어요……, 그것도 공정하게요. 기뻐요. 저는 당신이 오빠한테 너무 무비판적으로 헌신적인 분이라고 생각했거든요.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"오빠한테 여자의 보살핌이 필요하다는 당신 말씀이 옳다고 생각해요.\" 그녀가 사려 깊게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제가 그렇게 말한 건 아닙니다만, 당신 말씀이 옳을 겁니다, 다만…….\"\n\n\"다만 뭐요?\"\n\"그는 아무도 사랑하지 않고, 어쩌면 영영 사랑하지 못할 겁니다.\" 라주미힌이 단호하게 단언했다.\n\n\"그가 사랑할 능력이 없다는 말씀인가요?\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 당신은 정말 무섭도록 오빠를 닮았어요, 모든 면에서, 정말로요!\" 그는 자기도 놀랄 만큼 불쑥 내뱉었다가, 방금 전 그녀의 오빠에 대해 한 말을 곧장 떠올리고는 가재처럼 새빨개져서 당혹감에 휩싸였다. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 그를 보고 웃지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"두 사람 다 로댜에 대해 잘못 알고 있을지도 몰라요.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 살짝 언짢은 듯 말했다. \"지금 우리가 처한 어려움을 두고 하는 말은 아니야, 두냐. 표트르 페트로비치가 이 편지에 쓴 것이나 너랑 내가 짐작한 게 틀렸을 수도 있지. 하지만 드미트리 프로코피치, 그 애가 얼마나 변덕스럽고, 말하자면, 종잡을 수 없는지 당신은 상상도 못 할 거예요. 그 애가 겨우 열다섯 살이었을 때도 저는 그 애가 무슨 짓을 할지 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어요. 그리고 지금도 그 애는 분명 아무도 생각지 못할 일을 저지를 수 있어요……. 자, 예를 들어, 일 년 반 전에 그 애가 어떤 처녀랑 — 이름이 뭐였더라 — 자기 집주인 딸이랑 결혼하겠다는 생각을 했을 때, 그 애가 저를 얼마나 놀라게 했는지, 슬픔으로 거의 죽을 뻔할 만큼 충격을 줬는지 아세요?\"\n\n\"그 일에 대해 들으셨어요?\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 물었다.\n\n\"설마—\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 열을 올리며 말을 이었다. \"제 눈물이며, 제 애원이며, 제 병이며, 슬픔으로 죽을지도 모른다는 것이며, 우리의 가난이 그 애를 멈추게 했을 거라고 생각하세요? 천만에요, 그 애는 그 모든 장애물을 태연히 무시했을 거예요. 그런데도 그게 우리를 사랑하지 않아서가 아니라니까요!\"\n\n\"그는 제게 그 일에 대해 한마디도 한 적이 없습니다.\" 라주미힌이 조심스럽게 대답했다. \"하지만 프라스코비야 파블로브나한테서 직접 뭔가 듣긴 했지요, 그녀가 결코 수다스러운 사람은 아닙니다만. 그리고 제가 들은 건 분명 좀 이상한 이야기였습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 얘길 들으셨나요?\" 두 부인이 동시에 물었다.\n\n\"뭐, 별로 특별한 건 없습니다. 그저 그 결혼이 — 처녀가 죽는 바람에 성사되지 못한 그 결혼 말입니다만 — 프라스코비야 파블로브나의 마음에 전혀 들지 않았다는 것만 알았어요. 게다가 그 처녀가 전혀 예쁘지 않았다고 하더군요, 실은 딱 잘라 못생겼다고 들었습니다……, 게다가 그런 병약한 몸에다……, 또 별난 데가 있었다고요. 하지만 무언가 좋은 점이 있었던 모양입니다. 좋은 점이 있었던 게 틀림없어요, 안 그러면 도무지 설명이 안 되니까요……. 그 처녀도 돈이 없었고, 그가 그 처녀의 돈을 따졌을 리도 없고요……. 하지만 그런 문제는 늘 판단하기가 어려운 법이지요.\"\n\n\"분명 착한 처녀였을 거예요.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 짤막하게 말했다.\n\n\"하느님, 용서하소서, 저는 그저 그 애가 죽은 게 기뻤어요. 하기야 둘 중 누가 상대에게 더 큰 불행을 안겨 줬을지는 모르겠지만요 — 그 애가 그 처녀에게인지, 그 처녀가 그 애에게인지.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 맺었다. 그러고는 그에게 어제 루진과 있었던 장면에 대해 조심스럽게 물어보기 시작했는데, 머뭇거리며 끊임없이 두냐를 흘긋거렸고, 이는 분명 후자를 언짢게 했다. 다른 무엇보다도 바로 이 사건이 그녀를 불안하게, 심지어 경악하게 만든 것이 분명했다. 라주미힌은 그것을 다시 한번 상세히 묘사했지만, 이번에는 자기 나름의 결론을 덧붙였다. 그는 라스콜니코프가 일부러 표트르 페트로비치를 모욕했다고 노골적으로 비난했고, 그의 병을 핑계 삼아 그를 변호하려 들지 않았다.\n\n\"그는 병이 나기 전부터 그걸 계획했던 겁니다.\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"저도 그렇게 생각해요.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 풀죽은 기색으로 동의했다. 하지만 라주미힌이 표트르 페트로비치에 대해 그토록 조심스럽게, 심지어 어느 정도 존중하는 태도로 말하는 것을 듣고 그녀는 무척 놀랐다. 아브도티야 로마노브나도 그것에 놀랐다.\n\n\"그럼 표트르 페트로비치에 대한 당신 생각이 그렇다는 건가요?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 묻지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"제가 따님의 장래 남편에 대해 다른 생각을 가질 수는 없지요.\" 라주미힌이 단호하게, 그리고 열의를 담아 대답했다. \"그리고 이건 단순히 천박한 예의에서 하는 말이 아니라, 그저……, 그저 아브도티야 로마노브나께서 자유의지로 이 사람을 받아들이기로 하셨기 때문입니다. 어젯밤 제가 그에 대해 그토록 무례하게 떠들었다면, 그건 제가 역겨울 만큼 취해 있었고……, 게다가 미쳐 있었기 때문이지요. 그래요, 미쳤었어요, 정신이 나갔었어요, 완전히 이성을 잃었던 겁니다……, 그리고 오늘 아침 저는 그게 부끄럽습니다.\"\n\n그는 얼굴이 새빨개져 말을 멈췄다. 아브도티야 로마노브나도 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 침묵을 깨지는 않았다. 그녀는 그들이 루진 이야기를 시작한 순간부터 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n그녀의 도움 없이는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 분명 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 마침내 그녀는 머뭇거리며 끊임없이 딸을 흘긋거리다가, 한 가지 사정 때문에 몹시 걱정이 된다고 털어놓았다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 드미트리 프로코피치.\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. \"드미트리 프로코피치한테 솔직하게 다 말해도 되겠니, 두냐?\"\n\n\"물론이죠, 어머니.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"바로 이런 일이에요.\" 그녀는 자기 고민을 털어놓아도 좋다는 허락이 마음의 짐을 덜어 주기라도 한 듯 황급히 말을 시작했다. \"오늘 아침 아주 일찍, 우리가 도착한다고 알린 편지에 대한 답으로 표트르 페트로비치한테서 쪽지가 하나 왔어요. 그는 역에서 우리를 마중하겠다고 약속했었잖아요, 아시다시피. 그런데 그러는 대신, 하인을 보내 이 셋방 주소를 알려 주고 길을 안내하게 했어요. 그리고 오늘 아침에 자기가 직접 여기로 오겠다는 전갈을 보냈고요. 그런데 오늘 아침 그한테서 이 쪽지가 온 거예요. 직접 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠어요. 거기에 저를 무척 걱정스럽게 하는 대목이 하나 있는데……, 곧 그게 뭔지 아시게 될 거예요, 그리고……, 솔직한 의견을 말씀해 주세요, 드미트리 프로코피치! 당신은 누구보다 로댜의 성격을 잘 아시니, 누구도 당신만큼 우리에게 좋은 조언을 해 줄 수는 없을 거예요. 두냐는, 말해 두지만, 곧바로 결심을 했지만, 저는 아직 어떻게 처신해야 할지 모르겠어서……, 그래서 당신 의견을 기다리고 있었어요.\"\n\n라주미힌은 전날 저녁 날짜가 적힌 쪽지를 펼쳐 다음과 같이 읽었다.\n\n\"친애하는 부인, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 예기치 못한 장애로 인해 제가 기차역에서 부인을 마중할 수 없게 되었음을 알려 드리는 영광을 갖습니다. 같은 목적으로 매우 유능한 사람을 보냈습니다. 마찬가지로 내일 아침에도 지체할 수 없는 원로원 업무로 인해 부인을 뵙는 영광을 누리지 못하게 되었으며, 또한 부인께서 아드님을, 그리고 아브도티야 로마노브나께서 오라버니를 만나시는 동안 가족 모임에 끼어들지 않으려는 뜻도 있습니다. 늦어도 내일 저녁 정확히 여덟 시에 부인의 거처로 찾아뵙고 인사 올리는 영광을 갖겠으며, 이와 함께 한 가지 간곡하고, 덧붙이자면, 단호한 청을 감히 드리고자 합니다. 즉, 우리가 만나는 자리에 로디온 로마노비치가 동석하지 않게 해 주시기를 바랍니다. 그가 어제 병중인 그를 문병한 자리에서 제게 전례 없는 막대한 모욕을 가했기 때문이며, 더욱이 제가 부인께 직접, 어떤 점에 관해 반드시 필요하고 상세한 해명을 듣고자 하는데, 그에 대한 부인 자신의 해석을 알고 싶기 때문입니다. 미리 알려 드리는 영광을 갖건대, 만일 제 청에도 불구하고 제가 로디온 로마노비치와 마주치게 된다면, 저는 부득이 즉시 물러날 수밖에 없으며, 그렇게 되면 그 책임은 오로지 부인께 있게 될 것입니다. 제가 이렇게 쓰는 것은, 제 문병 때 그토록 위중해 보이던 로디온 로마노비치가 두 시간 후에 갑자기 회복되었으니, 따라서 집을 나설 수 있게 되어 부인 또한 찾아갈 수 있으리라는 추측에서입니다. 저는 마차에 치여 그 후 사망한 어느 술 취한 사내의 셋방에서 제 두 눈으로 직접 확인하여 그 믿음을 굳혔습니다. 그는 그 사내의 딸, 행실이 좋지 못하기로 소문난 젊은 여자에게 장례를 핑계로 이십오 루블을 주었는데, 부인께서 그 금액을 마련하느라 얼마나 애를 쓰셨는지 아는 저로서는 그것이 심히 놀라웠습니다. 이로써 부인의 존경스러운 따님 아브도티야 로마노브나께 각별한 경의를 표하며, 부인께서 삼가 올리는 이 충정의 인사를 받아 주시기를 청합니다.\n부인의 충실한 종,\n\n페. 루진 올림.\"\n\n\"이제 저는 어떻게 해야 하나요, 드미트리 프로코피치?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 거의 울먹이며 입을 열었다. \"어떻게 로댜한테 오지 말라고 할 수 있겠어요? 어제는 그 애가 그토록 간절하게 우리더러 표트르 페트로비치를 거절하라고 우겼는데, 이제는 우리더러 로댜를 들이지 말라는 명령이라니! 알게 되면 그 애는 일부러 올 거고, 그러면……, 그때 무슨 일이 벌어질까요?\"\n\n\"아브도티야 로마노브나의 결정에 따르십시오.\" 라주미힌이 곧바로 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"아이고, 맙소사! 이 애는……, 무슨 말을 하는지 도무지 모르겠어요, 자기 의도를 설명하질 않아요! 이 애 말로는, 적어도 그게 최선이라는 게 아니라, 로댜가 반드시 여덟 시에 여기 와 있어야 하고, 그들이 꼭 만나야 한다는 거예요……. 저는 그 애한테 편지를 보여 주기조차 싫었어요, 그저 당신 도움을 받아 무슨 수를 써서 그 애가 오지 못하게 막으려고요……, 그 애가 워낙 신경질적이니까요……. 게다가 저는 죽었다는 그 술꾼이며 그 딸 얘기도, 그 애가 어떻게 그 딸한테 돈을 다 줘 버릴 수 있었는지도 이해가 안 돼요……, 그 돈은…….\"\n\n\"그 돈은 어머니가 그토록 희생해서 마련한 거잖아요.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그는 어제 제정신이 아니었습니다.\" 라주미힌이 사려 깊게 말했다. \"그가 어제 어느 식당에서 무슨 짓을 벌였는지 두 분이 아신다면 — 하기야 거기에도 나름의 이치는 있었지만요……. 흠! 어제저녁 집으로 오는 길에 그가 죽은 사람과 어떤 처녀에 대해 무슨 말을 하긴 했는데, 저는 한마디도 알아듣지 못했습니다……. 하지만 어젯밤, 저 자신도…….\"\n\n\"제일 좋은 건, 어머니, 우리가 직접 그 애한테 가는 거예요, 거기서 가면 무엇을 해야 할지 단번에 알게 될 거라고 장담해요. 게다가 시간이 늦어지고 있어요 — 맙소사, 열 시가 넘었네요.\" 그녀는 가느다란 베네치아풍 사슬로 목에 걸려 있는, 에나멜을 입힌 멋진 금시계를 보며 외쳤는데, 그것은 나머지 차림새와는 전혀 어울리지 않았다. '약혼자한테 받은 선물이군.' 라주미힌은 생각했다.\n\n\"가야겠다, 두냐, 가야 해.\" 어머니가 안절부절못하며 외쳤다. \"우리가 이렇게 늦게 가면 그 애는 어제 일로 우리가 아직 화나 있다고 생각할 거야. 자비로우신 하느님!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 황급히 모자와 망토를 걸쳤다. 두냐도 채비를 했다. 라주미힌이 알아챘듯이 그녀의 장갑은 그저 낡았을 뿐 아니라 구멍까지 나 있었는데, 그럼에도 이 뚜렷한 가난은 두 부인에게 특별한 기품을 안겨 주었으니, 그것은 초라한 옷을 입을 줄 아는 사람들에게서 늘 발견되는 기품이었다. 라주미힌은 경건하게 두냐를 바라보았고, 그녀를 모시고 가는 것이 자랑스러웠다. '감옥에서 자기 양말을 기웠다는 그 여왕도,' 그는 생각했다, '그때 영락없는 여왕처럼, 호화로운 연회나 알현식에서보다 한층 더 여왕처럼 보였겠지.'\n\n\"맙소사!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. \"내가 내 아들을, 내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 로댜를 만나는 걸 두려워하게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요! 저는 두려워요, 드미트리 프로코피치.\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 그를 흘긋 보고는 덧붙였다.\n\n\"두려워 마세요, 어머니.\" 두냐가 어머니에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"차라리 오빠를 믿으세요.\"\n\n\"아이고, 그래, 그 애를 믿지, 하지만 밤새 한숨도 못 잤구나.\" 가엾은 여인이 외쳤다.\n\n그들은 거리로 나왔다.\n\n\"있잖니, 두냐, 오늘 아침에 내가 잠깐 졸았을 때 마르파 페트로브나 꿈을 꿨단다……, 온통 하얀 옷을 입고……, 내게 다가와 손을 잡고는 고개를 절레절레 흔드는데, 어찌나 엄하던지 마치 나를 나무라는 것 같았어……. 그게 좋은 징조일까? 아이고, 맙소사! 드미트리 프로코피치, 당신은 모르시겠지만, 마르파 페트로브나가 죽었답니다!\"\n\n\"네, 몰랐습니다. 마르파 페트로브나가 누구신가요?\"\n\n\"갑자기 돌아가셨어요. 그런데 글쎄…….\"\n\n\"나중에요, 어머니.\" 두냐가 끼어들었다. \"이분은 마르파 페트로브나가 누구신지 모르신다니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 모르세요? 저는 당신이 우리에 대해 다 아시는 줄 알았어요. 용서하세요, 드미트리 프로코피치, 요 며칠 제가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 저도 모르겠어요. 저는 당신을 정말 우리의 섭리처럼 여기고 있어서, 당신이 우리에 대해 다 아시는 걸 당연하게 여겼나 봐요. 저는 당신을 친척처럼 생각해요……. 이런 말 한다고 화내지 마세요. 아니, 세상에, 오른손이 어떻게 된 거예요? 어디 부딪치셨나요?\"\n\n\"네, 좀 멍이 들었어요.\" 라주미힌이 기쁨에 들떠 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"제가 가끔 너무 마음에서 우러나오는 대로 말을 많이 해서, 두냐가 저를 나무라곤 해요……. 그런데 아니, 세상에, 그 애가 얼마나 벽장 같은 데서 사는지! 깨어나 있을까요? 그 여자, 그 애 집주인은 이걸 방이라고 여기는 걸까요? 들어 보세요, 당신은 그 애가 자기 감정 드러내기를 싫어한다고 하셨는데, 그럼 제 이런……, 약한 모습이 그 애를 짜증나게 할까요? 조언 좀 해 주세요, 드미트리 프로코피치, 제가 그 애를 어떻게 대해야 하나요? 저는 정말이지 정신이 하나도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그가 얼굴을 찌푸리거든 무엇이든 너무 캐묻지 마세요. 건강에 대해서도 너무 묻지 마시고요. 그는 그런 걸 싫어하니까요.\"\n\n\"아, 드미트리 프로코피치, 어머니 노릇 하기가 어찌나 힘든지요! 그런데 여기 계단이 있네요……. 어쩜 이렇게 끔찍한 계단이람!\"\n\n\"어머니, 얼굴이 너무 창백하세요, 너무 마음 졸이지 마세요, 어머니.\" 두냐가 어머니를 다정하게 어루만지며 말하더니, 두 눈을 반짝이며 덧붙였다. \"오빠는 어머니를 보면 기뻐해야 마땅한데, 어머니는 이렇게 스스로를 괴롭히고 계시잖아요.\"\n\n\"잠깐, 제가 들여다보고 그 애가 깨어났는지 보고 올게요.\"\n\n부인들은 앞서 가는 라주미힌을 천천히 뒤따랐고, 사 층의 집주인 여자네 문에 이르렀을 때, 그 여자의 문이 손가락 하나 들어갈 만큼 빠끔히 열려 있고 그 안의 어둠 속에서 날카로운 검은 두 눈이 그들을 지켜보고 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그들의 눈길이 마주치자, 문이 갑자기 어찌나 쾅 닫히는지 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 하마터면 비명을 지를 뻔했다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n“다 나았습니다, 아주 멀쩡해요!” 그들이 들어서자 조시모프가 명랑하게 외쳤다.\n\n그는 십 분쯤 먼저 와서 전과 같은 자리, 소파에 앉아 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 맞은편 구석에 앉아 있었는데, 옷을 다 갖춰 입고 정성껏 씻고 빗질까지 한 모습이 한동안 본 적 없던 차림새였다. 방은 순식간에 사람들로 북적였지만, 나스타시야는 용케 방문객들을 따라 들어와 귀를 기울이며 서 있었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 전날의 상태와 비교하면 정말 거의 다 나은 셈이었지만, 여전히 창백하고 무기력하고 침울했다. 그는 부상당한 사람이나, 어떤 끔찍한 육체적 고통을 겪어 낸 사람처럼 보였다. 두 눈썹은 찌푸려져 있었고, 입술은 꾹 다물려 있었으며, 두 눈은 열에 들떠 있었다. 그는 마치 의무라도 수행하듯 마지못해, 말을 아꼈고, 그 동작에는 어딘가 안절부절못하는 기색이 있었다.\n\n이제 그에게는 한쪽 팔에 삼각건을 걸치거나 손가락에 붕대를 감는 것만 더하면, 통증이 심한 종기를 앓거나 팔이 부러진 사람이라는 인상이 완성될 것 같았다. 그 창백하고 침울한 얼굴은 어머니와 누이가 들어섰을 때 한순간 환해졌으나, 이는 도리어 그 무기력한 낙담 대신 더욱 강렬한 고통의 빛을 드리울 뿐이었다. 그 빛은 곧 사그라들었지만 고통의 표정만은 남았고, 갓 개업한 젊은 의사의 온 열의를 다해 환자를 지켜보고 연구하던 조시모프는, 그에게서 어머니와 누이의 도착에 대한 기쁨이라곤 조금도 발견하지 못한 채, 다만 한두 시간 더 피할 수 없는 고문을 견뎌 내려는 어떤 쓰디쓴, 감추어진 결의 같은 것을 알아차렸다. 이후 그는 뒤이은 대화에서 거의 모든 말 한마디 한마디가 어떤 아픈 곳을 건드려 자극하는 것 같다는 점을 보았다. 그러나 동시에 그는, 전날만 해도 단지증 환자처럼 사소한 말 한마디에 광란에 빠졌던 환자가 이렇게 자제하고 감정을 감추는 그 힘에 경탄했다.\n\n“네, 이제 저도 거의 다 나은 걸 알겠습니다.” 라스콜니코프가 말하며 어머니와 누이에게 환영의 입맞춤을 했고, 그 입맞춤에 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 단번에 환해졌다. “그리고 저는 이걸 어제처럼 말하는 게 아닙니다.” 그가 라주미힌을 향해 그의 손을 다정하게 꼭 쥐며 말했다.\n\n“그래, 정말이지 오늘은 이 친구가 놀라울 정도입니다.” 조시모프가 입을 열었는데, 부인들이 들어온 것에 무척 기뻐하고 있었으니, 그는 십 분 동안 환자와 대화를 이어 가는 데 성공하지 못했던 것이다. “사나흘만 더 이런 식으로 가면 예전과 똑같아질 겁니다, 그러니까 한 달 전, 아니 두 달…… 어쩌면 석 달 전 모습으로요. 이건 오래전부터 와 온 거라서요…… 그렇죠? 자, 솔직히 말씀해 보세요, 어쩌면 당신 자신의 잘못이었던 거 아닙니까?” 그가 여전히 그를 자극할까 두려운 듯, 조심스러운 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n“충분히 그럴 수 있죠.” 라스콜니코프가 차갑게 대답했다.\n\n“제 말씀은 이겁니다.” 조시모프가 열을 올리며 계속했다. “당신이 완전히 회복되느냐는 오로지 당신 자신에게 달려 있다는 거예요. 이제 당신과 말이 통하니, 꼭 일러두고 싶은 게 있는데, 당신의 병적인 상태를 만들어 내는, 말하자면 근본적인, 기초적인 원인들을 피하는 게 필수적이라는 겁니다. 그러면 나을 거고, 그러지 않으면 점점 더 나빠질 거예요. 이 근본적인 원인들이 뭔지 저는 모르지만, 당신은 분명 알고 있을 겁니다. 당신은 지적인 사람이니, 물론 자기 자신을 관찰해 왔겠죠. 제 생각엔 당신의 이상 증세가 시작된 첫 단계가 대학을 그만둔 시점과 맞아떨어지는 것 같습니다. 당신은 할 일 없이 지내선 안 돼요, 그러니 일과, 눈앞에 정해진 분명한 목표가 있다면, 제 생각엔 무척 이로울 것 같군요.”\n\n“네, 네, 지당하신 말씀입니다……. 서둘러 대학으로 돌아가겠습니다. 그러면 모든 게 순조롭게 풀리겠죠…….”\n\n부분적으로는 부인들 앞에서 인상을 남기려고 현명한 충고를 시작했던 조시모프는, 환자를 흘긋 보았다가 그 얼굴에서 틀림없는 조롱의 빛을 발견하고는 분명 다소 어리둥절해졌다. 하지만 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 즉시 조시모프에게, 특히 전날 밤 자신들의 숙소를 방문해 준 것에 대해 감사를 표하기 시작했다.\n\n“뭐라고요! 어젯밤에 어머니를 보셨다고요?” 라스콜니코프가 흠칫 놀란 듯 물었다. “그럼 어머니도 여행 후에 주무시지 못한 거잖아요.”\n\n“아, 로쟈, 그건 두 시까지뿐이었단다. 두냐와 나는 집에서도 두 시 전엔 잠자리에 든 적이 없으니까.”\n\n“저도 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지 모르겠군요.” 라스콜니코프가 갑자기 눈살을 찌푸리고 눈을 내리깔며 말을 이었다. “진료비 문제는 차치하고서라도 — 이 얘기를 꺼내서 죄송합니다(그는 조시모프를 향해 돌아섰다) — 도대체 제가 무얼 했기에 당신한테서 이런 각별한 관심을 받을 자격이 있는지 정말 모르겠습니다. 그냥 이해가 안 돼요…… 그리고…… 그리고…… 사실 그게 저를 짓누릅니다, 이해가 안 되니까요. 솔직하게 말씀드리는 겁니다.”\n\n“자극받지 마세요.” 조시모프가 억지로 웃었다. “당신이 제 첫 환자라고 칩시다 — 자, 우리처럼 갓 개업한 사람들은 첫 환자를 제 자식처럼 사랑하고, 더러는 거의 사랑에 빠지다시피 하지요. 그리고 물론 저는 환자가 그리 많지도 않고요.”\n\n“이 친구에 대해선 아무 말도 안 하겠습니다.” 라스콜니코프가 라주미힌을 가리키며 덧붙였다. “저한테서 받은 거라곤 모욕과 성가심뿐인데도 말이죠.”\n\n“참 별소릴 다 하는군! 아니, 자네 오늘 감상적인 기분에라도 빠진 건가?” 라주미힌이 소리쳤다.\n\n좀 더 통찰력이 있었더라면, 그는 라스콜니코프에게 감상의 흔적이라곤 없으며 오히려 정반대의 무언가가 있음을 보았을 것이다. 하지만 아브도티야 로마노브나는 그것을 알아차렸다. 그녀는 오빠를 골똘히, 불안하게 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n“어머니로 말하자면, 저는 감히 입을 떼지도 못하겠어요.” 그는 마치 외워 둔 교훈을 되뇌듯 말을 이었다. “어제 여기서 제가 돌아오기를 기다리며 어머니가 얼마나 애를 태우셨을지, 그걸 조금이나마 깨닫게 된 게 겨우 오늘에 와서니까요.”\n\n이 말을 마치고는 그는 갑자기 누이에게 손을 내밀며 말없이 미소 지었다. 그러나 그 미소 속에는 진실한, 꾸밈없는 감정의 섬광이 있었다. 두냐는 곧장 그것을 알아채고, 더없이 기쁘고 고마워하며 따뜻하게 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 전날 다툰 이후 그가 그녀에게 말을 건넨 것은 처음이었다. 이 결정적인 무언의 화해를 보자 어머니의 얼굴은 황홀한 행복으로 환하게 빛났다. “그래, 바로 저런 점 때문에 내가 저 친구를 좋아하는 거지.” 라주미힌이 그 모든 걸 부풀려 받아들이며 의자에서 힘차게 몸을 돌리며 혼자 중얼거렸다. “저 친구한텐 저런 움직임이 있단 말이야.”\n\n“그리고 저 애는 이 모든 걸 어쩜 저렇게 잘하는지.” 어머니는 속으로 생각하고 있었다. “저 애는 어찌나 너그러운 충동을 지녔는지, 그리고 누이와의 모든 오해를 어쩜 저리도 단순하게, 저리도 섬세하게 끝맺어 버렸을까 — 그저 알맞은 순간에 손을 내밀고 누이를 저렇게 바라보는 것만으로……. 게다가 저 애는 눈이 얼마나 고운지, 얼굴 전체가 또 얼마나 고운지!…… 두냐보다도 잘생겼어……. 하지만, 세상에, 저 양복 꼴이라니 — 옷차림이 어쩜 저렇게 끔찍한지!…… 아파나시 이바니치 가게의 심부름꾼 바샤가 저보다 더 잘 입고 다니겠네! 저 애한테 달려가 끌어안고 싶어…… 부둥켜안고 울고 싶어 — 하지만 두려워……. 오, 저런, 저 애는 어쩜 이리 낯설까! 다정하게 말하고 있는데도 나는 두려우니! 아니, 내가 대체 무얼 두려워하는 거지?……”\n\n“오, 로쟈, 너는 믿지 못할 거다.” 그녀가 갑자기, 그가 자신에게 했던 말에 서둘러 답하느라 입을 열었다. “어제 두냐와 내가 얼마나 불행했는지! 이제 다 지나가고 끝나서 우리가 다시 아주 행복해졌으니 — 너한테 말할 수 있구나. 글쎄, 우리는 기차에서 내리자마자 거의 곧장 너를 끌어안으러 이리로 달려왔단다, 그리고 저 여자분도 — 아, 여기 있구나! 안녕하세요, 나스타시야!…… 저분이 곧장 우리한테 일러 주더구나, 네가 고열로 누워 있다가, 헛소리를 하며 의사한테서 막 도망쳤고, 다들 거리에서 너를 찾고 있다고 말이야. 우리가 어떤 심정이었는지 너는 상상도 못 할 거다! 나는 네 아버지 친구분이셨던 포탄치코프 중위의 비극적인 최후가 자꾸 떠올랐단다 — 너는 그분을 기억 못 할 거다, 로쟈 — 그분도 꼭 그렇게 고열에 들떠 뛰쳐나갔다가 안마당 우물에 빠지셨는데, 다음 날에야 겨우 끌어 올렸지 뭐냐. 물론 우리가 일을 부풀려 생각했지. 우리는 표트르 페트로비치한테 달려가 도와 달라고 청할 참이었단다……. 우리는 외톨이였으니까, 정말이지 외톨이였으니까.” 그녀는 애처롭게 말하다가, “우리가 다시 아주 행복해졌다”고는 해도 표트르 페트로비치 얘기를 꺼내는 게 아직은 다소 위험하다는 걸 문득 떠올리고는, 갑자기 입을 뚝 다물었다.\n\n“네, 네……. 물론 그건 무척 짜증스러운 일이죠…….” 라스콜니코프가 대답으로 중얼거렸는데, 그 태도가 어찌나 딴 데 정신이 팔리고 무심한지, 두냐는 어리둥절한 눈으로 그를 응시했다.\n\n“내가 또 무슨 말을 하려고 했더라?” 그는 기억해 내려 애쓰며 말을 이었다. “아, 그래. 어머니, 그리고 너도, 두냐, 제발 오해하지 마세요, 제가 오늘 두 분을 찾아뵐 마음이 없어서 두 분이 먼저 오시기를 기다렸다고는요.”\n\n“그게 무슨 소리냐, 로쟈?” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. 그녀 역시 놀랐다.\n\n‘오빠는 의무처럼 우리한테 대답하는 걸까?’ 두냐는 의아해했다. ‘무슨 의식을 치르거나 외운 교훈을 되뇌듯이 화해하고 용서를 구하는 걸까?’\n\n“이제 막 잠에서 깨어 두 분께 가려던 참이었는데, 옷 때문에 지체됐어요. 어제 저 사람한테…… 나스타시야한테…… 피를 좀 씻어 달라고 부탁하는 걸 깜빡했거든요…… 이제 막 옷을 입었어요.”\n\n“피라니! 무슨 피?” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n“아, 아무것도 아니에요 — 걱정 마세요. 어제 좀 헛것에 들떠 헤매고 다닐 때, 마차에 치인 사람을 우연히 만났거든요…… 어떤 관리를…….”\n\n“헛것에 들떠서? 그런데 넌 다 기억하잖아!” 라주미힌이 끼어들었다.\n\n“그건 사실이야.” 라스콜니코프가 유난히 조심스럽게 대답했다. “나는 아주 사소한 것까지 다 기억해. 그런데도 — 내가 왜 그런 짓을 했고 거기 갔고 그런 말을 했는지는, 지금 분명히 설명할 수가 없어.”\n\n“익히 알려진 현상이지.” 조시모프가 끼어들었다. “행동은 때때로 능숙하고 더없이 교묘하게 수행되는데, 그 행동의 방향만큼은 헝클어져서 갖가지 병적인 인상에 좌우되는 거예요 — 마치 꿈처럼 말이죠.”\n\n‘저자가 나를 거의 미치광이로 여기는 게 어쩌면 오히려 잘된 일인지도 몰라.’ 라스콜니코프는 생각했다.\n\n“아니, 완전히 건강한 사람들도 똑같이 행동하는걸요.” 두냐가 조시모프를 불안하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“당신 말씀에도 일리가 있습니다.” 그가 대답했다. “그런 의미에서라면 우리 모두 분명 적잖이 미치광이 같죠, 다만 정신이 헝클어진 사람들이 조금 더 미쳐 있다는 자그마한 차이가 있을 뿐입니다, 어디엔가는 선을 그어야 하니까요. 정상적인 인간은, 사실 거의 존재하지 않습니다. 수십 명 가운데 — 어쩌면 수십만 명 가운데 — 한 명 만나기도 어렵죠.”\n\n조시모프가 자신이 좋아하는 주제로 떠들다가 무심코 흘린 “미치광이”라는 말에, 모두가 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 주의를 기울이지 않는 듯이 앉아, 창백한 입술에 기묘한 미소를 띤 채 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그는 여전히 무언가를 골똘히 곱씹고 있었다.\n\n“그건 그렇고, 마차에 치인 그 사람은 어떻게 됐어? 내가 자네 말을 끊었지!” 라주미힌이 다급히 외쳤다.\n\n“뭐라고?” 라스콜니코프가 정신이 든 것 같았다. “아…… 그를 거처까지 옮기는 걸 도우면서 피를 뒤집어쓴 거야. 그건 그렇고, 어머니, 저는 어제 용서받을 수 없는 짓을 저질렀어요. 말 그대로 제정신이 아니었거든요. 어머니가 보내 주신 돈을 몽땅 줘 버렸어요…… 장례식에 쓰라고 그 사람 아내한테요. 그 여자는 이제 과부에, 폐병을 앓는, 가엾은 사람이에요…… 어린 자식이 셋이나 되는데 굶주리고…… 집엔 아무것도 없고…… 딸도 하나 있고요…… 어머니도 그들을 보셨더라면 직접 돈을 주셨을지도 몰라요. 하지만 제게 그럴 권리가 없었다는 건 인정합니다, 특히 어머니 자신이 그 돈을 얼마나 필요로 하는지 알고 있었으니까요. 남을 도우려면 그럴 권리가 있어야 하는 법이죠, 안 그러면, ‘크레베, 시앵, 시 부 네트 파 콩탕’이고요.” 그가 웃었다. “그렇지, 두냐?”\n\n“아니, 그렇지 않아.” 두냐가 단호하게 대답했다.\n\n“바!너도 이상이라는 걸 갖고 있구나.” 그가 거의 증오에 차 그녀를 바라보며 비꼬듯 미소 지으며 중얼거렸다. “그걸 고려했어야 했는데……. 뭐, 칭찬받을 만한 일이고, 너한텐 더 나은 거지…… 그리고 네가 넘어서지 않을 선에 다다르면 너는 불행해질 거고…… 만일 그 선을 넘어선다면 어쩌면 더더욱 불행해질 거다……. 하지만 이 모든 게 다 헛소리야.” 그가 휩쓸린 게 분해서 짜증스레 덧붙였다. “저는 그저, 용서를 빈다는 말을 하고 싶었을 뿐이에요, 어머니.” 그가 짧고 퉁명스럽게 끝맺었다.\n\n“그만하면 됐다, 로쟈, 네가 하는 일은 다 아주 훌륭하다고 믿는다.” 어머니가 기뻐하며 말했다.\n\n“너무 확신하진 마세요.” 그가 입을 비틀어 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n침묵이 뒤따랐다. 이 모든 대화에도, 그 침묵에도, 화해에도, 용서에도 어딘가 어색함이 있었고, 모두가 그것을 느끼고 있었다.\n\n‘마치 다들 나를 두려워하는 것 같군.’ 라스콜니코프는 어머니와 누이를 곁눈질하며 속으로 생각했다. 실제로 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 침묵이 길어질수록 점점 더 겁을 먹어 갔다.\n\n‘그런데도 두 사람이 곁에 없을 땐 어찌나 사랑스럽게 느껴졌던지.’ 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n“있잖니, 로쟈, 마르파 페트로브나가 죽었단다.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n“마르파 페트로브나가 누구죠?”\n\n“오, 맙소사 — 마르파 페트로브나 스비드리가일로바 말이다. 내가 편지에 그분 얘기를 그렇게 많이 썼잖니.”\n\n“아 — 아 — 하! 네, 기억나요……. 그래서 그분이 죽었다고요! 오, 정말요?” 그가 잠에서 깨어나듯 갑자기 정신을 차렸다. “무엇 때문에 돌아가셨대요?”\n\n“글쎄, 정말이지 너무도 갑작스럽게.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 그의 호기심에 힘을 얻어 서둘러 대답했다. “바로 내가 너한테 그 편지를 부치던 그날에! 믿어지니, 그 끔찍한 작자가 그분 죽음의 원인인 것 같다는구나. 사람들 말로는 그자가 그분을 무섭게 때렸다지 뭐냐.”\n\n“아니, 그 사람들 사이가 그렇게나 나빴어요?” 그가 누이를 향해 물었다.\n\n“전혀. 사실은 정반대야. 그 여자한테 그는 늘 무척 참을성 있게, 심지어 자상하게 대했어. 사실 결혼 생활 칠 년 내내 그는 그 여자한테 양보했지, 정말이지 많은 경우엔 지나칠 정도로. 그런데 갑자기 인내심을 잃은 것 같아.”\n\n“그렇다면 칠 년이나 자제했다면 그렇게 끔찍한 사람일 리는 없잖아? 너는 그 사람을 두둔하는 것 같구나, 두냐?”\n\n“아니, 아니야, 그는 끔찍한 사람이야! 그보다 더 끔찍한 건 상상도 못 하겠어!” 두냐가 거의 몸서리치다시피 하며, 눈썹을 찌푸리고 생각에 잠겨 들며 대답했다.\n\n“그 일은 아침에 일어났단다.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 서둘러 말을 이었다. “그러고는 곧바로 그 여자가 말에 마구를 채우라고 시켰어, 저녁 식사 후에 곧장 읍내로 마차를 몰고 가려고 말이야. 그 여자는 그런 일이 있을 때면 늘 읍내로 마차를 몰고 가곤 했지. 듣자 하니 그 여자가 아주 잘 먹었다더구나, 저녁을…….”\n\n“얻어맞고 나서요?”\n\n“그게 늘 그 여자의…… 버릇이었어. 그러고는 저녁을 먹자마자, 출발이 늦어지지 않도록 목욕탕에 갔단다……. 알다시피 그 여자는 목욕으로 무슨 치료를 받고 있었거든. 거기 찬 샘물이 있어서, 그 여자는 날마다 거기서 규칙적으로 목욕을 하곤 했는데, 물에 들어서자마자 갑자기 발작을 일으킨 거야!”\n\n“그러고도 남죠.” 조시모프가 말했다.\n\n“그래서 그가 그 여자를 심하게 때렸대요?”\n\n“그게 무슨 상관이야!” 두냐가 끼어들었다.\n\n“흠! 그런데 어머니가 왜 우리한테 이런 뜬소문을 들려주려고 하시는지 모르겠네요.” 라스콜니코프가 자신도 모르게 짜증스레 말했다.\n\n“아, 얘야, 무슨 얘기를 해야 할지 모르겠구나.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 입에서 새어 나왔다.\n\n“아니, 다들 나를 두려워하는 거예요?” 그가 어색한 미소를 띠며 물었다.\n\n“그건 분명 사실이에요.” 두냐가 오빠를 똑바로, 엄하게 바라보며 말했다. “어머니는 계단을 올라오면서 무서워서 성호를 그으셨는걸요.”\n\n그의 얼굴이 경련하듯 일그러졌다.\n\n“아, 무슨 소리를 하는 거니, 두냐! 제발 화내지 마라, 로쟈……. 두냐, 왜 그런 말을 하니?” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 입을 열었다. “있잖니, 이리로 오면서 나는 기차에서 내내 꿈꿨단다, 우리가 어떻게 만날까, 어떻게 다 같이 모든 걸 얘기 나눌까 하고……. 그리고 어찌나 행복했던지 여행하는 것도 느끼지 못했어! 그런데 내가 무슨 말을 하는 거지? 나는 지금 행복하단다……. 그러면 못쓴다, 두냐……. 나는 지금 행복해 — 그저 너를 보는 것만으로도, 로쟈…….”\n\n“쉿, 어머니.” 그가 어머니를 보지 않고 그 손을 꼭 쥐며 당혹스럽게 중얼거렸다. “모든 걸 마음 놓고 얘기할 시간은 얼마든지 있을 테니까요!”\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그는 갑자기 당혹감에 휩싸여 창백해졌다. 다시금 요즘 익히 알게 된 그 끔찍한 감각이 죽음 같은 한기를 몰고 그의 영혼을 스쳐 지나갔다. 다시금 그에게는, 자신이 방금 무서운 거짓말을 했다는 것이 — 이제는 결코 모든 걸 마음 놓고 얘기할 수 없으리라는 것이 — 그 누구에게도 무엇 하나 다시는 입에 올릴 수 없으리라는 것이 갑자기 또렷하고 분명하게 느껴졌다. 이 생각의 고뇌가 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 한순간 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했다. 그는 자리에서 일어나, 아무도 쳐다보지 않고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n“뭐 하는 거야?” 라주미힌이 그의 팔을 붙들며 외쳤다.\n\n그는 다시 자리에 앉아, 말없이 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다. 모두가 어리둥절한 채 그를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n“그런데 다들 왜 이렇게 침울해요?” 그가 갑자기, 전혀 뜻밖에 소리쳤다. “무슨 말이라도 좀 해 보세요! 이렇게 앉아 있어 봐야 무슨 소용이에요? 자, 말 좀 하세요. 얘기를 합시다……. 모처럼 모여서 잠자코 앉아 있기만 하다니……. 자, 아무거나요!”\n\n“다행이구나. 어제 같은 일이 또 시작되는 줄 알고 겁이 났단다.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 성호를 그으며 말했다.\n\n“무슨 일이에요, 로쟈?” 아브도티야 로마노브나가 미심쩍게 물었다.\n\n“아, 아무것도 아니야! 뭔가 생각이 나서 그래.” 그가 대답하고는 갑자기 웃었다.\n\n“뭐, 뭔가 생각이 났다면야 괜찮습니다!…… 저는 또 생각하기 시작했던 게…….” 조시모프가 소파에서 일어나며 중얼거렸다. “이제 저는 갈 시간이군요. 어쩌면 다시 들르겠습니다…… 할 수 있다면요…….” 그는 인사를 하고 나갔다.\n\n“참 훌륭한 분이야!” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말했다.\n\n“네, 훌륭하죠, 멋지고, 교양 있고, 영리하고요.” 라스콜니코프가 지금껏 보이지 않던 놀라운 속도와 생기로 갑자기 말하기 시작했다. “병나기 전에 그를 어디서 만났는지 기억이 안 나네요……. 어딘가에서 만난 것 같긴 한데——……. 그리고 이 친구도 좋은 사람이에요.” 그가 라주미힌을 향해 고갯짓했다. “이 사람이 마음에 들어, 두냐?” 그가 누이에게 묻고는, 갑자기, 무슨 까닭인지 모르게 웃었다.\n\n“아주 많이.” 두냐가 대답했다.\n\n“푸! — 이 돼지 같은 자식!” 라주미힌이 끔찍한 당혹감에 얼굴을 붉히며 항의하고는, 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 희미하게 미소 지었지만, 라스콜니코프는 소리 내어 웃었다.\n\n“어디 가는 거야?”\n\n“가 봐야 해.”\n\n“전혀 그럴 필요 없어. 있어. 조시모프도 갔으니 자네는 더더욱 있어야지. 가지 마. 몇 시야? 열두 시인가? 시계가 참 예쁘구나, 두냐. 그런데 다들 왜 또 잠자코 있어요? 나 혼자만 떠들고 있잖아.”\n\n“마르파 페트로브나가 준 거야.” 두냐가 대답했다.\n\n“그것도 아주 값비싼 거란다!” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 덧붙였다.\n\n“아 — 하! 어쩜 이리 크지! 숙녀용 같지가 않은걸.”\n\n“난 이런 게 좋아.” 두냐가 말했다.\n\n‘그럼 약혼자가 준 선물은 아니로군.’ 라주미힌은 생각하며, 까닭 없이 기뻐졌다.\n\n“루진이 준 선물인 줄 알았어.” 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n“아니, 그분은 아직 두냐한테 아무 선물도 안 했단다.”\n\n“아 — 하! 그런데 어머니, 기억하세요, 제가 사랑에 빠져 결혼하고 싶어 했던 거?” 그가 갑자기 어머니를 바라보며 말했고, 어머니는 화제가 갑자기 바뀌고 그가 그 얘기를 꺼내는 방식에 당황했다.\n\n“오, 그럼, 얘야.”\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 두냐, 라주미힌과 눈빛을 주고받았다.\n\n“흠, 그래요. 무슨 얘기를 해 드릴까요? 사실 별로 기억나는 게 없어요. 어찌나 병약한 처녀였던지.” 그가 다시 몽롱해지며 눈을 내리깔고 말을 이었다. “완전히 환자였죠. 그 애는 가난한 이들에게 적선하기를 좋아했고, 늘 수녀원에 들어가는 걸 꿈꿨는데, 한번은 그 얘기를 저한테 꺼내다가 그만 울음을 터뜨렸어요. 네, 네, 기억나요. 아주 잘 기억나요. 못생기고 자그마한 애였죠. 그때 제가 왜 그 애한테 끌렸는지 정말 모르겠어요 — 늘 아팠기 때문이었던 것 같아요. 만일 그 애가 절름발이거나 곱사등이였다면, 아마 그 애를 더더욱 좋아했을걸요.” 그가 몽롱하게 미소 지었다. “네, 그건 일종의 봄날의 헛것에 들뜬 거였어요.”\n\n“아니, 그건 그저 봄날의 헛것만은 아니었어.” 두냐가 따뜻한 감정을 담아 말했다.\n\n그는 긴장하여 골똘한 눈길을 누이에게 고정했지만, 그녀의 말을 듣지 못했거나 이해하지 못했다. 그러더니 완전히 생각에 잠겨 일어나, 어머니에게 다가가 입을 맞추고는, 제자리로 돌아와 앉았다.\n\n“지금도 그 애를 사랑하니?” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 감동하여 말했다.\n\n“그 애요? 지금이요? 오, 네……. 그 애에 대해 물으시는 거예요? 아니요…… 이제 그 모든 건, 말하자면, 다른 세상의 일 같아요…… 그리고 너무도 오래전 일이죠. 사실 여기서 일어나는 모든 일도 어쩐지 멀게만 느껴져요.” 그가 그들을 유심히 바라보았다. “지금 두 분도요…… 저는 두 분을 천 마일 밖에서 바라보고 있는 것 같아요…… 하지만, 우리가 왜 그런 얘기를 하고 있는지 누가 알겠어요! 그리고 그런 걸 물어서 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?” 그가 짜증스럽게 덧붙이고는 손톱을 깨물며, 다시 몽롱한 침묵에 빠져들었다.\n\n“얼마나 비참한 셋방이냐, 로쟈! 꼭 무덤 같구나.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 갑자기 그 무거운 침묵을 깨며 말했다. “네가 이렇게 우울해진 건 분명 절반은 이 셋방 탓일 게다.”\n\n“셋방이요.” 그가 무기력하게 대답했다. “네, 셋방이 큰 몫을 했죠……. 저도 그렇게 생각했어요……. 하지만 어머니가 방금 얼마나 이상한 말씀을 하셨는지 아세요.” 그가 기묘하게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n조금만 더 가면, 삼 년의 부재 끝에 그와 함께한 이 어머니와 이 누이라는 동반자도, 정작 무엇 하나 진정으로 이야기할 수 없는 처지에 놓인 채 나누는 이 친밀한 어조의 대화도, 그의 인내력으로는 감당할 수 없는 것이 되고 말 터였다. 그러나 그날 어떤 식으로든 결판을 내야 할 긴박한 문제가 하나 있었으니 — 잠에서 깼을 때 그가 그렇게 결심했던 것이다. 이제 그는 그것을 탈출구 삼아 떠올린 것을 다행으로 여겼다.\n\n“들어 봐, 두냐.” 그가 진지하고 무미건조하게 입을 열었다. “물론 어제 일은 사과할게, 하지만 다시 한번 말해 두는 게 내 의무라고 생각해, 나는 내 주요한 입장을 철회하지 않아. 나냐 루진이냐 둘 중 하나야. 내가 비열한 놈이라면, 너만은 그래선 안 돼. 한 사람으로 족하니까. 네가 루진과 결혼한다면, 나는 그 즉시 너를 누이로 여기지 않을 거야.”\n\n“로쟈, 로쟈! 또 어제랑 똑같구나.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 슬프게 외쳤다. “그리고 너는 어째서 자신을 비열한 놈이라고 부르니? 나는 그걸 견딜 수가 없다. 어제도 똑같은 말을 했잖니.”\n\n“오빠.” 두냐가 똑같이 무미건조하게, 단호히 대답했다. “이 모든 게 오빠 쪽의 오해야. 밤새 곰곰이 생각해 보고 그 오해가 뭔지 알아냈어. 이게 다 오빠가 내가 누군가에게, 누군가를 위해 나 자신을 희생한다고 멋대로 상상하기 때문이야. 그건 결코 사실이 아니야. 나는 그저 내 자신을 위해 결혼하는 거야, 내 처지가 힘들기 때문에. 물론, 그렇게 해서 가족에게 도움이 될 수 있다면 기쁘겠지. 하지만 그게 내 결정의 주된 동기는 아니야…….”\n\n‘거짓말을 하고 있군.’ 그는 앙심을 품고 손톱을 깨물며 속으로 생각했다. ‘오만한 것! 자선으로 그러는 거라는 걸 인정하려 들지 않아! 너무도 도도하지! 오, 천박한 인간들! 저들은 사랑조차 미워하듯이 하는군……. 오, 어쩜 이리…… 저들 모두가 다 미울까!’\n\n“사실,” 두냐가 말을 이었다. “나는 두 가지 악 가운데 덜한 쪽을 택해서 표트르 페트로비치와 결혼하는 거야. 그가 나한테 기대하는 모든 걸 정직하게 할 작정이니까, 나는 그를 속이는 게 아니야……. 방금 왜 미소 지었어?” 그녀 역시 얼굴을 붉혔고, 두 눈에는 분노의 빛이 번뜩였다.\n\n“모든 걸?” 그가 악의에 찬 히죽거림으로 물었다.\n\n“어느 정도 한계 안에서. 표트르 페트로비치의 구애 방식과 그 태도가 그가 무엇을 원하는지 단번에 내게 보여 줬어. 물론 그가 자기 자신을 너무 좋게 여길지도 모르지만, 나 역시 존중해 주리라고 기대해……. 왜 또 웃는 거야?”\n\n“그럼 너는 왜 또 얼굴을 붉히는데? 거짓말을 하고 있군, 누이. 너는 일부러 거짓말을 하고 있어, 그저 여자의 고집에서, 그저 나한테 지지 않으려고……. 너는 루진을 존경할 수 없어. 나는 그를 만나 봤고 얘기도 나눠 봤으니까. 그러니까 너는 돈에 자신을 팔고 있는 거고, 그러니 어떤 경우든 너는 비열하게 행동하고 있는 거야, 그래도 적어도 네가 그걸 부끄러워하며 얼굴을 붉힐 수는 있다니 다행이군.”\n\n“사실이 아니야. 나는 거짓말을 하는 게 아니야.” 두냐가 평정을 잃고 외쳤다. “그가 나를 존중하고 높이 평가한다고 확신하지 못했다면 나는 그와 결혼하지 않을 거야. 내가 그를 존경할 수 있다고 굳게 확신하지 못했다면 나는 그와 결혼하지 않을 거야. 다행히도 나는 바로 오늘 그에 대한 확실한 증거를 얻을 수 있어…… 그리고 그런 결혼은 오빠 말처럼 비열한 짓이 아니야! 게다가 설령 오빠 말이 옳다 해도, 정말로 내가 비열한 행동을 결심했다 한들, 나한테 그렇게 말하는 건 오빠 쪽에서 무자비한 짓 아니야? 어쩌면 오빠 자신도 갖지 못한 영웅적 행위를 왜 나한테 요구하는 거지? 그건 전제(專制)야, 폭정이라고. 내가 누군가를 망친다면, 그건 오직 나 자신뿐이야……. 나는 살인을 저지르는 게 아니야. 왜 그렇게 나를 쳐다봐? 왜 그렇게 창백해진 거야? 로쟈, 오빠, 무슨 일이야?”\n\n“맙소사! 네가 오빠를 기절시켰구나!” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다.\n\n“아니, 아니에요, 별것 아니에요! 아무것도 아닙니다. 잠깐 어지러운 것뿐이에요 — 기절은 아니고요. 어머니는 머릿속이 온통 기절 생각뿐이시군요. 흠, 그래, 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었지? 아, 그래. 너는 오늘 어떤 식으로 그를 존경할 수 있다는 확실한 증거를, 그리고 그가…… 네 말마따나 너를 존중한다는 증거를 얻겠다는 거지. 오늘이라고 했지, 아마?”\n\n“어머니, 표트르 페트로비치의 편지를 로쟈한테 보여 주세요.” 두냐가 말했다.\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 떨리는 손으로 그에게 편지를 건넸다. 그는 큰 흥미를 보이며 그것을 받았지만, 펼치기 전에 갑자기 일종의 경이로움을 담고 두냐를 바라보았다.\n\n“이상하군.” 그가 새로운 생각에 충격받은 듯 천천히 말했다. “내가 왜 이런 야단법석을 떠는 거지? 이게 다 무슨 일이야? 네가 좋아하는 사람과 결혼하렴!”\n\n그는 이 말을 마치 혼잣말처럼, 그러나 소리 내어 했고, 한동안 어리둥절한 듯 누이를 바라보았다. 마침내 그는, 여전히 그 기묘한 경이로움의 표정을 얼굴에 띤 채 편지를 펼쳤다. 그러고는 천천히, 주의 깊게 읽기 시작해, 두 번을 통독했다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 뚜렷한 불안을 드러냈고, 사실 모두가 무언가 특별한 것을 기대하고 있었다.\n\n“내가 놀라운 건,” 그가 잠시 멈췄다가, 편지를 어머니에게 건네면서, 그러나 딱히 누구를 향한 것도 아닌 채 입을 열었다. “이 사람이 사업가에 법률가이고, 말하는 것도 정말 거창한데, 정작 이렇게 무식한 편지를 쓴다는 점이에요.”\n\n모두가 흠칫했다. 그들은 전혀 다른 것을 기대하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n“하지만 다들 그렇게 쓰는걸, 자네도 알잖아.” 라주미힌이 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n“너도 이걸 읽었어?”\n\n“응.”\n\n“우리가 저 친구한테 보여 줬단다, 로쟈. 우리가…… 방금 저 친구와 의논했어.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 멋쩍게 입을 열었다.\n\n“그게 바로 법정의 상투어야.” 라주미힌이 끼어들었다. “법률 문서는 지금까지도 그렇게 쓰이거든.”\n\n“법률? 그래, 딱 법률식이지 — 사무용 언어 — 그리 무식하지도 않고, 그렇다고 아주 교양 있지도 않은 — 사무용 언어!”\n\n“표트르 페트로비치는 자신이 변변찮은 교육을 받았다는 걸 숨기지 않아요, 오히려 자기 힘으로 일어선 걸 자랑스러워하죠.” 아브도티야 로마노브나가 오빠의 어조에 다소 기분이 상한 듯 말했다.\n\n“뭐, 그게 자랑스럽다면 그럴 만한 이유가 있는 거겠지, 그건 부정 않아. 너는 기분이 상한 모양이구나, 누이, 내가 편지에 대해 이렇게 하찮은 비평이나 한다고, 그리고 내가 너를 짜증 나게 하려고 일부러 이런 시시한 얘기를 한다고 생각하면서. 하지만 정반대야, 문체에 관해서 한 가지 짚이는 게 있는데, 지금 형편으로 봐선 결코 무관한 게 아니거든. ‘너희들 자신을 탓하라’는 표현이 한 군데 있는데, 무척 의미심장하고도 명백하게 박혀 있고, 게다가 내가 그 자리에 있으면 당장 떠나 버리겠다는 협박도 있어. 떠나겠다는 그 협박은, 두 사람이 말을 듣지 않으면 둘 다 버리겠다는 협박과 다름없고, 페테르부르크로 불러 놓고는 이제 와서 버리겠다는 거지. 자, 어떻게 생각해? 만일 이 친구가(그가 라주미힌을 가리켰다), 혹은 조시모프가, 혹은 우리 중 누군가가 이런 말을 썼다면 우리가 분개했을 텐데, 루진한테서 나온 그런 표현은 분개해도 되는 거 아닌가?”\n\n“아 — 니.” 두냐가 좀 더 활기를 띠고 대답했다. “나는 그게 너무 순진하게 표현됐다는 것을, 그리고 어쩌면 그가 그저 글재주가 없는 거라는 걸 분명히 알았어……. 그건 정확한 비평이야, 오빠. 사실 나는 미처 생각지 못했는데…….”\n\n“그건 법률식으로 표현된 거라, 어쩌면 그가 의도한 것보다 더 거칠게 들리는 거야. 하지만 너의 환상을 조금 깨야겠구나. 이 편지엔 한 가지 표현이, 나에 대한 비방이 하나 있는데, 그것도 꽤나 비열한 거야. 나는 어젯밤에 그 돈을, 슬픔에 짓눌린 폐병 걸린 과부한테 줬어, 그것도 ‘장례를 핑계 삼아’가 아니라 그저 장례 비용으로 쓰라고, 그리고 그가 쓴 대로 ‘소문난 품행의 젊은 여자’라는 그 딸한테(나는 어젯밤에 난생처음 그 여자를 봤다)가 아니라 과부한테 준 거야. 이 모든 데서 나는 나를 비방하고 우리 사이에 불화를 일으키려는 너무도 성급한 욕망을 봐. 그것도 다시 법률식 상투어로, 다시 말해 목적이 너무 빤히 드러나게, 그리고 무척 순진한 열의로 표현돼 있지. 그는 영리한 사람이지만, 분별 있게 행동하려면 영리함만으로는 부족한 법이야. 이 모든 게 그 사람됨을 보여 주고…… 나는 그가 너를 그리 존중하는 것 같지 않아. 나는 이 말을 그저 너한테 경고하려고 하는 거야, 진심으로 너의 행복을 바라니까…….”\n\n두냐는 대꾸하지 않았다. 그녀의 결심은 이미 굳어져 있었다. 그녀는 그저 저녁을 기다릴 뿐이었다.\n\n“그래서 네 결정은 뭐냐, 로쟈?” 그의 말이 갑작스레 새로 띤 사무적인 어조에 그 어느 때보다 불안해진 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 물었다.\n\n“무슨 결정이요?”\n\n“있잖니, 표트르 페트로비치가 오늘 저녁에 네가 우리와 함께 있어선 안 되고, 만일 네가 오면 자기가 떠나 버리겠다고 썼잖니. 그러니 너는…… 올 거니?”\n\n“그건, 물론, 제가 정할 일이 아니라 먼저 어머니가 정하실 일이죠, 어머니가 그런 부탁에 기분 상하지 않으셨다면요. 그리고 두 번째로는 두냐가 정할 일이고요, 두냐 역시 기분 상하지 않았다면. 저는 두 분이 가장 좋다고 여기시는 대로 하겠습니다.” 그가 무미건조하게 덧붙였다.\n\n“두냐가 이미 결정했고, 나도 그 애한테 전적으로 동의한다.” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 서둘러 단언했다.\n\n“나는 오빠한테 부탁하기로 결정했어, 로쟈, 이 만남에 꼭 우리와 함께 있어 달라고 간곡히 청하려고.” 두냐가 말했다. “와 줄 거지?”\n\n“그래.”\n\n“당신께도 부탁드릴게요, 여덟 시에 우리와 함께 있어 주세요.” 그녀가 라주미힌을 향해 말했다. “어머니, 저는 이분도 초대하겠어요.”\n\n“잘 생각했다, 두냐. 그래, 네가 결정했다면,” 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 덧붙였다. “그렇게 하자꾸나. 나도 마음이 한결 편하겠다. 나는 숨기고 속이는 걸 좋아하지 않아. 차라리 우리가 모든 진실을 드러내는 편이 낫지……. 표트르 페트로비치가 화를 내든 말든, 이제!”\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n바로 그 순간 문이 살그머니 열리더니, 한 처녀가 겁먹은 듯 주위를 둘러보며 방 안으로 들어왔다. 모두가 놀라움과 호기심에 차서 그녀에게로 고개를 돌렸다. 라스콜니코프는 첫눈에 그녀를 알아보지 못했다. 그녀는 소피야 세묘노브나 마르멜라도프였다. 그는 어제 처음 그녀를 보았지만, 그것은 너무도 다른 순간, 다른 환경, 다른 옷차림 속에서였기에 그의 기억에는 사뭇 다른 모습이 남아 있었다. 지금 그녀는 수수하고 초라한 옷차림의 처녀였는데, 정말이지 무척 어려서, 거의 어린아이 같았고, 단정하고 우아한 몸가짐에, 솔직하면서도 어딘가 겁먹은 듯한 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 그녀는 너무도 평범한 실내복을 입고, 낡고 구식인 모자를 쓰고 있었으나, 여전히 양산만은 들고 있었다. 뜻밖에도 방 안이 사람들로 가득한 것을 보고, 그녀는 당황했다기보다는 마치 어린아이처럼 수줍음에 완전히 압도되어 버렸다. 심지어 물러나려고까지 했다. \"아…… 당신이군요!\" 라스콜니코프가 몹시 놀라 말했고, 그 자신도 당황했다. 그는 어머니와 누이가 루진의 편지를 통해 '품행이 좋지 않다고 소문난 어느 젊은 여자'에 대해 알고 있다는 사실이 곧장 떠올랐다. 그는 방금 전 루진의 중상을 반박하며, 어젯밤 그 처녀를 처음 보았노라고 단언하던 참이었는데, 갑자기 그녀가 걸어 들어온 것이다. 또한 그는 '품행이 좋지 않다고 소문난'이라는 표현에 대해서는 반박하지 않았던 것도 기억해 냈다. 이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 어렴풋하고도 덧없이 스쳐 지나갔지만, 그녀를 좀 더 유심히 바라보니, 이 모욕당한 존재가 너무도 심하게 모욕당해 있어서 그는 갑자기 그녀가 가엾어졌다. 그녀가 겁에 질려 물러나려고 몸을 움직였을 때, 그것은 그의 가슴을 찌르는 듯한 아픔을 안겨 주었다.\n\n\"당신이 올 줄은 몰랐습니다.\" 그는 황급히, 그녀를 멈춰 세우는 듯한 눈길로 말했다. \"제발 앉으세요. 카테리나 이바노브나가 보내서 오셨겠지요. 잠깐만요 — 거기 말고. 여기 앉으세요…….\"\n\n소냐가 들어서자, 라스콜니코프의 의자 셋 가운데 하나에 문 가까이 앉아 있던 라주미힌이 그녀가 들어올 수 있도록 일어섰다. 라스콜니코프는 처음에 그녀에게 조시모프가 앉아 있던 소파 자리를 가리켰으나, 침대 노릇을 하는 그 소파가 너무 사적인 자리라는 생각이 들어, 황급히 라주미힌의 의자 쪽으로 그녀에게 손짓했다.\n\n\"자네는 여기 앉게.\" 그는 라주미힌을 소파에 앉히며 말했다.\n\n소냐는 겁에 질려 거의 떨다시피 하며 앉았고, 두 부인을 겁먹은 듯 바라보았다. 자신이 그들 곁에 앉을 수 있다는 것이 그녀 자신에게는 거의 상상조차 할 수 없는 일인 듯했다. 그 생각만으로도 너무 겁이 나서 그녀는 황급히 다시 일어났고, 완전히 당황한 채 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"저…… 저는…… 잠깐 들렀어요. 방해해서 죄송합니다.\" 그녀는 더듬더듬 말을 시작했다. \"카테리나 이바노브나가 보내서 왔어요, 보낼 사람이 달리 없었거든요. 카테리나 이바노브나가 부탁드리랬어요…… 미사에 참석해 주시라고…… 아침에…… 미트로파니옙스키에서…… 그러고 나서…… 저희 집으로…… 그분 집으로…… 와 주시는 영광을…… 부탁드리랬어요…….\" 소냐는 말을 더듬다가 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"노력하겠습니다, 꼭, 반드시요.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다. 그도 일어섰고, 그도 말을 더듬으며 문장을 끝맺지 못했다. \"제발 앉으세요.\" 그가 불쑥 말했다. \"당신과 얘기하고 싶습니다. 바쁘신지도 모르지만, 부디 친절을 베풀어 이 분만 시간을 내주세요.\" 그러고는 그녀에게 의자를 끌어다 주었다.\n\n소냐는 다시 앉았고, 또다시 겁먹은 듯 두 부인을 허둥지둥 흘끔거리고는 눈을 떨구었다. 라스콜니코프의 창백한 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올랐고, 전율이 그를 스쳤으며, 그의 눈이 이글거렸다.\n\n\"어머니.\" 그가 단호하고 끈질기게 말했다. \"이분은 소피야 세묘노브나 마르멜라도프입니다, 어제 제 눈앞에서 마차에 치인, 그 불행한 마르멜라도프 씨의 따님이지요, 방금 제가 어머니께 이야기했던 분요.\"\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 소냐를 흘끗 바라보더니 눈을 살짝 가늘게 떴다. 로쟈의 절박하고 도전적인 눈길 앞에서 당황하면서도, 그녀는 그 만족감만은 차마 억누를 수가 없었다. 두냐는 진지하고 골똘하게 그 가엾은 처녀의 얼굴을 응시하며, 의아한 표정으로 그녀를 뜯어보았다. 소냐는 자신이 소개받는 것을 듣고 다시 눈을 들어 올리려 했으나, 어느 때보다 더 당황하고 말았다.\n\n\"여쭤보고 싶었는데요.\" 라스콜니코프가 서둘러 말했다. \"어제 일은 어떻게 처리됐나요? 이를테면 경찰이 당신들을 괴롭히지는 않았나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 다 괜찮았어요…… 사인이 너무 명백했거든요…… 우릴 괴롭히지 않았어요…… 다만 세입자들이 화가 나 있어요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"시신이 너무 오래 남아 있어서요. 보세요, 지금은 더우니까요. 그래서 오늘 시신을 묘지로, 예배당으로 옮기게 돼요, 내일까지요. 처음엔 카테리나 이바노브나가 꺼렸지만, 이제는 그분 자신도 그게 어쩔 수 없다는 걸 알아요…….\"\n\n\"그럼 오늘이군요?\"\n\n\"그분은 내일 교회에서 미사에 참석해 주시는 영광을, 그러고 나서 장례 식사 자리에도 참석해 주시기를 부탁드린대요.\"\n\n\"장례 식사를 차린다고요?\"\n\n\"네…… 그냥 조촐하게요……. 어제 저희를 도와주신 데 대해 정말 감사드린다고 전해 달라셨어요. 당신이 아니었다면 저희는 장례에 쓸 게 아무것도 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n별안간 그녀의 입술과 턱이 떨리기 시작했지만, 그녀는 안간힘을 다해 자신을 억누르며 다시 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n대화가 오가는 동안 라스콜니코프는 그녀를 유심히 살폈다. 그녀는 야위고, 너무도 야윈, 창백한 작은 얼굴에, 다소 고르지 못하고 모가 나 있었으며, 작고 뾰족한 코와 턱을 지니고 있었다. 예쁘다고는 할 수 없었지만, 그녀의 파란 눈은 너무도 맑았고, 그 눈이 빛날 때면 표정 속에 어찌나 다정함과 순박함이 어리는지 마음이 끌리지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀의 얼굴은, 아니 사실 그녀의 온몸은 또 다른 독특한 특징을 지니고 있었다. 열여덟이라는 나이에도 불구하고, 그녀는 거의 어린 소녀처럼 — 거의 어린아이처럼 보였다. 그리고 그녀의 어떤 몸짓에서는 이 어린애 같은 면이 거의 우스꽝스럽게 느껴지기까지 했다.\n\n\"하지만 그렇게 적은 돈으로 카테리나 이바노브나가 감당할 수 있었나요? 정말 장례 식사까지 차릴 생각인가요?\" 라스콜니코프가 끈질기게 대화를 이어 가며 물었다.\n\n\"관은 물론 소박할 거예요…… 모든 게 다 소박할 테니까, 비용이 많이 들진 않을 거예요. 카테리나 이바노브나랑 제가 다 셈을 해 봤거든요, 그래서 남는 돈도 좀 있을 거예요…… 카테리나 이바노브나는 꼭 그래야 한다고 무척 애를 쓰셨어요. 아시잖아요, 그러지 않을 수가 없는 걸…… 그게 그분에겐 위안이 되거든요…… 그분은 그런 분이세요, 아시잖아요…….\"\n\n\"이해합니다, 이해해요…… 물론이죠…… 왜 그렇게 제 방을 보시는 거죠? 어머니도 방금 무덤 같다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 어제 가진 걸 전부 저희에게 주셨잖아요.\" 소냐가 갑자기 큰 소리로 빠르게 속삭이듯 대답했다. 그러고는 또다시 당황하여 고개를 떨구었다. 그녀의 입술과 턱이 또 한 번 떨리고 있었다. 그녀는 라스콜니코프의 초라한 환경에 곧장 충격을 받았고, 이제 그 말이 절로 터져 나온 것이었다. 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 두냐의 눈에는 빛이 어렸고, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나조차 소냐를 다정한 눈길로 바라보았다.\n\n\"로쟈.\" 그녀가 일어서며 말했다. \"우리 함께 저녁을 먹어야지, 당연히. 가자, 두냐……. 그리고 너, 로쟈, 산책이라도 좀 하다가, 우리한테 오기 전에 쉬면서 좀 누워 있는 게 좋겠다……. 우리가 너를 너무 지치게 한 것 같구나…….\"\n\n\"네, 네, 갈게요.\" 그는 부산스레 일어나며 대답했다. \"하지만 처리할 일이 좀 있어서요.\"\n\n\"하지만 분명 함께 저녁을 먹는 거지?\" 라주미힌이 놀란 눈으로 라스콜니코프를 바라보며 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 소린가?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 갑니다…… 물론이죠, 물론이고말고요! 그리고 자네는 잠깐만 남아 있게. 어머니, 지금 당장 얘를 데려가실 건 아니죠? 아니면 제가 어머니한테서 얘를 빼앗는 건가요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니, 아니다. 그러면, 드미트리 프로코피치, 우리와 함께 저녁 식사를 해 주는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"부디 그래 주세요.\" 두냐가 덧붙였다.\n\n라주미힌은 그야말로 환히 빛나며 고개를 숙였다. 한순간 그들 모두가 묘하게 당황했다.\n\n\"잘 있어, 로쟈, 아니, 또 만날 때까지구나. 난 작별 인사하는 걸 좋아하지 않아. 잘 있어요, 나스타시야. 아, 또 작별 인사를 하고 말았네.\"\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 소냐에게도 인사를 건네려 했으나, 어쩐지 잘 되지 않았고, 그녀는 허둥거리며 방을 나갔다.\n\n그러나 아브도티야 로마노브나는 자기 차례를 기다리고 있던 듯, 어머니를 따라 나가면서 소냐에게 정중하고 예의 바른 인사를 건넸다. 소냐는 당황하여 황급히, 겁먹은 듯 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 답례했다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 가슴 저미는 불편함의 빛이 어렸으니, 마치 아브도티야 로마노브나의 정중함과 배려가 그녀에게는 짓누르는 듯 괴롭게 느껴지는 것 같았다.\n\n\"두냐, 잘 가.\" 라스콜니코프가 통로에서 불렀다. \"손 좀 줘 봐.\"\n\n\"아니, 아까 줬잖아. 잊었어?\" 두냐가 다정하면서도 어색하게 그에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"상관없어, 다시 한번 줘 봐.\" 그러고는 그녀의 손가락을 다정하게 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n두냐는 미소 짓고, 얼굴을 붉히며 손을 빼더니, 무척 행복한 기분으로 떠나갔다.\n\n\"자, 정말 좋군요.\" 그는 돌아와 그녀를 밝은 눈으로 바라보며 소냐에게 말했다. \"하느님이 죽은 이에게 평안을 주시기를, 산 사람은 그래도 살아가야 하는 법이죠. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n소냐는 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 환해진 데에 놀란 듯 그를 바라보았다. 그는 잠시 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다. 그 순간 죽은 아버지의 온갖 사연이 그의 기억 속에 떠올랐다…….\n\n\"맙소사, 두냐.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 거리로 나서자마자 입을 열었다. \"솔직히 나는 그곳을 떠나오니 마음이 놓이는구나 — 한결 편해졌어. 어제 기차에서, 내가 그런 마음을 다 기뻐하게 될 줄을 어찌 알았겠니.\"\n\n\"다시 말씀드리지만, 어머니, 오빠는 아직 많이 아파요. 안 보이세요? 어쩌면 우리 걱정 때문에 마음이 어지러워졌는지도 몰라요. 우리가 참아야 해요, 그러면 아주, 아주 많은 걸 용서할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 너는 별로 참지 않더구나!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 격하고도 시샘하듯 그녀의 말을 받아쳤다. \"있잖니, 두냐, 나는 너희 둘을 보고 있었단다. 너는 영락없이 그애를 빼다 박았어, 얼굴보다도 마음이 더 그래. 너희 둘 다 우울하고, 둘 다 시무룩하고 성미가 급하고, 둘 다 거만하면서도 둘 다 너그럽지……. 설마 그애가 이기주의자일 리는 없겠지, 두냐. 응? 오늘 저녁에 우리한테 무슨 일이 닥칠지 생각하면, 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는구나!\"\n\n\"마음 졸이지 마세요, 어머니. 될 일은 어차피 되게 마련이에요.\"\n\n\"두냐, 우리가 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 좀 생각해 보렴! 만약에 표트르 페트로비치가 파혼이라도 하면 어쩌니?\" 가엾은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 경솔하게 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그가 그런다면 그만한 가치도 없는 사람이죠.\" 두냐가 날카롭고 경멸하듯 대답했다.\n\n\"우리가 잘 빠져나온 거야.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 황급히 말을 끊었다. \"그애가 무슨 일인지 처리할 게 있어서 바쁘다고 했잖니. 밖에 나가서 바람이라도 좀 쐬면…… 그 방은 숨 막히게 답답하더구나……. 그런데 여기선 어디서 바람을 쐰단 말이냐? 여긴 거리조차 꽉 막힌 방 같구나. 맙소사! 이게 무슨 도시람!…… 잠깐…… 이쪽으로…… 깔리겠다 — 뭘 나르고 있어. 아니, 저건 피아노로구나, 정말이지…… 어쩜 저렇게 밀어 대는지!…… 나는 그 젊은 여자도 무척 마음에 걸리는구나.\"\n\n\"무슨 젊은 여자요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 소피야 세묘노브나 말이다, 방금 거기 있었던.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"예감이 들어, 두냐. 음, 믿든 안 믿든 네 맘이다만, 그 여자가 들어온 순간, 바로 그 순간, 나는 그 여자가 이 모든 골칫거리의 주된 원인이라는 걸 느꼈단다…….\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아요!\" 두냐가 화가 나서 외쳤다. \"그놈의 예감이라니, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리예요, 어머니! 오빠는 그 전날 저녁에야 그 여자를 알게 됐고, 그 여자가 들어왔을 때 알아보지도 못했잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 두고 보면 알 거다……. 그 여자가 신경 쓰여. 하지만 두고 봐, 두고 보라고! 나는 어찌나 겁이 나던지. 그 여자가 그런 눈으로 나를 응시하고 있었어. 그애가 그 여자를 소개하기 시작했을 때 나는 의자에 가만히 앉아 있기조차 힘들었단다, 기억나니? 참 이상한 일이야, 표트르 페트로비치는 그 여자에 대해 그렇게 써 놓고선, 그애는 그 여자를 우리한테 — 너한테 소개하다니! 그러니 그애가 그 여자를 무척 마음에 두고 있는 게 분명해.\"\n\n\"사람들은 무슨 말이든 써 댈 수 있어요. 우리에 대해서도 이러쿵저러쿵 말이 많았고 글로도 쓰였잖아요. 잊으셨어요? 분명 그 여자는 좋은 사람이고, 다 터무니없는 소리예요.\"\n\n\"제발 그랬으면 좋으련만!\"\n\n\"그리고 표트르 페트로비치는 비열한 중상모략꾼이에요.\" 두냐가 갑자기 쏘아붙였다.\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 풀이 죽었고, 대화는 더 이어지지 않았다.\n\n\"자네한테 부탁할 게 뭔지 말해 주지.\" 라스콜니코프가 라주미힌을 창가로 끌어당기며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 카테리나 이바노브나한테 당신이 오신다고 전할게요.\" 소냐가 떠날 채비를 하며 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 소피야 세묘노브나. 우리한테 비밀 같은 건 없습니다. 당신이 방해되는 건 아니에요. 당신과 한두 마디 더 나누고 싶군요. 들어 봐!\" 그는 다시 라주미힌에게로 불쑥 돌아섰다. \"그…… 이름이 뭐더라…… 포르피리 페트로비치 알지?\"\n\n\"알다마다! 친척인데. 왜?\" 라주미힌이 흥미를 보이며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그가 그 사건을 맡고 있지 않나…… 알잖아, 그 살인 사건 말이야……. 어제 자네가 그 얘기를 했었지.\"\n\n\"그래…… 그런데?\" 라주미힌의 눈이 휘둥그레졌다.\n\n\"그가 물건을 저당 잡힌 사람들을 수소문하고 있었는데, 나도 거기에 저당물이 몇 개 있거든 — 하찮은 거지만 — 집을 떠날 때 누이가 기념으로 준 반지하고, 아버지의 은시계인데 — 다 합쳐 봐야 오 루블이나 육 루블 값어치밖에 안 되지만…… 나한텐 소중한 거야. 그러니 이제 어떻게 해야 하지? 그 물건들을 잃고 싶지 않아, 특히 시계는. 아까 두냐의 시계 얘기가 나왔을 때, 어머니가 그걸 보자고 할까 봐 조마조마했다네. 그건 우리한테 남은 아버지의 유일한 유품이거든. 그게 없어지면 어머니가 앓아누우실 거야. 여자들이 어떤지 자네도 알잖아. 그러니 어떻게 해야 할지 말해 줘. 경찰서에 신고했어야 한다는 건 알지만, 곧장 포르피리한테 가는 게 낫지 않을까? 응? 어떻게 생각해? 일이 더 빨리 처리될 수도 있잖아. 알다시피, 어머니가 저녁 식사 전에 그걸 보자고 할지도 모르거든.\"\n\n\"절대 경찰서는 안 돼. 무조건 포르피리한테 가야지.\" 라주미힌이 유난히 흥분하여 소리쳤다. \"야, 정말 잘됐다. 당장 가자. 몇 걸음만 가면 돼. 분명 그를 만날 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"좋아, 가지.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 친구는 자네와 알게 되는 걸 무척, 무척 기뻐할 거야. 이런저런 때에 그한테 자네 얘기를 자주 했거든. 어제도 자네 얘기를 했어. 가자. 그러니까 자네가 그 노파를 알고 있었단 말이지? 그렇구나! 모든 게 멋지게 풀려 가는걸……. 아, 그래, 소피야 이바노브나……\"\n\n\"소피야 세묘노브나입니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 바로잡았다. \"소피야 세묘노브나, 이쪽은 제 친구 라주미힌인데, 좋은 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"이제 가셔야 한다면요.\" 소냐가 라주미힌은 아예 쳐다보지도 않고, 더욱더 당황하며 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"가지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 결정했다. \"오늘 당신을 찾아가겠습니다, 소피야 세묘노브나. 어디 사는지만 말해 줘요.\"\n\n그는 딱히 불편해하는 것은 아니었지만, 서두르는 듯했고, 그녀의 눈길을 피했다. 소냐는 주소를 일러 주었고, 그러면서 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그들은 모두 함께 밖으로 나왔다.\n\n\"문을 안 잠그는 거야?\" 라주미힌이 그를 따라 계단으로 나서며 물었다.\n\n\"전혀.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다. \"이 년째 자물쇠를 사야지 사야지 하고 있어. 자물쇠가 필요 없는 사람은 행복한 거지.\" 그가 소냐에게 웃으며 말했다. 그들은 대문간에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"오른쪽으로 가시나요, 소피야 세묘노브나? 그건 그렇고, 어떻게 나를 찾아냈죠?\" 그가 마치 전혀 다른 말을 하고 싶은 듯이 덧붙였다. 그는 그녀의 부드럽고 맑은 눈을 들여다보고 싶었지만, 그것은 쉽지 않았다.\n\n\"아니, 어제 폴렌카한테 주소를 알려 주셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"폴렌카? 아, 그래요, 폴렌카, 그 어린 여자아이 말이군요. 당신 동생인가요? 내가 그애한테 주소를 알려 줬다고요?\"\n\n\"아니, 잊으셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 기억나요.\"\n\n\"아버지가 당신 얘기를 하시는 걸 들었어요…… 다만 저는 성함을 몰랐고, 아버지도 모르셨죠. 그래서 오늘 와서…… 성함을 알게 됐으니, '라스콜니코프 씨는 어디 사시나요?' 하고 물었던 거예요. 당신도 방 한 칸만 세 들어 사시는 줄은 몰랐어요……. 안녕히 계세요, 카테리나 이바노브나한테 전할게요.\"\n\n그녀는 마침내 빠져나가게 된 것이 너무도 기뻤다. 그녀는 고개를 떨군 채 떠나갔고, 한시바삐 시야에서 사라져, 오른쪽 모퉁이까지 스무 걸음을 걸어 마침내 혼자가 되려고 서둘렀으며, 그런 다음에는 아무도 쳐다보지 않고, 아무것도 알아차리지 못한 채 빠르게 걸어가면서, 생각에 잠기고, 떠올리고, 한마디 한마디, 사소한 것 하나하나를 곱씹어 보려고 했다. 그녀는 이런 것을 한 번도, 단 한 번도 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 어렴풋이, 무의식중에 온전히 새로운 세계가 그녀 앞에 열리고 있었다. 그녀는 라스콜니코프가 오늘, 어쩌면 당장이라도 자신을 찾아오려 한다는 사실이 문득 떠올랐다!\n\n\"그저 오늘만은, 제발, 오늘만은 안 돼요!\" 그녀는 마치 누군가에게 애원하듯, 겁먹은 어린아이처럼, 가슴이 철렁 내려앉은 채 줄곧 중얼거렸다. \"맙소사! 나한테…… 그 방으로…… 그가 보게 될 텐데…… 오, 어쩌면 좋아!\"\n\n그녀는 그 순간 자신을 지켜보며 바짝 뒤를 밟고 있던 낯선 신사를 알아챌 처지가 못 되었다. 그는 대문간에서부터 그녀를 따라왔던 것이다. 라주미힌과 라스콜니코프, 그리고 그녀가 인도에 멈춰 서서 작별 인사를 나누던 그 순간, 마침 그곳을 지나가던 이 신사는 소냐의 말, \"그래서 라스콜니코프 씨가 어디 사시느냐고 물었어요?\"라는 말을 듣고 흠칫했다. 그는 빠르면서도 주의 깊은 눈길을 세 사람 모두에게, 특히 소냐가 말을 걸고 있던 라스콜니코프에게 던졌다. 그러고는 뒤를 돌아보며 그 집을 눈여겨 두었다. 이 모든 일을 그는 지나가면서 순식간에 해치웠고, 자신의 관심을 들키지 않으려 애쓰며, 마치 무언가를 기다리는 듯 좀 더 천천히 걸어갔다. 그는 소냐를 기다리고 있었던 것이다. 그들이 헤어지고 있으며, 소냐가 집으로 가고 있다는 것을 보았던 것이다.\n\n'집으로? 어디로? 저 얼굴을 어디선가 본 적이 있는데.' 그는 생각했다. '알아내야겠어.'\n\n모퉁이에서 그는 길을 건너 주위를 둘러보았고, 소냐가 아무것도 알아차리지 못한 채 같은 길로 오고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 모퉁이를 돌았다. 그는 반대편에서 그녀를 따라갔다. 쉰 걸음쯤 가서 그는 다시 길을 건너, 그녀를 따라잡고는 두세 걸음 뒤에 바짝 붙어 갔다.\n\n그는 쉰쯤 된 남자로, 키가 꽤 크고 다부졌으며, 넓고 떡 벌어진 어깨 탓에 어쩐지 약간 구부정해 보였다. 그는 좋고 멋스러운 옷을 입었고, 지체 있는 신사처럼 보였다. 그는 멋진 지팡이를 들고 한 걸음마다 인도를 톡톡 두드렸으며, 장갑은 티 하나 없이 깨끗했다. 그는 넓적하고 다소 호감 가는 얼굴에 광대뼈가 높고, 페테르부르크에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 싱그러운 혈색을 띠고 있었다. 그의 아마빛 머리칼은 아직 숱이 많았고, 여기저기 희끗희끗 셀 뿐이었으며, 네모지고 숱 많은 턱수염은 머리칼보다도 더 옅었다. 그의 눈은 파랗고 차갑고 사색적인 빛을 띠었으며, 입술은 진홍색이었다. 그는 유난히 정정하게 잘 보존된 남자로, 나이보다 훨씬 젊어 보였다.\n\n소냐가 운하 둑으로 나섰을 때, 인도에는 그들 둘뿐이었다. 그는 그녀가 몽상에 잠겨 골똘히 생각에 빠져 있는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그녀가 세 들어 사는 집에 다다르자, 소냐는 대문 안으로 꺾어 들어갔다. 그는 다소 놀란 듯한 표정으로 그녀를 따라갔다. 안뜰에서 그녀는 오른쪽 모퉁이로 향했다. \"어럽쇼!\" 낯선 신사가 중얼거리더니, 그녀를 뒤따라 계단을 올랐다. 그제야 소냐는 그를 알아챘다. 그녀는 삼 층에 이르러 통로로 꺾어 들더니, 9호실 초인종을 울렸다. 문에는 분필로 \"재단사 카페르나우모프\"라고 적혀 있었다. \"어럽쇼!\" 낯선 사내가 그 묘한 우연에 놀라며 다시 중얼거렸고, 바로 옆집, 8호실 초인종을 울렸다. 두 문은 두세 걸음 떨어져 있었다.\n\n\"카페르나우모프네 집에 사시는군요.\" 그는 소냐를 바라보며 웃으면서 말했다. \"어제 그 사람이 내 조끼를 손봐 줬지요. 나는 바로 이 근처, 레슬리히 부인 댁에 묵고 있어요. 참 묘하네요!\" 소냐가 그를 유심히 바라보았다.\n\n\"우리는 이웃이군요.\" 그가 유쾌하게 말을 이었다. \"나는 그저께 막 시내에 왔답니다. 그럼 이만 안녕히.\"\n\n소냐는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 문이 열리자 그녀는 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다. 그녀는 어쩐지 부끄럽고 불안한 기분이 들었다.\n\n포르피리한테 가는 길에 라주미힌은 눈에 띄게 들떠 있었다.\n\n\"정말 잘됐어, 이 친구야.\" 그는 몇 번이고 되풀이했다. \"정말 기뻐! 기쁘다고!\"\n\n'뭐가 그렇게 기쁘다는 거지?' 라스콜니코프는 속으로 생각했다.\n\n\"자네도 그 노파한테 물건을 저당 잡혔는지는 몰랐네. 그런데…… 오래전 일이야? 그러니까, 거기 갔던 게 오래됐냐고?\"\n\n'얼마나 순진하고 어리석은 녀석인지!'\n\n\"언제였더라?\" 라스콜니코프는 기억을 더듬으려고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. \"그 노파가 죽기 이삼 일 전이었을 거야. 하지만 이제 그 물건들을 되찾을 생각은 없어.\" 그는 그 물건들에 대해 어쩐지 황급하고 유난스러운 관심을 내비치며 덧붙였다. \"은화 일 루블밖에 안 남았거든…… 어젯밤 그 빌어먹을 헛소리 소동 끝에 말이야!\"\n\n그는 헛소리 소동에 특히 힘주어 강조했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 라주미힌이 — 무엇에 대해서인지는 분명치 않으나 — 황급히 맞장구쳤다. \"그래서 자네가…… 그렇게 매여 있었던 거구나…… 어느 정도는…… 알잖아, 헛소리하면서 자네가 줄곧 무슨 반지니 사슬이니 하는 걸 입에 올렸거든! 그래, 그래…… 그렇구나, 이제 다 알겠어.\"\n\n'어이쿠! 그 생각이 저들 사이에 어지간히도 퍼져 있었던 모양이군. 이 친구는 나를 위해서라면 화형대에라도 오를 사람인데, 내가 헛소리하면서 반지 얘기를 한 까닭이 밝혀졌다고 좋아하고 있다니! 그 생각이 저들 모두를 얼마나 단단히 사로잡고 있는 건지!'\n\n\"그를 만날 수 있을까?\" 그가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그럼.\" 라주미힌이 잽싸게 대답했다. \"그는 좋은 친구야, 만나 보면 알 거야. 좀 서투른 데가 있지, 그러니까, 예의 바른 사람이긴 한데, 다른 의미로 서투르단 말이지. 영리한 친구야, 정말이지 무척 영리하지, 하지만 자기만의 사고방식이 있어……. 의심이 많고, 회의적이고, 냉소적이지……. 사람들을 속여 먹기를, 아니 차라리 골려 주기를 좋아한다고나 할까. 그가 쓰는 건 옛날식의, 정황을 따지는 방식이지……. 하지만 자기 일은 알아, 철저하게……. 작년엔 경찰이 단서조차 거의 잡지 못한 살인 사건을 해결했거든. 그는 자네와 알게 되기를 무척, 무척 바라고 있어!\"\n\n\"무슨 까닭으로 그렇게 바란다는 거야?\"\n\n\"아, 딱히 그런 건 아니고…… 그러니까, 자네가 앓고 있는 동안 내가 어쩌다 자네 얘기를 몇 번 했거든……. 그래서 자네 얘기를 듣고는…… 자네가 법학도인데 학업을 마치지 못하게 됐다는 걸 듣고, '저런, 안됐군!' 하더라고. 그래서 나는 결론을 내린 거지…… 그 모든 걸 종합해서, 그것만이 아니라 말이야. 어제 자메토프가…… 알잖아, 로쟈, 어제 자네 집으로 가는 길에 내가 술에 취해서 헛소리를 좀 했는데…… 이 친구야, 자네가 그걸 부풀려 생각할까 봐 걱정이야, 알겠어?\"\n\n\"뭘? 저들이 나를 미친놈으로 여긴다고? 어쩌면 저들이 옳은지도 모르지.\" 그는 어색한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래……. 그러니까, 에이, 아냐!…… 하지만 내가 말한 그 모든 게 (다른 것도 좀 있었지만) 다 헛소리였어, 술 취해서 한 헛소리였다고.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 사과를 하는 거야? 난 그 모든 게 진절머리가 난다고!\" 라스콜니코프가 과장되게 짜증을 내며 외쳤다. 하지만 그것은 어느 정도 꾸며 낸 것이었다.\n\n\"알아, 알아, 이해해. 정말이지, 이해한다고. 그런 얘기를 입에 올리는 것도 부끄럽지.\"\n\n\"부끄러우면 입에 올리지 마.\"\n\n둘 다 잠잠해졌다. 라주미힌은 그저 황홀해할 따름이었고, 라스콜니코프는 그것을 혐오감과 함께 알아챘다. 그는 또한 라주미힌이 방금 포르피리에 대해 한 말에 불안을 느꼈다.\n\n'그자 앞에서도 시치미를 떼야겠군.' 그는 가슴이 두근거리는 채로 생각했고, 얼굴이 하얘졌다. '그것도 자연스럽게 말이야. 하지만 가장 자연스러운 건 아무것도 하지 않는 거지. 조심스럽게 아무것도 하지 않는 것! 아니, 조심스럽게 굴면 또 자연스럽지 않을 거야……. 에이, 뭐, 어떻게 되는지 두고 보자고……. 두고 봐야지…… 곧. 가는 게 좋을까, 아닐까? 나방은 불빛으로 날아드는 법이지. 가슴이 쿵쾅거려, 그게 문제야!'\n\n\"이 회색 건물이야.\" 라주미힌이 말했다.\n\n'무엇보다 중요한 건, 어제 내가 그 늙은이의 집에 갔던 것을…… 그리고 핏자국에 대해 물었던 것을 포르피리가 알고 있느냐는 거야. 그건 당장, 들어가자마자 알아내야 해, 그의 얼굴에서 알아내야지. 그러지 않으면…… 그게 내 파멸이 될지라도 기어이 알아내고 말겠어.'\n\n\"이봐, 친구.\" 그는 교활한 미소를 띠며 갑자기 라주미힌에게 말을 건넸다. \"하루 종일 보니까 자네가 어쩐지 묘하게 들떠 있는 것 같던데. 안 그래?\"\n\n\"들떠 있다고? 천만에.\" 라주미힌이 정곡을 찔린 듯 발끈하며 말했다.\n\n\"아냐, 친구, 분명히 말하는데 티가 나. 글쎄, 자네가 평소엔 절대 안 그러던데, 의자에 어쩐지 가장자리에 걸터앉아서, 내내 몸을 비비 꼬더라고. 까닭도 없이 자꾸 벌떡벌떡 일어나고. 어느 순간엔 화가 나 있다가, 다음 순간엔 얼굴이 사탕처럼 달콤해지고. 얼굴까지 붉히던걸. 특히 저녁 식사에 초대받았을 땐, 어찌나 새빨개지던지.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아, 말도 안 돼! 무슨 소릴 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 학생처럼 발뺌을 하는 거지? 이런, 또 얼굴이 빨개지네.\"\n\n\"이런 망할 자식 같으니!\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 그렇게 부끄러워하는 거야? 로미오 같으니! 가만있어 봐, 오늘 다 일러바칠 테니까. 하하하! 어머니를 웃겨 드려야지, 또 다른 누군가도 말이야…….\"\n\n\"잘 들어, 들어, 들어, 이건 진지한 일이야……. 이 악마 같은 자식, 그담엔 또 뭘 어쩔 셈이야!\" 라주미힌은 완전히 압도되어 공포에 질려 새파랗게 질렸다. \"뭘 일러바치겠다는 거야? 이봐, 친구…… 아이고! 이런 망할 자식 같으니!\"\n\n\"자넨 여름 장미 같다니까. 그게 자네한테 얼마나 잘 어울리는지 알기나 해? 키가 육 척이 넘는 로미오라니! 게다가 오늘 어찌나 말끔히 씻었는지 — 손톱까지 다듬었더군, 정말이지. 응? 그건 듣도 보도 못한 일이야! 어럽쇼, 머리에 포마드까지 발랐구먼! 좀 숙여 봐!\"\n\n\"이 돼지 같은 자식!\"\n라스콜니코프는 도저히 못 참겠다는 듯 웃어 댔다. 그렇게 웃으며 그들은 포르피리 페트로비치의 집으로 들어섰다. 이것이야말로 라스콜니코프가 바라던 바였다. 안에서는 그들이 들어오면서 웃는 소리가 들렸고, 통로에서도 여전히 껄껄대고 있었다.\n\n\"여기선 한마디도 하지 마, 안 그러면 내가…… 네 머리통을 박살 낼 거야!\" 라주미힌이 라스콜니코프의 어깨를 움켜쥐며 격하게 속삭였다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n라스콜니코프는 이미 방으로 들어서고 있었다. 그는 또다시 폭소가 터지는 것을 가까스로 참고 있는 듯한 얼굴로 들어왔다. 그 뒤를 따라 라주미힌이 껑충하고 어색하게, 부끄러움으로 작약처럼 시뻘게진 얼굴에 완전히 풀이 죽으면서도 사납기 짝이 없는 표정을 한 채 성큼성큼 들어왔다. 그 순간 그의 얼굴과 온몸은 정말이지 우스꽝스러웠고, 라스콜니코프의 웃음을 충분히 정당화해 주었다. 라스콜니코프는 누가 소개해 주기를 기다리지도 않고, 방 한가운데 서서 캐묻듯 그들을 바라보고 있던 포르피리 페트로비치에게 인사를 했다. 그는 손을 내밀어 악수를 했는데, 여전히 자신의 즐거움을 억누르고 자기소개를 위해 몇 마디라도 내뱉으려 필사적으로 애쓰는 듯했다. 그러나 가까스로 진지한 태도를 꾸며 내고 무언가를 중얼거리는 데 성공하자마자, 그는 갑자기 우연인 듯 다시 라주미힌을 흘끗 보았고, 더는 자신을 통제할 수 없었다. 억눌렀던 웃음이 참으려 애쓰면 애쓸수록 더욱 걷잡을 수 없이 터져 나왔다. 라주미힌이 이 '저절로 터진' 즐거움을 받아들이는 그 유별난 사나움이 이 장면 전체에 더없이 진실하고 자연스러운 즐거움의 분위기를 부여했다. 라주미힌은 마치 일부러 그러는 듯 이 인상을 더욱 강화시켰다.\n\n\"이 바보 같으니! 이 악마야.\" 그가 으르렁거리며 팔을 휘둘렀는데, 그 팔이 곧장 빈 찻잔이 놓인 작고 둥근 탁자를 후려쳤다. 모든 것이 와장창 날아가 떨어졌다.\n\n\"하지만 의자는 왜 부수는 거요, 신사 양반? 그건 국고의 손실인 줄 아실 텐데.\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 유쾌하게 인용했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 여전히 포르피리 페트로비치의 손을 잡은 채 웃고 있었지만, 지나치지 않으려 신경을 곤두세우며 자연스럽게 마무리할 적절한 순간을 기다렸다. 탁자를 뒤엎고 잔을 깨뜨려 완전히 당황한 라주미힌은 침울하게 깨진 조각들을 바라보다가 욕을 내뱉고는, 홱 창가로 돌아서더니 사람들에게 등을 돌리고 사납게 눈살을 찌푸린 얼굴로, 아무것도 보지 못한 채 밖을 내다보며 서 있었다. 포르피리 페트로비치는 웃었고 계속 웃을 태세였지만, 분명 설명을 바라는 눈치였다. 자메토프는 구석에 앉아 있다가 방문객들이 들어오자 일어나, 입가에 미소를 띤 채 기다리며 서 있었는데, 다만 이 장면 전체와 라스콜니코프를 놀라움, 아니 심지어 믿지 못하겠다는 듯 어떤 당혹감을 띠고 바라보았다. 자메토프의 뜻밖의 출현이 라스콜니코프에게 불쾌한 충격을 주었다.\n\n'이건 생각해 둬야겠어.' 그는 생각했다. \"실례합니다, 부디.\" 그가 극도의 당혹감을 가장하며 말을 꺼냈다. \"라스콜니코프입니다.\"\n\n\"별말씀을, 만나 뵙게 되어 무척 반갑습니다…… 그리고 어찌나 유쾌하게 들어오셨던지……. 아니, 이 친구는 아침 인사도 안 할 셈인가?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 라주미힌을 향해 고갯짓했다.\n\n\"맹세코 이 친구가 왜 나한테 저렇게 화가 났는지 모르겠습니다. 오는 길에 그냥 로미오 같다고 말했을 뿐인데…… 그러고는 그걸 증명해 보였지요. 그게 다였던 것 같은데요!\"\n\n\"돼지 같은 놈!\" 라주미힌이 돌아보지도 않고 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그 말 한마디에 저렇게 펄펄 뛰는 걸 보면, 분명 아주 심각한 근거가 있었던 모양이군.\" 포르피리가 웃었다.\n\n\"오, 이 약삭빠른 법률가 양반!…… 다들 빌어먹을!\" 라주미힌이 쏘아붙이더니, 갑자기 자기도 웃음을 터뜨리며 아무 일도 없었던 듯 한결 밝아진 얼굴로 포르피리에게 다가갔다. \"이제 그만! 우린 다 바보들이지. 본론으로 들어가자고. 이 친구는 내 친구 로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프인데, 첫째로 자네 얘기를 듣고 알고 지내고 싶어 했고, 둘째로 자네한테 처리할 자그마한 용무가 있다네. 어라! 자메토프, 자네가 여긴 어쩐 일이야? 전에 만난 적이 있나? 안 지 오래됐어?\"\n\n'이게 무슨 뜻이지?' 라스콜니코프는 불안하게 생각했다.\n\n자메토프는 좀 당황한 듯했으나, 그리 심하지는 않았다.\n\n\"아니, 어제 자네 방에서 만났잖아.\" 그가 태연하게 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 수고를 덜었군. 지난주 내내 자네한테 소개해 달라고 졸라 대더니. 포르피리하고 자넨 나 없이도 서로 냄새를 맡았구먼. 담배 어디 있어?\"\n\n포르피리 페트로비치는 실내복 차림에 아주 깨끗한 속옷을 받쳐 입고, 뒤축이 닳은 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다. 그는 서른다섯쯤 된 사내로, 키가 작고 비만이라 할 만큼 뚱뚱했으며, 수염은 말끔히 깎여 있었다. 머리는 짧게 깎았고, 둥근 큰 머리통은 특히 뒤통수가 두드러졌다. 부드럽고 둥글며 다소 들창코인 그의 얼굴은 병약한 누런빛을 띠었지만, 활기차고 다소 빈정대는 표정을 하고 있었다. 거의 흰빛에 가깝게 깜박이는 속눈썹 아래 물기 어린 감상적인 빛으로 반짝이는 그 눈빛만 아니었다면, 호인의 얼굴이라 할 만했다. 그 눈의 표정은 다소 여자 같은 그의 몸매와 묘하게 어울리지 않았고, 첫눈에 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 진지한 무언가를 그에게 부여하고 있었다.\n\n포르피리 페트로비치는 방문객에게 자그마한 용무가 있다는 말을 듣자마자 그에게 소파에 앉기를 청하고는 자신은 그 반대편 끝에 앉아, 용무를 설명하기를 기다리는데, 그 신중하고 지나치게 진지한 주의력이란, 특히 낯선 사람에게는, 더욱이 논의하려는 일이 그토록 유별난 엄숙함을 받기에는 너무 사소하다고 여겨지는 경우에는, 그 자체로 짓누르는 듯하면서도 당혹스러운 것이었다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 짧고 조리 있는 말로 자신의 용무를 명료하고 정확하게 설명했고, 어찌나 만족스러웠는지 심지어 포르피리를 잘 살펴보는 데까지 성공했다. 포르피리 페트로비치는 단 한 순간도 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 같은 탁자 맞은편에 앉은 라주미힌은 열렬하면서도 조바심을 내며 귀를 기울였고, 매 순간 다소 과도한 관심을 띠고 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라보았다.\n\n'바보 같으니.' 라스콜니코프는 속으로 욕했다.\n\n\"경찰에 신고하셔야 합니다.\" 포르피리가 더없이 사무적인 태도로 대답했다. \"이 사건, 그러니까 살인 사건을 알게 되어, 이러이러한 물건들이 자기 것이니 그것들을 되찾고 싶다고 담당 수사관에게 알리고자 한다고 말이지요…… 또는…… 어쨌든 그쪽에서 통지를 보내올 겁니다.\"\n\n\"문제는 바로 그겁니다, 지금 당장은.\" 라스콜니코프는 있는 힘껏 당혹감을 꾸며 내려 애썼다. \"제가 형편이 그리 넉넉지 않아서…… 이런 하찮은 액수조차 제게는 벅찹니다……. 그저, 보시다시피, 지금으로서는 그 물건들이 제 것이라는 것만 신고해 두고, 돈이 생기면 그때…….\"\n\n\"그건 문제 될 것 없습니다.\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 그의 금전 사정에 관한 설명을 냉담하게 받아넘기며 대답했다. \"하지만 원하신다면, 곧장 저한테 글로 써 주셔도 됩니다. 이 사건을 알게 되어 이러이러한 것이 자기 소유임을 주장하며, 청컨대…… 라고 말이지요.\"\n\n\"보통 종이에다요?\" 라스콜니코프가 다시 이 문제의 재정적인 측면에 관심을 보이며 다급하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"오, 아주 평범한 종이면 됩니다.\" 그러더니 갑자기 포르피리 페트로비치가 노골적으로 빈정대는 눈빛으로 그를 바라보며, 눈을 가늘게 뜨고는 마치 그에게 윙크를 하는 듯했다. 하지만 어쩌면 라스콜니코프의 착각이었는지도 모르니, 모든 것이 한순간에 지나갔던 것이다. 분명 그 비슷한 무언가가 있긴 했고, 라스콜니코프는 그가 자기에게 윙크를 했다고 맹세라도 할 수 있었지만, 도대체 왜 그랬는지는 알 수 없었다.\n\n'저자가 알고 있군.' 그의 머릿속을 번개처럼 스쳤다.\n\n\"이런 하찮은 일로 번거롭게 해 드려 죄송합니다.\" 그가 조금 당황한 채 말을 이었다. \"그 물건들은 고작 오 루블어치밖에 안 되지만, 제게 그것들을 물려준 분들을 생각해서 각별히 아끼는 거라서요, 솔직히 말하면 소식을 듣고 깜짝 놀랐지요…….\"\n\n\"그래서 자네가 그렇게 충격을 받았던 거로군, 내가 조시모프한테 포르피리가 저당물 잡힌 사람들을 죄다 조사하고 있다고 말했을 때 말이야!\" 라주미힌이 노골적인 의도를 품고 끼어들었다.\n\n이건 정말 참을 수 없는 노릇이었다. 라스콜니코프는 자기도 모르게 그를 흘끗 보았고 검은 눈에 복수심 어린 분노가 번뜩였으나, 곧바로 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"자네 날 비웃는 모양이지, 친구?\" 그가 제법 그럴듯하게 짜증을 가장하며 그에게 말했다. \"이런 하찮은 것에 우습도록 안달하는 것처럼 자네 눈엔 보이겠지. 하지만 그렇다고 날 이기적이라거나 욕심 사납다고 여기진 말게, 내 눈에는 이 두 물건이 결코 하찮은 게 아닐 수도 있으니까. 방금도 말했지만, 그 은시계는 한 푼어치 값도 안 나가지만 아버지가 우리한테 남긴 유일한 유품이야. 날 비웃어도 좋지만, 어머니가 여기 와 계신데,\" 그는 갑자기 포르피리 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"만일 어머니가 아신다면,\" 그는 다시 황급히 라주미힌 쪽으로 돌아서며, 애써 목소리를 떨리게 했다. \"그 시계를 잃어버렸다는 걸 아신다면, 절망하실 거라고! 여자들이 어떤지 자네도 알잖아!\"\n\n\"천만에! 난 절대 그런 뜻이 아니었어! 정반대라고!\" 라주미힌이 괴로워하며 소리쳤다.\n\n'제대로 한 건가? 자연스러웠나? 너무 과했나?' 라스콜니코프는 떨리는 마음으로 스스로에게 물었다. '왜 여자들 얘기 같은 걸 했지?'\n\n\"오, 어머님이 같이 와 계신가요?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 물었다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"언제 오셨습니까?\"\n\n\"어젯밤에요.\"\n\n포르피리는 무언가 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 잠시 멈추었다.\n\n\"어쨌든 당신 물건들은 잃어버리지 않을 겁니다.\" 그가 차분하고 냉담하게 말을 이었다. \"한동안 당신이 여기 오기를 기다리고 있었거든요.\"\n\n그러고는 그것이 별일 아니라는 듯, 그는 조심스럽게 라주미힌에게 재떨이를 내밀었는데, 라주미힌은 사정없이 양탄자 위에 담뱃재를 흩뿌리고 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 흠칫했지만, 포르피리는 그를 보고 있지 않은 듯했고, 여전히 라주미힌의 담배에만 신경을 쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 이 친구를 기다렸다고? 아니, 이 친구가 거기에 저당물을 잡혔다는 걸 알고 있었단 말이야?\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다.\n\n포르피리 페트로비치가 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"당신 물건들, 반지하고 시계가 함께 싸여 있었는데, 그 종이에는 당신 이름이 연필로 또박또박 적혀 있었고, 그것들을 그 여자한테 맡긴 날짜도 함께 적혀 있더군요…….\"\n\n\"정말 관찰력이 대단하시군요!\" 라스콜니코프는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보려 안간힘을 쓰며 어색하게 미소 지었으나, 실패하고는 갑자기 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 건, 저당물이 아주 많았을 테니까…… 그걸 다 기억하기는 어려울 거란 생각이 들어서요……. 그런데 그걸 다 그렇게 또렷이 기억하시고, 또…… 또…….\"\n\n'멍청하긴! 허약해!' 그는 생각했다. '왜 저런 말을 덧붙인 거지?'\n\n\"하지만 우리는 저당물을 잡힌 사람들을 다 알고 있고, 나서지 않은 사람은 당신 하나뿐입니다.\" 포르피리가 거의 알아챌 수 없는 빈정거림을 담아 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 몸이 좀 안 좋았어요.\"\n\n\"그것도 들었습니다. 사실, 무언가로 몹시 괴로워하고 있다는 얘기까지 들었지요. 아직도 안색이 창백하시군요.\"\n\n\"전혀 창백하지 않은데요……. 아니, 아주 멀쩡합니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 어조를 완전히 바꾸어 무례하고 성난 목소리로 쏘아붙였다. 분노가 치밀어 올랐고, 그는 그것을 억누를 수 없었다. '이러다 화를 못 참고 정체를 드러내겠어.' 또다시 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. '저들은 왜 날 고문하는 거지?'\n\n\"몸이 좀 안 좋다니!\" 라주미힌이 그의 말을 받았다. \"그게 무슨 소리야! 이 친구는 어제 종일 정신을 잃고 헛소리를 해 댔다고. 믿어지나, 포르피리, 우리가 등을 돌리기가 무섭게, 제대로 서지도 못하면서 옷을 챙겨 입고 우릴 따돌리고는 어디론가 빠져나가 자정까지 진탕 돌아다녔다고, 내내 정신이 오락가락하면서 말이야! 믿어져? 기가 막힐 노릇이지!\"\n\n\"정말로 정신이 오락가락했다고? 설마!\" 포르피리가 여자처럼 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"헛소리! 그런 거 믿지 마쇼! 어차피 당신은 안 믿겠지만.\" 라스콜니코프가 분노에 차서 그만 입 밖에 내고 말았다. 그러나 포르피리 페트로비치는 그 묘한 말을 알아채지 못한 듯했다.\n\n\"하지만 정신이 멀쩡했다면 어떻게 나갈 수 있었겠어?\" 라주미힌이 갑자기 발끈했다. \"뭐 하러 나간 거야? 무슨 목적으로? 그것도 왜 몰래? 그럴 때 자네 제정신이긴 했어? 이제 위험이 다 지나갔으니 솔직히 말할 수 있는데.\"\n\n\"어제 그자들한테 진절머리가 나서 말이지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 갑자기 뻔뻔스러운 도전의 미소를 띠며 포르피리에게 말을 건넸다. \"그자들을 피해 도망쳐서 날 못 찾을 곳에 셋방을 얻으려고, 돈도 잔뜩 챙겨서 나갔지요. 저기 자메토프 씨가 봤어요. 어이, 자메토프 씨, 내가 어제 제정신이었소, 아니면 헛소리를 했소? 우리 논쟁을 좀 정리해 주시오.\"\n\n그 순간 그는 자메토프를 목 졸라 죽이고 싶었으니, 그의 표정과 침묵이 그토록 가증스러웠던 것이다.\n\n\"제 생각에는 당신이 조리 있게, 심지어 교묘하게 말했지만, 극도로 예민한 상태였습니다.\" 자메토프가 무뚝뚝하게 단언했다.\n\n\"그리고 니코딤 포미치가 오늘 제게 말하기를,\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 끼어들었다. \"어젯밤 아주 늦은 시각에 마차에 치인 사람의 셋방에서 당신을 만났다더군요.\"\n\n\"그리고 거기서,\" 라주미힌이 말했다. \"자넨 그때도 미친 게 아니었나? 장례 비용에 쓰라고 과부한테 마지막 한 푼까지 다 줘 버렸잖아. 돕고 싶었으면 십오 루블이나 이십 루블이라도 주되, 적어도 자기 몫으로 삼 루블쯤은 남겨 둬야지, 그걸 이십오 루블을 통째로 단번에 내던지다니!\"\n\n\"어쩌면 어디서 보물이라도 찾았는데 자네들이 모르는 것일 수도 있잖아? 그래서 어제 내가 그렇게 통이 컸던 거지……. 자메토프 씨는 내가 보물을 찾았다는 걸 알아! 이런 시시한 일로 삼십 분이나 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다.\" 그가 입술을 떨며 포르피리 페트로비치를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"지루하게 해 드리고 있지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"오 천만에요, 정반대입니다, 정반대예요! 당신이 제게 얼마나 흥미로운지 아신다면! 이렇게 보고 듣는 게 흥미롭습니다……. 그리고 마침내 이렇게 나서 주셔서 정말 기쁘고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 차라도 좀 내주지 그래! 목이 타는군.\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다.\n\n\"좋은 생각이오! 어쩌면 우리 모두 함께하게 될지도 모르겠군요. 차 마시기 전에…… 좀 더 든든한 걸 드시지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러지 좀 말라고!\"\n\n포르피리 페트로비치는 차를 시키러 나갔다.\n\n라스콜니코프의 머릿속은 어지럽게 소용돌이쳤다. 그는 끔찍한 격분 상태에 빠져 있었다.\n\n'무엇보다 최악인 건, 저자들이 그걸 숨기지도 않는다는 거야. 격식 차릴 생각조차 없어! 그리고 만일 나를 전혀 모른다면, 어떻게 니코딤 포미치한테 나에 관해 얘기하게 됐단 말인가? 그러니 한 무리의 개떼처럼 나를 추적하고 있다는 걸 숨길 생각도 없는 거지. 그냥 내 얼굴에 침을 뱉는 거야.' 그는 분노로 부들부들 떨었다. '자, 차라리 대놓고 날 쳐라, 고양이가 쥐를 가지고 놀듯 날 가지고 놀지 말고. 이건 도무지 예의가 아니오, 포르피리 페트로비치, 하지만 어쩌면 내가 가만두지 않겠어! 벌떡 일어나 너희 그 추한 낯짝에 진실을 통째로 내던지면, 너희는 내가 너희를 얼마나 경멸하는지 알게 되겠지.' 그는 숨조차 제대로 쉬기 어려웠다. '그런데 이게 그저 내 착각이라면? 내가 잘못 짚은 거고, 경험이 없어서 화를 내며 내 못된 역할을 지켜 내지 못하는 거라면? 어쩌면 다 의도한 게 아닐지도 몰라. 저들의 말은 죄다 흔해 빠진 것들이지만, 거기엔 무언가가 있어……. 그 무엇이든 할 수 있는 말이긴 한데, 그래도 무언가가 있단 말이야. 왜 저자는 노골적으로 \"그 여자한테\"라고 했지? 왜 자메토프는 내가 교묘하게 말했다고 덧붙인 거고? 왜 저런 어조로 말하는 거지? 그래, 그 어조 말이야……. 라주미힌이 여기 앉아 있는데, 왜 아무것도 못 보는 거지? 저 순진한 멍청이는 도무지 아무것도 못 보지! 또 열이 오르는군! 방금 포르피리가 나한테 윙크를 했나? 물론 헛소리야! 윙크할 이유가 뭐가 있겠어? 저들이 내 신경을 흔들려는 건가, 아니면 날 놀리는 건가? 이건 병적인 착각이거나, 아니면 저들이 알고 있거나 둘 중 하나야! 자메토프조차 무례하잖아……. 자메토프가 무례한가? 자메토프는 마음을 바꿨어. 저자가 마음을 바꾸리라는 걸 난 예감했지! 저자는 여기서 제 집처럼 굴고, 나는 처음 온 거고. 포르피리는 저자를 방문객으로 여기지 않아, 등을 돌리고 앉아 있잖아. 의심할 여지 없이 저들은 나를 두고 한통속이 된 거야! 우리가 오기 전에 나에 관해 얘기하고 있었던 게 틀림없어. 셋방 일을 알고 있나? 좀 서둘러 주면 좋으련만! 내가 셋방을 얻으러 도망쳤다고 했을 때 저자는 그냥 넘겼어……. 셋방 얘기는 영리하게 끼워 넣었지, 나중에 쓸모가 있을지도 몰라……. 정신이 오락가락했다니, 참……. 하하하! 저자는 어젯밤 일을 죄다 알고 있어! 어머니가 오신 건 몰랐고! 그 노파가 날짜를 연필로 적어 뒀던 거지! 틀렸어, 너희는 날 잡지 못해! 증거가 없잖아…… 죄다 추측일 뿐이야! 증거를 내놔 봐! 셋방조차 증거가 아니라 헛소리지. 저들한테 뭐라고 말해야 할지 난 알아……. 셋방 일을 알고 있을까? 알아내지 못하면 가지 않겠어. 그런데 난 뭐 하러 온 거지? 하지만 내가 지금 화내는 것, 어쩌면 그게 증거일지도 몰라! 바보 같으니, 어찌 이리 신경질적인지! 어쩌면 이게 맞는 건지도 모르지. 환자 행세를 하는 것……. 저자는 나를 떠보고 있어. 날 잡으려 들겠지. 난 뭐 하러 온 거지?'\n\n이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 번개처럼 스쳤다.\n\n포르피리 페트로비치가 금세 돌아왔다. 그는 갑자기 한결 쾌활해졌다.\n\n\"어제 자네 모임 탓에 머리가 좀…… 그리고 어쩐지 영 컨디션이 말이 아니야.\" 그가 라주미힌에게 웃어 보이며 사뭇 다른 어조로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"재미있었나? 어제 가장 흥미로운 대목에서 자넬 두고 나왔는데. 누가 이겼어?\"\n\n\"오, 물론 아무도 못 이겼지. 영원한 문제들로 빠져들더니, 허공으로 둥둥 떠가 버렸어.\"\n\n\"한번 상상해 봐, 로쟈, 우리가 어제 무슨 얘기까지 갔는지. 과연 범죄라는 게 존재하느냐 마느냐 하는 거였어. 정신없이 떠들어 댔다고 했잖아.\"\n\n\"이상할 게 뭐 있어? 흔해 빠진 사회 문제잖아.\" 라스콜니코프가 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n\"문제가 꼭 그런 식으로 제기된 건 아니었습니다.\" 포르피리가 지적했다.\n\n\"꼭 그런 건 아니었지, 그건 맞아.\" 라주미힌이 평소처럼 열을 올리고 다급해하며 곧바로 동의했다. \"들어 봐, 로디온, 자네 의견을 말해 봐, 듣고 싶어. 난 저들과 죽기 살기로 싸웠고 자네가 좀 거들어 주길 바랐어. 자네가 올 거라고 말해 뒀지……. 사회주의 학설에서 시작된 거야. 자네도 그들의 학설을 알잖아. 범죄란 사회 조직의 비정상성에 대한 항거일 뿐, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아니고, 다른 어떤 원인도 인정하지 않는다는 거지!……\"\n\n\"거기서 자네가 틀렸어.\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 외쳤다. 그는 눈에 띄게 활기를 띠며 라주미힌을 바라보면서 연신 웃어 댔고, 그 바람에 라주미힌은 그 어느 때보다 흥분했다.\n\n\"아무것도 인정하지 않는다고.\" 라주미힌이 발끈하며 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"난 틀리지 않았어. 그들의 소책자를 보여 주지. 그들에겐 모든 게 '환경의 영향'이고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 없어. 그들이 즐겨 쓰는 표현이지! 그러니 사회가 정상적으로 조직되기만 하면 모든 범죄가 즉각 사라진다는 거야. 항거할 대상이 없어지고 모든 인간이 한순간에 의롭게 될 테니까. 인간 본성은 고려되지도 않아, 배제되어 버려, 아예 존재하지도 않는 것처럼 여겨진다고! 인류가 역사적이고 생동하는 과정을 통해 발전하여 마침내 정상적인 사회가 되리라는 건 인정하지 않으면서, 어떤 수학적 두뇌에서 튀어나온 사회 체계가 한순간에 온 인류를 조직하여, 그 어떤 생동하는 과정보다도 빠르게, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 인류를 의롭고 죄 없게 만들어 주리라고 믿는단 말이야! 그래서 그들은 본능적으로 역사를 싫어하지, '거기엔 추악함과 어리석음밖에 없다'면서 모든 걸 어리석음으로 설명해 버려! 그래서 그들은 삶의 그 생동하는 과정을 그토록 싫어하는 거야. 살아 있는 영혼을 원치 않는 거지! 살아 있는 영혼은 삶을 요구하고, 영혼은 기계의 법칙에 복종하려 들지 않고, 영혼은 의심의 대상이며, 영혼은 반동이거든! 하지만 그들이 원하는 건, 비록 죽음의 냄새가 나고 고무로 만들 수 있는 것일망정, 적어도 살아 있지 않고, 의지도 없고, 비굴하며, 반항하지 않는 것이지! 그러다 결국 모든 걸 팔랑스테르의 벽을 쌓고 방과 복도를 설계하는 일로 환원시켜 버리는 거야! 팔랑스테르야 정말 다 준비됐겠지만, 자네의 그 인간 본성은 팔랑스테르를 받아들일 준비가 안 됐단 말이야. 본성은 삶을 원하고, 아직 제 생동하는 과정을 완성하지 못했고, 무덤으로 가기엔 너무 일러! 논리로 본성을 건너뛸 수는 없어. 논리는 세 가지 가능성을 전제하지만, 실제로는 수백만 가지가 있다고! 그 수백만 가지를 잘라 내고, 모든 걸 안락의 문제로 환원시켜 버리는 거지! 그게 문제의 가장 손쉬운 해결책이니까! 유혹적일 만큼 명쾌해서, 굳이 생각할 필요도 없어. 바로 그게 핵심이지, 생각해선 안 된다는 거! 삶의 모든 비밀이 인쇄된 두 페이지에 다 들어 있다니까!\"\n\n\"이제 또 시작이군, 북을 두드려 대네! 저자 좀 붙잡아 주게, 어서!\" 포르피리가 웃었다. \"한번 상상해 보세요.\" 그가 라스콜니코프 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"어젯밤 여섯 사람이 한 방에서, 우선 펀치 술부터 걸치고는 저런 식으로 떠들어 댔다니까요! 아니, 친구, 자네가 틀렸어, 환경은 범죄에서 큰 몫을 차지해, 그건 내가 장담하지.\"\n\n\"오, 그거야 나도 알지, 하지만 한 가지만 말해 봐. 마흔 살 먹은 사내가 열 살짜리 아이를 욕보였다면, 그건 환경이 그자를 그렇게 몰아간 건가?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 엄밀히 말하면, 그랬다고 봐야지.\" 포르피리가 주목할 만큼 진지하게 말했다. \"그런 종류의 범죄도 능히 환경의 영향으로 돌릴 수 있어.\"\n\n라주미힌은 거의 미칠 지경이었다. \"오, 그렇게 나온다면,\" 그가 으르렁거렸다. \"자네 그 흰 속눈썹도 이반 대제의 종탑 높이가 이백오십 피트인 탓으로 능히 돌릴 수 있다는 걸 증명해 보이지, 그것도 명료하게, 정확하게, 단계적으로, 심지어 진보적 경향까지 곁들여서 말이야! 내가 해 보이겠어! 내기할 텐가?\"\n\n\"좋아! 자, 어디 저 친구가 어떻게 증명하는지 들어 봅시다!\"\n\n\"저 작자는 늘 사람을 놀려 먹는다니까, 빌어먹을.\" 라주미힌이 벌떡 일어나 손짓 발짓을 해 대며 외쳤다. \"자네하고 얘기해 봐야 무슨 소용이야? 죄다 일부러 그러는 거라고. 자넨 저자를 몰라, 로디온! 어제 저자는 순전히 그들을 바보로 만들려고 그들 편을 들었어. 어제 했던 소리들 좀 보라지! 그런데도 그들은 좋아 어쩔 줄 모르더라니까! 저자는 그 짓을 보름이고 내리 계속할 수 있어. 작년엔 자기가 수도원에 들어갈 거라고 우리를 믿게 만들더니, 두 달이나 그 소리를 고집했지. 얼마 전엔 또 결혼을 하겠다며, 혼례 준비를 죄다 끝냈다고 떠들어 댔다고. 정말로 새 옷까지 맞췄지. 우리 모두 축하해 주기 시작했어. 그런데 신부도 없고, 아무것도 없고, 죄다 순전히 환상이었던 거야!\"\n\n\"아, 그건 자네가 틀렸어! 옷은 진작에 마련했었다고. 사실은 그 새 옷이 자네들을 골탕 먹일 생각을 하게 만든 거였지.\"\n\n\"당신은 그렇게 능숙한 거짓말쟁이입니까?\" 라스콜니코프가 무심하게 물었다.\n\n\"설마 그러리라고는 생각 못 하셨겠죠, 응? 조금만 기다려 보세요, 당신도 골탕 먹여 드릴 테니. 하하하! 아니, 사실대로 말씀드리지요. 범죄니 환경이니 아이들이니 하는 이 모든 문제를 듣자니, 당시 제 흥미를 끌었던 당신의 어떤 논문이 떠오르는군요. '범죄에 관하여'…… 뭐 그런 제목이었는데, 제목은 잊어버렸습니다, 두 달 전에 《주기 평론》에서 아주 즐겁게 읽었지요.\"\n\n\"제 논문이요? 《주기 평론》에서요?\" 라스콜니코프가 놀라서 물었다. \"확실히 반년 전 대학을 그만둘 무렵에 어떤 책에 관한 논문을 쓰긴 했지만, 그건 《주간 평론》에 보냈는데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 《주기 평론》에 실렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 《주간 평론》이 폐간되는 바람에, 그래서 그땐 인쇄되지 못한 거고요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다. 하지만 《주간 평론》이 폐간될 때 《주기 평론》과 합병되었고, 그래서 당신 논문이 두 달 전에 후자에 실린 겁니다. 모르셨나요?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 모르고 있었다.\n\n\"아니, 그 논문으로 그쪽에서 돈을 좀 받아 낼 수 있을 텐데! 당신도 참 별난 분이군요! 어찌나 고립된 생활을 하시는지 자기와 직접 관련된 일조차 까맣게 모르시다니. 정말입니다, 장담하지요.\"\n\n\"브라보, 로쟈! 나도 그 얘긴 전혀 몰랐다고!\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다. \"오늘 당장 열람실로 달려가서 그 호를 찾아봐야겠어. 두 달 전이라고? 날짜가 언제였지? 뭐, 상관없어, 내가 찾아낼 테니. 우리한텐 말도 안 하고 말이야!\"\n\n\"제 논문인 줄 어떻게 아셨습니까? 머리글자로만 서명되어 있는데요.\"\n\n\"그저 우연히 알게 됐지요, 며칠 전에. 편집자를 통해서요, 그자를 알거든요……. 아주 흥미로웠습니다.\"\n\n\"제 기억으로는, 범죄를 저지르기 전과 후의 범죄자의 심리를 분석한 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 그리고 당신은 범죄의 실행에는 언제나 병이 동반된다고 주장했지요. 아주, 아주 독창적이었습니다, 하지만…… 제 흥미를 그토록 끈 건 논문의 그 부분이 아니라, 끝부분에 나온 어떤 발상이었는데, 유감스럽게도 당신은 그걸 명확히 전개하지 않고 그저 암시만 해 두셨더군요. 기억하실지 모르겠지만, 어떤 부류의 사람들은…… 그러니까, 정확히 말해 그럴 능력이 있다는 게 아니라, 도덕을 위반하고 범죄를 저지를 완전한 권리를 가지고 있으며, 법이 그들에게는 해당되지 않는다는 암시 말입니다.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 자기 발상을 과장하고 의도적으로 왜곡한 데 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 그게 무슨 소리야? 범죄를 저지를 권리라고? 그것도 환경의 영향 때문이 아니라?\" 라주미힌이 심지어 다소 놀라기까지 하며 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 정확히 그것 때문은 아니지.\" 포르피리가 대답했다. \"그의 논문에서는 모든 인간이 '평범한' 자와 '비범한' 자로 나뉘어. 평범한 자들은 복종하며 살아야 하고 법을 위반할 권리가 없는데, 알다시피, 그들이 평범하기 때문이지. 하지만 비범한 자들은 그 어떤 범죄든 저지르고 그 어떤 식으로든 법을 위반할 권리가 있어, 바로 그들이 비범하기 때문에 말이야. 그게 당신 발상이었지요, 제가 틀리지 않았다면?\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리야? 그건 옳을 리가 없잖아?\" 라주미힌이 어리둥절해하며 중얼거렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 다시 미소를 지었다. 그는 단번에 핵심을 알아챘고, 그들이 자기를 어디로 몰아가려는지 간파했다. 그는 그 도전을 받아들이기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"제가 주장한 바는 꼭 그런 건 아니었습니다.\" 그가 간단하고 겸손하게 입을 열었다. \"하지만 거의 정확하게 진술하셨다는 건 인정하지요. 원하신다면, 어쩌면 완벽하게 정확하다고도 하겠습니다.\" (이를 인정하는 것이 그에게는 거의 쾌감을 주었다.) \"다만 차이가 있다면, 저는 당신이 말씀하시는 그 도덕의 위반을 비범한 자들이 언제나 반드시 저질러야 한다고 주장하지는 않는다는 점입니다. 사실 그런 논지가 발표될 수 있을지도 의문이고요. 저는 그저 '비범한' 인간에게는 권리가 있다고…… 그러니까 공적인 권리가 아니라, 어떤 장애물들을 뛰어넘을지 자기 양심에 따라 스스로 결정할 내적인 권리가 있다고 암시했을 뿐입니다. 그것도 오로지 자기 사상의 실천적 실현을 위해 (때로는 어쩌면 온 인류에게 이로울 사상을 위해) 그것이 필수적인 경우에 한해서 말이지요. 제 논문이 명확하지 않다고 하셨는데, 제가 할 수 있는 한 명확히 해 드리지요. 어쩌면 당신이 그걸 바라신다고 짐작하는 게 옳을 듯하니, 좋습니다. 만일 케플러나 뉴턴의 발견이 한 사람, 열 사람, 백 사람, 혹은 그 이상의 목숨을 희생시키지 않고서는 세상에 알려질 수 없었다면, 뉴턴에게는 그 발견을 온 인류에게 알리기 위해 그 열 사람이나 백 사람을 제거할 권리가 있었을 것이고, 실은 그렇게 해야 할 의무까지 있었을 거라고 저는 주장합니다. 하지만 그렇다고 해서 뉴턴에게 닥치는 대로 사람을 죽이거나 날마다 시장에서 도둑질할 권리가 있다는 말은 아닙니다. 그리고 제 기억에, 저는 논문에서 모든…… 그러니까 입법자와 인류의 지도자들, 이를테면 리쿠르고스, 솔론, 마호메트, 나폴레옹 같은 자들은 예외 없이 죄다 범죄자였다고 주장합니다. 새로운 법을 만들면서 조상으로부터 물려받아 백성이 신성시하던 옛 법을 위반했다는 바로 그 사실 때문에요. 그리고 그들은 피 흘리는 일 앞에서도 멈추지 않았어요, 그 유혈이—흔히 옛 법을 지키려 용감히 싸우던 무고한 사람들의 피가—자기 대의에 도움이 된다면 말입니다. 사실 주목할 만한 건, 이 인류의 은인이자 지도자라는 자들의 대다수가 끔찍한 살육의 죄를 지었다는 점입니다. 요컨대 저는 모든 위인은, 아니 조금이라도 비범한 인간은, 다시 말해 무언가 새로운 말을 할 능력이 있는 인간은, 그 본성상 범죄자일 수밖에 없다고—물론 정도의 차이는 있지만—주장하는 겁니다. 그렇지 않으면 그들이 평범한 틀에서 벗어나기란 어려우니까요. 그리고 평범한 틀 안에 머무는 것, 그건 그들이 다시 또 그 본성상 도무지 받아들일 수 없는 노릇이고, 제 생각엔 사실 받아들여서도 안 됩니다. 보시다시피 이 모든 데 특별히 새로운 건 없어요. 같은 얘기가 이전에도 수천 번 인쇄되고 읽혔으니까요. 사람을 평범한 자와 비범한 자로 나눈 것에 관해서는, 다소 자의적이라는 점은 인정하지만, 정확한 수치를 고집하지는 않습니다. 다만 제가 믿는 건, 인간이 자연의 법칙에 의해 일반적으로 두 부류로 나뉜다는 그 기본 발상입니다. 하나는 열등한 (평범한) 부류, 그러니까 말하자면 오로지 제 종족을 재생산하는 데에만 쓰이는 재료고, 다른 하나는 새로운 말을 할 재능이나 능력을 지닌 인간들이지요. 물론 무수한 하위 분류가 있겠지만, 두 부류의 구별되는 특징은 제법 뚜렷합니다. 첫 번째 부류는 일반적으로 말해 기질이 보수적이고 법을 잘 지키는 인간들로, 통제 아래 살아가며 통제받기를 좋아하지요. 제 생각엔 통제받는 게 그들의 의무인데, 그게 그들의 소명이고, 그들에게는 거기에 굴욕적인 데가 전혀 없으니까요. 두 번째 부류는 모두 법을 위반합니다. 그들은 자기 역량에 따라 파괴자거나 파괴를 지향하는 자들이지요. 이들의 범죄야 물론 상대적이고 가지각색이지만, 대부분은 더 나은 것을 위해 더없이 다양한 방식으로 현재의 파괴를 추구합니다. 하지만 만일 그런 자가 자기 사상을 위해 시체를 넘어가거나 피를 헤치고 나아가야 한다면, 그는, 단언컨대, 자기 안에서, 자기 양심 속에서, 그 피를 헤치고 나아갈 정당한 근거를 찾아낼 수 있어요—그건 사상과 그 규모에 달린 거지요, 이 점에 유의하십시오. 제가 논문에서 그들의 범죄 권리를 말한 건 오직 그런 의미에서였습니다 (기억하시겠지만 그건 법률 문제로 시작됐지요). 하지만 그렇게 불안해하실 필요는 없습니다. 대중은 좀처럼 이 권리를 인정하지 않을 테니까요. 그들은 이런 자들을 처벌하거나 교수형에 처하는데 (정도의 차이는 있지만), 그렇게 함으로써 더없이 정당하게 자신들의 보수적 소명을 완수하는 거지요. 하지만 바로 그 대중이 다음 세대에 가서는 이 범죄자들을 받침대 위에 올려놓고 숭배하게 됩니다 (정도의 차이는 있지만). 첫 번째 부류는 언제나 현재의 인간이고, 두 번째 부류는 미래의 인간이지요. 첫째는 세계를 보존하고 인구를 늘리며, 둘째는 세계를 움직여 그 목표로 이끌어 갑니다. 두 부류 모두 존재할 동등한 권리가 있어요. 사실, 다들 저하고 동등한 권리를 가졌지요—그리고 영원한 전쟁이여 만세—물론, 새 예루살렘이 올 때까지 말입니다!\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 새 예루살렘을 믿는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"믿습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 단호히 대답했다. 이 말을 하면서, 또 앞서 늘어놓은 장광설 내내, 그는 양탄자의 한 지점에 눈길을 고정하고 있었다.\n\n\"그럼…… 그럼 신은 믿습니까? 호기심을 용서하십시오.\"\n\n\"믿습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 포르피리에게로 눈을 들며 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그럼…… 나사로가 죽은 자들 가운데서 부활한 것도 믿으시고요?\"\n\n\"전…… 믿습니다. 왜 이런 걸 다 물으시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"문자 그대로 믿으십니까?\"\n\n\"문자 그대로요.\"\n\n\"설마요……. 그저 호기심에서 물어본 겁니다. 용서하십시오. 한데 다시 그 문제로 돌아가 보지요. 그들이 늘 처형당하는 건 아니지요. 더러는 도리어…….\"\n\n\"살아생전에 승리한다고요? 오, 그렇지요, 더러는 이승에서 자기 목적을 이루고, 그러고는…….\"\n\n\"다른 사람들을 처형하기 시작하나요?\"\n\n\"필요하다면요. 실은 대부분 그렇게 하지요. 아주 재치 있는 지적이십니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다. 그런데 이건 좀 말씀해 주시지요. 그 비범한 사람들을 평범한 사람들과 어떻게 구별합니까? 태어날 때부터 무슨 표지라도 있나요? 제 생각엔 좀 더 정밀함이, 좀 더 외적인 규정이 있어야 할 것 같아서요. 실무적이고 법을 잘 지키는 시민으로서 당연한 불안을 용서해 주십시오만, 가령 그들에게 특별한 제복을 입힐 수는 없을까요, 무언가를 걸치게 하거나, 어떤 식으로든 낙인을 찍을 수는 없을까요? 아시다시피 만일 혼란이 생겨서 한쪽 부류에 속한 자가 자기를 다른 쪽이라고 착각하고, 당신이 그토록 절묘하게 표현하신 대로 '장애물을 제거'하기 시작한다면, 그땐…….\"\n\n\"오, 그런 일은 아주 흔히 일어나지요! 그 지적은 아까 것보다 더 재치 있군요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"별말씀을요. 다만 한 가지 유의하실 점은, 그런 착오는 오직 첫 번째 부류에서만, 그러니까 평범한 사람들 (제가 불행히도 그렇게 불렀을지 모르지만) 사이에서만 생긴다는 겁니다. 복종하려는 성향에도 불구하고, 그들 중 상당수는, 때로는 암소한테조차 베풀어지는 본성의 장난기 탓에, 스스로를 진보한 사람, '파괴자'라 상상하고는 '새로운 운동'에 끼어들고 싶어 하는데, 그것도 아주 진심으로 말이지요. 한편 정작 진짜로 새로운 사람들은 그들 눈에 띄지 않는 경우가 허다하고, 심지어 비굴한 성향의 반동분자로 멸시당하기까지 합니다. 하지만 여기에 무슨 대단한 위험이 있다고는 생각지 않으니, 정말 불안해하실 것 없습니다. 그들은 결코 멀리까지 나아가지 못하니까요. 물론 공상에 휩쓸린 죄로, 또 제 분수를 가르쳐 주려고 이따금 매질을 당할 수도 있겠지만, 그뿐이지요. 사실은 이마저도 필요 없어요, 그들이 스스로를 징벌하니까요, 워낙 양심적이거든요. 어떤 자들은 서로 이 일을 대신해 주고, 또 어떤 자들은 제 손으로 자신을 매질하지요……. 그들은 스스로에게 갖가지 공개적인 참회 행위를 부과하는데, 그게 아름답고 교화적인 효과를 낸답니다. 요컨대 당신이 불안해하실 건 아무것도 없어요……. 그게 자연의 법칙이니까요.\"\n\n\"음, 그 점에 관해서는 확실히 제 마음이 한결 놓이는군요. 하지만 또 한 가지 마음에 걸리는 게 있습니다. 좀 말씀해 주시지요, 다른 사람을 죽일 권리를 가진 사람, 그 비범한 사람들이 많이 있습니까? 물론 저야 그들에게 기꺼이 머리를 조아릴 준비가 되어 있습니다만, 그런 자들이 아주 많다면 섬뜩한 노릇이 아니겠습니까, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"오, 그것도 걱정하실 것 없습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 같은 어조로 말을 이었다. \"새로운 사상을 가진 사람, 무언가 새로운 말을 할 능력을 조금이라도 가진 사람은 그 수가 극히 적습니다, 사실 유별나게 적지요. 한 가지만은 분명한데, 인간의 이 모든 등급과 하위 분류의 출현은 어떤 자연의 법칙을 한 치의 어김도 없이 따른다는 겁니다. 물론 그 법칙은 현재로선 알려져 있지 않지만, 저는 그게 존재한다고, 그리고 언젠가는 알려질 수도 있다고 확신합니다. 인류의 그 거대한 다수란 한낱 재료에 불과하며, 오직 어떤 위대한 노력을 통해, 어떤 신비로운 과정을 통해, 어떤 종족과 혈통의 교배를 통해, 마침내 독립성의 불꽃을 지닌 인간을 천에 하나쯤 세상에 내놓기 위해서만 존재하는 거지요. 만에 하나쯤은—대략적으로, 어림잡아 말하는 겁니다만—어느 정도의 독립성을 지니고 태어나고, 십만에 하나쯤은 더 큰 독립성을 지니고 태어납니다. 천재는 수백만에 하나꼴이고, 위대한 천재, 인류의 왕관이라 할 자들은 어쩌면 수십억에 하나쯤 이 땅에 나타나겠지요. 사실 저는 이 모든 일이 벌어지는 그 시험관 속을 들여다본 적은 없습니다. 하지만 분명 어떤 명확한 법칙이 있고, 또 있을 수밖에 없어요, 그게 우연의 문제일 리는 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"아니, 자네들 지금 농담하는 거야?\" 라주미힌이 마침내 외쳤다. \"거기 앉아서 서로 놀려 먹고 있잖아. 진심이야, 로쟈?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 창백하고 거의 비통하기까지 한 얼굴을 들었으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그리고 그 조용하고 비통한 얼굴 곁에서, 포르피리의 그 감추지 않는, 집요하고 신경질적이며 무례한 빈정거림이 라주미힌에게는 기이하게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"음, 친구, 자네가 정말 진심이라면…… 물론 자네 말이 옳아, 이게 새로울 게 없다는 것, 우리가 이미 수천 번 읽고 들은 것과 다름없다는 것 말이야. 하지만 이 모든 데서 정말로 독창적인 것, 오로지 자네만의 것은—나로서는 끔찍하게도—양심의 이름으로 유혈을 정당화한다는 점이고, 이런 말 해서 미안하지만, 그것도 그토록 광신적으로 말이야……. 내가 보기엔 그게 자네 논문의 요점인 것 같아. 하지만 양심에 의한 그 유혈의 정당화란 내 생각엔…… 공적이고 법적인 유혈의 정당화보다 더 끔찍한 거라고…….\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 그게 더 끔찍하지요.\" 포르피리가 동의했다.\n\n\"그래, 자네가 과장한 게 틀림없어! 무슨 착오가 있는 거야, 내가 읽어 보겠어. 자네가 그런 생각을 할 리 없어! 읽어 보겠다고.\"\n\n\"그건 죄다 논문엔 없는 얘기야, 거기엔 그저 암시만 있을 뿐이지.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요.\" 포르피리는 가만히 앉아 있질 못했다. \"범죄에 대한 당신의 태도는 이제 제게 꽤 분명해졌습니다, 하지만…… 제 무례를 용서하십시오 (이렇게 당신을 괴롭히다니 정말 부끄럽군요), 보십시오, 당신은 두 등급이 뒤섞이는 데 대한 제 불안은 없애 주셨지만…… 저를 불안하게 하는 갖가지 실제적인 가능성들이 있단 말입니다! 만일 어떤 사내나 젊은이가 자기를 리쿠르고스나 마호메트라고—물론 미래의 인물이라고—상상하고는, 그래서 모든 장애물을 제거하기 시작한다면 어쩌지요……. 그자 앞에 무슨 위대한 사업이 놓여 있고 거기엔 돈이 필요한데…… 그 돈을 마련하려 든다면…… 무슨 말인지 아시겠습니까?\"\n\n자메토프가 자기 구석에서 갑자기 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 라스콜니코프는 그에게 눈길조차 들지 않았다.\n\n\"인정합니다.\" 그가 차분하게 말을 이었다. \"그런 경우가 분명 생기긴 할 겁니다. 허영심 많고 어리석은 자들이 특히 그 덫에 빠지기 쉽지요, 특히 젊은이들이요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 보십시오. 그럼 어떻게 됩니까?\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 되긴요?\" 라스콜니코프가 대답 삼아 미소 지었다. \"그건 제 잘못이 아니지요. 일이 그러하고, 또 늘 그러할 테니까요. 저 친구가 방금 (그가 라주미힌을 향해 고갯짓했다) 제가 유혈을 정당화한다고 했지요. 사회는 감옥과 유형, 형사 수사관, 강제 노역으로 충분히 잘 보호되고 있습니다. 불안해하실 것 없어요. 그저 도둑을 잡기만 하면 되니까요.\"\n\n\"그래서 우리가 그자를 잡는다면요?\"\n\n\"그럼 그자는 마땅한 벌을 받는 거지요.\"\n\n\"확실히 논리적이시군요. 하지만 그자의 양심은 어떻게 되지요?\"\n\n\"그게 왜 신경 쓰이십니까?\"\n\n\"그저 인간애에서요.\"\n\n\"양심이 있는 자라면 자기 잘못 때문에 괴로워하겠지요. 그게 그자의 벌이 될 겁니다—감옥과 더불어 말이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 진짜 천재들은,\" 라주미힌이 눈살을 찌푸리며 물었다. \"살인할 권리를 가진 그자들은? 그자들은 자기가 흘린 피에 대해서도 전혀 괴로워하지 않아도 되는 거야?\"\n\n\"왜 '되는'이라는 말을 쓰지? 허락이나 금지의 문제가 아니야. 자기 희생자를 가엾게 여긴다면 괴로워하겠지. 고통과 괴로움이란 위대한 지성과 깊은 마음에는 언제나 피할 수 없는 거니까. 진정으로 위대한 인간은, 내 생각엔, 이 땅에서 커다란 슬픔을 짊어질 수밖에 없어.\" 그가 대화의 어조와는 다르게, 꿈결처럼 덧붙였다.\n\n그는 눈을 들어 그들 모두를 진지하게 바라보고는, 미소 짓더니 모자를 집어 들었다. 그는 들어올 때의 태도에 비해 너무 조용해져 있었고, 스스로도 그것을 느꼈다. 모두가 일어섰다.\n\n\"음, 절 욕하셔도 좋고, 원하신다면 화를 내셔도 좋습니다.\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 다시 입을 열었다. \"하지만 도무지 참을 수가 없군요. 자그마한 질문 하나만 허락해 주십시오 (당신을 번거롭게 하는 줄 압니다). 그저 잊어버리지 않으려고, 표현해 두고 싶은 자그마한 생각이 하나 있어서요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 그 자그마한 생각이란 걸 말씀하시지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 창백하고 근엄한 얼굴로 그 앞에 서서 기다렸다.\n\n\"음, 보십시오…… 제대로 어떻게 표현해야 할지 정말 모르겠군요……. 장난스러운, 심리적인 발상입니다……. 당신이 그 논문을 쓰실 때, 분명 어쩔 수 없이, 헤헤! 스스로를 그려 보지 않았을까요…… 아주 조금이나마, 당신이 말한 그 의미에서 새로운 말을 내뱉는 '비범한' 인간이라고 말이지요……. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"충분히 그럴 수 있지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 경멸하듯 대답했다.\n\n라주미힌이 움찔했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 세상살이의 곤란과 고난을 만났을 때, 혹은 인류에 대한 어떤 봉사를 위해—당신은 장애물을 뛰어넘는 일을 감행할 수 있겠습니까?…… 가령, 도둑질을 하고 살인을 저지르는 일을요?\"\n\n그러고는 그가 다시 왼쪽 눈으로 윙크를 하더니, 아까처럼 소리 없이 웃었다.\n\n\"만일 그랬다 한들 당신한테 결코 말하지는 않겠지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 도전적이고 오만한 경멸을 담아 대답했다.\n\n\"아니요, 전 그저 당신 논문 때문에, 문학적인 관점에서 흥미가 있었을 뿐입니다…….\"\n\n'쳇! 어찌나 빤하고 무례한지!' 라스콜니코프는 혐오스럽게 생각했다.\n\n\"한 말씀 드리지요.\" 그가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다. \"전 제 자신을 마호메트나 나폴레옹이라고도, 그런 부류의 어떤 인물이라고도 여기지 않습니다. 그러니 그런 자가 아닌 이상, 제가 어떻게 행동할지 당신께 말씀드릴 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"오, 이런, 요즘 러시아에서 우리 모두가 자기를 나폴레옹쯤으로 여기지 않습니까?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 섬뜩할 만큼 친근하게 말했다.\n\n그의 바로 그 목소리의 억양에 무언가 묘한 것이 배어 나왔다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그런 미래의 나폴레옹들 중 하나가 지난주에 알료나 이바노브나를 해치운 게 아닐까요?\" 자메토프가 구석에서 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 말없이, 단단하고 뚫어지게 포르피리를 바라보았다. 라주미힌은 침울하게 눈살을 찌푸리고 있었다. 그는 이미 그전부터 무언가를 알아채고 있는 듯했다. 그는 성난 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보았다. 잠시 침울한 침묵이 흘렀다. 라스콜니코프가 가려고 돌아섰다.\n\n\"벌써 가시려고요?\" 포르피리가 다정하게 말하며, 지나칠 만큼 정중하게 손을 내밀었다. \"알게 되어 정말, 정말 기쁩니다. 당신 요청에 관해서는, 불안해하지 마시고, 제가 말씀드린 대로 글로 써 주시거나, 아니면 더 나은 건, 하루 이틀 안에 직접 저한테 거기로 오시는 겁니다…… 사실 내일이라도요. 제가 열한 시엔 틀림없이 거기 있을 테니까요. 다 정리해 드리지요, 얘기도 좀 나누고요. 거기 마지막으로 갔던 분들 중 한 명으로서, 어쩌면 우리한테 뭔가 들려주실 수 있을지도 모르고요.\" 그가 더없이 호인다운 표정으로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"날 정식으로, 격식대로 심문하시겠다는 겁니까?\" 라스콜니코프가 날카롭게 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그럴 리가요? 지금으로선 그럴 필요 없습니다. 오해하셨군요. 보십시오, 저는 어떤 기회도 놓치지 않거든요, 그래서…… 저당물을 잡힌 분들과 죄다 얘기를 나눴지요……. 그중 몇 분한테서는 진술을 얻어 냈고, 당신이 마지막입니다……. 아, 그래, 그건 그렇고,\" 그가 갑자기 반색한 듯 외쳤다. \"방금 생각났네요, 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었던 거지?\" 그가 라주미힌을 향해 돌아섰다. \"자네가 그 니콜라이 얘기로 내 귀가 닳도록 떠들어 댔잖아…… 물론, 나도 알아, 아주 잘 알지,\" 그가 라스콜니코프에게로 돌아섰다. \"그자가 결백하다는 건 말입니다, 하지만 어쩌겠습니까? 드미트리까지 번거롭게 해야 했지요……. 요점은 이겁니다, 이게 전부예요. 당신이 계단을 올라갔을 때가 일곱 시가 지난 무렵이었지요, 안 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"네.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했는데, 그 말을 내뱉는 바로 그 순간 굳이 할 필요 없는 말이었다는 불쾌한 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"그럼 일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이에 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 이 층에 활짝 열려 있던 셋집 안에서, 기억하십니까? 인부 두 사람, 적어도 그중 하나라도 보지 못했나요? 그들이 거기서 페인트칠을 하고 있었는데, 눈치채지 못하셨습니까? 그게 그들한테는 아주, 아주 중요한 문제라서요.\"\n\n\"페인트공이요? 아니요, 못 봤습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 마치 기억을 샅샅이 뒤지는 듯 천천히 대답했지만, 바로 그 순간 그는 온 신경을 곤두세운 채, 덫이 어디에 놓여 있는지 한시바삐 알아채고 무엇 하나 놓치지 않으려는 불안에 거의 정신을 잃을 지경이었다. \"아니요, 그들을 보지 못했고, 그렇게 활짝 열린 셋집을 봤던 것 같지도 않은데요……. 하지만 사 층에서는\" (이제 그는 덫을 간파했고 의기양양했다) \"지금 생각나는데, 알료나 이바노브나의 맞은편 셋집에서 누군가 이사를 나가고 있었어요……. 기억나요…… 또렷이 기억납니다. 인부 몇 사람이 소파를 들어내면서 절 벽에 밀어붙였거든요. 하지만 페인트공은…… 아니요, 페인트공이 있었던 기억은 없고, 어디에도 활짝 열린 셋집이 있었던 것 같지 않습니다, 아니, 없었어요.\"\n\n\"이게 무슨 소린가?\" 라주미힌이 무언가 곰곰이 생각하다 퍼뜩 깨달은 듯 갑자기 소리쳤다. \"아니, 페인트공들이 일한 건 살인이 있던 날이었고, 이 친구가 거기 갔던 건 그 사흘 전이었잖아? 자넨 대체 뭘 묻고 있는 거야?\"\n\n\"쳇! 내가 헷갈렸군!\" 포르피리가 자기 이마를 탁 쳤다. \"젠장맞을! 이놈의 사건이 내 머리를 어떻게 만드는지 원!\" 그가 다소 변명조로 라스콜니코프에게 말했다. \"일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이에 그 셋집에서 그들을 본 사람이 있는지 알아내는 게 우리한테는 워낙 중대한 일이라서, 어쩌면 당신이 뭔가 들려줄 수 있을까 싶었던 겁니다……. 완전히 헷갈렸네요.\"\n\n\"그럼 좀 더 조심하셔야지요.\" 라주미힌이 무뚝뚝하게 한마디 했다.\n\n마지막 말들은 복도에서 나눈 것이었다. 포르피리 페트로비치는 지나칠 만큼 정중하게 그들을 문까지 배웅했다.\n\n그들은 침울하고 시무룩한 채 거리로 나섰고, 몇 걸음을 걷는 동안 한마디도 하지 않았다. 라스콜니코프는 깊게 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n「믿을 수 없어, 도저히 믿을 수가 없다고!」 라주미힌은 당혹스러워하며 라스콜니코프의 논거를 반박하려 애쓰면서 거듭 말했다.\n\n그들은 어느덧 바칼레예프 하숙집에 다다르고 있었는데, 그곳에서는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나와 두냐가 오래도록 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 라주미힌은 가는 길에 자꾸만 멈춰 서곤 했으니, 논쟁의 열기에 휩싸여, 두 사람이 처음으로 그 일에 대해 터놓고 이야기하고 있다는 사실 자체에 어쩔 줄 모르고 흥분했던 것이다.\n\n「그럼 믿지 말든가!」 라스콜니코프가 차갑고 무심한 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. 「자넨 늘 그렇듯 아무것도 알아차리지 못했지만, 난 말 한마디 한마디를 다 따져 봤어.」\n\n「자넨 의심이 많아. 그래서 그들의 말을 그렇게 따져 본 거지……, 흠……, 그래, 동의해, 포르피리의 어조가 좀 이상하긴 했어, 게다가 그 비열한 자메토프 녀석은 더했고!……, 자네 말이 맞아, 그자한테 뭔가 있긴 했어 — 한데 왜? 왜 그러는 거지?」\n\n「어젯밤 이후로 그자가 마음을 바꾼 거야.」\n\n「오히려 정반대지! 만약 그들이 그런 멍청한 생각을 품었다면, 그걸 숨기려고 갖은 애를 쓰고 패를 감췄을 거야, 나중에 자넬 잡으려고……. 한데 모든 게 뻔뻔하고 부주의했잖아.」\n\n「만약 그들에게 증거가 — 그러니까 진짜 증거가 — 아니면 적어도 의심할 근거라도 있었다면, 분명 자기 패를 숨기려 했겠지, 더 많은 걸 얻어 내려고 (게다가 진작 가택 수색을 했을 거고). 하지만 그들에겐 증거가 없어, 단 하나도. 죄다 신기루야 — 죄다 모호하기만 하지. 그저 떠도는 생각일 뿐이라고. 그래서 뻔뻔하게 굴어서 날 흔들어 보려는 거야. 어쩌면 그자는 증거가 없어서 짜증이 났고, 그 분풀이로 불쑥 내뱉었는지도 몰라 — 아니면 무슨 계획이 있는 건지도……, 똑똑한 사람 같으니까. 어쩌면 다 아는 척하면서 날 겁주려 했는지도 모르지. 그들에게는 그들 나름의 심리학이 있거든, 이 사람아. 하지만 이 모든 걸 설명하려니 역겹군. 그만하지!」\n\n「그래, 모욕적이야, 모욕적이라고! 자네 심정 이해해. 하지만……, 이제 우리가 터놓고 이야기했으니까 (드디어 그렇게 된 게 정말 잘된 일이야 — 난 기뻐) 솔직히 털어놓자면, 나도 진작부터 그들한테서 그런 낌새를, 그런 생각을 눈치챘어. 물론 아주 희미한 암시뿐이었지 — 넌지시 떠보는 정도 — 한데 떠보는 것조차 왜 그러는 거야? 감히 어떻게 그래? 무슨 근거로? 내가 얼마나 화가 났는지 자네가 알기만 한다면. 생각 좀 해 봐! 그저 가난뱅이 대학생 하나가, 가난과 우울증에 짓눌리고, 극심한 섬망성 열병에 걸리기 직전에 (이 점에 주목하라고), 의심 많고, 자존심 강하고, 거만하고, 여섯 달 동안 말 한마디 나눌 사람조차 만나지 못한 채, 누더기를 걸치고 밑창도 없는 장화를 신고서, 어떤 한심한 경찰 나부랭이들과 맞닥뜨려 그들의 무례함을 참아 내야 하고, 게다가 느닷없이 빚 독촉을 코앞에 들이대고, 체바로프가 차용증을 내밀고, 새로 칠한 페인트 냄새에, 섭씨 삼십도가 넘는 무더위에 숨 막히는 공기에, 사람들이 북적대고, 바로 직전에 머물렀던 곳에서 누군가가 살해당했다는 이야기가 오가고, 그 모든 게 빈속에서 벌어졌으니 — 까무러칠 만도 하지! 한데 바로 그것을, 바로 그것을 근거로 삼다니! 빌어먹을 놈들! 짜증나는 거 이해해, 하지만 내가 자네라면, 로쟈, 그자들을 비웃어 주겠어, 아니 그보다 더 좋은 건, 그 추악한 낯짝에 침을 뱉어 주겠어, 사방으로 열두 번은 뱉어 주겠다고. 사방으로 후려갈겨 주겠어, 그것도 깔끔하게, 그래서 끝장을 내 버리겠어. 빌어먹을 놈들! 기죽지 마. 부끄러운 일이야!」\n\n'그래도 녀석, 말 한번 잘하는군.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다.\n\n「빌어먹을 놈들이라고? 그래도 내일 또 심문이 있잖아?」 그가 쓰라리게 말했다. 「정말 내가 그들과 일일이 변명을 주고받아야 하나? 어제 식당에서 자메토프한테 굽실거리며 말을 건 것만 해도 짜증이 나는데…….」\n\n「제기랄! 내가 직접 포르피리한테 가겠어. 한 식구나 다름없으니, 그자한테서 죄다 짜내고 말겠어. 그자는 이 모든 게 어찌 된 영문인지 나한테 다 털어놔야 해! 그리고 자메토프 그놈은…….」\n\n'드디어 녀석이 그자의 속을 꿰뚫어 보는군!' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다.\n\n「잠깐!」 라주미힌이 다시 그의 어깨를 붙잡으며 외쳤다. 「잠깐만! 자네가 틀렸어. 곰곰이 생각해 봤거든. 자네가 틀렸다고! 그게 어떻게 함정이야? 자넨 인부들에 관한 질문이 함정이었다고 하잖아. 한데 만약 자네가 그 일을 저질렀다면, 그 집에 페인트칠을 하고 있는 걸 봤다고……, 그 인부들을 봤다고 말할 수 있었겠어? 오히려 아무것도 못 봤을 거야, 설령 봤다 해도 말이야. 누가 자기한테 불리한 걸 인정하겠어?」\n\n「만약 내가 그 일을 저질렀다면, 분명 인부들과 그 집을 봤다고 말했을 거야.」 라스콜니코프가 마지못해, 노골적인 혐오감을 드러내며 대답했다.\n\n「하지만 왜 자기한테 불리하게 말한단 말이야?」\n\n「왜냐하면 심문에서 무조건 죄다 잡아떼는 건 농사꾼이나 가장 풋내기 초짜들뿐이거든. 사람이 조금이라도 머리가 트이고 경험이 있으면, 분명 피할 수 없는 외적 사실들은 죄다 인정하려 들면서도, 그것들에 대해 다른 설명을 찾아내려 하고, 뭔가 특별하고 뜻밖의 반전을 끌어들여서, 그것들에 다른 의미를 부여하고 다른 빛깔로 보이게 하려 들지. 포르피리는 내가 분명 그렇게 대답하리라고, 그러니까 진실처럼 보이려고 그들을 봤다고 말하고 나서 무슨 설명을 갖다 붙이리라고 충분히 계산했을 거야.」\n\n「하지만 그자는 곧장 자네한테 이렇게 말했겠지, 인부들이 이틀 전에 거기 있었을 리 없다고, 그러니 자넨 살인이 있던 날 여덟 시에 거기 있었던 게 틀림없다고. 그렇게 해서 자넬 사소한 데서 걸려들게 했겠지.」\n\n「그래, 바로 그걸 노린 거야, 내가 미처 곰곰이 따져 볼 겨를도 없이 서둘러 가장 그럴듯한 대답을 하려 들 거고, 그래서 인부들이 이틀 전에 거기 있었을 리 없다는 걸 까먹으리라고 말이야.」\n\n「하지만 어떻게 그걸 까먹을 수가 있어?」\n\n「그보다 더 쉬운 일도 없어. 영리한 사람들은 바로 그런 멍청한 일에 가장 쉽게 걸려드는 법이거든. 사람이 교활할수록, 단순한 일에 걸려들 거라고는 덜 의심하는 법이야. 사람이 교활할수록, 더 단순한 함정에 걸려들게 마련이지. 포르피리는 자네가 생각하는 것만큼 바보가 아니야…….」\n\n「그렇다면 그자는 악당이로군, 정말 그렇다면!」\n\n라스콜니코프는 웃지 않을 수 없었다. 하지만 바로 그 순간, 그는 자기 자신의 솔직함에, 그리고 이 설명을 늘어놓은 그 열의에 흠칫 놀랐다. 앞선 대화는 죄다 음울한 거부감으로, 분명 어떤 의도가 있어서, 필요에 의해 이어 왔으면서도 말이다.\n\n'어떤 면들이 슬슬 입맛에 맞아 가는군!' 그는 속으로 생각했다. 하지만 거의 같은 순간, 그는 마치 뜻밖의 불안한 생각이 떠오른 듯 갑자기 께름칙해졌다. 그의 불안은 점점 더 커져만 갔다. 그들은 막 바칼레예프 하숙집 입구에 다다른 참이었다.\n\n「혼자 들어가게!」 라스콜니코프가 불쑥 말했다. 「난 곧 돌아올 테니.」\n\n「어딜 가는 거야? 아니, 우린 막 다 왔는데.」\n\n「어쩔 수 없어……. 삼십 분 안에 올게. 그들한테 그렇게 전해 줘.」\n\n「뭐라고 하든, 난 자네랑 같이 가겠어.」\n\n「자네까지도 날 괴롭히려 드는군!」 그가 비명을 질렀는데, 그 눈에 어린 쓰라린 짜증, 그 절망이 어찌나 깊던지 라주미힌의 두 손이 스르르 떨어졌다. 라주미힌은 얼마 동안 계단에 서서, 라스콜니코프가 자기 하숙집 쪽으로 성큼성큼 빠르게 걸어가는 모습을 음울하게 바라보았다. 마침내 그는 이를 악물고 주먹을 불끈 쥐며, 바로 오늘 안으로 포르피리를 레몬 짜듯 짜내고 말겠다고 다짐하고는, 두 사람이 오래도록 보이지 않아 이젠 불안해하고 있을 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나를 안심시키러 계단을 올라갔다.\n\n라스콜니코프가 집에 다다랐을 때, 그의 머리칼은 땀에 흠뻑 젖어 있었고 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬고 있었다. 그는 재빨리 계단을 올라, 잠겨 있지 않은 자기 방으로 들어가 곧장 빗장을 걸었다. 그러고는 영문 모를 공포에 사로잡혀 구석으로, 물건들을 숨겨 두었던 벽지 밑 그 구멍으로 달려가, 손을 집어넣고 몇 분 동안이나 그 구멍 속을, 벽지의 갈라진 틈과 접힌 곳을 하나하나 조심스레 더듬어 보았다. 아무것도 찾지 못하자, 그는 몸을 일으켜 깊은 숨을 내쉬었다. 바칼레예프 하숙집 계단에 막 이르렀을 때, 문득 그는 무언가가 — 사슬이든, 단추든, 아니면 노파의 글씨가 적힌, 물건들을 쌌던 종잇조각이라도 — 어쩌다 흘러나와 어떤 틈새에 떨어져 사라졌다가, 어느 순간 느닷없이 자신에게 불리한 결정적인 증거로 불쑥 튀어나올지도 모른다는 생각이 들었던 것이다.\n\n그는 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있었고, 그의 입술에는 기이하고 비굴한, 반쯤은 얼이 빠진 미소가 어른거렸다. 마침내 그는 모자를 집어 들고 조용히 방을 나섰다. 그의 생각은 죄다 뒤엉켜 있었다. 그는 몽롱한 채로 대문을 지나쳤다.\n\n「저기 본인이 오는구먼.」 우렁찬 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n그는 고개를 들었다.\n\n문지기가 자기 작은 방 문간에 서서, 직공처럼 보이는 키 작은 사내에게 그를 가리키고 있었다. 사내는 긴 외투에 조끼를 걸쳤는데, 멀리서 보면 놀랄 만큼 여자처럼 보였다. 그는 구부정했고, 기름때 묻은 모자를 쓴 머리는 앞으로 푹 숙여져 있었다. 주름지고 축 늘어진 얼굴로 보아 쉰은 넘긴 듯했고, 작은 두 눈은 살에 파묻힌 채 음침하고 매섭고 못마땅한 빛을 띠고 밖을 내다보고 있었다.\n\n「무슨 일입니까?」 라스콜니코프가 문지기에게 다가가며 물었다.\n\n사내는 눈썹 밑으로 그를 슬쩍 훔쳐보더니, 주의 깊게, 천천히 그를 바라보았다. 그러고는 느릿느릿 돌아서서 한마디 말도 없이 대문을 나서 거리로 가 버렸다.\n\n「무슨 일이오?」 라스콜니코프가 외쳤다.\n\n「저기, 저 사람이 여기 대학생이 사느냐고 물으면서, 당신 이름이랑 누구네 집에 세 들어 사는지 대더군요. 마침 당신이 오는 게 보여서 가리켰더니, 저렇게 가 버리네요. 거참 이상하구먼.」\n\n문지기도 다소 어리둥절한 듯했지만, 그리 대수롭지는 않은 듯, 잠시 의아해하다가 돌아서서 자기 방으로 들어가 버렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 그 낯선 사내를 뒤쫓아 달려갔고, 곧 그가 거리 맞은편을 따라, 아까와 똑같이 한결같고 느릿한 걸음으로, 마치 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 시선을 땅에 박은 채 걸어가는 모습을 발견했다. 그는 곧 사내를 따라잡았지만, 한동안은 그 뒤를 따라 걸었다. 마침내 그와 나란히 서게 되자, 그는 사내의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 사내는 곧장 그를 알아채고 재빨리 그를 쳐다보았다가, 이내 다시 눈길을 떨구었다. 그렇게 두 사람은 한마디 말도 없이 잠시 나란히 걸었다.\n\n「저를 찾으셨더군요……, 문지기한테?」 라스콜니코프가 마침내 입을 열었는데, 이상하리만치 조용한 목소리였다.\n\n사내는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그를 쳐다보지도 않았다. 다시 두 사람은 입을 다물었다.\n\n「왜……, 와서 절 찾으시고는……, 아무 말도 안 하시는 겁니까……. 이게 무슨 영문이지요?」\n\n라스콜니코프의 목소리가 갈라졌고, 그는 말을 또렷이 입에 올리지도 못하는 것 같았다.\n\n이번에는 사내가 눈을 들어, 음울하고 불길한 눈초리로 라스콜니코프를 돌아보았다.\n\n「살인자.」 그가 불쑥, 조용하지만 또렷하고 분명한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 계속 그의 곁에서 걸어갔다. 두 다리가 갑자기 힘이 풀리고, 등줄기로 차가운 전율이 흘렀으며, 심장이 한순간 멎는 듯하다가, 이내 풀려난 듯 갑자기 마구 뛰기 시작했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 백 걸음쯤 말없이 나란히 걸었다.\n\n사내는 그를 쳐다보지 않았다.\n\n「무슨 말씀입니까……, 그게 무슨……. 누가 살인자라는 거죠?」 라스콜니코프가 거의 들리지도 않게 중얼거렸다.\n\n「바로 당신이 살인자야.」 사내가 한층 더 또렷하고 단호하게, 승리에 찬 증오의 미소를 지으며 대답했고, 다시 한번 라스콜니코프의 창백한 얼굴과 충격에 휩싸인 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다.\n\n두 사람은 막 사거리에 다다른 참이었다. 사내는 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 왼쪽으로 꺾어 들었다. 라스콜니코프는 그 자리에 선 채로, 그의 뒷모습을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그는 사내가 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 돌아서서, 여전히 그 자리에 서 있는 자신을 돌아보는 것을 보았다. 라스콜니코프는 또렷이 볼 수는 없었지만, 사내가 다시 그 차가운 증오와 승리의 미소를 짓고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n느리고 비틀거리는 걸음으로, 무릎을 후들거리며, 라스콜니코프는 온몸에 한기를 느끼며 자기 작은 다락방으로 돌아갔다. 그는 모자를 벗어 탁자에 올려놓고, 십 분 동안이나 꼼짝도 않고 서 있었다. 그러고는 기진맥진하여 소파에 쓰러져, 고통에 찬 가냘픈 신음을 내며 그 위에 몸을 뻗었다. 그렇게 그는 삼십 분을 누워 있었다.\n\n그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 어떤 생각들, 혹은 생각의 파편들이, 두서도 맥락도 없는 어떤 영상들이 그의 마음 앞에 떠다녔다 — 어린 시절에 보았거나 언젠가 어딘가에서 마주쳤던, 결코 다시는 떠올리지 못했을 사람들의 얼굴, V 마을 교회의 종루, 어느 식당의 당구대와 당구를 치던 몇몇 장교들, 지하 어느 담배 가게의 시가 냄새, 어느 선술집 방, 더러운 물로 온통 질척거리고 달걀 껍질이 나뒹구는 새카만 뒷계단, 그리고 어디선가 들려오는 일요일의 종소리……. 영상들은 회오리바람처럼 소용돌이치며 잇따라 떠올랐다. 그 가운데 몇몇은 마음에 들어서 붙잡으려 해 보았지만, 이내 스러져 갔고, 그동안 내내 그의 안에는 억눌린 무언가가 있었으나, 그것은 견딜 수 없을 정도는 아니었고, 때로는 심지어 기분 좋기까지 했다……. 가벼운 오한은 여전히 가시지 않았지만, 그것 또한 거의 기분 좋은 감각이었다.\n\n그는 라주미힌의 다급한 발소리를 들었다. 그는 눈을 감고 자는 척했다. 라주미힌이 문을 열고 잠시 문간에 서 있었는데, 마치 망설이는 듯하다가, 살그머니 방 안으로 들어와 조심스레 소파로 다가왔다. 라스콜니코프는 나스타시야가 속삭이는 소리를 들었다.\n\n「깨우지 마세요! 자게 두세요. 식사는 나중에 해도 되니까.」\n\n「그러게.」 라주미힌이 대답했다. 두 사람은 조심스레 물러나 문을 닫았다. 다시 삼십 분이 흘렀다. 라스콜니코프는 눈을 뜨고, 다시 등을 대고 돌아누워, 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 꼈다.\n\n'그자는 누구지? 땅속에서 솟아난 듯한 그 사내는 누구란 말인가? 그자는 어디 있었던 거지, 뭘 본 거고? 모든 걸 봤어, 그건 분명해. 그렇다면 그때 그자는 어디 있었던 거지? 어디서 본 거고? 왜 이제 와서야 땅속에서 솟아난 거지? 그리고 도대체 어떻게 볼 수 있었던 거야? 가능한 일인가? 흠…….' 라스콜니코프는 온몸이 싸늘해지며 오한에 떨면서 생각을 이어 갔다. '그리고 니콜라이가 문 뒤에서 찾아냈다는 그 보석 상자 — 그게 가능한 일이었나? 단서라고? 아주 미세한 한 가닥을 놓치면, 그걸 증거의 피라미드로 쌓아 올릴 수도 있는 거지! 파리 한 마리가 날아와 그걸 본 거라고! 가능한 일인가?' 그는 자신이 얼마나 약해졌는지, 육체적으로 얼마나 쇠약해졌는지 문득 혐오감과 함께 느꼈다. '진작 알았어야 했어.' 그는 쓰라린 미소를 지으며 생각했다. '그리고 내 자신을 알면서, 내가 어떻게 될지 알면서, 감히 어떻게 도끼를 집어 들고 피를 흘렸단 말인가! 미리 알았어야 했어……. 아, 하지만 난 알고 있었어!' 그는 절망에 차서 속삭였다. 이따금 그는 어떤 생각에 우뚝 멈춰 서곤 했다.\n\n'아니야, 그런 인간들은 그렇게 생겨먹지 않았어. 모든 것이 허용되는 진정한 지배자라면 툴롱을 함락시키고, 파리에서 학살을 자행하고, 이집트에 군대를 잊고 내버려 두고, 모스크바 원정에서 오십만 명을 허비하고도, 빌나에서 농담 한마디로 빠져나가지. 게다가 죽은 뒤에는 그에게 제단까지 바쳐지고, 그러니 모든 것이 허용되는 거야. 아니, 그런 사람들은 아무래도 살로 빚어진 게 아니라 청동으로 만들어진 모양이야!'\n\n문득 떠오른 엉뚱한 생각 하나에 그는 하마터면 웃음이 터질 뻔했다. 나폴레옹, 피라미드, 워털루 — 그리고 침대 밑에 빨간 궤짝을 둔, 비쩍 마른 한심한 노파 전당포 주인이라니 — 포르피리 페트로비치가 소화하기엔 참으로 딱 알맞은 잡탕이로군! 그들이 어떻게 그걸 소화할 수 있겠어! 너무 예술성이 없잖아. '나폴레옹이 노파의 침대 밑을 기어 다니다니! 윽, 어찌나 역겨운지!'\n\n이따금 그는 자신이 헛소리를 하고 있다고 느꼈다. 그는 열에 들뜬 흥분 상태로 빠져들었다. '그 노파는 아무것도 아니야.' 그는 격하게, 두서없이 생각했다. '그 노파는 어쩌면 실수였는지도 몰라, 하지만 그게 중요한 건 아니지! 그 노파는 그저 하나의 병이었을 뿐이야……. 난 선을 넘으려고 서둘렀던 거고……. 난 한 인간을 죽인 게 아니라, 하나의 원칙을 죽인 거야! 원칙은 죽였지만, 선을 넘지는 못했어, 난 이쪽 편에 멈춰 서고 말았지……. 난 그저 죽일 줄만 알았던 거야. 그런데 그것조차 제대로 할 줄 몰랐던 것 같군……. 원칙이라고? 그 멍청한 라주미힌은 왜 사회주의자들을 욕했던 거지? 그들은 부지런하고 실속 있는 사람들이야. '만인의 행복'이 그들의 대의지. 아니, 인생은 내게 단 한 번만 주어졌고, 다시는 결코 얻지 못할 텐데. 난 '만인의 행복'을 기다리고 싶지 않아. 난 나 자신이 살고 싶어, 그게 아니라면 차라리 아예 살지 않는 게 낫지. 난 도저히 굶주리는 어머니를 모른 척 지나치면서, '만인의 행복'을 기다리며 내 호주머니 속 일 루블을 움켜쥐고 있을 수가 없었어. 난 만인의 행복에 내 작은 벽돌 한 장을 보태고 있는 거고, 그래서 내 마음이 편한 거야. 하하! 너희는 왜 날 놓아준 거지? 난 단 한 번 사는 거고, 나 역시 원해……. 에이, 난 미학적인 이[蝨] 한 마리에 지나지 않아.' 그는 갑자기 미친 사람처럼 웃으며 덧붙였다. '그래, 난 분명 이 한 마리야.' 그는 그 생각을 붙들고, 거기에 흡족해하며, 앙심에 찬 쾌감으로 그것을 가지고 놀면서 말을 이어 갔다. '첫째로, 내가 스스로 이라고 따져 낼 수 있기 때문이고, 둘째로, 지난 한 달 내내 자비로운 섭리를 괴롭히면서, 내가 이 일을 벌인 건 내 육신의 욕망 때문이 아니라 위대하고 고귀한 목적을 위해서라고 그것을 증인으로 불러 댔기 때문이지 — 하하! 셋째로, 가능한 한 공정하게 이 일을 해치우려고, 저울질하고 재고 따졌기 때문이야. 그 모든 이 가운데서 가장 쓸모없는 한 마리를 골라내, 그것에게서 첫걸음에 필요한 만큼만, 더도 덜도 말고 딱 그만큼만 가져가려 했지 (그러니 나머지는 그 노파의 유언대로 수도원으로 갔겠지, 하하!). 그리고 내가 완전히 이 한 마리라는 걸 보여 주는 건,' 그는 이를 갈며 덧붙였다, '내가 어쩌면 내가 죽인 그 이보다 더 비열하고 더 역겨운 존재일지도 모른다는 점이고, 또 그 노파를 죽인 뒤에 내가 스스로에게 이렇게 말하게 되리라는 걸 미리 느꼈다는 점이야. 그 끔찍함에 견줄 만한 게 어디 있겠어? 그 천박함! 그 비열함! 난 칼을 들고 말에 올라탄 '예언자'를 이해해. 알라가 명하면 '벌벌 떠는' 피조물은 복종해야 하지! 그 '예언자'는 옳아, 거리를 가로질러 포대를 세워 놓고 죄 있는 자건 죄 없는 자건 가리지 않고, 굳이 설명할 가치도 없다는 듯 날려 버릴 때, 그는 옳은 거야! 복종하는 것이 너희가 할 일이다, 벌벌 떠는 피조물들아, 욕망을 품는 것이 아니라. 그건 너희에게 허락된 게 아니니까!……. 난 결코, 결코 그 노파를 용서하지 않을 거야!'\n\n그의 머리칼은 땀에 흠뻑 젖었고, 떨리는 입술은 바싹 말라 있었으며, 두 눈은 천장에 박혀 있었다.\n\n'어머니, 누이 — 내가 그들을 얼마나 사랑했던가! 그런데 왜 지금은 그들을 증오하는 거지? 그래, 난 그들을 증오해, 그들에게 육체적인 증오를 느껴, 그들이 내 곁에 있는 걸 견딜 수가 없어……. 어머니께 다가가 입을 맞췄지, 기억나……. 어머니를 끌어안고서 생각했어, 어머니가 알기만 한다면……, 그러면 어머니께 말씀드릴까? 정말 그럴 수도 있겠지……. 어머니도 나와 같은 사람일 게 틀림없어.' 그는 마치 섬망과 씨름하듯 안간힘을 다해 생각을 짜내며 덧붙였다. '아, 지금 그 노파가 얼마나 증오스러운지! 그 노파가 되살아난다면 다시 죽여 버릴 것만 같아! 가엾은 리자베타! 그애는 왜 들어왔던 걸까?……. 한데 이상해, 왜 난 마치 그애를 죽이지도 않은 것처럼, 그애 생각은 거의 하지 않는 거지? 리자베타! 소냐! 가엾고 온순한 것들, 온순한 눈을 한……. 사랑스러운 여자들! 그들은 왜 울지 않는 거지? 왜 신음하지 않는 거지? 그들은 모든 걸 내어 주지……, 그들의 눈은 부드럽고 온순해……. 소냐, 소냐! 온순한 소냐!'\n\n그는 의식을 잃었다. 어떻게 거리로 나왔는지 기억나지 않는 것이 그에게는 이상하게 여겨졌다. 늦은 저녁이었다. 땅거미가 내려앉았고 보름달이 점점 더 환하게 빛나고 있었다. 하지만 공기 중에는 묘하게 숨 막히는 답답함이 감돌았다. 거리에는 사람들이 북적였다. 노동자들과 장사치들은 집으로 돌아가는 길이었고, 다른 사람들은 산책을 나온 참이었다. 회반죽과 먼지, 고인 물의 냄새가 풍겼다. 라스콜니코프는 침울하고 불안한 채로 걸어갔다. 그는 자신이 어떤 목적을 품고 나왔다는 것을, 서둘러 무언가를 해야 한다는 것을 또렷이 의식하고 있었지만, 그게 무엇인지는 잊어버렸다. 문득 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 어떤 사내가 거리 맞은편에 서서 자신에게 손짓하는 것을 보았다. 그는 사내에게 건너갔지만, 사내는 곧장 돌아서더니, 마치 그에게 아무런 신호도 보내지 않았다는 듯 고개를 푹 숙인 채 걸어가 버렸다. '잠깐, 저자가 정말 손짓을 한 건가?' 라스콜니코프는 의아해하면서도, 그를 따라잡으려 애썼다. 열 걸음쯤 거리로 다가갔을 때 그는 사내를 알아보고 흠칫 놀랐다. 긴 외투를 걸친, 어깨가 구부정한 바로 그 사내였다. 라스콜니코프는 거리를 두고 그를 뒤따랐다. 그의 심장이 마구 뛰었다. 그들은 어느 모퉁이를 돌아 들어갔지만, 사내는 여전히 뒤를 돌아보지 않았다. '저자는 내가 뒤따르고 있는 걸 알까?' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. 사내는 어느 커다란 건물의 대문 안으로 들어갔다. 라스콜니코프는 대문으로 서둘러 다가가, 사내가 돌아서서 자신에게 신호를 보내는지 들여다보았다. 안뜰에서 사내는 정말로 돌아서더니, 다시 그에게 손짓하는 것 같았다. 라스콜니코프는 곧장 그를 따라 안뜰로 들어갔지만, 사내는 사라지고 없었다. 분명 첫 번째 계단을 올라간 것이 틀림없었다. 라스콜니코프는 그를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다. 그는 두 층쯤 위에서 느리고 한결같은 발소리를 들었다. 그 계단은 이상하게 낯익었다. 그는 이 층 창가에 다다랐다. 달빛이 우울하고 신비로운 빛을 띠고 유리창을 통해 비쳐 들었다. 그러고는 삼 층에 다다랐다. 아니! 여기는 인부들이 일하던 그 집이잖아……. 한데 어째서 곧장 알아보지 못한 거지? 위에 있던 사내의 발소리는 잦아들어 있었다. '그렇다면 어딘가에 멈춰 섰거나 숨은 게 틀림없어.' 그는 사 층에 다다랐다. 계속 올라가야 하나? 끔찍한 정적이 감돌았다……. 하지만 그는 계속 올라갔다. 자기 자신의 발소리에 그는 흠칫 놀라 겁에 질렸다. 어찌나 어두운지! 사내는 분명 여기 어느 구석에 숨어 있을 터였다. 아! 그 집은 문이 활짝 열려 있었고, 그는 망설이다가 안으로 들어갔다. 통로는 마치 모든 게 치워진 듯 몹시 어둡고 텅 비어 있었다. 그는 발끝으로 살금살금 응접실로 기어들었는데, 그곳은 달빛으로 흠뻑 잠겨 있었다. 그곳의 모든 것이 예전 그대로였다. 의자들, 거울, 노란 소파, 그리고 액자에 끼운 그림들. 거대하고 둥근, 구릿빛으로 붉은 달이 창문으로 들여다보고 있었다. '저 달 때문에 이렇게 고요한 거야, 무슨 신비를 자아내면서.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. 그는 서서 기다렸다, 오래도록 기다렸고, 달빛이 고요해질수록 그의 심장은 더욱 격렬하게 뛰어, 마침내 고통스러울 지경이었다. 그래도 여전히 똑같은 정적이었다. 문득 그는 나뭇조각이 부러지는 듯한 날카롭고 짧은 소리를 들었고, 다시 모든 것이 고요해졌다. 파리 한 마리가 갑자기 날아올라 애처로운 윙윙 소리를 내며 유리창에 부딪쳤다. 그 순간 그는 창문과 작은 찬장 사이 구석에, 벽에 망토 같은 무언가가 걸려 있는 것을 알아챘다. '저 망토가 왜 여기 있지?' 그는 생각했다. '전에는 저기 없었는데…….' 그는 조용히 그쪽으로 다가가, 그 뒤에 누군가가 숨어 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 조심스레 망토를 젖혔고, 구석 의자에 노파가 앉아 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 있어서 그 얼굴을 볼 수 없는 것을 보았다. 하지만 그건 그 노파였다. 그는 노파 위로 우뚝 섰다. '저 여자가 겁을 먹었군.' 그는 생각했다. 그는 슬그머니 올가미에서 도끼를 빼 들고, 노파의 정수리에 한 번, 그러고는 또 한 번 내리쳤다. 그런데 이상하게도 노파는 마치 나무로 만들어진 듯 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다. 그는 겁이 나서 더 가까이 몸을 숙여 노파를 들여다보려 했다. 하지만 노파 역시 고개를 더 깊이 떨구었다. 그는 땅바닥까지 몸을 숙여 아래에서 위로 노파의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 들여다보다가 공포로 온몸이 싸늘하게 식었다. 노파는 앉은 채로 웃고 있었다, 소리 없는 웃음에 몸을 들썩이면서, 그에게 그 소리가 들리지 않게 하려고 안간힘을 쓰면서. 문득 그는 침실 문이 조금 열려 있고, 그 안에서 웃음소리와 속삭임이 새어 나오는 것만 같았다. 그는 광기에 휩싸여, 있는 힘껏 노파의 머리를 내리치기 시작했지만, 도끼를 한 번 휘두를 때마다 침실에서 새어 나오는 웃음과 속삭임은 점점 더 커졌고, 노파는 그저 웃음으로 몸을 들썩일 뿐이었다. 그는 달아나려 했지만, 통로는 사람들로 가득 차 있었고, 셋집들의 문이 활짝 열려 있었으며, 층계참에도, 계단에도, 아래쪽 어디에나 사람들이, 줄지어 늘어선 머리들이 모조리 그를 바라보면서도, 침묵 속에 숨죽인 채 무언가를 기다리며 빽빽이 몰려 있었다. 무언가가 그의 심장을 움켜쥐었고, 두 다리는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯, 도무지 움직이려 하지 않았다……. 그는 비명을 지르려 하다가 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n그는 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다 — 그런데 꿈이 이상하게도 그대로 이어지는 것 같았다. 그의 방문이 활짝 열려 있었고, 한 번도 본 적 없는 사내가 문간에 서서 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보고 있었던 것이다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 눈을 뜨자마자 곧바로 다시 감아 버렸다. 그는 등을 대고 누운 채 꼼짝도 하지 않았다.\n\n'아직도 꿈인가?' 그는 의아해하며 다시 눈꺼풀을 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼만 들어 올렸다. 그 낯선 사내는 똑같은 자리에 서서, 여전히 그를 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n사내는 조심스레 방 안으로 발을 들여놓고, 등 뒤로 문을 신중히 닫고는, 탁자로 다가가 잠시 멈춰 섰는데, 그러는 내내 라스콜니코프에게서 눈을 떼지 않았으며, 소리 없이 소파 옆 의자에 앉았다. 그는 모자를 옆 바닥에 내려놓고, 두 손을 지팡이에 얹고 그 손 위에 턱을 괴었다. 그가 한없이 기다릴 채비를 한 것이 분명했다. 라스콜니코프가 훔쳐보는 눈길로 가늠해 보건대, 그는 더는 젊지 않은, 살집이 있고, 풍성하고 옅은, 거의 희끄무레한 수염을 기른 사내였다.\n\n십 분이 흘렀다. 아직 환했지만, 어스름이 깔리기 시작하고 있었다. 방 안은 완전한 정적에 잠겨 있었다. 계단에서도 아무 소리 들리지 않았다. 오직 큰 파리 한 마리가 윙윙거리며 유리창에 부딪쳐 파닥거릴 뿐이었다. 마침내 견딜 수 없는 지경이 되었다. 라스콜니코프는 불쑥 몸을 일으켜 소파에 앉았다.\n\n「자, 원하는 게 뭔지 말해 보시오.」\n\n「당신이 자는 게 아니라 그저 자는 척하고 있다는 걸 알고 있었소.」 낯선 사내가 차분하게 웃으며 묘하게 대답했다. 「아르카디 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프올시다, 제 소개를 하지요…….」\n\n\"이것도 아직 꿈인가?\" 라스콜니코프는 또다시 생각했다.\n\n그는 뜻밖의 방문객을 주의 깊게, 의심스러운 눈초리로 바라보았다.\n\n\"스비드리가일로프! 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리! 그럴 리가 없어!\" 마침내 그가 어리둥절한 채 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n방문객은 이런 외침에 전혀 놀라는 기색이 없었다.\n\n\"두 가지 이유로 당신을 찾아왔소. 우선, 당신과 직접 알고 지내고 싶었소. 이미 당신에 관해 흥미롭고 칭찬할 만한 이야기를 많이 들었으니까. 그리고 둘째로, 당신 누이 아브도티야 로마노브나의 행복과 직접 관련된 일에서 당신이 나를 도와주기를 거절하지 않으리라는 희망을 품고 있소. 당신의 지원이 없으면 누이는 지금 나를 가까이 오게 하지 않을 것이오. 그녀는 내게 편견을 품고 있으니까. 하지만 당신의 도움이 있다면 나는…….\"\n\n\"잘못 짚었소.\" 라스콜니코프가 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"실례지만, 그분들은 어제 도착했지요?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"어제였소, 알고 있소. 나 자신도 그저께야 도착했고. 자, 이거 하나는 말해 두지요, 로디온 로마노비치. 나로서는 굳이 변명할 필요를 느끼지 않소만, 부디 말해 보시오, 편견 없이, 상식적으로 봐서, 이 모든 일에서 내 쪽에 무슨 특별히 죄가 될 만한 일이 있었소?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 여전히 말없이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내 집에서 무력한 처녀를 못살게 굴고 '파렴치한 제안으로 그녀를 모욕했다'는 거요? 그거요? (당신이 할 말을 내가 미리 하는 거요.) 하지만 나 또한 한 인간이며 인간적인 것이라면 무엇 하나 낯설지 않다고 가정해 보시오……, 한마디로, 나도 누군가에게 끌리고 사랑에 빠질 수 있다고(이건 우리 의지에 달린 일이 아니지요) 말이오. 그러면 모든 것이 더없이 자연스럽게 설명될 수 있소. 문제는 이거요, 내가 괴물인가, 아니면 나 자신이 희생자인가? 그리고 만약 내가 희생자라면? 내 열정의 대상에게 함께 미국이나 스위스로 달아나자고 제안하면서, 나는 그녀에게 더없이 깊은 존경을 품고 있었을 수도, 또 우리 둘의 행복을 도모하고 있다고 여겼을 수도 있소! 이성은 열정의 노예라고들 하지 않소. 그러니 아마 나는 누구보다도 나 자신에게 더 큰 해를 끼치고 있었던 셈이오!\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 요점이 아니오.\" 라스콜니코프가 혐오스럽다는 듯 말을 끊었다. \"당신이 옳든 그르든, 우리는 당신이 싫소. 우린 당신과 아무 상관도 갖고 싶지 않소. 우린 당신에게 나가는 문을 가리키는 거요. 나가시오!\"\n\n스비드리가일로프가 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은……, 정말 당신은 어쩔 도리가 없는 사람이군.\" 그가 더없이 솔직하게 웃으며 말했다. \"당신을 구슬려 볼 작정이었는데, 당신은 단번에 정곡을 짚어 버렸으니!\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 지금도 나를 구슬리려 들고 있잖소!\"\n\n\"그게 어떻단 말이오? 그게 뭐 어때서?\" 스비드리가일로프가 드러내 놓고 웃으며 외쳤다. \"하지만 이건 프랑스 사람들이 말하는 정정당당한 싸움이고, 더없이 순진한 형태의 속임수일 뿐이오!…… 그건 그렇고, 당신이 내 말을 끊었군. 어느 쪽이든, 다시 한 번 말하지만, 정원에서 일어난 그 일만 아니었다면 아무런 불미스러운 일도 없었을 거요. 마르파 페트로브나가…….\"\n\n\"마르파 페트로브나도 당신이 처치해 버렸다지요, 다들 그러던데?\" 라스콜니코프가 거칠게 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"오, 그것도 들으셨군? 하기야 당신이라면 분명 들었겠지……. 한데 당신 질문에 대해서는, 정말이지 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠소. 그 점에 관해서라면 내 양심은 더없이 편안하지만 말이오. 내가 그 일로 어떤 두려움에 떨고 있다고는 생각하지 마시오. 모든 게 적법하게, 순리대로 처리됐소. 의학적 조사 결과는 푸짐한 식사와 포도주 한 병을 들이켜고 곧장 목욕을 한 탓에 일어난 뇌졸중으로 진단됐고, 사실 그 외에 다른 무엇도 밝혀질 수 없었지요. 하지만 이거 하나는 말해 두지요, 요즘 들어, 특히 여기로 오는 기차 안에서 나 혼자 곰곰이 생각해 본 게 있는데, 내가 어떤 식으로든, 도덕적으로, 그 모든…… 재앙에 일조한 건 아니었을까, 짜증을 돋운다든지 그 비슷한 걸로 말이오. 하지만 나는 그것 또한 전혀 있을 수 없는 일이라는 결론에 이르렀소.\"\n\n라스콜니코프가 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"당신이 그런 일로 골머리를 썩이다니 신기하군!\"\n\n\"한데 뭐가 그리 우습소? 한번 생각해 보시오, 나는 그녀를 채찍으로 딱 두 번 때렸을 뿐이고, 자국조차 남지 않았소……. 제발 나를 냉소가로 여기지는 마시오. 그게 얼마나 끔찍한 짓이었는지, 그 모든 걸 나도 잘 알고 있으니까. 하지만 마르파 페트로브나가 나의, 말하자면, 그 열기에 오히려 기뻐했으리라는 것 또한 나는 확실히 알고 있소. 당신 누이에 관한 이야기는 마지막 한 방울까지 짜낼 대로 짜낸 상태였소. 사흘 내내 마르파 페트로브나는 집에 틀어박혀 있어야만 했지요. 시내에 나가 떠벌릴 거리가 하나도 남지 않았으니까. 게다가 그 편지로 사람들을 어찌나 진저리 나게 했던지(그녀가 편지를 낭독하고 다닌 거 들으셨지요). 그런데 난데없이 그 두 차례의 채찍질이 하늘에서 떨어진 거요! 그녀가 맨 먼저 한 일은 마차를 대령시키라고 명한 거였소……. 게다가, 여자들이 온갖 분개한 시늉을 다 하면서도 사실은 모욕당하는 것을 아주, 아주 기뻐하는 경우가 있다는 건 말할 것도 없지요. 누구에게나 그런 경우가 있소. 사실 인간이란 대체로 모욕당하는 것을 무척 좋아하는데, 당신은 그걸 눈치챈 적 있소? 하지만 특히 여자들이 그렇지. 그게 그들의 유일한 낙이라고 해도 좋을 정도요.\"\n\n한순간 라스콜니코프는 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나가 버림으로써 이 면담을 끝내 버릴까 생각했다. 하지만 어떤 호기심이, 게다가 일종의 신중함 같은 것이 그를 잠시 더 머무르게 했다.\n\n\"당신은 싸움을 좋아하는군요?\" 그는 무심한 듯 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 그렇게 좋아하진 않소.\" 스비드리가일로프가 차분하게 대답했다. \"마르파 페트로브나와 나는 거의 싸운 적이 없소. 우린 아주 화목하게 살았고, 그녀는 늘 나를 흡족해했지요. 칠 년을 함께 사는 동안 채찍을 쓴 건 딱 두 번뿐이었소(아주 애매한 성격의 세 번째 경우는 빼고). 첫 번째는 결혼한 지 두 달 뒤, 시골에 막 내려간 직후였고, 마지막이 바로 지금 우리가 얘기하는 그 일이오. 당신은 내가 그런 괴물에, 그런 반동분자에, 그런 농노 부리는 폭군이라고 생각했소? 하, 하! 그건 그렇고, 로디온 로마노비치, 기억하시오? 몇 해 전, 그 자비로운 공개 비판의 시절에, 어느 귀족이 — 이름은 잊었소만 — 기차 안에서 독일 여자를 채찍질했다는 이유로 모든 신문에서, 사방에서 망신을 당한 일 말이오. 기억하시오? 바로 그 시절, 아마 그해였을 거요, '시대'지의 그 '치욕적인 사건'이 일어난 게(아시잖소, '이집트의 밤', 그 공개 낭독회 말이오, 기억하시오? 그 검은 눈동자! 아, 우리 젊은 날의 황금기여, 그 시절은 다 어디로 갔는지?). 자, 그 독일 여자를 매질한 신사로 말하자면, 나는 그에게 아무런 동정도 느끼지 않소. 결국 동정할 게 뭐 있겠소? 하지만 이건 말해 둬야겠소, 때때로 어찌나 약을 올리는 '독일 여자'들이 있는지, 자기 자신을 온전히 책임질 수 있는 진보주의자가 과연 있을지 의심스러울 정도요. 그 당시엔 아무도 그 일을 그런 관점에서 보지 않았지만, 그게야말로 진정으로 인도적인 관점이오, 정말이지.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 스비드리가일로프는 또다시 갑작스레 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 라스콜니코프는 이 사내가 마음속에 굳은 목적을 품고 있으면서도 그것을 혼자 간직할 줄 아는 인간임을 또렷이 알아차렸다.\n\n\"당신은 며칠째 아무하고도 이야기를 안 한 것 같군요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"거의 아무하고도. 내가 이렇게 잘 적응하는 사람이라는 게 신기하신가 보군?\"\n\n\"아니, 그저 당신이 지나치게 적응을 잘하는 사람이라는 게 신기할 따름이오.\"\n\n\"내가 당신의 무례한 질문들에 화를 내지 않아서 그렇소? 그거요? 한데 화낼 게 뭐 있소? 당신이 물으니 내가 대답했을 뿐인걸.\" 그가 놀라우리만치 순박한 표정으로 대답했다. \"있잖소, 내가 흥미를 느끼는 일이라곤 거의 아무것도 없소.\" 그는 마치 꿈을 꾸듯 말을 이었다. \"특히 지금은, 할 일이 아무것도 없으니……. 물론 당신은 내가 어떤 속셈을 갖고 당신에게 아첨하는 거라고 상상할 자유가 있소, 더구나 당신 누이를 어떤 일로 만나고 싶다고 말했으니. 하지만 솔직히 고백하자면, 나는 몹시 따분하오. 특히 지난 사흘이 그랬소, 그래서 당신을 만나니 더없이 반갑소……. 화내지 마시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 한데 당신이야말로 어쩐지 끔찍하게 이상해 보이오. 뭐라 하든, 당신에겐 어딘가 잘못된 데가 있소, 지금도……, 바로 이 순간을 말하는 게 아니라, 그냥 요즘 들어 전반적으로 말이오……. 자, 자, 그만하겠소, 그만, 인상 쓰지 마시오! 나는 당신이 생각하는 만큼 그렇게 곰 같은 인간이 아니오.\"\n\n라스콜니코프가 침울하게 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은 어쩌면 전혀 곰 같은 인간이 아닌지도 모르오.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 오히려 당신은 매우 교양 있는 사람이거나, 적어도 필요할 때 그렇게 처신할 줄 아는 사람 같소.\"\n\n\"나는 누구의 의견에도 별 관심이 없소.\" 스비드리가일로프가 무뚝뚝하게, 심지어 약간 거만한 기색까지 띠며 대답했다. \"그러니 우리 기후에서는 천박함이 그토록 편리한 외투가 되어 주는데, 어찌 때때로 천박해지지 않을 수 있겠소……. 게다가 본디 그쪽으로 타고난 기질이 있다면 더더욱.\" 그가 또다시 웃으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"하지만 듣자 하니 당신은 여기 친구가 많다더군요. 사람들 말마따나 '연줄이 없지 않은' 사람이라죠. 그렇다면 특별한 목적이 없는 한, 도대체 나한테서 무얼 바라는 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 여기 친구들이 있다는 건 사실이오.\" 스비드리가일로프가 핵심에는 답하지 않은 채 인정했다. \"벌써 몇 사람 만나기도 했고. 사흘 동안 어슬렁거리며 돌아다녔으니, 그들을 보았거나 아니면 그들이 나를 보았지요. 그건 당연한 일이오. 나는 옷차림도 번듯하고 가난한 사람으로 여겨지지도 않으니까. 농노 해방도 내겐 영향이 없었소. 내 재산은 주로 삼림과 물대는 초원으로 이루어져 있어 수입이 줄지 않았으니. 하지만…… 나는 그들을 만나러 가지 않을 거요, 이미 오래전에 진절머리가 났소. 여기 온 지 사흘인데 아무도 찾아가지 않았소……. 이게 무슨 도시란 말이오! 어떻게 우리한테 이런 게 생겨났는지, 말 좀 해 보시오? 온갖 종류의 관리와 학생들의 도시지. 그렇소, 팔 년 전 내가 여기서 빈둥거리며 발길질이나 해 댈 때는 미처 눈치채지 못한 게 한둘이 아니오……. 이제 내 유일한 희망은 해부학에 있소, 정말이지, 그렇소!\"\n\n\"해부학이라고요?\"\n\n\"한데 그 클럽이니, 뒤소 식당이니, 열병식이니, 진보니 하는 것들은, 그래, 어쩌면, 뭐 — 그런 건 다 나 없이도 굴러갈 수 있소.\" 그는 또다시 질문을 못 들은 척 말을 이었다. \"게다가, 누가 사기 도박꾼 노릇을 하고 싶겠소?\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 당신은 사기 도박꾼이었소?\"\n\n\"어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠소? 팔 년 전엔 우리끼리 제대로 된 패거리가 있었소, 최상류 사회의 인사들이었지. 우린 멋진 시절을 보냈소. 게다가 다들 교양 있는 사람들이었지요, 알겠소, 시인이며 재산가들 말이오. 사실 우리 러시아 사회에서는 대체로 매를 맞아 본 자들에게서 가장 훌륭한 예법을 찾아볼 수 있는데, 당신은 그걸 눈치챈 적 있소? 나는 시골에서 망가져 버렸소. 한데 네진에서 온 어느 비열한 그리스인 때문에 빚을 지고 감옥에 갇히는 신세가 됐지. 그때 마르파 페트로브나가 나타나서는, 그자와 흥정을 벌여 은화 삼만 냥에 나를 빼내 줬소(난 칠만 냥을 빚지고 있었지). 우린 합법적인 혼인으로 맺어졌고, 그녀는 나를 무슨 보물이라도 되는 양 시골로 데려갔소. 알다시피 그녀는 나보다 다섯 살 위였소. 나를 무척 사랑했지. 칠 년 동안 나는 시골을 한 번도 떠나지 않았소. 그리고, 눈여겨보시오, 그녀는 평생토록 삼만 루블짜리 차용증서라는 문서를 내 머리 위에 쥐고 있었소, 그러니 내가 무슨 일로든 반항하기로 마음먹는다면 당장 덫에 걸리는 셈이었지! 게다가 그녀라면 정말 그렇게 했을 거요! 여자들은 그런 데서 아무런 모순도 느끼지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"그게 아니었다면, 당신은 그녀를 버리고 달아났겠지요?\"\n\n\"뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠소. 나를 붙잡아 둔 게 그 문서였던 것 같지는 않소. 나는 딱히 다른 어디로 가고 싶지도 않았소. 내가 따분해하는 걸 보고 마르파 페트로브나가 직접 외국에 다녀오라고 권하기도 했지만, 나는 전에도 외국에 가 봤고 늘 거기서는 속이 메스꺼웠소. 별 이유도 없이, 그저 일출이며, 나폴리 만이며, 바다며 — 그런 걸 바라보고 있으면 서글퍼지더란 말이오. 가장 역겨운 건, 정말로 서글퍼진다는 거지! 아니오, 고향이 더 낫소. 적어도 여기선 만사를 남 탓으로 돌리고 자기 자신은 변명할 수 있으니까. 어쩌면 나는 북극 탐험이라도 떠났을지 모르오, 왜냐하면 나는 술버릇이 사나운 데다 술 마시는 걸 싫어하는데, 그런데도 남은 거라곤 술밖에 없으니. 시도는 해 봤소. 한데, 이거 말이오, 듣자 하니 다음 일요일에 베르크가 유수포프 정원에서 거대한 기구를 타고 올라간다는데, 요금을 받고 손님도 태운다더군. 사실이오?\"\n\"아니, 당신이 타고 올라가기라도 하려고요?\"\n\n\"내가……, 아니, 오, 아니오.\" 스비드리가일로프가 정말로 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯한 표정으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n'이게 무슨 소린가? 진심인가?' 라스콜니코프는 의아해했다.\n\n\"아니, 나를 붙잡아 둔 건 그 문서가 아니었소.\" 스비드리가일로프가 생각에 잠긴 채 말을 이었다. \"시골을 떠나지 않은 건 내 뜻이었소, 그리고 거의 일 년 전에 마르파 페트로브나는 내 명명일에 그 문서를 도로 돌려주었고, 게다가 상당한 액수의 돈까지 선물해 주었소. 알다시피 그녀는 재산이 있었으니까. '아르카디 이바노비치, 내가 당신을 이만큼이나 믿는다는 걸 알겠지요' — 실제로 그녀가 한 말이오. 그녀가 그런 말을 썼다는 게 믿기지 않소? 한데 말이오, 나는 영지를 꽤 점잖게 관리했소, 인근에서도 다들 나를 인정하지요. 책도 주문했고. 마르파 페트로브나는 처음엔 찬성했지만, 나중엔 내가 공부를 지나치게 할까 봐 두려워했소.\"\n\n\"당신은 마르파 페트로브나를 무척 그리워하는 것 같군요?\"\n\n\"그녀를 그리워한다고? 어쩌면. 정말로, 어쩌면 그런지도 모르오. 그건 그렇고, 당신은 유령을 믿소?\"\n\n\"무슨 유령 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니, 그냥 흔한 유령 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 유령을 믿소?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 안 믿는지도, 당신 마음에 들자고 하는 말이오만……. 딱 잘라 안 믿는다고는 못 하겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 유령을 본다는 거요?\"\n\n스비드리가일로프가 어쩐지 묘한 눈초리로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"마르파 페트로브나가 황송하게도 나를 찾아오신다오.\" 그가 입을 비틀어 이상한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"'황송하게도 당신을 찾아온다'니, 그게 무슨 말이오?\"\n\n\"세 번 찾아왔소. 처음 본 건 장례식 바로 그날, 그녀를 묻은 지 한 시간 뒤였소. 내가 여기로 떠나기 전날이었지. 두 번째는 그저께, 동틀 무렵, 오는 길에 말라야 비셰라 역에서였고, 세 번째는 두 시간 전, 내가 묵고 있는 방에서였소. 나는 혼자였소.\"\n\n\"당신은 깨어 있었소?\"\n\n\"완전히 깨어 있었소. 매번 정신이 또렷했소. 그녀가 와서, 잠시 내게 말을 걸고는, 문으로 나가지요 — 늘 문으로. 그녀의 기척이 거의 들릴 정도요.\"\n\n\"왜 나는 당신에게 그런 비슷한 일이 일어나고 있는 게 틀림없다고 생각했을까?\" 라스콜니코프가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n그러고는 그 순간 자신이 그런 말을 했다는 데 스스로 놀랐다. 그는 몹시 흥분해 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 당신이 그렇게 생각했다고?\" 스비드리가일로프가 놀라서 물었다. \"정말로? 우리 둘 사이에 뭔가 공통점이 있다고 내가 말하지 않았소, 응?\"\n\n\"당신은 그런 말 한 적 없소!\" 라스콜니코프가 날카롭게, 발끈하며 외쳤다.\n\"안 했던가?\"\n\n\"안 했소!\"\n\n\"한 줄 알았는데. 내가 들어와서 당신이 눈을 감고 자는 척하며 누워 있는 걸 보았을 때, 나는 곧장 속으로 말했소, '바로 이 사람이로군' 하고.\"\n\n\"'이 사람'이라니 그게 무슨 뜻이오? 도대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거요?\" 라스콜니코프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이냐고? 나도 정말 모르겠소…….\" 스비드리가일로프가, 마치 그 자신도 어리둥절하다는 듯 순박하게 중얼거렸다.\n\n잠시 두 사람은 말이 없었다. 그들은 서로의 얼굴을 빤히 응시했다.\n\n\"다 헛소리야!\" 라스콜니코프가 짜증스럽게 소리쳤다. \"그녀가 당신을 찾아오면 무슨 말을 하오?\"\n\n\"그녀가! 믿어지겠소, 더없이 시시한 자질구레한 얘기를 한단 말이오, 그런데 — 인간이란 묘한 존재라 — 그게 나를 화나게 하오. 처음 들어왔을 때(나는 지쳐 있었소, 알겠소, 장례 예배, 장례 의식, 그러고 나서 점심까지. 마침내 나는 서재에 혼자 남게 됐지. 시가에 불을 붙이고 생각에 잠기기 시작했소), 그녀가 문으로 들어왔소. '아르카디 이바노비치, 당신 오늘 너무 바빠서 식당 시계 태엽 감는 걸 잊었어요' 하고 말이오. 칠 년 내내 나는 매주 그 시계의 태엽을 감았고, 내가 잊을라치면 그녀가 늘 일깨워 주곤 했소. 이튿날 나는 여기로 길을 떠났소. 동틀 무렵 역에 내렸지. 지쳐서 눈을 반쯤 뜬 채 졸고 있다가 커피를 마시고 있었소. 문득 고개를 들어 보니, 난데없이 마르파 페트로브나가 카드 한 벌을 손에 들고 내 곁에 앉아 있는 거요. '여행길 운수를 점쳐 드릴까요, 아르카디 이바노비치?' 그녀는 점치는 데 아주 능했소. 그때 그녀에게 점쳐 달라고 청하지 않은 걸 나는 영영 자신을 용서하지 못할 거요. 나는 겁에 질려 달아났소, 게다가 마침 종이 울리기도 했고. 오늘 나는 싸구려 음식점에서 시원찮은 점심을 먹고 몹시 더부룩한 기분으로 앉아 있었소. 담배를 피우며 앉아 있는데, 난데없이 또 마르파 페트로브나가. 그녀는 긴 자락이 달린 새 초록색 비단 드레스를 차려입고 무척 맵시 있게 들어왔소. '안녕하세요, 아르카디 이바노비치! 내 드레스 어때요? 아니스카는 이런 건 못 짓지요.'(아니스카는 시골의 침모로, 본디 우리 농노 처녀였는데 모스크바에서 솜씨를 익혔소, 예쁜 계집애였지.) 그녀는 내 앞에서 빙글빙글 돌아 보였소. 나는 드레스를 보고, 그러고 나서 그녀의 얼굴을 유심히, 아주 유심히 들여다봤소. '마르파 페트로브나, 당신이 이런 자질구레한 일로 나를 찾아오느라 수고하다니 신기하구려.' '어머나, 당신은 무슨 일로든 사람이 당신을 귀찮게 하는 걸 못 견디는군요!' 그녀를 놀려 줄 셈으로 내가 말했소. '나는 결혼하고 싶소, 마르파 페트로브나.' '딱 당신답군요, 아르카디 이바노비치, 아내를 묻기가 무섭게 신붓감을 찾아 나서다니 그게 무슨 꼴이람. 게다가 적어도 좋은 선택이라도 하면 모를까, 나는 알아요, 그게 당신 행복도, 그 여자 행복도 되지 못하고, 그저 모든 선량한 사람들의 웃음거리만 될 거라는 걸요.' 그러고는 나가 버렸는데, 자락이 사락거리는 소리가 들리는 것 같았소. 이거 헛소리 아니오, 응?\"\n\n\"하지만 혹시 거짓말하고 있는 건 아니오?\" 라스콜니코프가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"나는 거짓말을 좀처럼 안 하오.\" 스비드리가일로프가, 그 질문의 무례함은 알아채지 못한 듯, 생각에 잠겨 대답했다.\n\n\"그러면 그 전에도, 예전에 유령을 본 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"그, 그렇소, 본 적이 있긴 하오, 한데 평생 딱 한 번, 육 년 전이었소. 내겐 필카라는 농노가 있었지. 그를 묻은 직후에, 나는 깜빡 잊고 '필카, 내 파이프!' 하고 불렀소. 그가 들어와서 내 파이프들이 있는 찬장 쪽으로 가더군. 나는 가만히 앉아서 생각했소, '복수하느라 저러는구나' 하고, 왜냐하면 그가 죽기 직전에 우린 격렬하게 다퉜으니까. '어디 감히 팔꿈치에 구멍 난 꼴로 들어오느냐?' 하고 내가 말했소. '꺼져, 이 망나니야!' 그가 돌아서 나가더니, 다시는 오지 않았소. 그때 나는 마르파 페트로브나에게 그 얘기를 하지 않았소. 그를 위해 추도 예배를 올려 주고 싶었지만, 부끄러웠소.\"\n\n\"의사한테 가 봐야겠소.\"\n\n\"당신이 말해 주지 않아도 내가 성치 않다는 건 알고 있소, 어디가 잘못된 건진 모르겠지만 말이오. 나는 당신보다 다섯 배는 더 튼튼할 거요. 나는 당신에게 유령이 보이는 걸 믿느냐고 물은 게 아니라, 유령이 존재한다는 걸 믿느냐고 물은 거요.\"\n\n\"아니, 난 그런 거 안 믿소!\" 라스콜니코프가 영락없는 분노에 차 외쳤다.\n\n\"사람들이 보통 뭐라고 하오?\" 스비드리가일로프가, 마치 혼잣말하듯, 곁눈질을 하며 고개를 숙인 채 중얼거렸다. \"이렇게들 말하지요, '당신은 병들었다, 그러니 당신에게 보이는 건 실재하지 않는 환영일 뿐이다'라고. 하지만 그건 엄밀히 논리적이지 않소. 유령이 오로지 병든 자에게만 나타난다는 데는 나도 동의하오, 하지만 그건 단지 유령이 병든 자에게만 나타날 수 있다는 걸 입증할 뿐, 유령이 존재하지 않는다는 걸 입증하진 못하오.\"\n\n\"천만의 말씀.\" 라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 우겼다.\n\n\"아니라고? 그렇게 생각 안 하오?\" 스비드리가일로프가 그를 찬찬히 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"한데 이런 논리에는 뭐라 하겠소(나 좀 거들어 주시오), 유령이란 말하자면 다른 세계들의 자투리요 조각이며, 그 세계들의 시작이라고 한다면. 건강한 사람은 물론 그것들을 볼 이유가 없소, 왜냐하면 그는 무엇보다도 이 지상의 인간이며, 완전함과 질서를 위하여 오직 이 삶 속에서만 살아가도록 묶여 있으니까. 하지만 병이 들기가 무섭게, 유기체의 정상적인 지상의 질서가 깨지기가 무섭게, 사람은 다른 세계의 가능성을 깨닫기 시작하오. 그리고 더 심하게 앓을수록 그 다른 세계와의 접촉은 더 긴밀해지지요, 그래서 사람이 죽기가 무섭게 곧장 그 세계로 발을 들이게 되는 거요. 나는 오래전부터 이 생각을 했소. 당신이 내세를 믿는다면, 이것도 믿을 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"나는 내세를 안 믿소.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 생각에 잠긴 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 만약 거기에 거미들이나 그 비슷한 것만 있다면 어떻겠소?\" 그가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n'미친 인간이로군.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다.\n\n\"우린 늘 영원을 우리 머리로는 헤아릴 수 없는 무언가로, 거대한, 거대한 무언가로 상상하지! 하지만 어째서 꼭 거대해야 하오? 그 모든 것 대신, 만약 그게 작은 방 하나라면, 시골의 목욕탕 같은, 검댕과 때투성이에 구석마다 거미가 우글거리는, 그런 거라면, 그리고 영원이라는 게 고작 그뿐이라면 어떻겠소? 나는 가끔 그것을 그렇게 그려 보곤 하오.\"\n\n\"당신은 정녕 그보다 더 옳고 더 위안이 되는 무언가는 상상조차 못 한단 말이오?\" 라스콜니코프가 고뇌에 찬 심정으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"더 옳다고? 한데 어떻게 알겠소, 어쩌면 그게 옳은 것일지도, 게다가 말이오, 내가 만들었더라도 분명 그렇게 만들었을 거요.\" 스비드리가일로프가 어렴풋한 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n이 끔찍한 대답에 라스콜니코프는 등골이 서늘해졌다. 스비드리가일로프는 고개를 들어 그를 바라보더니, 갑자기 웃기 시작했다.\n\n\"생각해 보시오.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"반 시간 전만 해도 우린 서로 본 적도 없고, 서로를 적으로 여겼소. 우리 사이엔 매듭짓지 못한 일이 있소. 그런데 우린 그걸 옆으로 밀쳐 두고는, 추상적인 얘기로 빠져 버렸지! 우리가 한통속이라고 한 내 말이 옳지 않았소?\"\n\n\"부디 허락해 주시오.\" 라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 말을 이었다. \"당신이 무슨 영광스러운 일로 나를 찾아왔는지 설명해 달라고 청하는 걸 말이오……, 그리고……, 그리고 나는 급하오, 허비할 시간이 없소. 나가 봐야 하오.\"\n\n\"여부가 있겠소, 여부가. 당신 누이 아브도티야 로마노브나가 표트르 페트로비치 루진 씨와 결혼한다지요?\"\n\n\"내 누이에 관한 질문은 일절 삼가고, 그 이름도 입에 올리지 말아 주겠소? 당신이 정말 스비드리가일로프라면, 어떻게 감히 내 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 담을 수 있는지 도무지 이해가 안 되오.\"\n\n\"아니, 하지만 나는 바로 그녀에 관해 얘기하러 여기 온 거요. 그러니 어찌 그녀 이름을 입에 올리지 않을 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"좋소, 말해 보시오, 단 서두르시오.\"\n\n\"당신이 이 루진 씨를 단 반 시간이라도 만나 봤거나 그에 관해 무슨 사실이라도 들었다면, 그에 대해 나름의 의견을 세웠으리라고 나는 확신하오. 그는 내 아내를 통해 나와 인척이 되는 사람인데, 아브도티야 로마노브나에겐 어울리는 짝이 못 되오. 내 생각엔 아브도티야 로마노브나가……, 가족을 위하여, 가족을 위하여 자신을 너그럽게, 그러나 분별없이 희생하려는 것 같소. 당신에 관해 들은 모든 걸 종합해 볼 때, 나는 세속적인 이득을 희생하지 않고도 이 혼사를 깨뜨릴 수 있다면 당신이 무척 기뻐하리라고 짐작했소. 이제 당신을 직접 알게 되니, 그 확신이 더 굳어졌소.\"\n\n\"이 모든 게 무척 순진하군요……, 실례지만, 뻔뻔하다고 말했어야겠소, 당신 쪽에서 말이오.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 내 잇속을 챙기려 한다는 말이군. 마음 놓으시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 내가 내 이득을 위해 일하는 거라면, 이토록 노골적으로 털어놓진 않았을 거요. 나는 그렇게까지 바보는 아니오. 그 점에 관해 심리적으로 흥미로운 걸 하나 고백하겠소. 방금 아브도티야 로마노브나를 향한 내 사랑을 변호하면서, 나는 나 자신이 희생자라고 말했소. 한데 말해 두지요, 지금 나는 사랑의 감정이라곤 없소, 손톱만큼도, 그래서 나 자신도 정말 의아할 정도요, 왜냐하면 정말로 뭔가를 느끼긴 했으니까…….\"\n\n\"한가함과 방탕 탓이겠지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 한가하고 방탕한 인간이긴 하오, 하지만 당신 누이는 나 같은 인간조차 감명받지 않을 수 없을 만큼 훌륭한 자질을 지녔소. 하지만 그것도 다 헛소리지, 이제 나 자신이 알겠소.\"\n\n\"그걸 안 지 오래됐소?\"\n\n\"전부터 어렴풋이 깨닫고 있었지만, 완전히 확신한 건 그저께, 거의 페테르부르크에 도착하는 바로 그 순간이었소. 그래도 모스크바에 있을 땐 아직, 아브도티야 로마노브나의 손을 얻으려, 루진 씨를 밀어내려 가는 거라고 여겼소만.\"\n\n\"말을 끊어 미안하오. 부디 짧게 줄여서, 방문의 목적으로 들어가 주시오. 나는 급하오, 나가 봐야 하니까…….\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요. 여기 도착해서 어떤……, 여행을 결심하고 보니, 나는 필요한 사전 정리를 좀 해 두고 싶소. 아이들은 이모에게 맡겨 두었소, 부족함 없이 지내고 있고, 나라는 사람이 직접 필요하지도 않소. 사실 나라고 한들 좋은 아버지가 될 리도 없지! 나는 일 년 전 마르파 페트로브나가 준 것 말고는 아무것도 가져오지 않았소. 그걸로 충분하오. 실례, 이제 곧 본론으로 들어가리다. 어쩌면 떠나게 될 그 여행에 앞서, 나는 루진 씨 문제도 매듭짓고 싶소. 그자가 그토록 밉살스러워서가 아니라, 마르파 페트로브나가 이 혼사를 꾸몄다는 걸 알게 됐을 때 그자 때문에 그녀와 다퉜기 때문이오. 이제 나는 당신의 중개를 통해, 그리고 당신이 원한다면 당신이 보는 앞에서 아브도티야 로마노브나를 만나, 우선 그녀에게 루진 씨에게서는 해악 말고는 결코 아무것도 얻지 못하리라는 걸 설명해 주고 싶소. 그러고 나서, 지난날의 온갖 불미스러운 일에 대해 그녀에게 용서를 구하고, 만 루블을 선물로 드려, 루진 씨와의 파혼을 도와주고 싶소, 그 파혼으로 말하자면, 그녀가 그 길을 찾을 수만 있다면 그녀 자신도 마다하지 않으리라고 나는 믿소.\"\n\n\"당신 정말 미쳤군.\" 라스콜니코프가, 화가 났다기보다 어이없다는 듯 외쳤다. \"어떻게 감히 그런 말을 하는 거요!\"\n\n\"당신이 내게 소리칠 줄 알았소. 하지만 우선, 내가 부자는 아니어도 이 만 루블은 완전히 여유 자금이라 내겐 전혀 필요가 없소. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 받지 않는다면, 나는 그걸 더 어리석은 데다 탕진해 버릴 거요. 그게 첫째요. 둘째로, 내 양심은 더없이 편안하오, 나는 아무런 흑심 없이 이 제안을 하는 거요. 당신은 안 믿을지 몰라도, 결국엔 아브도티야 로마노브나도 당신도 알게 될 거요. 요점은, 내가 깊이 존경하는 당신 누이에게 내가 실제로 얼마간의 곤란과 불미스러운 일을 끼쳤다는 것, 그래서 진심으로 그걸 뉘우치는 마음에서, 나는 — 보상하려는 것도, 그 불미스러움을 갚으려는 것도 아니라, 그저 그녀에게 이로운 무언가를 하고 싶을 뿐이오, 내가 결국 해악만 끼칠 특권을 지닌 인간은 아니라는 걸 보여 주고 싶은 거지. 내 제안에 백만분의 일이라도 사심이 있다면, 이토록 드러내 놓고 제안하진 않았을 거요. 그리고 고작 만 루블만 제안하지도 않았을 거요, 다섯 주 전엔 그녀에게 더 많은 걸 제안했으니까. 게다가 나는 어쩌면 머잖아 어느 젊은 처녀와 결혼할지도 모르고, 그것 하나만으로도 내가 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 무슨 흑심을 품었다는 의심은 막아 줄 거요. 끝으로 한마디 하자면, 그녀가 루진 씨와 결혼한다 해도 마찬가지로 돈을 받는 셈이오, 다만 다른 남자에게서 받는 것일 뿐. 화내지 마시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 냉정하고 차분하게 곰곰이 생각해 보시오.\"\n\n이 말을 하는 스비드리가일로프 자신은 더없이 냉정하고 차분했다.\n\n\"더는 말하지 말아 주시오.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다. \"어쨌든 이건 용서할 수 없는 무례요.\"\n\n\"천만에. 그렇다면 인간은 이 세상에서 이웃에게 해악만 끼칠 수 있을 뿐, 시시한 관습적 형식 따위에 가로막혀 아주 손톱만큼의 선조차 베풀지 못한단 말이오. 터무니없는 일이오. 가령 내가 죽으면서 유언으로 그 돈을 당신 누이에게 남긴다면, 설마 그녀가 그걸 거절하겠소?\"\n\n\"아마 거절할 거요.\"\n\n\"오, 천만에, 정말로. 하지만, 당신이 거절한다면야 할 수 없지, 그래도 만 루블이란 때에 따라 꽤 요긴한 물건인데. 어쨌든 부탁이니, 내가 한 말을 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 전해 주시오.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 그러겠소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 어쩔 수 없이 직접 그녀를 만나려고 애써야 할 테고, 그러느라 그녀를 성가시게 하겠지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 내가 그녀에게 전하면, 당신은 그녀를 만나려 들지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"정말이지 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠소. 그녀를 꼭 한 번 더 보고 싶긴 한데.\"\n\n\"기대하지 마시오.\"\n\n\"유감이오. 하지만 당신은 나를 모르오. 어쩌면 우리가 더 좋은 사이가 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"우리가 친구가 될 수 있다고 생각하오?\"\n\n\"안 될 거 뭐 있소?\" 스비드리가일로프가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그는 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다. \"딱히 당신을 방해할 작정은 아니었소, 그럴 셈도 없이 여기 온 거요……, 그래도 오늘 아침엔 당신 얼굴에 무척 충격을 받았소만.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 어디서 나를 봤소?\" 라스콜니코프가 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"우연히 봤소……. 자꾸만 당신에게 나와 닮은 무언가가 있다는 생각이 들더군……. 하지만 마음 놓으시오. 나는 주제넘게 굴지 않소. 사기 도박꾼들과도 곧잘 어울려 지냈고, 내 먼 친척뻘 되는 대단한 양반 스비르베이 공작을 한 번도 따분하게 한 적 없고, 프릴루코프 부인의 앨범에 라파엘로의 '마돈나'에 관한 글을 써 줄 줄도 알고, 칠 년 동안 마르파 페트로브나 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않았고, 옛날엔 건초 시장의 비아젬스키 여관에서 밤을 지새우기도 했고, 또 어쩌면 베르크와 함께 기구를 타고 올라갈지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"아, 그러시구려. 한데, 실례지만 그 여행은 곧 떠나는 거요?\"\n\n\"무슨 여행 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니, 그 '여행' 말이오, 당신이 직접 얘기했잖소.\"\n\n\"여행이라? 오, 그래. 여행 얘길 하긴 했지. 글쎄, 그건 폭넓은 주제라서……, 당신이 뭘 묻고 있는지 알기만 한다면 말이오.\" 그가 덧붙이며, 별안간 큼직하고 짤막한 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"어쩌면 여행 대신 결혼할지도 모르오. 다들 내 혼사를 주선하고 있으니.\"\n\n\"여기서요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\"언제 그럴 틈이 있었소?\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 아브도티야 로마노브나를 꼭 한 번 보고 싶소. 간절히 부탁하오. 자, 그럼 이만 작별하지요. 오, 그래. 깜빡한 게 있군. 당신 누이에게 전해 주시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 마르파 페트로브나가 유언에서 그녀를 기억하고 삼천 루블을 남겼다고 말이오. 이건 틀림없는 사실이오. 마르파 페트로브나가 죽기 일주일 전에 그렇게 정해 두었고, 내가 보는 앞에서 처리됐소. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 두세 주 안에 그 돈을 받을 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"진실을 말하는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그녀에게 전하시오. 자, 이만 실례하리다. 나는 당신네 아주 가까이에 묵고 있소.\"\n\n밖으로 나가던 스비드리가일로프는 문간에서 라주미힌과 맞부딪쳤다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n여덟 시가 다 되어 가고 있었다. 두 청년은 루진보다 먼저 도착하려고 바칼레예프의 하숙으로 서둘러 갔다.\n\n\"그런데, 그 사람은 누구였나?\" 거리로 나서자마자 라주미힌이 물었다.\n\n\"스비드리가일로프였어. 누이가 가정교사로 있던 그 집 지주 말이야, 거기서 누이가 모욕을 당했지. 그자가 끈질기게 추근거리는 바람에, 그 아내 마르파 페트로브나가 누이를 내쫓아 버렸어. 그런데 이 마르파 페트로브나가 나중에 두냐에게 용서를 빌었고, 이번에 갑자기 죽어 버렸지. 오늘 아침에 우리가 얘기했던 게 바로 그 여자였어. 왜 그런지 나는 그 사람이 두려워. 아내 장례를 치르자마자 곧장 이리로 왔어. 무척 이상한 사람이고, 무언가 작정한 게 있어……. 두냐를 그자한테서 지켜 줘야 해…… 바로 이걸 너한테 말하고 싶었어, 알겠나?\"\n\n\"지켜 준다고! 그자가 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 무슨 해코지를 할 수 있단 말인가? 고맙네, 로쟈, 나한테 그렇게 말해 줘서……. 우리가 지킬 거야, 지켜 주고말고. 그자는 어디 사나?\"\n\n\"몰라.\"\n\n\"왜 묻지 않았나? 저런, 아쉽군! 하지만 내가 알아내고 말겠어.\"\n\n\"그 사람을 봤나?\" 잠시 사이를 두고 라스콜니코프가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 봤어, 똑똑히 봐 두었지.\"\n\n\"정말로 봤단 말이지? 분명히 봤어?\" 라스콜니코프가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"그래, 완벽하게 기억해, 천 명 가운데서도 알아볼 거야. 나는 얼굴 기억력이 좋거든.\"\n\n그들은 다시 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"흠!…… 그렇다면 됐어.\" 라스콜니코프가 중얼거렸다. \"그런데 말이야, 나는 자꾸…… 어쩌면 그게 환각이었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이야? 무슨 소린지 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"그러니까, 너희들 모두 말하잖아.\" 라스콜니코프는 입가를 비틀어 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"내가 미쳤다고. 방금 나는 어쩌면 정말로 내가 미쳤고, 그저 헛것을 본 게 아닐까 생각했어.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이야?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 누가 알겠나? 어쩌면 나는 정말 미쳤는지도 모르고, 어쩌면 요 며칠 동안 일어난 모든 일이 그저 망상일 뿐인지도.\"\n\n\"아, 로쟈, 너 또 흥분했구나!…… 그런데 그 사람이 뭐라고 했어, 뭣 때문에 왔다던?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 대답하지 않았다. 라주미힌은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"자, 이제 내 얘기를 들려줄게.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"너한테 갔더니, 넌 자고 있더군. 그다음 우리는 식사를 했고, 그러고 나서 나는 포르피리한테 갔어, 자메토프도 아직 거기 있더라고. 말을 꺼내 보려 했지만 소용없었어. 제대로 말이 안 나오더라. 그들은 알아듣지도 못하고, 알아들을 수도 없는 것 같은데, 그러면서도 조금도 부끄러워하지 않아. 포르피리를 창가로 끌고 가서 얘기를 시작했지만, 그래도 소용없었어. 그자는 딴 데를 보고, 나도 딴 데를 봤지. 결국 나는 그자의 못생긴 얼굴에 대고 주먹을 흔들면서, 사촌이니까 대가리를 박살 내 버리겠다고 했어. 그자는 그저 나를 쳐다보기만 하더군. 나는 욕을 퍼붓고 나와 버렸지. 그게 다야. 정말 멍청한 짓이었어. 자메토프한테는 한마디도 안 했고. 그런데 말이야, 나는 일을 망쳐 버렸다고 생각했는데, 계단을 내려오다가 기막힌 생각이 떠올랐어. 우리가 왜 골치를 썩여야 하지? 물론 네가 무슨 위험에 처했거나 그런 거라면 모를까, 그게 아니라면 네가 신경 쓸 게 뭐 있어? 그자들 따위에 눈곱만큼도 신경 쓸 거 없어. 나중에 우리가 그자들을 비웃어 주면 돼, 내가 네 입장이라면 그자들을 더더욱 혼란에 빠뜨릴 거야. 나중에 그자들이 얼마나 부끄러워할까! 빌어먹을 놈들! 나중에 흠씬 두들겨 줄 수도 있겠지만, 지금은 그냥 비웃어 주자고!\"\n\n\"그렇긴 해.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다. '그런데 너는 내일 뭐라고 할까?' 그는 속으로 생각했다. 이상한 일이지만, 그 순간까지 그는 라주미힌이 사실을 알게 되면 어떻게 생각할까 궁금해한 적이 한 번도 없었다. 이런 생각을 하며 라스콜니코프는 그를 바라보았다. 포르피리를 찾아갔다는 라주미힌의 이야기는 그에게 거의 흥미를 주지 못했으니, 그 사이에 너무도 많은 일이 오고 갔던 것이다.\n\n복도에서 그들은 루진과 마주쳤다. 그는 정확히 여덟 시에 도착해서 호수를 찾고 있던 참이라, 셋이 함께 들어가게 되었는데, 인사도 없고 서로 쳐다보지도 않았다. 두 청년이 먼저 걸어 들어갔고, 표트르 페트로비치는 예의를 차린답시고 통로에서 외투를 벗으며 잠시 머뭇거렸다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 곧장 문간으로 나와 그를 맞이했고, 두냐는 오빠를 반겼다. 표트르 페트로비치는 안으로 들어와, 자못 상냥하게, 그러나 갑절로 위엄을 차리며 두 부인에게 고개를 숙였다. 하지만 그는 어딘가 약간 마음이 상해 아직 평정을 되찾지 못한 듯 보였다. 역시 조금 당황한 듯한 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 서둘러 모두를 둥근 탁자 앞에 앉혔는데, 그 위에는 사모바르가 끓고 있었다. 두냐와 루진은 탁자를 사이에 두고 서로 마주 앉았다. 라주미힌과 라스콜니코프는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나를 마주 보고 앉았는데, 라주미힌은 루진 옆에, 라스콜니코프는 누이 곁에 자리했다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 표트르 페트로비치는 일부러 향내가 진동하는 고운 무명 손수건을 꺼내, 마치 자신이 모욕을 당했다고 여기며 기어이 해명을 받아 내고야 말겠다고 단단히 작정한 너그러운 사람 같은 태도로 코를 풀었다. 통로에서 그는 외투를 벗지 않고 그대로 나가 버려, 두 부인에게 따끔하고 단호한 본때를 보여 주고 사태의 심각성을 깨닫게 해 줄까 하는 생각도 했더랬다. 그러나 차마 그렇게까지는 할 수 없었다. 게다가 그는 불확실한 상태를 견딜 수가 없었고, 해명을 듣고 싶었다. 자신의 요구가 그토록 공공연하게 무시당했다면 거기에는 무슨 곡절이 있는 것이고, 그렇다면 그것을 미리 알아 두는 편이 나았다. 그들을 벌하는 것은 자기 손에 달린 일이었고, 그럴 시간이야 언제든 있을 터였다.\n\n\"여행은 순조로우셨겠지요.\" 그가 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나에게 사무적으로 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아주 순조로웠어요, 표트르 페트로비치.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 기쁘군요. 아브도티야 로마노브나도 너무 지치지는 않으셨고요?\"\n\n\"저는 젊고 튼튼해서 지치지 않습니다만, 어머니께는 무척 힘드셨어요.\" 두냐가 대답했다.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없는 일이지요! 우리 나라 철도는 끔찍하게 기니까요. '어머니 러시아'라고들 하잖습니까, 광활한 나라지요……. 그토록 가고 싶었습니다만, 어제는 도저히 마중을 나갈 수가 없었습니다. 하지만 별 불편 없이 다 잘 지나갔으리라 믿습니다만?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 표트르 페트로비치, 모든 게 끔찍하게 맥 빠지는 일들뿐이었답니다.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 독특한 억양으로 서둘러 말했다. \"드미트리 프로코피치를 하느님께서 친히 우리에게 보내 주지 않으셨더라면, 정말이지 우리는 완전히 길을 잃고 말았을 거예요. 자, 이 사람이랍니다! 드미트리 프로코피치 라주미힌이에요.\" 그녀는 루진에게 그를 소개하며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"어제…… 인사를 나눌 기회가 있었지요.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 라주미힌을 곁눈질로 적대적으로 흘겨보며 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 얼굴을 찌푸리고 입을 다물었다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 겉으로는 사교계에서 무척 정중하고 격식을 대단히 중시하는 부류에 속했지만, 무슨 일에서든 일단 뜻이 어긋나기만 하면 완전히 당황해서, 우아하고 활기찬 사교계 신사라기보다는 차라리 밀가루 자루처럼 되어 버리는 인간이었다. 다시 모두가 입을 다물었다. 라스콜니코프는 고집스레 말이 없었고, 아브도티야 로마노브나는 너무 일찍 대화를 시작하고 싶지 않았다. 라주미힌은 할 말이 없었으므로, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 또다시 불안해졌다.\n\n\"마르파 페트로브나가 죽었어요, 들으셨나요?\" 그녀는 자신의 으뜸가는 화제로 되돌아가며 입을 열었다.\n\n\"물론, 들었습니다. 곧바로 전갈을 받았지요. 그래서 제가 온 것은 아르카지 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프가 아내의 장례를 치르자마자 황급히 페테르부르크로 떠났다는 사실을 알려 드리려는 것입니다. 적어도 저는 그렇게 믿을 만한 확실한 근거를 가지고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"페테르부르크로요? 여기로?\" 두냐가 놀라 물으며 어머니를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 그것도 분명 어떤 속셈이 없지는 않을 겁니다, 그토록 서둘러 떠난 데다, 그에 앞선 온갖 정황을 보면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그자가 여기서도 두냐를 가만 놔두지 않으려는 걸까요?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 부인이나 아브도티야 로마노브나나 불안해하실 근거는 전혀 없습니다, 물론 두 분이 스스로 그자와 연락을 트고 싶어 하시는 게 아니라면 말이지요. 저로서는 경계를 늦추지 않고 있고, 지금 그자가 어디에 묵고 있는지 알아내는 중입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 표트르 페트로비치, 당신이 저를 얼마나 놀라게 했는지 모르실 거예요.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 이었다. \"저는 그자를 딱 두 번 봤을 뿐인데, 끔찍하다, 끔찍하다 싶었답니다! 마르파 페트로브나의 죽음도 그자 탓이라고 저는 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그건 확실히 단정할 수 없는 일입니다. 저는 정확한 정보를 가지고 있습니다. 그자가, 말하자면 그 모욕의 도덕적 영향으로, 사태의 진행을 앞당기는 데 일조했을 가능성을 부인하지는 않습니다. 하지만 그 인물의 전반적인 행실과 도덕적 성격에 관해서라면, 저도 부인과 같은 생각입니다. 그자가 지금 형편이 넉넉한지, 마르파 페트로브나가 그에게 정확히 무엇을 남겼는지는 저도 모릅니다. 이는 머지않아 제가 알게 될 것입니다. 하지만 여기 페테르부르크에서 그에게 금전적 여유가 조금이라도 생긴다면, 틀림없이 곧장 옛 버릇으로 돌아가고 말 겁니다. 그자는 그런 부류의 인간들 가운데서도 가장 타락하고 비열할 만큼 사악한 표본입니다. 저는 마르파 페트로브나가, 팔 년 전에 불운하게도 그자를 사랑하게 되어 그의 빚을 갚아 준 그 여자가, 또 다른 식으로도 그를 도와주었다고 믿을 만한 상당한 근거를 가지고 있습니다. 오로지 그녀의 노력과 희생으로, 환상적이고 살인적인 잔혹성이 얽힌, 그 때문에 시베리아 유형을 선고받아도 마땅했을 형사 사건이 묻혀 버렸지요. 알고 싶으시다면, 그자가 바로 그런 인간이랍니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. 라스콜니코프는 주의 깊게 듣고 있었다.\n\n\"이 모든 것에 확실한 증거가 있다고 하신 게 정말입니까?\" 두냐가 단호하고 힘주어 물었다.\n\n\"저는 다만 마르파 페트로브나가 은밀히 제게 들려준 이야기를 그대로 옮길 뿐입니다. 다만 법적인 관점에서 보자면 그 사건이 결코 명백하지는 않았다는 점은 짚어 두어야겠습니다. 여기에 레슬리히라는 여자가 살고 있었는데, 지금도 살고 있는 것 같습니다만, 외국인으로 이자를 받고 푼돈을 빌려주거나 이런저런 심부름을 해 주는 여자였지요. 스비드리가일로프는 이 여자와 오랫동안 은밀하고 수상쩍은 관계를 맺고 있었습니다. 그 여자에게는 함께 사는 친척이, 아마 조카딸이었던 듯한데, 열다섯, 아니 어쩌면 열넷밖에 안 된 귀먹고 말 못 하는 소녀가 있었지요. 레슬리히는 이 소녀를 미워해서 빵 한 조각까지 아까워했고, 사정없이 두들겨 패곤 했답니다. 그러던 어느 날, 그 소녀가 다락방에서 목매단 채로 발견되었지요. 검시 결과는 자살이었습니다. 통상적인 절차를 거쳐 사건은 마무리되었습니다만, 나중에 그 아이가…… 스비드리가일로프에게 잔혹하게 능욕을 당했다는 정보가 들어왔지요. 사실 이것은 명백히 밝혀지지는 않았고, 그 정보를 준 것은 말을 믿을 수 없는 또 다른 방탕한 독일 여자였습니다. 경찰에 정식으로 진술된 것은 아무것도 없었으니, 이는 마르파 페트로브나의 돈과 노력 덕분이었지요. 결국 뜬소문 이상으로는 번지지 않았습니다. 그렇긴 해도 이 이야기는 무척 의미심장한 것입니다. 아브도티야 로마노브나, 분명 그 집에 계실 때 농노 해방 이전, 육 년 전에 학대를 받고 죽은 하인 필립의 이야기를 들으셨을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"오히려 저는 이 필립이 스스로 목을 맸다고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 하지만 그를 자살로 몰아넣은 것, 아니 어쩌면 자살하도록 만든 것은 스비드리가일로프 씨의 체계적인 박해와 가혹함이었습니다.\"\n\n\"저는 그건 모르겠어요.\" 두냐가 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다. \"저는 다만 이상한 이야기를 들었을 뿐이에요, 필립이 일종의 우울증 환자, 일종의 집안 철학자 같은 사람이라, 하인들 말로는 '책을 너무 읽어서 머리가 돌았다'더군요, 그리고 그가 목을 맨 것은 어느 정도 스비드리가일로프 씨의 매질 때문이 아니라 그의 조롱 때문이었다고요. 제가 거기 있을 때 그분은 하인들에게 잘 대해 주었고, 하인들도 그분을 정말 좋아했어요, 물론 필립의 죽음에 대해서는 그분을 탓했지만요.\"\n\n\"보아하니, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 갑자기 그자를 변호하고 나서실 생각이신 모양이군요.\" 루진이 입술을 비틀어 모호한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그자가 교활한 인간이고, 부인네들 앞에서는 알랑거릴 줄 안다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없지요. 그토록 기이하게 죽은 마르파 페트로브나가 끔찍한 본보기 아닙니까. 제 바람은 오로지, 그자한테서 분명 또다시 예상되는 시도들에 대비해, 충고로나마 부인과 어머님께 도움이 되고자 하는 것이었습니다. 저로서는, 그자가 결국 또다시 채무자 감옥에서 생을 마칠 거라고 굳게 확신합니다. 마르파 페트로브나는 자기 아이들의 이익을 생각해서 그자에게 무언가 상당한 재산을 물려줄 마음은 손톱만큼도 없었습니다. 설령 무언가를 남겼다 해도, 그건 그저 겨우 입에 풀칠할 정도의, 하찮고 덧없는 것이어서, 그자 같은 습성을 가진 인간에게는 일 년도 못 갈 겁니다.\"\n\n\"표트르 페트로비치, 부탁드려요.\" 두냐가 말했다. \"스비드리가일로프 씨 이야기는 그만하세요. 저를 비참하게 만드는군요.\"\n\n\"방금 그 사람이 저를 찾아왔습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 처음으로 침묵을 깨고 말했다.\n\n모두가 탄성을 지르며 그를 돌아보았다. 표트르 페트로비치까지 정신이 번쩍 든 듯했다.\n\n\"한 시간 반쯤 전에, 제가 자고 있을 때 들어와서, 저를 깨우고 자기소개를 하더군요.\" 라스콜니코프가 말을 이었다. \"꽤나 명랑하고 느긋했고, 우리가 친구가 되기를 자못 바라더군요. 그건 그렇고, 두냐, 특히 너와의 면담을 간절히 바라면서, 그걸 주선해 달라고 나한테 부탁했어. 너한테 제안할 게 있다며, 그게 뭔지 나한테 말해 줬지. 또 마르파 페트로브나가 죽기 일주일 전에 유언으로 너한테 삼천 루블을 남겼다고, 두냐, 그리고 그 돈을 머지않아 받을 수 있을 거라고 하더군.\"\n\n\"하느님, 감사합니다!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 성호를 그으며 외쳤다. \"그 여자의 영혼을 위해 기도하자, 두냐!\"\n\n\"사실입니다!\" 루진의 입에서 튀어나왔다.\n\n\"말해 봐, 또 뭐라고 했어?\" 두냐가 라스콜니코프를 재촉했다.\n\n\"그러고는 자기가 부자가 아니고, 모든 재산은 지금 고모와 함께 있는 자기 아이들한테 남겨졌다고 하더군. 그다음 자기가 내 집에서 그리 멀지 않은 어딘가에 묵고 있다고 했는데, 어딘지는 몰라, 묻지 않았으니까…….\"\n\n\"그런데 그자가, 그자가 두냐한테 무슨 제안을 하려는 거니?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 겁에 질려 외쳤다. \"너한테 말하더냐?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐였니?\"\n\n\"나중에 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 말을 멈추고 자기 차로 주의를 돌렸다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 시계를 보았다.\n\n\"제가 부득이 사업상 약속을 지켜야 해서, 더는 폐가 되지 않겠습니다.\" 그는 다소 발끈한 기색으로 덧붙이며 일어서기 시작했다.\n\n\"가지 마세요, 표트르 페트로비치.\" 두냐가 말했다. \"저녁을 함께 보내실 작정이었잖아요. 게다가 어머니와 해명하고 싶은 게 있다고 직접 편지에 쓰셨고요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다, 아브도티야 로마노브나.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 묵직하게 대꾸하며 다시 자리에 앉았으나, 모자는 여전히 손에 든 채였다. \"저는 분명 당신과 존경하는 어머님과 더불어, 참으로 중대한 사안에 관해 해명을 나누고자 했습니다. 하지만 당신 오라버니가 제 앞에서 스비드리가일로프 씨의 어떤 제안들을 터놓고 말할 수 없는 것처럼, 저 또한…… 다른 사람들이 있는 자리에서…… 더없이 중대한 어떤 문제들을 터놓고 말하고 싶지도 않고, 말할 수도 없습니다. 더구나 저의 가장 무겁고 절박한 요청이 묵살되었으니…….\"\n\n상심한 기색을 지으며, 루진은 위엄 있는 침묵 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n\"오라버니가 우리 만남에 동석하지 말아 달라는 당신의 요청을 묵살한 것은 오로지 제 고집 때문이었어요.\" 두냐가 말했다. \"당신은 제 오빠한테서 모욕을 당했다고 쓰셨지요. 저는 이걸 지금 당장 풀어야 한다고 생각해요, 그래서 두 분이 화해하셔야 한다고요. 만일 로쟈가 정말로 당신을 모욕했다면, 마땅히 사과해야 하고, 사과할 거예요.\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 한층 강경하게 나왔다.\n\n\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 그 어떤 호의로도 잊을 수 없는 모욕이라는 게 있습니다. 무슨 일에든 넘어서는 안 될 선이 있고, 일단 그 선을 넘어 버리면 되돌릴 수 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드리려는 건 바로 그게 아니에요, 표트르 페트로비치.\" 두냐가 다소 조바심을 내며 가로막았다. \"부디 이해해 주세요, 지금 우리의 모든 앞날은 이 모든 일이 가능한 한 빨리 풀려서 바로잡히느냐에 달려 있어요. 처음부터 솔직히 말씀드리는데, 저는 이걸 다른 식으로는 도저히 볼 수가 없어요, 그리고 저를 조금이라도 생각하신다면, 이 일은 아무리 힘들더라도 오늘 안에 끝나야 해요. 다시 말씀드리지만, 제 오빠한테 잘못이 있다면 당신께 용서를 빌 거예요.\"\n\n\"문제를 그런 식으로 제기하시다니 놀랍군요.\" 루진이 점점 더 짜증을 내며 말했다. \"당신을 존경하고, 말하자면 흠모하면서도, 저는 동시에 당신 가족 중 누군가를 매우, 정말이지 매우 싫어할 수도 있는 겁니다. 비록 제가 당신의 손을 잡는 행복을 누리고자 한다 해도, 그와 양립할 수 없는 의무까지 받아들일 수는…….\"\n\n\"아, 그렇게 쉽게 발끈하지 마세요, 표트르 페트로비치.\" 두냐가 감정을 실어 가로막았다. \"그리고 제가 늘 여겨 왔고, 또 그렇게 여기고 싶은 대로, 분별 있고 너그러운 분이 되어 주세요. 저는 당신께 큰 약속을 했어요, 저는 당신의 약혼녀입니다. 이 일에서 저를 믿어 주세요, 그리고 믿어 주세요, 저는 공정하게 판단할 줄 아는 사람입니다. 제가 심판자 역할을 떠맡은 건 당신께만큼이나 제 오빠한테도 뜻밖의 일이에요. 당신 편지를 받고 오늘 만남에 오빠를 꼭 데려오라고 고집했을 때, 제가 어떻게 할 작정인지는 오빠한테 아무 말도 하지 않았어요. 이해해 주세요, 만일 두 분이 화해하지 못한다면, 저는 두 분 중 한 사람을 택해야 해요. 당신이냐 오빠냐, 둘 중 하나여야 한다고요. 당신 편에서나 오빠 편에서나 문제는 그렇게 걸려 있어요. 저는 제 선택에서 잘못을 저지르고 싶지 않고, 또 그래서도 안 됩니다. 당신을 위해서라면 저는 오빠와 인연을 끊어야 하고, 오빠를 위해서라면 당신과 인연을 끊어야 해요. 저는 이제 오빠가 정말 제게 오빠다운 사람인지 확실히 알아낼 수 있고, 또 알고 싶어요. 그리고 당신에 대해서는, 제가 당신께 소중한 사람인지, 당신이 저를 존중하시는지, 당신이 제게 맞는 남편인지 알고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"아브도티야 로마노브나,\" 루진이 잔뜩 골이 나서 단언했다. \"당신의 말씀은 제게 너무도 중대한 의미를 지닙니다. 더 말씀드리자면, 제가 당신에 대해 누리는 영광스러운 지위를 생각할 때 모욕적이기까지 합니다. 당신이 저를 무례한 애송이와 같은 수준에 놓는 그 기이하고 모욕적인 처사는 차치하더라도, 당신은 제게 한 약속을 깰 수 있다는 가능성을 인정하시는 겁니다. 당신은 '당신이냐 오빠냐'라고 말함으로써, 당신 눈에 제가 얼마나 하찮은 존재인지를 드러내고 계십니다……. 우리 사이에 존재하는 관계와…… 의무를 생각할 때, 저는 이를 그냥 넘길 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 두냐가 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. \"저는 당신의 이익을, 지금껏 제 인생에서 가장 소중했던 모든 것, 제 인생의 전부를 이루던 것과 나란히 놓았어요, 그런데 당신은 제가 당신을 너무 가볍게 여긴다고 기분이 상하셨다는 거군요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 빈정대듯 미소 지었고, 라주미힌은 안절부절못했지만, 표트르 페트로비치는 그 질책을 받아들이지 않았다. 오히려 그는 한마디 한마디 할 때마다 점점 더 고집스럽고 신경질적으로 굴었으니, 마치 그걸 즐기는 듯했다.\n\n\"앞으로 함께 살아갈 반려에 대한, 남편에 대한 사랑은, 오라버니에 대한 사랑보다 무거워야 합니다.\" 그가 잠언이라도 늘어놓듯 말했다. \"그리고 어떤 경우에도 저를 그와 같은 수준에 놓을 수는 없습니다……. 비록 제가 당신 오라버니 앞에서는 터놓고 말하지 않겠다고 그토록 힘주어 말하긴 했습니다만, 그럼에도 저는 이제 존경하는 어머님께, 제 존엄과 긴밀히 관련된 중대한 사안에 관해 꼭 필요한 해명을 청하려 합니다. 부인의 아드님이,\" 그가 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나에게 몸을 돌렸다. \"어제 라즈수드킨 씨(아니면…… 그게 맞나요? 죄송합니다, 성함을 잊었군요\"—그가 라주미힌에게 정중히 고개를 숙였다)\"가 있는 자리에서, 제가 사사로운 대화 중에, 그러니까 커피를 마시며 부인께 표현했던 생각을 왜곡함으로써 저를 모욕했습니다. 곤경을 겪어 본 가난한 처녀와의 결혼이, 호사를 누리며 살아온 처녀와의 결혼보다 부부 관계의 관점에서 더 유리하다는, 도덕적 성품을 위해서 더 이롭다는 그 생각 말입니다. 부인의 아드님은 일부러 제 말의 의미를 과장하여 우스꽝스럽게 만들고, 제게 악의가 있었다고 몰아붙였는데, 제가 보기엔 부인과 주고받은 편지를 믿고 그런 것 같습니다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 만일 부인께서 제게 그 반대의 결론을 납득시켜 주시고, 그리하여 사려 깊게 저를 안심시켜 주실 수 있다면, 저는 행복하겠습니다. 부디 부인께서 로지온 로마노비치에게 보내신 편지에서, 제 말을 정확히 어떤 식으로 옮기셨는지 알려 주시겠습니까.\"\n\n\"저는 기억이 안 나요.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 더듬거렸다. \"저는 제가 이해한 대로 옮겼어요. 로쟈가 그걸 당신께 어떻게 옮겼는지는 모르겠고요, 어쩌면 과장했는지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"부인께서 부추기지 않았다면, 아드님이 그걸 과장했을 리가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"표트르 페트로비치,\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 위엄 있게 단언했다. \"두냐와 제가 당신 말을 그리 나쁘게 받아들이지 않았다는 증거는, 우리가 이렇게 여기 있다는 사실 그 자체랍니다.\"\n\n\"잘 말씀하셨어요, 어머니.\" 두냐가 흡족해하며 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 이번에도 제 잘못이군요.\" 루진이 상심한 듯 말했다.\n\n\"이거 봐요, 표트르 페트로비치, 당신은 자꾸 로지온을 탓하지만, 정작 당신 자신도 방금 그 애에 대해 거짓을 써 보냈잖아요.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 용기를 내어 덧붙였다.\n\n\"저는 거짓을 썼던 기억이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 썼소,\" 라스콜니코프가 루진 쪽으로 돌아보지도 않은 채 날카롭게 말했다. \"내가 어제 돈을 준 게, 사실대로라면 살해당한 남자의 미망인에게 준 건데, 그게 아니라 그 딸(어제 처음 본)에게 줬다고 말이오. 당신이 이걸 쓴 건 나와 내 가족 사이를 이간질하려는 거였고, 그러려고 당신이 알지도 못하는 처녀의 행실에 대해 천박한 표현까지 덧붙였소. 그건 죄다 비열한 중상모략이오.\"\n\n\"실례합니다만, 선생,\" 루진이 분노로 떨며 말했다. \"제가 편지에서 당신의 됨됨이와 행실을 들춘 것은, 오로지 당신 누이와 어머님의 물음에 답하느라, 제가 당신을 어떻게 보았고 어떤 인상을 받았는지를 말씀드린 것뿐입니다. 제 편지에서 당신이 언급한 그 대목에 관해서라면, 거기서 거짓말이라곤 한마디라도 짚어 보시지요, 그러니까 당신이 돈을 헛되이 내던지지 않았다는 것, 그리고 그 가족 안에, 아무리 불행하다 한들, 쓸모없는 인간들이 없다는 것을 보여 주시란 말입니다.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 당신은 그 온갖 미덕을 다 갖췄어도, 당신이 돌을 던지는 그 불행한 처녀의 새끼손가락만 한 가치도 없는 사람이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 그 여자를 당신 어머니, 누이와 어울리게 하는 데까지 나가시겠다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"이미 그렇게 했소, 알고 싶으시다면. 오늘 그 여자를 어머니, 두냐와 함께 앉게 했지.\"\n\n\"로쟈!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. 두냐는 새빨개졌고, 라주미힌은 눈썹을 찌푸렸다. 루진은 거만한 빈정거림을 머금고 미소 지었다.\n\n\"직접 보십시오, 아브도티야 로마노브나,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 과연 뜻이 맞을 수 있는지 말입니다. 이제 이 문제가 단번에 영원히 매듭지어졌기를 바랍니다. 저는 물러가겠습니다, 단란한 가족의 즐거움과 비밀스러운 이야기에 방해가 되지 않도록 말이지요.\" 그는 의자에서 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다. \"다만 물러가면서, 감히 청하건대, 앞으로는 저를 이런 모임이나, 말하자면 이런 타협에서 면해 주시기를 바랍니다. 특히 이 점에 관해서는 존경하는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 부인께 간곡히 호소드립니다. 제 편지가 다른 누구도 아닌 부인께 보낸 것이었으니 더더욱 그렇습니다.\"\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 조금 기분이 상했다.\n\n\"당신은 우리가 완전히 당신 손아귀에 있다고 여기시는 모양이군요, 표트르 페트로비치. 두냐가 당신의 바람이 묵살된 이유를 말씀드렸잖아요, 그 애는 더없이 좋은 뜻에서 그랬어요. 게다가 당신은 마치 제게 명령이라도 내리듯 편지를 쓰셨더군요. 우리가 당신의 바람을 하나하나 명령으로 받들어야 하나요? 오히려 제가 말씀드리지요, 당신은 지금 우리에게 각별히 섬세하고 사려 깊게 대하셔야 마땅합니다, 우리는 모든 걸 내던지고 당신만 믿고 여기까지 왔으니까요, 그러니 어떤 의미에서 우리는 어쨌든 당신 손에 달려 있는 셈이잖아요.\"\n\n\"그건 그리 정확한 말씀이 아닙니다, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 특히 지금처럼 마르파 페트로브나의 유산 소식이 전해진 마당에는요, 부인이 제게 취하시는 그 새로운 말투로 미루어 보건대, 참으로 시의적절한 소식이지요.\" 그가 빈정대며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그 말씀으로 미루어 보건대, 당신이 우리의 무력함을 계산에 넣고 있었다는 걸 확실히 짐작할 수 있겠군요.\" 두냐가 신경질적으로 한마디 했다.\n\n\"하지만 이제 어쨌든 저는 그걸 계산에 넣을 수가 없게 됐고, 특히 아르카지 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프가 당신 오라버니에게 맡긴 그 은밀한 제안, 제가 보기엔 당신께 크나큰, 어쩌면 무척 흐뭇한 관심거리가 될 그 이야기를 나누시는 데 방해가 되지 않기를 각별히 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다.\n\n라주미힌은 의자에 가만히 앉아 있을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"이래도 부끄럽지 않나, 누이?\" 라스콜니코프가 물었다.\n\n\"부끄러워요, 로쟈.\" 두냐가 말했다. \"표트르 페트로비치, 가세요.\" 그녀가 분노로 새파래진 얼굴로 그에게 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 이런 결말은 전혀 예상치 못했던 게 분명했다. 그는 자기 자신과 자기 힘, 그리고 자기 희생자들의 무력함을 너무 굳게 믿었던 것이다. 그는 지금 이 순간에도 이를 믿을 수가 없었다. 그는 창백해졌고, 입술이 떨렸다.\n\n\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 만일 제가 이런 식으로 쫓겨나, 지금 이 문을 나선다면, 명심하십시오, 저는 다시는 돌아오지 않을 겁니다. 당신이 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 잘 생각하십시오. 제 말은 결코 번복되지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 뻔뻔한 소리예요!\" 두냐가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"다시는 돌아오지 마세요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그래, 일이 그렇게 됐다 이거군요!\" 루진이 외쳤는데, 마지막 순간까지도 이 파국을 도무지 믿지 못했고, 그래서 이제 완전히 계산이 어긋나 버린 것이다. \"그래, 일이 그렇게 됐단 말이지! 하지만 아십니까, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 저도 이의를 제기할 수 있다는 걸요?\"\n\n\"무슨 권리로 그 애한테 그런 식으로 말하는 거예요?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 발끈하여 끼어들었다. \"그리고 무슨 이의를 제기할 수 있다는 거죠? 당신한테 무슨 권리가 있다고요? 제가 당신 같은 사람한테 우리 두냐를 줘야 하나요? 가세요, 우리한테서 아주 떠나 버려요! 우리야말로 그릇된 일에 동의한 게 잘못이지요, 그중에서도 누구보다 제가…….\"\n\n\"하지만 부인은 저를 옭아매셨습니다, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나,\" 루진이 광분하여 펄펄 뛰었다. \"약속으로 말입니다, 그래 놓고 이제 와서 그걸 부인하시고…… 게다가…… 저는 그 때문에 비용까지 들였단 말입니다…….\"\n\n이 마지막 푸념이 어찌나 표트르 페트로비치다웠던지, 분노로 창백해진 채 그것을 억누르느라 안간힘을 쓰던 라스콜니코프는 그만 웃음을 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 격분했다.\n\n\"비용이라고요? 무슨 비용 말인가요? 우리 트렁크 말씀인가요? 그거야 차장이 당신을 위해 공짜로 가져다준 거잖아요. 맙소사, 우리가 당신을 옭아맸다니요! 도대체 무슨 생각이세요, 표트르 페트로비치, 우리를 손발까지 옭아맨 건 바로 당신이지, 우리가 아니에요!\"\n\n\"그만하세요, 어머니, 제발 더는요.\" 아브도티야 로마노브나가 애원했다. \"표트르 페트로비치, 부디 그만 가 주세요!\"\n\n\"가지요, 하지만 마지막으로 한마디 하겠습니다.\" 그가 도무지 자제하지 못하고 말했다. \"당신 어머님께서는 까맣게 잊으신 모양인데, 제가 당신을, 말하자면 거두기로 마음먹은 것은, 당신의 평판에 관한 그 동네 소문이 온 고을에 쫙 퍼진 뒤였습니다. 당신을 위해 세간의 평판을 무릅쓰고 당신의 명예를 되살려 준 만큼, 저는 마땅히 그에 걸맞은 보답을 기대할 수 있었고, 실로 당신 쪽에서 감사를 바랄 수도 있었지요. 그런데 이제야 제 눈이 뜨였습니다! 제가 그 세상 사람들의 한결같은 평결을 무시한 것이, 참으로, 참으로 경솔한 짓이었을지도 모른다는 걸 이제 알겠군요…….\"\n\n\"이자가 대가리가 박살 나고 싶은 건가?\" 라주미힌이 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신은 비열하고 악의에 찬 인간이에요!\" 두냐가 외쳤다.\n\n\"한마디도 마! 꼼짝도 마!\" 라스콜니코프가 라주미힌을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. 그러고는 루진에게 바싹 다가가, \"이 방에서 나가 주시지.\" 그가 나직하고 또렷하게 말했다. \"한마디만 더 했다간…….\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 분노로 일그러진 창백한 얼굴로 몇 초 동안 그를 노려보더니, 몸을 돌려 나가 버렸다. 라스콜니코프에게 품은 만큼 앙심에 찬 증오를 가슴에 담고 떠난 사람도 드물 것이다. 그는 모든 일을 그자, 오직 그자 탓으로 돌렸다. 주목할 만한 것은, 계단을 내려가면서도 그는 여전히 자기 처지가 어쩌면 완전히 끝장난 것은 아닐지도 모르며, 적어도 두 부인에 관한 한 모든 것이 '참으로 잘' 다시 바로잡힐 수도 있으리라고 상상하고 있었다는 점이다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n사실 그는 마지막 순간까지도 이런 결말을 단 한 번도 예상하지 못했다. 그는 극도로 거만했고, 가진 것 없고 의지할 데도 없는 두 여인이 자신의 손아귀에서 벗어날 수 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했다. 이 확신은 그의 허영심과 자만심에 의해, 어리석음에 가까울 만큼 부풀어 오른 자만심에 의해 한층 강해졌다. 보잘것없는 처지에서 스스로 출세한 표트르 페트로비치는 병적으로 자기 도취에 빠져 있었고, 자신의 지성과 능력을 더없이 높이 평가하며, 때로는 홀로 거울 앞에서 자기 모습에 흐뭇해하기까지 했다. 그러나 그가 무엇보다 사랑하고 소중히 여긴 것은 자신의 노력과 온갖 술수로 긁어모은 돈이었다. 그 돈이 한때 그의 윗사람이었던 모든 이들과 그를 대등한 존재로 만들어 주었기 때문이다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치가 두냐에게 나쁜 소문에도 불구하고 그녀를 아내로 맞기로 결심했노라고 쓰라리게 일깨웠을 때, 그는 진심으로 말한 것이었고, 실제로 그런 \"새카만 배은망덕\"에 진정으로 분개하고 있었다. 그런데도 그가 두냐에게 청혼했던 그때, 그는 그 모든 풍문이 근거 없는 것임을 충분히 알고 있었다. 그 이야기는 이미 마르파 페트로브나에 의해 곳곳에서 부정되었고, 두냐를 열렬히 옹호하던 마을 사람들 모두가 그것을 믿지 않게 되었다. 그리고 그는 자신이 그 당시 이 모든 것을 알고 있었음을 부인하지 않았으리라. 그럼에도 그는 두냐를 자기 수준으로 끌어올리겠다는 자신의 결단을 여전히 높이 평가했고, 그것을 무슨 영웅적인 행위쯤으로 여겼다. 두냐에게 그 이야기를 하면서 그는 자신이 품고 흠모하던 은밀한 감정을 드러내고 말았고, 다른 사람들이 그것을 함께 흠모하지 않는다는 것을 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 그는 자신의 선행의 열매를 거두고 기분 좋은 아첨을 듣게 되리라 기대하는 은인의 심정으로 라스콜니코프를 찾아왔던 것이다. 그리고 지금 계단을 내려가면서 그는 자신이 너무도 부당하게 모욕당하고 인정받지 못했다고 여겼다.\n\n두냐는 그에게 그야말로 없어서는 안 될 존재였다. 그녀 없이 지낸다는 것은 상상조차 할 수 없었다. 그는 오랜 세월 동안 결혼에 대한 음탕한 공상을 품어 왔지만, 그저 기다리며 돈을 긁어모았다. 그는 깊디깊은 비밀 속에서 한 처녀의 모습을 흐뭇하게 곱씹곤 했다. 정숙하고, 가난하고(반드시 가난해야 했다), 아주 젊고, 아주 예쁘며, 좋은 집안에서 태어나 교육을 받았으되, 몹시 수줍어하고, 많은 고생을 겪었으며, 그의 앞에서 완전히 자신을 낮추는, 평생토록 그를 자신의 구원자로 우러러보며 숭배하고, 오직 그만을 흠모할 그런 처녀를. 일을 마친 뒤면 이 매혹적이고 짓궂은 주제를 두고 그는 얼마나 많은 장면을, 얼마나 많은 사랑의 일화를 상상해 보았던가! 그런데 보라, 그 오랜 세월의 꿈이 거의 실현되려던 참이었다. 아브도티야 로마노브나의 아름다움과 교양은 그를 사로잡았고, 그녀의 무력한 처지는 더없는 유혹이었으며, 그는 그녀에게서 자신이 꿈꿔 온 것 이상을 발견했던 것이다. 여기 자존심과 개성과 미덕을 갖추고, 자신보다 우월한 교양과 가정교육을 받은(그는 그것을 느꼈다) 처녀가 있었으니, 이 여인은 자신의 영웅적인 너그러움에 평생토록 노예처럼 감사할 것이고, 그의 발밑에서 먼지 속에 자신을 낮출 것이며, 그는 그녀에 대해 절대적이고 무한한 권력을 쥐게 될 터였다!…… 게다가 얼마 전 그는 오랜 숙고와 망설임 끝에 자신의 경력에 중대한 변화를 주어, 이제 한층 넓은 사업의 무대로 발을 들이고 있었다. 이 변화와 더불어, 더 높은 계층의 사교계로 올라서겠다는 그의 소중한 꿈도 실현될 듯 보였다…… 사실 그는 페테르부르크에서 자신의 운을 시험해 보기로 결심한 터였다. 그는 여자가 아주 많은 일을 해낼 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 매력적이고 정숙하며 높은 교양을 갖춘 여인의 매혹은 그의 길을 한결 수월하게 만들고, 사람들을 그에게로 끌어들이며 그를 후광으로 감싸는, 그런 기적을 일으킬 수도 있었다. 그런데 지금 모든 것이 폐허가 되어 버린 것이다! 이 갑작스럽고 끔찍한 파국은 그에게 마치 벼락처럼 들이닥쳤다. 그것은 흉측한 농담이자 부조리 같았다. 그는 그저 아주 조금 위세를 부렸을 뿐이고, 미처 속내를 다 털어놓을 새도 없었으며, 그저 농담을 했을 뿐이고, 흥에 겨워 휩쓸렸을 뿐인데 — 그것이 이렇게 심각하게 끝나 버린 것이다. 그리고 물론 그는 그 나름의 방식으로 두냐를 사랑하고 있었다. 그는 이미 공상 속에서 그녀를 소유하고 있었는데 — 그것이 단번에! 안 돼! 내일, 바로 내일이면 이 모든 것을 바로잡고, 매끄럽게 다독여, 결말지어야 한다. 무엇보다도 이 모든 일의 원흉인 그 거만한 풋내기를 짓밟아 버려야 한다. 그는 메스꺼운 기분 속에서 라주미힌도 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으나, 곧 그 점에 대해서는 마음을 놓았다. 그따위 작자를 자신과 동등한 위치에 놓을 수 있기나 하겠는가! 그가 진정으로 두려워한 사내는 스비드리가일로프였다…… 요컨대, 그에게는 신경 써야 할 일이 한둘이 아니었다……\n\n\"아니에요, 제가, 제가 누구보다 잘못했어요!\" 두냐가 어머니에게 입을 맞추고 끌어안으며 말했다. \"전 그 사람 돈에 마음이 흔들렸던 거예요, 하지만 맹세코, 오빠, 그가 그렇게 천박한 인간일 줄은 몰랐어요. 진작에 그 속을 꿰뚫어 봤더라면, 그 무엇으로도 절 흔들지 못했을 거예요! 절 나무라지 말아요, 오빠!\"\n\n\"하느님께서 우리를 구원하셨다! 하느님께서 우리를 구원하셨어!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 중얼거렸으나, 그것은 반쯤 정신이 나간 채로, 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지조차 거의 실감하지 못하는 듯한 것이었다.\n\n모두가 안도했고, 오 분 뒤에는 웃음을 터뜨리고 있었다. 다만 두냐만이 이따금 지나간 일을 떠올리며 새파랗게 질려 눈살을 찌푸렸다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 자신마저 기뻐하고 있다는 데 놀랐다. 그녀는 바로 그날 아침만 해도 루진과의 결별을 끔찍한 불행으로 여겼던 것이다. 라주미힌은 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰랐다. 아직 그 기쁨을 마음껏 드러낼 엄두는 나지 않았으나, 마치 가슴에서 천근 무게가 떨어져 나간 듯 흥분으로 들떠 있었다. 이제 그는 자신의 삶을 그들에게 바치고, 그들을 위해 헌신할 권리를 얻은 것이다…… 이제 무슨 일이든 일어날 수 있었다! 그러나 그는 그 이상의 가능성을 생각하기가 두려웠고, 감히 상상의 나래를 펴지 못했다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 같은 자리에 가만히 앉은 채, 거의 시무룩하고 무관심해 보였다. 루진을 떼어 내자고 누구보다 강하게 고집했던 그가, 정작 지금 벌어진 일에는 가장 무심한 듯했다. 두냐는 그가 여전히 자신에게 화가 나 있는 것이라고 생각하지 않을 수 없었고, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 겁먹은 듯 그를 지켜보았다.\n\n\"스비드리가일로프가 오빠한테 무슨 말을 했어요?\" 두냐가 그에게 다가서며 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다.\n\n라스콜니코프가 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"그자는 너에게 만 루블을 선사하고 싶어 하고, 내가 있는 자리에서 너를 한 번 만나 보길 바라더구나.\"\n\n\"그애를 만나겠다고요! 절대로 안 돼요!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. \"그리고 감히 그애에게 돈을 주겠다니!\"\n\n그러고 나서 라스콜니코프는 (꽤 무미건조하게) 스비드리가일로프와의 대화를 되풀이해 들려주었는데, 불필요한 이야기는 모두 피하고 싶어서 마르파 페트로브나의 유령이 나타난 일에 대해서는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"그래서 오빠는 그에게 뭐라고 대답했어요?\" 두냐가 물었다.\n\n\"처음엔 너에게 아무런 전갈도 전하지 않겠다고 했지. 그랬더니 그자는 내 도움 없이도 너와의 면담을 얻어 내려 온 힘을 다하겠다고 하더구나. 너를 향한 자신의 열정은 한때의 도취였을 뿐, 이제는 너에게 아무 감정도 없다고 장담하면서. 그자는 네가 루진과 결혼하는 걸 원치 않더라…… 그의 말은 통틀어 꽤 횡설수설이었어.\"\n\n\"오빠는 그 사람을 어떻게 이해해요, 로쟈? 어떤 인상을 받았어요?\"\n\n\"솔직히 나도 그자를 잘 이해하진 못하겠다. 그자는 너에게 만 루블을 내놓으면서도, 정작 자기는 형편이 넉넉지 않다고 하더구나. 떠나겠다고 해 놓고는 십 분도 안 돼 그 말을 했다는 걸 잊어버려. 그러다가 또 결혼할 거라면서 이미 신붓감까지 점찍어 두었다고 하지…… 분명 무슨 속셈이 있는 거고, 아마 좋지 못한 속셈일 게다. 하지만 너에게 무슨 흉계가 있다면, 그렇게 서툴게 군다는 게 이상해…… 물론 나는 너를 위해 단호히, 한 번에 그 돈을 거절했다. 통틀어, 나는 그자가 무척 이상하다고 생각했어…… 거의 미친 사람이라고 여겨도 될 정도야. 하지만 내가 틀렸을지도 모르지. 어쩌면 그저 그자가 꾸며 내는 역할일 뿐인지도 몰라. 마르파 페트로브나의 죽음이 그자에게 크나큰 충격을 준 모양이더라.\"\n\n\"그분의 영혼이 평안하시기를.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. \"나는 늘, 언제까지나 그분을 위해 기도할 거야! 두냐, 이 삼천 루블이 없었다면 우리가 지금 어찌 됐겠니! 마치 하늘에서 떨어진 것 같구나! 글쎄, 로쟈, 오늘 아침만 해도 우리 주머니엔 고작 삼 루블밖에 없어서, 그 사람이 도와주겠다고 나서기 전까진 그자에게 빌리지 않으려고 두냐와 내가 막 그애 시계를 저당 잡힐 궁리를 하던 참이었단다.\"\n\n두냐는 스비드리가일로프의 제안에 묘하게 마음이 동요된 듯했다. 그녀는 여전히 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 채 서 있었다.\n\n\"그 사람에겐 무슨 끔찍한 계획이 있어요.\" 그녀가 거의 몸서리치며, 반쯤 속삭이듯 혼잣말로 말했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 이 지나친 공포를 눈여겨보았다.\n\n\"아무래도 나는 그자를 한 번 이상 더 만나야 할 것 같구나.\" 그가 두냐에게 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 그자를 감시하지요! 내가 그자를 끝까지 추적하겠습니다!\" 라주미힌이 힘차게 외쳤다. \"그자에게서 눈을 떼지 않을 겁니다. 로쟈가 나에게 허락했어요. 방금 자기 입으로 직접 말했죠. '내 누이를 돌봐 줘'라고요. 당신도 나에게 허락해 주시겠습니까, 아브도티야 로마노브나?\"\n\n두냐는 미소 지으며 손을 내밀었으나, 얼굴에서 근심의 빛은 가시지 않았다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 겁먹은 듯 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그래도 삼천 루블이 그녀에게 위안을 주는 효과를 발휘하고 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n십오 분쯤 뒤, 그들은 모두 활기찬 대화에 빠져 있었다. 라스콜니코프조차 한동안 — 비록 말은 하지 않았으나 — 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. 말하는 이는 라주미힌이었다.\n\n\"그런데 왜, 왜 떠나려고 하십니까?\" 그가 황홀해하며 거침없이 말을 쏟아 냈다. \"작은 시골 마을에서 대체 무얼 하시려고요? 가장 중요한 건, 여러분 모두가 여기 함께 있고 서로가 서로를 필요로 한다는 겁니다 — 정말 서로가 필요해요, 믿어 주십시오. 적어도 한동안은요…… 저를 동업자로 받아들여 주십시오, 그러면 우리가 멋진 사업을 꾸려 낼 거라고 장담합니다. 들어 보세요! 제가 다 자세히 설명해 드리지요, 계획 전부를요! 오늘 아침, 아무 일도 벌어지기 전에 이 모든 게 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐답니다…… 자, 이런 겁니다. 저에게 백부님 한 분이 계신데, 여러분께 꼭 소개해 드려야겠어요(더없이 너그럽고 점잖은 노인이시지요). 이 백부님께 천 루블의 자본이 있는데, 연금으로 사시니 그 돈이 필요 없으십니다. 지난 이 년 동안 백부님은 자기한테서 그 돈을 빌려 가 육 푼 이자를 내라고 저를 들볶으셨어요. 그게 무슨 뜻인지 저는 압니다. 그저 저를 돕고 싶으신 거예요. 작년엔 그 돈이 필요 없었지만, 올해는 백부님이 도착하시는 대로 빌리기로 마음먹었습니다. 그러면 여러분이 가진 삼천 루블 중에서 천 루블을 저에게 빌려 주시고, 그러면 우리에겐 시작할 만한 충분한 밑천이 생기니, 동업을 하는 거죠. 그래서 우리가 무얼 하느냐고요?\"\n\n이어서 라주미힌은 자신의 계획을 펼쳐 보이기 시작했는데, 우리네 출판업자와 서적상은 거의 전부가 자기네가 파는 것이 무엇인지 도통 모르고, 그런 까닭에 대개는 형편없는 출판업자이며, 어지간히 괜찮은 출판물은 으레 수지가 맞고 이윤을, 때로는 상당한 이윤을 안겨 준다는 것을 장황하게 설명했다. 사실 라주미힌은 출판업자로 자리 잡는 것을 꿈꿔 왔다. 지난 이 년 동안 그는 출판사에서 일하며 유럽 언어 세 가지에 능통했는데, 엿새 전에 라스콜니코프에게는 자신이 독일어가 \"취약하다\"고 말한 적이 있었으니, 그것은 번역의 절반과 그 보수의 절반을 라스콜니코프가 맡도록 설득하려는 속셈에서였다. 그때 그는 거짓말을 한 것이었고, 라스콜니코프도 그가 거짓말하고 있다는 걸 알고 있었다.\n\n\"아니, 왜, 성공의 가장 중요한 수단 가운데 하나인 자기 자본이 있는데도 우리가 이 기회를 놓쳐야 합니까!\" 라주미힌이 열을 올려 외쳤다. \"물론 일거리야 많겠지만, 우리가 일하면 됩니다, 당신이, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 그리고 저, 로지온이…… 요즘은 어떤 책으로는 굉장한 이윤을 거둘 수 있어요! 그리고 이 사업의 핵심은, 우리가 무엇을 번역해야 할지 정확히 알게 되리라는 점이고, 우리가 번역하고, 출판하고, 익히는 일을 한꺼번에 해낸다는 겁니다. 저는 경험이 있으니 쓸모가 있을 거예요. 거의 이 년 동안 출판업자들 틈을 분주히 누비고 다녔으니, 이제 그쪽 일을 세세한 데까지 다 압니다. 단지를 빚는 데 성인(聖人)이 될 필요는 없는 법이죠, 믿어 주십시오! 그런데 왜, 왜 우리가 이 기회를 놓쳐야 합니까! 글쎄요, 저는 알고 있어요 — 비밀로 간직해 왔지만 — 그저 번역해서 출판할 생각만 해도 백 루블쯤은 거뜬히 받아 낼 만한 책이 두세 권 있다는 걸 말입니다. 정말이지, 그중 한 권에 대한 착상만 가지고도 저는 오백 루블을 줘도 넘기지 않을 겁니다. 그런데 어떨 것 같습니까? 제가 출판업자에게 말한들, 아마 그자는 망설이기나 하겠죠 — 그들은 어찌나 멍청한지! 그리고 사업적인 면, 그러니까 인쇄며 종이며 판매 같은 건 저를 믿고 맡겨 주십시오, 그쪽 길은 제가 훤히 압니다. 우리는 작게 시작해서 크게 키워 나가는 겁니다. 어떻게 되든 이 일이 우리 입에 풀칠을 시켜 줄 것이고, 우리 자본도 되찾게 될 거예요.\"\n\n두냐의 눈이 빛났다.\n\n\"당신 말씀이 마음에 들어요, 드미트리 프로코피치!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"나야 물론 그런 건 통 모르지만,\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 끼어들었다. \"좋은 생각일 수도 있겠지요, 하지만 그것도 하느님만 아실 일이에요. 새롭고 또 해 본 적 없는 일이니까요. 물론, 우리는 적어도 한동안은 여기 머물러야겠지요.\" 그녀는 로쟈를 바라보았다.\n\n\"오빠는 어떻게 생각해요?\" 두냐가 물었다.\n\n\"내가 보기엔 아주 좋은 생각이야.\" 그가 대답했다. \"물론 출판사를 차리겠다는 꿈을 꾸기엔 너무 이르지만, 다섯 권이나 여섯 권쯤은 분명 펴낼 수 있을 테고, 그 성공은 틀림없을 거다. 나도 분명 잘 팔릴 책 한 권을 알고 있어. 그리고 저 친구가 그 일을 잘 꾸려 갈 수 있는가 하면, 그것도 의심할 여지가 없지. 저 친구는 그 일을 잘 아니까…… 하지만 그건 나중에 의논해 보면 돼……\"\n\n\"만세!\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다. \"자, 가만, 이 건물 안에 셋방이 하나 있는데, 같은 주인 소유랍니다. 여기 이 하숙과는 통하지 않는, 따로 떨어진 별채 같은 셋방이에요. 가구도 딸려 있고, 집세도 적당하고, 방이 셋입니다. 우선 그걸 빌리시는 게 어떨까요. 제가 내일 당신네 시계를 저당 잡혀 돈을 마련해 드릴 테니, 그러면 모든 게 다 처리될 겁니다. 세 분이 함께 사실 수 있고, 로쟈도 여러분과 함께 있게 되는 거죠. 그런데 어디 가는 거야, 로쟈?\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 로쟈, 벌써 가려고?\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 당황하여 물었다.\n\n\"이런 순간에?\" 라주미힌이 외쳤다.\n\n두냐는 믿기지 않는다는 듯 놀라며 오빠를 바라보았다. 그는 모자를 손에 쥔 채, 그들을 두고 떠날 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n\"마치 나를 묻으러 가거나 영영 작별 인사라도 하는 것 같구나.\" 그가 어딘지 묘하게 말했다. 그는 미소 지으려 했으나, 그것은 미소가 되지 못했다. \"하지만 누가 알겠어, 어쩌면 우리가 서로를 보는 게 이번이 마지막일지도……\" 그가 무심코 말을 흘렸다. 그것은 그가 생각하고 있던 바였는데, 어쩌다 소리 내어 입 밖으로 나와 버린 것이었다.\n\n\"너 무슨 일이니?\" 그의 어머니가 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디 가는 거예요, 로쟈?\" 두냐가 다소 이상하다는 듯 물었다.\n\n\"아, 난 꼭 가야만 해서……\" 그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 머뭇거리듯 막연하게 대답했다. 그러나 핏기 없는 그의 얼굴에는 날카로운 결의의 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"내가 하려던 말은…… 여기로 오는 길에…… 어머니, 그리고 두냐, 너에게 말하려던 건, 우리가 한동안 떨어져 지내는 편이 좋겠다는 거였어. 난 몸이 아프고, 마음이 편치 않아…… 나중에 갈게, 내 발로 찾아갈게…… 그게 가능해지면. 나는 어머니와 너를 기억하고 사랑해…… 날 내버려 둬, 나를 혼자 있게 해 줘. 난 이걸 그전부터 이미 결심했어…… 절대로 마음을 바꾸지 않을 거야. 나에게 무슨 일이 닥치든, 내가 파멸하든 말든, 나는 혼자 있고 싶어. 날 아주 잊어버려, 그게 더 나아. 내 소식은 묻지 마. 가능해지면 내가 직접 가거나…… 사람을 보낼게. 어쩌면 모든 게 제자리로 돌아올지도 몰라, 하지만 지금은, 나를 사랑한다면, 나를 단념해 줘…… 안 그러면 난 어머니와 너를 미워하기 시작할 거야, 그게 느껴져…… 안녕히!\"\n\n\"오, 하느님!\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. 그의 어머니와 누이 모두 끔찍하게 놀랐다. 라주미힌도 마찬가지였다.\n\n\"로쟈, 로쟈, 우리와 화해하자! 예전처럼 지내자꾸나!\" 가엾은 어머니가 외쳤다.\n\n그는 천천히 문 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 천천히 방을 나섰다. 두냐가 그를 따라잡았다.\n\n\"오빠, 어머니한테 무슨 짓을 하는 거예요?\" 그녀가 분노로 눈을 번득이며 속삭였다.\n\n그는 멍하니 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"괜찮아, 갈 테니까…… 갈게.\" 그가 마치 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 온전히 의식하지 못하는 듯 낮은 목소리로 중얼거리고는, 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"못되고 무정한 이기주의자!\" 두냐가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 친구는 제정신이 아니에요, 하지만 무정한 건 아닙니다. 미친 거예요! 그게 안 보이십니까? 그런 말을 하다니, 당신이야말로 무정하군요!\" 라주미힌이 그녀의 귀에 대고 속삭이며, 그녀의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐었다. \"곧 돌아오겠습니다.\" 그가 공포에 질린 어머니에게 소리치고는, 방 밖으로 뛰어나갔다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 복도 끝에서 그를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"네가 나를 쫓아 달려 나올 줄 알았어.\" 그가 말했다. \"그분들께 돌아가, 그분들과 함께 있어 줘…… 내일도, 그리고 언제까지나 그분들 곁에 있어 줘…… 나는…… 어쩌면 내가 갈지도 몰라…… 갈 수 있으면. 잘 있어.\"\n\n그러고는 손도 내밀지 않은 채 그는 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n\"그런데 어디로 가는 거야? 뭘 하려는 거야? 너 무슨 일이야? 어떻게 이런 식으로 계속 가 버릴 수가 있어?\" 라주미힌이 어쩔 줄 몰라 중얼거렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프가 다시 한번 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"단번에 말해 두는데, 나에 대해서는 절대 아무것도 묻지 마. 너에게 들려줄 말은 아무것도 없어. 나를 보러 오지도 마. 어쩌면 내가 여기로 올지도 모르지…… 나는 내버려 두고, 그분들은 떠나지 마. 내 말 알아듣겠어?\"\n\n복도 안은 어두웠고, 그들은 등불 가까이에 서 있었다. 한순간 그들은 말없이 서로를 바라보았다. 라주미힌은 평생토록 그 순간을 기억했다. 라스콜니코프의 불타는 듯 골똘한 두 눈이 매 순간 점점 더 꿰뚫는 듯해져, 그의 영혼 속으로, 그의 의식 속으로 파고들었다. 갑자기 라주미힌이 움찔했다. 무언가 기이한 것이, 말하자면 두 사람 사이를 스쳐 지나갔다…… 어떤 생각이, 어떤 암시 같은 것이 새어 나왔다. 무언가 무섭고, 흉측하며, 양쪽 모두가 불현듯 깨닫게 된 그 무엇이…… 라주미힌의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다.\n\n\"이제 알겠어?\" 라스콜니코프가 신경질적으로 얼굴을 씰룩거리며 말했다. \"돌아가, 그분들께 가.\" 그가 갑자기 말하더니, 잽싸게 몸을 돌려 건물 밖으로 나가 버렸다.\n\n라주미힌이 어떻게 부인들에게 돌아갔는지, 어떻게 그들을 달랬는지, 로쟈가 병중이라 안정이 필요하다고 어떻게 항변했는지, 로쟈가 틀림없이 올 것이고, 매일 올 것이며, 그가 무척, 무척이나 심란해 있을 뿐이니, 그를 자극해서는 안 된다고, 자기 라주미힌이 그를 보살피고, 의사를, 최고의 의사를 불러와 진찰을 받게 하겠다고 어떻게 항변했는지…… 그 모든 것을 나는 굳이 묘사하지 않으려 한다. 요컨대 그날 저녁부터 라주미힌은 아들이자 형제로서 그들 곁에 자리를 잡게 되었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n라스콜니코프는 소냐가 사는 운하 강가의 집으로 곧장 걸어갔다. 그것은 삼 층짜리 낡은 초록색 집이었다. 그는 문지기를 찾아내 재단사 카페르나우모프가 어디쯤 사는지에 관해 막연한 설명을 얻어 냈다. 안뜰 한구석에서 어둡고 좁은 계단으로 통하는 입구를 찾아낸 그는 이 층으로 올라가, 안뜰 위로 이 층 전체를 빙 두르고 있는 복도로 나왔다. 카페르나우모프의 문을 어디서 찾아야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 어둠 속을 헤매고 있는데, 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 문 하나가 열렸다. 그는 기계적으로 그 문을 붙들었다.\n\n\"누구세요?\" 한 여자의 목소리가 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"나요…… 당신을 보러 왔소.\" 라스콜니코프는 대답하고, 비좁은 현관으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n부서진 의자 위에는 우그러진 구리 촛대에 양초 하나가 꽂혀 있었다.\n\n\"당신이라니! 어머나, 세상에!\" 소냐가 가냘프게 외치며, 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다.\n\n\"당신 방은 어느 쪽이오? 이쪽인가?\" 라스콜니코프는 그녀를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 서둘러 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n잠시 후 소냐도 촛불을 들고 들어와 촛대를 내려놓고는, 완전히 어쩔 줄 모르는 채 그의 앞에 서 있었는데, 이루 말할 수 없이 동요하고 그의 뜻밖의 방문에 분명 겁을 먹은 듯했다. 갑자기 그녀의 창백한 얼굴로 핏기가 확 솟구쳤고 두 눈에는 눈물이 고였다……. 그녀는 메스껍고 부끄러우면서도, 또한 행복했다……. 라스콜니코프는 재빨리 몸을 돌려 탁자 옆 의자에 앉았다. 그는 재빠른 눈길로 방 안을 훑었다.\n\n그것은 넓지만 천장이 지독히도 낮은 방으로, 카페르나우모프 식구들이 세놓은 단 하나의 방이었고, 왼쪽 벽에는 그들의 방으로 통하는 닫힌 문이 나 있었다. 그 맞은편 오른쪽 벽에는 또 다른 문이 있었는데, 늘 잠겨 있었다. 그 문은 별개의 거처를 이루는 옆 셋집으로 통했다. 소냐의 방은 헛간 같았다. 그것은 몹시 일그러진 사각형이어서 기괴한 인상을 주었다. 운하 쪽으로 난 세 개의 창이 있는 벽이 비스듬히 뻗어 있어 한쪽 구석은 아주 예리한 각을 이루었고, 그래서 아주 강한 빛이 아니고서는 그 안을 들여다보기가 어려웠다. 다른 쪽 구석은 균형이 맞지 않게 둔한 각이었다. 그 큰 방에는 가구라고 할 만한 것이 거의 없었다. 오른쪽 구석에는 침대가 있었고, 그 곁의, 문에서 가장 가까운 자리에는 의자가 하나 있었다. 푸른 천을 덮은 평범한 전나무 탁자가 같은 벽에, 옆 셋집으로 통하는 문 가까이에 놓여 있었다. 골풀로 엮은 의자 두 개가 탁자 옆에 있었다. 맞은편 벽, 예각을 이룬 구석 가까이에는 작고 수수한 나무 서랍장이 하나 서 있었는데, 마치 사막에 버려진 듯 보였다. 방 안에 있는 것은 그게 전부였다. 누렇게 바래고 긁히고 낡아 빠진 벽지는 구석마다 거뭇했다. 겨울이면 분명 눅눅하고 매캐한 냄새로 가득했으리라. 온통 가난의 흔적이었다. 침대에조차 휘장 하나 없었다.\n\n소냐는 자기 방을 이토록 주의 깊게, 또 거리낌 없이 살피는 방문객을 말없이 바라보았고, 마침내는 마치 자신의 심판관이자 운명의 결정자 앞에 서 있기라도 한 듯 공포에 떨기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 늦었군요……. 열한 시인가요?\" 그는 여전히 눈을 들지 않은 채 물었다.\n\n\"네.\" 소냐가 중얼거렸다. \"아, 네, 그래요.\" 그녀는 마치 그 안에 빠져나갈 구실이 있기라도 한 듯 서둘러 덧붙였다. \"방금 주인집 시계가 쳤어요……. 제 귀로 들었거든요…….\"\n\n\"이렇게 당신을 보러 오는 것도 마지막이오.\" 라스콜니코프는 우울하게 말을 이었다. 사실 이번이 처음이었는데도. \"어쩌면 당신을 다시는 못 볼지도 모르오…….\"\n\n\"어디…… 떠나시는 건가요?\"\n\n\"모르겠소……. 내일…….\"\n\n\"그럼 내일 카테리나 이바노브나에게 안 오시는 건가요?\" 소냐의 목소리가 떨렸다.\n\n\"모르겠소. 내일 아침이면 알게 되겠지……. 그건 그렇고, 한마디 하러 왔소…….\"\n\n그는 수심에 잠긴 두 눈을 들어 그녀를 보다가, 문득 자기는 앉아 있는데 그녀는 내내 그 앞에 서 있다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"왜 서 있소? 앉으시오.\" 그가 달라진 목소리로, 부드럽고 다정하게 말했다.\n\n그녀가 앉았다. 그는 다정하게, 거의 측은해하는 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"어쩜 이렇게 야위었소! 이 손 좀 봐! 아주 투명하군, 죽은 사람 손 같아.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 소냐는 희미하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"저는 늘 이랬어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"집에 살 때도?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지, 물론.\" 그는 불쑥 덧붙였고, 그의 표정과 목소리가 또다시 갑자기 변했다.\n\n그는 다시 한번 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"이 방을 카페르나우모프 식구들한테서 세 들었소?\"\n\n\"네…….\"\n\n\"저들이 저기, 저 문 너머에 사는 거요?\"\n\n\"네……. 저들은 이런 방을 하나 더 갖고 있어요.\"\n\n\"식구가 다 한방에서?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"나라면 밤에 당신 방에 있는 게 무서울 것 같소.\" 그가 우울하게 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋은 사람들이에요, 무척 친절하고요.\" 소냐가 대답했는데, 여전히 어리둥절한 듯했다. \"그리고 가구며 모든 것이…… 전부 저들 거예요. 그리고 다들 무척 친절하고, 아이들도 자주 절 보러 와요.\"\n\n\"다들 말을 더듬는다죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"네……. 그분은 말을 더듬고 다리도 절어요. 그분 부인도요……. 정확히 더듬는다기보다는, 또렷이 말을 못 하는 거예요. 아주 친절한 분이죠. 그리고 그분은 전에 농노였대요. 그리고 아이가 일곱인데…… 말을 더듬는 건 맏이뿐이고 다른 애들은 그냥 병약할 뿐이에요…… 더듬지는 않아요……. 그런데 저 사람들 얘기는 어디서 들으셨어요?\" 그녀가 다소 놀라며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그럼 당신 아버지가 내게 말해 줬소. 당신에 관해 죄다 얘기해 줬지……. 그리고 당신이 여섯 시에 나가 아홉 시에 돌아오고, 카테리나 이바노브나가 당신 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다는 것까지도.\"\n\n소냐는 당황했다.\n\n\"오늘 그분을 본 것 같았어요.\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 속삭였다.\n\n\"누구를?\"\n\n\"아버지요. 거리를 걷고 있었는데, 저쪽 모퉁이에서, 열 시쯤이었어요, 그런데 그분이 앞서 걸어가는 것 같았어요. 꼭 그분처럼 보였거든요. 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가고 싶었어요…….\"\n\n\"거리를 걷고 있었다고?\"\n\n\"네.\" 소냐가 불쑥 속삭였는데, 또다시 당혹감에 휩싸여 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나가 당신을 때리곤 했겠지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요? 아니에요!\" 소냐는 거의 경악한 듯 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그럼 그 여자를 사랑하오?\"\n\n\"그분을 사랑하느냐고요? 당연하죠!\" 소냐가 애절하게 힘주어 말하며, 괴로운 듯 두 손을 맞잡았다. \"아, 모르셔서 그래요……. 아신다면! 그분은 정말 아이 같은 분이에요……. 그분 마음은 완전히 무너져 버렸어요, 그러니까…… 슬픔 때문에요. 게다가 얼마나 영리한 분이었는데요…… 얼마나 너그럽고요…… 얼마나 다정하고요! 아, 모르세요, 모르신다고요!\"\n\n소냐는 마치 절망에 빠진 듯 이렇게 말하며, 흥분과 괴로움에 두 손을 비틀었다. 창백한 두 뺨이 발갛게 달아올랐고, 두 눈에는 고뇌의 빛이 어렸다. 그녀가 마음속 깊은 곳까지 흔들렸음이, 무언가를 말하고, 변호하고, 토로하고 싶어 애태우고 있음이 분명했다. 이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 일종의 채울 수 없는 연민이 그녀 얼굴의 모든 결에 비쳤다.\n\n\"저를 때리다니요! 어떻게 그런 말씀을? 세상에, 저를 때려요! 설령 때렸다 한들, 그게 어쨌다는 거예요? 그게 무슨 대수죠? 당신은 아무것도, 그 일에 관해 아무것도 모르세요……. 그분은 너무도 불행해요…… 아, 얼마나 불행한지! 게다가 병까지 들었고요……. 그분은 정의를 구하고 있고, 순결한 분이에요. 어디에나 정의가 있어야 한다고 그토록 굳게 믿으면서, 그걸 기다리고 있어요……. 설령 그분을 고문한다 해도, 그분은 옳지 않은 일은 하지 않을 거예요. 사람들이 정의롭기란 불가능하다는 걸 보지 못하고, 그래서 그것에 화를 내요. 아이처럼, 꼭 아이처럼요. 그분은 선해요!\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 어떻게 되는 거요?\"\n\n소냐는 묻고 싶은 듯한 눈길로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저들이 당신 손에 떠넘겨진 거잖소. 하긴 전에도 다 당신 손에 매달려 있었지만……. 게다가 당신 아버지는 술값을 구걸하러 당신에게 왔고. 자, 이제 어떻게 될 거요?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 소냐가 구슬프게 말했다.\n\n\"저들이 거기 계속 있을 거요?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요……. 집세가 밀려 있는데, 주인 여자가 오늘 저들을 내쫓고 싶다고 말했다더라고요, 그리고 카테리나 이바노브나는 단 일 분도 더 머물지 않겠다고 하고요.\"\n\n\"그 여자가 어떻게 그리 대담한 거요? 당신을 믿고 그러는 거요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요……. 우리는 하나예요, 한 몸처럼 살아요.\" 소냐는 다시 동요하며, 심지어 화까지 냈는데, 마치 카나리아나 다른 작은 새가 화를 내는 것 같았다. \"그리고 그분이 뭘 할 수 있겠어요? 뭘, 대체 뭘 할 수 있겠어요?\" 그녀는 얼굴이 달아오르고 흥분하며 우겼다. \"그리고 오늘은 얼마나 우셨는지! 그분 마음은 무너져 버렸어요, 못 알아차리셨어요? 어느 순간엔 아이처럼 안달하며 내일은 모든 게 잘되어야 한다고, 점심이며 그런 것들이며……. 그러다가는 두 손을 비틀며 피를 토하고, 울다가, 갑자기 절망에 빠져 벽에 머리를 찧기 시작해요. 그러다 또 마음을 가라앉히고요. 그분은 온 희망을 당신에게 걸고 있어요. 이제 당신이 자기를 도와줄 거라고, 그래서 어디선가 돈을 좀 빌려 저와 함께 고향으로 가서 양갓집 딸들을 위한 기숙학교를 세우고, 저를 데려가 그 일을 맡길 거라고, 그렇게 멋진 새 삶을 시작할 거라고 말해요. 그러고는 저를 입 맞추고 끌어안고, 위로해 주는데, 그 공상을 어찌나 굳게, 굳게 믿는지! 도저히 거스를 수가 없어요. 그리고 하루 종일 빨래하고, 청소하고, 깁고 했어요. 그 약한 손으로 빨래통을 방으로 끌고 들어오고는, 숨을 헐떡이며 침대에 쓰러졌어요. 오늘 아침엔 폴렌카와 리다 신발을 사러 가게에 갔어요, 그애들 신발이 완전히 다 닳았거든요. 그런데 우리가 헤아렸던 돈으로는 모자랐어요, 어림도 없이 모자랐죠. 그래도 그분은 그렇게 예쁘고 자그마한 부츠를 골랐어요, 안목이 있으시거든요, 당신은 모르시겠지만. 그러고는 거기 가게에서, 점원들 앞에서, 돈이 모자라다고 울음을 터뜨렸어요……. 아, 그 모습을 보니 너무 서글펐어요…….\"\n\n\"그런 얘기를 들으니, 당신이 이렇게 사는 까닭을 알 만하군.\" 라스콜니코프가 쓰디쓴 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 당신은 저들이 가엾지 않으세요? 가엾지 않으세요?\" 소냐가 다시 그에게 달려들었다. \"아니, 저는 알아요, 당신은 그 모습을 아무것도 보지 못했으면서도 마지막 한 푼까지 직접 내주셨잖아요, 만일 다 보셨더라면, 오, 세상에! 그리고 제가 얼마나 자주, 얼마나 자주 그분을 울렸는데요! 바로 지난주에도요! 그래요, 제가요! 아버지가 돌아가시기 꼭 일주일 전에요. 저는 잔인했어요! 그것도 얼마나 자주 그랬는지! 아, 온종일 그 생각에 비참했어요!\"\n\n소냐는 그것을 떠올리는 고통에 말하면서 두 손을 비틀었다.\n\n\"당신이 잔인했다고?\"\n\n\"네, 제가, 제가요. 저들을 보러 갔어요.\" 그녀는 울면서 말을 이었다. \"그런데 아버지가 그러셨어요. '소냐, 뭐 좀 읽어 다오, 머리가 아프구나, 내게 읽어 주렴, 여기 책이 있다.' 그분은 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프에게서 얻은 책을 갖고 계셨어요, 그분은 거기 사는데, 늘 그런 우스꽝스러운 책들을 구해 오곤 했죠. 그래서 저는 '못 있어요'라고 했어요, 읽고 싶지 않았거든요, 게다가 주로 카테리나 이바노브나에게 깃 몇 개를 보여 주려고 들렀던 거였어요. 행상하는 리자베타가 깃이랑 소맷동을 싸게 팔았거든요, 예쁘고, 새것에, 수가 놓인 것들로요. 카테리나 이바노브나가 그걸 무척 마음에 들어 했어요. 그걸 달고 거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 보며 아주 기뻐했죠. '소냐, 그걸 내게 선물로 다오.' 그분이 말했어요. '제발 그래 다오.' 그렇게요, 그걸 무척 갖고 싶어 했어요. 그런데 그걸 언제 달아 볼 수 있겠어요? 그저 옛날 행복했던 시절을 떠올리게 했을 뿐이죠. 거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 보며 흐뭇해했어요, 그분은 옷이라곤 하나도 없어요, 자기 물건이 하나도 없어요, 이 여러 해 동안 한 번도요! 그리고 누구한테 뭘 달라고 하는 법이 없어요, 자존심이 강해서 차라리 모든 걸 다 내주려고 하죠. 그런데 그걸 달라고 했어요, 그토록 마음에 들어 했거든요. 그런데 저는 그걸 주기가 아까웠어요. '그게 카테리나 이바노브나한테 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?' 제가 말했어요. 그분에게 그렇게 말했어요, 그런 말을 하지 말았어야 했는데! 그분이 저를 그런 눈으로 바라봤어요. 그리고 너무도 슬퍼했어요, 제가 거절한 것을 너무도 슬퍼했어요. 그 모습을 보니 정말 서글펐어요……. 그분이 슬퍼한 건 깃 때문이 아니라 제가 거절했기 때문이었어요, 저는 그걸 알았어요. 아, 그 모든 걸 되돌릴 수만 있다면, 바꿀 수만 있다면, 그 말들을 되삼킬 수만 있다면! 아, 만일 제가…… 하지만 그게 당신한테 무슨 상관이겠어요!\"\n\n\"행상하는 리자베타를 알았소?\"\n\n\"네……. 그분을 아셨어요?\" 소냐가 다소 놀라며 물었다.\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나는 폐병에 걸렸소, 급성 폐병이오. 곧 죽을 거요.\" 라스콜니코프는 그녀의 물음에는 답하지 않은 채 잠시 뜸을 들이고 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요!\"\n그리고 소냐는 그러지 말아 달라고 애원이라도 하듯, 무심결에 그의 두 손을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"하지만 차라리 죽는 게 나을 거요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 나을 게 없어요, 전혀 나을 게 없어요!\" 소냐가 경악하여 무심결에 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그럼 아이들은? 당신과 함께 데리고 사는 것 말고 뭘 할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 모르겠어요.\" 소냐가 거의 절망하여 외치며, 두 손으로 머리를 감쌌다.\n\n그 생각이 전에도 그녀에게 자주 떠올랐었고, 그가 그저 그것을 다시 일깨운 것임이 분명했다.\n\n\"그럼, 만일 지금이라도, 카테리나 이바노브나가 살아 있는 동안에, 당신이 병이 들어 병원에 실려 가면, 그땐 어떻게 되는 거요?\" 그가 무자비하게 우겼다.\n\n\"어떻게 그런 말씀을? 그럴 리 없어요!\"\n\n그리고 소냐의 얼굴이 끔찍한 공포로 일그러졌다.\n\n\"그럴 리 없다고?\" 라스콜니코프가 모진 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"당신은 그걸 막을 보험이라도 들었소? 그럼 저들은 어떻게 되는 거요? 길거리에 나앉게 되겠지, 죄다, 그 여자는 기침을 하며 구걸하고 오늘처럼 어디 벽에다 머리를 찧을 거고, 아이들은 울어 댈 거요……. 그러다 그 여자는 쓰러져 경찰서로, 또 병원으로 끌려가 죽을 거고, 그럼 아이들은…….\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요……. 하느님께서 그렇게 두지 않으실 거예요!\" 마침내 소냐의 짓눌린 가슴에서 터져 나왔다.\n\n그녀는 마치 모든 것이 그에게 달려 있기라도 한 듯, 애원하는 눈길로 그를 바라보며, 말없는 간청 속에 두 손을 맞잡고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 일어나 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다. 일 분이 흘렀다. 소냐는 두 손과 머리를 늘어뜨린 채 끔찍한 낙담에 빠져 서 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 저축은 못 하오? 어려운 날을 대비해 떼어 두는 거 말이오?\" 그가 갑자기 그녀 앞에 멈춰 서며 물었다.\n\n\"못 해요.\" 소냐가 속삭였다.\n\n\"물론 못 하겠지. 해 보긴 했소?\" 그가 거의 빈정대듯 덧붙였다.\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그런데 안 됐겠지! 물론 그랬을 거고! 물어볼 것도 없군.\"\n\n그러고는 다시 방 안을 거닐었다. 또 일 분이 흘렀다.\n\n\"날마다 돈을 받는 건 아니지?\"\n\n소냐는 그 어느 때보다 당황했고, 다시 얼굴로 핏기가 솟구쳤다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 괴로운 듯 안간힘을 쓰며 속삭였다.\n\n\"폴렌카도 분명 같은 신세가 되겠군.\" 그가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요! 그럴 리 없어요, 아니에요!\" 소냐가, 마치 칼에 찔리기라도 한 듯 절망하여 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"하느님께서 그토록 끔찍한 일을 허락하지 않으실 거예요!\" \"하느님은 다른 사람들도 그 지경에 이르게 두시잖아.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼! 하느님이 그 애를 지켜 주실 거예요, 하느님이!\" 그녀가 제정신이 아닌 듯 되뇌었다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 하느님 같은 건 아예 없는지도 모르죠.\" 라스콜니코프가 일종의 악의를 품고 대꾸하더니 웃으며 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n소냐의 얼굴이 갑자기 변했다. 전율이 그 위로 스쳐 지나갔다. 그녀는 이루 말할 수 없는 비난의 눈길로 그를 바라보았고, 무언가 말하려 했으나 입을 떼지 못한 채 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 쓰디쓰게, 쓰디쓰게 흐느껴 울었다.\n\n\"당신은 카테리나 이바노브나의 마음이 무너졌다고 했지만, 당신 마음도 무너졌소.\" 그가 짧은 침묵 끝에 말했다.\n\n오 분이 흘렀다. 그는 여전히 말없이 방 안을 오르내리며 그녀를 바라보지 않았다. 마침내 그가 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그의 두 눈이 번득였다. 그는 두 손을 그녀의 어깨에 얹고 눈물 젖은 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 그의 눈은 모질고, 열에 들뜨고, 꿰뚫는 듯했고, 입술은 실룩거렸다. 갑자기 그가 재빨리 몸을 숙여 바닥에 엎드리더니 그녀의 발에 입을 맞췄다. 소냐는 미친 사람한테서 물러나듯 그에게서 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 정말이지 그는 미친 사람 같아 보였다.\n\n\"제게 왜 이러세요?\" 그녀가 창백해지며 중얼거렸고, 별안간 고뇌가 그녀의 가슴을 움켜쥐었다.\n\n그는 곧 일어섰다.\n\n\"나는 당신에게 머리를 숙인 게 아니라, 인류의 모든 고통 앞에 머리를 숙인 거요.\" 그가 광기 어린 어조로 말하고는 창가로 걸어갔다. \"들어 보시오.\" 그가 잠시 후 그녀에게 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"나는 방금 어떤 오만한 자에게 그는 당신 새끼손가락만 한 가치도 없다고 말했소…… 그리고 당신 곁에 내 누이를 앉게 한 것은 그애에게 영광이라고 말했소.\"\n\n\"아, 저들에게 그런 말씀을 하셨다고요! 게다가 그분 앞에서요?\" 소냐가 겁에 질려 외쳤다. \"저랑 같이 앉다니요! 영광이라니! 아니, 저는…… 떳떳지 못한 몸인데……. 아, 왜 그런 말씀을 하셨어요?\"\n\n\"내가 당신을 두고 그렇게 말한 건 당신의 치욕이나 죄 때문이 아니라, 당신의 그 크나큰 고통 때문이오. 하지만 당신이 큰 죄인인 건 사실이오.\" 그가 거의 엄숙하게 덧붙였다. \"그리고 당신의 가장 큰 죄는 아무 보람도 없이 자기 자신을 망치고 배신했다는 거요. 그게 끔찍하지 않소? 당신이 그토록 혐오하는 이 오물 속에 살면서, 동시에 (그저 눈만 뜨면 알 수 있을 텐데) 그걸로 그 누구도 돕지 못하고, 그 누구도 무엇으로부터 구하지 못한다는 걸 스스로 알고 있다는 게 끔찍하지 않냐고? 말해 보시오.\" 그가 거의 광란에 빠진 듯 말을 이었다. \"이 수치와 타락이 어떻게 당신 안에서 다른, 정반대의, 성스러운 감정들과 나란히 공존할 수 있는 거요? 차라리 물속으로 뛰어들어 모든 걸 끝내는 편이 천 배는 더 낫고 현명할 거요!\"\n\n\"하지만 그럼 저들은 어떻게 되나요?\" 소냐가 고뇌에 찬 눈으로 그를 응시하면서도 그의 제안에 놀라는 기색은 없이, 힘없이 물었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 묘한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그는 그녀의 얼굴에서 모든 것을 읽어 냈다. 그러니까 그녀는 이미 그런 생각을, 어쩌면 여러 번 했던 게 분명했고, 절망 속에서 어떻게 모든 걸 끝낼지를 진지하게, 너무도 진지하게 곰곰이 생각해 온 나머지, 이제 그의 제안에도 거의 놀라지 않은 것이다. 그녀는 그의 말이 잔인하다는 것조차 알아차리지 못했다. (그의 비난이 지닌 뜻도, 자기 치욕에 대한 그의 묘한 태도도 그녀는 물론 알아차리지 못했고, 그 또한 그에게는 분명했다.) 하지만 그는 자신의 치욕스럽고 부끄러운 처지에 대한 생각이 얼마나 끔찍하게 그녀를 괴롭히고, 또 오랫동안 괴롭혀 왔는지를 보았다. '대체 무엇이,' 그는 생각했다. '지금까지 그녀가 이걸 끝내지 못하도록 막아 왔단 말인가?' 그제야 그는 저 가엾고 어린 고아들과, 폐병을 앓으며 벽에 머리를 찧는 저 가련하고 반쯤 미친 카테리나 이바노브나가 소냐에게 어떤 의미인지를 깨달았다.\n\n그러나 그럼에도, 그녀의 성품과 어쨌든 그녀가 받았던 그만큼의 교육으로 보아 어떤 경우든 그녀가 이대로 머물 수는 없으리라는 것이 그에게는 다시금 분명해졌다. 여전히 그의 앞에는 이런 의문이 놓여 있었으니, 물속으로 뛰어들 결심조차 하지 못하면서, 어떻게 그녀가 미쳐 버리지 않고 이토록 오래 그 처지에 머물 수 있었단 말인가? 물론 그는 소냐의 처지가 예외적인 경우임을 알았는데, 안타깝게도 유일무이한 것도, 실제로 드문 것도 아니었지만. 그러나 바로 그 예외성, 그녀가 받은 약간의 교육, 그녀의 지난 삶이, 생각건대 그 역겨운 길로 들어선 첫걸음에서 그녀를 죽여 버렸어야 마땅했다. 무엇이 그녀를 떠받쳐 왔단 말인가, 설마 타락은 아니겠지? 그 모든 치욕은 분명 기계적으로 그녀를 스쳐 갔을 뿐, 진짜 타락은 단 한 방울도 그녀의 마음속으로 스며들지 않았다. 그는 그것을 보았다. 그 앞에 서 있는 그녀를 그는 꿰뚫어 보았다…….\n\n'그녀 앞에는 세 가지 길이 있다.' 그는 생각했다. '운하, 정신병원, 아니면…… 끝내는 마음을 흐리게 하고 심장을 돌로 만드는 타락에 빠져드는 것.'\n\n마지막 생각이 가장 역겨웠지만, 그는 회의주의자였고, 젊었고, 추상적이었으며, 따라서 잔인했으니, 그래서 그 마지막 결말이 가장 그럴 법하다고 믿지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n'하지만 그게 정말일 수 있을까?' 그는 속으로 외쳤다. '여태껏 영혼의 순결함을 지켜 온 저 존재가 결국 그 오물과 죄악의 구렁텅이로 의식적으로 끌려 들어갈 수 있단 말인가? 그 과정이 이미 시작된 건 아닐까? 그녀가 지금까지 견딜 수 있었던 것은, 단지 악덕이 그녀에게 덜 역겨워지기 시작했기 때문은 아닐까? 아니야, 아니야, 그럴 리 없어!' 그는 방금 전 소냐가 그랬듯 외쳤다. '아니야, 지금까지 그녀를 운하에서 막아 온 건 죄에 대한 관념과, 저들, 저 아이들이야……. 그리고 만일 그녀가 미쳐 버리지 않았다면…… 하지만 그녀가 미치지 않았다고 누가 말하는가? 그녀가 제정신인가? 그녀처럼 말하고, 그녀처럼 따질 수 있는 게 제정신일까? 미끄러져 들어가고 있는 그 역겨움의 심연 가장자리에 어떻게 저렇게 걸터앉아, 위험하다는 말을 들으면서도 귀 기울이기를 거부할 수 있단 말인가? 기적이라도 바라는 걸까? 분명 그럴 거야. 그게 다 미친 게 아니고 뭐란 말인가?'\n\n그는 고집스레 그 생각에 매달렸다. 사실 그는 다른 어떤 설명보다도 그 설명이 마음에 들었다. 그는 더 골똘히 그녀를 바라보기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래서 당신은 하느님께 기도를 많이 하오, 소냐?\" 그가 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n소냐는 말이 없었다. 그는 그녀 곁에 서서 대답을 기다렸다.\n\n\"하느님이 안 계시면 제가 어떻게 되겠어요?\" 그녀는 갑자기 번득이는 눈으로 그를 흘끗 보며, 그의 손을 꽉 쥐고는 빠르게, 힘주어 속삭였다.\n\n'아, 바로 그거였군!' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"그럼 하느님은 당신을 위해 무얼 해 주시오?\" 그가 그녀를 더 캐물었다.\n\n소냐는 마치 대답을 못 하기라도 하듯 오래도록 잠자코 있었다. 그녀의 가냘픈 가슴은 격정에 들먹였다.\n\n\"잠자코 계세요! 묻지 마세요! 당신은 그럴 자격이 없어요!\" 그녀가 엄하고 노여운 눈길로 그를 바라보며 갑자기 외쳤다.\n\n'그래, 바로 그거야, 바로 그거.' 그는 속으로 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그분은 모든 걸 해 주세요.\" 그녀가 다시 고개를 떨구며 빠르게 속삭였다.\n\n'바로 그게 출구로군! 바로 그게 설명이야.' 그는 새롭고, 낯설고, 거의 병적인 감정으로, 열렬한 호기심에 차서 그녀를 뜯어보며 단정 지었다. 그는 그 창백하고, 야위고, 일그러지고, 모난 작은 얼굴을, 그토록 불처럼, 그토록 엄한 기운으로 번득일 수 있는 그 부드러운 푸른 눈을, 여전히 분노와 노여움에 떨고 있는 그 작은 몸을 응시했다 — 그러자 그 모든 것이 점점 더 낯설게, 거의 있을 수 없는 일처럼 여겨졌다. '저 여자는 종교적 광신자야!' 그는 속으로 되풀이했다.\n\n서랍장 위에 책 한 권이 놓여 있었다. 그는 방 안을 오르내릴 때마다 그것을 보았다. 이제 그는 그것을 집어 들여다보았다. 그것은 러시아어로 번역된 신약성서였다. 가죽으로 장정된, 낡고 닳은 것이었다.\n\n\"이걸 어디서 났소?\" 그가 방 저편의 그녀에게 소리쳐 물었다.\n\n그녀는 여전히 같은 자리에, 탁자에서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에 서 있었다.\n\n\"누가 갖다줬어요.\" 그녀는 마지못한 듯, 그를 보지 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"누가 갖다줬소?\"\n\n\"리자베타요, 제가 부탁했거든요.\"\n\n'리자베타라니! 이상하군!' 그는 생각했다.\n\n소냐에 관한 모든 것이 그에게는 매 순간 점점 더 낯설고 기이하게 느껴졌다. 그는 책을 촛불 가까이 가져가 페이지를 넘기기 시작했다.\n\n\"나사로 이야기는 어디 있소?\" 그가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n소냐는 고집스레 바닥을 바라보며 대답하려 하지 않았다. 그녀는 탁자 옆에 모로 서 있었다.\n\n\"나사로를 살리는 대목 말이오. 그걸 찾아 주시오, 소냐.\"\n\n그녀가 그를 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다.\n\n\"엉뚱한 데서 찾고 계세요……. 그건 네 번째 복음서에 있어요.\" 그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채 엄하게 속삭였다.\n\n\"그걸 찾아서 내게 읽어 주시오.\" 그가 말했다. 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉아, 머리를 손에 기대고는, 들을 채비를 한 채 시무룩하게 딴 데를 바라보았다.\n\n'삼 주만 지나면 저들은 나를 정신병원에서 맞아 주겠지! 더 못한 데가 아니라면 거기에 있을 거고.' 그는 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n소냐는 라스콜니코프의 청을 미심쩍게 들으며 머뭇머뭇 탁자로 다가갔다. 그래도 그녀는 책을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"이걸 안 읽으셨어요?\" 그녀가 탁자 너머로 그를 올려다보며 물었다.\n\n그녀의 목소리가 점점 더 엄해졌다.\n\n\"오래전에…… 학교 다닐 때. 읽어 주시오!\"\n\n\"교회에서도 못 들으셨고요?\"\n\n\"난…… 안 갔으니까. 당신은 자주 가오?\"\n\n\"아, 아니요.\" 소냐가 속삭였다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 미소 지었다.\n\n\"알겠소……. 그럼 내일 당신 아버지 장례에도 안 가겠군?\"\n\n\"아니요, 갈 거예요. 지난주에도 교회에 갔었는데요…… 위령 미사를 드렸어요.\"\n\n\"누구를 위해?\"\n\n\"리자베타를 위해서요. 그분은 도끼에 살해당했어요.\"\n\n그의 신경은 점점 더 팽팽해졌다. 머리가 빙빙 돌기 시작했다.\n\n\"리자베타와 친했소?\"\n\n\"네……. 그분은 선했어요…… 절 보러 오곤 했어요…… 자주는 아니고요…… 그럴 수가 없었거든요……. 우리는 함께 책을 읽고…… 이야기하곤 했어요. 그분은 하느님을 뵐 거예요.\"\n\n마지막 말이 그의 귀에는 이상하게 들렸다. 게다가 또다시 새로운 무언가가 있었다. 리자베타와의 그 은밀한 만남들, 그리고 두 사람 다 — 종교적 광신자들이라니.\n\n'나도 곧 종교적 광신자가 되겠군! 이건 전염되는 거야!'\n\n\"읽어요!\" 그가 짜증스럽게, 고집스레 외쳤다.\n\n소냐는 여전히 머뭇거렸다. 그녀의 심장이 두근거렸다. 그녀는 그에게 읽어 주기가 좀처럼 엄두가 나지 않았다. 그는 거의 분통이 터지는 듯 그 '불행한 광인'을 바라보았다.\n\n\"무엇 때문에요? 당신은 믿지 않으시잖아요……?\" 그녀가 부드럽게, 마치 숨이 차듯 속삭였다.\n\n\"읽어요! 그러길 바라오.\" 그가 우겼다. \"당신은 리자베타에게도 읽어 주곤 했잖소.\"\n\n소냐는 책을 펴고 그 대목을 찾았다. 그녀의 손이 떨렸고, 목소리가 나오지 않았다. 두 번이나 시작하려 했지만 첫 음절조차 내뱉지 못했다.\n\n\"어떤 병든 사람이 있으니 이는 베다니의 나사로라…….\" 그녀는 마침내 안간힘을 다해 읽기 시작했지만, 세 번째 단어에서 그녀의 목소리는 지나치게 팽팽해진 현처럼 끊겨 버렸다. 숨이 턱 막혔다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 소냐가 자기에게 읽어 주기를 왜 그토록 꺼리는지 어느 정도 알았고, 이를 알면 알수록 그녀에게 더 거칠고 짜증스럽게 읽기를 강요했다. 그는 그녀에게 자기만의 것을 죄다 드러내 보이는 일이 얼마나 고통스러운지 너무도 잘 이해했다. 그는 이 감정들이 정말이지 그녀의 은밀한 보물이며, 어쩌면 여러 해 동안, 어쩌면 어린 시절부터, 불행한 아버지와 슬픔에 미쳐 버린 정신없는 새어머니 곁에서, 굶주리는 아이들과 차마 입에 담기 어려운 욕설과 비난 한가운데서 그녀가 간직해 온 것임을 이해했다. 그러나 동시에 그는 이제 알았고, 또한 확실히 알았으니, 비록 그것이 그녀를 두려움과 고통으로 가득 채울지라도, 그녀가 읽고 싶어, 또 그에게, 그가 듣도록 읽어 주고 싶어 못 견디게 갈망하고 있으며, 그 결과야 어찌 되든 지금 읽고 싶어 한다는 것을……! 그는 그것을 그녀의 눈에서 읽어 냈고, 그녀의 격렬한 감정에서 알아챌 수 있었다. 그녀는 자신을 다잡아, 목구멍의 경련을 억누르고, 요한복음 십일 장을 계속 읽어 나갔다. 그녀는 십구 절까지 나아갔다.\n\n\"많은 유대인이 마르다와 마리아에게 그 오라비의 일로 위로하러 왔더니,\n\n마르다는 예수께서 오신다는 말을 듣고 곧 나가 맞이하되 마리아는 집에 앉았더라.\n\n마르다가 예수께 여짜오되, 주여 주께서 여기 계셨더라면 내 오라비가 죽지 아니하였겠나이다.\n\n그러나 나는 이제라도 주께서 무엇이든지 하느님께 구하시는 것을 하느님이 주실 줄을 아나이다…….\"\n\n그러고는 자기 목소리가 또다시 떨리고 끊겨 버릴 것 같은 부끄러운 마음에 다시 멈추었다.\n\n\"예수께서 이르시되, 네 오라비가 다시 살아나리라.\n\n마르다가 이르되, 마지막 날 부활 때에는 다시 살아날 줄을 내가 아나이다.\n\n예수께서 이르시되, 나는 부활이요 생명이니 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고,\n\n무릇 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리니 이것을 네가 믿느냐?\n\n이르되,\"\n\n(그리고 고통스러운 숨을 들이쉬며, 소냐는 마치 공개적으로 신앙을 고백하기라도 하듯 또박또박, 힘주어 읽었다.)\n\n\"주여 그러하외다, 주는 그리스도시요 세상에 오시는 하느님의 아들이신 줄 내가 믿나이다.\"\n\n그녀는 멈추고 재빨리 그를 올려다보았으나, 자신을 다잡고 계속 읽어 나갔다. 라스콜니코프는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 눈은 딴 데로 돌린 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 삼십이 절까지 읽었다.\n\n\"마리아가 예수 계신 곳에 와서 뵙고 그 발 앞에 엎드리어 이르되, 주여 주께서 여기 계셨더라면 내 오라비가 죽지 아니하였겠나이다.\n\n예수께서 그가 우는 것과 또 함께 온 유대인들이 우는 것을 보시고 심령에 비통히 여기시고 불쌍히 여기사,\n\n이르시되, 그를 어디 두었느냐. 이르되, 주여 와서 보옵소서 하니,\n예수께서 눈물을 흘리시더라.\n\n이에 유대인들이 말하되, 보라 그를 얼마나 사랑하셨는가 하며,\n\n그중 어떤 이는 말하되, 맹인의 눈을 뜨게 한 이 사람이 그 사람은 죽지 않게 할 수 없었더냐 하더라.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 몸을 돌려 격한 감정으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그래, 그는 알고 있었다! 그녀는 진짜 육신의 열병에 떨고 있었다. 그는 그것을 예상했다. 그녀는 가장 큰 기적의 이야기에 다가가고 있었고, 어마어마한 승리의 감정이 그녀를 사로잡았다. 그녀의 목소리는 종소리처럼 울려 퍼졌다. 승리와 환희가 그것에 힘을 실어 주었다. 글줄들이 그녀의 눈앞에서 춤추었지만, 그녀는 자신이 읽는 것을 외고 있었다. 마지막 구절 \"맹인의 눈을 뜨게 한 이 사람이……\"에서, 그녀는 목소리를 낮추어, 한순간 뒤면 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그의 발 앞에 엎드려 흐느끼며 믿게 될, 눈멀고 믿지 않는 유대인들의 의심과 비난과 질책을 열정적으로 되살려 냈다……. '그리고 그도, 그 사람도 — 마찬가지로 눈멀어 믿지 않고 있지만, 그도 듣게 될 거야, 그도 믿게 될 거야, 그래, 그래! 지금 당장, 이 자리에서.' 그녀가 꿈꾼 것은 그것이었고, 그녀는 행복한 기대에 떨고 있었다.\n\n\"이에 예수께서 다시 속으로 비통히 여기시며 무덤에 가시니 무덤이 굴이라 돌로 막았거늘,\n\n예수께서 이르시되, 돌을 옮겨 놓으라 하시니, 그 죽은 자의 누이 마르다가 이르되, 주여 죽은 지가 나흘이 되었으매 벌써 냄새가 나나이다.\"\n\n그녀는 나흘이라는 말에 힘을 주었다.\n\n\"예수께서 이르시되, 내 말이 네가 믿으면 하느님의 영광을 보리라 하지 아니하였느냐 하시니,\n\n돌을 옮겨 놓으니 예수께서 눈을 들어 우러러 보시고 이르시되, 아버지여 내 말을 들으신 것을 감사하나이다.\n\n항상 내 말을 들으시는 줄을 내가 알았나이다마는 둘러선 무리를 위하여 이 말씀을 하옵나니 곧 아버지께서 나를 보내신 것을 그들로 믿게 하려 함이니이다.\n\n이 말씀을 하시고 큰 소리로 나사로야 나오라 부르시니,\n\n죽은 자가 나오는데,\"\n\n(그녀는 마치 그것을 눈앞에서 보기라도 하듯, 황홀경에 차갑게 떨면서 큰 소리로 읽었다.)\n\n\"손발은 베로 동였고 얼굴은 수건에 싸였더라. 예수께서 이르시되, 풀어 놓아 다니게 하라 하시니라.\n\n마리아에게 와서 예수께서 하신 일을 본 많은 유대인이 그를 믿었더라.\"\n\n그녀는 더는 읽지 못하고, 책을 덮고는 의자에서 얼른 일어섰다.\n\n\"나사로를 살리는 이야기는 그게 다예요.\" 그녀가 엄하게, 또 불쑥 속삭이고는, 몸을 돌려 감히 그에게 눈을 들지 못한 채 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 그녀는 여전히 열병처럼 떨고 있었다. 우그러진 촛대에서 타다 남은 양초가 가물거리며 꺼져 가면서, 그 가난에 찌든 방 안에서 그토록 기이하게 영원한 책을 함께 읽고 있던 살인자와 창녀를 어슴푸레 비추었다. 오 분, 아니 그 이상이 흘렀다.\n\n\"한 가지 할 말이 있어서 왔소.\" 라스콜니코프가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 소리 내어 말했다. 그는 일어나 소냐에게 다가갔다. 그녀는 말없이 그에게 눈을 들었다. 그의 얼굴은 유난히 엄했고, 그 안에는 일종의 사나운 결의가 어려 있었다.\n\n\"나는 오늘 가족을 버렸소.\" 그가 말했다. \"어머니와 누이를. 나는 저들을 보러 가지 않을 거요. 저들과 완전히 인연을 끊었소.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에요?\" 소냐가 깜짝 놀라 물었다. 얼마 전 그의 어머니, 누이와의 만남은 그녀로서는 헤아릴 수 없는 깊은 인상을 남겼던 터였다. 그녀는 거의 공포에 질려 그의 소식을 들었다.\n\n\"이제 내겐 당신밖에 없소.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"함께 갑시다……. 나는 당신에게 왔소, 우리 둘 다 저주받은 몸이오, 함께 우리의 길을 갑시다!\"\n\n그의 두 눈이 번득였는데, 이번에는 소냐가 '마치 미친 사람 같다'고 생각했다.\n\n\"어디로 가요?\" 그녀가 겁에 질려 물으며, 무심결에 뒷걸음질 쳤다.\n\n\"낸들 어찌 알겠소? 다만 그게 같은 길이라는 것만은 알아, 그것만 알 뿐 그 이상은 모르오. 같은 목표라는 것만!\"\n\n그녀는 그를 바라보았으나 아무것도 이해하지 못했다. 그녀가 아는 것은 다만 그가 끔찍하게, 한없이 불행하다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"당신이 저들에게 말한들 그 누구도 이해하지 못할 거요, 하지만 나는 이해했소. 나는 당신이 필요하오, 그래서 당신에게 온 거요.\"\n\n\"이해할 수가 없어요.\" 소냐가 속삭였다.\n\n\"나중에 이해하게 될 거요. 당신도 똑같은 짓을 하지 않았소? 당신도 마찬가지로 죄를 범했소…… 죄를 범할 힘이 있었던 거요. 당신은 스스로에게 손을 댔소, 한 생명을 파괴했소…… 당신 자신의 (그게 그거지!). 당신은 정신과 깨달음 속에서 살 수도 있었을 텐데, 결국 건초 시장에서 끝나고 말 거요……. 하지만 당신은 그걸 견딜 수 없을 거고, 혼자 남는다면 나처럼 미쳐 버릴 거요. 당신은 이미 미친 사람 같소. 그러니 우리는 함께 같은 길을 가야 하오! 갑시다!\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에요? 이게 다 무엇 때문이에요?\" 소냐가 그의 말에 야릇하고 격렬하게 동요하며 말했다.\n\n\"무엇 때문이냐고? 당신이 이대로 머물 수 없으니까, 그래서요! 당신은 마침내 모든 걸 똑바로 직시해야 하고, 아이처럼 울거나 하느님이 그걸 허락하지 않으실 거라고 울부짖어서는 안 되오. 만일 당신이 정말 내일이라도 병원에 실려 가면 어떻게 되겠소? 그 여자는 미쳤고 폐병에 걸렸으니 곧 죽을 거고, 그럼 아이들은? 폴렌카가 비참한 신세가 되지 않으리라고 말할 셈이오? 어미들이 구걸하라고 길모퉁이로 내보낸 아이들을 여기서 못 봤소? 나는 그 어미들이 어디서 어떤 환경 속에 사는지 알아냈소. 거기서 아이들은 아이로 남아 있을 수가 없소! 일곱 살이면 그 아이는 타락하고 도둑이 되오. 하지만 아이들이란, 알다시피, 그리스도의 형상이오. '천국이 그들의 것이니라.' 그분은 우리에게 그들을 공경하고 사랑하라 명하셨소, 그들은 미래의 인류요…….\"\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하죠, 어떻게 해야 하죠?\" 소냐가 히스테릭하게 울며 두 손을 비틀며 되풀이했다.\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하느냐고? 부숴야 할 것을 단번에 영영 부숴 버리는 거요, 그게 다요, 그리고 그 고통을 스스로 짊어지는 거요. 뭐, 이해가 안 되오? 나중에 이해하게 될 거요……. 자유와 권력, 그리고 무엇보다 권력! 떨고 있는 모든 피조물과 온 개미 떼 위에 군림하는!…… 그게 목표요, 그걸 기억하시오! 그게 내가 작별하며 남기는 말이오. 어쩌면 당신에게 말하는 게 이번이 마지막일지도 모르오. 내가 내일 오지 않으면, 당신은 그 모든 걸 듣게 될 거고, 그때 이 말들을 기억하시오. 그리고 언젠가 훗날, 세월이 흐른 뒤에, 어쩌면 당신은 그 말들이 무슨 뜻이었는지 이해하게 될 거요. 만일 내일 온다면, 누가 리자베타를 죽였는지 당신에게 말해 주겠소……. 잘 있으시오.\"\n\n소냐는 공포로 흠칫했다.\n\n\"아니, 누가 그분을 죽였는지 아세요?\" 그녀가 공포에 얼어붙어, 정신없는 눈으로 그를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"알고 있고, 또 말해 주겠소…… 당신에게, 오직 당신에게만. 나는 당신을 골랐소. 나는 용서를 구하러 당신에게 온 게 아니라, 그저 말해 주려고 온 거요. 나는 오래전에 이 이야기를 들을 사람으로 당신을 골라 뒀소, 당신 아버지가 당신 얘기를 했을 때, 또 리자베타가 살아 있을 때, 나는 그 생각을 했소. 잘 있으시오, 악수는 하지 맙시다. 내일!\"\n\n그는 나갔다. 소냐는 미친 사람을 보듯 그를 응시했다. 하지만 그녀 자신도 정신 나간 사람 같았고, 또 그것을 느꼈다. 머리가 빙빙 돌았다.\n\n'세상에, 저이가 누가 리자베타를 죽였는지 어떻게 안단 말인가? 그 말들은 무슨 뜻이었을까? 끔찍해!' 하지만 동시에 그 생각은 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오르지 않았다, 한순간도! '오, 저이는 분명 끔찍하게 불행한 거야!…… 어머니와 누이를 버렸다니……. 무엇 때문에? 무슨 일이 있었던 걸까? 그리고 무슨 생각을 품고 있었던 걸까? 내게 무슨 말을 한 거지? 그이가 내 발에 입 맞추고 말했지…… 말했어 (그래, 분명히 그렇게 말했어) 나 없이는 살 수 없다고……. 오, 자비로우신 하느님!'\n\n소냐는 밤새도록 열에 들떠 헛소리를 했다. 이따금 벌떡 일어나 울며 두 손을 비틀다가, 다시 열에 들뜬 잠에 빠져들어 폴렌카와, 카테리나 이바노브나와, 리자베타의 꿈을, 복음서를 읽는 꿈을, 그리고 그의 꿈을 꾸었다…… 창백한 얼굴에, 불타는 눈으로…… 그녀의 발에 입 맞추며 우는 그의 꿈을.\n\n소냐의 방을 레슬리히 부인의 셋집과 가르는 오른쪽 문 너머에는, 오래도록 비어 있던 방이 하나 있었다. 대문에는 광고 패가 붙어 있었고 운하 쪽으로 난 창에는 세놓는다는 안내문이 끼워져 있었다. 소냐는 오래전부터 그 방이 비어 있다는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그러나 그동안 내내 스비드리가일로프 씨가 그 빈방 문 앞에 서서 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 라스콜니코프가 나갔을 때 그는 가만히 서서 잠시 생각하다가, 발끝으로 살금살금 그 빈방에 잇닿은 자기 방으로 가서 의자 하나를 가져와, 소리 없이 그것을 소냐의 방으로 통하는 문 앞으로 옮겨 놓았다. 그 대화는 그에게 흥미롭고 주목할 만한 것으로 다가왔고, 그는 그것을 무척 즐겼던 터라 — 어찌나 즐겼는지, 앞으로, 이를테면 내일 같은 때 한 시간 내내 서 있어야 하는 불편을 겪지 않고 편히 들을 수 있도록 의자를 가져온 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n이튿날 아침 정확히 열한 시에 라스콜니코프가 형사 사건 수사부로 들어가 포르피리 페트로비치에게 자기 이름을 전했을 때, 그는 너무 오래 기다리게 한 것에 놀랐다. 호출될 때까지 적어도 십 분은 걸렸다. 그는 그들이 자기에게 와락 달려들 줄 알았다. 그러나 그는 대기실에 서 있었고, 그와는 전혀 상관없어 보이는 사람들이 그의 앞을 끊임없이 오갔다. 사무실처럼 보이는 옆방에서는 여러 서기가 앉아 무언가를 쓰고 있었는데, 라스콜니코프가 누구인지, 무엇 하는 자인지 도통 관심이 없는 게 분명했다. 그는 자신의 도주를 막기 위해 무슨 경비나 은밀한 감시가 붙어 있지는 않은지 살피며 불안하고 의심스러운 눈초리로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 그런 것은 전혀 없었다. 그저 자질구레한 일에 골몰한 서기들의 얼굴과 다른 사람들이 보일 뿐, 그 누구도 그에게 신경 쓰는 것 같지 않았다. 그들로서는 그가 어디로 가든 상관없는 일이었다. 어제의 그 수수께끼 같은 사내, 땅에서 솟아난 그 유령이 모든 것을 보았다면 자신을 이렇게 세워 두고 기다리게 하지는 않았으리라는 확신이 그의 마음속에서 점점 강해졌다. 게다가 그들이 그가 마음 내켜 열한 시에 나타날 때까지 기다리기나 했겠는가? 그 사내가 아직 밀고하지 않았거나, 아니면…… 아니면 단지 그가 아무것도 모르고, 아무것도 보지 못했거나(대체 무엇을 볼 수 있었겠는가?), 그래서 어제 그에게 일어난 모든 일은 또다시 병들고 과도하게 긴장된 상상력이 부풀려 놓은 유령에 불과한 것이었다. 이 추측은 어제, 온갖 불안과 절망 속에서 이미 강해지기 시작했었다. 이제 그 모든 것을 곱씹으며 새로운 싸움을 준비하던 그는 문득 자신이 떨고 있음을 깨달았고, 저 가증스러운 포르피리 페트로비치를 마주하는 것에 두려움으로 떨고 있다는 생각에 분노가 치밀어 올랐다. 그가 무엇보다 두려워한 것은 그 사내를 다시 만나는 일이었다. 그는 강렬하고 누그러질 줄 모르는 증오로 그를 미워했고, 그 증오가 자신을 드러낼까 봐 두려웠다. 그의 분노가 어찌나 컸던지 그는 곧장 떨기를 멈추었다. 그는 차갑고 오만한 태도로 들어갈 채비를 했고, 가능한 한 입을 다물고, 지켜보고 듣기만 하며, 적어도 이번 한 번만큼은 과도하게 곤두선 신경을 다스리겠다고 스스로에게 맹세했다. 바로 그 순간 그는 포르피리 페트로비치에게로 호출되었다.\n\n그는 포르피리 페트로비치가 자기 집무실에 혼자 있는 것을 보았다. 그의 집무실은 크지도 작지도 않은 방으로, 체크무늬 천을 씌운 소파 앞에 놓인 커다란 책상, 작은 사무용 책상, 한쪽 구석의 책장, 그리고 의자 몇 개가 비치되어 있었는데, 모두 반들반들 윤이 나는 노란 목재로 된 관청 가구였다. 안쪽 벽에는 닫힌 문이 하나 있었고, 그 너머에는 분명 다른 방들이 있을 터였다. 라스콜니코프가 들어서자 포르피리 페트로비치는 곧장 자기가 들어온 문을 닫았고, 둘만 남게 되었다. 그는 겉보기엔 상냥하고 싹싹한 태도로 손님을 맞았는데, 몇 분이 지나서야 라스콜니코프는 그에게서 어떤 어색함의 기색을, 마치 계산이 어긋났거나 아주 은밀한 무언가를 들킨 듯한 기색을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"아, 이보게! 자네가 왔군…… 우리 영역으로 말일세……\" 포르피리는 두 손을 내밀며 말을 꺼냈다. \"자, 앉게, 친구…… 아니, 어쩌면 자네는 '이보게'니 '친구'니 하고 불리는 게 싫을지도 모르겠군, 투 쿠르(tout court)로 말이야? 제발 너무 허물없다고 여기지는 말게나……. 자, 여기, 소파에 앉게.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 앉았다. \"우리 영역\"이라는 말, 허물없음에 대한 변명, 투 쿠르라는 프랑스어 표현, 이 모든 것이 특징적인 징후였다.\n\n\"그는 나에게 두 손을 내밀었지만, 손은 한 번도 내주지 않았어 — 제때 거두어들였지\" 하는 생각이 그에게 의심스럽게 박혔다. 둘은 서로를 살피고 있었으나, 눈길이 마주치면 번개처럼 재빨리 시선을 돌렸다.\n\n\"이 서류를 가져왔습니다…… 시계에 관한 것 말입니다. 여기 있습니다. 이대로 괜찮습니까, 아니면 다시 베껴 써야 합니까?\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 서류? 그래요, 그래요, 걱정 마세요, 괜찮습니다.\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 서두르듯 말하고 나서 서류를 받아 들여다보았다. \"그래요, 괜찮습니다. 더 필요한 건 없어요.\" 그는 똑같이 재빠르게 단언하고는 서류를 책상 위에 내려놓았다.\n\n잠시 후 그는 다른 이야기를 하다가 그것을 책상에서 집어 자기 사무용 책상 위로 옮겨 놓았다.\n\n\"어제 말씀하시기를, 저를 심문하고 싶다고 하셨던 것 같은데요…… 정식으로…… 살해당한 여인과의 제 친분에 관해서 말입니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"왜 내가 '같은데요'라고 붙였지?\" 하는 생각이 번개처럼 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. \"왜 내가 그 '같은데요'를 붙인 것에 이토록 불안해하는 거지?\" 두 번째 번개가 뒤따랐다. 그리고 그는 문득, 포르피리와 그저 접촉하는 것만으로도, 첫마디에, 첫 눈길에 느낀 자신의 불안이 순식간에 괴물 같은 크기로 자라났음을, 그리고 이것이 끔찍하게 위험하다는 것을 느꼈다. 그의 신경은 떨리고, 흥분은 점점 커져 갔다. \"나쁘다, 나빠! 또 너무 많은 말을 하겠어.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 그래! 서두를 것 없습니다, 서두를 것 없어요.\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 별다른 목적도 없이 책상 주위를 이리저리 오가며 중얼거렸는데, 마치 창문이며 사무용 책상이며 책상으로 불쑥불쑥 달려가는 듯하다가, 어느 순간엔 라스콜니코프의 의심스러운 눈길을 피하고, 또 어느 순간엔 가만히 멈춰 서서 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다.\n\n뚱뚱하고 동그란 그의 작은 몸뚱이는 한쪽에서 다른 쪽으로 굴러갔다가 다시 튕겨 돌아오는 공처럼 무척 기묘해 보였다.\n\n\"우리에겐 시간이 넉넉합니다. 담배 피우시나요? 자기 것이 있나요? 자, 여기, 담배 한 대!\" 그는 손님에게 담배를 권하며 말을 이었다. \"아시다시피 나는 지금 여기서 손님을 맞고 있지만, 내 거처는 저쪽 너머에 있지요, 아시겠지만, 내 관사 말입니다. 그런데 당분간은 바깥에 나가 살고 있어요, 여기 수리를 좀 해야 했거든요. 이제 거의 다 끝나갑니다만……. 관사라는 게, 아시다시피, 굉장한 거지요. 어떻게 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"그래요, 굉장한 거지요.\" 라스콜니코프는 거의 비꼬는 눈으로 그를 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"굉장한 거예요, 굉장한 거지.\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 마치 방금 전혀 다른 무언가가 떠오른 듯 되풀이했다. \"그래요, 굉장한 거지요.\" 마침내 그는 거의 소리치다시피 하며 불쑥 라스콜니코프를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그에게서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n이 어리석은 되풀이는 그 서투름이, 그가 손님에게 던진 진지하고 침울하며 수수께끼 같은 눈길과 너무도 어울리지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이것은 그 어느 때보다도 라스콜니코프의 비위를 건드렸고, 그는 빈정대는, 다소 경솔한 도발을 참을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"한 가지 여쭙겠습니다.\" 그가 거의 무례하다 싶게 그를 바라보며, 자기 무례함에 일종의 쾌감을 느끼면서 불쑥 물었다. \"이게 일종의 법적 규칙, 일종의 법적 관행인 것 같던데요 — 모든 예심판사들에게 말입니다 — 멀리서부터, 사소한, 적어도 본론과 무관한 주제로 공격을 시작해서, 심문받는 자를 격려하거나, 차라리 주의를 흩뜨려서, 그 경계심을 무장해제시킨 다음, 느닷없이 어떤 치명적인 질문으로 예기치 못한 일격을 가하는 것 말입니다. 그렇지 않습니까? 모든 기술 교본에 언급되어 있을 법한, 신성한 관행이지요?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요……. 아니, 자네는 내가 그래서 관사 얘기를 꺼냈다고 생각하는 건가…… 응?\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 포르피리 페트로비치는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 윙크를 했다. 그의 얼굴에는 유쾌하고 교활한 표정이 스쳤다. 이마의 주름이 펴지고, 두 눈이 오므라들고, 이목구비가 넓어지더니, 그는 갑자기 온몸을 흔들며 라스콜니코프의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보면서 신경질적이고 길게 늘어지는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 라스콜니코프도 억지로 웃었으나, 포르피리가 그가 웃는 것을 보고 얼굴이 거의 새빨개지도록 너털웃음을 터뜨리자, 라스콜니코프의 혐오감이 모든 경계심을 압도했다. 그는 웃음을 멈추고, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 포르피리의 일부러 길게 끄는 웃음이 이어지는 내내 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않고 증오의 눈길로 노려보았다. 그러나 경계심의 결여는 양쪽 모두에게 있었으니, 포르피리 페트로비치도 손님의 얼굴에 대고 웃어 대는 듯했고, 손님이 그것을 짜증스럽게 받아들이는데도 거의 개의치 않는 듯했기 때문이다. 이 후자의 사실은 라스콜니코프의 눈에 매우 의미심장했다. 그는 포르피리 페트로비치가 방금 전에도 결코 당황한 것이 아니었으며, 오히려 자신이, 라스콜니코프 자신이 어쩌면 함정에 빠진 것인지도 모른다는 것, 여기에는 무언가, 그가 모르는 어떤 동기가 분명 있으리라는 것, 어쩌면 모든 것이 이미 준비되어 있어 다음 순간 그에게 들이닥칠지도 모른다는 것을 알아챘다…….\n\n그는 곧장 본론으로 들어가, 자리에서 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"포르피리 페트로비치.\" 그는 상당한 짜증을 품고서도 단호하게 말을 꺼냈다. \"어제 당신은 제가 몇 가지 심문을 위해 당신에게 와 주기를 바란다고 하셨습니다.\" (그는 \"심문\"이라는 말에 특별히 힘을 주었다.) \"그래서 왔습니다. 제게 물어볼 것이 있으면 물어보시고, 없으면 물러가게 해 주십시오. 저는 한가하지 않습니다……. 마차에 치인 그 사람의 장례식에 가 봐야 하는데, 그 사람은 당신도…… 아실 겁니다.\" 그는 이 말을 덧붙인 것에 곧장 화가 났고, 그 화에 더욱 짜증이 나서 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"이 모든 것에 진절머리가 납니다, 들리십니까? 진작부터요. 그것 때문에 병까지 났던 겁니다. 한마디로 말해서,\" 그는 자기 병에 관한 말이 더더욱 어울리지 않는다는 것을 느끼며 소리쳤다. \"한마디로, 부디 저를 심문하시든지 보내 주시든지, 지금 당장 그렇게 하십시오. 그리고 꼭 심문해야 한다면, 정식 형식대로 하십시오! 그렇지 않으면 저는 응하지 않겠습니다. 그러니 그동안은 안녕히 계십시오, 우리를 여기 붙잡아 둘 만한 게 분명 아무것도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그게 무슨 말입니까? 내가 무엇에 대해 자네를 심문하겠나?\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 어조를 바꾸어 깔깔거리며, 곧장 웃음을 그쳤다. \"제발 마음 쓰지 마세요.\" 그는 이곳저곳으로 안절부절못하며 부산스럽게 라스콜니코프를 앉혔다. \"서두를 것 없습니다, 서두를 것 없어요, 다 부질없는 소리예요. 아니, 아니, 마침내 자네가 나를 찾아와 주어서 정말 기쁘답니다……. 나는 자네를 그저 손님으로 여기고 있어요. 그리고 그 망할 내 웃음에 대해서는, 부디 용서해 주게, 로디온 로마노비치. 로디온 로마노비치? 자네 이름이 그렇지요?…… 내 신경 탓이에요, 자네의 그 재치 있는 말이 어찌나 나를 간질이던지. 정말이지, 가끔 나는 고무공처럼 반시간씩 들썩이며 웃어 댄답니다……. 종종 중풍이라도 올까 봐 겁이 나요. 자, 앉게. 제발 앉아 주게, 안 그러면 자네가 화가 났다고 생각하겠어요…….\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 말이 없었다. 그는 여전히 화난 듯 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 그를 지켜보며 듣고 있었다. 그는 앉기는 했으나, 여전히 모자를 쥐고 있었다.\n\n\"내 자신에 대해 한 가지 말해 두어야겠네, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치.\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 방 안을 거닐며, 또다시 손님의 눈을 피하면서 말을 이었다. \"보다시피 나는 독신이고, 보잘것없는 사람이라 사교에 익숙하지 않아요. 게다가 내 앞엔 아무것도 없고, 자리 잡았고, 이젠 시들어 가고 있지요, 그리고…… 그리고 알아챘는지 모르겠네만, 로디온 로마노비치, 우리 페테르부르크 사교계에서는 친밀하지는 않으나 서로를 존중하는 영리한 두 사람이, 가령 자네와 나 같은 사람이 만나면, 대화의 주제를 찾기까지 반시간은 걸린다네 — 둘은 꿀 먹은 벙어리가 되어, 서로 마주 앉아 어색해하지요. 누구에게나 대화의 주제가 있어요, 가령 부인들에게는…… 상류 사회 사람들에게는 늘 대화 주제가 있지요, 세 드 리괴르(c'est de rigueur)요, 하지만 우리 같은 중간 부류 사람들은, 그러니까 생각하는 사람들은 늘 말문이 막히고 어색해한다네. 그 이유가 뭘까요? 공공의 관심사가 없어서인지, 아니면 우리가 너무 정직해서 서로 속이고 싶지 않은 건지, 나도 모르겠어요. 자네 생각은 어떤가? 그 모자는 내려놓게, 마치 금방이라도 떠나려는 것 같아 내가 불편하군……. 나는 이렇게나 기쁜데…….\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 모자를 내려놓고, 진지하게 찌푸린 얼굴로 포르피리 페트로비치의 막연하고 공허한 수다를 잠자코 계속 듣고 있었다. \"저자가 정말로 이 시시한 잡담으로 내 주의를 흩뜨리려는 걸까?\"\n\n\"여기서 자네에게 커피를 대접할 순 없지만, 친구와 오 분쯤 보내는 게 어떻겠나?\" 포르피리는 재잘재잘 말을 이었다. \"그리고 알다시피 이 모든 관청 업무라는 게……. 부디 내가 이렇게 오르락내리락하는 걸 개의치 말게, 미안하네, 친구, 자네 기분을 상하게 할까 봐 무척 겁이 나지만, 운동이라는 게 내겐 절대적으로 필요해서 말이야. 늘 앉아만 있다 보니 오 분이라도 움직이면 어찌나 좋은지……. 앉아서 일하는 생활 때문에 고생이거든……. 늘 체조 교실에 다닐 작정이긴 한데, 거기선 온갖 직급의 관리들이, 심지어 추밀 고문관까지도 신나게 줄넘기를 한다더군. 자, 보게나, 현대 과학이라는 거지요……. 그래요, 그래……. 한데 여기서 내가 하는 업무, 심문이며 그 모든 형식적 절차로 말하자면…… 자네가 방금 직접 심문을 입에 올렸지만…… 정말이지 이런 신문이라는 게 때로는 신문받는 자보다 신문하는 자에게 더 거북한 법이라네……. 자네가 방금 직접 아주 적절하고 재치 있게 그 점을 지적했지.\" (라스콜니코프는 그런 지적을 한 적이 없었다.) \"엉망진창이 되어 버린다오! 완전히 뒤죽박죽! 북처럼 같은 가락만 줄곧 두들겨 대는 거지요! 개혁이 있을 거고, 그러면 적어도 우리 호칭이라도 달라지겠지요, 헤-헤-헤! 그리고 우리의 법적 관행에 대해서라면, 자네가 그토록 재치 있게 일컬은 대로, 나도 전적으로 동감이라오. 재판받는 죄수라면 누구나, 가장 무지한 농사꾼이라도, 먼저 본론과 무관한 질문으로 무장해제시킨 다음(자네가 그토록 멋지게 표현한 대로) 일격을 가한다는 걸 알고 있지요, 헤-헤-헤! 자네의 그 절묘한 비유 말이야, 헤-헤! 그래, 자네는 정말로 내가 '관사'라는 말로 그런 뜻을…… 헤-헤! 자네는 빈정대는 사람이야. 자, 그만하지! 아, 그래, 참! 말이 말을 부르는군. 자네가 방금 형식을 운운했지, 심문에 관해서 말이야. 한데 형식이라는 게 무슨 소용인가? 많은 경우 그건 부질없는 거지요. 때로는 친근하게 한담을 나누면서 훨씬 더 많은 걸 얻기도 한답니다. 형식이야 언제든 의지할 수 있는 거예요, 정말 그렇다네. 그리고 따지고 보면 그게 다 뭐겠나? 예심판사가 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 형식에 얽매일 수는 없는 노릇이지. 수사라는 일은, 말하자면, 그 나름의 자유로운 예술이거든요, 헤-헤-헤!\"\n\n포르피리 페트로비치는 잠시 숨을 돌렸다. 그는 그저 공허한 말들을 늘어놓으며 재잘거렸고, 수수께끼 같은 말 몇 마디를 슬쩍 흘렸다가 다시 두서없는 이야기로 돌아갔다. 그는 거의 방 안을 뛰어다니다시피 하며 뚱뚱하고 짧은 다리를 점점 더 빨리 놀렸고, 오른손은 등 뒤로 돌린 채 바닥을 내려다보면서, 왼손으로는 자기 말과 유난히 어울리지 않는 손짓을 해 댔다. 라스콜니코프는 그가 방 안을 뛰어다니다가 두 번쯤, 마치 무언가에 귀를 기울이는 듯, 문 근처에서 잠시 멈춰 서는 것을 문득 알아챘다.\n\n\"저자가 무언가를 기다리고 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"자네 말이 정말 옳아요.\" 포르피리는 유난히 천진한 표정으로 라스콜니코프를 바라보며 명랑하게 말을 꺼냈는데(이것이 그를 놀라게 하여 곧장 경계하게 만들었다), \"우리 법적 형식을 그토록 재치 있게 비웃은 게 정말 옳다네, 헤-헤! 이 정교한 심리적 방법들 중 어떤 건 형식에 너무 매달리면 지독히 우스꽝스럽고 어쩌면 쓸모도 없지요. 그래요…… 또 형식 얘기를 하고 있군. 자, 만일 내가 내게 맡겨진 어떤 사건에서 누군가를 범인이라고 인정하거나, 좀 더 엄밀히 말해 의심한다면…… 자네도 물론 법학을 공부하고 있겠지, 로디온 로마노비치?\"\n\n\"네, 했습니다만…….\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 이게 자네에게 장차 하나의 선례가 되겠군 — 하긴 자네가 범죄에 관해 발표한 논문들을 보고서야 내가 감히 자네를 가르치려 든다고는 여기지 말게나! 아니, 나는 그저 사실 삼아 대담하게 말해 보는 거라네. 만일 내가 이 사람 저 사람을 범인으로 본다면, 그래, 내가 묻겠는데, 비록 그자에게 불리한 증거가 있다 한들 어째서 내가 그를 미리 들볶아야 하겠나? 어떤 경우엔, 가령, 내가 그 사람을 즉시 체포해야만 할 수도 있지만, 또 다른 경우엔 사정이 전혀 달라서, 알다시피, 그러니 그자를 잠깐 시내나 돌아다니게 두면 안 될 게 뭐 있겠나? 헤-헤-헤! 한데 자네가 잘 이해하지 못하는 것 같으니, 좀 더 분명한 예를 들어 보겠네. 만일 내가 그를 너무 일찍 감옥에 처넣으면, 십중팔구 나는, 말하자면, 그에게 정신적 지지를 주게 되는 거라네, 헤-헤! 자네 웃고 있나?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 웃을 생각이 조금도 없었다. 그는 입술을 꾹 다물고, 열에 들뜬 눈을 포르피리 페트로비치의 눈에 고정한 채 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"그렇지만 사실이 그렇다네, 특히 어떤 부류의 인간들에게는 말이야, 사람이란 워낙 제각각이니까. 자네는 '증거'를 말하지. 자, 증거야 있을 수 있어요. 하지만 증거라는 건, 알다시피, 대개 두 가지로 해석될 수 있지요. 나는 예심판사이고, 약한 인간이라네, 고백하건대. 나는 증거를, 말하자면, 수학적으로 명백하게 만들고 싶어요. 둘에 둘을 더하면 넷이 되듯 그런 증거의 사슬을 만들고 싶다네, 직접적이고 반박할 수 없는 증거여야 하지요! 그런데 만일 내가 그를 너무 일찍 가둬 버리면 — 비록 내가 그자가 범인이라고 확신한다 해도 — 십중팔구 나는 그에게 불리한 증거를 더 얻을 수단을 스스로 박탈하게 되는 거라네. 어째서냐고? 그에게, 말하자면, 확정된 처지를 줌으로써, 나는 그를 불안에서 벗어나게 하고 마음을 놓이게 하여, 결국 그가 자기 껍데기 속으로 움츠러들게 만들기 때문이지요. 듣자 하니 세바스토폴에서, 알마 전투 직후에, 영리한 자들이 적이 대놓고 공격해 와서 세바스토폴을 단숨에 함락시킬까 봐 끔찍하게 두려워했다더군. 그런데 적이 정규적인 포위 공격을 택하는 걸 보고는, 안도하고 기뻐했다는 거예요, 적어도 두 달은 질질 끌 테니까 말이지요. 자네 웃는군, 또 나를 안 믿는 거야? 물론 자네 말도 옳아요. 자네가 옳아, 옳다네. 이건 특수한 경우들이지, 인정하네. 하지만 자네는 이 점을 눈여겨봐야 해요, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치, 모든 법적 형식과 규칙이 겨냥하는 일반적인 경우, 그것들이 그것을 위해 계산되고 책에 명시되어 있는 그런 일반적인 경우란 애당초 존재하지 않는다네. 왜냐하면 모든 사건은, 가령 모든 범죄는, 실제로 일어나는 순간 곧장 철저히 특수한 사건이 되어 버리고, 때로는 그 이전의 어떤 것과도 닮지 않은 사건이 되기 때문이지요. 그런 종류의 무척 우스꽝스러운 사건들이 가끔 일어난다오. 만일 내가 어떤 사람을 완전히 가만히 내버려 둔다면, 그를 건드리지도 들볶지도 않으면서, 다만 내가 모든 것을 알고 있으며 밤낮으로 그를 지켜보고 있다는 것을 매 순간 알게 하거나 적어도 의심하게 만든다면, 그래서 그가 끊임없는 의심과 공포 속에 있게 한다면, 그는 반드시 제정신을 잃게 될 거예요. 그는 제 발로 찾아오거나, 어쩌면 둘에 둘을 더하면 넷이 되는 것만큼이나 명백한 무언가를 저지를 걸세 — 정말 즐거운 일이지요. 단순한 농사꾼이라면 그럴 수도 있지만, 우리 같은 부류, 어떤 면에서 교양을 갖춘 지식인이라면 그건 확실하다네. 왜냐하면, 친구, 사람이 어느 면에서 교양을 쌓았는지를 아는 건 매우 중요한 일이거든요. 그리고 거기엔 또 신경이라는 게 있어요, 신경이 있단 말이야, 자네는 그걸 간과했어! 아니, 그들은 죄다 병들고, 신경질적이고, 짜증을 내거든!…… 그리고 또 그들은 다들 얼마나 비위가 상해 있는지! 그건, 정말이지, 우리에겐 그야말로 금광이라네. 그러니 그자가 자유로이 시내를 돌아다닌다 해도 내겐 아무런 걱정거리가 아니야! 내버려 두게, 잠깐 돌아다니라지! 나는 이미 그를 잡았고 그가 내게서 빠져나가지 못하리라는 걸 잘 알고 있으니까. 그가 어디로 도망칠 수 있겠나, 헤-헤? 외국으로? 폴란드 놈이라면 외국으로 달아나겠지만, 여기서는 안 돼요, 더구나 내가 지켜보며 조치를 취해 놓았으니. 어쩌면 시골 깊숙이 달아날까? 하지만 알다시피 거기엔 농사꾼들이 살고 있어요, 진짜 거칠고 무지한 러시아 농사꾼들이. 현대의 교양 있는 사람이라면 우리 농사꾼 같은 그런 낯선 자들과 함께 사느니 차라리 감옥을 택할 거라네. 헤-헤! 하지만 그건 다 부질없는 소리고, 겉치레일 뿐이지요. 그자가 달아날 곳이 없을 뿐만 아니라, 그는 심리적으로 내게서 빠져나갈 수가 없다네, 헤-헤! 이 무슨 표현인가! 자연의 법칙에 따라, 설령 갈 곳이 있다 해도 그는 내게서 달아날 수 없어요. 자네 촛불 주위를 도는 나방을 본 적 있나? 그자도 그렇게 내 주위를 빙글빙글 맴돌 걸세. 자유가 그 매력을 잃어버릴 거야. 그는 골똘히 생각에 잠기기 시작하고, 제 둘레에 뒤엉킨 그물을 짜고, 스스로를 죽도록 들볶을 거라네! 게다가 그는 내게 수학적 증거를 제공할 거예요 — 내가 충분한 시간만 준다면 말이야……. 그러고는 내 주위를 빙글빙글 맴돌며, 점점 더 가까이, 더 가까이 다가오다가 — 풀썩! 곧장 내 입속으로 날아들 테고, 나는 그를 삼켜 버리겠지요, 그러면 그게 아주 재미있을 거예요, 헤-헤-헤! 자네 나를 안 믿나?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 대답하지 않았다. 그는 창백하게 굳은 채 앉아, 여전히 똑같은 강렬함으로 포르피리의 얼굴을 응시하고 있었다.\n\n\"이건 본보기를 보여 주는 거야.\" 그는 오싹해지며 생각했다. \"이건 어제처럼 고양이가 쥐를 가지고 노는 것 이상이야. 아무런 동기 없이 제 힘을 과시할 리가 없어…… 나를 부추기는 거지, 그러기엔 저자는 너무 영리해…… 분명 다른 목적이 있어. 그게 뭘까? 다 부질없는 짓이야, 이 친구야, 너는 나를 겁주려고 연기하는 거지! 너에겐 아무 증거도 없고, 내가 본 그 사내는 실제로 존재하지도 않았어. 너는 그저 내가 제정신을 잃게 하고, 미리 나를 들쑤셔 놓아서 짓뭉개려는 거야. 하지만 너는 틀렸어, 그렇게는 안 될 거다! 한데 어째서 내게 이런 암시를 주는 거지? 내 망가진 신경을 노리고 있는 건가? 아니야, 이 친구야, 너는 틀렸어, 설령 나를 위한 함정을 마련해 두었다 해도 그렇게는 안 될 거다…… 어디 네가 나를 위해 무엇을 준비해 두었는지 보자.\"\n\n그리고 그는 끔찍하고 알 수 없는 시련에 맞설 각오를 다졌다. 때때로 그는 포르피리에게 달려들어 목을 졸라 버리고 싶었다. 이 분노야말로 그가 처음부터 두려워했던 것이었다. 그는 자신의 바싹 마른 입술에 거품이 일고, 심장이 두근거리는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 여전히 적절한 순간이 오기 전까지는 입을 열지 않으리라 다짐했다. 그는 이것이 자신의 처지에서 최선의 방책임을 깨달았으니, 너무 많은 말을 하는 대신 침묵으로 적을 짜증나게 하여 그가 함부로 떠벌리도록 부추길 수 있을 터였다. 어쨌든 이것이 그가 바라는 바였다.\n\n\"아니, 보아하니 자네는 나를 안 믿는군, 내가 자네에게 악의 없는 농담이나 하고 있다고 생각하나 보군.\" 포르피리는 점점 더 활기를 띠며, 매 순간 낄낄거리고 다시 방 안을 거닐면서 다시 입을 열었다. \"그래, 정말이지 자네 말이 옳아요. 하느님은 내게 남들 눈엔 우스꽝스러운 생각밖에 일으키지 못하는 외모를 주셨지요, 어릿광대 말이오. 하지만 한마디 하겠네, 거듭 말하지만, 늙은이를 용서해 주게, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치, 자네는 아직 젊은 사람이야, 말하자면, 청춘의 한복판에 있지요, 그래서 모든 젊은이가 그렇듯 지성을 무엇보다 위에 두지요. 장난스러운 재치와 추상적인 논변이 자네를 매혹하는데, 그건 영락없이 저 옛 오스트리아의 호프크리크스라트(Hof-kriegsrath) 같은 거라네, 내가 군사 문제를 좀 안다고 판단하건대 말이야. 그들은 종이 위에서는 나폴레옹을 무찌르고 포로로 잡았지요, 자기네 집무실에서 그 모든 걸 더없이 영리하게 짜냈단 말이야. 그런데 보라고, 마크 장군은 전군을 이끌고 항복하고 말았다네, 헤-헤-헤! 알아요, 알아, 로디온 로마노비치, 자네는 나 같은 민간인이 군사(軍史)에서 예를 끌어다 쓰는 걸 비웃고 있겠지! 하지만 어쩔 수 없어요, 그게 내 약점이거든. 나는 군사학을 좋아한다네. 그리고 온갖 전사(戰史)를 읽는 걸 그렇게나 좋아해요. 나는 확실히 제 길을 놓쳤지요. 나는 군대에 갔어야 했어요, 정말이지 그랬어야 했지. 나폴레옹은 못 되었겠지만, 소령쯤은 되었을지도 몰라, 헤-헤! 자, 이제 자네에게 모든 진실을 말해 주겠네, 친구, 이 특수한 경우에 관해서 말이야. 즉, 실제 사실과 인간의 기질은, 친애하는 선생, 무게 있는 문제라서, 때때로 그것들이 어찌나 가장 예리한 계산마저 속여 넘기는지 놀라울 따름이라네! 나는 — 늙은이의 말을 들어 보게 — 진지하게 말하고 있어요, 로디온 로마노비치.\" (이렇게 말하면서 겨우 서른다섯 남짓밖에 안 된 포르피리 페트로비치는 실제로 늙어 버린 것처럼 보였다. 목소리마저 달라지고, 몸도 쪼그라드는 듯했다.) \"게다가 나는 솔직한 사람이라네…… 내가 솔직한 사람인가 아닌가? 자네 생각은 어떤가? 정말로 그런 것 같은데. 나는 이런 걸 거저 말해 주고 있고 그에 대한 보답조차 기대하지 않으니까, 헤-헤! 자, 계속하자면, 재치란 내 생각엔 멋진 거예요, 그건, 말하자면, 자연의 장식이요 삶의 위안이지요, 그리고 그게 얼마나 별별 술수를 부릴 수 있는지! 그래서 때로는 가련한 예심판사가 자기가 어디에 있는지조차 알기 어려울 지경이라네, 특히 그 자신도 제 공상에 휩쓸리기 쉬울 때는 더더욱 그렇지요, 왜냐하면 아시다시피 그도 결국 인간이니까! 하지만 그 가련한 친구를 구해 주는 건 죄인의 기질이라네, 죄인으로서는 운 나쁘게도 말이야! 한데 자기 재치에 휩쓸린 젊은이들은 그걸 생각하지 못하지요, '모든 장애물을 뛰어넘을 때'에는 말이야, 어제 자네가 재치 있고 영리하게 표현한 대로. 그는 거짓말을 할 거예요 — 즉, 특수한 경우인 그 사람, 그 익명의 사내는 거짓말을 할 거고, 게다가 잘할 거예요, 더없이 영리하게 말이지요. 자네는 그가 의기양양하게 제 재치의 열매를 만끽하리라 여기겠지만, 가장 흥미로운, 가장 결정적인 순간에 그는 기절해 버린다네. 물론 병 때문일 수도 있고 답답한 방 탓일 수도 있지요, 하지만 아무튼! 아무튼 그는 우리에게 단서를 준 거예요! 그는 비할 데 없이 거짓말을 했지만, 자기 기질은 계산에 넣지 못했지요. 바로 그게 그를 드러내는 거라네! 또 어떤 때는 자기 장난스러운 재치에 휩쓸려, 자기를 의심하는 사람을 비웃기까지 한다네, 일부러 오도하려는 듯 창백해지기도 하지만, 그 창백함이 너무 자연스럽거든요, 너무 진짜 같단 말이야, 또다시 그는 우리에게 단서를 준 거예요! 비록 그를 신문하는 자가 처음엔 속을지 몰라도, 바보가 아닌 이상 이튿날엔 달리 생각하겠지요, 그리고 물론, 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 그런 식이라네! 그자는 필요하지도 않은 데서 나서고, 입을 다물어야 할 때 끊임없이 지껄이고, 온갖 우의적인 암시를 끌어들이지요, 헤-헤! 찾아와서는 왜 진작 나를 잡아가지 않았느냐고 묻는다네, 헤-헤-헤! 그리고 그런 일이, 아시다시피, 가장 영리한 사람, 심리학자, 문필가에게도 일어날 수 있어요. 기질이란 거울처럼 모든 걸 비추거든! 들여다보고 거기 비친 것에 감탄하라지! 한데 어째서 자네는 그렇게 창백한가, 로디온 로마노비치? 방이 답답한가? 창문을 좀 열어 줄까요?\"\n\n\"아, 제발 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\" 라스콜니코프가 외치더니 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"제발 신경 쓰지 마세요.\"\n\n포르피리는 그와 마주 서서 잠시 멈췄다가, 갑자기 그도 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 라스콜니코프는 자신의 발작적인 웃음을 불쑥 억누르며 소파에서 일어났다.\n\n\"포르피리 페트로비치.\" 그는 다리가 후들거려 거의 서 있기조차 힘들었으나, 크고 또렷한 목소리로 말을 꺼냈다. \"이제 마침내 똑똑히 알겠습니다, 당신은 실제로 제가 그 노파와 그 동생 리자베타를 살해했다고 의심하고 있다는 걸요. 제 입장에서 말씀드리겠는데, 저는 이게 진절머리가 납니다. 만일 당신이 저를 법적으로 기소하고 체포할 권리가 있다고 보신다면, 그렇다면 저를 기소하고 체포하십시오. 하지만 저는 면전에서 비웃음을 당하고 들볶이는 건 용납하지 않겠습니다…….\"\n\n그의 입술이 떨렸고, 두 눈은 분노로 이글거렸으며, 그는 목소리를 억누를 수가 없었다.\n\n\"용납하지 않겠습니다!\" 그는 주먹으로 책상을 내리치며 소리쳤다. \"들리십니까, 포르피리 페트로비치? 용납하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 이게 무슨 뜻인가?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 외쳤는데, 분명 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯했다. \"로디온 로마노비치, 친구, 자네 도대체 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"용납하지 않겠습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 다시 소리쳤다.\n\n\"쉿, 이보게! 저들이 듣고 들어올 걸세. 생각해 보게, 우리가 저들에게 뭐라고 하겠나?\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 라스콜니코프의 얼굴에 자기 얼굴을 바짝 들이대며 겁에 질려 속삭였다.\n\n\"용납하지 않겠습니다, 용납하지 않겠어요.\" 라스콜니코프는 기계적으로 되풀이했으나, 그 역시 갑자기 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n포르피리는 재빨리 돌아서서 창문을 열러 달려갔다.\n\n\"바깥 공기를 좀! 그리고 자네 물을 좀 마셔야겠네, 친구. 자네 아프잖나!\" 그는 물을 가져오라고 부르려 문 쪽으로 달려가다가, 구석에서 물병을 발견했다. \"자, 좀 마시게.\" 그는 물병을 들고 그에게 달려와 속삭였다. \"분명 도움이 될 걸세.\"\n\n포르피리 페트로비치의 놀람과 동정이 어찌나 자연스러웠던지, 라스콜니코프는 입을 다물고 거친 호기심으로 그를 바라보기 시작했다. 그러나 물은 받지 않았다.\n\n\"로디온 로마노비치, 친구, 자네 그러다 제정신이 아니게 되겠어, 정말이지, 아흐, 아흐! 물 좀 마시게, 좀 마셔요.\"\n\n그는 억지로 그에게 잔을 쥐여 주었다. 라스콜니코프는 기계적으로 그것을 입가로 가져갔다가, 혐오감에 다시 책상 위에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"그래, 자네 가벼운 발작을 일으켰군! 자네 또 병이 도지겠어, 친구.\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 여전히 다소 당황한 기색이긴 했으나, 다정한 동정으로 깔깔거렸다. \"맙소사, 자네 몸을 좀 더 아껴야 해! 어제 드미트리 프로코피치가 여기 왔었네, 나를 보러 왔지 — 알아요, 알아, 내가 짓궂고 빈정대는 성미를 지녔다는 거, 하지만 그자들이 그걸로 무슨 짓을 했는지!…… 맙소사, 그가 자네가 다녀간 뒤에 어제 왔어요. 우린 식사를 했고 그는 끊임없이 떠들어 댔지, 나는 그저 절망하며 두 손 들 수밖에 없었다네! 그가 자네가 보내서 온 건가? 한데 제발 앉게, 부디 앉아 주게!\"\n\n\"아니요, 제가 보낸 게 아닙니다, 하지만 그가 당신에게 갔다는 것도, 왜 갔는지도 알고 있었습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 날카롭게 대답했다.\n\n\"알고 있었다고?\"\n\n\"알고 있었습니다. 그게 어쨌다는 겁니까?\"\n\"왜냐하면 이렇다네, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 자네에 관해 그것보다 더 많은 걸 알고 있어요. 나는 모든 걸 알고 있다네. 자네가 한밤중에, 어두울 때 셋집을 빌리러 가서 어떻게 초인종을 울리고 피에 대해 물었는지, 그래서 인부들과 문지기가 그걸 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 몰라 했는지 다 알고 있어요. 그래, 나는 그때 자네의 마음 상태를 이해해요…… 하지만 그러다간 자네 정말 미쳐 버릴 걸세, 정말이지! 제정신을 잃을 거야! 자네는 자네가 받은 부당한 일들에, 처음엔 운명에게서, 그다음엔 경찰관들에게서 받은 그것들에 고결한 분노로 가득 차서, 이 일 저 일로 마구 내달리며 저들이 분명히 말하게 만들어 이 모든 걸 끝장내려 하는 거지요, 이 모든 의심과 어리석은 짓에 진절머리가 났으니까. 그렇지 않은가? 내가 자네 심정을 알아맞혔지, 안 그래요? 다만 그런 식으로는 자네가 제정신을 잃고, 라주미힌의 정신까지 잃게 할 거예요. 그는 그런 처지에 놓이기엔 너무 좋은 사람이거든, 자네도 알아야 해. 자네는 병들었고 그는 좋은 사람인데, 자네 병이 그에게 옮는 거라네……. 자네가 좀 진정되면 그 얘기를 해 주지요……. 한데 제발 앉게나. 부디 좀 쉬게, 자네 안색이 끔찍해, 어서 앉아요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 앉았다. 그는 더는 떨지 않았으나, 온몸이 뜨거웠다. 그는 놀라움 속에서, 여전히 겁먹은 듯 다정한 염려로 자기를 살피는 포르피리 페트로비치의 말에 긴장된 주의를 기울이며 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그는 그의 말을 한마디도 믿지 않았으니, 비록 묘하게도 믿고 싶은 마음이 들기는 했지만. 셋집에 관한 포르피리의 예기치 못한 말이 그를 완전히 압도해 버렸다. \"어떻게 이럴 수가, 그러니까 저자가 셋집 일을 알고 있다니,\" 그는 문득 생각했다. \"게다가 그걸 제 입으로 내게 말하다니!\"\n\n\"그래, 우리 법률 실무에서도 거의 똑같은 사건이 있었지요, 병적인 심리의 사건 말이야.\" 포르피리가 재빨리 말을 이었다. \"한 사내가 살인을 자백했는데, 어찌나 끝까지 우겨 대던지! 완전한 환각이었어요. 그는 사실들을 들이밀고, 모두를 속였지요, 그런데 왜? 그는 부분적으로, 하지만 어디까지나 부분적으로, 본의 아니게 한 살인의 원인이 되었던 거고, 자기가 살인자들에게 기회를 주었다는 걸 알게 되자, 의기소침에 빠져, 그게 마음을 짓누르고 머리를 돌게 해서, 그는 이런저런 것들을 상상하기 시작했고, 자기가 살인자라고 스스로 믿어 버린 거예요. 하지만 결국 고등 항소 법원이 이 일을 검토했고, 그 가련한 친구는 무죄를 선고받아 적절한 보호를 받게 되었지요. 항소 법원 덕분이지! 쯧-쯧-쯧! 아니, 친구, 자네도 자기 신경을 들쑤시고, 한밤중에 초인종을 울리며 피에 대해 묻고 싶은 충동에 빠지면 스스로를 섬망 상태로 몰아넣을 수 있어요! 나는 실무에서 이 모든 병적인 심리를 연구했다네. 사람은 때로 창문이나 종탑에서 뛰어내리고 싶은 유혹에 빠지지요. 초인종을 울리는 것도 꼭 마찬가지라네……. 그건 다 병이에요, 로디온 로마노비치! 자네는 자기 병을 방치하기 시작했어요. 노련한 의사에게 진찰을 받아야 해, 그 뚱뚱한 친구가 무슨 소용이 있겠나? 자네 머리가 어떻게 된 거예요! 그 모든 짓을 할 때 자네는 섬망 상태였다고!\"\n\n한순간 라스콜니코프는 모든 것이 빙글빙글 도는 듯했다.\n\n\"있을 수 있는 일인가, 있을 수 있는 일인가,\" 하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. \"저자가 아직도 거짓말을 하고 있다는 게? 그럴 리 없어, 그럴 리 없어.\" 그는 그 생각이 자신을 어느 정도까지 분노로 몰아갈 수 있는지를, 그 분노가 자신을 미치게 할 수 있음을 느끼며, 그 생각을 떨쳐 버렸다.\n\n\"저는 섬망 상태가 아니었습니다. 제가 무엇을 하는지 알고 있었어요.\" 그는 포르피리의 속셈을 꿰뚫어 보려고 온 능력을 짜내며 외쳤다. \"저는 완전히 제정신이었습니다, 들리십니까?\"\n\n\"그래요, 듣고 있고 이해하고 있어요. 어제도 자네는 섬망 상태가 아니었다고 했지요, 특히 그 점을 힘주어 강조했어요! 자네가 내게 말할 수 있는 건 다 이해한다네! 아-흐!…… 들어 보게, 로디온 로마노비치, 친구. 만일 자네가 실제로 죄인이거나, 어떻게든 이 빌어먹을 사건에 연루되었다면, 자네가 섬망 상태가 아니라 온전히 제정신이었다고 우기겠나? 그것도 그토록 힘주어 끈질기게? 그게 가능한 일이겠나? 내 생각엔 도무지 불가능해요. 만일 자네 양심에 걸리는 게 있다면, 자네는 분명 섬망 상태였다고 우겨야 마땅하지요. 그렇지 않은가?\"\n\n이 물음에는 교활한 가락이 깃들어 있었다. 포르피리가 그에게 몸을 숙이자 라스콜니코프는 소파에서 물러나며, 말없이 어리둥절한 채 그를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"라주미힌에 관해서도 또 한 가지 — 자네는 분명 그가 제 발로 왔다고 말해서, 거기에 자네가 한 몫을 숨겼어야 마땅해요! 그런데 자네는 숨기지 않아! 자네는 그가 자네가 부추겨서 왔다는 걸 강조한다니까.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 그런 적이 없었다. 등줄기가 오싹해졌다.\n\n\"당신은 계속 거짓말을 하는군요.\" 그는 입술을 병약한 미소로 일그러뜨리며 천천히, 힘없이 말했다. \"당신은 또다시 내 속셈을 다 안다는 걸, 내가 무슨 말을 할지 미리 다 안다는 걸 보여 주려는 거예요.\" 그는 자기가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 말을 가려 하지 못하고 있음을 스스로 의식하며 말했다. \"당신은 나를 겁주려는 거예요…… 아니면 그저 나를 비웃고 있거나…….\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서도 그는 여전히 그를 빤히 바라보았고, 그의 눈에는 또다시 강렬한 증오의 빛이 어렸다.\n\n\"당신은 계속 거짓말을 하는군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 죄인에게 최선의 방책이 가능한 한 진실에 가깝게 말하는 것임을…… 가능한 한 적게 숨기는 것임을 아주 잘 알고 있어요. 나는 당신을 믿지 않습니다!\"\n\n\"자네 참 교활한 사람이군!\" 포르피리가 킥킥거렸다. \"자네는 잡을 수가 없어요. 자네는 완벽한 편집광이야. 그래서 나를 안 믿는다고? 하지만 그래도 자네는 나를 믿고 있어요, 사분의 일은 믿지요. 곧 자네가 전부를 믿게 만들겠네, 왜냐하면 나는 자네를 진심으로 아끼고 진정으로 자네가 잘되기를 바라니까.\"\n\n라스콜니코프의 입술이 떨렸다.\n\n\"그래, 정말이라네.\" 포르피리가 라스콜니코프의 팔을 다정하게 어루만지며 말을 이었다. \"자네는 자기 병을 돌봐야 해요. 게다가 지금 자네 어머니와 누이가 여기 와 있잖나. 그들을 생각해야지요. 자네는 그들을 달래고 위로해야 하는데, 자네가 하는 거라곤 그들을 겁주는 것뿐이니…….\"\n\n\"그게 당신과 무슨 상관입니까? 그걸 어떻게 압니까? 그게 당신이 알 바예요? 당신은 나를 감시하면서 그걸 내게 알리고 싶은 거군요?\"\n\n\"맙소사! 아니, 그 모든 걸 자네 입으로 직접 알려 준 거라네! 자네는 흥분하면 나에게도 남들에게도 모든 걸 털어놓는다는 걸 깨닫지 못하는군. 라주미힌에게서도 어제 흥미로운 세부 사항을 잔뜩 알아냈지요. 아니, 자네가 내 말을 끊었지만, 이건 말해 두어야겠어, 그 모든 재치에도 불구하고 자네의 의심하는 버릇 탓에 자네는 사물을 보는 상식적인 눈을 잃어버린다네. 가령 초인종 얘기로 다시 돌아가 보지요. 나는, 예심판사인 내가, 그런 귀중한 것을, 진짜 사실을 누설했는데(그건 손에 넣을 만한 가치가 있는 사실이거든), 자네는 거기서 아무것도 보지 못해요! 아니, 내가 자네를 조금이라도 의심했다면 그렇게 행동했겠나? 천만에, 나라면 먼저 자네의 의심을 무장해제시키고, 내가 그 사실을 안다는 걸 자네가 눈치채지 못하게 하고, 자네 주의를 다른 데로 돌렸다가 느닷없이 일격을(자네 표현이지) 가했겠지요, 이렇게 말하면서 말이야. '그래, 선생, 부디 말씀해 보시오, 열 시인지 거의 열한 시쯤에 살해당한 여인의 셋집에서 무얼 하고 있었으며, 왜 초인종을 울렸고, 왜 피에 대해 물었소? 그리고 왜 문지기들에게 함께 경찰서로, 부서장에게 가자고 청했소?' 자네를 손톱만큼이라도 의심했다면 나는 그렇게 행동했어야 마땅하지요. 정식 형식대로 자네의 진술을 받고, 자네 숙소를 수색하고, 어쩌면 자네를 체포까지 했어야 해요……. 그러니 나는 자네를 의심하지 않는 거라네, 그렇게 하지 않았으니까! 한데 자네는 그걸 정상적으로 보지 못하고 아무것도 보지 못하는군, 거듭 말하지만 말이야.\"\n\n라스콜니코프가 어찌나 흠칫했던지 포르피리 페트로비치가 그것을 알아채지 못할 리 없었다.\n\n\"당신은 줄곧 거짓말을 하는군요.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 목적이 뭔지는 모르겠지만, 당신은 거짓말을 하고 있어요. 당신은 방금 그렇게 말하지 않았어요, 내가 잘못 봤을 리 없습니다!\"\n\n\"내가 거짓말을 한다고?\" 포르피리는 분명 발끈한 듯 되풀이했으나, 라스콜니코프가 자기를 어떻게 여기든 조금도 개의치 않는 듯, 유쾌하고 빈정대는 표정을 유지했다. \"내가 거짓말을 한다고…… 하지만 내가 방금 자네를 어떻게 대했나, 예심판사인 내가? 자네를 부추기며 변호할 온갖 수단을 다 일러 주었지요. 병이다, 섬망이다, 모욕감이다, 우울증이다, 경찰관들이다, 하고 그 모든 걸 말이야? 아! 헤-헤-헤! 하긴, 정말이지 그런 심리적 변호 수단이라는 게 그리 믿을 만한 건 못 되고 양날의 칼이지요. 병, 섬망, 기억나지 않는다 — 그건 다 좋아요, 한데 어째서, 친애하는 선생, 자네는 병중에, 섬망 중에 하필 바로 그런 망상에 사로잡혔지, 다른 망상이 아니라? 다른 것들도 있었을 수 있잖나, 응? 헤-헤-헤!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 거만하고 경멸하는 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"간단히 말해서,\" 그는 자리에서 일어나며, 그러면서 포르피리를 약간 뒤로 밀치며 크고 위압적으로 말했다. \"간단히 말해서, 저는 알고 싶습니다, 당신은 제가 의심에서 완전히 벗어났다고 인정합니까, 아닙니까? 말씀하십시오, 포르피리 페트로비치, 단번에 분명히 말씀하시고, 어서요!\"\n\n\"자네하곤 정말 못 말리겠군!\" 포르피리는 더없이 유쾌하고 교활하면서도 침착한 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"한데 왜 알고 싶어 하나, 왜 그렇게나 알고 싶어 해요, 아직 자네를 들볶기 시작하지도 않았는데? 아니, 자네는 성냥을 달라고 조르는 어린애 같군! 그리고 왜 그렇게 안절부절못하나? 왜 우리에게 자꾸 들이대는 거지요, 응? 헤-헤-헤!\"\n\n\"다시 말하지만,\" 라스콜니코프가 격분하여 외쳤다. \"저는 이걸 견딜 수가 없습니다!\"\n\n\"무엇을 말인가? 불확실함을?\" 포르피리가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"비웃지 마십시오! 그런 건 용납하지 않겠어요! 용납하지 않겠다고 말했습니다. 견딜 수 없고 견디지 않겠어요, 들립니까, 들려요?\" 그는 또다시 주먹으로 책상을 내리치며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"쉿! 쉿! 저들이 엿듣겠어요! 진지하게 경고하는데, 자네 몸조심하게. 나는 농담하는 게 아니야.\" 포르피리가 속삭였는데, 이번에는 그의 얼굴에 노파 같은 선량함과 놀람의 기색이 없었다. 이제 그는 단호하고, 엄격하고, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 단 한 번 모든 눈속임을 내려놓았다.\n\n그러나 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 라스콜니코프는 어리둥절한 채 갑자기 진짜 광란에 빠져들었으나, 이상하게도, 완전한 분노의 발작 속에 있으면서도 그는 또다시 조용히 말하라는 명령에 복종했다.\n\n\"저는 고문당하는 걸 가만두지 않겠습니다.\" 그는 자신이 그 명령에 복종하지 않을 수 없다는 것을 증오와 함께 곧장 깨달으며, 그리고 그 생각에 더더욱 큰 분노에 휩싸이며 속삭였다. \"저를 체포하시든 수색하시든 하되, 부디 정식 형식대로 하시고 저를 가지고 놀지 마십시오! 감히 그러지 마세요!\"\n\n\"형식 걱정은 말게.\" 포르피리가 똑같이 교활한 미소를 띠며, 마치 라스콜니코프를 즐거이 음미하듯 끼어들었다. \"나는 자네를 아주 친근하게 보러 오라고 청했을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"당신의 친근함 따위 필요 없고, 침이나 뱉어 주겠습니다! 들립니까? 자, 여기, 저는 모자를 들고 가겠습니다. 저를 체포할 작정이라면 이제 뭐라고 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n그는 모자를 집어 들고 문 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n\"내 작은 깜짝 선물은 안 보고 가려나?\" 포르피리가 또다시 그의 팔을 붙잡아 문 앞에서 그를 멈춰 세우며 낄낄거렸다.\n\n그는 점점 더 장난스럽고 유쾌해지는 듯했는데, 그것이 라스콜니코프를 미치게 했다.\n\n\"무슨 깜짝 선물 말입니까?\" 그가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 겁먹은 듯 포르피리를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"내 작은 깜짝 선물, 저 문 뒤에 앉아 있다네, 헤-헤-헤!\" (그는 잠긴 문을 가리켰다.) \"도망치지 못하게 그를 가둬 두었지요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭡니까? 어디요? 뭐라고요?……\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 문 쪽으로 걸어가 열려고 했으나, 문은 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"잠겨 있지요, 열쇠는 여기 있네!\"\n\n그러고는 그가 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"당신은 거짓말을 하고 있어요.\" 라스콜니코프가 거리낌 없이 으르렁거렸다. \"거짓말이야, 이 빌어먹을 어릿광대야!\" 그러고는 포르피리에게 달려들었으나, 포르피리는 조금도 놀라지 않고 다른 문 쪽으로 물러섰다.\n\n\"다 알겠어요! 당신은 거짓말을 하고 비웃어서 내가 스스로를 당신에게 드러내게 하려는 거지요…….\"\n\n\"아니, 자네는 더 이상 자신을 드러낼 것도 없네, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치. 자네는 격앙되어 있어요. 소리치지 말게, 서기들을 부르겠네.\"\n\n\"당신은 거짓말을 하고 있어요! 서기들을 부르시지요! 당신은 내가 아프다는 걸 알고서 나를 광란으로 몰아넣어 스스로를 드러내게 하려 했어요, 그게 당신 목적이었지요! 증거를 내놓으시지요! 다 알겠어요. 당신에겐 아무 증거도 없고, 자메토프 같은 한심하고 시시한 의심만 있을 뿐이야! 당신은 내 성격을 알고서 나를 분노로 몰아간 다음 사제들과 입회인들로 나를 짓뭉개려 했지요……. 그들을 기다리고 있는 겁니까? 응! 뭘 기다리는 거예요? 그들이 어디 있어요? 내놓으시지요!\"\n\n\"입회인이라니 무슨 소린가, 이 사람아? 사람들이 별별 상상을 다 하는군! 게다가 그렇게 하는 건 자네 말마따나 형식대로 하는 게 아니라네, 자네는 이 일을 모르는군, 친구……. 그리고 보다시피 형식을 피할 길은 없지요.\" 포르피리는 소리가 들려오는 문에 귀를 기울이며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아, 그들이 오는군요.\" 라스콜니코프가 외쳤다. \"당신이 그들을 부른 거예요! 그들을 기다리고 있었어! 자, 그들을 다 내놓으시지요. 당신의 입회인들이든 증인들이든, 뭐든 좋소!…… 저는 준비됐습니다!\"\n\n그러나 바로 이 순간 기묘한 사건이 벌어졌으니, 너무도 예기치 못한 일이라 라스콜니코프도 포르피리 페트로비치도 그들의 면담이 이런 식으로 끝나리라고는 도저히 예상할 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n나중에 그 장면을 떠올렸을 때, 라스콜니코프에게 그것은 이렇게 보였다.\n\n문 너머의 소란이 점점 커지더니, 갑자기 문이 조금 열렸다.\n\n\"뭐야?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"내가 분명히 일러두지 않았나…….\"\n\n한순간 아무 대답도 없었지만, 문가에 여러 사람이 있는 것이 분명했고, 누군가를 떠밀어 내고 있는 모양이었다.\n\n\"뭐냐고?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 불안한 듯 되풀이했다.\n\n\"죄수 니콜라이를 데려왔습니다.\" 누군가 대답했다.\n\n\"그자는 필요 없어! 데려가! 기다리라고 해! 여기서 뭐 하는 거야? 이런 무례한 짓이!\" 포르피리는 문 쪽으로 달려가며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 이자가…….\" 같은 목소리가 말을 꺼내다 갑자기 뚝 끊겼다.\n\n이 초, 그 이상은 아니게, 실제로 몸싸움이 벌어졌고, 그러더니 누군가 거칠게 떠밀자, 한 사내가, 몹시 창백한 얼굴로, 성큼성큼 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n이 사내의 행색은 첫눈에 매우 기이했다. 그는 아무것도 보이지 않는 듯 똑바로 앞만 응시했다. 그의 두 눈에는 결연한 빛이 번득였고, 동시에 그의 얼굴은 마치 처형대로 끌려가는 사람처럼 죽은 듯 창백했다. 핏기 없는 입술이 희미하게 실룩거렸다.\n\n그는 노동자처럼 차려입었고 중간 키에, 무척 젊고, 호리호리했으며, 머리는 둥글게 짧게 깎았고 야위고 메마른 이목구비를 지니고 있었다. 그가 떠밀어 냈던 사내가 그를 뒤따라 방으로 들어와 가까스로 그의 어깨를 붙잡았는데, 그는 간수였다. 하지만 니콜라이는 그의 팔을 뿌리쳤다.\n\n여러 사람이 호기심에 차서 문간으로 몰려들었다. 그중 몇몇은 안으로 들어오려 했다. 이 모든 일이 거의 눈 깜짝할 사이에 일어났다.\n\n\"물러가, 아직 일러! 부를 때까지 기다려!…… 어쩌자고 이렇게 일찍 데려온 거야?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 몹시 짜증스러운 듯, 그리고 마치 셈이 완전히 틀어진 듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n그러나 니콜라이가 갑자기 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐?\" 포르피리가 놀라며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"제가 죄인입니다! 죄는 제가 저질렀습니다! 제가 살인자입니다.\" 니콜라이가 다소 숨이 찬 듯, 그러나 꽤 큰 소리로 갑자기 또박또박 말했다.\n\n십 초 동안 마치 모두가 벙어리가 된 듯 침묵이 흘렀다. 간수마저 뒷걸음질 쳐, 기계적으로 문 쪽으로 물러나더니, 꼼짝 않고 섰다.\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 순간적인 망연자실에서 깨어나며 외쳤다.\n\n\"제가…… 살인자입니다.\" 니콜라이가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"뭐라고…… 네가…… 뭐라고…… 누구를 죽였다는 거냐?\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 분명 어리둥절해하고 있었다.\n\n니콜라이는 또다시 잠시 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"알료나 이바노브나와 그 동생 리자베타 이바노브나를, 제가…… 도끼로…… 죽였습니다. 정신이 캄캄해졌습니다.\" 그가 갑자기 덧붙이고는 다시 입을 다물었다.\n\n그는 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채였다. 포르피리 페트로비치는 한동안 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있다가, 갑자기 정신을 차리고는 청하지도 않은 구경꾼들을 손짓으로 물리쳤다. 그들은 순식간에 사라지고 문을 닫았다. 그러고 나서 그는 한쪽 구석에 서서 사납게 니콜라이를 응시하고 있던 라스콜니코프 쪽을 바라보더니, 그에게로 다가가다가, 문득 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 니콜라이를 보았다가 라스콜니코프를 보고 다시 니콜라이를 보았으며, 스스로를 억누르지 못하는 듯 후자에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"너무 서두르는군.\" 그가 거의 화가 난 듯 니콜라이에게 소리쳤다. \"나는 너에게 무슨 일이 일어났느냐고 묻지 않았어……. 말해 봐, 네가 그들을 죽였단 말이지?\"\n\n\"제가 살인자입니다……. 진술하고 싶습니다.\" 니콜라이가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 무엇으로 그들을 죽였나?\"\n\n\"도끼로요. 미리 준비해 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 이자가 서두르는군! 혼자서?\"\n\n니콜라이는 질문을 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n\"혼자서 한 거냐?\"\n\n\"네, 혼자서요. 그리고 미트카는 죄가 없고 거기에 가담하지도 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"미트카 얘기는 서두르지 마라! 아! 그런데 너는 그때 어쩌자고 그렇게 계단을 뛰어 내려갔나? 문지기들이 너희 둘을 마주쳤다던데!\"\n\n\"그건 추적을 따돌리려고요…… 미트카를 쫓아 달린 겁니다.\" 니콜라이가 마치 미리 답을 준비해 둔 듯 서둘러 대답했다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았어!\" 포르피리가 분한 듯 외쳤다. \"저건 제 입에서 나온 얘기가 아니야.\" 그는 혼잣말하듯 중얼거렸고, 갑자기 그의 시선이 다시 라스콜니코프에게 머물렀다.\n\n그는 니콜라이에게 어찌나 정신이 팔려 있었던지 한순간 라스콜니코프를 잊고 있었던 것이 분명했다. 그는 조금 당황했다.\n\n\"친애하는 로디온 로마노비치, 미안하오!\" 그가 라스콜니코프에게 휙 날아들었다. \"이래선 안 되겠소, 아무래도 가셔야겠소…… 더 계셔 봤자 소용없으니…… 내가…… 보다시피, 이런 뜻밖의 일이!…… 그럼 안녕히 가시오!\"\n\n그러고는 그의 팔을 잡고 문 쪽으로 안내했다.\n\n\"이런 건 예상 못 하셨겠지요?\" 라스콜니코프가 말했는데, 그는 아직 상황을 완전히 파악하지는 못했어도 용기를 되찾은 터였다.\n\n\"당신도 예상 못 하긴 마찬가지였소, 친구. 손이 이렇게 떨리는 걸 좀 보시오! 헤헤!\"\n\n\"당신도 떨고 있군요, 포르피리 페트로비치!\"\n\n\"그렇소, 나도 떨고 있소. 나도 예상 못 했으니까.\"\n\n그들은 이미 문가에 와 있었다. 포르피리는 라스콜니코프가 어서 가 주기를 안달했다.\n\n\"그런데 당신의 그 깜짝 선물 말이오, 나한테 보여 주지 않으시려오?\" 라스콜니코프가 비꼬듯 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 묻는 동안에도 이가 딱딱 부딪치는구려, 헤헤! 당신은 빈정대기 좋아하는 사람이야! 자, 또 봅시다!\"\n\n\"이젠 작별 인사를 해도 될 것 같은데요!\"\n\n\"그건 하느님 손에 달린 일이지.\" 포르피리가 부자연스러운 미소를 지으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n사무실을 지나며 라스콜니코프는 많은 사람이 자신을 쳐다보고 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그들 중에서 그는 그날 밤 자신이 경찰서로 가자고 했던 바로 그 집의 문지기 둘을 보았다. 그들은 그곳에 서서 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 그가 막 계단에 들어서자마자 등 뒤에서 포르피리 페트로비치의 목소리가 들렸다. 돌아보니, 후자가 숨을 헐떡이며 그를 뒤쫓아 달려오고 있었다.\n\n\"한마디만, 로디온 로마노비치. 나머지야 모두 하느님 손에 달렸지만, 형식상 당신에게 물어봐야 할 게 몇 가지 있어서…… 그러니 우리 또 만나게 되겠지요, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n그러고는 포르피리가 미소를 띤 채 그와 마주 보며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"안 그렇소?\" 그가 또 한 번 덧붙였다.\n\n그는 무언가 더 말하고 싶어 하는 듯했으나, 차마 입 밖에 내지 못했다.\n\n\"방금 일은 용서해 주셔야겠습니다, 포르피리 페트로비치…… 제가 그만 성질을 부렸으니까요.\" 라스콜니코프가 입을 열었는데, 그는 이제 용기를 되찾은 나머지 자신의 침착함을 과시하고 싶은 마음을 도무지 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀을, 무슨 말씀을.\" 포르피리가 거의 즐거운 듯 대답했다. \"나 역시도…… 나도 성질이 고약하오, 인정하지! 하지만 우리 또 만날 거요. 하느님 뜻이라면, 우린 서로 아주 자주 보게 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그래서 서로를 속속들이 알게 되겠지요?\" 라스콜니코프가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그렇소, 서로를 속속들이 알게 되겠지.\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 맞장구치며 두 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 라스콜니코프를 진지하게 바라보았다. \"이제 생일 잔치에라도 가시는 길이오?\"\n\n\"장례식에요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지, 장례식이지! 몸조심하고, 어서 쾌차하시오.\"\n\n\"당신에게는 무슨 인사를 해야 할지 모르겠군요.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했는데, 그는 계단을 내려가기 시작하다가 다시 돌아보았다. \"성공을 빌어 드리고 싶지만, 당신의 직무라는 게 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지요.\"\n\n\"왜 우스꽝스럽다는 거요?\" 포르피리 페트로비치는 돌아서서 가려던 참이었으나, 이 말에 귀가 번쩍 뜨이는 것 같았다.\n\n\"아니, 당신이 그 가엾은 니콜라이를 자백할 때까지 당신 식으로 심리적으로 얼마나 고문하고 들들 볶았을지 말입니다! 분명 밤낮으로 그를 닦달하며 그가 살인자라고 입증해 댔겠지요. 그런데 이제 그가 자백을 했으니, 당신은 또다시 그를 산 채로 해부하기 시작하겠지요. '거짓말이야.' 당신은 이렇게 말할 겁니다. '넌 살인자가 아니야! 그럴 리가 없어! 그건 네 입에서 나온 얘기가 아니야!' 그러니 우스꽝스러운 일이라는 걸 인정하셔야지요!\"\n\n\"헤헤헤! 그러니까 당신은 내가 방금 니콜라이에게 그건 네 입에서 나온 얘기가 아니라고 말한 걸 알아챘구려?\"\n\n\"어떻게 못 알아챌 수 있겠습니까!\"\n\n\"헤헤! 당신은 재치가 빠르군. 뭐든 알아채니 말이오! 정말 장난기 넘치는 머리를 가졌어! 게다가 늘 우스운 구석에 집착하고…… 헤헤! 작가들 중에서는 그게 고골의 두드러진 특징이었다더군.\"\n\n\"네, 고골이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 고골이지…… 당신과 다시 만날 날을 고대하겠소.\"\n\n\"저도요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 곧장 집으로 걸어갔다. 그는 어찌나 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽이고 어리둥절했던지 집에 도착해서는 십오 분 동안이나 소파에 앉아 생각을 가다듬으려 애썼다. 그는 니콜라이에 대해 생각하려 들지 않았다. 그는 멍해져 있었다. 그는 그 자백이 무언가 설명할 수 없고, 놀라운 것이라고, 자신의 이해를 넘어서는 것이라고 느꼈다. 그러나 니콜라이의 자백은 엄연한 사실이었다. 이 사실의 결과가 그에게는 당장 분명히 보였다. 그 거짓이 들통나지 않을 리 없고, 그러면 그들은 또다시 그를 쫓을 것이다. 적어도 그때까지는 그가 자유로운 몸이니 자신을 위해 무언가 해야 했다. 위험이 코앞에 닥쳐 있었으니까.\n\n그런데 얼마나 코앞일까? 그의 처지가 차츰 분명해졌다. 방금 전 포르피리와 겪은 장면의 큰 윤곽을 어렴풋이 떠올리자, 그는 또다시 공포로 몸서리치지 않을 수 없었다. 물론, 그는 아직 포르피리의 의도를 전부 알지는 못했고, 그의 속셈을 다 꿰뚫어 볼 수도 없었다. 그러나 그는 이미 자신의 패를 일부 내보였고, 포르피리의 그 '유도'가 자신에게 얼마나 무서운 것이었는지는 라스콜니코프 자신보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 없었다. 조금만 더 갔더라면 그는 자신을 완전히, 구체적으로 드러내 버렸을지도 모른다. 그의 신경질적인 기질을 알고 첫눈에 그를 꿰뚫어 본 포르피리는, 비록 대담한 도박을 벌이고 있었지만, 이길 수밖에 없었다. 라스콜니코프가 자신을 심각하게 위태롭게 만들었다는 것은 부인할 수 없었으나, 아직 그 어떤 사실도 드러나지 않았고, 확실한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그런데 그가 이 상황을 제대로 보고 있는 것일까? 잘못 짚은 건 아닐까? 포르피리는 무엇을 알아내려 했던 걸까? 그가 정말로 자신을 위해 어떤 깜짝 선물을 준비해 두었던 걸까? 그게 대체 뭐였을까? 그가 정말로 무언가를 기대하고 있었던 걸까, 아니었을까? 니콜라이가 뜻밖에 나타나지 않았더라면 그들은 어떻게 헤어졌을까?\n\n포르피리는 거의 모든 패를 다 보여 주었다. 물론, 그것을 보여 주면서 무언가를 걸기는 했다. 그리고 만일 그가 정말로 소맷자락에 감춰 둔 것이 있었다면(라스콜니코프는 곰곰이 생각했다) 그것도 보여 주었을 것이다. 그 '깜짝 선물'이란 무엇이었을까? 농담이었을까? 무슨 의미가 있었던 걸까? 그 안에 무슨 사실 같은 것, 확실한 증거 같은 것이 숨겨져 있었을까? 어제의 그 방문객? 그자는 어떻게 된 걸까? 오늘 어디 있는 걸까? 만일 포르피리에게 정말 어떤 증거가 있다면, 그건 그자와 연관된 것이 틀림없다…….\n\n그는 두 팔꿈치를 무릎에 괸 채 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 소파에 앉아 있었다. 그는 여전히 신경질적으로 떨고 있었다. 마침내 그는 일어나 모자를 집어 들고, 잠시 생각하다가, 문 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n그는 적어도 오늘만큼은 위험에서 벗어났다고 여겨도 좋으리라는 일종의 예감이 들었다. 그는 문득 거의 기쁨에 가까운 감정을 느꼈다. 그는 어서 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가고 싶었다. 물론 장례식에는 너무 늦겠지만, 추도 만찬에는 늦지 않게 도착할 테고, 거기서 곧바로 소냐를 보게 될 것이다.\n\n그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 잠시 생각했고, 한순간 고통스러운 미소가 그의 입가에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"오늘! 오늘이다.\" 그는 스스로에게 되뇌었다. \"그래, 오늘! 그래야만 해…….\"\n\n그러나 그가 막 문을 열려는 순간, 문이 저절로 열리기 시작했다. 그는 흠칫 놀라 뒤로 물러섰다. 문이 부드럽고 천천히 열리더니, 갑자기 한 사람의 모습이 나타났다. 어제 그 지하에서 온 방문객이었다.\n\n사내는 문간에 서서, 말없이 라스콜니코프를 바라보다가, 방 안으로 한 걸음 들어섰다. 그는 어제와 똑같았다. 똑같은 행색에, 똑같은 옷차림이었으나, 얼굴에는 커다란 변화가 있었다. 그는 풀이 죽어 보였고 깊이 한숨을 내쉬었다. 만일 그가 손을 뺨에 갖다 대고 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이기만 했더라면, 영락없이 농촌 아낙처럼 보였을 것이다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 라스콜니코프가 공포에 얼어붙은 채 물었다. 사내는 여전히 말이 없다가, 갑자기 거의 땅에 닿도록 허리를 숙여, 손가락으로 바닥을 짚었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고?\" 라스콜니코프가 외쳤다.\n\n\"제가 죄를 지었습니다.\" 사내가 나직이 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"못된 생각을 품어서요.\"\n\n그들은 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"제가 화가 났었습니다. 당신이, 어쩌면 술에 취해서 왔을 때, 문지기들에게 경찰서로 가자고 하고 피에 대해 물었을 때, 저는 그들이 당신을 보내 주고 당신을 술꾼으로 여긴 것에 화가 났습니다. 어찌나 화가 났던지 잠을 다 설쳤지요. 그러다 주소를 기억해 내고 어제 이리로 와서 당신을 찾았습니다…….\"\n\n\"누가 왔다고?\" 라스콜니코프가 가로막으며, 순식간에 기억을 떠올리기 시작했다.\n\n\"제가요, 제가 당신에게 못된 짓을 했습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신이 그 집에서 온 사람이오?\"\n\n\"저는 그들과 함께 대문 앞에 서 있었지요…… 기억 안 나십니까? 우리는 그 집에서 몇 년째 장사를 해 오고 있습니다. 가죽을 무두질하고 다듬는 일을 하는데, 일감을 집으로 가져와서…… 무엇보다 제가 화가 났던 건…….\"\n\n그러자 그저께 대문간에서 있었던 그 장면 전체가 라스콜니코프의 머릿속에 또렷이 떠올랐다. 그는 그곳에 문지기들 말고도 여러 사람이, 그중에는 여자들도 있었음을 기억해 냈다. 그는 한 목소리가 자신을 곧장 경찰서로 데려가자고 했던 것을 기억했다. 그는 말한 사람의 얼굴을 떠올릴 수 없었고, 지금도 그를 알아보지 못했지만, 자신이 돌아서서 그에게 무언가 대꾸했던 것은 기억했다…….\n\n그러니까 이것이 어제 그 공포의 정체였다. 무엇보다 끔찍한 생각은, 자신이 그토록 하찮은 정황 하나 때문에 실제로 거의 파멸할 뻔했다는, 거의 자기 손으로 끝장낼 뻔했다는 것이었다. 그러니까 이 사내는 자신이 셋집과 핏자국에 대해 물었던 것 외에는 아무것도 말할 수 없었던 것이다. 그러니까 포르피리에게도 그 헛소리 말고는, 양쪽으로 다 통하는 그 심리학 말고는 아무것도 없었던 것이다. 확실한 것은 아무것도. 그러니 더 이상 사실이 드러나지 않는다면(그래서는 안 된다, 절대 안 된다!) 그렇다면…… 그렇다면 그들이 자신에게 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가? 설령 자신을 체포한다 해도 어떻게 유죄를 입증할 수 있단 말인가? 그리고 포르피리는 그때 셋집에 대해서 방금 막 들었을 뿐, 그 전에는 그것을 알지 못했던 것이다.\n\n\"포르피리에게…… 내가 거기 갔었다고 말한 게 당신이오?\" 그가 갑작스러운 생각에 충격을 받아 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 포르피리요?\"\n\n\"수사과의 책임자 말이오.\"\n\n\"네. 문지기들은 거기 가지 않았지만, 제가 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"오늘?\"\n\n\"당신보다 이 분 먼저 도착했지요. 그리고 저는 들었습니다, 그가 당신을 어떻게 들들 볶는지 다 들었어요.\"\n\n\"어디서? 뭘? 언제?\"\n\n\"아니, 옆방에서요. 저는 내내 거기 앉아 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 아니, 그렇다면 당신이 그 깜짝 선물이었군? 한데 어떻게 그런 일이? 맙소사!\"\n\n\"문지기들이 제 말대로 하기 싫어하는 걸 보고서요.\" 사내가 말을 시작했다. \"너무 늦었다, 그리고 우리가 그때 가지 않았다고 어쩌면 화를 낼지도 모른다, 하고 그들이 말하더군요. 저는 화가 나서 잠을 다 설쳤고, 이리저리 알아보기 시작했지요. 그리고 어제 어디로 가야 할지 알아내고는, 오늘 갔습니다. 처음 갔을 때는 그가 없었고, 한 시간 뒤에 가니 저를 만나 줄 수 없다더군요. 세 번째로 갔더니, 저를 안으로 들여보내 주었습니다. 저는 모든 것을, 일어난 그대로 그에게 알렸지요. 그러자 그가 방 안을 폴짝폴짝 뛰어다니며 자기 가슴을 주먹으로 치기 시작했습니다. '이 악당 같은 놈들, 대체 무슨 짓이냐? 내가 그걸 알았더라면 그놈을 체포했을 텐데!' 그러더니 밖으로 뛰어나가, 누군가를 불러서 구석에서 그와 이야기를 나누고, 그런 다음 저를 향해 돌아서서, 야단치고 캐물었습니다. 저를 잔뜩 나무라더군요. 그래서 저는 그에게 모든 것을 말했고, 당신이 어제 제 말에 한마디도 감히 대꾸하지 못했다는 것과 저를 알아보지 못했다는 것도 말했습니다. 그러자 그가 또다시 뛰어다니기 시작하며 연신 자기 가슴을 치고, 화를 내고 뛰어다니더니, 당신이 왔다는 전갈이 오자 저더러 옆방으로 들어가라고 했습니다. '거기 좀 앉아 있게.' 그가 말했어요. '무슨 소리를 듣든 꼼짝 마.' 그러고는 거기에 의자를 놓아 주고 저를 가두었습니다. '어쩌면 자네를 부를지도 모르니까.' 그가 말했지요. 그리고 니콜라이가 끌려오자, 당신이 가자마자 저를 내보내 주었습니다. '내가 다시 사람을 보내 자네를 부르고 심문하겠네.' 하고 그가 말하더군요.\"\n\n\"그러면 그가 당신이 거기 있는 동안 니콜라이를 심문했소?\"\n\n\"그가 당신을 내보낸 것처럼 저를 내보낸 다음에야 니콜라이와 이야기했습니다.\"\n\n사내는 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 다시 갑자기 허리를 숙여 손가락으로 바닥을 짚었다.\n\n\"제 못된 생각과 중상을 용서해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 당신을 용서하시기를.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다.\n\n그리고 그가 이렇게 말하자, 사내는 다시 허리를 숙였으나, 이번에는 땅에 닿도록은 아니었고, 천천히 돌아서서 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"이제 모든 게 양쪽으로 다 통하는군, 이제 모든 게 양쪽으로 다 통해.\" 라스콜니코프가 되뇌었고, 그는 그 어느 때보다 자신감에 차서 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"이제 한판 싸워 보는 거다.\" 그가 계단을 내려가며 표독스러운 미소를 띤 채 말했다. 그의 표독스러움은 자기 자신을 겨냥한 것이었다. 그는 수치와 경멸을 느끼며 자신의 '비겁함'을 떠올렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n두냐와 그녀의 어머니와의 운명적인 면담이 있은 다음 날 아침, 표트르 페트로비치에게는 정신이 번쩍 들게 하는 일들이 닥쳐왔다. 무척이나 불쾌한 일이었으나, 그는 바로 전날까지만 해도 환상적이고 믿기 어려운 것으로만 여겨졌던 일을 돌이킬 수 없는 사실로 조금씩 받아들이지 않을 수 없었다. 상처 입은 허영심이라는 검은 뱀이 밤새도록 그의 심장을 갉아먹고 있었던 것이다. 침대에서 일어나자마자 표트르 페트로비치는 곧장 거울을 들여다보았다. 그는 황달에 걸린 것이 아닐까 두려웠다. 그러나 건강은 아직 멀쩡해 보였고, 요즘 들어 다소 살이 오른 그 고결하고 깨끗한 피부의 얼굴을 바라보며, 표트르 페트로비치는 한순간 또 다른 신붓감을, 어쩌면 더 나은 신붓감을 찾을 수 있으리라는 확신에 진심으로 위안을 얻었다. 그러나 다시금 자신의 현재 처지가 떠오르자, 그는 고개를 돌리고 거칠게 침을 뱉었는데, 이는 그가 함께 묵고 있는 젊은 친구 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프의 입가에 비웃는 미소를 자아냈다. 표트르 페트로비치는 그 미소를 놓치지 않았고, 즉시 그것을 젊은 친구의 잘못으로 적어 두었다. 요즘 들어 그는 그에게 적잖은 흠을 적어 두던 참이었다. 어제 면담의 결과를 안드레이 세묘노비치에게 떠벌리지 말았어야 했다는 생각이 들자 그의 분노는 곱절로 커졌다. 그것은 충동과 짜증 때문에 욱하는 김에 저지른 두 번째 실수였다……. 게다가 그날 아침 내내 불쾌한 일들이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 이어졌다. 원로원에 걸려 있던 그의 소송에서도 차질이 기다리고 있었다. 특히 그를 짜증 나게 한 것은, 다가오는 결혼을 염두에 두고 빌려 자비를 들여 새로 단장하던 그 셋집의 주인이었다. 부유한 독일인 상인인 주인은, 표트르 페트로비치가 사실상 새로 단장된 셋집을 도로 넘겨주는 셈인데도 막 서명한 계약을 파기한다는 생각은 받아들이려 하지 않고, 위약금 전액을 요구하며 고집을 부렸다. 마찬가지로 가구상도, 사들였으나 아직 셋집으로 옮기지 않은 가구의 할부금을 단 일 루블도 돌려주려 하지 않았다.\n\n'고작 가구 때문에 결혼이라도 해야 한단 말인가?' 표트르 페트로비치는 이를 갈았고, 그와 동시에 다시금 절망적인 희망의 빛이 번뜩였다. '정말 이 모든 것이 그렇게 돌이킬 수 없이 끝나 버린 걸까? 다시 한번 애써 볼 여지는 없는 것일까?' 두냐를 생각하자 그의 심장에 관능적인 통증이 짜릿하게 스쳤다. 그 순간 그는 고통을 견디고 있었으니, 만일 바라기만 해도 라스콜니코프를 당장 죽일 수 있었다면, 표트르 페트로비치는 즉각 그 소원을 입에 올렸으리라.\n\n'그들에게 돈을 주지 않은 것도 내 실수였어.' 그는 레베쟈트니코프의 방으로 풀이 죽어 돌아오며 생각했다. '그런데 도대체 왜 내가 그렇게 유대인처럼 굴었을까? 그건 잘못된 절약이었어! 나는 그들을 한 푼도 없이 두어서 나를 자기들의 구세주로 여기게 만들 작정이었는데, 꼴이 이게 뭐람! 흥! 혼수며 선물이며, 자질구레한 장식품, 화장 도구함, 보석, 옷감 따위, 그리고 크노프 상점과 영국 상점에서 파는 그런 잡동사니에 천오백 루블쯤만 썼더라면, 내 입지는 더 나았을 테고…… 더 단단했겠지! 그들이 나를 그렇게 쉽게 거절하지는 못했을 거야! 그들은 파혼하게 되면 돈과 선물을 돌려줘야 한다고 느낄 만한 부류의 사람들이고, 그러기가 쉽지 않았을 텐데! 그리고 양심이 그들을 찔렀겠지. 지금껏 이토록 너그럽고 섬세했던 사람을 어떻게 내칠 수 있단 말인가?…… 흠! 내가 큰 실수를 저질렀군.'\n\n그러고는 다시 이를 갈며, 표트르 페트로비치는 스스로를 바보라고 불렀다 — 물론 소리 내어서는 아니었다.\n\n그는 전보다 갑절이나 짜증과 화가 치민 채 집으로 돌아왔다. 카테리나 이바노브나의 집에서 벌어지는 장례 만찬 준비가 지나는 길에 그의 호기심을 자극했다. 그는 전날 그 일에 대해 들은 바 있었고, 사실 자신도 초대받은 것 같다고 어렴풋이 느꼈으나, 자기 걱정에 빠져 있던 터라 신경 쓰지 않았던 것이다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 묘지에 가 있는 동안 식탁을 차리느라 분주하던 리페베흐젤 부인에게 물어보니, 잔치가 대단한 규모로 차려질 것이며, 세입자들이 모두 초대받았고, 그중에는 고인을 알지도 못하던 이들도 있으며, 카테리나 이바노브나와 전에 다투었던 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프조차 초대받았고, 자기, 즉 표트르 페트로비치는 초대받았을 뿐 아니라 가장 중요한 세입자인 만큼 손꼽아 기다려지고 있다는 것이었다. 아말리아 이바노브나 본인도 최근의 불쾌한 일에도 불구하고 정중히 초대받았고, 그래서 그녀는 준비에 무척 바빴으며 거기에 진심으로 즐거워하고 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 한껏 빼입었으니, 온통 새 검은 비단옷 차림으로 그것을 자랑스러워했다. 이 모든 것이 표트르 페트로비치에게 어떤 생각을 떠올리게 했고, 그는 다소 생각에 잠긴 채 자기 방으로, 아니 더 정확히는 레베쟈트니코프의 방으로 들어갔다. 그는 라스콜니코프도 손님 중 하나로 올 것이라는 사실을 알게 되었던 것이다.\n\n안드레이 세묘노비치는 아침 내내 집에 있었다. 표트르 페트로비치가 이 신사를 대하는 태도는 기이했으나, 어쩌면 자연스러운 것이기도 했다. 표트르 페트로비치는 그와 함께 묵으러 온 날부터 그를 경멸하고 미워했으면서도, 동시에 그를 다소 두려워하는 것처럼 보였다. 그가 페테르부르크에 도착해 그와 함께 묵게 된 것은 단순히 인색함 때문만은 아니었으나, 어쩌면 그것이 주된 목적이었는지도 모른다. 그는 한때 자신이 후견하던 안드레이 세묘노비치가 어떤 흥미로운 모임들에서 중요한 역할을 하는 손꼽히는 젊은 진보주의자라는 소문을 들었는데, 그 모임의 활동은 지방에서는 전설처럼 떠돌았다. 그것이 표트르 페트로비치에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다. 모두를 경멸하고 모두의 정체를 폭로하는 그 강력하고 전지全知한 모임들은 오래전부터 그에게 묘하지만 막연하기 짝이 없는 불안감을 불러일으켰다. 물론 그는 그것들이 무엇을 뜻하는지에 대해 대략적인 개념조차 잡지 못했다. 그도 다른 모든 사람처럼, 특히 페테르부르크에 어떤 진보주의자들, 허무주의자들 따위가 있다는 말을 들은 바 있었고, 많은 사람들처럼 그 단어들의 의미를 터무니없을 만큼 부풀리고 왜곡했다. 지난 여러 해 동안 그가 무엇보다 두려워했던 것은 바로 정체를 폭로당하는 일이었고, 이것이 자기 사업을 페테르부르크로 옮긴다는 생각에 끊임없이 마음을 졸이게 한 주된 이유였다. 그는 어린아이들이 이따금 까닭 없이 공포에 사로잡히듯 이를 두려워했다. 몇 해 전, 그가 막 자기 경력을 시작하려던 무렵, 그는 지방에서 자신의 후원자였던 꽤 중요한 인물 두 사람이 잔인하게 폭로당한 두 가지 사건을 마주쳤다. 한 경우는 폭로당한 인물에게 큰 추문으로 끝났고, 다른 한 경우는 하마터면 심각한 곤경으로 끝날 뻔했다. 이런 까닭에 표트르 페트로비치는 페테르부르크에 도착하는 즉시 이 문제를 파고들어, 필요하다면 '우리의 젊은 세대'의 호의를 구함으로써 만일의 사태에 미리 대비할 작정이었다. 이 일에서 그는 안드레이 세묘노비치를 믿고 의지했으며, 라스콜니코프를 방문하기에 앞서 유행하는 표현 몇 가지를 주워 익히는 데 성공했다. 그는 곧 안드레이 세묘노비치가 평범한 멍청이라는 것을 알아챘으나, 그렇다고 해서 표트르 페트로비치가 결코 안심한 것은 아니었다. 설령 모든 진보주의자가 그와 같은 바보들이라고 확신했다 한들, 그의 불안이 가라앉지는 않았으리라. 안드레이 세묘노비치가 끈질기게 늘어놓는 온갖 학설이며 사상, 체계 따위에는 그는 아무런 흥미도 없었다. 그에게는 그 자신의 목적이 있었으니 — 그는 그저 이곳에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 당장 알아내고 싶을 뿐이었다. 이 사람들에게 힘이 있는가, 없는가? 그들에게서 두려워할 무언가가 있는가? 그들이 그의 어떤 사업이라도 폭로할까? 그리고 지금 그들이 공격하는 대상은 정확히 무엇인가? 그들이 정말로 힘이 있다면, 어떻게든 비위를 맞추어 구워삶을 수 있을까? 그렇게 하는 것이 옳은가, 옳지 않은가? 그들을 통해 뭔가 얻어 낼 수는 없을까? 요컨대 수백 가지 의문이 떠올랐다.\n\n안드레이 세묘노비치는 빈혈에 걸린, 연주창 기운이 있는 자그마한 사내로, 이상하게 아마빛이 도는 구레나룻을 길렀는데 그것을 무척 자랑스러워했다. 그는 관청 서기였고, 거의 늘 눈에 무슨 탈이 나 있었다. 마음은 다소 여린 편이었으나, 자신만만했고 때로는 말투가 극도로 거만해서, 그 자그마한 체구와 어울리지 않게 우스꽝스러운 인상을 주었다. 그는 아말리아 이바노브나가 가장 존중하는 세입자 중 하나였는데, 술에 취하지 않고 셋방세를 꼬박꼬박 냈기 때문이다. 안드레이 세묘노비치는 정말이지 꽤 멍청했다. 그는 진보의 대의와 '우리의 젊은 세대'에 열정적으로 몸을 던졌다. 그는 둔재들, 반쯤 살아 있는 미숙아들, 거만하고 어설프게 배운 멋쟁이들로 이루어진 수많고 잡다한 무리 가운데 하나였으니, 그들은 가장 유행하는 사상에 들러붙어 그것을 천박하게 만들 뿐이고, 아무리 진지하게 섬기는 대의라도 죄다 우스꽝스럽게 희화화하고 마는 자들이었다.\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 그토록 사람이 좋았으나, 그 역시 표트르 페트로비치를 싫어하기 시작했다. 이는 양쪽 모두 자신도 모르게 일어난 일이었다. 안드레이 세묘노비치가 아무리 단순하다 해도, 그는 표트르 페트로비치가 자기를 속이고 속으로는 경멸하고 있으며, '그가 제대로 된 인간이 아니'라는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다. 그는 그에게 푸리에의 체계와 다윈의 이론을 설명해 보려 했으나, 요즘 들어 표트르 페트로비치는 너무 비웃듯 듣기 시작했고 심지어 무례하게 굴기까지 했다. 사실 그는 본능적으로 레베쟈트니코프가 단순히 평범한 멍청이일 뿐 아니라 어쩌면 거짓말쟁이이기도 하며, 자기가 속한 모임에서조차 이렇다 할 인맥이 없고, 그저 남에게 전해 들은 이야기를 주워 모았을 뿐이라는 것을, 그리고 십중팔구 그가 자기 선전 활동에 대해서조차 잘 알지 못하리라는 것을 — 워낙 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽이었으니 — 짐작하기 시작했던 것이다. 그런 자가 누군가를 폭로하기에 적당한 인물이라니! 그런데 한 가지 짚고 넘어갈 것은, 표트르 페트로비치가 그 열흘 동안 안드레이 세묘노비치에게서 가장 기이한 칭찬을 기꺼이 받아들였다는 점이다. 이를테면 안드레이 세묘노비치가, 그가 새로운 '코뮌'의 설립에 기꺼이 기여할 준비가 되어 있다거나, 장차 자식들에게 세례를 주지 않겠다거나, 두냐가 결혼 한 달 만에 정부를 둔다 해도 묵인하겠다거나 하는 식으로 그를 추켜세웠을 때도, 그는 항의하지 않았던 것이다. 표트르 페트로비치는 자기에 대한 칭찬을 듣는 것을 어찌나 즐겼는지, 그런 미덕이 자기에게 돌려질 때조차 그것을 마다하지 않았다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 그날 아침 오 푼 이자가 붙는 채권 몇 장을 현금으로 바꿀 일이 있었고, 이제 탁자에 앉아 지폐 다발을 세어 보고 있었다. 거의 한 푼도 없던 안드레이 세묘노비치는 방 안을 거닐면서, 그 모든 지폐를 무관심하게, 심지어 경멸하듯 바라보는 척 스스로에게 꾸미고 있었다. 그 무엇으로도 표트르 페트로비치에게, 안드레이 세묘노비치가 정말로 그 돈을 동요 없이 바라볼 수 있으리라는 것을 납득시킬 수는 없었을 것이며, 한편 안드레이 세묘노비치는, 표트르 페트로비치가 자기에 대해 그런 생각을 품을 수 있고, 또 어쩌면 젊은 친구에게 그의 열등함과 둘 사이의 큰 차이를 일깨워 약 올릴 기회를 반기고 있으리라는 생각에 씁쓸한 마음을 누르지 못했다.\n\n비록 그, 안드레이 세묘노비치가 자신이 좋아하는 주제인 새로운 특별 '코뮌'의 설립에 관해 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했음에도, 그는 표트르 페트로비치가 믿기 어려울 만큼 건성이고 짜증스러워한다는 것을 알아챘다. 주판알을 딸각이는 사이사이 표트르 페트로비치의 입에서 떨어지는 짤막한 말들에는 누가 봐도 분명한, 무례하기까지 한 빈정거림이 배어 있었다. 그러나 '인도주의자' 안드레이 세묘노비치는 표트르 페트로비치의 언짢은 기분을 최근 두냐와의 파혼 탓으로 돌렸고, 바로 그 주제에 관해 이야기하고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그에게는 그 점에 관해 자기의 훌륭한 친구를 위로해 줄, 그리고 '틀림없이' 그의 발전을 도모해 줄 진보적인 할 말이 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"저쪽에서…… 그 과부 집에서 무슨 잔치인가가 준비되고 있는 모양이지요?\" 표트르 페트로비치가 가장 흥미로운 대목에서 안드레이 세묘노비치의 말을 가로막으며 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 모르셨어요? 아니, 제가 어젯밤에 그런 의식들에 대해 어떻게 생각하는지 말씀드렸잖아요. 그리고 그 여자가 당신도 초대했다던데요, 들었어요. 어제 그 여자랑 이야기하셨다면서요…….\"\n\n\"그 거지 같은 바보 여자가, 또 다른 바보인 라스콜니코프한테서 받은 돈을 죄다 이 잔치에 쏟아부으리라고는 정말 생각도 못 했군. 방금 지나오면서 거기 준비해 놓은 걸 보고 깜짝 놀랐어, 포도주들 좀 보게! 사람들도 여럿 초대했더군. 도가 지나쳐도 한참 지나쳤어!\" 표트르 페트로비치는 이 대화를 이어 가는 데 무슨 속셈이 있는 듯 계속 말했다. \"뭐라고? 나도 초대받았다고? 그게 언제였지? 기억이 안 나는걸. 하지만 난 안 갈 거야. 내가 왜 가? 나는 그저 어제 지나는 길에 그 여자한테 한마디 했을 뿐이야, 관청 서기의 가난한 미망인으로서 일 년치 봉급을 받을 수도 있겠다고. 아마 그 때문에 나를 초대한 거겠지, 안 그래? 헤헤헤!\"\n\n\"저도 갈 생각은 없습니다.\" 레베쟈트니코프가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지, 그 여자를 두들겨 팼으니! 망설일 만도 하군, 헤헤!\"\n\n\"누가 두들겨 팼다는 거예요? 누구를요?\" 레베쟈트니코프가 당황하여 얼굴을 붉히며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 자네가 한 달 전에 카테리나 이바노브나를 두들겨 팼다며. 어제 그렇게 들었는데…… 그러니까 자네의 신념이라는 게 고작 그 정도였군…… 그리고 여성 문제도 그리 건전하지는 못했고, 헤헤헤!\" 그러고는 표트르 페트로비치는 위안이라도 얻은 듯 다시 주판알을 딸각거리기 시작했다.\n\n\"그건 죄다 비방이고 헛소리예요!\" 그 주제에 대한 암시라면 늘 두려워하던 레베쟈트니코프가 외쳤다. \"전혀 그렇지 않았어요, 완전히 달랐다고요. 잘못 들으신 거예요. 그건 중상모략입니다. 저는 그저 제 몸을 지켰을 뿐이에요. 그 여자가 먼저 손톱을 세우고 달려들어서, 제 구레나룻을 죄다 뽑아 버렸다고요……. 누구든 제 몸을 지킬 권리는 있는 것 아니겠어요, 부디 그렇기를 바랍니다만. 그리고 저는 원칙상 누구에게도 제게 폭력을 쓰도록 결코 허락하지 않습니다, 그건 전제專制의 행위니까요. 그럼 제가 어떻게 했어야 합니까? 저는 그저 그 여자를 밀쳐 냈을 뿐이라고요.\"\n\n\"헤헤헤!\" 루진은 짓궂게 계속 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"당신은 본인 기분이 언짢으니까 자꾸 그러는 거예요……. 하지만 그건 헛소리고, 여성 문제하고는 아무런, 정말 아무런 상관도 없어요! 당신은 이해를 못 하시는군요. 사실 저는 여자가 모든 면에서, 심지어 체력에서까지 (요즘 그렇게들 주장하듯) 남자와 동등하다면, 그 점에서도 평등이 있어야 한다고 생각하곤 했어요. 물론 나중에 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그런 문제는 사실 제기될 필요조차 없는 것이었지요, 싸움이라는 게 있어서는 안 되고, 미래 사회에서 싸움이란 생각조차 할 수 없는 것이니까요……. 그러니 싸움에서 평등을 찾겠다는 건 우스운 일이겠지요. 제가 그렇게 멍청하지는 않아요…… 물론 싸움이 있긴 하지만…… 나중에는 없어질 테고, 다만 지금은 있다는 거지요…… 빌어먹을! 당신하고 얘기하면 이렇게 머리가 뒤죽박죽이 된다니까요! 제가 안 가는 건 그 때문이 아니에요. 저는 원칙상 안 가는 겁니다, 추도 만찬이라는 그 역겨운 관습에 가담하지 않으려고요, 바로 그래서예요! 물론 그걸 비웃으러 갈 수야 있겠지만요……. 거기에 사제들이 없을 거라는 게 유감이군요. 사제들이 있다면야 꼭 가겠는데.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 남의 식탁에 앉아서 그 식탁과 자네를 초대한 사람들을 모욕하겠다는 거로군. 어때?\"\n\n\"모욕이 아니라 항의지요. 저는 좋은 목적을 가지고 그렇게 할 겁니다. 저는 간접적으로나마 계몽과 선전의 대의를 도울 수 있을 거예요. 계몽과 선전을 위해 일하는 것은 모든 인간의 의무이고, 어쩌면 더 가혹하게 할수록 더 좋을 겁니다. 저는 씨앗을, 사상을 뿌릴 수 있을 거예요……. 그리고 그 씨앗에서 뭔가가 자라날 수도 있겠지요. 제가 어떻게 그들을 모욕한단 말입니까? 처음에는 기분이 상할지 몰라도, 나중에는 제가 그들에게 봉사한 거라는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 아시다시피 테레뱌예바는 (지금 코뮌에 있지요) 자기 가족을 떠나 …… 자신을…… 바칠 때, 부모에게 더는 관습적으로 살지 않고 자유 결혼에 들어가겠다고 편지를 썼다는 이유로 비난을 받았어요. 그게 너무 가혹했다느니, 부모를 좀 봐주어 더 다정하게 썼어야 했다느니 하면서요. 제 생각엔 그건 다 헛소리고 다정할 필요 따위는 없어요. 오히려 필요한 건 항의지요. 바렌츠는 칠 년을 결혼 생활을 했는데, 두 아이를 버리고 남편에게 편지로 단도직입적으로 말했어요. '나는 당신과는 행복할 수 없다는 걸 깨달았어요. 코뮌을 통한 또 다른 사회 조직이 존재한다는 사실을 내게 숨겨 나를 속인 것을 결코 용서할 수 없어요. 나는 이 사실을 한 위대한 마음을 지닌 남자에게서 최근에야 알게 되었고, 그에게 나 자신을 바쳐 그와 함께 코뮌을 세우고 있어요. 당신을 속이는 것은 떳떳하지 못하다고 여기기에 솔직히 말하는 거예요. 좋을 대로 하세요. 나를 되찾을 생각은 마세요, 너무 늦었으니까. 부디 행복하시길 바라요.' 이렇게 그런 편지는 써야 하는 거라고요!\"\n\n\"그 테레뱌예바라는 여자가, 자네가 세 번째 자유 결혼을 했다던 그 여자인가?\"\n\n\"아니요, 사실은 두 번째일 뿐이에요! 하지만 그게 네 번째라 한들, 열다섯 번째라 한들 무슨 상관이에요, 그런 건 다 헛소리지요! 그리고 제가 부모님의 죽음을 후회한 적이 있다면 바로 지금이에요. 저는 가끔 생각해요, 만약 부모님이 살아 계셨다면 그분들을 향해 얼마나 통렬한 항의를 겨누었을지를! 일부러 무슨 짓이라도 했겠지요…… 그분들에게 보여 드렸을 거예요! 그분들을 깜짝 놀라게 했겠지요! 정말이지 그럴 사람이 없다는 게 유감이라니까요!\"\n\n\"놀라게 한다라! 헤헤! 뭐, 그건 자네 마음대로 하게나.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 말을 가로막았다. \"하지만 이것 좀 물어보세, 자네 그 죽은 사내의 딸을 아나, 그 가냘파 보이는 자그마한 처녀 말일세? 그 처녀에 대해 사람들이 하는 말, 그게 사실이지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"그게 어떻다는 거예요? 제 생각엔, 그러니까 이건 제 개인적인 신념입니다만, 그게 여성의 정상적인 상태예요. 왜 아니겠어요? 그러니까 제 말은, 구분해야 한다는 거지요. 우리의 현재 사회에서야 그게 완전히 정상은 아니지요, 강제된 것이니까요. 하지만 미래 사회에서는 완전히 정상일 거예요, 자발적인 것이 될 테니까요. 지금 이대로도 그 처녀는 전적으로 옳았어요. 그녀는 고통받고 있었고 그게 말하자면 그녀의 자산, 그녀가 마음대로 처분할 완전한 권리를 가진 자본이었던 거지요. 물론 미래 사회에서는 자산 따위가 필요 없을 테지만, 그녀의 역할은 다른 의미를, 합리적이고 자기 환경과 조화를 이루는 의미를 갖게 될 거예요. 소피야 세묘노브나 개인으로 말하자면, 저는 그녀의 행동을 사회 조직에 대한 강력한 항의로 여기고, 그 점에서 그녀를 깊이 존경합니다. 그녀를 바라보면 정말이지 기쁘다니까요!\"\n\n\"자네가 그녀를 이 셋집에서 쫓겨나게 했다고 들었네만.\"\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 격분했다.\n\n\"그것도 또 다른 비방이에요.\" 그가 악을 썼다. \"전혀 그렇지 않았어요! 그건 죄다 카테리나 이바노브나가 지어낸 거라고요, 그 여자가 이해를 못 했으니까요! 그리고 저는 소피야 세묘노브나에게 결코 수작을 건 적이 없어요! 저는 그저 그녀를, 전혀 사심 없이 계발시키려 했을 뿐이에요, 그녀를 분발시켜 항의하도록 일깨우려고요……. 제가 원한 건 오직 그녀의 항의뿐이었고, 어차피 소피야 세묘노브나는 여기 머물 수 없었을 거예요!\"\n\n\"자네 코뮌에 들어오라고 그녀에게 권했나?\"\n\n\"당신은 자꾸 웃기만 하는데, 분명히 말씀드리지만 정말 부적절하군요. 당신은 이해를 못 해요! 코뮌에는 그런 역할 따위는 없어요. 코뮌은 그런 역할이 없어야 한다는 취지로 세워지는 것이니까요. 코뮌에서는 그런 역할이 본질적으로 변모되고, 여기서는 어리석은 것이 거기서는 분별 있는 것이 되며, 현재 조건에서 부자연스러운 것이 코뮌에서는 완전히 자연스러운 것이 됩니다. 모든 게 환경에 달려 있어요. 모든 게 환경이고 인간 자신은 아무것도 아니에요. 그리고 저는 지금까지도 소피야 세묘노브나와 사이가 좋은데, 이는 그녀가 결코 저를 자기에게 해를 끼친 사람으로 여기지 않았다는 증거지요. 저는 지금 그녀를 코뮌으로 끌어들이려 애쓰고 있어요, 다만 전혀, 전혀 다른 토대 위에서요. 뭘 그렇게 웃으세요? 우리는 우리만의 코뮌을, 특별한 코뮌을 더 넓은 토대 위에 세우려 하고 있어요. 우리는 신념에서 더 멀리 나아갔지요. 우리는 더 많은 것을 거부합니다! 그리고 그러는 한편 저는 여전히 소피야 세묘노브나를 계발시키고 있고요. 그녀는 아름다운, 정말 아름다운 성품을 지녔어요!\"\n\n\"그러면서 자네는 그녀의 그 훌륭한 성품을 이용하는 거로군, 어때? 헤헤!\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니에요! 오, 아니에요! 오히려 그 반대예요.\"\n\n\"오, 그 반대라! 헤헤헤! 거참 묘한 소리를 하는군!\"\n\n\"믿어 주세요! 제가 뭘 숨기겠어요? 사실 저 자신도 이상하다 싶을 만큼, 그녀는 제 앞에서 소심하고 정숙하고 또 현대적이라니까요!\"\n\n\"그래서 자네는 물론 그녀를 계발시키는 거겠지…… 헤헤! 그 모든 정숙함이 헛소리라는 걸 그녀에게 증명하려고?\"\n\n\"천만에요, 천만에! 얼마나 거칠게, 얼마나 멍청하게 — 이런 말씀 드려 죄송합니다만 — 당신은 계발이라는 말을 오해하고 있는 거예요! 맙소사, 당신은 아직도 어쩜 그렇게…… 미숙한지! 우리는 여성의 자유를 위해 분투하고 있는데, 당신 머릿속에는 단 한 가지 생각밖에 없군요……. 정숙함이니 여성의 수치심이니 하는 일반적인 문제는 그 자체로 무익하고 사실은 편견에 불과하니 제쳐 두더라도, 저는 제 앞에서의 그녀의 정숙함을 전적으로 인정합니다, 그건 그녀가 결정할 일이니까요. 물론 만약 그녀가 직접 저를 원한다고 말한다면 저는 스스로를 무척 운이 좋다고 여기겠지요, 그 처녀를 무척 좋아하니까요. 하지만 지금은 어느 누구도 저보다 그녀를 더 정중하게, 그녀의 존엄을 더 존중하며 대한 적이 없어요…… 저는 그저 희망을 품고 기다리는 거지요, 그게 다예요!\"\n\n\"자네는 그녀에게 차라리 뭔가 선물이라도 하는 게 훨씬 낫겠군. 장담컨대 그런 생각은 한 번도 못 해 봤을걸.\"\n\n\"당신은 이해를 못 해요, 이미 말씀드렸잖아요! 물론 그녀가 그런 처지에 있긴 하지만, 그건 별개의 문제예요. 전혀 다른 문제라고요! 당신은 그저 그녀를 경멸하는 거예요. 당신이 잘못 생각해서 경멸받아 마땅하다고 여기는 하나의 사실을 보고는, 같은 인간을 인도주의적으로 바라보기를 거부하는 거지요. 당신은 그녀가 어떤 성품인지 몰라요! 다만 안타까운 건, 그녀가 요즘 들어 책 읽는 것도, 책 빌리는 것도 아예 그만뒀다는 거예요. 제가 그녀에게 책을 빌려주곤 했거든요. 또 안타까운 건, 항의하는 데 있어 그토록 큰 에너지와 결단력을 — 그녀는 이미 한 번 그걸 보여 줬지요 — 지녔으면서도, 어떤 편견들과 어떤 어리석은 생각들에서 떨쳐 나올 만큼의 자기 확신이, 말하자면 자립심이 부족하다는 거예요. 그래도 그녀는 어떤 문제들은 철저히 이해하고 있어요, 이를테면 손에 입 맞추는 것에 관한 문제 같은 것 말이지요, 그러니까 남자가 여자의 손에 입 맞추는 건 여자에 대한 모욕이라는 거예요, 그게 불평등의 표시니까요. 우리는 그 문제로 토론을 했고, 제가 그걸 그녀에게 설명해 줬지요. 그녀는 프랑스의 노동자 조합에 관한 이야기도 주의 깊게 들었어요. 지금은 미래 사회에서 방에 들어가는 문제에 관해 설명하고 있는 중이고요.\"\n\n\"그게 대체 무슨 문제인데?\"\n\n\"우리는 최근에 이런 문제로 토론을 했어요. 코뮌의 일원이 남자든 여자든 다른 일원의 방에 언제든 들어갈 권리가 있는가…… 그래서 우리는 그럴 권리가 있다고 결론을 내렸지요!\"\n\n\"마침 곤란한 순간일 수도 있겠는걸, 헤헤!\"\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 정말로 화가 났다.\n\n\"당신은 늘 뭔가 불쾌한 것만 생각하는군요.\" 그가 진저리를 치며 외쳤다. \"쳇! 우리 체계를 설명하다가 사적인 사생활 문제를 너무 성급하게 언급한 게 어찌나 후회되는지! 그건 늘 당신 같은 사람들에게는 걸림돌이 돼요, 이해하기도 전에 비웃음거리로 만들어 버리지요. 게다가 또 그걸 어찌나 으스대는지! 쳇! 저는 그 문제는 체계에 대한 확고한 믿음이 생기기 전에는 초심자가 접근해서는 안 된다고 누차 주장해 왔어요. 그런데 부디 말씀해 보시지요, 당신은 시궁창에 도대체 뭐가 그리 부끄러울 게 있다고 보는 겁니까? 저라면 어떤 시궁창이든 가장 먼저 나서서 기꺼이 칠 거예요. 그리고 그건 자기희생의 문제도 아니에요, 그저 노동일 뿐이지요, 그 어떤 일에도 못지않고 라파엘로나 푸시킨의 일보다 훨씬 나은, 명예롭고 유익한 노동 말이에요, 더 유익하니까요.\"\n\n\"게다가 더 명예롭다, 더 명예로워, 헤헤헤!\"\n\n\"'더 명예롭다'는 게 무슨 뜻이에요? 저는 인간의 활동을 묘사하는 데 그런 표현들은 이해하지 못하겠어요. '더 명예롭다', '더 고귀하다' — 그런 건 다 제가 거부하는 낡은 편견이에요. 인류에게 쓸모 있는 것은 무엇이든 명예로운 거예요. 저는 단 한 단어만 이해합니다, 유익하다는 말이지요! 당신이야 실컷 비웃으셔도 좋지만, 그게 사실이라고요!\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 한바탕 큰 소리로 웃었다. 그는 돈을 다 세어 챙겨 넣은 참이었다. 그러나 지폐 몇 장은 탁자 위에 그대로 두었다. '시궁창 문제'는 이미 두 사람 사이에서 논쟁의 대상이 된 적이 있었다. 우스운 것은, 그 문제가 레베쟈트니코프를 정말로 화나게 한다는 점이었고, 그것이 루진을 즐겁게 하던 차에, 마침 그 순간 그는 유난히 젊은 친구를 화나게 하고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"어제 운이 나빴던 탓에 당신이 그렇게 기분이 언짢고 짓궂게 구는 거예요.\" 레베쟈트니코프가 불쑥 내뱉었는데, 그는 자신의 '자립'과 '항의'에도 불구하고 감히 표트르 페트로비치에게 대놓고 맞서지는 못했고, 여전히 이전 시절의 버릇이 밴 어느 정도의 존경심을 가지고 그를 대하고 있었다.\n\n\"자네 차라리 이걸 말해 주게.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 거만한 불쾌함을 드러내며 말을 가로막았다. \"자네…… 아니 그보다, 자네 정말로 그 젊은 처자와 충분히 친해서, 잠깐 여기 들르라고 청할 수 있겠나? 다들 묘지에서 돌아온 것 같던데…… 발소리가 들렸어……. 그 처자를 좀 만나고 싶네.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에요?\" 레베쟈트니코프가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그냥 만나고 싶어서. 나는 오늘이나 내일 여기를 떠날 텐데, 그래서 그녀에게 …… 에 관해 이야기하고 싶어서 말이야. 그래도 자네가 면담 자리에 함께 있어도 좋네. 사실 그러는 편이 더 낫겠어. 자네가 무슨 상상을 할지 알 수 없으니 말이야.\"\n\n\"저는 아무것도 상상하지 않을 거예요. 그저 물어본 것뿐이고, 그녀에게 하실 말씀이 있다면 그녀를 불러오는 것보다 쉬운 일은 없지요. 제가 곧장 가서 데려올게요, 그리고 제가 결코 방해되지 않을 거라고 믿으셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n오 분 후 레베쟈트니코프는 소냐와 함께 들어왔다. 그녀는 무척 놀란 채로, 늘 그렇듯 수줍음에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 들어왔다. 그녀는 이런 상황에서면 늘 수줍어했고 낯선 사람을 늘 두려워했는데, 어릴 적에도 그랬고 지금은 더더욱 그랬다……. 표트르 페트로비치는 그녀를 '정중하고 상냥하게' 맞이했으나, 그녀처럼 어리고 흥미로운 존재를 상대할 때 자기 같은 점잖고 비중 있는 사람에게 어울린다고 여기는, 어딘가 놀리는 듯한 친밀함의 기색이 묻어 있었다. 그는 서둘러 그녀를 '안심시키고' 탁자에서 자기 맞은편에 앉혔다. 소냐는 자리에 앉아 주위를 둘러보았다 — 레베쟈트니코프를, 탁자 위에 놓인 지폐들을, 그러고 나서 다시 표트르 페트로비치를 — 그리고 그녀의 두 눈은 그에게 못 박힌 채 떨어지지 않았다. 레베쟈트니코프가 문 쪽으로 걸어가려 했다. 표트르 페트로비치는 소냐에게 그대로 앉아 있으라고 손짓하고는 레베쟈트니코프를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"라스콜니코프가 저 안에 있나? 그가 왔어?\" 그가 그에게 속삭이듯 물었다.\n\n\"라스콜니코프요? 네. 왜요? 네, 거기 있어요. 방금 들어오는 걸 봤는데요……. 왜요?\"\n\n\"음, 자네에게 특별히 부탁하건대 여기 우리와 함께 있어 주고, 이…… 젊은 여자와 나만 단둘이 남게 하지 말아 주게. 나는 그저 그녀와 몇 마디 나누고 싶을 뿐이지만, 사람들이 그걸 어떻게 받아들일지 누가 알겠나. 라스콜니코프가 무슨 이야기든 옮기는 건 바라지 않아서 말이야……. 내 말뜻을 알겠나?\"\n\n\"알겠어요!\" 레베쟈트니코프는 요점을 알아챘다. \"네, 당신 말이 맞아요……. 물론 개인적으로 저는 당신이 불안해할 이유가 없다고 확신하지만…… 그래도 당신 말이 옳아요. 분명히 제가 남아 있겠습니다. 여기 창가에 서서 방해되지 않게 하지요…… 당신 말이 옳은 것 같아요…….\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 소파로 돌아와 소냐의 맞은편에 앉아 그녀를 주의 깊게 바라보더니, 극도로 위엄 있는, 심지어 엄숙하기까지 한 표정을 지었으니, 마치 \"공연한 착각은 하지 마시오, 아가씨\"라고 말하는 듯했다. 소냐는 당혹감에 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다.\n\n\"우선 말이오, 소피야 세묘노브나, 당신의 존경하는 어머님께 내 사과의 말을 전해 주겠소……. 그게 맞지요, 안 그렇소? 카테리나 이바노브나가 당신에게는 어머니 자리를 대신하고 있으니 말이오?\" 표트르 페트로비치는 상냥하게나마 대단한 위엄을 갖추고 운을 뗐다.\n\n그의 의도가 우호적이라는 것은 분명해 보였다.\n\n\"네, 그래요. 어머니 자리를 대신하고 계세요.\" 소냐가 소심하고 황급하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그러면 그분께 내 사과의 말을 전해 주겠소? 피치 못할 사정으로 인해 나는 자리를 비울 수밖에 없게 되어, 당신 어머님의 친절한 초대에도 불구하고 만찬에 참석하지 못하게 되었소.\"\n\n\"네…… 전해 드릴게요…… 곧장요.\"\n\n그러고는 소냐가 황급히 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"기다리시오, 그게 다가 아니오.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 그녀의 순진함과 예법에 대한 무지에 미소 지으며 그녀를 붙들었다. \"그리고 친애하는 소피야 세묘노브나, 만약 당신이, 내가 나 자신에게만 관계되는 이런 사소한 일로 당신 같은 사람을 굳이 번거롭게 했으리라 여긴다면, 당신은 나를 잘 모르는 거요. 내게는 또 다른 목적이 있소.\"\n\n소냐는 황급히 자리에 앉았다. 그녀의 두 눈은 다시 한순간 탁자 위에 남아 있는 회색과 무지갯빛 지폐에 머물렀으나, 그녀는 얼른 눈길을 돌려 표트르 페트로비치에게 고정했다. 그녀는, 특히 자기 같은 사람이 남의 돈을 바라보는 것이 끔찍이도 무례한 일이라고 느꼈다. 그녀는 표트르 페트로비치가 왼손에 들고 있던 금테 안경과, 그의 가운뎃손가락에 끼워진, 노란 보석이 박힌 묵직하고 무척 멋진 반지를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그러나 갑자기 그녀는 눈길을 돌렸고, 어디를 봐야 할지 몰라 결국 다시 표트르 페트로비치의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 말았다. 한층 더 위엄 있게 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 어제 우연히 지나는 길에 카테리나 이바노브나와, 그 가엾은 여인과 두어 마디 말을 나누었소. 그것만으로도 그녀가, 이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 비정상적인 처지에 놓여 있음을 충분히 알 수 있었소.\"\n\n\"네…… 비정상적이지요…….\" 소냐가 황급히 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 더 간단하고 알기 쉽게 말하자면, 병들었다고 하는 게 낫겠소.\"\n\n\"네, 더 간단하고 알기 쉬…… 네, 병들었어요.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그러니 인도주의적인 감정에서, 말하자면 동정심에서, 나는 그녀의 불행한 처지를 내다보고 어떻게든 그녀에게 도움이 되어 드리고 싶소. 이 가난에 찌든 가족 전체가 지금은 전적으로 당신에게 의지하고 있는 것 같은데, 맞소?\"\n\n\"여쭤봐도 될까요.\" 소냐가 자리에서 일어났다. \"어제 그분께 연금을 받을 수 있을지도 모른다는 말씀을 하셨나요? 그분이 제게 당신이 연금을 받게 해 주기로 했다고 말씀하셨거든요. 그게 사실인가요?\"\n\n\"천만에, 게다가 그건 정말 터무니없는 소리요! 나는 그저 그녀가, 재직 중에 사망한 관리의 미망인으로서 임시 보조금을 받을 수도 있겠다고 넌지시 비쳤을 뿐이오 — 그것도 후원자가 있어야만 가능한 일이지만……. 한데 보아하니 당신의 돌아가신 아버님은 근무 연한을 다 채우지도 못했고, 사실 최근에는 아예 근무도 하지 않으셨더군. 요컨대 무슨 희망이 있다 한들 그건 무척 덧없는 것이오, 그런 경우에는 보조금을 청구할 근거가 전혀 없으니, 어림도 없는 일이지요……. 그런데 벌써 연금을 꿈꾸고 있다니, 헤헤헤!…… 성미도 급한 부인이로군!\"\n\n\"네, 그래요. 그분은 잘 믿고 마음이 착해서, 마음씨가 고운 나머지 무엇이든 다 믿어 버리거든요, 그리고…… 또…… 그분은 원래 그런 분이세요…… 네……. 그분을 너그러이 봐주셔야 해요.\" 소냐가 말하고는 다시 가려고 일어섰다.\n\n\"한데 당신은 아직 내가 할 말을 듣지도 않았소.\"\n\n\"네, 못 들었어요.\" 소냐가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그럼 앉으시오.\" 그녀는 끔찍이도 당황했다. 세 번째로 그녀는 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"그녀의 불행한 어린것들과 함께 처한 그 처지를 보아하니, 아까도 말했듯이, 나는 내 힘이 닿는 한 기꺼이 도움이 되어 드리고 싶소, 그러니까 내 힘이 닿는 한에서지, 그 이상은 아니오. 이를테면 그녀를 위해 모금을 한다거나, 복권 같은 것을, 그런 부류의 무언가를 마련할 수도 있겠지요, 이런 경우에 친구들이나 심지어 남을 돕고자 하는 외부인들이 늘 그렇게 하듯 말이오. 바로 그 일에 관해 당신에게 이야기하려던 것이오. 해 볼 만한 일이지요.\"\n\n\"네, 네…… 하느님께서 그 일을 갚아 주실 거예요.\" 소냐가 표트르 페트로비치를 뚫어지게 바라보며 더듬더듬 말했다.\n\n\"해 볼 수야 있지만, 그건 나중에 이야기합시다. 오늘 당장 시작해도 되겠소, 오늘 저녁에 그 일을 의논하고 말하자면 그 토대를 마련합시다. 일곱 시에 내게 오시오. 레베쟈트니코프 씨도 우리를 도와주실 거라 믿소. 한데 한 가지 사정이 있어 미리 당신에게 일러두어야겠고, 그래서 소피야 세묘노브나, 감히 당신을 번거롭게 하여 여기로 오시라 한 것이오. 내 생각에 돈을, 정말이지 카테리나 이바노브나 본인의 손에 쥐여 주는 것은 위험하오. 오늘의 만찬이 그 증거지요. 그녀에게는, 말하자면, 내일 먹을 빵 한 조각도 없고…… 음, 장화든 신발이든, 아무것도 없는데, 오늘 자메이카 럼주를 사고, 게다가 내 생각엔 마데이라 포도주에…… 그리고 커피까지 산 것 같소. 지나오면서 봤지요. 내일이면 그 모든 게 다시 당신에게 떨어질 테고, 그들에겐 빵 한 조각도 없을 거요. 정말이지 터무니없는 일이오. 그러니 내 생각에는, 그 불행한 미망인이 돈에 대해 알지 못하도록, 이를테면 오직 당신만 알도록 모금을 하는 게 좋겠소. 내 말이 맞소?\"\n\n\"잘 모르겠어요…… 이건 오늘뿐이에요, 평생에 한 번……. 그분은 그토록 예를 갖추고, 추모를 기리고 싶어 하셨어요……. 그리고 그분은 무척 분별 있는 분이에요…… 하지만 당신 생각대로 하세요, 그러면 저는 정말, 정말로…… 그들도 모두…… 그리고 하느님께서 갚아 주실 거고…… 또 저 고아들도…….\"\n\n소냐가 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"그럼 좋소, 그 점을 명심해 두시오. 그리고 이제 당신 친척을 위해, 내가 개인적으로, 내가 마련할 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 돈을 받아 주겠소. 나는 이 일에 내 이름이 거론되지 않기를 무척 바라오. 자, 여기…… 나도 말하자면 나름의 걱정거리가 있어서, 더는 못 하오…….\"\n\n그러고는 표트르 페트로비치가 소냐에게 조심스레 펼친 십 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 내밀었다. 소냐는 그것을 받아 들고는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 벌떡 일어나, 뭔가를 중얼거리고는 작별 인사를 하기 시작했다. 표트르 페트로비치는 의례적으로 그녀를 문까지 배웅했다. 그녀는 마침내 동요하고 괴로워하며 방을 빠져나와, 당혹감에 어쩔 줄 모른 채 카테리나 이바노브나에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n이 모든 시간 동안 레베쟈트니코프는 창가에 서 있거나 방 안을 거닐며 대화를 방해하지 않으려 신경 썼다. 소냐가 가 버리자 그는 표트르 페트로비치에게 다가가 엄숙하게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"저는 모든 걸 듣고 또 보았어요.\" 그가 마지막 동사에 힘주어 말했다. \"그건 명예로운 일이에요, 그러니까 제 말은, 그건 인도주의적이라는 거지요! 당신은 감사받는 걸 피하려 하셨어요, 저는 봤다고요! 그리고 솔직히 저는 원칙상 사적인 자선에는 공감할 수 없지만, 그게 악을 뿌리 뽑기는커녕 오히려 조장하기까지 하니까요, 그래도 당신의 행동을 기쁘게 지켜봤다는 건 인정해야겠어요 — 네, 네, 마음에 들어요.\"\n\n\"그건 다 헛소릴세.\" 표트르 페트로비치가 다소 당황하여 레베쟈트니코프를 유심히 바라보며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"아니요, 헛소리가 아니에요! 어제 당신처럼 곤경과 괴로움을 겪고도 여전히 남의 불행에 공감할 수 있는 사람, 그런 사람은…… 비록 사회적 오류를 범하고 있다 해도…… 여전히 존경받을 만하지요! 정말이지 당신에게서 그런 걸 기대하지 못했어요, 표트르 페트로비치, 특히 당신의 사상에 따르면…… 오, 당신의 사상이 당신에게 얼마나 걸림돌이 되는지! 이를테면 어제의 불운에 당신이 얼마나 괴로워하는지 좀 보세요.\" 표트르 페트로비치에게 다시 애정이 솟구치는 것을 느낀 마음씨 순박한 레베쟈트니코프가 외쳤다. \"그런데 당신은 결혼을, 그 합법적인 결혼을 도대체 왜 하려는 거예요, 친애하는, 고결한 표트르 페트로비치? 어째서 결혼의 그 합법성에 그토록 매달리는 거예요? 자, 원하시면 저를 때리셔도 좋지만, 저는 기뻐요, 그 결혼이 성사되지 않은 게 정말로 기쁘다고요, 당신이 자유로워졌다는 게, 당신이 인류에게 완전히 못 쓸 사람은 아니라는 게요……. 보세요, 제가 속마음을 다 털어놓았어요!\"\n\n\"내가 자네의 그 자유 결혼에서 바보 취급을 당하고 남의 자식을 키우고 싶지 않으니까, 그래서 합법적인 결혼을 하려는 걸세.\" 루진이 그저 무슨 대답이라도 하려고 응수했다.\n\n그는 무언가에 골몰해 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"자식이요? 자식 이야기를 하셨군요.\" 레베쟈트니코프가 나팔 소리를 들은 군마처럼 펄쩍 뛰며 말을 꺼냈다. \"자식은 사회적 문제이고, 또 더없이 중요한 문제예요, 그건 동의해요. 하지만 자식 문제에는 또 다른 해결책이 있어요. 어떤 이들은 아예 자식 갖기를 거부하지요, 그게 가족이라는 제도를 떠올리게 하니까요. 자식 이야기는 나중에 하기로 하고, 지금은 명예 문제에 관해서라면, 솔직히 그건 제 약점이라는 걸 인정합니다. 그 끔찍하고 군대식인, 푸시킨식 표현은 미래의 사전에서는 생각조차 할 수 없는 거예요. 그게 도대체 무슨 뜻이에요? 그건 헛소리지요, 자유 결혼에는 속임수란 없을 테니까요! 그건 그저 합법적 결혼의 자연스러운 결과, 말하자면 그것의 교정책, 항의일 뿐이에요. 그러니 사실 그건 굴욕적인 일이 아니지요…… 그리고 만약 제가 언젠가, 터무니없는 가정입니다만, 합법적으로 결혼하게 된다면, 저는 오히려 그걸 기뻐할 거예요. 저는 제 아내에게 말하겠어요. '여보, 지금까지 나는 당신을 사랑했지만, 이제 나는 당신을 존경하오, 당신이 항의할 줄 안다는 걸 보여 줬으니 말이오!' 웃으시는군요! 그건 당신이 편견에서 벗어나지 못하기 때문이에요. 빌어먹을! 이제 저는 합법적 결혼에서 속는다는 게 어디가 불쾌한 일인지 알겠어요, 하지만 그건 그저, 둘 다 굴욕을 당하는 비열한 처지에서 비롯되는 비열한 결과일 뿐이에요. 자유 결혼에서처럼 속임수가 공공연하다면, 그건 존재하지 않는 거예요, 생각조차 할 수 없는 거지요. 당신의 아내는, 당신이 그녀의 행복을 가로막거나 그녀의 새 남편 때문에 그녀에게 복수할 수 없는 사람이라고 여김으로써, 오히려 자기가 당신을 얼마나 존경하는지를 증명할 뿐이에요. 빌어먹을! 저는 가끔 꿈꾼답니다, 만약 제가 결혼하게 된다면, 푸! 그러니까 만약 제가, 합법적이든 아니든, 그건 매한가지지요, 결혼하게 된다면, 저는 제 아내가 직접 정부를 찾지 못했을 경우 제가 그녀에게 정부를 마련해 줄 거예요. '여보,' 하고 저는 말하겠지요, '나는 당신을 사랑하지만, 그보다 더, 나는 당신이 나를 존경해 주기를 바라오. 자, 보시오!' 제 말이 맞지 않나요?\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 들으면서 낄낄 웃었으나, 그다지 즐거워하지는 않았다. 사실 그는 그 말을 거의 듣고 있지도 않았다. 그는 다른 무언가에 골몰해 있었고, 마침내 레베쟈트니코프조차 그것을 알아챘다. 표트르 페트로비치는 흥분한 듯 보였고 두 손을 비벼 댔다. 레베쟈트니코프는 이 모든 것을 기억해 두었고 나중에 그것을 곰곰이 되새겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n카테리나 이바노브나의 어지러운 머릿속에서 도대체 무엇이 그 어처구니없는 만찬이라는 생각을 불러일으켰는지를 정확히 설명하기란 어려운 일이다. 마르멜라도프의 장례를 위해 라스콜니코프가 건넨 이십 루블 가운데 거의 십 루블이 그 일에 탕진되었다. 어쩌면 카테리나 이바노브나는 고인의 명복을 “격식에 맞게” 빌어야 한다고, 그래서 모든 하숙인들이, 그리고 누구보다 아말리아 이바노브나가 “그가 결코 그들보다 못한 사람이 아니며, 어쩌면 그들보다 훨씬 윗사람이었다”는 것을, 그리고 누구도 “그를 깔볼” 권리가 없다는 것을 알아야 한다고 여겼는지도 모른다. 어쩌면 그 핵심은 그 특유의 “가난뱅이의 자존심”이었으리라. 그것은 많은 가난한 이들로 하여금 그저 “남들처럼” 하기 위해, 그리고 “업신여김을 당하지” 않기 위해, 어떤 전통적인 사회적 의례에 마지막 한 푼까지 쏟아붓게 만드는 것이다. 또한 이 기회에, 모두에게 버림받은 것만 같았던 그 순간에, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그 “비참하고 경멸스러운 하숙인들”에게 자신이 “어떻게 일을 처리하는지, 어떻게 손님을 대접하는지”를 알고 있으며, 자신이 “점잖은, 거의 귀족적이라 해도 좋을 대령 가문에서” 자랐으며, 바닥을 쓸고 밤마다 아이들의 누더기를 빨라고 태어난 사람이 결코 아님을 보여 주고 싶어 안달했을 가능성도 매우 높다. 가장 가난하고 가장 의기소침한 사람들조차도 때로는 이러한 자존심과 허영심의 발작에 빠지곤 하는데, 그것은 거역할 수 없는 신경증적 갈망의 형태로 나타난다. 그리고 카테리나 이바노브나는 의기소침한 사람이 아니었다. 그녀는 환경에 의해 죽임을 당할 수는 있어도, 그 기개만은 꺾일 수 없었으니, 다시 말해 그녀는 위협당할 수도, 그 의지가 짓밟힐 수도 없는 사람이었다. 게다가 소냐는 그녀의 정신이 온전치 못하다고 말한 적이 있는데, 그것은 충분히 일리가 있었다. 그녀를 미쳤다고는 할 수 없었으나, 지난 한 해 동안 그녀는 너무도 시달려 온 탓에 정신이 과도하게 긴장되었을 수도 있었다. 의사들의 말로는, 폐병의 후기 단계는 지적 능력에 영향을 미치기 쉽다고 한다.\n\n포도주의 종류는 많지 않았고, 마데이라 포도주 같은 것도 없었다. 그래도 포도주는 있었다. 보드카와 럼주, 리스본 포도주가 있었는데, 모두 가장 질이 떨어지는 것들이었지만 양만은 넉넉했다. 전통적인 쌀밥과 꿀 말고도 서너 가지 요리가 있었고, 그중 하나는 팬케이크였는데, 모두 아말리아 이바노브나의 부엌에서 마련된 것이었다. 사모바르 두 개가 끓고 있어서, 만찬이 끝난 뒤에는 차와 펀치를 대접할 수 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 손수 식료품 구입을 챙겼는데, 하숙인 중 하나인 어느 가엾고 자그마한 폴란드 사람의 도움을 받았다. 그는 어쩌다 보니 리페베흐젤 부인의 집에 정착해 있던 사람이었다. 그는 즉시 카테리나 이바노브나에게 자기 몸을 내맡기다시피 했고, 그날 아침 내내, 또 그 전날 하루 종일을 제 다리가 닿는 한 빠르게 이리저리 뛰어다녔으며, 모두가 그 사실을 알아주기를 몹시도 바랐다. 사소한 일마다 그는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 달려갔고, 심지어 시장통까지 그녀를 찾아 헤맸으며, 시도 때도 없이 그녀를 “파니”라고 불러 댔다. 처음에는 이 “쓸모 있고 도량 넓은 사람”이 없었다면 일을 해내지 못했을 거라고 단언했던 그녀였지만, 결국에는 진절머리가 났다. 만나는 사람마다 가장 화려한 빛깔로 그려 내는 것이 카테리나 이바노브나의 특징 중 하나였다. 그녀의 칭찬은 너무 과장되어 때로는 듣는 이가 거북할 지경이었다. 그녀는 새로 알게 된 사람을 추켜세우려 온갖 사정을 지어내고는, 그것이 진짜라고 진심으로 믿어 버리곤 했다. 그러다가 별안간 환멸을 느끼고, 불과 몇 시간 전까지만 해도 그야말로 떠받들던 그 사람을 무례하고 경멸스럽게 내쳐 버리는 것이었다. 그녀는 본래 명랑하고 활기차며 화목을 사랑하는 기질이었지만, 끊임없는 실패와 불행을 겪으며, 모두가 평화롭고 기쁘게 살아야 하며 누구도 감히 그 평화를 깨뜨려서는 안 된다고 너무도 절실하게 바라게 된 나머지, 아주 작은 충돌, 아주 사소한 재난에도 거의 광란 상태에 빠지곤 했고, 가장 밝은 희망과 공상에서 한순간에 자신의 운명을 저주하고 발광하며 벽에 머리를 찧어 대는 지경으로 옮아가곤 했다.\n\n아말리아 이바노브나 역시 카테리나 이바노브나의 눈에 별안간 대단한 중요성을 띠게 되었고, 그녀로부터 각별한 존중을 받았는데, 아마도 아말리아 이바노브나가 그 준비에 온 마음을 다 쏟아부었기 때문이었을 것이다. 그녀는 식탁을 차리고, 식탁보며 그릇 따위를 마련하고, 제 부엌에서 요리를 하기로 했으며, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그 모든 것을 그녀의 손에 맡긴 채 자신은 묘지로 갔다. 모든 것이 잘 마련되어 있었다. 식탁보마저 거의 깨끗했고, 그릇이며 칼, 포크, 잔은 물론 갖가지 모양과 무늬로, 여러 하숙인들에게서 빌려 온 것이었지만, 식탁은 정해진 시각에 제대로 차려져 있었고, 아말리아 이바노브나는 자기 일을 훌륭히 해냈다는 뿌듯함에 검은 비단 드레스를 입고 새 상복 리본을 단 모자를 쓰고서, 돌아오는 사람들을 다소 자랑스러운 기색으로 맞이했다. 이 자랑스러움은, 정당한 것이긴 했으되, 어쩐 영문인지 카테리나 이바노브나의 비위를 거슬렀다. “마치 아말리아 이바노브나가 아니면 식탁을 차릴 수 없었던 것처럼!” 그녀는 새 리본을 단 모자도 마음에 들지 않았다. “이 멍청한 독일 여편네가, 제가 집주인이고 가엾은 하숙인들을 인심 써서 거들어 주기로 했다고 해서 우쭐대는 건가! 인심을 써서! 어이가 없어서! 카테리나 이바노브나의 아버지는 대령에다 거의 지사나 다름없는 분이어서, 때로는 마흔 명을 위한 식탁을 차리기도 했는데, 그때라면 아말리아 이바노브나, 아니 차라리 루드비고브나 같은 여자는 부엌에조차 발을 들이지 못했을 것이다.”\n\n그렇지만 카테리나 이바노브나는 당분간 자기 감정을 드러내기를 미루고, 그녀를 차갑게 대하는 것으로 만족했는데, 속으로는 반드시 아말리아 이바노브나의 콧대를 꺾어 제자리에 앉혀 놓아야겠다고 마음먹었으니, 도대체 저 여자가 자신을 뭐라고 여기고 있는지 알 수가 없었던 것이다. 카테리나 이바노브나를 짜증나게 한 또 다른 사실은, 초대한 하숙인들 가운데 장례식에 온 사람이 거의 없었다는 점이었다. 겨우 묘지로 뛰어 들어오는 데 성공한 폴란드 사람을 빼고는 말이다. 한편 추도 만찬에는 그들 가운데 가장 가난하고 가장 보잘것없는 자들이, 그 비참한 인간들이, 그것도 상당수가 멀쩡한 정신이 아닌 채로 나타났다. 더 나이 들고 더 점잖은 자들은, 마치 무슨 약속이라도 한 듯, 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 이를테면 모든 하숙인 중에서 가장 점잖은 사람이라 할 만한 표트르 페트로비치 루진은 나타나지 않았다. 그 전날 저녁 카테리나 이바노브나가 온 세상에다, 그러니까 아말리아 이바노브나며 폴렌카, 소냐, 그리고 그 폴란드 사람에게다 대고, 그가 막대한 재산과 광범위한 인맥을 지닌, 더없이 관대하고 고결한 사람이며, 자기 첫 남편의 친구였고 자기 아버지의 집에 손님으로 드나들던 사람이라고, 또 자신에게 상당한 액수의 연금을 받아 주려고 온갖 영향력을 동원하겠노라 약속했다고 떠벌렸음에도 불구하고 말이다. 짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 카테리나 이바노브나가 누군가의 인맥과 재산을 치켜세울 때면 거기에는 어떤 다른 속셈도 없이, 그야말로 사심 없이, 단지 칭찬받는 사람의 위신을 더해 주는 그 즐거움만을 위해서였다는 점이다. 아마도 루진을 “본받아”, “그 경멸스러운 인간 레베쟈트니코프 역시 나타나지 않았다. 도대체 제가 뭐라도 되는 줄 아나? 그는 그저 호의로, 그리고 표트르 페트로비치와 같은 방을 쓰고 있고 그의 친구이니 초대하지 않으면 거북할까 봐 불러 준 것뿐인데.”\n\n나타나지 않은 사람들 가운데에는 “점잖은 부인과 그 노처녀 딸”도 있었는데, 그들은 이 집에 하숙한 지 겨우 보름밖에 안 되었으면서도, 카테리나 이바노브나의 방에서 나는 소란과 시끄러움에 대해, 특히 마르멜라도프가 술에 취해 돌아왔을 때의 소동에 대해 여러 번 불평을 늘어놓았다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 이를 들은 것은 아말리아 이바노브나를 통해서였다. 아말리아 이바노브나는 카테리나 이바노브나와 다투며 식구들을 죄다 길거리로 내쫓아 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓던 중에, 그들이 “자기네 소동에 시달리는 그 점잖은 하숙인들의 발끝에도 못 미친다”고 그녀에게 소리쳤던 것이다. 그래서 카테리나 이바노브나는 이번에 그 “발끝에도 못 미친다”던 부인과 그 딸을 초대하기로 마음먹었으니, 우연히 마주쳤을 때 거만하게 고개를 돌려 버리던 그들에게, “자신이 생각과 감정에 있어서는 더 고결하며 악의를 품지 않는다”는 것을 알게 하고, 또 자신이 그런 식의 삶에 익숙한 사람이 아님을 보여 주려는 것이었다. 그녀는 만찬 자리에서 작고한 아버지의 지사직을 넌지시 언급하며 이 점을 그들에게 분명히 하려 했고, 동시에 자신을 마주쳤을 때 고개를 돌려 버린 것이 더없이 어리석은 짓이었음을 은근히 일러 줄 작정이었다. 그 뚱뚱한 퇴역 소령(실은 하급 퇴역 장교였다) 역시 자리에 없었는데, 듣자 하니 그는 지난 이틀 동안 “제정신이 아니었다”고 했다. 모인 사람들이라곤 그 폴란드 사람과, 얼굴에 부스럼이 돋고 기름때 낀 외투를 걸친 채 입 한번 떼지 못하고 지독한 악취를 풍기는 초라한 몰골의 서기 하나, 그리고 한때 우체국에서 일했으나 이제는 귀가 멀고 거의 눈도 보이지 않는 데다 아주 오래전부터 누군가에 의해 아말리아 이바노브나의 집에서 부양받아 온 노인 한 사람이었다.\n\n병참부의 퇴직 서기도 왔는데, 그는 술에 취해 더없이 꼴사납게 큰 소리로 웃어 댔고, 게다가 글쎄 — 조끼도 걸치지 않은 채였다! 손님 중 하나는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 인사조차 없이 곧장 식탁에 앉아 버렸다. 마침내 정장이 없어 실내복 차림으로 나타난 사람도 하나 있었지만, 이건 너무 심했던 터라 아말리아 이바노브나와 폴란드 사람의 노력으로 그를 내보내는 데 성공했다. 그러나 그 폴란드 사람은 아말리아 이바노브나의 집에 살지도 않고 이곳에서 누구도 본 적 없는 다른 폴란드 사람 둘을 데려왔다. 이 모든 것이 카테리나 이바노브나를 극도로 짜증나게 했다. “그렇다면 도대체 누구를 위해 이 모든 준비를 했단 말인가?” 손님들에게 자리를 내주느라 아이들 몫조차 식탁에 차려 주지 못했고, 그래서 어린 두 아이는 가장 구석진 자리의 긴 의자에 앉아 상자 위에 차려진 식사를 했으며, 다 큰 처녀인 폴렌카가 그들을 보살피며 먹여 주고, 잘 자란 아이들답게 콧물을 닦아 주어야 했다.\n\n사실 카테리나 이바노브나는 한층 더한 위엄으로, 심지어 거만함마저 곁들여 손님들을 맞을 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 그들 중 몇몇을 유난히 매서운 눈초리로 노려보다가, 거드름을 피우며 자리에 앉으라고 청했다. 자리에 없는 사람들에 대한 책임이 분명 아말리아 이바노브나에게 있다는 결론으로 성급히 치달은 그녀는, 그녀를 극도로 무심하게 대하기 시작했고, 상대는 즉각 그것을 알아채고 발끈했다. 이런 시작은 끝을 위해 결코 좋은 징조가 아니었다. 마침내 모두 자리에 앉았다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 그들이 묘지에서 막 돌아온 무렵에 들어섰다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 그를 보고 무척 기뻐했는데, 첫째로 그가 유일한 “교양 있는 손님이며, 모두가 알다시피 이 년 뒤면 대학교수가 될” 사람이었기 때문이고, 둘째로 그가 장례식에 참석하지 못한 것을 즉시 정중하게 사과했기 때문이었다. 그녀는 그야말로 그에게 달려들다시피 하더니, 자기 왼쪽에 앉혔다(아말리아 이바노브나는 오른쪽이었다). 요리가 제대로 돌아가는지, 모두가 한 입씩이라도 맛보는지 끊임없이 신경 쓰면서도, 또 일 분마다 그녀를 끊어 놓는, 지난 며칠 사이 더 심해진 듯한 그 고통스러운 기침에도 불구하고, 그녀는 만찬이 엉망이 된 데 대한 억눌린 감정과 정당한 분노를 라스콜니코프에게 반쯤 속삭이듯 서둘러 쏟아 냈고, 그 와중에도 손님들을, 특히 집주인 여자를 두고 활기차고 걷잡을 수 없는 웃음을 사이사이 터뜨렸다.\n\n“다 저 뻐꾸기 탓이에요! 누굴 말하는지 아시겠죠? 저 여자, 저 여자 말이에요!” 카테리나 이바노브나가 집주인 여자 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다. “저것 좀 봐요, 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 있잖아요, 우리가 자기 얘기를 하고 있다는 건 느끼면서도 알아듣지는 못하는 거예요. 흥, 부엉이 같으니! 하하! (콜록콜록콜록.) 그런데 저 모자는 왜 쓴 거람? (콜록콜록콜록.) 저 여자가 여기 와 줌으로써 자기가 나를 비호해 주고 영광을 베푸는 거라고 다들 여겨 주기를 바란다는 거, 눈치채셨어요? 나는 분별 있는 여자한테 부탁하듯 사람들을 좀 초대해 달라고, 특히 내 죽은 남편을 알던 이들을 불러 달라고 했는데, 저 여자가 데려온 멍청이 떼거리 좀 보세요! 굴뚝청소부 같은 작자들! 저기 얼굴에 부스럼 난 작자 좀 봐요. 그리고 저 비참한 폴란드 사람들, 하하하! (콜록콜록콜록.) 단 한 놈도 여기 코빼기를 들이민 적이 없어요, 나는 저들을 본 적도 없다고요. 도대체 무엇하러 여기 왔답니까? 줄지어 앉아 있는 꼴이라니. 이봐요, 판!” 그녀가 별안간 그들 중 하나에게 소리쳤다. “팬케이크는 좀 드셨어요? 더 드세요! 맥주도 좀 들고요! 보드카는 안 하시겠어요? 봐요, 벌떡 일어나서 절을 하잖아요, 가엾은 것들이 어지간히 굶주린 모양이에요. 괜찮아요, 먹게 둡시다! 어쨌든 소란은 안 피우니까, 다만 집주인 여자의 은수저가 정말 걱정이긴 하지만……. 아말리아 이바노브나!” 그녀가 별안간, 거의 큰 소리로 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. “혹시 댁의 수저가 없어지더라도 나는 책임 안 져요, 미리 일러두는 거예요! 하하하!” 그녀는 라스콜니코프 쪽으로 돌아서서 웃고는, 자기 농담에 몹시 신이 나 다시 집주인 여자 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다. “못 알아들었어요, 또 못 알아들었다고요! 입 벌리고 앉아 있는 꼴 좀 봐요! 부엉이, 진짜 부엉이라니까! 새 리본을 단 부엉이, 하하하!”\n\n이쯤에서 그녀의 웃음은 또다시 오 분이나 계속된 견딜 수 없는 기침 발작으로 변해 버렸다. 이마에는 땀방울이 맺히고 손수건은 피로 얼룩졌다. 그녀는 말없이 라스콜니코프에게 그 피를 보여 주었고, 숨을 돌리자마자 다시 극도로 흥분한 채, 두 뺨에 병적인 홍조를 띠고서 그에게 속삭이기 시작했다.\n\n“글쎄, 내가 그 부인과 딸을 초대하는 일에 대해서, 말하자면 더없이 섬세한 지시를 저 여자한테 내렸거든요, 누구 얘긴지 아시겠죠? 그건 지극한 세심함과 더없는 정중함이 필요한 일이었는데, 저 여자가 일을 어떻게 망쳐 놓았는지, 그래서 그 멍청한 여자가, 그 잘난 척하는 천박한 여편네가, 그 시골뜨기 보잘것없는 여자가, 그저 소령의 미망인이라는 이유로, 또 연금을 타 보겠다고 관청 문턱이나 닳도록 드나드는 주제에, 쉰 살에 얼굴에 분칠이나 해 대는(다들 아는 일이에요)……, 그런 인간이 와 줄 가치도 없다고 여기고는, 가장 평범한 예의범절이라면 응당 해야 할 초대장에 대한 답조차 보내지 않은 거예요! 도대체 표트르 페트로비치는 왜 안 오는지 모르겠네……. 그런데 소냐는 어디 있지? 어디 간 거야? 아, 저기 드디어 오는구나! 무슨 일이니, 소냐, 어디 갔었어? 네 아버지 장례식에까지 이렇게 시간을 안 지키다니 이상하구나. 로디온 로마노비치, 이 애가 당신 옆에 앉게 자리 좀 내주세요. 거기가 네 자리야, 소냐……, 먹고 싶은 거 들렴. 젤리를 곁들인 차가운 전채 요리를 좀 들어, 그게 제일 맛있으니까. 곧 팬케이크를 가져올 거야. 아이들한테는 좀 줬니? 폴렌카, 다 챙겼어? (콜록콜록콜록.) 됐다, 그럼. 착하게 굴어라, 리다, 그리고 콜랴, 발을 자꾸 꼼지락대지 말고 점잖은 신사처럼 앉아 있어. 무슨 말이니, 소냐?”\n\n소냐는 서둘러 표트르 페트로비치의 사과를 전했는데, 모두가 들을 수 있을 만큼 큰 소리로 말하려 애쓰면서, 표트르 페트로비치가 한 말이라며 가장 정중한 표현들을 신중히 골라냈다. 그녀는 표트르 페트로비치가 특별히 당부하기를, 형편이 되는 대로 곧장 찾아와 단둘이 용건을 의논하고 그녀를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 궁리하겠다고, 운운, 운운했다고 덧붙였다.\n\n소냐는 이 말이 카테리나 이바노브나를 위로하고, 그녀의 비위를 맞추며 자존심을 흡족하게 하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 라스콜니코프 옆에 앉으면서, 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 흘끗 보고는 황급히 목례를 했다. 그러나 그 뒤로는 줄곧 그를 쳐다보거나 말을 거는 것을 피하는 듯했다. 카테리나 이바노브나를 기쁘게 하려고 계속 그쪽을 바라보면서도, 그녀는 어쩐지 넋이 나간 듯이 보였다. 그녀도 카테리나 이바노브나도 상복을 구하지 못했던 터라, 소냐는 짙은 갈색 옷을, 카테리나 이바노브나는 하나뿐인 드레스인 짙은 줄무늬 무명옷을 입고 있었다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치의 전갈은 대단히 성공적이었다. 위엄을 갖추고 소냐의 말을 듣고 난 카테리나 이바노브나는 똑같은 위엄으로 표트르 페트로비치의 안부를 물었고, 그러고는 곧장 라스콜니코프에게 거의 큰 소리로 속삭이기를, 표트르 페트로비치 같은 지위와 신분의 사람이 자기 가족에 대한 헌신과 자기 아버지와의 오랜 우정에도 불구하고 이런 “괴상한 패거리” 속에 끼어 있게 되었다면 그것이야말로 분명 이상한 일이었을 거라고 말했다.\n\n“그래서 나는 당신께 정말 감사한답니다, 로디온 로마노비치, 이런 자리에서까지 내 환대를 마다하지 않으셨으니까요.” 그녀가 거의 큰 소리로 덧붙였다. “하지만 내 가엾은 남편에 대한 당신의 각별한 애정만이 당신으로 하여금 약속을 지키게 한 것이라고 나는 믿어요.”\n\n그러고는 다시 한번 자존심과 위엄을 담아 손님들을 훑어보더니, 별안간 식탁 너머로 귀먹은 노인에게 큰 소리로 물었다. 고기를 좀 더 들지 않겠느냐고, 그리고 포도주는 받았느냐고. 노인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 한참 동안 무슨 질문을 받았는지 알아듣지 못했다. 옆에 앉은 자들이 그를 쿡쿡 찌르고 흔들어 대며 재미있어했는데도 말이다. 그는 그저 입을 벌린 채 멍하니 주위를 둘러볼 뿐이었고, 그 모습은 좌중의 흥겨움만 더해 줄 따름이었다.\n\n“저런 멍청이라니! 봐요, 봐! 무엇하러 데려왔담? 하지만 표트르 페트로비치로 말하자면, 나는 늘 그분을 믿어 왔어요.” 카테리나 이바노브나가 말을 이었다. “그분은 물론 저런 작자들과는 다르죠…….” 그녀는 더없이 험상궂은 얼굴로 아말리아 이바노브나를 향해 어찌나 날카롭고 크게 말을 던졌던지, 상대는 어찌할 바를 몰라 했다. “당신이 데려온 저 차려입은 칠칠치 못한 여편네들과는 다르단 말이에요, 우리 아버지라면 부엌에 요리사로도 들이지 않았을 것들, 내 죽은 남편이라면 마음씨가 고와서 호의로 불러 줬다면 그것만으로도 영광이었을 그런 것들 말이에요.”\n\n“그래요, 그분은 술을 좋아하셨죠, 좋아하셨어요, 마셨다니까요!” 병참부 서기가 열두 잔째 보드카를 꿀꺽 들이켜며 외쳤다.\n\n“내 죽은 남편한테 분명 그런 약점이 있었던 건 사실이고, 그건 다들 아는 일이에요.” 카테리나 이바노브나가 즉시 그에게 달려들었다. “하지만 그분은 친절하고 명예로운 분이었고, 가족을 사랑하고 존중했어요. 무엇보다 안타까운 건, 그 선량한 성품 탓에 온갖 못된 인간들을 다 믿었다는 거예요, 자기 신발 밑창만도 못한 작자들과 어울려 술을 마셨던 거죠. 믿어지세요, 로디온 로마노비치, 사람들이 그분 주머니에서 생강과자로 만든 수탉을 찾아냈답니다. 곤드레만드레 취해서도 아이들을 잊지 않았던 거예요!”\n\n“수탉? 수탉이라고 했소?” 병참부 서기가 소리쳤다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 대꾸할 가치도 없다는 듯 답하지 않았다. 그녀는 한숨을 쉬며 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n“당신도 분명, 다들 그렇듯이, 내가 그분한테 너무 모질게 굴었다고 생각하시겠죠.” 그녀가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말을 이었다. “하지만 그렇지 않아요! 그분은 나를 존중했어요, 무척이나 존중했다고요! 마음씨 고운 분이었어요! 그리고 나는 가끔 그분이 얼마나 가엾던지! 그분이 한구석에 앉아 나를 바라보고 있으면, 나는 너무도 안쓰러워서, 다정하게 대해 주고 싶다가도, 속으로 이렇게 생각하곤 했죠. ‘다정하게 굴면 또 술을 마실 텐데.’ 오로지 모질게 굴어야만 그분을 절제시킬 수 있었던 거예요.”\n\n“그래요, 그 양반 머리채깨나 잡혔지!” 병참부 서기가 보드카를 또 한 잔 삼키며 다시 고함쳤다.\n\n“어떤 멍청이들은 머리채를 잡히는 것뿐 아니라 흠씬 두들겨 맞아야 정신을 차릴 텐데. 지금 내 죽은 남편 얘길 하는 게 아니에요!” 카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 쏘아붙였다.\n\n그녀의 두 뺨에 어린 홍조가 점점 더 짙어졌고, 가슴은 들썩였다. 일 분만 더 있으면 한바탕 소동을 벌일 기세였다. 손님들 중 다수가 분명 신이 나서 킬킬거렸다. 그들은 병참부 서기를 쿡쿡 찌르며 무언가를 속삭이기 시작했다. 그를 부추기려는 게 분명했다.\n\n“그게 무슨 말씀인지 좀 물읍시다.” 서기가 입을 열었다. “그러니까 누구를……, 누구에 대해서……, 방금 말씀하신 게……. 하지만 난 상관없소! 다 헛소리지! 미망인이여! 내 당신을 용서하리다……. 자, 한 잔!”\n\n그러고는 보드카를 또 들이켰다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 말없이 앉아 역겨움 속에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그는 그저 예의상, 카테리나 이바노브나가 끊임없이 자기 접시에 놓아 주는 음식을, 그녀의 감정을 상하게 하지 않으려고 맛만 보았을 뿐이었다. 그는 소냐를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러나 소냐는 점점 더 불안하고 괴로워했다. 그녀 역시 이 만찬이 평온하게 끝나지 않으리라는 것을 예감하고, 점점 커져 가는 카테리나 이바노브나의 짜증을 두려운 마음으로 지켜보고 있었다. 그녀는, 바로 자신, 소냐가 그 “점잖은” 부인들이 카테리나 이바노브나의 초대를 경멸스럽게 대한 주된 이유라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 아말리아 이바노브나에게서 들어 알고 있었으니, 그 어머니는 초대받은 것을 그야말로 모욕으로 여기며 이렇게 물었다는 것이다. “어떻게 제 딸을 그런 처녀애 곁에 앉게 한단 말이오?” 소냐는 카테리나 이바노브나가 이미 이 말을 들었으리라는 느낌이 들었고, 카테리나 이바노브나에게는 자신이나 자기 아이들, 자기 아버지에 대한 모욕보다 소냐에 대한 모욕이 더 큰 의미를 지닌다는 것을 알고 있었다. 소냐는 카테리나 이바노브나가 이제 “그 칠칠치 못한 여편네들에게 그 둘이 다 같은 부류임을 보여 주기 전에는” 결코 만족하지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. 설상가상으로, 식탁 저쪽 끝에서 누군가 소냐에게 검은 빵을 오려 만든, 화살에 꿰뚫린 두 개의 하트가 놓인 접시를 건넸다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 새빨개진 얼굴로 즉시 식탁 너머로 큰 소리로, 그걸 보낸 자는 “술에 취한 멍청한 당나귀”라고 말했다.\n\n아말리아 이바노브나는 무언가 잘못되어 가고 있음을 예감하는 동시에, 카테리나 이바노브나의 거만함에 깊이 상처받은 터라, 좌중의 기분을 다시 좋게 만들고 자신의 위신을 세워 보고자, 난데없이 자기가 아는 사람 “약국의 카를”에 관한 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그가 어느 날 밤 삯마차를 타고 가는데, “마부가 그를 죽이려 했고, 카를이 죽이지 말라고 무척 빌었고, 울면서 두 손을 모았고, 겁에 질려 무서워서 그의 심장을 찔렀다”는 것이었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 미소를 짓긴 했으나, 곧 아말리아 이바노브나에게 러시아어로 일화를 늘어놓아서는 안 된다고 지적했다. 상대는 한층 더 발끈해서는, 자기 “베를린 출신 아버지는 매우 중요한 인물이었고, 늘 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 다녔다”고 응수했다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 도저히 참을 수가 없어 어찌나 크게 웃어 댔던지, 아말리아 이바노브나는 인내심을 잃고 가까스로 자제하는 지경이 되었다.\n\n“저 부엉이 좀 봐요!” 카테리나 이바노브나가 즉시, 기분이 거의 풀어진 채 속삭였다. “자기 아버지가 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 다녔다고 말하려던 건데, 사람들 주머니에 손을 넣고 다녔다고 말한 거예요. (콜록콜록.) 그리고 눈치채셨어요, 로디온 로마노비치, 이 페테르부르크의 외국인들, 특히 독일 사람들은 죄다 우리보다 멍청하다는 거? 우리 중에 누가 ‘약국의 카를’이 ‘겁에 질려 심장을 찔렀다’느니, 그 멍청이가 마부를 벌하기는커녕 ‘두 손을 모으고 울면서 무척 빌었다’느니 하는 얘기를 늘어놓는 걸 상상이나 할 수 있겠어요? 아, 저 바보! 게다가 글쎄 저 여자는 그게 무척 감동적인 얘기라고 여기면서, 자기가 얼마나 멍청한지는 짐작도 못한다니까요! 내 생각엔 저 술 취한 병참부 서기가 차라리 훨씬 똑똑해요, 적어도 저자는 술로 머리를 망쳐 놨다는 게 눈에 보이기라도 하지, 이 외국인들은 늘 저렇게 점잖고 진지한 척만 하니까……. 저것 봐요, 노려보고 앉아 있는 꼴이라니! 화가 났네, 하하! (콜록콜록콜록.)”\n\n기분을 되찾은 카테리나 이바노브나는 곧장 라스콜니코프에게, 연금을 받게 되면 고향인 T시에서 양갓집 따님들을 위한 학교를 열 작정이라고 말하기 시작했다. 그녀가 그에게 이 계획을 이야기한 것은 처음이었고, 그녀는 더없이 매혹적인 세부 사항들을 늘어놓았다. 그러다 별안간, 카테리나 이바노브나가 바로 그 표창장을 손에 쥐고 있다는 사실이 드러났는데, 그것은 마르멜라도프가 선술집에서 라스콜니코프에게, 자기 아내 카테리나 이바노브나가 학교를 졸업할 때 지사와 그 밖의 높으신 분들 앞에서 숄춤을 추었다고 말하며 언급했던 바로 그 표창장이었다. 이 표창장은 분명 이제 카테리나 이바노브나가 기숙학교를 열 자격이 있음을 증명하기 위한 것이었지만, 그녀가 그것으로 무장한 주된 목적은, 만일 그 “두 잘난 척하는 칠칠치 못한 여편네들”이 만찬에 온다면 그들을 압도하고, 카테리나 이바노브나가 더없이 고귀한, “귀족적이라 해도 좋을 가문 출신이며, 대령의 딸이고, 요즘 들어 그토록 설쳐 대는 어떤 모험적인 여자들보다 훨씬 윗사람”임을 반박할 수 없이 증명하려는 데 있었다. 표창장은 곧 술 취한 손님들의 손으로 넘어갔고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 굳이 붙들려 하지 않았으니, 거기에는 그녀의 아버지가 소령의 계급이었으며 또한 어느 훈장의 수훈자였다고, 그러니 그녀가 실로 거의 대령의 딸이나 다름없다고 글자 그대로 명백히 적혀 있었기 때문이다.\n\n신이 난 카테리나 이바노브나는, T시에서 그들이 누리게 될 평화롭고 행복한 삶에 대해, 자기 기숙학교에서 수업을 맡기려고 고용할 김나지움 교사들에 대해 계속 늘어놓았다. 그중 하나는 더없이 점잖은 늙은 프랑스인 망고라는 사람으로, 옛날에 카테리나 이바노브나 자신을 가르친 적이 있고 아직도 T시에 살고 있으니, 틀림없이 적당한 보수로 자기 학교에서 가르쳐 줄 거라고 했다. 다음으로 그녀는 자기와 함께 T시로 가서 자기 모든 계획을 도와줄 소냐에 대해 이야기했다. 이때 식탁 저쪽 끝에서 누군가 별안간 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 경멸스럽게 모른 척하려 애쓰면서도, 목소리를 높여 곧장 소냐가 자기를 도울 분명한 능력을 지녔음을, “그 애의 온순함, 인내심, 헌신, 너그러움, 그리고 훌륭한 교양”에 대해 확신에 차서 이야기하기 시작했고, 소냐의 뺨을 토닥이고는 따뜻하게 두 번 입을 맞추었다. 소냐는 새빨개졌고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 별안간 울음을 터뜨리고는, 곧바로 자신이 “신경이 예민하고 어리석으며, 너무 흥분했으니, 이제 끝낼 때가 되었고, 만찬도 끝났으니 차를 돌릴 때가 되었다”고 말했다.\n\n바로 그때, 대화에 끼어들지 못하고 아무도 자기 말을 들어 주지 않는 것에 깊이 분개해 있던 아말리아 이바노브나가 마지막으로 한 번 더 시도하여, 남몰래 불안해하면서도 지극히 심오하고 무게 있는 견해를 과감히 내놓았으니, “앞으로 그 기숙학교에서는 디 베셰(세탁물)에 각별히 신경을 써야 할 것이고, 반드시 빨랫감을 돌볼 좋은 담(부인)이 있어야 하며, 둘째로 그 아가씨들이 밤에 소설을 읽어서는 안 된다”는 것이었다.\n\n분명 마음이 상하고 몹시 지쳐 있던 데다 만찬에도 진절머리가 나 있던 카테리나 이바노브나는, 즉시 아말리아 이바노브나의 말을 끊으며, “그녀는 아무것도 모르면서 헛소리를 하고 있고, 디 베셰를 돌보는 것은 세탁부의 일이지 고급 기숙학교 교장의 일이 아니며, 소설 읽기로 말하자면 그것은 그저 무례한 소리이니, 입을 다물어 달라”고 말했다. 아말리아 이바노브나는 발끈하여 화를 내며, 자기는 그저 “그녀를 위하는 마음”에서 한 말이고, “그녀를 무척이나 위하는 마음”에서 한 것이며, “하숙비로 금화를 받지 못한 지가 오래되었다”고 했다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 즉시 그녀의 “기를 꺾으며”, 자기를 위한다는 건 거짓말이라고, 바로 어제 죽은 남편이 탁자에 누워 있던 마당에 하숙비로 자기를 들볶지 않았느냐고 말했다. 이에 아말리아 이바노브나는 매우 적절하게도, 자기는 그 부인들을 초대했지만 “그 부인들이 오지 않은 것은, 그 부인들은 진짜 부인들이라 부인 아닌 부인에게는 올 수 없기 때문”이라고 응수했다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 곧장 그녀에게, 너 같은 칠칠치 못한 여자가 무엇이 진짜 부인을 만드는지 판단할 자격이나 있느냐고 일러 주었다. 아말리아 이바노브나는 즉시 자기 “베를린 출신 아버지는 매우, 매우 중요한 인물이었고, 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 다니며 늘 ‘푸! 푸!’ 하고 말하곤 했다”고 선언하더니, 자기 아버지 흉내를 내겠다며 식탁에서 벌떡 일어나, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 두 뺨을 부풀린 채 “푸! 푸!” 비슷한 알 수 없는 소리를 내뱉었고, 싸움이 벌어지기를 바라며 일부러 아말리아 이바노브나를 부추기던 하숙인들이 일제히 큰 소리로 웃어 댔다.\n\n그러나 이것은 카테리나 이바노브나에게 너무 심한 일이었으니, 그녀는 즉시, 모두가 들을 수 있도록, 아말리아 이바노브나에게는 아마 아버지란 게 애초에 없었을 거라고, 그저 술 취한 페테르부르크의 핀란드 여자일 뿐이며, 분명 한때는 요리사였고 아마 그보다 더 못한 무엇이었을 거라고 단언했다. 아말리아 이바노브나는 가재처럼 빨개져서는, 어쩌면 카테리나 이바노브나야말로 아버지란 게 없었을 거라고, “하지만 자기에게는 베를린 출신 아버지가 있었고, 그분은 긴 외투를 입고 늘 푸-푸-푸! 하고 말했다”고 꽥꽥 소리쳤다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 경멸스럽게, 자기 가문이 어떤 가문인지는 다들 알고 있고, 바로 그 표창장에 자기 아버지가 대령이었다고 인쇄되어 있는 반면, 아말리아 이바노브나의 아버지는 — 만일 그녀에게 정말 아버지란 게 있었다면 — 아마 어느 핀란드 우유 장수였을 테지만, 아마 그녀에게는 애초에 아버지가 없었을 거라고, 왜냐하면 그녀의 이름이 아말리아 이바노브나인지 아말리아 루드비고브나인지조차 아직 불확실하기 때문이라고 말했다.\n\n이 말에 아말리아 이바노브나는 격노하여, 주먹으로 식탁을 내리치며, 자기는 아말리아 이바노브나이지 루드비고브나가 아니라고, “자기 아버지의 이름은 요한이었고 그분은 시장이었으며, 카테리나 이바노브나의 아버지는 결코 시장 따위가 아니었다”고 째지는 소리로 외쳤다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 의자에서 일어나, 험상궂으면서도 겉으로는 차분한 목소리로(비록 얼굴은 창백했고 가슴은 들썩였지만), “만일 그녀가 단 한순간이라도 그 경멸스러운 인간인 자기 아버지를 우리 아빠와 같은 반열에 감히 올려놓는다면, 자신, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그녀의 머리에서 모자를 잡아채 발로 짓밟아 버리겠다”고 말했다. 아말리아 이바노브나는 목청껏 소리를 지르며 방 안을 이리저리 뛰어다니더니, 자기가 이 집 주인이고 카테리나 이바노브나는 당장 이 집에서 나가야 한다고 외쳤다. 그러고는 무슨 영문인지 식탁의 은수저를 거두러 달려들었다. 일대 아우성과 소동이 벌어졌고, 아이들은 울기 시작했다. 소냐가 카테리나 이바노브나를 말리려 달려갔지만, 아말리아 이바노브나가 “노란 딱지”에 대해 무어라 소리치자, 카테리나 이바노브나는 소냐를 밀쳐 내고 자기 으름장을 실행에 옮기려 집주인 여자에게 달려들었다.\n\n바로 그 순간 문이 열리고, 표트르 페트로비치 루진이 문지방에 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 매섭고 빈틈없는 눈초리로 좌중을 훑어보며 서 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 달려갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n\"표트르 페트로비치,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"저를 지켜 주세요…… 적어도 당신만은! 이 어리석은 여자에게, 불행에 빠진 귀부인을 이런 식으로 대해서는 안 된다는 걸 알게 해 주세요…… 이런 일에는 법이라는 게 있다고요…… 저는 총독 각하께라도 직접 찾아가겠어요…… 이 여자는 책임을 져야 할 거예요…… 제 아버지가 베푸신 환대를 기억하셔서 이 고아들을 지켜 주세요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 부인…… 잠깐만요.\" 표트르 페트로비치는 그녀를 물리쳤다. \"당신 아버님은, 잘 아시다시피, 제가 알고 지내는 영광을 누리지 못했고요\" (누군가 큰 소리로 웃었다) \"당신과 아말리야 이바노브나 사이의 끝없는 말다툼에 끼어들 생각도 없습니다…… 저는 제 용건이 있어 여기 왔어요…… 당신 의붓딸인 소피야와 한마디 나누고자 합니다…… 이바노브나, 였던가요? 좀 지나가게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 그녀를 비켜 지나 소냐가 있는 맞은편 구석으로 갔다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다. 표트르 페트로비치가 자기 아버지의 환대를 누린 적이 없다고 부인하다니, 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다. 그것을 스스로 꾸며 낸 이야기이긴 했지만, 어느새 그녀는 그것을 굳게 믿고 있었던 것이다. 또한 그녀는 표트르 페트로비치의 사무적이고 메마르며 심지어 경멸이 어린 위협조에 충격을 받았다. 그가 들어서자 그 모든 소란이 차츰 잦아들었다. 이 \"진지한 사업가\"는 나머지 사람들과 두드러지게 어울리지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 무언가 중대한 일로 찾아왔으며, 무언가 예사롭지 않은 까닭이 그를 데려왔음이, 따라서 무언가 일이 벌어지리라는 것이 분명해 보였다. 소냐 곁에 서 있던 라스콜니코프는 그가 지나가도록 옆으로 비켜섰다. 표트르 페트로비치는 그를 알아채지도 못하는 듯했다. 잠시 후 레베쟈트니코프도 문간에 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 안으로 들어오지 않고 가만히 선 채, 두드러진 흥미를, 거의 경이에 가까운 표정으로 귀를 기울였고, 한동안 어리둥절해하는 것 같았다.\n\n\"제가 혹시 여러분을 방해하게 된다면 용서하십시오만, 이건 다소 중요한 문제입니다.\" 표트르 페트로비치는 좌중 전체를 향해 말했다. \"다른 분들이 와 계셔서 저로서는 정말 다행입니다. 아말리야 이바노브나, 이 집 안주인이신 당신께 삼가 청하니, 제가 소피야 이바노브나에게 할 말을 주의 깊게 들어 주십시오. 소피야 이바노브나,\" 그는 매우 놀라며 이미 불안에 떨고 있는 소냐를 향해 말을 이었다. \"당신이 다녀가신 직후에, 제 친구 레베쟈트니코프 씨의 방에 있는 제 탁자에서 백 루블짜리 지폐 한 장이 없어진 것을 발견했습니다. 만일 어떤 식으로든 당신이 그것이 지금 어디 있는지 알고 우리에게 말해 준다면, 명예를 걸고 단언하건대, 그리고 여기 계신 모든 분을 증인으로 삼아 말하건대, 이 일은 거기서 끝낼 것입니다. 그렇지 않을 경우 저는 매우 엄중한 조치를 취할 수밖에 없을 것이고, 그때는…… 당신 자신을 탓해야 할 것입니다.\"\n\n방 안에는 완전한 침묵이 감돌았다. 울던 아이들조차 잠잠해졌다. 소냐는 새파랗게 질린 채 서서 루진을 응시했으나 한마디도 하지 못했다. 그녀는 이해하지 못하는 것 같았다. 몇 초가 흘렀다.\n\n\"자, 그래서 어떻게 된 겁니까?\" 루진이 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠어요…… 저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라요.\" 마침내 소냐가 가냘프게 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아무것도 모른다고요?\" 루진은 되풀이하고는 다시 몇 초 동안 입을 다물었다. \"잠깐 생각해 보세요, 아가씨.\" 그는 엄하게, 그러면서도 어딘지 타이르는 듯한 어조로 입을 열었다. \"곰곰이 생각해 보세요, 생각할 시간을 드릴 용의가 있습니다. 이 점을 부디 유념하십시오. 만일 제가 이토록 완전히 확신하지 못했다면, 분명히 말하지만 제 경험상 이렇게 직접적으로 당신을 고발하는 모험은 하지 않았을 겁니다. 증인들 앞에서 이렇게 직접 고발했다가, 만일 그것이 거짓이거나 심지어 착오라 하더라도, 어떤 의미에서는 저 자신이 책임을 지게 될 테니까요, 그건 저도 알고 있습니다. 오늘 아침 저는 제 용도에 따라 대략 삼천 루블에 해당하는 오 푼 이자 채권 몇 장을 현금으로 바꿨습니다. 그 내역은 제 수첩에 적혀 있습니다. 집에 돌아와서 저는 돈을 세기 시작했고 — 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 증언해 줄 것입니다 — 이천삼백 루블을 센 다음 나머지는 외투 주머니의 수첩 안에 넣었습니다. 탁자 위에는 오백 루블가량이 남아 있었고, 그중에는 백 루블짜리 지폐 세 장이 있었습니다. 바로 그때 당신이 (제 초대로) 들어왔지요 — 그리고 머무는 내내 당신은 몹시 안절부절못했습니다. 그래서 이야기를 나누다가 세 번이나 벌떡 일어나 나가려 했고요. 이건 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 증언해 줄 수 있습니다. 아가씨, 당신 자신도, 제가 레베쟈트니코프 씨를 통해 당신을 초대한 것은 오로지 당신의 친척인 카테리나 이바노브나의 (제가 그분의 만찬에는 참석하지 못했습니다만) 절망적이고 궁핍한 처지에 대해, 그리고 그분을 위해 모금이나 추첨 같은 것을 마련하는 게 어떨지를 의논하기 위해서였다는 제 말을 부인하지 않으실 겁니다. 당신은 제게 고마워하며 심지어 눈물까지 흘렸지요. 제가 이 모든 것을 있었던 그대로 묘사하는 것은, 첫째로 당신의 기억을 되살리기 위해서이고, 둘째로 사소한 것 하나도 제 기억에서 빠져나가지 않았음을 보여 드리기 위해서입니다. 그러고 나서 저는 탁자에서 십 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 집어 당신의 친척을 위한 제 첫 기부금 명목으로 당신에게 건넸습니다. 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 이 모든 것을 보았습니다. 그런 다음 저는 당신을 문까지 배웅했는데 — 당신은 여전히 똑같이 안절부절못하는 상태였습니다 — 그 후 레베쟈트니코프 씨와 단둘이 남아 십 분쯤 이야기를 나누었고, 그러고 나서 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 나갔고, 저는 돈이 놓여 있는 탁자로 돌아가, 아까 하려던 대로 그것을 세어 따로 치워 두려 했습니다. 그런데 놀랍게도 백 루블짜리 지폐 한 장이 사라져 있었습니다. 부디 사정을 헤아려 주십시오. 레베쟈트니코프 씨를 의심할 수는 없습니다. 그런 가정을 입에 올리는 것조차 부끄럽습니다. 제가 셈을 잘못했을 리도 없으니, 당신이 들어오기 직전에 이미 셈을 마치고 총액이 맞는 것을 확인했기 때문입니다. 당신의 그 안절부절못하던 태도, 어서 떠나려던 그 조급함, 그리고 한동안 탁자 위에 손을 올려 두고 있던 사실을 떠올리고, 또 당신의 사회적 처지와 그에 결부된 습성을 고려한다면, 제가, 말하자면 공포에 휩싸여, 그야말로 제 의지에 반하여, 어떤 의심을 — 잔혹하지만 정당한 의심을 — 품지 않을 수 없었음을 당신도 인정하실 겁니다! 한 가지 더 덧붙이고 거듭 말하건대, 제 확고한 확신에도 불구하고 이런 고발을 하는 데 제가 어느 정도 위험을 무릅쓰고 있음을 잘 압니다. 하지만 보시다시피 저는 이 일을 그냥 넘길 수 없었습니다. 저는 조치를 취했고, 그 이유를 말씀드리지요. 오로지, 부인, 오로지 당신의 그 시커먼 배은망덕 때문입니다! 아니! 저는 당신의 궁핍한 친척을 위해 당신을 초대하고, 십 루블의 기부금을 건넸는데, 당신은 그 자리에서 이런 짓으로 그 모든 것을 갚다니요. 이건 너무 심합니다! 당신은 따끔한 교훈이 필요해요. 잘 생각해 보세요! 게다가 진정한 벗으로서 부탁드립니다 — 지금 이 순간 당신에게 저보다 나은 벗은 없을 겁니다 — 당신이 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 생각해 보세요, 그러지 않으면 저는 결코 물러서지 않을 테니까요! 자, 어떻게 하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"저는 아무것도 안 가져갔어요.\" 소냐가 공포에 질려 속삭였다. \"당신이 십 루블을 주셨고, 여기 있어요, 가져가세요.\"\n\n소냐는 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 한쪽 모서리의 매듭을 풀고는 십 루블짜리 지폐를 꺼내 루진에게 건넸다.\n\n\"그럼 백 루블을 가져간 것은 인정하지 않겠다는 거요?\" 그는 지폐를 받지 않고 나무라듯 다그쳤다.\n\n소냐는 주위를 둘러보았다. 모두가 그토록 무섭고, 엄하고, 비웃는 듯한, 적의에 찬 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다. 그녀는 라스콜니코프를 보았다…… 그는 팔짱을 낀 채 벽에 기대서서 이글거리는 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 하느님!\" 소냐의 입에서 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"아말리야 이바노브나, 경찰에 알려야 할 테니, 그동안 부디 문지기를 불러 주시기를 삼가 청합니다.\" 루진이 부드럽게, 심지어 친절하게 말했다.\n\"고트 데어 바름헤르치게!* 저 여자가 도둑인 줄 알았다니까요.\" 아말리야 이바노브나가 두 손을 번쩍 쳐들며 외쳤다.\n\n* 고트 데어 바름헤르치게(Gott der Barmherzige): 독일어로 \"자비로우신 하느님\"이라는 뜻.\n\n\"알고 있었다고요?\" 루진이 그녀의 말을 받았다. \"그렇다면 진작부터 그렇게 생각할 만한 무슨 이유가 있었던 모양이군요. 훌륭하신 아말리야 이바노브나, 증인들 앞에서 하신 그 말을 기억해 두시기를 부탁드립니다.\"\n\n사방에서 시끄러운 말소리가 웅성거렸다. 모두가 들썩이기 시작했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 사태를 불현듯 깨닫고는 소리치며 루진에게 달려들었다. \"뭐라고요! 이 애가 도둑질을 했다고 고발하는 거예요? 소냐가? 아, 악당 같으니, 악당 같으니!\"\n\n그러고는 소냐에게 달려가 그 야윈 두 팔로 그녀를 끌어안고 죔쇠처럼 꼭 붙들었다.\n\n\"소냐! 어떻게 이 사람한테서 십 루블을 받았니? 어리석은 것! 그거 이리 줘! 당장 그 십 루블을 내게 줘 — 여기!\"\n\n그러고는 소냐에게서 지폐를 낚아채어, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 구겨 곧장 루진의 얼굴에 내던졌다. 지폐는 그의 눈에 맞고 바닥에 떨어졌다. 아말리야 이바노브나가 얼른 그것을 주우려 달려들었다. 표트르 페트로비치는 화를 참지 못했다.\n\n\"저 미친 여자를 붙잡아요!\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n바로 그때 레베쟈트니코프 말고도 몇 사람이 더 문간에 나타났는데, 그중에는 그 두 부인도 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 미쳤다고요? 내가 미쳤다고요? 멍청이!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 새된 소리로 악을 썼다. \"당신이야말로 멍청이예요, 너절한 협잡꾼 변호사 같으니, 비열한 인간! 소냐, 소냐가 이 사람 돈을 가져갔다고요! 소냐가 도둑이라니! 아니, 이 애는 마지막 한 푼까지 남에게 줘 버릴 애예요!\" 그러더니 카테리나 이바노브나는 발작적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"이런 멍청이를 본 적이 있어요?\" 그녀는 이쪽저쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다. \"당신도 그래요?\" 그녀는 문득 안주인을 보았다. \"당신도 그래, 소시지나 처먹는 주제에, 이 애가 도둑이라고 단언하는 거야, 이 크리놀린이나 두른 너절한 프로이센 닭다리 같으니라고! 이 애는 이 방에서 나간 적이 없어. 당신한테서 곧장 와서, 이 못된 것아, 내 옆에 앉았다고, 다들 봤어. 여기, 로지온 로마노비치 옆에 앉아 있었어. 이 애를 뒤져 봐! 방을 나간 적이 없으니, 돈이 있다면 이 애 몸에 있을 거 아냐! 뒤져 봐, 뒤져 보라고! 하지만 못 찾으면, 미안하지만 이보세요, 당신이 책임져야 할 거예요! 저는 우리 폐하께, 우리 폐하께, 우리 자애로우신 차르 폐하께 직접 찾아가서, 그분 발밑에 엎드리겠어요, 오늘, 지금 당장! 저는 이 세상에 혼자예요! 그분들은 저를 들여보내 주실 거예요! 안 그러실 것 같아요? 천만에요, 저는 들어갈 거예요! 들어갈 거라고요! 당신은 이 애가 온순한 걸 노린 거예요! 그걸 믿고 그런 거지! 하지만 나는 그렇게 고분고분하지 않아요, 그건 알아 두라고요! 당신이 너무 지나쳤어요. 뒤져 봐, 뒤져 보라고!\"\n\n그러고는 카테리나 이바노브나가 광란에 빠져 루진을 흔들며 소냐 쪽으로 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"좋습니다, 제가 책임지지요…… 하지만 진정하세요, 부인, 진정하시라고요. 당신이 그렇게 고분고분하지 않다는 건 알겠습니다!…… 자, 자, 그건 그렇고…….\" 루진이 중얼거렸다. \"그건 경찰 앞에서 해야 할 일인데…… 하긴 사실 증인은 이미 충분하니…… 저는 준비됐습니다…… 하지만 어쨌든 남자로서는 곤란하군요…… 이 여자의 성별 때문에…… 그래도 아말리야 이바노브나의 도움이 있으면…… 물론 이런 식으로 처리할 일은 아니지만…… 어떻게 하면 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"마음대로 해요! 원하는 사람 누구든 이 애를 뒤져 봐요!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다. \"소냐, 주머니를 뒤집어 봐! 봐! 보라고, 괴물 같으니, 주머니가 비었잖아, 여기 손수건이 있었고! 여기 다른 주머니도, 봐! 보여, 보이냐고?\"\n\n그러고는 카테리나 이바노브나가 양쪽 주머니를 뒤집었다 — 아니, 차라리 거머쥐듯 홱 뒤집었다. 그런데 오른쪽 주머니에서 종잇조각 하나가 튀어나와 공중에 포물선을 그리며 루진의 발치에 떨어졌다. 모두가 그것을 보았고, 몇 사람은 소리를 질렀다. 표트르 페트로비치가 몸을 굽혀 두 손가락으로 종이를 집어 들고는 모두가 볼 수 있도록 치켜들어 펼쳤다. 그것은 여덟 겹으로 접은 백 루블짜리 지폐였다. 표트르 페트로비치는 그 지폐를 치켜들어 모두에게 보여 주었다.\n\n\"도둑년! 내 집에서 나가! 경찰, 경찰!\" 아말리야 이바노브나가 악을 썼다. \"이런 것들은 시베리아로 보내 버려야 해! 꺼져!\"\n\n사방에서 고함이 터져 나왔다. 라스콜니코프는 잠자코, 이따금 루진에게 재빠른 눈길을 던지는 것 말고는 줄곧 소냐에게 시선을 고정한 채였다. 소냐는 정신을 잃은 듯 가만히 서 있었다. 그녀는 놀랄 여력조차 거의 없었다. 갑자기 그녀의 두 뺨으로 핏기가 확 몰려들었다. 그녀는 외마디 소리를 지르며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 제가 아니에요! 저는 안 가져갔어요! 저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라요.\" 그녀가 가슴이 찢어질 듯한 울부짖음으로 외치고는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 달려갔고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 온 세상으로부터 그녀를 감싸 주려는 듯 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다.\n\n\"소냐! 소냐! 나는 안 믿어! 봐, 나는 안 믿는다고!\" 그녀는 명백한 사실을 앞에 두고도 외치며, 갓난아기처럼 그녀를 품에 안고 앞뒤로 흔들고, 끊임없이 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추다가, 다시 그 손을 붙들고 거기에도 입을 맞추었다. \"네가 가져갔다니! 이 사람들은 어쩜 이리도 멍청할까! 아이고! 너희는 바보야, 바보들이라고.\" 그녀는 방 안 전체를 향해 외쳤다. \"너희는 몰라, 이 애가 얼마나 고운 마음씨를 가졌는지, 이 애가 어떤 아이인지 너희는 몰라! 이 애가 가져갔다고, 이 애가? 이 애는 자기 마지막 누더기까지 팔아서라도, 너희가 어려우면 맨발로라도 도우러 갈 애야, 그런 애라고! 이 애가 누런 감찰을 받게 된 것도 내 아이들이 굶주리고 있었기 때문이야, 우리를 위해 제 몸을 판 거라고! 아, 여보, 여보! 보여요? 보이냐고요? 이게 당신을 위한 추도 만찬이라니! 자비로우신 하느님! 이 애를 지켜 줘요, 왜 다들 가만히 서 있는 거예요? 로지온 로마노비치, 왜 당신은 이 애 편을 들어 주지 않는 거예요? 당신도 그걸 믿는 거예요? 너희는 전부 다 합쳐도 이 애 새끼손가락만도 못해! 오, 하느님! 적어도 지금만이라도 이 애를 지켜 주세요!\"\n\n가엾고, 폐병을 앓고, 무력한 이 여인의 울부짖음은 듣는 이들에게 커다란 영향을 미치는 듯했다. 고통에 찌들고, 야위고, 폐병 든 얼굴, 바싹 마르고 피가 엉긴 입술, 쉰 목소리, 아이처럼 거침없이 흐르는 눈물, 그 미덥고 어린아이 같으면서도 절망적인 도움의 호소가 어찌나 애처로운지, 모두가 그녀에게 연민을 느끼는 것 같았다. 적어도 표트르 페트로비치만큼은 곧바로 연민에 마음이 움직였다.\n\n\"부인, 부인, 이 사건은 당신과는 무관합니다!\" 그가 인상적으로 외쳤다. \"누구도 당신을 이 일의 교사자, 심지어 공범이라고 감히 고발하지는 않을 겁니다. 더구나 당신이 직접 이 애의 주머니를 뒤집어 죄를 입증하셨으니, 미리 알지 못하셨다는 게 드러난 셈이지요. 만일, 말하자면 가난이 소피야 세묘노브나를 거기까지 내몰았다면, 저는 기꺼이, 기꺼이 연민을 베풀 용의가 있습니다만, 아가씨, 어째서 당신은 자백하기를 거부했습니까? 그 불명예가 두려웠나요? 첫걸음이라서? 아마도 정신이 아득해졌던 거겠지요? 충분히 이해할 만한 일입니다…… 그러나 어떻게 그런 짓에까지 자신을 떨어뜨릴 수 있단 말입니까? 여러분,\" 그가 좌중 전체를 향해 말했다. \"여러분! 이 사람들을 동정하고, 말하자면 가엾이 여기는 마음에서, 저는 제게 퍼부어진 개인적인 모욕에도 불구하고 지금이라도 이 일을 눈감아 줄 용의가 있습니다! 그리고 이 불명예가 앞으로 당신에게 교훈이 되기를 바랍니다.\" 그는 소냐를 향해 말했다. \"그리고 저는 이 일을 더 이상 추궁하지 않겠습니다. 이만하면 됐습니다!\"\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 라스콜니코프를 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 라스콜니코프의 눈 속 불길은 당장이라도 그를 재로 만들어 버릴 것만 같았다. 한편 카테리나 이바노브나는 아무것도 듣지 못하는 모양이었다. 그녀는 미친 여자처럼 소냐에게 입을 맞추고 끌어안고 있었다. 아이들도 사방에서 소냐를 껴안았고, 폴렌카는 — 무엇이 잘못된 것인지 온전히 이해하지는 못했지만 — 눈물에 잠겨 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨며, 울어서 퉁퉁 부은 그 예쁘고 작은 얼굴을 소냐의 어깨에 파묻었다.\n\n\"이 무슨 비열한 짓이야!\" 갑자기 문간에서 큰 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치가 재빨리 돌아보았다.\n\n\"이 무슨 비열한 짓이냐고!\" 레베쟈트니코프가 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 노려보며 되풀이했다.\n\n표트르 페트로비치는 분명히 흠칫했다 — 모두가 그것을 알아챘고 나중에 그 일을 떠올렸다. 레베쟈트니코프가 성큼성큼 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n\"그러고도 감히 나를 증인이라고 내세웠단 말입니까?\" 그가 표트르 페트로비치에게 다가가며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리요? 무슨 말을 하는 거요?\" 루진이 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"내 말은, 당신이…… 중상모략가라는 거요, 내 말은 바로 그 뜻이오!\" 레베쟈트니코프가 근시안의 눈으로 그를 엄하게 노려보며 격하게 말했다.\n\n그는 몹시 화가 나 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 한마디 한마디를 붙잡아 저울질하듯 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 다시 침묵이 흘렀다. 표트르 페트로비치는 첫 순간 정말 거의 말문이 막힌 듯했다.\n\n\"만약 그게 나를 두고 하는 말이라면…….\" 그가 더듬거리며 입을 열었다. \"한데 대체 당신 왜 이러는 거요? 정신이 나갔소?\"\n\n\"내 정신은 멀쩡하고, 당신은 악당이오! 아, 이 무슨 비열한 짓인가! 나는 다 들었소. 그걸 이해하려고 일부러 기다리고 있었는데, 솔직히 말해 지금도 완전히 앞뒤가 맞지는 않소…… 당신이 대체 무엇 때문에 이런 짓을 했는지 나는 도무지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"아니, 그래 내가 뭘 했단 말이오? 그 말도 안 되는 수수께끼 같은 소리는 그만하시오! 아니면 술에 취한 거요!\"\n\n\"당신이야 술꾼일지 모르지, 비열한 인간 같으니, 하지만 나는 아니오! 나는 보드카는 입에도 안 대요, 그건 내 신념에 어긋나니까. 믿어지겠소, 저자가, 저자가 직접, 제 손으로 소피야 세묘노브나에게 그 백 루블짜리 지폐를 건넸소 — 내가 봤소, 내가 증인이오, 맹세할 수 있소! 저자가 한 짓이오, 저자가!\" 레베쟈트니코프가 모두를 향해 되풀이했다.\n\n\"제정신이오, 이 풋내기야?\" 루진이 새된 소리를 질렀다. \"저 여자가 당신 앞에 있잖소 — 저 여자 자신이 방금 모두 앞에서 내가 십 루블만 줬다고 단언했소. 그러니 내가 어떻게 그걸 줬다는 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 봤소, 내가 봤단 말이오.\" 레베쟈트니코프가 되풀이했다. \"그리고 비록 내 원칙에는 어긋나지만, 나는 지금 당장 법정에서 당신이 원하는 어떤 맹세라도 할 용의가 있소, 왜냐하면 당신이 그것을 저 여자 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣는 걸 내가 봤으니까. 다만 나는 바보같이 당신이 친절한 마음에서 그러는 줄로만 알았던 거요! 당신이 문에서 저 여자에게 작별 인사를 하면서, 한 손으로 그 손을 잡고 있는 동안, 다른 손, 그 왼손으로 지폐를 저 여자 주머니에 슬쩍 밀어 넣었소. 내가 봤소, 내가 봤단 말이오!\"\n\n루진의 얼굴이 창백해졌다.\n\n\"무슨 거짓말을!\" 그가 뻔뻔하게 외쳤다. \"아니, 창가에 서 있던 당신이 어떻게 그 지폐를 봤단 말이오? 당신의 그 근시안으로 헛것을 본 거겠지. 헛소리를 지껄이고 있구려!\"\n\n\"아니, 나는 헛것을 본 게 아니오. 그리고 비록 좀 떨어진 데 서 있었지만, 나는 그걸 다 봤소. 물론 창가에서 지폐를 분간하기는 어려웠을 거요 — 그건 사실이오 — 하지만 나는 그게 백 루블짜리 지폐라는 걸 확실히 알고 있었소, 왜냐하면 당신이 소피야 세묘노브나에게 십 루블을 주려 할 때, 탁자에서 백 루블짜리 지폐를 집어 들었으니까 (그때 내가 가까이 서 있어서 봤고, 그 순간 어떤 생각이 떠올라서 당신 손에 그게 들려 있다는 걸 잊지 않았던 거요). 당신은 그걸 접어서 줄곧 손에 쥐고 있었소. 그러다가 당신이 일어설 때 그걸 오른손에서 왼손으로 옮기면서 하마터면 떨어뜨릴 뻔했는데, 그제야 나는 다시 그 생각이 났소. 그게 눈에 띈 건, 당신이 내가 보지 못하게 저 여자에게 친절을 베풀려는 거라는 똑같은 생각이 또 들었기 때문이오. 내가 당신을 얼마나 유심히 지켜봤을지 상상이 갈 거요, 그래서 당신이 그걸 저 여자 주머니에 용케 슬쩍 집어넣는 걸 봤소. 내가 봤소, 내가 봤단 말이오, 맹세하겠소.\"\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 거의 숨이 넘어갈 지경이었다. 사방에서 고함이 터져 나왔는데, 주로 놀라움을 나타내는 것이었으나 더러는 위협적인 어조도 있었다. 그들은 모두 표트르 페트로비치 주위로 몰려들었다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 레베쟈트니코프에게 달려갔다.\n\n\"제가 당신을 잘못 봤어요! 이 애를 지켜 주세요! 이 애 편을 들어 줄 사람은 당신뿐이에요! 이 애는 고아예요. 하느님이 당신을 보내 주신 거예요!\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 거의 모른 채 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"한 무더기 헛소리로군!\" 루진이 격분하여 악을 썼다. \"당신이 지껄인 건 죄다 헛소리요! '어떤 생각이 떠올랐다, 생각하지 않았다, 알아챘다' — 그게 다 무슨 소용이오? 그래, 내가 일부러 저 여자한테 몰래 그걸 줬다고? 무엇 때문에? 무슨 목적으로? 내가 이 여자와 무슨 상관이 있다고……?\"\n\n\"무엇 때문이냐고? 그건 나도 모르겠소, 하지만 내가 당신에게 하는 말이 사실이라는 건 확실하오! 천만에, 나는 잘못 본 게 아니오, 이 파렴치한 범죄자 같으니, 나는 기억하오, 그 일 때문에, 내가 당신에게 고마워하며 당신 손을 잡고 있던 바로 그때, 한 가지 의문이 곧바로 떠올랐던 걸. 당신은 왜 그걸 저 여자 주머니에 몰래 집어넣었을까? 그러니까, 왜 몰래 그랬을까? 그저 내게 감추려는 거였을까, 내 신념이 당신 것과 반대이고 내가 근본적인 치유가 못 되는 사사로운 자선을 인정하지 않는다는 걸 알고서? 그래서 나는, 당신이 내 앞에서 그렇게 큰돈을 주는 게 정말로 부끄러웠던 거라고 결론지었소. 어쩌면, 하고 나는 또 생각했지, 저 여자가 주머니에서 백 루블짜리 지폐를 통째로 발견하면 깜짝 놀라게 해 주려는 거라고. (나도 알거든, 어떤 자선가들은 그런 식으로 제 선행을 치장하기를 무척 좋아한다는 걸.) 그러다가 또 이런 생각도 들었소, 당신이 저 여자를 시험해 보려는 거라고, 저 여자가 그걸 발견하면 고맙다고 찾아오는지 보려고. 그리고 또, 당신이 감사 인사를 피하고 싶어서, 속담처럼, 오른손이 하는 일을 왼손이 모르게…… 뭐 그런 식이라고. 하도 여러 가능성을 생각하느라 나는 그걸 곰곰이 따져 보는 걸 미뤄 두었는데, 그래도 당신 비밀을 안다고 당신에게 내색하는 건 무례한 일이라고 여겼소. 그런데 또 다른 생각이 들었소, 소피야 세묘노브나가 미처 알아채기도 전에 그 돈을 잃어버리기 쉽겠다는 거였소, 그래서 나는 여기 들어와 저 여자를 방에서 불러내어, 당신이 백 루블을 저 여자 주머니에 넣었다고 말해 주기로 마음먹었던 거요. 그런데 오는 길에 먼저 코빌랴트니코프 부인 댁에 들러 『실증적 방법에 관한 일반 개론』을 가져다주고, 특히 피데리트의 논문을 (그리고 바그너의 것도) 추천하고 나서, 여기로 와 보니 이런 꼴이 벌어져 있는 거요! 자, 그러니 내가, 내가, 당신이 백 루블짜리 지폐를 저 여자 주머니에 넣는 걸 보지 않았더라면, 이 모든 생각과 궁리를 할 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n레베쟈트니코프가 마지막에 논리적인 결론까지 곁들여 그 장황한 일장 연설을 끝마쳤을 때, 그는 완전히 지쳐 얼굴에서 땀이 줄줄 흘러내렸다. 가엾게도 그는 러시아어 말고는 다른 언어를 알지도 못하면서, 러시아어조차 제대로 구사하지 못했던 터라, 이 영웅적인 위업을 치르고 나니 완전히 기진맥진, 거의 수척해진 꼴이었다. 그러나 그의 연설은 강력한 효과를 발휘했다. 그가 어찌나 격렬하게, 어찌나 확신에 차서 말했던지 모두가 분명 그를 믿었다. 표트르 페트로비치는 일이 자기에게 불리하게 돌아가고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"당신한테 어리석은 생각들이 떠올랐다 한들 그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"그건 증거가 못 돼. 당신이 꿈을 꾼 건지도 모르지, 그게 다요! 그리고 분명히 말하건대, 당신은 거짓말을 하고 있소, 선생. 당신은 나에 대한 무슨 악감정에서, 그저 앙심에서 거짓말을 하고 중상하는 거요, 내가 당신의 그 자유사상적이고 무신론적인 사회주의 주장에 동의하지 않았다는 이유로!\"\n\n그러나 이 반박은 표트르 페트로비치에게 득이 되지 않았다. 사방에서 못마땅해하는 웅성거림이 들려왔다.\n\n\"아하, 이제는 그렇게 나오시겠다, 이거요!\" 레베쟈트니코프가 외쳤다. \"그건 헛소리요! 경찰을 부르시오, 나는 맹세하겠소! 한 가지만은 도무지 모르겠소. 무엇 때문에 저자가 이런 비열한 짓을 무릅썼는지. 오, 가련하고, 비열한 인간 같으니!\"\n\n\"왜 저 사람이 그런 짓을 무릅썼는지 제가 설명할 수 있고, 필요하다면 저 또한 맹세하겠습니다.\" 마침내 라스콜니코프가 단호한 목소리로 말하며 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n그는 단호하고 침착해 보였다. 그의 모습만 보고도 모두가, 그가 정말로 이 일을 알고 있으며 이제 그 수수께끼가 풀리리라는 것을 분명히 느꼈다.\n\n\"이제야 저는 이 모든 것을 제 나름대로 설명할 수 있습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 레베쟈트니코프를 향해 말했다. \"이 일의 맨 처음부터 저는 그 밑바닥에 무슨 악당 같은 음모가 깔려 있다고 의심했습니다. 저만 아는 어떤 특별한 정황들 때문에 그것을 의심하기 시작했는데, 그것을 지금 모두에게 곧바로 설명하겠습니다. 그것들이 모든 것을 설명해 줍니다. 당신의 그 귀중한 증언이 마침내 제게 모든 것을 분명히 밝혀 주었습니다. 부디 여러분 모두, 모두 들어 주시기 바랍니다. 이 신사는 (그는 루진을 가리켰다) 얼마 전 어느 젊은 아가씨와 약혼한 사이였습니다 — 제 누이, 아브도티야 로마노브나 라스콜니코바입니다. 그런데 페테르부르크에 와서 그제, 우리가 처음 만난 자리에서 그는 저와 다투었고, 저는 그를 제 방에서 쫓아냈습니다 — 이를 증명할 증인이 두 사람 있습니다. 그는 매우 앙심 깊은 사람입니다…… 그제 저는 그가 여기, 당신 방에 묵고 있다는 것을 몰랐고, 따라서 우리가 다툰 바로 그날 — 그제 — 그가, 고인이 된 마르멜라도프 씨의 친구로서 제가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 장례 비용으로 얼마간의 돈을 건네는 것을 보았다는 사실도 몰랐습니다. 그는 즉시 제 어머니에게 편지를 써서, 제가 가진 돈을 전부, 카테리나 이바노브나가 아니라 소피야 세묘노브나에게 줘 버렸다고 알렸고, 더없이 비열한 방식으로 소피야 세묘노브나의…… 품행을 들먹였습니다, 그러니까 소피야 세묘노브나에 대한 제 태도의 성격을 넌지시 암시한 것이지요. 보시다시피 이 모든 것은, 제가 그들이 보내 준, 그것이 그들의 전 재산인 그 돈을 부당한 대상에게 탕진하고 있다고 넌지시 흘림으로써, 저를 어머니와 누이로부터 떼어 놓으려는 목적에서 한 짓이었습니다. 어제저녁, 어머니와 누이 앞에서, 그리고 그가 있는 자리에서, 저는 그 돈을 장례 비용으로 카테리나 이바노브나에게 준 것이지 소피야 세묘노브나에게 준 것이 아니며, 소피야 세묘노브나와는 일면식도 없고 그날까지 한 번도 본 적조차 없다고 분명히 밝혔습니다. 동시에 저는, 그가, 표트르 페트로비치 루진이, 그 온갖 미덕을 다 가졌다 한들, 그렇게 험담을 해 대는 소피야 세묘노브나의 새끼손가락만도 못한 사람이라고 덧붙였습니다. 소피야 세묘노브나를 제 누이 곁에 앉히겠느냐는 그의 물음에, 저는 그날 이미 그렇게 했다고 대답했습니다. 제 어머니와 누이가 그의 빈정거림에 넘어가 저와 다투려 하지 않자 화가 난 그는, 차츰 그들에게 용서할 수 없을 만큼 무례하게 굴기 시작했습니다. 결국 완전히 결렬되었고 그는 집에서 쫓겨났습니다. 이 모든 것이 어제저녁에 일어난 일입니다. 이제 특별히 주목해 주시기 바랍니다. 생각해 보십시오. 만일 그가 지금 소피야 세묘노브나가 도둑이라는 것을 증명하는 데 성공했다면, 그는 제 어머니와 누이에게 자기 의심이 거의 옳았음을, 제가 제 누이를 소피야 세묘노브나와 같은 수준에 놓은 데 화를 낼 만한 이유가 있었음을, 그리고 저를 공격함으로써 자기 약혼녀인 제 누이의 명예를 지키고 보전하고 있었음을 보여 줄 수 있었을 겁니다. 사실 그는 이 모든 것을 통해 저를 제 가족에게서 멀어지게 할 수도 있었을 테고, 분명 그들의 환심을 되찾기를 바랐을 겁니다. 저에게 개인적으로 복수하는 것은 말할 것도 없고요, 왜냐하면 그에게는 소피야 세묘노브나의 명예와 행복이 제게 매우 소중하다고 짐작할 만한 근거가 있으니까요. 그가 노린 게 바로 그것이었습니다! 저는 그렇게 이해합니다. 이게 이 모든 일의 전모이고, 다른 이유란 있을 수 없습니다!\"\n\n이렇게, 혹은 대략 이렇게 라스콜니코프는 자신의 연설을 마무리지었는데, 그 연설은 청중의 고함에 자주 끊기긴 했어도 매우 주의 깊게 경청되었다. 그러나 방해에도 불구하고 그는 명료하고, 차분하고, 정확하고, 단호하게 말했다. 그의 단호한 목소리와 확신에 찬 어조, 그리고 엄한 얼굴은 모두에게 커다란 인상을 남겼다.\n\n\"맞아, 맞아, 바로 그거요.\" 레베쟈트니코프가 신이 나서 맞장구쳤다. \"틀림없이 그럴 거요, 왜냐하면 소피야 세묘노브나가 우리 방에 들어오자마자 저자가 나에게, 당신이 여기 있는지, 내가 카테리나 이바노브나의 손님들 가운데서 당신을 봤는지 물었으니까. 저자는 나를 창가로 따로 불러내어 몰래 물었소. 당신이 여기 있는 게 저자에게는 꼭 필요했던 거요! 바로 그거요, 바로 그거라고!\"\n\n루진은 경멸하듯 미소 지을 뿐 말이 없었다. 그러나 그는 무척 창백했다. 어떤 빠져나갈 방도를 궁리하고 있는 듯했다. 어쩌면 그는 모든 것을 포기하고 자리를 뜨고 싶었을지도 모르지만, 지금 이 순간 그것은 거의 불가능했다. 그것은 자기에게 제기된 고발이 사실임을 인정하는 꼴이 될 테니까. 게다가 이미 술로 흥분해 있던 사람들이 이제는 너무 들끓어서 그것을 용납하지 않았다. 군수품 담당 서기는, 사실 사태 전모를 파악하지는 못했으면서도, 누구보다 큰 소리로 외쳐 대며 루진에게 매우 불쾌한 제안들을 늘어놓고 있었다. 그러나 거기 있던 사람들이 다 취한 것은 아니었으니, 온 방에서 세입자들이 몰려들었다. 폴란드인 세 사람은 엄청나게 흥분하여 끊임없이 그에게 \"이 판은 라이다크*다!\" 하고 외쳐 대며 폴란드어로 위협을 중얼거렸다. 소냐는 잔뜩 긴장하여 귀를 기울이고 있었으나, 그녀 역시 그 모든 것을 다 파악하지는 못하는 듯했다. 그녀는 마치 방금 의식을 되찾은 사람 같았다. 그녀는 라스콜니코프에게서 눈을 떼지 못했으니, 자신의 모든 안전이 그에게 달려 있음을 느꼈던 것이다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 힘겹고 고통스럽게 숨을 몰아쉬며 몹시 기진맥진해 보였다. 아말리야 이바노브나는 누구보다 멍청한 표정으로, 입을 떡 벌린 채 무슨 일이 일어난 것인지 갈피를 못 잡고 서 있었다. 그녀는 그저 표트르 페트로비치가 어쩌다 곤경에 빠졌다는 것만 알 뿐이었다.\n* 판(pan): 폴란드어로 \"나리, 양반\"이라는 뜻. ** 라이다크(lajdak): 폴란드어로 \"비열한 놈, 악당\"이라는 뜻.\n\n라스콜니코프는 다시 말을 하려 했으나 사람들이 그러도록 내버려 두지 않았다. 모두가 위협과 욕설을 퍼부으며 루진 주위로 몰려들었다. 그러나 표트르 페트로비치는 겁먹지 않았다. 소냐에 대한 자기 고발이 완전히 실패했음을 알아채자, 그는 뻔뻔함에 호소했다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 여러분, 잠깐만! 밀지 마시고, 지나가게 해 주십시오!\" 그가 군중을 헤치고 나아가며 말했다. \"그리고 제발 위협은 하지 마세요! 분명히 말하지만 그래 봐야 소용없고, 얻을 것도 없을 겁니다. 오히려 여러분이, 폭력으로 사법 절차를 방해한 데 대해 책임을 지셔야 할 겁니다. 도둑년의 정체는 백일하에 드러나고도 남았으니, 저는 고소할 것입니다. 우리 재판관들은 그렇게 눈멀지도 않았고…… 그렇게 취하지도 않았으니, 개인적인 복수심에서 — 어리석게도 그 동기를 스스로 인정한 — 저를 고발하는, 두 명의 악명 높은 불신자에 선동가요 무신론자인 자들의 증언을 믿지 않을 겁니다…… 자, 지나가게 해 주십시오!\"\n\n\"내 방에서 당신 흔적이라도 다시 보게 되는 일이 없도록 하시오! 당장 나가 주시오, 그러면 우리 사이는 모든 게 끝이오! 내가 들인 그 수고를, 내가 어떻게 설명해 왔는지를…… 이 보름 내내 말이오, 그걸 생각하면!\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 붙잡으려 할 때 오늘 내가 직접 나가겠다고 말했잖소. 이제 그저 한마디 덧붙이자면, 당신은 바보요. 당신 머리와 그 근시안을 위해 의사한테 가 보길 권하오. 지나가게 해 주시오, 여러분!\"\n\n그는 억지로 길을 헤치고 나아갔다. 그러나 군수품 담당 서기는 그를 그렇게 쉽게 놓아줄 마음이 없었다. 그는 탁자에서 잔 하나를 집어 들어 공중에 휘두르고는 표트르 페트로비치에게 던졌으나, 잔은 곧장 아말리야 이바노브나에게로 날아갔다. 그녀가 비명을 질렀고, 서기는 몸의 중심을 잃고 탁자 밑으로 쿵 하고 쓰러졌다. 표트르 페트로비치는 제 방으로 빠져나갔고, 삼십 분 후에는 그 집을 떠났다. 본디 소심한 소냐는, 그날 이전부터 자신이 누구보다 쉽게 학대받을 수 있고, 아무 탈 없이 부당한 일을 당할 수 있는 존재라는 것을 느끼고 있었다. 그럼에도 그 순간까지 그녀는, 모두 앞에서 조심하고, 온순하고, 고분고분하게 굶으로써 불행을 피할 수 있으리라 여겨 왔다. 그녀의 실망은 너무도 컸다. 물론 그녀는 무엇이든, 심지어 이런 일까지도, 인내심을 가지고 거의 군말 없이 견딜 수 있었다. 그러나 첫 순간만큼은 너무도 쓰라리게 느껴졌다. 자신의 승리와 결백이 입증되었음에도 — 처음의 공포와 망연자실이 가시고 모든 것을 분명히 이해할 수 있게 되자 — 자신의 무력함과 자신에게 가해진 부당함에 대한 느낌이 그녀의 심장을 고뇌로 두근거리게 했고, 그녀는 발작적인 울음에 사로잡혔다. 마침내 더는 견디지 못하고 그녀는 루진이 떠난 거의 직후에 방에서 뛰쳐나가 집으로 달려갔다. 요란한 웃음소리 가운데 잔이 아말리야 이바노브나에게 날아갔을 때, 그것은 안주인이 참을 수 있는 한계를 넘어선 일이었다. 비명을 지르며 그녀는 분노의 화신처럼 카테리나 이바노브나에게 달려들었으니, 모든 것이 그녀 탓이라고 여겼던 것이다.\n\n\"내 집에서 나가! 당장! 어서 꺼져!\"\n\n그러고는 이 말과 함께 그녀는 손에 잡히는 대로 카테리나 이바노브나의 물건이란 물건은 죄다 낚아채어 바닥에 내던지기 시작했다. 카테리나 이바노브나는, 창백하게, 거의 실신할 지경으로, 숨을 헐떡이며, 기진하여 쓰러져 있던 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 아말리야 이바노브나에게 달려들었다. 그러나 그 싸움은 너무도 불공평했다. 안주인은 그녀를 깃털처럼 가볍게 뿌리쳐 버렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그 신을 모르는 중상모략으로도 모자라서 — 이 비열한 것이 나한테 덤벼들어! 뭐라고요! 남편 장례식 날에 내가 이 집에서 쫓겨나다니! 내 빵과 소금을 얻어먹고서, 내 고아들과 함께 나를 길거리로 내쫓다니! 나더러 어디로 가란 말이야?\" 가엾은 여인이 흐느끼고 헐떡이며 울부짖었다. \"오, 하느님!\" 그녀가 눈을 번뜩이며 외쳤다. \"이 땅에 정의란 없단 말입니까? 우리 고아들이 아니면 누구를 지켜 주셔야 한단 말입니까? 두고 봅시다! 이 땅에 법과 정의는 있어요, 있고말고요, 내가 찾아내고 말 거예요! 두고 봐, 신을 모르는 것아! 폴렌카, 아이들이랑 같이 있어라, 엄마가 돌아올 테니. 나를 기다려, 길거리에서 기다려야 하더라도. 이 땅에 정의가 있는지 없는지 두고 봅시다!\"\n\n그러고는 마르멜라도프가 라스콜니코프에게 이야기했던 바로 그 초록색 숄을 머리에 뒤집어쓰고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 아직도 방을 가득 메우고 있던 어수선하고 술 취한 세입자들 무리를 비집고 나가, 울부짖으며 눈물을 흘리며 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다 — 당장 어디든 가서 정의를 찾겠다는 막연한 작정으로. 폴렌카는 어린 두 아이를 품에 안고, 겁에 질려 방 한구석의 트렁크 위에 웅크린 채, 어머니가 돌아오기를 떨면서 기다렸다. 아말리야 이바노브나는 방 안을 미친 듯이 돌아다니며 비명을 지르고, 한탄하고, 닥치는 대로 물건을 바닥에 내던졌다. 세입자들은 두서없이 떠들어 댔는데, 더러는 무슨 일이 일어났는지 저마다 아는 대로 논평하고, 더러는 서로 다투며 욕설을 퍼붓고, 또 더러는 노래를 부르기 시작했다……\n\n'이제 내가 갈 때로군.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. '자, 소피야 세묘노브나, 이제 당신이 무슨 말을 할지 두고 봅시다!'\n\n그러고는 그는 소냐의 거처를 향해 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n라스콜니코프는 가슴속에 그토록 무거운 공포와 번민을 짊어지고 있었음에도, 루진에 맞서 소냐를 위해 힘차고도 적극적으로 싸운 투사였다. 그러나 오전 내내 그토록 많은 일을 겪고 난 뒤라, 소냐를 변호하도록 그를 떠민 강렬한 개인적 감정과는 별개로, 그는 감각의 변화 속에서 일종의 안도를 느꼈다. 또한 그는, 특히 어떤 순간들에는, 곧 다가올 소냐와의 대면을 생각하며 동요했다. 그는 리자베타를 죽인 자가 누구인지 그녀에게 말해야만 했다. 그것이 자신에게 얼마나 끔찍한 고통이 될지 알았기에, 그는 말하자면 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리려 했다. 그래서 카테리나 이바노브나의 집을 나서며 \"자, 소피야 세묘노브나, 이제 당신이 뭐라고 할지 두고 봅시다!\" 하고 외쳤을 때만 해도, 그는 여전히 겉으로는 흥분해 있었고, 루진을 이긴 승리감으로 여전히 기운차고 도전적이었다. 그러나 이상하게도, 소냐의 거처에 다다랐을 무렵 그는 갑작스러운 무력감과 두려움에 사로잡혔다. 그는 문 앞에서 망설이며 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 스스로에게 기묘한 질문을 던졌다. \"리자베타를 죽인 자가 누구인지 그녀에게 정말 말해야만 하는가?\" 그것은 기묘한 질문이었으니, 바로 그 순간 그는 그녀에게 말하지 않을 수 없을 뿐 아니라 그 고백을 미룰 수조차 없다는 것을 느끼고 있었기 때문이다. 왜 그래야만 하는지 그는 아직 알지 못했고, 그저 그렇게 느낄 뿐이었으며, 피할 수 없는 것 앞에서의 자신의 무력함에 대한 괴로운 자각이 그를 거의 짓눌러 버릴 듯했다. 자신의 망설임과 고통을 단번에 끊어 버리려고, 그는 재빨리 문을 열고 문지방에서 소냐를 바라보았다. 그녀는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 앉아 있었으나, 라스콜니코프를 보자 곧장 일어나 마치 그를 기다리고 있었던 듯 그를 맞으러 다가왔다.\n\n\"당신이 아니었다면 저는 어떻게 됐을까요?\" 그녀는 방 한가운데서 그를 맞으며 빠르게 말했다.\n\n분명 그녀는 이 말을 그에게 서둘러 하고 싶어 했다. 그것은 그녀가 줄곧 기다려 온 말이었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 탁자로 가서, 그녀가 방금 일어선 그 의자에 앉았다. 그녀는 어제 그랬듯이 그에게서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에 마주 서 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 소냐?\" 그가 말했고, 자기 목소리가 떨리고 있음을 느꼈다. \"이게 다 '당신의 사회적 처지와 거기 따라붙는 습관들' 때문이지. 방금 그 말을 알아들었소?\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴에 괴로움이 드러났다.\n\n\"제발 어제처럼 저한테 말하지 말아요.\" 그녀가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"제발 그러지 마세요. 그러지 않아도 비참한 일은 충분하니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 이 책망을 언짢아할까 봐 서둘러 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"거기서 나와 버리다니 제가 어리석었어요. 지금 거기서는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있을까요? 곧장 돌아가고 싶었지만, 자꾸만…… 당신이 올 거라는 생각이 들어서요.\"\n\n그는 아말리야 이바노브나가 그들을 셋집에서 내쫓고 있으며, 카테리나 이바노브나가 \"정의를 찾겠다\"며 어디론가 뛰쳐나갔다고 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 하느님!\" 소냐가 외쳤다. \"당장 가요…….\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 망토를 낚아챘다.\n\n\"늘 똑같은 소리로군!\" 라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 말했다. \"당신은 그들 생각밖에 없구려! 잠깐만 나하고 있어 주시오.\"\n\n\"하지만…… 카테리나 이바노브나가요?\"\n\n\"카테리나 이바노브나를 놓칠 일은 없으니 걱정 마시오, 뛰쳐나갔으니 제 발로 당신을 찾아올 거요.\" 그가 토라진 듯 덧붙였다. \"여기서 당신을 못 찾으면, 도리어 당신 탓을 할 텐데…….\"\n\n소냐는 괴로운 불안 속에서 자리에 앉았다. 라스콜니코프는 입을 다문 채 바닥을 응시하며 무언가를 곰곰이 따져 보고 있었다.\n\n\"이번에 루진은 당신을 고발하려 하지 않았소.\" 그가 소냐를 보지 않은 채 입을 열었다. \"하지만 그가 원했다면, 그게 자기 계획에 맞아떨어졌다면, 레베쟈트니코프와 내가 없었더라면 그는 당신을 감옥에 처넣었을 거요. 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 힘없는 목소리로 수긍했다. \"네.\" 그녀는 무언가에 골몰한 채 괴로워하며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 마침 거기 없었을 수도 있었지. 레베쟈트니코프가 나타난 것도 순전히 우연이었고.\"\n\n소냐는 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"그래서 당신이 감옥에 갔다면, 그땐 어떻게 됐겠소? 어제 내가 했던 말, 기억하오?\"\n\n이번에도 그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그가 기다렸다.\n\n\"당신이 또 '그런 말 하지 말아요, 그만둬요' 하고 소리칠 줄 알았는데.\" 라스콜니코프는 웃음을 터뜨렸으나, 어딘가 억지스러운 웃음이었다. \"뭐요, 또 침묵이오?\" 잠시 후 그가 물었다. \"무슨 얘기든 해야 하지 않겠소. 레베쟈트니코프식으로 말하자면, 어떤 '문제'를 당신이라면 어떻게 결정할지 알아보는 것도 내겐 흥미로울 것 같소.\" (그는 이야기의 갈피를 놓치기 시작했다.) \"아니, 정말로, 나는 진지하오. 상상해 보시오, 소냐, 당신이 루진의 의도를 미리 다 알고 있었다고. 다시 말해 그것들이 카테리나 이바노브나와 아이들을, 그리고 덤으로 당신 자신까지 — 당신은 스스로를 아무것도 아니라고 여기니까 — 파멸시키리라는 것을 사실로서 알고 있었다고 말이오. 폴렌카도…… 그애도 같은 길을 가게 될 테니까. 자, 만일 갑자기 모든 게 당신의 결정에 달리게 된다면, 그가 계속 살아야 할지 아니면 그들이 계속 살아야 할지, 다시 말해 루진이 계속 살며 못된 짓을 하도록 둘지 아니면 카테리나 이바노브나가 죽어야 할지 말이오. 당신은 둘 중 누가 죽어야 할지 어떻게 결정하겠소? 묻겠소, 당신이라면?\"\n\n소냐가 불안한 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다. 이 머뭇거리는 질문에는 무언가 기묘한 데가 있었으니, 마치 빙 둘러 무언가에 다가가는 것 같았다.\n\n\"당신이 그런 비슷한 질문을 하리라는 걸 저는 느끼고 있었어요.\" 그녀는 캐묻듯 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그랬을 법도 하군. 하지만 거기엔 어떻게 대답하겠소?\"\n\n\"왜 일어날 수도 없는 일을 물으세요?\" 소냐가 마지못해 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 루진이 계속 살며 못된 짓을 하는 편이 낫다는 거요? 당신은 그것조차 결정할 엄두를 못 내는군!\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 하느님의 섭리를 알 수 없어요……. 그리고 왜 대답할 수 없는 걸 물으시나요? 그런 어리석은 질문이 무슨 소용이에요? 어떻게 그게 제 결정에 달릴 수 있겠어요 — 누가 저를 누가 살고 누가 죽어야 할지 결정하는 심판관으로 세웠다고요?\"\n\n\"오, 하느님의 섭리까지 끌어들인다면야, 더 할 얘기가 없지.\" 라스콜니코프가 침울하게 투덜거렸다.\n\n\"원하는 걸 차라리 똑바로 말해 주세요!\" 소냐가 괴로워하며 외쳤다. \"또 무언가로 끌고 가시는군요……. 그저 저를 괴롭히려고 오신 건가요?\"\n\n그녀는 스스로를 다잡지 못하고 비통하게 울기 시작했다. 그는 음울한 비참함 속에서 그녀를 바라보았다. 오 분이 흘렀다.\n\n\"물론 당신 말이 옳소, 소냐.\" 마침내 그가 부드럽게 말했다. 그는 돌연 달라져 있었다. 짐짓 꾸민 오만함과 속절없는 반항의 어조가 사라지고 없었다. 목소리마저 갑자기 약해져 있었다. \"어제 나는 용서를 구하러 오는 게 아니라고 당신에게 말했는데, 와서 처음으로 한 말이 거의 용서를 구하는 거였구려……. 루진과 섭리에 관한 그 얘기는 나 자신을 위해 한 거요. 나는 용서를 구하고 있었던 거요, 소냐…….\"\n\n그는 미소 지으려 했으나, 그 창백한 미소에는 무언가 속절없고 마무리되지 못한 것이 깃들어 있었다. 그는 고개를 떨구고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n그러자 갑자기, 소냐에 대한 일종의 쓰디쓴 증오와도 같은 기묘하고 놀라운 감각이 그의 가슴을 스쳐 지나갔다. 이 감각에 놀라고 두려워하는 듯 그는 고개를 들어 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러나 그는 자신에게 고정된 그녀의 불안하고 고통스럽게 애타는 눈길과 마주쳤다. 그 안에는 사랑이 있었다. 그의 증오는 환영처럼 사라졌다. 그것은 진짜 감정이 아니었다. 그는 한 감정을 다른 감정으로 착각했던 것이다. 그저 그 순간이 왔다는 뜻일 뿐이었다.\n\n그는 다시 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 고개를 떨구었다. 갑자기 그는 창백해지더니, 의자에서 일어나 소냐를 바라보았고, 한마디 말도 없이 기계적으로 그녀의 침대에 앉았다.\n\n그 순간 그의 감각은, 손에 도끼를 든 채 노파 위에 서서 \"단 일 분도 더 지체해서는 안 된다\"고 느꼈던 그 순간과 끔찍하리만치 닮아 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 소냐가 몹시 겁에 질려 물었다.\n\n그는 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없었다. 이것은 그가 \"말하려고\" 작정했던 방식과는 전혀, 전혀 달랐고, 그는 지금 자신에게 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는지 알 수 없었다. 그녀는 조용히 그에게 다가가 침대에 나란히 앉았고, 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 기다렸다. 그녀의 가슴이 두근거리다 철렁 내려앉았다. 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴을 그녀에게로 돌렸다. 그의 입술이 무언가를 내뱉으려 속절없이 애쓰며 달싹였다. 소냐의 가슴을 공포의 통증이 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 그녀는 그에게서 조금 물러나며 되물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니오, 소냐, 겁내지 마시오……. 헛소리요. 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 정말 헛소리지.\" 그는 헛소리에 빠진 사람처럼 중얼거렸다. \"내가 왜 당신을 괴롭히러 왔을까?\" 그가 그녀를 바라보며 불쑥 덧붙였다. \"정말, 왜일까? 자꾸 스스로에게 그 질문을 던지게 되는구려, 소냐…….\"\n\n어쩌면 그는 십오 분 전에도 스스로에게 그 질문을 던지고 있었을 테지만, 지금 그는 무력하게, 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 알지 못한 채, 온몸이 끊임없이 떨려 오는 것을 느끼며 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 괴로워하나요!\" 그녀가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 괴로움에 차서 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"다 헛소리요……. 들어 보시오, 소냐.\" 그가 갑자기 미소 지었는데, 이 초쯤 이어진 창백하고 속절없는 미소였다. \"어제 내가 당신에게 하려던 말, 기억하오?\"\n\n소냐가 불안하게 기다렸다.\n\n\"떠나면서 내가 그랬소, 어쩌면 영영 작별 인사를 하는 건지도 모르지만, 만일 오늘 다시 온다면 누가…… 누가 리자베타를 죽였는지 당신에게 말해 주겠다고.\"\n\n그녀는 온몸을 떨기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 이렇게 말하러 왔소.\"\n\n\"그럼 어제 그 말이 정말 진심이었어요?\" 그녀가 힘겹게 속삭였다. \"어떻게 아세요?\" 그녀가 별안간 이성을 되찾은 듯 다급히 물었다.\n\n소냐의 얼굴이 점점 더 창백해졌고, 그녀는 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬었다.\n\n\"나는 알고 있소.\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 멈칫했다.\n\n\"그를 찾아냈나요?\" 그녀가 조심스레 물었다.\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 그 일을 아세요?\" 그녀가 또다시, 거의 들리지 않을 만큼, 또 한 번의 침묵 뒤에 물었다.\n\n그는 그녀에게로 몸을 돌려 그녀를 아주 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"맞혀 보시오.\" 그가 아까와 똑같이 일그러지고 속절없는 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n그녀의 온몸에 전율이 스쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 당신…… 왜 저를 이렇게 겁주시나요?\" 그녀가 어린아이처럼 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 그의 아주 가까운 친구임이 틀림없지……, 알고 있으니까.\" 라스콜니코프가, 마치 눈을 돌릴 수 없는 듯 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말을 이었다. \"그는…… 그 리자베타를 죽일 생각은 없었소……. 그는…… 우연히 그녀를 죽인 거요……. 노파가 혼자 있을 때 죽일 작정으로 거기 갔는데…… 그때 리자베타가 들어왔고…… 그래서 그녀까지 죽인 거요.\"\n\n또 한 번 끔찍한 순간이 흘렀다. 둘은 여전히 서로를 응시하고 있었다.\n\n\"그럼, 맞히지 못하겠소?\" 그가, 마치 첨탑 꼭대기에서 몸을 던지는 듯한 기분으로 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"아…… 아니요…….\" 소냐가 속삭였다.\n\n\"잘 봐 두시오.\"\n\n그가 이 말을 다시 내뱉자마자, 그 익숙한 감각이 또 그의 가슴을 얼어붙게 했다. 그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 별안간 그녀의 얼굴에서 리자베타의 얼굴이 보이는 듯했다. 그는 도끼를 들고 다가갔을 때 리자베타의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 표정을 또렷이 떠올렸다. 그녀가 벽 쪽으로 물러서며 손을 내밀던 모습, 얼굴에 어린 어린아이 같은 공포, 마치 어린아이들이 무언가에 막 겁을 먹기 시작할 때처럼, 자기를 놀라게 하는 것을 뚫어지게 불안하게 바라보며, 뒤로 움츠러들어 막 울음을 터뜨릴 듯 작은 손을 내미는 그 모습을. 지금 소냐에게도 거의 똑같은 일이 일어났다. 똑같은 무력함과 똑같은 공포에 휩싸여 그녀는 한동안 그를 바라보더니, 별안간 왼손을 내밀어 손가락으로 그의 가슴을 희미하게 누르고는, 그에게서 점점 멀어지며 두 눈을 더욱더 미동도 없이 그에게 고정한 채 천천히 침대에서 일어서기 시작했다. 그녀의 공포가 그에게 옮아붙었다. 똑같은 두려움이 그의 얼굴에도 드러났다. 그 역시 똑같이 그녀를 응시했고, 거의 똑같은 어린아이 같은 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"맞혔소?\" 마침내 그가 속삭였다.\n\n\"오, 하느님!\" 그녀의 가슴에서 끔찍한 비명이 터져 나왔다.\n\n그녀는 얼굴을 베개에 파묻은 채 속절없이 침대로 쓰러졌으나, 잠시 후 일어나 재빨리 그에게 다가가더니, 두 손을 붙잡아 가느다란 손가락으로 꽉 움켜쥐고는, 다시 아까와 똑같이 뚫어지게 그의 얼굴을 들여다보기 시작했다. 이 마지막 필사적인 눈길로 그녀는 그의 속을 들여다보며 어떤 마지막 희망을 붙잡으려 했다. 그러나 희망은 없었다. 의심의 여지는 남아 있지 않았다. 모든 게 사실이었다! 정말이지, 나중에 그 순간을 떠올렸을 때 그녀는 그것을 이상하게 여겼고, 왜 자기가 의심의 여지가 없음을 단번에 알아챘는지 의아해했다. 이를테면 그녀는 그런 비슷한 일을 예감하고 있었다고는 말할 수 없었으련만 — 그런데도 지금, 그가 말하자마자, 그녀는 문득 자기가 바로 이 일을 정말로 예감하고 있었던 것 같은 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"그만, 소냐, 됐소! 나를 괴롭히지 마시오.\" 그가 비참하게 그녀에게 애원했다.\n\n이것은 그가 그녀에게 말하려고 생각했던 방식과는 전혀, 전혀 달랐으나, 일은 이렇게 벌어지고 말았다.\n\n그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모르는 듯 벌떡 일어나, 두 손을 쥐어짜며 방 한가운데로 걸어갔다. 그러나 곧 되돌아와 다시 그의 곁에 앉았는데, 어깨가 그의 어깨에 거의 닿을 정도였다. 별안간 그녀는 칼에 찔린 듯 흠칫하더니, 비명을 지르며 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇었는데, 그녀 자신도 왜 그러는지 알지 못했다.\n\n\"무슨 짓을 한 거예요 — 당신 스스로에게 무슨 짓을 한 거예요?\" 그녀가 절망에 차서 말하고는, 벌떡 일어나 그의 목에 매달려 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안고 꼭 끌어안았다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 몸을 빼며 구슬픈 미소를 지은 채 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은 이상한 처녀구려, 소냐 — 내가 그런 얘기를 하는데 입을 맞추고 끌어안다니……. 당신은 지금 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 모르고 있소.\"\n\n\"이제 온 세상에 당신보다 더 불행한 사람은 아무도 — 아무도 없어요!\" 그녀는 그의 말을 듣지도 않은 채 광란에 차서 외쳤고, 별안간 격렬한 히스테릭한 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n오랫동안 그에게 낯설었던 어떤 감정이 그의 가슴에 밀려들어 단번에 그것을 누그러뜨렸다. 그는 그것에 맞서 싸우지 않았다. 두 줄기 눈물이 그의 눈에 솟아 속눈썹에 맺혔다.\n\n\"그럼 나를 떠나지 않겠소, 소냐?\" 그가 거의 희망에 찬 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"안 떠나요, 안 떠나, 절대로, 어디로도!\" 소냐가 외쳤다. \"당신을 따라가겠어요, 어디든 따라가겠어요. 오, 하느님! 오, 저는 어쩌면 이렇게 비참한지!……. 왜, 왜 저는 진작 당신을 알지 못했을까요! 왜 진작 오지 않으셨어요? 오, 가엾어라!\"\n\n\"이렇게 왔잖소.\"\n\n\"네, 이제야! 이제 어떻게 하면 좋아요?…… 함께, 함께!\" 그녀는 거의 무의식적으로 되뇌며 그를 다시 끌어안았다. \"시베리아까지 당신을 따라가겠어요!\"\n\n이 말에 그는 흠칫 물러섰고, 그의 입술에는 아까와 똑같은 적대적이고 거의 거만하기까지 한 미소가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"어쩌면 나는 아직 시베리아에 갈 마음이 없는지도 모르오, 소냐.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n소냐가 재빨리 그를 바라보았다.\n\n이 불행한 사내에 대한 처음의 격렬하고 애끓는 연민이 지나자, 다시 살인이라는 그 끔찍한 생각이 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 그의 달라진 어조 속에서 그녀는 살인자가 말하는 소리를 듣는 듯했다. 그녀는 어리둥절하여 그를 바라보았다. 그녀는 아직 아무것도, 왜, 어떻게, 무슨 목적으로 그런 일이 있었는지 알지 못했다. 이제 이 모든 의문이 한꺼번에 그녀의 머릿속으로 밀려들었다. 그리고 그녀는 또다시 그것을 믿을 수 없었다. \"이 사람이, 이 사람이 살인자라니! 그게 사실일 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"이게 다 무슨 뜻인가요? 제가 지금 어디 있는 거죠?\" 그녀가 아직도 정신을 차리지 못한 듯 완전히 어리둥절해하며 말했다. \"어떻게 당신이, 당신이, 당신 같은 사람이……. 어떻게 그런 일을 저지를 수 있었어요?…… 이게 다 무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n\"오, 뭐 — 강탈하려고 그랬소. 그만둡시다, 소냐.\" 그가 지친 듯, 거의 짜증스럽게 대답했다.\n\n소냐는 말문이 막힌 듯 서 있다가, 별안간 외쳤다.\n\n\"배가 고팠던 거군요! 그게…… 어머니를 도우려고요? 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아니오, 소냐, 아니오.\" 그가 외면하며 고개를 떨군 채 중얼거렸다. \"그렇게까지 배가 고프지는 않았소……. 물론 어머니를 돕고 싶기는 했지만…… 그것도 진짜 이유는 아니오……. 나를 괴롭히지 마시오, 소냐.\"\n\n소냐가 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"그게, 그게 다 사실일 수 있을까요? 오, 하느님, 이 무슨 진실인가요! 누가 이걸 믿겠어요? 그리고 어떻게 당신이 마지막 한 푼까지 내주면서 강탈하고 살인을 할 수 있나요! 아.\" 그녀가 별안간 외쳤다. \"카테리나 이바노브나에게 주신 그 돈…… 그 돈……. 설마 그 돈이…….\"\n\n\"아니오, 소냐.\" 그가 황급히 말을 가로막았다. \"그 돈은 그게 아니오. 걱정 마시오! 그 돈은 어머니가 보내 주신 건데, 내가 앓고 있을 때 도착했소, 당신에게 그 돈을 준 바로 그날에……. 라주미힌이 봤소…… 나 대신 받아 줬으니까……. 그 돈은 내 거였소 — 내 거였다고.\"\n\n소냐는 어리둥절하여 그의 말을 들으며 이해하려고 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n\"그리고 그 돈은……. 사실 거기 돈이 있기나 했는지도 나는 잘 모르겠소.\" 그가 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 부드럽게 덧붙였다. \"그녀의 목에서 지갑을 하나 벗겨 냈소, 사슴 가죽으로 만든…… 무언가가 잔뜩 채워진 지갑을…… 하지만 그 안을 들여다보지는 않았소. 아마 그럴 틈이 없었던 거겠지……. 그리고 그 물건들 — 사슬이며 장신구들 — 은 다음 날 아침 V 거리 어귀의 어느 마당에서 지갑과 함께 돌 밑에 묻어 버렸소. 지금도 다 거기 있을 거요…….\"\n\n소냐는 온 신경을 곤두세워 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그럼 왜…… 왜, 당신은 강탈하려고 그랬다면서 아무것도 가져가지 않았어요?\" 그녀가 지푸라기라도 잡듯 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠소……. 그 돈을 가질지 말지 아직 결정하지 못했소.\" 그가 다시 생각에 잠겨 말하고는, 흠칫 깨어나는 듯 짤막하고 빈정대는 미소를 지었다. \"아, 내가 무슨 어리석은 소리를 지껄이고 있는 거지, 응?\"\n\n저이가 미친 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 소냐의 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그러나 그녀는 곧바로 그것을 떨쳐 버렸다. \"아니, 뭔가 다른 거야.\" 그녀는 그것을 도무지 이해할 수 없었다, 도무지.\n\n\"당신 아오, 소냐.\" 그가 별안간 확신에 차서 말했다. \"내가 말해 주겠소. 만일 내가 그저 배가 고파서 죽였다면,\" 그는 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주며, 알 수 없으면서도 진지한 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보았다. \"지금 나는 행복할 거요. 이 말을 믿어야 하오! 그게 당신에게 무슨 상관이오,\" 잠시 후 그가 일종의 절망에 차서 외쳤다. \"내가 잘못을 저질렀다고 고백한들 그게 당신에게 무슨 상관이오? 나를 이긴 그런 어리석은 승리에서 당신이 무엇을 얻겠소? 아, 소냐, 내가 오늘 당신에게 온 게 그러려고였소?\"\n\n소냐는 다시 무언가 말하려 했으나, 입을 떼지 못했다.\n\n\"어제 내가 당신더러 함께 가자고 한 건, 당신이 내게 남은 전부이기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"어디로요?\" 소냐가 조심스레 물었다.\n\n\"훔치러도 죽이러도 아니니, 걱정 마시오.\" 그가 쓰게 미소 지었다. \"우리는 너무도 다르오……. 그리고 당신 아오, 소냐, 이제야, 바로 이 순간에야 나는 어제 내가 당신더러 함께 가자고 했던 그 '어디로'가 어딘지 깨달았소! 어제 그 말을 할 때만 해도 나는 어딘지 몰랐던 거요. 나는 한 가지만을 청했고, 한 가지를 위해 당신에게 왔소 — 나를 떠나지 말아 달라고. 떠나지 않겠지, 소냐?\"\n\n그녀가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"그런데 왜, 왜 나는 그녀에게 말했을까? 왜 그녀가 알게 했을까?\" 잠시 후 그가 절망에 차서, 그지없는 번민의 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보며 외쳤다. \"여기 당신은 내게서 해명을 기대하고 있구려, 소냐. 앉아서 그걸 기다리고 있어, 다 보이오. 하지만 내가 당신에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠소? 당신은 이해하지 못할 테고, 그저 비참하게 괴로워할 뿐일 거요…… 나 때문에! 자, 당신은 또 울면서 나를 끌어안고 있구려. 왜 그러는 거요? 내가 내 짐을 견딜 수 없어서, 그걸 다른 사람에게 떠넘기러 왔기 때문이오. 당신도 괴로워하면 나는 좀 나아질 테니! 그런데 당신이 이런 비열한 인간을 사랑할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"하지만 당신도 괴로워하고 있잖아요?\" 소냐가 외쳤다.\n\n또다시 똑같은 감정의 물결이 그의 가슴으로 밀려들었고, 또 한순간 그것을 누그러뜨렸다.\n\n\"소냐, 나는 마음이 못된 사람이오, 그걸 명심하시오. 그것으로 많은 것이 설명될지도 모르오. 내가 온 건 내가 못된 인간이기 때문이오. 오지 않았을 사람들도 있을 거요. 하지만 나는 겁쟁이고…… 비열한 인간이오. 그러나…… 아무래도 좋소! 그건 요점이 아니니까. 이제 말해야 하는데, 어떻게 시작해야 할지 모르겠소.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"아, 우리는 너무도 다르오.\" 그가 다시 외쳤다. \"우리는 닮지 않았소. 그런데 왜, 왜 내가 왔을까? 나는 결코 나 자신을 용서하지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요, 오신 건 잘한 일이에요.\" 소냐가 외쳤다. \"제가 아는 게 나아요, 훨씬 나아요!\"\n\n그는 번민에 차서 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"정말로 그게 이유였다면 어쩌겠소?\" 그가 결론에 다다른 듯 말했다. \"그래, 바로 그거였소! 나는 나폴레옹이 되고 싶었던 거요, 그래서 그녀를 죽인 거요……. 이제 알겠소?\"\n\n\"아…… 아니요.\" 소냐가 순진하고 조심스럽게 속삭였다. \"그냥 말씀하세요, 말씀하세요, 제가 이해할게요, 제가 스스로 이해해 볼게요!\" 그녀는 그에게 자꾸만 애원했다.\n\n\"이해하겠다고? 좋소, 두고 봅시다!\" 그는 말을 멈추고 한동안 깊은 사색에 잠겼다.\n\n\"이런 거였소. 어느 날 나는 스스로에게 이런 질문을 던졌소 — 만일 나폴레옹이, 이를테면, 내 처지에 놓였더라면, 그래서 자기 경력을 시작할 툴롱도 이집트도 몽블랑 횡단도 없이, 그 그림 같고 기념비적인 일들 대신, 그저 우스꽝스러운 노파 하나가, 전당포 노파가 있어서, 그 궤짝에서 돈을 빼내려면 (그의 경력을 위해서 말이오, 알겠소?) 그녀까지 죽여야 했다면 어땠을까 하고. 자, 다른 수가 없었다면 그가 그 일을 해냈을까? 그게 너무도 기념비적이지 못하고…… 게다가 죄스러운 일이라는 것에 가책을 느끼지 않았을까? 자, 내가 당신에게 말해 두지만, 나는 그 '질문'을 두고 무섭도록 스스로를 괴롭혔소. 그래서 마침내 (어찌 된 일인지 불현듯) 깨달았을 때 나는 끔찍이도 부끄러웠소. 그가 손톱만큼의 가책도 느끼지 않았으리라는 것, 그게 기념비적이지 못하다는 생각조차 그에게는 떠오르지 않았으리라는 것을…… 거기에 망설일 만한 무언가가 있다는 것을 그는 보지도 못했으리라는 것, 그리고 다른 길이 없었다면 그는 일 분 만에 아무 생각 없이 그녀의 목을 졸랐으리라는 것을! 자, 나도…… 그것에 대해 생각하기를 그만두고…… 그의 본보기를 따라 그녀를 죽인 거요. 바로 그렇게 된 거요! 우습소? 그래, 소냐, 무엇보다 우스운 건, 어쩌면 정말로 그렇게 된 건지도 모른다는 거요.\"\n\n소냐는 그것을 조금도 우습게 여기지 않았다.\n\n\"차라리 똑바로 말해 주세요…… 그런 본보기 같은 것 없이요.\" 그녀는 더욱더 조심스레, 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 애원했다.\n\n그는 그녀에게로 몸을 돌려 슬프게 그녀를 바라보며 두 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"또 당신 말이 옳소, 소냐. 물론 그건 다 헛소리지, 거의 다 말장난이오! 보시오, 당신도 물론 알겠지만, 내 어머니는 가진 게 거의 없고, 누이는 좋은 교육을 받았으면서도 가정 교사로 죽도록 일하는 신세가 됐소. 그들의 모든 희망은 내게 걸려 있었지. 나는 대학생이었지만 학비를 댈 수 없어 한동안 학교를 그만둘 수밖에 없었소. 그렇게 질질 끌었다 한들, 십 년이나 십이 년쯤 지나면 (운이 좋으면) 일 년에 천 루블쯤 받는 어떤 교사나 서기 정도는 될 수 있었을 거요.\" (그는 마치 외운 것을 읊듯 되뇌었다.) \"그러나 그때쯤이면 어머니는 슬픔과 근심으로 다 쇠하셨을 테고, 나는 그분을 편안히 모시지도 못했을 테고, 누이는…… 글쎄, 누이는 더 나쁜 신세가 됐을지도 모르오! 게다가 평생 모든 것을 그냥 지나치고, 모든 것에 등을 돌리고, 어머니를 잊고, 누이에게 가해진 모욕을 점잖게 받아들이는 것 — 그건 힘든 일이오. 무엇 때문에? 그들을 묻고 나서 또 다른 이들을 — 아내와 자식들을 — 떠안고는, 다시 그들을 한 푼 없이 남겨 두려고? 그래서 나는 그 노파의 돈을 손에 넣어, 어머니를 괴롭히지 않고 처음 몇 해를 그것으로 버티며, 대학을 다니고 졸업한 뒤에도 잠시 그것으로 지내기로 결심한 거요 — 그리고 이 모든 것을 넓고 철저한 규모로 해서, 완전히 새로운 경력을 쌓고 독립된 새 삶으로 들어서기로……. 자…… 그게 다요……. 자, 물론 그 노파를 죽임으로써 나는 잘못을 저질렀소……. 자, 됐소.\"\n\n그는 기진맥진하여 간신히 말을 끝맺고는 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"오, 그게 아니에요, 그게 아니에요.\" 소냐가 괴로워하며 외쳤다. \"어떻게 사람이…… 아니, 그건 옳지 않아요, 옳지 않아요.\"\n\n\"옳지 않다는 건 당신도 알겠지. 하지만 나는 진실을 말했소, 그게 진실이오.\"\n\n\"그게 진실일 수가 있나요! 오, 하느님!\"\n\n\"나는 그저 이 한 마리를 죽였을 뿐이오, 소냐, 쓸모없고 역겹고 해로운 벌레를.\"\n\n\"사람이 — 이라니요!\"\n\n\"그게 이가 아니었다는 건 나도 아오.\" 그가 그녀를 기묘하게 바라보며 대답했다. \"하지만 나는 지금 헛소리를 하고 있소, 소냐.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"오랫동안 헛소리만 하고 있었지……. 그게 아니오, 거기선 당신 말이 옳소. 거기엔 전혀, 전혀 다른 이유들이 있었소! 내가 이렇게 오랫동안 아무하고도 말을 안 했구려, 소냐……. 지금 머리가 끔찍하게 아프오.\"\n\n그의 눈이 열병에 들뜬 광채로 빛났다. 그는 거의 헛소리를 하고 있었다. 불안한 미소가 그의 입가를 떠돌았다. 그의 흥분 너머로 그 끔찍한 탈진이 비쳐 보였다. 소냐는 그가 얼마나 괴로워하는지 보았다. 그녀 역시 어지러워지고 있었다. 그리고 그는 너무도 기묘하게 말했다. 어딘가 이해될 것 같다가도, 그러나……. \"하지만 어떻게, 어떻게! 오, 하느님!\" 그녀는 절망에 차서 두 손을 쥐어짰다.\n\n\"아니오, 소냐, 그게 아니오.\" 그가 또다시 불쑥, 마치 새롭고 갑작스러운 생각의 갈래가 그를 치고 들어와 그를 일깨운 듯, 고개를 들며 입을 열었다. \"그게 아니오! 차라리…… 상상해 보시오 — 그래, 그게 분명 낫겠소 — 내가 허영심 많고, 시기심 많고, 악의에 차 있고, 비천하고, 앙심을 품고, 또…… 글쎄, 어쩌면 광기로 기울기까지 한 인간이라고 상상해 보시오. (단번에 다 털어놓읍시다! 사람들이 이미 광기 얘기를 하더군, 내가 알아챘소.) 방금 나는 학비를 댈 수 없었다고 당신에게 말했소. 하지만 어쩌면 나는 그럴 수 있었는지도 모른다는 걸 아오? 어머니가 학비에 필요한 걸 보내 주셨을 테고, 옷이며 장화며 먹을 것은 틀림없이 내가 벌 수 있었을 거요. 반 루블짜리 과외 자리도 나왔으니까. 라주미힌은 일하잖소! 그런데 나는 토라져서 그러지 않았소. (그래, 토라짐, 그게 딱 맞는 말이오!) 나는 거미처럼 내 방에 틀어박혀 있었소. 당신도 내 굴에 와 봤으니, 봤겠지……. 그리고 당신 아오, 소냐, 낮은 천장과 비좁은 방이 영혼과 정신을 옥죈다는 걸? 아, 나는 그 다락방이 얼마나 싫었는지! 그런데도 거기서 나가려 하지 않았소! 일부러 안 나갔지! 며칠씩 밖에 나가지 않았고, 일하려고도 하지 않았고, 먹으려고조차 하지 않은 채, 그저 아무것도 안 하고 드러누워 있었소. 나스타시야가 뭔가 가져다주면 먹었고, 안 가져다주면 온종일 굶었소. 일부러, 토라져서 달라고 하지 않았던 거요! 밤에는 불도 없이, 어둠 속에 누워 있었지, 양초 살 돈도 벌려 하지 않았으니까. 공부를 해야 했지만 책을 팔아 버렸고, 탁자 위 공책에는 먼지가 한 치나 쌓여 있소. 나는 가만히 누워 생각하는 편을 택했지. 그리고 자꾸만 생각했소……. 그리고 줄곧 꿈을 꿨소, 온갖 기묘한 꿈을, 굳이 말할 것도 없는! 그러다 그때 나는 이런 공상을 하기 시작했소…… 아니, 그게 아니오! 또 잘못 말하고 있구려! 보시오, 그때 나는 자꾸만 스스로에게 물었소. 왜 나는 이토록 어리석은가, 남들이 어리석다면 — 나는 그들이 어리석다는 걸 아오 — 그렇다면 나는 왜 더 현명해지려 하지 않는가? 그러다 나는 깨달았소, 소냐, 모두가 현명해지기를 기다린다면 너무 오래 걸리리라는 것을……. 그 뒤에 나는 그런 일은 결코 일어나지 않으리라는 것, 인간은 변하지 않으리라는 것, 아무도 그걸 바꿀 수 없으리라는 것, 그러니 그것에 애쓸 가치가 없다는 것을 이해했소. 그래, 그렇소. 그게 그들의 본성의 법칙이오, 소냐…… 그렇단 말이오!…… 그리고 이제 나는 아오, 소냐, 정신과 영혼이 강한 자는 누구든 그들을 지배하는 권력을 갖게 되리라는 것을. 크게 대담한 자는 누구든 그들의 눈에 옳소. 가장 많은 것을 경멸하는 자가 그들 가운데서 입법자가 될 테고, 가장 크게 감행하는 자가 가장 옳을 거요! 지금까지 그래 왔고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거요. 그걸 못 보는 자는 눈먼 자임이 틀림없소!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 이 말을 하면서 소냐를 바라보고 있었지만, 이제 그녀가 이해하든 말든 더는 개의치 않았다. 열병이 그를 완전히 사로잡고 있었다. 그는 일종의 음울한 황홀경에 빠져 있었다 (그는 분명 너무 오랫동안 아무하고도 말을 하지 않았던 것이다). 소냐는 그의 음울한 신조가 그의 신앙이자 율법이 되었음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그때 나는 깨달았소, 소냐,\" 그가 열을 올리며 말을 이었다. \"권력은 감히 몸을 굽혀 그것을 집어 드는 자에게만 주어진다는 것을. 단 한 가지, 단 한 가지가 필요할 뿐이오. 그저 감행하기만 하면 되는 거요! 그때 내 평생 처음으로, 나보다 앞서 그 누구도 생각해 본 적 없는 한 가지 생각이 내 머릿속에 모습을 갖췄소, 그 누구도! 나는 대낮처럼 환히 보았소, 이 미친 세상에 살고 있는 단 한 사람도 감히 그 모든 것을 향해 곧장 나아가 그것을 악마에게 날려 버릴 엄두를 내지 못했다는 게 얼마나 기이한지를! 나는…… 나는 감행할 엄두를 내고 싶었소…… 그래서 그녀를 죽인 거요. 나는 그저 감행할 엄두를 내고 싶었던 거요, 소냐! 그게 전부였소!\"\n\n\"오, 그만, 그만하세요.\" 소냐가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"당신은 하느님에게서 등을 돌렸고, 하느님이 당신을 치셨어요, 당신을 악마에게 넘겨주신 거예요!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 소냐, 내가 어둠 속에 누워 이 모든 게 내게 환히 보였을 때, 그게 악마의 유혹이었단 말이오, 응?\"\n\n\"그만, 웃지 마세요, 신성 모독자! 당신은 이해 못 해요, 이해 못 한다고요! 오, 하느님! 이 사람은 이해를 못 해요!\"\n\n\"그만하시오, 소냐! 나는 웃는 게 아니오. 악마가 나를 끌고 갔다는 걸 나 자신이 알고 있소. 그만, 소냐, 그만!\" 그가 음울한 집요함으로 되뇌었다. \"나는 다 아오, 그걸 거듭거듭 곱씹었고, 어둠 속에 누워 그걸 죄다 스스로에게 속삭였소……. 나는 그것을 한 점 한 점, 나 자신과 다 따져 봤고, 다 아오, 죄다! 그리고 그때 그 모든 걸 되짚는 게 얼마나 진저리 나고, 얼마나 진저리 났는지! 나는 그걸 잊고 새로 시작하고 싶었소, 소냐, 그리고 생각하기를 그만두고 싶었소. 그리고 당신은 내가 바보처럼 무턱대고 그 일에 뛰어들었다고 여기진 않겠지? 나는 현명한 사람처럼 그 일에 들어갔고, 바로 그게 내 파멸이었소. 그리고 이를테면 내가 권력을 가질 권리가 있는지 스스로에게 묻기 시작했다면 — 나는 분명 그럴 권리가 없었소 — 혹은 인간이 이인지 스스로에게 물었다면 그건 내게는 그렇지 않다는 것을 증명하는 셈이라는 걸, 비록 질문 없이 곧장 자기 목표로 나아가는 자에게는 그럴지언정 — 그걸 내가 몰랐다고 여기진 마시오……. 만일 내가 그 며칠 내내 나폴레옹이라면 그 일을 했을지 안 했을지 곱씹으며 스스로를 괴롭혔다면, 나는 물론 내가 나폴레옹이 아니라는 걸 똑똑히 느낀 거요. 나는 그 관념들의 싸움의 온갖 고뇌를 견뎌야 했소, 소냐, 그래서 그걸 떨쳐 버리고 싶었소. 나는 궤변 없이 죽이고 싶었소, 나 자신을 위해, 오직 나만을 위해 죽이고 싶었소! 나는 그것에 대해 나 자신에게조차 거짓말하고 싶지 않았소. 어머니를 돕기 위해 살인을 한 게 아니오 — 그건 헛소리요 — 나는 부와 권력을 얻고 인류의 은인이 되려고 살인을 한 게 아니오. 헛소리! 나는 그저 했소. 나 자신을 위해, 오직 나만을 위해 살인을 했고, 내가 남들에게 은인이 되든, 거미처럼 거미줄에 사람들을 가두어 그 생명을 빨아먹으며 평생을 보내든, 그 순간 나는 조금도 개의치 않았을 거요……. 그리고 그 일을 할 때 내가 원한 건 돈이 아니었소, 소냐. 내가 그토록 원했던 건 돈이 아니라 다른 무엇이었소……. 이제 다 알겠소……. 나를 이해해 주시오! 어쩌면 나는 다시는 살인을 저지르지 않았을지도 모르오. 나는 다른 무언가를 알아내고 싶었소. 나를 끌고 간 건 다른 무엇이었소. 나는 그때 빨리 알아내고 싶었소, 내가 다른 모든 이처럼 이인지, 아니면 인간인지를. 내가 장벽을 넘어설 수 있는지 없는지, 감히 몸을 굽혀 집어 들 엄두를 낼 수 있는지 없는지, 내가 벌벌 떠는 미물인지 아니면 내게 권리가 있는지를…….\"\n\n\"죽일? 죽일 권리가요?\" 소냐가 두 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"아, 소냐!\" 그가 짜증스레 외치며 무언가 대꾸하려는 듯하다가, 경멸하듯 입을 다물었다. \"내 말을 막지 마시오, 소냐. 나는 단 한 가지만을 증명하고 싶소. 그때 악마가 나를 끌고 갔고, 그 뒤로 악마가 내게 보여 주었다는 것을, 내게는 그 길을 갈 권리가 없었다는 것을, 왜냐하면 나도 다른 모든 이와 꼭 같은 이일 뿐이니까! 악마는 나를 조롱하고 있었고, 그래서 나는 이렇게 지금 당신에게 온 거요! 당신의 손님을 맞이하시오! 만일 내가 이가 아니었다면, 내가 당신에게 왔겠소? 들어 보시오, 그때 내가 노파에게 갔을 때, 나는 그저 시험해 보러 갔을 뿐이오……. 그건 믿어도 좋소!\"\n\n\"그래 놓고 그녀를 죽였잖아요!\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 어떻게 그녀를 죽였소? 사람이 살인을 그렇게 하오? 사람이 그때 내가 갔던 식으로 살인을 저지르러 가오? 언젠가 내가 어떻게 갔는지 당신에게 말해 주겠소! 내가 그 노파를 죽인 거요? 나는 나 자신을 죽인 거요, 그녀가 아니라! 나는 단번에, 영영 나 자신을 짓뭉개 버린 거요……. 하지만 그 노파를 죽인 건 악마지, 내가 아니오. 됐소, 됐소, 소냐, 됐소! 나를 내버려 두시오!\" 그가 별안간 고뇌의 발작에 차서 외쳤다. \"나를 내버려 두시오!\"\n\n그는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 머리를 죔쇠처럼 죄었다.\n\n\"이 무슨 고통인가요!\" 소냐의 입에서 번민의 비탄이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"자, 이제 나는 어떻게 하면 좋소?\" 그가 별안간 고개를 들고, 절망으로 흉측하게 일그러진 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"어떻게 하면 좋으냐고요?\" 그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤고, 눈물로 가득했던 그녀의 두 눈이 별안간 빛나기 시작했다. \"일어서요!\" (그녀가 그의 어깨를 붙잡자, 그는 거의 어리둥절한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 일어섰다.) \"지금 당장, 이 순간 가서, 네거리에 서서, 무릎 꿇고, 먼저 당신이 더럽힌 땅에 입을 맞추고, 그런 다음 온 세상을 향해 절하며 모든 사람에게 큰 소리로 외치세요, '내가 살인자다!' 하고요. 그러면 하느님이 당신에게 다시 생명을 보내 주실 거예요. 가시겠어요, 가시겠어요?\" 그녀는 온몸을 떨며, 그의 두 손을 와락 붙잡아 자기 손안에 꼭 쥐고는, 불길로 가득한 눈으로 그를 응시하며 물었다.\n\n그는 그녀의 갑작스러운 황홀경에 놀랐다.\n\n\"시베리아 말이오, 소냐? 자수를 해야 한다는 거요?\" 그가 음울하게 물었다.\n\n\"고통받고 그것으로 당신 죄를 속죄하세요, 그게 당신이 해야 할 일이에요.\"\n\n\"안 돼! 나는 그들에게 가지 않겠소, 소냐!\"\n\n\"하지만 그러면 어떻게 계속 살아가실 거예요? 무엇을 위해 살아가실 거예요?\" 소냐가 외쳤다. \"이제 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요? 아니, 어떻게 어머니께 말씀드릴 수 있겠어요? (오, 이제 그분들은 어떻게 되나요?) 그런데 제가 무슨 소릴 하는 거죠? 당신은 이미 어머니와 누이를 버렸잖아요. 이 사람은 벌써 그분들을 버렸어! 오, 하느님!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"아니, 이 사람은 그걸 다 스스로 알고 있어요. 어떻게, 어떻게 이 사람이 혼자 살아갈 수 있겠어요! 이제 당신은 어떻게 되는 거예요?\"\n\n\"어린애처럼 굴지 마시오, 소냐.\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다. \"내가 그들에게 무슨 잘못을 했소? 무엇 때문에 그들에게 가야 하오? 그들에게 무슨 말을 해야 하오? 그건 그저 환영일 뿐이오……. 그들은 스스로 수백만 명씩 사람을 죽이면서도 그걸 미덕으로 여기오. 그들은 사기꾼이고 악당이오, 소냐! 나는 그들에게 가지 않겠소. 그리고 내가 그들에게 무슨 말을 해야 하오 — 내가 그녀를 죽였지만 돈을 가져갈 엄두는 못 내고 돌 밑에 숨겼다고?\" 그가 쓴웃음을 지으며 덧붙였다. \"아니, 그들은 나를 비웃으며, 그걸 챙기지 못한 바보라고 부를 거요. 겁쟁이에 바보라고! 그들은 이해하지 못할 테고, 이해할 자격도 없소. 무엇 때문에 그들에게 가겠소? 가지 않겠소. 어린애처럼 굴지 마시오, 소냐…….\"\n\n\"당신이 견디기엔 너무 버거워요, 너무 버겁다고요!\" 그녀는 절망적인 애원으로 두 손을 내밀며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 내가 나 자신에게 너무 가혹했는지도 모르오.\" 그가 음울하게, 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"어쩌면 결국 나는 이가 아니라 인간이고, 너무 성급하게 나 자신을 단죄한 건지도 모르오. 다시 한번 싸워 보겠소.\"\n\n거만한 미소가 그의 입술에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그 무슨 짊어질 짐인가요! 게다가 평생을, 평생을 말이에요!\"\n\n\"익숙해질 거요.\" 그가 침울하게, 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"들어 보시오,\" 잠시 후 그가 입을 열었다. \"그만 우시오, 이제 사실들을 얘기할 때요. 경찰이 나를 쫓고 있다는 걸, 내 뒤를 밟고 있다는 걸 말해 주러 왔소…….\"\n\n\"아!\" 소냐가 공포에 차서 외쳤다.\n\n\"자, 왜 소리를 지르오? 나더러 시베리아에 가라더니, 이제 와 겁을 내오? 하지만 말해 두겠소. 나는 자수하지 않겠소. 한번 싸워 보겠고, 그들은 내게 아무 짓도 못 할 거요. 그들에겐 진짜 증거가 없으니까. 어제 나는 큰 위기에 처해 끝장났다고 믿었지만, 오늘은 사정이 나아지고 있소. 그들이 아는 사실들은 죄다 두 가지로 해석될 수 있소, 다시 말해 나는 그들의 고발을 도리어 내게 유리하게 돌릴 수 있다는 거요, 알겠소? 그리고 나는 그렇게 할 거요, 교훈을 얻었으니까. 하지만 그들은 분명 나를 체포할 거요. 어떤 일이 일어나지 않았더라면, 그들은 오늘 틀림없이 그렇게 했을 거요. 어쩌면 지금이라도 그들은 오늘 나를 체포할지도 모르오……. 하지만 그건 상관없소, 소냐. 그들은 나를 다시 풀어 줄 거요…… 나에 대한 진짜 증거가 없고, 앞으로도 없을 테니까, 내 장담하오. 그리고 그들이 가진 것만으로는 사람을 유죄로 만들 수 없소. 됐소……. 그저 당신이 알도록 말해 주는 것뿐이오……. 어머니와 누이에게는 그들이 놀라지 않도록 어떻게든 잘 말해 볼 작정이오……. 그래도 누이의 앞날은 이제 안전한 것 같소, 아마도…… 어머니도 분명 그럴 거요……. 자, 그게 다요. 하지만 조심하시오. 내가 감옥에 가면 나를 보러 와 주겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 갈게요, 갈게요.\"\n\n그들은 나란히 앉아 있었다. 둘 다, 마치 폭풍에 떠밀려 어느 황량한 기슭에 홀로 내던져진 듯, 비통하고 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그는 소냐를 바라보며 그녀의 사랑이 얼마나 큰지를 느꼈고, 이상하게도 그토록 사랑받는다는 것이 별안간 짐스럽고 고통스럽게 느껴졌다. 그래, 그것은 기묘하고 끔찍한 감각이었다! 소냐를 만나러 오는 길에 그는 자신의 모든 희망이 그녀에게 달려 있다고 느꼈다. 그는 적어도 자기 고통의 일부만이라도 덜어 내리라 기대했건만, 이제 그녀의 온 마음이 그를 향하자, 그는 별안간 자신이 전보다 헤아릴 수 없이 더 불행하다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"소냐,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 감옥에 있을 땐 차라리 나를 보러 오지 않는 게 낫겠소.\"\n\n소냐는 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 울고 있었다. 몇 분이 흘렀다.\n\n\"몸에 십자가를 지니고 있나요?\" 그녀가 문득 그 생각이 떠오른 듯 물었다.\n\n그는 처음에는 그 질문을 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n\"아니, 물론 없소. 자, 이걸 받으세요, 삼나무로 만든 거예요. 제겐 또 하나, 리자베타의 것이었던 구리 십자가가 있어요. 리자베타와 바꿨거든요. 그녀는 제게 자기 십자가를 주고, 저는 그녀에게 제 작은 성상을 줬어요. 이제 저는 리자베타의 걸 걸고, 당신에게 이걸 드릴게요. 받으세요…… 제 거예요! 제 거라니까요, 아시잖아요.\" 그녀가 그에게 애원했다. \"우리 함께 고통받으러 가요, 함께 우리 십자가를 짊어져요!\"\n\n\"이리 주시오.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀의 마음을 상하게 하고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 곧장 그는 십자가를 받으려 내밀었던 손을 도로 거두었다.\n\n\"지금은 말고, 소냐. 나중이 낫겠소.\" 그가 그녀를 달래려 덧붙였다.\n\n\"네, 네, 그게 낫겠어요.\" 그녀가 확신에 차서 되뇌었다. \"당신이 당신의 고통을 맞으러 갈 때, 그때 거시면 돼요. 제게 오세요, 제가 걸어 드릴게요, 우리 기도하고 함께 가요.\"\n\n그 순간 누군가 문을 세 번 두드렸다.\n\n\"소피야 세묘노브나, 들어가도 될까요?\" 아주 귀에 익고 정중한 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n소냐는 깜짝 놀라 문으로 달려갔다. 레베쟈트니코프 씨의 아마빛 머리가 문 앞에 나타났다.\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 안절부절못하는 기색이었다.\n\n\"제가 댁에게 왔습니다, 소피야 세묘노브나.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"실례합니다……, 댁을 만날 수 있으리라 생각했어요.\" 그가 갑자기 라스콜니코프를 향해 말했다. \"그러니까, 무슨 그런……, 그런 뜻으로 한 말은 아닙니다……. 다만 제가 생각하기에는……, 카테리나 이바노브나가 정신이 나가 버렸어요.\" 그가 불쑥 내뱉으며 라스콜니코프에게서 소냐 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n소냐가 비명을 질렀다.\n\n\"적어도 그렇게 보입니다. 하지만……, 어떻게 해야 할지 우리도 모르겠어요, 그게 문제예요! 그녀가 돌아왔는데——어딘가에서 쫓겨난 것 같고, 어쩌면 얻어맞기까지 한 것 같습니다……. 적어도 그렇게 보여요……. 댁 아버지의 옛 상관에게 달려갔는데, 그 사람이 집에 없었던 거죠. 다른 어느 장군 집에서 식사를 하고 있었답니다……. 글쎄, 그녀가 그리로, 그 다른 장군 집으로 쫓아가서는, 어찌나 고집을 부렸던지 그 상관을 만나는 데 기어이 성공했다지 뭡니까, 식사 중인 사람을 불러내게 했나 봐요. 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 짐작이 가시죠. 물론 쫓겨났습니다. 하지만 그녀 자신의 말로는, 그 사람에게 욕을 퍼붓고 뭔가를 집어 던졌다더군요. 충분히 그러고도 남았겠죠……. 어쩌다 붙잡혀 가지 않았는지, 저는 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요! 지금 그녀는 아말리야 이바노브나를 포함해 모두에게 그 얘기를 떠들어 대고 있는데, 알아듣기가 어렵습니다, 비명을 지르며 마구 몸을 내던지고 있으니까요……. 아, 그래요, 그녀가 소리치기를, 모두가 자기를 버렸으니 아이들을 데리고 손풍금을 들고 거리로 나가겠다는 겁니다, 아이들은 노래하고 춤추고, 자기도 그러면서, 돈을 거두고, 매일 그 장군의 창문 밑으로 가겠다고요……. '관리였던 아버지를 둔, 좋은 집안의 아이들이 거리에서 구걸하는 꼴을 모두가 똑똑히 보게 해 주겠다'면서요. 그녀는 줄곧 아이들을 때리고, 아이들은 죄다 울고 있어요. 리다에게는 '나의 작은 마을'을 부르게 가르치고, 사내아이에게는 춤을, 폴렌카에게도 마찬가지로 시키고 있죠. 옷을 죄다 찢어서 배우들처럼 작은 모자를 만들어 주고요. 음악 대신 양철 대야를 들고 다니며 댕댕 두드리겠다는 겁니다……. 무슨 말을 해도 듣질 않아요……. 그 꼴을 상상해 보세요! 도무지 말이 안 되는 지경입니다!\"\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 계속 말하려 했으나, 거의 숨도 못 쉬며 듣고 있던 소냐가 망토와 모자를 낚아채더니, 옷을 걸치면서 방 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 라스콜니코프가 그 뒤를 따랐고, 레베쟈트니코프도 그를 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"틀림없이 미쳐 버린 거예요!\" 거리로 나서며 그가 라스콜니코프에게 말했다. \"소피야 세묘노브나를 겁주고 싶지 않아서 '그렇게 보인다'고 말했지만, 의심할 여지가 없어요. 결핵에 걸리면 가끔 결절이 뇌에 생기기도 한다더군요. 제가 의학을 전혀 모르는 게 안타깝습니다. 그녀를 설득해 보려고 했지만, 통 들으려 하질 않았어요.\"\n\n\"결절 얘기를 한 겁니까?\"\n\n\"꼭 결절 얘기는 아니었습니다. 게다가 알아듣지도 못했을 거예요! 하지만 제 말은, 어떤 사람에게 울 까닭이 전혀 없다는 걸 논리적으로 납득시키면, 그 사람은 울음을 그치게 된다는 겁니다. 그건 명백하죠. 그가 그치지 않을 거라는 게 댁의 확신입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 인생이 너무 쉬울 텐데요.\" 라스콜니코프가 대답했다.\n\n\"실례지만, 실례지만요. 물론 카테리나 이바노브나로서는 알아듣기가 다소 어렵겠죠. 하지만 파리에서는 단지 논리적인 논증만으로 정신병자를 치료할 수 있는지 진지하게 실험을 해 왔다는 걸 아십니까? 거기 어느 교수가, 명망 있는 과학자였는데, 최근에 죽었습니다만, 그런 치료의 가능성을 믿었어요. 그의 생각은, 정신병자의 신체 기관에는 사실 아무 이상이 없다는 것, 그리고 광기란 말하자면 논리적 오류, 판단의 착오, 사물에 대한 그릇된 견해라는 거였죠. 그는 차츰차츰 미친 사람에게 그 잘못을 보여 주었고, 글쎄, 믿어지십니까, 성공했다지 뭡니까? 하지만 물 치료법도 함께 썼기 때문에, 그 치료가 어디까지 효과가 있었는지는 여전히 불분명합니다……. 적어도 그렇게 보여요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 이미 오래전에 듣기를 그만두고 있었다. 자기가 사는 집에 다다르자, 그는 레베쟈트니코프에게 고개를 까딱하고는 대문 안으로 들어갔다. 레베쟈트니코프는 흠칫 정신을 차리더니, 주위를 둘러보고는 서둘러 가 버렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 자기 작은 방으로 들어가 한가운데 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 왜 여기로 돌아온 것일까? 그는 누렇게 바래고 너덜너덜한 벽지를, 먼지를, 자기 소파를 바라보았다……. 마당에서 크고 끊임없는 두드리는 소리가 들려왔다. 누군가가 망치질을 하는 것 같았다……. 그는 창가로 가서 발끝으로 서서는, 골똘히 몰두한 표정으로 오랫동안 마당을 내다보았다. 하지만 마당은 텅 비어 있어서, 누가 망치질을 하는지 알 수 없었다. 왼편 건물에는 열린 창문 몇 개가 보였고, 창턱에는 시들시들한 제라늄 화분들이 놓여 있었다. 창밖으로는 빨래가 널려 있었다……. 그는 그 모든 것을 훤히 알고 있었다. 그는 몸을 돌려 소파에 앉았다.\n\n이토록 무섭게 혼자라고 느껴 본 적은 단 한 번도 없었다!\n\n그렇다, 그는 다시금, 이제 그녀를 더 비참하게 만들어 놓은 지금, 어쩌면 자신이 소냐를 미워하게 될지도 모른다고 느꼈다.\n\n'무엇 때문에 그녀에게 가서 그녀의 눈물을 구걸했단 말인가? 무엇 때문에 그녀의 삶에 독을 풀어야 했단 말인가? 오, 이 비열함이라니!'\n\n\"나는 혼자 남겠어.\" 그가 단호히 말했다. \"그리고 그녀를 감옥에 오지 못하게 하겠어!\"\n\n오 분쯤 지나 그는 묘한 미소를 띠며 고개를 들었다. 그것은 묘한 생각이었다.\n\n'어쩌면 시베리아가 정말로 더 나을지도 몰라.' 그는 문득 생각했다.\n\n막연한 생각들이 머릿속을 물결치며 지나가는 동안 자기가 거기 얼마나 오래 앉아 있었는지 그는 알 수 없었다. 갑자기 문이 열리며 두냐가 들어왔다. 처음에 그녀는 가만히 서서 문가에서 그를 바라보았는데, 마치 그가 소냐를 바라보았던 것과 똑같았다. 그러고는 안으로 들어와 어제와 같은 자리, 그의 맞은편 의자에 앉았다. 그는 말없이, 거의 멍하니 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"화내지 마, 오빠. 잠깐만 들렀어.\" 두냐가 말했다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴은 생각에 잠긴 듯했으나 엄하지는 않았다. 두 눈은 맑고 부드러웠다. 그는 그녀 역시 사랑을 품고 자기에게 왔음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"오빠, 이제 나는 다 알아, 모두 다. 드미트리 프로코피치가 다 설명해 주고 이야기해 줬어. 어리석고 비열한 의심으로 사람들이 오빠를 괴롭히고 들볶고 있다는 거 말이야……. 드미트리 프로코피치 말이, 아무 위험도 없고, 오빠가 그걸 그렇게 끔찍하게 받아들이는 건 잘못이래. 나는 그렇게 생각하진 않아, 오빠가 얼마나 분개할 수밖에 없는지, 그리고 그 분노가 오빠한테 영영 사라지지 않는 상처를 남길 수도 있다는 걸 잘 이해하니까. 바로 그게 내가 두려워하는 거야. 오빠가 우리한테서 떨어져 나가는 것에 대해서는, 나는 오빠를 탓하지 않아, 감히 탓할 수 없어, 그 일로 오빠를 비난했던 걸 용서해 줘. 나 역시, 그토록 큰 괴로움이 있었다면, 모두에게서 멀리 떨어져 있었을 거라는 걸 느끼니까. 어머니께는 이 일을 아무것도 말하지 않을게, 그래도 오빠 얘기는 끊임없이 할 거고, 오빠가 곧 오겠다고 했다고 전할게. 어머니 걱정은 하지 마. 내가 어머니 마음을 가라앉혀 드릴 테니까. 하지만 오빠도 어머니를 너무 시험하지는 마——적어도 한 번은 와 줘. 어머니라는 걸 잊지 마. 그리고 지금은 그저 이 말을 하러 온 거야\"——두냐가 일어서기 시작했다——\"혹시 내가 필요하거나, 혹은 필요하면……, 내 평생이든 무엇이든……, 나를 불러, 그럼 갈게. 잘 있어!\"\n\n그녀는 불쑥 몸을 돌려 문 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n\"두냐!\" 라스콜니코프가 그녀를 멈춰 세우며 그녀에게로 다가갔다. \"그 라주미힌, 드미트리 프로코피치 말이야, 아주 좋은 사람이다.\"\n\n두냐의 얼굴이 살짝 붉어졌다.\n\n\"그래서?\" 그녀가 잠깐 기다리며 물었다.\n\n\"유능하고, 부지런하고, 정직하고, 진정한 사랑을 할 수 있는 사람이야……. 잘 있어, 두냐.\"\n\n두냐의 얼굴이 새빨갛게 물들더니, 갑자기 그녀는 놀라는 빛을 띠었다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 무슨 뜻이야, 오빠? 우리가 정말 영영 헤어지기라도 하는 거야, 오빠가……, 그런 작별 인사를 다 하다니?\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마……. 잘 있어.\"\n\n그는 몸을 돌려 창가로 걸어갔다. 그녀는 잠시 서서 불안하게 그를 바라보다가, 마음이 어지러운 채 나가 버렸다.\n\n아니, 그가 그녀에게 냉담했던 건 아니다. 한순간(바로 그 마지막 순간) 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 작별을 고하고 싶은, 심지어 모든 것을 말해 버리고 싶은 마음이 간절했으나, 그는 감히 그녀의 손조차 건드리지 못했다.\n\n'나중에 그녀는 내가 자기를 끌어안았던 걸 떠올리며 몸서리칠 테고, 내가 자기 입맞춤을 훔쳤다고 느끼겠지.'\n\n'게다가 그녀가 그 시험을 견뎌 낼 수 있을까?' 그는 몇 분 뒤 다시 혼잣말로 이어 갔다. '아니, 못 견뎌. 그런 여자애들은 그런 걸 견뎌 내지 못해! 절대로 못 하지.'\n\n그러고는 소냐를 떠올렸다.\n\n창문에서 신선한 공기가 한 줄기 불어 들어왔다. 햇빛이 스러져 가고 있었다. 그는 모자를 집어 들고 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n물론 그는 자신이 얼마나 아픈지 헤아릴 수도, 헤아리려 하지도 않았다. 하지만 이 끊임없는 불안과 마음의 고통이 그에게 영향을 미치지 않을 리 없었다. 그가 고열에 누워 있지 않은 것은, 어쩌면 바로 이 끊임없는 내면의 긴장이 그를 두 발로 서 있게 하고 제정신을 붙들고 있게 도와준 덕분인지도 몰랐다. 하지만 이 인위적인 흥분이 오래갈 수는 없었다.\n\n그는 정처 없이 헤매고 다녔다. 해가 지고 있었다. 요즘 들어 어떤 특별한 형태의 비참함이 그를 짓누르기 시작했다. 거기에는 사무치게 아픈 것도, 날카로운 것도 없었다. 그러나 거기에는 영속성의, 영원의 느낌이 깃들어 있었다. 그것은 이 차갑고 납덩이 같은 비참함이 가득한 절망의 세월에 대한 예감을, '한 평 남짓한 공간에서의' 영원에 대한 예감을 가져다주었다. 저녁 무렵이면 이 감각은 으레 그를 더욱 무겁게 짓누르기 시작했다.\n\n'이 어리석고 순전히 육체적인 쇠약, 해넘이인지 뭔지에 좌우되는 이런 것 때문에, 사람은 어쩔 수 없이 멍청한 짓을 저지르게 되는 거야! 소냐한테뿐 아니라 두냐한테까지 가게 되겠지.' 그가 쓰디쓰게 중얼거렸다.\n\n누군가 그의 이름을 부르는 소리가 들렸다. 그는 돌아보았다. 레베쟈트니코프가 그에게로 달려왔다.\n\n\"글쎄 말입니다, 댁을 찾으러 방에 다녀왔어요. 글쎄, 그녀가 자기 계획을 실행에 옮겨서 아이들을 데리고 나가 버렸어요. 소피야 세묘노브나와 제가 그들을 찾느라 한바탕 애를 먹었습니다. 그녀가 프라이팬을 두드리며 아이들을 춤추게 하고 있어요. 아이들은 울고 있고요. 그들은 자꾸 네거리며 가게 앞에서 멈춰 서는데, 멍청한 구경꾼들이 떼를 지어 뒤를 따라다니고 있습니다. 어서 가시죠!\"\n\n\"그럼 소냐는요?\" 라스콜니코프가 레베쟈트니코프를 서둘러 뒤따르며 걱정스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"그냥 제정신이 아니에요. 아니, 제정신이 아닌 건 소피야 세묘노브나가 아니라 카테리나 이바노브나입니다, 물론 소피야 세묘노브나도 제정신이 아니긴 하죠. 하지만 카테리나 이바노브나는 완전히 제정신이 아니에요. 정말이지 그녀는 완전히 미쳤습니다. 경찰에 끌려갈 거예요. 그게 어떤 결과를 가져올지 짐작이 가시겠죠……. 그들은 지금 운하 둑, 다리 근처에 있어요, 소피야 세묘노브나 집에서 멀지 않은 데, 아주 가까운 곳에요.\"\n\n운하 둑, 다리 근처, 소냐가 세 들어 사는 집에서 두 집도 채 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사람들이 떼를 지어 모여 있었는데, 주로 길거리 부랑아들이었다. 카테리나 이바노브나의 쉬고 갈라진 목소리가 다리에서부터 들려왔으니, 정말이지 거리의 군중을 끌어모을 만한 기이한 광경이었다. 낡은 옷에 초록 숄을 두르고, 한쪽으로 보기 흉하게 짜부라진 찢어진 밀짚모자를 쓴 카테리나 이바노브나는 정말로 제정신이 아니었다. 그녀는 기진맥진해 숨을 헐떡이고 있었다. 결핵으로 야윈 그녀의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 고통스러워 보였는데, 사실 폐병 환자는 집 안에 있을 때보다 햇빛 아래 바깥에 있을 때 늘 더 안 좋아 보이는 법이다. 그러나 그녀의 흥분은 가라앉지 않았고, 매 순간 그녀의 짜증은 더욱 격해졌다. 그녀는 아이들에게 달려들어 소리를 지르고, 어르고 달래고, 군중 앞에서 어떻게 춤추고 무엇을 노래해야 하는지 가르치고, 왜 이래야만 하는지 설명하기 시작했다가, 아이들이 알아듣지 못하자 절망에 사로잡혀 매질을 했다……. 그러다가 군중에게로 달려들곤 했다. 점잖게 차려입은 사람이 멈춰 서서 구경하는 걸 눈치채면, 즉시 그에게 호소하기를, '점잖은, 가히 귀족이라 할 만한 집안의' 이 아이들이 어떤 신세로 전락했는지 좀 보라는 것이었다. 군중 속에서 웃음소리나 비웃는 소리가 들리면, 그녀는 당장 그 조롱꾼들에게 달려들어 말다툼을 벌이기 시작했다. 어떤 이들은 웃었고, 어떤 이들은 고개를 절레절레 흔들었으나, 겁에 질린 아이들을 데리고 있는 미친 여자의 모습에 모두가 호기심을 느꼈다. 레베쟈트니코프가 말했던 프라이팬은 거기 없었는데, 적어도 라스콜니코프의 눈에는 보이지 않았다. 하지만 프라이팬을 두드리는 대신, 카테리나 이바노브나는 리다와 콜랴를 춤추게 하고 폴렌카에게 노래를 시킬 때면 야윈 두 손으로 박수를 치기 시작했다. 그녀 자신도 노래에 끼어들었으나, 무서운 기침 때문에 두 번째 음에서 그만 끊기고 말았고, 그 바람에 절망에 차서 악담을 퍼붓고 심지어 눈물까지 흘렸다. 무엇보다 그녀를 격분시킨 것은 콜랴와 리다가 울며 겁에 질려 있는 것이었다. 아이들에게 거리의 가수들이 차려입는 식으로 옷을 입혀 보려는 시도가 더러 있었다. 사내아이는 터키인처럼 보이게 하려고 붉고 흰 무언가로 만든 터번을 두르고 있었다. 리다에게는 변변한 의상이 없어서, 그저 붉은 뜨개 모자를, 아니 차라리 마르멜라도프의 것이었던 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 거기에는 부러진 흰 타조 깃털 조각이 장식으로 달려 있었으니, 그것은 카테리나 이바노브나의 할머니의 것으로 집안의 가보로 간직되어 온 것이었다. 폴렌카는 평상복 차림이었다. 그녀는 겁먹은 듯 어쩔 줄 모르며 어머니를 바라보고는, 눈물을 감추며 어머니 곁에 바싹 붙어 있었다. 그녀는 어머니의 상태를 어렴풋이 알아차렸고, 불안하게 주위를 두리번거렸다. 그녀는 거리와 군중이 끔찍하게 무서웠다. 소냐는 카테리나 이바노브나를 뒤따르며, 울면서 집으로 돌아가자고 애원했으나, 카테리나 이바노브나는 도무지 설득되지 않았다.\n\n\"그만둬, 소냐, 그만두라니까.\" 그녀가 빠르게 말하며 외쳤다, 숨을 헐떡이고 기침을 하면서. \"네가 무슨 소릴 하는지 모르는구나. 넌 꼭 어린애 같아! 전에도 말했잖아, 그 술주정뱅이 독일 여자한테는 다시 돌아가지 않겠다고. 아버지가 평생을 진실과 충성으로 봉직하다가, 가히 직무 중에 순직했다고 할 만한 훌륭한 사람이었는데도, 그 아이들이 거리에서 구걸하는 꼴을 모두가, 페테르부르크 전체가 다 보게 하겠어.\" (카테리나 이바노브나는 이미 이 황당한 이야기를 지어내어 철석같이 믿고 있었다.) \"그 못된 장군 놈이 그걸 보게 하겠어! 그리고 넌 어리석구나, 소냐. 우리가 뭘 먹고 살지? 말해 봐. 우린 너를 충분히 괴롭혔어, 더는 그렇게 살지 않겠어! 아, 로지온 로마노비치, 당신이군요?\" 그녀가 라스콜니코프를 보고는 그에게 달려들며 외쳤다. \"제발 이 어리석은 애한테 좀 설명해 주세요, 이보다 더 나은 방법은 없다고요! 손풍금쟁이들도 제 밥벌이는 하는데, 우리가 그들과는 다르다는 걸, 우리가 거지 신세로 전락한 점잖고 불행한 집안이라는 걸 사람들이 단번에 알아볼 거예요. 그리고 그 장군은 자리에서 쫓겨날 거예요, 두고 보세요! 우린 매일 그자의 창문 밑에서 공연을 할 거고, 만약 황제 폐하께서 마차로 지나가시면, 나는 무릎을 꿇고 아이들을 앞에 세워 폐하께 보이며 이렇게 말씀드릴 거예요. '저희를 지켜 주십시오, 아버지시여.' 그분은 아비 없는 자들의 아버지이시고, 자비로우시니, 우리를 보호해 주실 거예요, 두고 보세요, 그리고 그 못된 장군 놈은……. 리다, 똑바로 서!* 콜랴, 또 춤춰야지. 왜 또 칭얼대니? 또 칭얼대! 뭐가 무서워서, 이 바보야? 아이고, 이 애들을 대체 어쩌면 좋아요, 로지온 로마노비치? 이 애들이 얼마나 멍청한지 당신이 알기나 하면! 이런 애들을 데리고 뭘 어쩌란 말이에요?\"\n\n* tenez vous droite: (프랑스어) 똑바로 서라.\n그러고는 그녀 자신도 거의 울먹이면서——그래도 끊임없이 빠르게 쏟아지는 말은 멈추지 않았다——우는 아이들을 가리켰다. 라스콜니코프는 그녀를 달래어 집으로 돌아가게 하려고 애썼고, 심지어 그녀의 허영심을 자극하려는 속셈으로, 기숙학교의 교장이 되려는 사람이 손풍금쟁이처럼 거리를 떠돌아다니는 것은 어울리지 않는다고까지 말했다.\n\n\"기숙학교라니, 하-하-하! 그림의 떡이죠.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤고, 그 웃음은 기침으로 끝났다. \"아니에요, 로지온 로마노비치, 그 꿈은 끝났어요! 다들 우리를 버렸으니까요!…… 그리고 그 장군이라는 작자는……. 있잖아요, 로지온 로마노비치, 내가 그자에게 잉크병을 집어 던졌어요——마침 대기실에, 이름을 적는 그 서류 옆에 놓여 있었거든요. 내 이름을 적고, 그걸 그자에게 던지고는 달아났죠. 오, 그 악당들, 그 악당들! 하지만 그자들 얘긴 그만하죠, 이제 내가 직접 아이들을 먹여 살릴 거예요, 아무한테도 굽실거리지 않겠어요! 이 애는 우리 때문에 충분히 고생했어요!\" 그녀가 소냐를 가리켰다. \"폴렌카, 얼마나 모았니? 보여 줘! 뭐, 고작 두 닢이야! 오, 그 인색한 인간들! 우리한테 아무것도 안 주면서, 그저 뒤를 졸졸 쫓아다니며 혀나 날름거리지. 저런, 저 멍청이는 뭐가 우습다고 웃는 거야?\" (그녀가 군중 속의 한 남자를 가리켰다.) \"이게 다 여기 이 콜랴가 어찌나 멍청한지, 이 애 때문에 골치가 다 아파요. 뭘 원하니, 폴렌카? 프랑스어로 말해 봐, 나한테 프랑스어로 말해 봐.* 아니, 내가 가르쳐 줬잖니, 너 몇 마디 알잖아. 안 그러면 너희가 좋은 집안 출신에 잘 교육받은 아이들이라는 걸, 다른 손풍금쟁이들과는 전혀 다르다는 걸 어떻게 보여 주겠니? 우린 거리에서 인형극 따위를 하려는 게 아니라, 점잖은 노래를 부르려는 거니까……. 아, 그래……. 우리 뭘 부르지? 네가 자꾸 내 정신을 흩뜨리는구나, 하지만 우린……, 보세요, 우린 여기 서서, 로지온 로마노비치, 부를 노래를 찾아 돈을 벌려는 거예요, 콜랴가 춤출 수 있는 무언가를요……. 보시다시피 우리 공연은 죄다 즉흥이라서요……. 다 같이 의논하고 철저히 연습해야 해요, 그런 다음에 네프스키로 갈 거예요, 거긴 상류 사회 사람들이 훨씬 많으니까, 우린 단번에 눈에 띄겠죠. 리다는 '나의 작은 마을'밖에 몰라요, 오로지 '나의 작은 마을'뿐인데, 그건 누구나 부르는 노래죠. 우린 훨씬 더 점잖은 걸 불러야 해요……. 자, 뭐 생각난 거 있니, 폴렌카? 네가 엄마를 좀 도와주기만 한다면! 내 기억력이 완전히 가 버렸어, 안 그랬으면 뭔가 생각해 냈을 텐데. '경기병' 같은 건 정말 부를 수 없지. 아, 프랑스어로 부르자, '다섯 수,'* 내가 너한테 가르쳐 줬잖니, 가르쳐 줬어. 그게 프랑스어니까, 사람들이 너희가 좋은 집안 아이들이라는 걸 단번에 알아볼 거고, 그게 훨씬 더 가슴을 울리겠지……. '말버러는 전쟁터로 떠나네'를 불러도 되겠다, 그건 완전히 어린이 노래라서 귀족 집안마다 자장가로 부르는 거니까.\n* parlez-moi français: (프랑스어) 나에게 프랑스어로 말해 봐. Cinq sous: (프랑스어) 다섯 수(=수는 프랑스 동전).\n\n\"말버러는 전쟁터로 떠나네, 언제 돌아올지 알 수 없네……\" 그녀가 노래를 부르기 시작했다. \"하지만 안 되겠어, '다섯 수'를 부르는 게 낫겠다. 자, 콜랴, 두 손을 허리에 얹고, 어서, 그리고 리다, 너는 계속 반대쪽으로 돌고, 폴렌카랑 나는 노래하며 손뼉을 칠 테니!\n\n\"다섯 수, 다섯 수, 우리 살림 차리려고.\"\n\n(기침-기침-기침!) \"옷매무새 똑바로 해, 폴렌카, 어깨에서 흘러내렸잖니.\" 그녀가 기침으로 숨을 헐떡이며 일렀다. \"지금은 특히 얌전하고 점잖게 굴어야 해, 너희가 좋은 집안 아이들이라는 걸 모두가 알 수 있게 말이야. 그때 내가 말했지, 몸통 부분을 더 길게 재단하고 폭을 두 배로 해야 한다고. 그건 네 잘못이야, 소냐, 더 짧게 만들라고 했잖니, 그래서 이제 봐, 애가 옷 때문에 아주 볼품없어졌잖아……. 아니, 너희 또 다 우는구나! 무슨 일이야, 이 바보들아? 자, 콜랴, 시작해. 어서, 어서! 오, 정말 못 견디겠는 애로구나!\n\n\"다섯 수, 다섯 수.\n\n\"또 경찰이잖아! 뭘 원하는 거예요?\"\n\n정말로 경찰관 하나가 군중을 헤치며 다가오고 있었다. 그런데 바로 그때 사복 차림에 외투를 걸친 한 신사가——목에 훈장을 단(이것은 카테리나 이바노브나를 기쁘게 했고 경찰관에게도 효과를 발휘했다) 쉰 살쯤 된 점잖아 보이는 관리가——다가오더니, 말없이 그녀에게 초록색 삼 루블짜리 지폐를 건넸다. 그의 얼굴에는 진심 어린 동정의 빛이 어려 있었다. 카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 받아 들고는, 정중하다 못해 의례적이기까지 한 인사를 했다.\n\n\"감사합니다, 존경하는 어르신.\" 그녀가 거만하게 입을 열었다. \"저희로 하여금 이리하게 만든 사정인즉슨(돈을 받으렴, 폴렌카, 봐라, 곤경에 처한 가련한 양가의 부인을 기꺼이 도우려는 너그럽고 고결한 분들이 계시잖니). 보십시오, 존경하는 어르신, 이 좋은 집안의——가히 귀족 집안과 연이 닿았다고도 할 수 있는——고아들을요, 그런데 그 못된 장군 놈은 들꿩 요리나 처먹고 앉아서는……, 내가 방해한다고 발을 쾅쾅 굴렀답니다. '각하,' 내가 말했죠, '이 고아들을 지켜 주십시오, 각하께서는 제 고인이 된 남편 세묜 자하로비치를 아셨으니까요, 그리고 그이가 세상을 떠난 바로 그날, 더없이 비열한 악당이 그이의 외동딸을 모함했습니다.'…… 또 저 경찰이잖아! 저를 지켜 주세요.\" 그녀가 그 관리를 향해 외쳤다. \"왜 저 경찰이 자꾸 제게로 다가오는 거죠? 방금 그런 자한테서 겨우 도망쳐 왔는데. 뭘 원하는 거예요, 이 바보야?\"\n\n\"거리에서 이러시면 안 됩니다. 소란을 피우시면 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"소란을 피우는 건 당신이잖아. 이건 내가 손풍금을 돌리는 거랑 똑같은 거라고. 당신이 무슨 상관이야?\"\n\n\"손풍금에는 허가증을 받아야 하는데, 당신은 그게 없잖소, 게다가 이런 식으로 군중을 끌어모으고 있고. 어디 사시오?\"\n\n\"뭐, 허가증이라고?\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 울부짖었다. \"내가 오늘 남편을 묻었어. 허가증이 무슨 소용이야?\"\n\n\"진정하세요, 부인, 진정하시고요.\" 그 관리가 입을 열었다. \"자, 같이 가시죠, 제가 모셔다드리겠습니다……. 이 군중 속은 부인이 계실 곳이 아니에요. 편찮으시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"존경하는 어르신, 존경하는 어르신, 모르시는군요.\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 비명을 질렀다. \"우린 네프스키로 갈 거예요……. 소냐, 소냐! 그앤 어디 있지? 그애도 울고 있네! 너희 다 왜들 그래? 콜랴, 리다, 어디 가니?\" 그녀가 갑자기 겁에 질려 외쳤다. \"오, 어리석은 애들! 콜랴, 리다, 저 애들이 어디로 가는 거야?……\"\n\n콜랴와 리다는 군중과 어머니의 미친 짓거리에 넋이 나갈 만큼 겁에 질려, 갑자기 서로 손을 맞잡고는, 자기들을 어딘가로 데려가려는 경찰관을 보고 달아나 버렸다. 가엾은 카테리나 이바노브나는 울부짖으며 그들 뒤를 쫓았다. 울면서 숨을 헐떡이며 달리는 그녀의 모습은 가련하고도 꼴사나운 광경이었다. 소냐와 폴렌카가 그들을 뒤쫓아 달려갔다.\n\n\"데려와, 데려오라니까, 소냐! 오, 어리석고 배은망덕한 애들!…… 폴렌카! 저 애들을 붙잡아……. 다 너희를 위해서 내가…….\"\n\n그녀는 달리다가 발이 걸려 넘어졌다.\n\n\"다치셨어, 피가 나! 오, 어떡해!\" 소냐가 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히며 외쳤다.\n\n모두가 달려와 주위에 몰려들었다. 라스콜니코프와 레베쟈트니코프가 맨 먼저 그녀 곁에 이르렀고, 그 관리도 서둘러 달려왔으며, 그 뒤로 경찰관이 \"성가시게 됐군!\" 하고 짜증스러운 몸짓으로 중얼거리며 다가왔으니, 그는 이 일이 골치 아픈 사건이 되리라는 것을 직감했던 것이다.\n\n\"비키시오! 비켜요!\" 그가 앞으로 밀려드는 군중을 향해 말했다.\n\n\"이 여자 죽어 가요.\" 누군가가 외쳤다.\n\n\"정신이 나갔어.\" 다른 누군가가 말했다.\n\n\"주여, 저희를 불쌍히 여기소서.\" 한 여자가 성호를 그으며 말했다. \"그 어린 계집애랑 사내애는 잡았답니까? 데려오고 있네, 큰애가 붙들었어……. 아이고, 저 말썽꾸러기들!\"\n\n카테리나 이바노브나를 찬찬히 살펴보고 나서야, 사람들은 그녀가 소냐의 생각처럼 돌에 부딪혀 다친 것이 아니라, 포도(鋪道)를 붉게 물들인 피가 그녀의 가슴에서 나온 것임을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"전에도 저런 걸 본 적이 있어요.\" 그 관리가 라스콜니코프와 레베쟈트니코프에게 중얼거렸다. \"저건 폐병이오. 피가 솟구쳐서 환자를 질식시키는 거죠. 얼마 전에 내 친척한테서도 똑같은 걸 봤습니다……, 거의 한 파인트나 되는 피가, 순식간에……. 하지만 어쩌겠소? 이 여자는 죽어 가고 있어요.\"\n\n\"이쪽으로, 이쪽으로, 제 방으로요!\" 소냐가 애원했다. \"제가 여기 살아요!…… 보세요, 저 집, 여기서 두 번째예요……. 저한테로 데려와 주세요, 어서요.\" 그녀가 이 사람 저 사람에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"의사를 불러 주세요! 오, 어떡해!\"\n\n그 관리의 노력 덕분에 이 방안이 채택되었고, 경찰관까지도 카테리나 이바노브나를 옮기는 일을 거들었다. 그녀는 거의 의식을 잃은 채 소냐의 방으로 옮겨져 침대에 뉘었다. 피는 여전히 흐르고 있었으나, 그녀는 정신이 드는 듯했다. 라스콜니코프, 레베쟈트니코프, 그리고 그 관리가 소냐와 함께 방으로 들어갔고, 경찰관도 그 뒤를 따랐는데, 그는 먼저 문간까지 따라온 군중을 몰아냈다. 폴렌카가 떨며 울고 있는 콜랴와 리다를 데리고 들어왔다. 카페르나우모프네 방에서도 여러 사람이 들어왔으니, 집주인은 절름발이에 애꾸눈인 기이한 행색의 남자로, 구레나룻과 솔처럼 빳빳이 곤두선 머리칼을 하고 있었고, 그의 아내는 영원히 겁에 질린 듯한 표정의 여자였으며, 입을 헤벌린 채 신기해하는 얼굴의 아이들도 여럿 따라 들어왔다. 이들 가운데 스비드리가일로프가 불쑥 모습을 드러냈다. 라스콜니코프는 그가 어디서 나타났는지도 모르고 군중 속에서도 그를 알아채지 못했던 터라, 놀라서 그를 바라보았다. 의사와 사제 얘기가 오갔다. 그 관리는 라스콜니코프에게, 이제 의사를 부르기엔 너무 늦은 것 같다고 속삭였으나, 그래도 사람을 보내 부르라고 일렀다. 카페르나우모프가 직접 달려갔다.\n\n그러는 사이 카테리나 이바노브나는 숨을 돌렸다. 출혈이 한동안 멎었다. 그녀는 병색이 완연하면서도 골똘하고 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈으로, 창백하게 떨며 손수건으로 이마의 땀을 닦고 있는 소냐를 바라보았다. 마침내 그녀가 일으켜 달라고 청했다. 사람들이 양쪽에서 그녀를 부축하여 침대에 일으켜 앉혔다.\n\n\"아이들은 어디 있니?\" 그녀가 희미한 목소리로 말했다. \"네가 데려왔구나, 폴렌카? 오, 이 바보들! 왜 도망쳤어……. 어!\"\n\n또다시 그녀의 메마른 입술이 피로 뒤덮였다. 그녀는 눈을 굴리며 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"그래, 네가 이렇게 살고 있었구나, 소냐! 네 방에 한 번도 와 본 적이 없었어.\"\n\n그녀는 고통스러운 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"우리가 너를 망쳐 놓았어, 소냐. 폴렌카, 리다, 콜랴, 이리 오너라! 자, 여기 있다, 소냐, 다 받아 다오! 이 애들을 너한테 넘기마, 나는 이제 됐어! 무도회는 끝났어.\" (기침!) \"날 좀 눕혀 다오, 편히 죽게 해 줘.\"\n\n사람들이 그녀를 다시 베개에 뉘었다.\n\n\"뭐, 사제라고? 난 필요 없어. 너희한테 남는 루블도 없잖니. 나한테 무슨 죄가 있다고. 그런 거 없이도 하느님은 나를 용서하셔야 해. 그분은 내가 얼마나 고통받았는지 아시니까……. 만약 그분이 나를 용서하지 않으신대도, 난 상관없어!\"\n\n그녀는 점점 더 불안한 헛소리 속으로 빠져들었다. 이따금 몸을 떨고, 눈을 이리저리 굴리고, 잠깐씩 모두를 알아보았으나, 곧바로 다시 헛소리에 빠지곤 했다. 그녀의 숨소리는 거칠고 힘겨웠고, 목구멍에서는 가르랑거리는 소리 같은 것이 났다.\n\n\"내가 그자에게 말했죠, 각하.\" 그녀가 한 마디 한 마디 헐떡이며 내뱉었다. \"그 아말리야 루드비고브나가, 아! 리다, 콜랴, 두 손을 허리에, 어서! 미끄러지듯, 미끄러지듯! 파드바스크!* 발뒤꿈치로 박자를 맞추고, 우아한 아이가 되어야지!\n* Glissez, glissez! pas de basque!: (프랑스어) 미끄러지듯, 미끄러지듯! 파드바스크(발레의 한 스텝).\n\n\"그대는 다이아몬드와 진주를 가졌네*\n\n* Du hast Diamanten und Perlen: (독일어) 그대는 다이아몬드와 진주를 가졌네. 하이네의 시.\n\n\"다음은 뭐지? 이게 부를 노래야.\n\n\"그대는 더없이 아름다운 두 눈을 가졌네, 아가씨, 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라는가?*\n\n* Du hast die schönsten Augen Mädchen, was willst du mehr?: (독일어) 그대는 더없이 아름다운 두 눈을 가졌네, 아가씨, 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라는가?\n\"무슨 생각이람! 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라는가? 저 바보가 별 희한한 소리를 다 지어내네! 아, 그래!\n\n\"한낮의 무더위 속, 다게스탄 골짜기에서.\n\"아, 내가 이 노래를 얼마나 사랑했던지! 미치도록 그 노래를 사랑했단다, 폴렌카! 네 아버지가, 알겠니, 우리가 약혼했을 때 이 노래를 부르곤 했지……. 오, 그 시절! 오, 이게 바로 우리가 부를 노래야! 어떻게 되더라? 잊어버렸네. 좀 일러 줘! 어떻게 됐지?\"\n\n그녀는 격렬하게 흥분하여 일어나 앉으려 애썼다. 마침내, 무섭게 쉬고 갈라진 목소리로, 한 마디 한 마디마다 비명을 지르고 숨을 헐떡이며, 점점 커져 가는 공포의 표정을 띤 채 그녀가 노래를 부르기 시작했다.\n\n\"한낮의 무더위 속!…… 골짜기에서!…… 다게스탄의!…… 가슴에 박힌 납덩이를 안고서!……\"\n\"각하!\" 그녀가 갑자기 가슴이 미어지는 비명과 함께 눈물을 쏟으며 울부짖었다. \"이 고아들을 지켜 주십시오! 각하께서는 이 아이들 아버지의 손님이셨으니까요……, 가히 귀족이라 할 만한…….\" 그녀는 흠칫하며 의식을 되찾았고, 일종의 공포에 차서 모두를 둘러보았으나, 곧 소냐를 알아보았다.\n\n\"소냐, 소냐!\" 그녀가 거기서 소냐를 발견한 것이 놀랍다는 듯, 부드럽고 다정하게 또렷이 말했다. \"소냐, 얘야, 너도 여기 있었니?\"\n\n사람들이 그녀를 다시 일으켜 세웠다.\n\n\"됐어! 다 끝났어! 잘 있어, 가엾은 것! 난 끝장이야! 난 부서져 버렸어!\" 그녀가 원한에 사무친 절망으로 외쳤고, 그녀의 머리가 베개 위로 무겁게 툭 떨어졌다.\n\n그녀는 다시 의식을 잃었으나, 이번에는 오래가지 않았다. 창백하고 누렇게 야윈 그녀의 얼굴이 뒤로 젖혀지고, 입이 벌어지고, 다리가 경련하듯 움찔하더니, 그녀는 깊디깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 숨을 거두었다.\n\n소냐가 그녀에게 엎드려 두 팔로 그녀를 끌어안고는, 죽은 여자의 야윈 가슴에 머리를 묻은 채 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 폴렌카는 어머니의 발치에 몸을 던져 그 발에 입 맞추며 격렬하게 울었다. 콜랴와 리다는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알지 못했지만, 무언가 끔찍한 일이라는 느낌은 들었던지, 서로의 작은 어깨에 손을 얹고 서로를 똑바로 바라보다가, 둘이 동시에 입을 벌리고 비명을 지르기 시작했다. 둘 다 여전히 분장한 차림 그대로였는데, 하나는 터번을 두르고, 다른 하나는 타조 깃털 달린 모자를 쓰고 있었다.\n\n그런데 어쩌다 그 '표창장'이 카테리나 이바노브나 곁의 침대 위에 놓이게 된 것일까? 그것은 베개 옆에 놓여 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그것을 보았다.\n\n그는 창가로 걸어갔다. 레베쟈트니코프가 그에게로 깡충 다가왔다.\n\n\"죽었어요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"로지온 로마노비치, 자네와 두어 마디 나눠야겠네.\" 스비드리가일로프가 그들에게 다가오며 말했다.\n\n레베쟈트니코프는 곧바로 자리를 비켜 주고는 점잖게 물러났다. 스비드리가일로프는 라스콜니코프를 좀 더 멀리 끌고 갔다.\n\n\"내가 모든 준비를, 장례며 그런 것들을 도맡겠네. 알다시피 이건 돈 문제이고, 전에 말했듯이 나는 남아도는 돈이 많으니까. 저 어린 두 아이와 폴렌카는 어디 괜찮은 고아원에 넣고, 성년이 될 때 각자에게 천오백 루블씩 지급되도록 정해 두겠네, 소피야 세묘노브나가 그 애들 걱정은 하지 않아도 되게 말이야. 그리고 그녀도 이 진창에서 끌어내 주겠어, 착한 아가씨잖나, 안 그런가? 그러니 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 전하게, 내가 그녀의 만 루블을 이렇게 쓰고 있다고.\"\n\n\"무슨 속셈으로 그런 선행을 베푸는 겁니까?\" 라스콜니코프가 물었다.\n\n\"아! 의심 많은 양반이로군!\" 스비드리가일로프가 웃었다. \"내가 그 돈은 필요 없다고 말하지 않았나. 그저 인정에서 우러나 하는 일이라고는 인정하지 못하겠나? 저 여자는 '이' 같은 게 아니었잖나\"(그가 죽은 여자가 누워 있는 구석을 가리켰다), \"안 그런가, 무슨 늙은 전당포 노파처럼 말이야? 자, 자네도 동의할 걸세, 루진 같은 자는 계속 살아남아 못된 짓을 저질러도 되고, 저 여자는 죽어야 한단 말인가? 그리고 내가 저 애들을 돕지 않으면, 폴렌카도 똑같은 길을 가게 될 거야.\"\n\n그는 일종의 유쾌하게 눈을 찡긋하는 듯한 능청스러움을 띠고 이 말을 하면서, 라스콜니코프에게서 눈을 떼지 않았는데, 라스콜니코프는 자기가 소냐에게 했던 바로 그 말을 듣고는 창백해지고 싸늘해졌다. 그는 재빨리 뒷걸음질 치며 미친 듯한 눈으로 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아는 거요?\" 그가 숨도 제대로 못 쉬며 속삭였다.\n\n\"아니, 나는 여기, 벽 너머에 레슬리히 부인 댁에 세 들어 살고 있다네. 여기는 카페르나우모프네 집이고, 저기엔 레슬리히 부인이 사는데, 나의 오랜 절친한 친구지. 나는 이웃이야.\"\n\n\"당신이?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\" 스비드리가일로프가 웃음으로 몸을 흔들며 말을 이었다. \"맹세코 정말일세, 친애하는 로지온 로마노비치, 자네는 나에게 엄청난 흥미를 불러일으켰어. 우리가 친구가 되리라고 내가 말하지 않았나, 내가 그걸 예언했지. 자, 이렇게 됐군. 그리고 자네는 내가 얼마나 융통성 있는 사람인지 알게 될 걸세. 자네는 나와 잘 지낼 수 있다는 걸 알게 될 거야!\"\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n라스콜니코프에게 기묘한 시기가 시작되었다. 마치 안개가 그에게 내려앉아 그를 빠져나갈 길 없는 음울한 고독 속으로 감싸 버린 것 같았다. 오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤 그 시기를 떠올리면서, 그는 자신의 정신이 때때로 흐려져 있었고, 그 상태가 간헐적으로 최후의 파국에 이르기까지 계속되었으리라고 믿었다. 그는 그때 자신이 많은 것들에 대해, 이를테면 어떤 사건들이 일어난 날짜 같은 것에 대해 착각하고 있었다고 확신했다. 어쨌든 나중에 자신의 기억들을 끼워 맞추려 애쓰면서, 그는 다른 사람들이 해 준 이야기를 통해 자기 자신에 관해 많은 것을 알게 되었다. 그는 여러 사건들을 뒤섞어 놓았고, 어떤 일들을 오직 자신의 상상 속에서만 존재하던 정황 탓으로 설명하곤 했다. 때때로 그는 병적인 불안의 고통에 사로잡혔는데, 그 불안은 이따금 공황 상태에까지 이르렀다. 그러나 그는 또한 완전한 무감각의 순간들, 시간들, 어쩌면 며칠을 통째로 기억하고 있었다. 그 무감각은 이전의 공포에 대한 반작용으로 그에게 찾아왔으며, 죽어 가는 자들에게서 이따금 보이는 비정상적인 둔감함에 견줄 만한 것이었다. 그는 그 후자의 단계에서 자신의 처지를 온전하고 분명하게 이해하는 일로부터 달아나려 애쓰는 것 같았다. 당장 고려해야 할 어떤 본질적인 사실들이 그에게는 유난히 성가셨다. 어떤 근심들로부터 자유로울 수 있다면 그는 얼마나 기뻤을까. 그 근심들을 등한시한다면 완전하고 피할 수 없는 파멸의 위협을 받게 될 텐데도 말이다.\n\n그는 특히 스비드리가일로프 때문에 골머리를 앓았으니, 끊임없이 스비드리가일로프를 생각하고 있었다고 해도 좋을 정도였다. 카테리나 이바노브나가 죽던 순간 소냐의 방에서 스비드리가일로프가 너무도 위협적이고 분명하게 내뱉었던 말들이 있은 뒤로, 그의 정신의 정상적인 작동은 무너져 버린 듯했다. 그러나 이 새로운 사실이 그에게 극도의 불안을 안겨 주었음에도, 라스콜니코프는 그것을 서둘러 해명하려 들지 않았다. 때때로 그는 도시의 한적하고 외진 곳, 어느 초라한 식당에 홀로 앉아 깊은 생각에 잠겨, 자신이 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지조차 거의 알지 못한 채로 있다가, 문득 스비드리가일로프를 떠올렸다. 그는 자신이 즉시 그자와 어떤 양해에 도달해, 가능한 한 합의를 보아야 한다는 것을 갑작스레, 분명하게, 그리고 경악과 함께 깨달았다. 어느 날 성문 밖을 거닐면서, 그는 정말로 자신이 거기서 만나기로 약속을 했고, 스비드리가일로프를 기다리고 있는 것이라고 상상했다. 또 한번은 어느 덤불 아래 땅바닥에 누운 채 동트기 전에 깨어났는데, 처음에는 자신이 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 카테리나 이바노브나가 죽은 뒤 이삼 일 동안, 그는 두세 차례 소냐의 하숙집에서 스비드리가일로프와 마주쳤다. 그는 별 목적도 없이 잠깐씩 그곳에 갔던 것이다. 두 사람은 몇 마디 말을 주고받았을 뿐, 그 핵심적인 화제에 대해서는 전혀 언급하지 않았으니, 마치 한동안 그것에 대해 말하지 않기로 암묵적으로 합의라도 한 듯했다.\n\n카테리나 이바노브나의 시신은 아직 관 속에 누워 있었고, 스비드리가일로프는 장례 준비에 분주했다. 소냐 역시 무척 바빴다. 마지막으로 만났을 때 스비드리가일로프는 라스콜니코프에게, 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이들을 위해 어떤 조처를, 그것도 아주 만족스러운 조처를 취해 두었다고 알려 주었다. 그가 어떤 연줄을 통해 어떤 인사들을 손에 넣는 데 성공했고, 그들의 도움으로 세 고아를 즉시 아주 적당한 시설에 들여보낼 수 있게 되었으며, 그가 그 아이들 앞으로 정해 놓은 돈이 큰 도움이 되었으니, 재산이 좀 있는 고아를 들이는 것이 빈털터리 고아보다 훨씬 수월하기 때문이라는 것이었다. 그는 소냐에 관해서도 무언가를 말했고, 하루 이틀 안에 라스콜니코프를 보러 직접 찾아오겠다고 약속하면서, \"그와 의논하고 싶은 것이 있다, 두 사람이 꼭 이야기해야 할 일들이 있다……\"고 언급했다.\n\n이 대화는 계단의 통로에서 이루어졌다. 스비드리가일로프는 라스콜니코프를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤 불쑥 목소리를 낮추어 물었다. \"한데 어찌 된 일입니까, 로지온 로마노비치, 당신 같지가 않군요? 보고 듣고는 있지만, 도무지 알아듣지 못하는 것 같소. 기운을 내요! 우리 차차 이야기를 나눕시다. 다만 유감스러운 건, 내 일이며 남의 일이며 할 일이 너무 많다는 거요. 아, 로지온 로마노비치.\" 그가 갑자기 덧붙였다. \"모든 인간에게 필요한 건 신선한 공기요, 신선한 공기……, 무엇보다도 그게 필요하지요!\"\n\n그는 한쪽으로 비켜서서 계단을 올라오던 사제와 복사에게 길을 내주었다. 그들은 위령 미사를 드리러 온 것이었다. 스비드리가일로프의 지시로 미사는 하루에 두 번씩 어김없이 거행되었다. 스비드리가일로프는 제 갈 길을 갔다. 라스콜니코프는 잠시 가만히 서서 생각하다가, 사제를 따라 소냐의 방으로 들어갔다. 그는 문가에 섰다. 사람들은 조용히, 느릿하고 애처롭게 미사를 노래하기 시작했다. 어린 시절부터 죽음에 대한 생각과 죽음의 존재는 그에게 무언가 짓누르는 듯하고 신비롭게 끔찍한 것이었다. 게다가 그가 위령 미사를 들어 본 지도 오래되었다. 또한 여기에는 그 밖에도 무언가, 너무도 끔찍하고 마음을 어지럽히는 것이 있었다. 그는 아이들을 바라보았다. 아이들은 모두 관 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 있었고, 폴렌카는 울고 있었다. 그 뒤에서 소냐가 기도를 드리며, 나직이, 말하자면 수줍은 듯이 울고 있었다.\n\n'지난 이틀 동안 그녀는 내게 한 마디도 하지 않았고, 나를 한 번 쳐다보지도 않았어.' 라스콜니코프는 문득 생각했다. 햇살이 방 안에 환했고, 향연이 구름처럼 피어올랐다. 사제가 읽었다. \"오, 주여, 안식을 주소서…….\" 라스콜니코프는 미사가 끝날 때까지 내내 머물렀다. 사람들에게 축복을 내리고 작별을 고하면서, 사제는 묘한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다. 미사가 끝난 뒤, 라스콜니코프는 소냐에게 다가갔다. 그녀는 그의 두 손을 잡더니 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댔다. 이 작고 다정한 몸짓이 라스콜니코프를 당혹스럽게 했다. 거기에 혐오의 흔적도, 역겨움의 흔적도, 그녀의 손에 떨림조차 없다는 것이 그에게는 이상하게 여겨졌다. 그것은 자기희생의 극한이었다, 적어도 그는 그렇게 해석했다.\n\n소냐는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 라스콜니코프는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 밖으로 나왔다. 그는 몹시 비참한 기분이었다. 어딘가 고독한 곳으로 달아날 수만 있다면, 설령 그곳에서 평생을 보내야 한다 해도 자신을 운이 좋다고 여겼을 것이다. 그러나 비록 그는 요즈음 거의 언제나 혼자였지만, 한 번도 혼자라고 느낄 수 없었다. 때때로 그는 도시를 벗어나 한길로 나갔고, 한번은 작은 숲에까지 이른 적도 있었지만, 그 장소가 외지면 외질수록 그는 자기 곁에 어떤 불안한 존재가 있다는 것을 더욱 의식하는 것 같았다. 그것은 그를 두렵게 하지는 않았지만 몹시 짜증나게 했고, 그래서 그는 서둘러 도시로 돌아와, 군중 속에 섞이고, 음식점과 선술집으로 들어가고, 번잡한 큰길을 걸었다. 거기서 그는 더 마음이 편했고 오히려 더 고독했다. 어느 날 황혼 녘에 그는 한 시간 동안 선술집에 앉아 노래를 들었는데, 그것을 정말로 즐겼던 것을 그는 기억했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 또다시 그 똑같은 불안을 불쑥 느꼈으니, 마치 양심이 그를 찌르는 것 같았다. '여기 앉아 노래나 듣고 있다니, 이게 내가 해야 할 일인가?' 그는 생각했다. 하지만 그는 그것이 자기 불안의 유일한 원인이 아니라는 것을 곧바로 느꼈다. 무언가 당장 결정을 요하는 것이 있었지만, 그것은 그가 분명히 이해하거나 말로 옮길 수 없는 무엇이었다. 그것은 가망 없는 뒤엉킴이었다. '아니, 차라리 다시 싸움이 낫지! 차라리 포르피리를 다시…… 아니면 스비드리가일로프를……. 차라리 또 어떤 도전이 낫지…… 어떤 공격이. 그래, 그래!' 그는 생각했다. 그는 선술집을 나와 거의 뛰다시피 달아났다. 두냐와 어머니 생각이 갑자기 그를 거의 공황 상태로 몰아넣었다. 그날 밤 그는 크레스톱스키 섬의 어느 덤불 사이에서 아침이 되기 전에 깨어났는데, 온몸이 열로 부들부들 떨리고 있었다. 그는 집으로 걸어갔고, 도착했을 때는 이른 아침이었다. 몇 시간을 자고 나자 열이 가셨지만, 그가 늦게, 오후 두 시에야 깨어났다.\n\n그는 카테리나 이바노브나의 장례가 그날로 정해져 있었음을 떠올렸고, 자신이 거기 참석하지 않은 것이 다행스러웠다. 나스타시야가 그에게 음식을 좀 가져다주었다. 그는 식욕을 느끼며, 거의 게걸스럽게 먹고 마셨다. 머리가 한결 맑아졌고, 지난 사흘 동안보다 마음이 차분해졌다. 그는 이전에 자신을 덮쳤던 공황의 발작에 대해 지나가는 의아함마저 느꼈다.\n\n문이 열리고 라주미힌이 들어왔다.\n\n\"아, 먹고 있군, 그럼 아프진 않다는 거지.\" 라주미힌이 말했다. 그는 의자를 끌어다 탁자 맞은편의 라스콜니코프 앞에 앉았다.\n\n그는 심란해하면서도 그것을 감추려 하지 않았다. 그는 분명한 짜증을 담아, 그러나 서두르거나 목소리를 높이지 않고 말했다. 마치 어떤 특별한, 굳게 정해진 결심을 품은 듯한 모습이었다.\n\n\"잘 들어.\" 그가 단호하게 입을 열었다. \"나로 말할 것 같으면, 너희 모두 지옥에나 가 버리라지, 하지만 내가 본 바로는, 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수가 없다는 게 분명해. 제발 내가 너한테 뭘 캐물으러 왔다고 생각진 마. 알고 싶지도 않으니까, 빌어먹을! 네가 비밀을 털어놓기 시작하면, 장담컨대 나는 듣고 있지도 않고 욕설을 퍼부으며 나가 버릴 거야. 나는 그저 단 한 가지, 네가 정말로 미친 게 사실인지 확인하러 왔을 뿐이야. 네가 미쳤거나 거의 미친 거나 다름없다는 믿음이 떠돌고 있거든. 솔직히 나도 그렇게 생각하는 쪽으로 기울어 있었어, 너의 그 멍청하고 역겹고 도무지 설명할 수 없는 행동들로 미루어 보나, 최근에 네가 어머니와 누이를 대한 태도로 보나 말이야. 괴물이나 미치광이가 아니고서야 그분들을 너처럼 대할 수는 없으니까, 그러니 너는 미친 게 틀림없어.\"\n\n\"그분들을 마지막으로 본 게 언제야?\"\n\n\"방금 전에. 너는 그 후로 그분들을 못 봤다는 거야? 도대체 너 자신을 어떻게 하고 다닌 거야? 제발 말 좀 해 봐. 나는 벌써 너한테 세 번이나 왔었어. 어머니는 어제부터 심하게 앓아누우셨어. 너한테 오겠다고 마음을 굳히셨지. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 말리려 했지만, 한 마디도 들으려 하지 않으셨어. '그애가 아프다면, 정신이 이상해진 거라면, 어미 말고 누가 그애를 돌볼 수 있겠니?' 하고 말씀하셨지. 우리는 다 함께 여기로 왔어. 그분 혼자 그 먼 길을 오시게 둘 수는 없었으니까. 우리는 거듭 진정하시라고 빌었어. 우리가 들어왔을 때 너는 여기 없었지. 그분은 앉으셔서 십 분쯤 머무르셨고, 그동안 우리는 말없이 서서 기다렸어. 그분은 일어서며 말씀하셨어. '그애가 나갔다면, 그러니까 멀쩡한데 제 어미를 잊었다면, 어미가 그애 집 문 앞에 서서 다정함을 구걸하는 건 모욕적이고 꼴사나운 일이지.' 그분은 집으로 돌아가 자리에 누우셨고, 지금은 열에 들떠 계셔. '알겠다,' 그분이 말씀하셨어. '그애는 제 여자한테 쓸 시간은 있구나.' 제 여자라는 건 소피야 세묘노브나를 두고 하시는 말씀이야, 네 약혼녀든 정부든, 나는 모르겠지만. 나는 곧장 소피야 세묘노브나한테 갔어, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알고 싶었으니까. 둘러보니 관이 있고, 아이들이 울고 있고, 소피야 세묘노브나가 아이들한테 상복을 입혀 보고 있더군. 네 모습은 보이지 않았어. 나는 사과하고 나와서 아브도티야 로마노브나한테 보고했지. 그러니 그건 다 헛소리고, 너한테 여자 같은 건 없는 거야. 가장 그럴듯한 건 네가 미쳤다는 거지. 그런데 여기 너는 앉아서, 사흘이나 한 입도 못 먹은 사람처럼 삶은 쇠고기를 게걸스레 먹고 있어. 하기야 그것만 가지고 따지자면 미치광이도 먹긴 하지, 하지만 네가 아직 나한테 한 마디도 하지 않았는데도…… 너는 미치지 않았어! 그건 내가 맹세할 수 있어! 무엇보다도, 너는 미치지 않았어! 그러니 너희 모두 지옥에나 가 버려, 거기엔 무슨 수수께끼가, 무슨 비밀이 있는 거고, 나는 너희 비밀 따위로 골머리를 썩일 생각이 없으니까. 그래서 나는 그저 너한테 욕이나 퍼부으러 온 거야.\" 그는 일어서며 말을 맺었다. \"속이 좀 후련해지라고. 그리고 이제 어떻게 해야 할지 알겠어.\"\n\n\"이제 어떻게 할 작정인데?\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 할 작정이든 그게 너랑 무슨 상관이야?\"\n\n\"술판을 벌이러 가겠지.\"\n\n\"어떻게…… 어떻게 알았어?\"\n\n\"뭐, 빤하잖아.\"\n\n라주미힌은 잠시 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"너는 언제나 무척 이성적인 사람이었어, 한 번도 미친 적이 없었지, 결코.\" 그가 갑자기 열을 띠며 말했다. \"네 말이 맞아. 나는 술을 마실 거야. 잘 있어!\"\n\n그러고는 나가려고 몸을 움직였다.\n\n\"내가 누이와 이야기를 나눴어, 그저께였던 것 같은데, 너에 대해서 말이야, 라주미힌.\"\n\n\"나에 대해서! 하지만…… 그저께 네가 누이를 어디서 봤다는 거야?\" 라주미힌은 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 심지어 얼굴이 약간 창백해졌다.\n\n그의 심장이 느리고 격렬하게 뛰고 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"누이가 혼자 여기 와서, 저기 앉아 나와 이야기를 했어.\"\n\n\"누이가 그랬다고!\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\"누이한테 뭐라고 했어…… 그러니까, 나에 대해서 말이야?\"\n\n\"네가 아주 선량하고, 정직하고, 부지런한 사람이라고 했어. 네가 누이를 사랑한다고는 말하지 않았어, 그건 누이 자신이 알고 있으니까.\"\n\n\"누이가 자기 자신이 그걸 안다고?\"\n\n\"뭐, 빤한 일이지. 내가 어디로 가든, 나한테 무슨 일이 일어나든, 너는 남아서 그분들을 돌볼 거야. 나는, 말하자면, 그분들을 네 손에 맡기는 거야, 라주미힌. 내가 이렇게 말하는 건, 네가 누이를 얼마나 사랑하는지 잘 알고, 네 마음의 순수함을 확신하기 때문이야. 누이 역시 너를 사랑할 수 있고, 어쩌면 이미 사랑하고 있을지도 모른다는 걸 나는 알아. 자, 이제 네가 스스로 결정해, 네가 제일 잘 알 테니, 네가 술판을 벌이러 가야 할지 말지 말이야.\"\n\n\"로쟈! 너 봐…… 그러니까……. 아, 빌어먹을! 그런데 너는 어디로 갈 작정이야? 물론, 그게 다 비밀이라면, 상관없어……. 하지만 나는…… 나는 그 비밀을 알아낼 거야…… 그리고 그게 틀림없이 무슨 우스꽝스러운 헛소리이고 네가 다 지어낸 거라고 확신해. 어쨌든 너는 굉장한 녀석이야, 굉장한 녀석!……\"\n\n\"내가 덧붙이고 싶었던 게 바로 그거야, 네가 가로막았을 뿐이지, 그 비밀들을 알아내지 않겠다는 건 네 아주 훌륭한 결정이라고 말이야. 시간에 맡겨 둬, 그 일로 애태우지 마. 때가 되면 너는 모든 걸 알게 될 거야, 마땅히 알아야 할 때가 되면. 어제 어떤 사람이 나한테, 인간에게 필요한 건 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기라고 말하더군. 나는 지금 곧장 그 사람한테 가서 그 말이 무슨 뜻이었는지 알아낼 작정이야.\"\n\n라주미힌은 생각과 흥분에 잠긴 채 서서, 말없이 어떤 결론을 내리고 있었다.\n\n'그는 정치적 음모가야! 틀림없어. 그리고 그는 무슨 절망적인 행동을 앞두고 있는 거야, 그건 확실해. 그것 말고는 있을 수 없어! 그리고…… 그리고 두냐가 알고 있어.' 그는 갑자기 생각했다.\n\n'그러니까 아브도티야 로마노브나가 너를 보러 오고,' 그는 한 음절 한 음절 가늠하듯이 생각했다. '너는 우리한테 더 많은 공기가 필요하다고 말하는 사람을 보러 가겠다는 거지, 그러니 당연히 그 편지도…… 그것도 이 일과 무슨 관련이 있는 게 틀림없어.' 그는 속으로 결론지었다.\n\n\"무슨 편지?\"\n\n\"누이가 오늘 편지를 받았어. 그게 누이를 무척 동요시켰지, 정말 무척이나. 너무 지나칠 정도로. 내가 네 얘기를 꺼내기 시작하자, 누이는 그러지 말아 달라고 빌었어. 그러더니…… 그러더니 어쩌면 우리가 아주 곧 헤어지게 될지도 모른다고 하더군…… 그러고는 무언가에 대해 나한테 열렬히 고마워하기 시작했어. 그러더니 자기 방으로 가서 문을 잠가 버렸어.\"\n\n\"누이가 편지를 받았다고?\" 라스콜니코프가 생각에 잠겨 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 몰랐어? 흠…….\"\n\n두 사람은 모두 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"잘 있어, 로지온. 한때는, 이 친구야, 내가……. 아니, 됐어, 잘 있어. 알다시피, 한때는……. 뭐, 잘 있어! 나도 가 봐야겠어. 나는 술 안 마실 거야. 이제는 그럴 필요가 없어졌으니까……. 그건 다 헛소리야!\"\n\n그는 서둘러 나갔다. 그러나 거의 문을 닫았을 무렵, 그는 갑자기 다시 문을 열고는, 시선을 돌린 채 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그건 그렇고, 너 그 살인 사건 기억해, 그러니까 포르피리의, 그 노파 말이야? 범인이 잡혔다는 거 알아, 자백하고 증거까지 댔대. 바로 그 인부들 중 하나야, 페인트칠하던 자, 어디 한번 상상해 봐! 내가 여기서 그자들을 변호했던 거 기억해? 믿어지겠어, 문지기와 두 증인이 올라가던 그 모든 광경, 계단에서 동료들과 싸우고 웃고 했던 그 장면을, 그자가 의심을 따돌리려고 일부러 꾸민 거였대. 그 어린 개자식의 그 교활함, 그 침착함이라니! 좀처럼 믿기지 않지만, 그게 바로 그자 자신의 설명이고, 그자가 모든 걸 자백한 거야. 그리고 나는 그 일로 얼마나 바보였는지! 뭐, 그자는 그저 위선과, 변호사들의 의심을 따돌리는 임기응변의 천재인 거지, 그러니 별로 놀랄 것도 없는 셈이지! 물론 그런 자들은 언제든 있을 수 있어. 그리고 그자가 그 연기를 끝까지 유지하지 못하고 자백했다는 사실이, 오히려 그자를 믿기 쉽게 만들어. 그렇지만 내가 얼마나 바보였는지! 나는 그자들 편을 들며 미쳐 날뛰었으니!\"\n\n\"제발 말해 줘, 그 얘기를 누구한테서 들었고, 너는 왜 그렇게 그 일에 관심을 갖는 거야?\" 라스콜니코프가 분명한 동요를 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"이건 또 뭐야? 내가 왜 그 일에 관심을 갖느냐고 묻다니!…… 뭐, 다른 사람들 중에서도 포르피리한테서 들었어…… 거의 다 그 사람한테서 들은 거지.\"\n\n\"포르피리한테서?\"\n\n\"포르피리한테서.\"\n\n\"뭐라고…… 그가 뭐라고 했어?\" 라스콜니코프가 경악하며 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 그 일을 아주 멋들어지게 설명해 주더군. 심리학적으로, 자기 방식대로 말이야.\"\n\n\"그가 그걸 설명했다고? 자기가 직접 설명했어?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 잘 있어. 다음에 그 얘길 다 해 줄게, 지금은 바쁘니까. 한때는 내가 상상하기를…… 아니, 됐어, 다음에!…… 이제 내가 술을 마실 필요가 어디 있겠어? 너는 술도 없이 나를 취하게 만들어 놨어. 나는 취했어, 로쟈! 잘 있어, 나는 간다. 아주 곧 다시 올게.\"\n\n그는 나갔다.\n\n'그는 정치적 음모가야, 그건 의심할 여지가 없어.' 라주미힌은 천천히 계단을 내려가며 단정했다. '그리고 그는 누이까지 끌어들였어. 그건 정말이지, 아주 아브도티야 로마노브나의 성격과 들어맞아. 두 사람 사이에 만남이 있는 거야!…… 누이도 그걸 넌지시 비쳤고……. 그 숱한 말들이…… 그리고 암시들이…… 다 그런 뜻을 담고 있어! 그렇지 않고서야 이 모든 뒤엉킴을 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠어? 흠! 그리고 나는 하마터면…… 맙소사, 내가 무슨 생각을 했던 거야! 그래, 나는 제정신이 아니었고 그에게 못할 짓을 한 거야! 그건 그날 복도의 등불 아래서 그가 한 짓이었지. 푸! 내 딴에 얼마나 거칠고, 추잡하고, 비열한 생각이었는지! 니콜라이는 자백을 했으니, 멋진 녀석이야……. 그리고 이제 모든 게 얼마나 분명해졌는지! 그때 그의 병, 그 모든 이상한 행동들이…… 이 일이 있기 전에, 대학에서, 그가 얼마나 침울했던지, 얼마나 우울했던지……. 그런데 그 편지는 이제 무슨 의미일까? 거기에도 무언가가 있어, 어쩌면. 누구한테서 온 거지? 나는 의심스러워……! 아니, 내가 알아내고 말 거야!'\n\n그는 두냐를 떠올렸고, 자신이 들은 모든 것을 깨달으며 심장이 두근거렸고, 갑자기 뛰기 시작했다.\n\n라주미힌이 나가자마자, 라스콜니코프는 일어나서 창가로 돌아섰다가, 자기 방이 얼마나 작은지도 잊은 듯 한쪽 구석으로 갔다가 또 다른 구석으로 갔다가, 다시 소파에 앉았다. 그는, 말하자면, 새로워진 기분이었다. 또다시 싸움이, 그러니까 빠져나갈 방도가 찾아온 것이다.\n\n'그래, 빠져나갈 방도가 찾아왔어! 너무도 숨이 막혔고, 너무도 옥죄었고, 그 짐이 너무도 고통스러웠어. 때때로 그에게는 무기력증이 찾아오곤 했다. 포르피리의 집에서 니콜라이와의 그 장면이 있은 순간부터, 그는 숨이 막혀, 빠져나갈 희망도 없이 갇혀 있었다. 니콜라이의 자백 뒤, 바로 그날 소냐와의 장면이 찾아왔다. 그의 행동과 그가 마지막에 한 말들은, 그가 미리 상상할 수 있었던 그 어떤 것과도 전혀 달랐다. 그는 즉각적으로, 또 근본적으로 더 무력해진 것이다! 그리고 그는 그때 소냐와 의견이 일치했고, 마음속으로 동의했으니, 이런 것을 마음에 품은 채로는 더 이상 혼자서 살아갈 수 없다는 데 동의한 것이다!\n\n'그리고 스비드리가일로프는 수수께끼였다……. 그가 자신을 괴롭히는 것은 사실이었지만, 어쩐지 같은 지점에서는 아니었다. 스비드리가일로프와는 아직 다가올 싸움이 있을 수도 있었다. 스비드리가일로프 역시 빠져나갈 방도일 수도 있었다. 하지만 포르피리는 다른 문제였다.\n\n'그러니까 포르피리가 직접 라주미힌에게 그것을 설명한 것이고, 그것을 심리학적으로 설명한 것이다. 그가 또 그 빌어먹을 심리학을 끌어들이기 시작한 거야! 포르피리가? 하지만 니콜라이가 나타나기 전에 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일들이 있은 뒤에, 단 하나의 해석밖에는 있을 수 없었던 그 단둘이만의 만남이 있은 뒤에, 포르피리가 단 한순간이라도 니콜라이가 유죄라고 믿으리라고 생각하다니? (그 며칠 동안 라스콜니코프는 포르피리와의 그 장면의 대목들을 자주 떠올렸으나, 거기에 정신을 멈추고 머무를 수가 없었다.) 그런 말들이, 그런 몸짓들이 두 사람 사이에 오갔고, 그런 눈길을 주고받았고, 어떤 것들은 그런 어조로 말해졌으며, 사태가 그런 지경에까지 이르렀으니, 포르피리가 첫 마디에, 첫 몸짓에 이미 속을 꿰뚫어 본 니콜라이가, 그의 확신을 뒤흔들 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n'그리고 라주미힌마저 의심하기 시작했다니! 그렇다면 등불 아래 복도에서의 그 장면이 효과를 낸 것이다. 그는 포르피리에게 달려갔던 거고……. 그런데 무엇이 그자로 하여금 그를 그런 식으로 맞이하게 했을까? 라주미힌을 니콜라이로 따돌리려 한 그자의 목적은 무엇이었을까? 그자에게는 분명 어떤 계획이 있고, 어떤 속셈이 있는데, 그게 무엇일까? 사실 그날 아침 이후로 오랜 시간이 흘렀고, 너무도 오랜 시간이 흘렀는데도, 포르피리의 모습도 소식도 없었다. 그래, 그건 나쁜 징조였다…….'\n\n라스콜니코프는 모자를 집어 들고, 여전히 곰곰이 생각하며 방을 나섰다. 적어도 정신만은 맑다고 느낀 것은 참으로 오랜만이었다. '스비드리가일로프를 처리해야 해.' 그는 생각했다. '그것도 되도록 빨리. 그자 역시 내가 제 발로 자기를 찾아오기를 기다리고 있는 것 같으니.' 그리고 그 순간 그의 지친 가슴에는 그런 증오가 솟구쳐 올라, 그는 그 두 사람 중 누구라도, 포르피리든 스비드리가일로프든 죽일 수도 있을 것 같았다. 적어도 그는 지금은 아니더라도 나중에는 그것을 해낼 수 있으리라고 느꼈다.\n\n'두고 보자, 두고 보자.' 그는 속으로 되뇌었다.\n\n그러나 그가 문을 열기가 무섭게, 그는 통로에서 다름 아닌 포르피리와 맞닥뜨렸다. 그는 그를 만나러 들어오는 참이었다. 라스콜니코프는 잠시 어안이 벙벙했으나, 단지 잠시뿐이었다. 이상하게도, 그는 포르피리를 보고 그다지 크게 놀라지 않았고 그가 거의 두렵지도 않았다. 그는 그저 흠칫했을 뿐, 곧, 즉각적으로 경계 태세를 갖추었다. '어쩌면 이것이 끝을 의미하는 걸까? 그런데 어떻게 포르피리가 이렇게 고양이처럼 조용히 다가와서, 내가 아무 소리도 못 들었단 말인가? 그가 문가에서 엿듣고 있었던 걸까?'\n\n\"방문객은 기대하지 않으셨겠지요, 로지온 로마노비치.\" 포르피리가 웃으며 설명했다. \"오래전부터 들러 봐야겠다고 생각하고 있었습니다. 지나가던 길에, 잠깐 오 분쯤 들어가 보면 어떨까 했지요. 나가시는 길입니까? 오래 붙들지는 않겠습니다. 그저 담배 한 대만 피우게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"앉으시지요, 포르피리 페트로비치, 앉으세요.\" 라스콜니코프는 손님에게 자리를 권했는데, 그 표정이 어찌나 기쁘고 다정해 보였던지, 만일 자기 모습을 볼 수 있었다면 스스로도 놀라워했을 것이다.\n\n마지막 순간이 온 것이다, 마지막 한 방울까지 다 마셔 버려야만 했다! 이렇게 사람은 때때로 강도와 함께 죽음의 공포 속에서 삼십 분을 보내고도, 마침내 칼이 목에 들이대어지면 아무런 두려움도 느끼지 않는 법이다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 포르피리와 정면으로 마주하고 앉아, 움찔하지도 않고 그를 바라보았다. 포르피리는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 담배에 불을 붙이기 시작했다.\n\n'말해, 말해 봐.' 라스콜니코프의 가슴에서 터져 나올 듯했다. '자, 왜 말을 안 하는 거지?'\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“아, 이 담배라니!” 포르피리 페트로비치가 마침내 한 개비에 불을 붙이고는 내뱉었다. “해로워요, 정말이지 해롭다니까요, 그런데도 끊을 수가 없으니! 기침을 하고, 목이 간질거리기 시작하고, 숨쉬기가 곤란해지지요. 나는 겁쟁이라서, 얼마 전에 B 박사를 찾아갔는데, 그 양반은 환자 한 사람한테 늘 적어도 삼십 분은 할애하지요. 나를 보더니 그냥 웃더군요. 진찰을 하고는, ‘담배는 당신한테 나쁩니다’ 하고 말합디다, ‘폐가 상했어요.’ 하지만 내가 어떻게 끊겠습니까? 무엇으로 그걸 대신하겠어요? 술도 안 마시는데, 그게 문제지요, 헤헤헤, 술을 안 마시니. 모든 것은 상대적이지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 모든 게 상대적이라고요!”\n\n‘저자가 또 직업적인 수작을 부리는군.’ 라스콜니코프는 혐오스러운 마음으로 생각했다. 지난번 만남의 모든 정황이 불현듯 그의 머릿속에 되살아났고, 그때 자신을 덮쳤던 그 감정이 다시 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n“그저께 저녁에 댁을 찾아왔었지요, 모르셨나요?” 포르피리 페트로비치가 방 안을 둘러보며 말을 이었다. “바로 이 방으로 들어왔답니다. 마침 지나가는 길에, 오늘처럼 말이지요, 답방이나 해야겠다고 생각했지요. 댁의 문이 활짝 열려 있길래 들어와서, 둘러보고, 기다리다가, 댁의 하녀한테 이름도 남기지 않고 나갔답니다. 문을 안 잠그시나요?”\n\n라스콜니코프의 얼굴이 점점 더 어두워졌다. 포르피리는 그의 심중을 알아챈 듯했다.\n\n“그동안 쌓인 걸 댁과 분명히 매듭짓고 싶어서 왔습니다, 로디온 로마노비치, 친애하는 양반! 나는 댁에게 해명할 빚이 있고, 그걸 해야만 합니다.” 그는 가벼운 미소를 지으며 라스콜니코프의 무릎을 살짝 두드리고는 말을 이었다.\n\n그러나 거의 같은 순간 진지하고 근심스러운 빛이 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐다. 놀랍게도 라스콜니코프는 거기서 한 줄기 슬픔의 기색을 보았다. 그는 그의 얼굴에서 그런 표정을 한 번도 본 적이 없었고, 짐작조차 한 적이 없었다.\n\n“지난번에 우리가 만났을 때 이상한 장면이 있었지요, 로디온 로마노비치. 우리의 첫 만남도 이상한 것이었고요. 한데 그때는…… 그리고 일이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고! 요점은 이겁니다. 어쩌면 내가 댁에게 부당하게 굴었는지도 모르겠어요. 그게 느껴집니다. 우리가 어떻게 헤어졌는지 기억하시나요? 댁은 신경이 곤두서 무릎이 떨렸고, 나도 마찬가지였지요. 한데 말입니다, 우리의 행동은 꼴사나웠어요, 신사답지조차 못했지요. 그런데도 우리는 신사이고, 무엇보다도, 어떤 경우든, 신사입니다. 그건 알아 둬야 해요. 우리가 어디까지 갔는지 기억하시나요?…… 정말이지 꼴불견이었지요.”\n\n‘저자가 무슨 수작이지, 나를 뭘로 보는 거야?’ 라스콜니코프는 어리둥절하여 스스로에게 물으며, 고개를 들어 눈을 크게 뜨고 포르피리를 바라보았다.\n\n“우리 사이엔 솔직한 게 낫겠다고 결론을 내렸지요.” 포르피리 페트로비치가 고개를 옆으로 돌리고 눈을 내리깔며 말을 이었는데, 마치 자신의 옛 먹잇감을 당황하게 하고 싶지 않다는 듯, 또 자신의 옛 술수를 경멸한다는 듯한 태도였다. “그래요, 그런 의심과 그런 장면들이 오래 계속될 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 니콜라이가 거기에 종지부를 찍어 줬으니 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 우리가 어디까지 갔을지 모르겠어요. 그 망할 직공이 그때 옆방에 앉아 있었답니다, 상상이 되십니까? 댁도 물론 알고 계시지요. 그가 나중에 댁을 찾아갔다는 것도 압니다. 하지만 그때 댁이 짐작한 건 사실이 아니었어요. 나는 아무도 부르지 않았고, 어떤 준비도 해 놓지 않았답니다. 왜 안 그랬느냐고 물으시겠지요? 뭐라고 말씀드려야 할까요? 모든 게 너무도 갑작스럽게 닥쳤거든요. 나는 가까스로 문지기들을 부른 정도였지요(나가실 때 그들을 보셨을 겁니다, 아마도). 한 가지 생각이 번뜩 스쳤어요. 그때 나는 확신하고 있었거든요, 아시겠습니까, 로디온 로마노비치. 자, 생각했지요, 비록 한동안 한 가지를 놓치더라도, 다른 무언가는 붙잡게 될 거다, 어쨌든 내가 원하는 건 놓치지 않으리라. 댁은 신경질적으로 예민한 사람이지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 천성이 그래요. 그게 댁의 마음과 성격의 다른 자질들과는 균형이 안 맞는데, 그 자질들을 나는 어느 정도 꿰뚫어 봤다고 자부합니다. 물론 그때도 나는 이렇게 생각했지요, 사람이란 게 항상 벌떡 일어나 제 이야기를 죄다 불쑥 털어놓는 건 아니라고. 그런 일이 가끔 있긴 하지요, 사람을 인내심의 끝까지 몰아붙이면 말입니다, 하지만 그래도 드문 일이에요. 나는 그걸 알아챌 수 있었지요. 단 하나의 사실이라도, 발 디딜 아주 작은 사실 하나라도 있으면, 단지 심리적인 게 아니라 붙잡을 수 있는, 손에 잡히는 무언가가 있으면, 하고 생각했답니다. 사람이 죄가 있으면, 거기서 뭔가 실질적인 걸 끌어낼 수 있는 법이니까요. 정말이지 가장 놀라운 결과들을 기대해 볼 만하지요. 나는 댁의 기질에 기대를 걸고 있었어요, 로디온 로마노비치, 무엇보다도 댁의 기질에! 그때 나는 댁에게 큰 기대를 품고 있었답니다.”\n\n“그런데 지금 무슨 말을 하려는 겁니까?” 라스콜니코프가 마침내 중얼거렸는데, 생각도 없이 던진 물음이었다.\n\n‘저자가 무슨 소리를 하는 거지?’ 그는 정신없이 의아해했다. ‘정말로 나를 결백하다고 여기는 건가?’\n\n“무슨 말을 하려느냐고요? 나 자신을 해명하러 왔습니다, 그게 내 의무라고 여기거든요, 말하자면 말이지요. 이 모든 일이, 이 모든 오해가 어떻게 생겨났는지 댁에게 분명히 밝히고 싶어요. 나는 댁에게 큰 고통을 안겼지요, 로디온 로마노비치. 나는 괴물이 아닙니다. 불행을 겪었으되 자존심 강하고, 위압적이며, 무엇보다 참을성 없는 사람이 그런 취급을 견뎌야 한다는 게 어떤 의미인지 이해해요! 나는 어떤 경우든 댁을 고결한 성품을 지닌, 관대함의 요소도 없지 않은 사람으로 봅니다, 비록 댁의 모든 신념에 동의하는 건 아니지만요. 이걸 먼저 말씀드리고 싶었어요, 솔직하게, 아주 진심으로 말입니다, 무엇보다 댁을 속이고 싶지 않으니까요. 댁과 알게 됐을 때, 나는 댁에게 끌렸답니다. 어쩌면 내가 이런 말을 하는 걸 비웃으실지도 모르지요. 그럴 권리가 있어요. 댁이 처음부터 나를 싫어했다는 걸 압니다, 사실 나를 좋아할 이유도 없지요. 무엇을 생각하시든 좋습니다만, 나는 이제 그 인상을 지우기 위해, 그리고 내가 마음과 양심을 지닌 사람임을 보여 주기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 걸 하고 싶어요. 진심으로 하는 말입니다.”\n\n포르피리 페트로비치는 위엄 있게 잠시 말을 멈췄다. 라스콜니코프는 새로운 경계심이 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다. 포르피리가 자신을 결백하다고 믿는다는 생각이 그를 불안하게 하기 시작했다.\n\n“모든 걸 세세히 짚어 볼 필요는 거의 없겠지요.” 포르피리 페트로비치가 말을 이었다. “사실 그러려고 시도조차 못 할 겁니다. 우선 소문들이 있었어요. 누구를 통해, 어떻게, 언제 그 소문들이 내게 들어왔는지…… 그리고 그것들이 댁에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지는 굳이 들어갈 필요가 없겠지요. 내 의심을 불러일으킨 건 순전한 우연이었는데, 그건 그만큼 쉽게 일어나지 않았을 수도 있는 일이었어요. 그게 뭐였느냐고요? 흠! 그것도 들어갈 필요는 없을 것 같군요. 그 소문들과 그 우연이 내 머릿속에서 한 가지 생각으로 이어졌지요. 솔직히 인정하겠습니다 — 차라리 깨끗이 털어놓는 게 나을 테니 — 댁을 처음 점찍은 게 바로 나였어요. 노파가 저당물에 적어 놓은 메모니 뭐니 하는 것들 — 그건 다 아무 소용이 없었지요. 댁은 수백 명 중 하나일 뿐이었어요. 게다가 우연히 그 관청에서의 장면도 들었지요, 그걸 기막히게 묘사해 준 사람한테서 말입니다, 자기도 모르게 그 장면을 아주 생생하게 재현하더군요. 그저 일이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물었지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 친애하는 양반! 어떻게 내가 어떤 생각에 이르지 않을 수 있었겠습니까? 토끼 백 마리로 말 한 마리를 만들 수 없고, 의심 백 개로 증거가 되지 않는다고, 영국 속담에도 있지요, 하지만 그건 이성적인 관점에서만 그런 겁니다 — 편견을 안 가질 수 없거든요, 따지고 보면 법률가도 그저 인간일 뿐이니까요. 그 잡지에 실린 댁의 논문도 생각났지요, 기억하시나요, 첫 방문 때 우리가 그 얘기를 했지요? 그때 나는 댁을 비웃었지만, 그건 그저 댁을 끌어내기 위해서였어요. 다시 말하지만, 로디온 로마노비치, 댁은 병들었고 참을성이 없어요. 댁이 대담하고, 고집스럽고, 진지하며…… 많은 것을 느껴 왔다는 건 나도 오래전부터 알아봤지요. 나 역시 똑같은 걸 느껴 본 적이 있어서, 댁의 논문이 내게 익숙하게 다가왔답니다. 그건 잠 못 이루는 밤들에, 두근거리는 가슴으로, 황홀경과 억눌린 열정 속에서 잉태된 것이지요. 그리고 젊은 사람들의 그 오만하게 억눌린 열정은 위험합니다! 그때 나는 댁을 비웃었지만, 한마디 하지요, 문학 애호가로서 나는 그런 첫 시도들을, 젊음의 열기로 가득한 것들을 끔찍이도 좋아합니다. 거기엔 안개 같은 것이, 그리고 그 안개 속에서 떨리는 한 가닥 현이 있지요. 댁의 논문은 터무니없고 환상적이지만, 거기엔 투명한 진실함이, 젊은이의 타락하지 않은 자존심과 절망의 대담함이 있어요. 음울한 논문이지만, 바로 그게 그 글의 훌륭한 점이지요. 나는 댁의 논문을 읽고 한쪽에 치워 두면서, 이렇게 생각했답니다, ‘이 사람은 흔한 길로는 가지 않겠군.’ 자, 묻겠습니다만, 그렇게 시작된 뒤에, 내가 그다음에 일어난 일에 휩쓸리지 않을 수 있었겠습니까? 오, 이런, 나는 아무 말도 하는 게 아닙니다, 지금 무슨 진술을 하는 게 아니에요. 그저 그때 그렇게 적어 뒀을 뿐이지요. 거기에 뭐가 있느냐고요? 나는 곰곰이 생각했어요. 거기엔 아무것도 없다, 정말 아무것도 없고 어쩌면 절대로 아무것도 없는 거다. 그리고 검사가 그런 관념들에 휩쓸리게 두는 건 전혀 온당치 못한 일이지요. 여기 내 손에는 니콜라이가, 그것도 그에게 불리한 실제 증거를 들고 있단 말입니다 — 그걸 어떻게 생각하시든 좋지만, 어쨌든 증거예요. 그자도 자기 나름의 심리를 들이밀지요. 그자도 고려해야 합니다, 목숨이 걸린 문제니까요. 내가 왜 이걸 댁에게 설명하느냐고요? 댁이 이해하고, 그때 내 짓궂은 행동을 탓하지 않도록 하기 위해서지요. 악의가 있었던 게 아니에요, 정말입니다, 헤헤! 내가 그때 댁의 방을 수색하러 오지 않았다고 생각하십니까? 했지요, 했어요, 헤헤! 댁이 앓아누워 있을 때 내가 여기 있었답니다, 공식적으로는 아니고, 내 신분으로 온 건 아니지만, 여기 있었어요. 첫 의심이 들었을 때 댁의 방은 실오라기 하나까지 샅샅이 수색당했지요. 하지만 움존스트(헛수고)였어요! 나는 속으로 생각했지요, 이제 저 사람이 올 거다, 제 발로, 그것도 빨리 올 거다, 죄가 있다면 틀림없이 올 거야. 다른 사람이라면 안 오겠지만, 이 사람은 올 거다. 그리고 라주미힌 씨가 어떻게 댁과 그 문제를 논하기 시작했는지 기억하시지요? 댁을 자극하려고 우리가 꾸민 일이었답니다, 그래서 일부러 소문을 퍼뜨렸지요, 그가 댁과 그 사건을 논하도록 말입니다, 라주미힌은 자기 분노를 억누를 만한 사람이 아니니까요. 자메토프 씨는 댁의 분노와 노골적인 대담함에 엄청나게 놀랐답니다. 식당에서 ‘내가 그 여자를 죽였다’고 불쑥 내뱉다니요. 너무 대담했어요, 너무 무모했지요. 나 자신도 그렇게 생각했답니다, 만일 저자가 죄가 있다면 만만찮은 적수가 되겠구나. 그게 그때 내 생각이었어요. 나는 댁을 기다리고 있었지요. 그런데 댁은 그냥 자메토프를 나가떨어지게 만들고…… 자, 보세요, 모든 게 여기에 있는 겁니다 — 그 빌어먹을 심리라는 게 두 가지로 해석될 수 있다는 거지요! 자, 나는 줄곧 댁을 기다리고 있었고, 과연 그대로, 댁이 왔어요! 내 심장이 정말이지 두근거렸답니다. 아!\n\n“자, 댁은 도대체 왜 와야 했던 걸까요? 들어오면서 짓던 그 웃음도, 기억하시나요? 나는 그 모든 걸 대낮처럼 환히 봤답니다, 하지만 내가 그렇게 특별히 댁을 기다리지 않았더라면, 그 웃음에서 아무것도 알아채지 못했을 거예요. 기분이라는 게 어떤 영향을 미치는지 아시겠지요! 그때 라주미힌 씨가 — 아, 그 돌, 물건들을 숨겨 둔 그 돌 말입니다! 어딘가 채마밭에 있는 것 같던데요. 채마밭에 있었지요, 댁이 자메토프한테 그렇게 말하고 나서 내 사무실에서 그 말을 또 했지요? 그리고 우리가 댁의 논문을 조목조목 따지기 시작했을 때, 댁이 그걸 어떻게 설명했는지! 댁의 말은 한마디 한마디가 두 가지 뜻으로 받아들여질 수 있었어요, 마치 또 다른 의미가 숨어 있는 것처럼 말이지요.\n\n“그래서 이런 식으로, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 막다른 한계까지 다다랐고, 기둥에 머리를 부딪치고 나서야 정신을 차리며, 내가 지금 뭘 하고 있는 건가 스스로에게 물었지요. 결국, 하고 나는 말했어요, 원한다면 이 모든 걸 다른 뜻으로 받아들일 수도 있다, 사실 그게 더 자연스럽기까지 하다. 그게 더 자연스럽다는 걸 인정하지 않을 수 없었지요. 나는 골치가 아팠어요! ‘안 되겠다, 작은 사실이라도 하나 붙잡는 게 낫겠다’ 하고 나는 말했지요. 그래서 그 초인종 소동 얘기를 들었을 때, 나는 숨을 죽이고 온몸을 떨었답니다. ‘여기 내 작은 사실이 있다’ 하고 생각했고, 더는 곰곰이 따져 보지도 않았어요, 그냥 그러기가 싫었지요. 그 순간 나는 천 루블이라도 내놨을 겁니다, 댁을 내 두 눈으로 직접 봤더라면 말입니다, 그 직공이 댁의 면전에 대고 살인자라고 한 뒤에 댁이 그자 곁에서 백 걸음이나 걸으면서, 가는 내내 감히 그자에게 한마디도 묻지 못했던 그 모습을요. 그리고 또 댁의 그 떨림은 어떻고, 병중에, 반쯤 헛소리를 하며 초인종을 울려 댄 건 또 어떻고요?\n\n“그러니, 로디온 로마노비치, 내가 댁에게 그런 장난을 친 게 뭐가 이상하겠습니까? 그런데 도대체 무엇 때문에 댁은 바로 그 순간에 온 걸까요? 누군가가 댁을 보낸 것만 같았지요, 정말이지! 그리고 니콜라이가 우리를 갈라놓지 않았더라면…… 그때 니콜라이를 기억하시나요? 똑똑히 기억하십니까? 그건 청천벽력이었어요, 그야말로 청천벽력이었지요! 그리고 내가 그걸 어떻게 맞이했던가요! 나는 그 벽력을 믿지 않았답니다, 한순간도요. 댁도 직접 봤을 겁니다. 그러니 내가 어떻게 믿겠어요? 나중에도, 댁이 가고 나서 그자가 어떤 점들에 대해 아주, 아주 그럴듯한 대답을 하기 시작해서 나 자신도 그자에게 놀랐을 때조차, 그때도 나는 그자의 이야기를 믿지 않았어요! 바위처럼 굳건하다는 게 어떤 건지 아시겠지요! 안 되겠다, 하고 나는 생각했지요, 모르겐프뤼(천만에)! 니콜라이가 이 일과 무슨 상관이람!”\n\n“라주미힌이 방금 말해 줬어요, 당신은 니콜라이가 죄가 있다고 여기며, 당신 입으로 그에게 그렇게 장담했다고……”\n\n그의 목소리가 끊기며 말을 멈췄다. 자신을 속속들이 꿰뚫어 보았던 이 사내가 다시 제 말을 뒤집는 동안, 그는 형언할 수 없는 동요 속에서 듣고 있었던 것이다. 그는 그것을 믿기가 두려웠고, 또 믿지 않았다. 여전히 애매한 그 말들 속에서 그는 무언가 더 분명하고 결정적인 것을 애타게 찾고 있었다.\n\n“라주미힌 씨라!” 포르피리 페트로비치가 외쳤는데, 그때까지 입을 다물고 있던 라스콜니코프의 질문이 반가운 듯했다. “헤헤헤! 하지만 나는 라주미힌 씨를 떼어 놓아야 했지요. 둘이면 좋은 짝이지만 셋이면 군더더기니까요. 라주미힌 씨는 알맞은 사람이 아닐뿐더러, 외부인이지요. 그가 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 내게 달려왔답니다…… 하지만 그 사람은 됐고, 왜 그를 끌어들이겠습니까? 니콜라이 얘기로 돌아가서, 그자가 어떤 부류인지, 내가 그자를 어떻게 이해하는지, 그러니까 알고 싶으십니까? 우선, 그자는 아직 어린애이고 딱히 겁쟁이는 아니지만, 어딘가 예술가 같은 데가 있어요. 정말이지, 내가 그자를 이렇게 묘사한다고 비웃지 마세요. 그자는 순진하고 남의 영향을 잘 받지요. 마음씨가 있고, 환상적인 친구예요. 노래하고 춤추고, 이야기를 어찌나 잘하는지, 듣자 하니 다른 마을에서까지 사람들이 들으러 온다더군요. 학교도 다니는데, 손가락 하나만 들어 올려도 눈물이 날 때까지 웃지요. 정신을 잃을 때까지 마시기도 하는데 — 고질적인 악습은 아니고, 가끔, 사람들이 한턱낼 때, 어린애처럼 말입니다. 그리고 그때도 자기도 모르게 훔쳤지요, ‘떨어진 걸 주운 게 어떻게 도둑질이람?’ 하면서요. 그리고 그자가 구교도(舊敎徒), 아니 정확히는 분리파라는 걸 아십니까? 그자 집안엔 유랑파*들이 있었고, 그자는 이 년 동안 자기 마을에서 어떤 장로의 영적 지도를 받았답니다. 이 모든 걸 나는 니콜라이와 그 마을 사람들한테서 알아냈지요. 게다가 그자는 황야로 달아나고 싶어 했어요! 열정에 가득 차서, 밤마다 기도하고, ‘참된’ 옛 책들을 읽고, 읽다가 정신이 나가 버렸지요.\n\n* 유랑파: 한 종교 분파다.—옮긴이 주.\n\n“페테르부르크가 그자에게 큰 영향을 미쳤어요, 특히 여자들과 술이 말이지요. 그자는 매사에 반응하니, 장로고 뭐고 다 잊어버렸답니다. 듣자 하니 여기 어떤 화가가 그자를 좋아해서 보러 다니곤 했는데, 그러다 이번 사건이 그자한테 들이닥친 거지요.\n\n“그래서 그자는 겁에 질려서, 목을 매려고 했어요! 달아났지요! 러시아 사법 절차에 대해 민중이 품고 있는 그 관념을 어떻게 깨겠습니까? ‘재판’이라는 말 자체가 그들 중 몇몇을 겁먹게 하지요. 그게 누구 탓이겠습니까? 새 배심원들이 어떻게 하는지 두고 봅시다. 신께서 그들이 잘하도록 도우시기를! 자, 감옥에서, 그자는 그 존경스러운 장로를 떠올린 모양입니다. 성경도 다시 등장했고요. 아십니까, 로디온 로마노비치, 그런 사람들 사이에서 ‘수난’이라는 말이 지닌 힘을! 누군가를 위해 고통받는다는 게 아니라, 그저 ‘고통받아야 한다’는 거지요. 당국의 손에 고통받는다면, 그건 더 좋은 일이고요. 내가 일하던 시절에 아주 온순하고 유순한 죄수가 하나 있었는데, 꼬박 일 년을 감옥에서 밤마다 난로 위에서 성경을 읽다가 정신이 나가 버렸지요, 그것도 어찌나 단단히 나갔는지, 아십니까, 어느 날 난데없이 벽돌을 집어 들어 소장한테 던졌답니다, 그 사람이 자기한테 아무 해도 끼치지 않았는데 말이지요. 게다가 던진 모양새가 또, 일부러 한 걸음 옆을 겨냥했어요, 그 사람을 다치게 할까 봐서요. 자, 무기로 관리를 폭행한 죄수가 어떻게 되는지는 우리도 알지요. 그렇게 그자는 ‘자기 수난을 받아들인’ 겁니다.\n\n“그래서 지금 나는 니콜라이가 자기 수난이나 뭐 그런 비슷한 걸 받아들이고 싶어 한다고 의심합니다. 사실 여러 정황으로 봐서 확실히 알고 있지요. 다만 그자는 내가 안다는 걸 모를 뿐이에요. 뭐라고요, 농부들 사이에 그런 환상적인 사람들이 있다는 걸 인정 못 하시겠다고요? 수두룩합니다. 이제 그 장로가 그자에게 영향을 미치기 시작했어요, 특히 그자가 목을 매려 한 뒤로 말이지요. 하지만 그자는 와서 내게 죄다 제 입으로 털어놓을 겁니다. 그자가 버틸 거라고 생각하십니까? 좀 기다려 보세요, 그자는 자기 말을 거둬들일 겁니다. 나는 시시각각 기다리고 있어요, 그자가 와서 자기 증언을 부인하기를 말입니다. 나는 그 니콜라이가 마음에 들기 시작해서 그자를 세세히 연구하고 있답니다. 그런데 어떻게 생각하십니까? 헤헤! 그자는 어떤 점들에선 내게 아주 그럴듯하게 대답했어요, 분명히 증거를 좀 모아서 영리하게 준비를 한 거지요. 하지만 다른 점들에선 그냥 갈피를 못 잡고, 아무것도 모르고, 자기가 모른다는 것조차 알아채지 못한답니다!\n\n“아니에요, 로디온 로마노비치, 니콜라이는 이 일에 끼어들지 않아요! 이건 환상적이고 음울한 사건, 현대적인 사건, 인간의 마음이 어지러운 오늘날의 사건이지요. 피가 ‘새롭게 한다’는 문구가 인용되고, 안락이 삶의 목적으로 설파되는 시대 말입니다. 여기엔 책에서 나온 몽상이, 이론들로 곤두선 마음이 있어요. 여기서 우리는 첫 단계의 결단을 봅니다만, 그건 특별한 종류의 결단이지요. 그는 그 일을 결행했어요, 마치 절벽에서 뛰어내리거나 종탑에서 뛰어내리듯이, 그리고 범행을 향해 가면서 그의 다리는 떨렸지요. 그는 뒤로 문 닫는 걸 잊었고, 이론 하나 때문에 두 사람을 죽였어요. 살인을 저지르고도 돈은 챙기지 못했고, 가까스로 움켜쥔 것은 돌 밑에 숨겼지요. 그들이 문을 두드리고 초인종을 울려 대는 동안 문 뒤에서 고뇌하는 것만으로는 모자랐던지, 아니, 그는 반쯤 헛소리를 하며 그 빈집까지 가야 했어요, 그 초인종 소리를 다시 떠올리려고, 그 오싹한 전율을 다시 한번 느끼고 싶어서 말이지요…… 자, 그건 병 탓이었다고 칩시다, 하지만 이걸 생각해 보세요. 그는 살인자이면서도 자기 자신을 정직한 사람으로 여기고, 남들을 경멸하며, 상처 입은 결백인 양 행세합니다. 아니에요, 그건 니콜라이가 할 짓이 아니지요, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치!”\n\n앞서 한 말들이 모두 너무도 자기 진술을 뒤집는 것처럼 들렸던 터라, 이 말은 너무도 큰 충격이었다. 라스콜니코프는 칼에 찔리기라도 한 듯 몸서리쳤다.\n\n“그럼…… 그럼 누가…… 살인자라는 겁니까?” 그는 스스로를 억누르지 못한 채 숨 가쁜 목소리로 물었다.\n\n포르피리 페트로비치는 그 질문에 놀라기라도 한 듯 의자에 등을 기댔다.\n\n“누가 살인자냐고요?” 그는 제 귀를 믿을 수 없다는 듯 되물었다. “아니, 당신이지요, 로디온 로마노비치! 당신이 살인자입니다.” 그는 거의 속삭이듯, 진정한 확신이 담긴 목소리로 덧붙였다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 소파에서 벌떡 일어나 몇 초쯤 서 있다가 한마디도 없이 다시 앉았다. 그의 얼굴이 경련하듯 씰룩거렸다.\n\n“입술이 전처럼 또 씰룩거리는군요.” 포르피리 페트로비치가 거의 동정하듯 말했다. “내 생각엔 댁이 나를 오해하고 있는 것 같아요, 로디온 로마노비치.” 그는 잠시 사이를 두었다가 덧붙였다. “그래서 그렇게 놀라는 거지요. 나는 일부러 모든 걸 댁에게 말하고 솔직하게 처신하려고 온 겁니다.”\n\n“그 여자를 죽인 건 내가 아니오.” 라스콜니코프는 현장에서 붙잡힌 겁먹은 아이처럼 속삭였다.\n\n“아니에요, 댁이었어요, 댁이지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 다른 누구도 아니에요.” 포르피리는 확신에 차서 엄하게 속삭였다.\n\n두 사람 다 입을 다물었고, 그 침묵은 이상하리만치 오래, 십 분쯤이나 이어졌다. 라스콜니코프는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손가락으로 머리칼을 쓸어 넘겼다. 포르피리 페트로비치는 조용히 앉아 기다렸다. 갑자기 라스콜니코프가 경멸하듯 포르피리를 바라보았다.\n\n“또 그 낡은 수작이군요, 포르피리 페트로비치! 또 그 낡은 수법이오. 신물도 안 나는 게 놀랍습니다!”\n\n“오, 그만두세요, 그게 지금 무슨 상관입니까? 증인들이 있다면 다른 문제겠지만, 우리는 단둘이 속삭이고 있잖아요. 댁도 보다시피 나는 댁을 토끼처럼 쫓아 잡으려고 온 게 아닙니다. 댁이 자백하든 안 하든 이제 내겐 아무래도 좋아요. 나로서는 그것 없이도 확신하고 있으니까요.”\n\n“그렇다면 무엇 하러 온 겁니까?” 라스콜니코프가 짜증스럽게 물었다. “같은 질문을 다시 하지요. 나를 죄인으로 여긴다면, 왜 나를 감옥에 데려가지 않는 겁니까?”\n\n“오, 바로 그게 댁의 질문이군요! 조목조목 답해 드리지요. 첫째, 댁을 그렇게 곧바로 체포하는 건 내게 이롭지 않습니다.”\n\n“어째서요? 확신한다면 마땅히…….”\n\n“아, 내가 확신한들 그게 어쨌다는 겁니까? 그건 지금으로선 그저 내 꿈일 뿐이에요. 내가 왜 댁을 안전한 곳에 넣어 두겠습니까? 댁이 그렇게 해 달라고 청하는 걸 보니 그게 안전한 걸 아는 거지요. 가령 내가 댁을 그 직공과 대질시키면 댁은 그자에게 이렇게 말하겠지요, ‘당신, 취했었소, 안 취했었소? 누가 나를 당신과 함께 봤다는 거요? 나는 그냥 당신이 취한 걸로 알았고, 당신도 취해 있었잖소.’ 자, 그러면 내가 뭐라고 답하겠습니까, 더구나 댁의 이야기가 그자 것보다 더 그럴듯한데 말입니다. 그자의 증언을 뒷받침하는 건 심리뿐이거든요 — 그 추한 상판에 그건 거의 꼴사납기까지 하지요, 반면에 댁은 정확히 정곡을 찌르고요, 그 악당은 소문난 골수 주정뱅이니까요. 게다가 나 자신도 이미 여러 번 솔직히 인정했지요, 그 심리라는 게 두 가지로 해석될 수 있고, 두 번째 해석이 더 유력하며 훨씬 더 그럴듯해 보인다고, 그리고 그것 말고는 아직 댁에게 불리한 게 아무것도 없다고 말입니다. 그래서 비록 내가 댁을 감옥에 넣을 거고, 사실 — 예의에 완전히 어긋나게도 — 그걸 미리 알리러 오긴 했지만, 그래도 솔직히 말씀드리지요, 이 또한 예의에 어긋나게, 그게 내게 득이 되지는 않을 겁니다. 자, 둘째로, 내가 댁을 찾아온 건…….”\n\n“그래요, 그래, 둘째는요?” 라스콜니코프는 숨을 죽이고 듣고 있었다.\n\n“방금 말씀드렸듯이, 내가 댁에게 해명할 빚이 있다고 여기기 때문이에요. 댁이 나를 괴물로 여기는 게 싫거든요, 나는 댁에게 진심으로 호감을 갖고 있으니까요, 믿든 안 믿든 말입니다. 그리고 셋째로, 나는 댁에게 직접적이고 솔직한 제안을 하러 왔습니다 — 댁이 자수하고 자백하라는 거지요. 그게 댁에게도, 내게도 한없이 더 이로울 겁니다, 내 임무가 끝날 테니까요. 자, 이게 내 쪽에서 솔직한 겁니까, 아닙니까?”\n\n라스콜니코프는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n“들어 보세요, 포르피리 페트로비치. 당신은 방금 심리 말고는 의지할 게 아무것도 없다고 했는데, 그러더니 이제 수학으로 넘어갔군요. 자, 만약 지금 당신 자신이 잘못 짚은 거라면 어쩌지요?”\n\n“아니에요, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 잘못 짚지 않았어요. 그때도 내겐 작은 사실 하나가 있었지요, 섭리가 내게 보내 준 겁니다.”\n\n“무슨 작은 사실이오?”\n\n“그게 뭔지는 말하지 않겠습니다, 로디온 로마노비치. 어쨌든 나는 더 이상 미룰 권리가 없어요, 댁을 체포해야 합니다. 그러니 잘 생각해 보세요. 지금으로선 내겐 아무 차이가 없으니, 나는 오직 댁을 위해 하는 말입니다. 믿으세요, 그게 더 나을 겁니다, 로디온 로마노비치.”\n\n라스콜니코프가 악의에 차서 미소 지었다.\n\n“그건 우스꽝스러운 정도가 아니라, 정말이지 파렴치한 짓이오. 아니, 내가 죄인이라 칩시다, 인정하는 건 아니지만, 감옥에 있는 게 더 안전하다고 당신 입으로 말하면서, 내게 무슨 이유로 자백하라는 겁니까?”\n\n“아, 로디온 로마노비치, 말을 너무 곧이곧대로 믿지 마세요, 어쩌면 감옥이 마냥 편안한 곳은 아닐지도 모르니까요. 그건 그저 이론일 뿐이고, 그것도 내 이론이에요, 그리고 내가 댁에게 무슨 권위가 되겠습니까? 어쩌면 지금도 내가 댁에게 뭔가를 숨기고 있는지도 모르지요? 모든 걸 다 드러낼 수는 없거든요, 헤헤! 그리고 무슨 이로움이 있느냐니, 어떻게 그런 걸 물으십니까? 그게 댁의 형을 얼마나 가볍게 해 줄지 모르신단 말입니까? 다른 사람이 그 죄를 자기가 떠맡아 사건 전체를 뒤죽박죽으로 만들어 놓은 그 순간에 댁이 자백하는 게 되니까요. 그걸 생각해 보세요! 신께 맹세컨대 나는 댁의 자백이 완전한 뜻밖의 일로 보이도록 손을 써 두겠습니다. 우리는 이 모든 심리적인 점들을, 댁에 대한 의심을 깨끗이 쓸어 없앨 거예요, 그래서 댁의 범죄가 일종의 착란 같은 걸로 보이게 말입니다, 사실 그건 정말로 착란이었으니까요. 나는 정직한 사람입니다, 로디온 로마노비치, 그리고 약속을 지킬 겁니다.”\n\n라스콜니코프는 침울한 침묵을 지키며 맥없이 고개를 떨구었다. 그는 오랫동안 골똘히 생각하다가 마침내 다시 미소 지었지만, 그 미소는 슬프고 온순했다.\n\n“아니오!” 그는 포르피리 앞에서 더는 체면을 차리려는 시도를 모두 내려놓은 듯 말했다. “그럴 가치 없소, 나는 형이 가벼워지든 말든 관심 없습니다!”\n\n“바로 그게 내가 두려워하던 겁니다!” 포르피리가 열을 띠고, 보아하니 저도 모르게 외쳤다. “바로 그게 내가 걱정했던 거예요, 댁이 형의 경감 따위엔 관심을 두지 않으리라는 거 말입니다.”\n\n라스콜니코프는 슬프고도 의미심장하게 그를 바라보았다.\n\n“아, 삶을 업신여기지 마세요!” 포르피리가 말을 이었다. “댁 앞엔 아직 삶이 많이 남아 있어요. 어떻게 형의 경감을 원치 않는다고 말할 수 있습니까? 댁은 참을성 없는 사람이로군요!”\n\n“내 앞에 많이 남은 게 무엇이오?”\n\n“삶 말입니다. 댁이 무슨 예언자라고, 그걸 그리 잘 아십니까? 구하라, 그러면 찾을 것이다. 이게 어쩌면 신께서 댁을 당신께로 이끄시려는 방편일지도 모릅니다. 그리고 그 속박이 영원한 건 아니에요…….”\n\n“형기가 단축되겠지요.” 라스콜니코프가 웃었다.\n\n“아니, 댁이 두려워하는 건 세속적인 치욕입니까? 어쩌면 댁은 그걸 모르고서 두려워하는 건지도 몰라요, 젊으니까요! 하지만 어쨌든 댁은 자수하고 자백하는 걸 두려워해선 안 됩니다.”\n\n“아, 빌어먹을!” 라스콜니코프는 큰 소리로 말하고 싶지 않다는 듯 혐오와 경멸을 담아 속삭였다.\n\n그는 갈 작정인 듯 다시 일어났지만, 역력한 절망에 잠겨 다시 앉았다.\n\n“빌어먹을, 그러고 싶으면 그러세요! 댁은 믿음을 잃었고, 내가 댁에게 노골적으로 아첨한다고 여기지요. 하지만 댁이 살아 봐야 얼마나 살았습니까? 얼마나 이해하고 있습니까? 댁은 이론 하나를 지어내고는, 그게 무너져 내려 전혀 독창적이지 않은 것으로 드러나자 부끄러워했지요! 그게 천박한 것으로 드러난 건 사실입니다, 하지만 댁은 구제 불능으로 천박한 사람은 아니에요. 결코 그렇게까지 천박하진 않아요! 적어도 댁은 오래 자기 자신을 속이진 않았지요, 단번에 가장 먼 지점까지 곧장 나아갔으니까요. 내가 댁을 어떻게 보느냐고요? 나는 댁을, 믿음이나 신을 찾기만 한다면, 고문자가 제 창자를 갈라내는 동안에도 서서 미소 지을 그런 사람들 중 하나로 봅니다. 그걸 찾으세요, 그러면 살아날 겁니다. 댁은 오래전부터 공기를 바꿀 필요가 있었어요. 수난 또한 좋은 것이지요. 고통받으세요! 어쩌면 니콜라이가 고통받기를 원하는 게 옳은지도 몰라요. 댁이 그걸 믿지 않는다는 걸 압니다 — 하지만 너무 영리하게 굴지 마세요. 머뭇거리지 말고 곧장 삶 속으로 몸을 던지세요. 두려워하지 마세요 — 홍수가 댁을 강기슭으로 실어다 다시 두 발로 안전하게 세워 줄 겁니다. 어떤 기슭이냐고요? 내가 어찌 알겠습니까? 다만 나는 댁 앞에 긴 삶이 있다고 믿어요. 댁이 지금 내 말을 죄다 미리 준비해 둔 연설쯤으로 여긴다는 걸 압니다, 하지만 어쩌면 나중에 이 말들을 기억하게 될 거예요. 언젠가 쓸모가 있을지도 모릅니다. 그래서 말하는 거예요. 댁이 그 노파만 죽인 건 그나마 다행이지요. 다른 이론을 지어냈더라면 어쩌면 천 배는 더 끔찍한 짓을 저질렀을지도 몰라요. 어쩌면 신께 감사해야 할지도 모릅니다. 댁이 어떻게 알겠어요? 어쩌면 신께서 무언가를 위해 댁을 살려 두시는지도 모르지요. 하지만 마음을 굳게 먹고, 두려움을 좀 덜어 내세요! 댁 앞에 놓인 그 큰 속죄가 두렵습니까? 아니, 그걸 두려워하는 건 부끄러운 일일 겁니다. 그런 걸음을 내디뎠으니, 댁은 마음을 단단히 해야 해요. 거기엔 정의가 있습니다. 댁은 정의의 요구를 다해야 해요. 댁이 그걸 믿지 않는다는 걸 압니다, 하지만 정말이지, 삶이 댁을 끝까지 이끌어 줄 겁니다. 시간이 지나면 댁은 그걸 견뎌 내고 극복할 거예요. 댁에게 지금 필요한 건 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기입니다!”\n\n라스콜니코프는 정말이지 흠칫했다.\n\n“하지만 당신은 누구요? 무슨 예언자란 말이오? 도대체 어떤 위엄에 찬 평온의 높이에서 이런 지혜의 말을 선포하는 겁니까?”\n\n“내가 누구냐고요? 나는 아무것도 바랄 게 없는 사람이지요, 그게 다입니다. 어쩌면 감정과 연민을 지닌 사람, 어쩌면 약간의 지식도 지닌 사람일지 모르지만, 내 시절은 끝났어요. 하지만 댁은 다른 문제예요, 댁에겐 삶이 기다리고 있지요. 하기야, 누가 알겠어요? 어쩌면 댁의 삶도 연기처럼 흩어져 아무것도 아닌 게 되어 버릴지도 모르지요. 자, 댁이 다른 부류의 인간들 틈으로 넘어간들 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까? 댁이, 그런 마음을 지니고서, 아쉬워하는 게 안락은 아니잖아요! 어쩌면 오랫동안 아무도 댁을 보지 못한들 그게 무슨 상관이겠습니까? 그건 시간이 아니라 댁 자신이 결정할 일이에요. 태양이 되세요, 그러면 모두가 댁을 볼 겁니다. 태양은 무엇보다 먼저 태양이어야 하니까요. 왜 또 미소 짓습니까? 내가 이렇게 실러처럼 군다고요? 장담컨대 댁은 내가 아첨으로 댁을 구워삶으려 한다고 상상하고 있겠지요. 뭐, 어쩌면 그런지도 모르지요, 헤헤헤! 어쩌면 댁은 내 말을 안 믿는 게 나을지도, 어쩌면 영영 통째로는 믿지 않는 게 나을지도 몰라요 — 나는 그렇게 생겨먹었거든요, 인정합니다. 하지만 한마디 덧붙이지요, 댁 스스로 판단할 수 있을 거라고 봅니다, 내가 어느 정도 천박한 사람이고 어느 정도 정직한 사람인지를 말이지요.”\n\n“언제 나를 체포할 작정이오?”\n\n“뭐, 하루 이틀쯤 더 돌아다니게 둘 수 있지요. 잘 생각해 보세요, 친애하는 양반, 그리고 신께 기도하세요. 그게 댁에게 더 이롭습니다, 믿으세요.”\n\n“그런데 내가 달아나면요?” 라스콜니코프가 묘한 미소를 띠며 물었다.\n\n“아니, 댁은 달아나지 않을 겁니다. 농부라면 달아나겠지요, 유행을 좇는 분리파라면 달아날 거예요, 남의 생각의 하인이라면 말입니다, 그런 자에게는 새끼손가락 끝만 보여 줘도 평생 무엇이든 믿을 채비가 되거든요. 하지만 댁은 이미 자기 이론을 믿지 않게 됐는데, 무얼 들고 달아나겠습니까? 그리고 숨어서 무얼 하겠어요? 댁에겐 가증스럽고 괴로운 일일 거예요, 그리고 무엇보다 댁이 삶에서 필요로 하는 건 분명한 위치, 댁에게 어울리는 분위기지요. 한데 댁이 어떤 분위기를 얻겠습니까? 달아난다면 댁은 제 발로 돌아올 거예요. 댁은 우리 없이는 살아갈 수 없으니까요. 그리고 내가 댁을 감옥에 넣는다면 — 한 달, 두 달, 석 달쯤 거기 있었다고 칩시다 — 내 말을 기억하세요, 댁은 제 발로 자백할 겁니다, 어쩌면 댁 자신도 놀라면서 말이지요. 한 시간 전까지도 댁은 자기가 자백하러 가는 줄 모를 거예요. 나는 확신합니다, 댁이 ‘자기 수난을 받아들이기로’ 마음먹으리라는 걸요. 댁은 지금 내 말을 믿지 않지만, 제 발로 거기에 이르게 될 겁니다. 수난이란, 로디온 로마노비치, 위대한 것이거든요. 내가 살이 쪘다고 마음 쓰지 마세요, 그래도 나는 다 압니다. 비웃지 마세요, 수난 속엔 하나의 사상이 있어요, 니콜라이가 옳습니다. 아니에요, 댁은 달아나지 않을 겁니다, 로디온 로마노비치.”\n\n라스콜니코프가 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다. 포르피리 페트로비치도 일어섰다.\n\n“산책하러 가십니까? 폭풍만 안 친다면 저녁이 좋을 겁니다. 공기를 좀 식히는 것도 좋은 일이겠지요.”\n\n그도 모자를 집어 들었다.\n\n“포르피리 페트로비치, 제발 내가 오늘 당신에게 자백했다는 식으로 생각하지 마시오.” 라스콜니코프가 침울하게 다그치듯 잘라 말했다. “당신은 이상한 사람이고, 나는 그저 호기심에서 당신 말을 들었을 뿐이오. 하지만 나는 아무것도 인정하지 않았소, 그걸 기억하시오!”\n\n“오, 압니다, 기억하지요. 저 사람 좀 보게, 떨고 있군요! 불안해하지 마세요, 친애하는 양반, 댁 뜻대로 하세요. 좀 돌아다니세요, 너무 멀리 가지는 못하겠지만요. 만일 무슨 일이 생긴다면, 댁에게 한 가지 부탁이 있습니다.” 그가 목소리를 낮추며 덧붙였다. “난처한 부탁이지만, 중요한 일이에요. 만일 무슨 일이 생긴다면(하기야 정말 나는 그걸 믿지도 않고, 댁이 그럴 위인이 전혀 못 된다고 여깁니다만), 그래도 이 사오십 시간 사이에 댁이 어떤 다른 방식으로, 어떤 환상적인 식으로 이 일을 끝장내려는 — 스스로 목숨을 끊으려는 — 생각에 사로잡힌다면(터무니없는 가정이지만, 그 점은 용서해 주셔야겠어요), 짧지만 분명한 쪽지를, 단 두 줄이라도 남겨 주세요, 그리고 그 돌을 언급해 주시고요. 그게 더 너그러운 일일 겁니다. 자, 또 만날 때까지! 좋은 생각과 건전한 결단이 함께하기를!”\n\n포르피리는 몸을 구부린 채 라스콜니코프를 쳐다보지 않으면서 나갔다. 라스콜니코프는 창가로 가서, 포르피리가 거리에 다다라 멀어졌으리라고 계산될 때까지 짜증스러운 초조함을 안고 기다렸다. 그러고는 그도 서둘러 방을 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n그는 스비드리가일로프에게 서둘러 갔다. 그 사내에게서 무엇을 기대해야 할지 그는 알지 못했다. 그러나 그 사내는 그에게 어떤 은밀한 힘을 행사하고 있었다. 일단 이를 깨달은 이상, 그는 마음을 가라앉힐 수 없었고, 이제 그때가 온 것이다.\n\n가는 길에 한 가지 의문이 유난히 그를 괴롭혔다. 스비드리가일로프가 포르피리에게 다녀왔을까?\n\n그가 판단할 수 있는 한, 그는 결코 가지 않았다고 단언할 수 있었다. 그는 거듭거듭 곰곰이 생각하며 포르피리의 방문을 되짚어 보았다. 아니, 그는 가지 않았어, 물론 가지 않았지.\n\n그러나 아직 가지 않았다면, 앞으로 갈까? 한편으로 지금으로서는 그가 갈 수 없으리라 여겨졌다. 왜? 그는 설명할 수 없었으나, 설령 설명할 수 있다 해도 지금 이 순간 그 일에 골몰하지는 않았을 것이다. 그 모든 것이 그를 괴롭혔지만 동시에 거기에 마음을 쏟을 수도 없었다. 이상한 일이지만, 어쩌면 아무도 믿지 못할 일이지만, 그는 자신의 임박한 미래에 대해서는 그저 희미하고 막연한 불안만을 느꼈을 뿐이다. 또 다른, 훨씬 더 중요한 불안이 그를 괴롭혔다 — 그것은 그 자신에 관한 것이었으나, 다른, 더 절박한 방식으로 그러했다. 게다가 그는 엄청난 정신적 피로를 의식하고 있었다, 비록 그날 아침 그의 머리는 요사이보다 더 잘 돌아갔지만.\n\n그리고 그 모든 일이 일어난 마당에, 이런 새로운 하찮은 곤경들과 씨름할 가치가 과연 있었을까? 가령 스비드리가일로프가 포르피리에게 가지 못하도록 책략을 꾸미는 일이 가치가 있었을까? 사실을 조사하고 확인하느라, 스비드리가일로프 따위의 인간에게 시간을 낭비하는 일이 가치가 있었을까?\n\n오, 그는 이 모든 것에 얼마나 진저리가 났던가!\n\n그러면서도 그는 스비드리가일로프에게 서둘러 가고 있었다. 그에게서 무언가 새로운 것을, 정보나 탈출구를 기대하고 있었던 것일까? 인간이란 지푸라기라도 붙잡으려 하는 법이지! 그들을 한데 끌어모은 것은 운명이었을까, 아니면 어떤 본능이었을까? 어쩌면 그저 피로였을지도, 절망이었을지도. 어쩌면 그에게 필요한 사람은 스비드리가일로프가 아니라 다른 누군가였고, 스비드리가일로프는 그저 우연히 나타났을 뿐인지도 몰랐다. 소냐? 하지만 이제 와서 그가 소냐에게 무엇하러 가겠는가? 또다시 그녀의 눈물을 구걸하려고? 그는 소냐도 두려웠다. 소냐는 그 앞에 돌이킬 수 없는 선고처럼 서 있었다. 그는 자신의 길을 가든가 그녀의 길을 가든가 해야 했다. 특히 지금 이 순간 그는 그녀를 만날 엄두가 나지 않았다. 아니, 차라리 스비드리가일로프를 한번 시험해 보는 편이 낫지 않을까? 그리고 그는 어떤 이유에서인지 오래전부터 그를 꼭 만나야만 한다고 느껴 왔음을 속으로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n하지만 그들 사이에 무슨 공통점이 있을 수 있단 말인가? 그들의 악행조차 같은 종류일 수가 없었다. 게다가 그 사내는 무척 불쾌했고, 분명 타락했으며, 의심할 여지 없이 교활하고 기만적이었으며, 어쩌면 악의에 차 있었다. 그에 관해서는 그런 이야기들이 떠돌았다. 사실 그는 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이들을 거두어 주고 있었지만, 무슨 동기로 그러는지, 또 그게 무엇을 뜻하는지 누가 알겠는가? 그 사내에게는 늘 어떤 속셈이, 어떤 계획이 있었다.\n\n요사이 라스콜니코프의 마음속에 끊임없이 맴돌며 그를 몹시 불안하게 하는 또 다른 생각이 있었다. 그것이 어찌나 고통스러운지 그는 그것을 떨쳐 버리려 분명한 노력을 기울였다. 그는 이따금 스비드리가일로프가 자신의 뒤를 밟고 있는 게 아닐까 생각했다. 스비드리가일로프는 그의 비밀을 알아냈고, 두냐에 대해 흑심을 품고 있었다. 만일 그가 여전히 그 흑심을 품고 있다면? 그가 그러리라는 게 사실상 확실하지 않은가? 그리고 만일 그가 그의 비밀을 알아내어 그에 대한 힘을 얻은 뒤, 그것을 두냐를 겨누는 무기로 사용한다면?\n\n이 생각은 때로 그의 꿈마저 괴롭혔지만, 스비드리가일로프에게 가는 길에서만큼 생생하게 떠오른 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그 생각만으로도 그는 음울한 분노에 휩싸였다. 우선 이것은 모든 것을, 그 자신의 처지마저 뒤바꿔 놓을 터였다. 그는 당장 두냐에게 자신의 비밀을 고백해야만 할 것이다. 두냐가 어떤 무모한 짓을 저지르지 못하도록 어쩌면 자수까지 해야 할까? 그 편지? 오늘 아침 두냐는 편지 한 통을 받았다. 페테르부르크에서 누구한테 편지를 받을 수 있단 말인가? 어쩌면 루진일까? 사실 라주미힌이 곁에서 그녀를 지켜 주고 있었지만, 라주미힌은 이 사정을 아무것도 몰랐다. 어쩌면 라주미힌에게 알리는 것이 그의 의무였을까? 그는 혐오감을 느끼며 그 생각을 떠올렸다.\n\n어쨌든 그는 되도록 빨리 스비드리가일로프를 만나야 한다고, 마침내 결심했다. 다행히도 면담의 세세한 내용은 별로 중요하지 않았으니, 그저 문제의 핵심에 가닿을 수만 있다면 그만이었다. 그러나 만일 스비드리가일로프가 그럴 수 있는 자라면…… 만일 그가 두냐를 겨누어 음모를 꾸미고 있다면 — 그렇다면…….\n\n라스콜니코프는 그 한 달 동안 겪은 일들로 어찌나 지쳐 있었던지, 그런 문제들을 단 한 가지 방식으로밖에 결정할 수 없었다. '그렇다면 나는 그를 죽이겠어.' 그는 싸늘한 절망 속에서 생각했다.\n\n별안간 고뇌가 그의 가슴을 짓눌렀고, 그는 거리 한복판에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 자신이 어디에 있는지, 어느 쪽으로 가고 있는지 둘러보기 시작했다. 그는 자신이 X 거리에, 자기가 지나온 건초 시장에서 서른 걸음인가 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에 있음을 알아차렸다. 왼쪽 건물의 이 층 전체가 선술집으로 쓰이고 있었다. 창문은 모조리 활짝 열려 있었는데, 창가에서 움직이는 사람들의 모습으로 미루어 보아 방 안은 넘쳐 날 만큼 사람들로 가득 차 있었다. 노랫소리며 클라리넷과 바이올린 소리, 터키 북의 둥둥거리는 울림이 들려왔다. 여자들이 꽥꽥 소리 지르는 것도 들렸다. 그는 도대체 무엇 때문에 X 거리에 왔을까 의아해하며 막 돌아서려던 참이었는데, 갑자기 맨 끝 창문 하나에서 스비드리가일로프가 활짝 열린 창가의 찻상 앞에 앉아 입에 파이프를 문 채 있는 것을 보았다. 라스콜니코프는 끔찍하게 당황했고, 거의 겁에 질렸다. 스비드리가일로프는 말없이 그를 지켜보며 뜯어보고 있었는데, 라스콜니코프를 단번에 놀라게 한 것은, 그가 자리에서 일어나 들키지 않게 슬그머니 사라지려는 듯 보였다는 점이었다. 라스콜니코프는 곧장 그를 보지 못한 척, 멍하니 딴 데를 보는 척하면서 곁눈질로 그를 살폈다. 그의 가슴은 격렬하게 뛰었다. 그러나 스비드리가일로프가 들키고 싶어 하지 않는다는 것은 분명했다. 그는 입에서 파이프를 빼고 몸을 숨기려던 참이었으나, 자리에서 일어나 의자를 뒤로 밀치다가, 라스콜니코프가 자신을 보았고 또 지켜보고 있음을 갑자기 알아차린 듯했다. 두 사람 사이에 오간 것은 라스콜니코프의 방에서 처음 만났을 때 일어났던 일과 거의 같았다. 스비드리가일로프의 얼굴에 교활한 미소가 떠올라 점점 더 크게 번져 갔다. 두 사람은 저마다 자신이 상대에게 들켰고 또 지켜보이고 있음을 알았다. 마침내 스비드리가일로프가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"이런, 이런, 내게 볼일이 있거든 들어오시구려. 나 여기 있소!\" 그가 창에서 소리쳤다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 선술집으로 올라갔다. 그는 스비드리가일로프를 어느 자그마한 안쪽 방에서 찾았는데, 그 방은 상인이며 점원이며 온갖 부류의 사람들이 합창단의 필사적인 고함 소리에 맞춰 스무 개쯤 되는 작은 탁자에 둘러앉아 차를 마시고 있는 큰 홀에 붙어 있었다. 멀리서 당구공 부딪치는 소리가 들렸다. 스비드리가일로프 앞 탁자에는 마개를 딴 병 하나와 샴페인이 반쯤 찬 잔 하나가 놓여 있었다. 방 안에는 또 작은 손풍금을 든 소년 하나와, 걷어 올린 줄무늬 치마에 리본 달린 티롤식 모자를 쓴, 건강해 보이고 볼이 발그레한 열여덟 살가량의 처녀가 있었다. 옆방의 합창에도 아랑곳없이, 그녀는 풍금 반주에 맞춰 다소 쉰 듯한 콘트랄토로 어떤 하인들이나 부를 법한 노래를 부르고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 그만하면 됐다.\" 라스콜니코프가 들어서자 스비드리가일로프가 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다. 처녀는 곧바로 노래를 그치고 공손하게 서서 기다렸다. 그녀는 그 목쉰 가락의 노래마저 진지하고 공손한 표정으로 부르고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"이봐, 필립, 잔 하나 가져와!\" 스비드리가일로프가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"저는 아무것도 안 마십니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 말했다.\n\n\"좋을 대로 하시오, 댁더러 마시라는 게 아니니까. 마셔라, 카탸! 오늘은 더 필요한 게 없으니 가도 좋다.\" 그는 그녀에게 잔을 가득 따라 주고는 노란 지폐 한 장을 내려놓았다.\n\n카탸는 여자들이 으레 그러듯 잔을 내려놓지도 않은 채 스무 모금에 와인을 단숨에 비우고는, 지폐를 받아 들고 스비드리가일로프의 손에 입을 맞추었는데, 그는 자못 진지하게 그것을 받아들였다. 그녀가 방에서 나가자 소년도 풍금을 끌며 그 뒤를 따라갔다. 둘 다 거리에서 불러들인 자들이었다. 스비드리가일로프는 페테르부르크에 온 지 일주일도 채 안 되었지만, 그를 둘러싼 모든 것은 이미, 말하자면 가부장적인 기반 위에 놓여 있었다. 웨이터인 필립은 어느새 오랜 친구처럼 굴며 무척 비굴하게 알랑거렸다.\n\n홀로 통하는 문에는 자물쇠가 달려 있었다. 스비드리가일로프는 이 방에서 제집처럼 편안히 지냈고, 어쩌면 온종일 이 안에서 보내는지도 몰랐다. 그 선술집은 더럽고 누추했으며, 이류 축에도 못 끼었다.\n\n\"당신을 만나러 가서 찾던 참이었소.\" 라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다. \"그런데 무엇 때문인지 지금 막 건초 시장에서 X 거리 쪽으로 꺾어 들고 말았소. 나는 이 길로는 한 번도 꺾어 든 적이 없는데. 늘 건초 시장에서 오른쪽으로 도는데 말이오. 게다가 이 길은 당신에게 가는 길도 아니고. 그저 무심코 꺾어 들었더니 당신이 여기 있었던 거요. 이상한 일이군!\"\n\n\"왜 단번에 '기적이다'라고 말하지 않으시오?\"\n\n\"그저 우연일 수도 있으니까.\"\n\n\"오, 댁 같은 사람들은 다 그렇지.\" 스비드리가일로프가 웃었다. \"속으로는 기적이라고 믿으면서도 절대 인정하려 들지 않거든! 여기서는 우연일 수도 있다고 말하고 말이오. 한데 여기 사람들이 자기 나름의 의견을 갖는 일에 얼마나 겁쟁이인지, 댁은 상상도 못 할 거요, 로디온 로마노비치. 댁 얘기가 아니오, 댁은 자기 나름의 의견을 갖고 있고 또 그걸 가지길 두려워하지 않으니까. 바로 그 점이 내 호기심을 끌었던 거요.\"\n\n\"그것뿐이오?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그거면 충분하지 않소.\" 스비드리가일로프는 보아하니 들떠 있었지만, 그저 약간 그럴 뿐이었다. 그는 와인을 반 잔도 채 마시지 않았으니까.\n\n\"내가 당신이 말하는 그 자기 나름의 의견이라는 걸 가질 수 있는 인간임을 당신이 알기도 전에, 당신은 나를 만나러 왔던 것 같은데.\" 라스콜니코프가 지적했다.\n\n\"오, 뭐, 그건 별개의 문제요. 누구나 제 나름의 속셈이 있는 법이니까. 그리고 기적 얘기가 나온 김에 한마디 하자면, 내 생각에 댁은 지난 이삼 일을 잠에 취해 보낸 것 같소. 이 선술집은 내가 직접 댁한테 일러 줬으니, 댁이 곧장 여기로 온 데에는 아무 기적도 없는 거요. 가는 길도 내가 직접 설명해 주었고, 여기가 어딘지, 또 몇 시에 나를 여기서 만날 수 있는지도 말해 줬소. 기억나시오?\"\n\n\"기억나지 않는데.\" 라스콜니코프가 놀라며 대답했다.\n\n\"댁 말을 믿겠소. 내가 두 번이나 말했는데. 그 주소가 댁의 기억 속에 기계적으로 새겨진 거요. 댁은 기계적으로 이 길로 꺾어 들었으면서도, 정작 자신은 알지 못한 채 정확히 그 방향대로 온 셈이지. 그때 내가 말했을 때, 댁이 내 말을 알아들었으리라곤 거의 기대하지 않았소. 댁은 자기 속을 너무 많이 드러낸단 말이오, 로디온 로마노비치. 그리고 또 한 가지, 나는 페테르부르크에 길을 걸으면서 혼잣말을 하는 사람이 수두룩하다고 확신하오. 여기는 미친 사람들의 도시요. 만일 우리에게 학자들만 있다면, 의사며 법률가며 철학자들이 페테르부르크에서 저마다 제 분야의 더없이 값진 연구를 해낼 수 있을 텐데. 페테르부르크만큼 인간의 영혼에 그토록 음울하고 강렬하고 기이한 영향을 끼치는 곳도 드물지. 기후의 영향만 해도 얼마나 큰지 모르오. 게다가 여기는 온 러시아의 행정 중심지이니, 그 성격이 온 나라에 반영될 수밖에 없소. 하지만 지금 그건 여기서 따질 문제가 아니지. 요점은 내가 여러 번 댁을 지켜봤다는 거요. 댁은 집을 나설 때면 — 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 — 집에서 스무 걸음쯤 가다가 고개를 떨구고 두 손을 등 뒤로 모으지. 댁은 바라보면서도 분명 앞도 옆도 아무것도 못 보고 있소. 마침내 입술을 움직이며 혼잣말을 하기 시작하고, 때로는 한 손을 휘저으며 무언가를 읊조리다가, 끝내는 길 한복판에 우뚝 멈춰 서기도 하지. 그건 전혀 좋은 일이 아니오. 나 말고 또 누군가 댁을 지켜보고 있을지도 모르고, 그게 댁한테 이로울 게 없으니까. 사실 나하곤 아무 상관도 없고, 나는 댁을 고쳐 줄 수도 없지만, 물론 댁은 내 말을 알아듣겠지.\"\n\n\"내가 미행당하고 있는 걸 당신이 아시오?\" 라스콜니코프가 캐묻듯 그를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 그런 건 전혀 모르오.\" 스비드리가일로프가 놀란 듯 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 좋소, 내 얘긴 그만둡시다.\" 라스콜니코프가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"좋소, 댁 얘긴 그만두지.\"\n\n\"그보다 말해 보시오, 당신이 여기 술을 마시러 오는 거고, 내게 두 번이나 여기로 당신을 찾아오라고 일러 줬다면, 방금 내가 거리에서 창문을 올려다봤을 때 왜 몸을 숨기고 빠져나가려 했던 거요? 내 눈으로 똑똑히 봤소.\"\n\n\"헤헤! 그러는 댁은 왜 내가 댁의 방문간에 서 있는 동안, 멀쩡히 깨어 있으면서도 눈을 감고 소파에 누워 자는 척했던 거요? 내 눈으로 똑똑히 봤소.\"\n\n\"내겐…… 그럴 만한 이유가 있었을 수도 있지. 그건 당신 자신이 알 거요.\"\n\n\"나 역시 내 나름의 이유가 있었을 수도 있지, 비록 댁은 모르겠지만.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고 오른손 손가락에 턱을 받친 채, 스비드리가일로프를 뚫어지게 응시했다. 그는 꼬박 일 분 동안, 전에도 자신에게 인상 깊었던 그의 얼굴을 뜯어보았다. 그것은 가면처럼 기이한 얼굴이었다. 희고도 붉었으며, 새빨간 입술에, 아마빛 턱수염과 아직도 숱 많은 아마빛 머리칼을 지니고 있었다. 그의 눈은 어딘가 지나치게 푸르렀고, 그 표정은 어딘가 지나치게 무겁고 굳어 있었다. 나이에 비해 놀랍도록 젊어 보이는 그 잘생긴 얼굴에는 무언가 끔찍하게 불쾌한 데가 있었다. 스비드리가일로프는 가벼운 여름옷을 맵시 있게 차려입었고, 특히 셔츠가 유난히 깔끔했다. 그는 보석이 박힌 커다란 반지를 끼고 있었다.\n\n\"이제 나는 당신 일까지 신경 써야 하는 거요?\" 라스콜니코프가 신경질적인 조급함으로 곧장 본론으로 들어가며 불쑥 말했다. \"당신이 나를 해치려 들기만 하면 어쩌면 가장 위험한 인간일지도 모르지만, 나는 더 이상 나를 괴롭히고 싶지 않소. 당신이 짐작하는 것만큼 내가 나 자신을 소중히 여기지 않는다는 걸 당장 보여 주겠소. 내가 단도직입적으로 말하러 온 거요. 만일 당신이 내 누이에 관해 예전의 의도를 그대로 품고 있다면, 그리고 요사이 밝혀진 일에서 그쪽으로 무슨 이득을 끌어내려 한다면, 당신이 나를 쇠고랑 채우기 전에 내가 당신을 죽여 버리겠소. 내 말을 믿어도 좋소. 내가 그 말을 지킬 수 있는 인간임을 당신도 알 테니까. 그리고 둘째로, 만일 당신이 내게 무슨 할 말이 있다면 — 줄곧 당신이 내게 뭔가 할 말이 있는 것 같은 느낌이 들었으니 — 어서 말하시오, 시간이 귀하고, 자칫하면 곧 너무 늦어 버릴 테니까.\"\n\n\"왜 그리 서두르시오?\" 스비드리가일로프가 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"누구나 제 나름의 속셈이 있는 법이오.\" 라스콜니코프가 음울하고 조급하게 대답했다.\n\n\"방금 댁이 직접 내게 솔직하라고 다그쳐 놓고, 첫 질문부터 대답을 거부하시는구려.\" 스비드리가일로프가 미소를 지으며 지적했다. \"댁은 내가 무슨 꿍꿍이를 품고 있다고 자꾸만 생각하니까 의심의 눈초리로 나를 바라보는 거요. 물론 댁의 처지에서는 지극히 자연스러운 일이지. 하지만 내가 댁과 친구가 되고 싶다 해도, 굳이 그 반대를 증명하느라 애쓰진 않겠소. 그럴 만한 가치도 없거니와, 나는 댁한테 무슨 특별한 얘기를 하려던 것도 아니었으니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 도대체 무엇 때문에 나를 찾았던 거요? 내 주위를 어슬렁거린 건 당신 쪽이었는데.\"\n\n\"뭐, 그저 흥미로운 관찰 대상이라서. 댁이 처한 처지의 그 환상적인 성격이 마음에 들었던 거요 — 바로 그거였소! 게다가 댁은 내 흥미를 무척 돋우었던 어떤 사람의 오라비이고, 그 사람한테서 나는 예전에 댁에 관해 아주 많은 얘기를 들었는데, 그걸로 미루어 댁이 그 사람한테 큰 영향력을 갖고 있다는 걸 알았소. 그만하면 충분하지 않소? 하하하! 그래도 인정하건대 댁의 질문은 꽤 복잡해서 내가 대답하기 어렵소. 자, 가령 댁은 내게 뚜렷한 목적이 있어서만이 아니라, 무언가 새로운 얘기를 들으려고 온 거요. 안 그렇소? 안 그렇소?\" 스비드리가일로프가 교활한 미소를 지으며 집요하게 물었다. \"그렇다면 댁은 나 역시 여기 오는 기차 안에서 댁을 셈에 넣고 있었다고는, 댁이 내게 뭔가 새로운 얘기를 해 주고, 내가 댁한테서 무슨 이득을 좀 보리라고 기대했다고는 상상도 못 하겠소? 우리가 얼마나 부유한 인간들인지 알겠소!\"\n\n\"당신이 무슨 이득을 볼 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"내가 어찌 말하겠소? 내가 어찌 알겠소? 보다시피 나는 이런 선술집에서 온종일을 보내고, 그게 내 낙이오. 그러니까 대단한 낙은 아니지만, 어딘가에는 앉아 있어야 하니까. 저 가엾은 카탸만 해도 — 댁도 봤지?…… 차라리 내가 대식가라면, 미식가 클럽의 한량이라면 모를까, 보다시피 나는 이런 걸 먹을 수 있는 사람이오.\"\n\n그는 구석에 놓인 작은 탁자를 가리켰는데, 거기 양철 접시 위에는 끔찍해 보이는 비프스테이크와 감자 찌꺼기가 남아 있었다.\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 식사는 했소? 나는 뭘 좀 먹어서 더는 아무것도 필요 없소. 가령 나는 술을 전혀 안 마시오. 샴페인 말고는 아무것도 입에 대지 않고, 그것도 저녁 내내 한 잔 이상은 안 마시는데, 그것마저 머리가 아파질 정도라오. 방금 이걸 시킨 건 기운을 좀 돋우려는 거였소, 이제 막 어디로 갈 참인 데다 댁이 보다시피 내 심사가 야릇한 상태라서. 그래서 아까 학생 녀석처럼 몸을 숨겼던 거요, 댁이 나를 방해할까 봐 겁이 났거든. 하지만 내 생각엔,\" 그는 회중시계를 꺼냈다, \"댁과 한 시간쯤은 보낼 수 있을 것 같소. 지금 네 시 반이오. 만일 내가 뭐라도 됐더라면, 지주든, 한 집안의 가장이든, 기병 장교든, 사진사든, 신문기자든…… 나는 아무것도 아니오, 아무 전문 분야도 없고, 그래서 때로는 정말이지 따분해 죽을 지경이오. 정말이지 댁이 내게 무슨 새로운 얘기를 해 주리라 생각했는데.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 도대체 뭐 하는 사람이고, 무엇 때문에 여기 온 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 뭐 하는 사람이냐고? 알다시피 신사지, 기병대에서 이 년 복무했고, 그다음엔 여기 페테르부르크에서 빈둥거렸고, 그러다 마르파 페트로브나와 결혼해서 시골에서 살았소. 자, 이게 내 약력이오!\"\n\n\"당신은 노름꾼이지, 아마?\"\n\n\"아니, 변변찮은 노름꾼이오. 야바위꾼이지 — 노름꾼이 아니라.\"\n\n\"그럼 야바위꾼 노릇을 했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 야바위도 했었지.\"\n\n\"그러다 가끔 두들겨 맞기도 했소?\"\n\n\"그런 적도 있었지. 왜 그러시오?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그자들한테 결투를 신청할 수도 있었을 테고…… 어쨌든 꽤나 활기찼겠군.\"\n\n\"댁 말을 반박하진 않겠소, 게다가 나는 철학엔 영 소질이 없으니. 고백하건대 나는 여자들 때문에 이곳으로 서둘러 왔소.\"\n\n\"마르파 페트로브나를 땅에 묻자마자?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 스비드리가일로프가 사람을 끌어당기는 솔직함으로 미소 지었다. \"그게 어쨌단 말이오? 내가 여자들에 대해 그렇게 말하는 데에서 댁은 뭔가 잘못된 점을 찾는 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"내가 악덕에서 뭔가 잘못된 점을 찾느냐고 묻는 거요?\"\n\n\"악덕! 오, 그게 댁의 관심사로군! 하지만 차근차근 대답해 주겠소, 우선 여자 일반에 관해서 말이지. 알다시피 나는 떠드는 걸 좋아하오. 말해 보시오, 내가 무엇 때문에 자제해야 하오? 여자들에게 열정을 품고 있는데, 무엇하러 그들을 단념해야 하오? 어쨌든 그건 하나의 소일거리니까.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 여기서 악덕 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않는단 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 좋소, 그럼 악덕이라 칩시다. 댁이 그걸 악덕이라 우기니까. 하지만 어쨌든 나는 단도직입적인 질문이 좋소. 적어도 이 악덕에는 무언가 영속적인 것이, 실로 본성에 뿌리박혀 환상에 좌우되지 않는 무언가가 있단 말이오, 피 속에 영원히 타오르는 불씨처럼 깃들어, 언제까지나 사람을 불태우며, 어쩌면 세월이 흘러도 쉽사리 꺼지지 않을 무언가가. 그것도 일종의 소일거리임은 댁도 인정할 거요.\"\n\n\"기뻐할 일이 못 되오, 그건 병이고, 그것도 위험한 병이오.\"\n\n\"오, 댁은 그렇게 생각하는군! 인정하오, 그게 절도를 넘어서는 모든 것이 그러하듯 병이라는 걸. 그리고 물론 이 일에선 절도를 넘어설 수밖에 없지. 하지만 첫째로, 누구나 이런저런 방식으로 그러는 법이고, 둘째로, 물론 아무리 비루하더라도 절제하고 신중해야 하겠지만, 그런데 나더러 어쩌란 말이오? 만일 내게 이게 없다면, 나는 차라리 총으로 자살이라도 해야 할 거요. 점잖은 사람은 따분함을 견뎌야 한다는 걸 나도 기꺼이 인정하지만, 그래도…….\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 자살할 수도 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 그만!\" 스비드리가일로프가 혐오스럽다는 듯 받아쳤다. \"제발 그 얘긴 하지 마시오.\" 그는 서둘러, 앞서 대화 내내 보였던 그 허세 어린 어조 따위는 전혀 없이 덧붙였다. 그의 얼굴빛이 완전히 변했다. \"용서받지 못할 약점임은 인정하지만, 나로서도 어쩔 수가 없소. 나는 죽음이 두렵고, 그게 입에 오르는 게 싫소. 내가 어느 정도는 신비주의자라는 걸 아시오?\"\n\n\"아, 마르파 페트로브나의 유령 말이오! 그게 아직도 당신을 찾아오고 있소?\"\n\n\"오, 그것들 얘긴 하지 마시오, 페테르부르크에선 더 이상 나타나지 않았으니까, 빌어먹을 것들!\" 그가 짜증스러운 기색으로 외쳤다. \"차라리 그 얘기나…… 하지만…… 흠! 시간이 많지 않아 댁과 오래 있을 수 없소, 안타깝군! 댁한테 들려줄 얘기가 잔뜩 있었는데.\"\n\n\"무슨 약속이오, 여자요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 여자, 어쩌다 생긴 일이라…… 아니, 내가 하려던 얘긴 그게 아니오.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신을 둘러싼 이 모든 것의 추악함, 그 더러움이 당신을 괴롭히지 않소? 당신은 스스로를 멈출 힘을 잃어버린 거요?\"\n\n\"그러는 댁은 무슨 힘이라도 있는 척하시는 거요? 헤헤헤! 댁은 방금 나를 놀라게 했소, 로디온 로마노비치, 비록 그러리라고 미리 알고는 있었지만. 댁이 내게 악덕이며 미학을 설교하다니! 댁이 — 실러라니, 댁이 — 이상주의자라니! 물론 다 마땅히 그래야 할 일이고, 그렇지 않다면 오히려 놀라운 일이겠지만, 그래도 실제로 보니 기이하군…… 아, 시간이 없어 정말 안타깝소, 댁은 더없이 흥미로운 인간형이니까! 그건 그렇고, 댁은 실러를 좋아하시오? 나는 그를 끔찍이도 좋아한다오.\"\n\n\"당신은 정말 허풍쟁이로군.\" 라스콜니코프가 다소 혐오스럽다는 듯 말했다.\n\n\"맹세컨대 나는 그렇지 않소.\" 스비드리가일로프가 웃으며 대답했다. \"하지만 굳이 다투진 않겠소, 그래, 내가 허풍쟁이라 칩시다, 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않는다면 허풍을 못 떨 이유가 어디 있겠소? 나는 마르파 페트로브나와 함께 시골에서 칠 년을 보냈으니, 이제 댁처럼 영리한 사람을 — 영리하고도 더없이 흥미로운 사람을 — 만나고 보니, 그저 떠드는 게 즐거울 따름이오, 게다가 그 반 잔의 샴페인을 마셨더니 머리가 좀 핑 도는구려. 그리고 또, 나를 굉장히 흥분시킨 어떤 일이 하나 있는데, 그것에 관해서는 나…… 입을 다물겠소. 어디 가시오?\" 그가 깜짝 놀라 물었다.\n\n라스콜니코프가 일어서기 시작했던 것이다. 그는 답답하고 숨이 막혔으며, 어쩐지 여기 온 것이 영 불편하게 느껴졌다. 그는 스비드리가일로프가 이 세상에서 가장 하찮은 악당이라고 확신했다.\n\n\"아아! 앉으시오, 좀 더 있다 가시오!\" 스비드리가일로프가 간청했다. \"어쨌든 차라도 좀 가져오게 하리다. 좀 더 있어 보시오, 헛소리는 안 하리다, 그러니까 내 얘기 말이오. 댁한테 들려줄 게 있소. 원한다면, 댁이라면 어떻게 말할까, 한 여자가 어떻게 나를 '구원'하려 했는지 들려주겠소. 그건 정말이지 댁의 첫 질문에 대한 답이 될 거요, 그 여자가 바로 댁의 누이였으니까. 들려줘도 되겠소? 시간 보내기에 도움이 될 거요.\"\n\n\"말해 보시오, 하지만 부디 당신이…….\"\n\n\"오, 불안해 마시오. 게다가 나처럼 하찮고 천한 인간한테도 아브도탸 로마노브나는 그저 가장 깊은 존경심만을 불러일으킬 뿐이오.\"\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n\"아마 당신도 알 거요 — 그래, 내가 직접 말했지.\" 스비드리가일로프가 입을 열었다. \"내가 여기 채무자 감옥에 갇혀 있었다는 거 말이오, 엄청난 액수의 빚 때문에, 그걸 갚을 가망이라곤 전혀 없었지. 마르파 페트로브나가 어떻게 나를 빼내 줬는지 시시콜콜 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠고. 여자가 때로 얼마나 미친 지경에까지 이르도록 사랑할 수 있는지 아시오? 그 여자는 정직한 여자였고, 아주 분별 있는 사람이었소, 비록 교육이라곤 전혀 못 받았지만. 이 정직하고 질투심 많은 여자가, 숱한 히스테리와 비난의 소동 끝에, 나와 일종의 계약을 맺는 데까지 굽히고 들어왔다면 믿겠소? 그러고는 결혼 생활 내내 그걸 지켰단 말이오. 그 여자는 나보다 한참 연상이었고, 게다가 늘 입에 정향이나 뭐 그런 걸 물고 있었지. 내 영혼 속에는 돼지 같은 추잡함이 가득했지만, 또 나름의 정직함도 있어서, 그 여자한테 당신한테 절대적으로 충실할 수는 없다고 대놓고 말해 버렸소. 이 고백에 그 여자는 미칠 듯이 날뛰었지만, 그래도 어쩐 일인지 내 짐승 같은 솔직함을 마음에 들어 한 모양이오. 이렇게 미리 경고하는 걸 보니 자기를 속일 마음은 없는 거라고 여긴 거지. 알다시피 질투심 많은 여자한테는 그게 무엇보다 중요한 거니까. 숱한 눈물 끝에 우리 사이에 명문화되지 않은 계약이 맺어졌소. 첫째, 나는 절대로 마르파 페트로브나를 떠나지 않으며 늘 그녀의 남편으로 남는다. 둘째, 나는 그녀의 허락 없이는 절대로 집을 비우지 않는다. 셋째, 나는 절대로 고정된 정부를 두지 않는다. 넷째, 그 대가로 마르파 페트로브나는 하녀들에 한해서는 나에게 자유를 허락하되, 오직 그녀가 은밀히 아는 한에서만 그렇게 한다. 다섯째, 우리 계급의 여자와 사랑에 빠지는 일은 결단코 안 된다. 여섯째, 만에 하나 내가 — 천만에 그럴 리야 없겠지만 — 어떤 크고 진지한 정념에 사로잡힌다면, 나는 그것을 마르파 페트로브나에게 반드시 털어놓아야 한다. 하지만 이 마지막 조항에 관해서라면 마르파 페트로브나는 꽤나 마음이 놓였던 모양이오. 그녀는 분별 있는 여자였으니, 나를 진정한 사랑이라곤 할 줄 모르는 방탕한 난봉꾼으로 볼 수밖에 없었던 거지. 그러나 분별 있는 여자와 질투심 많은 여자는 완전히 다른 두 가지요, 바로 거기서 골치 아픈 일이 생긴 거지. 하지만 어떤 사람들을 공정하게 판단하려면 우리는 어떤 선입견들과, 우리 주변의 평범한 사람들을 대하는 습관적인 태도를 버려야만 하오. 나는 다른 누구보다도 당신의 판단을 믿을 만한 이유가 있소. 아마 당신은 이미 마르파 페트로브나에 관해 우스꽝스럽고 터무니없는 얘기를 잔뜩 들었을 거요. 그 여자한테 분명 아주 우스꽝스러운 면들이 있긴 했지. 하지만 솔직히 말해서, 내가 원인이 됐던 그 헤아릴 수 없는 슬픔들에 대해 나는 정말로 마음이 아프오. 자, 이만하면 더없이 다정한 남편의 더없이 다정한 아내를 위한 점잖은 추도사로는 충분할 것 같군. 우리가 다툴 때면, 나는 대개 입을 다물고 그녀를 자극하지 않았는데, 그 신사다운 처신은 좀처럼 목적을 이루지 못하는 법이 없었소. 그게 그녀를 감화시켰고, 사실 그녀를 흡족하게 했지. 그럴 때면 그녀는 정말로 나를 자랑스러워했소. 하지만 당신 누이만큼은 도무지 견뎌 내지 못했다오. 도대체 그렇게 아름다운 여자를 가정교사로 자기 집에 들이는 모험을 어쩌자고 한 건지. 내 설명은 이렇소. 마르파 페트로브나는 열정적이고 감수성이 예민한 여자라서, 그저 자기가 직접 사랑에 빠진 거요 — 문자 그대로 사랑에 빠진 거지 — 당신 누이한테 말이오. 뭐, 놀랄 일도 아니지 — 아브도티야 로마노브나를 한번 보시오! 나는 첫눈에 위험을 알아챘고, 그래서 어떻게 했는지 아시오, 그녀를 쳐다보지조차 않기로 결심했소. 그런데 아브도티야 로마노브나가 먼저 첫걸음을 내디뎠다면, 믿겠소? 또 이건 믿겠소? 마르파 페트로브나가 처음엔 내가 당신 누이에 대해 끈질기게 침묵하는 것 때문에, 아브도티야 로마노브나를 향한 끝없는 찬미의 칭송을 내가 시큰둥하게 받아넘기는 것 때문에 나한테 정말로 화를 냈단 말이오. 도대체 그 여자가 뭘 원했던 건지 모르겠소! 뭐, 물론 마르파 페트로브나는 아브도티야 로마노브나한테 나에 관한 시시콜콜한 것까지 다 얘기했소. 그 여자한텐 우리 집안의 비밀이란 비밀은 문자 그대로 모두에게 떠벌리고 끊임없이 나에 대해 불평하는 불행한 버릇이 있었으니, 그렇게 사랑스러운 새 친구한테야 어찌 털어놓지 않았겠소? 짐작건대 그들은 나 말고는 다른 얘기는 하지도 않았을 테고, 의심할 바 없이 아브도티야 로마노브나는 나에 대해 떠도는 그 온갖 어둡고 음산한 소문들을 다 들었을 거요……. 당신도 이미 그런 비슷한 걸 들었을 거라고 내기를 걸어도 좋소만?\"\n\n\"들었소. 루진이 당신더러 어떤 아이를 죽음에 이르게 했다고 비난하더군. 그게 사실이오?\"\n\n\"제발, 그런 천박한 얘기는 끄집어내지 마시오.\" 스비드리가일로프가 혐오와 짜증을 드러내며 말했다. \"그 모든 멍청한 일에 대해 기어이 알고 싶다면, 언젠가 말해 주겠소, 하지만 지금은…….\"\n\n\"당신이 시골에서 어떤 하인을 학대했다는 얘기도 들었소.\"\n\n\"제발 그 얘긴 그만둡시다.\" 스비드리가일로프가 또다시 노골적인 짜증을 내며 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"그게 죽은 뒤에 당신한테 와서 당신 파이프에 담배를 채워 줬다던 그 하인이오?…… 당신이 직접 나한테 얘기했잖소.\" 라스콜니코프는 점점 더 짜증이 치밀었다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 그를 유심히 바라보았고, 라스콜니코프는 그 시선 속에서 심술궂은 조롱의 번득임을 언뜻 잡아챈 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그러나 스비드리가일로프는 자제하며 아주 정중하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇소, 바로 그 하인이오. 보아하니 당신도 무척 흥미가 동하는 모양이니, 기회가 닿는 대로 당신의 호기심을 채워 주는 게 내 의무라고 여기겠소. 맹세컨대! 보아하니 내가 정말 어떤 사람들한테는 낭만적인 인물로 통할 수도 있겠군. 마르파 페트로브나가 아브도티야 로마노브나한테 나에 관한 그토록 신비롭고 흥미로운 소문을 그대로 옮겨 준 것에 대해 내가 얼마나 고마워해야 할지 한번 헤아려 보시오. 그게 그녀한테 어떤 인상을 남겼을지는 감히 짐작도 못 하겠지만, 어쨌든 그건 내 이익에 보탬이 됐소. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 천성적으로 나를 혐오하고, 또 내가 변함없이 음울하고 거부감을 주는 인상이었음에도 불구하고 — 그녀는 적어도 나를 가엾게는 여겼소, 길 잃은 영혼을 향한 연민 말이오. 그리고 처녀의 마음이 일단 연민으로 움직이고 나면, 그건 무엇보다 위험한 거요. 그녀는 반드시 '그를 구원하고', 그를 정신 차리게 하고, 그를 일으켜 세워 더 고귀한 목표로 이끌고, 그를 새로운 삶과 쓸모 있는 존재로 되돌려 놓고 싶어 하게 마련이거든 — 뭐, 그런 꿈이 어디까지 갈 수 있는지야 우리 모두 알지 않소. 나는 그 새가 제 발로 새장 안으로 날아드는 걸 단박에 알아챘소. 그래서 나도 만반의 준비를 했지. 당신, 얼굴을 찌푸리는 것 같은데, 로지온 로마노비치? 그럴 필요 없소. 알다시피, 결국 다 물거품이 되고 말았으니까. (제기랄, 내가 술을 어지간히 마시는군!) 한데 말이오, 나는 처음부터 줄곧, 당신 누이가 서기 이삼 세기에, 군림하던 어느 군주나 소아시아의 어느 총독이나 부집정관의 딸로 태어나지 못한 게 늘 안타까웠소. 그녀는 틀림없이 순교를 견뎌 낼 사람들 중 하나였을 테고, 시뻘겋게 달군 집게로 가슴을 지질 때조차 미소를 지었을 거요. 그것도 제 발로 그리로 걸어갔겠지. 그리고 사오 세기였다면 이집트 사막으로 걸어 들어가 거기서 삼십 년을 풀뿌리와 황홀경과 환영으로 연명하며 머물렀을 거요. 그녀는 그저 누군가를 위해 어떤 고난과 맞서기를 갈망하고 있는 거고, 만약 그 고난을 얻지 못하면 창밖으로 몸을 던질 거요. 라주미힌이라는 분에 대해 좀 들었는데 — 분별 있는 친구라더군. 하긴 성씨부터가 그걸 암시하지 않소. 아마 신학생일 거요. 뭐, 그 친구가 당신 누이를 잘 돌보는 게 좋을 거요! 나는 그녀를 이해한다고 믿고, 또 그걸 자랑스럽게 여기오. 하지만 알다시피 사귐의 처음에는 누구나 더 부주의하고 어리석어지게 마련이지. 분명하게 보질 못하는 거요. 제기랄, 그녀는 왜 그렇게 아름다운 거요? 내 잘못은 아니잖소. 사실 내 쪽에서는 도저히 거역할 수 없는 육체적 욕망으로 시작됐소. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 무서울 만큼 순결한 여자요, 믿기지 않을 정도로, 경이로울 정도로. 명심하시오, 당신 누이에 관해 이걸 사실로서 말하는 거요. 그녀는 그 넓은 지성에도 불구하고 거의 병적일 만큼 순결한데, 그게 그녀의 앞길에 걸림돌이 될 거요. 그때 마침 집에 한 처녀가 있었소, 파라샤라고, 까만 눈의 계집애였는데, 나로선 처음 보는 얼굴이었지 — 막 다른 마을에서 온 참이었거든 — 아주 예뻤지만 믿기지 않을 만큼 멍청했소. 그애가 그만 울음을 터뜨리고는, 온 집 안에 들리도록 통곡을 해서 추문을 일으켰지. 어느 날 저녁 식사 후에 아브도티야 로마노브나가 정원 산책로로 나를 따라 나와서는, 눈을 번득이며 가엾은 파라샤를 가만 내버려 두라고 다그쳤소. 그게 우리 단둘이 나눈 거의 첫 대화였지. 나야 물론 그녀의 뜻을 따르는 게 더없이 기뻤으니, 당황하고 난처한 척하면서, 사실 내 역할을 제법 그럴듯하게 해냈소. 그러고 나서 만남이 이어졌소, 은밀한 대화들, 훈계, 간청, 애원, 심지어 눈물까지 — 믿겠소, 눈물까지 흘렸다오? 어떤 처녀들이 전도에 대한 열정 때문에 어디까지 가게 되는지 생각해 보시오! 나는 물론 모든 걸 내 운명 탓으로 돌렸고, 빛에 굶주리고 목말라하는 자인 양 행세하다가, 마침내 여자의 마음을 굴복시키는 가장 강력한 무기에 의지했는데, 결코 빗나가는 법이 없는 무기요. 그건 잘 알려진 수단이지 — 바로 아첨이오. 세상에서 진실을 말하는 것보다 어려운 일은 없고, 아첨보다 쉬운 일은 없소. 진실을 말할 때 백분의 일이라도 거짓된 가락이 섞이면 불협화음이 생기고, 그러면 골치 아픈 일이 생기지. 하지만 아첨이라면 마지막 음 하나까지 죄다 거짓이라 해도 한결같이 기분 좋게 들리고, 만족스럽게 받아들여지오. 천박한 만족일지언정, 그래도 만족이지. 그리고 아무리 천박한 아첨이라도 적어도 절반은 틀림없이 진실처럼 들리게 마련이오. 이건 발달의 모든 단계와 사회의 모든 계급에 해당하는 진리요. 베스타의 처녀라도 아첨에 넘어갈 수 있소. 한번은 자기 남편과 자식들과 신조에 헌신하던 어떤 부인을 내가 유혹한 일이 있는데, 떠올릴 때마다 웃음이 나지 않을 수가 없소. 얼마나 재미났던지, 또 얼마나 수고가 안 들던지! 그 부인은 정말로 신조가 있었소 — 어쨌든 자기 나름의 신조가. 내 모든 전술이라곤 그저 그녀의 순결 앞에서 완전히 무너지고 납작 엎드리는 것이었소. 나는 뻔뻔하게 그녀에게 아첨을 했고, 그녀의 손을 한 번 잡거나 눈길 한 번 받는 데 성공하기만 하면, 그걸 억지로 빼앗았다고 자책하며, 그녀가 저항했노라고, 그러니 내가 이토록 신조 없는 인간만 아니었다면 결코 아무것도 얻지 못했을 거라고 선언하곤 했소. 나는 그녀가 너무도 순진해서 내 배신을 미리 내다보지 못했고, 무의식적으로, 자기도 모르는 새에 나에게 굴복한 거라는 둥 그렇게 우겨 댔지. 결국 나는 승리를 거뒀고, 한편 내 부인께서는 자기가 결백하고 순결하며 자신의 모든 의무와 책무에 충실했는데 그저 순전히 우연으로 넘어가고 만 거라고 굳게 믿고 있었소. 그리고 내가 마침내 그녀에게 사실은 그녀도 나만큼이나 열을 올렸던 거라는 게 내 진심 어린 확신이라고 설명해 주었을 때, 그녀가 나한테 얼마나 화를 냈던지. 가엾은 마르파 페트로브나도 아첨이라면 무섭게 약했으니, 내가 마음만 먹었다면 그녀 생전에 그녀의 전 재산을 내 앞으로 돌려놓을 수도 있었을 거요. (지금 내가 술을 엄청나게 마셔 대고, 말도 너무 많이 하고 있군.) 이제 내가 아브도티야 로마노브나한테도 똑같은 효과를 내기 시작했다고 말하더라도 화내지 않기를 바라오. 하지만 나는 어리석고 성급해서 그걸 다 망쳐 버렸소. 아브도티야 로마노브나는 여러 번 — 특히 한 번은 — 내 눈빛 때문에 몹시 불쾌해했는데, 믿겠소? 거기엔 이따금 그녀를 두렵게 하는 어떤 불빛이 어렸고, 그게 점점 더 강해지고 더 함부로 드러나더니 마침내 그녀한테 혐오스러운 것이 되고 만 거지. 시시콜콜 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠고, 우리는 헤어졌소. 거기서 나는 또 어리석게 굴었지. 나는 그런 온갖 전도와 나를 개종시키려는 노력을 가장 천박한 방식으로 비웃기 시작했소. 파라샤가 다시 등장했고, 그것도 그애 혼자만이 아니었지. 사실상 엄청난 소동이 벌어진 거요. 아, 로지온 로마노비치, 당신 누이의 눈이 이따금 얼마나 번득일 수 있는지 당신이 볼 수만 있다면! 지금 내가 취해 있고 포도주를 한 잔이나 들이켰다는 건 신경 쓰지 마시오. 나는 진실을 말하고 있소. 단언컨대 그 눈길이 내 꿈속을 떠나지 않았고, 마침내는 그녀의 옷자락 스치는 소리조차 견딜 수 없을 지경이 됐소. 정말이지 나는 내가 간질에 걸리는 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했소. 내가 그런 광란의 지경까지 흔들릴 수 있으리라곤 결코 믿지 못했을 거요. 사실 화해를 하는 게 꼭 필요했지만, 그때는 이미 불가능했소. 그래서 내가 그때 무슨 짓을 했는지 상상해 보시오! 인간이 광란에 휩싸이면 얼마나 어리석은 지경까지 갈 수 있는지! 로지온 로마노비치, 절대로 광란 속에서 무슨 일을 벌이지 마시오. 나는 아브도티야 로마노브나가 어쨌든 거지나 다름없다는 데 생각이 미쳤소(아, 미안하오, 그건 적절한 말이 아니지만…… 뜻만 전해진다면 무슨 상관이겠소?). 그녀가 자기 노동으로 먹고살고, 어머니와 당신을 부양해야 한다는 것에 말이오(아, 제기랄, 또 얼굴을 찌푸리는군). 그래서 나는 그녀에게 내 돈을 다 주겠다고 — 그때 내가 마련할 수 있었던 삼만 루블을 — 그녀가 나와 함께 이리로, 페테르부르크로 달아나 준다면 그러겠다고 마음먹었소. 물론 영원한 사랑이며 황홀이며 그런 걸 맹세했겠지. 한데 말이오, 그때 나는 그녀한테 어찌나 미쳐 있었던지, 만약 그녀가 나더러 마르파 페트로브나를 독살하든지 목을 따든지 해서 자기와 결혼하라고 했다면, 당장에 해치웠을 거요! 하지만 그 일은 당신이 이미 아는 그 파국으로 끝나고 말았소. 마르파 페트로브나가 그 비열한 변호사 나부랭이 루진을 손아귀에 넣고 그 둘을 거의 맺어 줄 뻔했다는 걸 들었을 때 내가 얼마나 미칠 듯했을지 짐작이 갈 거요 — 그건 사실상 내가 제안하던 것과 똑같은 거였으니 말이오. 안 그렇소? 안 그렇소? 보아하니 당신이 아주 귀를 기울이기 시작했군…… 흥미로운 젊은이로구먼…….\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 짜증스레 주먹으로 탁자를 쳤다. 그의 얼굴은 벌겋게 달아올라 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그가 거의 무의식적으로 홀짝인 그 한 잔, 아니 한 잔 반쯤의 샴페인이 그에게 영향을 미치고 있음을 똑똑히 알아챘고 — 그래서 이 기회를 이용하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 스비드리가일로프가 몹시 미심쩍었다.\n\n\"자, 당신이 한 얘기를 듣고 나니, 당신이 내 누이를 노리고 페테르부르크에 왔다는 걸 완전히 확신하게 됐소.\" 그는 그를 더 자극하려고 스비드리가일로프에게 대놓고 말했다.\n\n\"오, 말도 안 되는 소리.\" 스비드리가일로프가 정신을 차리는 듯이 말했다. \"아니, 내가 말했잖소…… 게다가 당신 누이는 나를 견디지 못하는데.\"\n\n\"그래, 그녀가 견디지 못한다는 건 나도 확신하오, 하지만 그게 요점이 아니오.\"\n\n\"그녀가 견디지 못한다고 그렇게 확신하시오?\" 스비드리가일로프가 눈을 가늘게 뜨며 조롱하듯 미소 지었다. \"당신 말이 맞소, 그녀는 나를 사랑하지 않지. 하지만 남편과 아내 사이, 혹은 연인과 정부 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지는 결코 확신할 수 없는 법이오. 세상에는 늘 비밀로 남아 오직 그 두 사람만 아는 작은 구석이 있게 마련이지. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 나를 혐오의 눈으로 봤다고 당신이 장담할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"당신이 흘린 몇 마디로 보아, 당신이 두냐에게 여전히 어떤 속셈을 — 물론 사악한 속셈을 — 품고 있고, 그걸 곧 실행에 옮길 작정이라는 걸 알겠소.\"\n\n\"뭐, 내가 그런 말을 흘렸단 말이오?\" 스비드리가일로프가 순진한 당혹감을 띠며 물었는데, 자기 속셈에 붙은 그 형용사에는 조금도 신경 쓰지 않았다.\n\n\"아니, 지금도 흘리고 있잖소. 왜 그렇게 겁을 먹는 거요? 지금 뭘 그리 두려워하는 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 — 겁을 먹는다고? 당신을 두려워한다고? 오히려 당신이 나를 두려워해야 할 판인데, 친애하는 벗이여. 하지만 무슨 헛소릴. 그래도 내가 술을 너무 많이 마셨군, 그건 알겠소. 또 하마터면 말을 너무 많이 할 뻔했어. 빌어먹을 술! 이봐! 거기, 물 좀!\"\n\n그는 샴페인 병을 집어 들더니 거리낌 없이 창밖으로 내던졌다. 필립이 물을 가져왔다.\n\n\"그건 다 헛소리요!\" 스비드리가일로프가 수건을 적셔 머리에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"하지만 한마디로 당신한테 대답해서 당신의 모든 의심을 없애 줄 수 있소. 내가 결혼할 거란 걸 아시오?\"\n\n\"전에도 그렇게 말했잖소.\"\n\n\"그랬소? 잊어버렸군. 하지만 분명하게 말했을 리는 없는데, 그땐 약혼녀를 본 적조차 없었으니까. 그저 그러려고 작정만 했던 거지. 그런데 이제는 정말로 약혼녀가 생겼고, 다 정해진 일이오. 미룰 수 없는 볼일만 없었다면 지금 당장 당신을 데리고 그들을 보러 갔을 텐데, 당신 조언을 좀 구하고 싶거든. 아, 제기랄, 이제 십 분밖에 안 남았군! 자, 시계 좀 보시오. 하지만 당신한테 얘기해 줘야겠소, 내 결혼은 그 나름으로 흥미로운 이야기니까. 어디 가는 거요? 또 가려는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니, 지금은 안 갈 거요.\"\n\n\"아예 안 간다고? 두고 봅시다. 내가 당신을 거기 데려가서 약혼녀를 보여 주겠소, 다만 지금은 아니오. 당신은 곧 가 봐야 할 테니까. 당신은 오른쪽으로 가야 하고 나는 왼쪽으로 가야 하오. 레슬리히 부인이라고, 지금 내가 세 들어 사는 그 여자 말인데, 응? 당신이 무슨 생각을 하는지 알겠소, 겨울에 그 집 처녀가 물에 빠져 죽었다는 그 여자 아니냐고 말이지. 자, 듣고 있소? 그 여자가 나를 위해 다 주선해 준 거요. 당신은 따분해하니까, 시간을 채울 뭔가가 필요하다고 그러더군. 알다시피 나는 음울하고 우울한 사람이오. 내가 명랑한 사람인 줄 아시오? 아니, 나는 음울한 사람이오. 해코지는 안 하지만, 사흘씩 내리 한구석에 앉아 말 한마디 안 하지. 그리고 그 레슬리히는 교활한 계집이오, 정말이지. 나는 그 여자가 무슨 꿍꿍이를 품고 있는지 알지. 그 여자 생각은, 내가 결국 진절머리를 내고 아내를 버리고 떠나 버리면, 그때 자기가 그 여자를 손에 넣어 한밑천 챙기겠다는 거요 — 물론 우리 계급에서, 아니면 더 높은 데서 말이오. 그 여자 말로는 아버지란 사람이 영락한 퇴직 관리인데, 지난 삼 년 동안 다리가 마비된 채 의자에 앉아만 있다더군. 어머니는, 그 여자 말로는 분별 있는 여자라고 하오. 아들 하나가 지방에서 근무하는데 도움은 안 주고, 시집간 딸이 하나 있지만 찾아오질 않는답디다. 게다가 자기 자식들로도 모자란지 어린 조카 둘까지 떠맡고 있어서, 막내딸을 학교에서 빼냈다는 거요, 한 달만 있으면 열여섯이 되는 계집애를, 그래야 시집을 보낼 수 있으니까. 그애가 바로 내 상대요. 우리는 거기 갔소. 얼마나 우스웠던지! 내가 나서니 — 지주에, 홀아비에, 이름난 가문에, 연줄도 있고, 재산도 있는 사람이지. 내가 쉰 살이고 그애가 열여섯도 안 됐다 한들 그게 무슨 대수겠소? 누가 그런 걸 따진단 말이오? 하지만 매혹적이지 않소? 매혹적이오, 하하! 내가 그 아빠 엄마한테 어떻게 얘기했는지 당신이 봤어야 하는데. 그 순간의 나를 보는 데는 돈을 내도 아깝지 않았을 거요. 그애가 들어와서, 인사를 하는데, 상상해 보시오, 아직도 짧은 원피스 차림이라니 — 채 피지도 않은 봉오리지! 노을처럼 발갛게 물든 얼굴로 — 분명 미리 무슨 말을 들었겠지. 당신이 여자 얼굴을 어떻게 생각하는지는 모르겠소만, 내가 보기엔 그 열여섯이라는 나이, 그 앳된 눈, 수줍음과 부끄러움의 눈물이 미모보다 낫소. 게다가 그애는 정말 한 폭의 그림 같은 작은 미인이오. 양털처럼 작게 곱슬거리는 금발, 도톰하고 작은 장밋빛 입술, 앙증맞은 발 — 그야말로 매혹덩어리지!…… 자, 우리는 친해졌소. 나는 그들한테 집안 사정 때문에 서두른다고 했고, 이튿날, 그러니까 그저께 우리는 약혼을 했소. 이제 거기 가면 나는 곧장 그애를 무릎에 앉히고 거기 그대로 둔다오……. 그러면 그애는 노을처럼 발갛게 물들고, 나는 시시각각 입을 맞추지. 물론 그애 엄마는 이 사람이 네 남편이고 마땅히 이래야 하는 거라고 그애한테 단단히 일러 두지. 그야말로 황홀하오! 어쩌면 지금의 이 약혼 상태가 결혼보다 나을지도 모르오. 여기엔 이른바 '자연과 진실'이라는 게 있거든, 하하! 나는 그애와 두 번 얘기를 나눠 봤는데, 결코 바보가 아니오. 가끔 그애가 나를 흘끔 훔쳐보는데, 그 눈길이 정말이지 나를 불태워 버린다오. 그애 얼굴은 라파엘로의 성모 같소. 알잖소, 시스티나 성모의 얼굴엔 뭔가 환상적인 것이, 그 비통한 종교적 황홀의 얼굴이 있다는 걸. 당신은 못 알아챘소? 뭐, 그애가 그런 부류요. 약혼한 다음 날 나는 그애한테 천오백 루블어치 선물을 사 줬소 — 다이아몬드 세트 하나에 진주 세트 하나, 그리고 이만큼 큰 은제 화장 상자에 온갖 물건을 가득 채워서 말이오, 그래서 내 성모님의 얼굴까지 환히 빛나더군. 어제 나는 그애를 무릎에 앉혔는데, 아마 좀 너무 허물없이 굴었던 모양인지 — 그애가 새빨갛게 물들며 눈물을 글썽였지만, 그걸 내보이려 하진 않았소. 우리 단둘만 남게 되자, 그애가 갑자기 내 목에 매달려서는(제 발로 그런 건 처음이었지) 작은 두 팔로 나를 감싸 안고 입을 맞추더니, 순종하고 충실하고 착한 아내가 되겠노라, 나를 행복하게 해 주겠노라, 자기 평생을, 자기 삶의 매 순간을 바치겠노라, 모든 걸, 모든 걸 희생하겠노라 맹세하면서, 그 대신 바라는 건 오직 내 '존경'뿐이고, 나한테 '아무것도, 아무것도 더는 바라지 않는다, 선물도 필요 없다'고 하더군. 단둘이서, 모슬린 원피스를 입고 작은 곱슬머리에 두 뺨엔 처녀의 수줍은 홍조를 띠고 두 눈엔 감격의 눈물을 글썽이는 열여섯 살 천사한테서 그런 고백을 듣는 게 제법 매혹적이라는 건 당신도 인정할 거요! 매혹적이지 않소? 돈을 낼 만하지 않소? 자…… 들어 보시오, 우리 내 약혼녀를 보러 갑시다, 다만 지금 당장은 아니고!\"\n\n\"실은 그 끔찍한 나이 차와 성숙도의 차이가 당신의 음욕을 자극하는 거요! 정말로 그런 결혼을 하겠단 말이오?\"\n\n\"아니, 물론이지. 누구나 자기 생각만 하는 법이고, 자기 자신을 가장 잘 속일 줄 아는 자가 가장 즐겁게 사는 거요. 하하! 한데 당신은 왜 그리 미덕에 열을 올리는 거요? 나를 좀 봐주시오, 나의 좋은 벗이여. 나는 죄 많은 인간이오. 하하하!\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이들을 거둬 줬잖소. 비록…… 비록 당신 나름의 이유가 있긴 했겠지만……. 이제 다 알겠소.\"\n\n\"나는 늘 아이들을 좋아하오, 아주 좋아하지.\" 스비드리가일로프가 웃었다. \"그것에 관해 흥미로운 일화를 하나 들려줄 수 있소. 여기 온 첫날 나는 여러 소굴을 돌아다녔소, 칠 년 만이라 그저 거기로 내달았던 거지. 보아하니 당신도 알아챘겠지만, 나는 옛 친구들과 다시 사귀는 걸 서두르지 않소. 견딜 수 있는 한 그들 없이 지낼 거요. 한데 말이오, 시골에서 마르파 페트로브나와 지낼 때, 길만 잘 아는 사람이라면 누구든 많은 걸 찾아낼 수 있는 그런 장소들 생각이 나를 사로잡았소. 그렇소, 맹세컨대! 농민들한테는 보드카가 있고, 활동의 길이 막힌 교육받은 젊은이들은 불가능한 꿈과 환상 속에서 자신을 허비하며 이론에 절름발이가 되어 버리지. 유대인들이 생겨나 돈을 긁어모으고, 나머지는 죄다 방탕에 몸을 맡기고. 첫 순간부터 이 도시는 그 익숙한 냄새를 풍기더군. 나는 우연히 어느 끔찍한 소굴에 들어가게 됐는데 — 나는 더러운 소굴을 좋아하거든 — 이른바 무도회라는 거였소, 거기서 내 시절엔 한 번도 본 적 없는 그런 캉캉이 벌어지고 있었지. 그렇소, 거기에 진보라는 게 있는 거요. 그런데 별안간 곱게 차려입은 열세 살짜리 계집애가, 그 방면의 전문가와, 맞은편엔 또 다른 자와 짝을 이뤄 춤추는 게 보이지 뭐요. 그애 엄마는 벽 옆 의자에 앉아 있었소. 그게 어떤 캉캉이었는지는 당신은 상상도 못 할 거요! 그애는 부끄러워서 얼굴을 붉히다가, 마침내 모욕감을 느끼고 울기 시작했소. 그애의 짝은 그애를 붙들고 빙글빙글 돌리며 그애 앞에서 재주를 부려 댔지. 다들 웃어 댔고 — 나는 당신네 대중이 마음에 드오, 캉캉을 보러 온 대중조차도 — 그들은 웃고 소리치더군, '꼴좋다 — 꼴좋아! 애를 데려오면 안 되지!' 뭐, 그 위로가 되는 한마디가 논리적이었는지 아닌지는 내 알 바 아니오. 나는 즉시 계획을 세우고 그 엄마 옆에 앉아, 나도 이곳에 처음 온 사람이라느니, 여기 사람들은 버르장머리가 없어서 점잖은 사람들을 알아보지도 못하고 존중할 줄도 모른다느니 하는 말로 운을 떼면서, 내게 돈이 많다는 걸 넌지시 알리고는 내 마차로 그들을 집까지 데려다주겠다고 했소. 나는 그들을 집까지 데려다주고 알게 됐지. 그들은 비참하고 작은 구멍 같은 데서 셋방살이를 하고 있었고, 막 시골에서 올라온 참이었소. 그 여자는 자기와 딸이 나와 알게 된 걸 영광으로 여길 따름이라고 하더군. 알고 보니 그들은 가진 거라곤 아무것도 없었고, 무슨 소송 일로 도시에 올라온 거였소. 나는 도움과 돈을 내밀었지. 그들이 진짜 무용 교습소인 줄 알고 그 무도장에 잘못 들어갔다는 것도 알게 됐소. 나는 그 어린 처녀의 프랑스어와 무용 교육을 돕겠다고 제안했지. 내 제안은 영광이라며 열렬히 받아들여졌고 — 우리는 지금도 사이좋게 지낸다오……. 원한다면 우리 그들을 보러 갑시다, 다만 지금 당장은 아니고.\"\n\n\"그만하시오! 그 비열하고 추잡한 일화는 이제 됐소, 타락하고 비열하고 음탕한 인간 같으니!\"\n\n\"실러로군, 당신은 영락없는 실러요! '오 미덕은 도대체 어디에 둥지를 틀려는가?' 하지만 알아 두시오, 나는 일부러 당신한테 이런 얘기들을 들려주는 거요, 당신이 비명을 지르는 걸 듣는 즐거움 때문에 말이오!\"\n\n\"그렇겠지. 나 자신도 우스꽝스럽다는 걸 알겠소.\" 라스콜니코프가 성난 듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 유쾌하게 웃었다. 마침내 그는 필립을 불러 계산을 치르고는 자리에서 일어서기 시작했다.\n\n\"이런, 내가 취했군, 이제 그만 떠들지.\" 그가 말했다. \"즐거웠소.\"\n\n\"즐거웠을 거라고 생각하오!\" 라스콜니코프가 일어서며 소리쳤다. \"지칠 대로 지친 난봉꾼이 마음속에 똑같은 종류의 끔찍한 계획을 품은 채 그런 모험담을 늘어놓는 게 즐거운 일이 아닐 리 없겠지 — 특히 이런 상황에서, 게다가 나 같은 인간한테 말이오……. 그게 자극이 되는 거지!\"\n\n\"뭐, 정 그렇게 말한다면,\" 스비드리가일로프가 다소 놀란 표정으로 라스콜니코프를 뜯어보며 대답했다. \"정 그렇게 말한다면, 당신이야말로 철두철미한 냉소가요. 어쨌든 당신한테는 그렇게 될 만한 게 잔뜩 있으니까. 당신은 많은 걸 이해할 수 있고…… 또 많은 일을 해낼 수도 있지. 하지만 그만합시다. 당신과 더 얘기를 나누지 못해 진심으로 유감이지만, 당신을 시야에서 놓치진 않을 거요……. 그저 조금만 기다리시오.\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 음식점에서 걸어 나갔다. 라스콜니코프도 그를 뒤따라 나갔다. 그렇지만 스비드리가일로프는 그리 많이 취한 게 아니었으니, 술이 한순간 그에게 영향을 미쳤을 뿐, 그것도 시시각각 가시고 있었다. 그는 무언가 중요한 일에 골몰한 채 얼굴을 찌푸리고 있었다. 그는 분명 무언가를 기다리며 흥분하고 불안해하는 듯했다. 지난 몇 분 사이 라스콜니코프를 대하는 그의 태도가 달라져서, 시시각각 더 무례하고 더 비웃는 투가 되어 갔다. 라스콜니코프는 이 모든 걸 알아챘고, 그 역시 불안했다. 그는 스비드리가일로프가 몹시 미심쩍어져서 그를 뒤따르기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그들은 보도로 나왔다.\n\n\"당신은 오른쪽으로 가시오, 나는 왼쪽으로, 아니 원한다면 그 반대로 가든지. 그저 잘 가시오, 나의 즐거움이여, 또 만날 수 있기를.\"\n\n그러고는 그는 건초 시장 쪽을 향해 오른쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 그를 뒤따라갔다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 짓이오?\" 스비드리가일로프가 뒤를 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"내 분명 말한 것 같은데…….\"\n\n\"이제부터 당신을 절대 놓치지 않겠다는 뜻이오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\"\n\n두 사람은 멈춰 서서, 마치 서로의 힘을 가늠하기라도 하듯 상대를 노려보았다.\n\n\"당신이 반쯤 취해서 늘어놓은 그 모든 이야기로 미루어,\" 라스콜니코프가 거칠게 내뱉었다. \"나는 확신하오, 당신이 내 누이에 대한 흉계를 포기하기는커녕, 그 어느 때보다 더 적극적으로 밀어붙이고 있다는 걸 말이오. 오늘 아침 내 누이가 편지 한 통을 받았다는 걸 알고 있소. 당신은 그동안 도무지 가만히 앉아 있질 못했지……. 오는 길에 어디서 아내를 하나 파냈는지 모르겠지만, 그건 아무 의미도 없소. 나는 직접 확인하고 싶소.\"\n\n라스콜니코프 자신도 자기가 무엇을 원하는지, 무엇을 확인하고 싶은지조차 제대로 말할 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"이거 참! 경찰을 부르겠소!\"\n\n\"부르시오!\"\n\n또다시 두 사람은 잠시 서로 마주 보고 서 있었다. 마침내 스비드리가일로프의 얼굴빛이 달라졌다. 라스콜니코프가 자기 협박에 겁먹지 않는다는 것을 확인하고는, 그는 명랑하고 다정한 태도를 꾸며 보였다.\n\n\"이런 사람을 봤나! 사실 나는 호기심에 잡아먹힐 지경이면서도 일부러 당신 일을 입에 올리지 않았소. 그건 환상적인 사건이거든. 다음 기회로 미뤄 두려 했는데, 당신은 죽은 사람도 깨울 만한 양반이군……. 자, 그럼 갑시다. 다만 미리 일러두는데, 나는 잠깐 돈을 가지러 집에 들르려는 것뿐이오. 그러고 나서 셋집 문을 잠그고, 마차를 잡아 섬으로 저녁을 보내러 갈 거요. 자, 그래도 나를 따라오겠소?\"\n\n\"당신을 보러 가는 게 아니라 소피야 세묘노브나를 만나러 당신 하숙집에 가는 거요, 장례식에 가지 못해 죄송하다고 말하려고.\"\n\n\"그건 당신 좋을 대로 하시오. 하지만 소피야 세묘노브나는 집에 없소. 그녀는 아이 셋을 데리고 지체 높은 어느 노부인에게 갔거든. 고아원 몇 곳의 후원자인데, 내가 옛날부터 알던 사람이오. 나는 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이 셋을 위한 돈을 그 노부인에게 맡기고 그 시설에도 기부를 함으로써 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았소. 게다가 소피야 세묘노브나의 사연도 하나도 숨김없이 낱낱이 들려주었지. 그게 그녀에게 이루 말할 수 없는 감동을 주었소. 그래서 소피야 세묘노브나가 오늘 그 부인이 잠시 머무는 X 호텔로 와 달라는 초대를 받은 거요.\"\n\n\"상관없소, 그래도 가 보겠소.\"\n\n\"좋을 대로 하시오, 나야 아무래도 좋으니까. 하지만 나는 당신과 함께 가지는 않겠소. 자, 다 왔소. 그건 그렇고, 당신이 나를 의심스러운 눈초리로 바라보는 건, 단지 내가 이렇게 섬세하게 굴면서 지금껏 당신을 질문으로 괴롭히지 않았기 때문이라는 걸 나는 확신하오……. 무슨 말인지 알겠소? 그게 당신에겐 이상하게 여겨진 거지. 내기를 걸어도 좋소, 바로 그거요. 흠, 이래서 섬세하게 굴면 안 된다는 교훈을 얻는 거지!\"\n\n\"그리고 문에 귀를 갖다 대고 엿듣는 것도 말이오!\"\n\"아하, 그거였군, 응?\" 스비드리가일로프가 웃었다. \"그래, 그 모든 일이 있고 나서 당신이 그걸 그냥 넘겼다면 오히려 내가 놀랐을 거요. 하하! 하긴 나도 당신이 저질러 놓고 소피야 세묘노브나에게 들려주던 그 장난질을 어느 정도는 알아들었지만, 그게 다 무슨 뜻이었던 거요? 어쩌면 내가 시대에 한참 뒤떨어져서 이해를 못 하는 건지도 모르지. 제발이지 좀 설명해 주시오, 이보게 젊은이. 최신 이론을 한번 펼쳐 보시구려!\"\n\n\"당신은 아무것도 듣지 못했을 거요. 죄다 지어내는 거잖소!\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 그 얘기를 하는 게 아니오(하긴 뭔가 듣긴 했지만). 아니, 내가 말하려는 건 당신이 지금 이렇게 끊임없이 한숨을 쉬고 신음하는 그 꼴이오. 당신 안의 실러가 시도 때도 없이 반란을 일으키고 있는데, 그러면서 이제 와 나더러 문에 귀를 대지 말라고 하는군. 그렇게 느낀다면, 가서 경찰에 신고하시오, 이런 불운을 겪었노라고. 당신은 자기 이론에서 작은 실수를 저질렀다고 말이오. 하지만 문에 귀를 대선 안 되지만 노파쯤은 제 마음껏 죽여도 된다고 확신한다면, 차라리 어서 서둘러 미국으로 떠나는 게 낫겠소. 달아나시오, 젊은이! 어쩌면 아직 시간이 있을지도 모르오. 나는 진심으로 하는 말이오. 돈이 없소? 내가 차비를 주리다.\"\n\n\"나는 그런 건 전혀 생각하고 있지 않소.\" 라스콜니코프가 역겨운 듯 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"이해하오(하지만 무리하지 마시오, 말하고 싶지 않으면 따지지 말고). 당신이 골머리를 앓고 있는 그 문제들을 나도 이해한단 말이오. 도덕적인 문제들이지, 안 그렇소? 시민으로서, 또 인간으로서의 의무 말이오? 그딴 건 죄다 제쳐 두시오. 이제 당신에게는 아무 의미도 없으니까, 하하! 당신은 여전히 자기가 인간이고 시민이라고 말하겠지. 그렇다면 이런 곤경에 빠져들지 말았어야지. 자기에게 맞지도 않는 일에 손을 대 봐야 소용없는 법이오. 자, 차라리 권총으로 자살하는 게 낫겠소, 아니면 그러기도 싫소?\"\n\n\"당신은 나를 화나게 해서, 당신 곁을 떠나게 만들려는 모양이군.\"\n\n\"이 별난 양반을 봤나! 하지만 다 왔소. 계단에 온 걸 환영하오. 보시오, 이쪽이 소피야 세묘노브나에게 가는 길이오. 봐요, 집에 아무도 없잖소. 못 믿겠소? 카페르나우모프에게 물어보시오. 그녀는 그에게 열쇠를 맡겨 두니까. 자, 여기 카페르나우모프 부인이 직접 나오는군. 어이, 뭐요? 이 사람은 귀가 좀 먹었거든. 외출했소? 어디로? 들었소? 그녀는 집에 없고, 아마 저녁 늦게나 돼야 돌아올 거요. 자, 내 방으로 오시오. 당신, 나를 보러 오고 싶다고 했잖소, 안 그랬소? 자, 다 왔소. 레슬리히 부인은 집에 없군. 그 여자는 늘 바쁜 사람이오, 정말 훌륭한 여자지……. 당신이 조금만 더 분별이 있었다면 당신에게도 쓸모가 있었을 텐데. 자, 보시오! 내가 책상에서 이 오 푼 이자 채권을 꺼내는데, 아직 이렇게나 많이 가지고 있는 걸 보시오. 이건 오늘 현금으로 바꿀 거요. 더는 시간을 낭비할 수 없소. 책상도 잠그고, 셋집도 잠그고, 자, 우리는 다시 계단에 와 있소. 마차를 탈까? 나는 섬으로 가는 길이오. 좀 태워 드릴까? 이 마차를 잡겠소. 아, 거절하는 거요? 이젠 질렸군그래! 같이 드라이브나 갑시다! 곧 비가 올 것 같소. 상관없소, 포장을 내리면 되니까…….\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 어느새 마차에 올라타 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 적어도 이 순간만큼은 자기 의심이 부당한 것이라고 결론지었다. 그는 한마디 대꾸도 없이 몸을 돌려 건초 시장 쪽으로 되돌아 걸었다. 만일 그가 가던 길에 한 번이라도 뒤를 돌아보았더라면, 스비드리가일로프가 백 걸음도 채 못 가서 마차에서 내려 마차를 돌려보내고 보도를 따라 걸어가는 모습을 보았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 이미 모퉁이를 돌아 아무것도 볼 수 없었다. 강렬한 혐오가 그를 스비드리가일로프에게서 멀찍이 떼어 놓았다.\n\n\"내가 단 한순간이라도 저런 천박한 짐승, 저런 타락한 호색한이자 악당에게 도움을 구하려 했다니!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n라스콜니코프의 판단은 너무 가볍고 성급하게 내려진 것이었다. 스비드리가일로프에게는 어딘가 독특하고, 심지어 신비롭기까지 한 무언가가 있었던 것이다. 누이에 관한 한, 라스콜니코프는 스비드리가일로프가 그녀를 가만히 내버려 두지 않으리라 확신했다. 그러나 이 일을 두고 끝없이 생각하고 또 생각한다는 것은 너무도 지긋지긋하고 견딜 수 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n혼자가 되자, 그는 스무 걸음도 못 가서, 늘 그렇듯 깊은 생각에 잠겨 들었다. 다리 위에서 그는 난간 옆에 멈춰 서서 물을 바라보기 시작했다. 그리고 그의 누이가 바로 그 가까이에 서 있었다.\n\n그는 다리 입구에서 그녀와 마주쳤으나, 보지도 못한 채 그냥 지나쳐 버렸다. 두냐는 지금껏 거리에서 그가 이런 모습을 한 것을 본 적이 없었기에 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다. 그녀는 멈춰 선 채, 그를 불러야 할지 말아야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그때 갑자기 그녀는 스비드리가일로프가 건초 시장 쪽에서 빠르게 다가오는 것을 보았다.\n\n그는 조심스럽게 다가오는 듯했다. 그는 다리 위로 올라서지 않고 보도 한쪽에 비켜선 채, 라스콜니코프의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 안간힘을 다했다. 그는 한동안 두냐를 지켜보면서 그녀에게 신호를 보내고 있었다. 그녀는 그 신호가 오빠에게 말을 걸지 말고 자기에게로 와 달라고 청하는 것이라고 짐작했다.\n\n두냐는 그렇게 했다. 그녀는 오빠 곁을 살그머니 지나쳐 스비드리가일로프에게로 다가갔다.\n\n\"어서 자리를 뜹시다.\" 스비드리가일로프가 그녀에게 속삭였다. \"로디온 로마노비치가 우리가 만난 걸 알게 하고 싶지 않소. 말해 두지만, 나는 방금 바로 저 근처 식당에서 그와 함께 앉아 있었소. 그가 나를 찾아왔는데, 떼어 내느라 어찌나 애를 먹었는지. 그가 어찌어찌 내가 당신에게 보낸 편지에 대해 듣고는 뭔가를 의심하고 있소. 물론 당신이 그에게 말한 건 아니겠지만, 당신이 아니라면 대체 누구겠소?\"\n\n\"자, 이제 우리는 모퉁이를 돌았으니,\" 두냐가 말을 가로막았다. \"오빠는 우리를 보지 못할 거예요. 말해 두는데, 나는 더 이상 당신과 함께 가지 않겠어요. 여기서 말하세요. 거리에서도 다 말할 수 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"첫째, 거리에서는 말할 수가 없소. 둘째, 당신은 소피야 세묘노브나의 이야기도 들어야 하오. 셋째, 당신에게 몇 가지 서류를 보여 주겠소……. 흠, 좋소, 나와 함께 가는 데 동의하지 않겠다면, 나는 아무 설명도 하지 않고 당장 떠나 버리겠소. 다만 한 가지, 당신의 사랑하는 오빠의 아주 묘한 비밀이 전적으로 내 손에 쥐여 있다는 사실을 잊지 마시오.\"\n\n두냐는 망설이며 멈춰 서서, 캐묻는 눈으로 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다.\n\n\"무엇이 두렵소?\" 그가 조용히 말했다. \"도시는 시골과 다르오. 게다가 시골에서도 당신이 내게 끼친 해가 내가 당신에게 끼친 것보다 더 컸소.\"\n\n\"소피야 세묘노브나에게 미리 말해 두었나요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그녀에게는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 지금 그녀가 집에 있는지조차 확실치 않소. 하지만 십중팔구는 있을 거요. 오늘 계모를 묻었으니, 그런 날 어디 방문하러 나갈 리는 없겠지. 당분간은 이 일을 누구에게도 말하고 싶지 않고, 당신에게 말한 것조차 반쯤은 후회하고 있소. 이런 일에서는 아주 사소한 경솔함도 배신만큼이나 나쁜 거요. 나는 저 집에 살고 있소, 이제 거의 다 왔군. 저게 우리 집 문지기인데, 나를 아주 잘 알지. 보시오, 인사를 하잖소. 그는 내가 어떤 부인과 함께 오는 걸 보았고, 분명 이미 당신 얼굴도 눈여겨봤을 거요. 당신이 나를 두려워하고 의심한다면 그 점이 다행스러울 거요. 이렇게 거칠게 말하는 걸 용서하시오. 나는 셋집을 혼자 다 쓰지는 않소. 소피야 세묘노브나의 방이 내 방 옆에 붙어 있는데, 그녀는 옆 셋집에 세 들어 살고 있소. 이 층 전체가 셋방으로 나뉘어 있지. 어째서 어린애처럼 겁을 먹는 거요? 내가 정말 그렇게 무섭소?\"\n\n스비드리가일로프의 입술은 짐짓 너그러운 미소로 비틀려 있었다. 그러나 그는 웃을 기분이 전혀 아니었다. 심장이 두근거려 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없었다. 그는 점점 커져 가는 흥분을 감추려고 일부러 좀 큰 소리로 말했다. 그러나 두냐는 이 묘한 흥분을 알아채지 못했으니, 자기가 어린애처럼 그를 두려워한다느니, 그가 그렇게 무서우냐느니 하는 그의 말에 잔뜩 화가 나 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"당신이 명예로운…… 인간이 아니라는 걸 알지만, 나는 당신이 조금도 두렵지 않아요. 앞장서세요.\" 그녀는 짐짓 침착한 척 말했지만, 얼굴은 몹시 창백했다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 소냐의 방 앞에서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"그녀가 집에 있는지 여쭤봐도 되겠소……. 없군. 이런 운도 없을 데가! 하지만 곧 돌아올 수도 있다는 걸 나는 알고 있소. 외출했다면 고아들 일로 그 부인을 만나러 간 것밖에 없을 거요. 아이들 어머니가 죽었거든……. 내가 그 애들 일에 끼어들어 이런저런 조처를 해 두었소. 만일 소피야 세묘노브나가 십 분 안에 돌아오지 않으면, 그녀를 당신에게 보내겠소, 원한다면 오늘이라도. 이게 내 셋집이오. 이 두 개가 내 방이오. 내 집주인인 레슬리히 부인이 옆방을 쓰고 있소. 자, 이쪽을 보시오. 내 가장 중요한 증거를 보여 주겠소. 내 침실에서 난 이 문이 텅 빈 두 개의 방으로 통하는데, 그 방들은 세놓는 중이오. 자, 여기요……. 좀 주의 깊게 들여다봐야 하오.\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 가구가 딸린 제법 넓은 방 두 개를 쓰고 있었다. 두냐는 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았으나, 가구나 방의 배치에서 특별한 점은 아무것도 발견하지 못했다. 그러나 눈여겨볼 만한 것이 있었으니, 예컨대 스비드리가일로프의 셋집이 거의 사람이 살지 않는 두 채의 셋방 사이에 정확히 끼여 있다는 점이었다. 그의 방들은 복도에서 곧장 들어오는 게 아니라, 집주인의 거의 텅 빈 방 두 개를 거쳐야 들어올 수 있었다. 스비드리가일로프는 침실에서 통하는 문을 열어 두냐에게 세놓는 중인 텅 빈 방 두 개를 보여 주었다. 두냐는 자기가 무엇을 보라는 것인지 알 수 없어 문간에 멈춰 섰으나, 스비드리가일로프가 서둘러 설명했다.\n\n\"여기, 이 두 번째 큰 방을 보시오. 저 문을 눈여겨봐요, 잠겨 있소. 문 옆에 의자가 하나 놓여 있는데, 이 두 방을 통틀어 하나뿐인 의자요. 더 편하게 엿듣기 위해 내 방에서 가져온 거지. 바로 저 문 너머에 소피야 세묘노브나의 탁자가 있소. 그녀가 거기 앉아 로디온 로마노비치와 이야기를 나누었지. 그리고 나는 여기 앉아 이틀 밤을 잇따라, 매번 두 시간씩 귀를 기울였소. 그러니 당연히 뭔가를 알아낼 수 있었지, 어떻게 생각하오?\"\n\n\"엿들었군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 엿들었소. 자, 이제 내 방으로 돌아갑시다. 여기서는 앉아 있을 수가 없으니.\"\n\n그는 아브도티야 로마노브나를 자기 거실로 데려와 의자를 권했다. 그는 탁자 맞은편, 적어도 그녀에게서 일곱 자쯤 떨어진 자리에 앉았으나, 아마도 그의 눈에는 언젠가 두냐를 그토록 겁에 질리게 했던 바로 그 불꽃이 타오르고 있었을 것이다. 그녀는 몸서리치며 다시 한번 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그것은 자기도 모르게 나온 몸짓이었다. 그녀는 분명 자신의 불안을 드러내고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 스비드리가일로프의 하숙집이 외따로 떨어져 있다는 사실이 문득 그녀의 마음을 쳤다. 그녀는 적어도 집주인 여자라도 집에 있는지 묻고 싶었으나, 자존심 때문에 차마 묻지 못했다. 게다가 그녀의 마음속에는 자기 자신에 대한 두려움 따위와는 비교도 안 될 만큼 큰 또 다른 근심이 자리하고 있었다. 그녀는 깊은 고뇌에 빠져 있었다.\n\n\"여기 당신 편지가 있어요.\" 그녀가 그것을 탁자 위에 내려놓으며 말했다. \"여기 쓴 게 사실일 수 있나요? 당신은 내 오빠가 저질렀다는 범죄를 암시하고 있어요. 너무 노골적으로 암시했으니, 이제 와서 부인할 수는 없겠죠. 말해 두는데, 나는 당신이 이 편지를 쓰기 전에 이미 그 어처구니없는 이야기를 들었고, 한마디도 믿지 않아요. 그건 역겹고 우스꽝스러운 의심일 뿐이에요. 나는 그 이야기가 어떻게, 왜 꾸며진 것인지 알고 있어요. 당신에게는 아무런 증거도 있을 수 없어요. 당신은 그걸 증명하겠다고 약속했죠. 말해 보세요! 하지만 미리 경고하는데, 나는 당신을 믿지 않아요! 나는 당신을 믿지 않는다고요!\"\n\n두냐는 서둘러 이렇게 말했고, 한순간 그녀의 얼굴로 핏기가 솟구쳤다.\n\n\"믿지 않는다면, 어떻게 혼자서 위험을 무릅쓰고 내 방까지 왔단 말이오? 왜 온 거요? 그저 호기심에서?\"\n\n\"나를 괴롭히지 마세요. 말해요, 말하라고요!\"\n\n\"당신이 용감한 처녀라는 건 부정할 수 없군. 정말이지, 나는 당신이 라주미힌 씨더러 여기까지 데려다 달라고 청할 줄 알았소. 그런데 그는 당신과 함께 있지도, 근처 어디에도 있지 않았소. 나는 줄곧 살피고 있었거든. 당신의 그 기개라니, 그건 당신이 로디온 로마노비치를 감싸려 했다는 증거요. 하지만 당신에게는 모든 게 거룩하기만 하군……. 당신 오빠에 관해서라면, 내가 뭐라고 말해야 하겠소? 당신도 방금 직접 그를 보았잖소. 그가 어떻게 보였소?\"\n\n\"설마 당신이 근거로 삼는 게 그것뿐인가요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그것만은 아니오, 그 자신의 말에 근거한 거지. 그는 이틀 밤을 잇따라 소피야 세묘노브나를 만나러 여기 왔소. 두 사람이 어디 앉아 있었는지는 당신에게 보여 주었고. 그는 그녀에게 죄다 털어놓았소. 그는 살인자요. 그는 노파를, 자기가 직접 물건을 저당 잡혔던 어느 전당포 노파를 죽였소. 그리고 그녀의 동생도 죽였는데, 리자베타라는 행상 여자로, 그가 언니를 죽이고 있는 동안 마침 들어왔던 거요. 그는 그들을 자기가 가져간 도끼로 죽였소. 그는 그들을 죽이고 돈을 빼앗았고, 실제로 빼앗았소. 돈과 여러 물건을 가져갔지……. 그는 이 모든 걸 토씨 하나 틀리지 않고 소피야 세묘노브나에게 말했소, 그의 비밀을 아는 유일한 사람이지. 하지만 그녀는 말로도 행동으로도 그 살인에 가담하지 않았소. 그녀도 지금의 당신만큼이나 그 일에 경악했으니까. 걱정 마시오, 그녀는 그를 배신하지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없어요.\" 두냐가 새하얗게 질린 입술로 중얼거렸다. 그녀는 숨이 막혀 헐떡였다. \"그럴 리 없어요. 손톱만큼의 이유도, 그 어떤 까닭도 없었어요……. 거짓말이에요, 거짓말!\"\n\n\"그가 그녀의 물건을 훔쳤소, 그게 이유였지, 돈과 물건을 가져갔으니까. 하기야 그 자신의 고백으로는, 그 돈도 물건도 쓰지 않고 돌멩이 밑에 숨겨 두었는데, 지금도 거기 그대로 있다더군. 하지만 그건 감히 그것들을 쓸 엄두가 나지 않았기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 그가 도둑질을, 강도질을 할 수 있겠어요? 어떻게 그런 걸 꿈엔들 생각하겠어요?\" 두냐가 외치며 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다. \"아니, 당신도 그를 알고, 그를 직접 봤잖아요, 그가 도둑일 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n그녀는 마치 스비드리가일로프에게 애원하는 듯했다. 그녀는 자신의 두려움을 완전히 잊고 있었다.\n\n\"수천수만 가지 조합과 가능성이 있는 거요, 아브도티야 로마노브나. 도둑은 훔치면서 자기가 악당임을 알지만, 우편 마차를 털어 부순 어느 신사 이야기를 나는 들은 적이 있소. 누가 알겠소, 어쩌면 그는 자기가 신사다운 일을 하고 있다고 여겼을지도! 물론 나도, 당신처럼 누군가에게서 그 이야기를 전해 들었다면 믿지 않았겠지. 하지만 나는 내 두 귀를 믿소. 그는 소피야 세묘노브나에게도 그 모든 이유를 설명했소. 그녀도 처음에는 제 귀를 믿지 못했지만, 결국에는 제 눈을 믿게 되었지.\"\n\n\"무슨…… 이유였나요?\"\n\n\"긴 이야기요, 아브도티야 로마노브나. 자……, 어떻게 말해야 할까? 일종의 이론인데, 이를테면 나 자신도, 만일 주된 목적이 옳다면 단 한 번의 악행은 허용된다고 여기는 바로 그 이론이오. 하나의 악행에 수백 가지 선행이라! 물론, 재능 있고 자존심이 하늘을 찌르는 젊은이로서는, 예컨대 자기에게 하찮은 삼천 루블만 있었어도 자기 인생 전체, 미래 전체가 다르게 풀렸을 텐데 정작 그 삼천 루블이 없다는 걸 아는 게 분통 터지는 일이기도 하지. 거기에다 굶주림에서, 누추한 굴 같은 데서 사는 데서, 누더기에서, 그리고 자기 사회적 처지며 누이와 어머니의 처지를 생생히 느끼는 데서 오는 신경의 자극이 더해지는 거요. 무엇보다 허영심, 자존심과 허영심이지, 물론 그에게 좋은 자질도 있을지 모르지만 말이오……. 나는 그를 탓하는 게 아니오, 부디 그렇게 생각하지 마시오. 게다가 그건 내 알 바도 아니고. 거기에 또 하나의 특별하고 자그마한 이론이 끼어들었소. 일종의 이론인데, 알다시피 인류를 평범한 사람과 비범한 사람으로 나누는 거요. 즉 자신의 비범함 덕분에 법이 적용되지 않는 사람들, 나머지 인류, 그러니까 평범한 자들을 위해 법을 만드는 사람들 말이오. 이론으로서야 나쁘지 않지, 그저 다른 이론들과 다를 바 없는 하나의 이론이오. 나폴레옹이 그를 엄청나게 사로잡았는데, 다시 말해 그를 사로잡은 건, 수많은 천재들이 악행 앞에서 망설이지 않고 별생각 없이 법을 뛰어넘었다는 사실이오. 그는 자기도 천재라고 여겼던 모양이오. 다시 말해 한동안은 그렇게 확신했던 거지. 그는 자기가 이론은 만들어 낼 수 있었으나 대담하게 법을 뛰어넘을 능력은 없었고, 그래서 자기가 천재가 아니라는 생각 때문에 무척이나 괴로워했고 지금도 괴로워하고 있소. 그건 자존심 있는 젊은이에게는 굴욕적인 일이지, 특히 요즘 같은 시대에는…….\"\n\n\"하지만 양심의 가책은요? 그럼 당신은 그에게 도덕적 감정 따위는 전혀 없다는 건가요? 그가 그런 사람인가요?\"\n\n\"아, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 요즘은 모든 게 뒤죽박죽이오. 하긴 한 번도 그리 질서정연한 적은 없었지만. 러시아인들은 대체로 생각이 넓소, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 자기네 땅처럼 넓고, 환상적인 것, 혼돈스러운 것에 더할 나위 없이 기우는 경향이 있지. 하지만 특별한 천재성도 없이 넓기만 한 건 불행이오. 우리가 이 문제를 놓고 저녁마다 저녁 식사 후 테라스에 앉아 얼마나 많은 이야기를 나누었는지 기억하시오? 아니, 당신은 그 넓이를 두고 나를 나무라곤 했잖소! 누가 알겠소, 어쩌면 바로 그 무렵에 그는 여기 누워 자기 계획을 곰곰이 짜고 있었을지도 모르지. 우리에게는 신성한 전통이라는 게 없소, 특히 교육받은 계층에서는 말이오, 아브도티야 로마노브나. 기껏해야 누군가가 책이나 어떤 낡은 연대기에서 끄집어내 그럭저럭 자기 나름대로 만들어 내는 정도지. 하지만 그런 건 대개 학자들이나 케케묵은 늙은이들이 하는 일이라, 사교계 사람에게는 거의 무례한 짓이 될 거요. 하긴 당신도 대체로 내 견해를 알고 있잖소. 나는 결코 누구도 탓하지 않소. 나는 아무것도 하지 않고, 그 점을 한결같이 지켜 나가지. 하지만 우리는 전에도 이 얘기를 여러 번 나눴소. 사실 나는 당신이 내 견해에 흥미를 느끼게 할 만큼 행복하기도 했지……. 당신, 안색이 몹시 창백하군요, 아브도티야 로마노브나.\"\n\n\"나는 그의 이론을 알아요. 모든 게 허용되는 사람들에 관한 그의 논문을 읽었거든요. 라주미힌이 가져다줬어요.\"\n\n\"라주미힌 씨가? 당신 오빠의 논문을? 잡지에? 그런 논문이 있단 말이오? 나는 몰랐군. 흥미로울 게 틀림없소. 한데 어디로 가는 거요, 아브도티야 로마노브나?\"\n\n\"소피야 세묘노브나를 만나고 싶어요.\" 두냐가 가냘프게 말했다. \"그녀에게 어떻게 가죠? 어쩌면 돌아왔을지도 몰라요. 당장 그녀를 봐야겠어요. 어쩌면 그녀가…….\"\n\n아브도티야 로마노브나는 말을 맺지 못했다. 글자 그대로 숨이 막혔던 것이다.\n\n\"소피야 세묘노브나는 밤이 돼야 돌아올 거요, 적어도 내 생각엔 그렇소. 곧 돌아오기로 돼 있었지만, 그렇지 않다면 아주 늦게나 돌아올 거요.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼 당신은 거짓말을 하고 있는 거예요! 알겠어요……. 당신은 거짓말을 했어요……, 줄곧 거짓말을 했어요……. 나는 당신을 믿지 않아요! 당신을 믿지 않는다고요!\" 두냐가 완전히 정신을 놓은 채 외쳤다.\n\n거의 실신할 지경이 되어, 그녀는 스비드리가일로프가 황급히 받쳐 준 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 왜 이러시오? 정신을 차리시오! 여기 물이 있소. 조금만 마셔요…….\"\n\n그는 그녀에게 물을 끼얹었다. 두냐는 몸을 떨며 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"독하게 들었군.\" 스비드리가일로프가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"아브도티야 로마노브나, 진정하시오! 내 말을 믿어요, 그에게는 친구들이 있소. 우리가 그를 구할 거요. 내가 그를 외국으로 데려가길 바라오? 나에게는 돈이 있고, 사흘이면 표를 구할 수 있소. 살인이라면, 그도 앞으로 그걸 속죄하려고 온갖 선행을 베풀 거요. 진정하시오. 그는 아직 위대한 인물이 될 수도 있소. 자, 좀 어떻소? 기분이 어떻소?\"\n\n\"잔인한 사람! 그런 일을 두고 비웃을 수 있다니! 놓아줘요…….\"\n\n\"어디로 가려는 거요?\"\n\n\"그에게로요. 그가 어디 있죠? 알고 있나요? 이 문은 왜 잠겨 있죠? 우리는 저 문으로 들어왔는데 지금은 잠겨 있어요. 언제 문을 잠근 거예요?\"\n\n\"이런 일을 두고 셋집이 떠나가도록 소리를 질러 댈 수는 없잖소. 나는 비웃는 게 결코 아니오, 그저 이런 식으로 이야기하는 데 진력이 났을 뿐이오. 한데 그런 상태로 어떻게 가겠다는 거요? 그를 배신하고 싶소? 당신은 그를 광분하게 만들 거고, 그러면 그는 자수해 버릴 거요. 말해 두는데, 그는 이미 감시당하고 있소. 그들은 이미 그의 뒤를 쫓고 있단 말이오. 당신은 그저 그를 넘겨주는 셈이 될 뿐이오. 조금만 기다리시오. 나는 방금 그를 만나 이야기를 나누고 왔소. 그는 아직 구할 수 있소. 좀 기다려요, 앉으시오. 우리 함께 잘 궁리해 봅시다. 내가 당신더러 들어오라고 한 건 당신과 단둘이 이 일을 의논하고 철저히 따져 보기 위해서였소. 자, 좀 앉아요!\"\n\n\"당신이 어떻게 그를 구한다는 거죠? 그가 정말 구원받을 수 있나요?\"\n\n두냐는 자리에 앉았다. 스비드리가일로프도 그녀 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"그건 전적으로 당신에게, 당신에게, 오직 당신에게 달려 있소.\" 그는 이글거리는 눈으로, 거의 속삭이듯, 격정에 차 말조차 제대로 잇지 못하며 입을 열었다.\n\n두냐는 깜짝 놀라 그에게서 몸을 뒤로 뺐다. 그 역시 온몸을 떨고 있었다.\n\n\"당신의…… 당신의 말 한마디면, 그는 구원받소. 내가…… 내가 그를 구하겠소. 내게는 돈도 친구도 있소. 당장 그를 떠나보내겠소. 여권을 구하겠소, 두 개를, 하나는 그를 위해, 하나는 나를 위해. 내게는 친구들이 있소……, 유능한 자들이지……. 원한다면 당신을 위한 여권도 구하겠소……, 당신 어머니를 위한 것도……. 라주미힌이 당신에게 무슨 소용이오? 나도 당신을 사랑하오……. 나는 그 무엇보다 당신을 사랑하오……. 당신 옷자락에 입 맞추게 해 주오, 제발, 제발……. 그 옷자락 스치는 소리만으로도 나는 견딜 수가 없소. 내게 '이걸 하라'고 말만 하시오, 그럼 하겠소. 무엇이든 하겠소. 불가능한 일이라도 해내겠소. 당신이 믿는 것을 나도 믿겠소. 무엇이든, 무엇이든 하겠소! 그러지 마오, 그런 눈으로 나를 보지 마오. 당신이 나를 죽이고 있다는 걸 알기나 하오?……\"\n\n그는 거의 헛소리를 늘어놓기 시작했다……. 무언가가 갑자기 그의 머리로 치솟는 것 같았다. 두냐는 벌떡 일어나 문으로 달려갔다.\n\n\"열어요! 열어 줘요!\" 그녀가 문을 흔들며 외쳤다. \"열어 줘요! 거기 아무도 없어요?\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 일어서서 정신을 차렸다. 여전히 떨리는 그의 입술이 천천히 성난 조소의 미소로 비틀렸다.\n\n\"집에는 아무도 없소.\" 그가 조용히, 또박또박 힘주어 말했다. \"집주인 여자는 외출했고, 그렇게 소리를 질러 봐야 시간 낭비요. 당신은 공연히 자기만 흥분시키고 있을 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"열쇠는 어디 있죠? 당장 문을 열어요, 당장, 비열한 인간!\"\n\n\"열쇠를 잃어버려 찾을 수가 없소.\"\n\n\"이건 폭거예요.\" 두냐가 죽은 사람처럼 창백해지며 외쳤다. 그녀는 가장 먼 구석으로 달려가, 작은 탁자로 황급히 자기 앞을 가로막아 방벽을 쳤다.\n\n그녀는 비명을 지르지는 않았으나, 자기를 괴롭히는 자에게 두 눈을 박은 채 그의 일거수일투족을 지켜보았다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 그녀를 마주 본 채 방의 반대편 끝에 선 채로 있었다. 그는 적어도 겉보기엔 확실히 침착했으나, 얼굴은 여전히 창백했다. 조소의 미소는 그의 얼굴에서 떠나지 않았다.\n\n\"방금 폭거라고 했소, 아브도티야 로마노브나. 그렇다면 내가 만반의 조처를 취해 두었다는 걸 확신해도 좋소. 소피야 세묘노브나는 집에 없소. 카페르나우모프 일가는 멀리 떨어져 있고, 그 사이에는 잠긴 방이 다섯 개나 있소. 나는 적어도 당신보다 두 배는 힘이 세고, 게다가 두려울 게 아무것도 없소. 당신은 나중에 하소연조차 할 수 없을 테니까. 설마 당신이 정말로 제 오빠를 팔아넘기려 들지는 않겠지? 게다가 아무도 당신 말을 믿지 않을 거요. 처녀가 혼자서 외딴곳에 사는 사내의 하숙집을 찾아왔다니, 어디 말이나 되는 소리요? 그러니 설령 당신이 오빠를 희생시킨다 해도, 당신은 아무것도 증명할 수 없을 거요. 폭행을 입증하기란 무척 어려운 일이오, 아브도티야 로마노브나.\"\n\n\"악당!\" 두냐가 분개하여 속삭였다.\n\n\"좋을 대로 하시오, 하지만 내가 그저 일반론으로 말한 것뿐임을 알아 두시오. 내 개인적인 확신으로는 당신 말이 전적으로 옳소, 폭력은 가증스러운 거요. 내가 그렇게 말한 건 다만, 설령 당신이…… 내가 권하는 대로 제 발로 오빠를 구하려 마음먹는다 해도, 양심의 가책 따위는 가질 필요가 없다는 걸 보여 주기 위해서요. 당신은 그저 정황에, 사실상 폭력에, 굳이 그 말을 써야 한다면 말이오, 굴복하는 것뿐일 테니까. 잘 생각해 보시오. 당신 오빠와 어머니의 운명이 당신 손에 달려 있소. 나는 당신의 노예가 되겠소……, 평생토록……. 나는 여기서 기다리겠소.\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 두냐에게서 여덟 걸음쯤 떨어진 소파에 앉았다. 이제 그녀에게는 그의 굽힐 줄 모르는 결의에 대해 한 점의 의심도 없었다. 게다가 그녀는 그를 알고 있었다. 그녀는 별안간 주머니에서 권총을 꺼내, 공이치기를 당기고는 손에 쥔 채 탁자 위에 올려놓았다. 스비드리가일로프가 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"아하! 그러니까 그거였군, 응?\" 그가 놀라면서도 짓궂게 웃으며 외쳤다. \"흠, 그러면 사정이 완전히 달라지지. 당신이 일을 놀랍도록 쉽게 만들어 주는군, 아브도티야 로마노브나. 한데 그 권총은 어디서 났소? 라주미힌 씨한테서요? 아니, 이건 내 권총이잖소, 오랜 친구로군! 내가 이걸 얼마나 찾아 헤맸는데! 시골에서 내가 당신에게 가르쳐 준 사격 수업이 헛되지 않았군그래.\"\n\n\"이건 당신 권총이 아니에요, 당신이 죽인 마르파 페트로브나의 것이었어요, 이 비열한 인간! 그 집에 당신 물건이라곤 아무것도 없었어요. 당신이 어떤 짓까지 저지를 수 있는지 의심하기 시작했을 때 내가 가져온 거예요. 감히 한 발짝이라도 다가오면, 맹세코 당신을 죽여 버리겠어요.\" 그녀는 제정신이 아니었다.\n\n\"하지만 당신 오빠는 어쩌고? 호기심에서 묻는 거요.\" 스비드리가일로프가 여전히 제자리에 선 채 말했다.\n\n\"신고하고 싶으면 하세요! 꼼짝 마요! 더 다가오지 마요! 쏘겠어요! 당신은 아내를 독살했어요, 나는 알아요. 당신 자신이 살인자라고요!\" 그녀는 권총을 겨눈 채 들고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 마르파 페트로브나를 독살했다고 그렇게 확신하오?\"\n\n\"당신이 했어요! 당신 스스로 그걸 암시했잖아요. 나에게 독약 이야기를 했어요……. 당신이 그걸 구하러 갔다는 걸 알아요……. 미리 준비해 두었던 거예요……. 그건 당신 짓이었어요……. 당신 짓이 틀림없어요……. 악당!\"\n\n\"설령 그게 사실이라 해도, 그건 당신 때문이었을 거요……. 당신이 그 원인이었을 테니까.\"\n\n\"거짓말! 나는 늘 당신을 증오했어요, 늘, 언제나…….\"\n\n\"오호, 아브도티야 로마노브나! 당신은 선동의 열기 속에서 나에게 마음이 누그러졌던 걸 잊은 모양이군. 나는 당신 눈에서 그걸 봤소. 그 달밤을 기억하오? 나이팅게일이 노래하던 그날 밤 말이오.\"\n\n\"거짓말이에요.\" 두냐의 두 눈에서 분노의 불꽃이 번뜩였다. \"거짓말이고 모함이에요!\"\n\n\"거짓말이라? 흠, 좋소, 당신이 원한다면 거짓말이라고 해 둡시다. 내가 지어낸 거요. 여자에게 그런 일을 상기시켜선 안 되는 거지.\" 그가 미소 지었다. \"당신이 쏘리라는 걸 나는 알고 있소, 이 어여쁜 들짐승 같으니. 자, 쏘시오!\"\n\n두냐는 권총을 들어 올린 채, 죽은 사람처럼 창백해져 거리를 가늠하며 그가 처음 움직이기를 기다리며 그를 노려보았다. 그녀의 아랫입술은 새하얗게 질려 떨리고 있었고, 큼직한 검은 두 눈은 불꽃처럼 번뜩였다. 그는 그녀가 그토록 아름다워 보인 적이 없었다. 그녀가 권총을 들어 올리는 순간 그녀의 두 눈에서 이글거리던 불꽃은 그를 불사르는 듯했고, 그의 가슴에는 고통스러운 통증이 일었다. 그가 한 걸음 앞으로 내디뎠고, 총성이 울렸다. 총알은 그의 머리카락을 스치고 뒤쪽 벽으로 날아가 박혔다. 그는 멈춰 선 채 나직이 웃었다.\n\n\"말벌이 나를 쏘았군. 곧장 내 머리를 겨냥했어. 이게 뭐지? 피인가?\" 그는 손수건을 꺼내 오른쪽 관자놀이를 따라 가느다란 줄기로 흘러내리는 피를 닦았다. 총알은 그저 살갗을 스치고 지나간 듯했다.\n\n두냐는 권총을 내리고는, 공포라기보다는 일종의 격렬한 경악에 차 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다. 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지조차 이해하지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n\"흠, 빗나갔군! 다시 쏘시오, 기다리겠소.\" 스비드리가일로프가 여전히 미소를 지으면서도 음울하게 나직이 말했다. \"그렇게 꾸물대다간, 당신이 다시 공이치기를 당기기 전에 내가 당신을 붙잡을 시간이 충분하겠소.\"\n\n두냐는 흠칫하며 재빨리 권총의 공이치기를 당기고는 다시 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"날 내버려 둬요.\" 그녀가 절망에 차 외쳤다. \"맹세코 다시 쏘겠어요. 내가…… 내가 당신을 죽이겠어요.\"\n\n\"흠……, 세 걸음 거리에서야 못 맞히기도 어렵지. 하지만 만약 못 맞힌다면……, 그땐.\" 그의 눈이 번뜩였고, 그는 두 걸음 앞으로 내디뎠다. 두냐가 다시 쏘았으나, 불발이었다.\n\n\"제대로 장전하지 않았군. 괜찮소, 거기 한 발이 더 남아 있으니. 준비하시오, 기다리겠소.\"\n\n그는 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 그녀를 마주 본 채, 기다리며, 열에 들떠 격정에 찬, 고집스럽고 단호한 눈으로 그녀를 응시했다. 두냐는 그가 자기를 놓아주느니 차라리 죽음을 택하리라는 것을 알아차렸다. '그리고…… 이제 당연히, 두 걸음 거리에서라면 그녀는 그를 죽일 터였다!' 별안간 그녀는 권총을 내던져 버렸다.\n\n\"떨어뜨렸군!\" 스비드리가일로프가 놀라며 말하고는 깊은숨을 내쉬었다. 가슴에서 무거운 짐덩이가 굴러떨어진 듯했다. 어쩌면 그것은 단지 죽음의 공포만은 아니었으리라. 실은 그 순간 그는 죽음의 공포를 거의 느끼지도 못했을 것이다. 그것은 그 자신도 무어라 규정할 수 없는, 더 어둡고 더 쓰라린 또 다른 감정으로부터의 해방이었다.\n\n그는 두냐에게 다가가 그녀의 허리를 살며시 감싸 안았다. 그녀는 저항하지 않은 채, 그저 나뭇잎처럼 떨면서 애원하는 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그는 뭔가 말하려 했으나, 입술만 움직일 뿐 소리를 내지 못했다.\n\n\"놓아줘요.\" 두냐가 간청했다. 스비드리가일로프가 몸서리쳤다. 이제 그녀의 목소리는 완전히 달라져 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 당신은 나를 사랑하지 않는 거요?\" 그가 나직이 물었다. 두냐는 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그리고…… 앞으로도 사랑할 수 없는 거요? 결코?\" 그가 절망에 차 속삭였다.\n\n\"결코!\"\n\n스비드리가일로프의 가슴속에 끔찍하고도 말 없는 투쟁의 한순간이 이어졌다. 그는 형언할 수 없는 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다. 별안간 그는 팔을 거두고 재빨리 창가로 돌아서서 그것을 마주 보고 섰다. 또 한순간이 흘렀다.\n\n\"여기 열쇠가 있소.\"\n\n그는 외투 왼쪽 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내, 돌아서지도 두냐를 쳐다보지도 않은 채, 등 뒤 탁자 위에 내려놓았다.\n\n\"가져가시오! 어서!\"\n\n그는 고집스레 창밖을 내다보았다. 두냐는 열쇠를 집으러 탁자로 다가갔다.\n\n\"어서! 어서!\" 스비드리가일로프가 여전히 돌아서지도 움직이지도 않은 채 거듭 말했다. 그러나 그 \"어서\"라는 말의 어조에는 뭔가 끔찍한 의미가 담겨 있는 듯했다.\n\n두냐는 그것을 알아채고는 열쇠를 낚아채 문으로 날아가, 재빨리 잠금을 풀고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다. 잠시 후, 그녀는 정신없이 X 다리 쪽으로 운하 강둑을 따라 달려 나갔다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 삼 분 동안 창가에 선 채로 있었다. 마침내 그는 천천히 돌아서서 주위를 둘러보고는 한 손으로 이마를 쓸었다. 기묘한 미소가 그의 얼굴을 일그러뜨렸으니, 가련하고 슬프고 힘없는 미소, 절망의 미소였다. 이미 굳어 가던 피가 그의 손에 묻어났다. 그는 성난 눈으로 그것을 바라보다가, 수건을 적셔 관자놀이를 닦았다. 두냐가 내던진 권총이 문가에 떨어져 있었는데, 문득 그의 눈에 띄었다. 그는 그것을 집어 들어 살펴보았다. 구식으로 만들어진 작은 휴대용 삼연발 권총이었다. 그 안에는 아직 두 발의 탄환과 한 개의 뇌관이 남아 있었다. 한 번 더 쏠 수 있었다. 그는 잠시 생각하다가, 권총을 주머니에 넣고 모자를 집어 들고는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n그날 저녁 그는 열 시까지 이 술집 저 술집을 전전하며 보냈다. 카탸도 나타나 또 다른 저속한 노래를 불렀는데, 어떤 \"악당이자 폭군\"이\n\"카탸에게 입을 맞추기 시작했네\" 하는 노래였다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 카탸와 손풍금쟁이, 그리고 몇몇 가수들과 종업원들, 거기에 두 명의 말단 서기에게도 술을 샀다. 그가 이 서기들에게 유독 끌린 까닭은, 두 사람 다 코가 비뚤어져 있었기 때문이다 — 한 사람은 왼쪽으로, 다른 한 사람은 오른쪽으로. 결국 그들은 그를 어느 유원지로 데려갔고, 그가 입장료를 치렀다. 그 정원에는 비쩍 마른 삼 년생 소나무 한 그루와 관목 세 그루, 그 밖에 \"복스홀\"이라는 것이 있었는데, 실은 차도 파는 술집에 불과했고 그 주위에 초록색 탁자와 의자가 몇 개 놓여 있었다. 형편없는 합창단과, 술에 취했으나 지독히도 의기소침한, 코가 빨간 뮌헨 출신의 독일인 광대 하나가 손님들을 즐겁게 했다. 서기들은 다른 서기들과 시비가 붙어 곧 싸움이라도 벌어질 듯했다. 스비드리가일로프가 그 분쟁의 심판으로 뽑혔다. 그는 십오 분 동안이나 그들의 말을 들었지만, 어찌나 큰 소리로 떠들어 대는지 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다. 다만 한 가지 분명해 보이는 사실은, 그들 가운데 하나가 무언가를 훔쳐서는 그 자리에서 어느 유대인에게 팔아넘기기까지 했으면서도 동료와 그 몫을 나누려 하지 않는다는 것이었다. 마침내 밝혀진 바로는, 그 훔친 물건이란 복스홀에 속한 찻숟가락 하나였다. 그것이 없어진 것이 드러나 일이 성가시게 되어 가기 시작했다. 스비드리가일로프는 숟가락 값을 치르고 일어나 정원을 나섰다. 여섯 시쯤이었다. 그동안 그는 포도주를 단 한 방울도 마시지 않았고, 무엇보다 겉모양을 차리느라 차를 시켰을 뿐이었다.\n\n어둡고 숨 막히는 저녁이었다. 열 시쯤 위협적인 먹구름이 하늘을 덮었다. 천둥이 한 차례 치더니, 폭포처럼 비가 쏟아졌다. 빗물은 방울방울 떨어지는 것이 아니라 줄기를 이루어 대지를 두드렸다. 일 분마다 번개가 번쩍였고, 그 번쩍임은 다섯을 셀 만큼 길게 이어졌다.\n\n물에 흠뻑 젖은 채 그는 집으로 돌아와 문을 잠그고, 책상을 열어 돈을 모조리 꺼내고는 서류 두세 장을 찢어 버렸다. 그러고는 돈을 주머니에 넣고 옷을 갈아입으려 했으나, 창밖을 내다보며 천둥과 빗소리에 귀를 기울이다가 그 생각을 접고, 모자를 집어 들고는 문도 잠그지 않은 채 방을 나섰다. 그는 곧장 소냐에게 향했다. 그녀는 집에 있었다.\n\n그녀는 혼자가 아니었다. 카페르나우모프네 네 아이들이 그녀와 함께 있었다. 그녀는 아이들에게 차를 따라 주고 있었다. 그녀는 흠뻑 젖은 그의 옷을 의아하게 바라보며 공손한 침묵으로 스비드리가일로프를 맞았다. 아이들은 모두 형언할 수 없는 공포에 휩싸여 일제히 달아나 버렸다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 탁자에 앉아 소냐에게 옆에 앉으라고 청했다. 그녀는 머뭇거리며 들을 채비를 했다.\n\n\"저는 어쩌면 아메리카로 가게 될지도 모릅니다, 소피야 세묘노브나.\" 스비드리가일로프가 말했다. \"그리고 아마 당신을 보는 게 이번이 마지막일 테니, 몇 가지 정리해 둘 것이 있어 왔습니다. 그래, 오늘 그 부인을 만났습니까? 그녀가 당신에게 뭐라고 했는지 압니다, 말하지 않아도 돼요.\" (소냐가 움찔하며 얼굴을 붉혔다.) \"그런 사람들은 일을 처리하는 자기들만의 방식이 있지요. 당신의 동생들과 남동생으로 말하자면, 그들은 정말로 살길이 마련되었고, 그들에게 배정된 돈은 내가 안전한 곳에 맡겨 두고 보관증도 받아 두었습니다. 무슨 일이 생길 때를 대비해, 당신이 그 영수증을 맡아 두는 편이 좋겠어요. 자, 받으세요! 그럼 이제 그건 됐고. 여기 삼천 루블어치의 오 퍼센트 채권 세 장이 있습니다. 이것은 당신 몫이니, 오롯이 당신을 위해 받아 두세요. 그리고 무슨 소릴 듣더라도, 아무도 알지 못하도록 이건 우리 둘만의 비밀로 해 둡시다. 당신에게는 그 돈이 필요할 겁니다. 예전처럼 사는 것은, 소피야 세묘노브나, 좋지 못하고, 게다가 이제는 그럴 필요도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신께 너무도 큰 신세를 졌어요, 아이들도, 새어머니도요.\" 소냐가 황급히 말했다. \"그리고 제가 그동안 말씀을 너무 적게 드렸다면…… 부디 그렇게 여기지 말아 주세요…….\"\n\n\"됐습니다! 됐어요!\"\n\n\"하지만 그 돈으로 말하자면, 아르카디 이바노비치, 정말 감사하지만, 저는 이제 그게 필요 없어요. 저는 언제든 제 힘으로 먹고살 수 있어요. 제가 은혜를 모른다고 생각하지 마세요. 그렇게 자비를 베푸실 거라면, 그 돈은…….\"\n\n\"이건 당신 것입니다, 당신 것이에요, 소피야 세묘노브나. 부디 그 일로 말을 낭비하지 마세요. 제겐 그럴 시간이 없습니다. 당신에게 필요할 거예요. 로디온 로마노비치에게는 두 갈래 길이 있습니다. 머리에 총알을 박든가, 아니면 시베리아든가.\" (소냐가 사납게 그를 바라보며 흠칫했다.) \"불안해하지 마세요, 나는 그에게서 직접 다 들었고, 떠벌리는 사람이 아닙니다. 아무에게도 말하지 않아요. 당신이 그에게 자수해서 죄를 고백하라고 권한 것은 좋은 충고였습니다. 그에게는 훨씬 나을 거예요. 자, 만일 시베리아로 결판이 난다면, 그는 갈 것이고 당신은 그를 따라가겠지요. 그렇지 않습니까? 그렇다면 돈이 필요할 겁니다. 그를 위해 필요할 거예요, 아시겠습니까? 당신에게 주는 것은 그에게 주는 것과 같습니다. 게다가 당신은 아말리야 이바노브나에게 빚을 갚겠다고 약속했지요. 내가 들었습니다. 어떻게 그토록 경솔하게 그런 의무를 떠맡을 수 있습니까, 소피야 세묘노브나? 그건 카테리나 이바노브나의 빚이지 당신 빚이 아니니, 그 독일 여자 따위는 신경 쓰지 말았어야 했어요. 그래서야 세상을 헤쳐 나갈 수 없습니다. 혹시라도 누가 나에 대해 당신에게 묻거든 — 내일이나 모레쯤 물어볼 텐데 — 지금 내가 당신을 찾아온 일은 한마디도 말하지 말고, 그 돈도 누구에게도 보이거나 입에 올리지 마세요. 자, 이제 안녕히.\" (그가 일어섰다.) \"로디온 로마노비치에게 안부를 전해 주세요. 그건 그렇고, 그 돈은 당분간 라주미힌 씨에게 맡겨 두는 게 좋겠습니다. 라주미힌 씨를 알지요? 물론 알겠지요. 나쁜 친구가 아니에요. 내일이나…… 때가 되거든 그에게 가져다주세요. 그때까지는 잘 숨겨 두고요.\"\n\n소냐도 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 어찌할 바를 모르고 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다. 그녀는 무언가 말하고 싶고, 묻고 싶었으나, 처음 얼마간은 감히 그러지 못했고 어떻게 시작해야 할지도 알지 못했다.\n\n\"어떻게…… 어떻게 지금, 이런 빗속에 가시려고요?\"\n\n\"아니, 아메리카로 떠나려는데 비 따위에 발이 묶이겠습니까! 하하! 안녕히, 소피야 세묘노브나, 그대여! 부디 오래오래 사세요, 당신은 다른 이들에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 테니. 그건 그렇고…… 라주미힌 씨에게 내 안부를 전한다고 일러 주세요. 아르카디 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프가 안부를 전하더라고요. 꼭 그렇게 해 주세요.\"\n\n그는 소냐를 의아한 불안과 막연한 두려움 속에 남겨 둔 채 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n나중에 밝혀진 바로는, 바로 그날 저녁 열한 시 이십 분에 그는 또 한 차례 매우 기이하고 뜻밖의 방문을 했다. 비는 여전히 그치지 않고 있었다. 그는 물에 흠뻑 젖은 채, 바실리예프스키섬 삼번가에 있는, 자기 약혼녀의 부모가 사는 작은 셋집으로 걸어 들어갔다. 그는 한참을 두드리고 나서야 안으로 들어갈 수 있었고, 그의 방문은 처음에 큰 소동을 일으켰다. 그러나 스비드리가일로프는 마음만 먹으면 무척 매력적일 수 있었던지라, 이 분별 있는 부모가 처음에 떠올렸던, 사실 무척이나 영리한 추측 — 스비드리가일로프가 아마도 술을 너무 마셔 제정신이 아닌 모양이라는 생각 — 은 곧바로 사라져 버렸다. 다정하고 분별 있는 어머니가 노쇠한 아버지를 휠체어에 태워 스비드리가일로프 앞으로 밀고 나왔고, 그녀는 여느 때처럼 이런저런 동떨어진 질문들로 대화를 시작했다. 그녀는 결코 직접적으로 묻는 법이 없었고, 우선 미소를 지으며 두 손을 비비다가, 만일 무언가를 — 이를테면 스비드리가일로프가 언제쯤 결혼식을 올리고 싶어 하는지를 — 알아내야만 할 때면, 파리며 그곳 궁정 생활에 관한 흥미롭고 거의 들뜬 질문들로 운을 떼고는, 그저 차츰차츰 화제를 삼번가 쪽으로 돌려 가곤 했다. 다른 때 같으면 이것이 물론 무척 인상적이었겠지만, 이번에는 아르카디 이바노비치가 유난히 조바심을 내며, 무엇보다 먼저 약혼녀가 이미 잠자리에 들었다는 말을 들었는데도 당장 그녀를 보겠다고 고집을 부렸다. 처녀는 물론 나타났다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 그녀에게 곧바로, 매우 중대한 용무로 당분간 페테르부르크를 떠나야만 한다고 알리고는, 그래서 그녀에게 만 오천 루블을 가져왔으니 자기가 주는 선물로 받아 달라고 청했다. 결혼식 전에 이 사소한 선물을 그녀에게 하려고 진작부터 마음먹고 있었다는 것이었다. 이 선물과 그의 임박한 출발 사이의 논리적 연관, 그리고 그 목적으로 한밤중 쏟아지는 빗속에 굳이 그들을 찾아와야만 했던 절대적인 필요성은 분명히 설명되지 않았다. 그러나 모든 것이 무척 순조롭게 흘러갔다. 으레 따르기 마련인 놀라움과 아쉬움의 탄성, 으레 나오기 마련인 질문들마저 놀라울 만큼 적고 절제되어 있었다. 반면 표현된 감사만은 더없이 뜨거웠고, 더없이 분별 있는 그 어머니의 눈물로 한층 힘을 받았다. 스비드리가일로프는 일어나 웃으며 약혼녀에게 입을 맞추고 그녀의 뺨을 토닥이고는 곧 돌아오겠노라 말했는데, 그녀의 눈에서 어린아이 같은 호기심과 더불어 무언가 간절하고 말 없는 물음 같은 것을 알아채고는, 잠시 생각하다가 다시 한번 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 비록 자신의 선물이 더없이 분별 있는 그 어머니의 손에 곧장 자물쇠로 채워져 보관되리라는 생각에 속으로는 진심 어린 분노를 느꼈지만 말이다. 그는 그들 모두를 엄청난 흥분 속에 남겨 둔 채 떠났는데, 다정한 어머니는 반쯤 속삭이듯 나직이 말하며 그들의 가장 중대한 의혹 몇 가지를 정리했으니, 스비드리가일로프는 위대한 인물이요, 큰 사업과 인맥을 지니고 막대한 부를 가진 사람이라 — 그가 속으로 무슨 생각을 품고 있는지 알 도리가 없다는 것이었다. 그는 기분 내키는 대로 여행을 떠나기도 하고 돈을 내주기도 하는 사람이니, 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니라고 했다. 물론 그가 흠뻑 젖어 있는 것은 이상한 일이지만, 이를테면 영국인들은 더더욱 기이하며, 그런 상류 사회 사람들은 남들이 자기에 대해 뭐라고 하든 개의치 않고 격식을 차리지 않는다는 것이었다. 어쩌면 정말로 그는 자기가 누구도 두려워하지 않는다는 것을 보이려고 일부러 그런 꼴로 온 것인지도 몰랐다. 무엇보다도 이 일은 한마디도 입 밖에 내서는 안 되니, 무슨 일이 생길지 하느님만이 아실 일이고, 돈은 자물쇠로 채워 두어야 하며, 요리사 페도시야가 부엌을 떠나지 않은 것이 천만다행이라고 했다. 그리고 무엇보다도 저 늙은 암고양이 같은 레슬리히 부인에게는 한마디도 해서는 안 된다는 둥, 이런저런 이야기가 이어졌다. 그들은 두 시까지 속닥거리며 앉아 있었지만, 처녀는 어리둥절하고 어딘지 서글픈 마음으로 훨씬 일찍 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n한편 스비드리가일로프는 정각 자정에 다리를 건너 본토 쪽으로 돌아가는 길이었다. 비는 그쳤고 바람이 윙윙 울부짖었다. 그는 몸을 떨기 시작했고, 한순간 작은 네바강의 검은 물을 유달리 흥미로운, 아니 무언가를 캐묻는 듯한 눈길로 가만히 응시했다. 그러나 곧 물가에 서 있으려니 몹시 추워졌다. 그는 돌아서서 Y 대로 쪽으로 향했다. 그는 그 끝없는 거리를 오래도록, 거의 반 시간 가까이 걸으며, 어둠 속 나무 포도에서 몇 번이고 발을 헛디뎠으나, 줄곧 거리 오른편에서 무언가를 찾고 있었다. 그는 요즘 이 거리를 지나다니며, 끝자락 어디쯤에 나무로 지은, 그래도 제법 큰 호텔이 하나 있다는 것을 눈여겨봐 두었는데, 그 이름이 아드리아노플인가 뭔가 하는 것이었다고 기억하고 있었다. 그의 기억은 틀리지 않았다. 그 호텔은 그 하느님께 버림받은 곳에서 워낙 눈에 띄어, 어둠 속에서도 못 보고 지나칠 리가 없었다. 그것은 거뭇하게 변색된 길쭉한 목조 건물이었는데, 늦은 시각인데도 창마다 불이 켜져 있고 안에서 인기척이 났다. 그는 안으로 들어가, 복도에서 마주친 누더기 차림의 사내에게 방 하나를 청했다. 사내는 스비드리가일로프를 훑어보더니 정신을 차리고는, 곧바로 그를 멀찍이 떨어진, 복도 끝 계단 밑의 비좁고 답답한 작은 방으로 안내했다. 다른 방은 없었으니, 모두 차 있었던 것이다. 누더기 차림의 사내가 캐묻듯 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"차는 있나?\" 스비드리가일로프가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 있습니다.\"\n\n\"또 뭐가 있지?\"\n\n\"송아지 고기, 보드카, 안주가 있습니다.\"\n\n\"차와 송아지 고기를 가져오게.\"\n\n\"그것 말고는 아무것도 안 드십니까?\" 사내가 적이 놀란 듯 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도, 아무것도 필요 없네.\"\n\n누더기 차림의 사내는 완전히 김이 빠진 채 물러갔다.\n\n'괜찮은 곳임에 틀림없군.' 스비드리가일로프는 생각했다. '어쩌다 내가 이런 곳을 몰랐을까? 아마 나는 카페 샹탕에서 나와 오는 길에 무슨 모험이라도 겪은 사람처럼 보이겠지. 누가 여기 묵었는지 알면 흥미로울 텐데.'\n\n그는 촛불을 켜고 방을 좀 더 찬찬히 살펴보았다. 그 방은 천장이 너무 낮아 스비드리가일로프가 간신히 똑바로 설 수 있을 정도였다. 창이 하나 있었고, 무척 더러운 침대와, 칠이 거칠게 된 의자와 탁자가 방을 거의 다 채우고 있었다. 벽은 판자로 된 것처럼 보였고, 다 해진 벽지가 발려 있었는데, 어찌나 찢기고 먼지투성이인지 무늬는 분간할 수 없었으나, 전체적인 색깔 — 노란색 — 만큼은 그래도 알아볼 수 있었다. 한쪽 벽은 비스듬한 천장에 가로막혀 짧게 잘려 있었으니, 다락방은 아니고 그저 계단 밑이었다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 촛불을 내려놓고 침대에 앉아 생각에 잠겼다. 그러나 옆방에서 때때로 고함으로 치솟곤 하는 기이하고도 집요한 웅얼거림이 그의 주의를 끌었다. 그 웅얼거림은 그가 방에 들어선 순간부터 그친 적이 없었다. 그는 귀를 기울였다. 누군가가 거의 눈물이라도 흘릴 듯 책망하고 꾸짖고 있었으나, 들리는 것은 한 사람의 목소리뿐이었다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 일어나 손으로 불빛을 가렸고, 곧 벽 틈으로 새어 나오는 빛을 보았다. 그는 다가가 틈으로 들여다보았다. 그의 방보다 다소 큰 그 방에는 두 사람이 있었다. 그중 하나는 머리가 곱슬곱슬하고 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 사내로, 웃옷을 벗은 채 균형을 잡으려고 두 다리를 넓게 벌리고 서서 제 가슴을 치며 웅변가 같은 자세를 취하고 있었다. 그는 상대를 거지라느니, 아무런 지체도 없는 자라느니 하며 나무랐다. 그는 자기가 저자를 시궁창에서 건져 냈으며, 마음만 먹으면 언제든 내쫓을 수 있고, 이 모든 것은 오직 신의 손가락만이 굽어본다고 떠들어 댔다. 그 책망의 대상은 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 몹시 재채기를 하고 싶지만 못하는 사람 같은 꼴이었다. 그는 이따금 멍하고 흐리멍덩한 눈을 그 말하는 자에게 돌리곤 했으나, 분명 그가 무슨 말을 하는지 도무지 알지 못했고 제대로 듣지도 못하는 것이 분명했다. 탁자 위에서는 초 한 자루가 다 타들어 가고 있었고, 포도주 잔들과 거의 비어 가는 보드카 병 하나, 빵과 오이, 그리고 김빠진 차 찌꺼기가 남은 잔들이 놓여 있었다. 스비드리가일로프는 이 광경을 유심히 들여다보다가 무심히 돌아서서 침대에 앉았다.\n\n차를 들고 돌아온 누더기 차림의 종업원은 또다시 더 필요한 것이 없느냐고 묻지 않을 수 없었고, 또다시 없다는 대답을 듣고는 마침내 물러갔다. 스비드리가일로프는 몸을 덥히려 서둘러 차 한 잔을 마셨으나, 아무것도 먹을 수가 없었다. 그는 열이 오르는 것을 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 웃옷을 벗고 담요를 두른 채 침대에 누웠다. 그는 짜증이 났다. '하필 이런 때에 건강했더라면 좋았을 것을.' 그는 미소를 지으며 생각했다. 방은 답답했고, 촛불은 희미하게 타올랐으며, 밖에서는 바람이 울부짖었고, 구석에서 생쥐가 무언가를 갉아 대는 소리가 들렸으며, 방에서는 쥐 냄새와 가죽 냄새가 났다. 그는 일종의 몽상에 잠겨 누워 있었다. 한 생각이 또 다른 생각으로 이어졌다. 그는 무언가에 상상을 붙들어 매고 싶은 갈망을 느꼈다. '창밖에 정원이 있나 보군.' 그는 생각했다. '나무 소리가 나는군. 폭풍우 치는 밤, 어둠 속에서 나는 저 나무 소리가 어찌나 싫은지! 사람을 끔찍한 기분에 빠뜨린단 말이야.' 그는 방금 페트로프스키 공원을 지날 때 그 소리가 얼마나 싫었는지를 떠올렸다. 이것이 작은 네바강의 다리를 떠올리게 했고, 그는 거기 서 있을 때처럼 다시 한기를 느꼈다. '나는 물을 좋아한 적이 없어.' 그는 생각했다. '풍경 속의 물이라 해도 말이지.' 그러고는 문득 어떤 기이한 생각에 또다시 미소를 지었다. '이 마당에 이런 취향이니 안락이니 하는 문제가 무슨 상관이람. 그런데도 나는 더 까다로워졌어, 마치…… 그런 때를 위해 특별한 자리를 골라 두는 짐승처럼 말이야. 페트로프스키 공원으로 갔어야 했는데! 어두컴컴하고 추워 보여서 그랬겠지, 하하! 마치 내가 유쾌한 기분이라도 찾는 것처럼!…… 그건 그렇고, 왜 촛불을 끄지 않았지?' 그는 촛불을 불어 껐다. '옆방 사람들은 잠자리에 들었군.' 그는 틈에서 빛이 보이지 않는 것을 보고 생각했다. '자, 마르파 페트로브나, 이제 당신이 나타날 때가 되었군. 어둡고, 당신에게 딱 알맞은 때와 장소이니 말이야. 하지만 이제 당신은 오지 않겠지!'\n\n그는 두냐에게 손을 대려던 한 시간 전에, 라스콜니코프에게 그녀를 라주미힌의 손에 맡기라고 권했던 일을 문득 떠올렸다. '라스콜니코프가 짐작한 대로, 나는 정말로 나 자신을 괴롭히려고 그런 말을 한 것 같군. 하지만 그 라스콜니코프란 자는 얼마나 교활한 악당인가! 그는 꽤나 많은 일을 겪었어. 그 허튼소리를 다 떨쳐 내고 나면, 언젠가는 성공한 악당이 될지도 모르지. 하지만 지금 그는 삶에 너무도 안달이 나 있어. 이 젊은 녀석들은 그 점에서 경멸스럽단 말이야. 하지만 그 녀석은 될 대로 되라지! 제 좋을 대로 하라고, 나와는 상관없는 일이니까.'\n\n그는 잠들 수가 없었다. 차츰차츰 두냐의 모습이 눈앞에 떠올랐고, 전율이 그의 온몸을 휩쓸었다. '아니, 이제 그 모든 걸 단념해야 해.' 그는 정신을 추스르며 생각했다. '다른 걸 생각해야지. 참 기묘하고도 우스운 일이야. 나는 누구도 크게 미워해 본 적이 없고, 심지어 복수하고 싶은 마음조차 유독 품어 본 적이 없어. 그건 나쁜 징조야, 나쁜 징조, 나쁜 징조라고. 다투는 것도 좋아한 적이 없고, 성을 낸 적도 없지 — 그것도 나쁜 징조야. 게다가 방금 그녀에게 했던 그 약속들도 — 빌어먹을! 하지만 — 누가 알겠어 — 어쩌면 그녀가 어떻게든 나를 새사람으로 만들어 주었을지도……'\n\n그는 이를 갈며 다시 침묵에 잠겼다. 또다시 두냐의 모습이 눈앞에 떠올랐는데, 그녀가 처음으로 총을 쏘고 나서 공포에 질려 권총을 내리고는 멍하니 그를 바라보던 바로 그 모습이었다. 그래서 그가 일깨워 주지만 않았더라면 그는 두 번이라도 그녀를 붙잡을 수 있었고, 그녀는 손 하나 들어 자신을 지키려 하지 않았을 것이다. 그는 그 순간 그녀가 거의 가엾게 느껴졌던 것을, 가슴이 찌릿하게 아파 왔던 것을 떠올렸다……\n\n\"아이! 빌어먹을, 또 이 생각이군! 떨쳐 버려야 해!\"\n\n그는 깜박 잠이 들고 있었다. 열에 들뜬 오한도 가라앉았는데, 갑자기 이불 속에서 무언가가 그의 팔과 다리를 스쳐 지나가는 것 같았다. 그는 흠칫했다. '윽! 빌어먹을! 생쥐인가 보군.' 그는 생각했다. '탁자에 두고 온 송아지 고기 탓이야.' 담요를 걷어 내고 일어나 한기를 무릅쓰기가 끔찍이도 싫었으나, 별안간 또다시 무언가 불쾌한 것이 그의 다리를 스쳐 지나갔다. 그는 담요를 걷어 내고 촛불을 켰다. 열에 들뜬 한기로 몸을 떨며 그는 침대를 살피려 몸을 숙였다. 아무것도 없었다. 그가 담요를 흔들자 별안간 생쥐 한 마리가 시트 위로 뛰쳐나왔다. 그가 그것을 잡으려 했으나, 생쥐는 침대를 벗어나지 않은 채 지그재그로 이리저리 내달리며 그의 손가락 사이를 빠져나가고, 그의 손등을 타고 넘더니 별안간 베개 밑으로 쏜살같이 들어가 버렸다. 그가 베개를 내던졌으나, 순식간에 무언가가 그의 가슴팍으로 뛰어올라 몸을 가로질러 등줄기를 타고 셔츠 속으로 내달리는 것이 느껴졌다. 그는 신경질적으로 부르르 떨다가 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n방은 어두웠다. 그는 아까처럼 담요를 두른 채 침대에 누워 있었다. 창 밑에서는 바람이 울부짖고 있었다. '참 역겹기도 하지.' 그는 짜증스레 생각했다.\n\n그는 일어나 창을 등진 채 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉았다. '아예 안 자는 게 낫겠어.' 그는 마음을 정했다. 그러나 창에서는 차고 축축한 외풍이 들어왔다. 그는 일어나지 않은 채 담요를 끌어당겨 몸을 감쌌다. 그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았고, 생각하고 싶지도 않았다. 그러나 하나의 영상에 또 하나의 영상이 떠올랐고, 처음도 끝도 없는 두서없는 생각의 조각들이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다. 그는 졸음에 빠져들었다. 어쩌면 추위나, 습기, 혹은 어둠, 아니면 창 밑에서 울부짖으며 나무들을 뒤흔드는 바람이, 환상적인 것에 대한 일종의 집요한 갈망을 불러일으켰는지도 모른다. 그는 자꾸만 꽃의 영상에 마음을 머물렀다. 그는 매혹적인 화원을, 밝고 따스한, 거의 무더운 어느 날을, 어느 축제일을 — 삼위일체 축일을 떠올렸다. 향기로운 꽃들이 우거진, 영국식 취향의 아름답고 호화로운 시골 별장이 있었고, 집 둘레로 화단이 빙 둘러 있었다. 덩굴식물로 화환처럼 뒤덮인 현관 주위에는 장미 화단이 둘러져 있었다. 값진 양탄자가 깔린 가볍고 서늘한 계단은 도자기 화분에 담긴 진귀한 식물들로 장식되어 있었다. 그는 특히 창가에, 밝고 푸르고 굵고 긴 줄기 위로 고개를 숙인, 부드럽고 새하얗고 짙은 향기를 풍기는 수선화 다발들을 눈여겨보았다. 그는 그것들에게서 차마 떠나기 싫었으나, 계단을 올라 크고 천장이 높은 응접실로 들어갔는데, 거기에도 또다시 사방에 — 창가에, 발코니로 통하는 문에, 발코니 위에까지 — 꽃이 있었다. 바닥에는 갓 베어 낸 향기로운 건초가 흩뿌려져 있었고, 창문은 열려 있었으며, 신선하고 서늘하고 가벼운 공기가 방 안으로 들어왔다. 새들이 창 밑에서 지저귀고 있었고, 방 한가운데, 흰 새틴 천으로 덮인 탁자 위에는 관 하나가 놓여 있었다. 관은 흰 비단으로 덮이고 두툼한 흰 주름 장식으로 가장자리가 둘려 있었으며, 사방에서 꽃 화환이 그것을 둘러싸고 있었다. 그 꽃들 사이에, 흰 모슬린 드레스를 입은 한 소녀가, 두 팔을 가슴 위에 가지런히 포개고 누워 있었는데, 마치 대리석을 깎아 놓은 듯했다. 그러나 풀어 헤쳐진 그녀의 금빛 머리칼은 젖어 있었고, 머리에는 장미 화환이 얹혀 있었다. 엄숙하고 이미 굳어 버린 그녀 얼굴의 옆모습 역시 대리석으로 새긴 것 같았으며, 창백한 입술에 어린 미소에는 어린아이답지 않은 엄청난 비탄과 슬픈 호소가 가득했다. 스비드리가일로프는 그 소녀를 알고 있었다. 관 곁에는 성상도, 타오르는 촛불도 없었고, 기도 소리도 없었다. 그 소녀는 스스로 물에 몸을 던진 것이었다. 그녀는 겨우 열네 살이었으나, 그 마음은 부서져 버린 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는 스스로를 파멸시켰으니, 그 어린 영혼을 경악시키고 소스라치게 한 어떤 모욕에 짓눌려, 그 천사 같은 순결이 분에 넘치는 치욕으로 더럽혀지고, 차갑고 축축한 어둠의 밤, 바람이 울부짖는 가운데 누구의 귀에도 닿지 못한 채 잔혹하게 외면당한 마지막 절망의 비명이 그녀에게서 터져 나왔던 것이다……\n\n스비드리가일로프는 정신을 차리고 침대에서 일어나 창가로 갔다. 그는 더듬어 걸쇠를 찾아 창을 열었다. 바람이 작은 방 안으로 사납게 휘몰아쳐, 셔츠만 걸친 그의 얼굴과 가슴을 서리처럼 찔러 댔다. 창 밑에는 정원 비슷한 것이, 보아하니 유원지 같은 것이 있는 듯했다. 거기에도 아마 낮이면 찻상이 놓이고 노랫소리가 울렸으리라. 지금은 나무와 관목에서 빗방울이 창 안으로 흩날려 들어왔다. 마치 지하실 속처럼 캄캄해서, 그는 사물들의 거뭇한 윤곽 몇 개를 간신히 알아볼 수 있을 뿐이었다. 스비드리가일로프는 창턱에 팔꿈치를 괸 채 몸을 숙이고 오 분 동안 어둠 속을 응시했다. 대포 소리가, 뒤이어 두 번째 대포 소리가 밤의 어둠 속에 울려 퍼졌다. '아, 신호로군! 강물이 넘치고 있어.' 그는 생각했다. '아침이 되면 낮은 지대의 거리로 물이 소용돌이치며 흘러내려, 지하실과 지하 창고를 침수시키겠지. 지하실 쥐들이 헤엄쳐 나올 테고, 사람들은 제 살림살이를 위층으로 끌어 올리며 비바람 속에서 욕을 퍼붓겠지. 지금 몇 시쯤 됐을까?' 그가 채 그렇게 생각하기도 전에, 어디 가까운 곳에서 벽시계가 분주히 째깍거리다가 세 시를 쳤다.\n\n'아하! 한 시간이면 날이 밝겠군! 뭘 더 기다린담? 당장 나가서 곧장 공원으로 가야지. 거기서 비에 흠뻑 젖은 커다란 관목을 하나 고를 거야, 어깨가 닿기만 해도 수백만 개의 물방울이 머리 위로 뚝뚝 떨어지도록 말이야.'\n\n그는 창가에서 물러나 창을 닫고, 촛불을 켜고, 조끼와 외투를 입고 모자를 쓰고는, 촛불을 들고 복도로 나갔다. 초 토막들과 온갖 잡동사니 한가운데서 어디쯤 잠들어 있을 누더기 차림의 종업원을 찾아, 방값을 치르고 호텔을 떠나려는 것이었다. '지금이 가장 좋은 순간이야. 이보다 더 좋은 때를 고를 수는 없겠어.'\n\n그는 길고 좁은 복도를 한참 동안 걸었으나 아무도 찾지 못했고, 막 소리쳐 부르려던 참에, 별안간 낡은 찬장과 문 사이의 어두운 구석에서 살아 있는 것 같은 기이한 물체 하나가 눈에 들어왔다. 그가 촛불을 들고 몸을 숙여 보니, 다섯 살이 채 안 된 어린 계집아이가, 물에 푹 젖은 걸레짝처럼 옷이 흠뻑 젖은 채 떨며 울고 있었다. 아이는 스비드리가일로프를 무서워하는 것 같지는 않았고, 커다란 검은 두 눈으로 멍하니 놀란 듯 그를 바라보았다. 이따금 아이는, 한참을 울고 나서 이제 막 진정되기 시작하는 아이들이 그러듯, 흐느꼈다. 아이의 얼굴은 창백하고 지쳐 있었고, 추위로 곱아 있었다. '어쩌다 여기까지 왔을까? 여기 숨어서 밤새 한숨도 못 잔 모양이군.' 그는 아이에게 이것저것 묻기 시작했다. 아이는 갑자기 생기를 띠더니, 어린아이 말투로 \"엄마\"가 어떻고, \"엄마가 때릴 거야\" 하는 둥, \"깨뜨린\" 무슨 컵에 관해 재잘재잘 떠들어 댔다. 아이는 쉬지 않고 종알거렸다. 그는 아이의 말에서 그저 이렇게 짐작할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 아이는 보살핌을 받지 못하는 처지로, 그 어머니는 아마 이 호텔에서 일하는 술꾼 요리사일 텐데 아이를 때리고 겁주었던 것이고, 아이가 어머니의 컵 하나를 깨뜨리고는 너무 무서워 어제저녁 달아나, 한참을 밖에서 빗속 어딘가에 숨어 있다가, 마침내 이리로 기어 들어와 찬장 뒤에 숨어, 습기와 어둠, 그리고 그 일로 호되게 매를 맞으리라는 두려움에 울며 떨며 밤을 지새운 것이었다. 그는 아이를 품에 안고 방으로 돌아와 침대에 앉히고는, 아이의 옷을 벗기기 시작했다. 양말도 신지 않은 맨발에 신은 다 떨어진 신은, 밤새 웅덩이에 담가 두기라도 한 것처럼 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 옷을 벗긴 뒤 그는 아이를 침대에 눕히고, 이불을 덮고 머리부터 발끝까지 담요로 감싸 주었다. 아이는 곧바로 잠이 들었다. 그러고 나서 그는 다시 음울한 상념에 잠겼다.\n\n'괜한 짓으로 신경을 쓰다니 어리석기도 하지.' 그는 갑자기 짓누르는 듯한 짜증을 느끼며 마음을 정했다. '이 무슨 멍청한 짓인가!' 그는 짜증스레 촛불을 집어 들고, 다시 누더기 차림의 종업원을 찾아 서둘러 떠나려 했다. '빌어먹을 계집애 같으니!' 그는 문을 열며 생각했으나, 아이가 잠들었는지 보려고 다시 돌아섰다. 그는 조심스레 담요를 들어 올렸다. 아이는 깊이 잠들어 담요 밑에서 몸이 따뜻해졌고, 창백하던 두 뺨이 발그레해져 있었다. 그러나 이상하게도 그 홍조는 어린아이의 발그레한 뺨보다 더 짙고 거칠어 보였다. '열이 올라서 그런 거군.' 스비드리가일로프는 생각했다. 그것은 마치 술기운으로 인한 홍조 같았으니, 마치 누가 가득 찬 잔을 들이켜게 하기라도 한 것 같았다. 아이의 진홍빛 입술은 뜨겁게 달아올라 있었다. 그런데 이게 무슨 일인가? 그는 문득 아이의 길고 검은 속눈썹이 파르르 떨리는 것 같은 느낌이 들었으니, 마치 눈꺼풀이 열리며 그 속에서 교활하고 음흉한 눈 하나가, 어린아이답지 않게 눈을 찡긋하며 빠끔히 내다보는 것만 같았다. 마치 그 어린 계집아이가 잠든 게 아니라 자는 척하고 있는 것처럼. 그랬다, 정말 그러했다. 아이의 입술이 미소를 띠며 벌어졌다. 입가가 부르르 떨렸으니, 마치 그것을 억누르려 애쓰는 듯했다. 그러나 이제 아이는 모든 애를 완전히 포기했고, 이제 그것은 활짝 웃는 웃음, 한껏 벌어진 웃음이었다. 그 조금도 어린아이답지 않은 얼굴에는 무언가 뻔뻔스럽고 도발적인 것이 있었다. 그것은 타락이었고, 창녀의 얼굴이었으며, 프랑스 창녀의 뻔뻔한 낯짝이었다. 이제 두 눈이 활짝 열렸다. 그것은 달아오른, 뻔뻔한 눈길을 그에게 던졌고, 웃으며, 그를 꾀어 들였다……. 그 웃음에는, 그 두 눈에는, 어린아이의 얼굴에 어린 그 추잡함에는, 무한히 추악하고 충격적인 무언가가 있었다. \"뭐, 다섯 살짜리가?\" 스비드리가일로프는 진정한 공포에 휩싸여 중얼거렸다. \"이게 대체 무슨 일이야?\" 그리고 이제 아이는 환하게 달아오른 작은 얼굴로 그를 향해 돌아누우며 두 팔을 뻗었다……. \"이 저주받을 계집애!\" 스비드리가일로프는 아이를 내리치려 손을 치켜들며 소리쳤으나, 바로 그 순간 잠에서 깨어났다.\n\n그는 여전히 담요를 두른 채 같은 침대에 있었다. 촛불은 켜져 있지 않았고, 창으로는 햇빛이 흘러 들어오고 있었다.\n\n\"밤새 악몽에 시달렸군!\" 그는 화가 나서 일어났는데, 완전히 녹초가 된 느낌이었다. 뼈마디가 쑤셨다. 밖에는 짙은 안개가 끼어 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 거의 다섯 시였다. 그는 늦잠을 잔 것이다! 그는 일어나, 아직 축축한 재킷과 외투를 걸쳤다. 주머니 속의 권총이 만져지자 그것을 꺼냈다가 다시 자리에 앉아, 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼내 속표지의 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 큰 글씨로 몇 줄을 적었다. 그것을 다시 읽어 본 뒤, 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 생각에 잠겼다. 권총과 수첩이 그의 곁에 놓여 있었다. 파리 몇 마리가 깨어나, 여전히 탁자 위에 손도 대지 않은 채 놓여 있는 송아지 고기에 내려앉았다. 그는 그것들을 물끄러미 바라보다가, 마침내 비어 있는 오른손으로 한 마리를 잡으려 들기 시작했다. 그는 지칠 때까지 시도했으나 잡을 수 없었다. 마침내 자기가 이런 흥미로운 일에 몰두하고 있다는 것을 깨닫고는 흠칫하여, 일어서서 단호하게 방을 걸어 나갔다. 일 분 뒤 그는 거리에 나와 있었다.\n\n짙은 우윳빛 안개가 도시 위에 드리워 있었다. 스비드리가일로프는 미끄럽고 더러운 나무 포도를 따라 작은 네바강 쪽으로 걸었다. 그는 밤사이 불어난 작은 네바강의 물과, 페트로프스키섬과, 젖은 오솔길과, 젖은 풀과, 젖은 나무와 관목, 그리고 마침내 그 관목을 머릿속에 그려 보고 있었다……. 그는 다른 것을 생각해 보려고 언짢은 기색으로 집들을 노려보기 시작했다. 거리에는 마부도, 행인도 없었다. 밝은 노란빛의 작은 목조 가옥들은 덧문을 닫은 채 더럽고 풀이 죽어 보였다. 추위와 습기가 그의 온몸에 스며들었고, 그는 몸을 떨기 시작했다. 이따금 그는 상점 간판을 마주치면 하나하나 꼼꼼히 읽었다. 마침내 그는 나무 포도의 끝에 다다라 커다란 석조 건물 앞에 이르렀다. 더럽고 떨고 있는 개 한 마리가 다리 사이에 꼬리를 말아 넣은 채 그의 앞을 가로질러 갔다. 외투를 입은 사내 하나가 곤드레만드레 취해 보도를 가로질러 엎드려 죽은 듯 쓰러져 있었다. 그는 사내를 흘끗 보고는 계속 걸었다. 왼편에 높다란 탑 하나가 솟아 있었다. \"이런!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"여기야말로 제격이군. 왜 꼭 페트로프스키여야 하지? 어차피 공적인 증인이 지켜보는 가운데서 하게 될 테니까…….\"\n\n그는 이 새로운 생각에 거의 미소를 지으며, 탑이 있는 그 커다란 건물이 선 거리로 접어들었다. 건물의 굳게 닫힌 커다란 대문에, 회색 군용 외투를 두르고 머리에 구리로 된 아킬레우스 투구를 쓴 자그마한 사내 하나가 어깨를 문에 기댄 채 서 있었다. 그는 졸린 듯 무심한 눈길을 스비드리가일로프에게 던졌다. 그의 얼굴에는 유대 인종의 모든 얼굴에 예외 없이 시큰둥하게 찍혀 있는 그 영원한 심술궂은 우수의 표정이 어려 있었다. 두 사람은, 스비드리가일로프와 아킬레우스는, 몇 분 동안 아무 말 없이 서로를 빤히 바라보았다. 마침내 아킬레우스는, 취하지도 않은 사내가 자기에게서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에 서서 빤히 쳐다보며 한마디도 하지 않는 것이 이상하게 여겨졌다.\n\n\"여기서 뭘 하시오?\" 그는 꼼짝도 않고 자세도 바꾸지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아닐세, 이보게, 좋은 아침이군.\" 스비드리가일로프가 대답했다.\n\n\"여긴 그럴 데가 아니오.\"\n\n\"나는 외국으로 떠날 거라네, 이보게.\"\n\n\"외국으로?\"\n\n\"아메리카로.\"\n\n\"아메리카로.\"\n\n스비드리가일로프는 권총을 꺼내 공이치기를 당겼다. 아킬레우스가 눈썹을 치켜올렸다.\n\n\"이보시오, 여긴 그런 장난을 칠 데가 아니란 말이오!\"\n\n\"여기가 왜 그럴 데가 아니란 말인가?\"\n\n\"그럴 데가 아니니까 그렇소.\"\n\n\"뭐, 이보게, 나는 그런 건 상관없네. 좋은 곳이야. 누가 묻거든, 그냥 그자가 아메리카로 간다고 하더라고 말하게.\"\n\n그는 권총을 자기 오른쪽 관자놀이에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"여기선 그러면 안 되오, 여긴 그럴 데가 아니란 말이오!\" 아킬레우스가 정신이 번쩍 든 듯, 눈이 점점 더 휘둥그레지며 소리쳤다.\n\n스비드리가일로프는 방아쇠를 당겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n같은 날, 저녁 일곱 시쯤, 라스콜니코프는 어머니와 누이가 묵고 있는 셋집으로 가는 길이었다. 라주미힌이 그들을 위해 찾아 준, 바칼레예프의 집에 있는 그 셋집이었다. 계단은 거리에서 위로 이어져 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 갈까 말까 여전히 망설이는 듯, 굼뜬 걸음으로 걸었다. 그러나 그 무엇도 그를 돌이키게 하지는 못했을 것이다. 그의 결심은 이미 서 있었다.\n\n'게다가, 아무래도 상관없어, 아직 그들은 아무것도 모르니까.' 그는 생각했다. '그리고 그들은 나를 괴짜로 여기는 데 익숙하지.'\n\n그의 옷차림은 끔찍하리만치 형편없었다. 옷은 찢기고 더러웠으며, 밤새 내린 비에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 피로와, 비바람에 시달린 탓과, 스물네 시간 동안 이어진 내면의 갈등으로 거의 일그러져 있었다. 그는 지난밤을 통째로 어딘지도 모를 곳에서 홀로 보냈던 것이다. 그러나 어쨌든 그는 결심에 다다랐다.\n\n그는 문을 두드렸고, 문을 연 것은 어머니였다. 두냐는 집에 없었다. 하녀마저 마침 외출 중이었다. 처음에 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 기쁨과 놀라움에 말을 잇지 못했다. 그러다 그의 손을 잡고 방 안으로 끌어들였다.\n\n\"네가 왔구나!\" 그녀는 기쁨에 떨리는 목소리로 입을 열었다. \"로쟈, 이렇게 바보같이 눈물로 너를 맞는다고 화내지 마라. 우는 게 아니라 웃고 있는 거란다. 내가 운다고 생각했니? 아니야, 기뻐서 그래, 그저 이렇게 눈물을 흘리는 멍청한 버릇이 들어 버린 거야. 네 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 줄곧 이 모양이란다. 무슨 일에든 눈물이 나. 앉으렴, 얘야, 피곤하겠지. 피곤한 게 보이는구나. 아, 이렇게 흙투성이가 되다니.\"\n\n\"어제 비를 맞았어요, 어머니…….\" 라스콜니코프가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 황급히 말을 가로막았다. \"내가 예전처럼 여자들이나 하는 식으로 꼬치꼬치 캐물을 거라고 생각했겠지. 걱정 마라, 다 안다, 다 이해해. 이제 나도 이곳 방식을 익혔고, 정말이지 내가 보기에도 그편이 더 낫더구나. 한번에 마음을 정했단다. 내가 어떻게 네 계획들을 이해하고, 또 너더러 그걸 일일이 설명하라고 할 수 있겠니? 네가 무슨 걱정거리며 계획을 품고 있는지, 무슨 생각을 키우고 있는지 하느님만 아실 일인데. 그러니 내가 자꾸 네 팔꿈치를 쿡쿡 찌르며 무슨 생각을 하느냐고 물어볼 일이 아니지. 한데 어머나! 내가 왜 이렇게 미친 사람처럼 왔다 갔다 하는지……? 로쟈, 나는 잡지에 실린 네 글을 벌써 세 번째 읽고 있단다. 드미트리 프로코피치가 가져다주었어. 그걸 보자마자 나는 속으로 외쳤단다. '저런, 어리석은 것아,' 하고 생각했지. '저게 바로 그애가 몰두하고 있는 일이구나, 저게 수수께끼의 해답이야! 학식 있는 사람들은 다 저렇지. 지금 그애 머릿속엔 새로운 생각들이 들어 있을 거야. 그걸 곰곰이 따져 보고 있는데, 내가 괜히 그애를 성가시게 하고 어지럽히고 있는 거야.' 읽었단다, 얘야, 물론 이해 못 한 부분이 많았지만, 그야 당연하지. 내가 어떻게 알겠니?\"\n\n\"보여 주세요, 어머니.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 잡지를 받아 들고 자기 글을 훑어보았다. 그의 기분이나 처지와는 도무지 어울리지 않았지만, 그는 모든 작가가 활자화된 자기 자신을 처음 볼 때 느끼는, 그 야릇하고 씁쓸하면서도 달콤한 감각을 맛보았다. 게다가 그는 이제 겨우 스물세 살이었다. 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 몇 줄을 읽고 나자 그는 이맛살을 찌푸렸고, 가슴은 고통으로 욱신거렸다. 지난 몇 달 동안의 그 모든 내면의 갈등이 떠올랐다. 그는 혐오와 분노에 차서 잡지를 탁자 위로 내던졌다.\n\n\"하지만, 내가 아무리 어리석어도, 로쟈, 네가 머지않아 러시아 사상계의 지도적인 인물들 가운데 하나가, 아니 어쩌면 가장 앞서는 인물이 되리라는 건 내 눈으로도 알 수 있단다. 그런데 사람들이 감히 너를 미쳤다고 생각하다니! 너는 모르겠지만, 정말로 그렇게들 생각했어. 아, 그 비열한 인간들이, 천재를 어떻게 알아보겠니! 두냐도, 두냐마저도 하마터면 그 말을 믿을 뻔했단다 — 너는 그걸 어떻게 생각하니? 네 아버지도 잡지사에 두 번이나 원고를 보냈었지 — 처음엔 시였고(나한테 그 원고가 있으니 보여 주마), 두 번째엔 소설 한 편을 통째로 보냈단다(내가 베껴 쓰게 해 달라고 졸랐지). 그게 채택되게 해 달라고 우리가 얼마나 기도했는지 — 한데 안 됐어! 로쟈, 엿새인가 이레 전만 해도 나는 네 끼니며 옷이며 네가 사는 꼴 때문에 가슴이 미어졌단다. 하지만 이제 보니 내가 또 어리석었던 거야. 너는 네 지성과 재능으로 어떤 지위든 얻을 수 있으니까. 물론 지금은 그런 데 마음을 쓰지 않고 훨씬 더 중요한 일들에 매달려 있겠지만…….\"\n\n\"두냐는 집에 없나요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"없단다, 로쟈. 나도 그애를 자주 못 봐. 나를 혼자 두고 나가 버리거든. 드미트리 프로코피치가 나를 보러 와 준단다, 참 고마운 일이지, 그리고 늘 네 얘기를 해. 그 사람은 너를 사랑하고 존경한단다, 얘야. 두냐가 사려가 몹시 부족하다는 말은 아니야. 불평하는 게 아니란다. 그애한테는 그애 나름의 방식이 있고, 나한텐 내 방식이 있는 거지. 그애가 요즘 들어 무슨 비밀이 생긴 것 같은데, 나야 너희 둘한테 비밀이라곤 통 없잖니. 물론 두냐가 분별이 넘칠 만큼 많다는 건 확실하고, 게다가 그앤 너와 나를 사랑하지……, 하지만 이 모든 게 어디로 흘러갈지 모르겠구나. 네가 지금 와 줘서 나는 이리도 기쁜데, 그애는 나가는 바람에 너를 놓쳤구나. 그애가 들어오면 이렇게 말해 줘야지. '네가 나간 사이에 오라비가 다녀갔단다. 그동안 어디 가 있었니?' 로쟈, 너 나를 너무 응석받이로 만들면 안 돼, 알지. 올 수 있을 때 오렴, 못 오면 괜찮아, 나는 기다릴 수 있으니까. 어쨌든 네가 나를 아낀다는 걸 알 테니, 나한테는 그걸로 족하단다. 네가 쓰는 글을 읽고, 모두에게서 네 소식을 듣고, 가끔은 네가 직접 나를 보러 와 주고. 이보다 더 좋은 게 어디 있겠니? 봐라, 지금도 어미를 위로하러 와 줬잖니, 나는 다 안단다.\"\n\n여기서 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"또 이러는구나! 내 어리석음을 마음에 두지 마라. 어머나, 내가 왜 여기 이러고 앉아 있지?\" 그녀는 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"커피가 있는데 너한테 권하지도 않았구나. 아, 이게 늙은이의 이기심이라는 거야. 당장 가져오마!\"\n\n\"어머니, 수고하지 마세요, 저는 곧 가야 해요. 그것 때문에 온 게 아니거든요. 제발 제 말 좀 들어 주세요.\"\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 머뭇거리며 그에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"어머니, 무슨 일이 일어나든, 저에 대해 무슨 소문을 듣든, 사람들이 저에 대해 무슨 말을 하든, 지금처럼 늘 저를 사랑해 주시겠어요?\" 그는 자기 말을 생각하지도, 가늠하지도 않은 채, 가슴 가득 차오르는 마음에서 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"로쟈, 로쟈, 무슨 일이니? 어떻게 나한테 그런 걸 묻니? 아니, 누가 너에 대해 나한테 뭐라고 말하겠니? 게다가 나는 누구 말도 믿지 않을 거고, 아예 들으려고도 하지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"제가 늘 어머니를 사랑해 왔다는 걸 확실히 말씀드리려고 왔어요. 그리고 우리 둘만 있어서 다행이에요, 두냐가 없는 것까지도 기뻐요.\" 그는 똑같은 충동에 휩싸여 말을 이었다. \"비록 어머니가 불행해지시겠지만, 어머니 아들이 지금 자기 자신보다 어머니를 더 사랑한다는 것을, 그리고 제가 잔인하고 어머니를 아끼지 않는다고 어머니가 생각하셨던 게 모두 잘못된 거였다는 걸 믿어 주십사 말씀드리러 온 거예요. 저는 결코 어머니를 사랑하기를 멈추지 않을 겁니다……. 자, 이걸로 충분해요. 이렇게 해야 한다고, 이걸로 시작해야 한다고 생각했어요…….\"\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 말없이 그를 끌어안고 가슴에 꼭 품은 채 가만히 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"네가 뭐가 잘못된 건지 모르겠구나, 로쟈.\" 그녀가 마침내 입을 열었다. \"그동안 나는 우리가 그저 너를 성가시게 하는 거라고만 생각했는데, 이제 보니 너한테 커다란 슬픔이 닥쳐오고 있고, 그래서 네가 괴로운 거였구나. 나는 오래전부터 그걸 예감하고 있었단다, 로쟈. 이런 말을 하는 걸 용서해라. 자꾸 그 생각이 들어서 밤에 잠을 못 이룬단다. 네 누이가 어젯밤 내내 잠꼬대를 했는데, 온통 네 얘기뿐이었어. 몇 마디 알아들었지만, 무슨 뜻인지는 알 수가 없었지. 오늘 아침 내내 마치 교수형에라도 처해질 것처럼, 무언가를 기다리며, 무언가를 예감하며 지냈는데, 이제 그게 닥쳐온 거야! 로쟈, 로쟈, 어디로 가려는 거니? 어디론가 멀리 떠나는 거니?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았다! 나도 너랑 같이 갈 수 있어, 알지, 네가 나를 필요로 한다면. 두냐도 함께. 그앤 너를 사랑한단다, 너를 끔찍이 사랑해 — 그리고 네가 원하면 소피야 세묘노브나도 우리와 함께 가도 좋아. 봐라, 나는 그애를 딸로 여기는 것까지도 기쁘니까……. 드미트리 프로코피치가 우리가 같이 갈 수 있도록 도와줄 거야. 한데……, 어디로……, 가려는 거니?\"\n\n\"안녕히 계세요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 오늘?\" 그녀는 그를 영영 잃기라도 하는 듯 외쳤다.\n\n\"머무를 수가 없어요, 지금 가야 해요…….\"\n\n\"나도 너랑 같이 갈 순 없니?\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 그저 무릎 꿇고 저를 위해 하느님께 기도해 주세요. 어머니 기도라면 어쩌면 그분께 가닿을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"너를 축복하고 성호를 그어 주마. 됐다, 됐어. 오, 하느님, 우리가 지금 무엇을 하고 있는 건지?\"\n\n그렇다, 그는 기뻤다, 거기에 아무도 없다는 것이, 어머니와 단둘이라는 것이 그는 무척 기뻤다. 그 끔찍한 몇 달이 지난 뒤 처음으로 그의 마음이 누그러졌다. 그는 어머니 앞에 엎드려 그 발에 입을 맞추었고, 둘은 서로 끌어안은 채 울었다. 그리고 이번에 그녀는 놀라지도, 그에게 캐묻지도 않았다. 며칠 전부터 그녀는 자기 아들에게 무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지고 있으며, 이제 그에게 어떤 무서운 순간이 닥쳐왔음을 깨닫고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"로쟈, 내 사랑, 내 맏이.\" 그녀가 흐느끼며 말했다. \"지금 너는 꼭 어릴 적 모습이로구나. 그땐 이렇게 나한테 달려와 끌어안고 입을 맞추곤 했지. 네 아버지가 살아 계시고 우리가 가난했을 때, 너는 그저 우리 곁에 있어 주는 것만으로 우리를 위로했단다. 그리고 네 아버지를 묻었을 때, 우리가 그 무덤가에서 얼마나 자주 함께 울며 끌어안았는지, 지금처럼 말이야. 요즘 내가 울었다면, 그건 어미의 마음이 화가 닥칠 걸 예감했기 때문이야. 처음 너를 본 그날 저녁, 기억하니, 우리가 여기 도착하자마자, 나는 단지 네 눈빛만 보고도 짐작했단다. 그 순간 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았고, 오늘 문을 열고 너를 봤을 땐, 운명의 시각이 왔구나 싶었지. 로쟈, 로쟈, 오늘 떠나는 건 아니지?\"\n\n\"아니에요!\"\n\"또 올 거지?\"\n\n\"네……, 올게요.\"\n\n\"로쟈, 화내지 마라, 나는 감히 너한테 캐묻지 않으마. 그래선 안 된다는 걸 안다. 그저 두 마디만 해 다오 — 네가 가는 곳이 머니?\"\n\n\"아주 멀어요.\"\n\n\"거기서 너를 기다리는 게 무엇이니? 무슨 자리나 일자리라도 있는 거니?\"\n\n\"하느님이 보내 주시는 것이지요……, 그저 저를 위해 기도해 주세요.\" 라스콜니코프는 문 쪽으로 갔지만, 그녀가 그를 붙들고 절망적인 눈길로 그의 눈을 들여다보았다. 그녀의 얼굴은 공포로 일그러졌다.\n\n\"이제 됐어요, 어머니.\" 라스콜니코프가, 여기 온 것을 깊이 후회하며 말했다.\n\n\"영영은 아니지, 아직 영영은 아니지? 올 거지, 내일 올 거지?\"\n\n\"올게요, 올게요, 안녕히 계세요.\" 그는 마침내 몸을 떼어 냈다.\n\n따뜻하고 상쾌하고 밝은 저녁이었다. 아침나절에 날이 개었던 것이다. 라스콜니코프는 자기 하숙집으로 향했다. 그는 서둘렀다. 해가 지기 전에 모든 것을 끝내고 싶었다. 그때까지는 아무와도 마주치고 싶지 않았다. 계단을 올라가다가 그는 나스타시야가 사모바르 곁에서 달려 나와 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보는 것을 알아챘다. '누가 나를 보러 온 건가?' 그는 의아해했다. 포르피리의 모습이 역겹게 떠올랐다. 그러나 문을 열자 두냐가 보였다. 그녀는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 홀로 앉아 있었는데, 오래 기다린 사람 같았다. 그는 문간에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그녀는 당황하여 소파에서 일어나 그를 마주하고 섰다. 그를 응시하는 그녀의 눈에는 공포와 끝없는 비탄이 어려 있었다. 그리고 그 눈빛만으로 그는 그녀가 알고 있다는 것을 단번에 알아차렸다.\n\n\"들어가도 되니, 아니면 가야 하니?\" 그가 머뭇머뭇 물었다.\n\n\"하루 종일 소피야 세묘노브나랑 있었어요. 우리 둘 다 오빠를 기다렸어요. 오빠가 분명 거기로 올 거라고 생각했거든요.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 방 안으로 들어가 기진맥진한 채 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"기운이 없구나, 두냐, 몹시 피곤해. 그리고 지금 이 순간만큼은 나 자신을 추스를 수 있었으면 했는데.\"\n\n그는 미심쩍은 눈길로 그녀를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"밤새 어디 계셨어요?\"\n\n\"잘 기억이 안 나. 그게 말이야, 누이야, 나는 한번에 마음을 정하고 싶었어, 그래서 몇 번이나 네바 강가를 걸었지, 거기서 모든 걸 끝내 버리고 싶었던 건 기억나는데, 하지만……, 마음을 정할 수가 없었어.\" 그는 또다시 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 보며 속삭였다.\n\n\"다행이에요! 그게 바로 우리가, 소피야 세묘노브나랑 제가 두려워했던 거예요. 그럼 오빠는 아직 삶에 대한 믿음이 있는 거네요? 다행이에요, 정말 다행이에요!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 씁쓸하게 웃었다.\n\n\"나한텐 믿음이 없어, 하지만 방금 어머니 품에 안겨 울었지. 믿음이 없으면서도, 방금 어머니한테 나를 위해 기도해 달라고 했어. 어떻게 그런 건지 모르겠다, 두냐, 나도 이해할 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"어머니한테 다녀오셨어요? 어머니한테 말씀드렸어요?\" 두냐가 공포에 질려 외쳤다. \"설마 그러신 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 말씀은……, 입으로는 안 드렸어. 하지만 어머니는 많은 걸 이해하셨어. 네 잠꼬대를 들으셨거든. 어머니는 이미 절반쯤은 짐작하고 계셔, 분명히. 어쩌면 어머니를 보러 간 게 잘못이었는지도 몰라. 내가 왜 갔는지 모르겠어. 나는 비열한 인간이야, 두냐.\"\n\n\"비열한 인간이라도, 고통을 마주할 각오는 되어 있잖아요! 그렇죠, 그렇잖아요?\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 갈 거야. 당장. 그래, 치욕을 피하려고 물에 빠져 죽을 생각도 했어, 두냐, 하지만 물속을 들여다보면서 생각했지, 지금껏 나 자신을 강하다고 여겨 왔다면 치욕 따위 두려워하지 않는 편이 낫다고.\" 그는 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"그건 자존심이야, 두냐.\"\n\n\"자존심이죠, 로쟈.\"\n\n생기 없던 그의 눈에 불꽃이 번뜩였다. 자신이 아직 자존심을 지니고 있다는 생각이 기뻤던 모양이다.\n\n\"누이야, 너는 내가 그저 물이 무서웠던 거라고 생각하진 않니?\" 그가 음험한 미소를 띠며 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 물었다.\n\n\"오, 로쟈, 그만해요!\" 두냐가 쓰라리게 외쳤다. 이 분 동안 침묵이 이어졌다. 그는 바닥에 눈을 박은 채 앉아 있었고, 두냐는 탁자 반대편 끝에 서서 비통한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았다. 갑자기 그가 일어섰다.\n\n\"늦었어, 갈 시간이야! 지금 당장 가서 자수할 거야. 하지만 내가 왜 자수하러 가는 건지는 모르겠어.\"\n\n굵은 눈물방울이 그녀의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"누이야, 너는 울고 있지만, 그래도 나한테 손을 내밀어 줄 수 있겠니?\"\n\n\"그걸 의심하셨어요?\"\n\n그녀는 두 팔로 그를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"고통을 마주하는 것만으로도 오빠는 죄를 절반쯤 속죄하는 거 아닌가요?\" 그녀는 그를 꼭 끌어안고 입을 맞추며 외쳤다.\n\n\"죄? 무슨 죄?\" 그가 별안간 격분하여 외쳤다. \"내가 비열하고 해로운 벌레 한 마리를, 아무에게도 쓸모없는 늙은 전당포 노파를 죽였다는 거? ……그 여자를 죽인 건 마흔 가지 죄를 갚은 셈이야. 그 여자는 가난한 사람들의 피를 빨아먹고 있었어. 그게 죄야? 나는 그걸 죄라고 생각하지 않고, 그걸 속죄할 생각도 없어, 그런데 왜들 사방에서 그걸 나한테 들이미는 거지? '죄라니! 죄라니!' 이 불필요한 치욕을 마주하기로 결심한 지금에서야, 내 비겁함이 얼마나 천치 같은 짓이었는지 똑똑히 보이는구나. 그저 내가 비열하고 내 안에 아무것도 없으니까 결심한 거야, 어쩌면 그……, 포르피리가……, 넌지시 일러 준 대로 내 이득을 위해서이기도 하고!\"\n\n\"오빠, 오빠, 무슨 말을 하는 거예요? 아니, 오빠는 피를 흘리게 했잖아요?\" 두냐가 절망에 차서 외쳤다.\n\n\"모든 인간이 흘리게 하는 피지.\" 그는 거의 미친 듯이 끼어들었다. \"강물처럼 흐르고 늘 흘러왔던, 샴페인처럼 쏟아지는 피, 그것 때문에 사람들이 카피톨리움에서 월계관을 쓰고, 나중에 인류의 은인이라 불리는 그런 피야. 더 잘 들여다보고 이해해 봐! 나도 사람들에게 선을 베풀고 싶었고, 그 한 번의 어리석은 짓을 — 아니, 어리석은 짓도 아니야, 그저 서투른 짓이지, 왜냐하면 이제 와 실패하고 나니 어리석어 보일 뿐, 그 발상 자체는 결코 그렇게 어리석은 게 아니었거든…… (실패하면 모든 게 어리석어 보이는 법이지.) — 만회하려고 수백, 수천 가지 선행을 베풀었을 거야. 그 어리석은 짓으로 나는 그저 독립된 처지에 서고, 첫걸음을 내딛고, 수단을 손에 넣고 싶었을 뿐이야, 그러고 나면 그에 비할 수 없이 막대한 선행으로 모든 게 무마됐겠지……. 하지만 나는……, 나는 첫걸음조차 해내지 못했어, 내가 비열한 인간이니까, 문제는 바로 그거야! 그래도 나는 너처럼 그걸 바라보진 않을 거야. 만약 내가 성공했다면 영광의 월계관을 썼겠지만, 지금은 덫에 걸린 거지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 그렇지 않아요, 그렇지 않아요! 오빠, 무슨 말을 하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아, 그게 그림처럼 멋지지가 않고, 미적으로 매혹적이지가 않다는 거지! 나는 왜 정식으로 포위해서 사람들을 포격하는 게 더 명예로운 일인지 이해할 수가 없어. 겉모습을 두려워하는 건 무능의 첫 번째 징후야. 지금처럼 이것을 또렷이 깨달은 적이 없고, 내가 한 짓이 죄였다고 보는 것과는 그 어느 때보다 멀어졌어. 지금처럼 강하고 확신에 찬 적이 없어.\"\n\n그의 창백하고 지친 얼굴에 핏기가 확 올랐지만, 마지막 변론을 입에 올리던 순간, 그는 우연히 두냐의 눈과 마주쳤고, 거기서 어찌나 깊은 비탄을 보았던지 멈칫하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 어쨌든 자신이 이 가엾은 두 여인을 비참하게 만들었음을, 어쨌든 자신이 그 원인임을 느꼈다…….\n\n\"두냐, 내 사랑, 내가 죄인이라면 나를 용서해 줘(하긴 내가 죄인이라면 용서받을 수 없겠지만). 잘 있어! 다투지 말자. 갈 시간이야, 이미 한참 늦었어. 나를 따라오지 마, 제발 부탁이야, 들를 데가 따로 있어……. 그러니 너는 당장 가서 어머니 곁에 있어 줘. 제발 그래 주렴! 너한테 하는 마지막 부탁이야. 어머니 곁을 절대 떠나지 마. 나는 어머니를 불안한 상태로 두고 왔는데, 어머니가 견뎌 내기 힘든 상태야. 어머니가 돌아가시거나 정신을 놓아 버릴지도 몰라. 어머니 곁에 있어 줘! 라주미힌이 너희와 함께 있을 거야. 내가 그 친구한테 얘기해 뒀어……. 나 때문에 울지 마. 비록 살인자라 해도, 나는 평생 정직하고 의연하게 살려고 애쓸 거야. 어쩌면 언젠가 이름을 떨칠지도 몰라. 너희를 욕되게 하지 않을게, 두고 봐. 그래도 보여 줄 테니까……. 자, 이제 당분간 안녕.\" 그는 자신의 마지막 말과 약속에 두냐의 눈에 또다시 떠오른 야릇한 표정을 알아채고는 서둘러 말을 맺었다. \"왜 우는 거야? 울지 마, 울지 마. 우리가 영영 헤어지는 게 아니잖아! 아, 그래! 잠깐만, 깜빡할 뻔했네!\"\n\n그는 탁자로 가서 먼지 앉은 두꺼운 책 한 권을 집어 펼치더니, 책장 사이에서 상아에 그린 작은 수채화 초상 하나를 꺼냈다. 그것은 열병으로 죽은 하숙집 여주인의 딸, 수녀가 되고 싶어 했던 그 기묘한 처녀의 초상이었다. 그는 잠시 자기 약혼녀의 섬세하고 표정 어린 얼굴을 응시하다가, 초상에 입을 맞추고는 두냐에게 건넸다.\n\n\"나는 이 일을 그 여자한테 많이 얘기하곤 했어, 오직 그 여자한테만.\" 그가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"그 여자의 마음에다 나는, 그 뒤로 이토록 흉측하게 실현되어 버린 많은 것을 털어놓았지. 마음 쓰지 마.\" 그가 두냐에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그 여자도 너만큼이나 반대했어, 그래서 나는 그 여자가 떠난 게 다행이야. 중요한 건, 이제 모든 게 달라지리라는 거, 둘로 쪼개지리라는 거야.\" 그가 갑자기 다시 침울함에 빠져들며 외쳤다. \"모든 게, 모든 게, 한데 나는 그것에 대비가 되어 있나? 나 스스로 그걸 원하긴 하나? 사람들은 내가 고통받아야 한다고 말하지! 이 무의미한 고통의 목적이 대체 뭐야? 이십 년 징역살이 끝에 고난과 천치 같은 짓에 짓눌려 늙은이처럼 쇠약해지고 나면, 그 고통이 무엇을 위한 것이었는지 내가 더 잘 알게 되기라도 하나? 그러고 나면 나는 무엇을 위해 살아야 하지? 왜 나는 지금 그런 삶에 동의하고 있는 거지? 오, 오늘 동틀 녘 네바 강을 바라보며 서 있을 때, 나는 내가 비열한 인간이라는 걸 알았어!\"\n\n마침내 둘은 함께 밖으로 나왔다. 두냐에게는 힘겨운 일이었지만, 그녀는 그를 사랑했다. 그녀는 걸어갔지만, 쉰 걸음쯤 가다가 다시 그를 보려고 돌아섰다. 그는 아직 시야에 있었다. 모퉁이에서 그도 돌아섰고, 마지막으로 둘의 눈이 마주쳤다. 그러나 그녀가 자신을 바라보고 있는 것을 알아채자, 그는 짜증스럽게, 심지어 언짢은 듯 손짓으로 그녀를 물리치고는 휙 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다.\n\n'나는 못된 인간이야, 그건 알아.' 그는 잠시 후 두냐에게 짜증스러운 손짓을 한 것이 부끄러워지며 속으로 생각했다. '하지만 내가 그럴 자격도 없는데 왜들 나를 그토록 아끼는 거지? 오, 차라리 내가 혼자였고 아무도 나를 사랑하지 않았고, 나 역시 그 누구도 사랑한 적이 없었더라면! 이 모든 일은 일어나지도 않았을 텐데. 그런데 과연 나는 그 십오 년이나 이십 년 동안 그토록 온순해져서, 사람들 앞에 굽실거리고 한마디 할 때마다 나는 죄인입니다 하고 훌쩍거리게 될까? 그래, 바로 그거야, 그거야, 사람들이 나를 거기로 보내는 이유가 바로 그거지, 그게 그들이 원하는 거야. 저들이 거리를 이리저리 분주히 오가는 꼴을 봐, 하나같이 마음속은 악당이고 죄인이고, 더 나쁘게는 천치들이지. 그런데 나를 풀어 주려고 해 봐라, 의로운 분노로 펄펄 날뛸걸. 오, 나는 저들 모두가 얼마나 미운지!'\n\n그는 어떤 과정을 거쳐, 자신이 저들 모두 앞에, 가리지 않고, 굴복하게 될 수 있는지를 — 확신에 의해 굴복하게 될 수 있는지를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다. 그런데 안 될 게 또 뭐람? 분명 그렇게 될 것이다. 이십 년간 끊임없이 이어지는 속박이 그를 완전히 으스러뜨리지 않겠는가? 물은 돌도 닳게 하는 법. 그러고 나서 그는 왜, 왜 살아야 하는가? 그렇게 되리라는 걸 알면서 왜 지금 가야 하는가? 어제저녁부터 그가 자신에게 이 질문을 던진 것이 어쩌면 백 번째였지만, 그래도 그는 갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n그가 소냐의 방으로 들어섰을 때는 이미 어둑어둑해지고 있었다. 소냐는 하루 종일 끔찍한 불안 속에서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 두냐도 그녀와 함께 기다렸다. 두냐는 그날 아침, 소냐가 알고 있다던 스비드리가일로프의 말을 떠올리고 그녀를 찾아온 것이었다. 두 처녀의 대화와 눈물을, 그리고 그들이 얼마나 다정해졌는지를 여기서 구구절절 묘사하지는 않겠다. 두냐는 적어도 이 만남에서 한 가지 위안만큼은 얻었는데, 자기 오빠가 혼자가 아니리라는 것이었다. 오빠는 자신의 고백을 안고 가장 먼저 그녀, 소냐에게로 갔다. 인간의 정이 필요했을 때 그녀에게로 갔던 것이다. 그녀는 운명이 그를 어디로 보내든 그와 함께 갈 터였다. 두냐는 묻지 않았지만, 그것이 그러하다는 걸 알았다. 그녀는 거의 경외에 가까운 눈길로 소냐를 바라보았고, 처음엔 그 시선에 소냐가 거의 당황할 지경이었다. 소냐는 금방이라도 눈물을 쏟을 것만 같았다. 오히려 그녀는 자신이 두냐를 마주 볼 자격조차 없다고 느꼈다. 라스콜니코프의 방에서 처음 만났을 때 두냐가 그토록 정중하고 공손하게 고개 숙여 인사하던 그 우아한 모습은, 소냐의 마음속에 평생 가장 아름다운 환영 중 하나로 남아 있었다.\n\n두냐는 마침내 더는 참지 못하고 소냐를 남겨 둔 채 오빠를 기다리려 그의 방으로 갔다. 오빠가 그곳으로 먼저 오리라는 생각이 자꾸만 들었던 것이다. 그녀가 떠난 뒤, 소냐는 그가 자살하지나 않을까 하는 공포에 시달리기 시작했고, 두냐 역시 그것을 두려워했다. 그러나 그들은 그럴 리 없다고 서로를 설득하며 하루를 보냈고, 함께 있는 동안에는 둘 다 덜 불안했다. 하지만 헤어지자마자 각자 다른 것은 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다. 소냐는 전날 스비드리가일로프가 자기에게 라스콜니코프에게는 두 가지 길밖에 없다고 — 시베리아 아니면…… 하고 말했던 것을 떠올렸다. 게다가 그녀는 그의 허영심, 그의 자존심, 그리고 그의 신앙 없음을 알고 있었다.\n\n'그가 그저 비겁함과 죽음에 대한 두려움 때문에 살아간다는 게 가능한 일일까?' 그녀는 마침내 절망 속에서 생각했다.\n\n그러는 사이 해가 지고 있었다. 소냐는 의기소침하게 서서 창밖을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었지만, 그 창으로는 옆집의 회칠도 안 된 텅 빈 벽 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 마침내 그녀가 그의 죽음을 확신하기 시작했을 때 — 그가 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n그녀는 기쁨의 비명을 질렀으나, 그의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보고는 새파랗게 질렸다.\n\n\"그래.\" 라스콜니코프가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신의 십자가를 받으러 왔어, 소냐. 사거리로 가라고 한 건 당신이었잖아. 그런데 정작 그렇게 됐는데 왜 지금 겁을 먹는 거지?\"\n\n소냐는 놀란 눈으로 그를 응시했다. 그의 어조가 그녀에게는 이상하게 들렸다. 싸늘한 전율이 그녀의 온몸을 훑고 지나갔지만, 곧 그녀는 그 어조와 말들이 가면이라는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 그녀의 눈길과 마주치기를 피하려는 듯 딴 곳을 보며 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"있잖아, 소냐, 나는 이렇게 하는 게 더 낫겠다고 결심했어. 한 가지 사실이 있는데…… 하지만 긴 얘기라 굳이 따질 필요는 없지. 그런데 내가 뭣 때문에 화가 나는지 알아? 그 멍청하고 짐승 같은 낯짝들이 곧장 나를 빤히 쳐다보면서, 멍청한 질문들로 나를 들볶고, 나는 거기에 일일이 대답해야 한다는 거야 — 손가락질을 하겠지……. 퉤! 알다시피 나는 포르피리한테는 안 갈 거야, 그자한테는 신물이 나니까. 차라리 내 친구, 그 화약 같은 중위한테 가겠어. 내가 그자를 얼마나 놀라게 할지, 얼마나 큰 소동을 일으킬지! 하지만 좀 더 냉정해져야 해. 요즘 나는 너무 신경질적이 됐어. 알다시피 방금도 내 누이동생한테 주먹질을 할 뻔했다고, 그애가 나를 마지막으로 한 번 더 보겠다고 돌아섰다는 이유로. 이건 짐승 같은 상태야! 아! 내가 도대체 어떻게 되어 가는 거지! 자, 십자가들은 어디 있지?\"\n\n그는 자신이 무얼 하고 있는지조차 잘 모르는 듯했다. 그는 가만히 있지도, 무언가에 정신을 집중하지도 못했다. 그의 생각들은 서로의 뒤를 쫓아 질주하는 것 같았고, 두서없이 지껄였으며, 손은 가볍게 떨렸다.\n\n소냐는 말없이 서랍에서 십자가 두 개를 꺼냈다. 하나는 노송나무로 된 것이고 다른 하나는 구리로 된 것이었다. 그녀는 자기 몸 위에, 그리고 그의 몸 위에 성호를 긋고는, 나무 십자가를 그의 목에 걸어 주었다.\n\n\"이건 내가 십자가를 짊어진다는 상징이로군.\" 그가 웃었다. \"마치 지금까지 내가 별로 고통받지 않았다는 듯이! 나무 십자가, 그건 농민의 것이고, 구리 십자가는 리자베타의 것이지 — 당신이 직접 걸어, 어디 보여 봐! 그래, 그여자가 그걸 걸고 있었지…… 그 순간에? 나도 이것들과 비슷한 걸 두 개 기억해, 은으로 된 거랑 작은 성상 하나. 나는 그것들을 노파의 목에다 도로 던져 버렸어. 그것들이야말로 지금 어울릴 텐데, 정말이지, 그게 바로 지금 내가 걸어야 할 것들인데……. 하지만 내가 헛소리를 하고 있군, 정작 중요한 건 잊어버리고. 어쩐지 나는 자꾸 잊어버려……. 봐, 나는 당신한테 알려 주려고 왔어, 소냐, 당신이 알 수 있도록…… 그게 다야 — 그것 때문에 온 거야. 하지만 할 말이 더 있었던 것 같은데. 당신이 직접 나더러 가라고 했잖아. 그래, 이제 나는 감옥으로 가고, 당신은 소원을 이루는 거야. 자, 뭣 때문에 우는 거지? 당신마저? 그러지 마. 그만둬! 오, 이 모든 게 얼마나 싫은지!\"\n\n그러나 그의 감정은 흔들렸다. 그녀를 바라보자 그의 가슴이 아려 왔다. '왜 이 여자도 슬퍼하는 거지?' 그는 속으로 생각했다. '내가 이 여자한테 무엇이라고? 왜 우는 거지? 왜 어머니나 두냐처럼 나를 보살피려는 거지? 이 여자는 내 간병인이 되겠군.'\n\n\"성호를 그어요, 적어도 기도 하나만이라도 올려요.\" 소냐가 겁먹은 듯 떨리는 목소리로 애원했다.\n\n\"오 물론이지, 당신이 원하는 만큼! 그리고 진심으로, 소냐, 진심으로…….\" 그러나 그가 하고 싶었던 말은 전혀 다른 것이었다.\n\n그는 여러 번 성호를 그었다. 소냐는 숄을 집어 들어 머리에 둘렀다. 그것은 마르멜라도프가 말했던 그 초록색 드 담 숄, \"집안 공용 숄\"이었다. 라스콜니코프는 그것을 보며 그 일을 떠올렸지만, 묻지는 않았다. 그는 자기가 정말로 무언가를 자꾸 잊고 있으며, 역겨울 만큼 동요하고 있다는 것을 스스로 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 이에 겁이 났다. 게다가 소냐가 자기와 함께 가려 한다는 생각이 불현듯 그를 강타했다.\n\n\"당신 뭐 하는 거야? 어디 가려고? 여기 있어, 여기! 나 혼자 갈 거야.\" 그가 비겁한 짜증을 담아 소리쳤고, 거의 분개한 듯 문 쪽으로 움직였다. \"행렬을 지어 갈 필요가 뭐 있어?\" 그는 나가면서 중얼거렸다.\n\n소냐는 방 한가운데 선 채 남겨졌다. 그는 그녀에게 작별 인사조차 하지 않았다. 그녀를 잊어버린 것이다. 가슴을 후벼 파는 반항적인 의혹이 그의 마음속에서 솟구쳤다.\n\n'이게 옳은 일이었나, 옳은 일이었나, 이 모든 게?' 그는 계단을 내려가며 다시 생각했다. '멈춰 서서 이 모든 걸 철회하고…… 가지 않을 수는 없는 걸까?'\n\n그러나 그는 그래도 갔다. 그는 문득, 단번에, 자신에게 질문 따위를 던져서는 안 된다고 느꼈다. 거리로 접어들면서 그는 소냐에게 작별 인사를 하지 않았다는 것을, 자기가 그녀에게 소리를 지른 뒤 그녀가 감히 꼼짝도 못 한 채 초록색 숄을 두르고 방 한가운데 서 있던 것을 떠올렸고, 잠시 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 바로 그 순간, 또 다른 생각이, 마치 그때 그를 칠 기회를 노리고 숨어 있었다는 듯, 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다.\n\n'아니, 도대체 무슨 목적으로 방금 그 여자한테 갔던 거지? 나는 그여자한테 — 볼일이 있다고 말했어. 무슨 볼일? 나한테는 그 어떤 볼일도 없었어! 내가 간다고 말하려고? 하지만 그럴 필요가 어디 있었나? 내가 그 여자를 사랑하나? 아니, 아니야, 방금도 개처럼 그 여자를 쫓아냈잖아. 내가 그 여자의 십자가를 원했던가? 오, 내가 얼마나 비천하게 떨어졌는지! 아니야, 나는 그 여자의 눈물을 원했어, 그 여자의 공포를 보고 싶었어, 그 여자의 가슴이 어떻게 미어지는지 보고 싶었던 거야! 나는 매달릴 무언가가, 나를 붙들어 둘 무언가가, 바라볼 다정한 얼굴 하나가 필요했던 거야! 그러고도 나는 감히 나 자신을 믿고, 내가 무슨 일을 할지를 꿈꾸었다니! 나는 거지같이 비열한 놈이야, 비열한 놈!'\n\n그는 운하 둑을 따라 걸었고, 이제 갈 길은 그리 멀지 않았다. 그러나 다리에 다다르자 그는 멈춰 서더니, 가던 길에서 벗어나 다리를 따라 건초 시장 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n그는 오른쪽 왼쪽을 간절히 둘러보며 모든 사물을 뚫어지게 응시했지만 무엇에도 정신을 붙들어 둘 수 없었다. 모든 것이 미끄러져 빠져나갔다. '일주일 뒤, 한 달 뒤면 나는 죄수 호송차에 실려 이 다리를 건너가겠지, 그때 나는 이 운하를 어떤 눈으로 바라볼까? 이걸 기억해 두고 싶군!' 하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. '이 간판을 봐! 그때 나는 저 글자들을 어떻게 읽을까? 여기 「캄파니」라고 쓰여 있군, 기억해 둘 만한 거야, 저 a라는 글자를, 그리고 한 달 뒤에 다시 보는 거지 — 그때 나는 저걸 어떻게 바라볼까? 그때 나는 무엇을 느끼고 무엇을 생각하고 있을까?…… 지금 내가 안달복달하고 있는 이 모든 게 얼마나 하찮은 것일까! 물론 이 모든 게 흥미롭긴 하겠지…… 나름대로…… (하-하-하! 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거지?) 나는 어린애가 되어 가는군, 나 자신한테 으스대고 있어. 내가 왜 부끄러워하는 거지? 퉤! 사람들이 어찌 이리 밀쳐 대는지! 저 뚱뚱한 사내 — 분명 독일 놈일 텐데 — 나한테 부딪힌 저자는, 자기가 누구한테 부딪혔는지 알기나 할까? 저기 아기를 안고 구걸하는 농가 아낙이 있군. 저 여자가 나를 자기보다 더 행복하다고 여긴다는 게 묘하구나. 그 부조리함 때문에라도 저 여자한테 뭔가 줄 수도 있겠지. 여기 주머니에 오 코페이카짜리 동전 하나가 남아 있군, 이걸 어디서 났더라? 여기, 여기…… 받아요, 아주머니!'\n\n\"하느님의 축복이 있으시길.\" 거지 여인이 눈물 어린 목소리로 읊조렸다.\n\n그는 건초 시장으로 들어갔다. 군중 속에 있는 것은 불쾌하기 짝이 없는, 정말이지 불쾌하기 짝이 없는 일이었지만, 그는 사람이 가장 많이 보이는 바로 그곳으로 걸어 들어갔다. 혼자 있을 수만 있다면 세상의 무엇이라도 내주었으련만, 그는 자신이 단 한순간도 혼자 있지 못하리라는 것을 스스로 알고 있었다. 군중 속에 술에 취해 행패를 부리는 사내가 하나 있었는데, 그는 자꾸만 춤을 추려다가 넘어지곤 했다. 그 주위로 사람들이 둥글게 모여 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 군중을 비집고 들어가 몇 분 동안 그 취한 사내를 빤히 바라보다가, 갑자기 짧고 발작적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 잠시 후 그는 그 사내를 잊어버렸고, 여전히 빤히 보면서도 그를 보고 있지 않았다. 마침내 그는 자기가 어디 있는지도 기억하지 못한 채 자리를 떴다. 그러나 광장 한복판에 이르렀을 때, 한 감정이 불현듯 그를 덮쳐 와 몸과 마음을 송두리째 휩쓸어 버렸다.\n\n그는 갑자기 소냐의 말을 떠올렸다. \"사거리로 가서 사람들에게 절을 하고, 대지에 입을 맞춰요, 당신은 대지에도 죄를 지었으니까요, 그리고 온 세상을 향해 큰 소리로 외쳐요, '나는 살인자다.'라고.\" 그것을 떠올리며 그는 부르르 떨었다. 그리고 그 모든 시간의, 특히 지난 몇 시간의 절망적인 비참함과 불안이 그를 너무도 무겁게 짓눌렀던 터라, 그는 이 새롭고 순수하고 온전한 감정의 기회를 그야말로 덥석 움켜쥐었다. 그것은 발작처럼 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 그것은 그의 영혼 속에 점화된 단 하나의 불꽃이 그의 온몸으로 불길을 번져 가는 것과도 같았다. 그의 안에 있던 모든 것이 단번에 누그러지면서 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. 그는 그 자리에서 대지에 엎드렸다…….\n\n그는 광장 한복판에 무릎을 꿇고 대지에 절을 하고는, 그 더러운 대지에 황홀과 환희에 차서 입을 맞추었다. 그는 일어서서 두 번째로 절을 했다.\n\n\"술에 절었구먼.\" 그의 곁에 있던 젊은이 하나가 한마디 했다.\n\n웃음소리가 와르르 터졌다.\n\n\"저 사람 예루살렘으로 가는 거야, 형제들, 자기 자식들이랑 고국에 작별을 고하는 거라고. 온 세상에 절을 하고 위대한 도시 페테르부르크와 그 보도에 입을 맞추는 거지.\" 약간 취한 노동자 하나가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"게다가 아주 젊은 사람이군!\" 세 번째 사람이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그리고 신사야.\" 누군가가 진지하게 한마디 보탰다.\n\n\"요즘 세상엔 누가 신사고 누가 아닌지 알 수가 없지.\"\n\n이런 외침들과 말들이 라스콜니코프를 멈칫하게 했고, 어쩌면 막 그의 입술에서 떨어지려던 \"나는 살인자다.\"라는 말은 사그라들고 말았다. 그러나 그는 이 말들을 조용히 견뎌 내고는, 주위를 둘러보지도 않은 채, 경찰서로 통하는 골목으로 접어들었다. 도중에 그는 어떤 광경을 언뜻 보았는데, 그것은 그를 놀라게 하지 않았다. 그는 분명 그러리라고 느끼고 있었던 것이다. 건초 시장에서 두 번째로 절을 할 때, 그는 자기에게서 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 왼쪽에 소냐가 서 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 시장 광장의 목조 가건물 뒤에 숨어 그에게서 몸을 가리고 있었다. 그러니까 그녀는 그의 고통스러운 길을 줄곧 뒤따라온 것이었다! 그 순간 라스콜니코프는, 소냐가 영원히 자기와 함께 있으리라는 것을, 운명이 그를 어디로 데려가든 땅끝까지 그를 따라오리라는 것을, 단번에 느끼고 또 알았다. 그것이 그의 가슴을 쥐어뜯었지만…… 그는 이미 그 숙명의 장소에 다다르고 있었다.\n\n그는 제법 단호하게 마당 안으로 들어섰다. 그는 사 층까지 올라가야 했다. '한참 걸리겠군.' 그는 생각했다. 그는 마치 그 숙명의 순간이 아직 멀리 있는 듯, 마치 숙고할 시간이 아직 많이 남아 있는 듯 느꼈다.\n\n또다시 그 똑같은 쓰레기더미, 나선형 계단에 여기저기 흩어진 그 똑같은 달걀 껍데기들, 또다시 열려 있는 셋집들의 문, 또다시 그 똑같은 부엌들과 거기서 풍겨 나오는 그 똑같은 김과 악취. 라스콜니코프는 그날 이후로 이곳에 와 본 적이 없었다. 그의 다리는 감각이 없고 풀려 버렸지만, 그래도 발은 앞으로 움직였다. 그는 숨을 고르고, 정신을 가다듬어 사내답게 들어가기 위해 잠시 멈춰 섰다. '하지만 무엇 때문에? 무엇을 위해서?' 그는 곰곰이 생각하며 의아해했다. '어차피 이 잔을 마셔야 한다면 그게 무슨 차이가 있겠나? 역겨우면 역겨울수록 더 나은 거지.' 그는 한순간 그 \"화약 같은 중위\", 일리야 페트로비치의 모습을 떠올렸다. 정말 그자에게 가려는 걸까? 다른 누군가에게 갈 수는 없을까? 니코딤 포미치에게? 발길을 돌려 곧장 니코딤 포미치의 거처로 갈 수는 없을까? 적어도 그러면 은밀하게 처리될 텐데……. 아니, 아니야! \"화약 같은 중위\"에게로! 마셔야 한다면, 단숨에 들이켜는 거다.\n\n싸늘하게 식어 거의 정신이 나간 채로, 그는 사무실 문을 열었다. 이번에는 안에 사람이 거의 없었다 — 문지기 하나와 농부 하나뿐이었다. 수위는 칸막이 뒤에서 고개조차 내밀지 않았다. 라스콜니코프는 옆방으로 걸어 들어갔다. '어쩌면 아직은 말하지 않아도 될지 몰라.' 하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. 제복을 입지 않은 어떤 서기 하나가 책상 앞에 자리를 잡고 무언가를 쓰려 하고 있었다. 한쪽 구석에서는 또 다른 서기가 막 자리에 앉고 있었다. 자메토프는 거기 없었고, 물론 니코딤 포미치도 없었다.\n\n\"아무도 안 계십니까?\" 라스콜니코프가 책상 앞의 사람에게 물었다.\n\n\"누굴 찾으시오?\"\n\n\"아-아! 소리도 들리지 않고, 모습도 보이지 않건만, 러시아 사람의 냄새가 풍기는구나…… 그 옛이야기에서 다음이 어떻게 되더라…… 잊어버렸네! '대령했습니다!'\" 익숙한 목소리가 갑자기 외쳤다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 흠칫했다. 그 화약 같은 중위가 그의 앞에 서 있었다. 그는 방금 세 번째 방에서 나온 참이었다. '이건 운명의 손길이야.' 라스콜니코프는 생각했다. '왜 그가 여기 있는 거지?'\n\n\"우리를 보러 오셨소? 무슨 일로?\" 일리야 페트로비치가 외쳤다. 그는 분명 기분이 몹시 좋았고, 어쩌면 약간 들떠 있는 듯도 했다. \"볼일이 있어서 오신 거라면 좀 이르오.[*] 내가 여기 있는 것도 그저 우연이긴 한데…… 어쨌든 내가 할 수 있는 건 해 드리리다. 솔직히 나는…… 무슨 일이오, 무슨 일이오? 실례지만…….\"\n\n[*] 도스토옙스키는 지금이 해가 진 뒤이며, 라스콜니코프가 지난번 오후 두 시에 경찰서를 찾았을 때 너무 늦게 왔다고 핀잔을 들었던 것을 잊은 듯하다. — 옮긴이.\n\n\"라스콜니코프입니다.\"\n\n\"물론, 라스콜니코프지. 내가 잊어버렸으리라 생각했소? 내가 그런 사람이라고 여기진 마시오…… 로디온 로 — 로 — 로디오노비치, 맞지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"로디온 로마노비치입니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 물론이지, 로디온 로마노비치! 막 떠올리던 참이었소. 당신에 대해 여러 가지를 알아봤다오. 단언컨대 나는 그 일 이후로…… 그렇게 처신한 이후로…… 진심으로 마음이 아팠소…… 나중에 듣자 하니 당신이 문필가였다더군…… 게다가 학식 있는 사람이고…… 말하자면 첫발을 내딛는…… 맙소사! 어떤 문필가나 학자가 행동거지에 좀 별난 데 없이 시작하겠소! 나도 내 아내도 문학에 더없는 경의를 품고 있소, 내 아내로 말하자면 그건 진정한 열정이라오! 문학과 예술! 사람이 신사이기만 하다면, 그 나머지는 재능이며 학식이며 양식이며 천재성으로 다 얻을 수 있는 거요. 모자로 말할 것 같으면 — 뭐, 모자가 무슨 대수겠소? 나는 모자쯤이야 빵 한 덩이 사듯 쉽게 살 수 있소. 하지만 모자 아래에 있는 것, 모자가 가리고 있는 것, 그건 살 수 없는 거요! 나는 심지어 당신을 찾아가 사과할까도 생각했지만, 어쩌면 당신이…… 아 참, 묻는 걸 깜빡했는데, 정말로 무슨 원하는 게 있으시오? 듣자 하니 가족이 오셨다던데?\"\n\n\"네, 어머니와 누이동생이요.\"\n\n\"실은 나도 당신 누이동생을 뵙는 영광과 기쁨을 누렸소 — 대단히 교양 있고 매력적인 분이더군. 솔직히 당신한테 그렇게 발끈했던 게 후회스러웠소. 바로 그렇소! 하지만 당신의 그 졸도 발작을 의심스럽게 본 것으로 말할 것 같으면 — 그 일은 멋지게 해명됐소! 광신과 맹신이었지! 당신의 분노도 이해하오. 가족이 오셔서 거처를 옮기시려는 거요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 그냥 잠깐 들른 겁니다…… 물어볼 게 있어서 왔어요…… 여기서 자메토프를 만날 수 있을 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지! 물론, 두 분이 친구가 됐다고 들었소. 그런데 안됐지만, 자메토프는 여기 없소. 그래요, 우리는 자메토프를 잃었다오. 그자는 어제부터 여기 없소…… 떠나면서 모두와 다투었는데…… 아주 무례하게 굴었지. 그저 머리가 텅 빈 풋내기일 뿐이오. 그자한테 뭔가 기대해 볼 만도 했지만, 뭐, 당신도 그런 자들을 알지 않소, 우리네 그 잘난 젊은이들 말이오. 무슨 시험인가를 보겠다고 했지만, 그저 떠벌리고 자랑이나 할 뿐, 그 이상은 안 갈 거요. 물론 당신이나 저기 당신 친구 라주미힌 씨라면 전혀 다른 얘기지. 당신들의 길은 지적인 길이고, 실패에 좌절하지도 않을 테니. 당신으로 말하자면, 인생의 온갖 즐거움이 니힐 에스트라고 할 만하지 — 당신은 금욕가요, 수도승이요, 은둔자란 말이오!…… 책 한 권, 귀에 꽂은 펜 한 자루, 학문적 탐구 — 거기에 당신의 정신이 날아오르는 거요! 나도 똑같은 사람이라오……. 리빙스턴의 여행기를 읽어 보셨소?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"오, 나는 읽었소. 요즘 세상엔 니힐리스트가 아주 많지 않소, 그런데 사실 놀랄 일도 아니지. 대체 어떤 시대인지, 내 당신께 묻겠소. 그런데 우리는 생각했소만…… 당신은 물론 니힐리스트는 아니겠지? 솔직히, 솔직히 대답해 보시오!\"\n\"네…… 아니요…….\"\n\n\"내 말을 믿으시오, 당신은 나한테 자기 자신한테 하듯 솔직히 말해도 되오! 공무란 별개의 것이지만…… 당신은 내가 우정을 말하려 한다고 생각하지요? 아니, 틀렸소! 우정이 아니라, 한 인간으로서, 한 시민으로서의 감정, 인류애와 전능하신 분에 대한 사랑의 감정이오. 내가 관리이긴 하나, 나는 언제나 나 자신을 한 인간이자 한 시민으로 느낄 의무가 있소……. 당신은 자메토프에 대해 물었지. 자메토프 그자야 평판 나쁜 집에서 샴페인 한 잔 걸치고 프랑스식 추문이나 일으킬 자라오…… 당신네 그 자메토프가 쓸모 있는 거라곤 그게 다요! 그에 비해 나는, 말하자면 헌신과 고결한 감정으로 불타고 있고, 게다가 나한테는 지위와 위신과 직책이 있단 말이오! 나는 처자식이 있고, 한 인간으로서, 한 시민으로서의 의무를 다하고 있소, 한데 그자는 뭐란 말이오, 내 묻겠소? 나는 교양으로 고결해진 한 인간으로서 당신께 호소하는 거요…… 그리고 또 그 산파라는 것들도 엄청나게 늘어났단 말이오.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 묻는 듯 눈썹을 치켜올렸다. 분명 식사를 거나하게 한 일리야 페트로비치의 말은 그에게 대부분 공허한 소리의 흐름일 뿐이었다. 그래도 그중 일부는 알아들었다. 그는 이것이 어떻게 끝날지 알지 못한 채 묻는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내 말은 그 머리를 짧게 친 계집애들 얘기요.\" 수다스러운 일리야 페트로비치가 말을 이었다. \"산파라는 게 내가 그들한테 붙인 이름이오. 아주 흡족한 이름이라고 생각하오, 하-하! 그것들이 학교에 다니면서 해부학을 공부한단 말이오. 내가 병이라도 나면, 새파란 처녀를 불러다 치료를 받아야 하오? 어떻게 생각하시오? 하-하!\" 일리야 페트로비치는 자기 재담에 흡족해하며 웃었다. \"교육에 대한 도를 넘은 열의지, 하지만 교육을 받았으면 그것으로 족한 거요. 왜 그걸 남용하느냐 말이오? 왜 그 악당 자메토프처럼 점잖은 사람들을 모욕하느냐 말이오? 그자가 왜 나를 모욕했소, 내 묻겠소? 또 이 자살이라는 것들 좀 보시오, 얼마나 흔한지, 당신은 상상도 못 할 거요! 사람들이 마지막 한 푼까지 다 써 버리고는 스스로 목숨을 끊는단 말이오, 사내애도 계집애도 노인네도. 바로 오늘 아침에도 막 시내에 온 어떤 신사 얘기를 들었소. 닐 파블리치, 내가 묻겠는데, 스스로 총을 쏜 그 신사 이름이 뭐였더라?\"\n\n\"스비드리가일로프요.\" 누군가 옆방에서 졸린 듯 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 움찔했다.\n\n\"스비드리가일로프! 스비드리가일로프가 총으로 자살을 했다고요!\" 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 스비드리가일로프를 아시오?\"\n\n\"네…… 알았습니다……. 그가 이곳에 온 지 얼마 안 됐지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소. 그자는 아내를 잃었고, 무절제한 생활을 하던 자였는데, 별안간 스스로 총을 쏘았다오, 그것도 아주 충격적인 방식으로……. 수첩에 몇 마디 남겼더군, 자기는 온전한 정신으로 죽는 것이며 누구도 자기 죽음에 책임이 없다고. 돈도 있었다던데. 어떻게 그자를 알게 됐소?\"\n\n\"저는…… 아는 사이였습니다…… 제 누이동생이 그의 집에서 가정교사를 했지요.\"\n\n\"바-바-바! 그렇다면 분명 그자에 대해 우리한테 뭔가 얘기해 줄 수 있겠군. 의심스러운 점은 없었소?\"\n\n\"어제 그를 봤습니다…… 그는…… 술을 마시고 있었어요. 저는 아무것도 몰랐습니다.\" 라스콜니코프는 무언가가 자기 위로 떨어져 숨통을 조여 오는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"또 안색이 창백해지셨군. 여기가 워낙 숨이 막혀서…….\"\n\n\"네, 가 봐야겠습니다.\" 라스콜니코프가 중얼거렸다. \"폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다…….\"\n\n\"오, 천만에요, 언제든 오시오. 당신을 보니 반갑소, 정말 그렇게 말할 수 있어 기쁘오.\"\n\n일리야 페트로비치가 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"그저…… 자메토프를 보러 온 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"알겠소, 알겠소, 그래도 당신을 보니 반갑소.\"\n\n\"저는…… 정말 기쁩니다…… 안녕히 계십시오.\" 라스콜니코프가 미소 지었다.\n\n그는 밖으로 나왔다. 그는 비틀거렸고, 현기증에 사로잡혀 자기가 무얼 하는지도 몰랐다. 그는 오른손으로 벽을 짚으며 계단을 내려가기 시작했다. 어떤 문지기가 경찰서로 올라가는 길에 그를 밀치고 지나간 것 같았고, 아래층에서는 개 한 마리가 날카롭게 짖어 대고, 어떤 여자가 그 개한테 밀방망이를 집어 던지며 소리를 지른 것만 같았다. 그는 계단을 다 내려가 마당으로 나왔다. 거기, 출입구에서 멀지 않은 곳에 소냐가 서 있었다, 새파랗게 질려 공포에 사로잡힌 채로. 그녀는 넋이 나간 듯 그를 바라보았다. 그는 그녀 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 가슴을 후벼 파는 고뇌와 절망의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 두 손을 맞잡았다. 그의 입술이 추하고 의미 없는 미소로 일그러졌다. 그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 이를 드러내고 씩 웃더니, 다시 경찰서로 돌아갔다.\n\n일리야 페트로비치는 자리에 앉아 어떤 서류들을 뒤적이고 있었다. 그의 앞에는 아까 계단에서 그를 밀치고 지나갔던 바로 그 농부가 서 있었다.\n\n\"이런! 또 오셨군! 뭘 두고 가셨소? 무슨 일이오?\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 입술이 새하얗게 질리고 눈을 부릅뜬 채 천천히 다가왔다. 그는 곧장 책상으로 걸어가 손을 짚고 무언가 말하려 했으나 할 수 없었다. 그저 두서없는 소리만 들릴 뿐이었다.\n\n\"몸이 안 좋으시군, 의자를! 여기, 앉으시오! 물 좀 가져와!\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 의자에 털썩 주저앉았지만, 불쾌한 놀라움이 어린 일리야 페트로비치의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 둘은 잠시 서로를 바라보며 기다렸다. 물이 왔다.\n\n\"제가…….\" 라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"물을 좀 드시오.\"\n\n라스콜니코프는 손으로 물을 거절하고는, 나직하고 띄엄띄엄, 그러나 또렷하게 말했다.\n\n\"노파 전당포 주인과 그 동생 리자베타를 도끼로 죽이고 물건을 훔친 건 바로 저였습니다.\"\n\n일리야 페트로비치는 입을 떡 벌렸다. 사방에서 사람들이 달려왔다. 라스콜니코프는 자기 진술을 되풀이했다.\n\n# I\n\n시베리아. 넓고 적막한 강가에 한 도시가 서 있는데, 러시아 행정 중심지의 하나다. 그 도시에는 요새가 있고, 그 요새 안에는 감옥이 있다. 그 감옥에 이등 죄수 로디온 라스콜니코프가 아홉 달째 갇혀 있다. 그의 범행 이후로 거의 일 년 반이 흘렀다.\n\n그의 재판에는 별 어려움이 없었다. 죄인은 자신의 진술을 정확하고 단호하고 명료하게 고수했다. 그는 사실을 혼동하거나 왜곡하지 않았고, 자기에게 유리하도록 누그러뜨리지도 않았으며, 아주 사소한 세부 하나도 빠뜨리지 않았다. 그는 살인의 모든 정황을, 살해당한 여인의 손에서 발견된 그 저당물(쇳조각을 댄 나뭇조각)의 비밀을 설명했다. 그는 어떻게 그녀의 열쇠들을 꺼냈는지, 그것들이 어떻게 생겼는지를, 또한 궤짝과 그 안의 내용물을 상세히 진술했다. 그는 리자베타 살인의 수수께끼를 설명했고, 코흐가, 그다음으로 대학생이 어떻게 문을 두드렸는지, 그리고 그들이 서로 주고받은 말을 모두 그대로 되풀이했다. 그가 어떻게 계단을 뛰어 내려가 니콜라이와 드미트리가 소리치는 것을 들었는지, 어떻게 빈 셋집에 숨었다가 그 후 집으로 돌아갔는지를. 그는 마지막으로 보즈네센스키 거리 옆 마당에 있는, 지갑과 장신구들이 발견된 그 돌을 가리키며 진술을 끝맺었다. 사실 모든 것이 완벽하게 명료했다. 변호사들과 판사들은 무엇보다도, 그가 장신구들과 지갑을 사용하지도 않고 돌 밑에 숨겨 두었다는 사실에, 게다가 그가 이제 와서는 그 장신구들이 어떻게 생겼는지, 심지어 몇 개나 됐는지조차 기억하지 못한다는 사실에 큰 충격을 받았다. 그가 지갑을 한 번도 열어 보지 않았고 그 안에 얼마가 들었는지조차 몰랐다는 사실은 믿기 어려운 일로 보였다. 지갑 속에는 삼백십칠 루블 육십 코페이카가 들어 있던 것으로 드러났다. 너무 오래 돌 밑에 있었던 탓에, 맨 위에 놓여 있던 가장 값나가는 지폐 몇 장은 습기로 상해 있었다. 그들은 다른 모든 것에 대해서는 진실하고 솔직하게 자백한 피고가, 어째서 이 점에 대해서만은 거짓말을 하는지 한참 동안 알아내려 애썼다. 마침내 심리학에 좀 더 정통한 몇몇 변호사들은, 그가 정말로 지갑을 들여다보지 않았고, 그래서 그것을 돌 밑에 숨길 때 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지 몰랐을 가능성이 있다고 인정했다. 그러나 그들은 곧장, 그 범행이 일시적인 정신 착란을 통해, 목적도 이득의 추구도 없는 살인광적 광기를 통해서만 저질러질 수 있었다는 결론을 끌어냈다. 이는 요즘 형사 사건에 너무도 빈번히 적용되는, 일시적 정신 이상이라는 가장 최신 유행의 이론과 맞아떨어졌다. 게다가 라스콜니코프의 우울증적 상태는 많은 증인들에 의해, 즉 조시모프 의사, 그의 옛 동창들, 그의 하숙집 주인 여자와 그 하녀에 의해 입증되었다. 이 모든 것은 라스콜니코프가 여느 평범한 살인범이나 강도와는 사뭇 다르며, 이 사건에는 또 다른 요소가 있다는 결론을 강하게 가리켰다.\n\n이 견해를 주장하던 이들의 극심한 짜증에도 불구하고, 죄인은 자신을 변호하려는 시도를 거의 하지 않았다. 무엇이 그를 살인과 강도로 내몰았는가 하는 결정적 질문에, 그는 더없이 거친 솔직함으로 아주 명료하게 대답했다. 그 원인은 자신의 비참한 처지, 자신의 가난과 무력함이었으며, 손에 넣을 수 있으리라 계산했던 삼천 루블의 도움으로 인생의 첫발을 마련하려는 욕망이었다고. 그는 자신의 천박하고 비겁한 본성 때문에, 게다가 궁핍과 실패로 더욱 격앙되어 살인으로 이끌렸다고 했다. 무엇이 자백하게 했는가 하는 질문에는, 마음에서 우러난 회개 때문이라고 대답했다. 이 모든 것이 거의 거칠다 싶을 정도였다…….\n\n그러나 선고는 예상보다 자비로웠는데, 어쩌면 그건 부분적으로 죄인이 자신을 정당화하려 하기는커녕 오히려 자기 죄를 과장하려는 기색을 보였기 때문이었다. 범행의 온갖 기이하고 특수한 정황이 참작되었다. 범행 당시 죄인이 비정상적이고 가난에 찌든 상태였다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그가 훔친 것을 사용하지 않았다는 사실은 한편으로는 양심의 가책 탓으로, 한편으로는 범행 당시 그의 비정상적인 정신 상태 탓으로 돌려졌다. 더구나 리자베타 살인은 마지막 가설을 확증하는 데 한몫했다. 한 사내가 두 건의 살인을 저지르면서 문이 열려 있다는 것을 잊다니! 마지막으로, 니콜라이가 우울증과 광신 탓에 거짓 증언을 함으로써 사건이 절망적으로 뒤엉켜 버린 바로 그 순간에, 게다가 진범에 대한 그 어떤 증거도, 심지어 혐의조차 없던 때에(포르피리 페트로비치는 자신의 약속을 온전히 지켰다) 자백이 이루어진 것 — 이 모든 것이 선고를 누그러뜨리는 데 크게 작용했다. 죄수에게 유리한 다른 정황들도 전혀 뜻밖에 드러났다. 라주미힌은 어떻게 알아냈는지, 라스콜니코프가 대학에 다닐 때 폐병을 앓던 가난한 동급생 하나를 도왔으며, 여섯 달 동안 마지막 한 푼까지 그를 부양하는 데 썼다는 것을, 그리고 그 학생이 죽으면서 거의 열세 살 때부터 자기가 부양해 온 노쇠한 늙은 아버지를 남기자, 라스콜니코프가 그 노인을 병원에 입원시키고 그가 죽었을 때 장례 비용까지 댔다는 것을 밝혀내 입증했다. 라스콜니코프의 하숙집 주인 여자 역시 증언하기를, 그들이 '다섯 모퉁이'의 다른 집에 살 때 라스콜니코프가 불난 집에서 어린아이 둘을 구해 냈고 그 과정에서 화상을 입었다고 했다. 이는 조사되어 여러 증인들에 의해 상당히 잘 확증되었다. 이런 사실들은 그에게 유리한 인상을 남겼다.\n\n그리하여 결국 죄인은 정상 참작을 고려하여 이등 징역형에 처해졌는데, 형기는 겨우 팔 년이었다.\n\n재판이 막 시작될 무렵 라스콜니코프의 어머니가 병이 났다. 두냐와 라주미힌은 재판이 진행되는 동안 그녀를 페테르부르크에서 데리고 나가는 것이 가능하다고 판단했다. 라주미힌은 페테르부르크에서 멀지 않은 철도변의 한 도시를 골랐는데, 재판의 모든 진행을 좇으면서 동시에 가능한 한 자주 아브도탸 로마노브나를 볼 수 있도록 하기 위함이었다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 병은 기이한 신경성 질환이었고, 부분적인 지적 착란을 동반했다.\n\n두냐는 오빠와의 마지막 면회에서 돌아왔을 때, 어머니가 이미 병이 나 열에 들떠 헛소리를 하고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그날 저녁 라주미힌과 그녀는 어머니가 라스콜니코프에 대해 묻는 질문에 어떻게 대답해야 할지 합의를 보고, 어머니를 위해 완전한 이야기를 하나 지어냈다. 그가 사업상의 임무로 러시아의 먼 지방으로 떠나야 했으며, 그것이 결국엔 그에게 돈과 명성을 가져다주리라는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그들은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 그 일에 대해 그때도 그 후로도 아무것도 묻지 않는다는 사실에 놀랐다. 오히려 그녀는 아들의 갑작스러운 출발에 대해 나름의 해석을 가지고 있었다. 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 그들에게 아들이 어떻게 자기에게 작별 인사를 하러 왔는지 이야기했고, 자기만이 많은 신비롭고 중요한 사실들을 알고 있다는 듯, 또 로댜에게는 매우 강력한 적들이 많아서 그가 몸을 숨길 수밖에 없다는 듯 암시했다. 그의 앞날에 대해서는, 어떤 사악한 영향력들만 제거되면 그것이 찬란하리라는 것을 그녀는 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 그녀는 라주미힌에게, 자기 아들이 언젠가 위대한 정치가가 될 것이며, 그의 논문과 빛나는 문학적 재능이 그것을 증명한다고 장담했다. 이 논문을 그녀는 끊임없이 읽었고, 심지어 소리 내어 읽기도 했으며, 거의 잠자리에까지 가지고 들어갈 정도였지만, 정작 로댜가 어디 있는지는 거의 묻지 않았는데, 다른 이들이 그 주제를 분명히 피하는 것만으로도 그녀의 의심을 불러일으키기에 충분했을 법한데도 그랬다.\n\n그들은 마침내 어떤 주제들에 대한 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 기이한 침묵에 겁이 나기 시작했다. 예컨대 그녀는 그에게서 편지가 오지 않는다고 한 번도 불평하지 않았는데, 지난 몇 해 동안에는 사랑하는 로댜의 편지에 대한 희망만으로 살아왔던 그녀였다. 이것이 두냐에게는 큰 불안의 원인이었다. 어머니가 아들의 운명에 무언가 끔찍한 것이 있음을 의심하면서도, 더욱 무서운 무언가를 듣게 될까 두려워 차마 묻지 못하는 것이라는 생각이 그녀에게 떠올랐다. 어쨌든 두냐는 어머니가 온전히 제정신이 아니라는 것을 분명히 알 수 있었다.\n\n그러나 한두 번은, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 대화를 그런 식으로 몰아가서, 로댜가 어디 있는지를 언급하지 않고는 도저히 대답할 수 없게 만든 적도 있었는데, 그럴 때 미흡하고 의심스러운 대답을 듣고 나면 그녀는 곧장 침울해져 입을 다물었고, 그런 기분이 한참 동안 이어졌다. 두냐는 마침내 어머니를 속이기가 어렵다는 것을 깨닫고, 어떤 점들에 대해서는 아예 입을 다무는 편이 낫겠다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그러나 그 가엾은 어머니가 무언가 끔찍한 것을 의심하고 있다는 것이 점점 더 분명해졌다. 두냐는 오빠가 자기에게 했던 말을 떠올렸다. 그녀가 스비드리가일로프와 면회한 다음 날 밤, 자백이라는 그 숙명적인 날을 앞두고, 어머니가 자기가 잠결에 하는 말을 엿들었다던 이야기를. 어머니가 거기서 무언가를 알아챈 것은 아닐까?\n때로는 며칠씩, 심지어 몇 주씩 침울한 침묵과 눈물이 이어지다가, 발작적으로 활기를 띠는 시기가 뒤따랐고, 그러면 환자는 거의 쉬지 않고 자기 아들에 대해, 그의 앞날에 대한 자기 희망에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다……. 그녀의 공상은 때때로 무척 기이했다. 그들은 그녀를 달래며 그녀의 말에 동의하는 척했고(어쩌면 그녀는 그들이 척하고 있다는 것을 알았을지도 모른다), 그래도 그녀는 계속 이야기했다.\n\n라스콜니코프가 자백한 지 다섯 달 만에 그에게 선고가 내려졌다. 라주미힌과 소냐는 가능한 한 자주 감옥으로 그를 면회하러 갔다. 마침내 이별의 순간이 왔다. 두냐는 오빠에게 이 이별이 영원하지는 않으리라고 맹세했고, 라주미힌도 똑같이 했다. 라주미힌은 젊은이다운 열정으로, 앞으로 삼사 년 안에 적어도 안정된 생계의 기반은 닦아 놓고, 일정한 액수를 모은 뒤, 온갖 천연자원이 풍부하고 일꾼과 활동적인 사람들과 자본을 필요로 하는 나라, 시베리아로 이주하기로 굳게 결심했다. 거기서 그들은 로댜가 있는 도시에 정착하여 다 함께 새 삶을 시작할 터였다. 그들은 모두 헤어지면서 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 며칠 전부터 무척 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그는 어머니에 대해 많은 것을 물었고 끊임없이 그녀를 걱정했다. 그가 어찌나 어머니를 염려하는지 두냐가 다 불안할 정도였다. 어머니의 병에 대해 들었을 때 그는 매우 침울해졌다. 그는 줄곧 소냐에게 특히 말이 없었다. 스비드리가일로프가 그녀에게 남긴 돈의 도움으로, 소냐는 오래전부터 그가 시베리아로 호송되는 죄수 무리를 따라갈 채비를 해 두었다. 라스콜니코프와 그녀 사이에 이 일에 대해 단 한 마디도 오가지 않았지만, 둘 다 그러리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 마지막 작별 인사를 나눌 때, 그는 자기가 출옥하면 함께 누릴 행복한 미래에 대한 누이동생과 라주미힌의 뜨거운 기대를 두고 기이하게 미소 지었다. 그는 어머니의 병이 곧 죽음으로 끝나리라고 예언했다. 소냐와 그는 마침내 길을 떠났다.\n\n두 달 뒤 두냐는 라주미힌과 결혼했다. 그것은 조용하고 슬픈 결혼식이었다. 그래도 포르피리 페트로비치와 조시모프가 초대되었다. 이 모든 기간 동안 라주미힌은 단호한 결의에 찬 모습이었다. 두냐는 그가 자기 계획을 실행에 옮기리라는 것을 굳게 믿었고, 사실 그를 믿지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 보기 드문 강한 의지를 보여 주었다. 무엇보다도 그는 학위를 따기 위해 다시 대학 강의에 출석하기 시작했다. 그들은 끊임없이 미래에 대한 계획을 세웠다. 둘 다 적어도 오 년 안에는 시베리아에 정착할 작정이었다. 그때까지 그들은 소냐에게 희망을 걸었다.\n\n풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 두냐와 라주미힌의 결혼에 기꺼이 축복을 내려 주었다. 그러나 결혼 후 그녀는 한층 더 우울하고 불안해졌다. 그녀를 기쁘게 해 주려고 라주미힌은 라스콜니코프가 어떻게 그 가난한 학생과 그의 노쇠한 아버지를 보살폈는지, 또 일 년 전 어떻게 불난 집에서 어린아이 둘을 구해 내다 화상을 입었는지를 이야기해 주었다. 이 두 가지 소식은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 어지러운 상상력을 거의 황홀경에 이를 만큼 흥분시켰다. 그녀는 끊임없이 그 이야기를 했고, 두냐가 늘 동행하는데도 길거리에서 낯선 사람들과 대화를 나누기까지 했다. 대중교통이나 가게에서, 들어 줄 사람만 붙잡을 수 있으면 어디서든 그녀는 자기 아들 이야기를, 그의 논문 이야기를, 그가 어떻게 그 학생을 도왔는지, 어떻게 불 속에서 화상을 입었는지 따위를 늘어놓기 시작했다! 두냐는 어머니를 어떻게 말려야 할지 몰랐다. 그녀의 병적인 흥분이 위험할 뿐 아니라, 누군가가 라스콜니코프의 이름을 떠올리고 최근의 재판에 대해 입에 올릴 위험도 있었다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 아들이 구해 준 두 아이의 어머니 주소를 알아내서는 기어이 그녀를 찾아가겠다고 고집했다.\n\n마침내 그녀의 안절부절못함은 극에 달했다. 그녀는 때때로 갑자기 울음을 터뜨리곤 했고, 자주 병이 나 열에 들떠 헛소리를 했다. 어느 날 아침 그녀는, 자기 계산으로는 로댜가 곧 집에 돌아올 때가 되었다고, 작별 인사를 할 때 그가 아홉 달 뒤에는 돌아올 테니 기다리라고 했던 것이 기억난다고 선언했다. 그녀는 그가 올 것에 대비하기 시작했고, 그를 위해 방을 단장하고, 가구를 닦고, 새 휘장을 빨아서 다는 등 부산을 떨었다. 두냐는 불안했지만 아무 말도 하지 않고 어머니가 방을 꾸미는 것을 도왔다. 끊임없는 공상과 기쁨에 찬 백일몽과 눈물로 진을 빼는 하루를 보낸 뒤, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 밤사이 병이 났고 아침이 되자 열에 들떠 헛소리를 했다. 뇌막염이었다. 그녀는 보름도 못 되어 세상을 떠났다. 헛소리를 하는 가운데 그녀는, 그들이 짐작했던 것보다 아들의 끔찍한 운명에 대해 훨씬 더 많이 알고 있었음을 드러내는 말들을 흘렸다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 오랫동안 어머니의 죽음을 알지 못했는데, 그가 시베리아에 도착한 때부터 정기적인 서신 왕래가 유지되고 있었는데도 그랬다. 그것은 소냐를 통해 이루어졌으니, 그녀는 매달 라주미힌 부부에게 편지를 썼고 어김없이 규칙적으로 답장을 받았다. 처음에 그들은 소냐의 편지가 무미건조하고 미흡하다고 여겼지만, 나중에는 그 편지들이 더할 나위 없다는 결론에 이르렀는데, 그 편지들로부터 그들은 불행한 오빠의 삶의 완전한 모습을 전해 받았기 때문이다. 소냐의 편지는 더없이 사실적인 세부로, 죄수로서의 라스콜니코프를 둘러싼 모든 것에 대한 가장 단순하고 명료한 묘사로 가득 차 있었다. 자기 자신의 희망에 대한 말도, 미래에 대한 추측도, 자기 감정에 대한 묘사도 없었다. 그의 정신 상태나 내면의 삶을 해석하려는 시도 대신, 그녀는 단순한 사실들을 — 즉 그가 직접 한 말, 그의 건강에 대한 정확한 보고, 면회 때 그가 청한 것, 그가 그녀에게 맡긴 심부름 따위를 전했다. 이 모든 사실을 그녀는 놀라울 만큼 세밀하게 전했다. 불행한 오빠의 모습이 마침내 매우 또렷하고 정확하게 드러났다. 오류가 있을 수 없었으니, 사실 외에는 아무것도 전하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 두냐와 그녀의 남편은 그 소식에서 위안을 거의 얻을 수 없었는데, 특히 처음에는 그랬다. 소냐의 편지에 따르면, 그는 늘 침울하고 말할 마음이 없으며, 그녀가 그들의 편지에서 전해 주는 소식에도 거의 관심이 없는 듯하고, 때때로 어머니의 안부를 물으며, 그가 진실을 짐작했음을 알아챈 그녀가 마침내 어머니의 죽음을 알렸을 때, 그가 적어도 겉으로는 그 일에 크게 동요하지 않는 것처럼 보여 그녀가 놀랐다고 했다. 그녀가 그들에게 전하기를, 그는 그토록 자기 안에 틀어박혀, 말하자면 모두에게서 자신을 차단한 듯 보였지만 — 그래도 자신의 새 삶을 매우 직접적이고 단순하게 받아들였으며, 자기 처지를 이해했고, 당분간 더 나은 것을 기대하지 않았으며, (그런 처지에서 흔히 그러하듯) 근거 없는 희망도 품지 않았고, 예전에 알던 그 어떤 것과도 너무나 다른 자기 주변의 어떤 것에도 거의 놀라지 않는 듯하다고 했다. 그녀는 그의 건강이 양호하며, 그가 일을 게을리하지도 더 하려 들지도 않고 해낸다고 썼다. 그는 음식에 거의 무관심했지만, 일요일과 축일을 빼면 음식이 너무 형편없어서 마침내 매일 자기 차를 마실 수 있도록 그녀, 소냐에게서 약간의 돈을 기꺼이 받았다고 했다. 그는 다른 어떤 것에도 신경 쓰지 말라고 그녀에게 부탁하며, 자기에 대한 이 모든 부산스러움이 그저 짜증스러울 뿐이라고 단언했다. 소냐는 또한, 감옥에서 그가 나머지 사람들과 한 방을 쓰며, 그들의 막사 안은 자기가 본 적이 없지만, 그곳이 비좁고 비참하고 비위생적이리라 짐작한다고 썼다. 그가 밑에 깔개를 깐 널빤지 침상에서 잔다고, 그리고 다른 어떤 조치도 마련하기를 꺼린다고. 그러나 그가 그렇게 가난하고 거칠게 사는 것은 무슨 계획이나 의도에서가 아니라, 그저 무관심과 무신경에서일 뿐이라고 했다.\n\n소냐는 단순히, 그가 처음에는 자기 면회에 아무런 관심도 보이지 않았으며, 사실 자기가 찾아오는 것에 거의 짜증을 냈고, 말하기를 꺼리며 자기에게 무례하게 굴었다고 썼다. 그러나 결국 이 면회가 그에게 하나의 습관이, 거의 필수가 되어서, 그녀가 며칠 아파서 면회를 갈 수 없었을 때 그가 정말로 괴로워했다고 했다. 그녀는 축일이면 감옥 정문에서, 혹은 그가 그녀를 만나러 몇 분 동안 끌려 나오는 위병소에서 그를 보곤 했다. 평일이면 그녀는 그가 일하는 곳으로, 작업장이나 벽돌 가마, 혹은 이르티시 강가의 헛간으로 그를 보러 가곤 했다.\n\n자기 자신에 대해 소냐는, 그 도시에서 몇몇 사람들과 알고 지내게 되었고, 바느질을 하는데, 그 도시에 양재사가 거의 없는 터라 많은 집에서 없어서는 안 될 사람으로 여겨진다고 썼다. 그러나 그녀는 당국이 자기를 통해 라스콜니코프에게 관심을 두고 있다는 것, 그래서 그의 일이 가벼워졌다는 것 따위는 언급하지 않았다.\n\n마침내 소식이 왔는데(사실 두냐는 그에 앞선 편지들에서 불안과 동요의 기미를 알아챘다), 그가 모두에게서 거리를 두고 있으며, 동료 죄수들이 그를 좋아하지 않고, 며칠씩 입을 다물고 있으며, 몹시 창백해지고 있다는 것이었다. 마지막 편지에서 소냐는, 그가 매우 위중하게 병이 나 병원의 죄수 병동에 누워 있다고 썼다.\n\n# II\n\n그는 오랫동안 앓았다. 그러나 그를 무너뜨린 것은 감옥살이의 참상도, 중노동도, 형편없는 음식도, 빡빡 깎인 머리도, 누덕누덕 기운 옷도 아니었다. 그까짓 시련과 고난쯤이 그에게 무슨 상관이랴! 그는 중노동이 차라리 반갑기까지 했다. 육체적으로 진이 빠지면 적어도 몇 시간은 편히 잠들 수 있었으니까. 그리고 음식 따위가 그에게 무슨 대수랴 — 딱정벌레가 둥둥 떠다니는 멀건 양배추 수프가? 학생 시절에 그는 그것조차 먹지 못한 적이 많았다. 그의 옷은 따뜻했고 그의 생활 방식에 맞았다. 그는 족쇄조차 느끼지 못했다. 그가 빡빡 깎인 머리와 얼룩덜룩한 죄수복을 부끄러워했을까? 누구 앞에서? 소냐 앞에서? 소냐가 그를 무서워하는데, 그녀 앞에서 그가 어찌 부끄러워할 수 있겠는가? 그런데도 그는 소냐 앞에서조차 부끄러워했으며, 그 때문에 자신의 경멸하는 듯한 거친 태도로 그녀를 괴롭혔다. 하지만 그가 부끄러워한 것은 빡빡 깎인 머리도 족쇄도 아니었다. 그의 자존심이 깊이 찔렸던 것이다. 그를 병들게 한 것은 상처 입은 자존심이었다. 오, 만일 자기 자신을 탓할 수 있었다면 그는 얼마나 행복했으랴! 그랬다면 그는 그 무엇이라도, 수치와 치욕마저도 견뎌 냈으리라. 그러나 그는 자신을 엄혹하게 심판했고, 그의 격앙된 양심은 자신의 과거에서 그 누구에게나 일어날 수 있는 단순한 실수 하나 말고는 딱히 끔찍한 잘못을 찾아내지 못했다. 그가 부끄러워한 것은 다름 아니라, 자기, 라스콜니코프가 눈먼 운명의 어떤 명령에 의해 그토록 절망적으로, 어리석게 파멸하고 말았다는 것, 그리고 조금이라도 마음의 평안을 얻으려면 그 선고의 \"어리석음\"에 머리를 숙이고 복종해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n현재에는 막연하고 목적 없는 불안이, 미래에는 아무것에도 이르지 못할 끊임없는 희생이 — 그것이 그의 앞에 놓인 전부였다. 팔 년의 끝에 가서 그가 겨우 서른두 살이 되어 새 삶을 시작할 수 있으리라는 것이 그에게 무슨 위안이 되겠는가! 무엇을 위해 살아야 한단 말인가? 무엇을 기대해야 한단 말인가? 무엇을 위해 애써야 한단 말인가? 그저 존재하기 위해 살아야 하나? 아니, 그는 이전에도 천 번이나, 하나의 사상을 위해, 하나의 희망을 위해, 심지어 하나의 공상을 위해 존재를 내던질 각오가 되어 있었다. 단순한 존재는 그에게 늘 너무 보잘것없었다. 그는 늘 그 이상을 원했다. 어쩌면 그가 자신을, 남들보다 더 많은 것이 허용되는 인간이라고 여겼던 것도 바로 그 욕망의 강렬함 때문이었으리라. 그리고 운명이 그에게 회개만이라도 보내 주었더라면 — 가슴을 갈가리 찢고 잠을 앗아 가는 불타는 회개를, 그 끔찍한 고뇌가 목매다는 환영이나 물에 빠지는 환영을 불러오는 그런 회개를! 오, 그는 그것을 반겼으련만! 눈물과 고뇌도 적어도 삶이었으련만. 그러나 그는 자신의 범죄를 회개하지 않았다.\n\n적어도 그는 자신의 어리석음을 향해 분노함으로써 위안을 얻을 수도 있었으련만, 자기를 감옥으로 몰아넣은 그 터무니없는 실수들을 향해 분노했던 것처럼. 그러나 이제 감옥에서, 자유로운 가운데, 그는 자신의 모든 행동을 다시 곱씹어 보고 비판했고, 그것들이 그 숙명적인 때에 보였던 것만큼 그렇게 실수투성이도 터무니없는 것도 결코 아니라고 여기게 되었다.\n\n'대체 무슨 점에서,' 그는 자문했다. '내 이론이 세상이 시작된 이래로 우글거리며 충돌해 온 다른 이론들보다 더 어리석단 말인가? 이 문제를 완전히 독립적으로, 폭넓게, 그리고 진부한 관념에 휘둘리지 않고 바라보기만 하면, 내 사상은 결코 그렇게…… 기이해 보이지 않을 텐데. 오, 회의론자들, 한 푼짜리 철학자들이여, 어찌하여 그대들은 중도에 멈춰 서는가!\n\n'왜 내 행위가 그들에게 그토록 끔찍하게 비치는 걸까?' 그는 혼잣말을 했다. '그것이 범죄였기 때문에? 범죄란 무엇을 뜻하는가? 내 양심은 평온하다. 물론 그것은 법적인 범죄였고, 물론 법조문이 깨졌으며 피가 흘렀다. 그래, 법조문에 대해서라면 나를 벌하라…… 그것으로 족하다. 물론 그렇게 따진다면, 권력을 물려받는 대신 스스로 움켜쥔 인류의 은인들 가운데 상당수도 그 첫걸음에서 벌을 받았어야 했다. 그러나 그자들은 성공했고 그래서 그들이 옳았으며, 나는 성공하지 못했으니 그 걸음을 내디딜 권리가 없었던 것이다.'\n\n그가 자신의 범죄성을 인정한 것은 오직 그 점에서뿐이었다. 오직 자기가 성공하지 못했고 그것을 자백했다는 사실에서뿐.\n\n그는 또 하나의 물음으로도 괴로워했다. 왜 나는 자살하지 않았을까? 왜 나는 강을 바라보며 서 있다가 차라리 자백하는 쪽을 택했을까? 살고자 하는 욕망이 그토록 강하고, 그것을 이겨 내기가 그토록 어려운 일이었던가? 스비드리가일로프는, 죽음을 두려워했으면서도, 그것을 이겨 내지 않았던가?\n\n비참함 속에서 그는 이 물음을 스스로에게 던졌고, 강물을 들여다보며 서 있던 바로 그 순간에 어쩌면 자기 자신과 자기 신념 속의 근본적인 거짓을 어렴풋이 의식하고 있었을지도 모른다는 것을 이해하지 못했다. 그 의식이야말로 미래의 위기에 대한, 삶에 대한 새로운 관점에 대한, 자신의 미래의 부활에 대한 약속일 수 있다는 것을 그는 이해하지 못했다.\n\n그는 그것을, 자신이 넘어설 수 없는 본능의 죽은 무게 탓으로 돌리는 쪽을 택했다, 또다시 나약함과 비열함 탓으로. 그는 동료 죄수들을 바라보며, 그들 모두가 얼마나 삶을 사랑하고 그것을 소중히 여기는지를 보고 놀랐다. 그에게는 그들이 자유로울 때보다 감옥에서 삶을 더 사랑하고 더 소중히 여기는 것처럼 보였다. 그들 중 어떤 이들, 가령 부랑자들은 얼마나 끔찍한 고통과 궁핍을 견뎌 냈던가! 그런데도 그들이 한 줄기 햇살을, 원시림을, 어딘가 보이지 않는 곳에 숨겨진, 부랑자가 삼 년 전에 표시해 두고 마치 연인을 보고 싶어 하듯 다시 보기를 갈망하는 그 차가운 샘을, 그 주위의 푸른 풀과 덤불에서 지저귀는 새를 꿈꾸며 그토록 소중히 여길 수 있단 말인가? 더 나아가면서 그는 한층 더 설명할 수 없는 예들을 보았다.\n\n물론 감옥에서 그가 보지 못한 것, 보고 싶어 하지 않은 것이 많았다. 그는 말하자면 눈을 내리깐 채 살았다. 바라보는 것이 그에게는 역겹고 견딜 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 결국 그를 놀라게 하는 것이 많아졌고, 그는 말하자면 자기도 모르게, 전에는 짐작조차 못 했던 많은 것을 알아차리기 시작했다. 무엇보다 그를 놀라게 한 것은 그와 나머지 모두 사이에 가로놓인 그 끔찍하고 불가능한 심연이었다. 그들은 다른 종족인 것 같았고, 그는 그들을, 그들은 그를 불신과 적의의 눈으로 바라보았다. 그는 자신이 고립된 이유를 느끼고 또 알았지만, 그 이유가 그토록 깊고 강한 것이라고는 그때까지 결코 인정하려 들지 않았었다. 그들 가운데는 폴란드 유형수, 정치범 몇 사람이 있었다. 그들은 나머지 모두를 그저 무지한 촌놈들로 깔보았다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 그들을 그렇게 볼 수 없었다. 그는 이 무지한 사람들이 여러 면에서 폴란드인들보다 훨씬 더 현명하다는 것을 알았다. 똑같이 경멸을 일삼는 러시아인 몇몇도 있었으니, 전직 장교 하나와 신학생 둘이었다. 라스콜니코프는 그들의 잘못 또한 똑똑히 보았다. 그는 모두에게서 미움을 받고 따돌림을 당했다. 그들은 마침내 그를 증오하기까지 했는데 — 왜인지 그는 알 수 없었다. 그보다 훨씬 더 죄가 무거운 자들이 그의 범죄를 경멸하고 비웃었다.\n\n\"넌 신사야.\" 그들은 말하곤 했다. \"넌 도끼를 휘둘러선 안 됐어. 그건 신사가 할 일이 아니거든.\"\n\n사순절 둘째 주에, 자기 작업조와 함께 성찬을 받을 차례가 그에게 돌아왔다. 그는 교회에 가서 다른 이들과 함께 기도했다. 어느 날 어쩌다 그런지도 모르게 다툼이 벌어졌다. 모두가 한꺼번에 격분하여 그에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"넌 무신론자야! 넌 하느님을 안 믿잖아.\" 그들이 소리쳤다. \"넌 죽어 마땅해.\"\n\n그는 그들에게 하느님에 대해서도 자기 신앙에 대해서도 결코 이야기한 적이 없었지만, 그들은 그를 무신론자라며 죽이려 했다. 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 죄수 하나가 완전히 광란에 빠져 그에게 달려들었다. 라스콜니코프는 침착하고 말없이 그를 기다렸다. 그의 눈썹은 떨리지 않았고, 그의 얼굴은 움찔하지도 않았다. 경비병이 가까스로 그와 그 습격자 사이에 끼어들었기에 망정이지, 그러지 않았더라면 피를 보았을 것이다.\n\n그가 풀 수 없는 또 하나의 물음이 있었다. 왜 그들 모두 그토록 소냐를 좋아하는 걸까? 그녀는 그들의 호의를 얻으려 애쓰지 않았다. 그녀는 그들과 좀처럼 마주치지 않았고, 이따금 그가 일하는 곳으로 잠깐 그를 보러 올 뿐이었다. 그런데도 모두가 그녀를 알았고, 그녀가 그를 따라 나왔다는 것을, 그녀가 어떻게 어디서 사는지를 알았다. 그녀는 그들에게 돈을 준 적도, 딱히 무슨 도움을 준 적도 없었다. 단 한 번, 크리스마스 때 그녀가 그들 모두에게 파이와 빵을 선물로 보낸 적은 있었다. 그러나 차츰 그들과 소냐 사이에 더 가까운 관계가 생겨났다. 그녀는 그들을 위해 그들 친척에게 보내는 편지를 써서 부쳐 주곤 했다. 그 도시를 찾은 죄수들의 친척들은, 그들의 부탁에 따라, 소냐에게 그들에게 줄 선물과 돈을 맡기곤 했다. 그들의 아내와 연인들도 그녀를 알고 그녀를 찾아오곤 했다. 그리고 그녀가 일하는 라스콜니코프를 면회하러 오거나 길에서 죄수 한 무리와 마주치면, 그들 모두 그녀에게 모자를 벗어 인사했다. \"소피야 세묘노브나 어머니, 당신은 우리의 다정하고 착한 작은 어머니예요.\" 거칠고 낙인찍힌 죄인들이 그 가냘프고 자그마한 존재에게 그렇게 말했다. 그녀가 미소 지으며 그들에게 고개를 숙이면 모두가 기뻐했다. 그들은 그녀의 걸음걸이마저 좋아해 그녀가 걸어가는 모습을 보려고 돌아보았고, 그녀가 그토록 자그마한 것도 좋아했으니, 사실 무엇 때문에 그녀를 가장 좋아하는지 자신들도 알지 못했다. 그들은 병이 나면 도움을 청하러 그녀에게 오기까지 했다.\n\n그는 사순절 한가운데부터 부활절이 지난 뒤까지 병원에 있었다. 병세가 나아졌을 때, 그는 열에 들떠 헛소리를 하는 동안 꾸었던 꿈들을 떠올렸다. 그는 온 세계가 아시아 깊은 곳에서 유럽으로 건너온 어떤 끔찍하고 새로운 기이한 역병에 의해 멸망하도록 선고받은 꿈을 꾸었다. 극소수의 선택된 자들을 제외하고는 모두가 죽게 되어 있었다. 어떤 새로운 종류의 미생물이 인간의 몸을 공격했는데, 그 미생물에는 지능과 의지가 부여되어 있었다. 그것들에게 공격당한 사람들은 당장 미쳐 날뛰게 되었다. 그러나 인간이 이 환자들만큼 스스로를 그토록 지적이며 그토록 완벽하게 진리를 소유하고 있다고 여긴 적은 일찍이 없었고, 자신들의 결정, 자신들의 과학적 결론, 자신들의 도덕적 신념을 그토록 오류 없는 것으로 여긴 적도 일찍이 없었다. 마을 전체가, 도시 전체가, 민족 전체가 그 감염으로 미쳐 갔다. 모두가 흥분했고 서로를 이해하지 못했다. 저마다 자기 혼자만 진리를 가졌다고 여기며 남들을 바라보고는 비참해하고, 가슴을 치고, 울고, 두 손을 쥐어짰다. 그들은 어떻게 판단해야 할지 몰랐고, 무엇을 악으로 무엇을 선으로 여길지 합의하지 못했다. 누구를 탓하고 누구를 옳다 할지 몰랐다. 사람들은 일종의 무의미한 적의에 차서 서로를 죽였다. 그들은 서로에 맞서 군대를 이루어 모였지만, 행군하는 도중에도 그 군대는 자기들끼리 공격하기 시작했고, 대열이 무너지면 병사들이 서로에게 덤벼들어 찌르고 베고, 물어뜯고 잡아먹었다. 도시에서는 하루 종일 비상종이 울렸다. 사람들이 우르르 모여들었지만, 무엇 때문에 소집된 것인지, 누가 그들을 소집한 것인지 아무도 몰랐다. 가장 평범한 생업도 버려졌으니, 저마다 자기 생각, 자기 식의 개선책을 내세워 합의를 볼 수 없었기 때문이다. 농토 또한 버려졌다. 사람들은 무리 지어 모여 무언가를 합의하고 함께하기로 맹세했지만, 곧장 자기들이 제안한 것과는 전혀 다른 일을 시작했다. 그들은 서로를 비난하고, 싸우고, 죽였다. 큰불과 기근이 일었다. 모든 인간과 모든 것이 파멸에 휩쓸렸다. 역병은 점점 더 멀리멀리 번져 갔다. 온 세상에서 단지 몇 사람만이 구원받을 수 있었다. 그들은 순결한 선택된 백성으로, 새로운 종족과 새로운 삶을 세우고, 대지를 새롭게 하고 정화하도록 운명 지어진 자들이었다. 그러나 아무도 이 사람들을 본 적이 없었고, 아무도 그들의 말과 그들의 목소리를 들은 적이 없었다.\n\n라스콜니코프는 이 무의미한 꿈이 그토록 비참하게 기억에 들러붙어 있는 것이, 이 열병의 헛소리가 남긴 인상이 그토록 오래 가시지 않는 것이 마음에 걸렸다. 부활절이 지난 둘째 주가 왔다. 따뜻하고 화창한 봄날이었다. 죄수 병동에서는 보초가 그 아래로 오가는 창살 달린 창문들이 열렸다. 소냐는 그가 앓는 동안 두 번밖에 그를 면회할 수 없었는데, 매번 허가를 받아야 했고 그것이 어려웠다. 그러나 그녀는 자주 병원 마당으로 오곤 했는데, 특히 저녁이면 그랬고, 때로는 그저 잠깐 서서 병동의 창문들을 올려다보기 위해서였다.\n\n어느 저녁, 그가 거의 회복되었을 무렵, 라스콜니코프는 잠이 들었다. 깨어나서 그는 우연히 창가로 갔다가, 곧장 저 멀리 병원 정문에 서 있는 소냐를 보았다. 그녀는 누군가를 기다리는 듯했다. 그 순간 무언가가 그의 가슴을 찔렀다. 그는 부르르 떨며 창가에서 물러났다. 이튿날 소냐는 오지 않았고, 그 다음 날도 오지 않았다. 그는 자기가 불안한 마음으로 그녀를 기다리고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 마침내 그는 퇴원했다. 감옥에 돌아와 그는 죄수들에게서, 소피야 세묘노브나가 병이 나 집에 누워 있어 밖에 나오지 못한다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n그는 몹시 불안하여 사람을 보내 그녀의 안부를 물었다. 곧 그는 그녀의 병이 위험하지 않다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 자기를 걱정한다는 말을 듣고, 소냐는 연필로 쓴 쪽지를 보내, 자기는 훨씬 나아졌고, 가벼운 감기에 걸렸을 뿐이며, 곧, 아주 곧 그가 일하는 곳으로 그를 보러 가겠다고 전했다. 그것을 읽으며 그의 심장은 고통스럽게 두근거렸다.\n\n또다시 따뜻하고 화창한 날이었다. 이른 아침 여섯 시에 그는 강가로 일을 나갔는데, 그곳에서 그들은 설화석고를 빻았고 헛간 안에는 그것을 굽는 가마가 있었다. 보내진 것은 세 사람뿐이었다. 죄수 하나는 연장을 가지러 경비병과 함께 요새로 갔고, 다른 하나는 장작을 준비해 가마에 넣기 시작했다. 라스콜니코프는 헛간에서 나와 강가로 가서, 헛간 옆 통나무 더미에 앉아 넓고 적막한 강을 바라보기 시작했다. 높은 강둑에서 드넓은 풍경이 그의 앞에 펼쳐졌고, 노랫소리가 맞은편 강가에서 희미하게 들려왔다. 햇살에 잠긴 광막한 초원에는 유목민의 천막들이 검은 점들처럼 겨우 보였다. 거기에는 자유가 있었고, 거기에는 이곳 사람들과는 전혀 다른 사람들이 살고 있었다. 거기서는 시간 자체가 멈춰 선 듯했으니, 마치 아브라함과 그의 양 떼의 시대가 아직 지나가지 않은 것만 같았다. 라스콜니코프는 앉아 바라보았고, 그의 생각은 백일몽으로, 명상으로 흘러갔다. 그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았지만, 막연한 불안이 그를 흥분시키고 어지럽혔다. 갑자기 그는 소냐가 자기 곁에 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 소리 없이 다가와 그의 곁에 앉았던 것이다. 아직 무척 일렀고, 아침 한기가 여전히 매서웠다. 그녀는 낡고 초라한 외투와 초록색 숄을 두르고 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 아직 병색이 남아 있어, 한층 야위고 창백했다. 그녀는 그에게 반가운 기쁨의 미소를 지어 보였지만, 손은 여느 때처럼 수줍게 내밀었다. 그녀는 늘 그에게 손을 내미는 것을 수줍어했고, 때로는 그가 뿌리칠까 두려운 듯 아예 내밀지 않기도 했다. 그는 늘 마지못한 듯 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 늘 그녀를 만나는 것이 짜증스러운 듯했으며, 때로는 그녀가 면회하는 내내 고집스레 입을 다물고 있었다. 어떤 때는 그녀가 그 앞에서 부들부들 떨다가 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 돌아가곤 했다. 그러나 이번에는 그들의 손이 떨어지지 않았다. 그는 그녀를 재빨리 흘끗 보고는 아무 말 없이 땅바닥으로 눈을 떨구었다. 그들은 단둘이었고, 아무도 그들을 보지 못했다. 경비병은 잠시 자리를 비우고 있었다. 어떻게 그런 일이 일어났는지 그는 알지 못했다. 그러나 별안간 무언가가\n\n그를 사로잡아 그녀의 발치에 내던졌다. 그는 흐느끼며 두 팔로 그녀의 무릎을 끌어안았다. 처음 한순간 그녀는 끔찍하게 놀라 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 부들부들 떨며 그를 바라보았다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그녀는 깨달았고, 그녀의 두 눈에 무한한 행복의 빛이 떠올랐다. 그녀는 그가 무엇보다도 자신을 사랑한다는 것을, 그리고 마침내 그 순간이 왔다는 것을 알았고, 또 의심하지 않았다…….\n\n그들은 말을 하려 했으나 할 수 없었다. 두 사람의 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었다. 둘 다 창백하고 야위었지만, 그 병들고 핼쑥한 얼굴은 새로운 미래의 여명으로, 새로운 삶으로의 완전한 부활로 환하게 빛났다. 그들은 사랑으로 새로워졌다. 두 사람의 가슴은 서로의 가슴을 위해 무한한 생명의 샘을 품고 있었다.\n\n그들은 기다리며 인내하기로 마음먹었다. 그들에게는 아직 칠 년이라는 세월이 남아 있었고, 그 앞에는 얼마나 끔찍한 고통이, 그리고 얼마나 무한한 행복이 놓여 있었던가! 그러나 그는 다시 일어섰고, 자신의 온 존재로 그것을 알고 또 느꼈다. 한편 그녀는 — 그녀는 오로지 그의 삶 속에서만 살았다.\n\n같은 날 저녁, 막사가 잠겼을 때, 라스콜니코프는 판자 침상에 누워 그녀를 생각했다. 그날따라 그는 그동안 자신의 적이었던 죄수들조차 그를 다른 눈으로 바라보는 것만 같다는 생각마저 들었다. 그는 그들에게 말을 걸기까지 했고, 그들도 다정하게 대답해 주었다. 이제 그것을 떠올리며, 그는 그렇게 되는 것이 마땅하다고 생각했다. 이제 모든 것이 변하는 게 당연하지 않은가?\n\n그는 그녀를 생각했다. 그는 자신이 얼마나 끊임없이 그녀를 괴롭혔고 그녀의 마음에 상처를 입혔는지를 떠올렸다. 그는 그녀의 창백하고 야윈 작은 얼굴을 떠올렸다. 그러나 이런 회상들은 이제 그를 거의 괴롭히지 못했다. 그는 자신이 이제 얼마나 무한한 사랑으로 그녀의 모든 고통을 갚아 줄 것인지 알고 있었다. 그러니 과거의 그 모든, 그 모든 고뇌가 다 무엇이란 말인가! 모든 것이, 심지어 그의 범죄도, 그의 선고와 유형마저도, 이제 솟구치는 감정의 첫 물결 속에서는 그와는 아무 상관도 없는, 외적이고 낯선 사실처럼 느껴졌다. 그러나 그날 저녁 그는 무엇 하나에 대해서도 오래 생각을 이어 가지 못했고, 그 무엇도 의식적으로 분석할 수 없었다. 그는 그저 느낄 뿐이었다. 이론의 자리에 삶이 들어섰고, 그의 마음속에서는 무언가 전혀 다른 것이 스스로 펼쳐지려 하고 있었다.\n\n그의 베개 밑에는 신약 성경이 놓여 있었다. 그는 무심코 그것을 집어 들었다. 그 책은 소냐의 것으로, 그녀가 그에게 나사로의 부활을 읽어 주었던 바로 그 책이었다. 처음에 그는 그녀가 종교 문제로 자신을 들볶고, 복음에 대해 떠들며 책으로 귀찮게 굴까 봐 두려웠다. 그러나 놀랍게도 그녀는 단 한 번도 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았고, 성경을 권하지조차 않았다. 그는 병에 걸리기 얼마 전에 직접 그것을 그녀에게 청했고, 그녀는 말없이 그 책을 가져다주었다. 지금까지 그는 그것을 펼쳐 본 적이 없었다.\n\n그는 지금도 그것을 펼치지 않았지만, 한 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. '이제 그녀의 신념이 나의 것이 될 수는 없을까? 적어도 그녀의 감정, 그녀의 갈망만이라도…….'\n\n그날 그녀 역시 몹시 동요했고, 밤에는 다시 앓아누웠다. 그러나 그녀는 너무도 행복했고 — 너무도 뜻밖에 행복했기에 — 자신의 행복이 거의 두렵기까지 했다. 칠 년, 겨우 칠 년이라니! 행복이 시작된 무렵 어떤 순간에는, 두 사람 모두 그 칠 년을 마치 이레인 양 여길 채비가 되어 있었다. 그는 새로운 삶이 거저 주어지지 않으리라는 것을, 그것을 위해 값비싼 대가를 치러야 하리라는 것을, 그것이 그에게 엄청난 분투와 엄청난 고통을 요구하리라는 것을 알지 못했다.\n\n그러나 여기서부터 새로운 이야기가 시작된다 — 한 인간이 점차 새로워져 가는 이야기, 그가 점차 거듭나는 이야기, 그가 한 세계에서 다른 세계로 건너가, 미지의 새로운 삶에 발을 들여놓는 이야기가. 그것은 새로운 이야기의 주제가 될 수 있겠지만, 지금 우리의 이야기는 여기서 끝난다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-2554"]=[[["A few words about Dostoevsky himself may help the English reader to understand his work.", "7월 초의 유난히 무더운 어느 저녁, 한 청년이 S 골목에 세 들어 살던 다락방에서 나와, 망설이듯 천천히 K 다리 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Dostoevsky was the son of a doctor.", "그는 계단에서 집주인 여자와 마주치는 일을 용케 피했다."], ["His parents were very hard-working and deeply religious people, but so poor that they lived with their five children in only two rooms.", "그의 다락방은 오 층짜리 높은 건물의 지붕 바로 밑에 있었는데, 방이라기보다는 차라리 벽장에 가까웠다."], ["The father and mother spent their evenings in reading aloud to their children, generally from books of a serious character.", "다락방과 식사, 잔심부름까지 챙겨 주는 집주인 여자는 아래층에 살았고, 그래서 그가 밖으로 나갈 때면 매번 그녀의 부엌을 지나가야만 했는데, 그 부엌문은 언제나 열려 있었다."], ["Though always sickly and delicate Dostoevsky came out third in the final examination of the Petersburg school of Engineering.", "그리고 그 앞을 지날 때마다 청년은 메스껍고 두려운 기분에 휩싸여 얼굴을 찌푸리고 부끄러움을 느꼈다."], ["There he had already begun his first work, “Poor Folk.”", "그는 집주인 여자에게 절망적일 만큼 빚을 지고 있었고, 그녀와 마주치는 것이 두려웠다."]], [["This story was published by the poet Nekrassov in his review and was received with acclamations. The shy, unknown youth found himself instantly something of a celebrity.", "이는 그가 비겁하고 비굴해서가 아니었다."], ["A brilliant and successful career seemed to open before him, but those hopes were soon dashed. In 1849 he was arrested.", "오히려 정반대였다."], ["Though neither by temperament nor conviction a revolutionist, Dostoevsky was one of a little group of young men who met together to read Fourier and Proudhon. He was accused of “taking part in conversations against the censorship, of reading a letter from Byelinsky to Gogol, and of knowing of the intention to set up a printing press.”", "그러나 얼마 전부터 그는 우울증에 가까운, 과도하게 긴장되고 신경질적인 상태에 빠져 있었다."], ["Under Nicholas I. (that “stern and just man,”", "자기 자신에게 너무도 완전히 몰두한 나머지 주변 사람들로부터 고립되어, 집주인 여자뿐 아니라 그 누구와 마주치는 것조차 끔찍해했다."], ["as Maurice Baring calls him) this was enough, and he was condemned to death. After eight months’ imprisonment he was with twenty-one others taken out to the Semyonovsky Square to be shot.", "그는 가난에 짓눌려 있었지만, 최근에는 자기 처지에 대한 근심마저 그를 더는 짓누르지 못했다."], ["Writing to his brother Mihail, Dostoevsky says: “They snapped words over our heads, and they made us put on the white shirts worn by persons condemned to death. Thereupon we were bound in threes to stakes, to suffer execution.", "실질적으로 중요한 일들에는 신경 쓰기를 그만두었고, 그러고 싶은 마음조차 깡그리 사라져 버렸다."], ["Being the third in the row, I concluded I had only a few minutes of life before me. I thought of you and your dear ones and I contrived to kiss Plestcheiev and Dourov, who were next to me, and to bid them farewell.", "집주인 여자가 무슨 짓을 한들 그에게는 진짜 두려움이 되지 못했다."], ["Suddenly the troops beat a tattoo, we were unbound, brought back upon the scaffold, and informed that his Majesty had spared us our lives.” The sentence was commuted to hard labour.", "하지만 계단에서 붙잡혀, 그녀의 시시하고 부질없는 잔소리며 빚 독촉, 협박과 불평을 억지로 듣고 있어야 하고, 변명거리를 짜내느라 머리를 쥐어짜고, 얼버무리고, 거짓말을 해야 한다……, 아니, 그럴 바엔 차라리 고양이처럼 계단을 살금살금 내려가 아무도 모르게 빠져나가는 편이 나았다."]], [["One of the prisoners, Grigoryev, went mad as soon as he was untied, and never regained his sanity.", "그러나 이날 저녁, 거리로 나서면서 그는 자신의 두려움을 예리하게 의식하게 되었다."]], [["The intense suffering of this experience left a lasting stamp on Dostoevsky’s mind. Though his religious temper led him in the end to accept every suffering with resignation and to regard it as a blessing in his own case, he constantly recurs to the subject in his writings. He describes the awful agony of the condemned man and insists on the cruelty of inflicting such torture. Then followed four years of penal servitude, spent in the company of common criminals in Siberia, where he began the “Dead House,” and some years of service in a disciplinary battalion.", "'나는 그런 일을 저지르려 하면서, 이런 하찮은 것들에 겁을 먹고 있군.' 그는 묘한 미소를 지으며 생각했다. '흠……, 그래, 모든 것은 인간의 손에 달려 있는데, 인간은 그 모든 걸 비겁함 때문에 놓쳐 버리지, 이건 공리야. 인간이 가장 두려워하는 게 뭔지 안다면 흥미로울 텐데. 새로운 발걸음을 내딛는 것, 새로운 말을 입에 올리는 것, 바로 그것을 가장 두려워하지……. 하지만 내가 말이 너무 많군. 내가 떠벌리기만 하니까 아무것도 하지 못하는 거야. 아니 어쩌면 아무것도 하지 않으니까 떠벌리는 건지도. 지난 한 달 동안 나는 떠벌리는 법을 배웠어, 며칠이고 내 굴속에 누워서…… 동화 같은 생각이나 하면서. 그런데 내가 지금 왜 거기로 가고 있는 거지? 내가 정말 그런 일을 할 수 있을까? 그게 진심이긴 한가? 전혀 진심이 아니야. 그저 나를 즐겁게 하려는 공상일 뿐이지, 장난감이야! 그래, 어쩌면 장난감인지도.'"]], [["He had shown signs of some obscure nervous disease before his arrest and this now developed into violent attacks of epilepsy, from which he suffered for the rest of his life.", "거리의 더위는 끔찍했다. 게다가 숨 막히는 공기와 북적임, 사방에 널린 회반죽과 비계며 벽돌, 먼지, 그리고 여름에 도시를 빠져나가지 못하는 모든 이들에게 너무도 익숙한 페테르부르크 특유의 악취 — 이 모든 것이 이미 과도하게 곤두선 청년의 신경을 고통스럽게 자극했다."], ["The fits occurred three or four times a year and were more frequent in periods of great strain. In 1859 he was allowed to return to Russia.", "이 동네에 유난히 많은 선술집에서 풍겨 나오는 견딜 수 없는 악취, 그리고 평일인데도 끊임없이 마주치는 술 취한 사내들이 이 혐오스러운 풍경을 더없이 비참하게 완성시켰다."], ["He started a journal--“Vremya,” which was forbidden by the Censorship through a misunderstanding.", "청년의 섬세한 얼굴에 한순간 깊디깊은 혐오의 표정이 스쳤다."], ["In 1864 he lost his first wife and his brother Mihail. He was in terrible poverty, yet he took upon himself the payment of his brother’s debts.", "그건 그렇고, 그는 유난히 잘생긴 청년으로, 평균보다 키가 크고 호리호리하면서도 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 아름다운 검은 눈과 짙은 갈색 머리칼을 지니고 있었다."], ["He started another journal--“The Epoch,” which within a few months was also prohibited.", "곧 그는 깊은 생각에, 아니 더 정확히 말하면 완전한 정신의 공허 속에 빠져들었다."], ["He was weighed down by debt, his brother’s family was dependent on him, he was forced to write at heart-breaking speed, and is said never to have corrected his work.", "그는 주변의 무엇도 보지 않고, 보려고도 하지 않은 채 걸어갔다."], ["The later years of his life were much softened by the tenderness and devotion of his second wife.", "가끔씩 그는 무언가를 중얼거렸는데, 방금 스스로 고백했던 그 혼잣말하는 버릇 탓이었다."], ["In June 1880 he made his famous speech at the unveiling of the monument to Pushkin in Moscow and he was received with extraordinary demonstrations of love and honour.", "그런 순간이면 그는 자신의 생각이 때때로 뒤엉켜 있고 자신이 몹시 쇠약해져 있음을 자각하곤 했다. 이틀 동안 그는 거의 아무것도 먹지 못했던 것이다."]], [["A few months later Dostoevsky died.", "그는 옷차림이 너무도 형편없어서, 누더기에 익숙한 사람이라 해도 그런 차림으로 거리에 나서기는 부끄러워했을 정도였다."], ["He was followed to the grave by a vast multitude of mourners, who “gave the hapless man the funeral of a king.”", "하지만 이 동네에서는 옷차림이 아무리 변변찮아도 놀랄 일이 거의 없었다. 건초 시장이 가까운 데다, 평판 나쁜 업소가 즐비하고, 페테르부르크 한복판의 이 거리와 골목에 장사치며 노동자 계층이 빽빽이 몰려든 탓에, 거리에는 워낙 별의별 인간들이 다 보여서 아무리 괴상한 행색이라도 놀라움을 자아내지 못했다."], ["He is still probably the most widely read writer in Russia.", "그러나 청년의 마음속에는 쌓이고 쌓인 쓰라림과 경멸이 가득해서, 젊은이 특유의 까다로움에도 불구하고 거리에서만큼은 자신의 누더기를 가장 개의치 않았다."], ["In the words of a Russian critic, who seeks to explain the feeling inspired by Dostoevsky: “He was one of ourselves, a man of our blood and our bone, but one who has suffered and has seen so much more deeply than we have his insight impresses us as wisdom...", "아는 사람이나 옛 동창들과 마주칠 때는 사정이 달랐는데, 사실 그는 언제든 그들과 마주치는 것을 싫어했다. 그런데 어쩐 영문인지 거대한 짐마차에 실려 어디론가 끌려가던 술 취한 사내 하나가 — 무거운 짐말이 끄는 마차였다 — 그의 곁을 지나치며 갑자기 목청껏 소리를 지르고 그를 손가락질하며 \"야, 거기, 독일 모자쟁이야!\""], ["that wisdom of the heart which we seek that we may learn from it how to live.", "하고 악을 써 댔을 때, 청년은 갑자기 걸음을 멈추고 부들부들 떨며 자기 모자를 움켜쥐었다."], ["All his other gifts came to him from nature, this he won for himself and through it he became great.”", "그것은 치머만 가게에서 산 키 높은 둥근 모자였는데, 이제는 완전히 닳아 빠지고 세월에 누렇게 절고, 온통 찢기고 얼룩진 데다, 챙도 없이 한쪽으로 보기 흉하게 꺾여 있었다. 그러나 그를 사로잡은 것은 부끄러움이 아니라, 차라리 공포에 가까운 전혀 다른 감정이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["On an exceptionally hot evening early in July a young man came out of the garret in which he lodged in S.", "'그럴 줄 알았어.' 그는 당황한 채 중얼거렸다."], ["Place and walked slowly, as though in hesitation, towards K.", "'그럴 거라고 생각했다고! 이게 무엇보다 최악이야!"], ["bridge. He had successfully avoided meeting his landlady on the staircase.", "아니, 이런 멍청한 것, 이렇게 사소한 것 하나가 계획 전체를 망쳐 버릴 수도 있어."], ["His garret was under the roof of a high, five-storied house and was more like a cupboard than a room.", "그래, 내 모자가 너무 눈에 띄어……. 우스꽝스러우니까 눈에 띄는 거지……."], ["The landlady who provided him with garret, dinners, and attendance, lived on the floor below, and every time he went out he was obliged to pass her kitchen, the door of which invariably stood open.", "이런 누더기 차림이면 차라리 챙 달린 모자를, 아무 낡아빠진 납작모자라도 써야지, 이런 괴상한 물건이 아니라. 아무도 이런 모자를 쓰지 않으니, 일 마일 밖에서도 눈에 띄고 기억에 남을 거야……."], ["And each time he passed, the young man had a sick, frightened feeling, which made him scowl and feel ashamed.", "중요한 건 사람들이 이걸 기억할 거라는 점이고, 그게 그들에게 단서가 되겠지. 이런 일에서는 가능한 한 눈에 띄지 않아야 하는데……."], ["He was hopelessly in debt to his landlady, and was afraid of meeting her.", "사소한 것들, 사소한 것들이 중요하단 말이야! 아니, 바로 그런 사소한 것들이 늘 모든 걸 망쳐 놓지…….'"]], [["This was not because he was cowardly and abject, quite the contrary; but for some time past he had been in an overstrained irritable condition, verging on hypochondria.", "갈 길은 멀지 않았다."], ["He had become so completely absorbed in himself, and isolated from his fellows that he dreaded meeting, not only his landlady, but anyone at all.", "사실 그는 자기 하숙집 대문에서 거기까지가 몇 걸음인지도 알고 있었다. 정확히 칠백서른 걸음이었다."], ["He was crushed by poverty, but the anxieties of his position had of late ceased to weigh upon him.", "언젠가 공상에 푹 빠져 있을 때 한번 세어 본 적이 있었다."], ["He had given up attending to matters of practical importance; he had lost all desire to do so. Nothing that any landlady could do had a real terror for him.", "그때만 해도 그는 그 공상을 믿지 않았고, 그 끔찍하면서도 대담한 무모함으로 그저 스스로를 안달하게 했을 뿐이었다."], ["But to be stopped on the stairs, to be forced to listen to her trivial, irrelevant gossip, to pestering demands for payment, threats and complaints, and to rack his brains for excuses, to prevaricate, to lie--no, rather than that, he would creep down the stairs like a cat and slip out unseen.", "그런데 한 달이 지난 지금, 그는 그것을 다르게 바라보기 시작했고, 자신의 무력함과 우유부단함을 비웃는 독백을 늘어놓으면서도, 어느새 자신도 모르게 이 '끔찍한' 공상을 한번 시도해 볼 만한 위업으로 여기게 되었던 것이다."], ["This evening, however, on coming out into the street, he became acutely aware of his fears.", "비록 그 자신은 아직 이를 깨닫지 못하고 있었지만. 그는 지금 분명 자기 계획의 '예행연습'을 하러 가는 길이었고, 한 걸음 한 걸음 옮길 때마다 그의 흥분은 점점 더 격렬해졌다."]], [["“I want to attempt a thing _like that_ and am frightened by these trifles,” he thought, with an odd smile. “Hm... yes, all is in a man’s hands and he lets it all slip from cowardice, that’s an axiom. It would be interesting to know what it is men are most afraid of. Taking a new step, uttering a new word is what they fear most.... But I am talking too much. It’s because I chatter that I do nothing. Or perhaps it is that I chatter because I do nothing. I’ve learned to chatter this last month, lying for days together in my den thinking... of Jack the Giant-killer. Why am I going there now? Am I capable of _that_? Is _that_ serious? It is not serious at all. It’s simply a fantasy to amuse myself; a plaything! Yes, maybe it is a plaything.”", "가슴이 철렁 내려앉고 신경이 떨려 오는 가운데, 그는 한쪽으로는 운하를, 다른 한쪽으로는 거리를 향해 있는 어느 거대한 건물로 다가갔다. 이 건물은 작디작은 셋방들로 쪼개져 세 놓여 있었고, 온갖 부류의 노동자들이 살고 있었다 — 재단사, 자물쇠공, 요리사, 갖가지 독일인들, 닥치는 대로 입에 풀칠하는 처녀들, 말단 관리들 등등. 두 개의 출입문과 두 개의 안뜰로는 사람들이 끊임없이 드나들었다. 건물에는 서너 명의 문지기가 고용되어 있었다. 청년은 그들 중 누구와도 마주치지 않은 것이 무척 다행스러웠고, 곧장 눈에 띄지 않게 오른쪽 문으로 빠져나가 계단을 올랐다. 그것은 어둡고 좁은 뒷계단이었지만, 그는 이미 그곳에 익숙해 길을 잘 알았고, 이 모든 환경이 마음에 들었다. 이런 어둠 속에서라면 아무리 캐묻기 좋아하는 눈길이라도 두려워할 필요가 없었으니까."]], [["The heat in the street was terrible: and the airlessness, the bustle and the plaster, scaffolding, bricks, and dust all about him, and that special Petersburg stench, so familiar to all who are unable to get out of town in summer--all worked painfully upon the young man’s already overwrought nerves.", "'지금도 이렇게 겁을 먹는데, 만일 어쩌다 내가 정말로 그 일을 하러 가게 된다면 어떨까?'"], ["The insufferable stench from the pot-houses, which are particularly numerous in that part of the town, and the drunken men whom he met continually, although it was a working day, completed the revolting misery of the picture.", "사 층에 다다르며 그는 스스로에게 이렇게 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 거기서 그의 발걸음은, 어느 셋집에서 가구를 들어내고 있던 인부 몇 사람에게 가로막혔다."], ["An expression of the profoundest disgust gleamed for a moment in the young man’s refined face.", "그는 그 셋집에 관청에 다니는 독일인 관리와 그 가족이 살고 있었다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He was, by the way, exceptionally handsome, above the average in height, slim, well-built, with beautiful dark eyes and dark brown hair.", "그러니까 그 독일인이 이사를 나가고 있었던 것이고, 따라서 이 계단의 사 층은 그 노파를 빼고는 텅 비게 될 터였다. '어쨌든 잘된 일이야.'"], ["Soon he sank into deep thought, or more accurately speaking into a complete blankness of mind; he walked along not observing what was about him and not caring to observe it.", "그는 노파의 셋집 초인종을 울리며 속으로 생각했다. 초인종은 구리가 아니라 양철로 만든 것인 양 희미하게 딸랑거렸다."], ["From time to time, he would mutter something, from the habit of talking to himself, to which he had just confessed.", "이런 건물의 작은 셋집들은 늘 그렇게 울리는 초인종을 달고 있었다."], ["At these moments he would become conscious that his ideas were sometimes in a tangle and that he was very weak; for two days he had scarcely tasted food.", "그는 그 초인종 소리를 잊고 있었는데, 이제 그 독특한 딸랑임이 무언가를 일깨우며 또렷이 눈앞에 떠올려 주는 것 같았다……."], ["He was so badly dressed that even a man accustomed to shabbiness would have been ashamed to be seen in the street in such rags.", "그는 흠칫했다. 이제 그의 신경은 끔찍하게 곤두서 있었다."], ["In that quarter of the town, however, scarcely any shortcoming in dress would have created surprise.", "잠시 후, 문이 아주 조금 빠끔히 열렸다."], ["Owing to the proximity of the Hay Market, the number of establishments of bad character, the preponderance of the trading and working class population crowded in these streets and alleys in the heart of Petersburg, types so various were to be seen in the streets that no figure, however queer, would have caused surprise.", "노파는 그 틈으로 방문객을 노골적인 불신의 눈초리로 살펴보았는데, 어둠 속에서 반짝이는 그녀의 작은 두 눈 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 층계참에 여러 사람이 있는 것을 보자 그녀는 좀 더 대담해져서 문을 활짝 열었다."], ["But there was such accumulated bitterness and contempt in the young man’s heart, that, in spite of all the fastidiousness of youth, he minded his rags least of all in the street.", "청년은 작디작은 부엌과 칸막이로 나뉜 어두운 현관으로 발을 들여놓았다. 노파는 말없이 그와 마주 서서 캐묻듯 그를 바라보았다."], ["It was a different matter when he met with acquaintances or with former fellow students, whom, indeed, he disliked meeting at any time.", "그녀는 예순쯤 된, 자그마하고 바싹 야윈 노파로, 날카롭고 표독스러운 눈과 뾰족한 작은 코를 지니고 있었다."], ["And yet when a drunken man who, for some unknown reason, was being taken somewhere in a huge waggon dragged by a heavy dray horse, suddenly shouted at him as he drove past: “Hey there, German hatter”", "윤기 없이 희끗희끗 센 머리칼은 기름을 잔뜩 발라 떡이 졌고, 그 위에 두건 따위는 쓰지 않았다. 닭다리처럼 가늘고 긴 목에는 플란넬 누더기 같은 것이 묶여 있었고, 더위에도 불구하고 어깨에는 세월에 누렇게 바랜 다 해진 모피 망토가 펄럭이며 늘어져 있었다."], ["bawling at the top of his voice and pointing at him--the young man stopped suddenly and clutched tremulously at his hat.", "노파는 시도 때도 없이 기침을 하고 끙끙거렸다."], ["It was a tall round hat from Zimmerman’s, but completely worn out, rusty with age, all torn and bespattered, brimless and bent on one side in a most unseemly fashion. Not shame, however, but quite another feeling akin to terror had overtaken him.", "청년이 어지간히 묘한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보았던 모양인지, 그녀의 눈에는 또다시 불신의 빛이 어렸다."]], [["“I knew it,” he muttered in confusion, “I thought so! That’s the worst of all! Why, a stupid thing like this, the most trivial detail might spoil the whole plan. Yes, my hat is too noticeable.... It looks absurd and that makes it noticeable.... With my rags I ought to wear a cap, any sort of old pancake, but not this grotesque thing. Nobody wears such a hat, it would be noticed a mile off, it would be remembered.... What matters is that people would remember it, and that would give them a clue. For this business one should be as little conspicuous as possible.... Trifles, trifles are what matter! Why, it’s just such trifles that always ruin everything....” He had not far to go; he knew indeed how many steps it was from the gate of his lodging house: exactly seven hundred and thirty. He had counted them once when he had been lost in dreams. At the time he had put no faith in those dreams and was only tantalising himself by their hideous but daring recklessness. Now, a month later, he had begun to look upon them differently, and, in spite of the monologues in which he jeered at his own impotence and indecision, he had involuntarily come to regard this “hideous” dream as an exploit to be attempted, although he still did not realise this himself. He was positively going now for a “rehearsal” of his project, and at every step his excitement grew more and more violent.", "\"라스콜니코프입니다, 대학생이고요, 한 달 전에 여기 왔었지요.\" 청년은 좀 더 공손해야 한다는 것을 떠올리고는 반쯤 고개를 숙이며 서둘러 중얼거렸다."]], [["With a sinking heart and a nervous tremor, he went up to a huge house which on one side looked on to the canal, and on the other into the street. This house was let out in tiny tenements and was inhabited by working people of all kinds--tailors, locksmiths, cooks, Germans of sorts, girls picking up a living as best they could, petty clerks, etc. There was a continual coming and going through the two gates and in the two courtyards of the house. Three or four door-keepers were employed on the building. The young man was very glad to meet none of them, and at once slipped unnoticed through the door on the right, and up the staircase. It was a back staircase, dark and narrow, but he was familiar with it already, and knew his way, and he liked all these surroundings: in such darkness even the most inquisitive eyes were not to be dreaded. “If I am so scared now, what would it be if it somehow came to pass that I were really going to do it?” he could not help asking himself as he reached the fourth storey. There his progress was barred by some porters who were engaged in moving furniture out of a flat. He knew that the flat had been occupied by a German clerk in the civil service, and his family. This German was moving out then, and so the fourth floor on this staircase would be untenanted except by the old woman. “That’s a good thing anyway,” he thought to himself, as he rang the bell of the old woman’s flat. The bell gave a faint tinkle as though it were made of tin and not of copper. The little flats in such houses always have bells that ring like that. He had forgotten the note of that bell, and now its peculiar tinkle seemed to remind him of something and to bring it clearly before him.... He started, his nerves were terribly overstrained by now. In a little while, the door was opened a tiny crack: the old woman eyed her visitor with evident distrust through the crack, and nothing could be seen but her little eyes, glittering in the darkness. But, seeing a number of people on the landing, she grew bolder, and opened the door wide. The young man stepped into the dark entry, which was partitioned off from the tiny kitchen. The old woman stood facing him in silence and looking inquiringly at him. She was a diminutive, withered up old woman of sixty, with sharp malignant eyes and a sharp little nose. Her colourless, somewhat grizzled hair was thickly smeared with oil, and she wore no kerchief over it. Round her thin long neck, which looked like a hen’s leg, was knotted some sort of flannel rag, and, in spite of the heat, there hung flapping on her shoulders, a mangy fur cape, yellow with age. The old woman coughed and groaned at every instant. The young man must have looked at her with a rather peculiar expression, for a gleam of mistrust came into her eyes again.", "\"기억하고말고요, 선생, 잘 기억하고 있어요, 댁이 여기 왔던 거.\" 노파는 또박또박 말하면서도 여전히 캐묻는 눈길을 그의 얼굴에서 떼지 않았다."]], [["“Raskolnikov, a student, I came here a month ago,”", "\"그래서…… 또 같은 일로 왔습니다.\""], ["the young man made haste to mutter, with a half bow, remembering that he ought to be more polite.", "라스콜니코프는 노파의 불신에 다소 당황하고 놀라며 말을 이었다."], ["“I remember, my good sir, I remember quite well your coming here,”", "'하지만 늘 저런 식인지도 모르지, 지난번에는 미처 알아차리지 못했을 뿐이고.'"], ["the old woman said distinctly, still keeping her inquiring eyes on his face.", "그는 께름칙한 기분으로 생각했다."]], [["“And here... I am again on the same errand,” Raskolnikov continued, a little disconcerted and surprised at the old woman’s mistrust. “Perhaps she is always like that though, only I did not notice it the other time,” he thought with an uneasy feeling. The old woman paused, as though hesitating; then stepped on one side, and pointing to the door of the room, she said, letting her visitor pass in front of her:", "노파는 망설이는 듯 잠시 가만히 있다가, 한쪽으로 비켜서더니 방문을 가리키며 방문객을 앞세우고 말했다."]], [["“Step in, my good sir.”", "\"들어오시구려, 선생.\""]], [["The little room into which the young man walked, with yellow paper on the walls, geraniums and muslin curtains in the windows, was brightly lighted up at that moment by the setting sun.", "청년이 들어선 작은 방은, 벽에 노란 벽지가 발려 있고 창가에는 제라늄 화분과 모슬린 커튼이 드리워져 있었는데, 그 순간 지는 해에 환하게 물들어 있었다."]], [["“So the sun will shine like this _then_ too!”", "'그렇다면 그때도 해가 이렇게 비치겠군!'"], ["flashed as it were by chance through Raskolnikov’s mind, and with a rapid glance he scanned everything in the room, trying as far as possible to notice and remember its arrangement.", "어쩐지 우연인 듯 라스콜니코프의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔고, 그는 재빠른 눈길로 방 안의 모든 것을 훑으며, 가능한 한 그 배치를 눈여겨보고 기억해 두려 애썼다."], ["But there was nothing special in the room.", "하지만 방 안에 특별한 것은 없었다."], ["The furniture, all very old and of yellow wood, consisted of a sofa with a huge bent wooden back, an oval table in front of the sofa, a dressing-table with a looking-glass fixed on it between the windows, chairs along the walls and two or three half-penny prints in yellow frames, representing German damsels with birds in their hands--that was all.", "가구는 모두 아주 낡고 노란 목재로 된 것들로, 거대하게 휘어진 나무 등받이가 달린 소파, 소파 앞의 타원형 탁자, 창과 창 사이에 거울이 붙은 화장대, 벽을 따라 늘어선 의자들, 그리고 노란 액자에 끼운 두서너 점의 싸구려 판화 — 손에 새를 든 독일 처녀들을 그린 — 가 전부였다."], ["In the corner a light was burning before a small ikon.", "한쪽 구석에는 작은 성상 앞에 등불이 켜져 있었다."], ["Everything was very clean; the floor and the furniture were brightly polished; everything shone.", "모든 것이 무척 깨끗했다. 바닥도 가구도 반들반들 닦여, 모든 것이 반짝였다."]], [["“Lizaveta’s work,” thought the young man.", "'리자베타가 한 일이군.'"], ["There was not a speck of dust to be seen in the whole flat.", "청년은 생각했다. 셋집 전체에 먼지 한 점 보이지 않았다."]], [["“It’s in the houses of spiteful old widows that one finds such cleanliness,”", "'이렇게 깔끔한 건 심술궂은 늙은 과부들 집에서나 볼 수 있는 거지.'"], ["Raskolnikov thought again, and he stole a curious glance at the cotton curtain over the door leading into another tiny room, in which stood the old woman’s bed and chest of drawers and into which he had never looked before.", "라스콜니코프는 다시 생각하며, 다른 작은 방으로 통하는 문에 드리운 무명 커튼을 호기심 어린 눈길로 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다. 그 방에는 노파의 침대와 서랍장이 놓여 있었는데, 그는 지금껏 한 번도 그 안을 들여다본 적이 없었다."], ["These two rooms made up the whole flat.", "이 두 개의 방이 셋집 전부였다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 원하시나?\""], ["the old woman said severely, coming into the room and, as before, standing in front of him so as to look him straight in the face.", "노파는 방으로 들어와 아까처럼 그의 정면에 서서 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 엄하게 말했다."]], [["“I’ve brought something to pawn here,”", "\"여기에 저당 잡힐 물건을 가져왔습니다.\""], ["and he drew out of his pocket an old-fashioned flat silver watch, on the back of which was engraved a globe; the chain was of steel.", "그러고는 주머니에서 구식의 납작한 은시계를 꺼냈는데, 뒷면에는 지구본이 새겨져 있었고, 줄은 강철이었다."]], [["“But the time is up for your last pledge.", "\"하지만 지난번 저당물 기한이 다 됐는데."], ["The month was up the day before yesterday.”", "그저께가 한 달째였어.\""]], [["“I will bring you the interest for another month; wait a little.”", "\"한 달 치 이자를 더 가져다드리겠습니다, 조금만 기다려 주세요.\""]], [["“But that’s for me to do as I please, my good sir, to wait or to sell your pledge at once.”", "\"기다리든 댁의 저당물을 당장 팔아 버리든, 그건 내 맘대로 할 일이지요, 선생.\""]], [["“How much will you give me for the watch, Alyona Ivanovna?”", "\"이 시계는 얼마나 쳐주시겠습니까, 알료나 이바노브나?\""]], [["“You come with such trifles, my good sir, it’s scarcely worth anything.", "\"이런 하찮은 걸 가져오니, 선생, 거의 값어치가 없구려."], ["I gave you two roubles last time for your ring and one could buy it quite new at a jeweler’s for a rouble and a half.”", "지난번엔 반지에 이 루블이나 쳐줬지만, 그건 보석상에서 한 루블 반이면 새것으로 살 수 있는 거였어요.\""]], [["“Give me four roubles for it, I shall redeem it, it was my father’s.", "\"이걸로 사 루블만 주십시오, 다시 찾으러 오겠습니다, 아버지 유품이거든요."], ["I shall be getting some money soon.”", "곧 돈이 좀 들어올 겁니다.\""]], [["“A rouble and a half, and interest in advance, if you like!”", "\"한 루블 반에, 이자는 선불이오, 그게 싫으면 그만두시고!\""]], [["“A rouble and a half!”", "\"한 루블 반이라니요!\""], ["cried the young man.", "청년이 외쳤다."]], [["“Please yourself”--and the old woman handed him back the watch.", "\"맘대로 하시구려.\" 그러고는 노파가 시계를 그에게 도로 건넸다."], ["The young man took it, and was so angry that he was on the point of going away; but checked himself at once, remembering that there was nowhere else he could go, and that he had had another object also in coming.", "청년은 시계를 받아 들었는데, 어찌나 화가 치미는지 그대로 나가 버릴 뻔했다. 그러나 곧 마음을 다잡았으니, 달리 갈 데도 없거니와, 여기 온 데에는 또 다른 목적이 있었음을 떠올렸기 때문이다."]], [["“Hand it over,”", "\"이리 주십시오.\""], ["he said roughly.", "그가 퉁명스레 말했다."]], [["The old woman fumbled in her pocket for her keys, and disappeared behind the curtain into the other room.", "노파는 주머니를 뒤적여 열쇠를 찾더니, 커튼 뒤 다른 방으로 사라졌다."], ["The young man, left standing alone in the middle of the room, listened inquisitively, thinking.", "방 한가운데 홀로 남겨진 청년은 생각에 잠긴 채 귀를 기울이며 호기심 어린 표정으로 들었다."], ["He could hear her unlocking the chest of drawers.", "그는 그녀가 서랍장을 여는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."]], [["“It must be the top drawer,”", "'분명 맨 위 서랍이겠지.'"], ["he reflected.", "그는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["“So she carries the keys in a pocket on the right.", "'그러니까 열쇠를 오른쪽 주머니에 넣고 다니는군."], ["All in one bunch on a steel ring....", "강철 고리에 한꺼번에 꿰어서……."], ["And there’s one key there, three times as big as all the others, with deep notches; that can’t be the key of the chest of drawers... then there must be some other chest or strong-box...", "그런데 거기 다른 열쇠들보다 세 배는 큰 게 하나 있어, 홈이 깊게 팬……, 그건 서랍장 열쇠일 리가 없는데……, 그렇다면 다른 궤짝이나 금고가 있다는 거지……, 알아 둘 만한 일이야."], ["that’s worth knowing. Strong-boxes always have keys like that...", "금고에는 늘 그런 열쇠가 달려 있으니까……."], ["but how degrading it all is.”", "하지만 이 모든 게 얼마나 치사한 짓인지.'"]], [["The old woman came back.", "노파가 돌아왔다."]], [["“Here, sir: as we say ten copecks the rouble a month, so I must take fifteen copecks from a rouble and a half for the month in advance.", "\"여기요, 선생. 우리야 한 달에 한 루블당 십 코페이카로 치니까, 한 루블 반에서 한 달 선불로 십오 코페이카를 떼야겠구려."], ["But for the two roubles I lent you before, you owe me now twenty copecks on the same reckoning in advance.", "한데 전에 빌려준 이 루블에 대해서도, 같은 셈으로 선불 이십 코페이카를 빚진 셈이오."], ["That makes thirty-five copecks altogether.", "그러니 다 합쳐 삼십오 코페이카요."], ["So I must give you a rouble and fifteen copecks for the watch.", "그래서 시계 값으로는 한 루블 십오 코페이카를 줘야겠소."], ["Here it is.”", "자, 여기 있어요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["only a rouble and fifteen copecks now!”", "이제 와서 고작 한 루블 십오 코페이카라니!\""]], [["“Just so.”", "\"바로 그렇소.\""]], [["The young man did not dispute it and took the money.", "청년은 따지지 않고 돈을 받았다."], ["He looked at the old woman, and was in no hurry to get away, as though there was still something he wanted to say or to do, but he did not himself quite know what.", "그는 노파를 바라보았고, 떠날 생각도 않은 채 머뭇거렸으니, 마치 아직 무언가 하고 싶거나 말하고 싶은 것이 남은 듯했으나, 정작 그 자신도 그게 무엇인지 잘 알지 못했다."]], [["“I may be bringing you something else in a day or two, Alyona Ivanovna--a valuable thing--silver--a cigarette-box, as soon as I get it back from a friend...”", "\"하루 이틀 안에 다른 물건을 또 가져올지도 모릅니다, 알료나 이바노브나 — 값나가는 물건이지요 — 은으로 된 — 담뱃갑인데, 친구한테 돌려받는 대로…….\""], ["he broke off in confusion.", "그는 당황하여 말끝을 흐렸다."]], [["“Well, we will talk about it then, sir.”", "\"뭐, 그땐 그때 얘기하지요, 선생.\""]], [["“Good-bye--are you always at home alone, your sister is not here with you?”", "\"안녕히 계세요 — 늘 혼자 집에 계시나요, 동생분은 같이 안 계시고요?\""], ["He asked her as casually as possible as he went out into the passage.", "그는 통로로 나가면서 가능한 한 아무렇지 않은 듯 물었다."]], [["“What business is she of yours, my good sir?”", "\"그애가 댁이랑 무슨 상관이 있다고 그러시나, 선생?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing particular, I simply asked.", "\"아, 별것 아닙니다, 그냥 물어본 거예요."], ["You are too quick....", "너무 성급하시군요……."], ["Good-day, Alyona Ivanovna.”", "안녕히 계세요, 알료나 이바노브나.\""]], [["Raskolnikov went out in complete confusion.", "라스콜니코프는 완전히 당황한 채 밖으로 나왔다."], ["This confusion became more and more intense.", "이 당혹감은 점점 더 격렬해졌다."], ["As he went down the stairs, he even stopped short, two or three times, as though suddenly struck by some thought.", "계단을 내려오면서 그는 두세 번이나 우뚝 멈춰 서기까지 했는데, 마치 어떤 생각에 불현듯 부딪힌 것 같았다."], ["When he was in the street he cried out, “Oh, God, how loathsome it all is!", "거리에 나섰을 때 그는 외쳤다. \"오, 하느님, 이 모든 게 어찌나 역겨운지!"], ["and can I, can I possibly....", "그리고 내가, 정말 내가 그럴 수 있을까……."], ["No, it’s nonsense, it’s rubbish!”", "아니, 말도 안 돼, 헛소리야!\""], ["he added resolutely.", "그는 단호하게 덧붙였다."], ["“And how could such an atrocious thing come into my head?", "\"도대체 어떻게 이런 끔찍한 일이 내 머릿속에 떠올랐단 말인가?"], ["What filthy things my heart is capable of.", "내 마음이 이토록 추잡한 짓을 저지를 수 있다니."], ["Yes, filthy above all, disgusting, loathsome, loathsome!--and for a whole month I’ve been....”", "그래, 무엇보다 추잡하고, 역겹고, 혐오스럽고, 또 혐오스러워! — 그런데 꼬박 한 달이나 나는…….\""], ["But no words, no exclamations, could express his agitation.", "그러나 그 어떤 말로도, 그 어떤 외침으로도 그의 동요를 다 표현할 수는 없었다."], ["The feeling of intense repulsion, which had begun to oppress and torture his heart while he was on his way to the old woman, had by now reached such a pitch and had taken such a definite form that he did not know what to do with himself to escape from his wretchedness.", "노파에게 가는 길에 이미 그의 마음을 짓누르고 괴롭히기 시작했던 그 강렬한 혐오의 감정은, 이제 너무도 극에 달하고 너무도 뚜렷한 형태를 갖춘 나머지, 그는 이 비참함에서 벗어나려고 어찌해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["He walked along the pavement like a drunken man, regardless of the passers-by, and jostling against them, and only came to his senses when he was in the next street.", "그는 행인들도 아랑곳없이, 그들과 부딪치면서, 술 취한 사람처럼 보도를 걸었고, 옆 거리에 이르러서야 비로소 정신을 차렸다."], ["Looking round, he noticed that he was standing close to a tavern which was entered by steps leading from the pavement to the basement.", "둘러보니 그는 어느 선술집 바로 곁에 서 있었는데, 그곳은 보도에서 지하로 내려가는 계단을 통해 들어가게 되어 있었다."], ["At that instant two drunken men came out at the door, and abusing and supporting one another, they mounted the steps.", "바로 그 순간 술 취한 사내 둘이 문에서 나와, 서로 욕설을 퍼붓고 부축하며 계단을 올라왔다."], ["Without stopping to think, Raskolnikov went down the steps at once.", "라스콜니코프는 생각할 겨를도 없이 곧장 계단을 내려갔다."], ["Till that moment he had never been into a tavern, but now he felt giddy and was tormented by a burning thirst.", "그 순간까지 그는 한 번도 선술집에 들어가 본 적이 없었지만, 지금은 어지럼증이 일고 타는 듯한 갈증에 시달리고 있었다."], ["He longed for a drink of cold beer, and attributed his sudden weakness to the want of food.", "그는 차가운 맥주가 간절했고, 자신의 갑작스러운 쇠약을 음식을 먹지 못한 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["He sat down at a sticky little table in a dark and dirty corner; ordered some beer, and eagerly drank off the first glassful.", "그는 어둡고 더러운 구석의 끈적끈적한 작은 탁자에 앉아, 맥주를 시키고는 첫 잔을 게걸스레 들이켰다."], ["At once he felt easier; and his thoughts became clear.", "곧 한결 편안해졌고, 생각도 또렷해졌다."]], [["“All that’s nonsense,”", "\"이 모든 게 다 헛소리야.\""], ["he said hopefully, “and there is nothing in it all to worry about!", "그는 희망에 차서 말했다. \"걱정할 거라곤 아무것도 없어!"], ["It’s simply physical derangement.", "그저 신체의 이상일 뿐이라고."], ["Just a glass of beer, a piece of dry bread--and in one moment the brain is stronger, the mind is clearer and the will is firm!", "맥주 한 잔, 마른 빵 한 조각이면 — 한순간에 머리가 단단해지고, 정신이 또렷해지고, 의지가 굳건해지는걸!"], ["Phew, how utterly petty it all is!”", "흥, 이 모든 게 얼마나 하찮은지!\""]], [["But in spite of this scornful reflection, he was by now looking cheerful as though he were suddenly set free from a terrible burden: and he gazed round in a friendly way at the people in the room.", "그러나 이런 경멸 어린 생각에도 불구하고, 그는 이제 마치 끔찍한 짐에서 갑자기 풀려난 듯 명랑한 표정이 되어, 방 안의 사람들을 다정한 눈길로 둘러보았다."], ["But even at that moment he had a dim foreboding that this happier frame of mind was also not normal.", "하지만 바로 그 순간에도 그는 이 한결 행복해진 기분 역시 정상이 아니라는 것을 어렴풋이 예감했다."]], [["There were few people at the time in the tavern.", "그때 선술집에는 사람이 별로 없었다."], ["Besides the two drunken men he had met on the steps, a group consisting of about five men and a girl with a concertina had gone out at the same time.", "계단에서 마주친 술 취한 두 사내 말고도, 사내 다섯쯤과 콘서티나를 든 처녀 하나로 이루어진 무리가 같은 때에 나가 버렸다."], ["Their departure left the room quiet and rather empty.", "그들이 떠나자 방 안은 조용하고 꽤 텅 비었다."], ["The persons still in the tavern were a man who appeared to be an artisan, drunk, but not extremely so, sitting before a pot of beer, and his companion, a huge, stout man with a grey beard, in a short full-skirted coat.", "아직 선술집에 남아 있는 이들은, 직공으로 보이는 사내 하나로, 맥주 단지를 앞에 두고 술에 취해 있었지만 그리 심하지는 않았고, 또 그 일행인, 회색 수염을 기르고 짧고 품이 넉넉한 외투를 걸친 거대하고 뚱뚱한 사내가 있었다."], ["He was very drunk: and had dropped asleep on the bench; every now and then, he began as though in his sleep, cracking his fingers, with his arms wide apart and the upper part of his body bounding about on the bench, while he hummed some meaningless refrain, trying to recall some such lines as these:", "그는 몹시 취해서 긴 의자에 쓰러져 잠들어 있었는데, 이따금 잠결인 듯 화들짝 놀라며 손가락을 딱딱 튕기고, 두 팔을 활짝 벌린 채 상체를 의자 위에서 들썩거리면서, 무슨 이런 시구 같은 것을 떠올리려 애쓰며 의미도 없는 후렴을 흥얼거리곤 했다."]], [["“His wife a year he fondly loved His wife a--a year he--fondly loved.”", "\"그는 일 년 동안 아내를 애틋이 사랑했네 그의 아내를 — 일 년 동안 그는 — 애틋이 사랑했네.\""]], [["Or suddenly waking up again:", "또는 갑자기 다시 잠에서 깨어나서는,"]], [["“Walking along the crowded row He met the one he used to know.”", "\"북적이는 거리를 걸어가다가 그는 옛정 든 그 여인을 만났다네.\""]], [["But no one shared his enjoyment: his silent companion looked with positive hostility and mistrust at all these manifestations.", "그러나 누구도 그의 흥겨움에 함께하지 않았다. 말없는 그의 일행은 이 모든 행태를 노골적인 적의와 불신의 눈으로 바라보고 있었다."], ["There was another man in the room who looked somewhat like a retired government clerk.", "방 안에는 또 다른 사내 하나가 있었는데, 어쩐지 퇴직한 관청 서기처럼 보였다."], ["He was sitting apart, now and then sipping from his pot and looking round at the company.", "그는 한쪽에 떨어져 앉아 이따금 단지째 홀짝이며 좌중을 둘러보고 있었다."], ["He, too, appeared to be in some agitation.", "그 역시 어떤 동요에 사로잡혀 있는 듯했다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["Raskolnikov was not used to crowds, and, as we said before, he avoided society of every sort, more especially of late.", "라스콜니코프는 사람들 틈에 끼는 일에 익숙하지 않았고, 앞서 말했듯이 모든 종류의 사교를 피해 왔는데, 특히 요즈음 들어 더욱 그러했다."], ["But now all at once he felt a desire to be with other people.", "그런데 지금 그는 갑자기 다른 사람들과 함께 있고 싶다는 욕망을 느꼈다."], ["Something new seemed to be taking place within him, and with it he felt a sort of thirst for company.", "그의 내면에서 무언가 새로운 것이 일어나고 있는 듯했고, 그와 더불어 일종의 동석에 대한 갈증 같은 것이 느껴졌다."], ["He was so weary after a whole month of concentrated wretchedness and gloomy excitement that he longed to rest, if only for a moment, in some other world, whatever it might be; and, in spite of the filthiness of the surroundings, he was glad now to stay in the tavern.", "꼬박 한 달 동안 응축된 비참함과 음울한 흥분에 너무도 지쳐 버린 나머지, 그는 단 한순간만이라도 어떤 다른 세계에서 — 그것이 어떤 세계든 — 쉬고 싶었다. 그래서 주변이 더럽기 그지없는데도 그는 지금 이 선술집에 머물게 된 것이 반가웠다."]], [["The master of the establishment was in another room, but he frequently came down some steps into the main room, his jaunty, tarred boots with red turn-over tops coming into view each time before the rest of his person.", "가게 주인은 다른 방에 있었지만, 자주 계단 몇 개를 내려와 큰 방으로 들어오곤 했는데, 그럴 때면 빨간 접단이 달린, 콜타르를 칠한 멋들어진 부츠가 늘 그의 몸뚱이보다 먼저 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He wore a full coat and a horribly greasy black satin waistcoat, with no cravat, and his whole face seemed smeared with oil like an iron lock.", "그는 긴 외투에 끔찍하게 기름때가 낀 검은 새틴 조끼를 걸쳤는데, 넥타이는 매지 않았고, 그의 얼굴 전체가 쇠 자물쇠처럼 기름에 절어 번들거리는 듯했다."], ["At the counter stood a boy of about fourteen, and there was another boy somewhat younger who handed whatever was wanted.", "계산대에는 열네 살쯤 된 소년이 서 있었고, 좀 더 어린 또 다른 소년이 손님이 원하는 것이면 무엇이든 가져다주었다."], ["On the counter lay some sliced cucumber, some pieces of dried black bread, and some fish, chopped up small, all smelling very bad.", "계산대 위에는 얇게 썬 오이 몇 조각, 말린 흑빵 몇 쪽, 그리고 잘게 다진 생선이 놓여 있었는데, 모두 지독한 악취를 풍겼다."], ["It was insufferably close, and so heavy with the fumes of spirits that five minutes in such an atmosphere might well make a man drunk.", "공기는 견딜 수 없이 답답했고, 독한 술 냄새가 어찌나 짙게 배어 있는지 이런 공기 속에서는 오 분만 있어도 능히 사람을 취하게 만들 만했다."]], [["There are chance meetings with strangers that interest us from the first moment, before a word is spoken.", "낯선 사람과의 우연한 만남 가운데는, 한마디 말이 오가기도 전에 첫 순간부터 우리의 흥미를 끄는 것들이 있다."], ["Such was the impression made on Raskolnikov by the person sitting a little distance from him, who looked like a retired clerk.", "라스콜니코프에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 앉아 있던, 퇴직한 관리처럼 보이는 사람이 바로 그런 인상을 주었다."], ["The young man often recalled this impression afterwards, and even ascribed it to presentiment.", "청년은 나중에도 이 인상을 자주 떠올렸고, 심지어 그것을 일종의 예감 탓으로 돌리기까지 했다."], ["He looked repeatedly at the clerk, partly no doubt because the latter was staring persistently at him, obviously anxious to enter into conversation.", "그는 그 관리를 거듭 쳐다보았는데, 의심할 바 없이 그 관리 쪽에서도 그를 집요하게 빤히 바라보면서 분명 대화를 트고 싶어 안달하는 듯했기 때문이기도 했다."], ["At the other persons in the room, including the tavern-keeper, the clerk looked as though he were used to their company, and weary of it, showing a shade of condescending contempt for them as persons of station and culture inferior to his own, with whom it would be useless for him to converse.", "술집 주인을 포함해 방 안의 다른 사람들을 대하는 그 관리의 태도는, 마치 그들과 어울리는 데 익숙하면서도 이제는 진력이 난 듯했고, 자기보다 신분과 교양이 낮아 함께 이야기를 나눠 봐야 소용없다고 여기는 사람들에 대한 약간의 너그러운 경멸 같은 것을 내비치고 있었다."], ["He was a man over fifty, bald and grizzled, of medium height, and stoutly built.", "그는 쉰이 넘은 사내로, 머리가 벗어졌고 희끗희끗했으며, 중간 키에 다부진 몸집이었다."], ["His face, bloated from continual drinking, was of a yellow, even greenish, tinge, with swollen eyelids out of which keen reddish eyes gleamed like little chinks.", "끊임없는 음주로 부어오른 그의 얼굴은 누렇다 못해 푸르스름한 빛마저 띠었고, 부은 눈꺼풀 사이로 작은 틈처럼 가느다란 두 눈이 날카로운 불그스름한 빛을 발했다."], ["But there was something very strange in him; there was a light in his eyes as though of intense feeling--perhaps there were even thought and intelligence, but at the same time there was a gleam of something like madness.", "그러나 그에게는 무언가 무척 기이한 데가 있었다. 그의 두 눈에는 격렬한 감정에서 비롯된 듯한 빛이 어려 있었으며 — 어쩌면 사색과 지성마저 깃들어 있는 듯했으나 — 동시에 광기 비슷한 것이 번뜩였다."], ["He was wearing an old and hopelessly ragged black dress coat, with all its buttons missing except one, and that one he had buttoned, evidently clinging to this last trace of respectability.", "그는 낡고 손쓸 수 없을 만큼 너덜너덜한 검은 연미복을 걸치고 있었는데, 단추는 단 하나만 남고 모조리 떨어져 나갔으며, 그는 그 단 하나의 단추를 채우고 있었으니, 분명 이 마지막 체면의 흔적에 매달리고 있는 셈이었다."], ["A crumpled shirt front, covered with spots and stains, protruded from his canvas waistcoat.", "무명 조끼 밖으로는 얼룩투성이의 구겨진 셔츠 앞섶이 비어져 나와 있었다."], ["Like a clerk, he wore no beard, nor moustache, but had been so long unshaven that his chin looked like a stiff greyish brush.", "관리답게 그는 턱수염도 콧수염도 기르지 않았으나, 너무 오래 면도를 하지 않은 탓에 그의 턱은 뻣뻣한 잿빛 솔처럼 보였다."], ["And there was something respectable and like an official about his manner too.", "그리고 그의 태도에도 무언가 점잖고 관리다운 데가 있었다."], ["But he was restless; he ruffled up his hair and from time to time let his head drop into his hands dejectedly resting his ragged elbows on the stained and sticky table.", "그러나 그는 안절부절못했다. 머리칼을 헝클어뜨리는가 하면, 이따금 너덜너덜한 팔꿈치를 얼룩지고 끈적이는 탁자에 괴고는 절망한 듯 두 손에 머리를 파묻기도 했다."], ["At last he looked straight at Raskolnikov, and said loudly and resolutely:", "마침내 그는 라스콜니코프를 똑바로 바라보더니, 큰 소리로 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“May I venture, honoured sir, to engage you in polite conversation?", "\"실례지만 선생, 외람되게도 정중한 담소를 좀 청해도 되겠습니까?"], ["Forasmuch as, though your exterior would not command respect, my experience admonishes me that you are a man of education and not accustomed to drinking.", "비록 선생의 행색은 존경을 자아낼 만한 것은 못 되나, 제 경험으로 미루어 선생이 교양 있는 분이며 음주에는 익숙하지 않은 분임을 알겠기 때문입니다."], ["I have always respected education when in conjunction with genuine sentiments, and I am besides a titular counsellor in rank.", "저는 진실한 감정과 어우러진 교양이라면 늘 존경해 왔거니와, 게다가 직급으로 말하자면 9등 문관입니다."], ["Marmeladov--such is my name; titular counsellor.", "마르멜라도프 — 제 이름이 그렇습니다, 9등 문관이지요."], ["I make bold to inquire--have you been in the service?”", "외람되나 여쭙겠는데 — 선생은 관청에 몸담은 적이 있으신지요?\""]], [["“No, I am studying,”", "\"아뇨, 저는 공부하는 학생입니다.\""], ["answered the young man, somewhat surprised at the grandiloquent style of the speaker and also at being so directly addressed.", "청년은 그 거창한 말투에, 또 자기에게 그토록 대놓고 말을 거는 것에 다소 놀라며 대답했다."], ["In spite of the momentary desire he had just been feeling for company of any sort, on being actually spoken to he felt immediately his habitual irritable and uneasy aversion for any stranger who approached or attempted to approach him.", "방금 전까지만 해도 어떤 동석이든 마다하지 않겠다는 순간적인 욕망을 느꼈건만, 막상 누군가가 실제로 말을 걸어오자 그는 자기에게 다가오거나 다가오려는 낯선 이라면 누구에게나 품게 마련인, 늘 그렇듯 신경질적이고 거북한 혐오감을 곧바로 느꼈다."]], [["“A student then, or formerly a student,”", "\"그렇다면 학생이거나, 아니면 예전에 학생이었군요!\""], ["cried the clerk.", "관리가 외쳤다."], ["“Just what I thought!", "\"내 그럴 줄 알았지요!"], ["I’m a man of experience, immense experience, sir,”", "나는 경험이 많은 사람이올시다, 어마어마한 경험을요, 선생.\""], ["and he tapped his forehead with his fingers in self-approval.", "그러고는 자화자찬하듯 손가락으로 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드렸다."], ["“You’ve been a student or have attended some learned institution!...", "\"선생은 학생이었거나, 아니면 무슨 학문 기관에 다닌 적이 있으시군요!……"], ["But allow me....”", "한데 잠깐만 실례를…….\""], ["He got up, staggered, took up his jug and glass, and sat down beside the young man, facing him a little sideways.", "그는 일어나서 비틀거리며 자기 술병과 잔을 집어 들고는 청년 곁으로 와, 비스듬히 마주 보는 자세로 앉았다."], ["He was drunk, but spoke fluently and boldly, only occasionally losing the thread of his sentences and drawling his words.", "그는 취해 있었지만 말은 막힘없이 대담하게 이어 갔고, 다만 이따금 문장의 갈피를 잃거나 말을 길게 끄는 정도였다."], ["He pounced upon Raskolnikov as greedily as though he too had not spoken to a soul for a month.", "그는 마치 자기 역시 한 달 동안 그 누구와도 말 한마디 나누지 못했다는 듯, 게걸스레 라스콜니코프에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Honoured sir,”", "\"존경하는 선생,\""], ["he began almost with solemnity, “poverty is not a vice, that’s a true saying.", "그는 거의 엄숙하다 할 만큼 입을 열었다. \"가난은 죄가 아니다, 이건 참된 격언이지요."], ["Yet I know too that drunkenness is not a virtue, and that that’s even truer.", "한데 술주정 또한 미덕이 아니라는 것도 나는 잘 알고, 그건 더더욱 참되지요."], ["But beggary, honoured sir, beggary is a vice.", "하지만 거지 신세는, 존경하는 선생, 거지 신세는 죄악이올시다."], ["In poverty you may still retain your innate nobility of soul, but in beggary--never--no one.", "가난 속에서도 사람은 타고난 영혼의 고귀함을 그래도 지킬 수 있지만, 거지 신세에서는 — 결코 — 그 누구도 못 합니다."], ["For beggary a man is not chased out of human society with a stick, he is swept out with a broom, so as to make it as humiliating as possible; and quite right, too, forasmuch as in beggary I am ready to be the first to humiliate myself.", "거지 신세가 되면 사람은 몽둥이로 인간 사회에서 쫓겨나는 게 아니라, 빗자루로 쓸려 나가지요, 그래야 최대한 모욕적이게 되니까요. 그리고 그게 또 지당하기도 한 것이, 거지 신세가 되면 나야말로 누구보다 먼저 스스로를 모욕할 각오가 되어 있거든요."], ["Hence the pot-house!", "그래서 이 선술집이올시다!"], ["Honoured sir, a month ago Mr. Lebeziatnikov gave my wife a beating, and my wife is a very different matter from me!", "존경하는 선생, 한 달 전에 레베지아트니코프 씨가 내 아내를 두들겨 팼는데, 내 아내는 나하고는 영 딴판인 사람이란 말입니다!"], ["Do you understand?", "아시겠습니까?"], ["Allow me to ask you another question out of simple curiosity: have you ever spent a night on a hay barge, on the Neva?”", "단순한 호기심에서 한 가지 더 여쭙겠는데, 선생은 네바 강의 건초 거룻배에서 하룻밤을 지새워 본 적이 있으십니까?\""]], [["“No, I have not happened to,”", "\"아뇨, 그런 적은 없습니다.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."], ["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이신지요?\""]], [["“Well, I’ve just come from one and it’s the fifth night I’ve slept so....”", "\"글쎄요, 나는 방금 거기서 오는 길이고, 그렇게 잔 게 벌써 닷새째올시다…….\""], ["He filled his glass, emptied it and paused.", "그는 잔을 채워 단숨에 비우고는 잠시 입을 다물었다."], ["Bits of hay were in fact clinging to his clothes and sticking to his hair.", "실제로 그의 옷에는 건초 부스러기가 들러붙어 있었고, 머리칼에도 엉겨 있었다."], ["It seemed quite probable that he had not undressed or washed for the last five days.", "지난 닷새 동안 옷을 벗거나 몸을 씻은 적이 없는 게 분명해 보였다."], ["His hands, particularly, were filthy.", "특히 그의 두 손은 더러웠다."], ["They were fat and red, with black nails.", "살이 찌고 벌건 손에 손톱은 새카맸다."]], [["His conversation seemed to excite a general though languid interest.", "그의 이야기는 전반적으로 미적지근하나마 사람들의 관심을 자아내는 듯했다."], ["The boys at the counter fell to sniggering.", "계산대의 소년들이 킥킥거리기 시작했다."], ["The innkeeper came down from the upper room, apparently on purpose to listen to the “funny fellow” and sat down at a little distance, yawning lazily, but with dignity.", "술집 주인은 이 \"익살꾼\"의 말을 들으려는 게 분명한 양 위층에서 내려와, 조금 떨어진 곳에 앉아서는 게으르게, 그러나 점잖게 하품을 했다."], ["Evidently Marmeladov was a familiar figure here, and he had most likely acquired his weakness for high-flown speeches from the habit of frequently entering into conversation with strangers of all sorts in the tavern.", "마르멜라도프는 이곳에서 낯익은 인물인 게 분명했고, 그의 이 거창한 연설벽도 십중팔구 선술집에서 온갖 부류의 낯선 이들과 자주 대화를 트는 버릇에서 비롯된 것이리라."], ["This habit develops into a necessity in some drunkards, and especially in those who are looked after sharply and kept in order at home.", "이런 버릇은 어떤 주정뱅이들에게는, 특히 집에서 매섭게 감시당하며 단속받는 자들에게는 하나의 필요로 자라난다."], ["Hence in the company of other drinkers they try to justify themselves and even if possible obtain consideration.", "그래서 다른 술꾼들과 어울릴 때면 그들은 자기 자신을 변호하려 애쓰고, 가능하다면 인정마저 얻어 내려 드는 것이다."]], [["“Funny fellow!”", "\"익살꾼 같으니!\""], ["pronounced the innkeeper.", "술집 주인이 한마디 했다."], ["“And why don’t you work, why aren’t you at your duty, if you are in the service?”", "\"한데 자넨 왜 일을 안 하나, 관청에 몸담았다면서 왜 근무처에 안 가고 이러나?\""]], [["“Why am I not at my duty, honoured sir,”", "\"내가 왜 근무처에 안 가느냐고요, 존경하는 선생,\""], ["Marmeladov went on, addressing himself exclusively to Raskolnikov, as though it had been he who put that question to him.", "마르멜라도프는 마치 그 질문을 던진 게 라스콜니코프이기라도 한 양, 오로지 그에게만 대고 말을 이어 갔다."], ["“Why am I not at my duty?", "\"내가 왜 근무처에 안 가느냐?"], ["Does not my heart ache to think what a useless worm I am?", "내가 얼마나 쓸모없는 벌레인지를 생각하면 내 가슴이 미어지지 않겠습니까?"], ["A month ago when Mr. Lebeziatnikov beat my wife with his own hands, and I lay drunk, didn’t I suffer?", "한 달 전에 레베지아트니코프 씨가 제 손으로 내 아내를 두들겨 팰 때, 내가 곤드레만드레 취해 누워 있을 때, 내가 괴로워하지 않았겠습니까?"], ["Excuse me, young man, has it ever happened to you...", "실례지만 젊은이, 자네는 혹시 이런 적이 있었나……"], ["hm...", "흠……"], ["well, to petition hopelessly for a loan?”", "그러니까, 가망 없이 돈을 좀 꿔 달라고 애걸해 본 적이?\""]], [["“Yes, it has.", "\"네, 있습니다."], ["But what do you mean by hopelessly?”", "한데 가망 없이라는 게 무슨 뜻이신지요?\""]], [["“Hopelessly in the fullest sense, when you know beforehand that you will get nothing by it.", "\"가망 없이라 함은 가장 온전한 의미에서, 그래 봐야 아무것도 못 얻으리라는 걸 미리부터 아는 경우올시다."], ["You know, for instance, beforehand with positive certainty that this man, this most reputable and exemplary citizen, will on no consideration give you money; and indeed I ask you why should he?", "가령 이 사람, 이 더없이 평판 좋고 모범적인 시민이 무슨 일이 있어도 자네에게 돈을 내주지 않으리라는 걸 미리부터 분명하게 확신하는 거지요. 하기야 내가 자네에게 묻겠는데, 그가 왜 주겠습니까?"], ["For he knows of course that I shan’t pay it back.", "내가 갚지 않으리란 걸 그도 물론 알고 있으니 말입니다."], ["From compassion?", "동정심에서요?"], ["But Mr. Lebeziatnikov who keeps up with modern ideas explained the other day that compassion is forbidden nowadays by science itself, and that that’s what is done now in England, where there is political economy.", "하지만 요즘 사상에 밝은 레베지아트니코프 씨가 일전에 설명해 주기를, 동정심이란 건 요즘 과학 그 자체에서 금하는 바이며, 정치 경제학이 있는 영국에서는 이미 그렇게들 한다고 하더이다."], ["Why, I ask you, should he give it to me?", "그러니 내가 자네에게 묻겠는데, 그가 무엇 때문에 내게 주겠습니까?"], ["And yet though I know beforehand that he won’t, I set off to him and...”", "그런데도 그가 주지 않으리란 걸 미리 알면서도, 나는 그에게 가서…….\""]], [["“Why do you go?”", "\"왜 가시는 겁니까?\""], ["put in Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Well, when one has no one, nowhere else one can go!", "\"글쎄, 아무도 없고, 달리 갈 데가 한 군데도 없을 땐 말입니다!"], ["For every man must have somewhere to go.", "사람이란 모름지기 어딘가 갈 데가 있어야 하는 법이니까요."], ["Since there are times when one absolutely must go somewhere!", "어쨌든 반드시 어딘가로 가야만 하는 때가 있는 법이거든요!"], ["When my own daughter first went out with a yellow ticket, then I had to go...", "내 친딸이 처음으로 노란 딱지를 들고 나갔을 때, 그때 나도 가야만 했지요……"], ["(for my daughter has a yellow passport),”", "(내 딸은 노란 신분증을 갖고 있으니까요).\""], ["he added in parenthesis, looking with a certain uneasiness at the young man.", "그가 청년을 다소 불안하게 바라보며 괄호를 치듯 덧붙였다."], ["“No matter, sir, no matter!”", "\"괜찮습니다, 선생, 괜찮아요!\""], ["he went on hurriedly and with apparent composure when both the boys at the counter guffawed and even the innkeeper smiled--“No matter, I am not confounded by the wagging of their heads; for everyone knows everything about it already, and all that is secret is made open.", "계산대의 두 소년이 와락 폭소를 터뜨리고 술집 주인마저 빙긋 웃자, 그는 짐짓 태연한 척 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"괜찮아요, 저들이 고개를 절레절레 흔든다 해도 나는 당황하지 않습니다."], ["And I accept it all, not with contempt, but with humility.", "다들 이미 그 일에 대해 죄다 알고 있고, 비밀이란 비밀은 죄다 까발려졌으니까요. 나는 그 모든 걸 받아들입니다, 경멸로써가 아니라 겸허함으로써요."], ["So be it!", "될 대로 되라지!"], ["So be it!", "될 대로 되라지!"], ["‘Behold the man!’ Excuse me, young man, can you....", "'이 사람을 보라!' 실례지만 젊은이, 자네는……"], ["No, to put it more strongly and more distinctly; not _can_ you but _dare_ you, looking upon me, assert that I am not a pig?”", "아니, 좀 더 강하고 좀 더 또렷하게 말하지요. 자네가 할 수 있느냐가 아니라, 감히 나를 보면서 내가 돼지가 아니라고 단언할 수 있겠나?\""]], [["The young man did not answer a word.", "청년은 한마디도 대꾸하지 않았다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"뭐,\""], ["the orator began again stolidly and with even increased dignity, after waiting for the laughter in the room to subside.", "연설가는 방 안의 웃음이 잦아들기를 기다렸다가, 무덤덤하게, 한층 더 늘어난 위엄을 갖추고 다시 입을 열었다."], ["“Well, so be it, I am a pig, but she is a lady!", "\"그래, 좋소, 나는 돼지요, 하지만 그녀는 귀부인이오!"], ["I have the semblance of a beast, but Katerina Ivanovna, my spouse, is a person of education and an officer’s daughter.", "나는 짐승의 형상을 하고 있으나, 내 아내 카테리나 이바노브나는 교양 있는 사람이며 장교의 딸이올시다."], ["Granted, granted, I am a scoundrel, but she is a woman of a noble heart, full of sentiments, refined by education.", "좋소, 그래요, 나는 비열한이오, 하지만 그녀는 고결한 마음을 지닌 여인이며, 갖가지 감정으로 가득 차 있고, 교양으로 다듬어진 사람이지요."], ["And yet...", "그런데도……"], ["oh, if only she felt for me!", "오, 그녀가 나를 가엾게만 여겨 준다면!"], ["Honoured sir, honoured sir, you know every man ought to have at least one place where people feel for him!", "존경하는 선생, 존경하는 선생, 사람이란 적어도 누군가가 자기를 가엾게 여겨 주는 곳이 한 군데는 있어야 하는 법 아닙니까!"], ["But Katerina Ivanovna, though she is magnanimous, she is unjust....", "한데 카테리나 이바노브나는 도량은 넓은 사람이지만, 공정하지가 못해서요……"], ["And yet, although I realise that when she pulls my hair she only does it out of pity--for I repeat without being ashamed, she pulls my hair, young man,”", "게다가, 내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯는 것도 실은 가여워서 그러는 줄 알면서도 — 다시 말하지만 부끄러워하지 않고 말하건대, 그녀는 내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯거든요, 젊은이,\""], ["he declared with redoubled dignity, hearing the sniggering again--“but, my God, if she would but once....", "그는 다시 킥킥대는 소리를 듣고는 갑절로 위엄을 갖추며 단언했다. \"하지만, 맙소사, 그녀가 단 한 번만이라도……"], ["But no, no!", "아니, 아니!"], ["It’s all in vain and it’s no use talking!", "다 부질없는 짓이고, 말해 봐야 소용없어요!"], ["No use talking!", "말해 봐야 소용없다고요!"], ["For more than once, my wish did come true and more than once she has felt for me but...", "한두 번이 아니라 여러 번 내 소원이 정말로 이루어져 그녀가 나를 가엾게 여긴 적도 있지만……"], ["such is my fate and I am a beast by nature!”", "이게 내 운명이고, 나는 천성이 짐승인 게지요!\""]], [["“Rather!”", "\"그럴 만도 하지!\""], ["assented the innkeeper yawning.", "술집 주인이 하품을 하며 맞장구쳤다."], ["Marmeladov struck his fist resolutely on the table.", "마르멜라도프는 주먹으로 탁자를 단호히 내리쳤다."]], [["“Such is my fate!", "\"이게 내 운명이올시다!"], ["Do you know, sir, do you know, I have sold her very stockings for drink?", "아시겠습니까, 선생, 아시겠어요, 나는 술 마시려고 그녀의 양말까지 팔아 치웠습니다!"], ["Not her shoes--that would be more or less in the order of things, but her stockings, her stockings I have sold for drink!", "신발이 아니라 — 그거야 그래도 어느 정도 순리에 맞는 일이겠지만 — 양말을, 그녀의 양말을 술값으로 팔아 치웠다 이 말입니다!"], ["Her mohair shawl I sold for drink, a present to her long ago, her own property, not mine; and we live in a cold room and she caught cold this winter and has begun coughing and spitting blood too.", "그녀의 모헤어 숄도 술값으로 팔았지요, 오래전에 누가 선물한 것이고, 내 것이 아니라 그녀 소유였는데도 말입니다. 우리는 추운 방에서 사는데, 그녀는 이번 겨울에 감기가 들어 기침을 하기 시작하더니 이젠 피까지 토하기 시작했어요."], ["We have three little children and Katerina Ivanovna is at work from morning till night; she is scrubbing and cleaning and washing the children, for she’s been used to cleanliness from a child.", "우리에겐 어린 자식이 셋 있는데, 카테리나 이바노브나는 아침부터 밤까지 일을 합니다. 닦고 청소하고 아이들을 씻기지요, 어릴 적부터 청결이 몸에 밴 사람이라서요."], ["But her chest is weak and she has a tendency to consumption and I feel it!", "한데 그녀는 가슴이 약하고 폐병에 걸리기 쉬운 체질이라, 나도 그걸 느낍니다!"], ["Do you suppose I don’t feel it?", "내가 그걸 못 느낄 것 같습니까?"], ["And the more I drink the more I feel it.", "술을 마시면 마실수록 나는 그걸 더 절절히 느낍니다."], ["That’s why I drink too.", "그래서 술을 마시는 것이기도 해요."], ["I try to find sympathy and feeling in drink....", "술 속에서 동정과 연민을 찾으려는 거지요……"], ["I drink so that I may suffer twice as much!”", "두 배로 더 괴로워하려고 마시는 것이올시다!\""], ["And as though in despair he laid his head down on the table.", "그러고는 절망한 듯 탁자에 머리를 떨궜다."]], [["“Young man,”", "\"젊은이,\""], ["he went on, raising his head again, “in your face I seem to read some trouble of mind.", "그가 다시 고개를 들며 말을 이었다. \"자네 얼굴에서 무언가 마음의 괴로움이 읽히는 것 같소."], ["When you came in I read it, and that was why I addressed you at once.", "자네가 들어왔을 때 그걸 읽었고, 그래서 곧바로 자네에게 말을 건 것이올시다."], ["For in unfolding to you the story of my life, I do not wish to make myself a laughing-stock before these idle listeners, who indeed know all about it already, but I am looking for a man of feeling and education.", "내가 자네에게 내 인생 이야기를 풀어놓는 것은, 사실 다 알고 있는 저 빈둥거리는 구경꾼들 앞에서 나 자신을 웃음거리로 만들려는 게 아니라, 감정과 교양을 갖춘 사람을 찾고 있기 때문이지요."], ["Know then that my wife was educated in a high-class school for the daughters of noblemen, and on leaving she danced the shawl dance before the governor and other personages for which she was presented with a gold medal and a certificate of merit.", "그러니 알아 두시오, 내 아내는 귀족 자제들을 위한 명문 학교에서 교육받았고, 졸업할 때는 지사를 비롯한 여러 고관 앞에서 숄 춤을 추어 금메달과 표창장을 받았소."], ["The medal...", "메달은……"], ["well, the medal of course was sold--long ago, hm...", "뭐, 메달이야 물론 팔아 치웠지요, 오래전에, 흠……"], ["but the certificate of merit is in her trunk still and not long ago she showed it to our landlady.", "하지만 표창장은 아직도 그녀의 트렁크에 들어 있어서, 얼마 전에도 우리 집주인 여자에게 그걸 보여 주더이다."], ["And although she is most continually on bad terms with the landlady, yet she wanted to tell someone or other of her past honours and of the happy days that are gone.", "그녀는 집주인 여자와 노상 사이가 틀어져 있는데도, 누군가에게 자기의 지난 영예와 흘러가 버린 행복한 시절을 이야기하고 싶었던 거지요."], ["I don’t condemn her for it, I don’t blame her, for the one thing left her is recollection of the past, and all the rest is dust and ashes.", "나는 그걸 두고 그녀를 탓하지 않습니다, 나무라지 않아요, 그녀에게 남은 유일한 것이 지난날의 추억뿐이고 나머지는 죄다 티끌과 잿더미니까요."], ["Yes, yes, she is a lady of spirit, proud and determined.", "그래요, 그래, 그녀는 기개 있는 귀부인이고, 자존심 강하고 의지가 굳은 사람입니다."], ["She scrubs the floors herself and has nothing but black bread to eat, but won’t allow herself to be treated with disrespect.", "손수 방바닥을 닦고 흑빵밖에 먹을 게 없어도, 자신이 무례한 대접을 받는 것은 결코 용납하지 않지요."], ["That’s why she would not overlook Mr. Lebeziatnikov’s rudeness to her, and so when he gave her a beating for it, she took to her bed more from the hurt to her feelings than from the blows.", "그래서 레베지아트니코프 씨의 무례를 그냥 넘기지 못한 거고, 그가 그 일로 그녀를 두들겨 팼을 때 그녀는 매를 맞아서라기보다 자존심에 입은 상처 때문에 자리에 드러눕고 말았던 겁니다."], ["She was a widow when I married her, with three children, one smaller than the other.", "내가 그녀와 결혼할 때 그녀는 아이 셋 딸린 과부였는데, 하나같이 줄줄이 어렸지요."], ["She married her first husband, an infantry officer, for love, and ran away with him from her father’s house.", "그녀는 첫 남편인 보병 장교와 사랑에 빠져 결혼했고, 그 사람과 함께 친정집에서 도망쳤소."], ["She was exceedingly fond of her husband; but he gave way to cards, got into trouble and with that he died.", "남편을 끔찍이도 사랑했지만, 그는 도박에 빠져 곤경에 처했고 그러다 죽고 말았지요. 끝에 가서는 그녀를 두들겨 패곤 했답니다."], ["He used to beat her at the end: and although she paid him back, of which I have authentic documentary evidence, to this day she speaks of him with tears and she throws him up to me; and I am glad, I am glad that, though only in imagination, she should think of herself as having once been happy....", "그녀도 그에게 되갚아 주긴 했지만 — 나한테 그걸 입증할 진짜 문서 증거까지 있소만 — 그녀는 오늘날까지도 눈물을 흘리며 그를 이야기하고, 그를 들먹이며 나를 닦달하지요. 한데 나는 기쁩니다, 비록 상상 속에서나마 그녀가 자신이 한때 행복했었노라 여길 수 있다는 게 기뻐요……"], ["And she was left at his death with three children in a wild and remote district where I happened to be at the time; and she was left in such hopeless poverty that, although I have seen many ups and downs of all sort, I don’t feel equal to describing it even.", "그렇게 그녀는 남편이 죽고 나서 마침 그때 내가 머물던 어느 황량하고 외진 고장에 아이 셋을 데리고 남겨졌는데, 어찌나 가망 없는 가난에 빠져 있었던지, 나도 온갖 부침을 숱하게 겪어 봤지만 차마 그걸 묘사할 엄두조차 나지 않을 지경이올시다."], ["Her relations had all thrown her off.", "친척들은 죄다 그녀를 내쳤지요."], ["And she was proud, too, excessively proud....", "게다가 그녀는 자존심도 셌고, 지나치다 싶을 만큼 자존심이 강했소……"], ["And then, honoured sir, and then, I, being at the time a widower, with a daughter of fourteen left me by my first wife, offered her my hand, for I could not bear the sight of such suffering.", "그래서, 존경하는 선생, 그래서, 마침 그때 첫 아내가 남긴 열네 살 난 딸을 거느린 홀아비였던 내가 그녀에게 청혼을 했지요, 그런 고통을 차마 두고 볼 수가 없었으니까요."], ["You can judge the extremity of her calamities, that she, a woman of education and culture and distinguished family, should have consented to be my wife.", "교양 있고 명문가 출신인 그녀가 나 같은 사람의 아내가 되는 데 동의했다는 것만 봐도, 그녀가 처한 재앙이 얼마나 극에 달했는지 짐작이 가실 거요. 한데 그녀는 동의했습니다!"], ["But she did! Weeping and sobbing and wringing her hands, she married me!", "울며불며 두 손을 부르쥐고 흐느끼면서, 그녀는 나와 결혼했어요!"], ["For she had nowhere to turn!", "달리 갈 데가 한 군데도 없었으니까요!"], ["Do you understand, sir, do you understand what it means when you have absolutely nowhere to turn?", "아시겠습니까, 선생, 정말이지 갈 데가 한 군데도 없다는 게 무슨 뜻인지 아시겠어요?"], ["No, that you don’t understand yet....", "아니, 자네는 아직 그걸 모를 거요……"], ["And for a whole year, I performed my duties conscientiously and faithfully, and did not touch this”", "그리고 꼬박 일 년 동안 나는 양심껏 충실히 직무를 다했고, 이것에는\""], ["(he tapped the jug with his finger), “for I have feelings.", "(그가 손가락으로 술병을 톡톡 두드렸다) \"손도 대지 않았지요, 나도 감정이란 게 있는 사람이니까요."], ["But even so, I could not please her; and then I lost my place too, and that through no fault of mine but through changes in the office; and then I did touch it!...", "한데 그렇게 했는데도 그녀를 만족시킬 수가 없었고, 그러다 일자리마저 잃었는데, 그게 내 잘못이 아니라 관청의 인사 개편 때문이었습니다."], ["It will be a year and a half ago soon since we found ourselves at last after many wanderings and numerous calamities in this magnificent capital, adorned with innumerable monuments.", "그러고 나서 나는 정말 이것에 손을 대고 말았지요!…… 우리가 숱한 방랑과 무수한 재난 끝에 마침내 이 무수한 기념비로 장식된 웅장한 수도에 정착한 지도 곧 일 년 반이 됩니다."], ["Here I obtained a situation....", "여기서 나는 자리를 하나 얻었지요……"], ["I obtained it and I lost it again.", "얻었다가 다시 잃었습니다."], ["Do you understand?", "아시겠어요?"], ["This time it was through my own fault I lost it: for my weakness had come out....", "이번에는 내 잘못으로 잃은 것이올시다, 내 약점이 도졌으니까요……"], ["We have now part of a room at Amalia Fyodorovna Lippevechsel’s; and what we live upon and what we pay our rent with, I could not say.", "지금 우리는 아말리야 표도로브나 리페베흐젤네 집에서 방 한 칸의 일부를 빌려 살고 있는데, 우리가 무얼로 먹고살고 무얼로 집세를 치르는지는 나도 차마 말 못 하겠소."], ["There are a lot of people living there besides ourselves.", "거기엔 우리 말고도 많은 사람들이 살고 있지요."], ["Dirt and disorder, a perfect Bedlam... hm...", "더럽고 무질서한, 그야말로 아수라장이올시다……"], ["yes...", "흠……"], ["And meanwhile my daughter by my first wife has grown up; and what my daughter has had to put up with from her step-mother whilst she was growing up, I won’t speak of.", "그래요…… 그러는 사이 첫 아내가 남긴 내 딸은 다 컸는데, 그 애가 자라는 동안 계모한테서 무슨 일을 겪었는지는 입에 올리지 않겠소."], ["For, though Katerina Ivanovna is full of generous feelings, she is a spirited lady, irritable and short-tempered....", "카테리나 이바노브나는 너그러운 감정으로 가득 찬 사람이긴 해도, 기개 있는 귀부인인 데다 신경질적이고 성미가 급하니까요…… 그래요."], ["Yes. But it’s no use going over that!", "한데 그 얘긴 들춰 봐야 소용없지요!"], ["Sonia, as you may well fancy, has had no education.", "짐작하시겠지만 소냐는 교육을 받지 못했습니다."], ["I did make an effort four years ago to give her a course of geography and universal history, but as I was not very well up in those subjects myself and we had no suitable books, and what books we had...", "사 년 전에 내가 그 애에게 지리와 세계사를 좀 가르쳐 보려고 애를 써 봤지만, 나 자신이 그 과목들에 그리 밝지 못한 데다 마땅한 책도 없었고, 있는 책이라곤……"], ["hm, anyway we have not even those now, so all our instruction came to an end.", "흠, 어쨌든 지금은 그 책마저 없으니, 우리의 모든 교육은 거기서 끝나고 말았지요."], ["We stopped at Cyrus of Persia.", "우리는 페르시아의 키루스에서 멈추고 말았소."], ["Since she has attained years of maturity, she has read other books of romantic tendency and of late she had read with great interest a book she got through Mr.", "다 큰 뒤로 그 애는 연애물에 가까운 다른 책들을 읽었고, 요즘은 레베지아트니코프 씨를 통해 구한 루이스의 『생리학』이라는 책을 — 아시오? — 무척 흥미롭게 읽고는 그 발췌문을 우리에게 들려주기까지 했지요."], ["Lebeziatnikov, Lewes’ Physiology--do you know it?--and even recounted extracts from it to us: and that’s the whole of her education.", "그게 그 애 교육의 전부올시다. 그러면 이제, 존경하는 선생, 외람되나 제 개인적인 일로 한 가지 사사로운 질문을 여쭤도 되겠습니까."], ["And now may I venture to address you, honoured sir, on my own account with a private question.", "선생은 점잖은 가난한 처녀가 정직한 노동으로 많이 벌 수 있다고 보십니까? 정직하고 별다른 재주가 없다면, 한순간도 손을 놓지 않고 일해도 하루에 십오 파딩도 못 법니다!"], ["Do you suppose that a respectable poor girl can earn much by honest work?", "게다가 더 기막힌 건, 클롭슈토크라는 5등 문관 — 이반 이바니치 클롭슈토크 말이오, 들어 보셨소?"], ["Not fifteen farthings a day can she earn, if she is respectable and has no special talent and that without putting her work down for an instant!", "— 이 그 애가 만들어 준 아마포 셔츠 여섯 벌의 삯을 오늘날까지 안 주고 있을뿐더러, 셔츠 깃이 본과 다르게 비뚜로 박혔다는 핑계로 발을 동동 구르며 욕설을 퍼붓고 그 애를 거칠게 쫓아냈다는 거지요."], ["And what’s more, Ivan Ivanitch Klopstock the civil counsellor--have you heard of him?--has not to this day paid her for the half-dozen linen shirts she made him and drove her roughly away, stamping and reviling her, on the pretext that the shirt collars were not made like the pattern and were put in askew.", "그런데 집에는 어린것들이 굶주리고 있고…… 카테리나 이바노브나는 두 손을 부르쥐고 방 안을 서성이는데, 그 병에 걸린 사람들이 늘 그렇듯 두 뺨이 벌겋게 상기되어서는, '여기서 우리하고 빌붙어 살면서,'"], ["And there are the little ones hungry....", "그러는 거요, '먹고 마시고 따뜻하게 지내면서, 거드는 게 아무것도 없잖니'"], ["And Katerina Ivanovna walking up and down and wringing her hands, her cheeks flushed red, as they always are in that disease: ‘Here you live with us,’ says she, ‘you eat and drink and are kept warm and you do nothing to help.’ And much she gets to eat and drink when there is not a crust for the little ones for three days!", "하고요. 한데 사흘째 어린것들 입에 빵 부스러기 하나 들어가지 못하는 판국에 무얼 그리 먹고 마시겠소!"], ["I was lying at the time...", "나는 그때 누워 있었는데……"], ["well, what of it!", "뭐, 어쨌든!"], ["I was lying drunk and I heard my Sonia speaking (she is a gentle creature with a soft little voice...", "나는 곤드레만드레 취해 누워서, 우리 소냐가 하는 말을 들었지요 (그 애는 부드러운 목소리를 가진 온순한 아이올시다…… 머리칼은 금빛이고, 얼굴은 그렇게 창백하고 야위었지요)."], ["fair hair and such a pale, thin little face).", "그 애가 그러더이다. '카테리나 이바노브나, 제가 정말 그런 짓을 해야 하나요?'"], ["She said: ‘Katerina Ivanovna, am I really to do a thing like that?’ And Darya Frantsovna, a woman of evil character and very well known to the police, had two or three times tried to get at her through the landlady.", "다리야 프란초브나라는, 행실이 나쁘고 경찰에도 잘 알려진 여자가 두세 번이나 집주인 여자를 통해 그 애에게 수작을 걸려 한 적이 있었거든요. '안 될 게 또 뭐냐?'"], ["‘And why not?’ said Katerina Ivanovna with a jeer, ‘you are something mighty precious to be so careful of!’ But don’t blame her, don’t blame her, honoured sir, don’t blame her!", "카테리나 이바노브나가 비웃듯이 그러더이다, '뭐 그리 대단한 보물이라고 그렇게 애지중지하니!' 하지만 그녀를 탓하지 마시오, 그녀를 탓하지 마세요, 존경하는 선생, 그녀를 탓하지 말아요!"], ["She was not herself when she spoke, but driven to distraction by her illness and the crying of the hungry children; and it was said more to wound her than anything else....", "그녀는 그 말을 할 때 제정신이 아니었습니다, 병과 굶주린 아이들의 울음소리에 미칠 지경으로 내몰린 거였지요. 그리고 그건 무슨 뜻이 있어서라기보다 그저 그 애 마음을 아프게 하려고 한 말이었소……"], ["For that’s Katerina Ivanovna’s character, and when children cry, even from hunger, she falls to beating them at once.", "그게 카테리나 이바노브나의 성미라서요, 아이들이 울면, 굶주려서 우는 거라도 당장 두들겨 패기 시작하지요."], ["At six o’clock I saw Sonia get up, put on her kerchief and her cape, and go out of the room and about nine o’clock she came back.", "여섯 시에 나는 소냐가 일어나 머릿수건을 두르고 망토를 걸치고 방을 나가는 걸 보았고, 아홉 시쯤 그 애가 돌아왔지요."], ["She walked straight up to Katerina Ivanovna and she laid thirty roubles on the table before her in silence.", "그 애는 곧장 카테리나 이바노브나에게로 걸어가더니, 말없이 그녀 앞 탁자에 삼십 루블을 내려놓았소."], ["She did not utter a word, she did not even look at her, she simply picked up our big green _drap de dames_ shawl (we have a shawl, made of _drap de dames_), put it over her head and face and lay down on the bed with her face to the wall; only her little shoulders and her body kept shuddering....", "한마디도 하지 않았고, 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않았어요, 그저 우리의 그 커다란 초록빛 드라드담 숄을 (우리한테 드라드담으로 만든 숄이 하나 있거든요) 집어서 머리와 얼굴을 뒤덮고는, 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌린 채 침대에 누웠지요. 다만 그 가냘픈 어깨와 몸뚱이가 계속 떨리고 있을 뿐이었소……"], ["And I went on lying there, just as before....", "그리고 나는 아까처럼 그대로 누워 있었지요……"], ["And then I saw, young man, I saw Katerina Ivanovna, in the same silence go up to Sonia’s little bed; she was on her knees all the evening kissing Sonia’s feet, and would not get up, and then they both fell asleep in each other’s arms...", "그러고 나서 나는 보았소, 젊은이, 나는 카테리나 이바노브나가 마찬가지로 말없이 소냐의 작은 침대로 다가가는 걸 보았습니다. 그녀는 저녁 내내 무릎을 꿇고 소냐의 발에 입을 맞추며 도무지 일어나려 들지 않다가, 마침내 둘은 서로 부둥켜안은 채 잠이 들고 말았지요……"], ["together, together...", "함께, 함께요…… 그래요……"], ["yes...", "그런데 나는……"], ["and I... lay drunk.”", "곤드레만드레 취해 누워 있었습니다.\""]], [["Marmeladov stopped short, as though his voice had failed him.", "마르멜라도프는 목소리가 잠겨 버리기라도 한 듯 뚝 말을 멈췄다."], ["Then he hurriedly filled his glass, drank, and cleared his throat.", "그러더니 황급히 잔을 채워 들이켜고는 목을 가다듬었다."]], [["“Since then, sir,”", "\"그때 이후로, 선생,\""], ["he went on after a brief pause--“Since then, owing to an unfortunate occurrence and through information given by evil-intentioned persons--in all which Darya Frantsovna took a leading part on the pretext that she had been treated with want of respect--since then my daughter Sofya Semyonovna has been forced to take a yellow ticket, and owing to that she is unable to go on living with us.", "그는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말을 이었다. \"그때 이후로, 어떤 불행한 사건과 악의를 품은 자들의 밀고 탓에 — 자신이 무례한 대접을 받았다는 핑계를 댄 다리야 프란초브나가 거기서 주도적인 역할을 했지요 — 그때 이후로 내 딸 소피야 세묘노브나는 노란 딱지를 받지 않을 수 없게 되었고, 그 탓에 우리와 함께 살 수도 없게 되고 말았소."], ["For our landlady, Amalia Fyodorovna would not hear of it (though she had backed up Darya Frantsovna before) and Mr. Lebeziatnikov too...", "우리 집주인 여자인 아말리야 표도로브나가 그건 들으려고도 하지 않았으니까요 (전에는 다리야 프란초브나를 거들어 주더니 말이오), 레베지아트니코프 씨 역시……"], ["hm....", "흠……"], ["All the trouble between him and Katerina Ivanovna was on Sonia’s account.", "그 사람과 카테리나 이바노브나 사이의 모든 분란은 소냐 때문에 일어난 거올시다."], ["At first he was for making up to Sonia himself and then all of a sudden he stood on his dignity: ‘how,’ said he, ‘can a highly educated man like me live in the same rooms with a girl like that?’ And Katerina Ivanovna would not let it pass, she stood up for her...", "처음엔 그 사람이 소냐에게 직접 수작을 걸더니, 그러다 갑자기 점잔을 빼면서 '나처럼 고등 교육을 받은 사람이 어떻게 저런 계집애와 한방에 살 수 있겠소?' 하고 그러더란 말이지요."], ["and so that’s how it happened.", "그러자 카테리나 이바노브나가 그냥 넘어가지 않고 그 애 편을 들었고…… 그래서 일이 그렇게 된 겁니다."], ["And Sonia comes to us now, mostly after dark; she comforts Katerina Ivanovna and gives her all she can....", "그래서 이제 소냐는, 주로 날이 어두워진 뒤에 우리한테 옵니다. 카테리나 이바노브나를 위로해 주고, 자기가 할 수 있는 건 다 갖다주지요……"], ["She has a room at the Kapernaumovs’ the tailors, she lodges with them; Kapernaumov is a lame man with a cleft palate and all of his numerous family have cleft palates too.", "그 애는 재단사 카페르나우모프네 집에 방을 하나 얻어 그 집에 세 들어 살고 있습니다. 카페르나우모프는 절름발이에다 언청이인데, 그 많은 식구들도 죄다 언청이지요."], ["And his wife, too, has a cleft palate.", "그 사람 아내 또한 언청이고요."], ["They all live in one room, but Sonia has her own, partitioned off....", "다들 한방에서 사는데, 소냐만은 칸막이로 막아 놓은 제 방을 따로 쓰고 있소……"], ["Hm...", "흠……"], ["yes...", "그래요……"], ["very poor people and all with cleft palates...", "무척 가난한 사람들이고 죄다 언청이지요…… 그래요."], ["yes. Then I got up in the morning, and put on my rags, lifted up my hands to heaven and set off to his excellency Ivan Afanasyvitch.", "그러고 나서 나는 아침에 일어나 누더기를 걸치고, 두 손을 하늘로 쳐들고는 이반 아파나시예비치 각하를 찾아갔습니다."], ["His excellency Ivan Afanasyvitch, do you know him?", "이반 아파나시예비치 각하를 아시오? 모른다고요?"], ["No? Well, then, it’s a man of God you don’t know.", "그럼 선생은 하느님 같은 사람을 모르는 거외다."], ["He is wax...", "그분은 밀랍이지요……"], ["wax before the face of the Lord; even as wax melteth!...", "주님 앞의 밀랍이올시다, 밀랍이 녹듯이 말이지요!…… 내 이야기를 듣고 그분의 두 눈이 흐려지더이다."], ["His eyes were dim when he heard my story.", "'마르멜라도프, 자네는 이미 한 번 내 기대를 저버렸지…… 내 책임으로 한 번 더 자네를 받아 주겠네'"], ["‘Marmeladov, once already you have deceived my expectations...", "— 그분이 그렇게 말씀하셨소, '명심하게,' 그렇게 말씀하시고는, '이제 가 보게'"], ["I’ll take you once more on my own responsibility’--that’s what he said, ‘remember,’ he said, ‘and now you can go.’ I kissed the dust at his feet--in thought only, for in reality he would not have allowed me to do it, being a statesman and a man of modern political and enlightened ideas.", "하셨지요. 나는 그분 발치의 먼지에 입을 맞췄소 — 마음속으로만요, 실제로는 그분이 그렇게 하도록 두지 않으셨을 테니까요, 국정을 맡으신 분이자 근대적이고 계몽된 정치사상을 지닌 분이라서요."], ["I returned home, and when I announced that I’d been taken back into the service and should receive a salary, heavens, what a to-do there was!...”", "나는 집으로 돌아왔고, 내가 다시 관청에 복직해서 봉급을 받게 되었다고 알리자, 맙소사, 얼마나 야단법석이 났던지!……\""]], [["Marmeladov stopped again in violent excitement.", "마르멜라도프는 격한 흥분에 휩싸여 다시 말을 멈췄다."], ["At that moment a whole party of revellers already drunk came in from the street, and the sounds of a hired concertina and the cracked piping voice of a child of seven singing “The Hamlet” were heard in the entry.", "바로 그때, 이미 거나하게 취한 한 무리의 술꾼들이 거리에서 우르르 들어왔고, 입구 쪽에서는 빌린 손풍금 소리와 「소촌(小村)」을 부르는 일곱 살배기 아이의 갈라진 새된 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["The room was filled with noise.", "방 안은 소음으로 가득 찼다."], ["The tavern-keeper and the boys were busy with the new-comers.", "술집 주인과 소년들은 새로 온 손님들을 치르느라 분주했다."], ["Marmeladov paying no attention to the new arrivals continued his story.", "마르멜라도프는 새로 온 이들에게는 아랑곳하지 않고 자기 이야기를 계속했다."], ["He appeared by now to be extremely weak, but as he became more and more drunk, he became more and more talkative.", "이제 그는 몹시 쇠약해진 듯 보였으나, 점점 더 취해 갈수록 점점 더 수다스러워졌다."], ["The recollection of his recent success in getting the situation seemed to revive him, and was positively reflected in a sort of radiance on his face.", "일자리를 얻은 최근의 성공을 떠올리자 그는 되살아나는 듯했고, 그것이 그의 얼굴에 일종의 광채가 되어 또렷이 비치기까지 했다."], ["Raskolnikov listened attentively.", "라스콜니코프는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“That was five weeks ago, sir. Yes.... As soon as Katerina Ivanovna and Sonia heard of it, mercy on us, it was as though I stepped into the kingdom of Heaven. It used to be: you can lie like a beast, nothing but abuse. Now they were walking on tiptoe, hushing the children. ‘Semyon Zaharovitch is tired with his work at the office, he is resting, shh!’ They made me coffee before I went to work and boiled cream for me! They began to get real cream for me, do you hear that? And how they managed to get together the money for a decent outfit--eleven roubles, fifty copecks, I can’t guess. Boots, cotton shirt-fronts--most magnificent, a uniform, they got up all in splendid style, for eleven roubles and a half. The first morning I came back from the office I found Katerina Ivanovna had cooked two courses for dinner--soup and salt meat with horse radish--which we had never dreamed of till then. She had not any dresses... none at all, but she got herself up as though she were going on a visit; and not that she’d anything to do it with, she smartened herself up with nothing at all, she’d done her hair nicely, put on a clean collar of some sort, cuffs, and there she was, quite a different person, she was younger and better looking. Sonia, my little darling, had only helped with money ‘for the time,’ she said, ‘it won’t do for me to come and see you too often. After dark maybe when no one can see.’ Do you hear, do you hear? I lay down for a nap after dinner and what do you think: though Katerina Ivanovna had quarrelled to the last degree with our landlady Amalia Fyodorovna only a week before, she could not resist then asking her in to coffee. For two hours they were sitting, whispering together. ‘Semyon Zaharovitch is in the service again, now, and receiving a salary,’ says she, ‘and he went himself to his excellency and his excellency himself came out to him, made all the others wait and led Semyon Zaharovitch by the hand before everybody into his study.’ Do you hear, do you hear? ‘To be sure,’ says he, ‘Semyon Zaharovitch, remembering your past services,’ says he, ‘and in spite of your propensity to that foolish weakness, since you promise now and since moreover we’ve got on badly without you,’ (do you hear, do you hear;) ‘and so,’ says he, ‘I rely now on your word as a gentleman.’ And all that, let me tell you, she has simply made up for herself, and not simply out of wantonness, for the sake of bragging; no, she believes it all herself, she amuses herself with her own fancies, upon my word she does! And I don’t blame her for it, no, I don’t blame her!... Six days ago when I brought her my first earnings in full--twenty-three roubles forty copecks altogether--she called me her poppet: ‘poppet,’ said she, ‘my little poppet.’ And when we were by ourselves, you understand? You would not think me a beauty, you would not think much of me as a husband, would you?... Well, she pinched my cheek, ‘my little poppet,’ said she.”", "\"그게 다섯 주 전이올시다, 선생. 그래요…… 카테리나 이바노브나와 소냐가 그 소식을 듣자마자, 맙소사, 나는 마치 천국에 발을 들여놓은 것만 같았지요. 전에는 이랬소. 짐승처럼 누워 있어 봐, 욕설밖에 안 돌아왔지요. 그런데 이젠 둘이 발끝으로 살금살금 걸으며 아이들을 쉬쉬 달래더이다. '세묜 자하로비치가 관청 일로 피곤하셔서 쉬고 계셔, 쉿!' 그러는 거지요. 내가 일하러 나가기 전에 커피를 끓여 주고, 크림을 데워 주었습니다! 나를 위해 진짜 크림을 구해 오기 시작했단 말이오, 들립니까? 게다가 그 둘이 어떻게 변변한 옷차림 한 벌 값을 — 십일 루블 오십 코페이카를 — 마련했는지, 나는 도무지 짐작도 못 하겠소. 부츠며 면 셔츠 앞섶이며 — 더없이 훌륭한 것들로요 — 제복까지, 십일 루블 반으로 죄다 멋들어지게 갖춰 주었지요. 관청에서 처음 돌아온 날 아침에 보니 카테리나 이바노브나가 점심으로 두 가지 요리를 — 수프와 고추냉이를 곁들인 소금에 절인 고기를 — 만들어 놓았더이다, 그때까지는 꿈도 못 꾸던 거였지요. 그녀에겐 변변한 드레스가 한 벌도…… 단 한 벌도 없었지만, 마치 손님을 맞으러 가기라도 하는 양 차려입었더이다. 한데 차려입을 거리가 있어서 그런 게 아니라, 아무것도 없는 채로 멋을 낸 거였지요, 머리를 곱게 매만지고, 무슨 깨끗한 깃을 달고, 소맷동을 달고 하니, 거기 전혀 딴사람이 서 있더란 말입니다, 더 젊고 더 고와 보였지요. 내 어여쁜 소냐는 그저 돈만 좀 보탰을 뿐, '잠시 동안만요,' 그러더이다, '제가 아버지를 너무 자주 뵈러 와선 안 되니까요. 아마 날이 어두워진 뒤에, 아무도 못 볼 때라면 몰라도요' 하고요. 들립니까, 들려요? 나는 점심 후에 한숨 자려고 누웠는데, 글쎄 어찌 된 줄 아시오. 카테리나 이바노브나가 불과 일주일 전에 집주인 여자 아말리야 표도로브나와 더할 나위 없이 심하게 다퉜는데도, 그때만큼은 못 참고 그 여자를 커피에 초대하더란 말이오. 둘이 두 시간 동안 마주 앉아 소곤소곤 이야기를 나눴지요. '세묜 자하로비치가 이제 다시 관청에 나가게 됐고, 봉급도 받는답니다,' 그녀가 그러더이다, '그이가 직접 각하를 찾아갔는데, 각하께서 친히 그이에게 나오셔서, 다른 사람들은 죄다 기다리게 하시고는, 세묜 자하로비치의 손을 잡고 모두가 보는 앞에서 당신 집무실로 데리고 들어가셨다지 뭐예요' 하고요. 들립니까, 들려요? '암,' 각하께서 그러셨다지요, '세묜 자하로비치, 자네의 지난 공로를 기억하고,' 그러시고는, '자네가 그 어리석은 약점에 빠지는 버릇이 있긴 하지만, 이제 자네가 약속을 했고, 더구나 자네가 없으니 우리도 일이 영 안 풀렸던 터라,' (들립니까, 들려요) '그러니,' 각하께서 그러셨다지요, '이제 나는 신사로서의 자네 말을 믿겠네' 하고요. 한데 말이오, 그 모든 게 그녀가 그냥 혼자 지어낸 것이올시다, 그저 허영심에서, 떠벌리고 싶어서 그런 게 아니라요. 아니, 그녀는 그걸 전부 스스로 믿고 있어요, 제가 지어낸 공상으로 스스로 즐거워하는 거지요, 정말이지 그렇다니까요! 그리고 나는 그걸 두고 그녀를 탓하지 않습니다, 아니, 나는 그녀를 탓하지 않아요!…… 엿새 전에 내가 첫 봉급을 고스란히 — 다 합쳐 이십삼 루블 사십 코페이카를 — 그녀에게 가져다주었을 때, 그녀는 나를 자기 귀염둥이라고 부르더이다. '귀염둥이,' 그러는 거예요, '내 귀여운 귀염둥이' 하고요. 그것도 우리 둘만 있을 때였지요, 아시겠소? 자네는 나를 미남이라고도, 변변한 남편이라고도 여기지 않겠지요?…… 한데 그녀가 내 뺨을 꼬집으며, '내 귀여운 귀염둥이' 하고 그러더란 말입니다.\""]], [["Marmeladov broke off, tried to smile, but suddenly his chin began to twitch.", "마르멜라도프는 말을 뚝 끊고는 미소를 지으려 애썼으나, 갑자기 그의 턱이 실룩거리기 시작했다."], ["He controlled himself however.", "그러나 그는 가까스로 자제했다."], ["The tavern, the degraded appearance of the man, the five nights in the hay barge, and the pot of spirits, and yet this poignant love for his wife and children bewildered his listener.", "이 선술집, 이 사내의 추락한 행색, 건초 거룻배에서 보낸 닷새 밤, 술 단지, 그러면서도 아내와 자식들을 향한 이 가슴 저미는 사랑이 듣는 이를 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["Raskolnikov listened intently but with a sick sensation.", "라스콜니코프는 골똘히 귀를 기울였으나, 속이 메스꺼웠다."], ["He felt vexed that he had come here.", "그는 여기 온 것이 짜증스러웠다."]], [["“Honoured sir, honoured sir,”", "\"존경하는 선생, 존경하는 선생,\""], ["cried Marmeladov recovering himself--“Oh, sir, perhaps all this seems a laughing matter to you, as it does to others, and perhaps I am only worrying you with the stupidity of all the trivial details of my home life, but it is not a laughing matter to me.", "마르멜라도프가 정신을 추스르며 외쳤다. \"오, 선생, 어쩌면 이 모든 게 다른 이들에게 그렇듯 선생에게도 웃음거리로 보일지 모르고, 어쩌면 나는 그저 우리 집안 살림의 자질구레하고 하찮은 일들을 시시콜콜 늘어놓아 선생을 성가시게 하고 있는지도 모르지요, 하지만 나에게는 그게 결코 웃음거리가 아니올시다."], ["For I can feel it all....", "나는 그 모든 걸 절절히 느낄 수 있으니까요……"], ["And the whole of that heavenly day of my life and the whole of that evening I passed in fleeting dreams of how I would arrange it all, and how I would dress all the children, and how I should give her rest, and how I should rescue my own daughter from dishonour and restore her to the bosom of her family....", "내 인생의 그 천국 같은 하루와 그날 저녁 내내, 나는 이 모든 걸 어떻게 꾸려 나갈까, 아이들을 죄다 어떻게 입힐까, 그녀를 어떻게 쉬게 해 줄까, 내 친딸을 치욕에서 어떻게 구해 내어 가족의 품으로 되돌릴까 하는 덧없는 꿈에 잠겨 보냈소……"], ["And a great deal more....", "그 밖에도 숱한 꿈을요……"], ["Quite excusable, sir. Well, then, sir”", "충분히 그럴 만하지 않겠습니까, 선생."], ["(Marmeladov suddenly gave a sort of start, raised his head and gazed intently at his listener) “well, on the very next day after all those dreams, that is to say, exactly five days ago, in the evening, by a cunning trick, like a thief in the night, I stole from Katerina Ivanovna the key of her box, took out what was left of my earnings, how much it was I have forgotten, and now look at me, all of you!", "자, 그런데, 선생,\" (마르멜라도프가 갑자기 흠칫하더니, 고개를 들고 듣는 이를 뚫어지게 바라보았다) \"자, 그 모든 꿈을 꾼 바로 다음 날, 그러니까 정확히 닷새 전 저녁에, 나는 교활한 수작으로, 한밤의 도둑처럼, 카테리나 이바노브나에게서 그녀의 궤짝 열쇠를 슬쩍해서는 봉급에서 남은 걸 꺼냈는데, 그게 얼마였는지는 이젠 잊어버렸소, 자, 이제 나를 좀 보시오, 여러분 모두!"], ["It’s the fifth day since I left home, and they are looking for me there and it’s the end of my employment, and my uniform is lying in a tavern on the Egyptian bridge.", "내가 집을 나온 지 닷새째이고, 그쪽에서는 나를 찾고 있을 테고, 내 일자리도 이걸로 끝장이고, 내 제복은 이집트 다리 옆 어느 선술집에 잡혀 있소."], ["I exchanged it for the garments I have on...", "나는 그걸 지금 입고 있는 이 옷가지와 바꿔치기했지요……"], ["and it’s the end of everything!”", "그러니 이제 만사가 끝장이올시다!\""]], [["Marmeladov struck his forehead with his fist, clenched his teeth, closed his eyes and leaned heavily with his elbow on the table.", "마르멜라도프는 주먹으로 자기 이마를 치고는, 이를 악물고 두 눈을 감은 채, 팔꿈치로 탁자를 무겁게 짚었다."], ["But a minute later his face suddenly changed and with a certain assumed slyness and affectation of bravado, he glanced at Raskolnikov, laughed and said:", "그러나 잠시 후 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 변하더니, 짐짓 꾸민 듯한 능청과 허세를 부리며 라스콜니코프를 흘끗 바라보고는, 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“This morning I went to see Sonia, I went to ask her for a pick-me-up!", "\"오늘 아침에 나는 소냐를 찾아갔소, 해장술 값 좀 달라고 갔지요!"], ["He-he-he!”", "헤헤헤!\""]], [["“You don’t say she gave it to you?”", "\"설마 그 애가 줬다는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["cried one of the new-comers; he shouted the words and went off into a guffaw.", "새로 온 손님 하나가 외쳤다. 그는 그 말을 큰 소리로 내뱉고는 폭소를 터뜨렸다."]], [["“This very quart was bought with her money,”", "\"바로 이 한 쿼트가 그 애 돈으로 산 거올시다.\""], ["Marmeladov declared, addressing himself exclusively to Raskolnikov.", "마르멜라도프가 오로지 라스콜니코프에게만 대고 단언했다."], ["“Thirty copecks she gave me with her own hands, her last, all she had, as I saw....", "\"그 애가 제 손으로 내게 삼십 코페이카를 주었지요, 마지막 남은 돈을, 가진 전부를, 내가 본 그대로요……"], ["She said nothing, she only looked at me without a word....", "그 애는 아무 말도 하지 않았소, 그저 말없이 나를 바라보기만 했지요……"], ["Not on earth, but up yonder...", "이 땅에서가 아니라, 저 높은 곳에서……"], ["they grieve over men, they weep, but they don’t blame them, they don’t blame them!", "그분들은 사람들을 가엾어하며 슬퍼하고 눈물 흘리지만, 그들을 탓하지는 않습니다, 탓하지 않아요!"], ["But it hurts more, it hurts more when they don’t blame!", "하지만 탓하지 않으면 더 아프지요, 탓하지 않으면 더 아파요!"], ["Thirty copecks yes!", "삼십 코페이카, 그래요!"], ["And maybe she needs them now, eh? What do you think, my dear sir?", "한데 어쩌면 그 애한텐 지금 그 돈이 필요했을지도 모르지요, 안 그렇소?"], ["For now she’s got to keep up her appearance.", "어떻게 생각하시오, 선생? 이제 그 애는 겉모양을 갖춰야 하니까요."], ["It costs money, that smartness, that special smartness, you know?", "그게 돈이 든단 말이오, 그 맵시, 그 특별한 맵시 말이오, 아시겠소?"], ["Do you understand?", "알아듣겠소?"], ["And there’s pomatum, too, you see, she must have things; petticoats, starched ones, shoes, too, real jaunty ones to show off her foot when she has to step over a puddle.", "그리고 포마드도 있어야 하고, 알다시피, 그 애한테는 이것저것 필요한 게 있어요. 풀 먹인 페티코트도, 신발도, 웅덩이를 건널 때 발을 내보일 수 있는 진짜 멋들어진 신발 말이오."], ["Do you understand, sir, do you understand what all that smartness means?", "아시겠소, 선생, 그 모든 맵시가 무얼 뜻하는지 아시겠어요?"], ["And here I, her own father, here I took thirty copecks of that money for a drink!", "그런데 나는, 그 애의 친아비인 나는, 그 돈에서 삼십 코페이카를 술값으로 챙겼단 말이오!"], ["And I am drinking it!", "그리고 그걸 마시고 있어요!"], ["And I have already drunk it!", "게다가 이미 다 마셔 버렸지요!"], ["Come, who will have pity on a man like me, eh?", "자, 나 같은 인간을 누가 가엾어해 주겠소, 응?"], ["Are you sorry for me, sir, or not?", "선생, 나를 가엾게 여기시오, 아니면 안 그러시오?"], ["Tell me, sir, are you sorry or not?", "말해 보시오, 선생, 가엾소, 안 가엾소?"], ["He-he-he!”", "헤헤헤!\""]], [["He would have filled his glass, but there was no drink left.", "그는 잔을 채우려 했으나, 마실 게 남아 있지 않았다."], ["The pot was empty.", "단지가 비어 있었던 것이다."]], [["“What are you to be pitied for?”", "\"자네가 가엾을 게 뭐가 있나?\""], ["shouted the tavern-keeper who was again near them.", "다시 그들 곁으로 다가온 술집 주인이 외쳤다."]], [["Shouts of laughter and even oaths followed.", "폭소와 욕설마저 뒤따랐다."], ["The laughter and the oaths came from those who were listening and also from those who had heard nothing but were simply looking at the figure of the discharged government clerk.", "그 웃음과 욕설은 이야기를 듣고 있던 자들에게서도, 그리고 아무것도 듣지 못한 채 그저 그 해고당한 관리의 꼴을 바라보고 있던 자들에게서도 터져 나왔다."]], [["“To be pitied!", "\"가엾다니!"], ["Why am I to be pitied?”", "내가 왜 가엾어야 하느냐고?\""], ["Marmeladov suddenly declaimed, standing up with his arm outstretched, as though he had been only waiting for that question.", "마르멜라도프가 마치 바로 그 질문을 기다리고 있었다는 듯, 팔을 쭉 뻗고 벌떡 일어서며 갑자기 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다."]], [["“Why am I to be pitied, you say?", "\"내가 왜 가엾어야 하느냐, 그 말이오?"], ["Yes!", "그래!"], ["there’s nothing to pity me for!", "나를 가엾어할 거리라곤 하나도 없소!"], ["I ought to be crucified, crucified on a cross, not pitied!", "나는 십자가에 못 박혀야 마땅하지, 십자가에 못 박혀야 해, 가엾어할 게 아니라! 나를 못 박으시오, 오 심판자여, 나를 못 박되 또한 나를 가엾어해 주시오!"], ["Crucify me, oh judge, crucify me but pity me!", "그러면 나는 제 발로 못 박히러 가리다, 내가 찾는 건 흥겨움이 아니라 눈물과 고난이니까요!…… 술 파는 자네는 이 술 한 파인트가 내게 달콤했을 거라 생각하시오?"], ["And then I will go of myself to be crucified, for it’s not merry-making I seek but tears and tribulation!...", "나는 그 술잔 밑바닥에서 고난을 찾았소, 눈물과 고난을 찾았고, 그걸 찾아냈고, 그걸 맛보았지요. 하지만 모든 사람을 가엾어하신 분, 모든 사람과 모든 것을 헤아리신 분, 그분께서 우리를 가엾어하실 것이오."], ["Do you suppose, you that sell, that this pint of yours has been sweet to me?", "그분은 유일한 분이고, 그분이야말로 심판자시니까요. 그분께서 그날에 오시어 물으실 거요."], ["It was tribulation I sought at the bottom of it, tears and tribulation, and have found it, and I have tasted it; but He will pity us Who has had pity on all men, Who has understood all men and all things, He is the One, He too is the judge.", "'폐병 든 계모와 남의 어린것들을 위해 제 몸을 십자가에 바친 그 딸은 어디 있느냐? 짐승 같은 짓에도 굴하지 않고 더럽고 술에 절은 제 지상의 아비를 가엾어한 그 딸은 어디 있느냐?'"], ["He will come in that day and He will ask: ‘Where is the daughter who gave herself for her cross, consumptive step-mother and for the little children of another?", "그러고는 이렇게 말씀하시겠지요. '내게로 오너라!"], ["Where is the daughter who had pity upon the filthy drunkard, her earthly father, undismayed by his beastliness?’ And He will say, ‘Come to me!", "나는 이미 한 번 너를 용서하였노라…… 이미 한 번 너를 용서하였노라……"], ["I have already forgiven thee once....", "네 죄가 많으나 다 사함을 받았으니, 이는 네가 많이 사랑하였음이라……' 그리고 그분은 내 소냐를 용서하실 거요, 용서하실 거예요, 나는 그걸 압니다……"], ["I have forgiven thee once....", "방금 그 애와 함께 있을 때 내 가슴으로 그걸 느꼈지요!"], ["Thy sins which are many are forgiven thee for thou hast loved much....’ And he will forgive my Sonia, He will forgive, I know it...", "그리고 그분은 만인을 심판하시고 용서하시리다, 선한 자도 악한 자도, 지혜로운 자도 온순한 자도요…… 그리고 그들 모두를 다 처리하시고 난 뒤, 그때 우리를 부르시겠지요."], ["I felt it in my heart when I was with her just now!", "'너희도 나오너라,' 그분께서 그러실 거요, '나오너라, 너희 주정뱅이들아, 나오너라, 너희 나약한 자들아, 나오너라, 너희 치욕의 자식들아!'"], ["And He will judge and will forgive all, the good and the evil, the wise and the meek....", "그러면 우리는 모두 부끄러움 없이 나아가 그분 앞에 서리다. 그러면 그분께서 우리에게 이르시겠지요."], ["And when He has done with all of them, then He will summon us.", "'너희는 돼지로다, 짐승의 형상으로 빚어졌고 그 낙인이 찍혔도다. 하지만 너희도 오너라!'"], ["‘You too come forth,’ He will say, ‘Come forth ye drunkards, come forth, ye weak ones, come forth, ye children of shame!’ And we shall all come forth, without shame and shall stand before him.", "그러면 지혜로운 자들과 분별 있는 자들이 말하겠지요. '오 주여, 어찌하여 이런 자들을 받아 주시나이까?'"], ["And He will say unto us, ‘Ye are swine, made in the Image of the Beast and with his mark; but come ye also!’ And the wise ones and those of understanding will say, ‘Oh Lord, why dost Thou receive these men?’ And He will say, ‘This is why I receive them, oh ye wise, this is why I receive them, oh ye of understanding, that not one of them believed himself to be worthy of this.’ And He will hold out His hands to us and we shall fall down before him...", "그러면 그분께서 이르시리다. '내가 이들을 받아 주는 까닭은, 오 지혜로운 자들아, 내가 이들을 받아 주는 까닭은, 오 분별 있는 자들아, 이들 가운데 단 하나도 스스로 이럴 자격이 있다고 여기지 않았기 때문이니라.'"], ["and we shall weep... and we shall understand all things!", "그러고는 우리에게 두 손을 내미실 것이고, 우리는 그분 앞에 엎드리리다……"], ["Then we shall understand all!...", "그리고 우리는 울 것이고…… 그리고 우리는 모든 것을 깨달으리다!"], ["and all will understand, Katerina Ivanovna even...", "그때 우리는 모든 것을 깨달으리다!…… 그리고 모두가 깨달으리다, 카테리나 이바노브나마저도……"], ["she will understand....", "그녀도 깨달으리다……"], ["Lord, Thy kingdom come!”", "주여, 당신의 나라가 임하소서!\""], ["And he sank down on the bench exhausted, and helpless, looking at no one, apparently oblivious of his surroundings and plunged in deep thought.", "그러고는 기진맥진하여 어쩔 줄 모른 채 의자에 털썩 주저앉았는데, 아무도 쳐다보지 않고, 주위를 까맣게 잊은 듯 깊은 상념에 잠겨 들었다. 그의 말은 어떤 인상을 자아냈다."], ["His words had created a certain impression; there was a moment of silence; but soon laughter and oaths were heard again.", "한순간 침묵이 흘렀다. 그러나 곧 다시 웃음소리와 욕설이 들려왔다."]], [["“That’s his notion!”", "\"저게 저 사람 생각이라고!\""]], [["“Talked himself silly!”", "\"떠벌리다 제풀에 정신 줄을 놓았구먼!\""]], [["“A fine clerk he is!”", "\"관리 났네, 관리 났어!\""]], [["And so on, and so on.", "이런 식으로, 계속해서 이어졌다."]], [["“Let us go, sir,”", "\"갑시다, 선생,\""], ["said Marmeladov all at once, raising his head and addressing Raskolnikov--“come along with me...", "마르멜라도프가 갑자기 고개를 들고 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건넸다. \"나하고 같이 갑시다……"], ["Kozel’s house, looking into the yard.", "코젤네 집인데, 안뜰 쪽으로 나 있지요."], ["I’m going to Katerina Ivanovna--time I did.”", "카테리나 이바노브나한테 가야겠소 — 갈 때도 됐고요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov had for some time been wanting to go and he had meant to help him.", "라스콜니코프는 얼마 전부터 떠나고 싶던 차였고, 그를 거들어 줄 생각이었다."], ["Marmeladov was much unsteadier on his legs than in his speech and leaned heavily on the young man.", "마르멜라도프는 말보다도 다리가 훨씬 더 불안정해서, 청년에게 몸을 무겁게 기댔다."], ["They had two or three hundred paces to go.", "가야 할 거리는 이삼백 걸음쯤이었다."], ["The drunken man was more and more overcome by dismay and confusion as they drew nearer the house.", "그 술 취한 사내는 집에 가까워질수록 점점 더 두려움과 혼란에 사로잡혔다."]], [["“It’s not Katerina Ivanovna I am afraid of now,”", "\"내가 지금 두려운 건 카테리나 이바노브나가 아니올시다.\""], ["he muttered in agitation--“and that she will begin pulling my hair.", "그가 동요하며 중얼거렸다. \"그녀가 내 머리칼을 쥐어뜯기 시작할까 봐 두려운 것도 아니고요."], ["What does my hair matter!", "내 머리칼이 무슨 대수겠소!"], ["Bother my hair! That’s what I say!", "머리칼 따위 될 대로 되라지요!"], ["Indeed it will be better if she does begin pulling it, that’s not what I am afraid of...", "내 말이 그 말이외다! 차라리 그녀가 머리칼을 쥐어뜯기 시작하는 편이 나을 거요, 그게 두려운 게 아니니까요……"], ["it’s her eyes I am afraid of...", "내가 두려운 건 그녀의 눈이올시다……"], ["yes, her eyes...", "그래요, 그녀의 눈……"], ["the red on her cheeks, too, frightens me...", "두 뺨에 어린 그 붉은빛도 나를 겁먹게 하고요……"], ["and her breathing too....", "그리고 그녀의 숨소리도요……"], ["Have you noticed how people in that disease breathe...", "그 병에 걸린 사람들이 어떻게 숨 쉬는지 눈여겨본 적 있소……"], ["when they are excited?", "흥분했을 때 말이오?"], ["I am frightened of the children’s crying, too....", "아이들의 울음소리도 두렵소……"], ["For if Sonia has not taken them food...", "혹시 소냐가 그 애들에게 먹을 걸 갖다주지 않았다면……"], ["I don’t know what’s happened!", "무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 모르겠소!"], ["I don’t know!", "모르겠어요!"], ["But blows I am not afraid of....", "하지만 매질은 두렵지 않소이다……"], ["Know, sir, that such blows are not a pain to me, but even an enjoyment.", "알아 두시오, 선생, 그런 매질은 내게 고통이 아니라 오히려 즐거움이라는 걸요."], ["In fact I can’t get on without it....", "사실 나는 그게 없으면 못 견디겠소……"], ["It’s better so.", "그게 차라리 나아요."], ["Let her strike me, it relieves her heart... it’s better so...", "그녀가 나를 때리게 두시오, 그러면 그녀의 가슴이 후련해지니까요……"], ["There is the house.", "그게 차라리 나아요……"], ["The house of Kozel, the cabinet-maker...", "저기 그 집이 있군. 가구장이 코젤네 집이오……"], ["a German, well-to-do.", "독일 사람인데, 형편이 넉넉하지요."], ["Lead the way!”", "앞장서시오!\""]], [["They went in from the yard and up to the fourth storey.", "그들은 안뜰로 들어가 사 층으로 올라갔다."], ["The staircase got darker and darker as they went up.", "위로 올라갈수록 계단은 점점 더 어두워졌다."], ["It was nearly eleven o’clock and although in summer in Petersburg there is no real night, yet it was quite dark at the top of the stairs.", "거의 열한 시였고, 페테르부르크의 여름에는 진짜 밤이라는 게 없다지만, 그래도 계단 꼭대기는 캄캄했다."]], [["A grimy little door at the very top of the stairs stood ajar.", "계단 맨 꼭대기에 있는 때 묻은 작은 문이 빠끔히 열려 있었다."], ["A very poor-looking room about ten paces long was lighted up by a candle-end; the whole of it was visible from the entrance.", "열 걸음 남짓 되는 무척 초라한 방이 양초 토막 하나로 밝혀져 있었는데, 입구에서 방 전체가 다 들여다보였다."], ["It was all in disorder, littered up with rags of all sorts, especially children’s garments.", "방은 온통 어수선했고, 온갖 누더기, 특히 아이들 옷가지가 너저분하게 널려 있었다."], ["Across the furthest corner was stretched a ragged sheet.", "가장 안쪽 구석에는 너덜너덜한 홑이불 한 장이 가로질러 쳐져 있었다."], ["Behind it probably was the bed.", "아마 그 뒤에 침대가 있는 모양이었다."], ["There was nothing in the room except two chairs and a sofa covered with American leather, full of holes, before which stood an old deal kitchen-table, unpainted and uncovered.", "방 안에는 의자 두 개와, 구멍이 숭숭 뚫린 아메리카산 가죽을 씌운 소파 하나밖에 없었고, 그 앞에는 칠도 안 하고 덮개도 없는 낡은 전나무 부엌 탁자가 놓여 있었다."], ["At the edge of the table stood a smoldering tallow-candle in an iron candlestick.", "탁자 가장자리에는 쇠 촛대에 꽂힌 우지 양초 하나가 가물가물 타고 있었다."], ["It appeared that the family had a room to themselves, not part of a room, but their room was practically a passage.", "보아하니 이 가족은 방의 일부가 아니라 방 한 칸을 통째로 쓰고 있는 듯했으나, 그 방은 사실상 통로나 다름없었다."], ["The door leading to the other rooms, or rather cupboards, into which Amalia Lippevechsel’s flat was divided stood half open, and there was shouting, uproar and laughter within.", "아말리야 리페베흐젤의 셋집을 나눈 다른 방들, 아니 차라리 벽장들로 통하는 문이 반쯤 열려 있었고, 그 안에서는 고함과 소란, 웃음소리가 들려왔다."], ["People seemed to be playing cards and drinking tea there.", "거기서는 사람들이 카드놀이를 하며 차를 마시고 있는 듯했다."], ["Words of the most unceremonious kind flew out from time to time.", "이따금 더없이 무람없는 말들이 튀어나왔다."]], [["Raskolnikov recognised Katerina Ivanovna at once.", "라스콜니코프는 카테리나 이바노브나를 단번에 알아보았다."], ["She was a rather tall, slim and graceful woman, terribly emaciated, with magnificent dark brown hair and with a hectic flush in her cheeks.", "그녀는 꽤 키가 크고 호리호리하며 우아한 여인이었는데, 끔찍하게 야위었고, 짙은 갈색의 탐스러운 머리칼에 두 뺨은 병적인 홍조를 띠고 있었다."], ["She was pacing up and down in her little room, pressing her hands against her chest; her lips were parched and her breathing came in nervous broken gasps.", "그녀는 두 손을 가슴에 꼭 누른 채 그 작은 방 안을 서성이고 있었다. 입술은 바싹 말랐고, 숨은 신경질적으로 끊어지듯 헐떡이며 새어 나왔다."], ["Her eyes glittered as in fever and looked about with a harsh immovable stare.", "그녀의 두 눈은 열병에라도 걸린 듯 번뜩였고, 매섭게 굳은 시선으로 주위를 두리번거렸다."], ["And that consumptive and excited face with the last flickering light of the candle-end playing upon it made a sickening impression.", "그 폐병 들고 흥분한 얼굴에 다 타들어 가는 양초 토막의 마지막 가물거리는 불빛이 어른거리니, 메스꺼운 인상을 자아냈다."], ["She seemed to Raskolnikov about thirty years old and was certainly a strange wife for Marmeladov....", "라스콜니코프가 보기에 그녀는 서른쯤 되어 보였고, 마르멜라도프와는 분명 어울리지 않는 아내였다……"], ["She had not heard them and did not notice them coming in.", "그녀는 그들이 오는 소리를 듣지도 못했고 알아차리지도 못했다."], ["She seemed to be lost in thought, hearing and seeing nothing.", "그녀는 무언가에 골몰한 듯, 아무것도 듣지도 보지도 못하는 것 같았다."], ["The room was close, but she had not opened the window; a stench rose from the staircase, but the door on to the stairs was not closed.", "방 안은 답답했지만, 그녀는 창문을 열지 않았다. 계단에서는 악취가 올라왔지만, 계단으로 통하는 문을 닫지 않았다."], ["From the inner rooms clouds of tobacco smoke floated in, she kept coughing, but did not close the door.", "안쪽 방들에서는 담배 연기가 뭉게뭉게 흘러들었고, 그녀는 연신 기침을 하면서도 문을 닫지 않았다."], ["The youngest child, a girl of six, was asleep, sitting curled up on the floor with her head on the sofa.", "막내인 여섯 살배기 계집아이는 바닥에 웅크려 앉아 소파에 머리를 기댄 채 잠들어 있었다."], ["A boy a year older stood crying and shaking in the corner, probably he had just had a beating.", "한 살쯤 위인 사내아이는 구석에서 울며 떨고 있었는데, 아마 방금 매를 맞은 모양이었다."], ["Beside him stood a girl of nine years old, tall and thin, wearing a thin and ragged chemise with an ancient cashmere pelisse flung over her bare shoulders, long outgrown and barely reaching her knees.", "그 곁에는 아홉 살 난 계집아이가 서 있었는데, 키가 크고 야위었으며, 얇고 너덜너덜한 속옷 위에 맨 어깨에는 오래되어 케케묵은 캐시미어 외투를 걸치고 있었으나, 너무 오래되어 작아진 그 옷은 무릎에도 채 닿지 않았다."], ["Her arm, as thin as a stick, was round her brother’s neck.", "막대기처럼 가느다란 그 아이의 팔이 동생의 목을 감싸고 있었다."], ["She was trying to comfort him, whispering something to him, and doing all she could to keep him from whimpering again.", "아이는 동생을 달래며 무언가 소곤소곤 속삭였고, 동생이 다시 칭얼대지 않도록 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다."], ["At the same time her large dark eyes, which looked larger still from the thinness of her frightened face, were watching her mother with alarm.", "그러면서도 겁먹은 야윈 얼굴 탓에 한층 더 커 보이는 큼직한 검은 눈으로, 두려운 듯 어머니를 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Marmeladov did not enter the door, but dropped on his knees in the very doorway, pushing Raskolnikov in front of him.", "마르멜라도프는 문 안으로 들어서지 않고, 바로 그 문간에서 무릎을 꿇으며 라스콜니코프를 앞으로 떠밀었다."], ["The woman seeing a stranger stopped indifferently facing him, coming to herself for a moment and apparently wondering what he had come for.", "그 여인은 낯선 사람을 보자 무심하게 멈춰 서서 그를 마주 보았는데, 잠시 정신이 든 듯 그가 무슨 일로 왔는지 의아해하는 것 같았다."], ["But evidently she decided that he was going into the next room, as he had to pass through hers to get there.", "그러나 그녀는 그가 옆방으로 가려는 것이려니 결론을 내린 게 분명했으니, 거기로 가려면 자기네 방을 지나가야 했기 때문이다."], ["Taking no further notice of him, she walked towards the outer door to close it and uttered a sudden scream on seeing her husband on his knees in the doorway.", "그녀는 더는 그에게 신경 쓰지 않고 바깥문을 닫으려 그쪽으로 걸어가다가, 문간에 무릎 꿇고 있는 남편을 보고는 별안간 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"앗!\""], ["she cried out in a frenzy, “he has come back!", "그녀가 광란하듯 외쳤다. \"이 인간이 돌아왔어!"], ["The criminal!", "이 죄인이!"], ["the monster!...", "이 괴물이!……"], ["And where is the money?", "그래, 돈은 어디 있어?"], ["What’s in your pocket, show me!", "주머니에 든 게 뭐야, 보여 봐!"], ["And your clothes are all different!", "옷도 죄다 딴것이잖아!"], ["Where are your clothes?", "당신 옷은 어디 갔어?"], ["Where is the money!", "돈은 어디 있느냐고!"], ["Speak!”", "말해!\""]], [["And she fell to searching him.", "그러고는 그를 뒤지기 시작했다."], ["Marmeladov submissively and obediently held up both arms to facilitate the search.", "마르멜라도프는 고분고분 순순히 두 팔을 들어 올려 수색을 도왔다."], ["Not a farthing was there.", "그러나 한 파딩도 나오지 않았다."]], [["“Where is the money?”", "\"돈은 어디 있어?\""], ["she cried--“Mercy on us, can he have drunk it all?", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"맙소사, 그걸 죄다 마셔 버린 거야?"], ["There were twelve silver roubles left in the chest!”", "궤짝에 은화로 십이 루블이 남아 있었는데!\""], ["and in a fury she seized him by the hair and dragged him into the room.", "그러고는 격분하여 그의 머리칼을 움켜쥐고 그를 방 안으로 끌고 들어갔다."], ["Marmeladov seconded her efforts by meekly crawling along on his knees.", "마르멜라도프는 온순하게 무릎으로 기어가며 그녀의 수고를 거들었다."]], [["“And this is a consolation to me!", "\"이게 내겐 위안이올시다!"], ["This does not hurt me, but is a positive con-so-la-tion, ho-nou-red sir,”", "이게 내겐 고통이 아니라 진정한 위, 안, 이, 라, 고요, 존, 경, 하, 는, 선생,\""], ["he called out, shaken to and fro by his hair and even once striking the ground with his forehead.", "그가 머리칼을 잡힌 채 이리저리 흔들리며, 심지어 한 번은 이마로 바닥을 쿵 찧기까지 하면서 외쳤다."], ["The child asleep on the floor woke up, and began to cry.", "바닥에서 잠들어 있던 아이가 깨어나 울기 시작했다."], ["The boy in the corner losing all control began trembling and screaming and rushed to his sister in violent terror, almost in a fit.", "구석에 있던 사내아이는 자제력을 완전히 잃고 부들부들 떨며 비명을 지르더니, 거의 발작이라도 일으킬 듯 격렬한 공포에 사로잡혀 누나에게 달려갔다."], ["The eldest girl was shaking like a leaf.", "맏이인 계집아이는 사시나무처럼 떨고 있었다."]], [["“He’s drunk it!", "\"마셔 버렸어!"], ["he’s drunk it all,”", "죄다 마셔 버렸다고,\""], ["the poor woman screamed in despair--“and his clothes are gone!", "가엾은 여인이 절망하여 비명을 질렀다. \"옷마저 없어졌어!"], ["And they are hungry, hungry!”--and wringing her hands she pointed to the children.", "그리고 이 애들은 굶주리고 있다고, 굶주리고 있어!\" 그러고는 두 손을 부르쥐고 아이들을 가리켰다."], ["“Oh, accursed life!", "\"오, 이 저주받은 인생!"], ["And you, are you not ashamed?”--she pounced all at once upon Raskolnikov--“from the tavern!", "그리고 당신, 부끄럽지도 않아?\" 그녀가 별안간 라스콜니코프에게 달려들었다."], ["Have you been drinking with him?", "\"선술집에서 왔구먼! 이 인간이랑 같이 마셨지?"], ["You have been drinking with him, too!", "당신도 이 인간이랑 같이 마신 거지!"], ["Go away!”", "썩 나가!\""]], [["The young man was hastening away without uttering a word.", "청년은 한마디도 하지 않고 황급히 자리를 떴다."], ["The inner door was thrown wide open and inquisitive faces were peering in at it.", "그 사이 안쪽 문이 활짝 열리며, 호기심 어린 얼굴들이 그리로 기웃거렸다."], ["Coarse laughing faces with pipes and cigarettes and heads wearing caps thrust themselves in at the doorway.", "파이프며 담배를 문 거칠고 히죽거리는 얼굴들, 챙모자를 쓴 머리들이 문간으로 불쑥불쑥 들이밀어졌다."], ["Further in could be seen figures in dressing gowns flung open, in costumes of unseemly scantiness, some of them with cards in their hands.", "좀 더 안쪽에는 앞섶을 풀어헤친 실내복 차림에, 꼴사납게 헐벗다시피 한 차림새의 사람들이 보였고, 더러는 손에 카드를 쥐고 있었다."], ["They were particularly diverted, when Marmeladov, dragged about by his hair, shouted that it was a consolation to him.", "마르멜라도프가 머리칼을 잡힌 채 이리저리 끌려다니면서 이게 자기에겐 위안이라고 외쳐 대자, 그들은 유난히 흥겨워했다."], ["They even began to come into the room; at last a sinister shrill outcry was heard: this came from Amalia Lippevechsel herself pushing her way amongst them and trying to restore order after her own fashion and for the hundredth time to frighten the poor woman by ordering her with coarse abuse to clear out of the room next day.", "그들은 급기야 방 안으로 들어오기 시작했다. 마침내 불길하게 새된 고함 소리가 들려왔으니, 바로 아말리야 리페베흐젤 본인이 그들 틈을 비집고 나오며 제 나름의 방식으로 질서를 잡으려 하면서, 백 번째로 그 가엾은 여인에게 거친 욕설을 퍼부으며 내일 당장 방을 비우라 윽박질러 겁을 주려는 것이었다."], ["As he went out, Raskolnikov had time to put his hand into his pocket, to snatch up the coppers he had received in exchange for his rouble in the tavern and to lay them unnoticed on the window.", "라스콜니코프는 나가는 길에, 주머니에 손을 넣어 선술집에서 자기 루블을 헐어 받은 동전들을 움켜쥐고는, 아무도 모르게 창턱에 내려놓을 짬을 낼 수 있었다."], ["Afterwards on the stairs, he changed his mind and would have gone back.", "그러나 나중에 계단에서 그는 마음이 바뀌어 도로 들어가려 했다."]], [["“What a stupid thing I’ve done,”", "'내가 어쩌자고 이런 멍청한 짓을,'"], ["he thought to himself, “they have Sonia and I want it myself.”", "그는 속으로 생각했다. '저들에겐 소냐가 있지만, 나는 내가 그 돈이 필요한데.'"], ["But reflecting that it would be impossible to take it back now and that in any case he would not have taken it, he dismissed it with a wave of his hand and went back to his lodging.", "그러나 이제 와서 도로 가져오기란 불가능하고, 어차피 가져오지도 못하리라는 데 생각이 미치자, 그는 손을 한 번 내젓고는 자기 하숙집으로 돌아갔다."], ["“Sonia wants pomatum too,”", "'소냐도 포마드가 필요하겠지.'"], ["he said as he walked along the street, and he laughed malignantly--“such smartness costs money....", "그는 거리를 걸으며 이렇게 말하고는, 표독스럽게 웃었다. '그런 맵시에도 돈이 드는 법이니까……"], ["Hm!", "흠!"], ["And maybe Sonia herself will be bankrupt to-day, for there is always a risk, hunting big game...", "게다가 어쩌면 소냐 본인도 오늘 빈털터리가 될지 모르지, 큰 사냥감을 쫓는 데는…… 금맥을 캐는 데는……"], ["digging for gold...", "늘 위험이 따르는 법이니까."], ["then they would all be without a crust to-morrow except for my money.", "그렇게 되면 내 돈이 아니고서야 저들은 내일 빵 부스러기 하나 없을 테고."], ["Hurrah for Sonia!", "소냐 만세!"], ["What a mine they’ve dug there!", "거 참 굉장한 금광을 파 놓으셨군!"], ["And they’re making the most of it!", "게다가 그걸 알뜰히도 우려먹고 있어!"], ["Yes, they are making the most of it!", "그래, 알뜰히도 우려먹고 있지!"], ["They’ve wept over it and grown used to it.", "다들 그 일로 눈물깨나 흘리다가도 이젠 익숙해져 버렸어."], ["Man grows used to everything, the scoundrel!”", "인간이란 무엇에든 익숙해지는 법이니까, 비열한 작자 같으니!'"]], [["He sank into thought.", "그는 깊은 상념에 잠겨 들었다."]], [["“And what if I am wrong,”", "'한데 만일 내가 틀렸다면?'"], ["he cried suddenly after a moment’s thought.", "그는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 갑자기 외쳤다."], ["“What if man is not really a scoundrel, man in general, I mean, the whole race of mankind--then all the rest is prejudice, simply artificial terrors and there are no barriers and it’s all as it should be.”", "'만일 인간이 정말로 비열한 게 아니라면, 인간 일반이, 그러니까 인류 전체가 말이야 — 그렇다면 나머지는 죄다 편견에 불과하고, 그저 인위적으로 지어낸 공포일 뿐이며, 아무런 장벽도 없는 것이고, 모든 게 마땅히 그래야 할 대로인 셈이지.'"]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["He waked up late next day after a broken sleep.", "그는 이튿날 늦게야 끊긴 잠에서 깨어났다."], ["But his sleep had not refreshed him; he waked up bilious, irritable, ill-tempered, and looked with hatred at his room.", "그러나 잠은 그를 개운하게 해 주지 못했다. 그는 담즙이 끓는 듯 신경질적이고 심술궂은 기분으로 깨어나, 증오에 찬 눈길로 자기 방을 둘러보았다."], ["It was a tiny cupboard of a room about six paces in length.", "그것은 길이가 여섯 걸음쯤 되는, 벽장만 한 작디작은 방이었다."], ["It had a poverty-stricken appearance with its dusty yellow paper peeling off the walls, and it was so low-pitched that a man of more than average height was ill at ease in it and felt every moment that he would knock his head against the ceiling.", "벽에서 벗겨져 가는 먼지투성이 노란 벽지 탓에 방은 궁색해 보였고, 천장이 어찌나 낮은지 키가 보통보다 조금만 큰 사람도 그 안에서는 불편하기 짝이 없어, 매 순간 머리를 천장에 부딪칠 것만 같았다."], ["The furniture was in keeping with the room: there were three old chairs, rather rickety; a painted table in the corner on which lay a few manuscripts and books; the dust that lay thick upon them showed that they had been long untouched.", "가구도 방과 어울렸다. 다리가 흔들거리는 낡은 의자 세 개, 구석에 놓인 칠한 탁자 — 그 위에는 원고 몇 장과 책 몇 권이 놓여 있었는데, 그 위에 두껍게 쌓인 먼지는 그것들이 오랫동안 손길이 닿지 않았음을 보여 주었다."], ["A big clumsy sofa occupied almost the whole of one wall and half the floor space of the room; it was once covered with chintz, but was now in rags and served Raskolnikov as a bed.", "한쪽 벽 거의 전부와 방바닥의 절반을 큼직하고 볼품없는 소파가 차지하고 있었다. 한때는 친츠 천으로 싸여 있었으나 지금은 누더기가 된 그것이 라스콜니코프의 침대 노릇을 했다."], ["Often he went to sleep on it, as he was, without undressing, without sheets, wrapped in his old student’s overcoat, with his head on one little pillow, under which he heaped up all the linen he had, clean and dirty, by way of a bolster.", "그는 흔히 그 위에서, 옷도 벗지 않고 시트도 깔지 않은 채, 낡은 학생 외투를 둘러쓰고, 작은 베개 하나에 머리를 얹고 잠들곤 했는데, 그 베개 밑에는 깨끗한 것이든 더러운 것이든 가진 속옷가지를 죄다 쌓아 올려 긴 베개 대용으로 삼았다."], ["A little table stood in front of the sofa.", "소파 앞에는 작은 탁자가 하나 놓여 있었다."]], [["It would have been difficult to sink to a lower ebb of disorder, but to Raskolnikov in his present state of mind this was positively agreeable.", "이보다 더 낮은 무질서의 밑바닥으로 가라앉기란 어려웠을 테지만, 지금 같은 마음 상태의 라스콜니코프에게 이것은 도리어 흡족한 일이었다."], ["He had got completely away from everyone, like a tortoise in its shell, and even the sight of a servant girl who had to wait upon him and looked sometimes into his room made him writhe with nervous irritation.", "그는 등딱지 속의 거북이처럼 모든 사람에게서 완전히 멀어져 있었고, 시중을 들어 주느라 이따금 그의 방을 들여다보아야 하는 하녀의 모습마저도 신경질적인 짜증으로 그를 몸부림치게 했다."], ["He was in the condition that overtakes some monomaniacs entirely concentrated upon one thing.", "그는 한 가지 일에만 온통 집중하는 어떤 편집광들을 사로잡는 그런 상태에 빠져 있었다."], ["His landlady had for the last fortnight given up sending him in meals, and he had not yet thought of expostulating with her, though he went without his dinner.", "집주인 여자는 지난 두 주 동안 그에게 식사 보내는 일을 그만두었으나, 그는 끼니를 거르면서도 그녀에게 따져 볼 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["Nastasya, the cook and only servant, was rather pleased at the lodger’s mood and had entirely given up sweeping and doing his room, only once a week or so she would stray into his room with a broom.", "요리사이자 유일한 하녀인 나스타시야는 이 하숙인의 기분을 차라리 반기는 편이어서, 그의 방을 쓸고 치우는 일을 완전히 그만두었고, 그저 일주일에 한 번쯤 빗자루를 들고 그의 방에 들어오는 게 고작이었다."], ["She waked him up that day.", "그날도 그를 깨운 것은 그녀였다."]], [["“Get up, why are you asleep?”", "\"일어나요, 왜 아직 자고 있어요?\""], ["she called to him.", "그녀가 그를 불렀다."], ["“It’s past nine, I have brought you some tea; will you have a cup?", "\"벌써 아홉 시가 넘었어요, 차를 좀 가져왔는데, 한 잔 들겠어요?"], ["I should think you’re fairly starving?”", "어지간히 굶주렸을 것 같은데요?\""]], [["Raskolnikov opened his eyes, started and recognised Nastasya.", "라스콜니코프는 눈을 떴고, 흠칫 놀라 나스타시야를 알아보았다."]], [["“From the landlady, eh?”", "\"집주인 여자가 보냈나, 응?\""], ["he asked, slowly and with a sickly face sitting up on the sofa.", "그는 병색이 도는 얼굴로 천천히 소파에 일어나 앉으며 물었다."]], [["“From the landlady, indeed!”", "\"집주인 여자가 보내긴요!\""]], [["She set before him her own cracked teapot full of weak and stale tea and laid two yellow lumps of sugar by the side of it.", "그녀는 그의 앞에, 묽고 김빠진 차가 가득한 자기 것 금 간 찻주전자를 내려놓고, 그 옆에 누런 설탕 덩어리 두 개를 놓았다."]], [["“Here, Nastasya, take it please,”", "\"여기, 나스타시야, 이것 좀 받아 줘.\""], ["he said, fumbling in his pocket (for he had slept in his clothes) and taking out a handful of coppers--“run and buy me a loaf.", "그는 (옷을 입은 채 잤기에) 주머니를 뒤져 동전 한 줌을 꺼내며 말했다. \"달려가서 빵 한 덩이만 사다 줘."], ["And get me a little sausage, the cheapest, at the pork-butcher’s.”", "그리고 돼지고기 가게에서 제일 싼 걸로 소시지도 조금만 사다 주고.\""]], [["“The loaf I’ll fetch you this very minute, but wouldn’t you rather have some cabbage soup instead of sausage?", "\"빵은 당장 가져다드릴게요, 근데 소시지 대신 양배추 수프를 좀 들지 않겠어요?"], ["It’s capital soup, yesterday’s.", "아주 훌륭한 수프예요, 어제 거고요."], ["I saved it for you yesterday, but you came in late.", "어제 댁한테 남겨 뒀는데 늦게 들어왔잖아요."], ["It’s fine soup.”", "정말 맛 좋은 수프예요.\""]], [["When the soup had been brought, and he had begun upon it, Nastasya sat down beside him on the sofa and began chatting.", "수프가 들어오고 그가 먹기 시작하자, 나스타시야는 소파에 그의 곁에 앉아 수다를 떨기 시작했다."], ["She was a country peasant-woman and a very talkative one.", "그녀는 시골 출신의 농사꾼 아낙으로, 무척이나 말이 많았다."]], [["“Praskovya Pavlovna means to complain to the police about you,”", "\"프라스코비야 파블로브나가 댁을 경찰에 고발하려고 해요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He scowled.", "그는 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“To the police?", "\"경찰에?"], ["What does she want?”", "그 여자가 뭘 원하는 거야?\""]], [["“You don’t pay her money and you won’t turn out of the room.", "\"돈은 안 갚지, 방은 안 비우지."], ["That’s what she wants, to be sure.”", "그러니 원하는 게 뻔하죠.\""]], [["“The devil, that’s the last straw,”", "\"제기랄, 이젠 갈 데까지 갔군.\""], ["he muttered, grinding his teeth, “no, that would not suit me...", "그는 이를 갈며 중얼거렸다. \"안 돼, 그건 나한테 안 맞아……"], ["just now.", "지금은."], ["She is a fool,”", "멍청한 여자야.\""], ["he added aloud.", "그가 소리 내어 덧붙였다."], ["“I’ll go and talk to her to-day.”", "\"오늘 가서 얘기를 좀 해야겠어.\""]], [["“Fool she is and no mistake, just as I am.", "\"멍청하긴 멍청하죠, 틀림없이, 나처럼요."], ["But why, if you are so clever, do you lie here like a sack and have nothing to show for it?", "근데 댁은 그렇게 똑똑하다면서 왜 여기 자루처럼 누워서 아무것도 내보이는 게 없어요?"], ["One time you used to go out, you say, to teach children.", "한때는 애들 가르치러 나갔다면서요."], ["But why is it you do nothing now?”", "근데 지금은 왜 아무것도 안 해요?\""]], [["“I am doing...”", "\"나는 하고 있어…….\""], ["Raskolnikov began sullenly and reluctantly.", "라스콜니코프가 시무룩하고 마지못한 투로 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“What are you doing?”", "\"뭘 하고 있는데요?\""]], [["“Work...”", "\"일을…….\""]], [["“What sort of work?”", "\"무슨 일을요?\""]], [["“I am thinking,”", "\"생각하고 있어.\""], ["he answered seriously after a pause.", "그는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 진지하게 대답했다."]], [["Nastasya was overcome with a fit of laughter.", "나스타시야는 웃음보가 터졌다."], ["She was given to laughter and when anything amused her, she laughed inaudibly, quivering and shaking all over till she felt ill.", "그녀는 잘 웃는 편이었는데, 무언가 우스운 일이 있으면 소리 없이 온몸을 부들부들 떨고 흔들며 속이 거북해질 지경까지 웃어 댔다."]], [["“And have you made much money by your thinking?”", "\"그래, 그 생각으로 돈은 많이 벌었어요?\""], ["she managed to articulate at last.", "그녀가 가까스로 한마디 입에 올렸다."]], [["“One can’t go out to give lessons without boots.", "\"장화 없이는 애들 가르치러 나갈 수가 없어."], ["And I’m sick of it.”", "게다가 지긋지긋하고.\""]], [["“Don’t quarrel with your bread and butter.”", "\"밥줄하고는 싸우지 마요.\""]], [["“They pay so little for lessons.", "\"수업료를 너무 쥐꼬리만큼 준단 말이야."], ["What’s the use of a few coppers?”", "동전 몇 푼이 무슨 소용이 있어?\""], ["he answered, reluctantly, as though replying to his own thought.", "그는 마치 자기 자신의 생각에 답하듯 마지못해 대꾸했다."]], [["“And you want to get a fortune all at once?”", "\"그래서 댁은 한꺼번에 한밑천 잡고 싶다는 거예요?\""]], [["He looked at her strangely.", "그는 묘한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, I want a fortune,”", "\"그래, 나는 한밑천을 원해.\""], ["he answered firmly, after a brief pause.", "그가 잠깐 사이를 두었다가 단호하게 대답했다."]], [["“Don’t be in such a hurry, you quite frighten me!", "\"그렇게 서두르지 좀 마요, 사람 정말 무섭게 하네!"], ["Shall I get you the loaf or not?”", "빵은 가져올까요, 말까요?\""]], [["“As you please.”", "\"맘대로 해.\""]], [["“Ah, I forgot!", "\"아, 깜박했네!"], ["A letter came for you yesterday when you were out.”", "어제 댁이 나갔을 때 편지가 한 통 왔어요.\""]], [["“A letter?", "\"편지?"], ["for me!", "나한테!"], ["from whom?”", "누구한테서?\""]], [["“I can’t say.", "\"몰라요."], ["I gave three copecks of my own to the postman for it.", "우편배달부한테 내 돈으로 삼 코페이카를 줬어요."], ["Will you pay me back?”", "갚을 거죠?\""]], [["“Then bring it to me, for God’s sake, bring it,”", "\"그럼 그걸 이리 가져와, 제발, 가져오라고.\""], ["cried Raskolnikov greatly excited--“good God!”", "라스콜니코프가 몹시 흥분하여 외쳤다. \"맙소사!\""]], [["A minute later the letter was brought him.", "잠시 후 편지가 그에게 전해졌다."], ["That was it: from his mother, from the province of R----.", "바로 그것이었다. 어머니에게서 온, R 현(縣)에서 온 편지였다."], ["He turned pale when he took it.", "그는 그것을 받아 들면서 얼굴이 새파래졌다."], ["It was a long while since he had received a letter, but another feeling also suddenly stabbed his heart.", "편지를 받아 본 지 오래되었지만, 또 다른 감정이 갑자기 그의 가슴을 찔러 왔다."]], [["“Nastasya, leave me alone, for goodness’ sake; here are your three copecks, but for goodness’ sake, make haste and go!”", "\"나스타시야, 제발 나를 혼자 좀 내버려 둬. 여기 삼 코페이카 받아, 그러니 제발 서둘러서 좀 나가 줘!\""]], [["The letter was quivering in his hand; he did not want to open it in her presence; he wanted to be left _alone_ with this letter. When Nastasya had gone out, he lifted it quickly to his lips and kissed it; then he gazed intently at the address, the small, sloping handwriting, so dear and familiar, of the mother who had once taught him to read and write. He delayed; he seemed almost afraid of something. At last he opened it; it was a thick heavy letter, weighing over two ounces, two large sheets of note paper were covered with very small handwriting.", "편지가 그의 손안에서 떨렸다. 그녀 앞에서 그것을 뜯고 싶지 않았다. 그는 이 편지와 단둘이 남겨지기를 바랐다. 나스타시야가 나가자 그는 편지를 재빨리 입술로 가져가 입을 맞추었다. 그러고는 한때 그에게 읽고 쓰기를 가르쳐 준 어머니의, 그토록 정겹고 낯익은 작고 비스듬한 필체로 쓰인 수신인을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그는 머뭇거렸다. 무언가가 두려운 것 같았다. 마침내 그는 편지를 뜯었다. 두툼하고 묵직한 편지로, 무게가 두 온스가 넘었고, 큼직한 편지지 두 장에 아주 작은 글씨가 빼곡히 적혀 있었다."]], [["“My dear Rodya,”", "\"사랑하는 나의 로쟈.\""], ["wrote his mother--“it’s two months since I last had a talk with you by letter which has distressed me and even kept me awake at night, thinking.", "어머니는 이렇게 썼다. \"내가 너와 편지로 이야기를 나눈 지 두 달이 지났구나, 그게 나를 얼마나 괴롭히던지, 밤에도 잠 못 이루며 골똘히 생각하곤 했단다."], ["But I am sure you will not blame me for my inevitable silence.", "하지만 내가 어쩔 수 없이 침묵했던 것을 너는 탓하지 않으리라 믿는다."], ["You know how I love you; you are all we have to look to, Dounia and I, you are our all, our one hope, our one stay.", "내가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지 너도 알지. 두냐와 나에게는 너밖에 의지할 데가 없단다, 너는 우리의 전부, 우리의 단 하나뿐인 희망, 단 하나뿐인 버팀목이야."], ["What a grief it was to me when I heard that you had given up the university some months ago, for want of means to keep yourself and that you had lost your lessons and your other work!", "몇 달 전에 네가 자기 생활을 꾸려 갈 돈이 없어서 대학을 그만두었고, 수업도 다른 일거리도 다 잃었다는 소식을 들었을 때 내가 얼마나 슬펐는지!"], ["How could I help you out of my hundred and twenty roubles a year pension?", "일 년에 연금 백이십 루블을 받는 내가 어떻게 너를 도울 수 있었겠니?"], ["The fifteen roubles I sent you four months ago I borrowed, as you know, on security of my pension, from Vassily Ivanovitch Vahrushin a merchant of this town.", "넉 달 전에 네게 보낸 십오 루블도, 너도 알다시피, 이 고장 상인 바실리 이바노비치 바흐루신에게서 내 연금을 담보로 빌린 것이었단다."], ["He is a kind-hearted man and was a friend of your father’s too.", "그분은 마음씨 고운 사람이고, 또 네 아버지의 친구이기도 했지."], ["But having given him the right to receive the pension, I had to wait till the debt was paid off and that is only just done, so that I’ve been unable to send you anything all this time.", "그러나 그분에게 연금을 받을 권리를 넘겼기 때문에 빚이 다 청산될 때까지 기다려야 했고, 이제야 겨우 그것이 끝났구나, 그래서 그동안 너에게 아무것도 보내 줄 수가 없었단다."], ["But now, thank God, I believe I shall be able to send you something more and in fact we may congratulate ourselves on our good fortune now, of which I hasten to inform you.", "하지만 이제는, 다행히도, 무언가 좀 더 보낼 수 있을 것 같고, 사실 지금 우리는 우리의 행운을 스스로 축하해도 좋을 듯해서 서둘러 네게 알리는 거란다."], ["In the first place, would you have guessed, dear Rodya, that your sister has been living with me for the last six weeks and we shall not be separated in the future.", "우선, 사랑하는 로쟈, 네가 짐작이나 했겠니, 네 누이가 지난 여섯 주 동안 나와 함께 지내고 있고 앞으로도 우리는 떨어지지 않으리라는 것을."], ["Thank God, her sufferings are over, but I will tell you everything in order, so that you may know just how everything has happened and all that we have hitherto concealed from you.", "다행히도 그애의 고생은 끝났지만, 모든 일을 차근차근 다 말해 주마, 그래야 네가 모든 일이 어떻게 된 것인지, 그리고 우리가 지금껏 네게 숨겨 온 것들을 다 알 수 있을 테니까."], ["When you wrote to me two months ago that you had heard that Dounia had a great deal to put up with in the Svidrigaïlovs’ house, when you wrote that and asked me to tell you all about it--what could I write in answer to you?", "두 달 전에 네가 나에게, 두냐가 스비드리가일로프 댁에서 무척 힘든 일을 많이 겪고 있다는 소문을 들었다고 쓰면서, 그 일을 다 이야기해 달라고 했을 때 — 내가 무어라 답을 쓸 수 있었겠니?"], ["If I had written the whole truth to you, I dare say you would have thrown up everything and have come to us, even if you had to walk all the way, for I know your character and your feelings, and you would not let your sister be insulted.", "만일 내가 너에게 모든 진실을 다 써 보냈다면, 너는 분명 모든 걸 내팽개치고 우리에게 달려왔을 거다, 설령 내내 걸어서라도 말이야, 나는 네 성격과 감정을 잘 아니까, 너는 네 누이가 모욕당하도록 내버려 두지 않았을 테니."], ["I was in despair myself, but what could I do?", "나 자신도 절망에 빠져 있었지만, 내가 무얼 할 수 있었겠니?"], ["And, besides, I did not know the whole truth myself then.", "게다가 나도 그때는 진실을 다 알지 못했단다."], ["What made it all so difficult was that Dounia received a hundred roubles in advance when she took the place as governess in their family, on condition of part of her salary being deducted every month, and so it was impossible to throw up the situation without repaying the debt.", "모든 걸 더 어렵게 만든 건, 두냐가 그 집의 가정교사 자리를 맡으면서 백 루블을 선불로 받았고, 매달 봉급에서 그 일부를 제하기로 한 조건이었다는 점이었다. 그러니 빚을 다 갚기 전에는 그 자리를 내팽개칠 수가 없었던 거야."], ["This sum (now I can explain it all to you, my precious Rodya) she took chiefly in order to send you sixty roubles, which you needed so terribly then and which you received from us last year.", "이 돈은 (이제야 너에게 다 설명할 수 있겠구나, 소중한 로쟈), 그애가 무엇보다 그때 네가 그토록 절실히 필요로 했던, 그래서 작년에 우리에게서 받은 그 육십 루블을 너에게 보내 주려고 받은 것이었단다."], ["We deceived you then, writing that this money came from Dounia’s savings, but that was not so, and now I tell you all about it, because, thank God, things have suddenly changed for the better, and that you may know how Dounia loves you and what a heart she has.", "그때 우리는 이 돈이 두냐의 저축에서 나온 것이라고 써서 너를 속였지만, 그건 사실이 아니었어, 이제 너에게 다 말하는 건, 다행히도 사정이 갑자기 좋은 쪽으로 바뀌었고, 또 두냐가 너를 얼마나 사랑하는지, 그애가 얼마나 따뜻한 마음을 지녔는지 네가 알았으면 해서란다."], ["At first indeed Mr. Svidrigaïlov treated her very rudely and used to make disrespectful and jeering remarks at table....", "처음에 사실 스비드리가일로프 씨는 그애를 아주 무례하게 대했고, 식탁에서 모욕적이고 비웃는 듯한 말을 하곤 했단다……"], ["But I don’t want to go into all those painful details, so as not to worry you for nothing when it is now all over. In short, in spite of the kind and generous behaviour of Marfa Petrovna, Mr.", "하지만 이제 다 끝난 마당에 괜히 너를 걱정시키지 않으려고, 그 고통스러운 자질구레한 일들까지 다 들춰내고 싶지는 않구나."], ["Svidrigaïlov’s wife, and all the rest of the household, Dounia had a very hard time, especially when Mr. Svidrigaïlov, relapsing into his old regimental habits, was under the influence of Bacchus.", "한마디로, 스비드리가일로프 씨의 부인인 마르파 페트로브나와 나머지 식구들이 친절하고 너그럽게 대해 주었음에도, 두냐는 무척 힘든 시간을 보냈단다, 특히 스비드리가일로프 씨가 옛 연대 시절의 버릇이 도져서 바쿠스의 영향 아래 있을 때면 더욱 그랬지."], ["And how do you think it was all explained later on?", "그런데 나중에 이 모든 게 어떻게 밝혀졌는지 아니?"], ["Would you believe that the crazy fellow had conceived a passion for Dounia from the beginning, but had concealed it under a show of rudeness and contempt.", "그 미친 사람이 처음부터 두냐에게 욕정을 품고 있었으면서도, 그것을 무례하고 경멸하는 태도로 감춰 왔다면 너는 믿겠니?"], ["Possibly he was ashamed and horrified himself at his own flighty hopes, considering his years and his being the father of a family; and that made him angry with Dounia.", "어쩌면 그는 자기 나이며 한 집안의 가장이라는 처지를 생각하면서, 자기 자신의 그 경솔한 욕망에 스스로 부끄러워하고 소스라쳤는지도 모른다. 그래서 두냐에게 화풀이를 했던 거지."], ["And possibly, too, he hoped by his rude and sneering behaviour to hide the truth from others.", "또 어쩌면 그는 무례하고 비웃는 듯한 태도로 다른 사람들에게 진실을 감추려 했는지도 모르겠다."], ["But at last he lost all control and had the face to make Dounia an open and shameful proposal, promising her all sorts of inducements and offering, besides, to throw up everything and take her to another estate of his, or even abroad.", "그러나 마침내 그는 자제력을 완전히 잃고서 뻔뻔스럽게도 두냐에게 노골적이고 수치스러운 제안을 하기에 이르렀단다, 온갖 것들로 그애를 꾀면서, 게다가 모든 걸 다 내던지고 그애를 자기의 다른 영지로, 심지어 외국으로 데려가겠다고까지 했다는구나."], ["You can imagine all she went through!", "그애가 어떤 일을 겪었을지 너도 짐작이 갈 게다!"], ["To leave her situation at once was impossible not only on account of the money debt, but also to spare the feelings of Marfa Petrovna, whose suspicions would have been aroused: and then Dounia would have been the cause of a rupture in the family.", "그 자리를 당장 그만두는 것은, 돈 빚 때문만이 아니라, 마르파 페트로브나의 마음을 다치지 않기 위해서도 불가능했단다, 그랬다간 그분의 의심을 사게 될 테고, 그러면 두냐가 집안에 불화를 일으킨 원인이 되었을 테니까."], ["And it would have meant a terrible scandal for Dounia too; that would have been inevitable.", "그리고 그건 두냐에게도 끔찍한 추문이 되었을 거다, 그건 피할 수 없는 일이었지."], ["There were various other reasons owing to which Dounia could not hope to escape from that awful house for another six weeks.", "그 밖에도 여러 다른 이유가 있어서 두냐는 그 끔찍한 집에서 여섯 주를 더 견디는 수밖에 도리가 없었단다."], ["You know Dounia, of course; you know how clever she is and what a strong will she has.", "너도 물론 두냐를 잘 알지. 그애가 얼마나 영리하고 또 얼마나 의지가 굳센지 알 테고."], ["Dounia can endure a great deal and even in the most difficult cases she has the fortitude to maintain her firmness.", "두냐는 많은 것을 견뎌 낼 수 있고, 아무리 어려운 상황에서도 꿋꿋함을 잃지 않는 의연함을 지녔지."], ["She did not even write to me about everything for fear of upsetting me, although we were constantly in communication.", "우리가 끊임없이 연락을 주고받았는데도, 그애는 나를 속상하게 할까 봐 모든 걸 다 써 보내지도 않았단다."], ["It all ended very unexpectedly.", "그런데 그 모든 일이 아주 뜻밖에 끝나 버렸어."], ["Marfa Petrovna accidentally overheard her husband imploring Dounia in the garden, and, putting quite a wrong interpretation on the position, threw the blame upon her, believing her to be the cause of it all.", "마르파 페트로브나가 우연히 정원에서 자기 남편이 두냐에게 애원하는 것을 엿듣고는, 상황을 완전히 잘못 해석해서 모든 책임을 그애에게 돌리고, 그애가 모든 일의 원인이라고 믿어 버렸단다. 그 자리에서, 정원에서 그들 사이에 끔찍한 소동이 벌어졌어."], ["An awful scene took place between them on the spot in the garden; Marfa Petrovna went so far as to strike Dounia, refused to hear anything and was shouting at her for a whole hour and then gave orders that Dounia should be packed off at once to me in a plain peasant’s cart, into which they flung all her things, her linen and her clothes, all pell-mell, without folding it up and packing it.", "마르파 페트로브나는 두냐를 때리기까지 했고, 아무 말도 들으려 하지 않은 채 꼬박 한 시간 동안 그애에게 소리를 질러 댔단다. 그러고는 두냐를 당장 허름한 농사꾼 짐마차에 태워 나에게 보내라고 명령했지, 그 마차에 그애의 짐이며 속옷이며 옷가지를 죄다, 개거나 챙기지도 않고 마구잡이로 뒤죽박죽 던져 넣었단다."], ["And a heavy shower of rain came on, too, and Dounia, insulted and put to shame, had to drive with a peasant in an open cart all the seventeen versts into town.", "게다가 억수같이 소나기까지 쏟아졌는데, 모욕당하고 수치를 당한 두냐는 덮개도 없는 마차에 농사꾼과 함께 타고 읍내까지 십칠 베르스타를 내내 달려와야 했단다."], ["Only think now what answer could I have sent to the letter I received from you two months ago and what could I have written?", "자, 이제 생각해 보렴, 두 달 전에 네게서 받은 그 편지에 내가 무어라 답을 보낼 수 있었겠으며, 무어라 쓸 수 있었겠니?"], ["I was in despair; I dared not write to you the truth because you would have been very unhappy, mortified and indignant, and yet what could you do?", "나는 절망에 빠져 있었단다. 너에게 진실을 차마 써 보낼 수 없었던 건, 그랬다간 네가 몹시 불행하고 비통하고 분개했을 테니까, 그렇다고 네가 무얼 할 수 있었겠니?"], ["You could only perhaps ruin yourself, and, besides, Dounia would not allow it; and fill up my letter with trifles when my heart was so full of sorrow, I could not.", "기껏해야 너 자신을 망쳐 버릴 수 있었을 뿐이지, 게다가 두냐가 그걸 허락하지도 않았을 테고. 그렇다고 내 마음이 그토록 슬픔으로 가득 차 있는데 자질구레한 이야기로 편지를 채울 수도 없었단다."], ["For a whole month the town was full of gossip about this scandal, and it came to such a pass that Dounia and I dared not even go to church on account of the contemptuous looks, whispers, and even remarks made aloud about us.", "꼬박 한 달 동안 읍내는 이 추문에 관한 소문으로 들끓었고, 급기야 두냐와 나는 경멸 어린 시선과 수군거림, 심지어 우리에 대한 노골적인 험담 때문에 교회에조차 갈 엄두를 못 낼 지경에 이르렀단다."], ["All our acquaintances avoided us, nobody even bowed to us in the street, and I learnt that some shopmen and clerks were intending to insult us in a shameful way, smearing the gates of our house with pitch, so that the landlord began to tell us we must leave.", "아는 사람들은 모두 우리를 피했고, 길에서 인사를 건네는 이조차 없었으며, 어떤 가게 점원들과 사무원들이 우리 집 대문에 콜타르를 칠하는 그런 치욕적인 방법으로 우리를 모욕하려 한다는 말까지 들렸단다, 그래서 집주인이 우리더러 집을 비우라고 말하기 시작했지."], ["All this was set going by Marfa Petrovna who managed to slander Dounia and throw dirt at her in every family.", "이 모든 일은 마르파 페트로브나가 꾸민 짓이었어, 그분이 집집마다 다니며 두냐를 헐뜯고 진흙탕에 처박았던 거야."], ["She knows everyone in the neighbourhood, and that month she was continually coming into the town, and as she is rather talkative and fond of gossiping about her family affairs and particularly of complaining to all and each of her husband--which is not at all right--so in a short time she had spread her story not only in the town, but over the whole surrounding district.", "그분은 이웃 사람들을 죄다 알고, 그달에는 끊임없이 읍내로 나왔는데, 워낙 말이 많은 데다 자기 집안일을 떠벌리기를 좋아하고, 특히 자기 남편에 대한 불평을 만나는 사람마다 늘어놓는 — 도무지 옳지 못한 일이지 — 버릇이 있어서, 순식간에 자기 이야기를 읍내뿐 아니라 인근 일대에 죄다 퍼뜨려 버렸단다."], ["It made me ill, but Dounia bore it better than I did, and if only you could have seen how she endured it all and tried to comfort me and cheer me up!", "그 때문에 나는 앓아눕고 말았지만, 두냐는 나보다 더 잘 견뎌 냈단다, 그애가 그 모든 걸 어떻게 견디며 나를 위로하고 북돋아 주려 애썼는지 네가 보았더라면!"], ["She is an angel!", "그애는 천사란다!"], ["But by God’s mercy, our sufferings were cut short: Mr.", "하지만 하느님의 자비로 우리의 고통은 짧게 끝났어."], ["Svidrigaïlov returned to his senses and repented and, probably feeling sorry for Dounia, he laid before Marfa Petrovna a complete and unmistakable proof of Dounia’s innocence, in the form of a letter Dounia had been forced to write and give to him, before Marfa Petrovna came upon them in the garden. This letter, which remained in Mr.", "스비드리가일로프 씨가 제정신을 차리고 뉘우치고는, 아마도 두냐가 가엾어서였겠지, 마르파 페트로브나 앞에 두냐의 결백을 완전하고도 명백하게 증명하는 것을 내놓았단다, 마르파 페트로브나가 정원에서 그들을 맞닥뜨리기 전에 두냐가 어쩔 수 없이 써서 그에게 건넸던 편지의 형태로 말이야."], ["Svidrigaïlov’s hands after her departure, she had written to refuse personal explanations and secret interviews, for which he was entreating her.", "그애가 떠난 뒤 스비드리가일로프 씨의 손에 남아 있던 이 편지에서, 그애는 그가 애원하던 직접적인 해명이나 은밀한 만남을 거절했던 거야."], ["In that letter she reproached him with great heat and indignation for the baseness of his behaviour in regard to Marfa Petrovna, reminding him that he was the father and head of a family and telling him how infamous it was of him to torment and make unhappy a defenceless girl, unhappy enough already.", "그 편지에서 그애는, 마르파 페트로브나에 대한 그의 비열한 행동을 두고 격렬한 분노로 그를 책망하면서, 그가 한 집안의 아버지요 가장임을 일깨우고, 이미 충분히 불행한 무력한 처녀를 괴롭히고 불행하게 만드는 것이 얼마나 파렴치한 짓인지 말했단다."], ["Indeed, dear Rodya, the letter was so nobly and touchingly written that I sobbed when I read it and to this day I cannot read it without tears.", "정말이지, 사랑하는 로쟈, 그 편지는 어찌나 고결하고 가슴 뭉클하게 쓰였던지, 나는 그것을 읽으며 흐느꼈고 지금까지도 눈물 없이는 읽지를 못한단다."], ["Moreover, the evidence of the servants, too, cleared Dounia’s reputation; they had seen and known a great deal more than Mr.", "게다가 하인들의 증언도 두냐의 명예를 회복시켜 주었어."], ["Svidrigaïlov had himself supposed--as indeed is always the case with servants.", "그들은 스비드리가일로프 씨 자신이 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 더 많은 것을 보고 알고 있었거든 — 하인들이란 늘 그런 법이지."], ["Marfa Petrovna was completely taken aback, and ‘again crushed’ as she said herself to us, but she was completely convinced of Dounia’s innocence.", "마르파 페트로브나는 완전히 허를 찔려, 자기 입으로 우리에게 말했듯이 '또다시 무너져 버렸지만', 두냐의 결백을 온전히 확신하게 되었단다."], ["The very next day, being Sunday, she went straight to the Cathedral, knelt down and prayed with tears to Our Lady to give her strength to bear this new trial and to do her duty.", "바로 다음 날, 일요일이었는데, 그분은 곧장 대성당으로 가서 무릎을 꿇고 눈물로 성모님께 기도드렸어, 이 새로운 시련을 견디고 자기 도리를 다할 힘을 달라고 말이야."], ["Then she came straight from the Cathedral to us, told us the whole story, wept bitterly and, fully penitent, she embraced Dounia and besought her to forgive her.", "그러고는 대성당에서 곧장 우리에게 와서 모든 이야기를 털어놓고, 비통하게 울며, 완전히 뉘우치고는 두냐를 끌어안고 용서해 달라고 빌었단다."], ["The same morning without any delay, she went round to all the houses in the town and everywhere, shedding tears, she asserted in the most flattering terms Dounia’s innocence and the nobility of her feelings and her behavior.", "같은 날 아침, 그분은 지체 없이 읍내의 집집마다 돌아다니며, 가는 곳마다 눈물을 흘리면서 더없이 칭송하는 말로 두냐의 결백과 그애 감정의 고결함, 그애 행동의 고귀함을 단언했단다."], ["What was more, she showed and read to everyone the letter in Dounia’s own handwriting to Mr. Svidrigaïlov and even allowed them to take copies of it--which I must say I think was superfluous.", "그뿐만 아니라 그분은 두냐가 스비드리가일로프 씨에게 직접 쓴 그 편지를 모두에게 보여 주고 읽어 주었으며, 심지어 그것을 베껴 가도록까지 허락했단다 — 솔직히 나로서는 그것까지는 지나친 일이었다고 생각하지만 말이야."], ["In this way she was busy for several days in driving about the whole town, because some people had taken offence through precedence having been given to others.", "이런 식으로 그분은 며칠 동안이나 읍내 전체를 마차로 돌아다니느라 바빴는데, 어떤 사람들은 자기보다 다른 사람들이 먼저 들었다고 기분 상해했기 때문이지."], ["And therefore they had to take turns, so that in every house she was expected before she arrived, and everyone knew that on such and such a day Marfa Petrovna would be reading the letter in such and such a place and people assembled for every reading of it, even many who had heard it several times already both in their own houses and in other people’s.", "그래서 차례를 정해야 했고, 그분이 도착하기도 전에 집집마다 그분을 기다렸으며, 아무 날 아무 데서 마르파 페트로브나가 그 편지를 읽으리라는 것을 다들 알고서 그것을 읽을 때마다 사람들이 모여들었단다, 이미 자기 집에서며 남의 집에서 여러 번 들은 사람들까지 많이 말이야."], ["In my opinion a great deal, a very great deal of all this was unnecessary; but that’s Marfa Petrovna’s character.", "내 생각에는 이 모든 게 상당히, 아주 상당히 불필요한 일이었지만, 그게 마르파 페트로브나의 성격인 걸 어쩌겠니."], ["Anyway she succeeded in completely re-establishing Dounia’s reputation and the whole ignominy of this affair rested as an indelible disgrace upon her husband, as the only person to blame, so that I really began to feel sorry for him; it was really treating the crazy fellow too harshly.", "어쨌든 그분은 두냐의 명예를 완전히 회복시키는 데 성공했고, 이 일의 온갖 치욕은 유일한 죄인인 그분 남편에게 지울 수 없는 오명으로 고스란히 남게 되었단다, 그래서 나는 도리어 그가 가엾어지기 시작할 정도였지. 그 미친 사람을 너무 가혹하게 대하는 게 아닌가 싶어서 말이야."], ["Dounia was at once asked to give lessons in several families, but she refused.", "두냐는 곧바로 여러 집안에서 가정교사를 해 달라는 청을 받았지만 거절했단다."], ["All of a sudden everyone began to treat her with marked respect and all this did much to bring about the event by which, one may say, our whole fortunes are now transformed.", "갑자기 모두가 그애를 눈에 띄게 존중하며 대하기 시작했고, 이 모든 것이, 말하자면 지금 우리의 운명 전체를 뒤바꿔 놓은 그 일을 이루는 데 크게 한몫을 했단다."], ["You must know, dear Rodya, that Dounia has a suitor and that she has already consented to marry him.", "사랑하는 로쟈, 너는 알아야 한다, 두냐에게 청혼자가 생겼고 그애가 이미 그와 결혼하기로 승낙했다는 것을."], ["I hasten to tell you all about the matter, and though it has been arranged without asking your consent, I think you will not be aggrieved with me or with your sister on that account, for you will see that we could not wait and put off our decision till we heard from you.", "서둘러 이 일을 죄다 너에게 알리려 한다만, 비록 너의 동의를 구하지 않고 정해진 일이라 해도, 너는 그 일로 나나 네 누이를 원망하지 않으리라 생각한다, 우리가 너의 소식을 들을 때까지 결정을 미루며 기다릴 수 없었다는 것을 너도 알 테니까."], ["And you could not have judged all the facts without being on the spot.", "게다가 너는 현장에 있지 않고서는 모든 사정을 다 헤아릴 수 없었을 거다."], ["This was how it happened.", "일이 이렇게 된 거란다."], ["He is already of the rank of a counsellor, Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin, and is distantly related to Marfa Petrovna, who has been very active in bringing the match about.", "그분은 이미 고문관 직위에 있는 표트르 페트로비치 루진이라는 분으로, 마르파 페트로브나와 먼 친척뻘인데, 그분이 이 혼사를 맺는 데 무척 적극적으로 나서 주었단다."], ["It began with his expressing through her his desire to make our acquaintance.", "그분이 마르파 페트로브나를 통해 우리와 알고 지내고 싶다는 뜻을 표한 데서 시작되었어."], ["He was properly received, drank coffee with us and the very next day he sent us a letter in which he very courteously made an offer and begged for a speedy and decided answer.", "그분은 정중하게 영접을 받았고, 우리와 함께 커피를 마셨으며, 바로 그다음 날 우리에게 편지를 보내 무척 공손하게 청혼하면서 빠르고 분명한 답을 청했단다."], ["He is a very busy man and is in a great hurry to get to Petersburg, so that every moment is precious to him.", "그분은 무척 바쁜 사람이고, 페테르부르크로 가야 해서 몹시 서두르고 있어, 그래서 그분에게는 한순간 한순간이 소중하단다."], ["At first, of course, we were greatly surprised, as it had all happened so quickly and unexpectedly.", "물론 처음에 우리는 모든 일이 너무도 빠르고 뜻밖에 일어나서 몹시 놀랐지."], ["We thought and talked it over the whole day.", "하루 종일 그 일을 곰곰이 생각하고 의논했단다."], ["He is a well-to-do man, to be depended upon, he has two posts in the government and has already made his fortune.", "그분은 살림이 넉넉하고 믿을 만한 사람으로, 관청에 두 자리를 맡고 있고 이미 재산도 모았단다."], ["It is true that he is forty-five years old, but he is of a fairly prepossessing appearance and might still be thought attractive by women, and he is altogether a very respectable and presentable man, only he seems a little morose and somewhat conceited.", "마흔다섯이라는 게 사실이긴 하지만, 그분은 꽤 호감 가는 풍모에 아직도 여자들이 매력을 느낄 만하고, 대체로 무척 점잖고 풍채 좋은 사람이야, 다만 조금 음울하고 다소 거만해 보이긴 한다만."], ["But possibly that may only be the impression he makes at first sight.", "하지만 어쩌면 그건 첫인상에 불과할지도 모르지."], ["And beware, dear Rodya, when he comes to Petersburg, as he shortly will do, beware of judging him too hastily and severely, as your way is, if there is anything you do not like in him at first sight.", "그리고 사랑하는 로쟈, 그분이 곧 그러겠지만 페테르부르크에 오거든, 첫눈에 그분에게서 무언가 마음에 들지 않는 점이 있더라도, 네 버릇대로 너무 성급하고 가혹하게 그를 판단하지 않도록 조심하렴."], ["I give you this warning, although I feel sure that he will make a favourable impression upon you.", "그분이 너에게 좋은 인상을 주리라 확신하기는 하지만, 그래도 이렇게 미리 일러두는 거란다."], ["Moreover, in order to understand any man one must be deliberate and careful to avoid forming prejudices and mistaken ideas, which are very difficult to correct and get over afterwards.", "게다가 어떤 사람이든 이해하려면 신중하고 조심스러워야 하고, 나중에 바로잡고 떨쳐 내기가 무척 어려운 편견과 그릇된 생각을 갖지 않도록 해야 하는 법이지."], ["And Pyotr Petrovitch, judging by many indications, is a thoroughly estimable man.", "그리고 표트르 페트로비치는 여러 정황으로 미루어 보건대 진실로 존경할 만한 사람이란다."], ["At his first visit, indeed, he told us that he was a practical man, but still he shares, as he expressed it, many of the convictions ‘of our most rising generation’ and he is an opponent of all prejudices.", "사실 첫 방문 때 그분은 자기가 현실적인 사람이라고 말하면서도, 그분 표현으로는 '우리의 가장 성장하는 세대'의 신념 가운데 많은 것을 공유하며 온갖 편견에 반대한다고 했단다."], ["He said a good deal more, for he seems a little conceited and likes to be listened to, but this is scarcely a vice.", "그분은 그 밖에도 말을 많이 했는데, 조금 거만해서 남들이 자기 말에 귀 기울여 주는 걸 좋아하는 듯했지만, 그게 무슨 큰 흠이야 되겠니."], ["I, of course, understood very little of it, but Dounia explained to me that, though he is not a man of great education, he is clever and seems to be good-natured.", "나는 물론 그 말을 거의 이해하지 못했지만, 두냐가 설명해 주기를, 그분은 대단히 교육받은 사람은 아니어도 영리하고 마음씨도 좋아 보인다고 하더구나."], ["You know your sister’s character, Rodya.", "너도 네 누이의 성격을 알지, 로쟈."], ["She is a resolute, sensible, patient and generous girl, but she has a passionate heart, as I know very well.", "그애는 단호하고 분별 있고 인내심 강하고 너그러운 아이지만, 내가 잘 알다시피 정열적인 가슴을 지녔단다."], ["Of course, there is no great love either on his side, or on hers, but Dounia is a clever girl and has the heart of an angel, and will make it her duty to make her husband happy who on his side will make her happiness his care.", "물론 그분 쪽에도 그애 쪽에도 대단한 사랑이 있는 건 아니지만, 두냐는 영리한 아이고 천사 같은 마음을 지녔으니, 남편을 행복하게 해 주는 것을 자기 도리로 삼을 게다, 그러면 그분도 그분대로 그애의 행복을 보살피는 것을 자기 일로 삼겠지."], ["Of that we have no good reason to doubt, though it must be admitted the matter has been arranged in great haste.", "그 점은 의심할 만한 이유가 없단다, 비록 일이 무척 서둘러 정해진 건 인정해야겠지만 말이야."], ["Besides he is a man of great prudence and he will see, to be sure, of himself, that his own happiness will be the more secure, the happier Dounia is with him.", "게다가 그분은 매우 신중한 사람이니, 분명 스스로 알 거다, 두냐가 그와 함께 행복할수록 자기 자신의 행복도 더욱 든든해지리라는 것을."], ["And as for some defects of character, for some habits and even certain differences of opinion--which indeed are inevitable even in the happiest marriages--Dounia has said that, as regards all that, she relies on herself, that there is nothing to be uneasy about, and that she is ready to put up with a great deal, if only their future relationship can be an honourable and straightforward one.", "그리고 성격상의 몇 가지 결함이나 어떤 버릇, 심지어 의견의 차이 같은 것은 — 사실 그런 거야 더없이 행복한 결혼에서도 피할 수 없는 거지만 — 그 모든 것에 관해서는 두냐가 자기 자신을 믿으니 불안해할 것이 없고, 두 사람의 앞날의 관계가 정직하고 떳떳하기만 하다면 많은 것을 참아 낼 각오가 되어 있다고 하더구나."], ["He struck me, for instance, at first, as rather abrupt, but that may well come from his being an outspoken man, and that is no doubt how it is.", "예컨대 그분은 처음에 나에게 다소 퉁명스러운 사람으로 비쳤지만, 그건 충분히 솔직한 사람이기 때문일 수도 있고, 틀림없이 그래서일 게다."], ["For instance, at his second visit, after he had received Dounia’s consent, in the course of conversation, he declared that before making Dounia’s acquaintance, he had made up his mind to marry a girl of good reputation, without dowry and, above all, one who had experienced poverty, because, as he explained, a man ought not to be indebted to his wife, but that it is better for a wife to look upon her husband as her benefactor.", "예를 들면, 두냐의 승낙을 받은 뒤 두 번째 방문 때, 이야기를 나누던 중에 그분이 이런 말을 하더구나, 두냐와 알게 되기 전부터 평판 좋고 지참금 없는, 무엇보다 가난을 겪어 본 처녀와 결혼하기로 마음먹었다고 말이야, 왜냐하면 그분 설명으로는, 남편이 아내에게 신세를 져서는 안 되고, 아내가 남편을 자기 은인으로 우러러보는 편이 낫기 때문이라는 거였단다."], ["I must add that he expressed it more nicely and politely than I have done, for I have forgotten his actual phrases and only remember the meaning.", "덧붙여 두자면 그분은 내가 옮긴 것보다 훨씬 더 점잖고 정중하게 표현했단다, 나는 그분이 실제로 한 말은 잊어버리고 그저 그 뜻만 기억하고 있으니까."], ["And, besides, it was obviously not said of design, but slipped out in the heat of conversation, so that he tried afterwards to correct himself and smooth it over, but all the same it did strike me as somewhat rude, and I said so afterwards to Dounia.", "게다가 그건 분명 일부러 한 말이 아니라 이야기에 열을 올리다 무심코 튀어나온 거였어, 그래서 나중에는 그분도 그것을 바로잡고 무마하려 애쓰긴 했지만, 그래도 나에게는 어쩐지 좀 무례하게 들렸고, 나중에 두냐에게 그렇게 말했단다."], ["But Dounia was vexed, and answered that ‘words are not deeds,’ and that, of course, is perfectly true.", "하지만 두냐는 언짢아하면서, '말은 행동이 아니'라고 대꾸하더구나, 물론 그건 더없이 옳은 말이지."], ["Dounia did not sleep all night before she made up her mind, and, thinking that I was asleep, she got out of bed and was walking up and down the room all night; at last she knelt down before the ikon and prayed long and fervently and in the morning she told me that she had decided.", "두냐는 마음을 정하기 전 밤새도록 잠을 이루지 못했단다, 내가 잠든 줄 알고 침대에서 일어나 밤새도록 방 안을 오락가락하더니, 마침내 성상 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 오래도록 간절히 기도드렸고, 아침에 나에게 결심했다고 말했단다."]], [["“I have mentioned already that Pyotr Petrovitch is just setting off for Petersburg, where he has a great deal of business, and he wants to open a legal bureau.", "\"이미 말했다시피 표트르 페트로비치는 막 페테르부르크로 떠나려는 참이고, 그곳에 처리할 일이 무척 많아서 법률 사무소를 열려고 한단다."], ["He has been occupied for many years in conducting civil and commercial litigation, and only the other day he won an important case.", "그분은 여러 해 동안 민사 및 상사 소송을 맡아 왔고, 바로 며칠 전에도 중요한 소송 하나를 이겼지."], ["He has to be in Petersburg because he has an important case before the Senate.", "그분이 페테르부르크에 가야 하는 것은, 원로원에 걸려 있는 중요한 사건이 있어서란다."], ["So, Rodya dear, he may be of the greatest use to you, in every way indeed, and Dounia and I have agreed that from this very day you could definitely enter upon your career and might consider that your future is marked out and assured for you.", "그러니, 사랑하는 로쟈, 그분이 너에게 더없이 큰 도움이 될 수 있을 거다, 정말이지 온갖 면에서 말이야, 그래서 두냐와 나는 바로 오늘부터 네가 분명히 너의 앞길에 발을 들여놓을 수 있고, 너의 장래가 너를 위해 정해지고 보장되었다고 여겨도 된다고 의견을 모았단다."], ["Oh, if only this comes to pass!", "오, 부디 이 일이 이루어진다면!"], ["This would be such a benefit that we could only look upon it as a providential blessing.", "이것은 우리가 그저 하늘의 축복으로밖에 여길 수 없을 만큼 크나큰 은혜가 될 거다."], ["Dounia is dreaming of nothing else.", "두냐는 다른 건 아무것도 꿈꾸지 않는단다."], ["We have even ventured already to drop a few words on the subject to Pyotr Petrovitch.", "우리는 이미 그 일에 관해 표트르 페트로비치에게 몇 마디 슬쩍 꺼내 보기까지 했어."], ["He was cautious in his answer, and said that, of course, as he could not get on without a secretary, it would be better to be paying a salary to a relation than to a stranger, if only the former were fitted for the duties (as though there could be doubt of your being fitted!) but then he expressed doubts whether your studies at the university would leave you time for work at his office.", "그분은 신중하게 대답하기를, 물론 자기는 비서 없이는 일을 해 나갈 수 없으니, 그 직무에 적합하기만 하다면 낯선 사람보다는 친척에게 봉급을 주는 편이 낫다고 하더구나(마치 네가 그 일에 적합한지 의심할 여지라도 있다는 듯이 말이야!), 하지만 그러고는 너의 대학 공부 때문에 자기 사무소에서 일할 시간이 나겠느냐는 의문을 내비쳤단다."], ["The matter dropped for the time, but Dounia is thinking of nothing else now.", "그 이야기는 일단 그쯤에서 그쳤지만, 두냐는 지금 다른 건 아무것도 생각하지 않는단다."], ["She has been in a sort of fever for the last few days, and has already made a regular plan for your becoming in the end an associate and even a partner in Pyotr Petrovitch’s business, which might well be, seeing that you are a student of law.", "그애는 요 며칠 동안 일종의 열병에 들뜬 상태로, 네가 끝내는 표트르 페트로비치 사업의 동업자, 심지어 공동 경영자가 되도록 하는 정식 계획까지 벌써 세워 놓았어, 네가 법학도이니 충분히 가능한 일이지."], ["I am in complete agreement with her, Rodya, and share all her plans and hopes, and think there is every probability of realising them.", "나도 그애와 전적으로 같은 생각이란다, 로쟈, 그애의 모든 계획과 희망을 함께 나누며, 그것을 실현할 가능성이 충분하다고 본다."], ["And in spite of Pyotr Petrovitch’s evasiveness, very natural at present (since he does not know you), Dounia is firmly persuaded that she will gain everything by her good influence over her future husband; this she is reckoning upon.", "그리고 표트르 페트로비치의 회피하는 태도에도 불구하고 — 지금으로서는 (그분이 너를 모르니) 지극히 당연한 일이지 — 두냐는 자기가 미래의 남편에게 미칠 좋은 영향력으로 모든 것을 얻어 내리라 굳게 믿고 있단다, 그애는 그것을 믿고 있어."], ["Of course we are careful not to talk of any of these more remote plans to Pyotr Petrovitch, especially of your becoming his partner.", "물론 우리는 이런 좀 더 먼 계획들, 특히 네가 그분의 동업자가 되는 일은 표트르 페트로비치에게 입 밖에 내지 않도록 조심하고 있어."], ["He is a practical man and might take this very coldly, it might all seem to him simply a day-dream.", "그분은 현실적인 사람이라 이 일을 아주 냉담하게 받아들일 수도 있고, 그 모든 게 그분에게는 그저 백일몽처럼 비칠지도 모르니까."], ["Nor has either Dounia or I breathed a word to him of the great hopes we have of his helping us to pay for your university studies; we have not spoken of it in the first place, because it will come to pass of itself, later on, and he will no doubt without wasting words offer to do it of himself, (as though he could refuse Dounia that) the more readily since you may by your own efforts become his right hand in the office, and receive this assistance not as a charity, but as a salary earned by your own work.", "또 두냐도 나도, 그분이 너의 대학 학비를 대 주는 일을 도와주리라는 우리의 큰 희망에 대해서도 그분에게 한마디도 비치지 않았단다. 그 이야기를 하지 않은 것은, 우선 그 일이 나중에 저절로 이루어질 것이고, 그분도 틀림없이 군말 없이 스스로 그렇게 해 주겠다고 나설 테니까(마치 그분이 두냐에게 그것을 거절할 수 있기라도 하겠니), 더구나 네가 너 자신의 노력으로 사무소에서 그분의 오른팔이 될 수 있으니, 이 도움을 자선이 아니라 네가 일해서 번 봉급으로 받게 될 테니 더욱 그렇지."], ["Dounia wants to arrange it all like this and I quite agree with her.", "두냐는 이 모든 걸 이렇게 짜 놓고 싶어 하고, 나도 그애와 전적으로 같은 생각이란다."], ["And we have not spoken of our plans for another reason, that is, because I particularly wanted you to feel on an equal footing when you first meet him.", "우리가 이 계획들을 말하지 않은 데는 또 다른 이유도 있는데, 그건 네가 그분을 처음 만날 때 대등한 처지에 있다고 느끼기를 내가 특히 바랐기 때문이야."], ["When Dounia spoke to him with enthusiasm about you, he answered that one could never judge of a man without seeing him close, for oneself, and that he looked forward to forming his own opinion when he makes your acquaintance.", "두냐가 그분에게 너에 대해 열을 올리며 이야기했을 때, 그분은 어떤 사람이든 직접 가까이서 자기 눈으로 보지 않고서는 결코 판단할 수 없다고, 너와 알게 되면 자기 나름의 견해를 갖게 되기를 기대한다고 대답했단다."], ["Do you know, my precious Rodya, I think that perhaps for some reasons (nothing to do with Pyotr Petrovitch though, simply for my own personal, perhaps old-womanish, fancies) I should do better to go on living by myself, apart, than with them, after the wedding.", "그런데 있잖니, 소중한 로쟈, 나는 어쩌면 어떤 이유들로(표트르 페트로비치와는 아무 상관 없는, 그저 나 개인의, 어쩌면 늙은이다운 부질없는 생각이긴 하다만), 결혼식 후에 그들과 함께 사는 것보다 따로 떨어져 혼자 사는 편이 나을 것 같구나."], ["I am convinced that he will be generous and delicate enough to invite me and to urge me to remain with my daughter for the future, and if he has said nothing about it hitherto, it is simply because it has been taken for granted; but I shall refuse.", "나는 그분이 너그럽고 세심한 마음으로 나를 청해서, 앞으로 딸과 함께 지내라고 권하리라 믿고, 만일 그분이 지금껏 그 말을 하지 않았다면 그저 그것이 당연한 일로 여겨졌기 때문이겠지만, 나는 사양할 작정이다."], ["I have noticed more than once in my life that husbands don’t quite get on with their mothers-in-law, and I don’t want to be the least bit in anyone’s way, and for my own sake, too, would rather be quite independent, so long as I have a crust of bread of my own, and such children as you and Dounia.", "나는 살아오면서 사위들이 장모와는 그리 잘 지내지 못하는 것을 한두 번 본 게 아니고, 나는 누구에게도 조금이라도 거치적거리는 사람이 되고 싶지 않으며, 나 자신을 위해서도, 내 빵 한 조각과 너와 두냐 같은 자식들이 있는 한, 차라리 완전히 독립해 있고 싶구나."], ["If possible, I would settle somewhere near you, for the most joyful piece of news, dear Rodya, I have kept for the end of my letter: know then, my dear boy, that we may, perhaps, be all together in a very short time and may embrace one another again after a separation of almost three years!", "가능하다면 너희 가까이에 자리를 잡고 싶다, 그런데 사랑하는 로쟈, 가장 기쁜 소식은 편지 끝까지 아껴 두었단다. 그러니 알아 두렴, 사랑하는 내 아들아, 우리는 어쩌면 머지않아 다 함께 모여, 거의 삼 년 만의 이별 끝에 다시 서로를 끌어안게 될지도 모른단다!"], ["It is settled _for certain_ that Dounia and I are to set off for Petersburg, exactly when I don’t know, but very, very soon, possibly in a week.", "두냐와 내가 페테르부르크로 떠나기로 분명히 정해졌단다, 정확히 언제일지는 모르지만, 아주, 아주 곧, 어쩌면 일주일 안에 말이야."], ["It all depends on Pyotr Petrovitch who will let us know when he has had time to look round him in Petersburg.", "모든 것은 표트르 페트로비치에게 달렸는데, 그분이 페테르부르크에서 둘러볼 짬이 생기는 대로 우리에게 알려 줄 거란다."], ["To suit his own arrangements he is anxious to have the ceremony as soon as possible, even before the fast of Our Lady, if it could be managed, or if that is too soon to be ready, immediately after.", "그분은 자기 사정에 맞추려고 가능한 한 빨리 식을 올리고 싶어 해, 될 수만 있다면 성모 축일 단식 전이라도, 혹은 그게 준비하기에 너무 이르다면 단식이 끝나는 즉시 말이야."], ["Oh, with what happiness I shall press you to my heart!", "오, 내가 얼마나 행복하게 너를 가슴에 끌어안게 될지!"], ["Dounia is all excitement at the joyful thought of seeing you, she said one day in joke that she would be ready to marry Pyotr Petrovitch for that alone.", "두냐는 너를 만난다는 기쁜 생각에 온통 들떠서, 언젠가 농담 삼아 그것만을 위해서라도 표트르 페트로비치와 결혼할 수 있겠다고 하더구나."], ["She is an angel!", "그애는 천사란다!"], ["She is not writing anything to you now, and has only told me to write that she has so much, so much to tell you that she is not going to take up her pen now, for a few lines would tell you nothing, and it would only mean upsetting herself; she bids me send you her love and innumerable kisses.", "그애는 지금 너에게 아무것도 써 보내지 않으면서, 그저 나더러 이렇게 전하라고만 하는구나, 너에게 할 이야기가 너무도, 너무도 많아서 지금은 펜을 들지 않겠다고, 몇 줄로는 아무것도 전할 수 없을 테고 괜히 자기 마음만 어지럽힐 뿐이라고 말이야. 그애가 너에게 사랑과 수없는 입맞춤을 보내라고 하는구나."], ["But although we shall be meeting so soon, perhaps I shall send you as much money as I can in a day or two.", "하지만 우리가 곧 만나게 될 텐데도, 어쩌면 하루 이틀 안에 내가 보낼 수 있는 만큼 돈을 부쳐 줄지도 모르겠다."], ["Now that everyone has heard that Dounia is to marry Pyotr Petrovitch, my credit has suddenly improved and I know that Afanasy Ivanovitch will trust me now even to seventy-five roubles on the security of my pension, so that perhaps I shall be able to send you twenty-five or even thirty roubles.", "두냐가 표트르 페트로비치와 결혼한다는 소식을 다들 듣고 나니 내 신용이 갑자기 좋아져서, 아파나시 이바노비치가 이제는 내 연금을 담보로 칠십오 루블까지도 빌려줄 거라는 걸 알겠구나, 그러니 어쩌면 너에게 이십오 루블, 아니 삼십 루블까지도 보낼 수 있을 게다."], ["I would send you more, but I am uneasy about our travelling expenses; for though Pyotr Petrovitch has been so kind as to undertake part of the expenses of the journey, that is to say, he has taken upon himself the conveyance of our bags and big trunk (which will be conveyed through some acquaintances of his), we must reckon upon some expense on our arrival in Petersburg, where we can’t be left without a halfpenny, at least for the first few days.", "더 보내고 싶다만 우리 여비가 걱정이구나. 표트르 페트로비치가 친절히도 여행 경비의 일부를, 그러니까 우리의 가방과 큰 트렁크 운반을(이건 그분이 아는 사람들 편으로 보내질 거란다) 맡아 주긴 했지만, 페테르부르크에 도착하면 어느 정도 비용이 들 것을 셈해 둬야지, 적어도 처음 며칠은 한 푼도 없이 지낼 수는 없을 테니까."], ["But we have calculated it all, Dounia and I, to the last penny, and we see that the journey will not cost very much.", "하지만 두냐와 나는 마지막 한 푼까지 다 계산해 보았고, 여행에 그리 큰돈이 들지는 않으리라는 걸 알았단다."], ["It is only ninety versts from us to the railway and we have come to an agreement with a driver we know, so as to be in readiness; and from there Dounia and I can travel quite comfortably third class.", "우리 집에서 철도까지는 구십 베르스타밖에 안 되고, 우리가 아는 마부와 미리 이야기를 맞춰 두었으니까. 그리고 거기서부터는 두냐와 내가 삼등칸으로 아주 편안히 갈 수 있단다."], ["So that I may very likely be able to send to you not twenty-five, but thirty roubles.", "그러니 어쩌면 너에게 이십오 루블이 아니라 삼십 루블을 보낼 수 있을 것 같구나. 하지만 이만 줄여야겠다."], ["But enough; I have covered two sheets already and there is no space left for more; our whole history, but so many events have happened!", "벌써 두 장을 다 채워서 더 쓸 자리가 없구나. 우리의 모든 이야기를 다 적었다만, 어쩌면 이리도 많은 일이 일어났는지!"], ["And now, my precious Rodya, I embrace you and send you a mother’s blessing till we meet.", "그럼, 소중한 로쟈, 나는 너를 끌어안으며 다시 만날 때까지 어미의 축복을 보낸다."], ["Love Dounia your sister, Rodya; love her as she loves you and understand that she loves you beyond everything, more than herself.", "네 누이 두냐를 사랑하렴, 로쟈, 그애가 너를 사랑하는 만큼 그애를 사랑하고, 그애가 그 무엇보다도, 자기 자신보다도 더 너를 사랑한다는 것을 헤아려 주렴."], ["She is an angel and you, Rodya, you are everything to us--our one hope, our one consolation.", "그애는 천사고, 너는, 로쟈, 너는 우리의 전부란다 — 우리의 단 하나뿐인 희망, 단 하나뿐인 위안이지."], ["If only you are happy, we shall be happy.", "네가 행복하기만 하다면, 우리도 행복할 거란다."], ["Do you still say your prayers, Rodya, and believe in the mercy of our Creator and our Redeemer?", "너는 아직도 기도를 드리니, 로쟈, 그리고 우리의 창조주이자 구원자이신 분의 자비를 믿니?"], ["I am afraid in my heart that you may have been visited by the new spirit of infidelity that is abroad to-day; If it is so, I pray for you.", "나는 마음속으로 두렵구나, 요즘 세상에 퍼져 있는 그 새로운 불신의 정신이 행여 너에게 찾아오지나 않았을까. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 너를 위해 기도하마."], ["Remember, dear boy, how in your childhood, when your father was living, you used to lisp your prayers at my knee, and how happy we all were in those days.", "기억하렴, 사랑하는 아들아, 네 아버지가 살아 계시던 어린 시절에, 네가 내 무릎에 기대 혀 짧은 소리로 기도를 외던 것을, 그리고 그 시절 우리 모두가 얼마나 행복했는지를."], ["Good-bye, till we meet then--I embrace you warmly, warmly, with many kisses.", "잘 있으렴, 그럼 다시 만날 때까지 — 나는 너를 따뜻이, 따뜻이 끌어안고, 수없이 입을 맞추마."]], [["“Yours till death,", "\"죽는 날까지 너의,"]], [["Almost from the first, while he read the letter, Raskolnikov’s face was wet with tears; but when he finished it, his face was pale and distorted and a bitter, wrathful and malignant smile was on his lips.", "\"풀헤리야 라스콜니코바.\" 편지를 읽는 거의 처음부터 라스콜니코프의 얼굴은 눈물로 젖어 있었다."], ["He laid his head down on his threadbare dirty pillow and pondered, pondered a long time.", "그러나 다 읽고 나자 그의 얼굴은 창백하게 일그러졌고, 입술에는 쓰디쓰고 분노에 차고 악의에 찬 미소가 어렸다."], ["His heart was beating violently, and his brain was in a turmoil.", "그는 닳아빠진 더러운 베개에 머리를 누이고 골똘히, 오래도록 생각에 잠겼다."], ["At last he felt cramped and stifled in the little yellow room that was like a cupboard or a box.", "심장이 격렬하게 뛰었고, 머릿속은 온통 소용돌이쳤다. 마침내 그는 벽장이나 상자 같은 그 작은 노란 방 안에서 갑갑하고 숨이 막혀 왔다."], ["His eyes and his mind craved for space.", "그의 눈도 정신도 공간을 갈망했다."], ["He took up his hat and went out, this time without dread of meeting anyone; he had forgotten his dread.", "그는 모자를 집어 들고 밖으로 나갔는데, 이번에는 누군가와 마주칠까 두려워하지도 않았다. 그는 그 두려움을 잊고 있었다."], ["He turned in the direction of the Vassilyevsky Ostrov, walking along Vassilyevsky Prospect, as though hastening on some business, but he walked, as his habit was, without noticing his way, muttering and even speaking aloud to himself, to the astonishment of the passers-by.", "그는 바실리옙스키 섬 쪽으로 방향을 잡아 바실리옙스키 대로를 따라 걸었다, 마치 무슨 급한 볼일이라도 보러 가듯이. 그러나 그는 늘 그렇듯 자기가 가는 길도 의식하지 못한 채, 무언가를 중얼거리고 심지어 소리 내어 혼잣말까지 하면서 걸었고, 지나가는 사람들을 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["Many of them took him to be drunk.", "그들 중 많은 이들이 그를 술 취한 사람으로 여겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["His mother’s letter had been a torture to him, but as regards the chief fact in it, he had felt not one moment’s hesitation, even whilst he was reading the letter.", "어머니의 편지는 그에게 고문이었다. 그러나 거기에 담긴 핵심적인 사실에 관해서는, 편지를 읽고 있는 동안조차 단 한순간도 망설이지 않았다."], ["The essential question was settled, and irrevocably settled, in his mind: “Never such a marriage while I am alive and Mr.", "본질적인 문제는 그의 마음속에서 이미 결판이 났고, 그것도 돌이킬 수 없게 결판이 났다. \"내가 살아 있는 한 그런 결혼은 절대 안 돼, 루진 씨 따위는 지옥에나 떨어지라지!\""], ["Luzhin be damned!” “The thing is perfectly clear,”", "\"이건 너무도 명백한 일이야.\""], ["he muttered to himself, with a malignant smile anticipating the triumph of his decision.", "그는 자기 결정의 승리를 미리 음미하듯 악의에 찬 미소를 지으며 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"안 돼, 어머니, 안 돼요, 두냐, 당신들은 나를 속일 수 없어!"], ["“No, mother, no, Dounia, you won’t deceive me!", "게다가 내게 의논도 없이, 나를 빼놓고 결정해 버린 것을 사과하다니! 참 잘들 하셨군!"], ["and then they apologise for not asking my advice and for taking the decision without me!", "그들은 이미 다 정해졌으니 깨뜨릴 수 없다고 여기겠지. 하지만 깨뜨릴 수 있는지 없는지는 두고 봐야지!"], ["I dare say!", "근사한 핑계로군."], ["They imagine it is arranged now and can’t be broken off; but we will see whether it can or not!", "'표트르 페트로비치는 워낙 바쁜 분이라 결혼식조차 부랴부랴, 거의 급행으로 치러야 할 정도다.'"], ["A magnificent excuse: ‘Pyotr Petrovitch is such a busy man that even his wedding has to be in post-haste, almost by express.’ No, Dounia, I see it all and I know what you want to say to me; and I know too what you were thinking about, when you walked up and down all night, and what your prayers were like before the Holy Mother of Kazan who stands in mother’s bedroom.", "아니, 두냐, 나는 다 알아, 네가 내게 무슨 말을 하고 싶은지도 알고. 그리고 밤새도록 방 안을 서성였을 때 네가 무슨 생각을 했는지, 어머니 침실에 놓인 카잔의 성모 앞에서 무슨 기도를 올렸는지도 알지."], ["Bitter is the ascent to Golgotha.... Hm...", "골고다로 오르는 길은 쓰라린 법이니까……."], ["so it is finally settled; you have determined to marry a sensible business man, Avdotya Romanovna, one who has a fortune (has _already_ made his fortune, that is so much more solid and impressive), a man who holds two government posts and who shares the ideas of our most rising generation, as mother writes, and who _seems_ to be kind, as Dounia herself observes.", "흠……, 그래, 결국 다 정해졌단 말이지. 너는 분별 있는 사업가와 결혼하기로 마음을 굳혔구나, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 재산이 있는 자(그것도 이미 재산을 모은 자이니, 그게 훨씬 더 견실하고 인상적이지), 두 개의 관직을 차지하고, 어머니가 쓴 대로 우리의 가장 떠오르는 세대의 사상을 공유하며, 두냐 자신이 관찰한 대로 친절해 보이는 자와."], ["That _seems_ beats everything!", "그 보인다는 게 무엇보다 압권이로군!"], ["And that very Dounia for that very ‘_seems_’ is marrying him!", "게다가 바로 그 두냐가 바로 그 보인다라는 것 때문에 그와 결혼한다니!"], ["Splendid!", "훌륭하군!"], ["splendid!", "훌륭해!"]], [["“... But I should like to know why mother has written to me about ‘our most rising generation’?", "\"……하지만 나는 어머니가 왜 '우리의 가장 떠오르는 세대'에 관해 편지에 써 보냈는지 알고 싶군."], ["Simply as a descriptive touch, or with the idea of prepossessing me in favour of Mr. Luzhin?", "그저 묘사적인 곁들임일까, 아니면 나를 루진 씨 편으로 호감을 갖게 하려는 속셈일까?"], ["Oh, the cunning of them!", "오, 저들의 교활함이라니!"], ["I should like to know one thing more: how far they were open with one another that day and night and all this time since?", "한 가지 더 알고 싶은 게 있어. 그날 밤낮 동안, 그리고 그 후로 지금껏 그들이 서로에게 얼마나 솔직했을까?"], ["Was it all put into _words_, or did both understand that they had the same thing at heart and in their minds, so that there was no need to speak of it aloud, and better not to speak of it.", "그 모든 게 말로 표현되었을까, 아니면 두 사람 다 마음속에 같은 것을 품고 있음을 알아채고, 그래서 굳이 입 밖에 낼 필요가 없었고, 오히려 입 밖에 내지 않는 편이 나았던 걸까."], ["Most likely it was partly like that, from mother’s letter it’s evident: he struck her as rude _a little_, and mother in her simplicity took her observations to Dounia.", "십중팔구 어느 정도는 그랬을 거야, 어머니 편지를 보면 분명해. 그는 어머니에게 약간 무례하게 느껴졌고, 어머니는 그 순박함에 자기 생각을 두냐에게 털어놓은 거지."], ["And she was sure to be vexed and ‘answered her angrily.’ I should think so!", "그러자 두냐는 분명 언짢아져서 '화를 내며 대답했을' 거야."], ["Who would not be angered when it was quite clear without any naïve questions and when it was understood that it was useless to discuss it.", "당연하지! 천진한 질문 따위 없이도 모든 게 명백한데, 그걸 따져 봐야 소용없다는 걸 다 알고 있는데, 누군들 화가 나지 않겠어."], ["And why does she write to me, ‘love Dounia, Rodya, and she loves you more than herself’?", "그런데 어머니는 왜 내게 '두냐를 사랑해 다오, 로쟈, 그애는 너를 자기 자신보다 더 사랑한단다'라고 쓴 걸까?"], ["Has she a secret conscience-prick at sacrificing her daughter to her son?", "아들을 위해 딸을 희생시키는 것에 대해 양심의 가책이라도 은근히 느끼는 걸까?"], ["‘You are our one comfort, you are everything to us.’ Oh, mother!”", "'너는 우리의 유일한 위안이고, 너는 우리의 전부란다.' 오, 어머니!\""]], [["His bitterness grew more and more intense, and if he had happened to meet Mr. Luzhin at the moment, he might have murdered him.", "그의 쓰라림은 점점 더 격렬해졌고, 만약 그 순간 루진 씨와 마주쳤더라면, 그를 죽여 버렸을지도 모른다."]], [["“Hm... yes, that’s true,”", "\"흠……, 그래, 맞는 말이지.\""], ["he continued, pursuing the whirling ideas that chased each other in his brain, “it is true that ‘it needs time and care to get to know a man,’ but there is no mistake about Mr.", "그는 머릿속에서 꼬리를 물고 빙빙 도는 생각들을 좇으며 말을 이었다. \"'사람을 제대로 알려면 시간과 정성이 필요하다'는 건 맞는 말이지만, 루진 씨에 관해서라면 오해의 여지가 없어."], ["Luzhin. The chief thing is he is ‘a man of business and _seems_ kind,’ that was something, wasn’t it, to send the bags and big box for them!", "가장 중요한 건 그가 '사업가이고 친절해 보인다'는 건데, 그들을 위해 가방과 큰 짐 상자를 보냈으니, 그것도 대단한 일 아닌가!"], ["A kind man, no doubt after that!", "그러고 보니 친절한 사람이 틀림없겠군!"], ["But his _bride_ and her mother are to drive in a peasant’s cart covered with sacking (I know, I have been driven in it).", "하지만 그의 신부와 신부의 어머니는 거적때기를 덮은 농부의 짐마차를 타고 가야 하지(나도 알아, 나도 그걸 타 봤으니까)."], ["No matter!", "상관없어!"], ["It is only ninety versts and then they can ‘travel very comfortably, third class,’ for a thousand versts!", "고작 구십 베르스타니까, 그다음엔 천 베르스타를 '아주 편안하게, 삼등칸으로' 여행할 수 있을 테니."], ["Quite right, too.", "그것도 지당한 말이지."], ["One must cut one’s coat according to one’s cloth, but what about you, Mr.", "형편에 맞춰 살아야 하는 법이니까. 하지만 당신은 어떻소, 루진 씨?"], ["Luzhin? She is your bride....", "그녀는 당신의 신부 아니오……."], ["And you must be aware that her mother has to raise money on her pension for the journey.", "그리고 당신은 그녀의 어머니가 여행 경비를 마련하려고 연금을 담보로 돈을 빌려야 한다는 걸 알고 있을 텐데."], ["To be sure it’s a matter of business, a partnership for mutual benefit, with equal shares and expenses;--food and drink provided, but pay for your tobacco.", "하긴 그건 사업의 문제, 서로의 이익을 위한 동업이지, 똑같은 지분에 똑같은 비용으로. 먹고 마시는 건 제공되지만 담배는 각자 부담하시오."], ["The business man has got the better of them, too.", "그 사업가는 그들에게서도 잇속을 챙긴 거야."], ["The luggage will cost less than their fares and very likely go for nothing.", "짐 운반비가 그들의 차비보다 적을 테고, 어쩌면 공짜로 갈지도 모르지."], ["How is it that they don’t both see all that, or is it that they don’t want to see?", "어째서 그들은 둘 다 그걸 못 보는 걸까, 아니면 보고 싶지 않은 걸까?"], ["And they are pleased, pleased!", "게다가 만족하고 있어, 만족하고 있다고!"], ["And to think that this is only the first blossoming, and that the real fruits are to come!", "그런데 이건 겨우 첫 봉오리에 불과하고, 진짜 열매는 아직 오지 않았다는 걸 생각하면!"], ["But what really matters is not the stinginess, is not the meanness, but the _tone_ of the whole thing.", "하지만 정말로 문제가 되는 건 인색함도, 쩨쩨함도 아니야. 그 모든 것의 어조야."], ["For that will be the tone after marriage, it’s a foretaste of it.", "그게 바로 결혼 후의 어조가 될 테니까, 그건 그 맛보기인 거지."], ["And mother too, why should she be so lavish?", "어머니도 그래, 어째서 그렇게 헤퍼야 하지?"], ["What will she have by the time she gets to Petersburg?", "페테르부르크에 도착할 무렵엔 무엇이 남을까?"], ["Three silver roubles or two ‘paper ones’ as _she_ says....", "은화 삼 루블, 아니면 어머니 말마따나 '지폐로'"], ["that old woman... hm.", "두 장……, 그 늙은 어머니가……, 흠."], ["What does she expect to live upon in Petersburg afterwards?", "그 후에 페테르부르크에서 무엇으로 살아갈 작정인 거지?"], ["She has her reasons already for guessing that she _could not_ live with Dounia after the marriage, even for the first few months.", "어머니는 이미, 결혼 후에는 단 몇 달이라도 두냐와 함께 살 수 없으리라는 걸 짐작할 만한 이유가 있어."], ["The good man has no doubt let slip something on that subject also, though mother would deny it: ‘I shall refuse,’ says she.", "그 훌륭한 양반이 그 문제에 관해서도 분명 무슨 말을 흘렸을 테지, 어머니야 부인하겠지만. '나는 사양하마.'"], ["On whom is she reckoning then?", "어머니는 그렇게 말하지."], ["Is she counting on what is left of her hundred and twenty roubles of pension when Afanasy Ivanovitch’s debt is paid?", "그렇다면 누구를 믿고 있는 걸까? 아파나시 이바노비치의 빚을 갚고 나서 백이십 루블의 연금에서 남은 것에 기대고 있는 걸까?"], ["She knits woollen shawls and embroiders cuffs, ruining her old eyes.", "어머니는 늙은 눈을 망쳐 가며 모직 숄을 뜨고 토시에 수를 놓지."], ["And all her shawls don’t add more than twenty roubles a year to her hundred and twenty, I know that.", "그런데 그 모든 숄을 다 합쳐도 백이십 루블에 일 년 이십 루블 이상은 보태지 못한다는 걸, 나는 알아. 그러니까 어머니는 줄곧 루진 씨의 너그러움에 모든 희망을 걸고 있는 거야."], ["So she is building all her hopes all the time on Mr.", "'그가 스스로 내놓을 거다, 그가 내게 권할 거다'라면서."], ["Luzhin’s generosity; ‘he will offer it of himself, he will press it on me.’ You may wait a long time for that!", "그걸 기다리려면 오래 기다려야 할걸! 그 실러풍의 고결한 마음들은 늘 그런 식이지."], ["That’s how it always is with these Schilleresque noble hearts; till the last moment every goose is a swan with them, till the last moment, they hope for the best and will see nothing wrong, and although they have an inkling of the other side of the picture, yet they won’t face the truth till they are forced to; the very thought of it makes them shiver; they thrust the truth away with both hands, until the man they deck out in false colours puts a fool’s cap on them with his own hands.", "마지막 순간까지 모든 거위가 백조로 보이고, 마지막 순간까지 최선을 바라며 아무 잘못도 보려 하지 않아. 그림의 다른 면을 어렴풋이 눈치채면서도, 억지로 떠밀리기 전까지는 결코 진실을 마주하려 하지 않지."], ["I should like to know whether Mr.", "그 생각만 해도 몸서리를 치고, 두 손으로 진실을 밀어내."], ["Luzhin has any orders of merit; I bet he has the Anna in his buttonhole and that he puts it on when he goes to dine with contractors or merchants.", "거짓 빛깔로 치장해 준 그 남자가 제 손으로 그들 머리에 바보의 고깔모자를 씌워 줄 때까지. 루진 씨에게 무슨 훈장이라도 있는지 알고 싶군."], ["He will be sure to have it for his wedding, too!", "단춧구멍에 안나 훈장을 달고 있을 거라고 장담하지, 그리고 도급업자나 상인들과 식사하러 갈 때면 그걸 달고 가겠지. 결혼식에도 틀림없이 그걸 달 거야!"], ["Enough of him, confound him!", "그 작자 얘긴 이쯤 하지, 빌어먹을!"]], [["“Well,... mother I don’t wonder at, it’s like her, God bless her, but how could Dounia?", "\"뭐……, 어머니야 놀랄 것도 없어, 어머니다운 일이니까, 신의 가호가 있기를."], ["Dounia darling, as though I did not know you!", "하지만 두냐가 어떻게? 두냐, 사랑하는 두냐, 마치 내가 너를 모르는 것처럼!"], ["You were nearly twenty when I saw you last: I understood you then.", "마지막으로 너를 봤을 때 너는 스물이 다 되어 가고 있었지. 그때 나는 너를 이해했어."], ["Mother writes that ‘Dounia can put up with a great deal.’ I know that very well.", "어머니는 '두냐는 많은 걸 견딜 수 있다'고 쓰셨지. 나도 그건 아주 잘 알아."], ["I knew that two years and a half ago, and for the last two and a half years I have been thinking about it, thinking of just that, that ‘Dounia can put up with a great deal.’ If she could put up with Mr.", "이 년 반 전에도 알고 있었고, 지난 이 년 반 동안 줄곧 그것에 대해, 바로 그것에 대해 생각해 왔어. '두냐는 많은 걸 견딜 수 있다'는 것을."], ["Svidrigaïlov and all the rest of it, she certainly can put up with a great deal.", "스비드리가일로프 씨와 그 모든 일을 견뎌 낼 수 있었다면, 분명 많은 걸 견딜 수 있겠지."], ["And now mother and she have taken it into their heads that she can put up with Mr.", "그런데 지금 어머니와 그애는, 그애가 루진 씨를 견딜 수 있다고 머릿속에 단단히 박아 넣은 거야."], ["Luzhin, who propounds the theory of the superiority of wives raised from destitution and owing everything to their husband’s bounty--who propounds it, too, almost at the first interview.", "빈곤에서 끌어올려져 남편의 은혜에 모든 걸 빚진 아내가 우월하다는 이론을 내세우는 자를. 그것도 거의 첫 만남에서부터 그 이론을 늘어놓는 자를."], ["Granted that he ‘let it slip,’ though he is a sensible man, (yet maybe it was not a slip at all, but he meant to make himself clear as soon as possible) but Dounia, Dounia?", "그가 그걸 '무심코 흘렸다'고 치자, 분별 있는 사람이긴 하지만(어쩌면 무심코 흘린 게 전혀 아니라, 가능한 한 빨리 제 뜻을 분명히 해 두려는 거였는지도 몰라). 하지만 두냐는, 두냐는?"], ["She understands the man, of course, but she will have to live with the man.", "물론 그애는 그 인간을 꿰뚫어 보지, 그러나 그 인간과 함께 살아야 하잖아."], ["Why!", "아니!"], ["she’d live on black bread and water, she would not sell her soul, she would not barter her moral freedom for comfort; she would not barter it for all Schleswig-Holstein, much less Mr.", "그애라면 검은 빵과 물만 먹고 살지언정 제 영혼을 팔지는 않을 거야, 안락을 위해 제 도덕적 자유를 거래하지는 않을 거라고. 슐레스비히홀슈타인 전부를 준대도 그애는 그걸 팔지 않을 텐데, 하물며 루진 씨의 돈 따위로야."], ["Luzhin’s money. No, Dounia was not that sort when I knew her and...", "아니, 내가 알던 두냐는 그런 부류가 아니었고……, 물론 지금도 여전히 그대로일 테지!"], ["she is still the same, of course! Yes, there’s no denying, the Svidrigaïlovs are a bitter pill!", "그래, 부정할 수 없어, 스비드리가일로프 집안은 쓰디쓴 알약이지!"], ["It’s a bitter thing to spend one’s life a governess in the provinces for two hundred roubles, but I know she would rather be a nigger on a plantation or a Lett with a German master than degrade her soul, and her moral dignity, by binding herself for ever to a man whom she does not respect and with whom she has nothing in common--for her own advantage.", "시골에서 이백 루블에 가정교사로 평생을 보내는 건 쓰라린 일이지만, 그래도 나는 알아. 그애는 존경하지도 않고 아무 공통점도 없는 남자에게 제 이익을 위해 영원히 매이느니, 차라리 농장의 흑인 노예가 되거나 독일인 주인을 둔 라트비아인이 되는 편을 택할 거야, 제 영혼과 도덕적 존엄을 더럽히기보다는."], ["And if Mr. Luzhin had been of unalloyed gold, or one huge diamond, she would never have consented to become his legal concubine.", "설령 루진 씨가 순금 덩어리였다 해도, 거대한 다이아몬드 하나였다 해도, 그애는 결코 그의 합법적인 첩이 되는 데 동의하지 않았을 거야."], ["Why is she consenting then?", "그렇다면 왜 동의하는 거지?"], ["What’s the point of it?", "그 의미가 뭐야? 그 답이 뭐냐고?"], ["What’s the answer?", "충분히 명백해."], ["It’s clear enough: for herself, for her comfort, to save her life she would not sell herself, but for someone else she is doing it!", "자기 자신을 위해서라면, 자기 안락을 위해서라면 그애는 목숨을 구하기 위해서라도 자신을 팔지 않을 거야. 그러나 다른 누군가를 위해서라면 그렇게 하는 거지!"], ["For one she loves, for one she adores, she will sell herself!", "사랑하는 사람을 위해, 흠모하는 사람을 위해서라면, 그애는 자신을 팔 거야!"], ["That’s what it all amounts to; for her brother, for her mother, she will sell herself!", "그게 결국 전부야. 제 오라비를 위해, 제 어머니를 위해, 그애는 자신을 파는 거지!"], ["She will sell everything!", "그애는 모든 걸 팔 거야!"], ["In such cases, ‘we overcome our moral feeling if necessary,’ freedom, peace, conscience even, all, all are brought into the market.", "그런 경우엔 '필요하다면 우리의 도덕적 감정도 극복한다'면서, 자유도, 평안도, 양심마저도, 전부, 전부 시장에 내놓는 거야."], ["Let my life go, if only my dear ones may be happy!", "사랑하는 이들이 행복할 수만 있다면 내 인생쯤 내던져도 좋아!"], ["More than that, we become casuists, we learn to be Jesuitical and for a time maybe we can soothe ourselves, we can persuade ourselves that it is one’s duty for a good object.", "그뿐인가, 우리는 궤변가가 되고, 예수회 신자처럼 둘러대는 법을 배우고, 한동안은 어쩌면 스스로를 달랠 수 있을지도 모르지, 좋은 목적을 위한 의무라고 자신을 설득할 수 있을지도."], ["That’s just like us, it’s as clear as daylight.", "그게 딱 우리다운 거야, 대낮처럼 명백한 일이지."], ["It’s clear that Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov is the central figure in the business, and no one else.", "이 일의 중심인물이 로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프이고 다른 누구도 아니라는 게 명백해."], ["Oh, yes, she can ensure his happiness, keep him in the university, make him a partner in the office, make his whole future secure; perhaps he may even be a rich man later on, prosperous, respected, and may even end his life a famous man!", "오, 그래, 그애는 그의 행복을 보장하고, 그를 대학에 계속 다니게 하고, 그를 사무소의 동업자로 만들고, 그의 미래 전체를 안전하게 해 줄 수 있겠지. 어쩌면 그는 나중에 부자가 될지도 몰라, 번창하고, 존경받고, 어쩌면 유명인사로 생을 마칠지도 모르지!"], ["But my mother?", "그런데 우리 어머니는?"], ["It’s all Rodya, precious Rodya, her first born!", "다 로쟈를 위해서야, 소중한 로쟈, 첫아들을!"], ["For such a son who would not sacrifice such a daughter!", "그런 아들을 위해서라면 누군들 그런 딸을 희생시키지 않겠어!"], ["Oh, loving, over-partial hearts!", "오, 사랑이 넘치다 못해 편애하는 마음들이여!"], ["Why, for his sake we would not shrink even from Sonia’s fate.", "아무렴, 그를 위해서라면 우리는 소냐의 운명조차 마다하지 않을 거야."], ["Sonia, Sonia Marmeladov, the eternal victim so long as the world lasts.", "소냐, 소냐 마르멜라도프, 세상이 존속하는 한 영원한 희생자."], ["Have you taken the measure of your sacrifice, both of you?", "너희 둘 다, 너희의 희생을 가늠해 보기는 한 거니?"], ["Is it right?", "그게 옳은 일이야?"], ["Can you bear it?", "너희가 견딜 수 있어?"], ["Is it any use?", "무슨 소용이 있어?"], ["Is there sense in it?", "거기에 분별이 있긴 한 거니?"], ["And let me tell you, Dounia, Sonia’s life is no worse than life with Mr. Luzhin.", "그리고 내가 말해 두마, 두냐, 소냐의 삶이 루진 씨와의 삶보다 나쁠 게 없어."], ["‘There can be no question of love,’ mother writes.", "'사랑은 문제가 될 수 없다'고 어머니는 쓰셨지."], ["And what if there can be no respect either, if on the contrary there is aversion, contempt, repulsion, what then?", "그런데 만약 존경마저 있을 수 없다면, 오히려 혐오와 경멸과 거부감만 있다면, 그땐 어쩔 거야?"], ["So you will have to ‘keep up your appearance,’ too.", "그러니 너 역시 '겉모습을 유지해야' 할 거야."], ["Is not that so?", "그렇지 않아?"], ["Do you understand what that smartness means?", "그 말쑥함이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알겠어?"], ["Do you understand that the Luzhin smartness is just the same thing as Sonia’s and may be worse, viler, baser, because in your case, Dounia, it’s a bargain for luxuries, after all, but with Sonia it’s simply a question of starvation.", "루진식 말쑥함이 소냐의 것과 똑같은 것이며, 어쩌면 더 나쁘고, 더 비열하고, 더 천박할 수 있다는 걸 알겠어? 왜냐하면, 두냐, 네 경우엔 결국 사치를 위한 거래지만, 소냐의 경우엔 그저 굶주림의 문제일 뿐이니까."], ["It has to be paid for, it has to be paid for, Dounia, this smartness.", "대가를 치러야 해, 대가를 치러야 한다고, 두냐, 그 말쑥함의."], ["And what if it’s more than you can bear afterwards, if you regret it?", "그리고 만약 나중에 그게 네가 견딜 수 있는 것 이상이라면, 만약 후회하게 된다면 어쩌지?"], ["The bitterness, the misery, the curses, the tears hidden from all the world, for you are not a Marfa Petrovna.", "그 쓰라림, 그 비참함, 그 저주, 온 세상에 숨긴 눈물, 너는 마르파 페트로브나가 아니니까."], ["And how will your mother feel then?", "그럼 그때 어머니는 어떤 심정일까?"], ["Even now she is uneasy, she is worried, but then, when she sees it all clearly?", "지금조차도 불안해하고, 걱정하는데, 그때, 모든 걸 또렷이 보게 된다면? 그리고 나는?"], ["And I? Yes, indeed, what have you taken me for?", "그래, 정말로, 너희는 나를 어떤 인간으로 본 거야?"], ["I won’t have your sacrifice, Dounia, I won’t have it, mother!", "나는 네 희생을 받아들이지 않겠어, 두냐, 받아들이지 않겠어요, 어머니!"], ["It shall not be, so long as I am alive, it shall not, it shall not!", "그렇게는 안 돼, 내가 살아 있는 한 그렇게는 안 돼, 안 돼, 안 된다고!"], ["I won’t accept it!”", "나는 그걸 받아들이지 않겠어!\""]], [["He suddenly paused in his reflection and stood still.", "그는 갑자기 사색을 멈추고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“It shall not be?", "\"그렇게는 안 된다고?"], ["But what are you going to do to prevent it?", "그런데 그걸 막으려고 네가 뭘 할 건데?"], ["You’ll forbid it?", "금지하기라도 할 거야?"], ["And what right have you?", "무슨 권리로?"], ["What can you promise them on your side to give you such a right?", "그런 권리를 갖게 하려고 네가 그들에게 무엇을 약속할 수 있는데?"], ["Your whole life, your whole future, you will devote to them _when you have finished your studies and obtained a post_?", "네 인생 전부, 네 미래 전부를, 학업을 마치고 일자리를 얻은 다음에 그들에게 바치겠다고?"], ["Yes, we have heard all that before, and that’s all _words_, but now?", "그래, 그런 말은 전에도 다 들어 봤어, 그건 죄다 말뿐이고, 그럼 지금은?"], ["Now something must be done, now, do you understand that?", "지금 당장 뭔가를 해야 해, 지금, 그걸 알겠어?"], ["And what are you doing now?", "그런데 지금 너는 뭘 하고 있지?"], ["You are living upon them.", "너는 그들에게 얹혀살고 있어."], ["They borrow on their hundred roubles pension.", "그들은 백 루블 연금을 담보로 돈을 빌리고 있어."], ["They borrow from the Svidrigaïlovs.", "스비드리가일로프 집안에서 빌리고 있다고."], ["How are you going to save them from Svidrigaïlovs, from Afanasy Ivanovitch Vahrushin, oh, future millionaire Zeus who would arrange their lives for them?", "너는 그들을 스비드리가일로프 집안으로부터, 아파나시 이바노비치 바흐루신으로부터 어떻게 구해 낼 작정이지, 오, 그들의 삶을 대신 꾸려 줄 미래의 백만장자 제우스여?"], ["In another ten years?", "앞으로 십 년 뒤에?"], ["In another ten years, mother will be blind with knitting shawls, maybe with weeping too.", "십 년 뒤면 어머니는 숄을 뜨다가 눈이 멀어 있을 거야, 어쩌면 울다가도."], ["She will be worn to a shadow with fasting; and my sister?", "단식하느라 그림자처럼 말라 있겠지. 그럼 내 누이는?"], ["Imagine for a moment what may have become of your sister in ten years?", "잠깐 상상해 봐, 십 년 후에 네 누이가 어떻게 되어 있을지를?"], ["What may happen to her during those ten years?", "그 십 년 동안 그애에게 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있을지를?"], ["Can you fancy?”", "짐작이나 할 수 있어?\""]], [["So he tortured himself, fretting himself with such questions, and finding a kind of enjoyment in it.", "그리하여 그는 그런 질문들로 자신을 안달하게 하며 스스로를 고문했고, 거기서 일종의 쾌감마저 느꼈다."], ["And yet all these questions were not new ones suddenly confronting him, they were old familiar aches.", "그러면서도 이 모든 질문은 갑자기 그 앞에 들이닥친 새로운 것들이 아니라, 오래되고 낯익은 쓰라림이었다."], ["It was long since they had first begun to grip and rend his heart.", "그것들이 처음으로 그의 마음을 움켜쥐고 찢기 시작한 것은 오래전부터였다."], ["Long, long ago his present anguish had its first beginnings; it had waxed and gathered strength, it had matured and concentrated, until it had taken the form of a fearful, frenzied and fantastic question, which tortured his heart and mind, clamouring insistently for an answer.", "오래, 아주 오래전에 지금의 이 고뇌는 처음 싹텄고, 점점 자라 힘을 모으고, 무르익고 응축되어, 마침내 그의 마음과 머리를 고문하며 집요하게 답을 요구하는, 무섭고 광적이며 환상적인 질문의 형태를 띠게 된 것이었다."], ["Now his mother’s letter had burst on him like a thunderclap.", "그런데 이제 어머니의 편지가 청천벽력처럼 그에게 들이닥쳤다."], ["It was clear that he must not now suffer passively, worrying himself over unsolved questions, but that he must do something, do it at once, and do it quickly.", "분명한 건, 그가 이제 풀리지 않은 질문들에 안달하며 수동적으로 괴로워해서는 안 되고, 무언가를 해야 한다는 것, 그것도 당장, 빨리 해야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Anyway he must decide on something, or else...", "어쨌든 그는 무언가를 결정해야 했다, 그렇지 않으면……."]], [["“Or throw up life altogether!”", "\"아니면 인생을 통째로 내던지든가!\""], ["he cried suddenly, in a frenzy--“accept one’s lot humbly as it is, once for all and stifle everything in oneself, giving up all claim to activity, life and love!”", "그는 광기에 휩싸여 불쑥 외쳤다. \"있는 그대로의 운명을 단번에, 영원히 겸허하게 받아들이고, 자기 안의 모든 걸 짓눌러 죽이고, 활동도 삶도 사랑도 모든 권리를 단념하든가!\""]], [["“Do you understand, sir, do you understand what it means when you have absolutely nowhere to turn?”", "\"당신은 아십니까, 선생, 정말 갈 곳이 아무 데도 없다는 게 무슨 뜻인지 아십니까?\""], ["Marmeladov’s question came suddenly into his mind, “for every man must have somewhere to turn....”", "마르멜라도프의 질문이 불현듯 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. \"왜냐하면 모든 인간은 어딘가 갈 곳이 있어야 하니까요…….\""]], [["He gave a sudden start; another thought, that he had had yesterday, slipped back into his mind.", "그는 갑자기 흠칫했다. 어제 했던 또 다른 생각 하나가 다시 그의 머릿속으로 슬며시 미끄러져 들어왔다."], ["But he did not start at the thought recurring to him, for he knew, he had _felt beforehand_, that it must come back, he was expecting it; besides it was not only yesterday’s thought.", "그러나 그 생각이 되살아난 것에 흠칫한 것은 아니었으니, 그것이 반드시 돌아오리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었고, 미리 느끼고 있었으며, 그것을 기다리고 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 그건 단지 어제의 생각만도 아니었다."], ["The difference was that a month ago, yesterday even, the thought was a mere dream: but now...", "다른 점이 있다면, 한 달 전만 해도, 아니 바로 어제만 해도, 그 생각은 한낱 공상에 지나지 않았다는 것이다."], ["now it appeared not a dream at all, it had taken a new menacing and quite unfamiliar shape, and he suddenly became aware of this himself....", "그러나 지금……, 지금 그것은 전혀 공상이 아닌 것처럼 보였고, 새롭고 위협적이며 너무도 낯선 형태를 띠게 되었으며, 그는 갑자기 그것을 스스로 자각하게 되었다……."], ["He felt a hammering in his head, and there was a darkness before his eyes.", "그는 머릿속에서 쾅쾅 두드리는 소리를 느꼈고, 눈앞이 캄캄해졌다."]], [["He looked round hurriedly, he was searching for something.", "그는 황급히 주위를 둘러보았다. 무언가를 찾고 있었다."], ["He wanted to sit down and was looking for a seat; he was walking along the K---- Boulevard.", "앉고 싶어서 자리를 찾고 있었던 것이다. 그는 K 가로수 길을 따라 걷고 있었다."], ["There was a seat about a hundred paces in front of him.", "그의 앞 백 걸음쯤 되는 곳에 벤치 하나가 있었다."], ["He walked towards it as fast he could; but on the way he met with a little adventure which absorbed all his attention.", "그는 할 수 있는 한 빨리 그쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그러나 가는 도중에 그의 온 주의를 사로잡는 작은 사건과 마주쳤다."], ["Looking for the seat, he had noticed a woman walking some twenty paces in front of him, but at first he took no more notice of her than of other objects that crossed his path.", "벤치를 찾던 중에 그는 자기 앞 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳을 걷고 있는 한 여자를 알아챘는데, 처음에는 길을 가로지르는 다른 물체들보다 더 눈여겨보지는 않았다."], ["It had happened to him many times going home not to notice the road by which he was going, and he was accustomed to walk like that.", "집으로 돌아가면서 자신이 가고 있는 길을 알아채지 못한 적이 그에게는 여러 번 있었고, 그는 그런 식으로 걷는 데 익숙했다."], ["But there was at first sight something so strange about the woman in front of him, that gradually his attention was riveted upon her, at first reluctantly and, as it were, resentfully, and then more and more intently.", "그러나 그의 앞에 있는 여자에게는 첫눈에 무언가 너무도 이상한 데가 있어서, 차츰 그의 주의가 그녀에게 못 박혔으니, 처음에는 마지못해, 말하자면 못마땅한 심정으로, 그러다 점점 더 골똘히."], ["He felt a sudden desire to find out what it was that was so strange about the woman.", "그는 그 여자에게 그토록 이상한 점이 무엇인지 알아내고 싶은 욕구를 불현듯 느꼈다."], ["In the first place, she appeared to be a girl quite young, and she was walking in the great heat bareheaded and with no parasol or gloves, waving her arms about in an absurd way.", "우선 그녀는 아주 젊은 처녀로 보였는데, 이 무더위 속에 모자도 쓰지 않고 양산도 장갑도 없이, 우스꽝스럽게 팔을 휘저으며 걷고 있었다."], ["She had on a dress of some light silky material, but put on strangely awry, not properly hooked up, and torn open at the top of the skirt, close to the waist: a great piece was rent and hanging loose.", "그녀는 얇고 비단 같은 천으로 만든 드레스를 입었으나, 이상하게 비뚤어지게 걸쳐 입어 단추도 제대로 채워지지 않았고, 허리께 치맛자락 윗부분이 찢어져 벌어져 있었다. 커다란 천 조각이 뜯겨 너덜너덜 늘어져 있었다."], ["A little kerchief was flung about her bare throat, but lay slanting on one side.", "드러난 목에는 작은 손수건이 둘러져 있었으나 한쪽으로 비뚜름하게 걸쳐져 있었다."], ["The girl was walking unsteadily, too, stumbling and staggering from side to side.", "게다가 처녀는 비틀비틀, 발을 헛디디고 좌우로 휘청거리며 걷고 있었다."], ["She drew Raskolnikov’s whole attention at last.", "마침내 그녀는 라스콜니코프의 온 주의를 끌었다."], ["He overtook the girl at the seat, but, on reaching it, she dropped down on it, in the corner; she let her head sink on the back of the seat and closed her eyes, apparently in extreme exhaustion.", "그는 벤치 근처에서 그녀를 따라잡았는데, 거기에 다다르자 그녀는 벤치 구석에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그녀는 고개를 등받이에 떨어뜨리고 눈을 감았는데, 보아하니 극도로 지친 모양이었다."], ["Looking at her closely, he saw at once that she was completely drunk.", "가까이서 그녀를 들여다본 그는, 그녀가 완전히 취해 있다는 것을 단번에 알아챘다."], ["It was a strange and shocking sight.", "그것은 이상하고도 충격적인 광경이었다."], ["He could hardly believe that he was not mistaken.", "그는 자신이 잘못 본 게 아니라는 사실을 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다."], ["He saw before him the face of a quite young, fair-haired girl--sixteen, perhaps not more than fifteen, years old, pretty little face, but flushed and heavy looking and, as it were, swollen.", "그의 눈앞에는 아주 젊고 금발인 처녀의 얼굴이 있었으니, 열여섯, 어쩌면 열다섯밖에 안 되어 보였고, 예쁘장한 작은 얼굴이었으나 발갛게 상기되고 둔해 보이며, 말하자면 부어 있었다."], ["The girl seemed hardly to know what she was doing; she crossed one leg over the other, lifting it indecorously, and showed every sign of being unconscious that she was in the street.", "처녀는 자신이 무얼 하고 있는지 거의 모르는 듯했다. 한쪽 다리를 다른 쪽 다리 위에 무례하게 들어 올려 포개고는, 자신이 거리에 있다는 것을 전혀 의식하지 못하는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["Raskolnikov did not sit down, but he felt unwilling to leave her, and stood facing her in perplexity.", "라스콜니코프는 앉지 않았으나, 그녀를 떠날 마음도 내키지 않아, 당혹스러운 채로 그녀를 마주하고 서 있었다."], ["This boulevard was never much frequented; and now, at two o’clock, in the stifling heat, it was quite deserted.", "이 가로수 길은 늘 사람이 그리 많지 않았는데, 지금, 두 시, 숨 막히는 더위 속에서는 거의 인적이 끊겨 있었다."], ["And yet on the further side of the boulevard, about fifteen paces away, a gentleman was standing on the edge of the pavement.", "그런데도 가로수 길 저편, 한 열다섯 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에 한 신사가 보도 가장자리에 서 있었다."], ["He, too, would apparently have liked to approach the girl with some object of his own.", "그 역시 보아하니 무언가 제 나름의 속셈을 품고 그 처녀에게 다가가고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["He, too, had probably seen her in the distance and had followed her, but found Raskolnikov in his way.", "그 역시 멀리서 그녀를 보고 뒤따라왔을 테지만, 라스콜니코프가 길을 가로막고 있는 걸 알게 된 것이다."], ["He looked angrily at him, though he tried to escape his notice, and stood impatiently biding his time, till the unwelcome man in rags should have moved away.", "그는 라스콜니코프의 눈에 띄지 않으려 애쓰면서도 화가 난 눈으로 그를 노려보았고, 이 달갑잖은 누더기 차림의 사내가 비켜 주기만을 초조하게 때를 기다리며 서 있었다."], ["His intentions were unmistakable.", "그의 의도는 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["The gentleman was a plump, thickly-set man, about thirty, fashionably dressed, with a high colour, red lips and moustaches.", "그 신사는 서른쯤 된, 통통하고 다부진 체격의 사내로, 유행에 맞춰 차려입었으며, 혈색이 좋고 입술이 붉으며 콧수염을 길렀다."], ["Raskolnikov felt furious; he had a sudden longing to insult this fat dandy in some way.", "라스콜니코프는 격분했다. 그는 이 뚱뚱한 멋쟁이를 어떻게든 모욕해 주고 싶은 갑작스러운 충동을 느꼈다."], ["He left the girl for a moment and walked towards the gentleman.", "그는 잠시 처녀를 두고 그 신사에게로 걸어갔다."]], [["“Hey!", "\"이봐!"], ["You Svidrigaïlov!", "당신 스비드리가일로프!"], ["What do you want here?”", "여기서 뭘 원하는 거요?\""], ["he shouted, clenching his fists and laughing, spluttering with rage.", "그는 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 웃어 대며, 분노에 침을 튀기며 소리쳤다."]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소리요?\""], ["the gentleman asked sternly, scowling in haughty astonishment.", "신사가 거만한 놀라움에 얼굴을 찌푸리며 엄하게 물었다."]], [["“Get away, that’s what I mean.”", "\"꺼지란 말이오, 그게 내 말이오.\""]], [["“How dare you, you low fellow!”", "\"감히, 이 천한 놈이!\""]], [["He raised his cane.", "그는 지팡이를 치켜들었다."], ["Raskolnikov rushed at him with his fists, without reflecting that the stout gentleman was a match for two men like himself.", "라스콜니코프는, 그 건장한 신사가 자기 같은 사내 둘은 너끈히 상대할 만하다는 것도 헤아리지 못한 채 주먹을 휘두르며 그에게 달려들었다."], ["But at that instant someone seized him from behind, and a police constable stood between them.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 누군가가 뒤에서 그를 붙들었고, 한 순경이 두 사람 사이에 끼어들었다."]], [["“That’s enough, gentlemen, no fighting, please, in a public place.", "\"이만하면 됐소, 신사 양반들, 공공장소에서 싸움질은 하지 마시오, 제발."], ["What do you want?", "무슨 일이오?"], ["Who are you?”", "당신은 누구요?\""], ["he asked Raskolnikov sternly, noticing his rags.", "그는 라스콜니코프의 누더기를 눈여겨보며 엄하게 물었다."]], [["Raskolnikov looked at him intently.", "라스콜니코프는 그를 골똘히 바라보았다."], ["He had a straight-forward, sensible, soldierly face, with grey moustaches and whiskers.", "그는 솔직하고 분별 있는 군인다운 얼굴에, 희끗한 콧수염과 구레나룻을 기르고 있었다."]], [["“You are just the man I want,”", "\"당신이야말로 내가 찾던 사람이오.\""], ["Raskolnikov cried, catching at his arm.", "라스콜니코프가 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 외쳤다."], ["“I am a student, Raskolnikov....", "\"나는 대학생이오, 라스콜니코프라고 하지요……."], ["You may as well know that too,”", "당신도 알아 두는 게 좋겠소.\""], ["he added, addressing the gentleman, “come along, I have something to show you.”", "그가 신사를 향해 덧붙였다. \"이리 와 보시오, 당신에게 보여 줄 게 있소.\""]], [["And taking the policeman by the hand he drew him towards the seat.", "그러고는 순경의 손을 잡아 벤치 쪽으로 끌고 갔다."]], [["“Look here, hopelessly drunk, and she has just come down the boulevard.", "\"여기 좀 보시오, 가망 없이 취해 있고, 방금 가로수 길을 걸어 내려왔소."], ["There is no telling who and what she is, she does not look like a professional.", "이 여자가 누구인지, 어떤 사람인지는 알 길이 없지만, 직업적인 여자로는 보이지 않소. 어딘가에서 술을 먹이고 속였을 가능성이 더 크지……, 처음으로……, 무슨 말인지 알겠소?"], ["It’s more likely she has been given drink and deceived somewhere...", "그러고는 이렇게 거리로 내쫓은 거요. 드레스가 어떻게 찢겨 있는지, 어떻게 입혀져 있는지 좀 보시오."], ["for the first time... you understand?", "누군가가 그녀에게 옷을 입힌 거지, 스스로 입은 게 아니오."], ["and they’ve put her out into the street like that.", "그것도 서툰 손으로, 남자의 손으로 입힌 거요."], ["Look at the way her dress is torn, and the way it has been put on: she has been dressed by somebody, she has not dressed herself, and dressed by unpractised hands, by a man’s hands; that’s evident.", "그게 분명하오. 그리고 이제 저쪽을 보시오."], ["And now look there: I don’t know that dandy with whom I was going to fight, I see him for the first time, but he, too, has seen her on the road, just now, drunk, not knowing what she is doing, and now he is very eager to get hold of her, to get her away somewhere while she is in this state...", "내가 싸우려던 저 멋쟁이는 모르는 자고, 처음 보는 작자지만, 그도 방금 길에서, 취해서 자기가 뭘 하는지도 모르는 이 여자를 본 거요. 그러고는 이런 상태의 그녀를 어떻게든 붙잡아 어딘가로 데려가려고 안달이 난 거지……."], ["that’s certain, believe me, I am not wrong.", "그건 확실하오, 내 말을 믿으시오, 나는 틀리지 않았소."], ["I saw him myself watching her and following her, but I prevented him, and he is just waiting for me to go away.", "그가 그녀를 지켜보고 뒤따르는 걸 내 눈으로 봤고, 내가 막은 거요. 그래서 그는 내가 비켜 주기만을 기다리고 있는 거지."], ["Now he has walked away a little, and is standing still, pretending to make a cigarette....", "지금 조금 물러나서 가만히 서서, 담배라도 마는 척하고 있군……."], ["Think how can we keep her out of his hands, and how are we to get her home?”", "어떻게 하면 그녀를 그의 손아귀에서 지키고, 또 어떻게 집으로 데려갈 수 있을지 생각해 봅시다.\""]], [["The policeman saw it all in a flash.", "순경은 단번에 모든 걸 알아챘다."], ["The stout gentleman was easy to understand, he turned to consider the girl.", "그 뚱뚱한 신사야 이해하기 쉬웠고, 그는 처녀를 살피려 몸을 돌렸다."], ["The policeman bent over to examine her more closely, and his face worked with genuine compassion.", "순경은 그녀를 좀 더 가까이 들여다보려 허리를 굽혔고, 그의 얼굴이 진정한 연민으로 일그러졌다."]], [["“Ah, what a pity!”", "\"아, 가엾어라!\""], ["he said, shaking his head--“why, she is quite a child!", "그가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아니, 아직 애나 다름없잖아!"], ["She has been deceived, you can see that at once.", "속은 거야, 한눈에 알 수 있군."], ["Listen, lady,”", "이봐요, 아가씨.\""], ["he began addressing her, “where do you live?”", "그가 그녀에게 말을 걸기 시작했다."], ["The girl opened her weary and sleepy-looking eyes, gazed blankly at the speaker and waved her hand.", "\"어디 사시오?\" 처녀는 지치고 졸린 듯한 눈을 떠서 말하는 이를 멍하니 바라보고는 손을 내저었다."]], [["“Here,”", "\"여기.\""], ["said Raskolnikov feeling in his pocket and finding twenty copecks, “here, call a cab and tell him to drive her to her address.", "라스콜니코프가 주머니를 뒤져 이십 코페이카를 찾아내며 말했다. \"여기, 삯마차를 불러서 이 여자를 집까지 데려다 달라고 하시오."], ["The only thing is to find out her address!”", "다만 그녀의 주소를 알아내는 게 문제로군!\""]], [["“Missy, missy!”", "\"아가씨, 아가씨!\""], ["the policeman began again, taking the money.", "순경이 돈을 받아 들고 다시 말을 걸기 시작했다."], ["“I’ll fetch you a cab and take you home myself.", "\"마차를 불러서 내가 직접 집까지 데려다주리다."], ["Where shall I take you, eh?", "어디로 데려가면 되겠소, 응?"], ["Where do you live?”", "어디 사시오?\""]], [["“Go away!", "\"저리 가요!"], ["They won’t let me alone,”", "나를 가만 안 둬.\""], ["the girl muttered, and once more waved her hand.", "처녀가 중얼거리며 또 한 번 손을 내저었다."]], [["“Ach, ach, how shocking!", "\"아아, 아아, 어찌나 망측한지!"], ["It’s shameful, missy, it’s a shame!”", "부끄러운 일이오, 아가씨, 부끄러운 일이야!\""], ["He shook his head again, shocked, sympathetic and indignant.", "그는 충격을 받고, 가엾어하면서도 분개하여 다시금 고개를 저었다."]], [["“It’s a difficult job,”", "\"이건 어려운 일이군.\""], ["the policeman said to Raskolnikov, and as he did so, he looked him up and down in a rapid glance.", "순경이 라스콜니코프에게 말하면서, 그러는 동안 재빠른 눈길로 그를 위아래로 훑어보았다."], ["He, too, must have seemed a strange figure to him: dressed in rags and handing him money!", "그에게도 라스콜니코프는 분명 이상한 인물로 보였을 것이다. 누더기를 걸친 채 자신에게 돈을 건네다니!"]], [["“Did you meet her far from here?”", "\"이 여자를 여기서 먼 데서 만났소?\""], ["he asked him.", "그가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“I tell you she was walking in front of me, staggering, just here, in the boulevard.", "\"내 말했잖소, 내 앞에서 걷고 있었다고, 비틀거리면서, 바로 여기, 가로수 길에서."], ["She only just reached the seat and sank down on it.”", "그녀는 겨우 이 벤치까지 와서는 여기 털썩 주저앉았소.\""]], [["“Ah, the shameful things that are done in the world nowadays, God have mercy on us!", "\"아, 요즘 세상에 벌어지는 망측한 일들이라니, 하느님, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!"], ["An innocent creature like that, drunk already!", "저런 순진한 것이 벌써 취하다니!"], ["She has been deceived, that’s a sure thing.", "속은 거야, 그건 틀림없어."], ["See how her dress has been torn too....", "드레스가 어떻게 찢겼는지도 좀 보게……."], ["Ah, the vice one sees nowadays!", "아, 요즘 보이는 이 타락이라니!"], ["And as likely as not she belongs to gentlefolk too, poor ones maybe....", "게다가 십중팔구 양갓집 출신일 것 같단 말이야, 어쩌면 가난한 집의……."], ["There are many like that nowadays.", "요즘엔 그런 경우가 많지."], ["She looks refined, too, as though she were a lady,”", "보아하니 기품도 있어, 마치 귀한 집 아가씨 같단 말이야.\""], ["and he bent over her once more.", "그러고는 다시 한 번 그녀에게 허리를 굽혔다."]], [["Perhaps he had daughters growing up like that, “looking like ladies and refined” with pretensions to gentility and smartness....", "어쩌면 그에게도 그렇게 자라나는 딸들이, \"귀한 집 아가씨처럼 기품 있어 보이는\", 고상함과 말쑥함에 대한 허세를 품은 딸들이 있는지도 몰랐다……."]], [["“The chief thing is,”", "\"중요한 건 말이오,\""], ["Raskolnikov persisted, “to keep her out of this scoundrel’s hands!", "라스콜니코프가 고집스레 말했다. \"저 악당의 손아귀에서 그녀를 지키는 거요!"], ["Why should he outrage her?", "어째서 그가 그녀를 능욕하게 둔단 말이오?"], ["It’s as clear as day what he is after; ah, the brute, he is not moving off!”", "그가 무얼 노리는지는 대낮처럼 명백하지. 아, 저 짐승 같은 놈, 떠날 생각을 안 하는군!\""]], [["Raskolnikov spoke aloud and pointed to him.", "라스콜니코프는 큰 소리로 말하며 그를 가리켰다."], ["The gentleman heard him, and seemed about to fly into a rage again, but thought better of it, and confined himself to a contemptuous look.", "신사는 그 말을 듣고 또다시 발끈 화를 낼 듯하더니, 마음을 고쳐먹고는 경멸하는 눈길 하나로 그쳤다."], ["He then walked slowly another ten paces away and again halted.", "그러고는 천천히 열 걸음쯤 더 멀어졌다가 다시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Keep her out of his hands we can,”", "\"그의 손아귀에서 그녀를 지킬 수는 있소.\""], ["said the constable thoughtfully, “if only she’d tell us where to take her, but as it is....", "순경이 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"그녀가 어디로 데려가야 할지 말해 주기만 한다면 말이오, 하지만 이대로는……."], ["Missy, hey, missy!”", "아가씨, 이봐요, 아가씨!\""], ["he bent over her once more.", "그는 다시 한 번 그녀에게 허리를 굽혔다."]], [["She opened her eyes fully all of a sudden, looked at him intently, as though realising something, got up from the seat and walked away in the direction from which she had come.", "그녀는 갑자기 눈을 활짝 뜨더니, 무언가를 깨달은 듯 그를 골똘히 바라보다가, 벤치에서 일어나 자기가 왔던 방향으로 걸어가 버렸다."], ["“Oh shameful wretches, they won’t let me alone!”", "\"오, 부끄러운 줄도 모르는 작자들, 나를 가만 안 둬!\""], ["she said, waving her hand again.", "그녀가 다시 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["She walked quickly, though staggering as before.", "그녀는 여전히 비틀거리면서도 빠르게 걸었다."], ["The dandy followed her, but along another avenue, keeping his eye on her.", "멋쟁이는 그녀를 뒤따랐으나, 다른 길을 따라가며 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["“Don’t be anxious, I won’t let him have her,”", "\"걱정 마시오, 그자가 저 여자를 차지하게 두진 않을 테니.\""], ["the policeman said resolutely, and he set off after them.", "순경이 단호하게 말하고는 그들을 뒤쫓아 출발했다."]], [["“Ah, the vice one sees nowadays!”", "\"아, 요즘 보이는 이 타락이라니!\""], ["he repeated aloud, sighing.", "그가 한숨을 쉬며 큰 소리로 되뇌었다."]], [["At that moment something seemed to sting Raskolnikov; in an instant a complete revulsion of feeling came over him.", "바로 그 순간 무언가가 라스콜니코프를 콕 찌르는 것 같았다. 일순간 그에게 완전한 감정의 역전이 밀려왔다."]], [["“Hey, here!”", "\"이봐, 거기!\""], ["he shouted after the policeman.", "그가 순경의 등 뒤로 소리쳤다."]], [["The latter turned round.", "순경이 돌아보았다."]], [["“Let them be!", "\"내버려 두시오!"], ["What is it to do with you?", "그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오?"], ["Let her go!", "그녀를 가게 두라고!"], ["Let him amuse himself.”", "그가 즐기게 내버려 둬.\""], ["He pointed at the dandy, “What is it to do with you?”", "그가 멋쟁이를 가리켰다. \"그게 당신과 무슨 상관이냔 말이오?\""]], [["The policeman was bewildered, and stared at him open-eyed.", "순경은 어리둥절하여 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 그를 빤히 바라보았다."], ["Raskolnikov laughed.", "라스콜니코프는 웃었다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"원, 참!\""], ["ejaculated the policeman, with a gesture of contempt, and he walked after the dandy and the girl, probably taking Raskolnikov for a madman or something even worse.", "순경은 경멸하는 몸짓과 함께 내뱉고는, 멋쟁이와 처녀를 뒤쫓아 걸어갔다. 아마도 라스콜니코프를 미친 사람이거나, 어쩌면 그보다 더한 무엇으로 여기는 모양이었다."]], [["“He has carried off my twenty copecks,”", "\"내 이십 코페이카를 가져가 버렸군.\""], ["Raskolnikov murmured angrily when he was left alone.", "혼자 남게 되자 라스콜니코프가 화가 나서 중얼거렸다."], ["“Well, let him take as much from the other fellow to allow him to have the girl and so let it end.", "\"흥, 저쪽 작자한테서도 그만큼 받아 챙기고서 그자가 처녀를 차지하게 두라지, 그렇게 끝내 버리라고."], ["And why did I want to interfere?", "그런데 내가 왜 끼어들고 싶어 했지?"], ["Is it for me to help?", "내가 도울 처지인가?"], ["Have I any right to help?", "내게 도울 권리가 있기나 해?"], ["Let them devour each other alive--what is it to me?", "저들끼리 산 채로 서로 잡아먹게 두지, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이야?"], ["How did I dare to give him twenty copecks?", "어떻게 감히 그에게 이십 코페이카를 줬을까?"], ["Were they mine?”", "그게 내 돈이었던가?\""]], [["In spite of those strange words he felt very wretched.", "그 이상한 말들에도 불구하고 그는 몹시 비참한 기분이 들었다."], ["He sat down on the deserted seat.", "그는 텅 빈 벤치에 앉았다."], ["His thoughts strayed aimlessly....", "그의 생각은 정처 없이 헤매었다……."], ["He found it hard to fix his mind on anything at that moment.", "그 순간 그는 무엇에든 마음을 붙들어 매기가 힘들었다."], ["He longed to forget himself altogether, to forget everything, and then to wake up and begin life anew....", "그는 자기 자신을 완전히 잊고, 모든 걸 잊은 다음, 깨어나 새로운 삶을 시작하고 싶었다……."]], [["“Poor girl!”", "\"가엾은 처녀!\""], ["he said, looking at the empty corner where she had sat--“She will come to herself and weep, and then her mother will find out....", "그녀가 앉아 있던 텅 빈 구석을 바라보며 그가 말했다. \"정신을 차리면 울겠지, 그러면 어머니가 알게 될 테고……."], ["She will give her a beating, a horrible, shameful beating and then maybe, turn her out of doors....", "어머니는 그애를 때릴 거야, 끔찍하고 망측하게 두들겨 패겠지, 그러고는 어쩌면 문밖으로 내쫓을지도 몰라……."], ["And even if she does not, the Darya Frantsovnas will get wind of it, and the girl will soon be slipping out on the sly here and there.", "설령 내쫓지 않더라도, 다리야 프란초브나 같은 여자들이 낌새를 챌 테고, 그러면 그 처녀는 곧 이리저리 몰래 빠져나다니기 시작하겠지."], ["Then there will be the hospital directly (that’s always the luck of those girls with respectable mothers, who go wrong on the sly) and then...", "그러고는 곧장 병원행이고(점잖은 어머니를 둔, 몰래 빗나가는 그런 처녀들의 운명이란 늘 그렇지), 그러고는……, 또 병원……, 술……, 선술집……, 그리고 또 병원, 이삼 년 안에 폐인이 되어, 열여덟이나 열아홉에 인생이 끝나 버리는 거야……."], ["again the hospital... drink...", "내가 그런 경우를 못 봤던가?"], ["the taverns... and more hospital, in two or three years--a wreck, and her life over at eighteen or nineteen....", "그애들이 어쩌다 그 지경이 됐냐고?"], ["Have not I seen cases like that? And how have they been brought to it?", "그래, 다들 그런 식으로 그 지경이 된 거지."], ["Why, they’ve all come to it like that.", "윽! 하지만 그게 무슨 상관이야?"], ["Ugh! But what does it matter?", "그게 마땅한 거라고들 하지."], ["That’s as it should be, they tell us. A certain percentage, they tell us, must every year go...", "매년 일정한 퍼센트가, 그렇게들 말하더군, 그쪽으로……, 악마에게로 가야 한다고, 아마 그래야 나머지가 순결하게 남아, 방해받지 않을 수 있다는 거겠지."], ["that way...", "퍼센트라!"], ["to the devil, I suppose, so that the rest may remain chaste, and not be interfered with. A percentage!", "저들은 참 근사한 말들을 가졌단 말이야."], ["What splendid words they have; they are so scientific, so consolatory....", "어찌나 과학적이고, 어찌나 위안이 되는지……."], ["Once you’ve said ‘percentage’ there’s nothing more to worry about.", "일단 '퍼센트'라고 말해 버리면 더는 걱정할 게 없어지지."], ["If we had any other word... maybe we might feel more uneasy....", "만약 다른 말이었다면……, 어쩌면 우리는 좀 더 불안했을지도 몰라……."], ["But what if Dounia were one of the percentage!", "그런데 만약 두냐가 그 퍼센트의 하나라면!"], ["Of another one if not that one?", "그 하나가 아니라면 또 다른 하나라면?\""]], [["“But where am I going?”", "\"그런데 나는 어디로 가고 있는 거지?\""], ["he thought suddenly.", "그가 문득 생각했다."], ["“Strange, I came out for something.", "\"이상하군, 나는 무언가 때문에 나왔는데."], ["As soon as I had read the letter I came out....", "편지를 읽자마자 나는 밖으로 나왔어……."], ["I was going to Vassilyevsky Ostrov, to Razumihin.", "바실리옙스키 섬으로, 라주미힌에게 가려던 거였지."], ["That’s what it was... now I remember.", "그래, 바로 그거였어……, 이제야 기억나는군."], ["What for, though?", "그런데 무엇 때문이지?"], ["And what put the idea of going to Razumihin into my head just now?", "게다가 하필 지금 라주미힌에게 가야겠다는 생각이 어쩌다 내 머릿속에 떠오른 거지?"], ["That’s curious.”", "그것참 묘하군.\""]], [["He wondered at himself.", "그는 스스로에게 놀랐다."], ["Razumihin was one of his old comrades at the university.", "라주미힌은 대학 시절의 옛 동료들 가운데 하나였다."], ["It was remarkable that Raskolnikov had hardly any friends at the university; he kept aloof from everyone, went to see no one, and did not welcome anyone who came to see him, and indeed everyone soon gave him up.", "라스콜니코프가 대학에 친구라고는 거의 없었다는 점은 주목할 만했다. 그는 모두에게서 거리를 두었고, 아무도 찾아가지 않았으며, 누가 자기를 찾아와도 반기지 않았으니, 사실 모두가 곧 그를 단념해 버렸다."], ["He took no part in the students’ gatherings, amusements or conversations.", "그는 학생들의 모임이나 오락, 대화에 끼지 않았다."], ["He worked with great intensity without sparing himself, and he was respected for this, but no one liked him.", "그는 자신을 아끼지 않고 대단한 열정으로 공부했고, 그 점에서 존경을 받았으나, 아무도 그를 좋아하지는 않았다."], ["He was very poor, and there was a sort of haughty pride and reserve about him, as though he were keeping something to himself.", "그는 몹시 가난했고, 무언가를 혼자 간직하고 있기라도 한 듯, 일종의 오만한 자존심과 과묵함을 지니고 있었다."], ["He seemed to some of his comrades to look down upon them all as children, as though he were superior in development, knowledge and convictions, as though their beliefs and interests were beneath him.", "몇몇 동료에게 그는, 발달이나 지식, 신념에서 자신이 우월하기라도 한 듯, 그들의 믿음과 관심사가 자기보다 한참 아래에 있기라도 한 듯, 그들 모두를 어린애처럼 내려다보는 사람으로 비쳤다."]], [["With Razumihin he had got on, or, at least, he was more unreserved and communicative with him.", "라주미힌과는 사이가 잘 맞았다, 아니, 적어도 그에게만큼은 더 거리낌 없고 더 흉금을 털어놓았다."], ["Indeed it was impossible to be on any other terms with Razumihin.", "사실 라주미힌과는 다른 식의 관계를 맺는 것이 불가능했다."], ["He was an exceptionally good-humoured and candid youth, good-natured to the point of simplicity, though both depth and dignity lay concealed under that simplicity.", "그는 유난히 쾌활하고 솔직한 청년으로, 순박할 만큼 선량했는데, 그 순박함 아래에는 깊이와 위엄이 감추어져 있었다."], ["The better of his comrades understood this, and all were fond of him.", "동료들 중 나은 자들은 이 점을 알았고, 모두가 그를 좋아했다."], ["He was extremely intelligent, though he was certainly rather a simpleton at times.", "그는 대단히 영리했지만, 때로는 확실히 다소 어수룩하기도 했다."], ["He was of striking appearance--tall, thin, black-haired and always badly shaved.", "그는 눈에 띄는 외모를 지녔으니, 키가 크고 마른 데다 머리칼이 검고 늘 면도가 엉성했다."], ["He was sometimes uproarious and was reputed to be of great physical strength.", "그는 이따금 떠들썩하게 굴었고, 굉장한 완력의 소유자로 정평이 나 있었다."], ["One night, when out in a festive company, he had with one blow laid a gigantic policeman on his back.", "어느 날 밤, 흥겨운 무리와 어울려 나갔다가, 그는 단 한 방에 거인 같은 순경 하나를 나가떨어지게 한 적도 있었다."], ["There was no limit to his drinking powers, but he could abstain from drink altogether; he sometimes went too far in his pranks; but he could do without pranks altogether.", "그의 주량에는 한계가 없었으나, 술을 아예 끊을 수도 있었다. 이따금 장난이 지나칠 때도 있었으나, 장난 따위 아예 안 할 수도 있었다."], ["Another thing striking about Razumihin, no failure distressed him, and it seemed as though no unfavourable circumstances could crush him.", "라주미힌의 또 한 가지 두드러진 점은, 어떤 실패도 그를 괴롭히지 못했고, 어떤 불리한 처지도 그를 짓누르지 못할 듯하다는 것이었다."], ["He could lodge anywhere, and bear the extremes of cold and hunger.", "그는 어디서든 묵을 수 있었고, 극심한 추위와 굶주림도 견딜 수 있었다."], ["He was very poor, and kept himself entirely on what he could earn by work of one sort or another.", "그는 몹시 가난했고, 이런저런 일로 벌어들이는 것만으로 온전히 자기 생계를 꾸렸다."], ["He knew of no end of resources by which to earn money.", "그는 돈을 벌 수단이라면 끝도 없이 알고 있었다."], ["He spent one whole winter without lighting his stove, and used to declare that he liked it better, because one slept more soundly in the cold.", "한번은 겨우내 난로에 불 한 번 때지 않고 지냈는데, 추운 데서 잠이 더 잘 온다며 그편이 더 좋다고 단언하곤 했다."], ["For the present he, too, had been obliged to give up the university, but it was only for a time, and he was working with all his might to save enough to return to his studies again.", "지금으로서는 그 역시 대학을 그만둘 수밖에 없었으나, 그건 잠시일 뿐이었고, 그는 다시 학업으로 돌아가기 위한 돈을 마련하려 온 힘을 다해 일하고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov had not been to see him for the last four months, and Razumihin did not even know his address.", "라스콜니코프는 지난 넉 달 동안 그를 찾아간 적이 없었고, 라주미힌은 그의 주소조차 몰랐다."], ["About two months before, they had met in the street, but Raskolnikov had turned away and even crossed to the other side that he might not be observed.", "두 달쯤 전에 두 사람은 거리에서 마주쳤으나, 라스콜니코프는 눈에 띄지 않으려고 몸을 돌려 길 건너편으로 건너가기까지 했다."], ["And though Razumihin noticed him, he passed him by, as he did not want to annoy him.", "그리고 라주미힌은 그를 알아보았으면서도, 그를 성가시게 하고 싶지 않아 그냥 지나쳐 주었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["“Of course, I’ve been meaning lately to go to Razumihin’s to ask for work, to ask him to get me lessons or something...”", "“물론 요즘 나는 라주미힌한테 가서 일거리를 부탁할까, 가정교사 자리라도 얻어 달라고 부탁할까, 그런 생각을 했지…….” 라스콜니코프는 생각했다."], ["Raskolnikov thought, “but what help can he be to me now?", "“하지만 그가 지금 나에게 무슨 도움이 될 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Suppose he gets me lessons, suppose he shares his last farthing with me, if he has any farthings, so that I could get some boots and make myself tidy enough to give lessons...", "가정교사 자리를 구해 준다 치자, 푼돈이라도 있다면 마지막 한 푼까지 나눠 준다 치자, 그래서 내가 장화라도 사고 가정교사를 할 만큼 말끔히 차려입을 수 있다 치자……."], ["hm...", "흠……."], ["Well and what then?", "그래, 그러고 나서는?"], ["What shall I do with the few coppers I earn?", "내가 벌어들인 몇 푼의 동전으로 무엇을 한단 말인가?"], ["That’s not what I want now.", "지금 내가 원하는 건 그게 아니야."], ["It’s really absurd for me to go to Razumihin....”", "라주미힌한테 간다는 건 정말이지 터무니없는 일이지…….”"]], [["The question why he was now going to Razumihin agitated him even more than he was himself aware; he kept uneasily seeking for some sinister significance in this apparently ordinary action.", "자신이 지금 왜 라주미힌에게 가고 있는가 하는 의문은, 그 자신이 의식하는 것보다 훨씬 더 그를 동요시켰다. 그는 이 겉보기에 평범한 행동 속에서 어떤 불길한 의미를 불안하게 찾고 또 찾았다."]], [["“Could I have expected to set it all straight and to find a way out by means of Razumihin alone?”", "“정말로 라주미힌 하나만으로 이 모든 걸 바로잡고 빠져나갈 길을 찾을 수 있다고 기대했던 걸까?”"], ["he asked himself in perplexity.", "그는 당혹스러워하며 자신에게 물었다."]], [["He pondered and rubbed his forehead, and, strange to say, after long musing, suddenly, as if it were spontaneously and by chance, a fantastic thought came into his head.", "그는 곰곰이 생각하며 이마를 문질렀다. 그런데 이상하게도, 오랜 사색 끝에 갑자기, 마치 저절로 우연히 떠오른 듯, 한 가지 기이한 생각이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다."]], [["“Hm... to Razumihin’s,”", "“흠……, 라주미힌한테로.”"], ["he said all at once, calmly, as though he had reached a final determination.", "그는 마치 최종 결심에 다다른 사람처럼 침착하게 단번에 말했다."], ["“I shall go to Razumihin’s of course, but... not now.", "“라주미힌한테 가는 거야, 물론, 하지만……, 지금은 아니지."], ["I shall go to him... on the next day after It, when It will be over and everything will begin afresh....”", "그에게 가는 거야……, 그 일 다음 날에, 그 일이 다 끝나고 모든 것이 새롭게 시작될 때…….”"]], [["And suddenly he realised what he was thinking.", "그러다 그는 문득 자신이 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지를 깨달았다."]], [["“After It,”", "“그 일 다음에라니!” 그는 벤치에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다."], ["he shouted, jumping up from the seat, “but is It really going to happen?", "“하지만 정말로 그 일이 일어난단 말인가? 정말로 그게 일어날 수 있단 말인가?”"], ["Is it possible it really will happen?”", "그는 벤치를 떠나 거의 뛰다시피 걸어갔다. 집으로, 발길을 돌리려 했으나 집으로 돌아간다는 생각이 갑자기 격렬한 혐오로 그를 가득 채웠다."], ["He left the seat, and went off almost at a run; he meant to turn back, homewards, but the thought of going home suddenly filled him with intense loathing; in that hole, in that awful little cupboard of his, all _this_ had for a month past been growing up in him; and he walked on at random.", "바로 그 구멍 속에서, 그 끔찍하고 작은 벽장 같은 방에서, 이 모든 일이 지난 한 달 동안 그의 안에서 자라나고 있었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 발길 닿는 대로 걸어갔다."]], [["His nervous shudder had passed into a fever that made him feel shivering; in spite of the heat he felt cold.", "신경의 떨림은 어느새 그를 오슬오슬 떨게 하는 열기로 변해 있었다. 더위에도 불구하고 그는 한기를 느꼈다."], ["With a kind of effort he began almost unconsciously, from some inner craving, to stare at all the objects before him, as though looking for something to distract his attention; but he did not succeed, and kept dropping every moment into brooding.", "그는 일종의 안간힘으로, 거의 무의식적으로, 어떤 내면의 갈망에 떠밀려, 마치 주의를 다른 데로 돌릴 무언가를 찾는 사람처럼 눈앞의 모든 사물을 뚫어지게 바라보기 시작했다. 그러나 뜻대로 되지 않았고, 매 순간 다시 골똘한 생각 속으로 빠져들고 말았다."], ["When with a start he lifted his head again and looked round, he forgot at once what he had just been thinking about and even where he was going.", "흠칫하며 고개를 들어 다시 둘러볼 때면, 그는 방금 무엇을 생각하고 있었는지, 심지어 자신이 어디로 가고 있었는지조차 곧바로 잊어버렸다."], ["In this way he walked right across Vassilyevsky Ostrov, came out on to the Lesser Neva, crossed the bridge and turned towards the islands.", "이런 식으로 그는 바실리옙스키 섬을 가로질러, 작은 네바 강가로 나와 다리를 건너 여러 섬 쪽으로 방향을 틀었다."], ["The greenness and freshness were at first restful to his weary eyes after the dust of the town and the huge houses that hemmed him in and weighed upon him.", "도시의 먼지와, 그를 둘러싸고 짓누르던 거대한 건물들 뒤에 마주한 녹음과 신선함은 처음에는 그의 지친 눈에 안식이 되어 주었다."], ["Here there were no taverns, no stifling closeness, no stench.", "여기에는 선술집도, 숨 막히는 답답함도, 악취도 없었다."], ["But soon these new pleasant sensations passed into morbid irritability.", "그러나 곧 이 새롭고 기분 좋은 감각들은 병적인 신경질로 바뀌었다."], ["Sometimes he stood still before a brightly painted summer villa standing among green foliage, he gazed through the fence, he saw in the distance smartly dressed women on the verandahs and balconies, and children running in the gardens.", "이따금 그는 푸른 나뭇잎 사이에 자리한, 밝게 칠한 별장 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 울타리 너머를 들여다보았고, 멀리 베란다와 발코니에 말쑥하게 차려입은 여인들과, 정원에서 뛰노는 아이들을 보았다."], ["The flowers especially caught his attention; he gazed at them longer than at anything.", "특히 꽃들이 그의 눈길을 사로잡았다. 그는 그 무엇보다도 오래도록 꽃들을 바라보았다."], ["He was met, too, by luxurious carriages and by men and women on horseback; he watched them with curious eyes and forgot about them before they had vanished from his sight.", "그는 또한 호화로운 마차들과 말 탄 남녀들과도 마주쳤다. 그는 호기심 어린 눈으로 그들을 바라보다가, 그들이 시야에서 사라지기도 전에 그들에 관해 잊어버렸다."], ["Once he stood still and counted his money; he found he had thirty copecks.", "한번은 그가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 자기 돈을 세어 보았다. 삼십 코페이카가 있었다."], ["“Twenty to the policeman, three to Nastasya for the letter, so I must have given forty-seven or fifty to the Marmeladovs yesterday,”", "“경찰한테 이십, 편지 때문에 나스타시야한테 삼, 그러니까 어제 마르멜라도프네 사람들에게 사십칠이나 오십을 준 셈이군.”"], ["he thought, reckoning it up for some unknown reason, but he soon forgot with what object he had taken the money out of his pocket.", "무슨 영문인지 그는 그렇게 셈을 따져 보며 생각했지만, 곧 자신이 무슨 목적으로 주머니에서 돈을 꺼냈는지조차 잊어버렸다."], ["He recalled it on passing an eating-house or tavern, and felt that he was hungry....", "어느 음식점인지 선술집인지 앞을 지나면서 그는 그것을 떠올렸고, 자신이 배가 고프다는 것을 느꼈다……."], ["Going into the tavern he drank a glass of vodka and ate a pie of some sort.", "선술집으로 들어가 그는 보드카 한 잔을 마시고 무슨 파이 같은 것을 먹었다."], ["He finished eating it as he walked away.", "걸어 나오면서 그는 파이를 다 먹었다."], ["It was a long while since he had taken vodka and it had an effect upon him at once, though he only drank a wineglassful.", "보드카를 입에 댄 지 오래된지라 그것은 비록 한 잔뿐이었는데도 즉시 그에게 작용했다."], ["His legs felt suddenly heavy and a great drowsiness came upon him.", "그의 두 다리가 갑자기 무거워지고 굉장한 졸음이 밀려왔다."], ["He turned homewards, but reaching Petrovsky Ostrov he stopped completely exhausted, turned off the road into the bushes, sank down upon the grass and instantly fell asleep.", "그는 집 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸으나, 페트롭스키 섬에 이르렀을 때 완전히 기진맥진하여 멈춰 섰고, 길에서 벗어나 덤불 속으로 들어가 풀밭에 주저앉더니 그 자리에서 곧바로 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["In a morbid condition of the brain, dreams often have a singular actuality, vividness, and extraordinary semblance of reality.", "병적인 상태의 뇌에서는 꿈이 종종 기이한 현실감과 생생함, 그리고 더없이 실제와 흡사한 모습을 띠곤 한다."], ["At times monstrous images are created, but the setting and the whole picture are so truth-like and filled with details so delicate, so unexpectedly, but so artistically consistent, that the dreamer, were he an artist like Pushkin or Turgenev even, could never have invented them in the waking state.", "때로는 기괴한 형상들이 만들어지지만, 그 배경과 전체 정경이 너무도 진실에 가깝고, 너무도 섬세하면서도 의외이면서도 예술적으로 일관된 세부로 가득해서, 꿈꾸는 자가 설령 푸시킨이나 투르게네프 같은 예술가라 할지라도 깨어 있는 상태에서는 결코 만들어 낼 수 없을 정도다."], ["Such sick dreams always remain long in the memory and make a powerful impression on the overwrought and deranged nervous system.", "그런 병든 꿈들은 언제나 오래도록 기억에 남아, 과도하게 곤두서고 어지러워진 신경 계통에 강렬한 인상을 남긴다."]], [["Raskolnikov had a fearful dream.", "라스콜니코프는 무서운 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["He dreamt he was back in his childhood in the little town of his birth.", "꿈속에서 그는 다시 어린 시절로 돌아가, 자신이 태어난 작은 마을에 있었다."], ["He was a child about seven years old, walking into the country with his father on the evening of a holiday.", "그는 일곱 살쯤 된 어린아이였고, 어느 축일 저녁 아버지와 함께 시골길을 걷고 있었다."], ["It was a grey and heavy day, the country was exactly as he remembered it; indeed he recalled it far more vividly in his dream than he had done in memory.", "흐리고 무거운 날이었고, 시골 풍경은 그가 기억하던 그대로였다. 아니, 사실 그는 기억 속에서보다 꿈속에서 그것을 훨씬 더 생생하게 떠올렸다."], ["The little town stood on a level flat as bare as the hand, not even a willow near it; only in the far distance, a copse lay, a dark blur on the very edge of the horizon.", "작은 마을은 손바닥처럼 헐벗은 평평한 들판 위에 서 있었고, 근처에는 버드나무 한 그루조차 없었다. 다만 저 멀리 지평선의 맨 가장자리에 잡목림이 하나, 거뭇한 얼룩처럼 누워 있을 뿐이었다."], ["A few paces beyond the last market garden stood a tavern, a big tavern, which had always aroused in him a feeling of aversion, even of fear, when he walked by it with his father.", "마지막 채마밭에서 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에 선술집이 하나 서 있었는데, 큼직한 선술집이었다. 아버지와 함께 그 앞을 지날 때면 그것은 늘 그의 마음속에 혐오감을, 심지어 두려움까지 불러일으켰다."], ["There was always a crowd there, always shouting, laughter and abuse, hideous hoarse singing and often fighting.", "거기에는 늘 사람들이 북적였고, 늘 고함과 웃음과 욕설, 흉측하고 쉰 목소리의 노래, 그리고 종종 싸움이 벌어졌다."], ["Drunken and horrible-looking figures were hanging about the tavern.", "술 취한, 끔찍한 몰골의 사내들이 선술집 주변을 어슬렁거렸다."], ["He used to cling close to his father, trembling all over when he met them.", "그들과 마주치면 그는 온몸을 떨며 아버지에게 바짝 매달리곤 했다."], ["Near the tavern the road became a dusty track, the dust of which was always black.", "선술집 근처에서 길은 먼지투성이 오솔길로 바뀌었는데, 그 먼지는 늘 검었다."], ["It was a winding road, and about a hundred paces further on, it turned to the right to the graveyard.", "구불구불한 길이었고, 백 걸음쯤 더 가면 오른쪽으로 꺾여 묘지로 이어졌다."], ["In the middle of the graveyard stood a stone church with a green cupola where he used to go to mass two or three times a year with his father and mother, when a service was held in memory of his grandmother, who had long been dead, and whom he had never seen.", "묘지 한가운데에는 초록색 둥근 지붕을 인 돌로 지은 교회가 서 있었는데, 그는 일 년에 두세 번씩 아버지 어머니와 함께 그곳으로 미사를 드리러 가곤 했다. 오래전에 돌아가셔서 그가 한 번도 본 적 없는 할머니를 추모하는 예배가 열릴 때였다."], ["On these occasions they used to take on a white dish tied up in a table napkin a special sort of rice pudding with raisins stuck in it in the shape of a cross.", "그럴 때면 그들은 식탁용 냅킨에 싸 매단 하얀 접시에, 십자가 모양으로 건포도를 박아 넣은 특별한 쌀 푸딩을 담아 가져가곤 했다."], ["He loved that church, the old-fashioned, unadorned ikons and the old priest with the shaking head.", "그는 그 교회를, 그 구식의, 꾸밈없는 성상들을, 고개를 흔드는 늙은 사제를 사랑했다."], ["Near his grandmother’s grave, which was marked by a stone, was the little grave of his younger brother who had died at six months old.", "돌로 표시된 할머니의 무덤 곁에는, 생후 여섯 달 만에 죽은 그의 남동생의 작은 무덤이 있었다."], ["He did not remember him at all, but he had been told about his little brother, and whenever he visited the graveyard he used religiously and reverently to cross himself and to bow down and kiss the little grave.", "그는 그 동생을 전혀 기억하지 못했으나, 어린 동생에 관한 이야기를 들어 왔던지라, 묘지를 찾을 때면 경건하고 정성스럽게 성호를 긋고 절을 하며 그 작은 무덤에 입을 맞추곤 했다."], ["And now he dreamt that he was walking with his father past the tavern on the way to the graveyard; he was holding his father’s hand and looking with dread at the tavern.", "그런데 지금 그는 꿈속에서, 묘지로 가는 길에 아버지와 함께 선술집 앞을 지나고 있었다. 그는 아버지의 손을 잡고 두려움 어린 눈으로 선술집을 바라보았다."], ["A peculiar circumstance attracted his attention: there seemed to be some kind of festivity going on, there were crowds of gaily dressed townspeople, peasant women, their husbands, and riff-raff of all sorts, all singing and all more or less drunk.", "한 가지 특이한 광경이 그의 주의를 끌었다. 무슨 축제 같은 것이 벌어지고 있는 듯, 화려하게 차려입은 마을 사람들과 농촌 아낙들, 그 남편들, 온갖 잡것들이 무리 지어 있었는데, 모두가 노래를 부르며 너나없이 어지간히 취해 있었다."], ["Near the entrance of the tavern stood a cart, but a strange cart.", "선술집 입구 근처에는 짐마차 한 대가 서 있었는데, 이상한 짐마차였다."], ["It was one of those big carts usually drawn by heavy cart-horses and laden with casks of wine or other heavy goods.", "그것은 흔히 무거운 짐말이 끌고, 포도주 통이나 다른 무거운 짐을 싣는 그런 커다란 짐마차들 중 하나였다."], ["He always liked looking at those great cart-horses, with their long manes, thick legs, and slow even pace, drawing along a perfect mountain with no appearance of effort, as though it were easier going with a load than without it.", "그는 그 거대한 짐말들을, 긴 갈기와 굵은 다리와 느릿느릿 한결같은 걸음걸이로, 마치 짐을 진 편이 진 게 없는 편보다 오히려 더 수월하다는 듯 힘들이는 기색 하나 없이 산더미 같은 짐을 끌어가는 그 모습을 늘 바라보기 좋아했다."], ["But now, strange to say, in the shafts of such a cart he saw a thin little sorrel beast, one of those peasants’ nags which he had often seen straining their utmost under a heavy load of wood or hay, especially when the wheels were stuck in the mud or in a rut.", "그런데 지금, 이상하게도, 그런 짐마차의 끌채에 그는 깡마르고 작은 밤색 짐승 한 마리를 보았다. 그 짐승은 그가 흔히 보아 온, 무거운 장작이나 건초 짐 아래서, 특히 바퀴가 진창이나 바큇자국에 빠졌을 때 죽을힘을 다해 버둥거리던 그런 농사꾼의 늙은 말이었다."], ["And the peasants would beat them so cruelly, sometimes even about the nose and eyes, and he felt so sorry, so sorry for them that he almost cried, and his mother always used to take him away from the window.", "그럴 때면 농사꾼들은 그 말을 어찌나 잔인하게 두들겨 패는지, 때로는 콧등이며 눈까지 후려쳤고, 그는 그 짐승들이 너무도, 너무도 가여워서 거의 울 지경이 되곤 했으며, 그러면 어머니는 늘 그를 창가에서 데리고 나가곤 했다."], ["All of a sudden there was a great uproar of shouting, singing and the balalaïka, and from the tavern a number of big and very drunken peasants came out, wearing red and blue shirts and coats thrown over their shoulders.", "그런데 별안간 고함과 노래, 발랄라이카 소리로 요란한 소동이 일더니, 선술집에서 덩치 크고 잔뜩 취한 농사꾼 여럿이, 빨갛고 파란 셔츠를 입고 외투를 어깨에 걸친 채 우르르 몰려나왔다."]], [["“Get in, get in!”", "“타라, 타!”"], ["shouted one of them, a young thick-necked peasant with a fleshy face red as a carrot.", "그들 중 하나가 소리쳤다. 목이 굵고 젊은 농사꾼으로, 당근처럼 시뻘건 살집 좋은 얼굴이었다."], ["“I’ll take you all, get in!”", "“다들 태워 주마, 타라!”"]], [["But at once there was an outbreak of laughter and exclamations in the crowd.", "그러자 곧장 군중 속에서 웃음과 외침이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Take us all with a beast like that!”", "“저런 짐승을 가지고 우리를 다 태우겠다고!”"]], [["“Why, Mikolka, are you crazy to put a nag like that in such a cart?”", "“이봐, 미콜카, 저런 비루먹은 말을 이런 짐마차에 매다니 미친 거 아냐?”"]], [["“And this mare is twenty if she is a day, mates!”", "“게다가 이 암말은 적어도 스무 살은 됐다고, 친구들!”"]], [["“Get in, I’ll take you all,”", "“타라, 다들 태워 주마.”"], ["Mikolka shouted again, leaping first into the cart, seizing the reins and standing straight up in front.", "미콜카가 다시 소리치며 맨 먼저 짐마차에 펄쩍 뛰어올라 고삐를 움켜쥐고 앞쪽에 똑바로 버티고 섰다."], ["“The bay has gone with Matvey,”", "“밤색 말은 마트베이가 끌고 가 버렸어.”"], ["he shouted from the cart--“and this brute, mates, is just breaking my heart, I feel as if I could kill her.", "그가 짐마차 위에서 소리쳤다. “그래서 이 못된 것이, 친구들, 내 속을 어찌나 썩이는지, 죽여 버리고 싶을 지경이라고."], ["She’s just eating her head off.", "그저 처먹기만 하잖아. 타라니까!"], ["Get in, I tell you! I’ll make her gallop!", "내가 이놈을 달리게 만들 거야!"], ["She’ll gallop!”", "달리고말고!”"], ["and he picked up the whip, preparing himself with relish to flog the little mare.", "그러고는 채찍을 집어 들더니, 신이 나서 작은 암말을 후려칠 채비를 했다."]], [["“Get in!", "“타!"], ["Come along!”", "어서 와!”"], ["The crowd laughed.", "군중이 웃어 댔다."], ["“D’you hear, she’ll gallop!”", "“들었지, 이놈이 달린다잖아!”"]], [["“Gallop indeed!", "“달린다고!"], ["She has not had a gallop in her for the last ten years!”", "저놈은 지난 십 년 동안 한 번도 달려 본 적이 없을 텐데!”"]], [["“She’ll jog along!”", "“그냥 터덜터덜 가겠지!”"]], [["“Don’t you mind her, mates, bring a whip each of you, get ready!”", "“신경 쓰지 마, 친구들, 다들 채찍을 하나씩 들고 준비해!”"]], [["“All right!", "“좋았어!"], ["Give it to her!”", "한 대 갈겨!”"]], [["They all clambered into Mikolka’s cart, laughing and making jokes.", "그들은 모두 웃고 농지거리하며 미콜카의 짐마차에 기어올랐다."], ["Six men got in and there was still room for more.", "여섯 사내가 올라탔는데도 자리가 더 남아 있었다."], ["They hauled in a fat, rosy-cheeked woman.", "그들은 살집 좋고 발그레한 뺨의 여자 하나를 끌어 올렸다."], ["She was dressed in red cotton, in a pointed, beaded headdress and thick leather shoes; she was cracking nuts and laughing.", "그녀는 빨간 무명옷을 입고, 끝이 뾰족하고 구슬을 단 머리쓰개를 쓰고, 두꺼운 가죽신을 신고 있었다. 그녀는 호두를 까먹으며 웃고 있었다."], ["The crowd round them was laughing too and indeed, how could they help laughing?", "그들을 둘러싼 군중도 웃고 있었으니, 사실 어찌 웃지 않을 수 있겠는가?"], ["That wretched nag was to drag all the cartload of them at a gallop!", "저 가련한 늙은 말이 그 짐마차에 탄 사람들을 몽땅 끌고 달려야 하는 판이니!"], ["Two young fellows in the cart were just getting whips ready to help Mikolka.", "짐마차 위의 두 젊은 녀석은 미콜카를 거들려고 막 채찍을 챙기고 있었다."], ["With the cry of “now,”", "“이랴!”"], ["the mare tugged with all her might, but far from galloping, could scarcely move forward; she struggled with her legs, gasping and shrinking from the blows of the three whips which were showered upon her like hail.", "하는 외침과 함께 암말은 죽을힘을 다해 끌었지만, 달리기는커녕 앞으로 거의 움직이지도 못했다. 그것은 우박처럼 쏟아지는 세 개의 채찍질에 헐떡이며 움츠러든 채 다리를 버둥거렸다."], ["The laughter in the cart and in the crowd was redoubled, but Mikolka flew into a rage and furiously thrashed the mare, as though he supposed she really could gallop.", "짐마차 위와 군중 속의 웃음이 곱절로 커졌으나, 미콜카는 격분하여, 마치 정말로 이놈이 달릴 수 있다고 믿기라도 하는 양 미친 듯이 암말을 후려쳤다."]], [["“Let me get in, too, mates,”", "“나도 좀 태워 줘, 친구들.”"], ["shouted a young man in the crowd whose appetite was aroused.", "군중 속에서 한 젊은이가 흥이 동했는지 소리쳤다."]], [["“Get in, all get in,”", "“타, 다들 타.”"], ["cried Mikolka, “she will draw you all.", "미콜카가 외쳤다. “이놈이 너희 다 끌고 갈 거야."], ["I’ll beat her to death!”", "내가 이놈을 죽도록 패 줄 테니!”"], ["And he thrashed and thrashed at the mare, beside himself with fury.", "그러고는 분노에 제정신을 잃고 암말을 후려치고 또 후려쳤다."]], [["“Father, father,”", "“아버지, 아버지.”"], ["he cried, “father, what are they doing?", "그가 울먹였다. “아버지, 저 사람들이 뭐 하는 거예요?"], ["Father, they are beating the poor horse!”", "아버지, 저 사람들이 불쌍한 말을 때리고 있어요!”"]], [["“Come along, come along!”", "“가자, 가자!”"], ["said his father.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["“They are drunken and foolish, they are in fun; come away, don’t look!”", "“저들은 취해서 어리석은 짓을 하는 거란다, 장난질이야. 가자, 보지 마라!”"], ["and he tried to draw him away, but he tore himself away from his hand, and, beside himself with horror, ran to the horse.", "그러고는 그를 끌어당기려 했지만, 그는 아버지의 손을 뿌리치고는, 공포에 제정신을 잃은 채 말에게로 달려갔다."], ["The poor beast was in a bad way.", "가련한 짐승은 처참한 꼴이었다."], ["She was gasping, standing still, then tugging again and almost falling.", "그것은 헐떡이며 멈춰 섰다가, 다시 끌어 보려 안간힘을 쓰다가 거의 쓰러질 지경이었다."]], [["“Beat her to death,”", "“죽도록 패 버려.”"], ["cried Mikolka, “it’s come to that.", "미콜카가 소리쳤다. “이젠 그 수밖에 없어."], ["I’ll do for her!”", "내가 이놈을 끝장내 주마!”"]], [["“What are you about, are you a Christian, you devil?”", "“대체 무슨 짓이야, 너 그리스도교인 맞아, 이 악마 같은 놈아?”"], ["shouted an old man in the crowd.", "군중 속에서 한 노인이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Did anyone ever see the like?", "“이런 꼴을 누가 본 적이나 있나?"], ["A wretched nag like that pulling such a cartload,”", "저런 비루먹은 말이 저런 짐을 끌다니.”"], ["said another.", "또 다른 사람이 말했다."]], [["“You’ll kill her,”", "“넌 저놈을 죽일 거야.”"], ["shouted the third.", "세 번째 사람이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Don’t meddle!", "“참견 마!"], ["It’s my property, I’ll do what I choose.", "내 거니까, 내 맘대로 한다고."], ["Get in, more of you!", "타, 더 타라고!"], ["Get in, all of you!", "다들 타!"], ["I will have her go at a gallop!...”", "내가 이놈을 기어이 달리게 만들 테니까……!”"]], [["All at once laughter broke into a roar and covered everything: the mare, roused by the shower of blows, began feebly kicking.", "별안간 웃음이 와르르 터져 나와 모든 것을 뒤덮었다. 빗발치는 채찍질에 발끈한 암말이 힘없이 뒷발질을 하기 시작한 것이다."], ["Even the old man could not help smiling.", "노인조차 웃음을 참지 못했다."], ["To think of a wretched little beast like that trying to kick!", "저런 비루먹은 작은 짐승이 뒷발질을 하려 들다니!"]], [["Two lads in the crowd snatched up whips and ran to the mare to beat her about the ribs.", "군중 속의 두 사내가 채찍을 낚아채더니 옆구리를 후려치려고 암말에게 달려갔다."], ["One ran each side.", "각자 한쪽씩 맡았다."]], [["“Hit her in the face, in the eyes, in the eyes,”", "“얼굴을 갈겨, 눈을, 눈을 갈겨.”"], ["cried Mikolka.", "미콜카가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Give us a song, mates,”", "“노래나 한 곡 뽑자고, 친구들.”"], ["shouted someone in the cart and everyone in the cart joined in a riotous song, jingling a tambourine and whistling.", "짐마차 위에서 누군가 소리쳤고, 짐마차 위의 모두가 탬버린을 흔들고 휘파람을 불며 떠들썩한 노래에 함께 어울렸다."], ["The woman went on cracking nuts and laughing.", "여자는 여전히 호두를 까먹으며 웃고 있었다."]], [["... He ran beside the mare, ran in front of her, saw her being whipped across the eyes, right in the eyes!", "……그는 암말 곁에서 달렸고, 그 앞에서 달리며, 채찍이 눈을 가로질러, 바로 그 눈을 후려치는 것을 보았다!"], ["He was crying, he felt choking, his tears were streaming.", "그는 울고 있었고, 목이 메었으며, 눈물이 줄줄 흘러내렸다."], ["One of the men gave him a cut with the whip across the face, he did not feel it.", "사내들 중 하나가 채찍으로 그의 얼굴을 후려쳤지만, 그는 그것을 느끼지도 못했다."], ["Wringing his hands and screaming, he rushed up to the grey-headed old man with the grey beard, who was shaking his head in disapproval.", "두 손을 비틀며 비명을 지르면서, 그는 못마땅하다는 듯 고개를 가로젓고 있던, 잿빛 머리에 잿빛 수염의 노인에게로 달려갔다."], ["One woman seized him by the hand and would have taken him away, but he tore himself from her and ran back to the mare.", "한 여자가 그의 손을 잡고 데려가려 했으나, 그는 그녀에게서 몸을 뿌리치고 다시 암말에게로 달려갔다."], ["She was almost at the last gasp, but began kicking once more.", "그것은 거의 마지막 숨을 헐떡이고 있었으나, 또다시 뒷발질을 하기 시작했다."]], [["“I’ll teach you to kick,” Mikolka shouted ferociously.", "“네놈에게 뒷발질하는 법을 가르쳐 주마.”"], ["He threw down the whip, bent forward and picked up from the bottom of the cart a long, thick shaft, he took hold of one end with both hands and with an effort brandished it over the mare.", "미콜카가 사납게 소리쳤다. 그는 채찍을 내던지고 몸을 숙여 짐마차 바닥에서 길고 굵은 끌채 하나를 집어 들더니, 두 손으로 한쪽 끝을 움켜쥐고 안간힘을 다해 암말 위로 휘둘렀다."]], [["“He’ll crush her,”", "“저놈이 말을 으스러뜨리겠어.”"], ["was shouted round him.", "그의 주위에서 사람들이 외쳤다."], ["“He’ll kill her!”", "“저놈이 말을 죽이겠어!”"]], [["“It’s my property,”", "“내 거야.”"], ["shouted Mikolka and brought the shaft down with a swinging blow.", "미콜카가 소리치며 끌채를 힘껏 휘둘러 내리쳤다."], ["There was a sound of a heavy thud.", "둔중한 타격 소리가 났다."]], [["“Thrash her, thrash her!", "“패, 패라고!"], ["Why have you stopped?”", "왜 멈춰 섰어?”"], ["shouted voices in the crowd.", "군중 속에서 여러 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["And Mikolka swung the shaft a second time and it fell a second time on the spine of the luckless mare.", "그러자 미콜카가 두 번째로 끌채를 휘둘렀고, 그것은 다시금 불운한 암말의 등뼈 위로 떨어졌다."], ["She sank back on her haunches, but lurched forward and tugged forward with all her force, tugged first on one side and then on the other, trying to move the cart.", "암말은 뒷다리를 꺾고 주저앉았으나, 다시 앞으로 비틀거리며 온 힘을 다해 끌었다. 한쪽으로 끌었다가 또 다른 쪽으로 끌며, 짐마차를 움직여 보려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["But the six whips were attacking her in all directions, and the shaft was raised again and fell upon her a third time, then a fourth, with heavy measured blows.", "그러나 여섯 개의 채찍이 사방에서 그것을 공격했고, 끌채가 다시 들려 올라가 세 번째로, 다음엔 네 번째로 묵직하고 규칙적인 타격으로 그것 위에 떨어졌다."], ["Mikolka was in a fury that he could not kill her at one blow.", "미콜카는 단번에 그것을 죽이지 못한 것에 격분했다."]], [["“She’s a tough one,”", "“질긴 놈이군.”"], ["was shouted in the crowd.", "군중 속에서 누군가 외쳤다."]], [["“She’ll fall in a minute, mates, there will soon be an end of her,”", "“곧 쓰러질 거야, 친구들, 이제 곧 끝장날 거라고.”"], ["said an admiring spectator in the crowd.", "군중 속의 한 구경꾼이 감탄하듯 말했다."]], [["“Fetch an axe to her!", "“도끼를 가져다 줘!"], ["Finish her off,”", "끝장을 내 버려.”"], ["shouted a third.", "세 번째 사람이 외쳤다."]], [["“I’ll show you!", "“내가 본때를 보여 주지!"], ["Stand off,”", "비켜.”"], ["Mikolka screamed frantically; he threw down the shaft, stooped down in the cart and picked up an iron crowbar.", "미콜카가 미친 듯이 비명을 질렀다. 그는 끌채를 내던지고 짐마차 안에서 몸을 굽혀 쇠 지렛대를 집어 들었다."], ["“Look out,”", "“비켜.”"], ["he shouted, and with all his might he dealt a stunning blow at the poor mare.", "그가 외치며, 있는 힘을 다해 가련한 암말에게 정신이 아찔할 만큼 무서운 일격을 가했다."], ["The blow fell; the mare staggered, sank back, tried to pull, but the bar fell again with a swinging blow on her back and she fell on the ground like a log.", "타격이 떨어졌다. 암말은 휘청거리며 주저앉았다가 다시 끌어 보려 안간힘을 썼으나, 지렛대가 또다시 그것의 등 위로 힘껏 휘둘려 떨어졌고, 그것은 통나무처럼 땅바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["“Finish her off,”", "“끝장을 내라고.”"], ["shouted Mikolka and he leapt beside himself, out of the cart.", "미콜카가 소리치며 제정신을 잃고 짐마차에서 펄쩍 뛰어내렸다."], ["Several young men, also flushed with drink, seized anything they could come across--whips, sticks, poles, and ran to the dying mare.", "역시 술기운에 얼굴이 벌게진 젊은이 몇이 닥치는 대로 손에 잡히는 것을 — 채찍이며 막대기며 장대를 — 움켜쥐고 죽어 가는 암말에게로 달려갔다."], ["Mikolka stood on one side and began dealing random blows with the crowbar.", "미콜카는 한쪽에 서서 지렛대로 아무렇게나 후려치기 시작했다."], ["The mare stretched out her head, drew a long breath and died.", "암말은 목을 쭉 뻗고 길게 한 번 숨을 들이쉬더니 죽어 버렸다."]], [["“You butchered her,”", "“네놈이 도륙해 버렸어.”"], ["someone shouted in the crowd.", "군중 속에서 누군가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Why wouldn’t she gallop then?”", "“그럼 어째서 달리지 않은 거야?”"]], [["“My property!”", "“내 거라고!”"], ["shouted Mikolka, with bloodshot eyes, brandishing the bar in his hands.", "미콜카가 핏발 선 눈으로 소리치며 두 손에 지렛대를 휘둘렀다."], ["He stood as though regretting that he had nothing more to beat.", "그는 마치 더 이상 때릴 것이 없는 게 아쉽다는 듯 서 있었다."]], [["“No mistake about it, you are not a Christian,”", "“틀림없어, 넌 그리스도교인이 아니야.”"], ["many voices were shouting in the crowd.", "군중 속에서 많은 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["But the poor boy, beside himself, made his way, screaming, through the crowd to the sorrel nag, put his arms round her bleeding dead head and kissed it, kissed the eyes and kissed the lips....", "그러나 가련한 소년은 제정신을 잃고 비명을 지르며 군중을 헤치고 밤색 늙은 말에게로 다가가, 피투성이가 된 죽은 머리를 두 팔로 끌어안고 입을 맞추었다. 그 눈에 입을 맞추고, 그 입술에 입을 맞추었다……."], ["Then he jumped up and flew in a frenzy with his little fists out at Mikolka.", "그러더니 벌떡 일어나 작은 두 주먹을 내지르며 광란하듯 미콜카에게 달려들었다."], ["At that instant his father, who had been running after him, snatched him up and carried him out of the crowd.", "바로 그 순간, 줄곧 그를 뒤쫓던 아버지가 그를 낚아채어 군중 밖으로 데리고 나갔다."]], [["“Come along, come!", "“가자, 어서!"], ["Let us go home,”", "집으로 가자.”"], ["he said to him.", "아버지가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Father!", "“아버지!"], ["Why did they... kill...", "왜 저 사람들이……, 불쌍한 말을……, 죽인 거예요!”"], ["the poor horse!” he sobbed, but his voice broke and the words came in shrieks from his panting chest.", "그는 흐느꼈으나, 목소리가 갈라지며 헐떡이는 가슴에서 말이 비명처럼 터져 나왔다."]], [["“They are drunk....", "“저들은 취했단다……."], ["They are brutal... it’s not our business!”", "짐승 같은 놈들이지……, 우리가 상관할 바 아니야!”"], ["said his father.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["He put his arms round his father but he felt choked, choked.", "그는 두 팔로 아버지를 끌어안았으나, 숨이 막히고 또 막혔다."], ["He tried to draw a breath, to cry out--and woke up.", "그는 숨을 한 번 들이쉬려, 소리를 지르려 애썼고 — 그러다 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["He waked up, gasping for breath, his hair soaked with perspiration, and stood up in terror.", "그는 숨을 헐떡이며 깨어났다. 머리카락은 땀에 흠뻑 젖어 있었고, 그는 공포에 질려 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Thank God, that was only a dream,”", "“오, 하느님, 감사합니다, 그저 꿈이었어.”"], ["he said, sitting down under a tree and drawing deep breaths.", "그는 나무 아래 주저앉아 깊은 숨을 들이쉬며 말했다."], ["“But what is it?", "“하지만 이게 뭐지?"], ["Is it some fever coming on?", "무슨 열병이라도 오는 건가?"], ["Such a hideous dream!”", "이토록 흉측한 꿈을 꾸다니!”"]], [["He felt utterly broken: darkness and confusion were in his soul.", "그는 온몸이 완전히 부서진 듯한 기분이었다. 그의 영혼 속에는 어둠과 혼란이 깃들어 있었다."], ["He rested his elbows on his knees and leaned his head on his hands.", "그는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 머리를 떨구었다."]], [["“Good God!”", "“오, 하느님!”"], ["he cried, “can it be, can it be, that I shall really take an axe, that I shall strike her on the head, split her skull open...", "그가 부르짖었다."], ["that I shall tread in the sticky warm blood, break the lock, steal and tremble; hide, all spattered in the blood...", "“정말로, 정말로 내가 진짜 도끼를 들고, 그녀의 머리를 내리쳐, 그 두개골을 쪼개 버린다는 게……, 내가 끈적하고 따뜻한 피를 밟고, 자물쇠를 부수고, 훔치고 떨면서, 온통 피를 뒤집어쓴 채 숨는다는 게……, 도끼를 들고서……."], ["with the axe.... Good God, can it be?”", "오, 하느님, 정말 그럴 수 있단 말인가?”"]], [["He was shaking like a leaf as he said this.", "이 말을 하면서 그는 나뭇잎처럼 떨고 있었다."]], [["“But why am I going on like this?”", "“그런데 내가 왜 이러고 있는 거지?”"], ["he continued, sitting up again, as it were in profound amazement.", "그는 깊은 경악에 빠진 듯 다시 몸을 일으키며 말을 이었다."], ["“I knew that I could never bring myself to it, so what have I been torturing myself for till now?", "“나는 결코 그 일을 해내지 못하리라는 걸 알고 있었는데, 그렇다면 지금까지 무엇 때문에 나 자신을 괴롭혀 온 거지?"], ["Yesterday, yesterday, when I went to make that... _experiment_, yesterday I realised completely that I could never bear to do it....", "어제, 어제, 내가 그……, 예행연습을 하러 갔을 때, 어제 나는 결코 그런 짓을 견뎌 낼 수 없으리라는 걸 완전히 깨달았어……."], ["Why am I going over it again, then?", "그런데 무엇 때문에 또 이걸 되풀이하고 있는 거지?"], ["Why am I hesitating?", "무엇 때문에 망설이고 있는 거지?"], ["As I came down the stairs yesterday, I said myself that it was base, loathsome, vile, vile... the very thought of it made me feel sick and filled me with horror.", "어제 계단을 내려오면서, 나 스스로 그건 비열하고, 역겹고, 추잡하고, 추잡한 짓이라고 말했잖아……, 그걸 생각하는 것만으로도 메스껍고 공포에 휩싸였잖아."]], [["“No, I couldn’t do it, I couldn’t do it!", "“아니, 나는 그 일을 할 수 없어, 할 수 없어!"], ["Granted, granted that there is no flaw in all that reasoning, that all that I have concluded this last month is clear as day, true as arithmetic....", "그래, 인정한다, 그 모든 추론에 한 점 결함도 없다 치고, 지난 한 달 동안 내가 내린 모든 결론이 대낮처럼 명백하고 산수처럼 옳다 치자……. 오, 하느님!"], ["My God! Anyway I couldn’t bring myself to it!", "어쨌든 나는 그 일을 해낼 수 없어!"], ["I couldn’t do it, I couldn’t do it!", "나는 할 수 없어, 할 수 없다고!"], ["Why, why then am I still...?”", "그런데, 그런데 왜 나는 아직도……?”"]], [["He rose to his feet, looked round in wonder as though surprised at finding himself in this place, and went towards the bridge.", "그는 자리에서 일어나, 마치 자신이 이런 곳에 있다는 것에 놀란 듯 의아한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보고는 다리 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["He was pale, his eyes glowed, he was exhausted in every limb, but he seemed suddenly to breathe more easily.", "그는 창백했고, 두 눈은 이글거렸으며, 온몸이 사지마다 기진맥진해 있었으나, 갑자기 한결 숨쉬기가 편해진 것 같았다."], ["He felt he had cast off that fearful burden that had so long been weighing upon him, and all at once there was a sense of relief and peace in his soul.", "그는 그토록 오래도록 자신을 짓눌러 온 그 무서운 짐을 벗어 던진 듯한 기분이었고, 별안간 그의 영혼 속에 안도와 평온의 느낌이 깃들었다. “주여,”"], ["“Lord,”", "그는 기도했다."], ["he prayed, “show me my path--I renounce that accursed... dream of mine.”", "“저에게 제 길을 보여 주소서 — 저는 저 저주받은……, 저의 그 꿈을 단념하겠나이다.”"]], [["Crossing the bridge, he gazed quietly and calmly at the Neva, at the glowing red sun setting in the glowing sky.", "다리를 건너며 그는 네바 강을, 이글거리는 하늘로 지고 있는 새빨갛게 타오르는 해를 조용하고 차분하게 바라보았다."], ["In spite of his weakness he was not conscious of fatigue.", "쇠약한 몸에도 불구하고 그는 피로를 의식하지 못했다."], ["It was as though an abscess that had been forming for a month past in his heart had suddenly broken.", "마치 지난 한 달 동안 그의 가슴속에서 곪아 가던 종기가 별안간 터져 버린 것만 같았다."], ["Freedom, freedom!", "자유, 자유다!"], ["He was free from that spell, that sorcery, that obsession!", "그는 그 주술에서, 그 마법에서, 그 망상에서 벗어난 것이다!"]], [["Later on, when he recalled that time and all that happened to him during those days, minute by minute, point by point, he was superstitiously impressed by one circumstance, which, though in itself not very exceptional, always seemed to him afterwards the predestined turning-point of his fate.", "훗날 그 시절을, 그 며칠 동안 자신에게 일어난 모든 일을 시시각각, 하나하나 떠올릴 때면, 그는 한 가지 정황에 미신적인 전율을 느끼곤 했다. 그것은 그 자체로는 그다지 특별할 것이 없었으나, 그에게는 언제나 자신의 운명이 예정된 전환점처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He could never understand and explain to himself why, when he was tired and worn out, when it would have been more convenient for him to go home by the shortest and most direct way, he had returned by the Hay Market where he had no need to go.", "그는 도무지 이해할 수도, 스스로에게 설명할 수도 없었다. 어째서 자신이 지치고 기진맥진했을 때, 가장 짧고 가장 곧은 길로 집에 돌아가는 편이 더 편했을 텐데, 굳이 갈 필요도 없는 건초 시장을 거쳐 돌아왔는가를."], ["It was obviously and quite unnecessarily out of his way, though not much so.", "그리 많이는 아니었지만, 그것은 명백히, 그리고 전혀 불필요하게 길을 돌아가는 것이었다."], ["It is true that it happened to him dozens of times to return home without noticing what streets he passed through.", "물론 그가 어느 거리를 지나는지도 모른 채 집으로 돌아온 일은 수십 번이나 있었다. 그러나 그는 늘 자문하곤 했다."], ["But why, he was always asking himself, why had such an important, such a decisive and at the same time such an absolutely chance meeting happened in the Hay Market (where he had moreover no reason to go) at the very hour, the very minute of his life when he was just in the very mood and in the very circumstances in which that meeting was able to exert the gravest and most decisive influence on his whole destiny?", "어째서 그토록 중요하고, 그토록 결정적이면서 동시에 그토록 완전히 우연한 만남이, 하필 건초 시장(게다가 그가 갈 이유도 없던 곳)에서, 자신의 일생에서 바로 그 시각에, 바로 그 순간에, 그 만남이 자신의 운명 전체에 가장 중대하고 가장 결정적인 영향을 미칠 수 있는 바로 그런 기분과 바로 그런 상황에 처해 있던 바로 그때 일어났는가를."], ["As though it had been lying in wait for him on purpose!", "마치 그 만남이 일부러 그를 노리고 숨어서 기다리고 있기라도 했던 것처럼!"]], [["It was about nine o’clock when he crossed the Hay Market.", "그가 건초 시장을 가로지른 것은 아홉 시쯤이었다."], ["At the tables and the barrows, at the booths and the shops, all the market people were closing their establishments or clearing away and packing up their wares and, like their customers, were going home.", "탁자며 손수레며 노점이며 가게에서, 장사꾼들은 모두 점포를 닫거나 물건을 치우고 꾸려 넣으며, 손님들과 마찬가지로 집으로 돌아가는 중이었다."], ["Rag pickers and costermongers of all kinds were crowding round the taverns in the dirty and stinking courtyards of the Hay Market.", "건초 시장의 더럽고 악취 나는 안뜰에서는 온갖 부류의 넝마주이와 행상들이 선술집 주변에 북적이고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov particularly liked this place and the neighbouring alleys, when he wandered aimlessly in the streets.", "라스콜니코프는 거리를 정처 없이 헤맬 때면 특히 이곳과 이웃한 골목들을 좋아했다."], ["Here his rags did not attract contemptuous attention, and one could walk about in any attire without scandalising people.", "여기서는 그의 누더기가 경멸 어린 시선을 끌지 않았고, 어떤 차림새로 돌아다녀도 사람들의 빈축을 사지 않았다."], ["At the corner of an alley a huckster and his wife had two tables set out with tapes, thread, cotton handkerchiefs, etc.", "어느 골목 모퉁이에서는 한 행상과 그의 아내가 탁자 두 개에 끈이며 실이며 무명 손수건 따위를 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["They, too, had got up to go home, but were lingering in conversation with a friend, who had just come up to them.", "그들 역시 집에 가려고 일어선 참이었으나, 막 다가온 한 친구와 이야기를 나누느라 우물쭈물 머뭇거리고 있었다."], ["This friend was Lizaveta Ivanovna, or, as everyone called her, Lizaveta, the younger sister of the old pawnbroker, Alyona Ivanovna, whom Raskolnikov had visited the previous day to pawn his watch and make his _experiment_....", "이 친구는 리자베타 이바노브나, 모두가 그저 리자베타라 부르는, 늙은 전당포 노파 알료나 이바노브나의 동생이었다. 라스콜니코프는 바로 전날 그 노파를 찾아가 시계를 저당 잡히고 자신의 예행연습을 했던 것이다……."], ["He already knew all about Lizaveta and she knew him a little too.", "그는 이미 리자베타에 관해 모든 것을 알고 있었고, 그녀 또한 그를 조금은 알고 있었다."], ["She was a single woman of about thirty-five, tall, clumsy, timid, submissive and almost idiotic.", "그녀는 서른다섯쯤 된 독신녀로, 키가 크고 굼뜨며 소심하고 고분고분한, 거의 백치에 가까운 여자였다."], ["She was a complete slave and went in fear and trembling of her sister, who made her work day and night, and even beat her.", "그녀는 완전히 노예나 다름없어서, 밤낮없이 일을 시키고 심지어 매질까지 하는 언니를 두려움에 떨며 받들었다."], ["She was standing with a bundle before the huckster and his wife, listening earnestly and doubtfully.", "그녀는 보따리를 든 채 행상과 그의 아내 앞에 서서, 진지하면서도 미심쩍은 표정으로 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["They were talking of something with special warmth.", "그들은 무언가에 관해 유독 열을 올리며 이야기하는 중이었다."], ["The moment Raskolnikov caught sight of her, he was overcome by a strange sensation as it were of intense astonishment, though there was nothing astonishing about this meeting.", "라스콜니코프는 그녀를 알아본 순간, 비록 이 만남에 놀랄 만한 구석은 전혀 없었음에도, 일종의 강렬한 경악에 가까운 기이한 감각에 사로잡혔다."]], [["“You could make up your mind for yourself, Lizaveta Ivanovna,”", "“알아서 결정하시구려, 리자베타 이바노브나.”"], ["the huckster was saying aloud.", "행상이 큰 소리로 말하고 있었다."], ["“Come round to-morrow about seven.", "“내일 일곱 시쯤 오시오."], ["They will be here too.”", "그쪽 사람들도 여기 와 있을 거요.”"]], [["“To-morrow?”", "“내일이요?”"], ["said Lizaveta slowly and thoughtfully, as though unable to make up her mind.", "리자베타는 마음을 정하지 못하는 듯, 천천히 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“Upon my word, what a fright you are in of Alyona Ivanovna,”", "“원, 댁은 알료나 이바노브나를 어쩜 그리도 무서워하는지.”"], ["gabbled the huckster’s wife, a lively little woman.", "행상의 아내가, 기운차고 자그마한 여자가 재잘거렸다."], ["“I look at you, you are like some little babe.", "“댁을 보고 있자니, 꼭 갓난아기 같구려."], ["And she is not your own sister either--nothing but a step-sister and what a hand she keeps over you!”", "게다가 그 여자가 친언니도 아니잖소 — 그저 의붓언니일 뿐인데, 어쩜 그리 댁을 쥐고 흔드는지!”"]], [["“But this time don’t say a word to Alyona Ivanovna,”", "“하지만 이번엔 알료나 이바노브나한테 한마디도 하지 마시오.”"], ["her husband interrupted; “that’s my advice, but come round to us without asking.", "남편이 말을 가로챘다. “내 조언이오, 묻지도 말고 그냥 우리한테 오시구려."], ["It will be worth your while.", "그럴 만한 값어치가 있을 테니까."], ["Later on your sister herself may have a notion.”", "나중엔 언니도 알아서 짐작하겠지.”"]], [["“Am I to come?”", "“가야 하나요?”"]], [["“About seven o’clock to-morrow.", "“내일 일곱 시쯤이오."], ["And they will be here.", "그쪽 사람들도 여기 와 있을 거고."], ["You will be able to decide for yourself.”", "댁이 직접 결정할 수 있을 거요.”"]], [["“And we’ll have a cup of tea,”", "“그리고 차도 한 잔 합시다.”"], ["added his wife.", "그의 아내가 덧붙였다."]], [["“All right, I’ll come,”", "“좋아요, 갈게요.”"], ["said Lizaveta, still pondering, and she began slowly moving away.", "리자베타는 여전히 곰곰이 생각하며 말하고는, 천천히 자리를 뜨기 시작했다."]], [["Raskolnikov had just passed and heard no more.", "라스콜니코프는 막 그 곁을 지나친 참이라 더는 듣지 못했다."], ["He passed softly, unnoticed, trying not to miss a word.", "그는 한마디도 놓치지 않으려 애쓰며, 들키지 않게 살그머니 지나갔다."], ["His first amazement was followed by a thrill of horror, like a shiver running down his spine.", "처음의 경악에 뒤이어, 마치 등줄기를 타고 내리는 한기처럼 공포의 전율이 그를 휩쓸었다."], ["He had learnt, he had suddenly quite unexpectedly learnt, that the next day at seven o’clock Lizaveta, the old woman’s sister and only companion, would be away from home and that therefore at seven o’clock precisely the old woman _would be left alone_.", "그는 알게 된 것이다, 갑자기 너무도 뜻밖에 알게 된 것이다, 내일 일곱 시에 그 노파의 동생이자 유일한 동거인인 리자베타가 집을 비울 것이며, 따라서 정확히 일곱 시에 그 노파가 혼자 남게 되리라는 것을."]], [["He was only a few steps from his lodging.", "그의 하숙집까지는 불과 몇 걸음이었다."], ["He went in like a man condemned to death.", "그는 사형 선고를 받은 사람처럼 안으로 들어갔다."], ["He thought of nothing and was incapable of thinking; but he felt suddenly in his whole being that he had no more freedom of thought, no will, and that everything was suddenly and irrevocably decided.", "그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았고, 생각할 수도 없었다. 다만 그는 자기 존재 전체로 갑자기 느꼈다, 자신에게 더는 생각의 자유도, 의지도 없으며, 모든 것이 갑작스럽게 그리고 돌이킬 수 없이 결정되어 버렸음을."]], [["Certainly, if he had to wait whole years for a suitable opportunity, he could not reckon on a more certain step towards the success of the plan than that which had just presented itself.", "분명, 설령 적당한 기회를 얻기 위해 몇 년을 통째로 기다려야 했다 한들, 그는 방금 자신 앞에 나타난 것보다 그 계획의 성공을 향한 더 확실한 발판을 기대할 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["In any case, it would have been difficult to find out beforehand and with certainty, with greater exactness and less risk, and without dangerous inquiries and investigations, that next day at a certain time an old woman, on whose life an attempt was contemplated, would be at home and entirely alone.", "어찌 됐든, 위험한 캐물음이나 탐색 없이, 더 정확하게 그리고 더 적은 위험으로, 다음 날 어느 시각에 목숨을 노리는 그 노파가 집에 완전히 혼자 있으리라는 것을 미리, 그리고 확실하게 알아낸다는 것은 어려운 일이었을 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["Later on Raskolnikov happened to find out why the huckster and his wife had invited Lizaveta.", "나중에 라스콜니코프는 그 잡화상과 그의 아내가 무슨 일로 리자베타를 불렀는지 우연히 알게 되었다."], ["It was a very ordinary matter and there was nothing exceptional about it.", "그것은 지극히 평범한 일이었고, 별다른 게 없었다."], ["A family who had come to the town and been reduced to poverty were selling their household goods and clothes, all women’s things.", "이 도시로 와서 가난에 쪼들리게 된 어느 가족이 살림살이며 옷가지를 팔고 있었는데, 죄다 여자들 물건이었다."], ["As the things would have fetched little in the market, they were looking for a dealer.", "그 물건들은 시장에 내다 팔아 봤자 얼마 받지 못할 터라, 그들은 중개상을 찾고 있었다."], ["This was Lizaveta’s business.", "그게 바로 리자베타가 하는 일이었다."], ["She undertook such jobs and was frequently employed, as she was very honest and always fixed a fair price and stuck to it.", "그녀는 그런 일을 맡아 했고, 자주 불려 다녔는데, 워낙 정직하고 늘 공정한 값을 정해 그대로 지켰기 때문이다."], ["She spoke as a rule little and, as we have said already, she was very submissive and timid.", "그녀는 대개 말수가 적었고, 앞서 말했듯이 무척 순종적이고 소심했다."]], [["But Raskolnikov had become superstitious of late.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 요즘 들어 미신에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["The traces of superstition remained in him long after, and were almost ineradicable.", "그 미신의 흔적은 그 후로도 오래도록 그에게 남아, 거의 지워지지 않을 정도였다."], ["And in all this he was always afterwards disposed to see something strange and mysterious, as it were, the presence of some peculiar influences and coincidences.", "그리고 이 모든 일에서 그는 훗날 늘 무언가 기이하고 신비로운 것, 말하자면 어떤 독특한 영향력과 우연의 일치 같은 것을 보려는 경향이 있었다."], ["In the previous winter a student he knew called Pokorev, who had left for Harkov, had chanced in conversation to give him the address of Alyona Ivanovna, the old pawnbroker, in case he might want to pawn anything.", "지난겨울, 그가 아는 포코레프라는 대학생이 — 하리코프로 떠난 — 대화 중에 우연히, 혹시 무언가를 저당 잡힐 일이 생길 경우를 대비해 늙은 전당포 노파 알료나 이바노브나의 주소를 그에게 알려 주었다."], ["For a long while he did not go to her, for he had lessons and managed to get along somehow.", "그는 오랫동안 그녀를 찾아가지 않았으니, 과외 수업이 있어 그럭저럭 살아갈 수 있었기 때문이다. 여섯 주 전에 그는 그 주소를 떠올렸다."], ["Six weeks ago he had remembered the address; he had two articles that could be pawned: his father’s old silver watch and a little gold ring with three red stones, a present from his sister at parting.", "그에게는 저당 잡힐 만한 물건이 두 개 있었다. 아버지의 낡은 은시계와, 빨간 보석 세 개가 박힌 작은 금반지였는데, 후자는 헤어질 때 여동생이 준 선물이었다."], ["He decided to take the ring.", "그는 반지를 가져가기로 했다."], ["When he found the old woman he had felt an insurmountable repulsion for her at the first glance, though he knew nothing special about her.", "그 노파를 찾아갔을 때, 그는 그녀에 대해 특별히 아는 것이 없었음에도 첫눈에 도저히 억누를 수 없는 혐오감을 느꼈다."], ["He got two roubles from her and went into a miserable little tavern on his way home.", "그는 그녀에게서 이 루블을 받아 들고, 집으로 가는 길에 어느 초라하고 작은 선술집에 들어갔다."], ["He asked for tea, sat down and sank into deep thought.", "그는 차를 주문하고 자리에 앉아 깊은 생각에 잠겼다."], ["A strange idea was pecking at his brain like a chicken in the egg, and very, very much absorbed him.", "어떤 기이한 생각이 마치 알 속의 병아리처럼 그의 뇌리를 톡톡 쪼아 대며, 그를 너무도, 너무도 깊이 사로잡았다."]], [["Almost beside him at the next table there was sitting a student, whom he did not know and had never seen, and with him a young officer.", "거의 그의 곁, 옆 탁자에는 그가 알지도 못하고 한 번도 본 적 없는 어느 대학생이 앉아 있었고, 그와 함께 젊은 장교 하나가 있었다."], ["They had played a game of billiards and began drinking tea.", "그들은 당구를 한 판 친 뒤 차를 마시기 시작했다."], ["All at once he heard the student mention to the officer the pawnbroker Alyona Ivanovna and give him her address.", "그때 갑자기 그는 그 대학생이 장교에게 전당포 노파 알료나 이바노브나의 얘기를 하며 그녀의 주소를 일러 주는 것을 들었다."], ["This of itself seemed strange to Raskolnikov; he had just come from her and here at once he heard her name.", "이것만으로도 라스콜니코프에게는 기이하게 느껴졌다. 그는 방금 그녀에게서 왔는데, 여기서 곧장 그녀의 이름을 듣게 된 것이다."], ["Of course it was a chance, but he could not shake off a very extraordinary impression, and here someone seemed to be speaking expressly for him; the student began telling his friend various details about Alyona Ivanovna.", "물론 우연이었지만, 그는 어떤 너무도 별난 인상을 떨쳐 버릴 수가 없었으니, 마치 누군가가 일부러 그를 위해 말해 주는 것 같았다. 대학생은 친구에게 알료나 이바노브나에 관한 갖가지 세세한 얘기를 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“She is first-rate,”", "\"그 여자는 일류야.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You can always get money from her.", "\"언제든 거기서 돈을 빌릴 수 있지."], ["She is as rich as a Jew, she can give you five thousand roubles at a time and she is not above taking a pledge for a rouble.", "유대인만큼이나 부자라서, 한 번에 오천 루블도 내줄 수 있는데, 그러면서도 일 루블짜리 저당물을 받는 것도 마다하지 않는다고."], ["Lots of our fellows have had dealings with her.", "우리 패거리 중에도 그 여자랑 거래한 놈이 수두룩해."], ["But she is an awful old harpy....”", "하지만 끔찍한 늙은 흡혈귀야…….\""]], [["And he began describing how spiteful and uncertain she was, how if you were only a day late with your interest the pledge was lost; how she gave a quarter of the value of an article and took five and even seven percent a month on it and so on.", "그러고는 그녀가 얼마나 심술궂고 변덕스러운지, 이자를 하루만 늦게 내도 저당물이 날아가 버린다는 것, 물건 값의 사분의 일밖에 쳐주지 않으면서 한 달에 오 퍼센트, 심지어 칠 퍼센트나 이자를 받는다는 것 따위를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["The student chattered on, saying that she had a sister Lizaveta, whom the wretched little creature was continually beating, and kept in complete bondage like a small child, though Lizaveta was at least six feet high.", "대학생은 계속 떠들어 댔는데, 그녀에게 리자베타라는 여동생이 있다는 것, 그 비참하고 자그마한 노파가 끊임없이 그녀를 두들겨 패며, 리자베타가 적어도 육 피트는 되는데도 어린애처럼 완전히 종처럼 부려 먹는다는 얘기였다."]], [["“There’s a phenomenon for you,”", "\"그런 별난 존재가 또 어디 있겠어.\""], ["cried the student and he laughed.", "대학생은 이렇게 외치고는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["They began talking about Lizaveta.", "그들은 리자베타에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["The student spoke about her with a peculiar relish and was continually laughing and the officer listened with great interest and asked him to send Lizaveta to do some mending for him.", "대학생은 유독 즐거운 듯 그녀 얘기를 하며 연신 웃어 댔고, 장교는 대단한 흥미를 가지고 듣더니 자기 옷을 수선하게 리자베타를 보내 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Raskolnikov did not miss a word and learned everything about her.", "라스콜니코프는 한 마디도 놓치지 않았고, 그녀에 관한 모든 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Lizaveta was younger than the old woman and was her half-sister, being the child of a different mother.", "리자베타는 노파보다 어렸고, 어머니가 다른 이복 여동생이었다."], ["She was thirty-five.", "그녀는 서른다섯이었다."], ["She worked day and night for her sister, and besides doing the cooking and the washing, she did sewing and worked as a charwoman and gave her sister all she earned.", "그녀는 언니를 위해 밤낮없이 일했고, 요리와 빨래를 하는 것 말고도 바느질을 하고 허드렛일까지 하면서 번 돈을 죄다 언니에게 갖다 바쳤다."], ["She did not dare to accept an order or job of any kind without her sister’s permission.", "그녀는 언니의 허락 없이는 그 어떤 주문이나 일거리도 감히 받지 못했다."], ["The old woman had already made her will, and Lizaveta knew of it, and by this will she would not get a farthing; nothing but the movables, chairs and so on; all the money was left to a monastery in the province of N----, that prayers might be said for her in perpetuity.", "노파는 이미 유언장을 써 두었는데, 리자베타도 그것을 알고 있었으니, 그 유언에 따르면 그녀는 한 푼도 받지 못하고, 오직 동산(動産)인 의자 따위만 받게 될 뿐이었다. 모든 돈은 N현(縣)의 어느 수도원에 남겨져, 자기 영혼을 위한 기도가 영원히 올려지도록 했다."], ["Lizaveta was of lower rank than her sister, unmarried and awfully uncouth in appearance, remarkably tall with long feet that looked as if they were bent outwards.", "리자베타는 언니보다 신분이 낮았고, 미혼인 데다 외모는 끔찍하게 우락부락했으며, 유난히 키가 크고 긴 발은 마치 바깥쪽으로 굽은 것처럼 보였다."], ["She always wore battered goatskin shoes, and was clean in her person.", "그녀는 늘 닳아 빠진 염소 가죽 신발을 신고 다녔으며, 몸가짐은 깔끔했다."], ["What the student expressed most surprise and amusement about was the fact that Lizaveta was continually with child.", "대학생이 가장 놀라워하고 재미있어한 것은, 리자베타가 끊임없이 임신을 한다는 사실이었다."]], [["“But you say she is hideous?”", "\"그런데 그 여자가 흉측하다며?\""], ["observed the officer.", "장교가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, she is so dark-skinned and looks like a soldier dressed up, but you know she is not at all hideous.", "\"그래, 살갗이 어찌나 거무튀튀한지 변장한 병사처럼 보이지만, 사실 전혀 흉측하지 않아."], ["She has such a good-natured face and eyes.", "얼굴하고 눈이 어찌나 선량한지."], ["Strikingly so.", "놀랄 만큼 그렇다고."], ["And the proof of it is that lots of people are attracted by her.", "그 증거로, 그 여자한테 끌리는 사람이 한둘이 아냐."], ["She is such a soft, gentle creature, ready to put up with anything, always willing, willing to do anything.", "어찌나 부드럽고 순한 존재인지, 뭐든 다 참아 내고, 늘 기꺼이, 뭐든 다 하려 들지."], ["And her smile is really very sweet.”", "게다가 그 미소는 정말이지 무척 사랑스러워.\""]], [["“You seem to find her attractive yourself,”", "\"자네 자신이 그 여자한테 끌린 모양이군.\""], ["laughed the officer.", "장교가 웃었다."]], [["“From her queerness.", "\"별나서 그렇지."], ["No, I’ll tell you what.", "아니, 자네한테 이런 얘기를 해 주겠어."], ["I could kill that damned old woman and make off with her money, I assure you, without the faintest conscience-prick,”", "나라면 그 빌어먹을 늙은이를 죽이고 돈을 털어 달아날 수 있어, 장담하는데, 양심의 가책 따위는 눈곱만큼도 없이 말이야.\""], ["the student added with warmth.", "대학생이 열을 올리며 덧붙였다."], ["The officer laughed again while Raskolnikov shuddered.", "장교가 다시 웃었고, 그때 라스콜니코프는 몸서리를 쳤다."], ["How strange it was!", "이 얼마나 기이한 일인가!"]], [["“Listen, I want to ask you a serious question,”", "\"들어 봐, 진지한 질문을 하나 하고 싶은데.\""], ["the student said hotly.", "대학생이 열띤 어조로 말했다."], ["“I was joking of course, but look here; on one side we have a stupid, senseless, worthless, spiteful, ailing, horrid old woman, not simply useless but doing actual mischief, who has not an idea what she is living for herself, and who will die in a day or two in any case.", "\"물론 농담이긴 했지만, 봐, 한편에는 멍청하고, 무의미하고, 무가치하고, 심술궂고, 병들고, 끔찍한 늙은이가 있어, 단지 쓸모없을 뿐 아니라 실제로 해악을 끼치는, 자기가 무엇 때문에 사는지조차 모르는, 어차피 하루 이틀이면 죽을 늙은이가 말이야."], ["You understand?", "무슨 말인지 알겠어?"], ["You understand?”", "알겠냐고?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I understand,”", "\"그래, 그래, 알겠어.\""], ["answered the officer, watching his excited companion attentively.", "장교는 흥분한 동료를 유심히 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["“Well, listen then.", "\"그럼 들어 봐."], ["On the other side, fresh young lives thrown away for want of help and by thousands, on every side!", "다른 한편에는, 도움이 없어 헛되이 버려지는 싱싱한 젊은 목숨들이, 그것도 수천씩, 사방에 널려 있다고!"], ["A hundred thousand good deeds could be done and helped, on that old woman’s money which will be buried in a monastery!", "수도원에 묻혀 버릴 그 늙은이의 돈으로 십만 가지 선행을 베풀고 도울 수 있어!"], ["Hundreds, thousands perhaps, might be set on the right path; dozens of families saved from destitution, from ruin, from vice, from the Lock hospitals--and all with her money.", "수백, 어쩌면 수천의 사람들을 올바른 길로 이끌 수 있고, 수십 가구를 궁핍에서, 파멸에서, 타락에서, 성병 병원에서 구해 낼 수 있단 말이야 — 그 모든 게 그 여자 돈으로 말이지."], ["Kill her, take her money and with the help of it devote oneself to the service of humanity and the good of all.", "그 여자를 죽이고, 돈을 빼앗아, 그 돈으로 인류를 위해, 만인의 행복을 위해 헌신하는 거야."], ["What do you think, would not one tiny crime be wiped out by thousands of good deeds?", "어떻게 생각해, 단 하나의 자그마한 범죄가 수천의 선행으로 씻기지 않겠어?"], ["For one life thousands would be saved from corruption and decay.", "한 목숨 대신 수천이 타락과 부패에서 구원받는 거야."], ["One death, and a hundred lives in exchange--it’s simple arithmetic!", "죽음 하나에, 그 대가로 백 개의 목숨이라니 — 이건 단순한 산수라고!"], ["Besides, what value has the life of that sickly, stupid, ill-natured old woman in the balance of existence!", "게다가 그 병들고, 멍청하고, 심술궂은 늙은이의 목숨이 존재의 저울 위에서 무슨 가치가 있겠어!"], ["No more than the life of a louse, of a black-beetle, less in fact because the old woman is doing harm.", "이[蝨]나 검정 풍뎅이의 목숨만도 못해, 사실은 그보다 못하지, 그 늙은이는 해악을 끼치니까."], ["She is wearing out the lives of others; the other day she bit Lizaveta’s finger out of spite; it almost had to be amputated.”", "그 여자는 남들의 삶을 갉아먹고 있어. 며칠 전에는 심술이 나서 리자베타의 손가락을 깨물었는데, 하마터면 잘라 내야 할 뻔했다고.\""]], [["“Of course she does not deserve to live,”", "\"물론 그 여자는 살 자격이 없지.\""], ["remarked the officer, “but there it is, it’s nature.”", "장교가 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌겠어, 그게 자연인걸.\""]], [["“Oh, well, brother, but we have to correct and direct nature, and, but for that, we should drown in an ocean of prejudice.", "\"아, 그래, 형제, 하지만 우리는 자연을 바로잡고 이끌어야 하고, 그러지 않으면 편견의 바다에 빠져 죽고 말 거야."], ["But for that, there would never have been a single great man.", "그러지 않았다면 단 한 명의 위대한 인물도 없었을 테고."], ["They talk of duty, conscience--I don’t want to say anything against duty and conscience;--but the point is, what do we mean by them?", "사람들은 의무니, 양심이니 떠들어 대지 — 나는 의무와 양심에 반대하려는 게 아니야 — 하지만 문제는, 우리가 그것들을 무슨 뜻으로 쓰느냐는 거지."], ["Stay, I have another question to ask you.", "잠깐, 자네한테 또 하나 물어볼 게 있어."], ["Listen!”", "들어 봐!\""]], [["“No, you stay, I’ll ask you a question.", "\"아니, 자네가 잠깐 기다려, 내가 자네한테 질문을 하나 하지."], ["Listen!”", "들어 봐!\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"뭔데?\""]], [["“You are talking and speechifying away, but tell me, would you kill the old woman _yourself_?”", "\"자네는 입을 놀리며 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 있지만, 말해 봐, 자네라면 그 늙은이를 자네 손으로 직접 죽일 수 있겠어?\""]], [["“Of course not!", "\"물론 못하지!"], ["I was only arguing the justice of it....", "난 그저 그 일의 정당함을 따져 본 것뿐이야……."], ["It’s nothing to do with me....”", "나하고는 아무 상관 없는 일이고…….\""]], [["“But I think, if you would not do it yourself, there’s no justice about it....", "\"하지만 내 생각엔, 자네가 직접 못한다면, 거기엔 아무런 정당함도 없는 거야……."], ["Let us have another game.”", "한 판 더 치자고.\""]], [["Raskolnikov was violently agitated.", "라스콜니코프는 격렬하게 동요했다."], ["Of course, it was all ordinary youthful talk and thought, such as he had often heard before in different forms and on different themes.", "물론 그것은 죄다 평범한 젊은이들의 얘기와 사고였고, 그가 전에도 갖가지 형태와 갖가지 주제로 자주 들어 온 것이었다."], ["But why had he happened to hear such a discussion and such ideas at the very moment when his own brain was just conceiving...", "하지만 어째서 그는 하필 자기 자신의 머릿속이 바로 그…… 똑같은 생각을 막 잉태하던 바로 그 순간에 그런 토론과 그런 사상을 듣게 되었을까?"], ["_the very same ideas_? And why, just at the moment when he had brought away the embryo of his idea from the old woman had he dropped at once upon a conversation about her?", "그리고 어째서, 노파에게서 자기 생각의 싹을 막 가져 나온 바로 그 순간에, 그녀에 관한 대화에 곧장 맞닥뜨린 것일까?"], ["This coincidence always seemed strange to him.", "이 우연의 일치는 늘 그에게 기이하게 느껴졌다."], ["This trivial talk in a tavern had an immense influence on him in his later action; as though there had really been in it something preordained, some guiding hint.... *****", "선술집에서 오간 이 사소한 대화는 훗날 그의 행동에 엄청난 영향을 끼쳤으니, 마치 그 안에 정말로 무언가 예정된 것, 어떤 인도하는 암시 같은 것이 들어 있기라도 했던 것처럼……."]], [["On returning from the Hay Market he flung himself on the sofa and sat for a whole hour without stirring.", "건초 시장에서 돌아온 그는 소파에 몸을 내던지고, 꼬박 한 시간을 꼼짝도 하지 않은 채 앉아 있었다."], ["Meanwhile it got dark; he had no candle and, indeed, it did not occur to him to light up.", "그러는 사이 날이 어두워졌다. 그에게는 양초가 없었고, 사실 불을 켤 생각조차 들지 않았다."], ["He could never recollect whether he had been thinking about anything at that time.", "그때 자기가 무언가를 생각하고 있었는지조차 그는 끝내 기억해 낼 수 없었다."], ["At last he was conscious of his former fever and shivering, and he realised with relief that he could lie down on the sofa.", "마침내 그는 예의 그 열과 오한을 의식했고, 소파에 누울 수 있다는 사실에 안도감을 느꼈다."], ["Soon heavy, leaden sleep came over him, as it were crushing him.", "곧 무겁고 납덩이 같은 잠이, 마치 그를 짓누르듯이 덮쳐 왔다."]], [["He slept an extraordinarily long time and without dreaming.", "그는 유난히 오랫동안, 꿈도 꾸지 않고 잤다."], ["Nastasya, coming into his room at ten o’clock the next morning, had difficulty in rousing him.", "이튿날 아침 열 시에 그의 방에 들어온 나스타시야는 그를 깨우느라 애를 먹었다."], ["She brought him in tea and bread.", "그녀는 그에게 차와 빵을 가져왔다."], ["The tea was again the second brew and again in her own tea-pot.", "차는 이번에도 두 번 우려낸 것이었고, 이번에도 그녀 자신의 찻주전자에 담겨 있었다."]], [["“My goodness, how he sleeps!”", "\"세상에, 어쩜 이렇게 잠을 자담!\""], ["she cried indignantly.", "그녀가 분개해 소리쳤다."], ["“And he is always asleep.”", "\"노상 잠만 잔다니까.\""]], [["He got up with an effort.", "그는 힘겹게 몸을 일으켰다."], ["His head ached, he stood up, took a turn in his garret and sank back on the sofa again.", "머리가 아팠다. 그는 일어서서 다락방을 한 바퀴 돌더니 다시 소파에 풀썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“Going to sleep again,”", "\"또 자려고 그러네.\""], ["cried Nastasya.", "나스타시야가 외쳤다."], ["“Are you ill, eh?” He made no reply.", "\"어디 아픈 거예요, 응?\""]], [["“Do you want some tea?”", "그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Afterwards,”", "\"차 좀 마실래요?\""], ["he said with an effort, closing his eyes again and turning to the wall.", "\"이따가.\" 그는 다시 눈을 감고 벽 쪽으로 돌아누우며 힘겹게 말했다."]], [["Nastasya stood over him.", "나스타시야는 그를 내려다보며 서 있었다."]], [["“Perhaps he really is ill,”", "\"정말로 아픈 모양이네.\""], ["she said, turned and went out.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 돌아서서 나갔다."], ["She came in again at two o’clock with soup.", "그녀는 두 시에 수프를 들고 다시 들어왔다."], ["He was lying as before.", "그는 여전히 아까처럼 누워 있었다."], ["The tea stood untouched.", "차는 손도 대지 않은 채 그대로였다."], ["Nastasya felt positively offended and began wrathfully rousing him.", "나스타시야는 노골적으로 기분이 상해 화를 내며 그를 흔들어 깨우기 시작했다."]], [["“Why are you lying like a log?”", "\"어쩜 이렇게 통나무처럼 누워만 있어요?\""], ["she shouted, looking at him with repulsion.", "그녀가 혐오스러운 듯 그를 바라보며 소리쳤다."]], [["He got up, and sat down again, but said nothing and stared at the floor.", "그는 일어났다가 다시 주저앉았으나, 아무 말 없이 바닥만 뚫어지게 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Are you ill or not?”", "\"아픈 거예요, 아닌 거예요?\""], ["asked Nastasya and again received no answer.", "나스타시야가 물었지만, 이번에도 아무 대답을 듣지 못했다."], ["“You’d better go out and get a breath of air,”", "\"차라리 밖에 나가서 바람이라도 좀 쐬는 게 낫겠어요.\""], ["she said after a pause.", "그녀는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 말했다."], ["“Will you eat it or not?”", "\"이거 먹을 거예요, 말 거예요?\""]], [["“Afterwards,”", "\"이따가.\""], ["he said weakly.", "그가 힘없이 말했다."], ["“You can go.”", "\"그만 가 봐.\""]], [["And he motioned her out.", "그러고는 그녀에게 나가라는 손짓을 했다."]], [["She remained a little longer, looked at him with compassion and went out.", "그녀는 잠시 더 머무르며 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보다가 나갔다."]], [["A few minutes afterwards, he raised his eyes and looked for a long while at the tea and the soup.", "몇 분 후, 그는 눈을 들어 차와 수프를 한참 동안 바라보았다."], ["Then he took the bread, took up a spoon and began to eat.", "그러고는 빵을 집고, 숟가락을 들어 먹기 시작했다."]], [["He ate a little, three or four spoonfuls, without appetite, as it were mechanically.", "그는 조금, 서너 숟가락쯤 먹었는데, 식욕도 없이, 마치 기계적으로 먹는 것 같았다."], ["His head ached less.", "머리는 덜 아팠다."], ["After his meal he stretched himself on the sofa again, but now he could not sleep; he lay without stirring, with his face in the pillow.", "식사를 마친 그는 다시 소파에 몸을 뻗었으나, 이번에는 잠이 오지 않았다. 그는 베개에 얼굴을 묻은 채 꼼짝도 않고 누워 있었다."], ["He was haunted by day-dreams and such strange day-dreams; in one, that kept recurring, he fancied that he was in Africa, in Egypt, in some sort of oasis.", "그는 백일몽에, 그것도 너무도 기이한 백일몽에 시달렸다. 그중 자꾸만 되풀이되는 하나에서, 그는 자기가 아프리카에, 이집트에, 어떤 오아시스에 있는 것 같았다."], ["The caravan was resting, the camels were peacefully lying down; the palms stood all around in a complete circle; all the party were at dinner.", "대상(隊商)은 쉬고 있었고, 낙타들은 평온히 누워 있었다. 야자수들이 사방에 빙 둘러 완전한 원을 이루고 서 있었다."], ["But he was drinking water from a spring which flowed gurgling close by.", "일행은 모두 식사 중이었다. 그러나 그는 가까이에서 콸콸 흐르는 샘물을 마시고 있었다."], ["And it was so cool, it was wonderful, wonderful, blue, cold water running among the parti-coloured stones and over the clean sand which glistened here and there like gold....", "그것은 어찌나 시원한지, 너무도, 너무도 경이로운, 푸르고 차가운 물이 알록달록한 돌들 사이로, 군데군데 황금처럼 반짝이는 깨끗한 모래 위로 흘러갔다……."], ["Suddenly he heard a clock strike.", "갑자기 그는 시계 치는 소리를 들었다."], ["He started, roused himself, raised his head, looked out of the window, and seeing how late it was, suddenly jumped up wide awake as though someone had pulled him off the sofa.", "그는 흠칫 놀라 정신을 차렸고, 고개를 들어 창밖을 내다보더니, 시간이 얼마나 늦었는지 깨닫고는, 갑자기 누군가가 그를 소파에서 끌어내리기라도 한 것처럼 완전히 잠이 깨어 벌떡 일어났다."], ["He crept on tiptoe to the door, stealthily opened it and began listening on the staircase.", "그는 발끝으로 살금살금 문가로 다가가, 살며시 문을 열고 계단의 동정에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."], ["His heart beat terribly.", "심장이 끔찍하게 두근거렸다."], ["But all was quiet on the stairs as if everyone was asleep....", "그러나 계단은 마치 모두가 잠든 듯 온통 고요했다……."], ["It seemed to him strange and monstrous that he could have slept in such forgetfulness from the previous day and had done nothing, had prepared nothing yet....", "전날부터 그렇게 정신없이 잠들어, 아무것도 하지 않고, 아직 아무것도 준비해 두지 않았다는 것이 그에게는 기이하고 끔찍하게 느껴졌다……."], ["And meanwhile perhaps it had struck six.", "그러는 사이 어쩌면 여섯 시를 쳤는지도 몰랐다."], ["And his drowsiness and stupefaction were followed by an extraordinary, feverish, as it were distracted haste.", "그리고 졸음과 멍한 상태에 뒤이어, 유난히 열에 들뜬, 말하자면 정신 나간 듯한 다급함이 찾아왔다."], ["But the preparations to be made were few.", "그러나 해 둘 준비라야 별로 없었다."], ["He concentrated all his energies on thinking of everything and forgetting nothing; and his heart kept beating and thumping so that he could hardly breathe.", "그는 모든 것을 빠짐없이 생각하고 아무것도 잊지 않는 데 온 정신을 집중했다. 그러는 내내 심장이 어찌나 두근거리고 쿵쿵 뛰는지 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없었다."], ["First he had to make a noose and sew it into his overcoat--a work of a moment.", "우선 그는 올가미를 만들어 외투에 꿰매 넣어야 했는데 — 이건 순식간에 끝낼 일이었다."], ["He rummaged under his pillow and picked out amongst the linen stuffed away under it, a worn out, old unwashed shirt.", "그는 베개 밑을 뒤져, 그 아래 쑤셔 넣어 둔 속옷가지 사이에서 닳아 빠진, 빨지 않은 낡은 셔츠를 골라냈다."], ["From its rags he tore a long strip, a couple of inches wide and about sixteen inches long.", "그 누더기에서 그는 폭이 두 인치쯤, 길이가 열여섯 인치쯤 되는 기다란 천 조각을 찢어 냈다."], ["He folded this strip in two, took off his wide, strong summer overcoat of some stout cotton material (his only outer garment) and began sewing the two ends of the rag on the inside, under the left armhole.", "그는 이 천 조각을 둘로 접고, 두툼한 무명천으로 된 헐렁하고 튼튼한 여름 외투를 (그의 유일한 겉옷이었다) 벗어, 안쪽 왼쪽 겨드랑이 밑에 그 천의 양 끝을 꿰매기 시작했다."], ["His hands shook as he sewed, but he did it successfully so that nothing showed outside when he put the coat on again.", "바느질을 하는 동안 손이 떨렸지만, 그는 그것을 무사히 해냈고, 다시 외투를 입었을 때 겉으로는 아무것도 드러나지 않았다."], ["The needle and thread he had got ready long before and they lay on his table in a piece of paper.", "바늘과 실은 오래전에 준비해 두어 종이에 싸인 채 그의 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["As for the noose, it was a very ingenious device of his own; the noose was intended for the axe.", "올가미로 말하자면, 그것은 그 자신이 고안한 무척 기발한 장치였다. 그 올가미는 도끼를 매달기 위한 것이었다."], ["It was impossible for him to carry the axe through the street in his hands.", "도끼를 손에 들고 거리를 지나갈 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["And if hidden under his coat he would still have had to support it with his hand, which would have been noticeable.", "외투 안에 숨긴다 해도 손으로 받쳐 들어야 할 텐데, 그러면 눈에 띄고 말 것이었다."], ["Now he had only to put the head of the axe in the noose, and it would hang quietly under his arm on the inside.", "그러나 이제 그는 도끼의 날 부분을 올가미에 끼우기만 하면 되었고, 그러면 도끼가 안쪽 겨드랑이 밑에 얌전히 매달릴 터였다."], ["Putting his hand in his coat pocket, he could hold the end of the handle all the way, so that it did not swing; and as the coat was very full, a regular sack in fact, it could not be seen from outside that he was holding something with the hand that was in the pocket.", "외투 주머니에 손을 넣어 자루 끝을 줄곧 붙잡고 있으면 도끼가 흔들리지 않을 것이고, 게다가 외투가 워낙 헐렁해서 사실상 자루나 다름없었으므로, 주머니에 든 손으로 무언가를 붙잡고 있다는 것이 겉으로는 보이지 않을 터였다."], ["This noose, too, he had designed a fortnight before.", "이 올가미 역시 그가 두 주 전에 고안해 둔 것이었다."]], [["When he had finished with this, he thrust his hand into a little opening between his sofa and the floor, fumbled in the left corner and drew out the _pledge_, which he had got ready long before and hidden there.", "이 일을 끝내자, 그는 소파와 바닥 사이의 작은 틈으로 손을 밀어 넣어 왼쪽 구석을 더듬었고, 오래전에 준비해 거기 숨겨 둔 저당물을 끄집어냈다."], ["This pledge was, however, only a smoothly planed piece of wood the size and thickness of a silver cigarette case.", "그러나 이 저당물이라는 것은 사실 은제 담뱃갑 크기에 그만한 두께로 매끈하게 대패질한 나뭇조각에 불과했다."], ["He picked up this piece of wood in one of his wanderings in a courtyard where there was some sort of a workshop.", "그는 어느 작업장 비슷한 것이 있던 안뜰을 헤매던 중에 이 나뭇조각을 주웠다."], ["Afterwards he had added to the wood a thin smooth piece of iron, which he had also picked up at the same time in the street.", "나중에 그는 그 나뭇조각에 얇고 매끈한 쇳조각을 덧붙였는데, 그것 역시 같은 때에 거리에서 주운 것이었다."], ["Putting the iron which was a little the smaller on the piece of wood, he fastened them very firmly, crossing and re-crossing the thread round them; then wrapped them carefully and daintily in clean white paper and tied up the parcel so that it would be very difficult to untie it.", "나뭇조각보다 조금 작은 그 쇳조각을 나뭇조각 위에 얹고, 실을 그 둘레로 가로세로 교차시켜 가며 단단히 묶었다. 그러고는 그것을 깨끗한 흰 종이로 조심스럽고 정갈하게 싸서, 풀기가 무척 어렵도록 꾸러미를 묶었다."], ["This was in order to divert the attention of the old woman for a time, while she was trying to undo the knot, and so to gain a moment.", "이것은 노파가 매듭을 풀려고 애쓰는 동안 잠시 그녀의 주의를 딴 데로 돌려, 그렇게 한순간을 벌기 위한 것이었다."], ["The iron strip was added to give weight, so that the woman might not guess the first minute that the “thing” was made of wood.", "쇳조각을 덧붙인 것은 무게를 주어, 노파가 처음 한순간에 그 '물건'이 나무로 되어 있다는 것을 눈치채지 못하도록 하기 위해서였다."], ["All this had been stored by him beforehand under the sofa.", "이 모든 것을 그는 미리 소파 밑에 간직해 두었다."], ["He had only just got the pledge out when he heard someone suddenly about in the yard.", "저당물을 막 끄집어냈을 때, 그는 안뜰에서 갑자기 누군가가 움직이는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“It struck six long ago.”", "\"여섯 시는 친 지 한참 됐어.\""]], [["“Long ago!", "\"한참 됐다고!"], ["My God!”", "맙소사!\""]], [["He rushed to the door, listened, caught up his hat and began to descend his thirteen steps cautiously, noiselessly, like a cat.", "그는 문가로 달려가 귀를 기울였고, 모자를 집어 들고는 열세 개의 계단을 조심스럽게, 소리 없이, 고양이처럼 내려가기 시작했다."], ["He had still the most important thing to do--to steal the axe from the kitchen.", "그에게는 아직 가장 중요한 일이 남아 있었으니 — 부엌에서 도끼를 훔쳐 내는 일이었다."], ["That the deed must be done with an axe he had decided long ago.", "도끼로 그 일을 해야 한다는 것은 오래전에 결정해 둔 바였다."], ["He had also a pocket pruning-knife, but he could not rely on the knife and still less on his own strength, and so resolved finally on the axe.", "그에게는 접이식 전지용 칼도 있었지만, 칼을 믿을 수 없었고 자기 힘은 더더욱 믿을 수 없었기에, 결국 도끼로 마음을 굳힌 것이었다."], ["We may note in passing, one peculiarity in regard to all the final resolutions taken by him in the matter; they had one strange characteristic: the more final they were, the more hideous and the more absurd they at once became in his eyes.", "한 가지 짚고 넘어가자면, 이 일과 관련해 그가 내린 모든 최종 결심에는 한 가지 기이한 특징이 있었다. 그것들이 더 최종적인 것이 될수록, 그 즉시 그의 눈에는 더 끔찍하고 더 터무니없는 것으로 비쳤다는 점이다."], ["In spite of all his agonising inward struggle, he never for a single instant all that time could believe in the carrying out of his plans.", "그 모든 괴로운 내적 투쟁에도 불구하고, 그는 그동안 단 한순간도 자기 계획이 실행되리라고는 믿을 수 없었다."]], [["And, indeed, if it had ever happened that everything to the least point could have been considered and finally settled, and no uncertainty of any kind had remained, he would, it seems, have renounced it all as something absurd, monstrous and impossible.", "그리고 정말이지, 만일 모든 것이 사소한 점 하나까지 다 검토되어 최종적으로 매듭지어지고, 어떤 종류의 불확실함도 남지 않게 되는 일이 일어났더라면, 그는 아마도 그 모든 것을 터무니없고, 끔찍하며, 불가능한 일로 여겨 포기해 버렸을 것이다."], ["But a whole mass of unsettled points and uncertainties remained.", "그러나 정리되지 않은 점들과 불확실함이 산더미처럼 남아 있었다."], ["As for getting the axe, that trifling business cost him no anxiety, for nothing could be easier.", "도끼를 구하는 일로 말하자면, 그 하찮은 일은 그에게 아무런 근심도 끼치지 않았으니, 그보다 더 쉬운 일도 없었기 때문이다."], ["Nastasya was continually out of the house, especially in the evenings; she would run in to the neighbours or to a shop, and always left the door ajar.", "나스타시야는 늘 집을 비웠는데, 특히 저녁이면 그랬다. 그녀는 이웃집이나 가게에 들르려고 뛰쳐나가곤 했고, 늘 문을 빠끔히 열어 두었다."], ["It was the one thing the landlady was always scolding her about.", "그것은 집주인 여자가 노상 그녀를 나무라는 단 한 가지 일이었다."], ["And so, when the time came, he would only have to go quietly into the kitchen and to take the axe, and an hour later (when everything was over) go in and put it back again.", "그러니 때가 되면 그는 그저 부엌으로 슬며시 들어가 도끼를 집어 들기만 하면 되었고, 한 시간 후에 (모든 일이 끝난 뒤) 들어가 다시 제자리에 갖다 놓기만 하면 되었다."], ["But these were doubtful points.", "그러나 여기엔 미심쩍은 점들이 있었다."], ["Supposing he returned an hour later to put it back, and Nastasya had come back and was on the spot.", "한 시간 후에 그것을 도로 갖다 놓으러 돌아왔는데, 나스타시야가 돌아와 그 자리에 있다고 치자."], ["He would of course have to go by and wait till she went out again.", "물론 그는 그냥 지나쳐 그녀가 다시 나갈 때까지 기다려야 할 것이다."], ["But supposing she were in the meantime to miss the axe, look for it, make an outcry--that would mean suspicion or at least grounds for suspicion.", "하지만 그러는 사이 그녀가 도끼가 없어진 것을 알아채고, 그것을 찾으며 소동을 벌인다고 치면 — 그것은 의심을, 적어도 의심할 근거를 의미하게 될 것이었다."]], [["But those were all trifles which he had not even begun to consider, and indeed he had no time.", "그러나 그런 것들은 죄다 사소한 일들이라 그는 아직 고려하기 시작하지도 않았고, 사실 그럴 겨를도 없었다."], ["He was thinking of the chief point, and put off trifling details, until _he could believe in it all_.", "그는 가장 중요한 점을 생각하고 있었고, 사소한 세부 사항들은 이 모든 것을 믿을 수 있게 될 때까지 미뤄 두었다."], ["But that seemed utterly unattainable.", "그러나 그것은 도저히 다다를 수 없는 일처럼 보였다."], ["So it seemed to himself at least.", "적어도 그 자신에게는 그렇게 느껴졌다."], ["He could not imagine, for instance, that he would sometime leave off thinking, get up and simply go there.... Even his late experiment (i.e.", "이를테면 그는 자기가 언젠가 생각을 멈추고, 일어나서, 그저 그곳으로 가게 되리라는 것을 도무지 상상할 수 없었다……."], ["his visit with the object of a final survey of the place) was simply an attempt at an experiment, far from being the real thing, as though one should say “come, let us go and try it--why dream about it!”--and at once he had broken down and had run away cursing, in a frenzy with himself.", "심지어 요전의 시도조차 (즉, 현장을 최종적으로 둘러보려는 목적의 그 방문조차) 그저 시도를 위한 시도였을 뿐, 진짜와는 거리가 멀었으니, 마치 \"자, 가서 한번 해 보자고 — 뭐 하러 공상만 하고 있어!\" 하고 말해 놓고는, 그 즉시 무너져 자기 자신에게 화가 치밀어 욕설을 퍼부으며 달아나 버린 것과 같았다."], ["Meanwhile it would seem, as regards the moral question, that his analysis was complete; his casuistry had become keen as a razor, and he could not find rational objections in himself.", "그러는 한편, 도덕적 문제에 관한 한, 그의 분석은 완결된 듯 보였다. 그의 궤변은 면도날처럼 날카로워졌고, 그는 자기 안에서 이성적인 반론을 찾아낼 수 없었다."], ["But in the last resort he simply ceased to believe in himself, and doggedly, slavishly sought arguments in all directions, fumbling for them, as though someone were forcing and drawing him to it.", "그러나 최후의 순간에 그는 그저 자기 자신을 믿지 못하게 되었고, 마치 누군가가 그를 억지로 떠밀어 그쪽으로 끌고 가기라도 하는 것처럼, 끈질기게, 노예처럼, 사방을 더듬으며 온갖 방향에서 논거를 구했다."]], [["At first--long before indeed--he had been much occupied with one question; why almost all crimes are so badly concealed and so easily detected, and why almost all criminals leave such obvious traces?", "처음에는 — 사실 한참 전부터 — 그는 한 가지 문제에 몰두해 있었다. 어째서 거의 모든 범죄가 그토록 서투르게 은폐되어 그토록 쉽게 발각되는가, 그리고 어째서 거의 모든 범죄자가 그토록 명백한 흔적을 남기는가 하는 것이었다."], ["He had come gradually to many different and curious conclusions, and in his opinion the chief reason lay not so much in the material impossibility of concealing the crime, as in the criminal himself.", "그는 차츰 갖가지 다양하고 기이한 결론에 다다랐는데, 그의 생각으로는 그 주된 원인이 범죄를 은폐하는 일의 물리적 불가능성에 있다기보다는 범죄자 자신에게 있었다."], ["Almost every criminal is subject to a failure of will and reasoning power by a childish and phenomenal heedlessness, at the very instant when prudence and caution are most essential.", "거의 모든 범죄자는 신중함과 조심성이 가장 필수적인 바로 그 순간에, 어린애 같고 경이로울 정도의 부주의로 말미암아 의지력과 판단력의 상실에 빠진다."], ["It was his conviction that this eclipse of reason and failure of will power attacked a man like a disease, developed gradually and reached its highest point just before the perpetration of the crime, continued with equal violence at the moment of the crime and for longer or shorter time after, according to the individual case, and then passed off like any other disease.", "그의 확신에 따르면, 이러한 이성의 마비와 의지력의 상실은 마치 질병처럼 인간을 덮쳐, 차츰 발전하여 범행 직전에 그 정점에 이르고, 범행의 순간에도 똑같은 격렬함으로 지속되며, 그 후로도 개별적인 경우에 따라 더 길거나 짧은 시간 동안 이어지다가, 이윽고 여느 질병처럼 사라진다는 것이었다."], ["The question whether the disease gives rise to the crime, or whether the crime from its own peculiar nature is always accompanied by something of the nature of disease, he did not yet feel able to decide.", "질병이 범죄를 일으키는 것인지, 아니면 범죄가 그 고유한 본성으로 말미암아 늘 질병 비슷한 무언가를 동반하는 것인지, 그 문제에 대해서는 그는 아직 단정할 수 없다고 느꼈다."]], [["When he reached these conclusions, he decided that in his own case there could not be such a morbid reaction, that his reason and will would remain unimpaired at the time of carrying out his design, for the simple reason that his design was “not a crime....”", "이러한 결론에 다다르자, 그는 자기 자신의 경우에는 그런 병적인 반응이 있을 리 없다고, 자기 계획을 실행하는 순간에도 자신의 이성과 의지는 온전히 유지될 것이라고 결론지었는데, 그 단순한 이유는 자기 계획이 \"범죄가 아니기\" 때문이라는 것이었다……."], ["We will omit all the process by means of which he arrived at this last conclusion; we have run too far ahead already....", "그가 이 마지막 결론에 이르게 된 과정은 모두 생략하기로 하자. 우리는 이미 너무 앞서 나가 버렸으니까……."], ["We may add only that the practical, purely material difficulties of the affair occupied a secondary position in his mind.", "다만 이 일의 실제적이고 순전히 물질적인 어려움들이 그의 머릿속에서 부차적인 위치를 차지하고 있었다는 점만 덧붙여 두자."], ["“One has but to keep all one’s will-power and reason to deal with them, and they will all be overcome at the time when once one has familiarised oneself with the minutest details of the business....”", "\"그저 자신의 의지력과 이성을 온전히 지니고 그것들에 대처하기만 하면, 일단 이 일의 가장 사소한 세부 사항까지 익숙해지고 나면, 때가 됐을 때 그것들은 죄다 극복될 거야…….\""], ["But this preparation had never been begun.", "그러나 이런 준비는 결코 시작된 적이 없었다."], ["His final decisions were what he came to trust least, and when the hour struck, it all came to pass quite differently, as it were accidentally and unexpectedly.", "그가 가장 믿지 못하게 된 것은 다름 아닌 자신의 최종 결심들이었고, 막상 시간이 닥쳤을 때, 모든 것은 전혀 다르게, 말하자면 우발적이고 예기치 못하게 벌어졌다."]], [["One trifling circumstance upset his calculations, before he had even left the staircase.", "한 가지 사소한 사정이, 그가 미처 계단을 벗어나기도 전에 그의 계산을 뒤엎어 버렸다."], ["When he reached the landlady’s kitchen, the door of which was open as usual, he glanced cautiously in to see whether, in Nastasya’s absence, the landlady herself was there, or if not, whether the door to her own room was closed, so that she might not peep out when he went in for the axe.", "그가 집주인 여자의 부엌에 다다랐을 때, 그 문은 여느 때처럼 열려 있었는데, 그는 나스타시야가 없는 사이에 혹시 집주인 여자 본인이 거기 있는지, 그렇지 않다면 그가 도끼를 가지러 들어갈 때 그녀가 내다보지 못하도록 그녀 방문이 닫혀 있는지 살펴보려고 조심스레 안을 들여다보았다."], ["But what was his amazement when he suddenly saw that Nastasya was not only at home in the kitchen, but was occupied there, taking linen out of a basket and hanging it on a line.", "그러나 나스타시야가 부엌에 집에 있을 뿐 아니라, 거기서 바구니에서 빨랫감을 꺼내 빨랫줄에 널며 일까지 하고 있는 것을 갑자기 보았을 때 그가 얼마나 경악했던지!"], ["Seeing him, she left off hanging the clothes, turned to him and stared at him all the time he was passing.", "그를 보자 그녀는 빨래 너는 일을 멈추고 그에게 몸을 돌려, 그가 지나가는 내내 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["He turned away his eyes, and walked past as though he noticed nothing.", "그는 눈길을 돌려, 아무것도 알아채지 못한 척하며 지나갔다."], ["But it was the end of everything; he had not the axe!", "그러나 이것으로 모든 게 끝장이었다. 그에게는 도끼가 없었으니!"], ["He was overwhelmed.", "그는 망연자실했다."]], [["“What made me think,”", "'대체 무슨 생각으로,'"], ["he reflected, as he went under the gateway, “what made me think that she would be sure not to be at home at that moment!", "그는 대문 밑을 지나며 곱씹었다. '대체 무슨 생각으로 그 여자가 그 순간에 틀림없이 집에 없을 거라고 여겼던 걸까!"], ["Why, why, why did I assume this so certainly?”", "왜, 왜, 어째서 나는 그걸 그렇게 확신했지?'"]], [["He was crushed and even humiliated.", "그는 짓눌렸고, 심지어 굴욕감마저 느꼈다."], ["He could have laughed at himself in his anger....", "화가 치밀어 자기 자신을 비웃을 지경이었다……."], ["A dull animal rage boiled within him.", "둔중하고 짐승 같은 분노가 그의 안에서 부글부글 끓어올랐다."]], [["He stood hesitating in the gateway.", "그는 대문간에 서서 망설였다."], ["To go into the street, to go a walk for appearance’ sake was revolting; to go back to his room, even more revolting.", "거리로 나가, 남의 눈을 의식해 산책이라도 하는 것은 역겨웠고, 자기 방으로 돌아가는 것은 더더욱 역겨웠다."], ["“And what a chance I have lost for ever!”", "'이렇게 영영 다시없을 기회를 놓쳐 버리다니!'"], ["he muttered, standing aimlessly in the gateway, just opposite the porter’s little dark room, which was also open.", "그는 목적도 없이 대문간에, 마침 열려 있던 문지기의 작고 어두운 방 바로 맞은편에 서서 중얼거렸다."], ["Suddenly he started.", "갑자기 그는 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["From the porter’s room, two paces away from him, something shining under the bench to the right caught his eye....", "그에게서 두 걸음쯤 떨어진 문지기 방 안, 오른쪽 의자 밑에서 무언가 번쩍이는 것이 그의 눈을 사로잡았다……."], ["He looked about him--nobody.", "그는 주위를 둘러보았다 — 아무도 없었다."], ["He approached the room on tiptoe, went down two steps into it and in a faint voice called the porter.", "그는 발끝으로 살금살금 그 방으로 다가가, 두 계단을 내려가 안으로 들어가서는 가냘픈 목소리로 문지기를 불렀다."], ["“Yes, not at home!", "'그래, 집에 없군!"], ["Somewhere near though, in the yard, for the door is wide open.”", "하지만 어딘가 근처에, 안뜰에 있는 거야, 문이 활짝 열려 있으니.'"], ["He dashed to the axe (it was an axe) and pulled it out from under the bench, where it lay between two chunks of wood; at once, before going out, he made it fast in the noose, he thrust both hands into his pockets and went out of the room; no one had noticed him!", "그는 도끼로 (그것은 도끼였다) 달려들어, 장작 두 토막 사이에 놓여 있던 그것을 의자 밑에서 끄집어냈다. 그는 방을 나가기에 앞서 즉시 그것을 올가미에 단단히 끼우고, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 방을 나섰다."], ["“When reason fails, the devil helps!”", "아무도 그를 보지 못했다! '이성이 말을 듣지 않을 땐, 악마가 도와주는 법이지!'"], ["he thought with a strange grin.", "그는 묘한 쓴웃음을 지으며 생각했다."], ["This chance raised his spirits extraordinarily.", "이 우연은 그의 기운을 유난히 북돋워 주었다."]], [["He walked along quietly and sedately, without hurry, to avoid awakening suspicion.", "그는 의심을 사지 않으려고 서두르지 않고, 조용하고 차분하게 걸어갔다."], ["He scarcely looked at the passers-by, tried to escape looking at their faces at all, and to be as little noticeable as possible.", "그는 행인들을 거의 쳐다보지 않았고, 아예 그들의 얼굴을 보지 않으려, 가능한 한 눈에 띄지 않으려 애썼다."], ["Suddenly he thought of his hat.", "갑자기 그는 자기 모자가 떠올랐다."], ["“Good heavens!", "'맙소사!"], ["I had the money the day before yesterday and did not get a cap to wear instead!”", "그저께 돈이 있었는데도 대신 쓸 납작모자 하나를 사 두지 않았다니!'"], ["A curse rose from the bottom of his soul.", "영혼 깊은 곳에서 저주가 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["Glancing out of the corner of his eye into a shop, he saw by a clock on the wall that it was ten minutes past seven.", "곁눈질로 어느 가게 안을 흘끗 들여다본 그는 벽에 걸린 시계로 일곱 시 십 분이라는 것을 알았다."], ["He had to make haste and at the same time to go someway round, so as to approach the house from the other side....", "서둘러야 했고, 동시에 다른 쪽에서 그 집으로 다가가기 위해 빙 돌아가야 했다……."]], [["When he had happened to imagine all this beforehand, he had sometimes thought that he would be very much afraid.", "전에 이 모든 것을 미리 상상했을 때, 그는 이따금 자기가 몹시 무서워하리라고 생각했었다."], ["But he was not very much afraid now, was not afraid at all, indeed.", "그러나 지금 그는 그리 무섭지 않았으니, 사실 전혀 무섭지 않았다."], ["His mind was even occupied by irrelevant matters, but by nothing for long.", "그의 머릿속은 심지어 엉뚱한 일들로 가득 차 있었지만, 어느 것도 오래가지는 않았다."], ["As he passed the Yusupov garden, he was deeply absorbed in considering the building of great fountains, and of their refreshing effect on the atmosphere in all the squares.", "유수포프 정원을 지날 때, 그는 거대한 분수를 세우는 일과, 그것이 모든 광장의 대기에 가져다줄 상쾌한 효과에 대한 생각에 깊이 빠져들었다."], ["By degrees he passed to the conviction that if the summer garden were extended to the field of Mars, and perhaps joined to the garden of the Mihailovsky Palace, it would be a splendid thing and a great benefit to the town.", "차츰 그는, 만일 여름 정원을 마르스 광장까지 넓히고, 어쩌면 미하일롭스키 궁전의 정원과 잇는다면, 그것은 멋진 일이 될 것이며 이 도시에 큰 이로움이 되리라는 확신에 이르렀다."], ["Then he was interested by the question why in all great towns men are not simply driven by necessity, but in some peculiar way inclined to live in those parts of the town where there are no gardens nor fountains; where there is most dirt and smell and all sorts of nastiness.", "그러다가 그는 어째서 모든 대도시에서 사람들이 단지 필요에 떠밀려서가 아니라, 무언가 별난 방식으로, 정원도 분수도 없고, 흙먼지와 악취와 온갖 더러움이 가장 심한 도시의 그런 구역에 살려는 경향을 보이는가 하는 문제에 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["Then his own walks through the Hay Market came back to his mind, and for a moment he waked up to reality.", "그러자 건초 시장을 거닐던 자신의 산책이 떠올랐고, 한순간 그는 현실로 깨어났다. '이게 다 무슨 헛소리야!'"], ["“What nonsense!”", "그는 생각했다."], ["he thought, “better think of nothing at all!”", "'차라리 아무 생각도 하지 않는 게 낫겠어!'"]], [["“So probably men led to execution clutch mentally at every object that meets them on the way,”", "'사형장으로 끌려가는 사람도 아마 가는 길에 마주치는 온갖 것에 정신이 매달리겠지.'"], ["flashed through his mind, but simply flashed, like lightning; he made haste to dismiss this thought....", "이런 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔으나, 그저 번쩍, 번개처럼 스쳤을 뿐이었다. 그는 서둘러 이 생각을 떨쳐 냈다……."], ["And by now he was near; here was the house, here was the gate.", "그리고 이제 그는 가까이 와 있었다. 여기 그 집이, 여기 그 대문이 있었다."], ["Suddenly a clock somewhere struck once. “What!", "갑자기 어디선가 시계가 한 번 울렸다."], ["can it be half-past seven?", "'뭐라고! 일곱 시 반이라고?"], ["Impossible, it must be fast!”", "그럴 리가, 시계가 빠른 게 분명해!'"]], [["Luckily for him, everything went well again at the gates.", "그에게는 다행스럽게도, 대문에서 또다시 모든 일이 잘 풀렸다."], ["At that very moment, as though expressly for his benefit, a huge waggon of hay had just driven in at the gate, completely screening him as he passed under the gateway, and the waggon had scarcely had time to drive through into the yard, before he had slipped in a flash to the right.", "바로 그 순간, 마치 일부러 그를 위해서인 양, 거대한 건초 짐마차 한 대가 막 대문으로 들어와, 그가 대문 밑을 지나는 동안 그를 완전히 가려 주었고, 그 짐마차가 미처 안뜰로 다 들어가기도 전에 그는 번개같이 오른쪽으로 슬쩍 빠져 들어갔다."], ["On the other side of the waggon he could hear shouting and quarrelling; but no one noticed him and no one met him.", "짐마차 반대편에서는 고함 소리와 다투는 소리가 들려왔지만, 아무도 그를 알아채지 못했고 아무도 그와 마주치지 않았다."], ["Many windows looking into that huge quadrangular yard were open at that moment, but he did not raise his head--he had not the strength to.", "그 거대한 사각형 안뜰을 향한 많은 창문이 그 순간 열려 있었지만, 그는 고개를 들지 않았다 — 그럴 힘이 없었다."], ["The staircase leading to the old woman’s room was close by, just on the right of the gateway.", "노파의 방으로 통하는 계단은 바로 가까이, 대문 바로 오른쪽에 있었다."], ["He was already on the stairs....", "그는 어느새 계단을 오르고 있었다……."]], [["Drawing a breath, pressing his hand against his throbbing heart, and once more feeling for the axe and setting it straight, he began softly and cautiously ascending the stairs, listening every minute.", "숨을 들이쉬고, 두근거리는 심장에 손을 갖다 대고, 다시 한번 도끼를 더듬어 바로잡으며, 그는 매 순간 귀를 기울이면서 부드럽게, 조심스럽게 계단을 오르기 시작했다. 그러나 계단 역시 텅 비어 있었다."], ["But the stairs, too, were quite deserted; all the doors were shut; he met no one.", "문들은 죄다 닫혀 있었고, 그는 아무와도 마주치지 않았다."], ["One flat indeed on the first floor was wide open and painters were at work in it, but they did not glance at him.", "다만 이 층의 한 셋집이 활짝 열려 있고 그 안에서 도장공들이 일하고 있었지만, 그들은 그에게 눈길조차 주지 않았다."], ["He stood still, thought a minute and went on.", "그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 잠시 생각하더니 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["“Of course it would be better if they had not been here, but...", "'물론 저들이 여기 없었다면 더 좋았겠지만……"], ["it’s two storeys above them.”", "저긴 그녀 집보다 두 층 아래잖아.'"]], [["And there was the fourth storey, here was the door, here was the flat opposite, the empty one.", "그리고 어느새 사 층이었다. 여기 그 문이 있었고, 여기 맞은편의, 비어 있는 셋집이 있었다."], ["The flat underneath the old woman’s was apparently empty also; the visiting card nailed on the door had been torn off--they had gone away!...", "노파 집 바로 아래층 셋집도 보아하니 비어 있는 모양이었다. 문에 박아 두었던 명함이 떼어져 있었으니 — 그들은 떠나 버린 것이다!……"], ["He was out of breath.", "그는 숨이 가빴다."], ["For one instant the thought floated through his mind, “Shall I go back?”", "한순간 '돌아갈까?' 하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["But he made no answer and began listening at the old woman’s door, a dead silence.", "그러나 그는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 노파의 방문에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 죽음 같은 정적뿐이었다."], ["Then he listened again on the staircase, listened long and intently...", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 계단의 동정에 귀를 기울였으니, 오래도록 골똘히 귀를 기울였다……."], ["then looked about him for the last time, pulled himself together, drew himself up, and once more tried the axe in the noose.", "그런 다음 마지막으로 한 번 주위를 둘러보고, 정신을 가다듬고, 몸을 곧추세우고, 다시 한번 올가미에 든 도끼를 점검해 보았다. '내 얼굴이 너무 창백한가?'"], ["“Am I very pale?”", "그는 마음속으로 물었다."], ["he wondered. “Am I not evidently agitated?", "'내가 동요한 게 빤히 보이는 건 아닐까?"], ["She is mistrustful....", "저 여자는 의심이 많은데……."], ["Had I better wait a little longer...", "차라리 좀 더 기다리는 게 나을까……"], ["till my heart leaves off thumping?”", "심장이 쿵쿵거리는 게 멎을 때까지?'"]], [["But his heart did not leave off.", "그러나 그의 심장은 멎지 않았다."], ["On the contrary, as though to spite him, it throbbed more and more violently.", "오히려, 마치 그를 약 올리기라도 하듯, 점점 더 격렬하게 두근거렸다."], ["He could stand it no longer, he slowly put out his hand to the bell and rang.", "그는 더는 견딜 수 없어, 천천히 손을 뻗어 초인종을 울렸다."], ["Half a minute later he rang again, more loudly.", "삼십 초쯤 후, 그는 다시, 좀 더 크게 울렸다."]], [["No answer.", "대답이 없었다."], ["To go on ringing was useless and out of place.", "계속 초인종을 울려 봐야 소용없는 데다 어울리지도 않는 짓이었다."], ["The old woman was, of course, at home, but she was suspicious and alone.", "노파는 물론 집에 있었지만, 의심이 많은 데다 혼자였다."], ["He had some knowledge of her habits...", "그는 그녀의 습관을 좀 알고 있었다……."], ["and once more he put his ear to the door.", "그래서 그는 다시 한번 문에 귀를 갖다 댔다."], ["Either his senses were peculiarly keen (which it is difficult to suppose), or the sound was really very distinct.", "그의 감각이 유난히 예민했던 것이거나 (이렇게 보기는 어렵지만), 아니면 그 소리가 정말로 또렷했던 것이거나, 어쨌든 그는 갑자기 자물쇠에 조심스레 손을 갖다 대는 것 같은 소리와, 바로 문 앞에서 치맛자락이 스치는 소리를 들었다."], ["Anyway, he suddenly heard something like the cautious touch of a hand on the lock and the rustle of a skirt at the very door.", "누군가가 자물쇠 가까이 살그머니 서서, 그가 바깥에서 하고 있는 것과 똑같이, 안에서 몰래 귀를 기울이며, 문에 귀를 갖다 댄 듯했다……."], ["Someone was standing stealthily close to the lock and just as he was doing on the outside was secretly listening within, and seemed to have her ear to the door....", "그는 숨어 있는 것처럼 보이지 않으려고 일부러 조금 몸을 움직이며 무언가를 소리 내어 중얼거렸고, 그러고는 세 번째로 초인종을 울렸는데, 이번에는 조용히, 차분하게, 조급한 기색 없이 울렸다."], ["He moved a little on purpose and muttered something aloud that he might not have the appearance of hiding, then rang a third time, but quietly, soberly, and without impatience, Recalling it afterwards, that moment stood out in his mind vividly, distinctly, for ever; he could not make out how he had had such cunning, for his mind was as it were clouded at moments and he was almost unconscious of his body....", "훗날 그것을 회상할 때면, 그 순간이 그의 머릿속에 생생하고 또렷하게, 영원히 남았다. 그는 자기에게 어떻게 그런 교활함이 있었는지 도무지 알 수 없었으니, 그 순간 그의 정신은 이를테면 흐릿해져 있었고, 자기 몸뚱이조차 거의 의식하지 못할 지경이었기 때문이다……."], ["An instant later he heard the latch unfastened.", "잠시 후, 그는 빗장이 풀리는 소리를 들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["The door was as before opened a tiny crack, and again two sharp and suspicious eyes stared at him out of the darkness.", "문은 아까처럼 아주 조금 빠끔히 열렸고, 또다시 날카롭고 의심에 찬 두 눈이 어둠 속에서 그를 노려보았다."], ["Then Raskolnikov lost his head and nearly made a great mistake.", "그 순간 라스콜니코프는 정신을 잃고 하마터면 큰 실수를 저지를 뻔했다."]], [["Fearing the old woman would be frightened by their being alone, and not hoping that the sight of him would disarm her suspicions, he took hold of the door and drew it towards him to prevent the old woman from attempting to shut it again.", "단둘이 있다는 사실에 노파가 겁을 먹을까 봐 두렵고, 또 자기 모습을 보더라도 그녀의 의심이 풀리리라는 기대도 들지 않았기에, 그는 문을 붙잡고 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 노파가 다시 문을 닫지 못하게 했다."], ["Seeing this she did not pull the door back, but she did not let go the handle so that he almost dragged her out with it on to the stairs.", "이를 보고 노파는 문을 도로 잡아당기지는 않았으나 손잡이를 놓지도 않았으므로, 그는 그녀를 문째 계단으로 거의 끌어낼 뻔했다."], ["Seeing that she was standing in the doorway not allowing him to pass, he advanced straight upon her.", "노파가 문간에 버티고 서서 자신을 들여보내지 않으려 하는 것을 보고, 그는 곧장 그녀를 향해 다가섰다."], ["She stepped back in alarm, tried to say something, but seemed unable to speak and stared with open eyes at him.", "그녀는 놀라서 뒷걸음질 치며 무언가 말하려 했으나, 말이 나오지 않는 듯 눈을 크게 뜨고 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Good evening, Alyona Ivanovna,” he began, trying to speak easily, but his voice would not obey him, it broke and shook.", "\"안녕하십니까, 알료나 이바노브나.\""], ["“I have come... I have brought something...", "그는 태연하게 말하려 애썼으나, 목소리가 말을 듣지 않아 갈라지고 떨려 나왔다."], ["but we’d better come in... to the light....”", "\"가져왔습니다……, 물건을 가져왔어요……, 그런데 안으로 들어가는 게 낫겠습니다……, 밝은 데로요…….\""]], [["And leaving her, he passed straight into the room uninvited.", "그러고는 그녀를 내버려 둔 채, 청하지도 않았건만 곧장 방 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The old woman ran after him; her tongue was unloosed.", "노파는 그를 뒤쫓아 달려왔다. 그녀의 혀가 풀렸다."]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["What it is?", "이게 뭐요?"], ["Who is it?", "누구야?"], ["What do you want?”", "뭘 원하는 거요?\""]], [["“Why, Alyona Ivanovna, you know me... Raskolnikov...", "\"아니, 알료나 이바노브나, 저를 아시잖아요……, 라스콜니코프입니다……, 자, 며칠 전에 약속한 저당물을 가져왔어요…….\""], ["here, I brought you the pledge I promised the other day...” And he held out the pledge.", "그러고는 저당물을 내밀었다."]], [["The old woman glanced for a moment at the pledge, but at once stared in the eyes of her uninvited visitor.", "노파는 잠시 저당물을 흘긋 쳐다보았으나, 곧장 청하지도 않은 방문객의 눈을 빤히 들여다보았다."], ["She looked intently, maliciously and mistrustfully.", "그녀는 뚫어지게, 악의에 차서, 불신에 가득 차 바라보았다."], ["A minute passed; he even fancied something like a sneer in her eyes, as though she had already guessed everything.", "일 분이 지났다. 그는 심지어 그녀의 눈에서 마치 모든 것을 이미 알아챈 듯한 비웃음 같은 것까지 본 것만 같았다."], ["He felt that he was losing his head, that he was almost frightened, so frightened that if she were to look like that and not say a word for another half minute, he thought he would have run away from her.", "그는 자신이 정신을 잃어 가고 있음을, 거의 겁에 질려 있음을 느꼈는데, 만일 그녀가 그렇게 바라보며 또 반 분쯤 말 한마디 없이 있는다면 그는 그녀에게서 달아나 버리고 말 것 같다는 생각이 들 만큼 겁을 먹고 있었다."]], [["“Why do you look at me as though you did not know me?”", "\"왜 저를 모르는 사람 보듯 쳐다보시는 겁니까?\""], ["he said suddenly, also with malice.", "그가 갑자기, 역시 악의에 차서 말했다."], ["“Take it if you like, if not I’ll go elsewhere, I am in a hurry.”", "\"원하시면 받으시고, 싫으면 다른 데로 가겠습니다, 저는 바쁘거든요.\""]], [["He had not even thought of saying this, but it was suddenly said of itself.", "이런 말을 하려고 생각조차 하지 않았는데, 갑자기 저절로 튀어나온 것이었다."], ["The old woman recovered herself, and her visitor’s resolute tone evidently restored her confidence.", "노파는 정신을 차렸고, 방문객의 단호한 어조에 분명 다시 마음을 놓는 듯했다."]], [["“But why, my good sir, all of a minute....", "\"하지만 어쩌자고, 선생, 이렇게 다짜고짜로……."], ["What is it?”", "그게 뭐요?\""], ["she asked, looking at the pledge.", "그녀가 저당물을 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“The silver cigarette case; I spoke of it last time, you know.”", "\"은 담뱃갑입니다, 지난번에 말씀드렸잖아요, 아시면서.\""]], [["She held out her hand.", "그녀가 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“But how pale you are, to be sure... and your hands are trembling too?", "\"한데 얼굴이 어쩜 이리도 창백하시나……, 손까지 떨고 있구려?"], ["Have you been bathing, or what?”", "목욕이라도 하다 왔소, 어쨌소?\""]], [["“Fever,”", "\"열이 나서요.\""], ["he answered abruptly.", "그가 퉁명스레 대답했다."], ["“You can’t help getting pale... if you’ve nothing to eat,”", "\"창백해질 수밖에 없지요……, 먹은 게 없으면.\""], ["he added, with difficulty articulating the words.", "그는 가까스로 말을 또박또박 내뱉었다."]], [["His strength was failing him again.", "다시금 기운이 빠져나가고 있었다."], ["But his answer sounded like the truth; the old woman took the pledge.", "그러나 그의 대답은 진실처럼 들렸으니, 노파는 저당물을 받아 들었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"이게 뭐요?\""], ["she asked once more, scanning Raskolnikov intently, and weighing the pledge in her hand.", "그녀가 라스콜니코프를 뚫어지게 살피고 손으로 저당물의 무게를 가늠하며 다시 한번 물었다."]], [["“A thing... cigarette case....", "\"물건입니다……, 담뱃갑이에요……."], ["Silver....", "은으로 된……."], ["Look at it.”", "보세요.\""]], [["“It does not seem somehow like silver....", "\"어쩐지 은 같지가 않은데……."], ["How he has wrapped it up!”", "어쩜 이리도 칭칭 싸 놨담!\""]], [["Trying to untie the string and turning to the window, to the light (all her windows were shut, in spite of the stifling heat), she left him altogether for some seconds and stood with her back to him.", "끈을 풀려고 애쓰며 그녀가 창문 쪽으로, 빛이 드는 쪽으로 돌아섰을 때(숨 막히는 더위에도 불구하고 그녀의 창문은 죄다 닫혀 있었다), 그녀는 몇 초 동안 그에게서 완전히 등을 돌리고 그를 등진 채 서 있게 되었다."], ["He unbuttoned his coat and freed the axe from the noose, but did not yet take it out altogether, simply holding it in his right hand under the coat.", "그는 외투의 단추를 풀고 도끼를 고리에서 빼냈으나, 아직 완전히 꺼내지는 않은 채 그저 외투 밑 오른손에 쥐고 있었다."], ["His hands were fearfully weak, he felt them every moment growing more numb and more wooden.", "그의 손은 끔찍하게 힘이 없어, 매 순간 점점 더 마비되고 나무토막처럼 굳어 가는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["He was afraid he would let the axe slip and fall....", "그는 도끼가 미끄러져 떨어질까 봐 두려웠다……."], ["A sudden giddiness came over him.", "갑자기 현기증이 그를 덮쳤다."]], [["“But what has he tied it up like this for?”", "\"한데 어쩌자고 이걸 이렇게 칭칭 싸 놨담?\""], ["the old woman cried with vexation and moved towards him.", "노파가 짜증스레 외치며 그에게로 몸을 움직였다."]], [["He had not a minute more to lose.", "이제 더는 단 일 분도 허비할 수 없었다."], ["He pulled the axe quite out, swung it with both arms, scarcely conscious of himself, and almost without effort, almost mechanically, brought the blunt side down on her head.", "그는 도끼를 완전히 꺼내 두 팔로 휘둘렀는데, 거의 자기 자신을 의식하지 못한 채, 거의 힘도 들이지 않고, 거의 기계적으로 도끼의 무딘 등을 그녀의 머리에 내리쳤다."], ["He seemed not to use his own strength in this.", "이때 그는 자기 힘을 쓰는 것 같지도 않았다."], ["But as soon as he had once brought the axe down, his strength returned to him.", "그러나 일단 도끼를 한 번 내리치고 나자 그에게 기운이 돌아왔다."]], [["The old woman was as always bareheaded.", "노파는 늘 그렇듯 맨머리였다."], ["Her thin, light hair, streaked with grey, thickly smeared with grease, was plaited in a rat’s tail and fastened by a broken horn comb which stood out on the nape of her neck.", "희끗희끗 센 가닥이 섞인 그 가늘고 옅은 머리칼은 기름을 잔뜩 발라 떡이 진 채 쥐꼬리처럼 땋여, 부러진 뿔 빗으로 고정되어 뒤통수에 삐죽 솟아 있었다."], ["As she was so short, the blow fell on the very top of her skull.", "그녀가 워낙 키가 작아서 일격은 정수리 한복판에 떨어졌다."], ["She cried out, but very faintly, and suddenly sank all of a heap on the floor, raising her hands to her head.", "그녀는 비명을 질렀으나 아주 희미했고, 갑자기 두 손을 머리로 가져가며 풀썩 바닥에 주저앉았다."], ["In one hand she still held “the pledge.”", "한 손에는 여전히 \"저당물\"을 쥔 채였다."], ["Then he dealt her another and another blow with the blunt side and on the same spot.", "그러자 그는 또 한 번, 또 한 번 무딘 등으로 같은 자리를 내리쳤다."], ["The blood gushed as from an overturned glass, the body fell back.", "피가 뒤집힌 잔에서처럼 쏟아져 나오고, 몸뚱이가 뒤로 넘어갔다."], ["He stepped back, let it fall, and at once bent over her face; she was dead.", "그는 한 발짝 물러나 시체가 쓰러지게 두고는, 곧장 그녀의 얼굴 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["Her eyes seemed to be starting out of their sockets, the brow and the whole face were drawn and contorted convulsively.", "그녀는 죽어 있었다. 두 눈은 눈구멍에서 튀어나올 듯했고, 이마와 온 얼굴은 경련을 일으키며 일그러지고 뒤틀려 있었다."]], [["He laid the axe on the ground near the dead body and felt at once in her pocket (trying to avoid the streaming body)--the same right-hand pocket from which she had taken the key on his last visit.", "그는 시체 곁 바닥에 도끼를 내려놓고는 곧장 그녀의 주머니를 뒤졌다(흘러나오는 피를 피하려 애쓰면서). 지난번 방문 때 그녀가 열쇠를 꺼냈던 바로 그 오른쪽 주머니였다."], ["He was in full possession of his faculties, free from confusion or giddiness, but his hands were still trembling.", "그는 정신을 온전히 가다듬은 상태였고, 혼란도 현기증도 없었으나, 손은 여전히 떨리고 있었다."], ["He remembered afterwards that he had been particularly collected and careful, trying all the time not to get smeared with blood....", "그는 훗날, 자신이 유난히 침착하고 조심스러웠으며 줄곧 피를 묻히지 않으려 애썼던 것을 기억해 냈다……."], ["He pulled out the keys at once, they were all, as before, in one bunch on a steel ring.", "그는 곧 열쇠들을 꺼냈는데, 전과 마찬가지로 모두 강철 고리 하나에 한 다발로 꿰여 있었다."], ["He ran at once into the bedroom with them.", "그는 그것들을 들고 곧장 침실로 달려갔다."], ["It was a very small room with a whole shrine of holy images.", "그곳은 아주 작은 방으로, 성상이 한가득 모셔진 사당 같은 것이 있었다."], ["Against the other wall stood a big bed, very clean and covered with a silk patchwork wadded quilt.", "맞은편 벽에는 큼직한 침대가 놓여 있었는데, 무척 깨끗했고 비단 조각보를 이어 만든 누비이불이 덮여 있었다."], ["Against a third wall was a chest of drawers.", "또 다른 벽에는 서랍장이 있었다."], ["Strange to say, so soon as he began to fit the keys into the chest, so soon as he heard their jingling, a convulsive shudder passed over him.", "이상하게도, 그가 열쇠를 서랍장에 끼우기 시작하자마자, 그 열쇠들이 절그럭거리는 소리를 듣자마자 경련하듯 떨림이 그를 훑고 지나갔다."], ["He suddenly felt tempted again to give it all up and go away.", "그는 갑자기 또다시 모든 걸 포기하고 떠나 버리고 싶은 유혹에 휩싸였다."], ["But that was only for an instant; it was too late to go back.", "그러나 그것도 한순간뿐, 되돌리기에는 너무 늦었다."], ["He positively smiled at himself, when suddenly another terrifying idea occurred to his mind.", "그는 자기 자신을 보며 분명 미소까지 지었는데, 그때 갑자기 또 다른 끔찍한 생각이 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["He suddenly fancied that the old woman might be still alive and might recover her senses.", "그는 갑자기 노파가 아직 살아 있어 정신을 차릴지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Leaving the keys in the chest, he ran back to the body, snatched up the axe and lifted it once more over the old woman, but did not bring it down.", "그는 열쇠를 서랍장에 꽂아 둔 채 시체로 달려가 도끼를 움켜쥐고 다시 한번 노파 위로 쳐들었으나, 내리치지는 않았다."], ["There was no doubt that she was dead.", "그녀가 죽었다는 데는 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["Bending down and examining her again more closely, he saw clearly that the skull was broken and even battered in on one side.", "몸을 숙여 다시 한번 좀 더 가까이 살펴보니, 두개골이 깨지고 한쪽은 심지어 짓이겨져 있는 것이 또렷이 보였다."], ["He was about to feel it with his finger, but drew back his hand and indeed it was evident without that.", "그는 손가락으로 만져 보려 했으나 손을 도로 거두었으니, 사실 그러지 않아도 분명했다."], ["Meanwhile there was a perfect pool of blood.", "그러는 사이 완연한 핏웅덩이가 고였다."], ["All at once he noticed a string on her neck; he tugged at it, but the string was strong and did not snap and besides, it was soaked with blood.", "갑자기 그는 그녀의 목에 끈이 걸려 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 그것을 잡아당겼으나 끈은 질겨서 끊어지지 않았고, 게다가 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다."], ["He tried to pull it out from the front of the dress, but something held it and prevented its coming.", "그는 옷 앞섶에서 그것을 빼내려 했으나, 무언가가 걸려 빠져나오질 않았다."], ["In his impatience he raised the axe again to cut the string from above on the body, but did not dare, and with difficulty, smearing his hand and the axe in the blood, after two minutes’ hurried effort, he cut the string and took it off without touching the body with the axe; he was not mistaken--it was a purse.", "조바심이 나서 그는 다시 도끼를 쳐들어 끈을 몸 위에서 위쪽부터 끊으려 했으나 감히 그러지는 못하고, 가까스로 자기 손과 도끼에 피를 묻혀 가며 이 분간 허둥지둥 애쓴 끝에, 시체에 도끼를 대지 않고 끈을 끊어 벗겨 냈다. 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다 — 그것은 돈주머니였다."], ["On the string were two crosses, one of Cyprus wood and one of copper, and an image in silver filigree, and with them a small greasy chamois leather purse with a steel rim and ring.", "끈에는 십자가 두 개가 달려 있었는데, 하나는 키프로스 나무로, 다른 하나는 구리로 된 것이었고, 또 은 세공으로 만든 성상 하나, 그리고 그것들과 함께 강철 테와 고리가 달린 작고 기름때 묻은 섀미 가죽 돈주머니가 매달려 있었다."], ["The purse was stuffed very full; Raskolnikov thrust it in his pocket without looking at it, flung the crosses on the old woman’s body and rushed back into the bedroom, this time taking the axe with him.", "돈주머니는 빵빵하게 채워져 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그것을 들여다보지도 않고 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고, 십자가들은 노파의 몸뚱이 위로 내던지고는, 이번에는 도끼를 챙겨 들고 침실로 달려 들어갔다."]], [["He was in terrible haste, he snatched the keys, and began trying them again.", "그는 끔찍하게 서둘렀다. 그는 열쇠들을 움켜쥐고 다시 끼워 보기 시작했다."], ["But he was unsuccessful.", "그러나 잘되지 않았다."], ["They would not fit in the locks.", "열쇠가 자물쇠에 맞지 않았다."], ["It was not so much that his hands were shaking, but that he kept making mistakes; though he saw for instance that a key was not the right one and would not fit, still he tried to put it in.", "손이 떨려서라기보다는 자꾸 헛손질을 했던 탓이었다. 이를테면 그는 어떤 열쇠가 맞지 않는다는 것을 빤히 보면서도 여전히 그것을 끼워 보려 했다."], ["Suddenly he remembered and realised that the big key with the deep notches, which was hanging there with the small keys could not possibly belong to the chest of drawers (on his last visit this had struck him), but to some strong box, and that everything perhaps was hidden in that box.", "갑자기 그는, 작은 열쇠들과 함께 거기 매달려 있던 홈이 깊이 팬 그 큰 열쇠가(지난번 방문 때 이것이 그의 눈에 띄었다) 서랍장 것일 리 없고, 어떤 튼튼한 궤짝의 것이며, 어쩌면 모든 것이 그 궤짝 안에 숨겨져 있을지도 모른다는 사실을 떠올리고 깨달았다."], ["He left the chest of drawers, and at once felt under the bedstead, knowing that old women usually keep boxes under their beds.", "그는 서랍장을 내버려 두고 곧장 침대 밑을 더듬었으니, 노파들이 대개 궤짝을 침대 밑에 둔다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["And so it was; there was a good-sized box under the bed, at least a yard in length, with an arched lid covered with red leather and studded with steel nails.", "과연 그러했다. 침대 밑에는 길이가 적어도 일 아르신은 되는 꽤 큼직한 궤짝이 있었는데, 아치형 뚜껑에 붉은 가죽이 씌워지고 강철 못이 촘촘히 박혀 있었다."], ["The notched key fitted at once and unlocked it.", "홈이 팬 열쇠가 곧장 들어맞아 궤짝이 열렸다."], ["At the top, under a white sheet, was a coat of red brocade lined with hareskin; under it was a silk dress, then a shawl and it seemed as though there was nothing below but clothes.", "맨 위에는 흰 천 아래로 토끼 가죽을 댄 붉은 비단 외투가 있었고, 그 아래에는 비단 드레스, 그다음에는 숄이 있었는데, 그 밑에는 옷가지밖에 없는 것 같았다."], ["The first thing he did was to wipe his blood-stained hands on the red brocade.", "그가 가장 먼저 한 일은 피 묻은 손을 그 붉은 비단에 닦는 것이었다."], ["“It’s red, and on red blood will be less noticeable,”", "'붉은색이니까, 붉은 데서는 피가 덜 눈에 띄겠지.'"], ["the thought passed through his mind; then he suddenly came to himself.", "이런 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그러다 그는 갑자기 정신이 들었다."], ["“Good God, am I going out of my senses?”", "'맙소사, 내가 미쳐 가는 건가?'"], ["he thought with terror.", "그는 공포에 질려 생각했다."]], [["But no sooner did he touch the clothes than a gold watch slipped from under the fur coat.", "그러나 그가 옷가지를 건드리자마자 모피 외투 밑에서 금시계 하나가 미끄러져 나왔다."], ["He made haste to turn them all over.", "그는 서둘러 모든 것을 뒤집어 보았다."], ["There turned out to be various articles made of gold among the clothes--probably all pledges, unredeemed or waiting to be redeemed--bracelets, chains, ear-rings, pins and such things.", "옷가지들 사이에서 금으로 된 갖가지 물건들이 나왔는데 — 아마도 찾아가지 못했거나 찾아가기를 기다리는 저당물들이리라 — 팔찌, 사슬, 귀걸이, 핀 따위였다."], ["Some were in cases, others simply wrapped in newspaper, carefully and exactly folded, and tied round with tape.", "어떤 것들은 갑에 들어 있고, 어떤 것들은 그저 신문지에 정성스럽고 가지런히 접어 싸여 끈으로 동여매여 있었다."], ["Without any delay, he began filling up the pockets of his trousers and overcoat without examining or undoing the parcels and cases; but he had not time to take many....", "그는 지체 없이, 꾸러미와 갑들을 살피거나 풀어 보지도 않은 채 바지와 외투 주머니를 채우기 시작했다. 그러나 미처 많이 챙길 새도 없었다……."]], [["He suddenly heard steps in the room where the old woman lay.", "그는 갑자기 노파가 누워 있는 방에서 발소리를 들었다."], ["He stopped short and was still as death.", "그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 죽은 듯이 가만히 있었다."], ["But all was quiet, so it must have been his fancy.", "그러나 모든 것이 고요했으니, 분명 그의 착각이었으리라."], ["All at once he heard distinctly a faint cry, as though someone had uttered a low broken moan.", "그러다 갑자기 그는 누군가가 나직이 끊어지는 신음을 내뱉은 듯, 희미한 비명을 또렷이 들었다."], ["Then again dead silence for a minute or two.", "그러고 나서 다시 일이 분간 죽은 듯한 정적이 흘렀다."], ["He sat squatting on his heels by the box and waited holding his breath.", "그는 궤짝 옆에 쪼그려 앉아 숨을 죽인 채 기다렸다."], ["Suddenly he jumped up, seized the axe and ran out of the bedroom.", "갑자기 그는 벌떡 일어나 도끼를 움켜쥐고 침실에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["In the middle of the room stood Lizaveta with a big bundle in her arms.", "방 한가운데에 리자베타가 큼직한 보따리를 품에 안고 서 있었다."], ["She was gazing in stupefaction at her murdered sister, white as a sheet and seeming not to have the strength to cry out.", "그녀는 살해된 언니를 멍하니 바라보고 있었는데, 백지장처럼 하얗게 질려 비명을 지를 힘조차 없는 듯했다."], ["Seeing him run out of the bedroom, she began faintly quivering all over, like a leaf, a shudder ran down her face; she lifted her hand, opened her mouth, but still did not scream.", "그가 침실에서 뛰쳐나오는 것을 보자 그녀는 나뭇잎처럼 온몸을 희미하게 떨기 시작했고, 얼굴에 경련이 스쳤다. 그녀는 손을 들어 입을 벌렸으나, 여전히 비명을 지르지는 못했다."], ["She began slowly backing away from him into the corner, staring intently, persistently at him, but still uttered no sound, as though she could not get breath to scream.", "그녀는 그에게서 천천히 뒷걸음질 치며 구석으로 물러나면서, 그를 뚫어지게, 집요하게 바라보았으나, 여전히 아무 소리도 내지 못했으니, 마치 비명을 지를 숨조차 들이쉬지 못하는 듯했다."], ["He rushed at her with the axe; her mouth twitched piteously, as one sees babies’ mouths, when they begin to be frightened, stare intently at what frightens them and are on the point of screaming.", "그는 도끼를 들고 그녀에게 달려들었다. 그녀의 입이 가련하게 씰룩거렸는데, 마치 갓난아기들이 겁을 먹기 시작할 때, 자기를 겁먹게 하는 것을 뚫어지게 바라보며 막 울음을 터뜨리려 할 때 짓는 그런 입 모양이었다."], ["And this hapless Lizaveta was so simple and had been so thoroughly crushed and scared that she did not even raise a hand to guard her face, though that was the most necessary and natural action at the moment, for the axe was raised over her face.", "그리고 이 가엾은 리자베타는 어찌나 천진하고, 또 어찌나 철저하게 짓눌리고 겁에 질려 있었던지, 그 순간 무엇보다 절실하고 자연스러운 행동이었을 텐데도 자기 얼굴을 막으려고 손조차 들지 않았으니, 도끼가 그녀의 얼굴 위로 쳐들려 있었는데도 말이다."], ["She only put up her empty left hand, but not to her face, slowly holding it out before her as though motioning him away.", "그녀는 그저 빈 왼손을 들었을 뿐인데, 그것도 얼굴 쪽이 아니라, 마치 그를 밀어내려는 듯 천천히 앞으로 뻗었을 따름이었다."], ["The axe fell with the sharp edge just on the skull and split at one blow all the top of the head.", "도끼는 날카로운 날이 정통으로 정수리에 떨어져, 단 한 번에 머리 윗부분을 쪼개 놓았다."], ["She fell heavily at once.", "그녀는 곧장 무겁게 쓰러졌다."], ["Raskolnikov completely lost his head, snatching up her bundle, dropped it again and ran into the entry.", "라스콜니코프는 완전히 정신을 잃은 채, 그녀의 보따리를 움켜쥐었다가 다시 떨어뜨리고 현관으로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["Fear gained more and more mastery over him, especially after this second, quite unexpected murder.", "공포가 점점 더 그를 사로잡았으니, 특히 이 두 번째의, 전혀 예기치 못한 살인 이후로는 더더욱 그랬다."], ["He longed to run away from the place as fast as possible.", "그는 한시바삐 그곳에서 달아나고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다."], ["And if at that moment he had been capable of seeing and reasoning more correctly, if he had been able to realise all the difficulties of his position, the hopelessness, the hideousness and the absurdity of it, if he could have understood how many obstacles and, perhaps, crimes he had still to overcome or to commit, to get out of that place and to make his way home, it is very possible that he would have flung up everything, and would have gone to give himself up, and not from fear, but from simple horror and loathing of what he had done.", "그리고 만일 그 순간 그가 좀 더 올바르게 보고 헤아릴 수 있었더라면, 만일 그가 자기 처지의 온갖 곤란함과 절망, 그 끔찍함과 부조리를 깨달을 수 있었더라면, 만일 그곳을 빠져나가 집으로 돌아가기까지 자신이 아직 얼마나 많은 장애를, 어쩌면 또 얼마나 많은 범죄를 극복하거나 저질러야 하는지를 이해할 수 있었더라면, 그는 십중팔구 모든 것을 내팽개치고 자수하러 갔을지도 모른다. 그것도 두려움 때문이 아니라, 그저 자신이 저지른 일에 대한 공포와 혐오 때문에."], ["The feeling of loathing especially surged up within him and grew stronger every minute.", "특히 혐오의 감정이 그의 내면에서 솟구쳐 매 순간 점점 더 강해졌다."], ["He would not now have gone to the box or even into the room for anything in the world.", "그는 이제 세상 그 무엇을 준다 해도 궤짝으로는, 아니 그 방으로조차 다시 들어가지 않았으리라."]], [["But a sort of blankness, even dreaminess, had begun by degrees to take possession of him; at moments he forgot himself, or rather, forgot what was of importance, and caught at trifles.", "그러나 일종의 멍한 상태가, 심지어 꿈결 같은 느낌마저 차츰 그를 사로잡기 시작했다. 어떤 순간이면 그는 자기 자신을 잊어버렸으니, 아니 더 정확히 말하면, 중요한 것을 잊어버린 채 사소한 것들에 매달렸다."], ["Glancing, however, into the kitchen and seeing a bucket half full of water on a bench, he bethought him of washing his hands and the axe.", "그러나 부엌을 흘긋 들여다보고 의자 위에 물이 반쯤 찬 양동이가 놓인 것을 보자, 그는 손과 도끼를 씻을 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["His hands were sticky with blood.", "그의 손은 피로 끈적거렸다."], ["He dropped the axe with the blade in the water, snatched a piece of soap that lay in a broken saucer on the window, and began washing his hands in the bucket.", "그는 도끼를 날이 물에 잠기도록 떨어뜨려 넣고, 창가의 깨진 접시에 놓인 비누 한 조각을 집어 들고 양동이에 손을 씻기 시작했다."], ["When they were clean, he took out the axe, washed the blade and spent a long time, about three minutes, washing the wood where there were spots of blood rubbing them with soap.", "손이 깨끗해지자 그는 도끼를 꺼내 날을 씻고, 핏자국이 있는 나무 자루를 비누로 문지르며 오랫동안, 한 삼 분쯤 씻었다."], ["Then he wiped it all with some linen that was hanging to dry on a line in the kitchen and then he was a long while attentively examining the axe at the window.", "그런 다음 부엌에 줄을 매어 말리고 있던 무명천으로 모두 닦아 내고는, 또 한참을 창가에서 도끼를 꼼꼼히 살펴보았다."], ["There was no trace left on it, only the wood was still damp.", "도끼에는 아무런 흔적도 남아 있지 않았고, 다만 나무 자루가 아직 축축할 뿐이었다."], ["He carefully hung the axe in the noose under his coat.", "그는 조심스레 외투 밑 고리에 도끼를 걸었다."], ["Then as far as was possible, in the dim light in the kitchen, he looked over his overcoat, his trousers and his boots.", "그러고 나서 그는 부엌의 희미한 빛 속에서 가능한 한 자기 외투와 바지, 장화를 살펴보았다."], ["At the first glance there seemed to be nothing but stains on the boots.", "언뜻 보기에는 장화에 묻은 얼룩 말고는 아무것도 없는 듯했다."], ["He wetted the rag and rubbed the boots.", "그는 헝겊을 적셔 장화를 문질렀다."], ["But he knew he was not looking thoroughly, that there might be something quite noticeable that he was overlooking.", "그러나 그는 자신이 제대로 살피지 못하고 있다는 것을, 자신이 놓치고 있는 아주 눈에 띄는 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He stood in the middle of the room, lost in thought.", "그는 방 한가운데 서서 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Dark agonising ideas rose in his mind--the idea that he was mad and that at that moment he was incapable of reasoning, of protecting himself, that he ought perhaps to be doing something utterly different from what he was now doing.", "어둡고 괴로운 상념들이 마음속에 떠올랐다 — 자신이 미쳤다는 생각, 그리고 그 순간 자신이 헤아릴 능력도, 스스로를 지킬 능력도 없으며, 어쩌면 지금 하고 있는 것과는 전혀 다른 무언가를 해야 마땅하다는 생각이었다."], ["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["he muttered, “I must fly, fly,”", "그가 중얼거렸다. \"달아나야 해, 달아나야 해.\""], ["and he rushed into the entry.", "그러고는 현관으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["But here a shock of terror awaited him such as he had never known before.", "그러나 여기서 그는 일찍이 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 공포의 충격과 맞닥뜨렸다."]], [["He stood and gazed and could not believe his eyes: the door, the outer door from the stairs, at which he had not long before waited and rung, was standing unfastened and at least six inches open.", "그는 멈춰 서서 바라보았으나 자기 눈을 믿을 수 없었다. 문이, 그가 조금 전 기다리며 초인종을 울렸던 계단 쪽 바깥문이 잠기지 않은 채 적어도 여섯 인치는 열려 있었다."], ["No lock, no bolt, all the time, all that time!", "자물쇠도, 빗장도 없이, 그동안 줄곧, 그 모든 시간 동안 말이다!"], ["The old woman had not shut it after him perhaps as a precaution.", "노파가 어쩌면 조심하느라 그의 뒤로 문을 닫지 않았던 것이다."], ["But, good God!", "그러나, 맙소사!"], ["Why, he had seen Lizaveta afterwards!", "아니, 그는 그 뒤에 리자베타를 보지 않았던가!"], ["And how could he, how could he have failed to reflect that she must have come in somehow!", "그런데 어떻게, 어떻게 그녀가 어떻게든 들어왔으리라는 데 생각이 미치지 못했단 말인가!"], ["She could not have come through the wall!", "그녀가 벽을 뚫고 들어왔을 리는 없지 않은가!"]], [["He dashed to the door and fastened the latch.", "그는 문으로 달려가 빗장을 걸었다."]], [["“But no, the wrong thing again!", "\"아니, 또 잘못했어!"], ["I must get away, get away....”", "빠져나가야 해, 빠져나가야 한다고…….\""]], [["He unfastened the latch, opened the door and began listening on the staircase.", "그는 빗장을 풀고 문을 열어 계단의 소리에 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."]], [["He listened a long time.", "그는 오랫동안 귀를 기울였다."], ["Somewhere far away, it might be in the gateway, two voices were loudly and shrilly shouting, quarrelling and scolding.", "어딘가 멀리서, 어쩌면 대문께에서 두 목소리가 크고 날카롭게 외치며 다투고 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었다."], ["“What are they about?”", "\"저들은 무슨 일이지?\""], ["He waited patiently.", "그는 참을성 있게 기다렸다."], ["At last all was still, as though suddenly cut off; they had separated.", "마침내 모든 것이 마치 갑자기 끊긴 듯 잠잠해졌다. 그들이 헤어진 것이다."], ["He was meaning to go out, but suddenly, on the floor below, a door was noisily opened and someone began going downstairs humming a tune.", "그가 막 나가려는 참인데, 갑자기 한 층 아래에서 문이 요란하게 열리더니 누군가가 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 계단을 내려가기 시작했다."], ["“How is it they all make such a noise?”", "'어째서 다들 이렇게 시끄럽게 군담?'"], ["flashed through his mind.", "그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["Once more he closed the door and waited.", "그는 다시 문을 닫고 기다렸다."], ["At last all was still, not a soul stirring.", "마침내 모든 것이 잠잠해졌고, 단 한 사람도 움직이지 않았다."], ["He was just taking a step towards the stairs when he heard fresh footsteps.", "그가 막 계단 쪽으로 한 발짝 내디디려는데, 또다시 새로운 발소리가 들려왔다."]], [["The steps sounded very far off, at the very bottom of the stairs, but he remembered quite clearly and distinctly that from the first sound he began for some reason to suspect that this was someone coming _there_, to the fourth floor, to the old woman.", "발소리는 아주 멀리서, 계단의 맨 아래에서 들려왔으나, 그는 첫 소리부터 어쩐 영문인지 이것이 누군가가 이리로, 사 층으로, 노파에게로 올라오는 소리라고 의심하기 시작했음을 아주 또렷하고 분명하게 기억했다. 어째서일까?"], ["Why? Were the sounds somehow peculiar, significant?", "그 소리들이 어쩐지 유별나고 심상찮았던 것일까?"], ["The steps were heavy, even and unhurried.", "발소리는 무겁고, 고르고, 서두르지 않았다."], ["Now _he_ had passed the first floor, now he was mounting higher, it was growing more and more distinct!", "자, 그가 이제 이 층을 지났다, 자, 더 높이 올라오고 있다, 점점 더 또렷해지고 있었다!"], ["He could hear his heavy breathing.", "그의 거친 숨소리까지 들렸다."], ["And now the third storey had been reached.", "그리고 이제 삼 층에 다다랐다."], ["Coming here!", "이리로 오고 있다!"], ["And it seemed to him all at once that he was turned to stone, that it was like a dream in which one is being pursued, nearly caught and will be killed, and is rooted to the spot and cannot even move one’s arms.", "그러자 갑자기 그는 자신이 돌로 변해 버린 것만 같았고, 마치 누군가에게 쫓기다 거의 붙잡혀 살해당할 처지인데 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯 꼼짝 못 하고 팔조차 움직일 수 없는 그런 꿈속 같았다."]], [["At last when the unknown was mounting to the fourth floor, he suddenly started, and succeeded in slipping neatly and quickly back into the flat and closing the door behind him.", "마침내 그 낯선 자가 사 층으로 올라오자, 그는 갑자기 흠칫하더니, 잽싸고 날쌔게 셋집 안으로 도로 미끄러져 들어가 등 뒤로 문을 닫는 데 성공했다."], ["Then he took the hook and softly, noiselessly, fixed it in the catch.", "그러고는 걸쇠를 잡아 부드럽게, 소리 없이 고리에 끼웠다."], ["Instinct helped him.", "본능이 그를 도왔다."], ["When he had done this, he crouched holding his breath, by the door.", "그러고 나서 그는 숨을 죽인 채 문 옆에 웅크렸다."], ["The unknown visitor was by now also at the door.", "낯선 방문객도 이제 문 앞에 와 있었다."], ["They were now standing opposite one another, as he had just before been standing with the old woman, when the door divided them and he was listening.", "그들은 이제 서로 마주 보고 서 있었는데, 마치 조금 전 그가 노파와 마주 서 있을 때, 문이 둘 사이를 가르고 그가 귀를 기울이고 있던 그때처럼."]], [["The visitor panted several times.", "방문객은 여러 차례 헐떡거렸다."], ["“He must be a big, fat man,”", "'덩치 크고 뚱뚱한 사내가 분명해.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov, squeezing the axe in his hand.", "라스콜니코프는 손에 쥔 도끼를 움켜쥐며 생각했다."], ["It seemed like a dream indeed.", "정말이지 꿈만 같았다."], ["The visitor took hold of the bell and rang it loudly.", "방문객은 초인종을 잡고 요란하게 울렸다."]], [["As soon as the tin bell tinkled, Raskolnikov seemed to be aware of something moving in the room.", "양철 초인종이 딸랑거리자마자, 라스콜니코프는 방 안에서 무언가 움직이는 것을 알아차린 듯했다."], ["For some seconds he listened quite seriously.", "몇 초 동안 그는 자못 진지하게 귀를 기울였다."], ["The unknown rang again, waited and suddenly tugged violently and impatiently at the handle of the door.", "낯선 자는 다시 초인종을 울리고 기다리다가, 갑자기 문손잡이를 거칠고 조급하게 잡아당겼다."], ["Raskolnikov gazed in horror at the hook shaking in its fastening, and in blank terror expected every minute that the fastening would be pulled out.", "라스콜니코프는 고리에서 흔들리는 걸쇠를 공포에 질려 바라보며, 매 순간 그 걸쇠가 뽑혀 나오지 않을까 망연한 두려움 속에서 기다렸다."], ["It certainly did seem possible, so violently was he shaking it.", "사내가 어찌나 거칠게 흔들어 대는지 정말 그럴 수도 있을 것만 같았다."], ["He was tempted to hold the fastening, but _he_ might be aware of it.", "걸쇠를 붙잡고 싶었으나, 그러면 그자가 눈치챌지도 몰랐다."], ["A giddiness came over him again.", "다시금 현기증이 그를 덮쳤다."], ["“I shall fall down!”", "'쓰러지고 말겠어!'"], ["flashed through his mind, but the unknown began to speak and he recovered himself at once.", "하는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤으나, 낯선 자가 말을 하기 시작하자 그는 곧 정신을 차렸다."]], [["“What’s up?", "\"무슨 일이야?"], ["Are they asleep or murdered?", "잠든 거야, 아니면 뒈진 거야?"], ["D-damn them!”", "빌어먹을 것들!\""], ["he bawled in a thick voice, “Hey, Alyona Ivanovna, old witch!", "그가 걸걸한 목소리로 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"이봐, 알료나 이바노브나, 이 늙은 마녀야!"], ["Lizaveta Ivanovna, hey, my beauty!", "리자베타 이바노브나, 이봐, 내 미인 아가씨!"], ["open the door!", "문 열어!"], ["Oh, damn them!", "아, 빌어먹을 것들!"], ["Are they asleep or what?”", "잠든 거야, 뭐야?\""]], [["And again, enraged, he tugged with all his might a dozen times at the bell.", "그러고는 또다시 화가 치밀어 있는 힘껏 열두 번이나 초인종을 잡아당겼다."], ["He must certainly be a man of authority and an intimate acquaintance.", "분명 권세깨나 있고 친하게 드나드는 자임이 틀림없었다."]], [["At this moment light hurried steps were heard not far off, on the stairs.", "이 순간 멀지 않은 계단에서 가볍고 다급한 발소리가 들려왔다."], ["Someone else was approaching.", "또 누군가가 다가오고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov had not heard them at first.", "라스콜니코프는 처음에는 그 소리를 듣지 못했다."]], [["“You don’t say there’s no one at home,”", "\"설마 집에 아무도 없다는 건 아니겠죠.\""], ["the new-comer cried in a cheerful, ringing voice, addressing the first visitor, who still went on pulling the bell.", "새로 온 자가 여전히 초인종을 잡아당기고 있던 첫 번째 방문객에게 명랑하고 낭랑한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Good evening, Koch.”", "\"안녕하십니까, 코흐.\""]], [["“From his voice he must be quite young,”", "'목소리를 들어 보니 꽤 젊은 자로군.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."]], [["“Who the devil can tell?", "\"누가 알겠나, 빌어먹을?"], ["I’ve almost broken the lock,”", "자물쇠를 거의 부술 뻔했어.\""], ["answered Koch.", "코흐가 대답했다."], ["“But how do you come to know me?”", "\"그런데 자네가 어떻게 나를 아나?\""]], [["“Why!", "\"아니!"], ["The day before yesterday I beat you three times running at billiards at Gambrinus’.”", "그저께 감브리누스에서 당구로 당신을 내리 세 판이나 이겼잖아요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""]], [["“So they are not at home?", "\"그래서 집에 없다는 거예요?"], ["That’s queer.", "거참 이상하네."], ["It’s awfully stupid though.", "하지만 정말 어처구니없는 일이군요."], ["Where could the old woman have gone?", "그 노파가 대체 어딜 갔을까요?"], ["I’ve come on business.”", "일이 있어 왔는데.\""]], [["“Yes; and I have business with her, too.”", "\"그래, 나도 그 여자한테 볼일이 있단 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, what can we do?", "\"그럼 어쩌죠?"], ["Go back, I suppose, Aie--aie!", "돌아가는 수밖에 없겠네요."], ["And I was hoping to get some money!”", "아이고, 아이고! 돈 좀 받을까 했더니!\""], ["cried the young man.", "젊은이가 외쳤다."]], [["“We must give it up, of course, but what did she fix this time for?", "\"물론 포기하는 수밖에 없지, 한데 이번엔 어쩌자고 시간을 잡아 놓은 거야?"], ["The old witch fixed the time for me to come herself.", "그 늙은 마녀가 나더러 오라고 직접 시간을 정해 놓고선."], ["It’s out of my way. And where the devil she can have got to, I can’t make out.", "여기 오는 게 내 길에서 한참 벗어나는데도 말이야."], ["She sits here from year’s end to year’s end, the old hag; her legs are bad and yet here all of a sudden she is out for a walk!”", "게다가 그 여자가 대체 어딜 갔는지 도무지 알 수가 없군. 일 년 내내 여기 죽치고 앉아 있는 늙은 할망구가, 다리도 성치 않으면서, 한데 별안간 산책이라도 나갔단 말인가!\""]], [["“Hadn’t we better ask the porter?”", "\"문지기한테 물어보는 게 낫지 않을까요?\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""]], [["“Where she’s gone and when she’ll be back.”", "\"그 여자가 어디 갔는지, 언제 돌아올지 말이에요.\""]], [["“Hm....", "\"흠……."], ["Damn it all!...", "빌어먹을!……."], ["We might ask....", "물어볼 수야 있겠지……."], ["But you know she never does go anywhere.”", "하지만 그 여자는 어디 나가는 법이 없는데 말이야.\""]], [["And he once more tugged at the door-handle.", "그러고는 다시 한번 문손잡이를 잡아당겼다."]], [["“Damn it all.", "\"빌어먹을."], ["There’s nothing to be done, we must go!”", "어쩔 도리가 없군, 가야겠어!\""]], [["“Stay!”", "\"잠깐만요!\""], ["cried the young man suddenly.", "젊은이가 갑자기 외쳤다."], ["“Do you see how the door shakes if you pull it?”", "\"당겨 보면 문이 어떻게 흔들리는지 보이세요?\" \"그게 어쨌다고?\""]], [["“That shows it’s not locked, but fastened with the hook!", "\"이건 문이 잠긴 게 아니라 걸쇠로 걸려 있다는 뜻이에요!"], ["Do you hear how the hook clanks?” “Well?”", "걸쇠가 절그럭거리는 소리 들리세요?\""]], [["“Why, don’t you see?", "\"그게 어쨌다고?\""], ["That proves that one of them is at home.", "\"아니, 모르시겠어요?"], ["If they were all out, they would have locked the door from the outside with the key and not with the hook from inside.", "이건 둘 중 하나가 집에 있다는 증거잖아요. 만일 다들 나갔다면 바깥에서 열쇠로 문을 잠갔을 거예요, 안에서 걸쇠로 거는 게 아니라."], ["There, do you hear how the hook is clanking?", "자, 걸쇠가 절그럭거리는 소리 들리시죠?"], ["To fasten the hook on the inside they must be at home, don’t you see?", "안에서 걸쇠를 걸려면 집 안에 있어야 하잖아요, 모르시겠어요?"], ["So there they are sitting inside and don’t open the door!”", "그러니까 저들은 안에 들어앉아 있으면서 문을 안 여는 거라고요!\""]], [["“Well!", "\"이런!"], ["And so they must be!”", "그러고 보니 정말 그렇겠군!\""], ["cried Koch, astonished.", "코흐가 놀라 외쳤다."], ["“What are they about in there?”", "\"대체 안에서 뭘 하고 있는 거야?\""], ["And he began furiously shaking the door.", "그러고는 미친 듯이 문을 흔들기 시작했다."]], [["“Stay!”", "\"잠깐만요!\""], ["cried the young man again.", "젊은이가 다시 외쳤다."], ["“Don’t pull at it!", "\"당기지 마세요!"], ["There must be something wrong....", "분명 뭔가 잘못된 거예요……."], ["Here, you’ve been ringing and pulling at the door and still they don’t open!", "자, 당신이 초인종을 울리고 문을 잡아당겨도 저들이 안 열잖아요!"], ["So either they’ve both fainted or...”", "그러니 둘 다 기절했거나, 아니면…….\" \"아니면 뭐?\""]], [["“What?” “I tell you what.", "\"내 말 들어 봐요."], ["Let’s go fetch the porter, let him wake them up.”", "가서 문지기를 데려옵시다, 그 사람더러 저들을 깨우게 하자고요.\""]], [["“All right.”", "\"좋아.\""]], [["Both were going down.", "둘은 내려가려 했다."]], [["“Stay.", "\"잠깐만요."], ["You stop here while I run down for the porter.”", "당신은 여기 계세요, 내가 뛰어가서 문지기를 데려올 테니.\""]], [["“What for?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“Well, you’d better.”", "\"아, 그러는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["“All right.”", "\"좋아.\""]], [["“I’m studying the law you see!", "\"내가 법학을 공부하고 있거든요!"], ["It’s evident, e-vi-dent there’s something wrong here!”", "분명해요, 분-명-히 여기 뭔가 잘못된 거라고요!\""], ["the young man cried hotly, and he ran downstairs.", "젊은이가 흥분해 외치고는 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["Koch remained.", "코흐는 남았다."], ["Once more he softly touched the bell which gave one tinkle, then gently, as though reflecting and looking about him, began touching the door-handle pulling it and letting it go to make sure once more that it was only fastened by the hook.", "그는 다시 한번 초인종을 부드럽게 건드려 한 번 딸랑 울리게 하고는, 가만히, 마치 곰곰이 생각하고 주위를 둘러보듯, 문손잡이를 만지작거리며 당겼다 놓았다 하면서, 그것이 그저 걸쇠로만 걸려 있다는 것을 다시 한번 확인했다."], ["Then puffing and panting he bent down and began looking at the keyhole: but the key was in the lock on the inside and so nothing could be seen.", "그런 다음 씩씩거리고 헐떡거리며 몸을 숙여 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보기 시작했다. 그러나 열쇠가 안쪽에서 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있어 아무것도 보이지 않았다."]], [["Raskolnikov stood keeping tight hold of the axe.", "라스콜니코프는 도끼를 꽉 움켜쥐고 서 있었다."], ["He was in a sort of delirium.", "그는 일종의 착란 상태였다."], ["He was even making ready to fight when they should come in.", "그는 그들이 들어오면 맞서 싸울 채비까지 하고 있었다."], ["While they were knocking and talking together, the idea several times occurred to him to end it all at once and shout to them through the door.", "그들이 문을 두드리며 서로 이야기를 나누는 동안, 한꺼번에 모든 것을 끝장내고 문 너머로 그들에게 소리쳐 버릴까 하는 생각이 몇 번이나 떠올랐다."], ["Now and then he was tempted to swear at them, to jeer at them, while they could not open the door!", "이따금 그는 그들이 문을 열지 못하는 사이에 그들에게 욕설을 퍼붓고, 비웃어 주고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔다!"], ["“Only make haste!”", "\"제발 좀 서둘러라!\""], ["was the thought that flashed through his mind.", "하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."]], [["“But what the devil is he about?...”", "\"한데 저놈은 대체 뭘 하고 있는 거야?……\""], ["Time was passing, one minute, and another--no one came.", "시간이 흘러갔다, 일 분, 또 일 분 — 아무도 오지 않았다."], ["Koch began to be restless.", "코흐가 안절부절못하기 시작했다."]], [["“What the devil?”", "\"빌어먹을, 대체!\""], ["he cried suddenly and in impatience deserting his sentry duty, he, too, went down, hurrying and thumping with his heavy boots on the stairs.", "그가 갑자기 외치더니, 조바심을 못 이겨 보초 임무를 팽개치고 그 역시 무거운 장화로 계단을 쿵쿵 울리며 서둘러 내려갔다."], ["The steps died away.", "발소리가 잦아들었다."]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["What am I to do?”", "어떻게 해야 하나?\""]], [["Raskolnikov unfastened the hook, opened the door--there was no sound.", "라스콜니코프는 걸쇠를 풀고 문을 열었다 — 아무 소리도 없었다."], ["Abruptly, without any thought at all, he went out, closing the door as thoroughly as he could, and went downstairs.", "그는 불쑥, 아무 생각도 없이 밖으로 나가 가능한 한 꼼꼼하게 문을 닫고 계단을 내려가기 시작했다."]], [["He had gone down three flights when he suddenly heard a loud voice below--where could he go!", "그가 세 층을 내려왔을 때 갑자기 아래쪽에서 큰 목소리가 들려왔다 — 어디로 가야 하나!"], ["There was nowhere to hide.", "숨을 곳이 없었다."], ["He was just going back to the flat.", "그는 막 셋집으로 되돌아가려 했다."]], [["“Hey there!", "\"이봐, 거기!"], ["Catch the brute!”", "저 짐승 같은 놈을 잡아!\""]], [["Somebody dashed out of a flat below, shouting, and rather fell than ran down the stairs, bawling at the top of his voice.", "누군가가 아래층 셋집에서 고함을 지르며 뛰쳐나와, 목청껏 악을 쓰면서 계단을 뛰어 내려간다기보다 차라리 굴러떨어졌다."]], [["“Mitka!", "\"미트카!"], ["Mitka!", "미트카!"], ["Mitka!", "미트카!"], ["Mitka!", "미트카!"], ["Mitka!", "미트카!"], ["Blast him!”", "빌어먹을 놈!\""]], [["The shout ended in a shriek; the last sounds came from the yard; all was still.", "고함은 비명으로 끝났다. 마지막 소리들은 안뜰에서 들려왔다."], ["But at the same instant several men talking loud and fast began noisily mounting the stairs.", "모든 것이 잠잠해졌다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 여럿이 크고 빠르게 떠들어 대며 요란하게 계단을 올라오기 시작했다."], ["There were three or four of them.", "서너 명이었다."], ["He distinguished the ringing voice of the young man.", "그는 그 젊은이의 낭랑한 목소리를 알아들었다."], ["“Hey!”", "\"이봐!\""]], [["Filled with despair he went straight to meet them, feeling “come what must!”", "절망에 사로잡혀 그는 곧장 그들을 마주하러 갔으니, '될 대로 되라!' 하는 심정이었다."], ["If they stopped him--all was lost; if they let him pass--all was lost too; they would remember him.", "그들이 그를 멈춰 세운다면 — 모든 것이 끝장이고, 그를 지나가게 둔다 해도 — 역시 모든 것이 끝장이었으니, 그들이 그를 기억할 테니까."], ["They were approaching; they were only a flight from him--and suddenly deliverance!", "그들이 다가오고 있었다. 그들은 그에게서 한 층밖에 떨어져 있지 않았다 — 그런데 갑자기 구원이 나타났다!"], ["A few steps from him on the right, there was an empty flat with the door wide open, the flat on the second floor where the painters had been at work, and which, as though for his benefit, they had just left.", "그에게서 오른쪽으로 몇 걸음 떨어진 곳에, 문이 활짝 열린 빈 셋집이 있었다. 페인트공들이 일하고 있던 이 층 셋집이었는데, 마치 그를 위해서인 듯 그들이 방금 비워 둔 것이었다."], ["It was they, no doubt, who had just run down, shouting.", "방금 고함을 지르며 뛰어 내려간 것이 바로 그들임에 틀림없었다."], ["The floor had only just been painted, in the middle of the room stood a pail and a broken pot with paint and brushes.", "바닥은 막 칠을 한 참이었고, 방 한가운데에는 양동이 하나와 페인트와 붓이 담긴 깨진 단지가 놓여 있었다."], ["In one instant he had whisked in at the open door and hidden behind the wall and only in the nick of time; they had already reached the landing.", "그는 순식간에 열린 문 안으로 휙 들어가 벽 뒤에 숨었고, 그것도 아슬아슬한 순간이었다. 그들은 이미 층계참에 다다라 있었다."], ["Then they turned and went on up to the fourth floor, talking loudly.", "그러더니 그들은 방향을 돌려 큰 소리로 떠들며 사 층으로 올라갔다."], ["He waited, went out on tiptoe and ran down the stairs.", "그는 기다렸다가 발끝으로 살금살금 나와 계단을 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["No one was on the stairs, nor in the gateway.", "계단에도, 대문께에도 아무도 없었다."], ["He passed quickly through the gateway and turned to the left in the street.", "그는 재빨리 대문을 지나 거리에서 왼쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["He knew, he knew perfectly well that at that moment they were at the flat, that they were greatly astonished at finding it unlocked, as the door had just been fastened, that by now they were looking at the bodies, that before another minute had passed they would guess and completely realise that the murderer had just been there, and had succeeded in hiding somewhere, slipping by them and escaping.", "그는 알았다, 그 순간 그들이 셋집에 있다는 것을, 방금 문이 걸려 있던 셋집이 잠겨 있지 않은 것을 발견하고 몹시 놀라리라는 것을, 이제 그들이 시체를 들여다보고 있으리라는 것을, 그리고 또 일 분이 채 지나기 전에 그들이 살인자가 방금 거기 있었으며 어딘가에 숨어 그들 곁을 빠져나가 달아나는 데 성공했다는 것을 짐작하고 완전히 깨달으리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다."], ["They would guess most likely that he had been in the empty flat, while they were going upstairs.", "그들은 십중팔구, 자기들이 계단을 올라가는 동안 그가 그 빈 셋집에 있었으리라고 짐작하리라."], ["And meanwhile he dared not quicken his pace much, though the next turning was still nearly a hundred yards away.", "그러는 사이에도 그는 감히 걸음을 그리 빨리할 수가 없었으니, 다음 모퉁이까지는 아직 거의 백 야드나 남아 있었다."], ["“Should he slip through some gateway and wait somewhere in an unknown street?", "'어느 대문으로든 슬쩍 들어가 낯선 거리 어딘가에서 기다려 볼까?"], ["No, hopeless!", "아니야, 가망 없어!"], ["Should he fling away the axe?", "도끼를 내던져 버릴까?"], ["Should he take a cab?", "마차를 잡아탈까?"], ["Hopeless, hopeless!”", "가망 없어, 가망 없다고!'"]], [["At last he reached the turning.", "마침내 그는 모퉁이에 다다랐다."], ["He turned down it more dead than alive.", "그는 산 것도 죽은 것도 아닌 꼴로 그 모퉁이를 돌아 내려갔다."], ["Here he was half way to safety, and he understood it; it was less risky because there was a great crowd of people, and he was lost in it like a grain of sand.", "여기까지 오니 그는 안전한 곳까지 절반은 온 셈이었고, 그도 그것을 알고 있었다. 사람들이 잔뜩 붐벼 그가 모래알처럼 그 속에 묻혀 버렸기에 위험이 덜했다."], ["But all he had suffered had so weakened him that he could scarcely move.", "그러나 그가 겪은 그 모든 일이 그를 어찌나 쇠약하게 만들었던지 그는 거의 몸을 움직일 수조차 없었다."], ["Perspiration ran down him in drops, his neck was all wet.", "땀이 방울방울 그를 타고 흘러내려, 목덜미가 온통 젖어 있었다."], ["“My word, he has been going it!”", "\"이런, 저 친구 어지간히 마셔 댔구먼!\""], ["someone shouted at him when he came out on the canal bank.", "그가 운하 강둑으로 나섰을 때 누군가가 그에게 소리쳤다."]], [["He was only dimly conscious of himself now, and the farther he went the worse it was.", "그는 이제 자기 자신을 어렴풋이밖에 의식하지 못했고, 멀리 갈수록 더 심해졌다."], ["He remembered however, that on coming out on to the canal bank, he was alarmed at finding few people there and so being more conspicuous, and he had thought of turning back.", "그러나 그는 운하 강둑으로 나섰을 때, 거기에 사람이 거의 없어 자신이 더 눈에 띈다는 데 놀라 되돌아갈 생각을 했던 것을 기억했다."], ["Though he was almost falling from fatigue, he went a long way round so as to get home from quite a different direction.", "그는 피로로 거의 쓰러질 지경이었으나, 전혀 다른 방향에서 집으로 가려고 한참을 빙 둘러서 갔다."]], [["He was not fully conscious when he passed through the gateway of his house!", "자기 집 대문을 지날 때 그는 온전히 정신이 들어 있지 않았다!"], ["He was already on the staircase before he recollected the axe.", "그는 이미 계단에 올라서고 나서야 도끼를 떠올렸다."], ["And yet he had a very grave problem before him, to put it back and to escape observation as far as possible in doing so.", "그런데 그에게는 도끼를 제자리에 돌려놓되 그러는 동안 가능한 한 남의 눈에 띄지 않아야 한다는 자못 중대한 문제가 놓여 있었다."], ["He was of course incapable of reflecting that it might perhaps be far better not to restore the axe at all, but to drop it later on in somebody’s yard.", "그는 물론 도끼를 아예 돌려놓지 않고 나중에 누군가의 안뜰에 떨어뜨려 두는 편이 훨씬 나을지도 모른다는 데 생각이 미칠 능력이 없었다."], ["But it all happened fortunately, the door of the porter’s room was closed but not locked, so that it seemed most likely that the porter was at home.", "그러나 모든 것이 다행스레 풀렸으니, 문지기 방의 문이 닫혀 있긴 했으나 잠겨 있지는 않아서, 십중팔구 문지기가 집에 있는 것 같았다."], ["But he had so completely lost all power of reflection that he walked straight to the door and opened it.", "그러나 그는 헤아릴 능력을 어찌나 완전히 잃어버렸던지 곧장 그 문으로 걸어가 문을 열었다."], ["If the porter had asked him, “What do you want?”", "만일 문지기가 그에게 \"뭘 원하시오?\""], ["he would perhaps have simply handed him the axe.", "하고 물었더라면, 그는 어쩌면 그냥 도끼를 건네주었을지도 모른다."], ["But again the porter was not at home, and he succeeded in putting the axe back under the bench, and even covering it with the chunk of wood as before.", "그러나 다행히 문지기는 또 집에 없었고, 그는 도끼를 의자 밑 제자리에 돌려놓고 전처럼 장작 토막으로 덮어 두는 데까지 성공했다."], ["He met no one, not a soul, afterwards on the way to his room; the landlady’s door was shut.", "그 후 방으로 가는 길에 그는 아무도, 단 한 사람도 마주치지 않았다. 집주인 여자의 문은 닫혀 있었다."], ["When he was in his room, he flung himself on the sofa just as he was--he did not sleep, but sank into blank forgetfulness.", "자기 방에 들어서자 그는 입은 그대로 소파에 몸을 내던졌다 — 그는 잠든 것이 아니라, 망연한 망각 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["If anyone had come into his room then, he would have jumped up at once and screamed.", "만일 그때 누군가 그의 방에 들어왔더라면, 그는 곧장 벌떡 일어나 비명을 질렀으리라."], ["Scraps and shreds of thoughts were simply swarming in his brain, but he could not catch at one, he could not rest on one, in spite of all his efforts....", "토막 나고 너덜너덜한 생각들이 그의 머릿속에 그저 우글거렸으나, 아무리 애를 써도 그는 그중 어느 하나도 붙잡을 수 없었고, 어느 하나에도 머물 수 없었다……."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["So he lay a very long while.", "그렇게 그는 아주 오랫동안 누워 있었다."], ["Now and then he seemed to wake up, and at such moments he noticed that it was far into the night, but it did not occur to him to get up.", "이따금 잠에서 깨어나는 듯했고, 그런 순간이면 밤이 깊었다는 것을 알아차렸지만, 일어나야겠다는 생각은 들지 않았다."], ["At last he noticed that it was beginning to get light.", "마침내 그는 날이 밝아 오기 시작하는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He was lying on his back, still dazed from his recent oblivion.", "그는 등을 대고 누운 채, 조금 전의 망각으로부터 여전히 멍한 상태였다."], ["Fearful, despairing cries rose shrilly from the street, sounds which he heard every night, indeed, under his window after two o’clock.", "거리에서 무섭고 절망적인 비명 소리가 째지듯 솟아올랐는데, 사실 그가 매일 밤 두 시가 지나면 자기 창문 아래로 듣던 소리였다."], ["They woke him up now.", "이제 그 소리가 그를 깨웠다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["the drunken men are coming out of the taverns,”", "술 취한 사내들이 선술집에서 쏟아져 나오는군.\""], ["he thought, “it’s past two o’clock,”", "그는 생각했다. \"두 시가 지났어.\""], ["and at once he leaped up, as though someone had pulled him from the sofa.", "그러고는 누군가 소파에서 끌어 내리기라도 한 듯 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Past two o’clock!”", "두 시가 지났다고!\""]], [["He sat down on the sofa--and instantly recollected everything!", "그는 소파에 앉았다 — 그리고 순간 모든 것이 떠올랐다!"], ["All at once, in one flash, he recollected everything.", "갑자기, 단번에, 번개처럼 그는 모든 것을 기억해 냈다."]], [["For the first moment he thought he was going mad.", "처음 한순간 그는 자신이 미쳐 가는 줄 알았다."], ["A dreadful chill came over him; but the chill was from the fever that had begun long before in his sleep.", "끔찍한 한기가 그를 덮쳤다. 그러나 그 한기는 이미 잠 속에서 한참 전부터 시작된 열병에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["Now he was suddenly taken with violent shivering, so that his teeth chattered and all his limbs were shaking.", "이제 그는 갑자기 격렬한 오한에 사로잡혀, 이가 딱딱 부딪치고 온 사지가 떨려 왔다."], ["He opened the door and began listening--everything in the house was asleep.", "그는 문을 열고 귀를 기울이기 시작했다 — 집 안의 모든 것이 잠들어 있었다."], ["With amazement he gazed at himself and everything in the room around him, wondering how he could have come in the night before without fastening the door, and have flung himself on the sofa without undressing, without even taking his hat off.", "그는 자기 자신과 방 안의 모든 것을 놀란 눈으로 바라보며, 어떻게 어젯밤에 문도 잠그지 않고 들어와서는, 옷도 벗지 않고, 심지어 모자조차 벗지 않은 채로 소파에 몸을 던졌는지 의아해했다."], ["It had fallen off and was lying on the floor near his pillow.", "모자는 벗겨져 베개 곁 바닥에 떨어져 있었다."]], [["“If anyone had come in, what would he have thought?", "\"누가 들어왔더라면 어떻게 생각했을까?"], ["That I’m drunk but...”", "내가 취했다고 — 하지만…….\""]], [["He rushed to the window.", "그는 창가로 달려갔다."], ["There was light enough, and he began hurriedly looking himself all over from head to foot, all his clothes; were there no traces?", "빛은 충분했고, 그는 서둘러 머리끝부터 발끝까지, 옷이란 옷은 죄다 살펴보기 시작했다. 흔적은 없는가?"], ["But there was no doing it like that; shivering with cold, he began taking off everything and looking over again.", "그러나 그런 식으로는 안 될 일이었다. 추위에 떨면서 그는 모든 것을 벗어 다시 살펴보기 시작했다."], ["He turned everything over to the last threads and rags, and mistrusting himself, went through his search three times.", "그는 마지막 실오라기와 누더기 한 조각까지 죄다 뒤집어 보았고, 자기 자신조차 믿지 못해 세 번이나 거듭 뒤졌다."]], [["But there seemed to be nothing, no trace, except in one place, where some thick drops of congealed blood were clinging to the frayed edge of his trousers.", "그러나 아무것도 없는 듯했다, 흔적 하나 없었다, 다만 한 군데, 바지의 해진 가장자리에 엉겨 붙은 검붉은 핏방울 몇 개가 들러붙어 있을 뿐이었다."], ["He picked up a big claspknife and cut off the frayed threads.", "그는 큼직한 접이식 칼을 집어 들어 해진 실밥들을 잘라 냈다."], ["There seemed to be nothing more.", "그 밖에는 더 이상 아무것도 없는 듯했다."]], [["Suddenly he remembered that the purse and the things he had taken out of the old woman’s box were still in his pockets!", "문득 그는 노파의 궤짝에서 꺼낸 지갑과 물건들이 아직 주머니 속에 그대로 들어 있다는 것을 떠올렸다!"], ["He had not thought till then of taking them out and hiding them!", "그때까지 그것들을 꺼내 숨길 생각조차 하지 못했던 것이다!"], ["He had not even thought of them while he was examining his clothes!", "옷을 살펴보는 동안에도 그것들을 떠올리지조차 못했다! 이게 무슨 일이람?"], ["What next? Instantly he rushed to take them out and fling them on the table.", "그는 즉시 달려가 그것들을 꺼내 탁자 위에 내던졌다."], ["When he had pulled out everything, and turned the pocket inside out to be sure there was nothing left, he carried the whole heap to the corner.", "모든 것을 꺼내고, 주머니를 뒤집어 아무것도 남지 않았는지 확인한 다음, 그는 그 무더기 전부를 구석으로 가져갔다."], ["The paper had come off the bottom of the wall and hung there in tatters.", "벽 아래쪽 벽지가 떨어져 너덜너덜 늘어져 있었다."], ["He began stuffing all the things into the hole under the paper: “They’re in!", "그는 벽지 밑 그 구멍 속으로 물건들을 죄다 쑤셔 넣기 시작했다. \"들어갔어!"], ["All out of sight, and the purse too!”", "다 보이지 않게 됐어, 지갑까지도!\""], ["he thought gleefully, getting up and gazing blankly at the hole which bulged out more than ever.", "그는 신이 나서 생각하며 일어나, 어느 때보다 불룩 튀어나온 그 구멍을 멍하니 바라보았다."], ["Suddenly he shuddered all over with horror; “My God!”", "갑자기 그는 공포로 온몸을 부르르 떨었다. \"맙소사!\""], ["he whispered in despair: “what’s the matter with me?", "그는 절망 속에서 속삭였다. \"내가 도대체 왜 이러지?"], ["Is that hidden?", "이게 숨긴 건가?"], ["Is that the way to hide things?”", "이게 물건을 숨기는 방식인가?\""]], [["He had not reckoned on having trinkets to hide.", "그는 자질구레한 패물들을 숨겨야 하리라고는 미처 계산하지 못했었다."], ["He had only thought of money, and so had not prepared a hiding-place.", "그저 돈만 생각했기에, 숨길 장소를 마련해 두지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“But now, now, what am I glad of?”", "\"하지만 이제, 이제 내가 뭘 기뻐하는 거지?\""], ["he thought, “Is that hiding things?", "그는 생각했다. \"이게 물건을 숨기는 건가?"], ["My reason’s deserting me--simply!”", "내 이성이 나를 버리는군 — 그저 그뿐이야!\""]], [["He sat down on the sofa in exhaustion and was at once shaken by another unbearable fit of shivering.", "그는 기진맥진하여 소파에 주저앉았고, 곧바로 또 한 차례 견딜 수 없는 오한이 그를 뒤흔들었다."], ["Mechanically he drew from a chair beside him his old student’s winter coat, which was still warm though almost in rags, covered himself up with it and once more sank into drowsiness and delirium.", "무심결에 그는 곁의 의자에서 낡은 대학생 시절 겨울 외투를 끌어당겼는데, 거의 누더기가 다 됐어도 아직은 따뜻한 것이었다. 그는 그것을 덮고 다시 한번 졸음과 헛소리 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["He lost consciousness.", "그는 의식을 잃었다."]], [["Not more than five minutes had passed when he jumped up a second time, and at once pounced in a frenzy on his clothes again.", "오 분도 채 지나지 않아 그는 두 번째로 벌떡 일어났고, 즉시 미친 듯이 또다시 자기 옷에 달려들었다."]], [["“How could I go to sleep again with nothing done?", "\"어떻게 아무것도 안 해 놓고 또 잠들 수 있었지?"], ["Yes, yes; I have not taken the loop off the armhole!", "그래, 그래, 겨드랑이 안쪽에서 고리를 떼어 내지 않았잖아!"], ["I forgot it, forgot a thing like that!", "깜빡했어, 그런 걸 깜빡하다니!"], ["Such a piece of evidence!”", "이런 결정적인 증거를!\""]], [["He pulled off the noose, hurriedly cut it to pieces and threw the bits among his linen under the pillow.", "그는 그 매듭을 뜯어내, 황급히 잘게 잘라서는 그 조각들을 베개 밑 속옷 사이에 던져 넣었다."]], [["“Pieces of torn linen couldn’t rouse suspicion, whatever happened; I think not, I think not, any way!”", "\"찢어진 속옷 조각들이야 무슨 일이 생겨도 의심을 살 리 없지. 그렇고말고, 그렇고말고, 어쨌든!\""], ["he repeated, standing in the middle of the room, and with painful concentration he fell to gazing about him again, at the floor and everywhere, trying to make sure he had not forgotten anything.", "그는 방 한가운데 서서 거듭 되뇌었고, 고통스러우리만치 정신을 집중하며 또다시 주위를, 바닥을, 사방을 둘러보기 시작했다, 무언가 빠뜨린 것은 없는지 확인하려고 애쓰면서."], ["The conviction that all his faculties, even memory, and the simplest power of reflection were failing him, began to be an insufferable torture.", "자기의 모든 능력이, 기억마저, 그리고 가장 단순한 사고력조차 자신을 저버리고 있다는 확신이 견딜 수 없는 고문이 되어 가기 시작했다."]], [["“Surely it isn’t beginning already!", "\"정말 벌써 시작된 건 아니겠지!"], ["Surely it isn’t my punishment coming upon me?", "정말 내 형벌이 내게 닥쳐오는 건 아니겠지?"], ["It is!”", "그래, 바로 그거야!\""]], [["The frayed rags he had cut off his trousers were actually lying on the floor in the middle of the room, where anyone coming in would see them!", "바지에서 잘라 낸 해진 누더기 조각들이 정말로 방 한가운데 바닥에, 들어오는 사람이라면 누구나 볼 수 있는 곳에 떨어져 있었던 것이다!"]], [["“What is the matter with me!”", "\"내가 도대체 왜 이러지!\""], ["he cried again, like one distraught.", "그는 넋이 나간 사람처럼 또다시 외쳤다."]], [["Then a strange idea entered his head; that, perhaps, all his clothes were covered with blood, that, perhaps, there were a great many stains, but that he did not see them, did not notice them because his perceptions were failing, were going to pieces...", "그때 이상한 생각이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 어쩌면 자기 옷이 죄다 피로 뒤덮여 있을지도, 어쩌면 수많은 얼룩이 있는데 다만 그것을 보지 못하고, 알아차리지 못하는 것일 뿐인지도 모른다고, 자신의 지각이 무너지고 산산조각 나고 있어서……"], ["his reason was clouded....", "이성이 흐려지고 있어서……."], ["Suddenly he remembered that there had been blood on the purse too.", "문득 그는 지갑에도 피가 묻어 있었다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Then there must be blood on the pocket too, for I put the wet purse in my pocket!”", "그렇다면 주머니에도 피가 묻어 있을 게 분명해, 젖은 지갑을 주머니에 넣었으니까!\""]], [["In a flash he had turned the pocket inside out and, yes!--there were traces, stains on the lining of the pocket!", "번개처럼 그는 주머니를 뒤집어 보았고, 그렇다! — 흔적이 있었다, 주머니 안감에 얼룩이 있었다!"]], [["“So my reason has not quite deserted me, so I still have some sense and memory, since I guessed it of myself,”", "\"그러니 내 이성이 아주 나를 버린 건 아니야, 아직 분별력과 기억이 남아 있어, 내 스스로 이걸 알아차렸으니까.\""], ["he thought triumphantly, with a deep sigh of relief; “it’s simply the weakness of fever, a moment’s delirium,”", "그는 깊은 안도의 한숨을 내쉬며 의기양양하게 생각했다. \"이건 그저 열병으로 인한 쇠약일 뿐, 한순간의 헛소리일 뿐이야.\""], ["and he tore the whole lining out of the left pocket of his trousers.", "그러고는 바지 왼쪽 주머니의 안감을 통째로 뜯어냈다."], ["At that instant the sunlight fell on his left boot; on the sock which poked out from the boot, he fancied there were traces!", "그 순간 햇빛이 그의 왼쪽 장화 위로 떨어졌다. 장화에서 삐져나온 양말에, 흔적이 있는 것만 같았다!"], ["He flung off his boots; “traces indeed!", "그는 장화를 벗어 던졌다. \"정말 흔적이군!"], ["The tip of the sock was soaked with blood;”", "양말 끝이 피에 흠뻑 젖어 있어.\""], ["he must have unwarily stepped into that pool....", "무심결에 그 핏웅덩이를 밟았던 게 분명했다……."], ["“But what am I to do with this now?", "\"하지만 이제 이걸 어떻게 하지?"], ["Where am I to put the sock and rags and pocket?”", "이 양말이며 누더기며 주머니를 어디에 둔단 말인가?\""]], [["He gathered them all up in his hands and stood in the middle of the room.", "그는 그것들을 죄다 손에 그러모은 채 방 한가운데 섰다."]], [["“In the stove?", "\"난로 속에?"], ["But they would ransack the stove first of all.", "하지만 무엇보다 먼저 난로부터 샅샅이 뒤지겠지."], ["Burn them?", "태워 버릴까?"], ["But what can I burn them with?", "하지만 무엇으로 태운담?"], ["There are no matches even.", "성냥조차 없는데."], ["No, better go out and throw it all away somewhere.", "아니야, 차라리 나가서 어디든 죄다 던져 버리는 게 나아."], ["Yes, better throw it away,”", "그래, 던져 버리는 게 낫겠어.\""], ["he repeated, sitting down on the sofa again, “and at once, this minute, without lingering...”", "그는 다시 소파에 앉으며 되뇌었다. \"그것도 당장, 바로 지금, 꾸물대지 말고…….\""]], [["But his head sank on the pillow instead.", "그러나 그의 머리는 도리어 베개 위로 떨어졌다."], ["Again the unbearable icy shivering came over him; again he drew his coat over him.", "다시 견딜 수 없는 얼음장 같은 오한이 그를 덮쳤다. 그는 다시 외투를 끌어다 덮었다."]], [["And for a long while, for some hours, he was haunted by the impulse to “go off somewhere at once, this moment, and fling it all away, so that it may be out of sight and done with, at once, at once!”", "그리고 오랫동안, 몇 시간이나, 그는 \"지금 당장, 이 순간에 어디로든 나가서 그것들을 죄다 던져 버려, 눈앞에서 사라지게 하고 끝장을 내자, 당장, 당장!\" 하는 충동에 시달렸다."], ["Several times he tried to rise from the sofa, but could not.", "그는 몇 번이나 소파에서 일어나려 했지만 일어날 수가 없었다."]], [["He was thoroughly waked up at last by a violent knocking at his door.", "마침내 그는 문을 사납게 두드리는 소리에 완전히 잠이 깼다."]], [["“Open, do, are you dead or alive?", "\"열어요, 좀, 죽었어요, 살았어요?"], ["He keeps sleeping here!”", "여기서 내내 잠만 자는군!\""], ["shouted Nastasya, banging with her fist on the door.", "나스타시야가 주먹으로 문을 쾅쾅 두드리며 소리쳤다."], ["“For whole days together he’s snoring here like a dog!", "\"며칠씩 내내 여기서 개처럼 코나 골고 말이야!"], ["A dog he is too.", "정말 개라니까."], ["Open, I tell you.", "열라고요."], ["It’s past ten.”", "열 시가 지났어요.\""]], [["“Maybe he’s not at home,”", "\"집에 없을지도 모르지.\""], ["said a man’s voice.", "한 남자의 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“Ha!", "\"하!"], ["that’s the porter’s voice....", "저건 문지기 목소리잖아……."], ["What does he want?”", "저 사람이 뭘 원하는 거지?\""]], [["He jumped up and sat on the sofa.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 소파에 앉았다."], ["The beating of his heart was a positive pain.", "심장이 뛰는 것이 그야말로 고통스러웠다."]], [["“Then who can have latched the door?”", "\"그럼 누가 문을 걸어 잠갔겠어요?\""], ["retorted Nastasya.", "나스타시야가 받아쳤다."], ["“He’s taken to bolting himself in!", "\"이젠 문을 안에서 걸어 잠그기까지 하네!"], ["As if he were worth stealing!", "훔쳐 갈 게 있기라도 한 것처럼!"], ["Open, you stupid, wake up!”", "열어요, 이 멍청아, 일어나라고요!\""]], [["“What do they want?", "\"저들이 뭘 원하는 거지?"], ["Why the porter?", "왜 문지기가?"], ["All’s discovered.", "다 발각된 거야."], ["Resist or open?", "버틸까, 열까?"], ["Come what may!...”", "될 대로 되라지!……\""]], [["He half rose, stooped forward and unlatched the door.", "그는 반쯤 몸을 일으켜, 앞으로 구부려 문고리를 벗겼다."]], [["His room was so small that he could undo the latch without leaving the bed.", "그의 방은 워낙 작아서 침대에서 내려오지 않고도 문고리를 풀 수 있었다."], ["Yes; the porter and Nastasya were standing there.", "그렇다, 문지기와 나스타시야가 거기 서 있었다."]], [["Nastasya stared at him in a strange way.", "나스타시야가 묘한 눈길로 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["He glanced with a defiant and desperate air at the porter, who without a word held out a grey folded paper sealed with bottle-wax.", "그는 도전적이면서도 절망적인 태도로 문지기를 흘끗 보았는데, 문지기는 아무 말 없이 병마개 밀랍으로 봉인된 회색 접지 한 장을 내밀었다."]], [["“A notice from the office,”", "\"관청에서 온 통지요.\""], ["he announced, as he gave him the paper.", "그가 종이를 건네며 알렸다."]], [["“From what office?”", "\"무슨 관청에서?\""]], [["“A summons to the police office, of course.", "\"경찰서 출두 명령이지요, 당연히."], ["You know which office.”", "어느 관청인지 댁이 잘 알 거요.\""]], [["“To the police?...", "\"경찰서로?……"], ["What for?...”", "무슨 일로?……\""]], [["“How can I tell?", "\"내가 어떻게 알겠소?"], ["You’re sent for, so you go.”", "부르니까 가는 거지.\""]], [["The man looked at him attentively, looked round the room and turned to go away.", "사내는 그를 유심히 바라보고, 방 안을 둘러보더니, 돌아서 가려 했다."]], [["“He’s downright ill!”", "\"이 양반, 완전히 앓고 있네!\""], ["observed Nastasya, not taking her eyes off him.", "나스타시야가 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다."], ["The porter turned his head for a moment.", "문지기가 잠깐 고개를 돌렸다."], ["“He’s been in a fever since yesterday,”", "\"어제부터 열병에 걸렸어요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["Raskolnikov made no response and held the paper in his hands, without opening it.", "라스콜니코프는 아무 대꾸도 없이 종이를 손에 든 채 펴 보지도 않았다."], ["“Don’t you get up then,”", "\"그럼 일어나지 말아요.\""], ["Nastasya went on compassionately, seeing that he was letting his feet down from the sofa.", "나스타시야가 그가 소파에서 발을 내리는 것을 보고 측은한 듯 말을 이었다."], ["“You’re ill, and so don’t go; there’s no such hurry.", "\"앓고 있으니 가지 말아요. 그렇게 서두를 것도 없는데."], ["What have you got there?”", "그건 또 뭐예요?\""]], [["He looked; in his right hand he held the shreds he had cut from his trousers, the sock, and the rags of the pocket.", "그는 들여다보았다. 오른손에 그는 바지에서 잘라 낸 조각들과 양말, 그리고 주머니 누더기를 쥐고 있었다."], ["So he had been asleep with them in his hand.", "그러니까 그것들을 손에 쥔 채 잠들었던 것이다."], ["Afterwards reflecting upon it, he remembered that half waking up in his fever, he had grasped all this tightly in his hand and so fallen asleep again.", "나중에 곰곰이 따져 보고서 그는, 열병 속에서 반쯤 깨어났을 때 이 모든 것을 손에 꽉 움켜쥐고는 그대로 다시 잠들었던 것을 기억해 냈다."]], [["“Look at the rags he’s collected and sleeps with them, as though he has got hold of a treasure...”", "\"좀 봐, 이 양반 누더기를 그러모아서는, 무슨 보물이라도 잡은 것처럼 끌어안고 잔다니까…….\""]], [["And Nastasya went off into her hysterical giggle.", "그러고는 나스타시야가 신경질적인 키득거림을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Instantly he thrust them all under his great coat and fixed his eyes intently upon her.", "순간 그는 그것들을 죄다 외투 밑으로 쑤셔 넣고 그녀에게 뚫어지게 시선을 박았다."], ["Far as he was from being capable of rational reflection at that moment, he felt that no one would behave like that with a person who was going to be arrested.", "그 순간 이성적으로 사고할 능력과는 한참 거리가 멀었지만, 그는 곧 체포될 사람을 두고 저런 식으로 행동하지는 않으리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["“But...", "\"하지만……"], ["the police?”", "경찰서는?\""]], [["“You’d better have some tea!", "\"차라리 차나 좀 들어요! 어때요?"], ["Yes? I’ll bring it, there’s some left.”", "가져다줄게요, 남은 게 좀 있으니.\""]], [["“No...", "\"아니……"], ["I’m going; I’ll go at once,”", "가야 해, 당장 가겠어.\""], ["he muttered, getting on to his feet.", "그는 발을 디디고 일어서며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Why, you’ll never get downstairs!”", "\"아니, 댁은 아래층까지도 못 내려갈걸요!\""]], [["“Yes, I’ll go.”", "\"아니, 가야 해.\""]], [["“As you please.”", "\"맘대로 해요.\""]], [["She followed the porter out.", "그녀는 문지기를 따라 나갔다."]], [["At once he rushed to the light to examine the sock and the rags.", "곧바로 그는 밝은 데로 달려가 양말과 누더기를 살펴보았다."]], [["“There are stains, but not very noticeable; all covered with dirt, and rubbed and already discoloured.", "\"얼룩이 있긴 한데, 그리 눈에 띄지 않아. 죄다 흙먼지로 덮이고, 문질려서 벌써 변색됐어."], ["No one who had no suspicion could distinguish anything.", "의심을 품지 않은 사람이라면 누구도 아무것도 분간하지 못할 거야."], ["Nastasya from a distance could not have noticed, thank God!”", "나스타시야도 멀리서는 알아차리지 못했어, 다행이야!\""], ["Then with a tremor he broke the seal of the notice and began reading; he was a long while reading, before he understood.", "그러고 나서 그는 떨리는 손으로 통지의 봉인을 뜯고 읽기 시작했다. 그는 한참 동안 읽고서야 비로소 내용을 이해했다."], ["It was an ordinary summons from the district police-station to appear that day at half-past nine at the office of the district superintendent.", "그것은 그날 아홉 시 반에 관할 경찰서장 사무실로 출두하라는, 관할 경찰서의 평범한 출두 명령서였다."]], [["“But when has such a thing happened?", "\"하지만 이런 일이 언제 있었던가?"], ["I never have anything to do with the police!", "난 경찰과는 아무 상관도 없는데!"], ["And why just to-day?”", "그런데 왜 하필 오늘이지?\""], ["he thought in agonising bewilderment.", "그는 괴로운 당혹감 속에서 생각했다."], ["“Good God, only get it over soon!”", "\"맙소사, 그저 어서 끝나기나 했으면!\""]], [["He was flinging himself on his knees to pray, but broke into laughter--not at the idea of prayer, but at himself.", "그는 기도하려고 무릎을 꿇으려다가, 웃음을 터뜨렸다 — 기도라는 생각 때문이 아니라, 자기 자신을 향한 웃음이었다."]], [["He began, hurriedly dressing.", "그는 서둘러 옷을 입기 시작했다."], ["“If I’m lost, I am lost, I don’t care!", "\"끝장이라면, 끝장인 거지, 상관없어!"], ["Shall I put the sock on?”", "양말을 신을까?\""], ["he suddenly wondered, “it will get dustier still and the traces will be gone.”", "그는 문득 의아해했다. \"더 먼지투성이가 될 테고 흔적도 사라질 테니까.\""]], [["But no sooner had he put it on than he pulled it off again in loathing and horror.", "그러나 그것을 신자마자 그는 혐오와 공포에 사로잡혀 다시 벗어 버렸다."], ["He pulled it off, but reflecting that he had no other socks, he picked it up and put it on again--and again he laughed.", "벗긴 했지만, 다른 양말이 없다는 데 생각이 미쳐, 그는 그것을 집어 들어 다시 신었다 — 그러고는 또다시 웃었다."]], [["“That’s all conventional, that’s all relative, merely a way of looking at it,”", "\"이건 죄다 관습적인 거야, 죄다 상대적인 거지, 그저 보기 나름인 거라고.\""], ["he thought in a flash, but only on the top surface of his mind, while he was shuddering all over, “there, I’ve got it on!", "그는 번개처럼 생각했다, 다만 마음의 표면에서만, 온몸을 부르르 떨면서. \"자, 신었어!"], ["I have finished by getting it on!”", "결국 신고야 말았군!\""]], [["But his laughter was quickly followed by despair.", "그러나 그의 웃음은 이내 절망으로 바뀌었다."]], [["“No, it’s too much for me...”", "\"아니, 내 힘으로는 감당이 안 돼…….\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["His legs shook.", "그의 다리가 후들거렸다."], ["“From fear,”", "\"두려움 때문이지.\""], ["he muttered.", "그는 중얼거렸다."], ["His head swam and ached with fever.", "그의 머리가 빙빙 돌고 열로 지끈거렸다."], ["“It’s a trick!", "\"이건 함정이야!"], ["They want to decoy me there and confound me over everything,”", "날 거기로 꾀어내서는 모든 일을 두고 혼란에 빠뜨리려는 거지.\""], ["he mused, as he went out on to the stairs--“the worst of it is I’m almost light-headed...", "그는 계단으로 나서면서 곰곰이 생각했다. \"무엇보다 곤란한 건 내가 거의 정신이 나가 있다는 거야……."], ["I may blurt out something stupid...”", "멍청한 소리를 불쑥 내뱉을지도 몰라…….\""]], [["On the stairs he remembered that he was leaving all the things just as they were in the hole in the wall, “and very likely, it’s on purpose to search when I’m out,”", "계단에서 그는 물건들을 죄다 벽 구멍 속에 그대로 둔 채 나왔다는 것을 떠올렸다. \"그리고 십중팔구는, 내가 나간 사이에 수색하려고 일부러 그런 거겠지.\""], ["he thought, and stopped short.", "그는 생각하며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["But he was possessed by such despair, such cynicism of misery, if one may so call it, that with a wave of his hand he went on.", "그러나 그는 너무도 깊은 절망에, 굳이 그렇게 부를 수 있다면, 비참함이 빚어낸 냉소에 사로잡혀, 손을 한번 내젓고는 계속 걸어갔다."], ["“Only to get it over!”", "\"그저 끝내 버리기만 하자!\""]], [["In the street the heat was insufferable again; not a drop of rain had fallen all those days.", "거리는 또다시 견딜 수 없이 무더웠다. 그 며칠 동안 비라곤 한 방울도 내리지 않았다."], ["Again dust, bricks and mortar, again the stench from the shops and pot-houses, again the drunken men, the Finnish pedlars and half-broken-down cabs.", "또다시 먼지, 벽돌과 회반죽, 또다시 가게와 선술집에서 풍기는 악취, 또다시 술 취한 사내들, 핀란드 행상들, 그리고 다 망가져 가는 삯마차들."], ["The sun shone straight in his eyes, so that it hurt him to look out of them, and he felt his head going round--as a man in a fever is apt to feel when he comes out into the street on a bright sunny day.", "햇빛이 곧장 그의 눈을 찔러서 밖을 내다보는 것조차 고통스러웠고, 그는 머리가 핑 도는 것을 느꼈다 — 열병에 걸린 사람이 화창하게 해가 비치는 날 거리로 나섰을 때 으레 느끼게 되는 그 기분이었다."]], [["When he reached the turning into _the_ street, in an agony of trepidation he looked down it...", "그가 바로 그 거리로 꺾어 드는 모퉁이에 다다랐을 때, 그는 두려움의 고통 속에서 그 거리를 내려다보았다……"], ["at _the_ house...", "그 집을……"], ["and at once averted his eyes.", "그러고는 즉시 눈길을 돌려 버렸다."]], [["“If they question me, perhaps I’ll simply tell,”", "\"저들이 내게 캐물으면, 어쩌면 그냥 다 털어놓을지도 몰라.\""], ["he thought, as he drew near the police-station.", "그는 경찰서로 다가가며 생각했다."]], [["The police-station was about a quarter of a mile off.", "경찰서는 사 분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 있었다."], ["It had lately been moved to new rooms on the fourth floor of a new house.", "최근에 어느 새 건물 사 층의 새 사무실로 옮겨 온 참이었다."], ["He had been once for a moment in the old office but long ago.", "그는 예전 사무실에 잠깐 들렀던 적이 있었지만 그건 한참 전의 일이었다."], ["Turning in at the gateway, he saw on the right a flight of stairs which a peasant was mounting with a book in his hand.", "대문 안으로 들어서면서 그는 오른쪽에 계단이 하나 있고, 어떤 농부 하나가 손에 장부를 든 채 그 계단을 올라가는 것을 보았다."], ["“A house-porter, no doubt; so then, the office is here,”", "\"필시 건물 문지기겠지, 그렇다면 사무실은 여기야.\""], ["and he began ascending the stairs on the chance.", "그러고는 그는 어림짐작으로 계단을 오르기 시작했다."], ["He did not want to ask questions of anyone.", "그는 누구에게도 묻고 싶지 않았다."]], [["“I’ll go in, fall on my knees, and confess everything...”", "\"들어가서, 무릎을 꿇고, 모든 것을 자백해야지…….\""], ["he thought, as he reached the fourth floor.", "사 층에 다다르며 그는 생각했다."]], [["The staircase was steep, narrow and all sloppy with dirty water.", "계단은 가파르고 좁았으며, 온통 더러운 물로 질척거렸다."], ["The kitchens of the flats opened on to the stairs and stood open almost the whole day.", "셋방마다 부엌이 계단 쪽으로 나 있었고, 거의 온종일 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["So there was a fearful smell and heat.", "그래서 끔찍한 악취와 열기가 가득했다."], ["The staircase was crowded with porters going up and down with their books under their arms, policemen, and persons of all sorts and both sexes.", "계단에는 장부를 옆구리에 낀 채 오르내리는 문지기들과 경찰들, 온갖 부류의 남녀로 붐볐다. 사무실 문 역시 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["The door of the office, too, stood wide open.", "안에서는 농부들이 서서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Peasants stood waiting within. There, too, the heat was stifling and there was a sickening smell of fresh paint and stale oil from the newly decorated rooms.", "거기에도 열기가 숨 막힐 듯했고, 새로 단장한 방에서 나는 새 페인트와 묵은 기름의 역겨운 냄새가 진동했다."]], [["After waiting a little, he decided to move forward into the next room.", "잠시 기다린 뒤, 그는 다음 방으로 들어가기로 마음먹었다."], ["All the rooms were small and low-pitched.", "방들은 죄다 작고 천장이 낮았다."], ["A fearful impatience drew him on and on.", "끔찍한 조바심이 그를 자꾸만 앞으로 떠밀었다."], ["No one paid attention to him.", "아무도 그에게 주의를 기울이지 않았다."], ["In the second room some clerks sat writing, dressed hardly better than he was, and rather a queer-looking set.", "두 번째 방에는 서기 몇이 앉아서 글을 쓰고 있었는데, 그보다 별로 나을 것 없는 옷차림에다, 어쩐지 괴상해 보이는 무리였다."], ["He went up to one of them.", "그는 그들 중 하나에게 다가갔다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["He showed the notice he had received.", "그는 자기가 받은 통지를 내보였다."]], [["“You are a student?”", "\"대학생이오?\""], ["the man asked, glancing at the notice.", "사내가 통지를 흘끗 보며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, formerly a student.”", "\"네, 예전엔 대학생이었습니다.\""]], [["The clerk looked at him, but without the slightest interest.", "서기는 그를 바라보았지만, 조금의 흥미도 없었다."], ["He was a particularly unkempt person with the look of a fixed idea in his eye.", "그는 유난히 부스스한 사람으로, 눈에는 어떤 고정관념에 사로잡힌 듯한 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["“There would be no getting anything out of him, because he has no interest in anything,”", "\"이 사람한테서는 아무것도 얻어 낼 게 없겠어, 무엇에도 관심이 없으니까.\""], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."]], [["“Go in there to the head clerk,”", "\"저기 수석 서기한테 가 보시오.\""], ["said the clerk, pointing towards the furthest room.", "서기가 가장 먼 방을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["He went into that room--the fourth in order; it was a small room and packed full of people, rather better dressed than in the outer rooms.", "그는 그 방으로 들어갔다 — 순서로는 네 번째 방이었다. 작은 방인데도 사람들로 빽빽이 들어차 있었고, 바깥 방들보다는 다소 나은 옷차림이었다."], ["Among them were two ladies.", "그들 가운데 부인 둘이 있었다."], ["One, poorly dressed in mourning, sat at the table opposite the chief clerk, writing something at his dictation.", "하나는 상복 차림의 초라한 행색으로, 탁자 앞 수석 서기 맞은편에 앉아 그가 불러 주는 대로 무언가를 받아쓰고 있었다."], ["The other, a very stout, buxom woman with a purplish-red, blotchy face, excessively smartly dressed with a brooch on her bosom as big as a saucer, was standing on one side, apparently waiting for something.", "다른 하나는 몹시 살집 좋고 풍만한 여자로, 불그죽죽한 자줏빛에 얼룩덜룩한 얼굴을 하고, 가슴에 받침접시만 한 브로치를 단 지나치게 화려한 차림새로, 한쪽에 서서 무언가를 기다리는 듯했다."], ["Raskolnikov thrust his notice upon the head clerk.", "라스콜니코프는 수석 서기에게 자기 통지를 들이밀었다."], ["The latter glanced at it, said: “Wait a minute,” and went on attending to the lady in mourning.", "그는 그것을 흘끗 보고는 \"잠깐 기다리시오\"라고 말하고는 다시 상복 입은 부인에게 주의를 돌렸다."]], [["He breathed more freely.", "그는 한결 편하게 숨을 쉬었다."], ["“It can’t be that!”", "\"그 일일 리가 없어!\""]], [["By degrees he began to regain confidence, he kept urging himself to have courage and be calm.", "차츰차츰 그는 자신감을 되찾기 시작했고, 용기를 내라고, 침착하라고 끊임없이 스스로를 다그쳤다."]], [["“Some foolishness, some trifling carelessness, and I may betray myself!", "\"어떤 어리석은 짓, 사소한 부주의 하나로도 나는 나 자신을 드러내고 말지도 몰라!"], ["Hm...", "흠……"], ["it’s a pity there’s no air here,”", "여기 공기가 없는 게 유감이군.\""], ["he added, “it’s stifling....", "그는 덧붙였다. \"숨이 막혀……."], ["It makes one’s head dizzier than ever...", "머리가 어느 때보다 더 어지러워지고……"], ["and one’s mind too...”", "정신도 마찬가지야…….\""]], [["He was conscious of a terrible inner turmoil.", "그는 끔찍한 내면의 동요를 의식하고 있었다."], ["He was afraid of losing his self-control; he tried to catch at something and fix his mind on it, something quite irrelevant, but he could not succeed in this at all.", "그는 자제력을 잃을까 두려웠다. 무언가를 붙잡아 거기에 정신을 붙들어 매려 애썼다, 전혀 무관한 무언가를."], ["Yet the head clerk greatly interested him, he kept hoping to see through him and guess something from his face.", "그러나 그것은 도무지 되지 않았다. 그래도 수석 서기가 그의 관심을 크게 끌었고, 그는 그를 꿰뚫어 보고 그의 얼굴에서 무언가를 알아채기를 줄곧 바랐다."]], [["He was a very young man, about two and twenty, with a dark mobile face that looked older than his years.", "그는 스물두 살쯤 된 아주 젊은 사내로, 가무잡잡하고 변화무쌍한 얼굴은 나이보다 늙어 보였다."], ["He was fashionably dressed and foppish, with his hair parted in the middle, well combed and pomaded, and wore a number of rings on his well-scrubbed fingers and a gold chain on his waistcoat.", "그는 멋쟁이답게 세련되게 차려입고서, 머리를 한가운데로 가르고 곱게 빗어 포마드를 발랐으며, 깨끗이 씻은 손가락마다 여러 개의 반지를 끼고, 조끼에는 금줄을 늘어뜨리고 있었다."], ["He said a couple of words in French to a foreigner who was in the room, and said them fairly correctly.", "그는 방 안에 있던 어느 외국인에게 프랑스어로 두어 마디를 건넸는데, 제법 정확하게 말했다."]], [["“Luise Ivanovna, you can sit down,”", "\"루이자 이바노브나, 앉으셔도 됩니다.\""], ["he said casually to the gaily-dressed, purple-faced lady, who was still standing as though not venturing to sit down, though there was a chair beside her.", "그는 화려하게 차려입은 자줏빛 얼굴의 부인에게 무심히 말했는데, 그녀는 곁에 의자가 있는데도 감히 앉지 못하겠다는 듯 여전히 서 있었다."]], [["“Ich danke,”", "\"이히 단케.*\""], ["said the latter, and softly, with a rustle of silk she sank into the chair.", "그녀가 말하고는, 비단 옷이 사락거리는 소리와 함께 사뿐히 의자에 내려앉았다."], ["Her light blue dress trimmed with white lace floated about the table like an air-balloon and filled almost half the room.", "흰 레이스로 장식한 그녀의 연푸른 드레스가 기구처럼 탁자 둘레로 부풀어 올라 방의 거의 절반을 채웠다. 그녀에게서는 향수 냄새가 났다."], ["She smelt of scent.", "하지만 그녀는 방의 절반을 차지하고 그토록 향수 냄새를 강하게 풍기는 것이 분명 거북한 듯했다."], ["But she was obviously embarrassed at filling half the room and smelling so strongly of scent; and though her smile was impudent as well as cringing, it betrayed evident uneasiness.", "그녀의 미소는 뻔뻔스러우면서도 굽실거리는 것이었으나, 역력한 불안을 드러내고 있었다. * 이히 단케(Ich danke): 독일어로 \"고맙습니다.\""]], [["The lady in mourning had done at last, and got up.", "상복 입은 부인이 마침내 일을 마치고 일어섰다."], ["All at once, with some noise, an officer walked in very jauntily, with a peculiar swing of his shoulders at each step.", "갑자기 무언가 소란스러운 소리와 함께 한 장교가 걸음마다 어깨를 묘하게 흔들면서 무척이나 경쾌하게 걸어 들어왔다."], ["He tossed his cockaded cap on the table and sat down in an easy-chair.", "그는 모표 달린 모자를 탁자 위로 휙 던지고는 안락의자에 앉았다."], ["The small lady positively skipped from her seat on seeing him, and fell to curtsying in a sort of ecstasy; but the officer took not the smallest notice of her, and she did not venture to sit down again in his presence.", "그 자그마한 부인은 그를 보자마자 자리에서 펄쩍 뛰어 일어나 일종의 황홀경에 빠진 듯 굽실굽실 절을 해 댔다. 그러나 장교는 그녀에게 조금의 주의도 기울이지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 있는 앞에서는 감히 다시 앉지 못했다."], ["He was the assistant superintendent.", "그는 부서장 보좌관이었다."], ["He had a reddish moustache that stood out horizontally on each side of his face, and extremely small features, expressive of nothing much except a certain insolence.", "그는 얼굴 양옆으로 수평으로 뻗친 불그스름한 콧수염을 기르고 있었고, 이목구비는 지극히 작아서 어떤 거만함 말고는 별다른 것을 드러내지 않았다."], ["He looked askance and rather indignantly at Raskolnikov; he was so very badly dressed, and in spite of his humiliating position, his bearing was by no means in keeping with his clothes.", "그는 라스콜니코프를 곁눈으로, 다소 못마땅하게 바라보았다. 라스콜니코프의 옷차림이 너무도 형편없는 데다, 그 굴욕적인 처지에도 불구하고 그의 태도가 결코 옷차림과 어울리지 않았던 것이다."], ["Raskolnikov had unwarily fixed a very long and direct look on him, so that he felt positively affronted.", "라스콜니코프가 무심결에 그에게 아주 길고 똑바른 시선을 박았던 탓에, 그는 그야말로 모욕을 당한 기분이 들었다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["he shouted, apparently astonished that such a ragged fellow was not annihilated by the majesty of his glance.", "그가 소리쳤는데, 자기 시선의 위엄으로도 저런 누더기 차림의 사내가 박살 나지 않은 것이 자못 놀랍다는 투였다."]], [["“I was summoned...", "\"통지를 받고……"], ["by a notice...”", "출두했습니다…….\""], ["Raskolnikov faltered.", "라스콜니코프가 더듬거렸다."]], [["“For the recovery of money due, from _the student_,”", "\"밀린 빚을 받으려는 건이지요, 그 대학생한테서.\""], ["the head clerk interfered hurriedly, tearing himself from his papers.", "수석 서기가 서류에서 몸을 떼면서 황급히 끼어들었다. \"여기요!\""], ["“Here!” and he flung Raskolnikov a document and pointed out the place.", "그러고는 라스콜니코프에게 문서 한 장을 던지며 그 자리를 가리켰다."], ["“Read that!”", "\"이걸 읽어 보시오!\""]], [["“Money?", "\"돈?"], ["What money?”", "무슨 돈?\""], ["thought Raskolnikov, “but...", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다. \"하지만……"], ["then...", "그렇다면……"], ["it’s certainly not _that_.”", "분명 그 일은 아니로군.\""]], [["And he trembled with joy.", "그러고는 그는 기쁨에 떨었다."], ["He felt sudden intense indescribable relief.", "그는 갑작스럽고 강렬한, 형언할 수 없는 안도를 느꼈다."], ["A load was lifted from his back.", "등에서 무거운 짐이 내려졌다."]], [["“And pray, what time were you directed to appear, sir?”", "\"그래, 도대체 몇 시까지 출두하라고 지시받았소, 선생?\""], ["shouted the assistant superintendent, seeming for some unknown reason more and more aggrieved.", "부서장 보좌관이 무슨 영문인지 점점 더 못마땅해하는 기색으로 소리쳤다."], ["“You are told to come at nine, and now it’s twelve!”", "\"아홉 시까지 오라고 했는데, 지금이 열두 시잖소!\""]], [["“The notice was only brought me a quarter of an hour ago,”", "\"통지를 십오 분 전에야 받았습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered loudly over his shoulder.", "라스콜니코프가 어깨 너머로 큰 소리로 대꾸했다."], ["To his own surprise he, too, grew suddenly angry and found a certain pleasure in it.", "자기도 놀랍게, 그 역시 갑자기 화가 치밀었고, 거기서 어떤 쾌감마저 느꼈다."], ["“And it’s enough that I have come here ill with fever.”", "\"게다가 열병으로 앓는 몸을 끌고 여기 온 것만 해도 충분하지 않습니까.\""]], [["“Kindly refrain from shouting!”", "\"제발 소리 좀 지르지 마시오!\""]], [["“I’m not shouting, I’m speaking very quietly, it’s you who are shouting at me.", "\"전 소리 지르는 게 아니라 아주 차분하게 말하고 있습니다, 도리어 당신이 제게 소리를 지르고 있지요."], ["I’m a student, and allow no one to shout at me.”", "전 대학생이고, 누구도 제게 소리 지르는 걸 용납하지 않습니다.\""]], [["The assistant superintendent was so furious that for the first minute he could only splutter inarticulately.", "부서장 보좌관은 어찌나 격분했는지 처음 한순간은 알아들을 수 없는 소리만 푸푸 내뱉을 뿐이었다."], ["He leaped up from his seat.", "그가 자리에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Be silent!", "\"조용히 하시오!"], ["You are in a government office.", "여긴 관청이오."], ["Don’t be impudent, sir!”", "무례하게 굴지 마시오, 선생!\""]], [["“You’re in a government office, too,”", "\"당신도 관청에 있는 거지요.\""], ["cried Raskolnikov, “and you’re smoking a cigarette as well as shouting, so you are showing disrespect to all of us.”", "라스콜니코프가 외쳤다. \"게다가 당신은 소리를 지르면서 담배까지 피우고 있으니, 우리 모두에게 무례를 범하고 있는 거 아닙니까.\""]], [["He felt an indescribable satisfaction at having said this.", "이렇게 말하고 나니 그는 형언할 수 없는 만족감을 느꼈다."]], [["The head clerk looked at him with a smile.", "수석 서기가 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보았다."], ["The angry assistant superintendent was obviously disconcerted.", "성난 부서장 보좌관은 분명 당황한 기색이었다."]], [["“That’s not your business!”", "\"그건 당신이 상관할 일이 아니오!\""], ["he shouted at last with unnatural loudness.", "그가 마침내 부자연스러울 만큼 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Kindly make the declaration demanded of you.", "\"당신에게 요구된 진술서나 작성하시오."], ["Show him. Alexandr Grigorievitch.", "그에게 보여 주게, 알렉산드르 그리고리예비치."], ["There is a complaint against you!", "당신에 대한 고소가 들어왔소!"], ["You don’t pay your debts!", "당신은 빚을 갚지 않고 있소!"], ["You’re a fine bird!”", "참 잘난 양반이군!\""]], [["But Raskolnikov was not listening now; he had eagerly clutched at the paper, in haste to find an explanation.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 이제 듣고 있지 않았다. 그는 다급히 설명을 찾으려고 그 종이를 열렬히 거머쥐었다."], ["He read it once, and a second time, and still did not understand.", "그는 한 번 읽고, 두 번째로 읽었으나, 여전히 이해하지 못했다."]], [["“What is this?”", "\"이게 뭡니까?\""], ["he asked the head clerk.", "그가 수석 서기에게 물었다."]], [["“It is for the recovery of money on an I O U, a writ.", "\"차용증에 따른 채무 변제 청구, 그러니까 영장이오."], ["You must either pay it, with all expenses, costs and so on, or give a written declaration when you can pay it, and at the same time an undertaking not to leave the capital without payment, and nor to sell or conceal your property.", "당신은 비용이며 소송 경비 등 일체와 함께 그것을 변제하든지, 아니면 언제 갚을 수 있는지 서면으로 진술하고, 동시에 변제 전까지 수도를 떠나지 않으며 재산을 매각하거나 은닉하지 않겠다는 서약서를 제출해야 하오."], ["The creditor is at liberty to sell your property, and proceed against you according to the law.”", "채권자는 당신의 재산을 매각하고 법에 따라 당신을 상대로 소송을 제기할 자유가 있소.\""]], [["“But I...", "\"하지만 전……"], ["am not in debt to anyone!”", "누구에게도 빚을 지지 않았습니다!\""]], [["“That’s not our business.", "\"그건 우리가 알 바 아니오."], ["Here, an I O U for a hundred and fifteen roubles, legally attested, and due for payment, has been brought us for recovery, given by you to the widow of the assessor Zarnitsyn, nine months ago, and paid over by the widow Zarnitsyn to one Mr.", "자, 법적으로 공증되고 변제 기일이 지난 백십오 루블짜리 차용증이 변제 청구를 위해 우리에게 접수됐는데, 이건 당신이 구 개월 전에 평정관 자르니친의 미망인에게 써 준 것이고, 미망인 자르니친이 어떤 체바로프 씨에게 넘긴 것이오."], ["Tchebarov. We therefore summon you, hereupon.”", "그래서 우리가 이 건으로 당신을 소환한 거요.\""]], [["“But she is my landlady!”", "\"하지만 그분은 제 집주인입니다!\""]], [["“And what if she is your landlady?”", "\"집주인이면 또 어떻소?\""]], [["The head clerk looked at him with a condescending smile of compassion, and at the same time with a certain triumph, as at a novice under fire for the first time--as though he would say: “Well, how do you feel now?”", "수석 서기는 너그러운 연민의 미소를 띠고, 동시에 어떤 승리감마저 어린 표정으로 그를 바라보았는데, 마치 처음으로 포화 속에 놓인 신참을 보듯, \"자, 이제 기분이 어떤가?\" 하고 말하는 듯했다."], ["But what did he care now for an I O U, for a writ of recovery!", "그러나 이제 그가 차용증이며 변제 영장 따위에 무슨 관심이 있겠는가!"], ["Was that worth worrying about now, was it worth attention even!", "그게 지금 걱정할 만한 일이던가, 주의를 기울일 가치라도 있던가!"], ["He stood, he read, he listened, he answered, he even asked questions himself, but all mechanically.", "그는 서 있었고, 읽었고, 들었고, 대답했고, 심지어 스스로 질문까지 던졌지만, 그 모든 것이 기계적이었다."], ["The triumphant sense of security, of deliverance from overwhelming danger, that was what filled his whole soul that moment without thought for the future, without analysis, without suppositions or surmises, without doubts and without questioning.", "안전하다는, 압도적인 위험에서 벗어났다는 의기양양한 감각, 바로 그것이 그 순간 그의 온 영혼을 가득 채웠다, 미래에 대한 생각도 없이, 분석도 없이, 가정이나 추측도 없이, 의심도 없이, 자문도 없이."], ["It was an instant of full, direct, purely instinctive joy.", "그것은 충만하고 직접적이며 순수하게 본능적인 기쁨의 한순간이었다."], ["But at that very moment something like a thunderstorm took place in the office.", "그러나 바로 그 순간, 사무실에서 마치 뇌우 같은 일이 벌어졌다."], ["The assistant superintendent, still shaken by Raskolnikov’s disrespect, still fuming and obviously anxious to keep up his wounded dignity, pounced on the unfortunate smart lady, who had been gazing at him ever since he came in with an exceedingly silly smile.", "라스콜니코프의 무례함에 여전히 부르르 떨며, 여전히 씩씩대면서 상처 입은 자존심을 지키려고 안달하던 부서장 보좌관이, 그가 들어선 뒤로 줄곧 더없이 멍청한 미소를 띤 채 그를 바라보고 있던 그 가련한 멋쟁이 부인에게 달려들었던 것이다."]], [["“You shameful hussy!”", "\"이 파렴치한 계집!\""], ["he shouted suddenly at the top of his voice.", "그가 갑자기 목청껏 소리쳤다."], ["(The lady in mourning had left the office.) “What was going on at your house last night?", "(상복 입은 부인은 이미 사무실을 떠난 뒤였다.) \"어젯밤 네 집에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌어?"], ["Eh!", "엉!"], ["A disgrace again, you’re a scandal to the whole street.", "또 추태를 부렸지, 넌 온 거리의 골칫거리야."], ["Fighting and drinking again.", "또 싸움질에 술판이지."], ["Do you want the house of correction?", "감화원에 들어가고 싶어?"], ["Why, I have warned you ten times over that I would not let you off the eleventh!", "아니, 내가 열 번이나 경고했잖아, 열한 번째는 그냥 넘어가지 않겠다고!"], ["And here you are again, again, you...", "그런데 또, 또 이러는군, 네가……"], ["you...!”", "네가……!\""]], [["The paper fell out of Raskolnikov’s hands, and he looked wildly at the smart lady who was so unceremoniously treated.", "라스콜니코프의 손에서 종이가 떨어졌고, 그는 그토록 무례하게 다뤄지는 멋쟁이 부인을 멍하니 바라보았다."], ["But he soon saw what it meant, and at once began to find positive amusement in the scandal.", "그러나 곧 그는 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알아차렸고, 즉시 그 소란에서 진짜 재미를 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He listened with pleasure, so that he longed to laugh and laugh...", "그는 즐겁게 귀를 기울였고, 어찌나 웃고 또 웃고 싶었는지 모른다……"], ["all his nerves were on edge.", "그의 신경이 죄다 곤두서 있었다."]], [["“Ilya Petrovitch!”", "\"일리야 페트로비치!\""], ["the head clerk was beginning anxiously, but stopped short, for he knew from experience that the enraged assistant could not be stopped except by force.", "수석 서기가 걱정스럽게 입을 떼다가, 도중에 멈추었다. 격분한 보좌관은 힘으로 막지 않고서는 멈출 수 없다는 것을 경험으로 알았기 때문이다."]], [["As for the smart lady, at first she positively trembled before the storm.", "멋쟁이 부인으로 말하자면, 처음에는 그 폭풍 앞에서 그야말로 벌벌 떨었다."], ["But, strange to say, the more numerous and violent the terms of abuse became, the more amiable she looked, and the more seductive the smiles she lavished on the terrible assistant.", "그러나 이상하게도, 욕설이 늘어나고 거세질수록 그녀의 표정은 더욱 상냥해졌고, 그 무서운 보좌관에게 쏟아붓는 미소는 더욱 유혹적으로 변해 갔다."], ["She moved uneasily, and curtsied incessantly, waiting impatiently for a chance of putting in her word: and at last she found it.", "그녀는 안절부절못하며 끊임없이 굽실거렸고, 자기 말을 끼워 넣을 기회를 초조하게 기다렸다. 그리고 마침내 그 기회를 잡았다."]], [["“There was no sort of noise or fighting in my house, Mr.", "\"제 집에서는 어떤 소란도 싸움도 없었어요, 대장님.\""], ["Captain,” she pattered all at once, like peas dropping, speaking Russian confidently, though with a strong German accent, “and no sort of scandal, and his honour came drunk, and it’s the whole truth I am telling, Mr.", "그녀가 갑자기 콩알 굴러가듯 또르르 지껄이기 시작했는데, 러시아어를 자신만만하게 구사하긴 했으나 독일어 억양이 강했다."], ["Captain, and I am not to blame.... Mine is an honourable house, Mr.", "\"어떤 추태도 없었고, 그분이 취해서 왔던 거예요, 이게 다 진실이에요, 대장님, 전 잘못이 없어요……."], ["Captain, and honourable behaviour, Mr. Captain, and I always, always dislike any scandal myself.", "제 집은 점잖은 집이에요, 대장님, 점잖게 처신하는 집이고요, 대장님, 그리고 저는 늘, 늘 소란 같은 건 질색이라고요."], ["But he came quite tipsy, and asked for three bottles again, and then he lifted up one leg, and began playing the pianoforte with one foot, and that is not at all right in an honourable house, and he _ganz_ broke the piano, and it was very bad manners indeed and I said so.", "그런데 그분이 잔뜩 취해서 와서는 또 세 병을 달라고 하더니, 그러고 나서 한쪽 다리를 들어 올려서는 한 발로 피아노를 치기 시작했어요, 점잖은 집에서 그건 도무지 옳지 않은 짓이잖아요, 그리고 피아노를 완전히 간츠* 부숴 버렸어요, 정말이지 너무도 무례한 짓이라 제가 그렇게 말했죠. 그랬더니 그분이 병 하나를 집어 들고 모두를 그걸로 때리기 시작했어요."], ["And he took up a bottle and began hitting everyone with it.", "그래서 제가 문지기를 불렀고, 카를이 왔는데, 그분이 카를을 붙잡아 눈을 쳤어요. 헨리에테의 눈도 때렸고요, 제 뺨도 다섯 대나 갈겼어요."], ["And then I called the porter, and Karl came, and he took Karl and hit him in the eye; and he hit Henriette in the eye, too, and gave me five slaps on the cheek.", "점잖은 집에서 그건 너무도 신사답지 못한 짓이었어요, 대장님, 그래서 제가 비명을 질렀죠. 그러자 그분이 운하 쪽으로 난 창문을 열고, 창가에 서서, 새끼 돼지처럼 꽥꽥거렸어요."], ["And it was so ungentlemanly in an honourable house, Mr.", "정말 망신스러웠죠. 거리로 난 창가에서 새끼 돼지처럼 꽥꽥거리다니, 원!"], ["Captain, and I screamed.", "망측해라!"], ["And he opened the window over the canal, and stood in the window, squealing like a little pig; it was a disgrace.", "그래서 카를이 그분의 외투를 잡아 창가에서 끌어 내렸는데, 정말이에요, 대장님, 그분이 자인 로크,* 그러니까 자기 외투를 찢어 버렸어요."], ["The idea of squealing like a little pig at the window into the street! Fie upon him!", "그러고는 그분이 손해 배상으로 만 무스,* 그러니까 자기한테 십오 루블을 물어내야 한다고 소리쳤어요."], ["And Karl pulled him away from the window by his coat, and it is true, Mr.", "그래서 제가 물어 줬어요, 대장님, 자인 로크 값으로 오 루블을요."], ["Captain, he tore _sein rock_.", "그분은 신사답지 못한 손님이고 이 모든 소란을 일으킨 장본인이에요."], ["And then he shouted that _man muss_ pay him fifteen roubles damages. And I did pay him, Mr.", "'내가 당신 본때를 보여 주겠어, 신문이란 신문에 죄다 당신 얘길 쓸 수 있으니까'라고 그분이 말했다고요.\""], ["Captain, five roubles for _sein rock_. And he is an ungentlemanly visitor and caused all the scandal.", "* 간츠(ganz), 자인 로크(sein Rock), 만 무스(man muss): 각각 독일어로 \"완전히\", \"자기 외투\", \"사람은 ~해야 한다\"는 뜻."], ["‘I will show you up,’ he said, ‘for I can write to all the papers about you.’”", "화자가 러시아어에 독일어를 섞어 말하고 있다."]], [["“Then he was an author?”", "\"그럼 그자가 작가였단 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, Mr. Captain, and what an ungentlemanly visitor in an honourable house....”", "\"네, 대장님, 그런데 점잖은 집에서 그렇게 신사답지 못한 손님이라니…….\""]], [["“Now then!", "\"자, 됐소!"], ["Enough!", "그만하면 충분해!"], ["I have told you already...”", "내가 이미 말했잖소…….\""]], [["“Ilya Petrovitch!”", "\"일리야 페트로비치!\""], ["the head clerk repeated significantly.", "수석 서기가 의미심장하게 거듭 불렀다."]], [["The assistant glanced rapidly at him; the head clerk slightly shook his head.", "보좌관이 그를 재빨리 흘끗 보았다. 수석 서기가 가볍게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“... So I tell you this, most respectable Luise Ivanovna, and I tell it you for the last time,”", "\"……그러니 내가 이 말을 하지, 더없이 존경스러운 루이자 이바노브나, 그리고 이건 마지막으로 하는 말이오.\""], ["the assistant went on.", "보좌관이 말을 이었다."], ["“If there is a scandal in your honourable house once again, I will put you yourself in the lock-up, as it is called in polite society.", "\"당신의 그 점잖은 집에서 또 한 번 소란이 벌어지면, 점잖게 말해서 당신을 직접 유치장에 처넣겠소."], ["Do you hear?", "알아들었소?"], ["So a literary man, an author took five roubles for his coat-tail in an ‘honourable house’?", "그래, 문필가라는 작자가, 작가라는 자가 '점잖은 집'에서 자기 외투 자락 값으로 오 루블을 받아 챙겼다?"], ["A nice set, these authors!”", "참 잘난 족속들이야, 이 작가라는 것들은!\""]], [["And he cast a contemptuous glance at Raskolnikov.", "그러고는 그는 라스콜니코프에게 경멸 어린 눈길을 던졌다."], ["“There was a scandal the other day in a restaurant, too.", "\"요전에도 어느 식당에서 소란이 있었지."], ["An author had eaten his dinner and would not pay; ‘I’ll write a satire on you,’ says he.", "작가라는 자가 저녁을 먹고는 돈을 안 내려 하더래. '당신을 풍자하는 글을 써 주겠어'라고 했다지."], ["And there was another of them on a steamer last week used the most disgraceful language to the respectable family of a civil councillor, his wife and daughter.", "또 지난주에는 그런 작자 하나가 증기선에서 어느 점잖은 5등 문관 가족한테, 그 부인이며 딸한테 입에 담지 못할 욕을 퍼부었어."], ["And there was one of them turned out of a confectioner’s shop the other day.", "또 요전에는 그런 작자 하나가 어느 과자점에서 쫓겨났고."], ["They are like that, authors, literary men, students, town-criers....", "다들 그런 식이라니까, 작가니, 문필가니, 대학생이니, 거리의 떠벌이들이……."], ["Pfoo!", "풰!"], ["You get along!", "가 보시오!"], ["I shall look in upon you myself one day.", "내가 직접 당신네 집을 한번 들여다보겠소."], ["Then you had better be careful!", "그땐 조심하는 게 좋을 거요!"], ["Do you hear?”", "알아들었소?\""]], [["With hurried deference, Luise Ivanovna fell to curtsying in all directions, and so curtsied herself to the door.", "루이자 이바노브나는 황급히 굽실거리며 사방에 절을 해 댔고, 그렇게 절을 하면서 문 쪽으로 물러갔다."], ["But at the door, she stumbled backwards against a good-looking officer with a fresh, open face and splendid thick fair whiskers.", "그러나 문간에서 그녀는 뒷걸음치다가, 싱싱하고 시원한 얼굴에 멋들어지게 숱 많은 금빛 구레나룻을 기른 잘생긴 장교와 부딪혔다."], ["This was the superintendent of the district himself, Nikodim Fomitch.", "이 사람이 바로 그 관할 부서장, 니코딤 포미치였다."], ["Luise Ivanovna made haste to curtsy almost to the ground, and with mincing little steps, she fluttered out of the office.", "루이자 이바노브나는 허둥지둥 거의 땅에 닿도록 절을 하고는, 종종걸음으로 사무실에서 팔랑팔랑 빠져나갔다."]], [["“Again thunder and lightning--a hurricane!”", "\"또 천둥에 번개로군 — 태풍이야!\""], ["said Nikodim Fomitch to Ilya Petrovitch in a civil and friendly tone.", "니코딤 포미치가 일리야 페트로비치에게 점잖고 다정한 어조로 말했다."], ["“You are aroused again, you are fuming again!", "\"자넨 또 흥분했군, 또 씩씩대고 있어!"], ["I heard it on the stairs!”", "계단에서부터 다 들리던걸!\""]], [["“Well, what then!”", "\"흥, 그러면 또 어떤가!\""], ["Ilya Petrovitch drawled with gentlemanly nonchalance; and he walked with some papers to another table, with a jaunty swing of his shoulders at each step.", "일리야 페트로비치가 신사다운 무심함으로 느릿느릿 내뱉고는, 서류 몇 장을 들고 다른 탁자로, 걸음마다 어깨를 경쾌하게 흔들면서 걸어갔다."], ["“Here, if you will kindly look: an author, or a student, has been one at least, does not pay his debts, has given an I O U, won’t clear out of his room, and complaints are constantly being lodged against him, and here he has been pleased to make a protest against my smoking in his presence!", "\"여기, 한번 봐 주게나. 작가인지, 적어도 한때는 대학생이었다는 자가, 빚을 갚지 않고, 차용증까지 써 줘 놓고, 방에서 나가려 하지도 않아서, 끊임없이 고소가 들어오는 판인데, 게다가 이자가 자기 앞에서 내가 담배 피우는 것에 황송하게도 항의까지 하셨다네!"], ["He behaves like a cad himself, and just look at him, please.", "자기가 막돼먹은 놈처럼 굴면서, 자, 이자를 좀 보게나."], ["Here’s the gentleman, and very attractive he is!”", "여기 이 신사 양반이야, 아주 매력적이지!\""]], [["“Poverty is not a vice, my friend, but we know you go off like powder, you can’t bear a slight, I daresay you took offence at something and went too far yourself,”", "\"가난은 죄가 아니라네, 친구. 하지만 자네가 화약처럼 폭발하는 거야 우리도 다 알지, 자넨 모욕을 못 견디니까."], ["continued Nikodim Fomitch, turning affably to Raskolnikov.", "짐작건대 무언가에 발끈해서는 자네 쪽에서도 도가 지나쳤을 게야.\""], ["“But you were wrong there; he is a capital fellow, I assure you, but explosive, explosive!", "니코딤 포미치가 라스콜니코프를 향해 상냥하게 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"하지만 자네가 거기서 잘못한 거라네."], ["He gets hot, fires up, boils over, and no stopping him!", "단언컨대 이 사람은 더없이 훌륭한 친구지만, 다혈질이야, 다혈질이라고! 욱하고, 발끈하고, 펄펄 끓어오르면 아무도 못 말려!"], ["And then it’s all over!", "그러고 나면 다 끝나는 거지!"], ["And at the bottom he’s a heart of gold!", "그리고 속을 들여다보면 황금 같은 마음씨를 지녔다네!"], ["His nickname in the regiment was the Explosive Lieutenant....”", "연대에서 별명이 '화약 중위'였지…….\""]], [["“And what a regiment it was, too,”", "\"그런데 그 연대 또한 어떤 곳이었는데!\""], ["cried Ilya Petrovitch, much gratified at this agreeable banter, though still sulky.", "일리야 페트로비치가 외쳤는데, 이 유쾌한 농담에 무척 흡족해하면서도 여전히 뾰로통한 기색이었다."]], [["Raskolnikov had a sudden desire to say something exceptionally pleasant to them all.", "라스콜니코프는 문득 그들 모두에게 유달리 기분 좋은 무언가를 말하고 싶은 마음이 들었다."], ["“Excuse me, Captain,”", "\"죄송합니다, 대장님.\""], ["he began easily, suddenly addressing Nikodim Fomitch, “will you enter into my position?...", "그가 느닷없이 니코딤 포미치를 향해 가볍게 입을 열었다. \"제 처지를 좀 헤아려 주시겠습니까?……"], ["I am ready to ask pardon, if I have been ill-mannered.", "제가 무례했다면 기꺼이 용서를 빌겠습니다."], ["I am a poor student, sick and shattered (shattered was the word he used) by poverty.", "저는 가난한 대학생으로, 병들고 가난에 짓눌려 만신창이가 됐습니다(만신창이라는 말을 그는 썼다)."], ["I am not studying, because I cannot keep myself now, but I shall get money....", "지금은 제 한 몸 건사할 수 없어 학업도 중단했지만, 곧 돈이 들어올 겁니다……."], ["I have a mother and sister in the province of X.", "X 도에 어머니와 누이가 있습니다."], ["They will send it to me, and I will pay.", "그들이 돈을 보내 줄 거고, 그러면 갚겠습니다."], ["My landlady is a good-hearted woman, but she is so exasperated at my having lost my lessons, and not paying her for the last four months, that she does not even send up my dinner...", "제 집주인은 마음씨 좋은 여자지만, 제가 과외 자리를 잃은 데다 넉 달째 방세를 못 내고 있는 것에 어찌나 화가 났는지, 이젠 제 밥상조차 올려 보내 주지 않습니다……. 그리고 저는 이 차용증이라는 게 도무지 이해가 안 됩니다."], ["and I don’t understand this I O U at all. She is asking me to pay her on this I O U.", "그녀가 이 차용증으로 제게 갚으라고 요구하고 있는 건데, 제가 어떻게 갚는단 말입니까?"], ["How am I to pay her? Judge for yourselves!...”", "여러분이 한번 판단해 보십시오!……\""]], [["“But that is not our business, you know,”", "\"하지만 그건 우리가 알 바 아니잖소.\""], ["the head clerk was observing.", "수석 서기가 한마디 했다."]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"네, 네."], ["I perfectly agree with you.", "전적으로 동의합니다."], ["But allow me to explain...”", "하지만 설명을 좀 드리게 해 주십시오…….\""], ["Raskolnikov put in again, still addressing Nikodim Fomitch, but trying his best to address Ilya Petrovitch also, though the latter persistently appeared to be rummaging among his papers and to be contemptuously oblivious of him.", "라스콜니코프가 다시 끼어들었는데, 여전히 니코딤 포미치를 향해 말하면서도 일리야 페트로비치에게도 한껏 말을 건네려 애썼다. 비록 후자는 줄곧 서류를 뒤적이며 그를 경멸하듯 안중에도 두지 않는 척했지만 말이다."], ["“Allow me to explain that I have been living with her for nearly three years and at first...", "\"설명을 좀 드리자면, 저는 거의 삼 년 가까이 그녀와 함께 살았고, 처음엔……"], ["at first...", "처음엔……"], ["for why should I not confess it, at the very beginning I promised to marry her daughter, it was a verbal promise, freely given...", "뭐, 고백 못 할 것도 없지요, 맨 처음에 저는 그녀의 딸과 결혼하겠다고 약속했었습니다. 말로 한 약속이었고, 자발적으로 한 거였지요……"], ["she was a girl...", "그녀는 처녀였고……"], ["indeed, I liked her, though I was not in love with her...", "사실 저는 그녀가 마음에 들긴 했지만, 사랑에 빠진 건 아니었어요……"], ["a youthful affair in fact...", "한마디로 젊은 날의 치기였지요……"], ["that is, I mean to say, that my landlady gave me credit freely in those days, and I led a life of...", "그러니까, 제 말은, 그 시절에 집주인이 제게 외상을 후하게 줬다는 거고, 저는 그런 생활을……"], ["I was very heedless...”", "저는 무척 경솔했습니다…….\""]], [["“Nobody asks you for these personal details, sir, we’ve no time to waste,”", "\"누가 당신더러 그런 사사로운 얘기를 늘어놓으라고 했소, 선생, 우리는 허비할 시간이 없소.\""], ["Ilya Petrovitch interposed roughly and with a note of triumph; but Raskolnikov stopped him hotly, though he suddenly found it exceedingly difficult to speak.", "일리야 페트로비치가 거칠게, 그리고 어딘가 의기양양한 어조로 가로막았다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 그를 격하게 제지했다, 비록 갑자기 말하기가 몹시 힘겨워졌지만."]], [["“But excuse me, excuse me.", "\"하지만 죄송합니다, 죄송하지만."], ["It is for me to explain...", "제가 설명해야 할 일입니다……"], ["how it all happened...", "이 모든 게 어떻게 된 일인지를……"], ["In my turn...", "제 차례니까요……"], ["though I agree with you...", "저도 당신 말에 동의하긴 합니다만……"], ["it is unnecessary.", "그게 불필요한 일이라는 데에요."], ["But a year ago, the girl died of typhus.", "하지만 일 년 전에, 그 처녀가 티푸스로 죽었습니다."], ["I remained lodging there as before, and when my landlady moved into her present quarters, she said to me...", "저는 전과 다름없이 거기에 세 들어 살았고, 집주인이 지금의 거처로 옮겼을 때, 그녀가 제게 말했습니다……"], ["and in a friendly way...", "그것도 다정하게……"], ["that she had complete trust in me, but still, would I not give her an I O U for one hundred and fifteen roubles, all the debt I owed her.", "자기는 저를 완전히 믿지만, 그래도 자기한테 진 빚 전부, 백십오 루블에 대해 차용증을 하나 써 주지 않겠느냐고요."], ["She said if only I gave her that, she would trust me again, as much as I liked, and that she would never, never--those were her own words--make use of that I O U till I could pay of myself...", "그것만 써 주면 다시 얼마든지 저를 믿어 주겠다고, 그리고 절대로, 절대로 — 이게 그녀가 한 말 그대로입니다 — 제가 스스로 갚을 수 있을 때까지는 그 차용증을 쓰지 않겠다고 했습니다……"], ["and now, when I have lost my lessons and have nothing to eat, she takes action against me.", "그런데 지금, 제가 과외 자리를 잃고 먹을 것조차 없는 마당에, 그녀가 저를 상대로 소송을 거는 겁니다."], ["What am I to say to that?”", "이걸 두고 제가 무슨 말을 하겠습니까?\""]], [["“All these affecting details are no business of ours.”", "\"그 모든 가슴 아픈 사연이야 우리가 알 바 아니오.\""], ["Ilya Petrovitch interrupted rudely.", "일리야 페트로비치가 무례하게 가로막았다."], ["“You must give a written undertaking but as for your love affairs and all these tragic events, we have nothing to do with that.”", "\"당신은 서면 서약서를 제출해야 하오. 하지만 당신의 연애 사건이며 이 모든 비극적인 일들로 말할 것 같으면, 우리하고는 아무 상관 없는 일이오.\""]], [["“Come now...", "\"이봐, 자네……"], ["you are harsh,”", "너무 박정하군.\""], ["muttered Nikodim Fomitch, sitting down at the table and also beginning to write.", "니코딤 포미치가 탁자에 앉아 자기도 무언가를 쓰기 시작하면서 중얼거렸다."], ["He looked a little ashamed.", "그는 다소 멋쩍은 기색이었다."]], [["“Write!”", "\"쓰시오!\""], ["said the head clerk to Raskolnikov.", "수석 서기가 라스콜니코프에게 말했다."]], [["“Write what?”", "\"뭘 쓰란 말입니까?\""], ["the latter asked, gruffly.", "그가 무뚝뚝하게 물었다."]], [["“I will dictate to you.”", "\"내가 불러 주겠소.\""]], [["Raskolnikov fancied that the head clerk treated him more casually and contemptuously after his speech, but strange to say he suddenly felt completely indifferent to anyone’s opinion, and this revulsion took place in a flash, in one instant.", "라스콜니코프는 수석 서기가 그 연설을 듣고 난 뒤로 자기를 더 무심하고 경멸적으로 대한다고 느꼈지만, 이상하게도 그는 문득 누구의 평가에도 완전히 무관심해졌다. 그리고 이 반전은 번개처럼, 한순간에 일어났다."], ["If he had cared to think a little, he would have been amazed indeed that he could have talked to them like that a minute before, forcing his feelings upon them.", "그가 조금이라도 생각해 보려 했다면, 불과 일 분 전에 자기가 그들에게 그런 식으로 말을 걸며 자기감정을 떠안기려 했다는 사실에 정말이지 놀랐으리라."], ["And where had those feelings come from?", "그런데 그 감정들은 도대체 어디서 나온 것이었을까?"], ["Now if the whole room had been filled, not with police officers, but with those nearest and dearest to him, he would not have found one human word for them, so empty was his heart.", "이제 이 방이 경찰관들이 아니라 그에게 가장 가깝고 소중한 사람들로 가득 찼다 한들, 그는 그들에게 건넬 인간적인 말 한마디조차 찾지 못했으리라. 그만큼 그의 마음은 텅 비어 있었다."], ["A gloomy sensation of agonising, everlasting solitude and remoteness, took conscious form in his soul.", "고통스럽고 영원한 고독과 격리의 음울한 감각이 그의 영혼 속에서 또렷한 형태를 갖추었다."], ["It was not the meanness of his sentimental effusions before Ilya Petrovitch, nor the meanness of the latter’s triumph over him that had caused this sudden revulsion in his heart.", "그의 마음속에 이 갑작스러운 반전을 일으킨 것은, 일리야 페트로비치 앞에서 보인 자기 감상적 토로의 비루함도, 그를 누르고 거둔 후자의 승리의 비루함도 아니었다."], ["Oh, what had he to do now with his own baseness, with all these petty vanities, officers, German women, debts, police-offices?", "오, 이제 그가 자기 자신의 비열함이며, 이 모든 하찮은 허영이며, 장교들이며, 독일 여자들이며, 빚이며, 경찰서 따위와 무슨 상관이 있겠는가?"], ["If he had been sentenced to be burnt at that moment, he would not have stirred, would hardly have heard the sentence to the end.", "만일 그 순간 그에게 화형이 선고되었다 해도, 그는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았을 것이고, 그 판결을 끝까지 듣지도 않았으리라. 무언가 전혀 새롭고, 갑작스럽고, 알 수 없는 일이 그에게 일어나고 있었다."], ["Something was happening to him entirely new, sudden and unknown.", "그가 이해한 것은 아니었지만, 그는 감각의 온 강렬함으로 또렷이 느꼈다."], ["It was not that he understood, but he felt clearly with all the intensity of sensation that he could never more appeal to these people in the police-office with sentimental effusions like his recent outburst, or with anything whatever; and that if they had been his own brothers and sisters and not police-officers, it would have been utterly out of the question to appeal to them in any circumstance of life.", "자기는 이제 다시는 이 경찰서 사람들에게 조금 전의 그 폭발 같은 감상적 토로로도, 그 무엇으로도 결코 호소할 수 없으리라는 것을. 그리고 설령 이들이 경찰관이 아니라 자기 친형제자매라 해도, 인생의 그 어떤 상황에서도 이들에게 호소한다는 것은 전혀 있을 수 없는 일이리라는 것을."], ["He had never experienced such a strange and awful sensation.", "그는 이토록 기이하고 끔찍한 감각을 일찍이 경험한 적이 없었다."], ["And what was most agonising--it was more a sensation than a conception or idea, a direct sensation, the most agonising of all the sensations he had known in his life.", "그리고 무엇보다 고통스러운 것은 — 그것이 개념이나 관념이라기보다는 하나의 감각이라는 점, 직접적인 감각이라는 점, 그가 평생 알아 온 모든 감각 중에 가장 고통스러운 감각이라는 점이었다."]], [["The head clerk began dictating to him the usual form of declaration, that he could not pay, that he undertook to do so at a future date, that he would not leave the town, nor sell his property, and so on.", "수석 서기가 그에게, 변제할 수 없다는 것, 후일 변제할 것을 약속한다는 것, 도시를 떠나지 않고 재산을 매각하지 않겠다는 것 등을 적은 통상적인 서식의 진술서를 불러 주기 시작했다."]], [["“But you can’t write, you can hardly hold the pen,”", "\"하지만 당신은 쓸 수가 없겠군요, 펜조차 제대로 못 쥐는데.\""], ["observed the head clerk, looking with curiosity at Raskolnikov.", "수석 서기가 라스콜니코프를 호기심 어린 눈으로 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Are you ill?”", "\"어디 아프시오?\""]], [["“Yes, I am giddy.", "\"네, 어지럽습니다."], ["Go on!”", "계속하시지요!\""]], [["“That’s all.", "\"이게 다요."], ["Sign it.”", "서명하시오.\""]], [["The head clerk took the paper, and turned to attend to others.", "수석 서기는 종이를 받아 들고 다른 사람들을 응대하러 돌아섰다."]], [["Raskolnikov gave back the pen; but instead of getting up and going away, he put his elbows on the table and pressed his head in his hands.", "라스콜니코프는 펜을 돌려주었다. 그러나 일어나서 나가는 대신, 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 머리를 짓눌렀다."], ["He felt as if a nail were being driven into his skull.", "두개골에 못이 박히는 것만 같았다."], ["A strange idea suddenly occurred to him, to get up at once, to go up to Nikodim Fomitch, and tell him everything that had happened yesterday, and then to go with him to his lodgings and to show him the things in the hole in the corner.", "문득 이상한 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다, 지금 당장 일어나 니코딤 포미치에게 가서 어제 일어난 모든 일을 다 털어놓고, 그러고는 그와 함께 자기 하숙으로 가서 구석 구멍 속의 물건들을 보여 주자는. 그 충동이 어찌나 강했던지 그는 그것을 실행하려고 자리에서 일어났다."], ["The impulse was so strong that he got up from his seat to carry it out.", "\"잠깐 생각이라도 좀 해 보는 게 낫지 않을까?\""], ["“Hadn’t I better think a minute?”", "하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["flashed through his mind. “No, better cast off the burden without thinking.”", "\"아니, 생각하지 말고 차라리 짐을 벗어 던지는 게 나아.\""], ["But all at once he stood still, rooted to the spot.", "그러나 갑자기 그는 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Nikodim Fomitch was talking eagerly with Ilya Petrovitch, and the words reached him:", "니코딤 포미치가 일리야 페트로비치와 열심히 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 그 말이 그에게 들려왔던 것이다."]], [["“It’s impossible, they’ll both be released.", "\"그건 불가능해, 둘 다 풀려날 거야."], ["To begin with, the whole story contradicts itself.", "우선, 이야기 전체가 앞뒤가 안 맞아."], ["Why should they have called the porter, if it had been their doing?", "자기들이 한 짓이라면 뭐하러 문지기를 불렀겠나?"], ["To inform against themselves?", "스스로를 고발하려고?"], ["Or as a blind?", "아니면 눈속임으로?"], ["No, that would be too cunning!", "아니, 그건 너무 교활한 짓이지!"], ["Besides, Pestryakov, the student, was seen at the gate by both the porters and a woman as he went in.", "게다가 그 대학생 페스트랴코프는 들어갈 때 문지기 둘과 한 여자한테 대문에서 목격됐어."], ["He was walking with three friends, who left him only at the gate, and he asked the porters to direct him, in the presence of the friends.", "친구 셋과 함께 걷고 있었는데, 그들이 바로 그 대문에서 헤어졌고, 그자가 친구들이 보는 앞에서 문지기들에게 길을 물었단 말이야."], ["Now, would he have asked his way if he had been going with such an object?", "그런 목적을 품고 가는 자라면 길을 물었겠나?"], ["As for Koch, he spent half an hour at the silversmith’s below, before he went up to the old woman and he left him at exactly a quarter to eight.", "코흐로 말하면, 그자는 노파한테 올라가기 전에 아래층 은세공인 가게에서 삼십 분을 보냈고, 정확히 여덟 시 십오 분 전에 거기서 나왔어."], ["Now just consider...”", "자, 한번 생각해 보게…….\""]], [["“But excuse me, how do you explain this contradiction?", "\"하지만 잠깐, 그 모순은 어떻게 설명하나?"], ["They state themselves that they knocked and the door was locked; yet three minutes later when they went up with the porter, it turned out the door was unfastened.”", "그들 자신이 진술하기를, 문을 두드렸는데 잠겨 있었다고 했어. 그런데 삼 분 뒤에 문지기와 함께 올라가 보니 문이 잠겨 있지 않았다는 거 아닌가.\""]], [["“That’s just it; the murderer must have been there and bolted himself in; and they’d have caught him for a certainty if Koch had not been an ass and gone to look for the porter too. _He_ must have seized the interval to get downstairs and slip by them somehow. Koch keeps crossing himself and saying: ‘If I had been there, he would have jumped out and killed me with his axe.’ He is going to have a thanksgiving service--ha, ha!”", "\"바로 그거야. 살인자가 거기 있다가 안에서 빗장을 걸어 잠갔던 게 분명해. 코흐가 멍청이처럼 굴어서 자기도 문지기를 찾으러 가지만 않았더라면 틀림없이 그자를 잡았을 거야. 그자는 그 틈을 타서 아래층으로 내려가 어떻게든 그들 곁을 빠져나간 게 분명해. 코흐는 연신 성호를 그으며 말하지. '내가 거기 있었더라면, 그자가 뛰쳐나와 도끼로 날 죽였을 거요'라고. 감사 예배를 드리겠다는군 — 하, 하!\""]], [["“And no one saw the murderer?”", "\"그래서 아무도 그 살인자를 못 봤단 말인가?\""]], [["“They might well not see him; the house is a regular Noah’s Ark,”", "\"못 볼 만도 하지. 그 집은 영락없는 노아의 방주니까.\""], ["said the head clerk, who was listening.", "듣고 있던 수석 서기가 말했다."]], [["“It’s clear, quite clear,”", "\"명백해, 아주 명백하다고.\""], ["Nikodim Fomitch repeated warmly.", "니코딤 포미치가 열을 올리며 거듭 말했다."]], [["“No, it is anything but clear,”", "\"아니, 명백하긴커녕 전혀 아니야.\""], ["Ilya Petrovitch maintained.", "일리야 페트로비치가 우겼다."]], [["Raskolnikov picked up his hat and walked towards the door, but he did not reach it....", "라스콜니코프는 모자를 집어 들고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔지만, 거기에 이르지는 못했다……"]], [["When he recovered consciousness, he found himself sitting in a chair, supported by someone on the right side, while someone else was standing on the left, holding a yellowish glass filled with yellow water, and Nikodim Fomitch standing before him, looking intently at him.", "정신을 차렸을 때, 그는 의자에 앉아 있는 자신을 발견했다. 오른쪽에서 누군가가 그를 부축하고 있었고, 왼쪽에서는 또 다른 누군가가 누르스름한 물이 담긴 누런 유리잔을 들고 서 있었으며, 니코딤 포미치가 그의 앞에 서서 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다."], ["He got up from the chair.", "그는 의자에서 일어났다."]], [["“What’s this?", "\"이게 무슨 일이오?"], ["Are you ill?”", "어디 아프시오?\""], ["Nikodim Fomitch asked, rather sharply.", "니코딤 포미치가 다소 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["“He could hardly hold his pen when he was signing,”", "\"서명할 때도 펜을 제대로 못 쥐던걸요.\""], ["said the head clerk, settling back in his place, and taking up his work again.", "수석 서기가 자기 자리로 돌아가 다시 일을 시작하면서 말했다."]], [["“Have you been ill long?”", "\"앓은 지 오래됐소?\""], ["cried Ilya Petrovitch from his place, where he, too, was looking through papers.", "일리야 페트로비치가 자기 자리에서 외쳤는데, 그 역시 서류를 들여다보고 있었다."], ["He had, of course, come to look at the sick man when he fainted, but retired at once when he recovered.", "그는 물론 라스콜니코프가 기절했을 때 환자를 보러 다가왔다가, 그가 정신을 차리자 곧장 물러난 참이었다."]], [["“Since yesterday,”", "\"어제부터입니다.\""], ["muttered Raskolnikov in reply.", "라스콜니코프가 중얼거리며 대답했다."]], [["“Did you go out yesterday?”", "\"어제 외출했었소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Though you were ill?”", "\"앓는 몸으로 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“At what time?”", "\"몇 시에?\""]], [["“About seven.”", "\"일곱 시쯤요.\""]], [["“And where did you go, may I ask?”", "\"그래, 어디를 갔는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Along the street.”", "\"거리를 따라서요.\""]], [["“Short and clear.”", "\"짤막하고 분명하군.\""]], [["Raskolnikov, white as a handkerchief, had answered sharply, jerkily, without dropping his black feverish eyes before Ilya Petrovitch’s stare.", "라스콜니코프는 손수건처럼 새하얗게 질린 채, 일리야 페트로비치의 응시 앞에서 자신의 검고 열에 들뜬 눈을 떨어뜨리지 않은 채로 날카롭게, 뚝뚝 끊어 가며 대답했다."]], [["“He can scarcely stand upright.", "\"이 사람 똑바로 서 있기도 힘들어하는데."], ["And you...”", "그런데 자넨…….\""], ["Nikodim Fomitch was beginning.", "니코딤 포미치가 입을 열려 했다."]], [["“No matter,”", "\"상관없어.\""], ["Ilya Petrovitch pronounced rather peculiarly.", "일리야 페트로비치가 어딘가 묘한 어조로 내뱉었다."]], [["Nikodim Fomitch would have made some further protest, but glancing at the head clerk who was looking very hard at him, he did not speak.", "니코딤 포미치는 무언가 더 항변하려 했지만, 자기를 아주 뚫어지게 바라보고 있는 수석 서기를 흘끗 보고는 입을 다물었다."], ["There was a sudden silence.", "갑자기 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["It was strange.", "기묘한 일이었다."]], [["“Very well, then,”", "\"그럼, 좋소.\""], ["concluded Ilya Petrovitch, “we will not detain you.”", "일리야 페트로비치가 결론을 내렸다. \"더 붙잡아 두지 않겠소.\""]], [["Raskolnikov went out.", "라스콜니코프는 밖으로 나갔다."], ["He caught the sound of eager conversation on his departure, and above the rest rose the questioning voice of Nikodim Fomitch.", "그가 나가자마자 활기찬 대화 소리가 들려왔고, 그 가운데서도 니코딤 포미치의 캐묻는 목소리가 두드러졌다."], ["In the street, his faintness passed off completely.", "거리에 나서자 그의 어지럼증은 말끔히 가셨다."]], [["“A search--there will be a search at once,”", "\"수색 — 당장 수색이 있을 거야.\""], ["he repeated to himself, hurrying home.", "그는 집으로 서둘러 가면서 거듭 되뇌었다."], ["“The brutes!", "\"짐승 같은 놈들!"], ["they suspect.”", "의심하고 있어.\""]], [["His former terror mastered him completely again.", "조금 전의 그 공포가 다시금 그를 완전히 사로잡았다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["“And what if there has been a search already?", "\"그런데 만약 벌써 수색이 있었다면?"], ["What if I find them in my room?”", "만약 그들이 내 방에서 기다리고 있다면?\""]], [["But here was his room.", "그러나 여기 그의 방이 있었다."], ["Nothing and no one in it.", "방 안에는 아무것도, 아무도 없었다."], ["No one had peeped in.", "누구도 들여다보지 않았다."], ["Even Nastasya had not touched it.", "나스타시야조차 손대지 않았다."], ["But heavens!", "하지만 맙소사!"], ["how could he have left all those things in the hole?", "어떻게 그 모든 물건을 저 구멍 속에 그대로 둘 수 있었단 말인가?"]], [["He rushed to the corner, slipped his hand under the paper, pulled the things out and lined his pockets with them.", "그는 구석으로 달려가 벽지 밑으로 손을 밀어 넣고는 물건들을 끄집어내어 주머니마다 쑤셔 넣었다. 전부 여덟 점이었다."], ["There were eight articles in all: two little boxes with ear-rings or something of the sort, he hardly looked to see; then four small leather cases.", "작은 상자 두 개에는 귀고리인지 뭔지가 들어 있었으나, 그는 제대로 들여다보지도 않았다. 그다음엔 가죽으로 된 작은 갑 네 개."], ["There was a chain, too, merely wrapped in newspaper and something else in newspaper, that looked like a decoration....", "사슬도 하나 있었는데, 그저 신문지에 둘둘 말려 있었고, 또 신문지에 싸인 다른 무언가는 훈장처럼 보였다……."], ["He put them all in the different pockets of his overcoat, and the remaining pocket of his trousers, trying to conceal them as much as possible.", "그는 그것들을 모두 외투의 여러 주머니와 바지의 남은 한쪽 주머니에 나눠 넣으며, 되도록 눈에 띄지 않게 감추려 애썼다."], ["He took the purse, too.", "지갑도 챙겼다."], ["Then he went out of his room, leaving the door open.", "그러고는 문을 열어 둔 채 방을 나섰다."], ["He walked quickly and resolutely, and though he felt shattered, he had his senses about him.", "그는 빠르고 단호하게 걸었고, 비록 만신창이가 된 듯했으나 정신만은 또렷했다."], ["He was afraid of pursuit, he was afraid that in another half-hour, another quarter of an hour perhaps, instructions would be issued for his pursuit, and so at all costs, he must hide all traces before then.", "그는 추적을 두려워했고, 앞으로 삼십 분, 어쩌면 십오 분 뒤면 그를 쫓으라는 지령이 떨어지리라는 것이 두려웠다. 그러니 무슨 일이 있어도 그전에 모든 흔적을 감춰야 했다."], ["He must clear everything up while he still had some strength, some reasoning power left him....", "아직 얼마간의 기력이, 얼마간의 판단력이 남아 있는 동안 모든 것을 정리해 두어야만 했다……."], ["Where was he to go?", "어디로 가야 한단 말인가?"]], [["That had long been settled: “Fling them into the canal, and all traces hidden in the water, the thing would be at an end.”", "그것은 이미 오래전에 정해 두었다. \"운하에 던져 버리는 거야, 그러면 모든 흔적이 물속에 묻히고, 이 일도 끝나는 거지.\""], ["So he had decided in the night of his delirium when several times he had had the impulse to get up and go away, to make haste, and get rid of it all.", "그렇게 그는 헛소리에 시달리던 그날 밤에 결심했었다. 그날 밤 그는 몇 번이고 벌떡 일어나 나가서, 서둘러 이 모든 것을 없애 버리고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔던 것이다."], ["But to get rid of it, turned out to be a very difficult task.", "그러나 그것을 없애 버리기란 막상 무척 어려운 일임이 드러났다."], ["He wandered along the bank of the Ekaterininsky Canal for half an hour or more and looked several times at the steps running down to the water, but he could not think of carrying out his plan; either rafts stood at the steps’ edge, and women were washing clothes on them, or boats were moored there, and people were swarming everywhere.", "그는 예카테리닌스키 운하의 둑을 따라 삼십 분 남짓 헤매며 물가로 내려가는 계단을 몇 번이나 바라보았으나, 도무지 계획을 실행할 엄두가 나지 않았다. 계단 끝에는 뗏목이 떠 있고 그 위에서 여자들이 빨래를 하고 있거나, 보트들이 매여 있고 사방에 사람들이 우글거렸기 때문이다."], ["Moreover he could be seen and noticed from the banks on all sides; it would look suspicious for a man to go down on purpose, stop, and throw something into the water.", "게다가 그는 양쪽 둑 어디에서든 눈에 띄어 사람들의 주목을 받을 수 있었다. 한 사내가 일부러 물가로 내려가 멈춰 서서 무언가를 물에 던진다면 의심을 살 만한 일이었다."], ["And what if the boxes were to float instead of sinking?", "그리고 만약 상자들이 가라앉지 않고 떠오르기라도 하면?"], ["And of course they would.", "당연히 떠오를 것이었다."], ["Even as it was, everyone he met seemed to stare and look round, as if they had nothing to do but to watch him.", "지금만 해도 마주치는 사람마다 그를 빤히 쳐다보고 돌아보는 것 같았으니, 마치 그를 지켜보는 것 말고는 달리 할 일이 없는 듯했다."], ["“Why is it, or can it be my fancy?”", "\"왜 이러지, 아니면 그저 내 착각인가?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["At last the thought struck him that it might be better to go to the Neva.", "마침내 차라리 네바강으로 가는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 그를 스쳤다."], ["There were not so many people there, he would be less observed, and it would be more convenient in every way, above all it was further off.", "거기엔 사람이 그리 많지 않아 덜 눈에 띌 것이고, 모든 면에서 더 편할 터였으며, 무엇보다 더 멀리 떨어져 있었다."], ["He wondered how he could have been wandering for a good half-hour, worried and anxious in this dangerous past without thinking of it before.", "그는 이 위험한 시간을 어떻게 꼬박 삼십 분이나 노심초사하며 헤매고 다니면서도 진작 그 생각을 떠올리지 못했는지 의아했다."], ["And that half-hour he had lost over an irrational plan, simply because he had thought of it in delirium!", "게다가 그 삼십 분을 한낱 터무니없는 계획에 허비한 것이, 단지 헛소리에 시달리던 중에 그 계획을 떠올렸다는 이유만으로 그러했던 것이다!"], ["He had become extremely absent and forgetful and he was aware of it.", "그는 극도로 멍해지고 건망증이 심해졌으며, 스스로도 그것을 자각하고 있었다."], ["He certainly must make haste.", "정말이지 서둘러야만 했다."]], [["He walked towards the Neva along V---- Prospect, but on the way another idea struck him.", "그는 V 거리를 따라 네바강 쪽으로 걸었으나, 가는 길에 또 다른 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["“Why to the Neva?", "\"왜 네바강이지?"], ["Would it not be better to go somewhere far off, to the Islands again, and there hide the things in some solitary place, in a wood or under a bush, and mark the spot perhaps?”", "차라리 어딘가 멀리, 다시 섬들로 가서 인적 없는 어느 곳에, 숲속이나 덤불 밑에 물건을 숨기고 그 자리를 표시해 두는 편이 낫지 않을까?\""], ["And though he felt incapable of clear judgment, the idea seemed to him a sound one.", "그리고 비록 명료하게 판단할 능력은 없다고 느꼈지만, 그 생각은 그럴듯하게 여겨졌다."], ["But he was not destined to go there.", "그러나 그는 그곳까지 갈 운명이 아니었다."], ["For coming out of V---- Prospect towards the square, he saw on the left a passage leading between two blank walls to a courtyard.", "V 거리에서 광장 쪽으로 나오던 그는, 왼편에 휑한 두 벽 사이로 안뜰로 이어지는 통로 하나를 보았던 것이다."], ["On the right hand, the blank unwhitewashed wall of a four-storied house stretched far into the court; on the left, a wooden hoarding ran parallel with it for twenty paces into the court, and then turned sharply to the left.", "오른편으로는 회칠하지 않은 사 층 건물의 휑한 벽이 안뜰 깊숙이 뻗어 있었고, 왼편으로는 나무 울타리가 그것과 나란히 안뜰 안쪽으로 스무 걸음쯤 이어지다가, 다시 왼쪽으로 급히 꺾여 있었다."], ["Here was a deserted fenced-off place where rubbish of different sorts was lying.", "거기엔 온갖 잡동사니가 널브러진, 울타리로 막힌 인적 없는 공터가 있었다."], ["At the end of the court, the corner of a low, smutty, stone shed, apparently part of some workshop, peeped from behind the hoarding.", "안뜰 끝에는 낮고 거뭇한 돌 헛간의 모서리가 울타리 뒤로 빠끔히 내다보였는데, 어느 작업장의 일부인 듯했다. 아마 마차 제작소나 목공소의 헛간인 모양이었다."], ["It was probably a carriage builder’s or carpenter’s shed; the whole place from the entrance was black with coal dust.", "입구에서부터 그 일대 전체가 석탄 먼지로 시커멨다. 여기야말로 던져 버리기에 딱 좋은 곳이라고 그는 생각했다."], ["Here would be the place to throw it, he thought.", "안뜰에 아무도 없는 것을 확인하고 그는 슬그머니 들어섰고, 곧 대문 가까이에 하수구 하나가 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Not seeing anyone in the yard, he slipped in, and at once saw near the gate a sink, such as is often put in yards where there are many workmen or cab-drivers; and on the hoarding above had been scribbled in chalk the time-honoured witticism, “Standing here strictly forbidden.”", "인부나 마부가 많이 드나드는 안뜰에 흔히 마련되어 있는 그런 것이었다. 그 위 울타리에는 분필로, \"여기서 볼일 보는 것을 엄금함\"이라는 케케묵은 농지거리가 휘갈겨져 있었다."], ["This was all the better, for there would be nothing suspicious about his going in.", "이것은 오히려 잘된 일이었으니, 그가 들어선 데에 의심스러울 것이 전혀 없었기 때문이다."], ["“Here I could throw it all in a heap and get away!”", "\"여기다 한꺼번에 무더기로 던져 버리고 내빼면 되겠군!\""]], [["Looking round once more, with his hand already in his pocket, he noticed against the outer wall, between the entrance and the sink, a big unhewn stone, weighing perhaps sixty pounds.", "다시 한번 주위를 둘러보며, 이미 손을 주머니에 넣은 채, 그는 입구와 하수구 사이의 바깥벽에 기대어 놓인, 어쩌면 육십 파운드는 됨 직한 다듬지 않은 큼직한 돌 하나를 발견했다."], ["The other side of the wall was a street.", "벽 너머는 거리였다."], ["He could hear passers-by, always numerous in that part, but he could not be seen from the entrance, unless someone came in from the street, which might well happen indeed, so there was need of haste.", "그쪽 동네에는 늘 그렇듯 행인이 많아 그들의 발소리가 들렸으나, 누군가 거리에서 들어오지 않는 한 입구에서 그가 보이지는 않았다. 물론 그런 일이 충분히 일어날 수도 있었으니, 서둘러야 했다."]], [["He bent down over the stone, seized the top of it firmly in both hands, and using all his strength turned it over.", "그는 돌 위로 몸을 굽혀 두 손으로 그 윗부분을 단단히 움켜쥐고는, 온 힘을 다해 뒤집었다."], ["Under the stone was a small hollow in the ground, and he immediately emptied his pocket into it.", "돌 밑 땅바닥에는 작은 구멍이 패여 있었고, 그는 즉시 주머니 속 물건들을 그 안에 쏟아부었다."], ["The purse lay at the top, and yet the hollow was not filled up.", "지갑이 맨 위에 놓였는데도 구멍은 다 차지 않았다."], ["Then he seized the stone again and with one twist turned it back, so that it was in the same position again, though it stood a very little higher.", "그런 다음 그는 다시 돌을 붙잡아 한 번 비틀어 제자리로 돌려놓았고, 돌은 아주 약간 높아지긴 했으나 본래 자리에 다시 놓였다."], ["But he scraped the earth about it and pressed it at the edges with his foot.", "하지만 그는 돌 주위로 흙을 긁어모으고 발로 가장자리를 꾹꾹 눌러 다졌다."], ["Nothing could be noticed.", "아무것도 눈에 띌 게 없었다."]], [["Then he went out, and turned into the square.", "그러고 나서 그는 밖으로 나와 광장으로 접어들었다."], ["Again an intense, almost unbearable joy overwhelmed him for an instant, as it had in the police-office.", "또다시 경찰서에서 그랬듯이, 강렬하고 거의 견디기 힘든 기쁨이 한순간 그를 휩쌌다."], ["“I have buried my tracks!", "\"흔적을 묻어 버렸어!"], ["And who, who can think of looking under that stone?", "그 누가, 도대체 누가 저 돌 밑을 들여다볼 생각을 하겠어?"], ["It has been lying there most likely ever since the house was built, and will lie as many years more.", "저건 아마 이 집이 지어졌을 때부터 거기 있었을 테고, 앞으로도 그만큼 더 오래 그대로 있을 거야."], ["And if it were found, who would think of me?", "설령 발견된다 한들, 누가 나를 떠올리겠어? 다 끝났어!"], ["It is all over!", "단서는 없어!\""], ["No clue!” And he laughed.", "그러고는 그가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Yes, he remembered that he began laughing a thin, nervous noiseless laugh, and went on laughing all the time he was crossing the square.", "그렇다, 그는 자신이 가늘고 신경질적이며 소리 없는 웃음을 터뜨리기 시작했고, 광장을 가로지르는 내내 줄곧 웃었다는 것을 기억했다."], ["But when he reached the K---- Boulevard where two days before he had come upon that girl, his laughter suddenly ceased.", "그러나 이틀 전 그 소녀와 마주쳤던 K 대로에 이르자, 그의 웃음은 돌연 멎었다."], ["Other ideas crept into his mind.", "다른 생각들이 그의 머릿속으로 기어들었다."], ["He felt all at once that it would be loathsome to pass that seat on which after the girl was gone, he had sat and pondered, and that it would be hateful, too, to meet that whiskered policeman to whom he had given the twenty copecks: “Damn him!”", "그는 문득, 소녀가 떠난 뒤 자신이 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겼던 그 벤치를 지나치는 것이 혐오스럽게 느껴졌고, 이십 코페이카를 건네주었던 그 구레나룻 난 순경과 마주치는 것 또한 끔찍하게 여겨졌다. \"빌어먹을 놈!\""]], [["He walked, looking about him angrily and distractedly.", "그는 성난, 그리고 넋이 나간 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 걸었다."], ["All his ideas now seemed to be circling round some single point, and he felt that there really was such a point, and that now, now, he was left facing that point--and for the first time, indeed, during the last two months.", "이제 그의 모든 생각이 어떤 단 하나의 점을 맴도는 듯했고, 그는 실제로 그런 점이 존재함을, 그리고 지금, 바로 지금 자신이 그 점과 정면으로 마주하게 되었음을 느꼈다 — 게다가 사실 지난 두 달 동안 처음으로."]], [["“Damn it all!”", "\"다 빌어먹을!\""], ["he thought suddenly, in a fit of ungovernable fury.", "그는 걷잡을 수 없는 격분에 휩싸여 불현듯 생각했다."], ["“If it has begun, then it has begun.", "\"이미 시작된 일이면, 시작된 거야."], ["Hang the new life!", "새로운 삶 따위 집어치워!"], ["Good Lord, how stupid it is!...", "맙소사, 이 얼마나 어리석은가!……"], ["And what lies I told to-day!", "오늘 내가 얼마나 거짓말을 늘어놓았던가!"], ["How despicably I fawned upon that wretched Ilya Petrovitch!", "저 한심한 일리야 페트로비치한테 얼마나 비굴하게 알랑댔던가!"], ["But that is all folly!", "하지만 그딴 건 다 부질없어!"], ["What do I care for them all, and my fawning upon them!", "그자들이, 또 그자들한테 알랑댄 게 나한테 무슨 대수라고!"], ["It is not that at all!", "그게 문제가 아니야!"], ["It is not that at all!”", "전혀 그게 문제가 아니라고!\""]], [["Suddenly he stopped; a new utterly unexpected and exceedingly simple question perplexed and bitterly confounded him.", "갑자기 그는 걸음을 멈추었다. 전혀 예기치 못한, 더없이 단순한 새로운 물음 하나가 그를 당혹스럽게, 그리고 쓰라리게 혼란에 빠뜨렸다."]], [["“If it all has really been done deliberately and not idiotically, if I really had a certain and definite object, how is it I did not even glance into the purse and don’t know what I had there, for which I have undergone these agonies, and have deliberately undertaken this base, filthy degrading business?", "\"만약 이 모든 일이 정말로 멍청하게가 아니라 의도적으로 이루어진 거라면, 만약 내게 정말로 분명하고 확실한 목적이 있었다면, 어째서 나는 지갑 속을 들여다보지도 않았고, 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지도 모른단 말인가? 바로 그것을 위해 이런 고통을 겪고, 이렇게 비열하고 추잡하고 천한 짓을 의도적으로 저질렀으면서도?"], ["And here I wanted at once to throw into the water the purse together with all the things which I had not seen either...", "게다가 나는 그 지갑을, 들여다보지도 않은 물건들과 함께 곧장 물에 던져 버리려 하지 않았던가……"], ["how’s that?”", "이게 어찌 된 일이지?\""]], [["Yes, that was so, that was all so.", "그래, 그건 그랬다, 모든 게 그랬다."], ["Yet he had known it all before, and it was not a new question for him, even when it was decided in the night without hesitation and consideration, as though so it must be, as though it could not possibly be otherwise....", "그렇지만 그는 그 모든 것을 이미 전부터 알고 있었으며, 그것은 그에게 새로운 물음도 아니었다. 그날 밤, 망설임도 숙고도 없이, 마치 그래야만 하는 듯이, 마치 달리는 도저히 있을 수 없는 듯이 결정을 내렸을 때조차도……."], ["Yes, he had known it all, and understood it all; it surely had all been settled even yesterday at the moment when he was bending over the box and pulling the jewel-cases out of it....", "그래, 그는 그 모든 것을 알고 있었고, 그 모든 것을 이해하고 있었다. 분명 그것은 어제, 그가 궤짝 위로 몸을 굽혀 보석갑들을 끄집어내던 그 순간에 이미 모두 정해져 있었던 것이다……."], ["Yes, so it was.", "그래, 그랬던 것이다."]], [["“It is because I am very ill,”", "\"이건 내가 몹시 아프기 때문이야.\""], ["he decided grimly at last, “I have been worrying and fretting myself, and I don’t know what I am doing....", "마침내 그는 침울하게 결론지었다. \"나는 줄곧 노심초사하며 안달복달했고, 내가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 몰라……."], ["Yesterday and the day before yesterday and all this time I have been worrying myself....", "어제도, 그저께도, 이 모든 시간 내내 나는 스스로를 들볶아 왔어……."], ["I shall get well and I shall not worry....", "나으면, 나는 안달하지 않을 거야……."], ["But what if I don’t get well at all?", "그런데 만약 영영 낫지 않으면?"], ["Good God, how sick I am of it all!”", "맙소사, 이 모든 게 정말 지긋지긋해!\""]], [["He walked on without resting.", "그는 쉬지 않고 계속 걸었다."], ["He had a terrible longing for some distraction, but he did not know what to do, what to attempt.", "그는 무언가 기분을 돌릴 거리가 절실히 그리웠으나, 무엇을 해야 할지, 무엇을 시도해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["A new overwhelming sensation was gaining more and more mastery over him every moment; this was an immeasurable, almost physical, repulsion for everything surrounding him, an obstinate, malignant feeling of hatred.", "새로운, 압도적인 감각 하나가 매 순간 점점 더 그를 사로잡아 갔다. 그것은 자기를 둘러싼 모든 것에 대한 가늠할 수 없는, 거의 육체적인 혐오, 끈질기고 악의에 찬 증오의 감정이었다."], ["All who met him were loathsome to him--he loathed their faces, their movements, their gestures.", "마주치는 모든 이가 그에게 역겨웠다 — 그들의 얼굴이, 그들의 움직임이, 그들의 몸짓이 역겨웠다."], ["If anyone had addressed him, he felt that he might have spat at him or bitten him....", "만약 누군가 그에게 말을 걸었다면, 그는 그자에게 침을 뱉거나 물어뜯을 것만 같았다……."]], [["He stopped suddenly, on coming out on the bank of the Little Neva, near the bridge to Vassilyevsky Ostrov.", "바실리옙스키 섬으로 건너가는 다리 근처, 작은 네바강 둑으로 나서다가 그는 문득 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["“Why, he lives here, in that house,”", "\"참, 그가 여기 살지, 저 집에.\""], ["he thought, “why, I have not come to Razumihin of my own accord!", "그는 생각했다. \"참, 내가 라주미힌한테 제 발로 온 게 아닌데!"], ["Here it’s the same thing over again....", "또 똑같은 일이 되풀이되는군……."], ["Very interesting to know, though; have I come on purpose or have I simply walked here by chance?", "그래도 알아 두면 흥미롭겠어. 일부러 온 건가, 아니면 그냥 우연히 여기까지 걸어온 건가?"], ["Never mind, I said the day before yesterday that I would go and see him the day _after_; well, and so I will!", "아무려면 어때, 그저께 내가 모레쯤 그를 보러 가겠다고 했었지. 그러니, 그래, 가는 거다!"], ["Besides I really cannot go further now.”", "게다가 지금 더는 정말 걸을 수도 없으니까.\""]], [["He went up to Razumihin’s room on the fifth floor.", "그는 오 층에 있는 라주미힌의 방으로 올라갔다."]], [["The latter was at home in his garret, busily writing at the moment, and he opened the door himself.", "라주미힌은 자기 다락방에 있었고, 마침 무언가를 부지런히 쓰던 중이었는데, 직접 문을 열어 주었다."], ["It was four months since they had seen each other.", "두 사람이 서로 만나지 못한 지 넉 달째였다."], ["Razumihin was sitting in a ragged dressing-gown, with slippers on his bare feet, unkempt, unshaven and unwashed.", "라주미힌은 다 해진 실내복 차림에 맨발로 슬리퍼를 신고 있었고, 머리는 헝클어지고 수염도 깎지 않았으며 세수도 하지 않은 모습이었다."], ["His face showed surprise.", "그의 얼굴에 놀라움이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Is it you?”", "\"자네였나?\""], ["he cried.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["He looked his comrade up and down; then after a brief pause, he whistled.", "그는 동무를 위아래로 훑어보더니, 잠시 말이 없다가 휘파람을 불었다."], ["“As hard up as all that!", "\"그렇게까지 쪼들리나!"], ["Why, brother, you’ve cut me out!”", "아니, 이 친구야, 나를 완전히 따돌렸군그래!\""], ["he added, looking at Raskolnikov’s rags.", "그는 라스콜니코프의 누더기를 보며 덧붙였다."], ["“Come sit down, you are tired, I’ll be bound.”", "\"자, 앉게, 피곤하겠지, 틀림없이.\""]], [["And when he had sunk down on the American leather sofa, which was in even worse condition than his own, Razumihin saw at once that his visitor was ill.", "그리고 라스콜니코프가, 라주미힌 자신의 것보다도 한층 더 너덜너덜한 인조 가죽 소파에 털썩 주저앉자, 라주미힌은 단번에 손님이 아프다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Why, you are seriously ill, do you know that?”", "\"아니, 자네 중병이로군, 알고 있나?\""], ["He began feeling his pulse.", "그가 그의 맥을 짚기 시작했다."], ["Raskolnikov pulled away his hand.", "라스콜니코프는 손을 홱 뿌리쳤다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"괜찮아.\""], ["he said, “I have come for this: I have no lessons....", "그가 말했다. \"이래서 온 거야."], ["I wanted,...", "일자리가 없어서……"], ["but I don’t really want lessons....”", "그러니까 가정교사 자리를 원했는데…… 아니, 사실 가정교사 자리는 필요 없어…….\""]], [["“But I say!", "\"이봐!"], ["You are delirious, you know!”", "자네 헛소리를 하고 있군, 알아!\""], ["Razumihin observed, watching him carefully.", "라주미힌이 그를 유심히 지켜보며 말했다."]], [["“No, I am not.”", "\"아니, 그렇지 않아.\""]], [["Raskolnikov got up from the sofa.", "라스콜니코프가 소파에서 일어섰다."], ["As he had mounted the stairs to Razumihin’s, he had not realised that he would be meeting his friend face to face.", "라주미힌의 집으로 계단을 올라올 때만 해도 그는 친구와 얼굴을 맞대게 되리라는 것을 미처 깨닫지 못했다."], ["Now, in a flash, he knew, that what he was least of all disposed for at that moment was to be face to face with anyone in the wide world.", "그런데 이제, 순식간에, 그 순간 자기가 이 너른 세상에서 무엇보다도 원치 않는 것이 바로 누군가와 얼굴을 맞대는 일임을 깨달았다."], ["His spleen rose within him.", "그의 속에서 울화가 치밀었다."], ["He almost choked with rage at himself as soon as he crossed Razumihin’s threshold.", "라주미힌의 문턱을 넘어서기가 무섭게 그는 자기 자신에 대한 분노로 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었다."]], [["“Good-bye,”", "\"잘 있게.\""], ["he said abruptly, and walked to the door.", "그가 불쑥 말하고는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“Stop, stop!", "\"멈춰, 멈추라고!"], ["You queer fish.”", "이 별난 친구야.\""]], [["“I don’t want to,”", "\"가고 싶지 않아.\""], ["said the other, again pulling away his hand.", "라스콜니코프가 다시 손을 뿌리치며 말했다."]], [["“Then why the devil have you come?", "\"그럼 도대체 뭣 하러 온 건가?"], ["Are you mad, or what?", "미쳤나, 어쩐 일이야?"], ["Why, this is...", "아니, 이건……"], ["almost insulting!", "거의 모욕이잖아!"], ["I won’t let you go like that.”", "그렇게는 못 보내.\""]], [["“Well, then, I came to you because I know no one but you who could help...", "\"그럼, 말하지, 내가 자네한테 온 건, 일을 시작하는 데에…… 도와줄 만한 사람이 자네밖에 없어서야……"], ["to begin... because you are kinder than anyone--cleverer, I mean, and can judge...", "자네가 누구보다 친절하니까 — 아니, 똑똑하다는 거지, 그러니까 판단을 할 줄 아니까……"], ["and now I see that I want nothing. Do you hear?", "그런데 이제 보니 난 아무것도 필요 없어."], ["Nothing at all...", "알겠나? 아무것도……"], ["no one’s services...", "누구의 도움도……"], ["no one’s sympathy.", "누구의 동정도."], ["I am by myself... alone.", "난 혼자야……"], ["Come, that’s enough.", "혼자라고. 자, 됐어."], ["Leave me alone.”", "날 좀 내버려 둬.\""]], [["“Stay a minute, you sweep!", "\"잠깐만, 이 망나니야!"], ["You are a perfect madman.", "자넨 완전히 미치광이로군."], ["As you like for all I care.", "자네 좋을 대로 하게, 난 상관없으니까."], ["I have no lessons, do you see, and I don’t care about that, but there’s a bookseller, Heruvimov--and he takes the place of a lesson.", "사실 나도 가정교사 자리가 없네, 알겠나, 그런데 그딴 건 아무래도 좋아. 헤루비모프라는 책 장수가 있는데 — 그자가 가정교사 자리만큼은 해 준단 말이지."], ["I would not exchange him for five lessons.", "난 그자를 가정교사 자리 다섯 개랑도 안 바꿔."], ["He’s doing publishing of a kind, and issuing natural science manuals and what a circulation they have!", "그자가 일종의 출판이란 걸 하는데, 자연과학 입문서 같은 걸 찍어 내거든. 그게 얼마나 잘 팔리는지 몰라!"], ["The very titles are worth the money!", "제목만 봐도 돈값을 한다니까!"], ["You always maintained that I was a fool, but by Jove, my boy, there are greater fools than I am!", "자넨 늘 나를 바보라고 우겨댔지만, 이 친구야, 정말이지 나보다 더한 바보들이 수두룩해! 지금 그자가 진보적인 척하고 나서는데, 뭘 쥐뿔도 모르면서 그런 거지, 하지만 물론 나야 부추겨 주지."], ["Now he is setting up for being advanced, not that he has an inkling of anything, but, of course, I encourage him.", "자, 여기 독일어 원고 두 장이 있는데 — 내 생각엔 더없이 조잡한 사기야. '여자는 인간인가?'라는 문제를 다루는 거지."], ["Here are two signatures of the German text--in my opinion, the crudest charlatanism; it discusses the question, ‘Is woman a human being?’ And, of course, triumphantly proves that she is.", "그러고는 물론 여자가 인간임을 의기양양하게 증명해 보이지. 헤루비모프가 이걸 여성 문제에 기여하는 저작으로 내놓을 참이야."], ["Heruvimov is going to bring out this work as a contribution to the woman question; I am translating it; he will expand these two and a half signatures into six, we shall make up a gorgeous title half a page long and bring it out at half a rouble.", "내가 그걸 번역하고 있어. 그자는 이 두 장 반짜리를 여섯 장으로 늘리고, 우리는 반 페이지나 되는 거창한 제목을 지어 붙여서 반 루블에 내놓을 거야."], ["It will do!", "그럼 잘 팔리겠지!"], ["He pays me six roubles the signature, it works out to about fifteen roubles for the job, and I’ve had six already in advance.", "그자가 한 장당 육 루블을 주니까, 이 일로 한 십오 루블쯤 떨어지는 셈이고, 난 벌써 육 루블을 선금으로 받았어. 이걸 끝내면, 우린 고래에 관한 번역을 시작할 거고, 그다음엔 『고백록』 2부에서 가장 따분한 추문 몇 개를 골라 번역하기로 점찍어 뒀지."], ["When we have finished this, we are going to begin a translation about whales, and then some of the dullest scandals out of the second part of _Les Confessions_ we have marked for translation; somebody has told Heruvimov, that Rousseau was a kind of Radishchev.", "누가 헤루비모프한테 루소가 일종의 라디셰프 같은 자라고 일러 줬거든. 내가 반박할 리야 없지, 빌어먹을 자식!"], ["You may be sure I don’t contradict him, hang him!", "그래, 자네 '여자는 인간인가?' 두 번째 장을 좀 해 보겠나?"], ["Well, would you like to do the second signature of ‘_Is woman a human being?_’ If you would, take the German and pens and paper--all those are provided, and take three roubles; for as I have had six roubles in advance on the whole thing, three roubles come to you for your share.", "하겠다면, 독일어 원고랑 펜이랑 종이를 가져가게 — 그건 다 마련돼 있으니까 — 그리고 삼 루블을 받아 가. 내가 일 전체에 대해 육 루블을 선금으로 받았으니, 자네 몫으로 삼 루블이 가는 셈이야."], ["And when you have finished the signature there will be another three roubles for you.", "그리고 그 장을 끝내면 자네한테 또 삼 루블이 갈 거고. 그리고 제발 내가 자네한테 호의를 베푸는 거라곤 생각 말게."], ["And please don’t think I am doing you a service; quite the contrary, as soon as you came in, I saw how you could help me; to begin with, I am weak in spelling, and secondly, I am sometimes utterly adrift in German, so that I make it up as I go along for the most part.", "그 반대야. 자네가 들어오자마자, 난 자네가 날 어떻게 도와줄 수 있을지 알아봤거든."], ["The only comfort is, that it’s bound to be a change for the better.", "우선, 난 철자에 약하고, 둘째로, 난 가끔 독일어에서 완전히 헤매서, 대개는 그냥 멋대로 지어내곤 해. 한 가지 위안이라면, 이게 분명 더 나아질 거라는 점이지."], ["Though who can tell, maybe it’s sometimes for the worse.", "하긴 누가 알겠어, 어쩌면 가끔은 더 나빠질지도."], ["Will you take it?”", "자, 맡겠나?\""]], [["Raskolnikov took the German sheets in silence, took the three roubles and without a word went out.", "라스콜니코프는 말없이 독일어 원고를 받아 들고, 삼 루블을 챙긴 뒤, 한마디도 없이 나가 버렸다."], ["Razumihin gazed after him in astonishment.", "라주미힌은 어리둥절하여 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."], ["But when Raskolnikov was in the next street, he turned back, mounted the stairs to Razumihin’s again and laying on the table the German article and the three roubles, went out again, still without uttering a word.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 다음 거리에 이르렀을 때 발길을 돌려, 다시 라주미힌의 집으로 계단을 올라가, 탁자 위에 독일어 글과 삼 루블을 내려놓고는, 여전히 한마디도 내뱉지 않은 채 다시 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Are you raving, or what?”", "\"자네 헛소리를 하는 건가, 어쩐 일이야?\""], ["Razumihin shouted, roused to fury at last.", "라주미힌이 마침내 분통이 터져 고함쳤다."], ["“What farce is this?", "\"이게 무슨 광대 짓이야?"], ["You’ll drive me crazy too...", "자네 나까지 미치게 만들 셈이군……"], ["what did you come to see me for, damn you?”", "도대체 뭣 하러 날 보러 온 거냐고, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["“I don’t want...", "\"번역은……"], ["translation,”", "필요 없어.\""], ["muttered Raskolnikov from the stairs.", "라스콜니코프가 계단에서 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Then what the devil do you want?”", "\"그럼 도대체 뭘 원하는 거야?\""], ["shouted Razumihin from above.", "라주미힌이 위에서 소리쳤다."], ["Raskolnikov continued descending the staircase in silence.", "라스콜니코프는 말없이 계단을 계속 내려갔다."]], [["“Hey, there!", "\"이봐, 거기!"], ["Where are you living?”", "어디 사는 거야?\""]], [["No answer.", "대답이 없었다."]], [["“Well, confound you then!”", "\"그래, 그럼 빌어먹어라!\""]], [["But Raskolnikov was already stepping into the street.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 이미 거리로 발을 내딛고 있었다."], ["On the Nikolaevsky Bridge he was roused to full consciousness again by an unpleasant incident.", "니콜라옙스키 다리 위에서 그는 어떤 불쾌한 사건으로 다시금 완전히 정신이 들었다."], ["A coachman, after shouting at him two or three times, gave him a violent lash on the back with his whip, for having almost fallen under his horses’ hoofs.", "한 마부가 그에게 두세 번 소리를 지른 끝에, 그가 하마터면 말발굽 밑에 깔릴 뻔하자, 채찍으로 그의 등을 세차게 후려쳤던 것이다."], ["The lash so infuriated him that he dashed away to the railing (for some unknown reason he had been walking in the very middle of the bridge in the traffic).", "그 채찍질에 그는 격분하여 난간으로 달려갔다(어쩐 영문인지 그는 마차들이 오가는 다리 한복판을 걷고 있었던 것이다)."], ["He angrily clenched and ground his teeth.", "그는 화가 치밀어 이를 악물고 빠득빠득 갈았다."], ["He heard laughter, of course.", "물론 사람들의 웃음소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Serves him right!”", "\"꼴좋다!\""]], [["“A pickpocket I dare say.”", "\"소매치기겠지, 아마.\""]], [["“Pretending to be drunk, for sure, and getting under the wheels on purpose; and you have to answer for him.”", "\"틀림없이 취한 척하다가, 일부러 바퀴 밑으로 기어든 거야. 그러면 자네가 책임을 져야 하잖아.\""]], [["“It’s a regular profession, that’s what it is.”", "\"저게 아주 직업이라니까, 그렇다고.\""]], [["But while he stood at the railing, still looking angry and bewildered after the retreating carriage, and rubbing his back, he suddenly felt someone thrust money into his hand.", "그러나 그가 멀어져 가는 마차 뒤로 여전히 성나고 어리둥절한 표정을 지으며 등을 문지른 채 난간 곁에 서 있을 때, 문득 누군가가 그의 손에 돈을 쥐여 주는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["He looked.", "그는 바라보았다."], ["It was an elderly woman in a kerchief and goatskin shoes, with a girl, probably her daughter, wearing a hat, and carrying a green parasol.", "두건을 쓰고 염소가죽 신을 신은 어느 나이 든 여자였는데, 모자를 쓰고 초록색 양산을 든, 아마 그녀의 딸인 듯한 소녀와 함께였다."]], [["“Take it, my good man, in Christ’s name.”", "\"받으세요, 선량한 양반, 그리스도의 이름으로.\""]], [["He took it and they passed on.", "그가 그것을 받아 들자 그들은 지나갔다."], ["It was a piece of twenty copecks.", "이십 코페이카짜리 동전이었다."], ["From his dress and appearance they might well have taken him for a beggar asking alms in the streets, and the gift of the twenty copecks he doubtless owed to the blow, which made them feel sorry for him.", "그의 옷차림과 행색으로 보아, 그들은 그를 거리에서 동냥하는 거지로 여겼던 모양이고, 이십 코페이카를 적선받은 것은 분명 그가 맞은 채찍질 덕분이었으니, 그것이 그들의 측은한 마음을 자아냈던 것이다."]], [["He closed his hand on the twenty copecks, walked on for ten paces, and turned facing the Neva, looking towards the palace.", "그는 이십 코페이카를 손에 꼭 쥔 채 열 걸음쯤 걷다가, 몸을 돌려 네바강을 향해 궁전 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["The sky was without a cloud and the water was almost bright blue, which is so rare in the Neva.", "하늘에는 구름 한 점 없었고, 네바강에서는 좀처럼 보기 드문, 거의 새파란 빛으로 물이 빛나고 있었다."], ["The cupola of the cathedral, which is seen at its best from the bridge about twenty paces from the chapel, glittered in the sunlight, and in the pure air every ornament on it could be clearly distinguished.", "다리 위에서, 예배당으로부터 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 가장 멋지게 보이는 대성당의 둥근 지붕이 햇빛 속에 반짝였고, 맑은 공기 속에 그 위의 장식 하나하나까지 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다."], ["The pain from the lash went off, and Raskolnikov forgot about it; one uneasy and not quite definite idea occupied him now completely.", "채찍질의 통증은 가셨고, 라스콜니코프는 그것을 잊어버렸다. 이제 불안하고 그리 분명치 않은 하나의 생각이 그를 온통 사로잡았다."], ["He stood still, and gazed long and intently into the distance; this spot was especially familiar to him.", "그는 가만히 멈춰 서서, 오랫동안 뚫어지게 먼 곳을 응시했다. 이 자리는 그에게 유난히 낯익은 곳이었다."], ["When he was attending the university, he had hundreds of times--generally on his way home--stood still on this spot, gazed at this truly magnificent spectacle and almost always marvelled at a vague and mysterious emotion it roused in him.", "대학에 다니던 시절, 그는 수백 번이나 — 대개 집으로 돌아가는 길에 — 바로 이 자리에 가만히 서서, 이 참으로 장엄한 광경을 바라보며, 그것이 자기 안에 불러일으키는 막연하고 신비로운 감정에 거의 언제나 의아해하곤 했다."], ["It left him strangely cold; this gorgeous picture was for him blank and lifeless.", "그것은 그를 묘하게 냉담하게 만들었다. 이 화려한 풍경이 그에게는 공허하고 생기 없는 것이었다."], ["He wondered every time at his sombre and enigmatic impression and, mistrusting himself, put off finding the explanation of it.", "그는 매번 자신의 침울하고 알 수 없는 인상에 의아해하면서도, 스스로를 믿지 못한 채 그 까닭을 밝히기를 미루곤 했다."], ["He vividly recalled those old doubts and perplexities, and it seemed to him that it was no mere chance that he recalled them now.", "그는 그 옛날의 의혹과 곤혹을 생생히 떠올렸고, 지금 자신이 그것들을 떠올린 것이 한낱 우연만은 아닌 듯 여겨졌다."], ["It struck him as strange and grotesque, that he should have stopped at the same spot as before, as though he actually imagined he could think the same thoughts, be interested in the same theories and pictures that had interested him...", "자기가 예전과 똑같은 자리에 멈춰 섰다는 것이, 마치 자기가 정말로 똑같은 생각을 할 수 있고, 그토록…… 얼마 전까지만 해도 자신의 관심을 끌었던 똑같은 이론과 풍경에 흥미를 느낄 수 있다고 상상이라도 하는 듯하여, 그에게는 기이하고 그로테스크하게 느껴졌다."], ["so short a time ago. He felt it almost amusing, and yet it wrung his heart.", "그것이 거의 우습게까지 여겨졌고, 그러면서도 그의 가슴을 쥐어짰다."], ["Deep down, hidden far away out of sight all that seemed to him now--all his old past, his old thoughts, his old problems and theories, his old impressions and that picture and himself and all, all....", "깊은 곳, 시야에서 까마득히 멀리 감추어진 채, 이제 그 모든 것이 그에게는 그렇게 여겨졌다 — 그의 온 지난날, 그의 옛 생각들, 그의 옛 문제들과 이론들, 그의 옛 인상들, 그리고 저 풍경과 자기 자신, 그 모든 것, 그 모든 것이……."], ["He felt as though he were flying upwards, and everything were vanishing from his sight.", "그는 마치 자기가 위로 날아오르고, 모든 것이 시야에서 사라지는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["Making an unconscious movement with his hand, he suddenly became aware of the piece of money in his fist.", "무심코 손을 움직이다가, 그는 문득 주먹 속에 든 그 동전을 의식했다."], ["He opened his hand, stared at the coin, and with a sweep of his arm flung it into the water; then he turned and went home.", "그는 손을 펴서 동전을 물끄러미 바라보다가, 팔을 휘둘러 그것을 물속에 내던졌다. 그러고는 몸을 돌려 집으로 향했다."], ["It seemed to him, he had cut himself off from everyone and from everything at that moment.", "그 순간 그는 자신이 모든 이로부터, 모든 것으로부터 자신을 끊어 낸 듯한 느낌이 들었다."]], [["Evening was coming on when he reached home, so that he must have been walking about six hours.", "집에 다다랐을 때는 저녁이 다가오고 있었으니, 그는 여섯 시간쯤 돌아다녔던 셈이다."], ["How and where he came back he did not remember.", "어떻게, 어느 길로 돌아왔는지 그는 기억하지 못했다."], ["Undressing, and quivering like an overdriven horse, he lay down on the sofa, drew his greatcoat over him, and at once sank into oblivion....", "옷을 벗고, 혹사당한 말처럼 부들부들 떨면서, 그는 소파에 드러누워 외투를 끌어다 덮고는, 이내 망각 속으로 빠져들었다……."]], [["It was dusk when he was waked up by a fearful scream.", "무시무시한 비명에 그가 잠에서 깼을 때는 땅거미가 지고 있었다."], ["Good God, what a scream!", "맙소사, 그 무슨 비명이란 말인가!"], ["Such unnatural sounds, such howling, wailing, grinding, tears, blows and curses he had never heard.", "그토록 부자연스러운 소리들, 그토록 울부짖고 통곡하고 이를 갈고 눈물을 쏟고 후려치고 욕설을 퍼붓는 소리를 그는 일찍이 들어 본 적이 없었다."]], [["He could never have imagined such brutality, such frenzy.", "그런 잔혹함, 그런 광란을 그는 도무지 상상조차 할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["In terror he sat up in bed, almost swooning with agony.", "공포에 질려 그는 침대에서 몸을 일으켰는데, 고통에 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다."], ["But the fighting, wailing and cursing grew louder and louder.", "그러나 싸우고 통곡하고 욕설하는 소리는 점점 더 커져 갔다."], ["And then to his intense amazement he caught the voice of his landlady.", "그러다 더없는 경악 속에서 그는 집주인 여자의 목소리를 알아들었다."], ["She was howling, shrieking and wailing, rapidly, hurriedly, incoherently, so that he could not make out what she was talking about; she was beseeching, no doubt, not to be beaten, for she was being mercilessly beaten on the stairs.", "그녀는 빠르게, 다급하게, 두서없이 울부짖고 비명을 지르고 통곡하고 있어서, 그는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하는지 알아들을 수 없었다. 그녀는 분명 때리지 말아 달라고 애원하고 있었으니, 계단에서 무자비하게 얻어맞고 있었던 것이다."], ["The voice of her assailant was so horrible from spite and rage that it was almost a croak; but he, too, was saying something, and just as quickly and indistinctly, hurrying and spluttering.", "그녀를 때리는 자의 목소리는 악의와 분노로 어찌나 끔찍한지 거의 쉰 소리를 내고 있었으나, 그자 역시 무언가를 지껄이고 있었는데, 마찬가지로 빠르고 알아들을 수 없게, 서두르며 침을 튀기고 있었다."], ["All at once Raskolnikov trembled; he recognised the voice--it was the voice of Ilya Petrovitch.", "별안간 라스콜니코프는 부르르 떨었다. 그 목소리를 알아챘던 것이다 — 그것은 일리야 페트로비치의 목소리였다."], ["Ilya Petrovitch here and beating the landlady!", "일리야 페트로비치가 여기서 집주인 여자를 때리고 있다니!"], ["He is kicking her, banging her head against the steps--that’s clear, that can be told from the sounds, from the cries and the thuds.", "그자가 그녀를 발로 차고, 그녀의 머리를 계단에 짓찧고 있어 — 그건 분명해, 그 소리로, 그 비명과 둔탁한 쿵쿵 소리로 알 수 있어."], ["How is it, is the world topsy-turvy?", "어찌 된 일인가, 세상이 뒤집히기라도 했단 말인가?"], ["He could hear people running in crowds from all the storeys and all the staircases; he heard voices, exclamations, knocking, doors banging.", "그는 모든 층, 모든 계단에서 사람들이 떼 지어 달려 나오는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 목소리들, 외침들, 두드리는 소리, 문이 쾅쾅 닫히는 소리가 들렸다."], ["“But why, why, and how could it be?”", "\"그런데 왜, 왜, 어째서 이런 일이?\""], ["he repeated, thinking seriously that he had gone mad.", "그는 자기가 정말 미쳐 버린 것이라고 진지하게 생각하며 되뇌었다."], ["But no, he heard too distinctly!", "하지만 아니야, 너무도 또렷이 들리잖아!"], ["And they would come to him then next, “for no doubt...", "그러면 다음엔 그자들이 그에게 올 것이었다, \"왜냐하면 분명……"], ["it’s all about that...", "모두 그 일 때문이야……"], ["about yesterday....", "어제 일 때문이라고……"], ["Good God!”", "맙소사!\""], ["He would have fastened his door with the latch, but he could not lift his hand...", "그는 빗장을 걸어 문을 잠그고 싶었으나, 손을 들 수가 없었다……"], ["besides, it would be useless.", "게다가 그래 봐야 소용없을 터였다."], ["Terror gripped his heart like ice, tortured him and numbed him....", "공포가 얼음처럼 그의 심장을 옥죄어, 그를 괴롭히고 마비시켰다……."], ["But at last all this uproar, after continuing about ten minutes, began gradually to subside.", "그러나 마침내 이 모든 소동은, 십 분쯤 이어진 끝에 차츰 가라앉기 시작했다."], ["The landlady was moaning and groaning; Ilya Petrovitch was still uttering threats and curses....", "집주인 여자는 끙끙 신음하고 있었다. 일리야 페트로비치는 여전히 위협과 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었다……."], ["But at last he, too, seemed to be silent, and now he could not be heard.", "그러나 마침내 그자도 잠잠해진 듯했고, 이제 그의 목소리는 들리지 않았다."], ["“Can he have gone away?", "\"가 버린 걸까?"], ["Good Lord!”", "오, 하느님!\""], ["Yes, and now the landlady is going too, still weeping and moaning...", "그래, 이제 집주인 여자도 가는군, 여전히 흐느끼고 신음하면서……"], ["and then her door slammed....", "그러더니 그녀의 방문이 쾅 닫혔다……."], ["Now the crowd was going from the stairs to their rooms, exclaiming, disputing, calling to one another, raising their voices to a shout, dropping them to a whisper.", "이제 사람들이 계단에서 저마다 자기 방으로 돌아가며, 소리치고, 다투고, 서로를 부르고, 목소리를 고함으로 높였다가 속삭임으로 낮추곤 했다."], ["There must have been numbers of them--almost all the inmates of the block.", "분명 수가 많았을 것이다 — 이 건물에 사는 거의 모든 사람들이었으리라."], ["“But, good God, how could it be!", "\"그런데, 맙소사, 어떻게 이런 일이!"], ["And why, why had he come here!”", "그리고 왜, 왜 그자가 여기 왔단 말인가!\""]], [["Raskolnikov sank worn out on the sofa, but could not close his eyes.", "라스콜니코프는 기진맥진하여 소파에 쓰러졌으나, 눈을 감을 수가 없었다."], ["He lay for half an hour in such anguish, such an intolerable sensation of infinite terror as he had never experienced before.", "그는 일찍이 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 그런 고뇌 속에서, 끝 모를 공포의 그토록 견딜 수 없는 감각 속에서 삼십 분가량 누워 있었다."], ["Suddenly a bright light flashed into his room.", "갑자기 밝은 빛이 그의 방으로 확 비쳐 들었다."], ["Nastasya came in with a candle and a plate of soup.", "나스타시야가 촛불과 수프 한 접시를 들고 들어왔다."], ["Looking at him carefully and ascertaining that he was not asleep, she set the candle on the table and began to lay out what she had brought--bread, salt, a plate, a spoon.", "그녀는 그를 유심히 살펴 그가 잠들지 않은 것을 확인하고는, 촛불을 탁자 위에 내려놓고 가져온 것들을 차려 놓기 시작했다 — 빵, 소금, 접시, 숟가락."]], [["“You’ve eaten nothing since yesterday, I warrant.", "\"어제부터 아무것도 안 먹었지, 틀림없이."], ["You’ve been trudging about all day, and you’re shaking with fever.”", "온종일 쏘다니더니, 열까지 나서 덜덜 떨고 있구먼.\""]], [["“Nastasya...", "\"나스타시야……"], ["what were they beating the landlady for?”", "사람들이 왜 집주인 여자를 때린 거지?\""]], [["She looked intently at him.", "그녀가 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Who beat the landlady?”", "\"누가 집주인 여자를 때렸다고?\""]], [["“Just now...", "\"방금……"], ["half an hour ago, Ilya Petrovitch, the assistant superintendent, on the stairs....", "삼십 분 전에, 일리야 페트로비치, 부서장 보좌가, 계단에서……."], ["Why was he ill-treating her like that, and...", "왜 그자가 그녀를 그렇게 학대한 거지, 그리고……"], ["why was he here?”", "왜 그자가 여기 왔지?\""]], [["Nastasya scrutinised him, silent and frowning, and her scrutiny lasted a long time.", "나스타시야는 말없이 이맛살을 찌푸린 채 그를 뜯어보았고, 그 뜯어보는 눈길은 오래도록 이어졌다."], ["He felt uneasy, even frightened at her searching eyes.", "그는 그녀의 캐묻는 듯한 눈초리에 불안해졌고, 심지어 겁이 났다."]], [["“Nastasya, why don’t you speak?”", "\"나스타시야, 왜 말이 없어?\""], ["he said timidly at last in a weak voice.", "마침내 그가 기어드는 목소리로 머뭇머뭇 말했다."]], [["“It’s the blood,”", "\"피야.\""], ["she answered at last softly, as though speaking to herself.", "마침내 그녀가, 마치 혼잣말을 하듯 나직이 대답했다."]], [["“Blood?", "\"피?"], ["What blood?”", "무슨 피?\""], ["he muttered, growing white and turning towards the wall.", "그가 새파랗게 질려 벽 쪽으로 돌아누우며 중얼거렸다."]], [["Nastasya still looked at him without speaking.", "나스타시야는 여전히 말없이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Nobody has been beating the landlady,”", "\"아무도 집주인 여자를 안 때렸어.\""], ["she declared at last in a firm, resolute voice.", "마침내 그녀가 단호하고 결연한 목소리로 잘라 말했다."]], [["He gazed at her, hardly able to breathe.", "그는 거의 숨도 쉬지 못한 채 그녀를 응시했다."]], [["“I heard it myself....", "\"내가 직접 들었는걸……"], ["I was not asleep...", "난 잠들지 않았어……"], ["I was sitting up,”", "일어나 앉아 있었다고.\""], ["he said still more timidly.", "그가 한층 더 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“I listened a long while.", "\"오랫동안 귀를 기울였어."], ["The assistant superintendent came....", "부서장 보좌가 왔어……."], ["Everyone ran out on to the stairs from all the flats.”", "모든 집에서 사람들이 계단으로 뛰쳐나왔다고.\""]], [["“No one has been here.", "\"여긴 아무도 안 왔어."], ["That’s the blood crying in your ears.", "그건 댁의 귓속에서 피가 울부짖는 거야."], ["When there’s no outlet for it and it gets clotted, you begin fancying things....", "피가 빠져나갈 데가 없어서 엉겨 붙으면, 자꾸 헛것이 보이기 시작하는 거지……."], ["Will you eat something?”", "뭐 좀 먹겠어?\""]], [["He made no answer.", "그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Nastasya still stood over him, watching him.", "나스타시야는 여전히 그를 굽어보며 지켜보고 서 있었다."]], [["“Give me something to drink...", "\"마실 것 좀 줘……"], ["Nastasya.”", "나스타시야.\""]], [["She went downstairs and returned with a white earthenware jug of water.", "그녀는 아래층으로 내려가 흰 질그릇 물병에 물을 담아 돌아왔다."], ["He remembered only swallowing one sip of the cold water and spilling some on his neck.", "그는 차가운 물을 한 모금 삼키고 목에 조금 흘렸던 것만을 기억했다."], ["Then followed forgetfulness.", "그다음엔 망각이 뒤따랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["He was not completely unconscious, however, all the time he was ill; he was in a feverish state, sometimes delirious, sometimes half conscious.", "그렇지만 그는 앓는 내내 완전히 의식을 잃고 있었던 것은 아니었다. 열에 들떠 있었고, 때로는 헛소리를 했으며, 때로는 반쯤 정신이 들어 있었다."], ["He remembered a great deal afterwards.", "그는 나중에 많은 것을 기억해 냈다."], ["Sometimes it seemed as though there were a number of people round him; they wanted to take him away somewhere, there was a great deal of squabbling and discussing about him.", "어떤 때는 여러 사람이 자기 주위에 둘러서 있는 것 같았다. 그들은 그를 어딘가로 데려가려 했고, 그를 두고 한바탕 옥신각신하며 논쟁을 벌이고 있었다."], ["Then he would be alone in the room; they had all gone away afraid of him, and only now and then opened the door a crack to look at him; they threatened him, plotted something together, laughed, and mocked at him.", "그러다가 그는 방 안에 홀로 남겨졌다. 다들 그를 무서워하며 가 버렸고, 이따금 문틈을 빠끔히 열어 그를 들여다볼 뿐이었다."], ["He remembered Nastasya often at his bedside; he distinguished another person, too, whom he seemed to know very well, though he could not remember who he was, and this fretted him, even made him cry.", "그들은 그를 위협하고, 무언가 함께 모의하고, 웃어 대며, 그를 비웃었다. 그는 나스타시야가 자주 자기 머리맡에 있었던 것을 기억했고, 또 다른 사람 하나도 분간해 냈는데, 아주 잘 아는 사람인 것 같으면서도 그가 누구인지 도무지 기억나지 않아 마음이 안달났고, 심지어 울음이 나올 지경이었다."], ["Sometimes he fancied he had been lying there a month; at other times it all seemed part of the same day.", "어떤 때는 자신이 거기 한 달이나 누워 있었던 것 같았고, 또 어떤 때는 그 모든 일이 같은 하루의 일부분처럼 느껴졌다."], ["But of _that_--of _that_ he had no recollection, and yet every minute he felt that he had forgotten something he ought to remember.", "그러나 그것에 대해서는 — 그것에 대해서는 — 아무런 기억이 없었지만, 그럼에도 매 순간 그는 자신이 마땅히 기억해야 할 무언가를 잊어버렸다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He worried and tormented himself trying to remember, moaned, flew into a rage, or sank into awful, intolerable terror.", "그는 기억해 내려 애쓰며 안달하고 스스로를 괴롭혔고, 신음하고, 격분에 휩싸이거나, 끔찍하고 견딜 수 없는 공포 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["Then he struggled to get up, would have run away, but someone always prevented him by force, and he sank back into impotence and forgetfulness.", "그러다가 일어나려고 발버둥 쳐 달아나려 했지만, 늘 누군가가 힘으로 그를 가로막았고, 그는 다시 무기력과 망각 속으로 주저앉았다."], ["At last he returned to complete consciousness.", "마침내 그는 완전히 의식을 되찾았다."]], [["It happened at ten o’clock in the morning.", "그 일은 아침 열 시에 일어났다."], ["On fine days the sun shone into the room at that hour, throwing a streak of light on the right wall and the corner near the door.", "날이 맑은 날이면 그 시각에 해가 방 안으로 비쳐 들어와, 오른쪽 벽과 문 가까운 구석에 한 줄기 빛을 던지곤 했다."], ["Nastasya was standing beside him with another person, a complete stranger, who was looking at him very inquisitively.", "나스타시야가 그의 곁에, 또 다른 사람 하나와 함께 서 있었는데, 그 사람은 전혀 모르는 자로, 무척이나 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보고 있었다."], ["He was a young man with a beard, wearing a full, short-waisted coat, and looked like a messenger.", "그는 수염이 난 젊은 사내로, 품이 넉넉하고 허리가 짧은 외투를 걸치고 있었고, 심부름꾼처럼 보였다."], ["The landlady was peeping in at the half-opened door.", "집주인 여자는 반쯤 열린 문틈으로 안을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov sat up.", "라스콜니코프는 몸을 일으켜 앉았다."]], [["“Who is this, Nastasya?”", "\"이 사람은 누구야, 나스타시야?\""], ["he asked, pointing to the young man.", "그는 젊은 사내를 가리키며 물었다."]], [["“I say, he’s himself again!”", "\"아니, 정신이 돌아왔네!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“He is himself,”", "\"정신이 돌아왔습니다.\""], ["echoed the man.", "사내가 따라 말했다."]], [["Concluding that he had returned to his senses, the landlady closed the door and disappeared.", "그가 제정신을 되찾았다고 결론짓자, 집주인 여자는 문을 닫고 사라졌다."], ["She was always shy and dreaded conversations or discussions.", "그녀는 늘 수줍어했고 대화나 논쟁을 두려워했다."], ["She was a woman of forty, not at all bad-looking, fat and buxom, with black eyes and eyebrows, good-natured from fatness and laziness, and absurdly bashful.", "마흔쯤 된 여자로, 결코 못생기지 않았으며, 살집이 있고 풍만했고, 검은 눈과 검은 눈썹을 지녔는데, 살이 찌고 게을러서 마음이 좋았고, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 수줍음을 탔다."]], [["“Who...", "\"당신은……"], ["are you?”", "누구요?\""], ["he went on, addressing the man.", "그가 사내를 향해 말을 이었다."], ["But at that moment the door was flung open, and, stooping a little, as he was so tall, Razumihin came in.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 문이 활짝 열리며, 키가 워낙 커서 몸을 약간 숙인 채로 라주미힌이 들어왔다."]], [["“What a cabin it is!”", "\"이게 무슨 오두막이야!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“I am always knocking my head.", "\"나는 늘 여기서 머리를 박는다니까."], ["You call this a lodging!", "자네는 이런 데를 하숙이라고 부르나!"], ["So you are conscious, brother?", "그래, 정신이 들었나, 이 사람아?"], ["I’ve just heard the news from Pashenka.”", "방금 파셴카한테서 소식을 들었네.\""]], [["“He has just come to,”", "\"방금 막 정신이 돌아왔어요.\""], ["said Nastasya.", "나스타시야가 말했다."]], [["“Just come to,”", "\"방금 정신이 돌아왔습니다.\""], ["echoed the man again, with a smile.", "사내가 미소를 지으며 또 따라 말했다."]], [["“And who are you?”", "\"그런데 당신은 누구요?\""], ["Razumihin asked, suddenly addressing him.", "라주미힌이 불쑥 그에게 말을 걸었다. \"내 이름은 브라주미힌이오, 잘 부탁하오."], ["“My name is Vrazumihin, at your service; not Razumihin, as I am always called, but Vrazumihin, a student and gentleman; and he is my friend.", "다들 나를 라주미힌이라 부르지만, 브라주미힌이오, 대학생이자 신사지. 그리고 이 사람은 내 친구요."], ["And who are you?”", "그래, 당신은 누구요?\""]], [["“I am the messenger from our office, from the merchant Shelopaev, and I’ve come on business.”", "\"저는 저희 사무소에서 온 심부름꾼입니다, 셸로파예프 상인 어른 댁에서요, 볼일이 있어 왔습니다.\""]], [["“Please sit down.”", "\"앉으시오.\""], ["Razumihin seated himself on the other side of the table.", "라주미힌은 탁자 맞은편에 자리를 잡았다."], ["“It’s a good thing you’ve come to, brother,”", "\"자네가 정신을 차린 건 잘된 일이야, 이 사람아.\""], ["he went on to Raskolnikov.", "그는 라스콜니코프를 향해 말을 이었다."], ["“For the last four days you have scarcely eaten or drunk anything.", "\"지난 나흘 동안 자네는 거의 먹지도 마시지도 못했다네."], ["We had to give you tea in spoonfuls.", "차를 숟가락으로 떠 넣어 줘야 했어."], ["I brought Zossimov to see you twice.", "조시모프를 두 번이나 데려와 자네를 봐 달라고 했네."], ["You remember Zossimov?", "조시모프 기억나나?"], ["He examined you carefully and said at once it was nothing serious--something seemed to have gone to your head.", "그가 자네를 꼼꼼히 진찰하더니 대뜸 별일 아니라고, 머리에 뭔가 좀 올라간 것 같다고 하더군. 잘 못 먹어서 생긴 신경성 헛소리라는 거야."], ["Some nervous nonsense, the result of bad feeding, he says you have not had enough beer and radish, but it’s nothing much, it will pass and you will be all right.", "맥주랑 무를 충분히 못 먹어서 그렇다더군. 하지만 대단한 건 아니고, 곧 지나가서 멀쩡해질 거라는 거야."], ["Zossimov is a first-rate fellow!", "조시모프는 일류 의사라네!"], ["He is making quite a name.", "이름깨나 날리고 있지."], ["Come, I won’t keep you,”", "자, 더는 붙잡지 않으리다.\""], ["he said, addressing the man again.", "그는 다시 사내를 향해 말했다."], ["“Will you explain what you want?", "\"당신이 원하는 게 뭔지 설명해 주겠소?"], ["You must know, Rodya, this is the second time they have sent from the office; but it was another man last time, and I talked to him.", "자네도 알아 둬야 하는데, 로쟈, 사무소에서 사람을 보낸 게 이번이 두 번째라네. 다만 지난번엔 다른 사람이었고, 내가 그 사람과 얘기를 나눴지."], ["Who was it came before?”", "지난번에 온 게 누구였소?\""]], [["“That was the day before yesterday, I venture to say, if you please, sir.", "\"그저께였습니다, 외람되지만 말씀드리자면요, 나리."], ["That was Alexey Semyonovitch; he is in our office, too.”", "알렉세이 세묘노비치였습니다. 그 사람도 저희 사무소에 있지요.\""]], [["“He was more intelligent than you, don’t you think so?”", "\"그 사람이 당신보다 더 똑똑하던데, 그렇게 생각하지 않소?\""]], [["“Yes, indeed, sir, he is of more weight than I am.”", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 나리, 그 사람이 저보다 더 무게가 있지요.\""]], [["“Quite so; go on.”", "\"바로 그렇소. 자, 계속하시오.\""]], [["“At your mamma’s request, through Afanasy Ivanovitch Vahrushin, of whom I presume you have heard more than once, a remittance is sent to you from our office,”", "\"댁의 어머님 부탁으로, 아파나시 이바노비치 바흐루신 씨를 통해서요 — 그분에 대해선 한두 번쯤 들으셨으리라 생각합니다만 — 저희 사무소에서 송금이 보내졌습니다.\""], ["the man began, addressing Raskolnikov.", "사내가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“If you are in an intelligible condition, I’ve thirty-five roubles to remit to you, as Semyon Semyonovitch has received from Afanasy Ivanovitch at your mamma’s request instructions to that effect, as on previous occasions.", "\"알아들으실 만한 상태이시라면, 댁에게 전해 드릴 삼십오 루블이 있습니다. 세묜 세묘노비치가 댁의 어머님 부탁에 따라 아파나시 이바노비치한테서 그런 지시를 받았기 때문이지요, 지난번에도 그랬듯이 말입니다."], ["Do you know him, sir?”", "그분을 아십니까, 나리?\""]], [["“Yes, I remember...", "\"네, 기억합니다……"], ["Vahrushin,”", "바흐루신.\""], ["Raskolnikov said dreamily.", "라스콜니코프가 꿈결처럼 말했다."]], [["“You hear, he knows Vahrushin,”", "\"들었나, 바흐루신을 안다는군!\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."], ["“He is in ‘an intelligible condition’!", "\"'알아들을 만한 상태'라는 거지!"], ["And I see you are an intelligent man too.", "그리고 보아하니 당신도 똑똑한 사람이로군."], ["Well, it’s always pleasant to hear words of wisdom.”", "음, 지혜로운 말은 언제 들어도 기분이 좋단 말이야.\""]], [["“That’s the gentleman, Vahrushin, Afanasy Ivanovitch.", "\"그분이 바로 그 어른이십니다, 바흐루신, 아파나시 이바노비치요."], ["And at the request of your mamma, who has sent you a remittance once before in the same manner through him, he did not refuse this time also, and sent instructions to Semyon Semyonovitch some days since to hand you thirty-five roubles in the hope of better to come.”", "그리고 댁의 어머님 부탁으로 — 어머님은 전에도 같은 방식으로 그분을 통해 한 번 송금을 보내셨지요 — 이번에도 거절하지 않으시고, 며칠 전 세묜 세묘노비치에게 삼십오 루블을 댁에게 전해 드리라는 지시를 보내셨습니다, 앞으로 더 나아지기를 바라며 말입니다.\""]], [["“That ‘hoping for better to come’ is the best thing you’ve said, though ‘your mamma’ is not bad either.", "\"그 '앞으로 더 나아지기를 바란다'는 게 당신이 한 말 중에 최고로군, 하긴 '댁의 어머님'도 나쁘지 않았어."], ["Come then, what do you say?", "자, 그래서, 당신 의견은 어떻소?"], ["Is he fully conscious, eh?”", "이 사람이 완전히 정신이 든 것 같소, 응?\""]], [["“That’s all right.", "\"괜찮습니다."], ["If only he can sign this little paper.”", "이 작은 서류에 서명만 하실 수 있으면 됩니다.\""]], [["“He can scrawl his name.", "\"제 이름 정도는 휘갈길 수 있소."], ["Have you got the book?”", "장부 가져왔소?\""]], [["“Yes, here’s the book.”", "\"네, 여기 장부 있습니다.\""]], [["“Give it to me.", "\"이리 주시오."], ["Here, Rodya, sit up.", "자, 로쟈, 일어나 앉게."], ["I’ll hold you.", "내가 잡아 주지."], ["Take the pen and scribble ‘Raskolnikov’ for him.", "펜을 들고 '라스콜니코프'라고 휘갈겨 주게."], ["For just now, brother, money is sweeter to us than treacle.”", "지금 우리한테는, 이 사람아, 돈이 당밀보다도 더 달콤하다네.\""]], [["“I don’t want it,”", "\"그건 필요 없어요.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, pushing away the pen.", "라스콜니코프가 펜을 밀쳐 내며 말했다."]], [["“Not want it?”", "\"필요 없다고?\""]], [["“I won’t sign it.”", "\"서명 안 하겠어요.\""]], [["“How the devil can you do without signing it?”", "\"아니, 도대체 서명을 안 하면 어쩌란 말인가?\""]], [["“I don’t want...", "\"그 돈은……"], ["the money.”", "필요 없어요.\""]], [["“Don’t want the money!", "\"돈이 필요 없다니!"], ["Come, brother, that’s nonsense, I bear witness.", "이봐, 이 사람아, 그건 말도 안 되는 소리야, 내가 증인이라네."], ["Don’t trouble, please, it’s only that he is on his travels again.", "부디 신경 쓰지 마시오, 그저 또 헛것을 보고 있을 뿐이니까."], ["But that’s pretty common with him at all times though....", "하긴 이 친구한테는 늘 흔한 일이오……"], ["You are a man of judgment and we will take him in hand, that is, more simply, take his hand and he will sign it.", "당신은 분별 있는 사람이니, 우리가 이 친구를 다뤄 봅시다. 그러니까, 더 간단히 말해, 손을 잡아 주면 서명을 할 거요."], ["Here.”", "자.\""]], [["“But I can come another time.”", "\"하지만 다음에 다시 와도 됩니다.\""]], [["“No, no.", "\"아니, 아니."], ["Why should we trouble you?", "뭐하러 당신을 또 번거롭게 하겠소?"], ["You are a man of judgment....", "당신은 분별 있는 사람이니……"], ["Now, Rodya, don’t keep your visitor, you see he is waiting,”", "자, 로쟈, 손님을 기다리게 하지 마, 봐, 기다리고 있잖나.\""], ["and he made ready to hold Raskolnikov’s hand in earnest.", "그러고는 정말로 라스콜니코프의 손을 잡으려고 채비했다."]], [["“Stop, I’ll do it alone,”", "\"그만, 내가 혼자 하겠어요.\""], ["said the latter, taking the pen and signing his name.", "라스콜니코프는 펜을 받아 들며 자기 이름을 서명했다."]], [["The messenger took out the money and went away.", "심부름꾼은 돈을 꺼내 놓고 가 버렸다."]], [["“Bravo!", "\"훌륭해!"], ["And now, brother, are you hungry?”", "자, 이제, 이 사람아, 배고픈가?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."]], [["“Is there any soup?”", "\"수프 좀 있나?\""]], [["“Some of yesterday’s,”", "\"어제 끓인 게 좀 있어요.\""], ["answered Nastasya, who was still standing there.", "여전히 거기 서 있던 나스타시야가 대답했다."]], [["“With potatoes and rice in it?”", "\"감자랑 쌀이 든 거?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I know it by heart.", "\"내가 그 맛을 훤히 알지."], ["Bring soup and give us some tea.”", "수프 가져오고, 우리한테 차도 좀 주게.\""]], [["“Very well.”", "\"알겠어요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov looked at all this with profound astonishment and a dull, unreasoning terror.", "라스콜니코프는 이 모든 것을 깊은 놀라움과 무겁고 까닭 모를 공포에 휩싸여 바라보았다."], ["He made up his mind to keep quiet and see what would happen.", "그는 잠자코 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 지켜보기로 마음먹었다."], ["“I believe I am not wandering.", "'내가 헛것을 보는 게 아닌 것 같아."], ["I believe it’s reality,”", "이건 현실인 것 같아.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["In a couple of minutes Nastasya returned with the soup, and announced that the tea would be ready directly.", "이삼 분 만에 나스타시야가 수프를 들고 돌아와, 차도 곧 준비될 거라고 알렸다."], ["With the soup she brought two spoons, two plates, salt, pepper, mustard for the beef, and so on.", "수프와 함께 그녀는 숟가락 두 개, 접시 두 개, 소금, 후추, 쇠고기에 곁들일 겨자 따위를 가져왔다."], ["The table was set as it had not been for a long time.", "식탁이 오랜만에 제대로 차려졌다."], ["The cloth was clean.", "식탁보도 깨끗했다."]], [["“It would not be amiss, Nastasya, if Praskovya Pavlovna were to send us up a couple of bottles of beer.", "\"나스타시야, 프라스코비야 파블로브나가 우리한테 맥주 두어 병만 올려 보내 주면 나쁘지 않을 텐데."], ["We could empty them.”", "우리가 비워 줄 수 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“Well, you are a cool hand,”", "\"흥, 참 뻔뻔하기도 하네.\""], ["muttered Nastasya, and she departed to carry out his orders.", "나스타시야가 중얼거리더니, 그의 분부를 따르러 자리를 떴다."]], [["Raskolnikov still gazed wildly with strained attention.", "라스콜니코프는 여전히 잔뜩 긴장한 채로 정신없이 응시하고 있었다."], ["Meanwhile Razumihin sat down on the sofa beside him, as clumsily as a bear put his left arm round Raskolnikov’s head, although he was able to sit up, and with his right hand gave him a spoonful of soup, blowing on it that it might not burn him.", "그러는 동안 라주미힌이 그의 곁 소파에 앉았는데, 곰처럼 어설프게 왼팔로 라스콜니코프의 머리를 감싸 안았다."], ["But the soup was only just warm.", "라스콜니코프는 혼자 일어나 앉을 수 있었는데도 말이다."], ["Raskolnikov swallowed one spoonful greedily, then a second, then a third.", "그러고는 오른손으로 수프를 한 숟가락 떠서, 데지 않도록 후후 불어 가며 먹여 주었다. 하지만 수프는 겨우 미지근할 뿐이었다."], ["But after giving him a few more spoonfuls of soup, Razumihin suddenly stopped, and said that he must ask Zossimov whether he ought to have more.", "라스콜니코프는 한 숟가락을 게걸스럽게 삼켰고, 이어 두 숟가락, 세 숟가락을 삼켰다. 그러나 몇 숟가락을 더 떠먹여 준 뒤, 라주미힌은 갑자기 멈추더니, 더 먹여도 되는지 조시모프에게 물어봐야겠다고 말했다."]], [["Nastasya came in with two bottles of beer.", "나스타시야가 맥주 두 병을 들고 들어왔다."]], [["“And will you have tea?”", "\"차도 드릴까요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“Cut along, Nastasya, and bring some tea, for tea we may venture on without the faculty.", "\"빨리 가서, 나스타시야, 차 좀 가져오게. 차야 의사 양반 없이도 마셔 봄직하니까."], ["But here is the beer!”", "그런데 맥주가 여기 있군!\""], ["He moved back to his chair, pulled the soup and meat in front of him, and began eating as though he had not touched food for three days.", "그는 자기 의자로 돌아가 수프와 고기를 앞으로 끌어당기고는, 사흘은 굶은 사람처럼 먹기 시작했다."]], [["“I must tell you, Rodya, I dine like this here every day now,”", "\"내가 말해 둘 게 있는데, 로쟈, 요즘 나는 매일 여기서 이렇게 식사를 한다네.\""], ["he mumbled with his mouth full of beef, “and it’s all Pashenka, your dear little landlady, who sees to that; she loves to do anything for me.", "그는 쇠고기를 입에 가득 문 채 우물거렸다. \"그게 다 파셴카, 자네의 사랑스러운 집주인 아주머니가 챙겨 주는 덕분이지."], ["I don’t ask for it, but, of course, I don’t object.", "그 여자는 날 위해 뭐든 해 주는 걸 좋아해. 내가 부탁하는 것도 아닌데, 물론 마다하지도 않지."], ["And here’s Nastasya with the tea.", "그리고 여기 나스타시야가 차를 가져왔군."], ["She is a quick girl.", "손이 빠른 처녀야."], ["Nastasya, my dear, won’t you have some beer?”", "나스타시야, 우리 아가씨, 맥주 한잔 안 하겠나?\""]], [["“Get along with your nonsense!”", "\"그런 헛소리는 집어치워요!\""]], [["“A cup of tea, then?”", "\"그럼 차 한 잔은?\""]], [["“A cup of tea, maybe.”", "\"차 한 잔은 뭐, 좋아요.\""]], [["“Pour it out.", "\"따라 주게."], ["Stay, I’ll pour it out myself.", "가만, 내가 직접 따르지."], ["Sit down.”", "앉게나.\""]], [["He poured out two cups, left his dinner, and sat on the sofa again.", "그는 두 잔을 따르고는, 식사를 내버려 둔 채 다시 소파에 앉았다."], ["As before, he put his left arm round the sick man’s head, raised him up and gave him tea in spoonfuls, again blowing each spoonful steadily and earnestly, as though this process was the principal and most effective means towards his friend’s recovery.", "아까처럼 왼팔로 병자의 머리를 감싸 안아 일으키고는, 한 숟가락씩 차를 먹여 주었는데, 또다시 매 숟가락마다 한결같이 진지하게 후후 불어 주었으니, 마치 이 과정이 친구를 회복시키는 가장 중요하고 효과적인 수단이라도 되는 듯했다."], ["Raskolnikov said nothing and made no resistance, though he felt quite strong enough to sit up on the sofa without support and could not merely have held a cup or a spoon, but even perhaps could have walked about.", "라스콜니코프는 아무 말도 하지 않았고 저항도 하지 않았지만, 사실은 받쳐 주지 않아도 혼자서 소파에 충분히 일어나 앉을 만큼, 단지 잔이나 숟가락을 쥘 수 있을 뿐 아니라 어쩌면 돌아다닐 수도 있을 만큼 기운이 있었다."], ["But from some queer, almost animal, cunning he conceived the idea of hiding his strength and lying low for a time, pretending if necessary not to be yet in full possession of his faculties, and meanwhile listening to find out what was going on.", "그러나 어떤 묘한, 거의 짐승 같은 교활함에서 그는 잠시 자기 힘을 숨기고 가만히 엎드려 있자는, 필요하다면 아직 제정신을 온전히 차리지 못한 척하면서, 그러는 사이에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 귀 기울여 알아내자는 생각을 품었다."], ["Yet he could not overcome his sense of repugnance.", "그렇지만 그는 자신을 사로잡은 혐오감을 극복할 수 없었다."], ["After sipping a dozen spoonfuls of tea, he suddenly released his head, pushed the spoon away capriciously, and sank back on the pillow.", "차를 열 숟가락쯤 홀짝인 뒤, 그는 갑자기 머리를 빼내고는, 변덕스레 숟가락을 밀쳐 내고, 베개에 도로 쓰러졌다."], ["There were actually real pillows under his head now, down pillows in clean cases, he observed that, too, and took note of it.", "이제 그의 머리 밑에는 진짜 베개가, 깨끗한 잇을 씌운 깃털 베개가 놓여 있었으니, 그는 그것도 알아챘고 눈여겨보았다."]], [["“Pashenka must give us some raspberry jam to-day to make him some raspberry tea,”", "\"파셴카가 오늘 우리한테 산딸기 잼을 좀 줘야겠어, 산딸기 차를 만들어 먹이게.\""], ["said Razumihin, going back to his chair and attacking his soup and beer again.", "라주미힌은 자기 의자로 돌아가 다시 수프와 맥주에 달려들며 말했다."]], [["“And where is she to get raspberries for you?”", "\"그 여자가 당신 줄 산딸기를 어디서 구한대요?\""], ["asked Nastasya, balancing a saucer on her five outspread fingers and sipping tea through a lump of sugar.", "나스타시야가 다섯 손가락을 쫙 펴서 그 위에 받침 접시를 받쳐 들고, 각설탕을 입에 문 채 차를 홀짝이며 물었다."]], [["“She’ll get it at the shop, my dear.", "\"가게에서 구하겠지, 이 사람아."], ["You see, Rodya, all sorts of things have been happening while you have been laid up.", "그게, 로쟈, 자네가 앓아누워 있는 동안 별의별 일이 다 있었다네."], ["When you decamped in that rascally way without leaving your address, I felt so angry that I resolved to find you out and punish you.", "자네가 그렇게 못된 식으로 주소도 안 남기고 줄행랑을 쳤을 때, 내가 어찌나 화가 나던지 자넬 기어이 찾아내서 혼쭐을 내 주기로 결심했지."], ["I set to work that very day.", "바로 그날부터 나섰어."], ["How I ran about making inquiries for you!", "자넬 찾아 수소문하느라 얼마나 쏘다녔던지!"], ["This lodging of yours I had forgotten, though I never remembered it, indeed, because I did not know it; and as for your old lodgings, I could only remember it was at the Five Corners, Harlamov’s house.", "이 하숙집은 까맣게 잊고 있었네, 하긴 애초에 몰랐으니 기억할 것도 없었지. 게다가 자네 옛날 하숙집은, 다섯 모퉁이에 있는 하를라모프네 집이었다는 것밖에 기억이 안 나더군."], ["I kept trying to find that Harlamov’s house, and afterwards it turned out that it was not Harlamov’s, but Buch’s.", "그 하를라모프네 집을 줄곧 찾아다녔는데, 나중에 보니 그게 하를라모프네가 아니라 부흐네 집이지 뭔가!"], ["How one muddles up sound sometimes!", "사람이 가끔 소리를 어찌나 헷갈리는지!"], ["So I lost my temper, and I went on the chance to the address bureau next day, and only fancy, in two minutes they looked you up!", "그래서 나는 화가 나서, 이튿날 혹시나 하고 주소 관리소에 갔는데, 글쎄, 단 이 분 만에 자넬 찾아내지 뭔가!"], ["Your name is down there.”", "자네 이름이 거기 올라 있더라고.\""]], [["“My name!”", "\"내 이름이!\""]], [["“I should think so; and yet a General Kobelev they could not find while I was there.", "\"그렇고말고. 그런데 내가 거기 있는 동안 코벨레프 장군은 못 찾아내더군."], ["Well, it’s a long story.", "뭐, 긴 얘기지."], ["But as soon as I did land on this place, I soon got to know all your affairs--all, all, brother, I know everything; Nastasya here will tell you.", "어쨌든 이곳을 찾아내자마자, 나는 곧 자네 일을 죄다 알게 됐어 — 전부, 전부 다, 이 사람아, 내가 다 안다네."], ["I made the acquaintance of Nikodim Fomitch and Ilya Petrovitch, and the house-porter and Mr.", "여기 나스타시야가 자네한테 말해 줄 거야."], ["Zametov, Alexandr Grigorievitch, the head clerk in the police office, and, last, but not least, of Pashenka; Nastasya here knows....”", "나는 니코딤 포미치와 일리야 페트로비치, 그리고 문지기와, 자메토프 씨, 알렉산드르 그리고리예비치 — 경찰서 서기장 말이야 — 와도 알게 됐고, 마지막으로 결코 빼놓을 수 없는 파셴카와도 알게 됐지. 여기 나스타시야가 알아…….\""]], [["“He’s got round her,”", "\"이이가 그 여자를 구워삶았다니까요.\""], ["Nastasya murmured, smiling slyly.", "나스타시야가 능청맞게 웃으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Why don’t you put the sugar in your tea, Nastasya Nikiforovna?”", "\"자네는 어째서 차에 설탕을 안 넣나, 나스타시야 니키포로브나?\""]], [["“You are a one!”", "\"별사람 다 보겠네!\""], ["Nastasya cried suddenly, going off into a giggle.", "나스타시야가 갑자기 까르르 웃음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["“I am not Nikiforovna, but Petrovna,”", "\"나는 니키포로브나가 아니라 페트로브나예요.\""], ["she added suddenly, recovering from her mirth.", "그녀는 웃음을 가라앉히며 불쑥 덧붙였다."]], [["“I’ll make a note of it.", "\"잘 새겨 두지."], ["Well, brother, to make a long story short, I was going in for a regular explosion here to uproot all malignant influences in the locality, but Pashenka won the day.", "자, 이 사람아, 짧게 줄여 말하자면, 나는 여기서 한바탕 폭발이라도 일으켜 이 동네의 온갖 못된 기운을 죄다 뿌리 뽑을 작정이었는데, 파셴카가 이기더란 말이지. 나는, 이 사람아, 그 여자가 그렇게……"], ["I had not expected, brother, to find her so...", "사람을 끄는 데가 있을 줄은 미처 몰랐다네."], ["prepossessing. Eh, what do you think?”", "응, 자네는 어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["Raskolnikov did not speak, but he still kept his eyes fixed upon him, full of alarm.", "라스콜니코프는 말이 없었지만, 여전히 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 두려움이 가득한 눈길로 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“And all that could be wished, indeed, in every respect,”", "\"정말이지, 어느 모로 보나 바랄 게 없는 여자야.\""], ["Razumihin went on, not at all embarrassed by his silence.", "라주미힌은 그의 침묵에 조금도 당황하지 않고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Ah, the sly dog!”", "\"아유, 이 능청맞은 양반!\""], ["Nastasya shrieked again.", "나스타시야가 또 한번 비명을 질렀다."], ["This conversation afforded her unspeakable delight.", "이 대화는 그녀에게 이루 말할 수 없는 즐거움을 안겨 주고 있었다."]], [["“It’s a pity, brother, that you did not set to work in the right way at first.", "\"안타까운 건, 이 사람아, 자네가 처음부터 일을 제대로 풀어 가지 못했다는 거야."], ["You ought to have approached her differently.", "자네는 그 여자한테 다르게 다가갔어야 했어."], ["She is, so to speak, a most unaccountable character.", "그 여자는, 말하자면, 도무지 종잡을 수 없는 성격이거든."], ["But we will talk about her character later....", "하긴 그 여자 성격 얘기는 나중에 하기로 하고……"], ["How could you let things come to such a pass that she gave up sending you your dinner?", "자네는 어쩌다 일을 그 지경으로 만들어서, 그 여자가 자네 식사를 보내 주는 걸 그만두게 했나?"], ["And that I O U?", "그리고 그 차용증은 또 뭐고?"], ["You must have been mad to sign an I O U.", "차용증에 서명을 하다니 자네 미쳤던 게 분명해."], ["And that promise of marriage when her daughter, Natalya Yegorovna, was alive?...", "게다가 그 여자 딸 나탈리야 예고로브나가 살아 있을 때 했다는 그 결혼 약속은?……"], ["I know all about it!", "나는 다 안다네!"], ["But I see that’s a delicate matter and I am an ass; forgive me.", "하지만 보아하니 이건 민감한 문제이고, 내가 멍청이로군. 미안하네."], ["But, talking of foolishness, do you know Praskovya Pavlovna is not nearly so foolish as you would think at first sight?”", "그건 그렇고, 어리석은 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데, 자네 아나, 프라스코비야 파블로브나가 처음 보기처럼 그렇게 어리석은 여자가 결코 아니라는 거?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그래.\""], ["mumbled Raskolnikov, looking away, but feeling that it was better to keep up the conversation.", "라스콜니코프가 시선을 돌리며 우물거렸지만, 대화를 이어 가는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다."]], [["“She isn’t, is she?”", "\"안 그렇지?\""], ["cried Razumihin, delighted to get an answer out of him.", "라주미힌이 그에게서 대답을 얻어 낸 것이 기뻐 외쳤다."], ["“But she is not very clever either, eh?", "\"하지만 그렇다고 아주 영리한 것도 아니야, 응?"], ["She is essentially, essentially an unaccountable character!", "그 여자는 본질적으로, 본질적으로 종잡을 수 없는 성격이라고!"], ["I am sometimes quite at a loss, I assure you....", "나도 가끔은 정말 어찌할 바를 모르겠다네, 정말일세……"], ["She must be forty; she says she is thirty-six, and of course she has every right to say so.", "마흔은 됐을 거야. 본인은 서른여섯이라고 하지만, 물론 그렇게 말할 권리야 충분히 있지."], ["But I swear I judge her intellectually, simply from the metaphysical point of view; there is a sort of symbolism sprung up between us, a sort of algebra or what not!", "하지만 맹세코 나는 그 여자를 지적으로, 순전히 형이상학적인 관점에서 판단하는 거야. 우리 사이에는 일종의 상징 같은 게 싹터서, 일종의 대수학이라고 할까 뭐라고 할까!"], ["I don’t understand it!", "나도 도무지 모르겠어!"], ["Well, that’s all nonsense.", "뭐, 다 헛소리지."], ["Only, seeing that you are not a student now and have lost your lessons and your clothes, and that through the young lady’s death she has no need to treat you as a relation, she suddenly took fright; and as you hid in your den and dropped all your old relations with her, she planned to get rid of you.", "다만, 자네가 이제 학생도 아니고, 가정교사 자리도 잃고 옷가지도 잃은 데다, 그 아가씨가 죽는 바람에 그 여자가 자넬 친척처럼 대할 까닭도 없어지니까, 그 여자가 별안간 겁을 집어먹은 거야. 게다가 자네가 자네 굴속에 틀어박혀서 그 여자와 옛정을 죄다 끊어 버리니까, 자넬 떼어 낼 궁리를 한 거지."], ["And she’s been cherishing that design a long time, but was sorry to lose the I O U, for you assured her yourself that your mother would pay.”", "그리고 그 궁리를 오래도록 품어 왔는데, 차용증을 포기하기가 아까웠던 거야. 자네가 직접 자네 어머니가 갚아 줄 거라고 장담했으니까.\""]], [["“It was base of me to say that....", "\"그런 말을 하다니 비열한 짓이었어……"], ["My mother herself is almost a beggar...", "우리 어머니는 거의 거지나 다름없는데…… 나는 하숙을 지키려고……"], ["and I told a lie to keep my lodging...", "그리고 밥을 얻어먹으려고 거짓말을 했어.\""], ["and be fed,” Raskolnikov said loudly and distinctly.", "라스콜니코프가 크고 또렷하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, you did very sensibly.", "\"그래, 자넨 아주 분별 있게 처신했지."], ["But the worst of it is that at that point Mr.", "다만 가장 고약한 건 거기서 체바로프 씨라는 작자가 나타났다는 거야, 사업가 말이지. 파셴카 혼자서야 무슨 일을 벌일 생각조차 못 했을 거야, 너무 내성적이니까."], ["Tchebarov turns up, a business man.", "하지만 그 사업가는 결코 내성적이지가 않아서, 대뜸 이렇게 묻는 거지."], ["Pashenka would never have thought of doing anything on her own account, she is too retiring; but the business man is by no means retiring, and first thing he puts the question, ‘Is there any hope of realising the I O U?’ Answer: there is, because he has a mother who would save her Rodya with her hundred and twenty-five roubles pension, if she has to starve herself; and a sister, too, who would go into bondage for his sake.", "'이 차용증을 받아 낼 가망이 좀 있소?' 대답인즉슨, 가망이 있다, 왜냐하면 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 백이십오 루블 연금으로 제 아들 로쟈를 구할 어머니가 있고, 또 그를 위해서라면 몸이라도 팔 누이가 있으니까."], ["That’s what he was building upon....", "그자가 노린 게 바로 그거였어……"], ["Why do you start?", "왜 흠칫하나?"], ["I know all the ins and outs of your affairs now, my dear boy--it’s not for nothing that you were so open with Pashenka when you were her prospective son-in-law, and I say all this as a friend....", "이제 나는 자네 일의 속사정을 다 안다네, 친구. 자네가 파셴카의 예비 사위였을 때 그 여자한테 그렇게 속을 다 털어놓은 게 괜한 일이 아니었지."], ["But I tell you what it is; an honest and sensitive man is open; and a business man ‘listens and goes on eating’ you up.", "그리고 이 모든 얘기를 나는 친구로서 하는 걸세…… 하지만 내가 무슨 말을 하려는지 아나."], ["Well, then she gave the I O U by way of payment to this Tchebarov, and without hesitation he made a formal demand for payment.", "정직하고 예민한 사람은 속을 다 털어놓고, 사업가는 '귀 기울여 듣는 척하면서 자넬 야금야금 잡아먹는다'는 거야."], ["When I heard of all this I wanted to blow him up, too, to clear my conscience, but by that time harmony reigned between me and Pashenka, and I insisted on stopping the whole affair, engaging that you would pay.", "그래서, 그 여자가 빚 대신으로 그 체바로프한테 차용증을 넘겼고, 그자는 망설임도 없이 정식으로 변제를 요구한 거야. 이 모든 얘기를 듣고서 나도 양심을 풀려고 그자를 한바탕 날려 버리고 싶었지만, 그때쯤엔 나와 파셴카 사이에 화목이 깃들어서, 자네가 갚겠다고 내가 보증을 서면서 이 일을 통째로 중단시키자고 우겼다네."], ["I went security for you, brother.", "자네를 위해 내가 보증을 섰어, 이 사람아."], ["Do you understand?", "알겠나?"], ["We called Tchebarov, flung him ten roubles and got the I O U back from him, and here I have the honour of presenting it to you.", "우리는 체바로프를 불러서, 십 루블을 던져 주고 그자한테서 차용증을 되찾아 왔지. 자, 그래서 영광스럽게도 그걸 자네한테 돌려주는 바일세."], ["She trusts your word now.", "이제 그 여자는 자네 말을 믿는다네."], ["Here, take it, you see I have torn it.”", "자, 받게. 봐, 내가 찢어 놨어.\""]], [["Razumihin put the note on the table.", "라주미힌은 차용증을 탁자 위에 놓았다."], ["Raskolnikov looked at him and turned to the wall without uttering a word.", "라스콜니코프는 그를 바라보더니, 한마디 말도 없이 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다."], ["Even Razumihin felt a twinge.", "라주미힌조차 가슴이 뜨끔했다."]], [["“I see, brother,”", "\"내가 보니, 이 사람아,\""], ["he said a moment later, “that I have been playing the fool again.", "잠시 후 그가 말했다. \"또 멍청한 짓을 한 모양이군."], ["I thought I should amuse you with my chatter, and I believe I have only made you cross.”", "내 수다로 자넬 즐겁게 해 주려던 건데, 아무래도 화만 돋운 것 같아.\""]], [["“Was it you I did not recognise when I was delirious?”", "\"내가 헛소리할 때 알아보지 못했던 게 자네였나?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked, after a moment’s pause without turning his head.", "라스콜니코프가 잠시 사이를 두고, 고개도 돌리지 않은 채 물었다."]], [["“Yes, and you flew into a rage about it, especially when I brought Zametov one day.”", "\"그래, 자네는 그 일로 잔뜩 화를 냈지, 특히 어느 날 내가 자메토프를 데려왔을 때 말이야.\""]], [["“Zametov?", "\"자메토프?"], ["The head clerk?", "그 서기장?"], ["What for?”", "뭐하러?\""], ["Raskolnikov turned round quickly and fixed his eyes on Razumihin.", "라스콜니코프가 홱 돌아누우며 라주미힌에게 눈길을 박았다."]], [["“What’s the matter with you?...", "\"왜 그러나?……"], ["What are you upset about?", "뭐 때문에 그렇게 흥분하나?"], ["He wanted to make your acquaintance because I talked to him a lot about you....", "그 사람이 자네랑 사귀고 싶어 했어, 내가 자네 얘기를 그 사람한테 많이 했거든……"], ["How could I have found out so much except from him?", "그 사람한테서가 아니면 내가 어떻게 그 많은 걸 알아냈겠나?"], ["He is a capital fellow, brother, first-rate...", "그 사람은 대단한 친구라네, 이 사람아, 일류지……"], ["in his own way, of course.", "물론 제 나름대로 말이지만."], ["Now we are friends--see each other almost every day.", "이제 우리는 친구가 돼서 — 거의 매일 만나."], ["I have moved into this part, you know.", "내가 이 동네로 이사 왔거든, 알지."], ["I have only just moved.", "막 이사 왔어."], ["I’ve been with him to Luise Ivanovna once or twice....", "그 사람이랑 같이 루이자 이바노브나한테도 한두 번 갔었지……"], ["Do you remember Luise, Luise Ivanovna?”", "루이자 기억나나, 루이자 이바노브나?\""]], [["“Did I say anything in delirium?”", "\"내가 헛소리하면서 무슨 말을 했나?\""]], [["“I should think so!", "\"무슨 말이라니!"], ["You were beside yourself.”", "자넨 제정신이 아니었어.\""]], [["“What did I rave about?”", "\"내가 무슨 헛소리를 했냐고?\""]], [["“What next?", "\"또 그 소린가?"], ["What did you rave about?", "무슨 헛소리를 했냐고?"], ["What people do rave about....", "사람들이 헛소리할 때 하는 그런 소리들이지……"], ["Well, brother, now I must not lose time. To work.”", "자, 이 사람아, 이제 시간을 더 허비하면 안 되겠어."], ["He got up from the table and took up his cap.", "일하러 가야지.\" 그는 탁자에서 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다."]], [["“What did I rave about?”", "\"내가 무슨 헛소리를 했냐니까?\""]], [["“How he keeps on!", "\"어찌나 끈질긴지!"], ["Are you afraid of having let out some secret?", "무슨 비밀이라도 흘렸을까 봐 겁나나?"], ["Don’t worry yourself; you said nothing about a countess.", "걱정 말게, 백작 부인 얘기 같은 건 안 했어."], ["But you said a lot about a bulldog, and about ear-rings and chains, and about Krestovsky Island, and some porter, and Nikodim Fomitch and Ilya Petrovitch, the assistant superintendent.", "다만 불도그 얘기는 잔뜩 했고, 또 귀고리며 목걸이 얘기, 크레스토프스키 섬 얘기, 어떤 문지기 얘기, 그리고 니코딤 포미치와 일리야 페트로비치 부서장 얘기를 했지."], ["And another thing that was of special interest to you was your own sock.", "그리고 또 한 가지 자네가 유난히 관심을 보인 건 자네 양말 한 짝이었어. 자네는 '내 양말 내놔'"], ["You whined, ‘Give me my sock.’ Zametov hunted all about your room for your socks, and with his own scented, ring-bedecked fingers he gave you the rag.", "하고 칭얼댔지. 자메토프가 자네 양말을 찾으려고 방 안을 온통 뒤졌고, 향수를 뿌리고 반지를 낀 제 손가락으로 그 누더기 같은 걸 자네한테 건네줬어."], ["And only then were you comforted, and for the next twenty-four hours you held the wretched thing in your hand; we could not get it from you.", "그제야 자네는 안심이 됐는지, 그 후로 꼬박 하루 동안 그 너절한 걸 손에 꼭 쥐고 있었어. 우리가 그걸 도무지 자네한테서 못 떼어 냈다네."], ["It is most likely somewhere under your quilt at this moment.", "지금도 십중팔구 자네 이불 밑 어딘가에 있을걸."], ["And then you asked so piteously for fringe for your trousers.", "그러고 나서 자네는 바짓단에 술 장식을 달아 달라고 어찌나 애처롭게 조르던지."], ["We tried to find out what sort of fringe, but we could not make it out.", "우리는 어떤 술 장식인지 알아내려 했지만, 도무지 알 수가 없었어. 자, 이제 일 얘기로 돌아가지!"], ["Now to business!", "여기 삼십오 루블일세."], ["Here are thirty-five roubles; I take ten of them, and shall give you an account of them in an hour or two.", "내가 그중에서 십 루블을 가져가고, 한두 시간 안에 셈을 해 주겠네."], ["I will let Zossimov know at the same time, though he ought to have been here long ago, for it is nearly twelve.", "그러는 김에 조시모프한테도 알리겠어, 진작에 왔어야 하는 사람인데, 벌써 열두 시가 다 됐으니 말이야."], ["And you, Nastasya, look in pretty often while I am away, to see whether he wants a drink or anything else.", "그리고 나스타시야, 자네는 내가 없는 동안 이 사람이 마실 거나 다른 게 필요하지 않은지 자주 들여다봐 주게."], ["And I will tell Pashenka what is wanted myself.", "그리고 뭐가 필요한지는 내가 직접 파셴카한테 말하겠네."], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있게!\""]], [["“He calls her Pashenka!", "\"파셴카라고 부르네!"], ["Ah, he’s a deep one!”", "아유, 보통내기가 아니라니까!\""], ["said Nastasya as he went out; then she opened the door and stood listening, but could not resist running downstairs after him.", "나스타시야는 그가 나가자 이렇게 말하더니, 문을 열고 서서 귀를 기울이다가, 끝내 참지 못하고 그를 따라 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다."], ["She was very eager to hear what he would say to the landlady.", "그녀는 그가 집주인 여자에게 무슨 말을 하는지 무척이나 듣고 싶었다."], ["She was evidently quite fascinated by Razumihin.", "그녀는 분명 라주미힌에게 단단히 홀려 있었다."]], [["No sooner had she left the room than the sick man flung off the bedclothes and leapt out of bed like a madman.", "그녀가 방을 나서기가 무섭게 병자는 이불을 걷어차고 미친 사람처럼 침대에서 벌떡 뛰어나왔다."], ["With burning, twitching impatience he had waited for them to be gone so that he might set to work.", "그는 일에 착수하려고, 그들이 가 버리기를 타는 듯한, 경련하듯 떨리는 조바심으로 기다려 왔던 것이다."], ["But to what work?", "그런데 무슨 일에?"], ["Now, as though to spite him, it eluded him.", "이제, 마치 그를 약 올리기라도 하듯, 그것은 그의 머릿속에서 빠져나가 버렸다."]], [["“Good God, only tell me one thing: do they know of it yet or not?", "'오, 하느님, 한 가지만 말해 주세요. 그들이 벌써 그걸 알고 있는 겁니까, 아닙니까?"], ["What if they know it and are only pretending, mocking me while I am laid up, and then they will come in and tell me that it’s been discovered long ago and that they have only...", "만일 그들이 알면서도 그저 모르는 척, 내가 누워 있는 동안 나를 비웃다가, 들이닥쳐서는 그게 진작에 발각됐다고, 그저 자기들이…… 라고 말하면 어쩌지?"], ["What am I to do now?", "이제 난 어떻게 해야 하지?"], ["That’s what I’ve forgotten, as though on purpose; forgotten it all at once, I remembered a minute ago.”", "바로 그게 잊혀 버렸어, 마치 일부러 그런 것처럼. 한꺼번에 잊어버렸어, 방금 전엔 기억했는데.'"]], [["He stood in the middle of the room and gazed in miserable bewilderment about him; he walked to the door, opened it, listened; but that was not what he wanted.", "그는 방 한가운데 서서 비참한 당혹감에 휩싸여 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 문으로 걸어가 문을 열고 귀를 기울였다."], ["Suddenly, as though recalling something, he rushed to the corner where there was a hole under the paper, began examining it, put his hand into the hole, fumbled--but that was not it.", "하지만 그건 그가 원하던 게 아니었다."], ["He went to the stove, opened it and began rummaging in the ashes; the frayed edges of his trousers and the rags cut off his pocket were lying there just as he had thrown them.", "갑자기, 무언가 생각난 듯, 그는 벽지 아래 구멍이 있는 구석으로 달려가 그것을 살피기 시작했고, 구멍 속에 손을 넣어 더듬었다. 하지만 그것도 아니었다."], ["No one had looked, then!", "그는 난로로 가서 뚜껑을 열고 잿더미를 뒤지기 시작했다."], ["Then he remembered the sock about which Razumihin had just been telling him.", "거기엔 닳아 해진 바짓단과 주머니에서 잘라 낸 누더기 조각들이, 그가 던져 둔 그대로 놓여 있었다. 그렇다면 아무도 들여다보지 않았던 것이다!"], ["Yes, there it lay on the sofa under the quilt, but it was so covered with dust and grime that Zametov could not have seen anything on it.", "그제야 그는 라주미힌이 방금 들려준 그 양말이 떠올랐다. 그래, 그건 이불 밑 소파 위에 놓여 있었는데, 어찌나 먼지와 때투성이인지 자메토프가 거기서 무엇 하나 알아챘을 리 없었다."]], [["“Bah, Zametov!", "'쳇, 자메토프!"], ["The police office!", "경찰서!"], ["And why am I sent for to the police office?", "그런데 왜 나더러 경찰서로 오라는 거지?"], ["Where’s the notice?", "그 소환장은 어디 있지?"], ["Bah!", "쳇!"], ["I am mixing it up; that was then.", "내가 헷갈리고 있군. 그건 그때 일이야."], ["I looked at my sock then, too, but now...", "그때도 나는 내 양말을 들여다봤지만, 지금은……"], ["now I have been ill.", "지금은 내가 앓았으니까."], ["But what did Zametov come for?", "그런데 자메토프는 뭐하러 온 거지?"], ["Why did Razumihin bring him?”", "라주미힌은 왜 그를 데려왔지?'"], ["he muttered, helplessly sitting on the sofa again.", "그는 맥없이 다시 소파에 주저앉으며 중얼거렸다."], ["“What does it mean?", "'이게 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["Am I still in delirium, or is it real?", "내가 아직 헛것을 보는 건가, 아니면 현실인가?"], ["I believe it is real....", "현실인 것 같은데……"], ["Ah, I remember; I must escape!", "아, 생각났어. 달아나야 해!"], ["Make haste to escape.", "어서 달아나야 해."], ["Yes, I must, I must escape!", "그래, 달아나야 해, 달아나야 해!"], ["Yes...", "그래……"], ["but where?", "하지만 어디로?"], ["And where are my clothes?", "게다가 내 옷은 어디 있지?"], ["I’ve no boots.", "장화가 없어."], ["They’ve taken them away!", "그들이 가져가 버렸어!"], ["They’ve hidden them!", "숨겨 버렸다고!"], ["I understand!", "알겠어!"], ["Ah, here is my coat--they passed that over!", "아, 여기 외투는 있군 — 이건 그냥 둔 거야!"], ["And here is money on the table, thank God!", "그리고 탁자 위에 돈이 있어, 다행이야!"], ["And here’s the I O U...", "그리고 여기 차용증도……"], ["I’ll take the money and go and take another lodging.", "돈을 챙겨서 다른 하숙으로 가자."], ["They won’t find me!...", "그러면 그들이 날 못 찾을 거야!……"], ["Yes, but the address bureau?", "그래, 하지만 주소 관리소는?"], ["They’ll find me, Razumihin will find me.", "그들이 날 찾아낼 거야, 라주미힌이 날 찾아낼 거야."], ["Better escape altogether...", "차라리 아예 멀리 달아나는 게 낫겠어……"], ["far away...", "아주 멀리……"], ["to America, and let them do their worst!", "미국으로, 그래서 마음대로 하라고 내버려 두자!"], ["And take the I O U...", "차용증도 챙기고……"], ["it would be of use there....", "거기서 쓸모가 있을 거야……"], ["What else shall I take?", "또 뭘 챙기지?"], ["They think I am ill!", "그들은 내가 아픈 줄 알아!"], ["They don’t know that I can walk, ha-ha-ha!", "내가 걸을 수 있는 줄은 모르지, 하하하!"], ["I could see by their eyes that they know all about it!", "그들 눈빛을 보니 다 알고 있더군!"], ["If only I could get downstairs!", "어떻게든 아래층까지만 내려갈 수 있다면!"], ["And what if they have set a watch there--policemen!", "그런데 만일 거기 감시를 세워 뒀다면 — 경찰을!"], ["What’s this tea?", "이 차는 뭐지?"], ["Ah, and here is beer left, half a bottle, cold!”", "아, 여기 맥주가 남았네, 반 병, 차갑게!'"]], [["He snatched up the bottle, which still contained a glassful of beer, and gulped it down with relish, as though quenching a flame in his breast.", "그는 한 잔 분량의 맥주가 아직 남아 있는 병을 낚아채서, 마치 가슴속 불길을 끄기라도 하듯 흡족하게 들이켰다."], ["But in another minute the beer had gone to his head, and a faint and even pleasant shiver ran down his spine.", "그러나 또 한순간이 지나자 맥주가 머리로 올라왔고, 등줄기를 따라 희미하면서도 오히려 기분 좋은 전율이 흘러내렸다."], ["He lay down and pulled the quilt over him.", "그는 자리에 누워 이불을 끌어 덮었다."], ["His sick and incoherent thoughts grew more and more disconnected, and soon a light, pleasant drowsiness came upon him.", "병들고 두서없던 그의 생각은 점점 더 갈피를 잃어 갔고, 곧 가볍고 기분 좋은 졸음이 그를 덮쳐 왔다."], ["With a sense of comfort he nestled his head into the pillow, wrapped more closely about him the soft, wadded quilt which had replaced the old, ragged greatcoat, sighed softly and sank into a deep, sound, refreshing sleep.", "그는 안락한 기분으로 머리를 베개에 파묻고, 낡고 해진 외투를 대신한 부드럽고 솜이 든 이불로 몸을 더욱 단단히 감싼 채, 조용히 한숨을 내쉬고는 깊고 든든하며 개운한 잠 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["He woke up, hearing someone come in.", "그는 누군가 들어오는 소리에 잠에서 깼다."], ["He opened his eyes and saw Razumihin standing in the doorway, uncertain whether to come in or not.", "눈을 떠 보니 라주미힌이 문간에 서서, 들어갈지 말지 망설이고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov sat up quickly on the sofa and gazed at him, as though trying to recall something.", "라스콜니코프는 소파에서 얼른 몸을 일으켜 앉아, 무언가 기억해 내려는 듯 그를 응시했다."]], [["“Ah, you are not asleep!", "\"아, 안 자고 있었군!"], ["Here I am!", "자, 내가 왔네!"], ["Nastasya, bring in the parcel!”", "나스타시야, 꾸러미 가져오게!\""], ["Razumihin shouted down the stairs.", "라주미힌이 계단 아래로 소리쳤다."], ["“You shall have the account directly.”", "\"셈은 곧 해 주겠네.\""]], [["“What time is it?”", "\"지금 몇 시야?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov, looking round uneasily.", "라스콜니코프가 불안하게 주위를 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, you had a fine sleep, brother, it’s almost evening, it will be six o’clock directly.", "\"그래, 잘도 잤군, 이 사람아, 거의 저녁때야, 곧 여섯 시가 될 걸세."], ["You have slept more than six hours.”", "자네는 여섯 시간도 넘게 잤어.\""]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Have I?”", "내가 그랬다고?\""]], [["“And why not?", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있나?"], ["It will do you good.", "자네한테 도움이 될 거야."], ["What’s the hurry?", "뭐가 그리 급한가?"], ["A tryst, is it?", "밀회라도 있나?"], ["We’ve all time before us.", "우리한텐 시간이 얼마든지 있어."], ["I’ve been waiting for the last three hours for you; I’ve been up twice and found you asleep.", "지난 세 시간 동안 자넬 기다렸다네. 두 번이나 올라왔는데 자네가 자고 있더군."], ["I’ve called on Zossimov twice; not at home, only fancy!", "조시모프한테도 두 번이나 들렀어. 집에 없더라고, 글쎄!"], ["But no matter, he will turn up.", "하지만 상관없어, 곧 나타나겠지."], ["And I’ve been out on my own business, too.", "나도 내 볼일을 좀 보러 다녔어."], ["You know I’ve been moving to-day, moving with my uncle.", "알다시피 나는 오늘 이사를 했거든, 삼촌이랑 같이 이사했지."], ["I have an uncle living with me now.", "이젠 삼촌이 나랑 같이 사신다네."], ["But that’s no matter, to business.", "하지만 그건 상관없는 얘기고, 일 얘기로 돌아가지."], ["Give me the parcel, Nastasya.", "꾸러미 이리 주게, 나스타시야."], ["We will open it directly.", "곧 풀어 보세."], ["And how do you feel now, brother?”", "그래, 이제 좀 어떤가, 이 사람아?\""]], [["“I am quite well, I am not ill.", "\"멀쩡해, 난 아프지 않아."], ["Razumihin, have you been here long?”", "라주미힌, 자네 여기 온 지 오래됐나?\""]], [["“I tell you I’ve been waiting for the last three hours.”", "\"내가 지난 세 시간 동안 자넬 기다렸다고 하잖나.\""]], [["“No, before.”", "\"아니, 그 전에.\""]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말인가?\""]], [["“How long have you been coming here?”", "\"여기 드나든 지 얼마나 됐냐고.\""]], [["“Why I told you all about it this morning.", "\"아니, 오늘 아침에 내가 다 얘기했잖나."], ["Don’t you remember?”", "기억 안 나?\""]], [["Raskolnikov pondered.", "라스콜니코프는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["The morning seemed like a dream to him.", "아침 일이 그에게는 꿈처럼 느껴졌다."], ["He could not remember alone, and looked inquiringly at Razumihin.", "혼자서는 기억해 낼 수가 없어, 묻는 듯한 눈길로 라주미힌을 바라보았다."]], [["“Hm!”", "\"흠!\""], ["said the latter, “he has forgotten.", "라주미힌이 말했다. \"잊어버렸군."], ["I fancied then that you were not quite yourself.", "그때도 자네가 제정신이 아닌 것 같다고 생각했지."], ["Now you are better for your sleep....", "이제 한숨 자고 나니 좀 나아졌군……"], ["You really look much better.", "정말 안색이 훨씬 좋아졌어."], ["First-rate!", "일류야!"], ["Well, to business.", "자, 일 얘기로 돌아가지."], ["Look here, my dear boy.”", "이걸 좀 보게, 친구.\""]], [["He began untying the bundle, which evidently interested him.", "그는 보따리를 풀기 시작했는데, 그 일에 자못 흥미를 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["“Believe me, brother, this is something specially near my heart.", "\"믿어 주게, 이 사람아, 이건 내가 유난히 마음을 쓴 일이라네."], ["For we must make a man of you.", "우리가 자넬 어엿한 사람으로 만들어야 하니까."], ["Let’s begin from the top.", "위에서부터 시작하지."], ["Do you see this cap?”", "이 모자 보이나?\""], ["he said, taking out of the bundle a fairly good though cheap and ordinary cap.", "그는 보따리에서 제법 괜찮은, 비록 값싸고 평범한 것이긴 했지만, 모자 하나를 꺼내며 말했다."], ["“Let me try it on.”", "\"한번 씌워 보세.\""]], [["“Presently, afterwards,”", "\"이따, 나중에.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, waving it off pettishly.", "라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 손을 내저으며 말했다."]], [["“Come, Rodya, my boy, don’t oppose it, afterwards will be too late; and I shan’t sleep all night, for I bought it by guess, without measure.", "\"이러지 말게, 로쟈, 이 사람아, 마다하지 마."], ["Just right!”", "나중엔 너무 늦어."], ["he cried triumphantly, fitting it on, “just your size!", "게다가 나는 밤새 한숨도 못 잘 거야, 치수도 안 재고 어림짐작으로 산 거니까. 딱 맞는군!\""], ["A proper head-covering is the first thing in dress and a recommendation in its own way.", "그가 모자를 씌워 보고는 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"꼭 자네 머리에 맞아!"], ["Tolstyakov, a friend of mine, is always obliged to take off his pudding basin when he goes into any public place where other people wear their hats or caps.", "제대로 된 모자야말로 옷차림에서 으뜸가는 것이고, 그 나름대로 추천장 노릇을 하지. 내 친구 톨스탸코프는 모자나 챙모자를 쓰는 사람들이 모이는 공공장소에 갈 때마다 늘 자기 푸딩 그릇 같은 모자를 벗어야만 한다네."], ["People think he does it from slavish politeness, but it’s simply because he is ashamed of his bird’s nest; he is such a boastful fellow!", "사람들은 그가 노예처럼 굽실거려서 그러는 줄 알지만, 사실은 그저 자기 까치집 같은 머리가 부끄러워서 그러는 거야. 그렇게 허세 부리기 좋아하는 친구거든!"], ["Look, Nastasya, here are two specimens of headgear: this Palmerston”--he took from the corner Raskolnikov’s old, battered hat, which for some unknown reason, he called a Palmerston--“or this jewel!", "보게, 나스타시야, 여기 모자 두 종류가 있네. 이 파머스턴이냐\""], ["Guess the price, Rodya, what do you suppose I paid for it, Nastasya!”", "— 그는 무슨 영문인지 라스콜니코프의 낡고 찌그러진 모자를 파머스턴이라 부르며 구석에서 집어 들었다 — \"아니면 이 보물이냐! 값을 맞혀 보게, 로쟈, 내가 이걸 얼마 주고 샀을 것 같나, 나스타시야!\""], ["he said, turning to her, seeing that Raskolnikov did not speak.", "그는 라스콜니코프가 말이 없는 것을 보고 그녀를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Twenty copecks, no more, I dare say,”", "\"이십 코페이카, 그 이상은 아닐걸요, 아마.\""], ["answered Nastasya.", "나스타시야가 대답했다."]], [["“Twenty copecks, silly!”", "\"이십 코페이카라니, 멍청하긴!\""], ["he cried, offended.", "그가 발끈하며 외쳤다."], ["“Why, nowadays you would cost more than that--eighty copecks!", "\"아니, 요즘 같으면 자네 같은 사람도 그것보단 비싸겠다 — 팔십 코페이카는 줘야 한다고!"], ["And that only because it has been worn.", "그것도 한 번 쓴 거라 그렇지."], ["And it’s bought on condition that when’s it’s worn out, they will give you another next year.", "게다가 조건까지 붙어서 산 거야. 이게 다 닳으면 내년에 새것 하나를 더 준다는 거지."], ["Yes, on my word!", "그래, 정말이라니까!"], ["Well, now let us pass to the United States of America, as they called them at school.", "자, 이제 미국 합중국으로 넘어가 볼까, 학교에서 그렇게들 불렀듯이 말이야."], ["I assure you I am proud of these breeches,”", "분명히 말하는데, 나는 이 바지가 자랑스럽다네.\""], ["and he exhibited to Raskolnikov a pair of light, summer trousers of grey woollen material.", "그러고는 회색 모직으로 된 가볍고 시원한 여름 바지 한 벌을 라스콜니코프에게 펼쳐 보였다."], ["“No holes, no spots, and quite respectable, although a little worn; and a waistcoat to match, quite in the fashion.", "\"구멍도 없고, 얼룩도 없고, 아주 멀쩡해, 조금 닳긴 했지만. 게다가 거기 어울리는 조끼도 있어, 아주 최신 유행이지."], ["And its being worn really is an improvement, it’s softer, smoother....", "그리고 좀 닳은 게 사실 더 좋아, 한결 부드럽고 매끈하거든……"], ["You see, Rodya, to my thinking, the great thing for getting on in the world is always to keep to the seasons; if you don’t insist on having asparagus in January, you keep your money in your purse; and it’s the same with this purchase.", "자, 로쟈, 내 생각엔 말이야, 세상을 살아가는 데 가장 중요한 건 늘 계절에 맞춰 사는 거야. 일월에 아스파라거스를 꼭 먹겠다고 고집부리지 않으면, 지갑 속에 돈을 지킬 수 있지."], ["It’s summer now, so I’ve been buying summer things--warmer materials will be wanted for autumn, so you will have to throw these away in any case...", "이 물건도 마찬가지야. 지금은 여름이니 여름옷을 산 거고 — 가을엔 더 따뜻한 옷감이 필요할 테니, 어차피 이건 버려야 할 거야……"], ["especially as they will be done for by then from their own lack of coherence if not your higher standard of luxury.", "더구나 그때쯤이면 자네의 고상한 사치 기준 때문이 아니더라도 옷감 자체가 제풀에 다 해어질 테니까."], ["Come, price them!", "자, 값을 매겨 보게!"], ["What do you say?", "얼마라고 하겠나?"], ["Two roubles twenty-five copecks!", "이 루블 이십오 코페이카야!"], ["And remember the condition: if you wear these out, you will have another suit for nothing!", "그리고 그 조건을 잊지 말게. 이걸 다 닳도록 입으면, 다음 옷 한 벌을 공짜로 받는 거라고!"], ["They only do business on that system at Fedyaev’s; if you’ve bought a thing once, you are satisfied for life, for you will never go there again of your own free will.", "그런 식으로 장사하는 데는 페댜예프네뿐이야. 거기선 한번 물건을 사면 평생 만족이지, 다시는 제 발로 거길 안 가게 되니까."], ["Now for the boots.", "자, 이제 장화 차례야."], ["What do you say?", "어떤가?"], ["You see that they are a bit worn, but they’ll last a couple of months, for it’s foreign work and foreign leather; the secretary of the English Embassy sold them last week--he had only worn them six days, but he was very short of cash.", "보다시피 좀 닳긴 했지만, 두어 달은 갈 거야, 외제 솜씨에 외제 가죽이거든. 지난주에 영국 대사관 비서가 판 거야 — 엿새밖에 안 신었는데, 돈이 몹시 궁했던 모양이야."], ["Price--a rouble and a half.", "값은 — 일 루블 반."], ["A bargain?”", "싸지?\""]], [["“But perhaps they won’t fit,”", "\"하지만 안 맞을지도 몰라요.\""], ["observed Nastasya.", "나스타시야가 말했다."]], [["“Not fit?", "\"안 맞다니?"], ["Just look!”", "이걸 좀 봐!\""], ["and he pulled out of his pocket Raskolnikov’s old, broken boot, stiffly coated with dry mud.", "그러고는 그가 주머니에서 라스콜니코프의 낡고 터진 장화를 꺼냈는데, 마른 진흙이 뻣뻣하게 들러붙어 있었다."], ["“I did not go empty-handed--they took the size from this monster.", "\"나도 빈손으로 간 게 아니야 — 이 괴물을 가지고 치수를 쟀다고."], ["We all did our best.", "우리 모두 최선을 다했지."], ["And as to your linen, your landlady has seen to that.", "그리고 자네 속옷은 집주인 아주머니가 챙겨 주셨어."], ["Here, to begin with are three shirts, hempen but with a fashionable front....", "자, 우선 셔츠가 세 벌인데, 삼베지만 유행하는 가슴 장식이 달렸어……"], ["Well now then, eighty copecks the cap, two roubles twenty-five copecks the suit--together three roubles five copecks--a rouble and a half for the boots--for, you see, they are very good--and that makes four roubles fifty-five copecks; five roubles for the underclothes--they were bought in the lot--which makes exactly nine roubles fifty-five copecks.", "자, 그럼, 모자가 팔십 코페이카, 옷 한 벌이 이 루블 이십오 코페이카 — 합쳐서 삼 루블 오 코페이카 — 장화가 일 루블 반인데, 보다시피 아주 좋은 거고 — 그러면 사 루블 오십오 코페이카, 속옷이 오 루블 — 한꺼번에 산 거야 — 그러니 딱 구 루블 오십오 코페이카로군."], ["Forty-five copecks change in coppers.", "잔돈 사십오 코페이카는 동전으로."], ["Will you take it?", "받겠나?"], ["And so, Rodya, you are set up with a complete new rig-out, for your overcoat will serve, and even has a style of its own.", "그래서, 로쟈, 자네는 완전히 새 차림으로 갖춰 입게 됐어. 외투야 쓸 만하고, 게다가 나름대로 멋도 있으니까."], ["That comes from getting one’s clothes from Sharmer’s!", "그게 다 샤르메르네에서 옷을 맞춘 덕분이지!"], ["As for your socks and other things, I leave them to you; we’ve twenty-five roubles left.", "양말이나 다른 것들은 자네한테 맡기겠네. 우리한텐 아직 이십오 루블이 남았어."], ["And as for Pashenka and paying for your lodging, don’t you worry.", "그리고 파셴카랑 하숙비 문제는 걱정 말게."], ["I tell you she’ll trust you for anything.", "내가 장담하는데, 그 여자는 자네한테 뭐든 외상으로 줄 거야."], ["And now, brother, let me change your linen, for I daresay you will throw off your illness with your shirt.”", "자, 이제, 이 사람아, 속옷을 갈아입혀 주지, 아마 셔츠와 함께 자네 병도 떨쳐 버릴 테니까.\""]], [["“Let me be!", "\"날 내버려 둬!"], ["I don’t want to!”", "싫어!\""], ["Raskolnikov waved him off.", "라스콜니코프가 그를 밀쳐 냈다."], ["He had listened with disgust to Razumihin’s efforts to be playful about his purchases.", "그는 자기가 산 물건들을 두고 익살을 떨려는 라주미힌의 애씀을 혐오스럽게 듣고 있었다."]], [["“Come, brother, don’t tell me I’ve been trudging around for nothing,”", "\"이러지 말게, 이 사람아, 내가 헛걸음만 하고 다녔다는 소린 말게나.\""], ["Razumihin insisted.", "라주미힌이 우겼다."], ["“Nastasya, don’t be bashful, but help me--that’s it,”", "\"나스타시야, 부끄러워 말고 좀 도와주게 — 옳지, 그렇지.\""], ["and in spite of Raskolnikov’s resistance he changed his linen.", "그러고는 라스콜니코프의 저항에도 불구하고 그의 속옷을 갈아입혔다."], ["The latter sank back on the pillows and for a minute or two said nothing.", "라스콜니코프는 베개에 도로 쓰러져 한두 마디도 하지 않고 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“It will be long before I get rid of them,”", "'이것들을 떨쳐 버리려면 한참 걸리겠군.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“What money was all that bought with?”", "\"그게 다 무슨 돈으로 산 거야?\""], ["he asked at last, gazing at the wall.", "그가 마침내 벽을 응시하며 물었다."]], [["“Money?", "\"돈?"], ["Why, your own, what the messenger brought from Vahrushin, your mother sent it.", "아니, 자네 돈이지, 심부름꾼이 바흐루신한테서 가져온 거 말이야, 자네 어머니가 보내셨잖나."], ["Have you forgotten that, too?”", "그것도 잊어버렸나?\""]], [["“I remember now,”", "\"이제 기억나.\""], ["said Raskolnikov after a long, sullen silence.", "라스콜니코프가 오래도록 시무룩한 침묵 끝에 말했다."], ["Razumihin looked at him, frowning and uneasy.", "라주미힌은 인상을 찌푸린 채 불안하게 그를 바라보았다."]], [["The door opened and a tall, stout man whose appearance seemed familiar to Raskolnikov came in.", "문이 열리고, 라스콜니코프에게 낯이 익은 듯한, 키가 크고 뚱뚱한 사내가 들어왔다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["Zossimov was a tall, fat man with a puffy, colourless, clean-shaven face and straight flaxen hair.", "조시모프는 키가 크고 뚱뚱한 사내로, 부석부석하고 핏기 없는 말끔히 면도한 얼굴에 곧은 아마빛 머리칼을 가지고 있었다."], ["He wore spectacles, and a big gold ring on his fat finger.", "그는 안경을 쓰고 있었고, 살찐 손가락에는 커다란 금반지를 끼고 있었다."], ["He was twenty-seven.", "나이는 스물일곱이었다."], ["He had on a light grey fashionable loose coat, light summer trousers, and everything about him loose, fashionable and spick and span; his linen was irreproachable, his watch-chain was massive.", "밝은 회색의 유행하는 헐렁한 외투를 걸치고, 가벼운 여름 바지를 입었으며, 몸에 걸친 모든 것이 헐렁하고 유행을 따랐으며 티 하나 없이 말끔했다. 속옷은 흠잡을 데가 없었고, 시곗줄은 묵직했다."], ["In manner he was slow and, as it were, nonchalant, and at the same time studiously free and easy; he made efforts to conceal his self-importance, but it was apparent at every instant.", "행동거지는 굼떴고, 말하자면 무심한 듯했으며, 동시에 일부러 거리낌 없고 격의 없는 척했다. 자신의 거만함을 감추려 애썼지만, 그것은 매 순간 드러났다."], ["All his acquaintances found him tedious, but said he was clever at his work.", "그를 아는 사람들은 하나같이 그를 지루한 사람이라 여겼지만, 일에 있어서는 영리하다고들 했다."]], [["“I’ve been to you twice to-day, brother.", "\"오늘 두 번이나 자네한테 들렀었다네, 이 친구야."], ["You see, he’s come to himself,”", "보게나, 이 친구가 정신을 차렸어.\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."]], [["“I see, I see; and how do we feel now, eh?”", "\"보이는군, 보여. 그래, 지금은 기분이 어떤가, 응?\""], ["said Zossimov to Raskolnikov, watching him carefully and, sitting down at the foot of the sofa, he settled himself as comfortably as he could.", "조시모프가 라스콜니코프에게 말하며 그를 주의 깊게 살피더니, 소파 발치에 앉아 가능한 한 편안하게 자리를 잡았다."]], [["“He is still depressed,”", "\"아직 우울해하고 있어.\""], ["Razumihin went on.", "라주미힌이 말을 이었다."], ["“We’ve just changed his linen and he almost cried.”", "\"방금 속옷을 갈아입혔는데 거의 울 뻔했다네.\""]], [["“That’s very natural; you might have put it off if he did not wish it....", "\"그야 아주 자연스러운 일이지. 본인이 원치 않으면 나중으로 미뤘어도 됐을 텐데……."], ["His pulse is first-rate.", "맥박은 더할 나위 없이 좋군."], ["Is your head still aching, eh?”", "머리는 아직도 아픈가, 응?\""]], [["“I am well, I am perfectly well!”", "\"멀쩡합니다, 아주 멀쩡해요!\""], ["Raskolnikov declared positively and irritably.", "라스콜니코프가 단호하게, 그리고 짜증스럽게 잘라 말했다."], ["He raised himself on the sofa and looked at them with glittering eyes, but sank back on to the pillow at once and turned to the wall.", "그는 소파에서 몸을 일으켜 반짝이는 눈으로 그들을 바라보았으나, 곧 다시 베개에 몸을 누이고 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다."], ["Zossimov watched him intently.", "조시모프가 그를 유심히 지켜보았다."]], [["“Very good....", "\"아주 좋아……."], ["Going on all right,”", "잘 회복되고 있군.\""], ["he said lazily.", "그가 나른하게 말했다."], ["“Has he eaten anything?”", "\"뭘 좀 먹었나?\""]], [["They told him, and asked what he might have.", "그들이 그에게 말해 주고는, 무엇을 먹여도 되는지 물었다."]], [["“He may have anything... soup, tea...", "\"뭐든 먹어도 돼……."], ["mushrooms and cucumbers, of course, you must not give him; he’d better not have meat either, and...", "수프, 차……, 물론 버섯이나 오이는 주면 안 되지. 고기도 안 먹는 게 낫고, 그리고……, 뭐 자네한테 굳이 일러 줄 필요도 없겠지!\""], ["but no need to tell you that!” Razumihin and he looked at each other.", "라주미힌과 그가 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["“No more medicine or anything.", "\"약 같은 건 더 이상 필요 없네."], ["I’ll look at him again to-morrow.", "내일 다시 들여다보지."], ["Perhaps, to-day even... but never mind...”", "어쩌면 오늘이라도……, 아니, 됐어…….\""]], [["“To-morrow evening I shall take him for a walk,”", "\"내일 저녁에는 저 친구를 데리고 산책하러 갈 거야.\""], ["said Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 말했다."], ["“We are going to the Yusupov garden and then to the Palais de Cristal.”", "\"유수포프 정원에 갔다가 그다음엔 팔레 드 크리스탈에 가려고.\""]], [["“I would not disturb him to-morrow at all, but I don’t know... a little, maybe... but we’ll see.”", "\"내일은 절대 저 친구를 들쑤셔 놓지 않겠는데, 글쎄……, 조금이라면 모르지……, 두고 보자고.\""]], [["“Ach, what a nuisance!", "\"아, 정말 골치 아프군!"], ["I’ve got a house-warming party to-night; it’s only a step from here.", "오늘 밤 집들이 모임이 있는데, 여기서 코앞이야."], ["Couldn’t he come?", "이 친구가 올 수는 없을까?"], ["He could lie on the sofa.", "소파에 누워 있으면 되잖아."], ["You are coming?”", "자네도 오는 거지?\""], ["Razumihin said to Zossimov.", "라주미힌이 조시모프에게 말했다."], ["“Don’t forget, you promised.”", "\"잊지 말게, 약속했으니까.\""]], [["“All right, only rather later.", "\"좋아, 다만 좀 늦게 가겠네."], ["What are you going to do?”", "뭘 차릴 건가?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing--tea, vodka, herrings. There will be a pie...", "\"오, 별거 없어 — 차, 보드카, 청어."], ["just our friends.” “And who?”", "파이도 나올 거고……, 그냥 우리 친구들 모임이야.\""]], [["“All neighbours here, almost all new friends, except my old uncle, and he is new too--he only arrived in Petersburg yesterday to see to some business of his.", "\"그래, 누가 오는데?\" \"여기 이웃들 전부, 거의 다 새로 사귄 친구들이지, 우리 늙은 삼촌만 빼면."], ["We meet once in five years.”", "그분도 사실 처음이긴 해 — 어제야 무슨 볼일이 있어서 페테르부르크에 막 도착했거든. 우린 오 년에 한 번 보는 사이야.\""]], [["“He’s been stagnating all his life as a district postmaster; gets a little pension.", "\"그분은 뭐 하는 분인데?\" \"평생을 지방 우체국장으로 빌빌대며 지내셨지."], ["He is sixty-five--not worth talking about....", "쥐꼬리만 한 연금이나 받고. 예순다섯이야 — 입에 올릴 거리도 없지……."], ["But I am fond of him.", "그래도 난 그분을 좋아해."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch, the head of the Investigation Department here...", "포르피리 페트로비치는, 여기 수사과 과장인데……."], ["But you know him.”", "뭐 자네도 알겠지.\""]], [["“Is he a relation of yours, too?”", "\"그 사람도 자네 친척인가?\""]], [["“A very distant one.", "\"아주 먼 친척이야."], ["But why are you scowling?", "한데 왜 인상을 찌푸리는 건가?"], ["Because you quarrelled once, won’t you come then?”", "한 번 다툰 적 있다고 그럼 안 오겠다는 거야?\""]], [["“I don’t care a damn for him.”", "\"그 사람 따위 알 게 뭐야.\""]], [["“So much the better.", "\"그럼 더 잘됐군."], ["Well, there will be some students, a teacher, a government clerk, a musician, an officer and Zametov.”", "자, 그리고 학생 몇 명에다 교사 하나, 관청 서기 하나, 음악가 하나, 장교 하나, 그리고 자메토프가 올 거야.\""]], [["“Do tell me, please, what you or he”--Zossimov nodded at Raskolnikov--“can have in common with this Zametov?”", "\"그런데 말이지, 제발 좀 말해 주게, 자네나 저 친구가\" — 조시모프가 라스콜니코프 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다 — \"그 자메토프란 자와 무슨 공통점이 있을 수 있다는 건가?\""]], [["“Oh, you particular gentleman!", "\"오, 까다로우신 양반 같으니!"], ["Principles!", "원칙이라!"], ["You are worked by principles, as it were by springs; you won’t venture to turn round on your own account.", "자넨 원칙에 휘둘려, 마치 태엽에 감긴 것처럼. 제 멋대로 한 바퀴 돌아볼 엄두도 못 내지."], ["If a man is a nice fellow, that’s the only principle I go upon.", "사람이 괜찮은 녀석이면, 내가 따르는 원칙은 그것 하나뿐이야."], ["Zametov is a delightful person.”", "자메토프는 유쾌한 친구라네.\""]], [["“Though he does take bribes.”", "\"뇌물을 받기는 하지만 말이지.\""]], [["“Well, he does!", "\"흠, 그래, 받지!"], ["and what of it?", "그게 어쨌단 말인가?"], ["I don’t care if he does take bribes,”", "그가 뇌물을 받든 말든 난 상관 안 해.\""], ["Razumihin cried with unnatural irritability.", "라주미힌이 부자연스러울 만큼 짜증스럽게 외쳤다."], ["“I don’t praise him for taking bribes.", "\"뇌물을 받는다고 그를 칭찬하는 게 아니야."], ["I only say he is a nice man in his own way!", "그저 그가 나름대로 괜찮은 사람이라는 거지!"], ["But if one looks at men in all ways--are there many good ones left?", "한데 사람을 모든 면에서 따지고 들면 — 멀쩡한 자가 몇이나 남겠나?"], ["Why, I am sure I shouldn’t be worth a baked onion myself...", "아니, 나 자신부터가 구운 양파 한 알 값어치도 못 될 거라고 장담하네……."], ["perhaps with you thrown in.”", "어쩌면 자네까지 끼워 넣어도 그럴걸.\""]], [["“That’s too little; I’d give two for you.”", "\"그건 너무 박하군. 난 자네한테 양파 두 알은 쳐주겠는데.\""]], [["“And I wouldn’t give more than one for you.", "\"난 자네한테 한 알도 더는 안 쳐주겠어."], ["No more of your jokes!", "농담은 그만하자고!"], ["Zametov is no more than a boy.", "자메토프는 애송이에 지나지 않아."], ["I can pull his hair and one must draw him not repel him.", "머리채를 휘어잡을 만한 녀석이지만, 그런 자는 밀어내지 말고 끌어당겨야 하는 법이야."], ["You’ll never improve a man by repelling him, especially a boy.", "사람을 밀어내서야 절대 나아지게 만들 수 없어, 더구나 애송이는."], ["One has to be twice as careful with a boy.", "애송이는 두 배로 조심해서 다뤄야 한다고."], ["Oh, you progressive dullards!", "오, 이 진보적인 멍청이들 같으니!"], ["You don’t understand.", "자넨 이해를 못 해."], ["You harm yourselves running another man down....", "남을 깎아내리면서 제 발등을 찍는 거야……."], ["But if you want to know, we really have something in common.”", "한데 알고 싶다면 말해 주지, 우린 정말로 공통점이 좀 있다네.\""]], [["“I should like to know what.”", "\"그게 뭔지 알고 싶군.\""]], [["“Why, it’s all about a house-painter....", "\"아니, 그게 다 어떤 칠장이에 관한 일이라네……."], ["We are getting him out of a mess!", "우리가 그 친구를 곤경에서 빼내 주고 있거든!"], ["Though indeed there’s nothing to fear now.", "하긴 이제 두려워할 건 전혀 없지만."], ["The matter is absolutely self-evident.", "사건이야 더없이 자명하니까."], ["We only put on steam.”", "우린 그저 박차를 가하고 있을 뿐이야.\""]], [["“A painter?”", "\"칠장이라고?\""]], [["“Why, haven’t I told you about it?", "\"아니, 그 얘기 안 했던가?"], ["I only told you the beginning then about the murder of the old pawnbroker-woman.", "그때 자네한텐 늙은 전당포 노파가 살해된 일의 시작만 말해 줬었지."], ["Well, the painter is mixed up in it...”", "그런데 그 칠장이가 거기 얽혀 있다는 거야…….\""]], [["“Oh, I heard about that murder before and was rather interested in it... partly...", "\"오, 그 살인 사건이라면 전에 들었지, 그리고 좀 흥미를 느꼈어……, 부분적으로……, 어떤 이유 때문에……."], ["for one reason.... I read about it in the papers, too....”", "신문에서도 그 일을 읽었고…….\""]], [["“Lizaveta was murdered, too,”", "\"리자베타도 살해됐어요.\""], ["Nastasya blurted out, suddenly addressing Raskolnikov.", "나스타시야가 갑자기 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건네며 불쑥 끼어들었다."], ["She remained in the room all the time, standing by the door listening.", "그녀는 줄곧 방 안에 머물러 문가에 서서 듣고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Lizaveta,”", "\"리자베타라니.\""], ["murmured Raskolnikov hardly audibly.", "라스콜니코프가 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 작게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Lizaveta, who sold old clothes.", "\"리자베타요, 헌 옷 팔던 여자 말이에요."], ["Didn’t you know her?", "모르셨어요?"], ["She used to come here.", "여기도 자주 왔었는데."], ["She mended a shirt for you, too.”", "당신 셔츠도 한 번 기워 줬잖아요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov turned to the wall where in the dirty, yellow paper he picked out one clumsy, white flower with brown lines on it and began examining how many petals there were in it, how many scallops in the petals and how many lines on them.", "라스콜니코프는 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 더러운 노란 벽지에서 그는 갈색 줄무늬가 들어간 볼품없는 흰 꽃 한 송이를 골라내어, 거기에 꽃잎이 몇 개나 되는지, 꽃잎마다 가장자리 굴곡이 몇 개인지, 그 위에 줄무늬가 몇 개인지 하나하나 헤아리기 시작했다."], ["He felt his arms and legs as lifeless as though they had been cut off.", "그는 자기 팔다리가 마치 잘려 나간 것처럼 생기 없이 굳어 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He did not attempt to move, but stared obstinately at the flower.", "그는 움직이려 들지도 않은 채 고집스레 그 꽃만 노려보았다."]], [["“But what about the painter?”", "\"그래서 그 칠장이는 어떻게 됐는데?\""], ["Zossimov interrupted Nastasya’s chatter with marked displeasure.", "조시모프가 노골적인 못마땅함으로 나스타시야의 수다를 가로막았다."], ["She sighed and was silent.", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬고는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Why, he was accused of the murder,”", "\"아니, 그 친구가 살인범으로 몰렸다는 거야.\""], ["Razumihin went on hotly.", "라주미힌이 흥분해서 말을 이었다."]], [["“Was there evidence against him then?”", "\"그럼 그를 옭아맬 증거가 있었나?\""]], [["“Evidence, indeed!", "\"증거라니, 흥!"], ["Evidence that was no evidence, and that’s what we have to prove.", "증거랄 것도 못 되는 증거지, 바로 그걸 우리가 입증해야 하는 거고."], ["It was just as they pitched on those fellows, Koch and Pestryakov, at first.", "처음에 코흐와 페스트랴코프, 그자들한테 누명을 씌웠던 거랑 똑같아."], ["Foo!", "흥!"], ["how stupidly it’s all done, it makes one sick, though it’s not one’s business!", "죄다 어찌나 멍청하게 처리하는지, 내 일이 아닌데도 구역질이 날 지경이라니까! 페스트랴코프가 오늘 밤 올지도 몰라……."], ["Pestryakov may be coming to-night....", "그건 그렇고, 로쟈, 자넨 이미 이 일을 들어서 알지."], ["By the way, Rodya, you’ve heard about the business already; it happened before you were ill, the day before you fainted at the police office while they were talking about it.”", "자네가 앓아눕기 전에 벌어진 일이니까. 경찰서에서 사람들이 그 얘기를 하고 있을 때 자네가 기절했던 그 전날이지.\""]], [["Zossimov looked curiously at Raskolnikov.", "조시모프가 호기심 어린 눈으로 라스콜니코프를 바라보았다."], ["He did not stir.", "그는 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."]], [["“But I say, Razumihin, I wonder at you.", "\"한데 말이야, 라주미힌, 난 자네가 신기해."], ["What a busybody you are!”", "어쩜 그리 오지랖이 넓은가!\""], ["Zossimov observed.", "조시모프가 한마디 했다."]], [["“Maybe I am, but we will get him off anyway,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지, 어쨌든 우린 그 친구를 빼낼 거야.\""], ["shouted Razumihin, bringing his fist down on the table.", "라주미힌이 주먹으로 탁자를 내리치며 소리쳤다."], ["“What’s the most offensive is not their lying--one can always forgive lying--lying is a delightful thing, for it leads to truth--what is offensive is that they lie and worship their own lying....", "\"무엇보다 거슬리는 건 그들이 거짓말을 한다는 게 아니야 — 거짓말이야 언제든 용서할 수 있지 — 거짓말이란 즐거운 거라고, 진실로 이어지니까 — 거슬리는 건 그들이 거짓말을 하면서 제 거짓말을 떠받든다는 거지……."], ["I respect Porfiry, but...", "난 포르피리를 존경하네, 하지만……."], ["What threw them out at first?", "처음에 뭐가 그들을 헷갈리게 했는지 아나?"], ["The door was locked, and when they came back with the porter it was open.", "문이 잠겨 있었는데, 문지기를 데리고 다시 와 보니 열려 있었거든."], ["So it followed that Koch and Pestryakov were the murderers--that was their logic!”", "그러니 코흐와 페스트랴코프가 살인범이라는 거였어 — 그게 그들의 논리지!\""]], [["“But don’t excite yourself; they simply detained them, they could not help that....", "\"하지만 흥분하지 말게. 그들은 그저 두 사람을 붙잡아 둔 것뿐이야, 그럴 수밖에 없었던 거고……."], ["And, by the way, I’ve met that man Koch.", "그건 그렇고, 나도 그 코흐란 사람을 만난 적이 있어."], ["He used to buy unredeemed pledges from the old woman?", "그자가 노파한테서 기한 지난 저당물을 사들이곤 했다지?"], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["“Yes, he is a swindler.", "\"맞아, 사기꾼이지."], ["He buys up bad debts, too.", "악성 채권도 사들이고."], ["He makes a profession of it.", "그걸 업으로 삼는 자야."], ["But enough of him!", "한데 그 작자 얘긴 그만하지!"], ["Do you know what makes me angry?", "내가 뭣 때문에 화가 나는지 아나?"], ["It’s their sickening rotten, petrified routine....", "그들의 진절머리 나고 썩어 빠지고 화석처럼 굳어 버린 관행 때문이야……."], ["And this case might be the means of introducing a new method.", "그런데 이 사건이야말로 새로운 방법을 도입하는 계기가 될 수 있었어."], ["One can show from the psychological data alone how to get on the track of the real man.", "심리적 자료만으로도 진범의 자취를 어떻게 쫓아가는지 보여 줄 수 있단 말이지."], ["‘We have facts,’ they say.", "'우리한텐 사실이 있다'고들 하지."], ["But facts are not everything--at least half the business lies in how you interpret them!”", "하지만 사실이 전부는 아니야 — 적어도 일의 절반은 그것을 어떻게 해석하느냐에 달려 있다고!\""]], [["“Can you interpret them, then?”", "\"그럼 자넨 그것들을 해석할 수 있다는 건가?\""]], [["“Anyway, one can’t hold one’s tongue when one has a feeling, a tangible feeling, that one might be a help if only.... Eh!", "\"어쨌든, 만약에 자기가 도움이 될 수도 있겠다는 느낌이, 손에 잡힐 듯 생생한 느낌이 들면, 입을 다물고 있을 수는 없는 거야……."], ["Do you know the details of the case?”", "에이! 자넨 이 사건의 세세한 내막을 아나?\""]], [["“I am waiting to hear about the painter.”", "\"난 그 칠장이 얘기를 기다리고 있다네.\""]], [["“Oh, yes!", "\"오, 그렇지!"], ["Well, here’s the story.", "자, 그럼 얘기는 이렇네."], ["Early on the third day after the murder, when they were still dandling Koch and Pestryakov--though they accounted for every step they took and it was as plain as a pikestaff--an unexpected fact turned up.", "살인이 일어나고 사흘째 되던 날 아침 일찍, 그들이 아직 코흐와 페스트랴코프를 어르고 있을 때 — 하긴 그 둘은 자기 행적을 한 걸음 한 걸음 다 해명했고, 그게 대낮처럼 명백했지만 — 예기치 못한 사실 하나가 튀어나왔어. 그 집 맞은편에서 술집을 하는 두시킨이란 농사꾼이 금귀고리가 든 보석함을 경찰서로 가져와서는 장황한 사설을 늘어놓았다네."], ["A peasant called Dushkin, who keeps a dram-shop facing the house, brought to the police office a jeweller’s case containing some gold ear-rings, and told a long rigamarole.", "'그저께, 여덟 시가 막 지났을 무렵에' — 날짜와 시각을 잘 새겨 두게!"], ["‘The day before yesterday, just after eight o’clock’--mark the day and the hour!--‘a journeyman house-painter, Nikolay, who had been in to see me already that day, brought me this box of gold ear-rings and stones, and asked me to give him two roubles for them.", "— '그날 이미 한 번 절 찾아왔던 칠장이 견습공 니콜라이가 이 금귀고리와 보석이 든 상자를 가져와선, 이 루블만 빌려 달라고 했습니다. 어디서 났느냐고 물으니, 길에서 주웠다고 하더군요."], ["When I asked him where he got them, he said that he picked them up in the street.", "더는 캐묻지 않았습니다.' 이게 두시킨이 한 얘기야."], ["I did not ask him anything more.’ I am telling you Dushkin’s story.", "'한 장짜리 지폐를' — 그러니까 일 루블이지 — '그에게 줬습니다."], ["‘I gave him a note’--a rouble that is--‘for I thought if he did not pawn it with me he would with another.", "저한테 저당 잡히지 않으면 다른 데 가서 잡힐 테고, 어차피 술값으로 날려 버릴 게 뻔하니, 차라리 제가 갖고 있는 게 낫겠다 싶었거든요. 깊이 감출수록 빨리 찾는 법이고, 무슨 일이 생기거나 소문이라도 들리면 경찰에 갖다주려고요.'"], ["It would all come to the same thing--he’d spend it on drink, so the thing had better be with me.", "물론 죄다 허튼소리지. 거짓말을 입에 침도 안 바르고 하는 거야."], ["The further you hide it the quicker you will find it, and if anything turns up, if I hear any rumours, I’ll take it to the police.’ Of course, that’s all taradiddle; he lies like a horse, for I know this Dushkin, he is a pawnbroker and a receiver of stolen goods, and he did not cheat Nikolay out of a thirty-rouble trinket in order to give it to the police.", "나도 이 두시킨이란 자를 아는데, 전당포 주인에다 장물아비거든. 그자가 니콜라이한테서 삼십 루블짜리 패물을 가로채고선 그걸 경찰에 갖다 바칠 위인이 아니야."], ["He was simply afraid.", "그냥 겁이 났던 거지."], ["But no matter, to return to Dushkin’s story.", "어쨌든 그건 됐고, 두시킨 얘기로 돌아가지."], ["‘I’ve known this peasant, Nikolay Dementyev, from a child; he comes from the same province and district of Zaraïsk, we are both Ryazan men.", "'저는 이 니콜라이 데멘티예프란 농사꾼을 어릴 적부터 알았습니다. 자라이스크의 같은 군, 같은 면 출신이라, 둘 다 랴잔 사람이지요."], ["And though Nikolay is not a drunkard, he drinks, and I knew he had a job in that house, painting work with Dmitri, who comes from the same village, too.", "니콜라이가 술꾼은 아니어도 술을 마시긴 하는데, 그가 그 집에서 일하는 걸 알고 있었습니다, 역시 같은 마을 출신인 드미트리랑 칠일을 하고 있었지요."], ["As soon as he got the rouble he changed it, had a couple of glasses, took his change and went out.", "일 루블을 받자마자 그걸 헐어서 두어 잔 들이켜고는, 거스름돈을 챙겨 나가더군요."], ["But I did not see Dmitri with him then.", "그땐 드미트리가 같이 있는 걸 보진 못했습니다."], ["And the next day I heard that someone had murdered Alyona Ivanovna and her sister, Lizaveta Ivanovna, with an axe.", "그런데 이튿날, 누군가 도끼로 알료나 이바노브나와 그 동생 리자베타 이바노브나를 죽였다는 얘기를 들었지요."], ["I knew them, and I felt suspicious about the ear-rings at once, for I knew the murdered woman lent money on pledges.", "전 그 두 사람을 알았고, 그 귀고리에 대해 곧장 의심이 들었습니다, 죽은 그 여자가 저당 잡고 돈을 빌려준다는 걸 알았으니까요."], ["I went to the house, and began to make careful inquiries without saying a word to anyone.", "그래서 그 집에 가서, 아무한테도 말 한마디 없이 조심스레 알아보기 시작했습니다."], ["First of all I asked, “Is Nikolay here?”", "우선 \"니콜라이가 여기 있소?\" 하고 물었지요."], ["Dmitri told me that Nikolay had gone off on the spree; he had come home at daybreak drunk, stayed in the house about ten minutes, and went out again.", "드미트리가 말하기를, 니콜라이는 놀러 나갔다는 겁니다. 동틀 녘에 술에 취해 들어와선 십 분쯤 집에 있다가 다시 나갔다고요."], ["Dmitri didn’t see him again and is finishing the job alone.", "드미트리는 그 후로 그를 못 봤고 혼자 일을 마무리하고 있었습니다."], ["And their job is on the same staircase as the murder, on the second floor.", "그리고 그들이 일하는 곳이 살인이 난 그 계단, 이 층이었지요."], ["When I heard all that I did not say a word to anyone’--that’s Dushkin’s tale--‘but I found out what I could about the murder, and went home feeling as suspicious as ever.", "그 얘길 다 듣고도 전 아무한테도 한마디 안 했습니다' — 이게 두시킨이 한 말이야 — '하지만 살인에 대해 알아낼 수 있는 건 다 알아내고, 여전히 의심을 품은 채 집으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["And at eight o’clock this morning’--that was the third day, you understand--‘I saw Nikolay coming in, not sober, though not to say very drunk--he could understand what was said to him.", "그리고 오늘 아침 여덟 시에' — 그러니까 사흘째 되는 날이지, 알겠나 — '니콜라이가 들어오는 걸 봤습니다, 멀쩡하진 않았지만 그렇다고 곤드레만드레도 아니어서 — 무슨 말을 하면 알아들을 정도는 됐지요."], ["He sat down on the bench and did not speak.", "그가 의자에 앉더니 말이 없었습니다."], ["There was only one stranger in the bar and a man I knew asleep on a bench and our two boys.", "술집엔 낯선 손님 하나에 제가 아는 사내 하나가 의자에서 자고 있었고, 우리 집 아이 둘이 있었지요. \"드미트리 봤나?\""], ["“Have you seen Dmitri?”", "하고 제가 물었습니다."], ["said I. “No, I haven’t,”", "\"아뇨, 못 봤습니다\""], ["said he.", "하더군요."], ["“And you’ve not been here either?”", "\"그럼 자네도 여기 안 왔었나?\""], ["“Not since the day before yesterday,”", "\"그저께 이후로는요.\" \"어젯밤엔 어디서 잤나?\""], ["said he. “And where did you sleep last night?”", "\"페스키에서, 콜로멘스키 사람들이랑요.\""], ["“In Peski, with the Kolomensky men.”", "\"그 귀고린 어디서 났고?\""], ["“And where did you get those ear-rings?”", "하고 물었지요. \"길에서 주웠습니다\""], ["I asked. “I found them in the street,”", "하는데, 말하는 품새가 어딘가 좀 이상했어요."], ["and the way he said it was a bit queer; he did not look at me.", "절 쳐다보지도 않더군요."], ["“Did you hear what happened that very evening, at that very hour, on that same staircase?”", "\"바로 그날 저녁, 바로 그 시각에, 바로 그 계단에서 무슨 일이 있었는지 들었나?\""], ["said I. “No,”", "하고 제가 물었습니다."], ["said he, “I had not heard,”", "\"아뇨, 못 들었는데요\""], ["and all the while he was listening, his eyes were staring out of his head and he turned as white as chalk.", "하면서도, 내내 귀를 기울이고 있었고, 눈은 튀어나올 듯하더니 백묵처럼 하얗게 질려 가더군요."], ["I told him all about it and he took his hat and began getting up.", "제가 다 말해 주니까 그가 모자를 집어 들고 일어서려는 겁니다."], ["I wanted to keep him. “Wait a bit, Nikolay,”", "붙들고 싶어서 \"잠깐만, 니콜라이\""], ["said I, “won’t you have a drink?”", "하고 말했지요, \"한잔 안 할 텐가?\""], ["And I signed to the boy to hold the door, and I came out from behind the bar; but he darted out and down the street to the turning at a run.", "그러면서 아이한테 문을 막으라고 눈짓을 하고 바 뒤에서 나왔습니다. 한데 그가 휙 뛰쳐나가더니 길을 따라 모퉁이까지 냅다 달아나는 거예요."], ["I have not seen him since.", "그 후론 못 봤습니다."], ["Then my doubts were at an end--it was his doing, as clear as could be....’”", "그러니 제 의심도 끝난 거지요 — 그자 짓이었던 겁니다, 더할 나위 없이 명백하게…….'\""]], [["“I should think so,”", "\"내 생각도 그렇네.\""], ["said Zossimov.", "조시모프가 말했다."]], [["“Wait! Hear the end. Of course they sought high and low for Nikolay; they detained Dushkin and searched his house; Dmitri, too, was arrested; the Kolomensky men also were turned inside out. And the day before yesterday they arrested Nikolay in a tavern at the end of the town. He had gone there, taken the silver cross off his neck and asked for a dram for it. They gave it to him. A few minutes afterwards the woman went to the cowshed, and through a crack in the wall she saw in the stable adjoining he had made a noose of his sash from the beam, stood on a block of wood, and was trying to put his neck in the noose. The woman screeched her hardest; people ran in. ‘So that’s what you are up to!’ ‘Take me,’ he says, ‘to such-and-such a police officer; I’ll confess everything.’ Well, they took him to that police station--that is here--with a suitable escort. So they asked him this and that, how old he is, ‘twenty-two,’ and so on. At the question, ‘When you were working with Dmitri, didn’t you see anyone on the staircase at such-and-such a time?’--answer: ‘To be sure folks may have gone up and down, but I did not notice them.’ ‘And didn’t you hear anything, any noise, and so on?’ ‘We heard nothing special.’ ‘And did you hear, Nikolay, that on the same day Widow So-and-so and her sister were murdered and robbed?’ ‘I never knew a thing about it. The first I heard of it was from Afanasy Pavlovitch the day before yesterday.’ ‘And where did you find the ear-rings?’ ‘I found them on the pavement.’ ‘Why didn’t you go to work with Dmitri the other day?’ ‘Because I was drinking.’ ‘And where were you drinking?’ ‘Oh, in such-and-such a place.’ ‘Why did you run away from Dushkin’s?’ ‘Because I was awfully frightened.’ ‘What were you frightened of?’ ‘That I should be accused.’ ‘How could you be frightened, if you felt free from guilt?’ Now, Zossimov, you may not believe me, that question was put literally in those words. I know it for a fact, it was repeated to me exactly! What do you say to that?”", "\"잠깐! 끝까지 들어 보게. 물론 그들은 사방팔방으로 니콜라이를 찾아 헤맸지. 두시킨을 붙잡아 두고 그 집을 수색했고, 드미트리도 체포했어. 콜로멘스키 사람들도 탈탈 털렸고. 그러다 그저께 도시 변두리의 어느 선술집에서 니콜라이를 붙잡았다네. 거기에 가서, 목에 걸고 있던 은십자가를 풀어 술 한 잔과 바꿔 달라고 했다는 거야. 사람들이 술을 줬지. 몇 분 뒤에 술집 여자가 외양간에 갔다가 벽 틈으로 들여다보니, 옆에 붙은 마구간에서 그가 들보에 허리띠로 올가미를 매달아 놓고, 나무토막 위에 올라서서 목을 그 올가미에 넣으려 하고 있더라는 거야. 여자가 있는 힘껏 비명을 질렀고, 사람들이 달려갔지. '아니, 이게 무슨 짓이야!' '날 데려가 주시오' 하고 그가 말했어, '아무개 경관한테 말이오. 다 자백하겠소.' 자, 그래서 적당한 호송을 붙여 그를 경찰서로 — 그러니까 여기로 — 데려갔지. 그래서 이것저것 물었어, 나이가 몇이냐, '스물둘입니다', 뭐 그런 식으로. '드미트리와 함께 일하고 있을 때, 아무 시각에 계단에서 누굴 못 봤느냐?'는 물음에 — 대답이, '뭐 사람들이야 오르내렸겠지만, 눈여겨보진 않았습니다.' '그럼 아무 소리도, 무슨 소음 같은 것도 못 들었느냐?' '별다른 건 못 들었습니다.' '그럼 니콜라이, 같은 날 아무개 과부와 그 동생이 살해당하고 강도를 당했다는 걸 들었느냐?' '그 일은 전혀 몰랐습니다. 그저께 아파나시 파블로비치한테 처음 들었습니다.' '그럼 그 귀고린 어디서 났느냐?' '길바닥에서 주웠습니다.' '그날 왜 드미트리랑 같이 일하러 안 갔느냐?' '술을 마시느라요.' '어디서 마셨느냐?' '아, 아무 데서요.' '왜 두시킨네서 달아났느냐?' '몹시 겁이 나서요.' '뭐가 무서웠느냐?' '제가 누명을 쓸까 봐요.' '죄가 없다고 느꼈다면 어째서 무서웠겠느냐?' 자, 조시모프, 자넨 못 믿을지도 모르지만, 그 질문은 글자 그대로 그렇게 던져진 거야. 난 그게 사실인 걸 알아, 토씨 하나 안 틀리고 내게 전해 줬으니까! 자넨 이걸 어떻게 생각하나?\""]], [["“Well, anyway, there’s the evidence.”", "\"뭐, 어쨌든, 증거는 거기 있는 셈이군.\""]], [["“I am not talking of the evidence now, I am talking about that question, of their own idea of themselves.", "\"난 지금 증거 얘길 하는 게 아니야, 그 질문 얘길 하는 거지, 자기 자신들에 대한 그들의 생각 말이야. 자, 그래서 그를 들들 볶고 또 볶으니까 자백을 했어."], ["Well, so they squeezed and squeezed him and he confessed: ‘I did not find it in the street, but in the flat where I was painting with Dmitri.’ ‘And how was that?’ ‘Why, Dmitri and I were painting there all day, and we were just getting ready to go, and Dmitri took a brush and painted my face, and he ran off and I after him.", "'길에서 주운 게 아니라, 드미트리랑 칠을 하던 그 셋집에서 주웠습니다.' '그럼 어떻게 된 거냐?'"], ["I ran after him, shouting my hardest, and at the bottom of the stairs I ran right against the porter and some gentlemen--and how many gentlemen were there I don’t remember.", "'아니, 드미트리랑 제가 거기서 온종일 칠을 하다가, 막 나가려던 참이었는데, 드미트리가 솔을 집어 들고 제 얼굴에 칠을 하고선 달아나는 거예요. 그래서 제가 쫓아갔지요."], ["And the porter swore at me, and the other porter swore, too, and the porter’s wife came out, and swore at us, too; and a gentleman came into the entry with a lady, and he swore at us, too, for Dmitri and I lay right across the way.", "있는 힘껏 소리치며 뒤쫓아 내려가다가, 계단 맨 아래서 문지기랑 신사 몇 분이랑 정면으로 부딪쳤습니다 — 신사가 몇이었는지는 기억이 안 나요. 문지기가 욕을 퍼붓고, 다른 문지기도 욕을 하고, 문지기 마누라도 나와서 우리한테 욕을 했지요."], ["I got hold of Dmitri’s hair and knocked him down and began beating him.", "그리고 어떤 신사가 부인이랑 입구로 들어오다가, 우리한테 또 욕을 했습니다, 드미트리랑 제가 길을 가로막고 누워 있었으니까요."], ["And Dmitri, too, caught me by the hair and began beating me.", "제가 드미트리의 머리채를 휘어잡아 넘어뜨리고 두들겨 패기 시작했지요."], ["But we did it all not for temper but in a friendly way, for sport.", "그러자 드미트리도 제 머리채를 붙잡고 두들겨 패는 겁니다."], ["And then Dmitri escaped and ran into the street, and I ran after him; but I did not catch him, and went back to the flat alone; I had to clear up my things.", "한데 그게 다 홧김이 아니라, 그저 장난삼아, 친한 사이라서 그런 거였어요. 그러고는 드미트리가 빠져나가 길로 달아났고, 제가 뒤를 쫓았지요."], ["I began putting them together, expecting Dmitri to come, and there in the passage, in the corner by the door, I stepped on the box.", "한데 못 잡고 혼자 셋집으로 돌아갔습니다, 짐을 챙겨야 했거든요. 드미트리가 오려니 하며 짐을 한데 모으기 시작했는데, 거기 통로 문 옆 구석에서 그 상자를 밟았지 뭡니까."], ["I saw it lying there wrapped up in paper.", "그게 종이에 싸여 거기 놓여 있는 게 보이더군요."], ["I took off the paper, saw some little hooks, undid them, and in the box were the ear-rings....’”", "종이를 벗기니 작은 갈고리들이 있어서 그걸 끄르니까, 상자 안에 귀고리가…….'\""]], [["“Behind the door?", "\"문 뒤에라고?"], ["Lying behind the door?", "문 뒤에 놓여 있었다고?"], ["Behind the door?”", "문 뒤에?\""], ["Raskolnikov cried suddenly, staring with a blank look of terror at Razumihin, and he slowly sat up on the sofa, leaning on his hand.", "라스콜니코프가 느닷없이 외치며, 공포로 멍해진 눈길로 라주미힌을 노려보더니, 한 손으로 몸을 짚으며 천천히 소파에서 일어나 앉았다."]], [["“Yes... why?", "\"그래……, 왜?"], ["What’s the matter?", "무슨 일이야?"], ["What’s wrong?”", "뭐가 잘못됐나?\""], ["Razumihin, too, got up from his seat.", "라주미힌도 자기 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아냐.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered faintly, turning to the wall.", "라스콜니코프가 힘없이 대답하며 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다."], ["All were silent for a while.", "잠시 모두가 말이 없었다."]], [["“He must have waked from a dream,”", "\"꿈에서 막 깬 모양이군.\""], ["Razumihin said at last, looking inquiringly at Zossimov.", "마침내 라주미힌이 조시모프를 캐묻듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["The latter slightly shook his head.", "조시모프는 가볍게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Well, go on,”", "\"자, 계속하게.\""], ["said Zossimov.", "조시모프가 말했다."], ["“What next?”", "\"그다음은?\""]], [["“What next? As soon as he saw the ear-rings, forgetting Dmitri and everything, he took up his cap and ran to Dushkin and, as we know, got a rouble from him. He told a lie saying he found them in the street, and went off drinking. He keeps repeating his old story about the murder: ‘I know nothing of it, never heard of it till the day before yesterday.’ ‘And why didn’t you come to the police till now?’ ‘I was frightened.’ ‘And why did you try to hang yourself?’ ‘From anxiety.’ ‘What anxiety?’ ‘That I should be accused of it.’ Well, that’s the whole story. And now what do you suppose they deduced from that?”", "\"그다음? 그가 귀고리를 보자마자, 드미트리고 뭐고 다 잊어버리고 모자를 집어 들고 두시킨한테 달려갔지, 그래서 우리도 알다시피 그한테서 일 루블을 받았고. 길에서 주웠다고 거짓말을 하고는 술을 마시러 갔지. 그리고 살인에 대해선 줄곧 같은 얘길 되풀이해. '그건 전혀 모릅니다, 그저께까지 들어 본 적도 없어요.' '그럼 왜 이제껏 경찰에 안 왔느냐?' '겁이 나서요.' '그럼 왜 목을 매려 했느냐?' '불안해서요.' '무슨 불안?' '그 일로 누명을 쓸까 봐서요.' 자, 이게 전부야. 그래, 자넨 그들이 거기서 무얼 추론해 냈을 것 같나?\""]], [["“Why, there’s no supposing.", "\"아니, 추론할 것도 없지."], ["There’s a clue, such as it is, a fact.", "단서가, 그런대로 단서가, 사실 하나가 있잖나."], ["You wouldn’t have your painter set free?”", "자네라고 그 칠장이를 풀어 주자고는 못 할걸?\""]], [["“Now they’ve simply taken him for the murderer.", "\"그들은 이제 그냥 그를 살인범으로 단정해 버렸어."], ["They haven’t a shadow of doubt.”", "한 점 의심도 없이.\""]], [["“That’s nonsense.", "\"그건 말도 안 돼."], ["You are excited.", "자넨 흥분하고 있어."], ["But what about the ear-rings?", "한데 그 귀고리는 어떻게 된 건가?"], ["You must admit that, if on the very same day and hour ear-rings from the old woman’s box have come into Nikolay’s hands, they must have come there somehow.", "바로 그날 그 시각에 노파의 상자에서 나온 귀고리가 니콜라이의 손에 들어왔다면, 어떻게든 거기로 들어간 게 분명하다는 건 자네도 인정해야지."], ["That’s a good deal in such a case.”", "이런 사건에선 그게 상당한 무게를 지니거든.\""]], [["“How did they get there?", "\"어떻게 거기로 들어갔느냐고?"], ["How did they get there?”", "어떻게 거기로 들어갔겠나?\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 소리쳤다."], ["“How can you, a doctor, whose duty it is to study man and who has more opportunity than anyone else for studying human nature--how can you fail to see the character of the man in the whole story?", "\"자네 같은 의사가, 인간을 연구하는 게 본분이고 그 누구보다 인간 본성을 연구할 기회가 많은 자네가 — 어떻게 그 모든 이야기 속에서 그 사내의 됨됨이를 못 본단 말인가?"], ["Don’t you see at once that the answers he has given in the examination are the holy truth?", "그가 심문에서 한 대답이 신성한 진실이란 걸 단박에 모르겠나?"], ["They came into his hand precisely as he has told us--he stepped on the box and picked it up.”", "귀고리는 정확히 그가 우리한테 말한 그대로 그의 손에 들어간 거야 — 상자를 밟고 그걸 집어 든 거라고.\""]], [["“The holy truth!", "\"신성한 진실이라!"], ["But didn’t he own himself that he told a lie at first?”", "하지만 그자 스스로 처음엔 거짓말을 했다고 인정하지 않았나?\""]], [["“Listen to me, listen attentively.", "\"내 말을 듣게, 잘 들어 보라고."], ["The porter and Koch and Pestryakov and the other porter and the wife of the first porter and the woman who was sitting in the porter’s lodge and the man Kryukov, who had just got out of a cab at that minute and went in at the entry with a lady on his arm, that is eight or ten witnesses, agree that Nikolay had Dmitri on the ground, was lying on him beating him, while Dmitri hung on to his hair, beating him, too.", "문지기와 코흐와 페스트랴코프, 그리고 다른 문지기, 첫 번째 문지기의 마누라, 문지기 방에 앉아 있던 여자, 그리고 바로 그 순간 마차에서 내려 부인을 팔에 끼고 입구로 들어온 크류코프라는 사내, 그러니까 여덟에서 열 명쯤 되는 증인들이 입을 모아, 니콜라이가 드미트리를 바닥에 깔고 올라타 두들겨 패고 있었고, 한편 드미트리는 그의 머리채에 매달려 역시 그를 패고 있었다고 증언했어."], ["They lay right across the way, blocking the thoroughfare.", "둘이 길을 정면으로 가로막고 통로를 틀어막은 채 누워 있었던 거지."], ["They were sworn at on all sides while they ‘like children’ (the very words of the witnesses) were falling over one another, squealing, fighting and laughing with the funniest faces, and, chasing one another like children, they ran into the street.", "사방에서 욕을 먹는 동안에도 그 둘은 '어린애들처럼'(증인들이 한 말 그대로야) 서로 뒤엉켜 나뒹굴며, 꽥꽥대고 치고받고 더없이 우스꽝스러운 표정으로 웃어 대다가, 어린애들처럼 서로를 쫓으며 길로 달려 나갔다는 거야."], ["Now take careful note.", "자, 잘 새겨듣게."], ["The bodies upstairs were warm, you understand, warm when they found them!", "위층의 시신들은 아직 따뜻했어, 알겠나, 발견됐을 때 따뜻했단 말이야!"], ["If they, or Nikolay alone, had murdered them and broken open the boxes, or simply taken part in the robbery, allow me to ask you one question: do their state of mind, their squeals and giggles and childish scuffling at the gate fit in with axes, bloodshed, fiendish cunning, robbery?", "만약 그 둘이, 아니면 니콜라이 혼자서 그들을 죽이고 상자를 부숴 열었거나, 그저 강도질에 가담만 했다 치더라도, 자네한테 한 가지만 묻겠네. 그들의 정신 상태가, 그 꽥꽥거림과 낄낄거림과 대문간에서의 어린애 같은 몸싸움이, 도끼며 유혈이며 악마 같은 교활함이며 강도질과 들어맞는가?"], ["They’d just killed them, not five or ten minutes before, for the bodies were still warm, and at once, leaving the flat open, knowing that people would go there at once, flinging away their booty, they rolled about like children, laughing and attracting general attention.", "그들은 막 사람을 죽인 거야, 채 오 분도 십 분도 안 됐어, 시신이 아직 따뜻했으니까. 그런데 곧장 셋집 문을 열어 둔 채로, 사람들이 당장 거기로 갈 걸 뻔히 알면서, 훔친 물건을 내팽개치고는, 어린애들처럼 나뒹굴며 웃어 대고 뭇사람의 눈길을 끌었단 말이지."], ["And there are a dozen witnesses to swear to that!”", "게다가 그걸 증언할 증인이 열두 명이나 된다고!\""]], [["“Of course it is strange!", "\"물론 이상하긴 하군!"], ["It’s impossible, indeed, but...”", "정말이지 있을 수 없는 일이야, 하지만…….\""]], [["“No, brother, no _buts_.", "\"아니, 이 사람아, 하지만은 없어."], ["And if the ear-rings being found in Nikolay’s hands at the very day and hour of the murder constitutes an important piece of circumstantial evidence against him--although the explanation given by him accounts for it, and therefore it does not tell seriously against him--one must take into consideration the facts which prove him innocent, especially as they are facts that _cannot be denied_.", "그리고 만약 살인이 일어난 바로 그날 그 시각에 니콜라이의 손에서 귀고리가 발견된 것이 그에게 불리한 중요한 정황 증거가 된다 해도 — 그가 한 해명이 그걸 설명해 주고, 따라서 그에게 결정적으로 불리하게 작용하진 못하지만 — 그가 무죄임을 입증하는 사실들도 고려해야 하는 거야, 더구나 그건 부인할 수 없는 사실들이거든."], ["And do you suppose, from the character of our legal system, that they will accept, or that they are in a position to accept, this fact--resting simply on a psychological impossibility--as irrefutable and conclusively breaking down the circumstantial evidence for the prosecution?", "그런데 자넨 우리 사법 제도의 성격으로 미루어, 그들이 이 사실을 — 순전히 심리적 불가능성에 근거한 이 사실을 — 반박할 수 없는 것으로, 검찰 측의 정황 증거를 결정적으로 무너뜨리는 것으로 받아들일 거라고, 또는 받아들일 입장이라고 보나? 아니, 그들은 받아들이지 않을 거야, 결코 받아들이지 않아."], ["No, they won’t accept it, they certainly won’t, because they found the jewel-case and the man tried to hang himself, ‘which he could not have done if he hadn’t felt guilty.’ That’s the point, that’s what excites me, you must understand!”", "보석함을 찾아냈고 그 사내가 목을 매려 했으니까, '죄가 없었다면 그러지 않았을 것'이라는 거지. 바로 그게 핵심이야, 바로 그게 날 흥분하게 만든다고, 자네가 좀 알아줘야 해!\""]], [["“Oh, I see you are excited!", "\"오, 자네가 흥분한 건 알겠네!"], ["Wait a bit.", "잠깐만."], ["I forgot to ask you; what proof is there that the box came from the old woman?”", "한 가지 물어보는 걸 깜빡했군. 그 상자가 노파한테서 나온 거라는 증거는 뭔가?\""]], [["“That’s been proved,”", "\"그건 입증됐어.\""], ["said Razumihin with apparent reluctance, frowning.", "라주미힌이 인상을 찌푸리며 마지못한 듯 대답했다."], ["“Koch recognised the jewel-case and gave the name of the owner, who proved conclusively that it was his.”", "\"코흐가 그 보석함을 알아보고 주인 이름을 댔는데, 그 주인이 자기 것임을 확실하게 입증했지.\""]], [["“That’s bad.", "\"그거 안 좋은데."], ["Now another point.", "자, 또 한 가지."], ["Did anyone see Nikolay at the time that Koch and Pestryakov were going upstairs at first, and is there no evidence about that?”", "코흐와 페스트랴코프가 처음 위층으로 올라가던 그 시각에 니콜라이를 본 사람이 있나, 그에 관한 증언은 없고?\""]], [["“Nobody did see him,”", "\"본 사람은 아무도 없어.\""], ["Razumihin answered with vexation.", "라주미힌이 분한 듯 대답했다."], ["“That’s the worst of it.", "\"그게 가장 골치 아픈 점이야."], ["Even Koch and Pestryakov did not notice them on their way upstairs, though, indeed, their evidence could not have been worth much.", "코흐와 페스트랴코프조차 위층으로 올라가는 길에 그들을 못 봤다는군, 하긴 그자들 증언이야 큰 값어치도 없겠지만."], ["They said they saw the flat was open, and that there must be work going on in it, but they took no special notice and could not remember whether there actually were men at work in it.”", "그들은 셋집 문이 열려 있는 걸 봤고, 그 안에서 무슨 작업이 진행 중인 게 분명하다고 봤다지만, 특별히 눈여겨보진 않아서 실제로 거기 일하는 사람들이 있었는지는 기억하지 못한다더군.\""]], [["“Hm!... So the only evidence for the defence is that they were beating one another and laughing.", "\"흠!……, 그렇다면 변호에 쓸 유일한 증거는 그들이 서로 치고받으며 웃고 있었다는 것뿐이군."], ["That constitutes a strong presumption, but...", "그게 강력한 추정 근거가 되긴 하지만……."], ["How do you explain the facts yourself?”", "자네는 그 사실들을 어떻게 설명하나?\""]], [["“How do I explain them?", "\"내가 어떻게 설명하느냐고?"], ["What is there to explain?", "설명할 게 뭐 있나?"], ["It’s clear.", "명백하잖아."], ["At any rate, the direction in which explanation is to be sought is clear, and the jewel-case points to it.", "적어도 설명을 찾아야 할 방향만큼은 명백해, 그리고 그 보석함이 그 방향을 가리키고 있지."], ["The real murderer dropped those ear-rings.", "진범이 그 귀고리를 떨어뜨린 거야."], ["The murderer was upstairs, locked in, when Koch and Pestryakov knocked at the door.", "코흐와 페스트랴코프가 문을 두드렸을 때, 살인범은 위층에서 안에 갇혀 있었던 거지."], ["Koch, like an ass, did not stay at the door; so the murderer popped out and ran down, too; for he had no other way of escape.", "코흐가 멍청하게도 문 앞에 머물러 있지 않았으니, 살인범은 톡 튀어나와 역시 아래로 달아난 거야. 달리 빠져나갈 길이 없었으니까."], ["He hid from Koch, Pestryakov and the porter in the flat when Nikolay and Dmitri had just run out of it.", "니콜라이와 드미트리가 막 뛰쳐나간 그 셋집에서, 그는 코흐와 페스트랴코프와 문지기를 피해 몸을 숨긴 거지."], ["He stopped there while the porter and others were going upstairs, waited till they were out of hearing, and then went calmly downstairs at the very minute when Dmitri and Nikolay ran out into the street and there was no one in the entry; possibly he was seen, but not noticed.", "문지기와 다른 사람들이 위층으로 올라가는 동안 거기 머물러 있다가, 그들의 인기척이 들리지 않을 때까지 기다린 다음, 드미트리와 니콜라이가 길로 뛰쳐나가 입구에 아무도 없는 바로 그 순간에 태연히 아래층으로 내려간 거야. 어쩌면 누군가 그를 봤겠지만, 눈여겨보진 않았겠지."], ["There are lots of people going in and out.", "드나드는 사람이 워낙 많으니까."], ["He must have dropped the ear-rings out of his pocket when he stood behind the door, and did not notice he dropped them, because he had other things to think of.", "그는 문 뒤에 서 있을 때 주머니에서 귀고리를 떨어뜨린 게 분명해, 그리고 떨어뜨린 줄도 몰랐던 거지, 신경 쓸 다른 일들이 있었으니까."], ["The jewel-case is a conclusive proof that he did stand there....", "그 보석함이 바로 그가 거기 서 있었다는 결정적인 증거야……."], ["That’s how I explain it.”", "난 그렇게 설명하네.\""]], [["“Too clever!", "\"너무 영리하군!"], ["No, my boy, you’re too clever.", "아니, 이 친구야, 자넨 너무 영리해."], ["That beats everything.”", "그건 정말 압권이야.\""]], [["“But, why, why?”", "\"한데 왜, 왜 그러나?\""]], [["“Why, because everything fits too well... it’s too melodramatic.”", "\"왜냐하면 모든 게 너무 딱 들어맞으니까……, 너무 신파조라고.\""]], [["“A-ach!”", "\"아-아!\""], ["Razumihin was exclaiming, but at that moment the door opened and a personage came in who was a stranger to all present.", "라주미힌이 막 외치려던 참에, 바로 그 순간 문이 열리며 그 자리에 있던 모두에게 낯선 한 인물이 들어왔다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["This was a gentleman no longer young, of a stiff and portly appearance, and a cautious and sour countenance.", "이자는 더 이상 젊지 않은, 뻣뻣하고 풍채 좋은 신사로, 조심스럽고 떨떠름한 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["He began by stopping short in the doorway, staring about him with offensive and undisguised astonishment, as though asking himself what sort of place he had come to.", "그는 우선 문간에 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 마치 자기가 도대체 어떤 곳에 들어선 것인가를 스스로에게 묻기라도 하듯 무례하고 노골적인 놀라움으로 주변을 둘러보았다."], ["Mistrustfully and with an affectation of being alarmed and almost affronted, he scanned Raskolnikov’s low and narrow “cabin.”", "불신에 차서, 그리고 짐짓 놀란 듯, 거의 모욕이라도 당한 듯한 태도로, 그는 라스콜니코프의 나지막하고 좁은 '선실'을 훑어보았다."], ["With the same amazement he stared at Raskolnikov, who lay undressed, dishevelled, unwashed, on his miserable dirty sofa, looking fixedly at him.", "그 똑같은 경악의 표정으로 그는 라스콜니코프를 빤히 바라보았는데, 그는 옷도 제대로 걸치지 않고 헝클어진 채 씻지도 않은 모습으로 그 초라하고 더러운 소파에 누워 그를 뚫어지게 쳐다보고 있었다."], ["Then with the same deliberation he scrutinised the uncouth, unkempt figure and unshaven face of Razumihin, who looked him boldly and inquiringly in the face without rising from his seat.", "그러고는 또 그 똑같은 신중한 태도로, 자리에서 일어나지도 않은 채 대담하고 캐묻듯 그의 얼굴을 마주 보고 있던 라주미힌의 우락부락하고 헝클어진 행색과 면도하지 않은 얼굴을 자세히 뜯어보았다."], ["A constrained silence lasted for a couple of minutes, and then, as might be expected, some scene-shifting took place.", "어색한 침묵이 한 이 분쯤 이어졌고, 이윽고 으레 그렇듯 무대 전환 같은 것이 일어났다."], ["Reflecting, probably from certain fairly unmistakable signs, that he would get nothing in this “cabin” by attempting to overawe them, the gentleman softened somewhat, and civilly, though with some severity, emphasising every syllable of his question, addressed Zossimov:", "아마도 어지간히 분명한 몇 가지 징후로 미루어, 이 '선실'에서 위세를 부려 본들 아무것도 얻을 게 없으리라는 것을 깨달았는지, 신사는 다소 누그러져서, 비록 약간의 엄격함은 남아 있었지만 정중하게, 자기 질문의 음절 하나하나에 힘을 주며 조시모프를 향해 말했다."]], [["“Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov, a student, or formerly a student?”", "\"로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프, 대학생, 아니면 한때 대학생이었던 분 맞소?\""]], [["Zossimov made a slight movement, and would have answered, had not Razumihin anticipated him.", "조시모프는 가볍게 몸을 움직이며 대답하려 했으나, 라주미힌이 한발 앞섰다."]], [["“Here he is lying on the sofa!", "\"여기 소파에 누워 있는 사람이오!"], ["What do you want?”", "무슨 일이시오?\""]], [["This familiar “what do you want” seemed to cut the ground from the feet of the pompous gentleman.", "이 허물없는 \"무슨 일이시오\"는 거드름 피우는 신사의 발밑을 단번에 허물어 버리는 것 같았다."], ["He was turning to Razumihin, but checked himself in time and turned to Zossimov again.", "그는 라주미힌 쪽으로 몸을 돌리려다가, 제때 마음을 고쳐먹고 다시 조시모프를 향했다."]], [["“This is Raskolnikov,”", "\"이쪽이 라스콜니코프요.\""], ["mumbled Zossimov, nodding towards him.", "조시모프가 그를 향해 고갯짓하며 우물거렸다."], ["Then he gave a prolonged yawn, opening his mouth as wide as possible.", "그러고는 길게 하품을 하며 입을 한껏 크게 벌렸다."], ["Then he lazily put his hand into his waistcoat-pocket, pulled out a huge gold watch in a round hunter’s case, opened it, looked at it and as slowly and lazily proceeded to put it back.", "이어 그는 나른하게 조끼 주머니에 손을 넣더니, 둥근 사냥꾼용 뚜껑이 달린 커다란 금시계를 꺼내어 뚜껑을 열고 들여다본 다음, 똑같이 느리고 나른한 동작으로 다시 집어넣었다."]], [["Raskolnikov himself lay without speaking, on his back, gazing persistently, though without understanding, at the stranger.", "라스콜니코프 자신은 말없이 반듯이 누운 채, 영문도 모르면서 그 낯선 사람을 집요하게 바라보고 있었다."], ["Now that his face was turned away from the strange flower on the paper, it was extremely pale and wore a look of anguish, as though he had just undergone an agonising operation or just been taken from the rack.", "벽지의 그 기묘한 꽃무늬에서 얼굴을 돌리고 나니, 그의 얼굴은 극도로 창백했고, 마치 방금 끔찍한 수술을 받았거나 고문대에서 막 끌려 내려오기라도 한 듯 고뇌의 빛을 띠고 있었다."], ["But the new-comer gradually began to arouse his attention, then his wonder, then suspicion and even alarm.", "그러나 이 새로운 방문객은 차츰 그의 주의를 끌더니, 이어 호기심을, 그다음에는 의혹을, 심지어 두려움까지 불러일으키기 시작했다."], ["When Zossimov said “This is Raskolnikov” he jumped up quickly, sat on the sofa and with an almost defiant, but weak and breaking, voice articulated:", "조시모프가 \"이쪽이 라스콜니코프요\"라고 말했을 때, 그는 벌떡 일어나 소파에 앉더니, 거의 도전적이지만 힘없이 갈라지는 목소리로 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I am Raskolnikov!", "\"그렇소, 내가 라스콜니코프요!"], ["What do you want?”", "무슨 일이시오?\""]], [["The visitor scrutinised him and pronounced impressively:", "방문객은 그를 유심히 살펴보고는 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin.", "\"표트르 페트로비치 루진이오."], ["I believe I have reason to hope that my name is not wholly unknown to you?”", "내 이름이 댁에게 아주 생소하지는 않으리라 기대할 만한 이유가 있다고 생각하오만?\""]], [["But Raskolnikov, who had expected something quite different, gazed blankly and dreamily at him, making no reply, as though he heard the name of Pyotr Petrovitch for the first time.", "그러나 전혀 다른 무언가를 예상하고 있던 라스콜니코프는, 마치 표트르 페트로비치라는 이름을 난생처음 듣기라도 한 듯, 아무 대답도 없이 멍하니, 꿈꾸듯 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Is it possible that you can up to the present have received no information?”", "\"설마 지금까지 아무런 소식도 받지 못했단 말이오?\""], ["asked Pyotr Petrovitch, somewhat disconcerted.", "표트르 페트로비치가 다소 당황하여 물었다."]], [["In reply Raskolnikov sank languidly back on the pillow, put his hands behind his head and gazed at the ceiling.", "대답 대신 라스콜니코프는 맥없이 베개에 도로 몸을 묻고, 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 낀 채 천장을 바라보았다."], ["A look of dismay came into Luzhin’s face.", "루진의 얼굴에 낭패의 빛이 떠올랐다."], ["Zossimov and Razumihin stared at him more inquisitively than ever, and at last he showed unmistakable signs of embarrassment.", "조시모프와 라주미힌이 그 어느 때보다 캐묻듯 그를 바라보자, 마침내 그는 영락없이 당황한 기색을 드러냈다."]], [["“I had presumed and calculated,”", "\"내 짐작에는, 또 계산으로는,\""], ["he faltered, “that a letter posted more than ten days, if not a fortnight ago...”", "그가 더듬거렸다. \"보름은 아니더라도 열흘은 더 전에 부친 편지가…….\""]], [["“I say, why are you standing in the doorway?”", "\"이보시오, 왜 문간에 서 계시오?\""], ["Razumihin interrupted suddenly.", "라주미힌이 불쑥 말을 가로챘다."], ["“If you’ve something to say, sit down.", "\"할 말이 있으면 앉으시오."], ["Nastasya and you are so crowded.", "거기선 나스타시야하고 너무 비좁잖소."], ["Nastasya, make room.", "나스타시야, 자리 좀 비켜."], ["Here’s a chair, thread your way in!”", "여기 의자 있소, 비집고 들어오시오!\""]], [["He moved his chair back from the table, made a little space between the table and his knees, and waited in a rather cramped position for the visitor to “thread his way in.”", "그는 탁자에서 의자를 뒤로 빼어 탁자와 자기 무릎 사이에 작은 공간을 만들고는, 다소 옹색한 자세로 방문객이 \"비집고 들어오기를\""], ["The minute was so chosen that it was impossible to refuse, and the visitor squeezed his way through, hurrying and stumbling.", "기다렸다. 거절할 수 없는 절묘한 순간이었던지라, 방문객은 서두르고 비틀거리며 그 틈을 비집고 들어왔다."], ["Reaching the chair, he sat down, looking suspiciously at Razumihin.", "의자에 다다른 그는 의심스러운 눈초리로 라주미힌을 보며 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“No need to be nervous,”", "\"긴장하실 것 없소.\""], ["the latter blurted out.", "라주미힌이 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["“Rodya has been ill for the last five days and delirious for three, but now he is recovering and has got an appetite.", "\"로쟈는 닷새째 앓고 있고 사흘은 헛소리를 했지만, 이제 회복 중이고 입맛도 돌아왔소."], ["This is his doctor, who has just had a look at him.", "이쪽은 방금 진찰을 한 그의 의사요."], ["I am a comrade of Rodya’s, like him, formerly a student, and now I am nursing him; so don’t you take any notice of us, but go on with your business.”", "나는 로쟈의 동료인데, 그처럼 한때 대학생이었고 지금은 그를 간병하고 있소. 그러니 우리는 신경 쓰지 마시고 댁 용건이나 보시오.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고맙소."], ["But shall I not disturb the invalid by my presence and conversation?”", "그런데 내가 있고 또 이야기를 나누면 환자에게 방해가 되지 않겠소?\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch asked of Zossimov.", "표트르 페트로비치가 조시모프에게 물었다."]], [["“N-no,”", "\"아-니오.\""], ["mumbled Zossimov; “you may amuse him.”", "조시모프가 우물거렸다. \"오히려 기분 전환이 될 거요.\""], ["He yawned again.", "그는 다시 하품을 했다."]], [["“He has been conscious a long time, since the morning,”", "\"이 친구는 진작부터, 아침부터 의식이 또렷하오.\""], ["went on Razumihin, whose familiarity seemed so much like unaffected good-nature that Pyotr Petrovitch began to be more cheerful, partly, perhaps, because this shabby and impudent person had introduced himself as a student.", "라주미힌이 말을 이었는데, 그의 허물없는 태도가 어찌나 꾸밈없는 호의처럼 보였던지, 표트르 페트로비치는 좀 더 기분이 밝아지기 시작했다. 어쩌면 이 남루하고 뻔뻔한 자가 자신을 대학생이라고 소개한 까닭도 한몫했을 것이다."]], [["“Your mamma,”", "\"댁의 어머님께서는,\""], ["began Luzhin.", "루진이 입을 열었다."]], [["“Hm!”", "\"흠!\""], ["Razumihin cleared his throat loudly.", "라주미힌이 큰 소리로 헛기침을 했다."], ["Luzhin looked at him inquiringly.", "루진이 캐묻듯 그를 쳐다보았다."]], [["“That’s all right, go on.”", "\"아무것도 아니오, 계속하시오.\""]], [["Luzhin shrugged his shoulders.", "루진이 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“Your mamma had commenced a letter to you while I was sojourning in her neighbourhood.", "\"댁의 어머님께서는 내가 그분 근처에 머무는 동안 댁에게 보낼 편지를 쓰기 시작하셨소."], ["On my arrival here I purposely allowed a few days to elapse before coming to see you, in order that I might be fully assured that you were in full possession of the tidings; but now, to my astonishment...”", "나는 이곳에 도착해서도 일부러 며칠을 흘려보낸 뒤에 댁을 찾아왔는데, 댁이 그 소식을 온전히 전해 들었는지 확실히 해 두기 위해서였소. 그런데 이제 와서, 놀랍게도…….\""]], [["“I know, I know!”", "\"알고 있소, 알고 있다고요!\""], ["Raskolnikov cried suddenly with impatient vexation.", "라스콜니코프가 갑자기 못 참겠다는 듯 짜증스럽게 외쳤다."], ["“So you are the _fiancé_?", "\"그러니까 당신이 그 약혼자란 말이죠?"], ["I know, and that’s enough!”", "알고 있으니, 그걸로 됐소!\""]], [["There was no doubt about Pyotr Petrovitch’s being offended this time, but he said nothing.", "이번에는 표트르 페트로비치가 모욕을 느낀 것이 분명했지만, 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He made a violent effort to understand what it all meant.", "그는 이 모든 게 무슨 영문인지 이해하려고 안간힘을 썼다."], ["There was a moment’s silence.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["Meanwhile Raskolnikov, who had turned a little towards him when he answered, began suddenly staring at him again with marked curiosity, as though he had not had a good look at him yet, or as though something new had struck him; he rose from his pillow on purpose to stare at him.", "그러는 사이 대답을 할 때 그를 향해 몸을 약간 돌렸던 라스콜니코프가, 마치 아직 그를 제대로 뜯어보지 못했거나 무언가 새로운 점이 눈에 띄기라도 한 듯, 갑자기 또렷한 호기심을 가지고 다시 그를 빤히 바라보기 시작했다. 그는 일부러 베개에서 몸을 일으켜 그를 응시했다."], ["There certainly was something peculiar in Pyotr Petrovitch’s whole appearance, something which seemed to justify the title of “fiancé” so unceremoniously applied to him.", "과연 표트르 페트로비치의 전반적인 외양에는 무언가 독특한 데가 있었으니, 그토록 격의 없이 그에게 붙여진 '약혼자'라는 칭호를 정당화해 주는 듯한 무엇이었다."], ["In the first place, it was evident, far too much so indeed, that Pyotr Petrovitch had made eager use of his few days in the capital to get himself up and rig himself out in expectation of his betrothed--a perfectly innocent and permissible proceeding, indeed.", "우선, 표트르 페트로비치가 수도에서의 그 며칠을, 약혼녀를 맞이할 기대 속에 자신을 단장하고 차려입는 데 열심히 썼다는 것이 너무도, 실로 지나칠 만큼 분명했다. 사실 그 자체로는 전혀 흠잡을 데 없고 허용될 만한 처사였다."], ["Even his own, perhaps too complacent, consciousness of the agreeable improvement in his appearance might have been forgiven in such circumstances, seeing that Pyotr Petrovitch had taken up the rôle of fiancé.", "자기 외모가 보기 좋게 나아진 데 대한, 어쩌면 지나치게 흡족해하는 자의식조차도, 표트르 페트로비치가 약혼자의 역할을 떠맡은 마당이니 이런 상황에서는 용서받을 만했다."], ["All his clothes were fresh from the tailor’s and were all right, except for being too new and too distinctly appropriate.", "그의 옷은 모두 양복점에서 갓 나온 것으로 나무랄 데 없었지만, 다만 너무 새것이고 너무 노골적으로 용도에 들어맞는다는 게 흠이었다."], ["Even the stylish new round hat had the same significance.", "멋스러운 새 둥근 모자조차 같은 의미를 띠고 있었다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch treated it too respectfully and held it too carefully in his hands.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그 모자를 지나치게 정중하게 다루며 너무도 조심스럽게 손에 들고 있었다."], ["The exquisite pair of lavender gloves, real Louvain, told the same tale, if only from the fact of his not wearing them, but carrying them in his hand for show.", "진짜 루뱅산인 그 고급스러운 라벤더색 장갑 한 켤레도 같은 이야기를 들려주었으니, 그가 그것을 끼지 않고 과시하듯 손에 들고만 있다는 사실만 보아도 그러했다."], ["Light and youthful colours predominated in Pyotr Petrovitch’s attire.", "표트르 페트로비치의 옷차림에는 밝고 젊은 색조가 두드러졌다."], ["He wore a charming summer jacket of a fawn shade, light thin trousers, a waistcoat of the same, new and fine linen, a cravat of the lightest cambric with pink stripes on it, and the best of it was, this all suited Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그는 담황색의 멋스러운 여름 재킷에, 가볍고 얇은 바지를 입고, 같은 색 조끼를 받쳐 입었으며, 새것의 고운 셔츠에, 분홍색 줄무늬가 있는 더없이 가벼운 케임브릭 천 넥타이를 매고 있었다. 그리고 무엇보다 좋은 점은, 이 모든 게 표트르 페트로비치에게 잘 어울린다는 것이었다."], ["His very fresh and even handsome face looked younger than his forty-five years at all times.", "그의 매우 생기 있고 심지어 잘생기기까지 한 얼굴은 언제나 마흔다섯이라는 나이보다 젊어 보였다."], ["His dark, mutton-chop whiskers made an agreeable setting on both sides, growing thickly upon his shining, clean-shaven chin.", "검은 구레나룻이 양 볼에 보기 좋은 테를 둘러, 윤기 흐르는 말끔히 면도한 턱 위로 무성하게 자라 있었다."], ["Even his hair, touched here and there with grey, though it had been combed and curled at a hairdresser’s, did not give him a stupid appearance, as curled hair usually does, by inevitably suggesting a German on his wedding-day.", "군데군데 희끗희끗하게 센 머리칼조차도, 미용사에게서 빗어 곱슬곱슬하게 다듬은 것이었지만, 보통 곱슬머리가 그러하듯 결혼식 날의 독일인을 떠올리게 하여 그를 멍청해 보이게 하지는 않았다."], ["If there really was something unpleasing and repulsive in his rather good-looking and imposing countenance, it was due to quite other causes.", "만일 제법 잘생기고 위풍당당한 그의 얼굴에 정말로 불쾌하고 혐오스러운 무언가가 있었다면, 그것은 전혀 다른 원인에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["After scanning Mr. Luzhin unceremoniously, Raskolnikov smiled malignantly, sank back on the pillow and stared at the ceiling as before.", "격의 없이 루진 씨를 훑어본 뒤, 라스콜니코프는 짓궂게 웃고는 다시 베개에 몸을 묻고 아까처럼 천장을 응시했다."]], [["But Mr. Luzhin hardened his heart and seemed to determine to take no notice of their oddities.", "그러나 루진 씨는 마음을 다잡고, 그들의 괴상한 행동거지에는 일절 신경 쓰지 않기로 작정한 듯했다."]], [["“I feel the greatest regret at finding you in this situation,”", "\"이런 처지에 놓인 댁을 보게 되어 더없이 유감이오.\""], ["he began, again breaking the silence with an effort.", "그는 애써 다시 침묵을 깨며 입을 열었다."], ["“If I had been aware of your illness I should have come earlier.", "\"댁이 앓고 있는 줄 알았더라면 더 일찍 왔을 거요."], ["But you know what business is.", "하지만 일이라는 게 어떤 건지 댁도 알 거요."], ["I have, too, a very important legal affair in the Senate, not to mention other preoccupations which you may well conjecture.", "게다가 나는 원로원에 걸린 아주 중요한 법적 소송도 있고, 댁도 능히 짐작할 다른 용무들도 있다는 건 말할 것도 없소."], ["I am expecting your mamma and sister any minute.”", "댁의 어머님과 누이가 곧 도착하실 예정이오.\""]], [["Raskolnikov made a movement and seemed about to speak; his face showed some excitement.", "라스콜니코프가 몸을 움직이며 무슨 말을 하려는 듯했다. 그의 얼굴에는 약간의 동요가 비쳤다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch paused, waited, but as nothing followed, he went on:", "표트르 페트로비치는 말을 멈추고 기다렸으나, 아무 말도 이어지지 않자 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“... Any minute.", "\"……곧 도착하실 거요."], ["I have found a lodging for them on their arrival.”", "그분들이 오시면 묵을 숙소도 마련해 두었소.\""]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디에 말이오?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov weakly.", "라스콜니코프가 힘없이 물었다."]], [["“Very near here, in Bakaleyev’s house.”", "\"바로 이 근처, 바칼레예프의 집이오.\""]], [["“That’s in Voskresensky,”", "\"보스크레센스키에 있는 곳이군요.\""], ["put in Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 끼어들었다."], ["“There are two storeys of rooms, let by a merchant called Yushin; I’ve been there.”", "\"유신이라는 상인이 세놓는 방이 두 층 있는데, 나도 거기 가 본 적 있소.\""]], [["“Yes, rooms...”", "\"그래요, 방들이…….\""]], [["“A disgusting place--filthy, stinking and, what’s more, of doubtful character.", "\"역겨운 곳이오. 더럽고 악취가 진동하는 데다, 게다가 평판도 수상쩍은 곳이지."], ["Things have happened there, and there are all sorts of queer people living there.", "거기서 별일이 다 있었고, 온갖 괴상한 인간들이 살고 있소."], ["And I went there about a scandalous business.", "나도 어떤 추잡한 사건 때문에 거길 갔었지."], ["It’s cheap, though...”", "하긴 값은 싸지만…….\""]], [["“I could not, of course, find out so much about it, for I am a stranger in Petersburg myself,”", "\"나야 물론 그렇게까지 자세히는 알아볼 수 없었소. 나 역시 페테르부르크에는 낯선 사람이니까.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch replied huffily.", "표트르 페트로비치가 못마땅한 듯 대꾸했다."], ["“However, the two rooms are exceedingly clean, and as it is for so short a time...", "\"어쨌든 그 두 방은 무척 깨끗하고, 또 아주 잠깐 머무를 거니까……."], ["I have already taken a permanent, that is, our future flat,”", "나는 이미 영구적인, 그러니까 우리가 앞으로 살 집은 따로 구해 두었소.\""], ["he said, addressing Raskolnikov, “and I am having it done up.", "그가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말했다. \"지금 손을 보게 하는 중이오."], ["And meanwhile I am myself cramped for room in a lodging with my friend Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, in the flat of Madame Lippevechsel; it was he who told me of Bakaleyev’s house, too...”", "그동안 나 자신은 친구 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프와 함께, 리페베흐젤 부인의 셋집에 비좁게 끼어 지내고 있소. 바칼레예프의 집을 일러 준 것도 그 사람이오…….\""]], [["“Lebeziatnikov?”", "\"레베쟈트니코프라고요?\""], ["said Raskolnikov slowly, as if recalling something.", "라스콜니코프가 무언가를 떠올리는 듯 천천히 말했다."]], [["“Yes, Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, a clerk in the Ministry.", "\"그렇소, 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프, 부처에 다니는 관리요."], ["Do you know him?”", "그를 아시오?\""]], [["“Yes...", "\"네……"], ["no,”", "아니오.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."]], [["“Excuse me, I fancied so from your inquiry.", "\"미안하오, 댁이 물어보기에 아는 줄로 알았소."], ["I was once his guardian....", "한때 내가 그의 후견인이었지……."], ["A very nice young man and advanced.", "아주 괜찮은 청년이고 진보적이오."], ["I like to meet young people: one learns new things from them.”", "나는 젊은이들 만나는 걸 좋아하오. 그들에게서 새로운 것을 배우게 되니까.\""], ["Luzhin looked round hopefully at them all.", "루진이 기대에 차서 모두를 둘러보았다."]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["asked Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 물었다."]], [["“In the most serious and essential matters,”", "\"가장 중대하고 본질적인 문제들에서 말이오.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch replied, as though delighted at the question.", "표트르 페트로비치가 그 질문이 반갑기라도 한 듯 대답했다."], ["“You see, it’s ten years since I visited Petersburg.", "\"보다시피 내가 페테르부르크에 들른 게 십 년 만이오."], ["All the novelties, reforms, ideas have reached us in the provinces, but to see it all more clearly one must be in Petersburg.", "온갖 새로운 것들, 개혁, 사상이 우리 지방까지도 다 전해지긴 했지만, 그 모든 걸 더 또렷이 보려면 페테르부르크에 있어야 하는 법이오."], ["And it’s my notion that you observe and learn most by watching the younger generation.", "그리고 내 생각에는, 젊은 세대를 지켜봄으로써 가장 많이 관찰하고 배울 수 있는 것 같소."], ["And I confess I am delighted...”", "솔직히 고백하건대 나는 기쁘오…….\""]], [["“At what?”", "\"무엇이 말이오?\""]], [["“Your question is a wide one.", "\"댁의 질문은 폭이 넓군."], ["I may be mistaken, but I fancy I find clearer views, more, so to say, criticism, more practicality...”", "내 착각일지도 모르지만, 내가 보기엔 더 명료한 견해, 말하자면 더 많은 비판, 더 많은 실천성을 발견하게 되는 것 같소…….\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"그건 사실이지요.\""], ["Zossimov let drop.", "조시모프가 한마디 흘렸다."]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"헛소리!"], ["There’s no practicality.”", "실천성이라니 어디 있단 말이오.\""], ["Razumihin flew at him.", "라주미힌이 그에게 달려들었다."], ["“Practicality is a difficult thing to find; it does not drop down from heaven.", "\"실천성이란 찾기 어려운 거요. 하늘에서 뚝 떨어지는 게 아니란 말이오."], ["And for the last two hundred years we have been divorced from all practical life.", "지난 이백 년 동안 우리는 온갖 실천적 삶에서 동떨어져 있었소. 사상이라면, 좋소, 들끓고 있지.\""], ["Ideas, if you like, are fermenting,”", "그가 표트르 페트로비치에게 말했다."], ["he said to Pyotr Petrovitch, “and desire for good exists, though it’s in a childish form, and honesty you may find, although there are crowds of brigands.", "\"선을 향한 갈망도 존재하긴 하지만 유치한 형태일 뿐이고, 정직함도 찾을 수야 있지만 도둑놈들이 우글거리지. 어쨌거나 실천성이란 없소."], ["Anyway, there’s no practicality. Practicality goes well shod.”", "실천성은 제대로 된 신발을 신고 다니는 법이오.\""]], [["“I don’t agree with you,”", "\"나는 댁의 의견에 동의하지 않소.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch replied, with evident enjoyment.", "표트르 페트로비치가 눈에 띄게 즐거워하며 대꾸했다."], ["“Of course, people do get carried away and make mistakes, but one must have indulgence; those mistakes are merely evidence of enthusiasm for the cause and of abnormal external environment.", "\"물론 사람들이 도가 지나쳐 실수를 저지르기도 하지만, 너그럽게 보아야 하오. 그런 실수들은 그저 대의에 대한 열정과 비정상적인 외부 환경의 증거일 뿐이오."], ["If little has been done, the time has been but short; of means I will not speak.", "이루어진 게 별로 없다면, 그건 시간이 짧았던 탓이지. 수단에 관해서는 말하지 않겠소."], ["It’s my personal view, if you care to know, that something has been accomplished already.", "알고 싶다면 말하건대, 이미 무언가는 이루어졌다는 게 내 개인적 견해요."], ["New valuable ideas, new valuable works are circulating in the place of our old dreamy and romantic authors.", "우리의 낡고 몽상적이고 낭만적인 작가들 대신에 새롭고 값진 사상, 새롭고 값진 저작들이 유통되고 있소."], ["Literature is taking a maturer form, many injurious prejudices have been rooted up and turned into ridicule....", "문학은 더 성숙한 형태를 갖추어 가고, 해로운 편견들이 숱하게 뿌리 뽑혀 조롱거리가 되었지……."], ["In a word, we have cut ourselves off irrevocably from the past, and that, to my thinking, is a great thing...”", "한마디로, 우리는 과거와 돌이킬 수 없이 결별했고, 내 생각에 그건 대단한 일이오…….\""]], [["“He’s learnt it by heart to show off!”", "\"외워서 잘난 척하는군!\""], ["Raskolnikov pronounced suddenly.", "라스콜니코프가 갑자기 내뱉었다."]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["asked Pyotr Petrovitch, not catching his words; but he received no reply.", "표트르 페트로비치가 그 말을 알아듣지 못하고 물었다. 그러나 아무 대답도 돌아오지 않았다."]], [["“That’s all true,”", "\"다 옳은 말씀이오.\""], ["Zossimov hastened to interpose.", "조시모프가 서둘러 끼어들었다."]], [["“Isn’t it so?”", "\"그렇지 않소?\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch went on, glancing affably at Zossimov.", "표트르 페트로비치가 조시모프를 상냥하게 흘끔거리며 말을 이었다. \"인정해야 하오.\""], ["“You must admit,”", "그가 약간의 승리감과 거만함을 띠고 라주미힌을 향해 말을 이었다."], ["he went on, addressing Razumihin with a shade of triumph and superciliousness--he almost added “young man”--“that there is an advance, or, as they say now, progress in the name of science and economic truth...”", "하마터면 \"젊은이\"라는 말까지 덧붙일 뻔했다. \"과학과 경제적 진리의 이름으로 진전, 아니 요즘 말로 하면 진보가 있다는 것을…….\""]], [["“A commonplace.”", "\"진부한 소리요.\""]], [["“No, not a commonplace!", "\"아니, 진부한 소리가 아니오!"], ["Hitherto, for instance, if I were told, ‘love thy neighbour,’ what came of it?”", "이를테면 지금까지는 '네 이웃을 사랑하라'는 말을 들으면, 그게 어떤 결과를 가져왔소?\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch went on, perhaps with excessive haste.", "표트르 페트로비치가, 어쩌면 지나치게 서둘러 말을 이었다."], ["“It came to my tearing my coat in half to share with my neighbour and we both were left half naked.", "\"내가 내 외투를 반으로 찢어 이웃과 나눠 입고, 우리 둘 다 반쯤 헐벗은 채로 남는 거였소."], ["As a Russian proverb has it, ‘Catch several hares and you won’t catch one.’ Science now tells us, love yourself before all men, for everything in the world rests on self-interest.", "러시아 속담에도 있듯이, '여러 마리 토끼를 쫓다가는 한 마리도 못 잡는다'는 거지. 이제 과학은 우리에게 이렇게 말하오."], ["You love yourself and manage your own affairs properly and your coat remains whole.", "모든 사람보다 먼저 너 자신을 사랑하라, 세상의 모든 것은 사리사욕에 기초해 있으니."], ["Economic truth adds that the better private affairs are organised in society--the more whole coats, so to say--the firmer are its foundations and the better is the common welfare organised too.", "자기 자신을 사랑하고 자기 일을 제대로 꾸려 나가면 외투는 멀쩡히 남는다는 거지. 경제적 진리는 여기에 덧붙이오."], ["Therefore, in acquiring wealth solely and exclusively for myself, I am acquiring, so to speak, for all, and helping to bring to pass my neighbour’s getting a little more than a torn coat; and that not from private, personal liberality, but as a consequence of the general advance.", "사회에서 사적인 일들이 잘 조직될수록 — 말하자면 멀쩡한 외투가 많을수록 — 그 토대는 더 견고해지고 공공의 복리도 더 잘 조직된다고. 따라서 오로지 나만을 위해 부를 쌓을 때, 나는 말하자면 모두를 위해 쌓는 셈이며, 내 이웃이 찢어진 외투보다는 조금 더 나은 걸 얻게 되도록 돕는 셈이오."], ["The idea is simple, but unhappily it has been a long time reaching us, being hindered by idealism and sentimentality.", "그것도 사적이고 개인적인 자선에서가 아니라, 전반적인 진보의 결과로서 말이오. 단순한 생각이지만, 불행히도 이상주의와 감상주의에 가로막혀 우리에게 닿기까지 오랜 시간이 걸렸소."], ["And yet it would seem to want very little wit to perceive it...”", "게다가 그걸 알아차리는 데는 머리가 거의 필요 없을 것 같은데도 말이오…….\""]], [["“Excuse me, I’ve very little wit myself,”", "\"미안하지만, 나는 머리가 영 없는 사람이오.\""], ["Razumihin cut in sharply, “and so let us drop it.", "라주미힌이 날카롭게 말을 잘랐다. \"그러니 그 얘긴 그만둡시다."], ["I began this discussion with an object, but I’ve grown so sick during the last three years of this chattering to amuse oneself, of this incessant flow of commonplaces, always the same, that, by Jove, I blush even when other people talk like that.", "나도 어떤 목적이 있어서 이 논쟁을 시작한 거지만, 지난 삼 년 동안 이렇게 자기 좋자고 지껄여 대는 것, 늘 똑같은 이 진부한 소리의 끝없는 흐름에 어찌나 신물이 났는지, 맹세코 다른 사람들이 그렇게 떠들어도 내가 다 낯이 뜨거울 지경이오."], ["You are in a hurry, no doubt, to exhibit your acquirements; and I don’t blame you, that’s quite pardonable.", "댁은 분명 자기 학식을 뽐내고 싶어 안달일 테고, 나도 그걸 탓하진 않소, 충분히 용서할 만한 일이니까."], ["I only wanted to find out what sort of man you are, for so many unscrupulous people have got hold of the progressive cause of late and have so distorted in their own interests everything they touched, that the whole cause has been dragged in the mire.", "나는 그저 댁이 어떤 사람인지 알아보고 싶었을 뿐이오. 요즘 들어 양심 없는 인간들이 어찌나 진보의 대의를 거머쥐고서, 손대는 것마다 제 잇속에 맞게 왜곡해 놓는지, 그 대의 전체가 진흙탕에 끌려 들어가고 말았단 말이오."], ["That’s enough!”", "그만하면 됐소!\""]], [["“Excuse me, sir,”", "\"미안하오만, 선생.\""], ["said Luzhin, affronted, and speaking with excessive dignity.", "루진이 모욕을 느끼고 지나치게 위엄을 부리며 말했다."], ["“Do you mean to suggest so unceremoniously that I too...”", "\"이렇게 격의 없이, 나까지도 그렇다고 넌지시 말씀하시는 거요…….\""]], [["“Oh, my dear sir...", "\"오, 이런, 선생……."], ["how could I?...", "내가 어찌 감히?……."], ["Come, that’s enough,”", "자, 그만합시다.\""], ["Razumihin concluded, and he turned abruptly to Zossimov to continue their previous conversation.", "라주미힌은 그렇게 말을 맺고는 홱 조시모프 쪽으로 돌아서서 아까 나누던 대화를 이어 갔다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch had the good sense to accept the disavowal.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그 부인을 받아들일 만한 분별은 있었다."], ["He made up his mind to take leave in another minute or two.", "그는 일 이 분 안에 자리를 뜨기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“I trust our acquaintance,”", "\"우리의 교분이,\""], ["he said, addressing Raskolnikov, “may, upon your recovery and in view of the circumstances of which you are aware, become closer...", "그가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말했다. \"댁이 회복되고 나면, 또 댁도 알고 있는 사정들에 비추어, 더 가까워지기를 바라오……."], ["Above all, I hope for your return to health...”", "무엇보다도, 댁이 건강을 되찾기를 바라오…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov did not even turn his head.", "라스콜니코프는 고개조차 돌리지 않았다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch began getting up from his chair.", "표트르 페트로비치는 의자에서 일어서기 시작했다."]], [["“One of her customers must have killed her,”", "\"그 노파의 고객 중 누군가가 죽인 게 틀림없소.\""], ["Zossimov declared positively.", "조시모프가 단정 짓듯 말했다."]], [["“Not a doubt of it,”", "\"의심할 여지가 없지.\""], ["replied Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 대답했다."], ["“Porfiry doesn’t give his opinion, but is examining all who have left pledges with her there.”", "\"포르피리는 자기 의견을 밝히지 않지만, 거기에 저당물을 맡긴 사람들을 모조리 조사하고 있소.\""]], [["“Examining them?”", "\"조사를 한다고?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked aloud.", "라스콜니코프가 큰 소리로 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["What then?”", "그게 어쨌다고?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도 아니야.\""]], [["“How does he get hold of them?”", "\"그가 어떻게 그들을 찾아내는데?\""], ["asked Zossimov.", "조시모프가 물었다."]], [["“Koch has given the names of some of them, other names are on the wrappers of the pledges and some have come forward of themselves.”", "\"코흐가 그중 몇 사람의 이름을 댔고, 다른 이름들은 저당물 포장지에 적혀 있고, 또 몇몇은 제 발로 나섰다더군.\""]], [["“It must have been a cunning and practised ruffian!", "\"교활하고 노련한 악당이 틀림없소!"], ["The boldness of it!", "그 대담함이라니!"], ["The coolness!”", "그 침착함이라니!\""]], [["“That’s just what it wasn’t!”", "\"바로 그게 아니란 말이오!\""], ["interposed Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 끼어들었다."], ["“That’s what throws you all off the scent.", "\"다들 그것 때문에 헛다리를 짚고 있는 거요."], ["But I maintain that he is not cunning, not practised, and probably this was his first crime!", "하지만 내 단언컨대 그자는 교활하지도 노련하지도 않고, 아마 이게 첫 범행이었을 거요!"], ["The supposition that it was a calculated crime and a cunning criminal doesn’t work.", "계산된 범죄에 교활한 범인이라는 가정은 들어맞지 않소."], ["Suppose him to have been inexperienced, and it’s clear that it was only a chance that saved him--and chance may do anything.", "그자가 미숙했다고 쳐 보시오, 그럼 오로지 우연만이 그를 구해 줬다는 게 분명해지오 — 그리고 우연이란 무슨 일이든 다 해낼 수 있는 거지."], ["Why, he did not foresee obstacles, perhaps!", "아니, 그자는 어쩌면 장애물조차 예상하지 못했을 거요!"], ["And how did he set to work?", "그런데 일은 어떻게 시작했나?"], ["He took jewels worth ten or twenty roubles, stuffing his pockets with them, ransacked the old woman’s trunks, her rags--and they found fifteen hundred roubles, besides notes, in a box in the top drawer of the chest!", "십 루블, 이십 루블어치 보석을 집어 주머니에 쑤셔 넣고, 노파의 궤짝이며 누더기를 마구 뒤졌는데 — 정작 사람들이 서랍장 맨 위 칸 상자 안에서 어음 말고도 천오백 루블이나 찾아냈단 말이오!"], ["He did not know how to rob; he could only murder.", "그자는 훔칠 줄을 몰랐던 거요. 죽일 줄만 알았던 거지."], ["It was his first crime, I assure you, his first crime; he lost his head.", "그게 첫 범행이었소, 내 장담하건대, 첫 범행이었고, 그자는 정신을 놓았던 거요."], ["And he got off more by luck than good counsel!”", "그러니 머리를 잘 써서가 아니라 운이 좋아서 빠져나간 거란 말이오!\""]], [["“You are talking of the murder of the old pawnbroker, I believe?”", "\"노파, 그 전당포 노파의 살인 사건을 말하는 것 같은데, 맞소?\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch put in, addressing Zossimov.", "표트르 페트로비치가 조시모프를 향해 끼어들었다."], ["He was standing, hat and gloves in hand, but before departing he felt disposed to throw off a few more intellectual phrases.", "그는 모자와 장갑을 손에 든 채 서 있었지만, 떠나기에 앞서 지적인 말을 몇 마디 더 던지고 싶은 마음이 들었던 것이다."], ["He was evidently anxious to make a favourable impression and his vanity overcame his prudence.", "그는 분명 좋은 인상을 남기고 싶어 안달이었고, 그의 허영심이 신중함을 이기고 있었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["You’ve heard of it?”", "그 얘길 들으셨소?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, being in the neighbourhood.”", "\"오, 그렇소, 근처에 있다 보니.\""]], [["“Do you know the details?”", "\"자세한 내막을 아시오?\""]], [["“I can’t say that; but another circumstance interests me in the case--the whole question, so to say.", "\"그렇다고는 못 하겠소. 하지만 이 사건에서 나의 흥미를 끄는 건 다른 점이오 — 말하자면 문제 전체란 말이오."], ["Not to speak of the fact that crime has been greatly on the increase among the lower classes during the last five years, not to speak of the cases of robbery and arson everywhere, what strikes me as the strangest thing is that in the higher classes, too, crime is increasing proportionately.", "지난 오 년 동안 하층 계급에서 범죄가 크게 늘었다는 사실은 차치하고, 도처에서 일어나는 강도와 방화 사건들도 차치하더라도, 내가 보기에 가장 기이한 점은, 상류 계급에서도 마찬가지로 범죄가 비례해서 늘고 있다는 것이오."], ["In one place one hears of a student’s robbing the mail on the high road; in another place people of good social position forge false banknotes; in Moscow of late a whole gang has been captured who used to forge lottery tickets, and one of the ringleaders was a lecturer in universal history; then our secretary abroad was murdered from some obscure motive of gain....", "어느 곳에서는 대학생이 큰길에서 우편 마차를 털었다는 소리가 들리고, 또 어느 곳에서는 사회적 지위가 번듯한 사람들이 위조지폐를 만들고, 최근 모스크바에서는 복권을 위조하던 일당이 통째로 붙잡혔는데 그 주모자 중 하나가 세계사 강사였다더군. 그런가 하면 외국에 나가 있던 우리 서기관이 무슨 모호한 사리사욕 때문에 살해당했고……."], ["And if this old woman, the pawnbroker, has been murdered by someone of a higher class in society--for peasants don’t pawn gold trinkets--how are we to explain this demoralisation of the civilised part of our society?”", "그리고 이 노파, 이 전당포 주인이 사회 상류층의 누군가에게 살해당한 거라면 — 농민들은 금붙이를 저당 잡히지 않으니까 — 우리 사회의 문명화된 부분의 이 도덕적 타락을 대체 어떻게 설명해야 하겠소?\""]], [["“There are many economic changes,”", "\"경제적 변화가 많지요.\""], ["put in Zossimov.", "조시모프가 끼어들었다."]], [["“How are we to explain it?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 설명하느냐고요?\""], ["Razumihin caught him up.", "라주미힌이 그 말을 받아챘다."], ["“It might be explained by our inveterate impracticality.”", "\"그건 우리의 고질적인 무실천성으로 설명할 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“What answer had your lecturer in Moscow to make to the question why he was forging notes?", "\"모스크바의 그 강사가 왜 지폐를 위조했느냐는 물음에 뭐라고 대답했소?"], ["‘Everybody is getting rich one way or another, so I want to make haste to get rich too.’ I don’t remember the exact words, but the upshot was that he wants money for nothing, without waiting or working!", "'다들 이런저런 방법으로 부자가 되고 있으니, 나도 어서 부자가 되고 싶었다'는 거였소. 정확한 말은 기억나지 않지만, 요지는 그자가 기다리지도 일하지도 않고 거저 돈을 원했다는 거요!"], ["We’ve grown used to having everything ready-made, to walking on crutches, to having our food chewed for us.", "우리는 만사를 다 차려진 채로 받는 데, 목발을 짚고 걷는 데, 음식까지 씹어서 떠먹여 주는 데 익숙해져 버렸소."], ["Then the great hour struck,[*] and every man showed himself in his true colours.”", "그러다 그 위대한 시간이 닥치니, 너 나 할 것 없이 제 본색을 드러낸 거지.\""]], [["[*] The emancipation of the serfs in 1861 is meant.", "* 1861년 농노 해방을 가리킨다."], ["--TRANSLATOR’S NOTE.", "─옮긴이 주."]], [["“But morality?", "\"하지만 도덕은?"], ["And so to speak, principles...”", "또 말하자면, 원칙은…….\""]], [["“But why do you worry about it?”", "\"그런데 댁은 왜 그런 걸 걱정하시오?\""], ["Raskolnikov interposed suddenly.", "라스콜니코프가 갑자기 끼어들었다."], ["“It’s in accordance with your theory!”", "\"그건 당신 이론에 들어맞는 거잖소!\""]], [["“In accordance with my theory?”", "\"내 이론에 들어맞는다고?\""]], [["“Why, carry out logically the theory you were advocating just now, and it follows that people may be killed...”", "\"아니, 방금 당신이 주장하던 그 이론을 논리적으로 끝까지 밀고 나가면, 사람을 죽여도 된다는 결론이 나오잖소…….\""]], [["“Upon my word!”", "\"세상에 이럴 수가!\""], ["cried Luzhin.", "루진이 외쳤다."]], [["“No, that’s not so,”", "\"아니, 그렇지 않소.\""], ["put in Zossimov.", "조시모프가 끼어들었다."]], [["Raskolnikov lay with a white face and twitching upper lip, breathing painfully.", "라스콜니코프는 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 윗입술을 실룩이며 괴로운 듯 숨을 쉬고 누워 있었다."]], [["“There’s a measure in all things,”", "\"무슨 일에든 정도라는 게 있는 법이오.\""], ["Luzhin went on superciliously.", "루진이 거만하게 말을 이었다."], ["“Economic ideas are not an incitement to murder, and one has but to suppose...”", "\"경제 사상이 살인을 부추기는 게 아니고, 다만 가정해 보면…….\""]], [["“And is it true,”", "\"그리고 그게 사실이오?\""], ["Raskolnikov interposed once more suddenly, again in a voice quivering with fury and delight in insulting him, “is it true that you told your _fiancée_...", "라스콜니코프가 또다시 갑자기 끼어들었는데, 이번에도 분노와 그를 모욕하는 데서 오는 쾌감으로 떨리는 목소리였다. \"당신이 약혼녀에게……"], ["within an hour of her acceptance, that what pleased you most...", "그녀가 청혼을 받아들인 지 한 시간도 안 돼서, 당신을 가장 기쁘게 한 게……"], ["was that she was a beggar...", "그녀가 거지라는 사실이었다고 말한 게 사실이오?"], ["because it was better to raise a wife from poverty, so that you may have complete control over her, and reproach her with your being her benefactor?”", "가난에서 아내를 끌어올리는 편이 더 낫기 때문에, 그래야 그녀를 완전히 손아귀에 넣고, 자기가 은인이라는 걸 빌미로 그녀를 책망할 수 있으니까 말이오?\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"세상에 이럴 수가.\""], ["Luzhin cried wrathfully and irritably, crimson with confusion, “to distort my words in this way!", "루진이 당혹감에 새빨개진 얼굴로 분노하여 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"내 말을 이런 식으로 왜곡하다니!"], ["Excuse me, allow me to assure you that the report which has reached you, or rather, let me say, has been conveyed to you, has no foundation in truth, and I...", "미안하오만, 분명히 말해 두겠소. 댁의 귀에 들어간, 아니 차라리 누가 댁에게 전한 그 이야기는 사실에 아무런 근거도 없으며, 나는……"], ["suspect who...", "누군지 짐작이 가오만……"], ["in a word...", "한마디로……"], ["this arrow...", "이 화살은……"], ["in a word, your mamma...", "한마디로, 댁의 어머님이……"], ["She seemed to me in other things, with all her excellent qualities, of a somewhat high-flown and romantic way of thinking....", "그분은 다른 일들에서도, 그 온갖 훌륭한 자질에도 불구하고, 다소 과장되고 낭만적인 사고방식을 지니신 듯 보였소……."], ["But I was a thousand miles from supposing that she would misunderstand and misrepresent things in so fanciful a way....", "하지만 그분이 일을 이토록 공상적으로 오해하고 잘못 전하시리라고는 천 리 밖에서도 짐작 못 했소……."], ["And indeed...", "더구나……"], ["indeed...”", "더구나…….\""]], [["“I tell you what,”", "\"내 말해 두겠는데,\""], ["cried Raskolnikov, raising himself on his pillow and fixing his piercing, glittering eyes upon him, “I tell you what.”", "라스콜니코프가 베개에서 몸을 일으켜 날카롭게 번뜩이는 눈을 그에게 못 박으며 외쳤다. \"내 말해 두겠는데.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐를 말이오?\""], ["Luzhin stood still, waiting with a defiant and offended face.", "루진이 도전적이고 모욕당한 얼굴로 기다리며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Silence lasted for some seconds.", "침묵이 몇 초간 이어졌다."]], [["“Why, if ever again...", "\"앞으로 또 한 번이라도……"], ["you dare to mention a single word...", "감히 단 한 마디라도……"], ["about my mother...", "내 어머니에 관해 입에 올린다면……"], ["I shall send you flying downstairs!”", "당신을 계단 아래로 날려 버리겠소!\""]], [["“What’s the matter with you?”", "\"아니, 자네 왜 그러나?\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."]], [["“So that’s how it is?”", "\"그래, 이런 식이란 말이오?\""], ["Luzhin turned pale and bit his lip.", "루진이 새파랗게 질려 입술을 깨물었다."], ["“Let me tell you, sir,”", "\"내 말해 두겠소, 선생.\""], ["he began deliberately, doing his utmost to restrain himself but breathing hard, “at the first moment I saw you you were ill-disposed to me, but I remained here on purpose to find out more.", "그가 안간힘을 다해 자제하면서도 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬며 또박또박 말하기 시작했다. \"처음 댁을 본 순간부터 댁이 내게 적의를 품고 있다는 걸 알았지만, 나는 좀 더 알아보려고 일부러 여기 남아 있었던 거요."], ["I could forgive a great deal in a sick man and a connection, but you...", "병든 사람에다 인척이 될 사람이니 많은 것을 용서할 수 있었소만, 댁은……"], ["never after this...”", "이 일이 있은 뒤로는 결코…….\""]], [["“I am not ill,”", "\"나는 병들지 않았소!\""], ["cried Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 외쳤다."]], [["“So much the worse...”", "\"그렇다면 더 나쁘군…….\""]], [["“Go to hell!”", "\"썩 꺼지시오!\""]], [["But Luzhin was already leaving without finishing his speech, squeezing between the table and the chair; Razumihin got up this time to let him pass.", "그러나 루진은 이미 말을 끝맺지도 않고 탁자와 의자 사이를 비집으며 나가고 있었다. 이번에는 라주미힌이 그가 지나가도록 일어섰다."], ["Without glancing at anyone, and not even nodding to Zossimov, who had for some time been making signs to him to let the sick man alone, he went out, lifting his hat to the level of his shoulders to avoid crushing it as he stooped to go out of the door.", "그는 누구에게도 눈길 한번 주지 않고, 한동안 환자를 내버려 두라고 신호를 보내던 조시모프에게 고갯짓조차 하지 않은 채, 문을 나서며 몸을 숙일 때 모자가 찌부러지지 않도록 모자를 어깨 높이까지 들어 올리고는 밖으로 나갔다."], ["And even the curve of his spine was expressive of the horrible insult he had received.", "그리고 굽은 등의 곡선마저 그가 받은 끔찍한 모욕을 고스란히 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“How could you--how could you!”", "\"자네 어떻게 그럴 수가 — 어떻게 그럴 수가 있나!\""], ["Razumihin said, shaking his head in perplexity.", "라주미힌이 어리둥절하여 고개를 내저으며 말했다."]], [["“Let me alone--let me alone all of you!”", "\"날 내버려 둬 — 다들 날 내버려 두란 말이야!\""], ["Raskolnikov cried in a frenzy.", "라스콜니코프가 미친 듯이 외쳤다."], ["“Will you ever leave off tormenting me?", "\"언제까지 날 못살게 굴 거야?"], ["I am not afraid of you!", "난 자네들이 무섭지 않아!"], ["I am not afraid of anyone, anyone now!", "이제 난 누구도, 누구도 무섭지 않다고!"], ["Get away from me!", "다들 내게서 떨어져!"], ["I want to be alone, alone, alone!”", "혼자 있고 싶어, 혼자, 혼자!\""]], [["“Come along,”", "\"가세.\""], ["said Zossimov, nodding to Razumihin.", "조시모프가 라주미힌에게 고갯짓하며 말했다."]], [["“But we can’t leave him like this!”", "\"하지만 이대로 내버려 둘 순 없잖나!\""]], [["“Come along,”", "\"가세.\""], ["Zossimov repeated insistently, and he went out.", "조시모프가 다그치듯 거듭 말하고는 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Razumihin thought a minute and ran to overtake him.", "라주미힌은 잠시 생각하다가 그를 따라잡으려고 뛰어나갔다."]], [["“It might be worse not to obey him,”", "\"저 친구 말을 안 듣는 게 더 나쁠 수도 있네.\""], ["said Zossimov on the stairs.", "조시모프가 계단에서 말했다."], ["“He mustn’t be irritated.”", "\"자극하면 안 돼.\""]], [["“What’s the matter with him?”", "\"대체 저 친구한테 무슨 일이 있는 건가?\""]], [["“If only he could get some favourable shock, that’s what would do it!", "\"무언가 좋은 충격이라도 받을 수 있다면, 바로 그게 효험이 있을 텐데!"], ["At first he was better....", "처음엔 좀 나아지더니만……."], ["You know he has got something on his mind!", "자네도 알다시피 저 친구 마음에 뭔가 걸린 게 있어!"], ["Some fixed idea weighing on him....", "무슨 고정관념이 그를 짓누르고 있는 거지……."], ["I am very much afraid so; he must have!”", "정말 그런 것 같아 몹시 걱정스럽네. 틀림없이 그래!\""]], [["“Perhaps it’s that gentleman, Pyotr Petrovitch.", "\"어쩌면 그 신사, 표트르 페트로비치 때문인지도 모르지."], ["From his conversation I gather he is going to marry his sister, and that he had received a letter about it just before his illness....”", "그 사람 얘기로 미루어 보면 로쟈의 누이와 결혼하려는 모양이고, 로쟈가 앓아눕기 바로 직전에 그 일에 관한 편지를 받았던 것 같던데…….\""]], [["“Yes, confound the man!", "\"그래, 그 작자가 망쳐 놓은 거지!"], ["he may have upset the case altogether.", "일을 통째로 그르쳤을지도 몰라."], ["But have you noticed, he takes no interest in anything, he does not respond to anything except one point on which he seems excited--that’s the murder?”", "그런데 자네 눈치챘나, 저 친구는 무엇에도 관심이 없고 무엇에도 반응하지 않는데, 딱 한 가지, 흥분하는 듯한 점이 있단 말이지 — 바로 그 살인 사건이야.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["Razumihin agreed, “I noticed that, too.", "라주미힌이 동의했다. \"나도 그건 눈치챘네."], ["He is interested, frightened.", "관심을 보이고, 또 겁을 먹고 있어."], ["It gave him a shock on the day he was ill in the police office; he fainted.”", "경찰서에서 앓아누웠던 그날도 그것 때문에 충격을 받았던 거지. 기절까지 했으니까.\""]], [["“Tell me more about that this evening and I’ll tell you something afterwards.", "\"그 얘기를 오늘 저녁에 좀 더 자세히 해 주게, 그럼 나중에 내가 자네한테 들려줄 게 있네."], ["He interests me very much!", "저 친구한테 무척 흥미가 동하는군!"], ["In half an hour I’ll go and see him again....", "삼십 분 뒤에 다시 가서 들여다보겠네……."], ["There’ll be no inflammation though.”", "하긴 염증은 없을 거야.\""]], [["“Thanks!", "\"고맙네!"], ["And I’ll wait with Pashenka meantime and will keep watch on him through Nastasya....”", "그동안 나는 파셴카하고 같이 기다리면서 나스타시야를 통해 저 친구를 지켜보겠네…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov, left alone, looked with impatience and misery at Nastasya, but she still lingered.", "홀로 남겨진 라스콜니코프는 초조하고 비참한 심정으로 나스타시야를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 여전히 우물쭈물 머물러 있었다."]], [["“Won’t you have some tea now?”", "\"이제 차 좀 드시겠어요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Later!", "\"나중에!"], ["I am sleepy!", "졸려."], ["Leave me.”", "날 내버려 둬.\""]], [["He turned abruptly to the wall; Nastasya went out.", "그는 홱 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다. 나스타시야는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["But as soon as she went out, he got up, latched the door, undid the parcel which Razumihin had brought in that evening and had tied up again and began dressing.", "그러나 그녀가 나가자마자, 그는 일어나 문에 빗장을 걸고, 라주미힌이 그날 저녁 가져와 다시 묶어 두었던 보따리를 풀고 옷을 입기 시작했다."], ["Strange to say, he seemed immediately to have become perfectly calm; not a trace of his recent delirium nor of the panic fear that had haunted him of late.", "이상한 일이지만, 그는 순식간에 완전히 평온해진 것 같았다. 조금 전의 헛소리도, 최근 그를 따라다니던 공황의 공포도 흔적조차 남지 않았다."], ["It was the first moment of a strange sudden calm.", "그것은 어떤 기묘하고 돌연한 평온의 첫 순간이었다."], ["His movements were precise and definite; a firm purpose was evident in them.", "그의 동작은 정확하고 분명했으며, 굳은 결의가 거기서 엿보였다."], ["“To-day, to-day,”", "\"오늘이야, 오늘.\""], ["he muttered to himself.", "그는 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["He understood that he was still weak, but his intense spiritual concentration gave him strength and self-confidence.", "그는 자신이 여전히 쇠약하다는 것을 알고 있었지만, 격렬한 정신적 집중이 그에게 힘과 자신감을 주었다."], ["He hoped, moreover, that he would not fall down in the street.", "더욱이 그는 길거리에서 쓰러지지 않으리라 기대했다."], ["When he had dressed in entirely new clothes, he looked at the money lying on the table, and after a moment’s thought put it in his pocket.", "완전히 새 옷을 입고 나서, 그는 탁자 위에 놓인 돈을 바라보다가, 잠시 생각한 뒤 그것을 주머니에 넣었다."], ["It was twenty-five roubles.", "이십오 루블이었다."], ["He took also all the copper change from the ten roubles spent by Razumihin on the clothes.", "그는 라주미힌이 옷에 쓴 십 루블에서 남은 동전 잔돈도 모두 챙겼다."], ["Then he softly unlatched the door, went out, slipped downstairs and glanced in at the open kitchen door.", "그런 다음 살그머니 문빗장을 풀고 나가, 계단을 미끄러지듯 내려가 열린 부엌문 안을 들여다보았다."], ["Nastasya was standing with her back to him, blowing up the landlady’s samovar.", "나스타시야가 등을 돌린 채 서서 집주인 여자의 사모바르에 불을 지피고 있었다. 그녀는 아무 소리도 듣지 못했다."], ["She heard nothing. Who would have dreamed of his going out, indeed?", "사실 그가 밖으로 나가리라고 누가 꿈엔들 생각했겠는가?"], ["A minute later he was in the street.", "잠시 후 그는 거리에 나와 있었다."]], [["It was nearly eight o’clock, the sun was setting.", "여덟 시가 다 되어 가고, 해가 지고 있었다."], ["It was as stifling as before, but he eagerly drank in the stinking, dusty town air.", "전과 다름없이 숨이 막혔지만, 그는 악취 나고 먼지투성이인 도시의 공기를 게걸스럽게 들이마셨다. 머리가 다소 어지러웠다."], ["His head felt rather dizzy; a sort of savage energy gleamed suddenly in his feverish eyes and his wasted, pale and yellow face.", "일종의 야성적인 에너지가 그의 열에 들뜬 눈과 수척하고 창백하고 누런 얼굴에서 별안간 번뜩였다. 그는 자신이 어디로 가는지 알지도 못했고 생각하지도 않았다."], ["He did not know and did not think where he was going, he had one thought only: “that all _this_ must be ended to-day, once for all, immediately; that he would not return home without it, because he _would not go on living like that_.”", "그에게는 단 하나의 생각뿐이었다. \"이 모든 것을 오늘, 단번에, 당장 끝내야 한다, 그러기 전에는 집으로 돌아가지 않으리라, 왜냐하면 그렇게 더는 살아갈 수 없으니까.\""], ["How, with what to make an end?", "어떻게, 무엇으로 끝장을 낸단 말인가?"], ["He had not an idea about it, he did not even want to think of it.", "그는 그에 대해 아무런 생각도 없었고, 생각하고 싶지도 않았다."], ["He drove away thought; thought tortured him.", "그는 생각을 떨쳐 냈다. 생각이 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["All he knew, all he felt was that everything must be changed “one way or another,”", "그가 아는 것, 그가 느끼는 것은 단지 모든 것이 \"어떻게든\" 바뀌어야 한다는 것뿐이었다."], ["he repeated with desperate and immovable self-confidence and determination.", "그는 절망적이면서도 흔들림 없는 자신감과 결의로 그렇게 되뇌었다."]], [["From old habit he took his usual walk in the direction of the Hay Market.", "오랜 습관대로 그는 늘 가던 대로 건초 시장 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."], ["A dark-haired young man with a barrel organ was standing in the road in front of a little general shop and was grinding out a very sentimental song.", "검은 머리의 젊은 사내가 손풍금을 들고 작은 잡화점 앞 길거리에 서서 몹시 감상적인 노래를 켜고 있었다."], ["He was accompanying a girl of fifteen, who stood on the pavement in front of him.", "그는 자기 앞 보도에 선 열다섯 살쯤 된 처녀의 노래에 반주를 넣어 주고 있었다."], ["She was dressed up in a crinoline, a mantle and a straw hat with a flame-coloured feather in it, all very old and shabby.", "처녀는 크리놀린에 망토를 걸치고 불꽃 빛깔의 깃털이 꽂힌 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 죄다 무척 낡고 초라했다."], ["In a strong and rather agreeable voice, cracked and coarsened by street singing, she sang in hope of getting a copper from the shop.", "거리에서 노래하느라 갈라지고 거칠어졌지만 힘 있고 제법 듣기 좋은 목소리로, 처녀는 가게에서 동전 한 닢이라도 받을까 하는 기대에 노래를 불렀다."], ["Raskolnikov joined two or three listeners, took out a five copeck piece and put it in the girl’s hand.", "라스콜니코프는 두세 명의 구경꾼 틈에 끼어, 오 코페이카짜리 동전을 꺼내 처녀의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["She broke off abruptly on a sentimental high note, shouted sharply to the organ grinder “Come on,”", "처녀는 감상적인 고음에서 뚝 노래를 끊더니, 손풍금쟁이에게 날카롭게 \"가자\""], ["and both moved on to the next shop.", "하고 외쳤고, 둘은 다음 가게로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["“Do you like street music?”", "\"거리의 음악을 좋아하십니까?\""], ["said Raskolnikov, addressing a middle-aged man standing idly by him.", "라스콜니코프가 곁에 멀거니 서 있던 중년 사내에게 말을 걸었다."], ["The man looked at him, startled and wondering.", "사내는 깜짝 놀라 의아한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I love to hear singing to a street organ,”", "\"저는 손풍금에 맞춰 부르는 노래를 듣는 게 좋아요.\" 라스콜니코프는 말했는데, 그의 태도는 화제와는 이상하게 어울리지 않는 듯했다."], ["said Raskolnikov, and his manner seemed strangely out of keeping with the subject--“I like it on cold, dark, damp autumn evenings--they must be damp--when all the passers-by have pale green, sickly faces, or better still when wet snow is falling straight down, when there’s no wind--you know what I mean?--and the street lamps shine through it...”", "\"춥고 어둡고 축축한 가을 저녁에 듣는 게 좋지요 — 반드시 축축해야 합니다 — 지나가는 사람들 얼굴이 죄다 파리하고 병색이 짙을 때, 아니면 더 좋게는 바람 한 점 없이 진눈깨비가 곧장 내리쏟아질 때 말이지요 — 무슨 말인지 아시겠어요? — 그리고 가로등이 그 사이로 비칠 때…….\""]], [["“I don’t know....", "\"모르겠는데요……."], ["Excuse me...”", "실례합니다…….\""], ["muttered the stranger, frightened by the question and Raskolnikov’s strange manner, and he crossed over to the other side of the street.", "낯선 사내는 그 질문과 라스콜니코프의 기이한 태도에 겁을 먹고 중얼거리더니, 길 건너편으로 가 버렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov walked straight on and came out at the corner of the Hay Market, where the huckster and his wife had talked with Lizaveta; but they were not there now.", "라스콜니코프는 곧장 걸어가 건초 시장 모퉁이로 나왔는데, 바로 그곳에서 행상과 그 아내가 리자베타와 이야기를 나누었던 것이다. 하지만 지금 그들은 거기 없었다."], ["Recognising the place, he stopped, looked round and addressed a young fellow in a red shirt who stood gaping before a corn chandler’s shop.", "그 장소를 알아본 그는 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보다가, 곡물상 가게 앞에서 입을 헤벌리고 서 있던 붉은 셔츠 차림의 젊은이에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Isn’t there a man who keeps a booth with his wife at this corner?”", "\"이 모퉁이에서 아내랑 같이 노점을 하는 사람이 있지 않나?\""]], [["“All sorts of people keep booths here,”", "\"여기선 별의별 사람이 다 노점을 합니다요.\""], ["answered the young man, glancing superciliously at Raskolnikov.", "젊은이가 라스콜니코프를 깔보듯 흘끗 보며 대답했다."]], [["“What’s his name?”", "\"그 사람 이름이 뭐지?\""]], [["“What he was christened.”", "\"세례받은 이름이지요.\""]], [["“Aren’t you a Zaraïsky man, too?", "\"자네도 자라이스크 사람 아닌가?"], ["Which province?”", "어느 현(縣) 출신이지?\""]], [["The young man looked at Raskolnikov again.", "젊은이는 다시 라스콜니코프를 바라보았다."]], [["“It’s not a province, your excellency, but a district.", "\"현이 아니라 군(郡)입죠, 나리."], ["Graciously forgive me, your excellency!”", "너그러이 용서하십쇼, 나리!\""]], [["“Is that a tavern at the top there?”", "\"저기 위쪽에 있는 게 선술집인가?\""]], [["“Yes, it’s an eating-house and there’s a billiard-room and you’ll find princesses there too....", "\"예, 음식점인데 당구장도 있고, 공주님들도 거기서 만나실 수 있습죠……."], ["La-la!”", "랄라!\""]], [["Raskolnikov crossed the square.", "라스콜니코프는 광장을 가로질렀다."], ["In that corner there was a dense crowd of peasants.", "그 모퉁이에는 농부들이 빽빽이 몰려 있었다."], ["He pushed his way into the thickest part of it, looking at the faces.", "그는 사람들 얼굴을 살피며 그 가장 빽빽한 무리 속으로 비집고 들어갔다."], ["He felt an unaccountable inclination to enter into conversation with people.", "그는 까닭 모를 충동에 사람들과 이야기를 나누고 싶어졌다."], ["But the peasants took no notice of him; they were all shouting in groups together.", "하지만 농부들은 그에게 아무 관심도 두지 않았다. 그들은 무리 지어 다 같이 떠들어 대고 있었다."], ["He stood and thought a little and took a turning to the right in the direction of V.", "그는 잠시 서서 생각하다가, 오른쪽으로 꺾어 V 거리 쪽으로 발길을 옮겼다."]], [["He had often crossed that little street which turns at an angle, leading from the market-place to Sadovy Street.", "그는 시장 광장에서 사도바야 거리로 비스듬히 꺾어지며 이어지는 그 작은 골목을 자주 지나다녔다."], ["Of late he had often felt drawn to wander about this district, when he felt depressed, that he might feel more so.", "최근 들어 그는 우울할 때면 더욱 우울해지려고 이 동네를 자주 배회하고 싶은 충동을 느끼곤 했다."]], [["Now he walked along, thinking of nothing.", "지금 그는 아무 생각 없이 걸어가고 있었다."], ["At that point there is a great block of buildings, entirely let out in dram shops and eating-houses; women were continually running in and out, bare-headed and in their indoor clothes.", "그곳에는 거대한 건물 한 채가 있었는데, 통째로 술집과 음식점에 세 놓여 있었다. 여자들이 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않고 실내복 차림으로 끊임없이 들락날락했다."], ["Here and there they gathered in groups, on the pavement, especially about the entrances to various festive establishments in the lower storeys.", "여기저기 보도 위에, 특히 아래층의 갖가지 유흥업소 입구 근처에 그들이 무리 지어 모여 있었다."], ["From one of these a loud din, sounds of singing, the tinkling of a guitar and shouts of merriment, floated into the street.", "그중 한 곳에서 요란한 소음과 노랫소리, 기타의 딩동거림, 흥겨운 고함이 거리로 흘러나왔다."], ["A crowd of women were thronging round the door; some were sitting on the steps, others on the pavement, others were standing talking.", "한 무리의 여자들이 문간을 둘러싸고 북적였다. 더러는 계단에, 더러는 보도에 앉아 있었고, 더러는 선 채로 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["A drunken soldier, smoking a cigarette, was walking near them in the road, swearing; he seemed to be trying to find his way somewhere, but had forgotten where.", "술 취한 군인 하나가 담배를 피우며 욕설을 퍼부으면서 그들 근처 길을 어슬렁거렸는데, 어딘가로 가는 길을 찾으려는 듯하면서도 그곳이 어딘지 잊어버린 것 같았다."], ["One beggar was quarrelling with another, and a man dead drunk was lying right across the road.", "거지 하나가 또 다른 거지와 다투고 있었고, 곤드레만드레 취한 사내 하나가 길 한복판에 가로로 뻗어 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov joined the throng of women, who were talking in husky voices.", "라스콜니코프는 쉰 목소리로 떠들어 대는 여자들 무리에 끼어들었다."], ["They were bare-headed and wore cotton dresses and goatskin shoes.", "그들은 머리에 아무것도 쓰지 않고 무명옷에 염소 가죽 신발을 신고 있었다."], ["There were women of forty and some not more than seventeen; almost all had blackened eyes.", "마흔 살쯤 된 여자도, 열일곱밖에 안 돼 보이는 여자도 있었는데, 거의 모두가 눈이 시퍼렇게 멍들어 있었다."]], [["He felt strangely attracted by the singing and all the noise and uproar in the saloon below....", "그는 아래쪽 술집의 노랫소리와 온갖 소음, 떠들썩한 소동에 이상하게 끌렸다……."], ["someone could be heard within dancing frantically, marking time with his heels to the sounds of the guitar and of a thin falsetto voice singing a jaunty air.", "안에서 누군가가 기타 소리와, 경쾌한 곡조를 부르는 가느다란 가성에 맞춰 발뒤꿈치로 박자를 맞추며 미친 듯이 춤추는 소리가 들렸다."], ["He listened intently, gloomily and dreamily, bending down at the entrance and peeping inquisitively in from the pavement.", "그는 입구에 몸을 숙이고 보도에서 호기심 어린 눈으로 안을 들여다보며, 음울하고 몽롱하게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Oh, my handsome soldier Don’t beat me for nothing,”", "\"오, 나의 잘생긴 병사여 까닭 없이 날 때리지 말아요,\""]], [["trilled the thin voice of the singer.", "가수의 가느다란 목소리가 떨리듯 흘러나왔다."], ["Raskolnikov felt a great desire to make out what he was singing, as though everything depended on that.", "라스콜니코프는 마치 모든 것이 거기에 달려 있기라도 한 듯, 그가 무슨 노래를 부르는지 알아내고 싶은 강렬한 욕망에 사로잡혔다."]], [["“Shall I go in?”", "'들어가 볼까?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“They are laughing.", "'저들은 웃고 있군."], ["From drink.", "술 때문이지."], ["Shall I get drunk?”", "나도 취해 볼까?'"]], [["“Won’t you come in?”", "\"안 들어오시겠어요?\""], ["one of the women asked him.", "여자들 중 하나가 그에게 물었다."], ["Her voice was still musical and less thick than the others, she was young and not repulsive--the only one of the group.", "그녀의 목소리는 아직 곱고 다른 여자들보다 덜 탁했다. 그녀는 젊고 혐오스럽지 않았다 — 그 무리에서 유일하게."]], [["“Why, she’s pretty,”", "\"아니, 예쁘네요.\""], ["he said, drawing himself up and looking at her.", "그는 몸을 일으키며 그녀를 바라보고 말했다."]], [["She smiled, much pleased at the compliment.", "그녀는 그 칭찬에 무척 기뻐하며 미소 지었다."]], [["“You’re very nice looking yourself,”", "\"손님도 아주 잘생기셨는걸요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Isn’t he thin though!”", "\"근데 어쩜 저렇게 비쩍 말랐담!\""], ["observed another woman in a deep bass.", "다른 여자가 굵은 저음으로 한마디 했다."], ["“Have you just come out of a hospital?”", "\"병원에서 막 나왔수?\""]], [["“They’re all generals’ daughters, it seems, but they have all snub noses,”", "\"보아하니 죄다 장군님 따님들인 모양인데, 하나같이 들창코구먼.\""], ["interposed a tipsy peasant with a sly smile on his face, wearing a loose coat.", "헐렁한 외투를 걸친 거나하게 취한 농부가 얼굴에 능청맞은 미소를 띠며 끼어들었다."], ["“See how jolly they are.”", "\"거참 흥겹기도 하지.\""]], [["“Go along with you!”", "\"썩 꺼져!\""]], [["“I’ll go, sweetie!”", "\"가지, 귀여운 것!\""]], [["And he darted down into the saloon below.", "그러더니 그는 아래쪽 술집으로 쏜살같이 뛰어들었다."], ["Raskolnikov moved on.", "라스콜니코프는 발길을 옮겼다."]], [["“I say, sir,”", "\"저기요, 손님.\""], ["the girl shouted after him.", "처녀가 그의 뒤에다 대고 외쳤다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭐요?\""]], [["She hesitated.", "그녀는 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“I’ll always be pleased to spend an hour with you, kind gentleman, but now I feel shy.", "\"언제든 손님이랑 한 시간 즐겁게 보내 드릴게요, 친절한 양반. 근데 지금은 좀 부끄러워서요."], ["Give me six copecks for a drink, there’s a nice young man!”", "한잔 마시게 육 코페이카만 주세요, 착한 도련님!\""]], [["Raskolnikov gave her what came first--fifteen copecks.", "라스콜니코프는 손에 먼저 잡히는 대로 — 십오 코페이카를 주었다."]], [["“Ah, what a good-natured gentleman!”", "\"어머, 정말 마음씨 고운 양반이네!\""]], [["“What’s your name?”", "\"이름이 뭐요?\""]], [["“Ask for Duclida.”", "\"두클리다라고 찾으세요.\""]], [["“Well, that’s too much,”", "\"아니, 그건 너무하잖아.\""], ["one of the women observed, shaking her head at Duclida.", "여자들 중 하나가 두클리다를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 한마디 했다."], ["“I don’t know how you can ask like that.", "\"어떻게 그렇게 손을 벌릴 수 있담."], ["I believe I should drop with shame....”", "나 같으면 창피해서 쓰러질 텐데…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov looked curiously at the speaker.", "라스콜니코프는 호기심 어린 눈으로 그 말을 한 여자를 바라보았다."], ["She was a pock-marked wench of thirty, covered with bruises, with her upper lip swollen.", "그녀는 서른 살쯤 된, 얼굴이 곰보투성이에 온몸이 멍투성이고 윗입술이 부어오른 계집이었다."], ["She made her criticism quietly and earnestly.", "그녀는 조용하고 진지하게 핀잔을 주었다."], ["“Where is it,”", "'어디서였더라.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."], ["“Where is it I’ve read that someone condemned to death says or thinks, an hour before his death, that if he had to live on some high rock, on such a narrow ledge that he’d only room to stand, and the ocean, everlasting darkness, everlasting solitude, everlasting tempest around him, if he had to remain standing on a square yard of space all his life, a thousand years, eternity, it were better to live so than to die at once!", "'어디서 읽었더라, 사형을 선고받은 누군가가 죽기 한 시간 전에 이렇게 말하거나 생각했다는 것을. 만일 어느 높은 바위 위, 겨우 발 디딜 만큼 좁은 턱 위에서 살아야 한다 해도, 그리고 사방에 대양과 영원한 어둠, 영원한 고독, 영원한 폭풍이 둘러싸고 있다 해도, 평생을, 천 년을, 영원토록 그 한 평짜리 공간에 서 있어야 한다 해도, 당장 죽는 것보다는 그렇게라도 사는 게 낫다고!"], ["Only to live, to live and live!", "오직 사는 것, 살고 또 사는 것!"], ["Life, whatever it may be!...", "그것이 어떤 삶이든 간에!……."], ["How true it is!", "이 얼마나 진실한가!"], ["Good God, how true!", "오 하느님, 얼마나 진실한가!"], ["Man is a vile creature!...", "인간이란 비열한 피조물이다!……."], ["And vile is he who calls him vile for that,”", "그리고 그런 이유로 인간을 비열하다 부르는 자 또한 비열하다.'"], ["he added a moment later.", "그는 잠시 후 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["He went into another street.", "그는 다른 거리로 들어섰다."], ["“Bah, the Palais de Cristal!", "'아하, 팔레 드 크리스탈*!"], ["Razumihin was just talking of the Palais de Cristal.", "라주미힌이 방금 팔레 드 크리스탈 얘기를 했었지."], ["But what on earth was it I wanted?", "그런데 내가 도대체 뭘 원했더라?"], ["Yes, the newspapers....", "그래, 신문이지……."], ["Zossimov said he’d read it in the papers.", "조시모프가 신문에서 읽었다고 했어."], ["Have you the papers?”", "신문 있나요?'"], ["he asked, going into a very spacious and positively clean restaurant, consisting of several rooms, which were, however, rather empty.", "그는 여러 개의 방으로 이루어진, 무척 널찍하고 정말이지 깨끗한 음식점으로 들어가며 물었는데, 방들은 그래도 다소 비어 있었다."], ["Two or three people were drinking tea, and in a room further away were sitting four men drinking champagne.", "두세 사람이 차를 마시고 있었고, 더 안쪽 방에는 네 명의 사내가 앉아 샴페인을 마시고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov fancied that Zametov was one of them, but he could not be sure at that distance.", "라스콜니코프는 그중 하나가 자메토프인 것 같았으나, 그 거리에서는 확신할 수 없었다."], ["“What if it is?”", "'그래 본들 어떤가?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Will you have vodka?”", "\"보드카 드시겠습니까?\""], ["asked the waiter.", "종업원이 물었다."]], [["“Give me some tea and bring me the papers, the old ones for the last five days, and I’ll give you something.”", "\"차를 주게, 그리고 신문을 가져다줘, 지난 닷새치 묵은 걸로, 그러면 내 뭔가 주지.\""]], [["“Yes, sir, here’s to-day’s.", "\"예, 손님, 여기 오늘 신문입니다."], ["No vodka?”", "보드카는 안 하시고요?\""]], [["The old newspapers and the tea were brought.", "묵은 신문과 차가 나왔다."], ["Raskolnikov sat down and began to look through them.", "라스콜니코프는 자리에 앉아 그것들을 훑어보기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh, damn...", "'오, 제기랄……."], ["these are the items of intelligence.", "이게 사건 기사로군."], ["An accident on a staircase, spontaneous combustion of a shopkeeper from alcohol, a fire in Peski... a fire in the Petersburg quarter...", "계단에서의 사고, 술 때문에 가게 주인이 저절로 불타 죽음, 페스키에서의 화재……, 페테르부르크 지구의 화재……, 또 페테르부르크 지구의 화재……, 또 페테르부르크 지구의 화재……."], ["another fire in the Petersburg quarter...", "아, 여기 있군!'"], ["and another fire in the Petersburg quarter.... Ah, here it is!”", "그는 마침내 찾던 것을 발견하고 읽기 시작했다."], ["He found at last what he was seeking and began to read it. The lines danced before his eyes, but he read it all and began eagerly seeking later additions in the following numbers.", "글자들이 눈앞에서 춤을 췄지만, 그는 끝까지 다 읽고는 이어지는 신문들에서 그 후의 속보를 게걸스럽게 찾기 시작했다."], ["His hands shook with nervous impatience as he turned the sheets.", "신문장을 넘기는 그의 손이 초조한 신경질로 떨렸다."], ["Suddenly someone sat down beside him at his table.", "갑자기 누군가가 그의 탁자 옆에 와 앉았다."], ["He looked up, it was the head clerk Zametov, looking just the same, with the rings on his fingers and the watch-chain, with the curly, black hair, parted and pomaded, with the smart waistcoat, rather shabby coat and doubtful linen.", "그가 고개를 들어 보니, 그 서기장 자메토프였다. 손가락에 반지를 끼고 시곗줄을 늘어뜨린 채, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 가르마 타서 포마드를 바르고, 맵시 있는 조끼에 다소 낡은 외투, 미심쩍은 셔츠를 걸친 여전한 모습이었다."], ["He was in a good humour, at least he was smiling very gaily and good-humouredly.", "그는 기분이 좋았다. 적어도 무척 명랑하고 유쾌하게 웃고 있었다."], ["His dark face was rather flushed from the champagne he had drunk.", "마신 샴페인 탓에 그의 가무잡잡한 얼굴이 다소 발그레했다."]], [["“What, you here?”", "\"아니, 여긴 웬일이오?\""], ["he began in surprise, speaking as though he’d known him all his life.", "그는 마치 평생 알고 지낸 사이인 양 말을 걸며 놀라서 입을 열었다."], ["“Why, Razumihin told me only yesterday you were unconscious.", "\"라주미힌이 바로 어제 당신이 의식이 없다고 그러던데. 참 희한한 일이군!"], ["How strange! And do you know I’ve been to see you?”", "그리고 말이오, 내가 당신을 보러 갔던 거 아시오?\""]], [["Raskolnikov knew he would come up to him.", "라스콜니코프는 그가 다가오리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He laid aside the papers and turned to Zametov.", "그는 신문을 옆으로 치우고 자메토프 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["There was a smile on his lips, and a new shade of irritable impatience was apparent in that smile.", "그의 입가에 미소가 떠올랐고, 그 미소에는 짜증스러운 초조함의 새로운 기색이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I know you have,”", "\"왔다는 거 압니다.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I’ve heard it.", "\"들었어요."], ["You looked for my sock....", "내 양말을 찾았다지요……."], ["And you know Razumihin has lost his heart to you?", "그리고 라주미힌이 당신한테 홀딱 반했다는 것도 아시오?"], ["He says you’ve been with him to Luise Ivanovna’s--you know, the woman you tried to befriend, for whom you winked to the Explosive Lieutenant and he would not understand.", "그가 그러더군요, 당신이 같이 루이자 이바노브나 집에 갔었다고 — 왜, 당신이 도와주려고 했던 그 여자 말이오, 그 여자 때문에 당신이 그 화약 같은 중위한테 눈짓을 했는데 그가 알아채지 못했다지요."], ["Do you remember?", "기억나오?"], ["How could he fail to understand--it was quite clear, wasn’t it?”", "어떻게 그가 못 알아챌 수가 있나요 — 아주 분명했는데, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“What a hot head he is!”", "\"그 양반 정말 다혈질이라니까!\""]], [["“The explosive one?”", "\"그 화약 같은 양반 말이오?\""]], [["“No, your friend Razumihin.”", "\"아니, 당신 친구 라주미힌 말이오.\""]], [["“You must have a jolly life, Mr.", "\"당신은 즐겁게 사는군요, 자메토프 씨."], ["Zametov; entrance free to the most agreeable places.", "가장 기분 좋은 곳에 공짜로 드나드니 말이오."], ["Who’s been pouring champagne into you just now?”", "방금 누가 당신한테 샴페인을 부어 줬소?\""]], [["“We’ve just been...", "\"우린 그냥……"], ["having a drink together....", "같이 한잔하고 있었던 거요……."], ["You talk about pouring it into me!”", "부어 줬다니, 무슨 말씀을!\""]], [["“By way of a fee!", "\"보수 삼아 받았겠지!"], ["You profit by everything!”", "당신은 매사에서 이득을 보는구먼!\""], ["Raskolnikov laughed, “it’s all right, my dear boy,”", "라스콜니코프가 웃었다. \"괜찮소, 이 친구야.\""], ["he added, slapping Zametov on the shoulder.", "그는 자메토프의 어깨를 툭 치며 덧붙였다."], ["“I am not speaking from temper, but in a friendly way, for sport, as that workman of yours said when he was scuffling with Dmitri, in the case of the old woman....”", "\"악의가 있어 하는 말이 아니라, 친근하게, 장난삼아 하는 거요, 그 노파 사건에서 당신네 직공이 드미트리랑 드잡이하면서 말한 것처럼 말이지…….\""]], [["“How do you know about it?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 아시오?\""]], [["“Perhaps I know more about it than you do.”", "\"어쩌면 당신보다 내가 더 잘 알고 있을지도 모르지.\""]], [["“How strange you are....", "\"당신 참 이상하군……."], ["I am sure you are still very unwell.", "분명 아직 몸이 많이 안 좋은 게요."], ["You oughtn’t to have come out.”", "나오지 말았어야 했는데.\""]], [["“Oh, do I seem strange to you?”", "\"오, 내가 당신한테 이상해 보이오?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["What are you doing, reading the papers?” “Yes.”", "뭘 하고 있는 거요, 신문 읽는 거요?\""]], [["“There’s a lot about the fires.”", "\"그렇소.\" \"화재 기사가 많지요.\""]], [["“No, I am not reading about the fires.”", "\"아니, 난 화재 기사를 읽는 게 아니오.\""], ["Here he looked mysteriously at Zametov; his lips were twisted again in a mocking smile.", "여기서 그는 의미심장하게 자메토프를 바라보았다. 그의 입술이 다시 조롱하는 미소로 비틀렸다."], ["“No, I am not reading about the fires,”", "\"아니, 난 화재 기사를 읽는 게 아니오.\""], ["he went on, winking at Zametov.", "그는 자메토프에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말을 이었다."], ["“But confess now, my dear fellow, you’re awfully anxious to know what I am reading about?”", "\"그런데 솔직히 말해 보시오, 이 친구야, 당신 내가 뭘 읽는지 끔찍이도 알고 싶지 않소?\""]], [["“I am not in the least.", "\"전혀 아니오."], ["Mayn’t I ask a question?", "질문 하나 못 하오?"], ["Why do you keep on...?”", "왜 자꾸……?\""]], [["“Listen, you are a man of culture and education?”", "\"이봐요, 당신은 교양 있고 학식 있는 사람이지요?\""]], [["“I was in the sixth class at the gymnasium,”", "\"김나지움 육 학년까지 다녔소.\""], ["said Zametov with some dignity.", "자메토프가 다소 위엄을 부리며 말했다."]], [["“Sixth class!", "\"육 학년이라!"], ["Ah, my cock-sparrow!", "아, 이 참새 같은 양반아!"], ["With your parting and your rings--you are a gentleman of fortune.", "그 가르마에 반지까지 — 당신은 팔자 좋은 신사로구먼."], ["Foo!", "이런!"], ["what a charming boy!”", "정말 매력적인 도련님이야!\""], ["Here Raskolnikov broke into a nervous laugh right in Zametov’s face.", "여기서 라스콜니코프는 자메토프의 얼굴에 대고 신경질적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The latter drew back, more amazed than offended.", "상대는 모욕감보다 놀라움이 더해 몸을 뒤로 물렸다."]], [["“Foo!", "\"어휴!"], ["how strange you are!”", "당신 정말 이상하군!\""], ["Zametov repeated very seriously.", "자메토프가 무척 진지하게 되풀이했다."], ["“I can’t help thinking you are still delirious.”", "\"아직도 헛소리를 하는 것 같다는 생각이 자꾸 드는구려.\""]], [["“I am delirious?", "\"내가 헛소리를 한다고?"], ["You are fibbing, my cock-sparrow!", "거짓말 말아요, 이 참새 양반아!"], ["So I am strange?", "그래, 내가 이상하다고?"], ["You find me curious, do you?”", "내가 흥미롭소, 그렇소?\""]], [["“Yes, curious.”", "\"그렇소, 흥미롭소.\""]], [["“Shall I tell you what I was reading about, what I was looking for?", "\"내가 뭘 읽고 있었는지, 뭘 찾고 있었는지 말해 줄까요?"], ["See what a lot of papers I’ve made them bring me.", "보시오, 내가 얼마나 많은 신문을 가져다 달라고 했는지."], ["Suspicious, eh?”", "수상하지, 응?\""]], [["“Well, what is it?”", "\"그래, 그게 뭐요?\""]], [["“You prick up your ears?”", "\"귀가 솔깃해지지?\""]], [["“How do you mean--‘prick up my ears’?”", "\"'귀가 솔깃해진다'니, 무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“I’ll explain that afterwards, but now, my boy, I declare to you...", "\"그건 나중에 설명하리다, 한데 지금은, 이 친구야, 당신한테 분명히 말하겠소……. 아니, '고백하겠소'가 낫겠군……."], ["no, better ‘I confess’...", "아니, 그것도 맞지 않아."], ["No, that’s not right either; ‘I make a deposition and you take it.’ I depose that I was reading, that I was looking and searching....”", "'내가 진술할 테니 당신이 받아 적으시오.' 나는 진술하노니, 내가 읽고 있었던 것은, 내가 보고 찾고 있었던 것은…….\""], ["he screwed up his eyes and paused.", "그는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 잠시 멈추었다."], ["“I was searching--and came here on purpose to do it--for news of the murder of the old pawnbroker woman,”", "\"내가 찾고 있었던 것은 — 그리고 일부러 그러려고 여기 온 것은 — 그 늙은 전당포 노파를 살해한 사건에 관한 소식이었소.\""], ["he articulated at last, almost in a whisper, bringing his face exceedingly close to the face of Zametov.", "그는 마침내, 거의 속삭이듯 또박또박 말하며, 자기 얼굴을 자메토프의 얼굴에 바싹 갖다 댔다."], ["Zametov looked at him steadily, without moving or drawing his face away.", "자메토프는 움직이지도, 얼굴을 떼지도 않은 채 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["What struck Zametov afterwards as the strangest part of it all was that silence followed for exactly a minute, and that they gazed at one another all the while.", "나중에 자메토프에게 이 모든 것 중 가장 기이하게 느껴진 것은, 정확히 일 분 동안 침묵이 이어졌고, 그 내내 둘이 서로를 응시했다는 점이었다."]], [["“What if you have been reading about it?”", "\"당신이 그걸 읽고 있었던들 어떻단 말이오?\""], ["he cried at last, perplexed and impatient.", "그가 당혹스럽고 초조한 듯 마침내 외쳤다."], ["“That’s no business of mine!", "\"그게 나랑 무슨 상관이오!"], ["What of it?”", "그래서 뭐 어쨌다는 거요?\""]], [["“The same old woman,”", "\"바로 그 노파 말이오.\""], ["Raskolnikov went on in the same whisper, not heeding Zametov’s explanation, “about whom you were talking in the police-office, you remember, when I fainted.", "라스콜니코프는 자메토프의 해명에 아랑곳 않고 여전히 속삭이며 말을 이었다. \"당신이 경찰서에서 얘기했던, 내가 기절했을 때 말이오, 기억하시오?"], ["Well, do you understand now?”", "자, 이제 알겠소?\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 말이오? 알다니……"], ["Understand...", "뭘 말이오?\""], ["what?” Zametov brought out, almost alarmed.", "자메토프가 거의 겁에 질려 내뱉었다."]], [["Raskolnikov’s set and earnest face was suddenly transformed, and he suddenly went off into the same nervous laugh as before, as though utterly unable to restrain himself.", "라스콜니코프의 굳고 진지하던 얼굴이 별안간 일변하더니, 그는 마치 도저히 자제할 수 없다는 듯 갑자기 아까와 똑같은 신경질적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["And in one flash he recalled with extraordinary vividness of sensation a moment in the recent past, that moment when he stood with the axe behind the door, while the latch trembled and the men outside swore and shook it, and he had a sudden desire to shout at them, to swear at them, to put out his tongue at them, to mock them, to laugh, and laugh, and laugh!", "그리고 한순간, 그는 가까운 과거의 어느 순간을 비상하리만큼 생생한 감각으로 떠올렸다. 그가 도끼를 들고 문 뒤에 서 있는 동안 빗장이 덜컹거리고 밖에 있던 사내들이 욕설을 퍼부으며 문을 흔들어 대던 그 순간, 그때 그는 별안간 그들에게 소리치고, 욕하고, 혀를 내밀고, 비웃고, 웃고, 웃고, 또 웃고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔던 것이다!"]], [["“You are either mad, or...”", "\"당신은 미쳤거나, 아니면…….\""], ["began Zametov, and he broke off, as though stunned by the idea that had suddenly flashed into his mind.", "자메토프가 입을 열다가, 별안간 머릿속에 번뜩 떠오른 생각에 망연자실한 듯 말을 뚝 끊었다."]], [["“Or?", "\"아니면?"], ["Or what?", "아니면 뭐요?"], ["What?", "뭐?"], ["Come, tell me!”", "자, 말해 보시오!\""]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아니오.\""], ["said Zametov, getting angry, “it’s all nonsense!”", "자메토프가 화를 내며 말했다. \"다 헛소리요!\""]], [["Both were silent.", "둘 다 입을 다물었다."], ["After his sudden fit of laughter Raskolnikov became suddenly thoughtful and melancholy.", "갑작스러운 웃음의 발작이 지나간 뒤 라스콜니코프는 별안간 생각에 잠기며 우울해졌다."], ["He put his elbow on the table and leaned his head on his hand.", "그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손에 머리를 기댔다."], ["He seemed to have completely forgotten Zametov.", "그는 자메토프를 완전히 잊어버린 듯했다."], ["The silence lasted for some time.", "침묵이 한동안 이어졌다."]], [["“Why don’t you drink your tea?", "\"차를 왜 안 마시오?"], ["It’s getting cold,”", "식어 가는데.\""], ["said Zametov.", "자메토프가 말했다."]], [["“What! Tea?", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Oh, yes....”", "차?"], ["Raskolnikov sipped the glass, put a morsel of bread in his mouth and, suddenly looking at Zametov, seemed to remember everything and pulled himself together.", "아, 그래…….\" 라스콜니코프는 잔을 홀짝이고 빵 한 조각을 입에 넣었다가, 갑자기 자메토프를 바라보더니 모든 것을 떠올린 듯 정신을 차렸다."], ["At the same moment his face resumed its original mocking expression.", "동시에 그의 얼굴은 본래의 조롱하는 표정을 되찾았다."], ["He went on drinking tea.", "그는 계속 차를 마셨다."]], [["“There have been a great many of these crimes lately,”", "\"요즘 이런 범죄가 굉장히 많아졌어요.\""], ["said Zametov.", "자메토프가 말했다."], ["“Only the other day I read in the _Moscow News_ that a whole gang of false coiners had been caught in Moscow.", "\"바로 며칠 전에도 '모스크바 통신'에서 읽었는데, 모스크바에서 위조 화폐범 일당이 통째로 잡혔다더군요."], ["It was a regular society.", "아주 정식 조직이었답니다."], ["They used to forge tickets!”", "채권을 위조했다지 뭐요!\""]], [["“Oh, but it was a long time ago!", "\"오, 하지만 그건 오래전 일이오!"], ["I read about it a month ago,”", "난 한 달 전에 읽었소.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered calmly.", "라스콜니코프가 차분하게 대답했다."], ["“So you consider them criminals?”", "\"그래, 당신은 그들을 범죄자로 여기오?\""], ["he added, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Of course they are criminals.”", "\"물론 범죄자지요.\""]], [["“They?", "\"그들이?"], ["They are children, simpletons, not criminals!", "그들은 어린애요, 얼간이지, 범죄자가 아니오!"], ["Why, half a hundred people meeting for such an object--what an idea!", "아니, 그런 목적으로 쉰 명이나 되는 사람이 모이다니 — 무슨 발상이람!"], ["Three would be too many, and then they want to have more faith in one another than in themselves!", "세 명도 너무 많은데, 게다가 그들은 자기 자신보다 서로를 더 믿어야 한단 말이지!"], ["One has only to blab in his cups and it all collapses.", "한 놈이 취해서 입을 놀리기만 하면 죄다 무너지는 거요."], ["Simpletons!", "얼간이들!"], ["They engaged untrustworthy people to change the notes--what a thing to trust to a casual stranger!", "그들은 못 믿을 자들을 시켜 지폐를 바꾸게 했어 — 그런 일을 어쩌다 만난 낯선 자한테 맡기다니!"], ["Well, let us suppose that these simpletons succeed and each makes a million, and what follows for the rest of their lives?", "자, 그럼 이 얼간이들이 성공해서 저마다 백만 루블씩 벌었다고 칩시다, 그러면 남은 평생이 어떻게 되겠소?"], ["Each is dependent on the others for the rest of his life!", "저마다 남은 평생 동안 서로에게 매여 있어야 하는 거요!"], ["Better hang oneself at once!", "차라리 당장 목을 매는 게 낫지!"], ["And they did not know how to change the notes either; the man who changed the notes took five thousand roubles, and his hands trembled.", "게다가 그들은 지폐를 바꿀 줄도 몰랐어. 지폐를 바꾸러 간 자가 오천 루블을 받았는데 손을 벌벌 떨더라는 거요."], ["He counted the first four thousand, but did not count the fifth thousand--he was in such a hurry to get the money into his pocket and run away.", "처음 사천 루블은 세었는데, 다섯 번째 천 루블은 세지도 않았소 — 돈을 주머니에 넣고 달아나기에 급급했던 거지."], ["Of course he roused suspicion.", "당연히 의심을 샀고."], ["And the whole thing came to a crash through one fool!", "그래서 그 모든 게 멍청이 하나 때문에 와르르 무너진 거요!"], ["Is it possible?”", "어떻게 그럴 수가 있소?\""]], [["“That his hands trembled?”", "\"손을 떨었다는 거 말이오?\""], ["observed Zametov, “yes, that’s quite possible.", "자메토프가 한마디 했다. \"그래, 그건 충분히 있을 법한 일이오."], ["That, I feel quite sure, is possible.", "그건, 분명 그럴 수 있다고 나는 확신하오."], ["Sometimes one can’t stand things.”", "때로는 사람이 견뎌 내지 못하는 법이지요.\""]], [["“Can’t stand that?”", "\"그걸 못 견딘다고?\""]], [["“Why, could you stand it then? No, I couldn’t.", "\"왜요, 당신이라면 그걸 견디겠소?"], ["For the sake of a hundred roubles to face such a terrible experience?", "아니, 나라면 못 견디오. 고작 백 루블 때문에 그런 끔찍한 일을 겪다니?"], ["To go with false notes into a bank where it’s their business to spot that sort of thing!", "그런 일을 적발하는 게 본업인 은행에 위조지폐를 들고 들어가다니!"], ["No, I should not have the face to do it.", "아니, 난 그럴 낯짝이 없소."], ["Would you?”", "당신은 어떻소?\""]], [["Raskolnikov had an intense desire again “to put his tongue out.”", "라스콜니코프는 다시금 \"혀를 내밀고 싶은\" 강렬한 욕망에 사로잡혔다."], ["Shivers kept running down his spine.", "등줄기를 따라 자꾸만 오한이 흘렀다."]], [["“I should do it quite differently,” Raskolnikov began. “This is how I would change the notes: I’d count the first thousand three or four times backwards and forwards, looking at every note and then I’d set to the second thousand; I’d count that half-way through and then hold some fifty-rouble note to the light, then turn it, then hold it to the light again--to see whether it was a good one. ‘I am afraid,’ I would say, ‘a relation of mine lost twenty-five roubles the other day through a false note,’ and then I’d tell them the whole story. And after I began counting the third, ‘No, excuse me,’ I would say, ‘I fancy I made a mistake in the seventh hundred in that second thousand, I am not sure.’ And so I would give up the third thousand and go back to the second and so on to the end. And when I had finished, I’d pick out one from the fifth and one from the second thousand and take them again to the light and ask again, ‘Change them, please,’ and put the clerk into such a stew that he would not know how to get rid of me. When I’d finished and had gone out, I’d come back, ‘No, excuse me,’ and ask for some explanation. That’s how I’d do it.”", "\"나라면 전혀 다르게 했을 거요.\" 라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다. \"내가 지폐를 바꾼다면 이렇게 했겠지. 처음 천 루블을 서너 번씩 앞뒤로 세면서 한 장 한 장 들여다본 다음, 두 번째 천 루블로 넘어가는 거요. 그것도 절반쯤 세다가 오십 루블짜리 한 장을 불빛에 비춰 보고, 뒤집어 보고, 다시 불빛에 비춰 보고 — 진짜인지 확인하는 거지. '걱정이 돼서요,' 하고 내가 말하겠소, '며칠 전에 우리 친척이 위조지폐 때문에 이십오 루블을 날렸거든요,' 그러고는 그 이야기를 죄다 늘어놓는 거요. 그리고 세 번째 천 루블을 세기 시작한 뒤에, '아니, 죄송합니다,' 하고 내가 말하지, '아무래도 두 번째 천 루블의 칠백 번째에서 제가 실수한 것 같은데, 확실치가 않네요.' 그렇게 세 번째 천 루블을 그만두고 두 번째로 되돌아가고, 그런 식으로 끝까지 가는 거요. 그러고 나서 다 끝나면, 다섯 번째 천 루블에서 한 장, 두 번째 천 루블에서 한 장을 골라 다시 불빛에 비춰 보고 또 묻는 거지, '이것 좀 바꿔 주세요,' 하고. 그래서 그 직원을 어쩔 줄 모를 정도로 진땀 빼게 만드는 거요. 다 끝내고 나갔다가, 다시 돌아와서, '아니, 죄송합니다,' 하고 무슨 설명을 청하는 거지. 나라면 이렇게 하겠소.\""]], [["“Foo!", "\"어휴!"], ["what terrible things you say!”", "당신 정말 끔찍한 소릴 하는군!\""], ["said Zametov, laughing.", "자메토프가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“But all that is only talk.", "\"하지만 그 모든 게 말뿐이지요."], ["I dare say when it came to deeds you’d make a slip.", "막상 실행에 옮기면 당신도 실수할걸요."], ["I believe that even a practised, desperate man cannot always reckon on himself, much less you and I.", "단언컨대, 노련하고 필사적인 자도 늘 자기 자신을 믿을 수 있는 건 아니오, 하물며 당신이나 나야 말할 것도 없지."], ["To take an example near home--that old woman murdered in our district.", "가까운 예를 하나 들자면 — 우리 구역에서 살해된 그 노파 말이오."], ["The murderer seems to have been a desperate fellow, he risked everything in open daylight, was saved by a miracle--but his hands shook, too.", "살인자는 필사적인 자였던 모양인데, 백주 대낮에 모든 걸 걸었고, 기적적으로 모면했지만 — 그자도 손을 떨었어요."], ["He did not succeed in robbing the place, he couldn’t stand it.", "그자는 그곳을 다 털어 가지 못했소, 견뎌 내질 못한 거지."], ["That was clear from the...”", "그건 분명했어요, 왜냐하면…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov seemed offended.", "라스콜니코프는 모욕감을 느낀 듯했다."]], [["“Clear?", "\"분명하다고?"], ["Why don’t you catch him then?”", "그럼 왜 그자를 잡지 않는 거요?\""], ["he cried, maliciously gibing at Zametov.", "그가 자메토프를 악의적으로 비웃으며 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, they will catch him.”", "\"뭐, 곧 잡힐 거요.\""]], [["“Who?", "\"누가?"], ["You?", "당신이?"], ["Do you suppose you could catch him?", "당신이 그자를 잡을 수 있다고 생각하오?"], ["You’ve a tough job!", "만만찮은 일일 텐데!"], ["A great point for you is whether a man is spending money or not.", "당신네한테 중요한 건 어떤 자가 돈을 쓰느냐 안 쓰느냐 하는 거지."], ["If he had no money and suddenly begins spending, he must be the man.", "돈이 없던 자가 갑자기 돈을 쓰기 시작하면, 그자가 범인이 틀림없다, 이거 아니오."], ["So that any child can mislead you.”", "그러니 어린애라도 당신네를 속여 넘길 수 있소.\""]], [["“The fact is they always do that, though,”", "\"사실 그자들은 늘 그렇게 하니까요.\""], ["answered Zametov.", "자메토프가 대답했다."], ["“A man will commit a clever murder at the risk of his life and then at once he goes drinking in a tavern.", "\"어떤 자가 목숨을 걸고 교묘하게 살인을 저지르고는, 곧장 선술집에 가서 마셔 댄단 말이오."], ["They are caught spending money, they are not all as cunning as you are.", "돈을 쓰다가 잡히는 거지요, 그들 모두가 당신처럼 교활한 건 아니거든."], ["You wouldn’t go to a tavern, of course?”", "당신이라면 물론 선술집에 가지 않겠지요?\""]], [["Raskolnikov frowned and looked steadily at Zametov.", "라스콜니코프는 얼굴을 찌푸리며 자메토프를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“You seem to enjoy the subject and would like to know how I should behave in that case, too?”", "\"당신은 이 화제가 꽤 즐거운가 보군. 그래서 그런 경우에 내가 어떻게 행동할지도 알고 싶은 거요?\""], ["he asked with displeasure.", "그가 불쾌한 듯 물었다."]], [["“I should like to,”", "\"알고 싶소.\""], ["Zametov answered firmly and seriously.", "자메토프가 단호하고 진지하게 대답했다."], ["Somewhat too much earnestness began to appear in his words and looks.", "그의 말과 표정에 다소 지나친 진지함이 어리기 시작했다."]], [["“Very much?”", "\"몹시?\""]], [["“Very much!”", "\"몹시!\""]], [["“All right then.", "\"좋소, 그럼."], ["This is how I should behave,”", "나라면 이렇게 행동하겠소.\""], ["Raskolnikov began, again bringing his face close to Zametov’s, again staring at him and speaking in a whisper, so that the latter positively shuddered.", "라스콜니코프는 다시금 자기 얼굴을 자메토프의 얼굴에 바싹 갖다 대고, 다시 그를 빤히 응시하며 속삭이듯 말하기 시작했는데, 상대가 정말이지 몸서리를 칠 정도였다."], ["“This is what I should have done.", "\"나라면 이렇게 했을 거요."], ["I should have taken the money and jewels, I should have walked out of there and have gone straight to some deserted place with fences round it and scarcely anyone to be seen, some kitchen garden or place of that sort.", "돈과 보석을 챙겨서, 거기서 나와 곧장 어딘가 인적 없는, 울타리가 둘러쳐지고 사람이라곤 거의 보이지 않는 곳으로 갔겠지, 무슨 채마밭이나 그 비슷한 데로 말이오."], ["I should have looked out beforehand some stone weighing a hundredweight or more which had been lying in the corner from the time the house was built.", "미리 봐 두는 거요, 집이 지어질 때부터 구석에 놓여 있던, 백 푸드*쯤 혹은 그 이상 나가는 돌덩이를."], ["I would lift that stone--there would sure to be a hollow under it, and I would put the jewels and money in that hole.", "그 돌을 들어 올리면 — 분명 그 밑에 움푹한 데가 있을 거고, 그 구덩이에 보석과 돈을 넣는 거지."], ["Then I’d roll the stone back so that it would look as before, would press it down with my foot and walk away.", "그런 다음 돌을 다시 굴려서 전처럼 보이게 해 놓고, 발로 꾹 눌러 놓고 가 버리는 거요."], ["And for a year or two, three maybe, I would not touch it.", "그러고는 일 년이고 이 년이고, 어쩌면 삼 년이고 손도 대지 않는 거지."], ["And, well, they could search!", "그러니 자, 마음껏 수색해 보라지!"], ["There’d be no trace.”", "아무런 흔적도 없을 테니.\""]], [["“You are a madman,”", "\"당신은 미친 사람이오.\""], ["said Zametov, and for some reason he too spoke in a whisper, and moved away from Raskolnikov, whose eyes were glittering.", "자메토프가 말했는데, 어찌 된 영문인지 그 역시 속삭이듯 말하며, 눈을 번뜩이는 라스콜니코프에게서 몸을 물렸다."], ["He had turned fearfully pale and his upper lip was twitching and quivering.", "라스콜니코프는 무섭도록 창백해져 있었고, 그의 윗입술이 씰룩씰룩 떨리고 있었다."], ["He bent down as close as possible to Zametov, and his lips began to move without uttering a word.", "그는 자메토프에게 가능한 한 바싹 몸을 기울였고, 그의 입술이 한마디 말도 내뱉지 못한 채 움직이기 시작했다."], ["This lasted for half a minute; he knew what he was doing, but could not restrain himself.", "이것이 반 분 동안 이어졌다. 그는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 알면서도 자제할 수가 없었다."], ["The terrible word trembled on his lips, like the latch on that door; in another moment it will break out, in another moment he will let it go, he will speak out.", "그 끔찍한 말이 그의 입술 위에서 떨고 있었으니, 마치 그때 그 문의 빗장처럼. 다음 순간이면 그 말이 터져 나올 것이고, 다음 순간이면 그가 그것을 놓아 버리고 입 밖에 내고 말 것이었다."]], [["“And what if it was I who murdered the old woman and Lizaveta?”", "\"그런데 만일 그 노파와 리자베타를 죽인 게 바로 나라면 어떻겠소?\""], ["he said suddenly and--realised what he had done.", "그가 별안간 말했고 — 자신이 무슨 짓을 했는지 깨달았다."]], [["Zametov looked wildly at him and turned white as the tablecloth.", "자메토프는 미친 듯이 그를 바라보았고 식탁보처럼 새하얗게 질렸다."], ["His face wore a contorted smile.", "그의 얼굴에 일그러진 미소가 어렸다."]], [["“But is it possible?”", "\"하지만 그게 가능한 일이오?\""], ["he brought out faintly.", "그가 가냘프게 내뱉었다."], ["Raskolnikov looked wrathfully at him.", "라스콜니코프는 노기 띤 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Own up that you believed it, yes, you did?”", "\"솔직히 인정하시오, 당신 그 말을 믿었지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Not a bit of it, I believe it less than ever now,”", "\"전혀 아니오, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 안 믿게 됐소.\""], ["Zametov cried hastily.", "자메토프가 황급히 외쳤다."]], [["“I’ve caught my cock-sparrow!", "\"드디어 내 참새를 잡았군!"], ["So you did believe it before, if now you believe less than ever?”", "그러니까 전엔 믿었던 거구먼, 지금 그 어느 때보다 덜 믿는다면 말이지?\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"전혀 아니라니까.\""], ["cried Zametov, obviously embarrassed.", "자메토프가 외쳤는데, 분명 당황한 기색이었다."], ["“Have you been frightening me so as to lead up to this?”", "\"당신은 이 말을 끌어내려고 날 그렇게 겁준 거요?\""]], [["“You don’t believe it then?", "\"그럼 안 믿는단 말이오?"], ["What were you talking about behind my back when I went out of the police-office?", "그럼 내가 경찰서에서 나갔을 때 당신들은 등 뒤에서 무슨 얘기를 했던 거요?"], ["And why did the explosive lieutenant question me after I fainted?", "그리고 내가 기절한 뒤에 그 화약 같은 중위는 왜 날 심문했던 거요?"], ["Hey, there,”", "어이, 거기.\""], ["he shouted to the waiter, getting up and taking his cap, “how much?”", "그는 일어나 모자를 집어 들며 종업원에게 소리쳤다. \"얼마요?\""]], [["“Thirty copecks,”", "\"삼십 코페이카입니다.\""], ["the latter replied, running up.", "종업원이 달려오며 대답했다."]], [["“And there is twenty copecks for vodka.", "\"여기 보드카 값으로 이십 코페이카 더."], ["See what a lot of money!”", "봐요, 이게 얼마나 많은 돈인지!\""], ["he held out his shaking hand to Zametov with notes in it.", "그는 떨리는 손에 지폐를 쥐고 자메토프에게 내밀었다."], ["“Red notes and blue, twenty-five roubles.", "\"빨간 지폐에 파란 지폐, 이십오 루블이오."], ["Where did I get them?", "이걸 내가 어디서 났겠소?"], ["And where did my new clothes come from?", "그리고 이 새 옷은 어디서 났겠소?"], ["You know I had not a copeck.", "내가 한 푼도 없었던 거 당신도 알잖소."], ["You’ve cross-examined my landlady, I’ll be bound....", "분명 우리 집주인 여자도 캐물어 봤겠지……."], ["Well, that’s enough!", "자, 이제 됐소!"], ["_Assez causé!_ Till we meet again!”", "Assez causé*! 또 봅시다!\""]], [["He went out, trembling all over from a sort of wild hysterical sensation, in which there was an element of insufferable rapture.", "그는 일종의 광적인 히스테리 감각에 온몸을 떨며 밖으로 나갔는데, 그 속에는 견딜 수 없는 황홀감의 요소도 섞여 있었다."], ["Yet he was gloomy and terribly tired.", "그러면서도 그는 음울하고 끔찍하게 지쳐 있었다."], ["His face was twisted as after a fit.", "그의 얼굴은 발작이 지난 뒤처럼 일그러져 있었다."], ["His fatigue increased rapidly.", "피로가 급속히 더해 갔다."], ["Any shock, any irritating sensation stimulated and revived his energies at once, but his strength failed as quickly when the stimulus was removed.", "어떤 충격이든, 어떤 자극적인 감각이든 그의 기력을 단번에 자극하고 되살렸지만, 그 자극이 사라지면 힘도 그만큼 빠르게 빠져나갔다."]], [["Zametov, left alone, sat for a long time in the same place, plunged in thought.", "홀로 남겨진 자메토프는 오랫동안 같은 자리에 앉아 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Raskolnikov had unwittingly worked a revolution in his brain on a certain point and had made up his mind for him conclusively.", "라스콜니코프는 어떤 한 가지 점에 대해 자기도 모르게 그의 머릿속에 일대 변혁을 일으켰고, 그를 대신해 결정적으로 마음을 정해 준 셈이었다."]], [["“Ilya Petrovitch is a blockhead,”", "\"일리야 페트로비치는 멍청이야.\""], ["he decided.", "그는 결론지었다."]], [["Raskolnikov had hardly opened the door of the restaurant when he stumbled against Razumihin on the steps.", "라스콜니코프가 음식점 문을 열기가 무섭게 그는 계단에서 라주미힌과 부딪쳤다."], ["They did not see each other till they almost knocked against each other.", "둘은 거의 충돌할 뻔할 때까지 서로를 보지 못했다."], ["For a moment they stood looking each other up and down.", "한순간 그들은 서서 서로를 위아래로 훑어보았다."], ["Razumihin was greatly astounded, then anger, real anger gleamed fiercely in his eyes.", "라주미힌은 깜짝 놀랐다가, 이내 분노가, 진짜 분노가 그의 눈에서 사납게 번뜩였다."]], [["“So here you are!”", "\"여기 있었군!\""], ["he shouted at the top of his voice--“you ran away from your bed!", "그가 목청껏 소리쳤다. \"침대에서 도망쳐 나왔단 말이지!"], ["And here I’ve been looking for you under the sofa!", "그런데 난 자넬 소파 밑에서 찾고 있었다고!"], ["We went up to the garret.", "우린 다락방까지 올라갔어."], ["I almost beat Nastasya on your account.", "자네 때문에 나스타시야를 하마터면 두들겨 팰 뻔했네."], ["And here he is after all.", "그런데 결국 여기 있었군."], ["Rodya!", "로쟈!"], ["What is the meaning of it?", "이게 도대체 무슨 영문이야?"], ["Tell me the whole truth!", "죄다 사실대로 말해!"], ["Confess!", "털어놔!"], ["Do you hear?”", "듣고 있나?\""]], [["“It means that I’m sick to death of you all and I want to be alone,”", "\"자네들 모두가 죽도록 지긋지긋해서 혼자 있고 싶다는 뜻이야.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered calmly.", "라스콜니코프가 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["“Alone?", "\"혼자라니?"], ["When you are not able to walk, when your face is as white as a sheet and you are gasping for breath!", "제대로 걷지도 못하고, 얼굴은 백지장처럼 하얗고, 숨도 헐떡이면서! 이 멍청한 것!……."], ["Idiot!... What have you been doing in the Palais de Cristal?", "자넨 팔레 드 크리스탈에서 뭘 하고 있었던 거야?"], ["Own up at once!”", "당장 털어놔!\""]], [["“Let me go!”", "\"날 좀 내버려 둬!\""], ["said Raskolnikov and tried to pass him.", "라스콜니코프는 이렇게 말하며 그를 지나치려 했다."], ["This was too much for Razumihin; he gripped him firmly by the shoulder.", "이건 라주미힌에게 너무한 일이었다. 그는 라스콜니코프의 어깨를 단단히 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Let you go?", "\"널 내버려 두라고?"], ["You dare tell me to let you go?", "감히 나더러 널 내버려 두라는 거야?"], ["Do you know what I’ll do with you directly?", "내가 지금 당장 자네한테 무슨 짓을 할지 알아?"], ["I’ll pick you up, tie you up in a bundle, carry you home under my arm and lock you up!”", "자넬 번쩍 들어 올려서 보따리처럼 꽁꽁 묶어 옆구리에 끼고 집으로 데려가 가둬 버릴 거야!\""]], [["“Listen, Razumihin,”", "\"이봐, 라주미힌.\""], ["Raskolnikov began quietly, apparently calm--“can’t you see that I don’t want your benevolence?", "라스콜니코프가 겉보기엔 차분하게, 조용히 입을 열었다. \"내가 자네 호의를 원하지 않는다는 게 안 보이나?"], ["A strange desire you have to shower benefits on a man who...", "자기를 저주하는 사람, 사실 그걸 짐스럽게 여기는 사람한테……"], ["curses them, who feels them a burden in fact!", "은혜를 퍼붓고 싶어 하다니, 참 이상한 욕망이로군!"], ["Why did you seek me out at the beginning of my illness?", "내 병이 시작될 때 자넨 왜 날 찾아냈나?"], ["Maybe I was very glad to die.", "어쩌면 난 죽고 싶어서 무척 기뻤는지도 몰라."], ["Didn’t I tell you plainly enough to-day that you were torturing me, that I was...", "오늘 내가 충분히 분명하게 말하지 않았나, 자네가 날 괴롭히고 있다고, 자네가……"], ["sick of you!", "지긋지긋하다고!"], ["You seem to want to torture people!", "자넨 사람들을 괴롭히고 싶어 안달이 난 것 같아!"], ["I assure you that all that is seriously hindering my recovery, because it’s continually irritating me.", "단언컨대 그 모든 게 내 회복을 심각하게 방해하고 있어, 끊임없이 날 자극하니까 말이야."], ["You saw Zossimov went away just now to avoid irritating me.", "봤지, 조시모프가 방금 날 자극하지 않으려고 가 버린 거."], ["You leave me alone too, for goodness’ sake!", "자네도 제발 좀 날 내버려 둬!"], ["What right have you, indeed, to keep me by force?", "도대체 무슨 권리로 날 억지로 붙들어 두는 거야?"], ["Don’t you see that I am in possession of all my faculties now?", "내가 지금 온전히 제정신이라는 게 안 보여?"], ["How, how can I persuade you not to persecute me with your kindness?", "어떻게, 어떻게 해야 자네 친절로 날 괴롭히지 말라고 설득할 수 있겠나?"], ["I may be ungrateful, I may be mean, only let me be, for God’s sake, let me be!", "내가 배은망덕할 수도 있지, 내가 비열할 수도 있어, 그러니 제발 날 좀 내버려 둬, 제발!"], ["Let me be, let me be!”", "날 내버려 둬, 날 내버려 두라고!\""]], [["He began calmly, gloating beforehand over the venomous phrases he was about to utter, but finished, panting for breath, in a frenzy, as he had been with Luzhin.", "그는 차분하게, 자기가 막 내뱉으려는 독설을 미리 음미하며 입을 열었지만, 끝에 가서는 루진과 그랬던 것처럼 숨을 헐떡이며 광란 속에서 말을 맺었다."]], [["Razumihin stood a moment, thought and let his hand drop.", "라주미힌은 잠시 서서 생각하다가, 손을 떨구었다."]], [["“Well, go to hell then,”", "\"좋아, 그럼 지옥에나 가 버려.\""], ["he said gently and thoughtfully.", "그가 부드럽고 사려 깊게 말했다."], ["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐.\""], ["he roared, as Raskolnikov was about to move.", "라스콜니코프가 막 발을 떼려 하자 그가 고함쳤다."], ["“Listen to me.", "\"내 말 좀 들어."], ["Let me tell you, that you are all a set of babbling, posing idiots!", "한마디 하겠는데, 자네들은 죄다 떠들어 대고 거드름이나 피우는 바보들의 무리야!"], ["If you’ve any little trouble you brood over it like a hen over an egg.", "무슨 사소한 고민거리라도 생기면 암탉이 알을 품듯 그걸 끼고 끙끙대지."], ["And you are plagiarists even in that!", "그것마저도 자넨 남의 걸 표절한 거고!"], ["There isn’t a sign of independent life in you!", "자네 안엔 독자적인 삶의 기미라곤 한 점도 없어!"], ["You are made of spermaceti ointment and you’ve lymph in your veins instead of blood.", "자넨 경뇌유(鯨腦油) 연고로 만들어졌고, 혈관엔 피 대신 림프액이 흐른단 말이야!"], ["I don’t believe in anyone of you!", "난 자네들 중 누구도 안 믿어!"], ["In any circumstances the first thing for all of you is to be unlike a human being!", "어떤 상황에서든 자네들 모두에게 가장 중요한 건 인간답지 않게 구는 거야!"], ["Stop!”", "잠깐!\""], ["he cried with redoubled fury, noticing that Raskolnikov was again making a movement--“hear me out!", "라스콜니코프가 다시 움직이려는 것을 알아채고 그가 곱절의 분노로 외쳤다. \"내 말 끝까지 들어!"], ["You know I’m having a house-warming this evening, I dare say they’ve arrived by now, but I left my uncle there--I just ran in--to receive the guests.", "알다시피 오늘 저녁에 내가 집들이를 하잖아, 손님들이 지금쯤 다 왔을 게야, 한데 난 삼촌더러 거기서 손님을 맞으라고 하고는 — 잠깐 뛰쳐나온 거라고."], ["And if you weren’t a fool, a common fool, a perfect fool, if you were an original instead of a translation...", "그런데 자네가 바보가 아니라면, 흔해 빠진 바보, 완전한 바보가 아니라면, 자네가 번역이 아니라 원작이라면……."], ["you see, Rodya, I recognise you’re a clever fellow, but you’re a fool!--and if you weren’t a fool you’d come round to me this evening instead of wearing out your boots in the street!", "보게, 로쟈, 난 자네가 영리한 친구라는 걸 인정해, 하지만 자넨 바보야! — 자네가 바보가 아니라면 거리에서 신발이나 닳게 할 게 아니라 오늘 저녁 내 집에 들렀을 거야!"], ["Since you have gone out, there’s no help for it!", "이왕 나왔으니 어쩔 수 없지!"], ["I’d give you a snug easy chair, my landlady has one...", "자네한테 아늑하고 편안한 안락의자를 내주겠네, 우리 집주인 여자한테 하나 있거든……."], ["a cup of tea, company....", "차 한잔에, 친구들도 있고……."], ["Or you could lie on the sofa--any way you would be with us....", "아니면 소파에 누워도 돼 — 어쨌든 우리랑 함께 있는 거지……."], ["Zossimov will be there too.", "조시모프도 거기 올 거야."], ["Will you come?” “No.”", "올 텐가?\""]], [["“R-rubbish!”", "\"안 가.\""], ["Razumihin shouted, out of patience.", "\"허, 헛소리!\" 라주미힌이 참다못해 소리쳤다."], ["“How do you know?", "\"그걸 자네가 어떻게 알아?"], ["You can’t answer for yourself!", "자넨 자기 자신도 장담 못 해!"], ["You don’t know anything about it....", "자넨 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라……."], ["Thousands of times I’ve fought tooth and nail with people and run back to them afterwards....", "나도 수천 번이나 사람들이랑 죽기 살기로 싸우고선 나중에 다시 그들한테 달려갔다고……."], ["One feels ashamed and goes back to a man!", "부끄러워져서 사람한테 다시 돌아가게 되는 거야!"], ["So remember, Potchinkov’s house on the third storey....”", "그러니 기억해 둬, 포친코프의 집 삼 층이야…….\""]], [["“Why, Mr. Razumihin, I do believe you’d let anybody beat you from sheer benevolence.”", "\"이봐, 라주미힌, 자넨 순전히 선의 때문에 누구한테든 얻어맞고도 가만히 있을 사람이야.\""]], [["“Beat?", "\"얻어맞아?"], ["Whom? Me?", "누구한테?"], ["I’d twist his nose off at the mere idea!", "내가? 그런 생각만 해도 그놈 코를 비틀어 떼어 버릴걸!"], ["Potchinkov’s house, 47, Babushkin’s flat....”", "포친코프의 집, 47번지, 바부시킨의 셋방이야…….\""]], [["“I shall not come, Razumihin.”", "\"안 갈 거야, 라주미힌.\""], ["Raskolnikov turned and walked away.", "라스콜니코프는 돌아서서 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["“I bet you will,”", "\"올 거라고 장담하네.\""], ["Razumihin shouted after him.", "라주미힌이 그의 뒤에다 대고 소리쳤다."], ["“I refuse to know you if you don’t!", "\"안 오면 자넬 아는 척도 안 할 테야!"], ["Stay, hey, is Zametov in there?”", "잠깐, 어이, 자메토프가 거기 안에 있나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"있어.\""]], [["“Did you see him?”", "\"그를 봤나?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"봤어.\""]], [["“Talked to him?”", "\"얘기도 했고?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"했어.\""]], [["“What about?", "\"무슨 얘길?"], ["Confound you, don’t tell me then.", "에이, 빌어먹을, 그럼 말 안 해도 돼."], ["Potchinkov’s house, 47, Babushkin’s flat, remember!”", "포친코프의 집, 47번지, 바부시킨의 셋방, 기억해!\""]], [["Raskolnikov walked on and turned the corner into Sadovy Street.", "라스콜니코프는 걸음을 옮겨 모퉁이를 돌아 사도바야 거리로 들어섰다."], ["Razumihin looked after him thoughtfully.", "라주미힌은 생각에 잠겨 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았다."], ["Then with a wave of his hand he went into the house but stopped short of the stairs.", "그러더니 손을 한번 내젓고는 집 안으로 들어갔으나, 계단 앞에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Confound it,”", "\"빌어먹을.\""], ["he went on almost aloud.", "그는 거의 소리 내어 말을 이었다."], ["“He talked sensibly but yet...", "\"녀석이 분별 있게 말하긴 했지만, 그래도……."], ["I am a fool!", "난 바보야!"], ["As if madmen didn’t talk sensibly!", "미친 사람이라고 분별 있게 말 못 하란 법 있나!"], ["And this was just what Zossimov seemed afraid of.”", "그리고 바로 이걸 조시모프가 두려워하는 것 같았는데.\""], ["He struck his finger on his forehead.", "그는 손가락으로 자기 이마를 탁 쳤다."], ["“What if... how could I let him go off alone?", "\"만일……, 어떻게 녀석을 혼자 가게 내버려 둔 거지?"], ["He may drown himself....", "녀석이 물에 빠져 죽을지도 몰라……."], ["Ach, what a blunder!", "아, 이런 큰 실수를!"], ["I can’t.”", "안 되겠어.\""], ["And he ran back to overtake Raskolnikov, but there was no trace of him.", "그러고는 라스콜니코프를 따라잡으려 되돌아 뛰어갔지만, 그의 흔적은 어디에도 없었다."], ["With a curse he returned with rapid steps to the Palais de Cristal to question Zametov.", "그는 욕설을 내뱉으며 빠른 걸음으로 자메토프에게 물어보려고 팔레 드 크리스탈로 되돌아갔다."]], [["Raskolnikov walked straight to X---- Bridge, stood in the middle, and leaning both elbows on the rail stared into the distance.", "라스콜니코프는 곧장 X 다리로 걸어가 한복판에 서서, 두 팔꿈치를 난간에 괴고 먼 곳을 응시했다."], ["On parting with Razumihin, he felt so much weaker that he could scarcely reach this place.", "라주미힌과 헤어지면서 그는 어찌나 기운이 빠졌는지 이곳까지 겨우 다다랐을 정도였다."], ["He longed to sit or lie down somewhere in the street.", "그는 거리 어딘가에 앉거나 눕고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["Bending over the water, he gazed mechanically at the last pink flush of the sunset, at the row of houses growing dark in the gathering twilight, at one distant attic window on the left bank, flashing as though on fire in the last rays of the setting sun, at the darkening water of the canal, and the water seemed to catch his attention.", "물 위로 몸을 숙인 채, 그는 노을의 마지막 분홍빛 잔영을, 짙어 가는 어스름 속에 어두워져 가는 줄지어 선 집들을, 지는 해의 마지막 햇살에 불붙은 듯 번쩍이는 왼편 강기슭의 멀리 있는 다락방 창문 하나를, 그리고 어두워져 가는 운하의 물을 무심히 바라보았는데, 그 물이 그의 시선을 사로잡는 듯했다."], ["At last red circles flashed before his eyes, the houses seemed moving, the passers-by, the canal banks, the carriages, all danced before his eyes.", "마침내 그의 눈앞에 붉은 원들이 어른거렸고, 집들이 움직이는 것 같았으며, 지나가는 사람들도, 운하의 둑도, 마차들도 죄다 그의 눈앞에서 춤추는 것 같았다."], ["Suddenly he started, saved again perhaps from swooning by an uncanny and hideous sight.", "갑자기 그는 흠칫했는데, 어쩌면 어떤 섬뜩하고 흉측한 광경 덕분에 다시금 실신을 면한 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["He became aware of someone standing on the right side of him; he looked and saw a tall woman with a kerchief on her head, with a long, yellow, wasted face and red sunken eyes.", "그는 자기 오른쪽에 누군가 서 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 바라보니, 머리에 수건을 두른 키 큰 여자였는데, 길쭉하고 누렇게 뜬 수척한 얼굴에 붉게 충혈된 움푹한 눈을 하고 있었다."], ["She was looking straight at him, but obviously she saw nothing and recognised no one.", "그녀는 그를 똑바로 바라보고 있었지만, 분명 아무것도 보지 못하고 누구도 알아보지 못했다."], ["Suddenly she leaned her right hand on the parapet, lifted her right leg over the railing, then her left and threw herself into the canal.", "갑자기 그녀는 오른손으로 난간을 짚더니 오른쪽 다리를, 이어 왼쪽 다리를 난간 위로 들어 올려, 운하 속으로 몸을 던졌다."], ["The filthy water parted and swallowed up its victim for a moment, but an instant later the drowning woman floated to the surface, moving slowly with the current, her head and legs in the water, her skirt inflated like a balloon over her back.", "더러운 물이 갈라지며 한순간 제물을 집어삼켰으나, 다음 순간 물에 빠진 여자가 수면 위로 떠올라, 머리와 두 다리는 물속에 잠긴 채 치맛자락이 등 위로 풍선처럼 부풀어 오른 채 물살을 따라 천천히 떠내려갔다."]], [["“A woman drowning!", "\"여자가 빠졌다!"], ["A woman drowning!”", "여자가 빠졌어!\""], ["shouted dozens of voices; people ran up, both banks were thronged with spectators, on the bridge people crowded about Raskolnikov, pressing up behind him.", "수십 개의 목소리가 외쳤다. 사람들이 달려왔고, 양쪽 기슭이 구경꾼들로 빽빽이 들어찼으며, 다리 위에서는 사람들이 라스콜니코프 주위로 몰려들어 그의 등 뒤에서 밀어 댔다."]], [["“Mercy on it!", "\"맙소사!"], ["it’s our Afrosinya!”", "우리 아프로시니야잖아!\""], ["a woman cried tearfully close by. “Mercy!", "바로 곁에서 한 여자가 눈물 어린 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["save her!", "\"맙소사!"], ["kind people, pull her out!”", "저 사람 좀 구해 줘요! 착한 분들, 끌어내 줘요!\""]], [["“A boat, a boat!”", "\"배다, 배!\""], ["was shouted in the crowd.", "군중 속에서 누군가 외쳤다."], ["But there was no need of a boat; a policeman ran down the steps to the canal, threw off his great coat and his boots and rushed into the water.", "하지만 배는 필요 없었다. 경찰관 하나가 운하로 내려가는 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 외투와 장화를 벗어 던지고 물속으로 뛰어들었다."], ["It was easy to reach her: she floated within a couple of yards from the steps, he caught hold of her clothes with his right hand and with his left seized a pole which a comrade held out to him; the drowning woman was pulled out at once.", "그녀에게 다가가기는 쉬웠다. 그녀가 계단에서 두어 야드 안쪽에 떠 있었으므로, 그는 오른손으로 그녀의 옷을 붙잡고 왼손으로는 동료가 내민 막대를 움켜잡았다."], ["They laid her on the granite pavement of the embankment.", "물에 빠진 여자는 곧바로 끌어올려졌다."], ["She soon recovered consciousness, raised her head, sat up and began sneezing and coughing, stupidly wiping her wet dress with her hands.", "사람들은 그녀를 둑의 화강암 포석 위에 눕혔다. 그녀는 곧 의식을 되찾아 고개를 들고 일어나 앉더니, 재채기와 기침을 하며 멍하니 손으로 젖은 옷을 닦기 시작했다."], ["She said nothing.", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“She’s drunk herself out of her senses,”", "\"술을 퍼마시고 제정신이 아니었던 게야.\""], ["the same woman’s voice wailed at her side.", "아까 그 여자의 목소리가 그녀 곁에서 한탄했다."], ["“Out of her senses.", "\"제정신이 아니었어."], ["The other day she tried to hang herself, we cut her down.", "며칠 전에도 목을 매려는 걸 우리가 끊어 내렸지."], ["I ran out to the shop just now, left my little girl to look after her--and here she’s in trouble again!", "방금 내가 가게에 잠깐 다녀오느라 우리 딸애더러 저 사람을 봐 달라고 했는데 — 그새 또 이 사달이 났네!"], ["A neighbour, gentleman, a neighbour, we live close by, the second house from the end, see yonder....”", "이웃이에요, 나리, 이웃이라고요, 바로 근처에 살아요, 저 끝에서 두 번째 집인데, 저기 보세요…….\""]], [["The crowd broke up.", "군중이 흩어졌다."], ["The police still remained round the woman, someone mentioned the police station....", "경찰관들은 여전히 그 여자 곁에 남아 있었고, 누군가가 경찰서를 입에 올렸다……."], ["Raskolnikov looked on with a strange sensation of indifference and apathy.", "라스콜니코프는 기이한 무관심과 냉담의 감각에 휩싸인 채 바라보았다."], ["He felt disgusted.", "그는 역겨움을 느꼈다."], ["“No, that’s loathsome... water...", "'아니야, 저건 혐오스러워……, 물은……, 그걸로는 안 돼.'"], ["it’s not good enough,”", "그는 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["he muttered to himself.", "'아무 소용 없어.'"], ["“Nothing will come of it,”", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["he added, “no use to wait.", "'기다려 봤자 헛수고지."], ["What about the police office...?", "경찰서는 어떨까……?"], ["And why isn’t Zametov at the police office?", "그런데 자메토프는 왜 경찰서에 없는 거지?"], ["The police office is open till ten o’clock....”", "경찰서는 열 시까지 문을 여는데…….'"], ["He turned his back to the railing and looked about him.", "그는 난간에 등을 돌리고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Very well then!”", "\"그래, 좋아!\""], ["he said resolutely; he moved from the bridge and walked in the direction of the police office.", "그는 단호하게 말했다. 그는 다리에서 발길을 옮겨 경찰서 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["His heart felt hollow and empty.", "그의 가슴은 텅 비고 공허하게 느껴졌다."], ["He did not want to think.", "그는 아무 생각도 하고 싶지 않았다."], ["Even his depression had passed, there was not a trace now of the energy with which he had set out “to make an end of it all.”", "우울함마저 가시고, \"이 모든 것을 끝장내려고\" 길을 나섰을 때의 그 에너지는 이제 흔적조차 없었다."], ["Complete apathy had succeeded to it.", "그 자리에 완전한 냉담이 들어선 것이다."]], [["“Well, it’s a way out of it,”", "'뭐, 이것도 하나의 출구지.'"], ["he thought, walking slowly and listlessly along the canal bank.", "그는 운하 둑을 따라 천천히 맥없이 걸으며 생각했다."], ["“Anyway, I’ll make an end, for I want to....", "'어쨌든 끝을 내겠어, 내가 그러고 싶으니까……."], ["But is it a way out?", "하지만 이게 출구이긴 한가?"], ["What does it matter!", "그게 무슨 상관이람!"], ["There’ll be the square yard of space--ha!", "한 평짜리 공간이 있겠지 — 하! 하지만 이 무슨 끝이람!"], ["But what an end!", "정말 이게 끝인가?"], ["Is it really the end?", "그들에게 말할까, 말까?"], ["Shall I tell them or not? Ah...", "아……, 빌어먹을!"], ["damn! How tired I am!", "어쩜 이렇게 피곤할까!"], ["If I could find somewhere to sit or lie down soon!", "어디든 앉거나 누울 데를 빨리 찾을 수만 있다면!"], ["What I am most ashamed of is its being so stupid.", "가장 부끄러운 건 이게 이토록 어리석다는 거야."], ["But I don’t care about that either!", "하지만 그것도 아무래도 상관없어!"], ["What idiotic ideas come into one’s head.”", "별 멍청한 생각이 다 머릿속에 떠오르는군.'"]], [["To reach the police office he had to go straight forward and take the second turning to the left.", "경찰서에 가려면 곧장 앞으로 가다가 두 번째 길에서 왼쪽으로 꺾어야 했다."], ["It was only a few paces away.", "겨우 몇 걸음 거리였다."], ["But at the first turning he stopped and, after a minute’s thought, turned into a side street and went two streets out of his way, possibly without any object, or possibly to delay a minute and gain time.", "하지만 첫 번째 모퉁이에서 그는 멈춰 섰고, 잠시 생각하더니, 옆길로 접어들어 가던 길에서 두 거리나 벗어났는데, 어쩌면 아무 목적도 없이, 아니 어쩌면 잠깐이라도 미루며 시간을 벌려는 것이었는지도 몰랐다. 그는 땅을 바라보며 걸었다."], ["He walked, looking at the ground; suddenly someone seemed to whisper in his ear; he lifted his head and saw that he was standing at the very gate of _the_ house.", "갑자기 누군가 그의 귀에 속삭이는 것 같았다. 그가 고개를 들어 보니, 자신이 바로 그 집의 대문 앞에 서 있는 것이었다."], ["He had not passed it, he had not been near it since _that_ evening.", "그날 저녁 이후로 그는 그 앞을 지난 적도, 그 근처에 간 적도 없었다."], ["An overwhelming, unaccountable prompting drew him on.", "압도적이고 설명할 길 없는 충동이 그를 끌어당겼다."], ["He went into the house, passed through the gateway, then into the first entrance on the right, and began mounting the familiar staircase to the fourth storey.", "그는 집 안으로 들어가 대문을 지나, 오른쪽 첫 번째 입구로 들어서서, 사 층으로 이어지는 낯익은 계단을 오르기 시작했다."], ["The narrow, steep staircase was very dark.", "좁고 가파른 계단은 무척 어두웠다."], ["He stopped at each landing and looked round him with curiosity; on the first landing the framework of the window had been taken out.", "그는 층계참마다 멈춰 서서 호기심 어린 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 첫 번째 층계참에서는 창틀이 뜯겨 나가 있었다."], ["“That wasn’t so then,”", "'그땐 이렇지 않았는데.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["Here was the flat on the second storey where Nikolay and Dmitri had been working.", "여기 이 층에는 니콜라이와 드미트리가 일하고 있던 셋집이 있었다."], ["“It’s shut up and the door newly painted.", "'문이 잠겨 있고 새로 칠을 했군."], ["So it’s to let.”", "세를 놓으려는 거야.'"], ["Then the third storey and the fourth.", "그다음 삼 층, 그리고 사 층."], ["“Here!”", "'여기다!'"], ["He was perplexed to find the door of the flat wide open.", "그는 그 셋집 문이 활짝 열려 있는 것을 보고 당황했다."], ["There were men there, he could hear voices; he had not expected that.", "거기엔 사람들이 있었다. 말소리가 들렸다."], ["After brief hesitation he mounted the last stairs and went into the flat.", "그는 그것을 예상하지 못했다. 잠시 망설인 뒤 그는 마지막 계단을 올라 셋집 안으로 들어섰다."], ["It, too, was being done up; there were workmen in it.", "그곳 역시 수리 중이었다. 안에 일꾼들이 있었다."], ["This seemed to amaze him; he somehow fancied that he would find everything as he left it, even perhaps the corpses in the same places on the floor.", "이것이 그를 놀라게 한 듯했다. 그는 어쩐지 모든 것이 자기가 떠났을 때 그대로일 거라고, 어쩌면 시체들마저 바닥의 그 자리에 그대로 있을 거라고 상상했던 것이다."], ["And now, bare walls, no furniture; it seemed strange.", "그런데 이제 벽은 휑하니 비고 가구도 없으니, 기이하게 느껴졌다."], ["He walked to the window and sat down on the window-sill.", "그는 창가로 걸어가 창턱에 걸터앉았다."], ["There were two workmen, both young fellows, but one much younger than the other.", "일꾼은 둘이었는데, 둘 다 젊은 친구였지만 하나가 다른 하나보다 훨씬 어렸다."], ["They were papering the walls with a new white paper covered with lilac flowers, instead of the old, dirty, yellow one.", "그들은 낡고 더러운 누런 벽지 대신, 라일락 꽃무늬가 그려진 새 흰 벽지를 바르고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov for some reason felt horribly annoyed by this.", "라스콜니코프는 어쩐 일인지 이것이 끔찍하게 거슬렸다."], ["He looked at the new paper with dislike, as though he felt sorry to have it all so changed.", "그는 모든 것이 이렇게 죄다 바뀐 것이 안타깝기라도 한 듯, 못마땅한 눈으로 새 벽지를 바라보았다."], ["The workmen had obviously stayed beyond their time and now they were hurriedly rolling up their paper and getting ready to go home.", "일꾼들은 분명 정해진 시간을 넘기고 있었던 터라, 이제 서둘러 벽지를 둘둘 말아 집에 갈 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["They took no notice of Raskolnikov’s coming in; they were talking.", "그들은 라스콜니코프가 들어온 것에 아무 관심도 두지 않은 채 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov folded his arms and listened.", "라스콜니코프는 팔짱을 끼고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“She comes to me in the morning,”", "\"그 여자가 아침에 나한테 오는데,\""], ["said the elder to the younger, “very early, all dressed up.", "나이 든 쪽이 어린 쪽에게 말했다. \"아주 일찍, 잔뜩 차려입고 말이야."], ["‘Why are you preening and prinking?’ says I.", "'뭘 그렇게 빼입고 멋을 부렸어?'"], ["‘I am ready to do anything to please you, Tit Vassilitch!’ That’s a way of going on!", "하고 내가 그러니까, '당신 마음에 들기 위해서라면 뭐든 할 수 있어요, 티트 바실리치!' 이러는 거야."], ["And she dressed up like a regular fashion book!”", "이건 뭐 한참을 그러는 식이지! 게다가 영락없이 패션 책에서 막 튀어나온 것처럼 빼입었더라니까!\""]], [["“And what is a fashion book?”", "\"그래, 패션 책이 뭔데?\""], ["the younger one asked.", "어린 쪽이 물었다."], ["He obviously regarded the other as an authority.", "그는 분명 상대를 권위자로 여기고 있었다."]], [["“A fashion book is a lot of pictures, coloured, and they come to the tailors here every Saturday, by post from abroad, to show folks how to dress, the male sex as well as the female.", "\"패션 책이란 건 그림이 잔뜩 든 거야, 색깔도 들어가 있고, 매주 토요일마다 외국에서 우편으로 여기 재단사들한테 오지, 사람들한테 어떻게 차려입는지 보여 주려고 말이야, 남자고 여자고."], ["They’re pictures.", "그게 그림이라고."], ["The gentlemen are generally wearing fur coats and for the ladies’ fluffles, they’re beyond anything you can fancy.”", "신사들은 대개 모피 외투를 입고 있고, 부인네들 옷의 그 보드라운 장식이란 건, 자네가 상상도 못 할 정도야.\""]], [["“There’s nothing you can’t find in Petersburg,”", "\"페테르부르크엔 없는 게 없어,\""], ["the younger cried enthusiastically, “except father and mother, there’s everything!”", "어린 쪽이 신이 나서 외쳤다. \"아버지하고 어머니만 빼고는 뭐든 다 있다니까!\""]], [["“Except them, there’s everything to be found, my boy,”", "\"그것들만 빼고는 뭐든 다 찾을 수 있지, 이 친구야.\""], ["the elder declared sententiously.", "나이 든 쪽이 점잔을 빼며 단언했다."]], [["Raskolnikov got up and walked into the other room where the strong box, the bed, and the chest of drawers had been; the room seemed to him very tiny without furniture in it.", "라스콜니코프는 일어나 다른 방으로 걸어 들어갔는데, 거기엔 금고와 침대, 서랍장이 놓여 있던 곳이었다. 가구가 없으니 그 방이 그에게는 무척 작아 보였다."], ["The paper was the same; the paper in the corner showed where the case of ikons had stood.", "벽지는 그대로였다. 한쪽 구석의 벽지는 성상 함이 놓여 있던 자리를 보여 주고 있었다."], ["He looked at it and went to the window.", "그는 그것을 바라보다가 창가로 갔다."], ["The elder workman looked at him askance.", "나이 든 일꾼이 그를 곁눈질로 흘끔거렸다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭐가 필요하슈?\""], ["he asked suddenly.", "그가 갑자기 물었다."]], [["Instead of answering Raskolnikov went into the passage and pulled the bell.", "라스콜니코프는 대답 대신 통로로 가서 초인종 줄을 잡아당겼다."], ["The same bell, the same cracked note.", "똑같은 초인종, 똑같이 갈라진 소리였다."], ["He rang it a second and a third time; he listened and remembered.", "그는 두 번, 세 번 그것을 울렸다. 그는 귀를 기울이며 기억을 떠올렸다."], ["The hideous and agonisingly fearful sensation he had felt then began to come back more and more vividly.", "그때 느꼈던 그 흉측하고 견딜 수 없이 두려운 감각이 점점 더 생생하게 되살아나기 시작했다."], ["He shuddered at every ring and it gave him more and more satisfaction.", "그는 초인종이 울릴 때마다 몸서리쳤고, 그것이 점점 더 큰 만족감을 주었다."]], [["“Well, what do you want?", "\"아니, 뭐가 필요하냐고?"], ["Who are you?”", "댁은 누구슈?\""], ["the workman shouted, going out to him.", "일꾼이 그에게 다가서며 소리쳤다."], ["Raskolnikov went inside again.", "라스콜니코프는 다시 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["“I want to take a flat,”", "\"셋방을 하나 얻으려고요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I am looking round.”", "\"둘러보는 중입니다.\""]], [["“It’s not the time to look at rooms at night!", "\"밤에 방을 보러 다닐 때가 아니지!"], ["and you ought to come up with the porter.”", "그리고 문지기랑 같이 와야 한단 말이오.\""]], [["“The floors have been washed, will they be painted?”", "\"바닥은 다 씻어 냈고, 칠을 할 건가요?\""], ["Raskolnikov went on.", "라스콜니코프가 계속 말했다."], ["“Is there no blood?”", "\"피는 없나요?\""]], [["“What blood?”", "\"무슨 피?\""]], [["“Why, the old woman and her sister were murdered here.", "\"아니, 여기서 노파랑 그 여동생이 살해당했잖소."], ["There was a perfect pool there.”", "저기 피가 흥건히 고여 있었는데.\""]], [["“But who are you?”", "\"한데 댁은 누구슈?\""], ["the workman cried, uneasy.", "일꾼이 불안한 듯 외쳤다."]], [["“Who am I?” “Yes.”", "\"내가 누구냐고?\""]], [["“You want to know?", "\"그렇소.\" \"알고 싶소?"], ["Come to the police station, I’ll tell you.”", "경찰서로 갑시다, 거기서 말해 주리다.\""]], [["The workmen looked at him in amazement.", "일꾼들은 어리둥절한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“It’s time for us to go, we are late.", "\"우린 갈 시간이오, 늦었어."], ["Come along, Alyoshka.", "가자, 알료시카."], ["We must lock up,”", "문을 잠가야겠어.\""], ["said the elder workman.", "나이 든 일꾼이 말했다."]], [["“Very well, come along,”", "\"좋소, 갑시다.\""], ["said Raskolnikov indifferently, and going out first, he went slowly downstairs.", "라스콜니코프가 무심하게 말하고는, 먼저 나가 천천히 계단을 내려갔다."], ["“Hey, porter,”", "\"어이, 문지기.\""], ["he cried in the gateway.", "그가 대문께에서 소리쳤다."]], [["At the entrance several people were standing, staring at the passers-by; the two porters, a peasant woman, a man in a long coat and a few others.", "입구에는 몇 사람이 서서 지나가는 행인들을 빤히 바라보고 있었다. 문지기 둘, 농가의 아낙 하나, 긴 외투를 걸친 사내 하나, 그리고 몇몇이 더 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov went straight up to them.", "라스콜니코프는 곧장 그들에게 다가갔다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭐가 필요하슈?\""], ["asked one of the porters.", "문지기 중 하나가 물었다."]], [["“Have you been to the police office?”", "\"경찰서에 다녀왔소?\""]], [["“I’ve just been there.", "\"방금 다녀왔는데."], ["What do you want?”", "뭐가 필요하냐니까?\""]], [["“Is it open?”", "\"문은 열려 있소?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"그럼요.\""]], [["“Is the assistant there?”", "\"담당자가 거기 있던가요?\""]], [["“He was there for a time.", "\"잠깐 있었지요."], ["What do you want?”", "뭐가 필요하슈?\""]], [["Raskolnikov made no reply, but stood beside them lost in thought.", "라스콜니코프는 아무 대답도 않고 그들 곁에 서서 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“He’s been to look at the flat,”", "\"이 양반이 셋방을 보러 왔었지요.\""], ["said the elder workman, coming forward.", "나이 든 일꾼이 앞으로 나서며 말했다."]], [["“Which flat?”", "\"어느 셋방?\""]], [["“Where we are at work.", "\"우리가 일하던 데요. '왜 피를 다 씻어 냈소?'"], ["‘Why have you washed away the blood?’ says he.", "하고 이 양반이 그러더라고요. '여기서 살인이 있었는데, 내가 이 방을 얻으러 왔다.'"], ["‘There has been a murder here,’ says he, ‘and I’ve come to take it.’ And he began ringing at the bell, all but broke it.", "이러면서요. 그러더니 초인종을 울려 대지 뭡니까, 하마터면 부숴 버릴 뻔했어요."], ["‘Come to the police station,’ says he.", "'경찰서로 갑시다,' 하고 그러더라고요."], ["‘I’ll tell you everything there.’ He wouldn’t leave us.”", "'거기서 죄다 말해 주겠다.' 우리한테서 떨어지질 않더라니까요.\""]], [["The porter looked at Raskolnikov, frowning and perplexed.", "문지기는 얼굴을 찌푸리고 어리둥절한 채 라스콜니코프를 바라보았다."]], [["“Who are you?”", "\"댁은 누구슈?\""], ["he shouted as impressively as he could.", "그가 가능한 한 위압적으로 소리쳤다."]], [["“I am Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov, formerly a student, I live in Shil’s house, not far from here, flat Number 14, ask the porter, he knows me.”", "\"나는 로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프, 전직 대학생이고, 여기서 멀지 않은 실의 집, 14호 셋방에 살고 있소, 문지기한테 물어보시오, 그가 날 아니까.\""], ["Raskolnikov said all this in a lazy, dreamy voice, not turning round, but looking intently into the darkening street.", "라스콜니코프는 돌아보지도 않고 어두워져 가는 거리를 뚫어지게 바라보며, 나른하고 꿈결 같은 목소리로 이 모든 것을 말했다."]], [["“Why have you been to the flat?”", "\"왜 그 셋방엘 갔던 거요?\""]], [["“To look at it.”", "\"보러 갔지요.\""]], [["“What is there to look at?”", "\"거기 볼 게 뭐가 있다고?\""]], [["“Take him straight to the police station,”", "\"이 작자를 곧장 경찰서로 끌고 갑시다.\""], ["the man in the long coat jerked in abruptly.", "긴 외투를 걸친 사내가 불쑥 끼어들었다."]], [["Raskolnikov looked intently at him over his shoulder and said in the same slow, lazy tones:", "라스콜니코프는 어깨너머로 그를 뚫어지게 바라보더니, 여전히 느리고 나른한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Come along.”", "\"갑시다.\""]], [["“Yes, take him,”", "\"그래요, 데려가요.\""], ["the man went on more confidently.", "사내가 더 자신감 있게 말을 이었다."], ["“Why was he going into _that_, what’s in his mind, eh?”", "\"왜 그런 데를 들어갔겠소, 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있는 거지, 응?\""]], [["“He’s not drunk, but God knows what’s the matter with him,”", "\"술에 취한 건 아닌데, 무슨 영문인지 알 게 뭐람.\""], ["muttered the workman.", "일꾼이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“But what do you want?”", "\"아니, 도대체 뭐가 필요하냐니까?\""], ["the porter shouted again, beginning to get angry in earnest--“Why are you hanging about?”", "문지기가 이제 정말로 화가 나기 시작하며 다시 소리쳤다. \"왜 여기서 얼쩡거리는 거요?\""]], [["“You funk the police station then?”", "\"그래, 당신은 경찰서가 무서워서 못 가는 거요?\""], ["said Raskolnikov jeeringly.", "라스콜니코프가 비웃듯 말했다."]], [["“How funk it?", "\"무섭긴 뭐가 무서워?"], ["Why are you hanging about?”", "왜 여기서 얼쩡거리느냐고?\""]], [["“He’s a rogue!”", "\"저자는 악당이야!\""], ["shouted the peasant woman.", "농가의 아낙이 외쳤다."]], [["“Why waste time talking to him?”", "\"저런 자랑 무슨 시간 낭비요?\""], ["cried the other porter, a huge peasant in a full open coat and with keys on his belt.", "다른 문지기가 외쳤는데, 그는 활짝 풀어 헤친 외투를 걸치고 허리띠에 열쇠를 매단 거구의 농부였다."], ["“Get along!", "\"썩 꺼져!"], ["He is a rogue and no mistake.", "영락없는 악당이야, 틀림없어."], ["Get along!”", "썩 꺼지라고!\""]], [["And seizing Raskolnikov by the shoulder he flung him into the street.", "그러고는 라스콜니코프의 어깨를 움켜쥐고 그를 거리로 내던졌다."], ["He lurched forward, but recovered his footing, looked at the spectators in silence and walked away.", "그는 앞으로 비틀거렸으나 다시 발을 디뎌 균형을 잡고는, 말없이 구경꾼들을 바라보고 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["“Strange man!”", "\"이상한 작자야!\""], ["observed the workman.", "일꾼이 한마디 했다."]], [["“There are strange folks about nowadays,”", "\"요즘엔 이상한 인간들이 다 돌아다닌다니까.\""], ["said the woman.", "아낙이 말했다."]], [["“You should have taken him to the police station all the same,”", "\"그래도 경찰서로 데려갔어야 했는데.\""], ["said the man in the long coat.", "긴 외투를 걸친 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Better have nothing to do with him,”", "\"저런 작자랑은 아예 엮이지 않는 게 상책이지.\""], ["decided the big porter.", "거구의 문지기가 단정 지었다."], ["“A regular rogue!", "\"영락없는 악당이라고!"], ["Just what he wants, you may be sure, but once take him up, you won’t get rid of him....", "분명 노리는 게 있을 거야, 틀림없어, 한데 한번 붙잡았다 하면 떼어 낼 수가 없거든……."], ["We know the sort!”", "우리가 그런 부류를 잘 알지!\""]], [["“Shall I go there or not?”", "'갈까, 말까?'"], ["thought Raskolnikov, standing in the middle of the thoroughfare at the cross-roads, and he looked about him, as though expecting from someone a decisive word.", "라스콜니코프는 네거리 한복판에 서서 생각하며, 마치 누군가에게서 결정적인 한마디를 기다리기라도 하듯 주위를 둘러보았다. 하지만 아무 소리도 들려오지 않았고, 모든 것이 그가 밟고 선 돌멩이들처럼 죽은 듯 고요했으니, 그에게는, 오직 그에게만은 죽어 있었다……."], ["But no sound came, all was dead and silent like the stones on which he walked, dead to him, to him alone....", "별안간 거리 끝, 이백 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 짙어 가는 어스름 속에 그는 군중을 보았고 말소리와 고함을 들었다."], ["All at once at the end of the street, two hundred yards away, in the gathering dusk he saw a crowd and heard talk and shouts.", "군중 한가운데에 마차 한 대가 서 있었다……. 거리 한복판에서 불빛 하나가 어른거렸다."], ["In the middle of the crowd stood a carriage....", "'뭐지?' 라스콜니코프는 오른쪽으로 꺾어 군중에게 다가갔다."], ["A light gleamed in the middle of the street. “What is it?”", "그는 무엇에든 매달리려는 듯했고, 그것이 무엇인지 알아보고는 차갑게 미소 지었으니, 이미 경찰서로 가기로 단단히 마음을 정했고 머지않아 이 모든 것이 끝나리라는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Raskolnikov turned to the right and went up to the crowd.", "* 팔레 드 크리스탈: '수정궁'이라는 뜻의 음식점 이름. * 푸드: 러시아의 무게 단위."], ["He seemed to clutch at everything and smiled coldly when he recognised it, for he had fully made up his mind to go to the police station and knew that it would all soon be over.", "1푸드는 약 16.38킬로그램. * Assez causé: 프랑스어로 \"이야기는 충분하다\"라는 뜻."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["An elegant carriage stood in the middle of the road with a pair of spirited grey horses; there was no one in it, and the coachman had got off his box and stood by; the horses were being held by the bridle....", "길 한복판에 우아한 마차 한 대가 기운 넘치는 잿빛 말 두 필을 매단 채 서 있었다. 마차 안에는 아무도 없었고, 마부는 마부석에서 내려와 곁에 서 있었으며, 말들은 누군가가 고삐를 붙들고 있었다……."], ["A mass of people had gathered round, the police standing in front.", "한 무리의 사람들이 그 주위로 몰려들었고, 그 앞에는 경찰들이 서 있었다."], ["One of them held a lighted lantern which he was turning on something lying close to the wheels.", "그중 하나가 불 켜진 등을 들고 바퀴 가까이에 놓인 무언가를 비추고 있었다."], ["Everyone was talking, shouting, exclaiming; the coachman seemed at a loss and kept repeating:", "모두가 이야기하고, 소리치고, 탄식했다. 마부는 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 같은 말만 되풀이했다."]], [["“What a misfortune!", "\"이런 변고가!"], ["Good Lord, what a misfortune!”", "맙소사, 이런 변고가!\""]], [["Raskolnikov pushed his way in as far as he could, and succeeded at last in seeing the object of the commotion and interest.", "라스콜니코프는 비집고 들어갈 수 있는 데까지 파고들어, 마침내 이 소동과 관심의 대상을 보는 데 성공했다."], ["On the ground a man who had been run over lay apparently unconscious, and covered with blood; he was very badly dressed, but not like a workman.", "땅바닥에는 마차에 치인 한 사내가 의식을 잃은 듯 피투성이가 된 채 누워 있었다. 그는 무척 형편없는 옷차림이었지만, 노동자 같지는 않았다."], ["Blood was flowing from his head and face; his face was crushed, mutilated and disfigured.", "머리와 얼굴에서 피가 흘러내리고 있었고, 얼굴은 으스러지고 짓이겨져 알아볼 수 없게 일그러져 있었다."], ["He was evidently badly injured.", "그가 심하게 다친 것은 분명했다."]], [["“Merciful heaven!”", "\"하느님 맙소사!\""], ["wailed the coachman, “what more could I do?", "마부가 울부짖었다. \"내가 더 어쩔 수 있었겠소?"], ["If I’d been driving fast or had not shouted to him, but I was going quietly, not in a hurry.", "내가 빨리 몰았거나 저 사람한테 소리치지 않았다면 모를까, 난 천천히, 서두르지 않고 가고 있었다고요."], ["Everyone could see I was going along just like everybody else.", "누구나 다 봤다시피 난 다른 사람들처럼 그냥 가고 있었어요."], ["A drunken man can’t walk straight, we all know....", "취한 사람이 똑바로 못 걷는다는 건 다들 알지 않습니까……."], ["I saw him crossing the street, staggering and almost falling.", "저 사람이 비틀거리면서, 거의 쓰러질 듯이 길을 건너는 걸 봤지요."], ["I shouted again and a second and a third time, then I held the horses in, but he fell straight under their feet!", "한 번 소리치고, 두 번, 세 번 소리쳤어요, 그러고는 말을 멈춰 세웠는데, 저 사람이 곧장 말발굽 밑으로 쓰러진 거예요!"], ["Either he did it on purpose or he was very tipsy....", "일부러 그랬거나, 아니면 잔뜩 취했거나……."], ["The horses are young and ready to take fright... they started, he screamed...", "말들은 어려서 잘 놀라는 놈들이라……, 펄쩍 뛰었고, 저 사람이 비명을 질렀고……, 그래서 더 날뛴 거지요."], ["that made them worse. That’s how it happened!”", "그렇게 된 일이에요!\""]], [["“That’s just how it was,”", "\"바로 그렇게 된 일이오.\""], ["a voice in the crowd confirmed.", "군중 속에서 누군가의 목소리가 거들었다."]], [["“He shouted, that’s true, he shouted three times,”", "\"그래요, 저 사람이 소리쳤어, 그건 사실이야, 세 번이나 소리쳤다고.\""], ["another voice declared.", "또 다른 목소리가 단언했다."]], [["“Three times it was, we all heard it,”", "\"세 번이었어요, 우리 다 들었다고.\""], ["shouted a third.", "세 번째 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["But the coachman was not very much distressed and frightened.", "그러나 마부는 그다지 괴로워하거나 겁먹지는 않았다."], ["It was evident that the carriage belonged to a rich and important person who was awaiting it somewhere; the police, of course, were in no little anxiety to avoid upsetting his arrangements.", "그 마차가 어딘가에서 기다리고 있을 부유하고 지체 높은 인물의 것임은 분명했고, 경찰들은 당연히 그분의 일정을 어그러뜨리지 않으려 적잖이 마음을 졸였다."], ["All they had to do was to take the injured man to the police station and the hospital.", "그들이 해야 할 일이라곤 다친 사람을 경찰서와 병원으로 옮기는 것뿐이었다."], ["No one knew his name.", "그의 이름은 아무도 몰랐다."]], [["Meanwhile Raskolnikov had squeezed in and stooped closer over him.", "그러는 동안 라스콜니코프는 비집고 들어가 그 위로 더 가까이 몸을 숙였다."], ["The lantern suddenly lighted up the unfortunate man’s face.", "등불이 불현듯 그 불운한 사내의 얼굴을 환히 비추었다."], ["He recognised him.", "그는 그를 알아보았다."]], [["“I know him! I know him!”", "\"이 사람을 압니다!"], ["he shouted, pushing to the front.", "이 사람을 알아요!\""], ["“It’s a government clerk retired from the service, Marmeladov.", "그는 앞으로 밀고 나가며 외쳤다. \"퇴직한 관리, 마르멜라도프입니다."], ["He lives close by in Kozel’s house....", "바로 근처 코젤의 집에 살아요……."], ["Make haste for a doctor!", "어서 의사를 불러요!"], ["I will pay, see?”", "내가 내겠습니다, 보세요?\""], ["He pulled money out of his pocket and showed it to the policeman.", "그는 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내 경찰에게 보여 주었다."], ["He was in violent agitation.", "그는 격렬하게 동요하고 있었다."]], [["The police were glad that they had found out who the man was.", "경찰들은 그 사내가 누구인지 알아낸 것을 다행으로 여겼다."], ["Raskolnikov gave his own name and address, and, as earnestly as if it had been his father, he besought the police to carry the unconscious Marmeladov to his lodging at once.", "라스콜니코프는 자신의 이름과 주소를 댔고, 마치 친아버지를 위한 일이라도 되는 양 간절하게, 의식을 잃은 마르멜라도프를 당장 그의 집으로 옮겨 달라고 경찰에게 애원했다."]], [["“Just here, three houses away,”", "\"바로 여기, 세 집 건너입니다.\""], ["he said eagerly, “the house belongs to Kozel, a rich German.", "그가 다급하게 말했다. \"그 집은 코젤의 것인데, 부유한 독일인이에요."], ["He was going home, no doubt drunk.", "이 사람은 집으로 가던 길이었어요, 틀림없이 취해서요."], ["I know him, he is a drunkard.", "내가 이 사람을 압니다, 술꾼이에요."], ["He has a family there, a wife, children, he has one daughter....", "거기 가족이 있어요, 아내, 아이들, 딸이 하나 있고……."], ["It will take time to take him to the hospital, and there is sure to be a doctor in the house.", "병원으로 데려가려면 시간이 걸리지만, 그 집에는 분명 의사가 있을 겁니다."], ["I’ll pay, I’ll pay!", "내가 내겠습니다, 내가 낼게요!"], ["At least he will be looked after at home... they will help him at once.", "적어도 집에서는 보살핌을 받을 테니……, 곧바로 도와줄 거예요."], ["But he’ll die before you get him to the hospital.”", "하지만 병원까지 데려가기 전에 죽고 말 겁니다.\""], ["He managed to slip something unseen into the policeman’s hand.", "그는 아무도 모르게 무언가를 경찰의 손에 슬쩍 쥐여 주는 데 성공했다."], ["But the thing was straightforward and legitimate, and in any case help was closer here.", "그러나 일이라는 게 명백하고 정당한 데다, 어차피 도움이 더 가까운 곳에 있었다."], ["They raised the injured man; people volunteered to help.", "사람들이 다친 사내를 들어 올렸고, 자청해서 거드는 이들도 있었다."]], [["Kozel’s house was thirty yards away.", "코젤의 집은 서른 걸음쯤 떨어져 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov walked behind, carefully holding Marmeladov’s head and showing the way.", "라스콜니코프는 마르멜라도프의 머리를 조심스레 받쳐 든 채 길을 안내하며 뒤따라 걸었다."]], [["“This way, this way!", "\"이쪽이요, 이쪽!"], ["We must take him upstairs head foremost.", "머리부터 위로 올려야 합니다."], ["Turn round!", "돌려요!"], ["I’ll pay, I’ll make it worth your while,”", "내가 내겠습니다, 후하게 사례하지요.\""], ["he muttered.", "그가 중얼거렸다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna had just begun, as she always did at every free moment, walking to and fro in her little room from window to stove and back again, with her arms folded across her chest, talking to herself and coughing.", "카테리나 이바노브나는, 늘 그렇듯 짬이 날 때마다 하던 대로, 그 작은 방을 창가에서 난로까지, 다시 그 반대로 오가며 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 안은 채 혼잣말을 하고 기침을 하기 시작한 참이었다."], ["Of late she had begun to talk more than ever to her eldest girl, Polenka, a child of ten, who, though there was much she did not understand, understood very well that her mother needed her, and so always watched her with her big clever eyes and strove her utmost to appear to understand.", "요즘 들어 그녀는 어느 때보다 더 자주 맏딸 폴렌카에게 말을 건넸는데, 폴렌카는 열 살배기 아이로, 알아듣지 못하는 것이 많았지만 엄마가 자기를 필요로 한다는 것만은 아주 잘 알았기에, 늘 그 크고 영리한 눈으로 엄마를 지켜보며 알아듣는 척하려 안간힘을 썼다."], ["This time Polenka was undressing her little brother, who had been unwell all day and was going to bed.", "이번에는 폴렌카가 하루 종일 몸이 좋지 않아 잠자리에 들려는 남동생의 옷을 벗기고 있었다."], ["The boy was waiting for her to take off his shirt, which had to be washed at night.", "사내아이는 누나가 셔츠를 벗겨 주기를 기다리고 있었는데, 그 셔츠는 밤에 빨아야 했다."], ["He was sitting straight and motionless on a chair, with a silent, serious face, with his legs stretched out straight before him--heels together and toes turned out.", "아이는 의자에 꼿꼿이, 미동도 없이 앉아 있었고, 말없는 진지한 얼굴로 두 다리를 앞으로 곧게 뻗은 채 — 발뒤꿈치는 모으고 발끝은 바깥으로 벌린 자세였다."]], [["He was listening to what his mother was saying to his sister, sitting perfectly still with pouting lips and wide-open eyes, just as all good little boys have to sit when they are undressed to go to bed.", "아이는 엄마가 누나에게 하는 말에 귀를 기울이며, 입술을 삐죽 내밀고 두 눈을 동그랗게 뜬 채 완벽하게 가만히 앉아 있었는데, 잠자리에 들려고 옷을 벗을 때면 모든 착한 사내아이들이 으레 그러는 모습 그대로였다."], ["A little girl, still younger, dressed literally in rags, stood at the screen, waiting for her turn.", "그보다 더 어린 여자아이 하나는, 말 그대로 누더기를 걸친 채, 칸막이 옆에 서서 제 차례를 기다리고 있었다."], ["The door on to the stairs was open to relieve them a little from the clouds of tobacco smoke which floated in from the other rooms and brought on long terrible fits of coughing in the poor, consumptive woman.", "계단으로 통하는 문은 열려 있었는데, 다른 방들에서 흘러들어와 가엾고 폐병을 앓는 여인에게 길고 끔찍한 기침 발작을 일으키는 담배 연기를 조금이나마 덜어 보려는 것이었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna seemed to have grown even thinner during that week and the hectic flush on her face was brighter than ever.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 그 한 주 사이에 더욱 야위어 버린 듯했고, 얼굴에 핀 병적인 홍조는 어느 때보다 짙었다."]], [["“You wouldn’t believe, you can’t imagine, Polenka,”", "\"넌 믿지 못할 거야, 상상도 못 할 거야, 폴렌카.\""], ["she said, walking about the room, “what a happy luxurious life we had in my papa’s house and how this drunkard has brought me, and will bring you all, to ruin!", "그녀가 방 안을 오가며 말했다. \"우리가 친정아버지 집에서 얼마나 행복하고 호사스럽게 살았는지, 그리고 이 술꾼이 날 어떻게 파멸로 몰아넣었는지, 그리고 앞으로 너희들 모두를 어떻게 파멸시킬지!"], ["Papa was a civil colonel and only a step from being a governor; so that everyone who came to see him said, ‘We look upon you, Ivan Mihailovitch, as our governor!’ When I...", "아버지는 문관 대령이셨고, 한 발짝만 더 가면 지사가 될 분이셨어. 그래서 아버지를 뵈러 오는 사람마다 이렇게 말했단다, '저희는 어른을 지사님처럼 우러릅니다, 이반 미하일로비치 어른!'"], ["when...” she coughed violently, “oh, cursed life,”", "내가……, 내가…….\""], ["she cried, clearing her throat and pressing her hands to her breast, “when I...", "그녀가 격렬하게 기침을 했다. \"오, 저주받은 인생.\""], ["when at the last ball... at the marshal’s...", "그녀는 목을 가다듬고 두 손을 가슴에 누르며 외쳤다."], ["Princess Bezzemelny saw me--who gave me the blessing when your father and I were married, Polenka--she asked at once ‘Isn’t that the pretty girl who danced the shawl dance at the breaking-up?’ (You must mend that tear, you must take your needle and darn it as I showed you, or to-morrow--cough, cough, cough--he will make the hole bigger,”", "\"내가……, 마지막 무도회에서……, 귀족 회장 댁에서……, 베즈제멜니 공작 부인이 날 보셨어 — 폴렌카, 네 아빠와 내가 결혼할 때 축복해 주신 분이지 — 그분이 곧바로 물으셨단다, '저 애가 졸업식 때 숄 댄스를 추던 그 예쁜 아가씨 아닌가?' (저기 찢어진 데 좀 꿰매라, 바늘을 들고 내가 가르쳐 준 대로 기워야지, 안 그러면 내일은 — 콜록, 콜록, 콜록 — 저이가 더 크게 찢어 놓을 거야.\""], ["she articulated with effort.) “Prince Schegolskoy, a kammerjunker, had just come from Petersburg then...", "그녀가 힘겹게 또박또박 말했다.) \"그때 시종보였던 셰골스코이 공작이 마침 페테르부르크에서 막 오셨는데……, 나하고 마주르카를 추고는 이튿날 청혼을 하려 했지."], ["he danced the mazurka with me and wanted to make me an offer next day; but I thanked him in flattering expressions and told him that my heart had long been another’s.", "하지만 난 듣기 좋은 말로 고마움을 전하면서, 내 마음은 이미 오래전부터 다른 사람의 것이라고 말했단다. 그 다른 사람이 바로 네 아빠였어, 폴랴."], ["That other was your father, Polya; papa was fearfully angry....", "아버지는 무섭게 화를 내셨지……. 물은 준비됐니?"], ["Is the water ready?", "셔츠를 다오, 양말도!"], ["Give me the shirt, and the stockings!", "리다.\" 그녀가 막내에게 말했다."], ["Lida,” said she to the youngest one, “you must manage without your chemise to-night...", "\"오늘 밤엔 속옷 없이 자야겠다……, 그리고 양말도 같이 벗어 놓으렴……, 한꺼번에 빨게."], ["and lay your stockings out with it... I’ll wash them together....", "저 술 취한 부랑자는 도대체 왜 안 들어오는 거야?"], ["How is it that drunken vagabond doesn’t come in? He has worn his shirt till it looks like a dish-clout, he has torn it to rags!", "셔츠를 행주처럼 될 때까지 입어 대고, 너덜너덜 다 찢어 놓았으니!"], ["I’d do it all together, so as not to have to work two nights running!", "한꺼번에 다 해치워야지, 이틀 밤 내리 일하지 않으려면! 아이고, 맙소사!"], ["Oh, dear! (Cough, cough, cough, cough!) Again!", "(콜록, 콜록, 콜록, 콜록!) 또 시작이군!"], ["What’s this?”", "이게 뭐야?\""], ["she cried, noticing a crowd in the passage and the men, who were pushing into her room, carrying a burden.", "그녀는 통로에 몰려든 사람들과, 무언가를 들고 방으로 밀고 들어오는 사내들을 알아채고 소리쳤다."], ["“What is it?", "\"이게 뭐예요?"], ["What are they bringing?", "뭘 들고 오는 거예요?"], ["Mercy on us!”", "맙소사!\""]], [["“Where are we to put him?”", "\"이 사람을 어디에 둘까요?\""], ["asked the policeman, looking round when Marmeladov, unconscious and covered with blood, had been carried in.", "의식을 잃고 피투성이가 된 마르멜라도프가 옮겨져 들어왔을 때, 경찰이 둘러보며 물었다."]], [["“On the sofa!", "\"소파에!"], ["Put him straight on the sofa, with his head this way,”", "소파에 곧장 눕혀요, 머리는 이쪽으로.\""], ["Raskolnikov showed him.", "라스콜니코프가 가리켰다."]], [["“Run over in the road!", "\"길에서 치였대!"], ["Drunk!”", "취해서!\""], ["someone shouted in the passage.", "통로에서 누군가 소리쳤다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna stood, turning white and gasping for breath.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 새파랗게 질려 숨을 헐떡이며 서 있었다."], ["The children were terrified.", "아이들은 겁에 질렸다."], ["Little Lida screamed, rushed to Polenka and clutched at her, trembling all over.", "어린 리다는 비명을 지르며 폴렌카에게 달려가 온몸을 떨며 매달렸다."]], [["Having laid Marmeladov down, Raskolnikov flew to Katerina Ivanovna.", "마르멜라도프를 눕히고 나서, 라스콜니코프는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 날 듯이 달려갔다."]], [["“For God’s sake be calm, don’t be frightened!”", "\"제발 진정하세요, 무서워하지 마세요!\""], ["he said, speaking quickly, “he was crossing the road and was run over by a carriage, don’t be frightened, he will come to, I told them bring him here...", "그가 빠르게 말했다. \"이분이 길을 건너다 마차에 치였습니다, 무서워하지 마세요, 곧 정신이 들 겁니다, 제가 이리로 옮기라고 했어요……, 제가 전에 여기 왔었지요, 기억하시죠?"], ["I’ve been here already, you remember? He will come to; I’ll pay!”", "곧 정신이 들 거예요, 제가 내겠습니다!\""]], [["“He’s done it this time!”", "\"이번엔 끝장이구나!\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna cried despairingly and she rushed to her husband.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 절망하여 외치며 남편에게 달려들었다."]], [["Raskolnikov noticed at once that she was not one of those women who swoon easily.", "라스콜니코프는 그녀가 쉬이 기절하는 부류의 여자가 아님을 곧바로 알아차렸다."], ["She instantly placed under the luckless man’s head a pillow, which no one had thought of and began undressing and examining him.", "그녀는 곧장 그 불운한 사내의 머리 밑에 베개를 받쳤는데 — 아무도 거기까지 생각하지 못했던 것이다 — 이내 옷을 벗기며 그를 살피기 시작했다."], ["She kept her head, forgetting herself, biting her trembling lips and stifling the screams which were ready to break from her.", "그녀는 자신을 잊은 채 정신을 차리고서, 떨리는 입술을 깨물며 터져 나오려는 비명을 억눌렀다."]], [["Raskolnikov meanwhile induced someone to run for a doctor.", "그러는 동안 라스콜니코프는 누군가에게 의사를 부르러 가도록 시켰다."], ["There was a doctor, it appeared, next door but one.", "알고 보니 한 집 건너에 의사가 있었다."]], [["“I’ve sent for a doctor,”", "\"의사를 부르러 보냈어요.\""], ["he kept assuring Katerina Ivanovna, “don’t be uneasy, I’ll pay.", "그는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 거듭 안심시켰다. \"걱정하지 마세요, 제가 내겠습니다."], ["Haven’t you water?... and give me a napkin or a towel, anything, as quick as you can....", "물 없습니까?……, 그리고 냅킨이든 수건이든, 뭐든 좋으니 어서 좀 주세요……."], ["He is injured, but not killed, believe me....", "다치긴 했지만 죽진 않았어요, 믿으세요……."], ["We shall see what the doctor says!”", "의사가 뭐라고 하는지 봅시다!\""]], [["Katerina Ivanovna ran to the window; there, on a broken chair in the corner, a large earthenware basin full of water had been stood, in readiness for washing her children’s and husband’s linen that night.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 창가로 달려갔다. 그곳, 구석의 망가진 의자 위에는 커다란 질그릇 대야에 물이 가득 담겨 있었는데, 그날 밤 아이들과 남편의 속옷을 빨려고 준비해 둔 것이었다."], ["This washing was done by Katerina Ivanovna at night at least twice a week, if not oftener.", "이 빨래를 카테리나 이바노브나는 밤마다, 적어도 일주일에 두 번은, 아니 그보다 자주 했다."], ["For the family had come to such a pass that they were practically without change of linen, and Katerina Ivanovna could not endure uncleanliness and, rather than see dirt in the house, she preferred to wear herself out at night, working beyond her strength when the rest were asleep, so as to get the wet linen hung on a line and dry by the morning.", "이 집 형편이 갈아입을 속옷조차 거의 없을 지경에 이른 데다, 카테리나 이바노브나는 불결함을 견디지 못해서, 집 안에 더러운 꼴을 보느니 차라리 밤에 식구들이 다 잠든 사이 제 힘에 부치도록 일해 가며 몸을 축내는 편을 택했던 것이다. 그래야 젖은 속옷을 줄에 널어 아침까지 말릴 수 있었으니까."], ["She took up the basin of water at Raskolnikov’s request, but almost fell down with her burden.", "그녀는 라스콜니코프가 청한 대로 물 대야를 집어 들었지만, 그 무게에 거의 쓰러질 뻔했다."], ["But the latter had already succeeded in finding a towel, wetted it and began washing the blood off Marmeladov’s face.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 이미 수건을 찾아내, 그것을 적셔 마르멜라도프의 얼굴에서 피를 닦아 내기 시작했다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna stood by, breathing painfully and pressing her hands to her breast.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 곁에 서서 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬며 두 손을 가슴에 누르고 있었다."], ["She was in need of attention herself.", "그녀 자신이 보살핌을 받아야 할 처지였다."], ["Raskolnikov began to realise that he might have made a mistake in having the injured man brought here.", "라스콜니코프는 다친 사내를 이리로 옮긴 것이 어쩌면 실수였을지도 모른다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["The policeman, too, stood in hesitation.", "경찰 역시 망설이며 서 있었다."]], [["“Polenka,”", "\"폴렌카.\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, “run to Sonia, make haste.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다. \"소냐한테 뛰어가렴, 어서."], ["If you don’t find her at home, leave word that her father has been run over and that she is to come here at once...", "집에 없거든 아빠가 마차에 치였으니 당장 이리로 오라고 말을 남겨 두렴……, 들어오는 대로 말이야."], ["when she comes in.", "뛰어가, 폴렌카!"], ["Run, Polenka! there, put on the shawl.”", "자, 숄을 두르고.\""]], [["“Run your fastest!”", "\"있는 힘껏 뛰어!\""], ["cried the little boy on the chair suddenly, after which he relapsed into the same dumb rigidity, with round eyes, his heels thrust forward and his toes spread out.", "의자에 앉아 있던 사내아이가 갑자기 소리치더니, 그러고는 다시 그 말없는 굳은 자세로 돌아가, 동그란 눈에 발뒤꿈치는 앞으로 내밀고 발끝은 벌린 채였다."]], [["Meanwhile the room had become so full of people that you couldn’t have dropped a pin.", "그러는 사이 방 안은 사람들로 가득 차서 바늘 하나 떨어뜨릴 틈도 없었다."], ["The policemen left, all except one, who remained for a time, trying to drive out the people who came in from the stairs.", "경찰들은 떠났고, 다만 한 명이 한동안 남아 계단에서 들어오는 사람들을 몰아내려 애썼다."], ["Almost all Madame Lippevechsel’s lodgers had streamed in from the inner rooms of the flat; at first they were squeezed together in the doorway, but afterwards they overflowed into the room.", "리페베흐젤 부인의 세입자란 세입자는 거의 다 집 안쪽 방에서 쏟아져 나왔는데, 처음에는 문간에 끼어 있다가 나중에는 방 안으로까지 넘쳐 들었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna flew into a fury.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 격노했다."]], [["“You might let him die in peace, at least,”", "\"적어도 편히 죽게는 해 줘야 할 거 아녜요.\""], ["she shouted at the crowd, “is it a spectacle for you to gape at?", "그녀가 군중을 향해 소리쳤다. \"이게 당신들 입 벌리고 구경할 거리예요?"], ["With cigarettes!", "담배까지 물고서!"], ["(Cough, cough, cough!) You might as well keep your hats on....", "(콜록, 콜록, 콜록!) 모자라도 쓰고 계시지 그래요……."], ["And there is one in his hat!... Get away!", "저기 하나는 모자까지 쓰고 있군!……, 썩 나가요!"], ["You should respect the dead, at least!”", "적어도 죽은 사람한테는 예의를 지켜야지!\""]], [["Her cough choked her--but her reproaches were not without result.", "기침이 그녀의 목을 틀어막았지만 — 그녀의 질책은 효과가 없지 않았다."], ["They evidently stood in some awe of Katerina Ivanovna.", "사람들은 분명 카테리나 이바노브나를 다소 두려워하고 있었다."], ["The lodgers, one after another, squeezed back into the doorway with that strange inner feeling of satisfaction which may be observed in the presence of a sudden accident, even in those nearest and dearest to the victim, from which no living man is exempt, even in spite of the sincerest sympathy and compassion.", "세입자들은 하나둘 다시 문간으로 밀려 나갔는데, 그것은 갑작스러운 사고를 마주할 때 사람들 마음속에 일어나는 그 묘한 내면의 만족감, 희생자와 가장 가깝고 가장 아끼는 이들에게서조차 엿보이는, 아무리 진실한 동정과 연민을 품었다 한들 살아 있는 그 누구도 면치 못하는 그런 감정 탓이었다."]], [["Voices outside were heard, however, speaking of the hospital and saying that they’d no business to make a disturbance here.", "그러나 바깥에서는 병원 이야기를 하며, 여기서 소동을 피울 일이 아니라고 떠드는 목소리들이 들려왔다."]], [["“No business to die!”", "\"죽을 일도 아니라니!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, and she was rushing to the door to vent her wrath upon them, but in the doorway came face to face with Madame Lippevechsel who had only just heard of the accident and ran in to restore order.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외치며 분풀이를 하려고 문 쪽으로 달려들었으나, 문간에서 리페베흐젤 부인과 정면으로 마주쳤다. 사고 소식을 막 듣고 질서를 잡으러 달려온 참이었다."], ["She was a particularly quarrelsome and irresponsible German.", "그녀는 유달리 다투기 좋아하고 무책임한 독일 여자였다."]], [["“Ah, my God!”", "\"아이고, 맙소사!\""], ["she cried, clasping her hands, “your husband drunken horses have trampled!", "그녀가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다. \"당신 남편을 취한 말이 짓밟았구먼!"], ["To the hospital with him!", "병원으로 데려가요!"], ["I am the landlady!”", "내가 이 집 주인이야!\""]], [["“Amalia Ludwigovna, I beg you to recollect what you are saying,”", "\"아말리야 루드비고브나, 부디 무슨 말을 하고 있는지 좀 새겨 보시지요.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna began haughtily (she always took a haughty tone with the landlady that she might “remember her place”", "카테리나 이바노브나가 거만하게 입을 열었다(그녀는 집주인에게 늘 거만한 어조를 썼는데, 그녀가 '제 분수를 깨닫게'"], ["and even now could not deny herself this satisfaction).", "하려는 것이었고, 지금 이 순간에도 그 만족을 스스로 마다할 수 없었다)."], ["“Amalia Ludwigovna...”", "\"아말리야 루드비고브나…….\""]], [["“I have you once before told that you to call me Amalia Ludwigovna may not dare; I am Amalia Ivanovna.”", "\"내가 전에 한 번 말했잖아, 날 아말리야 루드비고브나라고 부를 생각은 꿈도 꾸지 말라고. 난 아말리야 이바노브나야.\""]], [["“You are not Amalia Ivanovna, but Amalia Ludwigovna, and as I am not one of your despicable flatterers like Mr. Lebeziatnikov, who’s laughing behind the door at this moment (a laugh and a cry of ‘they are at it again’ was in fact audible at the door) so I shall always call you Amalia Ludwigovna, though I fail to understand why you dislike that name.", "\"당신은 아말리야 이바노브나가 아니라 아말리야 루드비고브나예요, 그리고 나는 지금 문 뒤에서 웃고 있는 레베쟈트니코프 씨 같은 당신의 천박한 아첨꾼이 아니니까 (실제로 문에서 웃음소리와 '또 시작이군'이라는 외침이 들려왔다) 나는 늘 당신을 아말리야 루드비고브나라고 부르겠어요, 도대체 왜 그 이름을 싫어하는지는 이해할 수 없지만요."], ["You can see for yourself what has happened to Semyon Zaharovitch; he is dying.", "세묜 자하로비치에게 무슨 일이 일어났는지 직접 보세요. 죽어 가고 있다고요."], ["I beg you to close that door at once and to admit no one.", "부탁이니 당장 저 문을 닫고 아무도 들이지 마세요."], ["Let him at least die in peace!", "적어도 편히 죽게는 해 줘야지!"], ["Or I warn you the Governor-General, himself, shall be informed of your conduct to-morrow.", "안 그러면 경고하건대, 내일 당장 총독 각하께 당신의 행실을 알릴 거예요."], ["The prince knew me as a girl; he remembers Semyon Zaharovitch well and has often been a benefactor to him.", "공작께서는 제가 처녀였을 때부터 저를 아셨고, 세묜 자하로비치도 잘 기억하시며 그분께 여러 차례 은혜를 베푸셨어요."], ["Everyone knows that Semyon Zaharovitch had many friends and protectors, whom he abandoned himself from an honourable pride, knowing his unhappy weakness, but now (she pointed to Raskolnikov) a generous young man has come to our assistance, who has wealth and connections and whom Semyon Zaharovitch has known from a child.", "세묜 자하로비치에게 친구와 후원자가 많았다는 건 누구나 다 아는 사실이지요, 그분이 자신의 불행한 약점을 알기에 고결한 자존심에서 스스로 그들을 멀리했을 뿐이지만요. 하지만 이제 (그녀가 라스콜니코프를 가리켰다) 너그러운 젊은이 한 분이 우리를 도우러 오셨는데, 재산도 연줄도 있는 분이고 세묜 자하로비치가 어릴 때부터 알던 분이에요."], ["You may rest assured, Amalia Ludwigovna...”", "안심하셔도 좋아요, 아말리야 루드비고브나…….\""]], [["All this was uttered with extreme rapidity, getting quicker and quicker, but a cough suddenly cut short Katerina Ivanovna’s eloquence.", "이 모든 말이 극도로 빠르게, 점점 더 빨라지며 쏟아져 나왔지만, 갑작스러운 기침이 카테리나 이바노브나의 달변을 뚝 끊어 놓았다."], ["At that instant the dying man recovered consciousness and uttered a groan; she ran to him.", "바로 그 순간 죽어 가던 사내가 의식을 되찾고 신음을 토했다. 그녀가 그에게 달려갔다."], ["The injured man opened his eyes and without recognition or understanding gazed at Raskolnikov who was bending over him.", "다친 사내는 눈을 떴으나, 알아보지도 이해하지도 못한 채 자기 위로 몸을 숙인 라스콜니코프를 멍하니 바라보았다."], ["He drew deep, slow, painful breaths; blood oozed at the corners of his mouth and drops of perspiration came out on his forehead.", "그는 깊고 느리고 괴로운 숨을 들이쉬었다. 입가에는 피가 배어 나오고 이마에는 땀방울이 맺혔다."], ["Not recognising Raskolnikov, he began looking round uneasily.", "라스콜니코프를 알아보지 못한 채, 그는 불안하게 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna looked at him with a sad but stern face, and tears trickled from her eyes.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 슬프지만 엄한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 주르륵 흘렀다."]], [["“My God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["His whole chest is crushed!", "가슴이 통째로 으스러졌어!"], ["How he is bleeding,”", "어쩌면 이렇게 피를 흘리는지.\""], ["she said in despair.", "그녀가 절망하여 말했다."], ["“We must take off his clothes.", "\"옷을 벗겨야 해요."], ["Turn a little, Semyon Zaharovitch, if you can,”", "조금만 돌아누워 봐요, 세묜 자하로비치, 할 수 있겠거든.\""], ["she cried to him.", "그녀가 그에게 외쳤다."]], [["Marmeladov recognised her.", "마르멜라도프가 그녀를 알아보았다."]], [["“A priest,”", "\"신부님을.\""], ["he articulated huskily.", "그가 쉰 목소리로 또박또박 말했다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna walked to the window, laid her head against the window frame and exclaimed in despair:", "카테리나 이바노브나는 창가로 걸어가, 창틀에 머리를 기댄 채 절망하여 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, cursed life!”", "\"오, 저주받은 인생!\""]], [["“A priest,”", "\"신부님을.\""], ["the dying man said again after a moment’s silence.", "죽어 가던 사내가 잠시 침묵한 뒤 다시 말했다."]], [["“They’ve gone for him,”", "\"부르러 갔어요.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna shouted to him, he obeyed her shout and was silent.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 소리쳤고, 그는 그 외침에 따르듯 입을 다물었다."], ["With sad and timid eyes he looked for her; she returned and stood by his pillow.", "슬프고 겁먹은 눈으로 그가 그녀를 찾았다. 그녀가 돌아와 그의 베개 곁에 섰다."], ["He seemed a little easier but not for long.", "그는 다소 편안해진 듯했으나, 오래가지는 않았다."]], [["Soon his eyes rested on little Lida, his favourite, who was shaking in the corner, as though she were in a fit, and staring at him with her wondering childish eyes.", "이윽고 그의 눈길이, 구석에서 발작이라도 일으킨 듯 떨면서 의아해하는 어린아이의 눈으로 그를 빤히 바라보고 있는, 그가 가장 아끼는 어린 리다에게 멈췄다."]], [["“A-ah,”", "\"아-아.\""], ["he signed towards her uneasily.", "그가 불안하게 그쪽을 가리키며 신음했다."], ["He wanted to say something.", "그는 무언가 말하고 싶어 했다."]], [["“What now?”", "\"또 뭐예요?\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다."]], [["“Barefoot, barefoot!”", "\"맨발이야, 맨발!\""], ["he muttered, indicating with frenzied eyes the child’s bare feet.", "그가 미친 듯한 눈으로 아이의 맨발을 가리키며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Be silent,”", "\"조용히 해요.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna cried irritably, “you know why she is barefooted.”", "카테리나 이바노브나가 짜증스럽게 외쳤다. \"저 애가 왜 맨발인지 당신도 알잖아요.\""]], [["“Thank God, the doctor,”", "\"하느님 감사합니다, 의사가.\""], ["exclaimed Raskolnikov, relieved.", "라스콜니코프가 안도하며 외쳤다."]], [["The doctor came in, a precise little old man, a German, looking about him mistrustfully; he went up to the sick man, took his pulse, carefully felt his head and with the help of Katerina Ivanovna he unbuttoned the blood-stained shirt, and bared the injured man’s chest.", "의사가 들어왔는데, 꼼꼼해 보이는 자그마한 독일인 노인으로, 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 환자에게 다가가 맥을 짚고, 조심스레 머리를 만져 보고는, 카테리나 이바노브나의 도움을 받아 피로 물든 셔츠의 단추를 풀고 다친 사내의 가슴을 드러냈다."], ["It was gashed, crushed and fractured, several ribs on the right side were broken.", "가슴은 찢기고 으스러지고 부서져 있었으며, 오른쪽 갈비뼈가 여러 대 부러져 있었다."], ["On the left side, just over the heart, was a large, sinister-looking yellowish-black bruise--a cruel kick from the horse’s hoof.", "왼쪽, 바로 심장 위에는 크고 불길해 보이는 누르스름한 검은 멍이 있었는데 — 말발굽에 모질게 채인 자국이었다."], ["The doctor frowned.", "의사가 미간을 찌푸렸다."], ["The policeman told him that he was caught in the wheel and turned round with it for thirty yards on the road.", "경찰이 그에게, 이 사람이 바퀴에 끼여 길에서 서른 걸음이나 함께 굴러갔다고 말했다."]], [["“It’s wonderful that he has recovered consciousness,”", "\"이 사람이 의식을 되찾았다는 게 놀랍군요.\""], ["the doctor whispered softly to Raskolnikov.", "의사가 라스콜니코프에게 나직이 속삭였다."]], [["“What do you think of him?”", "\"이 사람은 어떻습니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“He will die immediately.”", "\"곧 죽을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Is there really no hope?”", "\"정말 가망이 없습니까?\""]], [["“Not the faintest!", "\"손톱만큼도 없어요!"], ["He is at the last gasp....", "마지막 숨을 몰아쉬고 있어요……."], ["His head is badly injured, too... Hm...", "머리도 심하게 다쳤고……."], ["I could bleed him if you like, but... it would be useless.", "흠……, 원하신다면 피를 뽑아 줄 수는 있지만……, 소용없을 겁니다."], ["He is bound to die within the next five or ten minutes.”", "앞으로 오 분이나 십 분 안에 틀림없이 죽을 거예요.\""]], [["“Better bleed him then.”", "\"그럼 피라도 뽑아 주는 게 낫겠지요.\""]], [["“If you like....", "\"원하신다면야……."], ["But I warn you it will be perfectly useless.”", "하지만 미리 말해 두는데, 완전히 부질없는 짓일 겁니다.\""]], [["At that moment other steps were heard; the crowd in the passage parted, and the priest, a little, grey old man, appeared in the doorway bearing the sacrament.", "바로 그 순간 또 다른 발소리가 들렸다. 통로의 군중이 갈라지더니, 자그마한 백발노인인 신부가 성체를 받쳐 들고 문간에 나타났다."], ["A policeman had gone for him at the time of the accident.", "사고가 났을 때 경찰 하나가 그를 부르러 갔던 것이다."], ["The doctor changed places with him, exchanging glances with him.", "의사는 신부와 자리를 바꾸며 그와 눈짓을 주고받았다."], ["Raskolnikov begged the doctor to remain a little while.", "라스콜니코프는 의사에게 잠시만 더 머물러 달라고 청했다."], ["He shrugged his shoulders and remained.", "의사는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 남았다."]], [["All stepped back.", "모두가 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["The confession was soon over.", "고해는 금세 끝났다."], ["The dying man probably understood little; he could only utter indistinct broken sounds.", "죽어 가는 사내는 아마 거의 알아듣지 못했을 것이다. 그는 그저 또렷하지 않은 끊긴 소리만 낼 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna took little Lida, lifted the boy from the chair, knelt down in the corner by the stove and made the children kneel in front of her.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 어린 리다를 데려오고, 사내아이를 의자에서 들어 올린 뒤, 난로 옆 구석에 무릎을 꿇고 아이들을 자기 앞에 나란히 꿇어앉혔다."], ["The little girl was still trembling; but the boy, kneeling on his little bare knees, lifted his hand rhythmically, crossing himself with precision and bowed down, touching the floor with his forehead, which seemed to afford him especial satisfaction.", "여자아이는 여전히 떨고 있었지만, 사내아이는 작은 맨무릎을 꿇은 채 또박또박 손을 들어 정확하게 성호를 긋고는, 이마가 바닥에 닿도록 절을 했는데, 그것이 그 아이에게는 각별한 만족을 주는 듯했다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna bit her lips and held back her tears; she prayed, too, now and then pulling straight the boy’s shirt, and managed to cover the girl’s bare shoulders with a kerchief, which she took from the chest without rising from her knees or ceasing to pray.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 입술을 깨물고 눈물을 참았다. 그녀도 기도를 했고, 이따금 사내아이의 셔츠를 똑바로 잡아당기기도 하면서, 무릎을 꿇은 채 기도를 멈추지 않고 궤에서 꺼낸 손수건으로 여자아이의 드러난 어깨를 가까스로 덮어 주었다."], ["Meanwhile the door from the inner rooms was opened inquisitively again.", "그러는 동안 안쪽 방들로 통하는 문이 또다시 호기심에 못 이겨 열렸다."], ["In the passage the crowd of spectators from all the flats on the staircase grew denser and denser, but they did not venture beyond the threshold.", "통로에는 계단의 온 셋집에서 몰려든 구경꾼들이 점점 더 빽빽하게 들어찼지만, 그들은 문지방을 넘어설 엄두는 내지 못했다."], ["A single candle-end lighted up the scene.", "짤막한 양초 토막 하나가 이 광경을 비추고 있었다."]], [["At that moment Polenka forced her way through the crowd at the door.", "바로 그 순간 폴렌카가 문간의 군중을 헤치고 비집고 들어왔다."], ["She came in panting from running so fast, took off her kerchief, looked for her mother, went up to her and said, “She’s coming, I met her in the street.”", "그녀는 너무 빨리 달려온 탓에 숨을 헐떡이며 들어와, 머릿수건을 벗고, 엄마를 찾아 다가가서는 말했다. \"언니가 와요, 길에서 만났어요.\""], ["Her mother made her kneel beside her.", "엄마는 그녀를 자기 곁에 무릎 꿇렸다."]], [["Timidly and noiselessly a young girl made her way through the crowd, and strange was her appearance in that room, in the midst of want, rags, death and despair.", "겁먹은 듯 소리 없이 한 젊은 처녀가 군중을 헤치고 들어왔는데, 궁핍과 누더기와 죽음과 절망 한가운데 놓인 그 방에서 그녀의 모습은 기이했다."], ["She, too, was in rags, her attire was all of the cheapest, but decked out in gutter finery of a special stamp, unmistakably betraying its shameful purpose.", "그녀 역시 누더기를 걸쳤고, 차림새는 죄다 가장 값싼 것들이었지만, 거리에서 몸 파는 여자 특유의 천박한 치장으로 꾸며져 있어, 그 부끄러운 목적을 틀림없이 드러내고 있었다."], ["Sonia stopped short in the doorway and looked about her bewildered, unconscious of everything.", "소냐는 문간에 멈춰 서서 어쩔 줄 모른 채 주위를 둘러보았는데, 아무것도 의식하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["She forgot her fourth-hand, gaudy silk dress, so unseemly here with its ridiculous long train, and her immense crinoline that filled up the whole doorway, and her light-coloured shoes, and the parasol she brought with her, though it was no use at night, and the absurd round straw hat with its flaring flame-coloured feather.", "그녀는 자신의 네 번째 손을 거친 야한 비단 드레스를, 여기서는 우스꽝스러울 만큼 긴 자락이 도무지 어울리지 않는 그 옷을 잊었고, 문간을 가득 메운 거대한 크리놀린도, 밝은 색 구두도, 밤에는 쓸데도 없건만 들고 온 양산도, 불꽃처럼 새빨간 깃털이 나풀거리는 우스꽝스러운 둥근 밀짚모자도 잊고 있었다."], ["Under this rakishly-tilted hat was a pale, frightened little face with lips parted and eyes staring in terror.", "멋들어지게 비스듬히 기울인 그 모자 아래로는, 입술을 벌리고 두 눈을 공포에 질려 부릅뜬, 창백하고 겁에 질린 자그마한 얼굴이 있었다."], ["Sonia was a small thin girl of eighteen with fair hair, rather pretty, with wonderful blue eyes.", "소냐는 열여덟 살의 자그맣고 야윈 처녀로, 금발에, 제법 예뻤으며, 놀라운 푸른 눈을 지니고 있었다."], ["She looked intently at the bed and the priest; she too was out of breath with running.", "그녀는 침대와 신부를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그녀 역시 달려오느라 숨이 차 있었다."], ["At last whispers, some words in the crowd probably, reached her.", "마침내 속삭임이, 아마도 군중 속에서 들려온 몇 마디 말이 그녀의 귀에 닿았다."], ["She looked down and took a step forward into the room, still keeping close to the door.", "그녀는 눈을 내리깔고, 여전히 문 가까이에 붙은 채로 방 안으로 한 걸음 내디뎠다."]], [["The service was over.", "예식이 끝났다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna went up to her husband again.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 다시 남편에게 다가갔다."], ["The priest stepped back and turned to say a few words of admonition and consolation to Katerina Ivanovna on leaving.", "신부는 뒤로 물러서며, 떠나기 전에 카테리나 이바노브나에게 몇 마디 훈계와 위로의 말을 건네려고 돌아섰다."]], [["“What am I to do with these?”", "\"이 애들을 어쩌란 말이에요?\""], ["she interrupted sharply and irritably, pointing to the little ones.", "그녀가 어린것들을 가리키며 날카롭고 짜증스럽게 그의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“God is merciful; look to the Most High for succour,”", "\"하느님은 자비로우시니, 지존하신 분께 도움을 구하십시오.\""], ["the priest began.", "신부가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Ach!", "\"흥!"], ["He is merciful, but not to us.”", "그분은 자비로우시겠지요, 우리한테는 아니지만.\""]], [["“That’s a sin, a sin, madam,”", "\"그건 죄입니다, 죄예요, 부인.\""], ["observed the priest, shaking his head.", "신부가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“And isn’t that a sin?”", "\"그럼 저건 죄가 아니고요?\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, pointing to the dying man.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 죽어 가는 사내를 가리키며 외쳤다."]], [["“Perhaps those who have involuntarily caused the accident will agree to compensate you, at least for the loss of his earnings.”", "\"본의 아니게 사고를 낸 자들이, 적어도 그가 벌어들이던 몫의 손실에 대해서는 부인께 보상하기로 할지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“You don’t understand!”", "\"신부님은 이해를 못 하시는군요!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna angrily waving her hand.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 성을 내며 손을 내저었다."], ["“And why should they compensate me?", "\"그리고 그들이 왜 나한테 보상을 해요?"], ["Why, he was drunk and threw himself under the horses!", "아니, 저이는 취해서 제 발로 말 아래 뛰어든 거예요!"], ["What earnings?", "무슨 벌이요?"], ["He brought us in nothing but misery.", "저이가 우리한테 가져다준 거라곤 비참함뿐이었어요."], ["He drank everything away, the drunkard!", "다 마셔 없앴다고요, 저 술꾼이!"], ["He robbed us to get drink, he wasted their lives and mine for drink!", "술 마시려고 우릴 등쳐 먹고, 술 때문에 저 애들 인생도 내 인생도 다 망쳐 놨어요!"], ["And thank God he’s dying!", "그러니 죽으니 차라리 다행이지!"], ["One less to keep!”", "먹여 살릴 입 하나 줄었으니!\""]], [["“You must forgive in the hour of death, that’s a sin, madam, such feelings are a great sin.”", "\"죽음의 순간에는 용서해야 합니다, 그건 죄예요, 부인, 그런 마음은 큰 죄입니다.\""]], [["Katerina Ivanovna was busy with the dying man; she was giving him water, wiping the blood and sweat from his head, setting his pillow straight, and had only turned now and then for a moment to address the priest.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 죽어 가는 사내를 돌보느라 바빴다. 그에게 물을 먹이고, 머리에서 피와 땀을 닦아 내고, 베개를 똑바로 고쳐 주면서, 이따금 잠깐씩만 몸을 돌려 신부에게 말을 건넸을 뿐이다."], ["Now she flew at him almost in a frenzy.", "이제 그녀는 거의 광기에 사로잡힌 듯 그에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Ah, father!", "\"아, 신부님!"], ["That’s words and only words!", "그건 말, 말뿐이라고요!"], ["Forgive!", "용서하라니!"], ["If he’d not been run over, he’d have come home to-day drunk and his only shirt dirty and in rags and he’d have fallen asleep like a log, and I should have been sousing and rinsing till daybreak, washing his rags and the children’s and then drying them by the window and as soon as it was daylight I should have been darning them.", "저이가 치이지 않았다면, 오늘도 술에 취해 집에 들어와서는, 하나뿐인 셔츠를 더럽고 너덜너덜하게 만들어 놓고는, 통나무처럼 곯아떨어졌겠지요, 그럼 나는 동틀 때까지 물에 담그고 헹구면서 저이의 누더기랑 애들 옷을 빨고, 그걸 창가에서 말리다가, 날이 밝자마자 기워야 했겠지요."], ["That’s how I spend my nights!... What’s the use of talking of forgiveness!", "내가 밤을 그렇게 보낸다고요!……, 용서가 다 무슨 소용이람!"], ["I have forgiven as it is!”", "난 이미 용서했어요!\""]], [["A terrible hollow cough interrupted her words.", "끔찍하게 텅 빈 기침이 그녀의 말을 끊었다."], ["She put her handkerchief to her lips and showed it to the priest, pressing her other hand to her aching chest.", "그녀는 손수건을 입에 갖다 대더니, 다른 손으로 쑤시는 가슴을 누른 채 그것을 신부에게 내보였다."], ["The handkerchief was covered with blood.", "손수건은 피로 흥건했다."], ["The priest bowed his head and said nothing.", "신부는 고개를 숙인 채 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Marmeladov was in the last agony; he did not take his eyes off the face of Katerina Ivanovna, who was bending over him again.", "마르멜라도프는 마지막 단말마의 고통 속에 있었다. 그는 다시 자기 위로 몸을 숙인 카테리나 이바노브나의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 못했다."], ["He kept trying to say something to her; he began moving his tongue with difficulty and articulating indistinctly, but Katerina Ivanovna, understanding that he wanted to ask her forgiveness, called peremptorily to him:", "그는 그녀에게 무언가 말하려고 자꾸만 애썼다. 힘겹게 혀를 놀려 또렷하지 않게 말하기 시작했지만, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그가 용서를 빌고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아채고 단호하게 그를 향해 외쳤다."]], [["“Be silent! No need!", "\"조용히 해요!"], ["I know what you want to say!”", "그럴 것 없어요!"], ["And the sick man was silent, but at the same instant his wandering eyes strayed to the doorway and he saw Sonia.", "당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 다 알아요!\" 그러자 환자는 입을 다물었으나, 바로 그 순간 그의 떠도는 눈길이 문간으로 흘러가 소냐를 보았다."]], [["Till then he had not noticed her: she was standing in the shadow in a corner.", "그때까지 그는 그녀를 알아채지 못하고 있었다. 그녀는 구석의 그늘 속에 서 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Who’s that?", "\"저게 누구야?"], ["Who’s that?”", "저게 누구야?\""], ["he said suddenly in a thick gasping voice, in agitation, turning his eyes in horror towards the door where his daughter was standing, and trying to sit up.", "그가 갑자기 동요하며, 걸걸하게 헐떡이는 목소리로, 딸이 서 있는 문 쪽으로 공포에 질려 눈을 돌리며 말했다. 그러고는 몸을 일으키려 했다."]], [["“Lie down!", "\"누워 있어요!"], ["Lie do-own!”", "누워 있으라니까요!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다."]], [["With unnatural strength he had succeeded in propping himself on his elbow.", "그는 부자연스러운 힘으로 팔꿈치를 짚고 몸을 일으키는 데 성공했다."], ["He looked wildly and fixedly for some time on his daughter, as though not recognising her.", "그는 한동안 딸을 알아보지 못하는 듯, 거칠고 빤히 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["He had never seen her before in such attire.", "그는 이제껏 그녀의 그런 차림새를 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["Suddenly he recognised her, crushed and ashamed in her humiliation and gaudy finery, meekly awaiting her turn to say good-bye to her dying father.", "문득 그는 그녀를 알아보았다 — 모욕과 야한 치장 속에 짓눌리고 부끄러워하며, 죽어 가는 아버지에게 작별을 고할 제 차례를 온순히 기다리고 있는 딸을."], ["His face showed intense suffering.", "그의 얼굴에 극심한 고통이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Sonia!", "\"소냐!"], ["Daughter!", "딸아!"], ["Forgive!”", "용서해 다오!\""], ["he cried, and he tried to hold out his hand to her, but losing his balance, he fell off the sofa, face downwards on the floor.", "그가 외치며 그녀에게 손을 내밀려 했으나, 균형을 잃고 소파에서 굴러떨어져 얼굴을 바닥에 박고 엎어졌다."], ["They rushed to pick him up, they put him on the sofa; but he was dying.", "사람들이 그를 일으키려 달려들어 소파에 다시 눕혔다. 그러나 그는 죽어 가고 있었다."], ["Sonia with a faint cry ran up, embraced him and remained so without moving.", "소냐는 가냘프게 비명을 지르며 달려와 그를 끌어안고는, 그대로 미동도 없이 있었다."], ["He died in her arms.", "그는 그녀의 품 안에서 숨을 거두었다."]], [["“He’s got what he wanted,”", "\"바라던 대로 됐구나.\""], ["Katerina Ivanovna cried, seeing her husband’s dead body.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 남편의 시신을 보며 외쳤다."], ["“Well, what’s to be done now?", "\"그래, 이제 어쩌면 좋아?"], ["How am I to bury him!", "무슨 수로 묻어 줘?"], ["What can I give them to-morrow to eat?”", "내일 저 애들한테 뭘 먹여?\""]], [["Raskolnikov went up to Katerina Ivanovna.", "라스콜니코프가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna,”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나.\""], ["he began, “last week your husband told me all his life and circumstances....", "그가 입을 열었다."], ["Believe me, he spoke of you with passionate reverence.", "\"지난주에 부군께서 제게 당신의 살아온 이야기와 사정을 다 들려주셨습니다……."], ["From that evening, when I learnt how devoted he was to you all and how he loved and respected you especially, Katerina Ivanovna, in spite of his unfortunate weakness, from that evening we became friends.... Allow me now...", "믿어 주세요, 그분은 당신을 열렬한 경외심으로 말씀하셨어요."], ["to do something... to repay my debt to my dead friend.", "그분이 당신들 모두에게 얼마나 헌신적이었는지, 그리고 그 불행한 약점에도 불구하고 특히 당신을 얼마나 사랑하고 존경했는지 알게 된 그날 저녁부터, 카테리나 이바노브나, 그날 저녁부터 우리는 친구가 됐습니다……."], ["Here are twenty roubles, I think--and if that can be of any assistance to you, then... I...", "이제 제가……, 무언가를 하도록 허락해 주십시오……, 죽은 친구에게 진 빚을 갚도록 말입니다."], ["in short, I will come again, I will be sure to come again... I shall, perhaps, come again to-morrow....", "여기 이십 루블쯤 있습니다 — 이게 당신께 조금이나마 보탬이 된다면, 그럼……, 저는……, 요컨대, 다시 오겠습니다, 꼭 다시 오겠습니다……, 어쩌면 내일 또 올지도 모르고요……."], ["Good-bye!”", "안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["And he went quickly out of the room, squeezing his way through the crowd to the stairs.", "그러고는 그는 군중을 헤치며 계단 쪽으로 비집고 나가, 재빨리 방을 나섰다."], ["But in the crowd he suddenly jostled against Nikodim Fomitch, who had heard of the accident and had come to give instructions in person.", "그런데 군중 속에서 그는 불현듯 니코딤 포미치와 부딪혔다. 그는 사고 소식을 듣고 몸소 지시를 내리러 온 참이었다."], ["They had not met since the scene at the police station, but Nikodim Fomitch knew him instantly.", "두 사람은 경찰서에서의 그 소동 이후로 만난 적이 없었지만, 니코딤 포미치는 그를 단번에 알아보았다."]], [["“Ah, is that you?”", "\"아, 당신이오?\""], ["he asked him.", "그가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“He’s dead,” answered Raskolnikov.", "\"그분은 돌아가셨습니다.\""], ["“The doctor and the priest have been, all as it should have been.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다. \"의사도 신부도 다녀갔고, 모든 게 마땅한 대로 되었습니다."], ["Don’t worry the poor woman too much, she is in consumption as it is.", "저 가엾은 부인을 너무 괴롭히지 마세요, 안 그래도 폐병을 앓고 있으니까요."], ["Try and cheer her up, if possible... you are a kind-hearted man, I know...”", "되도록 기운을 북돋워 주세요……, 당신은 마음씨 고운 분이니까요, 제가 알지요…….\""], ["he added with a smile, looking straight in his face.", "그가 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 미소를 띤 채 덧붙였다."]], [["“But you are spattered with blood,”", "\"한데 당신, 피가 튀어 있구려.\""], ["observed Nikodim Fomitch, noticing in the lamplight some fresh stains on Raskolnikov’s waistcoat.", "니코딤 포미치가 등불 빛에 라스콜니코프의 조끼에 묻은 신선한 핏자국 몇 개를 알아채고 말했다."]], [["“Yes... I’m covered with blood,”", "\"네……, 온통 피투성이입니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov said with a peculiar air; then he smiled, nodded and went downstairs.", "라스콜니코프가 묘한 태도로 말했다. 그러고는 미소 짓고, 고개를 끄덕이고는 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["He walked down slowly and deliberately, feverish but not conscious of it, entirely absorbed in a new overwhelming sensation of life and strength that surged up suddenly within him.", "그는 천천히, 신중하게 내려갔다, 열에 들떠 있었으나 스스로는 그것을 의식하지 못한 채, 불현듯 마음속에 솟구쳐 오른 삶과 힘에 대한 새롭고도 압도적인 감각에 온통 사로잡혀서."], ["This sensation might be compared to that of a man condemned to death who has suddenly been pardoned.", "이 감각은 사형 선고를 받았다가 별안간 사면된 사람의 감각에 견줄 만했다. 계단을 반쯤 내려왔을 때 집으로 돌아가던 신부가 그를 앞질렀다."], ["Halfway down the staircase he was overtaken by the priest on his way home; Raskolnikov let him pass, exchanging a silent greeting with him.", "라스콜니코프는 그와 말없이 인사를 주고받으며 그를 먼저 보냈다. 그가 막 마지막 계단을 내려서던 참에, 등 뒤에서 빠른 발소리가 들렸다."], ["He was just descending the last steps when he heard rapid footsteps behind him.", "누군가 그를 따라잡았다. 폴렌카였다."], ["Someone overtook him; it was Polenka.", "그녀는 \"기다려요! 기다려요!\""], ["She was running after him, calling “Wait! wait!”", "하고 부르며 그를 뒤쫓아 달려오고 있었다."]], [["He turned round.", "그는 돌아보았다."], ["She was at the bottom of the staircase and stopped short a step above him.", "그녀는 계단 맨 아래에 서서, 그보다 한 단 위에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["A dim light came in from the yard.", "안뜰에서 희미한 빛이 새어 들었다."], ["Raskolnikov could distinguish the child’s thin but pretty little face, looking at him with a bright childish smile.", "라스콜니코프는 그 아이의 야위었지만 예쁘장한 자그마한 얼굴이, 환한 어린아이의 미소를 띠고 자기를 바라보고 있는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["She had run after him with a message which she was evidently glad to give.", "그녀는 무언가 전갈을 가지고 그를 뒤쫓아 온 것이었는데, 그것을 전하게 된 것이 분명 기쁜 듯했다."]], [["“Tell me, what is your name?... and where do you live?”", "\"이름이 뭐예요?……, 그리고 어디 사세요?\""], ["she said hurriedly in a breathless voice.", "그녀가 숨 가쁜 목소리로 다급하게 물었다."]], [["He laid both hands on her shoulders and looked at her with a sort of rapture.", "그는 두 손을 그녀의 어깨에 얹고, 일종의 황홀감에 젖어 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["It was such a joy to him to look at her, he could not have said why.", "그녀를 바라보는 것이 그에게는 어찌나 기쁜지, 그 까닭은 그 자신도 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Who sent you?”", "\"누가 보냈니?\""]], [["“Sister Sonia sent me,”", "\"소냐 언니가 보냈어요.\""], ["answered the girl, smiling still more brightly.", "여자아이가 더욱 환하게 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“I knew it was sister Sonia sent you.”", "\"소냐 언니가 보냈을 줄 알았다.\""]], [["“Mamma sent me, too...", "\"엄마도 보냈어요……, 소냐 언니가 날 보내려고 할 때, 엄마도 다가와서 '얼른 뛰어가, 폴렌카'"], ["when sister Sonia was sending me, mamma came up, too, and said ‘Run fast, Polenka.’”", "하고 말했거든요.\""]], [["“Do you love sister Sonia?”", "\"소냐 언니를 사랑하니?\""]], [["“I love her more than anyone,”", "\"누구보다도 사랑해요.\""], ["Polenka answered with a peculiar earnestness, and her smile became graver.", "폴렌카가 묘하게 진지한 태도로 대답했고, 그녀의 미소는 더 엄숙해졌다."]], [["“And will you love me?”", "\"그럼 나도 사랑해 주겠니?\""]], [["By way of answer he saw the little girl’s face approaching him, her full lips naïvely held out to kiss him.", "대답 대신, 그는 그 여자아이의 얼굴이 자기에게 다가오는 것을, 입맞추려고 통통한 입술을 천진하게 내미는 것을 보았다."], ["Suddenly her arms as thin as sticks held him tightly, her head rested on his shoulder and the little girl wept softly, pressing her face against him.", "별안간 막대기처럼 가는 그녀의 두 팔이 그를 꼭 끌어안았고, 머리가 그의 어깨에 기대어졌으며, 어린 여자아이는 그에게 얼굴을 묻은 채 가만히 흐느꼈다."]], [["“I am sorry for father,”", "\"아빠가 불쌍해요.\""], ["she said a moment later, raising her tear-stained face and brushing away the tears with her hands.", "잠시 후 그녀가 눈물범벅이 된 얼굴을 들고 손으로 눈물을 훔치며 말했다."], ["“It’s nothing but misfortunes now,”", "\"이제 온통 불행한 일들뿐이에요.\""], ["she added suddenly with that peculiarly sedate air which children try hard to assume when they want to speak like grown-up people.", "그녀가 갑자기, 아이들이 어른처럼 말하고 싶을 때 애써 꾸며 내는 그 유난히 점잖은 태도로 덧붙였다."]], [["“Did your father love you?”", "\"아빠가 너를 사랑하셨니?\""]], [["“He loved Lida most,”", "\"아빠는 리다를 제일 사랑하셨어요.\""], ["she went on very seriously without a smile, exactly like grown-up people, “he loved her because she is little and because she is ill, too.", "그녀는 미소도 없이, 어른들과 똑같이 아주 진지하게 말을 이었다. \"리다가 어리고, 또 아프기도 해서 사랑하셨어요."], ["And he always used to bring her presents.", "그리고 늘 그 애한테 선물을 가져다주셨어요."], ["But he taught us to read and me grammar and scripture, too,”", "하지만 아빠는 우리한테 읽기를 가르쳐 주셨고, 나한테는 문법이랑 성경도 가르쳐 주셨어요.\""], ["she added with dignity.", "그녀가 의젓하게 덧붙였다."], ["“And mother never used to say anything, but we knew that she liked it and father knew it, too.", "\"그리고 엄마는 한 번도 아무 말 안 하셨지만, 우린 엄마가 그걸 좋아한다는 걸 알았고, 아빠도 그걸 아셨어요."], ["And mother wants to teach me French, for it’s time my education began.”", "그리고 엄마는 나한테 프랑스어를 가르쳐 주고 싶어 하세요, 이제 내 교육을 시작할 때가 됐으니까요.\""]], [["“And do you know your prayers?”", "\"넌 기도문도 아니?\""]], [["“Of course, we do!", "\"그럼요, 알지요!"], ["We knew them long ago.", "오래전부터 알았어요."], ["I say my prayers to myself as I am a big girl now, but Kolya and Lida say them aloud with mother.", "난 이제 다 큰 애니까 기도를 혼자 마음속으로 외워요, 콜랴랑 리다는 엄마하고 소리 내어 외우고요."], ["First they repeat the ‘Ave Maria’ and then another prayer: ‘Lord, forgive and bless sister Sonia,’ and then another, ‘Lord, forgive and bless our second father.’ For our elder father is dead and this is another one, but we do pray for the other as well.”", "먼저 '성모송'을 외우고, 그다음에 또 다른 기도를 해요, '주여, 소냐 언니를 용서하시고 축복하소서', 그리고 또 하나, '주여, 우리의 둘째 아버지를 용서하시고 축복하소서.' 우리의 첫째 아버지는 돌아가셨고 이분은 또 다른 아버지거든요, 하지만 우린 그 첫째 아버지를 위해서도 기도해요.\""]], [["“Polenka, my name is Rodion.", "\"폴렌카, 내 이름은 로지온이란다."], ["Pray sometimes for me, too.", "가끔 나를 위해서도 기도해 주렴."], ["‘And Thy servant Rodion,’ nothing more.”", "'그리고 당신의 종 로지온을', 그것뿐이면 돼.\""]], [["“I’ll pray for you all the rest of my life,”", "\"평생토록 아저씨를 위해 기도할게요.\""], ["the little girl declared hotly, and suddenly smiling again she rushed at him and hugged him warmly once more.", "여자아이가 열렬하게 단언했고, 갑자기 다시 미소 지으며 그에게 달려들어 또 한 번 따뜻하게 끌어안았다."]], [["Raskolnikov told her his name and address and promised to be sure to come next day.", "라스콜니코프는 그녀에게 자기 이름과 주소를 일러 주고, 이튿날 꼭 오겠다고 약속했다."], ["The child went away quite enchanted with him.", "아이는 그에게 완전히 매혹되어 떠나갔다."], ["It was past ten when he came out into the street.", "그가 거리로 나왔을 때는 열 시가 지나 있었다."], ["In five minutes he was standing on the bridge at the spot where the woman had jumped in.", "오 분 뒤 그는 그 여인이 뛰어들었던 바로 그 자리, 다리 위에 서 있었다."]], [["“Enough,”", "\"이제 됐어.\""], ["he pronounced resolutely and triumphantly.", "그가 단호하고 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["“I’ve done with fancies, imaginary terrors and phantoms!", "\"공상도, 헛된 공포도, 망령도 다 끝장이다!"], ["Life is real!", "인생은 현실이야!"], ["haven’t I lived just now?", "내가 방금 살지 않았던가?"], ["My life has not yet died with that old woman!", "나의 삶은 저 노파와 함께 죽어 버린 게 아니야!"], ["The Kingdom of Heaven to her--and now enough, madam, leave me in peace!", "그녀에게는 천국을 — 그러니 이제 됐소, 부인, 날 좀 편히 내버려두시오!"], ["Now for the reign of reason and light... and of will, and of strength...", "이제부터는 이성과 빛의 시대다……, 그리고 의지의, 힘의 시대……, 자, 이제 두고 보자!"], ["and now we will see! We will try our strength!”", "우리의 힘을 시험해 보자!\""], ["he added defiantly, as though challenging some power of darkness.", "그는 마치 어떤 어둠의 힘에 도전하기라도 하듯 도전적으로 덧붙였다."], ["“And I was ready to consent to live in a square of space!", "\"그런데도 나는 한 평짜리 공간에서 사는 것에 동의할 작정이었다니!"]], [["“I am very weak at this moment, but... I believe my illness is all over.", "이 순간 나는 무척 쇠약하지만……, 내 병은 이제 다 끝난 것 같다."], ["I knew it would be over when I went out.", "밖에 나올 때부터 끝나리라는 걸 알았어."], ["By the way, Potchinkov’s house is only a few steps away.", "그건 그렇고, 포친코프의 집이 몇 걸음밖에 안 떨어져 있군."], ["I certainly must go to Razumihin even if it were not close by... let him win his bet!", "가까이 있지 않더라도 라주미힌한테는 꼭 가야겠어……, 그가 내기에서 이기게 해 주자!"], ["Let us give him some satisfaction, too--no matter!", "그에게도 좀 만족을 주자고 — 상관없어!"], ["Strength, strength is what one wants, you can get nothing without it, and strength must be won by strength--that’s what they don’t know,”", "힘이야, 사람한테 필요한 건 힘이지, 힘 없이는 아무것도 얻을 수 없어, 그리고 힘은 힘으로 얻어 내야 하는 거야 — 그게 바로 저들이 모르는 거지.\" 그가 자랑스럽고 자신만만하게 덧붙이고는, 맥 빠진 발걸음으로 다리에서 걸어 나왔다."], ["he added proudly and self-confidently and he walked with flagging footsteps from the bridge.", "자긍심과 자신감이 그의 안에서 끊임없이 더 강해졌다. 그는 매 순간 다른 사람이 되어 가고 있었다."], ["Pride and self-confidence grew continually stronger in him; he was becoming a different man every moment.", "도대체 무슨 일이 일어났기에 그의 안에서 이런 변혁이 일어난 것일까? 그 자신도 알지 못했다."], ["What was it had happened to work this revolution in him?", "지푸라기라도 붙드는 사람처럼, 그는 문득 자기 역시 '살 수 있다, 아직 자신에게 삶이 남아 있다, 자신의 삶은 저 노파와 함께 죽어 버린 게 아니다'"], ["He did not know himself; like a man catching at a straw, he suddenly felt that he, too, ‘could live, that there was still life for him, that his life had not died with the old woman.’ Perhaps he was in too great a hurry with his conclusions, but he did not think of that.", "하고 느꼈던 것이다. 어쩌면 그는 너무 성급하게 결론을 내린 것인지도 몰랐으나, 그는 그것을 생각하지 않았다."]], [["“But I did ask her to remember ‘Thy servant Rodion’ in her prayers,”", "\"하지만 나는 그 애한테 기도할 때 '당신의 종 로지온'을 기억해 달라고 부탁했지.\""], ["the idea struck him.", "문득 이런 생각이 그를 스쳤다."], ["“Well, that was... in case of emergency,”", "\"뭐, 그건……, 만일을 위해서였어.\""], ["he added and laughed himself at his boyish sally.", "그가 덧붙이고는, 자신의 소년 같은 객기에 스스로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["He was in the best of spirits.", "그는 더없이 좋은 기분이었다."]], [["He easily found Razumihin; the new lodger was already known at Potchinkov’s and the porter at once showed him the way.", "그는 라주미힌을 쉽게 찾아냈다. 새 세입자는 이미 포친코프의 집에 알려져 있어서 문지기가 곧바로 길을 일러 주었다."], ["Half-way upstairs he could hear the noise and animated conversation of a big gathering of people.", "계단을 반쯤 올라갔을 때, 그는 많은 사람이 모여 떠들썩하게 활기차게 이야기하는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["The door was wide open on the stairs; he could hear exclamations and discussion.", "계단 쪽으로 난 문은 활짝 열려 있었고, 탄성과 논쟁 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Razumihin’s room was fairly large; the company consisted of fifteen people.", "라주미힌의 방은 제법 넓었다. 모인 사람은 열다섯 명쯤이었다."], ["Raskolnikov stopped in the entry, where two of the landlady’s servants were busy behind a screen with two samovars, bottles, plates and dishes of pie and savouries, brought up from the landlady’s kitchen.", "라스콜니코프는 현관에 멈춰 섰는데, 그곳에서는 집주인의 하녀 둘이 칸막이 뒤에서 사모바르 두 개며 술병, 접시, 그리고 집주인의 부엌에서 가져온 파이와 안주 접시들을 가지고 분주히 움직이고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov sent in for Razumihin. He ran out delighted.", "라스콜니코프는 사람을 들여보내 라주미힌을 불렀다."], ["At the first glance it was apparent that he had had a great deal to drink and, though no amount of liquor made Razumihin quite drunk, this time he was perceptibly affected by it.", "그가 반색하며 뛰어나왔다. 한눈에 보기에도 그는 술을 잔뜩 마신 게 분명했고, 아무리 마셔도 라주미힌이 완전히 취하는 법은 없었지만, 이번에는 눈에 띄게 술기운이 올라 있었다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 봐.\""], ["Raskolnikov hastened to say, “I’ve only just come to tell you you’ve won your bet and that no one really knows what may not happen to him.", "라스콜니코프가 서둘러 말했다. \"난 그저 네가 내기에서 이겼다는 것, 그리고 사람한테 무슨 일이 안 일어날지는 정말 아무도 모른다는 걸 말하러 온 거야."], ["I can’t come in; I am so weak that I shall fall down directly.", "난 못 들어가, 너무 쇠약해서 금방 쓰러질 것 같아."], ["And so good evening and good-bye!", "그러니 잘 있어, 안녕!"], ["Come and see me to-morrow.”", "내일 나한테 와.\""]], [["“Do you know what?", "\"무슨 소리야?"], ["I’ll see you home.", "내가 집까지 바래다줄게."], ["If you say you’re weak yourself, you must...”", "네 입으로 쇠약하다면서, 그럼 더더욱…….\""]], [["“And your visitors?", "\"그럼 네 손님들은?"], ["Who is the curly-headed one who has just peeped out?”", "방금 빠끔히 내다본 그 곱슬머리는 누구야?\""]], [["“He? Goodness only knows!", "\"그 사람?"], ["Some friend of uncle’s, I expect, or perhaps he has come without being invited...", "누군지 누가 알겠어! 아마 삼촌 친구겠지, 아니면 초대도 안 했는데 온 건지도……."], ["I’ll leave uncle with them, he is an invaluable person, pity I can’t introduce you to him now.", "삼촌은 저 사람들한테 맡겨 두지, 그분은 둘도 없이 귀한 양반이야, 지금 너한테 소개 못 시켜 주는 게 아쉽군."], ["But confound them all now!", "하지만 이젠 다들 될 대로 되라지!"], ["They won’t notice me, and I need a little fresh air, for you’ve come just in the nick of time--another two minutes and I should have come to blows!", "저들은 날 신경도 안 쓸 테고, 난 바람 좀 쐬야겠어, 마침 네가 딱 맞춰 와 줬어 — 이 분만 더 있었으면 주먹다짐이 났을 거야!"], ["They are talking such a lot of wild stuff... you simply can’t imagine what men will say!", "어찌나 허튼소리들을 늘어놓는지……, 사람들이 무슨 말을 지껄여 댈지 넌 상상도 못 할 거야!"], ["Though why shouldn’t you imagine?", "하긴 왜 상상을 못 하겠어?"], ["Don’t we talk nonsense ourselves? And let them...", "우리도 헛소리를 지껄이지 않나?"], ["that’s the way to learn not to!... Wait a minute, I’ll fetch Zossimov.”", "그러니 떠들게 내버려둬……, 그래야 안 떠드는 법을 배우지!……, 잠깐만, 조시모프를 데려올게.\""]], [["Zossimov pounced upon Raskolnikov almost greedily; he showed a special interest in him; soon his face brightened.", "조시모프는 거의 게걸스럽다시피 라스콜니코프에게 달려들었다. 그는 그에게 각별한 관심을 보였다. 이내 그의 얼굴이 밝아졌다."]], [["“You must go to bed at once,”", "\"당장 잠자리에 들어야 합니다.\""], ["he pronounced, examining the patient as far as he could, “and take something for the night.", "그가 가능한 한 환자를 살펴보고는 말했다. \"그리고 밤에 먹을 걸 좀 들어요."], ["Will you take it?", "드시겠어요?"], ["I got it ready some time ago... a powder.”", "진작에 준비해 뒀거든요……, 가루약입니다.\""]], [["“Two, if you like,”", "\"두 봉지라도 드리지요.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."], ["The powder was taken at once.", "가루약을 그 자리에서 먹었다."]], [["“It’s a good thing you are taking him home,”", "\"자네가 그를 집에 데려가는 건 잘된 일이야.\""], ["observed Zossimov to Razumihin--“we shall see how he is to-morrow, to-day he’s not at all amiss--a considerable change since the afternoon.", "조시모프가 라주미힌에게 말했다. \"내일 그가 어떤지 봅시다, 오늘은 전혀 나쁘지 않아 — 낮보다 상당히 좋아졌어."], ["Live and learn...”", "살다 보면 배우는 법이니…….\""]], [["“Do you know what Zossimov whispered to me when we were coming out?”", "\"우리가 나올 때 조시모프가 나한테 뭐라고 속삭였는지 알아?\""], ["Razumihin blurted out, as soon as they were in the street.", "둘이 거리로 나서자마자 라주미힌이 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["“I won’t tell you everything, brother, because they are such fools.", "\"다는 얘기 안 할게, 형제, 워낙 바보 같은 소리라서."], ["Zossimov told me to talk freely to you on the way and get you to talk freely to me, and afterwards I am to tell him about it, for he’s got a notion in his head that you are... mad or close on it.", "조시모프가 그러는데, 가는 길에 너랑 허물없이 얘기를 나누면서 너도 나한테 허물없이 털어놓게 만든 다음, 나중에 그걸 자기한테 얘기해 달라는 거야, 글쎄 그자 머릿속에 네가……, 미쳤거나 미치기 직전이라는 생각이 박혀 있거든."], ["Only fancy!", "상상이나 되냐!"], ["In the first place, you’ve three times the brains he has; in the second, if you are not mad, you needn’t care a hang that he has got such a wild idea; and thirdly, that piece of beef whose specialty is surgery has gone mad on mental diseases, and what’s brought him to this conclusion about you was your conversation to-day with Zametov.”", "첫째로, 넌 그자보다 머리가 세 배는 좋고, 둘째로, 네가 미친 게 아니라면 그자가 그런 엉뚱한 생각을 품든 말든 콧방귀도 안 뀌면 그만이고, 셋째로, 전공이 외과인 그 고깃덩어리가 정신병에 푹 빠져 버린 거지, 그리고 그자가 너에 대해 그런 결론을 내리게 된 건 오늘 네가 자메토프랑 나눈 대화 때문이야.\""]], [["“Zametov told you all about it?”", "\"자메토프가 너한테 다 얘기했어?\""]], [["“Yes, and he did well. Now I understand what it all means and so does Zametov....", "\"응, 잘했지."], ["Well, the fact is, Rodya... the point is...", "이제 나도 이게 다 무슨 영문인지 알겠고, 자메토프도 알아……."], ["I am a little drunk now.... But that’s...", "그러니까, 사실은 말이야, 로쟈……, 요점은……, 나 지금 좀 취했어……."], ["no matter... the point is that this idea...", "하지만 그건……, 상관없고……, 요점은 이 생각이……, 알겠어?"], ["you understand? was just being hatched in their brains...", "저들 머릿속에서 막 부화하고 있었다는 거야……, 알겠어?"], ["you understand? That is, no one ventured to say it aloud, because the idea is too absurd and especially since the arrest of that painter, that bubble’s burst and gone for ever.", "그러니까, 아무도 감히 소리 내어 말하진 못했어, 그 생각이 너무 터무니없는 데다, 특히 그 페인트공이 체포된 뒤로는 그 거품이 터져 영영 사라져 버렸으니까."], ["But why are they such fools?", "그런데 저들은 왜 그렇게 바보 같은 거지?"], ["I gave Zametov a bit of a thrashing at the time--that’s between ourselves, brother; please don’t let out a hint that you know of it; I’ve noticed he is a ticklish subject; it was at Luise Ivanovna’s.", "그때 내가 자메토프를 좀 두들겨 줬어 — 이건 우리끼리 얘기야, 형제, 제발 내가 알고 있다는 티는 내지 마, 보니까 그자가 민감한 인물이더라고, 루이자 이바노브나 가게에서였어."], ["But to-day, to-day it’s all cleared up.", "하지만 오늘, 오늘로 다 밝혀졌어."], ["That Ilya Petrovitch is at the bottom of it!", "그 일리야 페트로비치가 죄다 꾸민 거라고!"], ["He took advantage of your fainting at the police station, but he is ashamed of it himself now; I know that...”", "그자가 네가 경찰서에서 기절한 걸 이용해 먹었지, 하지만 이제 자기도 그걸 부끄러워하고 있어, 나도 알아…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov listened greedily.", "라스콜니코프는 게걸스레 귀를 기울였다."], ["Razumihin was drunk enough to talk too freely.", "라주미힌은 입이 너무 헤퍼질 만큼 취해 있었다."]], [["“I fainted then because it was so close and the smell of paint,”", "\"그때 내가 기절한 건 너무 답답한 데다 페인트 냄새 때문이었어.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["“No need to explain that!", "\"그걸 굳이 설명할 필요 없어!"], ["And it wasn’t the paint only: the fever had been coming on for a month; Zossimov testifies to that!", "게다가 페인트 때문만도 아니었어, 열이 한 달째 오르고 있었으니까, 조시모프가 그 증인이야!"], ["But how crushed that boy is now, you wouldn’t believe!", "근데 그 친구가 지금 얼마나 풀이 죽었는지 넌 믿지 못할 거야!"], ["‘I am not worth his little finger,’ he says.", "'난 그 사람 새끼손가락만도 못해' 하고 말한다니까."], ["Yours, he means.", "너 말이야."], ["He has good feelings at times, brother.", "그 친구도 가끔은 마음씨가 괜찮아, 형제."], ["But the lesson, the lesson you gave him to-day in the Palais de Cristal, that was too good for anything!", "하지만 그 교훈, 오늘 네가 팔레 드 크리스탈에서 그자한테 준 그 교훈, 그건 정말이지 둘도 없이 멋졌어!"], ["You frightened him at first, you know, he nearly went into convulsions!", "처음엔 네가 그자를 겁먹게 했지, 알잖아, 그자가 거의 경련을 일으킬 뻔했다고!"], ["You almost convinced him again of the truth of all that hideous nonsense, and then you suddenly--put out your tongue at him: ‘There now, what do you make of it?’ It was perfect!", "넌 그 끔찍한 허튼소리가 죄다 사실이라고 그자를 다시금 거의 믿게 만들어 놓고는, 별안간 — 그자한테 혀를 쑥 내밀었지. '자, 어때, 이걸 어떻게 생각하나?'"], ["He is crushed, annihilated now!", "완벽했어! 그자는 이제 짓뭉개지고 박살이 났어!"], ["It was masterly, by Jove, it’s what they deserve!", "정말이지 명수다웠어, 맙소사, 저들한테는 그게 딱이지!"], ["Ah, that I wasn’t there!", "아, 내가 그 자리에 없었다니!"], ["He was hoping to see you awfully.", "그자는 너를 무지하게 보고 싶어 했어."], ["Porfiry, too, wants to make your acquaintance...”", "포르피리도 너랑 사귀고 싶어 하고…….\""]], [["“Ah!... he too... but why did they put me down as mad?”", "\"아!……, 그 사람도……, 한데 왜 날 미친 사람 취급한 거야?\""]], [["“Oh, not mad. I must have said too much, brother....", "\"아, 미쳤다는 게 아니라."], ["What struck him, you see, was that only that subject seemed to interest you; now it’s clear why it did interest you; knowing all the circumstances...", "내가 말이 좀 지나쳤나 봐, 형제……."], ["and how that irritated you and worked in with your illness... I am a little drunk, brother, only, confound him, he has some idea of his own...", "그자한테 충격이었던 건 말이야, 알겠지만, 오직 그 주제 하나에만 네가 관심 있어 보였다는 거였어, 이제는 왜 거기에 관심을 가졌는지 분명해졌지, 모든 정황을 알고 보니……, 그게 어떻게 너를 자극했고, 또 네 병하고 맞물려 들어갔는지 말이야……, 나 좀 취했어, 형제, 다만, 젠장, 그자한테는 자기 나름의 생각이 있어서……, 글쎄 그자가 정신병에 미쳐 있다니까."], ["I tell you, he’s mad on mental diseases. But don’t you mind him...”", "하지만 넌 그자 신경 쓰지 마…….\""]], [["For half a minute both were silent.", "삼십 초쯤 둘 다 말이 없었다."]], [["“Listen, Razumihin,” began Raskolnikov, “I want to tell you plainly: I’ve just been at a death-bed, a clerk who died...", "\"들어 봐, 라주미힌.\""], ["I gave them all my money...", "라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다."], ["and besides I’ve just been kissed by someone who, if I had killed anyone, would just the same... in fact I saw someone else there...", "\"너한테 솔직히 말하고 싶어."], ["with a flame-coloured feather... but I am talking nonsense; I am very weak, support me...", "난 방금 임종 자리에 있었어, 어떤 관리가 죽었거든……, 거기에 내 돈을 다 줬어……, 게다가 방금 누군가한테 입맞춤을 받았는데, 내가 만일 누구를 죽였다 해도 그 사람은 똑같이……, 사실 거기서 또 다른 누군가를 봤어……, 불꽃 같은 깃털을 단……, 아니 내가 헛소리를 하는군."], ["we shall be at the stairs directly...”", "난 무척 쇠약해, 좀 부축해 줘……, 곧 계단에 도착할 거야…….\""]], [["“What’s the matter?", "\"왜 그래?"], ["What’s the matter with you?”", "도대체 무슨 일이야?\""], ["Razumihin asked anxiously.", "라주미힌이 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["“I am a little giddy, but that’s not the point, I am so sad, so sad... like a woman.", "\"좀 어지러워, 하지만 그게 문제가 아니야, 너무 슬퍼, 너무 슬프다고……, 여자처럼 말이야."], ["Look, what’s that?", "봐, 저게 뭐지?"], ["Look, look!”", "봐, 저것 좀 봐!\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭔데?\""]], [["“Don’t you see?", "\"안 보여?"], ["A light in my room, you see?", "내 방에 불빛이 있잖아, 보이지?"], ["Through the crack...”", "저 틈으로…….\""]], [["They were already at the foot of the last flight of stairs, at the level of the landlady’s door, and they could, as a fact, see from below that there was a light in Raskolnikov’s garret.", "그들은 어느새 마지막 계단 층계 밑, 집주인의 문이 있는 높이에 이르러 있었고, 실제로 아래에서도 라스콜니코프의 다락방에 불빛이 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“Queer!", "\"이상하군!"], ["Nastasya, perhaps,”", "나스타시야인지도 모르지.\""], ["observed Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 말했다."]], [["“She is never in my room at this time and she must be in bed long ago, but... I don’t care!", "\"그 여자는 이 시간에 절대 내 방에 안 와, 진작에 잠자리에 들었을 텐데, 하지만……, 아무래도 상관없어!"], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있어!\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 소리야?"], ["I am coming with you, we’ll come in together!”", "나도 너랑 같이 들어가, 우리 같이 들어가자고!\""]], [["“I know we are going in together, but I want to shake hands here and say good-bye to you here.", "\"같이 들어갈 거라는 거 알아, 하지만 난 여기서 악수하고 너한테 작별 인사를 하고 싶어."], ["So give me your hand, good-bye!”", "그러니 손을 줘, 잘 있어!\""]], [["“What’s the matter with you, Rodya?”", "\"너 도대체 왜 그래, 로쟈?\""]], [["“Nothing... come along... you shall be witness.”", "\"아무것도 아니야……, 가자……, 네가 증인이 돼 줘.\""]], [["They began mounting the stairs, and the idea struck Razumihin that perhaps Zossimov might be right after all.", "그들은 계단을 오르기 시작했고, 라주미힌은 어쩌면 조시모프의 말이 결국 옳을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["“Ah, I’ve upset him with my chatter!”", "\"아, 내 수다로 저 친구를 들쑤셔 놨군!\""], ["he muttered to himself.", "그가 혼자 중얼거렸다."]], [["When they reached the door they heard voices in the room.", "문 앞에 이르렀을 때 그들은 방 안에서 사람 목소리를 들었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"이게 뭐지?\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."], ["Raskolnikov was the first to open the door; he flung it wide and stood still in the doorway, dumbfoundered.", "라스콜니코프가 먼저 문을 열었다. 그는 문을 활짝 젖히고는, 어안이 벙벙하여 문간에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["His mother and sister were sitting on his sofa and had been waiting an hour and a half for him.", "그의 어머니와 누이가 그의 소파에 앉아, 한 시간 반째 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Why had he never expected, never thought of them, though the news that they had started, were on their way and would arrive immediately, had been repeated to him only that day?", "그들이 출발했고, 오는 중이며, 곧 도착하리라는 소식을 바로 그날 거듭 들었건만, 어째서 그는 한 번도 그들을 예상하지 못하고, 생각조차 하지 않았던 것일까?"], ["They had spent that hour and a half plying Nastasya with questions.", "그들은 그 한 시간 반 동안 나스타시야를 붙들고 이것저것 캐물으며 보냈다."], ["She was standing before them and had told them everything by now.", "그녀는 그들 앞에 서서 이미 모든 것을 다 이야기한 터였다."], ["They were beside themselves with alarm when they heard of his “running away” to-day, ill and, as they understood from her story, delirious!", "그가 오늘 병든 몸으로, 그녀의 이야기로 미루어 보건대 헛소리까지 하면서 '뛰쳐나갔다'는 말을 듣고 그들은 불안에 어쩔 줄 몰라 했다."], ["“Good Heavens, what had become of him?”", "\"맙소사, 그애한테 무슨 일이 생긴 거지?\""], ["Both had been weeping, both had been in anguish for that hour and a half.", "두 사람 다 울었고, 두 사람 다 그 한 시간 반 동안 비탄에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["A cry of joy, of ecstasy, greeted Raskolnikov’s entrance.", "기쁨에 찬, 환희에 찬 외침이 라스콜니코프의 등장을 맞이했다."], ["Both rushed to him.", "두 사람이 그에게 달려들었다."], ["But he stood like one dead; a sudden intolerable sensation struck him like a thunderbolt.", "그러나 그는 죽은 사람처럼 서 있었다. 별안간 견딜 수 없는 감각이 벼락처럼 그를 내리쳤다."], ["He did not lift his arms to embrace them, he could not.", "그는 그들을 끌어안으려고 팔을 들지 못했다, 그럴 수가 없었다."], ["His mother and sister clasped him in their arms, kissed him, laughed and cried.", "어머니와 누이가 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고, 입맞추고, 웃고 울었다."], ["He took a step, tottered and fell to the ground, fainting.", "그는 한 걸음 내딛더니, 휘청거리다가 바닥에 쓰러져 기절하고 말았다."]], [["Anxiety, cries of horror, moans...", "불안, 공포의 외침, 신음 소리……."], ["Razumihin who was standing in the doorway flew into the room, seized the sick man in his strong arms and in a moment had him on the sofa.", "문간에 서 있던 라주미힌이 방 안으로 날 듯이 뛰어들어, 억센 두 팔로 환자를 붙잡아 순식간에 소파에 눕혔다."]], [["“It’s nothing, nothing!”", "\"괜찮습니다, 아무것도 아니에요!\""], ["he cried to the mother and sister--“it’s only a faint, a mere trifle!", "그가 어머니와 누이에게 외쳤다. \"그저 기절일 뿐, 별것 아닙니다!"], ["Only just now the doctor said he was much better, that he is perfectly well!", "방금도 의사가 그가 훨씬 좋아졌다고, 완전히 건강하다고 했는걸요!"], ["Water!", "물!"], ["See, he is coming to himself, he is all right again!”", "보세요, 정신이 돌아오고 있어요, 다시 멀쩡해졌어요!\""]], [["And seizing Dounia by the arm so that he almost dislocated it, he made her bend down to see that “he is all right again.”", "그러고는 두냐의 팔을 어찌나 세게 붙잡았는지 거의 탈구시킬 뻔하면서, 그는 그녀에게 '그가 다시 멀쩡해졌다'는 것을 보라고 몸을 굽히게 했다."], ["The mother and sister looked on him with emotion and gratitude, as their Providence.", "어머니와 누이는 그를 자신들의 섭리처럼 여기며 감격과 감사의 눈길로 바라보았다."], ["They had heard already from Nastasya all that had been done for their Rodya during his illness, by this “very competent young man,” as Pulcheria Alexandrovna Raskolnikov called him that evening in conversation with Dounia.", "그들은 이미 나스타시야에게서, 그들의 로쟈가 병을 앓는 동안 이 '무척 유능한 젊은이'가 그를 위해 해 준 모든 일을 들어 알고 있었으니, 그날 저녁 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나 라스콜니코바는 두냐와 이야기하면서 그를 그렇게 불렀던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["Raskolnikov got up, and sat down on the sofa.", "라스콜니코프는 일어나 소파에 걸터앉았다."], ["He waved his hand weakly to Razumihin to cut short the flow of warm and incoherent consolations he was addressing to his mother and sister, took them both by the hand and for a minute or two gazed from one to the other without speaking.", "그는 라주미힌이 자기 어머니와 누이에게 쏟아 내던 따뜻하지만 두서없는 위로의 말들을 끊으려는 듯 힘없이 손을 내저었고, 두 사람의 손을 한 손씩 잡고는 일이 분 동안 아무 말 없이 한 사람씩 번갈아 바라보았다."], ["His mother was alarmed by his expression.", "어머니는 그의 표정에 놀랐다."], ["It revealed an emotion agonisingly poignant, and at the same time something immovable, almost insane.", "거기에는 고통스러우리만치 절절한 감정이, 그러면서도 동시에 무언가 미동도 없는, 거의 광기에 가까운 무언가가 드러나 있었다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna began to cry.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 울기 시작했다."]], [["Avdotya Romanovna was pale; her hand trembled in her brother’s.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 창백했다. 그녀의 손은 오라비의 손안에서 떨리고 있었다."]], [["“Go home...", "\"집에 가세요……"], ["with him,”", "저 사람이랑.\""], ["he said in a broken voice, pointing to Razumihin, “good-bye till to-morrow; to-morrow everything...", "그는 라주미힌을 가리키며 갈라진 목소리로 말했다. \"내일까지 안녕히……"], ["Is it long since you arrived?”", "내일 모든 것을……. 도착하신 지 오래되셨어요?\""]], [["“This evening, Rodya,”", "\"오늘 저녁이란다, 로쟈.\""], ["answered Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “the train was awfully late.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 대답했다. \"기차가 끔찍하게 연착했어."], ["But, Rodya, nothing would induce me to leave you now!", "하지만 로쟈, 그 무엇으로도 지금 너를 두고 갈 수는 없구나!"], ["I will spend the night here, near you...”", "여기서, 네 곁에서 밤을 새우마…….\""]], [["“Don’t torture me!”", "\"저를 괴롭히지 마세요!\""], ["he said with a gesture of irritation.", "그는 짜증스레 손짓하며 말했다."]], [["“I will stay with him,”", "\"내가 이 친구랑 있을게요.\""], ["cried Razumihin, “I won’t leave him for a moment.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다. \"한순간도 곁을 떠나지 않겠습니다."], ["Bother all my visitors!", "저쪽 손님들이야 다 집어치우라죠!"], ["Let them rage to their hearts’ content!", "실컷 화내라고 내버려 두세요!"], ["My uncle is presiding there.”", "거긴 제 삼촌이 주재하고 계시니까요.\""]], [["“How, how can I thank you!”", "\"어떻게, 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지!\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was beginning, once more pressing Razumihin’s hands, but Raskolnikov interrupted her again.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 다시 한번 라주미힌의 두 손을 꼭 쥐며 말을 꺼냈으나, 라스콜니코프가 또다시 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“I can’t have it!", "\"이건 못 견디겠어요!"], ["I can’t have it!”", "못 견디겠다고요!\""], ["he repeated irritably, “don’t worry me!", "그는 신경질적으로 되풀이했다. \"저를 들볶지 마세요!"], ["Enough, go away...", "그만하면 됐어요, 가세요……."], ["I can’t stand it!”", "도저히 못 참겠어요!\""]], [["“Come, mamma, come out of the room at least for a minute,”", "\"가요, 엄마, 잠깐만이라도 방에서 나가요.\""], ["Dounia whispered in dismay; “we are distressing him, that’s evident.”", "두냐가 당황하여 속삭였다. \"우리가 오빠를 힘들게 하고 있어요, 그게 분명해요.\""]], [["“Mayn’t I look at him after three years?”", "\"삼 년 만에 아들 얼굴도 못 본단 말이냐?\""], ["wept Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 울먹였다."]], [["“Stay,”", "\"잠깐만요.\""], ["he stopped them again, “you keep interrupting me, and my ideas get muddled....", "그가 다시 그들을 멈춰 세웠다. \"자꾸 말을 끊으시니까 제 생각이 뒤죽박죽이 돼서요……."], ["Have you seen Luzhin?”", "루진은 만나셨어요?\""]], [["“No, Rodya, but he knows already of our arrival.", "\"아니다, 로쟈, 하지만 그분은 벌써 우리가 도착한 걸 알고 계셔."], ["We have heard, Rodya, that Pyotr Petrovitch was so kind as to visit you today,”", "로쟈, 우리가 들었는데, 표트르 페트로비치가 친절하게도 오늘 너를 찾아왔다더구나.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna added somewhat timidly.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 다소 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes...", "\"네……"], ["he was so kind...", "그렇게나 친절하셨죠……."], ["Dounia, I promised Luzhin I’d throw him downstairs and told him to go to hell....”", "두냐, 나는 루진한테 계단 아래로 던져 버리겠다고 약속하고는 지옥에나 가라고 했어…….\""]], [["“Rodya, what are you saying!", "\"로쟈, 무슨 소리를 하는 거니!"], ["Surely, you don’t mean to tell us...”", "설마, 우리한테 그런 말을 하려는 건…….\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna began in alarm, but she stopped, looking at Dounia.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 놀라서 입을 떼다가, 두냐를 바라보며 말을 멈췄다."]], [["Avdotya Romanovna was looking attentively at her brother, waiting for what would come next.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 기다리며 오라비를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["Both of them had heard of the quarrel from Nastasya, so far as she had succeeded in understanding and reporting it, and were in painful perplexity and suspense.", "두 사람은 나스타시야에게서 그 다툼에 관한 이야기를 — 그녀가 알아듣고 전할 수 있었던 한도 내에서 — 이미 들은 터라, 괴로운 당혹감과 불안 속에 있었다."]], [["“Dounia,”", "\"두냐.\""], ["Raskolnikov continued with an effort, “I don’t want that marriage, so at the first opportunity to-morrow you must refuse Luzhin, so that we may never hear his name again.”", "라스콜니코프가 안간힘을 쓰며 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 결혼이 싫어, 그러니 내일 첫 기회가 닿는 대로 루진을 거절해 버려, 다시는 그자 이름을 듣지 않아도 되게.\""]], [["“Good Heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 부르짖었다."]], [["“Brother, think what you are saying!”", "\"오빠, 무슨 말을 하는지 좀 생각해 봐!\""], ["Avdotya Romanovna began impetuously, but immediately checked herself.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 발끈해서 말을 시작했으나, 곧바로 스스로를 다잡았다."], ["“You are not fit to talk now, perhaps; you are tired,”", "\"지금은 말할 상태가 아닐지도 몰라, 피곤한 거겠지.\""], ["she added gently.", "그녀가 부드럽게 덧붙였다."]], [["“You think I am delirious? No...", "\"내가 헛소리를 하는 줄 알아?"], ["You are marrying Luzhin for _my_ sake.", "아니야……. 너는 나를 위해서 루진과 결혼하려는 거잖아."], ["But I won’t accept the sacrifice.", "하지만 나는 그 희생을 받아들이지 않겠어."], ["And so write a letter before to-morrow, to refuse him...", "그러니 내일 전에 편지를 써서 그자를 거절해……."], ["Let me read it in the morning and that will be the end of it!”", "내일 아침에 내가 읽어 보게 해 줘, 그럼 그걸로 끝이야!\""]], [["“That I can’t do!”", "\"난 그렇게는 못 해!\""], ["the girl cried, offended, “what right have you...”", "처녀가 모욕감에 외쳤다. \"오빠가 무슨 권리로…….\""]], [["“Dounia, you are hasty, too, be quiet, to-morrow...", "\"두냐, 너도 너무 성급하구나, 조용히 하렴, 내일……."], ["Don’t you see...”", "너도 알다시피…….\""], ["the mother interposed in dismay.", "어머니가 당황하여 끼어들었다."], ["“Better come away!”", "\"차라리 나가는 게 낫겠다!\""]], [["“He is raving,”", "\"오빠가 헛소리를 하는 겁니다.\""], ["Razumihin cried tipsily, “or how would he dare!", "라주미힌이 취기 어린 목소리로 외쳤다. \"안 그러면 어떻게 감히 그런 소릴!"], ["To-morrow all this nonsense will be over...", "내일이면 이 모든 헛소리가 다 사라질 겁니다……."], ["to-day he certainly did drive him away.", "오늘 그가 확실히 그자를 쫓아낸 건 맞아요."], ["That was so.", "그건 그래요."], ["And Luzhin got angry, too....", "그래서 루진도 화가 났죠……."], ["He made speeches here, wanted to show off his learning and he went out crest-fallen....”", "여기서 일장 연설을 늘어놓으며 제 학식을 자랑하려 들더니, 풀이 죽어서 나가 버렸지요…….\""]], [["“Then it’s true?”", "\"그럼 사실이란 말인가요?\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."]], [["“Good-bye till to-morrow, brother,”", "\"내일까지 안녕히, 오빠.\""], ["said Dounia compassionately--“let us go, mother...", "두냐가 가엾다는 듯 말했다. \"가요, 엄마……."], ["Good-bye, Rodya.”", "잘 있어, 로쟈.\""]], [["“Do you hear, sister,”", "\"내 말 들었니, 누이야.\""], ["he repeated after them, making a last effort, “I am not delirious; this marriage is--an infamy.", "그가 마지막 힘을 쥐어짜며 그들의 등 뒤에 대고 되풀이했다. \"나는 헛소리를 하는 게 아니야."], ["Let me act like a scoundrel, but you mustn’t...", "이 결혼은 — 치욕이야. 나야 비열한 놈처럼 굴어도 되지만, 너는 안 돼……."], ["one is enough...", "한 사람으로 충분해……."], ["and though I am a scoundrel, I wouldn’t own such a sister.", "그리고 내가 비열한 놈이라 해도, 그런 누이는 인정하지 않겠어."], ["It’s me or Luzhin!", "나냐, 루진이냐 둘 중 하나야!"], ["Go now....”", "이제 가…….\""]], [["“But you’re out of your mind!", "\"하지만 자넨 제정신이 아니야!"], ["Despot!”", "폭군 같으니!\""], ["roared Razumihin; but Raskolnikov did not and perhaps could not answer.", "라주미힌이 고함쳤다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 대답하지 않았고, 어쩌면 대답할 수도 없었을 것이다."], ["He lay down on the sofa, and turned to the wall, utterly exhausted.", "그는 완전히 기진맥진한 채 소파에 누워 벽 쪽으로 돌아누웠다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna looked with interest at Razumihin; her black eyes flashed; Razumihin positively started at her glance.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 흥미로운 눈길로 라주미힌을 바라보았다. 그녀의 검은 눈이 번득였고, 라주미힌은 그 눈길에 그만 움찔하고 말았다."]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna stood overwhelmed.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 망연자실한 채 서 있었다."]], [["“Nothing would induce me to go,”", "\"그 무엇으로도 저는 갈 수가 없어요.\""], ["she whispered in despair to Razumihin.", "그녀가 절망에 빠져 라주미힌에게 속삭였다."], ["“I will stay somewhere here...", "\"여기 어디서라도 머물겠어요……."], ["escort Dounia home.”", "두냐만 집까지 데려다주세요.\""]], [["“You’ll spoil everything,”", "\"그러시면 모든 걸 망치게 됩니다.\""], ["Razumihin answered in the same whisper, losing patience--“come out on to the stairs, anyway.", "라주미힌이 인내심을 잃고 똑같이 속삭이며 대답했다. \"어쨌든 계단으로 좀 나가시죠."], ["Nastasya, show a light!", "나스타시야, 불 좀 비춰 줘!"], ["I assure you,”", "정말입니다.\""], ["he went on in a half whisper on the stairs--“that he was almost beating the doctor and me this afternoon!", "그는 계단에서 반쯤 속삭이는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"오늘 오후엔 그가 의사와 저를 거의 두들겨 팰 뻔했다니까요!"], ["Do you understand?", "아시겠어요?"], ["The doctor himself!", "의사를요, 의사를!"], ["Even he gave way and left him, so as not to irritate him.", "그 양반도 그를 자극하지 않으려고 한발 물러나 나가 버렸어요."], ["I remained downstairs on guard, but he dressed at once and slipped off.", "저는 아래층에서 망을 보고 있었는데, 그가 곧장 옷을 챙겨 입고는 빠져나가 버렸지 뭡니까."], ["And he will slip off again if you irritate him, at this time of night, and will do himself some mischief....”", "어머님이 그를 자극하시면 이 야밤에 또 빠져나가 무슨 일을 저지를지 모릅니다…….\""]], [["“What are you saying?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“And Avdotya Romanovna can’t possibly be left in those lodgings without you.", "\"게다가 아브도티야 로마노브나를 어머님 없이 그런 하숙집에 둘 수는 없어요."], ["Just think where you are staying!", "두 분이 묵으시는 데가 어떤 곳인지 생각해 보세요!"], ["That blackguard Pyotr Petrovitch couldn’t find you better lodgings...", "그 못된 표트르 페트로비치가 그보다 더 나은 숙소를 못 구해 줬다니……."], ["But you know I’ve had a little to drink, and that’s what makes me...", "하지만 아시다시피 제가 술을 좀 걸쳤거든요, 그래서 이렇게…… 욕지거리를 하는 겁니다."], ["swear; don’t mind it....”", "괘념치 마세요…….\""]], [["“But I’ll go to the landlady here,”", "\"하지만 저는 여기 집주인 여자한테 가 보겠어요.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna insisted, “I’ll beseech her to find some corner for Dounia and me for the night.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 고집스레 말했다. \"두냐와 제가 오늘 밤 묵을 자리 한구석이라도 마련해 달라고 사정해 보겠어요."], ["I can’t leave him like that, I cannot!”", "저는 저 애를 저렇게 두고 갈 수가 없어요, 그럴 수가 없다고요!\""]], [["This conversation took place on the landing just before the landlady’s door.", "이 대화는 집주인 여자의 방문 바로 앞 층계참에서 이루어졌다."], ["Nastasya lighted them from a step below.", "나스타시야가 한 계단 아래에서 그들에게 불을 비추어 주었다."], ["Razumihin was in extraordinary excitement.", "라주미힌은 유난히 흥분해 있었다."], ["Half an hour earlier, while he was bringing Raskolnikov home, he had indeed talked too freely, but he was aware of it himself, and his head was clear in spite of the vast quantities he had imbibed.", "반 시간 전, 라스콜니코프를 집으로 데려올 때만 해도 그는 확실히 말이 지나치게 많기는 했으나, 그 자신도 그걸 알고 있었고, 엄청난 양을 들이켰음에도 머리만은 멀쩡했다."], ["Now he was in a state bordering on ecstasy, and all that he had drunk seemed to fly to his head with redoubled effect.", "그런데 지금은 거의 황홀경에 가까운 상태에 빠져, 마신 술이 죄다 갑절의 효력으로 머리로 솟구치는 듯했다."], ["He stood with the two ladies, seizing both by their hands, persuading them, and giving them reasons with astonishing plainness of speech, and at almost every word he uttered, probably to emphasise his arguments, he squeezed their hands painfully as in a vise.", "그는 두 부인 곁에 서서 양쪽의 손을 다 움켜쥐고는 설득하며 놀랍도록 거침없는 말투로 이런저런 이유를 늘어놓았는데, 한마디 한마디 할 때마다, 아마도 자기주장에 힘을 싣기 위해서인지, 두 사람의 손을 바이스로 죄듯 아프게 짓눌렀다."], ["He stared at Avdotya Romanovna without the least regard for good manners.", "그는 예의범절 따위는 조금도 아랑곳없이 아브도티야 로마노브나를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["They sometimes pulled their hands out of his huge bony paws, but far from noticing what was the matter, he drew them all the closer to him.", "두 사람은 이따금 그의 크고 앙상한 손아귀에서 손을 빼냈지만, 그는 무슨 영문인지 알아차리기는커녕 오히려 그들을 더 바싹 끌어당겼다."], ["If they’d told him to jump head foremost from the staircase, he would have done it without thought or hesitation in their service.", "만일 그들이 그에게 계단에서 머리부터 거꾸로 뛰어내리라고 했다면, 그는 그들을 위해 한 치의 생각이나 망설임도 없이 그렇게 했으리라."], ["Though Pulcheria Alexandrovna felt that the young man was really too eccentric and pinched her hand too much, in her anxiety over her Rodya she looked on his presence as providential, and was unwilling to notice all his peculiarities.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 이 청년이 정말이지 너무 별나고 자기 손을 너무 아프게 쥔다고 느꼈으나, 로쟈에 대한 근심으로 그의 존재를 하늘이 내려 준 도움처럼 여겼던 터라, 그의 온갖 유별난 점을 짐짓 모른 체하려 했다."], ["But though Avdotya Romanovna shared her anxiety, and was not of timorous disposition, she could not see the glowing light in his eyes without wonder and almost alarm.", "한편 아브도티야 로마노브나도 그 근심을 함께 나누고 있었고 겁이 많은 성품도 아니었지만, 그의 눈에 타오르는 빛을 놀라움과 거의 두려움 없이는 바라볼 수 없었다."], ["It was only the unbounded confidence inspired by Nastasya’s account of her brother’s queer friend, which prevented her from trying to run away from him, and to persuade her mother to do the same.", "나스타시야가 들려준, 오라비의 괴짜 친구에 관한 이야기가 안겨 준 무한한 신뢰만이 아니었다면, 그녀는 그에게서 달아나려 했을 것이고, 어머니에게도 그러자고 설득했을 것이다."], ["She realised, too, that even running away was perhaps impossible now.", "게다가 그녀는 지금 와서는 달아나는 것조차 어쩌면 불가능하리라는 것도 알아차렸다."], ["Ten minutes later, however, she was considerably reassured; it was characteristic of Razumihin that he showed his true nature at once, whatever mood he might be in, so that people quickly saw the sort of man they had to deal with.", "그러나 십 분쯤 지나자 그녀는 상당히 안심하게 되었으니, 어떤 기분에 사로잡혀 있든 자기 본성을 단번에 드러내 보여 사람들로 하여금 자기가 상대하는 인물이 어떤 부류인지 금세 알게 하는 것이 라주미힌의 특징이었기 때문이다."]], [["“You can’t go to the landlady, that’s perfect nonsense!”", "\"집주인 여자한테 가시면 안 됩니다, 그건 정말 말도 안 되는 소리예요!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“If you stay, though you are his mother, you’ll drive him to a frenzy, and then goodness knows what will happen!", "\"어머님이 여기 머무르시면, 비록 그의 어머니이시라 해도, 그를 광란에 빠뜨리실 거고, 그러면 무슨 일이 벌어질지 누가 알겠습니까! 들어 보세요, 제가 어떻게 할지 말씀드리죠."], ["Listen, I’ll tell you what I’ll do: Nastasya will stay with him now, and I’ll conduct you both home, you can’t be in the streets alone; Petersburg is an awful place in that way....", "지금은 나스타시야가 그와 함께 있을 테고, 제가 두 분을 집까지 모셔다드리겠습니다, 두 분이 길거리에 혼자 다니실 수는 없으니까요. 페테르부르크는 그런 점에선 끔찍한 곳이거든요……."], ["But no matter!", "하지만 그건 됐고요!"], ["Then I’ll run straight back here and a quarter of an hour later, on my word of honour, I’ll bring you news how he is, whether he is asleep, and all that.", "그런 다음 제가 곧장 여기로 달려와서, 명예를 걸고 십오 분 뒤엔 그가 어떤지, 자고 있는지 어떤지 죄다 소식을 전해 드리겠습니다. 그러고 나선, 들어 보세요!"], ["Then, listen! Then I’ll run home in a twinkling--I’ve a lot of friends there, all drunk--I’ll fetch Zossimov--that’s the doctor who is looking after him, he is there, too, but he is not drunk; he is not drunk, he is never drunk!", "그러고 나선 눈 깜짝할 새에 제 집으로 달려가겠어요 — 거기 친구들이 한 무리 있는데, 죄다 취해 있죠 — 조시모프를 데려오겠습니다 — 그가 바로 그를 돌보는 의사인데, 그 자리에도 있긴 하지만 취하진 않았어요."], ["I’ll drag him to Rodya, and then to you, so that you’ll get two reports in the hour--from the doctor, you understand, from the doctor himself, that’s a very different thing from my account of him!", "그는 취하지 않았어요, 절대 취하는 법이 없죠! 그를 끌고 로쟈한테 갔다가 다시 두 분께 데려오겠습니다, 그러면 한 시간 안에 두 가지 보고를 받으시는 거예요 — 의사한테서, 아시겠죠, 의사 본인한테서요, 그건 제가 그에 대해 전하는 것하곤 전혀 다른 얘기죠!"], ["If there’s anything wrong, I swear I’ll bring you here myself, but, if it’s all right, you go to bed.", "만약 뭔가 잘못된 게 있으면, 맹세코 제가 직접 어머님을 여기로 모셔 오겠습니다. 하지만 다 괜찮으면 주무세요."], ["And I’ll spend the night here, in the passage, he won’t hear me, and I’ll tell Zossimov to sleep at the landlady’s, to be at hand.", "저는 여기 복도에서 밤을 새우겠습니다, 그는 제가 있는 줄도 모를 거예요, 그리고 조시모프한테는 가까이 있도록 집주인 여자 방에서 자라고 하겠습니다."], ["Which is better for him: you or the doctor?", "그에게 어느 쪽이 더 낫겠습니까, 어머님입니까 의사입니까?"], ["So come home then!", "그러니 집으로 가시죠!"], ["But the landlady is out of the question; it’s all right for me, but it’s out of the question for you: she wouldn’t take you, for she’s...", "하지만 집주인 여자한테 가시는 건 말도 안 됩니다. 저야 괜찮지만 두 분께는 어림없는 일이에요."], ["for she’s a fool...", "그 여자가 두 분을 받아 주지 않을 겁니다, 왜냐면 그 여자는……"], ["She’d be jealous on my account of Avdotya Romanovna and of you, too, if you want to know...", "바보거든요……. 저 때문에 아브도티야 로마노브나를 질투할 거고, 알고 싶으시다면, 어머님까지도요……."], ["of Avdotya Romanovna certainly.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 확실히 그럴 거예요."], ["She is an absolutely, absolutely unaccountable character!", "그 여자는 정말이지, 정말이지 종잡을 수 없는 인간이거든요!"], ["But I am a fool, too!...", "하지만 저도 바보고요!……"], ["No matter!", "됐어요!"], ["Come along!", "가시죠!"], ["Do you trust me?", "저를 믿으십니까?"], ["Come, do you trust me or not?”", "자, 저를 믿으십니까, 안 믿으십니까?\""]], [["“Let us go, mother,”", "\"가요, 엄마.\""], ["said Avdotya Romanovna, “he will certainly do what he has promised.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 말했다. \"이분은 분명 약속한 대로 하실 거예요."], ["He has saved Rodya already, and if the doctor really will consent to spend the night here, what could be better?”", "이미 로쟈를 구해 주셨고, 의사 선생님이 정말로 여기서 밤을 새우는 데 동의하신다면, 그보다 더 좋은 게 어디 있겠어요?\""]], [["“You see, you... you...", "\"그것 보세요, 당신은……"], ["understand me, because you are an angel!”", "당신은…… 저를 이해하시는군요, 당신은 천사니까요!\""], ["Razumihin cried in ecstasy, “let us go!", "라주미힌이 황홀해하며 외쳤다. \"가시죠!"], ["Nastasya!", "나스타시야!"], ["Fly upstairs and sit with him with a light; I’ll come in a quarter of an hour.”", "위층으로 날아가서 불을 켜고 그 친구 곁에 앉아 있어, 십오 분 뒤에 갈 테니까.\""]], [["Though Pulcheria Alexandrovna was not perfectly convinced, she made no further resistance.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 완전히 납득한 것은 아니었으나, 더는 버티지 않았다."], ["Razumihin gave an arm to each and drew them down the stairs.", "라주미힌은 두 사람에게 한 팔씩 내주고는 계단 아래로 이끌었다."], ["He still made her uneasy, as though he was competent and good-natured, was he capable of carrying out his promise?", "그녀는 여전히 그가 마음에 걸렸으니, 비록 그가 유능하고 마음씨가 좋다 한들 과연 자기 약속을 지킬 수 있을까?"], ["He seemed in such a condition....", "그가 저런 상태이고 보면……."]], [["“Ah, I see you think I am in such a condition!”", "\"아, 어머님이 제가 저런 상태라고 여기시는 게 보이네요!\""], ["Razumihin broke in upon her thoughts, guessing them, as he strolled along the pavement with huge steps, so that the two ladies could hardly keep up with him, a fact he did not observe, however.", "라주미힌이 그녀의 생각을 알아채고는 끼어들며, 보도를 성큼성큼 걸어가는 바람에 두 부인은 그를 따라잡기가 버거울 지경이었으나, 정작 그는 그 사실을 알아차리지 못했다."], ["“Nonsense!", "\"당치도 않습니다!"], ["That is...", "그러니까……"], ["I am drunk like a fool, but that’s not it; I am not drunk from wine.", "제가 바보처럼 취하긴 했지만, 그게 아니라요. 저는 술 때문에 취한 게 아닙니다."], ["It’s seeing you has turned my head...", "두 분을 뵌 게 제 정신을 홀딱 빼앗아 가서……."], ["But don’t mind me!", "하지만 저한테 신경 쓰지 마세요!"], ["Don’t take any notice: I am talking nonsense, I am not worthy of you....", "괘념치 마세요. 저는 허튼소리를 지껄이고 있고, 두 분께 어울릴 자격이 없어요……."], ["I am utterly unworthy of you!", "저는 두 분께 도무지 어울리지 않는 인간입니다!"], ["The minute I’ve taken you home, I’ll pour a couple of pailfuls of water over my head in the gutter here, and then I shall be all right....", "두 분을 집에 모셔다드리는 그 순간, 저는 여기 도랑에서 머리에 물을 두어 양동이 끼얹을 거고, 그러면 멀쩡해질 겁니다……."], ["If only you knew how I love you both!", "제가 두 분을 얼마나 사랑하는지 아신다면!"], ["Don’t laugh, and don’t be angry!", "웃지도, 화내지도 마세요!"], ["You may be angry with anyone, but not with me!", "누구한테든 화를 내셔도 좋지만, 저한테만은 안 됩니다!"], ["I am his friend, and therefore I am your friend, too, I want to be...", "저는 그의 친구고, 그러니 두 분의 친구이기도 합니다, 그러고 싶어요……."], ["I had a presentiment...", "저는 예감이 있었어요……."], ["Last year there was a moment...", "작년에 어떤 순간이……"], ["though it wasn’t a presentiment really, for you seem to have fallen from heaven.", "하긴 사실 예감이랄 것도 아니었죠, 두 분은 마치 하늘에서 떨어진 것 같으니까요."], ["And I expect I shan’t sleep all night...", "그리고 저는 아마 밤새 한숨도 못 잘 겁니다……."], ["Zossimov was afraid a little time ago that he would go mad...", "조시모프가 조금 전에 그가 미쳐 버릴까 봐 걱정하더군요……."], ["that’s why he mustn’t be irritated.”", "그래서 그를 자극해선 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“What do you say?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["cried the mother.", "어머니가 외쳤다."]], [["“Did the doctor really say that?”", "\"정말 의사 선생님이 그렇게 말했나요?\""], ["asked Avdotya Romanovna, alarmed.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“Yes, but it’s not so, not a bit of it.", "\"네, 하지만 그렇진 않아요, 전혀 그렇지 않습니다."], ["He gave him some medicine, a powder, I saw it, and then your coming here....", "그가 그에게 약을, 가루약을 줬는데, 제가 봤어요, 그러고 나서 두 분이 여기 오신 거고요……."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["It would have been better if you had come to-morrow.", "두 분이 내일 오셨더라면 더 좋았을 텐데."], ["It’s a good thing we went away.", "우리가 나온 게 잘된 일이에요."], ["And in an hour Zossimov himself will report to you about everything.", "그리고 한 시간 뒤면 조시모프가 직접 모든 걸 보고드릴 겁니다."], ["He is not drunk!", "그는 취하지 않았어요!"], ["And I shan’t be drunk....", "그리고 저도 취하지 않을 거고요……."], ["And what made me get so tight?", "그런데 제가 왜 이렇게 곤드레가 됐을까요?"], ["Because they got me into an argument, damn them!", "그자들이 저를 논쟁에 끌어들였거든요, 빌어먹을 놈들!"], ["I’ve sworn never to argue! They talk such trash!", "다시는 논쟁 따위 안 하겠다고 맹세했는데!"], ["I almost came to blows!", "그자들은 어찌나 헛소리를 지껄이는지!"], ["I’ve left my uncle to preside.", "하마터면 주먹다짐을 할 뻔했어요!"], ["Would you believe, they insist on complete absence of individualism and that’s just what they relish!", "그래서 삼촌한테 주재를 맡기고 와 버렸죠. 믿으시겠어요, 그자들은 개성을 완전히 없애야 한다고 우기는데, 바로 그걸 즐긴단 말입니다!"], ["Not to be themselves, to be as unlike themselves as they can.", "자기 자신이 되지 않는 것, 될 수 있는 한 자기 자신과 닮지 않는 것 말이에요."], ["That’s what they regard as the highest point of progress.", "그자들은 그걸 진보의 최고 경지로 여깁니다."], ["If only their nonsense were their own, but as it is...”", "그 헛소리가 그자들 자신의 것이기라도 하면 모를까, 그런데 실상은…….\""]], [["“Listen!”", "\"저기요!\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna interrupted timidly, but it only added fuel to the flames.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 머뭇거리며 끼어들었으나, 그것은 도리어 불에 기름을 붓는 격이었다."]], [["“What do you think?”", "\"어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["shouted Razumihin, louder than ever, “you think I am attacking them for talking nonsense?", "라주미힌이 그 어느 때보다 큰 소리로 고함쳤다. \"제가 그자들이 헛소리를 한다고 공격하는 줄 아세요?"], ["Not a bit!", "천만에요!"], ["I like them to talk nonsense.", "저는 그자들이 헛소리하는 게 좋습니다."], ["That’s man’s one privilege over all creation.", "그건 인간이 만물에 대해 지닌 유일한 특권이니까요."], ["Through error you come to the truth!", "오류를 거쳐서 진리에 이르는 겁니다!"], ["I am a man because I err!", "저는 오류를 범하니까 인간인 거예요!"], ["You never reach any truth without making fourteen mistakes and very likely a hundred and fourteen.", "열네 번, 어쩌면 백열네 번쯤 실수를 저지르지 않고서는 그 어떤 진리에도 다다르지 못합니다."], ["And a fine thing, too, in its way; but we can’t even make mistakes on our own account!", "그것도 그 나름으로 멋진 일이죠. 그런데 우리는 우리 힘으로 실수조차 저지르질 못해요!"], ["Talk nonsense, but talk your own nonsense, and I’ll kiss you for it.", "헛소리를 하되, 자기 자신의 헛소리를 하라, 그러면 그것 때문에 입을 맞춰 주겠어요."], ["To go wrong in one’s own way is better than to go right in someone else’s.", "자기 식대로 잘못 가는 것이 남의 식대로 옳게 가는 것보다 낫습니다."], ["In the first case you are a man, in the second you’re no better than a bird.", "전자의 경우엔 인간이지만, 후자의 경우엔 새만도 못한 거예요."], ["Truth won’t escape you, but life can be cramped. There have been examples.", "진리는 달아나지 않지만, 삶은 옴짝달싹 못 하게 옥죄일 수 있습니다."], ["And what are we doing now?", "그런 예가 있었죠."], ["In science, development, thought, invention, ideals, aims, liberalism, judgment, experience and everything, everything, everything, we are still in the preparatory class at school.", "그런데 우리가 지금 뭘 하고 있습니까? 학문, 발전, 사상, 발명, 이상, 목표, 자유주의, 판단, 경험, 그 모든 것, 모든 것, 모든 것에서 우리는 여전히 학교 예비반 신세란 말입니다!"], ["We prefer to live on other people’s ideas, it’s what we are used to!", "우리는 남의 사상에 얹혀 사는 걸 더 좋아해요, 그게 우리한테 익숙하니까요!"], ["Am I right, am I right?”", "제 말이 맞죠, 맞죠?\""], ["cried Razumihin, pressing and shaking the two ladies’ hands.", "라주미힌이 두 부인의 손을 짓누르고 흔들며 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh, mercy, I do not know,”", "\"오, 저런, 저는 잘 모르겠네요.\""], ["cried poor Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "가엾은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 부르짖었다."]], [["“Yes, yes...", "\"맞아요, 맞아요……"], ["though I don’t agree with you in everything,”", "모든 면에서 동의하는 건 아니지만요.\""], ["added Avdotya Romanovna earnestly and at once uttered a cry, for he squeezed her hand so painfully.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 진지하게 덧붙였다가, 그가 손을 어찌나 아프게 쥐었던지 그만 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“Yes, you say yes...", "\"그렇죠, 당신이 그렇다고 하시니……"], ["well after that you...", "자, 그러고 나면 당신은……"], ["you...”", "당신은…….\""], ["he cried in a transport, “you are a fount of goodness, purity, sense...", "그가 도취되어 외쳤다. \"당신은 선함과 순결함과 분별력의……"], ["and perfection.", "그리고 완벽함의 샘이에요."], ["Give me your hand...", "손을 주세요……"], ["you give me yours, too!", "당신도 손을 주세요!"], ["I want to kiss your hands here at once, on my knees...”", "저는 지금 여기서, 무릎을 꿇고 두 분의 손에 입을 맞추고 싶습니다…….\""], ["and he fell on his knees on the pavement, fortunately at that time deserted.", "그러고는 보도 위에 털썩 무릎을 꿇었는데, 다행히도 그 시각엔 인적이 없었다."]], [["“Leave off, I entreat you, what are you doing?”", "\"그만하세요, 부탁이에요, 지금 뭐 하시는 거예요?\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna cried, greatly distressed.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 몹시 당황하여 외쳤다."]], [["“Get up, get up!”", "\"일어나세요, 일어나요!\""], ["said Dounia laughing, though she, too, was upset.", "두냐가 웃으며 말했으나, 그녀 역시 당혹스러워했다."]], [["“Not for anything till you let me kiss your hands! That’s it!", "\"두 분 손에 입 맞추게 해 주시기 전엔 무슨 일이 있어도 안 됩니다!"], ["Enough!", "됐어요!"], ["I get up and we’ll go on!", "그만하죠! 일어나서 다시 갑시다!"], ["I am a luckless fool, I am unworthy of you and drunk...", "저는 운도 지지리 없는 바보고, 두 분께 어울리지 않는데다 취하기까지 했죠……"], ["and I am ashamed....", "그래서 부끄럽습니다……."], ["I am not worthy to love you, but to do homage to you is the duty of every man who is not a perfect beast!", "저는 두 분을 사랑할 자격이 없지만, 두 분께 경의를 바치는 건 완전한 짐승이 아닌 한 모든 사내의 의무예요!"], ["And I’ve done homage....", "그래서 저도 경의를 바친 거고요……."], ["Here are your lodgings, and for that alone Rodya was right in driving your Pyotr Petrovitch away....", "여기가 두 분 숙소군요, 그리고 그 한 가지만으로도 로쟈가 두 분의 표트르 페트로비치를 쫓아낸 건 옳았어요……."], ["How dare he!", "그자가 어떻게 감히!"], ["how dare he put you in such lodgings!", "어떻게 감히 두 분을 이런 숙소에 두냔 말입니다!"], ["It’s a scandal!", "이건 망신이에요!"], ["Do you know the sort of people they take in here?", "여기서 어떤 부류의 인간들을 받아 주는지 아세요?"], ["And you his betrothed!", "그런데 두 분이 그자의 약혼녀라니!"], ["You are his betrothed?", "당신이 그의 약혼녀이신가요?"], ["Yes?", "그렇죠?"], ["Well, then, I’ll tell you, your _fiancé_ is a scoundrel.”", "그렇다면, 말씀드리는데, 당신의 약혼자는 비열한 놈입니다.\""]], [["“Excuse me, Mr. Razumihin, you are forgetting...”", "\"실례지만, 라주미힌 씨, 잊으신 모양인데…….\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was beginning.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Yes, yes, you are right, I did forget myself, I am ashamed of it,”", "\"네, 네, 맞습니다, 제가 그만 도를 넘었네요, 부끄럽습니다.\""], ["Razumihin made haste to apologise.", "라주미힌이 황급히 사과했다."], ["“But...", "\"하지만……"], ["but you can’t be angry with me for speaking so!", "하지만 제가 이렇게 말한다고 화내실 순 없어요!"], ["For I speak sincerely and not because...", "저는 진심으로 말하는 거지, 그게……"], ["hm, hm!", "흠, 흠!"], ["That would be disgraceful; in fact not because I’m in...", "그건 수치스러운 일일 테니까요. 사실 그게 제가……"], ["hm!", "흠!"], ["Well, anyway, I won’t say why, I daren’t....", "뭐, 어쨌든 이유는 말하지 않겠습니다, 감히 그럴 수 없으니……."], ["But we all saw to-day when he came in that that man is not of our sort.", "하지만 오늘 그가 들어왔을 때 우리 모두 봤죠, 저 사람은 우리 부류가 아니란 걸."], ["Not because he had his hair curled at the barber’s, not because he was in such a hurry to show his wit, but because he is a spy, a speculator, because he is a skin-flint and a buffoon.", "이발소에서 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 지지고 와서가 아니라, 제 재치를 그렇게 서둘러 뽐내려 들어서가 아니라, 그가 첩자이고 투기꾼이고, 구두쇠에다 어릿광대이기 때문이에요."], ["That’s evident.", "그건 명백합니다."], ["Do you think him clever?", "그가 영리하다고 여기세요?"], ["No, he is a fool, a fool.", "아니요, 그자는 바보예요, 바보."], ["And is he a match for you? Good heavens!", "그런데 그자가 당신께 어울리는 짝이라고요?"], ["Do you see, ladies?”", "맙소사! 보세요, 부인들.\""], ["he stopped suddenly on the way upstairs to their rooms, “though all my friends there are drunk, yet they are all honest, and though we do talk a lot of trash, and I do, too, yet we shall talk our way to the truth at last, for we are on the right path, while Pyotr Petrovitch...", "그가 두 사람의 방으로 올라가는 도중에 별안간 멈춰 섰다. \"저쪽에 있는 제 친구들은 죄다 취했어도 죄다 정직한 인간들이고, 우리가 허튼소리를 잔뜩 지껄이고 저도 그러긴 하지만, 그래도 우리는 마침내 우리 말로써 진리에 다다를 겁니다, 우리는 옳은 길 위에 있으니까요."], ["is not on the right path.", "반면에 표트르 페트로비치는……"], ["Though I’ve been calling them all sorts of names just now, I do respect them all...", "옳은 길 위에 있지 않아요. 제가 방금 그자들한테 온갖 욕을 퍼붓긴 했지만, 저는 그자들 모두를 존경합니다……."], ["though I don’t respect Zametov, I like him, for he is a puppy, and that bullock Zossimov, because he is an honest man and knows his work.", "자메토프는 존경하진 않아도 좋아하긴 해요, 강아지 같은 녀석이니까요, 그리고 저 황소 같은 조시모프는 정직한 인간이고 제 일을 잘 알기 때문에 좋아하고요."], ["But enough, it’s all said and forgiven.", "하지만 됐어요, 다 말했고 다 용서됐죠."], ["Is it forgiven?", "용서됐나요?"], ["Well, then, let’s go on.", "자, 그럼 마저 갑시다."], ["I know this corridor, I’ve been here, there was a scandal here at Number 3....", "저는 이 복도를 압니다, 와 본 적이 있거든요, 여기 3호실에서 한바탕 소동이 있었죠……."], ["Where are you here?", "두 분은 여기 어디시죠?"], ["Which number?", "몇 호인가요?"], ["eight?", "8호?"], ["Well, lock yourselves in for the night, then.", "자, 그럼 오늘 밤은 안에서 문을 잠그세요."], ["Don’t let anybody in.", "아무도 들이지 마시고요."], ["In a quarter of an hour I’ll come back with news, and half an hour later I’ll bring Zossimov, you’ll see!", "십오 분 뒤에 소식을 갖고 돌아오겠습니다, 그리고 반 시간 뒤엔 조시모프를 데려오겠어요, 두고 보세요!"], ["Good-bye, I’ll run.”", "안녕히, 저는 뛰어가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Good heavens, Dounia, what is going to happen?”", "\"맙소사, 두냐, 무슨 일이 벌어질까?\""], ["said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, addressing her daughter with anxiety and dismay.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 근심과 당혹감에 싸여 딸에게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t worry yourself, mother,”", "\"걱정 마세요, 엄마.\""], ["said Dounia, taking off her hat and cape.", "두냐가 모자와 망토를 벗으며 말했다."], ["“God has sent this gentleman to our aid, though he has come from a drinking party.", "\"비록 술자리에서 오긴 했지만, 하느님이 우리를 도우라고 이분을 보내신 거예요."], ["We can depend on him, I assure you.", "우린 저분을 믿어도 돼요, 정말이에요."], ["And all that he has done for Rodya....”", "게다가 저분이 로쟈를 위해 해 준 그 모든 일을 보면…….\""]], [["“Ah. Dounia, goodness knows whether he will come!", "\"아, 두냐, 저분이 정말 올지는 하느님만 아실 거다!"], ["How could I bring myself to leave Rodya?...", "내가 어떻게 로쟈를 두고 올 수 있었을까?……"], ["And how different, how different I had fancied our meeting!", "그리고 이 만남이 얼마나, 얼마나 다를 거라고 상상했던지!"], ["How sullen he was, as though not pleased to see us....”", "그 애는 어찌나 시무룩했는지, 우리를 보고도 반갑지 않은 것처럼…….\""]], [["Tears came into her eyes.", "그녀의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."]], [["“No, it’s not that, mother.", "\"아니에요, 그런 게 아니에요, 엄마."], ["You didn’t see, you were crying all the time.", "엄마는 못 보셨죠, 내내 울고 계셨으니까요."], ["He is quite unhinged by serious illness--that’s the reason.”", "오빠는 심각한 병으로 완전히 정신이 무너진 거예요 — 그게 이유라고요.\""]], [["“Ah, that illness!", "\"아, 그 병!"], ["What will happen, what will happen?", "무슨 일이 벌어질까, 무슨 일이 벌어질까?"], ["And how he talked to you, Dounia!”", "그리고 그 애가 너한테 어떻게 말했는지, 두냐!\""], ["said the mother, looking timidly at her daughter, trying to read her thoughts and, already half consoled by Dounia’s standing up for her brother, which meant that she had already forgiven him.", "어머니는 딸의 속내를 읽어 내려 머뭇머뭇 그녀를 바라보며 말했으나, 두냐가 오라비를 두둔하고 나선 것에 — 그건 그녀가 이미 그를 용서했다는 뜻이었으므로 — 벌써 반쯤은 위안을 얻고 있었다."], ["“I am sure he will think better of it to-morrow,”", "\"그 앤 내일이면 분명 다시 생각을 고쳐먹을 거다.\""], ["she added, probing her further.", "그녀가 딸의 속을 더 떠보며 덧붙였다."]], [["“And I am sure that he will say the same to-morrow...", "\"그리고 저는 오빠가 내일도 똑같이 말할 거라고 확신해요……"], ["about that,”", "그 일에 대해선.\""], ["Avdotya Romanovna said finally.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 마침내 말했다."], ["And, of course, there was no going beyond that, for this was a point which Pulcheria Alexandrovna was afraid to discuss.", "그리고 물론, 그 이상으로 나아갈 수는 없었으니, 그것은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 입에 올리기 두려워하던 화제였기 때문이다."], ["Dounia went up and kissed her mother.", "두냐는 다가가 어머니에게 입을 맞췄다."], ["The latter warmly embraced her without speaking.", "어머니는 아무 말 없이 그녀를 따뜻하게 끌어안았다."], ["Then she sat down to wait anxiously for Razumihin’s return, timidly watching her daughter who walked up and down the room with her arms folded, lost in thought.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 라주미힌이 돌아오기를 애타게 기다리며 앉아, 팔짱을 낀 채 생각에 잠겨 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있는 딸을 머뭇머뭇 바라보았다."], ["This walking up and down when she was thinking was a habit of Avdotya Romanovna’s and the mother was always afraid to break in on her daughter’s mood at such moments.", "생각에 잠길 때면 이렇게 방 안을 거니는 것이 아브도티야 로마노브나의 버릇이었고, 어머니는 그런 순간에 딸의 기분을 깨뜨릴까 늘 두려워했다."]], [["Razumihin, of course, was ridiculous in his sudden drunken infatuation for Avdotya Romanovna.", "물론 라주미힌이 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 별안간 취중에 빠져든 그 연정은 우스꽝스러웠다."], ["Yet apart from his eccentric condition, many people would have thought it justified if they had seen Avdotya Romanovna, especially at that moment when she was walking to and fro with folded arms, pensive and melancholy.", "그러나 그의 별난 상태를 차치하더라도, 많은 이들은 아브도티야 로마노브나를, 특히 그녀가 팔짱을 낀 채 수심에 잠겨 우울하게 이리저리 거니는 그 순간의 모습을 보았다면, 그럴 만도 하다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna was remarkably good-looking; she was tall, strikingly well-proportioned, strong and self-reliant--the latter quality was apparent in every gesture, though it did not in the least detract from the grace and softness of her movements.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 눈에 띄게 아름다웠다. 키가 크고, 놀랍도록 균형 잡힌 몸매에, 강인하고 자신만만했는데 — 마지막의 그 자질은 모든 몸짓에서 드러났으나, 그렇다고 해서 그녀 동작의 우아함과 부드러움을 조금도 해치지는 않았다."], ["In face she resembled her brother, but she might be described as really beautiful.", "얼굴은 오라비를 닮았으나, 정말로 아름답다고 할 만했다."], ["Her hair was dark brown, a little lighter than her brother’s; there was a proud light in her almost black eyes and yet at times a look of extraordinary kindness.", "머리칼은 짙은 갈색으로 오라비보다 조금 더 밝았고, 거의 검은 두 눈에는 도도한 빛이 어렸으면서도 때로는 유난한 다정함이 깃들곤 했다."], ["She was pale, but it was a healthy pallor; her face was radiant with freshness and vigour.", "창백했지만, 그것은 건강한 창백함이었다. 얼굴은 싱그러움과 생기로 환히 빛났다."], ["Her mouth was rather small; the full red lower lip projected a little as did her chin; it was the only irregularity in her beautiful face, but it gave it a peculiarly individual and almost haughty expression.", "입은 다소 작은 편이었고, 도톰한 붉은 아랫입술이 턱과 더불어 조금 앞으로 도드라져 있었는데, 그것이 그 아름다운 얼굴에서 유일하게 고르지 못한 점이었으나, 도리어 그 얼굴에 유난히 개성 있고 거의 오만하기까지 한 표정을 부여했다."], ["Her face was always more serious and thoughtful than gay; but how well smiles, how well youthful, lighthearted, irresponsible, laughter suited her face!", "그녀의 얼굴은 언제나 즐겁다기보다는 더 진지하고 사색적이었으나, 그 얼굴에 미소가, 젊고 가뿐하고 거리낌 없는 웃음이 얼마나 잘 어울리던지!"], ["It was natural enough that a warm, open, simple-hearted, honest giant like Razumihin, who had never seen anyone like her and was not quite sober at the time, should lose his head immediately.", "라주미힌처럼 따뜻하고 솔직하고 순박하고 정직한 거인이, 일찍이 그녀 같은 사람을 본 적이 없는데다 그 당시 술까지 얼근하게 취해 있었으니, 단번에 정신을 잃어버린 것도 무리는 아니었다."], ["Besides, as chance would have it, he saw Dounia for the first time transfigured by her love for her brother and her joy at meeting him.", "게다가 우연찮게도, 그는 오라비를 향한 사랑과 그를 만난 기쁨으로 환히 변모한 두냐의 모습을 처음으로 보았던 것이다."], ["Afterwards he saw her lower lip quiver with indignation at her brother’s insolent, cruel and ungrateful words--and his fate was sealed.", "이어서 그는 오라비의 무례하고 잔인하고 배은망덕한 말에 그녀의 아랫입술이 분노로 떨리는 것을 보았으니 — 그의 운명은 그것으로 결판이 났다."]], [["He had spoken the truth, moreover, when he blurted out in his drunken talk on the stairs that Praskovya Pavlovna, Raskolnikov’s eccentric landlady, would be jealous of Pulcheria Alexandrovna as well as of Avdotya Romanovna on his account.", "게다가 그가 계단에서 취중에 불쑥 내뱉었던 말, 즉 라스콜니코프의 별난 집주인 여자 프라스코비야 파블로브나가 자기 때문에 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나뿐 아니라 아브도티야 로마노브나까지 질투하리라는 그 말은 진실이었다."], ["Although Pulcheria Alexandrovna was forty-three, her face still retained traces of her former beauty; she looked much younger than her age, indeed, which is almost always the case with women who retain serenity of spirit, sensitiveness and pure sincere warmth of heart to old age.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 마흔셋이었지만, 그 얼굴에는 여전히 한때의 미모의 흔적이 남아 있었고, 사실 나이보다 훨씬 젊어 보였는데, 늙도록 마음의 평온과 감수성, 순수하고 진실한 가슴의 온기를 간직한 여인들이 거의 언제나 그렇듯 말이다."], ["We may add in parenthesis that to preserve all this is the only means of retaining beauty to old age.", "덧붙여 말하자면, 이 모든 것을 지키는 것이야말로 늙도록 아름다움을 간직하는 유일한 방법이다."], ["Her hair had begun to grow grey and thin, there had long been little crow’s foot wrinkles round her eyes, her cheeks were hollow and sunken from anxiety and grief, and yet it was a handsome face.", "그녀의 머리칼은 희끗희끗 세고 숱이 적어지기 시작했고, 눈가에는 잔주름이 진 지 오래였으며, 두 뺨은 근심과 슬픔으로 홀쭉하게 꺼졌으나, 그래도 그것은 고운 얼굴이었다."], ["She was Dounia over again, twenty years older, but without the projecting underlip.", "그녀는 스무 살 더 먹은 두냐, 그러나 도드라진 아랫입술만은 없는 두냐, 바로 그 자체였다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was emotional, but not sentimental, timid and yielding, but only to a certain point.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 다정다감했으나 감상적이지는 않았고, 소심하고 양보를 잘했으나 그것도 어느 선까지였다."], ["She could give way and accept a great deal even of what was contrary to her convictions, but there was a certain barrier fixed by honesty, principle and the deepest convictions which nothing would induce her to cross.", "그녀는 자기 신념에 어긋나는 일조차 상당 부분 양보하고 받아들일 수 있었지만, 정직과 원칙과 가장 깊은 신념이 그어 놓은 어떤 경계만은 그 무엇으로도 넘게 할 수 없었다."]], [["Exactly twenty minutes after Razumihin’s departure, there came two subdued but hurried knocks at the door: he had come back.", "라주미힌이 떠난 지 정확히 이십 분 뒤, 문을 두드리는 나직하지만 다급한 소리가 두 번 들렸다. 그가 돌아온 것이다."]], [["“I won’t come in, I haven’t time,”", "\"안 들어가겠습니다, 시간이 없어서요.\""], ["he hastened to say when the door was opened.", "문이 열리자 그가 황급히 말했다."], ["“He sleeps like a top, soundly, quietly, and God grant he may sleep ten hours.", "\"그는 통나무처럼 곤히, 조용히 잠들었어요, 부디 열 시간은 자기를 하느님께 빕니다."], ["Nastasya’s with him; I told her not to leave till I came.", "나스타시야가 그와 함께 있고요, 제가 올 때까지 떠나지 말라고 해 뒀습니다."], ["Now I am fetching Zossimov, he will report to you and then you’d better turn in; I can see you are too tired to do anything....”", "이제 조시모프를 데리러 가는데, 그가 두 분께 보고할 거고, 그러고 나면 두 분도 주무시는 게 좋겠어요. 보아하니 두 분 다 너무 지치셔서 아무것도 못 하실 것 같으니…….\""]], [["And he ran off down the corridor.", "그러고는 복도를 따라 달려가 버렸다."]], [["“What a very competent and...", "\"정말 유능하고……"], ["devoted young man!”", "헌신적인 청년이로구나!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna exceedingly delighted.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 더없이 기뻐하며 외쳤다."]], [["“He seems a splendid person!”", "\"훌륭한 분 같아요!\""], ["Avdotya Romanovna replied with some warmth, resuming her walk up and down the room.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 다소 온기를 담아 대답하고는, 다시 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."]], [["It was nearly an hour later when they heard footsteps in the corridor and another knock at the door.", "거의 한 시간이 지나서야 복도에서 발소리가 들리고 또 한 번 문 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["Both women waited this time completely relying on Razumihin’s promise; he actually had succeeded in bringing Zossimov.", "이번에는 두 여인 모두 라주미힌의 약속을 온전히 믿으며 기다렸고, 그는 정말로 조시모프를 데려오는 데 성공했다."], ["Zossimov had agreed at once to desert the drinking party to go to Raskolnikov’s, but he came reluctantly and with the greatest suspicion to see the ladies, mistrusting Razumihin in his exhilarated condition.", "조시모프는 라스콜니코프에게 가기 위해 술자리를 버리는 데는 단번에 동의했으나, 들떠 있는 라주미힌을 믿지 못한 탓에 마지못해, 그것도 잔뜩 미심쩍어하며 두 부인을 만나러 왔다."], ["But his vanity was at once reassured and flattered; he saw that they were really expecting him as an oracle.", "그러나 그의 허영심은 곧바로 안심하고 흡족해졌다. 그녀들이 정말로 그를 신탁이라도 되는 양 기다리고 있었음을 알아차린 것이다."], ["He stayed just ten minutes and succeeded in completely convincing and comforting Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "그는 딱 십 분만 머물렀으나 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나를 완전히 납득시키고 안심시키는 데 성공했다."], ["He spoke with marked sympathy, but with the reserve and extreme seriousness of a young doctor at an important consultation.", "그는 두드러진 동정을 담아 말하면서도, 중대한 진찰에 임한 젊은 의사다운 절제와 극도의 진지함을 유지했다."], ["He did not utter a word on any other subject and did not display the slightest desire to enter into more personal relations with the two ladies.", "그는 다른 어떤 화제에 대해서도 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았고, 두 부인과 좀 더 사사로운 관계를 맺으려는 기색도 조금도 드러내지 않았다."], ["Remarking at his first entrance the dazzling beauty of Avdotya Romanovna, he endeavoured not to notice her at all during his visit and addressed himself solely to Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "처음 들어설 때 아브도티야 로마노브나의 눈부신 미모를 알아챘으면서도, 방문하는 동안 그녀에게는 도무지 눈길을 주지 않으려 애쓰며 오로지 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나에게만 말을 건넸다."], ["All this gave him extraordinary inward satisfaction.", "이 모든 것이 그에게 유별난 내적 만족을 안겨 주었다."], ["He declared that he thought the invalid at this moment going on very satisfactorily.", "그는 환자가 지금으로선 아주 만족스럽게 회복되어 가고 있다고 보인다고 단언했다."], ["According to his observations the patient’s illness was due partly to his unfortunate material surroundings during the last few months, but it had partly also a moral origin, “was, so to speak, the product of several material and moral influences, anxieties, apprehensions, troubles, certain ideas...", "그의 관찰에 따르면 환자의 병은 한편으로는 지난 몇 달간의 불운한 물질적 환경 탓이었으나, 다른 한편으로는 정신적인 기원도 있어서, \"이를테면, 여러 물질적·정신적 영향과 근심, 불안, 고민, 어떤 관념들…… 등등의 산물\"이라고 했다."], ["and so on.” Noticing stealthily that Avdotya Romanovna was following his words with close attention, Zossimov allowed himself to enlarge on this theme.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 자기 말을 바싹 주의를 기울여 따라오고 있음을 슬그머니 알아챈 조시모프는 이 주제에 대해 좀 더 늘어놓는 것을 자신에게 허락했다."], ["On Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s anxiously and timidly inquiring as to “some suspicion of insanity,”", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 근심스럽고 머뭇거리는 어조로 \"혹시 광기가 의심되지는 않는지\""], ["he replied with a composed and candid smile that his words had been exaggerated; that certainly the patient had some fixed idea, something approaching a monomania--he, Zossimov, was now particularly studying this interesting branch of medicine--but that it must be recollected that until to-day the patient had been in delirium and...", "묻자, 그는 침착하고 솔직한 미소를 띠며 자기 말이 과장되었다고, 물론 환자가 어떤 고정관념을, 편집광에 가까운 무언가를 — 그, 조시모프는 지금 의학의 바로 이 흥미로운 분야를 특히 연구하는 중이었다 — 지니고 있긴 하지만, 환자가 오늘까지 섬망 상태에 있었음을 잊어선 안 되며……"], ["and that no doubt the presence of his family would have a favourable effect on his recovery and distract his mind, “if only all fresh shocks can be avoided,”", "또한 의심할 여지 없이 가족의 존재가 그의 회복에 호의적인 영향을 미쳐 그의 마음을 흐트러뜨려 줄 거라고, \"다만 모든 새로운 충격을 피할 수만 있다면\""], ["he added significantly.", "하고 의미심장하게 덧붙였다."], ["Then he got up, took leave with an impressive and affable bow, while blessings, warm gratitude, and entreaties were showered upon him, and Avdotya Romanovna spontaneously offered her hand to him.", "그러고는 일어나 인상적이고 다정한 인사로 작별을 고했고, 그 위로 축복과 따뜻한 감사와 간청이 쏟아졌으며, 아브도티야 로마노브나는 자청해서 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["He went out exceedingly pleased with his visit and still more so with himself.", "그는 자기 방문에 더없이 흡족해하며, 자기 자신에 대해서는 그보다 더 흡족해하며 나갔다."]], [["“We’ll talk to-morrow; go to bed at once!”", "\"내일 얘기하죠, 지금은 곧장 주무세요!\""], ["Razumihin said in conclusion, following Zossimov out.", "라주미힌이 조시모프를 따라 나가며 마무리로 말했다."], ["“I’ll be with you to-morrow morning as early as possible with my report.”", "\"내일 아침 되도록 일찍 보고를 들고 두 분께 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“That’s a fetching little girl, Avdotya Romanovna,”", "\"매력적인 아가씨로군, 아브도티야 로마노브나 말이야.\""], ["remarked Zossimov, almost licking his lips as they both came out into the street.", "둘이 거리로 나서자 조시모프가 거의 입맛을 다시듯 한마디 했다."]], [["“Fetching?", "\"매력적이라고?"], ["You said fetching?”", "매력적이라고 했어?\""], ["roared Razumihin and he flew at Zossimov and seized him by the throat.", "라주미힌이 고함을 지르며 조시모프에게 달려들어 멱살을 움켜쥐었다."], ["“If you ever dare.... Do you understand?", "\"네가 감히 한 번만 더……."], ["Do you understand?”", "알아들었어?"], ["he shouted, shaking him by the collar and squeezing him against the wall.", "알아들었냐고?\" 그가 멱살을 흔들며 벽으로 밀어붙이고 소리쳤다."], ["“Do you hear?”", "\"들었어?\""]], [["“Let me go, you drunken devil,”", "\"이거 놔, 이 술 취한 악마야.\""], ["said Zossimov, struggling and when he had let him go, he stared at him and went off into a sudden guffaw.", "조시모프가 버둥거리며 말했고, 라주미힌이 손을 놓자 그를 빤히 쳐다보더니 별안간 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Razumihin stood facing him in gloomy and earnest reflection.", "라주미힌은 침울하고 진지한 상념에 잠긴 채 그를 마주하고 서 있었다."]], [["“Of course, I am an ass,”", "\"물론, 나야 멍청한 놈이지.\""], ["he observed, sombre as a storm cloud, “but still...", "그가 먹구름처럼 어두운 얼굴로 말했다. \"하지만 그래도……"], ["you are another.”", "너도 마찬가지야.\""]], [["“No, brother, not at all such another.", "\"아니, 형제, 전혀 그런 부류가 아니지."], ["I am not dreaming of any folly.”", "나는 그런 어리석은 꿈은 꾸지도 않으니까.\""]], [["They walked along in silence and only when they were close to Raskolnikov’s lodgings, Razumihin broke the silence in considerable anxiety.", "그들은 말없이 걸었고, 라스콜니코프의 숙소에 가까워졌을 때에야 비로소 라주미힌이 적잖은 근심에 싸여 침묵을 깼다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"들어 봐.\""], ["he said, “you’re a first-rate fellow, but among your other failings, you’re a loose fish, that I know, and a dirty one, too.", "그가 말했다. \"넌 일류 사내지만, 다른 결점들 가운데서도 넌 방탕한 놈이야, 그건 내가 알지, 게다가 추잡한 놈이고."], ["You are a feeble, nervous wretch, and a mass of whims, you’re getting fat and lazy and can’t deny yourself anything--and I call that dirty because it leads one straight into the dirt.", "넌 나약하고 신경질적인 한심한 작자에다 변덕덩어리야, 살이 오르고 게을러져서 뭐 하나 절제할 줄을 모르지 — 그리고 나는 그걸 추잡하다고 부르는데, 그게 사람을 곧장 진흙탕으로 끌고 가기 때문이야."], ["You’ve let yourself get so slack that I don’t know how it is you are still a good, even a devoted doctor.", "넌 자신을 어찌나 풀어 놓았는지, 네가 아직도 어떻게 훌륭한, 심지어 헌신적인 의사로 남아 있는지 모르겠다니까."], ["You--a doctor--sleep on a feather bed and get up at night to your patients!", "너 같은 의사가 — 깃털 침대에서 자다가도 밤중에 환자한테 가려고 일어나잖아!"], ["In another three or four years you won’t get up for your patients...", "앞으로 삼사 년만 더 지나면 넌 환자를 위해 일어나지도 않을 거야……."], ["But hang it all, that’s not the point!...", "하지만 빌어먹을, 그게 요점이 아니지!……"], ["You are going to spend to-night in the landlady’s flat here.", "넌 오늘 밤 여기 집주인 여자 방에서 자게 될 거야."], ["(Hard work I’ve had to persuade her!) And I’ll be in the kitchen.", "(그 여자를 설득하느라 얼마나 애를 먹었는지 몰라!) 그리고 나는 부엌에 있을 거고."], ["So here’s a chance for you to get to know her better....", "그러니 너한테 그 여자를 좀 더 잘 알 기회가 생긴 거지……."], ["It’s not as you think!", "네가 생각하는 그런 게 아니야!"], ["There’s not a trace of anything of the sort, brother...!”", "그런 종류의 일 따위는 흔적도 없어, 형제……!\""]], [["“But I don’t think!”", "\"하지만 난 그런 생각 안 한다고!\""]], [["“Here you have modesty, brother, silence, bashfulness, a savage virtue...", "\"여기엔 정숙함이 있어, 형제, 침묵, 수줍음, 거친 미덕이……."], ["and yet she’s sighing and melting like wax, simply melting!", "그런데도 한숨을 쉬고 밀랍처럼 녹아내린다니까, 그저 녹아내려!"], ["Save me from her, by all that’s unholy!", "그 여자한테서 날 좀 구해 줘, 세상의 온갖 불경한 것에 걸고 빌건대!"], ["She’s most prepossessing...", "그 여잔 어찌나 사람 마음을 끄는지……."], ["I’ll repay you, I’ll do anything....”", "보답할게, 뭐든 해 줄 테니까…….\""]], [["Zossimov laughed more violently than ever.", "조시모프는 그 어느 때보다 격렬하게 웃어 댔다."]], [["“Well, you are smitten!", "\"이런, 너 단단히 반했구나!"], ["But what am I to do with her?”", "그런데 내가 그 여자를 어쩌란 말이냐?\""]], [["“It won’t be much trouble, I assure you.", "\"별 수고도 안 들어, 정말이야."], ["Talk any rot you like to her, as long as you sit by her and talk.", "그 여자 곁에 앉아서 얘기만 하면 되니까, 네 멋대로 무슨 허튼소리든 지껄여도 돼."], ["You’re a doctor, too; try curing her of something.", "너도 의사잖아, 무슨 병이든 하나 고쳐 보라고. 맹세코 후회하진 않을 거야."], ["I swear you won’t regret it.", "그 여잔 피아노가 있는데, 너도 알다시피 나도 좀 칠 줄 알거든."], ["She has a piano, and you know, I strum a little.", "내가 거기서 부르는 노래가 하나 있어, 진짜배기 러시아 노래로, '나는 뜨거운 눈물을 흘리네' 하는 거지."], ["I have a song there, a genuine Russian one: ‘I shed hot tears.’ She likes the genuine article--and well, it all began with that song; Now you’re a regular performer, a _maître_, a Rubinstein....", "그 여잔 진짜배기를 좋아해 — 그래, 그게 다 그 노래로 시작된 거야. 그런데 넌 어엿한 연주자에, 거장에, 루빈시테인이잖아……."], ["I assure you, you won’t regret it!”", "정말이지, 후회 안 할 거야!\""]], [["“But have you made her some promise?", "\"그런데 너 그 여자한테 무슨 약속이라도 했냐?"], ["Something signed?", "뭔가 서명한 거라도?"], ["A promise of marriage, perhaps?”", "혹시 결혼 약속이라도?\""]], [["“Nothing, nothing, absolutely nothing of the kind!", "\"아무것도, 아무것도, 그런 건 전혀 없어!"], ["Besides she is not that sort at all....", "게다가 그 여잔 그런 부류가 전혀 아니야……."], ["Tchebarov tried that....”", "체바로프가 그걸 시도했다가…….\""]], [["“Well then, drop her!”", "\"그럼 그 여잘 그냥 버려!\""]], [["“But I can’t drop her like that!”", "\"하지만 그렇게 버릴 순 없어!\""]], [["“Why can’t you?”", "\"왜 못 버려?\""]], [["“Well, I can’t, that’s all about it!", "\"뭐, 못 버려, 그게 다야!"], ["There’s an element of attraction here, brother.”", "여기엔 끌리는 요소가 있단 말이야, 형제.\""]], [["“Then why have you fascinated her?”", "\"그럼 넌 왜 그 여잘 홀린 거야?\""]], [["“I haven’t fascinated her; perhaps I was fascinated myself in my folly.", "\"내가 그 여잘 홀린 게 아니라, 어쩌면 내가 어리석게도 스스로 홀린 건지도 모르지."], ["But she won’t care a straw whether it’s you or I, so long as somebody sits beside her, sighing....", "하지만 그 여잔 너든 나든 눈곱만큼도 개의치 않아, 누군가 곁에 앉아서 한숨만 쉬어 준다면 말이야……."], ["I can’t explain the position, brother...", "이 상황을 설명할 수가 없네, 형제……."], ["look here, you are good at mathematics, and working at it now...", "이봐, 넌 수학을 잘하고 지금도 그걸 연구하고 있잖아……."], ["begin teaching her the integral calculus; upon my soul, I’m not joking, I’m in earnest, it’ll be just the same to her.", "그 여자한테 적분학을 가르치기 시작해 봐. 정말이지, 농담 아니야, 진심이라고, 그 여자한테야 매한가지일 테니까."], ["She will gaze at you and sigh for a whole year together.", "그 여잔 일 년 내내 널 빤히 바라보며 한숨이나 쉴 거야."], ["I talked to her once for two days at a time about the Prussian House of Lords (for one must talk of something)--she just sighed and perspired!", "내가 한번은 그 여자한테 프로이센 상원에 대해 이틀 동안 줄곧 얘기한 적이 있는데(뭐라도 얘기는 해야 하니까) — 그 여잔 그저 한숨을 쉬며 땀을 흘리더라니까!"], ["And you mustn’t talk of love--she’s bashful to hysterics--but just let her see you can’t tear yourself away--that’s enough.", "그리고 사랑 얘긴 꺼내지 마 — 그 여잔 발작이 일 만큼 수줍음을 타니까 — 그냥 네가 그 여자한테서 도무지 떨어지지 못하겠다는 걸 보여 주기만 해 — 그거면 충분해."], ["It’s fearfully comfortable; you’re quite at home, you can read, sit, lie about, write.", "끔찍하게 편안하다니까. 완전히 제집처럼 지내면서 책도 읽고, 앉아 있고, 드러눕고, 글도 쓰고."], ["You may even venture on a kiss, if you’re careful.”", "조심만 하면 입맞춤까지도 시도해 볼 만하지.\""]], [["“But what do I want with her?”", "\"하지만 내가 그 여자한테 뭘 바라겠냐?\""]], [["“Ach, I can’t make you understand!", "\"아, 도무지 너한테 이해를 못 시키겠네!"], ["You see, you are made for each other!", "봐, 너희 둘은 천생연분이라니까!"], ["I have often been reminded of you!...", "난 너를 보면 자주 그 여자가 떠올랐다고!……"], ["You’ll come to it in the end!", "넌 결국 그렇게 될 거야!"], ["So does it matter whether it’s sooner or later?", "그러니 그게 더 이르냐 늦냐가 무슨 상관이야?"], ["There’s the feather-bed element here, brother--ach!", "여기엔 깃털 침대의 요소가 있단 말이야, 형제 — 아!"], ["and not only that!", "게다가 그것만이 아니지!"], ["There’s an attraction here--here you have the end of the world, an anchorage, a quiet haven, the navel of the earth, the three fishes that are the foundation of the world, the essence of pancakes, of savoury fish-pies, of the evening samovar, of soft sighs and warm shawls, and hot stoves to sleep on--as snug as though you were dead, and yet you’re alive--the advantages of both at once!", "여기엔 끌어당기는 게 있어 — 여기엔 세상의 끝, 정박지, 고요한 안식처, 대지의 배꼽, 세상의 토대가 되는 세 마리 물고기, 블린의 정수, 고소한 생선 파이의 정수, 저녁 사모바르의 정수, 부드러운 한숨과 따스한 숄과 잠들기 좋은 뜨거운 페치카의 정수가 있단 말이야 — 죽은 듯이 아늑한데, 그러면서도 넌 살아 있는 거지 — 두 가지 이점을 한꺼번에 누리는 거야!"], ["Well, hang it, brother, what stuff I’m talking, it’s bedtime!", "자, 빌어먹을, 형제, 내가 무슨 헛소릴 지껄이는 거람, 잘 시간이야!"], ["Listen.", "들어 봐."], ["I sometimes wake up at night; so I’ll go in and look at him.", "난 가끔 밤에 깨거든. 그러니 내가 들어가서 그를 살펴볼게."], ["But there’s no need, it’s all right.", "하지만 그럴 필요도 없어, 다 괜찮으니까."], ["Don’t you worry yourself, yet if you like, you might just look in once, too.", "너무 걱정 말라고, 그래도 원한다면 너도 한 번쯤 들여다봐도 돼."], ["But if you notice anything--delirium or fever--wake me at once.", "그런데 만약 뭔가 눈에 띄거든 — 섬망이든 열이든 — 즉시 날 깨워. 하지만 그럴 리는 없을 거야…….\""], ["But there can’t be....”", "라주미힌은 이튿날 아침 여덟 시에 깨어났는데, 마음이 어지럽고 심각했다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["Razumihin waked up next morning at eight o’clock, troubled and serious.", "라주미힌은 이튿날 아침 여덟 시에 깨어났는데, 마음이 어지럽고 심각했다."], ["He found himself confronted with many new and unlooked-for perplexities.", "그는 새롭고 예기치 못한 갖가지 곤혹스러운 일들과 맞닥뜨려 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["He had never expected that he would ever wake up feeling like that.", "그는 자신이 언젠가 이런 기분으로 깨어나리라고는 한 번도 예상하지 못했다."], ["He remembered every detail of the previous day and he knew that a perfectly novel experience had befallen him, that he had received an impression unlike anything he had known before.", "그는 어제 일을 하나하나 빠짐없이 기억하고 있었고, 자신에게 전혀 색다른 경험이 일어났다는 것, 지금껏 알던 그 무엇과도 다른 인상을 받았다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["At the same time he recognised clearly that the dream which had fired his imagination was hopelessly unattainable--so unattainable that he felt positively ashamed of it, and he hastened to pass to the other more practical cares and difficulties bequeathed him by that “thrice accursed yesterday.”", "동시에 그는 자신의 상상에 불을 지폈던 그 꿈이 가망 없이 도달할 수 없는 것임을 똑똑히 깨달았다. 어찌나 도달할 수 없는지 그는 그것이 정말로 부끄럽게 느껴졌고, 그래서 그 '세 곱절로 저주받은 어제'가 그에게 물려준 다른, 더 실질적인 근심과 어려움 쪽으로 서둘러 넘어갔다."]], [["The most awful recollection of the previous day was the way he had shown himself “base and mean,”", "어제 일 중에서 가장 끔찍한 기억은 자신이 '비열하고 치사하게'"], ["not only because he had been drunk, but because he had taken advantage of the young girl’s position to abuse her _fiancé_ in his stupid jealousy, knowing nothing of their mutual relations and obligations and next to nothing of the man himself.", "굴었다는 것이었는데, 그것은 단지 그가 취해 있었기 때문만이 아니라, 어리석은 질투에 사로잡혀 그 젊은 처녀의 처지를 이용해 그녀의 약혼자를 헐뜯었기 때문이었다. 그는 그들 사이의 관계와 의무에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그 남자 자체에 대해서도 거의 아는 바가 없었으면서 말이다."], ["And what right had he to criticise him in that hasty and unguarded manner?", "도대체 무슨 권리로 그가 그토록 성급하고 경솔한 태도로 그 사람을 비판했단 말인가?"], ["Who had asked for his opinion?", "누가 그의 의견을 물었던가?"], ["Was it thinkable that such a creature as Avdotya Romanovna would be marrying an unworthy man for money?", "아브도티야 로마노브나 같은 사람이 돈 때문에 자격 없는 남자와 결혼하리라는 것이 생각이나 할 수 있는 일인가?"], ["So there must be something in him.", "그러니 그 남자에게는 무언가 있는 게 분명했다."], ["The lodgings?", "그 셋집은?"], ["But after all how could he know the character of the lodgings?", "하지만 따지고 보면 그가 어떻게 그 셋집의 됨됨이를 알 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He was furnishing a flat...", "그는 신혼집을 꾸미고 있었던 거지……."], ["Foo!", "쳇!"], ["how despicable it all was!", "이 모든 게 얼마나 한심한 일인가!"], ["And what justification was it that he was drunk?", "그리고 취해 있었다는 것이 무슨 변명이 되겠는가?"], ["Such a stupid excuse was even more degrading!", "그런 어리석은 핑계는 오히려 더 굴욕적인 것이었다!"], ["In wine is truth, and the truth had all come out, “that is, all the uncleanness of his coarse and envious heart”!", "술 속에 진실이 있는 법, 그리하여 그 진실이 죄다 튀어나와 버린 것이다, '말하자면, 그의 거칠고 시기심 많은 마음속 온갖 더러움이' 말이다!"], ["And would such a dream ever be permissible to him, Razumihin?", "그리고 그런 꿈이 그에게, 라주미힌에게 도대체 가당키나 한 것이겠는가?"], ["What was he beside such a girl--he, the drunken noisy braggart of last night?", "그런 처녀 곁에서 그는 무엇이란 말인가 — 어젯밤의 그 술 취해 떠들어 대던 허풍쟁이가?"], ["Was it possible to imagine so absurd and cynical a juxtaposition?", "그토록 터무니없고 파렴치한 짝짓기를 상상이나 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Razumihin blushed desperately at the very idea and suddenly the recollection forced itself vividly upon him of how he had said last night on the stairs that the landlady would be jealous of Avdotya Romanovna...", "라주미힌은 그 생각만으로도 절망적으로 얼굴을 붉혔고, 갑자기 어젯밤 계단에서 집주인 여자가 아브도티야 로마노브나를 질투하게 될 거라고 지껄였던 기억이 생생하게 그를 덮쳤다……."], ["that was simply intolerable.", "그것은 정말이지 견딜 수 없는 것이었다."], ["He brought his fist down heavily on the kitchen stove, hurt his hand and sent one of the bricks flying.", "그는 주먹으로 부엌 화덕을 세게 내리쳤고, 손을 다치고 벽돌 하나를 튕겨 내고 말았다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["he muttered to himself a minute later with a feeling of self-abasement, “of course, all these infamies can never be wiped out or smoothed over...", "잠시 후 그는 자기 비하의 감정에 휩싸여 중얼거렸다. \"물론, 이 모든 추잡한 짓은 결코 씻어 내거나 얼버무릴 수 없어……."], ["and so it’s useless even to think of it, and I must go to them in silence and do my duty...", "그러니 그걸 생각해 봤자 소용없고, 나는 잠자코 그들에게 가서 내 의무를 다해야 해……."], ["in silence, too... and not ask forgiveness, and say nothing...", "역시 잠자코……, 용서를 구하지도 말고, 아무 말도 하지 말고……."], ["for all is lost now!”", "이제 모든 게 끝장났으니까!\""]], [["And yet as he dressed he examined his attire more carefully than usual.", "그러면서도 옷을 입을 때 그는 평소보다 더 꼼꼼하게 자기 옷차림을 살폈다."], ["He hadn’t another suit--if he had had, perhaps he wouldn’t have put it on.", "다른 옷이 없었다 — 만약 있었다면, 어쩌면 그는 그걸 입지 않았을지도 모른다."], ["“I would have made a point of not putting it on.”", "\"일부러 안 입었을 거야.\""], ["But in any case he could not remain a cynic and a dirty sloven; he had no right to offend the feelings of others, especially when they were in need of his assistance and asking him to see them.", "그러나 어쨌든 그는 냉소적이고 지저분한 추레한 꼴로 있을 수는 없었다. 그에게는 남들의 감정을 상하게 할 권리가 없었으니, 더구나 그들이 그의 도움을 필요로 하며 자기들을 보러 와 달라고 청하고 있는 마당에야."], ["He brushed his clothes carefully.", "그는 정성껏 옷을 솔질했다."], ["His linen was always decent; in that respect he was especially clean.", "그의 속옷은 늘 멀쩡했다. 그 점에서 그는 유난히 청결했다."]], [["He washed that morning scrupulously--he got some soap from Nastasya--he washed his hair, his neck and especially his hands.", "그날 아침 그는 빈틈없이 몸을 씻었다 — 나스타시야한테서 비누를 좀 얻어 — 머리며, 목이며, 특히 손을 씻었다."], ["When it came to the question whether to shave his stubbly chin or not (Praskovya Pavlovna had capital razors that had been left by her late husband), the question was angrily answered in the negative.", "까칠한 턱수염을 깎을지 말지 하는 문제에 이르자(프라스코비야 파블로브나에게는 죽은 남편이 남긴 훌륭한 면도칼이 있었다), 그 문제는 성난 듯 부정적으로 결론지어졌다."], ["“Let it stay as it is!", "\"그냥 이대로 두자!"], ["What if they think that I shaved on purpose to...?", "만일 내가 일부러……, 하려고 면도를 했다고 생각하면 어쩌지?"], ["They certainly would think so!", "분명히 그렇게 생각할 거야!"], ["Not on any account!”", "절대 안 돼!\""]], [["“And... the worst of it was he was so coarse, so dirty, he had the manners of a pothouse; and...", "\"그리고……, 무엇보다 최악인 건 그가 너무 거칠고, 너무 지저분하고, 선술집 사람 같은 매너를 지녔다는 것이고……, 또……, 자기에게 신사의 본질적인 요소가 좀 있다는 걸 안다 치더라도……, 거기에 무슨 뽐낼 거리가 있단 말인가?"], ["and even admitting that he knew he had some of the essentials of a gentleman... what was there in that to be proud of?", "누구나 신사여야 하고, 그 이상이어야 하지……, 게다가 (그는 떠올렸다) 그 자신도 자잘한 짓들을 저질러 왔지……, 딱히 부정직하달 건 아니지만, 그래도……."], ["Everyone ought to be a gentleman and more than that...", "그리고 가끔 어떤 생각들을 했던가."], ["and all the same (he remembered) he, too, had done little things... not exactly dishonest, and yet....", "흠……, 그런데 그 모든 걸 아브도티야 로마노브나 곁에 갖다 놓다니!"], ["And what thoughts he sometimes had; hm... and to set all that beside Avdotya Romanovna!", "빌어먹을!"], ["Confound it!", "될 대로 되라지!"], ["So be it! Well, he’d make a point then of being dirty, greasy, pothouse in his manners and he wouldn’t care!", "그래, 그럼 일부러 지저분하고, 기름때 절고, 선술집 같은 매너로 굴어 주지, 그러든 말든 상관 안 해!"], ["He’d be worse!”", "더 험하게 굴어 줄 테다!\""]], [["He was engaged in such monologues when Zossimov, who had spent the night in Praskovya Pavlovna’s parlour, came in.", "그가 이런 독백에 빠져 있을 때, 프라스코비야 파블로브나의 거실에서 밤을 보낸 조시모프가 들어왔다."]], [["He was going home and was in a hurry to look at the invalid first.", "그는 집에 가려던 참이었는데, 먼저 환자를 들여다보려고 서두르고 있었다."], ["Razumihin informed him that Raskolnikov was sleeping like a dormouse.", "라주미힌은 그에게 라스콜니코프가 잠꾸러기 쥐처럼 곤히 자고 있다고 알려 주었다."], ["Zossimov gave orders that they shouldn’t wake him and promised to see him again about eleven.", "조시모프는 그를 깨우지 말라고 지시하고, 열한 시쯤 다시 들르겠다고 약속했다."]], [["“If he is still at home,” he added.", "\"만일 그가 아직 집에 있다면 말이지.\""], ["“Damn it all!", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["If one can’t control one’s patients, how is one to cure them?", "\"젠장할! 자기 환자를 통제하지 못한다면, 어떻게 그를 고친단 말인가?"], ["Do you know whether _he_ will go to them, or whether _they_ are coming here?”", "자네 혹시 아나, 그가 그들에게 갈지, 아니면 그들이 이리로 올지?\""]], [["“They are coming, I think,”", "\"올 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Razumihin, understanding the object of the question, “and they will discuss their family affairs, no doubt.", "라주미힌이 그 물음의 의도를 알아채고 말했다. \"그리고 분명 자기네 집안일을 의논하겠지요."], ["I’ll be off.", "저는 가겠습니다."], ["You, as the doctor, have more right to be here than I.”", "의사로서 당신이 저보다 여기 있을 권리가 더 있으니까요.\""]], [["“But I am not a father confessor; I shall come and go away; I’ve plenty to do besides looking after them.”", "\"하지만 난 고해 신부가 아닐세. 잠깐 들렀다 갈 거야. 그들을 돌보는 것 말고도 할 일이 많거든.\""]], [["“One thing worries me,” interposed Razumihin, frowning.", "\"한 가지 마음에 걸리는 게 있습니다.\""], ["“On the way home I talked a lot of drunken nonsense to him... all sorts of things...", "라주미힌이 얼굴을 찌푸리며 끼어들었다."], ["and amongst them that you were afraid that he... might become insane.”", "\"집으로 오는 길에 제가 그에게 취해서 헛소리를 잔뜩 늘어놓았거든요……, 별의별 얘기를……, 그중에는 당신이 그가……, 미치지 않을까 걱정한다는 얘기도 있었어요.\""]], [["“You told the ladies so, too.”", "\"그 부인들한테도 그렇게 말했잖나.\""]], [["“I know it was stupid!", "\"멍청한 짓이었다는 거 압니다!"], ["You may beat me if you like!", "원하시면 저를 때리셔도 됩니다!"], ["Did you think so seriously?”", "정말 그렇게 진지하게 생각하셨던 겁니까?\""]], [["“That’s nonsense, I tell you, how could I think it seriously? You, yourself, described him as a monomaniac when you fetched me to him...", "\"그건 헛소리라니까, 내가 어떻게 그걸 진지하게 생각하겠나?"], ["and we added fuel to the fire yesterday, you did, that is, with your story about the painter; it was a nice conversation, when he was, perhaps, mad on that very point!", "자네가 직접, 그에게 날 데려갔을 때 그를 편집광이라고 묘사했지……, 그리고 어제 우리가 거기에 기름을 부은 거야, 그러니까 자네가 말일세, 화가 얘기로 말이야. 어쩌면 그가 바로 그 점에 미쳐 있었을지도 모르는 판에, 그건 참 멋진 대화였지!"], ["If only I’d known what happened then at the police station and that some wretch... had insulted him with this suspicion!", "그때 경찰서에서 무슨 일이 있었는지, 어떤 못된 작자가……, 그런 의심으로 그를 모욕했다는 걸 내가 알았더라면!"], ["Hm... I would not have allowed that conversation yesterday.", "흠……, 어제 그런 대화를 하게 두진 않았을 텐데."], ["These monomaniacs will make a mountain out of a mole-hill... and see their fancies as solid realities....", "이런 편집광들은 두더지가 파 놓은 흙더미를 가지고 산을 만들고……, 자기 망상을 단단한 현실로 본단 말이지……."], ["As far as I remember, it was Zametov’s story that cleared up half the mystery, to my mind.", "내 기억으로는, 제 생각엔 자메토프의 이야기가 수수께끼의 절반을 풀어 줬어."], ["Why, I know one case in which a hypochondriac, a man of forty, cut the throat of a little boy of eight, because he couldn’t endure the jokes he made every day at table!", "아, 내가 아는 어떤 사례가 있는데, 마흔 살 먹은 우울증 환자가 여덟 살짜리 사내아이의 목을 베었지, 매일 식탁에서 그 애가 하는 농담을 견딜 수 없다는 이유로!"], ["And in this case his rags, the insolent police officer, the fever and this suspicion!", "그런데 이 경우에는 그의 누더기에다, 무례한 경찰관에다, 열병에다, 게다가 이 의심까지!"], ["All that working upon a man half frantic with hypochondria, and with his morbid exceptional vanity!", "그 모든 게 우울증으로 반쯤 미쳐 버린 인간한테, 게다가 그 병적이고 유별난 자존심까지 지닌 인간한테 작용한 거지!"], ["That may well have been the starting-point of illness.", "그게 병의 발단이 됐을 만도 해."], ["Well, bother it all!...", "에이, 다 집어치우자!……."], ["And, by the way, that Zametov certainly is a nice fellow, but hm... he shouldn’t have told all that last night.", "그건 그렇고, 그 자메토프란 자는 분명 괜찮은 친구긴 한데, 흠……, 어젯밤에 그 모든 걸 떠벌리지는 말았어야 했어."], ["He is an awful chatterbox!”", "정말 지독한 수다쟁이라니까!\""]], [["“But whom did he tell it to?", "\"그런데 그가 누구한테 말한 거지요?"], ["You and me?”", "당신과 저한테요?\""]], [["“And Porfiry.”", "\"포르피리한테도.\""]], [["“What does that matter?”", "\"그게 무슨 문제가 됩니까?\""]], [["“And, by the way, have you any influence on them, his mother and sister?", "\"그건 그렇고, 자네 그들한테, 그러니까 그의 어머니와 누이한테 무슨 영향력이 좀 있나?"], ["Tell them to be more careful with him to-day....”", "오늘은 그를 좀 더 조심스럽게 대하라고 말해 주게…….\""]], [["“They’ll get on all right!”", "\"잘들 해낼 겁니다!\""], ["Razumihin answered reluctantly.", "라주미힌이 마지못해 대답했다."]], [["“Why is he so set against this Luzhin?", "\"그는 왜 그렇게 그 루진이란 자한테 반감을 갖는 거지?"], ["A man with money and she doesn’t seem to dislike him... and they haven’t a farthing, I suppose?", "돈도 있는 사람이고, 그녀도 딱히 그를 싫어하는 것 같진 않은데……, 그리고 그들은 한 푼도 없는 것 같던데, 안 그래?"], ["eh?”", "어?\""]], [["“But what business is it of yours?”", "\"하지만 그게 당신과 무슨 상관입니까?\""], ["Razumihin cried with annoyance.", "라주미힌이 짜증스레 소리쳤다."], ["“How can I tell whether they’ve a farthing?", "\"그들에게 한 푼이라도 있는지 없는지 제가 어떻게 압니까?"], ["Ask them yourself and perhaps you’ll find out....”", "직접 물어보시면 어쩌면 알아내실 수도 있겠지요…….\""]], [["“Foo!", "\"쳇!"], ["what an ass you are sometimes!", "자넨 가끔 정말 멍청이라니까!"], ["Last night’s wine has not gone off yet....", "어젯밤 마신 술이 아직 안 깼군……. 잘 있게."], ["Good-bye; thank your Praskovya Pavlovna from me for my night’s lodging.", "자네의 프라스코비야 파블로브나한테 하룻밤 재워 줘서 고맙다고 전해 주게."], ["She locked herself in, made no reply to my _bonjour_ through the door; she was up at seven o’clock, the samovar was taken into her from the kitchen.", "그녀는 문을 잠가 버리고, 문 너머로 내가 보낸 인사에도 대답이 없더군. 일곱 시에 일어나서, 부엌에서 사모바르를 자기 방으로 가져가게 했고."], ["I was not vouchsafed a personal interview....”", "나한테는 직접 만나 주는 영광을 베풀지 않았다네…….\""]], [["At nine o’clock precisely Razumihin reached the lodgings at Bakaleyev’s house.", "정확히 아홉 시에 라주미힌은 바칼레예프의 집에 있는 셋방에 도착했다."], ["Both ladies were waiting for him with nervous impatience.", "두 부인은 초조하게 안달하며 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["They had risen at seven o’clock or earlier.", "그들은 일곱 시나 그 전에 일어났던 것이다."], ["He entered looking as black as night, bowed awkwardly and was at once furious with himself for it.", "그는 밤처럼 어두운 표정으로 들어와 어색하게 인사를 했고, 곧바로 그런 자신에게 화가 치밀었다."], ["He had reckoned without his host: Pulcheria Alexandrovna fairly rushed at him, seized him by both hands and was almost kissing them.", "그의 예상은 빗나갔다. 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 그에게 그야말로 달려들어 두 손을 붙잡고는 거의 입을 맞추려 했다."], ["He glanced timidly at Avdotya Romanovna, but her proud countenance wore at that moment an expression of such gratitude and friendliness, such complete and unlooked-for respect (in place of the sneering looks and ill-disguised contempt he had expected), that it threw him into greater confusion than if he had been met with abuse.", "그는 머뭇거리며 아브도티야 로마노브나를 흘긋 보았는데, 그 도도한 얼굴에는 그 순간 그런 감사와 다정함, 그토록 온전하고 뜻밖의 존경의 빛이 어려 있어서(그가 예상했던 비웃는 눈길과 미처 감추지 못한 경멸 대신에) 차라리 욕설로 맞이했을 때보다 그를 더 큰 혼란에 빠뜨렸다."], ["Fortunately there was a subject for conversation, and he made haste to snatch at it.", "다행히 대화거리가 있었고, 그는 서둘러 그것을 붙잡았다."]], [["Hearing that everything was going well and that Rodya had not yet waked, Pulcheria Alexandrovna declared that she was glad to hear it, because “she had something which it was very, very necessary to talk over beforehand.”", "모든 일이 잘 되어 가고 있으며 로댜가 아직 깨어나지 않았다는 말을 듣자, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 그 소식이 기쁘다고 밝혔는데, \"미리 아주, 아주 긴히 의논해야 할 일이 있기\" 때문이라고 했다."], ["Then followed an inquiry about breakfast and an invitation to have it with them; they had waited to have it with him.", "그러고는 아침 식사에 대해 묻고는 함께 들자고 청했다. 그들은 그와 함께 먹으려고 기다리고 있었던 것이다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna rang the bell: it was answered by a ragged dirty waiter, and they asked him to bring tea which was served at last, but in such a dirty and disorderly way that the ladies were ashamed.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 종을 울리자, 누더기 차림의 지저분한 종업원이 응답해 왔고, 그들은 그에게 차를 가져다 달라고 했다. 차는 마침내 나왔지만 어찌나 더럽고 어수선하게 차려져 나왔는지 부인들이 부끄러워할 정도였다."], ["Razumihin vigorously attacked the lodgings, but, remembering Luzhin, stopped in embarrassment and was greatly relieved by Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s questions, which showered in a continual stream upon him.", "라주미힌은 이 셋방을 격하게 비난하려다가, 루진을 떠올리고는 당황해 입을 다물었고, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 끊임없이 쏟아 내는 질문 덕분에 크게 안도했다."]], [["He talked for three quarters of an hour, being constantly interrupted by their questions, and succeeded in describing to them all the most important facts he knew of the last year of Raskolnikov’s life, concluding with a circumstantial account of his illness.", "그는 사십오 분 동안 이야기했는데, 그들의 질문에 끊임없이 가로막히면서도, 라스콜니코프의 지난 한 해 삶에 대해 자신이 아는 가장 중요한 사실들을 죄다 그들에게 들려주는 데 성공했고, 그의 병에 관한 상세한 설명으로 이야기를 맺었다."], ["He omitted, however, many things, which were better omitted, including the scene at the police station with all its consequences.", "그러나 그는 차라리 빼는 편이 나은 많은 것을, 경찰서에서 있었던 장면과 그 모든 여파를 포함해 생략했다."], ["They listened eagerly to his story, and, when he thought he had finished and satisfied his listeners, he found that they considered he had hardly begun.", "그들은 그의 이야기를 열심히 들었고, 그가 이야기를 마쳤고 듣는 이들을 만족시켰다고 생각했을 때, 그는 그들이 자기가 이제 겨우 시작했을 뿐이라고 여긴다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“Tell me, tell me!", "\"말씀해 주세요, 말씀을!"], ["What do you think...?", "당신 생각엔……?"], ["Excuse me, I still don’t know your name!”", "죄송해요, 아직도 성함을 모르네요!\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna put in hastily.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 황급히 끼어들었다."]], [["“Dmitri Prokofitch.”", "\"드미트리 프로코피치입니다.\""]], [["“I should like very, very much to know, Dmitri Prokofitch... how he looks...", "\"정말, 정말 알고 싶어요, 드미트리 프로코피치……, 그 애가 지금……, 전반적으로 모든 것에 대해 어떻게 보고 있는지, 그러니까, 뭐랄까, 어떻게 설명해야 하나, 그 애가 무얼 좋아하고 무얼 싫어하는지요?"], ["on things in general now, that is, how can I explain, what are his likes and dislikes? Is he always so irritable?", "늘 그렇게 신경질적인가요?"], ["Tell me, if you can, what are his hopes and, so to say, his dreams?", "할 수 있으시면 말씀해 주세요, 그 애의 희망은, 말하자면, 그 애의 꿈은 뭔가요?"], ["Under what influences is he now?", "지금 어떤 영향 아래 있나요?"], ["In a word, I should like...”", "한마디로, 알고 싶은 게…….\""]], [["“Ah, mother, how can he answer all that at once?”", "\"아, 어머니, 그분이 그걸 어떻게 한꺼번에 다 대답하시겠어요?\""], ["observed Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."]], [["“Good heavens, I had not expected to find him in the least like this, Dmitri Prokofitch!”", "\"맙소사, 그 애가 이런 모습일 줄은 조금도 예상 못 했어요, 드미트리 프로코피치!\""]], [["“Naturally,”", "\"당연하지요.\""], ["answered Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 대답했다."], ["“I have no mother, but my uncle comes every year and almost every time he can scarcely recognise me, even in appearance, though he is a clever man; and your three years’ separation means a great deal.", "\"저는 어머니가 안 계시지만, 제 삼촌은 해마다 오시는데, 거의 매번 저를 외양만으로도 알아보지 못하세요, 영리한 분이신데도 말이지요. 그러니 3년이나 떨어져 계셨다는 건 정말 큰 의미가 있습니다."], ["What am I to tell you?", "제가 뭐라고 말씀드려야 할까요?"], ["I have known Rodion for a year and a half; he is morose, gloomy, proud and haughty, and of late--and perhaps for a long time before--he has been suspicious and fanciful.", "저는 로디온을 1년 반 동안 알고 지냈습니다."], ["He has a noble nature and a kind heart.", "그는 침울하고, 음울하고, 자존심 세고, 오만합니다."], ["He does not like showing his feelings and would rather do a cruel thing than open his heart freely.", "그리고 최근에는 — 어쩌면 그 전부터 오랫동안 — 의심이 많고 공상에 잘 빠져 있었지요. 그는 고결한 본성과 따뜻한 마음을 지녔습니다."], ["Sometimes, though, he is not at all morbid, but simply cold and inhumanly callous; it’s as though he were alternating between two characters.", "그는 자기 감정을 드러내기를 좋아하지 않고, 차라리 마음을 자유로이 여는 것보다 잔인한 짓을 하려 들지요. 하지만 가끔은 전혀 병적이지 않고, 그저 차갑고 비인간적으로 무정할 때도 있어요."], ["Sometimes he is fearfully reserved!", "마치 두 인격 사이를 오가는 것 같습니다."], ["He says he is so busy that everything is a hindrance, and yet he lies in bed doing nothing.", "때로는 무섭도록 말이 없지요! 그는 자기가 너무 바빠서 모든 게 방해된다고 말하면서도, 정작 침대에 누워 아무것도 안 합니다."], ["He doesn’t jeer at things, not because he hasn’t the wit, but as though he hadn’t time to waste on such trifles.", "그는 세상일을 비웃지 않는데, 그건 재치가 없어서가 아니라 마치 그런 사소한 것들에 허비할 시간이 없다는 듯한 태도지요."], ["He never listens to what is said to him.", "그는 자기에게 하는 말을 결코 귀담아듣지 않아요."], ["He is never interested in what interests other people at any given moment.", "어느 순간이든 남들이 관심을 갖는 일에는 결코 흥미를 느끼지 못하고요."], ["He thinks very highly of himself and perhaps he is right.", "그는 자기 자신을 아주 높이 평가하는데, 어쩌면 그게 옳을지도 모르지요."], ["Well, what more?", "자, 또 뭐가 있을까요?"], ["I think your arrival will have a most beneficial influence upon him.”", "제 생각엔 두 분의 도착이 그에게 더없이 유익한 영향을 미칠 겁니다.\""]], [["“God grant it may,”", "\"하느님, 부디 그렇게 되게 해 주소서.\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna, distressed by Razumihin’s account of her Rodya.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 자기 로댜에 대한 라주미힌의 이야기에 가슴 아파하며 외쳤다."]], [["And Razumihin ventured to look more boldly at Avdotya Romanovna at last.", "그리하여 라주미힌은 마침내 좀 더 대담하게 아브도티야 로마노브나를 바라볼 용기를 냈다."], ["He glanced at her often while he was talking, but only for a moment and looked away again at once.", "그는 이야기하는 동안 그녀를 자주 흘긋거렸지만, 매번 한순간뿐이었고 곧바로 다시 눈길을 돌리곤 했다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna sat at the table, listening attentively, then got up again and began walking to and fro with her arms folded and her lips compressed, occasionally putting in a question, without stopping her walk.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 탁자에 앉아 주의 깊게 듣고 있다가, 다시 일어나서 팔짱을 끼고 입술을 꼭 다문 채 이리저리 거닐기 시작했고, 가끔씩 걸음을 멈추지 않고 질문을 던지곤 했다."], ["She had the same habit of not listening to what was said.", "그녀에게도 자기에게 하는 말을 귀담아듣지 않는 똑같은 버릇이 있었다."], ["She was wearing a dress of thin dark stuff and she had a white transparent scarf round her neck.", "그녀는 얇은 짙은 색 천으로 지은 옷을 입고, 목에는 하얗고 투명한 스카프를 두르고 있었다."], ["Razumihin soon detected signs of extreme poverty in their belongings.", "라주미힌은 곧 그들의 소지품에서 극심한 가난의 흔적을 알아챘다."], ["Had Avdotya Romanovna been dressed like a queen, he felt that he would not be afraid of her, but perhaps just because she was poorly dressed and that he noticed all the misery of her surroundings, his heart was filled with dread and he began to be afraid of every word he uttered, every gesture he made, which was very trying for a man who already felt diffident.", "만일 아브도티야 로마노브나가 여왕처럼 차려입었더라면 그는 그녀가 두렵지 않았을 거라고 느꼈지만, 어쩌면 바로 그녀가 초라하게 차려입었기 때문에, 그리고 그녀를 둘러싼 온갖 비참함을 그가 알아챘기 때문에 그의 가슴은 두려움으로 가득 찼고, 자기가 내뱉는 한마디 한마디, 자기가 하는 몸짓 하나하나가 두려워지기 시작했는데, 이는 이미 주눅 들어 있던 남자에게는 무척이나 힘든 일이었다."]], [["“You’ve told us a great deal that is interesting about my brother’s character... and have told it impartially.", "\"제 오빠의 성격에 관해 흥미로운 이야기를 많이 해 주셨어요……, 그것도 공정하게요."], ["I am glad.", "기뻐요."], ["I thought that you were too uncritically devoted to him,”", "저는 당신이 오빠한테 너무 무비판적으로 헌신적인 분이라고 생각했거든요.\""], ["observed Avdotya Romanovna with a smile.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I think you are right that he needs a woman’s care,”", "\"오빠한테 여자의 보살핌이 필요하다는 당신 말씀이 옳다고 생각해요.\""], ["she added thoughtfully.", "그녀가 사려 깊게 덧붙였다."]], [["“I didn’t say so; but I daresay you are right, only...” “What?”", "\"제가 그렇게 말한 건 아닙니다만, 당신 말씀이 옳을 겁니다, 다만…….\""]], [["“He loves no one and perhaps he never will,”", "\"다만 뭐요?\" \"그는 아무도 사랑하지 않고, 어쩌면 영영 사랑하지 못할 겁니다.\""], ["Razumihin declared decisively.", "라주미힌이 단호하게 단언했다."]], [["“You mean he is not capable of love?”", "\"그가 사랑할 능력이 없다는 말씀인가요?\""]], [["“Do you know, Avdotya Romanovna, you are awfully like your brother, in everything, indeed!”", "\"있잖아요, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 당신은 정말 무섭도록 오빠를 닮았어요, 모든 면에서, 정말로요!\""], ["he blurted out suddenly to his own surprise, but remembering at once what he had just before said of her brother, he turned as red as a crab and was overcome with confusion.", "그는 자기도 놀랄 만큼 불쑥 내뱉었다가, 방금 전 그녀의 오빠에 대해 한 말을 곧장 떠올리고는 가재처럼 새빨개져서 당혹감에 휩싸였다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna couldn’t help laughing when she looked at him.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 그를 보고 웃지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“You may both be mistaken about Rodya,”", "\"두 사람 다 로댜에 대해 잘못 알고 있을지도 몰라요.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna remarked, slightly piqued.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 살짝 언짢은 듯 말했다."], ["“I am not talking of our present difficulty, Dounia.", "\"지금 우리가 처한 어려움을 두고 하는 말은 아니야, 두냐."], ["What Pyotr Petrovitch writes in this letter and what you and I have supposed may be mistaken, but you can’t imagine, Dmitri Prokofitch, how moody and, so to say, capricious he is.", "표트르 페트로비치가 이 편지에 쓴 것이나 너랑 내가 짐작한 게 틀렸을 수도 있지. 하지만 드미트리 프로코피치, 그 애가 얼마나 변덕스럽고, 말하자면, 종잡을 수 없는지 당신은 상상도 못 할 거예요."], ["I never could depend on what he would do when he was only fifteen.", "그 애가 겨우 열다섯 살이었을 때도 저는 그 애가 무슨 짓을 할지 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어요."], ["And I am sure that he might do something now that nobody else would think of doing...", "그리고 지금도 그 애는 분명 아무도 생각지 못할 일을 저지를 수 있어요……."], ["Well, for instance, do you know how a year and a half ago he astounded me and gave me a shock that nearly killed me, when he had the idea of marrying that girl--what was her name--his landlady’s daughter?”", "자, 예를 들어, 일 년 반 전에 그 애가 어떤 처녀랑 — 이름이 뭐였더라 — 자기 집주인 딸이랑 결혼하겠다는 생각을 했을 때, 그 애가 저를 얼마나 놀라게 했는지, 슬픔으로 거의 죽을 뻔할 만큼 충격을 줬는지 아세요?\""]], [["“Did you hear about that affair?”", "\"그 일에 대해 들으셨어요?\""], ["asked Avdotya Romanovna.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 물었다."]], [["“Do you suppose----”", "\"설마—\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna continued warmly.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 열을 올리며 말을 이었다."], ["“Do you suppose that my tears, my entreaties, my illness, my possible death from grief, our poverty would have made him pause?", "\"제 눈물이며, 제 애원이며, 제 병이며, 슬픔으로 죽을지도 모른다는 것이며, 우리의 가난이 그 애를 멈추게 했을 거라고 생각하세요?"], ["No, he would calmly have disregarded all obstacles.", "천만에요, 그 애는 그 모든 장애물을 태연히 무시했을 거예요."], ["And yet it isn’t that he doesn’t love us!”", "그런데도 그게 우리를 사랑하지 않아서가 아니라니까요!\""]], [["“He has never spoken a word of that affair to me,”", "\"그는 제게 그 일에 대해 한마디도 한 적이 없습니다.\""], ["Razumihin answered cautiously.", "라주미힌이 조심스럽게 대답했다."], ["“But I did hear something from Praskovya Pavlovna herself, though she is by no means a gossip.", "\"하지만 프라스코비야 파블로브나한테서 직접 뭔가 듣긴 했지요, 그녀가 결코 수다스러운 사람은 아닙니다만."], ["And what I heard certainly was rather strange.”", "그리고 제가 들은 건 분명 좀 이상한 이야기였습니다.\""]], [["“And what did you hear?”", "\"무슨 얘길 들으셨나요?\""], ["both the ladies asked at once.", "두 부인이 동시에 물었다."]], [["“Well, nothing very special.", "\"뭐, 별로 특별한 건 없습니다."], ["I only learned that the marriage, which only failed to take place through the girl’s death, was not at all to Praskovya Pavlovna’s liking.", "그저 그 결혼이 — 처녀가 죽는 바람에 성사되지 못한 그 결혼 말입니다만 — 프라스코비야 파블로브나의 마음에 전혀 들지 않았다는 것만 알았어요."], ["They say, too, the girl was not at all pretty, in fact I am told positively ugly... and such an invalid...", "게다가 그 처녀가 전혀 예쁘지 않았다고 하더군요, 실은 딱 잘라 못생겼다고 들었습니다……, 게다가 그런 병약한 몸에다……, 또 별난 데가 있었다고요."], ["and queer. But she seems to have had some good qualities.", "하지만 무언가 좋은 점이 있었던 모양입니다."], ["She must have had some good qualities or it’s quite inexplicable....", "좋은 점이 있었던 게 틀림없어요, 안 그러면 도무지 설명이 안 되니까요……."], ["She had no money either and he wouldn’t have considered her money....", "그 처녀도 돈이 없었고, 그가 그 처녀의 돈을 따졌을 리도 없고요……."], ["But it’s always difficult to judge in such matters.”", "하지만 그런 문제는 늘 판단하기가 어려운 법이지요.\""]], [["“I am sure she was a good girl,”", "\"분명 착한 처녀였을 거예요.\""], ["Avdotya Romanovna observed briefly.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 짤막하게 말했다."]], [["“God forgive me, I simply rejoiced at her death.", "\"하느님, 용서하소서, 저는 그저 그 애가 죽은 게 기뻤어요."], ["Though I don’t know which of them would have caused most misery to the other--he to her or she to him,”", "하기야 둘 중 누가 상대에게 더 큰 불행을 안겨 줬을지는 모르겠지만요 — 그 애가 그 처녀에게인지, 그 처녀가 그 애에게인지.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna concluded.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 맺었다."], ["Then she began tentatively questioning him about the scene on the previous day with Luzhin, hesitating and continually glancing at Dounia, obviously to the latter’s annoyance.", "그러고는 그에게 어제 루진과 있었던 장면에 대해 조심스럽게 물어보기 시작했는데, 머뭇거리며 끊임없이 두냐를 흘긋거렸고, 이는 분명 후자를 언짢게 했다."], ["This incident more than all the rest evidently caused her uneasiness, even consternation.", "다른 무엇보다도 바로 이 사건이 그녀를 불안하게, 심지어 경악하게 만든 것이 분명했다."], ["Razumihin described it in detail again, but this time he added his own conclusions: he openly blamed Raskolnikov for intentionally insulting Pyotr Petrovitch, not seeking to excuse him on the score of his illness.", "라주미힌은 그것을 다시 한번 상세히 묘사했지만, 이번에는 자기 나름의 결론을 덧붙였다. 그는 라스콜니코프가 일부러 표트르 페트로비치를 모욕했다고 노골적으로 비난했고, 그의 병을 핑계 삼아 그를 변호하려 들지 않았다."]], [["“He had planned it before his illness,”", "\"그는 병이 나기 전부터 그걸 계획했던 겁니다.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“I think so, too,”", "\"저도 그렇게 생각해요.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna agreed with a dejected air.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 풀죽은 기색으로 동의했다."], ["But she was very much surprised at hearing Razumihin express himself so carefully and even with a certain respect about Pyotr Petrovitch.", "하지만 라주미힌이 표트르 페트로비치에 대해 그토록 조심스럽게, 심지어 어느 정도 존중하는 태도로 말하는 것을 듣고 그녀는 무척 놀랐다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna, too, was struck by it.", "아브도티야 로마노브나도 그것에 놀랐다."]], [["“So this is your opinion of Pyotr Petrovitch?”", "\"그럼 표트르 페트로비치에 대한 당신 생각이 그렇다는 건가요?\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna could not resist asking.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 묻지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I can have no other opinion of your daughter’s future husband,”", "\"제가 따님의 장래 남편에 대해 다른 생각을 가질 수는 없지요.\""], ["Razumihin answered firmly and with warmth, “and I don’t say it simply from vulgar politeness, but because...", "라주미힌이 단호하게, 그리고 열의를 담아 대답했다."], ["simply because Avdotya Romanovna has of her own free will deigned to accept this man.", "\"그리고 이건 단순히 천박한 예의에서 하는 말이 아니라, 그저……, 그저 아브도티야 로마노브나께서 자유의지로 이 사람을 받아들이기로 하셨기 때문입니다."], ["If I spoke so rudely of him last night, it was because I was disgustingly drunk and...", "어젯밤 제가 그에 대해 그토록 무례하게 떠들었다면, 그건 제가 역겨울 만큼 취해 있었고……, 게다가 미쳐 있었기 때문이지요."], ["mad besides; yes, mad, crazy, I lost my head completely... and this morning I am ashamed of it.”", "그래요, 미쳤었어요, 정신이 나갔었어요, 완전히 이성을 잃었던 겁니다……, 그리고 오늘 아침 저는 그게 부끄럽습니다.\""]], [["He crimsoned and ceased speaking.", "그는 얼굴이 새빨개져 말을 멈췄다."], ["Avdotya Romanovna flushed, but did not break the silence.", "아브도티야 로마노브나도 얼굴을 붉혔지만, 침묵을 깨지는 않았다."], ["She had not uttered a word from the moment they began to speak of Luzhin.", "그녀는 그들이 루진 이야기를 시작한 순간부터 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["Without her support Pulcheria Alexandrovna obviously did not know what to do.", "그녀의 도움 없이는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 분명 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."], ["At last, faltering and continually glancing at her daughter, she confessed that she was exceedingly worried by one circumstance.", "마침내 그녀는 머뭇거리며 끊임없이 딸을 흘긋거리다가, 한 가지 사정 때문에 몹시 걱정이 된다고 털어놓았다."]], [["“You see, Dmitri Prokofitch,”", "\"있잖아요, 드미트리 프로코피치.\""], ["she began.", "그녀가 입을 열었다."], ["“I’ll be perfectly open with Dmitri Prokofitch, Dounia?”", "\"드미트리 프로코피치한테 솔직하게 다 말해도 되겠니, 두냐?\""]], [["“Of course, mother,”", "\"물론이죠, 어머니.\""], ["said Avdotya Romanovna emphatically.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“This is what it is,”", "\"바로 이런 일이에요.\""], ["she began in haste, as though the permission to speak of her trouble lifted a weight off her mind.", "그녀는 자기 고민을 털어놓아도 좋다는 허락이 마음의 짐을 덜어 주기라도 한 듯 황급히 말을 시작했다."], ["“Very early this morning we got a note from Pyotr Petrovitch in reply to our letter announcing our arrival.", "\"오늘 아침 아주 일찍, 우리가 도착한다고 알린 편지에 대한 답으로 표트르 페트로비치한테서 쪽지가 하나 왔어요."], ["He promised to meet us at the station, you know; instead of that he sent a servant to bring us the address of these lodgings and to show us the way; and he sent a message that he would be here himself this morning.", "그는 역에서 우리를 마중하겠다고 약속했었잖아요, 아시다시피. 그런데 그러는 대신, 하인을 보내 이 셋방 주소를 알려 주고 길을 안내하게 했어요."], ["But this morning this note came from him.", "그리고 오늘 아침에 자기가 직접 여기로 오겠다는 전갈을 보냈고요."], ["You’d better read it yourself; there is one point in it which worries me very much...", "그런데 오늘 아침 그한테서 이 쪽지가 온 거예요. 직접 읽어 보시는 게 좋겠어요."], ["you will soon see what that is, and... tell me your candid opinion, Dmitri Prokofitch!", "거기에 저를 무척 걱정스럽게 하는 대목이 하나 있는데……, 곧 그게 뭔지 아시게 될 거예요, 그리고……, 솔직한 의견을 말씀해 주세요, 드미트리 프로코피치!"], ["You know Rodya’s character better than anyone and no one can advise us better than you can.", "당신은 누구보다 로댜의 성격을 잘 아시니, 누구도 당신만큼 우리에게 좋은 조언을 해 줄 수는 없을 거예요."], ["Dounia, I must tell you, made her decision at once, but I still don’t feel sure how to act and I... I’ve been waiting for your opinion.”", "두냐는, 말해 두지만, 곧바로 결심을 했지만, 저는 아직 어떻게 처신해야 할지 모르겠어서……, 그래서 당신 의견을 기다리고 있었어요.\""]], [["Razumihin opened the note which was dated the previous evening and read as follows:", "라주미힌은 전날 저녁 날짜가 적힌 쪽지를 펼쳐 다음과 같이 읽었다."]], [["“Dear Madam, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, I have the honour to inform you that owing to unforeseen obstacles I was rendered unable to meet you at the railway station; I sent a very competent person with the same object in view.", "\"친애하는 부인, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 예기치 못한 장애로 인해 제가 기차역에서 부인을 마중할 수 없게 되었음을 알려 드리는 영광을 갖습니다. 같은 목적으로 매우 유능한 사람을 보냈습니다."], ["I likewise shall be deprived of the honour of an interview with you to-morrow morning by business in the Senate that does not admit of delay, and also that I may not intrude on your family circle while you are meeting your son, and Avdotya Romanovna her brother.", "마찬가지로 내일 아침에도 지체할 수 없는 원로원 업무로 인해 부인을 뵙는 영광을 누리지 못하게 되었으며, 또한 부인께서 아드님을, 그리고 아브도티야 로마노브나께서 오라버니를 만나시는 동안 가족 모임에 끼어들지 않으려는 뜻도 있습니다. 늦어도 내일 저녁 정확히 여덟 시에 부인의 거처로 찾아뵙고 인사 올리는 영광을 갖겠으며, 이와 함께 한 가지 간곡하고, 덧붙이자면, 단호한 청을 감히 드리고자 합니다."], ["I shall have the honour of visiting you and paying you my respects at your lodgings not later than to-morrow evening at eight o’clock precisely, and herewith I venture to present my earnest and, I may add, imperative request that Rodion Romanovitch may not be present at our interview--as he offered me a gross and unprecedented affront on the occasion of my visit to him in his illness yesterday, and, moreover, since I desire from you personally an indispensable and circumstantial explanation upon a certain point, in regard to which I wish to learn your own interpretation.", "즉, 우리가 만나는 자리에 로디온 로마노비치가 동석하지 않게 해 주시기를 바랍니다. 그가 어제 병중인 그를 문병한 자리에서 제게 전례 없는 막대한 모욕을 가했기 때문이며, 더욱이 제가 부인께 직접, 어떤 점에 관해 반드시 필요하고 상세한 해명을 듣고자 하는데, 그에 대한 부인 자신의 해석을 알고 싶기 때문입니다."], ["I have the honour to inform you, in anticipation, that if, in spite of my request, I meet Rodion Romanovitch, I shall be compelled to withdraw immediately and then you have only yourself to blame.", "미리 알려 드리는 영광을 갖건대, 만일 제 청에도 불구하고 제가 로디온 로마노비치와 마주치게 된다면, 저는 부득이 즉시 물러날 수밖에 없으며, 그렇게 되면 그 책임은 오로지 부인께 있게 될 것입니다."], ["I write on the assumption that Rodion Romanovitch who appeared so ill at my visit, suddenly recovered two hours later and so, being able to leave the house, may visit you also.", "제가 이렇게 쓰는 것은, 제 문병 때 그토록 위중해 보이던 로디온 로마노비치가 두 시간 후에 갑자기 회복되었으니, 따라서 집을 나설 수 있게 되어 부인 또한 찾아갈 수 있으리라는 추측에서입니다."], ["I was confirmed in that belief by the testimony of my own eyes in the lodging of a drunken man who was run over and has since died, to whose daughter, a young woman of notorious behaviour, he gave twenty-five roubles on the pretext of the funeral, which gravely surprised me knowing what pains you were at to raise that sum.", "저는 마차에 치여 그 후 사망한 어느 술 취한 사내의 셋방에서 제 두 눈으로 직접 확인하여 그 믿음을 굳혔습니다. 그는 그 사내의 딸, 행실이 좋지 못하기로 소문난 젊은 여자에게 장례를 핑계로 이십오 루블을 주었는데, 부인께서 그 금액을 마련하느라 얼마나 애를 쓰셨는지 아는 저로서는 그것이 심히 놀라웠습니다."], ["Herewith expressing my special respect to your estimable daughter, Avdotya Romanovna, I beg you to accept the respectful homage of", "이로써 부인의 존경스러운 따님 아브도티야 로마노브나께 각별한 경의를 표하며, 부인께서 삼가 올리는 이 충정의 인사를 받아 주시기를 청합니다. 부인의 충실한 종,"]], [["“Your humble servant,", "페. 루진 올림.\""]], [["“What am I to do now, Dmitri Prokofitch?”", "\"이제 저는 어떻게 해야 하나요, 드미트리 프로코피치?\""], ["began Pulcheria Alexandrovna, almost weeping.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 거의 울먹이며 입을 열었다."], ["“How can I ask Rodya not to come?", "\"어떻게 로댜한테 오지 말라고 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Yesterday he insisted so earnestly on our refusing Pyotr Petrovitch and now we are ordered not to receive Rodya!", "어제는 그 애가 그토록 간절하게 우리더러 표트르 페트로비치를 거절하라고 우겼는데, 이제는 우리더러 로댜를 들이지 말라는 명령이라니!"], ["He will come on purpose if he knows, and... what will happen then?”", "알게 되면 그 애는 일부러 올 거고, 그러면……, 그때 무슨 일이 벌어질까요?\""]], [["“Act on Avdotya Romanovna’s decision,”", "\"아브도티야 로마노브나의 결정에 따르십시오.\""], ["Razumihin answered calmly at once.", "라주미힌이 곧바로 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, dear me!", "\"아이고, 맙소사!"], ["She says... goodness knows what she says, she doesn’t explain her object!", "이 애는……, 무슨 말을 하는지 도무지 모르겠어요, 자기 의도를 설명하질 않아요!"], ["She says that it would be best, at least, not that it would be best, but that it’s absolutely necessary that Rodya should make a point of being here at eight o’clock and that they must meet....", "이 애 말로는, 적어도 그게 최선이라는 게 아니라, 로댜가 반드시 여덟 시에 여기 와 있어야 하고, 그들이 꼭 만나야 한다는 거예요……."], ["I didn’t want even to show him the letter, but to prevent him from coming by some stratagem with your help... because he is so irritable....", "저는 그 애한테 편지를 보여 주기조차 싫었어요, 그저 당신 도움을 받아 무슨 수를 써서 그 애가 오지 못하게 막으려고요……, 그 애가 워낙 신경질적이니까요……."], ["Besides I don’t understand about that drunkard who died and that daughter, and how he could have given the daughter all the money... which...”", "게다가 저는 죽었다는 그 술꾼이며 그 딸 얘기도, 그 애가 어떻게 그 딸한테 돈을 다 줘 버릴 수 있었는지도 이해가 안 돼요……, 그 돈은…….\""]], [["“Which cost you such sacrifice, mother,”", "\"그 돈은 어머니가 그토록 희생해서 마련한 거잖아요.\""], ["put in Avdotya Romanovna.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 끼어들었다."]], [["“He was not himself yesterday,”", "\"그는 어제 제정신이 아니었습니다.\""], ["Razumihin said thoughtfully, “if you only knew what he was up to in a restaurant yesterday, though there was sense in it too....", "라주미힌이 사려 깊게 말했다. \"그가 어제 어느 식당에서 무슨 짓을 벌였는지 두 분이 아신다면 — 하기야 거기에도 나름의 이치는 있었지만요……."], ["Hm!", "흠!"], ["He did say something, as we were going home yesterday evening, about a dead man and a girl, but I didn’t understand a word....", "어제저녁 집으로 오는 길에 그가 죽은 사람과 어떤 처녀에 대해 무슨 말을 하긴 했는데, 저는 한마디도 알아듣지 못했습니다……."], ["But last night, I myself...”", "하지만 어젯밤, 저 자신도…….\""]], [["“The best thing, mother, will be for us to go to him ourselves and there I assure you we shall see at once what’s to be done.", "\"제일 좋은 건, 어머니, 우리가 직접 그 애한테 가는 거예요, 거기서 가면 무엇을 해야 할지 단번에 알게 될 거라고 장담해요."], ["Besides, it’s getting late--good heavens, it’s past ten,”", "게다가 시간이 늦어지고 있어요 — 맙소사, 열 시가 넘었네요.\""], ["she cried looking at a splendid gold enamelled watch which hung round her neck on a thin Venetian chain, and looked entirely out of keeping with the rest of her dress.", "그녀는 가느다란 베네치아풍 사슬로 목에 걸려 있는, 에나멜을 입힌 멋진 금시계를 보며 외쳤는데, 그것은 나머지 차림새와는 전혀 어울리지 않았다."], ["“A present from her _fiancé_,”", "'약혼자한테 받은 선물이군.'"], ["thought Razumihin.", "라주미힌은 생각했다."]], [["“We must start, Dounia, we must start,”", "\"가야겠다, 두냐, 가야 해.\""], ["her mother cried in a flutter.", "어머니가 안절부절못하며 외쳤다."], ["“He will be thinking we are still angry after yesterday, from our coming so late.", "\"우리가 이렇게 늦게 가면 그 애는 어제 일로 우리가 아직 화나 있다고 생각할 거야."], ["Merciful heavens!”", "자비로우신 하느님!\""]], [["While she said this she was hurriedly putting on her hat and mantle; Dounia, too, put on her things.", "이렇게 말하면서 그녀는 황급히 모자와 망토를 걸쳤다. 두냐도 채비를 했다."], ["Her gloves, as Razumihin noticed, were not merely shabby but had holes in them, and yet this evident poverty gave the two ladies an air of special dignity, which is always found in people who know how to wear poor clothes.", "라주미힌이 알아챘듯이 그녀의 장갑은 그저 낡았을 뿐 아니라 구멍까지 나 있었는데, 그럼에도 이 뚜렷한 가난은 두 부인에게 특별한 기품을 안겨 주었으니, 그것은 초라한 옷을 입을 줄 아는 사람들에게서 늘 발견되는 기품이었다."], ["Razumihin looked reverently at Dounia and felt proud of escorting her.", "라주미힌은 경건하게 두냐를 바라보았고, 그녀를 모시고 가는 것이 자랑스러웠다."], ["“The queen who mended her stockings in prison,”", "'감옥에서 자기 양말을 기웠다는 그 여왕도,'"], ["he thought, “must have looked then every inch a queen and even more a queen than at sumptuous banquets and levées.”", "그는 생각했다, '그때 영락없는 여왕처럼, 호화로운 연회나 알현식에서보다 한층 더 여왕처럼 보였겠지.'"]], [["“My God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “little did I think that I should ever fear seeing my son, my darling, darling Rodya!", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다. \"내가 내 아들을, 내 사랑하는, 사랑하는 로댜를 만나는 걸 두려워하게 될 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요!"], ["I am afraid, Dmitri Prokofitch,”", "저는 두려워요, 드미트리 프로코피치.\""], ["she added, glancing at him timidly.", "그녀가 머뭇거리며 그를 흘긋 보고는 덧붙였다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid, mother,”", "\"두려워 마세요, 어머니.\""], ["said Dounia, kissing her, “better have faith in him.”", "두냐가 어머니에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"차라리 오빠를 믿으세요.\""]], [["“Oh, dear, I have faith in him, but I haven’t slept all night,”", "\"아이고, 그래, 그 애를 믿지, 하지만 밤새 한숨도 못 잤구나.\""], ["exclaimed the poor woman.", "가엾은 여인이 외쳤다."]], [["They came out into the street.", "그들은 거리로 나왔다."]], [["“Do you know, Dounia, when I dozed a little this morning I dreamed of Marfa Petrovna... she was all in white...", "\"있잖니, 두냐, 오늘 아침에 내가 잠깐 졸았을 때 마르파 페트로브나 꿈을 꿨단다……, 온통 하얀 옷을 입고……, 내게 다가와 손을 잡고는 고개를 절레절레 흔드는데, 어찌나 엄하던지 마치 나를 나무라는 것 같았어……."], ["she came up to me, took my hand, and shook her head at me, but so sternly as though she were blaming me....", "그게 좋은 징조일까?"], ["Is that a good omen?", "아이고, 맙소사!"], ["Oh, dear me! You don’t know, Dmitri Prokofitch, that Marfa Petrovna’s dead!”", "드미트리 프로코피치, 당신은 모르시겠지만, 마르파 페트로브나가 죽었답니다!\""]], [["“No, I didn’t know; who is Marfa Petrovna?”", "\"네, 몰랐습니다. 마르파 페트로브나가 누구신가요?\""]], [["“She died suddenly; and only fancy...”", "\"갑자기 돌아가셨어요. 그런데 글쎄…….\""]], [["“Afterwards, mamma,”", "\"나중에요, 어머니.\""], ["put in Dounia.", "두냐가 끼어들었다."], ["“He doesn’t know who Marfa Petrovna is.”", "\"이분은 마르파 페트로브나가 누구신지 모르신다니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, you don’t know?", "\"아, 모르세요?"], ["And I was thinking that you knew all about us.", "저는 당신이 우리에 대해 다 아시는 줄 알았어요."], ["Forgive me, Dmitri Prokofitch, I don’t know what I am thinking about these last few days.", "용서하세요, 드미트리 프로코피치, 요 며칠 제가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 저도 모르겠어요."], ["I look upon you really as a providence for us, and so I took it for granted that you knew all about us.", "저는 당신을 정말 우리의 섭리처럼 여기고 있어서, 당신이 우리에 대해 다 아시는 걸 당연하게 여겼나 봐요."], ["I look on you as a relation....", "저는 당신을 친척처럼 생각해요……."], ["Don’t be angry with me for saying so.", "이런 말 한다고 화내지 마세요."], ["Dear me, what’s the matter with your right hand?", "아니, 세상에, 오른손이 어떻게 된 거예요?"], ["Have you knocked it?”", "어디 부딪치셨나요?\""]], [["“Yes, I bruised it,”", "\"네, 좀 멍이 들었어요.\""], ["muttered Razumihin overjoyed.", "라주미힌이 기쁨에 들떠 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I sometimes speak too much from the heart, so that Dounia finds fault with me....", "\"제가 가끔 너무 마음에서 우러나오는 대로 말을 많이 해서, 두냐가 저를 나무라곤 해요……."], ["But, dear me, what a cupboard he lives in!", "그런데 아니, 세상에, 그 애가 얼마나 벽장 같은 데서 사는지!"], ["I wonder whether he is awake?", "깨어나 있을까요?"], ["Does this woman, his landlady, consider it a room?", "그 여자, 그 애 집주인은 이걸 방이라고 여기는 걸까요?"], ["Listen, you say he does not like to show his feelings, so perhaps I shall annoy him with my...", "들어 보세요, 당신은 그 애가 자기 감정 드러내기를 싫어한다고 하셨는데, 그럼 제 이런……, 약한 모습이 그 애를 짜증나게 할까요?"], ["weaknesses? Do advise me, Dmitri Prokofitch, how am I to treat him?", "조언 좀 해 주세요, 드미트리 프로코피치, 제가 그 애를 어떻게 대해야 하나요?"], ["I feel quite distracted, you know.”", "저는 정말이지 정신이 하나도 없어요.\""]], [["“Don’t question him too much about anything if you see him frown; don’t ask him too much about his health; he doesn’t like that.”", "\"그가 얼굴을 찌푸리거든 무엇이든 너무 캐묻지 마세요. 건강에 대해서도 너무 묻지 마시고요. 그는 그런 걸 싫어하니까요.\""]], [["“Ah, Dmitri Prokofitch, how hard it is to be a mother!", "\"아, 드미트리 프로코피치, 어머니 노릇 하기가 어찌나 힘든지요!"], ["But here are the stairs....", "그런데 여기 계단이 있네요……."], ["What an awful staircase!”", "어쩜 이렇게 끔찍한 계단이람!\""]], [["“Mother, you are quite pale, don’t distress yourself, darling,”", "\"어머니, 얼굴이 너무 창백하세요, 너무 마음 졸이지 마세요, 어머니.\""], ["said Dounia caressing her, then with flashing eyes she added: “He ought to be happy at seeing you, and you are tormenting yourself so.”", "두냐가 어머니를 다정하게 어루만지며 말하더니, 두 눈을 반짝이며 덧붙였다. \"오빠는 어머니를 보면 기뻐해야 마땅한데, 어머니는 이렇게 스스로를 괴롭히고 계시잖아요.\""]], [["“Wait, I’ll peep in and see whether he has waked up.”", "\"잠깐, 제가 들여다보고 그 애가 깨어났는지 보고 올게요.\""]], [["The ladies slowly followed Razumihin, who went on before, and when they reached the landlady’s door on the fourth storey, they noticed that her door was a tiny crack open and that two keen black eyes were watching them from the darkness within.", "부인들은 앞서 가는 라주미힌을 천천히 뒤따랐고, 사 층의 집주인 여자네 문에 이르렀을 때, 그 여자의 문이 손가락 하나 들어갈 만큼 빠끔히 열려 있고 그 안의 어둠 속에서 날카로운 검은 두 눈이 그들을 지켜보고 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["When their eyes met, the door was suddenly shut with such a slam that Pulcheria Alexandrovna almost cried out.", "그들의 눈길이 마주치자, 문이 갑자기 어찌나 쾅 닫히는지 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 하마터면 비명을 지를 뻔했다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["“He is well, quite well!”", "“다 나았습니다, 아주 멀쩡해요!”"], ["Zossimov cried cheerfully as they entered.", "그들이 들어서자 조시모프가 명랑하게 외쳤다."]], [["He had come in ten minutes earlier and was sitting in the same place as before, on the sofa.", "그는 십 분쯤 먼저 와서 전과 같은 자리, 소파에 앉아 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov was sitting in the opposite corner, fully dressed and carefully washed and combed, as he had not been for some time past.", "라스콜니코프는 맞은편 구석에 앉아 있었는데, 옷을 다 갖춰 입고 정성껏 씻고 빗질까지 한 모습이 한동안 본 적 없던 차림새였다."], ["The room was immediately crowded, yet Nastasya managed to follow the visitors in and stayed to listen.", "방은 순식간에 사람들로 북적였지만, 나스타시야는 용케 방문객들을 따라 들어와 귀를 기울이며 서 있었다."]], [["Raskolnikov really was almost well, as compared with his condition the day before, but he was still pale, listless, and sombre.", "라스콜니코프는 전날의 상태와 비교하면 정말 거의 다 나은 셈이었지만, 여전히 창백하고 무기력하고 침울했다."], ["He looked like a wounded man or one who has undergone some terrible physical suffering.", "그는 부상당한 사람이나, 어떤 끔찍한 육체적 고통을 겪어 낸 사람처럼 보였다."], ["His brows were knitted, his lips compressed, his eyes feverish.", "두 눈썹은 찌푸려져 있었고, 입술은 꾹 다물려 있었으며, 두 눈은 열에 들떠 있었다."], ["He spoke little and reluctantly, as though performing a duty, and there was a restlessness in his movements.", "그는 마치 의무라도 수행하듯 마지못해, 말을 아꼈고, 그 동작에는 어딘가 안절부절못하는 기색이 있었다."]], [["He only wanted a sling on his arm or a bandage on his finger to complete the impression of a man with a painful abscess or a broken arm.", "이제 그에게는 한쪽 팔에 삼각건을 걸치거나 손가락에 붕대를 감는 것만 더하면, 통증이 심한 종기를 앓거나 팔이 부러진 사람이라는 인상이 완성될 것 같았다."], ["The pale, sombre face lighted up for a moment when his mother and sister entered, but this only gave it a look of more intense suffering, in place of its listless dejection.", "그 창백하고 침울한 얼굴은 어머니와 누이가 들어섰을 때 한순간 환해졌으나, 이는 도리어 그 무기력한 낙담 대신 더욱 강렬한 고통의 빛을 드리울 뿐이었다."], ["The light soon died away, but the look of suffering remained, and Zossimov, watching and studying his patient with all the zest of a young doctor beginning to practise, noticed in him no joy at the arrival of his mother and sister, but a sort of bitter, hidden determination to bear another hour or two of inevitable torture.", "그 빛은 곧 사그라들었지만 고통의 표정만은 남았고, 갓 개업한 젊은 의사의 온 열의를 다해 환자를 지켜보고 연구하던 조시모프는, 그에게서 어머니와 누이의 도착에 대한 기쁨이라곤 조금도 발견하지 못한 채, 다만 한두 시간 더 피할 수 없는 고문을 견뎌 내려는 어떤 쓰디쓴, 감추어진 결의 같은 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He saw later that almost every word of the following conversation seemed to touch on some sore place and irritate it.", "이후 그는 뒤이은 대화에서 거의 모든 말 한마디 한마디가 어떤 아픈 곳을 건드려 자극하는 것 같다는 점을 보았다."], ["But at the same time he marvelled at the power of controlling himself and hiding his feelings in a patient who the previous day had, like a monomaniac, fallen into a frenzy at the slightest word.", "그러나 동시에 그는, 전날만 해도 단지증 환자처럼 사소한 말 한마디에 광란에 빠졌던 환자가 이렇게 자제하고 감정을 감추는 그 힘에 경탄했다."]], [["“Yes, I see myself now that I am almost well,”", "“네, 이제 저도 거의 다 나은 걸 알겠습니다.”"], ["said Raskolnikov, giving his mother and sister a kiss of welcome which made Pulcheria Alexandrovna radiant at once.", "라스콜니코프가 말하며 어머니와 누이에게 환영의 입맞춤을 했고, 그 입맞춤에 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 단번에 환해졌다."], ["“And I don’t say this _as I did yesterday_,”", "“그리고 저는 이걸 어제처럼 말하는 게 아닙니다.”"], ["he said, addressing Razumihin, with a friendly pressure of his hand.", "그가 라주미힌을 향해 그의 손을 다정하게 꼭 쥐며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, indeed, I am quite surprised at him to-day,”", "“그래, 정말이지 오늘은 이 친구가 놀라울 정도입니다.”"], ["began Zossimov, much delighted at the ladies’ entrance, for he had not succeeded in keeping up a conversation with his patient for ten minutes.", "조시모프가 입을 열었는데, 부인들이 들어온 것에 무척 기뻐하고 있었으니, 그는 십 분 동안 환자와 대화를 이어 가는 데 성공하지 못했던 것이다."], ["“In another three or four days, if he goes on like this, he will be just as before, that is, as he was a month ago, or two...", "“사나흘만 더 이런 식으로 가면 예전과 똑같아질 겁니다, 그러니까 한 달 전, 아니 두 달……"], ["or perhaps even three.", "어쩌면 석 달 전 모습으로요."], ["This has been coming on for a long while....", "이건 오래전부터 와 온 거라서요…… 그렇죠?"], ["eh? Confess, now, that it has been perhaps your own fault?”", "자, 솔직히 말씀해 보세요, 어쩌면 당신 자신의 잘못이었던 거 아닙니까?”"], ["he added, with a tentative smile, as though still afraid of irritating him.", "그가 여전히 그를 자극할까 두려운 듯, 조심스러운 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["“It is very possible,”", "“충분히 그럴 수 있죠.”"], ["answered Raskolnikov coldly.", "라스콜니코프가 차갑게 대답했다."]], [["“I should say, too,”", "“제 말씀은 이겁니다.”"], ["continued Zossimov with zest, “that your complete recovery depends solely on yourself.", "조시모프가 열을 올리며 계속했다. “당신이 완전히 회복되느냐는 오로지 당신 자신에게 달려 있다는 거예요."], ["Now that one can talk to you, I should like to impress upon you that it is essential to avoid the elementary, so to speak, fundamental causes tending to produce your morbid condition: in that case you will be cured, if not, it will go from bad to worse.", "이제 당신과 말이 통하니, 꼭 일러두고 싶은 게 있는데, 당신의 병적인 상태를 만들어 내는, 말하자면 근본적인, 기초적인 원인들을 피하는 게 필수적이라는 겁니다. 그러면 나을 거고, 그러지 않으면 점점 더 나빠질 거예요."], ["These fundamental causes I don’t know, but they must be known to you.", "이 근본적인 원인들이 뭔지 저는 모르지만, 당신은 분명 알고 있을 겁니다."], ["You are an intelligent man, and must have observed yourself, of course.", "당신은 지적인 사람이니, 물론 자기 자신을 관찰해 왔겠죠."], ["I fancy the first stage of your derangement coincides with your leaving the university.", "제 생각엔 당신의 이상 증세가 시작된 첫 단계가 대학을 그만둔 시점과 맞아떨어지는 것 같습니다."], ["You must not be left without occupation, and so, work and a definite aim set before you might, I fancy, be very beneficial.”", "당신은 할 일 없이 지내선 안 돼요, 그러니 일과, 눈앞에 정해진 분명한 목표가 있다면, 제 생각엔 무척 이로울 것 같군요.”"]], [["“Yes, yes; you are perfectly right....", "“네, 네, 지당하신 말씀입니다……."], ["I will make haste and return to the university: and then everything will go smoothly....”", "서둘러 대학으로 돌아가겠습니다. 그러면 모든 게 순조롭게 풀리겠죠…….”"]], [["Zossimov, who had begun his sage advice partly to make an effect before the ladies, was certainly somewhat mystified, when, glancing at his patient, he observed unmistakable mockery on his face.", "부분적으로는 부인들 앞에서 인상을 남기려고 현명한 충고를 시작했던 조시모프는, 환자를 흘긋 보았다가 그 얼굴에서 틀림없는 조롱의 빛을 발견하고는 분명 다소 어리둥절해졌다."], ["This lasted an instant, however.", "하지만 그것은 한순간뿐이었다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna began at once thanking Zossimov, especially for his visit to their lodging the previous night.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 즉시 조시모프에게, 특히 전날 밤 자신들의 숙소를 방문해 준 것에 대해 감사를 표하기 시작했다."]], [["“What!", "“뭐라고요!"], ["he saw you last night?”", "어젯밤에 어머니를 보셨다고요?”"], ["Raskolnikov asked, as though startled.", "라스콜니코프가 흠칫 놀란 듯 물었다."], ["“Then you have not slept either after your journey.”", "“그럼 어머니도 여행 후에 주무시지 못한 거잖아요.”"]], [["“Ach, Rodya, that was only till two o’clock.", "“아, 로쟈, 그건 두 시까지뿐이었단다."], ["Dounia and I never go to bed before two at home.”", "두냐와 나는 집에서도 두 시 전엔 잠자리에 든 적이 없으니까.”"]], [["“I don’t know how to thank him either,”", "“저도 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지 모르겠군요.”"], ["Raskolnikov went on, suddenly frowning and looking down.", "라스콜니코프가 갑자기 눈살을 찌푸리고 눈을 내리깔며 말을 이었다."], ["“Setting aside the question of payment--forgive me for referring to it (he turned to Zossimov)--I really don’t know what I have done to deserve such special attention from you!", "“진료비 문제는 차치하고서라도 — 이 얘기를 꺼내서 죄송합니다(그는 조시모프를 향해 돌아섰다) — 도대체 제가 무얼 했기에 당신한테서 이런 각별한 관심을 받을 자격이 있는지 정말 모르겠습니다."], ["I simply don’t understand it...", "그냥 이해가 안 돼요…… 그리고……"], ["and... and...", "그리고……"], ["it weighs upon me, indeed, because I don’t understand it.", "사실 그게 저를 짓누릅니다, 이해가 안 되니까요."], ["I tell you so candidly.”", "솔직하게 말씀드리는 겁니다.”"]], [["“Don’t be irritated.”", "“자극받지 마세요.”"], ["Zossimov forced himself to laugh.", "조시모프가 억지로 웃었다."], ["“Assume that you are my first patient--well--we fellows just beginning to practise love our first patients as if they were our children, and some almost fall in love with them.", "“당신이 제 첫 환자라고 칩시다 — 자, 우리처럼 갓 개업한 사람들은 첫 환자를 제 자식처럼 사랑하고, 더러는 거의 사랑에 빠지다시피 하지요."], ["And, of course, I am not rich in patients.”", "그리고 물론 저는 환자가 그리 많지도 않고요.”"]], [["“I say nothing about him,”", "“이 친구에 대해선 아무 말도 안 하겠습니다.”"], ["added Raskolnikov, pointing to Razumihin, “though he has had nothing from me either but insult and trouble.”", "라스콜니코프가 라주미힌을 가리키며 덧붙였다. “저한테서 받은 거라곤 모욕과 성가심뿐인데도 말이죠.”"]], [["“What nonsense he is talking!", "“참 별소릴 다 하는군!"], ["Why, you are in a sentimental mood to-day, are you?”", "아니, 자네 오늘 감상적인 기분에라도 빠진 건가?”"], ["shouted Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 소리쳤다."]], [["If he had had more penetration he would have seen that there was no trace of sentimentality in him, but something indeed quite the opposite.", "좀 더 통찰력이 있었더라면, 그는 라스콜니코프에게 감상의 흔적이라곤 없으며 오히려 정반대의 무언가가 있음을 보았을 것이다."], ["But Avdotya Romanovna noticed it.", "하지만 아브도티야 로마노브나는 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["She was intently and uneasily watching her brother.", "그녀는 오빠를 골똘히, 불안하게 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["“As for you, mother, I don’t dare to speak,”", "“어머니로 말하자면, 저는 감히 입을 떼지도 못하겠어요.”"], ["he went on, as though repeating a lesson learned by heart.", "그는 마치 외워 둔 교훈을 되뇌듯 말을 이었다."], ["“It is only to-day that I have been able to realise a little how distressed you must have been here yesterday, waiting for me to come back.”", "“어제 여기서 제가 돌아오기를 기다리며 어머니가 얼마나 애를 태우셨을지, 그걸 조금이나마 깨닫게 된 게 겨우 오늘에 와서니까요.”"]], [["When he had said this, he suddenly held out his hand to his sister, smiling without a word.", "이 말을 마치고는 그는 갑자기 누이에게 손을 내밀며 말없이 미소 지었다."], ["But in this smile there was a flash of real unfeigned feeling.", "그러나 그 미소 속에는 진실한, 꾸밈없는 감정의 섬광이 있었다."], ["Dounia caught it at once, and warmly pressed his hand, overjoyed and thankful.", "두냐는 곧장 그것을 알아채고, 더없이 기쁘고 고마워하며 따뜻하게 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."], ["It was the first time he had addressed her since their dispute the previous day.", "전날 다툰 이후 그가 그녀에게 말을 건넨 것은 처음이었다."], ["The mother’s face lighted up with ecstatic happiness at the sight of this conclusive unspoken reconciliation.", "이 결정적인 무언의 화해를 보자 어머니의 얼굴은 황홀한 행복으로 환하게 빛났다."], ["“Yes, that is what I love him for,”", "“그래, 바로 저런 점 때문에 내가 저 친구를 좋아하는 거지.”"], ["Razumihin, exaggerating it all, muttered to himself, with a vigorous turn in his chair.", "라주미힌이 그 모든 걸 부풀려 받아들이며 의자에서 힘차게 몸을 돌리며 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["“He has these movements.”", "“저 친구한텐 저런 움직임이 있단 말이야.”"]], [["“And how well he does it all,” the mother was thinking to herself.", "“그리고 저 애는 이 모든 걸 어쩜 저렇게 잘하는지.”"], ["“What generous impulses he has, and how simply, how delicately he put an end to all the misunderstanding with his sister--simply by holding out his hand at the right minute and looking at her like that....", "어머니는 속으로 생각하고 있었다. “저 애는 어찌나 너그러운 충동을 지녔는지, 그리고 누이와의 모든 오해를 어쩜 저리도 단순하게, 저리도 섬세하게 끝맺어 버렸을까 — 그저 알맞은 순간에 손을 내밀고 누이를 저렇게 바라보는 것만으로……."], ["And what fine eyes he has, and how fine his whole face is!...", "게다가 저 애는 눈이 얼마나 고운지, 얼굴 전체가 또 얼마나 고운지!……"], ["He is even better looking than Dounia....", "두냐보다도 잘생겼어……."], ["But, good heavens, what a suit--how terribly he’s dressed!...", "하지만, 세상에, 저 양복 꼴이라니 — 옷차림이 어쩜 저렇게 끔찍한지!……"], ["Vasya, the messenger boy in Afanasy Ivanitch’s shop, is better dressed!", "아파나시 이바니치 가게의 심부름꾼 바샤가 저보다 더 잘 입고 다니겠네!"], ["I could rush at him and hug him...", "저 애한테 달려가 끌어안고 싶어……"], ["weep over him--but I am afraid....", "부둥켜안고 울고 싶어 — 하지만 두려워……."], ["Oh, dear, he’s so strange!", "오, 저런, 저 애는 어쩜 이리 낯설까!"], ["He’s talking kindly, but I’m afraid!", "다정하게 말하고 있는데도 나는 두려우니!"], ["Why, what am I afraid of?...”", "아니, 내가 대체 무얼 두려워하는 거지?……”"]], [["“Oh, Rodya, you wouldn’t believe,”", "“오, 로쟈, 너는 믿지 못할 거다.”"], ["she began suddenly, in haste to answer his words to her, “how unhappy Dounia and I were yesterday!", "그녀가 갑자기, 그가 자신에게 했던 말에 서둘러 답하느라 입을 열었다. “어제 두냐와 내가 얼마나 불행했는지!"], ["Now that it’s all over and done with and we are quite happy again--I can tell you.", "이제 다 지나가고 끝나서 우리가 다시 아주 행복해졌으니 — 너한테 말할 수 있구나."], ["Fancy, we ran here almost straight from the train to embrace you and that woman--ah, here she is!", "글쎄, 우리는 기차에서 내리자마자 거의 곧장 너를 끌어안으러 이리로 달려왔단다, 그리고 저 여자분도 — 아, 여기 있구나!"], ["Good morning, Nastasya!...", "안녕하세요, 나스타시야!……"], ["She told us at once that you were lying in a high fever and had just run away from the doctor in delirium, and they were looking for you in the streets.", "저분이 곧장 우리한테 일러 주더구나, 네가 고열로 누워 있다가, 헛소리를 하며 의사한테서 막 도망쳤고, 다들 거리에서 너를 찾고 있다고 말이야."], ["You can’t imagine how we felt!", "우리가 어떤 심정이었는지 너는 상상도 못 할 거다!"], ["I couldn’t help thinking of the tragic end of Lieutenant Potanchikov, a friend of your father’s--you can’t remember him, Rodya--who ran out in the same way in a high fever and fell into the well in the court-yard and they couldn’t pull him out till next day.", "나는 네 아버지 친구분이셨던 포탄치코프 중위의 비극적인 최후가 자꾸 떠올랐단다 — 너는 그분을 기억 못 할 거다, 로쟈 — 그분도 꼭 그렇게 고열에 들떠 뛰쳐나갔다가 안마당 우물에 빠지셨는데, 다음 날에야 겨우 끌어 올렸지 뭐냐."], ["Of course, we exaggerated things.", "물론 우리가 일을 부풀려 생각했지."], ["We were on the point of rushing to find Pyotr Petrovitch to ask him to help....", "우리는 표트르 페트로비치한테 달려가 도와 달라고 청할 참이었단다……."], ["Because we were alone, utterly alone,”", "우리는 외톨이였으니까, 정말이지 외톨이였으니까.”"], ["she said plaintively and stopped short, suddenly, recollecting it was still somewhat dangerous to speak of Pyotr Petrovitch, although “we are quite happy again.”", "그녀는 애처롭게 말하다가, “우리가 다시 아주 행복해졌다”고는 해도 표트르 페트로비치 얘기를 꺼내는 게 아직은 다소 위험하다는 걸 문득 떠올리고는, 갑자기 입을 뚝 다물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes....", "“네, 네……."], ["Of course it’s very annoying....”", "물론 그건 무척 짜증스러운 일이죠…….”"], ["Raskolnikov muttered in reply, but with such a preoccupied and inattentive air that Dounia gazed at him in perplexity.", "라스콜니코프가 대답으로 중얼거렸는데, 그 태도가 어찌나 딴 데 정신이 팔리고 무심한지, 두냐는 어리둥절한 눈으로 그를 응시했다."]], [["“What else was it I wanted to say?”", "“내가 또 무슨 말을 하려고 했더라?”"], ["He went on trying to recollect.", "그는 기억해 내려 애쓰며 말을 이었다."], ["“Oh, yes; mother, and you too, Dounia, please don’t think that I didn’t mean to come and see you to-day and was waiting for you to come first.”", "“아, 그래. 어머니, 그리고 너도, 두냐, 제발 오해하지 마세요, 제가 오늘 두 분을 찾아뵐 마음이 없어서 두 분이 먼저 오시기를 기다렸다고는요.”"]], [["“What are you saying, Rodya?”", "“그게 무슨 소리냐, 로쟈?”"], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."], ["She, too, was surprised.", "그녀 역시 놀랐다."]], [["“Is he answering us as a duty?” Dounia wondered.", "‘오빠는 의무처럼 우리한테 대답하는 걸까?’ 두냐는 의아해했다."], ["“Is he being reconciled and asking forgiveness as though he were performing a rite or repeating a lesson?”", "‘무슨 의식을 치르거나 외운 교훈을 되뇌듯이 화해하고 용서를 구하는 걸까?’"]], [["“I’ve only just waked up, and wanted to go to you, but was delayed owing to my clothes; I forgot yesterday to ask her...", "“이제 막 잠에서 깨어 두 분께 가려던 참이었는데, 옷 때문에 지체됐어요. 어제 저 사람한테……"], ["Nastasya...", "나스타시야한테……"], ["to wash out the blood...", "피를 좀 씻어 달라고 부탁하는 걸 깜빡했거든요……"], ["I’ve only just dressed.”", "이제 막 옷을 입었어요.”"]], [["“Blood!", "“피라니!"], ["What blood?”", "무슨 피?”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna asked in alarm.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing--don’t be uneasy.", "“아, 아무것도 아니에요 — 걱정 마세요."], ["It was when I was wandering about yesterday, rather delirious, I chanced upon a man who had been run over...", "어제 좀 헛것에 들떠 헤매고 다닐 때, 마차에 치인 사람을 우연히 만났거든요……"], ["a clerk...”", "어떤 관리를…….”"]], [["“Delirious?", "“헛것에 들떠서?"], ["But you remember everything!”", "그런데 넌 다 기억하잖아!”"], ["Razumihin interrupted.", "라주미힌이 끼어들었다."]], [["“That’s true,”", "“그건 사실이야.”"], ["Raskolnikov answered with special carefulness.", "라스콜니코프가 유난히 조심스럽게 대답했다."], ["“I remember everything even to the slightest detail, and yet--why I did that and went there and said that, I can’t clearly explain now.”", "“나는 아주 사소한 것까지 다 기억해. 그런데도 — 내가 왜 그런 짓을 했고 거기 갔고 그런 말을 했는지는, 지금 분명히 설명할 수가 없어.”"]], [["“A familiar phenomenon,”", "“익히 알려진 현상이지.”"], ["interposed Zossimov, “actions are sometimes performed in a masterly and most cunning way, while the direction of the actions is deranged and dependent on various morbid impressions--it’s like a dream.”", "조시모프가 끼어들었다. “행동은 때때로 능숙하고 더없이 교묘하게 수행되는데, 그 행동의 방향만큼은 헝클어져서 갖가지 병적인 인상에 좌우되는 거예요 — 마치 꿈처럼 말이죠.”"]], [["“Perhaps it’s a good thing really that he should think me almost a madman,” thought Raskolnikov.", "‘저자가 나를 거의 미치광이로 여기는 게 어쩌면 오히려 잘된 일인지도 몰라.’ 라스콜니코프는 생각했다."]], [["“Why, people in perfect health act in the same way too,”", "“아니, 완전히 건강한 사람들도 똑같이 행동하는걸요.”"], ["observed Dounia, looking uneasily at Zossimov.", "두냐가 조시모프를 불안하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“There is some truth in your observation,”", "“당신 말씀에도 일리가 있습니다.”"], ["the latter replied.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“In that sense we are certainly all not infrequently like madmen, but with the slight difference that the deranged are somewhat madder, for we must draw a line.", "“그런 의미에서라면 우리 모두 분명 적잖이 미치광이 같죠, 다만 정신이 헝클어진 사람들이 조금 더 미쳐 있다는 자그마한 차이가 있을 뿐입니다, 어디엔가는 선을 그어야 하니까요."], ["A normal man, it is true, hardly exists.", "정상적인 인간은, 사실 거의 존재하지 않습니다."], ["Among dozens--perhaps hundreds of thousands--hardly one is to be met with.”", "수십 명 가운데 — 어쩌면 수십만 명 가운데 — 한 명 만나기도 어렵죠.”"]], [["At the word “madman,” carelessly dropped by Zossimov in his chatter on his favourite subject, everyone frowned.", "조시모프가 자신이 좋아하는 주제로 떠들다가 무심코 흘린 “미치광이”라는 말에, 모두가 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov sat seeming not to pay attention, plunged in thought with a strange smile on his pale lips.", "라스콜니코프는 주의를 기울이지 않는 듯이 앉아, 창백한 입술에 기묘한 미소를 띤 채 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He was still meditating on something.", "그는 여전히 무언가를 골똘히 곱씹고 있었다."]], [["“Well, what about the man who was run over?", "“그건 그렇고, 마차에 치인 그 사람은 어떻게 됐어?"], ["I interrupted you!”", "내가 자네 말을 끊었지!”"], ["Razumihin cried hastily.", "라주미힌이 다급히 외쳤다."]], [["“What?”", "“뭐라고?”"], ["Raskolnikov seemed to wake up.", "라스콜니코프가 정신이 든 것 같았다."], ["“Oh...", "“아……"], ["I got spattered with blood helping to carry him to his lodging.", "그를 거처까지 옮기는 걸 도우면서 피를 뒤집어쓴 거야."], ["By the way, mamma, I did an unpardonable thing yesterday.", "그건 그렇고, 어머니, 저는 어제 용서받을 수 없는 짓을 저질렀어요."], ["I was literally out of my mind.", "말 그대로 제정신이 아니었거든요."], ["I gave away all the money you sent me...", "어머니가 보내 주신 돈을 몽땅 줘 버렸어요……"], ["to his wife for the funeral.", "장례식에 쓰라고 그 사람 아내한테요."], ["She’s a widow now, in consumption, a poor creature...", "그 여자는 이제 과부에, 폐병을 앓는, 가엾은 사람이에요……"], ["three little children, starving...", "어린 자식이 셋이나 되는데 굶주리고……"], ["nothing in the house...", "집엔 아무것도 없고……"], ["there’s a daughter, too...", "딸도 하나 있고요……"], ["perhaps you’d have given it yourself if you’d seen them.", "어머니도 그들을 보셨더라면 직접 돈을 주셨을지도 몰라요."], ["But I had no right to do it I admit, especially as I knew how you needed the money yourself.", "하지만 제게 그럴 권리가 없었다는 건 인정합니다, 특히 어머니 자신이 그 돈을 얼마나 필요로 하는지 알고 있었으니까요."], ["To help others one must have the right to do it, or else _Crevez, chiens, si vous n’êtes pas contents_.”", "남을 도우려면 그럴 권리가 있어야 하는 법이죠, 안 그러면, ‘크레베, 시앵, 시 부 네트 파 콩탕’이고요.”"], ["He laughed, “That’s right, isn’t it, Dounia?”", "그가 웃었다. “그렇지, 두냐?”"]], [["“No, it’s not,”", "“아니, 그렇지 않아.”"], ["answered Dounia firmly.", "두냐가 단호하게 대답했다."]], [["“Bah! you, too, have ideals,”", "“바!너도 이상이라는 걸 갖고 있구나.”"], ["he muttered, looking at her almost with hatred, and smiling sarcastically.", "그가 거의 증오에 차 그녀를 바라보며 비꼬듯 미소 지으며 중얼거렸다."], ["“I ought to have considered that....", "“그걸 고려했어야 했는데……."], ["Well, that’s praiseworthy, and it’s better for you...", "뭐, 칭찬받을 만한 일이고, 너한텐 더 나은 거지……"], ["and if you reach a line you won’t overstep, you will be unhappy...", "그리고 네가 넘어서지 않을 선에 다다르면 너는 불행해질 거고……"], ["and if you overstep it, maybe you will be still unhappier....", "만일 그 선을 넘어선다면 어쩌면 더더욱 불행해질 거다……."], ["But all that’s nonsense,”", "하지만 이 모든 게 다 헛소리야.”"], ["he added irritably, vexed at being carried away.", "그가 휩쓸린 게 분해서 짜증스레 덧붙였다."], ["“I only meant to say that I beg your forgiveness, mother,”", "“저는 그저, 용서를 빈다는 말을 하고 싶었을 뿐이에요, 어머니.”"], ["he concluded, shortly and abruptly.", "그가 짧고 퉁명스럽게 끝맺었다."]], [["“That’s enough, Rodya, I am sure that everything you do is very good,”", "“그만하면 됐다, 로쟈, 네가 하는 일은 다 아주 훌륭하다고 믿는다.”"], ["said his mother, delighted.", "어머니가 기뻐하며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t be too sure,”", "“너무 확신하진 마세요.”"], ["he answered, twisting his mouth into a smile.", "그가 입을 비틀어 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["A silence followed.", "침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["There was a certain constraint in all this conversation, and in the silence, and in the reconciliation, and in the forgiveness, and all were feeling it.", "이 모든 대화에도, 그 침묵에도, 화해에도, 용서에도 어딘가 어색함이 있었고, 모두가 그것을 느끼고 있었다."]], [["“It is as though they were afraid of me,” Raskolnikov was thinking to himself, looking askance at his mother and sister.", "‘마치 다들 나를 두려워하는 것 같군.’ 라스콜니코프는 어머니와 누이를 곁눈질하며 속으로 생각했다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was indeed growing more timid the longer she kept silent.", "실제로 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 침묵이 길어질수록 점점 더 겁을 먹어 갔다."]], [["“Yet in their absence I seemed to love them so much,” flashed through his mind.", "‘그런데도 두 사람이 곁에 없을 땐 어찌나 사랑스럽게 느껴졌던지.’ 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“Do you know, Rodya, Marfa Petrovna is dead,”", "“있잖니, 로쟈, 마르파 페트로브나가 죽었단다.”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna suddenly blurted out.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["“What Marfa Petrovna?”", "“마르파 페트로브나가 누구죠?”"]], [["“Oh, mercy on us--Marfa Petrovna Svidrigaïlov.", "“오, 맙소사 — 마르파 페트로브나 스비드리가일로바 말이다."], ["I wrote you so much about her.”", "내가 편지에 그분 얘기를 그렇게 많이 썼잖니.”"]], [["“A-a-h! Yes, I remember....", "“아 — 아 — 하!"], ["So she’s dead!", "네, 기억나요……."], ["Oh, really?”", "그래서 그분이 죽었다고요!"], ["he roused himself suddenly, as if waking up.", "오, 정말요?” 그가 잠에서 깨어나듯 갑자기 정신을 차렸다."], ["“What did she die of?”", "“무엇 때문에 돌아가셨대요?”"]], [["“Only imagine, quite suddenly,”", "“글쎄, 정말이지 너무도 갑작스럽게.”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna answered hurriedly, encouraged by his curiosity.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 그의 호기심에 힘을 얻어 서둘러 대답했다."], ["“On the very day I was sending you that letter!", "“바로 내가 너한테 그 편지를 부치던 그날에!"], ["Would you believe it, that awful man seems to have been the cause of her death.", "믿어지니, 그 끔찍한 작자가 그분 죽음의 원인인 것 같다는구나."], ["They say he beat her dreadfully.”", "사람들 말로는 그자가 그분을 무섭게 때렸다지 뭐냐.”"]], [["“Why, were they on such bad terms?”", "“아니, 그 사람들 사이가 그렇게나 나빴어요?”"], ["he asked, addressing his sister.", "그가 누이를 향해 물었다."]], [["“Not at all.", "“전혀."], ["Quite the contrary indeed.", "사실은 정반대야."], ["With her, he was always very patient, considerate even.", "그 여자한테 그는 늘 무척 참을성 있게, 심지어 자상하게 대했어."], ["In fact, all those seven years of their married life he gave way to her, too much so indeed, in many cases.", "사실 결혼 생활 칠 년 내내 그는 그 여자한테 양보했지, 정말이지 많은 경우엔 지나칠 정도로."], ["All of a sudden he seems to have lost patience.”", "그런데 갑자기 인내심을 잃은 것 같아.”"]], [["“Then he could not have been so awful if he controlled himself for seven years?", "“그렇다면 칠 년이나 자제했다면 그렇게 끔찍한 사람일 리는 없잖아?"], ["You seem to be defending him, Dounia?”", "너는 그 사람을 두둔하는 것 같구나, 두냐?”"]], [["“No, no, he’s an awful man!", "“아니, 아니야, 그는 끔찍한 사람이야!"], ["I can imagine nothing more awful!”", "그보다 더 끔찍한 건 상상도 못 하겠어!”"], ["Dounia answered, almost with a shudder, knitting her brows, and sinking into thought.", "두냐가 거의 몸서리치다시피 하며, 눈썹을 찌푸리고 생각에 잠겨 들며 대답했다."]], [["“That had happened in the morning,”", "“그 일은 아침에 일어났단다.”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna went on hurriedly.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 서둘러 말을 이었다."], ["“And directly afterwards she ordered the horses to be harnessed to drive to the town immediately after dinner.", "“그러고는 곧바로 그 여자가 말에 마구를 채우라고 시켰어, 저녁 식사 후에 곧장 읍내로 마차를 몰고 가려고 말이야."], ["She always used to drive to the town in such cases.", "그 여자는 그런 일이 있을 때면 늘 읍내로 마차를 몰고 가곤 했지."], ["She ate a very good dinner, I am told....”", "듣자 하니 그 여자가 아주 잘 먹었다더구나, 저녁을…….”"]], [["“After the beating?”", "“얻어맞고 나서요?”"]], [["“That was always her...", "“그게 늘 그 여자의……"], ["habit; and immediately after dinner, so as not to be late in starting, she went to the bath-house....", "버릇이었어. 그러고는 저녁을 먹자마자, 출발이 늦어지지 않도록 목욕탕에 갔단다……."], ["You see, she was undergoing some treatment with baths.", "알다시피 그 여자는 목욕으로 무슨 치료를 받고 있었거든."], ["They have a cold spring there, and she used to bathe in it regularly every day, and no sooner had she got into the water when she suddenly had a stroke!”", "거기 찬 샘물이 있어서, 그 여자는 날마다 거기서 규칙적으로 목욕을 하곤 했는데, 물에 들어서자마자 갑자기 발작을 일으킨 거야!”"]], [["“I should think so,”", "“그러고도 남죠.”"], ["said Zossimov.", "조시모프가 말했다."]], [["“And did he beat her badly?”", "“그래서 그가 그 여자를 심하게 때렸대요?”"]], [["“What does that matter!”", "“그게 무슨 상관이야!”"], ["put in Dounia.", "두냐가 끼어들었다."]], [["“H’m!", "“흠!"], ["But I don’t know why you want to tell us such gossip, mother,”", "그런데 어머니가 왜 우리한테 이런 뜬소문을 들려주려고 하시는지 모르겠네요.”"], ["said Raskolnikov irritably, as it were in spite of himself.", "라스콜니코프가 자신도 모르게 짜증스레 말했다."]], [["“Ah, my dear, I don’t know what to talk about,”", "“아, 얘야, 무슨 얘기를 해야 할지 모르겠구나.”"], ["broke from Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 입에서 새어 나왔다."]], [["“Why, are you all afraid of me?”", "“아니, 다들 나를 두려워하는 거예요?”"], ["he asked, with a constrained smile.", "그가 어색한 미소를 띠며 물었다."]], [["“That’s certainly true,”", "“그건 분명 사실이에요.”"], ["said Dounia, looking directly and sternly at her brother.", "두냐가 오빠를 똑바로, 엄하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Mother was crossing herself with terror as she came up the stairs.”", "“어머니는 계단을 올라오면서 무서워서 성호를 그으셨는걸요.”"]], [["His face worked, as though in convulsion.", "그의 얼굴이 경련하듯 일그러졌다."]], [["“Ach, what are you saying, Dounia!", "“아, 무슨 소리를 하는 거니, 두냐!"], ["Don’t be angry, please, Rodya....", "제발 화내지 마라, 로쟈……."], ["Why did you say that, Dounia?”", "두냐, 왜 그런 말을 하니?”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna began, overwhelmed--“You see, coming here, I was dreaming all the way, in the train, how we should meet, how we should talk over everything together....", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 입을 열었다. “있잖니, 이리로 오면서 나는 기차에서 내내 꿈꿨단다, 우리가 어떻게 만날까, 어떻게 다 같이 모든 걸 얘기 나눌까 하고……."], ["And I was so happy, I did not notice the journey!", "그리고 어찌나 행복했던지 여행하는 것도 느끼지 못했어!"], ["But what am I saying?", "그런데 내가 무슨 말을 하는 거지?"], ["I am happy now....", "나는 지금 행복하단다……."], ["You should not, Dounia....", "그러면 못쓴다, 두냐……."], ["I am happy now--simply in seeing you, Rodya....”", "나는 지금 행복해 — 그저 너를 보는 것만으로도, 로쟈…….”"]], [["“Hush, mother,”", "“쉿, 어머니.”"], ["he muttered in confusion, not looking at her, but pressing her hand.", "그가 어머니를 보지 않고 그 손을 꼭 쥐며 당혹스럽게 중얼거렸다."], ["“We shall have time to speak freely of everything!”", "“모든 걸 마음 놓고 얘기할 시간은 얼마든지 있을 테니까요!”"]], [["As he said this, he was suddenly overwhelmed with confusion and turned pale.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 갑자기 당혹감에 휩싸여 창백해졌다."], ["Again that awful sensation he had known of late passed with deadly chill over his soul.", "다시금 요즘 익히 알게 된 그 끔찍한 감각이 죽음 같은 한기를 몰고 그의 영혼을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["Again it became suddenly plain and perceptible to him that he had just told a fearful lie--that he would never now be able to speak freely of everything--that he would never again be able to _speak_ of anything to anyone.", "다시금 그에게는, 자신이 방금 무서운 거짓말을 했다는 것이 — 이제는 결코 모든 걸 마음 놓고 얘기할 수 없으리라는 것이 — 그 누구에게도 무엇 하나 다시는 입에 올릴 수 없으리라는 것이 갑자기 또렷하고 분명하게 느껴졌다."], ["The anguish of this thought was such that for a moment he almost forgot himself.", "이 생각의 고뇌가 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 한순간 거의 정신을 잃을 뻔했다."], ["He got up from his seat, and not looking at anyone walked towards the door.", "그는 자리에서 일어나, 아무도 쳐다보지 않고 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“What are you about?”", "“뭐 하는 거야?”"], ["cried Razumihin, clutching him by the arm.", "라주미힌이 그의 팔을 붙들며 외쳤다."]], [["He sat down again, and began looking about him, in silence.", "그는 다시 자리에 앉아, 말없이 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다."], ["They were all looking at him in perplexity.", "모두가 어리둥절한 채 그를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“But what are you all so dull for?”", "“그런데 다들 왜 이렇게 침울해요?”"], ["he shouted, suddenly and quite unexpectedly.", "그가 갑자기, 전혀 뜻밖에 소리쳤다."], ["“Do say something!", "“무슨 말이라도 좀 해 보세요!"], ["What’s the use of sitting like this?", "이렇게 앉아 있어 봐야 무슨 소용이에요?"], ["Come, do speak.", "자, 말 좀 하세요."], ["Let us talk....", "얘기를 합시다……."], ["We meet together and sit in silence....", "모처럼 모여서 잠자코 앉아 있기만 하다니……."], ["Come, anything!”", "자, 아무거나요!”"]], [["“Thank God; I was afraid the same thing as yesterday was beginning again,”", "“다행이구나. 어제 같은 일이 또 시작되는 줄 알고 겁이 났단다.”"], ["said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, crossing herself.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 성호를 그으며 말했다."]], [["“What is the matter, Rodya?”", "“무슨 일이에요, 로쟈?”"], ["asked Avdotya Romanovna, distrustfully.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 미심쩍게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing!", "“아, 아무것도 아니야!"], ["I remembered something,”", "뭔가 생각이 나서 그래.”"], ["he answered, and suddenly laughed.", "그가 대답하고는 갑자기 웃었다."]], [["“Well, if you remembered something; that’s all right!...", "“뭐, 뭔가 생각이 났다면야 괜찮습니다!……"], ["I was beginning to think...”", "저는 또 생각하기 시작했던 게…….”"], ["muttered Zossimov, getting up from the sofa.", "조시모프가 소파에서 일어나며 중얼거렸다."], ["“It is time for me to be off.", "“이제 저는 갈 시간이군요."], ["I will look in again perhaps...", "어쩌면 다시 들르겠습니다……"], ["if I can...”", "할 수 있다면요…….”"], ["He made his bows, and went out.", "그는 인사를 하고 나갔다."]], [["“What an excellent man!”", "“참 훌륭한 분이야!”"], ["observed Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, excellent, splendid, well-educated, intelligent,”", "“네, 훌륭하죠, 멋지고, 교양 있고, 영리하고요.”"], ["Raskolnikov began, suddenly speaking with surprising rapidity, and a liveliness he had not shown till then.", "라스콜니코프가 지금껏 보이지 않던 놀라운 속도와 생기로 갑자기 말하기 시작했다."], ["“I can’t remember where I met him before my illness....", "“병나기 전에 그를 어디서 만났는지 기억이 안 나네요……."], ["I believe I have met him somewhere----...", "어딘가에서 만난 것 같긴 한데——……."], ["And this is a good man, too,”", "그리고 이 친구도 좋은 사람이에요.”"], ["he nodded at Razumihin.", "그가 라주미힌을 향해 고갯짓했다."], ["“Do you like him, Dounia?”", "“이 사람이 마음에 들어, 두냐?”"], ["he asked her; and suddenly, for some unknown reason, laughed.", "그가 누이에게 묻고는, 갑자기, 무슨 까닭인지 모르게 웃었다."]], [["“Very much,”", "“아주 많이.”"], ["answered Dounia.", "두냐가 대답했다."]], [["“Foo!--what a pig you are!”", "“푸! — 이 돼지 같은 자식!”"], ["Razumihin protested, blushing in terrible confusion, and he got up from his chair.", "라주미힌이 끔찍한 당혹감에 얼굴을 붉히며 항의하고는, 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna smiled faintly, but Raskolnikov laughed aloud.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 희미하게 미소 지었지만, 라스콜니코프는 소리 내어 웃었다."]], [["“Where are you off to?”", "“어디 가는 거야?”"]], [["“I must go.”", "“가 봐야 해.”"]], [["“You need not at all.", "“전혀 그럴 필요 없어."], ["Stay.", "있어."], ["Zossimov has gone, so you must. Don’t go.", "조시모프도 갔으니 자네는 더더욱 있어야지."], ["What’s the time?", "가지 마."], ["Is it twelve o’clock?", "몇 시야? 열두 시인가?"], ["What a pretty watch you have got, Dounia.", "시계가 참 예쁘구나, 두냐."], ["But why are you all silent again?", "그런데 다들 왜 또 잠자코 있어요?"], ["I do all the talking.”", "나 혼자만 떠들고 있잖아.”"]], [["“It was a present from Marfa Petrovna,”", "“마르파 페트로브나가 준 거야.”"], ["answered Dounia.", "두냐가 대답했다."]], [["“And a very expensive one!”", "“그것도 아주 값비싼 거란다!”"], ["added Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 덧붙였다."]], [["“A-ah!", "“아 — 하!"], ["What a big one!", "어쩜 이리 크지!"], ["Hardly like a lady’s.”", "숙녀용 같지가 않은걸.”"]], [["“I like that sort,”", "“난 이런 게 좋아.”"], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."]], [["“So it is not a present from her _fiancé_,” thought Razumihin, and was unreasonably delighted.", "‘그럼 약혼자가 준 선물은 아니로군.’ 라주미힌은 생각하며, 까닭 없이 기뻐졌다."]], [["“I thought it was Luzhin’s present,”", "“루진이 준 선물인 줄 알았어.”"], ["observed Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["“No, he has not made Dounia any presents yet.”", "“아니, 그분은 아직 두냐한테 아무 선물도 안 했단다.”"]], [["“A-ah!", "“아 — 하!"], ["And do you remember, mother, I was in love and wanted to get married?”", "그런데 어머니, 기억하세요, 제가 사랑에 빠져 결혼하고 싶어 했던 거?”"], ["he said suddenly, looking at his mother, who was disconcerted by the sudden change of subject and the way he spoke of it.", "그가 갑자기 어머니를 바라보며 말했고, 어머니는 화제가 갑자기 바뀌고 그가 그 얘기를 꺼내는 방식에 당황했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, my dear.”", "“오, 그럼, 얘야.”"]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna exchanged glances with Dounia and Razumihin.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 두냐, 라주미힌과 눈빛을 주고받았다."]], [["“H’m, yes.", "“흠, 그래요."], ["What shall I tell you?", "무슨 얘기를 해 드릴까요?"], ["I don’t remember much indeed.", "사실 별로 기억나는 게 없어요."], ["She was such a sickly girl,”", "어찌나 병약한 처녀였던지.”"], ["he went on, growing dreamy and looking down again.", "그가 다시 몽롱해지며 눈을 내리깔고 말을 이었다."], ["“Quite an invalid.", "“완전히 환자였죠."], ["She was fond of giving alms to the poor, and was always dreaming of a nunnery, and once she burst into tears when she began talking to me about it.", "그 애는 가난한 이들에게 적선하기를 좋아했고, 늘 수녀원에 들어가는 걸 꿈꿨는데, 한번은 그 얘기를 저한테 꺼내다가 그만 울음을 터뜨렸어요."], ["Yes, yes, I remember.", "네, 네, 기억나요."], ["I remember very well.", "아주 잘 기억나요."], ["She was an ugly little thing.", "못생기고 자그마한 애였죠."], ["I really don’t know what drew me to her then--I think it was because she was always ill.", "그때 제가 왜 그 애한테 끌렸는지 정말 모르겠어요 — 늘 아팠기 때문이었던 것 같아요."], ["If she had been lame or hunchback, I believe I should have liked her better still,”", "만일 그 애가 절름발이거나 곱사등이였다면, 아마 그 애를 더더욱 좋아했을걸요.”"], ["he smiled dreamily.", "그가 몽롱하게 미소 지었다."], ["“Yes, it was a sort of spring delirium.”", "“네, 그건 일종의 봄날의 헛것에 들뜬 거였어요.”"]], [["“No, it was not only spring delirium,”", "“아니, 그건 그저 봄날의 헛것만은 아니었어.”"], ["said Dounia, with warm feeling.", "두냐가 따뜻한 감정을 담아 말했다."]], [["He fixed a strained intent look on his sister, but did not hear or did not understand her words.", "그는 긴장하여 골똘한 눈길을 누이에게 고정했지만, 그녀의 말을 듣지 못했거나 이해하지 못했다."], ["Then, completely lost in thought, he got up, went up to his mother, kissed her, went back to his place and sat down.", "그러더니 완전히 생각에 잠겨 일어나, 어머니에게 다가가 입을 맞추고는, 제자리로 돌아와 앉았다."]], [["“You love her even now?”", "“지금도 그 애를 사랑하니?”"], ["said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, touched.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 감동하여 말했다."]], [["“Her? Now?", "“그 애요?"], ["Oh, yes....", "지금이요? 오, 네……."], ["You ask about her? No...", "그 애에 대해 물으시는 거예요?"], ["that’s all now, as it were, in another world...", "아니요…… 이제 그 모든 건, 말하자면, 다른 세상의 일 같아요……"], ["and so long ago.", "그리고 너무도 오래전 일이죠."], ["And indeed everything happening here seems somehow far away.”", "사실 여기서 일어나는 모든 일도 어쩐지 멀게만 느껴져요.”"], ["He looked attentively at them.", "그가 그들을 유심히 바라보았다."], ["“You, now...", "“지금 두 분도요……"], ["I seem to be looking at you from a thousand miles away...", "저는 두 분을 천 마일 밖에서 바라보고 있는 것 같아요……"], ["but, goodness knows why we are talking of that!", "하지만, 우리가 왜 그런 얘기를 하고 있는지 누가 알겠어요!"], ["And what’s the use of asking about it?”", "그리고 그런 걸 물어서 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?”"], ["he added with annoyance, and biting his nails, fell into dreamy silence again.", "그가 짜증스럽게 덧붙이고는 손톱을 깨물며, 다시 몽롱한 침묵에 빠져들었다."]], [["“What a wretched lodging you have, Rodya!", "“얼마나 비참한 셋방이냐, 로쟈!"], ["It’s like a tomb,”", "꼭 무덤 같구나.”"], ["said Pulcheria Alexandrovna, suddenly breaking the oppressive silence.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 갑자기 그 무거운 침묵을 깨며 말했다."], ["“I am sure it’s quite half through your lodging you have become so melancholy.”", "“네가 이렇게 우울해진 건 분명 절반은 이 셋방 탓일 게다.”"]], [["“My lodging,”", "“셋방이요.”"], ["he answered, listlessly.", "그가 무기력하게 대답했다."], ["“Yes, the lodging had a great deal to do with it....", "“네, 셋방이 큰 몫을 했죠……."], ["I thought that, too....", "저도 그렇게 생각했어요……."], ["If only you knew, though, what a strange thing you said just now, mother,”", "하지만 어머니가 방금 얼마나 이상한 말씀을 하셨는지 아세요.”"], ["he said, laughing strangely.", "그가 기묘하게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["A little more, and their companionship, this mother and this sister, with him after three years’ absence, this intimate tone of conversation, in face of the utter impossibility of really speaking about anything, would have been beyond his power of endurance.", "조금만 더 가면, 삼 년의 부재 끝에 그와 함께한 이 어머니와 이 누이라는 동반자도, 정작 무엇 하나 진정으로 이야기할 수 없는 처지에 놓인 채 나누는 이 친밀한 어조의 대화도, 그의 인내력으로는 감당할 수 없는 것이 되고 말 터였다."], ["But there was one urgent matter which must be settled one way or the other that day--so he had decided when he woke.", "그러나 그날 어떤 식으로든 결판을 내야 할 긴박한 문제가 하나 있었으니 — 잠에서 깼을 때 그가 그렇게 결심했던 것이다."], ["Now he was glad to remember it, as a means of escape.", "이제 그는 그것을 탈출구 삼아 떠올린 것을 다행으로 여겼다."]], [["“Listen, Dounia,”", "“들어 봐, 두냐.”"], ["he began, gravely and drily, “of course I beg your pardon for yesterday, but I consider it my duty to tell you again that I do not withdraw from my chief point.", "그가 진지하고 무미건조하게 입을 열었다. “물론 어제 일은 사과할게, 하지만 다시 한번 말해 두는 게 내 의무라고 생각해, 나는 내 주요한 입장을 철회하지 않아."], ["It is me or Luzhin.", "나냐 루진이냐 둘 중 하나야."], ["If I am a scoundrel, you must not be.", "내가 비열한 놈이라면, 너만은 그래선 안 돼."], ["One is enough.", "한 사람으로 족하니까."], ["If you marry Luzhin, I cease at once to look on you as a sister.”", "네가 루진과 결혼한다면, 나는 그 즉시 너를 누이로 여기지 않을 거야.”"]], [["“Rodya, Rodya!", "“로쟈, 로쟈!"], ["It is the same as yesterday again,”", "또 어제랑 똑같구나.”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna cried, mournfully.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 슬프게 외쳤다."], ["“And why do you call yourself a scoundrel?", "“그리고 너는 어째서 자신을 비열한 놈이라고 부르니?"], ["I can’t bear it.", "나는 그걸 견딜 수가 없다."], ["You said the same yesterday.”", "어제도 똑같은 말을 했잖니.”"]], [["“Brother,”", "“오빠.”"], ["Dounia answered firmly and with the same dryness.", "두냐가 똑같이 무미건조하게, 단호히 대답했다."], ["“In all this there is a mistake on your part.", "“이 모든 게 오빠 쪽의 오해야."], ["I thought it over at night, and found out the mistake.", "밤새 곰곰이 생각해 보고 그 오해가 뭔지 알아냈어."], ["It is all because you seem to fancy I am sacrificing myself to someone and for someone.", "이게 다 오빠가 내가 누군가에게, 누군가를 위해 나 자신을 희생한다고 멋대로 상상하기 때문이야."], ["That is not the case at all.", "그건 결코 사실이 아니야."], ["I am simply marrying for my own sake, because things are hard for me.", "나는 그저 내 자신을 위해 결혼하는 거야, 내 처지가 힘들기 때문에."], ["Though, of course, I shall be glad if I succeed in being useful to my family.", "물론, 그렇게 해서 가족에게 도움이 될 수 있다면 기쁘겠지."], ["But that is not the chief motive for my decision....”", "하지만 그게 내 결정의 주된 동기는 아니야…….”"]], [["“She is lying,” he thought to himself, biting his nails vindictively.", "‘거짓말을 하고 있군.’ 그는 앙심을 품고 손톱을 깨물며 속으로 생각했다."], ["“Proud creature!", "‘오만한 것!"], ["She won’t admit she wants to do it out of charity!", "자선으로 그러는 거라는 걸 인정하려 들지 않아!"], ["Too haughty!", "너무도 도도하지!"], ["Oh, base characters!", "오, 천박한 인간들!"], ["They even love as though they hate....", "저들은 사랑조차 미워하듯이 하는군……."], ["Oh, how I...", "오, 어쩜 이리……"], ["hate them all!”", "저들 모두가 다 미울까!’"]], [["“In fact,”", "“사실,”"], ["continued Dounia, “I am marrying Pyotr Petrovitch because of two evils I choose the less.", "두냐가 말을 이었다. “나는 두 가지 악 가운데 덜한 쪽을 택해서 표트르 페트로비치와 결혼하는 거야."], ["I intend to do honestly all he expects of me, so I am not deceiving him....", "그가 나한테 기대하는 모든 걸 정직하게 할 작정이니까, 나는 그를 속이는 게 아니야……."], ["Why did you smile just now?”", "방금 왜 미소 지었어?”"], ["She, too, flushed, and there was a gleam of anger in her eyes.", "그녀 역시 얼굴을 붉혔고, 두 눈에는 분노의 빛이 번뜩였다."]], [["“All?”", "“모든 걸?”"], ["he asked, with a malignant grin.", "그가 악의에 찬 히죽거림으로 물었다."]], [["“Within certain limits.", "“어느 정도 한계 안에서."], ["Both the manner and form of Pyotr Petrovitch’s courtship showed me at once what he wanted.", "표트르 페트로비치의 구애 방식과 그 태도가 그가 무엇을 원하는지 단번에 내게 보여 줬어."], ["He may, of course, think too well of himself, but I hope he esteems me, too....", "물론 그가 자기 자신을 너무 좋게 여길지도 모르지만, 나 역시 존중해 주리라고 기대해……."], ["Why are you laughing again?”", "왜 또 웃는 거야?”"]], [["“And why are you blushing again?", "“그럼 너는 왜 또 얼굴을 붉히는데?"], ["You are lying, sister.", "거짓말을 하고 있군, 누이."], ["You are intentionally lying, simply from feminine obstinacy, simply to hold your own against me....", "너는 일부러 거짓말을 하고 있어, 그저 여자의 고집에서, 그저 나한테 지지 않으려고……."], ["You cannot respect Luzhin.", "너는 루진을 존경할 수 없어."], ["I have seen him and talked with him.", "나는 그를 만나 봤고 얘기도 나눠 봤으니까."], ["So you are selling yourself for money, and so in any case you are acting basely, and I am glad at least that you can blush for it.”", "그러니까 너는 돈에 자신을 팔고 있는 거고, 그러니 어떤 경우든 너는 비열하게 행동하고 있는 거야, 그래도 적어도 네가 그걸 부끄러워하며 얼굴을 붉힐 수는 있다니 다행이군.”"]], [["“It is not true.", "“사실이 아니야."], ["I am not lying,” cried Dounia, losing her composure.", "나는 거짓말을 하는 게 아니야.”"], ["“I would not marry him if I were not convinced that he esteems me and thinks highly of me.", "두냐가 평정을 잃고 외쳤다. “그가 나를 존중하고 높이 평가한다고 확신하지 못했다면 나는 그와 결혼하지 않을 거야."], ["I would not marry him if I were not firmly convinced that I can respect him.", "내가 그를 존경할 수 있다고 굳게 확신하지 못했다면 나는 그와 결혼하지 않을 거야."], ["Fortunately, I can have convincing proof of it this very day...", "다행히도 나는 바로 오늘 그에 대한 확실한 증거를 얻을 수 있어……"], ["and such a marriage is not a vileness, as you say!", "그리고 그런 결혼은 오빠 말처럼 비열한 짓이 아니야!"], ["And even if you were right, if I really had determined on a vile action, is it not merciless on your part to speak to me like that?", "게다가 설령 오빠 말이 옳다 해도, 정말로 내가 비열한 행동을 결심했다 한들, 나한테 그렇게 말하는 건 오빠 쪽에서 무자비한 짓 아니야?"], ["Why do you demand of me a heroism that perhaps you have not either?", "어쩌면 오빠 자신도 갖지 못한 영웅적 행위를 왜 나한테 요구하는 거지?"], ["It is despotism; it is tyranny.", "그건 전제(專制)야, 폭정이라고."], ["If I ruin anyone, it is only myself....", "내가 누군가를 망친다면, 그건 오직 나 자신뿐이야……."], ["I am not committing a murder.", "나는 살인을 저지르는 게 아니야."], ["Why do you look at me like that?", "왜 그렇게 나를 쳐다봐?"], ["Why are you so pale?", "왜 그렇게 창백해진 거야?"], ["Rodya, darling, what’s the matter?”", "로쟈, 오빠, 무슨 일이야?”"]], [["“Good heavens!", "“맙소사!"], ["You have made him faint,”", "네가 오빠를 기절시켰구나!”"], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."]], [["“No, no, nonsense!", "“아니, 아니에요, 별것 아니에요!"], ["It’s nothing.", "아무것도 아닙니다."], ["A little giddiness--not fainting.", "잠깐 어지러운 것뿐이에요 — 기절은 아니고요."], ["You have fainting on the brain.", "어머니는 머릿속이 온통 기절 생각뿐이시군요."], ["H’m, yes, what was I saying?", "흠, 그래, 내가 무슨 말을 하고 있었지?"], ["Oh, yes.", "아, 그래."], ["In what way will you get convincing proof to-day that you can respect him, and that he...", "너는 오늘 어떤 식으로 그를 존경할 수 있다는 확실한 증거를, 그리고 그가……"], ["esteems you, as you said.", "네 말마따나 너를 존중한다는 증거를 얻겠다는 거지."], ["I think you said to-day?”", "오늘이라고 했지, 아마?”"]], [["“Mother, show Rodya Pyotr Petrovitch’s letter,”", "“어머니, 표트르 페트로비치의 편지를 로쟈한테 보여 주세요.”"], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."]], [["With trembling hands, Pulcheria Alexandrovna gave him the letter.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 떨리는 손으로 그에게 편지를 건넸다."], ["He took it with great interest, but, before opening it, he suddenly looked with a sort of wonder at Dounia.", "그는 큰 흥미를 보이며 그것을 받았지만, 펼치기 전에 갑자기 일종의 경이로움을 담고 두냐를 바라보았다."]], [["“It is strange,”", "“이상하군.”"], ["he said, slowly, as though struck by a new idea.", "그가 새로운 생각에 충격받은 듯 천천히 말했다."], ["“What am I making such a fuss for?", "“내가 왜 이런 야단법석을 떠는 거지?"], ["What is it all about?", "이게 다 무슨 일이야?"], ["Marry whom you like!”", "네가 좋아하는 사람과 결혼하렴!”"]], [["He said this as though to himself, but said it aloud, and looked for some time at his sister, as though puzzled.", "그는 이 말을 마치 혼잣말처럼, 그러나 소리 내어 했고, 한동안 어리둥절한 듯 누이를 바라보았다."], ["He opened the letter at last, still with the same look of strange wonder on his face.", "마침내 그는, 여전히 그 기묘한 경이로움의 표정을 얼굴에 띤 채 편지를 펼쳤다."], ["Then, slowly and attentively, he began reading, and read it through twice.", "그러고는 천천히, 주의 깊게 읽기 시작해, 두 번을 통독했다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna showed marked anxiety, and all indeed expected something particular.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 뚜렷한 불안을 드러냈고, 사실 모두가 무언가 특별한 것을 기대하고 있었다."]], [["“What surprises me,”", "“내가 놀라운 건,”"], ["he began, after a short pause, handing the letter to his mother, but not addressing anyone in particular, “is that he is a business man, a lawyer, and his conversation is pretentious indeed, and yet he writes such an uneducated letter.”", "그가 잠시 멈췄다가, 편지를 어머니에게 건네면서, 그러나 딱히 누구를 향한 것도 아닌 채 입을 열었다. “이 사람이 사업가에 법률가이고, 말하는 것도 정말 거창한데, 정작 이렇게 무식한 편지를 쓴다는 점이에요.”"]], [["They all started.", "모두가 흠칫했다."], ["They had expected something quite different.", "그들은 전혀 다른 것을 기대하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“But they all write like that, you know,”", "“하지만 다들 그렇게 쓰는걸, 자네도 알잖아.”"], ["Razumihin observed, abruptly.", "라주미힌이 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Have you read it?”", "“너도 이걸 읽었어?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“응.”"]], [["“We showed him, Rodya. We...", "“우리가 저 친구한테 보여 줬단다, 로쟈."], ["consulted him just now,”", "우리가…… 방금 저 친구와 의논했어.”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna began, embarrassed.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 멋쩍게 입을 열었다."]], [["“That’s just the jargon of the courts,”", "“그게 바로 법정의 상투어야.”"], ["Razumihin put in.", "라주미힌이 끼어들었다."], ["“Legal documents are written like that to this day.”", "“법률 문서는 지금까지도 그렇게 쓰이거든.”"]], [["“Legal?", "“법률?"], ["Yes, it’s just legal--business language--not so very uneducated, and not quite educated--business language!”", "그래, 딱 법률식이지 — 사무용 언어 — 그리 무식하지도 않고, 그렇다고 아주 교양 있지도 않은 — 사무용 언어!”"]], [["“Pyotr Petrovitch makes no secret of the fact that he had a cheap education, he is proud indeed of having made his own way,”", "“표트르 페트로비치는 자신이 변변찮은 교육을 받았다는 걸 숨기지 않아요, 오히려 자기 힘으로 일어선 걸 자랑스러워하죠.”"], ["Avdotya Romanovna observed, somewhat offended by her brother’s tone.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 오빠의 어조에 다소 기분이 상한 듯 말했다."]], [["“Well, if he’s proud of it, he has reason, I don’t deny it.", "“뭐, 그게 자랑스럽다면 그럴 만한 이유가 있는 거겠지, 그건 부정 않아."], ["You seem to be offended, sister, at my making only such a frivolous criticism on the letter, and to think that I speak of such trifling matters on purpose to annoy you.", "너는 기분이 상한 모양이구나, 누이, 내가 편지에 대해 이렇게 하찮은 비평이나 한다고, 그리고 내가 너를 짜증 나게 하려고 일부러 이런 시시한 얘기를 한다고 생각하면서."], ["It is quite the contrary, an observation apropos of the style occurred to me that is by no means irrelevant as things stand.", "하지만 정반대야, 문체에 관해서 한 가지 짚이는 게 있는데, 지금 형편으로 봐선 결코 무관한 게 아니거든."], ["There is one expression, ‘blame yourselves’, put in very significantly and plainly, and there is besides a threat that he will go away at once if I am present.", "‘너희들 자신을 탓하라’는 표현이 한 군데 있는데, 무척 의미심장하고도 명백하게 박혀 있고, 게다가 내가 그 자리에 있으면 당장 떠나 버리겠다는 협박도 있어."], ["That threat to go away is equivalent to a threat to abandon you both if you are disobedient, and to abandon you now after summoning you to Petersburg.", "떠나겠다는 그 협박은, 두 사람이 말을 듣지 않으면 둘 다 버리겠다는 협박과 다름없고, 페테르부르크로 불러 놓고는 이제 와서 버리겠다는 거지."], ["Well, what do you think?", "자, 어떻게 생각해?"], ["Can one resent such an expression from Luzhin, as we should if he (he pointed to Razumihin) had written it, or Zossimov, or one of us?”", "만일 이 친구가(그가 라주미힌을 가리켰다), 혹은 조시모프가, 혹은 우리 중 누군가가 이런 말을 썼다면 우리가 분개했을 텐데, 루진한테서 나온 그런 표현은 분개해도 되는 거 아닌가?”"]], [["“N-no,”", "“아 — 니.”"], ["answered Dounia, with more animation.", "두냐가 좀 더 활기를 띠고 대답했다."], ["“I saw clearly that it was too naïvely expressed, and that perhaps he simply has no skill in writing...", "“나는 그게 너무 순진하게 표현됐다는 것을, 그리고 어쩌면 그가 그저 글재주가 없는 거라는 걸 분명히 알았어……."], ["that is a true criticism, brother.", "그건 정확한 비평이야, 오빠."], ["I did not expect, indeed...”", "사실 나는 미처 생각지 못했는데…….”"]], [["“It is expressed in legal style, and sounds coarser than perhaps he intended.", "“그건 법률식으로 표현된 거라, 어쩌면 그가 의도한 것보다 더 거칠게 들리는 거야."], ["But I must disillusion you a little.", "하지만 너의 환상을 조금 깨야겠구나."], ["There is one expression in the letter, one slander about me, and rather a contemptible one.", "이 편지엔 한 가지 표현이, 나에 대한 비방이 하나 있는데, 그것도 꽤나 비열한 거야."], ["I gave the money last night to the widow, a woman in consumption, crushed with trouble, and not ‘on the pretext of the funeral,’ but simply to pay for the funeral, and not to the daughter--a young woman, as he writes, of notorious behaviour (whom I saw last night for the first time in my life)--but to the widow.", "나는 어젯밤에 그 돈을, 슬픔에 짓눌린 폐병 걸린 과부한테 줬어, 그것도 ‘장례를 핑계 삼아’가 아니라 그저 장례 비용으로 쓰라고, 그리고 그가 쓴 대로 ‘소문난 품행의 젊은 여자’라는 그 딸한테(나는 어젯밤에 난생처음 그 여자를 봤다)가 아니라 과부한테 준 거야."], ["In all this I see a too hasty desire to slander me and to raise dissension between us.", "이 모든 데서 나는 나를 비방하고 우리 사이에 불화를 일으키려는 너무도 성급한 욕망을 봐."], ["It is expressed again in legal jargon, that is to say, with a too obvious display of the aim, and with a very naïve eagerness.", "그것도 다시 법률식 상투어로, 다시 말해 목적이 너무 빤히 드러나게, 그리고 무척 순진한 열의로 표현돼 있지."], ["He is a man of intelligence, but to act sensibly, intelligence is not enough.", "그는 영리한 사람이지만, 분별 있게 행동하려면 영리함만으로는 부족한 법이야."], ["It all shows the man and...", "이 모든 게 그 사람됨을 보여 주고……"], ["I don’t think he has a great esteem for you.", "나는 그가 너를 그리 존중하는 것 같지 않아."], ["I tell you this simply to warn you, because I sincerely wish for your good...”", "나는 이 말을 그저 너한테 경고하려고 하는 거야, 진심으로 너의 행복을 바라니까…….”"]], [["Dounia did not reply.", "두냐는 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["Her resolution had been taken.", "그녀의 결심은 이미 굳어져 있었다."], ["She was only awaiting the evening.", "그녀는 그저 저녁을 기다릴 뿐이었다."]], [["“Then what is your decision, Rodya?”", "“그래서 네 결정은 뭐냐, 로쟈?”"], ["asked Pulcheria Alexandrovna, who was more uneasy than ever at the sudden, new businesslike tone of his talk.", "그의 말이 갑작스레 새로 띤 사무적인 어조에 그 어느 때보다 불안해진 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 물었다."]], [["“What decision?”", "“무슨 결정이요?”"]], [["“You see Pyotr Petrovitch writes that you are not to be with us this evening, and that he will go away if you come.", "“있잖니, 표트르 페트로비치가 오늘 저녁에 네가 우리와 함께 있어선 안 되고, 만일 네가 오면 자기가 떠나 버리겠다고 썼잖니."], ["So will you...", "그러니 너는……"], ["come?”", "올 거니?”"]], [["“That, of course, is not for me to decide, but for you first, if you are not offended by such a request; and secondly, by Dounia, if she, too, is not offended.", "“그건, 물론, 제가 정할 일이 아니라 먼저 어머니가 정하실 일이죠, 어머니가 그런 부탁에 기분 상하지 않으셨다면요. 그리고 두 번째로는 두냐가 정할 일이고요, 두냐 역시 기분 상하지 않았다면."], ["I will do what you think best,”", "저는 두 분이 가장 좋다고 여기시는 대로 하겠습니다.”"], ["he added, drily.", "그가 무미건조하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Dounia has already decided, and I fully agree with her,”", "“두냐가 이미 결정했고, 나도 그 애한테 전적으로 동의한다.”"], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna hastened to declare.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 서둘러 단언했다."]], [["“I decided to ask you, Rodya, to urge you not to fail to be with us at this interview,”", "“나는 오빠한테 부탁하기로 결정했어, 로쟈, 이 만남에 꼭 우리와 함께 있어 달라고 간곡히 청하려고.”"], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."], ["“Will you come?”", "“와 줄 거지?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그래.”"]], [["“I will ask you, too, to be with us at eight o’clock,”", "“당신께도 부탁드릴게요, 여덟 시에 우리와 함께 있어 주세요.”"], ["she said, addressing Razumihin.", "그녀가 라주미힌을 향해 말했다."], ["“Mother, I am inviting him, too.”", "“어머니, 저는 이분도 초대하겠어요.”"]], [["“Quite right, Dounia.", "“잘 생각했다, 두냐."], ["Well, since you have decided,”", "그래, 네가 결정했다면,”"], ["added Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “so be it.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 덧붙였다. “그렇게 하자꾸나."], ["I shall feel easier myself.", "나도 마음이 한결 편하겠다."], ["I do not like concealment and deception.", "나는 숨기고 속이는 걸 좋아하지 않아."], ["Better let us have the whole truth....", "차라리 우리가 모든 진실을 드러내는 편이 낫지……."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch may be angry or not, now!”", "표트르 페트로비치가 화를 내든 말든, 이제!”"]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["At that moment the door was softly opened, and a young girl walked into the room, looking timidly about her.", "바로 그 순간 문이 살그머니 열리더니, 한 처녀가 겁먹은 듯 주위를 둘러보며 방 안으로 들어왔다."], ["Everyone turned towards her with surprise and curiosity.", "모두가 놀라움과 호기심에 차서 그녀에게로 고개를 돌렸다."], ["At first sight, Raskolnikov did not recognise her.", "라스콜니코프는 첫눈에 그녀를 알아보지 못했다."], ["It was Sofya Semyonovna Marmeladov.", "그녀는 소피야 세묘노브나 마르멜라도프였다."], ["He had seen her yesterday for the first time, but at such a moment, in such surroundings and in such a dress, that his memory retained a very different image of her.", "그는 어제 처음 그녀를 보았지만, 그것은 너무도 다른 순간, 다른 환경, 다른 옷차림 속에서였기에 그의 기억에는 사뭇 다른 모습이 남아 있었다."], ["Now she was a modestly and poorly-dressed young girl, very young, indeed, almost like a child, with a modest and refined manner, with a candid but somewhat frightened-looking face.", "지금 그녀는 수수하고 초라한 옷차림의 처녀였는데, 정말이지 무척 어려서, 거의 어린아이 같았고, 단정하고 우아한 몸가짐에, 솔직하면서도 어딘가 겁먹은 듯한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["She was wearing a very plain indoor dress, and had on a shabby old-fashioned hat, but she still carried a parasol.", "그녀는 너무도 평범한 실내복을 입고, 낡고 구식인 모자를 쓰고 있었으나, 여전히 양산만은 들고 있었다."], ["Unexpectedly finding the room full of people, she was not so much embarrassed as completely overwhelmed with shyness, like a little child.", "뜻밖에도 방 안이 사람들로 가득한 것을 보고, 그녀는 당황했다기보다는 마치 어린아이처럼 수줍음에 완전히 압도되어 버렸다."], ["She was even about to retreat.", "심지어 물러나려고까지 했다."], ["“Oh...", "\"아……"], ["it’s you!”", "당신이군요!\""], ["said Raskolnikov, extremely astonished, and he, too, was confused.", "라스콜니코프가 몹시 놀라 말했고, 그 자신도 당황했다."], ["He at once recollected that his mother and sister knew through Luzhin’s letter of “some young woman of notorious behaviour.”", "그는 어머니와 누이가 루진의 편지를 통해 '품행이 좋지 않다고 소문난 어느 젊은 여자'에 대해 알고 있다는 사실이 곧장 떠올랐다."], ["He had only just been protesting against Luzhin’s calumny and declaring that he had seen the girl last night for the first time, and suddenly she had walked in.", "그는 방금 전 루진의 중상을 반박하며, 어젯밤 그 처녀를 처음 보았노라고 단언하던 참이었는데, 갑자기 그녀가 걸어 들어온 것이다."], ["He remembered, too, that he had not protested against the expression “of notorious behaviour.”", "또한 그는 '품행이 좋지 않다고 소문난'이라는 표현에 대해서는 반박하지 않았던 것도 기억해 냈다."], ["All this passed vaguely and fleetingly through his brain, but looking at her more intently, he saw that the humiliated creature was so humiliated that he felt suddenly sorry for her.", "이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 어렴풋하고도 덧없이 스쳐 지나갔지만, 그녀를 좀 더 유심히 바라보니, 이 모욕당한 존재가 너무도 심하게 모욕당해 있어서 그는 갑자기 그녀가 가엾어졌다."], ["When she made a movement to retreat in terror, it sent a pang to his heart.", "그녀가 겁에 질려 물러나려고 몸을 움직였을 때, 그것은 그의 가슴을 찌르는 듯한 아픔을 안겨 주었다."]], [["“I did not expect you,”", "\"당신이 올 줄은 몰랐습니다.\""], ["he said, hurriedly, with a look that made her stop.", "그는 황급히, 그녀를 멈춰 세우는 듯한 눈길로 말했다."], ["“Please sit down.", "\"제발 앉으세요."], ["You come, no doubt, from Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 보내서 오셨겠지요."], ["Allow me--not there.", "잠깐만요 — 거기 말고."], ["Sit here....”", "여기 앉으세요…….\""]], [["At Sonia’s entrance, Razumihin, who had been sitting on one of Raskolnikov’s three chairs, close to the door, got up to allow her to enter.", "소냐가 들어서자, 라스콜니코프의 의자 셋 가운데 하나에 문 가까이 앉아 있던 라주미힌이 그녀가 들어올 수 있도록 일어섰다."], ["Raskolnikov had at first shown her the place on the sofa where Zossimov had been sitting, but feeling that the sofa which served him as a bed, was too _familiar_ a place, he hurriedly motioned her to Razumihin’s chair.", "라스콜니코프는 처음에 그녀에게 조시모프가 앉아 있던 소파 자리를 가리켰으나, 침대 노릇을 하는 그 소파가 너무 사적인 자리라는 생각이 들어, 황급히 라주미힌의 의자 쪽으로 그녀에게 손짓했다."]], [["“You sit here,”", "\"자네는 여기 앉게.\""], ["he said to Razumihin, putting him on the sofa.", "그는 라주미힌을 소파에 앉히며 말했다."]], [["Sonia sat down, almost shaking with terror, and looked timidly at the two ladies.", "소냐는 겁에 질려 거의 떨다시피 하며 앉았고, 두 부인을 겁먹은 듯 바라보았다."], ["It was evidently almost inconceivable to herself that she could sit down beside them.", "자신이 그들 곁에 앉을 수 있다는 것이 그녀 자신에게는 거의 상상조차 할 수 없는 일인 듯했다."], ["At the thought of it, she was so frightened that she hurriedly got up again, and in utter confusion addressed Raskolnikov.", "그 생각만으로도 너무 겁이 나서 그녀는 황급히 다시 일어났고, 완전히 당황한 채 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I... I...", "\"저……"], ["have come for one minute.", "저는…… 잠깐 들렀어요."], ["Forgive me for disturbing you,”", "방해해서 죄송합니다.\""], ["she began falteringly.", "그녀는 더듬더듬 말을 시작했다."], ["“I come from Katerina Ivanovna, and she had no one to send.", "\"카테리나 이바노브나가 보내서 왔어요, 보낼 사람이 달리 없었거든요."], ["Katerina Ivanovna told me to beg you...", "카테리나 이바노브나가 부탁드리랬어요……"], ["to be at the service...", "미사에 참석해 주시라고……"], ["in the morning...", "아침에……"], ["at Mitrofanievsky...", "미트로파니옙스키에서……"], ["and then...", "그러고 나서……"], ["to us... to her...", "저희 집으로……"], ["to do her the honour...", "그분 집으로…… 와 주시는 영광을……"], ["she told me to beg you...”", "부탁드리랬어요…….\""], ["Sonia stammered and ceased speaking.", "소냐는 말을 더듬다가 입을 다물었다."]], [["“I will try, certainly, most certainly,”", "\"노력하겠습니다, 꼭, 반드시요.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."], ["He, too, stood up, and he, too, faltered and could not finish his sentence.", "그도 일어섰고, 그도 말을 더듬으며 문장을 끝맺지 못했다."], ["“Please sit down,”", "\"제발 앉으세요.\""], ["he said, suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“I want to talk to you.", "\"당신과 얘기하고 싶습니다."], ["You are perhaps in a hurry, but please, be so kind, spare me two minutes,”", "바쁘신지도 모르지만, 부디 친절을 베풀어 이 분만 시간을 내주세요.\""], ["and he drew up a chair for her.", "그러고는 그녀에게 의자를 끌어다 주었다."]], [["Sonia sat down again, and again timidly she took a hurried, frightened look at the two ladies, and dropped her eyes.", "소냐는 다시 앉았고, 또다시 겁먹은 듯 두 부인을 허둥지둥 흘끔거리고는 눈을 떨구었다."], ["Raskolnikov’s pale face flushed, a shudder passed over him, his eyes glowed.", "라스콜니코프의 창백한 얼굴이 발갛게 달아올랐고, 전율이 그를 스쳤으며, 그의 눈이 이글거렸다."]], [["“Mother,”", "\"어머니.\""], ["he said, firmly and insistently, “this is Sofya Semyonovna Marmeladov, the daughter of that unfortunate Mr.", "그가 단호하고 끈질기게 말했다."], ["Marmeladov, who was run over yesterday before my eyes, and of whom I was just telling you.”", "\"이분은 소피야 세묘노브나 마르멜라도프입니다, 어제 제 눈앞에서 마차에 치인, 그 불행한 마르멜라도프 씨의 따님이지요, 방금 제가 어머니께 이야기했던 분요.\""]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna glanced at Sonia, and slightly screwed up her eyes.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 소냐를 흘끗 바라보더니 눈을 살짝 가늘게 떴다."], ["In spite of her embarrassment before Rodya’s urgent and challenging look, she could not deny herself that satisfaction.", "로쟈의 절박하고 도전적인 눈길 앞에서 당황하면서도, 그녀는 그 만족감만은 차마 억누를 수가 없었다."], ["Dounia gazed gravely and intently into the poor girl’s face, and scrutinised her with perplexity.", "두냐는 진지하고 골똘하게 그 가엾은 처녀의 얼굴을 응시하며, 의아한 표정으로 그녀를 뜯어보았다."], ["Sonia, hearing herself introduced, tried to raise her eyes again, but was more embarrassed than ever.", "소냐는 자신이 소개받는 것을 듣고 다시 눈을 들어 올리려 했으나, 어느 때보다 더 당황하고 말았다."]], [["“I wanted to ask you,”", "\"여쭤보고 싶었는데요.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, hastily, “how things were arranged yesterday.", "라스콜니코프가 서둘러 말했다. \"어제 일은 어떻게 처리됐나요?"], ["You were not worried by the police, for instance?”", "이를테면 경찰이 당신들을 괴롭히지는 않았나요?\""]], [["“No, that was all right...", "\"아니요, 다 괜찮았어요……"], ["it was too evident, the cause of death...", "사인이 너무 명백했거든요……"], ["they did not worry us...", "우릴 괴롭히지 않았어요……"], ["only the lodgers are angry.”", "다만 세입자들이 화가 나 있어요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“At the body’s remaining so long.", "\"시신이 너무 오래 남아 있어서요."], ["You see it is hot now.", "보세요, 지금은 더우니까요."], ["So that, to-day, they will carry it to the cemetery, into the chapel, until to-morrow.", "그래서 오늘 시신을 묘지로, 예배당으로 옮기게 돼요, 내일까지요."], ["At first Katerina Ivanovna was unwilling, but now she sees herself that it’s necessary...”", "처음엔 카테리나 이바노브나가 꺼렸지만, 이제는 그분 자신도 그게 어쩔 수 없다는 걸 알아요…….\""]], [["“To-day, then?”", "\"그럼 오늘이군요?\""]], [["“She begs you to do us the honour to be in the church to-morrow for the service, and then to be present at the funeral lunch.”", "\"그분은 내일 교회에서 미사에 참석해 주시는 영광을, 그러고 나서 장례 식사 자리에도 참석해 주시기를 부탁드린대요.\""]], [["“She is giving a funeral lunch?”", "\"장례 식사를 차린다고요?\""]], [["“Yes...", "\"네……"], ["just a little....", "그냥 조촐하게요……."], ["She told me to thank you very much for helping us yesterday.", "어제 저희를 도와주신 데 대해 정말 감사드린다고 전해 달라셨어요."], ["But for you, we should have had nothing for the funeral.”", "당신이 아니었다면 저희는 장례에 쓸 게 아무것도 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["All at once her lips and chin began trembling, but, with an effort, she controlled herself, looking down again.", "별안간 그녀의 입술과 턱이 떨리기 시작했지만, 그녀는 안간힘을 다해 자신을 억누르며 다시 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["During the conversation, Raskolnikov watched her carefully.", "대화가 오가는 동안 라스콜니코프는 그녀를 유심히 살폈다."], ["She had a thin, very thin, pale little face, rather irregular and angular, with a sharp little nose and chin.", "그녀는 야위고, 너무도 야윈, 창백한 작은 얼굴에, 다소 고르지 못하고 모가 나 있었으며, 작고 뾰족한 코와 턱을 지니고 있었다."], ["She could not have been called pretty, but her blue eyes were so clear, and when they lighted up, there was such a kindliness and simplicity in her expression that one could not help being attracted.", "예쁘다고는 할 수 없었지만, 그녀의 파란 눈은 너무도 맑았고, 그 눈이 빛날 때면 표정 속에 어찌나 다정함과 순박함이 어리는지 마음이 끌리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Her face, and her whole figure indeed, had another peculiar characteristic.", "그녀의 얼굴은, 아니 사실 그녀의 온몸은 또 다른 독특한 특징을 지니고 있었다."], ["In spite of her eighteen years, she looked almost a little girl--almost a child.", "열여덟이라는 나이에도 불구하고, 그녀는 거의 어린 소녀처럼 — 거의 어린아이처럼 보였다."], ["And in some of her gestures, this childishness seemed almost absurd.", "그리고 그녀의 어떤 몸짓에서는 이 어린애 같은 면이 거의 우스꽝스럽게 느껴지기까지 했다."]], [["“But has Katerina Ivanovna been able to manage with such small means?", "\"하지만 그렇게 적은 돈으로 카테리나 이바노브나가 감당할 수 있었나요?"], ["Does she even mean to have a funeral lunch?”", "정말 장례 식사까지 차릴 생각인가요?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked, persistently keeping up the conversation.", "라스콜니코프가 끈질기게 대화를 이어 가며 물었다."]], [["“The coffin will be plain, of course...", "\"관은 물론 소박할 거예요……"], ["and everything will be plain, so it won’t cost much.", "모든 게 다 소박할 테니까, 비용이 많이 들진 않을 거예요."], ["Katerina Ivanovna and I have reckoned it all out, so that there will be enough left...", "카테리나 이바노브나랑 제가 다 셈을 해 봤거든요, 그래서 남는 돈도 좀 있을 거예요……"], ["and Katerina Ivanovna was very anxious it should be so.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 꼭 그래야 한다고 무척 애를 쓰셨어요."], ["You know one can’t...", "아시잖아요, 그러지 않을 수가 없는 걸……"], ["it’s a comfort to her...", "그게 그분에겐 위안이 되거든요……"], ["she is like that, you know....”", "그분은 그런 분이세요, 아시잖아요…….\""]], [["“I understand, I understand...", "\"이해합니다, 이해해요……"], ["of course...", "물론이죠……"], ["why do you look at my room like that?", "왜 그렇게 제 방을 보시는 거죠?"], ["My mother has just said it is like a tomb.”", "어머니도 방금 무덤 같다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["“You gave us everything yesterday,”", "\"당신은 어제 가진 걸 전부 저희에게 주셨잖아요.\""], ["Sonia said suddenly, in reply, in a loud rapid whisper; and again she looked down in confusion.", "소냐가 갑자기 큰 소리로 빠르게 속삭이듯 대답했다. 그러고는 또다시 당황하여 고개를 떨구었다."], ["Her lips and chin were trembling once more.", "그녀의 입술과 턱이 또 한 번 떨리고 있었다."], ["She had been struck at once by Raskolnikov’s poor surroundings, and now these words broke out spontaneously.", "그녀는 라스콜니코프의 초라한 환경에 곧장 충격을 받았고, 이제 그 말이 절로 터져 나온 것이었다."], ["A silence followed.", "침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["There was a light in Dounia’s eyes, and even Pulcheria Alexandrovna looked kindly at Sonia.", "두냐의 눈에는 빛이 어렸고, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나조차 소냐를 다정한 눈길로 바라보았다."]], [["“Rodya,”", "\"로쟈.\""], ["she said, getting up, “we shall have dinner together, of course.", "그녀가 일어서며 말했다. \"우리 함께 저녁을 먹어야지, 당연히."], ["Come, Dounia....", "가자, 두냐……."], ["And you, Rodya, had better go for a little walk, and then rest and lie down before you come to see us....", "그리고 너, 로쟈, 산책이라도 좀 하다가, 우리한테 오기 전에 쉬면서 좀 누워 있는 게 좋겠다……."], ["I am afraid we have exhausted you....”", "우리가 너를 너무 지치게 한 것 같구나…….\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I’ll come,”", "\"네, 네, 갈게요.\""], ["he answered, getting up fussily.", "그는 부산스레 일어나며 대답했다."], ["“But I have something to see to.”", "\"하지만 처리할 일이 좀 있어서요.\""]], [["“But surely you will have dinner together?”", "\"하지만 분명 함께 저녁을 먹는 거지?\""], ["cried Razumihin, looking in surprise at Raskolnikov.", "라주미힌이 놀란 눈으로 라스콜니코프를 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I am coming...", "\"네, 네, 갑니다……"], ["of course, of course!", "물론이죠, 물론이고말고요!"], ["And you stay a minute.", "그리고 자네는 잠깐만 남아 있게."], ["You do not want him just now, do you, mother?", "어머니, 지금 당장 얘를 데려가실 건 아니죠?"], ["Or perhaps I am taking him from you?”", "아니면 제가 어머니한테서 얘를 빼앗는 건가요?\""]], [["“Oh, no, no.", "\"오, 아니, 아니다."], ["And will you, Dmitri Prokofitch, do us the favour of dining with us?”", "그러면, 드미트리 프로코피치, 우리와 함께 저녁 식사를 해 주는 호의를 베풀어 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Please do,”", "\"부디 그래 주세요.\""], ["added Dounia.", "두냐가 덧붙였다."]], [["Razumihin bowed, positively radiant.", "라주미힌은 그야말로 환히 빛나며 고개를 숙였다."], ["For one moment, they were all strangely embarrassed.", "한순간 그들 모두가 묘하게 당황했다."]], [["“Good-bye, Rodya, that is till we meet.", "\"잘 있어, 로쟈, 아니, 또 만날 때까지구나."], ["I do not like saying good-bye.", "난 작별 인사하는 걸 좋아하지 않아."], ["Good-bye, Nastasya.", "잘 있어요, 나스타시야."], ["Ah, I have said good-bye again.”", "아, 또 작별 인사를 하고 말았네.\""]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna meant to greet Sonia, too; but it somehow failed to come off, and she went in a flutter out of the room.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 소냐에게도 인사를 건네려 했으나, 어쩐지 잘 되지 않았고, 그녀는 허둥거리며 방을 나갔다."]], [["But Avdotya Romanovna seemed to await her turn, and following her mother out, gave Sonia an attentive, courteous bow.", "그러나 아브도티야 로마노브나는 자기 차례를 기다리고 있던 듯, 어머니를 따라 나가면서 소냐에게 정중하고 예의 바른 인사를 건넸다."], ["Sonia, in confusion, gave a hurried, frightened curtsy.", "소냐는 당황하여 황급히, 겁먹은 듯 무릎을 살짝 굽혀 답례했다."], ["There was a look of poignant discomfort in her face, as though Avdotya Romanovna’s courtesy and attention were oppressive and painful to her.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 가슴 저미는 불편함의 빛이 어렸으니, 마치 아브도티야 로마노브나의 정중함과 배려가 그녀에게는 짓누르는 듯 괴롭게 느껴지는 것 같았다."]], [["“Dounia, good-bye,”", "\"두냐, 잘 가.\""], ["called Raskolnikov, in the passage.", "라스콜니코프가 통로에서 불렀다."], ["“Give me your hand.”", "\"손 좀 줘 봐.\""]], [["“Why, I did give it to you.", "\"아니, 아까 줬잖아."], ["Have you forgotten?”", "잊었어?\""], ["said Dounia, turning warmly and awkwardly to him.", "두냐가 다정하면서도 어색하게 그에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“Never mind, give it to me again.”", "\"상관없어, 다시 한번 줘 봐.\""], ["And he squeezed her fingers warmly.", "그러고는 그녀의 손가락을 다정하게 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["Dounia smiled, flushed, pulled her hand away, and went off quite happy.", "두냐는 미소 짓고, 얼굴을 붉히며 손을 빼더니, 무척 행복한 기분으로 떠나갔다."]], [["“Come, that’s capital,”", "\"자, 정말 좋군요.\""], ["he said to Sonia, going back and looking brightly at her.", "그는 돌아와 그녀를 밝은 눈으로 바라보며 소냐에게 말했다."], ["“God give peace to the dead, the living have still to live.", "\"하느님이 죽은 이에게 평안을 주시기를, 산 사람은 그래도 살아가야 하는 법이죠."], ["That is right, isn’t it?”", "그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["Sonia looked surprised at the sudden brightness of his face.", "소냐는 그의 얼굴이 갑자기 환해진 데에 놀란 듯 그를 바라보았다."], ["He looked at her for some moments in silence.", "그는 잠시 말없이 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["The whole history of the dead father floated before his memory in those moments....", "그 순간 죽은 아버지의 온갖 사연이 그의 기억 속에 떠올랐다……."]], [["*****\n“Heavens, Dounia,”", "\"맙소사, 두냐.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna began, as soon as they were in the street, “I really feel relieved myself at coming away--more at ease.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 거리로 나서자마자 입을 열었다. \"솔직히 나는 그곳을 떠나오니 마음이 놓이는구나 — 한결 편해졌어."], ["How little did I think yesterday in the train that I could ever be glad of that.”", "어제 기차에서, 내가 그런 마음을 다 기뻐하게 될 줄을 어찌 알았겠니.\""]], [["“I tell you again, mother, he is still very ill.", "\"다시 말씀드리지만, 어머니, 오빠는 아직 많이 아파요."], ["Don’t you see it?", "안 보이세요?"], ["Perhaps worrying about us upset him.", "어쩌면 우리 걱정 때문에 마음이 어지러워졌는지도 몰라요."], ["We must be patient, and much, much can be forgiven.”", "우리가 참아야 해요, 그러면 아주, 아주 많은 걸 용서할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, you were not very patient!”", "\"하지만 너는 별로 참지 않더구나!\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna caught her up, hotly and jealously.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 격하고도 시샘하듯 그녀의 말을 받아쳤다."], ["“Do you know, Dounia, I was looking at you two.", "\"있잖니, 두냐, 나는 너희 둘을 보고 있었단다."], ["You are the very portrait of him, and not so much in face as in soul.", "너는 영락없이 그애를 빼다 박았어, 얼굴보다도 마음이 더 그래."], ["You are both melancholy, both morose and hot-tempered, both haughty and both generous....", "너희 둘 다 우울하고, 둘 다 시무룩하고 성미가 급하고, 둘 다 거만하면서도 둘 다 너그럽지……."], ["Surely he can’t be an egoist, Dounia.", "설마 그애가 이기주의자일 리는 없겠지, 두냐."], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["When I think of what is in store for us this evening, my heart sinks!”", "오늘 저녁에 우리한테 무슨 일이 닥칠지 생각하면, 가슴이 철렁 내려앉는구나!\""]], [["“Don’t be uneasy, mother.", "\"마음 졸이지 마세요, 어머니."], ["What must be, will be.”", "될 일은 어차피 되게 마련이에요.\""]], [["“Dounia, only think what a position we are in!", "\"두냐, 우리가 어떤 처지에 놓여 있는지 좀 생각해 보렴!"], ["What if Pyotr Petrovitch breaks it off?”", "만약에 표트르 페트로비치가 파혼이라도 하면 어쩌니?\""], ["poor Pulcheria Alexandrovna blurted out, incautiously.", "가엾은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 경솔하게 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["“He won’t be worth much if he does,”", "\"그가 그런다면 그만한 가치도 없는 사람이죠.\""], ["answered Dounia, sharply and contemptuously.", "두냐가 날카롭고 경멸하듯 대답했다."]], [["“We did well to come away,”", "\"우리가 잘 빠져나온 거야.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna hurriedly broke in.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 황급히 말을 끊었다."], ["“He was in a hurry about some business or other.", "\"그애가 무슨 일인지 처리할 게 있어서 바쁘다고 했잖니."], ["If he gets out and has a breath of air...", "밖에 나가서 바람이라도 좀 쐬면……"], ["it is fearfully close in his room....", "그 방은 숨 막히게 답답하더구나……."], ["But where is one to get a breath of air here?", "그런데 여기선 어디서 바람을 쐰단 말이냐?"], ["The very streets here feel like shut-up rooms.", "여긴 거리조차 꽉 막힌 방 같구나. 맙소사!"], ["Good heavens! what a town!...", "이게 무슨 도시람!……"], ["stay...", "잠깐……"], ["this side...", "이쪽으로……"], ["they will crush you--carrying something.", "깔리겠다 — 뭘 나르고 있어."], ["Why, it is a piano they have got, I declare...", "아니, 저건 피아노로구나, 정말이지……"], ["how they push!...", "어쩜 저렇게 밀어 대는지!……"], ["I am very much afraid of that young woman, too.”", "나는 그 젊은 여자도 무척 마음에 걸리는구나.\""]], [["“What young woman, mother?”", "\"무슨 젊은 여자요, 어머니?\""]], [["“Why, that Sofya Semyonovna, who was there just now.”", "\"아니, 그 소피야 세묘노브나 말이다, 방금 거기 있었던.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“I have a presentiment, Dounia.", "\"예감이 들어, 두냐."], ["Well, you may believe it or not, but as soon as she came in, that very minute, I felt that she was the chief cause of the trouble....”", "음, 믿든 안 믿든 네 맘이다만, 그 여자가 들어온 순간, 바로 그 순간, 나는 그 여자가 이 모든 골칫거리의 주된 원인이라는 걸 느꼈단다…….\""]], [["“Nothing of the sort!”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아요!\""], ["cried Dounia, in vexation.", "두냐가 화가 나서 외쳤다."], ["“What nonsense, with your presentiments, mother!", "\"그놈의 예감이라니, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리예요, 어머니!"], ["He only made her acquaintance the evening before, and he did not know her when she came in.”", "오빠는 그 전날 저녁에야 그 여자를 알게 됐고, 그 여자가 들어왔을 때 알아보지도 못했잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, you will see....", "\"그래, 두고 보면 알 거다……."], ["She worries me; but you will see, you will see!", "그 여자가 신경 쓰여. 하지만 두고 봐, 두고 보라고!"], ["I was so frightened.", "나는 어찌나 겁이 나던지."], ["She was gazing at me with those eyes.", "그 여자가 그런 눈으로 나를 응시하고 있었어."], ["I could scarcely sit still in my chair when he began introducing her, do you remember?", "그애가 그 여자를 소개하기 시작했을 때 나는 의자에 가만히 앉아 있기조차 힘들었단다, 기억나니?"], ["It seems so strange, but Pyotr Petrovitch writes like that about her, and he introduces her to us--to you!", "참 이상한 일이야, 표트르 페트로비치는 그 여자에 대해 그렇게 써 놓고선, 그애는 그 여자를 우리한테 — 너한테 소개하다니!"], ["So he must think a great deal of her.”", "그러니 그애가 그 여자를 무척 마음에 두고 있는 게 분명해.\""]], [["“People will write anything.", "\"사람들은 무슨 말이든 써 댈 수 있어요."], ["We were talked about and written about, too. Have you forgotten?", "우리에 대해서도 이러쿵저러쿵 말이 많았고 글로도 쓰였잖아요."], ["I am sure that she is a good girl, and that it is all nonsense.”", "잊으셨어요? 분명 그 여자는 좋은 사람이고, 다 터무니없는 소리예요.\""]], [["“God grant it may be!”", "\"제발 그랬으면 좋으련만!\""]], [["“And Pyotr Petrovitch is a contemptible slanderer,”", "\"그리고 표트르 페트로비치는 비열한 중상모략꾼이에요.\""], ["Dounia snapped out, suddenly.", "두냐가 갑자기 쏘아붙였다."]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was crushed; the conversation was not resumed.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 풀이 죽었고, 대화는 더 이어지지 않았다."]], [["*****\n“I will tell you what I want with you,”", "\"자네한테 부탁할 게 뭔지 말해 주지.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, drawing Razumihin to the window.", "라스콜니코프가 라주미힌을 창가로 끌어당기며 말했다."]], [["“Then I will tell Katerina Ivanovna that you are coming,”", "\"그럼 카테리나 이바노브나한테 당신이 오신다고 전할게요.\""], ["Sonia said hurriedly, preparing to depart.", "소냐가 떠날 채비를 하며 황급히 말했다."]], [["“One minute, Sofya Semyonovna.", "\"잠깐만요, 소피야 세묘노브나."], ["We have no secrets.", "우리한테 비밀 같은 건 없습니다."], ["You are not in our way.", "당신이 방해되는 건 아니에요."], ["I want to have another word or two with you.", "당신과 한두 마디 더 나누고 싶군요."], ["Listen!”", "들어 봐!\""], ["he turned suddenly to Razumihin again.", "그는 다시 라주미힌에게로 불쑥 돌아섰다."], ["“You know that...", "\"그……"], ["what’s his name...", "이름이 뭐더라……"], ["Porfiry Petrovitch?”", "포르피리 페트로비치 알지?\""]], [["“I should think so!", "\"알다마다!"], ["He is a relation.", "친척인데. 왜?\""], ["Why?” added the latter, with interest.", "라주미힌이 흥미를 보이며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Is not he managing that case...", "\"그가 그 사건을 맡고 있지 않나……"], ["you know, about that murder?...", "알잖아, 그 살인 사건 말이야……."], ["You were speaking about it yesterday.”", "어제 자네가 그 얘기를 했었지.\""]], [["“Yes...", "\"그래……"], ["well?”", "그런데?\""], ["Razumihin’s eyes opened wide.", "라주미힌의 눈이 휘둥그레졌다."]], [["“He was inquiring for people who had pawned things, and I have some pledges there, too--trifles--a ring my sister gave me as a keepsake when I left home, and my father’s silver watch--they are only worth five or six roubles altogether...", "\"그가 물건을 저당 잡힌 사람들을 수소문하고 있었는데, 나도 거기에 저당물이 몇 개 있거든 — 하찮은 거지만 — 집을 떠날 때 누이가 기념으로 준 반지하고, 아버지의 은시계인데 — 다 합쳐 봐야 오 루블이나 육 루블 값어치밖에 안 되지만……"], ["but I value them.", "나한텐 소중한 거야."], ["So what am I to do now?", "그러니 이제 어떻게 해야 하지?"], ["I do not want to lose the things, especially the watch.", "그 물건들을 잃고 싶지 않아, 특히 시계는."], ["I was quaking just now, for fear mother would ask to look at it, when we spoke of Dounia’s watch.", "아까 두냐의 시계 얘기가 나왔을 때, 어머니가 그걸 보자고 할까 봐 조마조마했다네."], ["It is the only thing of father’s left us.", "그건 우리한테 남은 아버지의 유일한 유품이거든."], ["She would be ill if it were lost.", "그게 없어지면 어머니가 앓아누우실 거야."], ["You know what women are. So tell me what to do.", "여자들이 어떤지 자네도 알잖아."], ["I know I ought to have given notice at the police station, but would it not be better to go straight to Porfiry?", "그러니 어떻게 해야 할지 말해 줘. 경찰서에 신고했어야 한다는 건 알지만, 곧장 포르피리한테 가는 게 낫지 않을까?"], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["What do you think?", "어떻게 생각해?"], ["The matter might be settled more quickly.", "일이 더 빨리 처리될 수도 있잖아."], ["You see, mother may ask for it before dinner.”", "알다시피, 어머니가 저녁 식사 전에 그걸 보자고 할지도 모르거든.\""]], [["“Certainly not to the police station.", "\"절대 경찰서는 안 돼."], ["Certainly to Porfiry,”", "무조건 포르피리한테 가야지.\""], ["Razumihin shouted in extraordinary excitement.", "라주미힌이 유난히 흥분하여 소리쳤다."], ["“Well, how glad I am.", "\"야, 정말 잘됐다."], ["Let us go at once.", "당장 가자."], ["It is a couple of steps.", "몇 걸음만 가면 돼."], ["We shall be sure to find him.”", "분명 그를 만날 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["“Very well, let us go.”", "\"좋아, 가지.\""]], [["“And he will be very, very glad to make your acquaintance.", "\"그리고 그 친구는 자네와 알게 되는 걸 무척, 무척 기뻐할 거야."], ["I have often talked to him of you at different times.", "이런저런 때에 그한테 자네 얘기를 자주 했거든."], ["I was speaking of you yesterday.", "어제도 자네 얘기를 했어. 가자."], ["Let us go. So you knew the old woman?", "그러니까 자네가 그 노파를 알고 있었단 말이지?"], ["So that’s it!", "그렇구나!"], ["It is all turning out splendidly....", "모든 게 멋지게 풀려 가는걸……."], ["Oh, yes, Sofya Ivanovna...”", "아, 그래, 소피야 이바노브나……\""]], [["“Sofya Semyonovna,”", "\"소피야 세묘노브나입니다.\""], ["corrected Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 바로잡았다."], ["“Sofya Semyonovna, this is my friend Razumihin, and he is a good man.”", "\"소피야 세묘노브나, 이쪽은 제 친구 라주미힌인데, 좋은 사람입니다.\""]], [["“If you have to go now,”", "\"이제 가셔야 한다면요.\""], ["Sonia was beginning, not looking at Razumihin at all, and still more embarrassed.", "소냐가 라주미힌은 아예 쳐다보지도 않고, 더욱더 당황하며 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Let us go,”", "\"가지요.\""], ["decided Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 결정했다."], ["“I will come to you to-day, Sofya Semyonovna.", "\"오늘 당신을 찾아가겠습니다, 소피야 세묘노브나."], ["Only tell me where you live.”", "어디 사는지만 말해 줘요.\""]], [["He was not exactly ill at ease, but seemed hurried, and avoided her eyes.", "그는 딱히 불편해하는 것은 아니었지만, 서두르는 듯했고, 그녀의 눈길을 피했다."], ["Sonia gave her address, and flushed as she did so.", "소냐는 주소를 일러 주었고, 그러면서 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["They all went out together.", "그들은 모두 함께 밖으로 나왔다."]], [["“Don’t you lock up?”", "\"문을 안 잠그는 거야?\""], ["asked Razumihin, following him on to the stairs.", "라주미힌이 그를 따라 계단으로 나서며 물었다."]], [["“Never,”", "\"전혀.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."], ["“I have been meaning to buy a lock for these two years.", "\"이 년째 자물쇠를 사야지 사야지 하고 있어."], ["People are happy who have no need of locks,”", "자물쇠가 필요 없는 사람은 행복한 거지.\""], ["he said, laughing, to Sonia.", "그가 소냐에게 웃으며 말했다."], ["They stood still in the gateway.", "그들은 대문간에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Do you go to the right, Sofya Semyonovna?", "\"오른쪽으로 가시나요, 소피야 세묘노브나?"], ["How did you find me, by the way?”", "그건 그렇고, 어떻게 나를 찾아냈죠?\""], ["he added, as though he wanted to say something quite different.", "그가 마치 전혀 다른 말을 하고 싶은 듯이 덧붙였다."], ["He wanted to look at her soft clear eyes, but this was not easy.", "그는 그녀의 부드럽고 맑은 눈을 들여다보고 싶었지만, 그것은 쉽지 않았다."]], [["“Why, you gave your address to Polenka yesterday.”", "\"아니, 어제 폴렌카한테 주소를 알려 주셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Polenka?", "\"폴렌카?"], ["Oh, yes; Polenka, that is the little girl.", "아, 그래요, 폴렌카, 그 어린 여자아이 말이군요."], ["She is your sister?", "당신 동생인가요?"], ["Did I give her the address?”", "내가 그애한테 주소를 알려 줬다고요?\""]], [["“Why, had you forgotten?”", "\"아니, 잊으셨어요?\""]], [["“No, I remember.”", "\"아니, 기억나요.\""]], [["“I had heard my father speak of you...", "\"아버지가 당신 얘기를 하시는 걸 들었어요……"], ["only I did not know your name, and he did not know it.", "다만 저는 성함을 몰랐고, 아버지도 모르셨죠."], ["And now I came...", "그래서 오늘 와서……"], ["and as I had learnt your name, I asked to-day, ‘Where does Mr.", "성함을 알게 됐으니, '라스콜니코프 씨는 어디 사시나요?'"], ["Raskolnikov live?’ I did not know you had only a room too....", "하고 물었던 거예요. 당신도 방 한 칸만 세 들어 사시는 줄은 몰랐어요……."], ["Good-bye, I will tell Katerina Ivanovna.”", "안녕히 계세요, 카테리나 이바노브나한테 전할게요.\""]], [["She was extremely glad to escape at last; she went away looking down, hurrying to get out of sight as soon as possible, to walk the twenty steps to the turning on the right and to be at last alone, and then moving rapidly along, looking at no one, noticing nothing, to think, to remember, to meditate on every word, every detail.", "그녀는 마침내 빠져나가게 된 것이 너무도 기뻤다. 그녀는 고개를 떨군 채 떠나갔고, 한시바삐 시야에서 사라져, 오른쪽 모퉁이까지 스무 걸음을 걸어 마침내 혼자가 되려고 서둘렀으며, 그런 다음에는 아무도 쳐다보지 않고, 아무것도 알아차리지 못한 채 빠르게 걸어가면서, 생각에 잠기고, 떠올리고, 한마디 한마디, 사소한 것 하나하나를 곱씹어 보려고 했다."], ["Never, never had she felt anything like this.", "그녀는 이런 것을 한 번도, 단 한 번도 느껴 본 적이 없었다."], ["Dimly and unconsciously a whole new world was opening before her.", "어렴풋이, 무의식중에 온전히 새로운 세계가 그녀 앞에 열리고 있었다."], ["She remembered suddenly that Raskolnikov meant to come to her that day, perhaps at once!", "그녀는 라스콜니코프가 오늘, 어쩌면 당장이라도 자신을 찾아오려 한다는 사실이 문득 떠올랐다!"]], [["“Only not to-day, please, not to-day!”", "\"그저 오늘만은, 제발, 오늘만은 안 돼요!\""], ["she kept muttering with a sinking heart, as though entreating someone, like a frightened child.", "그녀는 마치 누군가에게 애원하듯, 겁먹은 어린아이처럼, 가슴이 철렁 내려앉은 채 줄곧 중얼거렸다."], ["“Mercy!", "\"맙소사!"], ["to me...", "나한테……"], ["to that room...", "그 방으로……"], ["he will see...", "그가 보게 될 텐데……"], ["oh, dear!”", "오, 어쩌면 좋아!\""]], [["She was not capable at that instant of noticing an unknown gentleman who was watching her and following at her heels.", "그녀는 그 순간 자신을 지켜보며 바짝 뒤를 밟고 있던 낯선 신사를 알아챌 처지가 못 되었다."], ["He had accompanied her from the gateway.", "그는 대문간에서부터 그녀를 따라왔던 것이다."], ["At the moment when Razumihin, Raskolnikov, and she stood still at parting on the pavement, this gentleman, who was just passing, started on hearing Sonia’s words: “and I asked where Mr. Raskolnikov lived?”", "라주미힌과 라스콜니코프, 그리고 그녀가 인도에 멈춰 서서 작별 인사를 나누던 그 순간, 마침 그곳을 지나가던 이 신사는 소냐의 말, \"그래서 라스콜니코프 씨가 어디 사시느냐고 물었어요?\"라는 말을 듣고 흠칫했다."], ["He turned a rapid but attentive look upon all three, especially upon Raskolnikov, to whom Sonia was speaking; then looked back and noted the house.", "그는 빠르면서도 주의 깊은 눈길을 세 사람 모두에게, 특히 소냐가 말을 걸고 있던 라스콜니코프에게 던졌다. 그러고는 뒤를 돌아보며 그 집을 눈여겨 두었다."], ["All this was done in an instant as he passed, and trying not to betray his interest, he walked on more slowly as though waiting for something.", "이 모든 일을 그는 지나가면서 순식간에 해치웠고, 자신의 관심을 들키지 않으려 애쓰며, 마치 무언가를 기다리는 듯 좀 더 천천히 걸어갔다."], ["He was waiting for Sonia; he saw that they were parting, and that Sonia was going home.", "그는 소냐를 기다리고 있었던 것이다. 그들이 헤어지고 있으며, 소냐가 집으로 가고 있다는 것을 보았던 것이다."]], [["“Home?", "'집으로?"], ["Where?", "어디로?"], ["I’ve seen that face somewhere,”", "저 얼굴을 어디선가 본 적이 있는데.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“I must find out.”", "'알아내야겠어.'"]], [["At the turning he crossed over, looked round, and saw Sonia coming the same way, noticing nothing.", "모퉁이에서 그는 길을 건너 주위를 둘러보았고, 소냐가 아무것도 알아차리지 못한 채 같은 길로 오고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She turned the corner.", "그녀는 모퉁이를 돌았다."], ["He followed her on the other side.", "그는 반대편에서 그녀를 따라갔다."], ["After about fifty paces he crossed over again, overtook her and kept two or three yards behind her.", "쉰 걸음쯤 가서 그는 다시 길을 건너, 그녀를 따라잡고는 두세 걸음 뒤에 바짝 붙어 갔다."]], [["He was a man about fifty, rather tall and thickly set, with broad high shoulders which made him look as though he stooped a little.", "그는 쉰쯤 된 남자로, 키가 꽤 크고 다부졌으며, 넓고 떡 벌어진 어깨 탓에 어쩐지 약간 구부정해 보였다."], ["He wore good and fashionable clothes, and looked like a gentleman of position.", "그는 좋고 멋스러운 옷을 입었고, 지체 있는 신사처럼 보였다."], ["He carried a handsome cane, which he tapped on the pavement at each step; his gloves were spotless.", "그는 멋진 지팡이를 들고 한 걸음마다 인도를 톡톡 두드렸으며, 장갑은 티 하나 없이 깨끗했다."], ["He had a broad, rather pleasant face with high cheek-bones and a fresh colour, not often seen in Petersburg.", "그는 넓적하고 다소 호감 가는 얼굴에 광대뼈가 높고, 페테르부르크에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 싱그러운 혈색을 띠고 있었다."], ["His flaxen hair was still abundant, and only touched here and there with grey, and his thick square beard was even lighter than his hair.", "그의 아마빛 머리칼은 아직 숱이 많았고, 여기저기 희끗희끗 셀 뿐이었으며, 네모지고 숱 많은 턱수염은 머리칼보다도 더 옅었다."], ["His eyes were blue and had a cold and thoughtful look; his lips were crimson.", "그의 눈은 파랗고 차갑고 사색적인 빛을 띠었으며, 입술은 진홍색이었다."], ["He was a remarkedly well-preserved man and looked much younger than his years.", "그는 유난히 정정하게 잘 보존된 남자로, 나이보다 훨씬 젊어 보였다."]], [["When Sonia came out on the canal bank, they were the only two persons on the pavement.", "소냐가 운하 둑으로 나섰을 때, 인도에는 그들 둘뿐이었다."], ["He observed her dreaminess and preoccupation.", "그는 그녀가 몽상에 잠겨 골똘히 생각에 빠져 있는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["On reaching the house where she lodged, Sonia turned in at the gate; he followed her, seeming rather surprised.", "그녀가 세 들어 사는 집에 다다르자, 소냐는 대문 안으로 꺾어 들어갔다. 그는 다소 놀란 듯한 표정으로 그녀를 따라갔다."], ["In the courtyard she turned to the right corner.", "안뜰에서 그녀는 오른쪽 모퉁이로 향했다. \"어럽쇼!\""], ["“Bah!” muttered the unknown gentleman, and mounted the stairs behind her.", "낯선 신사가 중얼거리더니, 그녀를 뒤따라 계단을 올랐다."], ["Only then Sonia noticed him.", "그제야 소냐는 그를 알아챘다."], ["She reached the third storey, turned down the passage, and rang at No. 9.", "그녀는 삼 층에 이르러 통로로 꺾어 들더니, 9호실 초인종을 울렸다."], ["On the door was inscribed in chalk, “Kapernaumov, Tailor.” “Bah!”", "문에는 분필로 \"재단사 카페르나우모프\"라고 적혀 있었다."], ["the stranger repeated again, wondering at the strange coincidence, and he rang next door, at No.", "\"어럽쇼!\" 낯선 사내가 그 묘한 우연에 놀라며 다시 중얼거렸고, 바로 옆집, 8호실 초인종을 울렸다."], ["8. The doors were two or three yards apart.", "두 문은 두세 걸음 떨어져 있었다."]], [["“You lodge at Kapernaumov’s,”", "\"카페르나우모프네 집에 사시는군요.\""], ["he said, looking at Sonia and laughing.", "그는 소냐를 바라보며 웃으면서 말했다."], ["“He altered a waistcoat for me yesterday.", "\"어제 그 사람이 내 조끼를 손봐 줬지요."], ["I am staying close here at Madame Resslich’s.", "나는 바로 이 근처, 레슬리히 부인 댁에 묵고 있어요."], ["How odd!”", "참 묘하네요!\""], ["Sonia looked at him attentively.", "소냐가 그를 유심히 바라보았다."]], [["“We are neighbours,”", "\"우리는 이웃이군요.\""], ["he went on gaily.", "그가 유쾌하게 말을 이었다."], ["“I only came to town the day before yesterday.", "\"나는 그저께 막 시내에 왔답니다."], ["Good-bye for the present.”", "그럼 이만 안녕히.\""]], [["Sonia made no reply; the door opened and she slipped in.", "소냐는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 문이 열리자 그녀는 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다."], ["She felt for some reason ashamed and uneasy. *****", "그녀는 어쩐지 부끄럽고 불안한 기분이 들었다."]], [["On the way to Porfiry’s, Razumihin was obviously excited.", "포르피리한테 가는 길에 라주미힌은 눈에 띄게 들떠 있었다."]], [["“That’s capital, brother,”", "\"정말 잘됐어, 이 친구야.\""], ["he repeated several times, “and I am glad!", "그는 몇 번이고 되풀이했다. \"정말 기뻐!"], ["I am glad!”", "기쁘다고!\""]], [["“What are you glad about?”", "'뭐가 그렇게 기쁘다는 거지?'"], ["Raskolnikov thought to himself.", "라스콜니코프는 속으로 생각했다."]], [["“I didn’t know that you pledged things at the old woman’s, too.", "\"자네도 그 노파한테 물건을 저당 잡혔는지는 몰랐네."], ["And...", "그런데……"], ["was it long ago?", "오래전 일이야?"], ["I mean, was it long since you were there?”", "그러니까, 거기 갔던 게 오래됐냐고?\""]], [["“What a simple-hearted fool he is!”", "'얼마나 순진하고 어리석은 녀석인지!'"]], [["“When was it?”", "\"언제였더라?\""], ["Raskolnikov stopped still to recollect.", "라스콜니코프는 기억을 더듬으려고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["“Two or three days before her death it must have been.", "\"그 노파가 죽기 이삼 일 전이었을 거야."], ["But I am not going to redeem the things now,”", "하지만 이제 그 물건들을 되찾을 생각은 없어.\""], ["he put in with a sort of hurried and conspicuous solicitude about the things.", "그는 그 물건들에 대해 어쩐지 황급하고 유난스러운 관심을 내비치며 덧붙였다."], ["“I’ve not more than a silver rouble left...", "\"은화 일 루블밖에 안 남았거든……"], ["after last night’s accursed delirium!”", "어젯밤 그 빌어먹을 헛소리 소동 끝에 말이야!\""]], [["He laid special emphasis on the delirium.", "그는 헛소리 소동에 특히 힘주어 강조했다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["Razumihin hastened to agree--with what was not clear.", "라주미힌이 — 무엇에 대해서인지는 분명치 않으나 — 황급히 맞장구쳤다."], ["“Then that’s why you...", "\"그래서 자네가……"], ["were stuck... partly...", "그렇게 매여 있었던 거구나……"], ["you know in your delirium you were continually mentioning some rings or chains!", "어느 정도는…… 알잖아, 헛소리하면서 자네가 줄곧 무슨 반지니 사슬이니 하는 걸 입에 올렸거든!"], ["Yes, yes...", "그래, 그래……"], ["that’s clear, it’s all clear now.”", "그렇구나, 이제 다 알겠어.\""]], [["“Hullo!", "'어이쿠!"], ["How that idea must have got about among them.", "그 생각이 저들 사이에 어지간히도 퍼져 있었던 모양이군."], ["Here this man will go to the stake for me, and I find him delighted at having it _cleared up_ why I spoke of rings in my delirium!", "이 친구는 나를 위해서라면 화형대에라도 오를 사람인데, 내가 헛소리하면서 반지 얘기를 한 까닭이 밝혀졌다고 좋아하고 있다니!"], ["What a hold the idea must have on all of them!”", "그 생각이 저들 모두를 얼마나 단단히 사로잡고 있는 건지!'"]], [["“Shall we find him?”", "\"그를 만날 수 있을까?\""], ["he asked suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 그럼.\""], ["Razumihin answered quickly.", "라주미힌이 잽싸게 대답했다."], ["“He is a nice fellow, you will see, brother.", "\"그는 좋은 친구야, 만나 보면 알 거야."], ["Rather clumsy, that is to say, he is a man of polished manners, but I mean clumsy in a different sense.", "좀 서투른 데가 있지, 그러니까, 예의 바른 사람이긴 한데, 다른 의미로 서투르단 말이지."], ["He is an intelligent fellow, very much so indeed, but he has his own range of ideas....", "영리한 친구야, 정말이지 무척 영리하지, 하지만 자기만의 사고방식이 있어……."], ["He is incredulous, sceptical, cynical...", "의심이 많고, 회의적이고, 냉소적이지……."], ["he likes to impose on people, or rather to make fun of them.", "사람들을 속여 먹기를, 아니 차라리 골려 주기를 좋아한다고나 할까."], ["His is the old, circumstantial method....", "그가 쓰는 건 옛날식의, 정황을 따지는 방식이지……."], ["But he understands his work... thoroughly....", "하지만 자기 일은 알아, 철저하게……."], ["Last year he cleared up a case of murder in which the police had hardly a clue.", "작년엔 경찰이 단서조차 거의 잡지 못한 살인 사건을 해결했거든."], ["He is very, very anxious to make your acquaintance!”", "그는 자네와 알게 되기를 무척, 무척 바라고 있어!\""]], [["“On what grounds is he so anxious?”", "\"무슨 까닭으로 그렇게 바란다는 거야?\""]], [["“Oh, it’s not exactly...", "\"아, 딱히 그런 건 아니고……"], ["you see, since you’ve been ill I happen to have mentioned you several times....", "그러니까, 자네가 앓고 있는 동안 내가 어쩌다 자네 얘기를 몇 번 했거든……."], ["So, when he heard about you...", "그래서 자네 얘기를 듣고는……"], ["about your being a law student and not able to finish your studies, he said, ‘What a pity!’ And so I concluded...", "자네가 법학도인데 학업을 마치지 못하게 됐다는 걸 듣고, '저런, 안됐군!' 하더라고."], ["from everything together, not only that; yesterday Zametov...", "그래서 나는 결론을 내린 거지…… 그 모든 걸 종합해서, 그것만이 아니라 말이야."], ["you know, Rodya, I talked some nonsense on the way home to you yesterday, when I was drunk...", "어제 자메토프가…… 알잖아, 로쟈, 어제 자네 집으로 가는 길에 내가 술에 취해서 헛소리를 좀 했는데……"], ["I am afraid, brother, of your exaggerating it, you see.”", "이 친구야, 자네가 그걸 부풀려 생각할까 봐 걱정이야, 알겠어?\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭘?"], ["That they think I am a madman?", "저들이 나를 미친놈으로 여긴다고?"], ["Maybe they are right,”", "어쩌면 저들이 옳은지도 모르지.\""], ["he said with a constrained smile.", "그는 어색한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes....", "\"그래, 그래……."], ["That is, pooh, no!...", "그러니까, 에이, 아냐!……"], ["But all that I said (and there was something else too) it was all nonsense, drunken nonsense.”", "하지만 내가 말한 그 모든 게 (다른 것도 좀 있었지만) 다 헛소리였어, 술 취해서 한 헛소리였다고.\""]], [["“But why are you apologising?", "\"그런데 왜 사과를 하는 거야?"], ["I am so sick of it all!”", "난 그 모든 게 진절머리가 난다고!\""], ["Raskolnikov cried with exaggerated irritability.", "라스콜니코프가 과장되게 짜증을 내며 외쳤다."], ["It was partly assumed, however.", "하지만 그것은 어느 정도 꾸며 낸 것이었다."]], [["“I know, I know, I understand.", "\"알아, 알아, 이해해."], ["Believe me, I understand.", "정말이지, 이해한다고."], ["One’s ashamed to speak of it.”", "그런 얘기를 입에 올리는 것도 부끄럽지.\""]], [["“If you are ashamed, then don’t speak of it.”", "\"부끄러우면 입에 올리지 마.\""]], [["Both were silent.", "둘 다 잠잠해졌다."], ["Razumihin was more than ecstatic and Raskolnikov perceived it with repulsion.", "라주미힌은 그저 황홀해할 따름이었고, 라스콜니코프는 그것을 혐오감과 함께 알아챘다."], ["He was alarmed, too, by what Razumihin had just said about Porfiry.", "그는 또한 라주미힌이 방금 포르피리에 대해 한 말에 불안을 느꼈다."]], [["“I shall have to pull a long face with him too,”", "'그자 앞에서도 시치미를 떼야겠군.'"], ["he thought, with a beating heart, and he turned white, “and do it naturally, too.", "그는 가슴이 두근거리는 채로 생각했고, 얼굴이 하얘졌다. '그것도 자연스럽게 말이야."], ["But the most natural thing would be to do nothing at all.", "하지만 가장 자연스러운 건 아무것도 하지 않는 거지."], ["Carefully do nothing at all!", "조심스럽게 아무것도 하지 않는 것!"], ["No, _carefully_ would not be natural again....", "아니, 조심스럽게 굴면 또 자연스럽지 않을 거야……."], ["Oh, well, we shall see how it turns out....", "에이, 뭐, 어떻게 되는지 두고 보자고……."], ["We shall see...", "두고 봐야지……"], ["directly.", "곧."], ["Is it a good thing to go or not?", "가는 게 좋을까, 아닐까?"], ["The butterfly flies to the light.", "나방은 불빛으로 날아드는 법이지."], ["My heart is beating, that’s what’s bad!”", "가슴이 쿵쾅거려, 그게 문제야!'"]], [["“In this grey house,”", "\"이 회색 건물이야.\""], ["said Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 말했다."]], [["“The most important thing, does Porfiry know that I was at the old hag’s flat yesterday...", "'무엇보다 중요한 건, 어제 내가 그 늙은이의 집에 갔던 것을……"], ["and asked about the blood?", "그리고 핏자국에 대해 물었던 것을 포르피리가 알고 있느냐는 거야."], ["I must find that out instantly, as soon as I go in, find out from his face; otherwise...", "그건 당장, 들어가자마자 알아내야 해, 그의 얼굴에서 알아내야지. 그러지 않으면……"], ["I’ll find out, if it’s my ruin.”", "그게 내 파멸이 될지라도 기어이 알아내고 말겠어.'"]], [["“I say, brother,”", "\"이봐, 친구.\""], ["he said suddenly, addressing Razumihin, with a sly smile, “I have been noticing all day that you seem to be curiously excited.", "그는 교활한 미소를 띠며 갑자기 라주미힌에게 말을 건넸다. \"하루 종일 보니까 자네가 어쩐지 묘하게 들떠 있는 것 같던데."], ["Isn’t it so?”", "안 그래?\""]], [["“Excited?", "\"들떠 있다고?"], ["Not a bit of it,”", "천만에.\""], ["said Razumihin, stung to the quick.", "라주미힌이 정곡을 찔린 듯 발끈하며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, brother, I assure you it’s noticeable.", "\"아냐, 친구, 분명히 말하는데 티가 나."], ["Why, you sat on your chair in a way you never do sit, on the edge somehow, and you seemed to be writhing all the time.", "글쎄, 자네가 평소엔 절대 안 그러던데, 의자에 어쩐지 가장자리에 걸터앉아서, 내내 몸을 비비 꼬더라고."], ["You kept jumping up for nothing.", "까닭도 없이 자꾸 벌떡벌떡 일어나고."], ["One moment you were angry, and the next your face looked like a sweetmeat.", "어느 순간엔 화가 나 있다가, 다음 순간엔 얼굴이 사탕처럼 달콤해지고."], ["You even blushed; especially when you were invited to dinner, you blushed awfully.”", "얼굴까지 붉히던걸. 특히 저녁 식사에 초대받았을 땐, 어찌나 새빨개지던지.\""]], [["“Nothing of the sort, nonsense!", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아, 말도 안 돼!"], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 소릴 하는 거야?\""]], [["“But why are you wriggling out of it, like a schoolboy?", "\"그런데 왜 학생처럼 발뺌을 하는 거지?"], ["By Jove, there he’s blushing again.”", "이런, 또 얼굴이 빨개지네.\""]], [["“What a pig you are!”", "\"이런 망할 자식 같으니!\""]], [["“But why are you so shamefaced about it?", "\"그런데 왜 그렇게 부끄러워하는 거야? 로미오 같으니!"], ["Romeo! Stay, I’ll tell of you to-day.", "가만있어 봐, 오늘 다 일러바칠 테니까."], ["Ha-ha-ha!", "하하하!"], ["I’ll make mother laugh, and someone else, too...”", "어머니를 웃겨 드려야지, 또 다른 누군가도 말이야…….\""]], [["“Listen, listen, listen, this is serious....", "\"잘 들어, 들어, 들어, 이건 진지한 일이야……."], ["What next, you fiend!”", "이 악마 같은 자식, 그담엔 또 뭘 어쩔 셈이야!\""], ["Razumihin was utterly overwhelmed, turning cold with horror.", "라주미힌은 완전히 압도되어 공포에 질려 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["“What will you tell them?", "\"뭘 일러바치겠다는 거야?"], ["Come, brother...", "이봐, 친구……"], ["foo!", "아이고!"], ["what a pig you are!”", "이런 망할 자식 같으니!\""]], [["“You are like a summer rose.", "\"자넨 여름 장미 같다니까."], ["And if only you knew how it suits you; a Romeo over six foot high!", "그게 자네한테 얼마나 잘 어울리는지 알기나 해? 키가 육 척이 넘는 로미오라니!"], ["And how you’ve washed to-day--you cleaned your nails, I declare.", "게다가 오늘 어찌나 말끔히 씻었는지 — 손톱까지 다듬었더군, 정말이지."], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["That’s something unheard of!", "그건 듣도 보도 못한 일이야!"], ["Why, I do believe you’ve got pomatum on your hair!", "어럽쇼, 머리에 포마드까지 발랐구먼!"], ["Bend down.” “Pig!”", "좀 숙여 봐!\""]], [["Raskolnikov laughed as though he could not restrain himself.", "\"이 돼지 같은 자식!\" 라스콜니코프는 도저히 못 참겠다는 듯 웃어 댔다."], ["So laughing, they entered Porfiry Petrovitch’s flat.", "그렇게 웃으며 그들은 포르피리 페트로비치의 집으로 들어섰다."], ["This is what Raskolnikov wanted: from within they could be heard laughing as they came in, still guffawing in the passage.", "이것이야말로 라스콜니코프가 바라던 바였다. 안에서는 그들이 들어오면서 웃는 소리가 들렸고, 통로에서도 여전히 껄껄대고 있었다."]], [["“Not a word here or I’ll... brain you!”", "\"여기선 한마디도 하지 마, 안 그러면 내가……"], ["Razumihin whispered furiously, seizing Raskolnikov by the shoulder.", "네 머리통을 박살 낼 거야!\" 라주미힌이 라스콜니코프의 어깨를 움켜쥐며 격하게 속삭였다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["Raskolnikov was already entering the room.", "라스콜니코프는 이미 방으로 들어서고 있었다."], ["He came in looking as though he had the utmost difficulty not to burst out laughing again.", "그는 또다시 폭소가 터지는 것을 가까스로 참고 있는 듯한 얼굴로 들어왔다."], ["Behind him Razumihin strode in gawky and awkward, shamefaced and red as a peony, with an utterly crestfallen and ferocious expression.", "그 뒤를 따라 라주미힌이 껑충하고 어색하게, 부끄러움으로 작약처럼 시뻘게진 얼굴에 완전히 풀이 죽으면서도 사납기 짝이 없는 표정을 한 채 성큼성큼 들어왔다."], ["His face and whole figure really were ridiculous at that moment and amply justified Raskolnikov’s laughter.", "그 순간 그의 얼굴과 온몸은 정말이지 우스꽝스러웠고, 라스콜니코프의 웃음을 충분히 정당화해 주었다."], ["Raskolnikov, not waiting for an introduction, bowed to Porfiry Petrovitch, who stood in the middle of the room looking inquiringly at them.", "라스콜니코프는 누가 소개해 주기를 기다리지도 않고, 방 한가운데 서서 캐묻듯 그들을 바라보고 있던 포르피리 페트로비치에게 인사를 했다."], ["He held out his hand and shook hands, still apparently making desperate efforts to subdue his mirth and utter a few words to introduce himself.", "그는 손을 내밀어 악수를 했는데, 여전히 자신의 즐거움을 억누르고 자기소개를 위해 몇 마디라도 내뱉으려 필사적으로 애쓰는 듯했다."], ["But he had no sooner succeeded in assuming a serious air and muttering something when he suddenly glanced again as though accidentally at Razumihin, and could no longer control himself: his stifled laughter broke out the more irresistibly the more he tried to restrain it.", "그러나 가까스로 진지한 태도를 꾸며 내고 무언가를 중얼거리는 데 성공하자마자, 그는 갑자기 우연인 듯 다시 라주미힌을 흘끗 보았고, 더는 자신을 통제할 수 없었다. 억눌렀던 웃음이 참으려 애쓰면 애쓸수록 더욱 걷잡을 수 없이 터져 나왔다."], ["The extraordinary ferocity with which Razumihin received this “spontaneous”", "라주미힌이 이 '저절로 터진'"], ["mirth gave the whole scene the appearance of most genuine fun and naturalness.", "즐거움을 받아들이는 그 유별난 사나움이 이 장면 전체에 더없이 진실하고 자연스러운 즐거움의 분위기를 부여했다."], ["Razumihin strengthened this impression as though on purpose.", "라주미힌은 마치 일부러 그러는 듯 이 인상을 더욱 강화시켰다."]], [["“Fool! You fiend,”", "\"이 바보 같으니!"], ["he roared, waving his arm which at once struck a little round table with an empty tea-glass on it.", "이 악마야.\" 그가 으르렁거리며 팔을 휘둘렀는데, 그 팔이 곧장 빈 찻잔이 놓인 작고 둥근 탁자를 후려쳤다."], ["Everything was sent flying and crashing.", "모든 것이 와장창 날아가 떨어졌다."]], [["“But why break chairs, gentlemen?", "\"하지만 의자는 왜 부수는 거요, 신사 양반?"], ["You know it’s a loss to the Crown,”", "그건 국고의 손실인 줄 아실 텐데.\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch quoted gaily.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 유쾌하게 인용했다."]], [["Raskolnikov was still laughing, with his hand in Porfiry Petrovitch’s, but anxious not to overdo it, awaited the right moment to put a natural end to it.", "라스콜니코프는 여전히 포르피리 페트로비치의 손을 잡은 채 웃고 있었지만, 지나치지 않으려 신경을 곤두세우며 자연스럽게 마무리할 적절한 순간을 기다렸다."], ["Razumihin, completely put to confusion by upsetting the table and smashing the glass, gazed gloomily at the fragments, cursed and turned sharply to the window where he stood looking out with his back to the company with a fiercely scowling countenance, seeing nothing.", "탁자를 뒤엎고 잔을 깨뜨려 완전히 당황한 라주미힌은 침울하게 깨진 조각들을 바라보다가 욕을 내뱉고는, 홱 창가로 돌아서더니 사람들에게 등을 돌리고 사납게 눈살을 찌푸린 얼굴로, 아무것도 보지 못한 채 밖을 내다보며 서 있었다."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch laughed and was ready to go on laughing, but obviously looked for explanations.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 웃었고 계속 웃을 태세였지만, 분명 설명을 바라는 눈치였다."], ["Zametov had been sitting in the corner, but he rose at the visitors’ entrance and was standing in expectation with a smile on his lips, though he looked with surprise and even it seemed incredulity at the whole scene and at Raskolnikov with a certain embarrassment.", "자메토프는 구석에 앉아 있다가 방문객들이 들어오자 일어나, 입가에 미소를 띤 채 기다리며 서 있었는데, 다만 이 장면 전체와 라스콜니코프를 놀라움, 아니 심지어 믿지 못하겠다는 듯 어떤 당혹감을 띠고 바라보았다."], ["Zametov’s unexpected presence struck Raskolnikov unpleasantly.", "자메토프의 뜻밖의 출현이 라스콜니코프에게 불쾌한 충격을 주었다."]], [["“I’ve got to think of that,”", "'이건 생각해 둬야겠어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“Excuse me, please,”", "\"실례합니다, 부디.\""], ["he began, affecting extreme embarrassment.", "그가 극도의 당혹감을 가장하며 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Raskolnikov.”", "\"라스콜니코프입니다.\""]], [["“Not at all, very pleasant to see you...", "\"별말씀을, 만나 뵙게 되어 무척 반갑습니다……"], ["and how pleasantly you’ve come in....", "그리고 어찌나 유쾌하게 들어오셨던지……."], ["Why, won’t he even say good-morning?”", "아니, 이 친구는 아침 인사도 안 할 셈인가?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch nodded at Razumihin.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 라주미힌을 향해 고갯짓했다."]], [["“Upon my honour I don’t know why he is in such a rage with me.", "\"맹세코 이 친구가 왜 나한테 저렇게 화가 났는지 모르겠습니다."], ["I only told him as we came along that he was like Romeo...", "오는 길에 그냥 로미오 같다고 말했을 뿐인데…… 그러고는 그걸 증명해 보였지요."], ["and proved it. And that was all, I think!”", "그게 다였던 것 같은데요!\""]], [["“Pig!”", "\"돼지 같은 놈!\""], ["ejaculated Razumihin, without turning round.", "라주미힌이 돌아보지도 않고 내뱉었다."]], [["“There must have been very grave grounds for it, if he is so furious at the word,”", "\"그 말 한마디에 저렇게 펄펄 뛰는 걸 보면, 분명 아주 심각한 근거가 있었던 모양이군.\""], ["Porfiry laughed.", "포르피리가 웃었다."]], [["“Oh, you sharp lawyer!... Damn you all!”", "\"오, 이 약삭빠른 법률가 양반!……"], ["snapped Razumihin, and suddenly bursting out laughing himself, he went up to Porfiry with a more cheerful face as though nothing had happened.", "다들 빌어먹을!\" 라주미힌이 쏘아붙이더니, 갑자기 자기도 웃음을 터뜨리며 아무 일도 없었던 듯 한결 밝아진 얼굴로 포르피리에게 다가갔다."], ["“That’ll do!", "\"이제 그만!"], ["We are all fools.", "우린 다 바보들이지."], ["To come to business.", "본론으로 들어가자고."], ["This is my friend Rodion Romanovitch Raskolnikov; in the first place he has heard of you and wants to make your acquaintance, and secondly, he has a little matter of business with you.", "이 친구는 내 친구 로디온 로마노비치 라스콜니코프인데, 첫째로 자네 얘기를 듣고 알고 지내고 싶어 했고, 둘째로 자네한테 처리할 자그마한 용무가 있다네. 어라!"], ["Bah! Zametov, what brought you here?", "자메토프, 자네가 여긴 어쩐 일이야?"], ["Have you met before?", "전에 만난 적이 있나?"], ["Have you known each other long?”", "안 지 오래됐어?\""]], [["“What does this mean?”", "'이게 무슨 뜻이지?'"], ["thought Raskolnikov uneasily.", "라스콜니코프는 불안하게 생각했다."]], [["Zametov seemed taken aback, but not very much so.", "자메토프는 좀 당황한 듯했으나, 그리 심하지는 않았다."]], [["“Why, it was at your rooms we met yesterday,”", "\"아니, 어제 자네 방에서 만났잖아.\""], ["he said easily.", "그가 태연하게 말했다."]], [["“Then I have been spared the trouble.", "\"그럼 내가 수고를 덜었군."], ["All last week he was begging me to introduce him to you.", "지난주 내내 자네한테 소개해 달라고 졸라 대더니."], ["Porfiry and you have sniffed each other out without me.", "포르피리하고 자넨 나 없이도 서로 냄새를 맡았구먼."], ["Where is your tobacco?”", "담배 어디 있어?\""]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch was wearing a dressing-gown, very clean linen, and trodden-down slippers.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 실내복 차림에 아주 깨끗한 속옷을 받쳐 입고, 뒤축이 닳은 슬리퍼를 신고 있었다."], ["He was a man of about five and thirty, short, stout even to corpulence, and clean shaven.", "그는 서른다섯쯤 된 사내로, 키가 작고 비만이라 할 만큼 뚱뚱했으며, 수염은 말끔히 깎여 있었다."], ["He wore his hair cut short and had a large round head, particularly prominent at the back.", "머리는 짧게 깎았고, 둥근 큰 머리통은 특히 뒤통수가 두드러졌다."], ["His soft, round, rather snub-nosed face was of a sickly yellowish colour, but had a vigorous and rather ironical expression.", "부드럽고 둥글며 다소 들창코인 그의 얼굴은 병약한 누런빛을 띠었지만, 활기차고 다소 빈정대는 표정을 하고 있었다."], ["It would have been good-natured except for a look in the eyes, which shone with a watery, mawkish light under almost white, blinking eyelashes.", "거의 흰빛에 가깝게 깜박이는 속눈썹 아래 물기 어린 감상적인 빛으로 반짝이는 그 눈빛만 아니었다면, 호인의 얼굴이라 할 만했다."], ["The expression of those eyes was strangely out of keeping with his somewhat womanish figure, and gave it something far more serious than could be guessed at first sight.", "그 눈의 표정은 다소 여자 같은 그의 몸매와 묘하게 어울리지 않았고, 첫눈에 짐작할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 진지한 무언가를 그에게 부여하고 있었다."]], [["As soon as Porfiry Petrovitch heard that his visitor had a little matter of business with him, he begged him to sit down on the sofa and sat down himself on the other end, waiting for him to explain his business, with that careful and over-serious attention which is at once oppressive and embarrassing, especially to a stranger, and especially if what you are discussing is in your opinion of far too little importance for such exceptional solemnity.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 방문객에게 자그마한 용무가 있다는 말을 듣자마자 그에게 소파에 앉기를 청하고는 자신은 그 반대편 끝에 앉아, 용무를 설명하기를 기다리는데, 그 신중하고 지나치게 진지한 주의력이란, 특히 낯선 사람에게는, 더욱이 논의하려는 일이 그토록 유별난 엄숙함을 받기에는 너무 사소하다고 여겨지는 경우에는, 그 자체로 짓누르는 듯하면서도 당혹스러운 것이었다."], ["But in brief and coherent phrases Raskolnikov explained his business clearly and exactly, and was so well satisfied with himself that he even succeeded in taking a good look at Porfiry.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 짧고 조리 있는 말로 자신의 용무를 명료하고 정확하게 설명했고, 어찌나 만족스러웠는지 심지어 포르피리를 잘 살펴보는 데까지 성공했다."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch did not once take his eyes off him.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 단 한 순간도 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["Razumihin, sitting opposite at the same table, listened warmly and impatiently, looking from one to the other every moment with rather excessive interest.", "같은 탁자 맞은편에 앉은 라주미힌은 열렬하면서도 조바심을 내며 귀를 기울였고, 매 순간 다소 과도한 관심을 띠고 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라보았다."]], [["“Fool,”", "'바보 같으니.'"], ["Raskolnikov swore to himself.", "라스콜니코프는 속으로 욕했다."]], [["“You have to give information to the police,”", "\"경찰에 신고하셔야 합니다.\""], ["Porfiry replied, with a most businesslike air, “that having learnt of this incident, that is of the murder, you beg to inform the lawyer in charge of the case that such and such things belong to you, and that you desire to redeem them...", "포르피리가 더없이 사무적인 태도로 대답했다. \"이 사건, 그러니까 살인 사건을 알게 되어, 이러이러한 물건들이 자기 것이니 그것들을 되찾고 싶다고 담당 수사관에게 알리고자 한다고 말이지요……"], ["or...", "또는……"], ["but they will write to you.”", "어쨌든 그쪽에서 통지를 보내올 겁니다.\""]], [["“That’s just the point, that at the present moment,”", "\"문제는 바로 그겁니다, 지금 당장은.\""], ["Raskolnikov tried his utmost to feign embarrassment, “I am not quite in funds...", "라스콜니코프는 있는 힘껏 당혹감을 꾸며 내려 애썼다. \"제가 형편이 그리 넉넉지 않아서……"], ["and even this trifling sum is beyond me...", "이런 하찮은 액수조차 제게는 벅찹니다……."], ["I only wanted, you see, for the present to declare that the things are mine, and that when I have money....”", "그저, 보시다시피, 지금으로서는 그 물건들이 제 것이라는 것만 신고해 두고, 돈이 생기면 그때…….\""]], [["“That’s no matter,” answered Porfiry Petrovitch, receiving his explanation of his pecuniary position coldly, “but you can, if you prefer, write straight to me, to say, that having been informed of the matter, and claiming such and such as your property, you beg...”", "\"그건 문제 될 것 없습니다.\" 포르피리 페트로비치가 그의 금전 사정에 관한 설명을 냉담하게 받아넘기며 대답했다. \"하지만 원하신다면, 곧장 저한테 글로 써 주셔도 됩니다. 이 사건을 알게 되어 이러이러한 것이 자기 소유임을 주장하며, 청컨대…… 라고 말이지요.\""]], [["“On an ordinary sheet of paper?”", "\"보통 종이에다요?\""], ["Raskolnikov interrupted eagerly, again interested in the financial side of the question.", "라스콜니코프가 다시 이 문제의 재정적인 측면에 관심을 보이며 다급하게 끼어들었다."]], [["“Oh, the most ordinary,”", "\"오, 아주 평범한 종이면 됩니다.\""], ["and suddenly Porfiry Petrovitch looked with obvious irony at him, screwing up his eyes and, as it were, winking at him.", "그러더니 갑자기 포르피리 페트로비치가 노골적으로 빈정대는 눈빛으로 그를 바라보며, 눈을 가늘게 뜨고는 마치 그에게 윙크를 하는 듯했다."], ["But perhaps it was Raskolnikov’s fancy, for it all lasted but a moment.", "하지만 어쩌면 라스콜니코프의 착각이었는지도 모르니, 모든 것이 한순간에 지나갔던 것이다."], ["There was certainly something of the sort, Raskolnikov could have sworn he winked at him, goodness knows why.", "분명 그 비슷한 무언가가 있긴 했고, 라스콜니코프는 그가 자기에게 윙크를 했다고 맹세라도 할 수 있었지만, 도대체 왜 그랬는지는 알 수 없었다."]], [["“He knows,”", "'저자가 알고 있군.'"], ["flashed through his mind like lightning.", "그의 머릿속을 번개처럼 스쳤다."]], [["“Forgive my troubling you about such trifles,”", "\"이런 하찮은 일로 번거롭게 해 드려 죄송합니다.\""], ["he went on, a little disconcerted, “the things are only worth five roubles, but I prize them particularly for the sake of those from whom they came to me, and I must confess that I was alarmed when I heard...”", "그가 조금 당황한 채 말을 이었다. \"그 물건들은 고작 오 루블어치밖에 안 되지만, 제게 그것들을 물려준 분들을 생각해서 각별히 아끼는 거라서요, 솔직히 말하면 소식을 듣고 깜짝 놀랐지요…….\""]], [["“That’s why you were so much struck when I mentioned to Zossimov that Porfiry was inquiring for everyone who had pledges!”", "\"그래서 자네가 그렇게 충격을 받았던 거로군, 내가 조시모프한테 포르피리가 저당물 잡힌 사람들을 죄다 조사하고 있다고 말했을 때 말이야!\""], ["Razumihin put in with obvious intention.", "라주미힌이 노골적인 의도를 품고 끼어들었다."]], [["This was really unbearable.", "이건 정말 참을 수 없는 노릇이었다."], ["Raskolnikov could not help glancing at him with a flash of vindictive anger in his black eyes, but immediately recollected himself.", "라스콜니코프는 자기도 모르게 그를 흘끗 보았고 검은 눈에 복수심 어린 분노가 번뜩였으나, 곧바로 정신을 차렸다."]], [["“You seem to be jeering at me, brother?”", "\"자네 날 비웃는 모양이지, 친구?\""], ["he said to him, with a well-feigned irritability.", "그가 제법 그럴듯하게 짜증을 가장하며 그에게 말했다."], ["“I dare say I do seem to you absurdly anxious about such trash; but you mustn’t think me selfish or grasping for that, and these two things may be anything but trash in my eyes.", "\"이런 하찮은 것에 우습도록 안달하는 것처럼 자네 눈엔 보이겠지. 하지만 그렇다고 날 이기적이라거나 욕심 사납다고 여기진 말게, 내 눈에는 이 두 물건이 결코 하찮은 게 아닐 수도 있으니까."], ["I told you just now that the silver watch, though it’s not worth a cent, is the only thing left us of my father’s.", "방금도 말했지만, 그 은시계는 한 푼어치 값도 안 나가지만 아버지가 우리한테 남긴 유일한 유품이야."], ["You may laugh at me, but my mother is here,”", "날 비웃어도 좋지만, 어머니가 여기 와 계신데,\""], ["he turned suddenly to Porfiry, “and if she knew,”", "그는 갑자기 포르피리 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"만일 어머니가 아신다면,\""], ["he turned again hurriedly to Razumihin, carefully making his voice tremble, “that the watch was lost, she would be in despair!", "그는 다시 황급히 라주미힌 쪽으로 돌아서며, 애써 목소리를 떨리게 했다. \"그 시계를 잃어버렸다는 걸 아신다면, 절망하실 거라고!"], ["You know what women are!”", "여자들이 어떤지 자네도 알잖아!\""]], [["“Not a bit of it!", "\"천만에!"], ["I didn’t mean that at all!", "난 절대 그런 뜻이 아니었어!"], ["Quite the contrary!”", "정반대라고!\""], ["shouted Razumihin distressed.", "라주미힌이 괴로워하며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Was it right?", "'제대로 한 건가?"], ["Was it natural?", "자연스러웠나?"], ["Did I overdo it?”", "너무 과했나?'"], ["Raskolnikov asked himself in a tremor.", "라스콜니코프는 떨리는 마음으로 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“Why did I say that about women?”", "'왜 여자들 얘기 같은 걸 했지?'"]], [["“Oh, your mother is with you?”", "\"오, 어머님이 같이 와 계신가요?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch inquired.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“When did she come?”", "\"언제 오셨습니까?\""]], [["“Last night.”", "\"어젯밤에요.\""]], [["Porfiry paused as though reflecting.", "포르피리는 무언가 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 잠시 멈추었다."]], [["“Your things would not in any case be lost,”", "\"어쨌든 당신 물건들은 잃어버리지 않을 겁니다.\""], ["he went on calmly and coldly.", "그가 차분하고 냉담하게 말을 이었다."], ["“I have been expecting you here for some time.”", "\"한동안 당신이 여기 오기를 기다리고 있었거든요.\""]], [["And as though that was a matter of no importance, he carefully offered the ash-tray to Razumihin, who was ruthlessly scattering cigarette ash over the carpet.", "그러고는 그것이 별일 아니라는 듯, 그는 조심스럽게 라주미힌에게 재떨이를 내밀었는데, 라주미힌은 사정없이 양탄자 위에 담뱃재를 흩뿌리고 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov shuddered, but Porfiry did not seem to be looking at him, and was still concerned with Razumihin’s cigarette.", "라스콜니코프는 흠칫했지만, 포르피리는 그를 보고 있지 않은 듯했고, 여전히 라주미힌의 담배에만 신경을 쓰고 있었다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["Expecting him?", "이 친구를 기다렸다고?"], ["Why, did you know that he had pledges _there_?”", "아니, 이 친구가 거기에 저당물을 잡혔다는 걸 알고 있었단 말이야?\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch addressed himself to Raskolnikov.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 라스콜니코프에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Your things, the ring and the watch, were wrapped up together, and on the paper your name was legibly written in pencil, together with the date on which you left them with her...”", "\"당신 물건들, 반지하고 시계가 함께 싸여 있었는데, 그 종이에는 당신 이름이 연필로 또박또박 적혀 있었고, 그것들을 그 여자한테 맡긴 날짜도 함께 적혀 있더군요…….\""]], [["“How observant you are!”", "\"정말 관찰력이 대단하시군요!\""], ["Raskolnikov smiled awkwardly, doing his very utmost to look him straight in the face, but he failed, and suddenly added:", "라스콜니코프는 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보려 안간힘을 쓰며 어색하게 미소 지었으나, 실패하고는 갑자기 덧붙였다."]], [["“I say that because I suppose there were a great many pledges...", "\"제가 이런 말씀을 드리는 건, 저당물이 아주 많았을 테니까……"], ["that it must be difficult to remember them all....", "그걸 다 기억하기는 어려울 거란 생각이 들어서요……."], ["But you remember them all so clearly, and...", "그런데 그걸 다 그렇게 또렷이 기억하시고, 또……"], ["and...”", "또…….\""]], [["“Stupid!", "'멍청하긴!"], ["Feeble!”", "허약해!'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“Why did I add that?”", "'왜 저런 말을 덧붙인 거지?'"]], [["“But we know all who had pledges, and you are the only one who hasn’t come forward,”", "\"하지만 우리는 저당물을 잡힌 사람들을 다 알고 있고, 나서지 않은 사람은 당신 하나뿐입니다.\""], ["Porfiry answered with hardly perceptible irony.", "포르피리가 거의 알아챌 수 없는 빈정거림을 담아 대답했다."]], [["“I haven’t been quite well.”", "\"제가 몸이 좀 안 좋았어요.\""]], [["“I heard that too.", "\"그것도 들었습니다."], ["I heard, indeed, that you were in great distress about something.", "사실, 무언가로 몹시 괴로워하고 있다는 얘기까지 들었지요."], ["You look pale still.”", "아직도 안색이 창백하시군요.\""]], [["“I am not pale at all....", "\"전혀 창백하지 않은데요……."], ["No, I am quite well,”", "아니, 아주 멀쩡합니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov snapped out rudely and angrily, completely changing his tone.", "라스콜니코프가 어조를 완전히 바꾸어 무례하고 성난 목소리로 쏘아붙였다."], ["His anger was mounting, he could not repress it.", "분노가 치밀어 올랐고, 그는 그것을 억누를 수 없었다."], ["“And in my anger I shall betray myself,”", "'이러다 화를 못 참고 정체를 드러내겠어.'"], ["flashed through his mind again.", "또다시 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["“Why are they torturing me?”", "'저들은 왜 날 고문하는 거지?'"]], [["“Not quite well!”", "\"몸이 좀 안 좋다니!\""], ["Razumihin caught him up.", "라주미힌이 그의 말을 받았다."], ["“What next!", "\"그게 무슨 소리야!"], ["He was unconscious and delirious all yesterday.", "이 친구는 어제 종일 정신을 잃고 헛소리를 해 댔다고."], ["Would you believe, Porfiry, as soon as our backs were turned, he dressed, though he could hardly stand, and gave us the slip and went off on a spree somewhere till midnight, delirious all the time!", "믿어지나, 포르피리, 우리가 등을 돌리기가 무섭게, 제대로 서지도 못하면서 옷을 챙겨 입고 우릴 따돌리고는 어디론가 빠져나가 자정까지 진탕 돌아다녔다고, 내내 정신이 오락가락하면서 말이야! 믿어져?"], ["Would you believe it! Extraordinary!”", "기가 막힐 노릇이지!\""]], [["“Really delirious? You don’t say so!”", "\"정말로 정신이 오락가락했다고?"], ["Porfiry shook his head in a womanish way.", "설마!\" 포르피리가 여자처럼 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"헛소리!"], ["Don’t you believe it!", "그런 거 믿지 마쇼!"], ["But you don’t believe it anyway,”", "어차피 당신은 안 믿겠지만.\""], ["Raskolnikov let slip in his anger.", "라스콜니코프가 분노에 차서 그만 입 밖에 내고 말았다."], ["But Porfiry Petrovitch did not seem to catch those strange words.", "그러나 포르피리 페트로비치는 그 묘한 말을 알아채지 못한 듯했다."]], [["“But how could you have gone out if you hadn’t been delirious?”", "\"하지만 정신이 멀쩡했다면 어떻게 나갈 수 있었겠어?\""], ["Razumihin got hot suddenly.", "라주미힌이 갑자기 발끈했다."], ["“What did you go out for?", "\"뭐 하러 나간 거야?"], ["What was the object of it?", "무슨 목적으로?"], ["And why on the sly?", "그것도 왜 몰래?"], ["Were you in your senses when you did it?", "그럴 때 자네 제정신이긴 했어?"], ["Now that all danger is over I can speak plainly.”", "이제 위험이 다 지나갔으니 솔직히 말할 수 있는데.\""]], [["“I was awfully sick of them yesterday.”", "\"어제 그자들한테 진절머리가 나서 말이지요.\" 라스콜니코프가 갑자기 뻔뻔스러운 도전의 미소를 띠며 포르피리에게 말을 건넸다."], ["Raskolnikov addressed Porfiry suddenly with a smile of insolent defiance, “I ran away from them to take lodgings where they wouldn’t find me, and took a lot of money with me. Mr.", "\"그자들을 피해 도망쳐서 날 못 찾을 곳에 셋방을 얻으려고, 돈도 잔뜩 챙겨서 나갔지요."], ["Zametov there saw it. I say, Mr.", "저기 자메토프 씨가 봤어요."], ["Zametov, was I sensible or delirious yesterday; settle our dispute.”", "어이, 자메토프 씨, 내가 어제 제정신이었소, 아니면 헛소리를 했소? 우리 논쟁을 좀 정리해 주시오.\""]], [["He could have strangled Zametov at that moment, so hateful were his expression and his silence to him.", "그 순간 그는 자메토프를 목 졸라 죽이고 싶었으니, 그의 표정과 침묵이 그토록 가증스러웠던 것이다."]], [["“In my opinion you talked sensibly and even artfully, but you were extremely irritable,”", "\"제 생각에는 당신이 조리 있게, 심지어 교묘하게 말했지만, 극도로 예민한 상태였습니다.\""], ["Zametov pronounced dryly.", "자메토프가 무뚝뚝하게 단언했다."]], [["“And Nikodim Fomitch was telling me to-day,”", "\"그리고 니코딤 포미치가 오늘 제게 말하기를,\""], ["put in Porfiry Petrovitch, “that he met you very late last night in the lodging of a man who had been run over.”", "포르피리 페트로비치가 끼어들었다. \"어젯밤 아주 늦은 시각에 마차에 치인 사람의 셋방에서 당신을 만났다더군요.\""]], [["“And there,”", "\"그리고 거기서,\""], ["said Razumihin, “weren’t you mad then?", "라주미힌이 말했다. \"자넨 그때도 미친 게 아니었나?"], ["You gave your last penny to the widow for the funeral.", "장례 비용에 쓰라고 과부한테 마지막 한 푼까지 다 줘 버렸잖아."], ["If you wanted to help, give fifteen or twenty even, but keep three roubles for yourself at least, but he flung away all the twenty-five at once!”", "돕고 싶었으면 십오 루블이나 이십 루블이라도 주되, 적어도 자기 몫으로 삼 루블쯤은 남겨 둬야지, 그걸 이십오 루블을 통째로 단번에 내던지다니!\""]], [["“Maybe I found a treasure somewhere and you know nothing of it?", "\"어쩌면 어디서 보물이라도 찾았는데 자네들이 모르는 것일 수도 있잖아?"], ["So that’s why I was liberal yesterday....", "그래서 어제 내가 그렇게 통이 컸던 거지……."], ["Mr. Zametov knows I’ve found a treasure!", "자메토프 씨는 내가 보물을 찾았다는 걸 알아!"], ["Excuse us, please, for disturbing you for half an hour with such trivialities,”", "이런 시시한 일로 삼십 분이나 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다.\""], ["he said, turning to Porfiry Petrovitch, with trembling lips.", "그가 입술을 떨며 포르피리 페트로비치를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“We are boring you, aren’t we?”", "\"지루하게 해 드리고 있지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“Oh no, quite the contrary, quite the contrary!", "\"오 천만에요, 정반대입니다, 정반대예요!"], ["If only you knew how you interest me!", "당신이 제게 얼마나 흥미로운지 아신다면!"], ["It’s interesting to look on and listen...", "이렇게 보고 듣는 게 흥미롭습니다……."], ["and I am really glad you have come forward at last.”", "그리고 마침내 이렇게 나서 주셔서 정말 기쁘고요.\""]], [["“But you might give us some tea!", "\"하지만 차라도 좀 내주지 그래!"], ["My throat’s dry,”", "목이 타는군.\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."]], [["“Capital idea!", "\"좋은 생각이오!"], ["Perhaps we will all keep you company.", "어쩌면 우리 모두 함께하게 될지도 모르겠군요."], ["Wouldn’t you like...", "차 마시기 전에……"], ["something more essential before tea?”", "좀 더 든든한 걸 드시지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“Get along with you!”", "\"그러지 좀 말라고!\""]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch went out to order tea.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 차를 시키러 나갔다."]], [["Raskolnikov’s thoughts were in a whirl.", "라스콜니코프의 머릿속은 어지럽게 소용돌이쳤다."], ["He was in terrible exasperation.", "그는 끔찍한 격분 상태에 빠져 있었다."]], [["“The worst of it is they don’t disguise it; they don’t care to stand on ceremony!", "'무엇보다 최악인 건, 저자들이 그걸 숨기지도 않는다는 거야. 격식 차릴 생각조차 없어!"], ["And how if you didn’t know me at all, did you come to talk to Nikodim Fomitch about me?", "그리고 만일 나를 전혀 모른다면, 어떻게 니코딤 포미치한테 나에 관해 얘기하게 됐단 말인가?"], ["So they don’t care to hide that they are tracking me like a pack of dogs.", "그러니 한 무리의 개떼처럼 나를 추적하고 있다는 걸 숨길 생각도 없는 거지."], ["They simply spit in my face.”", "그냥 내 얼굴에 침을 뱉는 거야.'"], ["He was shaking with rage.", "그는 분노로 부들부들 떨었다."], ["“Come, strike me openly, don’t play with me like a cat with a mouse.", "'자, 차라리 대놓고 날 쳐라, 고양이가 쥐를 가지고 놀듯 날 가지고 놀지 말고."], ["It’s hardly civil, Porfiry Petrovitch, but perhaps I won’t allow it!", "이건 도무지 예의가 아니오, 포르피리 페트로비치, 하지만 어쩌면 내가 가만두지 않겠어!"], ["I shall get up and throw the whole truth in your ugly faces, and you’ll see how I despise you.”", "벌떡 일어나 너희 그 추한 낯짝에 진실을 통째로 내던지면, 너희는 내가 너희를 얼마나 경멸하는지 알게 되겠지.'"], ["He could hardly breathe.", "그는 숨조차 제대로 쉬기 어려웠다."], ["“And what if it’s only my fancy?", "'그런데 이게 그저 내 착각이라면?"], ["What if I am mistaken, and through inexperience I get angry and don’t keep up my nasty part?", "내가 잘못 짚은 거고, 경험이 없어서 화를 내며 내 못된 역할을 지켜 내지 못하는 거라면?"], ["Perhaps it’s all unintentional.", "어쩌면 다 의도한 게 아닐지도 몰라."], ["All their phrases are the usual ones, but there is something about them....", "저들의 말은 죄다 흔해 빠진 것들이지만, 거기엔 무언가가 있어……."], ["It all might be said, but there is something.", "그 무엇이든 할 수 있는 말이긴 한데, 그래도 무언가가 있단 말이야."], ["Why did he say bluntly, ‘With her’?", "왜 저자는 노골적으로 \"그 여자한테\"라고 했지?"], ["Why did Zametov add that I spoke artfully?", "왜 자메토프는 내가 교묘하게 말했다고 덧붙인 거고?"], ["Why do they speak in that tone?", "왜 저런 어조로 말하는 거지?"], ["Yes, the tone....", "그래, 그 어조 말이야……."], ["Razumihin is sitting here, why does he see nothing?", "라주미힌이 여기 앉아 있는데, 왜 아무것도 못 보는 거지?"], ["That innocent blockhead never does see anything!", "저 순진한 멍청이는 도무지 아무것도 못 보지!"], ["Feverish again!", "또 열이 오르는군!"], ["Did Porfiry wink at me just now?", "방금 포르피리가 나한테 윙크를 했나?"], ["Of course it’s nonsense!", "물론 헛소리야!"], ["What could he wink for?", "윙크할 이유가 뭐가 있겠어?"], ["Are they trying to upset my nerves or are they teasing me?", "저들이 내 신경을 흔들려는 건가, 아니면 날 놀리는 건가?"], ["Either it’s ill fancy or they know!", "이건 병적인 착각이거나, 아니면 저들이 알고 있거나 둘 중 하나야!"], ["Even Zametov is rude....", "자메토프조차 무례하잖아……."], ["Is Zametov rude?", "자메토프가 무례한가?"], ["Zametov has changed his mind.", "자메토프는 마음을 바꿨어."], ["I foresaw he would change his mind!", "저자가 마음을 바꾸리라는 걸 난 예감했지!"], ["He is at home here, while it’s my first visit.", "저자는 여기서 제 집처럼 굴고, 나는 처음 온 거고."], ["Porfiry does not consider him a visitor; sits with his back to him.", "포르피리는 저자를 방문객으로 여기지 않아, 등을 돌리고 앉아 있잖아."], ["They’re as thick as thieves, no doubt, over me!", "의심할 여지 없이 저들은 나를 두고 한통속이 된 거야!"], ["Not a doubt they were talking about me before we came.", "우리가 오기 전에 나에 관해 얘기하고 있었던 게 틀림없어."], ["Do they know about the flat?", "셋방 일을 알고 있나?"], ["If only they’d make haste!", "좀 서둘러 주면 좋으련만!"], ["When I said that I ran away to take a flat he let it pass....", "내가 셋방을 얻으러 도망쳤다고 했을 때 저자는 그냥 넘겼어……."], ["I put that in cleverly about a flat, it may be of use afterwards....", "셋방 얘기는 영리하게 끼워 넣었지, 나중에 쓸모가 있을지도 몰라……."], ["Delirious, indeed...", "정신이 오락가락했다니, 참……."], ["ha-ha-ha!", "하하하!"], ["He knows all about last night!", "저자는 어젯밤 일을 죄다 알고 있어!"], ["He didn’t know of my mother’s arrival!", "어머니가 오신 건 몰랐고!"], ["The hag had written the date on in pencil!", "그 노파가 날짜를 연필로 적어 뒀던 거지!"], ["You are wrong, you won’t catch me!", "틀렸어, 너희는 날 잡지 못해!"], ["There are no facts...", "증거가 없잖아……"], ["it’s all supposition!", "죄다 추측일 뿐이야!"], ["You produce facts!", "증거를 내놔 봐!"], ["The flat even isn’t a fact but delirium.", "셋방조차 증거가 아니라 헛소리지."], ["I know what to say to them....", "저들한테 뭐라고 말해야 할지 난 알아……."], ["Do they know about the flat?", "셋방 일을 알고 있을까?"], ["I won’t go without finding out.", "알아내지 못하면 가지 않겠어."], ["What did I come for?", "그런데 난 뭐 하러 온 거지?"], ["But my being angry now, maybe is a fact!", "하지만 내가 지금 화내는 것, 어쩌면 그게 증거일지도 몰라!"], ["Fool, how irritable I am!", "바보 같으니, 어찌 이리 신경질적인지!"], ["Perhaps that’s right; to play the invalid....", "어쩌면 이게 맞는 건지도 모르지. 환자 행세를 하는 것……."], ["He is feeling me.", "저자는 나를 떠보고 있어."], ["He will try to catch me.", "날 잡으려 들겠지."], ["Why did I come?”", "난 뭐 하러 온 거지?'"]], [["All this flashed like lightning through his mind.", "이 모든 것이 그의 머릿속을 번개처럼 스쳤다."]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch returned quickly.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 금세 돌아왔다."], ["He became suddenly more jovial.", "그는 갑자기 한결 쾌활해졌다."]], [["“Your party yesterday, brother, has left my head rather....", "\"어제 자네 모임 탓에 머리가 좀……"], ["And I am out of sorts altogether,”", "그리고 어쩐지 영 컨디션이 말이 아니야.\""], ["he began in quite a different tone, laughing to Razumihin.", "그가 라주미힌에게 웃어 보이며 사뭇 다른 어조로 입을 열었다."]], [["“Was it interesting?", "\"재미있었나?"], ["I left you yesterday at the most interesting point.", "어제 가장 흥미로운 대목에서 자넬 두고 나왔는데."], ["Who got the best of it?”", "누가 이겼어?\""]], [["“Oh, no one, of course.", "\"오, 물론 아무도 못 이겼지."], ["They got on to everlasting questions, floated off into space.”", "영원한 문제들로 빠져들더니, 허공으로 둥둥 떠가 버렸어.\""]], [["“Only fancy, Rodya, what we got on to yesterday.", "\"한번 상상해 봐, 로쟈, 우리가 어제 무슨 얘기까지 갔는지."], ["Whether there is such a thing as crime.", "과연 범죄라는 게 존재하느냐 마느냐 하는 거였어."], ["I told you that we talked our heads off.”", "정신없이 떠들어 댔다고 했잖아.\""]], [["“What is there strange?", "\"이상할 게 뭐 있어?"], ["It’s an everyday social question,”", "흔해 빠진 사회 문제잖아.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered casually.", "라스콜니코프가 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["“The question wasn’t put quite like that,”", "\"문제가 꼭 그런 식으로 제기된 건 아니었습니다.\""], ["observed Porfiry.", "포르피리가 지적했다."]], [["“Not quite, that’s true,”", "\"꼭 그런 건 아니었지, 그건 맞아.\""], ["Razumihin agreed at once, getting warm and hurried as usual.", "라주미힌이 평소처럼 열을 올리고 다급해하며 곧바로 동의했다."], ["“Listen, Rodion, and tell us your opinion, I want to hear it.", "\"들어 봐, 로디온, 자네 의견을 말해 봐, 듣고 싶어."], ["I was fighting tooth and nail with them and wanted you to help me.", "난 저들과 죽기 살기로 싸웠고 자네가 좀 거들어 주길 바랐어."], ["I told them you were coming....", "자네가 올 거라고 말해 뒀지……."], ["It began with the socialist doctrine.", "사회주의 학설에서 시작된 거야."], ["You know their doctrine; crime is a protest against the abnormality of the social organisation and nothing more, and nothing more; no other causes admitted!...”", "자네도 그들의 학설을 알잖아. 범죄란 사회 조직의 비정상성에 대한 항거일 뿐, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아니고, 다른 어떤 원인도 인정하지 않는다는 거지!……\""]], [["“You are wrong there,”", "\"거기서 자네가 틀렸어.\""], ["cried Porfiry Petrovitch; he was noticeably animated and kept laughing as he looked at Razumihin, which made him more excited than ever.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 외쳤다. 그는 눈에 띄게 활기를 띠며 라주미힌을 바라보면서 연신 웃어 댔고, 그 바람에 라주미힌은 그 어느 때보다 흥분했다."]], [["“Nothing is admitted,”", "\"아무것도 인정하지 않는다고.\""], ["Razumihin interrupted with heat.", "라주미힌이 발끈하며 말을 끊었다."]], [["“I am not wrong.", "\"난 틀리지 않았어."], ["I’ll show you their pamphlets.", "그들의 소책자를 보여 주지."], ["Everything with them is ‘the influence of environment,’ and nothing else.", "그들에겐 모든 게 '환경의 영향'이고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 없어."], ["Their favourite phrase!", "그들이 즐겨 쓰는 표현이지!"], ["From which it follows that, if society is normally organised, all crime will cease at once, since there will be nothing to protest against and all men will become righteous in one instant.", "그러니 사회가 정상적으로 조직되기만 하면 모든 범죄가 즉각 사라진다는 거야. 항거할 대상이 없어지고 모든 인간이 한순간에 의롭게 될 테니까."], ["Human nature is not taken into account, it is excluded, it’s not supposed to exist!", "인간 본성은 고려되지도 않아, 배제되어 버려, 아예 존재하지도 않는 것처럼 여겨진다고!"], ["They don’t recognise that humanity, developing by a historical living process, will become at last a normal society, but they believe that a social system that has come out of some mathematical brain is going to organise all humanity at once and make it just and sinless in an instant, quicker than any living process!", "인류가 역사적이고 생동하는 과정을 통해 발전하여 마침내 정상적인 사회가 되리라는 건 인정하지 않으면서, 어떤 수학적 두뇌에서 튀어나온 사회 체계가 한순간에 온 인류를 조직하여, 그 어떤 생동하는 과정보다도 빠르게, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 인류를 의롭고 죄 없게 만들어 주리라고 믿는단 말이야!"], ["That’s why they instinctively dislike history, ‘nothing but ugliness and stupidity in it,’ and they explain it all as stupidity!", "그래서 그들은 본능적으로 역사를 싫어하지, '거기엔 추악함과 어리석음밖에 없다'면서 모든 걸 어리석음으로 설명해 버려!"], ["That’s why they so dislike the _living_ process of life; they don’t want a _living soul_!", "그래서 그들은 삶의 그 생동하는 과정을 그토록 싫어하는 거야. 살아 있는 영혼을 원치 않는 거지!"], ["The living soul demands life, the soul won’t obey the rules of mechanics, the soul is an object of suspicion, the soul is retrograde!", "살아 있는 영혼은 삶을 요구하고, 영혼은 기계의 법칙에 복종하려 들지 않고, 영혼은 의심의 대상이며, 영혼은 반동이거든!"], ["But what they want though it smells of death and can be made of India-rubber, at least is not alive, has no will, is servile and won’t revolt!", "하지만 그들이 원하는 건, 비록 죽음의 냄새가 나고 고무로 만들 수 있는 것일망정, 적어도 살아 있지 않고, 의지도 없고, 비굴하며, 반항하지 않는 것이지!"], ["And it comes in the end to their reducing everything to the building of walls and the planning of rooms and passages in a phalanstery!", "그러다 결국 모든 걸 팔랑스테르의 벽을 쌓고 방과 복도를 설계하는 일로 환원시켜 버리는 거야!"], ["The phalanstery is ready, indeed, but your human nature is not ready for the phalanstery--it wants life, it hasn’t completed its vital process, it’s too soon for the graveyard!", "팔랑스테르야 정말 다 준비됐겠지만, 자네의 그 인간 본성은 팔랑스테르를 받아들일 준비가 안 됐단 말이야. 본성은 삶을 원하고, 아직 제 생동하는 과정을 완성하지 못했고, 무덤으로 가기엔 너무 일러!"], ["You can’t skip over nature by logic.", "논리로 본성을 건너뛸 수는 없어."], ["Logic presupposes three possibilities, but there are millions!", "논리는 세 가지 가능성을 전제하지만, 실제로는 수백만 가지가 있다고!"], ["Cut away a million, and reduce it all to the question of comfort!", "그 수백만 가지를 잘라 내고, 모든 걸 안락의 문제로 환원시켜 버리는 거지!"], ["That’s the easiest solution of the problem!", "그게 문제의 가장 손쉬운 해결책이니까!"], ["It’s seductively clear and you musn’t think about it.", "유혹적일 만큼 명쾌해서, 굳이 생각할 필요도 없어."], ["That’s the great thing, you mustn’t think!", "바로 그게 핵심이지, 생각해선 안 된다는 거!"], ["The whole secret of life in two pages of print!”", "삶의 모든 비밀이 인쇄된 두 페이지에 다 들어 있다니까!\""]], [["“Now he is off, beating the drum!", "\"이제 또 시작이군, 북을 두드려 대네!"], ["Catch hold of him, do!”", "저자 좀 붙잡아 주게, 어서!\""], ["laughed Porfiry.", "포르피리가 웃었다."], ["“Can you imagine,”", "\"한번 상상해 보세요.\""], ["he turned to Raskolnikov, “six people holding forth like that last night, in one room, with punch as a preliminary!", "그가 라스콜니코프 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"어젯밤 여섯 사람이 한 방에서, 우선 펀치 술부터 걸치고는 저런 식으로 떠들어 댔다니까요!"], ["No, brother, you are wrong, environment accounts for a great deal in crime; I can assure you of that.”", "아니, 친구, 자네가 틀렸어, 환경은 범죄에서 큰 몫을 차지해, 그건 내가 장담하지.\""]], [["“Oh, I know it does, but just tell me: a man of forty violates a child of ten; was it environment drove him to it?”", "\"오, 그거야 나도 알지, 하지만 한 가지만 말해 봐. 마흔 살 먹은 사내가 열 살짜리 아이를 욕보였다면, 그건 환경이 그자를 그렇게 몰아간 건가?\""]], [["“Well, strictly speaking, it did,”", "\"글쎄, 엄밀히 말하면, 그랬다고 봐야지.\""], ["Porfiry observed with noteworthy gravity; “a crime of that nature may be very well ascribed to the influence of environment.”", "포르피리가 주목할 만큼 진지하게 말했다. \"그런 종류의 범죄도 능히 환경의 영향으로 돌릴 수 있어.\""]], [["Razumihin was almost in a frenzy.", "라주미힌은 거의 미칠 지경이었다."], ["“Oh, if you like,” he roared.", "\"오, 그렇게 나온다면,\""], ["“I’ll prove to you that your white eyelashes may very well be ascribed to the Church of Ivan the Great’s being two hundred and fifty feet high, and I will prove it clearly, exactly, progressively, and even with a Liberal tendency!", "그가 으르렁거렸다. \"자네 그 흰 속눈썹도 이반 대제의 종탑 높이가 이백오십 피트인 탓으로 능히 돌릴 수 있다는 걸 증명해 보이지, 그것도 명료하게, 정확하게, 단계적으로, 심지어 진보적 경향까지 곁들여서 말이야!"], ["I undertake to!", "내가 해 보이겠어!"], ["Will you bet on it?”", "내기할 텐가?\""]], [["“Done!", "\"좋아!"], ["Let’s hear, please, how he will prove it!”", "자, 어디 저 친구가 어떻게 증명하는지 들어 봅시다!\""]], [["“He is always humbugging, confound him,”", "\"저 작자는 늘 사람을 놀려 먹는다니까, 빌어먹을.\""], ["cried Razumihin, jumping up and gesticulating.", "라주미힌이 벌떡 일어나 손짓 발짓을 해 대며 외쳤다."], ["“What’s the use of talking to you?", "\"자네하고 얘기해 봐야 무슨 소용이야?"], ["He does all that on purpose; you don’t know him, Rodion!", "죄다 일부러 그러는 거라고. 자넨 저자를 몰라, 로디온!"], ["He took their side yesterday, simply to make fools of them.", "어제 저자는 순전히 그들을 바보로 만들려고 그들 편을 들었어."], ["And the things he said yesterday!", "어제 했던 소리들 좀 보라지!"], ["And they were delighted!", "그런데도 그들은 좋아 어쩔 줄 모르더라니까!"], ["He can keep it up for a fortnight together.", "저자는 그 짓을 보름이고 내리 계속할 수 있어."], ["Last year he persuaded us that he was going into a monastery: he stuck to it for two months.", "작년엔 자기가 수도원에 들어갈 거라고 우리를 믿게 만들더니, 두 달이나 그 소리를 고집했지."], ["Not long ago he took it into his head to declare he was going to get married, that he had everything ready for the wedding.", "얼마 전엔 또 결혼을 하겠다며, 혼례 준비를 죄다 끝냈다고 떠들어 댔다고. 정말로 새 옷까지 맞췄지."], ["He ordered new clothes indeed.", "우리 모두 축하해 주기 시작했어."], ["We all began to congratulate him. There was no bride, nothing, all pure fantasy!”", "그런데 신부도 없고, 아무것도 없고, 죄다 순전히 환상이었던 거야!\""]], [["“Ah, you are wrong!", "\"아, 그건 자네가 틀렸어!"], ["I got the clothes before.", "옷은 진작에 마련했었다고."], ["It was the new clothes in fact that made me think of taking you in.”", "사실은 그 새 옷이 자네들을 골탕 먹일 생각을 하게 만든 거였지.\""]], [["“Are you such a good dissembler?”", "\"당신은 그렇게 능숙한 거짓말쟁이입니까?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked carelessly.", "라스콜니코프가 무심하게 물었다."]], [["“You wouldn’t have supposed it, eh?", "\"설마 그러리라고는 생각 못 하셨겠죠, 응?"], ["Wait a bit, I shall take you in, too.", "조금만 기다려 보세요, 당신도 골탕 먹여 드릴 테니."], ["Ha-ha-ha!", "하하하!"], ["No, I’ll tell you the truth.", "아니, 사실대로 말씀드리지요."], ["All these questions about crime, environment, children, recall to my mind an article of yours which interested me at the time.", "범죄니 환경이니 아이들이니 하는 이 모든 문제를 듣자니, 당시 제 흥미를 끌었던 당신의 어떤 논문이 떠오르는군요."], ["‘On Crime’...", "'범죄에 관하여'……"], ["or something of the sort, I forget the title, I read it with pleasure two months ago in the _Periodical Review_.”", "뭐 그런 제목이었는데, 제목은 잊어버렸습니다, 두 달 전에 《주기 평론》에서 아주 즐겁게 읽었지요.\""]], [["“My article?", "\"제 논문이요?"], ["In the _Periodical Review_?”", "《주기 평론》에서요?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked in astonishment.", "라스콜니코프가 놀라서 물었다."], ["“I certainly did write an article upon a book six months ago when I left the university, but I sent it to the _Weekly Review_.”", "\"확실히 반년 전 대학을 그만둘 무렵에 어떤 책에 관한 논문을 쓰긴 했지만, 그건 《주간 평론》에 보냈는데요.\""]], [["“But it came out in the _Periodical_.”", "\"하지만 그건 《주기 평론》에 실렸습니다.\""]], [["“And the _Weekly Review_ ceased to exist, so that’s why it wasn’t printed at the time.”", "\"그리고 《주간 평론》이 폐간되는 바람에, 그래서 그땐 인쇄되지 못한 거고요.\""]], [["“That’s true; but when it ceased to exist, the _Weekly Review_ was amalgamated with the _Periodical_, and so your article appeared two months ago in the latter.", "\"맞습니다. 하지만 《주간 평론》이 폐간될 때 《주기 평론》과 합병되었고, 그래서 당신 논문이 두 달 전에 후자에 실린 겁니다."], ["Didn’t you know?”", "모르셨나요?\""]], [["Raskolnikov had not known.", "라스콜니코프는 모르고 있었다."]], [["“Why, you might get some money out of them for the article!", "\"아니, 그 논문으로 그쪽에서 돈을 좀 받아 낼 수 있을 텐데!"], ["What a strange person you are!", "당신도 참 별난 분이군요!"], ["You lead such a solitary life that you know nothing of matters that concern you directly.", "어찌나 고립된 생활을 하시는지 자기와 직접 관련된 일조차 까맣게 모르시다니."], ["It’s a fact, I assure you.”", "정말입니다, 장담하지요.\""]], [["“Bravo, Rodya!", "\"브라보, 로쟈!"], ["I knew nothing about it either!”", "나도 그 얘긴 전혀 몰랐다고!\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."], ["“I’ll run to-day to the reading-room and ask for the number.", "\"오늘 당장 열람실로 달려가서 그 호를 찾아봐야겠어."], ["Two months ago?", "두 달 전이라고?"], ["What was the date?", "날짜가 언제였지?"], ["It doesn’t matter though, I will find it.", "뭐, 상관없어, 내가 찾아낼 테니."], ["Think of not telling us!”", "우리한텐 말도 안 하고 말이야!\""]], [["“How did you find out that the article was mine?", "\"제 논문인 줄 어떻게 아셨습니까?"], ["It’s only signed with an initial.”", "머리글자로만 서명되어 있는데요.\""]], [["“I only learnt it by chance, the other day.", "\"그저 우연히 알게 됐지요, 며칠 전에."], ["Through the editor; I know him....", "편집자를 통해서요, 그자를 알거든요……."], ["I was very much interested.”", "아주 흥미로웠습니다.\""]], [["“I analysed, if I remember, the psychology of a criminal before and after the crime.”", "\"제 기억으로는, 범죄를 저지르기 전과 후의 범죄자의 심리를 분석한 거였습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, and you maintained that the perpetration of a crime is always accompanied by illness.", "\"그렇지요, 그리고 당신은 범죄의 실행에는 언제나 병이 동반된다고 주장했지요."], ["Very, very original, but...", "아주, 아주 독창적이었습니다, 하지만……"], ["it was not that part of your article that interested me so much, but an idea at the end of the article which I regret to say you merely suggested without working it out clearly.", "제 흥미를 그토록 끈 건 논문의 그 부분이 아니라, 끝부분에 나온 어떤 발상이었는데, 유감스럽게도 당신은 그걸 명확히 전개하지 않고 그저 암시만 해 두셨더군요."], ["There is, if you recollect, a suggestion that there are certain persons who can...", "기억하실지 모르겠지만, 어떤 부류의 사람들은……"], ["that is, not precisely are able to, but have a perfect right to commit breaches of morality and crimes, and that the law is not for them.”", "그러니까, 정확히 말해 그럴 능력이 있다는 게 아니라, 도덕을 위반하고 범죄를 저지를 완전한 권리를 가지고 있으며, 법이 그들에게는 해당되지 않는다는 암시 말입니다.\""]], [["Raskolnikov smiled at the exaggerated and intentional distortion of his idea.", "라스콜니코프는 자기 발상을 과장하고 의도적으로 왜곡한 데 미소를 지었다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["What do you mean?", "그게 무슨 소리야?"], ["A right to crime?", "범죄를 저지를 권리라고?"], ["But not because of the influence of environment?”", "그것도 환경의 영향 때문이 아니라?\""], ["Razumihin inquired with some alarm even.", "라주미힌이 심지어 다소 놀라기까지 하며 물었다."]], [["“No, not exactly because of it,”", "\"아니, 정확히 그것 때문은 아니지.\" 포르피리가 대답했다."], ["answered Porfiry.", "\"그의 논문에서는 모든 인간이 '평범한' 자와 '비범한'"], ["“In his article all men are divided into ‘ordinary’ and ‘extraordinary.’ Ordinary men have to live in submission, have no right to transgress the law, because, don’t you see, they are ordinary.", "자로 나뉘어. 평범한 자들은 복종하며 살아야 하고 법을 위반할 권리가 없는데, 알다시피, 그들이 평범하기 때문이지."], ["But extraordinary men have a right to commit any crime and to transgress the law in any way, just because they are extraordinary.", "하지만 비범한 자들은 그 어떤 범죄든 저지르고 그 어떤 식으로든 법을 위반할 권리가 있어, 바로 그들이 비범하기 때문에 말이야."], ["That was your idea, if I am not mistaken?”", "그게 당신 발상이었지요, 제가 틀리지 않았다면?\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 소리야?"], ["That can’t be right?”", "그건 옳을 리가 없잖아?\""], ["Razumihin muttered in bewilderment.", "라주미힌이 어리둥절해하며 중얼거렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov smiled again.", "라스콜니코프는 다시 미소를 지었다."], ["He saw the point at once, and knew where they wanted to drive him.", "그는 단번에 핵심을 알아챘고, 그들이 자기를 어디로 몰아가려는지 간파했다."], ["He decided to take up the challenge.", "그는 그 도전을 받아들이기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“That wasn’t quite my contention,”", "\"제가 주장한 바는 꼭 그런 건 아니었습니다.\""], ["he began simply and modestly.", "그가 간단하고 겸손하게 입을 열었다."], ["“Yet I admit that you have stated it almost correctly; perhaps, if you like, perfectly so.”", "\"하지만 거의 정확하게 진술하셨다는 건 인정하지요. 원하신다면, 어쩌면 완벽하게 정확하다고도 하겠습니다.\""], ["(It almost gave him pleasure to admit this.) “The only difference is that I don’t contend that extraordinary people are always bound to commit breaches of morals, as you call it.", "(이를 인정하는 것이 그에게는 거의 쾌감을 주었다.) \"다만 차이가 있다면, 저는 당신이 말씀하시는 그 도덕의 위반을 비범한 자들이 언제나 반드시 저질러야 한다고 주장하지는 않는다는 점입니다."], ["In fact, I doubt whether such an argument could be published.", "사실 그런 논지가 발표될 수 있을지도 의문이고요."], ["I simply hinted that an ‘extraordinary’ man has the right...", "저는 그저 '비범한' 인간에게는 권리가 있다고……"], ["that is not an official right, but an inner right to decide in his own conscience to overstep...", "그러니까 공적인 권리가 아니라, 어떤 장애물들을 뛰어넘을지 자기 양심에 따라 스스로 결정할 내적인 권리가 있다고 암시했을 뿐입니다."], ["certain obstacles, and only in case it is essential for the practical fulfilment of his idea (sometimes, perhaps, of benefit to the whole of humanity).", "그것도 오로지 자기 사상의 실천적 실현을 위해 (때로는 어쩌면 온 인류에게 이로울 사상을 위해) 그것이 필수적인 경우에 한해서 말이지요."], ["You say that my article isn’t definite; I am ready to make it as clear as I can.", "제 논문이 명확하지 않다고 하셨는데, 제가 할 수 있는 한 명확히 해 드리지요."], ["Perhaps I am right in thinking you want me to; very well.", "어쩌면 당신이 그걸 바라신다고 짐작하는 게 옳을 듯하니, 좋습니다."], ["I maintain that if the discoveries of Kepler and Newton could not have been made known except by sacrificing the lives of one, a dozen, a hundred, or more men, Newton would have had the right, would indeed have been in duty-bound...", "만일 케플러나 뉴턴의 발견이 한 사람, 열 사람, 백 사람, 혹은 그 이상의 목숨을 희생시키지 않고서는 세상에 알려질 수 없었다면, 뉴턴에게는 그 발견을 온 인류에게 알리기 위해 그 열 사람이나 백 사람을 제거할 권리가 있었을 것이고, 실은 그렇게 해야 할 의무까지 있었을 거라고 저는 주장합니다."], ["to _eliminate_ the dozen or the hundred men for the sake of making his discoveries known to the whole of humanity.", "하지만 그렇다고 해서 뉴턴에게 닥치는 대로 사람을 죽이거나 날마다 시장에서 도둑질할 권리가 있다는 말은 아닙니다. 그리고 제 기억에, 저는 논문에서 모든……"], ["But it does not follow from that that Newton had a right to murder people right and left and to steal every day in the market. Then, I remember, I maintain in my article that all...", "그러니까 입법자와 인류의 지도자들, 이를테면 리쿠르고스, 솔론, 마호메트, 나폴레옹 같은 자들은 예외 없이 죄다 범죄자였다고 주장합니다."], ["well, legislators and leaders of men, such as Lycurgus, Solon, Mahomet, Napoleon, and so on, were all without exception criminals, from the very fact that, making a new law, they transgressed the ancient one, handed down from their ancestors and held sacred by the people, and they did not stop short at bloodshed either, if that bloodshed--often of innocent persons fighting bravely in defence of ancient law--were of use to their cause.", "새로운 법을 만들면서 조상으로부터 물려받아 백성이 신성시하던 옛 법을 위반했다는 바로 그 사실 때문에요. 그리고 그들은 피 흘리는 일 앞에서도 멈추지 않았어요, 그 유혈이—흔히 옛 법을 지키려 용감히 싸우던 무고한 사람들의 피가—자기 대의에 도움이 된다면 말입니다."], ["It’s remarkable, in fact, that the majority, indeed, of these benefactors and leaders of humanity were guilty of terrible carnage.", "사실 주목할 만한 건, 이 인류의 은인이자 지도자라는 자들의 대다수가 끔찍한 살육의 죄를 지었다는 점입니다."], ["In short, I maintain that all great men or even men a little out of the common, that is to say capable of giving some new word, must from their very nature be criminals--more or less, of course.", "요컨대 저는 모든 위인은, 아니 조금이라도 비범한 인간은, 다시 말해 무언가 새로운 말을 할 능력이 있는 인간은, 그 본성상 범죄자일 수밖에 없다고—물론 정도의 차이는 있지만—주장하는 겁니다."], ["Otherwise it’s hard for them to get out of the common rut; and to remain in the common rut is what they can’t submit to, from their very nature again, and to my mind they ought not, indeed, to submit to it.", "그렇지 않으면 그들이 평범한 틀에서 벗어나기란 어려우니까요. 그리고 평범한 틀 안에 머무는 것, 그건 그들이 다시 또 그 본성상 도무지 받아들일 수 없는 노릇이고, 제 생각엔 사실 받아들여서도 안 됩니다."], ["You see that there is nothing particularly new in all that.", "보시다시피 이 모든 데 특별히 새로운 건 없어요."], ["The same thing has been printed and read a thousand times before.", "같은 얘기가 이전에도 수천 번 인쇄되고 읽혔으니까요."], ["As for my division of people into ordinary and extraordinary, I acknowledge that it’s somewhat arbitrary, but I don’t insist upon exact numbers.", "사람을 평범한 자와 비범한 자로 나눈 것에 관해서는, 다소 자의적이라는 점은 인정하지만, 정확한 수치를 고집하지는 않습니다."], ["I only believe in my leading idea that men are _in general_ divided by a law of nature into two categories, inferior (ordinary), that is, so to say, material that serves only to reproduce its kind, and men who have the gift or the talent to utter _a new word_.", "다만 제가 믿는 건, 인간이 자연의 법칙에 의해 일반적으로 두 부류로 나뉜다는 그 기본 발상입니다. 하나는 열등한 (평범한) 부류, 그러니까 말하자면 오로지 제 종족을 재생산하는 데에만 쓰이는 재료고, 다른 하나는 새로운 말을 할 재능이나 능력을 지닌 인간들이지요."], ["There are, of course, innumerable sub-divisions, but the distinguishing features of both categories are fairly well marked.", "물론 무수한 하위 분류가 있겠지만, 두 부류의 구별되는 특징은 제법 뚜렷합니다."], ["The first category, generally speaking, are men conservative in temperament and law-abiding; they live under control and love to be controlled.", "첫 번째 부류는 일반적으로 말해 기질이 보수적이고 법을 잘 지키는 인간들로, 통제 아래 살아가며 통제받기를 좋아하지요."], ["To my thinking it is their duty to be controlled, because that’s their vocation, and there is nothing humiliating in it for them.", "제 생각엔 통제받는 게 그들의 의무인데, 그게 그들의 소명이고, 그들에게는 거기에 굴욕적인 데가 전혀 없으니까요."], ["The second category all transgress the law; they are destroyers or disposed to destruction according to their capacities.", "두 번째 부류는 모두 법을 위반합니다. 그들은 자기 역량에 따라 파괴자거나 파괴를 지향하는 자들이지요."], ["The crimes of these men are of course relative and varied; for the most part they seek in very varied ways the destruction of the present for the sake of the better.", "이들의 범죄야 물론 상대적이고 가지각색이지만, 대부분은 더 나은 것을 위해 더없이 다양한 방식으로 현재의 파괴를 추구합니다."], ["But if such a one is forced for the sake of his idea to step over a corpse or wade through blood, he can, I maintain, find within himself, in his conscience, a sanction for wading through blood--that depends on the idea and its dimensions, note that.", "하지만 만일 그런 자가 자기 사상을 위해 시체를 넘어가거나 피를 헤치고 나아가야 한다면, 그는, 단언컨대, 자기 안에서, 자기 양심 속에서, 그 피를 헤치고 나아갈 정당한 근거를 찾아낼 수 있어요—그건 사상과 그 규모에 달린 거지요, 이 점에 유의하십시오."], ["It’s only in that sense I speak of their right to crime in my article (you remember it began with the legal question).", "제가 논문에서 그들의 범죄 권리를 말한 건 오직 그런 의미에서였습니다 (기억하시겠지만 그건 법률 문제로 시작됐지요). 하지만 그렇게 불안해하실 필요는 없습니다."], ["There’s no need for such anxiety, however; the masses will scarcely ever admit this right, they punish them or hang them (more or less), and in doing so fulfil quite justly their conservative vocation.", "대중은 좀처럼 이 권리를 인정하지 않을 테니까요. 그들은 이런 자들을 처벌하거나 교수형에 처하는데 (정도의 차이는 있지만), 그렇게 함으로써 더없이 정당하게 자신들의 보수적 소명을 완수하는 거지요."], ["But the same masses set these criminals on a pedestal in the next generation and worship them (more or less).", "하지만 바로 그 대중이 다음 세대에 가서는 이 범죄자들을 받침대 위에 올려놓고 숭배하게 됩니다 (정도의 차이는 있지만)."], ["The first category is always the man of the present, the second the man of the future.", "첫 번째 부류는 언제나 현재의 인간이고, 두 번째 부류는 미래의 인간이지요."], ["The first preserve the world and people it, the second move the world and lead it to its goal.", "첫째는 세계를 보존하고 인구를 늘리며, 둘째는 세계를 움직여 그 목표로 이끌어 갑니다."], ["Each class has an equal right to exist.", "두 부류 모두 존재할 동등한 권리가 있어요."], ["In fact, all have equal rights with me--and _vive la guerre éternelle_--till the New Jerusalem, of course!”", "사실, 다들 저하고 동등한 권리를 가졌지요—그리고 영원한 전쟁이여 만세—물론, 새 예루살렘이 올 때까지 말입니다!\""]], [["“Then you believe in the New Jerusalem, do you?”", "\"그럼 당신은 새 예루살렘을 믿는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"믿습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered firmly; as he said these words and during the whole preceding tirade he kept his eyes on one spot on the carpet.", "라스콜니코프가 단호히 대답했다. 이 말을 하면서, 또 앞서 늘어놓은 장광설 내내, 그는 양탄자의 한 지점에 눈길을 고정하고 있었다."]], [["“And...", "\"그럼……"], ["and do you believe in God?", "그럼 신은 믿습니까?"], ["Excuse my curiosity.”", "호기심을 용서하십시오.\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"믿습니다.\""], ["repeated Raskolnikov, raising his eyes to Porfiry.", "라스콜니코프가 포르피리에게로 눈을 들며 거듭 말했다."]], [["“And...", "\"그럼……"], ["do you believe in Lazarus’ rising from the dead?”", "나사로가 죽은 자들 가운데서 부활한 것도 믿으시고요?\""]], [["“I...", "\"전……"], ["I do.", "믿습니다."], ["Why do you ask all this?”", "왜 이런 걸 다 물으시는 거지요?\""]], [["“You believe it literally?”", "\"문자 그대로 믿으십니까?\""]], [["“Literally.”", "\"문자 그대로요.\""]], [["“You don’t say so....", "\"설마요……."], ["I asked from curiosity.", "그저 호기심에서 물어본 겁니다."], ["Excuse me.", "용서하십시오."], ["But let us go back to the question; they are not always executed.", "한데 다시 그 문제로 돌아가 보지요. 그들이 늘 처형당하는 건 아니지요."], ["Some, on the contrary...”", "더러는 도리어…….\""]], [["“Triumph in their lifetime?", "\"살아생전에 승리한다고요?"], ["Oh, yes, some attain their ends in this life, and then...”", "오, 그렇지요, 더러는 이승에서 자기 목적을 이루고, 그러고는…….\""]], [["“They begin executing other people?”", "\"다른 사람들을 처형하기 시작하나요?\""]], [["“If it’s necessary; indeed, for the most part they do.", "\"필요하다면요. 실은 대부분 그렇게 하지요."], ["Your remark is very witty.”", "아주 재치 있는 지적이십니다.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["But tell me this: how do you distinguish those extraordinary people from the ordinary ones?", "그런데 이건 좀 말씀해 주시지요. 그 비범한 사람들을 평범한 사람들과 어떻게 구별합니까?"], ["Are there signs at their birth?", "태어날 때부터 무슨 표지라도 있나요?"], ["I feel there ought to be more exactitude, more external definition.", "제 생각엔 좀 더 정밀함이, 좀 더 외적인 규정이 있어야 할 것 같아서요."], ["Excuse the natural anxiety of a practical law-abiding citizen, but couldn’t they adopt a special uniform, for instance, couldn’t they wear something, be branded in some way?", "실무적이고 법을 잘 지키는 시민으로서 당연한 불안을 용서해 주십시오만, 가령 그들에게 특별한 제복을 입힐 수는 없을까요, 무언가를 걸치게 하거나, 어떤 식으로든 낙인을 찍을 수는 없을까요?"], ["For you know if confusion arises and a member of one category imagines that he belongs to the other, begins to ‘eliminate obstacles’ as you so happily expressed it, then...”", "아시다시피 만일 혼란이 생겨서 한쪽 부류에 속한 자가 자기를 다른 쪽이라고 착각하고, 당신이 그토록 절묘하게 표현하신 대로 '장애물을 제거'하기 시작한다면, 그땐…….\""]], [["“Oh, that very often happens!", "\"오, 그런 일은 아주 흔히 일어나지요!"], ["That remark is wittier than the other.”", "그 지적은 아까 것보다 더 재치 있군요.\""]], [["“Thank you.”", "\"고맙습니다.\""]], [["“No reason to; but take note that the mistake can only arise in the first category, that is among the ordinary people (as I perhaps unfortunately called them).", "\"별말씀을요. 다만 한 가지 유의하실 점은, 그런 착오는 오직 첫 번째 부류에서만, 그러니까 평범한 사람들 (제가 불행히도 그렇게 불렀을지 모르지만) 사이에서만 생긴다는 겁니다."], ["In spite of their predisposition to obedience very many of them, through a playfulness of nature, sometimes vouchsafed even to the cow, like to imagine themselves advanced people, ‘destroyers,’ and to push themselves into the ‘new movement,’ and this quite sincerely.", "복종하려는 성향에도 불구하고, 그들 중 상당수는, 때로는 암소한테조차 베풀어지는 본성의 장난기 탓에, 스스로를 진보한 사람, '파괴자'라 상상하고는 '새로운 운동'에 끼어들고 싶어 하는데, 그것도 아주 진심으로 말이지요. 한편 정작 진짜로 새로운 사람들은 그들 눈에 띄지 않는 경우가 허다하고, 심지어 비굴한 성향의 반동분자로 멸시당하기까지 합니다."], ["Meanwhile the really _new_ people are very often unobserved by them, or even despised as reactionaries of grovelling tendencies.", "하지만 여기에 무슨 대단한 위험이 있다고는 생각지 않으니, 정말 불안해하실 것 없습니다. 그들은 결코 멀리까지 나아가지 못하니까요."], ["But I don’t think there is any considerable danger here, and you really need not be uneasy for they never go very far.", "물론 공상에 휩쓸린 죄로, 또 제 분수를 가르쳐 주려고 이따금 매질을 당할 수도 있겠지만, 그뿐이지요."], ["Of course, they might have a thrashing sometimes for letting their fancy run away with them and to teach them their place, but no more; in fact, even this isn’t necessary as they castigate themselves, for they are very conscientious: some perform this service for one another and others chastise themselves with their own hands....", "사실은 이마저도 필요 없어요, 그들이 스스로를 징벌하니까요, 워낙 양심적이거든요. 어떤 자들은 서로 이 일을 대신해 주고, 또 어떤 자들은 제 손으로 자신을 매질하지요……."], ["They will impose various public acts of penitence upon themselves with a beautiful and edifying effect; in fact you’ve nothing to be uneasy about....", "그들은 스스로에게 갖가지 공개적인 참회 행위를 부과하는데, 그게 아름답고 교화적인 효과를 낸답니다. 요컨대 당신이 불안해하실 건 아무것도 없어요……."], ["It’s a law of nature.”", "그게 자연의 법칙이니까요.\""]], [["“Well, you have certainly set my mind more at rest on that score; but there’s another thing worries me.", "\"음, 그 점에 관해서는 확실히 제 마음이 한결 놓이는군요. 하지만 또 한 가지 마음에 걸리는 게 있습니다."], ["Tell me, please, are there many people who have the right to kill others, these extraordinary people?", "좀 말씀해 주시지요, 다른 사람을 죽일 권리를 가진 사람, 그 비범한 사람들이 많이 있습니까?"], ["I am ready to bow down to them, of course, but you must admit it’s alarming if there are a great many of them, eh?”", "물론 저야 그들에게 기꺼이 머리를 조아릴 준비가 되어 있습니다만, 그런 자들이 아주 많다면 섬뜩한 노릇이 아니겠습니까, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“Oh, you needn’t worry about that either,”", "\"오, 그것도 걱정하실 것 없습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov went on in the same tone.", "라스콜니코프가 같은 어조로 말을 이었다."], ["“People with new ideas, people with the faintest capacity for saying something _new_, are extremely few in number, extraordinarily so in fact.", "\"새로운 사상을 가진 사람, 무언가 새로운 말을 할 능력을 조금이라도 가진 사람은 그 수가 극히 적습니다, 사실 유별나게 적지요."], ["One thing only is clear, that the appearance of all these grades and sub-divisions of men must follow with unfailing regularity some law of nature.", "한 가지만은 분명한데, 인간의 이 모든 등급과 하위 분류의 출현은 어떤 자연의 법칙을 한 치의 어김도 없이 따른다는 겁니다."], ["That law, of course, is unknown at present, but I am convinced that it exists, and one day may become known.", "물론 그 법칙은 현재로선 알려져 있지 않지만, 저는 그게 존재한다고, 그리고 언젠가는 알려질 수도 있다고 확신합니다."], ["The vast mass of mankind is mere material, and only exists in order by some great effort, by some mysterious process, by means of some crossing of races and stocks, to bring into the world at last perhaps one man out of a thousand with a spark of independence.", "인류의 그 거대한 다수란 한낱 재료에 불과하며, 오직 어떤 위대한 노력을 통해, 어떤 신비로운 과정을 통해, 어떤 종족과 혈통의 교배를 통해, 마침내 독립성의 불꽃을 지닌 인간을 천에 하나쯤 세상에 내놓기 위해서만 존재하는 거지요."], ["One in ten thousand perhaps--I speak roughly, approximately--is born with some independence, and with still greater independence one in a hundred thousand.", "만에 하나쯤은—대략적으로, 어림잡아 말하는 겁니다만—어느 정도의 독립성을 지니고 태어나고, 십만에 하나쯤은 더 큰 독립성을 지니고 태어납니다."], ["The man of genius is one of millions, and the great geniuses, the crown of humanity, appear on earth perhaps one in many thousand millions.", "천재는 수백만에 하나꼴이고, 위대한 천재, 인류의 왕관이라 할 자들은 어쩌면 수십억에 하나쯤 이 땅에 나타나겠지요."], ["In fact I have not peeped into the retort in which all this takes place.", "사실 저는 이 모든 일이 벌어지는 그 시험관 속을 들여다본 적은 없습니다."], ["But there certainly is and must be a definite law, it cannot be a matter of chance.”", "하지만 분명 어떤 명확한 법칙이 있고, 또 있을 수밖에 없어요, 그게 우연의 문제일 리는 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Why, are you both joking?”", "\"아니, 자네들 지금 농담하는 거야?\""], ["Razumihin cried at last.", "라주미힌이 마침내 외쳤다."], ["“There you sit, making fun of one another.", "\"거기 앉아서 서로 놀려 먹고 있잖아."], ["Are you serious, Rodya?”", "진심이야, 로쟈?\""]], [["Raskolnikov raised his pale and almost mournful face and made no reply.", "라스콜니코프는 창백하고 거의 비통하기까지 한 얼굴을 들었으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["And the unconcealed, persistent, nervous, and _discourteous_ sarcasm of Porfiry seemed strange to Razumihin beside that quiet and mournful face.", "그리고 그 조용하고 비통한 얼굴 곁에서, 포르피리의 그 감추지 않는, 집요하고 신경질적이며 무례한 빈정거림이 라주미힌에게는 기이하게 느껴졌다."]], [["“Well, brother, if you are really serious...", "\"음, 친구, 자네가 정말 진심이라면……"], ["You are right, of course, in saying that it’s not new, that it’s like what we’ve read and heard a thousand times already; but what is really original in all this, and is exclusively your own, to my horror, is that you sanction bloodshed _in the name of conscience_, and, excuse my saying so, with such fanaticism....", "물론 자네 말이 옳아, 이게 새로울 게 없다는 것, 우리가 이미 수천 번 읽고 들은 것과 다름없다는 것 말이야. 하지만 이 모든 데서 정말로 독창적인 것, 오로지 자네만의 것은—나로서는 끔찍하게도—양심의 이름으로 유혈을 정당화한다는 점이고, 이런 말 해서 미안하지만, 그것도 그토록 광신적으로 말이야……."], ["That, I take it, is the point of your article.", "내가 보기엔 그게 자네 논문의 요점인 것 같아."], ["But that sanction of bloodshed _by conscience_ is to my mind...", "하지만 양심에 의한 그 유혈의 정당화란 내 생각엔……"], ["more terrible than the official, legal sanction of bloodshed....”", "공적이고 법적인 유혈의 정당화보다 더 끔찍한 거라고…….\""]], [["“You are quite right, it is more terrible,”", "\"맞습니다, 그게 더 끔찍하지요.\""], ["Porfiry agreed.", "포르피리가 동의했다."]], [["“Yes, you must have exaggerated!", "\"그래, 자네가 과장한 게 틀림없어!"], ["There is some mistake, I shall read it.", "무슨 착오가 있는 거야, 내가 읽어 보겠어."], ["You can’t think that!", "자네가 그런 생각을 할 리 없어!"], ["I shall read it.”", "읽어 보겠다고.\""]], [["“All that is not in the article, there’s only a hint of it,”", "\"그건 죄다 논문엔 없는 얘기야, 거기엔 그저 암시만 있을 뿐이지.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes.” Porfiry couldn’t sit still.", "\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요.\""], ["“Your attitude to crime is pretty clear to me now, but...", "포르피리는 가만히 앉아 있질 못했다."], ["excuse me for my impertinence (I am really ashamed to be worrying you like this), you see, you’ve removed my anxiety as to the two grades getting mixed, but...", "\"범죄에 대한 당신의 태도는 이제 제게 꽤 분명해졌습니다, 하지만…… 제 무례를 용서하십시오 (이렇게 당신을 괴롭히다니 정말 부끄럽군요), 보십시오, 당신은 두 등급이 뒤섞이는 데 대한 제 불안은 없애 주셨지만……"], ["there are various practical possibilities that make me uneasy!", "저를 불안하게 하는 갖가지 실제적인 가능성들이 있단 말입니다!"], ["What if some man or youth imagines that he is a Lycurgus or Mahomet--a future one of course--and suppose he begins to remove all obstacles....", "만일 어떤 사내나 젊은이가 자기를 리쿠르고스나 마호메트라고—물론 미래의 인물이라고—상상하고는, 그래서 모든 장애물을 제거하기 시작한다면 어쩌지요……."], ["He has some great enterprise before him and needs money for it...", "그자 앞에 무슨 위대한 사업이 놓여 있고 거기엔 돈이 필요한데…… 그 돈을 마련하려 든다면……"], ["and tries to get it... do you see?”", "무슨 말인지 아시겠습니까?\""]], [["Zametov gave a sudden guffaw in his corner.", "자메토프가 자기 구석에서 갑자기 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Raskolnikov did not even raise his eyes to him.", "라스콜니코프는 그에게 눈길조차 들지 않았다."]], [["“I must admit,”", "\"인정합니다.\""], ["he went on calmly, “that such cases certainly must arise.", "그가 차분하게 말을 이었다. \"그런 경우가 분명 생기긴 할 겁니다."], ["The vain and foolish are particularly apt to fall into that snare; young people especially.”", "허영심 많고 어리석은 자들이 특히 그 덫에 빠지기 쉽지요, 특히 젊은이들이요.\""]], [["“Yes, you see.", "\"그렇지요, 보십시오."], ["Well then?”", "그럼 어떻게 됩니까?\""]], [["“What then?”", "\"그럼 어떻게 되긴요?\""], ["Raskolnikov smiled in reply; “that’s not my fault.", "라스콜니코프가 대답 삼아 미소 지었다. \"그건 제 잘못이 아니지요."], ["So it is and so it always will be.", "일이 그러하고, 또 늘 그러할 테니까요."], ["He said just now (he nodded at Razumihin) that I sanction bloodshed.", "저 친구가 방금 (그가 라주미힌을 향해 고갯짓했다) 제가 유혈을 정당화한다고 했지요."], ["Society is too well protected by prisons, banishment, criminal investigators, penal servitude.", "사회는 감옥과 유형, 형사 수사관, 강제 노역으로 충분히 잘 보호되고 있습니다."], ["There’s no need to be uneasy.", "불안해하실 것 없어요."], ["You have but to catch the thief.”", "그저 도둑을 잡기만 하면 되니까요.\""]], [["“And what if we do catch him?”", "\"그래서 우리가 그자를 잡는다면요?\""]], [["“Then he gets what he deserves.”", "\"그럼 그자는 마땅한 벌을 받는 거지요.\""]], [["“You are certainly logical.", "\"확실히 논리적이시군요."], ["But what of his conscience?”", "하지만 그자의 양심은 어떻게 되지요?\""]], [["“Why do you care about that?”", "\"그게 왜 신경 쓰이십니까?\""]], [["“Simply from humanity.”", "\"그저 인간애에서요.\""]], [["“If he has a conscience he will suffer for his mistake.", "\"양심이 있는 자라면 자기 잘못 때문에 괴로워하겠지요."], ["That will be his punishment--as well as the prison.”", "그게 그자의 벌이 될 겁니다—감옥과 더불어 말이지요.\""]], [["“But the real geniuses,”", "\"하지만 진짜 천재들은,\""], ["asked Razumihin frowning, “those who have the right to murder?", "라주미힌이 눈살을 찌푸리며 물었다. \"살인할 권리를 가진 그자들은?"], ["Oughtn’t they to suffer at all even for the blood they’ve shed?”", "그자들은 자기가 흘린 피에 대해서도 전혀 괴로워하지 않아도 되는 거야?\""]], [["“Why the word _ought_?", "\"왜 '되는'이라는 말을 쓰지?"], ["It’s not a matter of permission or prohibition.", "허락이나 금지의 문제가 아니야."], ["He will suffer if he is sorry for his victim.", "자기 희생자를 가엾게 여긴다면 괴로워하겠지."], ["Pain and suffering are always inevitable for a large intelligence and a deep heart.", "고통과 괴로움이란 위대한 지성과 깊은 마음에는 언제나 피할 수 없는 거니까."], ["The really great men must, I think, have great sadness on earth,”", "진정으로 위대한 인간은, 내 생각엔, 이 땅에서 커다란 슬픔을 짊어질 수밖에 없어.\""], ["he added dreamily, not in the tone of the conversation.", "그가 대화의 어조와는 다르게, 꿈결처럼 덧붙였다."]], [["He raised his eyes, looked earnestly at them all, smiled, and took his cap.", "그는 눈을 들어 그들 모두를 진지하게 바라보고는, 미소 짓더니 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["He was too quiet by comparison with his manner at his entrance, and he felt this.", "그는 들어올 때의 태도에 비해 너무 조용해져 있었고, 스스로도 그것을 느꼈다."], ["Everyone got up.", "모두가 일어섰다."]], [["“Well, you may abuse me, be angry with me if you like,”", "\"음, 절 욕하셔도 좋고, 원하신다면 화를 내셔도 좋습니다.\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch began again, “but I can’t resist.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 다시 입을 열었다. \"하지만 도무지 참을 수가 없군요."], ["Allow me one little question (I know I am troubling you).", "자그마한 질문 하나만 허락해 주십시오 (당신을 번거롭게 하는 줄 압니다)."], ["There is just one little notion I want to express, simply that I may not forget it.”", "그저 잊어버리지 않으려고, 표현해 두고 싶은 자그마한 생각이 하나 있어서요.\""]], [["“Very good, tell me your little notion,”", "\"좋습니다, 그 자그마한 생각이란 걸 말씀하시지요.\""], ["Raskolnikov stood waiting, pale and grave before him.", "라스콜니코프가 창백하고 근엄한 얼굴로 그 앞에 서서 기다렸다."]], [["“Well, you see...", "\"음, 보십시오……"], ["I really don’t know how to express it properly....", "제대로 어떻게 표현해야 할지 정말 모르겠군요……."], ["It’s a playful, psychological idea....", "장난스러운, 심리적인 발상입니다……."], ["When you were writing your article, surely you couldn’t have helped, he-he!", "당신이 그 논문을 쓰실 때, 분명 어쩔 수 없이, 헤헤!"], ["fancying yourself...", "스스로를 그려 보지 않았을까요……"], ["just a little, an ‘extraordinary’ man, uttering a _new word_ in your sense....", "아주 조금이나마, 당신이 말한 그 의미에서 새로운 말을 내뱉는 '비범한' 인간이라고 말이지요……."], ["That’s so, isn’t it?”", "그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Quite possibly,”", "\"충분히 그럴 수 있지요.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered contemptuously.", "라스콜니코프가 경멸하듯 대답했다."]], [["Razumihin made a movement.", "라주미힌이 움찔했다."]], [["“And, if so, could you bring yourself in case of worldly difficulties and hardship or for some service to humanity--to overstep obstacles?...", "\"그렇다면, 세상살이의 곤란과 고난을 만났을 때, 혹은 인류에 대한 어떤 봉사를 위해—당신은 장애물을 뛰어넘는 일을 감행할 수 있겠습니까?……"], ["For instance, to rob and murder?”", "가령, 도둑질을 하고 살인을 저지르는 일을요?\""]], [["And again he winked with his left eye, and laughed noiselessly just as before.", "그러고는 그가 다시 왼쪽 눈으로 윙크를 하더니, 아까처럼 소리 없이 웃었다."]], [["“If I did I certainly should not tell you,”", "\"만일 그랬다 한들 당신한테 결코 말하지는 않겠지요.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered with defiant and haughty contempt.", "라스콜니코프가 도전적이고 오만한 경멸을 담아 대답했다."]], [["“No, I was only interested on account of your article, from a literary point of view...”", "\"아니요, 전 그저 당신 논문 때문에, 문학적인 관점에서 흥미가 있었을 뿐입니다…….\""]], [["“Foo!", "'쳇!"], ["how obvious and insolent that is!”", "어찌나 빤하고 무례한지!'"], ["Raskolnikov thought with repulsion.", "라스콜니코프는 혐오스럽게 생각했다."]], [["“Allow me to observe,”", "\"한 말씀 드리지요.\" 그가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."], ["he answered dryly, “that I don’t consider myself a Mahomet or a Napoleon, nor any personage of that kind, and not being one of them I cannot tell you how I should act.”", "\"전 제 자신을 마호메트나 나폴레옹이라고도, 그런 부류의 어떤 인물이라고도 여기지 않습니다. 그러니 그런 자가 아닌 이상, 제가 어떻게 행동할지 당신께 말씀드릴 수가 없군요.\""]], [["“Oh, come, don’t we all think ourselves Napoleons now in Russia?”", "\"오, 이런, 요즘 러시아에서 우리 모두가 자기를 나폴레옹쯤으로 여기지 않습니까?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch said with alarming familiarity.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 섬뜩할 만큼 친근하게 말했다."]], [["Something peculiar betrayed itself in the very intonation of his voice.", "그의 바로 그 목소리의 억양에 무언가 묘한 것이 배어 나왔다."]], [["“Perhaps it was one of these future Napoleons who did for Alyona Ivanovna last week?”", "\"어쩌면 그런 미래의 나폴레옹들 중 하나가 지난주에 알료나 이바노브나를 해치운 게 아닐까요?\""], ["Zametov blurted out from the corner.", "자메토프가 구석에서 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["Raskolnikov did not speak, but looked firmly and intently at Porfiry.", "라스콜니코프는 말없이, 단단하고 뚫어지게 포르피리를 바라보았다."], ["Razumihin was scowling gloomily.", "라주미힌은 침울하게 눈살을 찌푸리고 있었다."], ["He seemed before this to be noticing something.", "그는 이미 그전부터 무언가를 알아채고 있는 듯했다."], ["He looked angrily around.", "그는 성난 얼굴로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["There was a minute of gloomy silence.", "잠시 침울한 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Raskolnikov turned to go.", "라스콜니코프가 가려고 돌아섰다."]], [["“Are you going already?”", "\"벌써 가시려고요?\""], ["Porfiry said amiably, holding out his hand with excessive politeness.", "포르피리가 다정하게 말하며, 지나칠 만큼 정중하게 손을 내밀었다."], ["“Very, very glad of your acquaintance.", "\"알게 되어 정말, 정말 기쁩니다."], ["As for your request, have no uneasiness, write just as I told you, or, better still, come to me there yourself in a day or two...", "당신 요청에 관해서는, 불안해하지 마시고, 제가 말씀드린 대로 글로 써 주시거나, 아니면 더 나은 건, 하루 이틀 안에 직접 저한테 거기로 오시는 겁니다……"], ["to-morrow, indeed.", "사실 내일이라도요."], ["I shall be there at eleven o’clock for certain.", "제가 열한 시엔 틀림없이 거기 있을 테니까요."], ["We’ll arrange it all; we’ll have a talk.", "다 정리해 드리지요, 얘기도 좀 나누고요."], ["As one of the last to be _there_, you might perhaps be able to tell us something,”", "거기 마지막으로 갔던 분들 중 한 명으로서, 어쩌면 우리한테 뭔가 들려주실 수 있을지도 모르고요.\""], ["he added with a most good-natured expression.", "그가 더없이 호인다운 표정으로 덧붙였다."]], [["“You want to cross-examine me officially in due form?”", "\"날 정식으로, 격식대로 심문하시겠다는 겁니까?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked sharply.", "라스콜니코프가 날카롭게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, why?", "\"오, 그럴 리가요?"], ["That’s not necessary for the present.", "지금으로선 그럴 필요 없습니다. 오해하셨군요."], ["You misunderstand me. I lose no opportunity, you see, and...", "보십시오, 저는 어떤 기회도 놓치지 않거든요, 그래서……"], ["I’ve talked with all who had pledges....", "저당물을 잡힌 분들과 죄다 얘기를 나눴지요……."], ["I obtained evidence from some of them, and you are the last....", "그중 몇 분한테서는 진술을 얻어 냈고, 당신이 마지막입니다……."], ["Yes, by the way,”", "아, 그래, 그건 그렇고,\""], ["he cried, seemingly suddenly delighted, “I just remember, what was I thinking of?”", "그가 갑자기 반색한 듯 외쳤다. \"방금 생각났네요, 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었던 거지?\""], ["he turned to Razumihin, “you were talking my ears off about that Nikolay...", "그가 라주미힌을 향해 돌아섰다. \"자네가 그 니콜라이 얘기로 내 귀가 닳도록 떠들어 댔잖아……"], ["of course, I know, I know very well,”", "물론, 나도 알아, 아주 잘 알지,\""], ["he turned to Raskolnikov, “that the fellow is innocent, but what is one to do?", "그가 라스콜니코프에게로 돌아섰다. \"그자가 결백하다는 건 말입니다, 하지만 어쩌겠습니까?"], ["We had to trouble Dmitri too....", "드미트리까지 번거롭게 해야 했지요……."], ["This is the point, this is all: when you went up the stairs it was past seven, wasn’t it?”", "요점은 이겁니다, 이게 전부예요. 당신이 계단을 올라갔을 때가 일곱 시가 지난 무렵이었지요, 안 그렇습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov, with an unpleasant sensation at the very moment he spoke that he need not have said it.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했는데, 그 말을 내뱉는 바로 그 순간 굳이 할 필요 없는 말이었다는 불쾌한 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Then when you went upstairs between seven and eight, didn’t you see in a flat that stood open on a second storey, do you remember?", "\"그럼 일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이에 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 이 층에 활짝 열려 있던 셋집 안에서, 기억하십니까?"], ["two workmen or at least one of them?", "인부 두 사람, 적어도 그중 하나라도 보지 못했나요?"], ["They were painting there, didn’t you notice them?", "그들이 거기서 페인트칠을 하고 있었는데, 눈치채지 못하셨습니까?"], ["It’s very, very important for them.”", "그게 그들한테는 아주, 아주 중요한 문제라서요.\""]], [["“Painters?", "\"페인트공이요?"], ["No, I didn’t see them,”", "아니요, 못 봤습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered slowly, as though ransacking his memory, while at the same instant he was racking every nerve, almost swooning with anxiety to conjecture as quickly as possible where the trap lay and not to overlook anything.", "라스콜니코프가 마치 기억을 샅샅이 뒤지는 듯 천천히 대답했지만, 바로 그 순간 그는 온 신경을 곤두세운 채, 덫이 어디에 놓여 있는지 한시바삐 알아채고 무엇 하나 놓치지 않으려는 불안에 거의 정신을 잃을 지경이었다."], ["“No, I didn’t see them, and I don’t think I noticed a flat like that open....", "\"아니요, 그들을 보지 못했고, 그렇게 활짝 열린 셋집을 봤던 것 같지도 않은데요……."], ["But on the fourth storey”", "하지만 사 층에서는\""], ["(he had mastered the trap now and was triumphant) “I remember now that someone was moving out of the flat opposite Alyona Ivanovna’s....", "(이제 그는 덫을 간파했고 의기양양했다) \"지금 생각나는데, 알료나 이바노브나의 맞은편 셋집에서 누군가 이사를 나가고 있었어요……."], ["I remember...", "기억나요……"], ["I remember it clearly.", "또렷이 기억납니다."], ["Some porters were carrying out a sofa and they squeezed me against the wall.", "인부 몇 사람이 소파를 들어내면서 절 벽에 밀어붙였거든요."], ["But painters...", "하지만 페인트공은……"], ["no, I don’t remember that there were any painters, and I don’t think that there was a flat open anywhere, no, there wasn’t.”", "아니요, 페인트공이 있었던 기억은 없고, 어디에도 활짝 열린 셋집이 있었던 것 같지 않습니다, 아니, 없었어요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"이게 무슨 소린가?\""], ["Razumihin shouted suddenly, as though he had reflected and realised.", "라주미힌이 무언가 곰곰이 생각하다 퍼뜩 깨달은 듯 갑자기 소리쳤다."], ["“Why, it was on the day of the murder the painters were at work, and he was there three days before?", "\"아니, 페인트공들이 일한 건 살인이 있던 날이었고, 이 친구가 거기 갔던 건 그 사흘 전이었잖아?"], ["What are you asking?”", "자넨 대체 뭘 묻고 있는 거야?\""]], [["“Foo!", "\"쳇!"], ["I have muddled it!”", "내가 헷갈렸군!\""], ["Porfiry slapped himself on the forehead.", "포르피리가 자기 이마를 탁 쳤다. \"젠장맞을!"], ["“Deuce take it! This business is turning my brain!”", "이놈의 사건이 내 머리를 어떻게 만드는지 원!\""], ["he addressed Raskolnikov somewhat apologetically.", "그가 다소 변명조로 라스콜니코프에게 말했다."], ["“It would be such a great thing for us to find out whether anyone had seen them between seven and eight at the flat, so I fancied you could perhaps have told us something....", "\"일곱 시에서 여덟 시 사이에 그 셋집에서 그들을 본 사람이 있는지 알아내는 게 우리한테는 워낙 중대한 일이라서, 어쩌면 당신이 뭔가 들려줄 수 있을까 싶었던 겁니다……."], ["I quite muddled it.”", "완전히 헷갈렸네요.\""]], [["“Then you should be more careful,”", "\"그럼 좀 더 조심하셔야지요.\""], ["Razumihin observed grimly.", "라주미힌이 무뚝뚝하게 한마디 했다."]], [["The last words were uttered in the passage.", "마지막 말들은 복도에서 나눈 것이었다."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch saw them to the door with excessive politeness.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 지나칠 만큼 정중하게 그들을 문까지 배웅했다."]], [["They went out into the street gloomy and sullen, and for some steps they did not say a word.", "그들은 침울하고 시무룩한 채 거리로 나섰고, 몇 걸음을 걷는 동안 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Raskolnikov drew a deep breath.", "라스콜니코프는 깊게 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["“I don’t believe it, I can’t believe it!”", "「믿을 수 없어, 도저히 믿을 수가 없다고!」"], ["repeated Razumihin, trying in perplexity to refute Raskolnikov’s arguments.", "라주미힌은 당혹스러워하며 라스콜니코프의 논거를 반박하려 애쓰면서 거듭 말했다."]], [["They were by now approaching Bakaleyev’s lodgings, where Pulcheria Alexandrovna and Dounia had been expecting them a long while.", "그들은 어느덧 바칼레예프 하숙집에 다다르고 있었는데, 그곳에서는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나와 두냐가 오래도록 그들을 기다리고 있었다."], ["Razumihin kept stopping on the way in the heat of discussion, confused and excited by the very fact that they were for the first time speaking openly about _it_.", "라주미힌은 가는 길에 자꾸만 멈춰 서곤 했으니, 논쟁의 열기에 휩싸여, 두 사람이 처음으로 그 일에 대해 터놓고 이야기하고 있다는 사실 자체에 어쩔 줄 모르고 흥분했던 것이다."]], [["“Don’t believe it, then!”", "「그럼 믿지 말든가!」"], ["answered Raskolnikov, with a cold, careless smile.", "라스콜니코프가 차갑고 무심한 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다."], ["“You were noticing nothing as usual, but I was weighing every word.”", "「자넨 늘 그렇듯 아무것도 알아차리지 못했지만, 난 말 한마디 한마디를 다 따져 봤어.」"]], [["“You are suspicious. That is why you weighed their words...", "「자넨 의심이 많아."], ["h’m... certainly, I agree, Porfiry’s tone was rather strange, and still more that wretch Zametov!...", "그래서 그들의 말을 그렇게 따져 본 거지……, 흠……, 그래, 동의해, 포르피리의 어조가 좀 이상하긴 했어, 게다가 그 비열한 자메토프 녀석은 더했고!……, 자네 말이 맞아, 그자한테 뭔가 있긴 했어 — 한데 왜?"], ["You are right, there was something about him--but why? Why?”", "왜 그러는 거지?」"]], [["“He has changed his mind since last night.”", "「어젯밤 이후로 그자가 마음을 바꾼 거야.」"]], [["“Quite the contrary!", "「오히려 정반대지!"], ["If they had that brainless idea, they would do their utmost to hide it, and conceal their cards, so as to catch you afterwards....", "만약 그들이 그런 멍청한 생각을 품었다면, 그걸 숨기려고 갖은 애를 쓰고 패를 감췄을 거야, 나중에 자넬 잡으려고……."], ["But it was all impudent and careless.”", "한데 모든 게 뻔뻔하고 부주의했잖아.」"]], [["“If they had had facts--I mean, real facts--or at least grounds for suspicion, then they would certainly have tried to hide their game, in the hope of getting more (they would have made a search long ago besides).", "「만약 그들에게 증거가 — 그러니까 진짜 증거가 — 아니면 적어도 의심할 근거라도 있었다면, 분명 자기 패를 숨기려 했겠지, 더 많은 걸 얻어 내려고 (게다가 진작 가택 수색을 했을 거고)."], ["But they have no facts, not one.", "하지만 그들에겐 증거가 없어, 단 하나도."], ["It is all mirage--all ambiguous.", "죄다 신기루야 — 죄다 모호하기만 하지."], ["Simply a floating idea.", "그저 떠도는 생각일 뿐이라고."], ["So they try to throw me out by impudence.", "그래서 뻔뻔하게 굴어서 날 흔들어 보려는 거야."], ["And perhaps, he was irritated at having no facts, and blurted it out in his vexation--or perhaps he has some plan... he seems an intelligent man.", "어쩌면 그자는 증거가 없어서 짜증이 났고, 그 분풀이로 불쑥 내뱉었는지도 몰라 — 아니면 무슨 계획이 있는 건지도……, 똑똑한 사람 같으니까."], ["Perhaps he wanted to frighten me by pretending to know.", "어쩌면 다 아는 척하면서 날 겁주려 했는지도 모르지."], ["They have a psychology of their own, brother.", "그들에게는 그들 나름의 심리학이 있거든, 이 사람아."], ["But it is loathsome explaining it all.", "하지만 이 모든 걸 설명하려니 역겹군."], ["Stop!”", "그만하지!」"]], [["“And it’s insulting, insulting!", "「그래, 모욕적이야, 모욕적이라고!"], ["I understand you.", "자네 심정 이해해."], ["But... since we have spoken openly now (and it is an excellent thing that we have at last--I am glad) I will own now frankly that I noticed it in them long ago, this idea.", "하지만……, 이제 우리가 터놓고 이야기했으니까 (드디어 그렇게 된 게 정말 잘된 일이야 — 난 기뻐) 솔직히 털어놓자면, 나도 진작부터 그들한테서 그런 낌새를, 그런 생각을 눈치챘어."], ["Of course the merest hint only--an insinuation--but why an insinuation even? How dare they?", "물론 아주 희미한 암시뿐이었지 — 넌지시 떠보는 정도 — 한데 떠보는 것조차 왜 그러는 거야?"], ["What foundation have they?", "감히 어떻게 그래? 무슨 근거로?"], ["If only you knew how furious I have been.", "내가 얼마나 화가 났는지 자네가 알기만 한다면."], ["Think only!", "생각 좀 해 봐!"], ["Simply because a poor student, unhinged by poverty and hypochondria, on the eve of a severe delirious illness (note that), suspicious, vain, proud, who has not seen a soul to speak to for six months, in rags and in boots without soles, has to face some wretched policemen and put up with their insolence; and the unexpected debt thrust under his nose, the I.O.U. presented by Tchebarov, the new paint, thirty degrees Reaumur and a stifling atmosphere, a crowd of people, the talk about the murder of a person where he had been just before, and all that on an empty stomach--he might well have a fainting fit!", "그저 가난뱅이 대학생 하나가, 가난과 우울증에 짓눌리고, 극심한 섬망성 열병에 걸리기 직전에 (이 점에 주목하라고), 의심 많고, 자존심 강하고, 거만하고, 여섯 달 동안 말 한마디 나눌 사람조차 만나지 못한 채, 누더기를 걸치고 밑창도 없는 장화를 신고서, 어떤 한심한 경찰 나부랭이들과 맞닥뜨려 그들의 무례함을 참아 내야 하고, 게다가 느닷없이 빚 독촉을 코앞에 들이대고, 체바로프가 차용증을 내밀고, 새로 칠한 페인트 냄새에, 섭씨 삼십도가 넘는 무더위에 숨 막히는 공기에, 사람들이 북적대고, 바로 직전에 머물렀던 곳에서 누군가가 살해당했다는 이야기가 오가고, 그 모든 게 빈속에서 벌어졌으니 — 까무러칠 만도 하지!"], ["And that, that is what they found it all on!", "한데 바로 그것을, 바로 그것을 근거로 삼다니!"], ["Damn them!", "빌어먹을 놈들!"], ["I understand how annoying it is, but in your place, Rodya, I would laugh at them, or better still, spit in their ugly faces, and spit a dozen times in all directions.", "짜증나는 거 이해해, 하지만 내가 자네라면, 로쟈, 그자들을 비웃어 주겠어, 아니 그보다 더 좋은 건, 그 추악한 낯짝에 침을 뱉어 주겠어, 사방으로 열두 번은 뱉어 주겠다고."], ["I’d hit out in all directions, neatly too, and so I’d put an end to it. Damn them!", "사방으로 후려갈겨 주겠어, 그것도 깔끔하게, 그래서 끝장을 내 버리겠어."], ["Don’t be downhearted.", "빌어먹을 놈들! 기죽지 마."], ["It’s a shame!”", "부끄러운 일이야!」"]], [["“He really has put it well, though,”", "'그래도 녀석, 말 한번 잘하는군.'"], ["Raskolnikov thought.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."]], [["“Damn them?", "「빌어먹을 놈들이라고?"], ["But the cross-examination again, to-morrow?”", "그래도 내일 또 심문이 있잖아?」"], ["he said with bitterness.", "그가 쓰라리게 말했다."], ["“Must I really enter into explanations with them?", "「정말 내가 그들과 일일이 변명을 주고받아야 하나?"], ["I feel vexed as it is, that I condescended to speak to Zametov yesterday in the restaurant....”", "어제 식당에서 자메토프한테 굽실거리며 말을 건 것만 해도 짜증이 나는데…….」"]], [["“Damn it!", "「제기랄!"], ["I will go myself to Porfiry.", "내가 직접 포르피리한테 가겠어."], ["I will squeeze it out of him, as one of the family: he must let me know the ins and outs of it all!", "한 식구나 다름없으니, 그자한테서 죄다 짜내고 말겠어. 그자는 이 모든 게 어찌 된 영문인지 나한테 다 털어놔야 해!"], ["And as for Zametov...”", "그리고 자메토프 그놈은…….」"]], [["“At last he sees through him!”", "'드디어 녀석이 그자의 속을 꿰뚫어 보는군!'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."]], [["“Stay!”", "「잠깐!」"], ["cried Razumihin, seizing him by the shoulder again.", "라주미힌이 다시 그의 어깨를 붙잡으며 외쳤다."], ["“Stay!", "「잠깐만!"], ["you were wrong.", "자네가 틀렸어."], ["I have thought it out.", "곰곰이 생각해 봤거든."], ["You are wrong!", "자네가 틀렸다고!"], ["How was that a trap?", "그게 어떻게 함정이야?"], ["You say that the question about the workmen was a trap.", "자넨 인부들에 관한 질문이 함정이었다고 하잖아."], ["But if you had done _that_, could you have said you had seen them painting the flat... and the workmen?", "한데 만약 자네가 그 일을 저질렀다면, 그 집에 페인트칠을 하고 있는 걸 봤다고……, 그 인부들을 봤다고 말할 수 있었겠어?"], ["On the contrary, you would have seen nothing, even if you had seen it.", "오히려 아무것도 못 봤을 거야, 설령 봤다 해도 말이야."], ["Who would own it against himself?”", "누가 자기한테 불리한 걸 인정하겠어?」"]], [["“If I had done _that thing_, I should certainly have said that I had seen the workmen and the flat,”", "「만약 내가 그 일을 저질렀다면, 분명 인부들과 그 집을 봤다고 말했을 거야.」"], ["Raskolnikov answered, with reluctance and obvious disgust.", "라스콜니코프가 마지못해, 노골적인 혐오감을 드러내며 대답했다."]], [["“But why speak against yourself?”", "「하지만 왜 자기한테 불리하게 말한단 말이야?」"]], [["“Because only peasants, or the most inexperienced novices deny everything flatly at examinations.", "「왜냐하면 심문에서 무조건 죄다 잡아떼는 건 농사꾼이나 가장 풋내기 초짜들뿐이거든."], ["If a man is ever so little developed and experienced, he will certainly try to admit all the external facts that can’t be avoided, but will seek other explanations of them, will introduce some special, unexpected turn, that will give them another significance and put them in another light.", "사람이 조금이라도 머리가 트이고 경험이 있으면, 분명 피할 수 없는 외적 사실들은 죄다 인정하려 들면서도, 그것들에 대해 다른 설명을 찾아내려 하고, 뭔가 특별하고 뜻밖의 반전을 끌어들여서, 그것들에 다른 의미를 부여하고 다른 빛깔로 보이게 하려 들지."], ["Porfiry might well reckon that I should be sure to answer so, and say I had seen them to give an air of truth, and then make some explanation.”", "포르피리는 내가 분명 그렇게 대답하리라고, 그러니까 진실처럼 보이려고 그들을 봤다고 말하고 나서 무슨 설명을 갖다 붙이리라고 충분히 계산했을 거야.」"]], [["“But he would have told you at once that the workmen could not have been there two days before, and that therefore you must have been there on the day of the murder at eight o’clock.", "「하지만 그자는 곧장 자네한테 이렇게 말했겠지, 인부들이 이틀 전에 거기 있었을 리 없다고, 그러니 자넨 살인이 있던 날 여덟 시에 거기 있었던 게 틀림없다고."], ["And so he would have caught you over a detail.”", "그렇게 해서 자넬 사소한 데서 걸려들게 했겠지.」"]], [["“Yes, that is what he was reckoning on, that I should not have time to reflect, and should be in a hurry to make the most likely answer, and so would forget that the workmen could not have been there two days before.”", "「그래, 바로 그걸 노린 거야, 내가 미처 곰곰이 따져 볼 겨를도 없이 서둘러 가장 그럴듯한 대답을 하려 들 거고, 그래서 인부들이 이틀 전에 거기 있었을 리 없다는 걸 까먹으리라고 말이야.」"]], [["“But how could you forget it?”", "「하지만 어떻게 그걸 까먹을 수가 있어?」"]], [["“Nothing easier.", "「그보다 더 쉬운 일도 없어."], ["It is in just such stupid things clever people are most easily caught.", "영리한 사람들은 바로 그런 멍청한 일에 가장 쉽게 걸려드는 법이거든."], ["The more cunning a man is, the less he suspects that he will be caught in a simple thing.", "사람이 교활할수록, 단순한 일에 걸려들 거라고는 덜 의심하는 법이야."], ["The more cunning a man is, the simpler the trap he must be caught in.", "사람이 교활할수록, 더 단순한 함정에 걸려들게 마련이지."], ["Porfiry is not such a fool as you think....”", "포르피리는 자네가 생각하는 것만큼 바보가 아니야…….」"]], [["“He is a knave then, if that is so!”", "「그렇다면 그자는 악당이로군, 정말 그렇다면!」"]], [["Raskolnikov could not help laughing.", "라스콜니코프는 웃지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But at the very moment, he was struck by the strangeness of his own frankness, and the eagerness with which he had made this explanation, though he had kept up all the preceding conversation with gloomy repulsion, obviously with a motive, from necessity.", "하지만 바로 그 순간, 그는 자기 자신의 솔직함에, 그리고 이 설명을 늘어놓은 그 열의에 흠칫 놀랐다. 앞선 대화는 죄다 음울한 거부감으로, 분명 어떤 의도가 있어서, 필요에 의해 이어 왔으면서도 말이다."]], [["“I am getting a relish for certain aspects!”", "'어떤 면들이 슬슬 입맛에 맞아 가는군!'"], ["he thought to himself.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["But almost at the same instant he became suddenly uneasy, as though an unexpected and alarming idea had occurred to him.", "하지만 거의 같은 순간, 그는 마치 뜻밖의 불안한 생각이 떠오른 듯 갑자기 께름칙해졌다."], ["His uneasiness kept on increasing.", "그의 불안은 점점 더 커져만 갔다."], ["They had just reached the entrance to Bakaleyev’s.", "그들은 막 바칼레예프 하숙집 입구에 다다른 참이었다."]], [["“Go in alone!”", "「혼자 들어가게!」"], ["said Raskolnikov suddenly.", "라스콜니코프가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“I will be back directly.”", "「난 곧 돌아올 테니.」"]], [["“Where are you going?", "「어딜 가는 거야?"], ["Why, we are just here.”", "아니, 우린 막 다 왔는데.」"]], [["“I can’t help it....", "「어쩔 수 없어……."], ["I will come in half an hour.", "삼십 분 안에 올게."], ["Tell them.”", "그들한테 그렇게 전해 줘.」"]], [["“Say what you like, I will come with you.”", "「뭐라고 하든, 난 자네랑 같이 가겠어.」"]], [["“You, too, want to torture me!”", "「자네까지도 날 괴롭히려 드는군!」"], ["he screamed, with such bitter irritation, such despair in his eyes that Razumihin’s hands dropped.", "그가 비명을 질렀는데, 그 눈에 어린 쓰라린 짜증, 그 절망이 어찌나 깊던지 라주미힌의 두 손이 스르르 떨어졌다."], ["He stood for some time on the steps, looking gloomily at Raskolnikov striding rapidly away in the direction of his lodging.", "라주미힌은 얼마 동안 계단에 서서, 라스콜니코프가 자기 하숙집 쪽으로 성큼성큼 빠르게 걸어가는 모습을 음울하게 바라보았다."], ["At last, gritting his teeth and clenching his fist, he swore he would squeeze Porfiry like a lemon that very day, and went up the stairs to reassure Pulcheria Alexandrovna, who was by now alarmed at their long absence.", "마침내 그는 이를 악물고 주먹을 불끈 쥐며, 바로 오늘 안으로 포르피리를 레몬 짜듯 짜내고 말겠다고 다짐하고는, 두 사람이 오래도록 보이지 않아 이젠 불안해하고 있을 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나를 안심시키러 계단을 올라갔다."]], [["When Raskolnikov got home, his hair was soaked with sweat and he was breathing heavily.", "라스콜니코프가 집에 다다랐을 때, 그의 머리칼은 땀에 흠뻑 젖어 있었고 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬고 있었다."], ["He went rapidly up the stairs, walked into his unlocked room and at once fastened the latch.", "그는 재빨리 계단을 올라, 잠겨 있지 않은 자기 방으로 들어가 곧장 빗장을 걸었다."], ["Then in senseless terror he rushed to the corner, to that hole under the paper where he had put the things; put his hand in, and for some minutes felt carefully in the hole, in every crack and fold of the paper.", "그러고는 영문 모를 공포에 사로잡혀 구석으로, 물건들을 숨겨 두었던 벽지 밑 그 구멍으로 달려가, 손을 집어넣고 몇 분 동안이나 그 구멍 속을, 벽지의 갈라진 틈과 접힌 곳을 하나하나 조심스레 더듬어 보았다."], ["Finding nothing, he got up and drew a deep breath.", "아무것도 찾지 못하자, 그는 몸을 일으켜 깊은 숨을 내쉬었다."], ["As he was reaching the steps of Bakaleyev’s, he suddenly fancied that something, a chain, a stud or even a bit of paper in which they had been wrapped with the old woman’s handwriting on it, might somehow have slipped out and been lost in some crack, and then might suddenly turn up as unexpected, conclusive evidence against him.", "바칼레예프 하숙집 계단에 막 이르렀을 때, 문득 그는 무언가가 — 사슬이든, 단추든, 아니면 노파의 글씨가 적힌, 물건들을 쌌던 종잇조각이라도 — 어쩌다 흘러나와 어떤 틈새에 떨어져 사라졌다가, 어느 순간 느닷없이 자신에게 불리한 결정적인 증거로 불쑥 튀어나올지도 모른다는 생각이 들었던 것이다."]], [["He stood as though lost in thought, and a strange, humiliated, half senseless smile strayed on his lips.", "그는 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있었고, 그의 입술에는 기이하고 비굴한, 반쯤은 얼이 빠진 미소가 어른거렸다."], ["He took his cap at last and went quietly out of the room.", "마침내 그는 모자를 집어 들고 조용히 방을 나섰다."], ["His ideas were all tangled.", "그의 생각은 죄다 뒤엉켜 있었다."], ["He went dreamily through the gateway.", "그는 몽롱한 채로 대문을 지나쳤다."]], [["“Here he is himself,”", "「저기 본인이 오는구먼.」"], ["shouted a loud voice.", "우렁찬 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["He raised his head.", "그는 고개를 들었다."]], [["The porter was standing at the door of his little room and was pointing him out to a short man who looked like an artisan, wearing a long coat and a waistcoat, and looking at a distance remarkably like a woman.", "문지기가 자기 작은 방 문간에 서서, 직공처럼 보이는 키 작은 사내에게 그를 가리키고 있었다. 사내는 긴 외투에 조끼를 걸쳤는데, 멀리서 보면 놀랄 만큼 여자처럼 보였다."], ["He stooped, and his head in a greasy cap hung forward.", "그는 구부정했고, 기름때 묻은 모자를 쓴 머리는 앞으로 푹 숙여져 있었다."], ["From his wrinkled flabby face he looked over fifty; his little eyes were lost in fat and they looked out grimly, sternly and discontentedly.", "주름지고 축 늘어진 얼굴로 보아 쉰은 넘긴 듯했고, 작은 두 눈은 살에 파묻힌 채 음침하고 매섭고 못마땅한 빛을 띠고 밖을 내다보고 있었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "「무슨 일입니까?」"], ["Raskolnikov asked, going up to the porter.", "라스콜니코프가 문지기에게 다가가며 물었다."]], [["The man stole a look at him from under his brows and he looked at him attentively, deliberately; then he turned slowly and went out of the gate into the street without saying a word.", "사내는 눈썹 밑으로 그를 슬쩍 훔쳐보더니, 주의 깊게, 천천히 그를 바라보았다. 그러고는 느릿느릿 돌아서서 한마디 말도 없이 대문을 나서 거리로 가 버렸다."]], [["“What is it?”", "「무슨 일이오?」"], ["cried Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 외쳤다."]], [["“Why, he there was asking whether a student lived here, mentioned your name and whom you lodged with.", "「저기, 저 사람이 여기 대학생이 사느냐고 물으면서, 당신 이름이랑 누구네 집에 세 들어 사는지 대더군요."], ["I saw you coming and pointed you out and he went away.", "마침 당신이 오는 게 보여서 가리켰더니, 저렇게 가 버리네요."], ["It’s funny.”", "거참 이상하구먼.」"]], [["The porter too seemed rather puzzled, but not much so, and after wondering for a moment he turned and went back to his room.", "문지기도 다소 어리둥절한 듯했지만, 그리 대수롭지는 않은 듯, 잠시 의아해하다가 돌아서서 자기 방으로 들어가 버렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov ran after the stranger, and at once caught sight of him walking along the other side of the street with the same even, deliberate step with his eyes fixed on the ground, as though in meditation.", "라스콜니코프는 그 낯선 사내를 뒤쫓아 달려갔고, 곧 그가 거리 맞은편을 따라, 아까와 똑같이 한결같고 느릿한 걸음으로, 마치 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯 시선을 땅에 박은 채 걸어가는 모습을 발견했다."], ["He soon overtook him, but for some time walked behind him.", "그는 곧 사내를 따라잡았지만, 한동안은 그 뒤를 따라 걸었다."], ["At last, moving on to a level with him, he looked at his face.", "마침내 그와 나란히 서게 되자, 그는 사내의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["The man noticed him at once, looked at him quickly, but dropped his eyes again; and so they walked for a minute side by side without uttering a word.", "사내는 곧장 그를 알아채고 재빨리 그를 쳐다보았다가, 이내 다시 눈길을 떨구었다. 그렇게 두 사람은 한마디 말도 없이 잠시 나란히 걸었다."]], [["“You were inquiring for me... of the porter?”", "「저를 찾으셨더군요……, 문지기한테?」"], ["Raskolnikov said at last, but in a curiously quiet voice.", "라스콜니코프가 마침내 입을 열었는데, 이상하리만치 조용한 목소리였다."]], [["The man made no answer; he didn’t even look at him.", "사내는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그를 쳐다보지도 않았다."], ["Again they were both silent.", "다시 두 사람은 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Why do you... come and ask for me...", "「왜……, 와서 절 찾으시고는……, 아무 말도 안 하시는 겁니까……."], ["and say nothing.... What’s the meaning of it?”", "이게 무슨 영문이지요?」"]], [["Raskolnikov’s voice broke and he seemed unable to articulate the words clearly.", "라스콜니코프의 목소리가 갈라졌고, 그는 말을 또렷이 입에 올리지도 못하는 것 같았다."]], [["The man raised his eyes this time and turned a gloomy sinister look at Raskolnikov.", "이번에는 사내가 눈을 들어, 음울하고 불길한 눈초리로 라스콜니코프를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Murderer!”", "「살인자.」"], ["he said suddenly in a quiet but clear and distinct voice.", "그가 불쑥, 조용하지만 또렷하고 분명한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["Raskolnikov went on walking beside him.", "라스콜니코프는 계속 그의 곁에서 걸어갔다."], ["His legs felt suddenly weak, a cold shiver ran down his spine, and his heart seemed to stand still for a moment, then suddenly began throbbing as though it were set free.", "두 다리가 갑자기 힘이 풀리고, 등줄기로 차가운 전율이 흘렀으며, 심장이 한순간 멎는 듯하다가, 이내 풀려난 듯 갑자기 마구 뛰기 시작했다."], ["So they walked for about a hundred paces, side by side in silence.", "그렇게 두 사람은 백 걸음쯤 말없이 나란히 걸었다."]], [["The man did not look at him.", "사내는 그를 쳐다보지 않았다."]], [["“What do you mean... what is....", "「무슨 말씀입니까……, 그게 무슨……."], ["Who is a murderer?”", "누가 살인자라는 거죠?」"], ["muttered Raskolnikov hardly audibly.", "라스콜니코프가 거의 들리지도 않게 중얼거렸다."]], [["“_You_ are a murderer,”", "「바로 당신이 살인자야.」"], ["the man answered still more articulately and emphatically, with a smile of triumphant hatred, and again he looked straight into Raskolnikov’s pale face and stricken eyes.", "사내가 한층 더 또렷하고 단호하게, 승리에 찬 증오의 미소를 지으며 대답했고, 다시 한번 라스콜니코프의 창백한 얼굴과 충격에 휩싸인 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다."]], [["They had just reached the cross-roads.", "두 사람은 막 사거리에 다다른 참이었다."], ["The man turned to the left without looking behind him.", "사내는 뒤도 돌아보지 않고 왼쪽으로 꺾어 들었다."], ["Raskolnikov remained standing, gazing after him.", "라스콜니코프는 그 자리에 선 채로, 그의 뒷모습을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["He saw him turn round fifty paces away and look back at him still standing there.", "그는 사내가 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 돌아서서, 여전히 그 자리에 서 있는 자신을 돌아보는 것을 보았다."], ["Raskolnikov could not see clearly, but he fancied that he was again smiling the same smile of cold hatred and triumph.", "라스콜니코프는 또렷이 볼 수는 없었지만, 사내가 다시 그 차가운 증오와 승리의 미소를 짓고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["With slow faltering steps, with shaking knees, Raskolnikov made his way back to his little garret, feeling chilled all over.", "느리고 비틀거리는 걸음으로, 무릎을 후들거리며, 라스콜니코프는 온몸에 한기를 느끼며 자기 작은 다락방으로 돌아갔다."], ["He took off his cap and put it on the table, and for ten minutes he stood without moving.", "그는 모자를 벗어 탁자에 올려놓고, 십 분 동안이나 꼼짝도 않고 서 있었다."], ["Then he sank exhausted on the sofa and with a weak moan of pain he stretched himself on it.", "그러고는 기진맥진하여 소파에 쓰러져, 고통에 찬 가냘픈 신음을 내며 그 위에 몸을 뻗었다."], ["So he lay for half an hour.", "그렇게 그는 삼십 분을 누워 있었다."]], [["He thought of nothing.", "그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다."], ["Some thoughts or fragments of thoughts, some images without order or coherence floated before his mind--faces of people he had seen in his childhood or met somewhere once, whom he would never have recalled, the belfry of the church at V., the billiard table in a restaurant and some officers playing billiards, the smell of cigars in some underground tobacco shop, a tavern room, a back staircase quite dark, all sloppy with dirty water and strewn with egg-shells, and the Sunday bells floating in from somewhere....", "어떤 생각들, 혹은 생각의 파편들이, 두서도 맥락도 없는 어떤 영상들이 그의 마음 앞에 떠다녔다 — 어린 시절에 보았거나 언젠가 어딘가에서 마주쳤던, 결코 다시는 떠올리지 못했을 사람들의 얼굴, V 마을 교회의 종루, 어느 식당의 당구대와 당구를 치던 몇몇 장교들, 지하 어느 담배 가게의 시가 냄새, 어느 선술집 방, 더러운 물로 온통 질척거리고 달걀 껍질이 나뒹구는 새카만 뒷계단, 그리고 어디선가 들려오는 일요일의 종소리……."], ["The images followed one another, whirling like a hurricane.", "영상들은 회오리바람처럼 소용돌이치며 잇따라 떠올랐다."], ["Some of them he liked and tried to clutch at, but they faded and all the while there was an oppression within him, but it was not overwhelming, sometimes it was even pleasant....", "그 가운데 몇몇은 마음에 들어서 붙잡으려 해 보았지만, 이내 스러져 갔고, 그동안 내내 그의 안에는 억눌린 무언가가 있었으나, 그것은 견딜 수 없을 정도는 아니었고, 때로는 심지어 기분 좋기까지 했다……."], ["The slight shivering still persisted, but that too was an almost pleasant sensation.", "가벼운 오한은 여전히 가시지 않았지만, 그것 또한 거의 기분 좋은 감각이었다."]], [["He heard the hurried footsteps of Razumihin; he closed his eyes and pretended to be asleep.", "그는 라주미힌의 다급한 발소리를 들었다. 그는 눈을 감고 자는 척했다."], ["Razumihin opened the door and stood for some time in the doorway as though hesitating, then he stepped softly into the room and went cautiously to the sofa.", "라주미힌이 문을 열고 잠시 문간에 서 있었는데, 마치 망설이는 듯하다가, 살그머니 방 안으로 들어와 조심스레 소파로 다가왔다."], ["Raskolnikov heard Nastasya’s whisper:", "라스콜니코프는 나스타시야가 속삭이는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Don’t disturb him!", "「깨우지 마세요!"], ["Let him sleep.", "자게 두세요."], ["He can have his dinner later.”", "식사는 나중에 해도 되니까.」"]], [["“Quite so,”", "「그러게.」"], ["answered Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 대답했다."], ["Both withdrew carefully and closed the door.", "두 사람은 조심스레 물러나 문을 닫았다."], ["Another half-hour passed.", "다시 삼십 분이 흘렀다."], ["Raskolnikov opened his eyes, turned on his back again, clasping his hands behind his head.", "라스콜니코프는 눈을 뜨고, 다시 등을 대고 돌아누워, 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 꼈다."]], [["“Who is he?", "'그자는 누구지?"], ["Who is that man who sprang out of the earth?", "땅속에서 솟아난 듯한 그 사내는 누구란 말인가?"], ["Where was he, what did he see?", "그자는 어디 있었던 거지, 뭘 본 거고?"], ["He has seen it all, that’s clear.", "모든 걸 봤어, 그건 분명해."], ["Where was he then? And from where did he see?", "그렇다면 그때 그자는 어디 있었던 거지?"], ["Why has he only now sprung out of the earth?", "어디서 본 거고? 왜 이제 와서야 땅속에서 솟아난 거지?"], ["And how could he see?", "그리고 도대체 어떻게 볼 수 있었던 거야?"], ["Is it possible?", "가능한 일인가?"], ["Hm...”", "흠…….'"], ["continued Raskolnikov, turning cold and shivering, “and the jewel case Nikolay found behind the door--was that possible?", "라스콜니코프는 온몸이 싸늘해지며 오한에 떨면서 생각을 이어 갔다. '그리고 니콜라이가 문 뒤에서 찾아냈다는 그 보석 상자 — 그게 가능한 일이었나?"], ["A clue?", "단서라고?"], ["You miss an infinitesimal line and you can build it into a pyramid of evidence!", "아주 미세한 한 가닥을 놓치면, 그걸 증거의 피라미드로 쌓아 올릴 수도 있는 거지!"], ["A fly flew by and saw it!", "파리 한 마리가 날아와 그걸 본 거라고!"], ["Is it possible?”", "가능한 일인가?'"], ["He felt with sudden loathing how weak, how physically weak he had become.", "그는 자신이 얼마나 약해졌는지, 육체적으로 얼마나 쇠약해졌는지 문득 혐오감과 함께 느꼈다."], ["“I ought to have known it,”", "'진작 알았어야 했어.'"], ["he thought with a bitter smile.", "그는 쓰라린 미소를 지으며 생각했다."], ["“And how dared I, knowing myself, knowing how I should be, take up an axe and shed blood!", "'그리고 내 자신을 알면서, 내가 어떻게 될지 알면서, 감히 어떻게 도끼를 집어 들고 피를 흘렸단 말인가!"], ["I ought to have known beforehand....", "미리 알았어야 했어……."], ["Ah, but I did know!”", "아, 하지만 난 알고 있었어!'"], ["he whispered in despair.", "그는 절망에 차서 속삭였다."], ["At times he came to a standstill at some thought.", "이따금 그는 어떤 생각에 우뚝 멈춰 서곤 했다."]], [["“No, those men are not made so.", "'아니야, 그런 인간들은 그렇게 생겨먹지 않았어."], ["The real _Master_ to whom all is permitted storms Toulon, makes a massacre in Paris, _forgets_ an army in Egypt, _wastes_ half a million men in the Moscow expedition and gets off with a jest at Vilna.", "모든 것이 허용되는 진정한 지배자라면 툴롱을 함락시키고, 파리에서 학살을 자행하고, 이집트에 군대를 잊고 내버려 두고, 모스크바 원정에서 오십만 명을 허비하고도, 빌나에서 농담 한마디로 빠져나가지."], ["And altars are set up to him after his death, and so _all_ is permitted.", "게다가 죽은 뒤에는 그에게 제단까지 바쳐지고, 그러니 모든 것이 허용되는 거야."], ["No, such people, it seems, are not of flesh but of bronze!”", "아니, 그런 사람들은 아무래도 살로 빚어진 게 아니라 청동으로 만들어진 모양이야!'"]], [["One sudden irrelevant idea almost made him laugh.", "문득 떠오른 엉뚱한 생각 하나에 그는 하마터면 웃음이 터질 뻔했다."], ["Napoleon, the pyramids, Waterloo, and a wretched skinny old woman, a pawnbroker with a red trunk under her bed--it’s a nice hash for Porfiry Petrovitch to digest!", "나폴레옹, 피라미드, 워털루 — 그리고 침대 밑에 빨간 궤짝을 둔, 비쩍 마른 한심한 노파 전당포 주인이라니 — 포르피리 페트로비치가 소화하기엔 참으로 딱 알맞은 잡탕이로군!"], ["How can they digest it!", "그들이 어떻게 그걸 소화할 수 있겠어!"], ["It’s too inartistic.", "너무 예술성이 없잖아."], ["“A Napoleon creep under an old woman’s bed!", "'나폴레옹이 노파의 침대 밑을 기어 다니다니!"], ["Ugh, how loathsome!”", "윽, 어찌나 역겨운지!'"]], [["At moments he felt he was raving.", "이따금 그는 자신이 헛소리를 하고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["He sank into a state of feverish excitement.", "그는 열에 들뜬 흥분 상태로 빠져들었다."], ["“The old woman is of no consequence,”", "'그 노파는 아무것도 아니야.'"], ["he thought, hotly and incoherently.", "그는 격하게, 두서없이 생각했다."], ["“The old woman was a mistake perhaps, but she is not what matters!", "'그 노파는 어쩌면 실수였는지도 몰라, 하지만 그게 중요한 건 아니지!"], ["The old woman was only an illness....", "그 노파는 그저 하나의 병이었을 뿐이야……."], ["I was in a hurry to overstep....", "난 선을 넘으려고 서둘렀던 거고……."], ["I didn’t kill a human being, but a principle!", "난 한 인간을 죽인 게 아니라, 하나의 원칙을 죽인 거야!"], ["I killed the principle, but I didn’t overstep, I stopped on this side....", "원칙은 죽였지만, 선을 넘지는 못했어, 난 이쪽 편에 멈춰 서고 말았지……."], ["I was only capable of killing.", "난 그저 죽일 줄만 알았던 거야."], ["And it seems I wasn’t even capable of that...", "그런데 그것조차 제대로 할 줄 몰랐던 것 같군……."], ["Principle?", "원칙이라고?"], ["Why was that fool Razumihin abusing the socialists?", "그 멍청한 라주미힌은 왜 사회주의자들을 욕했던 거지? 그들은 부지런하고 실속 있는 사람들이야."], ["They are industrious, commercial people; ‘the happiness of all’ is their case.", "'만인의 행복'이 그들의 대의지. 아니, 인생은 내게 단 한 번만 주어졌고, 다시는 결코 얻지 못할 텐데."], ["No, life is only given to me once and I shall never have it again; I don’t want to wait for ‘the happiness of all.’ I want to live myself, or else better not live at all.", "난 '만인의 행복'을 기다리고 싶지 않아. 난 나 자신이 살고 싶어, 그게 아니라면 차라리 아예 살지 않는 게 낫지."], ["I simply couldn’t pass by my mother starving, keeping my rouble in my pocket while I waited for the ‘happiness of all.’ I am putting my little brick into the happiness of all and so my heart is at peace.", "난 도저히 굶주리는 어머니를 모른 척 지나치면서, '만인의 행복'을 기다리며 내 호주머니 속 일 루블을 움켜쥐고 있을 수가 없었어. 난 만인의 행복에 내 작은 벽돌 한 장을 보태고 있는 거고, 그래서 내 마음이 편한 거야."], ["Ha-ha!", "하하!"], ["Why have you let me slip?", "너희는 왜 날 놓아준 거지?"], ["I only live once, I too want....", "난 단 한 번 사는 거고, 나 역시 원해……."], ["Ech, I am an æsthetic louse and nothing more,”", "에이, 난 미학적인 이[蝨] 한 마리에 지나지 않아.'"], ["he added suddenly, laughing like a madman.", "그는 갑자기 미친 사람처럼 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["“Yes, I am certainly a louse,”", "'그래, 난 분명 이 한 마리야.'"], ["he went on, clutching at the idea, gloating over it and playing with it with vindictive pleasure.", "그는 그 생각을 붙들고, 거기에 흡족해하며, 앙심에 찬 쾌감으로 그것을 가지고 놀면서 말을 이어 갔다."], ["“In the first place, because I can reason that I am one, and secondly, because for a month past I have been troubling benevolent Providence, calling it to witness that not for my own fleshly lusts did I undertake it, but with a grand and noble object--ha-ha!", "'첫째로, 내가 스스로 이라고 따져 낼 수 있기 때문이고, 둘째로, 지난 한 달 내내 자비로운 섭리를 괴롭히면서, 내가 이 일을 벌인 건 내 육신의 욕망 때문이 아니라 위대하고 고귀한 목적을 위해서라고 그것을 증인으로 불러 댔기 때문이지 — 하하!"], ["Thirdly, because I aimed at carrying it out as justly as possible, weighing, measuring and calculating.", "셋째로, 가능한 한 공정하게 이 일을 해치우려고, 저울질하고 재고 따졌기 때문이야."], ["Of all the lice I picked out the most useless one and proposed to take from her only as much as I needed for the first step, no more nor less (so the rest would have gone to a monastery, according to her will, ha-ha!).", "그 모든 이 가운데서 가장 쓸모없는 한 마리를 골라내, 그것에게서 첫걸음에 필요한 만큼만, 더도 덜도 말고 딱 그만큼만 가져가려 했지 (그러니 나머지는 그 노파의 유언대로 수도원으로 갔겠지, 하하!)."], ["And what shows that I am utterly a louse,”", "그리고 내가 완전히 이 한 마리라는 걸 보여 주는 건,'"], ["he added, grinding his teeth, “is that I am perhaps viler and more loathsome than the louse I killed, and _I felt beforehand_ that I should tell myself so _after_ killing her.", "그는 이를 갈며 덧붙였다, '내가 어쩌면 내가 죽인 그 이보다 더 비열하고 더 역겨운 존재일지도 모른다는 점이고, 또 그 노파를 죽인 뒤에 내가 스스로에게 이렇게 말하게 되리라는 걸 미리 느꼈다는 점이야."], ["Can anything be compared with the horror of that?", "그 끔찍함에 견줄 만한 게 어디 있겠어?"], ["The vulgarity!", "그 천박함!"], ["The abjectness!", "그 비열함!"], ["I understand the ‘prophet’ with his sabre, on his steed: Allah commands and ‘trembling’ creation must obey!", "난 칼을 들고 말에 올라탄 '예언자'를 이해해. 알라가 명하면 '벌벌 떠는'"], ["The ‘prophet’ is right, he is right when he sets a battery across the street and blows up the innocent and the guilty without deigning to explain!", "피조물은 복종해야 하지! 그 '예언자'는 옳아, 거리를 가로질러 포대를 세워 놓고 죄 있는 자건 죄 없는 자건 가리지 않고, 굳이 설명할 가치도 없다는 듯 날려 버릴 때, 그는 옳은 거야!"], ["It’s for you to obey, trembling creation, and not _to have desires_, for that’s not for you!...", "복종하는 것이 너희가 할 일이다, 벌벌 떠는 피조물들아, 욕망을 품는 것이 아니라. 그건 너희에게 허락된 게 아니니까!……."], ["I shall never, never forgive the old woman!”", "난 결코, 결코 그 노파를 용서하지 않을 거야!'"]], [["His hair was soaked with sweat, his quivering lips were parched, his eyes were fixed on the ceiling.", "그의 머리칼은 땀에 흠뻑 젖었고, 떨리는 입술은 바싹 말라 있었으며, 두 눈은 천장에 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Mother, sister--how I loved them!", "'어머니, 누이 — 내가 그들을 얼마나 사랑했던가!"], ["Why do I hate them now?", "그런데 왜 지금은 그들을 증오하는 거지?"], ["Yes, I hate them, I feel a physical hatred for them, I can’t bear them near me....", "그래, 난 그들을 증오해, 그들에게 육체적인 증오를 느껴, 그들이 내 곁에 있는 걸 견딜 수가 없어……."], ["I went up to my mother and kissed her, I remember....", "어머니께 다가가 입을 맞췄지, 기억나……."], ["To embrace her and think if she only knew... shall I tell her then?", "어머니를 끌어안고서 생각했어, 어머니가 알기만 한다면……, 그러면 어머니께 말씀드릴까?"], ["That’s just what I might do....", "정말 그럴 수도 있겠지……."], ["_She_ must be the same as I am,”", "어머니도 나와 같은 사람일 게 틀림없어.'"], ["he added, straining himself to think, as it were struggling with delirium.", "그는 마치 섬망과 씨름하듯 안간힘을 다해 생각을 짜내며 덧붙였다."], ["“Ah, how I hate the old woman now!", "'아, 지금 그 노파가 얼마나 증오스러운지!"], ["I feel I should kill her again if she came to life!", "그 노파가 되살아난다면 다시 죽여 버릴 것만 같아!"], ["Poor Lizaveta!", "가엾은 리자베타!"], ["Why did she come in?...", "그애는 왜 들어왔던 걸까?……."], ["It’s strange though, why is it I scarcely ever think of her, as though I hadn’t killed her?", "한데 이상해, 왜 난 마치 그애를 죽이지도 않은 것처럼, 그애 생각은 거의 하지 않는 거지?"], ["Lizaveta!", "리자베타!"], ["Sonia!", "소냐!"], ["Poor gentle things, with gentle eyes....", "가엾고 온순한 것들, 온순한 눈을 한……."], ["Dear women!", "사랑스러운 여자들!"], ["Why don’t they weep?", "그들은 왜 울지 않는 거지?"], ["Why don’t they moan?", "왜 신음하지 않는 거지?"], ["They give up everything... their eyes are soft and gentle....", "그들은 모든 걸 내어 주지……, 그들의 눈은 부드럽고 온순해……."], ["Sonia, Sonia!", "소냐, 소냐!"], ["Gentle Sonia!”", "온순한 소냐!'"]], [["He lost consciousness; it seemed strange to him that he didn’t remember how he got into the street.", "그는 의식을 잃었다. 어떻게 거리로 나왔는지 기억나지 않는 것이 그에게는 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["It was late evening.", "늦은 저녁이었다."], ["The twilight had fallen and the full moon was shining more and more brightly; but there was a peculiar breathlessness in the air.", "땅거미가 내려앉았고 보름달이 점점 더 환하게 빛나고 있었다. 하지만 공기 중에는 묘하게 숨 막히는 답답함이 감돌았다."], ["There were crowds of people in the street; workmen and business people were making their way home; other people had come out for a walk; there was a smell of mortar, dust and stagnant water.", "거리에는 사람들이 북적였다. 노동자들과 장사치들은 집으로 돌아가는 길이었고, 다른 사람들은 산책을 나온 참이었다."], ["Raskolnikov walked along, mournful and anxious; he was distinctly aware of having come out with a purpose, of having to do something in a hurry, but what it was he had forgotten.", "회반죽과 먼지, 고인 물의 냄새가 풍겼다. 라스콜니코프는 침울하고 불안한 채로 걸어갔다."], ["Suddenly he stood still and saw a man standing on the other side of the street, beckoning to him.", "그는 자신이 어떤 목적을 품고 나왔다는 것을, 서둘러 무언가를 해야 한다는 것을 또렷이 의식하고 있었지만, 그게 무엇인지는 잊어버렸다. 문득 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 어떤 사내가 거리 맞은편에 서서 자신에게 손짓하는 것을 보았다."], ["He crossed over to him, but at once the man turned and walked away with his head hanging, as though he had made no sign to him.", "그는 사내에게 건너갔지만, 사내는 곧장 돌아서더니, 마치 그에게 아무런 신호도 보내지 않았다는 듯 고개를 푹 숙인 채 걸어가 버렸다. '잠깐, 저자가 정말 손짓을 한 건가?'"], ["“Stay, did he really beckon?”", "라스콜니코프는 의아해하면서도, 그를 따라잡으려 애썼다."], ["Raskolnikov wondered, but he tried to overtake him.", "열 걸음쯤 거리로 다가갔을 때 그는 사내를 알아보고 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["When he was within ten paces he recognised him and was frightened; it was the same man with stooping shoulders in the long coat.", "긴 외투를 걸친, 어깨가 구부정한 바로 그 사내였다. 라스콜니코프는 거리를 두고 그를 뒤따랐다."], ["Raskolnikov followed him at a distance; his heart was beating; they went down a turning; the man still did not look round.", "그의 심장이 마구 뛰었다. 그들은 어느 모퉁이를 돌아 들어갔지만, 사내는 여전히 뒤를 돌아보지 않았다."], ["“Does he know I am following him?”", "'저자는 내가 뒤따르고 있는 걸 알까?'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."], ["The man went into the gateway of a big house.", "사내는 어느 커다란 건물의 대문 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Raskolnikov hastened to the gate and looked in to see whether he would look round and sign to him.", "라스콜니코프는 대문으로 서둘러 다가가, 사내가 돌아서서 자신에게 신호를 보내는지 들여다보았다."], ["In the court-yard the man did turn round and again seemed to beckon him.", "안뜰에서 사내는 정말로 돌아서더니, 다시 그에게 손짓하는 것 같았다."], ["Raskolnikov at once followed him into the yard, but the man was gone.", "라스콜니코프는 곧장 그를 따라 안뜰로 들어갔지만, 사내는 사라지고 없었다."], ["He must have gone up the first staircase.", "분명 첫 번째 계단을 올라간 것이 틀림없었다."], ["Raskolnikov rushed after him.", "라스콜니코프는 그를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."], ["He heard slow measured steps two flights above.", "그는 두 층쯤 위에서 느리고 한결같은 발소리를 들었다."], ["The staircase seemed strangely familiar.", "그 계단은 이상하게 낯익었다. 그는 이 층 창가에 다다랐다."], ["He reached the window on the first floor; the moon shone through the panes with a melancholy and mysterious light; then he reached the second floor.", "달빛이 우울하고 신비로운 빛을 띠고 유리창을 통해 비쳐 들었다. 그러고는 삼 층에 다다랐다."], ["Bah!", "아니!"], ["this is the flat where the painters were at work...", "여기는 인부들이 일하던 그 집이잖아……."], ["but how was it he did not recognise it at once?", "한데 어째서 곧장 알아보지 못한 거지?"], ["The steps of the man above had died away.", "위에 있던 사내의 발소리는 잦아들어 있었다."], ["“So he must have stopped or hidden somewhere.”", "'그렇다면 어딘가에 멈춰 섰거나 숨은 게 틀림없어.'"], ["He reached the third storey, should he go on?", "그는 사 층에 다다랐다. 계속 올라가야 하나?"], ["There was a stillness that was dreadful....", "끔찍한 정적이 감돌았다……. 하지만 그는 계속 올라갔다."], ["But he went on. The sound of his own footsteps scared and frightened him.", "자기 자신의 발소리에 그는 흠칫 놀라 겁에 질렸다."], ["How dark it was!", "어찌나 어두운지!"], ["The man must be hiding in some corner here.", "사내는 분명 여기 어느 구석에 숨어 있을 터였다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["the flat was standing wide open, he hesitated and went in.", "그 집은 문이 활짝 열려 있었고, 그는 망설이다가 안으로 들어갔다."], ["It was very dark and empty in the passage, as though everything had been removed; he crept on tiptoe into the parlour which was flooded with moonlight.", "통로는 마치 모든 게 치워진 듯 몹시 어둡고 텅 비어 있었다. 그는 발끝으로 살금살금 응접실로 기어들었는데, 그곳은 달빛으로 흠뻑 잠겨 있었다."], ["Everything there was as before, the chairs, the looking-glass, the yellow sofa and the pictures in the frames.", "그곳의 모든 것이 예전 그대로였다. 의자들, 거울, 노란 소파, 그리고 액자에 끼운 그림들."], ["A huge, round, copper-red moon looked in at the windows.", "거대하고 둥근, 구릿빛으로 붉은 달이 창문으로 들여다보고 있었다."], ["“It’s the moon that makes it so still, weaving some mystery,”", "'저 달 때문에 이렇게 고요한 거야, 무슨 신비를 자아내면서.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."], ["He stood and waited, waited a long while, and the more silent the moonlight, the more violently his heart beat, till it was painful.", "그는 서서 기다렸다, 오래도록 기다렸고, 달빛이 고요해질수록 그의 심장은 더욱 격렬하게 뛰어, 마침내 고통스러울 지경이었다."], ["And still the same hush.", "그래도 여전히 똑같은 정적이었다."], ["Suddenly he heard a momentary sharp crack like the snapping of a splinter and all was still again.", "문득 그는 나뭇조각이 부러지는 듯한 날카롭고 짧은 소리를 들었고, 다시 모든 것이 고요해졌다."], ["A fly flew up suddenly and struck the window pane with a plaintive buzz.", "파리 한 마리가 갑자기 날아올라 애처로운 윙윙 소리를 내며 유리창에 부딪쳤다."], ["At that moment he noticed in the corner between the window and the little cupboard something like a cloak hanging on the wall.", "그 순간 그는 창문과 작은 찬장 사이 구석에, 벽에 망토 같은 무언가가 걸려 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["“Why is that cloak here?”", "'저 망토가 왜 여기 있지?'"], ["he thought, “it wasn’t there before....”", "그는 생각했다. '전에는 저기 없었는데…….'"], ["He went up to it quietly and felt that there was someone hiding behind it.", "그는 조용히 그쪽으로 다가가, 그 뒤에 누군가가 숨어 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He cautiously moved the cloak and saw, sitting on a chair in the corner, the old woman bent double so that he couldn’t see her face; but it was she.", "그는 조심스레 망토를 젖혔고, 구석 의자에 노파가 앉아 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 있어서 그 얼굴을 볼 수 없는 것을 보았다. 하지만 그건 그 노파였다."], ["He stood over her.", "그는 노파 위로 우뚝 섰다."], ["“She is afraid,” he thought.", "'저 여자가 겁을 먹었군.'"], ["He stealthily took the axe from the noose and struck her one blow, then another on the skull.", "그는 생각했다. 그는 슬그머니 올가미에서 도끼를 빼 들고, 노파의 정수리에 한 번, 그러고는 또 한 번 내리쳤다."], ["But strange to say she did not stir, as though she were made of wood.", "그런데 이상하게도 노파는 마치 나무로 만들어진 듯 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."], ["He was frightened, bent down nearer and tried to look at her; but she, too, bent her head lower.", "그는 겁이 나서 더 가까이 몸을 숙여 노파를 들여다보려 했다. 하지만 노파 역시 고개를 더 깊이 떨구었다."], ["He bent right down to the ground and peeped up into her face from below, he peeped and turned cold with horror: the old woman was sitting and laughing, shaking with noiseless laughter, doing her utmost that he should not hear it.", "그는 땅바닥까지 몸을 숙여 아래에서 위로 노파의 얼굴을 들여다보았고, 들여다보다가 공포로 온몸이 싸늘하게 식었다. 노파는 앉은 채로 웃고 있었다, 소리 없는 웃음에 몸을 들썩이면서, 그에게 그 소리가 들리지 않게 하려고 안간힘을 쓰면서."], ["Suddenly he fancied that the door from the bedroom was opened a little and that there was laughter and whispering within.", "문득 그는 침실 문이 조금 열려 있고, 그 안에서 웃음소리와 속삭임이 새어 나오는 것만 같았다."], ["He was overcome with frenzy and he began hitting the old woman on the head with all his force, but at every blow of the axe the laughter and whispering from the bedroom grew louder and the old woman was simply shaking with mirth.", "그는 광기에 휩싸여, 있는 힘껏 노파의 머리를 내리치기 시작했지만, 도끼를 한 번 휘두를 때마다 침실에서 새어 나오는 웃음과 속삭임은 점점 더 커졌고, 노파는 그저 웃음으로 몸을 들썩일 뿐이었다."], ["He was rushing away, but the passage was full of people, the doors of the flats stood open and on the landing, on the stairs and everywhere below there were people, rows of heads, all looking, but huddled together in silence and expectation.", "그는 달아나려 했지만, 통로는 사람들로 가득 차 있었고, 셋집들의 문이 활짝 열려 있었으며, 층계참에도, 계단에도, 아래쪽 어디에나 사람들이, 줄지어 늘어선 머리들이 모조리 그를 바라보면서도, 침묵 속에 숨죽인 채 무언가를 기다리며 빽빽이 몰려 있었다."], ["Something gripped his heart, his legs were rooted to the spot, they would not move....", "무언가가 그의 심장을 움켜쥐었고, 두 다리는 그 자리에 뿌리박힌 듯, 도무지 움직이려 하지 않았다……."], ["He tried to scream and woke up.", "그는 비명을 지르려 하다가 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["He drew a deep breath--but his dream seemed strangely to persist: his door was flung open and a man whom he had never seen stood in the doorway watching him intently.", "그는 깊은 숨을 들이쉬었다 — 그런데 꿈이 이상하게도 그대로 이어지는 것 같았다. 그의 방문이 활짝 열려 있었고, 한 번도 본 적 없는 사내가 문간에 서서 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Raskolnikov had hardly opened his eyes and he instantly closed them again.", "라스콜니코프는 눈을 뜨자마자 곧바로 다시 감아 버렸다."], ["He lay on his back without stirring.", "그는 등을 대고 누운 채 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Is it still a dream?”", "'아직도 꿈인가?'"], ["he wondered and again raised his eyelids hardly perceptibly; the stranger was standing in the same place, still watching him.", "그는 의아해하며 다시 눈꺼풀을 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼만 들어 올렸다. 그 낯선 사내는 똑같은 자리에 서서, 여전히 그를 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["He stepped cautiously into the room, carefully closing the door after him, went up to the table, paused a moment, still keeping his eyes on Raskolnikov, and noiselessly seated himself on the chair by the sofa; he put his hat on the floor beside him and leaned his hands on his cane and his chin on his hands.", "사내는 조심스레 방 안으로 발을 들여놓고, 등 뒤로 문을 신중히 닫고는, 탁자로 다가가 잠시 멈춰 섰는데, 그러는 내내 라스콜니코프에게서 눈을 떼지 않았으며, 소리 없이 소파 옆 의자에 앉았다. 그는 모자를 옆 바닥에 내려놓고, 두 손을 지팡이에 얹고 그 손 위에 턱을 괴었다."], ["It was evident that he was prepared to wait indefinitely.", "그가 한없이 기다릴 채비를 한 것이 분명했다."], ["As far as Raskolnikov could make out from his stolen glances, he was a man no longer young, stout, with a full, fair, almost whitish beard.", "라스콜니코프가 훔쳐보는 눈길로 가늠해 보건대, 그는 더는 젊지 않은, 살집이 있고, 풍성하고 옅은, 거의 희끄무레한 수염을 기른 사내였다."]], [["Ten minutes passed.", "십 분이 흘렀다."], ["It was still light, but beginning to get dusk.", "아직 환했지만, 어스름이 깔리기 시작하고 있었다."], ["There was complete stillness in the room.", "방 안은 완전한 정적에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Not a sound came from the stairs.", "계단에서도 아무 소리 들리지 않았다."], ["Only a big fly buzzed and fluttered against the window pane.", "오직 큰 파리 한 마리가 윙윙거리며 유리창에 부딪쳐 파닥거릴 뿐이었다."], ["It was unbearable at last.", "마침내 견딜 수 없는 지경이 되었다."], ["Raskolnikov suddenly got up and sat on the sofa.", "라스콜니코프는 불쑥 몸을 일으켜 소파에 앉았다."]], [["“Come, tell me what you want.”", "「자, 원하는 게 뭔지 말해 보시오.」"]], [["“I knew you were not asleep, but only pretending,”", "「당신이 자는 게 아니라 그저 자는 척하고 있다는 걸 알고 있었소.」"], ["the stranger answered oddly, laughing calmly.", "낯선 사내가 차분하게 웃으며 묘하게 대답했다."], ["“Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov, allow me to introduce myself....”", "「아르카디 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프올시다, 제 소개를 하지요…….」"]], [["“Can this be still a dream?”", "\"이것도 아직 꿈인가?\""], ["Raskolnikov thought once more.", "라스콜니코프는 또다시 생각했다."]], [["He looked carefully and suspiciously at the unexpected visitor.", "그는 뜻밖의 방문객을 주의 깊게, 의심스러운 눈초리로 바라보았다."]], [["“Svidrigaïlov!", "\"스비드리가일로프!"], ["What nonsense!", "무슨 말도 안 되는 소리!"], ["It can’t be!”", "그럴 리가 없어!\""], ["he said at last aloud in bewilderment.", "마침내 그가 어리둥절한 채 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["His visitor did not seem at all surprised at this exclamation.", "방문객은 이런 외침에 전혀 놀라는 기색이 없었다."]], [["“I’ve come to you for two reasons. In the first place, I wanted to make your personal acquaintance, as I have already heard a great deal about you that is interesting and flattering; secondly, I cherish the hope that you may not refuse to assist me in a matter directly concerning the welfare of your sister, Avdotya Romanovna. For without your support she might not let me come near her now, for she is prejudiced against me, but with your assistance I reckon on...”", "\"두 가지 이유로 당신을 찾아왔소. 우선, 당신과 직접 알고 지내고 싶었소. 이미 당신에 관해 흥미롭고 칭찬할 만한 이야기를 많이 들었으니까. 그리고 둘째로, 당신 누이 아브도티야 로마노브나의 행복과 직접 관련된 일에서 당신이 나를 도와주기를 거절하지 않으리라는 희망을 품고 있소. 당신의 지원이 없으면 누이는 지금 나를 가까이 오게 하지 않을 것이오. 그녀는 내게 편견을 품고 있으니까. 하지만 당신의 도움이 있다면 나는…….\""]], [["“You reckon wrongly,”", "\"잘못 짚었소.\""], ["interrupted Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“They only arrived yesterday, may I ask you?”", "\"실례지만, 그분들은 어제 도착했지요?\""]], [["Raskolnikov made no reply.", "라스콜니코프는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“It was yesterday, I know.", "\"어제였소, 알고 있소."], ["I only arrived myself the day before.", "나 자신도 그저께야 도착했고."], ["Well, let me tell you this, Rodion Romanovitch, I don’t consider it necessary to justify myself, but kindly tell me what was there particularly criminal on my part in all this business, speaking without prejudice, with common sense?”", "자, 이거 하나는 말해 두지요, 로디온 로마노비치. 나로서는 굳이 변명할 필요를 느끼지 않소만, 부디 말해 보시오, 편견 없이, 상식적으로 봐서, 이 모든 일에서 내 쪽에 무슨 특별히 죄가 될 만한 일이 있었소?\""]], [["Raskolnikov continued to look at him in silence.", "라스콜니코프는 여전히 말없이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“That in my own house I persecuted a defenceless girl and ‘insulted her with my infamous proposals’--is that it?", "\"내 집에서 무력한 처녀를 못살게 굴고 '파렴치한 제안으로 그녀를 모욕했다'는 거요? 그거요?"], ["(I am anticipating you.) But you’ve only to assume that I, too, am a man _et nihil humanum_... in a word, that I am capable of being attracted and falling in love (which does not depend on our will), then everything can be explained in the most natural manner.", "(당신이 할 말을 내가 미리 하는 거요.) 하지만 나 또한 한 인간이며 인간적인 것이라면 무엇 하나 낯설지 않다고 가정해 보시오……, 한마디로, 나도 누군가에게 끌리고 사랑에 빠질 수 있다고(이건 우리 의지에 달린 일이 아니지요) 말이오."], ["The question is, am I a monster, or am I myself a victim?", "그러면 모든 것이 더없이 자연스럽게 설명될 수 있소. 문제는 이거요, 내가 괴물인가, 아니면 나 자신이 희생자인가?"], ["And what if I am a victim?", "그리고 만약 내가 희생자라면?"], ["In proposing to the object of my passion to elope with me to America or Switzerland, I may have cherished the deepest respect for her and may have thought that I was promoting our mutual happiness!", "내 열정의 대상에게 함께 미국이나 스위스로 달아나자고 제안하면서, 나는 그녀에게 더없이 깊은 존경을 품고 있었을 수도, 또 우리 둘의 행복을 도모하고 있다고 여겼을 수도 있소!"], ["Reason is the slave of passion, you know; why, probably, I was doing more harm to myself than anyone!”", "이성은 열정의 노예라고들 하지 않소. 그러니 아마 나는 누구보다도 나 자신에게 더 큰 해를 끼치고 있었던 셈이오!\""]], [["“But that’s not the point,”", "\"하지만 그게 요점이 아니오.\""], ["Raskolnikov interrupted with disgust.", "라스콜니코프가 혐오스럽다는 듯 말을 끊었다."], ["“It’s simply that whether you are right or wrong, we dislike you.", "\"당신이 옳든 그르든, 우리는 당신이 싫소. 우린 당신과 아무 상관도 갖고 싶지 않소."], ["We don’t want to have anything to do with you. We show you the door.", "우린 당신에게 나가는 문을 가리키는 거요."], ["Go out!”", "나가시오!\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov broke into a sudden laugh.", "스비드리가일로프가 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“But you’re... but there’s no getting round you,”", "\"하지만 당신은……, 정말 당신은 어쩔 도리가 없는 사람이군.\""], ["he said, laughing in the frankest way.", "그가 더없이 솔직하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I hoped to get round you, but you took up the right line at once!”", "\"당신을 구슬려 볼 작정이었는데, 당신은 단번에 정곡을 짚어 버렸으니!\""]], [["“But you are trying to get round me still!”", "\"하지만 당신은 지금도 나를 구슬리려 들고 있잖소!\""]], [["“What of it?", "\"그게 어떻단 말이오?"], ["What of it?”", "그게 뭐 어때서?\""], ["cried Svidrigaïlov, laughing openly.", "스비드리가일로프가 드러내 놓고 웃으며 외쳤다."], ["“But this is what the French call _bonne guerre_, and the most innocent form of deception!...", "\"하지만 이건 프랑스 사람들이 말하는 정정당당한 싸움이고, 더없이 순진한 형태의 속임수일 뿐이오!……"], ["But still you have interrupted me; one way or another, I repeat again: there would never have been any unpleasantness except for what happened in the garden.", "그건 그렇고, 당신이 내 말을 끊었군. 어느 쪽이든, 다시 한 번 말하지만, 정원에서 일어난 그 일만 아니었다면 아무런 불미스러운 일도 없었을 거요."], ["Marfa Petrovna...”", "마르파 페트로브나가…….\""]], [["“You have got rid of Marfa Petrovna, too, so they say?”", "\"마르파 페트로브나도 당신이 처치해 버렸다지요, 다들 그러던데?\""], ["Raskolnikov interrupted rudely.", "라스콜니코프가 거칠게 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Oh, you’ve heard that, too, then?", "\"오, 그것도 들으셨군?"], ["You’d be sure to, though....", "하기야 당신이라면 분명 들었겠지……."], ["But as for your question, I really don’t know what to say, though my own conscience is quite at rest on that score.", "한데 당신 질문에 대해서는, 정말이지 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠소. 그 점에 관해서라면 내 양심은 더없이 편안하지만 말이오."], ["Don’t suppose that I am in any apprehension about it.", "내가 그 일로 어떤 두려움에 떨고 있다고는 생각하지 마시오."], ["All was regular and in order; the medical inquiry diagnosed apoplexy due to bathing immediately after a heavy dinner and a bottle of wine, and indeed it could have proved nothing else.", "모든 게 적법하게, 순리대로 처리됐소. 의학적 조사 결과는 푸짐한 식사와 포도주 한 병을 들이켜고 곧장 목욕을 한 탓에 일어난 뇌졸중으로 진단됐고, 사실 그 외에 다른 무엇도 밝혀질 수 없었지요."], ["But I’ll tell you what I have been thinking to myself of late, on my way here in the train, especially: didn’t I contribute to all that...", "하지만 이거 하나는 말해 두지요, 요즘 들어, 특히 여기로 오는 기차 안에서 나 혼자 곰곰이 생각해 본 게 있는데, 내가 어떤 식으로든, 도덕적으로, 그 모든……"], ["calamity, morally, in a way, by irritation or something of the sort.", "재앙에 일조한 건 아니었을까, 짜증을 돋운다든지 그 비슷한 걸로 말이오."], ["But I came to the conclusion that that, too, was quite out of the question.”", "하지만 나는 그것 또한 전혀 있을 수 없는 일이라는 결론에 이르렀소.\""]], [["Raskolnikov laughed.", "라스콜니코프가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“I wonder you trouble yourself about it!”", "\"당신이 그런 일로 골머리를 썩이다니 신기하군!\""]], [["“But what are you laughing at?", "\"한데 뭐가 그리 우습소?"], ["Only consider, I struck her just twice with a switch--there were no marks even...", "한번 생각해 보시오, 나는 그녀를 채찍으로 딱 두 번 때렸을 뿐이고, 자국조차 남지 않았소……. 제발 나를 냉소가로 여기지는 마시오."], ["don’t regard me as a cynic, please; I am perfectly aware how atrocious it was of me and all that; but I know for certain, too, that Marfa Petrovna was very likely pleased at my, so to say, warmth.", "그게 얼마나 끔찍한 짓이었는지, 그 모든 걸 나도 잘 알고 있으니까. 하지만 마르파 페트로브나가 나의, 말하자면, 그 열기에 오히려 기뻐했으리라는 것 또한 나는 확실히 알고 있소."], ["The story of your sister had been wrung out to the last drop; for the last three days Marfa Petrovna had been forced to sit at home; she had nothing to show herself with in the town.", "당신 누이에 관한 이야기는 마지막 한 방울까지 짜낼 대로 짜낸 상태였소. 사흘 내내 마르파 페트로브나는 집에 틀어박혀 있어야만 했지요."], ["Besides, she had bored them so with that letter (you heard about her reading the letter).", "시내에 나가 떠벌릴 거리가 하나도 남지 않았으니까. 게다가 그 편지로 사람들을 어찌나 진저리 나게 했던지(그녀가 편지를 낭독하고 다닌 거 들으셨지요)."], ["And all of a sudden those two switches fell from heaven!", "그런데 난데없이 그 두 차례의 채찍질이 하늘에서 떨어진 거요!"], ["Her first act was to order the carriage to be got out....", "그녀가 맨 먼저 한 일은 마차를 대령시키라고 명한 거였소……."], ["Not to speak of the fact that there are cases when women are very, very glad to be insulted in spite of all their show of indignation.", "게다가, 여자들이 온갖 분개한 시늉을 다 하면서도 사실은 모욕당하는 것을 아주, 아주 기뻐하는 경우가 있다는 건 말할 것도 없지요."], ["There are instances of it with everyone; human beings in general, indeed, greatly love to be insulted, have you noticed that?", "누구에게나 그런 경우가 있소. 사실 인간이란 대체로 모욕당하는 것을 무척 좋아하는데, 당신은 그걸 눈치챈 적 있소?"], ["But it’s particularly so with women.", "하지만 특히 여자들이 그렇지."], ["One might even say it’s their only amusement.”", "그게 그들의 유일한 낙이라고 해도 좋을 정도요.\""]], [["At one time Raskolnikov thought of getting up and walking out and so finishing the interview.", "한순간 라스콜니코프는 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나가 버림으로써 이 면담을 끝내 버릴까 생각했다."], ["But some curiosity and even a sort of prudence made him linger for a moment.", "하지만 어떤 호기심이, 게다가 일종의 신중함 같은 것이 그를 잠시 더 머무르게 했다."]], [["“You are fond of fighting?”", "\"당신은 싸움을 좋아하는군요?\""], ["he asked carelessly.", "그는 무심한 듯 물었다."]], [["“No, not very,”", "\"아니, 그렇게 좋아하진 않소.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov answered, calmly.", "스비드리가일로프가 차분하게 대답했다."], ["“And Marfa Petrovna and I scarcely ever fought.", "\"마르파 페트로브나와 나는 거의 싸운 적이 없소."], ["We lived very harmoniously, and she was always pleased with me.", "우린 아주 화목하게 살았고, 그녀는 늘 나를 흡족해했지요."], ["I only used the whip twice in all our seven years (not counting a third occasion of a very ambiguous character).", "칠 년을 함께 사는 동안 채찍을 쓴 건 딱 두 번뿐이었소(아주 애매한 성격의 세 번째 경우는 빼고)."], ["The first time, two months after our marriage, immediately after we arrived in the country, and the last time was that of which we are speaking.", "첫 번째는 결혼한 지 두 달 뒤, 시골에 막 내려간 직후였고, 마지막이 바로 지금 우리가 얘기하는 그 일이오."], ["Did you suppose I was such a monster, such a reactionary, such a slave driver?", "당신은 내가 그런 괴물에, 그런 반동분자에, 그런 농노 부리는 폭군이라고 생각했소?"], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["By the way, do you remember, Rodion Romanovitch, how a few years ago, in those days of beneficent publicity, a nobleman, I’ve forgotten his name, was put to shame everywhere, in all the papers, for having thrashed a German woman in the railway train.", "그건 그렇고, 로디온 로마노비치, 기억하시오? 몇 해 전, 그 자비로운 공개 비판의 시절에, 어느 귀족이 — 이름은 잊었소만 — 기차 안에서 독일 여자를 채찍질했다는 이유로 모든 신문에서, 사방에서 망신을 당한 일 말이오."], ["You remember?", "기억하시오?"], ["It was in those days, that very year I believe, the ‘disgraceful action of the _Age_’ took place (you know, ‘The Egyptian Nights,’ that public reading, you remember?", "바로 그 시절, 아마 그해였을 거요, '시대'지의 그 '치욕적인 사건'이 일어난 게(아시잖소, '이집트의 밤', 그 공개 낭독회 말이오, 기억하시오?"], ["The dark eyes, you know!", "그 검은 눈동자!"], ["Ah, the golden days of our youth, where are they?).", "아, 우리 젊은 날의 황금기여, 그 시절은 다 어디로 갔는지?)."], ["Well, as for the gentleman who thrashed the German, I feel no sympathy with him, because after all what need is there for sympathy?", "자, 그 독일 여자를 매질한 신사로 말하자면, 나는 그에게 아무런 동정도 느끼지 않소. 결국 동정할 게 뭐 있겠소?"], ["But I must say that there are sometimes such provoking ‘Germans’ that I don’t believe there is a progressive who could quite answer for himself.", "하지만 이건 말해 둬야겠소, 때때로 어찌나 약을 올리는 '독일 여자'들이 있는지, 자기 자신을 온전히 책임질 수 있는 진보주의자가 과연 있을지 의심스러울 정도요."], ["No one looked at the subject from that point of view then, but that’s the truly humane point of view, I assure you.”", "그 당시엔 아무도 그 일을 그런 관점에서 보지 않았지만, 그게야말로 진정으로 인도적인 관점이오, 정말이지.\""]], [["After saying this, Svidrigaïlov broke into a sudden laugh again.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 스비드리가일로프는 또다시 갑작스레 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Raskolnikov saw clearly that this was a man with a firm purpose in his mind and able to keep it to himself.", "라스콜니코프는 이 사내가 마음속에 굳은 목적을 품고 있으면서도 그것을 혼자 간직할 줄 아는 인간임을 또렷이 알아차렸다."]], [["“I expect you’ve not talked to anyone for some days?”", "\"당신은 며칠째 아무하고도 이야기를 안 한 것 같군요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Scarcely anyone.", "\"거의 아무하고도."], ["I suppose you are wondering at my being such an adaptable man?”", "내가 이렇게 잘 적응하는 사람이라는 게 신기하신가 보군?\""]], [["“No, I am only wondering at your being too adaptable a man.”", "\"아니, 그저 당신이 지나치게 적응을 잘하는 사람이라는 게 신기할 따름이오.\""]], [["“Because I am not offended at the rudeness of your questions?", "\"내가 당신의 무례한 질문들에 화를 내지 않아서 그렇소?"], ["Is that it?", "그거요?"], ["But why take offence?", "한데 화낼 게 뭐 있소?"], ["As you asked, so I answered,”", "당신이 물으니 내가 대답했을 뿐인걸.\""], ["he replied, with a surprising expression of simplicity.", "그가 놀라우리만치 순박한 표정으로 대답했다."], ["“You know, there’s hardly anything I take interest in,”", "\"있잖소, 내가 흥미를 느끼는 일이라곤 거의 아무것도 없소.\""], ["he went on, as it were dreamily, “especially now, I’ve nothing to do....", "그는 마치 꿈을 꾸듯 말을 이었다. \"특히 지금은, 할 일이 아무것도 없으니……."], ["You are quite at liberty to imagine though that I am making up to you with a motive, particularly as I told you I want to see your sister about something.", "물론 당신은 내가 어떤 속셈을 갖고 당신에게 아첨하는 거라고 상상할 자유가 있소, 더구나 당신 누이를 어떤 일로 만나고 싶다고 말했으니."], ["But I’ll confess frankly, I am very much bored.", "하지만 솔직히 고백하자면, 나는 몹시 따분하오."], ["The last three days especially, so I am delighted to see you....", "특히 지난 사흘이 그랬소, 그래서 당신을 만나니 더없이 반갑소……."], ["Don’t be angry, Rodion Romanovitch, but you seem to be somehow awfully strange yourself.", "화내지 마시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 한데 당신이야말로 어쩐지 끔찍하게 이상해 보이오."], ["Say what you like, there’s something wrong with you, and now, too... not this very minute, I mean, but now, generally....", "뭐라 하든, 당신에겐 어딘가 잘못된 데가 있소, 지금도……, 바로 이 순간을 말하는 게 아니라, 그냥 요즘 들어 전반적으로 말이오……."], ["Well, well, I won’t, I won’t, don’t scowl!", "자, 자, 그만하겠소, 그만, 인상 쓰지 마시오!"], ["I am not such a bear, you know, as you think.”", "나는 당신이 생각하는 만큼 그렇게 곰 같은 인간이 아니오.\""]], [["Raskolnikov looked gloomily at him.", "라스콜니코프가 침울하게 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“You are not a bear, perhaps, at all,” he said.", "\"당신은 어쩌면 전혀 곰 같은 인간이 아닌지도 모르오.\""], ["“I fancy indeed that you are a man of very good breeding, or at least know how on occasion to behave like one.”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 보기엔 오히려 당신은 매우 교양 있는 사람이거나, 적어도 필요할 때 그렇게 처신할 줄 아는 사람 같소.\""]], [["“I am not particularly interested in anyone’s opinion,”", "\"나는 누구의 의견에도 별 관심이 없소.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov answered, dryly and even with a shade of haughtiness, “and therefore why not be vulgar at times when vulgarity is such a convenient cloak for our climate...", "스비드리가일로프가 무뚝뚝하게, 심지어 약간 거만한 기색까지 띠며 대답했다. \"그러니 우리 기후에서는 천박함이 그토록 편리한 외투가 되어 주는데, 어찌 때때로 천박해지지 않을 수 있겠소……."], ["and especially if one has a natural propensity that way,”", "게다가 본디 그쪽으로 타고난 기질이 있다면 더더욱.\""], ["he added, laughing again.", "그가 또다시 웃으며 덧붙였다."]], [["“But I’ve heard you have many friends here.", "\"하지만 듣자 하니 당신은 여기 친구가 많다더군요. 사람들 말마따나 '연줄이 없지 않은'"], ["You are, as they say, ‘not without connections.’ What can you want with me, then, unless you’ve some special object?”", "사람이라죠. 그렇다면 특별한 목적이 없는 한, 도대체 나한테서 무얼 바라는 거요?\""]], [["“That’s true that I have friends here,”", "\"내가 여기 친구들이 있다는 건 사실이오.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov admitted, not replying to the chief point.", "스비드리가일로프가 핵심에는 답하지 않은 채 인정했다."], ["“I’ve met some already.", "\"벌써 몇 사람 만나기도 했고."], ["I’ve been lounging about for the last three days, and I’ve seen them, or they’ve seen me.", "사흘 동안 어슬렁거리며 돌아다녔으니, 그들을 보았거나 아니면 그들이 나를 보았지요."], ["That’s a matter of course.", "그건 당연한 일이오."], ["I am well dressed and reckoned not a poor man; the emancipation of the serfs hasn’t affected me; my property consists chiefly of forests and water meadows.", "나는 옷차림도 번듯하고 가난한 사람으로 여겨지지도 않으니까. 농노 해방도 내겐 영향이 없었소."], ["The revenue has not fallen off; but...", "내 재산은 주로 삼림과 물대는 초원으로 이루어져 있어 수입이 줄지 않았으니."], ["I am not going to see them, I was sick of them long ago.", "하지만…… 나는 그들을 만나러 가지 않을 거요, 이미 오래전에 진절머리가 났소."], ["I’ve been here three days and have called on no one....", "여기 온 지 사흘인데 아무도 찾아가지 않았소……."], ["What a town it is!", "이게 무슨 도시란 말이오!"], ["How has it come into existence among us, tell me that?", "어떻게 우리한테 이런 게 생겨났는지, 말 좀 해 보시오?"], ["A town of officials and students of all sorts.", "온갖 종류의 관리와 학생들의 도시지."], ["Yes, there’s a great deal I didn’t notice when I was here eight years ago, kicking up my heels....", "그렇소, 팔 년 전 내가 여기서 빈둥거리며 발길질이나 해 댈 때는 미처 눈치채지 못한 게 한둘이 아니오……."], ["My only hope now is in anatomy, by Jove, it is!”", "이제 내 유일한 희망은 해부학에 있소, 정말이지, 그렇소!\""]], [["“Anatomy?”", "\"해부학이라고요?\""]], [["“But as for these clubs, Dussauts, parades, or progress, indeed, maybe--well, all that can go on without me,”", "\"한데 그 클럽이니, 뒤소 식당이니, 열병식이니, 진보니 하는 것들은, 그래, 어쩌면, 뭐 — 그런 건 다 나 없이도 굴러갈 수 있소.\""], ["he went on, again without noticing the question.", "그는 또다시 질문을 못 들은 척 말을 이었다."], ["“Besides, who wants to be a card-sharper?”", "\"게다가, 누가 사기 도박꾼 노릇을 하고 싶겠소?\""]], [["“Why, have you been a card-sharper then?”", "\"아니, 그럼 당신은 사기 도박꾼이었소?\""]], [["“How could I help being?", "\"어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠소?"], ["There was a regular set of us, men of the best society, eight years ago; we had a fine time.", "팔 년 전엔 우리끼리 제대로 된 패거리가 있었소, 최상류 사회의 인사들이었지. 우린 멋진 시절을 보냈소."], ["And all men of breeding, you know, poets, men of property.", "게다가 다들 교양 있는 사람들이었지요, 알겠소, 시인이며 재산가들 말이오."], ["And indeed as a rule in our Russian society the best manners are found among those who’ve been thrashed, have you noticed that?", "사실 우리 러시아 사회에서는 대체로 매를 맞아 본 자들에게서 가장 훌륭한 예법을 찾아볼 수 있는데, 당신은 그걸 눈치챈 적 있소?"], ["I’ve deteriorated in the country.", "나는 시골에서 망가져 버렸소."], ["But I did get into prison for debt, through a low Greek who came from Nezhin.", "한데 네진에서 온 어느 비열한 그리스인 때문에 빚을 지고 감옥에 갇히는 신세가 됐지."], ["Then Marfa Petrovna turned up; she bargained with him and bought me off for thirty thousand silver pieces (I owed seventy thousand).", "그때 마르파 페트로브나가 나타나서는, 그자와 흥정을 벌여 은화 삼만 냥에 나를 빼내 줬소(난 칠만 냥을 빚지고 있었지)."], ["We were united in lawful wedlock and she bore me off into the country like a treasure.", "우린 합법적인 혼인으로 맺어졌고, 그녀는 나를 무슨 보물이라도 되는 양 시골로 데려갔소."], ["You know she was five years older than I.", "알다시피 그녀는 나보다 다섯 살 위였소."], ["She was very fond of me.", "나를 무척 사랑했지."], ["For seven years I never left the country.", "칠 년 동안 나는 시골을 한 번도 떠나지 않았소."], ["And, take note, that all my life she held a document over me, the IOU for thirty thousand roubles, so if I were to elect to be restive about anything I should be trapped at once!", "그리고, 눈여겨보시오, 그녀는 평생토록 삼만 루블짜리 차용증서라는 문서를 내 머리 위에 쥐고 있었소, 그러니 내가 무슨 일로든 반항하기로 마음먹는다면 당장 덫에 걸리는 셈이었지!"], ["And she would have done it!", "게다가 그녀라면 정말 그렇게 했을 거요!"], ["Women find nothing incompatible in that.”", "여자들은 그런 데서 아무런 모순도 느끼지 않으니까.\""]], [["“If it hadn’t been for that, would you have given her the slip?”", "\"그게 아니었다면, 당신은 그녀를 버리고 달아났겠지요?\""]], [["“I don’t know what to say.", "\"뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠소."], ["It was scarcely the document restrained me.", "나를 붙잡아 둔 게 그 문서였던 것 같지는 않소."], ["I didn’t want to go anywhere else.", "나는 딱히 다른 어디로 가고 싶지도 않았소."], ["Marfa Petrovna herself invited me to go abroad, seeing I was bored, but I’ve been abroad before, and always felt sick there.", "내가 따분해하는 걸 보고 마르파 페트로브나가 직접 외국에 다녀오라고 권하기도 했지만, 나는 전에도 외국에 가 봤고 늘 거기서는 속이 메스꺼웠소."], ["For no reason, but the sunrise, the bay of Naples, the sea--you look at them and it makes you sad.", "별 이유도 없이, 그저 일출이며, 나폴리 만이며, 바다며 — 그런 걸 바라보고 있으면 서글퍼지더란 말이오."], ["What’s most revolting is that one is really sad!", "가장 역겨운 건, 정말로 서글퍼진다는 거지!"], ["No, it’s better at home.", "아니오, 고향이 더 낫소."], ["Here at least one blames others for everything and excuses oneself.", "적어도 여기선 만사를 남 탓으로 돌리고 자기 자신은 변명할 수 있으니까."], ["I should have gone perhaps on an expedition to the North Pole, because _j’ai le vin mauvais_ and hate drinking, and there’s nothing left but wine.", "어쩌면 나는 북극 탐험이라도 떠났을지 모르오, 왜냐하면 나는 술버릇이 사나운 데다 술 마시는 걸 싫어하는데, 그런데도 남은 거라곤 술밖에 없으니."], ["I have tried it.", "시도는 해 봤소."], ["But, I say, I’ve been told Berg is going up in a great balloon next Sunday from the Yusupov Garden and will take up passengers at a fee.", "한데, 이거 말이오, 듣자 하니 다음 일요일에 베르크가 유수포프 정원에서 거대한 기구를 타고 올라간다는데, 요금을 받고 손님도 태운다더군. 사실이오?\""], ["Is it true?”", "\"아니, 당신이 타고 올라가기라도 하려고요?\""]], [["“Why, would you go up?” “I...", "\"내가……, 아니, 오, 아니오.\""], ["No, oh, no,” muttered Svidrigaïlov really seeming to be deep in thought.", "스비드리가일로프가 정말로 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯한 표정으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“What does he mean?", "'이게 무슨 소린가?"], ["Is he in earnest?”", "진심인가?'"], ["Raskolnikov wondered.", "라스콜니코프는 의아해했다."]], [["“No, the document didn’t restrain me,”", "\"아니, 나를 붙잡아 둔 건 그 문서가 아니었소.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov went on, meditatively.", "스비드리가일로프가 생각에 잠긴 채 말을 이었다."], ["“It was my own doing, not leaving the country, and nearly a year ago Marfa Petrovna gave me back the document on my name-day and made me a present of a considerable sum of money, too.", "\"시골을 떠나지 않은 건 내 뜻이었소, 그리고 거의 일 년 전에 마르파 페트로브나는 내 명명일에 그 문서를 도로 돌려주었고, 게다가 상당한 액수의 돈까지 선물해 주었소."], ["She had a fortune, you know.", "알다시피 그녀는 재산이 있었으니까."], ["‘You see how I trust you, Arkady Ivanovitch’--that was actually her expression.", "'아르카디 이바노비치, 내가 당신을 이만큼이나 믿는다는 걸 알겠지요' — 실제로 그녀가 한 말이오."], ["You don’t believe she used it?", "그녀가 그런 말을 썼다는 게 믿기지 않소?"], ["But do you know I managed the estate quite decently, they know me in the neighbourhood.", "한데 말이오, 나는 영지를 꽤 점잖게 관리했소, 인근에서도 다들 나를 인정하지요."], ["I ordered books, too.", "책도 주문했고."], ["Marfa Petrovna at first approved, but afterwards she was afraid of my over-studying.”", "마르파 페트로브나는 처음엔 찬성했지만, 나중엔 내가 공부를 지나치게 할까 봐 두려워했소.\""]], [["“You seem to be missing Marfa Petrovna very much?”", "\"당신은 마르파 페트로브나를 무척 그리워하는 것 같군요?\""]], [["“Missing her?", "\"그녀를 그리워한다고?"], ["Perhaps.", "어쩌면."], ["Really, perhaps I am.", "정말로, 어쩌면 그런지도 모르오."], ["And, by the way, do you believe in ghosts?”", "그건 그렇고, 당신은 유령을 믿소?\""]], [["“What ghosts?”", "\"무슨 유령 말이오?\""]], [["“Why, ordinary ghosts.”", "\"아니, 그냥 흔한 유령 말이오.\""]], [["“Do you believe in them?”", "\"당신은 유령을 믿소?\""]], [["“Perhaps not, _pour vous plaire_....", "\"어쩌면 안 믿는지도, 당신 마음에 들자고 하는 말이오만……."], ["I wouldn’t say no exactly.”", "딱 잘라 안 믿는다고는 못 하겠소.\""]], [["“Do you see them, then?”", "\"그렇다면 당신은 유령을 본다는 거요?\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov looked at him rather oddly.", "스비드리가일로프가 어쩐지 묘한 눈초리로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Marfa Petrovna is pleased to visit me,”", "\"마르파 페트로브나가 황송하게도 나를 찾아오신다오.\""], ["he said, twisting his mouth into a strange smile.", "그가 입을 비틀어 이상한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean ‘she is pleased to visit you’?”", "\"'황송하게도 당신을 찾아온다'니, 그게 무슨 말이오?\""]], [["“She has been three times.", "\"세 번 찾아왔소."], ["I saw her first on the very day of the funeral, an hour after she was buried.", "처음 본 건 장례식 바로 그날, 그녀를 묻은 지 한 시간 뒤였소."], ["It was the day before I left to come here.", "내가 여기로 떠나기 전날이었지."], ["The second time was the day before yesterday, at daybreak, on the journey at the station of Malaya Vishera, and the third time was two hours ago in the room where I am staying.", "두 번째는 그저께, 동틀 무렵, 오는 길에 말라야 비셰라 역에서였고, 세 번째는 두 시간 전, 내가 묵고 있는 방에서였소."], ["I was alone.”", "나는 혼자였소.\""]], [["“Were you awake?”", "\"당신은 깨어 있었소?\""]], [["“Quite awake.", "\"완전히 깨어 있었소."], ["I was wide awake every time.", "매번 정신이 또렷했소."], ["She comes, speaks to me for a minute and goes out at the door--always at the door.", "그녀가 와서, 잠시 내게 말을 걸고는, 문으로 나가지요 — 늘 문으로."], ["I can almost hear her.”", "그녀의 기척이 거의 들릴 정도요.\""]], [["“What made me think that something of the sort must be happening to you?”", "\"왜 나는 당신에게 그런 비슷한 일이 일어나고 있는 게 틀림없다고 생각했을까?\""], ["Raskolnikov said suddenly.", "라스콜니코프가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["At the same moment he was surprised at having said it.", "그러고는 그 순간 자신이 그런 말을 했다는 데 스스로 놀랐다."], ["He was much excited.", "그는 몹시 흥분해 있었다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Did you think so?”", "당신이 그렇게 생각했다고?\""], ["Svidrigaïlov asked in astonishment.", "스비드리가일로프가 놀라서 물었다."], ["“Did you really?", "\"정말로?"], ["Didn’t I say that there was something in common between us, eh?”", "우리 둘 사이에 뭔가 공통점이 있다고 내가 말하지 않았소, 응?\""]], [["“You never said so!”", "\"당신은 그런 말 한 적 없소!\""], ["Raskolnikov cried sharply and with heat.", "라스콜니코프가 날카롭게, 발끈하며 외쳤다. \"안 했던가?\""]], [["“Didn’t I?”", "\"안 했소!\""]], [["“No!” “I thought I did.", "\"한 줄 알았는데."], ["When I came in and saw you lying with your eyes shut, pretending, I said to myself at once, ‘Here’s the man.’”", "내가 들어와서 당신이 눈을 감고 자는 척하며 누워 있는 걸 보았을 때, 나는 곧장 속으로 말했소, '바로 이 사람이로군' 하고.\""]], [["“What do you mean by ‘the man?’ What are you talking about?”", "\"'이 사람'이라니 그게 무슨 뜻이오? 도대체 무슨 소릴 하는 거요?\""], ["cried Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 외쳤다."]], [["“What do I mean?", "\"무슨 뜻이냐고?"], ["I really don’t know....”", "나도 정말 모르겠소…….\""], ["Svidrigaïlov muttered ingenuously, as though he, too, were puzzled.", "스비드리가일로프가, 마치 그 자신도 어리둥절하다는 듯 순박하게 중얼거렸다."]], [["For a minute they were silent.", "잠시 두 사람은 말이 없었다."], ["They stared in each other’s faces.", "그들은 서로의 얼굴을 빤히 응시했다."]], [["“That’s all nonsense!”", "\"다 헛소리야!\""], ["Raskolnikov shouted with vexation.", "라스콜니코프가 짜증스럽게 소리쳤다."], ["“What does she say when she comes to you?”", "\"그녀가 당신을 찾아오면 무슨 말을 하오?\""]], [["“She!", "\"그녀가!"], ["Would you believe it, she talks of the silliest trifles and--man is a strange creature--it makes me angry.", "믿어지겠소, 더없이 시시한 자질구레한 얘기를 한단 말이오, 그런데 — 인간이란 묘한 존재라 — 그게 나를 화나게 하오."], ["The first time she came in (I was tired you know: the funeral service, the funeral ceremony, the lunch afterwards.", "처음 들어왔을 때(나는 지쳐 있었소, 알겠소, 장례 예배, 장례 의식, 그러고 나서 점심까지."], ["At last I was left alone in my study.", "마침내 나는 서재에 혼자 남게 됐지."], ["I lighted a cigar and began to think), she came in at the door.", "시가에 불을 붙이고 생각에 잠기기 시작했소), 그녀가 문으로 들어왔소."], ["‘You’ve been so busy to-day, Arkady Ivanovitch, you have forgotten to wind the dining-room clock,’ she said.", "'아르카디 이바노비치, 당신 오늘 너무 바빠서 식당 시계 태엽 감는 걸 잊었어요' 하고 말이오."], ["All those seven years I’ve wound that clock every week, and if I forgot it she would always remind me.", "칠 년 내내 나는 매주 그 시계의 태엽을 감았고, 내가 잊을라치면 그녀가 늘 일깨워 주곤 했소."], ["The next day I set off on my way here.", "이튿날 나는 여기로 길을 떠났소."], ["I got out at the station at daybreak; I’d been asleep, tired out, with my eyes half open, I was drinking some coffee.", "동틀 무렵 역에 내렸지. 지쳐서 눈을 반쯤 뜬 채 졸고 있다가 커피를 마시고 있었소."], ["I looked up and there was suddenly Marfa Petrovna sitting beside me with a pack of cards in her hands.", "문득 고개를 들어 보니, 난데없이 마르파 페트로브나가 카드 한 벌을 손에 들고 내 곁에 앉아 있는 거요. '여행길 운수를 점쳐 드릴까요, 아르카디 이바노비치?'"], ["‘Shall I tell your fortune for the journey, Arkady Ivanovitch?’ She was a great hand at telling fortunes.", "그녀는 점치는 데 아주 능했소. 그때 그녀에게 점쳐 달라고 청하지 않은 걸 나는 영영 자신을 용서하지 못할 거요."], ["I shall never forgive myself for not asking her to.", "나는 겁에 질려 달아났소, 게다가 마침 종이 울리기도 했고."], ["I ran away in a fright, and, besides, the bell rang.", "오늘 나는 싸구려 음식점에서 시원찮은 점심을 먹고 몹시 더부룩한 기분으로 앉아 있었소."], ["I was sitting to-day, feeling very heavy after a miserable dinner from a cookshop; I was sitting smoking, all of a sudden Marfa Petrovna again.", "담배를 피우며 앉아 있는데, 난데없이 또 마르파 페트로브나가. 그녀는 긴 자락이 달린 새 초록색 비단 드레스를 차려입고 무척 맵시 있게 들어왔소."], ["She came in very smart in a new green silk dress with a long train.", "'안녕하세요, 아르카디 이바노비치! 내 드레스 어때요?"], ["‘Good day, Arkady Ivanovitch! How do you like my dress?", "아니스카는 이런 건 못 짓지요.'(아니스카는 시골의 침모로, 본디 우리 농노 처녀였는데 모스크바에서 솜씨를 익혔소, 예쁜 계집애였지.) 그녀는 내 앞에서 빙글빙글 돌아 보였소."], ["Aniska can’t make like this.’ (Aniska was a dressmaker in the country, one of our former serf girls who had been trained in Moscow, a pretty wench.) She stood turning round before me.", "나는 드레스를 보고, 그러고 나서 그녀의 얼굴을 유심히, 아주 유심히 들여다봤소. '마르파 페트로브나, 당신이 이런 자질구레한 일로 나를 찾아오느라 수고하다니 신기하구려.'"], ["I looked at the dress, and then I looked carefully, very carefully, at her face.", "'어머나, 당신은 무슨 일로든 사람이 당신을 귀찮게 하는 걸 못 견디는군요!' 그녀를 놀려 줄 셈으로 내가 말했소."], ["‘I wonder you trouble to come to me about such trifles, Marfa Petrovna.’ ‘Good gracious, you won’t let one disturb you about anything!’ To tease her I said, ‘I want to get married, Marfa Petrovna.’ ‘That’s just like you, Arkady Ivanovitch; it does you very little credit to come looking for a bride when you’ve hardly buried your wife.", "'나는 결혼하고 싶소, 마르파 페트로브나.' '딱 당신답군요, 아르카디 이바노비치, 아내를 묻기가 무섭게 신붓감을 찾아 나서다니 그게 무슨 꼴이람."], ["And if you could make a good choice, at least, but I know it won’t be for your happiness or hers, you will only be a laughing-stock to all good people.’ Then she went out and her train seemed to rustle.", "게다가 적어도 좋은 선택이라도 하면 모를까, 나는 알아요, 그게 당신 행복도, 그 여자 행복도 되지 못하고, 그저 모든 선량한 사람들의 웃음거리만 될 거라는 걸요.' 그러고는 나가 버렸는데, 자락이 사락거리는 소리가 들리는 것 같았소."], ["Isn’t it nonsense, eh?”", "이거 헛소리 아니오, 응?\""]], [["“But perhaps you are telling lies?”", "\"하지만 혹시 거짓말하고 있는 건 아니오?\""], ["Raskolnikov put in.", "라스콜니코프가 끼어들었다."]], [["“I rarely lie,”", "\"나는 거짓말을 좀처럼 안 하오.\""], ["answered Svidrigaïlov thoughtfully, apparently not noticing the rudeness of the question.", "스비드리가일로프가, 그 질문의 무례함은 알아채지 못한 듯, 생각에 잠겨 대답했다."]], [["“And in the past, have you ever seen ghosts before?”", "\"그러면 그 전에도, 예전에 유령을 본 적이 있소?\""]], [["“Y-yes, I have seen them, but only once in my life, six years ago.", "\"그, 그렇소, 본 적이 있긴 하오, 한데 평생 딱 한 번, 육 년 전이었소. 내겐 필카라는 농노가 있었지."], ["I had a serf, Filka; just after his burial I called out forgetting ‘Filka, my pipe!’ He came in and went to the cupboard where my pipes were.", "그를 묻은 직후에, 나는 깜빡 잊고 '필카, 내 파이프!' 하고 불렀소."], ["I sat still and thought ‘he is doing it out of revenge,’ because we had a violent quarrel just before his death.", "그가 들어와서 내 파이프들이 있는 찬장 쪽으로 가더군. 나는 가만히 앉아서 생각했소, '복수하느라 저러는구나'"], ["‘How dare you come in with a hole in your elbow?’ I said.", "하고, 왜냐하면 그가 죽기 직전에 우린 격렬하게 다퉜으니까."], ["‘Go away, you scamp!’ He turned and went out, and never came again.", "'어디 감히 팔꿈치에 구멍 난 꼴로 들어오느냐?' 하고 내가 말했소."], ["I didn’t tell Marfa Petrovna at the time.", "'꺼져, 이 망나니야!' 그가 돌아서 나가더니, 다시는 오지 않았소."], ["I wanted to have a service sung for him, but I was ashamed.”", "그때 나는 마르파 페트로브나에게 그 얘기를 하지 않았소. 그를 위해 추도 예배를 올려 주고 싶었지만, 부끄러웠소.\""]], [["“You should go to a doctor.”", "\"의사한테 가 봐야겠소.\""]], [["“I know I am not well, without your telling me, though I don’t know what’s wrong; I believe I am five times as strong as you are.", "\"당신이 말해 주지 않아도 내가 성치 않다는 건 알고 있소, 어디가 잘못된 건진 모르겠지만 말이오. 나는 당신보다 다섯 배는 더 튼튼할 거요."], ["I didn’t ask you whether you believe that ghosts are seen, but whether you believe that they exist.”", "나는 당신에게 유령이 보이는 걸 믿느냐고 물은 게 아니라, 유령이 존재한다는 걸 믿느냐고 물은 거요.\""]], [["“No, I won’t believe it!”", "\"아니, 난 그런 거 안 믿소!\""], ["Raskolnikov cried, with positive anger.", "라스콜니코프가 영락없는 분노에 차 외쳤다."]], [["“What do people generally say?”", "\"사람들이 보통 뭐라고 하오?\""], ["muttered Svidrigaïlov, as though speaking to himself, looking aside and bowing his head.", "스비드리가일로프가, 마치 혼잣말하듯, 곁눈질을 하며 고개를 숙인 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“They say, ‘You are ill, so what appears to you is only unreal fantasy.’ But that’s not strictly logical.", "\"이렇게들 말하지요, '당신은 병들었다, 그러니 당신에게 보이는 건 실재하지 않는 환영일 뿐이다'라고. 하지만 그건 엄밀히 논리적이지 않소."], ["I agree that ghosts only appear to the sick, but that only proves that they are unable to appear except to the sick, not that they don’t exist.”", "유령이 오로지 병든 자에게만 나타난다는 데는 나도 동의하오, 하지만 그건 단지 유령이 병든 자에게만 나타날 수 있다는 걸 입증할 뿐, 유령이 존재하지 않는다는 걸 입증하진 못하오.\""]], [["“Nothing of the sort,”", "\"천만의 말씀.\""], ["Raskolnikov insisted irritably.", "라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 우겼다."]], [["“No? You don’t think so?”", "\"아니라고?"], ["Svidrigaïlov went on, looking at him deliberately.", "그렇게 생각 안 하오?\" 스비드리가일로프가 그를 찬찬히 바라보며 말을 이었다."], ["“But what do you say to this argument (help me with it): ghosts are, as it were, shreds and fragments of other worlds, the beginning of them.", "\"한데 이런 논리에는 뭐라 하겠소(나 좀 거들어 주시오), 유령이란 말하자면 다른 세계들의 자투리요 조각이며, 그 세계들의 시작이라고 한다면."], ["A man in health has, of course, no reason to see them, because he is above all a man of this earth and is bound for the sake of completeness and order to live only in this life.", "건강한 사람은 물론 그것들을 볼 이유가 없소, 왜냐하면 그는 무엇보다도 이 지상의 인간이며, 완전함과 질서를 위하여 오직 이 삶 속에서만 살아가도록 묶여 있으니까."], ["But as soon as one is ill, as soon as the normal earthly order of the organism is broken, one begins to realise the possibility of another world; and the more seriously ill one is, the closer becomes one’s contact with that other world, so that as soon as the man dies he steps straight into that world.", "하지만 병이 들기가 무섭게, 유기체의 정상적인 지상의 질서가 깨지기가 무섭게, 사람은 다른 세계의 가능성을 깨닫기 시작하오. 그리고 더 심하게 앓을수록 그 다른 세계와의 접촉은 더 긴밀해지지요, 그래서 사람이 죽기가 무섭게 곧장 그 세계로 발을 들이게 되는 거요."], ["I thought of that long ago.", "나는 오래전부터 이 생각을 했소."], ["If you believe in a future life, you could believe in that, too.”", "당신이 내세를 믿는다면, 이것도 믿을 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“I don’t believe in a future life,”", "\"나는 내세를 안 믿소.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov sat lost in thought.", "스비드리가일로프는 생각에 잠긴 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["“And what if there are only spiders there, or something of that sort,”", "\"그런데 만약 거기에 거미들이나 그 비슷한 것만 있다면 어떻겠소?\""], ["he said suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“He is a madman,”", "'미친 인간이로군.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."]], [["“We always imagine eternity as something beyond our conception, something vast, vast!", "\"우린 늘 영원을 우리 머리로는 헤아릴 수 없는 무언가로, 거대한, 거대한 무언가로 상상하지!"], ["But why must it be vast?", "하지만 어째서 꼭 거대해야 하오?"], ["Instead of all that, what if it’s one little room, like a bath house in the country, black and grimy and spiders in every corner, and that’s all eternity is?", "그 모든 것 대신, 만약 그게 작은 방 하나라면, 시골의 목욕탕 같은, 검댕과 때투성이에 구석마다 거미가 우글거리는, 그런 거라면, 그리고 영원이라는 게 고작 그뿐이라면 어떻겠소?"], ["I sometimes fancy it like that.”", "나는 가끔 그것을 그렇게 그려 보곤 하오.\""]], [["“Can it be you can imagine nothing juster and more comforting than that?”", "\"당신은 정녕 그보다 더 옳고 더 위안이 되는 무언가는 상상조차 못 한단 말이오?\""], ["Raskolnikov cried, with a feeling of anguish.", "라스콜니코프가 고뇌에 찬 심정으로 외쳤다."]], [["“Juster?", "\"더 옳다고?"], ["And how can we tell, perhaps that is just, and do you know it’s what I would certainly have made it,”", "한데 어떻게 알겠소, 어쩌면 그게 옳은 것일지도, 게다가 말이오, 내가 만들었더라도 분명 그렇게 만들었을 거요.\""], ["answered Svidrigaïlov, with a vague smile.", "스비드리가일로프가 어렴풋한 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["This horrible answer sent a cold chill through Raskolnikov.", "이 끔찍한 대답에 라스콜니코프는 등골이 서늘해졌다."], ["Svidrigaïlov raised his head, looked at him, and suddenly began laughing.", "스비드리가일로프는 고개를 들어 그를 바라보더니, 갑자기 웃기 시작했다."]], [["“Only think,”", "\"생각해 보시오.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["he cried, “half an hour ago we had never seen each other, we regarded each other as enemies; there is a matter unsettled between us; we’ve thrown it aside, and away we’ve gone into the abstract!", "\"반 시간 전만 해도 우린 서로 본 적도 없고, 서로를 적으로 여겼소. 우리 사이엔 매듭짓지 못한 일이 있소."], ["Wasn’t I right in saying that we were birds of a feather?”", "그런데 우린 그걸 옆으로 밀쳐 두고는, 추상적인 얘기로 빠져 버렸지! 우리가 한통속이라고 한 내 말이 옳지 않았소?\""]], [["“Kindly allow me,”", "\"부디 허락해 주시오.\""], ["Raskolnikov went on irritably, “to ask you to explain why you have honoured me with your visit...", "라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 말을 이었다."], ["and... and I am in a hurry, I have no time to waste.", "\"당신이 무슨 영광스러운 일로 나를 찾아왔는지 설명해 달라고 청하는 걸 말이오……, 그리고……, 그리고 나는 급하오, 허비할 시간이 없소."], ["I want to go out.”", "나가 봐야 하오.\""]], [["“By all means, by all means.", "\"여부가 있겠소, 여부가."], ["Your sister, Avdotya Romanovna, is going to be married to Mr. Luzhin, Pyotr Petrovitch?”", "당신 누이 아브도티야 로마노브나가 표트르 페트로비치 루진 씨와 결혼한다지요?\""]], [["“Can you refrain from any question about my sister and from mentioning her name?", "\"내 누이에 관한 질문은 일절 삼가고, 그 이름도 입에 올리지 말아 주겠소?"], ["I can’t understand how you dare utter her name in my presence, if you really are Svidrigaïlov.”", "당신이 정말 스비드리가일로프라면, 어떻게 감히 내 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 담을 수 있는지 도무지 이해가 안 되오.\""]], [["“Why, but I’ve come here to speak about her; how can I avoid mentioning her?”", "\"아니, 하지만 나는 바로 그녀에 관해 얘기하러 여기 온 거요. 그러니 어찌 그녀 이름을 입에 올리지 않을 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Very good, speak, but make haste.”", "\"좋소, 말해 보시오, 단 서두르시오.\""]], [["“I am sure that you must have formed your own opinion of this Mr.", "\"당신이 이 루진 씨를 단 반 시간이라도 만나 봤거나 그에 관해 무슨 사실이라도 들었다면, 그에 대해 나름의 의견을 세웠으리라고 나는 확신하오."], ["Luzhin, who is a connection of mine through my wife, if you have only seen him for half an hour, or heard any facts about him. He is no match for Avdotya Romanovna.", "그는 내 아내를 통해 나와 인척이 되는 사람인데, 아브도티야 로마노브나에겐 어울리는 짝이 못 되오."], ["I believe Avdotya Romanovna is sacrificing herself generously and imprudently for the sake of... for the sake of her family.", "내 생각엔 아브도티야 로마노브나가……, 가족을 위하여, 가족을 위하여 자신을 너그럽게, 그러나 분별없이 희생하려는 것 같소."], ["I fancied from all I had heard of you that you would be very glad if the match could be broken off without the sacrifice of worldly advantages.", "당신에 관해 들은 모든 걸 종합해 볼 때, 나는 세속적인 이득을 희생하지 않고도 이 혼사를 깨뜨릴 수 있다면 당신이 무척 기뻐하리라고 짐작했소."], ["Now I know you personally, I am convinced of it.”", "이제 당신을 직접 알게 되니, 그 확신이 더 굳어졌소.\""]], [["“All this is very naïve... excuse me, I should have said impudent on your part,”", "\"이 모든 게 무척 순진하군요……, 실례지만, 뻔뻔하다고 말했어야겠소, 당신 쪽에서 말이오.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["“You mean to say that I am seeking my own ends.", "\"내가 내 잇속을 챙기려 한다는 말이군."], ["Don’t be uneasy, Rodion Romanovitch, if I were working for my own advantage, I would not have spoken out so directly.", "마음 놓으시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 내가 내 이득을 위해 일하는 거라면, 이토록 노골적으로 털어놓진 않았을 거요."], ["I am not quite a fool.", "나는 그렇게까지 바보는 아니오."], ["I will confess something psychologically curious about that: just now, defending my love for Avdotya Romanovna, I said I was myself the victim.", "그 점에 관해 심리적으로 흥미로운 걸 하나 고백하겠소. 방금 아브도티야 로마노브나를 향한 내 사랑을 변호하면서, 나는 나 자신이 희생자라고 말했소."], ["Well, let me tell you that I’ve no feeling of love now, not the slightest, so that I wonder myself indeed, for I really did feel something...”", "한데 말해 두지요, 지금 나는 사랑의 감정이라곤 없소, 손톱만큼도, 그래서 나 자신도 정말 의아할 정도요, 왜냐하면 정말로 뭔가를 느끼긴 했으니까…….\""]], [["“Through idleness and depravity,”", "\"한가함과 방탕 탓이겠지요.\""], ["Raskolnikov put in.", "라스콜니코프가 끼어들었다."]], [["“I certainly am idle and depraved, but your sister has such qualities that even I could not help being impressed by them.", "\"내가 한가하고 방탕한 인간이긴 하오, 하지만 당신 누이는 나 같은 인간조차 감명받지 않을 수 없을 만큼 훌륭한 자질을 지녔소."], ["But that’s all nonsense, as I see myself now.”", "하지만 그것도 다 헛소리지, 이제 나 자신이 알겠소.\""]], [["“Have you seen that long?”", "\"그걸 안 지 오래됐소?\""]], [["“I began to be aware of it before, but was only perfectly sure of it the day before yesterday, almost at the moment I arrived in Petersburg.", "\"전부터 어렴풋이 깨닫고 있었지만, 완전히 확신한 건 그저께, 거의 페테르부르크에 도착하는 바로 그 순간이었소."], ["I still fancied in Moscow, though, that I was coming to try to get Avdotya Romanovna’s hand and to cut out Mr. Luzhin.”", "그래도 모스크바에 있을 땐 아직, 아브도티야 로마노브나의 손을 얻으려, 루진 씨를 밀어내려 가는 거라고 여겼소만.\""]], [["“Excuse me for interrupting you; kindly be brief, and come to the object of your visit.", "\"말을 끊어 미안하오. 부디 짧게 줄여서, 방문의 목적으로 들어가 주시오."], ["I am in a hurry, I want to go out...”", "나는 급하오, 나가 봐야 하니까…….\""]], [["“With the greatest pleasure.", "\"기꺼이 그러지요."], ["On arriving here and determining on a certain... journey, I should like to make some necessary preliminary arrangements.", "여기 도착해서 어떤……, 여행을 결심하고 보니, 나는 필요한 사전 정리를 좀 해 두고 싶소."], ["I left my children with an aunt; they are well provided for; and they have no need of me personally.", "아이들은 이모에게 맡겨 두었소, 부족함 없이 지내고 있고, 나라는 사람이 직접 필요하지도 않소."], ["And a nice father I should make, too!", "사실 나라고 한들 좋은 아버지가 될 리도 없지!"], ["I have taken nothing but what Marfa Petrovna gave me a year ago.", "나는 일 년 전 마르파 페트로브나가 준 것 말고는 아무것도 가져오지 않았소."], ["That’s enough for me.", "그걸로 충분하오."], ["Excuse me, I am just coming to the point.", "실례, 이제 곧 본론으로 들어가리다."], ["Before the journey which may come off, I want to settle Mr. Luzhin, too.", "어쩌면 떠나게 될 그 여행에 앞서, 나는 루진 씨 문제도 매듭짓고 싶소."], ["It’s not that I detest him so much, but it was through him I quarrelled with Marfa Petrovna when I learned that she had dished up this marriage.", "그자가 그토록 밉살스러워서가 아니라, 마르파 페트로브나가 이 혼사를 꾸몄다는 걸 알게 됐을 때 그자 때문에 그녀와 다퉜기 때문이오."], ["I want now to see Avdotya Romanovna through your mediation, and if you like in your presence, to explain to her that in the first place she will never gain anything but harm from Mr. Luzhin.", "이제 나는 당신의 중개를 통해, 그리고 당신이 원한다면 당신이 보는 앞에서 아브도티야 로마노브나를 만나, 우선 그녀에게 루진 씨에게서는 해악 말고는 결코 아무것도 얻지 못하리라는 걸 설명해 주고 싶소."], ["Then, begging her pardon for all past unpleasantness, to make her a present of ten thousand roubles and so assist the rupture with Mr. Luzhin, a rupture to which I believe she is herself not disinclined, if she could see the way to it.”", "그러고 나서, 지난날의 온갖 불미스러운 일에 대해 그녀에게 용서를 구하고, 만 루블을 선물로 드려, 루진 씨와의 파혼을 도와주고 싶소, 그 파혼으로 말하자면, 그녀가 그 길을 찾을 수만 있다면 그녀 자신도 마다하지 않으리라고 나는 믿소.\""]], [["“You are certainly mad,”", "\"당신 정말 미쳤군.\""], ["cried Raskolnikov not so much angered as astonished.", "라스콜니코프가, 화가 났다기보다 어이없다는 듯 외쳤다."], ["“How dare you talk like that!”", "\"어떻게 감히 그런 말을 하는 거요!\""]], [["“I knew you would scream at me; but in the first place, though I am not rich, this ten thousand roubles is perfectly free; I have absolutely no need for it.", "\"당신이 내게 소리칠 줄 알았소. 하지만 우선, 내가 부자는 아니어도 이 만 루블은 완전히 여유 자금이라 내겐 전혀 필요가 없소."], ["If Avdotya Romanovna does not accept it, I shall waste it in some more foolish way.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 받지 않는다면, 나는 그걸 더 어리석은 데다 탕진해 버릴 거요."], ["That’s the first thing.", "그게 첫째요."], ["Secondly, my conscience is perfectly easy; I make the offer with no ulterior motive.", "둘째로, 내 양심은 더없이 편안하오, 나는 아무런 흑심 없이 이 제안을 하는 거요."], ["You may not believe it, but in the end Avdotya Romanovna and you will know.", "당신은 안 믿을지 몰라도, 결국엔 아브도티야 로마노브나도 당신도 알게 될 거요."], ["The point is, that I did actually cause your sister, whom I greatly respect, some trouble and unpleasantness, and so, sincerely regretting it, I want--not to compensate, not to repay her for the unpleasantness, but simply to do something to her advantage, to show that I am not, after all, privileged to do nothing but harm.", "요점은, 내가 깊이 존경하는 당신 누이에게 내가 실제로 얼마간의 곤란과 불미스러운 일을 끼쳤다는 것, 그래서 진심으로 그걸 뉘우치는 마음에서, 나는 — 보상하려는 것도, 그 불미스러움을 갚으려는 것도 아니라, 그저 그녀에게 이로운 무언가를 하고 싶을 뿐이오, 내가 결국 해악만 끼칠 특권을 지닌 인간은 아니라는 걸 보여 주고 싶은 거지. 내 제안에 백만분의 일이라도 사심이 있다면, 이토록 드러내 놓고 제안하진 않았을 거요."], ["If there were a millionth fraction of self-interest in my offer, I should not have made it so openly; and I should not have offered her ten thousand only, when five weeks ago I offered her more, Besides, I may, perhaps, very soon marry a young lady, and that alone ought to prevent suspicion of any design on Avdotya Romanovna.", "그리고 고작 만 루블만 제안하지도 않았을 거요, 다섯 주 전엔 그녀에게 더 많은 걸 제안했으니까. 게다가 나는 어쩌면 머잖아 어느 젊은 처녀와 결혼할지도 모르고, 그것 하나만으로도 내가 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 무슨 흑심을 품었다는 의심은 막아 줄 거요."], ["In conclusion, let me say that in marrying Mr. Luzhin, she is taking money just the same, only from another man.", "끝으로 한마디 하자면, 그녀가 루진 씨와 결혼한다 해도 마찬가지로 돈을 받는 셈이오, 다만 다른 남자에게서 받는 것일 뿐."], ["Don’t be angry, Rodion Romanovitch, think it over coolly and quietly.”", "화내지 마시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 냉정하고 차분하게 곰곰이 생각해 보시오.\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov himself was exceedingly cool and quiet as he was saying this.", "이 말을 하는 스비드리가일로프 자신은 더없이 냉정하고 차분했다."]], [["“I beg you to say no more,”", "\"더는 말하지 말아 주시오.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."], ["“In any case this is unpardonable impertinence.”", "\"어쨌든 이건 용서할 수 없는 무례요.\""]], [["“Not in the least.", "\"천만에."], ["Then a man may do nothing but harm to his neighbour in this world, and is prevented from doing the tiniest bit of good by trivial conventional formalities.", "그렇다면 인간은 이 세상에서 이웃에게 해악만 끼칠 수 있을 뿐, 시시한 관습적 형식 따위에 가로막혀 아주 손톱만큼의 선조차 베풀지 못한단 말이오."], ["That’s absurd.", "터무니없는 일이오."], ["If I died, for instance, and left that sum to your sister in my will, surely she wouldn’t refuse it?”", "가령 내가 죽으면서 유언으로 그 돈을 당신 누이에게 남긴다면, 설마 그녀가 그걸 거절하겠소?\""]], [["“Very likely she would.”", "\"아마 거절할 거요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, indeed.", "\"오, 천만에, 정말로."], ["However, if you refuse it, so be it, though ten thousand roubles is a capital thing to have on occasion.", "하지만, 당신이 거절한다면야 할 수 없지, 그래도 만 루블이란 때에 따라 꽤 요긴한 물건인데."], ["In any case I beg you to repeat what I have said to Avdotya Romanovna.”", "어쨌든 부탁이니, 내가 한 말을 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 전해 주시오.\""]], [["“No, I won’t.”", "\"아니, 안 그러겠소.\""]], [["“In that case, Rodion Romanovitch, I shall be obliged to try and see her myself and worry her by doing so.”", "\"그렇다면, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 어쩔 수 없이 직접 그녀를 만나려고 애써야 할 테고, 그러느라 그녀를 성가시게 하겠지요.\""]], [["“And if I do tell her, will you not try to see her?”", "\"그래서 내가 그녀에게 전하면, 당신은 그녀를 만나려 들지 않겠소?\""]], [["“I don’t know really what to say.", "\"정말이지 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠소."], ["I should like very much to see her once more.”", "그녀를 꼭 한 번 더 보고 싶긴 한데.\""]], [["“Don’t hope for it.”", "\"기대하지 마시오.\""]], [["“I’m sorry.", "\"유감이오."], ["But you don’t know me.", "하지만 당신은 나를 모르오."], ["Perhaps we may become better friends.”", "어쩌면 우리가 더 좋은 사이가 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["“You think we may become friends?”", "\"우리가 친구가 될 수 있다고 생각하오?\""]], [["“And why not?”", "\"안 될 거 뭐 있소?\""], ["Svidrigaïlov said, smiling.", "스비드리가일로프가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["He stood up and took his hat.", "그는 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["“I didn’t quite intend to disturb you and I came here without reckoning on it... though I was very much struck by your face this morning.”", "\"딱히 당신을 방해할 작정은 아니었소, 그럴 셈도 없이 여기 온 거요……, 그래도 오늘 아침엔 당신 얼굴에 무척 충격을 받았소만.\""]], [["“Where did you see me this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침 어디서 나를 봤소?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked uneasily.", "라스콜니코프가 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“I saw you by chance....", "\"우연히 봤소……."], ["I kept fancying there is something about you like me....", "자꾸만 당신에게 나와 닮은 무언가가 있다는 생각이 들더군……."], ["But don’t be uneasy.", "하지만 마음 놓으시오."], ["I am not intrusive; I used to get on all right with card-sharpers, and I never bored Prince Svirbey, a great personage who is a distant relation of mine, and I could write about Raphael’s _Madonna_ in Madam Prilukov’s album, and I never left Marfa Petrovna’s side for seven years, and I used to stay the night at Viazemsky’s house in the Hay Market in the old days, and I may go up in a balloon with Berg, perhaps.”", "나는 주제넘게 굴지 않소. 사기 도박꾼들과도 곧잘 어울려 지냈고, 내 먼 친척뻘 되는 대단한 양반 스비르베이 공작을 한 번도 따분하게 한 적 없고, 프릴루코프 부인의 앨범에 라파엘로의 '마돈나'에 관한 글을 써 줄 줄도 알고, 칠 년 동안 마르파 페트로브나 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않았고, 옛날엔 건초 시장의 비아젬스키 여관에서 밤을 지새우기도 했고, 또 어쩌면 베르크와 함께 기구를 타고 올라갈지도 모르오.\""]], [["“Oh, all right.", "\"아, 그러시구려."], ["Are you starting soon on your travels, may I ask?”", "한데, 실례지만 그 여행은 곧 떠나는 거요?\""]], [["“What travels?”", "\"무슨 여행 말이오?\""]], [["“Why, on that ‘journey’; you spoke of it yourself.”", "\"아니, 그 '여행' 말이오, 당신이 직접 얘기했잖소.\""]], [["“A journey?", "\"여행이라?"], ["Oh, yes.", "오, 그래."], ["I did speak of a journey.", "여행 얘길 하긴 했지."], ["Well, that’s a wide subject.... if only you knew what you are asking,”", "글쎄, 그건 폭넓은 주제라서……, 당신이 뭘 묻고 있는지 알기만 한다면 말이오.\""], ["he added, and gave a sudden, loud, short laugh.", "그가 덧붙이며, 별안간 큼직하고 짤막한 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Perhaps I’ll get married instead of the journey.", "\"어쩌면 여행 대신 결혼할지도 모르오."], ["They’re making a match for me.”", "다들 내 혼사를 주선하고 있으니.\""]], [["“Here?” “Yes.”", "\"여기서요?\""]], [["“How have you had time for that?”", "\"그렇소.\" \"언제 그럴 틈이 있었소?\""]], [["“But I am very anxious to see Avdotya Romanovna once.", "\"하지만 나는 아브도티야 로마노브나를 꼭 한 번 보고 싶소."], ["I earnestly beg it.", "간절히 부탁하오."], ["Well, good-bye for the present.", "자, 그럼 이만 작별하지요."], ["Oh, yes.", "오, 그래."], ["I have forgotten something.", "깜빡한 게 있군."], ["Tell your sister, Rodion Romanovitch, that Marfa Petrovna remembered her in her will and left her three thousand roubles.", "당신 누이에게 전해 주시오, 로디온 로마노비치, 마르파 페트로브나가 유언에서 그녀를 기억하고 삼천 루블을 남겼다고 말이오."], ["That’s absolutely certain.", "이건 틀림없는 사실이오."], ["Marfa Petrovna arranged it a week before her death, and it was done in my presence.", "마르파 페트로브나가 죽기 일주일 전에 그렇게 정해 두었고, 내가 보는 앞에서 처리됐소."], ["Avdotya Romanovna will be able to receive the money in two or three weeks.”", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 두세 주 안에 그 돈을 받을 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“Are you telling the truth?”", "\"진실을 말하는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes, tell her.", "\"그렇소, 그녀에게 전하시오."], ["Well, your servant.", "자, 이만 실례하리다."], ["I am staying very near you.”", "나는 당신네 아주 가까이에 묵고 있소.\""]], [["As he went out, Svidrigaïlov ran up against Razumihin in the doorway.", "밖으로 나가던 스비드리가일로프는 문간에서 라주미힌과 맞부딪쳤다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["It was nearly eight o’clock.", "여덟 시가 다 되어 가고 있었다."], ["The two young men hurried to Bakaleyev’s, to arrive before Luzhin.", "두 청년은 루진보다 먼저 도착하려고 바칼레예프의 하숙으로 서둘러 갔다."]], [["“Why, who was that?”", "\"그런데, 그 사람은 누구였나?\""], ["asked Razumihin, as soon as they were in the street.", "거리로 나서자마자 라주미힌이 물었다."]], [["“It was Svidrigaïlov, that landowner in whose house my sister was insulted when she was their governess.", "\"스비드리가일로프였어. 누이가 가정교사로 있던 그 집 지주 말이야, 거기서 누이가 모욕을 당했지."], ["Through his persecuting her with his attentions, she was turned out by his wife, Marfa Petrovna.", "그자가 끈질기게 추근거리는 바람에, 그 아내 마르파 페트로브나가 누이를 내쫓아 버렸어."], ["This Marfa Petrovna begged Dounia’s forgiveness afterwards, and she’s just died suddenly.", "그런데 이 마르파 페트로브나가 나중에 두냐에게 용서를 빌었고, 이번에 갑자기 죽어 버렸지."], ["It was of her we were talking this morning.", "오늘 아침에 우리가 얘기했던 게 바로 그 여자였어."], ["I don’t know why I’m afraid of that man.", "왜 그런지 나는 그 사람이 두려워."], ["He came here at once after his wife’s funeral.", "아내 장례를 치르자마자 곧장 이리로 왔어."], ["He is very strange, and is determined on doing something....", "무척 이상한 사람이고, 무언가 작정한 게 있어……."], ["We must guard Dounia from him...", "두냐를 그자한테서 지켜 줘야 해……"], ["that’s what I wanted to tell you, do you hear?”", "바로 이걸 너한테 말하고 싶었어, 알겠나?\""]], [["“Guard her!", "\"지켜 준다고!"], ["What can he do to harm Avdotya Romanovna?", "그자가 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 무슨 해코지를 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Thank you, Rodya, for speaking to me like that....", "고맙네, 로쟈, 나한테 그렇게 말해 줘서……."], ["We will, we will guard her.", "우리가 지킬 거야, 지켜 주고말고."], ["Where does he live?”", "그자는 어디 사나?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"몰라.\""]], [["“Why didn’t you ask?", "\"왜 묻지 않았나?"], ["What a pity!", "저런, 아쉽군!"], ["I’ll find out, though.”", "하지만 내가 알아내고 말겠어.\""]], [["“Did you see him?”", "\"그 사람을 봤나?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov after a pause.", "잠시 사이를 두고 라스콜니코프가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I noticed him, I noticed him well.”", "\"그래, 봤어, 똑똑히 봐 두었지.\""]], [["“You did really see him?", "\"정말로 봤단 말이지?"], ["You saw him clearly?”", "분명히 봤어?\""], ["Raskolnikov insisted.", "라스콜니코프가 다그쳤다."]], [["“Yes, I remember him perfectly, I should know him in a thousand; I have a good memory for faces.”", "\"그래, 완벽하게 기억해, 천 명 가운데서도 알아볼 거야. 나는 얼굴 기억력이 좋거든.\""]], [["They were silent again.", "그들은 다시 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Hm!...", "\"흠!……"], ["that’s all right,”", "그렇다면 됐어.\""], ["muttered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Do you know, I fancied...", "\"그런데 말이야, 나는 자꾸……"], ["I keep thinking that it may have been an hallucination.”", "어쩌면 그게 환각이었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어서.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 말이야?"], ["I don’t understand you.”", "무슨 소린지 모르겠군.\""]], [["“Well, you all say,”", "\"그러니까, 너희들 모두 말하잖아.\""], ["Raskolnikov went on, twisting his mouth into a smile, “that I am mad.", "라스콜니코프는 입가를 비틀어 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"내가 미쳤다고."], ["I thought just now that perhaps I really am mad, and have only seen a phantom.”", "방금 나는 어쩌면 정말로 내가 미쳤고, 그저 헛것을 본 게 아닐까 생각했어.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말이야?\""]], [["“Why, who can tell?", "\"글쎄, 누가 알겠나?"], ["Perhaps I am really mad, and perhaps everything that happened all these days may be only imagination.”", "어쩌면 나는 정말 미쳤는지도 모르고, 어쩌면 요 며칠 동안 일어난 모든 일이 그저 망상일 뿐인지도.\""]], [["“Ach, Rodya, you have been upset again!...", "\"아, 로쟈, 너 또 흥분했구나!……"], ["But what did he say, what did he come for?”", "그런데 그 사람이 뭐라고 했어, 뭣 때문에 왔다던?\""]], [["Raskolnikov did not answer.", "라스콜니코프는 대답하지 않았다."], ["Razumihin thought a minute.", "라주미힌은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Now let me tell you my story,”", "\"자, 이제 내 얘기를 들려줄게.\""], ["he began, “I came to you, you were asleep.", "그가 입을 열었다. \"너한테 갔더니, 넌 자고 있더군."], ["Then we had dinner and then I went to Porfiry’s, Zametov was still with him.", "그다음 우리는 식사를 했고, 그러고 나서 나는 포르피리한테 갔어, 자메토프도 아직 거기 있더라고."], ["I tried to begin, but it was no use.", "말을 꺼내 보려 했지만 소용없었어."], ["I couldn’t speak in the right way.", "제대로 말이 안 나오더라."], ["They don’t seem to understand and can’t understand, but are not a bit ashamed.", "그들은 알아듣지도 못하고, 알아들을 수도 없는 것 같은데, 그러면서도 조금도 부끄러워하지 않아."], ["I drew Porfiry to the window, and began talking to him, but it was still no use.", "포르피리를 창가로 끌고 가서 얘기를 시작했지만, 그래도 소용없었어."], ["He looked away and I looked away.", "그자는 딴 데를 보고, 나도 딴 데를 봤지."], ["At last I shook my fist in his ugly face, and told him as a cousin I’d brain him.", "결국 나는 그자의 못생긴 얼굴에 대고 주먹을 흔들면서, 사촌이니까 대가리를 박살 내 버리겠다고 했어."], ["He merely looked at me, I cursed and came away.", "그자는 그저 나를 쳐다보기만 하더군. 나는 욕을 퍼붓고 나와 버렸지."], ["That was all.", "그게 다야."], ["It was very stupid.", "정말 멍청한 짓이었어."], ["To Zametov I didn’t say a word.", "자메토프한테는 한마디도 안 했고."], ["But, you see, I thought I’d made a mess of it, but as I went downstairs a brilliant idea struck me: why should we trouble?", "그런데 말이야, 나는 일을 망쳐 버렸다고 생각했는데, 계단을 내려오다가 기막힌 생각이 떠올랐어. 우리가 왜 골치를 썩여야 하지?"], ["Of course if you were in any danger or anything, but why need you care?", "물론 네가 무슨 위험에 처했거나 그런 거라면 모를까, 그게 아니라면 네가 신경 쓸 게 뭐 있어?"], ["You needn’t care a hang for them.", "그자들 따위에 눈곱만큼도 신경 쓸 거 없어."], ["We shall have a laugh at them afterwards, and if I were in your place I’d mystify them more than ever.", "나중에 우리가 그자들을 비웃어 주면 돼, 내가 네 입장이라면 그자들을 더더욱 혼란에 빠뜨릴 거야."], ["How ashamed they’ll be afterwards!", "나중에 그자들이 얼마나 부끄러워할까!"], ["Hang them!", "빌어먹을 놈들!"], ["We can thrash them afterwards, but let’s laugh at them now!”", "나중에 흠씬 두들겨 줄 수도 있겠지만, 지금은 그냥 비웃어 주자고!\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"그렇긴 해.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."], ["“But what will you say to-morrow?”", "'그런데 너는 내일 뭐라고 할까?'"], ["he thought to himself.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["Strange to say, till that moment it had never occurred to him to wonder what Razumihin would think when he knew.", "이상한 일이지만, 그 순간까지 그는 라주미힌이 사실을 알게 되면 어떻게 생각할까 궁금해한 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["As he thought it, Raskolnikov looked at him.", "이런 생각을 하며 라스콜니코프는 그를 바라보았다."], ["Razumihin’s account of his visit to Porfiry had very little interest for him, so much had come and gone since then.", "포르피리를 찾아갔다는 라주미힌의 이야기는 그에게 거의 흥미를 주지 못했으니, 그 사이에 너무도 많은 일이 오고 갔던 것이다."]], [["In the corridor they came upon Luzhin; he had arrived punctually at eight, and was looking for the number, so that all three went in together without greeting or looking at one another.", "복도에서 그들은 루진과 마주쳤다. 그는 정확히 여덟 시에 도착해서 호수를 찾고 있던 참이라, 셋이 함께 들어가게 되었는데, 인사도 없고 서로 쳐다보지도 않았다."], ["The young men walked in first, while Pyotr Petrovitch, for good manners, lingered a little in the passage, taking off his coat.", "두 청년이 먼저 걸어 들어갔고, 표트르 페트로비치는 예의를 차린답시고 통로에서 외투를 벗으며 잠시 머뭇거렸다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna came forward at once to greet him in the doorway, Dounia was welcoming her brother.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 곧장 문간으로 나와 그를 맞이했고, 두냐는 오빠를 반겼다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch walked in and quite amiably, though with redoubled dignity, bowed to the ladies.", "표트르 페트로비치는 안으로 들어와, 자못 상냥하게, 그러나 갑절로 위엄을 차리며 두 부인에게 고개를 숙였다."], ["He looked, however, as though he were a little put out and could not yet recover himself.", "하지만 그는 어딘가 약간 마음이 상해 아직 평정을 되찾지 못한 듯 보였다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna, who seemed also a little embarrassed, hastened to make them all sit down at the round table where a samovar was boiling.", "역시 조금 당황한 듯한 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 서둘러 모두를 둥근 탁자 앞에 앉혔는데, 그 위에는 사모바르가 끓고 있었다."], ["Dounia and Luzhin were facing one another on opposite sides of the table.", "두냐와 루진은 탁자를 사이에 두고 서로 마주 앉았다."], ["Razumihin and Raskolnikov were facing Pulcheria Alexandrovna, Razumihin was next to Luzhin and Raskolnikov was beside his sister.", "라주미힌과 라스콜니코프는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나를 마주 보고 앉았는데, 라주미힌은 루진 옆에, 라스콜니코프는 누이 곁에 자리했다."]], [["A moment’s silence followed.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch deliberately drew out a cambric handkerchief reeking of scent and blew his nose with an air of a benevolent man who felt himself slighted, and was firmly resolved to insist on an explanation.", "표트르 페트로비치는 일부러 향내가 진동하는 고운 무명 손수건을 꺼내, 마치 자신이 모욕을 당했다고 여기며 기어이 해명을 받아 내고야 말겠다고 단단히 작정한 너그러운 사람 같은 태도로 코를 풀었다."], ["In the passage the idea had occurred to him to keep on his overcoat and walk away, and so give the two ladies a sharp and emphatic lesson and make them feel the gravity of the position.", "통로에서 그는 외투를 벗지 않고 그대로 나가 버려, 두 부인에게 따끔하고 단호한 본때를 보여 주고 사태의 심각성을 깨닫게 해 줄까 하는 생각도 했더랬다. 그러나 차마 그렇게까지는 할 수 없었다."], ["But he could not bring himself to do this.", "게다가 그는 불확실한 상태를 견딜 수가 없었고, 해명을 듣고 싶었다."], ["Besides, he could not endure uncertainty, and he wanted an explanation: if his request had been so openly disobeyed, there was something behind it, and in that case it was better to find it out beforehand; it rested with him to punish them and there would always be time for that.", "자신의 요구가 그토록 공공연하게 무시당했다면 거기에는 무슨 곡절이 있는 것이고, 그렇다면 그것을 미리 알아 두는 편이 나았다. 그들을 벌하는 것은 자기 손에 달린 일이었고, 그럴 시간이야 언제든 있을 터였다."]], [["“I trust you had a favourable journey,”", "\"여행은 순조로우셨겠지요.\""], ["he inquired officially of Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "그가 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나에게 사무적으로 물었다."]], [["“Oh, very, Pyotr Petrovitch.”", "\"오, 아주 순조로웠어요, 표트르 페트로비치.\""]], [["“I am gratified to hear it.", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 기쁘군요."], ["And Avdotya Romanovna is not over-fatigued either?”", "아브도티야 로마노브나도 너무 지치지는 않으셨고요?\""]], [["“I am young and strong, I don’t get tired, but it was a great strain for mother,”", "\"저는 젊고 튼튼해서 지치지 않습니다만, 어머니께는 무척 힘드셨어요.\""], ["answered Dounia.", "두냐가 대답했다."]], [["“That’s unavoidable!", "\"어쩔 수 없는 일이지요!"], ["our national railways are of terrible length.", "우리 나라 철도는 끔찍하게 기니까요."], ["‘Mother Russia,’ as they say, is a vast country....", "'어머니 러시아'라고들 하잖습니까, 광활한 나라지요……."], ["In spite of all my desire to do so, I was unable to meet you yesterday.", "그토록 가고 싶었습니다만, 어제는 도저히 마중을 나갈 수가 없었습니다."], ["But I trust all passed off without inconvenience?”", "하지만 별 불편 없이 다 잘 지나갔으리라 믿습니다만?\""]], [["“Oh, no, Pyotr Petrovitch, it was all terribly disheartening,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 표트르 페트로비치, 모든 게 끔찍하게 맥 빠지는 일들뿐이었답니다.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna hastened to declare with peculiar intonation, “and if Dmitri Prokofitch had not been sent us, I really believe by God Himself, we should have been utterly lost.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 독특한 억양으로 서둘러 말했다. \"드미트리 프로코피치를 하느님께서 친히 우리에게 보내 주지 않으셨더라면, 정말이지 우리는 완전히 길을 잃고 말았을 거예요."], ["Here, he is!", "자, 이 사람이랍니다!"], ["Dmitri Prokofitch Razumihin,”", "드미트리 프로코피치 라주미힌이에요.\""], ["she added, introducing him to Luzhin.", "그녀는 루진에게 그를 소개하며 덧붙였다."]], [["“I had the pleasure...", "\"어제……"], ["yesterday,”", "인사를 나눌 기회가 있었지요.\""], ["muttered Pyotr Petrovitch with a hostile glance sidelong at Razumihin; then he scowled and was silent.", "표트르 페트로비치가 라주미힌을 곁눈질로 적대적으로 흘겨보며 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 얼굴을 찌푸리고 입을 다물었다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch belonged to that class of persons, on the surface very polite in society, who make a great point of punctiliousness, but who, directly they are crossed in anything, are completely disconcerted, and become more like sacks of flour than elegant and lively men of society.", "표트르 페트로비치는 겉으로는 사교계에서 무척 정중하고 격식을 대단히 중시하는 부류에 속했지만, 무슨 일에서든 일단 뜻이 어긋나기만 하면 완전히 당황해서, 우아하고 활기찬 사교계 신사라기보다는 차라리 밀가루 자루처럼 되어 버리는 인간이었다. 다시 모두가 입을 다물었다."], ["Again all was silent; Raskolnikov was obstinately mute, Avdotya Romanovna was unwilling to open the conversation too soon.", "라스콜니코프는 고집스레 말이 없었고, 아브도티야 로마노브나는 너무 일찍 대화를 시작하고 싶지 않았다."], ["Razumihin had nothing to say, so Pulcheria Alexandrovna was anxious again.", "라주미힌은 할 말이 없었으므로, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 또다시 불안해졌다."]], [["“Marfa Petrovna is dead, have you heard?”", "\"마르파 페트로브나가 죽었어요, 들으셨나요?\""], ["she began having recourse to her leading item of conversation.", "그녀는 자신의 으뜸가는 화제로 되돌아가며 입을 열었다."]], [["“To be sure, I heard so.", "\"물론, 들었습니다."], ["I was immediately informed, and I have come to make you acquainted with the fact that Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov set off in haste for Petersburg immediately after his wife’s funeral.", "곧바로 전갈을 받았지요. 그래서 제가 온 것은 아르카지 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프가 아내의 장례를 치르자마자 황급히 페테르부르크로 떠났다는 사실을 알려 드리려는 것입니다."], ["So at least I have excellent authority for believing.”", "적어도 저는 그렇게 믿을 만한 확실한 근거를 가지고 있습니다.\""]], [["“To Petersburg? here?”", "\"페테르부르크로요?"], ["Dounia asked in alarm and looked at her mother.", "여기로?\" 두냐가 놀라 물으며 어머니를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, indeed, and doubtless not without some design, having in view the rapidity of his departure, and all the circumstances preceding it.”", "\"그렇습니다, 그것도 분명 어떤 속셈이 없지는 않을 겁니다, 그토록 서둘러 떠난 데다, 그에 앞선 온갖 정황을 보면 말입니다.\""]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["won’t he leave Dounia in peace even here?”", "그자가 여기서도 두냐를 가만 놔두지 않으려는 걸까요?\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."]], [["“I imagine that neither you nor Avdotya Romanovna have any grounds for uneasiness, unless, of course, you are yourselves desirous of getting into communication with him.", "\"제 생각엔 부인이나 아브도티야 로마노브나나 불안해하실 근거는 전혀 없습니다, 물론 두 분이 스스로 그자와 연락을 트고 싶어 하시는 게 아니라면 말이지요."], ["For my part I am on my guard, and am now discovering where he is lodging.”", "저로서는 경계를 늦추지 않고 있고, 지금 그자가 어디에 묵고 있는지 알아내는 중입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, Pyotr Petrovitch, you would not believe what a fright you have given me,”", "\"오, 표트르 페트로비치, 당신이 저를 얼마나 놀라게 했는지 모르실 거예요.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna went on: “I’ve only seen him twice, but I thought him terrible, terrible!", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 이었다. \"저는 그자를 딱 두 번 봤을 뿐인데, 끔찍하다, 끔찍하다 싶었답니다!"], ["I am convinced that he was the cause of Marfa Petrovna’s death.”", "마르파 페트로브나의 죽음도 그자 탓이라고 저는 확신해요.\""]], [["“It’s impossible to be certain about that.", "\"그건 확실히 단정할 수 없는 일입니다."], ["I have precise information.", "저는 정확한 정보를 가지고 있습니다."], ["I do not dispute that he may have contributed to accelerate the course of events by the moral influence, so to say, of the affront; but as to the general conduct and moral characteristics of that personage, I am in agreement with you.", "그자가, 말하자면 그 모욕의 도덕적 영향으로, 사태의 진행을 앞당기는 데 일조했을 가능성을 부인하지는 않습니다. 하지만 그 인물의 전반적인 행실과 도덕적 성격에 관해서라면, 저도 부인과 같은 생각입니다."], ["I do not know whether he is well off now, and precisely what Marfa Petrovna left him; this will be known to me within a very short period; but no doubt here in Petersburg, if he has any pecuniary resources, he will relapse at once into his old ways.", "그자가 지금 형편이 넉넉한지, 마르파 페트로브나가 그에게 정확히 무엇을 남겼는지는 저도 모릅니다. 이는 머지않아 제가 알게 될 것입니다."], ["He is the most depraved, and abjectly vicious specimen of that class of men.", "하지만 여기 페테르부르크에서 그에게 금전적 여유가 조금이라도 생긴다면, 틀림없이 곧장 옛 버릇으로 돌아가고 말 겁니다."], ["I have considerable reason to believe that Marfa Petrovna, who was so unfortunate as to fall in love with him and to pay his debts eight years ago, was of service to him also in another way.", "그자는 그런 부류의 인간들 가운데서도 가장 타락하고 비열할 만큼 사악한 표본입니다. 저는 마르파 페트로브나가, 팔 년 전에 불운하게도 그자를 사랑하게 되어 그의 빚을 갚아 준 그 여자가, 또 다른 식으로도 그를 도와주었다고 믿을 만한 상당한 근거를 가지고 있습니다."], ["Solely by her exertions and sacrifices, a criminal charge, involving an element of fantastic and homicidal brutality for which he might well have been sentenced to Siberia, was hushed up.", "오로지 그녀의 노력과 희생으로, 환상적이고 살인적인 잔혹성이 얽힌, 그 때문에 시베리아 유형을 선고받아도 마땅했을 형사 사건이 묻혀 버렸지요."], ["That’s the sort of man he is, if you care to know.”", "알고 싶으시다면, 그자가 바로 그런 인간이랍니다.\""]], [["“Good heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."], ["Raskolnikov listened attentively.", "라스콜니코프는 주의 깊게 듣고 있었다."]], [["“Are you speaking the truth when you say that you have good evidence of this?”", "\"이 모든 것에 확실한 증거가 있다고 하신 게 정말입니까?\""], ["Dounia asked sternly and emphatically.", "두냐가 단호하고 힘주어 물었다."]], [["“I only repeat what I was told in secret by Marfa Petrovna.", "\"저는 다만 마르파 페트로브나가 은밀히 제게 들려준 이야기를 그대로 옮길 뿐입니다."], ["I must observe that from the legal point of view the case was far from clear.", "다만 법적인 관점에서 보자면 그 사건이 결코 명백하지는 않았다는 점은 짚어 두어야겠습니다."], ["There was, and I believe still is, living here a woman called Resslich, a foreigner, who lent small sums of money at interest, and did other commissions, and with this woman Svidrigaïlov had for a long while close and mysterious relations.", "여기에 레슬리히라는 여자가 살고 있었는데, 지금도 살고 있는 것 같습니다만, 외국인으로 이자를 받고 푼돈을 빌려주거나 이런저런 심부름을 해 주는 여자였지요. 스비드리가일로프는 이 여자와 오랫동안 은밀하고 수상쩍은 관계를 맺고 있었습니다."], ["She had a relation, a niece I believe, living with her, a deaf and dumb girl of fifteen, or perhaps not more than fourteen.", "그 여자에게는 함께 사는 친척이, 아마 조카딸이었던 듯한데, 열다섯, 아니 어쩌면 열넷밖에 안 된 귀먹고 말 못 하는 소녀가 있었지요."], ["Resslich hated this girl, and grudged her every crust; she used to beat her mercilessly.", "레슬리히는 이 소녀를 미워해서 빵 한 조각까지 아까워했고, 사정없이 두들겨 패곤 했답니다."], ["One day the girl was found hanging in the garret. At the inquest the verdict was suicide.", "그러던 어느 날, 그 소녀가 다락방에서 목매단 채로 발견되었지요."], ["After the usual proceedings the matter ended, but, later on, information was given that the child had been...", "검시 결과는 자살이었습니다. 통상적인 절차를 거쳐 사건은 마무리되었습니다만, 나중에 그 아이가……"], ["cruelly outraged by Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프에게 잔혹하게 능욕을 당했다는 정보가 들어왔지요."], ["It is true, this was not clearly established, the information was given by another German woman of loose character whose word could not be trusted; no statement was actually made to the police, thanks to Marfa Petrovna’s money and exertions; it did not get beyond gossip.", "사실 이것은 명백히 밝혀지지는 않았고, 그 정보를 준 것은 말을 믿을 수 없는 또 다른 방탕한 독일 여자였습니다. 경찰에 정식으로 진술된 것은 아무것도 없었으니, 이는 마르파 페트로브나의 돈과 노력 덕분이었지요."], ["And yet the story is a very significant one.", "결국 뜬소문 이상으로는 번지지 않았습니다."], ["You heard, no doubt, Avdotya Romanovna, when you were with them the story of the servant Philip who died of ill treatment he received six years ago, before the abolition of serfdom.”", "그렇긴 해도 이 이야기는 무척 의미심장한 것입니다. 아브도티야 로마노브나, 분명 그 집에 계실 때 농노 해방 이전, 육 년 전에 학대를 받고 죽은 하인 필립의 이야기를 들으셨을 텐데요.\""]], [["“I heard, on the contrary, that this Philip hanged himself.”", "\"오히려 저는 이 필립이 스스로 목을 맸다고 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Quite so, but what drove him, or rather perhaps disposed him, to suicide was the systematic persecution and severity of Mr. Svidrigaïlov.”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 하지만 그를 자살로 몰아넣은 것, 아니 어쩌면 자살하도록 만든 것은 스비드리가일로프 씨의 체계적인 박해와 가혹함이었습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t know that,”", "\"저는 그건 모르겠어요.\""], ["answered Dounia, dryly.", "두냐가 쌀쌀맞게 대답했다."], ["“I only heard a queer story that Philip was a sort of hypochondriac, a sort of domestic philosopher, the servants used to say, ‘he read himself silly,’ and that he hanged himself partly on account of Mr. Svidrigaïlov’s mockery of him and not his blows.", "\"저는 다만 이상한 이야기를 들었을 뿐이에요, 필립이 일종의 우울증 환자, 일종의 집안 철학자 같은 사람이라, 하인들 말로는 '책을 너무 읽어서 머리가 돌았다'더군요, 그리고 그가 목을 맨 것은 어느 정도 스비드리가일로프 씨의 매질 때문이 아니라 그의 조롱 때문이었다고요."], ["When I was there he behaved well to the servants, and they were actually fond of him, though they certainly did blame him for Philip’s death.”", "제가 거기 있을 때 그분은 하인들에게 잘 대해 주었고, 하인들도 그분을 정말 좋아했어요, 물론 필립의 죽음에 대해서는 그분을 탓했지만요.\""]], [["“I perceive, Avdotya Romanovna, that you seem disposed to undertake his defence all of a sudden,”", "\"보아하니, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 갑자기 그자를 변호하고 나서실 생각이신 모양이군요.\""], ["Luzhin observed, twisting his lips into an ambiguous smile, “there’s no doubt that he is an astute man, and insinuating where ladies are concerned, of which Marfa Petrovna, who has died so strangely, is a terrible instance.", "루진이 입술을 비틀어 모호한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그자가 교활한 인간이고, 부인네들 앞에서는 알랑거릴 줄 안다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없지요."], ["My only desire has been to be of service to you and your mother with my advice, in view of the renewed efforts which may certainly be anticipated from him.", "그토록 기이하게 죽은 마르파 페트로브나가 끔찍한 본보기 아닙니까. 제 바람은 오로지, 그자한테서 분명 또다시 예상되는 시도들에 대비해, 충고로나마 부인과 어머님께 도움이 되고자 하는 것이었습니다."], ["For my part it’s my firm conviction, that he will end in a debtor’s prison again.", "저로서는, 그자가 결국 또다시 채무자 감옥에서 생을 마칠 거라고 굳게 확신합니다."], ["Marfa Petrovna had not the slightest intention of settling anything substantial on him, having regard for his children’s interests, and, if she left him anything, it would only be the merest sufficiency, something insignificant and ephemeral, which would not last a year for a man of his habits.”", "마르파 페트로브나는 자기 아이들의 이익을 생각해서 그자에게 무언가 상당한 재산을 물려줄 마음은 손톱만큼도 없었습니다. 설령 무언가를 남겼다 해도, 그건 그저 겨우 입에 풀칠할 정도의, 하찮고 덧없는 것이어서, 그자 같은 습성을 가진 인간에게는 일 년도 못 갈 겁니다.\""]], [["“Pyotr Petrovitch, I beg you,”", "\"표트르 페트로비치, 부탁드려요.\" 두냐가 말했다."], ["said Dounia, “say no more of Mr. Svidrigaïlov.", "\"스비드리가일로프 씨 이야기는 그만하세요."], ["It makes me miserable.”", "저를 비참하게 만드는군요.\""]], [["“He has just been to see me,”", "\"방금 그 사람이 저를 찾아왔습니다.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, breaking his silence for the first time.", "라스콜니코프가 처음으로 침묵을 깨고 말했다."]], [["There were exclamations from all, and they all turned to him.", "모두가 탄성을 지르며 그를 돌아보았다."], ["Even Pyotr Petrovitch was roused.", "표트르 페트로비치까지 정신이 번쩍 든 듯했다."]], [["“An hour and a half ago, he came in when I was asleep, waked me, and introduced himself,”", "\"한 시간 반쯤 전에, 제가 자고 있을 때 들어와서, 저를 깨우고 자기소개를 하더군요.\""], ["Raskolnikov continued.", "라스콜니코프가 말을 이었다."], ["“He was fairly cheerful and at ease, and quite hopes that we shall become friends.", "\"꽤나 명랑하고 느긋했고, 우리가 친구가 되기를 자못 바라더군요."], ["He is particularly anxious, by the way, Dounia, for an interview with you, at which he asked me to assist.", "그건 그렇고, 두냐, 특히 너와의 면담을 간절히 바라면서, 그걸 주선해 달라고 나한테 부탁했어."], ["He has a proposition to make to you, and he told me about it.", "너한테 제안할 게 있다며, 그게 뭔지 나한테 말해 줬지."], ["He told me, too, that a week before her death Marfa Petrovna left you three thousand roubles in her will, Dounia, and that you can receive the money very shortly.”", "또 마르파 페트로브나가 죽기 일주일 전에 유언으로 너한테 삼천 루블을 남겼다고, 두냐, 그리고 그 돈을 머지않아 받을 수 있을 거라고 하더군.\""]], [["“Thank God!”", "\"하느님, 감사합니다!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna, crossing herself.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 성호를 그으며 외쳤다."], ["“Pray for her soul, Dounia!”", "\"그 여자의 영혼을 위해 기도하자, 두냐!\""]], [["“It’s a fact!”", "\"사실입니다!\""], ["broke from Luzhin.", "루진의 입에서 튀어나왔다."]], [["“Tell us, what more?”", "\"말해 봐, 또 뭐라고 했어?\""], ["Dounia urged Raskolnikov.", "두냐가 라스콜니코프를 재촉했다."]], [["“Then he said that he wasn’t rich and all the estate was left to his children who are now with an aunt, then that he was staying somewhere not far from me, but where, I don’t know, I didn’t ask....”", "\"그러고는 자기가 부자가 아니고, 모든 재산은 지금 고모와 함께 있는 자기 아이들한테 남겨졌다고 하더군. 그다음 자기가 내 집에서 그리 멀지 않은 어딘가에 묵고 있다고 했는데, 어딘지는 몰라, 묻지 않았으니까…….\""]], [["“But what, what does he want to propose to Dounia?”", "\"그런데 그자가, 그자가 두냐한테 무슨 제안을 하려는 거니?\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna in a fright.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 겁에 질려 외쳤다."], ["“Did he tell you?”", "\"너한테 말하더냐?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그래.\""]], [["“What was it?”", "\"그게 뭐였니?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you afterwards.”", "\"나중에 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov ceased speaking and turned his attention to his tea.", "라스콜니코프는 말을 멈추고 자기 차로 주의를 돌렸다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch looked at his watch.", "표트르 페트로비치는 시계를 보았다."]], [["“I am compelled to keep a business engagement, and so I shall not be in your way,”", "\"제가 부득이 사업상 약속을 지켜야 해서, 더는 폐가 되지 않겠습니다.\""], ["he added with an air of some pique and he began getting up.", "그는 다소 발끈한 기색으로 덧붙이며 일어서기 시작했다."]], [["“Don’t go, Pyotr Petrovitch,”", "\"가지 마세요, 표트르 페트로비치.\""], ["said Dounia, “you intended to spend the evening.", "두냐가 말했다. \"저녁을 함께 보내실 작정이었잖아요."], ["Besides, you wrote yourself that you wanted to have an explanation with mother.”", "게다가 어머니와 해명하고 싶은 게 있다고 직접 편지에 쓰셨고요.\""]], [["“Precisely so, Avdotya Romanovna,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다, 아브도티야 로마노브나.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch answered impressively, sitting down again, but still holding his hat.", "표트르 페트로비치가 묵직하게 대꾸하며 다시 자리에 앉았으나, 모자는 여전히 손에 든 채였다."], ["“I certainly desired an explanation with you and your honoured mother upon a very important point indeed.", "\"저는 분명 당신과 존경하는 어머님과 더불어, 참으로 중대한 사안에 관해 해명을 나누고자 했습니다."], ["But as your brother cannot speak openly in my presence of some proposals of Mr.", "하지만 당신 오라버니가 제 앞에서 스비드리가일로프 씨의 어떤 제안들을 터놓고 말할 수 없는 것처럼, 저 또한……"], ["Svidrigaïlov, I, too, do not desire and am not able to speak openly...", "다른 사람들이 있는 자리에서……"], ["in the presence of others... of certain matters of the greatest gravity.", "더없이 중대한 어떤 문제들을 터놓고 말하고 싶지도 않고, 말할 수도 없습니다."], ["Moreover, my most weighty and urgent request has been disregarded....”", "더구나 저의 가장 무겁고 절박한 요청이 묵살되었으니…….\""]], [["Assuming an aggrieved air, Luzhin relapsed into dignified silence.", "상심한 기색을 지으며, 루진은 위엄 있는 침묵 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["“Your request that my brother should not be present at our meeting was disregarded solely at my insistence,”", "\"오라버니가 우리 만남에 동석하지 말아 달라는 당신의 요청을 묵살한 것은 오로지 제 고집 때문이었어요.\""], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."], ["“You wrote that you had been insulted by my brother; I think that this must be explained at once, and you must be reconciled.", "\"당신은 제 오빠한테서 모욕을 당했다고 쓰셨지요. 저는 이걸 지금 당장 풀어야 한다고 생각해요, 그래서 두 분이 화해하셔야 한다고요."], ["And if Rodya really has insulted you, then he _should_ and _will_ apologise.”", "만일 로쟈가 정말로 당신을 모욕했다면, 마땅히 사과해야 하고, 사과할 거예요.\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch took a stronger line.", "표트르 페트로비치는 한층 강경하게 나왔다."]], [["“There are insults, Avdotya Romanovna, which no goodwill can make us forget.", "\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 그 어떤 호의로도 잊을 수 없는 모욕이라는 게 있습니다."], ["There is a line in everything which it is dangerous to overstep; and when it has been overstepped, there is no return.”", "무슨 일에든 넘어서는 안 될 선이 있고, 일단 그 선을 넘어 버리면 되돌릴 수 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["“That wasn’t what I was speaking of exactly, Pyotr Petrovitch,”", "\"제가 말씀드리려는 건 바로 그게 아니에요, 표트르 페트로비치.\""], ["Dounia interrupted with some impatience.", "두냐가 다소 조바심을 내며 가로막았다."], ["“Please understand that our whole future depends now on whether all this is explained and set right as soon as possible.", "\"부디 이해해 주세요, 지금 우리의 모든 앞날은 이 모든 일이 가능한 한 빨리 풀려서 바로잡히느냐에 달려 있어요."], ["I tell you frankly at the start that I cannot look at it in any other light, and if you have the least regard for me, all this business must be ended to-day, however hard that may be.", "처음부터 솔직히 말씀드리는데, 저는 이걸 다른 식으로는 도저히 볼 수가 없어요, 그리고 저를 조금이라도 생각하신다면, 이 일은 아무리 힘들더라도 오늘 안에 끝나야 해요."], ["I repeat that if my brother is to blame he will ask your forgiveness.”", "다시 말씀드리지만, 제 오빠한테 잘못이 있다면 당신께 용서를 빌 거예요.\""]], [["“I am surprised at your putting the question like that,”", "\"문제를 그런 식으로 제기하시다니 놀랍군요.\""], ["said Luzhin, getting more and more irritated.", "루진이 점점 더 짜증을 내며 말했다."], ["“Esteeming, and so to say, adoring you, I may at the same time, very well indeed, be able to dislike some member of your family.", "\"당신을 존경하고, 말하자면 흠모하면서도, 저는 동시에 당신 가족 중 누군가를 매우, 정말이지 매우 싫어할 수도 있는 겁니다."], ["Though I lay claim to the happiness of your hand, I cannot accept duties incompatible with...”", "비록 제가 당신의 손을 잡는 행복을 누리고자 한다 해도, 그와 양립할 수 없는 의무까지 받아들일 수는…….\""]], [["“Ah, don’t be so ready to take offence, Pyotr Petrovitch,”", "\"아, 그렇게 쉽게 발끈하지 마세요, 표트르 페트로비치.\""], ["Dounia interrupted with feeling, “and be the sensible and generous man I have always considered, and wish to consider, you to be.", "두냐가 감정을 실어 가로막았다. \"그리고 제가 늘 여겨 왔고, 또 그렇게 여기고 싶은 대로, 분별 있고 너그러운 분이 되어 주세요."], ["I’ve given you a great promise, I am your betrothed.", "저는 당신께 큰 약속을 했어요, 저는 당신의 약혼녀입니다."], ["Trust me in this matter and, believe me, I shall be capable of judging impartially.", "이 일에서 저를 믿어 주세요, 그리고 믿어 주세요, 저는 공정하게 판단할 줄 아는 사람입니다."], ["My assuming the part of judge is as much a surprise for my brother as for you.", "제가 심판자 역할을 떠맡은 건 당신께만큼이나 제 오빠한테도 뜻밖의 일이에요."], ["When I insisted on his coming to our interview to-day after your letter, I told him nothing of what I meant to do.", "당신 편지를 받고 오늘 만남에 오빠를 꼭 데려오라고 고집했을 때, 제가 어떻게 할 작정인지는 오빠한테 아무 말도 하지 않았어요."], ["Understand that, if you are not reconciled, I must choose between you--it must be either you or he.", "이해해 주세요, 만일 두 분이 화해하지 못한다면, 저는 두 분 중 한 사람을 택해야 해요. 당신이냐 오빠냐, 둘 중 하나여야 한다고요."], ["That is how the question rests on your side and on his.", "당신 편에서나 오빠 편에서나 문제는 그렇게 걸려 있어요."], ["I don’t want to be mistaken in my choice, and I must not be.", "저는 제 선택에서 잘못을 저지르고 싶지 않고, 또 그래서도 안 됩니다."], ["For your sake I must break off with my brother, for my brother’s sake I must break off with you.", "당신을 위해서라면 저는 오빠와 인연을 끊어야 하고, 오빠를 위해서라면 당신과 인연을 끊어야 해요."], ["I can find out for certain now whether he is a brother to me, and I want to know it; and of you, whether I am dear to you, whether you esteem me, whether you are the husband for me.”", "저는 이제 오빠가 정말 제게 오빠다운 사람인지 확실히 알아낼 수 있고, 또 알고 싶어요. 그리고 당신에 대해서는, 제가 당신께 소중한 사람인지, 당신이 저를 존중하시는지, 당신이 제게 맞는 남편인지 알고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Avdotya Romanovna,”", "\"아브도티야 로마노브나,\""], ["Luzhin declared huffily, “your words are of too much consequence to me; I will say more, they are offensive in view of the position I have the honour to occupy in relation to you.", "루진이 잔뜩 골이 나서 단언했다. \"당신의 말씀은 제게 너무도 중대한 의미를 지닙니다."], ["To say nothing of your strange and offensive setting me on a level with an impertinent boy, you admit the possibility of breaking your promise to me.", "더 말씀드리자면, 제가 당신에 대해 누리는 영광스러운 지위를 생각할 때 모욕적이기까지 합니다. 당신이 저를 무례한 애송이와 같은 수준에 놓는 그 기이하고 모욕적인 처사는 차치하더라도, 당신은 제게 한 약속을 깰 수 있다는 가능성을 인정하시는 겁니다."], ["You say ‘you or he,’ showing thereby of how little consequence I am in your eyes...", "당신은 '당신이냐 오빠냐'라고 말함으로써, 당신 눈에 제가 얼마나 하찮은 존재인지를 드러내고 계십니다……."], ["I cannot let this pass considering the relationship and...", "우리 사이에 존재하는 관계와……"], ["the obligations existing between us.”", "의무를 생각할 때, 저는 이를 그냥 넘길 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["cried Dounia, flushing.", "두냐가 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."], ["“I set your interest beside all that has hitherto been most precious in my life, what has made up the _whole_ of my life, and here you are offended at my making too _little_ account of you.”", "\"저는 당신의 이익을, 지금껏 제 인생에서 가장 소중했던 모든 것, 제 인생의 전부를 이루던 것과 나란히 놓았어요, 그런데 당신은 제가 당신을 너무 가볍게 여긴다고 기분이 상하셨다는 거군요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov smiled sarcastically, Razumihin fidgeted, but Pyotr Petrovitch did not accept the reproof; on the contrary, at every word he became more persistent and irritable, as though he relished it.", "라스콜니코프는 빈정대듯 미소 지었고, 라주미힌은 안절부절못했지만, 표트르 페트로비치는 그 질책을 받아들이지 않았다. 오히려 그는 한마디 한마디 할 때마다 점점 더 고집스럽고 신경질적으로 굴었으니, 마치 그걸 즐기는 듯했다."]], [["“Love for the future partner of your life, for your husband, ought to outweigh your love for your brother,”", "\"앞으로 함께 살아갈 반려에 대한, 남편에 대한 사랑은, 오라버니에 대한 사랑보다 무거워야 합니다.\""], ["he pronounced sententiously, “and in any case I cannot be put on the same level....", "그가 잠언이라도 늘어놓듯 말했다. \"그리고 어떤 경우에도 저를 그와 같은 수준에 놓을 수는 없습니다……."], ["Although I said so emphatically that I would not speak openly in your brother’s presence, nevertheless, I intend now to ask your honoured mother for a necessary explanation on a point of great importance closely affecting my dignity. Your son,”", "비록 제가 당신 오라버니 앞에서는 터놓고 말하지 않겠다고 그토록 힘주어 말하긴 했습니다만, 그럼에도 저는 이제 존경하는 어머님께, 제 존엄과 긴밀히 관련된 중대한 사안에 관해 꼭 필요한 해명을 청하려 합니다."], ["he turned to Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “yesterday in the presence of Mr.", "부인의 아드님이,\" 그가 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나에게 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Razsudkin (or...", "\"어제 라즈수드킨 씨(아니면……"], ["I think that’s it?", "그게 맞나요?"], ["excuse me, I have forgotten your surname”) (he bowed politely to Razumihin), “insulted me by misrepresenting the idea I expressed to you in a private conversation, drinking coffee, that is, that marriage with a poor girl who has had experience of trouble is more advantageous from the conjugal point of view than with one who has lived in luxury, since it is more profitable for the moral character.", "죄송합니다, 성함을 잊었군요\"—그가 라주미힌에게 정중히 고개를 숙였다)\"가 있는 자리에서, 제가 사사로운 대화 중에, 그러니까 커피를 마시며 부인께 표현했던 생각을 왜곡함으로써 저를 모욕했습니다. 곤경을 겪어 본 가난한 처녀와의 결혼이, 호사를 누리며 살아온 처녀와의 결혼보다 부부 관계의 관점에서 더 유리하다는, 도덕적 성품을 위해서 더 이롭다는 그 생각 말입니다."], ["Your son intentionally exaggerated the significance of my words and made them ridiculous, accusing me of malicious intentions, and, as far as I could see, relied upon your correspondence with him.", "부인의 아드님은 일부러 제 말의 의미를 과장하여 우스꽝스럽게 만들고, 제게 악의가 있었다고 몰아붙였는데, 제가 보기엔 부인과 주고받은 편지를 믿고 그런 것 같습니다."], ["I shall consider myself happy, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, if it is possible for you to convince me of an opposite conclusion, and thereby considerately reassure me.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 만일 부인께서 제게 그 반대의 결론을 납득시켜 주시고, 그리하여 사려 깊게 저를 안심시켜 주실 수 있다면, 저는 행복하겠습니다."], ["Kindly let me know in what terms precisely you repeated my words in your letter to Rodion Romanovitch.”", "부디 부인께서 로지온 로마노비치에게 보내신 편지에서, 제 말을 정확히 어떤 식으로 옮기셨는지 알려 주시겠습니까.\""]], [["“I don’t remember,”", "\"저는 기억이 안 나요.\""], ["faltered Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 더듬거렸다."], ["“I repeated them as I understood them.", "\"저는 제가 이해한 대로 옮겼어요."], ["I don’t know how Rodya repeated them to you, perhaps he exaggerated.”", "로쟈가 그걸 당신께 어떻게 옮겼는지는 모르겠고요, 어쩌면 과장했는지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“He could not have exaggerated them, except at your instigation.”", "\"부인께서 부추기지 않았다면, 아드님이 그걸 과장했을 리가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Pyotr Petrovitch,”", "\"표트르 페트로비치,\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna declared with dignity, “the proof that Dounia and I did not take your words in a very bad sense is the fact that we are here.”", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 위엄 있게 단언했다. \"두냐와 제가 당신 말을 그리 나쁘게 받아들이지 않았다는 증거는, 우리가 이렇게 여기 있다는 사실 그 자체랍니다.\""]], [["“Good, mother,”", "\"잘 말씀하셨어요, 어머니.\""], ["said Dounia approvingly.", "두냐가 흡족해하며 말했다."]], [["“Then this is my fault again,”", "\"그러면 이번에도 제 잘못이군요.\""], ["said Luzhin, aggrieved.", "루진이 상심한 듯 말했다."]], [["“Well, Pyotr Petrovitch, you keep blaming Rodion, but you yourself have just written what was false about him,”", "\"이거 봐요, 표트르 페트로비치, 당신은 자꾸 로지온을 탓하지만, 정작 당신 자신도 방금 그 애에 대해 거짓을 써 보냈잖아요.\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna added, gaining courage.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 용기를 내어 덧붙였다."]], [["“I don’t remember writing anything false.”", "\"저는 거짓을 썼던 기억이 없습니다.\""]], [["“You wrote,”", "\"당신은 썼소,\""], ["Raskolnikov said sharply, not turning to Luzhin, “that I gave money yesterday not to the widow of the man who was killed, as was the fact, but to his daughter (whom I had never seen till yesterday).", "라스콜니코프가 루진 쪽으로 돌아보지도 않은 채 날카롭게 말했다. \"내가 어제 돈을 준 게, 사실대로라면 살해당한 남자의 미망인에게 준 건데, 그게 아니라 그 딸(어제 처음 본)에게 줬다고 말이오."], ["You wrote this to make dissension between me and my family, and for that object added coarse expressions about the conduct of a girl whom you don’t know.", "당신이 이걸 쓴 건 나와 내 가족 사이를 이간질하려는 거였고, 그러려고 당신이 알지도 못하는 처녀의 행실에 대해 천박한 표현까지 덧붙였소."], ["All that is mean slander.”", "그건 죄다 비열한 중상모략이오.\""]], [["“Excuse me, sir,”", "\"실례합니다만, 선생,\""], ["said Luzhin, quivering with fury.", "루진이 분노로 떨며 말했다."], ["“I enlarged upon your qualities and conduct in my letter solely in response to your sister’s and mother’s inquiries, how I found you, and what impression you made on me.", "\"제가 편지에서 당신의 됨됨이와 행실을 들춘 것은, 오로지 당신 누이와 어머님의 물음에 답하느라, 제가 당신을 어떻게 보았고 어떤 인상을 받았는지를 말씀드린 것뿐입니다."], ["As for what you’ve alluded to in my letter, be so good as to point out one word of falsehood, show, that is, that you didn’t throw away your money, and that there are not worthless persons in that family, however unfortunate.”", "제 편지에서 당신이 언급한 그 대목에 관해서라면, 거기서 거짓말이라곤 한마디라도 짚어 보시지요, 그러니까 당신이 돈을 헛되이 내던지지 않았다는 것, 그리고 그 가족 안에, 아무리 불행하다 한들, 쓸모없는 인간들이 없다는 것을 보여 주시란 말입니다.\""]], [["“To my thinking, you, with all your virtues, are not worth the little finger of that unfortunate girl at whom you throw stones.”", "\"내 생각엔, 당신은 그 온갖 미덕을 다 갖췄어도, 당신이 돌을 던지는 그 불행한 처녀의 새끼손가락만 한 가치도 없는 사람이오.\""]], [["“Would you go so far then as to let her associate with your mother and sister?”", "\"그렇다면 당신은 그 여자를 당신 어머니, 누이와 어울리게 하는 데까지 나가시겠다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I have done so already, if you care to know.", "\"이미 그렇게 했소, 알고 싶으시다면."], ["I made her sit down to-day with mother and Dounia.”", "오늘 그 여자를 어머니, 두냐와 함께 앉게 했지.\""]], [["“Rodya!”", "\"로쟈!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."], ["Dounia crimsoned, Razumihin knitted his brows.", "두냐는 새빨개졌고, 라주미힌은 눈썹을 찌푸렸다."], ["Luzhin smiled with lofty sarcasm.", "루진은 거만한 빈정거림을 머금고 미소 지었다."]], [["“You may see for yourself, Avdotya Romanovna,”", "\"직접 보십시오, 아브도티야 로마노브나,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “whether it is possible for us to agree.", "\"우리가 과연 뜻이 맞을 수 있는지 말입니다."], ["I hope now that this question is at an end, once and for all.", "이제 이 문제가 단번에 영원히 매듭지어졌기를 바랍니다."], ["I will withdraw, that I may not hinder the pleasures of family intimacy, and the discussion of secrets.”", "저는 물러가겠습니다, 단란한 가족의 즐거움과 비밀스러운 이야기에 방해가 되지 않도록 말이지요.\""], ["He got up from his chair and took his hat.", "그는 의자에서 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["“But in withdrawing, I venture to request that for the future I may be spared similar meetings, and, so to say, compromises.", "\"다만 물러가면서, 감히 청하건대, 앞으로는 저를 이런 모임이나, 말하자면 이런 타협에서 면해 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["I appeal particularly to you, honoured Pulcheria Alexandrovna, on this subject, the more as my letter was addressed to you and to no one else.”", "특히 이 점에 관해서는 존경하는 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 부인께 간곡히 호소드립니다. 제 편지가 다른 누구도 아닌 부인께 보낸 것이었으니 더더욱 그렇습니다.\""]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was a little offended.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 조금 기분이 상했다."]], [["“You seem to think we are completely under your authority, Pyotr Petrovitch.", "\"당신은 우리가 완전히 당신 손아귀에 있다고 여기시는 모양이군요, 표트르 페트로비치."], ["Dounia has told you the reason your desire was disregarded, she had the best intentions.", "두냐가 당신의 바람이 묵살된 이유를 말씀드렸잖아요, 그 애는 더없이 좋은 뜻에서 그랬어요."], ["And indeed you write as though you were laying commands upon me.", "게다가 당신은 마치 제게 명령이라도 내리듯 편지를 쓰셨더군요."], ["Are we to consider every desire of yours as a command?", "우리가 당신의 바람을 하나하나 명령으로 받들어야 하나요?"], ["Let me tell you on the contrary that you ought to show particular delicacy and consideration for us now, because we have thrown up everything, and have come here relying on you, and so we are in any case in a sense in your hands.”", "오히려 제가 말씀드리지요, 당신은 지금 우리에게 각별히 섬세하고 사려 깊게 대하셔야 마땅합니다, 우리는 모든 걸 내던지고 당신만 믿고 여기까지 왔으니까요, 그러니 어떤 의미에서 우리는 어쨌든 당신 손에 달려 있는 셈이잖아요.\""]], [["“That is not quite true, Pulcheria Alexandrovna, especially at the present moment, when the news has come of Marfa Petrovna’s legacy, which seems indeed very apropos, judging from the new tone you take to me,”", "\"그건 그리 정확한 말씀이 아닙니다, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나, 특히 지금처럼 마르파 페트로브나의 유산 소식이 전해진 마당에는요, 부인이 제게 취하시는 그 새로운 말투로 미루어 보건대, 참으로 시의적절한 소식이지요.\""], ["he added sarcastically.", "그가 빈정대며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Judging from that remark, we may certainly presume that you were reckoning on our helplessness,”", "\"그 말씀으로 미루어 보건대, 당신이 우리의 무력함을 계산에 넣고 있었다는 걸 확실히 짐작할 수 있겠군요.\""], ["Dounia observed irritably.", "두냐가 신경질적으로 한마디 했다."]], [["“But now in any case I cannot reckon on it, and I particularly desire not to hinder your discussion of the secret proposals of Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov, which he has entrusted to your brother and which have, I perceive, a great and possibly a very agreeable interest for you.”", "\"하지만 이제 어쨌든 저는 그걸 계산에 넣을 수가 없게 됐고, 특히 아르카지 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프가 당신 오라버니에게 맡긴 그 은밀한 제안, 제가 보기엔 당신께 크나큰, 어쩌면 무척 흐뭇한 관심거리가 될 그 이야기를 나누시는 데 방해가 되지 않기를 각별히 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Good heavens!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."]], [["Razumihin could not sit still on his chair.", "라주미힌은 의자에 가만히 앉아 있을 수가 없었다."]], [["“Aren’t you ashamed now, sister?”", "\"이래도 부끄럽지 않나, 누이?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 물었다."]], [["“I am ashamed, Rodya,”", "\"부끄러워요, 로쟈.\""], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."], ["“Pyotr Petrovitch, go away,”", "\"표트르 페트로비치, 가세요.\""], ["she turned to him, white with anger.", "그녀가 분노로 새파래진 얼굴로 그에게 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch had apparently not at all expected such a conclusion.", "표트르 페트로비치는 이런 결말은 전혀 예상치 못했던 게 분명했다."], ["He had too much confidence in himself, in his power and in the helplessness of his victims.", "그는 자기 자신과 자기 힘, 그리고 자기 희생자들의 무력함을 너무 굳게 믿었던 것이다."], ["He could not believe it even now.", "그는 지금 이 순간에도 이를 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["He turned pale, and his lips quivered.", "그는 창백해졌고, 입술이 떨렸다."]], [["“Avdotya Romanovna, if I go out of this door now, after such a dismissal, then, you may reckon on it, I will never come back.", "\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 만일 제가 이런 식으로 쫓겨나, 지금 이 문을 나선다면, 명심하십시오, 저는 다시는 돌아오지 않을 겁니다."], ["Consider what you are doing.", "당신이 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 잘 생각하십시오."], ["My word is not to be shaken.”", "제 말은 결코 번복되지 않습니다.\""]], [["“What insolence!”", "\"무슨 뻔뻔한 소리예요!\""], ["cried Dounia, springing up from her seat.", "두냐가 자리에서 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."], ["“I don’t want you to come back again.”", "\"다시는 돌아오지 마세요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["So that’s how it stands!”", "그래, 일이 그렇게 됐다 이거군요!\""], ["cried Luzhin, utterly unable to the last moment to believe in the rupture and so completely thrown out of his reckoning now.", "루진이 외쳤는데, 마지막 순간까지도 이 파국을 도무지 믿지 못했고, 그래서 이제 완전히 계산이 어긋나 버린 것이다."], ["“So that’s how it stands!", "\"그래, 일이 그렇게 됐단 말이지!"], ["But do you know, Avdotya Romanovna, that I might protest?”", "하지만 아십니까, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 저도 이의를 제기할 수 있다는 걸요?\""]], [["“What right have you to speak to her like that?”", "\"무슨 권리로 그 애한테 그런 식으로 말하는 거예요?\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna intervened hotly.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 발끈하여 끼어들었다."], ["“And what can you protest about?", "\"그리고 무슨 이의를 제기할 수 있다는 거죠?"], ["What rights have you?", "당신한테 무슨 권리가 있다고요?"], ["Am I to give my Dounia to a man like you?", "제가 당신 같은 사람한테 우리 두냐를 줘야 하나요?"], ["Go away, leave us altogether!", "가세요, 우리한테서 아주 떠나 버려요!"], ["We are to blame for having agreed to a wrong action, and I above all....”", "우리야말로 그릇된 일에 동의한 게 잘못이지요, 그중에서도 누구보다 제가…….\""]], [["“But you have bound me, Pulcheria Alexandrovna,”", "\"하지만 부인은 저를 옭아매셨습니다, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나,\""], ["Luzhin stormed in a frenzy, “by your promise, and now you deny it and...", "루진이 광분하여 펄펄 뛰었다. \"약속으로 말입니다, 그래 놓고 이제 와서 그걸 부인하시고……"], ["besides...", "게다가……"], ["I have been led on account of that into expenses....”", "저는 그 때문에 비용까지 들였단 말입니다…….\""]], [["This last complaint was so characteristic of Pyotr Petrovitch, that Raskolnikov, pale with anger and with the effort of restraining it, could not help breaking into laughter.", "이 마지막 푸념이 어찌나 표트르 페트로비치다웠던지, 분노로 창백해진 채 그것을 억누르느라 안간힘을 쓰던 라스콜니코프는 그만 웃음을 터뜨리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But Pulcheria Alexandrovna was furious.", "그러나 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 격분했다."]], [["“Expenses?", "\"비용이라고요?"], ["What expenses?", "무슨 비용 말인가요?"], ["Are you speaking of our trunk?", "우리 트렁크 말씀인가요?"], ["But the conductor brought it for nothing for you.", "그거야 차장이 당신을 위해 공짜로 가져다준 거잖아요."], ["Mercy on us, we have bound you!", "맙소사, 우리가 당신을 옭아맸다니요!"], ["What are you thinking about, Pyotr Petrovitch, it was you bound us, hand and foot, not we!”", "도대체 무슨 생각이세요, 표트르 페트로비치, 우리를 손발까지 옭아맨 건 바로 당신이지, 우리가 아니에요!\""]], [["“Enough, mother, no more please,”", "\"그만하세요, 어머니, 제발 더는요.\""], ["Avdotya Romanovna implored.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 애원했다."], ["“Pyotr Petrovitch, do be kind and go!”", "\"표트르 페트로비치, 부디 그만 가 주세요!\""]], [["“I am going, but one last word,”", "\"가지요, 하지만 마지막으로 한마디 하겠습니다.\""], ["he said, quite unable to control himself.", "그가 도무지 자제하지 못하고 말했다."], ["“Your mamma seems to have entirely forgotten that I made up my mind to take you, so to speak, after the gossip of the town had spread all over the district in regard to your reputation.", "\"당신 어머님께서는 까맣게 잊으신 모양인데, 제가 당신을, 말하자면 거두기로 마음먹은 것은, 당신의 평판에 관한 그 동네 소문이 온 고을에 쫙 퍼진 뒤였습니다."], ["Disregarding public opinion for your sake and reinstating your reputation, I certainly might very well reckon on a fitting return, and might indeed look for gratitude on your part.", "당신을 위해 세간의 평판을 무릅쓰고 당신의 명예를 되살려 준 만큼, 저는 마땅히 그에 걸맞은 보답을 기대할 수 있었고, 실로 당신 쪽에서 감사를 바랄 수도 있었지요."], ["And my eyes have only now been opened!", "그런데 이제야 제 눈이 뜨였습니다!"], ["I see myself that I may have acted very, very recklessly in disregarding the universal verdict....”", "제가 그 세상 사람들의 한결같은 평결을 무시한 것이, 참으로, 참으로 경솔한 짓이었을지도 모른다는 걸 이제 알겠군요…….\""]], [["“Does the fellow want his head smashed?”", "\"이자가 대가리가 박살 나고 싶은 건가?\""], ["cried Razumihin, jumping up.", "라주미힌이 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."]], [["“You are a mean and spiteful man!”", "\"당신은 비열하고 악의에 찬 인간이에요!\""], ["cried Dounia.", "두냐가 외쳤다."]], [["“Not a word!", "\"한마디도 마!"], ["Not a movement!”", "꼼짝도 마!\""], ["cried Raskolnikov, holding Razumihin back; then going close up to Luzhin, “Kindly leave the room!”", "라스콜니코프가 라주미힌을 붙잡으며 외쳤다. 그러고는 루진에게 바싹 다가가, \"이 방에서 나가 주시지.\""], ["he said quietly and distinctly, “and not a word more or...”", "그가 나직하고 또렷하게 말했다. \"한마디만 더 했다간…….\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch gazed at him for some seconds with a pale face that worked with anger, then he turned, went out, and rarely has any man carried away in his heart such vindictive hatred as he felt against Raskolnikov.", "표트르 페트로비치는 분노로 일그러진 창백한 얼굴로 몇 초 동안 그를 노려보더니, 몸을 돌려 나가 버렸다. 라스콜니코프에게 품은 만큼 앙심에 찬 증오를 가슴에 담고 떠난 사람도 드물 것이다."], ["Him, and him alone, he blamed for everything.", "그는 모든 일을 그자, 오직 그자 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["It is noteworthy that as he went downstairs he still imagined that his case was perhaps not utterly lost, and that, so far as the ladies were concerned, all might “very well indeed”", "주목할 만한 것은, 계단을 내려가면서도 그는 여전히 자기 처지가 어쩌면 완전히 끝장난 것은 아닐지도 모르며, 적어도 두 부인에 관한 한 모든 것이 '참으로 잘'"], ["be set right again.", "다시 바로잡힐 수도 있으리라고 상상하고 있었다는 점이다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["The fact was that up to the last moment he had never expected such an ending; he had been overbearing to the last degree, never dreaming that two destitute and defenceless women could escape from his control.", "사실 그는 마지막 순간까지도 이런 결말을 단 한 번도 예상하지 못했다. 그는 극도로 거만했고, 가진 것 없고 의지할 데도 없는 두 여인이 자신의 손아귀에서 벗어날 수 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했다."], ["This conviction was strengthened by his vanity and conceit, a conceit to the point of fatuity.", "이 확신은 그의 허영심과 자만심에 의해, 어리석음에 가까울 만큼 부풀어 오른 자만심에 의해 한층 강해졌다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch, who had made his way up from insignificance, was morbidly given to self-admiration, had the highest opinion of his intelligence and capacities, and sometimes even gloated in solitude over his image in the glass.", "보잘것없는 처지에서 스스로 출세한 표트르 페트로비치는 병적으로 자기 도취에 빠져 있었고, 자신의 지성과 능력을 더없이 높이 평가하며, 때로는 홀로 거울 앞에서 자기 모습에 흐뭇해하기까지 했다."], ["But what he loved and valued above all was the money he had amassed by his labour, and by all sorts of devices: that money made him the equal of all who had been his superiors.", "그러나 그가 무엇보다 사랑하고 소중히 여긴 것은 자신의 노력과 온갖 술수로 긁어모은 돈이었다. 그 돈이 한때 그의 윗사람이었던 모든 이들과 그를 대등한 존재로 만들어 주었기 때문이다."]], [["When he had bitterly reminded Dounia that he had decided to take her in spite of evil report, Pyotr Petrovitch had spoken with perfect sincerity and had, indeed, felt genuinely indignant at such “black ingratitude.”", "표트르 페트로비치가 두냐에게 나쁜 소문에도 불구하고 그녀를 아내로 맞기로 결심했노라고 쓰라리게 일깨웠을 때, 그는 진심으로 말한 것이었고, 실제로 그런 \"새카만 배은망덕\"에 진정으로 분개하고 있었다."], ["And yet, when he made Dounia his offer, he was fully aware of the groundlessness of all the gossip.", "그런데도 그가 두냐에게 청혼했던 그때, 그는 그 모든 풍문이 근거 없는 것임을 충분히 알고 있었다."], ["The story had been everywhere contradicted by Marfa Petrovna, and was by then disbelieved by all the townspeople, who were warm in Dounia’s defence.", "그 이야기는 이미 마르파 페트로브나에 의해 곳곳에서 부정되었고, 두냐를 열렬히 옹호하던 마을 사람들 모두가 그것을 믿지 않게 되었다."], ["And he would not have denied that he knew all that at the time.", "그리고 그는 자신이 그 당시 이 모든 것을 알고 있었음을 부인하지 않았으리라."], ["Yet he still thought highly of his own resolution in lifting Dounia to his level and regarded it as something heroic.", "그럼에도 그는 두냐를 자기 수준으로 끌어올리겠다는 자신의 결단을 여전히 높이 평가했고, 그것을 무슨 영웅적인 행위쯤으로 여겼다."], ["In speaking of it to Dounia, he had let out the secret feeling he cherished and admired, and he could not understand that others should fail to admire it too.", "두냐에게 그 이야기를 하면서 그는 자신이 품고 흠모하던 은밀한 감정을 드러내고 말았고, 다른 사람들이 그것을 함께 흠모하지 않는다는 것을 도무지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["He had called on Raskolnikov with the feelings of a benefactor who is about to reap the fruits of his good deeds and to hear agreeable flattery.", "그는 자신의 선행의 열매를 거두고 기분 좋은 아첨을 듣게 되리라 기대하는 은인의 심정으로 라스콜니코프를 찾아왔던 것이다."], ["And as he went downstairs now, he considered himself most undeservedly injured and unrecognised.", "그리고 지금 계단을 내려가면서 그는 자신이 너무도 부당하게 모욕당하고 인정받지 못했다고 여겼다."]], [["Dounia was simply essential to him; to do without her was unthinkable.", "두냐는 그에게 그야말로 없어서는 안 될 존재였다. 그녀 없이 지낸다는 것은 상상조차 할 수 없었다."], ["For many years he had had voluptuous dreams of marriage, but he had gone on waiting and amassing money.", "그는 오랜 세월 동안 결혼에 대한 음탕한 공상을 품어 왔지만, 그저 기다리며 돈을 긁어모았다."], ["He brooded with relish, in profound secret, over the image of a girl--virtuous, poor (she must be poor), very young, very pretty, of good birth and education, very timid, one who had suffered much, and was completely humbled before him, one who would all her life look on him as her saviour, worship him, admire him and only him.", "그는 깊디깊은 비밀 속에서 한 처녀의 모습을 흐뭇하게 곱씹곤 했다. 정숙하고, 가난하고(반드시 가난해야 했다), 아주 젊고, 아주 예쁘며, 좋은 집안에서 태어나 교육을 받았으되, 몹시 수줍어하고, 많은 고생을 겪었으며, 그의 앞에서 완전히 자신을 낮추는, 평생토록 그를 자신의 구원자로 우러러보며 숭배하고, 오직 그만을 흠모할 그런 처녀를."], ["How many scenes, how many amorous episodes he had imagined on this seductive and playful theme, when his work was over!", "일을 마친 뒤면 이 매혹적이고 짓궂은 주제를 두고 그는 얼마나 많은 장면을, 얼마나 많은 사랑의 일화를 상상해 보았던가!"], ["And, behold, the dream of so many years was all but realised; the beauty and education of Avdotya Romanovna had impressed him; her helpless position had been a great allurement; in her he had found even more than he dreamed of.", "그런데 보라, 그 오랜 세월의 꿈이 거의 실현되려던 참이었다. 아브도티야 로마노브나의 아름다움과 교양은 그를 사로잡았고, 그녀의 무력한 처지는 더없는 유혹이었으며, 그는 그녀에게서 자신이 꿈꿔 온 것 이상을 발견했던 것이다."], ["Here was a girl of pride, character, virtue, of education and breeding superior to his own (he felt that), and this creature would be slavishly grateful all her life for his heroic condescension, and would humble herself in the dust before him, and he would have absolute, unbounded power over her!...", "여기 자존심과 개성과 미덕을 갖추고, 자신보다 우월한 교양과 가정교육을 받은(그는 그것을 느꼈다) 처녀가 있었으니, 이 여인은 자신의 영웅적인 너그러움에 평생토록 노예처럼 감사할 것이고, 그의 발밑에서 먼지 속에 자신을 낮출 것이며, 그는 그녀에 대해 절대적이고 무한한 권력을 쥐게 될 터였다!……"], ["Not long before, he had, too, after long reflection and hesitation, made an important change in his career and was now entering on a wider circle of business.", "게다가 얼마 전 그는 오랜 숙고와 망설임 끝에 자신의 경력에 중대한 변화를 주어, 이제 한층 넓은 사업의 무대로 발을 들이고 있었다."], ["With this change his cherished dreams of rising into a higher class of society seemed likely to be realised....", "이 변화와 더불어, 더 높은 계층의 사교계로 올라서겠다는 그의 소중한 꿈도 실현될 듯 보였다……"], ["He was, in fact, determined to try his fortune in Petersburg.", "사실 그는 페테르부르크에서 자신의 운을 시험해 보기로 결심한 터였다."], ["He knew that women could do a very great deal.", "그는 여자가 아주 많은 일을 해낼 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["The fascination of a charming, virtuous, highly educated woman might make his way easier, might do wonders in attracting people to him, throwing an aureole round him, and now everything was in ruins!", "매력적이고 정숙하며 높은 교양을 갖춘 여인의 매혹은 그의 길을 한결 수월하게 만들고, 사람들을 그에게로 끌어들이며 그를 후광으로 감싸는, 그런 기적을 일으킬 수도 있었다. 그런데 지금 모든 것이 폐허가 되어 버린 것이다!"], ["This sudden horrible rupture affected him like a clap of thunder; it was like a hideous joke, an absurdity.", "이 갑작스럽고 끔찍한 파국은 그에게 마치 벼락처럼 들이닥쳤다. 그것은 흉측한 농담이자 부조리 같았다."], ["He had only been a tiny bit masterful, had not even time to speak out, had simply made a joke, been carried away--and it had ended so seriously.", "그는 그저 아주 조금 위세를 부렸을 뿐이고, 미처 속내를 다 털어놓을 새도 없었으며, 그저 농담을 했을 뿐이고, 흥에 겨워 휩쓸렸을 뿐인데 — 그것이 이렇게 심각하게 끝나 버린 것이다. 그리고 물론 그는 그 나름의 방식으로 두냐를 사랑하고 있었다."], ["And, of course, too, he did love Dounia in his own way; he already possessed her in his dreams--and all at once!", "그는 이미 공상 속에서 그녀를 소유하고 있었는데 — 그것이 단번에! 안 돼!"], ["No! The next day, the very next day, it must all be set right, smoothed over, settled.", "내일, 바로 내일이면 이 모든 것을 바로잡고, 매끄럽게 다독여, 결말지어야 한다."], ["Above all he must crush that conceited milksop who was the cause of it all.", "무엇보다도 이 모든 일의 원흉인 그 거만한 풋내기를 짓밟아 버려야 한다."], ["With a sick feeling he could not help recalling Razumihin too, but, he soon reassured himself on that score; as though a fellow like that could be put on a level with him!", "그는 메스꺼운 기분 속에서 라주미힌도 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으나, 곧 그 점에 대해서는 마음을 놓았다. 그따위 작자를 자신과 동등한 위치에 놓을 수 있기나 하겠는가!"], ["The man he really dreaded in earnest was Svidrigaïlov....", "그가 진정으로 두려워한 사내는 스비드리가일로프였다……"], ["He had, in short, a great deal to attend to....", "요컨대, 그에게는 신경 써야 할 일이 한둘이 아니었다……"]], [["*****\n“No, I, I am more to blame than anyone!”", "\"아니에요, 제가, 제가 누구보다 잘못했어요!\""], ["said Dounia, kissing and embracing her mother.", "두냐가 어머니에게 입을 맞추고 끌어안으며 말했다."], ["“I was tempted by his money, but on my honour, brother, I had no idea he was such a base man.", "\"전 그 사람 돈에 마음이 흔들렸던 거예요, 하지만 맹세코, 오빠, 그가 그렇게 천박한 인간일 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["If I had seen through him before, nothing would have tempted me!", "진작에 그 속을 꿰뚫어 봤더라면, 그 무엇으로도 절 흔들지 못했을 거예요!"], ["Don’t blame me, brother!”", "절 나무라지 말아요, 오빠!\""]], [["“God has delivered us!", "\"하느님께서 우리를 구원하셨다!"], ["God has delivered us!”", "하느님께서 우리를 구원하셨어!\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna muttered, but half consciously, as though scarcely able to realise what had happened.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 중얼거렸으나, 그것은 반쯤 정신이 나간 채로, 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지조차 거의 실감하지 못하는 듯한 것이었다."]], [["They were all relieved, and in five minutes they were laughing.", "모두가 안도했고, 오 분 뒤에는 웃음을 터뜨리고 있었다."], ["Only now and then Dounia turned white and frowned, remembering what had passed.", "다만 두냐만이 이따금 지나간 일을 떠올리며 새파랗게 질려 눈살을 찌푸렸다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was surprised to find that she, too, was glad: she had only that morning thought rupture with Luzhin a terrible misfortune.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 자신마저 기뻐하고 있다는 데 놀랐다. 그녀는 바로 그날 아침만 해도 루진과의 결별을 끔찍한 불행으로 여겼던 것이다."], ["Razumihin was delighted.", "라주미힌은 기뻐 어쩔 줄 몰랐다."], ["He did not yet dare to express his joy fully, but he was in a fever of excitement as though a ton-weight had fallen off his heart.", "아직 그 기쁨을 마음껏 드러낼 엄두는 나지 않았으나, 마치 가슴에서 천근 무게가 떨어져 나간 듯 흥분으로 들떠 있었다."], ["Now he had the right to devote his life to them, to serve them....", "이제 그는 자신의 삶을 그들에게 바치고, 그들을 위해 헌신할 권리를 얻은 것이다……"], ["Anything might happen now!", "이제 무슨 일이든 일어날 수 있었다!"], ["But he felt afraid to think of further possibilities and dared not let his imagination range.", "그러나 그는 그 이상의 가능성을 생각하기가 두려웠고, 감히 상상의 나래를 펴지 못했다."], ["But Raskolnikov sat still in the same place, almost sullen and indifferent.", "그러나 라스콜니코프는 같은 자리에 가만히 앉은 채, 거의 시무룩하고 무관심해 보였다."], ["Though he had been the most insistent on getting rid of Luzhin, he seemed now the least concerned at what had happened.", "루진을 떼어 내자고 누구보다 강하게 고집했던 그가, 정작 지금 벌어진 일에는 가장 무심한 듯했다."], ["Dounia could not help thinking that he was still angry with her, and Pulcheria Alexandrovna watched him timidly.", "두냐는 그가 여전히 자신에게 화가 나 있는 것이라고 생각하지 않을 수 없었고, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 겁먹은 듯 그를 지켜보았다."]], [["“What did Svidrigaïlov say to you?”", "\"스비드리가일로프가 오빠한테 무슨 말을 했어요?\""], ["said Dounia, approaching him.", "두냐가 그에게 다가서며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"그래, 그래!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."]], [["Raskolnikov raised his head.", "라스콜니코프가 고개를 들었다."]], [["“He wants to make you a present of ten thousand roubles and he desires to see you once in my presence.”", "\"그자는 너에게 만 루블을 선사하고 싶어 하고, 내가 있는 자리에서 너를 한 번 만나 보길 바라더구나.\""]], [["“See her!", "\"그애를 만나겠다고요!"], ["On no account!”", "절대로 안 돼요!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."], ["“And how dare he offer her money!”", "\"그리고 감히 그애에게 돈을 주겠다니!\""]], [["Then Raskolnikov repeated (rather dryly) his conversation with Svidrigaïlov, omitting his account of the ghostly visitations of Marfa Petrovna, wishing to avoid all unnecessary talk.", "그러고 나서 라스콜니코프는 (꽤 무미건조하게) 스비드리가일로프와의 대화를 되풀이해 들려주었는데, 불필요한 이야기는 모두 피하고 싶어서 마르파 페트로브나의 유령이 나타난 일에 대해서는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“What answer did you give him?”", "\"그래서 오빠는 그에게 뭐라고 대답했어요?\""], ["asked Dounia.", "두냐가 물었다."]], [["“At first I said I would not take any message to you.", "\"처음엔 너에게 아무런 전갈도 전하지 않겠다고 했지."], ["Then he said that he would do his utmost to obtain an interview with you without my help.", "그랬더니 그자는 내 도움 없이도 너와의 면담을 얻어 내려 온 힘을 다하겠다고 하더구나."], ["He assured me that his passion for you was a passing infatuation, now he has no feeling for you.", "너를 향한 자신의 열정은 한때의 도취였을 뿐, 이제는 너에게 아무 감정도 없다고 장담하면서."], ["He doesn’t want you to marry Luzhin....", "그자는 네가 루진과 결혼하는 걸 원치 않더라……"], ["His talk was altogether rather muddled.”", "그의 말은 통틀어 꽤 횡설수설이었어.\""]], [["“How do you explain him to yourself, Rodya?", "\"오빠는 그 사람을 어떻게 이해해요, 로쟈?"], ["How did he strike you?”", "어떤 인상을 받았어요?\""]], [["“I must confess I don’t quite understand him.", "\"솔직히 나도 그자를 잘 이해하진 못하겠다."], ["He offers you ten thousand, and yet says he is not well off.", "그자는 너에게 만 루블을 내놓으면서도, 정작 자기는 형편이 넉넉지 않다고 하더구나."], ["He says he is going away, and in ten minutes he forgets he has said it.", "떠나겠다고 해 놓고는 십 분도 안 돼 그 말을 했다는 걸 잊어버려."], ["Then he says he is going to be married and has already fixed on the girl....", "그러다가 또 결혼할 거라면서 이미 신붓감까지 점찍어 두었다고 하지……"], ["No doubt he has a motive, and probably a bad one.", "분명 무슨 속셈이 있는 거고, 아마 좋지 못한 속셈일 게다."], ["But it’s odd that he should be so clumsy about it if he had any designs against you....", "하지만 너에게 무슨 흉계가 있다면, 그렇게 서툴게 군다는 게 이상해……"], ["Of course, I refused this money on your account, once for all.", "물론 나는 너를 위해 단호히, 한 번에 그 돈을 거절했다."], ["Altogether, I thought him very strange....", "통틀어, 나는 그자가 무척 이상하다고 생각했어……"], ["One might almost think he was mad.", "거의 미친 사람이라고 여겨도 될 정도야."], ["But I may be mistaken; that may only be the part he assumes.", "하지만 내가 틀렸을지도 모르지. 어쩌면 그저 그자가 꾸며 내는 역할일 뿐인지도 몰라."], ["The death of Marfa Petrovna seems to have made a great impression on him.”", "마르파 페트로브나의 죽음이 그자에게 크나큰 충격을 준 모양이더라.\""]], [["“God rest her soul,”", "\"그분의 영혼이 평안하시기를.\""], ["exclaimed Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."], ["“I shall always, always pray for her!", "\"나는 늘, 언제까지나 그분을 위해 기도할 거야!"], ["Where should we be now, Dounia, without this three thousand!", "두냐, 이 삼천 루블이 없었다면 우리가 지금 어찌 됐겠니!"], ["It’s as though it had fallen from heaven!", "마치 하늘에서 떨어진 것 같구나!"], ["Why, Rodya, this morning we had only three roubles in our pocket and Dounia and I were just planning to pawn her watch, so as to avoid borrowing from that man until he offered help.”", "글쎄, 로쟈, 오늘 아침만 해도 우리 주머니엔 고작 삼 루블밖에 없어서, 그 사람이 도와주겠다고 나서기 전까진 그자에게 빌리지 않으려고 두냐와 내가 막 그애 시계를 저당 잡힐 궁리를 하던 참이었단다.\""]], [["Dounia seemed strangely impressed by Svidrigaïlov’s offer.", "두냐는 스비드리가일로프의 제안에 묘하게 마음이 동요된 듯했다."], ["She still stood meditating.", "그녀는 여전히 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 채 서 있었다."]], [["“He has got some terrible plan,”", "\"그 사람에겐 무슨 끔찍한 계획이 있어요.\""], ["she said in a half whisper to herself, almost shuddering.", "그녀가 거의 몸서리치며, 반쯤 속삭이듯 혼잣말로 말했다."]], [["Raskolnikov noticed this disproportionate terror.", "라스콜니코프는 이 지나친 공포를 눈여겨보았다."]], [["“I fancy I shall have to see him more than once again,”", "\"아무래도 나는 그자를 한 번 이상 더 만나야 할 것 같구나.\""], ["he said to Dounia.", "그가 두냐에게 말했다."]], [["“We will watch him!", "\"우리가 그자를 감시하지요!"], ["I will track him out!”", "내가 그자를 끝까지 추적하겠습니다!\""], ["cried Razumihin, vigorously.", "라주미힌이 힘차게 외쳤다."], ["“I won’t lose sight of him.", "\"그자에게서 눈을 떼지 않을 겁니다."], ["Rodya has given me leave.", "로쟈가 나에게 허락했어요."], ["He said to me himself just now.", "방금 자기 입으로 직접 말했죠."], ["‘Take care of my sister.’ Will you give me leave, too, Avdotya Romanovna?”", "'내 누이를 돌봐 줘'라고요. 당신도 나에게 허락해 주시겠습니까, 아브도티야 로마노브나?\""]], [["Dounia smiled and held out her hand, but the look of anxiety did not leave her face.", "두냐는 미소 지으며 손을 내밀었으나, 얼굴에서 근심의 빛은 가시지 않았다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna gazed at her timidly, but the three thousand roubles had obviously a soothing effect on her.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 겁먹은 듯 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그래도 삼천 루블이 그녀에게 위안을 주는 효과를 발휘하고 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["A quarter of an hour later, they were all engaged in a lively conversation.", "십오 분쯤 뒤, 그들은 모두 활기찬 대화에 빠져 있었다."], ["Even Raskolnikov listened attentively for some time, though he did not talk.", "라스콜니코프조차 한동안 — 비록 말은 하지 않았으나 — 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."], ["Razumihin was the speaker.", "말하는 이는 라주미힌이었다."]], [["“And why, why should you go away?”", "\"그런데 왜, 왜 떠나려고 하십니까?\""], ["he flowed on ecstatically.", "그가 황홀해하며 거침없이 말을 쏟아 냈다."], ["“And what are you to do in a little town?", "\"작은 시골 마을에서 대체 무얼 하시려고요?"], ["The great thing is, you are all here together and you need one another--you do need one another, believe me.", "가장 중요한 건, 여러분 모두가 여기 함께 있고 서로가 서로를 필요로 한다는 겁니다 — 정말 서로가 필요해요, 믿어 주십시오."], ["For a time, anyway....", "적어도 한동안은요……"], ["Take me into partnership, and I assure you we’ll plan a capital enterprise. Listen!", "저를 동업자로 받아들여 주십시오, 그러면 우리가 멋진 사업을 꾸려 낼 거라고 장담합니다."], ["I’ll explain it all in detail to you, the whole project!", "들어 보세요! 제가 다 자세히 설명해 드리지요, 계획 전부를요!"], ["It all flashed into my head this morning, before anything had happened...", "오늘 아침, 아무 일도 벌어지기 전에 이 모든 게 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐답니다……"], ["I tell you what; I have an uncle, I must introduce him to you (a most accommodating and respectable old man).", "자, 이런 겁니다. 저에게 백부님 한 분이 계신데, 여러분께 꼭 소개해 드려야겠어요(더없이 너그럽고 점잖은 노인이시지요)."], ["This uncle has got a capital of a thousand roubles, and he lives on his pension and has no need of that money.", "이 백부님께 천 루블의 자본이 있는데, 연금으로 사시니 그 돈이 필요 없으십니다."], ["For the last two years he has been bothering me to borrow it from him and pay him six per cent interest.", "지난 이 년 동안 백부님은 자기한테서 그 돈을 빌려 가 육 푼 이자를 내라고 저를 들볶으셨어요."], ["I know what that means; he simply wants to help me.", "그게 무슨 뜻인지 저는 압니다. 그저 저를 돕고 싶으신 거예요."], ["Last year I had no need of it, but this year I resolved to borrow it as soon as he arrived.", "작년엔 그 돈이 필요 없었지만, 올해는 백부님이 도착하시는 대로 빌리기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["Then you lend me another thousand of your three and we have enough for a start, so we’ll go into partnership, and what are we going to do?”", "그러면 여러분이 가진 삼천 루블 중에서 천 루블을 저에게 빌려 주시고, 그러면 우리에겐 시작할 만한 충분한 밑천이 생기니, 동업을 하는 거죠. 그래서 우리가 무얼 하느냐고요?\""]], [["Then Razumihin began to unfold his project, and he explained at length that almost all our publishers and booksellers know nothing at all of what they are selling, and for that reason they are usually bad publishers, and that any decent publications pay as a rule and give a profit, sometimes a considerable one.", "이어서 라주미힌은 자신의 계획을 펼쳐 보이기 시작했는데, 우리네 출판업자와 서적상은 거의 전부가 자기네가 파는 것이 무엇인지 도통 모르고, 그런 까닭에 대개는 형편없는 출판업자이며, 어지간히 괜찮은 출판물은 으레 수지가 맞고 이윤을, 때로는 상당한 이윤을 안겨 준다는 것을 장황하게 설명했다."], ["Razumihin had, indeed, been dreaming of setting up as a publisher.", "사실 라주미힌은 출판업자로 자리 잡는 것을 꿈꿔 왔다."], ["For the last two years he had been working in publishers’ offices, and knew three European languages well, though he had told Raskolnikov six days before that he was “schwach” in German with an object of persuading him to take half his translation and half the payment for it.", "지난 이 년 동안 그는 출판사에서 일하며 유럽 언어 세 가지에 능통했는데, 엿새 전에 라스콜니코프에게는 자신이 독일어가 \"취약하다\"고 말한 적이 있었으니, 그것은 번역의 절반과 그 보수의 절반을 라스콜니코프가 맡도록 설득하려는 속셈에서였다."], ["He had told a lie then, and Raskolnikov knew he was lying.", "그때 그는 거짓말을 한 것이었고, 라스콜니코프도 그가 거짓말하고 있다는 걸 알고 있었다."]], [["“Why, why should we let our chance slip when we have one of the chief means of success--money of our own!”", "\"아니, 왜, 성공의 가장 중요한 수단 가운데 하나인 자기 자본이 있는데도 우리가 이 기회를 놓쳐야 합니까!\""], ["cried Razumihin warmly.", "라주미힌이 열을 올려 외쳤다."], ["“Of course there will be a lot of work, but we will work, you, Avdotya Romanovna, I, Rodion....", "\"물론 일거리야 많겠지만, 우리가 일하면 됩니다, 당신이, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 그리고 저, 로지온이……"], ["You get a splendid profit on some books nowadays!", "요즘은 어떤 책으로는 굉장한 이윤을 거둘 수 있어요!"], ["And the great point of the business is that we shall know just what wants translating, and we shall be translating, publishing, learning all at once.", "그리고 이 사업의 핵심은, 우리가 무엇을 번역해야 할지 정확히 알게 되리라는 점이고, 우리가 번역하고, 출판하고, 익히는 일을 한꺼번에 해낸다는 겁니다."], ["I can be of use because I have experience.", "저는 경험이 있으니 쓸모가 있을 거예요."], ["For nearly two years I’ve been scuttling about among the publishers, and now I know every detail of their business.", "거의 이 년 동안 출판업자들 틈을 분주히 누비고 다녔으니, 이제 그쪽 일을 세세한 데까지 다 압니다."], ["You need not be a saint to make pots, believe me!", "단지를 빚는 데 성인(聖人)이 될 필요는 없는 법이죠, 믿어 주십시오!"], ["And why, why should we let our chance slip!", "그런데 왜, 왜 우리가 이 기회를 놓쳐야 합니까!"], ["Why, I know--and I kept the secret--two or three books which one might get a hundred roubles simply for thinking of translating and publishing.", "글쎄요, 저는 알고 있어요 — 비밀로 간직해 왔지만 — 그저 번역해서 출판할 생각만 해도 백 루블쯤은 거뜬히 받아 낼 만한 책이 두세 권 있다는 걸 말입니다."], ["Indeed, and I would not take five hundred for the very idea of one of them.", "정말이지, 그중 한 권에 대한 착상만 가지고도 저는 오백 루블을 줘도 넘기지 않을 겁니다."], ["And what do you think?", "그런데 어떨 것 같습니까?"], ["If I were to tell a publisher, I dare say he’d hesitate--they are such blockheads!", "제가 출판업자에게 말한들, 아마 그자는 망설이기나 하겠죠 — 그들은 어찌나 멍청한지!"], ["And as for the business side, printing, paper, selling, you trust to me, I know my way about.", "그리고 사업적인 면, 그러니까 인쇄며 종이며 판매 같은 건 저를 믿고 맡겨 주십시오, 그쪽 길은 제가 훤히 압니다."], ["We’ll begin in a small way and go on to a large.", "우리는 작게 시작해서 크게 키워 나가는 겁니다."], ["In any case it will get us our living and we shall get back our capital.”", "어떻게 되든 이 일이 우리 입에 풀칠을 시켜 줄 것이고, 우리 자본도 되찾게 될 거예요.\""]], [["Dounia’s eyes shone.", "두냐의 눈이 빛났다."]], [["“I like what you are saying, Dmitri Prokofitch!”", "\"당신 말씀이 마음에 들어요, 드미트리 프로코피치!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I know nothing about it, of course,”", "\"나야 물론 그런 건 통 모르지만,\""], ["put in Pulcheria Alexandrovna, “it may be a good idea, but again God knows.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 끼어들었다. \"좋은 생각일 수도 있겠지요, 하지만 그것도 하느님만 아실 일이에요."], ["It’s new and untried.", "새롭고 또 해 본 적 없는 일이니까요."], ["Of course, we must remain here at least for a time.”", "물론, 우리는 적어도 한동안은 여기 머물러야겠지요.\""], ["She looked at Rodya.", "그녀는 로쟈를 바라보았다."]], [["“What do you think, brother?”", "\"오빠는 어떻게 생각해요?\""], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 물었다."]], [["“I think he’s got a very good idea,”", "\"내가 보기엔 아주 좋은 생각이야.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Of course, it’s too soon to dream of a publishing firm, but we certainly might bring out five or six books and be sure of success.", "\"물론 출판사를 차리겠다는 꿈을 꾸기엔 너무 이르지만, 다섯 권이나 여섯 권쯤은 분명 펴낼 수 있을 테고, 그 성공은 틀림없을 거다."], ["I know of one book myself which would be sure to go well.", "나도 분명 잘 팔릴 책 한 권을 알고 있어."], ["And as for his being able to manage it, there’s no doubt about that either.", "그리고 저 친구가 그 일을 잘 꾸려 갈 수 있는가 하면, 그것도 의심할 여지가 없지."], ["He knows the business....", "저 친구는 그 일을 잘 아니까……"], ["But we can talk it over later....”", "하지만 그건 나중에 의논해 보면 돼……\""]], [["“Hurrah!”", "\"만세!\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."], ["“Now, stay, there’s a flat here in this house, belonging to the same owner.", "\"자, 가만, 이 건물 안에 셋방이 하나 있는데, 같은 주인 소유랍니다."], ["It’s a special flat apart, not communicating with these lodgings.", "여기 이 하숙과는 통하지 않는, 따로 떨어진 별채 같은 셋방이에요."], ["It’s furnished, rent moderate, three rooms.", "가구도 딸려 있고, 집세도 적당하고, 방이 셋입니다."], ["Suppose you take them to begin with.", "우선 그걸 빌리시는 게 어떨까요."], ["I’ll pawn your watch to-morrow and bring you the money, and everything can be arranged then.", "제가 내일 당신네 시계를 저당 잡혀 돈을 마련해 드릴 테니, 그러면 모든 게 다 처리될 겁니다."], ["You can all three live together, and Rodya will be with you.", "세 분이 함께 사실 수 있고, 로쟈도 여러분과 함께 있게 되는 거죠."], ["But where are you off to, Rodya?”", "그런데 어디 가는 거야, 로쟈?\""]], [["“What, Rodya, you are going already?”", "\"뭐라고, 로쟈, 벌써 가려고?\""], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna asked in dismay.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 당황하여 물었다."]], [["“At such a minute?”", "\"이런 순간에?\""], ["cried Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 외쳤다."]], [["Dounia looked at her brother with incredulous wonder.", "두냐는 믿기지 않는다는 듯 놀라며 오빠를 바라보았다."], ["He held his cap in his hand, he was preparing to leave them.", "그는 모자를 손에 쥔 채, 그들을 두고 떠날 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["“One would think you were burying me or saying good-bye for ever,”", "\"마치 나를 묻으러 가거나 영영 작별 인사라도 하는 것 같구나.\""], ["he said somewhat oddly.", "그가 어딘지 묘하게 말했다."], ["He attempted to smile, but it did not turn out a smile.", "그는 미소 지으려 했으나, 그것은 미소가 되지 못했다."], ["“But who knows, perhaps it is the last time we shall see each other...”", "\"하지만 누가 알겠어, 어쩌면 우리가 서로를 보는 게 이번이 마지막일지도……\""], ["he let slip accidentally.", "그가 무심코 말을 흘렸다."], ["It was what he was thinking, and it somehow was uttered aloud.", "그것은 그가 생각하고 있던 바였는데, 어쩌다 소리 내어 입 밖으로 나와 버린 것이었다."]], [["“What is the matter with you?”", "\"너 무슨 일이니?\""], ["cried his mother.", "그의 어머니가 외쳤다."]], [["“Where are you going, Rodya?”", "\"어디 가는 거예요, 로쟈?\""], ["asked Dounia rather strangely.", "두냐가 다소 이상하다는 듯 물었다."]], [["“Oh, I’m quite obliged to...”", "\"아, 난 꼭 가야만 해서……\""], ["he answered vaguely, as though hesitating what he would say.", "그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 머뭇거리듯 막연하게 대답했다."], ["But there was a look of sharp determination in his white face.", "그러나 핏기 없는 그의 얼굴에는 날카로운 결의의 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["“I meant to say...", "\"내가 하려던 말은……"], ["as I was coming here...", "여기로 오는 길에……"], ["I meant to tell you, mother, and you, Dounia, that it would be better for us to part for a time.", "어머니, 그리고 두냐, 너에게 말하려던 건, 우리가 한동안 떨어져 지내는 편이 좋겠다는 거였어."], ["I feel ill, I am not at peace....", "난 몸이 아프고, 마음이 편치 않아……"], ["I will come afterwards, I will come of myself...", "나중에 갈게, 내 발로 찾아갈게……"], ["when it’s possible.", "그게 가능해지면."], ["I remember you and love you....", "나는 어머니와 너를 기억하고 사랑해……"], ["Leave me, leave me alone.", "날 내버려 둬, 나를 혼자 있게 해 줘."], ["I decided this even before...", "난 이걸 그전부터 이미 결심했어……"], ["I’m absolutely resolved on it.", "절대로 마음을 바꾸지 않을 거야."], ["Whatever may come to me, whether I come to ruin or not, I want to be alone.", "나에게 무슨 일이 닥치든, 내가 파멸하든 말든, 나는 혼자 있고 싶어."], ["Forget me altogether, it’s better.", "날 아주 잊어버려, 그게 더 나아."], ["Don’t inquire about me.", "내 소식은 묻지 마."], ["When I can, I’ll come of myself or...", "가능해지면 내가 직접 가거나…… 사람을 보낼게."], ["I’ll send for you. Perhaps it will all come back, but now if you love me, give me up...", "어쩌면 모든 게 제자리로 돌아올지도 몰라, 하지만 지금은, 나를 사랑한다면, 나를 단념해 줘……"], ["else I shall begin to hate you, I feel it....", "안 그러면 난 어머니와 너를 미워하기 시작할 거야, 그게 느껴져……"], ["Good-bye!”", "안녕히!\""]], [["“Good God!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""], ["cried Pulcheria Alexandrovna.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 외쳤다."], ["Both his mother and his sister were terribly alarmed.", "그의 어머니와 누이 모두 끔찍하게 놀랐다."], ["Razumihin was also.", "라주미힌도 마찬가지였다."]], [["“Rodya, Rodya, be reconciled with us!", "\"로쟈, 로쟈, 우리와 화해하자!"], ["Let us be as before!”", "예전처럼 지내자꾸나!\""], ["cried his poor mother.", "가엾은 어머니가 외쳤다."]], [["He turned slowly to the door and slowly went out of the room.", "그는 천천히 문 쪽으로 몸을 돌려, 천천히 방을 나섰다."], ["Dounia overtook him.", "두냐가 그를 따라잡았다."]], [["“Brother, what are you doing to mother?”", "\"오빠, 어머니한테 무슨 짓을 하는 거예요?\""], ["she whispered, her eyes flashing with indignation.", "그녀가 분노로 눈을 번득이며 속삭였다."]], [["He looked dully at her.", "그는 멍하니 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“No matter, I shall come....", "\"괜찮아, 갈 테니까……"], ["I’m coming,”", "갈게.\""], ["he muttered in an undertone, as though not fully conscious of what he was saying, and he went out of the room.", "그가 마치 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 온전히 의식하지 못하는 듯 낮은 목소리로 중얼거리고는, 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Wicked, heartless egoist!”", "\"못되고 무정한 이기주의자!\""], ["cried Dounia.", "두냐가 외쳤다."]], [["“He is insane, but not heartless.", "\"저 친구는 제정신이 아니에요, 하지만 무정한 건 아닙니다."], ["He is mad!", "미친 거예요!"], ["Don’t you see it?", "그게 안 보이십니까?"], ["You’re heartless after that!”", "그런 말을 하다니, 당신이야말로 무정하군요!\""], ["Razumihin whispered in her ear, squeezing her hand tightly.", "라주미힌이 그녀의 귀에 대고 속삭이며, 그녀의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐었다."], ["“I shall be back directly,”", "\"곧 돌아오겠습니다.\""], ["he shouted to the horror-stricken mother, and he ran out of the room.", "그가 공포에 질린 어머니에게 소리치고는, 방 밖으로 뛰어나갔다."]], [["Raskolnikov was waiting for him at the end of the passage.", "라스콜니코프는 복도 끝에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“I knew you would run after me,”", "\"네가 나를 쫓아 달려 나올 줄 알았어.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Go back to them--be with them...", "\"그분들께 돌아가, 그분들과 함께 있어 줘……"], ["be with them to-morrow and always....", "내일도, 그리고 언제까지나 그분들 곁에 있어 줘……"], ["I...", "나는……"], ["perhaps I shall come...", "어쩌면 내가 갈지도 몰라……"], ["if I can.", "갈 수 있으면."], ["Good-bye.”", "잘 있어.\""]], [["And without holding out his hand he walked away.", "그러고는 손도 내밀지 않은 채 그는 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["“But where are you going?", "\"그런데 어디로 가는 거야?"], ["What are you doing?", "뭘 하려는 거야?"], ["What’s the matter with you?", "너 무슨 일이야?"], ["How can you go on like this?”", "어떻게 이런 식으로 계속 가 버릴 수가 있어?\""], ["Razumihin muttered, at his wits’ end.", "라주미힌이 어쩔 줄 몰라 중얼거렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov stopped once more.", "라스콜니코프가 다시 한번 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Once for all, never ask me about anything.", "\"단번에 말해 두는데, 나에 대해서는 절대 아무것도 묻지 마."], ["I have nothing to tell you.", "너에게 들려줄 말은 아무것도 없어."], ["Don’t come to see me.", "나를 보러 오지도 마."], ["Maybe I’ll come here....", "어쩌면 내가 여기로 올지도 모르지……"], ["Leave me, but _don’t leave_ them.", "나는 내버려 두고, 그분들은 떠나지 마."], ["Do you understand me?”", "내 말 알아듣겠어?\""]], [["It was dark in the corridor, they were standing near the lamp.", "복도 안은 어두웠고, 그들은 등불 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["For a minute they were looking at one another in silence.", "한순간 그들은 말없이 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Razumihin remembered that minute all his life.", "라주미힌은 평생토록 그 순간을 기억했다."], ["Raskolnikov’s burning and intent eyes grew more penetrating every moment, piercing into his soul, into his consciousness.", "라스콜니코프의 불타는 듯 골똘한 두 눈이 매 순간 점점 더 꿰뚫는 듯해져, 그의 영혼 속으로, 그의 의식 속으로 파고들었다."], ["Suddenly Razumihin started.", "갑자기 라주미힌이 움찔했다."], ["Something strange, as it were, passed between them....", "무언가 기이한 것이, 말하자면 두 사람 사이를 스쳐 지나갔다……"], ["Some idea, some hint, as it were, slipped, something awful, hideous, and suddenly understood on both sides....", "어떤 생각이, 어떤 암시 같은 것이 새어 나왔다. 무언가 무섭고, 흉측하며, 양쪽 모두가 불현듯 깨닫게 된 그 무엇이……"], ["Razumihin turned pale.", "라주미힌의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다."]], [["“Do you understand now?”", "\"이제 알겠어?\""], ["said Raskolnikov, his face twitching nervously.", "라스콜니코프가 신경질적으로 얼굴을 씰룩거리며 말했다."], ["“Go back, go to them,”", "\"돌아가, 그분들께 가.\""], ["he said suddenly, and turning quickly, he went out of the house.", "그가 갑자기 말하더니, 잽싸게 몸을 돌려 건물 밖으로 나가 버렸다."]], [["I will not attempt to describe how Razumihin went back to the ladies, how he soothed them, how he protested that Rodya needed rest in his illness, protested that Rodya was sure to come, that he would come every day, that he was very, very much upset, that he must not be irritated, that he, Razumihin, would watch over him, would get him a doctor, the best doctor, a consultation....", "라주미힌이 어떻게 부인들에게 돌아갔는지, 어떻게 그들을 달랬는지, 로쟈가 병중이라 안정이 필요하다고 어떻게 항변했는지, 로쟈가 틀림없이 올 것이고, 매일 올 것이며, 그가 무척, 무척이나 심란해 있을 뿐이니, 그를 자극해서는 안 된다고, 자기 라주미힌이 그를 보살피고, 의사를, 최고의 의사를 불러와 진찰을 받게 하겠다고 어떻게 항변했는지…… 그 모든 것을 나는 굳이 묘사하지 않으려 한다."], ["In fact from that evening Razumihin took his place with them as a son and a brother.", "요컨대 그날 저녁부터 라주미힌은 아들이자 형제로서 그들 곁에 자리를 잡게 되었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["Raskolnikov went straight to the house on the canal bank where Sonia lived.", "라스콜니코프는 소냐가 사는 운하 강가의 집으로 곧장 걸어갔다."], ["It was an old green house of three storeys.", "그것은 삼 층짜리 낡은 초록색 집이었다."], ["He found the porter and obtained from him vague directions as to the whereabouts of Kapernaumov, the tailor.", "그는 문지기를 찾아내 재단사 카페르나우모프가 어디쯤 사는지에 관해 막연한 설명을 얻어 냈다."], ["Having found in the corner of the courtyard the entrance to the dark and narrow staircase, he mounted to the second floor and came out into a gallery that ran round the whole second storey over the yard.", "안뜰 한구석에서 어둡고 좁은 계단으로 통하는 입구를 찾아낸 그는 이 층으로 올라가, 안뜰 위로 이 층 전체를 빙 두르고 있는 복도로 나왔다."], ["While he was wandering in the darkness, uncertain where to turn for Kapernaumov’s door, a door opened three paces from him; he mechanically took hold of it.", "카페르나우모프의 문을 어디서 찾아야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못한 채 어둠 속을 헤매고 있는데, 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 문 하나가 열렸다. 그는 기계적으로 그 문을 붙들었다."]], [["“Who is there?”", "\"누구세요?\""], ["a woman’s voice asked uneasily.", "한 여자의 목소리가 불안하게 물었다."]], [["“It’s I...", "\"나요……"], ["come to see you,”", "당신을 보러 왔소.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov, and he walked into the tiny entry.", "라스콜니코프는 대답하고, 비좁은 현관으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["On a broken chair stood a candle in a battered copper candlestick.", "부서진 의자 위에는 우그러진 구리 촛대에 양초 하나가 꽂혀 있었다."]], [["“It’s you!", "\"당신이라니!"], ["Good heavens!”", "어머나, 세상에!\""], ["cried Sonia weakly, and she stood rooted to the spot.", "소냐가 가냘프게 외치며, 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다."]], [["“Which is your room?", "\"당신 방은 어느 쪽이오?"], ["This way?”", "이쪽인가?\""], ["and Raskolnikov, trying not to look at her, hastened in.", "라스콜니코프는 그녀를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 서둘러 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["A minute later Sonia, too, came in with the candle, set down the candlestick and, completely disconcerted, stood before him inexpressibly agitated and apparently frightened by his unexpected visit.", "잠시 후 소냐도 촛불을 들고 들어와 촛대를 내려놓고는, 완전히 어쩔 줄 모르는 채 그의 앞에 서 있었는데, 이루 말할 수 없이 동요하고 그의 뜻밖의 방문에 분명 겁을 먹은 듯했다."], ["The colour rushed suddenly to her pale face and tears came into her eyes...", "갑자기 그녀의 창백한 얼굴로 핏기가 확 솟구쳤고 두 눈에는 눈물이 고였다……."], ["She felt sick and ashamed and happy, too....", "그녀는 메스껍고 부끄러우면서도, 또한 행복했다……."], ["Raskolnikov turned away quickly and sat on a chair by the table.", "라스콜니코프는 재빨리 몸을 돌려 탁자 옆 의자에 앉았다."], ["He scanned the room in a rapid glance.", "그는 재빠른 눈길로 방 안을 훑었다."]], [["It was a large but exceedingly low-pitched room, the only one let by the Kapernaumovs, to whose rooms a closed door led in the wall on the left.", "그것은 넓지만 천장이 지독히도 낮은 방으로, 카페르나우모프 식구들이 세놓은 단 하나의 방이었고, 왼쪽 벽에는 그들의 방으로 통하는 닫힌 문이 나 있었다."], ["In the opposite side on the right hand wall was another door, always kept locked.", "그 맞은편 오른쪽 벽에는 또 다른 문이 있었는데, 늘 잠겨 있었다."], ["That led to the next flat, which formed a separate lodging.", "그 문은 별개의 거처를 이루는 옆 셋집으로 통했다."], ["Sonia’s room looked like a barn; it was a very irregular quadrangle and this gave it a grotesque appearance.", "소냐의 방은 헛간 같았다. 그것은 몹시 일그러진 사각형이어서 기괴한 인상을 주었다."], ["A wall with three windows looking out on to the canal ran aslant so that one corner formed a very acute angle, and it was difficult to see in it without very strong light.", "운하 쪽으로 난 세 개의 창이 있는 벽이 비스듬히 뻗어 있어 한쪽 구석은 아주 예리한 각을 이루었고, 그래서 아주 강한 빛이 아니고서는 그 안을 들여다보기가 어려웠다."], ["The other corner was disproportionately obtuse.", "다른 쪽 구석은 균형이 맞지 않게 둔한 각이었다."], ["There was scarcely any furniture in the big room: in the corner on the right was a bedstead, beside it, nearest the door, a chair.", "그 큰 방에는 가구라고 할 만한 것이 거의 없었다. 오른쪽 구석에는 침대가 있었고, 그 곁의, 문에서 가장 가까운 자리에는 의자가 하나 있었다."], ["A plain, deal table covered by a blue cloth stood against the same wall, close to the door into the other flat.", "푸른 천을 덮은 평범한 전나무 탁자가 같은 벽에, 옆 셋집으로 통하는 문 가까이에 놓여 있었다."], ["Two rush-bottom chairs stood by the table.", "골풀로 엮은 의자 두 개가 탁자 옆에 있었다."], ["On the opposite wall near the acute angle stood a small plain wooden chest of drawers looking, as it were, lost in a desert.", "맞은편 벽, 예각을 이룬 구석 가까이에는 작고 수수한 나무 서랍장이 하나 서 있었는데, 마치 사막에 버려진 듯 보였다."], ["That was all there was in the room.", "방 안에 있는 것은 그게 전부였다."], ["The yellow, scratched and shabby wall-paper was black in the corners.", "누렇게 바래고 긁히고 낡아 빠진 벽지는 구석마다 거뭇했다."], ["It must have been damp and full of fumes in the winter.", "겨울이면 분명 눅눅하고 매캐한 냄새로 가득했으리라."], ["There was every sign of poverty; even the bedstead had no curtain.", "온통 가난의 흔적이었다. 침대에조차 휘장 하나 없었다."]], [["Sonia looked in silence at her visitor, who was so attentively and unceremoniously scrutinising her room, and even began at last to tremble with terror, as though she was standing before her judge and the arbiter of her destinies.", "소냐는 자기 방을 이토록 주의 깊게, 또 거리낌 없이 살피는 방문객을 말없이 바라보았고, 마침내는 마치 자신의 심판관이자 운명의 결정자 앞에 서 있기라도 한 듯 공포에 떨기 시작했다."]], [["“I am late....", "\"내가 늦었군요……."], ["It’s eleven, isn’t it?”", "열한 시인가요?\""], ["he asked, still not lifting his eyes.", "그는 여전히 눈을 들지 않은 채 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["muttered Sonia, “oh yes, it is,”", "소냐가 중얼거렸다. \"아, 네, 그래요.\""], ["she added, hastily, as though in that lay her means of escape.", "그녀는 마치 그 안에 빠져나갈 구실이 있기라도 한 듯 서둘러 덧붙였다."], ["“My landlady’s clock has just struck...", "\"방금 주인집 시계가 쳤어요……."], ["I heard it myself....”", "제 귀로 들었거든요…….\""]], [["“I’ve come to you for the last time,”", "\"이렇게 당신을 보러 오는 것도 마지막이오.\""], ["Raskolnikov went on gloomily, although this was the first time.", "라스콜니코프는 우울하게 말을 이었다. 사실 이번이 처음이었는데도."], ["“I may perhaps not see you again...”", "\"어쩌면 당신을 다시는 못 볼지도 모르오…….\""]], [["“Are you...", "\"어디……"], ["going away?”", "떠나시는 건가요?\""]], [["“I don’t know...", "\"모르겠소……."], ["to-morrow....”", "내일…….\""]], [["“Then you are not coming to Katerina Ivanovna to-morrow?”", "\"그럼 내일 카테리나 이바노브나에게 안 오시는 건가요?\""], ["Sonia’s voice shook.", "소냐의 목소리가 떨렸다."]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠소."], ["I shall know to-morrow morning....", "내일 아침이면 알게 되겠지……."], ["Never mind that: I’ve come to say one word....”", "그건 그렇고, 한마디 하러 왔소…….\""]], [["He raised his brooding eyes to her and suddenly noticed that he was sitting down while she was all the while standing before him.", "그는 수심에 잠긴 두 눈을 들어 그녀를 보다가, 문득 자기는 앉아 있는데 그녀는 내내 그 앞에 서 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Why are you standing?", "\"왜 서 있소?"], ["Sit down,”", "앉으시오.\""], ["he said in a changed voice, gentle and friendly.", "그가 달라진 목소리로, 부드럽고 다정하게 말했다."]], [["She sat down.", "그녀가 앉았다."], ["He looked kindly and almost compassionately at her.", "그는 다정하게, 거의 측은해하는 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“How thin you are!", "\"어쩜 이렇게 야위었소!"], ["What a hand!", "이 손 좀 봐!"], ["Quite transparent, like a dead hand.”", "아주 투명하군, 죽은 사람 손 같아.\""]], [["He took her hand.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["Sonia smiled faintly.", "소냐는 희미하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“I have always been like that,”", "\"저는 늘 이랬어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Even when you lived at home?”", "\"집에 살 때도?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Of course, you were,”", "\"그렇겠지, 물론.\""], ["he added abruptly and the expression of his face and the sound of his voice changed again suddenly.", "그는 불쑥 덧붙였고, 그의 표정과 목소리가 또다시 갑자기 변했다."]], [["He looked round him once more.", "그는 다시 한번 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“You rent this room from the Kapernaumovs?”", "\"이 방을 카페르나우모프 식구들한테서 세 들었소?\""]], [["“Yes....”", "\"네…….\""]], [["“They live there, through that door?”", "\"저들이 저기, 저 문 너머에 사는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes....", "\"네……."], ["They have another room like this.”", "저들은 이런 방을 하나 더 갖고 있어요.\""]], [["“All in one room?”", "\"식구가 다 한방에서?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I should be afraid in your room at night,”", "\"나라면 밤에 당신 방에 있는 게 무서울 것 같소.\""], ["he observed gloomily.", "그가 우울하게 말했다."]], [["“They are very good people, very kind,”", "\"아주 좋은 사람들이에요, 무척 친절하고요.\""], ["answered Sonia, who still seemed bewildered, “and all the furniture, everything...", "소냐가 대답했는데, 여전히 어리둥절한 듯했다. \"그리고 가구며 모든 것이……"], ["everything is theirs.", "전부 저들 거예요."], ["And they are very kind and the children, too, often come to see me.”", "그리고 다들 무척 친절하고, 아이들도 자주 절 보러 와요.\""]], [["“They all stammer, don’t they?”", "\"다들 말을 더듬는다죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes....", "\"네……."], ["He stammers and he’s lame.", "그분은 말을 더듬고 다리도 절어요."], ["And his wife, too....", "그분 부인도요……."], ["It’s not exactly that she stammers, but she can’t speak plainly.", "정확히 더듬는다기보다는, 또렷이 말을 못 하는 거예요."], ["She is a very kind woman.", "아주 친절한 분이죠."], ["And he used to be a house serf.", "그리고 그분은 전에 농노였대요."], ["And there are seven children...", "그리고 아이가 일곱인데……"], ["and it’s only the eldest one that stammers and the others are simply ill...", "말을 더듬는 건 맏이뿐이고 다른 애들은 그냥 병약할 뿐이에요……"], ["but they don’t stammer....", "더듬지는 않아요……."], ["But where did you hear about them?”", "그런데 저 사람들 얘기는 어디서 들으셨어요?\""], ["she added with some surprise.", "그녀가 다소 놀라며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Your father told me, then.", "\"그럼 당신 아버지가 내게 말해 줬소."], ["He told me all about you....", "당신에 관해 죄다 얘기해 줬지……."], ["And how you went out at six o’clock and came back at nine and how Katerina Ivanovna knelt down by your bed.”", "그리고 당신이 여섯 시에 나가 아홉 시에 돌아오고, 카테리나 이바노브나가 당신 침대 곁에 무릎을 꿇었다는 것까지도.\""]], [["Sonia was confused.", "소냐는 당황했다."]], [["“I fancied I saw him to-day,”", "\"오늘 그분을 본 것 같았어요.\""], ["she whispered hesitatingly.", "그녀가 머뭇거리며 속삭였다."]], [["“Whom?”", "\"누구를?\""]], [["“Father.", "\"아버지요."], ["I was walking in the street, out there at the corner, about ten o’clock and he seemed to be walking in front.", "거리를 걷고 있었는데, 저쪽 모퉁이에서, 열 시쯤이었어요, 그런데 그분이 앞서 걸어가는 것 같았어요."], ["It looked just like him.", "꼭 그분처럼 보였거든요."], ["I wanted to go to Katerina Ivanovna....”", "카테리나 이바노브나에게 가고 싶었어요…….\""]], [["“You were walking in the streets?”", "\"거리를 걷고 있었다고?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["Sonia whispered abruptly, again overcome with confusion and looking down.", "소냐가 불쑥 속삭였는데, 또다시 당혹감에 휩싸여 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna used to beat you, I dare say?”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나가 당신을 때리곤 했겠지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Oh no, what are you saying? No!”", "\"오, 아니에요, 무슨 말씀을 하시는 거예요?"], ["Sonia looked at him almost with dismay.", "아니에요!\" 소냐는 거의 경악한 듯 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“You love her, then?”", "\"그럼 그 여자를 사랑하오?\""]], [["“Love her? Of course!”", "\"그분을 사랑하느냐고요?"], ["said Sonia with plaintive emphasis, and she clasped her hands in distress.", "당연하죠!\" 소냐가 애절하게 힘주어 말하며, 괴로운 듯 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["“Ah, you don’t.... If you only knew!", "\"아, 모르셔서 그래요……."], ["You see, she is quite like a child....", "아신다면! 그분은 정말 아이 같은 분이에요……."], ["Her mind is quite unhinged, you see...", "그분 마음은 완전히 무너져 버렸어요, 그러니까……"], ["from sorrow.", "슬픔 때문에요."], ["And how clever she used to be...", "게다가 얼마나 영리한 분이었는데요……"], ["how generous... how kind!", "얼마나 너그럽고요……"], ["Ah, you don’t understand, you don’t understand!”", "얼마나 다정하고요! 아, 모르세요, 모르신다고요!\""]], [["Sonia said this as though in despair, wringing her hands in excitement and distress.", "소냐는 마치 절망에 빠진 듯 이렇게 말하며, 흥분과 괴로움에 두 손을 비틀었다."], ["Her pale cheeks flushed, there was a look of anguish in her eyes.", "창백한 두 뺨이 발갛게 달아올랐고, 두 눈에는 고뇌의 빛이 어렸다."], ["It was clear that she was stirred to the very depths, that she was longing to speak, to champion, to express something.", "그녀가 마음속 깊은 곳까지 흔들렸음이, 무언가를 말하고, 변호하고, 토로하고 싶어 애태우고 있음이 분명했다."], ["A sort of _insatiable_ compassion, if one may so express it, was reflected in every feature of her face.", "이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 일종의 채울 수 없는 연민이 그녀 얼굴의 모든 결에 비쳤다."]], [["“Beat me!", "\"저를 때리다니요!"], ["how can you?", "어떻게 그런 말씀을?"], ["Good heavens, beat me!", "세상에, 저를 때려요!"], ["And if she did beat me, what then?", "설령 때렸다 한들, 그게 어쨌다는 거예요?"], ["What of it?", "그게 무슨 대수죠?"], ["You know nothing, nothing about it.... She is so unhappy...", "당신은 아무것도, 그 일에 관해 아무것도 모르세요……."], ["ah, how unhappy! And ill....", "그분은 너무도 불행해요……"], ["She is seeking righteousness, she is pure.", "아, 얼마나 불행한지! 게다가 병까지 들었고요……."], ["She has such faith that there must be righteousness everywhere and she expects it....", "그분은 정의를 구하고 있고, 순결한 분이에요. 어디에나 정의가 있어야 한다고 그토록 굳게 믿으면서, 그걸 기다리고 있어요……."], ["And if you were to torture her, she wouldn’t do wrong.", "설령 그분을 고문한다 해도, 그분은 옳지 않은 일은 하지 않을 거예요."], ["She doesn’t see that it’s impossible for people to be righteous and she is angry at it.", "사람들이 정의롭기란 불가능하다는 걸 보지 못하고, 그래서 그것에 화를 내요."], ["Like a child, like a child.", "아이처럼, 꼭 아이처럼요."], ["She is good!”", "그분은 선해요!\""]], [["“And what will happen to you?”", "\"그럼 당신은 어떻게 되는 거요?\""]], [["Sonia looked at him inquiringly.", "소냐는 묻고 싶은 듯한 눈길로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“They are left on your hands, you see.", "\"저들이 당신 손에 떠넘겨진 거잖소."], ["They were all on your hands before, though....", "하긴 전에도 다 당신 손에 매달려 있었지만……."], ["And your father came to you to beg for drink.", "게다가 당신 아버지는 술값을 구걸하러 당신에게 왔고."], ["Well, how will it be now?”", "자, 이제 어떻게 될 거요?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠어요.\""], ["Sonia articulated mournfully.", "소냐가 구슬프게 말했다."]], [["“Will they stay there?”", "\"저들이 거기 계속 있을 거요?\""]], [["“I don’t know....", "\"모르겠어요……."], ["They are in debt for the lodging, but the landlady, I hear, said to-day that she wanted to get rid of them, and Katerina Ivanovna says that she won’t stay another minute.”", "집세가 밀려 있는데, 주인 여자가 오늘 저들을 내쫓고 싶다고 말했다더라고요, 그리고 카테리나 이바노브나는 단 일 분도 더 머물지 않겠다고 하고요.\""]], [["“How is it she is so bold?", "\"그 여자가 어떻게 그리 대담한 거요?"], ["She relies upon you?”", "당신을 믿고 그러는 거요?\""]], [["“Oh, no, don’t talk like that....", "\"오, 아니에요, 그렇게 말씀하지 마세요……."], ["We are one, we live like one.”", "우리는 하나예요, 한 몸처럼 살아요.\""], ["Sonia was agitated again and even angry, as though a canary or some other little bird were to be angry.", "소냐는 다시 동요하며, 심지어 화까지 냈는데, 마치 카나리아나 다른 작은 새가 화를 내는 것 같았다."], ["“And what could she do?", "\"그리고 그분이 뭘 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["What, what could she do?”", "뭘, 대체 뭘 할 수 있겠어요?\""], ["she persisted, getting hot and excited.", "그녀는 얼굴이 달아오르고 흥분하며 우겼다."], ["“And how she cried to-day!", "\"그리고 오늘은 얼마나 우셨는지!"], ["Her mind is unhinged, haven’t you noticed it?", "그분 마음은 무너져 버렸어요, 못 알아차리셨어요?"], ["At one minute she is worrying like a child that everything should be right to-morrow, the lunch and all that....", "어느 순간엔 아이처럼 안달하며 내일은 모든 게 잘되어야 한다고, 점심이며 그런 것들이며……."], ["Then she is wringing her hands, spitting blood, weeping, and all at once she will begin knocking her head against the wall, in despair.", "그러다가는 두 손을 비틀며 피를 토하고, 울다가, 갑자기 절망에 빠져 벽에 머리를 찧기 시작해요."], ["Then she will be comforted again.", "그러다 또 마음을 가라앉히고요."], ["She builds all her hopes on you; she says that you will help her now and that she will borrow a little money somewhere and go to her native town with me and set up a boarding school for the daughters of gentlemen and take me to superintend it, and we will begin a new splendid life.", "그분은 온 희망을 당신에게 걸고 있어요. 이제 당신이 자기를 도와줄 거라고, 그래서 어디선가 돈을 좀 빌려 저와 함께 고향으로 가서 양갓집 딸들을 위한 기숙학교를 세우고, 저를 데려가 그 일을 맡길 거라고, 그렇게 멋진 새 삶을 시작할 거라고 말해요."], ["And she kisses and hugs me, comforts me, and you know she has such faith, such faith in her fancies!", "그러고는 저를 입 맞추고 끌어안고, 위로해 주는데, 그 공상을 어찌나 굳게, 굳게 믿는지!"], ["One can’t contradict her.", "도저히 거스를 수가 없어요."], ["And all the day long she has been washing, cleaning, mending.", "그리고 하루 종일 빨래하고, 청소하고, 깁고 했어요."], ["She dragged the wash tub into the room with her feeble hands and sank on the bed, gasping for breath.", "그 약한 손으로 빨래통을 방으로 끌고 들어오고는, 숨을 헐떡이며 침대에 쓰러졌어요."], ["We went this morning to the shops to buy shoes for Polenka and Lida for theirs are quite worn out.", "오늘 아침엔 폴렌카와 리다 신발을 사러 가게에 갔어요, 그애들 신발이 완전히 다 닳았거든요."], ["Only the money we’d reckoned wasn’t enough, not nearly enough.", "그런데 우리가 헤아렸던 돈으로는 모자랐어요, 어림도 없이 모자랐죠."], ["And she picked out such dear little boots, for she has taste, you don’t know.", "그래도 그분은 그렇게 예쁘고 자그마한 부츠를 골랐어요, 안목이 있으시거든요, 당신은 모르시겠지만."], ["And there in the shop she burst out crying before the shopmen because she hadn’t enough....", "그러고는 거기 가게에서, 점원들 앞에서, 돈이 모자라다고 울음을 터뜨렸어요……."], ["Ah, it was sad to see her....”", "아, 그 모습을 보니 너무 서글펐어요…….\""]], [["“Well, after that I can understand your living like this,”", "\"그런 얘기를 들으니, 당신이 이렇게 사는 까닭을 알 만하군.\""], ["Raskolnikov said with a bitter smile.", "라스콜니코프가 쓰디쓴 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“And aren’t you sorry for them?", "\"그럼 당신은 저들이 가엾지 않으세요?"], ["Aren’t you sorry?”", "가엾지 않으세요?\""], ["Sonia flew at him again.", "소냐가 다시 그에게 달려들었다."], ["“Why, I know, you gave your last penny yourself, though you’d seen nothing of it, and if you’d seen everything, oh dear!", "\"아니, 저는 알아요, 당신은 그 모습을 아무것도 보지 못했으면서도 마지막 한 푼까지 직접 내주셨잖아요, 만일 다 보셨더라면, 오, 세상에!"], ["And how often, how often I’ve brought her to tears!", "그리고 제가 얼마나 자주, 얼마나 자주 그분을 울렸는데요! 바로 지난주에도요!"], ["Only last week!", "그래요, 제가요!"], ["Yes, I! Only a week before his death.", "아버지가 돌아가시기 꼭 일주일 전에요."], ["I was cruel!", "저는 잔인했어요!"], ["And how often I’ve done it!", "그것도 얼마나 자주 그랬는지!"], ["Ah, I’ve been wretched at the thought of it all day!”", "아, 온종일 그 생각에 비참했어요!\""]], [["Sonia wrung her hands as she spoke at the pain of remembering it.", "소냐는 그것을 떠올리는 고통에 말하면서 두 손을 비틀었다."]], [["“You were cruel?”", "\"당신이 잔인했다고?\""]], [["“Yes, I--I.", "\"네, 제가, 제가요."], ["I went to see them,”", "저들을 보러 갔어요.\""], ["she went on, weeping, “and father said, ‘read me something, Sonia, my head aches, read to me, here’s a book.’ He had a book he had got from Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, he lives there, he always used to get hold of such funny books.", "그녀는 울면서 말을 이었다. \"그런데 아버지가 그러셨어요."], ["And I said, ‘I can’t stay,’ as I didn’t want to read, and I’d gone in chiefly to show Katerina Ivanovna some collars.", "'소냐, 뭐 좀 읽어 다오, 머리가 아프구나, 내게 읽어 주렴, 여기 책이 있다.' 그분은 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프에게서 얻은 책을 갖고 계셨어요, 그분은 거기 사는데, 늘 그런 우스꽝스러운 책들을 구해 오곤 했죠."], ["Lizaveta, the pedlar, sold me some collars and cuffs cheap, pretty, new, embroidered ones.", "그래서 저는 '못 있어요'라고 했어요, 읽고 싶지 않았거든요, 게다가 주로 카테리나 이바노브나에게 깃 몇 개를 보여 주려고 들렀던 거였어요."], ["Katerina Ivanovna liked them very much; she put them on and looked at herself in the glass and was delighted with them.", "행상하는 리자베타가 깃이랑 소맷동을 싸게 팔았거든요, 예쁘고, 새것에, 수가 놓인 것들로요. 카테리나 이바노브나가 그걸 무척 마음에 들어 했어요."], ["‘Make me a present of them, Sonia,’ she said, ‘please do.’ ‘_Please do_,’ she said, she wanted them so much.", "그걸 달고 거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 보며 아주 기뻐했죠. '소냐, 그걸 내게 선물로 다오.'"], ["And when could she wear them?", "그분이 말했어요. '제발 그래 다오.'"], ["They just reminded her of her old happy days.", "그렇게요, 그걸 무척 갖고 싶어 했어요. 그런데 그걸 언제 달아 볼 수 있겠어요?"], ["She looked at herself in the glass, admired herself, and she has no clothes at all, no things of her own, hasn’t had all these years!", "그저 옛날 행복했던 시절을 떠올리게 했을 뿐이죠. 거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 보며 흐뭇해했어요, 그분은 옷이라곤 하나도 없어요, 자기 물건이 하나도 없어요, 이 여러 해 동안 한 번도요!"], ["And she never asks anyone for anything; she is proud, she’d sooner give away everything.", "그리고 누구한테 뭘 달라고 하는 법이 없어요, 자존심이 강해서 차라리 모든 걸 다 내주려고 하죠."], ["And these she asked for, she liked them so much.", "그런데 그걸 달라고 했어요, 그토록 마음에 들어 했거든요."], ["And I was sorry to give them.", "그런데 저는 그걸 주기가 아까웠어요."], ["‘What use are they to you, Katerina Ivanovna?’ I said.", "'그게 카테리나 이바노브나한테 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?' 제가 말했어요."], ["I spoke like that to her, I ought not to have said that!", "그분에게 그렇게 말했어요, 그런 말을 하지 말았어야 했는데!"], ["She gave me such a look.", "그분이 저를 그런 눈으로 바라봤어요."], ["And she was so grieved, so grieved at my refusing her.", "그리고 너무도 슬퍼했어요, 제가 거절한 것을 너무도 슬퍼했어요."], ["And it was so sad to see....", "그 모습을 보니 정말 서글펐어요……."], ["And she was not grieved for the collars, but for my refusing, I saw that.", "그분이 슬퍼한 건 깃 때문이 아니라 제가 거절했기 때문이었어요, 저는 그걸 알았어요."], ["Ah, if only I could bring it all back, change it, take back those words!", "아, 그 모든 걸 되돌릴 수만 있다면, 바꿀 수만 있다면, 그 말들을 되삼킬 수만 있다면! 아, 만일 제가……"], ["Ah, if I... but it’s nothing to you!”", "하지만 그게 당신한테 무슨 상관이겠어요!\""]], [["“Did you know Lizaveta, the pedlar?”", "\"행상하는 리자베타를 알았소?\""]], [["“Yes....", "\"네……."], ["Did you know her?”", "그분을 아셨어요?\""], ["Sonia asked with some surprise.", "소냐가 다소 놀라며 물었다."]], [["“Katerina Ivanovna is in consumption, rapid consumption; she will soon die,”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나는 폐병에 걸렸소, 급성 폐병이오. 곧 죽을 거요.\""], ["said Raskolnikov after a pause, without answering her question. “Oh, no, no, no!”", "라스콜니코프는 그녀의 물음에는 답하지 않은 채 잠시 뜸을 들이고 말했다."]], [["And Sonia unconsciously clutched both his hands, as though imploring that she should not.", "\"오, 아니에요, 아니에요, 아니에요!\" 그리고 소냐는 그러지 말아 달라고 애원이라도 하듯, 무심결에 그의 두 손을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“But it will be better if she does die.”", "\"하지만 차라리 죽는 게 나을 거요.\""]], [["“No, not better, not at all better!”", "\"아니에요, 나을 게 없어요, 전혀 나을 게 없어요!\""], ["Sonia unconsciously repeated in dismay.", "소냐가 경악하여 무심결에 되풀이했다."]], [["“And the children?", "\"그럼 아이들은?"], ["What can you do except take them to live with you?”", "당신과 함께 데리고 사는 것 말고 뭘 할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t know,”", "\"오, 모르겠어요.\""], ["cried Sonia, almost in despair, and she put her hands to her head.", "소냐가 거의 절망하여 외치며, 두 손으로 머리를 감쌌다."]], [["It was evident that that idea had very often occurred to her before and he had only roused it again.", "그 생각이 전에도 그녀에게 자주 떠올랐었고, 그가 그저 그것을 다시 일깨운 것임이 분명했다."]], [["“And, what, if even now, while Katerina Ivanovna is alive, you get ill and are taken to the hospital, what will happen then?”", "\"그럼, 만일 지금이라도, 카테리나 이바노브나가 살아 있는 동안에, 당신이 병이 들어 병원에 실려 가면, 그땐 어떻게 되는 거요?\""], ["he persisted pitilessly.", "그가 무자비하게 우겼다."]], [["“How can you?", "\"어떻게 그런 말씀을?"], ["That cannot be!”", "그럴 리 없어요!\""]], [["And Sonia’s face worked with awful terror.", "그리고 소냐의 얼굴이 끔찍한 공포로 일그러졌다."]], [["“Cannot be?”", "\"그럴 리 없다고?\""], ["Raskolnikov went on with a harsh smile.", "라스콜니코프가 모진 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다."], ["“You are not insured against it, are you?", "\"당신은 그걸 막을 보험이라도 들었소?"], ["What will happen to them then?", "그럼 저들은 어떻게 되는 거요?"], ["They will be in the street, all of them, she will cough and beg and knock her head against some wall, as she did to-day, and the children will cry....", "길거리에 나앉게 되겠지, 죄다, 그 여자는 기침을 하며 구걸하고 오늘처럼 어디 벽에다 머리를 찧을 거고, 아이들은 울어 댈 거요……."], ["Then she will fall down, be taken to the police station and to the hospital, she will die, and the children...”", "그러다 그 여자는 쓰러져 경찰서로, 또 병원으로 끌려가 죽을 거고, 그럼 아이들은…….\""]], [["“Oh, no....", "\"오, 아니에요……."], ["God will not let it be!”", "하느님께서 그렇게 두지 않으실 거예요!\""], ["broke at last from Sonia’s overburdened bosom.", "마침내 소냐의 짓눌린 가슴에서 터져 나왔다."]], [["She listened, looking imploringly at him, clasping her hands in dumb entreaty, as though it all depended upon him.", "그녀는 마치 모든 것이 그에게 달려 있기라도 한 듯, 애원하는 눈길로 그를 바라보며, 말없는 간청 속에 두 손을 맞잡고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Raskolnikov got up and began to walk about the room.", "라스콜니코프는 일어나 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다."], ["A minute passed.", "일 분이 흘렀다."], ["Sonia was standing with her hands and her head hanging in terrible dejection.", "소냐는 두 손과 머리를 늘어뜨린 채 끔찍한 낙담에 빠져 서 있었다."]], [["“And can’t you save?", "\"그럼 저축은 못 하오?"], ["Put by for a rainy day?”", "어려운 날을 대비해 떼어 두는 거 말이오?\""], ["he asked, stopping suddenly before her.", "그가 갑자기 그녀 앞에 멈춰 서며 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"못 해요.\""], ["whispered Sonia.", "소냐가 속삭였다."]], [["“Of course not.", "\"물론 못 하겠지."], ["Have you tried?”", "해 보긴 했소?\""], ["he added almost ironically.", "그가 거의 빈정대듯 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“And it didn’t come off!", "\"그런데 안 됐겠지!"], ["Of course not!", "물론 그랬을 거고!"], ["No need to ask.”", "물어볼 것도 없군.\""]], [["And again he paced the room.", "그러고는 다시 방 안을 거닐었다."], ["Another minute passed.", "또 일 분이 흘렀다."]], [["“You don’t get money every day?”", "\"날마다 돈을 받는 건 아니지?\""]], [["Sonia was more confused than ever and colour rushed into her face again.", "소냐는 그 어느 때보다 당황했고, 다시 얼굴로 핏기가 솟구쳤다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["she whispered with a painful effort.", "그녀가 괴로운 듯 안간힘을 쓰며 속삭였다."]], [["“It will be the same with Polenka, no doubt,”", "\"폴렌카도 분명 같은 신세가 되겠군.\""], ["he said suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“No, no! It can’t be, no!”", "\"아니에요, 아니에요!"], ["Sonia cried aloud in desperation, as though she had been stabbed.", "그럴 리 없어요, 아니에요!\" 소냐가, 마치 칼에 찔리기라도 한 듯 절망하여 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["“God would not allow anything so awful!”", "\"하느님께서 그토록 끔찍한 일을 허락하지 않으실 거예요!\""], ["“He lets others come to it.”", "\"하느님은 다른 사람들도 그 지경에 이르게 두시잖아.\""]], [["“No, no!", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼!"], ["God will protect her, God!”", "하느님이 그 애를 지켜 주실 거예요, 하느님이!\""], ["she repeated beside herself.", "그녀가 제정신이 아닌 듯 되뇌었다."]], [["“But, perhaps, there is no God at all,”", "\"하지만 어쩌면 하느님 같은 건 아예 없는지도 모르죠.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered with a sort of malignance, laughed and looked at her.", "라스콜니코프가 일종의 악의를 품고 대꾸하더니 웃으며 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["Sonia’s face suddenly changed; a tremor passed over it.", "소냐의 얼굴이 갑자기 변했다. 전율이 그 위로 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["She looked at him with unutterable reproach, tried to say something, but could not speak and broke into bitter, bitter sobs, hiding her face in her hands.", "그녀는 이루 말할 수 없는 비난의 눈길로 그를 바라보았고, 무언가 말하려 했으나 입을 떼지 못한 채 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 쓰디쓰게, 쓰디쓰게 흐느껴 울었다."]], [["“You say Katerina Ivanovna’s mind is unhinged; your own mind is unhinged,”", "\"당신은 카테리나 이바노브나의 마음이 무너졌다고 했지만, 당신 마음도 무너졌소.\""], ["he said after a brief silence.", "그가 짧은 침묵 끝에 말했다."]], [["Five minutes passed.", "오 분이 흘렀다."], ["He still paced up and down the room in silence, not looking at her.", "그는 여전히 말없이 방 안을 오르내리며 그녀를 바라보지 않았다."], ["At last he went up to her; his eyes glittered.", "마침내 그가 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그의 두 눈이 번득였다."], ["He put his two hands on her shoulders and looked straight into her tearful face.", "그는 두 손을 그녀의 어깨에 얹고 눈물 젖은 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다."], ["His eyes were hard, feverish and piercing, his lips were twitching.", "그의 눈은 모질고, 열에 들뜨고, 꿰뚫는 듯했고, 입술은 실룩거렸다."], ["All at once he bent down quickly and dropping to the ground, kissed her foot.", "갑자기 그가 재빨리 몸을 숙여 바닥에 엎드리더니 그녀의 발에 입을 맞췄다."], ["Sonia drew back from him as from a madman.", "소냐는 미친 사람한테서 물러나듯 그에게서 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["And certainly he looked like a madman.", "정말이지 그는 미친 사람 같아 보였다."]], [["“What are you doing to me?”", "\"제게 왜 이러세요?\""], ["she muttered, turning pale, and a sudden anguish clutched at her heart.", "그녀가 창백해지며 중얼거렸고, 별안간 고뇌가 그녀의 가슴을 움켜쥐었다."]], [["He stood up at once.", "그는 곧 일어섰다."]], [["“I did not bow down to you, I bowed down to all the suffering of humanity,”", "\"나는 당신에게 머리를 숙인 게 아니라, 인류의 모든 고통 앞에 머리를 숙인 거요.\""], ["he said wildly and walked away to the window.", "그가 광기 어린 어조로 말하고는 창가로 걸어갔다."], ["“Listen,”", "\"들어 보시오.\""], ["he added, turning to her a minute later.", "그가 잠시 후 그녀에게 돌아서며 덧붙였다."], ["“I said just now to an insolent man that he was not worth your little finger...", "\"나는 방금 어떤 오만한 자에게 그는 당신 새끼손가락만 한 가치도 없다고 말했소……"], ["and that I did my sister honour making her sit beside you.”", "그리고 당신 곁에 내 누이를 앉게 한 것은 그애에게 영광이라고 말했소.\""]], [["“Ach, you said that to them!", "\"아, 저들에게 그런 말씀을 하셨다고요!"], ["And in her presence?”", "게다가 그분 앞에서요?\""], ["cried Sonia, frightened.", "소냐가 겁에 질려 외쳤다."], ["“Sit down with me!", "\"저랑 같이 앉다니요!"], ["An honour!", "영광이라니!"], ["Why, I’m...", "아니, 저는……"], ["dishonourable....", "떳떳지 못한 몸인데……."], ["Ah, why did you say that?”", "아, 왜 그런 말씀을 하셨어요?\""]], [["“It was not because of your dishonour and your sin I said that of you, but because of your great suffering.", "\"내가 당신을 두고 그렇게 말한 건 당신의 치욕이나 죄 때문이 아니라, 당신의 그 크나큰 고통 때문이오."], ["But you are a great sinner, that’s true,”", "하지만 당신이 큰 죄인인 건 사실이오.\""], ["he added almost solemnly, “and your worst sin is that you have destroyed and betrayed yourself _for nothing_.", "그가 거의 엄숙하게 덧붙였다. \"그리고 당신의 가장 큰 죄는 아무 보람도 없이 자기 자신을 망치고 배신했다는 거요."], ["Isn’t that fearful?", "그게 끔찍하지 않소?"], ["Isn’t it fearful that you are living in this filth which you loathe so, and at the same time you know yourself (you’ve only to open your eyes) that you are not helping anyone by it, not saving anyone from anything?", "당신이 그토록 혐오하는 이 오물 속에 살면서, 동시에 (그저 눈만 뜨면 알 수 있을 텐데) 그걸로 그 누구도 돕지 못하고, 그 누구도 무엇으로부터 구하지 못한다는 걸 스스로 알고 있다는 게 끔찍하지 않냐고?"], ["Tell me,”", "말해 보시오.\""], ["he went on almost in a frenzy, “how this shame and degradation can exist in you side by side with other, opposite, holy feelings?", "그가 거의 광란에 빠진 듯 말을 이었다. \"이 수치와 타락이 어떻게 당신 안에서 다른, 정반대의, 성스러운 감정들과 나란히 공존할 수 있는 거요?"], ["It would be better, a thousand times better and wiser to leap into the water and end it all!”", "차라리 물속으로 뛰어들어 모든 걸 끝내는 편이 천 배는 더 낫고 현명할 거요!\""]], [["“But what would become of them?”", "\"하지만 그럼 저들은 어떻게 되나요?\""], ["Sonia asked faintly, gazing at him with eyes of anguish, but not seeming surprised at his suggestion.", "소냐가 고뇌에 찬 눈으로 그를 응시하면서도 그의 제안에 놀라는 기색은 없이, 힘없이 물었다."]], [["Raskolnikov looked strangely at her.", "라스콜니코프는 묘한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["He read it all in her face; so she must have had that thought already, perhaps many times, and earnestly she had thought out in her despair how to end it and so earnestly, that now she scarcely wondered at his suggestion.", "그는 그녀의 얼굴에서 모든 것을 읽어 냈다. 그러니까 그녀는 이미 그런 생각을, 어쩌면 여러 번 했던 게 분명했고, 절망 속에서 어떻게 모든 걸 끝낼지를 진지하게, 너무도 진지하게 곰곰이 생각해 온 나머지, 이제 그의 제안에도 거의 놀라지 않은 것이다."], ["She had not even noticed the cruelty of his words.", "그녀는 그의 말이 잔인하다는 것조차 알아차리지 못했다."], ["(The significance of his reproaches and his peculiar attitude to her shame she had, of course, not noticed either, and that, too, was clear to him.) But he saw how monstrously the thought of her disgraceful, shameful position was torturing her and had long tortured her.", "(그의 비난이 지닌 뜻도, 자기 치욕에 대한 그의 묘한 태도도 그녀는 물론 알아차리지 못했고, 그 또한 그에게는 분명했다.) 하지만 그는 자신의 치욕스럽고 부끄러운 처지에 대한 생각이 얼마나 끔찍하게 그녀를 괴롭히고, 또 오랫동안 괴롭혀 왔는지를 보았다. '대체 무엇이,'"], ["“What, what,”", "그는 생각했다."], ["he thought, “could hitherto have hindered her from putting an end to it?”", "'지금까지 그녀가 이걸 끝내지 못하도록 막아 왔단 말인가?'"], ["Only then he realised what those poor little orphan children and that pitiful half-crazy Katerina Ivanovna, knocking her head against the wall in her consumption, meant for Sonia.", "그제야 그는 저 가엾고 어린 고아들과, 폐병을 앓으며 벽에 머리를 찧는 저 가련하고 반쯤 미친 카테리나 이바노브나가 소냐에게 어떤 의미인지를 깨달았다."]], [["But, nevertheless, it was clear to him again that with her character and the amount of education she had after all received, she could not in any case remain so.", "그러나 그럼에도, 그녀의 성품과 어쨌든 그녀가 받았던 그만큼의 교육으로 보아 어떤 경우든 그녀가 이대로 머물 수는 없으리라는 것이 그에게는 다시금 분명해졌다."], ["He was still confronted by the question, how could she have remained so long in that position without going out of her mind, since she could not bring herself to jump into the water?", "여전히 그의 앞에는 이런 의문이 놓여 있었으니, 물속으로 뛰어들 결심조차 하지 못하면서, 어떻게 그녀가 미쳐 버리지 않고 이토록 오래 그 처지에 머물 수 있었단 말인가?"], ["Of course he knew that Sonia’s position was an exceptional case, though unhappily not unique and not infrequent, indeed; but that very exceptionalness, her tinge of education, her previous life might, one would have thought, have killed her at the first step on that revolting path.", "물론 그는 소냐의 처지가 예외적인 경우임을 알았는데, 안타깝게도 유일무이한 것도, 실제로 드문 것도 아니었지만. 그러나 바로 그 예외성, 그녀가 받은 약간의 교육, 그녀의 지난 삶이, 생각건대 그 역겨운 길로 들어선 첫걸음에서 그녀를 죽여 버렸어야 마땅했다."], ["What held her up--surely not depravity?", "무엇이 그녀를 떠받쳐 왔단 말인가, 설마 타락은 아니겠지?"], ["All that infamy had obviously only touched her mechanically, not one drop of real depravity had penetrated to her heart; he saw that.", "그 모든 치욕은 분명 기계적으로 그녀를 스쳐 갔을 뿐, 진짜 타락은 단 한 방울도 그녀의 마음속으로 스며들지 않았다. 그는 그것을 보았다."], ["He saw through her as she stood before him....", "그 앞에 서 있는 그녀를 그는 꿰뚫어 보았다……."]], [["“There are three ways before her,”", "'그녀 앞에는 세 가지 길이 있다.'"], ["he thought, “the canal, the madhouse, or...", "그는 생각했다. '운하, 정신병원, 아니면……"], ["at last to sink into depravity which obscures the mind and turns the heart to stone.”", "끝내는 마음을 흐리게 하고 심장을 돌로 만드는 타락에 빠져드는 것.'"]], [["The last idea was the most revolting, but he was a sceptic, he was young, abstract, and therefore cruel, and so he could not help believing that the last end was the most likely.", "마지막 생각이 가장 역겨웠지만, 그는 회의주의자였고, 젊었고, 추상적이었으며, 따라서 잔인했으니, 그래서 그 마지막 결말이 가장 그럴 법하다고 믿지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“But can that be true?”", "'하지만 그게 정말일 수 있을까?'"], ["he cried to himself.", "그는 속으로 외쳤다."], ["“Can that creature who has still preserved the purity of her spirit be consciously drawn at last into that sink of filth and iniquity?", "'여태껏 영혼의 순결함을 지켜 온 저 존재가 결국 그 오물과 죄악의 구렁텅이로 의식적으로 끌려 들어갈 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Can the process already have begun?", "그 과정이 이미 시작된 건 아닐까?"], ["Can it be that she has only been able to bear it till now, because vice has begun to be less loathsome to her?", "그녀가 지금까지 견딜 수 있었던 것은, 단지 악덕이 그녀에게 덜 역겨워지기 시작했기 때문은 아닐까?"], ["No, no, that cannot be!”", "아니야, 아니야, 그럴 리 없어!'"], ["he cried, as Sonia had just before.", "그는 방금 전 소냐가 그랬듯 외쳤다."], ["“No, what has kept her from the canal till now is the idea of sin and they, the children....", "'아니야, 지금까지 그녀를 운하에서 막아 온 건 죄에 대한 관념과, 저들, 저 아이들이야……."], ["And if she has not gone out of her mind...", "그리고 만일 그녀가 미쳐 버리지 않았다면……"], ["but who says she has not gone out of her mind?", "하지만 그녀가 미치지 않았다고 누가 말하는가?"], ["Is she in her senses?", "그녀가 제정신인가?"], ["Can one talk, can one reason as she does?", "그녀처럼 말하고, 그녀처럼 따질 수 있는 게 제정신일까?"], ["How can she sit on the edge of the abyss of loathsomeness into which she is slipping and refuse to listen when she is told of danger?", "미끄러져 들어가고 있는 그 역겨움의 심연 가장자리에 어떻게 저렇게 걸터앉아, 위험하다는 말을 들으면서도 귀 기울이기를 거부할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Does she expect a miracle?", "기적이라도 바라는 걸까?"], ["No doubt she does.", "분명 그럴 거야."], ["Doesn’t that all mean madness?”", "그게 다 미친 게 아니고 뭐란 말인가?'"]], [["He stayed obstinately at that thought.", "그는 고집스레 그 생각에 매달렸다."], ["He liked that explanation indeed better than any other.", "사실 그는 다른 어떤 설명보다도 그 설명이 마음에 들었다."], ["He began looking more intently at her.", "그는 더 골똘히 그녀를 바라보기 시작했다."]], [["“So you pray to God a great deal, Sonia?”", "\"그래서 당신은 하느님께 기도를 많이 하오, 소냐?\""], ["he asked her.", "그가 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["Sonia did not speak; he stood beside her waiting for an answer.", "소냐는 말이 없었다. 그는 그녀 곁에 서서 대답을 기다렸다."]], [["“What should I be without God?”", "\"하느님이 안 계시면 제가 어떻게 되겠어요?\""], ["she whispered rapidly, forcibly, glancing at him with suddenly flashing eyes, and squeezing his hand.", "그녀는 갑자기 번득이는 눈으로 그를 흘끗 보며, 그의 손을 꽉 쥐고는 빠르게, 힘주어 속삭였다."]], [["“Ah, so that is it!”", "'아, 바로 그거였군!'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“And what does God do for you?”", "\"그럼 하느님은 당신을 위해 무얼 해 주시오?\""], ["he asked, probing her further.", "그가 그녀를 더 캐물었다."]], [["Sonia was silent a long while, as though she could not answer.", "소냐는 마치 대답을 못 하기라도 하듯 오래도록 잠자코 있었다."], ["Her weak chest kept heaving with emotion.", "그녀의 가냘픈 가슴은 격정에 들먹였다."]], [["“Be silent!", "\"잠자코 계세요!"], ["Don’t ask!", "묻지 마세요!"], ["You don’t deserve!”", "당신은 그럴 자격이 없어요!\""], ["she cried suddenly, looking sternly and wrathfully at him.", "그녀가 엄하고 노여운 눈길로 그를 바라보며 갑자기 외쳤다."]], [["“That’s it, that’s it,”", "'그래, 바로 그거야, 바로 그거.'"], ["he repeated to himself.", "그는 속으로 되풀이했다."]], [["“He does everything,”", "\"그분은 모든 걸 해 주세요.\""], ["she whispered quickly, looking down again.", "그녀가 다시 고개를 떨구며 빠르게 속삭였다."]], [["“That’s the way out!", "'바로 그게 출구로군!"], ["That’s the explanation,”", "바로 그게 설명이야.'"], ["he decided, scrutinising her with eager curiosity, with a new, strange, almost morbid feeling.", "그는 새롭고, 낯설고, 거의 병적인 감정으로, 열렬한 호기심에 차서 그녀를 뜯어보며 단정 지었다."], ["He gazed at that pale, thin, irregular, angular little face, those soft blue eyes, which could flash with such fire, such stern energy, that little body still shaking with indignation and anger--and it all seemed to him more and more strange, almost impossible.", "그는 그 창백하고, 야위고, 일그러지고, 모난 작은 얼굴을, 그토록 불처럼, 그토록 엄한 기운으로 번득일 수 있는 그 부드러운 푸른 눈을, 여전히 분노와 노여움에 떨고 있는 그 작은 몸을 응시했다 — 그러자 그 모든 것이 점점 더 낯설게, 거의 있을 수 없는 일처럼 여겨졌다."], ["“She is a religious maniac!”", "'저 여자는 종교적 광신자야!'"], ["he repeated to himself.", "그는 속으로 되풀이했다."]], [["There was a book lying on the chest of drawers.", "서랍장 위에 책 한 권이 놓여 있었다."], ["He had noticed it every time he paced up and down the room.", "그는 방 안을 오르내릴 때마다 그것을 보았다."], ["Now he took it up and looked at it.", "이제 그는 그것을 집어 들여다보았다."], ["It was the New Testament in the Russian translation.", "그것은 러시아어로 번역된 신약성서였다."], ["It was bound in leather, old and worn.", "가죽으로 장정된, 낡고 닳은 것이었다."]], [["“Where did you get that?”", "\"이걸 어디서 났소?\""], ["he called to her across the room.", "그가 방 저편의 그녀에게 소리쳐 물었다."]], [["She was still standing in the same place, three steps from the table.", "그녀는 여전히 같은 자리에, 탁자에서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에 서 있었다."]], [["“It was brought me,”", "\"누가 갖다줬어요.\""], ["she answered, as it were unwillingly, not looking at him.", "그녀는 마지못한 듯, 그를 보지 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["“Who brought it?”", "\"누가 갖다줬소?\""]], [["“Lizaveta, I asked her for it.”", "\"리자베타요, 제가 부탁했거든요.\""]], [["“Lizaveta!", "'리자베타라니!"], ["strange!”", "이상하군!'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["Everything about Sonia seemed to him stranger and more wonderful every moment.", "소냐에 관한 모든 것이 그에게는 매 순간 점점 더 낯설고 기이하게 느껴졌다."], ["He carried the book to the candle and began to turn over the pages.", "그는 책을 촛불 가까이 가져가 페이지를 넘기기 시작했다."]], [["“Where is the story of Lazarus?”", "\"나사로 이야기는 어디 있소?\""], ["he asked suddenly.", "그가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["Sonia looked obstinately at the ground and would not answer.", "소냐는 고집스레 바닥을 바라보며 대답하려 하지 않았다."], ["She was standing sideways to the table.", "그녀는 탁자 옆에 모로 서 있었다."]], [["“Where is the raising of Lazarus?", "\"나사로를 살리는 대목 말이오."], ["Find it for me, Sonia.”", "그걸 찾아 주시오, 소냐.\""]], [["She stole a glance at him.", "그녀가 그를 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다."]], [["“You are not looking in the right place....", "\"엉뚱한 데서 찾고 계세요……."], ["It’s in the fourth gospel,”", "그건 네 번째 복음서에 있어요.\""], ["she whispered sternly, without looking at him.", "그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채 엄하게 속삭였다."]], [["“Find it and read it to me,” he said.", "\"그걸 찾아서 내게 읽어 주시오.\""], ["He sat down with his elbow on the table, leaned his head on his hand and looked away sullenly, prepared to listen.", "그가 말했다. 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉아, 머리를 손에 기대고는, 들을 채비를 한 채 시무룩하게 딴 데를 바라보았다."]], [["“In three weeks’ time they’ll welcome me in the madhouse!", "'삼 주만 지나면 저들은 나를 정신병원에서 맞아 주겠지!"], ["I shall be there if I am not in a worse place,”", "더 못한 데가 아니라면 거기에 있을 거고.'"], ["he muttered to himself.", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["Sonia heard Raskolnikov’s request distrustfully and moved hesitatingly to the table.", "소냐는 라스콜니코프의 청을 미심쩍게 들으며 머뭇머뭇 탁자로 다가갔다."], ["She took the book however.", "그래도 그녀는 책을 집어 들었다."]], [["“Haven’t you read it?”", "\"이걸 안 읽으셨어요?\""], ["she asked, looking up at him across the table.", "그녀가 탁자 너머로 그를 올려다보며 물었다."]], [["Her voice became sterner and sterner.", "그녀의 목소리가 점점 더 엄해졌다."]], [["“Long ago....", "\"오래전에……"], ["When I was at school.", "학교 다닐 때."], ["Read!”", "읽어 주시오!\""]], [["“And haven’t you heard it in church?”", "\"교회에서도 못 들으셨고요?\""]], [["“I...", "\"난……"], ["haven’t been.", "안 갔으니까."], ["Do you often go?”", "당신은 자주 가오?\""]], [["“N-no,”", "\"아, 아니요.\""], ["whispered Sonia.", "소냐가 속삭였다."]], [["Raskolnikov smiled.", "라스콜니코프는 미소 지었다."]], [["“I understand....", "\"알겠소……."], ["And you won’t go to your father’s funeral to-morrow?”", "그럼 내일 당신 아버지 장례에도 안 가겠군?\""]], [["“Yes, I shall.", "\"아니요, 갈 거예요."], ["I was at church last week, too...", "지난주에도 교회에 갔었는데요……"], ["I had a requiem service.”", "위령 미사를 드렸어요.\""]], [["“For whom?”", "\"누구를 위해?\""]], [["“For Lizaveta.", "\"리자베타를 위해서요."], ["She was killed with an axe.”", "그분은 도끼에 살해당했어요.\""]], [["His nerves were more and more strained.", "그의 신경은 점점 더 팽팽해졌다."], ["His head began to go round.", "머리가 빙빙 돌기 시작했다."]], [["“Were you friends with Lizaveta?”", "\"리자베타와 친했소?\""]], [["“Yes....", "\"네……."], ["She was good...", "그분은 선했어요……"], ["she used to come...", "절 보러 오곤 했어요……"], ["not often...", "자주는 아니고요……"], ["she couldn’t....", "그럴 수가 없었거든요……."], ["We used to read together and...", "우리는 함께 책을 읽고…… 이야기하곤 했어요."], ["talk. She will see God.”", "그분은 하느님을 뵐 거예요.\""]], [["The last phrase sounded strange in his ears.", "마지막 말이 그의 귀에는 이상하게 들렸다."], ["And here was something new again: the mysterious meetings with Lizaveta and both of them--religious maniacs.", "게다가 또다시 새로운 무언가가 있었다. 리자베타와의 그 은밀한 만남들, 그리고 두 사람 다 — 종교적 광신자들이라니."]], [["“I shall be a religious maniac myself soon!", "'나도 곧 종교적 광신자가 되겠군!"], ["It’s infectious!”", "이건 전염되는 거야!'"]], [["“Read!”", "\"읽어요!\""], ["he cried irritably and insistently.", "그가 짜증스럽게, 고집스레 외쳤다."]], [["Sonia still hesitated.", "소냐는 여전히 머뭇거렸다."], ["Her heart was throbbing.", "그녀의 심장이 두근거렸다."], ["She hardly dared to read to him.", "그녀는 그에게 읽어 주기가 좀처럼 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["He looked almost with exasperation at the “unhappy lunatic.”", "그는 거의 분통이 터지는 듯 그 '불행한 광인'을 바라보았다."]], [["“What for?", "\"무엇 때문에요?"], ["You don’t believe?...”", "당신은 믿지 않으시잖아요……?\""], ["she whispered softly and as it were breathlessly.", "그녀가 부드럽게, 마치 숨이 차듯 속삭였다."]], [["“Read!", "\"읽어요!"], ["I want you to,”", "그러길 바라오.\""], ["he persisted.", "그가 우겼다."], ["“You used to read to Lizaveta.”", "\"당신은 리자베타에게도 읽어 주곤 했잖소.\""]], [["Sonia opened the book and found the place.", "소냐는 책을 펴고 그 대목을 찾았다."], ["Her hands were shaking, her voice failed her.", "그녀의 손이 떨렸고, 목소리가 나오지 않았다."], ["Twice she tried to begin and could not bring out the first syllable.", "두 번이나 시작하려 했지만 첫 음절조차 내뱉지 못했다."]], [["“Now a certain man was sick named Lazarus of Bethany...”", "\"어떤 병든 사람이 있으니 이는 베다니의 나사로라…….\""], ["she forced herself at last to read, but at the third word her voice broke like an overstrained string.", "그녀는 마침내 안간힘을 다해 읽기 시작했지만, 세 번째 단어에서 그녀의 목소리는 지나치게 팽팽해진 현처럼 끊겨 버렸다."], ["There was a catch in her breath.", "숨이 턱 막혔다."]], [["Raskolnikov saw in part why Sonia could not bring herself to read to him and the more he saw this, the more roughly and irritably he insisted on her doing so.", "라스콜니코프는 소냐가 자기에게 읽어 주기를 왜 그토록 꺼리는지 어느 정도 알았고, 이를 알면 알수록 그녀에게 더 거칠고 짜증스럽게 읽기를 강요했다."], ["He understood only too well how painful it was for her to betray and unveil all that was her _own_.", "그는 그녀에게 자기만의 것을 죄다 드러내 보이는 일이 얼마나 고통스러운지 너무도 잘 이해했다."], ["He understood that these feelings really were her _secret treasure_, which she had kept perhaps for years, perhaps from childhood, while she lived with an unhappy father and a distracted stepmother crazed by grief, in the midst of starving children and unseemly abuse and reproaches.", "그는 이 감정들이 정말이지 그녀의 은밀한 보물이며, 어쩌면 여러 해 동안, 어쩌면 어린 시절부터, 불행한 아버지와 슬픔에 미쳐 버린 정신없는 새어머니 곁에서, 굶주리는 아이들과 차마 입에 담기 어려운 욕설과 비난 한가운데서 그녀가 간직해 온 것임을 이해했다."], ["But at the same time he knew now and knew for certain that, although it filled her with dread and suffering, yet she had a tormenting desire to read and to read to _him_ that he might hear it, and to read _now_ whatever might come of it!...", "그러나 동시에 그는 이제 알았고, 또한 확실히 알았으니, 비록 그것이 그녀를 두려움과 고통으로 가득 채울지라도, 그녀가 읽고 싶어, 또 그에게, 그가 듣도록 읽어 주고 싶어 못 견디게 갈망하고 있으며, 그 결과야 어찌 되든 지금 읽고 싶어 한다는 것을……!"], ["He read this in her eyes, he could see it in her intense emotion.", "그는 그것을 그녀의 눈에서 읽어 냈고, 그녀의 격렬한 감정에서 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["She mastered herself, controlled the spasm in her throat and went on reading the eleventh chapter of St. John.", "그녀는 자신을 다잡아, 목구멍의 경련을 억누르고, 요한복음 십일 장을 계속 읽어 나갔다."], ["She went on to the nineteenth verse:", "그녀는 십구 절까지 나아갔다."]], [["“And many of the Jews came to Martha and Mary to comfort them concerning their brother.", "\"많은 유대인이 마르다와 마리아에게 그 오라비의 일로 위로하러 왔더니,"]], [["“Then Martha as soon as she heard that Jesus was coming went and met Him: but Mary sat still in the house.", "마르다는 예수께서 오신다는 말을 듣고 곧 나가 맞이하되 마리아는 집에 앉았더라."]], [["“Then said Martha unto Jesus, Lord, if Thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.", "마르다가 예수께 여짜오되, 주여 주께서 여기 계셨더라면 내 오라비가 죽지 아니하였겠나이다."]], [["“But I know that even now whatsoever Thou wilt ask of God, God will give it Thee....”", "그러나 나는 이제라도 주께서 무엇이든지 하느님께 구하시는 것을 하느님이 주실 줄을 아나이다…….\""]], [["Then she stopped again with a shamefaced feeling that her voice would quiver and break again.", "그러고는 자기 목소리가 또다시 떨리고 끊겨 버릴 것 같은 부끄러운 마음에 다시 멈추었다."]], [["“Jesus said unto her, thy brother shall rise again.", "\"예수께서 이르시되, 네 오라비가 다시 살아나리라."]], [["“Martha saith unto Him, I know that he shall rise again in the resurrection, at the last day.", "마르다가 이르되, 마지막 날 부활 때에는 다시 살아날 줄을 내가 아나이다."]], [["“Jesus said unto her, I am the resurrection and the life: he that believeth in Me though he were dead, yet shall he live.", "예수께서 이르시되, 나는 부활이요 생명이니 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고,"]], [["“And whosoever liveth and believeth in Me shall never die. Believest thou this?", "무릇 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리니 이것을 네가 믿느냐?"]], [["“She saith unto Him,”", "이르되,\""]], [["(And drawing a painful breath, Sonia read distinctly and forcibly as though she were making a public confession of faith.)", "(그리고 고통스러운 숨을 들이쉬며, 소냐는 마치 공개적으로 신앙을 고백하기라도 하듯 또박또박, 힘주어 읽었다.)"]], [["“Yea, Lord: I believe that Thou art the Christ, the Son of God Which should come into the world.”", "\"주여 그러하외다, 주는 그리스도시요 세상에 오시는 하느님의 아들이신 줄 내가 믿나이다.\""]], [["She stopped and looked up quickly at him, but controlling herself went on reading.", "그녀는 멈추고 재빨리 그를 올려다보았으나, 자신을 다잡고 계속 읽어 나갔다."], ["Raskolnikov sat without moving, his elbows on the table and his eyes turned away.", "라스콜니코프는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 눈은 딴 데로 돌린 채 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다."], ["She read to the thirty-second verse.", "그녀는 삼십이 절까지 읽었다."]], [["“Then when Mary was come where Jesus was and saw Him, she fell down at His feet, saying unto Him, Lord if Thou hadst been here, my brother had not died.", "\"마리아가 예수 계신 곳에 와서 뵙고 그 발 앞에 엎드리어 이르되, 주여 주께서 여기 계셨더라면 내 오라비가 죽지 아니하였겠나이다."]], [["“When Jesus therefore saw her weeping, and the Jews also weeping which came with her, He groaned in the spirit and was troubled,", "예수께서 그가 우는 것과 또 함께 온 유대인들이 우는 것을 보시고 심령에 비통히 여기시고 불쌍히 여기사,"]], [["“And said, Where have ye laid him?", "이르시되, 그를 어디 두었느냐."], ["They said unto Him, Lord, come and see.", "이르되, 주여 와서 보옵소서 하니,\n예수께서 눈물을 흘리시더라."]], [["“Jesus wept. “Then said the Jews, behold how He loved him!", "이에 유대인들이 말하되, 보라 그를 얼마나 사랑하셨는가 하며,"]], [["“And some of them said, could not this Man which opened the eyes of the blind, have caused that even this man should not have died?”", "그중 어떤 이는 말하되, 맹인의 눈을 뜨게 한 이 사람이 그 사람은 죽지 않게 할 수 없었더냐 하더라.\""]], [["Raskolnikov turned and looked at her with emotion.", "라스콜니코프는 몸을 돌려 격한 감정으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Yes, he had known it!", "그래, 그는 알고 있었다!"], ["She was trembling in a real physical fever.", "그녀는 진짜 육신의 열병에 떨고 있었다."], ["He had expected it.", "그는 그것을 예상했다."], ["She was getting near the story of the greatest miracle and a feeling of immense triumph came over her.", "그녀는 가장 큰 기적의 이야기에 다가가고 있었고, 어마어마한 승리의 감정이 그녀를 사로잡았다."], ["Her voice rang out like a bell; triumph and joy gave it power.", "그녀의 목소리는 종소리처럼 울려 퍼졌다."], ["The lines danced before her eyes, but she knew what she was reading by heart.", "승리와 환희가 그것에 힘을 실어 주었다."], ["At the last verse “Could not this Man which opened the eyes of the blind...”", "글줄들이 그녀의 눈앞에서 춤추었지만, 그녀는 자신이 읽는 것을 외고 있었다."], ["dropping her voice she passionately reproduced the doubt, the reproach and censure of the blind disbelieving Jews, who in another moment would fall at His feet as though struck by thunder, sobbing and believing....", "마지막 구절 \"맹인의 눈을 뜨게 한 이 사람이……\"에서, 그녀는 목소리를 낮추어, 한순간 뒤면 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그의 발 앞에 엎드려 흐느끼며 믿게 될, 눈멀고 믿지 않는 유대인들의 의심과 비난과 질책을 열정적으로 되살려 냈다……."], ["“And _he, he_--too, is blinded and unbelieving, he, too, will hear, he, too, will believe, yes, yes! At once, now,”", "'그리고 그도, 그 사람도 — 마찬가지로 눈멀어 믿지 않고 있지만, 그도 듣게 될 거야, 그도 믿게 될 거야, 그래, 그래!"], ["was what she was dreaming, and she was quivering with happy anticipation.", "지금 당장, 이 자리에서.' 그녀가 꿈꾼 것은 그것이었고, 그녀는 행복한 기대에 떨고 있었다."]], [["“Jesus therefore again groaning in Himself cometh to the grave. It was a cave, and a stone lay upon it.", "\"이에 예수께서 다시 속으로 비통히 여기시며 무덤에 가시니 무덤이 굴이라 돌로 막았거늘,"]], [["“Jesus said, Take ye away the stone. Martha, the sister of him that was dead, saith unto Him, Lord by this time he stinketh: for he hath been dead four days.”", "예수께서 이르시되, 돌을 옮겨 놓으라 하시니, 그 죽은 자의 누이 마르다가 이르되, 주여 죽은 지가 나흘이 되었으매 벌써 냄새가 나나이다.\""]], [["She laid emphasis on the word _four_.", "그녀는 나흘이라는 말에 힘을 주었다."]], [["“Jesus saith unto her, Said I not unto thee that if thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see the glory of God?", "\"예수께서 이르시되, 내 말이 네가 믿으면 하느님의 영광을 보리라 하지 아니하였느냐 하시니,"]], [["“Then they took away the stone from the place where the dead was laid. And Jesus lifted up His eyes and said, Father, I thank Thee that Thou hast heard Me.", "돌을 옮겨 놓으니 예수께서 눈을 들어 우러러 보시고 이르시되, 아버지여 내 말을 들으신 것을 감사하나이다."]], [["“And I knew that Thou hearest Me always; but because of the people which stand by I said it, that they may believe that Thou hast sent Me.", "항상 내 말을 들으시는 줄을 내가 알았나이다마는 둘러선 무리를 위하여 이 말씀을 하옵나니 곧 아버지께서 나를 보내신 것을 그들로 믿게 하려 함이니이다."]], [["“And when He thus had spoken, He cried with a loud voice, Lazarus, come forth.", "이 말씀을 하시고 큰 소리로 나사로야 나오라 부르시니,"]], [["“And he that was dead came forth.”", "죽은 자가 나오는데,\""]], [["(She read loudly, cold and trembling with ecstasy, as though she were seeing it before her eyes.)", "(그녀는 마치 그것을 눈앞에서 보기라도 하듯, 황홀경에 차갑게 떨면서 큰 소리로 읽었다.)"]], [["“Bound hand and foot with graveclothes; and his face was bound about with a napkin.", "\"손발은 베로 동였고 얼굴은 수건에 싸였더라."], ["Jesus saith unto them, Loose him and let him go.", "예수께서 이르시되, 풀어 놓아 다니게 하라 하시니라."]], [["“Then many of the Jews which came to Mary and had seen the things which Jesus did believed on Him.”", "마리아에게 와서 예수께서 하신 일을 본 많은 유대인이 그를 믿었더라.\""]], [["She could read no more, closed the book and got up from her chair quickly.", "그녀는 더는 읽지 못하고, 책을 덮고는 의자에서 얼른 일어섰다."]], [["“That is all about the raising of Lazarus,”", "\"나사로를 살리는 이야기는 그게 다예요.\""], ["she whispered severely and abruptly, and turning away she stood motionless, not daring to raise her eyes to him.", "그녀가 엄하게, 또 불쑥 속삭이고는, 몸을 돌려 감히 그에게 눈을 들지 못한 채 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["She still trembled feverishly.", "그녀는 여전히 열병처럼 떨고 있었다."], ["The candle-end was flickering out in the battered candlestick, dimly lighting up in the poverty-stricken room the murderer and the harlot who had so strangely been reading together the eternal book.", "우그러진 촛대에서 타다 남은 양초가 가물거리며 꺼져 가면서, 그 가난에 찌든 방 안에서 그토록 기이하게 영원한 책을 함께 읽고 있던 살인자와 창녀를 어슴푸레 비추었다."], ["Five minutes or more passed.", "오 분, 아니 그 이상이 흘렀다."]], [["“I came to speak of something,”", "\"한 가지 할 말이 있어서 왔소.\""], ["Raskolnikov said aloud, frowning.", "라스콜니코프가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 소리 내어 말했다."], ["He got up and went to Sonia.", "그는 일어나 소냐에게 다가갔다."], ["She lifted her eyes to him in silence.", "그녀는 말없이 그에게 눈을 들었다."], ["His face was particularly stern and there was a sort of savage determination in it.", "그의 얼굴은 유난히 엄했고, 그 안에는 일종의 사나운 결의가 어려 있었다."]], [["“I have abandoned my family to-day,”", "\"나는 오늘 가족을 버렸소.\""], ["he said, “my mother and sister.", "그가 말했다. \"어머니와 누이를."], ["I am not going to see them.", "나는 저들을 보러 가지 않을 거요."], ["I’ve broken with them completely.”", "저들과 완전히 인연을 끊었소.\""]], [["“What for?”", "\"무엇 때문에요?\""], ["asked Sonia amazed.", "소냐가 깜짝 놀라 물었다."], ["Her recent meeting with his mother and sister had left a great impression which she could not analyse.", "얼마 전 그의 어머니, 누이와의 만남은 그녀로서는 헤아릴 수 없는 깊은 인상을 남겼던 터였다."], ["She heard his news almost with horror.", "그녀는 거의 공포에 질려 그의 소식을 들었다."]], [["“I have only you now,”", "\"이제 내겐 당신밖에 없소.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["“Let us go together....", "\"함께 갑시다……."], ["I’ve come to you, we are both accursed, let us go our way together!”", "나는 당신에게 왔소, 우리 둘 다 저주받은 몸이오, 함께 우리의 길을 갑시다!\""]], [["His eyes glittered “as though he were mad,” Sonia thought, in her turn.", "그의 두 눈이 번득였는데, 이번에는 소냐가 '마치 미친 사람 같다'고 생각했다."]], [["“Go where?”", "\"어디로 가요?\""], ["she asked in alarm and she involuntarily stepped back.", "그녀가 겁에 질려 물으며, 무심결에 뒷걸음질 쳤다."]], [["“How do I know?", "\"낸들 어찌 알겠소?"], ["I only know it’s the same road, I know that and nothing more.", "다만 그게 같은 길이라는 것만은 알아, 그것만 알 뿐 그 이상은 모르오."], ["It’s the same goal!”", "같은 목표라는 것만!\""]], [["She looked at him and understood nothing.", "그녀는 그를 바라보았으나 아무것도 이해하지 못했다."], ["She knew only that he was terribly, infinitely unhappy.", "그녀가 아는 것은 다만 그가 끔찍하게, 한없이 불행하다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["“No one of them will understand, if you tell them, but I have understood.", "\"당신이 저들에게 말한들 그 누구도 이해하지 못할 거요, 하지만 나는 이해했소."], ["I need you, that is why I have come to you.”", "나는 당신이 필요하오, 그래서 당신에게 온 거요.\""]], [["“I don’t understand,”", "\"이해할 수가 없어요.\""], ["whispered Sonia.", "소냐가 속삭였다."]], [["“You’ll understand later.", "\"나중에 이해하게 될 거요."], ["Haven’t you done the same?", "당신도 똑같은 짓을 하지 않았소?"], ["You, too, have transgressed...", "당신도 마찬가지로 죄를 범했소……"], ["have had the strength to transgress.", "죄를 범할 힘이 있었던 거요."], ["You have laid hands on yourself, you have destroyed a life...", "당신은 스스로에게 손을 댔소, 한 생명을 파괴했소……"], ["_your own_ (it’s all the same!).", "당신 자신의 (그게 그거지!)."], ["You might have lived in spirit and understanding, but you’ll end in the Hay Market....", "당신은 정신과 깨달음 속에서 살 수도 있었을 텐데, 결국 건초 시장에서 끝나고 말 거요……."], ["But you won’t be able to stand it, and if you remain alone you’ll go out of your mind like me.", "하지만 당신은 그걸 견딜 수 없을 거고, 혼자 남는다면 나처럼 미쳐 버릴 거요."], ["You are like a mad creature already.", "당신은 이미 미친 사람 같소."], ["So we must go together on the same road!", "그러니 우리는 함께 같은 길을 가야 하오!"], ["Let us go!”", "갑시다!\""]], [["“What for?", "\"무엇 때문에요?"], ["What’s all this for?”", "이게 다 무엇 때문이에요?\""], ["said Sonia, strangely and violently agitated by his words.", "소냐가 그의 말에 야릇하고 격렬하게 동요하며 말했다."]], [["“What for?", "\"무엇 때문이냐고?"], ["Because you can’t remain like this, that’s why!", "당신이 이대로 머물 수 없으니까, 그래서요!"], ["You must look things straight in the face at last, and not weep like a child and cry that God won’t allow it.", "당신은 마침내 모든 걸 똑바로 직시해야 하고, 아이처럼 울거나 하느님이 그걸 허락하지 않으실 거라고 울부짖어서는 안 되오."], ["What will happen, if you should really be taken to the hospital to-morrow?", "만일 당신이 정말 내일이라도 병원에 실려 가면 어떻게 되겠소?"], ["She is mad and in consumption, she’ll soon die and the children?", "그 여자는 미쳤고 폐병에 걸렸으니 곧 죽을 거고, 그럼 아이들은?"], ["Do you mean to tell me Polenka won’t come to grief?", "폴렌카가 비참한 신세가 되지 않으리라고 말할 셈이오?"], ["Haven’t you seen children here at the street corners sent out by their mothers to beg?", "어미들이 구걸하라고 길모퉁이로 내보낸 아이들을 여기서 못 봤소? 나는 그 어미들이 어디서 어떤 환경 속에 사는지 알아냈소."], ["I’ve found out where those mothers live and in what surroundings.", "거기서 아이들은 아이로 남아 있을 수가 없소!"], ["Children can’t remain children there!", "일곱 살이면 그 아이는 타락하고 도둑이 되오."], ["At seven the child is vicious and a thief.", "하지만 아이들이란, 알다시피, 그리스도의 형상이오."], ["Yet children, you know, are the image of Christ: ‘theirs is the kingdom of Heaven.’ He bade us honour and love them, they are the humanity of the future....”", "'천국이 그들의 것이니라.' 그분은 우리에게 그들을 공경하고 사랑하라 명하셨소, 그들은 미래의 인류요…….\""]], [["“What’s to be done, what’s to be done?”", "\"어떻게 해야 하죠, 어떻게 해야 하죠?\""], ["repeated Sonia, weeping hysterically and wringing her hands.", "소냐가 히스테릭하게 울며 두 손을 비틀며 되풀이했다."]], [["“What’s to be done?", "\"어떻게 해야 하느냐고?"], ["Break what must be broken, once for all, that’s all, and take the suffering on oneself.", "부숴야 할 것을 단번에 영영 부숴 버리는 거요, 그게 다요, 그리고 그 고통을 스스로 짊어지는 거요."], ["What, you don’t understand?", "뭐, 이해가 안 되오?"], ["You’ll understand later....", "나중에 이해하게 될 거요……."], ["Freedom and power, and above all, power!", "자유와 권력, 그리고 무엇보다 권력!"], ["Over all trembling creation and all the ant-heap!...", "떨고 있는 모든 피조물과 온 개미 떼 위에 군림하는!……"], ["That’s the goal, remember that!", "그게 목표요, 그걸 기억하시오!"], ["That’s my farewell message.", "그게 내가 작별하며 남기는 말이오."], ["Perhaps it’s the last time I shall speak to you.", "어쩌면 당신에게 말하는 게 이번이 마지막일지도 모르오."], ["If I don’t come to-morrow, you’ll hear of it all, and then remember these words.", "내가 내일 오지 않으면, 당신은 그 모든 걸 듣게 될 거고, 그때 이 말들을 기억하시오."], ["And some day later on, in years to come, you’ll understand perhaps what they meant.", "그리고 언젠가 훗날, 세월이 흐른 뒤에, 어쩌면 당신은 그 말들이 무슨 뜻이었는지 이해하게 될 거요."], ["If I come to-morrow, I’ll tell you who killed Lizaveta....", "만일 내일 온다면, 누가 리자베타를 죽였는지 당신에게 말해 주겠소……."], ["Good-bye.”", "잘 있으시오.\""]], [["Sonia started with terror.", "소냐는 공포로 흠칫했다."]], [["“Why, do you know who killed her?”", "\"아니, 누가 그분을 죽였는지 아세요?\""], ["she asked, chilled with horror, looking wildly at him.", "그녀가 공포에 얼어붙어, 정신없는 눈으로 그를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“I know and will tell...", "\"알고 있고, 또 말해 주겠소……"], ["you, only you.", "당신에게, 오직 당신에게만."], ["I have chosen you out.", "나는 당신을 골랐소."], ["I’m not coming to you to ask forgiveness, but simply to tell you.", "나는 용서를 구하러 당신에게 온 게 아니라, 그저 말해 주려고 온 거요."], ["I chose you out long ago to hear this, when your father talked of you and when Lizaveta was alive, I thought of it.", "나는 오래전에 이 이야기를 들을 사람으로 당신을 골라 뒀소, 당신 아버지가 당신 얘기를 했을 때, 또 리자베타가 살아 있을 때, 나는 그 생각을 했소."], ["Good-bye, don’t shake hands.", "잘 있으시오, 악수는 하지 맙시다."], ["To-morrow!”", "내일!\""]], [["He went out.", "그는 나갔다."], ["Sonia gazed at him as at a madman.", "소냐는 미친 사람을 보듯 그를 응시했다."], ["But she herself was like one insane and felt it.", "하지만 그녀 자신도 정신 나간 사람 같았고, 또 그것을 느꼈다."], ["Her head was going round.", "머리가 빙빙 돌았다."]], [["“Good heavens, how does he know who killed Lizaveta?", "'세상에, 저이가 누가 리자베타를 죽였는지 어떻게 안단 말인가?"], ["What did those words mean?", "그 말들은 무슨 뜻이었을까?"], ["It’s awful!”", "끔찍해!'"], ["But at the same time _the idea_ did not enter her head, not for a moment!", "하지만 동시에 그 생각은 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오르지 않았다, 한순간도!"], ["“Oh, he must be terribly unhappy!...", "'오, 저이는 분명 끔찍하게 불행한 거야!……"], ["He has abandoned his mother and sister....", "어머니와 누이를 버렸다니……. 무엇 때문에?"], ["What for? What has happened?", "무슨 일이 있었던 걸까?"], ["And what had he in his mind?", "그리고 무슨 생각을 품고 있었던 걸까?"], ["What did he say to her?", "내게 무슨 말을 한 거지?"], ["He had kissed her foot and said...", "그이가 내 발에 입 맞추고 말했지……"], ["said (yes, he had said it clearly) that he could not live without her....", "말했어 (그래, 분명히 그렇게 말했어) 나 없이는 살 수 없다고……."], ["Oh, merciful heavens!”", "오, 자비로우신 하느님!'"]], [["Sonia spent the whole night feverish and delirious.", "소냐는 밤새도록 열에 들떠 헛소리를 했다."], ["She jumped up from time to time, wept and wrung her hands, then sank again into feverish sleep and dreamt of Polenka, Katerina Ivanovna and Lizaveta, of reading the gospel and him...", "이따금 벌떡 일어나 울며 두 손을 비틀다가, 다시 열에 들뜬 잠에 빠져들어 폴렌카와, 카테리나 이바노브나와, 리자베타의 꿈을, 복음서를 읽는 꿈을, 그리고 그의 꿈을 꾸었다……"], ["him with pale face, with burning eyes...", "창백한 얼굴에, 불타는 눈으로……"], ["kissing her feet, weeping.", "그녀의 발에 입 맞추며 우는 그의 꿈을."]], [["On the other side of the door on the right, which divided Sonia’s room from Madame Resslich’s flat, was a room which had long stood empty.", "소냐의 방을 레슬리히 부인의 셋집과 가르는 오른쪽 문 너머에는, 오래도록 비어 있던 방이 하나 있었다."], ["A card was fixed on the gate and a notice stuck in the windows over the canal advertising it to let.", "대문에는 광고 패가 붙어 있었고 운하 쪽으로 난 창에는 세놓는다는 안내문이 끼워져 있었다."], ["Sonia had long been accustomed to the room’s being uninhabited.", "소냐는 오래전부터 그 방이 비어 있다는 데 익숙해져 있었다."], ["But all that time Mr. Svidrigaïlov had been standing, listening at the door of the empty room.", "그러나 그동안 내내 스비드리가일로프 씨가 그 빈방 문 앞에 서서 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["When Raskolnikov went out he stood still, thought a moment, went on tiptoe to his own room which adjoined the empty one, brought a chair and noiselessly carried it to the door that led to Sonia’s room.", "라스콜니코프가 나갔을 때 그는 가만히 서서 잠시 생각하다가, 발끝으로 살금살금 그 빈방에 잇닿은 자기 방으로 가서 의자 하나를 가져와, 소리 없이 그것을 소냐의 방으로 통하는 문 앞으로 옮겨 놓았다."], ["The conversation had struck him as interesting and remarkable, and he had greatly enjoyed it--so much so that he brought a chair that he might not in the future, to-morrow, for instance, have to endure the inconvenience of standing a whole hour, but might listen in comfort.", "그 대화는 그에게 흥미롭고 주목할 만한 것으로 다가왔고, 그는 그것을 무척 즐겼던 터라 — 어찌나 즐겼는지, 앞으로, 이를테면 내일 같은 때 한 시간 내내 서 있어야 하는 불편을 겪지 않고 편히 들을 수 있도록 의자를 가져온 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["When next morning at eleven o’clock punctually Raskolnikov went into the department of the investigation of criminal causes and sent his name in to Porfiry Petrovitch, he was surprised at being kept waiting so long: it was at least ten minutes before he was summoned.", "이튿날 아침 정확히 열한 시에 라스콜니코프가 형사 사건 수사부로 들어가 포르피리 페트로비치에게 자기 이름을 전했을 때, 그는 너무 오래 기다리게 한 것에 놀랐다. 호출될 때까지 적어도 십 분은 걸렸다."], ["He had expected that they would pounce upon him.", "그는 그들이 자기에게 와락 달려들 줄 알았다."], ["But he stood in the waiting-room, and people, who apparently had nothing to do with him, were continually passing to and fro before him.", "그러나 그는 대기실에 서 있었고, 그와는 전혀 상관없어 보이는 사람들이 그의 앞을 끊임없이 오갔다."], ["In the next room which looked like an office, several clerks were sitting writing and obviously they had no notion who or what Raskolnikov might be.", "사무실처럼 보이는 옆방에서는 여러 서기가 앉아 무언가를 쓰고 있었는데, 라스콜니코프가 누구인지, 무엇 하는 자인지 도통 관심이 없는 게 분명했다."], ["He looked uneasily and suspiciously about him to see whether there was not some guard, some mysterious watch being kept on him to prevent his escape.", "그는 자신의 도주를 막기 위해 무슨 경비나 은밀한 감시가 붙어 있지는 않은지 살피며 불안하고 의심스러운 눈초리로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["But there was nothing of the sort: he saw only the faces of clerks absorbed in petty details, then other people, no one seemed to have any concern with him.", "그러나 그런 것은 전혀 없었다. 그저 자질구레한 일에 골몰한 서기들의 얼굴과 다른 사람들이 보일 뿐, 그 누구도 그에게 신경 쓰는 것 같지 않았다."], ["He might go where he liked for them.", "그들로서는 그가 어디로 가든 상관없는 일이었다."], ["The conviction grew stronger in him that if that enigmatic man of yesterday, that phantom sprung out of the earth, had seen everything, they would not have let him stand and wait like that.", "어제의 그 수수께끼 같은 사내, 땅에서 솟아난 그 유령이 모든 것을 보았다면 자신을 이렇게 세워 두고 기다리게 하지는 않았으리라는 확신이 그의 마음속에서 점점 강해졌다."], ["And would they have waited till he elected to appear at eleven?", "게다가 그들이 그가 마음 내켜 열한 시에 나타날 때까지 기다리기나 했겠는가?"], ["Either the man had not yet given information, or...", "그 사내가 아직 밀고하지 않았거나, 아니면……"], ["or simply he knew nothing, had seen nothing (and how could he have seen anything?) and so all that had happened to him the day before was again a phantom exaggerated by his sick and overstrained imagination.", "아니면 단지 그가 아무것도 모르고, 아무것도 보지 못했거나(대체 무엇을 볼 수 있었겠는가?), 그래서 어제 그에게 일어난 모든 일은 또다시 병들고 과도하게 긴장된 상상력이 부풀려 놓은 유령에 불과한 것이었다."], ["This conjecture had begun to grow strong the day before, in the midst of all his alarm and despair.", "이 추측은 어제, 온갖 불안과 절망 속에서 이미 강해지기 시작했었다."], ["Thinking it all over now and preparing for a fresh conflict, he was suddenly aware that he was trembling--and he felt a rush of indignation at the thought that he was trembling with fear at facing that hateful Porfiry Petrovitch.", "이제 그 모든 것을 곱씹으며 새로운 싸움을 준비하던 그는 문득 자신이 떨고 있음을 깨달았고, 저 가증스러운 포르피리 페트로비치를 마주하는 것에 두려움으로 떨고 있다는 생각에 분노가 치밀어 올랐다."], ["What he dreaded above all was meeting that man again; he hated him with an intense, unmitigated hatred and was afraid his hatred might betray him.", "그가 무엇보다 두려워한 것은 그 사내를 다시 만나는 일이었다. 그는 강렬하고 누그러질 줄 모르는 증오로 그를 미워했고, 그 증오가 자신을 드러낼까 봐 두려웠다."], ["His indignation was such that he ceased trembling at once; he made ready to go in with a cold and arrogant bearing and vowed to himself to keep as silent as possible, to watch and listen and for once at least to control his overstrained nerves.", "그의 분노가 어찌나 컸던지 그는 곧장 떨기를 멈추었다. 그는 차갑고 오만한 태도로 들어갈 채비를 했고, 가능한 한 입을 다물고, 지켜보고 듣기만 하며, 적어도 이번 한 번만큼은 과도하게 곤두선 신경을 다스리겠다고 스스로에게 맹세했다."], ["At that moment he was summoned to Porfiry Petrovitch.", "바로 그 순간 그는 포르피리 페트로비치에게로 호출되었다."]], [["He found Porfiry Petrovitch alone in his study.", "그는 포르피리 페트로비치가 자기 집무실에 혼자 있는 것을 보았다."], ["His study was a room neither large nor small, furnished with a large writing-table, that stood before a sofa, upholstered in checked material, a bureau, a bookcase in the corner and several chairs--all government furniture, of polished yellow wood.", "그의 집무실은 크지도 작지도 않은 방으로, 체크무늬 천을 씌운 소파 앞에 놓인 커다란 책상, 작은 사무용 책상, 한쪽 구석의 책장, 그리고 의자 몇 개가 비치되어 있었는데, 모두 반들반들 윤이 나는 노란 목재로 된 관청 가구였다."], ["In the further wall there was a closed door, beyond it there were no doubt other rooms.", "안쪽 벽에는 닫힌 문이 하나 있었고, 그 너머에는 분명 다른 방들이 있을 터였다."], ["On Raskolnikov’s entrance Porfiry Petrovitch had at once closed the door by which he had come in and they remained alone.", "라스콜니코프가 들어서자 포르피리 페트로비치는 곧장 자기가 들어온 문을 닫았고, 둘만 남게 되었다."], ["He met his visitor with an apparently genial and good-tempered air, and it was only after a few minutes that Raskolnikov saw signs of a certain awkwardness in him, as though he had been thrown out of his reckoning or caught in something very secret.", "그는 겉보기엔 상냥하고 싹싹한 태도로 손님을 맞았는데, 몇 분이 지나서야 라스콜니코프는 그에게서 어떤 어색함의 기색을, 마치 계산이 어긋났거나 아주 은밀한 무언가를 들킨 듯한 기색을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Ah, my dear fellow!", "\"아, 이보게!"], ["Here you are...", "자네가 왔군……"], ["in our domain”...", "우리 영역으로 말일세……\""], ["began Porfiry, holding out both hands to him.", "포르피리는 두 손을 내밀며 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Come, sit down, old man...", "\"자, 앉게, 친구……"], ["or perhaps you don’t like to be called ‘my dear fellow’ and ‘old man!’--_tout court_?", "아니, 어쩌면 자네는 '이보게'니 '친구'니 하고 불리는 게 싫을지도 모르겠군, 투 쿠르(tout court)로 말이야?"], ["Please don’t think it too familiar....", "제발 너무 허물없다고 여기지는 말게나……."], ["Here, on the sofa.”", "자, 여기, 소파에 앉게.\""]], [["Raskolnikov sat down, keeping his eyes fixed on him.", "라스콜니코프는 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 앉았다."], ["“In our domain,” the apologies for familiarity, the French phrase _tout court_, were all characteristic signs.", "\"우리 영역\"이라는 말, 허물없음에 대한 변명, 투 쿠르라는 프랑스어 표현, 이 모든 것이 특징적인 징후였다."]], [["“He held out both hands to me, but he did not give me one--he drew it back in time,”", "\"그는 나에게 두 손을 내밀었지만, 손은 한 번도 내주지 않았어 — 제때 거두어들였지\""], ["struck him suspiciously.", "하는 생각이 그에게 의심스럽게 박혔다."], ["Both were watching each other, but when their eyes met, quick as lightning they looked away.", "둘은 서로를 살피고 있었으나, 눈길이 마주치면 번개처럼 재빨리 시선을 돌렸다."]], [["“I brought you this paper...", "\"이 서류를 가져왔습니다……"], ["about the watch.", "시계에 관한 것 말입니다."], ["Here it is.", "여기 있습니다."], ["Is it all right or shall I copy it again?”", "이대로 괜찮습니까, 아니면 다시 베껴 써야 합니까?\""]], [["“What? A paper?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["Yes, yes, don’t be uneasy, it’s all right,”", "서류? 그래요, 그래요, 걱정 마세요, 괜찮습니다.\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch said as though in haste, and after he had said it he took the paper and looked at it.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 서두르듯 말하고 나서 서류를 받아 들여다보았다."], ["“Yes, it’s all right.", "\"그래요, 괜찮습니다."], ["Nothing more is needed,”", "더 필요한 건 없어요.\""], ["he declared with the same rapidity and he laid the paper on the table.", "그는 똑같이 재빠르게 단언하고는 서류를 책상 위에 내려놓았다."]], [["A minute later when he was talking of something else he took it from the table and put it on his bureau.", "잠시 후 그는 다른 이야기를 하다가 그것을 책상에서 집어 자기 사무용 책상 위로 옮겨 놓았다."]], [["“I believe you said yesterday you would like to question me...", "\"어제 말씀하시기를, 저를 심문하고 싶다고 하셨던 것 같은데요……"], ["formally...", "정식으로……"], ["about my acquaintance with the murdered woman?”", "살해당한 여인과의 제 친분에 관해서 말입니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov was beginning again.", "라스콜니코프가 다시 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Why did I put in ‘I believe’”", "\"왜 내가 '같은데요'라고 붙였지?\""], ["passed through his mind in a flash.", "하는 생각이 번개처럼 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["“Why am I so uneasy at having put in that ‘_I believe_’?”", "\"왜 내가 그 '같은데요'를 붙인 것에 이토록 불안해하는 거지?\""], ["came in a second flash.", "두 번째 번개가 뒤따랐다."], ["And he suddenly felt that his uneasiness at the mere contact with Porfiry, at the first words, at the first looks, had grown in an instant to monstrous proportions, and that this was fearfully dangerous.", "그리고 그는 문득, 포르피리와 그저 접촉하는 것만으로도, 첫마디에, 첫 눈길에 느낀 자신의 불안이 순식간에 괴물 같은 크기로 자라났음을, 그리고 이것이 끔찍하게 위험하다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["His nerves were quivering, his emotion was increasing.", "그의 신경은 떨리고, 흥분은 점점 커져 갔다."], ["“It’s bad, it’s bad!", "\"나쁘다, 나빠!"], ["I shall say too much again.”", "또 너무 많은 말을 하겠어.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes!", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그래!"], ["There’s no hurry, there’s no hurry,”", "서두를 것 없습니다, 서두를 것 없어요.\""], ["muttered Porfiry Petrovitch, moving to and fro about the table without any apparent aim, as it were making dashes towards the window, the bureau and the table, at one moment avoiding Raskolnikov’s suspicious glance, then again standing still and looking him straight in the face.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 별다른 목적도 없이 책상 주위를 이리저리 오가며 중얼거렸는데, 마치 창문이며 사무용 책상이며 책상으로 불쑥불쑥 달려가는 듯하다가, 어느 순간엔 라스콜니코프의 의심스러운 눈길을 피하고, 또 어느 순간엔 가만히 멈춰 서서 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다."]], [["His fat round little figure looked very strange, like a ball rolling from one side to the other and rebounding back.", "뚱뚱하고 동그란 그의 작은 몸뚱이는 한쪽에서 다른 쪽으로 굴러갔다가 다시 튕겨 돌아오는 공처럼 무척 기묘해 보였다."]], [["“We’ve plenty of time.", "\"우리에겐 시간이 넉넉합니다."], ["Do you smoke?", "담배 피우시나요?"], ["have you your own?", "자기 것이 있나요?"], ["Here, a cigarette!”", "자, 여기, 담배 한 대!\""], ["he went on, offering his visitor a cigarette.", "그는 손님에게 담배를 권하며 말을 이었다."], ["“You know I am receiving you here, but my own quarters are through there, you know, my government quarters.", "\"아시다시피 나는 지금 여기서 손님을 맞고 있지만, 내 거처는 저쪽 너머에 있지요, 아시겠지만, 내 관사 말입니다."], ["But I am living outside for the time, I had to have some repairs done here.", "그런데 당분간은 바깥에 나가 살고 있어요, 여기 수리를 좀 해야 했거든요."], ["It’s almost finished now....", "이제 거의 다 끝나갑니다만……."], ["Government quarters, you know, are a capital thing.", "관사라는 게, 아시다시피, 굉장한 거지요."], ["Eh, what do you think?”", "어떻게 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, a capital thing,”", "\"그래요, 굉장한 거지요.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov, looking at him almost ironically.", "라스콜니코프는 거의 비꼬는 눈으로 그를 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["“A capital thing, a capital thing,”", "\"굉장한 거예요, 굉장한 거지.\""], ["repeated Porfiry Petrovitch, as though he had just thought of something quite different.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 마치 방금 전혀 다른 무언가가 떠오른 듯 되풀이했다."], ["“Yes, a capital thing,”", "\"그래요, 굉장한 거지요.\""], ["he almost shouted at last, suddenly staring at Raskolnikov and stopping short two steps from him.", "마침내 그는 거의 소리치다시피 하며 불쑥 라스콜니코프를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그에게서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["This stupid repetition was too incongruous in its ineptitude with the serious, brooding and enigmatic glance he turned upon his visitor.", "이 어리석은 되풀이는 그 서투름이, 그가 손님에게 던진 진지하고 침울하며 수수께끼 같은 눈길과 너무도 어울리지 않았다."]], [["But this stirred Raskolnikov’s spleen more than ever and he could not resist an ironical and rather incautious challenge.", "그러나 이것은 그 어느 때보다도 라스콜니코프의 비위를 건드렸고, 그는 빈정대는, 다소 경솔한 도발을 참을 수가 없었다."]], [["“Tell me, please,”", "\"한 가지 여쭙겠습니다.\""], ["he asked suddenly, looking almost insolently at him and taking a kind of pleasure in his own insolence.", "그가 거의 무례하다 싶게 그를 바라보며, 자기 무례함에 일종의 쾌감을 느끼면서 불쑥 물었다."], ["“I believe it’s a sort of legal rule, a sort of legal tradition--for all investigating lawyers--to begin their attack from afar, with a trivial, or at least an irrelevant subject, so as to encourage, or rather, to divert the man they are cross-examining, to disarm his caution and then all at once to give him an unexpected knock-down blow with some fatal question.", "\"이게 일종의 법적 규칙, 일종의 법적 관행인 것 같던데요 — 모든 예심판사들에게 말입니다 — 멀리서부터, 사소한, 적어도 본론과 무관한 주제로 공격을 시작해서, 심문받는 자를 격려하거나, 차라리 주의를 흩뜨려서, 그 경계심을 무장해제시킨 다음, 느닷없이 어떤 치명적인 질문으로 예기치 못한 일격을 가하는 것 말입니다."], ["Isn’t that so?", "그렇지 않습니까?"], ["It’s a sacred tradition, mentioned, I fancy, in all the manuals of the art?”", "모든 기술 교본에 언급되어 있을 법한, 신성한 관행이지요?\""]], [["“Yes, yes....", "\"그래요, 그래요……."], ["Why, do you imagine that was why I spoke about government quarters...", "아니, 자네는 내가 그래서 관사 얘기를 꺼냈다고 생각하는 건가……"], ["eh?”", "응?\""]], [["And as he said this Porfiry Petrovitch screwed up his eyes and winked; a good-humoured, crafty look passed over his face.", "이렇게 말하면서 포르피리 페트로비치는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 윙크를 했다. 그의 얼굴에는 유쾌하고 교활한 표정이 스쳤다."], ["The wrinkles on his forehead were smoothed out, his eyes contracted, his features broadened and he suddenly went off into a nervous prolonged laugh, shaking all over and looking Raskolnikov straight in the face.", "이마의 주름이 펴지고, 두 눈이 오므라들고, 이목구비가 넓어지더니, 그는 갑자기 온몸을 흔들며 라스콜니코프의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보면서 신경질적이고 길게 늘어지는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["The latter forced himself to laugh, too, but when Porfiry, seeing that he was laughing, broke into such a guffaw that he turned almost crimson, Raskolnikov’s repulsion overcame all precaution; he left off laughing, scowled and stared with hatred at Porfiry, keeping his eyes fixed on him while his intentionally prolonged laughter lasted.", "라스콜니코프도 억지로 웃었으나, 포르피리가 그가 웃는 것을 보고 얼굴이 거의 새빨개지도록 너털웃음을 터뜨리자, 라스콜니코프의 혐오감이 모든 경계심을 압도했다. 그는 웃음을 멈추고, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 포르피리의 일부러 길게 끄는 웃음이 이어지는 내내 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않고 증오의 눈길로 노려보았다."], ["There was lack of precaution on both sides, however, for Porfiry Petrovitch seemed to be laughing in his visitor’s face and to be very little disturbed at the annoyance with which the visitor received it.", "그러나 경계심의 결여는 양쪽 모두에게 있었으니, 포르피리 페트로비치도 손님의 얼굴에 대고 웃어 대는 듯했고, 손님이 그것을 짜증스럽게 받아들이는데도 거의 개의치 않는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["The latter fact was very significant in Raskolnikov’s eyes: he saw that Porfiry Petrovitch had not been embarrassed just before either, but that he, Raskolnikov, had perhaps fallen into a trap; that there must be something, some motive here unknown to him; that, perhaps, everything was in readiness and in another moment would break upon him...", "이 후자의 사실은 라스콜니코프의 눈에 매우 의미심장했다. 그는 포르피리 페트로비치가 방금 전에도 결코 당황한 것이 아니었으며, 오히려 자신이, 라스콜니코프 자신이 어쩌면 함정에 빠진 것인지도 모른다는 것, 여기에는 무언가, 그가 모르는 어떤 동기가 분명 있으리라는 것, 어쩌면 모든 것이 이미 준비되어 있어 다음 순간 그에게 들이닥칠지도 모른다는 것을 알아챘다……."]], [["He went straight to the point at once, rose from his seat and took his cap.", "그는 곧장 본론으로 들어가, 자리에서 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다."]], [["“Porfiry Petrovitch,”", "\"포르피리 페트로비치.\""], ["he began resolutely, though with considerable irritation, “yesterday you expressed a desire that I should come to you for some inquiries”", "그는 상당한 짜증을 품고서도 단호하게 말을 꺼냈다. \"어제 당신은 제가 몇 가지 심문을 위해 당신에게 와 주기를 바란다고 하셨습니다.\""], ["(he laid special stress on the word “inquiries”).", "(그는 \"심문\"이라는 말에 특별히 힘을 주었다.) \"그래서 왔습니다."], ["“I have come and if you have anything to ask me, ask it, and if not, allow me to withdraw.", "제게 물어볼 것이 있으면 물어보시고, 없으면 물러가게 해 주십시오."], ["I have no time to spare....", "저는 한가하지 않습니다……."], ["I have to be at the funeral of that man who was run over, of whom you...", "마차에 치인 그 사람의 장례식에 가 봐야 하는데, 그 사람은 당신도……"], ["know also,”", "아실 겁니다.\""], ["he added, feeling angry at once at having made this addition and more irritated at his anger.", "그는 이 말을 덧붙인 것에 곧장 화가 났고, 그 화에 더욱 짜증이 나서 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["“I am sick of it all, do you hear?", "\"이 모든 것에 진절머리가 납니다, 들리십니까?"], ["and have long been.", "진작부터요."], ["It’s partly what made me ill.", "그것 때문에 병까지 났던 겁니다."], ["In short,”", "한마디로 말해서,\""], ["he shouted, feeling that the phrase about his illness was still more out of place, “in short, kindly examine me or let me go, at once.", "그는 자기 병에 관한 말이 더더욱 어울리지 않는다는 것을 느끼며 소리쳤다. \"한마디로, 부디 저를 심문하시든지 보내 주시든지, 지금 당장 그렇게 하십시오."], ["And if you must examine me, do so in the proper form!", "그리고 꼭 심문해야 한다면, 정식 형식대로 하십시오!"], ["I will not allow you to do so otherwise, and so meanwhile, good-bye, as we have evidently nothing to keep us now.”", "그렇지 않으면 저는 응하지 않겠습니다. 그러니 그동안은 안녕히 계십시오, 우리를 여기 붙잡아 둘 만한 게 분명 아무것도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["What do you mean?", "그게 무슨 말입니까?"], ["What shall I question you about?”", "내가 무엇에 대해 자네를 심문하겠나?\""], ["cackled Porfiry Petrovitch with a change of tone, instantly leaving off laughing.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 어조를 바꾸어 깔깔거리며, 곧장 웃음을 그쳤다."], ["“Please don’t disturb yourself,”", "\"제발 마음 쓰지 마세요.\""], ["he began fidgeting from place to place and fussily making Raskolnikov sit down.", "그는 이곳저곳으로 안절부절못하며 부산스럽게 라스콜니코프를 앉혔다."], ["“There’s no hurry, there’s no hurry, it’s all nonsense.", "\"서두를 것 없습니다, 서두를 것 없어요, 다 부질없는 소리예요."], ["Oh, no, I’m very glad you’ve come to see me at last...", "아니, 아니, 마침내 자네가 나를 찾아와 주어서 정말 기쁘답니다……."], ["I look upon you simply as a visitor.", "나는 자네를 그저 손님으로 여기고 있어요."], ["And as for my confounded laughter, please excuse it, Rodion Romanovitch.", "그리고 그 망할 내 웃음에 대해서는, 부디 용서해 주게, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["Rodion Romanovitch?", "로디온 로마노비치?"], ["That is your name?...", "자네 이름이 그렇지요?……"], ["It’s my nerves, you tickled me so with your witty observation; I assure you, sometimes I shake with laughter like an india-rubber ball for half an hour at a time....", "내 신경 탓이에요, 자네의 그 재치 있는 말이 어찌나 나를 간질이던지. 정말이지, 가끔 나는 고무공처럼 반시간씩 들썩이며 웃어 댄답니다……."], ["I’m often afraid of an attack of paralysis.", "종종 중풍이라도 올까 봐 겁이 나요."], ["Do sit down.", "자, 앉게."], ["Please do, or I shall think you are angry...”", "제발 앉아 주게, 안 그러면 자네가 화가 났다고 생각하겠어요…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov did not speak; he listened, watching him, still frowning angrily.", "라스콜니코프는 말이 없었다. 그는 여전히 화난 듯 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 그를 지켜보며 듣고 있었다."], ["He did sit down, but still held his cap.", "그는 앉기는 했으나, 여전히 모자를 쥐고 있었다."]], [["“I must tell you one thing about myself, my dear Rodion Romanovitch,”", "\"내 자신에 대해 한 가지 말해 두어야겠네, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치.\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch continued, moving about the room and again avoiding his visitor’s eyes.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 방 안을 거닐며, 또다시 손님의 눈을 피하면서 말을 이었다."], ["“You see, I’m a bachelor, a man of no consequence and not used to society; besides, I have nothing before me, I’m set, I’m running to seed and...", "\"보다시피 나는 독신이고, 보잘것없는 사람이라 사교에 익숙하지 않아요. 게다가 내 앞엔 아무것도 없고, 자리 잡았고, 이젠 시들어 가고 있지요, 그리고……"], ["and have you noticed, Rodion Romanovitch, that in our Petersburg circles, if two clever men meet who are not intimate, but respect each other, like you and me, it takes them half an hour before they can find a subject for conversation--they are dumb, they sit opposite each other and feel awkward.", "그리고 알아챘는지 모르겠네만, 로디온 로마노비치, 우리 페테르부르크 사교계에서는 친밀하지는 않으나 서로를 존중하는 영리한 두 사람이, 가령 자네와 나 같은 사람이 만나면, 대화의 주제를 찾기까지 반시간은 걸린다네 — 둘은 꿀 먹은 벙어리가 되어, 서로 마주 앉아 어색해하지요."], ["Everyone has subjects of conversation, ladies for instance...", "누구에게나 대화의 주제가 있어요, 가령 부인들에게는……"], ["people in high society always have their subjects of conversation, _c’est de rigueur_, but people of the middle sort like us, thinking people that is, are always tongue-tied and awkward.", "상류 사회 사람들에게는 늘 대화 주제가 있지요, 세 드 리괴르(c'est de rigueur)요, 하지만 우리 같은 중간 부류 사람들은, 그러니까 생각하는 사람들은 늘 말문이 막히고 어색해한다네."], ["What is the reason of it?", "그 이유가 뭘까요?"], ["Whether it is the lack of public interest, or whether it is we are so honest we don’t want to deceive one another, I don’t know.", "공공의 관심사가 없어서인지, 아니면 우리가 너무 정직해서 서로 속이고 싶지 않은 건지, 나도 모르겠어요."], ["What do you think?", "자네 생각은 어떤가?"], ["Do put down your cap, it looks as if you were just going, it makes me uncomfortable...", "그 모자는 내려놓게, 마치 금방이라도 떠나려는 것 같아 내가 불편하군……."], ["I am so delighted...”", "나는 이렇게나 기쁜데…….\""]], [["Raskolnikov put down his cap and continued listening in silence with a serious frowning face to the vague and empty chatter of Porfiry Petrovitch.", "라스콜니코프는 모자를 내려놓고, 진지하게 찌푸린 얼굴로 포르피리 페트로비치의 막연하고 공허한 수다를 잠자코 계속 듣고 있었다."], ["“Does he really want to distract my attention with his silly babble?”", "\"저자가 정말로 이 시시한 잡담으로 내 주의를 흩뜨리려는 걸까?\""]], [["“I can’t offer you coffee here; but why not spend five minutes with a friend?”", "\"여기서 자네에게 커피를 대접할 순 없지만, 친구와 오 분쯤 보내는 게 어떻겠나?\""], ["Porfiry pattered on, “and you know all these official duties...", "포르피리는 재잘재잘 말을 이었다. \"그리고 알다시피 이 모든 관청 업무라는 게……."], ["please don’t mind my running up and down, excuse it, my dear fellow, I am very much afraid of offending you, but exercise is absolutely indispensable for me.", "부디 내가 이렇게 오르락내리락하는 걸 개의치 말게, 미안하네, 친구, 자네 기분을 상하게 할까 봐 무척 겁이 나지만, 운동이라는 게 내겐 절대적으로 필요해서 말이야."], ["I’m always sitting and so glad to be moving about for five minutes...", "늘 앉아만 있다 보니 오 분이라도 움직이면 어찌나 좋은지……."], ["I suffer from my sedentary life...", "앉아서 일하는 생활 때문에 고생이거든……."], ["I always intend to join a gymnasium; they say that officials of all ranks, even Privy Councillors, may be seen skipping gaily there; there you have it, modern science...", "늘 체조 교실에 다닐 작정이긴 한데, 거기선 온갖 직급의 관리들이, 심지어 추밀 고문관까지도 신나게 줄넘기를 한다더군. 자, 보게나, 현대 과학이라는 거지요……."], ["yes, yes....", "그래요, 그래……."], ["But as for my duties here, inquiries and all such formalities...", "한데 여기서 내가 하는 업무, 심문이며 그 모든 형식적 절차로 말하자면……"], ["you mentioned inquiries yourself just now...", "자네가 방금 직접 심문을 입에 올렸지만……"], ["I assure you these interrogations are sometimes more embarrassing for the interrogator than for the interrogated....", "정말이지 이런 신문이라는 게 때로는 신문받는 자보다 신문하는 자에게 더 거북한 법이라네……."], ["You made the observation yourself just now very aptly and wittily.”", "자네가 방금 직접 아주 적절하고 재치 있게 그 점을 지적했지.\""], ["(Raskolnikov had made no observation of the kind.) “One gets into a muddle!", "(라스콜니코프는 그런 지적을 한 적이 없었다.) \"엉망진창이 되어 버린다오!"], ["A regular muddle!", "완전히 뒤죽박죽!"], ["One keeps harping on the same note, like a drum!", "북처럼 같은 가락만 줄곧 두들겨 대는 거지요!"], ["There is to be a reform and we shall be called by a different name, at least, he-he-he!", "개혁이 있을 거고, 그러면 적어도 우리 호칭이라도 달라지겠지요, 헤-헤-헤!"], ["And as for our legal tradition, as you so wittily called it, I thoroughly agree with you.", "그리고 우리의 법적 관행에 대해서라면, 자네가 그토록 재치 있게 일컬은 대로, 나도 전적으로 동감이라오."], ["Every prisoner on trial, even the rudest peasant, knows that they begin by disarming him with irrelevant questions (as you so happily put it) and then deal him a knock-down blow, he-he-he!--your felicitous comparison, he-he!", "재판받는 죄수라면 누구나, 가장 무지한 농사꾼이라도, 먼저 본론과 무관한 질문으로 무장해제시킨 다음(자네가 그토록 멋지게 표현한 대로) 일격을 가한다는 걸 알고 있지요, 헤-헤-헤! 자네의 그 절묘한 비유 말이야, 헤-헤!"], ["So you really imagined that I meant by ‘government quarters’...", "그래, 자네는 정말로 내가 '관사'라는 말로 그런 뜻을……"], ["he-he!", "헤-헤!"], ["You are an ironical person. Come.", "자네는 빈정대는 사람이야."], ["I won’t go on!", "자, 그만하지!"], ["Ah, by the way, yes!", "아, 그래, 참!"], ["One word leads to another.", "말이 말을 부르는군."], ["You spoke of formality just now, apropos of the inquiry, you know.", "자네가 방금 형식을 운운했지, 심문에 관해서 말이야."], ["But what’s the use of formality?", "한데 형식이라는 게 무슨 소용인가?"], ["In many cases it’s nonsense.", "많은 경우 그건 부질없는 거지요."], ["Sometimes one has a friendly chat and gets a good deal more out of it.", "때로는 친근하게 한담을 나누면서 훨씬 더 많은 걸 얻기도 한답니다."], ["One can always fall back on formality, allow me to assure you.", "형식이야 언제든 의지할 수 있는 거예요, 정말 그렇다네."], ["And after all, what does it amount to?", "그리고 따지고 보면 그게 다 뭐겠나?"], ["An examining lawyer cannot be bounded by formality at every step.", "예심판사가 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 형식에 얽매일 수는 없는 노릇이지."], ["The work of investigation is, so to speak, a free art in its own way, he-he-he!”", "수사라는 일은, 말하자면, 그 나름의 자유로운 예술이거든요, 헤-헤-헤!\""]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch took breath a moment.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 잠시 숨을 돌렸다."], ["He had simply babbled on uttering empty phrases, letting slip a few enigmatic words and again reverting to incoherence.", "그는 그저 공허한 말들을 늘어놓으며 재잘거렸고, 수수께끼 같은 말 몇 마디를 슬쩍 흘렸다가 다시 두서없는 이야기로 돌아갔다."], ["He was almost running about the room, moving his fat little legs quicker and quicker, looking at the ground, with his right hand behind his back, while with his left making gesticulations that were extraordinarily incongruous with his words.", "그는 거의 방 안을 뛰어다니다시피 하며 뚱뚱하고 짧은 다리를 점점 더 빨리 놀렸고, 오른손은 등 뒤로 돌린 채 바닥을 내려다보면서, 왼손으로는 자기 말과 유난히 어울리지 않는 손짓을 해 댔다."], ["Raskolnikov suddenly noticed that as he ran about the room he seemed twice to stop for a moment near the door, as though he were listening.", "라스콜니코프는 그가 방 안을 뛰어다니다가 두 번쯤, 마치 무언가에 귀를 기울이는 듯, 문 근처에서 잠시 멈춰 서는 것을 문득 알아챘다."]], [["“Is he expecting anything?”", "\"저자가 무언가를 기다리고 있는 건가?\""]], [["“You are certainly quite right about it,”", "\"자네 말이 정말 옳아요.\""], ["Porfiry began gaily, looking with extraordinary simplicity at Raskolnikov (which startled him and instantly put him on his guard); “certainly quite right in laughing so wittily at our legal forms, he-he!", "포르피리는 유난히 천진한 표정으로 라스콜니코프를 바라보며 명랑하게 말을 꺼냈는데(이것이 그를 놀라게 하여 곧장 경계하게 만들었다), \"우리 법적 형식을 그토록 재치 있게 비웃은 게 정말 옳다네, 헤-헤!"], ["Some of these elaborate psychological methods are exceedingly ridiculous and perhaps useless, if one adheres too closely to the forms.", "이 정교한 심리적 방법들 중 어떤 건 형식에 너무 매달리면 지독히 우스꽝스럽고 어쩌면 쓸모도 없지요. 그래요……"], ["Yes... I am talking of forms again.", "또 형식 얘기를 하고 있군."], ["Well, if I recognise, or more strictly speaking, if I suspect someone or other to be a criminal in any case entrusted to me...", "자, 만일 내가 내게 맡겨진 어떤 사건에서 누군가를 범인이라고 인정하거나, 좀 더 엄밀히 말해 의심한다면……"], ["you’re reading for the law, of course, Rodion Romanovitch?”", "자네도 물론 법학을 공부하고 있겠지, 로디온 로마노비치?\""]], [["“Yes, I was...”", "\"네, 했습니다만…….\""]], [["“Well, then it is a precedent for you for the future--though don’t suppose I should venture to instruct you after the articles you publish about crime!", "\"자, 그럼 이게 자네에게 장차 하나의 선례가 되겠군 — 하긴 자네가 범죄에 관해 발표한 논문들을 보고서야 내가 감히 자네를 가르치려 든다고는 여기지 말게나!"], ["No, I simply make bold to state it by way of fact, if I took this man or that for a criminal, why, I ask, should I worry him prematurely, even though I had evidence against him?", "아니, 나는 그저 사실 삼아 대담하게 말해 보는 거라네. 만일 내가 이 사람 저 사람을 범인으로 본다면, 그래, 내가 묻겠는데, 비록 그자에게 불리한 증거가 있다 한들 어째서 내가 그를 미리 들볶아야 하겠나?"], ["In one case I may be bound, for instance, to arrest a man at once, but another may be in quite a different position, you know, so why shouldn’t I let him walk about the town a bit?", "어떤 경우엔, 가령, 내가 그 사람을 즉시 체포해야만 할 수도 있지만, 또 다른 경우엔 사정이 전혀 달라서, 알다시피, 그러니 그자를 잠깐 시내나 돌아다니게 두면 안 될 게 뭐 있겠나?"], ["he-he-he!", "헤-헤-헤!"], ["But I see you don’t quite understand, so I’ll give you a clearer example.", "한데 자네가 잘 이해하지 못하는 것 같으니, 좀 더 분명한 예를 들어 보겠네."], ["If I put him in prison too soon, I may very likely give him, so to speak, moral support, he-he!", "만일 내가 그를 너무 일찍 감옥에 처넣으면, 십중팔구 나는, 말하자면, 그에게 정신적 지지를 주게 되는 거라네, 헤-헤!"], ["You’re laughing?”", "자네 웃고 있나?\""]], [["Raskolnikov had no idea of laughing.", "라스콜니코프는 웃을 생각이 조금도 없었다."], ["He was sitting with compressed lips, his feverish eyes fixed on Porfiry Petrovitch’s.", "그는 입술을 꾹 다물고, 열에 들뜬 눈을 포르피리 페트로비치의 눈에 고정한 채 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Yet that is the case, with some types especially, for men are so different.", "\"그렇지만 사실이 그렇다네, 특히 어떤 부류의 인간들에게는 말이야, 사람이란 워낙 제각각이니까."], ["You say ‘evidence’.", "자네는 '증거'를 말하지."], ["Well, there may be evidence.", "자, 증거야 있을 수 있어요."], ["But evidence, you know, can generally be taken two ways.", "하지만 증거라는 건, 알다시피, 대개 두 가지로 해석될 수 있지요."], ["I am an examining lawyer and a weak man, I confess it.", "나는 예심판사이고, 약한 인간이라네, 고백하건대."], ["I should like to make a proof, so to say, mathematically clear.", "나는 증거를, 말하자면, 수학적으로 명백하게 만들고 싶어요."], ["I should like to make a chain of evidence such as twice two are four, it ought to be a direct, irrefutable proof!", "둘에 둘을 더하면 넷이 되듯 그런 증거의 사슬을 만들고 싶다네, 직접적이고 반박할 수 없는 증거여야 하지요!"], ["And if I shut him up too soon--even though I might be convinced _he_ was the man, I should very likely be depriving myself of the means of getting further evidence against him.", "그런데 만일 내가 그를 너무 일찍 가둬 버리면 — 비록 내가 그자가 범인이라고 확신한다 해도 — 십중팔구 나는 그에게 불리한 증거를 더 얻을 수단을 스스로 박탈하게 되는 거라네."], ["And how?", "어째서냐고?"], ["By giving him, so to speak, a definite position, I shall put him out of suspense and set his mind at rest, so that he will retreat into his shell.", "그에게, 말하자면, 확정된 처지를 줌으로써, 나는 그를 불안에서 벗어나게 하고 마음을 놓이게 하여, 결국 그가 자기 껍데기 속으로 움츠러들게 만들기 때문이지요."], ["They say that at Sevastopol, soon after Alma, the clever people were in a terrible fright that the enemy would attack openly and take Sevastopol at once.", "듣자 하니 세바스토폴에서, 알마 전투 직후에, 영리한 자들이 적이 대놓고 공격해 와서 세바스토폴을 단숨에 함락시킬까 봐 끔찍하게 두려워했다더군."], ["But when they saw that the enemy preferred a regular siege, they were delighted, I am told and reassured, for the thing would drag on for two months at least.", "그런데 적이 정규적인 포위 공격을 택하는 걸 보고는, 안도하고 기뻐했다는 거예요, 적어도 두 달은 질질 끌 테니까 말이지요."], ["You’re laughing, you don’t believe me again?", "자네 웃는군, 또 나를 안 믿는 거야?"], ["Of course, you’re right, too.", "물론 자네 말도 옳아요."], ["You’re right, you’re right.", "자네가 옳아, 옳다네."], ["These are special cases, I admit.", "이건 특수한 경우들이지, 인정하네."], ["But you must observe this, my dear Rodion Romanovitch, the general case, the case for which all legal forms and rules are intended, for which they are calculated and laid down in books, does not exist at all, for the reason that every case, every crime, for instance, so soon as it actually occurs, at once becomes a thoroughly special case and sometimes a case unlike any that’s gone before.", "하지만 자네는 이 점을 눈여겨봐야 해요, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치, 모든 법적 형식과 규칙이 겨냥하는 일반적인 경우, 그것들이 그것을 위해 계산되고 책에 명시되어 있는 그런 일반적인 경우란 애당초 존재하지 않는다네. 왜냐하면 모든 사건은, 가령 모든 범죄는, 실제로 일어나는 순간 곧장 철저히 특수한 사건이 되어 버리고, 때로는 그 이전의 어떤 것과도 닮지 않은 사건이 되기 때문이지요."], ["Very comic cases of that sort sometimes occur.", "그런 종류의 무척 우스꽝스러운 사건들이 가끔 일어난다오."], ["If I leave one man quite alone, if I don’t touch him and don’t worry him, but let him know or at least suspect every moment that I know all about it and am watching him day and night, and if he is in continual suspicion and terror, he’ll be bound to lose his head.", "만일 내가 어떤 사람을 완전히 가만히 내버려 둔다면, 그를 건드리지도 들볶지도 않으면서, 다만 내가 모든 것을 알고 있으며 밤낮으로 그를 지켜보고 있다는 것을 매 순간 알게 하거나 적어도 의심하게 만든다면, 그래서 그가 끊임없는 의심과 공포 속에 있게 한다면, 그는 반드시 제정신을 잃게 될 거예요."], ["He’ll come of himself, or maybe do something which will make it as plain as twice two are four--it’s delightful.", "그는 제 발로 찾아오거나, 어쩌면 둘에 둘을 더하면 넷이 되는 것만큼이나 명백한 무언가를 저지를 걸세 — 정말 즐거운 일이지요."], ["It may be so with a simple peasant, but with one of our sort, an intelligent man cultivated on a certain side, it’s a dead certainty.", "단순한 농사꾼이라면 그럴 수도 있지만, 우리 같은 부류, 어떤 면에서 교양을 갖춘 지식인이라면 그건 확실하다네."], ["For, my dear fellow, it’s a very important matter to know on what side a man is cultivated.", "왜냐하면, 친구, 사람이 어느 면에서 교양을 쌓았는지를 아는 건 매우 중요한 일이거든요."], ["And then there are nerves, there are nerves, you have overlooked them!", "그리고 거기엔 또 신경이라는 게 있어요, 신경이 있단 말이야, 자네는 그걸 간과했어!"], ["Why, they are all sick, nervous and irritable!...", "아니, 그들은 죄다 병들고, 신경질적이고, 짜증을 내거든!……"], ["And then how they all suffer from spleen!", "그리고 또 그들은 다들 얼마나 비위가 상해 있는지!"], ["That I assure you is a regular gold-mine for us.", "그건, 정말이지, 우리에겐 그야말로 금광이라네."], ["And it’s no anxiety to me, his running about the town free!", "그러니 그자가 자유로이 시내를 돌아다닌다 해도 내겐 아무런 걱정거리가 아니야!"], ["Let him, let him walk about for a bit!", "내버려 두게, 잠깐 돌아다니라지!"], ["I know well enough that I’ve caught him and that he won’t escape me.", "나는 이미 그를 잡았고 그가 내게서 빠져나가지 못하리라는 걸 잘 알고 있으니까."], ["Where could he escape to, he-he?", "그가 어디로 도망칠 수 있겠나, 헤-헤?"], ["Abroad, perhaps?", "외국으로?"], ["A Pole will escape abroad, but not here, especially as I am watching and have taken measures.", "폴란드 놈이라면 외국으로 달아나겠지만, 여기서는 안 돼요, 더구나 내가 지켜보며 조치를 취해 놓았으니."], ["Will he escape into the depths of the country perhaps?", "어쩌면 시골 깊숙이 달아날까?"], ["But you know, peasants live there, real rude Russian peasants.", "하지만 알다시피 거기엔 농사꾼들이 살고 있어요, 진짜 거칠고 무지한 러시아 농사꾼들이."], ["A modern cultivated man would prefer prison to living with such strangers as our peasants.", "현대의 교양 있는 사람이라면 우리 농사꾼 같은 그런 낯선 자들과 함께 사느니 차라리 감옥을 택할 거라네."], ["He-he!", "헤-헤!"], ["But that’s all nonsense, and on the surface.", "하지만 그건 다 부질없는 소리고, 겉치레일 뿐이지요."], ["It’s not merely that he has nowhere to run to, he is _psychologically_ unable to escape me, he-he!", "그자가 달아날 곳이 없을 뿐만 아니라, 그는 심리적으로 내게서 빠져나갈 수가 없다네, 헤-헤!"], ["What an expression!", "이 무슨 표현인가!"], ["Through a law of nature he can’t escape me if he had anywhere to go.", "자연의 법칙에 따라, 설령 갈 곳이 있다 해도 그는 내게서 달아날 수 없어요."], ["Have you seen a butterfly round a candle?", "자네 촛불 주위를 도는 나방을 본 적 있나?"], ["That’s how he will keep circling and circling round me.", "그자도 그렇게 내 주위를 빙글빙글 맴돌 걸세."], ["Freedom will lose its attractions.", "자유가 그 매력을 잃어버릴 거야."], ["He’ll begin to brood, he’ll weave a tangle round himself, he’ll worry himself to death!", "그는 골똘히 생각에 잠기기 시작하고, 제 둘레에 뒤엉킨 그물을 짜고, 스스로를 죽도록 들볶을 거라네!"], ["What’s more, he will provide me with a mathematical proof--if I only give him long enough interval....", "게다가 그는 내게 수학적 증거를 제공할 거예요 — 내가 충분한 시간만 준다면 말이야……."], ["And he’ll keep circling round me, getting nearer and nearer and then--flop!", "그러고는 내 주위를 빙글빙글 맴돌며, 점점 더 가까이, 더 가까이 다가오다가 — 풀썩!"], ["He’ll fly straight into my mouth and I’ll swallow him, and that will be very amusing, he-he-he!", "곧장 내 입속으로 날아들 테고, 나는 그를 삼켜 버리겠지요, 그러면 그게 아주 재미있을 거예요, 헤-헤-헤!"], ["You don’t believe me?”", "자네 나를 안 믿나?\""]], [["Raskolnikov made no reply; he sat pale and motionless, still gazing with the same intensity into Porfiry’s face.", "라스콜니코프는 대답하지 않았다. 그는 창백하게 굳은 채 앉아, 여전히 똑같은 강렬함으로 포르피리의 얼굴을 응시하고 있었다."]], [["“It’s a lesson,” he thought, turning cold.", "\"이건 본보기를 보여 주는 거야.\""], ["“This is beyond the cat playing with a mouse, like yesterday.", "그는 오싹해지며 생각했다. \"이건 어제처럼 고양이가 쥐를 가지고 노는 것 이상이야."], ["He can’t be showing off his power with no motive...", "아무런 동기 없이 제 힘을 과시할 리가 없어……"], ["prompting me; he is far too clever for that...", "나를 부추기는 거지, 그러기엔 저자는 너무 영리해……"], ["he must have another object.", "분명 다른 목적이 있어."], ["What is it?", "그게 뭘까?"], ["It’s all nonsense, my friend, you are pretending, to scare me!", "다 부질없는 짓이야, 이 친구야, 너는 나를 겁주려고 연기하는 거지!"], ["You’ve no proofs and the man I saw had no real existence.", "너에겐 아무 증거도 없고, 내가 본 그 사내는 실제로 존재하지도 않았어."], ["You simply want to make me lose my head, to work me up beforehand and so to crush me.", "너는 그저 내가 제정신을 잃게 하고, 미리 나를 들쑤셔 놓아서 짓뭉개려는 거야."], ["But you are wrong, you won’t do it!", "하지만 너는 틀렸어, 그렇게는 안 될 거다!"], ["But why give me such a hint?", "한데 어째서 내게 이런 암시를 주는 거지?"], ["Is he reckoning on my shattered nerves?", "내 망가진 신경을 노리고 있는 건가?"], ["No, my friend, you are wrong, you won’t do it even though you have some trap for me...", "아니야, 이 친구야, 너는 틀렸어, 설령 나를 위한 함정을 마련해 두었다 해도 그렇게는 안 될 거다……"], ["let us see what you have in store for me.”", "어디 네가 나를 위해 무엇을 준비해 두었는지 보자.\""]], [["And he braced himself to face a terrible and unknown ordeal.", "그리고 그는 끔찍하고 알 수 없는 시련에 맞설 각오를 다졌다."], ["At times he longed to fall on Porfiry and strangle him.", "때때로 그는 포르피리에게 달려들어 목을 졸라 버리고 싶었다."], ["This anger was what he dreaded from the beginning.", "이 분노야말로 그가 처음부터 두려워했던 것이었다."], ["He felt that his parched lips were flecked with foam, his heart was throbbing.", "그는 자신의 바싹 마른 입술에 거품이 일고, 심장이 두근거리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["But he was still determined not to speak till the right moment.", "그러나 그는 여전히 적절한 순간이 오기 전까지는 입을 열지 않으리라 다짐했다."], ["He realised that this was the best policy in his position, because instead of saying too much he would be irritating his enemy by his silence and provoking him into speaking too freely.", "그는 이것이 자신의 처지에서 최선의 방책임을 깨달았으니, 너무 많은 말을 하는 대신 침묵으로 적을 짜증나게 하여 그가 함부로 떠벌리도록 부추길 수 있을 터였다."], ["Anyhow, this was what he hoped for.", "어쨌든 이것이 그가 바라는 바였다."]], [["“No, I see you don’t believe me, you think I am playing a harmless joke on you,”", "\"아니, 보아하니 자네는 나를 안 믿는군, 내가 자네에게 악의 없는 농담이나 하고 있다고 생각하나 보군.\""], ["Porfiry began again, getting more and more lively, chuckling at every instant and again pacing round the room.", "포르피리는 점점 더 활기를 띠며, 매 순간 낄낄거리고 다시 방 안을 거닐면서 다시 입을 열었다. \"그래, 정말이지 자네 말이 옳아요."], ["“And to be sure you’re right: God has given me a figure that can awaken none but comic ideas in other people; a buffoon; but let me tell you, and I repeat it, excuse an old man, my dear Rodion Romanovitch, you are a man still young, so to say, in your first youth and so you put intellect above everything, like all young people.", "하느님은 내게 남들 눈엔 우스꽝스러운 생각밖에 일으키지 못하는 외모를 주셨지요, 어릿광대 말이오. 하지만 한마디 하겠네, 거듭 말하지만, 늙은이를 용서해 주게, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치, 자네는 아직 젊은 사람이야, 말하자면, 청춘의 한복판에 있지요, 그래서 모든 젊은이가 그렇듯 지성을 무엇보다 위에 두지요."], ["Playful wit and abstract arguments fascinate you and that’s for all the world like the old Austrian _Hof-kriegsrath_, as far as I can judge of military matters, that is: on paper they’d beaten Napoleon and taken him prisoner, and there in their study they worked it all out in the cleverest fashion, but look you, General Mack surrendered with all his army, he-he-he!", "장난스러운 재치와 추상적인 논변이 자네를 매혹하는데, 그건 영락없이 저 옛 오스트리아의 호프크리크스라트(Hof-kriegsrath) 같은 거라네, 내가 군사 문제를 좀 안다고 판단하건대 말이야. 그들은 종이 위에서는 나폴레옹을 무찌르고 포로로 잡았지요, 자기네 집무실에서 그 모든 걸 더없이 영리하게 짜냈단 말이야."], ["I see, I see, Rodion Romanovitch, you are laughing at a civilian like me, taking examples out of military history!", "그런데 보라고, 마크 장군은 전군을 이끌고 항복하고 말았다네, 헤-헤-헤! 알아요, 알아, 로디온 로마노비치, 자네는 나 같은 민간인이 군사(軍史)에서 예를 끌어다 쓰는 걸 비웃고 있겠지!"], ["But I can’t help it, it’s my weakness.", "하지만 어쩔 수 없어요, 그게 내 약점이거든."], ["I am fond of military science.", "나는 군사학을 좋아한다네."], ["And I’m ever so fond of reading all military histories.", "그리고 온갖 전사(戰史)를 읽는 걸 그렇게나 좋아해요."], ["I’ve certainly missed my proper career.", "나는 확실히 제 길을 놓쳤지요."], ["I ought to have been in the army, upon my word I ought.", "나는 군대에 갔어야 했어요, 정말이지 그랬어야 했지."], ["I shouldn’t have been a Napoleon, but I might have been a major, he-he!", "나폴레옹은 못 되었겠지만, 소령쯤은 되었을지도 몰라, 헤-헤!"], ["Well, I’ll tell you the whole truth, my dear fellow, about this _special case_, I mean: actual fact and a man’s temperament, my dear sir, are weighty matters and it’s astonishing how they sometimes deceive the sharpest calculation!", "자, 이제 자네에게 모든 진실을 말해 주겠네, 친구, 이 특수한 경우에 관해서 말이야. 즉, 실제 사실과 인간의 기질은, 친애하는 선생, 무게 있는 문제라서, 때때로 그것들이 어찌나 가장 예리한 계산마저 속여 넘기는지 놀라울 따름이라네!"], ["I--listen to an old man--am speaking seriously, Rodion Romanovitch”", "나는 — 늙은이의 말을 들어 보게 — 진지하게 말하고 있어요, 로디온 로마노비치.\""], ["(as he said this Porfiry Petrovitch, who was scarcely five-and-thirty, actually seemed to have grown old; even his voice changed and he seemed to shrink together) “Moreover, I’m a candid man...", "(이렇게 말하면서 겨우 서른다섯 남짓밖에 안 된 포르피리 페트로비치는 실제로 늙어 버린 것처럼 보였다. 목소리마저 달라지고, 몸도 쪼그라드는 듯했다.) \"게다가 나는 솔직한 사람이라네……"], ["am I a candid man or not?", "내가 솔직한 사람인가 아닌가?"], ["What do you say?", "자네 생각은 어떤가?"], ["I fancy I really am: I tell you these things for nothing and don’t even expect a reward for it, he-he!", "정말로 그런 것 같은데. 나는 이런 걸 거저 말해 주고 있고 그에 대한 보답조차 기대하지 않으니까, 헤-헤!"], ["Well, to proceed, wit in my opinion is a splendid thing, it is, so to say, an adornment of nature and a consolation of life, and what tricks it can play!", "자, 계속하자면, 재치란 내 생각엔 멋진 거예요, 그건, 말하자면, 자연의 장식이요 삶의 위안이지요, 그리고 그게 얼마나 별별 술수를 부릴 수 있는지!"], ["So that it sometimes is hard for a poor examining lawyer to know where he is, especially when he’s liable to be carried away by his own fancy, too, for you know he is a man after all!", "그래서 때로는 가련한 예심판사가 자기가 어디에 있는지조차 알기 어려울 지경이라네, 특히 그 자신도 제 공상에 휩쓸리기 쉬울 때는 더더욱 그렇지요, 왜냐하면 아시다시피 그도 결국 인간이니까!"], ["But the poor fellow is saved by the criminal’s temperament, worse luck for him!", "하지만 그 가련한 친구를 구해 주는 건 죄인의 기질이라네, 죄인으로서는 운 나쁘게도 말이야!"], ["But young people carried away by their own wit don’t think of that ‘when they overstep all obstacles,’ as you wittily and cleverly expressed it yesterday.", "한데 자기 재치에 휩쓸린 젊은이들은 그걸 생각하지 못하지요, '모든 장애물을 뛰어넘을 때'에는 말이야, 어제 자네가 재치 있고 영리하게 표현한 대로."], ["He will lie--that is, the man who is a _special case_, the incognito, and he will lie well, in the cleverest fashion; you might think he would triumph and enjoy the fruits of his wit, but at the most interesting, the most flagrant moment he will faint.", "그는 거짓말을 할 거예요 — 즉, 특수한 경우인 그 사람, 그 익명의 사내는 거짓말을 할 거고, 게다가 잘할 거예요, 더없이 영리하게 말이지요. 자네는 그가 의기양양하게 제 재치의 열매를 만끽하리라 여기겠지만, 가장 흥미로운, 가장 결정적인 순간에 그는 기절해 버린다네."], ["Of course there may be illness and a stuffy room as well, but anyway!", "물론 병 때문일 수도 있고 답답한 방 탓일 수도 있지요, 하지만 아무튼!"], ["Anyway he’s given us the idea!", "아무튼 그는 우리에게 단서를 준 거예요!"], ["He lied incomparably, but he didn’t reckon on his temperament.", "그는 비할 데 없이 거짓말을 했지만, 자기 기질은 계산에 넣지 못했지요."], ["That’s what betrays him!", "바로 그게 그를 드러내는 거라네!"], ["Another time he will be carried away by his playful wit into making fun of the man who suspects him, he will turn pale as it were on purpose to mislead, but his paleness will be _too natural_, too much like the real thing, again he has given us an idea!", "또 어떤 때는 자기 장난스러운 재치에 휩쓸려, 자기를 의심하는 사람을 비웃기까지 한다네, 일부러 오도하려는 듯 창백해지기도 하지만, 그 창백함이 너무 자연스럽거든요, 너무 진짜 같단 말이야, 또다시 그는 우리에게 단서를 준 거예요!"], ["Though his questioner may be deceived at first, he will think differently next day if he is not a fool, and, of course, it is like that at every step!", "비록 그를 신문하는 자가 처음엔 속을지 몰라도, 바보가 아닌 이상 이튿날엔 달리 생각하겠지요, 그리고 물론, 한 걸음 한 걸음마다 그런 식이라네!"], ["He puts himself forward where he is not wanted, speaks continually when he ought to keep silent, brings in all sorts of allegorical allusions, he-he!", "그자는 필요하지도 않은 데서 나서고, 입을 다물어야 할 때 끊임없이 지껄이고, 온갖 우의적인 암시를 끌어들이지요, 헤-헤!"], ["Comes and asks why didn’t you take me long ago? he-he-he!", "찾아와서는 왜 진작 나를 잡아가지 않았느냐고 묻는다네, 헤-헤-헤!"], ["And that can happen, you know, with the cleverest man, the psychologist, the literary man.", "그리고 그런 일이, 아시다시피, 가장 영리한 사람, 심리학자, 문필가에게도 일어날 수 있어요."], ["The temperament reflects everything like a mirror!", "기질이란 거울처럼 모든 걸 비추거든!"], ["Gaze into it and admire what you see!", "들여다보고 거기 비친 것에 감탄하라지!"], ["But why are you so pale, Rodion Romanovitch?", "한데 어째서 자네는 그렇게 창백한가, 로디온 로마노비치?"], ["Is the room stuffy?", "방이 답답한가?"], ["Shall I open the window?”", "창문을 좀 열어 줄까요?\""]], [["“Oh, don’t trouble, please,”", "\"아, 제발 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\""], ["cried Raskolnikov and he suddenly broke into a laugh.", "라스콜니코프가 외치더니 갑자기 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Please don’t trouble.”", "\"제발 신경 쓰지 마세요.\""]], [["Porfiry stood facing him, paused a moment and suddenly he too laughed.", "포르피리는 그와 마주 서서 잠시 멈췄다가, 갑자기 그도 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Raskolnikov got up from the sofa, abruptly checking his hysterical laughter.", "라스콜니코프는 자신의 발작적인 웃음을 불쑥 억누르며 소파에서 일어났다."]], [["“Porfiry Petrovitch,”", "\"포르피리 페트로비치.\""], ["he began, speaking loudly and distinctly, though his legs trembled and he could scarcely stand.", "그는 다리가 후들거려 거의 서 있기조차 힘들었으나, 크고 또렷한 목소리로 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“I see clearly at last that you actually suspect me of murdering that old woman and her sister Lizaveta.", "\"이제 마침내 똑똑히 알겠습니다, 당신은 실제로 제가 그 노파와 그 동생 리자베타를 살해했다고 의심하고 있다는 걸요."], ["Let me tell you for my part that I am sick of this.", "제 입장에서 말씀드리겠는데, 저는 이게 진절머리가 납니다."], ["If you find that you have a right to prosecute me legally, to arrest me, then prosecute me, arrest me.", "만일 당신이 저를 법적으로 기소하고 체포할 권리가 있다고 보신다면, 그렇다면 저를 기소하고 체포하십시오."], ["But I will not let myself be jeered at to my face and worried...”", "하지만 저는 면전에서 비웃음을 당하고 들볶이는 건 용납하지 않겠습니다…….\""]], [["His lips trembled, his eyes glowed with fury and he could not restrain his voice.", "그의 입술이 떨렸고, 두 눈은 분노로 이글거렸으며, 그는 목소리를 억누를 수가 없었다."]], [["“I won’t allow it!”", "\"용납하지 않겠습니다!\""], ["he shouted, bringing his fist down on the table.", "그는 주먹으로 책상을 내리치며 소리쳤다."], ["“Do you hear that, Porfiry Petrovitch?", "\"들리십니까, 포르피리 페트로비치?"], ["I won’t allow it.”", "용납하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["What does it mean?”", "이게 무슨 뜻인가?\""], ["cried Porfiry Petrovitch, apparently quite frightened.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 외쳤는데, 분명 잔뜩 겁먹은 듯했다."], ["“Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow, what is the matter with you?”", "\"로디온 로마노비치, 친구, 자네 도대체 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["“I won’t allow it,”", "\"용납하지 않겠습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov shouted again.", "라스콜니코프가 다시 소리쳤다."]], [["“Hush, my dear man!", "\"쉿, 이보게!"], ["They’ll hear and come in.", "저들이 듣고 들어올 걸세."], ["Just think, what could we say to them?”", "생각해 보게, 우리가 저들에게 뭐라고 하겠나?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch whispered in horror, bringing his face close to Raskolnikov’s.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 라스콜니코프의 얼굴에 자기 얼굴을 바짝 들이대며 겁에 질려 속삭였다."]], [["“I won’t allow it, I won’t allow it,”", "\"용납하지 않겠습니다, 용납하지 않겠어요.\""], ["Raskolnikov repeated mechanically, but he too spoke in a sudden whisper.", "라스콜니코프는 기계적으로 되풀이했으나, 그 역시 갑자기 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["Porfiry turned quickly and ran to open the window.", "포르피리는 재빨리 돌아서서 창문을 열러 달려갔다."]], [["“Some fresh air!", "\"바깥 공기를 좀!"], ["And you must have some water, my dear fellow.", "그리고 자네 물을 좀 마셔야겠네, 친구."], ["You’re ill!”", "자네 아프잖나!\""], ["and he was running to the door to call for some when he found a decanter of water in the corner.", "그는 물을 가져오라고 부르려 문 쪽으로 달려가다가, 구석에서 물병을 발견했다."], ["“Come, drink a little,”", "\"자, 좀 마시게.\""], ["he whispered, rushing up to him with the decanter.", "그는 물병을 들고 그에게 달려와 속삭였다."], ["“It will be sure to do you good.”", "\"분명 도움이 될 걸세.\""]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch’s alarm and sympathy were so natural that Raskolnikov was silent and began looking at him with wild curiosity.", "포르피리 페트로비치의 놀람과 동정이 어찌나 자연스러웠던지, 라스콜니코프는 입을 다물고 거친 호기심으로 그를 바라보기 시작했다."], ["He did not take the water, however.", "그러나 물은 받지 않았다."]], [["“Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow, you’ll drive yourself out of your mind, I assure you, ach, ach!", "\"로디온 로마노비치, 친구, 자네 그러다 제정신이 아니게 되겠어, 정말이지, 아흐, 아흐!"], ["Have some water, do drink a little.”", "물 좀 마시게, 좀 마셔요.\""]], [["He forced him to take the glass.", "그는 억지로 그에게 잔을 쥐여 주었다."], ["Raskolnikov raised it mechanically to his lips, but set it on the table again with disgust.", "라스콜니코프는 기계적으로 그것을 입가로 가져갔다가, 혐오감에 다시 책상 위에 내려놓았다."]], [["“Yes, you’ve had a little attack!", "\"그래, 자네 가벼운 발작을 일으켰군!"], ["You’ll bring back your illness again, my dear fellow,”", "자네 또 병이 도지겠어, 친구.\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch cackled with friendly sympathy, though he still looked rather disconcerted.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 여전히 다소 당황한 기색이긴 했으나, 다정한 동정으로 깔깔거렸다."], ["“Good heavens, you must take more care of yourself!", "\"맙소사, 자네 몸을 좀 더 아껴야 해!"], ["Dmitri Prokofitch was here, came to see me yesterday--I know, I know, I’ve a nasty, ironical temper, but what they made of it!...", "어제 드미트리 프로코피치가 여기 왔었네, 나를 보러 왔지 — 알아요, 알아, 내가 짓궂고 빈정대는 성미를 지녔다는 거, 하지만 그자들이 그걸로 무슨 짓을 했는지!……"], ["Good heavens, he came yesterday after you’d been.", "맙소사, 그가 자네가 다녀간 뒤에 어제 왔어요."], ["We dined and he talked and talked away, and I could only throw up my hands in despair!", "우린 식사를 했고 그는 끊임없이 떠들어 댔지, 나는 그저 절망하며 두 손 들 수밖에 없었다네!"], ["Did he come from you?", "그가 자네가 보내서 온 건가?"], ["But do sit down, for mercy’s sake, sit down!”", "한데 제발 앉게, 부디 앉아 주게!\""]], [["“No, not from me, but I knew he went to you and why he went,”", "\"아니요, 제가 보낸 게 아닙니다, 하지만 그가 당신에게 갔다는 것도, 왜 갔는지도 알고 있었습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered sharply. “You knew?”", "라스콜니코프가 날카롭게 대답했다."]], [["“I knew. What of it?”", "\"알고 있었다고?\""]], [["“Why this, Rodion Romanovitch, that I know more than that about you; I know about everything.", "\"알고 있었습니다. 그게 어쨌다는 겁니까?\""], ["I know how you went _to take a flat_ at night when it was dark and how you rang the bell and asked about the blood, so that the workmen and the porter did not know what to make of it.", "\"왜냐하면 이렇다네, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 자네에 관해 그것보다 더 많은 걸 알고 있어요. 나는 모든 걸 알고 있다네."], ["Yes, I understand your state of mind at that time...", "자네가 한밤중에, 어두울 때 셋집을 빌리러 가서 어떻게 초인종을 울리고 피에 대해 물었는지, 그래서 인부들과 문지기가 그걸 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 몰라 했는지 다 알고 있어요."], ["but you’ll drive yourself mad like that, upon my word!", "그래, 나는 그때 자네의 마음 상태를 이해해요…… 하지만 그러다간 자네 정말 미쳐 버릴 걸세, 정말이지!"], ["You’ll lose your head!", "제정신을 잃을 거야!"], ["You’re full of generous indignation at the wrongs you’ve received, first from destiny, and then from the police officers, and so you rush from one thing to another to force them to speak out and make an end of it all, because you are sick of all this suspicion and foolishness.", "자네는 자네가 받은 부당한 일들에, 처음엔 운명에게서, 그다음엔 경찰관들에게서 받은 그것들에 고결한 분노로 가득 차서, 이 일 저 일로 마구 내달리며 저들이 분명히 말하게 만들어 이 모든 걸 끝장내려 하는 거지요, 이 모든 의심과 어리석은 짓에 진절머리가 났으니까."], ["That’s so, isn’t it?", "그렇지 않은가?"], ["I have guessed how you feel, haven’t I?", "내가 자네 심정을 알아맞혔지, 안 그래요?"], ["Only in that way you’ll lose your head and Razumihin’s, too; he’s too _good_ a man for such a position, you must know that.", "다만 그런 식으로는 자네가 제정신을 잃고, 라주미힌의 정신까지 잃게 할 거예요. 그는 그런 처지에 놓이기엔 너무 좋은 사람이거든, 자네도 알아야 해."], ["You are ill and he is good and your illness is infectious for him...", "자네는 병들었고 그는 좋은 사람인데, 자네 병이 그에게 옮는 거라네……."], ["I’ll tell you about it when you are more yourself....", "자네가 좀 진정되면 그 얘기를 해 주지요……."], ["But do sit down, for goodness’ sake.", "한데 제발 앉게나."], ["Please rest, you look shocking, do sit down.”", "부디 좀 쉬게, 자네 안색이 끔찍해, 어서 앉아요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov sat down; he no longer shivered, he was hot all over.", "라스콜니코프는 앉았다. 그는 더는 떨지 않았으나, 온몸이 뜨거웠다."], ["In amazement he listened with strained attention to Porfiry Petrovitch who still seemed frightened as he looked after him with friendly solicitude.", "그는 놀라움 속에서, 여전히 겁먹은 듯 다정한 염려로 자기를 살피는 포르피리 페트로비치의 말에 긴장된 주의를 기울이며 귀를 기울였다."], ["But he did not believe a word he said, though he felt a strange inclination to believe.", "그러나 그는 그의 말을 한마디도 믿지 않았으니, 비록 묘하게도 믿고 싶은 마음이 들기는 했지만."], ["Porfiry’s unexpected words about the flat had utterly overwhelmed him.", "셋집에 관한 포르피리의 예기치 못한 말이 그를 완전히 압도해 버렸다."], ["“How can it be, he knows about the flat then,”", "\"어떻게 이럴 수가, 그러니까 저자가 셋집 일을 알고 있다니,\""], ["he thought suddenly, “and he tells it me himself!”", "그는 문득 생각했다. \"게다가 그걸 제 입으로 내게 말하다니!\""]], [["“Yes, in our legal practice there was a case almost exactly similar, a case of morbid psychology,”", "\"그래, 우리 법률 실무에서도 거의 똑같은 사건이 있었지요, 병적인 심리의 사건 말이야.\""], ["Porfiry went on quickly.", "포르피리가 재빨리 말을 이었다."], ["“A man confessed to murder and how he kept it up!", "\"한 사내가 살인을 자백했는데, 어찌나 끝까지 우겨 대던지!"], ["It was a regular hallucination; he brought forward facts, he imposed upon everyone and why?", "완전한 환각이었어요. 그는 사실들을 들이밀고, 모두를 속였지요, 그런데 왜?"], ["He had been partly, but only partly, unintentionally the cause of a murder and when he knew that he had given the murderers the opportunity, he sank into dejection, it got on his mind and turned his brain, he began imagining things and he persuaded himself that he was the murderer.", "그는 부분적으로, 하지만 어디까지나 부분적으로, 본의 아니게 한 살인의 원인이 되었던 거고, 자기가 살인자들에게 기회를 주었다는 걸 알게 되자, 의기소침에 빠져, 그게 마음을 짓누르고 머리를 돌게 해서, 그는 이런저런 것들을 상상하기 시작했고, 자기가 살인자라고 스스로 믿어 버린 거예요."], ["But at last the High Court of Appeal went into it and the poor fellow was acquitted and put under proper care.", "하지만 결국 고등 항소 법원이 이 일을 검토했고, 그 가련한 친구는 무죄를 선고받아 적절한 보호를 받게 되었지요."], ["Thanks to the Court of Appeal!", "항소 법원 덕분이지!"], ["Tut-tut-tut!", "쯧-쯧-쯧!"], ["Why, my dear fellow, you may drive yourself into delirium if you have the impulse to work upon your nerves, to go ringing bells at night and asking about blood!", "아니, 친구, 자네도 자기 신경을 들쑤시고, 한밤중에 초인종을 울리며 피에 대해 묻고 싶은 충동에 빠지면 스스로를 섬망 상태로 몰아넣을 수 있어요!"], ["I’ve studied all this morbid psychology in my practice.", "나는 실무에서 이 모든 병적인 심리를 연구했다네."], ["A man is sometimes tempted to jump out of a window or from a belfry.", "사람은 때로 창문이나 종탑에서 뛰어내리고 싶은 유혹에 빠지지요."], ["Just the same with bell-ringing....", "초인종을 울리는 것도 꼭 마찬가지라네……."], ["It’s all illness, Rodion Romanovitch!", "그건 다 병이에요, 로디온 로마노비치!"], ["You have begun to neglect your illness.", "자네는 자기 병을 방치하기 시작했어요."], ["You should consult an experienced doctor, what’s the good of that fat fellow?", "노련한 의사에게 진찰을 받아야 해, 그 뚱뚱한 친구가 무슨 소용이 있겠나?"], ["You are lightheaded!", "자네 머리가 어떻게 된 거예요!"], ["You were delirious when you did all this!”", "그 모든 짓을 할 때 자네는 섬망 상태였다고!\""]], [["For a moment Raskolnikov felt everything going round.", "한순간 라스콜니코프는 모든 것이 빙글빙글 도는 듯했다."]], [["“Is it possible, is it possible,”", "\"있을 수 있는 일인가, 있을 수 있는 일인가,\""], ["flashed through his mind, “that he is still lying?", "하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. \"저자가 아직도 거짓말을 하고 있다는 게?"], ["He can’t be, he can’t be.”", "그럴 리 없어, 그럴 리 없어.\""], ["He rejected that idea, feeling to what a degree of fury it might drive him, feeling that that fury might drive him mad.", "그는 그 생각이 자신을 어느 정도까지 분노로 몰아갈 수 있는지를, 그 분노가 자신을 미치게 할 수 있음을 느끼며, 그 생각을 떨쳐 버렸다."]], [["“I was not delirious.", "\"저는 섬망 상태가 아니었습니다."], ["I knew what I was doing,”", "제가 무엇을 하는지 알고 있었어요.\""], ["he cried, straining every faculty to penetrate Porfiry’s game, “I was quite myself, do you hear?”", "그는 포르피리의 속셈을 꿰뚫어 보려고 온 능력을 짜내며 외쳤다. \"저는 완전히 제정신이었습니다, 들리십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, I hear and understand.", "\"그래요, 듣고 있고 이해하고 있어요."], ["You said yesterday you were not delirious, you were particularly emphatic about it!", "어제도 자네는 섬망 상태가 아니었다고 했지요, 특히 그 점을 힘주어 강조했어요!"], ["I understand all you can tell me!", "자네가 내게 말할 수 있는 건 다 이해한다네!"], ["A-ach!...", "아-흐!……"], ["Listen, Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow.", "들어 보게, 로디온 로마노비치, 친구."], ["If you were actually a criminal, or were somehow mixed up in this damnable business, would you insist that you were not delirious but in full possession of your faculties?", "만일 자네가 실제로 죄인이거나, 어떻게든 이 빌어먹을 사건에 연루되었다면, 자네가 섬망 상태가 아니라 온전히 제정신이었다고 우기겠나?"], ["And so emphatically and persistently?", "그것도 그토록 힘주어 끈질기게?"], ["Would it be possible?", "그게 가능한 일이겠나?"], ["Quite impossible, to my thinking.", "내 생각엔 도무지 불가능해요."], ["If you had anything on your conscience, you certainly ought to insist that you were delirious.", "만일 자네 양심에 걸리는 게 있다면, 자네는 분명 섬망 상태였다고 우겨야 마땅하지요."], ["That’s so, isn’t it?”", "그렇지 않은가?\""]], [["There was a note of slyness in this inquiry.", "이 물음에는 교활한 가락이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov drew back on the sofa as Porfiry bent over him and stared in silent perplexity at him.", "포르피리가 그에게 몸을 숙이자 라스콜니코프는 소파에서 물러나며, 말없이 어리둥절한 채 그를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["“Another thing about Razumihin--you certainly ought to have said that he came of his own accord, to have concealed your part in it!", "\"라주미힌에 관해서도 또 한 가지 — 자네는 분명 그가 제 발로 왔다고 말해서, 거기에 자네가 한 몫을 숨겼어야 마땅해요!"], ["But you don’t conceal it!", "그런데 자네는 숨기지 않아!"], ["You lay stress on his coming at your instigation.”", "자네는 그가 자네가 부추겨서 왔다는 걸 강조한다니까.\""]], [["Raskolnikov had not done so.", "라스콜니코프는 그런 적이 없었다."], ["A chill went down his back.", "등줄기가 오싹해졌다."]], [["“You keep telling lies,”", "\"당신은 계속 거짓말을 하는군요.\""], ["he said slowly and weakly, twisting his lips into a sickly smile, “you are trying again to show that you know all my game, that you know all I shall say beforehand,”", "그는 입술을 병약한 미소로 일그러뜨리며 천천히, 힘없이 말했다. \"당신은 또다시 내 속셈을 다 안다는 걸, 내가 무슨 말을 할지 미리 다 안다는 걸 보여 주려는 거예요.\""], ["he said, conscious himself that he was not weighing his words as he ought.", "그는 자기가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 말을 가려 하지 못하고 있음을 스스로 의식하며 말했다."], ["“You want to frighten me...", "\"당신은 나를 겁주려는 거예요……"], ["or you are simply laughing at me...”", "아니면 그저 나를 비웃고 있거나…….\""]], [["He still stared at him as he said this and again there was a light of intense hatred in his eyes.", "이렇게 말하면서도 그는 여전히 그를 빤히 바라보았고, 그의 눈에는 또다시 강렬한 증오의 빛이 어렸다."]], [["“You keep lying,” he said.", "\"당신은 계속 거짓말을 하는군요.\""], ["“You know perfectly well that the best policy for the criminal is to tell the truth as nearly as possible...", "그가 말했다. \"당신은 죄인에게 최선의 방책이 가능한 한 진실에 가깝게 말하는 것임을……"], ["to conceal as little as possible.", "가능한 한 적게 숨기는 것임을 아주 잘 알고 있어요."], ["I don’t believe you!”", "나는 당신을 믿지 않습니다!\""]], [["“What a wily person you are!”", "\"자네 참 교활한 사람이군!\" 포르피리가 킥킥거렸다."], ["Porfiry tittered, “there’s no catching you; you’ve a perfect monomania.", "\"자네는 잡을 수가 없어요. 자네는 완벽한 편집광이야."], ["So you don’t believe me?", "그래서 나를 안 믿는다고?"], ["But still you do believe me, you believe a quarter; I’ll soon make you believe the whole, because I have a sincere liking for you and genuinely wish you good.”", "하지만 그래도 자네는 나를 믿고 있어요, 사분의 일은 믿지요. 곧 자네가 전부를 믿게 만들겠네, 왜냐하면 나는 자네를 진심으로 아끼고 진정으로 자네가 잘되기를 바라니까.\""]], [["Raskolnikov’s lips trembled.", "라스콜니코프의 입술이 떨렸다."]], [["“Yes, I do,”", "\"그래, 정말이라네.\""], ["went on Porfiry, touching Raskolnikov’s arm genially, “you must take care of your illness.", "포르피리가 라스콜니코프의 팔을 다정하게 어루만지며 말을 이었다. \"자네는 자기 병을 돌봐야 해요."], ["Besides, your mother and sister are here now; you must think of them.", "게다가 지금 자네 어머니와 누이가 여기 와 있잖나. 그들을 생각해야지요."], ["You must soothe and comfort them and you do nothing but frighten them...”", "자네는 그들을 달래고 위로해야 하는데, 자네가 하는 거라곤 그들을 겁주는 것뿐이니…….\""]], [["“What has that to do with you?", "\"그게 당신과 무슨 상관입니까?"], ["How do you know it?", "그걸 어떻게 압니까?"], ["What concern is it of yours?", "그게 당신이 알 바예요?"], ["You are keeping watch on me and want to let me know it?”", "당신은 나를 감시하면서 그걸 내게 알리고 싶은 거군요?\""]], [["“Good heavens!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Why, I learnt it all from you yourself!", "아니, 그 모든 걸 자네 입으로 직접 알려 준 거라네!"], ["You don’t notice that in your excitement you tell me and others everything.", "자네는 흥분하면 나에게도 남들에게도 모든 걸 털어놓는다는 걸 깨닫지 못하는군."], ["From Razumihin, too, I learnt a number of interesting details yesterday.", "라주미힌에게서도 어제 흥미로운 세부 사항을 잔뜩 알아냈지요."], ["No, you interrupted me, but I must tell you that, for all your wit, your suspiciousness makes you lose the common-sense view of things.", "아니, 자네가 내 말을 끊었지만, 이건 말해 두어야겠어, 그 모든 재치에도 불구하고 자네의 의심하는 버릇 탓에 자네는 사물을 보는 상식적인 눈을 잃어버린다네."], ["To return to bell-ringing, for instance.", "가령 초인종 얘기로 다시 돌아가 보지요."], ["I, an examining lawyer, have betrayed a precious thing like that, a real fact (for it is a fact worth having), and you see nothing in it!", "나는, 예심판사인 내가, 그런 귀중한 것을, 진짜 사실을 누설했는데(그건 손에 넣을 만한 가치가 있는 사실이거든), 자네는 거기서 아무것도 보지 못해요!"], ["Why, if I had the slightest suspicion of you, should I have acted like that?", "아니, 내가 자네를 조금이라도 의심했다면 그렇게 행동했겠나?"], ["No, I should first have disarmed your suspicions and not let you see I knew of that fact, should have diverted your attention and suddenly have dealt you a knock-down blow (your expression) saying: ‘And what were you doing, sir, pray, at ten or nearly eleven at the murdered woman’s flat and why did you ring the bell and why did you ask about blood?", "천만에, 나라면 먼저 자네의 의심을 무장해제시키고, 내가 그 사실을 안다는 걸 자네가 눈치채지 못하게 하고, 자네 주의를 다른 데로 돌렸다가 느닷없이 일격을(자네 표현이지) 가했겠지요, 이렇게 말하면서 말이야. '그래, 선생, 부디 말씀해 보시오, 열 시인지 거의 열한 시쯤에 살해당한 여인의 셋집에서 무얼 하고 있었으며, 왜 초인종을 울렸고, 왜 피에 대해 물었소?"], ["And why did you invite the porters to go with you to the police station, to the lieutenant?’ That’s how I ought to have acted if I had a grain of suspicion of you.", "그리고 왜 문지기들에게 함께 경찰서로, 부서장에게 가자고 청했소?' 자네를 손톱만큼이라도 의심했다면 나는 그렇게 행동했어야 마땅하지요."], ["I ought to have taken your evidence in due form, searched your lodging and perhaps have arrested you, too...", "정식 형식대로 자네의 진술을 받고, 자네 숙소를 수색하고, 어쩌면 자네를 체포까지 했어야 해요……."], ["so I have no suspicion of you, since I have not done that!", "그러니 나는 자네를 의심하지 않는 거라네, 그렇게 하지 않았으니까!"], ["But you can’t look at it normally and you see nothing, I say again.”", "한데 자네는 그걸 정상적으로 보지 못하고 아무것도 보지 못하는군, 거듭 말하지만 말이야.\""]], [["Raskolnikov started so that Porfiry Petrovitch could not fail to perceive it.", "라스콜니코프가 어찌나 흠칫했던지 포르피리 페트로비치가 그것을 알아채지 못할 리 없었다."]], [["“You are lying all the while,”", "\"당신은 줄곧 거짓말을 하는군요.\""], ["he cried, “I don’t know your object, but you are lying.", "그가 외쳤다. \"당신의 목적이 뭔지는 모르겠지만, 당신은 거짓말을 하고 있어요."], ["You did not speak like that just now and I cannot be mistaken!”", "당신은 방금 그렇게 말하지 않았어요, 내가 잘못 봤을 리 없습니다!\""]], [["“I am lying?”", "\"내가 거짓말을 한다고?\""], ["Porfiry repeated, apparently incensed, but preserving a good-humoured and ironical face, as though he were not in the least concerned at Raskolnikov’s opinion of him.", "포르피리는 분명 발끈한 듯 되풀이했으나, 라스콜니코프가 자기를 어떻게 여기든 조금도 개의치 않는 듯, 유쾌하고 빈정대는 표정을 유지했다. \"내가 거짓말을 한다고……"], ["“I am lying... but how did I treat you just now, I, the examining lawyer?", "하지만 내가 방금 자네를 어떻게 대했나, 예심판사인 내가?"], ["Prompting you and giving you every means for your defence; illness, I said, delirium, injury, melancholy and the police officers and all the rest of it?", "자네를 부추기며 변호할 온갖 수단을 다 일러 주었지요. 병이다, 섬망이다, 모욕감이다, 우울증이다, 경찰관들이다, 하고 그 모든 걸 말이야?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["He-he-he!", "헤-헤-헤!"], ["Though, indeed, all those psychological means of defence are not very reliable and cut both ways: illness, delirium, I don’t remember--that’s all right, but why, my good sir, in your illness and in your delirium were you haunted by just those delusions and not by any others?", "하긴, 정말이지 그런 심리적 변호 수단이라는 게 그리 믿을 만한 건 못 되고 양날의 칼이지요. 병, 섬망, 기억나지 않는다 — 그건 다 좋아요, 한데 어째서, 친애하는 선생, 자네는 병중에, 섬망 중에 하필 바로 그런 망상에 사로잡혔지, 다른 망상이 아니라?"], ["There may have been others, eh?", "다른 것들도 있었을 수 있잖나, 응?"], ["He-he-he!”", "헤-헤-헤!\""]], [["Raskolnikov looked haughtily and contemptuously at him.", "라스콜니코프는 거만하고 경멸하는 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Briefly,”", "\"간단히 말해서,\""], ["he said loudly and imperiously, rising to his feet and in so doing pushing Porfiry back a little, “briefly, I want to know, do you acknowledge me perfectly free from suspicion or not?", "그는 자리에서 일어나며, 그러면서 포르피리를 약간 뒤로 밀치며 크고 위압적으로 말했다. \"간단히 말해서, 저는 알고 싶습니다, 당신은 제가 의심에서 완전히 벗어났다고 인정합니까, 아닙니까?"], ["Tell me, Porfiry Petrovitch, tell me once for all and make haste!”", "말씀하십시오, 포르피리 페트로비치, 단번에 분명히 말씀하시고, 어서요!\""]], [["“What a business I’m having with you!”", "\"자네하곤 정말 못 말리겠군!\""], ["cried Porfiry with a perfectly good-humoured, sly and composed face.", "포르피리는 더없이 유쾌하고 교활하면서도 침착한 얼굴로 외쳤다."], ["“And why do you want to know, why do you want to know so much, since they haven’t begun to worry you?", "\"한데 왜 알고 싶어 하나, 왜 그렇게나 알고 싶어 해요, 아직 자네를 들볶기 시작하지도 않았는데?"], ["Why, you are like a child asking for matches!", "아니, 자네는 성냥을 달라고 조르는 어린애 같군!"], ["And why are you so uneasy?", "그리고 왜 그렇게 안절부절못하나?"], ["Why do you force yourself upon us, eh?", "왜 우리에게 자꾸 들이대는 거지요, 응?"], ["He-he-he!”", "헤-헤-헤!\""]], [["“I repeat,”", "\"다시 말하지만,\""], ["Raskolnikov cried furiously, “that I can’t put up with it!”", "라스콜니코프가 격분하여 외쳤다. \"저는 이걸 견딜 수가 없습니다!\""]], [["“With what?", "\"무엇을 말인가?"], ["Uncertainty?”", "불확실함을?\""], ["interrupted Porfiry.", "포르피리가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Don’t jeer at me!", "\"비웃지 마십시오!"], ["I won’t have it!", "그런 건 용납하지 않겠어요!"], ["I tell you I won’t have it.", "용납하지 않겠다고 말했습니다."], ["I can’t and I won’t, do you hear, do you hear?”", "견딜 수 없고 견디지 않겠어요, 들립니까, 들려요?\""], ["he shouted, bringing his fist down on the table again.", "그는 또다시 주먹으로 책상을 내리치며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["They’ll overhear!", "저들이 엿듣겠어요!"], ["I warn you seriously, take care of yourself.", "진지하게 경고하는데, 자네 몸조심하게."], ["I am not joking,”", "나는 농담하는 게 아니야.\""], ["Porfiry whispered, but this time there was not the look of old womanish good nature and alarm in his face.", "포르피리가 속삭였는데, 이번에는 그의 얼굴에 노파 같은 선량함과 놀람의 기색이 없었다."], ["Now he was peremptory, stern, frowning and for once laying aside all mystification.", "이제 그는 단호하고, 엄격하고, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채, 단 한 번 모든 눈속임을 내려놓았다."]], [["But this was only for an instant.", "그러나 그것은 한순간뿐이었다."], ["Raskolnikov, bewildered, suddenly fell into actual frenzy, but, strange to say, he again obeyed the command to speak quietly, though he was in a perfect paroxysm of fury.", "라스콜니코프는 어리둥절한 채 갑자기 진짜 광란에 빠져들었으나, 이상하게도, 완전한 분노의 발작 속에 있으면서도 그는 또다시 조용히 말하라는 명령에 복종했다."]], [["“I will not allow myself to be tortured,”", "\"저는 고문당하는 걸 가만두지 않겠습니다.\""], ["he whispered, instantly recognising with hatred that he could not help obeying the command and driven to even greater fury by the thought.", "그는 자신이 그 명령에 복종하지 않을 수 없다는 것을 증오와 함께 곧장 깨달으며, 그리고 그 생각에 더더욱 큰 분노에 휩싸이며 속삭였다."], ["“Arrest me, search me, but kindly act in due form and don’t play with me!", "\"저를 체포하시든 수색하시든 하되, 부디 정식 형식대로 하시고 저를 가지고 놀지 마십시오!"], ["Don’t dare!”", "감히 그러지 마세요!\""]], [["“Don’t worry about the form,”", "\"형식 걱정은 말게.\""], ["Porfiry interrupted with the same sly smile, as it were, gloating with enjoyment over Raskolnikov.", "포르피리가 똑같이 교활한 미소를 띠며, 마치 라스콜니코프를 즐거이 음미하듯 끼어들었다."], ["“I invited you to see me quite in a friendly way.”", "\"나는 자네를 아주 친근하게 보러 오라고 청했을 뿐이야.\""]], [["“I don’t want your friendship and I spit on it!", "\"당신의 친근함 따위 필요 없고, 침이나 뱉어 주겠습니다!"], ["Do you hear?", "들립니까?"], ["And, here, I take my cap and go.", "자, 여기, 저는 모자를 들고 가겠습니다."], ["What will you say now if you mean to arrest me?”", "저를 체포할 작정이라면 이제 뭐라고 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["He took up his cap and went to the door.", "그는 모자를 집어 들고 문 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["“And won’t you see my little surprise?”", "\"내 작은 깜짝 선물은 안 보고 가려나?\""], ["chuckled Porfiry, again taking him by the arm and stopping him at the door.", "포르피리가 또다시 그의 팔을 붙잡아 문 앞에서 그를 멈춰 세우며 낄낄거렸다."]], [["He seemed to become more playful and good-humoured, which maddened Raskolnikov.", "그는 점점 더 장난스럽고 유쾌해지는 듯했는데, 그것이 라스콜니코프를 미치게 했다."]], [["“What surprise?”", "\"무슨 깜짝 선물 말입니까?\""], ["he asked, standing still and looking at Porfiry in alarm.", "그가 우뚝 멈춰 서서 겁먹은 듯 포르피리를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“My little surprise, it’s sitting there behind the door, he-he-he!”", "\"내 작은 깜짝 선물, 저 문 뒤에 앉아 있다네, 헤-헤-헤!\""], ["(He pointed to the locked door.) “I locked him in that he should not escape.”", "(그는 잠긴 문을 가리켰다.) \"도망치지 못하게 그를 가둬 두었지요.\""]], [["“What is it?", "\"그게 뭡니까?"], ["Where?", "어디요?"], ["What?...”", "뭐라고요?……\""]], [["Raskolnikov walked to the door and would have opened it, but it was locked.", "라스콜니코프는 문 쪽으로 걸어가 열려고 했으나, 문은 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“It’s locked, here is the key!”", "\"잠겨 있지요, 열쇠는 여기 있네!\""]], [["And he brought a key out of his pocket.", "그러고는 그가 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼냈다."]], [["“You are lying,”", "\"당신은 거짓말을 하고 있어요.\""], ["roared Raskolnikov without restraint, “you lie, you damned punchinello!”", "라스콜니코프가 거리낌 없이 으르렁거렸다. \"거짓말이야, 이 빌어먹을 어릿광대야!\""], ["and he rushed at Porfiry who retreated to the other door, not at all alarmed.", "그러고는 포르피리에게 달려들었으나, 포르피리는 조금도 놀라지 않고 다른 문 쪽으로 물러섰다."]], [["“I understand it all!", "\"다 알겠어요!"], ["You are lying and mocking so that I may betray myself to you...”", "당신은 거짓말을 하고 비웃어서 내가 스스로를 당신에게 드러내게 하려는 거지요…….\""]], [["“Why, you could not betray yourself any further, my dear Rodion Romanovitch.", "\"아니, 자네는 더 이상 자신을 드러낼 것도 없네, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치."], ["You are in a passion.", "자네는 격앙되어 있어요."], ["Don’t shout, I shall call the clerks.”", "소리치지 말게, 서기들을 부르겠네.\""]], [["“You are lying! Call the clerks!", "\"당신은 거짓말을 하고 있어요!"], ["You knew I was ill and tried to work me into a frenzy to make me betray myself, that was your object!", "서기들을 부르시지요! 당신은 내가 아프다는 걸 알고서 나를 광란으로 몰아넣어 스스로를 드러내게 하려 했어요, 그게 당신 목적이었지요!"], ["Produce your facts!", "증거를 내놓으시지요!"], ["I understand it all.", "다 알겠어요."], ["You’ve no evidence, you have only wretched rubbishly suspicions like Zametov’s!", "당신에겐 아무 증거도 없고, 자메토프 같은 한심하고 시시한 의심만 있을 뿐이야!"], ["You knew my character, you wanted to drive me to fury and then to knock me down with priests and deputies....", "당신은 내 성격을 알고서 나를 분노로 몰아간 다음 사제들과 입회인들로 나를 짓뭉개려 했지요……."], ["Are you waiting for them?", "그들을 기다리고 있는 겁니까?"], ["eh!", "응!"], ["What are you waiting for?", "뭘 기다리는 거예요?"], ["Where are they?", "그들이 어디 있어요?"], ["Produce them?”", "내놓으시지요!\""]], [["“Why deputies, my good man?", "\"입회인이라니 무슨 소린가, 이 사람아?"], ["What things people will imagine!", "사람들이 별별 상상을 다 하는군!"], ["And to do so would not be acting in form as you say, you don’t know the business, my dear fellow....", "게다가 그렇게 하는 건 자네 말마따나 형식대로 하는 게 아니라네, 자네는 이 일을 모르는군, 친구……."], ["And there’s no escaping form, as you see,”", "그리고 보다시피 형식을 피할 길은 없지요.\""], ["Porfiry muttered, listening at the door through which a noise could be heard.", "포르피리는 소리가 들려오는 문에 귀를 기울이며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Ah, they’re coming,”", "\"아, 그들이 오는군요.\""], ["cried Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 외쳤다."], ["“You’ve sent for them!", "\"당신이 그들을 부른 거예요!"], ["You expected them!", "그들을 기다리고 있었어!"], ["Well, produce them all: your deputies, your witnesses, what you like!...", "자, 그들을 다 내놓으시지요. 당신의 입회인들이든 증인들이든, 뭐든 좋소!……"], ["I am ready!”", "저는 준비됐습니다!\""]], [["But at this moment a strange incident occurred, something so unexpected that neither Raskolnikov nor Porfiry Petrovitch could have looked for such a conclusion to their interview.", "그러나 바로 이 순간 기묘한 사건이 벌어졌으니, 너무도 예기치 못한 일이라 라스콜니코프도 포르피리 페트로비치도 그들의 면담이 이런 식으로 끝나리라고는 도저히 예상할 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["When he remembered the scene afterwards, this is how Raskolnikov saw it.", "나중에 그 장면을 떠올렸을 때, 라스콜니코프에게 그것은 이렇게 보였다."]], [["The noise behind the door increased, and suddenly the door was opened a little.", "문 너머의 소란이 점점 커지더니, 갑자기 문이 조금 열렸다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭐야?\""], ["cried Porfiry Petrovitch, annoyed.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 짜증스럽게 외쳤다."], ["“Why, I gave orders...”", "\"내가 분명히 일러두지 않았나…….\""]], [["For an instant there was no answer, but it was evident that there were several persons at the door, and that they were apparently pushing somebody back.", "한순간 아무 대답도 없었지만, 문가에 여러 사람이 있는 것이 분명했고, 누군가를 떠밀어 내고 있는 모양이었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭐냐고?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch repeated, uneasily.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 불안한 듯 되풀이했다."]], [["“The prisoner Nikolay has been brought,”", "\"죄수 니콜라이를 데려왔습니다.\""], ["someone answered.", "누군가 대답했다."]], [["“He is not wanted!", "\"그자는 필요 없어!"], ["Take him away!", "데려가!"], ["Let him wait!", "기다리라고 해!"], ["What’s he doing here?", "여기서 뭐 하는 거야?"], ["How irregular!”", "이런 무례한 짓이!\""], ["cried Porfiry, rushing to the door.", "포르피리는 문 쪽으로 달려가며 소리쳤다."]], [["“But he...”", "\"하지만 이자가…….\""], ["began the same voice, and suddenly ceased.", "같은 목소리가 말을 꺼내다 갑자기 뚝 끊겼다."]], [["Two seconds, not more, were spent in actual struggle, then someone gave a violent shove, and then a man, very pale, strode into the room.", "이 초, 그 이상은 아니게, 실제로 몸싸움이 벌어졌고, 그러더니 누군가 거칠게 떠밀자, 한 사내가, 몹시 창백한 얼굴로, 성큼성큼 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["This man’s appearance was at first sight very strange.", "이 사내의 행색은 첫눈에 매우 기이했다."], ["He stared straight before him, as though seeing nothing.", "그는 아무것도 보이지 않는 듯 똑바로 앞만 응시했다."], ["There was a determined gleam in his eyes; at the same time there was a deathly pallor in his face, as though he were being led to the scaffold.", "그의 두 눈에는 결연한 빛이 번득였고, 동시에 그의 얼굴은 마치 처형대로 끌려가는 사람처럼 죽은 듯 창백했다."], ["His white lips were faintly twitching.", "핏기 없는 입술이 희미하게 실룩거렸다."]], [["He was dressed like a workman and was of medium height, very young, slim, his hair cut in round crop, with thin spare features.", "그는 노동자처럼 차려입었고 중간 키에, 무척 젊고, 호리호리했으며, 머리는 둥글게 짧게 깎았고 야위고 메마른 이목구비를 지니고 있었다."], ["The man whom he had thrust back followed him into the room and succeeded in seizing him by the shoulder; he was a warder; but Nikolay pulled his arm away.", "그가 떠밀어 냈던 사내가 그를 뒤따라 방으로 들어와 가까스로 그의 어깨를 붙잡았는데, 그는 간수였다. 하지만 니콜라이는 그의 팔을 뿌리쳤다."]], [["Several persons crowded inquisitively into the doorway.", "여러 사람이 호기심에 차서 문간으로 몰려들었다."], ["Some of them tried to get in.", "그중 몇몇은 안으로 들어오려 했다."], ["All this took place almost instantaneously.", "이 모든 일이 거의 눈 깜짝할 사이에 일어났다."]], [["“Go away, it’s too soon!", "\"물러가, 아직 일러!"], ["Wait till you are sent for!...", "부를 때까지 기다려!……"], ["Why have you brought him so soon?”", "어쩌자고 이렇게 일찍 데려온 거야?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch muttered, extremely annoyed, and as it were thrown out of his reckoning.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 몹시 짜증스러운 듯, 그리고 마치 셈이 완전히 틀어진 듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["But Nikolay suddenly knelt down.", "그러나 니콜라이가 갑자기 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""], ["cried Porfiry, surprised.", "포르피리가 놀라며 소리쳤다."]], [["“I am guilty!", "\"제가 죄인입니다!"], ["Mine is the sin!", "죄는 제가 저질렀습니다!"], ["I am the murderer,”", "제가 살인자입니다.\""], ["Nikolay articulated suddenly, rather breathless, but speaking fairly loudly.", "니콜라이가 다소 숨이 찬 듯, 그러나 꽤 큰 소리로 갑자기 또박또박 말했다."]], [["For ten seconds there was silence as though all had been struck dumb; even the warder stepped back, mechanically retreated to the door, and stood immovable.", "십 초 동안 마치 모두가 벙어리가 된 듯 침묵이 흘렀다. 간수마저 뒷걸음질 쳐, 기계적으로 문 쪽으로 물러나더니, 꼼짝 않고 섰다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["cried Porfiry Petrovitch, recovering from his momentary stupefaction.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 순간적인 망연자실에서 깨어나며 외쳤다."]], [["“I...", "\"제가……"], ["am the murderer,”", "살인자입니다.\""], ["repeated Nikolay, after a brief pause.", "니콜라이가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 되풀이했다."]], [["“What...", "\"뭐라고……"], ["you...", "네가……"], ["what...", "뭐라고……"], ["whom did you kill?”", "누구를 죽였다는 거냐?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch was obviously bewildered.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 분명 어리둥절해하고 있었다."]], [["Nikolay again was silent for a moment.", "니콜라이는 또다시 잠시 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Alyona Ivanovna and her sister Lizaveta Ivanovna, I...", "\"알료나 이바노브나와 그 동생 리자베타 이바노브나를, 제가……"], ["killed...", "도끼로……"], ["with an axe.", "죽였습니다."], ["Darkness came over me,”", "정신이 캄캄해졌습니다.\""], ["he added suddenly, and was again silent.", "그가 갑자기 덧붙이고는 다시 입을 다물었다."]], [["He still remained on his knees.", "그는 여전히 무릎을 꿇은 채였다."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch stood for some moments as though meditating, but suddenly roused himself and waved back the uninvited spectators.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 한동안 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있다가, 갑자기 정신을 차리고는 청하지도 않은 구경꾼들을 손짓으로 물리쳤다."], ["They instantly vanished and closed the door.", "그들은 순식간에 사라지고 문을 닫았다."], ["Then he looked towards Raskolnikov, who was standing in the corner, staring wildly at Nikolay and moved towards him, but stopped short, looked from Nikolay to Raskolnikov and then again at Nikolay, and seeming unable to restrain himself darted at the latter.", "그러고 나서 그는 한쪽 구석에 서서 사납게 니콜라이를 응시하고 있던 라스콜니코프 쪽을 바라보더니, 그에게로 다가가다가, 문득 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 니콜라이를 보았다가 라스콜니코프를 보고 다시 니콜라이를 보았으며, 스스로를 억누르지 못하는 듯 후자에게 달려들었다."]], [["“You’re in too great a hurry,”", "\"너무 서두르는군.\""], ["he shouted at him, almost angrily.", "그가 거의 화가 난 듯 니콜라이에게 소리쳤다."], ["“I didn’t ask you what came over you....", "\"나는 너에게 무슨 일이 일어났느냐고 묻지 않았어……."], ["Speak, did you kill them?”", "말해 봐, 네가 그들을 죽였단 말이지?\""]], [["“I am the murderer....", "\"제가 살인자입니다……."], ["I want to give evidence,”", "진술하고 싶습니다.\""], ["Nikolay pronounced.", "니콜라이가 말했다."]], [["“Ach!", "\"아!"], ["What did you kill them with?”", "무엇으로 그들을 죽였나?\""]], [["“An axe.", "\"도끼로요."], ["I had it ready.”", "미리 준비해 두었습니다.\""]], [["“Ach, he is in a hurry!", "\"아, 이자가 서두르는군!"], ["Alone?”", "혼자서?\""]], [["Nikolay did not understand the question.", "니콜라이는 질문을 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["“Did you do it alone?”", "\"혼자서 한 거냐?\""]], [["“Yes, alone.", "\"네, 혼자서요."], ["And Mitka is not guilty and had no share in it.”", "그리고 미트카는 죄가 없고 거기에 가담하지도 않았습니다.\""]], [["“Don’t be in a hurry about Mitka!", "\"미트카 얘기는 서두르지 마라!"], ["A-ach!", "아!"], ["How was it you ran downstairs like that at the time?", "그런데 너는 그때 어쩌자고 그렇게 계단을 뛰어 내려갔나?"], ["The porters met you both!”", "문지기들이 너희 둘을 마주쳤다던데!\""]], [["“It was to put them off the scent...", "\"그건 추적을 따돌리려고요……"], ["I ran after Mitka,”", "미트카를 쫓아 달린 겁니다.\""], ["Nikolay replied hurriedly, as though he had prepared the answer.", "니콜라이가 마치 미리 답을 준비해 둔 듯 서둘러 대답했다."]], [["“I knew it!”", "\"그럴 줄 알았어!\""], ["cried Porfiry, with vexation.", "포르피리가 분한 듯 외쳤다."], ["“It’s not his own tale he is telling,”", "\"저건 제 입에서 나온 얘기가 아니야.\""], ["he muttered as though to himself, and suddenly his eyes rested on Raskolnikov again.", "그는 혼잣말하듯 중얼거렸고, 갑자기 그의 시선이 다시 라스콜니코프에게 머물렀다."]], [["He was apparently so taken up with Nikolay that for a moment he had forgotten Raskolnikov.", "그는 니콜라이에게 어찌나 정신이 팔려 있었던지 한순간 라스콜니코프를 잊고 있었던 것이 분명했다."], ["He was a little taken aback.", "그는 조금 당황했다."]], [["“My dear Rodion Romanovitch, excuse me!”", "\"친애하는 로디온 로마노비치, 미안하오!\""], ["he flew up to him, “this won’t do; I’m afraid you must go...", "그가 라스콜니코프에게 휙 날아들었다. \"이래선 안 되겠소, 아무래도 가셔야겠소……"], ["it’s no good your staying...", "더 계셔 봤자 소용없으니……"], ["I will...", "내가……"], ["you see, what a surprise!...", "보다시피, 이런 뜻밖의 일이!……"], ["Good-bye!”", "그럼 안녕히 가시오!\""]], [["And taking him by the arm, he showed him to the door.", "그러고는 그의 팔을 잡고 문 쪽으로 안내했다."]], [["“I suppose you didn’t expect it?”", "\"이런 건 예상 못 하셨겠지요?\""], ["said Raskolnikov who, though he had not yet fully grasped the situation, had regained his courage.", "라스콜니코프가 말했는데, 그는 아직 상황을 완전히 파악하지는 못했어도 용기를 되찾은 터였다."]], [["“You did not expect it either, my friend.", "\"당신도 예상 못 하긴 마찬가지였소, 친구."], ["See how your hand is trembling!", "손이 이렇게 떨리는 걸 좀 보시오!"], ["He-he!”", "헤헤!\""]], [["“You’re trembling, too, Porfiry Petrovitch!”", "\"당신도 떨고 있군요, 포르피리 페트로비치!\""]], [["“Yes, I am; I didn’t expect it.”", "\"그렇소, 나도 떨고 있소. 나도 예상 못 했으니까.\""]], [["They were already at the door; Porfiry was impatient for Raskolnikov to be gone.", "그들은 이미 문가에 와 있었다. 포르피리는 라스콜니코프가 어서 가 주기를 안달했다."]], [["“And your little surprise, aren’t you going to show it to me?”", "\"그런데 당신의 그 깜짝 선물 말이오, 나한테 보여 주지 않으시려오?\""], ["Raskolnikov said, sarcastically.", "라스콜니코프가 비꼬듯 말했다."]], [["“Why, his teeth are chattering as he asks, he-he!", "\"이런, 묻는 동안에도 이가 딱딱 부딪치는구려, 헤헤!"], ["You are an ironical person!", "당신은 빈정대기 좋아하는 사람이야!"], ["Come, till we meet!”", "자, 또 봅시다!\""]], [["“I believe we can say _good-bye_!”", "\"이젠 작별 인사를 해도 될 것 같은데요!\""]], [["“That’s in God’s hands,”", "\"그건 하느님 손에 달린 일이지.\""], ["muttered Porfiry, with an unnatural smile.", "포르피리가 부자연스러운 미소를 지으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["As he walked through the office, Raskolnikov noticed that many people were looking at him.", "사무실을 지나며 라스콜니코프는 많은 사람이 자신을 쳐다보고 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Among them he saw the two porters from _the_ house, whom he had invited that night to the police station.", "그들 중에서 그는 그날 밤 자신이 경찰서로 가자고 했던 바로 그 집의 문지기 둘을 보았다."], ["They stood there waiting.", "그들은 그곳에 서서 기다리고 있었다."], ["But he was no sooner on the stairs than he heard the voice of Porfiry Petrovitch behind him.", "그러나 그가 막 계단에 들어서자마자 등 뒤에서 포르피리 페트로비치의 목소리가 들렸다."], ["Turning round, he saw the latter running after him, out of breath.", "돌아보니, 후자가 숨을 헐떡이며 그를 뒤쫓아 달려오고 있었다."]], [["“One word, Rodion Romanovitch; as to all the rest, it’s in God’s hands, but as a matter of form there are some questions I shall have to ask you...", "\"한마디만, 로디온 로마노비치. 나머지야 모두 하느님 손에 달렸지만, 형식상 당신에게 물어봐야 할 게 몇 가지 있어서……"], ["so we shall meet again, shan’t we?”", "그러니 우리 또 만나게 되겠지요, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["And Porfiry stood still, facing him with a smile.", "그러고는 포르피리가 미소를 띤 채 그와 마주 보며 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Shan’t we?”", "\"안 그렇소?\""], ["he added again.", "그가 또 한 번 덧붙였다."]], [["He seemed to want to say something more, but could not speak out.", "그는 무언가 더 말하고 싶어 하는 듯했으나, 차마 입 밖에 내지 못했다."]], [["“You must forgive me, Porfiry Petrovitch, for what has just passed...", "\"방금 일은 용서해 주셔야겠습니다, 포르피리 페트로비치…… 제가 그만 성질을 부렸으니까요.\""], ["I lost my temper,” began Raskolnikov, who had so far regained his courage that he felt irresistibly inclined to display his coolness.", "라스콜니코프가 입을 열었는데, 그는 이제 용기를 되찾은 나머지 자신의 침착함을 과시하고 싶은 마음을 도무지 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["“Don’t mention it, don’t mention it,”", "\"무슨 말씀을, 무슨 말씀을.\""], ["Porfiry replied, almost gleefully.", "포르피리가 거의 즐거운 듯 대답했다."], ["“I myself, too...", "\"나 역시도……"], ["I have a wicked temper, I admit it!", "나도 성질이 고약하오, 인정하지!"], ["But we shall meet again.", "하지만 우리 또 만날 거요."], ["If it’s God’s will, we may see a great deal of one another.”", "하느님 뜻이라면, 우린 서로 아주 자주 보게 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["“And will get to know each other through and through?”", "\"그래서 서로를 속속들이 알게 되겠지요?\""], ["added Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes; know each other through and through,”", "\"그렇소, 서로를 속속들이 알게 되겠지.\""], ["assented Porfiry Petrovitch, and he screwed up his eyes, looking earnestly at Raskolnikov.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 맞장구치며 두 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 라스콜니코프를 진지하게 바라보았다."], ["“Now you’re going to a birthday party?”", "\"이제 생일 잔치에라도 가시는 길이오?\""]], [["“To a funeral.”", "\"장례식에요.\""]], [["“Of course, the funeral!", "\"아, 그렇지, 장례식이지!"], ["Take care of yourself, and get well.”", "몸조심하고, 어서 쾌차하시오.\""]], [["“I don’t know what to wish you,”", "\"당신에게는 무슨 인사를 해야 할지 모르겠군요.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, who had begun to descend the stairs, but looked back again.", "라스콜니코프가 말했는데, 그는 계단을 내려가기 시작하다가 다시 돌아보았다."], ["“I should like to wish you success, but your office is such a comical one.”", "\"성공을 빌어 드리고 싶지만, 당신의 직무라는 게 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지요.\""]], [["“Why comical?”", "\"왜 우스꽝스럽다는 거요?\""], ["Porfiry Petrovitch had turned to go, but he seemed to prick up his ears at this.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 돌아서서 가려던 참이었으나, 이 말에 귀가 번쩍 뜨이는 것 같았다."]], [["“Why, how you must have been torturing and harassing that poor Nikolay psychologically, after your fashion, till he confessed!", "\"아니, 당신이 그 가엾은 니콜라이를 자백할 때까지 당신 식으로 심리적으로 얼마나 고문하고 들들 볶았을지 말입니다!"], ["You must have been at him day and night, proving to him that he was the murderer, and now that he has confessed, you’ll begin vivisecting him again.", "분명 밤낮으로 그를 닦달하며 그가 살인자라고 입증해 댔겠지요. 그런데 이제 그가 자백을 했으니, 당신은 또다시 그를 산 채로 해부하기 시작하겠지요."], ["‘You are lying,’ you’ll say.", "'거짓말이야.' 당신은 이렇게 말할 겁니다."], ["‘You are not the murderer!", "'넌 살인자가 아니야!"], ["You can’t be!", "그럴 리가 없어!"], ["It’s not your own tale you are telling!’ You must admit it’s a comical business!”", "그건 네 입에서 나온 얘기가 아니야!' 그러니 우스꽝스러운 일이라는 걸 인정하셔야지요!\""]], [["“He-he-he!", "\"헤헤헤!"], ["You noticed then that I said to Nikolay just now that it was not his own tale he was telling?”", "그러니까 당신은 내가 방금 니콜라이에게 그건 네 입에서 나온 얘기가 아니라고 말한 걸 알아챘구려?\""]], [["“How could I help noticing it!”", "\"어떻게 못 알아챌 수 있겠습니까!\""]], [["“He-he!", "\"헤헤!"], ["You are quick-witted.", "당신은 재치가 빠르군."], ["You notice everything!", "뭐든 알아채니 말이오!"], ["You’ve really a playful mind!", "정말 장난기 넘치는 머리를 가졌어!"], ["And you always fasten on the comic side...", "게다가 늘 우스운 구석에 집착하고……"], ["he-he!", "헤헤!"], ["They say that was the marked characteristic of Gogol, among the writers.”", "작가들 중에서는 그게 고골의 두드러진 특징이었다더군.\""]], [["“Yes, of Gogol.”", "\"네, 고골이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, of Gogol....", "\"그렇소, 고골이지……"], ["I shall look forward to meeting you.”", "당신과 다시 만날 날을 고대하겠소.\""]], [["“So shall I.”", "\"저도요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov walked straight home.", "라스콜니코프는 곧장 집으로 걸어갔다."], ["He was so muddled and bewildered that on getting home he sat for a quarter of an hour on the sofa, trying to collect his thoughts.", "그는 어찌나 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽이고 어리둥절했던지 집에 도착해서는 십오 분 동안이나 소파에 앉아 생각을 가다듬으려 애썼다. 그는 니콜라이에 대해 생각하려 들지 않았다."], ["He did not attempt to think about Nikolay; he was stupefied; he felt that his confession was something inexplicable, amazing--something beyond his understanding.", "그는 멍해져 있었다. 그는 그 자백이 무언가 설명할 수 없고, 놀라운 것이라고, 자신의 이해를 넘어서는 것이라고 느꼈다."], ["But Nikolay’s confession was an actual fact.", "그러나 니콜라이의 자백은 엄연한 사실이었다."], ["The consequences of this fact were clear to him at once, its falsehood could not fail to be discovered, and then they would be after him again.", "이 사실의 결과가 그에게는 당장 분명히 보였다. 그 거짓이 들통나지 않을 리 없고, 그러면 그들은 또다시 그를 쫓을 것이다."], ["Till then, at least, he was free and must do something for himself, for the danger was imminent.", "적어도 그때까지는 그가 자유로운 몸이니 자신을 위해 무언가 해야 했다. 위험이 코앞에 닥쳐 있었으니까."]], [["But how imminent?", "그런데 얼마나 코앞일까?"], ["His position gradually became clear to him.", "그의 처지가 차츰 분명해졌다."], ["Remembering, sketchily, the main outlines of his recent scene with Porfiry, he could not help shuddering again with horror.", "방금 전 포르피리와 겪은 장면의 큰 윤곽을 어렴풋이 떠올리자, 그는 또다시 공포로 몸서리치지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Of course, he did not yet know all Porfiry’s aims, he could not see into all his calculations.", "물론, 그는 아직 포르피리의 의도를 전부 알지는 못했고, 그의 속셈을 다 꿰뚫어 볼 수도 없었다."], ["But he had already partly shown his hand, and no one knew better than Raskolnikov how terrible Porfiry’s “lead” had been for him.", "그러나 그는 이미 자신의 패를 일부 내보였고, 포르피리의 그 '유도'가 자신에게 얼마나 무서운 것이었는지는 라스콜니코프 자신보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 없었다."], ["A little more and he _might_ have given himself away completely, circumstantially.", "조금만 더 갔더라면 그는 자신을 완전히, 구체적으로 드러내 버렸을지도 모른다."], ["Knowing his nervous temperament and from the first glance seeing through him, Porfiry, though playing a bold game, was bound to win.", "그의 신경질적인 기질을 알고 첫눈에 그를 꿰뚫어 본 포르피리는, 비록 대담한 도박을 벌이고 있었지만, 이길 수밖에 없었다."], ["There’s no denying that Raskolnikov had compromised himself seriously, but no _facts_ had come to light as yet; there was nothing positive.", "라스콜니코프가 자신을 심각하게 위태롭게 만들었다는 것은 부인할 수 없었으나, 아직 그 어떤 사실도 드러나지 않았고, 확실한 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["But was he taking a true view of the position?", "그런데 그가 이 상황을 제대로 보고 있는 것일까?"], ["Wasn’t he mistaken?", "잘못 짚은 건 아닐까?"], ["What had Porfiry been trying to get at?", "포르피리는 무엇을 알아내려 했던 걸까?"], ["Had he really some surprise prepared for him?", "그가 정말로 자신을 위해 어떤 깜짝 선물을 준비해 두었던 걸까?"], ["And what was it?", "그게 대체 뭐였을까?"], ["Had he really been expecting something or not?", "그가 정말로 무언가를 기대하고 있었던 걸까, 아니었을까?"], ["How would they have parted if it had not been for the unexpected appearance of Nikolay?", "니콜라이가 뜻밖에 나타나지 않았더라면 그들은 어떻게 헤어졌을까?"]], [["Porfiry had shown almost all his cards--of course, he had risked something in showing them--and if he had really had anything up his sleeve (Raskolnikov reflected), he would have shown that, too.", "포르피리는 거의 모든 패를 다 보여 주었다. 물론, 그것을 보여 주면서 무언가를 걸기는 했다."], ["What was that “surprise”? Was it a joke?", "그리고 만일 그가 정말로 소맷자락에 감춰 둔 것이 있었다면(라스콜니코프는 곰곰이 생각했다) 그것도 보여 주었을 것이다."], ["Had it meant anything?", "그 '깜짝 선물'이란 무엇이었을까? 농담이었을까?"], ["Could it have concealed anything like a fact, a piece of positive evidence?", "무슨 의미가 있었던 걸까? 그 안에 무슨 사실 같은 것, 확실한 증거 같은 것이 숨겨져 있었을까?"], ["His yesterday’s visitor?", "어제의 그 방문객?"], ["What had become of him?", "그자는 어떻게 된 걸까?"], ["Where was he to-day?", "오늘 어디 있는 걸까?"], ["If Porfiry really had any evidence, it must be connected with him....", "만일 포르피리에게 정말 어떤 증거가 있다면, 그건 그자와 연관된 것이 틀림없다……."]], [["He sat on the sofa with his elbows on his knees and his face hidden in his hands.", "그는 두 팔꿈치를 무릎에 괸 채 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 소파에 앉아 있었다."], ["He was still shivering nervously.", "그는 여전히 신경질적으로 떨고 있었다."], ["At last he got up, took his cap, thought a minute, and went to the door.", "마침내 그는 일어나 모자를 집어 들고, 잠시 생각하다가, 문 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["He had a sort of presentiment that for to-day, at least, he might consider himself out of danger.", "그는 적어도 오늘만큼은 위험에서 벗어났다고 여겨도 좋으리라는 일종의 예감이 들었다."], ["He had a sudden sense almost of joy; he wanted to make haste to Katerina Ivanovna’s.", "그는 문득 거의 기쁨에 가까운 감정을 느꼈다. 그는 어서 카테리나 이바노브나에게 가고 싶었다."], ["He would be too late for the funeral, of course, but he would be in time for the memorial dinner, and there at once he would see Sonia.", "물론 장례식에는 너무 늦겠지만, 추도 만찬에는 늦지 않게 도착할 테고, 거기서 곧바로 소냐를 보게 될 것이다."]], [["He stood still, thought a moment, and a suffering smile came for a moment on to his lips.", "그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 잠시 생각했고, 한순간 고통스러운 미소가 그의 입가에 떠올랐다."]], [["“To-day!", "\"오늘!"], ["To-day,”", "오늘이다.\""], ["he repeated to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 되뇌었다."], ["“Yes, to-day!", "\"그래, 오늘!"], ["So it must be....”", "그래야만 해…….\""]], [["But as he was about to open the door, it began opening of itself.", "그러나 그가 막 문을 열려는 순간, 문이 저절로 열리기 시작했다."], ["He started and moved back.", "그는 흠칫 놀라 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["The door opened gently and slowly, and there suddenly appeared a figure--yesterday’s visitor _from underground_.", "문이 부드럽고 천천히 열리더니, 갑자기 한 사람의 모습이 나타났다. 어제 그 지하에서 온 방문객이었다."]], [["The man stood in the doorway, looked at Raskolnikov without speaking, and took a step forward into the room.", "사내는 문간에 서서, 말없이 라스콜니코프를 바라보다가, 방 안으로 한 걸음 들어섰다. 그는 어제와 똑같았다."], ["He was exactly the same as yesterday; the same figure, the same dress, but there was a great change in his face; he looked dejected and sighed deeply.", "똑같은 행색에, 똑같은 옷차림이었으나, 얼굴에는 커다란 변화가 있었다. 그는 풀이 죽어 보였고 깊이 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["If he had only put his hand up to his cheek and leaned his head on one side he would have looked exactly like a peasant woman.", "만일 그가 손을 뺨에 갖다 대고 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이기만 했더라면, 영락없이 농촌 아낙처럼 보였을 것이다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov, numb with terror.", "라스콜니코프가 공포에 얼어붙은 채 물었다."], ["The man was still silent, but suddenly he bowed down almost to the ground, touching it with his finger.", "사내는 여전히 말이 없다가, 갑자기 거의 땅에 닿도록 허리를 숙여, 손가락으로 바닥을 짚었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이냐고?\""], ["cried Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 외쳤다."]], [["“I have sinned,”", "\"제가 죄를 지었습니다.\""], ["the man articulated softly.", "사내가 나직이 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“By evil thoughts.”", "\"못된 생각을 품어서요.\""]], [["They looked at one another.", "그들은 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“I was vexed.", "\"제가 화가 났었습니다."], ["When you came, perhaps in drink, and bade the porters go to the police station and asked about the blood, I was vexed that they let you go and took you for drunken.", "당신이, 어쩌면 술에 취해서 왔을 때, 문지기들에게 경찰서로 가자고 하고 피에 대해 물었을 때, 저는 그들이 당신을 보내 주고 당신을 술꾼으로 여긴 것에 화가 났습니다."], ["I was so vexed that I lost my sleep.", "어찌나 화가 났던지 잠을 다 설쳤지요."], ["And remembering the address we came here yesterday and asked for you....”", "그러다 주소를 기억해 내고 어제 이리로 와서 당신을 찾았습니다…….\""]], [["“Who came?”", "\"누가 왔다고?\""], ["Raskolnikov interrupted, instantly beginning to recollect.", "라스콜니코프가 가로막으며, 순식간에 기억을 떠올리기 시작했다."]], [["“I did, I’ve wronged you.”", "\"제가요, 제가 당신에게 못된 짓을 했습니다.\""]], [["“Then you come from that house?”", "\"그렇다면 당신이 그 집에서 온 사람이오?\""]], [["“I was standing at the gate with them...", "\"저는 그들과 함께 대문 앞에 서 있었지요……"], ["don’t you remember?", "기억 안 나십니까?"], ["We have carried on our trade in that house for years past.", "우리는 그 집에서 몇 년째 장사를 해 오고 있습니다."], ["We cure and prepare hides, we take work home...", "가죽을 무두질하고 다듬는 일을 하는데, 일감을 집으로 가져와서……"], ["most of all I was vexed....”", "무엇보다 제가 화가 났던 건…….\""]], [["And the whole scene of the day before yesterday in the gateway came clearly before Raskolnikov’s mind; he recollected that there had been several people there besides the porters, women among them.", "그러자 그저께 대문간에서 있었던 그 장면 전체가 라스콜니코프의 머릿속에 또렷이 떠올랐다. 그는 그곳에 문지기들 말고도 여러 사람이, 그중에는 여자들도 있었음을 기억해 냈다."], ["He remembered one voice had suggested taking him straight to the police-station.", "그는 한 목소리가 자신을 곧장 경찰서로 데려가자고 했던 것을 기억했다."], ["He could not recall the face of the speaker, and even now he did not recognise it, but he remembered that he had turned round and made him some answer....", "그는 말한 사람의 얼굴을 떠올릴 수 없었고, 지금도 그를 알아보지 못했지만, 자신이 돌아서서 그에게 무언가 대꾸했던 것은 기억했다……."]], [["So this was the solution of yesterday’s horror.", "그러니까 이것이 어제 그 공포의 정체였다."], ["The most awful thought was that he had been actually almost lost, had almost done for himself on account of such a _trivial_ circumstance.", "무엇보다 끔찍한 생각은, 자신이 그토록 하찮은 정황 하나 때문에 실제로 거의 파멸할 뻔했다는, 거의 자기 손으로 끝장낼 뻔했다는 것이었다."], ["So this man could tell nothing except his asking about the flat and the blood stains.", "그러니까 이 사내는 자신이 셋집과 핏자국에 대해 물었던 것 외에는 아무것도 말할 수 없었던 것이다."], ["So Porfiry, too, had nothing but that _delirium_, no facts but this _psychology_ which _cuts both ways_, nothing positive.", "그러니까 포르피리에게도 그 헛소리 말고는, 양쪽으로 다 통하는 그 심리학 말고는 아무것도 없었던 것이다. 확실한 것은 아무것도."], ["So if no more facts come to light (and they must not, they must not!) then...", "그러니 더 이상 사실이 드러나지 않는다면(그래서는 안 된다, 절대 안 된다!) 그렇다면……"], ["then what can they do to him?", "그렇다면 그들이 자신에게 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["How can they convict him, even if they arrest him?", "설령 자신을 체포한다 해도 어떻게 유죄를 입증할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["And Porfiry then had only just heard about the flat and had not known about it before.", "그리고 포르피리는 그때 셋집에 대해서 방금 막 들었을 뿐, 그 전에는 그것을 알지 못했던 것이다."]], [["“Was it you who told Porfiry...", "\"포르피리에게……"], ["that I’d been there?”", "내가 거기 갔었다고 말한 게 당신이오?\""], ["he cried, struck by a sudden idea.", "그가 갑작스러운 생각에 충격을 받아 외쳤다."]], [["“What Porfiry?”", "\"무슨 포르피리요?\""]], [["“The head of the detective department?”", "\"수사과의 책임자 말이오.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["The porters did not go there, but I went.”", "문지기들은 거기 가지 않았지만, 제가 갔습니다.\""]], [["“To-day?”", "\"오늘?\""]], [["“I got there two minutes before you.", "\"당신보다 이 분 먼저 도착했지요."], ["And I heard, I heard it all, how he worried you.”", "그리고 저는 들었습니다, 그가 당신을 어떻게 들들 볶는지 다 들었어요.\""]], [["“Where?", "\"어디서?"], ["What?", "뭘?"], ["When?”", "언제?\""]], [["“Why, in the next room.", "\"아니, 옆방에서요."], ["I was sitting there all the time.”", "저는 내내 거기 앉아 있었습니다.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["Why, then you were the surprise?", "아니, 그렇다면 당신이 그 깜짝 선물이었군?"], ["But how could it happen?", "한데 어떻게 그런 일이?"], ["Upon my word!”", "맙소사!\""]], [["“I saw that the porters did not want to do what I said,”", "\"문지기들이 제 말대로 하기 싫어하는 걸 보고서요.\""], ["began the man; “for it’s too late, said they, and maybe he’ll be angry that we did not come at the time.", "사내가 말을 시작했다. \"너무 늦었다, 그리고 우리가 그때 가지 않았다고 어쩌면 화를 낼지도 모른다, 하고 그들이 말하더군요."], ["I was vexed and I lost my sleep, and I began making inquiries.", "저는 화가 나서 잠을 다 설쳤고, 이리저리 알아보기 시작했지요."], ["And finding out yesterday where to go, I went to-day.", "그리고 어제 어디로 가야 할지 알아내고는, 오늘 갔습니다."], ["The first time I went he wasn’t there, when I came an hour later he couldn’t see me.", "처음 갔을 때는 그가 없었고, 한 시간 뒤에 가니 저를 만나 줄 수 없다더군요."], ["I went the third time, and they showed me in.", "세 번째로 갔더니, 저를 안으로 들여보내 주었습니다."], ["I informed him of everything, just as it happened, and he began skipping about the room and punching himself on the chest.", "저는 모든 것을, 일어난 그대로 그에게 알렸지요. 그러자 그가 방 안을 폴짝폴짝 뛰어다니며 자기 가슴을 주먹으로 치기 시작했습니다."], ["‘What do you scoundrels mean by it?", "'이 악당 같은 놈들, 대체 무슨 짓이냐?"], ["If I’d known about it I should have arrested him!’ Then he ran out, called somebody and began talking to him in the corner, then he turned to me, scolding and questioning me.", "내가 그걸 알았더라면 그놈을 체포했을 텐데!' 그러더니 밖으로 뛰어나가, 누군가를 불러서 구석에서 그와 이야기를 나누고, 그런 다음 저를 향해 돌아서서, 야단치고 캐물었습니다."], ["He scolded me a great deal; and I told him everything, and I told him that you didn’t dare to say a word in answer to me yesterday and that you didn’t recognise me.", "저를 잔뜩 나무라더군요. 그래서 저는 그에게 모든 것을 말했고, 당신이 어제 제 말에 한마디도 감히 대꾸하지 못했다는 것과 저를 알아보지 못했다는 것도 말했습니다."], ["And he fell to running about again and kept hitting himself on the chest, and getting angry and running about, and when you were announced he told me to go into the next room.", "그러자 그가 또다시 뛰어다니기 시작하며 연신 자기 가슴을 치고, 화를 내고 뛰어다니더니, 당신이 왔다는 전갈이 오자 저더러 옆방으로 들어가라고 했습니다. '거기 좀 앉아 있게.'"], ["‘Sit there a bit,’ he said.", "그가 말했어요. '무슨 소리를 듣든 꼼짝 마.'"], ["‘Don’t move, whatever you may hear.’ And he set a chair there for me and locked me in.", "그러고는 거기에 의자를 놓아 주고 저를 가두었습니다. '어쩌면 자네를 부를지도 모르니까.'"], ["‘Perhaps,’ he said, ‘I may call you.’ And when Nikolay’d been brought he let me out as soon as you were gone.", "그가 말했지요. 그리고 니콜라이가 끌려오자, 당신이 가자마자 저를 내보내 주었습니다."], ["‘I shall send for you again and question you,’ he said.”", "'내가 다시 사람을 보내 자네를 부르고 심문하겠네.' 하고 그가 말하더군요.\""]], [["“And did he question Nikolay while you were there?”", "\"그러면 그가 당신이 거기 있는 동안 니콜라이를 심문했소?\""]], [["“He got rid of me as he did of you, before he spoke to Nikolay.”", "\"그가 당신을 내보낸 것처럼 저를 내보낸 다음에야 니콜라이와 이야기했습니다.\""]], [["The man stood still, and again suddenly bowed down, touching the ground with his finger.", "사내는 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 다시 갑자기 허리를 숙여 손가락으로 바닥을 짚었다."]], [["“Forgive me for my evil thoughts, and my slander.”", "\"제 못된 생각과 중상을 용서해 주십시오.\""]], [["“May God forgive you,”", "\"하느님께서 당신을 용서하시기를.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."]], [["And as he said this, the man bowed down again, but not to the ground, turned slowly and went out of the room.", "그리고 그가 이렇게 말하자, 사내는 다시 허리를 숙였으나, 이번에는 땅에 닿도록은 아니었고, 천천히 돌아서서 방을 나갔다."]], [["“It all cuts both ways, now it all cuts both ways,”", "\"이제 모든 게 양쪽으로 다 통하는군, 이제 모든 게 양쪽으로 다 통해.\""], ["repeated Raskolnikov, and he went out more confident than ever.", "라스콜니코프가 되뇌었고, 그는 그 어느 때보다 자신감에 차서 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“Now we’ll make a fight for it,”", "\"이제 한판 싸워 보는 거다.\""], ["he said, with a malicious smile, as he went down the stairs.", "그가 계단을 내려가며 표독스러운 미소를 띤 채 말했다."], ["His malice was aimed at himself; with shame and contempt he recollected his “cowardice.”", "그의 표독스러움은 자기 자신을 겨냥한 것이었다. 그는 수치와 경멸을 느끼며 자신의 '비겁함'을 떠올렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["The morning that followed the fateful interview with Dounia and her mother brought sobering influences to bear on Pyotr Petrovitch.", "두냐와 그녀의 어머니와의 운명적인 면담이 있은 다음 날 아침, 표트르 페트로비치에게는 정신이 번쩍 들게 하는 일들이 닥쳐왔다."], ["Intensely unpleasant as it was, he was forced little by little to accept as a fact beyond recall what had seemed to him only the day before fantastic and incredible.", "무척이나 불쾌한 일이었으나, 그는 바로 전날까지만 해도 환상적이고 믿기 어려운 것으로만 여겨졌던 일을 돌이킬 수 없는 사실로 조금씩 받아들이지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The black snake of wounded vanity had been gnawing at his heart all night.", "상처 입은 허영심이라는 검은 뱀이 밤새도록 그의 심장을 갉아먹고 있었던 것이다."], ["When he got out of bed, Pyotr Petrovitch immediately looked in the looking-glass.", "침대에서 일어나자마자 표트르 페트로비치는 곧장 거울을 들여다보았다."], ["He was afraid that he had jaundice.", "그는 황달에 걸린 것이 아닐까 두려웠다."], ["However his health seemed unimpaired so far, and looking at his noble, clear-skinned countenance which had grown fattish of late, Pyotr Petrovitch for an instant was positively comforted in the conviction that he would find another bride and, perhaps, even a better one.", "그러나 건강은 아직 멀쩡해 보였고, 요즘 들어 다소 살이 오른 그 고결하고 깨끗한 피부의 얼굴을 바라보며, 표트르 페트로비치는 한순간 또 다른 신붓감을, 어쩌면 더 나은 신붓감을 찾을 수 있으리라는 확신에 진심으로 위안을 얻었다."], ["But coming back to the sense of his present position, he turned aside and spat vigorously, which excited a sarcastic smile in Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov, the young friend with whom he was staying.", "그러나 다시금 자신의 현재 처지가 떠오르자, 그는 고개를 돌리고 거칠게 침을 뱉었는데, 이는 그가 함께 묵고 있는 젊은 친구 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프의 입가에 비웃는 미소를 자아냈다."], ["That smile Pyotr Petrovitch noticed, and at once set it down against his young friend’s account.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그 미소를 놓치지 않았고, 즉시 그것을 젊은 친구의 잘못으로 적어 두었다."], ["He had set down a good many points against him of late.", "요즘 들어 그는 그에게 적잖은 흠을 적어 두던 참이었다."], ["His anger was redoubled when he reflected that he ought not to have told Andrey Semyonovitch about the result of yesterday’s interview.", "어제 면담의 결과를 안드레이 세묘노비치에게 떠벌리지 말았어야 했다는 생각이 들자 그의 분노는 곱절로 커졌다."], ["That was the second mistake he had made in temper, through impulsiveness and irritability....", "그것은 충동과 짜증 때문에 욱하는 김에 저지른 두 번째 실수였다……."], ["Moreover, all that morning one unpleasantness followed another.", "게다가 그날 아침 내내 불쾌한 일들이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 이어졌다."], ["He even found a hitch awaiting him in his legal case in the senate.", "원로원에 걸려 있던 그의 소송에서도 차질이 기다리고 있었다."], ["He was particularly irritated by the owner of the flat which had been taken in view of his approaching marriage and was being redecorated at his own expense; the owner, a rich German tradesman, would not entertain the idea of breaking the contract which had just been signed and insisted on the full forfeit money, though Pyotr Petrovitch would be giving him back the flat practically redecorated.", "특히 그를 짜증 나게 한 것은, 다가오는 결혼을 염두에 두고 빌려 자비를 들여 새로 단장하던 그 셋집의 주인이었다. 부유한 독일인 상인인 주인은, 표트르 페트로비치가 사실상 새로 단장된 셋집을 도로 넘겨주는 셈인데도 막 서명한 계약을 파기한다는 생각은 받아들이려 하지 않고, 위약금 전액을 요구하며 고집을 부렸다."], ["In the same way the upholsterers refused to return a single rouble of the instalment paid for the furniture purchased but not yet removed to the flat.", "마찬가지로 가구상도, 사들였으나 아직 셋집으로 옮기지 않은 가구의 할부금을 단 일 루블도 돌려주려 하지 않았다."]], [["“Am I to get married simply for the sake of the furniture?”", "'고작 가구 때문에 결혼이라도 해야 한단 말인가?'"], ["Pyotr Petrovitch ground his teeth and at the same time once more he had a gleam of desperate hope.", "표트르 페트로비치는 이를 갈았고, 그와 동시에 다시금 절망적인 희망의 빛이 번뜩였다."], ["“Can all that be really so irrevocably over?", "'정말 이 모든 것이 그렇게 돌이킬 수 없이 끝나 버린 걸까?"], ["Is it no use to make another effort?”", "다시 한번 애써 볼 여지는 없는 것일까?'"], ["The thought of Dounia sent a voluptuous pang through his heart.", "두냐를 생각하자 그의 심장에 관능적인 통증이 짜릿하게 스쳤다."], ["He endured anguish at that moment, and if it had been possible to slay Raskolnikov instantly by wishing it, Pyotr Petrovitch would promptly have uttered the wish.", "그 순간 그는 고통을 견디고 있었으니, 만일 바라기만 해도 라스콜니코프를 당장 죽일 수 있었다면, 표트르 페트로비치는 즉각 그 소원을 입에 올렸으리라."]], [["“It was my mistake, too, not to have given them money,”", "'그들에게 돈을 주지 않은 것도 내 실수였어.'"], ["he thought, as he returned dejectedly to Lebeziatnikov’s room, “and why on earth was I such a Jew?", "그는 레베쟈트니코프의 방으로 풀이 죽어 돌아오며 생각했다. '그런데 도대체 왜 내가 그렇게 유대인처럼 굴었을까?"], ["It was false economy!", "그건 잘못된 절약이었어!"], ["I meant to keep them without a penny so that they should turn to me as their providence, and look at them!", "나는 그들을 한 푼도 없이 두어서 나를 자기들의 구세주로 여기게 만들 작정이었는데, 꼴이 이게 뭐람!"], ["foo!", "흥!"], ["If I’d spent some fifteen hundred roubles on them for the trousseau and presents, on knick-knacks, dressing-cases, jewellery, materials, and all that sort of trash from Knopp’s and the English shop, my position would have been better and...", "혼수며 선물이며, 자질구레한 장식품, 화장 도구함, 보석, 옷감 따위, 그리고 크노프 상점과 영국 상점에서 파는 그런 잡동사니에 천오백 루블쯤만 썼더라면, 내 입지는 더 나았을 테고…… 더 단단했겠지!"], ["stronger! They could not have refused me so easily!", "그들이 나를 그렇게 쉽게 거절하지는 못했을 거야!"], ["They are the sort of people that would feel bound to return money and presents if they broke it off; and they would find it hard to do it!", "그들은 파혼하게 되면 돈과 선물을 돌려줘야 한다고 느낄 만한 부류의 사람들이고, 그러기가 쉽지 않았을 텐데!"], ["And their conscience would prick them: how can we dismiss a man who has hitherto been so generous and delicate?....", "그리고 양심이 그들을 찔렀겠지. 지금껏 이토록 너그럽고 섬세했던 사람을 어떻게 내칠 수 있단 말인가?……"], ["H’m!", "흠!"], ["I’ve made a blunder.”", "내가 큰 실수를 저질렀군.'"]], [["And grinding his teeth again, Pyotr Petrovitch called himself a fool--but not aloud, of course.", "그러고는 다시 이를 갈며, 표트르 페트로비치는 스스로를 바보라고 불렀다 — 물론 소리 내어서는 아니었다."]], [["He returned home, twice as irritated and angry as before.", "그는 전보다 갑절이나 짜증과 화가 치민 채 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["The preparations for the funeral dinner at Katerina Ivanovna’s excited his curiosity as he passed.", "카테리나 이바노브나의 집에서 벌어지는 장례 만찬 준비가 지나는 길에 그의 호기심을 자극했다."], ["He had heard about it the day before; he fancied, indeed, that he had been invited, but absorbed in his own cares he had paid no attention.", "그는 전날 그 일에 대해 들은 바 있었고, 사실 자신도 초대받은 것 같다고 어렴풋이 느꼈으나, 자기 걱정에 빠져 있던 터라 신경 쓰지 않았던 것이다."], ["Inquiring of Madame Lippevechsel who was busy laying the table while Katerina Ivanovna was away at the cemetery, he heard that the entertainment was to be a great affair, that all the lodgers had been invited, among them some who had not known the dead man, that even Andrey Semyonovitch Lebeziatnikov was invited in spite of his previous quarrel with Katerina Ivanovna, that he, Pyotr Petrovitch, was not only invited, but was eagerly expected as he was the most important of the lodgers.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 묘지에 가 있는 동안 식탁을 차리느라 분주하던 리페베흐젤 부인에게 물어보니, 잔치가 대단한 규모로 차려질 것이며, 세입자들이 모두 초대받았고, 그중에는 고인을 알지도 못하던 이들도 있으며, 카테리나 이바노브나와 전에 다투었던 안드레이 세묘노비치 레베쟈트니코프조차 초대받았고, 자기, 즉 표트르 페트로비치는 초대받았을 뿐 아니라 가장 중요한 세입자인 만큼 손꼽아 기다려지고 있다는 것이었다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna herself had been invited with great ceremony in spite of the recent unpleasantness, and so she was very busy with preparations and was taking a positive pleasure in them; she was moreover dressed up to the nines, all in new black silk, and she was proud of it.", "아말리아 이바노브나 본인도 최근의 불쾌한 일에도 불구하고 정중히 초대받았고, 그래서 그녀는 준비에 무척 바빴으며 거기에 진심으로 즐거워하고 있었다. 게다가 그녀는 한껏 빼입었으니, 온통 새 검은 비단옷 차림으로 그것을 자랑스러워했다."], ["All this suggested an idea to Pyotr Petrovitch and he went into his room, or rather Lebeziatnikov’s, somewhat thoughtful.", "이 모든 것이 표트르 페트로비치에게 어떤 생각을 떠올리게 했고, 그는 다소 생각에 잠긴 채 자기 방으로, 아니 더 정확히는 레베쟈트니코프의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["He had learnt that Raskolnikov was to be one of the guests.", "그는 라스콜니코프도 손님 중 하나로 올 것이라는 사실을 알게 되었던 것이다."]], [["Andrey Semyonovitch had been at home all the morning.", "안드레이 세묘노비치는 아침 내내 집에 있었다."], ["The attitude of Pyotr Petrovitch to this gentleman was strange, though perhaps natural.", "표트르 페트로비치가 이 신사를 대하는 태도는 기이했으나, 어쩌면 자연스러운 것이기도 했다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch had despised and hated him from the day he came to stay with him and at the same time he seemed somewhat afraid of him.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그와 함께 묵으러 온 날부터 그를 경멸하고 미워했으면서도, 동시에 그를 다소 두려워하는 것처럼 보였다."], ["He had not come to stay with him on his arrival in Petersburg simply from parsimony, though that had been perhaps his chief object.", "그가 페테르부르크에 도착해 그와 함께 묵게 된 것은 단순히 인색함 때문만은 아니었으나, 어쩌면 그것이 주된 목적이었는지도 모른다."], ["He had heard of Andrey Semyonovitch, who had once been his ward, as a leading young progressive who was taking an important part in certain interesting circles, the doings of which were a legend in the provinces.", "그는 한때 자신이 후견하던 안드레이 세묘노비치가 어떤 흥미로운 모임들에서 중요한 역할을 하는 손꼽히는 젊은 진보주의자라는 소문을 들었는데, 그 모임의 활동은 지방에서는 전설처럼 떠돌았다."], ["It had impressed Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그것이 표트르 페트로비치에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."], ["These powerful omniscient circles who despised everyone and showed everyone up had long inspired in him a peculiar but quite vague alarm.", "모두를 경멸하고 모두의 정체를 폭로하는 그 강력하고 전지全知한 모임들은 오래전부터 그에게 묘하지만 막연하기 짝이 없는 불안감을 불러일으켰다."], ["He had not, of course, been able to form even an approximate notion of what they meant.", "물론 그는 그것들이 무엇을 뜻하는지에 대해 대략적인 개념조차 잡지 못했다."], ["He, like everyone, had heard that there were, especially in Petersburg, progressives of some sort, nihilists and so on, and, like many people, he exaggerated and distorted the significance of those words to an absurd degree.", "그도 다른 모든 사람처럼, 특히 페테르부르크에 어떤 진보주의자들, 허무주의자들 따위가 있다는 말을 들은 바 있었고, 많은 사람들처럼 그 단어들의 의미를 터무니없을 만큼 부풀리고 왜곡했다."], ["What for many years past he had feared more than anything was _being shown up_ and this was the chief ground for his continual uneasiness at the thought of transferring his business to Petersburg.", "지난 여러 해 동안 그가 무엇보다 두려워했던 것은 바로 정체를 폭로당하는 일이었고, 이것이 자기 사업을 페테르부르크로 옮긴다는 생각에 끊임없이 마음을 졸이게 한 주된 이유였다."], ["He was afraid of this as little children are sometimes panic-stricken.", "그는 어린아이들이 이따금 까닭 없이 공포에 사로잡히듯 이를 두려워했다."], ["Some years before, when he was just entering on his own career, he had come upon two cases in which rather important personages in the province, patrons of his, had been cruelly shown up.", "몇 해 전, 그가 막 자기 경력을 시작하려던 무렵, 그는 지방에서 자신의 후원자였던 꽤 중요한 인물 두 사람이 잔인하게 폭로당한 두 가지 사건을 마주쳤다."], ["One instance had ended in great scandal for the person attacked and the other had very nearly ended in serious trouble.", "한 경우는 폭로당한 인물에게 큰 추문으로 끝났고, 다른 한 경우는 하마터면 심각한 곤경으로 끝날 뻔했다."], ["For this reason Pyotr Petrovitch intended to go into the subject as soon as he reached Petersburg and, if necessary, to anticipate contingencies by seeking the favour of “our younger generation.”", "이런 까닭에 표트르 페트로비치는 페테르부르크에 도착하는 즉시 이 문제를 파고들어, 필요하다면 '우리의 젊은 세대'의 호의를 구함으로써 만일의 사태에 미리 대비할 작정이었다."], ["He relied on Andrey Semyonovitch for this and before his visit to Raskolnikov he had succeeded in picking up some current phrases.", "이 일에서 그는 안드레이 세묘노비치를 믿고 의지했으며, 라스콜니코프를 방문하기에 앞서 유행하는 표현 몇 가지를 주워 익히는 데 성공했다."], ["He soon discovered that Andrey Semyonovitch was a commonplace simpleton, but that by no means reassured Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그는 곧 안드레이 세묘노비치가 평범한 멍청이라는 것을 알아챘으나, 그렇다고 해서 표트르 페트로비치가 결코 안심한 것은 아니었다."], ["Even if he had been certain that all the progressives were fools like him, it would not have allayed his uneasiness.", "설령 모든 진보주의자가 그와 같은 바보들이라고 확신했다 한들, 그의 불안이 가라앉지는 않았으리라."], ["All the doctrines, the ideas, the systems, with which Andrey Semyonovitch pestered him had no interest for him.", "안드레이 세묘노비치가 끈질기게 늘어놓는 온갖 학설이며 사상, 체계 따위에는 그는 아무런 흥미도 없었다."], ["He had his own object--he simply wanted to find out at once what was happening _here_.", "그에게는 그 자신의 목적이 있었으니 — 그는 그저 이곳에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 당장 알아내고 싶을 뿐이었다."], ["Had these people any power or not?", "이 사람들에게 힘이 있는가, 없는가?"], ["Had he anything to fear from them?", "그들에게서 두려워할 무언가가 있는가?"], ["Would they expose any enterprise of his?", "그들이 그의 어떤 사업이라도 폭로할까?"], ["And what precisely was now the object of their attacks?", "그리고 지금 그들이 공격하는 대상은 정확히 무엇인가?"], ["Could he somehow make up to them and get round them if they really were powerful?", "그들이 정말로 힘이 있다면, 어떻게든 비위를 맞추어 구워삶을 수 있을까?"], ["Was this the thing to do or not?", "그렇게 하는 것이 옳은가, 옳지 않은가?"], ["Couldn’t he gain something through them?", "그들을 통해 뭔가 얻어 낼 수는 없을까?"], ["In fact hundreds of questions presented themselves.", "요컨대 수백 가지 의문이 떠올랐다."]], [["Andrey Semyonovitch was an anæmic, scrofulous little man, with strangely flaxen mutton-chop whiskers of which he was very proud.", "안드레이 세묘노비치는 빈혈에 걸린, 연주창 기운이 있는 자그마한 사내로, 이상하게 아마빛이 도는 구레나룻을 길렀는데 그것을 무척 자랑스러워했다."], ["He was a clerk and had almost always something wrong with his eyes.", "그는 관청 서기였고, 거의 늘 눈에 무슨 탈이 나 있었다."], ["He was rather soft-hearted, but self-confident and sometimes extremely conceited in speech, which had an absurd effect, incongruous with his little figure.", "마음은 다소 여린 편이었으나, 자신만만했고 때로는 말투가 극도로 거만해서, 그 자그마한 체구와 어울리지 않게 우스꽝스러운 인상을 주었다."], ["He was one of the lodgers most respected by Amalia Ivanovna, for he did not get drunk and paid regularly for his lodgings.", "그는 아말리아 이바노브나가 가장 존중하는 세입자 중 하나였는데, 술에 취하지 않고 셋방세를 꼬박꼬박 냈기 때문이다."], ["Andrey Semyonovitch really was rather stupid; he attached himself to the cause of progress and “our younger generation”", "안드레이 세묘노비치는 정말이지 꽤 멍청했다. 그는 진보의 대의와 '우리의 젊은 세대'에 열정적으로 몸을 던졌다."], ["from enthusiasm. He was one of the numerous and varied legion of dullards, of half-animate abortions, conceited, half-educated coxcombs, who attach themselves to the idea most in fashion only to vulgarise it and who caricature every cause they serve, however sincerely.", "그는 둔재들, 반쯤 살아 있는 미숙아들, 거만하고 어설프게 배운 멋쟁이들로 이루어진 수많고 잡다한 무리 가운데 하나였으니, 그들은 가장 유행하는 사상에 들러붙어 그것을 천박하게 만들 뿐이고, 아무리 진지하게 섬기는 대의라도 죄다 우스꽝스럽게 희화화하고 마는 자들이었다."]], [["Though Lebeziatnikov was so good-natured, he, too, was beginning to dislike Pyotr Petrovitch.", "레베쟈트니코프는 그토록 사람이 좋았으나, 그 역시 표트르 페트로비치를 싫어하기 시작했다."], ["This happened on both sides unconsciously.", "이는 양쪽 모두 자신도 모르게 일어난 일이었다."], ["However simple Andrey Semyonovitch might be, he began to see that Pyotr Petrovitch was duping him and secretly despising him, and that “he was not the right sort of man.”", "안드레이 세묘노비치가 아무리 단순하다 해도, 그는 표트르 페트로비치가 자기를 속이고 속으로는 경멸하고 있으며, '그가 제대로 된 인간이 아니'라는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다."], ["He had tried expounding to him the system of Fourier and the Darwinian theory, but of late Pyotr Petrovitch began to listen too sarcastically and even to be rude.", "그는 그에게 푸리에의 체계와 다윈의 이론을 설명해 보려 했으나, 요즘 들어 표트르 페트로비치는 너무 비웃듯 듣기 시작했고 심지어 무례하게 굴기까지 했다."], ["The fact was he had begun instinctively to guess that Lebeziatnikov was not merely a commonplace simpleton, but, perhaps, a liar, too, and that he had no connections of any consequence even in his own circle, but had simply picked things up third-hand; and that very likely he did not even know much about his own work of propaganda, for he was in too great a muddle.", "사실 그는 본능적으로 레베쟈트니코프가 단순히 평범한 멍청이일 뿐 아니라 어쩌면 거짓말쟁이이기도 하며, 자기가 속한 모임에서조차 이렇다 할 인맥이 없고, 그저 남에게 전해 들은 이야기를 주워 모았을 뿐이라는 것을, 그리고 십중팔구 그가 자기 선전 활동에 대해서조차 잘 알지 못하리라는 것을 — 워낙 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽이었으니 — 짐작하기 시작했던 것이다."], ["A fine person he would be to show anyone up!", "그런 자가 누군가를 폭로하기에 적당한 인물이라니!"], ["It must be noted, by the way, that Pyotr Petrovitch had during those ten days eagerly accepted the strangest praise from Andrey Semyonovitch; he had not protested, for instance, when Andrey Semyonovitch belauded him for being ready to contribute to the establishment of the new “commune,”", "그런데 한 가지 짚고 넘어갈 것은, 표트르 페트로비치가 그 열흘 동안 안드레이 세묘노비치에게서 가장 기이한 칭찬을 기꺼이 받아들였다는 점이다. 이를테면 안드레이 세묘노비치가, 그가 새로운 '코뮌'의 설립에 기꺼이 기여할 준비가 되어 있다거나, 장차 자식들에게 세례를 주지 않겠다거나, 두냐가 결혼 한 달 만에 정부를 둔다 해도 묵인하겠다거나 하는 식으로 그를 추켜세웠을 때도, 그는 항의하지 않았던 것이다."], ["or to abstain from christening his future children, or to acquiesce if Dounia were to take a lover a month after marriage, and so on. Pyotr Petrovitch so enjoyed hearing his own praises that he did not disdain even such virtues when they were attributed to him.", "표트르 페트로비치는 자기에 대한 칭찬을 듣는 것을 어찌나 즐겼는지, 그런 미덕이 자기에게 돌려질 때조차 그것을 마다하지 않았다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch had had occasion that morning to realise some five-per-cent bonds and now he sat down to the table and counted over bundles of notes.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그날 아침 오 푼 이자가 붙는 채권 몇 장을 현금으로 바꿀 일이 있었고, 이제 탁자에 앉아 지폐 다발을 세어 보고 있었다."], ["Andrey Semyonovitch who hardly ever had any money walked about the room pretending to himself to look at all those bank notes with indifference and even contempt.", "거의 한 푼도 없던 안드레이 세묘노비치는 방 안을 거닐면서, 그 모든 지폐를 무관심하게, 심지어 경멸하듯 바라보는 척 스스로에게 꾸미고 있었다."], ["Nothing would have convinced Pyotr Petrovitch that Andrey Semyonovitch could really look on the money unmoved, and the latter, on his side, kept thinking bitterly that Pyotr Petrovitch was capable of entertaining such an idea about him and was, perhaps, glad of the opportunity of teasing his young friend by reminding him of his inferiority and the great difference between them.", "그 무엇으로도 표트르 페트로비치에게, 안드레이 세묘노비치가 정말로 그 돈을 동요 없이 바라볼 수 있으리라는 것을 납득시킬 수는 없었을 것이며, 한편 안드레이 세묘노비치는, 표트르 페트로비치가 자기에 대해 그런 생각을 품을 수 있고, 또 어쩌면 젊은 친구에게 그의 열등함과 둘 사이의 큰 차이를 일깨워 약 올릴 기회를 반기고 있으리라는 생각에 씁쓸한 마음을 누르지 못했다."]], [["He found him incredibly inattentive and irritable, though he, Andrey Semyonovitch, began enlarging on his favourite subject, the foundation of a new special “commune.”", "비록 그, 안드레이 세묘노비치가 자신이 좋아하는 주제인 새로운 특별 '코뮌'의 설립에 관해 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했음에도, 그는 표트르 페트로비치가 믿기 어려울 만큼 건성이고 짜증스러워한다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["The brief remarks that dropped from Pyotr Petrovitch between the clicking of the beads on the reckoning frame betrayed unmistakable and discourteous irony.", "주판알을 딸각이는 사이사이 표트르 페트로비치의 입에서 떨어지는 짤막한 말들에는 누가 봐도 분명한, 무례하기까지 한 빈정거림이 배어 있었다."], ["But the “humane”", "그러나 '인도주의자'"], ["Andrey Semyonovitch ascribed Pyotr Petrovitch’s ill-humour to his recent breach with Dounia and he was burning with impatience to discourse on that theme.", "안드레이 세묘노비치는 표트르 페트로비치의 언짢은 기분을 최근 두냐와의 파혼 탓으로 돌렸고, 바로 그 주제에 관해 이야기하고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["He had something progressive to say on the subject which might console his worthy friend and “could not fail”", "그에게는 그 점에 관해 자기의 훌륭한 친구를 위로해 줄, 그리고 '틀림없이'"], ["to promote his development.", "그의 발전을 도모해 줄 진보적인 할 말이 있었던 것이다."]], [["“There is some sort of festivity being prepared at that...", "\"저쪽에서…… 그 과부 집에서 무슨 잔치인가가 준비되고 있는 모양이지요?\""], ["at the widow’s, isn’t there?” Pyotr Petrovitch asked suddenly, interrupting Andrey Semyonovitch at the most interesting passage.", "표트르 페트로비치가 가장 흥미로운 대목에서 안드레이 세묘노비치의 말을 가로막으며 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Why, don’t you know?", "\"아니, 모르셨어요?"], ["Why, I was telling you last night what I think about all such ceremonies.", "아니, 제가 어젯밤에 그런 의식들에 대해 어떻게 생각하는지 말씀드렸잖아요."], ["And she invited you too, I heard.", "그리고 그 여자가 당신도 초대했다던데요, 들었어요."], ["You were talking to her yesterday...”", "어제 그 여자랑 이야기하셨다면서요…….\""]], [["“I should never have expected that beggarly fool would have spent on this feast all the money she got from that other fool, Raskolnikov.", "\"그 거지 같은 바보 여자가, 또 다른 바보인 라스콜니코프한테서 받은 돈을 죄다 이 잔치에 쏟아부으리라고는 정말 생각도 못 했군."], ["I was surprised just now as I came through at the preparations there, the wines!", "방금 지나오면서 거기 준비해 놓은 걸 보고 깜짝 놀랐어, 포도주들 좀 보게!"], ["Several people are invited.", "사람들도 여럿 초대했더군."], ["It’s beyond everything!”", "도가 지나쳐도 한참 지나쳤어!\""], ["continued Pyotr Petrovitch, who seemed to have some object in pursuing the conversation.", "표트르 페트로비치는 이 대화를 이어 가는 데 무슨 속셈이 있는 듯 계속 말했다."], ["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["You say I am asked too?", "나도 초대받았다고?"], ["When was that?", "그게 언제였지?"], ["I don’t remember.", "기억이 안 나는걸."], ["But I shan’t go.", "하지만 난 안 갈 거야."], ["Why should I?", "내가 왜 가?"], ["I only said a word to her in passing yesterday of the possibility of her obtaining a year’s salary as a destitute widow of a government clerk.", "나는 그저 어제 지나는 길에 그 여자한테 한마디 했을 뿐이야, 관청 서기의 가난한 미망인으로서 일 년치 봉급을 받을 수도 있겠다고."], ["I suppose she has invited me on that account, hasn’t she?", "아마 그 때문에 나를 초대한 거겠지, 안 그래?"], ["He-he-he!”", "헤헤헤!\""]], [["“I don’t intend to go either,”", "\"저도 갈 생각은 없습니다.\""], ["said Lebeziatnikov.", "레베쟈트니코프가 말했다."]], [["“I should think not, after giving her a thrashing!", "\"그렇겠지, 그 여자를 두들겨 팼으니!"], ["You might well hesitate, he-he!”", "망설일 만도 하군, 헤헤!\""]], [["“Who thrashed? Whom?”", "\"누가 두들겨 팼다는 거예요?"], ["cried Lebeziatnikov, flustered and blushing.", "누구를요?\" 레베쟈트니코프가 당황하여 얼굴을 붉히며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Why, you thrashed Katerina Ivanovna a month ago.", "\"아니, 자네가 한 달 전에 카테리나 이바노브나를 두들겨 팼다며."], ["I heard so yesterday...", "어제 그렇게 들었는데……"], ["so that’s what your convictions amount to...", "그러니까 자네의 신념이라는 게 고작 그 정도였군……"], ["and the woman question, too, wasn’t quite sound, he-he-he!”", "그리고 여성 문제도 그리 건전하지는 못했고, 헤헤헤!\""], ["and Pyotr Petrovitch, as though comforted, went back to clicking his beads.", "그러고는 표트르 페트로비치는 위안이라도 얻은 듯 다시 주판알을 딸각거리기 시작했다."]], [["“It’s all slander and nonsense!”", "\"그건 죄다 비방이고 헛소리예요!\""], ["cried Lebeziatnikov, who was always afraid of allusions to the subject.", "그 주제에 대한 암시라면 늘 두려워하던 레베쟈트니코프가 외쳤다."], ["“It was not like that at all, it was quite different.", "\"전혀 그렇지 않았어요, 완전히 달랐다고요."], ["You’ve heard it wrong; it’s a libel.", "잘못 들으신 거예요. 그건 중상모략입니다."], ["I was simply defending myself.", "저는 그저 제 몸을 지켰을 뿐이에요."], ["She rushed at me first with her nails, she pulled out all my whiskers....", "그 여자가 먼저 손톱을 세우고 달려들어서, 제 구레나룻을 죄다 뽑아 버렸다고요……."], ["It’s permissable for anyone, I should hope, to defend himself and I never allow anyone to use violence to me on principle, for it’s an act of despotism.", "누구든 제 몸을 지킬 권리는 있는 것 아니겠어요, 부디 그렇기를 바랍니다만. 그리고 저는 원칙상 누구에게도 제게 폭력을 쓰도록 결코 허락하지 않습니다, 그건 전제專制의 행위니까요."], ["What was I to do?", "그럼 제가 어떻게 했어야 합니까?"], ["I simply pushed her back.”", "저는 그저 그 여자를 밀쳐 냈을 뿐이라고요.\""]], [["“He-he-he!”", "\"헤헤헤!\""], ["Luzhin went on laughing maliciously.", "루진은 짓궂게 계속 웃어 댔다."]], [["“You keep on like that because you are out of humour yourself....", "\"당신은 본인 기분이 언짢으니까 자꾸 그러는 거예요……."], ["But that’s nonsense and it has nothing, nothing whatever to do with the woman question!", "하지만 그건 헛소리고, 여성 문제하고는 아무런, 정말 아무런 상관도 없어요!"], ["You don’t understand; I used to think, indeed, that if women are equal to men in all respects, even in strength (as is maintained now) there ought to be equality in that, too.", "당신은 이해를 못 하시는군요. 사실 저는 여자가 모든 면에서, 심지어 체력에서까지 (요즘 그렇게들 주장하듯) 남자와 동등하다면, 그 점에서도 평등이 있어야 한다고 생각하곤 했어요."], ["Of course, I reflected afterwards that such a question ought not really to arise, for there ought not to be fighting and in the future society fighting is unthinkable...", "물론 나중에 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그런 문제는 사실 제기될 필요조차 없는 것이었지요, 싸움이라는 게 있어서는 안 되고, 미래 사회에서 싸움이란 생각조차 할 수 없는 것이니까요……."], ["and that it would be a queer thing to seek for equality in fighting.", "그러니 싸움에서 평등을 찾겠다는 건 우스운 일이겠지요."], ["I am not so stupid...", "제가 그렇게 멍청하지는 않아요……"], ["though, of course, there is fighting...", "물론 싸움이 있긴 하지만……"], ["there won’t be later, but at present there is...", "나중에는 없어질 테고, 다만 지금은 있다는 거지요……"], ["confound it!", "빌어먹을!"], ["How muddled one gets with you!", "당신하고 얘기하면 이렇게 머리가 뒤죽박죽이 된다니까요!"], ["It’s not on that account that I am not going.", "제가 안 가는 건 그 때문이 아니에요."], ["I am not going on principle, not to take part in the revolting convention of memorial dinners, that’s why!", "저는 원칙상 안 가는 겁니다, 추도 만찬이라는 그 역겨운 관습에 가담하지 않으려고요, 바로 그래서예요!"], ["Though, of course, one might go to laugh at it....", "물론 그걸 비웃으러 갈 수야 있겠지만요……."], ["I am sorry there won’t be any priests at it.", "거기에 사제들이 없을 거라는 게 유감이군요."], ["I should certainly go if there were.”", "사제들이 있다면야 꼭 가겠는데.\""]], [["“Then you would sit down at another man’s table and insult it and those who invited you.", "\"그럼 자네는 남의 식탁에 앉아서 그 식탁과 자네를 초대한 사람들을 모욕하겠다는 거로군."], ["Eh?”", "어때?\""]], [["“Certainly not insult, but protest.", "\"모욕이 아니라 항의지요."], ["I should do it with a good object.", "저는 좋은 목적을 가지고 그렇게 할 겁니다."], ["I might indirectly assist the cause of enlightenment and propaganda.", "저는 간접적으로나마 계몽과 선전의 대의를 도울 수 있을 거예요."], ["It’s a duty of every man to work for enlightenment and propaganda and the more harshly, perhaps, the better.", "계몽과 선전을 위해 일하는 것은 모든 인간의 의무이고, 어쩌면 더 가혹하게 할수록 더 좋을 겁니다."], ["I might drop a seed, an idea....", "저는 씨앗을, 사상을 뿌릴 수 있을 거예요……."], ["And something might grow up from that seed.", "그리고 그 씨앗에서 뭔가가 자라날 수도 있겠지요."], ["How should I be insulting them?", "제가 어떻게 그들을 모욕한단 말입니까?"], ["They might be offended at first, but afterwards they’d see I’d done them a service.", "처음에는 기분이 상할지 몰라도, 나중에는 제가 그들에게 봉사한 거라는 걸 알게 될 거예요."], ["You know, Terebyeva (who is in the community now) was blamed because when she left her family and...", "아시다시피 테레뱌예바는 (지금 코뮌에 있지요) 자기 가족을 떠나 ……"], ["devoted...", "자신을……"], ["herself, she wrote to her father and mother that she wouldn’t go on living conventionally and was entering on a free marriage and it was said that that was too harsh, that she might have spared them and have written more kindly.", "바칠 때, 부모에게 더는 관습적으로 살지 않고 자유 결혼에 들어가겠다고 편지를 썼다는 이유로 비난을 받았어요. 그게 너무 가혹했다느니, 부모를 좀 봐주어 더 다정하게 썼어야 했다느니 하면서요."], ["I think that’s all nonsense and there’s no need of softness; on the contrary, what’s wanted is protest.", "제 생각엔 그건 다 헛소리고 다정할 필요 따위는 없어요. 오히려 필요한 건 항의지요."], ["Varents had been married seven years, she abandoned her two children, she told her husband straight out in a letter: ‘I have realised that I cannot be happy with you.", "바렌츠는 칠 년을 결혼 생활을 했는데, 두 아이를 버리고 남편에게 편지로 단도직입적으로 말했어요. '나는 당신과는 행복할 수 없다는 걸 깨달았어요."], ["I can never forgive you that you have deceived me by concealing from me that there is another organisation of society by means of the communities.", "코뮌을 통한 또 다른 사회 조직이 존재한다는 사실을 내게 숨겨 나를 속인 것을 결코 용서할 수 없어요."], ["I have only lately learned it from a great-hearted man to whom I have given myself and with whom I am establishing a community.", "나는 이 사실을 한 위대한 마음을 지닌 남자에게서 최근에야 알게 되었고, 그에게 나 자신을 바쳐 그와 함께 코뮌을 세우고 있어요."], ["I speak plainly because I consider it dishonest to deceive you.", "당신을 속이는 것은 떳떳하지 못하다고 여기기에 솔직히 말하는 거예요."], ["Do as you think best.", "좋을 대로 하세요."], ["Do not hope to get me back, you are too late.", "나를 되찾을 생각은 마세요, 너무 늦었으니까."], ["I hope you will be happy.’ That’s how letters like that ought to be written!”", "부디 행복하시길 바라요.' 이렇게 그런 편지는 써야 하는 거라고요!\""]], [["“Is that Terebyeva the one you said had made a third free marriage?”", "\"그 테레뱌예바라는 여자가, 자네가 세 번째 자유 결혼을 했다던 그 여자인가?\""]], [["“No, it’s only the second, really!", "\"아니요, 사실은 두 번째일 뿐이에요!"], ["But what if it were the fourth, what if it were the fifteenth, that’s all nonsense!", "하지만 그게 네 번째라 한들, 열다섯 번째라 한들 무슨 상관이에요, 그런 건 다 헛소리지요!"], ["And if ever I regretted the death of my father and mother, it is now, and I sometimes think if my parents were living what a protest I would have aimed at them!", "그리고 제가 부모님의 죽음을 후회한 적이 있다면 바로 지금이에요. 저는 가끔 생각해요, 만약 부모님이 살아 계셨다면 그분들을 향해 얼마나 통렬한 항의를 겨누었을지를!"], ["I would have done something on purpose...", "일부러 무슨 짓이라도 했겠지요……"], ["I would have shown them!", "그분들에게 보여 드렸을 거예요!"], ["I would have astonished them!", "그분들을 깜짝 놀라게 했겠지요!"], ["I am really sorry there is no one!”", "정말이지 그럴 사람이 없다는 게 유감이라니까요!\""]], [["“To surprise!", "\"놀라게 한다라!"], ["He-he!", "헤헤!"], ["Well, be that as you will,”", "뭐, 그건 자네 마음대로 하게나.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch interrupted, “but tell me this; do you know the dead man’s daughter, the delicate-looking little thing?", "표트르 페트로비치가 말을 가로막았다. \"하지만 이것 좀 물어보세, 자네 그 죽은 사내의 딸을 아나, 그 가냘파 보이는 자그마한 처녀 말일세?"], ["It’s true what they say about her, isn’t it?”", "그 처녀에 대해 사람들이 하는 말, 그게 사실이지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“What of it?", "\"그게 어떻다는 거예요?"], ["I think, that is, it is my own personal conviction that this is the normal condition of women.", "제 생각엔, 그러니까 이건 제 개인적인 신념입니다만, 그게 여성의 정상적인 상태예요. 왜 아니겠어요?"], ["Why not? I mean, _distinguons_.", "그러니까 제 말은, 구분해야 한다는 거지요."], ["In our present society it is not altogether normal, because it is compulsory, but in the future society it will be perfectly normal, because it will be voluntary.", "우리의 현재 사회에서야 그게 완전히 정상은 아니지요, 강제된 것이니까요. 하지만 미래 사회에서는 완전히 정상일 거예요, 자발적인 것이 될 테니까요."], ["Even as it is, she was quite right: she was suffering and that was her asset, so to speak, her capital which she had a perfect right to dispose of.", "지금 이대로도 그 처녀는 전적으로 옳았어요. 그녀는 고통받고 있었고 그게 말하자면 그녀의 자산, 그녀가 마음대로 처분할 완전한 권리를 가진 자본이었던 거지요."], ["Of course, in the future society there will be no need of assets, but her part will have another significance, rational and in harmony with her environment.", "물론 미래 사회에서는 자산 따위가 필요 없을 테지만, 그녀의 역할은 다른 의미를, 합리적이고 자기 환경과 조화를 이루는 의미를 갖게 될 거예요."], ["As to Sofya Semyonovna personally, I regard her action as a vigorous protest against the organisation of society, and I respect her deeply for it; I rejoice indeed when I look at her!”", "소피야 세묘노브나 개인으로 말하자면, 저는 그녀의 행동을 사회 조직에 대한 강력한 항의로 여기고, 그 점에서 그녀를 깊이 존경합니다. 그녀를 바라보면 정말이지 기쁘다니까요!\""]], [["“I was told that you got her turned out of these lodgings.”", "\"자네가 그녀를 이 셋집에서 쫓겨나게 했다고 들었네만.\""]], [["Lebeziatnikov was enraged.", "레베쟈트니코프는 격분했다."]], [["“That’s another slander,”", "\"그것도 또 다른 비방이에요.\""], ["he yelled.", "그가 악을 썼다."], ["“It was not so at all!", "\"전혀 그렇지 않았어요!"], ["That was all Katerina Ivanovna’s invention, for she did not understand!", "그건 죄다 카테리나 이바노브나가 지어낸 거라고요, 그 여자가 이해를 못 했으니까요!"], ["And I never made love to Sofya Semyonovna!", "그리고 저는 소피야 세묘노브나에게 결코 수작을 건 적이 없어요!"], ["I was simply developing her, entirely disinterestedly, trying to rouse her to protest....", "저는 그저 그녀를, 전혀 사심 없이 계발시키려 했을 뿐이에요, 그녀를 분발시켜 항의하도록 일깨우려고요……."], ["All I wanted was her protest and Sofya Semyonovna could not have remained here anyway!”", "제가 원한 건 오직 그녀의 항의뿐이었고, 어차피 소피야 세묘노브나는 여기 머물 수 없었을 거예요!\""]], [["“Have you asked her to join your community?”", "\"자네 코뮌에 들어오라고 그녀에게 권했나?\""]], [["“You keep on laughing and very inappropriately, allow me to tell you.", "\"당신은 자꾸 웃기만 하는데, 분명히 말씀드리지만 정말 부적절하군요."], ["You don’t understand!", "당신은 이해를 못 해요!"], ["There is no such rôle in a community.", "코뮌에는 그런 역할 따위는 없어요."], ["The community is established that there should be no such rôles.", "코뮌은 그런 역할이 없어야 한다는 취지로 세워지는 것이니까요."], ["In a community, such a rôle is essentially transformed and what is stupid here is sensible there, what, under present conditions, is unnatural becomes perfectly natural in the community.", "코뮌에서는 그런 역할이 본질적으로 변모되고, 여기서는 어리석은 것이 거기서는 분별 있는 것이 되며, 현재 조건에서 부자연스러운 것이 코뮌에서는 완전히 자연스러운 것이 됩니다."], ["It all depends on the environment.", "모든 게 환경에 달려 있어요."], ["It’s all the environment and man himself is nothing.", "모든 게 환경이고 인간 자신은 아무것도 아니에요."], ["And I am on good terms with Sofya Semyonovna to this day, which is a proof that she never regarded me as having wronged her.", "그리고 저는 지금까지도 소피야 세묘노브나와 사이가 좋은데, 이는 그녀가 결코 저를 자기에게 해를 끼친 사람으로 여기지 않았다는 증거지요."], ["I am trying now to attract her to the community, but on quite, quite a different footing.", "저는 지금 그녀를 코뮌으로 끌어들이려 애쓰고 있어요, 다만 전혀, 전혀 다른 토대 위에서요."], ["What are you laughing at?", "뭘 그렇게 웃으세요?"], ["We are trying to establish a community of our own, a special one, on a broader basis.", "우리는 우리만의 코뮌을, 특별한 코뮌을 더 넓은 토대 위에 세우려 하고 있어요. 우리는 신념에서 더 멀리 나아갔지요."], ["We have gone further in our convictions.", "우리는 더 많은 것을 거부합니다!"], ["We reject more! And meanwhile I’m still developing Sofya Semyonovna.", "그리고 그러는 한편 저는 여전히 소피야 세묘노브나를 계발시키고 있고요."], ["She has a beautiful, beautiful character!”", "그녀는 아름다운, 정말 아름다운 성품을 지녔어요!\""]], [["“And you take advantage of her fine character, eh?", "\"그러면서 자네는 그녀의 그 훌륭한 성품을 이용하는 거로군, 어때?"], ["He-he!”", "헤헤!\""]], [["“No, no!", "\"아니요, 아니에요!"], ["Oh, no!", "오, 아니에요!"], ["On the contrary.”", "오히려 그 반대예요.\""]], [["“Oh, on the contrary!", "\"오, 그 반대라!"], ["He-he-he!", "헤헤헤!"], ["A queer thing to say!”", "거참 묘한 소리를 하는군!\""]], [["“Believe me!", "\"믿어 주세요!"], ["Why should I disguise it?", "제가 뭘 숨기겠어요?"], ["In fact, I feel it strange myself how timid, chaste and modern she is with me!”", "사실 저 자신도 이상하다 싶을 만큼, 그녀는 제 앞에서 소심하고 정숙하고 또 현대적이라니까요!\""]], [["“And you, of course, are developing her...", "\"그래서 자네는 물론 그녀를 계발시키는 거겠지……"], ["he-he!", "헤헤!"], ["trying to prove to her that all that modesty is nonsense?”", "그 모든 정숙함이 헛소리라는 걸 그녀에게 증명하려고?\""]], [["“Not at all, not at all!", "\"천만에요, 천만에!"], ["How coarsely, how stupidly--excuse me saying so--you misunderstand the word development!", "얼마나 거칠게, 얼마나 멍청하게 — 이런 말씀 드려 죄송합니다만 — 당신은 계발이라는 말을 오해하고 있는 거예요!"], ["Good heavens, how... crude you still are!", "맙소사, 당신은 아직도 어쩜 그렇게……"], ["We are striving for the freedom of women and you have only one idea in your head....", "미숙한지! 우리는 여성의 자유를 위해 분투하고 있는데, 당신 머릿속에는 단 한 가지 생각밖에 없군요……."], ["Setting aside the general question of chastity and feminine modesty as useless in themselves and indeed prejudices, I fully accept her chastity with me, because that’s for her to decide.", "정숙함이니 여성의 수치심이니 하는 일반적인 문제는 그 자체로 무익하고 사실은 편견에 불과하니 제쳐 두더라도, 저는 제 앞에서의 그녀의 정숙함을 전적으로 인정합니다, 그건 그녀가 결정할 일이니까요."], ["Of course if she were to tell me herself that she wanted me, I should think myself very lucky, because I like the girl very much; but as it is, no one has ever treated her more courteously than I, with more respect for her dignity...", "물론 만약 그녀가 직접 저를 원한다고 말한다면 저는 스스로를 무척 운이 좋다고 여기겠지요, 그 처녀를 무척 좋아하니까요. 하지만 지금은 어느 누구도 저보다 그녀를 더 정중하게, 그녀의 존엄을 더 존중하며 대한 적이 없어요……"], ["I wait in hopes, that’s all!”", "저는 그저 희망을 품고 기다리는 거지요, 그게 다예요!\""]], [["“You had much better make her a present of something.", "\"자네는 그녀에게 차라리 뭔가 선물이라도 하는 게 훨씬 낫겠군."], ["I bet you never thought of that.”", "장담컨대 그런 생각은 한 번도 못 해 봤을걸.\""]], [["“You don’t understand, as I’ve told you already!", "\"당신은 이해를 못 해요, 이미 말씀드렸잖아요!"], ["Of course, she is in such a position, but it’s another question.", "물론 그녀가 그런 처지에 있긴 하지만, 그건 별개의 문제예요."], ["Quite another question!", "전혀 다른 문제라고요!"], ["You simply despise her.", "당신은 그저 그녀를 경멸하는 거예요."], ["Seeing a fact which you mistakenly consider deserving of contempt, you refuse to take a humane view of a fellow creature.", "당신이 잘못 생각해서 경멸받아 마땅하다고 여기는 하나의 사실을 보고는, 같은 인간을 인도주의적으로 바라보기를 거부하는 거지요."], ["You don’t know what a character she is!", "당신은 그녀가 어떤 성품인지 몰라요!"], ["I am only sorry that of late she has quite given up reading and borrowing books.", "다만 안타까운 건, 그녀가 요즘 들어 책 읽는 것도, 책 빌리는 것도 아예 그만뒀다는 거예요."], ["I used to lend them to her.", "제가 그녀에게 책을 빌려주곤 했거든요."], ["I am sorry, too, that with all the energy and resolution in protesting--which she has already shown once--she has little self-reliance, little, so to say, independence, so as to break free from certain prejudices and certain foolish ideas.", "또 안타까운 건, 항의하는 데 있어 그토록 큰 에너지와 결단력을 — 그녀는 이미 한 번 그걸 보여 줬지요 — 지녔으면서도, 어떤 편견들과 어떤 어리석은 생각들에서 떨쳐 나올 만큼의 자기 확신이, 말하자면 자립심이 부족하다는 거예요."], ["Yet she thoroughly understands some questions, for instance about kissing of hands, that is, that it’s an insult to a woman for a man to kiss her hand, because it’s a sign of inequality.", "그래도 그녀는 어떤 문제들은 철저히 이해하고 있어요, 이를테면 손에 입 맞추는 것에 관한 문제 같은 것 말이지요, 그러니까 남자가 여자의 손에 입 맞추는 건 여자에 대한 모욕이라는 거예요, 그게 불평등의 표시니까요."], ["We had a debate about it and I described it to her.", "우리는 그 문제로 토론을 했고, 제가 그걸 그녀에게 설명해 줬지요."], ["She listened attentively to an account of the workmen’s associations in France, too.", "그녀는 프랑스의 노동자 조합에 관한 이야기도 주의 깊게 들었어요."], ["Now I am explaining the question of coming into the room in the future society.”", "지금은 미래 사회에서 방에 들어가는 문제에 관해 설명하고 있는 중이고요.\""]], [["“And what’s that, pray?”", "\"그게 대체 무슨 문제인데?\""]], [["“We had a debate lately on the question: Has a member of the community the right to enter another member’s room, whether man or woman, at any time...", "\"우리는 최근에 이런 문제로 토론을 했어요. 코뮌의 일원이 남자든 여자든 다른 일원의 방에 언제든 들어갈 권리가 있는가……"], ["and we decided that he has!”", "그래서 우리는 그럴 권리가 있다고 결론을 내렸지요!\""]], [["“It might be at an inconvenient moment, he-he!”", "\"마침 곤란한 순간일 수도 있겠는걸, 헤헤!\""]], [["Lebeziatnikov was really angry.", "레베쟈트니코프는 정말로 화가 났다."]], [["“You are always thinking of something unpleasant,”", "\"당신은 늘 뭔가 불쾌한 것만 생각하는군요.\""], ["he cried with aversion.", "그가 진저리를 치며 외쳤다."], ["“Tfoo!", "\"쳇!"], ["How vexed I am that when I was expounding our system, I referred prematurely to the question of personal privacy!", "우리 체계를 설명하다가 사적인 사생활 문제를 너무 성급하게 언급한 게 어찌나 후회되는지!"], ["It’s always a stumbling-block to people like you, they turn it into ridicule before they understand it.", "그건 늘 당신 같은 사람들에게는 걸림돌이 돼요, 이해하기도 전에 비웃음거리로 만들어 버리지요."], ["And how proud they are of it, too!", "게다가 또 그걸 어찌나 으스대는지!"], ["Tfoo!", "쳇!"], ["I’ve often maintained that that question should not be approached by a novice till he has a firm faith in the system.", "저는 그 문제는 체계에 대한 확고한 믿음이 생기기 전에는 초심자가 접근해서는 안 된다고 누차 주장해 왔어요."], ["And tell me, please, what do you find so shameful even in cesspools?", "그런데 부디 말씀해 보시지요, 당신은 시궁창에 도대체 뭐가 그리 부끄러울 게 있다고 보는 겁니까?"], ["I should be the first to be ready to clean out any cesspool you like.", "저라면 어떤 시궁창이든 가장 먼저 나서서 기꺼이 칠 거예요."], ["And it’s not a question of self-sacrifice, it’s simply work, honourable, useful work which is as good as any other and much better than the work of a Raphael and a Pushkin, because it is more useful.”", "그리고 그건 자기희생의 문제도 아니에요, 그저 노동일 뿐이지요, 그 어떤 일에도 못지않고 라파엘로나 푸시킨의 일보다 훨씬 나은, 명예롭고 유익한 노동 말이에요, 더 유익하니까요.\""]], [["“And more honourable, more honourable, he-he-he!”", "\"게다가 더 명예롭다, 더 명예로워, 헤헤헤!\""]], [["“What do you mean by ‘more honourable’?", "\"'더 명예롭다'는 게 무슨 뜻이에요?"], ["I don’t understand such expressions to describe human activity.", "저는 인간의 활동을 묘사하는 데 그런 표현들은 이해하지 못하겠어요."], ["‘More honourable,’ ‘nobler’--all those are old-fashioned prejudices which I reject.", "'더 명예롭다', '더 고귀하다' — 그런 건 다 제가 거부하는 낡은 편견이에요."], ["Everything which is _of use_ to mankind is honourable.", "인류에게 쓸모 있는 것은 무엇이든 명예로운 거예요."], ["I only understand one word: _useful_!", "저는 단 한 단어만 이해합니다, 유익하다는 말이지요!"], ["You can snigger as much as you like, but that’s so!”", "당신이야 실컷 비웃으셔도 좋지만, 그게 사실이라고요!\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch laughed heartily.", "표트르 페트로비치는 한바탕 큰 소리로 웃었다."], ["He had finished counting the money and was putting it away.", "그는 돈을 다 세어 챙겨 넣은 참이었다."], ["But some of the notes he left on the table.", "그러나 지폐 몇 장은 탁자 위에 그대로 두었다."], ["The “cesspool question” had already been a subject of dispute between them.", "'시궁창 문제'는 이미 두 사람 사이에서 논쟁의 대상이 된 적이 있었다."], ["What was absurd was that it made Lebeziatnikov really angry, while it amused Luzhin and at that moment he particularly wanted to anger his young friend.", "우스운 것은, 그 문제가 레베쟈트니코프를 정말로 화나게 한다는 점이었고, 그것이 루진을 즐겁게 하던 차에, 마침 그 순간 그는 유난히 젊은 친구를 화나게 하고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["“It’s your ill-luck yesterday that makes you so ill-humoured and annoying,” blurted out Lebeziatnikov, who in spite of his “independence”", "\"어제 운이 나빴던 탓에 당신이 그렇게 기분이 언짢고 짓궂게 구는 거예요.\""], ["and his “protests” did not venture to oppose Pyotr Petrovitch and still behaved to him with some of the respect habitual in earlier years.", "레베쟈트니코프가 불쑥 내뱉었는데, 그는 자신의 '자립'과 '항의'에도 불구하고 감히 표트르 페트로비치에게 대놓고 맞서지는 못했고, 여전히 이전 시절의 버릇이 밴 어느 정도의 존경심을 가지고 그를 대하고 있었다."]], [["“You’d better tell me this,”", "\"자네 차라리 이걸 말해 주게.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch interrupted with haughty displeasure, “can you...", "표트르 페트로비치가 거만한 불쾌함을 드러내며 말을 가로막았다."], ["or rather are you really friendly enough with that young person to ask her to step in here for a minute?", "\"자네…… 아니 그보다, 자네 정말로 그 젊은 처자와 충분히 친해서, 잠깐 여기 들르라고 청할 수 있겠나?"], ["I think they’ve all come back from the cemetery...", "다들 묘지에서 돌아온 것 같던데……"], ["I heard the sound of steps...", "발소리가 들렸어……."], ["I want to see her, that young person.”", "그 처자를 좀 만나고 싶네.\""]], [["“What for?”", "\"무엇 때문에요?\""], ["Lebeziatnikov asked with surprise.", "레베쟈트니코프가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“Oh, I want to.", "\"아, 그냥 만나고 싶어서."], ["I am leaving here to-day or to-morrow and therefore I wanted to speak to her about...", "나는 오늘이나 내일 여기를 떠날 텐데, 그래서 그녀에게 …… 에 관해 이야기하고 싶어서 말이야."], ["However, you may be present during the interview.", "그래도 자네가 면담 자리에 함께 있어도 좋네."], ["It’s better you should be, indeed.", "사실 그러는 편이 더 낫겠어."], ["For there’s no knowing what you might imagine.”", "자네가 무슨 상상을 할지 알 수 없으니 말이야.\""]], [["“I shan’t imagine anything.", "\"저는 아무것도 상상하지 않을 거예요."], ["I only asked and, if you’ve anything to say to her, nothing is easier than to call her in.", "그저 물어본 것뿐이고, 그녀에게 하실 말씀이 있다면 그녀를 불러오는 것보다 쉬운 일은 없지요."], ["I’ll go directly and you may be sure I won’t be in your way.”", "제가 곧장 가서 데려올게요, 그리고 제가 결코 방해되지 않을 거라고 믿으셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["Five minutes later Lebeziatnikov came in with Sonia.", "오 분 후 레베쟈트니코프는 소냐와 함께 들어왔다."], ["She came in very much surprised and overcome with shyness as usual.", "그녀는 무척 놀란 채로, 늘 그렇듯 수줍음에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 들어왔다."], ["She was always shy in such circumstances and was always afraid of new people, she had been as a child and was even more so now....", "그녀는 이런 상황에서면 늘 수줍어했고 낯선 사람을 늘 두려워했는데, 어릴 적에도 그랬고 지금은 더더욱 그랬다……."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch met her “politely and affably,”", "표트르 페트로비치는 그녀를 '정중하고 상냥하게'"], ["but with a certain shade of bantering familiarity which in his opinion was suitable for a man of his respectability and weight in dealing with a creature so young and so _interesting_ as she.", "맞이했으나, 그녀처럼 어리고 흥미로운 존재를 상대할 때 자기 같은 점잖고 비중 있는 사람에게 어울린다고 여기는, 어딘가 놀리는 듯한 친밀함의 기색이 묻어 있었다. 그는 서둘러 그녀를 '안심시키고'"], ["He hastened to “reassure”", "탁자에서 자기 맞은편에 앉혔다."], ["her and made her sit down facing him at the table. Sonia sat down, looked about her--at Lebeziatnikov, at the notes lying on the table and then again at Pyotr Petrovitch and her eyes remained riveted on him.", "소냐는 자리에 앉아 주위를 둘러보았다 — 레베쟈트니코프를, 탁자 위에 놓인 지폐들을, 그러고 나서 다시 표트르 페트로비치를 — 그리고 그녀의 두 눈은 그에게 못 박힌 채 떨어지지 않았다."], ["Lebeziatnikov was moving to the door.", "레베쟈트니코프가 문 쪽으로 걸어가려 했다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch signed to Sonia to remain seated and stopped Lebeziatnikov.", "표트르 페트로비치는 소냐에게 그대로 앉아 있으라고 손짓하고는 레베쟈트니코프를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Is Raskolnikov in there?", "\"라스콜니코프가 저 안에 있나?"], ["Has he come?”", "그가 왔어?\""], ["he asked him in a whisper.", "그가 그에게 속삭이듯 물었다."]], [["“Raskolnikov?", "\"라스콜니코프요?"], ["Yes.", "네."], ["Why?", "왜요?"], ["Yes, he is there.", "네, 거기 있어요."], ["I saw him just come in....", "방금 들어오는 걸 봤는데요……."], ["Why?”", "왜요?\""]], [["“Well, I particularly beg you to remain here with us and not to leave me alone with this...", "\"음, 자네에게 특별히 부탁하건대 여기 우리와 함께 있어 주고, 이…… 젊은 여자와 나만 단둘이 남게 하지 말아 주게."], ["young woman. I only want a few words with her, but God knows what they may make of it.", "나는 그저 그녀와 몇 마디 나누고 싶을 뿐이지만, 사람들이 그걸 어떻게 받아들일지 누가 알겠나."], ["I shouldn’t like Raskolnikov to repeat anything....", "라스콜니코프가 무슨 이야기든 옮기는 건 바라지 않아서 말이야……."], ["You understand what I mean?”", "내 말뜻을 알겠나?\""]], [["“I understand!”", "\"알겠어요!\""], ["Lebeziatnikov saw the point.", "레베쟈트니코프는 요점을 알아챘다."], ["“Yes, you are right....", "\"네, 당신 말이 맞아요……."], ["Of course, I am convinced personally that you have no reason to be uneasy, but...", "물론 개인적으로 저는 당신이 불안해할 이유가 없다고 확신하지만……"], ["still, you are right.", "그래도 당신 말이 옳아요."], ["Certainly I’ll stay.", "분명히 제가 남아 있겠습니다."], ["I’ll stand here at the window and not be in your way...", "여기 창가에 서서 방해되지 않게 하지요……"], ["I think you are right...”", "당신 말이 옳은 것 같아요…….\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch returned to the sofa, sat down opposite Sonia, looked attentively at her and assumed an extremely dignified, even severe expression, as much as to say, “don’t you make any mistake, madam.”", "표트르 페트로비치는 소파로 돌아와 소냐의 맞은편에 앉아 그녀를 주의 깊게 바라보더니, 극도로 위엄 있는, 심지어 엄숙하기까지 한 표정을 지었으니, 마치 \"공연한 착각은 하지 마시오, 아가씨\"라고 말하는 듯했다."], ["Sonia was overwhelmed with embarrassment.", "소냐는 당혹감에 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."]], [["“In the first place, Sofya Semyonovna, will you make my excuses to your respected mamma....", "\"우선 말이오, 소피야 세묘노브나, 당신의 존경하는 어머님께 내 사과의 말을 전해 주겠소……."], ["That’s right, isn’t it?", "그게 맞지요, 안 그렇소?"], ["Katerina Ivanovna stands in the place of a mother to you?”", "카테리나 이바노브나가 당신에게는 어머니 자리를 대신하고 있으니 말이오?\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch began with great dignity, though affably.", "표트르 페트로비치는 상냥하게나마 대단한 위엄을 갖추고 운을 뗐다."]], [["It was evident that his intentions were friendly.", "그의 의도가 우호적이라는 것은 분명해 보였다."]], [["“Quite so, yes; the place of a mother,”", "\"네, 그래요. 어머니 자리를 대신하고 계세요.\""], ["Sonia answered, timidly and hurriedly.", "소냐가 소심하고 황급하게 대답했다."]], [["“Then will you make my apologies to her?", "\"그러면 그분께 내 사과의 말을 전해 주겠소?"], ["Through inevitable circumstances I am forced to be absent and shall not be at the dinner in spite of your mamma’s kind invitation.”", "피치 못할 사정으로 인해 나는 자리를 비울 수밖에 없게 되어, 당신 어머님의 친절한 초대에도 불구하고 만찬에 참석하지 못하게 되었소.\""]], [["“Yes...", "\"네……"], ["I’ll tell her...", "전해 드릴게요……"], ["at once.”", "곧장요.\""]], [["And Sonia hastily jumped up from her seat.", "그러고는 소냐가 황급히 자리에서 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Wait, that’s not all,”", "\"기다리시오, 그게 다가 아니오.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch detained her, smiling at her simplicity and ignorance of good manners, “and you know me little, my dear Sofya Semyonovna, if you suppose I would have ventured to trouble a person like you for a matter of so little consequence affecting myself only.", "표트르 페트로비치가 그녀의 순진함과 예법에 대한 무지에 미소 지으며 그녀를 붙들었다. \"그리고 친애하는 소피야 세묘노브나, 만약 당신이, 내가 나 자신에게만 관계되는 이런 사소한 일로 당신 같은 사람을 굳이 번거롭게 했으리라 여긴다면, 당신은 나를 잘 모르는 거요."], ["I have another object.”", "내게는 또 다른 목적이 있소.\""]], [["Sonia sat down hurriedly.", "소냐는 황급히 자리에 앉았다."], ["Her eyes rested again for an instant on the grey-and-rainbow-coloured notes that remained on the table, but she quickly looked away and fixed her eyes on Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그녀의 두 눈은 다시 한순간 탁자 위에 남아 있는 회색과 무지갯빛 지폐에 머물렀으나, 그녀는 얼른 눈길을 돌려 표트르 페트로비치에게 고정했다."], ["She felt it horribly indecorous, especially for _her_, to look at another person’s money.", "그녀는, 특히 자기 같은 사람이 남의 돈을 바라보는 것이 끔찍이도 무례한 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["She stared at the gold eye-glass which Pyotr Petrovitch held in his left hand and at the massive and extremely handsome ring with a yellow stone on his middle finger.", "그녀는 표트르 페트로비치가 왼손에 들고 있던 금테 안경과, 그의 가운뎃손가락에 끼워진, 노란 보석이 박힌 묵직하고 무척 멋진 반지를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["But suddenly she looked away and, not knowing where to turn, ended by staring Pyotr Petrovitch again straight in the face.", "그러나 갑자기 그녀는 눈길을 돌렸고, 어디를 봐야 할지 몰라 결국 다시 표트르 페트로비치의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 말았다."], ["After a pause of still greater dignity he continued.", "한층 더 위엄 있게 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“I chanced yesterday in passing to exchange a couple of words with Katerina Ivanovna, poor woman.", "\"나는 어제 우연히 지나는 길에 카테리나 이바노브나와, 그 가엾은 여인과 두어 마디 말을 나누었소."], ["That was sufficient to enable me to ascertain that she is in a position--preternatural, if one may so express it.”", "그것만으로도 그녀가, 이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 비정상적인 처지에 놓여 있음을 충분히 알 수 있었소.\""]], [["“Yes...", "\"네……"], ["preternatural...”", "비정상적이지요…….\""], ["Sonia hurriedly assented.", "소냐가 황급히 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“Or it would be simpler and more comprehensible to say, ill.”", "\"아니, 더 간단하고 알기 쉽게 말하자면, 병들었다고 하는 게 낫겠소.\""]], [["“Yes, simpler and more comprehen...", "\"네, 더 간단하고 알기 쉬……"], ["yes, ill.”", "네, 병들었어요.\""]], [["“Quite so.", "\"그렇소."], ["So then from a feeling of humanity and so to speak compassion, I should be glad to be of service to her in any way, foreseeing her unfortunate position.", "그러니 인도주의적인 감정에서, 말하자면 동정심에서, 나는 그녀의 불행한 처지를 내다보고 어떻게든 그녀에게 도움이 되어 드리고 싶소."], ["I believe the whole of this poverty-stricken family depends now entirely on you?”", "이 가난에 찌든 가족 전체가 지금은 전적으로 당신에게 의지하고 있는 것 같은데, 맞소?\""]], [["“Allow me to ask,”", "\"여쭤봐도 될까요.\""], ["Sonia rose to her feet, “did you say something to her yesterday of the possibility of a pension?", "소냐가 자리에서 일어났다. \"어제 그분께 연금을 받을 수 있을지도 모른다는 말씀을 하셨나요?"], ["Because she told me you had undertaken to get her one.", "그분이 제게 당신이 연금을 받게 해 주기로 했다고 말씀하셨거든요."], ["Was that true?”", "그게 사실인가요?\""]], [["“Not in the slightest, and indeed it’s an absurdity!", "\"천만에, 게다가 그건 정말 터무니없는 소리요!"], ["I merely hinted at her obtaining temporary assistance as the widow of an official who had died in the service--if only she has patronage...", "나는 그저 그녀가, 재직 중에 사망한 관리의 미망인으로서 임시 보조금을 받을 수도 있겠다고 넌지시 비쳤을 뿐이오 — 그것도 후원자가 있어야만 가능한 일이지만……."], ["but apparently your late parent had not served his full term and had not indeed been in the service at all of late.", "한데 보아하니 당신의 돌아가신 아버님은 근무 연한을 다 채우지도 못했고, 사실 최근에는 아예 근무도 하지 않으셨더군."], ["In fact, if there could be any hope, it would be very ephemeral, because there would be no claim for assistance in that case, far from it....", "요컨대 무슨 희망이 있다 한들 그건 무척 덧없는 것이오, 그런 경우에는 보조금을 청구할 근거가 전혀 없으니, 어림도 없는 일이지요……."], ["And she is dreaming of a pension already, he-he-he!...", "그런데 벌써 연금을 꿈꾸고 있다니, 헤헤헤!……"], ["A go-ahead lady!”", "성미도 급한 부인이로군!\""]], [["“Yes, she is.", "\"네, 그래요."], ["For she is credulous and good-hearted, and she believes everything from the goodness of her heart and...", "그분은 잘 믿고 마음이 착해서, 마음씨가 고운 나머지 무엇이든 다 믿어 버리거든요, 그리고……"], ["and...", "또……"], ["and she is like that...", "그분은 원래 그런 분이세요……"], ["yes...", "네……."], ["You must excuse her,”", "그분을 너그러이 봐주셔야 해요.\""], ["said Sonia, and again she got up to go.", "소냐가 말하고는 다시 가려고 일어섰다."]], [["“But you haven’t heard what I have to say.”", "\"한데 당신은 아직 내가 할 말을 듣지도 않았소.\""]], [["“No, I haven’t heard,”", "\"네, 못 들었어요.\""], ["muttered Sonia.", "소냐가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Then sit down.”", "\"그럼 앉으시오.\""], ["She was terribly confused; she sat down again a third time.", "그녀는 끔찍이도 당황했다. 세 번째로 그녀는 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Seeing her position with her unfortunate little ones, I should be glad, as I have said before, so far as lies in my power, to be of service, that is, so far as is in my power, not more.", "\"그녀의 불행한 어린것들과 함께 처한 그 처지를 보아하니, 아까도 말했듯이, 나는 내 힘이 닿는 한 기꺼이 도움이 되어 드리고 싶소, 그러니까 내 힘이 닿는 한에서지, 그 이상은 아니오."], ["One might for instance get up a subscription for her, or a lottery, something of the sort, such as is always arranged in such cases by friends or even outsiders desirous of assisting people.", "이를테면 그녀를 위해 모금을 한다거나, 복권 같은 것을, 그런 부류의 무언가를 마련할 수도 있겠지요, 이런 경우에 친구들이나 심지어 남을 돕고자 하는 외부인들이 늘 그렇게 하듯 말이오."], ["It was of that I intended to speak to you; it might be done.”", "바로 그 일에 관해 당신에게 이야기하려던 것이오. 해 볼 만한 일이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes...", "\"네, 네……"], ["God will repay you for it,”", "하느님께서 그 일을 갚아 주실 거예요.\""], ["faltered Sonia, gazing intently at Pyotr Petrovitch.", "소냐가 표트르 페트로비치를 뚫어지게 바라보며 더듬더듬 말했다."]], [["“It might be, but we will talk of it later.", "\"해 볼 수야 있지만, 그건 나중에 이야기합시다."], ["We might begin it to-day, we will talk it over this evening and lay the foundation so to speak.", "오늘 당장 시작해도 되겠소, 오늘 저녁에 그 일을 의논하고 말하자면 그 토대를 마련합시다."], ["Come to me at seven o’clock.", "일곱 시에 내게 오시오."], ["Mr. Lebeziatnikov, I hope, will assist us.", "레베쟈트니코프 씨도 우리를 도와주실 거라 믿소."], ["But there is one circumstance of which I ought to warn you beforehand and for which I venture to trouble you, Sofya Semyonovna, to come here.", "한데 한 가지 사정이 있어 미리 당신에게 일러두어야겠고, 그래서 소피야 세묘노브나, 감히 당신을 번거롭게 하여 여기로 오시라 한 것이오."], ["In my opinion money cannot be, indeed it’s unsafe to put it into Katerina Ivanovna’s own hands.", "내 생각에 돈을, 정말이지 카테리나 이바노브나 본인의 손에 쥐여 주는 것은 위험하오."], ["The dinner to-day is a proof of that.", "오늘의 만찬이 그 증거지요."], ["Though she has not, so to speak, a crust of bread for to-morrow and...", "그녀에게는, 말하자면, 내일 먹을 빵 한 조각도 없고……"], ["well, boots or shoes, or anything; she has bought to-day Jamaica rum, and even, I believe, Madeira and... and coffee.", "음, 장화든 신발이든, 아무것도 없는데, 오늘 자메이카 럼주를 사고, 게다가 내 생각엔 마데이라 포도주에……"], ["I saw it as I passed through.", "그리고 커피까지 산 것 같소."], ["To-morrow it will all fall upon you again, they won’t have a crust of bread.", "지나오면서 봤지요. 내일이면 그 모든 게 다시 당신에게 떨어질 테고, 그들에겐 빵 한 조각도 없을 거요."], ["It’s absurd, really, and so, to my thinking, a subscription ought to be raised so that the unhappy widow should not know of the money, but only you, for instance.", "정말이지 터무니없는 일이오. 그러니 내 생각에는, 그 불행한 미망인이 돈에 대해 알지 못하도록, 이를테면 오직 당신만 알도록 모금을 하는 게 좋겠소."], ["Am I right?”", "내 말이 맞소?\""]], [["“I don’t know...", "\"잘 모르겠어요……"], ["this is only to-day, once in her life....", "이건 오늘뿐이에요, 평생에 한 번……."], ["She was so anxious to do honour, to celebrate the memory....", "그분은 그토록 예를 갖추고, 추모를 기리고 싶어 하셨어요……."], ["And she is very sensible...", "그리고 그분은 무척 분별 있는 분이에요……"], ["but just as you think and I shall be very, very...", "하지만 당신 생각대로 하세요, 그러면 저는 정말, 정말로……"], ["they will all be...", "그들도 모두……"], ["and God will reward...", "그리고 하느님께서 갚아 주실 거고……"], ["and the orphans...”", "또 저 고아들도…….\""]], [["Sonia burst into tears.", "소냐가 와락 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Very well, then, keep it in mind; and now will you accept for the benefit of your relation the small sum that I am able to spare, from me personally.", "\"그럼 좋소, 그 점을 명심해 두시오. 그리고 이제 당신 친척을 위해, 내가 개인적으로, 내가 마련할 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 돈을 받아 주겠소."], ["I am very anxious that my name should not be mentioned in connection with it.", "나는 이 일에 내 이름이 거론되지 않기를 무척 바라오. 자, 여기……"], ["Here... having so to speak anxieties of my own, I cannot do more...”", "나도 말하자면 나름의 걱정거리가 있어서, 더는 못 하오…….\""]], [["And Pyotr Petrovitch held out to Sonia a ten-rouble note carefully unfolded.", "그러고는 표트르 페트로비치가 소냐에게 조심스레 펼친 십 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 내밀었다."], ["Sonia took it, flushed crimson, jumped up, muttered something and began taking leave.", "소냐는 그것을 받아 들고는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 벌떡 일어나, 뭔가를 중얼거리고는 작별 인사를 하기 시작했다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch accompanied her ceremoniously to the door.", "표트르 페트로비치는 의례적으로 그녀를 문까지 배웅했다."], ["She got out of the room at last, agitated and distressed, and returned to Katerina Ivanovna, overwhelmed with confusion.", "그녀는 마침내 동요하고 괴로워하며 방을 빠져나와, 당혹감에 어쩔 줄 모른 채 카테리나 이바노브나에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["All this time Lebeziatnikov had stood at the window or walked about the room, anxious not to interrupt the conversation; when Sonia had gone he walked up to Pyotr Petrovitch and solemnly held out his hand.", "이 모든 시간 동안 레베쟈트니코프는 창가에 서 있거나 방 안을 거닐며 대화를 방해하지 않으려 신경 썼다. 소냐가 가 버리자 그는 표트르 페트로비치에게 다가가 엄숙하게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“I heard and _saw_ everything,”", "\"저는 모든 걸 듣고 또 보았어요.\""], ["he said, laying stress on the last verb.", "그가 마지막 동사에 힘주어 말했다."], ["“That is honourable, I mean to say, it’s humane! You wanted to avoid gratitude, I saw!", "\"그건 명예로운 일이에요, 그러니까 제 말은, 그건 인도주의적이라는 거지요!"], ["And although I cannot, I confess, in principle sympathise with private charity, for it not only fails to eradicate the evil but even promotes it, yet I must admit that I saw your action with pleasure--yes, yes, I like it.”", "당신은 감사받는 걸 피하려 하셨어요, 저는 봤다고요! 그리고 솔직히 저는 원칙상 사적인 자선에는 공감할 수 없지만, 그게 악을 뿌리 뽑기는커녕 오히려 조장하기까지 하니까요, 그래도 당신의 행동을 기쁘게 지켜봤다는 건 인정해야겠어요 — 네, 네, 마음에 들어요.\""]], [["“That’s all nonsense,”", "\"그건 다 헛소릴세.\""], ["muttered Pyotr Petrovitch, somewhat disconcerted, looking carefully at Lebeziatnikov.", "표트르 페트로비치가 다소 당황하여 레베쟈트니코프를 유심히 바라보며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“No, it’s not nonsense!", "\"아니요, 헛소리가 아니에요!"], ["A man who has suffered distress and annoyance as you did yesterday and who yet can sympathise with the misery of others, such a man...", "어제 당신처럼 곤경과 괴로움을 겪고도 여전히 남의 불행에 공감할 수 있는 사람, 그런 사람은……"], ["even though he is making a social mistake--is still deserving of respect!", "비록 사회적 오류를 범하고 있다 해도…… 여전히 존경받을 만하지요!"], ["I did not expect it indeed of you, Pyotr Petrovitch, especially as according to your ideas...", "정말이지 당신에게서 그런 걸 기대하지 못했어요, 표트르 페트로비치, 특히 당신의 사상에 따르면……"], ["oh, what a drawback your ideas are to you!", "오, 당신의 사상이 당신에게 얼마나 걸림돌이 되는지!"], ["How distressed you are for instance by your ill-luck yesterday,”", "이를테면 어제의 불운에 당신이 얼마나 괴로워하는지 좀 보세요.\""], ["cried the simple-hearted Lebeziatnikov, who felt a return of affection for Pyotr Petrovitch.", "표트르 페트로비치에게 다시 애정이 솟구치는 것을 느낀 마음씨 순박한 레베쟈트니코프가 외쳤다."], ["“And, what do you want with marriage, with _legal_ marriage, my dear, noble Pyotr Petrovitch?", "\"그런데 당신은 결혼을, 그 합법적인 결혼을 도대체 왜 하려는 거예요, 친애하는, 고결한 표트르 페트로비치?"], ["Why do you cling to this _legality_ of marriage?", "어째서 결혼의 그 합법성에 그토록 매달리는 거예요?"], ["Well, you may beat me if you like, but I am glad, positively glad it hasn’t come off, that you are free, that you are not quite lost for humanity....", "자, 원하시면 저를 때리셔도 좋지만, 저는 기뻐요, 그 결혼이 성사되지 않은 게 정말로 기쁘다고요, 당신이 자유로워졌다는 게, 당신이 인류에게 완전히 못 쓸 사람은 아니라는 게요……."], ["you see, I’ve spoken my mind!”", "보세요, 제가 속마음을 다 털어놓았어요!\""]], [["“Because I don’t want in your free marriage to be made a fool of and to bring up another man’s children, that’s why I want legal marriage,”", "\"내가 자네의 그 자유 결혼에서 바보 취급을 당하고 남의 자식을 키우고 싶지 않으니까, 그래서 합법적인 결혼을 하려는 걸세.\""], ["Luzhin replied in order to make some answer.", "루진이 그저 무슨 대답이라도 하려고 응수했다."]], [["He seemed preoccupied by something.", "그는 무언가에 골몰해 있는 듯했다."]], [["“Children?", "\"자식이요?"], ["You referred to children,”", "자식 이야기를 하셨군요.\""], ["Lebeziatnikov started off like a warhorse at the trumpet call.", "레베쟈트니코프가 나팔 소리를 들은 군마처럼 펄쩍 뛰며 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Children are a social question and a question of first importance, I agree; but the question of children has another solution.", "\"자식은 사회적 문제이고, 또 더없이 중요한 문제예요, 그건 동의해요. 하지만 자식 문제에는 또 다른 해결책이 있어요."], ["Some refuse to have children altogether, because they suggest the institution of the family.", "어떤 이들은 아예 자식 갖기를 거부하지요, 그게 가족이라는 제도를 떠올리게 하니까요."], ["We’ll speak of children later, but now as to the question of honour, I confess that’s my weak point.", "자식 이야기는 나중에 하기로 하고, 지금은 명예 문제에 관해서라면, 솔직히 그건 제 약점이라는 걸 인정합니다."], ["That horrid, military, Pushkin expression is unthinkable in the dictionary of the future.", "그 끔찍하고 군대식인, 푸시킨식 표현은 미래의 사전에서는 생각조차 할 수 없는 거예요."], ["What does it mean indeed?", "그게 도대체 무슨 뜻이에요?"], ["It’s nonsense, there will be no deception in a free marriage!", "그건 헛소리지요, 자유 결혼에는 속임수란 없을 테니까요!"], ["That is only the natural consequence of a legal marriage, so to say, its corrective, a protest.", "그건 그저 합법적 결혼의 자연스러운 결과, 말하자면 그것의 교정책, 항의일 뿐이에요."], ["So that indeed it’s not humiliating...", "그러니 사실 그건 굴욕적인 일이 아니지요……"], ["and if I ever, to suppose an absurdity, were to be legally married, I should be positively glad of it.", "그리고 만약 제가 언젠가, 터무니없는 가정입니다만, 합법적으로 결혼하게 된다면, 저는 오히려 그걸 기뻐할 거예요."], ["I should say to my wife: ‘My dear, hitherto I have loved you, now I respect you, for you’ve shown you can protest!’ You laugh!", "저는 제 아내에게 말하겠어요. '여보, 지금까지 나는 당신을 사랑했지만, 이제 나는 당신을 존경하오, 당신이 항의할 줄 안다는 걸 보여 줬으니 말이오!'"], ["That’s because you are incapable of getting away from prejudices.", "웃으시는군요! 그건 당신이 편견에서 벗어나지 못하기 때문이에요."], ["Confound it all!", "빌어먹을!"], ["I understand now where the unpleasantness is of being deceived in a legal marriage, but it’s simply a despicable consequence of a despicable position in which both are humiliated.", "이제 저는 합법적 결혼에서 속는다는 게 어디가 불쾌한 일인지 알겠어요, 하지만 그건 그저, 둘 다 굴욕을 당하는 비열한 처지에서 비롯되는 비열한 결과일 뿐이에요."], ["When the deception is open, as in a free marriage, then it does not exist, it’s unthinkable.", "자유 결혼에서처럼 속임수가 공공연하다면, 그건 존재하지 않는 거예요, 생각조차 할 수 없는 거지요."], ["Your wife will only prove how she respects you by considering you incapable of opposing her happiness and avenging yourself on her for her new husband.", "당신의 아내는, 당신이 그녀의 행복을 가로막거나 그녀의 새 남편 때문에 그녀에게 복수할 수 없는 사람이라고 여김으로써, 오히려 자기가 당신을 얼마나 존경하는지를 증명할 뿐이에요."], ["Damn it all!", "빌어먹을!"], ["I sometimes dream if I were to be married, pfoo!", "저는 가끔 꿈꾼답니다, 만약 제가 결혼하게 된다면, 푸!"], ["I mean if I were to marry, legally or not, it’s just the same, I should present my wife with a lover if she had not found one for herself.", "그러니까 만약 제가, 합법적이든 아니든, 그건 매한가지지요, 결혼하게 된다면, 저는 제 아내가 직접 정부를 찾지 못했을 경우 제가 그녀에게 정부를 마련해 줄 거예요. '여보,'"], ["‘My dear,’ I should say, ‘I love you, but even more than that I desire you to respect me.", "하고 저는 말하겠지요, '나는 당신을 사랑하지만, 그보다 더, 나는 당신이 나를 존경해 주기를 바라오. 자, 보시오!'"], ["See!’ Am I not right?”", "제 말이 맞지 않나요?\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch sniggered as he listened, but without much merriment.", "표트르 페트로비치는 들으면서 낄낄 웃었으나, 그다지 즐거워하지는 않았다."], ["He hardly heard it indeed.", "사실 그는 그 말을 거의 듣고 있지도 않았다."], ["He was preoccupied with something else and even Lebeziatnikov at last noticed it.", "그는 다른 무언가에 골몰해 있었고, 마침내 레베쟈트니코프조차 그것을 알아챘다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch seemed excited and rubbed his hands.", "표트르 페트로비치는 흥분한 듯 보였고 두 손을 비벼 댔다."], ["Lebeziatnikov remembered all this and reflected upon it afterwards.", "레베쟈트니코프는 이 모든 것을 기억해 두었고 나중에 그것을 곰곰이 되새겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["It would be difficult to explain exactly what could have originated the idea of that senseless dinner in Katerina Ivanovna’s disordered brain.", "카테리나 이바노브나의 어지러운 머릿속에서 도대체 무엇이 그 어처구니없는 만찬이라는 생각을 불러일으켰는지를 정확히 설명하기란 어려운 일이다."], ["Nearly ten of the twenty roubles, given by Raskolnikov for Marmeladov’s funeral, were wasted upon it.", "마르멜라도프의 장례를 위해 라스콜니코프가 건넨 이십 루블 가운데 거의 십 루블이 그 일에 탕진되었다."], ["Possibly Katerina Ivanovna felt obliged to honour the memory of the deceased “suitably,”", "어쩌면 카테리나 이바노브나는 고인의 명복을 “격식에 맞게”"], ["that all the lodgers, and still more Amalia Ivanovna, might know “that he was in no way their inferior, and perhaps very much their superior,”", "빌어야 한다고, 그래서 모든 하숙인들이, 그리고 누구보다 아말리아 이바노브나가 “그가 결코 그들보다 못한 사람이 아니며, 어쩌면 그들보다 훨씬 윗사람이었다”는 것을, 그리고 누구도 “그를 깔볼” 권리가 없다는 것을 알아야 한다고 여겼는지도 모른다."], ["and that no one had the right “to turn up his nose at him.”", "어쩌면 그 핵심은 그 특유의 “가난뱅이의 자존심”이었으리라."], ["Perhaps the chief element was that peculiar “poor man’s pride,”", "그것은 많은 가난한 이들로 하여금 그저 “남들처럼”"], ["which compels many poor people to spend their last savings on some traditional social ceremony, simply in order to do “like other people,”", "하기 위해, 그리고 “업신여김을 당하지” 않기 위해, 어떤 전통적인 사회적 의례에 마지막 한 푼까지 쏟아붓게 만드는 것이다."], ["and not to “be looked down upon.” It is very probable, too, that Katerina Ivanovna longed on this occasion, at the moment when she seemed to be abandoned by everyone, to show those “wretched contemptible lodgers”", "또한 이 기회에, 모두에게 버림받은 것만 같았던 그 순간에, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그 “비참하고 경멸스러운 하숙인들”에게 자신이 “어떻게 일을 처리하는지, 어떻게 손님을 대접하는지”를 알고 있으며, 자신이 “점잖은, 거의 귀족적이라 해도 좋을 대령 가문에서”"], ["that she knew “how to do things, how to entertain” and that she had been brought up “in a genteel, she might almost say aristocratic colonel’s family”", "자랐으며, 바닥을 쓸고 밤마다 아이들의 누더기를 빨라고 태어난 사람이 결코 아님을 보여 주고 싶어 안달했을 가능성도 매우 높다."], ["and had not been meant for sweeping floors and washing the children’s rags at night. Even the poorest and most broken-spirited people are sometimes liable to these paroxysms of pride and vanity which take the form of an irresistible nervous craving.", "가장 가난하고 가장 의기소침한 사람들조차도 때로는 이러한 자존심과 허영심의 발작에 빠지곤 하는데, 그것은 거역할 수 없는 신경증적 갈망의 형태로 나타난다."], ["And Katerina Ivanovna was not broken-spirited; she might have been killed by circumstance, but her spirit could not have been broken, that is, she could not have been intimidated, her will could not be crushed.", "그리고 카테리나 이바노브나는 의기소침한 사람이 아니었다. 그녀는 환경에 의해 죽임을 당할 수는 있어도, 그 기개만은 꺾일 수 없었으니, 다시 말해 그녀는 위협당할 수도, 그 의지가 짓밟힐 수도 없는 사람이었다."], ["Moreover Sonia had said with good reason that her mind was unhinged.", "게다가 소냐는 그녀의 정신이 온전치 못하다고 말한 적이 있는데, 그것은 충분히 일리가 있었다."], ["She could not be said to be insane, but for a year past she had been so harassed that her mind might well be overstrained.", "그녀를 미쳤다고는 할 수 없었으나, 지난 한 해 동안 그녀는 너무도 시달려 온 탓에 정신이 과도하게 긴장되었을 수도 있었다."], ["The later stages of consumption are apt, doctors tell us, to affect the intellect.", "의사들의 말로는, 폐병의 후기 단계는 지적 능력에 영향을 미치기 쉽다고 한다."]], [["There was no great variety of wines, nor was there Madeira; but wine there was.", "포도주의 종류는 많지 않았고, 마데이라 포도주 같은 것도 없었다. 그래도 포도주는 있었다."], ["There was vodka, rum and Lisbon wine, all of the poorest quality but in sufficient quantity.", "보드카와 럼주, 리스본 포도주가 있었는데, 모두 가장 질이 떨어지는 것들이었지만 양만은 넉넉했다."], ["Besides the traditional rice and honey, there were three or four dishes, one of which consisted of pancakes, all prepared in Amalia Ivanovna’s kitchen.", "전통적인 쌀밥과 꿀 말고도 서너 가지 요리가 있었고, 그중 하나는 팬케이크였는데, 모두 아말리아 이바노브나의 부엌에서 마련된 것이었다."], ["Two samovars were boiling, that tea and punch might be offered after dinner.", "사모바르 두 개가 끓고 있어서, 만찬이 끝난 뒤에는 차와 펀치를 대접할 수 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna had herself seen to purchasing the provisions, with the help of one of the lodgers, an unfortunate little Pole who had somehow been stranded at Madame Lippevechsel’s.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 손수 식료품 구입을 챙겼는데, 하숙인 중 하나인 어느 가엾고 자그마한 폴란드 사람의 도움을 받았다. 그는 어쩌다 보니 리페베흐젤 부인의 집에 정착해 있던 사람이었다."], ["He promptly put himself at Katerina Ivanovna’s disposal and had been all that morning and all the day before running about as fast as his legs could carry him, and very anxious that everyone should be aware of it.", "그는 즉시 카테리나 이바노브나에게 자기 몸을 내맡기다시피 했고, 그날 아침 내내, 또 그 전날 하루 종일을 제 다리가 닿는 한 빠르게 이리저리 뛰어다녔으며, 모두가 그 사실을 알아주기를 몹시도 바랐다."], ["For every trifle he ran to Katerina Ivanovna, even hunting her out at the bazaar, at every instant called her “_Pani_.”", "사소한 일마다 그는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 달려갔고, 심지어 시장통까지 그녀를 찾아 헤맸으며, 시도 때도 없이 그녀를 “파니”라고 불러 댔다."], ["She was heartily sick of him before the end, though she had declared at first that she could not have got on without this “serviceable and magnanimous man.”", "처음에는 이 “쓸모 있고 도량 넓은 사람”이 없었다면 일을 해내지 못했을 거라고 단언했던 그녀였지만, 결국에는 진절머리가 났다."], ["It was one of Katerina Ivanovna’s characteristics to paint everyone she met in the most glowing colours.", "만나는 사람마다 가장 화려한 빛깔로 그려 내는 것이 카테리나 이바노브나의 특징 중 하나였다."], ["Her praises were so exaggerated as sometimes to be embarrassing; she would invent various circumstances to the credit of her new acquaintance and quite genuinely believe in their reality.", "그녀의 칭찬은 너무 과장되어 때로는 듣는 이가 거북할 지경이었다. 그녀는 새로 알게 된 사람을 추켜세우려 온갖 사정을 지어내고는, 그것이 진짜라고 진심으로 믿어 버리곤 했다."], ["Then all of a sudden she would be disillusioned and would rudely and contemptuously repulse the person she had only a few hours before been literally adoring.", "그러다가 별안간 환멸을 느끼고, 불과 몇 시간 전까지만 해도 그야말로 떠받들던 그 사람을 무례하고 경멸스럽게 내쳐 버리는 것이었다."], ["She was naturally of a gay, lively and peace-loving disposition, but from continual failures and misfortunes she had come to desire so _keenly_ that all should live in peace and joy and should not _dare_ to break the peace, that the slightest jar, the smallest disaster reduced her almost to frenzy, and she would pass in an instant from the brightest hopes and fancies to cursing her fate and raving, and knocking her head against the wall.", "그녀는 본래 명랑하고 활기차며 화목을 사랑하는 기질이었지만, 끊임없는 실패와 불행을 겪으며, 모두가 평화롭고 기쁘게 살아야 하며 누구도 감히 그 평화를 깨뜨려서는 안 된다고 너무도 절실하게 바라게 된 나머지, 아주 작은 충돌, 아주 사소한 재난에도 거의 광란 상태에 빠지곤 했고, 가장 밝은 희망과 공상에서 한순간에 자신의 운명을 저주하고 발광하며 벽에 머리를 찧어 대는 지경으로 옮아가곤 했다."]], [["Amalia Ivanovna, too, suddenly acquired extraordinary importance in Katerina Ivanovna’s eyes and was treated by her with extraordinary respect, probably only because Amalia Ivanovna had thrown herself heart and soul into the preparations.", "아말리아 이바노브나 역시 카테리나 이바노브나의 눈에 별안간 대단한 중요성을 띠게 되었고, 그녀로부터 각별한 존중을 받았는데, 아마도 아말리아 이바노브나가 그 준비에 온 마음을 다 쏟아부었기 때문이었을 것이다."], ["She had undertaken to lay the table, to provide the linen, crockery, etc., and to cook the dishes in her kitchen, and Katerina Ivanovna had left it all in her hands and gone herself to the cemetery.", "그녀는 식탁을 차리고, 식탁보며 그릇 따위를 마련하고, 제 부엌에서 요리를 하기로 했으며, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그 모든 것을 그녀의 손에 맡긴 채 자신은 묘지로 갔다."], ["Everything had been well done.", "모든 것이 잘 마련되어 있었다."], ["Even the table-cloth was nearly clean; the crockery, knives, forks and glasses were, of course, of all shapes and patterns, lent by different lodgers, but the table was properly laid at the time fixed, and Amalia Ivanovna, feeling she had done her work well, had put on a black silk dress and a cap with new mourning ribbons and met the returning party with some pride.", "식탁보마저 거의 깨끗했고, 그릇이며 칼, 포크, 잔은 물론 갖가지 모양과 무늬로, 여러 하숙인들에게서 빌려 온 것이었지만, 식탁은 정해진 시각에 제대로 차려져 있었고, 아말리아 이바노브나는 자기 일을 훌륭히 해냈다는 뿌듯함에 검은 비단 드레스를 입고 새 상복 리본을 단 모자를 쓰고서, 돌아오는 사람들을 다소 자랑스러운 기색으로 맞이했다."], ["This pride, though justifiable, displeased Katerina Ivanovna for some reason: “as though the table could not have been laid except by Amalia Ivanovna!”", "이 자랑스러움은, 정당한 것이긴 했으되, 어쩐 영문인지 카테리나 이바노브나의 비위를 거슬렀다. “마치 아말리아 이바노브나가 아니면 식탁을 차릴 수 없었던 것처럼!”"], ["She disliked the cap with new ribbons, too.", "그녀는 새 리본을 단 모자도 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["“Could she be stuck up, the stupid German, because she was mistress of the house, and had consented as a favour to help her poor lodgers!", "“이 멍청한 독일 여편네가, 제가 집주인이고 가엾은 하숙인들을 인심 써서 거들어 주기로 했다고 해서 우쭐대는 건가!"], ["As a favour!", "인심을 써서!"], ["Fancy that!", "어이가 없어서!"], ["Katerina Ivanovna’s father who had been a colonel and almost a governor had sometimes had the table set for forty persons, and then anyone like Amalia Ivanovna, or rather Ludwigovna, would not have been allowed into the kitchen.”", "카테리나 이바노브나의 아버지는 대령에다 거의 지사나 다름없는 분이어서, 때로는 마흔 명을 위한 식탁을 차리기도 했는데, 그때라면 아말리아 이바노브나, 아니 차라리 루드비고브나 같은 여자는 부엌에조차 발을 들이지 못했을 것이다.”"]], [["Katerina Ivanovna, however, put off expressing her feelings for the time and contented herself with treating her coldly, though she decided inwardly that she would certainly have to put Amalia Ivanovna down and set her in her proper place, for goodness only knew what she was fancying herself.", "그렇지만 카테리나 이바노브나는 당분간 자기 감정을 드러내기를 미루고, 그녀를 차갑게 대하는 것으로 만족했는데, 속으로는 반드시 아말리아 이바노브나의 콧대를 꺾어 제자리에 앉혀 놓아야겠다고 마음먹었으니, 도대체 저 여자가 자신을 뭐라고 여기고 있는지 알 수가 없었던 것이다. 카테리나 이바노브나를 짜증나게 한 또 다른 사실은, 초대한 하숙인들 가운데 장례식에 온 사람이 거의 없었다는 점이었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna was irritated too by the fact that hardly any of the lodgers invited had come to the funeral, except the Pole who had just managed to run into the cemetery, while to the memorial dinner the poorest and most insignificant of them had turned up, the wretched creatures, many of them not quite sober.", "겨우 묘지로 뛰어 들어오는 데 성공한 폴란드 사람을 빼고는 말이다. 한편 추도 만찬에는 그들 가운데 가장 가난하고 가장 보잘것없는 자들이, 그 비참한 인간들이, 그것도 상당수가 멀쩡한 정신이 아닌 채로 나타났다."], ["The older and more respectable of them all, as if by common consent, stayed away.", "더 나이 들고 더 점잖은 자들은, 마치 무슨 약속이라도 한 듯, 모습을 드러내지 않았다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin, for instance, who might be said to be the most respectable of all the lodgers, did not appear, though Katerina Ivanovna had the evening before told all the world, that is Amalia Ivanovna, Polenka, Sonia and the Pole, that he was the most generous, noble-hearted man with a large property and vast connections, who had been a friend of her first husband’s, and a guest in her father’s house, and that he had promised to use all his influence to secure her a considerable pension.", "이를테면 모든 하숙인 중에서 가장 점잖은 사람이라 할 만한 표트르 페트로비치 루진은 나타나지 않았다. 그 전날 저녁 카테리나 이바노브나가 온 세상에다, 그러니까 아말리아 이바노브나며 폴렌카, 소냐, 그리고 그 폴란드 사람에게다 대고, 그가 막대한 재산과 광범위한 인맥을 지닌, 더없이 관대하고 고결한 사람이며, 자기 첫 남편의 친구였고 자기 아버지의 집에 손님으로 드나들던 사람이라고, 또 자신에게 상당한 액수의 연금을 받아 주려고 온갖 영향력을 동원하겠노라 약속했다고 떠벌렸음에도 불구하고 말이다."], ["It must be noted that when Katerina Ivanovna exalted anyone’s connections and fortune, it was without any ulterior motive, quite disinterestedly, for the mere pleasure of adding to the consequence of the person praised.", "짚고 넘어가야 할 것은, 카테리나 이바노브나가 누군가의 인맥과 재산을 치켜세울 때면 거기에는 어떤 다른 속셈도 없이, 그야말로 사심 없이, 단지 칭찬받는 사람의 위신을 더해 주는 그 즐거움만을 위해서였다는 점이다."], ["Probably “taking his cue” from Luzhin, “that contemptible wretch Lebeziatnikov had not turned up either.", "아마도 루진을 “본받아”, “그 경멸스러운 인간 레베쟈트니코프 역시 나타나지 않았다."], ["What did he fancy himself?", "도대체 제가 뭐라도 되는 줄 아나?"], ["He was only asked out of kindness and because he was sharing the same room with Pyotr Petrovitch and was a friend of his, so that it would have been awkward not to invite him.”", "그는 그저 호의로, 그리고 표트르 페트로비치와 같은 방을 쓰고 있고 그의 친구이니 초대하지 않으면 거북할까 봐 불러 준 것뿐인데.”"]], [["Among those who failed to appear were “the genteel lady and her old-maidish daughter,” who had only been lodgers in the house for the last fortnight, but had several times complained of the noise and uproar in Katerina Ivanovna’s room, especially when Marmeladov had come back drunk.", "나타나지 않은 사람들 가운데에는 “점잖은 부인과 그 노처녀 딸”도 있었는데, 그들은 이 집에 하숙한 지 겨우 보름밖에 안 되었으면서도, 카테리나 이바노브나의 방에서 나는 소란과 시끄러움에 대해, 특히 마르멜라도프가 술에 취해 돌아왔을 때의 소동에 대해 여러 번 불평을 늘어놓았다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna heard this from Amalia Ivanovna who, quarrelling with Katerina Ivanovna, and threatening to turn the whole family out of doors, had shouted at her that they “were not worth the foot”", "카테리나 이바노브나가 이를 들은 것은 아말리아 이바노브나를 통해서였다."], ["of the honourable lodgers whom they were disturbing. Katerina Ivanovna determined now to invite this lady and her daughter, “whose foot she was not worth,”", "아말리아 이바노브나는 카테리나 이바노브나와 다투며 식구들을 죄다 길거리로 내쫓아 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓던 중에, 그들이 “자기네 소동에 시달리는 그 점잖은 하숙인들의 발끝에도 못 미친다”고 그녀에게 소리쳤던 것이다."], ["and who had turned away haughtily when she casually met them, so that they might know that “she was more noble in her thoughts and feelings and did not harbour malice,” and might see that she was not accustomed to her way of living.", "그래서 카테리나 이바노브나는 이번에 그 “발끝에도 못 미친다”던 부인과 그 딸을 초대하기로 마음먹었으니, 우연히 마주쳤을 때 거만하게 고개를 돌려 버리던 그들에게, “자신이 생각과 감정에 있어서는 더 고결하며 악의를 품지 않는다”는 것을 알게 하고, 또 자신이 그런 식의 삶에 익숙한 사람이 아님을 보여 주려는 것이었다."], ["She had proposed to make this clear to them at dinner with allusions to her late father’s governorship, and also at the same time to hint that it was exceedingly stupid of them to turn away on meeting her.", "그녀는 만찬 자리에서 작고한 아버지의 지사직을 넌지시 언급하며 이 점을 그들에게 분명히 하려 했고, 동시에 자신을 마주쳤을 때 고개를 돌려 버린 것이 더없이 어리석은 짓이었음을 은근히 일러 줄 작정이었다."], ["The fat colonel-major (he was really a discharged officer of low rank) was also absent, but it appeared that he had been “not himself”", "그 뚱뚱한 퇴역 소령(실은 하급 퇴역 장교였다) 역시 자리에 없었는데, 듣자 하니 그는 지난 이틀 동안 “제정신이 아니었다”고 했다."], ["for the last two days. The party consisted of the Pole, a wretched looking clerk with a spotty face and a greasy coat, who had not a word to say for himself, and smelt abominably, a deaf and almost blind old man who had once been in the post office and who had been from immemorial ages maintained by someone at Amalia Ivanovna’s.", "모인 사람들이라곤 그 폴란드 사람과, 얼굴에 부스럼이 돋고 기름때 낀 외투를 걸친 채 입 한번 떼지 못하고 지독한 악취를 풍기는 초라한 몰골의 서기 하나, 그리고 한때 우체국에서 일했으나 이제는 귀가 멀고 거의 눈도 보이지 않는 데다 아주 오래전부터 누군가에 의해 아말리아 이바노브나의 집에서 부양받아 온 노인 한 사람이었다."]], [["A retired clerk of the commissariat department came, too; he was drunk, had a loud and most unseemly laugh and only fancy--was without a waistcoat!", "병참부의 퇴직 서기도 왔는데, 그는 술에 취해 더없이 꼴사납게 큰 소리로 웃어 댔고, 게다가 글쎄 — 조끼도 걸치지 않은 채였다!"], ["One of the visitors sat straight down to the table without even greeting Katerina Ivanovna.", "손님 중 하나는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 인사조차 없이 곧장 식탁에 앉아 버렸다."], ["Finally one person having no suit appeared in his dressing-gown, but this was too much, and the efforts of Amalia Ivanovna and the Pole succeeded in removing him.", "마침내 정장이 없어 실내복 차림으로 나타난 사람도 하나 있었지만, 이건 너무 심했던 터라 아말리아 이바노브나와 폴란드 사람의 노력으로 그를 내보내는 데 성공했다."], ["The Pole brought with him, however, two other Poles who did not live at Amalia Ivanovna’s and whom no one had seen here before.", "그러나 그 폴란드 사람은 아말리아 이바노브나의 집에 살지도 않고 이곳에서 누구도 본 적 없는 다른 폴란드 사람 둘을 데려왔다."], ["All this irritated Katerina Ivanovna intensely.", "이 모든 것이 카테리나 이바노브나를 극도로 짜증나게 했다."], ["“For whom had they made all these preparations then?”", "“그렇다면 도대체 누구를 위해 이 모든 준비를 했단 말인가?”"], ["To make room for the visitors the children had not even been laid for at the table; but the two little ones were sitting on a bench in the furthest corner with their dinner laid on a box, while Polenka as a big girl had to look after them, feed them, and keep their noses wiped like well-bred children’s.", "손님들에게 자리를 내주느라 아이들 몫조차 식탁에 차려 주지 못했고, 그래서 어린 두 아이는 가장 구석진 자리의 긴 의자에 앉아 상자 위에 차려진 식사를 했으며, 다 큰 처녀인 폴렌카가 그들을 보살피며 먹여 주고, 잘 자란 아이들답게 콧물을 닦아 주어야 했다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna, in fact, could hardly help meeting her guests with increased dignity, and even haughtiness.", "사실 카테리나 이바노브나는 한층 더한 위엄으로, 심지어 거만함마저 곁들여 손님들을 맞을 수밖에 없었다."], ["She stared at some of them with special severity, and loftily invited them to take their seats.", "그녀는 그들 중 몇몇을 유난히 매서운 눈초리로 노려보다가, 거드름을 피우며 자리에 앉으라고 청했다."], ["Rushing to the conclusion that Amalia Ivanovna must be responsible for those who were absent, she began treating her with extreme nonchalance, which the latter promptly observed and resented.", "자리에 없는 사람들에 대한 책임이 분명 아말리아 이바노브나에게 있다는 결론으로 성급히 치달은 그녀는, 그녀를 극도로 무심하게 대하기 시작했고, 상대는 즉각 그것을 알아채고 발끈했다."], ["Such a beginning was no good omen for the end.", "이런 시작은 끝을 위해 결코 좋은 징조가 아니었다."], ["All were seated at last.", "마침내 모두 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Raskolnikov came in almost at the moment of their return from the cemetery.", "라스콜니코프는 그들이 묘지에서 막 돌아온 무렵에 들어섰다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna was greatly delighted to see him, in the first place, because he was the one “educated visitor, and, as everyone knew, was in two years to take a professorship in the university,”", "카테리나 이바노브나는 그를 보고 무척 기뻐했는데, 첫째로 그가 유일한 “교양 있는 손님이며, 모두가 알다시피 이 년 뒤면 대학교수가 될”"], ["and secondly because he immediately and respectfully apologised for having been unable to be at the funeral.", "사람이었기 때문이고, 둘째로 그가 장례식에 참석하지 못한 것을 즉시 정중하게 사과했기 때문이었다."], ["She positively pounced upon him, and made him sit on her left hand (Amalia Ivanovna was on her right).", "그녀는 그야말로 그에게 달려들다시피 하더니, 자기 왼쪽에 앉혔다(아말리아 이바노브나는 오른쪽이었다)."], ["In spite of her continual anxiety that the dishes should be passed round correctly and that everyone should taste them, in spite of the agonising cough which interrupted her every minute and seemed to have grown worse during the last few days, she hastened to pour out in a half whisper to Raskolnikov all her suppressed feelings and her just indignation at the failure of the dinner, interspersing her remarks with lively and uncontrollable laughter at the expense of her visitors and especially of her landlady.", "요리가 제대로 돌아가는지, 모두가 한 입씩이라도 맛보는지 끊임없이 신경 쓰면서도, 또 일 분마다 그녀를 끊어 놓는, 지난 며칠 사이 더 심해진 듯한 그 고통스러운 기침에도 불구하고, 그녀는 만찬이 엉망이 된 데 대한 억눌린 감정과 정당한 분노를 라스콜니코프에게 반쯤 속삭이듯 서둘러 쏟아 냈고, 그 와중에도 손님들을, 특히 집주인 여자를 두고 활기차고 걷잡을 수 없는 웃음을 사이사이 터뜨렸다."]], [["“It’s all that cuckoo’s fault!", "“다 저 뻐꾸기 탓이에요! 누굴 말하는지 아시겠죠?"], ["You know whom I mean? Her, her!”", "저 여자, 저 여자 말이에요!”"], ["Katerina Ivanovna nodded towards the landlady.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 집주인 여자 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다."], ["“Look at her, she’s making round eyes, she feels that we are talking about her and can’t understand.", "“저것 좀 봐요, 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 있잖아요, 우리가 자기 얘기를 하고 있다는 건 느끼면서도 알아듣지는 못하는 거예요."], ["Pfoo, the owl!", "흥, 부엉이 같으니!"], ["Ha-ha!", "하하!"], ["(Cough-cough-cough.) And what does she put on that cap for?", "(콜록콜록콜록.) 그런데 저 모자는 왜 쓴 거람?"], ["(Cough-cough-cough.) Have you noticed that she wants everyone to consider that she is patronising me and doing me an honour by being here?", "(콜록콜록콜록.) 저 여자가 여기 와 줌으로써 자기가 나를 비호해 주고 영광을 베푸는 거라고 다들 여겨 주기를 바란다는 거, 눈치채셨어요?"], ["I asked her like a sensible woman to invite people, especially those who knew my late husband, and look at the set of fools she has brought!", "나는 분별 있는 여자한테 부탁하듯 사람들을 좀 초대해 달라고, 특히 내 죽은 남편을 알던 이들을 불러 달라고 했는데, 저 여자가 데려온 멍청이 떼거리 좀 보세요! 굴뚝청소부 같은 작자들!"], ["The sweeps! Look at that one with the spotty face.", "저기 얼굴에 부스럼 난 작자 좀 봐요."], ["And those wretched Poles, ha-ha-ha!", "그리고 저 비참한 폴란드 사람들, 하하하!"], ["(Cough-cough-cough.) Not one of them has ever poked his nose in here, I’ve never set eyes on them.", "(콜록콜록콜록.) 단 한 놈도 여기 코빼기를 들이민 적이 없어요, 나는 저들을 본 적도 없다고요."], ["What have they come here for, I ask you?", "도대체 무엇하러 여기 왔답니까?"], ["There they sit in a row.", "줄지어 앉아 있는 꼴이라니."], ["Hey, _pan_!”", "이봐요, 판!”"], ["she cried suddenly to one of them, “have you tasted the pancakes?", "그녀가 별안간 그들 중 하나에게 소리쳤다. “팬케이크는 좀 드셨어요?"], ["Take some more!", "더 드세요!"], ["Have some beer!", "맥주도 좀 들고요!"], ["Won’t you have some vodka?", "보드카는 안 하시겠어요?"], ["Look, he’s jumped up and is making his bows, they must be quite starved, poor things.", "봐요, 벌떡 일어나서 절을 하잖아요, 가엾은 것들이 어지간히 굶주린 모양이에요."], ["Never mind, let them eat!", "괜찮아요, 먹게 둡시다!"], ["They don’t make a noise, anyway, though I’m really afraid for our landlady’s silver spoons...", "어쨌든 소란은 안 피우니까, 다만 집주인 여자의 은수저가 정말 걱정이긴 하지만……."], ["Amalia Ivanovna!”", "아말리아 이바노브나!”"], ["she addressed her suddenly, almost aloud, “if your spoons should happen to be stolen, I won’t be responsible, I warn you!", "그녀가 별안간, 거의 큰 소리로 그녀에게 말을 걸었다. “혹시 댁의 수저가 없어지더라도 나는 책임 안 져요, 미리 일러두는 거예요!"], ["Ha-ha-ha!”", "하하하!”"], ["She laughed turning to Raskolnikov, and again nodding towards the landlady, in high glee at her sally.", "그녀는 라스콜니코프 쪽으로 돌아서서 웃고는, 자기 농담에 몹시 신이 나 다시 집주인 여자 쪽으로 고갯짓을 했다."], ["“She didn’t understand, she didn’t understand again!", "“못 알아들었어요, 또 못 알아들었다고요!"], ["Look how she sits with her mouth open!", "입 벌리고 앉아 있는 꼴 좀 봐요!"], ["An owl, a real owl!", "부엉이, 진짜 부엉이라니까!"], ["An owl in new ribbons, ha-ha-ha!”", "새 리본을 단 부엉이, 하하하!”"]], [["Here her laugh turned again to an insufferable fit of coughing that lasted five minutes.", "이쯤에서 그녀의 웃음은 또다시 오 분이나 계속된 견딜 수 없는 기침 발작으로 변해 버렸다."], ["Drops of perspiration stood out on her forehead and her handkerchief was stained with blood.", "이마에는 땀방울이 맺히고 손수건은 피로 얼룩졌다."], ["She showed Raskolnikov the blood in silence, and as soon as she could get her breath began whispering to him again with extreme animation and a hectic flush on her cheeks.", "그녀는 말없이 라스콜니코프에게 그 피를 보여 주었고, 숨을 돌리자마자 다시 극도로 흥분한 채, 두 뺨에 병적인 홍조를 띠고서 그에게 속삭이기 시작했다."]], [["“Do you know, I gave her the most delicate instructions, so to speak, for inviting that lady and her daughter, you understand of whom I am speaking?", "“글쎄, 내가 그 부인과 딸을 초대하는 일에 대해서, 말하자면 더없이 섬세한 지시를 저 여자한테 내렸거든요, 누구 얘긴지 아시겠죠?"], ["It needed the utmost delicacy, the greatest nicety, but she has managed things so that that fool, that conceited baggage, that provincial nonentity, simply because she is the widow of a major, and has come to try and get a pension and to fray out her skirts in the government offices, because at fifty she paints her face (everybody knows it)... a creature like that did not think fit to come, and has not even answered the invitation, which the most ordinary good manners required!", "그건 지극한 세심함과 더없는 정중함이 필요한 일이었는데, 저 여자가 일을 어떻게 망쳐 놓았는지, 그래서 그 멍청한 여자가, 그 잘난 척하는 천박한 여편네가, 그 시골뜨기 보잘것없는 여자가, 그저 소령의 미망인이라는 이유로, 또 연금을 타 보겠다고 관청 문턱이나 닳도록 드나드는 주제에, 쉰 살에 얼굴에 분칠이나 해 대는(다들 아는 일이에요)……, 그런 인간이 와 줄 가치도 없다고 여기고는, 가장 평범한 예의범절이라면 응당 해야 할 초대장에 대한 답조차 보내지 않은 거예요!"], ["I can’t understand why Pyotr Petrovitch has not come?", "도대체 표트르 페트로비치는 왜 안 오는지 모르겠네……."], ["But where’s Sonia?", "그런데 소냐는 어디 있지?"], ["Where has she gone?", "어디 간 거야?"], ["Ah, there she is at last!", "아, 저기 드디어 오는구나!"], ["what is it, Sonia, where have you been?", "무슨 일이니, 소냐, 어디 갔었어?"], ["It’s odd that even at your father’s funeral you should be so unpunctual.", "네 아버지 장례식에까지 이렇게 시간을 안 지키다니 이상하구나."], ["Rodion Romanovitch, make room for her beside you.", "로디온 로마노비치, 이 애가 당신 옆에 앉게 자리 좀 내주세요."], ["That’s your place, Sonia... take what you like.", "거기가 네 자리야, 소냐……, 먹고 싶은 거 들렴."], ["Have some of the cold entrée with jelly, that’s the best.", "젤리를 곁들인 차가운 전채 요리를 좀 들어, 그게 제일 맛있으니까."], ["They’ll bring the pancakes directly.", "곧 팬케이크를 가져올 거야."], ["Have they given the children some?", "아이들한테는 좀 줬니?"], ["Polenka, have you got everything?", "폴렌카, 다 챙겼어?"], ["(Cough-cough-cough.) That’s all right.", "(콜록콜록콜록.) 됐다, 그럼."], ["Be a good girl, Lida, and, Kolya, don’t fidget with your feet; sit like a little gentleman.", "착하게 굴어라, 리다, 그리고 콜랴, 발을 자꾸 꼼지락대지 말고 점잖은 신사처럼 앉아 있어."], ["What are you saying, Sonia?”", "무슨 말이니, 소냐?”"]], [["Sonia hastened to give her Pyotr Petrovitch’s apologies, trying to speak loud enough for everyone to hear and carefully choosing the most respectful phrases which she attributed to Pyotr Petrovitch.", "소냐는 서둘러 표트르 페트로비치의 사과를 전했는데, 모두가 들을 수 있을 만큼 큰 소리로 말하려 애쓰면서, 표트르 페트로비치가 한 말이라며 가장 정중한 표현들을 신중히 골라냈다."], ["She added that Pyotr Petrovitch had particularly told her to say that, as soon as he possibly could, he would come immediately to discuss _business_ alone with her and to consider what could be done for her, etc., etc.", "그녀는 표트르 페트로비치가 특별히 당부하기를, 형편이 되는 대로 곧장 찾아와 단둘이 용건을 의논하고 그녀를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 궁리하겠다고, 운운, 운운했다고 덧붙였다."]], [["Sonia knew that this would comfort Katerina Ivanovna, would flatter her and gratify her pride.", "소냐는 이 말이 카테리나 이바노브나를 위로하고, 그녀의 비위를 맞추며 자존심을 흡족하게 하리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She sat down beside Raskolnikov; she made him a hurried bow, glancing curiously at him.", "그녀는 라스콜니코프 옆에 앉으면서, 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 흘끗 보고는 황급히 목례를 했다."], ["But for the rest of the time she seemed to avoid looking at him or speaking to him.", "그러나 그 뒤로는 줄곧 그를 쳐다보거나 말을 거는 것을 피하는 듯했다."], ["She seemed absent-minded, though she kept looking at Katerina Ivanovna, trying to please her.", "카테리나 이바노브나를 기쁘게 하려고 계속 그쪽을 바라보면서도, 그녀는 어쩐지 넋이 나간 듯이 보였다."], ["Neither she nor Katerina Ivanovna had been able to get mourning; Sonia was wearing dark brown, and Katerina Ivanovna had on her only dress, a dark striped cotton one.", "그녀도 카테리나 이바노브나도 상복을 구하지 못했던 터라, 소냐는 짙은 갈색 옷을, 카테리나 이바노브나는 하나뿐인 드레스인 짙은 줄무늬 무명옷을 입고 있었다."]], [["The message from Pyotr Petrovitch was very successful.", "표트르 페트로비치의 전갈은 대단히 성공적이었다."], ["Listening to Sonia with dignity, Katerina Ivanovna inquired with equal dignity how Pyotr Petrovitch was, then at once whispered almost aloud to Raskolnikov that it certainly would have been strange for a man of Pyotr Petrovitch’s position and standing to find himself in such “extraordinary company,”", "위엄을 갖추고 소냐의 말을 듣고 난 카테리나 이바노브나는 똑같은 위엄으로 표트르 페트로비치의 안부를 물었고, 그러고는 곧장 라스콜니코프에게 거의 큰 소리로 속삭이기를, 표트르 페트로비치 같은 지위와 신분의 사람이 자기 가족에 대한 헌신과 자기 아버지와의 오랜 우정에도 불구하고 이런 “괴상한 패거리”"], ["in spite of his devotion to her family and his old friendship with her father.", "속에 끼어 있게 되었다면 그것이야말로 분명 이상한 일이었을 거라고 말했다."]], [["“That’s why I am so grateful to you, Rodion Romanovitch, that you have not disdained my hospitality, even in such surroundings,”", "“그래서 나는 당신께 정말 감사한답니다, 로디온 로마노비치, 이런 자리에서까지 내 환대를 마다하지 않으셨으니까요.”"], ["she added almost aloud.", "그녀가 거의 큰 소리로 덧붙였다."], ["“But I am sure that it was only your special affection for my poor husband that has made you keep your promise.”", "“하지만 내 가엾은 남편에 대한 당신의 각별한 애정만이 당신으로 하여금 약속을 지키게 한 것이라고 나는 믿어요.”"]], [["Then once more with pride and dignity she scanned her visitors, and suddenly inquired aloud across the table of the deaf man: “Wouldn’t he have some more meat, and had he been given some wine?”", "그러고는 다시 한번 자존심과 위엄을 담아 손님들을 훑어보더니, 별안간 식탁 너머로 귀먹은 노인에게 큰 소리로 물었다. 고기를 좀 더 들지 않겠느냐고, 그리고 포도주는 받았느냐고."], ["The old man made no answer and for a long while could not understand what he was asked, though his neighbours amused themselves by poking and shaking him.", "노인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 한참 동안 무슨 질문을 받았는지 알아듣지 못했다. 옆에 앉은 자들이 그를 쿡쿡 찌르고 흔들어 대며 재미있어했는데도 말이다."], ["He simply gazed about him with his mouth open, which only increased the general mirth.", "그는 그저 입을 벌린 채 멍하니 주위를 둘러볼 뿐이었고, 그 모습은 좌중의 흥겨움만 더해 줄 따름이었다."]], [["“What an imbecile!", "“저런 멍청이라니!"], ["Look, look!", "봐요, 봐!"], ["Why was he brought?", "무엇하러 데려왔담?"], ["But as to Pyotr Petrovitch, I always had confidence in him,”", "하지만 표트르 페트로비치로 말하자면, 나는 늘 그분을 믿어 왔어요.”"], ["Katerina Ivanovna continued, “and, of course, he is not like...”", "카테리나 이바노브나가 말을 이었다. “그분은 물론 저런 작자들과는 다르죠…….”"], ["with an extremely stern face she addressed Amalia Ivanovna so sharply and loudly that the latter was quite disconcerted, “not like your dressed up draggletails whom my father would not have taken as cooks into his kitchen, and my late husband would have done them honour if he had invited them in the goodness of his heart.”", "그녀는 더없이 험상궂은 얼굴로 아말리아 이바노브나를 향해 어찌나 날카롭고 크게 말을 던졌던지, 상대는 어찌할 바를 몰라 했다. “당신이 데려온 저 차려입은 칠칠치 못한 여편네들과는 다르단 말이에요, 우리 아버지라면 부엌에 요리사로도 들이지 않았을 것들, 내 죽은 남편이라면 마음씨가 고와서 호의로 불러 줬다면 그것만으로도 영광이었을 그런 것들 말이에요.”"]], [["“Yes, he was fond of drink, he was fond of it, he did drink!”", "“그래요, 그분은 술을 좋아하셨죠, 좋아하셨어요, 마셨다니까요!”"], ["cried the commissariat clerk, gulping down his twelfth glass of vodka.", "병참부 서기가 열두 잔째 보드카를 꿀꺽 들이켜며 외쳤다."]], [["“My late husband certainly had that weakness, and everyone knows it,”", "“내 죽은 남편한테 분명 그런 약점이 있었던 건 사실이고, 그건 다들 아는 일이에요.”"], ["Katerina Ivanovna attacked him at once, “but he was a kind and honourable man, who loved and respected his family.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 즉시 그에게 달려들었다. “하지만 그분은 친절하고 명예로운 분이었고, 가족을 사랑하고 존중했어요."], ["The worst of it was his good nature made him trust all sorts of disreputable people, and he drank with fellows who were not worth the sole of his shoe.", "무엇보다 안타까운 건, 그 선량한 성품 탓에 온갖 못된 인간들을 다 믿었다는 거예요, 자기 신발 밑창만도 못한 작자들과 어울려 술을 마셨던 거죠."], ["Would you believe it, Rodion Romanovitch, they found a gingerbread cock in his pocket; he was dead drunk, but he did not forget the children!”", "믿어지세요, 로디온 로마노비치, 사람들이 그분 주머니에서 생강과자로 만든 수탉을 찾아냈답니다. 곤드레만드레 취해서도 아이들을 잊지 않았던 거예요!”"]], [["“A cock?", "“수탉?"], ["Did you say a cock?”", "수탉이라고 했소?”"], ["shouted the commissariat clerk.", "병참부 서기가 소리쳤다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna did not vouchsafe a reply.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 대꾸할 가치도 없다는 듯 답하지 않았다."], ["She sighed, lost in thought.", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬며 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“No doubt you think, like everyone, that I was too severe with him,”", "“당신도 분명, 다들 그렇듯이, 내가 그분한테 너무 모질게 굴었다고 생각하시겠죠.”"], ["she went on, addressing Raskolnikov.", "그녀가 라스콜니코프를 향해 말을 이었다."], ["“But that’s not so!", "“하지만 그렇지 않아요!"], ["He respected me, he respected me very much!", "그분은 나를 존중했어요, 무척이나 존중했다고요!"], ["He was a kind-hearted man!", "마음씨 고운 분이었어요!"], ["And how sorry I was for him sometimes!", "그리고 나는 가끔 그분이 얼마나 가엾던지!"], ["He would sit in a corner and look at me, I used to feel so sorry for him, I used to want to be kind to him and then would think to myself: ‘Be kind to him and he will drink again,’ it was only by severity that you could keep him within bounds.”", "그분이 한구석에 앉아 나를 바라보고 있으면, 나는 너무도 안쓰러워서, 다정하게 대해 주고 싶다가도, 속으로 이렇게 생각하곤 했죠. ‘다정하게 굴면 또 술을 마실 텐데.’ 오로지 모질게 굴어야만 그분을 절제시킬 수 있었던 거예요.”"]], [["“Yes, he used to get his hair pulled pretty often,”", "“그래요, 그 양반 머리채깨나 잡혔지!”"], ["roared the commissariat clerk again, swallowing another glass of vodka.", "병참부 서기가 보드카를 또 한 잔 삼키며 다시 고함쳤다."]], [["“Some fools would be the better for a good drubbing, as well as having their hair pulled.", "“어떤 멍청이들은 머리채를 잡히는 것뿐 아니라 흠씬 두들겨 맞아야 정신을 차릴 텐데."], ["I am not talking of my late husband now!”", "지금 내 죽은 남편 얘길 하는 게 아니에요!”"], ["Katerina Ivanovna snapped at him.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 쏘아붙였다."]], [["The flush on her cheeks grew more and more marked, her chest heaved.", "그녀의 두 뺨에 어린 홍조가 점점 더 짙어졌고, 가슴은 들썩였다."], ["In another minute she would have been ready to make a scene.", "일 분만 더 있으면 한바탕 소동을 벌일 기세였다."], ["Many of the visitors were sniggering, evidently delighted.", "손님들 중 다수가 분명 신이 나서 킬킬거렸다."], ["They began poking the commissariat clerk and whispering something to him.", "그들은 병참부 서기를 쿡쿡 찌르며 무언가를 속삭이기 시작했다."], ["They were evidently trying to egg him on.", "그를 부추기려는 게 분명했다."]], [["“Allow me to ask what are you alluding to,”", "“그게 무슨 말씀인지 좀 물읍시다.” 서기가 입을 열었다."], ["began the clerk, “that is to say, whose... about whom...", "“그러니까 누구를……, 누구에 대해서……, 방금 말씀하신 게……."], ["did you say just now...", "하지만 난 상관없소!"], ["But I don’t care!", "다 헛소리지! 미망인이여!"], ["That’s nonsense! Widow!", "내 당신을 용서하리다……."], ["I forgive you.... Pass!”", "자, 한 잔!”"]], [["And he took another drink of vodka.", "그러고는 보드카를 또 들이켰다."]], [["Raskolnikov sat in silence, listening with disgust.", "라스콜니코프는 말없이 앉아 역겨움 속에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["He only ate from politeness, just tasting the food that Katerina Ivanovna was continually putting on his plate, to avoid hurting her feelings.", "그는 그저 예의상, 카테리나 이바노브나가 끊임없이 자기 접시에 놓아 주는 음식을, 그녀의 감정을 상하게 하지 않으려고 맛만 보았을 뿐이었다."], ["He watched Sonia intently.", "그는 소냐를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["But Sonia became more and more anxious and distressed; she, too, foresaw that the dinner would not end peaceably, and saw with terror Katerina Ivanovna’s growing irritation.", "그러나 소냐는 점점 더 불안하고 괴로워했다. 그녀 역시 이 만찬이 평온하게 끝나지 않으리라는 것을 예감하고, 점점 커져 가는 카테리나 이바노브나의 짜증을 두려운 마음으로 지켜보고 있었다."], ["She knew that she, Sonia, was the chief reason for the ‘genteel’ ladies’ contemptuous treatment of Katerina Ivanovna’s invitation.", "그녀는, 바로 자신, 소냐가 그 “점잖은” 부인들이 카테리나 이바노브나의 초대를 경멸스럽게 대한 주된 이유라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She had heard from Amalia Ivanovna that the mother was positively offended at the invitation and had asked the question: “How could she let her daughter sit down beside _that young person_?”", "그녀는 아말리아 이바노브나에게서 들어 알고 있었으니, 그 어머니는 초대받은 것을 그야말로 모욕으로 여기며 이렇게 물었다는 것이다. “어떻게 제 딸을 그런 처녀애 곁에 앉게 한단 말이오?”"], ["Sonia had a feeling that Katerina Ivanovna had already heard this and an insult to Sonia meant more to Katerina Ivanovna than an insult to herself, her children, or her father.", "소냐는 카테리나 이바노브나가 이미 이 말을 들었으리라는 느낌이 들었고, 카테리나 이바노브나에게는 자신이나 자기 아이들, 자기 아버지에 대한 모욕보다 소냐에 대한 모욕이 더 큰 의미를 지닌다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Sonia knew that Katerina Ivanovna would not be satisfied now, “till she had shown those draggletails that they were both...”", "소냐는 카테리나 이바노브나가 이제 “그 칠칠치 못한 여편네들에게 그 둘이 다 같은 부류임을 보여 주기 전에는”"], ["To make matters worse someone passed Sonia, from the other end of the table, a plate with two hearts pierced with an arrow, cut out of black bread.", "결코 만족하지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. 설상가상으로, 식탁 저쪽 끝에서 누군가 소냐에게 검은 빵을 오려 만든, 화살에 꿰뚫린 두 개의 하트가 놓인 접시를 건넸다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna flushed crimson and at once said aloud across the table that the man who sent it was “a drunken ass!”", "카테리나 이바노브나는 새빨개진 얼굴로 즉시 식탁 너머로 큰 소리로, 그걸 보낸 자는 “술에 취한 멍청한 당나귀”라고 말했다."]], [["Amalia Ivanovna was foreseeing something amiss, and at the same time deeply wounded by Katerina Ivanovna’s haughtiness, and to restore the good-humour of the company and raise herself in their esteem she began, apropos of nothing, telling a story about an acquaintance of hers “Karl from the chemist’s,”", "아말리아 이바노브나는 무언가 잘못되어 가고 있음을 예감하는 동시에, 카테리나 이바노브나의 거만함에 깊이 상처받은 터라, 좌중의 기분을 다시 좋게 만들고 자신의 위신을 세워 보고자, 난데없이 자기가 아는 사람 “약국의 카를”에 관한 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["who was driving one night in a cab, and that “the cabman wanted him to kill, and Karl very much begged him not to kill, and wept and clasped hands, and frightened and from fear pierced his heart.”", "그가 어느 날 밤 삯마차를 타고 가는데, “마부가 그를 죽이려 했고, 카를이 죽이지 말라고 무척 빌었고, 울면서 두 손을 모았고, 겁에 질려 무서워서 그의 심장을 찔렀다”는 것이었다."], ["Though Katerina Ivanovna smiled, she observed at once that Amalia Ivanovna ought not to tell anecdotes in Russian; the latter was still more offended, and she retorted that her “_Vater aus Berlin_ was a very important man, and always went with his hands in pockets.”", "카테리나 이바노브나는 미소를 짓긴 했으나, 곧 아말리아 이바노브나에게 러시아어로 일화를 늘어놓아서는 안 된다고 지적했다. 상대는 한층 더 발끈해서는, 자기 “베를린 출신 아버지는 매우 중요한 인물이었고, 늘 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 다녔다”고 응수했다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna could not restrain herself and laughed so much that Amalia Ivanovna lost patience and could scarcely control herself.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 도저히 참을 수가 없어 어찌나 크게 웃어 댔던지, 아말리아 이바노브나는 인내심을 잃고 가까스로 자제하는 지경이 되었다."]], [["“Listen to the owl!”", "“저 부엉이 좀 봐요!”"], ["Katerina Ivanovna whispered at once, her good-humour almost restored, “she meant to say he kept his hands in his pockets, but she said he put his hands in people’s pockets.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 즉시, 기분이 거의 풀어진 채 속삭였다. “자기 아버지가 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 다녔다고 말하려던 건데, 사람들 주머니에 손을 넣고 다녔다고 말한 거예요."], ["(Cough-cough.) And have you noticed, Rodion Romanovitch, that all these Petersburg foreigners, the Germans especially, are all stupider than we!", "(콜록콜록.) 그리고 눈치채셨어요, 로디온 로마노비치, 이 페테르부르크의 외국인들, 특히 독일 사람들은 죄다 우리보다 멍청하다는 거?"], ["Can you fancy anyone of us telling how ‘Karl from the chemist’s’ ‘pierced his heart from fear’ and that the idiot, instead of punishing the cabman, ‘clasped his hands and wept, and much begged.’ Ah, the fool!", "우리 중에 누가 ‘약국의 카를’이 ‘겁에 질려 심장을 찔렀다’느니, 그 멍청이가 마부를 벌하기는커녕 ‘두 손을 모으고 울면서 무척 빌었다’느니 하는 얘기를 늘어놓는 걸 상상이나 할 수 있겠어요? 아, 저 바보!"], ["And you know she fancies it’s very touching and does not suspect how stupid she is!", "게다가 글쎄 저 여자는 그게 무척 감동적인 얘기라고 여기면서, 자기가 얼마나 멍청한지는 짐작도 못한다니까요!"], ["To my thinking that drunken commissariat clerk is a great deal cleverer, anyway one can see that he has addled his brains with drink, but you know, these foreigners are always so well behaved and serious....", "내 생각엔 저 술 취한 병참부 서기가 차라리 훨씬 똑똑해요, 적어도 저자는 술로 머리를 망쳐 놨다는 게 눈에 보이기라도 하지, 이 외국인들은 늘 저렇게 점잖고 진지한 척만 하니까……."], ["Look how she sits glaring!", "저것 봐요, 노려보고 앉아 있는 꼴이라니!"], ["She is angry, ha-ha!", "화가 났네, 하하!"], ["(Cough-cough-cough.)”", "(콜록콜록콜록.)”"]], [["Regaining her good-humour, Katerina Ivanovna began at once telling Raskolnikov that when she had obtained her pension, she intended to open a school for the daughters of gentlemen in her native town T----.", "기분을 되찾은 카테리나 이바노브나는 곧장 라스콜니코프에게, 연금을 받게 되면 고향인 T시에서 양갓집 따님들을 위한 학교를 열 작정이라고 말하기 시작했다."], ["This was the first time she had spoken to him of the project, and she launched out into the most alluring details.", "그녀가 그에게 이 계획을 이야기한 것은 처음이었고, 그녀는 더없이 매혹적인 세부 사항들을 늘어놓았다."], ["It suddenly appeared that Katerina Ivanovna had in her hands the very certificate of honour of which Marmeladov had spoken to Raskolnikov in the tavern, when he told him that Katerina Ivanovna, his wife, had danced the shawl dance before the governor and other great personages on leaving school.", "그러다 별안간, 카테리나 이바노브나가 바로 그 표창장을 손에 쥐고 있다는 사실이 드러났는데, 그것은 마르멜라도프가 선술집에서 라스콜니코프에게, 자기 아내 카테리나 이바노브나가 학교를 졸업할 때 지사와 그 밖의 높으신 분들 앞에서 숄춤을 추었다고 말하며 언급했던 바로 그 표창장이었다."], ["This certificate of honour was obviously intended now to prove Katerina Ivanovna’s right to open a boarding-school; but she had armed herself with it chiefly with the object of overwhelming “those two stuck-up draggletails” if they came to the dinner, and proving incontestably that Katerina Ivanovna was of the most noble, “she might even say aristocratic family, a colonel’s daughter and was far superior to certain adventuresses who have been so much to the fore of late.”", "이 표창장은 분명 이제 카테리나 이바노브나가 기숙학교를 열 자격이 있음을 증명하기 위한 것이었지만, 그녀가 그것으로 무장한 주된 목적은, 만일 그 “두 잘난 척하는 칠칠치 못한 여편네들”이 만찬에 온다면 그들을 압도하고, 카테리나 이바노브나가 더없이 고귀한, “귀족적이라 해도 좋을 가문 출신이며, 대령의 딸이고, 요즘 들어 그토록 설쳐 대는 어떤 모험적인 여자들보다 훨씬 윗사람”임을 반박할 수 없이 증명하려는 데 있었다."], ["The certificate of honour immediately passed into the hands of the drunken guests, and Katerina Ivanovna did not try to retain it, for it actually contained the statement _en toutes lettres_, that her father was of the rank of a major, and also a companion of an order, so that she really was almost the daughter of a colonel.", "표창장은 곧 술 취한 손님들의 손으로 넘어갔고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 굳이 붙들려 하지 않았으니, 거기에는 그녀의 아버지가 소령의 계급이었으며 또한 어느 훈장의 수훈자였다고, 그러니 그녀가 실로 거의 대령의 딸이나 다름없다고 글자 그대로 명백히 적혀 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Warming up, Katerina Ivanovna proceeded to enlarge on the peaceful and happy life they would lead in T----, on the gymnasium teachers whom she would engage to give lessons in her boarding-school, one a most respectable old Frenchman, one Mangot, who had taught Katerina Ivanovna herself in old days and was still living in T----, and would no doubt teach in her school on moderate terms.", "신이 난 카테리나 이바노브나는, T시에서 그들이 누리게 될 평화롭고 행복한 삶에 대해, 자기 기숙학교에서 수업을 맡기려고 고용할 김나지움 교사들에 대해 계속 늘어놓았다. 그중 하나는 더없이 점잖은 늙은 프랑스인 망고라는 사람으로, 옛날에 카테리나 이바노브나 자신을 가르친 적이 있고 아직도 T시에 살고 있으니, 틀림없이 적당한 보수로 자기 학교에서 가르쳐 줄 거라고 했다."], ["Next she spoke of Sonia who would go with her to T---- and help her in all her plans.", "다음으로 그녀는 자기와 함께 T시로 가서 자기 모든 계획을 도와줄 소냐에 대해 이야기했다."], ["At this someone at the further end of the table gave a sudden guffaw.", "이때 식탁 저쪽 끝에서 누군가 별안간 껄껄 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Though Katerina Ivanovna tried to appear to be disdainfully unaware of it, she raised her voice and began at once speaking with conviction of Sonia’s undoubted ability to assist her, of “her gentleness, patience, devotion, generosity and good education,” tapping Sonia on the cheek and kissing her warmly twice.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 경멸스럽게 모른 척하려 애쓰면서도, 목소리를 높여 곧장 소냐가 자기를 도울 분명한 능력을 지녔음을, “그 애의 온순함, 인내심, 헌신, 너그러움, 그리고 훌륭한 교양”에 대해 확신에 차서 이야기하기 시작했고, 소냐의 뺨을 토닥이고는 따뜻하게 두 번 입을 맞추었다."], ["Sonia flushed crimson, and Katerina Ivanovna suddenly burst into tears, immediately observing that she was “nervous and silly, that she was too much upset, that it was time to finish, and as the dinner was over, it was time to hand round the tea.”", "소냐는 새빨개졌고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 별안간 울음을 터뜨리고는, 곧바로 자신이 “신경이 예민하고 어리석으며, 너무 흥분했으니, 이제 끝낼 때가 되었고, 만찬도 끝났으니 차를 돌릴 때가 되었다”고 말했다."]], [["At that moment, Amalia Ivanovna, deeply aggrieved at taking no part in the conversation, and not being listened to, made one last effort, and with secret misgivings ventured on an exceedingly deep and weighty observation, that “in the future boarding-school she would have to pay particular attention to _die Wäsche_, and that there certainly must be a good _dame_ to look after the linen, and secondly that the young ladies must not novels at night read.”", "바로 그때, 대화에 끼어들지 못하고 아무도 자기 말을 들어 주지 않는 것에 깊이 분개해 있던 아말리아 이바노브나가 마지막으로 한 번 더 시도하여, 남몰래 불안해하면서도 지극히 심오하고 무게 있는 견해를 과감히 내놓았으니, “앞으로 그 기숙학교에서는 디 베셰(세탁물)에 각별히 신경을 써야 할 것이고, 반드시 빨랫감을 돌볼 좋은 담(부인)이 있어야 하며, 둘째로 그 아가씨들이 밤에 소설을 읽어서는 안 된다”는 것이었다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna, who certainly was upset and very tired, as well as heartily sick of the dinner, at once cut short Amalia Ivanovna, saying “she knew nothing about it and was talking nonsense, that it was the business of the laundry maid, and not of the directress of a high-class boarding-school to look after _die Wäsche_, and as for novel-reading, that was simply rudeness, and she begged her to be silent.” Amalia Ivanovna fired up and getting angry observed that she only “meant her good,”", "분명 마음이 상하고 몹시 지쳐 있던 데다 만찬에도 진절머리가 나 있던 카테리나 이바노브나는, 즉시 아말리아 이바노브나의 말을 끊으며, “그녀는 아무것도 모르면서 헛소리를 하고 있고, 디 베셰를 돌보는 것은 세탁부의 일이지 고급 기숙학교 교장의 일이 아니며, 소설 읽기로 말하자면 그것은 그저 무례한 소리이니, 입을 다물어 달라”고 말했다."], ["and that “she had meant her very good,” and that “it was long since she had paid her _gold_ for the lodgings.”", "아말리아 이바노브나는 발끈하여 화를 내며, 자기는 그저 “그녀를 위하는 마음”에서 한 말이고, “그녀를 무척이나 위하는 마음”에서 한 것이며, “하숙비로 금화를 받지 못한 지가 오래되었다”고 했다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna at once “set her down,” saying that it was a lie to say she wished her good, because only yesterday when her dead husband was lying on the table, she had worried her about the lodgings.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 즉시 그녀의 “기를 꺾으며”, 자기를 위한다는 건 거짓말이라고, 바로 어제 죽은 남편이 탁자에 누워 있던 마당에 하숙비로 자기를 들볶지 않았느냐고 말했다."], ["To this Amalia Ivanovna very appropriately observed that she had invited those ladies, but “those ladies had not come, because those ladies _are_ ladies and cannot come to a lady who is not a lady.”", "이에 아말리아 이바노브나는 매우 적절하게도, 자기는 그 부인들을 초대했지만 “그 부인들이 오지 않은 것은, 그 부인들은 진짜 부인들이라 부인 아닌 부인에게는 올 수 없기 때문”이라고 응수했다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna at once pointed out to her, that as she was a slut she could not judge what made one really a lady.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 곧장 그녀에게, 너 같은 칠칠치 못한 여자가 무엇이 진짜 부인을 만드는지 판단할 자격이나 있느냐고 일러 주었다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna at once declared that her “_Vater aus Berlin_ was a very, very important man, and both hands in pockets went, and always used to say: ‘Poof! poof!’”", "아말리아 이바노브나는 즉시 자기 “베를린 출신 아버지는 매우, 매우 중요한 인물이었고, 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 다니며 늘 ‘푸!"], ["and she leapt up from the table to represent her father, sticking her hands in her pockets, puffing her cheeks, and uttering vague sounds resembling “poof!", "푸!’ 하고 말하곤 했다”고 선언하더니, 자기 아버지 흉내를 내겠다며 식탁에서 벌떡 일어나, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 두 뺨을 부풀린 채 “푸!"], ["poof!”", "푸!”"], ["amid loud laughter from all the lodgers, who purposely encouraged Amalia Ivanovna, hoping for a fight.", "비슷한 알 수 없는 소리를 내뱉었고, 싸움이 벌어지기를 바라며 일부러 아말리아 이바노브나를 부추기던 하숙인들이 일제히 큰 소리로 웃어 댔다."]], [["But this was too much for Katerina Ivanovna, and she at once declared, so that all could hear, that Amalia Ivanovna probably never had a father, but was simply a drunken Petersburg Finn, and had certainly once been a cook and probably something worse.", "그러나 이것은 카테리나 이바노브나에게 너무 심한 일이었으니, 그녀는 즉시, 모두가 들을 수 있도록, 아말리아 이바노브나에게는 아마 아버지란 게 애초에 없었을 거라고, 그저 술 취한 페테르부르크의 핀란드 여자일 뿐이며, 분명 한때는 요리사였고 아마 그보다 더 못한 무엇이었을 거라고 단언했다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna turned as red as a lobster and squealed that perhaps Katerina Ivanovna never had a father, “but she had a _Vater aus Berlin_ and that he wore a long coat and always said poof-poof-poof!”", "아말리아 이바노브나는 가재처럼 빨개져서는, 어쩌면 카테리나 이바노브나야말로 아버지란 게 없었을 거라고, “하지만 자기에게는 베를린 출신 아버지가 있었고, 그분은 긴 외투를 입고 늘 푸-푸-푸! 하고 말했다”고 꽥꽥 소리쳤다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna observed contemptuously that all knew what her family was and that on that very certificate of honour it was stated in print that her father was a colonel, while Amalia Ivanovna’s father--if she really had one--was probably some Finnish milkman, but that probably she never had a father at all, since it was still uncertain whether her name was Amalia Ivanovna or Amalia Ludwigovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 경멸스럽게, 자기 가문이 어떤 가문인지는 다들 알고 있고, 바로 그 표창장에 자기 아버지가 대령이었다고 인쇄되어 있는 반면, 아말리아 이바노브나의 아버지는 — 만일 그녀에게 정말 아버지란 게 있었다면 — 아마 어느 핀란드 우유 장수였을 테지만, 아마 그녀에게는 애초에 아버지가 없었을 거라고, 왜냐하면 그녀의 이름이 아말리아 이바노브나인지 아말리아 루드비고브나인지조차 아직 불확실하기 때문이라고 말했다."]], [["At this Amalia Ivanovna, lashed to fury, struck the table with her fist, and shrieked that she was Amalia Ivanovna, and not Ludwigovna, “that her _Vater_ was named Johann and that he was a burgomeister, and that Katerina Ivanovna’s _Vater_ was quite never a burgomeister.”", "이 말에 아말리아 이바노브나는 격노하여, 주먹으로 식탁을 내리치며, 자기는 아말리아 이바노브나이지 루드비고브나가 아니라고, “자기 아버지의 이름은 요한이었고 그분은 시장이었으며, 카테리나 이바노브나의 아버지는 결코 시장 따위가 아니었다”고 째지는 소리로 외쳤다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna rose from her chair, and with a stern and apparently calm voice (though she was pale and her chest was heaving) observed that “if she dared for one moment to set her contemptible wretch of a father on a level with her papa, she, Katerina Ivanovna, would tear her cap off her head and trample it under foot.”", "카테리나 이바노브나는 의자에서 일어나, 험상궂으면서도 겉으로는 차분한 목소리로(비록 얼굴은 창백했고 가슴은 들썩였지만), “만일 그녀가 단 한순간이라도 그 경멸스러운 인간인 자기 아버지를 우리 아빠와 같은 반열에 감히 올려놓는다면, 자신, 카테리나 이바노브나가 그녀의 머리에서 모자를 잡아채 발로 짓밟아 버리겠다”고 말했다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna ran about the room, shouting at the top of her voice, that she was mistress of the house and that Katerina Ivanovna should leave the lodgings that minute; then she rushed for some reason to collect the silver spoons from the table.", "아말리아 이바노브나는 목청껏 소리를 지르며 방 안을 이리저리 뛰어다니더니, 자기가 이 집 주인이고 카테리나 이바노브나는 당장 이 집에서 나가야 한다고 외쳤다. 그러고는 무슨 영문인지 식탁의 은수저를 거두러 달려들었다."], ["There was a great outcry and uproar, the children began crying.", "일대 아우성과 소동이 벌어졌고, 아이들은 울기 시작했다."], ["Sonia ran to restrain Katerina Ivanovna, but when Amalia Ivanovna shouted something about “the yellow ticket,” Katerina Ivanovna pushed Sonia away, and rushed at the landlady to carry out her threat.", "소냐가 카테리나 이바노브나를 말리려 달려갔지만, 아말리아 이바노브나가 “노란 딱지”에 대해 무어라 소리치자, 카테리나 이바노브나는 소냐를 밀쳐 내고 자기 으름장을 실행에 옮기려 집주인 여자에게 달려들었다."]], [["At that minute the door opened, and Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin appeared on the threshold.", "바로 그 순간 문이 열리고, 표트르 페트로비치 루진이 문지방에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He stood scanning the party with severe and vigilant eyes.", "그는 매섭고 빈틈없는 눈초리로 좌중을 훑어보며 서 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna rushed to him.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 그에게 달려갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["“Pyotr Petrovitch,”", "\"표트르 페트로비치,\""], ["she cried, “protect me...", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"저를 지켜 주세요……"], ["you at least!", "적어도 당신만은!"], ["Make this foolish woman understand that she can’t behave like this to a lady in misfortune...", "이 어리석은 여자에게, 불행에 빠진 귀부인을 이런 식으로 대해서는 안 된다는 걸 알게 해 주세요……"], ["that there is a law for such things....", "이런 일에는 법이라는 게 있다고요……"], ["I’ll go to the governor-general himself....", "저는 총독 각하께라도 직접 찾아가겠어요……"], ["She shall answer for it....", "이 여자는 책임을 져야 할 거예요……"], ["Remembering my father’s hospitality protect these orphans.”", "제 아버지가 베푸신 환대를 기억하셔서 이 고아들을 지켜 주세요.\""]], [["“Allow me, madam....", "\"잠깐만요, 부인……"], ["Allow me.”", "잠깐만요.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch waved her off.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그녀를 물리쳤다."], ["“Your papa as you are well aware I had not the honour of knowing”", "\"당신 아버님은, 잘 아시다시피, 제가 알고 지내는 영광을 누리지 못했고요\""], ["(someone laughed aloud) “and I do not intend to take part in your everlasting squabbles with Amalia Ivanovna....", "(누군가 큰 소리로 웃었다) \"당신과 아말리야 이바노브나 사이의 끝없는 말다툼에 끼어들 생각도 없습니다……"], ["I have come here to speak of my own affairs...", "저는 제 용건이 있어 여기 왔어요……"], ["and I want to have a word with your stepdaughter, Sofya...", "당신 의붓딸인 소피야와 한마디 나누고자 합니다……"], ["Ivanovna, I think it is?", "이바노브나, 였던가요?"], ["Allow me to pass.”", "좀 지나가게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch, edging by her, went to the opposite corner where Sonia was.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그녀를 비켜 지나 소냐가 있는 맞은편 구석으로 갔다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna remained standing where she was, as though thunderstruck.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다."], ["She could not understand how Pyotr Petrovitch could deny having enjoyed her father’s hospitality.", "표트르 페트로비치가 자기 아버지의 환대를 누린 적이 없다고 부인하다니, 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["Though she had invented it herself, she believed in it firmly by this time.", "그것을 스스로 꾸며 낸 이야기이긴 했지만, 어느새 그녀는 그것을 굳게 믿고 있었던 것이다."], ["She was struck too by the businesslike, dry and even contemptuous menacing tone of Pyotr Petrovitch.", "또한 그녀는 표트르 페트로비치의 사무적이고 메마르며 심지어 경멸이 어린 위협조에 충격을 받았다."], ["All the clamour gradually died away at his entrance.", "그가 들어서자 그 모든 소란이 차츰 잦아들었다."], ["Not only was this “serious business man” strikingly incongruous with the rest of the party, but it was evident, too, that he had come upon some matter of consequence, that some exceptional cause must have brought him and that therefore something was going to happen.", "이 \"진지한 사업가\"는 나머지 사람들과 두드러지게 어울리지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 무언가 중대한 일로 찾아왔으며, 무언가 예사롭지 않은 까닭이 그를 데려왔음이, 따라서 무언가 일이 벌어지리라는 것이 분명해 보였다."], ["Raskolnikov, standing beside Sonia, moved aside to let him pass; Pyotr Petrovitch did not seem to notice him.", "소냐 곁에 서 있던 라스콜니코프는 그가 지나가도록 옆으로 비켜섰다. 표트르 페트로비치는 그를 알아채지도 못하는 듯했다."], ["A minute later Lebeziatnikov, too, appeared in the doorway; he did not come in, but stood still, listening with marked interest, almost wonder, and seemed for a time perplexed.", "잠시 후 레베쟈트니코프도 문간에 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 안으로 들어오지 않고 가만히 선 채, 두드러진 흥미를, 거의 경이에 가까운 표정으로 귀를 기울였고, 한동안 어리둥절해하는 것 같았다."]], [["“Excuse me for possibly interrupting you, but it’s a matter of some importance,”", "\"제가 혹시 여러분을 방해하게 된다면 용서하십시오만, 이건 다소 중요한 문제입니다.\""], ["Pyotr Petrovitch observed, addressing the company generally.", "표트르 페트로비치는 좌중 전체를 향해 말했다."], ["“I am glad indeed to find other persons present.", "\"다른 분들이 와 계셔서 저로서는 정말 다행입니다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna, I humbly beg you as mistress of the house to pay careful attention to what I have to say to Sofya Ivanovna.", "아말리야 이바노브나, 이 집 안주인이신 당신께 삼가 청하니, 제가 소피야 이바노브나에게 할 말을 주의 깊게 들어 주십시오."], ["Sofya Ivanovna,”", "소피야 이바노브나,\""], ["he went on, addressing Sonia, who was very much surprised and already alarmed, “immediately after your visit I found that a hundred-rouble note was missing from my table, in the room of my friend Mr.", "그는 매우 놀라며 이미 불안에 떨고 있는 소냐를 향해 말을 이었다. \"당신이 다녀가신 직후에, 제 친구 레베쟈트니코프 씨의 방에 있는 제 탁자에서 백 루블짜리 지폐 한 장이 없어진 것을 발견했습니다."], ["Lebeziatnikov. If in any way whatever you know and will tell us where it is now, I assure you on my word of honour and call all present to witness that the matter shall end there.", "만일 어떤 식으로든 당신이 그것이 지금 어디 있는지 알고 우리에게 말해 준다면, 명예를 걸고 단언하건대, 그리고 여기 계신 모든 분을 증인으로 삼아 말하건대, 이 일은 거기서 끝낼 것입니다."], ["In the opposite case I shall be compelled to have recourse to very serious measures and then...", "그렇지 않을 경우 저는 매우 엄중한 조치를 취할 수밖에 없을 것이고, 그때는……"], ["you must blame yourself.”", "당신 자신을 탓해야 할 것입니다.\""]], [["Complete silence reigned in the room.", "방 안에는 완전한 침묵이 감돌았다."], ["Even the crying children were still.", "울던 아이들조차 잠잠해졌다."], ["Sonia stood deadly pale, staring at Luzhin and unable to say a word.", "소냐는 새파랗게 질린 채 서서 루진을 응시했으나 한마디도 하지 못했다."], ["She seemed not to understand.", "그녀는 이해하지 못하는 것 같았다."], ["Some seconds passed.", "몇 초가 흘렀다."]], [["“Well, how is it to be then?”", "\"자, 그래서 어떻게 된 겁니까?\""], ["asked Luzhin, looking intently at her.", "루진이 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know....", "\"모르겠어요……"], ["I know nothing about it,”", "저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라요.\""], ["Sonia articulated faintly at last.", "마침내 소냐가 가냘프게 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“No, you know nothing?”", "\"아니, 아무것도 모른다고요?\""], ["Luzhin repeated and again he paused for some seconds.", "루진은 되풀이하고는 다시 몇 초 동안 입을 다물었다."], ["“Think a moment, mademoiselle,”", "\"잠깐 생각해 보세요, 아가씨.\""], ["he began severely, but still, as it were, admonishing her.", "그는 엄하게, 그러면서도 어딘지 타이르는 듯한 어조로 입을 열었다."], ["“Reflect, I am prepared to give you time for consideration.", "\"곰곰이 생각해 보세요, 생각할 시간을 드릴 용의가 있습니다."], ["Kindly observe this: if I were not so entirely convinced I should not, you may be sure, with my experience venture to accuse you so directly.", "이 점을 부디 유념하십시오. 만일 제가 이토록 완전히 확신하지 못했다면, 분명히 말하지만 제 경험상 이렇게 직접적으로 당신을 고발하는 모험은 하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["Seeing that for such direct accusation before witnesses, if false or even mistaken, I should myself in a certain sense be made responsible, I am aware of that.", "증인들 앞에서 이렇게 직접 고발했다가, 만일 그것이 거짓이거나 심지어 착오라 하더라도, 어떤 의미에서는 저 자신이 책임을 지게 될 테니까요, 그건 저도 알고 있습니다."], ["This morning I changed for my own purposes several five-per-cent securities for the sum of approximately three thousand roubles.", "오늘 아침 저는 제 용도에 따라 대략 삼천 루블에 해당하는 오 푼 이자 채권 몇 장을 현금으로 바꿨습니다."], ["The account is noted down in my pocket-book.", "그 내역은 제 수첩에 적혀 있습니다."], ["On my return home I proceeded to count the money--as Mr. Lebeziatnikov will bear witness--and after counting two thousand three hundred roubles I put the rest in my pocket-book in my coat pocket.", "집에 돌아와서 저는 돈을 세기 시작했고 — 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 증언해 줄 것입니다 — 이천삼백 루블을 센 다음 나머지는 외투 주머니의 수첩 안에 넣었습니다."], ["About five hundred roubles remained on the table and among them three notes of a hundred roubles each.", "탁자 위에는 오백 루블가량이 남아 있었고, 그중에는 백 루블짜리 지폐 세 장이 있었습니다."], ["At that moment you entered (at my invitation)--and all the time you were present you were exceedingly embarrassed; so that three times you jumped up in the middle of the conversation and tried to make off.", "바로 그때 당신이 (제 초대로) 들어왔지요 — 그리고 머무는 내내 당신은 몹시 안절부절못했습니다. 그래서 이야기를 나누다가 세 번이나 벌떡 일어나 나가려 했고요."], ["Mr. Lebeziatnikov can bear witness to this.", "이건 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 증언해 줄 수 있습니다."], ["You yourself, mademoiselle, probably will not refuse to confirm my statement that I invited you through Mr. Lebeziatnikov, solely in order to discuss with you the hopeless and destitute position of your relative, Katerina Ivanovna (whose dinner I was unable to attend), and the advisability of getting up something of the nature of a subscription, lottery or the like, for her benefit.", "아가씨, 당신 자신도, 제가 레베쟈트니코프 씨를 통해 당신을 초대한 것은 오로지 당신의 친척인 카테리나 이바노브나의 (제가 그분의 만찬에는 참석하지 못했습니다만) 절망적이고 궁핍한 처지에 대해, 그리고 그분을 위해 모금이나 추첨 같은 것을 마련하는 게 어떨지를 의논하기 위해서였다는 제 말을 부인하지 않으실 겁니다."], ["You thanked me and even shed tears.", "당신은 제게 고마워하며 심지어 눈물까지 흘렸지요."], ["I describe all this as it took place, primarily to recall it to your mind and secondly to show you that not the slightest detail has escaped my recollection.", "제가 이 모든 것을 있었던 그대로 묘사하는 것은, 첫째로 당신의 기억을 되살리기 위해서이고, 둘째로 사소한 것 하나도 제 기억에서 빠져나가지 않았음을 보여 드리기 위해서입니다."], ["Then I took a ten-rouble note from the table and handed it to you by way of first instalment on my part for the benefit of your relative. Mr.", "그러고 나서 저는 탁자에서 십 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 집어 당신의 친척을 위한 제 첫 기부금 명목으로 당신에게 건넸습니다."], ["Lebeziatnikov saw all this. Then I accompanied you to the door--you being still in the same state of embarrassment--after which, being left alone with Mr.", "레베쟈트니코프 씨가 이 모든 것을 보았습니다."], ["Lebeziatnikov I talked to him for ten minutes--then Mr. Lebeziatnikov went out and I returned to the table with the money lying on it, intending to count it and to put it aside, as I proposed doing before.", "그런 다음 저는 당신을 문까지 배웅했는데 — 당신은 여전히 똑같이 안절부절못하는 상태였습니다 — 그 후 레베쟈트니코프 씨와 단둘이 남아 십 분쯤 이야기를 나누었고, 그러고 나서 레베쟈트니코프 씨가 나갔고, 저는 돈이 놓여 있는 탁자로 돌아가, 아까 하려던 대로 그것을 세어 따로 치워 두려 했습니다."], ["To my surprise one hundred-rouble note had disappeared.", "그런데 놀랍게도 백 루블짜리 지폐 한 장이 사라져 있었습니다."], ["Kindly consider the position.", "부디 사정을 헤아려 주십시오."], ["Mr. Lebeziatnikov I cannot suspect.", "레베쟈트니코프 씨를 의심할 수는 없습니다."], ["I am ashamed to allude to such a supposition.", "그런 가정을 입에 올리는 것조차 부끄럽습니다."], ["I cannot have made a mistake in my reckoning, for the minute before your entrance I had finished my accounts and found the total correct.", "제가 셈을 잘못했을 리도 없으니, 당신이 들어오기 직전에 이미 셈을 마치고 총액이 맞는 것을 확인했기 때문입니다."], ["You will admit that recollecting your embarrassment, your eagerness to get away and the fact that you kept your hands for some time on the table, and taking into consideration your social position and the habits associated with it, I was, so to say, with horror and positively against my will, _compelled_ to entertain a suspicion--a cruel, but justifiable suspicion!", "당신의 그 안절부절못하던 태도, 어서 떠나려던 그 조급함, 그리고 한동안 탁자 위에 손을 올려 두고 있던 사실을 떠올리고, 또 당신의 사회적 처지와 그에 결부된 습성을 고려한다면, 제가, 말하자면 공포에 휩싸여, 그야말로 제 의지에 반하여, 어떤 의심을 — 잔혹하지만 정당한 의심을 — 품지 않을 수 없었음을 당신도 인정하실 겁니다!"], ["I will add further and repeat that in spite of my positive conviction, I realise that I run a certain risk in making this accusation, but as you see, I could not let it pass.", "한 가지 더 덧붙이고 거듭 말하건대, 제 확고한 확신에도 불구하고 이런 고발을 하는 데 제가 어느 정도 위험을 무릅쓰고 있음을 잘 압니다. 하지만 보시다시피 저는 이 일을 그냥 넘길 수 없었습니다."], ["I have taken action and I will tell you why: solely, madam, solely owing to your black ingratitude!", "저는 조치를 취했고, 그 이유를 말씀드리지요. 오로지, 부인, 오로지 당신의 그 시커먼 배은망덕 때문입니다!"], ["Why!", "아니!"], ["I invite you for the benefit of your destitute relative, I present you with my donation of ten roubles and you, on the spot, repay me for all that with such an action.", "저는 당신의 궁핍한 친척을 위해 당신을 초대하고, 십 루블의 기부금을 건넸는데, 당신은 그 자리에서 이런 짓으로 그 모든 것을 갚다니요."], ["It is too bad!", "이건 너무 심합니다!"], ["You need a lesson. Reflect!", "당신은 따끔한 교훈이 필요해요."], ["Moreover, like a true friend I beg you--and you could have no better friend at this moment--think what you are doing, otherwise I shall be immovable!", "잘 생각해 보세요! 게다가 진정한 벗으로서 부탁드립니다 — 지금 이 순간 당신에게 저보다 나은 벗은 없을 겁니다 — 당신이 지금 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 생각해 보세요, 그러지 않으면 저는 결코 물러서지 않을 테니까요!"], ["Well, what do you say?”", "자, 어떻게 하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I have taken nothing,”", "\"저는 아무것도 안 가져갔어요.\""], ["Sonia whispered in terror, “you gave me ten roubles, here it is, take it.”", "소냐가 공포에 질려 속삭였다. \"당신이 십 루블을 주셨고, 여기 있어요, 가져가세요.\""]], [["Sonia pulled her handkerchief out of her pocket, untied a corner of it, took out the ten-rouble note and gave it to Luzhin.", "소냐는 주머니에서 손수건을 꺼내 한쪽 모서리의 매듭을 풀고는 십 루블짜리 지폐를 꺼내 루진에게 건넸다."]], [["“And the hundred roubles you do not confess to taking?”", "\"그럼 백 루블을 가져간 것은 인정하지 않겠다는 거요?\""], ["he insisted reproachfully, not taking the note.", "그는 지폐를 받지 않고 나무라듯 다그쳤다."]], [["Sonia looked about her.", "소냐는 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["All were looking at her with such awful, stern, ironical, hostile eyes.", "모두가 그토록 무섭고, 엄하고, 비웃는 듯한, 적의에 찬 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다."], ["She looked at Raskolnikov...", "그녀는 라스콜니코프를 보았다……"], ["he stood against the wall, with his arms crossed, looking at her with glowing eyes.", "그는 팔짱을 낀 채 벽에 기대서서 이글거리는 눈으로 그녀를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Good God!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""], ["broke from Sonia.", "소냐의 입에서 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Amalia Ivanovna, we shall have to send word to the police and therefore I humbly beg you meanwhile to send for the house porter,”", "\"아말리야 이바노브나, 경찰에 알려야 할 테니, 그동안 부디 문지기를 불러 주시기를 삼가 청합니다.\" 루진이 부드럽게, 심지어 친절하게 말했다."], ["Luzhin said softly and even kindly.", "\"고트 데어 바름헤르치게!* 저 여자가 도둑인 줄 알았다니까요.\" 아말리야 이바노브나가 두 손을 번쩍 쳐들며 외쳤다."]], [["“_Gott der Barmherzige!_ I knew she was the thief,” cried Amalia Ivanovna, throwing up her hands.", "* 고트 데어 바름헤르치게(Gott der Barmherzige): 독일어로 \"자비로우신 하느님\"이라는 뜻."]], [["“You knew it?”", "\"알고 있었다고요?\""], ["Luzhin caught her up, “then I suppose you had some reason before this for thinking so.", "루진이 그녀의 말을 받았다. \"그렇다면 진작부터 그렇게 생각할 만한 무슨 이유가 있었던 모양이군요."], ["I beg you, worthy Amalia Ivanovna, to remember your words which have been uttered before witnesses.”", "훌륭하신 아말리야 이바노브나, 증인들 앞에서 하신 그 말을 기억해 두시기를 부탁드립니다.\""]], [["There was a buzz of loud conversation on all sides.", "사방에서 시끄러운 말소리가 웅성거렸다."], ["All were in movement.", "모두가 들썩이기 시작했다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고요!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, suddenly realising the position, and she rushed at Luzhin.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 사태를 불현듯 깨닫고는 소리치며 루진에게 달려들었다. \"뭐라고요!"], ["“What! You accuse her of stealing?", "이 애가 도둑질을 했다고 고발하는 거예요?"], ["Sonia?", "소냐가?"], ["Ah, the wretches, the wretches!”", "아, 악당 같으니, 악당 같으니!\""]], [["And running to Sonia she flung her wasted arms round her and held her as in a vise.", "그러고는 소냐에게 달려가 그 야윈 두 팔로 그녀를 끌어안고 죔쇠처럼 꼭 붙들었다."]], [["“Sonia!", "\"소냐!"], ["how dared you take ten roubles from him?", "어떻게 이 사람한테서 십 루블을 받았니?"], ["Foolish girl!", "어리석은 것!"], ["Give it to me!", "그거 이리 줘!"], ["Give me the ten roubles at once--here!”", "당장 그 십 루블을 내게 줘 — 여기!\""]], [["And snatching the note from Sonia, Katerina Ivanovna crumpled it up and flung it straight into Luzhin’s face.", "그러고는 소냐에게서 지폐를 낚아채어, 카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 구겨 곧장 루진의 얼굴에 내던졌다."], ["It hit him in the eye and fell on the ground.", "지폐는 그의 눈에 맞고 바닥에 떨어졌다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna hastened to pick it up.", "아말리야 이바노브나가 얼른 그것을 주우려 달려들었다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch lost his temper.", "표트르 페트로비치는 화를 참지 못했다."]], [["“Hold that mad woman!”", "\"저 미친 여자를 붙잡아요!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["At that moment several other persons, besides Lebeziatnikov, appeared in the doorway, among them the two ladies.", "바로 그때 레베쟈트니코프 말고도 몇 사람이 더 문간에 나타났는데, 그중에는 그 두 부인도 있었다."]], [["“What! Mad?", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["Am I mad?", "미쳤다고요?"], ["Idiot!”", "내가 미쳤다고요?"], ["shrieked Katerina Ivanovna.", "멍청이!\" 카테리나 이바노브나가 새된 소리로 악을 썼다."], ["“You are an idiot yourself, pettifogging lawyer, base man!", "\"당신이야말로 멍청이예요, 너절한 협잡꾼 변호사 같으니, 비열한 인간!"], ["Sonia, Sonia take his money!", "소냐, 소냐가 이 사람 돈을 가져갔다고요!"], ["Sonia a thief!", "소냐가 도둑이라니!"], ["Why, she’d give away her last penny!”", "아니, 이 애는 마지막 한 푼까지 남에게 줘 버릴 애예요!\""], ["and Katerina Ivanovna broke into hysterical laughter.", "그러더니 카테리나 이바노브나는 발작적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Did you ever see such an idiot?”", "\"이런 멍청이를 본 적이 있어요?\""], ["she turned from side to side.", "그녀는 이쪽저쪽으로 고개를 돌렸다."], ["“And you too?”", "\"당신도 그래요?\""], ["she suddenly saw the landlady, “and you too, sausage eater, you declare that she is a thief, you trashy Prussian hen’s leg in a crinoline!", "그녀는 문득 안주인을 보았다. \"당신도 그래, 소시지나 처먹는 주제에, 이 애가 도둑이라고 단언하는 거야, 이 크리놀린이나 두른 너절한 프로이센 닭다리 같으니라고!"], ["She hasn’t been out of this room: she came straight from you, you wretch, and sat down beside me, everyone saw her.", "이 애는 이 방에서 나간 적이 없어. 당신한테서 곧장 와서, 이 못된 것아, 내 옆에 앉았다고, 다들 봤어."], ["She sat here, by Rodion Romanovitch.", "여기, 로지온 로마노비치 옆에 앉아 있었어."], ["Search her!", "이 애를 뒤져 봐!"], ["Since she’s not left the room, the money would have to be on her!", "방을 나간 적이 없으니, 돈이 있다면 이 애 몸에 있을 거 아냐!"], ["Search her, search her!", "뒤져 봐, 뒤져 보라고!"], ["But if you don’t find it, then excuse me, my dear fellow, you’ll answer for it!", "하지만 못 찾으면, 미안하지만 이보세요, 당신이 책임져야 할 거예요!"], ["I’ll go to our Sovereign, to our Sovereign, to our gracious Tsar himself, and throw myself at his feet, to-day, this minute!", "저는 우리 폐하께, 우리 폐하께, 우리 자애로우신 차르 폐하께 직접 찾아가서, 그분 발밑에 엎드리겠어요, 오늘, 지금 당장!"], ["I am alone in the world!", "저는 이 세상에 혼자예요!"], ["They would let me in!", "그분들은 저를 들여보내 주실 거예요!"], ["Do you think they wouldn’t?", "안 그러실 것 같아요?"], ["You’re wrong, I will get in!", "천만에요, 저는 들어갈 거예요!"], ["I will get in!", "들어갈 거라고요!"], ["You reckoned on her meekness!", "당신은 이 애가 온순한 걸 노린 거예요!"], ["You relied upon that!", "그걸 믿고 그런 거지!"], ["But I am not so submissive, let me tell you!", "하지만 나는 그렇게 고분고분하지 않아요, 그건 알아 두라고요!"], ["You’ve gone too far yourself.", "당신이 너무 지나쳤어요."], ["Search her, search her!”", "뒤져 봐, 뒤져 보라고!\""]], [["And Katerina Ivanovna in a frenzy shook Luzhin and dragged him towards Sonia.", "그러고는 카테리나 이바노브나가 광란에 빠져 루진을 흔들며 소냐 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."]], [["“I am ready, I’ll be responsible...", "\"좋습니다, 제가 책임지지요……"], ["but calm yourself, madam, calm yourself.", "하지만 진정하세요, 부인, 진정하시라고요."], ["I see that you are not so submissive!...", "당신이 그렇게 고분고분하지 않다는 건 알겠습니다!……"], ["Well, well, but as to that...”", "자, 자, 그건 그렇고…….\""], ["Luzhin muttered, “that ought to be before the police...", "루진이 중얼거렸다. \"그건 경찰 앞에서 해야 할 일인데……"], ["though indeed there are witnesses enough as it is....", "하긴 사실 증인은 이미 충분하니……"], ["I am ready....", "저는 준비됐습니다……"], ["But in any case it’s difficult for a man...", "하지만 어쨌든 남자로서는 곤란하군요……"], ["on account of her sex....", "이 여자의 성별 때문에……"], ["But with the help of Amalia Ivanovna...", "그래도 아말리야 이바노브나의 도움이 있으면……"], ["though, of course, it’s not the way to do things....", "물론 이런 식으로 처리할 일은 아니지만……"], ["How is it to be done?”", "어떻게 하면 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“As you will!", "\"마음대로 해요!"], ["Let anyone who likes search her!”", "원하는 사람 누구든 이 애를 뒤져 봐요!\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤다."], ["“Sonia, turn out your pockets!", "\"소냐, 주머니를 뒤집어 봐!"], ["See!", "봐!"], ["Look, monster, the pocket is empty, here was her handkerchief!", "보라고, 괴물 같으니, 주머니가 비었잖아, 여기 손수건이 있었고!"], ["Here is the other pocket, look!", "여기 다른 주머니도, 봐!"], ["D’you see, d’you see?”", "보여, 보이냐고?\""]], [["And Katerina Ivanovna turned--or rather snatched--both pockets inside out.", "그러고는 카테리나 이바노브나가 양쪽 주머니를 뒤집었다 — 아니, 차라리 거머쥐듯 홱 뒤집었다."], ["But from the right pocket a piece of paper flew out and describing a parabola in the air fell at Luzhin’s feet.", "그런데 오른쪽 주머니에서 종잇조각 하나가 튀어나와 공중에 포물선을 그리며 루진의 발치에 떨어졌다."], ["Everyone saw it, several cried out.", "모두가 그것을 보았고, 몇 사람은 소리를 질렀다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch stooped down, picked up the paper in two fingers, lifted it where all could see it and opened it.", "표트르 페트로비치가 몸을 굽혀 두 손가락으로 종이를 집어 들고는 모두가 볼 수 있도록 치켜들어 펼쳤다."], ["It was a hundred-rouble note folded in eight.", "그것은 여덟 겹으로 접은 백 루블짜리 지폐였다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch held up the note showing it to everyone.", "표트르 페트로비치는 그 지폐를 치켜들어 모두에게 보여 주었다."]], [["“Thief!", "\"도둑년!"], ["Out of my lodging.", "내 집에서 나가!"], ["Police, police!”", "경찰, 경찰!\""], ["yelled Amalia Ivanovna.", "아말리야 이바노브나가 악을 썼다."], ["“They must to Siberia be sent!", "\"이런 것들은 시베리아로 보내 버려야 해!"], ["Away!”", "꺼져!\""]], [["Exclamations arose on all sides.", "사방에서 고함이 터져 나왔다."], ["Raskolnikov was silent, keeping his eyes fixed on Sonia, except for an occasional rapid glance at Luzhin.", "라스콜니코프는 잠자코, 이따금 루진에게 재빠른 눈길을 던지는 것 말고는 줄곧 소냐에게 시선을 고정한 채였다."], ["Sonia stood still, as though unconscious.", "소냐는 정신을 잃은 듯 가만히 서 있었다."], ["She was hardly able to feel surprise.", "그녀는 놀랄 여력조차 거의 없었다."], ["Suddenly the colour rushed to her cheeks; she uttered a cry and hid her face in her hands.", "갑자기 그녀의 두 뺨으로 핏기가 확 몰려들었다. 그녀는 외마디 소리를 지르며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“No, it wasn’t I!", "\"아니에요, 제가 아니에요!"], ["I didn’t take it!", "저는 안 가져갔어요!"], ["I know nothing about it,”", "저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰라요.\""], ["she cried with a heartrending wail, and she ran to Katerina Ivanovna, who clasped her tightly in her arms, as though she would shelter her from all the world.", "그녀가 가슴이 찢어질 듯한 울부짖음으로 외치고는 카테리나 이바노브나에게 달려갔고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 온 세상으로부터 그녀를 감싸 주려는 듯 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다."]], [["“Sonia!", "\"소냐!"], ["Sonia!", "소냐!"], ["I don’t believe it!", "나는 안 믿어!"], ["You see, I don’t believe it!”", "봐, 나는 안 믿는다고!\""], ["she cried in the face of the obvious fact, swaying her to and fro in her arms like a baby, kissing her face continually, then snatching at her hands and kissing them, too, “you took it!", "그녀는 명백한 사실을 앞에 두고도 외치며, 갓난아기처럼 그녀를 품에 안고 앞뒤로 흔들고, 끊임없이 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추다가, 다시 그 손을 붙들고 거기에도 입을 맞추었다. \"네가 가져갔다니!"], ["How stupid these people are!", "이 사람들은 어쩜 이리도 멍청할까!"], ["Oh dear!", "아이고!"], ["You are fools, fools,”", "너희는 바보야, 바보들이라고.\""], ["she cried, addressing the whole room, “you don’t know, you don’t know what a heart she has, what a girl she is!", "그녀는 방 안 전체를 향해 외쳤다. \"너희는 몰라, 이 애가 얼마나 고운 마음씨를 가졌는지, 이 애가 어떤 아이인지 너희는 몰라!"], ["She take it, she?", "이 애가 가져갔다고, 이 애가?"], ["She’d sell her last rag, she’d go barefoot to help you if you needed it, that’s what she is!", "이 애는 자기 마지막 누더기까지 팔아서라도, 너희가 어려우면 맨발로라도 도우러 갈 애야, 그런 애라고!"], ["She has the yellow passport because my children were starving, she sold herself for us!", "이 애가 누런 감찰을 받게 된 것도 내 아이들이 굶주리고 있었기 때문이야, 우리를 위해 제 몸을 판 거라고!"], ["Ah, husband, husband!", "아, 여보, 여보!"], ["Do you see?", "보여요?"], ["Do you see?", "보이냐고요?"], ["What a memorial dinner for you!", "이게 당신을 위한 추도 만찬이라니!"], ["Merciful heavens!", "자비로우신 하느님!"], ["Defend her, why are you all standing still?", "이 애를 지켜 줘요, 왜 다들 가만히 서 있는 거예요?"], ["Rodion Romanovitch, why don’t you stand up for her?", "로지온 로마노비치, 왜 당신은 이 애 편을 들어 주지 않는 거예요?"], ["Do you believe it, too?", "당신도 그걸 믿는 거예요?"], ["You are not worth her little finger, all of you together!", "너희는 전부 다 합쳐도 이 애 새끼손가락만도 못해! 오, 하느님!"], ["Good God! Defend her now, at least!”", "적어도 지금만이라도 이 애를 지켜 주세요!\""]], [["The wail of the poor, consumptive, helpless woman seemed to produce a great effect on her audience.", "가엾고, 폐병을 앓고, 무력한 이 여인의 울부짖음은 듣는 이들에게 커다란 영향을 미치는 듯했다."], ["The agonised, wasted, consumptive face, the parched blood-stained lips, the hoarse voice, the tears unrestrained as a child’s, the trustful, childish and yet despairing prayer for help were so piteous that everyone seemed to feel for her.", "고통에 찌들고, 야위고, 폐병 든 얼굴, 바싹 마르고 피가 엉긴 입술, 쉰 목소리, 아이처럼 거침없이 흐르는 눈물, 그 미덥고 어린아이 같으면서도 절망적인 도움의 호소가 어찌나 애처로운지, 모두가 그녀에게 연민을 느끼는 것 같았다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch at any rate was at once moved to _compassion_.", "적어도 표트르 페트로비치만큼은 곧바로 연민에 마음이 움직였다."]], [["“Madam, madam, this incident does not reflect upon you!”", "\"부인, 부인, 이 사건은 당신과는 무관합니다!\" 그가 인상적으로 외쳤다."], ["he cried impressively, “no one would take upon himself to accuse you of being an instigator or even an accomplice in it, especially as you have proved her guilt by turning out her pockets, showing that you had no previous idea of it.", "\"누구도 당신을 이 일의 교사자, 심지어 공범이라고 감히 고발하지는 않을 겁니다. 더구나 당신이 직접 이 애의 주머니를 뒤집어 죄를 입증하셨으니, 미리 알지 못하셨다는 게 드러난 셈이지요."], ["I am most ready, most ready to show compassion, if poverty, so to speak, drove Sofya Semyonovna to it, but why did you refuse to confess, mademoiselle?", "만일, 말하자면 가난이 소피야 세묘노브나를 거기까지 내몰았다면, 저는 기꺼이, 기꺼이 연민을 베풀 용의가 있습니다만, 아가씨, 어째서 당신은 자백하기를 거부했습니까?"], ["Were you afraid of the disgrace?", "그 불명예가 두려웠나요?"], ["The first step?", "첫걸음이라서?"], ["You lost your head, perhaps?", "아마도 정신이 아득해졌던 거겠지요?"], ["One can quite understand it....", "충분히 이해할 만한 일입니다……"], ["But how could you have lowered yourself to such an action?", "그러나 어떻게 그런 짓에까지 자신을 떨어뜨릴 수 있단 말입니까?"], ["Gentlemen,”", "여러분,\""], ["he addressed the whole company, “gentlemen!", "그가 좌중 전체를 향해 말했다. \"여러분!"], ["Compassionate and, so to say, commiserating these people, I am ready to overlook it even now in spite of the personal insult lavished upon me!", "이 사람들을 동정하고, 말하자면 가엾이 여기는 마음에서, 저는 제게 퍼부어진 개인적인 모욕에도 불구하고 지금이라도 이 일을 눈감아 줄 용의가 있습니다!"], ["And may this disgrace be a lesson to you for the future,”", "그리고 이 불명예가 앞으로 당신에게 교훈이 되기를 바랍니다.\""], ["he said, addressing Sonia, “and I will carry the matter no further.", "그는 소냐를 향해 말했다. \"그리고 저는 이 일을 더 이상 추궁하지 않겠습니다."], ["Enough!”", "이만하면 됐습니다!\""]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch stole a glance at Raskolnikov.", "표트르 페트로비치는 라스콜니코프를 슬쩍 훔쳐보았다."], ["Their eyes met, and the fire in Raskolnikov’s seemed ready to reduce him to ashes.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 라스콜니코프의 눈 속 불길은 당장이라도 그를 재로 만들어 버릴 것만 같았다."], ["Meanwhile Katerina Ivanovna apparently heard nothing.", "한편 카테리나 이바노브나는 아무것도 듣지 못하는 모양이었다."], ["She was kissing and hugging Sonia like a madwoman.", "그녀는 미친 여자처럼 소냐에게 입을 맞추고 끌어안고 있었다."], ["The children, too, were embracing Sonia on all sides, and Polenka--though she did not fully understand what was wrong--was drowned in tears and shaking with sobs, as she hid her pretty little face, swollen with weeping, on Sonia’s shoulder.", "아이들도 사방에서 소냐를 껴안았고, 폴렌카는 — 무엇이 잘못된 것인지 온전히 이해하지는 못했지만 — 눈물에 잠겨 흐느낌으로 몸을 떨며, 울어서 퉁퉁 부은 그 예쁘고 작은 얼굴을 소냐의 어깨에 파묻었다."]], [["“How vile!”", "\"이 무슨 비열한 짓이야!\""], ["a loud voice cried suddenly in the doorway.", "갑자기 문간에서 큰 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch looked round quickly.", "표트르 페트로비치가 재빨리 돌아보았다."]], [["“What vileness!”", "\"이 무슨 비열한 짓이냐고!\""], ["Lebeziatnikov repeated, staring him straight in the face.", "레베쟈트니코프가 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 노려보며 되풀이했다."]], [["Pyotr Petrovitch gave a positive start--all noticed it and recalled it afterwards.", "표트르 페트로비치는 분명히 흠칫했다 — 모두가 그것을 알아챘고 나중에 그 일을 떠올렸다."], ["Lebeziatnikov strode into the room.", "레베쟈트니코프가 성큼성큼 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["“And you dared to call me as witness?”", "\"그러고도 감히 나를 증인이라고 내세웠단 말입니까?\""], ["he said, going up to Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그가 표트르 페트로비치에게 다가가며 말했다."]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 소리요?"], ["What are you talking about?”", "무슨 말을 하는 거요?\""], ["muttered Luzhin.", "루진이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I mean that you...", "\"내 말은, 당신이……"], ["are a slanderer, that’s what my words mean!”", "중상모략가라는 거요, 내 말은 바로 그 뜻이오!\""], ["Lebeziatnikov said hotly, looking sternly at him with his short-sighted eyes.", "레베쟈트니코프가 근시안의 눈으로 그를 엄하게 노려보며 격하게 말했다."]], [["He was extremely angry.", "그는 몹시 화가 나 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov gazed intently at him, as though seizing and weighing each word.", "라스콜니코프는 한마디 한마디를 붙잡아 저울질하듯 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Again there was a silence.", "다시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch indeed seemed almost dumbfounded for the first moment.", "표트르 페트로비치는 첫 순간 정말 거의 말문이 막힌 듯했다."]], [["“If you mean that for me,...”", "\"만약 그게 나를 두고 하는 말이라면…….\""], ["he began, stammering.", "그가 더듬거리며 입을 열었다."], ["“But what’s the matter with you?", "\"한데 대체 당신 왜 이러는 거요?"], ["Are you out of your mind?”", "정신이 나갔소?\""]], [["“I’m in my mind, but you are a scoundrel!", "\"내 정신은 멀쩡하고, 당신은 악당이오!"], ["Ah, how vile! I have heard everything.", "아, 이 무슨 비열한 짓인가!"], ["I kept waiting on purpose to understand it, for I must own even now it is not quite logical....", "나는 다 들었소. 그걸 이해하려고 일부러 기다리고 있었는데, 솔직히 말해 지금도 완전히 앞뒤가 맞지는 않소……"], ["What you have done it all for I can’t understand.”", "당신이 대체 무엇 때문에 이런 짓을 했는지 나는 도무지 모르겠소.\""]], [["“Why, what have I done then?", "\"아니, 그래 내가 뭘 했단 말이오?"], ["Give over talking in your nonsensical riddles!", "그 말도 안 되는 수수께끼 같은 소리는 그만하시오!"], ["Or maybe you are drunk!”", "아니면 술에 취한 거요!\""]], [["“You may be a drunkard, perhaps, vile man, but I am not!", "\"당신이야 술꾼일지 모르지, 비열한 인간 같으니, 하지만 나는 아니오!"], ["I never touch vodka, for it’s against my convictions.", "나는 보드카는 입에도 안 대요, 그건 내 신념에 어긋나니까."], ["Would you believe it, he, he himself, with his own hands gave Sofya Semyonovna that hundred-rouble note--I saw it, I was a witness, I’ll take my oath!", "믿어지겠소, 저자가, 저자가 직접, 제 손으로 소피야 세묘노브나에게 그 백 루블짜리 지폐를 건넸소 — 내가 봤소, 내가 증인이오, 맹세할 수 있소!"], ["He did it, he!”", "저자가 한 짓이오, 저자가!\""], ["repeated Lebeziatnikov, addressing all.", "레베쟈트니코프가 모두를 향해 되풀이했다."]], [["“Are you crazy, milksop?”", "\"제정신이오, 이 풋내기야?\""], ["squealed Luzhin.", "루진이 새된 소리를 질렀다."], ["“She is herself before you--she herself here declared just now before everyone that I gave her only ten roubles.", "\"저 여자가 당신 앞에 있잖소 — 저 여자 자신이 방금 모두 앞에서 내가 십 루블만 줬다고 단언했소."], ["How could I have given it to her?”", "그러니 내가 어떻게 그걸 줬다는 거요?\""]], [["“I saw it, I saw it,”", "\"내가 봤소, 내가 봤단 말이오.\""], ["Lebeziatnikov repeated, “and though it is against my principles, I am ready this very minute to take any oath you like before the court, for I saw how you slipped it in her pocket.", "레베쟈트니코프가 되풀이했다. \"그리고 비록 내 원칙에는 어긋나지만, 나는 지금 당장 법정에서 당신이 원하는 어떤 맹세라도 할 용의가 있소, 왜냐하면 당신이 그것을 저 여자 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣는 걸 내가 봤으니까."], ["Only like a fool I thought you did it out of kindness!", "다만 나는 바보같이 당신이 친절한 마음에서 그러는 줄로만 알았던 거요!"], ["When you were saying good-bye to her at the door, while you held her hand in one hand, with the other, the left, you slipped the note into her pocket.", "당신이 문에서 저 여자에게 작별 인사를 하면서, 한 손으로 그 손을 잡고 있는 동안, 다른 손, 그 왼손으로 지폐를 저 여자 주머니에 슬쩍 밀어 넣었소."], ["I saw it, I saw it!”", "내가 봤소, 내가 봤단 말이오!\""]], [["Luzhin turned pale.", "루진의 얼굴이 창백해졌다."]], [["“What lies!”", "\"무슨 거짓말을!\""], ["he cried impudently, “why, how could you, standing by the window, see the note?", "그가 뻔뻔하게 외쳤다. \"아니, 창가에 서 있던 당신이 어떻게 그 지폐를 봤단 말이오?"], ["You fancied it with your short-sighted eyes.", "당신의 그 근시안으로 헛것을 본 거겠지."], ["You are raving!”", "헛소리를 지껄이고 있구려!\""]], [["“No, I didn’t fancy it.", "\"아니, 나는 헛것을 본 게 아니오."], ["And though I was standing some way off, I saw it all.", "그리고 비록 좀 떨어진 데 서 있었지만, 나는 그걸 다 봤소."], ["And though it certainly would be hard to distinguish a note from the window--that’s true--I knew for certain that it was a hundred-rouble note, because, when you were going to give Sofya Semyonovna ten roubles, you took up from the table a hundred-rouble note (I saw it because I was standing near then, and an idea struck me at once, so that I did not forget you had it in your hand).", "물론 창가에서 지폐를 분간하기는 어려웠을 거요 — 그건 사실이오 — 하지만 나는 그게 백 루블짜리 지폐라는 걸 확실히 알고 있었소, 왜냐하면 당신이 소피야 세묘노브나에게 십 루블을 주려 할 때, 탁자에서 백 루블짜리 지폐를 집어 들었으니까 (그때 내가 가까이 서 있어서 봤고, 그 순간 어떤 생각이 떠올라서 당신 손에 그게 들려 있다는 걸 잊지 않았던 거요)."], ["You folded it and kept it in your hand all the time.", "당신은 그걸 접어서 줄곧 손에 쥐고 있었소."], ["I didn’t think of it again until, when you were getting up, you changed it from your right hand to your left and nearly dropped it!", "그러다가 당신이 일어설 때 그걸 오른손에서 왼손으로 옮기면서 하마터면 떨어뜨릴 뻔했는데, 그제야 나는 다시 그 생각이 났소."], ["I noticed it because the same idea struck me again, that you meant to do her a kindness without my seeing.", "그게 눈에 띈 건, 당신이 내가 보지 못하게 저 여자에게 친절을 베풀려는 거라는 똑같은 생각이 또 들었기 때문이오."], ["You can fancy how I watched you and I saw how you succeeded in slipping it into her pocket.", "내가 당신을 얼마나 유심히 지켜봤을지 상상이 갈 거요, 그래서 당신이 그걸 저 여자 주머니에 용케 슬쩍 집어넣는 걸 봤소."], ["I saw it, I saw it, I’ll take my oath.”", "내가 봤소, 내가 봤단 말이오, 맹세하겠소.\""]], [["Lebeziatnikov was almost breathless.", "레베쟈트니코프는 거의 숨이 넘어갈 지경이었다."], ["Exclamations arose on all hands chiefly expressive of wonder, but some were menacing in tone.", "사방에서 고함이 터져 나왔는데, 주로 놀라움을 나타내는 것이었으나 더러는 위협적인 어조도 있었다."], ["They all crowded round Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그들은 모두 표트르 페트로비치 주위로 몰려들었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna flew to Lebeziatnikov.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 레베쟈트니코프에게 달려갔다."]], [["“I was mistaken in you!", "\"제가 당신을 잘못 봤어요!"], ["Protect her!", "이 애를 지켜 주세요!"], ["You are the only one to take her part!", "이 애 편을 들어 줄 사람은 당신뿐이에요!"], ["She is an orphan.", "이 애는 고아예요."], ["God has sent you!”", "하느님이 당신을 보내 주신 거예요!\""]], [["Katerina Ivanovna, hardly knowing what she was doing, sank on her knees before him.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 거의 모른 채 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“A pack of nonsense!”", "\"한 무더기 헛소리로군!\""], ["yelled Luzhin, roused to fury, “it’s all nonsense you’ve been talking!", "루진이 격분하여 악을 썼다. \"당신이 지껄인 건 죄다 헛소리요!"], ["‘An idea struck you, you didn’t think, you noticed’--what does it amount to?", "'어떤 생각이 떠올랐다, 생각하지 않았다, 알아챘다' — 그게 다 무슨 소용이오?"], ["So I gave it to her on the sly on purpose?", "그래, 내가 일부러 저 여자한테 몰래 그걸 줬다고?"], ["What for?", "무엇 때문에?"], ["With what object?", "무슨 목적으로?"], ["What have I to do with this...?”", "내가 이 여자와 무슨 상관이 있다고……?\""]], [["“What for?", "\"무엇 때문이냐고?"], ["That’s what I can’t understand, but that what I am telling you is the fact, that’s certain!", "그건 나도 모르겠소, 하지만 내가 당신에게 하는 말이 사실이라는 건 확실하오!"], ["So far from my being mistaken, you infamous criminal man, I remember how, on account of it, a question occurred to me at once, just when I was thanking you and pressing your hand.", "천만에, 나는 잘못 본 게 아니오, 이 파렴치한 범죄자 같으니, 나는 기억하오, 그 일 때문에, 내가 당신에게 고마워하며 당신 손을 잡고 있던 바로 그때, 한 가지 의문이 곧바로 떠올랐던 걸."], ["What made you put it secretly in her pocket?", "당신은 왜 그걸 저 여자 주머니에 몰래 집어넣었을까?"], ["Why you did it secretly, I mean?", "그러니까, 왜 몰래 그랬을까?"], ["Could it be simply to conceal it from me, knowing that my convictions are opposed to yours and that I do not approve of private benevolence, which effects no radical cure?", "그저 내게 감추려는 거였을까, 내 신념이 당신 것과 반대이고 내가 근본적인 치유가 못 되는 사사로운 자선을 인정하지 않는다는 걸 알고서?"], ["Well, I decided that you really were ashamed of giving such a large sum before me.", "그래서 나는, 당신이 내 앞에서 그렇게 큰돈을 주는 게 정말로 부끄러웠던 거라고 결론지었소."], ["Perhaps, too, I thought, he wants to give her a surprise, when she finds a whole hundred-rouble note in her pocket.", "어쩌면, 하고 나는 또 생각했지, 저 여자가 주머니에서 백 루블짜리 지폐를 통째로 발견하면 깜짝 놀라게 해 주려는 거라고."], ["(For I know, some benevolent people are very fond of decking out their charitable actions in that way.) Then the idea struck me, too, that you wanted to test her, to see whether, when she found it, she would come to thank you.", "(나도 알거든, 어떤 자선가들은 그런 식으로 제 선행을 치장하기를 무척 좋아한다는 걸.) 그러다가 또 이런 생각도 들었소, 당신이 저 여자를 시험해 보려는 거라고, 저 여자가 그걸 발견하면 고맙다고 찾아오는지 보려고."], ["Then, too, that you wanted to avoid thanks and that, as the saying is, your right hand should not know...", "그리고 또, 당신이 감사 인사를 피하고 싶어서, 속담처럼, 오른손이 하는 일을 왼손이 모르게……"], ["something of that sort, in fact.", "뭐 그런 식이라고."], ["I thought of so many possibilities that I put off considering it, but still thought it indelicate to show you that I knew your secret.", "하도 여러 가능성을 생각하느라 나는 그걸 곰곰이 따져 보는 걸 미뤄 두었는데, 그래도 당신 비밀을 안다고 당신에게 내색하는 건 무례한 일이라고 여겼소."], ["But another idea struck me again that Sofya Semyonovna might easily lose the money before she noticed it, that was why I decided to come in here to call her out of the room and to tell her that you put a hundred roubles in her pocket.", "그런데 또 다른 생각이 들었소, 소피야 세묘노브나가 미처 알아채기도 전에 그 돈을 잃어버리기 쉽겠다는 거였소, 그래서 나는 여기 들어와 저 여자를 방에서 불러내어, 당신이 백 루블을 저 여자 주머니에 넣었다고 말해 주기로 마음먹었던 거요."], ["But on my way I went first to Madame Kobilatnikov’s to take them the ‘General Treatise on the Positive Method’ and especially to recommend Piderit’s article (and also Wagner’s); then I come on here and what a state of things I find!", "그런데 오는 길에 먼저 코빌랴트니코프 부인 댁에 들러 『실증적 방법에 관한 일반 개론』을 가져다주고, 특히 피데리트의 논문을 (그리고 바그너의 것도) 추천하고 나서, 여기로 와 보니 이런 꼴이 벌어져 있는 거요!"], ["Now could I, could I, have all these ideas and reflections if I had not seen you put the hundred-rouble note in her pocket?”", "자, 그러니 내가, 내가, 당신이 백 루블짜리 지폐를 저 여자 주머니에 넣는 걸 보지 않았더라면, 이 모든 생각과 궁리를 할 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["When Lebeziatnikov finished his long-winded harangue with the logical deduction at the end, he was quite tired, and the perspiration streamed from his face.", "레베쟈트니코프가 마지막에 논리적인 결론까지 곁들여 그 장황한 일장 연설을 끝마쳤을 때, 그는 완전히 지쳐 얼굴에서 땀이 줄줄 흘러내렸다."], ["He could not, alas, even express himself correctly in Russian, though he knew no other language, so that he was quite exhausted, almost emaciated after this heroic exploit.", "가엾게도 그는 러시아어 말고는 다른 언어를 알지도 못하면서, 러시아어조차 제대로 구사하지 못했던 터라, 이 영웅적인 위업을 치르고 나니 완전히 기진맥진, 거의 수척해진 꼴이었다."], ["But his speech produced a powerful effect.", "그러나 그의 연설은 강력한 효과를 발휘했다."], ["He had spoken with such vehemence, with such conviction that everyone obviously believed him.", "그가 어찌나 격렬하게, 어찌나 확신에 차서 말했던지 모두가 분명 그를 믿었다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch felt that things were going badly with him.", "표트르 페트로비치는 일이 자기에게 불리하게 돌아가고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["“What is it to do with me if silly ideas did occur to you?”", "\"당신한테 어리석은 생각들이 떠올랐다 한들 그게 나와 무슨 상관이오?\""], ["he shouted, “that’s no evidence.", "그가 소리쳤다. \"그건 증거가 못 돼."], ["You may have dreamt it, that’s all!", "당신이 꿈을 꾼 건지도 모르지, 그게 다요!"], ["And I tell you, you are lying, sir.", "그리고 분명히 말하건대, 당신은 거짓말을 하고 있소, 선생."], ["You are lying and slandering from some spite against me, simply from pique, because I did not agree with your free-thinking, godless, social propositions!”", "당신은 나에 대한 무슨 악감정에서, 그저 앙심에서 거짓말을 하고 중상하는 거요, 내가 당신의 그 자유사상적이고 무신론적인 사회주의 주장에 동의하지 않았다는 이유로!\""]], [["But this retort did not benefit Pyotr Petrovitch.", "그러나 이 반박은 표트르 페트로비치에게 득이 되지 않았다."], ["Murmurs of disapproval were heard on all sides.", "사방에서 못마땅해하는 웅성거림이 들려왔다."]], [["“Ah, that’s your line now, is it!”", "\"아하, 이제는 그렇게 나오시겠다, 이거요!\""], ["cried Lebeziatnikov, “that’s nonsense!", "레베쟈트니코프가 외쳤다. \"그건 헛소리요!"], ["Call the police and I’ll take my oath!", "경찰을 부르시오, 나는 맹세하겠소!"], ["There’s only one thing I can’t understand: what made him risk such a contemptible action.", "한 가지만은 도무지 모르겠소. 무엇 때문에 저자가 이런 비열한 짓을 무릅썼는지."], ["Oh, pitiful, despicable man!”", "오, 가련하고, 비열한 인간 같으니!\""]], [["“I can explain why he risked such an action, and if necessary, I, too, will swear to it,”", "\"왜 저 사람이 그런 짓을 무릅썼는지 제가 설명할 수 있고, 필요하다면 저 또한 맹세하겠습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov said at last in a firm voice, and he stepped forward.", "마침내 라스콜니코프가 단호한 목소리로 말하며 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["He appeared to be firm and composed.", "그는 단호하고 침착해 보였다."], ["Everyone felt clearly, from the very look of him that he really knew about it and that the mystery would be solved.", "그의 모습만 보고도 모두가, 그가 정말로 이 일을 알고 있으며 이제 그 수수께끼가 풀리리라는 것을 분명히 느꼈다."]], [["“Now I can explain it all to myself,”", "\"이제야 저는 이 모든 것을 제 나름대로 설명할 수 있습니다.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, addressing Lebeziatnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 레베쟈트니코프를 향해 말했다."], ["“From the very beginning of the business, I suspected that there was some scoundrelly intrigue at the bottom of it.", "\"이 일의 맨 처음부터 저는 그 밑바닥에 무슨 악당 같은 음모가 깔려 있다고 의심했습니다."], ["I began to suspect it from some special circumstances known to me only, which I will explain at once to everyone: they account for everything.", "저만 아는 어떤 특별한 정황들 때문에 그것을 의심하기 시작했는데, 그것을 지금 모두에게 곧바로 설명하겠습니다. 그것들이 모든 것을 설명해 줍니다."], ["Your valuable evidence has finally made everything clear to me.", "당신의 그 귀중한 증언이 마침내 제게 모든 것을 분명히 밝혀 주었습니다."], ["I beg all, all to listen.", "부디 여러분 모두, 모두 들어 주시기 바랍니다."], ["This gentleman (he pointed to Luzhin) was recently engaged to be married to a young lady--my sister, Avdotya Romanovna Raskolnikov.", "이 신사는 (그는 루진을 가리켰다) 얼마 전 어느 젊은 아가씨와 약혼한 사이였습니다 — 제 누이, 아브도티야 로마노브나 라스콜니코바입니다."], ["But coming to Petersburg he quarrelled with me, the day before yesterday, at our first meeting and I drove him out of my room--I have two witnesses to prove it.", "그런데 페테르부르크에 와서 그제, 우리가 처음 만난 자리에서 그는 저와 다투었고, 저는 그를 제 방에서 쫓아냈습니다 — 이를 증명할 증인이 두 사람 있습니다."], ["He is a very spiteful man....", "그는 매우 앙심 깊은 사람입니다……"], ["The day before yesterday I did not know that he was staying here, in your room, and that consequently on the very day we quarrelled--the day before yesterday--he saw me give Katerina Ivanovna some money for the funeral, as a friend of the late Mr. Marmeladov.", "그제 저는 그가 여기, 당신 방에 묵고 있다는 것을 몰랐고, 따라서 우리가 다툰 바로 그날 — 그제 — 그가, 고인이 된 마르멜라도프 씨의 친구로서 제가 카테리나 이바노브나에게 장례 비용으로 얼마간의 돈을 건네는 것을 보았다는 사실도 몰랐습니다."], ["He at once wrote a note to my mother and informed her that I had given away all my money, not to Katerina Ivanovna but to Sofya Semyonovna, and referred in a most contemptible way to the...", "그는 즉시 제 어머니에게 편지를 써서, 제가 가진 돈을 전부, 카테리나 이바노브나가 아니라 소피야 세묘노브나에게 줘 버렸다고 알렸고, 더없이 비열한 방식으로 소피야 세묘노브나의……"], ["character of Sofya Semyonovna, that is, hinted at the character of my attitude to Sofya Semyonovna.", "품행을 들먹였습니다, 그러니까 소피야 세묘노브나에 대한 제 태도의 성격을 넌지시 암시한 것이지요."], ["All this you understand was with the object of dividing me from my mother and sister, by insinuating that I was squandering on unworthy objects the money which they had sent me and which was all they had.", "보시다시피 이 모든 것은, 제가 그들이 보내 준, 그것이 그들의 전 재산인 그 돈을 부당한 대상에게 탕진하고 있다고 넌지시 흘림으로써, 저를 어머니와 누이로부터 떼어 놓으려는 목적에서 한 짓이었습니다."], ["Yesterday evening, before my mother and sister and in his presence, I declared that I had given the money to Katerina Ivanovna for the funeral and not to Sofya Semyonovna and that I had no acquaintance with Sofya Semyonovna and had never seen her before, indeed.", "어제저녁, 어머니와 누이 앞에서, 그리고 그가 있는 자리에서, 저는 그 돈을 장례 비용으로 카테리나 이바노브나에게 준 것이지 소피야 세묘노브나에게 준 것이 아니며, 소피야 세묘노브나와는 일면식도 없고 그날까지 한 번도 본 적조차 없다고 분명히 밝혔습니다."], ["At the same time I added that he, Pyotr Petrovitch Luzhin, with all his virtues, was not worth Sofya Semyonovna’s little finger, though he spoke so ill of her.", "동시에 저는, 그가, 표트르 페트로비치 루진이, 그 온갖 미덕을 다 가졌다 한들, 그렇게 험담을 해 대는 소피야 세묘노브나의 새끼손가락만도 못한 사람이라고 덧붙였습니다."], ["To his question--would I let Sofya Semyonovna sit down beside my sister, I answered that I had already done so that day.", "소피야 세묘노브나를 제 누이 곁에 앉히겠느냐는 그의 물음에, 저는 그날 이미 그렇게 했다고 대답했습니다."], ["Irritated that my mother and sister were unwilling to quarrel with me at his insinuations, he gradually began being unpardonably rude to them.", "제 어머니와 누이가 그의 빈정거림에 넘어가 저와 다투려 하지 않자 화가 난 그는, 차츰 그들에게 용서할 수 없을 만큼 무례하게 굴기 시작했습니다."], ["A final rupture took place and he was turned out of the house.", "결국 완전히 결렬되었고 그는 집에서 쫓겨났습니다. 이 모든 것이 어제저녁에 일어난 일입니다."], ["All this happened yesterday evening.", "이제 특별히 주목해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["Now I beg your special attention: consider: if he had now succeeded in proving that Sofya Semyonovna was a thief, he would have shown to my mother and sister that he was almost right in his suspicions, that he had reason to be angry at my putting my sister on a level with Sofya Semyonovna, that, in attacking me, he was protecting and preserving the honour of my sister, his betrothed.", "생각해 보십시오. 만일 그가 지금 소피야 세묘노브나가 도둑이라는 것을 증명하는 데 성공했다면, 그는 제 어머니와 누이에게 자기 의심이 거의 옳았음을, 제가 제 누이를 소피야 세묘노브나와 같은 수준에 놓은 데 화를 낼 만한 이유가 있었음을, 그리고 저를 공격함으로써 자기 약혼녀인 제 누이의 명예를 지키고 보전하고 있었음을 보여 줄 수 있었을 겁니다."], ["In fact he might even, through all this, have been able to estrange me from my family, and no doubt he hoped to be restored to favour with them; to say nothing of revenging himself on me personally, for he has grounds for supposing that the honour and happiness of Sofya Semyonovna are very precious to me.", "사실 그는 이 모든 것을 통해 저를 제 가족에게서 멀어지게 할 수도 있었을 테고, 분명 그들의 환심을 되찾기를 바랐을 겁니다. 저에게 개인적으로 복수하는 것은 말할 것도 없고요, 왜냐하면 그에게는 소피야 세묘노브나의 명예와 행복이 제게 매우 소중하다고 짐작할 만한 근거가 있으니까요."], ["That was what he was working for!", "그가 노린 게 바로 그것이었습니다!"], ["That’s how I understand it.", "저는 그렇게 이해합니다."], ["That’s the whole reason for it and there can be no other!”", "이게 이 모든 일의 전모이고, 다른 이유란 있을 수 없습니다!\""]], [["It was like this, or somewhat like this, that Raskolnikov wound up his speech which was followed very attentively, though often interrupted by exclamations from his audience.", "이렇게, 혹은 대략 이렇게 라스콜니코프는 자신의 연설을 마무리지었는데, 그 연설은 청중의 고함에 자주 끊기긴 했어도 매우 주의 깊게 경청되었다."], ["But in spite of interruptions he spoke clearly, calmly, exactly, firmly.", "그러나 방해에도 불구하고 그는 명료하고, 차분하고, 정확하고, 단호하게 말했다."], ["His decisive voice, his tone of conviction and his stern face made a great impression on everyone.", "그의 단호한 목소리와 확신에 찬 어조, 그리고 엄한 얼굴은 모두에게 커다란 인상을 남겼다."]], [["“Yes, yes, that’s it,”", "\"맞아, 맞아, 바로 그거요.\""], ["Lebeziatnikov assented gleefully, “that must be it, for he asked me, as soon as Sofya Semyonovna came into our room, whether you were here, whether I had seen you among Katerina Ivanovna’s guests.", "레베쟈트니코프가 신이 나서 맞장구쳤다. \"틀림없이 그럴 거요, 왜냐하면 소피야 세묘노브나가 우리 방에 들어오자마자 저자가 나에게, 당신이 여기 있는지, 내가 카테리나 이바노브나의 손님들 가운데서 당신을 봤는지 물었으니까."], ["He called me aside to the window and asked me in secret.", "저자는 나를 창가로 따로 불러내어 몰래 물었소."], ["It was essential for him that you should be here!", "당신이 여기 있는 게 저자에게는 꼭 필요했던 거요!"], ["That’s it, that’s it!”", "바로 그거요, 바로 그거라고!\""]], [["Luzhin smiled contemptuously and did not speak.", "루진은 경멸하듯 미소 지을 뿐 말이 없었다."], ["But he was very pale.", "그러나 그는 무척 창백했다."], ["He seemed to be deliberating on some means of escape.", "어떤 빠져나갈 방도를 궁리하고 있는 듯했다."], ["Perhaps he would have been glad to give up everything and get away, but at the moment this was scarcely possible.", "어쩌면 그는 모든 것을 포기하고 자리를 뜨고 싶었을지도 모르지만, 지금 이 순간 그것은 거의 불가능했다."], ["It would have implied admitting the truth of the accusations brought against him.", "그것은 자기에게 제기된 고발이 사실임을 인정하는 꼴이 될 테니까."], ["Moreover, the company, which had already been excited by drink, was now too much stirred to allow it.", "게다가 이미 술로 흥분해 있던 사람들이 이제는 너무 들끓어서 그것을 용납하지 않았다."], ["The commissariat clerk, though indeed he had not grasped the whole position, was shouting louder than anyone and was making some suggestions very unpleasant to Luzhin.", "군수품 담당 서기는, 사실 사태 전모를 파악하지는 못했으면서도, 누구보다 큰 소리로 외쳐 대며 루진에게 매우 불쾌한 제안들을 늘어놓고 있었다. 그러나 거기 있던 사람들이 다 취한 것은 아니었으니, 온 방에서 세입자들이 몰려들었다."], ["But not all those present were drunk; lodgers came in from all the rooms.", "폴란드인 세 사람은 엄청나게 흥분하여 끊임없이 그에게 \"이 판은 라이다크*다!\""], ["The three Poles were tremendously excited and were continually shouting at him: “The _pan_ is a _lajdak_!”", "하고 외쳐 대며 폴란드어로 위협을 중얼거렸다. 소냐는 잔뜩 긴장하여 귀를 기울이고 있었으나, 그녀 역시 그 모든 것을 다 파악하지는 못하는 듯했다."], ["and muttering threats in Polish.", "그녀는 마치 방금 의식을 되찾은 사람 같았다."], ["Sonia had been listening with strained attention, though she too seemed unable to grasp it all; she seemed as though she had just returned to consciousness.", "그녀는 라스콜니코프에게서 눈을 떼지 못했으니, 자신의 모든 안전이 그에게 달려 있음을 느꼈던 것이다."], ["She did not take her eyes off Raskolnikov, feeling that all her safety lay in him.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 힘겹고 고통스럽게 숨을 몰아쉬며 몹시 기진맥진해 보였다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna breathed hard and painfully and seemed fearfully exhausted.", "아말리야 이바노브나는 누구보다 멍청한 표정으로, 입을 떡 벌린 채 무슨 일이 일어난 것인지 갈피를 못 잡고 서 있었다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna stood looking more stupid than anyone, with her mouth wide open, unable to make out what had happened.", "그녀는 그저 표트르 페트로비치가 어쩌다 곤경에 빠졌다는 것만 알 뿐이었다. * 판(pan): 폴란드어로 \"나리, 양반\"이라는 뜻."], ["She only saw that Pyotr Petrovitch had somehow come to grief.", "** 라이다크(lajdak): 폴란드어로 \"비열한 놈, 악당\"이라는 뜻."]], [["Raskolnikov was attempting to speak again, but they did not let him.", "라스콜니코프는 다시 말을 하려 했으나 사람들이 그러도록 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["Everyone was crowding round Luzhin with threats and shouts of abuse.", "모두가 위협과 욕설을 퍼부으며 루진 주위로 몰려들었다."], ["But Pyotr Petrovitch was not intimidated.", "그러나 표트르 페트로비치는 겁먹지 않았다."], ["Seeing that his accusation of Sonia had completely failed, he had recourse to insolence:", "소냐에 대한 자기 고발이 완전히 실패했음을 알아채자, 그는 뻔뻔함에 호소했다."]], [["“Allow me, gentlemen, allow me!", "\"잠깐만요, 여러분, 잠깐만!"], ["Don’t squeeze, let me pass!”", "밀지 마시고, 지나가게 해 주십시오!\""], ["he said, making his way through the crowd.", "그가 군중을 헤치고 나아가며 말했다."], ["“And no threats, if you please!", "\"그리고 제발 위협은 하지 마세요!"], ["I assure you it will be useless, you will gain nothing by it.", "분명히 말하지만 그래 봐야 소용없고, 얻을 것도 없을 겁니다."], ["On the contrary, you’ll have to answer, gentlemen, for violently obstructing the course of justice.", "오히려 여러분이, 폭력으로 사법 절차를 방해한 데 대해 책임을 지셔야 할 겁니다."], ["The thief has been more than unmasked, and I shall prosecute.", "도둑년의 정체는 백일하에 드러나고도 남았으니, 저는 고소할 것입니다."], ["Our judges are not so blind and...", "우리 재판관들은 그렇게 눈멀지도 않았고……"], ["not so drunk, and will not believe the testimony of two notorious infidels, agitators, and atheists, who accuse me from motives of personal revenge which they are foolish enough to admit....", "그렇게 취하지도 않았으니, 개인적인 복수심에서 — 어리석게도 그 동기를 스스로 인정한 — 저를 고발하는, 두 명의 악명 높은 불신자에 선동가요 무신론자인 자들의 증언을 믿지 않을 겁니다……"], ["Yes, allow me to pass!”", "자, 지나가게 해 주십시오!\""]], [["“Don’t let me find a trace of you in my room!", "\"내 방에서 당신 흔적이라도 다시 보게 되는 일이 없도록 하시오!"], ["Kindly leave at once, and everything is at an end between us!", "당장 나가 주시오, 그러면 우리 사이는 모든 게 끝이오!"], ["When I think of the trouble I’ve been taking, the way I’ve been expounding...", "내가 들인 그 수고를, 내가 어떻게 설명해 왔는지를……"], ["all this fortnight!”", "이 보름 내내 말이오, 그걸 생각하면!\""]], [["“I told you myself to-day that I was going, when you tried to keep me; now I will simply add that you are a fool.", "\"당신이 나를 붙잡으려 할 때 오늘 내가 직접 나가겠다고 말했잖소. 이제 그저 한마디 덧붙이자면, 당신은 바보요."], ["I advise you to see a doctor for your brains and your short sight.", "당신 머리와 그 근시안을 위해 의사한테 가 보길 권하오."], ["Let me pass, gentlemen!”", "지나가게 해 주시오, 여러분!\""]], [["He forced his way through.", "그는 억지로 길을 헤치고 나아갔다."], ["But the commissariat clerk was unwilling to let him off so easily: he picked up a glass from the table, brandished it in the air and flung it at Pyotr Petrovitch; but the glass flew straight at Amalia Ivanovna.", "그러나 군수품 담당 서기는 그를 그렇게 쉽게 놓아줄 마음이 없었다. 그는 탁자에서 잔 하나를 집어 들어 공중에 휘두르고는 표트르 페트로비치에게 던졌으나, 잔은 곧장 아말리야 이바노브나에게로 날아갔다."], ["She screamed, and the clerk, overbalancing, fell heavily under the table.", "그녀가 비명을 질렀고, 서기는 몸의 중심을 잃고 탁자 밑으로 쿵 하고 쓰러졌다."], ["Pyotr Petrovitch made his way to his room and half an hour later had left the house.", "표트르 페트로비치는 제 방으로 빠져나갔고, 삼십 분 후에는 그 집을 떠났다."], ["Sonia, timid by nature, had felt before that day that she could be ill-treated more easily than anyone, and that she could be wronged with impunity.", "본디 소심한 소냐는, 그날 이전부터 자신이 누구보다 쉽게 학대받을 수 있고, 아무 탈 없이 부당한 일을 당할 수 있는 존재라는 것을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Yet till that moment she had fancied that she might escape misfortune by care, gentleness and submissiveness before everyone.", "그럼에도 그 순간까지 그녀는, 모두 앞에서 조심하고, 온순하고, 고분고분하게 굶으로써 불행을 피할 수 있으리라 여겨 왔다."], ["Her disappointment was too great.", "그녀의 실망은 너무도 컸다."], ["She could, of course, bear with patience and almost without murmur anything, even this.", "물론 그녀는 무엇이든, 심지어 이런 일까지도, 인내심을 가지고 거의 군말 없이 견딜 수 있었다."], ["But for the first minute she felt it too bitter.", "그러나 첫 순간만큼은 너무도 쓰라리게 느껴졌다."], ["In spite of her triumph and her justification--when her first terror and stupefaction had passed and she could understand it all clearly--the feeling of her helplessness and of the wrong done to her made her heart throb with anguish and she was overcome with hysterical weeping.", "자신의 승리와 결백이 입증되었음에도 — 처음의 공포와 망연자실이 가시고 모든 것을 분명히 이해할 수 있게 되자 — 자신의 무력함과 자신에게 가해진 부당함에 대한 느낌이 그녀의 심장을 고뇌로 두근거리게 했고, 그녀는 발작적인 울음에 사로잡혔다."], ["At last, unable to bear any more, she rushed out of the room and ran home, almost immediately after Luzhin’s departure.", "마침내 더는 견디지 못하고 그녀는 루진이 떠난 거의 직후에 방에서 뛰쳐나가 집으로 달려갔다."], ["When amidst loud laughter the glass flew at Amalia Ivanovna, it was more than the landlady could endure.", "요란한 웃음소리 가운데 잔이 아말리야 이바노브나에게 날아갔을 때, 그것은 안주인이 참을 수 있는 한계를 넘어선 일이었다."], ["With a shriek she rushed like a fury at Katerina Ivanovna, considering her to blame for everything.", "비명을 지르며 그녀는 분노의 화신처럼 카테리나 이바노브나에게 달려들었으니, 모든 것이 그녀 탓이라고 여겼던 것이다."]], [["“Out of my lodgings!", "\"내 집에서 나가!"], ["At once!", "당장!"], ["Quick march!”", "어서 꺼져!\""]], [["And with these words she began snatching up everything she could lay her hands on that belonged to Katerina Ivanovna, and throwing it on the floor.", "그러고는 이 말과 함께 그녀는 손에 잡히는 대로 카테리나 이바노브나의 물건이란 물건은 죄다 낚아채어 바닥에 내던지기 시작했다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna, pale, almost fainting, and gasping for breath, jumped up from the bed where she had sunk in exhaustion and darted at Amalia Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나는, 창백하게, 거의 실신할 지경으로, 숨을 헐떡이며, 기진하여 쓰러져 있던 침대에서 벌떡 일어나 아말리야 이바노브나에게 달려들었다."], ["But the battle was too unequal: the landlady waved her away like a feather.", "그러나 그 싸움은 너무도 불공평했다. 안주인은 그녀를 깃털처럼 가볍게 뿌리쳐 버렸다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["As though that godless calumny was not enough--this vile creature attacks me! What!", "그 신을 모르는 중상모략으로도 모자라서 — 이 비열한 것이 나한테 덤벼들어!"], ["On the day of my husband’s funeral I am turned out of my lodging!", "뭐라고요! 남편 장례식 날에 내가 이 집에서 쫓겨나다니!"], ["After eating my bread and salt she turns me into the street, with my orphans!", "내 빵과 소금을 얻어먹고서, 내 고아들과 함께 나를 길거리로 내쫓다니!"], ["Where am I to go?”", "나더러 어디로 가란 말이야?\""], ["wailed the poor woman, sobbing and gasping.", "가엾은 여인이 흐느끼고 헐떡이며 울부짖었다."], ["“Good God!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""], ["she cried with flashing eyes, “is there no justice upon earth?", "그녀가 눈을 번뜩이며 외쳤다. \"이 땅에 정의란 없단 말입니까?"], ["Whom should you protect if not us orphans?", "우리 고아들이 아니면 누구를 지켜 주셔야 한단 말입니까?"], ["We shall see!", "두고 봅시다!"], ["There is law and justice on earth, there is, I will find it!", "이 땅에 법과 정의는 있어요, 있고말고요, 내가 찾아내고 말 거예요!"], ["Wait a bit, godless creature!", "두고 봐, 신을 모르는 것아!"], ["Polenka, stay with the children, I’ll come back.", "폴렌카, 아이들이랑 같이 있어라, 엄마가 돌아올 테니."], ["Wait for me, if you have to wait in the street.", "나를 기다려, 길거리에서 기다려야 하더라도."], ["We will see whether there is justice on earth!”", "이 땅에 정의가 있는지 없는지 두고 봅시다!\""]], [["And throwing over her head that green shawl which Marmeladov had mentioned to Raskolnikov, Katerina Ivanovna squeezed her way through the disorderly and drunken crowd of lodgers who still filled the room, and, wailing and tearful, she ran into the street--with a vague intention of going at once somewhere to find justice.", "그러고는 마르멜라도프가 라스콜니코프에게 이야기했던 바로 그 초록색 숄을 머리에 뒤집어쓰고, 카테리나 이바노브나는 아직도 방을 가득 메우고 있던 어수선하고 술 취한 세입자들 무리를 비집고 나가, 울부짖으며 눈물을 흘리며 거리로 뛰쳐나갔다 — 당장 어디든 가서 정의를 찾겠다는 막연한 작정으로."], ["Polenka with the two little ones in her arms crouched, terrified, on the trunk in the corner of the room, where she waited trembling for her mother to come back.", "폴렌카는 어린 두 아이를 품에 안고, 겁에 질려 방 한구석의 트렁크 위에 웅크린 채, 어머니가 돌아오기를 떨면서 기다렸다."], ["Amalia Ivanovna raged about the room, shrieking, lamenting and throwing everything she came across on the floor.", "아말리야 이바노브나는 방 안을 미친 듯이 돌아다니며 비명을 지르고, 한탄하고, 닥치는 대로 물건을 바닥에 내던졌다."], ["The lodgers talked incoherently, some commented to the best of their ability on what had happened, others quarrelled and swore at one another, while others struck up a song....", "세입자들은 두서없이 떠들어 댔는데, 더러는 무슨 일이 일어났는지 저마다 아는 대로 논평하고, 더러는 서로 다투며 욕설을 퍼붓고, 또 더러는 노래를 부르기 시작했다……"]], [["“Now it’s time for me to go,”", "'이제 내가 갈 때로군.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."], ["“Well, Sofya Semyonovna, we shall see what you’ll say now!”", "'자, 소피야 세묘노브나, 이제 당신이 무슨 말을 할지 두고 봅시다!'"]], [["And he set off in the direction of Sonia’s lodgings.", "그러고는 그는 소냐의 거처를 향해 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["Raskolnikov had been a vigorous and active champion of Sonia against Luzhin, although he had such a load of horror and anguish in his own heart.", "라스콜니코프는 가슴속에 그토록 무거운 공포와 번민을 짊어지고 있었음에도, 루진에 맞서 소냐를 위해 힘차고도 적극적으로 싸운 투사였다."], ["But having gone through so much in the morning, he found a sort of relief in a change of sensations, apart from the strong personal feeling which impelled him to defend Sonia.", "그러나 오전 내내 그토록 많은 일을 겪고 난 뒤라, 소냐를 변호하도록 그를 떠민 강렬한 개인적 감정과는 별개로, 그는 감각의 변화 속에서 일종의 안도를 느꼈다."], ["He was agitated too, especially at some moments, by the thought of his approaching interview with Sonia: he _had_ to tell her who had killed Lizaveta.", "또한 그는, 특히 어떤 순간들에는, 곧 다가올 소냐와의 대면을 생각하며 동요했다. 그는 리자베타를 죽인 자가 누구인지 그녀에게 말해야만 했다."], ["He knew the terrible suffering it would be to him and, as it were, brushed away the thought of it.", "그것이 자신에게 얼마나 끔찍한 고통이 될지 알았기에, 그는 말하자면 그 생각을 떨쳐 버리려 했다."], ["So when he cried as he left Katerina Ivanovna’s, “Well, Sofya Semyonovna, we shall see what you’ll say now!”", "그래서 카테리나 이바노브나의 집을 나서며 \"자, 소피야 세묘노브나, 이제 당신이 뭐라고 할지 두고 봅시다!\""], ["he was still superficially excited, still vigorous and defiant from his triumph over Luzhin.", "하고 외쳤을 때만 해도, 그는 여전히 겉으로는 흥분해 있었고, 루진을 이긴 승리감으로 여전히 기운차고 도전적이었다."], ["But, strange to say, by the time he reached Sonia’s lodging, he felt a sudden impotence and fear.", "그러나 이상하게도, 소냐의 거처에 다다랐을 무렵 그는 갑작스러운 무력감과 두려움에 사로잡혔다."], ["He stood still in hesitation at the door, asking himself the strange question: “Must he tell her who killed Lizaveta?”", "그는 문 앞에서 망설이며 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 스스로에게 기묘한 질문을 던졌다. \"리자베타를 죽인 자가 누구인지 그녀에게 정말 말해야만 하는가?\""], ["It was a strange question because he felt at the very time not only that he could not help telling her, but also that he could not put off the telling.", "그것은 기묘한 질문이었으니, 바로 그 순간 그는 그녀에게 말하지 않을 수 없을 뿐 아니라 그 고백을 미룰 수조차 없다는 것을 느끼고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He did not yet know why it must be so, he only _felt_ it, and the agonising sense of his impotence before the inevitable almost crushed him.", "왜 그래야만 하는지 그는 아직 알지 못했고, 그저 그렇게 느낄 뿐이었으며, 피할 수 없는 것 앞에서의 자신의 무력함에 대한 괴로운 자각이 그를 거의 짓눌러 버릴 듯했다."], ["To cut short his hesitation and suffering, he quickly opened the door and looked at Sonia from the doorway.", "자신의 망설임과 고통을 단번에 끊어 버리려고, 그는 재빨리 문을 열고 문지방에서 소냐를 바라보았다."], ["She was sitting with her elbows on the table and her face in her hands, but seeing Raskolnikov she got up at once and came to meet him as though she were expecting him.", "그녀는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 앉아 있었으나, 라스콜니코프를 보자 곧장 일어나 마치 그를 기다리고 있었던 듯 그를 맞으러 다가왔다."]], [["“What would have become of me but for you?”", "\"당신이 아니었다면 저는 어떻게 됐을까요?\""], ["she said quickly, meeting him in the middle of the room.", "그녀는 방 한가운데서 그를 맞으며 빠르게 말했다."]], [["Evidently she was in haste to say this to him.", "분명 그녀는 이 말을 그에게 서둘러 하고 싶어 했다."], ["It was what she had been waiting for.", "그것은 그녀가 줄곧 기다려 온 말이었다."]], [["Raskolnikov went to the table and sat down on the chair from which she had only just risen.", "라스콜니코프는 탁자로 가서, 그녀가 방금 일어선 그 의자에 앉았다."], ["She stood facing him, two steps away, just as she had done the day before.", "그녀는 어제 그랬듯이 그에게서 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에 마주 서 있었다."]], [["“Well, Sonia?” he said, and felt that his voice was trembling, “it was all due to ‘your social position and the habits associated with it.’ Did you understand that just now?”", "\"그래, 소냐?\" 그가 말했고, 자기 목소리가 떨리고 있음을 느꼈다. \"이게 다 '당신의 사회적 처지와 거기 따라붙는 습관들' 때문이지. 방금 그 말을 알아들었소?\""]], [["Her face showed her distress.", "그녀의 얼굴에 괴로움이 드러났다."]], [["“Only don’t talk to me as you did yesterday,”", "\"제발 어제처럼 저한테 말하지 말아요.\""], ["she interrupted him.", "그녀가 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“Please don’t begin it.", "\"제발 그러지 마세요."], ["There is misery enough without that.”", "그러지 않아도 비참한 일은 충분하니까요.\""]], [["She made haste to smile, afraid that he might not like the reproach.", "그녀는 그가 이 책망을 언짢아할까 봐 서둘러 미소를 지었다."]], [["“I was silly to come away from there.", "\"거기서 나와 버리다니 제가 어리석었어요."], ["What is happening there now?", "지금 거기서는 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있을까요?"], ["I wanted to go back directly, but I kept thinking that...", "곧장 돌아가고 싶었지만, 자꾸만……"], ["you would come.”", "당신이 올 거라는 생각이 들어서요.\""]], [["He told her that Amalia Ivanovna was turning them out of their lodging and that Katerina Ivanovna had run off somewhere “to seek justice.”", "그는 아말리야 이바노브나가 그들을 셋집에서 내쫓고 있으며, 카테리나 이바노브나가 \"정의를 찾겠다\"며 어디론가 뛰쳐나갔다고 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“My God!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""], ["cried Sonia, “let’s go at once....”", "소냐가 외쳤다. \"당장 가요…….\""]], [["And she snatched up her cape.", "그러고는 그녀가 망토를 낚아챘다."]], [["“It’s everlastingly the same thing!”", "\"늘 똑같은 소리로군!\""], ["said Raskolnikov, irritably.", "라스콜니코프가 짜증스레 말했다."], ["“You’ve no thought except for them!", "\"당신은 그들 생각밖에 없구려!"], ["Stay a little with me.”", "잠깐만 나하고 있어 주시오.\""]], [["“But...", "\"하지만……"], ["Katerina Ivanovna?”", "카테리나 이바노브나가요?\""]], [["“You won’t lose Katerina Ivanovna, you may be sure, she’ll come to you herself since she has run out,”", "\"카테리나 이바노브나를 놓칠 일은 없으니 걱정 마시오, 뛰쳐나갔으니 제 발로 당신을 찾아올 거요.\""], ["he added peevishly.", "그가 토라진 듯 덧붙였다."], ["“If she doesn’t find you here, you’ll be blamed for it....”", "\"여기서 당신을 못 찾으면, 도리어 당신 탓을 할 텐데…….\""]], [["Sonia sat down in painful suspense.", "소냐는 괴로운 불안 속에서 자리에 앉았다."], ["Raskolnikov was silent, gazing at the floor and deliberating.", "라스콜니코프는 입을 다문 채 바닥을 응시하며 무언가를 곰곰이 따져 보고 있었다."]], [["“This time Luzhin did not want to prosecute you,”", "\"이번에 루진은 당신을 고발하려 하지 않았소.\""], ["he began, not looking at Sonia, “but if he had wanted to, if it had suited his plans, he would have sent you to prison if it had not been for Lebeziatnikov and me.", "그가 소냐를 보지 않은 채 입을 열었다. \"하지만 그가 원했다면, 그게 자기 계획에 맞아떨어졌다면, 레베쟈트니코프와 내가 없었더라면 그는 당신을 감옥에 처넣었을 거요."], ["Ah?”", "안 그렇소?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she assented in a faint voice.", "그녀가 힘없는 목소리로 수긍했다."], ["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she repeated, preoccupied and distressed.", "그녀는 무언가에 골몰한 채 괴로워하며 되뇌었다."]], [["“But I might easily not have been there.", "\"하지만 내가 마침 거기 없었을 수도 있었지."], ["And it was quite an accident Lebeziatnikov’s turning up.”", "레베쟈트니코프가 나타난 것도 순전히 우연이었고.\""]], [["Sonia was silent.", "소냐는 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“And if you’d gone to prison, what then?", "\"그래서 당신이 감옥에 갔다면, 그땐 어떻게 됐겠소?"], ["Do you remember what I said yesterday?”", "어제 내가 했던 말, 기억하오?\""]], [["Again she did not answer.", "이번에도 그녀는 대답하지 않았다."], ["He waited.", "그가 기다렸다."]], [["“I thought you would cry out again ‘don’t speak of it, leave off.’”", "\"당신이 또 '그런 말 하지 말아요, 그만둬요' 하고 소리칠 줄 알았는데.\""], ["Raskolnikov gave a laugh, but rather a forced one.", "라스콜니코프는 웃음을 터뜨렸으나, 어딘가 억지스러운 웃음이었다."], ["“What, silence again?”", "\"뭐요, 또 침묵이오?\""], ["he asked a minute later.", "잠시 후 그가 물었다."], ["“We must talk about something, you know.", "\"무슨 얘기든 해야 하지 않겠소."], ["It would be interesting for me to know how you would decide a certain ‘problem’ as Lebeziatnikov would say.”", "레베쟈트니코프식으로 말하자면, 어떤 '문제'를 당신이라면 어떻게 결정할지 알아보는 것도 내겐 흥미로울 것 같소.\""], ["(He was beginning to lose the thread.) “No, really, I am serious.", "(그는 이야기의 갈피를 놓치기 시작했다.) \"아니, 정말로, 나는 진지하오."], ["Imagine, Sonia, that you had known all Luzhin’s intentions beforehand.", "상상해 보시오, 소냐, 당신이 루진의 의도를 미리 다 알고 있었다고."], ["Known, that is, for a fact, that they would be the ruin of Katerina Ivanovna and the children and yourself thrown in--since you don’t count yourself for anything--Polenka too...", "다시 말해 그것들이 카테리나 이바노브나와 아이들을, 그리고 덤으로 당신 자신까지 — 당신은 스스로를 아무것도 아니라고 여기니까 — 파멸시키리라는 것을 사실로서 알고 있었다고 말이오. 폴렌카도……"], ["for she’ll go the same way.", "그애도 같은 길을 가게 될 테니까."], ["Well, if suddenly it all depended on your decision whether he or they should go on living, that is whether Luzhin should go on living and doing wicked things, or Katerina Ivanovna should die?", "자, 만일 갑자기 모든 게 당신의 결정에 달리게 된다면, 그가 계속 살아야 할지 아니면 그들이 계속 살아야 할지, 다시 말해 루진이 계속 살며 못된 짓을 하도록 둘지 아니면 카테리나 이바노브나가 죽어야 할지 말이오."], ["How would you decide which of them was to die?", "당신은 둘 중 누가 죽어야 할지 어떻게 결정하겠소?"], ["I ask you?”", "묻겠소, 당신이라면?\""]], [["Sonia looked uneasily at him.", "소냐가 불안한 눈빛으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["There was something peculiar in this hesitating question, which seemed approaching something in a roundabout way.", "이 머뭇거리는 질문에는 무언가 기묘한 데가 있었으니, 마치 빙 둘러 무언가에 다가가는 것 같았다."]], [["“I felt that you were going to ask some question like that,”", "\"당신이 그런 비슷한 질문을 하리라는 걸 저는 느끼고 있었어요.\""], ["she said, looking inquisitively at him.", "그녀는 캐묻듯 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I dare say you did.", "\"그랬을 법도 하군."], ["But how is it to be answered?”", "하지만 거기엔 어떻게 대답하겠소?\""]], [["“Why do you ask about what could not happen?”", "\"왜 일어날 수도 없는 일을 물으세요?\""], ["said Sonia reluctantly.", "소냐가 마지못해 말했다."]], [["“Then it would be better for Luzhin to go on living and doing wicked things?", "\"그렇다면 루진이 계속 살며 못된 짓을 하는 편이 낫다는 거요?"], ["You haven’t dared to decide even that!”", "당신은 그것조차 결정할 엄두를 못 내는군!\""]], [["“But I can’t know the Divine Providence....", "\"하지만 저는 하느님의 섭리를 알 수 없어요……."], ["And why do you ask what can’t be answered?", "그리고 왜 대답할 수 없는 걸 물으시나요?"], ["What’s the use of such foolish questions?", "그런 어리석은 질문이 무슨 소용이에요?"], ["How could it happen that it should depend on my decision--who has made me a judge to decide who is to live and who is not to live?”", "어떻게 그게 제 결정에 달릴 수 있겠어요 — 누가 저를 누가 살고 누가 죽어야 할지 결정하는 심판관으로 세웠다고요?\""]], [["“Oh, if the Divine Providence is to be mixed up in it, there is no doing anything,”", "\"오, 하느님의 섭리까지 끌어들인다면야, 더 할 얘기가 없지.\""], ["Raskolnikov grumbled morosely.", "라스콜니코프가 침울하게 투덜거렸다."]], [["“You’d better say straight out what you want!”", "\"원하는 걸 차라리 똑바로 말해 주세요!\""], ["Sonia cried in distress.", "소냐가 괴로워하며 외쳤다."], ["“You are leading up to something again....", "\"또 무언가로 끌고 가시는군요……."], ["Can you have come simply to torture me?”", "그저 저를 괴롭히려고 오신 건가요?\""]], [["She could not control herself and began crying bitterly.", "그녀는 스스로를 다잡지 못하고 비통하게 울기 시작했다."], ["He looked at her in gloomy misery.", "그는 음울한 비참함 속에서 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Five minutes passed.", "오 분이 흘렀다."]], [["“Of course you’re right, Sonia,”", "\"물론 당신 말이 옳소, 소냐.\""], ["he said softly at last.", "마침내 그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["He was suddenly changed.", "그는 돌연 달라져 있었다."], ["His tone of assumed arrogance and helpless defiance was gone.", "짐짓 꾸민 오만함과 속절없는 반항의 어조가 사라지고 없었다."], ["Even his voice was suddenly weak.", "목소리마저 갑자기 약해져 있었다."], ["“I told you yesterday that I was not coming to ask forgiveness and almost the first thing I’ve said is to ask forgiveness....", "\"어제 나는 용서를 구하러 오는 게 아니라고 당신에게 말했는데, 와서 처음으로 한 말이 거의 용서를 구하는 거였구려……."], ["I said that about Luzhin and Providence for my own sake.", "루진과 섭리에 관한 그 얘기는 나 자신을 위해 한 거요."], ["I was asking forgiveness, Sonia....”", "나는 용서를 구하고 있었던 거요, 소냐…….\""]], [["He tried to smile, but there was something helpless and incomplete in his pale smile.", "그는 미소 지으려 했으나, 그 창백한 미소에는 무언가 속절없고 마무리되지 못한 것이 깃들어 있었다."], ["He bowed his head and hid his face in his hands.", "그는 고개를 떨구고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["And suddenly a strange, surprising sensation of a sort of bitter hatred for Sonia passed through his heart.", "그러자 갑자기, 소냐에 대한 일종의 쓰디쓴 증오와도 같은 기묘하고 놀라운 감각이 그의 가슴을 스쳐 지나갔다. 이 감각에 놀라고 두려워하는 듯 그는 고개를 들어 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["As it were wondering and frightened of this sensation, he raised his head and looked intently at her; but he met her uneasy and painfully anxious eyes fixed on him; there was love in them; his hatred vanished like a phantom.", "그러나 그는 자신에게 고정된 그녀의 불안하고 고통스럽게 애타는 눈길과 마주쳤다. 그 안에는 사랑이 있었다."], ["It was not the real feeling; he had taken the one feeling for the other.", "그의 증오는 환영처럼 사라졌다. 그것은 진짜 감정이 아니었다."], ["It only meant that _that_ minute had come.", "그는 한 감정을 다른 감정으로 착각했던 것이다. 그저 그 순간이 왔다는 뜻일 뿐이었다."]], [["He hid his face in his hands again and bowed his head.", "그는 다시 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 고개를 떨구었다."], ["Suddenly he turned pale, got up from his chair, looked at Sonia, and without uttering a word sat down mechanically on her bed.", "갑자기 그는 창백해지더니, 의자에서 일어나 소냐를 바라보았고, 한마디 말도 없이 기계적으로 그녀의 침대에 앉았다."]], [["His sensations that moment were terribly like the moment when he had stood over the old woman with the axe in his hand and felt that “he must not lose another minute.”", "그 순간 그의 감각은, 손에 도끼를 든 채 노파 위에 서서 \"단 일 분도 더 지체해서는 안 된다\"고 느꼈던 그 순간과 끔찍하리만치 닮아 있었다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이에요?\""], ["asked Sonia, dreadfully frightened.", "소냐가 몹시 겁에 질려 물었다."]], [["He could not utter a word.", "그는 한마디도 내뱉을 수 없었다."], ["This was not at all, not at all the way he had intended to “tell”", "이것은 그가 \"말하려고\""], ["and he did not understand what was happening to him now.", "작정했던 방식과는 전혀, 전혀 달랐고, 그는 지금 자신에게 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는지 알 수 없었다."], ["She went up to him, softly, sat down on the bed beside him and waited, not taking her eyes off him.", "그녀는 조용히 그에게 다가가 침대에 나란히 앉았고, 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 기다렸다."], ["Her heart throbbed and sank.", "그녀의 가슴이 두근거리다 철렁 내려앉았다."], ["It was unendurable; he turned his deadly pale face to her.", "견딜 수 없었다. 그는 죽은 사람처럼 창백한 얼굴을 그녀에게로 돌렸다."], ["His lips worked, helplessly struggling to utter something.", "그의 입술이 무언가를 내뱉으려 속절없이 애쓰며 달싹였다."], ["A pang of terror passed through Sonia’s heart.", "소냐의 가슴을 공포의 통증이 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이에요?\""], ["she repeated, drawing a little away from him.", "그녀는 그에게서 조금 물러나며 되물었다."]], [["“Nothing, Sonia, don’t be frightened....", "\"아무것도 아니오, 소냐, 겁내지 마시오……."], ["It’s nonsense.", "헛소리요."], ["It really is nonsense, if you think of it,”", "곰곰이 생각해 보면, 정말 헛소리지.\""], ["he muttered, like a man in delirium.", "그는 헛소리에 빠진 사람처럼 중얼거렸다."], ["“Why have I come to torture you?”", "\"내가 왜 당신을 괴롭히러 왔을까?\""], ["he added suddenly, looking at her.", "그가 그녀를 바라보며 불쑥 덧붙였다."], ["“Why, really?", "\"정말, 왜일까?"], ["I keep asking myself that question, Sonia....”", "자꾸 스스로에게 그 질문을 던지게 되는구려, 소냐…….\""]], [["He had perhaps been asking himself that question a quarter of an hour before, but now he spoke helplessly, hardly knowing what he said and feeling a continual tremor all over.", "어쩌면 그는 십오 분 전에도 스스로에게 그 질문을 던지고 있었을 테지만, 지금 그는 무력하게, 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 거의 알지 못한 채, 온몸이 끊임없이 떨려 오는 것을 느끼며 말하고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, how you are suffering!”", "\"오, 당신은 어쩌면 그렇게 괴로워하나요!\""], ["she muttered in distress, looking intently at him.", "그녀가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 괴로움에 차서 중얼거렸다."]], [["“It’s all nonsense....", "\"다 헛소리요……."], ["Listen, Sonia.”", "들어 보시오, 소냐.\""], ["He suddenly smiled, a pale helpless smile for two seconds.", "그가 갑자기 미소 지었는데, 이 초쯤 이어진 창백하고 속절없는 미소였다."], ["“You remember what I meant to tell you yesterday?”", "\"어제 내가 당신에게 하려던 말, 기억하오?\""]], [["Sonia waited uneasily.", "소냐가 불안하게 기다렸다."]], [["“I said as I went away that perhaps I was saying good-bye for ever, but that if I came to-day I would tell you who...", "\"떠나면서 내가 그랬소, 어쩌면 영영 작별 인사를 하는 건지도 모르지만, 만일 오늘 다시 온다면 누가……"], ["who killed Lizaveta.”", "누가 리자베타를 죽였는지 당신에게 말해 주겠다고.\""]], [["She began trembling all over.", "그녀는 온몸을 떨기 시작했다."]], [["“Well, here I’ve come to tell you.”", "\"자, 이렇게 말하러 왔소.\""]], [["“Then you really meant it yesterday?”", "\"그럼 어제 그 말이 정말 진심이었어요?\""], ["she whispered with difficulty.", "그녀가 힘겹게 속삭였다."], ["“How do you know?”", "\"어떻게 아세요?\""], ["she asked quickly, as though suddenly regaining her reason.", "그녀가 별안간 이성을 되찾은 듯 다급히 물었다."]], [["Sonia’s face grew paler and paler, and she breathed painfully.", "소냐의 얼굴이 점점 더 창백해졌고, 그녀는 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬었다."]], [["“I know.”", "\"나는 알고 있소.\""]], [["She paused a minute.", "그녀는 잠시 멈칫했다."]], [["“Have they found him?”", "\"그를 찾아냈나요?\""], ["she asked timidly.", "그녀가 조심스레 물었다."]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["“Then how do you know about _it_?”", "\"그럼 어떻게 그 일을 아세요?\""], ["she asked again, hardly audibly and again after a minute’s pause.", "그녀가 또다시, 거의 들리지 않을 만큼, 또 한 번의 침묵 뒤에 물었다."]], [["He turned to her and looked very intently at her.", "그는 그녀에게로 몸을 돌려 그녀를 아주 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“Guess,”", "\"맞혀 보시오.\""], ["he said, with the same distorted helpless smile.", "그가 아까와 똑같이 일그러지고 속절없는 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["A shudder passed over her.", "그녀의 온몸에 전율이 스쳤다."]], [["“But you...", "\"하지만 당신……"], ["why do you frighten me like this?”", "왜 저를 이렇게 겁주시나요?\""], ["she said, smiling like a child.", "그녀가 어린아이처럼 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I must be a great friend of _his_... since I know,”", "\"내가 그의 아주 가까운 친구임이 틀림없지……, 알고 있으니까.\""], ["Raskolnikov went on, still gazing into her face, as though he could not turn his eyes away.", "라스콜니코프가, 마치 눈을 돌릴 수 없는 듯 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말을 이었다."], ["“He...", "\"그는……"], ["did not mean to kill that Lizaveta...", "그 리자베타를 죽일 생각은 없었소……."], ["he...", "그는……"], ["killed her accidentally....", "우연히 그녀를 죽인 거요……."], ["He meant to kill the old woman when she was alone and he went there...", "노파가 혼자 있을 때 죽일 작정으로 거기 갔는데……"], ["and then Lizaveta came in...", "그때 리자베타가 들어왔고……"], ["he killed her too.”", "그래서 그녀까지 죽인 거요.\""]], [["Another awful moment passed.", "또 한 번 끔찍한 순간이 흘렀다."], ["Both still gazed at one another.", "둘은 여전히 서로를 응시하고 있었다."]], [["“You can’t guess, then?”", "\"그럼, 맞히지 못하겠소?\""], ["he asked suddenly, feeling as though he were flinging himself down from a steeple.", "그가, 마치 첨탑 꼭대기에서 몸을 던지는 듯한 기분으로 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“N-no...”", "\"아……"], ["whispered Sonia.", "아니요…….\" 소냐가 속삭였다."]], [["“Take a good look.”", "\"잘 봐 두시오.\""]], [["As soon as he had said this again, the same familiar sensation froze his heart.", "그가 이 말을 다시 내뱉자마자, 그 익숙한 감각이 또 그의 가슴을 얼어붙게 했다."], ["He looked at her and all at once seemed to see in her face the face of Lizaveta.", "그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 별안간 그녀의 얼굴에서 리자베타의 얼굴이 보이는 듯했다."], ["He remembered clearly the expression in Lizaveta’s face, when he approached her with the axe and she stepped back to the wall, putting out her hand, with childish terror in her face, looking as little children do when they begin to be frightened of something, looking intently and uneasily at what frightens them, shrinking back and holding out their little hands on the point of crying.", "그는 도끼를 들고 다가갔을 때 리자베타의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 표정을 또렷이 떠올렸다. 그녀가 벽 쪽으로 물러서며 손을 내밀던 모습, 얼굴에 어린 어린아이 같은 공포, 마치 어린아이들이 무언가에 막 겁을 먹기 시작할 때처럼, 자기를 놀라게 하는 것을 뚫어지게 불안하게 바라보며, 뒤로 움츠러들어 막 울음을 터뜨릴 듯 작은 손을 내미는 그 모습을."], ["Almost the same thing happened now to Sonia.", "지금 소냐에게도 거의 똑같은 일이 일어났다."], ["With the same helplessness and the same terror, she looked at him for a while and, suddenly putting out her left hand, pressed her fingers faintly against his breast and slowly began to get up from the bed, moving further from him and keeping her eyes fixed even more immovably on him.", "똑같은 무력함과 똑같은 공포에 휩싸여 그녀는 한동안 그를 바라보더니, 별안간 왼손을 내밀어 손가락으로 그의 가슴을 희미하게 누르고는, 그에게서 점점 멀어지며 두 눈을 더욱더 미동도 없이 그에게 고정한 채 천천히 침대에서 일어서기 시작했다."], ["Her terror infected him.", "그녀의 공포가 그에게 옮아붙었다."], ["The same fear showed itself on his face.", "똑같은 두려움이 그의 얼굴에도 드러났다."], ["In the same way he stared at her and almost with the same _childish_ smile.", "그 역시 똑같이 그녀를 응시했고, 거의 똑같은 어린아이 같은 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Have you guessed?”", "\"맞혔소?\""], ["he whispered at last.", "마침내 그가 속삭였다."]], [["“Good God!”", "\"오, 하느님!\""], ["broke in an awful wail from her bosom.", "그녀의 가슴에서 끔찍한 비명이 터져 나왔다."]], [["She sank helplessly on the bed with her face in the pillows, but a moment later she got up, moved quickly to him, seized both his hands and, gripping them tight in her thin fingers, began looking into his face again with the same intent stare.", "그녀는 얼굴을 베개에 파묻은 채 속절없이 침대로 쓰러졌으나, 잠시 후 일어나 재빨리 그에게 다가가더니, 두 손을 붙잡아 가느다란 손가락으로 꽉 움켜쥐고는, 다시 아까와 똑같이 뚫어지게 그의 얼굴을 들여다보기 시작했다."], ["In this last desperate look she tried to look into him and catch some last hope.", "이 마지막 필사적인 눈길로 그녀는 그의 속을 들여다보며 어떤 마지막 희망을 붙잡으려 했다."], ["But there was no hope; there was no doubt remaining; it was all true!", "그러나 희망은 없었다. 의심의 여지는 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Later on, indeed, when she recalled that moment, she thought it strange and wondered why she had seen at once that there was no doubt.", "모든 게 사실이었다! 정말이지, 나중에 그 순간을 떠올렸을 때 그녀는 그것을 이상하게 여겼고, 왜 자기가 의심의 여지가 없음을 단번에 알아챘는지 의아해했다."], ["She could not have said, for instance, that she had foreseen something of the sort--and yet now, as soon as he told her, she suddenly fancied that she had really foreseen this very thing.", "이를테면 그녀는 그런 비슷한 일을 예감하고 있었다고는 말할 수 없었으련만 — 그런데도 지금, 그가 말하자마자, 그녀는 문득 자기가 바로 이 일을 정말로 예감하고 있었던 것 같은 기분이 들었다."]], [["“Stop, Sonia, enough!", "\"그만, 소냐, 됐소!"], ["don’t torture me,”", "나를 괴롭히지 마시오.\""], ["he begged her miserably.", "그가 비참하게 그녀에게 애원했다."]], [["It was not at all, not at all like this he had thought of telling her, but this is how it happened.", "이것은 그가 그녀에게 말하려고 생각했던 방식과는 전혀, 전혀 달랐으나, 일은 이렇게 벌어지고 말았다."]], [["She jumped up, seeming not to know what she was doing, and, wringing her hands, walked into the middle of the room; but quickly went back and sat down again beside him, her shoulder almost touching his.", "그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 모르는 듯 벌떡 일어나, 두 손을 쥐어짜며 방 한가운데로 걸어갔다. 그러나 곧 되돌아와 다시 그의 곁에 앉았는데, 어깨가 그의 어깨에 거의 닿을 정도였다."], ["All of a sudden she started as though she had been stabbed, uttered a cry and fell on her knees before him, she did not know why.", "별안간 그녀는 칼에 찔린 듯 흠칫하더니, 비명을 지르며 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇었는데, 그녀 자신도 왜 그러는지 알지 못했다."]], [["“What have you done--what have you done to yourself?”", "\"무슨 짓을 한 거예요 — 당신 스스로에게 무슨 짓을 한 거예요?\""], ["she said in despair, and, jumping up, she flung herself on his neck, threw her arms round him, and held him tightly.", "그녀가 절망에 차서 말하고는, 벌떡 일어나 그의 목에 매달려 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안고 꼭 끌어안았다."]], [["Raskolnikov drew back and looked at her with a mournful smile.", "라스콜니코프는 몸을 빼며 구슬픈 미소를 지은 채 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“You are a strange girl, Sonia--you kiss me and hug me when I tell you about that....", "\"당신은 이상한 처녀구려, 소냐 — 내가 그런 얘기를 하는데 입을 맞추고 끌어안다니……."], ["You don’t think what you are doing.”", "당신은 지금 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 모르고 있소.\""]], [["“There is no one--no one in the whole world now so unhappy as you!”", "\"이제 온 세상에 당신보다 더 불행한 사람은 아무도 — 아무도 없어요!\""], ["she cried in a frenzy, not hearing what he said, and she suddenly broke into violent hysterical weeping.", "그녀는 그의 말을 듣지도 않은 채 광란에 차서 외쳤고, 별안간 격렬한 히스테릭한 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["A feeling long unfamiliar to him flooded his heart and softened it at once.", "오랫동안 그에게 낯설었던 어떤 감정이 그의 가슴에 밀려들어 단번에 그것을 누그러뜨렸다."], ["He did not struggle against it.", "그는 그것에 맞서 싸우지 않았다."], ["Two tears started into his eyes and hung on his eyelashes.", "두 줄기 눈물이 그의 눈에 솟아 속눈썹에 맺혔다."]], [["“Then you won’t leave me, Sonia?”", "\"그럼 나를 떠나지 않겠소, 소냐?\""], ["he said, looking at her almost with hope.", "그가 거의 희망에 찬 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“No, no, never, nowhere!” cried Sonia.", "\"안 떠나요, 안 떠나, 절대로, 어디로도!\""], ["“I will follow you, I will follow you everywhere.", "소냐가 외쳤다. \"당신을 따라가겠어요, 어디든 따라가겠어요."], ["Oh, my God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["Oh, how miserable I am!...", "오, 저는 어쩌면 이렇게 비참한지!……."], ["Why, why didn’t I know you before!", "왜, 왜 저는 진작 당신을 알지 못했을까요!"], ["Why didn’t you come before?", "왜 진작 오지 않으셨어요?"], ["Oh, dear!”", "오, 가엾어라!\""]], [["“Here I have come.”", "\"이렇게 왔잖소.\""]], [["“Yes, now!", "\"네, 이제야!"], ["What’s to be done now?...", "이제 어떻게 하면 좋아요?……"], ["Together, together!”", "함께, 함께!\""], ["she repeated as it were unconsciously, and she hugged him again.", "그녀는 거의 무의식적으로 되뇌며 그를 다시 끌어안았다."], ["“I’ll follow you to Siberia!”", "\"시베리아까지 당신을 따라가겠어요!\""]], [["He recoiled at this, and the same hostile, almost haughty smile came to his lips.", "이 말에 그는 흠칫 물러섰고, 그의 입술에는 아까와 똑같은 적대적이고 거의 거만하기까지 한 미소가 떠올랐다."]], [["“Perhaps I don’t want to go to Siberia yet, Sonia,”", "\"어쩌면 나는 아직 시베리아에 갈 마음이 없는지도 모르오, 소냐.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Sonia looked at him quickly.", "소냐가 재빨리 그를 바라보았다."]], [["Again after her first passionate, agonising sympathy for the unhappy man the terrible idea of the murder overwhelmed her.", "이 불행한 사내에 대한 처음의 격렬하고 애끓는 연민이 지나자, 다시 살인이라는 그 끔찍한 생각이 그녀를 짓눌렀다."], ["In his changed tone she seemed to hear the murderer speaking.", "그의 달라진 어조 속에서 그녀는 살인자가 말하는 소리를 듣는 듯했다."], ["She looked at him bewildered.", "그녀는 어리둥절하여 그를 바라보았다."], ["She knew nothing as yet, why, how, with what object it had been.", "그녀는 아직 아무것도, 왜, 어떻게, 무슨 목적으로 그런 일이 있었는지 알지 못했다."], ["Now all these questions rushed at once into her mind.", "이제 이 모든 의문이 한꺼번에 그녀의 머릿속으로 밀려들었다."], ["And again she could not believe it: “He, he is a murderer!", "그리고 그녀는 또다시 그것을 믿을 수 없었다. \"이 사람이, 이 사람이 살인자라니!"], ["Could it be true?”", "그게 사실일 수 있을까?\""]], [["“What’s the meaning of it? Where am I?”", "\"이게 다 무슨 뜻인가요?"], ["she said in complete bewilderment, as though still unable to recover herself.", "제가 지금 어디 있는 거죠?\" 그녀가 아직도 정신을 차리지 못한 듯 완전히 어리둥절해하며 말했다."], ["“How could you, you, a man like you....", "\"어떻게 당신이, 당신이, 당신 같은 사람이……."], ["How could you bring yourself to it?...", "어떻게 그런 일을 저지를 수 있었어요?……"], ["What does it mean?”", "이게 다 무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["“Oh, well--to plunder.", "\"오, 뭐 — 강탈하려고 그랬소."], ["Leave off, Sonia,”", "그만둡시다, 소냐.\""], ["he answered wearily, almost with vexation.", "그가 지친 듯, 거의 짜증스럽게 대답했다."]], [["Sonia stood as though struck dumb, but suddenly she cried:", "소냐는 말문이 막힌 듯 서 있다가, 별안간 외쳤다."]], [["“You were hungry!", "\"배가 고팠던 거군요!"], ["It was...", "그게……"], ["to help your mother?", "어머니를 도우려고요?"], ["Yes?”", "그렇죠?\""]], [["“No, Sonia, no,”", "\"아니오, 소냐, 아니오.\""], ["he muttered, turning away and hanging his head.", "그가 외면하며 고개를 떨군 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“I was not so hungry....", "\"그렇게까지 배가 고프지는 않았소……."], ["I certainly did want to help my mother, but...", "물론 어머니를 돕고 싶기는 했지만……"], ["that’s not the real thing either....", "그것도 진짜 이유는 아니오……."], ["Don’t torture me, Sonia.”", "나를 괴롭히지 마시오, 소냐.\""]], [["Sonia clasped her hands.", "소냐가 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Could it, could it all be true?", "\"그게, 그게 다 사실일 수 있을까요?"], ["Good God, what a truth!", "오, 하느님, 이 무슨 진실인가요!"], ["Who could believe it?", "누가 이걸 믿겠어요?"], ["And how could you give away your last farthing and yet rob and murder!", "그리고 어떻게 당신이 마지막 한 푼까지 내주면서 강탈하고 살인을 할 수 있나요!"], ["Ah,”", "아.\""], ["she cried suddenly, “that money you gave Katerina Ivanovna...", "그녀가 별안간 외쳤다. \"카테리나 이바노브나에게 주신 그 돈……"], ["that money....", "그 돈……."], ["Can that money...”", "설마 그 돈이…….\""]], [["“No, Sonia,”", "\"아니오, 소냐.\""], ["he broke in hurriedly, “that money was not it.", "그가 황급히 말을 가로막았다. \"그 돈은 그게 아니오."], ["Don’t worry yourself!", "걱정 마시오!"], ["That money my mother sent me and it came when I was ill, the day I gave it to you....", "그 돈은 어머니가 보내 주신 건데, 내가 앓고 있을 때 도착했소, 당신에게 그 돈을 준 바로 그날에……."], ["Razumihin saw it...", "라주미힌이 봤소……"], ["he received it for me....", "나 대신 받아 줬으니까……."], ["That money was mine--my own.”", "그 돈은 내 거였소 — 내 거였다고.\""]], [["Sonia listened to him in bewilderment and did her utmost to comprehend.", "소냐는 어리둥절하여 그의 말을 들으며 이해하려고 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["“And _that_ money....", "\"그리고 그 돈은……."], ["I don’t even know really whether there was any money,”", "사실 거기 돈이 있기나 했는지도 나는 잘 모르겠소.\""], ["he added softly, as though reflecting.", "그가 곰곰이 생각하는 듯 부드럽게 덧붙였다."], ["“I took a purse off her neck, made of chamois leather...", "\"그녀의 목에서 지갑을 하나 벗겨 냈소, 사슴 가죽으로 만든……"], ["a purse stuffed full of something...", "무언가가 잔뜩 채워진 지갑을……"], ["but I didn’t look in it; I suppose I hadn’t time....", "하지만 그 안을 들여다보지는 않았소. 아마 그럴 틈이 없었던 거겠지……."], ["And the things--chains and trinkets--I buried under a stone with the purse next morning in a yard off the V---- Prospect.", "그리고 그 물건들 — 사슬이며 장신구들 — 은 다음 날 아침 V 거리 어귀의 어느 마당에서 지갑과 함께 돌 밑에 묻어 버렸소."], ["They are all there now....”", "지금도 다 거기 있을 거요…….\""]], [["Sonia strained every nerve to listen.", "소냐는 온 신경을 곤두세워 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Then why...", "\"그럼 왜……"], ["why, you said you did it to rob, but you took nothing?”", "왜, 당신은 강탈하려고 그랬다면서 아무것도 가져가지 않았어요?\""], ["she asked quickly, catching at a straw.", "그녀가 지푸라기라도 잡듯 다급히 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know....", "\"모르겠소……."], ["I haven’t yet decided whether to take that money or not,”", "그 돈을 가질지 말지 아직 결정하지 못했소.\""], ["he said, musing again; and, seeming to wake up with a start, he gave a brief ironical smile.", "그가 다시 생각에 잠겨 말하고는, 흠칫 깨어나는 듯 짤막하고 빈정대는 미소를 지었다."], ["“Ach, what silly stuff I am talking, eh?”", "\"아, 내가 무슨 어리석은 소리를 지껄이고 있는 거지, 응?\""]], [["The thought flashed through Sonia’s mind, wasn’t he mad?", "저이가 미친 게 아닐까 하는 생각이 소냐의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["But she dismissed it at once.", "그러나 그녀는 곧바로 그것을 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["“No, it was something else.”", "\"아니, 뭔가 다른 거야.\""], ["She could make nothing of it, nothing.", "그녀는 그것을 도무지 이해할 수 없었다, 도무지."]], [["“Do you know, Sonia,”", "\"당신 아오, 소냐.\""], ["he said suddenly with conviction, “let me tell you: if I’d simply killed because I was hungry,”", "그가 별안간 확신에 차서 말했다. \"내가 말해 주겠소."], ["laying stress on every word and looking enigmatically but sincerely at her, “I should be _happy_ now.", "만일 내가 그저 배가 고파서 죽였다면,\" 그는 한 마디 한 마디에 힘을 주며, 알 수 없으면서도 진지한 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["You must believe that!", "\"지금 나는 행복할 거요."], ["What would it matter to you,”", "이 말을 믿어야 하오! 그게 당신에게 무슨 상관이오,\""], ["he cried a moment later with a sort of despair, “what would it matter to you if I were to confess that I did wrong?", "잠시 후 그가 일종의 절망에 차서 외쳤다. \"내가 잘못을 저질렀다고 고백한들 그게 당신에게 무슨 상관이오?"], ["What do you gain by such a stupid triumph over me?", "나를 이긴 그런 어리석은 승리에서 당신이 무엇을 얻겠소?"], ["Ah, Sonia, was it for that I’ve come to you to-day?”", "아, 소냐, 내가 오늘 당신에게 온 게 그러려고였소?\""]], [["Again Sonia tried to say something, but did not speak.", "소냐는 다시 무언가 말하려 했으나, 입을 떼지 못했다."]], [["“I asked you to go with me yesterday because you are all I have left.”", "\"어제 내가 당신더러 함께 가자고 한 건, 당신이 내게 남은 전부이기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Go where?”", "\"어디로요?\""], ["asked Sonia timidly.", "소냐가 조심스레 물었다."]], [["“Not to steal and not to murder, don’t be anxious,”", "\"훔치러도 죽이러도 아니니, 걱정 마시오.\""], ["he smiled bitterly.", "그가 쓰게 미소 지었다."], ["“We are so different....", "\"우리는 너무도 다르오……."], ["And you know, Sonia, it’s only now, only this moment that I understand _where_ I asked you to go with me yesterday!", "그리고 당신 아오, 소냐, 이제야, 바로 이 순간에야 나는 어제 내가 당신더러 함께 가자고 했던 그 '어디로'가 어딘지 깨달았소!"], ["Yesterday when I said it I did not know where.", "어제 그 말을 할 때만 해도 나는 어딘지 몰랐던 거요."], ["I asked you for one thing, I came to you for one thing--not to leave me.", "나는 한 가지만을 청했고, 한 가지를 위해 당신에게 왔소 — 나를 떠나지 말아 달라고."], ["You won’t leave me, Sonia?”", "떠나지 않겠지, 소냐?\""]], [["She squeezed his hand.", "그녀가 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“And why, why did I tell her?", "\"그런데 왜, 왜 나는 그녀에게 말했을까?"], ["Why did I let her know?”", "왜 그녀가 알게 했을까?\""], ["he cried a minute later in despair, looking with infinite anguish at her.", "잠시 후 그가 절망에 차서, 그지없는 번민의 눈빛으로 그녀를 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["“Here you expect an explanation from me, Sonia; you are sitting and waiting for it, I see that.", "\"여기 당신은 내게서 해명을 기대하고 있구려, 소냐. 앉아서 그걸 기다리고 있어, 다 보이오."], ["But what can I tell you?", "하지만 내가 당신에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠소?"], ["You won’t understand and will only suffer misery...", "당신은 이해하지 못할 테고, 그저 비참하게 괴로워할 뿐일 거요……"], ["on my account!", "나 때문에!"], ["Well, you are crying and embracing me again.", "자, 당신은 또 울면서 나를 끌어안고 있구려."], ["Why do you do it?", "왜 그러는 거요?"], ["Because I couldn’t bear my burden and have come to throw it on another: you suffer too, and I shall feel better!", "내가 내 짐을 견딜 수 없어서, 그걸 다른 사람에게 떠넘기러 왔기 때문이오. 당신도 괴로워하면 나는 좀 나아질 테니!"], ["And can you love such a mean wretch?”", "그런데 당신이 이런 비열한 인간을 사랑할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“But aren’t you suffering, too?”", "\"하지만 당신도 괴로워하고 있잖아요?\""], ["cried Sonia.", "소냐가 외쳤다."]], [["Again a wave of the same feeling surged into his heart, and again for an instant softened it.", "또다시 똑같은 감정의 물결이 그의 가슴으로 밀려들었고, 또 한순간 그것을 누그러뜨렸다."]], [["“Sonia, I have a bad heart, take note of that.", "\"소냐, 나는 마음이 못된 사람이오, 그걸 명심하시오."], ["It may explain a great deal.", "그것으로 많은 것이 설명될지도 모르오."], ["I have come because I am bad.", "내가 온 건 내가 못된 인간이기 때문이오."], ["There are men who wouldn’t have come.", "오지 않았을 사람들도 있을 거요."], ["But I am a coward and...", "하지만 나는 겁쟁이고……"], ["a mean wretch.", "비열한 인간이오."], ["But...", "그러나……"], ["never mind!", "아무래도 좋소!"], ["That’s not the point.", "그건 요점이 아니니까."], ["I must speak now, but I don’t know how to begin.”", "이제 말해야 하는데, 어떻게 시작해야 할지 모르겠소.\""]], [["He paused and sank into thought.", "그는 말을 멈추고 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Ach, we are so different,”", "\"아, 우리는 너무도 다르오.\""], ["he cried again, “we are not alike.", "그가 다시 외쳤다. \"우리는 닮지 않았소."], ["And why, why did I come?", "그런데 왜, 왜 내가 왔을까?"], ["I shall never forgive myself that.”", "나는 결코 나 자신을 용서하지 못할 거요.\""]], [["“No, no, it was a good thing you came,”", "\"아니에요, 아니에요, 오신 건 잘한 일이에요.\""], ["cried Sonia.", "소냐가 외쳤다."], ["“It’s better I should know, far better!”", "\"제가 아는 게 나아요, 훨씬 나아요!\""]], [["He looked at her with anguish.", "그는 번민에 차서 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“What if it were really that?”", "\"정말로 그게 이유였다면 어쩌겠소?\""], ["he said, as though reaching a conclusion.", "그가 결론에 다다른 듯 말했다."], ["“Yes, that’s what it was!", "\"그래, 바로 그거였소!"], ["I wanted to become a Napoleon, that is why I killed her....", "나는 나폴레옹이 되고 싶었던 거요, 그래서 그녀를 죽인 거요……."], ["Do you understand now?”", "이제 알겠소?\""]], [["“N-no,”", "\"아……"], ["Sonia whispered naïvely and timidly.", "아니요.\" 소냐가 순진하고 조심스럽게 속삭였다."], ["“Only speak, speak, I shall understand, I shall understand _in myself_!”", "\"그냥 말씀하세요, 말씀하세요, 제가 이해할게요, 제가 스스로 이해해 볼게요!\""], ["she kept begging him.", "그녀는 그에게 자꾸만 애원했다."]], [["“You’ll understand?", "\"이해하겠다고?"], ["Very well, we shall see!”", "좋소, 두고 봅시다!\""], ["He paused and was for some time lost in meditation.", "그는 말을 멈추고 한동안 깊은 사색에 잠겼다."]], [["“It was like this: I asked myself one day this question--what if Napoleon, for instance, had happened to be in my place, and if he had not had Toulon nor Egypt nor the passage of Mont Blanc to begin his career with, but instead of all those picturesque and monumental things, there had simply been some ridiculous old hag, a pawnbroker, who had to be murdered too to get money from her trunk (for his career, you understand).", "\"이런 거였소. 어느 날 나는 스스로에게 이런 질문을 던졌소 — 만일 나폴레옹이, 이를테면, 내 처지에 놓였더라면, 그래서 자기 경력을 시작할 툴롱도 이집트도 몽블랑 횡단도 없이, 그 그림 같고 기념비적인 일들 대신, 그저 우스꽝스러운 노파 하나가, 전당포 노파가 있어서, 그 궤짝에서 돈을 빼내려면 (그의 경력을 위해서 말이오, 알겠소?) 그녀까지 죽여야 했다면 어땠을까 하고."], ["Well, would he have brought himself to that if there had been no other means?", "자, 다른 수가 없었다면 그가 그 일을 해냈을까? 그게 너무도 기념비적이지 못하고……"], ["Wouldn’t he have felt a pang at its being so far from monumental and... and sinful, too?", "게다가 죄스러운 일이라는 것에 가책을 느끼지 않았을까?"], ["Well, I must tell you that I worried myself fearfully over that ‘question’ so that I was awfully ashamed when I guessed at last (all of a sudden, somehow) that it would not have given him the least pang, that it would not even have struck him that it was not monumental...", "자, 내가 당신에게 말해 두지만, 나는 그 '질문'을 두고 무섭도록 스스로를 괴롭혔소. 그래서 마침내 (어찌 된 일인지 불현듯) 깨달았을 때 나는 끔찍이도 부끄러웠소."], ["that he would not have seen that there was anything in it to pause over, and that, if he had had no other way, he would have strangled her in a minute without thinking about it!", "그가 손톱만큼의 가책도 느끼지 않았으리라는 것, 그게 기념비적이지 못하다는 생각조차 그에게는 떠오르지 않았으리라는 것을…… 거기에 망설일 만한 무언가가 있다는 것을 그는 보지도 못했으리라는 것, 그리고 다른 길이 없었다면 그는 일 분 만에 아무 생각 없이 그녀의 목을 졸랐으리라는 것을!"], ["Well, I too...", "자, 나도……"], ["left off thinking about it...", "그것에 대해 생각하기를 그만두고……"], ["murdered her, following his example.", "그의 본보기를 따라 그녀를 죽인 거요."], ["And that’s exactly how it was!", "바로 그렇게 된 거요! 우습소?"], ["Do you think it funny? Yes, Sonia, the funniest thing of all is that perhaps that’s just how it was.”", "그래, 소냐, 무엇보다 우스운 건, 어쩌면 정말로 그렇게 된 건지도 모른다는 거요.\""]], [["Sonia did not think it at all funny.", "소냐는 그것을 조금도 우습게 여기지 않았다."]], [["“You had better tell me straight out...", "\"차라리 똑바로 말해 주세요……"], ["without examples,”", "그런 본보기 같은 것 없이요.\""], ["she begged, still more timidly and scarcely audibly.", "그녀는 더욱더 조심스레, 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 애원했다."]], [["He turned to her, looked sadly at her and took her hands.", "그는 그녀에게로 몸을 돌려 슬프게 그녀를 바라보며 두 손을 잡았다."]], [["“You are right again, Sonia.", "\"또 당신 말이 옳소, 소냐."], ["Of course that’s all nonsense, it’s almost all talk!", "물론 그건 다 헛소리지, 거의 다 말장난이오!"], ["You see, you know of course that my mother has scarcely anything, my sister happened to have a good education and was condemned to drudge as a governess.", "보시오, 당신도 물론 알겠지만, 내 어머니는 가진 게 거의 없고, 누이는 좋은 교육을 받았으면서도 가정 교사로 죽도록 일하는 신세가 됐소."], ["All their hopes were centered on me.", "그들의 모든 희망은 내게 걸려 있었지."], ["I was a student, but I couldn’t keep myself at the university and was forced for a time to leave it.", "나는 대학생이었지만 학비를 댈 수 없어 한동안 학교를 그만둘 수밖에 없었소."], ["Even if I had lingered on like that, in ten or twelve years I might (with luck) hope to be some sort of teacher or clerk with a salary of a thousand roubles”", "그렇게 질질 끌었다 한들, 십 년이나 십이 년쯤 지나면 (운이 좋으면) 일 년에 천 루블쯤 받는 어떤 교사나 서기 정도는 될 수 있었을 거요.\""], ["(he repeated it as though it were a lesson) “and by that time my mother would be worn out with grief and anxiety and I could not succeed in keeping her in comfort while my sister...", "(그는 마치 외운 것을 읊듯 되뇌었다.) \"그러나 그때쯤이면 어머니는 슬픔과 근심으로 다 쇠하셨을 테고, 나는 그분을 편안히 모시지도 못했을 테고, 누이는……"], ["well, my sister might well have fared worse!", "글쎄, 누이는 더 나쁜 신세가 됐을지도 모르오!"], ["And it’s a hard thing to pass everything by all one’s life, to turn one’s back upon everything, to forget one’s mother and decorously accept the insults inflicted on one’s sister.", "게다가 평생 모든 것을 그냥 지나치고, 모든 것에 등을 돌리고, 어머니를 잊고, 누이에게 가해진 모욕을 점잖게 받아들이는 것 — 그건 힘든 일이오."], ["Why should one?", "무엇 때문에?"], ["When one has buried them to burden oneself with others--wife and children--and to leave them again without a farthing?", "그들을 묻고 나서 또 다른 이들을 — 아내와 자식들을 — 떠안고는, 다시 그들을 한 푼 없이 남겨 두려고?"], ["So I resolved to gain possession of the old woman’s money and to use it for my first years without worrying my mother, to keep myself at the university and for a little while after leaving it--and to do this all on a broad, thorough scale, so as to build up a completely new career and enter upon a new life of independence....", "그래서 나는 그 노파의 돈을 손에 넣어, 어머니를 괴롭히지 않고 처음 몇 해를 그것으로 버티며, 대학을 다니고 졸업한 뒤에도 잠시 그것으로 지내기로 결심한 거요 — 그리고 이 모든 것을 넓고 철저한 규모로 해서, 완전히 새로운 경력을 쌓고 독립된 새 삶으로 들어서기로……."], ["Well...", "자……"], ["that’s all....", "그게 다요……."], ["Well, of course in killing the old woman I did wrong....", "자, 물론 그 노파를 죽임으로써 나는 잘못을 저질렀소……."], ["Well, that’s enough.”", "자, 됐소.\""]], [["He struggled to the end of his speech in exhaustion and let his head sink.", "그는 기진맥진하여 간신히 말을 끝맺고는 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“Oh, that’s not it, that’s not it,”", "\"오, 그게 아니에요, 그게 아니에요.\""], ["Sonia cried in distress.", "소냐가 괴로워하며 외쳤다."], ["“How could one...", "\"어떻게 사람이……"], ["no, that’s not right, not right.”", "아니, 그건 옳지 않아요, 옳지 않아요.\""]], [["“You see yourself that it’s not right.", "\"옳지 않다는 건 당신도 알겠지."], ["But I’ve spoken truly, it’s the truth.”", "하지만 나는 진실을 말했소, 그게 진실이오.\""]], [["“As though that could be the truth!", "\"그게 진실일 수가 있나요!"], ["Good God!”", "오, 하느님!\""]], [["“I’ve only killed a louse, Sonia, a useless, loathsome, harmful creature.”", "\"나는 그저 이 한 마리를 죽였을 뿐이오, 소냐, 쓸모없고 역겹고 해로운 벌레를.\""]], [["“A human being--a louse!”", "\"사람이 — 이라니요!\""]], [["“I too know it wasn’t a louse,”", "\"그게 이가 아니었다는 건 나도 아오.\""], ["he answered, looking strangely at her.", "그가 그녀를 기묘하게 바라보며 대답했다."], ["“But I am talking nonsense, Sonia,” he added.", "\"하지만 나는 지금 헛소리를 하고 있소, 소냐.\""], ["“I’ve been talking nonsense a long time....", "그가 덧붙였다. \"오랫동안 헛소리만 하고 있었지……."], ["That’s not it, you are right there.", "그게 아니오, 거기선 당신 말이 옳소."], ["There were quite, quite other causes for it!", "거기엔 전혀, 전혀 다른 이유들이 있었소!"], ["I haven’t talked to anyone for so long, Sonia....", "내가 이렇게 오랫동안 아무하고도 말을 안 했구려, 소냐……."], ["My head aches dreadfully now.”", "지금 머리가 끔찍하게 아프오.\""]], [["His eyes shone with feverish brilliance.", "그의 눈이 열병에 들뜬 광채로 빛났다."], ["He was almost delirious; an uneasy smile strayed on his lips.", "그는 거의 헛소리를 하고 있었다. 불안한 미소가 그의 입가를 떠돌았다."], ["His terrible exhaustion could be seen through his excitement.", "그의 흥분 너머로 그 끔찍한 탈진이 비쳐 보였다."], ["Sonia saw how he was suffering.", "소냐는 그가 얼마나 괴로워하는지 보았다."], ["She too was growing dizzy.", "그녀 역시 어지러워지고 있었다."], ["And he talked so strangely; it seemed somehow comprehensible, but yet...", "그리고 그는 너무도 기묘하게 말했다. 어딘가 이해될 것 같다가도, 그러나……."], ["“But how, how!", "\"하지만 어떻게, 어떻게!"], ["Good God!”", "오, 하느님!\""], ["And she wrung her hands in despair.", "그녀는 절망에 차서 두 손을 쥐어짰다."]], [["“No, Sonia, that’s not it,”", "\"아니오, 소냐, 그게 아니오.\""], ["he began again suddenly, raising his head, as though a new and sudden train of thought had struck and as it were roused him--“that’s not it!", "그가 또다시 불쑥, 마치 새롭고 갑작스러운 생각의 갈래가 그를 치고 들어와 그를 일깨운 듯, 고개를 들며 입을 열었다. \"그게 아니오!"], ["Better...", "차라리……"], ["imagine--yes, it’s certainly better--imagine that I am vain, envious, malicious, base, vindictive and...", "상상해 보시오 — 그래, 그게 분명 낫겠소 — 내가 허영심 많고, 시기심 많고, 악의에 차 있고, 비천하고, 앙심을 품고, 또……"], ["well, perhaps with a tendency to insanity.", "글쎄, 어쩌면 광기로 기울기까지 한 인간이라고 상상해 보시오."], ["(Let’s have it all out at once!", "(단번에 다 털어놓읍시다!"], ["They’ve talked of madness already, I noticed.) I told you just now I could not keep myself at the university.", "사람들이 이미 광기 얘기를 하더군, 내가 알아챘소.) 방금 나는 학비를 댈 수 없었다고 당신에게 말했소."], ["But do you know that perhaps I might have done?", "하지만 어쩌면 나는 그럴 수 있었는지도 모른다는 걸 아오?"], ["My mother would have sent me what I needed for the fees and I could have earned enough for clothes, boots and food, no doubt.", "어머니가 학비에 필요한 걸 보내 주셨을 테고, 옷이며 장화며 먹을 것은 틀림없이 내가 벌 수 있었을 거요."], ["Lessons had turned up at half a rouble.", "반 루블짜리 과외 자리도 나왔으니까."], ["Razumihin works!", "라주미힌은 일하잖소!"], ["But I turned sulky and wouldn’t.", "그런데 나는 토라져서 그러지 않았소."], ["(Yes, sulkiness, that’s the right word for it!) I sat in my room like a spider.", "(그래, 토라짐, 그게 딱 맞는 말이오!) 나는 거미처럼 내 방에 틀어박혀 있었소."], ["You’ve been in my den, you’ve seen it....", "당신도 내 굴에 와 봤으니, 봤겠지……."], ["And do you know, Sonia, that low ceilings and tiny rooms cramp the soul and the mind?", "그리고 당신 아오, 소냐, 낮은 천장과 비좁은 방이 영혼과 정신을 옥죈다는 걸?"], ["Ah, how I hated that garret!", "아, 나는 그 다락방이 얼마나 싫었는지!"], ["And yet I wouldn’t go out of it!", "그런데도 거기서 나가려 하지 않았소!"], ["I wouldn’t on purpose!", "일부러 안 나갔지!"], ["I didn’t go out for days together, and I wouldn’t work, I wouldn’t even eat, I just lay there doing nothing.", "며칠씩 밖에 나가지 않았고, 일하려고도 하지 않았고, 먹으려고조차 하지 않은 채, 그저 아무것도 안 하고 드러누워 있었소."], ["If Nastasya brought me anything, I ate it, if she didn’t, I went all day without; I wouldn’t ask, on purpose, from sulkiness!", "나스타시야가 뭔가 가져다주면 먹었고, 안 가져다주면 온종일 굶었소. 일부러, 토라져서 달라고 하지 않았던 거요!"], ["At night I had no light, I lay in the dark and I wouldn’t earn money for candles.", "밤에는 불도 없이, 어둠 속에 누워 있었지, 양초 살 돈도 벌려 하지 않았으니까."], ["I ought to have studied, but I sold my books; and the dust lies an inch thick on the notebooks on my table.", "공부를 해야 했지만 책을 팔아 버렸고, 탁자 위 공책에는 먼지가 한 치나 쌓여 있소."], ["I preferred lying still and thinking.", "나는 가만히 누워 생각하는 편을 택했지."], ["And I kept thinking....", "그리고 자꾸만 생각했소……."], ["And I had dreams all the time, strange dreams of all sorts, no need to describe!", "그리고 줄곧 꿈을 꿨소, 온갖 기묘한 꿈을, 굳이 말할 것도 없는!"], ["Only then I began to fancy that...", "그러다 그때 나는 이런 공상을 하기 시작했소……"], ["No, that’s not it!", "아니, 그게 아니오!"], ["Again I am telling you wrong!", "또 잘못 말하고 있구려!"], ["You see I kept asking myself then: why am I so stupid that if others are stupid--and I know they are--yet I won’t be wiser?", "보시오, 그때 나는 자꾸만 스스로에게 물었소. 왜 나는 이토록 어리석은가, 남들이 어리석다면 — 나는 그들이 어리석다는 걸 아오 — 그렇다면 나는 왜 더 현명해지려 하지 않는가?"], ["Then I saw, Sonia, that if one waits for everyone to get wiser it will take too long....", "그러다 나는 깨달았소, 소냐, 모두가 현명해지기를 기다린다면 너무 오래 걸리리라는 것을……."], ["Afterwards I understood that that would never come to pass, that men won’t change and that nobody can alter it and that it’s not worth wasting effort over it.", "그 뒤에 나는 그런 일은 결코 일어나지 않으리라는 것, 인간은 변하지 않으리라는 것, 아무도 그걸 바꿀 수 없으리라는 것, 그러니 그것에 애쓸 가치가 없다는 것을 이해했소."], ["Yes, that’s so.", "그래, 그렇소."], ["That’s the law of their nature, Sonia,...", "그게 그들의 본성의 법칙이오, 소냐……"], ["that’s so!...", "그렇단 말이오!……"], ["And I know now, Sonia, that whoever is strong in mind and spirit will have power over them.", "그리고 이제 나는 아오, 소냐, 정신과 영혼이 강한 자는 누구든 그들을 지배하는 권력을 갖게 되리라는 것을."], ["Anyone who is greatly daring is right in their eyes.", "크게 대담한 자는 누구든 그들의 눈에 옳소."], ["He who despises most things will be a lawgiver among them and he who dares most of all will be most in the right!", "가장 많은 것을 경멸하는 자가 그들 가운데서 입법자가 될 테고, 가장 크게 감행하는 자가 가장 옳을 거요!"], ["So it has been till now and so it will always be.", "지금까지 그래 왔고, 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거요."], ["A man must be blind not to see it!”", "그걸 못 보는 자는 눈먼 자임이 틀림없소!\""]], [["Though Raskolnikov looked at Sonia as he said this, he no longer cared whether she understood or not.", "라스콜니코프는 이 말을 하면서 소냐를 바라보고 있었지만, 이제 그녀가 이해하든 말든 더는 개의치 않았다."], ["The fever had complete hold of him; he was in a sort of gloomy ecstasy (he certainly had been too long without talking to anyone).", "열병이 그를 완전히 사로잡고 있었다. 그는 일종의 음울한 황홀경에 빠져 있었다 (그는 분명 너무 오랫동안 아무하고도 말을 하지 않았던 것이다)."], ["Sonia felt that his gloomy creed had become his faith and code.", "소냐는 그의 음울한 신조가 그의 신앙이자 율법이 되었음을 느꼈다."]], [["“I divined then, Sonia,”", "\"그때 나는 깨달았소, 소냐,\""], ["he went on eagerly, “that power is only vouchsafed to the man who dares to stoop and pick it up.", "그가 열을 올리며 말을 이었다. \"권력은 감히 몸을 굽혀 그것을 집어 드는 자에게만 주어진다는 것을."], ["There is only one thing, one thing needful: one has only to dare!", "단 한 가지, 단 한 가지가 필요할 뿐이오. 그저 감행하기만 하면 되는 거요!"], ["Then for the first time in my life an idea took shape in my mind which no one had ever thought of before me, no one!", "그때 내 평생 처음으로, 나보다 앞서 그 누구도 생각해 본 적 없는 한 가지 생각이 내 머릿속에 모습을 갖췄소, 그 누구도!"], ["I saw clear as daylight how strange it is that not a single person living in this mad world has had the daring to go straight for it all and send it flying to the devil!", "나는 대낮처럼 환히 보았소, 이 미친 세상에 살고 있는 단 한 사람도 감히 그 모든 것을 향해 곧장 나아가 그것을 악마에게 날려 버릴 엄두를 내지 못했다는 게 얼마나 기이한지를!"], ["I...", "나는……"], ["I wanted _to have the daring_...", "나는 감행할 엄두를 내고 싶었소……"], ["and I killed her.", "그래서 그녀를 죽인 거요."], ["I only wanted to have the daring, Sonia!", "나는 그저 감행할 엄두를 내고 싶었던 거요, 소냐!"], ["That was the whole cause of it!”", "그게 전부였소!\""]], [["“Oh hush, hush,”", "\"오, 그만, 그만하세요.\""], ["cried Sonia, clasping her hands.", "소냐가 두 손을 맞잡으며 외쳤다."], ["“You turned away from God and God has smitten you, has given you over to the devil!”", "\"당신은 하느님에게서 등을 돌렸고, 하느님이 당신을 치셨어요, 당신을 악마에게 넘겨주신 거예요!\""]], [["“Then Sonia, when I used to lie there in the dark and all this became clear to me, was it a temptation of the devil, eh?”", "\"그렇다면 소냐, 내가 어둠 속에 누워 이 모든 게 내게 환히 보였을 때, 그게 악마의 유혹이었단 말이오, 응?\""]], [["“Hush, don’t laugh, blasphemer!", "\"그만, 웃지 마세요, 신성 모독자!"], ["You don’t understand, you don’t understand!", "당신은 이해 못 해요, 이해 못 한다고요! 오, 하느님!"], ["Oh God! He won’t understand!”", "이 사람은 이해를 못 해요!\""]], [["“Hush, Sonia!", "\"그만하시오, 소냐!"], ["I am not laughing.", "나는 웃는 게 아니오."], ["I know myself that it was the devil leading me.", "악마가 나를 끌고 갔다는 걸 나 자신이 알고 있소."], ["Hush, Sonia, hush!”", "그만, 소냐, 그만!\""], ["he repeated with gloomy insistence.", "그가 음울한 집요함으로 되뇌었다."], ["“I know it all, I have thought it all over and over and whispered it all over to myself, lying there in the dark....", "\"나는 다 아오, 그걸 거듭거듭 곱씹었고, 어둠 속에 누워 그걸 죄다 스스로에게 속삭였소……."], ["I’ve argued it all over with myself, every point of it, and I know it all, all!", "나는 그것을 한 점 한 점, 나 자신과 다 따져 봤고, 다 아오, 죄다!"], ["And how sick, how sick I was then of going over it all!", "그리고 그때 그 모든 걸 되짚는 게 얼마나 진저리 나고, 얼마나 진저리 났는지!"], ["I have kept wanting to forget it and make a new beginning, Sonia, and leave off thinking.", "나는 그걸 잊고 새로 시작하고 싶었소, 소냐, 그리고 생각하기를 그만두고 싶었소."], ["And you don’t suppose that I went into it headlong like a fool?", "그리고 당신은 내가 바보처럼 무턱대고 그 일에 뛰어들었다고 여기진 않겠지?"], ["I went into it like a wise man, and that was just my destruction.", "나는 현명한 사람처럼 그 일에 들어갔고, 바로 그게 내 파멸이었소."], ["And you mustn’t suppose that I didn’t know, for instance, that if I began to question myself whether I had the right to gain power--I certainly hadn’t the right--or that if I asked myself whether a human being is a louse it proved that it wasn’t so for me, though it might be for a man who would go straight to his goal without asking questions....", "그리고 이를테면 내가 권력을 가질 권리가 있는지 스스로에게 묻기 시작했다면 — 나는 분명 그럴 권리가 없었소 — 혹은 인간이 이인지 스스로에게 물었다면 그건 내게는 그렇지 않다는 것을 증명하는 셈이라는 걸, 비록 질문 없이 곧장 자기 목표로 나아가는 자에게는 그럴지언정 — 그걸 내가 몰랐다고 여기진 마시오……."], ["If I worried myself all those days, wondering whether Napoleon would have done it or not, I felt clearly of course that I wasn’t Napoleon.", "만일 내가 그 며칠 내내 나폴레옹이라면 그 일을 했을지 안 했을지 곱씹으며 스스로를 괴롭혔다면, 나는 물론 내가 나폴레옹이 아니라는 걸 똑똑히 느낀 거요."], ["I had to endure all the agony of that battle of ideas, Sonia, and I longed to throw it off: I wanted to murder without casuistry, to murder for my own sake, for myself alone!", "나는 그 관념들의 싸움의 온갖 고뇌를 견뎌야 했소, 소냐, 그래서 그걸 떨쳐 버리고 싶었소. 나는 궤변 없이 죽이고 싶었소, 나 자신을 위해, 오직 나만을 위해 죽이고 싶었소!"], ["I didn’t want to lie about it even to myself.", "나는 그것에 대해 나 자신에게조차 거짓말하고 싶지 않았소."], ["It wasn’t to help my mother I did the murder--that’s nonsense--I didn’t do the murder to gain wealth and power and to become a benefactor of mankind.", "어머니를 돕기 위해 살인을 한 게 아니오 — 그건 헛소리요 — 나는 부와 권력을 얻고 인류의 은인이 되려고 살인을 한 게 아니오."], ["Nonsense!", "헛소리!"], ["I simply did it; I did the murder for myself, for myself alone, and whether I became a benefactor to others, or spent my life like a spider catching men in my web and sucking the life out of men, I couldn’t have cared at that moment....", "나는 그저 했소. 나 자신을 위해, 오직 나만을 위해 살인을 했고, 내가 남들에게 은인이 되든, 거미처럼 거미줄에 사람들을 가두어 그 생명을 빨아먹으며 평생을 보내든, 그 순간 나는 조금도 개의치 않았을 거요……."], ["And it was not the money I wanted, Sonia, when I did it.", "그리고 그 일을 할 때 내가 원한 건 돈이 아니었소, 소냐."], ["It was not so much the money I wanted, but something else....", "내가 그토록 원했던 건 돈이 아니라 다른 무엇이었소……."], ["I know it all now....", "이제 다 알겠소……."], ["Understand me!", "나를 이해해 주시오!"], ["Perhaps I should never have committed a murder again.", "어쩌면 나는 다시는 살인을 저지르지 않았을지도 모르오."], ["I wanted to find out something else; it was something else led me on.", "나는 다른 무언가를 알아내고 싶었소. 나를 끌고 간 건 다른 무엇이었소."], ["I wanted to find out then and quickly whether I was a louse like everybody else or a man.", "나는 그때 빨리 알아내고 싶었소, 내가 다른 모든 이처럼 이인지, 아니면 인간인지를."], ["Whether I can step over barriers or not, whether I dare stoop to pick up or not, whether I am a trembling creature or whether I have the _right_...”", "내가 장벽을 넘어설 수 있는지 없는지, 감히 몸을 굽혀 집어 들 엄두를 낼 수 있는지 없는지, 내가 벌벌 떠는 미물인지 아니면 내게 권리가 있는지를…….\""]], [["“To kill?", "\"죽일?"], ["Have the right to kill?”", "죽일 권리가요?\""], ["Sonia clasped her hands.", "소냐가 두 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“Ach, Sonia!”", "\"아, 소냐!\""], ["he cried irritably and seemed about to make some retort, but was contemptuously silent.", "그가 짜증스레 외치며 무언가 대꾸하려는 듯하다가, 경멸하듯 입을 다물었다."], ["“Don’t interrupt me, Sonia.", "\"내 말을 막지 마시오, 소냐."], ["I want to prove one thing only, that the devil led me on then and he has shown me since that I had not the right to take that path, because I am just such a louse as all the rest.", "나는 단 한 가지만을 증명하고 싶소. 그때 악마가 나를 끌고 갔고, 그 뒤로 악마가 내게 보여 주었다는 것을, 내게는 그 길을 갈 권리가 없었다는 것을, 왜냐하면 나도 다른 모든 이와 꼭 같은 이일 뿐이니까!"], ["He was mocking me and here I’ve come to you now!", "악마는 나를 조롱하고 있었고, 그래서 나는 이렇게 지금 당신에게 온 거요!"], ["Welcome your guest!", "당신의 손님을 맞이하시오!"], ["If I were not a louse, should I have come to you?", "만일 내가 이가 아니었다면, 내가 당신에게 왔겠소?"], ["Listen: when I went then to the old woman’s I only went to _try_....", "들어 보시오, 그때 내가 노파에게 갔을 때, 나는 그저 시험해 보러 갔을 뿐이오……."], ["You may be sure of that!”", "그건 믿어도 좋소!\""]], [["“And you murdered her!”", "\"그래 놓고 그녀를 죽였잖아요!\""]], [["“But how did I murder her?", "\"하지만 내가 어떻게 그녀를 죽였소?"], ["Is that how men do murders?", "사람이 살인을 그렇게 하오?"], ["Do men go to commit a murder as I went then?", "사람이 그때 내가 갔던 식으로 살인을 저지르러 가오?"], ["I will tell you some day how I went!", "언젠가 내가 어떻게 갔는지 당신에게 말해 주겠소!"], ["Did I murder the old woman?", "내가 그 노파를 죽인 거요?"], ["I murdered myself, not her!", "나는 나 자신을 죽인 거요, 그녀가 아니라!"], ["I crushed myself once for all, for ever....", "나는 단번에, 영영 나 자신을 짓뭉개 버린 거요……."], ["But it was the devil that killed that old woman, not I.", "하지만 그 노파를 죽인 건 악마지, 내가 아니오."], ["Enough, enough, Sonia, enough!", "됐소, 됐소, 소냐, 됐소!"], ["Let me be!”", "나를 내버려 두시오!\""], ["he cried in a sudden spasm of agony, “let me be!”", "그가 별안간 고뇌의 발작에 차서 외쳤다. \"나를 내버려 두시오!\""]], [["He leaned his elbows on his knees and squeezed his head in his hands as in a vise.", "그는 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 머리를 죔쇠처럼 죄었다."]], [["“What suffering!”", "\"이 무슨 고통인가요!\""], ["A wail of anguish broke from Sonia.", "소냐의 입에서 번민의 비탄이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Well, what am I to do now?”", "\"자, 이제 나는 어떻게 하면 좋소?\""], ["he asked, suddenly raising his head and looking at her with a face hideously distorted by despair.", "그가 별안간 고개를 들고, 절망으로 흉측하게 일그러진 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“What are you to do?”", "\"어떻게 하면 좋으냐고요?\""], ["she cried, jumping up, and her eyes that had been full of tears suddenly began to shine.", "그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤고, 눈물로 가득했던 그녀의 두 눈이 별안간 빛나기 시작했다."], ["“Stand up!”", "\"일어서요!\""], ["(She seized him by the shoulder, he got up, looking at her almost bewildered.) “Go at once, this very minute, stand at the cross-roads, bow down, first kiss the earth which you have defiled and then bow down to all the world and say to all men aloud, ‘I am a murderer!’ Then God will send you life again.", "(그녀가 그의 어깨를 붙잡자, 그는 거의 어리둥절한 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 일어섰다.) \"지금 당장, 이 순간 가서, 네거리에 서서, 무릎 꿇고, 먼저 당신이 더럽힌 땅에 입을 맞추고, 그런 다음 온 세상을 향해 절하며 모든 사람에게 큰 소리로 외치세요, '내가 살인자다!' 하고요."], ["Will you go, will you go?”", "그러면 하느님이 당신에게 다시 생명을 보내 주실 거예요."], ["she asked him, trembling all over, snatching his two hands, squeezing them tight in hers and gazing at him with eyes full of fire.", "가시겠어요, 가시겠어요?\" 그녀는 온몸을 떨며, 그의 두 손을 와락 붙잡아 자기 손안에 꼭 쥐고는, 불길로 가득한 눈으로 그를 응시하며 물었다."]], [["He was amazed at her sudden ecstasy.", "그는 그녀의 갑작스러운 황홀경에 놀랐다."]], [["“You mean Siberia, Sonia?", "\"시베리아 말이오, 소냐?"], ["I must give myself up?”", "자수를 해야 한다는 거요?\""], ["he asked gloomily.", "그가 음울하게 물었다."]], [["“Suffer and expiate your sin by it, that’s what you must do.”", "\"고통받고 그것으로 당신 죄를 속죄하세요, 그게 당신이 해야 할 일이에요.\""]], [["“No!", "\"안 돼!"], ["I am not going to them, Sonia!”", "나는 그들에게 가지 않겠소, 소냐!\""]], [["“But how will you go on living?", "\"하지만 그러면 어떻게 계속 살아가실 거예요?"], ["What will you live for?”", "무엇을 위해 살아가실 거예요?\""], ["cried Sonia, “how is it possible now?", "소냐가 외쳤다. \"이제 어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요?"], ["Why, how can you talk to your mother?", "아니, 어떻게 어머니께 말씀드릴 수 있겠어요?"], ["(Oh, what will become of them now?) But what am I saying?", "(오, 이제 그분들은 어떻게 되나요?) 그런데 제가 무슨 소릴 하는 거죠?"], ["You have abandoned your mother and your sister already.", "당신은 이미 어머니와 누이를 버렸잖아요. 이 사람은 벌써 그분들을 버렸어!"], ["He has abandoned them already!", "오, 하느님!\""], ["Oh, God!”", "그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried, “why, he knows it all himself.", "\"아니, 이 사람은 그걸 다 스스로 알고 있어요."], ["How, how can he live by himself!", "어떻게, 어떻게 이 사람이 혼자 살아갈 수 있겠어요!"], ["What will become of you now?”", "이제 당신은 어떻게 되는 거예요?\""]], [["“Don’t be a child, Sonia,”", "\"어린애처럼 굴지 마시오, 소냐.\""], ["he said softly.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“What wrong have I done them?", "\"내가 그들에게 무슨 잘못을 했소?"], ["Why should I go to them?", "무엇 때문에 그들에게 가야 하오?"], ["What should I say to them?", "그들에게 무슨 말을 해야 하오?"], ["That’s only a phantom....", "그건 그저 환영일 뿐이오……."], ["They destroy men by millions themselves and look on it as a virtue.", "그들은 스스로 수백만 명씩 사람을 죽이면서도 그걸 미덕으로 여기오."], ["They are knaves and scoundrels, Sonia!", "그들은 사기꾼이고 악당이오, 소냐!"], ["I am not going to them.", "나는 그들에게 가지 않겠소."], ["And what should I say to them--that I murdered her, but did not dare to take the money and hid it under a stone?”", "그리고 내가 그들에게 무슨 말을 해야 하오 — 내가 그녀를 죽였지만 돈을 가져갈 엄두는 못 내고 돌 밑에 숨겼다고?\""], ["he added with a bitter smile.", "그가 쓴웃음을 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["“Why, they would laugh at me, and would call me a fool for not getting it.", "\"아니, 그들은 나를 비웃으며, 그걸 챙기지 못한 바보라고 부를 거요."], ["A coward and a fool!", "겁쟁이에 바보라고!"], ["They wouldn’t understand and they don’t deserve to understand.", "그들은 이해하지 못할 테고, 이해할 자격도 없소."], ["Why should I go to them?", "무엇 때문에 그들에게 가겠소?"], ["I won’t.", "가지 않겠소."], ["Don’t be a child, Sonia....”", "어린애처럼 굴지 마시오, 소냐…….\""]], [["“It will be too much for you to bear, too much!”", "\"당신이 견디기엔 너무 버거워요, 너무 버겁다고요!\""], ["she repeated, holding out her hands in despairing supplication.", "그녀는 절망적인 애원으로 두 손을 내밀며 되뇌었다."]], [["“Perhaps I’ve been unfair to myself,”", "\"어쩌면 내가 나 자신에게 너무 가혹했는지도 모르오.\""], ["he observed gloomily, pondering, “perhaps after all I am a man and not a louse and I’ve been in too great a hurry to condemn myself.", "그가 음울하게, 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"어쩌면 결국 나는 이가 아니라 인간이고, 너무 성급하게 나 자신을 단죄한 건지도 모르오."], ["I’ll make another fight for it.”", "다시 한번 싸워 보겠소.\""]], [["A haughty smile appeared on his lips.", "거만한 미소가 그의 입술에 떠올랐다."]], [["“What a burden to bear!", "\"그 무슨 짊어질 짐인가요!"], ["And your whole life, your whole life!”", "게다가 평생을, 평생을 말이에요!\""]], [["“I shall get used to it,”", "\"익숙해질 거요.\""], ["he said grimly and thoughtfully.", "그가 침울하게, 생각에 잠겨 말했다. \"들어 보시오,\""], ["“Listen,”", "잠시 후 그가 입을 열었다."], ["he began a minute later, “stop crying, it’s time to talk of the facts: I’ve come to tell you that the police are after me, on my track....”", "\"그만 우시오, 이제 사실들을 얘기할 때요. 경찰이 나를 쫓고 있다는 걸, 내 뒤를 밟고 있다는 걸 말해 주러 왔소…….\""]], [["“Ach!”", "\"아!\""], ["Sonia cried in terror.", "소냐가 공포에 차서 외쳤다."]], [["“Well, why do you cry out?", "\"자, 왜 소리를 지르오?"], ["You want me to go to Siberia and now you are frightened?", "나더러 시베리아에 가라더니, 이제 와 겁을 내오?"], ["But let me tell you: I shall not give myself up.", "하지만 말해 두겠소. 나는 자수하지 않겠소."], ["I shall make a struggle for it and they won’t do anything to me.", "한번 싸워 보겠고, 그들은 내게 아무 짓도 못 할 거요."], ["They’ve no real evidence.", "그들에겐 진짜 증거가 없으니까."], ["Yesterday I was in great danger and believed I was lost; but to-day things are going better.", "어제 나는 큰 위기에 처해 끝장났다고 믿었지만, 오늘은 사정이 나아지고 있소."], ["All the facts they know can be explained two ways, that’s to say I can turn their accusations to my credit, do you understand?", "그들이 아는 사실들은 죄다 두 가지로 해석될 수 있소, 다시 말해 나는 그들의 고발을 도리어 내게 유리하게 돌릴 수 있다는 거요, 알겠소?"], ["And I shall, for I’ve learnt my lesson.", "그리고 나는 그렇게 할 거요, 교훈을 얻었으니까."], ["But they will certainly arrest me.", "하지만 그들은 분명 나를 체포할 거요."], ["If it had not been for something that happened, they would have done so to-day for certain; perhaps even now they will arrest me to-day....", "어떤 일이 일어나지 않았더라면, 그들은 오늘 틀림없이 그렇게 했을 거요. 어쩌면 지금이라도 그들은 오늘 나를 체포할지도 모르오……."], ["But that’s no matter, Sonia; they’ll let me out again...", "하지만 그건 상관없소, 소냐. 그들은 나를 다시 풀어 줄 거요……"], ["for there isn’t any real proof against me, and there won’t be, I give you my word for it.", "나에 대한 진짜 증거가 없고, 앞으로도 없을 테니까, 내 장담하오."], ["And they can’t convict a man on what they have against me.", "그리고 그들이 가진 것만으로는 사람을 유죄로 만들 수 없소."], ["Enough....", "됐소……."], ["I only tell you that you may know....", "그저 당신이 알도록 말해 주는 것뿐이오……."], ["I will try to manage somehow to put it to my mother and sister so that they won’t be frightened....", "어머니와 누이에게는 그들이 놀라지 않도록 어떻게든 잘 말해 볼 작정이오……."], ["My sister’s future is secure, however, now, I believe...", "그래도 누이의 앞날은 이제 안전한 것 같소, 아마도……"], ["and my mother’s must be too....", "어머니도 분명 그럴 거요……."], ["Well, that’s all.", "자, 그게 다요."], ["Be careful, though.", "하지만 조심하시오."], ["Will you come and see me in prison when I am there?”", "내가 감옥에 가면 나를 보러 와 주겠소?\""]], [["“Oh, I will, I will.”", "\"오, 갈게요, 갈게요.\""]], [["They sat side by side, both mournful and dejected, as though they had been cast up by the tempest alone on some deserted shore.", "그들은 나란히 앉아 있었다. 둘 다, 마치 폭풍에 떠밀려 어느 황량한 기슭에 홀로 내던져진 듯, 비통하고 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["He looked at Sonia and felt how great was her love for him, and strange to say he felt it suddenly burdensome and painful to be so loved.", "그는 소냐를 바라보며 그녀의 사랑이 얼마나 큰지를 느꼈고, 이상하게도 그토록 사랑받는다는 것이 별안간 짐스럽고 고통스럽게 느껴졌다."], ["Yes, it was a strange and awful sensation!", "그래, 그것은 기묘하고 끔찍한 감각이었다!"], ["On his way to see Sonia he had felt that all his hopes rested on her; he expected to be rid of at least part of his suffering, and now, when all her heart turned towards him, he suddenly felt that he was immeasurably unhappier than before.", "소냐를 만나러 오는 길에 그는 자신의 모든 희망이 그녀에게 달려 있다고 느꼈다. 그는 적어도 자기 고통의 일부만이라도 덜어 내리라 기대했건만, 이제 그녀의 온 마음이 그를 향하자, 그는 별안간 자신이 전보다 헤아릴 수 없이 더 불행하다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Sonia,”", "\"소냐,\""], ["he said, “you’d better not come and see me when I am in prison.”", "그가 말했다. \"내가 감옥에 있을 땐 차라리 나를 보러 오지 않는 게 낫겠소.\""]], [["Sonia did not answer, she was crying.", "소냐는 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 울고 있었다."], ["Several minutes passed.", "몇 분이 흘렀다."]], [["“Have you a cross on you?”", "\"몸에 십자가를 지니고 있나요?\""], ["she asked, as though suddenly thinking of it.", "그녀가 문득 그 생각이 떠오른 듯 물었다."]], [["He did not at first understand the question.", "그는 처음에는 그 질문을 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["“No, of course not.", "\"아니, 물론 없소."], ["Here, take this one, of cypress wood.", "자, 이걸 받으세요, 삼나무로 만든 거예요."], ["I have another, a copper one that belonged to Lizaveta.", "제겐 또 하나, 리자베타의 것이었던 구리 십자가가 있어요."], ["I changed with Lizaveta: she gave me her cross and I gave her my little ikon.", "리자베타와 바꿨거든요. 그녀는 제게 자기 십자가를 주고, 저는 그녀에게 제 작은 성상을 줬어요."], ["I will wear Lizaveta’s now and give you this.", "이제 저는 리자베타의 걸 걸고, 당신에게 이걸 드릴게요."], ["Take it...", "받으세요……"], ["it’s mine!", "제 거예요!"], ["It’s mine, you know,”", "제 거라니까요, 아시잖아요.\""], ["she begged him.", "그녀가 그에게 애원했다."], ["“We will go to suffer together, and together we will bear our cross!”", "\"우리 함께 고통받으러 가요, 함께 우리 십자가를 짊어져요!\""]], [["“Give it me,”", "\"이리 주시오.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["He did not want to hurt her feelings.", "그는 그녀의 마음을 상하게 하고 싶지 않았다."], ["But immediately he drew back the hand he held out for the cross.", "그러나 곧장 그는 십자가를 받으려 내밀었던 손을 도로 거두었다."]], [["“Not now, Sonia.", "\"지금은 말고, 소냐."], ["Better later,”", "나중이 낫겠소.\""], ["he added to comfort her.", "그가 그녀를 달래려 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, yes, better,”", "\"네, 네, 그게 낫겠어요.\""], ["she repeated with conviction, “when you go to meet your suffering, then put it on.", "그녀가 확신에 차서 되뇌었다. \"당신이 당신의 고통을 맞으러 갈 때, 그때 거시면 돼요."], ["You will come to me, I’ll put it on you, we will pray and go together.”", "제게 오세요, 제가 걸어 드릴게요, 우리 기도하고 함께 가요.\""]], [["At that moment someone knocked three times at the door.", "그 순간 누군가 문을 세 번 두드렸다."]], [["“Sofya Semyonovna, may I come in?”", "\"소피야 세묘노브나, 들어가도 될까요?\""], ["they heard in a very familiar and polite voice.", "아주 귀에 익고 정중한 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Sonia rushed to the door in a fright.", "소냐는 깜짝 놀라 문으로 달려갔다."], ["The flaxen head of Mr. Lebeziatnikov appeared at the door.", "레베쟈트니코프 씨의 아마빛 머리가 문 앞에 나타났다."]], [["Lebeziatnikov looked perturbed.", "레베쟈트니코프는 안절부절못하는 기색이었다."]], [["“I’ve come to you, Sofya Semyonovna,”", "\"제가 댁에게 왔습니다, 소피야 세묘노브나.\""], ["he began.", "그가 입을 열었다."], ["“Excuse me... I thought I should find you,”", "\"실례합니다……, 댁을 만날 수 있으리라 생각했어요.\""], ["he said, addressing Raskolnikov suddenly, “that is, I didn’t mean anything...", "그가 갑자기 라스콜니코프를 향해 말했다."], ["of that sort... But I just thought...", "\"그러니까, 무슨 그런……, 그런 뜻으로 한 말은 아닙니다……."], ["Katerina Ivanovna has gone out of her mind,”", "다만 제가 생각하기에는……, 카테리나 이바노브나가 정신이 나가 버렸어요.\""], ["he blurted out suddenly, turning from Raskolnikov to Sonia.", "그가 불쑥 내뱉으며 라스콜니코프에게서 소냐 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["Sonia screamed.", "소냐가 비명을 질렀다."]], [["“At least it seems so.", "\"적어도 그렇게 보입니다."], ["But... we don’t know what to do, you see!", "하지만……, 어떻게 해야 할지 우리도 모르겠어요, 그게 문제예요!"], ["She came back--she seems to have been turned out somewhere, perhaps beaten....", "그녀가 돌아왔는데——어딘가에서 쫓겨난 것 같고, 어쩌면 얻어맞기까지 한 것 같습니다……."], ["So it seems at least....", "적어도 그렇게 보여요……."], ["She had run to your father’s former chief, she didn’t find him at home: he was dining at some other general’s....", "댁 아버지의 옛 상관에게 달려갔는데, 그 사람이 집에 없었던 거죠. 다른 어느 장군 집에서 식사를 하고 있었답니다……."], ["Only fancy, she rushed off there, to the other general’s, and, imagine, she was so persistent that she managed to get the chief to see her, had him fetched out from dinner, it seems.", "글쎄, 그녀가 그리로, 그 다른 장군 집으로 쫓아가서는, 어찌나 고집을 부렸던지 그 상관을 만나는 데 기어이 성공했다지 뭡니까, 식사 중인 사람을 불러내게 했나 봐요."], ["You can imagine what happened.", "무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 짐작이 가시죠."], ["She was turned out, of course; but, according to her own story, she abused him and threw something at him.", "물론 쫓겨났습니다. 하지만 그녀 자신의 말로는, 그 사람에게 욕을 퍼붓고 뭔가를 집어 던졌다더군요."], ["One may well believe it....", "충분히 그러고도 남았겠죠……."], ["How it is she wasn’t taken up, I can’t understand!", "어쩌다 붙잡혀 가지 않았는지, 저는 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요!"], ["Now she is telling everyone, including Amalia Ivanovna; but it’s difficult to understand her, she is screaming and flinging herself about....", "지금 그녀는 아말리야 이바노브나를 포함해 모두에게 그 얘기를 떠들어 대고 있는데, 알아듣기가 어렵습니다, 비명을 지르며 마구 몸을 내던지고 있으니까요……."], ["Oh yes, she shouts that since everyone has abandoned her, she will take the children and go into the street with a barrel-organ, and the children will sing and dance, and she too, and collect money, and will go every day under the general’s window...", "아, 그래요, 그녀가 소리치기를, 모두가 자기를 버렸으니 아이들을 데리고 손풍금을 들고 거리로 나가겠다는 겁니다, 아이들은 노래하고 춤추고, 자기도 그러면서, 돈을 거두고, 매일 그 장군의 창문 밑으로 가겠다고요……."], ["‘to let everyone see well-born children, whose father was an official, begging in the street.’ She keeps beating the children and they are all crying.", "'관리였던 아버지를 둔, 좋은 집안의 아이들이 거리에서 구걸하는 꼴을 모두가 똑똑히 보게 해 주겠다'면서요. 그녀는 줄곧 아이들을 때리고, 아이들은 죄다 울고 있어요."], ["She is teaching Lida to sing ‘My Village,’ the boy to dance, Polenka the same.", "리다에게는 '나의 작은 마을'을 부르게 가르치고, 사내아이에게는 춤을, 폴렌카에게도 마찬가지로 시키고 있죠."], ["She is tearing up all the clothes, and making them little caps like actors; she means to carry a tin basin and make it tinkle, instead of music....", "옷을 죄다 찢어서 배우들처럼 작은 모자를 만들어 주고요. 음악 대신 양철 대야를 들고 다니며 댕댕 두드리겠다는 겁니다……."], ["She won’t listen to anything....", "무슨 말을 해도 듣질 않아요……."], ["Imagine the state of things!", "그 꼴을 상상해 보세요!"], ["It’s beyond anything!”", "도무지 말이 안 되는 지경입니다!\""]], [["Lebeziatnikov would have gone on, but Sonia, who had heard him almost breathless, snatched up her cloak and hat, and ran out of the room, putting on her things as she went.", "레베쟈트니코프는 계속 말하려 했으나, 거의 숨도 못 쉬며 듣고 있던 소냐가 망토와 모자를 낚아채더니, 옷을 걸치면서 방 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Raskolnikov followed her and Lebeziatnikov came after him.", "라스콜니코프가 그 뒤를 따랐고, 레베쟈트니코프도 그를 뒤따랐다."]], [["“She has certainly gone mad!”", "\"틀림없이 미쳐 버린 거예요!\""], ["he said to Raskolnikov, as they went out into the street.", "거리로 나서며 그가 라스콜니코프에게 말했다."], ["“I didn’t want to frighten Sofya Semyonovna, so I said ‘it seemed like it,’ but there isn’t a doubt of it.", "\"소피야 세묘노브나를 겁주고 싶지 않아서 '그렇게 보인다'고 말했지만, 의심할 여지가 없어요."], ["They say that in consumption the tubercles sometimes occur in the brain; it’s a pity I know nothing of medicine.", "결핵에 걸리면 가끔 결절이 뇌에 생기기도 한다더군요. 제가 의학을 전혀 모르는 게 안타깝습니다."], ["I did try to persuade her, but she wouldn’t listen.”", "그녀를 설득해 보려고 했지만, 통 들으려 하질 않았어요.\""]], [["“Did you talk to her about the tubercles?”", "\"결절 얘기를 한 겁니까?\""]], [["“Not precisely of the tubercles.", "\"꼭 결절 얘기는 아니었습니다."], ["Besides, she wouldn’t have understood!", "게다가 알아듣지도 못했을 거예요!"], ["But what I say is, that if you convince a person logically that he has nothing to cry about, he’ll stop crying.", "하지만 제 말은, 어떤 사람에게 울 까닭이 전혀 없다는 걸 논리적으로 납득시키면, 그 사람은 울음을 그치게 된다는 겁니다."], ["That’s clear.", "그건 명백하죠."], ["Is it your conviction that he won’t?”", "그가 그치지 않을 거라는 게 댁의 확신입니까?\""]], [["“Life would be too easy if it were so,”", "\"그렇다면 인생이 너무 쉬울 텐데요.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 대답했다."]], [["“Excuse me, excuse me; of course it would be rather difficult for Katerina Ivanovna to understand, but do you know that in Paris they have been conducting serious experiments as to the possibility of curing the insane, simply by logical argument?", "\"실례지만, 실례지만요. 물론 카테리나 이바노브나로서는 알아듣기가 다소 어렵겠죠."], ["One professor there, a scientific man of standing, lately dead, believed in the possibility of such treatment.", "하지만 파리에서는 단지 논리적인 논증만으로 정신병자를 치료할 수 있는지 진지하게 실험을 해 왔다는 걸 아십니까?"], ["His idea was that there’s nothing really wrong with the physical organism of the insane, and that insanity is, so to say, a logical mistake, an error of judgment, an incorrect view of things.", "거기 어느 교수가, 명망 있는 과학자였는데, 최근에 죽었습니다만, 그런 치료의 가능성을 믿었어요. 그의 생각은, 정신병자의 신체 기관에는 사실 아무 이상이 없다는 것, 그리고 광기란 말하자면 논리적 오류, 판단의 착오, 사물에 대한 그릇된 견해라는 거였죠."], ["He gradually showed the madman his error and, would you believe it, they say he was successful?", "그는 차츰차츰 미친 사람에게 그 잘못을 보여 주었고, 글쎄, 믿어지십니까, 성공했다지 뭡니까?"], ["But as he made use of douches too, how far success was due to that treatment remains uncertain....", "하지만 물 치료법도 함께 썼기 때문에, 그 치료가 어디까지 효과가 있었는지는 여전히 불분명합니다……."], ["So it seems at least.”", "적어도 그렇게 보여요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov had long ceased to listen.", "라스콜니코프는 이미 오래전에 듣기를 그만두고 있었다."], ["Reaching the house where he lived, he nodded to Lebeziatnikov and went in at the gate.", "자기가 사는 집에 다다르자, 그는 레베쟈트니코프에게 고개를 까딱하고는 대문 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Lebeziatnikov woke up with a start, looked about him and hurried on.", "레베쟈트니코프는 흠칫 정신을 차리더니, 주위를 둘러보고는 서둘러 가 버렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov went into his little room and stood still in the middle of it.", "라스콜니코프는 자기 작은 방으로 들어가 한가운데 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["Why had he come back here?", "왜 여기로 돌아온 것일까?"], ["He looked at the yellow and tattered paper, at the dust, at his sofa....", "그는 누렇게 바래고 너덜너덜한 벽지를, 먼지를, 자기 소파를 바라보았다……."], ["From the yard came a loud continuous knocking; someone seemed to be hammering...", "마당에서 크고 끊임없는 두드리는 소리가 들려왔다. 누군가가 망치질을 하는 것 같았다……."], ["He went to the window, rose on tiptoe and looked out into the yard for a long time with an air of absorbed attention.", "그는 창가로 가서 발끝으로 서서는, 골똘히 몰두한 표정으로 오랫동안 마당을 내다보았다."], ["But the yard was empty and he could not see who was hammering.", "하지만 마당은 텅 비어 있어서, 누가 망치질을 하는지 알 수 없었다."], ["In the house on the left he saw some open windows; on the window-sills were pots of sickly-looking geraniums.", "왼편 건물에는 열린 창문 몇 개가 보였고, 창턱에는 시들시들한 제라늄 화분들이 놓여 있었다."], ["Linen was hung out of the windows...", "창밖으로는 빨래가 널려 있었다……."], ["He knew it all by heart.", "그는 그 모든 것을 훤히 알고 있었다."], ["He turned away and sat down on the sofa.", "그는 몸을 돌려 소파에 앉았다."]], [["Never, never had he felt himself so fearfully alone!", "이토록 무섭게 혼자라고 느껴 본 적은 단 한 번도 없었다!"]], [["Yes, he felt once more that he would perhaps come to hate Sonia, now that he had made her more miserable.", "그렇다, 그는 다시금, 이제 그녀를 더 비참하게 만들어 놓은 지금, 어쩌면 자신이 소냐를 미워하게 될지도 모른다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Why had he gone to her to beg for her tears?", "'무엇 때문에 그녀에게 가서 그녀의 눈물을 구걸했단 말인가?"], ["What need had he to poison her life?", "무엇 때문에 그녀의 삶에 독을 풀어야 했단 말인가?"], ["Oh, the meanness of it!”", "오, 이 비열함이라니!'"]], [["“I will remain alone,”", "\"나는 혼자 남겠어.\""], ["he said resolutely, “and she shall not come to the prison!”", "그가 단호히 말했다. \"그리고 그녀를 감옥에 오지 못하게 하겠어!\""]], [["Five minutes later he raised his head with a strange smile.", "오 분쯤 지나 그는 묘한 미소를 띠며 고개를 들었다."], ["That was a strange thought.", "그것은 묘한 생각이었다."]], [["“Perhaps it really would be better in Siberia,”", "'어쩌면 시베리아가 정말로 더 나을지도 몰라.'"], ["he thought suddenly.", "그는 문득 생각했다."]], [["He could not have said how long he sat there with vague thoughts surging through his mind.", "막연한 생각들이 머릿속을 물결치며 지나가는 동안 자기가 거기 얼마나 오래 앉아 있었는지 그는 알 수 없었다."], ["All at once the door opened and Dounia came in.", "갑자기 문이 열리며 두냐가 들어왔다."], ["At first she stood still and looked at him from the doorway, just as he had done at Sonia; then she came in and sat down in the same place as yesterday, on the chair facing him.", "처음에 그녀는 가만히 서서 문가에서 그를 바라보았는데, 마치 그가 소냐를 바라보았던 것과 똑같았다. 그러고는 안으로 들어와 어제와 같은 자리, 그의 맞은편 의자에 앉았다."], ["He looked silently and almost vacantly at her.", "그는 말없이, 거의 멍하니 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Don’t be angry, brother; I’ve only come for one minute,”", "\"화내지 마, 오빠. 잠깐만 들렀어.\""], ["said Dounia.", "두냐가 말했다."]], [["Her face looked thoughtful but not stern.", "그녀의 얼굴은 생각에 잠긴 듯했으나 엄하지는 않았다."], ["Her eyes were bright and soft.", "두 눈은 맑고 부드러웠다."], ["He saw that she too had come to him with love.", "그는 그녀 역시 사랑을 품고 자기에게 왔음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Brother, now I know all, _all_.", "\"오빠, 이제 나는 다 알아, 모두 다."], ["Dmitri Prokofitch has explained and told me everything.", "드미트리 프로코피치가 다 설명해 주고 이야기해 줬어."], ["They are worrying and persecuting you through a stupid and contemptible suspicion....", "어리석고 비열한 의심으로 사람들이 오빠를 괴롭히고 들볶고 있다는 거 말이야……."], ["Dmitri Prokofitch told me that there is no danger, and that you are wrong in looking upon it with such horror.", "드미트리 프로코피치 말이, 아무 위험도 없고, 오빠가 그걸 그렇게 끔찍하게 받아들이는 건 잘못이래."], ["I don’t think so, and I fully understand how indignant you must be, and that that indignation may have a permanent effect on you.", "나는 그렇게 생각하진 않아, 오빠가 얼마나 분개할 수밖에 없는지, 그리고 그 분노가 오빠한테 영영 사라지지 않는 상처를 남길 수도 있다는 걸 잘 이해하니까."], ["That’s what I am afraid of.", "바로 그게 내가 두려워하는 거야."], ["As for your cutting yourself off from us, I don’t judge you, I don’t venture to judge you, and forgive me for having blamed you for it.", "오빠가 우리한테서 떨어져 나가는 것에 대해서는, 나는 오빠를 탓하지 않아, 감히 탓할 수 없어, 그 일로 오빠를 비난했던 걸 용서해 줘."], ["I feel that I too, if I had so great a trouble, should keep away from everyone.", "나 역시, 그토록 큰 괴로움이 있었다면, 모두에게서 멀리 떨어져 있었을 거라는 걸 느끼니까."], ["I shall tell mother nothing _of this_, but I shall talk about you continually and shall tell her from you that you will come very soon.", "어머니께는 이 일을 아무것도 말하지 않을게, 그래도 오빠 얘기는 끊임없이 할 거고, 오빠가 곧 오겠다고 했다고 전할게. 어머니 걱정은 하지 마."], ["Don’t worry about her; _I_ will set her mind at rest; but don’t you try her too much--come once at least; remember that she is your mother.", "내가 어머니 마음을 가라앉혀 드릴 테니까. 하지만 오빠도 어머니를 너무 시험하지는 마——적어도 한 번은 와 줘."], ["And now I have come simply to say”", "어머니라는 걸 잊지 마."], ["(Dounia began to get up) “that if you should need me or should need... all my life or anything...", "그리고 지금은 그저 이 말을 하러 온 거야\"——두냐가 일어서기 시작했다——\"혹시 내가 필요하거나, 혹은 필요하면……, 내 평생이든 무엇이든……, 나를 불러, 그럼 갈게."], ["call me, and I’ll come. Good-bye!”", "잘 있어!\""]], [["She turned abruptly and went towards the door.", "그녀는 불쑥 몸을 돌려 문 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["“Dounia!”", "\"두냐!\""], ["Raskolnikov stopped her and went towards her.", "라스콜니코프가 그녀를 멈춰 세우며 그녀에게로 다가갔다."], ["“That Razumihin, Dmitri Prokofitch, is a very good fellow.”", "\"그 라주미힌, 드미트리 프로코피치 말이야, 아주 좋은 사람이다.\""]], [["Dounia flushed slightly.", "두냐의 얼굴이 살짝 붉어졌다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""], ["she asked, waiting a moment.", "그녀가 잠깐 기다리며 물었다."]], [["“He is competent, hardworking, honest and capable of real love....", "\"유능하고, 부지런하고, 정직하고, 진정한 사랑을 할 수 있는 사람이야……."], ["Good-bye, Dounia.”", "잘 있어, 두냐.\""]], [["Dounia flushed crimson, then suddenly she took alarm.", "두냐의 얼굴이 새빨갛게 물들더니, 갑자기 그녀는 놀라는 빛을 띠었다."]], [["“But what does it mean, brother?", "\"하지만 그게 무슨 뜻이야, 오빠?"], ["Are we really parting for ever that you... give me such a parting message?”", "우리가 정말 영영 헤어지기라도 하는 거야, 오빠가……, 그런 작별 인사를 다 하다니?\""]], [["“Never mind....", "\"신경 쓰지 마……."], ["Good-bye.”", "잘 있어.\""]], [["He turned away, and walked to the window.", "그는 몸을 돌려 창가로 걸어갔다."], ["She stood a moment, looked at him uneasily, and went out troubled.", "그녀는 잠시 서서 불안하게 그를 바라보다가, 마음이 어지러운 채 나가 버렸다."]], [["No, he was not cold to her.", "아니, 그가 그녀에게 냉담했던 건 아니다."], ["There was an instant (the very last one) when he had longed to take her in his arms and _say good-bye_ to her, and even _to tell_ her, but he had not dared even to touch her hand.", "한순간(바로 그 마지막 순간) 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안고 작별을 고하고 싶은, 심지어 모든 것을 말해 버리고 싶은 마음이 간절했으나, 그는 감히 그녀의 손조차 건드리지 못했다."]], [["“Afterwards she may shudder when she remembers that I embraced her, and will feel that I stole her kiss.”", "'나중에 그녀는 내가 자기를 끌어안았던 걸 떠올리며 몸서리칠 테고, 내가 자기 입맞춤을 훔쳤다고 느끼겠지.'"]], [["“And would _she_ stand that test?”", "'게다가 그녀가 그 시험을 견뎌 낼 수 있을까?'"], ["he went on a few minutes later to himself.", "그는 몇 분 뒤 다시 혼잣말로 이어 갔다."], ["“No, she wouldn’t; girls like that can’t stand things!", "'아니, 못 견뎌. 그런 여자애들은 그런 걸 견뎌 내지 못해!"], ["They never do.”", "절대로 못 하지.'"]], [["And he thought of Sonia.", "그러고는 소냐를 떠올렸다."]], [["There was a breath of fresh air from the window.", "창문에서 신선한 공기가 한 줄기 불어 들어왔다."], ["The daylight was fading.", "햇빛이 스러져 가고 있었다."], ["He took up his cap and went out.", "그는 모자를 집어 들고 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["He could not, of course, and would not consider how ill he was.", "물론 그는 자신이 얼마나 아픈지 헤아릴 수도, 헤아리려 하지도 않았다."], ["But all this continual anxiety and agony of mind could not but affect him.", "하지만 이 끊임없는 불안과 마음의 고통이 그에게 영향을 미치지 않을 리 없었다."], ["And if he were not lying in high fever it was perhaps just because this continual inner strain helped to keep him on his legs and in possession of his faculties.", "그가 고열에 누워 있지 않은 것은, 어쩌면 바로 이 끊임없는 내면의 긴장이 그를 두 발로 서 있게 하고 제정신을 붙들고 있게 도와준 덕분인지도 몰랐다."], ["But this artificial excitement could not last long.", "하지만 이 인위적인 흥분이 오래갈 수는 없었다."]], [["He wandered aimlessly.", "그는 정처 없이 헤매고 다녔다."], ["The sun was setting.", "해가 지고 있었다."], ["A special form of misery had begun to oppress him of late.", "요즘 들어 어떤 특별한 형태의 비참함이 그를 짓누르기 시작했다. 거기에는 사무치게 아픈 것도, 날카로운 것도 없었다."], ["There was nothing poignant, nothing acute about it; but there was a feeling of permanence, of eternity about it; it brought a foretaste of hopeless years of this cold leaden misery, a foretaste of an eternity “on a square yard of space.”", "그러나 거기에는 영속성의, 영원의 느낌이 깃들어 있었다. 그것은 이 차갑고 납덩이 같은 비참함이 가득한 절망의 세월에 대한 예감을, '한 평 남짓한 공간에서의'"], ["Towards evening this sensation usually began to weigh on him more heavily.", "영원에 대한 예감을 가져다주었다. 저녁 무렵이면 이 감각은 으레 그를 더욱 무겁게 짓누르기 시작했다."]], [["“With this idiotic, purely physical weakness, depending on the sunset or something, one can’t help doing something stupid!", "'이 어리석고 순전히 육체적인 쇠약, 해넘이인지 뭔지에 좌우되는 이런 것 때문에, 사람은 어쩔 수 없이 멍청한 짓을 저지르게 되는 거야!"], ["You’ll go to Dounia, as well as to Sonia,”", "소냐한테뿐 아니라 두냐한테까지 가게 되겠지.'"], ["he muttered bitterly.", "그가 쓰디쓰게 중얼거렸다."]], [["He heard his name called.", "누군가 그의 이름을 부르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["He looked round.", "그는 돌아보았다."], ["Lebeziatnikov rushed up to him.", "레베쟈트니코프가 그에게로 달려왔다."]], [["“Only fancy, I’ve been to your room looking for you.", "\"글쎄 말입니다, 댁을 찾으러 방에 다녀왔어요."], ["Only fancy, she’s carried out her plan, and taken away the children.", "글쎄, 그녀가 자기 계획을 실행에 옮겨서 아이들을 데리고 나가 버렸어요."], ["Sofya Semyonovna and I have had a job to find them.", "소피야 세묘노브나와 제가 그들을 찾느라 한바탕 애를 먹었습니다."], ["She is rapping on a frying-pan and making the children dance.", "그녀가 프라이팬을 두드리며 아이들을 춤추게 하고 있어요."], ["The children are crying.", "아이들은 울고 있고요."], ["They keep stopping at the cross-roads and in front of shops; there’s a crowd of fools running after them.", "그들은 자꾸 네거리며 가게 앞에서 멈춰 서는데, 멍청한 구경꾼들이 떼를 지어 뒤를 따라다니고 있습니다."], ["Come along!”", "어서 가시죠!\""]], [["“And Sonia?”", "\"그럼 소냐는요?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked anxiously, hurrying after Lebeziatnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 레베쟈트니코프를 서둘러 뒤따르며 걱정스럽게 물었다."]], [["“Simply frantic.", "\"그냥 제정신이 아니에요."], ["That is, it’s not Sofya Semyonovna’s frantic, but Katerina Ivanovna, though Sofya Semyonova’s frantic too.", "아니, 제정신이 아닌 건 소피야 세묘노브나가 아니라 카테리나 이바노브나입니다, 물론 소피야 세묘노브나도 제정신이 아니긴 하죠."], ["But Katerina Ivanovna is absolutely frantic.", "하지만 카테리나 이바노브나는 완전히 제정신이 아니에요."], ["I tell you she is quite mad.", "정말이지 그녀는 완전히 미쳤습니다."], ["They’ll be taken to the police.", "경찰에 끌려갈 거예요."], ["You can fancy what an effect that will have....", "그게 어떤 결과를 가져올지 짐작이 가시겠죠……."], ["They are on the canal bank, near the bridge now, not far from Sofya Semyonovna’s, quite close.”", "그들은 지금 운하 둑, 다리 근처에 있어요, 소피야 세묘노브나 집에서 멀지 않은 데, 아주 가까운 곳에요.\""]], [["On the canal bank near the bridge and not two houses away from the one where Sonia lodged, there was a crowd of people, consisting principally of gutter children.", "운하 둑, 다리 근처, 소냐가 세 들어 사는 집에서 두 집도 채 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사람들이 떼를 지어 모여 있었는데, 주로 길거리 부랑아들이었다."], ["The hoarse broken voice of Katerina Ivanovna could be heard from the bridge, and it certainly was a strange spectacle likely to attract a street crowd.", "카테리나 이바노브나의 쉬고 갈라진 목소리가 다리에서부터 들려왔으니, 정말이지 거리의 군중을 끌어모을 만한 기이한 광경이었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna in her old dress with the green shawl, wearing a torn straw hat, crushed in a hideous way on one side, was really frantic.", "낡은 옷에 초록 숄을 두르고, 한쪽으로 보기 흉하게 짜부라진 찢어진 밀짚모자를 쓴 카테리나 이바노브나는 정말로 제정신이 아니었다."], ["She was exhausted and breathless.", "그녀는 기진맥진해 숨을 헐떡이고 있었다."], ["Her wasted consumptive face looked more suffering than ever, and indeed out of doors in the sunshine a consumptive always looks worse than at home.", "결핵으로 야윈 그녀의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 고통스러워 보였는데, 사실 폐병 환자는 집 안에 있을 때보다 햇빛 아래 바깥에 있을 때 늘 더 안 좋아 보이는 법이다."], ["But her excitement did not flag, and every moment her irritation grew more intense.", "그러나 그녀의 흥분은 가라앉지 않았고, 매 순간 그녀의 짜증은 더욱 격해졌다."], ["She rushed at the children, shouted at them, coaxed them, told them before the crowd how to dance and what to sing, began explaining to them why it was necessary, and driven to desperation by their not understanding, beat them....", "그녀는 아이들에게 달려들어 소리를 지르고, 어르고 달래고, 군중 앞에서 어떻게 춤추고 무엇을 노래해야 하는지 가르치고, 왜 이래야만 하는지 설명하기 시작했다가, 아이들이 알아듣지 못하자 절망에 사로잡혀 매질을 했다……. 그러다가 군중에게로 달려들곤 했다."], ["Then she would make a rush at the crowd; if she noticed any decently dressed person stopping to look, she immediately appealed to him to see what these children “from a genteel, one may say aristocratic, house”", "점잖게 차려입은 사람이 멈춰 서서 구경하는 걸 눈치채면, 즉시 그에게 호소하기를, '점잖은, 가히 귀족이라 할 만한 집안의' 이 아이들이 어떤 신세로 전락했는지 좀 보라는 것이었다."], ["had been brought to. If she heard laughter or jeering in the crowd, she would rush at once at the scoffers and begin squabbling with them.", "군중 속에서 웃음소리나 비웃는 소리가 들리면, 그녀는 당장 그 조롱꾼들에게 달려들어 말다툼을 벌이기 시작했다."], ["Some people laughed, others shook their heads, but everyone felt curious at the sight of the madwoman with the frightened children.", "어떤 이들은 웃었고, 어떤 이들은 고개를 절레절레 흔들었으나, 겁에 질린 아이들을 데리고 있는 미친 여자의 모습에 모두가 호기심을 느꼈다."], ["The frying-pan of which Lebeziatnikov had spoken was not there, at least Raskolnikov did not see it.", "레베쟈트니코프가 말했던 프라이팬은 거기 없었는데, 적어도 라스콜니코프의 눈에는 보이지 않았다."], ["But instead of rapping on the pan, Katerina Ivanovna began clapping her wasted hands, when she made Lida and Kolya dance and Polenka sing.", "하지만 프라이팬을 두드리는 대신, 카테리나 이바노브나는 리다와 콜랴를 춤추게 하고 폴렌카에게 노래를 시킬 때면 야윈 두 손으로 박수를 치기 시작했다."], ["She too joined in the singing, but broke down at the second note with a fearful cough, which made her curse in despair and even shed tears.", "그녀 자신도 노래에 끼어들었으나, 무서운 기침 때문에 두 번째 음에서 그만 끊기고 말았고, 그 바람에 절망에 차서 악담을 퍼붓고 심지어 눈물까지 흘렸다."], ["What made her most furious was the weeping and terror of Kolya and Lida.", "무엇보다 그녀를 격분시킨 것은 콜랴와 리다가 울며 겁에 질려 있는 것이었다."], ["Some effort had been made to dress the children up as street singers are dressed.", "아이들에게 거리의 가수들이 차려입는 식으로 옷을 입혀 보려는 시도가 더러 있었다."], ["The boy had on a turban made of something red and white to look like a Turk.", "사내아이는 터키인처럼 보이게 하려고 붉고 흰 무언가로 만든 터번을 두르고 있었다."], ["There had been no costume for Lida; she simply had a red knitted cap, or rather a night cap that had belonged to Marmeladov, decorated with a broken piece of white ostrich feather, which had been Katerina Ivanovna’s grandmother’s and had been preserved as a family possession.", "리다에게는 변변한 의상이 없어서, 그저 붉은 뜨개 모자를, 아니 차라리 마르멜라도프의 것이었던 잠자리 모자를 쓰고 있었는데, 거기에는 부러진 흰 타조 깃털 조각이 장식으로 달려 있었으니, 그것은 카테리나 이바노브나의 할머니의 것으로 집안의 가보로 간직되어 온 것이었다."], ["Polenka was in her everyday dress; she looked in timid perplexity at her mother, and kept at her side, hiding her tears.", "폴렌카는 평상복 차림이었다. 그녀는 겁먹은 듯 어쩔 줄 모르며 어머니를 바라보고는, 눈물을 감추며 어머니 곁에 바싹 붙어 있었다."], ["She dimly realised her mother’s condition, and looked uneasily about her.", "그녀는 어머니의 상태를 어렴풋이 알아차렸고, 불안하게 주위를 두리번거렸다."], ["She was terribly frightened of the street and the crowd.", "그녀는 거리와 군중이 끔찍하게 무서웠다."], ["Sonia followed Katerina Ivanovna, weeping and beseeching her to return home, but Katerina Ivanovna was not to be persuaded.", "소냐는 카테리나 이바노브나를 뒤따르며, 울면서 집으로 돌아가자고 애원했으나, 카테리나 이바노브나는 도무지 설득되지 않았다."]], [["“Leave off, Sonia, leave off,”", "\"그만둬, 소냐, 그만두라니까.\""], ["she shouted, speaking fast, panting and coughing.", "그녀가 빠르게 말하며 외쳤다, 숨을 헐떡이고 기침을 하면서."], ["“You don’t know what you ask; you are like a child!", "\"네가 무슨 소릴 하는지 모르는구나. 넌 꼭 어린애 같아!"], ["I’ve told you before that I am not coming back to that drunken German.", "전에도 말했잖아, 그 술주정뱅이 독일 여자한테는 다시 돌아가지 않겠다고."], ["Let everyone, let all Petersburg see the children begging in the streets, though their father was an honourable man who served all his life in truth and fidelity, and one may say died in the service.”", "아버지가 평생을 진실과 충성으로 봉직하다가, 가히 직무 중에 순직했다고 할 만한 훌륭한 사람이었는데도, 그 아이들이 거리에서 구걸하는 꼴을 모두가, 페테르부르크 전체가 다 보게 하겠어.\""], ["(Katerina Ivanovna had by now invented this fantastic story and thoroughly believed it.) “Let that wretch of a general see it!", "(카테리나 이바노브나는 이미 이 황당한 이야기를 지어내어 철석같이 믿고 있었다.) \"그 못된 장군 놈이 그걸 보게 하겠어!"], ["And you are silly, Sonia: what have we to eat?", "그리고 넌 어리석구나, 소냐. 우리가 뭘 먹고 살지?"], ["Tell me that.", "말해 봐."], ["We have worried you enough, I won’t go on so!", "우린 너를 충분히 괴롭혔어, 더는 그렇게 살지 않겠어!"], ["Ah, Rodion Romanovitch, is that you?”", "아, 로지온 로마노비치, 당신이군요?\""], ["she cried, seeing Raskolnikov and rushing up to him.", "그녀가 라스콜니코프를 보고는 그에게 달려들며 외쳤다."], ["“Explain to this silly girl, please, that nothing better could be done!", "\"제발 이 어리석은 애한테 좀 설명해 주세요, 이보다 더 나은 방법은 없다고요!"], ["Even organ-grinders earn their living, and everyone will see at once that we are different, that we are an honourable and bereaved family reduced to beggary.", "손풍금쟁이들도 제 밥벌이는 하는데, 우리가 그들과는 다르다는 걸, 우리가 거지 신세로 전락한 점잖고 불행한 집안이라는 걸 사람들이 단번에 알아볼 거예요."], ["And that general will lose his post, you’ll see!", "그리고 그 장군은 자리에서 쫓겨날 거예요, 두고 보세요!"], ["We shall perform under his windows every day, and if the Tsar drives by, I’ll fall on my knees, put the children before me, show them to him, and say ‘Defend us father.’ He is the father of the fatherless, he is merciful, he’ll protect us, you’ll see, and that wretch of a general....", "우린 매일 그자의 창문 밑에서 공연을 할 거고, 만약 황제 폐하께서 마차로 지나가시면, 나는 무릎을 꿇고 아이들을 앞에 세워 폐하께 보이며 이렇게 말씀드릴 거예요. '저희를 지켜 주십시오, 아버지시여.'"], ["Lida, _tenez vous droite_! Kolya, you’ll dance again.", "그분은 아비 없는 자들의 아버지이시고, 자비로우시니, 우리를 보호해 주실 거예요, 두고 보세요, 그리고 그 못된 장군 놈은……."], ["Why are you whimpering?", "리다, 똑바로 서!* 콜랴, 또 춤춰야지."], ["Whimpering again!", "왜 또 칭얼대니?"], ["What are you afraid of, stupid?", "또 칭얼대! 뭐가 무서워서, 이 바보야?"], ["Goodness, what am I to do with them, Rodion Romanovitch?", "아이고, 이 애들을 대체 어쩌면 좋아요, 로지온 로마노비치?"], ["If you only knew how stupid they are!", "이 애들이 얼마나 멍청한지 당신이 알기나 하면!"], ["What’s one to do with such children?”", "이런 애들을 데리고 뭘 어쩌란 말이에요?\""]], [["And she, almost crying herself--which did not stop her uninterrupted, rapid flow of talk--pointed to the crying children.", "* tenez vous droite: (프랑스어) 똑바로 서라. 그러고는 그녀 자신도 거의 울먹이면서——그래도 끊임없이 빠르게 쏟아지는 말은 멈추지 않았다——우는 아이들을 가리켰다."], ["Raskolnikov tried to persuade her to go home, and even said, hoping to work on her vanity, that it was unseemly for her to be wandering about the streets like an organ-grinder, as she was intending to become the principal of a boarding-school.", "라스콜니코프는 그녀를 달래어 집으로 돌아가게 하려고 애썼고, 심지어 그녀의 허영심을 자극하려는 속셈으로, 기숙학교의 교장이 되려는 사람이 손풍금쟁이처럼 거리를 떠돌아다니는 것은 어울리지 않는다고까지 말했다."]], [["“A boarding-school, ha-ha-ha!", "\"기숙학교라니, 하-하-하!"], ["A castle in the air,”", "그림의 떡이죠.\""], ["cried Katerina Ivanovna, her laugh ending in a cough.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 외쳤고, 그 웃음은 기침으로 끝났다."], ["“No, Rodion Romanovitch, that dream is over!", "\"아니에요, 로지온 로마노비치, 그 꿈은 끝났어요!"], ["All have forsaken us!...", "다들 우리를 버렸으니까요!……"], ["And that general....", "그리고 그 장군이라는 작자는……."], ["You know, Rodion Romanovitch, I threw an inkpot at him--it happened to be standing in the waiting-room by the paper where you sign your name.", "있잖아요, 로지온 로마노비치, 내가 그자에게 잉크병을 집어 던졌어요——마침 대기실에, 이름을 적는 그 서류 옆에 놓여 있었거든요."], ["I wrote my name, threw it at him and ran away.", "내 이름을 적고, 그걸 그자에게 던지고는 달아났죠."], ["Oh, the scoundrels, the scoundrels!", "오, 그 악당들, 그 악당들!"], ["But enough of them, now I’ll provide for the children myself, I won’t bow down to anybody!", "하지만 그자들 얘긴 그만하죠, 이제 내가 직접 아이들을 먹여 살릴 거예요, 아무한테도 굽실거리지 않겠어요!"], ["She has had to bear enough for us!”", "이 애는 우리 때문에 충분히 고생했어요!\""], ["she pointed to Sonia.", "그녀가 소냐를 가리켰다."], ["“Polenka, how much have you got?", "\"폴렌카, 얼마나 모았니?"], ["Show me!", "보여 줘!"], ["What, only two farthings!", "뭐, 고작 두 닢이야!"], ["Oh, the mean wretches!", "오, 그 인색한 인간들!"], ["They give us nothing, only run after us, putting their tongues out.", "우리한테 아무것도 안 주면서, 그저 뒤를 졸졸 쫓아다니며 혀나 날름거리지."], ["There, what is that blockhead laughing at?”", "저런, 저 멍청이는 뭐가 우습다고 웃는 거야?\""], ["(She pointed to a man in the crowd.) “It’s all because Kolya here is so stupid; I have such a bother with him.", "(그녀가 군중 속의 한 남자를 가리켰다.) \"이게 다 여기 이 콜랴가 어찌나 멍청한지, 이 애 때문에 골치가 다 아파요."], ["What do you want, Polenka?", "뭘 원하니, 폴렌카?"], ["Tell me in French, _parlez-moi français_. Why, I’ve taught you, you know some phrases.", "프랑스어로 말해 봐, 나한테 프랑스어로 말해 봐.* 아니, 내가 가르쳐 줬잖니, 너 몇 마디 알잖아."], ["Else how are you to show that you are of good family, well brought-up children, and not at all like other organ-grinders?", "안 그러면 너희가 좋은 집안 출신에 잘 교육받은 아이들이라는 걸, 다른 손풍금쟁이들과는 전혀 다르다는 걸 어떻게 보여 주겠니?"], ["We aren’t going to have a Punch and Judy show in the street, but to sing a genteel song....", "우린 거리에서 인형극 따위를 하려는 게 아니라, 점잖은 노래를 부르려는 거니까……."], ["Ah, yes,...", "아, 그래……."], ["What are we to sing?", "우리 뭘 부르지?"], ["You keep putting me out, but we... you see, we are standing here, Rodion Romanovitch, to find something to sing and get money, something Kolya can dance to....", "네가 자꾸 내 정신을 흩뜨리는구나, 하지만 우린……, 보세요, 우린 여기 서서, 로지온 로마노비치, 부를 노래를 찾아 돈을 벌려는 거예요, 콜랴가 춤출 수 있는 무언가를요……."], ["For, as you can fancy, our performance is all impromptu....", "보시다시피 우리 공연은 죄다 즉흥이라서요……."], ["We must talk it over and rehearse it all thoroughly, and then we shall go to Nevsky, where there are far more people of good society, and we shall be noticed at once.", "다 같이 의논하고 철저히 연습해야 해요, 그런 다음에 네프스키로 갈 거예요, 거긴 상류 사회 사람들이 훨씬 많으니까, 우린 단번에 눈에 띄겠죠."], ["Lida knows ‘My Village’ only, nothing but ‘My Village,’ and everyone sings that.", "리다는 '나의 작은 마을'밖에 몰라요, 오로지 '나의 작은 마을'뿐인데, 그건 누구나 부르는 노래죠."], ["We must sing something far more genteel....", "우린 훨씬 더 점잖은 걸 불러야 해요……."], ["Well, have you thought of anything, Polenka?", "자, 뭐 생각난 거 있니, 폴렌카?"], ["If only you’d help your mother!", "네가 엄마를 좀 도와주기만 한다면!"], ["My memory’s quite gone, or I should have thought of something.", "내 기억력이 완전히 가 버렸어, 안 그랬으면 뭔가 생각해 냈을 텐데. '경기병'"], ["We really can’t sing ‘An Hussar.’ Ah, let us sing in French, ‘Cinq sous,’ I have taught it you, I have taught it you.", "같은 건 정말 부를 수 없지. 아, 프랑스어로 부르자, '다섯 수,'* 내가 너한테 가르쳐 줬잖니, 가르쳐 줬어."], ["And as it is in French, people will see at once that you are children of good family, and that will be much more touching....", "그게 프랑스어니까, 사람들이 너희가 좋은 집안 아이들이라는 걸 단번에 알아볼 거고, 그게 훨씬 더 가슴을 울리겠지……. '말버러는 전쟁터로 떠나네'를 불러도 되겠다, 그건 완전히 어린이 노래라서 귀족 집안마다 자장가로 부르는 거니까."], ["You might sing ‘Marlborough s’en va-t-en guerre,’ for that’s quite a child’s song and is sung as a lullaby in all the aristocratic houses.", "* parlez-moi français: (프랑스어) 나에게 프랑스어로 말해 봐. Cinq sous: (프랑스어) 다섯 수(=수는 프랑스 동전)."]], [["“_Marlborough s’en va-t-en guerre Ne sait quand reviendra_...”", "\"말버러는 전쟁터로 떠나네, 언제 돌아올지 알 수 없네……\""], ["she began singing.", "그녀가 노래를 부르기 시작했다."], ["“But no, better sing ‘Cinq sous.’ Now, Kolya, your hands on your hips, make haste, and you, Lida, keep turning the other way, and Polenka and I will sing and clap our hands!", "\"하지만 안 되겠어, '다섯 수'를 부르는 게 낫겠다. 자, 콜랴, 두 손을 허리에 얹고, 어서, 그리고 리다, 너는 계속 반대쪽으로 돌고, 폴렌카랑 나는 노래하며 손뼉을 칠 테니!"]], [["“_Cinq sous, cinq sous Pour monter notre menage_.”", "\"다섯 수, 다섯 수, 우리 살림 차리려고.\""]], [["(Cough-cough-cough!) “Set your dress straight, Polenka, it’s slipped down on your shoulders,”", "(기침-기침-기침!) \"옷매무새 똑바로 해, 폴렌카, 어깨에서 흘러내렸잖니.\""], ["she observed, panting from coughing.", "그녀가 기침으로 숨을 헐떡이며 일렀다."], ["“Now it’s particularly necessary to behave nicely and genteelly, that all may see that you are well-born children.", "\"지금은 특히 얌전하고 점잖게 굴어야 해, 너희가 좋은 집안 아이들이라는 걸 모두가 알 수 있게 말이야."], ["I said at the time that the bodice should be cut longer, and made of two widths.", "그때 내가 말했지, 몸통 부분을 더 길게 재단하고 폭을 두 배로 해야 한다고."], ["It was your fault, Sonia, with your advice to make it shorter, and now you see the child is quite deformed by it....", "그건 네 잘못이야, 소냐, 더 짧게 만들라고 했잖니, 그래서 이제 봐, 애가 옷 때문에 아주 볼품없어졌잖아……."], ["Why, you’re all crying again!", "아니, 너희 또 다 우는구나!"], ["What’s the matter, stupids?", "무슨 일이야, 이 바보들아?"], ["Come, Kolya, begin.", "자, 콜랴, 시작해."], ["Make haste, make haste!", "어서, 어서!"], ["Oh, what an unbearable child!", "오, 정말 못 견디겠는 애로구나!"]], [["“Cinq sous, cinq sous.", "\"다섯 수, 다섯 수."]], [["“A policeman again!", "\"또 경찰이잖아!"], ["What do you want?”", "뭘 원하는 거예요?\""]], [["A policeman was indeed forcing his way through the crowd.", "정말로 경찰관 하나가 군중을 헤치며 다가오고 있었다."], ["But at that moment a gentleman in civilian uniform and an overcoat--a solid-looking official of about fifty with a decoration on his neck (which delighted Katerina Ivanovna and had its effect on the policeman)--approached and without a word handed her a green three-rouble note.", "그런데 바로 그때 사복 차림에 외투를 걸친 한 신사가——목에 훈장을 단(이것은 카테리나 이바노브나를 기쁘게 했고 경찰관에게도 효과를 발휘했다) 쉰 살쯤 된 점잖아 보이는 관리가——다가오더니, 말없이 그녀에게 초록색 삼 루블짜리 지폐를 건넸다."], ["His face wore a look of genuine sympathy.", "그의 얼굴에는 진심 어린 동정의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["Katerina Ivanovna took it and gave him a polite, even ceremonious, bow.", "카테리나 이바노브나는 그것을 받아 들고는, 정중하다 못해 의례적이기까지 한 인사를 했다."]], [["“I thank you, honoured sir,”", "\"감사합니다, 존경하는 어르신.\""], ["she began loftily.", "그녀가 거만하게 입을 열었다."], ["“The causes that have induced us (take the money, Polenka: you see there are generous and honourable people who are ready to help a poor gentlewoman in distress).", "\"저희로 하여금 이리하게 만든 사정인즉슨(돈을 받으렴, 폴렌카, 봐라, 곤경에 처한 가련한 양가의 부인을 기꺼이 도우려는 너그럽고 고결한 분들이 계시잖니)."], ["You see, honoured sir, these orphans of good family--I might even say of aristocratic connections--and that wretch of a general sat eating grouse... and stamped at my disturbing him.", "보십시오, 존경하는 어르신, 이 좋은 집안의——가히 귀족 집안과 연이 닿았다고도 할 수 있는——고아들을요, 그런데 그 못된 장군 놈은 들꿩 요리나 처먹고 앉아서는……, 내가 방해한다고 발을 쾅쾅 굴렀답니다."], ["‘Your excellency,’ I said, ‘protect the orphans, for you knew my late husband, Semyon Zaharovitch, and on the very day of his death the basest of scoundrels slandered his only daughter.’...", "'각하,' 내가 말했죠, '이 고아들을 지켜 주십시오, 각하께서는 제 고인이 된 남편 세묜 자하로비치를 아셨으니까요, 그리고 그이가 세상을 떠난 바로 그날, 더없이 비열한 악당이 그이의 외동딸을 모함했습니다.'……"], ["That policeman again!", "또 저 경찰이잖아!"], ["Protect me,”", "저를 지켜 주세요.\""], ["she cried to the official.", "그녀가 그 관리를 향해 외쳤다."], ["“Why is that policeman edging up to me?", "\"왜 저 경찰이 자꾸 제게로 다가오는 거죠?"], ["We have only just run away from one of them.", "방금 그런 자한테서 겨우 도망쳐 왔는데."], ["What do you want, fool?”", "뭘 원하는 거예요, 이 바보야?\""]], [["“It’s forbidden in the streets.", "\"거리에서 이러시면 안 됩니다."], ["You mustn’t make a disturbance.”", "소란을 피우시면 안 돼요.\""]], [["“It’s you’re making a disturbance.", "\"소란을 피우는 건 당신이잖아."], ["It’s just the same as if I were grinding an organ.", "이건 내가 손풍금을 돌리는 거랑 똑같은 거라고."], ["What business is it of yours?”", "당신이 무슨 상관이야?\""]], [["“You have to get a licence for an organ, and you haven’t got one, and in that way you collect a crowd.", "\"손풍금에는 허가증을 받아야 하는데, 당신은 그게 없잖소, 게다가 이런 식으로 군중을 끌어모으고 있고."], ["Where do you lodge?”", "어디 사시오?\""]], [["“What, a license?”", "\"뭐, 허가증이라고?\""], ["wailed Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 울부짖었다."], ["“I buried my husband to-day.", "\"내가 오늘 남편을 묻었어."], ["What need of a license?”", "허가증이 무슨 소용이야?\""]], [["“Calm yourself, madam, calm yourself,”", "\"진정하세요, 부인, 진정하시고요.\""], ["began the official.", "그 관리가 입을 열었다."], ["“Come along; I will escort you....", "\"자, 같이 가시죠, 제가 모셔다드리겠습니다……."], ["This is no place for you in the crowd.", "이 군중 속은 부인이 계실 곳이 아니에요."], ["You are ill.”", "편찮으시잖습니까.\""]], [["“Honoured sir, honoured sir, you don’t know,”", "\"존경하는 어르신, 존경하는 어르신, 모르시는군요.\""], ["screamed Katerina Ivanovna.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 비명을 질렀다."], ["“We are going to the Nevsky....", "\"우린 네프스키로 갈 거예요……."], ["Sonia, Sonia!", "소냐, 소냐!"], ["Where is she?", "그앤 어디 있지?"], ["She is crying too!", "그애도 울고 있네!"], ["What’s the matter with you all?", "너희 다 왜들 그래?"], ["Kolya, Lida, where are you going?”", "콜랴, 리다, 어디 가니?\""], ["she cried suddenly in alarm.", "그녀가 갑자기 겁에 질려 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, silly children!", "\"오, 어리석은 애들!"], ["Kolya, Lida, where are they off to?...”", "콜랴, 리다, 저 애들이 어디로 가는 거야?……\""]], [["Kolya and Lida, scared out of their wits by the crowd, and their mother’s mad pranks, suddenly seized each other by the hand, and ran off at the sight of the policeman who wanted to take them away somewhere.", "콜랴와 리다는 군중과 어머니의 미친 짓거리에 넋이 나갈 만큼 겁에 질려, 갑자기 서로 손을 맞잡고는, 자기들을 어딘가로 데려가려는 경찰관을 보고 달아나 버렸다."], ["Weeping and wailing, poor Katerina Ivanovna ran after them.", "가엾은 카테리나 이바노브나는 울부짖으며 그들 뒤를 쫓았다."], ["She was a piteous and unseemly spectacle, as she ran, weeping and panting for breath.", "울면서 숨을 헐떡이며 달리는 그녀의 모습은 가련하고도 꼴사나운 광경이었다."], ["Sonia and Polenka rushed after them.", "소냐와 폴렌카가 그들을 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."]], [["“Bring them back, bring them back, Sonia!", "\"데려와, 데려오라니까, 소냐!"], ["Oh stupid, ungrateful children!...", "오, 어리석고 배은망덕한 애들!……"], ["Polenka!", "폴렌카!"], ["catch them....", "저 애들을 붙잡아……."], ["It’s for your sakes I...”", "다 너희를 위해서 내가…….\""]], [["She stumbled as she ran and fell down.", "그녀는 달리다가 발이 걸려 넘어졌다."]], [["“She’s cut herself, she’s bleeding!", "\"다치셨어, 피가 나! 오, 어떡해!\""], ["Oh, dear!” cried Sonia, bending over her.", "소냐가 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히며 외쳤다."]], [["All ran up and crowded around.", "모두가 달려와 주위에 몰려들었다."], ["Raskolnikov and Lebeziatnikov were the first at her side, the official too hastened up, and behind him the policeman who muttered, “Bother!”", "라스콜니코프와 레베쟈트니코프가 맨 먼저 그녀 곁에 이르렀고, 그 관리도 서둘러 달려왔으며, 그 뒤로 경찰관이 \"성가시게 됐군!\""], ["with a gesture of impatience, feeling that the job was going to be a troublesome one.", "하고 짜증스러운 몸짓으로 중얼거리며 다가왔으니, 그는 이 일이 골치 아픈 사건이 되리라는 것을 직감했던 것이다."]], [["“Pass on!", "\"비키시오!"], ["Pass on!”", "비켜요!\""], ["he said to the crowd that pressed forward.", "그가 앞으로 밀려드는 군중을 향해 말했다."]], [["“She’s dying,”", "\"이 여자 죽어 가요.\""], ["someone shouted.", "누군가가 외쳤다."]], [["“She’s gone out of her mind,”", "\"정신이 나갔어.\""], ["said another.", "다른 누군가가 말했다."]], [["“Lord have mercy upon us,”", "\"주여, 저희를 불쌍히 여기소서.\""], ["said a woman, crossing herself.", "한 여자가 성호를 그으며 말했다."], ["“Have they caught the little girl and the boy?", "\"그 어린 계집애랑 사내애는 잡았답니까?"], ["They’re being brought back, the elder one’s got them....", "데려오고 있네, 큰애가 붙들었어……."], ["Ah, the naughty imps!”", "아이고, 저 말썽꾸러기들!\""]], [["When they examined Katerina Ivanovna carefully, they saw that she had not cut herself against a stone, as Sonia thought, but that the blood that stained the pavement red was from her chest.", "카테리나 이바노브나를 찬찬히 살펴보고 나서야, 사람들은 그녀가 소냐의 생각처럼 돌에 부딪혀 다친 것이 아니라, 포도(鋪道)를 붉게 물들인 피가 그녀의 가슴에서 나온 것임을 알게 되었다."]], [["“I’ve seen that before,”", "\"전에도 저런 걸 본 적이 있어요.\""], ["muttered the official to Raskolnikov and Lebeziatnikov; “that’s consumption; the blood flows and chokes the patient.", "그 관리가 라스콜니코프와 레베쟈트니코프에게 중얼거렸다. \"저건 폐병이오."], ["I saw the same thing with a relative of my own not long ago...", "피가 솟구쳐서 환자를 질식시키는 거죠. 얼마 전에 내 친척한테서도 똑같은 걸 봤습니다……, 거의 한 파인트나 되는 피가, 순식간에……."], ["nearly a pint of blood, all in a minute....", "하지만 어쩌겠소?"], ["What’s to be done though? She is dying.”", "이 여자는 죽어 가고 있어요.\""]], [["“This way, this way, to my room!”", "\"이쪽으로, 이쪽으로, 제 방으로요!\""], ["Sonia implored.", "소냐가 애원했다."], ["“I live here!...", "\"제가 여기 살아요!……"], ["See, that house, the second from here....", "보세요, 저 집, 여기서 두 번째예요……."], ["Come to me, make haste,”", "저한테로 데려와 주세요, 어서요.\""], ["she turned from one to the other.", "그녀가 이 사람 저 사람에게로 몸을 돌리며 말했다."], ["“Send for the doctor!", "\"의사를 불러 주세요!"], ["Oh, dear!”", "오, 어떡해!\""]], [["Thanks to the official’s efforts, this plan was adopted, the policeman even helping to carry Katerina Ivanovna.", "그 관리의 노력 덕분에 이 방안이 채택되었고, 경찰관까지도 카테리나 이바노브나를 옮기는 일을 거들었다."], ["She was carried to Sonia’s room, almost unconscious, and laid on the bed.", "그녀는 거의 의식을 잃은 채 소냐의 방으로 옮겨져 침대에 뉘었다."], ["The blood was still flowing, but she seemed to be coming to herself.", "피는 여전히 흐르고 있었으나, 그녀는 정신이 드는 듯했다."], ["Raskolnikov, Lebeziatnikov, and the official accompanied Sonia into the room and were followed by the policeman, who first drove back the crowd which followed to the very door.", "라스콜니코프, 레베쟈트니코프, 그리고 그 관리가 소냐와 함께 방으로 들어갔고, 경찰관도 그 뒤를 따랐는데, 그는 먼저 문간까지 따라온 군중을 몰아냈다."], ["Polenka came in holding Kolya and Lida, who were trembling and weeping.", "폴렌카가 떨며 울고 있는 콜랴와 리다를 데리고 들어왔다."], ["Several persons came in too from the Kapernaumovs’ room; the landlord, a lame one-eyed man of strange appearance with whiskers and hair that stood up like a brush, his wife, a woman with an everlastingly scared expression, and several open-mouthed children with wonder-struck faces.", "카페르나우모프네 방에서도 여러 사람이 들어왔으니, 집주인은 절름발이에 애꾸눈인 기이한 행색의 남자로, 구레나룻과 솔처럼 빳빳이 곤두선 머리칼을 하고 있었고, 그의 아내는 영원히 겁에 질린 듯한 표정의 여자였으며, 입을 헤벌린 채 신기해하는 얼굴의 아이들도 여럿 따라 들어왔다."], ["Among these, Svidrigaïlov suddenly made his appearance.", "이들 가운데 스비드리가일로프가 불쑥 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Raskolnikov looked at him with surprise, not understanding where he had come from and not having noticed him in the crowd.", "라스콜니코프는 그가 어디서 나타났는지도 모르고 군중 속에서도 그를 알아채지 못했던 터라, 놀라서 그를 바라보았다."], ["A doctor and priest were spoken of.", "의사와 사제 얘기가 오갔다."], ["The official whispered to Raskolnikov that he thought it was too late now for the doctor, but he ordered him to be sent for.", "그 관리는 라스콜니코프에게, 이제 의사를 부르기엔 너무 늦은 것 같다고 속삭였으나, 그래도 사람을 보내 부르라고 일렀다."], ["Kapernaumov ran himself.", "카페르나우모프가 직접 달려갔다."]], [["Meanwhile Katerina Ivanovna had regained her breath.", "그러는 사이 카테리나 이바노브나는 숨을 돌렸다."], ["The bleeding ceased for a time.", "출혈이 한동안 멎었다."], ["She looked with sick but intent and penetrating eyes at Sonia, who stood pale and trembling, wiping the sweat from her brow with a handkerchief.", "그녀는 병색이 완연하면서도 골똘하고 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈으로, 창백하게 떨며 손수건으로 이마의 땀을 닦고 있는 소냐를 바라보았다."], ["At last she asked to be raised.", "마침내 그녀가 일으켜 달라고 청했다."], ["They sat her up on the bed, supporting her on both sides.", "사람들이 양쪽에서 그녀를 부축하여 침대에 일으켜 앉혔다."]], [["“Where are the children?”", "\"아이들은 어디 있니?\""], ["she said in a faint voice.", "그녀가 희미한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“You’ve brought them, Polenka?", "\"네가 데려왔구나, 폴렌카?"], ["Oh the sillies!", "오, 이 바보들!"], ["Why did you run away....", "왜 도망쳤어……."], ["Och!”", "어!\""]], [["Once more her parched lips were covered with blood.", "또다시 그녀의 메마른 입술이 피로 뒤덮였다."], ["She moved her eyes, looking about her.", "그녀는 눈을 굴리며 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“So that’s how you live, Sonia!", "\"그래, 네가 이렇게 살고 있었구나, 소냐!"], ["Never once have I been in your room.”", "네 방에 한 번도 와 본 적이 없었어.\""]], [["She looked at her with a face of suffering.", "그녀는 고통스러운 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“We have been your ruin, Sonia.", "\"우리가 너를 망쳐 놓았어, 소냐."], ["Polenka, Lida, Kolya, come here!", "폴렌카, 리다, 콜랴, 이리 오너라!"], ["Well, here they are, Sonia, take them all!", "자, 여기 있다, 소냐, 다 받아 다오!"], ["I hand them over to you, I’ve had enough!", "이 애들을 너한테 넘기마, 나는 이제 됐어!"], ["The ball is over.”", "무도회는 끝났어.\""], ["(Cough!) “Lay me down, let me die in peace.”", "(기침!) \"날 좀 눕혀 다오, 편히 죽게 해 줘.\""]], [["They laid her back on the pillow.", "사람들이 그녀를 다시 베개에 뉘었다."]], [["“What, the priest?", "\"뭐, 사제라고?"], ["I don’t want him.", "난 필요 없어."], ["You haven’t got a rouble to spare.", "너희한테 남는 루블도 없잖니."], ["I have no sins.", "나한테 무슨 죄가 있다고."], ["God must forgive me without that.", "그런 거 없이도 하느님은 나를 용서하셔야 해."], ["He knows how I have suffered....", "그분은 내가 얼마나 고통받았는지 아시니까……."], ["And if He won’t forgive me, I don’t care!”", "만약 그분이 나를 용서하지 않으신대도, 난 상관없어!\""]], [["She sank more and more into uneasy delirium.", "그녀는 점점 더 불안한 헛소리 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["At times she shuddered, turned her eyes from side to side, recognised everyone for a minute, but at once sank into delirium again.", "이따금 몸을 떨고, 눈을 이리저리 굴리고, 잠깐씩 모두를 알아보았으나, 곧바로 다시 헛소리에 빠지곤 했다."], ["Her breathing was hoarse and difficult, there was a sort of rattle in her throat.", "그녀의 숨소리는 거칠고 힘겨웠고, 목구멍에서는 가르랑거리는 소리 같은 것이 났다."]], [["“I said to him, your excellency,”", "\"내가 그자에게 말했죠, 각하.\""], ["she ejaculated, gasping after each word.", "그녀가 한 마디 한 마디 헐떡이며 내뱉었다. \"그 아말리야 루드비고브나가, 아!"], ["“That Amalia Ludwigovna, ah!", "리다, 콜랴, 두 손을 허리에, 어서!"], ["Lida, Kolya, hands on your hips, make haste!", "미끄러지듯, 미끄러지듯! 파드바스크!* 발뒤꿈치로 박자를 맞추고, 우아한 아이가 되어야지!"], ["_Glissez, glissez!", "* Glissez, glissez!"], ["pas de basque!_ Tap with your heels, be a graceful child!", "pas de basque!: (프랑스어) 미끄러지듯, 미끄러지듯! 파드바스크(발레의 한 스텝)."]], [["“_Du hast Diamanten und Perlen_", "\"그대는 다이아몬드와 진주를 가졌네*"]], [["“What next? That’s the thing to sing.", "* Du hast Diamanten und Perlen: (독일어) 그대는 다이아몬드와 진주를 가졌네."], ["“_Du hast die schönsten Augen Mädchen, was willst du mehr?_", "하이네의 시."]], [["“What an idea!", "\"다음은 뭐지?"], ["_Was willst du mehr?_ What things the fool invents! Ah, yes!", "이게 부를 노래야."]], [["“In the heat of midday in the vale of Dagestan.", "\"그대는 더없이 아름다운 두 눈을 가졌네, 아가씨, 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라는가?*"]], [["“Ah, how I loved it! I loved that song to distraction, Polenka!", "* Du hast die schönsten Augen Mädchen, was willst du mehr?: (독일어) 그대는 더없이 아름다운 두 눈을 가졌네, 아가씨, 그 이상 무엇을 더 바라는가?"], ["Your father, you know, used to sing it when we were engaged.... Oh, those days!", "\"무슨 생각이람!"], ["Oh, that’s the thing for us to sing!", "그 이상 무엇을 더 바라는가?"], ["How does it go? I’ve forgotten.", "저 바보가 별 희한한 소리를 다 지어내네!"], ["Remind me! How was it?”", "아, 그래!"]], [["She was violently excited and tried to sit up. At last, in a horribly hoarse, broken voice, she began, shrieking and gasping at every word, with a look of growing terror.", "\"한낮의 무더위 속, 다게스탄 골짜기에서. \"아, 내가 이 노래를 얼마나 사랑했던지! 미치도록 그 노래를 사랑했단다, 폴렌카! 네 아버지가, 알겠니, 우리가 약혼했을 때 이 노래를 부르곤 했지……. 오, 그 시절! 오, 이게 바로 우리가 부를 노래야! 어떻게 되더라? 잊어버렸네. 좀 일러 줘! 어떻게 됐지?\""]], [["“In the heat of midday!... in the vale!...", "그녀는 격렬하게 흥분하여 일어나 앉으려 애썼다."], ["of Dagestan!... With lead in my breast!...”", "마침내, 무섭게 쉬고 갈라진 목소리로, 한 마디 한 마디마다 비명을 지르고 숨을 헐떡이며, 점점 커져 가는 공포의 표정을 띤 채 그녀가 노래를 부르기 시작했다."]], [["“Your excellency!”", "\"한낮의 무더위 속!…… 골짜기에서!……"], ["she wailed suddenly with a heart-rending scream and a flood of tears, “protect the orphans!", "다게스탄의!…… 가슴에 박힌 납덩이를 안고서!……\""], ["You have been their father’s guest...", "\"각하!\" 그녀가 갑자기 가슴이 미어지는 비명과 함께 눈물을 쏟으며 울부짖었다."], ["one may say aristocratic....”", "\"이 고아들을 지켜 주십시오!"], ["She started, regaining consciousness, and gazed at all with a sort of terror, but at once recognised Sonia.", "각하께서는 이 아이들 아버지의 손님이셨으니까요……, 가히 귀족이라 할 만한…….\" 그녀는 흠칫하며 의식을 되찾았고, 일종의 공포에 차서 모두를 둘러보았으나, 곧 소냐를 알아보았다."]], [["“Sonia, Sonia!”", "\"소냐, 소냐!\""], ["she articulated softly and caressingly, as though surprised to find her there.", "그녀가 거기서 소냐를 발견한 것이 놀랍다는 듯, 부드럽고 다정하게 또렷이 말했다."], ["“Sonia darling, are you here, too?”", "\"소냐, 얘야, 너도 여기 있었니?\""]], [["They lifted her up again.", "사람들이 그녀를 다시 일으켜 세웠다."]], [["“Enough!", "\"됐어!"], ["It’s over!", "다 끝났어!"], ["Farewell, poor thing!", "잘 있어, 가엾은 것!"], ["I am done for!", "난 끝장이야!"], ["I am broken!”", "난 부서져 버렸어!\""], ["she cried with vindictive despair, and her head fell heavily back on the pillow.", "그녀가 원한에 사무친 절망으로 외쳤고, 그녀의 머리가 베개 위로 무겁게 툭 떨어졌다."]], [["She sank into unconsciousness again, but this time it did not last long.", "그녀는 다시 의식을 잃었으나, 이번에는 오래가지 않았다."], ["Her pale, yellow, wasted face dropped back, her mouth fell open, her leg moved convulsively, she gave a deep, deep sigh and died.", "창백하고 누렇게 야윈 그녀의 얼굴이 뒤로 젖혀지고, 입이 벌어지고, 다리가 경련하듯 움찔하더니, 그녀는 깊디깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는 숨을 거두었다."]], [["Sonia fell upon her, flung her arms about her, and remained motionless with her head pressed to the dead woman’s wasted bosom.", "소냐가 그녀에게 엎드려 두 팔로 그녀를 끌어안고는, 죽은 여자의 야윈 가슴에 머리를 묻은 채 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["Polenka threw herself at her mother’s feet, kissing them and weeping violently.", "폴렌카는 어머니의 발치에 몸을 던져 그 발에 입 맞추며 격렬하게 울었다."], ["Though Kolya and Lida did not understand what had happened, they had a feeling that it was something terrible; they put their hands on each other’s little shoulders, stared straight at one another and both at once opened their mouths and began screaming.", "콜랴와 리다는 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 알지 못했지만, 무언가 끔찍한 일이라는 느낌은 들었던지, 서로의 작은 어깨에 손을 얹고 서로를 똑바로 바라보다가, 둘이 동시에 입을 벌리고 비명을 지르기 시작했다."], ["They were both still in their fancy dress; one in a turban, the other in the cap with the ostrich feather.", "둘 다 여전히 분장한 차림 그대로였는데, 하나는 터번을 두르고, 다른 하나는 타조 깃털 달린 모자를 쓰고 있었다."]], [["And how did “the certificate of merit” come to be on the bed beside Katerina Ivanovna?", "그런데 어쩌다 그 '표창장'이 카테리나 이바노브나 곁의 침대 위에 놓이게 된 것일까?"], ["It lay there by the pillow; Raskolnikov saw it.", "그것은 베개 옆에 놓여 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그것을 보았다."]], [["He walked away to the window.", "그는 창가로 걸어갔다."], ["Lebeziatnikov skipped up to him.", "레베쟈트니코프가 그에게로 깡충 다가왔다."]], [["“She is dead,”", "\"죽었어요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Rodion Romanovitch, I must have two words with you,”", "\"로지온 로마노비치, 자네와 두어 마디 나눠야겠네.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov, coming up to them.", "스비드리가일로프가 그들에게 다가오며 말했다."]], [["Lebeziatnikov at once made room for him and delicately withdrew.", "레베쟈트니코프는 곧바로 자리를 비켜 주고는 점잖게 물러났다."], ["Svidrigaïlov drew Raskolnikov further away.", "스비드리가일로프는 라스콜니코프를 좀 더 멀리 끌고 갔다."]], [["“I will undertake all the arrangements, the funeral and that.", "\"내가 모든 준비를, 장례며 그런 것들을 도맡겠네."], ["You know it’s a question of money and, as I told you, I have plenty to spare.", "알다시피 이건 돈 문제이고, 전에 말했듯이 나는 남아도는 돈이 많으니까."], ["I will put those two little ones and Polenka into some good orphan asylum, and I will settle fifteen hundred roubles to be paid to each on coming of age, so that Sofya Semyonovna need have no anxiety about them.", "저 어린 두 아이와 폴렌카는 어디 괜찮은 고아원에 넣고, 성년이 될 때 각자에게 천오백 루블씩 지급되도록 정해 두겠네, 소피야 세묘노브나가 그 애들 걱정은 하지 않아도 되게 말이야."], ["And I will pull her out of the mud too, for she is a good girl, isn’t she?", "그리고 그녀도 이 진창에서 끌어내 주겠어, 착한 아가씨잖나, 안 그런가?"], ["So tell Avdotya Romanovna that that is how I am spending her ten thousand.”", "그러니 아브도티야 로마노브나에게 전하게, 내가 그녀의 만 루블을 이렇게 쓰고 있다고.\""]], [["“What is your motive for such benevolence?”", "\"무슨 속셈으로 그런 선행을 베푸는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 물었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you sceptical person!”", "의심 많은 양반이로군!\""], ["laughed Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프가 웃었다."], ["“I told you I had no need of that money.", "\"내가 그 돈은 필요 없다고 말하지 않았나."], ["Won’t you admit that it’s simply done from humanity?", "그저 인정에서 우러나 하는 일이라고는 인정하지 못하겠나?"], ["She wasn’t ‘a louse,’ you know”", "저 여자는 '이'"], ["(he pointed to the corner where the dead woman lay), “was she, like some old pawnbroker woman?", "같은 게 아니었잖나\"(그가 죽은 여자가 누워 있는 구석을 가리켰다), \"안 그런가, 무슨 늙은 전당포 노파처럼 말이야?"], ["Come, you’ll agree, is Luzhin to go on living, and doing wicked things or is she to die?", "자, 자네도 동의할 걸세, 루진 같은 자는 계속 살아남아 못된 짓을 저질러도 되고, 저 여자는 죽어야 한단 말인가?"], ["And if I didn’t help them, Polenka would go the same way.”", "그리고 내가 저 애들을 돕지 않으면, 폴렌카도 똑같은 길을 가게 될 거야.\""]], [["He said this with an air of a sort of gay winking slyness, keeping his eyes fixed on Raskolnikov, who turned white and cold, hearing his own phrases, spoken to Sonia.", "그는 일종의 유쾌하게 눈을 찡긋하는 듯한 능청스러움을 띠고 이 말을 하면서, 라스콜니코프에게서 눈을 떼지 않았는데, 라스콜니코프는 자기가 소냐에게 했던 바로 그 말을 듣고는 창백해지고 싸늘해졌다."], ["He quickly stepped back and looked wildly at Svidrigaïlov.", "그는 재빨리 뒷걸음질 치며 미친 듯한 눈으로 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다."]], [["“How do you know?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 아는 거요?\""], ["he whispered, hardly able to breathe.", "그가 숨도 제대로 못 쉬며 속삭였다."]], [["“Why, I lodge here at Madame Resslich’s, the other side of the wall.", "\"아니, 나는 여기, 벽 너머에 레슬리히 부인 댁에 세 들어 살고 있다네."], ["Here is Kapernaumov, and there lives Madame Resslich, an old and devoted friend of mine.", "여기는 카페르나우모프네 집이고, 저기엔 레슬리히 부인이 사는데, 나의 오랜 절친한 친구지."], ["I am a neighbour.”", "나는 이웃이야.\""]], [["“You?”", "\"당신이?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇다네.\""], ["continued Svidrigaïlov, shaking with laughter.", "스비드리가일로프가 웃음으로 몸을 흔들며 말을 이었다."], ["“I assure you on my honour, dear Rodion Romanovitch, that you have interested me enormously.", "\"맹세코 정말일세, 친애하는 로지온 로마노비치, 자네는 나에게 엄청난 흥미를 불러일으켰어."], ["I told you we should become friends, I foretold it.", "우리가 친구가 되리라고 내가 말하지 않았나, 내가 그걸 예언했지."], ["Well, here we have.", "자, 이렇게 됐군."], ["And you will see what an accommodating person I am.", "그리고 자네는 내가 얼마나 융통성 있는 사람인지 알게 될 걸세."], ["You’ll see that you can get on with me!”", "자네는 나와 잘 지낼 수 있다는 걸 알게 될 거야!\""]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["A strange period began for Raskolnikov: it was as though a fog had fallen upon him and wrapped him in a dreary solitude from which there was no escape.", "라스콜니코프에게 기묘한 시기가 시작되었다. 마치 안개가 그에게 내려앉아 그를 빠져나갈 길 없는 음울한 고독 속으로 감싸 버린 것 같았다."], ["Recalling that period long after, he believed that his mind had been clouded at times, and that it had continued so, with intervals, till the final catastrophe.", "오랜 세월이 흐른 뒤 그 시기를 떠올리면서, 그는 자신의 정신이 때때로 흐려져 있었고, 그 상태가 간헐적으로 최후의 파국에 이르기까지 계속되었으리라고 믿었다."], ["He was convinced that he had been mistaken about many things at that time, for instance as to the date of certain events.", "그는 그때 자신이 많은 것들에 대해, 이를테면 어떤 사건들이 일어난 날짜 같은 것에 대해 착각하고 있었다고 확신했다."], ["Anyway, when he tried later on to piece his recollections together, he learnt a great deal about himself from what other people told him.", "어쨌든 나중에 자신의 기억들을 끼워 맞추려 애쓰면서, 그는 다른 사람들이 해 준 이야기를 통해 자기 자신에 관해 많은 것을 알게 되었다."], ["He had mixed up incidents and had explained events as due to circumstances which existed only in his imagination.", "그는 여러 사건들을 뒤섞어 놓았고, 어떤 일들을 오직 자신의 상상 속에서만 존재하던 정황 탓으로 설명하곤 했다."], ["At times he was a prey to agonies of morbid uneasiness, amounting sometimes to panic.", "때때로 그는 병적인 불안의 고통에 사로잡혔는데, 그 불안은 이따금 공황 상태에까지 이르렀다."], ["But he remembered, too, moments, hours, perhaps whole days, of complete apathy, which came upon him as a reaction from his previous terror and might be compared with the abnormal insensibility, sometimes seen in the dying.", "그러나 그는 또한 완전한 무감각의 순간들, 시간들, 어쩌면 며칠을 통째로 기억하고 있었다. 그 무감각은 이전의 공포에 대한 반작용으로 그에게 찾아왔으며, 죽어 가는 자들에게서 이따금 보이는 비정상적인 둔감함에 견줄 만한 것이었다."], ["He seemed to be trying in that latter stage to escape from a full and clear understanding of his position.", "그는 그 후자의 단계에서 자신의 처지를 온전하고 분명하게 이해하는 일로부터 달아나려 애쓰는 것 같았다."], ["Certain essential facts which required immediate consideration were particularly irksome to him.", "당장 고려해야 할 어떤 본질적인 사실들이 그에게는 유난히 성가셨다."], ["How glad he would have been to be free from some cares, the neglect of which would have threatened him with complete, inevitable ruin.", "어떤 근심들로부터 자유로울 수 있다면 그는 얼마나 기뻤을까. 그 근심들을 등한시한다면 완전하고 피할 수 없는 파멸의 위협을 받게 될 텐데도 말이다."]], [["He was particularly worried about Svidrigaïlov, he might be said to be permanently thinking of Svidrigaïlov.", "그는 특히 스비드리가일로프 때문에 골머리를 앓았으니, 끊임없이 스비드리가일로프를 생각하고 있었다고 해도 좋을 정도였다."], ["From the time of Svidrigaïlov’s too menacing and unmistakable words in Sonia’s room at the moment of Katerina Ivanovna’s death, the normal working of his mind seemed to break down.", "카테리나 이바노브나가 죽던 순간 소냐의 방에서 스비드리가일로프가 너무도 위협적이고 분명하게 내뱉었던 말들이 있은 뒤로, 그의 정신의 정상적인 작동은 무너져 버린 듯했다."], ["But although this new fact caused him extreme uneasiness, Raskolnikov was in no hurry for an explanation of it.", "그러나 이 새로운 사실이 그에게 극도의 불안을 안겨 주었음에도, 라스콜니코프는 그것을 서둘러 해명하려 들지 않았다."], ["At times, finding himself in a solitary and remote part of the town, in some wretched eating-house, sitting alone lost in thought, hardly knowing how he had come there, he suddenly thought of Svidrigaïlov.", "때때로 그는 도시의 한적하고 외진 곳, 어느 초라한 식당에 홀로 앉아 깊은 생각에 잠겨, 자신이 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지조차 거의 알지 못한 채로 있다가, 문득 스비드리가일로프를 떠올렸다."], ["He recognised suddenly, clearly, and with dismay that he ought at once to come to an understanding with that man and to make what terms he could.", "그는 자신이 즉시 그자와 어떤 양해에 도달해, 가능한 한 합의를 보아야 한다는 것을 갑작스레, 분명하게, 그리고 경악과 함께 깨달았다."], ["Walking outside the city gates one day, he positively fancied that they had fixed a meeting there, that he was waiting for Svidrigaïlov.", "어느 날 성문 밖을 거닐면서, 그는 정말로 자신이 거기서 만나기로 약속을 했고, 스비드리가일로프를 기다리고 있는 것이라고 상상했다."], ["Another time he woke up before daybreak lying on the ground under some bushes and could not at first understand how he had come there.", "또 한번은 어느 덤불 아래 땅바닥에 누운 채 동트기 전에 깨어났는데, 처음에는 자신이 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었다."]], [["But during the two or three days after Katerina Ivanovna’s death, he had two or three times met Svidrigaïlov at Sonia’s lodging, where he had gone aimlessly for a moment.", "그러나 카테리나 이바노브나가 죽은 뒤 이삼 일 동안, 그는 두세 차례 소냐의 하숙집에서 스비드리가일로프와 마주쳤다. 그는 별 목적도 없이 잠깐씩 그곳에 갔던 것이다."], ["They exchanged a few words and made no reference to the vital subject, as though they were tacitly agreed not to speak of it for a time.", "두 사람은 몇 마디 말을 주고받았을 뿐, 그 핵심적인 화제에 대해서는 전혀 언급하지 않았으니, 마치 한동안 그것에 대해 말하지 않기로 암묵적으로 합의라도 한 듯했다."]], [["Katerina Ivanovna’s body was still lying in the coffin, Svidrigaïlov was busy making arrangements for the funeral.", "카테리나 이바노브나의 시신은 아직 관 속에 누워 있었고, 스비드리가일로프는 장례 준비에 분주했다."], ["Sonia too was very busy.", "소냐 역시 무척 바빴다."], ["At their last meeting Svidrigaïlov informed Raskolnikov that he had made an arrangement, and a very satisfactory one, for Katerina Ivanovna’s children; that he had, through certain connections, succeeded in getting hold of certain personages by whose help the three orphans could be at once placed in very suitable institutions; that the money he had settled on them had been of great assistance, as it is much easier to place orphans with some property than destitute ones.", "마지막으로 만났을 때 스비드리가일로프는 라스콜니코프에게, 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이들을 위해 어떤 조처를, 그것도 아주 만족스러운 조처를 취해 두었다고 알려 주었다. 그가 어떤 연줄을 통해 어떤 인사들을 손에 넣는 데 성공했고, 그들의 도움으로 세 고아를 즉시 아주 적당한 시설에 들여보낼 수 있게 되었으며, 그가 그 아이들 앞으로 정해 놓은 돈이 큰 도움이 되었으니, 재산이 좀 있는 고아를 들이는 것이 빈털터리 고아보다 훨씬 수월하기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["He said something too about Sonia and promised to come himself in a day or two to see Raskolnikov, mentioning that “he would like to consult with him, that there were things they must talk over....”", "그는 소냐에 관해서도 무언가를 말했고, 하루 이틀 안에 라스콜니코프를 보러 직접 찾아오겠다고 약속하면서, \"그와 의논하고 싶은 것이 있다, 두 사람이 꼭 이야기해야 할 일들이 있다……\"고 언급했다."]], [["This conversation took place in the passage on the stairs.", "이 대화는 계단의 통로에서 이루어졌다."], ["Svidrigaïlov looked intently at Raskolnikov and suddenly, after a brief pause, dropping his voice, asked: “But how is it, Rodion Romanovitch; you don’t seem yourself?", "스비드리가일로프는 라스콜니코프를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 잠시 말을 멈춘 뒤 불쑥 목소리를 낮추어 물었다. \"한데 어찌 된 일입니까, 로지온 로마노비치, 당신 같지가 않군요?"], ["You look and you listen, but you don’t seem to understand.", "보고 듣고는 있지만, 도무지 알아듣지 못하는 것 같소."], ["Cheer up!", "기운을 내요!"], ["We’ll talk things over; I am only sorry, I’ve so much to do of my own business and other people’s.", "우리 차차 이야기를 나눕시다. 다만 유감스러운 건, 내 일이며 남의 일이며 할 일이 너무 많다는 거요."], ["Ah, Rodion Romanovitch,”", "아, 로지온 로마노비치.\" 그가 갑자기 덧붙였다."], ["he added suddenly, “what all men need is fresh air, fresh air... more than anything!”", "\"모든 인간에게 필요한 건 신선한 공기요, 신선한 공기……, 무엇보다도 그게 필요하지요!\""]], [["He moved to one side to make way for the priest and server, who were coming up the stairs.", "그는 한쪽으로 비켜서서 계단을 올라오던 사제와 복사에게 길을 내주었다."], ["They had come for the requiem service.", "그들은 위령 미사를 드리러 온 것이었다."], ["By Svidrigaïlov’s orders it was sung twice a day punctually.", "스비드리가일로프의 지시로 미사는 하루에 두 번씩 어김없이 거행되었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov went his way.", "스비드리가일로프는 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["Raskolnikov stood still a moment, thought, and followed the priest into Sonia’s room.", "라스콜니코프는 잠시 가만히 서서 생각하다가, 사제를 따라 소냐의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["He stood at the door.", "그는 문가에 섰다."], ["They began quietly, slowly and mournfully singing the service.", "사람들은 조용히, 느릿하고 애처롭게 미사를 노래하기 시작했다."], ["From his childhood the thought of death and the presence of death had something oppressive and mysteriously awful; and it was long since he had heard the requiem service.", "어린 시절부터 죽음에 대한 생각과 죽음의 존재는 그에게 무언가 짓누르는 듯하고 신비롭게 끔찍한 것이었다. 게다가 그가 위령 미사를 들어 본 지도 오래되었다."], ["And there was something else here as well, too awful and disturbing.", "또한 여기에는 그 밖에도 무언가, 너무도 끔찍하고 마음을 어지럽히는 것이 있었다."], ["He looked at the children: they were all kneeling by the coffin; Polenka was weeping.", "그는 아이들을 바라보았다. 아이들은 모두 관 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 있었고, 폴렌카는 울고 있었다."], ["Behind them Sonia prayed, softly and, as it were, timidly weeping.", "그 뒤에서 소냐가 기도를 드리며, 나직이, 말하자면 수줍은 듯이 울고 있었다."]], [["“These last two days she hasn’t said a word to me, she hasn’t glanced at me,”", "'지난 이틀 동안 그녀는 내게 한 마디도 하지 않았고, 나를 한 번 쳐다보지도 않았어.'"], ["Raskolnikov thought suddenly.", "라스콜니코프는 문득 생각했다."], ["The sunlight was bright in the room; the incense rose in clouds; the priest read, “Give rest, oh Lord....”", "햇살이 방 안에 환했고, 향연이 구름처럼 피어올랐다. 사제가 읽었다."], ["Raskolnikov stayed all through the service.", "\"오, 주여, 안식을 주소서…….\""], ["As he blessed them and took his leave, the priest looked round strangely.", "라스콜니코프는 미사가 끝날 때까지 내내 머물렀다. 사람들에게 축복을 내리고 작별을 고하면서, 사제는 묘한 눈길로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["After the service, Raskolnikov went up to Sonia.", "미사가 끝난 뒤, 라스콜니코프는 소냐에게 다가갔다."], ["She took both his hands and let her head sink on his shoulder.", "그녀는 그의 두 손을 잡더니 그의 어깨에 머리를 기댔다."], ["This slight friendly gesture bewildered Raskolnikov.", "이 작고 다정한 몸짓이 라스콜니코프를 당혹스럽게 했다."], ["It seemed strange to him that there was no trace of repugnance, no trace of disgust, no tremor in her hand.", "거기에 혐오의 흔적도, 역겨움의 흔적도, 그녀의 손에 떨림조차 없다는 것이 그에게는 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["It was the furthest limit of self-abnegation, at least so he interpreted it.", "그것은 자기희생의 극한이었다, 적어도 그는 그렇게 해석했다."]], [["Sonia said nothing.", "소냐는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Raskolnikov pressed her hand and went out.", "라스콜니코프는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 밖으로 나왔다."], ["He felt very miserable.", "그는 몹시 비참한 기분이었다."], ["If it had been possible to escape to some solitude, he would have thought himself lucky, even if he had to spend his whole life there.", "어딘가 고독한 곳으로 달아날 수만 있다면, 설령 그곳에서 평생을 보내야 한다 해도 자신을 운이 좋다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["But although he had almost always been by himself of late, he had never been able to feel alone.", "그러나 비록 그는 요즈음 거의 언제나 혼자였지만, 한 번도 혼자라고 느낄 수 없었다."], ["Sometimes he walked out of the town on to the high road, once he had even reached a little wood, but the lonelier the place was, the more he seemed to be aware of an uneasy presence near him.", "때때로 그는 도시를 벗어나 한길로 나갔고, 한번은 작은 숲에까지 이른 적도 있었지만, 그 장소가 외지면 외질수록 그는 자기 곁에 어떤 불안한 존재가 있다는 것을 더욱 의식하는 것 같았다."], ["It did not frighten him, but greatly annoyed him, so that he made haste to return to the town, to mingle with the crowd, to enter restaurants and taverns, to walk in busy thoroughfares.", "그것은 그를 두렵게 하지는 않았지만 몹시 짜증나게 했고, 그래서 그는 서둘러 도시로 돌아와, 군중 속에 섞이고, 음식점과 선술집으로 들어가고, 번잡한 큰길을 걸었다."], ["There he felt easier and even more solitary.", "거기서 그는 더 마음이 편했고 오히려 더 고독했다."], ["One day at dusk he sat for an hour listening to songs in a tavern and he remembered that he positively enjoyed it.", "어느 날 황혼 녘에 그는 한 시간 동안 선술집에 앉아 노래를 들었는데, 그것을 정말로 즐겼던 것을 그는 기억했다."], ["But at last he had suddenly felt the same uneasiness again, as though his conscience smote him.", "그러나 마침내 그는 또다시 그 똑같은 불안을 불쑥 느꼈으니, 마치 양심이 그를 찌르는 것 같았다."], ["“Here I sit listening to singing, is that what I ought to be doing?”", "'여기 앉아 노래나 듣고 있다니, 이게 내가 해야 할 일인가?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["Yet he felt at once that that was not the only cause of his uneasiness; there was something requiring immediate decision, but it was something he could not clearly understand or put into words.", "하지만 그는 그것이 자기 불안의 유일한 원인이 아니라는 것을 곧바로 느꼈다. 무언가 당장 결정을 요하는 것이 있었지만, 그것은 그가 분명히 이해하거나 말로 옮길 수 없는 무엇이었다."], ["It was a hopeless tangle.", "그것은 가망 없는 뒤엉킴이었다."], ["“No, better the struggle again!", "'아니, 차라리 다시 싸움이 낫지!"], ["Better Porfiry again...", "차라리 포르피리를 다시……"], ["or Svidrigaïlov....", "아니면 스비드리가일로프를……."], ["Better some challenge again...", "차라리 또 어떤 도전이 낫지……"], ["some attack.", "어떤 공격이."], ["Yes, yes!” he thought.", "그래, 그래!'"], ["He went out of the tavern and rushed away almost at a run.", "그는 생각했다. 그는 선술집을 나와 거의 뛰다시피 달아났다."], ["The thought of Dounia and his mother suddenly reduced him almost to a panic.", "두냐와 어머니 생각이 갑자기 그를 거의 공황 상태로 몰아넣었다."], ["That night he woke up before morning among some bushes in Krestovsky Island, trembling all over with fever; he walked home, and it was early morning when he arrived.", "그날 밤 그는 크레스톱스키 섬의 어느 덤불 사이에서 아침이 되기 전에 깨어났는데, 온몸이 열로 부들부들 떨리고 있었다. 그는 집으로 걸어갔고, 도착했을 때는 이른 아침이었다."], ["After some hours’ sleep the fever left him, but he woke up late, two o’clock in the afternoon.", "몇 시간을 자고 나자 열이 가셨지만, 그가 늦게, 오후 두 시에야 깨어났다."]], [["He remembered that Katerina Ivanovna’s funeral had been fixed for that day, and was glad that he was not present at it.", "그는 카테리나 이바노브나의 장례가 그날로 정해져 있었음을 떠올렸고, 자신이 거기 참석하지 않은 것이 다행스러웠다."], ["Nastasya brought him some food; he ate and drank with appetite, almost with greediness.", "나스타시야가 그에게 음식을 좀 가져다주었다. 그는 식욕을 느끼며, 거의 게걸스럽게 먹고 마셨다."], ["His head was fresher and he was calmer than he had been for the last three days.", "머리가 한결 맑아졌고, 지난 사흘 동안보다 마음이 차분해졌다."], ["He even felt a passing wonder at his previous attacks of panic.", "그는 이전에 자신을 덮쳤던 공황의 발작에 대해 지나가는 의아함마저 느꼈다."]], [["The door opened and Razumihin came in.", "문이 열리고 라주미힌이 들어왔다."]], [["“Ah, he’s eating, then he’s not ill,”", "\"아, 먹고 있군, 그럼 아프진 않다는 거지.\""], ["said Razumihin.", "라주미힌이 말했다."], ["He took a chair and sat down at the table opposite Raskolnikov.", "그는 의자를 끌어다 탁자 맞은편의 라스콜니코프 앞에 앉았다."]], [["He was troubled and did not attempt to conceal it.", "그는 심란해하면서도 그것을 감추려 하지 않았다."], ["He spoke with evident annoyance, but without hurry or raising his voice.", "그는 분명한 짜증을 담아, 그러나 서두르거나 목소리를 높이지 않고 말했다."], ["He looked as though he had some special fixed determination.", "마치 어떤 특별한, 굳게 정해진 결심을 품은 듯한 모습이었다."]], [["“Listen,”", "\"잘 들어.\""], ["he began resolutely.", "그가 단호하게 입을 열었다."], ["“As far as I am concerned, you may all go to hell, but from what I see, it’s clear to me that I can’t make head or tail of it; please don’t think I’ve come to ask you questions.", "\"나로 말할 것 같으면, 너희 모두 지옥에나 가 버리라지, 하지만 내가 본 바로는, 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수가 없다는 게 분명해. 제발 내가 너한테 뭘 캐물으러 왔다고 생각진 마."], ["I don’t want to know, hang it!", "알고 싶지도 않으니까, 빌어먹을!"], ["If you begin telling me your secrets, I dare say I shouldn’t stay to listen, I should go away cursing.", "네가 비밀을 털어놓기 시작하면, 장담컨대 나는 듣고 있지도 않고 욕설을 퍼부으며 나가 버릴 거야."], ["I have only come to find out once for all whether it’s a fact that you are mad?", "나는 그저 단 한 가지, 네가 정말로 미친 게 사실인지 확인하러 왔을 뿐이야."], ["There is a conviction in the air that you are mad or very nearly so.", "네가 미쳤거나 거의 미친 거나 다름없다는 믿음이 떠돌고 있거든."], ["I admit I’ve been disposed to that opinion myself, judging from your stupid, repulsive and quite inexplicable actions, and from your recent behavior to your mother and sister.", "솔직히 나도 그렇게 생각하는 쪽으로 기울어 있었어, 너의 그 멍청하고 역겹고 도무지 설명할 수 없는 행동들로 미루어 보나, 최근에 네가 어머니와 누이를 대한 태도로 보나 말이야."], ["Only a monster or a madman could treat them as you have; so you must be mad.”", "괴물이나 미치광이가 아니고서야 그분들을 너처럼 대할 수는 없으니까, 그러니 너는 미친 게 틀림없어.\""]], [["“When did you see them last?”", "\"그분들을 마지막으로 본 게 언제야?\""]], [["“Just now.", "\"방금 전에."], ["Haven’t you seen them since then?", "너는 그 후로 그분들을 못 봤다는 거야?"], ["What have you been doing with yourself?", "도대체 너 자신을 어떻게 하고 다닌 거야?"], ["Tell me, please.", "제발 말 좀 해 봐."], ["I’ve been to you three times already.", "나는 벌써 너한테 세 번이나 왔었어."], ["Your mother has been seriously ill since yesterday.", "어머니는 어제부터 심하게 앓아누우셨어."], ["She had made up her mind to come to you; Avdotya Romanovna tried to prevent her; she wouldn’t hear a word.", "너한테 오겠다고 마음을 굳히셨지. 아브도티야 로마노브나가 말리려 했지만, 한 마디도 들으려 하지 않으셨어."], ["‘If he is ill, if his mind is giving way, who can look after him like his mother?’ she said.", "'그애가 아프다면, 정신이 이상해진 거라면, 어미 말고 누가 그애를 돌볼 수 있겠니?' 하고 말씀하셨지."], ["We all came here together, we couldn’t let her come alone all the way.", "우리는 다 함께 여기로 왔어. 그분 혼자 그 먼 길을 오시게 둘 수는 없었으니까."], ["We kept begging her to be calm.", "우리는 거듭 진정하시라고 빌었어."], ["We came in, you weren’t here; she sat down, and stayed ten minutes, while we stood waiting in silence.", "우리가 들어왔을 때 너는 여기 없었지. 그분은 앉으셔서 십 분쯤 머무르셨고, 그동안 우리는 말없이 서서 기다렸어."], ["She got up and said: ‘If he’s gone out, that is, if he is well, and has forgotten his mother, it’s humiliating and unseemly for his mother to stand at his door begging for kindness.’ She returned home and took to her bed; now she is in a fever.", "그분은 일어서며 말씀하셨어. '그애가 나갔다면, 그러니까 멀쩡한데 제 어미를 잊었다면, 어미가 그애 집 문 앞에 서서 다정함을 구걸하는 건 모욕적이고 꼴사나운 일이지.'"], ["‘I see,’ she said, ‘that he has time for _his girl_.’ She means by _your girl_ Sofya Semyonovna, your betrothed or your mistress, I don’t know.", "그분은 집으로 돌아가 자리에 누우셨고, 지금은 열에 들떠 계셔. '알겠다,'"], ["I went at once to Sofya Semyonovna’s, for I wanted to know what was going on.", "그분이 말씀하셨어. '그애는 제 여자한테 쓸 시간은 있구나.'"], ["I looked round, I saw the coffin, the children crying, and Sofya Semyonovna trying them on mourning dresses.", "제 여자라는 건 소피야 세묘노브나를 두고 하시는 말씀이야, 네 약혼녀든 정부든, 나는 모르겠지만. 나는 곧장 소피야 세묘노브나한테 갔어, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알고 싶었으니까."], ["No sign of you. I apologised, came away, and reported to Avdotya Romanovna.", "둘러보니 관이 있고, 아이들이 울고 있고, 소피야 세묘노브나가 아이들한테 상복을 입혀 보고 있더군."], ["So that’s all nonsense and you haven’t got a girl; the most likely thing is that you are mad.", "네 모습은 보이지 않았어. 나는 사과하고 나와서 아브도티야 로마노브나한테 보고했지."], ["But here you sit, guzzling boiled beef as though you’d not had a bite for three days.", "그러니 그건 다 헛소리고, 너한테 여자 같은 건 없는 거야. 가장 그럴듯한 건 네가 미쳤다는 거지."], ["Though as far as that goes, madmen eat too, but though you have not said a word to me yet...", "그런데 여기 너는 앉아서, 사흘이나 한 입도 못 먹은 사람처럼 삶은 쇠고기를 게걸스레 먹고 있어. 하기야 그것만 가지고 따지자면 미치광이도 먹긴 하지, 하지만 네가 아직 나한테 한 마디도 하지 않았는데도……"], ["you are not mad!", "너는 미치지 않았어!"], ["That I’d swear!", "그건 내가 맹세할 수 있어!"], ["Above all, you are not mad!", "무엇보다도, 너는 미치지 않았어!"], ["So you may go to hell, all of you, for there’s some mystery, some secret about it, and I don’t intend to worry my brains over your secrets.", "그러니 너희 모두 지옥에나 가 버려, 거기엔 무슨 수수께끼가, 무슨 비밀이 있는 거고, 나는 너희 비밀 따위로 골머리를 썩일 생각이 없으니까."], ["So I’ve simply come to swear at you,”", "그래서 나는 그저 너한테 욕이나 퍼부으러 온 거야.\""], ["he finished, getting up, “to relieve my mind.", "그는 일어서며 말을 맺었다. \"속이 좀 후련해지라고."], ["And I know what to do now.”", "그리고 이제 어떻게 해야 할지 알겠어.\""]], [["“What do you mean to do now?”", "\"이제 어떻게 할 작정인데?\""]], [["“What business is it of yours what I mean to do?”", "\"내가 어떻게 할 작정이든 그게 너랑 무슨 상관이야?\""]], [["“You are going in for a drinking bout.”", "\"술판을 벌이러 가겠지.\""]], [["“How...", "\"어떻게……"], ["how did you know?”", "어떻게 알았어?\""]], [["“Why, it’s pretty plain.”", "\"뭐, 빤하잖아.\""]], [["Razumihin paused for a minute.", "라주미힌은 잠시 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“You always have been a very rational person and you’ve never been mad, never,”", "\"너는 언제나 무척 이성적인 사람이었어, 한 번도 미친 적이 없었지, 결코.\""], ["he observed suddenly with warmth.", "그가 갑자기 열을 띠며 말했다."], ["“You’re right: I shall drink.", "\"네 말이 맞아. 나는 술을 마실 거야."], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있어!\""]], [["And he moved to go out.", "그러고는 나가려고 몸을 움직였다."]], [["“I was talking with my sister--the day before yesterday, I think it was--about you, Razumihin.”", "\"내가 누이와 이야기를 나눴어, 그저께였던 것 같은데, 너에 대해서 말이야, 라주미힌.\""]], [["“About me! But...", "\"나에 대해서!"], ["where can you have seen her the day before yesterday?”", "하지만…… 그저께 네가 누이를 어디서 봤다는 거야?\""], ["Razumihin stopped short and even turned a little pale.", "라주미힌은 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 심지어 얼굴이 약간 창백해졌다."]], [["One could see that his heart was throbbing slowly and violently.", "그의 심장이 느리고 격렬하게 뛰고 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["“She came here by herself, sat there and talked to me.”", "\"누이가 혼자 여기 와서, 저기 앉아 나와 이야기를 했어.\""]], [["“She did!” “Yes.”", "\"누이가 그랬다고!\""]], [["“What did you say to her...", "\"그래.\" \"누이한테 뭐라고 했어……"], ["I mean, about me?”", "그러니까, 나에 대해서 말이야?\""]], [["“I told her you were a very good, honest, and industrious man.", "\"네가 아주 선량하고, 정직하고, 부지런한 사람이라고 했어."], ["I didn’t tell her you love her, because she knows that herself.”", "네가 누이를 사랑한다고는 말하지 않았어, 그건 누이 자신이 알고 있으니까.\""]], [["“She knows that herself?”", "\"누이가 자기 자신이 그걸 안다고?\""]], [["“Well, it’s pretty plain.", "\"뭐, 빤한 일이지."], ["Wherever I might go, whatever happened to me, you would remain to look after them.", "내가 어디로 가든, 나한테 무슨 일이 일어나든, 너는 남아서 그분들을 돌볼 거야."], ["I, so to speak, give them into your keeping, Razumihin.", "나는, 말하자면, 그분들을 네 손에 맡기는 거야, 라주미힌."], ["I say this because I know quite well how you love her, and am convinced of the purity of your heart.", "내가 이렇게 말하는 건, 네가 누이를 얼마나 사랑하는지 잘 알고, 네 마음의 순수함을 확신하기 때문이야."], ["I know that she too may love you and perhaps does love you already.", "누이 역시 너를 사랑할 수 있고, 어쩌면 이미 사랑하고 있을지도 모른다는 걸 나는 알아."], ["Now decide for yourself, as you know best, whether you need go in for a drinking bout or not.”", "자, 이제 네가 스스로 결정해, 네가 제일 잘 알 테니, 네가 술판을 벌이러 가야 할지 말지 말이야.\""]], [["“Rodya!", "\"로쟈!"], ["You see...", "너 봐……"], ["well....", "그러니까……."], ["Ach, damn it!", "아, 빌어먹을!"], ["But where do you mean to go?", "그런데 너는 어디로 갈 작정이야?"], ["Of course, if it’s all a secret, never mind....", "물론, 그게 다 비밀이라면, 상관없어……."], ["But I...", "하지만 나는……"], ["I shall find out the secret...", "나는 그 비밀을 알아낼 거야……"], ["and I am sure that it must be some ridiculous nonsense and that you’ve made it all up.", "그리고 그게 틀림없이 무슨 우스꽝스러운 헛소리이고 네가 다 지어낸 거라고 확신해."], ["Anyway you are a capital fellow, a capital fellow!...”", "어쨌든 너는 굉장한 녀석이야, 굉장한 녀석!……\""]], [["“That was just what I wanted to add, only you interrupted, that that was a very good decision of yours not to find out these secrets.", "\"내가 덧붙이고 싶었던 게 바로 그거야, 네가 가로막았을 뿐이지, 그 비밀들을 알아내지 않겠다는 건 네 아주 훌륭한 결정이라고 말이야."], ["Leave it to time, don’t worry about it.", "시간에 맡겨 둬, 그 일로 애태우지 마."], ["You’ll know it all in time when it must be.", "때가 되면 너는 모든 걸 알게 될 거야, 마땅히 알아야 할 때가 되면."], ["Yesterday a man said to me that what a man needs is fresh air, fresh air, fresh air.", "어제 어떤 사람이 나한테, 인간에게 필요한 건 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기라고 말하더군."], ["I mean to go to him directly to find out what he meant by that.”", "나는 지금 곧장 그 사람한테 가서 그 말이 무슨 뜻이었는지 알아낼 작정이야.\""]], [["Razumihin stood lost in thought and excitement, making a silent conclusion.", "라주미힌은 생각과 흥분에 잠긴 채 서서, 말없이 어떤 결론을 내리고 있었다."]], [["“He’s a political conspirator!", "'그는 정치적 음모가야!"], ["He must be.", "틀림없어."], ["And he’s on the eve of some desperate step, that’s certain.", "그리고 그는 무슨 절망적인 행동을 앞두고 있는 거야, 그건 확실해."], ["It can only be that!", "그것 말고는 있을 수 없어!"], ["And...", "그리고……"], ["and Dounia knows,”", "그리고 두냐가 알고 있어.'"], ["he thought suddenly.", "그는 갑자기 생각했다."]], [["“So Avdotya Romanovna comes to see you,”", "'그러니까 아브도티야 로마노브나가 너를 보러 오고,'"], ["he said, weighing each syllable, “and you’re going to see a man who says we need more air, and so of course that letter...", "그는 한 음절 한 음절 가늠하듯이 생각했다. '너는 우리한테 더 많은 공기가 필요하다고 말하는 사람을 보러 가겠다는 거지, 그러니 당연히 그 편지도……"], ["that too must have something to do with it,”", "그것도 이 일과 무슨 관련이 있는 게 틀림없어.'"], ["he concluded to himself.", "그는 속으로 결론지었다."]], [["“What letter?”", "\"무슨 편지?\""]], [["“She got a letter to-day.", "\"누이가 오늘 편지를 받았어."], ["It upset her very much--very much indeed.", "그게 누이를 무척 동요시켰지, 정말 무척이나."], ["Too much so.", "너무 지나칠 정도로."], ["I began speaking of you, she begged me not to.", "내가 네 얘기를 꺼내기 시작하자, 누이는 그러지 말아 달라고 빌었어."], ["Then...", "그러더니……"], ["then she said that perhaps we should very soon have to part...", "그러더니 어쩌면 우리가 아주 곧 헤어지게 될지도 모른다고 하더군……"], ["then she began warmly thanking me for something; then she went to her room and locked herself in.”", "그러고는 무언가에 대해 나한테 열렬히 고마워하기 시작했어. 그러더니 자기 방으로 가서 문을 잠가 버렸어.\""]], [["“She got a letter?”", "\"누이가 편지를 받았다고?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked thoughtfully.", "라스콜니코프가 생각에 잠겨 물었다."]], [["“Yes, and you didn’t know?", "\"그래, 너는 몰랐어?"], ["hm...”", "흠…….\""]], [["They were both silent.", "두 사람은 모두 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Good-bye, Rodion.", "\"잘 있어, 로지온."], ["There was a time, brother, when I....", "한때는, 이 친구야, 내가……."], ["Never mind, good-bye.", "아니, 됐어, 잘 있어."], ["You see, there was a time....", "알다시피, 한때는……."], ["Well, good-bye!", "뭐, 잘 있어!"], ["I must be off too.", "나도 가 봐야겠어."], ["I am not going to drink.", "나는 술 안 마실 거야."], ["There’s no need now....", "이제는 그럴 필요가 없어졌으니까……."], ["That’s all stuff!”", "그건 다 헛소리야!\""]], [["He hurried out; but when he had almost closed the door behind him, he suddenly opened it again, and said, looking away:", "그는 서둘러 나갔다. 그러나 거의 문을 닫았을 무렵, 그는 갑자기 다시 문을 열고는, 시선을 돌린 채 말했다."]], [["“Oh, by the way, do you remember that murder, you know Porfiry’s, that old woman?", "\"아, 그건 그렇고, 너 그 살인 사건 기억해, 그러니까 포르피리의, 그 노파 말이야?"], ["Do you know the murderer has been found, he has confessed and given the proofs.", "범인이 잡혔다는 거 알아, 자백하고 증거까지 댔대."], ["It’s one of those very workmen, the painter, only fancy!", "바로 그 인부들 중 하나야, 페인트칠하던 자, 어디 한번 상상해 봐!"], ["Do you remember I defended them here?", "내가 여기서 그자들을 변호했던 거 기억해?"], ["Would you believe it, all that scene of fighting and laughing with his companions on the stairs while the porter and the two witnesses were going up, he got up on purpose to disarm suspicion.", "믿어지겠어, 문지기와 두 증인이 올라가던 그 모든 광경, 계단에서 동료들과 싸우고 웃고 했던 그 장면을, 그자가 의심을 따돌리려고 일부러 꾸민 거였대."], ["The cunning, the presence of mind of the young dog!", "그 어린 개자식의 그 교활함, 그 침착함이라니!"], ["One can hardly credit it; but it’s his own explanation, he has confessed it all.", "좀처럼 믿기지 않지만, 그게 바로 그자 자신의 설명이고, 그자가 모든 걸 자백한 거야."], ["And what a fool I was about it!", "그리고 나는 그 일로 얼마나 바보였는지!"], ["Well, he’s simply a genius of hypocrisy and resourcefulness in disarming the suspicions of the lawyers--so there’s nothing much to wonder at, I suppose!", "뭐, 그자는 그저 위선과, 변호사들의 의심을 따돌리는 임기응변의 천재인 거지, 그러니 별로 놀랄 것도 없는 셈이지!"], ["Of course people like that are always possible.", "물론 그런 자들은 언제든 있을 수 있어."], ["And the fact that he couldn’t keep up the character, but confessed, makes him easier to believe in.", "그리고 그자가 그 연기를 끝까지 유지하지 못하고 자백했다는 사실이, 오히려 그자를 믿기 쉽게 만들어."], ["But what a fool I was!", "그렇지만 내가 얼마나 바보였는지!"], ["I was frantic on their side!”", "나는 그자들 편을 들며 미쳐 날뛰었으니!\""]], [["“Tell me, please, from whom did you hear that, and why does it interest you so?”", "\"제발 말해 줘, 그 얘기를 누구한테서 들었고, 너는 왜 그렇게 그 일에 관심을 갖는 거야?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked with unmistakable agitation.", "라스콜니코프가 분명한 동요를 보이며 물었다."]], [["“What next?", "\"이건 또 뭐야?"], ["You ask me why it interests me!...", "내가 왜 그 일에 관심을 갖느냐고 묻다니!……"], ["Well, I heard it from Porfiry, among others...", "뭐, 다른 사람들 중에서도 포르피리한테서 들었어……"], ["It was from him I heard almost all about it.”", "거의 다 그 사람한테서 들은 거지.\""]], [["“From Porfiry?”", "\"포르피리한테서?\""]], [["“From Porfiry.”", "\"포르피리한테서.\""]], [["“What...", "\"뭐라고……"], ["what did he say?”", "그가 뭐라고 했어?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked in dismay.", "라스콜니코프가 경악하며 물었다."]], [["“He gave me a capital explanation of it.", "\"그 사람이 그 일을 아주 멋들어지게 설명해 주더군."], ["Psychologically, after his fashion.”", "심리학적으로, 자기 방식대로 말이야.\""]], [["“He explained it?", "\"그가 그걸 설명했다고?"], ["Explained it himself?”", "자기가 직접 설명했어?\""]], [["“Yes, yes; good-bye.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["I’ll tell you all about it another time, but now I’m busy.", "잘 있어. 다음에 그 얘길 다 해 줄게, 지금은 바쁘니까."], ["There was a time when I fancied...", "한때는 내가 상상하기를……"], ["But no matter, another time!...", "아니, 됐어, 다음에!……"], ["What need is there for me to drink now?", "이제 내가 술을 마실 필요가 어디 있겠어?"], ["You have made me drunk without wine.", "너는 술도 없이 나를 취하게 만들어 놨어."], ["I am drunk, Rodya!", "나는 취했어, 로쟈!"], ["Good-bye, I’m going.", "잘 있어, 나는 간다."], ["I’ll come again very soon.”", "아주 곧 다시 올게.\""]], [["He went out.", "그는 나갔다."]], [["“He’s a political conspirator, there’s not a doubt about it,”", "'그는 정치적 음모가야, 그건 의심할 여지가 없어.'"], ["Razumihin decided, as he slowly descended the stairs.", "라주미힌은 천천히 계단을 내려가며 단정했다."], ["“And he’s drawn his sister in; that’s quite, quite in keeping with Avdotya Romanovna’s character.", "'그리고 그는 누이까지 끌어들였어. 그건 정말이지, 아주 아브도티야 로마노브나의 성격과 들어맞아."], ["There are interviews between them!...", "두 사람 사이에 만남이 있는 거야!……"], ["She hinted at it too...", "누이도 그걸 넌지시 비쳤고……."], ["So many of her words....", "그 숱한 말들이……"], ["and hints...", "그리고 암시들이……"], ["bear that meaning!", "다 그런 뜻을 담고 있어!"], ["And how else can all this tangle be explained?", "그렇지 않고서야 이 모든 뒤엉킴을 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠어?"], ["Hm!", "흠!"], ["And I was almost thinking...", "그리고 나는 하마터면……"], ["Good heavens, what I thought!", "맙소사, 내가 무슨 생각을 했던 거야!"], ["Yes, I took leave of my senses and I wronged him!", "그래, 나는 제정신이 아니었고 그에게 못할 짓을 한 거야!"], ["It was his doing, under the lamp in the corridor that day.", "그건 그날 복도의 등불 아래서 그가 한 짓이었지."], ["Pfoo!", "푸!"], ["What a crude, nasty, vile idea on my part!", "내 딴에 얼마나 거칠고, 추잡하고, 비열한 생각이었는지!"], ["Nikolay is a brick, for confessing....", "니콜라이는 자백을 했으니, 멋진 녀석이야……."], ["And how clear it all is now!", "그리고 이제 모든 게 얼마나 분명해졌는지!"], ["His illness then, all his strange actions...", "그때 그의 병, 그 모든 이상한 행동들이……"], ["before this, in the university, how morose he used to be, how gloomy....", "이 일이 있기 전에, 대학에서, 그가 얼마나 침울했던지, 얼마나 우울했던지……."], ["But what’s the meaning now of that letter?", "그런데 그 편지는 이제 무슨 의미일까?"], ["There’s something in that, too, perhaps.", "거기에도 무언가가 있어, 어쩌면. 누구한테서 온 거지?"], ["Whom was it from?", "나는 의심스러워……!"], ["I suspect...! No, I must find out!”", "아니, 내가 알아내고 말 거야!'"]], [["He thought of Dounia, realising all he had heard and his heart throbbed, and he suddenly broke into a run.", "그는 두냐를 떠올렸고, 자신이 들은 모든 것을 깨달으며 심장이 두근거렸고, 갑자기 뛰기 시작했다."]], [["As soon as Razumihin went out, Raskolnikov got up, turned to the window, walked into one corner and then into another, as though forgetting the smallness of his room, and sat down again on the sofa.", "라주미힌이 나가자마자, 라스콜니코프는 일어나서 창가로 돌아섰다가, 자기 방이 얼마나 작은지도 잊은 듯 한쪽 구석으로 갔다가 또 다른 구석으로 갔다가, 다시 소파에 앉았다."], ["He felt, so to speak, renewed; again the struggle, so a means of escape had come.", "그는, 말하자면, 새로워진 기분이었다. 또다시 싸움이, 그러니까 빠져나갈 방도가 찾아온 것이다."]], [["“Yes, a means of escape had come!", "'그래, 빠져나갈 방도가 찾아왔어!"], ["It had been too stifling, too cramping, the burden had been too agonising.", "너무도 숨이 막혔고, 너무도 옥죄었고, 그 짐이 너무도 고통스러웠어."], ["A lethargy had come upon him at times.", "때때로 그에게는 무기력증이 찾아오곤 했다."], ["From the moment of the scene with Nikolay at Porfiry’s he had been suffocating, penned in without hope of escape.", "포르피리의 집에서 니콜라이와의 그 장면이 있은 순간부터, 그는 숨이 막혀, 빠져나갈 희망도 없이 갇혀 있었다."], ["After Nikolay’s confession, on that very day had come the scene with Sonia; his behaviour and his last words had been utterly unlike anything he could have imagined beforehand; he had grown feebler, instantly and fundamentally!", "니콜라이의 자백 뒤, 바로 그날 소냐와의 장면이 찾아왔다. 그의 행동과 그가 마지막에 한 말들은, 그가 미리 상상할 수 있었던 그 어떤 것과도 전혀 달랐다."], ["And he had agreed at the time with Sonia, he had agreed in his heart he could not go on living alone with such a thing on his mind!", "그는 즉각적으로, 또 근본적으로 더 무력해진 것이다! 그리고 그는 그때 소냐와 의견이 일치했고, 마음속으로 동의했으니, 이런 것을 마음에 품은 채로는 더 이상 혼자서 살아갈 수 없다는 데 동의한 것이다!"]], [["“And Svidrigaïlov was a riddle...", "'그리고 스비드리가일로프는 수수께끼였다……."], ["He worried him, that was true, but somehow not on the same point.", "그가 자신을 괴롭히는 것은 사실이었지만, 어쩐지 같은 지점에서는 아니었다."], ["He might still have a struggle to come with Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프와는 아직 다가올 싸움이 있을 수도 있었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov, too, might be a means of escape; but Porfiry was a different matter.", "스비드리가일로프 역시 빠져나갈 방도일 수도 있었다. 하지만 포르피리는 다른 문제였다."]], [["“And so Porfiry himself had explained it to Razumihin, had explained it _psychologically_.", "'그러니까 포르피리가 직접 라주미힌에게 그것을 설명한 것이고, 그것을 심리학적으로 설명한 것이다."], ["He had begun bringing in his damned psychology again!", "그가 또 그 빌어먹을 심리학을 끌어들이기 시작한 거야!"], ["Porfiry?", "포르피리가?"], ["But to think that Porfiry should for one moment believe that Nikolay was guilty, after what had passed between them before Nikolay’s appearance, after that tête-à-tête interview, which could have only _one_ explanation?", "하지만 니콜라이가 나타나기 전에 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일들이 있은 뒤에, 단 하나의 해석밖에는 있을 수 없었던 그 단둘이만의 만남이 있은 뒤에, 포르피리가 단 한순간이라도 니콜라이가 유죄라고 믿으리라고 생각하다니?"], ["(During those days Raskolnikov had often recalled passages in that scene with Porfiry; he could not bear to let his mind rest on it.) Such words, such gestures had passed between them, they had exchanged such glances, things had been said in such a tone and had reached such a pass, that Nikolay, whom Porfiry had seen through at the first word, at the first gesture, could not have shaken his conviction.", "(그 며칠 동안 라스콜니코프는 포르피리와의 그 장면의 대목들을 자주 떠올렸으나, 거기에 정신을 멈추고 머무를 수가 없었다.) 그런 말들이, 그런 몸짓들이 두 사람 사이에 오갔고, 그런 눈길을 주고받았고, 어떤 것들은 그런 어조로 말해졌으며, 사태가 그런 지경에까지 이르렀으니, 포르피리가 첫 마디에, 첫 몸짓에 이미 속을 꿰뚫어 본 니콜라이가, 그의 확신을 뒤흔들 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“And to think that even Razumihin had begun to suspect!", "'그리고 라주미힌마저 의심하기 시작했다니!"], ["The scene in the corridor under the lamp had produced its effect then.", "그렇다면 등불 아래 복도에서의 그 장면이 효과를 낸 것이다."], ["He had rushed to Porfiry....", "그는 포르피리에게 달려갔던 거고……."], ["But what had induced the latter to receive him like that?", "그런데 무엇이 그자로 하여금 그를 그런 식으로 맞이하게 했을까?"], ["What had been his object in putting Razumihin off with Nikolay?", "라주미힌을 니콜라이로 따돌리려 한 그자의 목적은 무엇이었을까?"], ["He must have some plan; there was some design, but what was it?", "그자에게는 분명 어떤 계획이 있고, 어떤 속셈이 있는데, 그게 무엇일까?"], ["It was true that a long time had passed since that morning--too long a time--and no sight nor sound of Porfiry.", "사실 그날 아침 이후로 오랜 시간이 흘렀고, 너무도 오랜 시간이 흘렀는데도, 포르피리의 모습도 소식도 없었다."], ["Well, that was a bad sign....”", "그래, 그건 나쁜 징조였다…….'"]], [["Raskolnikov took his cap and went out of the room, still pondering.", "라스콜니코프는 모자를 집어 들고, 여전히 곰곰이 생각하며 방을 나섰다."], ["It was the first time for a long while that he had felt clear in his mind, at least.", "적어도 정신만은 맑다고 느낀 것은 참으로 오랜만이었다. '스비드리가일로프를 처리해야 해.'"], ["“I must settle Svidrigaïlov,”", "그는 생각했다. '그것도 되도록 빨리."], ["he thought, “and as soon as possible; he, too, seems to be waiting for me to come to him of my own accord.”", "그자 역시 내가 제 발로 자기를 찾아오기를 기다리고 있는 것 같으니.'"], ["And at that moment there was such a rush of hate in his weary heart that he might have killed either of those two--Porfiry or Svidrigaïlov.", "그리고 그 순간 그의 지친 가슴에는 그런 증오가 솟구쳐 올라, 그는 그 두 사람 중 누구라도, 포르피리든 스비드리가일로프든 죽일 수도 있을 것 같았다."], ["At least he felt that he would be capable of doing it later, if not now.", "적어도 그는 지금은 아니더라도 나중에는 그것을 해낼 수 있으리라고 느꼈다."]], [["“We shall see, we shall see,”", "'두고 보자, 두고 보자.'"], ["he repeated to himself.", "그는 속으로 되뇌었다."]], [["But no sooner had he opened the door than he stumbled upon Porfiry himself in the passage.", "그러나 그가 문을 열기가 무섭게, 그는 통로에서 다름 아닌 포르피리와 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["He was coming in to see him.", "그는 그를 만나러 들어오는 참이었다."], ["Raskolnikov was dumbfounded for a minute, but only for one minute.", "라스콜니코프는 잠시 어안이 벙벙했으나, 단지 잠시뿐이었다."], ["Strange to say, he was not very much astonished at seeing Porfiry and scarcely afraid of him.", "이상하게도, 그는 포르피리를 보고 그다지 크게 놀라지 않았고 그가 거의 두렵지도 않았다."], ["He was simply startled, but was quickly, instantly, on his guard.", "그는 그저 흠칫했을 뿐, 곧, 즉각적으로 경계 태세를 갖추었다."], ["“Perhaps this will mean the end?", "'어쩌면 이것이 끝을 의미하는 걸까?"], ["But how could Porfiry have approached so quietly, like a cat, so that he had heard nothing?", "그런데 어떻게 포르피리가 이렇게 고양이처럼 조용히 다가와서, 내가 아무 소리도 못 들었단 말인가?"], ["Could he have been listening at the door?”", "그가 문가에서 엿듣고 있었던 걸까?'"]], [["“You didn’t expect a visitor, Rodion Romanovitch,”", "\"방문객은 기대하지 않으셨겠지요, 로지온 로마노비치.\""], ["Porfiry explained, laughing.", "포르피리가 웃으며 설명했다."], ["“I’ve been meaning to look in a long time; I was passing by and thought why not go in for five minutes.", "\"오래전부터 들러 봐야겠다고 생각하고 있었습니다. 지나가던 길에, 잠깐 오 분쯤 들어가 보면 어떨까 했지요."], ["Are you going out?", "나가시는 길입니까?"], ["I won’t keep you long.", "오래 붙들지는 않겠습니다."], ["Just let me have one cigarette.”", "그저 담배 한 대만 피우게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Sit down, Porfiry Petrovitch, sit down.”", "\"앉으시지요, 포르피리 페트로비치, 앉으세요.\""], ["Raskolnikov gave his visitor a seat with so pleased and friendly an expression that he would have marvelled at himself, if he could have seen it.", "라스콜니코프는 손님에게 자리를 권했는데, 그 표정이 어찌나 기쁘고 다정해 보였던지, 만일 자기 모습을 볼 수 있었다면 스스로도 놀라워했을 것이다."]], [["The last moment had come, the last drops had to be drained!", "마지막 순간이 온 것이다, 마지막 한 방울까지 다 마셔 버려야만 했다!"], ["So a man will sometimes go through half an hour of mortal terror with a brigand, yet when the knife is at his throat at last, he feels no fear.", "이렇게 사람은 때때로 강도와 함께 죽음의 공포 속에서 삼십 분을 보내고도, 마침내 칼이 목에 들이대어지면 아무런 두려움도 느끼지 않는 법이다."]], [["Raskolnikov seated himself directly facing Porfiry, and looked at him without flinching.", "라스콜니코프는 포르피리와 정면으로 마주하고 앉아, 움찔하지도 않고 그를 바라보았다."], ["Porfiry screwed up his eyes and began lighting a cigarette.", "포르피리는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 담배에 불을 붙이기 시작했다."]], [["“Speak, speak,”", "'말해, 말해 봐.'"], ["seemed as though it would burst from Raskolnikov’s heart.", "라스콜니코프의 가슴에서 터져 나올 듯했다."], ["“Come, why don’t you speak?”", "'자, 왜 말을 안 하는 거지?'"]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["“Ah these cigarettes!”", "“아, 이 담배라니!”"], ["Porfiry Petrovitch ejaculated at last, having lighted one.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 마침내 한 개비에 불을 붙이고는 내뱉었다."], ["“They are pernicious, positively pernicious, and yet I can’t give them up!", "“해로워요, 정말이지 해롭다니까요, 그런데도 끊을 수가 없으니!"], ["I cough, I begin to have tickling in my throat and a difficulty in breathing.", "기침을 하고, 목이 간질거리기 시작하고, 숨쉬기가 곤란해지지요."], ["You know I am a coward, I went lately to Dr. B----n; he always gives at least half an hour to each patient.", "나는 겁쟁이라서, 얼마 전에 B 박사를 찾아갔는데, 그 양반은 환자 한 사람한테 늘 적어도 삼십 분은 할애하지요."], ["He positively laughed looking at me; he sounded me: ‘Tobacco’s bad for you,’ he said, ‘your lungs are affected.’ But how am I to give it up?", "나를 보더니 그냥 웃더군요. 진찰을 하고는, ‘담배는 당신한테 나쁩니다’ 하고 말합디다, ‘폐가 상했어요.’ 하지만 내가 어떻게 끊겠습니까?"], ["What is there to take its place?", "무엇으로 그걸 대신하겠어요?"], ["I don’t drink, that’s the mischief, he-he-he, that I don’t.", "술도 안 마시는데, 그게 문제지요, 헤헤헤, 술을 안 마시니."], ["Everything is relative, Rodion Romanovitch, everything is relative!”", "모든 것은 상대적이지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 모든 게 상대적이라고요!”"]], [["“Why, he’s playing his professional tricks again,” Raskolnikov thought with disgust.", "‘저자가 또 직업적인 수작을 부리는군.’ 라스콜니코프는 혐오스러운 마음으로 생각했다."], ["All the circumstances of their last interview suddenly came back to him, and he felt a rush of the feeling that had come upon him then.", "지난번 만남의 모든 정황이 불현듯 그의 머릿속에 되살아났고, 그때 자신을 덮쳤던 그 감정이 다시 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I came to see you the day before yesterday, in the evening; you didn’t know?”", "“그저께 저녁에 댁을 찾아왔었지요, 모르셨나요?”"], ["Porfiry Petrovitch went on, looking round the room.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 방 안을 둘러보며 말을 이었다."], ["“I came into this very room.", "“바로 이 방으로 들어왔답니다."], ["I was passing by, just as I did to-day, and I thought I’d return your call.", "마침 지나가는 길에, 오늘처럼 말이지요, 답방이나 해야겠다고 생각했지요."], ["I walked in as your door was wide open, I looked round, waited and went out without leaving my name with your servant.", "댁의 문이 활짝 열려 있길래 들어와서, 둘러보고, 기다리다가, 댁의 하녀한테 이름도 남기지 않고 나갔답니다."], ["Don’t you lock your door?”", "문을 안 잠그시나요?”"]], [["Raskolnikov’s face grew more and more gloomy.", "라스콜니코프의 얼굴이 점점 더 어두워졌다."], ["Porfiry seemed to guess his state of mind.", "포르피리는 그의 심중을 알아챈 듯했다."]], [["“I’ve come to have it out with you, Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow!", "“그동안 쌓인 걸 댁과 분명히 매듭짓고 싶어서 왔습니다, 로디온 로마노비치, 친애하는 양반!"], ["I owe you an explanation and must give it to you,”", "나는 댁에게 해명할 빚이 있고, 그걸 해야만 합니다.”"], ["he continued with a slight smile, just patting Raskolnikov’s knee.", "그는 가벼운 미소를 지으며 라스콜니코프의 무릎을 살짝 두드리고는 말을 이었다."]], [["But almost at the same instant a serious and careworn look came into his face; to his surprise Raskolnikov saw a touch of sadness in it.", "그러나 거의 같은 순간 진지하고 근심스러운 빛이 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐다. 놀랍게도 라스콜니코프는 거기서 한 줄기 슬픔의 기색을 보았다."], ["He had never seen and never suspected such an expression in his face.", "그는 그의 얼굴에서 그런 표정을 한 번도 본 적이 없었고, 짐작조차 한 적이 없었다."]], [["“A strange scene passed between us last time we met, Rodion Romanovitch.", "“지난번에 우리가 만났을 때 이상한 장면이 있었지요, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["Our first interview, too, was a strange one; but then...", "우리의 첫 만남도 이상한 것이었고요. 한데 그때는……"], ["and one thing after another!", "그리고 일이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고!"], ["This is the point: I have perhaps acted unfairly to you; I feel it.", "요점은 이겁니다. 어쩌면 내가 댁에게 부당하게 굴었는지도 모르겠어요."], ["Do you remember how we parted?", "그게 느껴집니다."], ["Your nerves were unhinged and your knees were shaking and so were mine.", "우리가 어떻게 헤어졌는지 기억하시나요? 댁은 신경이 곤두서 무릎이 떨렸고, 나도 마찬가지였지요."], ["And, you know, our behaviour was unseemly, even ungentlemanly.", "한데 말입니다, 우리의 행동은 꼴사나웠어요, 신사답지조차 못했지요."], ["And yet we are gentlemen, above all, in any case, gentlemen; that must be understood.", "그런데도 우리는 신사이고, 무엇보다도, 어떤 경우든, 신사입니다. 그건 알아 둬야 해요."], ["Do you remember what we came to?...", "우리가 어디까지 갔는지 기억하시나요?……"], ["and it was quite indecorous.”", "정말이지 꼴불견이었지요.”"]], [["“What is he up to, what does he take me for?” Raskolnikov asked himself in amazement, raising his head and looking with open eyes on Porfiry.", "‘저자가 무슨 수작이지, 나를 뭘로 보는 거야?’ 라스콜니코프는 어리둥절하여 스스로에게 물으며, 고개를 들어 눈을 크게 뜨고 포르피리를 바라보았다."]], [["“I’ve decided openness is better between us,”", "“우리 사이엔 솔직한 게 낫겠다고 결론을 내렸지요.”"], ["Porfiry Petrovitch went on, turning his head away and dropping his eyes, as though unwilling to disconcert his former victim and as though disdaining his former wiles.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 고개를 옆으로 돌리고 눈을 내리깔며 말을 이었는데, 마치 자신의 옛 먹잇감을 당황하게 하고 싶지 않다는 듯, 또 자신의 옛 술수를 경멸한다는 듯한 태도였다."], ["“Yes, such suspicions and such scenes cannot continue for long.", "“그래요, 그런 의심과 그런 장면들이 오래 계속될 수는 없는 노릇이지요."], ["Nikolay put a stop to it, or I don’t know what we might not have come to.", "니콜라이가 거기에 종지부를 찍어 줬으니 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 우리가 어디까지 갔을지 모르겠어요."], ["That damned workman was sitting at the time in the next room--can you realise that?", "그 망할 직공이 그때 옆방에 앉아 있었답니다, 상상이 되십니까?"], ["You know that, of course; and I am aware that he came to you afterwards.", "댁도 물론 알고 계시지요. 그가 나중에 댁을 찾아갔다는 것도 압니다."], ["But what you supposed then was not true: I had not sent for anyone, I had made no kind of arrangements.", "하지만 그때 댁이 짐작한 건 사실이 아니었어요. 나는 아무도 부르지 않았고, 어떤 준비도 해 놓지 않았답니다."], ["You ask why I hadn’t?", "왜 안 그랬느냐고 물으시겠지요?"], ["What shall I say to you?", "뭐라고 말씀드려야 할까요?"], ["it had all come upon me so suddenly.", "모든 게 너무도 갑작스럽게 닥쳤거든요."], ["I had scarcely sent for the porters (you noticed them as you went out, I dare say).", "나는 가까스로 문지기들을 부른 정도였지요(나가실 때 그들을 보셨을 겁니다, 아마도)."], ["An idea flashed upon me; I was firmly convinced at the time, you see, Rodion Romanovitch.", "한 가지 생각이 번뜩 스쳤어요. 그때 나는 확신하고 있었거든요, 아시겠습니까, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["Come, I thought--even if I let one thing slip for a time, I shall get hold of something else--I shan’t lose what I want, anyway.", "자, 생각했지요, 비록 한동안 한 가지를 놓치더라도, 다른 무언가는 붙잡게 될 거다, 어쨌든 내가 원하는 건 놓치지 않으리라."], ["You are nervously irritable, Rodion Romanovitch, by temperament; it’s out of proportion with other qualities of your heart and character, which I flatter myself I have to some extent divined.", "댁은 신경질적으로 예민한 사람이지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 천성이 그래요. 그게 댁의 마음과 성격의 다른 자질들과는 균형이 안 맞는데, 그 자질들을 나는 어느 정도 꿰뚫어 봤다고 자부합니다."], ["Of course I did reflect even then that it does not always happen that a man gets up and blurts out his whole story.", "물론 그때도 나는 이렇게 생각했지요, 사람이란 게 항상 벌떡 일어나 제 이야기를 죄다 불쑥 털어놓는 건 아니라고."], ["It does happen sometimes, if you make a man lose all patience, though even then it’s rare.", "그런 일이 가끔 있긴 하지요, 사람을 인내심의 끝까지 몰아붙이면 말입니다, 하지만 그래도 드문 일이에요."], ["I was capable of realising that.", "나는 그걸 알아챌 수 있었지요."], ["If I only had a fact, I thought, the least little fact to go upon, something I could lay hold of, something tangible, not merely psychological.", "단 하나의 사실이라도, 발 디딜 아주 작은 사실 하나라도 있으면, 단지 심리적인 게 아니라 붙잡을 수 있는, 손에 잡히는 무언가가 있으면, 하고 생각했답니다."], ["For if a man is guilty, you must be able to get something substantial out of him; one may reckon upon most surprising results indeed.", "사람이 죄가 있으면, 거기서 뭔가 실질적인 걸 끌어낼 수 있는 법이니까요. 정말이지 가장 놀라운 결과들을 기대해 볼 만하지요."], ["I was reckoning on your temperament, Rodion Romanovitch, on your temperament above all things!", "나는 댁의 기질에 기대를 걸고 있었어요, 로디온 로마노비치, 무엇보다도 댁의 기질에!"], ["I had great hopes of you at that time.”", "그때 나는 댁에게 큰 기대를 품고 있었답니다.”"]], [["“But what are you driving at now?”", "“그런데 지금 무슨 말을 하려는 겁니까?”"], ["Raskolnikov muttered at last, asking the question without thinking.", "라스콜니코프가 마침내 중얼거렸는데, 생각도 없이 던진 물음이었다."]], [["“What is he talking about?”", "‘저자가 무슨 소리를 하는 거지?’ 그는 정신없이 의아해했다."], ["he wondered distractedly, “does he really take me to be innocent?”", "‘정말로 나를 결백하다고 여기는 건가?’"]], [["“What am I driving at?", "“무슨 말을 하려느냐고요?"], ["I’ve come to explain myself, I consider it my duty, so to speak.", "나 자신을 해명하러 왔습니다, 그게 내 의무라고 여기거든요, 말하자면 말이지요."], ["I want to make clear to you how the whole business, the whole misunderstanding arose.", "이 모든 일이, 이 모든 오해가 어떻게 생겨났는지 댁에게 분명히 밝히고 싶어요."], ["I’ve caused you a great deal of suffering, Rodion Romanovitch.", "나는 댁에게 큰 고통을 안겼지요, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["I am not a monster.", "나는 괴물이 아닙니다."], ["I understand what it must mean for a man who has been unfortunate, but who is proud, imperious and above all, impatient, to have to bear such treatment!", "불행을 겪었으되 자존심 강하고, 위압적이며, 무엇보다 참을성 없는 사람이 그런 취급을 견뎌야 한다는 게 어떤 의미인지 이해해요!"], ["I regard you in any case as a man of noble character and not without elements of magnanimity, though I don’t agree with all your convictions.", "나는 어떤 경우든 댁을 고결한 성품을 지닌, 관대함의 요소도 없지 않은 사람으로 봅니다, 비록 댁의 모든 신념에 동의하는 건 아니지만요."], ["I wanted to tell you this first, frankly and quite sincerely, for above all I don’t want to deceive you.", "이걸 먼저 말씀드리고 싶었어요, 솔직하게, 아주 진심으로 말입니다, 무엇보다 댁을 속이고 싶지 않으니까요."], ["When I made your acquaintance, I felt attracted by you.", "댁과 알게 됐을 때, 나는 댁에게 끌렸답니다."], ["Perhaps you will laugh at my saying so.", "어쩌면 내가 이런 말을 하는 걸 비웃으실지도 모르지요."], ["You have a right to.", "그럴 권리가 있어요."], ["I know you disliked me from the first and indeed you’ve no reason to like me.", "댁이 처음부터 나를 싫어했다는 걸 압니다, 사실 나를 좋아할 이유도 없지요."], ["You may think what you like, but I desire now to do all I can to efface that impression and to show that I am a man of heart and conscience.", "무엇을 생각하시든 좋습니다만, 나는 이제 그 인상을 지우기 위해, 그리고 내가 마음과 양심을 지닌 사람임을 보여 주기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 걸 하고 싶어요."], ["I speak sincerely.”", "진심으로 하는 말입니다.”"]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch made a dignified pause.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 위엄 있게 잠시 말을 멈췄다."], ["Raskolnikov felt a rush of renewed alarm.", "라스콜니코프는 새로운 경계심이 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The thought that Porfiry believed him to be innocent began to make him uneasy.", "포르피리가 자신을 결백하다고 믿는다는 생각이 그를 불안하게 하기 시작했다."]], [["“It’s scarcely necessary to go over everything in detail,”", "“모든 걸 세세히 짚어 볼 필요는 거의 없겠지요.”"], ["Porfiry Petrovitch went on.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 말을 이었다."], ["“Indeed, I could scarcely attempt it.", "“사실 그러려고 시도조차 못 할 겁니다."], ["To begin with there were rumours.", "우선 소문들이 있었어요."], ["Through whom, how, and when those rumours came to me...", "누구를 통해, 어떻게, 언제 그 소문들이 내게 들어왔는지……"], ["and how they affected you, I need not go into.", "그리고 그것들이 댁에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지는 굳이 들어갈 필요가 없겠지요."], ["My suspicions were aroused by a complete accident, which might just as easily not have happened.", "내 의심을 불러일으킨 건 순전한 우연이었는데, 그건 그만큼 쉽게 일어나지 않았을 수도 있는 일이었어요."], ["What was it?", "그게 뭐였느냐고요?"], ["Hm!", "흠!"], ["I believe there is no need to go into that either.", "그것도 들어갈 필요는 없을 것 같군요."], ["Those rumours and that accident led to one idea in my mind.", "그 소문들과 그 우연이 내 머릿속에서 한 가지 생각으로 이어졌지요."], ["I admit it openly--for one may as well make a clean breast of it--I was the first to pitch on you.", "솔직히 인정하겠습니다 — 차라리 깨끗이 털어놓는 게 나을 테니 — 댁을 처음 점찍은 게 바로 나였어요."], ["The old woman’s notes on the pledges and the rest of it--that all came to nothing.", "노파가 저당물에 적어 놓은 메모니 뭐니 하는 것들 — 그건 다 아무 소용이 없었지요."], ["Yours was one of a hundred.", "댁은 수백 명 중 하나일 뿐이었어요."], ["I happened, too, to hear of the scene at the office, from a man who described it capitally, unconsciously reproducing the scene with great vividness.", "게다가 우연히 그 관청에서의 장면도 들었지요, 그걸 기막히게 묘사해 준 사람한테서 말입니다, 자기도 모르게 그 장면을 아주 생생하게 재현하더군요."], ["It was just one thing after another, Rodion Romanovitch, my dear fellow!", "그저 일이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물었지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 친애하는 양반!"], ["How could I avoid being brought to certain ideas?", "어떻게 내가 어떤 생각에 이르지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["From a hundred rabbits you can’t make a horse, a hundred suspicions don’t make a proof, as the English proverb says, but that’s only from the rational point of view--you can’t help being partial, for after all a lawyer is only human.", "토끼 백 마리로 말 한 마리를 만들 수 없고, 의심 백 개로 증거가 되지 않는다고, 영국 속담에도 있지요, 하지만 그건 이성적인 관점에서만 그런 겁니다 — 편견을 안 가질 수 없거든요, 따지고 보면 법률가도 그저 인간일 뿐이니까요."], ["I thought, too, of your article in that journal, do you remember, on your first visit we talked of it?", "그 잡지에 실린 댁의 논문도 생각났지요, 기억하시나요, 첫 방문 때 우리가 그 얘기를 했지요?"], ["I jeered at you at the time, but that was only to lead you on.", "그때 나는 댁을 비웃었지만, 그건 그저 댁을 끌어내기 위해서였어요."], ["I repeat, Rodion Romanovitch, you are ill and impatient.", "다시 말하지만, 로디온 로마노비치, 댁은 병들었고 참을성이 없어요."], ["That you were bold, headstrong, in earnest and...", "댁이 대담하고, 고집스럽고, 진지하며……"], ["had felt a great deal I recognised long before.", "많은 것을 느껴 왔다는 건 나도 오래전부터 알아봤지요."], ["I, too, have felt the same, so that your article seemed familiar to me.", "나 역시 똑같은 걸 느껴 본 적이 있어서, 댁의 논문이 내게 익숙하게 다가왔답니다."], ["It was conceived on sleepless nights, with a throbbing heart, in ecstasy and suppressed enthusiasm.", "그건 잠 못 이루는 밤들에, 두근거리는 가슴으로, 황홀경과 억눌린 열정 속에서 잉태된 것이지요."], ["And that proud suppressed enthusiasm in young people is dangerous!", "그리고 젊은 사람들의 그 오만하게 억눌린 열정은 위험합니다!"], ["I jeered at you then, but let me tell you that, as a literary amateur, I am awfully fond of such first essays, full of the heat of youth.", "그때 나는 댁을 비웃었지만, 한마디 하지요, 문학 애호가로서 나는 그런 첫 시도들을, 젊음의 열기로 가득한 것들을 끔찍이도 좋아합니다."], ["There is a mistiness and a chord vibrating in the mist.", "거기엔 안개 같은 것이, 그리고 그 안개 속에서 떨리는 한 가닥 현이 있지요."], ["Your article is absurd and fantastic, but there’s a transparent sincerity, a youthful incorruptible pride and the daring of despair in it.", "댁의 논문은 터무니없고 환상적이지만, 거기엔 투명한 진실함이, 젊은이의 타락하지 않은 자존심과 절망의 대담함이 있어요."], ["It’s a gloomy article, but that’s what’s fine in it.", "음울한 논문이지만, 바로 그게 그 글의 훌륭한 점이지요."], ["I read your article and put it aside, thinking as I did so ‘that man won’t go the common way.’ Well, I ask you, after that as a preliminary, how could I help being carried away by what followed?", "나는 댁의 논문을 읽고 한쪽에 치워 두면서, 이렇게 생각했답니다, ‘이 사람은 흔한 길로는 가지 않겠군.’ 자, 묻겠습니다만, 그렇게 시작된 뒤에, 내가 그다음에 일어난 일에 휩쓸리지 않을 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["Oh, dear, I am not saying anything, I am not making any statement now.", "오, 이런, 나는 아무 말도 하는 게 아닙니다, 지금 무슨 진술을 하는 게 아니에요."], ["I simply noted it at the time.", "그저 그때 그렇게 적어 뒀을 뿐이지요."], ["What is there in it?", "거기에 뭐가 있느냐고요?"], ["I reflected.", "나는 곰곰이 생각했어요."], ["There’s nothing in it, that is really nothing and perhaps absolutely nothing.", "거기엔 아무것도 없다, 정말 아무것도 없고 어쩌면 절대로 아무것도 없는 거다."], ["And it’s not at all the thing for the prosecutor to let himself be carried away by notions: here I have Nikolay on my hands with actual evidence against him--you may think what you like of it, but it’s evidence.", "그리고 검사가 그런 관념들에 휩쓸리게 두는 건 전혀 온당치 못한 일이지요. 여기 내 손에는 니콜라이가, 그것도 그에게 불리한 실제 증거를 들고 있단 말입니다 — 그걸 어떻게 생각하시든 좋지만, 어쨌든 증거예요."], ["He brings in his psychology, too; one has to consider him, too, for it’s a matter of life and death.", "그자도 자기 나름의 심리를 들이밀지요. 그자도 고려해야 합니다, 목숨이 걸린 문제니까요."], ["Why am I explaining this to you?", "내가 왜 이걸 댁에게 설명하느냐고요?"], ["That you may understand, and not blame my malicious behaviour on that occasion.", "댁이 이해하고, 그때 내 짓궂은 행동을 탓하지 않도록 하기 위해서지요."], ["It was not malicious, I assure you, he-he!", "악의가 있었던 게 아니에요, 정말입니다, 헤헤!"], ["Do you suppose I didn’t come to search your room at the time?", "내가 그때 댁의 방을 수색하러 오지 않았다고 생각하십니까?"], ["I did, I did, he-he!", "했지요, 했어요, 헤헤!"], ["I was here when you were lying ill in bed, not officially, not in my own person, but I was here.", "댁이 앓아누워 있을 때 내가 여기 있었답니다, 공식적으로는 아니고, 내 신분으로 온 건 아니지만, 여기 있었어요."], ["Your room was searched to the last thread at the first suspicion; but _umsonst_!", "첫 의심이 들었을 때 댁의 방은 실오라기 하나까지 샅샅이 수색당했지요. 하지만 움존스트(헛수고)였어요!"], ["I thought to myself, now that man will come, will come of himself and quickly, too; if he’s guilty, he’s sure to come.", "나는 속으로 생각했지요, 이제 저 사람이 올 거다, 제 발로, 그것도 빨리 올 거다, 죄가 있다면 틀림없이 올 거야."], ["Another man wouldn’t, but he will.", "다른 사람이라면 안 오겠지만, 이 사람은 올 거다."], ["And you remember how Mr. Razumihin began discussing the subject with you?", "그리고 라주미힌 씨가 어떻게 댁과 그 문제를 논하기 시작했는지 기억하시지요?"], ["We arranged that to excite you, so we purposely spread rumours, that he might discuss the case with you, and Razumihin is not a man to restrain his indignation. Mr.", "댁을 자극하려고 우리가 꾸민 일이었답니다, 그래서 일부러 소문을 퍼뜨렸지요, 그가 댁과 그 사건을 논하도록 말입니다, 라주미힌은 자기 분노를 억누를 만한 사람이 아니니까요."], ["Zametov was tremendously struck by your anger and your open daring.", "자메토프 씨는 댁의 분노와 노골적인 대담함에 엄청나게 놀랐답니다."], ["Think of blurting out in a restaurant ‘I killed her.’ It was too daring, too reckless.", "식당에서 ‘내가 그 여자를 죽였다’고 불쑥 내뱉다니요. 너무 대담했어요, 너무 무모했지요."], ["I thought so myself, if he is guilty he will be a formidable opponent.", "나 자신도 그렇게 생각했답니다, 만일 저자가 죄가 있다면 만만찮은 적수가 되겠구나."], ["That was what I thought at the time.", "그게 그때 내 생각이었어요."], ["I was expecting you.", "나는 댁을 기다리고 있었지요."], ["But you simply bowled Zametov over and...", "그런데 댁은 그냥 자메토프를 나가떨어지게 만들고……"], ["well, you see, it all lies in this--that this damnable psychology can be taken two ways!", "자, 보세요, 모든 게 여기에 있는 겁니다 — 그 빌어먹을 심리라는 게 두 가지로 해석될 수 있다는 거지요!"], ["Well, I kept expecting you, and so it was, you came!", "자, 나는 줄곧 댁을 기다리고 있었고, 과연 그대로, 댁이 왔어요!"], ["My heart was fairly throbbing.", "내 심장이 정말이지 두근거렸답니다."], ["Ach!", "아!"]], [["“Now, why need you have come?", "“자, 댁은 도대체 왜 와야 했던 걸까요?"], ["Your laughter, too, as you came in, do you remember?", "들어오면서 짓던 그 웃음도, 기억하시나요?"], ["I saw it all plain as daylight, but if I hadn’t expected you so specially, I should not have noticed anything in your laughter.", "나는 그 모든 걸 대낮처럼 환히 봤답니다, 하지만 내가 그렇게 특별히 댁을 기다리지 않았더라면, 그 웃음에서 아무것도 알아채지 못했을 거예요."], ["You see what influence a mood has! Mr.", "기분이라는 게 어떤 영향을 미치는지 아시겠지요!"], ["Razumihin then--ah, that stone, that stone under which the things were hidden!", "그때 라주미힌 씨가 — 아, 그 돌, 물건들을 숨겨 둔 그 돌 말입니다!"], ["I seem to see it somewhere in a kitchen garden.", "어딘가 채마밭에 있는 것 같던데요."], ["It was in a kitchen garden, you told Zametov and afterwards you repeated that in my office?", "채마밭에 있었지요, 댁이 자메토프한테 그렇게 말하고 나서 내 사무실에서 그 말을 또 했지요?"], ["And when we began picking your article to pieces, how you explained it!", "그리고 우리가 댁의 논문을 조목조목 따지기 시작했을 때, 댁이 그걸 어떻게 설명했는지!"], ["One could take every word of yours in two senses, as though there were another meaning hidden.", "댁의 말은 한마디 한마디가 두 가지 뜻으로 받아들여질 수 있었어요, 마치 또 다른 의미가 숨어 있는 것처럼 말이지요."]], [["“So in this way, Rodion Romanovitch, I reached the furthest limit, and knocking my head against a post, I pulled myself up, asking myself what I was about.", "“그래서 이런 식으로, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 막다른 한계까지 다다랐고, 기둥에 머리를 부딪치고 나서야 정신을 차리며, 내가 지금 뭘 하고 있는 건가 스스로에게 물었지요."], ["After all, I said, you can take it all in another sense if you like, and it’s more natural so, indeed.", "결국, 하고 나는 말했어요, 원한다면 이 모든 걸 다른 뜻으로 받아들일 수도 있다, 사실 그게 더 자연스럽기까지 하다."], ["I couldn’t help admitting it was more natural.", "그게 더 자연스럽다는 걸 인정하지 않을 수 없었지요."], ["I was bothered!", "나는 골치가 아팠어요!"], ["‘No, I’d better get hold of some little fact’ I said.", "‘안 되겠다, 작은 사실이라도 하나 붙잡는 게 낫겠다’ 하고 나는 말했지요."], ["So when I heard of the bell-ringing, I held my breath and was all in a tremor.", "그래서 그 초인종 소동 얘기를 들었을 때, 나는 숨을 죽이고 온몸을 떨었답니다."], ["‘Here is my little fact,’ thought I, and I didn’t think it over, I simply wouldn’t.", "‘여기 내 작은 사실이 있다’ 하고 생각했고, 더는 곰곰이 따져 보지도 않았어요, 그냥 그러기가 싫었지요."], ["I would have given a thousand roubles at that minute to have seen you with my own eyes, when you walked a hundred paces beside that workman, after he had called you murderer to your face, and you did not dare to ask him a question all the way.", "그 순간 나는 천 루블이라도 내놨을 겁니다, 댁을 내 두 눈으로 직접 봤더라면 말입니다, 그 직공이 댁의 면전에 대고 살인자라고 한 뒤에 댁이 그자 곁에서 백 걸음이나 걸으면서, 가는 내내 감히 그자에게 한마디도 묻지 못했던 그 모습을요."], ["And then what about your trembling, what about your bell-ringing in your illness, in semi-delirium?", "그리고 또 댁의 그 떨림은 어떻고, 병중에, 반쯤 헛소리를 하며 초인종을 울려 댄 건 또 어떻고요?"]], [["“And so, Rodion Romanovitch, can you wonder that I played such pranks on you?", "“그러니, 로디온 로마노비치, 내가 댁에게 그런 장난을 친 게 뭐가 이상하겠습니까?"], ["And what made you come at that very minute?", "그런데 도대체 무엇 때문에 댁은 바로 그 순간에 온 걸까요?"], ["Someone seemed to have sent you, by Jove!", "누군가가 댁을 보낸 것만 같았지요, 정말이지!"], ["And if Nikolay had not parted us...", "그리고 니콜라이가 우리를 갈라놓지 않았더라면……"], ["and do you remember Nikolay at the time?", "그때 니콜라이를 기억하시나요?"], ["Do you remember him clearly?", "똑똑히 기억하십니까?"], ["It was a thunderbolt, a regular thunderbolt!", "그건 청천벽력이었어요, 그야말로 청천벽력이었지요!"], ["And how I met him!", "그리고 내가 그걸 어떻게 맞이했던가요!"], ["I didn’t believe in the thunderbolt, not for a minute.", "나는 그 벽력을 믿지 않았답니다, 한순간도요."], ["You could see it for yourself; and how could I?", "댁도 직접 봤을 겁니다. 그러니 내가 어떻게 믿겠어요?"], ["Even afterwards, when you had gone and he began making very, very plausible answers on certain points, so that I was surprised at him myself, even then I didn’t believe his story!", "나중에도, 댁이 가고 나서 그자가 어떤 점들에 대해 아주, 아주 그럴듯한 대답을 하기 시작해서 나 자신도 그자에게 놀랐을 때조차, 그때도 나는 그자의 이야기를 믿지 않았어요!"], ["You see what it is to be as firm as a rock!", "바위처럼 굳건하다는 게 어떤 건지 아시겠지요!"], ["No, thought I, _Morgenfrüh_.", "안 되겠다, 하고 나는 생각했지요, 모르겐프뤼(천만에)!"], ["What has Nikolay got to do with it!”", "니콜라이가 이 일과 무슨 상관이람!”"]], [["“Razumihin told me just now that you think Nikolay guilty and had yourself assured him of it....”", "“라주미힌이 방금 말해 줬어요, 당신은 니콜라이가 죄가 있다고 여기며, 당신 입으로 그에게 그렇게 장담했다고……”"]], [["His voice failed him, and he broke off.", "그의 목소리가 끊기며 말을 멈췄다."], ["He had been listening in indescribable agitation, as this man who had seen through and through him, went back upon himself.", "자신을 속속들이 꿰뚫어 보았던 이 사내가 다시 제 말을 뒤집는 동안, 그는 형언할 수 없는 동요 속에서 듣고 있었던 것이다."], ["He was afraid of believing it and did not believe it.", "그는 그것을 믿기가 두려웠고, 또 믿지 않았다."], ["In those still ambiguous words he kept eagerly looking for something more definite and conclusive.", "여전히 애매한 그 말들 속에서 그는 무언가 더 분명하고 결정적인 것을 애타게 찾고 있었다."]], [["“Mr. Razumihin!”", "“라주미힌 씨라!”"], ["cried Porfiry Petrovitch, seeming glad of a question from Raskolnikov, who had till then been silent.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 외쳤는데, 그때까지 입을 다물고 있던 라스콜니코프의 질문이 반가운 듯했다. “헤헤헤!"], ["“He-he-he! But I had to put Mr.", "하지만 나는 라주미힌 씨를 떼어 놓아야 했지요."], ["Razumihin off; two is company, three is none.", "둘이면 좋은 짝이지만 셋이면 군더더기니까요."], ["Mr. Razumihin is not the right man, besides he is an outsider.", "라주미힌 씨는 알맞은 사람이 아닐뿐더러, 외부인이지요."], ["He came running to me with a pale face....", "그가 새파랗게 질린 얼굴로 내게 달려왔답니다……"], ["But never mind him, why bring him in?", "하지만 그 사람은 됐고, 왜 그를 끌어들이겠습니까?"], ["To return to Nikolay, would you like to know what sort of a type he is, how I understand him, that is?", "니콜라이 얘기로 돌아가서, 그자가 어떤 부류인지, 내가 그자를 어떻게 이해하는지, 그러니까 알고 싶으십니까?"], ["To begin with, he is still a child and not exactly a coward, but something by way of an artist.", "우선, 그자는 아직 어린애이고 딱히 겁쟁이는 아니지만, 어딘가 예술가 같은 데가 있어요."], ["Really, don’t laugh at my describing him so.", "정말이지, 내가 그자를 이렇게 묘사한다고 비웃지 마세요."], ["He is innocent and responsive to influence.", "그자는 순진하고 남의 영향을 잘 받지요."], ["He has a heart, and is a fantastic fellow.", "마음씨가 있고, 환상적인 친구예요."], ["He sings and dances, he tells stories, they say, so that people come from other villages to hear him.", "노래하고 춤추고, 이야기를 어찌나 잘하는지, 듣자 하니 다른 마을에서까지 사람들이 들으러 온다더군요."], ["He attends school too, and laughs till he cries if you hold up a finger to him; he will drink himself senseless--not as a regular vice, but at times, when people treat him, like a child.", "학교도 다니는데, 손가락 하나만 들어 올려도 눈물이 날 때까지 웃지요. 정신을 잃을 때까지 마시기도 하는데 — 고질적인 악습은 아니고, 가끔, 사람들이 한턱낼 때, 어린애처럼 말입니다."], ["And he stole, too, then, without knowing it himself, for ‘How can it be stealing, if one picks it up?’ And do you know he is an Old Believer, or rather a dissenter?", "그리고 그때도 자기도 모르게 훔쳤지요, ‘떨어진 걸 주운 게 어떻게 도둑질이람?’ 하면서요. 그리고 그자가 구교도(舊敎徒), 아니 정확히는 분리파라는 걸 아십니까?"], ["There have been Wanderers[*] in his family, and he was for two years in his village under the spiritual guidance of a certain elder.", "그자 집안엔 유랑파*들이 있었고, 그자는 이 년 동안 자기 마을에서 어떤 장로의 영적 지도를 받았답니다."], ["I learnt all this from Nikolay and from his fellow villagers.", "이 모든 걸 나는 니콜라이와 그 마을 사람들한테서 알아냈지요."], ["And what’s more, he wanted to run into the wilderness!", "게다가 그자는 황야로 달아나고 싶어 했어요!"], ["He was full of fervour, prayed at night, read the old books, ‘the true’ ones, and read himself crazy.", "열정에 가득 차서, 밤마다 기도하고, ‘참된’ 옛 책들을 읽고, 읽다가 정신이 나가 버렸지요."]], [["[*] A religious sect.--TRANSLATOR’S NOTE.", "* 유랑파: 한 종교 분파다.—옮긴이 주."]], [["“Petersburg had a great effect upon him, especially the women and the wine.", "“페테르부르크가 그자에게 큰 영향을 미쳤어요, 특히 여자들과 술이 말이지요."], ["He responds to everything and he forgot the elder and all that.", "그자는 매사에 반응하니, 장로고 뭐고 다 잊어버렸답니다."], ["I learnt that an artist here took a fancy to him, and used to go and see him, and now this business came upon him.", "듣자 하니 여기 어떤 화가가 그자를 좋아해서 보러 다니곤 했는데, 그러다 이번 사건이 그자한테 들이닥친 거지요."]], [["“Well, he was frightened, he tried to hang himself!", "“그래서 그자는 겁에 질려서, 목을 매려고 했어요!"], ["He ran away!", "달아났지요!"], ["How can one get over the idea the people have of Russian legal proceedings?", "러시아 사법 절차에 대해 민중이 품고 있는 그 관념을 어떻게 깨겠습니까?"], ["The very word ‘trial’ frightens some of them.", "‘재판’이라는 말 자체가 그들 중 몇몇을 겁먹게 하지요."], ["Whose fault is it?", "그게 누구 탓이겠습니까?"], ["We shall see what the new juries will do.", "새 배심원들이 어떻게 하는지 두고 봅시다."], ["God grant they do good!", "신께서 그들이 잘하도록 도우시기를!"], ["Well, in prison, it seems, he remembered the venerable elder; the Bible, too, made its appearance again.", "자, 감옥에서, 그자는 그 존경스러운 장로를 떠올린 모양입니다. 성경도 다시 등장했고요."], ["Do you know, Rodion Romanovitch, the force of the word ‘suffering’ among some of these people!", "아십니까, 로디온 로마노비치, 그런 사람들 사이에서 ‘수난’이라는 말이 지닌 힘을!"], ["It’s not a question of suffering for someone’s benefit, but simply, ‘one must suffer.’ If they suffer at the hands of the authorities, so much the better.", "누군가를 위해 고통받는다는 게 아니라, 그저 ‘고통받아야 한다’는 거지요. 당국의 손에 고통받는다면, 그건 더 좋은 일이고요."], ["In my time there was a very meek and mild prisoner who spent a whole year in prison always reading his Bible on the stove at night and he read himself crazy, and so crazy, do you know, that one day, apropos of nothing, he seized a brick and flung it at the governor; though he had done him no harm.", "내가 일하던 시절에 아주 온순하고 유순한 죄수가 하나 있었는데, 꼬박 일 년을 감옥에서 밤마다 난로 위에서 성경을 읽다가 정신이 나가 버렸지요, 그것도 어찌나 단단히 나갔는지, 아십니까, 어느 날 난데없이 벽돌을 집어 들어 소장한테 던졌답니다, 그 사람이 자기한테 아무 해도 끼치지 않았는데 말이지요."], ["And the way he threw it too: aimed it a yard on one side on purpose, for fear of hurting him.", "게다가 던진 모양새가 또, 일부러 한 걸음 옆을 겨냥했어요, 그 사람을 다치게 할까 봐서요."], ["Well, we know what happens to a prisoner who assaults an officer with a weapon.", "자, 무기로 관리를 폭행한 죄수가 어떻게 되는지는 우리도 알지요."], ["So ‘he took his suffering.’", "그렇게 그자는 ‘자기 수난을 받아들인’ 겁니다."]], [["“So I suspect now that Nikolay wants to take his suffering or something of the sort.", "“그래서 지금 나는 니콜라이가 자기 수난이나 뭐 그런 비슷한 걸 받아들이고 싶어 한다고 의심합니다."], ["I know it for certain from facts, indeed.", "사실 여러 정황으로 봐서 확실히 알고 있지요."], ["Only he doesn’t know that I know.", "다만 그자는 내가 안다는 걸 모를 뿐이에요."], ["What, you don’t admit that there are such fantastic people among the peasants?", "뭐라고요, 농부들 사이에 그런 환상적인 사람들이 있다는 걸 인정 못 하시겠다고요?"], ["Lots of them.", "수두룩합니다."], ["The elder now has begun influencing him, especially since he tried to hang himself.", "이제 그 장로가 그자에게 영향을 미치기 시작했어요, 특히 그자가 목을 매려 한 뒤로 말이지요."], ["But he’ll come and tell me all himself.", "하지만 그자는 와서 내게 죄다 제 입으로 털어놓을 겁니다."], ["You think he’ll hold out?", "그자가 버틸 거라고 생각하십니까?"], ["Wait a bit, he’ll take his words back.", "좀 기다려 보세요, 그자는 자기 말을 거둬들일 겁니다."], ["I am waiting from hour to hour for him to come and abjure his evidence.", "나는 시시각각 기다리고 있어요, 그자가 와서 자기 증언을 부인하기를 말입니다."], ["I have come to like that Nikolay and am studying him in detail.", "나는 그 니콜라이가 마음에 들기 시작해서 그자를 세세히 연구하고 있답니다."], ["And what do you think?", "그런데 어떻게 생각하십니까?"], ["He-he!", "헤헤!"], ["He answered me very plausibly on some points, he obviously had collected some evidence and prepared himself cleverly.", "그자는 어떤 점들에선 내게 아주 그럴듯하게 대답했어요, 분명히 증거를 좀 모아서 영리하게 준비를 한 거지요."], ["But on other points he is simply at sea, knows nothing and doesn’t even suspect that he doesn’t know!", "하지만 다른 점들에선 그냥 갈피를 못 잡고, 아무것도 모르고, 자기가 모른다는 것조차 알아채지 못한답니다!"]], [["“No, Rodion Romanovitch, Nikolay doesn’t come in!", "“아니에요, 로디온 로마노비치, 니콜라이는 이 일에 끼어들지 않아요!"], ["This is a fantastic, gloomy business, a modern case, an incident of to-day when the heart of man is troubled, when the phrase is quoted that blood ‘renews,’ when comfort is preached as the aim of life.", "이건 환상적이고 음울한 사건, 현대적인 사건, 인간의 마음이 어지러운 오늘날의 사건이지요. 피가 ‘새롭게 한다’는 문구가 인용되고, 안락이 삶의 목적으로 설파되는 시대 말입니다."], ["Here we have bookish dreams, a heart unhinged by theories.", "여기엔 책에서 나온 몽상이, 이론들로 곤두선 마음이 있어요."], ["Here we see resolution in the first stage, but resolution of a special kind: he resolved to do it like jumping over a precipice or from a bell tower and his legs shook as he went to the crime.", "여기서 우리는 첫 단계의 결단을 봅니다만, 그건 특별한 종류의 결단이지요. 그는 그 일을 결행했어요, 마치 절벽에서 뛰어내리거나 종탑에서 뛰어내리듯이, 그리고 범행을 향해 가면서 그의 다리는 떨렸지요."], ["He forgot to shut the door after him, and murdered two people for a theory.", "그는 뒤로 문 닫는 걸 잊었고, 이론 하나 때문에 두 사람을 죽였어요."], ["He committed the murder and couldn’t take the money, and what he did manage to snatch up he hid under a stone.", "살인을 저지르고도 돈은 챙기지 못했고, 가까스로 움켜쥔 것은 돌 밑에 숨겼지요."], ["It wasn’t enough for him to suffer agony behind the door while they battered at the door and rung the bell, no, he had to go to the empty lodging, half delirious, to recall the bell-ringing, he wanted to feel the cold shiver over again....", "그들이 문을 두드리고 초인종을 울려 대는 동안 문 뒤에서 고뇌하는 것만으로는 모자랐던지, 아니, 그는 반쯤 헛소리를 하며 그 빈집까지 가야 했어요, 그 초인종 소리를 다시 떠올리려고, 그 오싹한 전율을 다시 한번 느끼고 싶어서 말이지요……"], ["Well, that we grant, was through illness, but consider this: he is a murderer, but looks upon himself as an honest man, despises others, poses as injured innocence.", "자, 그건 병 탓이었다고 칩시다, 하지만 이걸 생각해 보세요. 그는 살인자이면서도 자기 자신을 정직한 사람으로 여기고, 남들을 경멸하며, 상처 입은 결백인 양 행세합니다."], ["No, that’s not the work of a Nikolay, my dear Rodion Romanovitch!”", "아니에요, 그건 니콜라이가 할 짓이 아니지요, 친애하는 로디온 로마노비치!”"]], [["All that had been said before had sounded so like a recantation that these words were too great a shock.", "앞서 한 말들이 모두 너무도 자기 진술을 뒤집는 것처럼 들렸던 터라, 이 말은 너무도 큰 충격이었다."], ["Raskolnikov shuddered as though he had been stabbed.", "라스콜니코프는 칼에 찔리기라도 한 듯 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“Then...", "“그럼……"], ["who then...", "그럼 누가……"], ["is the murderer?”", "살인자라는 겁니까?”"], ["he asked in a breathless voice, unable to restrain himself.", "그는 스스로를 억누르지 못한 채 숨 가쁜 목소리로 물었다."]], [["Porfiry Petrovitch sank back in his chair, as though he were amazed at the question.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 그 질문에 놀라기라도 한 듯 의자에 등을 기댔다."]], [["“Who is the murderer?”", "“누가 살인자냐고요?”"], ["he repeated, as though unable to believe his ears.", "그는 제 귀를 믿을 수 없다는 듯 되물었다."], ["“Why, _you_, Rodion Romanovitch!", "“아니, 당신이지요, 로디온 로마노비치!"], ["You are the murderer,”", "당신이 살인자입니다.”"], ["he added, almost in a whisper, in a voice of genuine conviction.", "그는 거의 속삭이듯, 진정한 확신이 담긴 목소리로 덧붙였다."]], [["Raskolnikov leapt from the sofa, stood up for a few seconds and sat down again without uttering a word.", "라스콜니코프는 소파에서 벌떡 일어나 몇 초쯤 서 있다가 한마디도 없이 다시 앉았다."], ["His face twitched convulsively.", "그의 얼굴이 경련하듯 씰룩거렸다."]], [["“Your lip is twitching just as it did before,”", "“입술이 전처럼 또 씰룩거리는군요.”"], ["Porfiry Petrovitch observed almost sympathetically.", "포르피리 페트로비치가 거의 동정하듯 말했다."], ["“You’ve been misunderstanding me, I think, Rodion Romanovitch,”", "“내 생각엔 댁이 나를 오해하고 있는 것 같아요, 로디온 로마노비치.”"], ["he added after a brief pause, “that’s why you are so surprised.", "그는 잠시 사이를 두었다가 덧붙였다. “그래서 그렇게 놀라는 거지요."], ["I came on purpose to tell you everything and deal openly with you.”", "나는 일부러 모든 걸 댁에게 말하고 솔직하게 처신하려고 온 겁니다.”"]], [["“It was not I murdered her,”", "“그 여자를 죽인 건 내가 아니오.”"], ["Raskolnikov whispered like a frightened child caught in the act.", "라스콜니코프는 현장에서 붙잡힌 겁먹은 아이처럼 속삭였다."]], [["“No, it was you, you Rodion Romanovitch, and no one else,”", "“아니에요, 댁이었어요, 댁이지요, 로디온 로마노비치, 다른 누구도 아니에요.”"], ["Porfiry whispered sternly, with conviction.", "포르피리는 확신에 차서 엄하게 속삭였다."]], [["They were both silent and the silence lasted strangely long, about ten minutes.", "두 사람 다 입을 다물었고, 그 침묵은 이상하리만치 오래, 십 분쯤이나 이어졌다."], ["Raskolnikov put his elbow on the table and passed his fingers through his hair.", "라스콜니코프는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손가락으로 머리칼을 쓸어 넘겼다."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch sat quietly waiting.", "포르피리 페트로비치는 조용히 앉아 기다렸다."], ["Suddenly Raskolnikov looked scornfully at Porfiry.", "갑자기 라스콜니코프가 경멸하듯 포르피리를 바라보았다."]], [["“You are at your old tricks again, Porfiry Petrovitch!", "“또 그 낡은 수작이군요, 포르피리 페트로비치!"], ["Your old method again.", "또 그 낡은 수법이오."], ["I wonder you don’t get sick of it!”", "신물도 안 나는 게 놀랍습니다!”"]], [["“Oh, stop that, what does that matter now?", "“오, 그만두세요, 그게 지금 무슨 상관입니까?"], ["It would be a different matter if there were witnesses present, but we are whispering alone.", "증인들이 있다면 다른 문제겠지만, 우리는 단둘이 속삭이고 있잖아요."], ["You see yourself that I have not come to chase and capture you like a hare.", "댁도 보다시피 나는 댁을 토끼처럼 쫓아 잡으려고 온 게 아닙니다."], ["Whether you confess it or not is nothing to me now; for myself, I am convinced without it.”", "댁이 자백하든 안 하든 이제 내겐 아무래도 좋아요. 나로서는 그것 없이도 확신하고 있으니까요.”"]], [["“If so, what did you come for?”", "“그렇다면 무엇 하러 온 겁니까?”"], ["Raskolnikov asked irritably.", "라스콜니코프가 짜증스럽게 물었다."], ["“I ask you the same question again: if you consider me guilty, why don’t you take me to prison?”", "“같은 질문을 다시 하지요. 나를 죄인으로 여긴다면, 왜 나를 감옥에 데려가지 않는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Oh, that’s your question!", "“오, 바로 그게 댁의 질문이군요!"], ["I will answer you, point for point.", "조목조목 답해 드리지요."], ["In the first place, to arrest you so directly is not to my interest.”", "첫째, 댁을 그렇게 곧바로 체포하는 건 내게 이롭지 않습니다.”"]], [["“How so?", "“어째서요?"], ["If you are convinced you ought....”", "확신한다면 마땅히…….”"]], [["“Ach, what if I am convinced?", "“아, 내가 확신한들 그게 어쨌다는 겁니까?"], ["That’s only my dream for the time.", "그건 지금으로선 그저 내 꿈일 뿐이에요."], ["Why should I put you in safety?", "내가 왜 댁을 안전한 곳에 넣어 두겠습니까?"], ["You know that’s it, since you ask me to do it.", "댁이 그렇게 해 달라고 청하는 걸 보니 그게 안전한 걸 아는 거지요."], ["If I confront you with that workman for instance and you say to him ‘were you drunk or not?", "가령 내가 댁을 그 직공과 대질시키면 댁은 그자에게 이렇게 말하겠지요, ‘당신, 취했었소, 안 취했었소?"], ["Who saw me with you?", "누가 나를 당신과 함께 봤다는 거요?"], ["I simply took you to be drunk, and you were drunk, too.’ Well, what could I answer, especially as your story is a more likely one than his?", "나는 그냥 당신이 취한 걸로 알았고, 당신도 취해 있었잖소.’ 자, 그러면 내가 뭐라고 답하겠습니까, 더구나 댁의 이야기가 그자 것보다 더 그럴듯한데 말입니다."], ["for there’s nothing but psychology to support his evidence--that’s almost unseemly with his ugly mug, while you hit the mark exactly, for the rascal is an inveterate drunkard and notoriously so.", "그자의 증언을 뒷받침하는 건 심리뿐이거든요 — 그 추한 상판에 그건 거의 꼴사납기까지 하지요, 반면에 댁은 정확히 정곡을 찌르고요, 그 악당은 소문난 골수 주정뱅이니까요."], ["And I have myself admitted candidly several times already that that psychology can be taken in two ways and that the second way is stronger and looks far more probable, and that apart from that I have as yet nothing against you.", "게다가 나 자신도 이미 여러 번 솔직히 인정했지요, 그 심리라는 게 두 가지로 해석될 수 있고, 두 번째 해석이 더 유력하며 훨씬 더 그럴듯해 보인다고, 그리고 그것 말고는 아직 댁에게 불리한 게 아무것도 없다고 말입니다."], ["And though I shall put you in prison and indeed have come--quite contrary to etiquette--to inform you of it beforehand, yet I tell you frankly, also contrary to etiquette, that it won’t be to my advantage.", "그래서 비록 내가 댁을 감옥에 넣을 거고, 사실 — 예의에 완전히 어긋나게도 — 그걸 미리 알리러 오긴 했지만, 그래도 솔직히 말씀드리지요, 이 또한 예의에 어긋나게, 그게 내게 득이 되지는 않을 겁니다."], ["Well, secondly, I’ve come to you because...”", "자, 둘째로, 내가 댁을 찾아온 건…….”"]], [["“Yes, yes, secondly?”", "“그래요, 그래, 둘째는요?”"], ["Raskolnikov was listening breathless.", "라스콜니코프는 숨을 죽이고 듣고 있었다."]], [["“Because, as I told you just now, I consider I owe you an explanation.", "“방금 말씀드렸듯이, 내가 댁에게 해명할 빚이 있다고 여기기 때문이에요."], ["I don’t want you to look upon me as a monster, as I have a genuine liking for you, you may believe me or not.", "댁이 나를 괴물로 여기는 게 싫거든요, 나는 댁에게 진심으로 호감을 갖고 있으니까요, 믿든 안 믿든 말입니다."], ["And in the third place I’ve come to you with a direct and open proposition--that you should surrender and confess.", "그리고 셋째로, 나는 댁에게 직접적이고 솔직한 제안을 하러 왔습니다 — 댁이 자수하고 자백하라는 거지요."], ["It will be infinitely more to your advantage and to my advantage too, for my task will be done.", "그게 댁에게도, 내게도 한없이 더 이로울 겁니다, 내 임무가 끝날 테니까요."], ["Well, is this open on my part or not?”", "자, 이게 내 쪽에서 솔직한 겁니까, 아닙니까?”"]], [["Raskolnikov thought a minute.", "라스콜니코프는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Listen, Porfiry Petrovitch.", "“들어 보세요, 포르피리 페트로비치."], ["You said just now you have nothing but psychology to go on, yet now you’ve gone on mathematics.", "당신은 방금 심리 말고는 의지할 게 아무것도 없다고 했는데, 그러더니 이제 수학으로 넘어갔군요."], ["Well, what if you are mistaken yourself, now?”", "자, 만약 지금 당신 자신이 잘못 짚은 거라면 어쩌지요?”"]], [["“No, Rodion Romanovitch, I am not mistaken.", "“아니에요, 로디온 로마노비치, 나는 잘못 짚지 않았어요."], ["I have a little fact even then, Providence sent it me.”", "그때도 내겐 작은 사실 하나가 있었지요, 섭리가 내게 보내 준 겁니다.”"]], [["“What little fact?”", "“무슨 작은 사실이오?”"]], [["“I won’t tell you what, Rodion Romanovitch.", "“그게 뭔지는 말하지 않겠습니다, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["And in any case, I haven’t the right to put it off any longer, I must arrest you.", "어쨌든 나는 더 이상 미룰 권리가 없어요, 댁을 체포해야 합니다. 그러니 잘 생각해 보세요."], ["So think it over: it makes no difference to me _now_ and so I speak only for your sake.", "지금으로선 내겐 아무 차이가 없으니, 나는 오직 댁을 위해 하는 말입니다."], ["Believe me, it will be better, Rodion Romanovitch.”", "믿으세요, 그게 더 나을 겁니다, 로디온 로마노비치.”"]], [["Raskolnikov smiled malignantly.", "라스콜니코프가 악의에 차서 미소 지었다."]], [["“That’s not simply ridiculous, it’s positively shameless.", "“그건 우스꽝스러운 정도가 아니라, 정말이지 파렴치한 짓이오."], ["Why, even if I were guilty, which I don’t admit, what reason should I have to confess, when you tell me yourself that I shall be in greater safety in prison?”", "아니, 내가 죄인이라 칩시다, 인정하는 건 아니지만, 감옥에 있는 게 더 안전하다고 당신 입으로 말하면서, 내게 무슨 이유로 자백하라는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Ah, Rodion Romanovitch, don’t put too much faith in words, perhaps prison will not be altogether a restful place.", "“아, 로디온 로마노비치, 말을 너무 곧이곧대로 믿지 마세요, 어쩌면 감옥이 마냥 편안한 곳은 아닐지도 모르니까요."], ["That’s only theory and my theory, and what authority am I for you?", "그건 그저 이론일 뿐이고, 그것도 내 이론이에요, 그리고 내가 댁에게 무슨 권위가 되겠습니까?"], ["Perhaps, too, even now I am hiding something from you?", "어쩌면 지금도 내가 댁에게 뭔가를 숨기고 있는지도 모르지요?"], ["I can’t lay bare everything, he-he!", "모든 걸 다 드러낼 수는 없거든요, 헤헤!"], ["And how can you ask what advantage?", "그리고 무슨 이로움이 있느냐니, 어떻게 그런 걸 물으십니까?"], ["Don’t you know how it would lessen your sentence?", "그게 댁의 형을 얼마나 가볍게 해 줄지 모르신단 말입니까?"], ["You would be confessing at a moment when another man has taken the crime on himself and so has muddled the whole case.", "다른 사람이 그 죄를 자기가 떠맡아 사건 전체를 뒤죽박죽으로 만들어 놓은 그 순간에 댁이 자백하는 게 되니까요."], ["Consider that!", "그걸 생각해 보세요!"], ["I swear before God that I will so arrange that your confession shall come as a complete surprise.", "신께 맹세컨대 나는 댁의 자백이 완전한 뜻밖의 일로 보이도록 손을 써 두겠습니다."], ["We will make a clean sweep of all these psychological points, of a suspicion against you, so that your crime will appear to have been something like an aberration, for in truth it was an aberration.", "우리는 이 모든 심리적인 점들을, 댁에 대한 의심을 깨끗이 쓸어 없앨 거예요, 그래서 댁의 범죄가 일종의 착란 같은 걸로 보이게 말입니다, 사실 그건 정말로 착란이었으니까요."], ["I am an honest man, Rodion Romanovitch, and will keep my word.”", "나는 정직한 사람입니다, 로디온 로마노비치, 그리고 약속을 지킬 겁니다.”"]], [["Raskolnikov maintained a mournful silence and let his head sink dejectedly.", "라스콜니코프는 침울한 침묵을 지키며 맥없이 고개를 떨구었다."], ["He pondered a long while and at last smiled again, but his smile was sad and gentle.", "그는 오랫동안 골똘히 생각하다가 마침내 다시 미소 지었지만, 그 미소는 슬프고 온순했다."]], [["“No!”", "“아니오!”"], ["he said, apparently abandoning all attempt to keep up appearances with Porfiry, “it’s not worth it, I don’t care about lessening the sentence!”", "그는 포르피리 앞에서 더는 체면을 차리려는 시도를 모두 내려놓은 듯 말했다. “그럴 가치 없소, 나는 형이 가벼워지든 말든 관심 없습니다!”"]], [["“That’s just what I was afraid of!”", "“바로 그게 내가 두려워하던 겁니다!”"], ["Porfiry cried warmly and, as it seemed, involuntarily.", "포르피리가 열을 띠고, 보아하니 저도 모르게 외쳤다."], ["“That’s just what I feared, that you wouldn’t care about the mitigation of sentence.”", "“바로 그게 내가 걱정했던 거예요, 댁이 형의 경감 따위엔 관심을 두지 않으리라는 거 말입니다.”"]], [["Raskolnikov looked sadly and expressively at him.", "라스콜니코프는 슬프고도 의미심장하게 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Ah, don’t disdain life!”", "“아, 삶을 업신여기지 마세요!”"], ["Porfiry went on.", "포르피리가 말을 이었다."], ["“You have a great deal of it still before you.", "“댁 앞엔 아직 삶이 많이 남아 있어요."], ["How can you say you don’t want a mitigation of sentence?", "어떻게 형의 경감을 원치 않는다고 말할 수 있습니까?"], ["You are an impatient fellow!”", "댁은 참을성 없는 사람이로군요!”"]], [["“A great deal of what lies before me?”", "“내 앞에 많이 남은 게 무엇이오?”"]], [["“Of life.", "“삶 말입니다."], ["What sort of prophet are you, do you know much about it?", "댁이 무슨 예언자라고, 그걸 그리 잘 아십니까?"], ["Seek and ye shall find.", "구하라, 그러면 찾을 것이다."], ["This may be God’s means for bringing you to Him.", "이게 어쩌면 신께서 댁을 당신께로 이끄시려는 방편일지도 모릅니다."], ["And it’s not for ever, the bondage....”", "그리고 그 속박이 영원한 건 아니에요…….”"]], [["“The time will be shortened,”", "“형기가 단축되겠지요.”"], ["laughed Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 웃었다."]], [["“Why, is it the bourgeois disgrace you are afraid of?", "“아니, 댁이 두려워하는 건 세속적인 치욕입니까?"], ["It may be that you are afraid of it without knowing it, because you are young!", "어쩌면 댁은 그걸 모르고서 두려워하는 건지도 몰라요, 젊으니까요!"], ["But anyway _you_ shouldn’t be afraid of giving yourself up and confessing.”", "하지만 어쨌든 댁은 자수하고 자백하는 걸 두려워해선 안 됩니다.”"]], [["“Ach, hang it!”", "“아, 빌어먹을!”"], ["Raskolnikov whispered with loathing and contempt, as though he did not want to speak aloud.", "라스콜니코프는 큰 소리로 말하고 싶지 않다는 듯 혐오와 경멸을 담아 속삭였다."]], [["He got up again as though he meant to go away, but sat down again in evident despair.", "그는 갈 작정인 듯 다시 일어났지만, 역력한 절망에 잠겨 다시 앉았다."]], [["“Hang it, if you like!", "“빌어먹을, 그러고 싶으면 그러세요!"], ["You’ve lost faith and you think that I am grossly flattering you; but how long has your life been?", "댁은 믿음을 잃었고, 내가 댁에게 노골적으로 아첨한다고 여기지요. 하지만 댁이 살아 봐야 얼마나 살았습니까?"], ["How much do you understand?", "얼마나 이해하고 있습니까?"], ["You made up a theory and then were ashamed that it broke down and turned out to be not at all original!", "댁은 이론 하나를 지어내고는, 그게 무너져 내려 전혀 독창적이지 않은 것으로 드러나자 부끄러워했지요!"], ["It turned out something base, that’s true, but you are not hopelessly base.", "그게 천박한 것으로 드러난 건 사실입니다, 하지만 댁은 구제 불능으로 천박한 사람은 아니에요."], ["By no means so base!", "결코 그렇게까지 천박하진 않아요!"], ["At least you didn’t deceive yourself for long, you went straight to the furthest point at one bound.", "적어도 댁은 오래 자기 자신을 속이진 않았지요, 단번에 가장 먼 지점까지 곧장 나아갔으니까요."], ["How do I regard you?", "내가 댁을 어떻게 보느냐고요?"], ["I regard you as one of those men who would stand and smile at their torturer while he cuts their entrails out, if only they have found faith or God.", "나는 댁을, 믿음이나 신을 찾기만 한다면, 고문자가 제 창자를 갈라내는 동안에도 서서 미소 지을 그런 사람들 중 하나로 봅니다. 그걸 찾으세요, 그러면 살아날 겁니다."], ["Find it and you will live.", "댁은 오래전부터 공기를 바꿀 필요가 있었어요."], ["You have long needed a change of air.", "수난 또한 좋은 것이지요. 고통받으세요!"], ["Suffering, too, is a good thing. Suffer!", "어쩌면 니콜라이가 고통받기를 원하는 게 옳은지도 몰라요."], ["Maybe Nikolay is right in wanting to suffer.", "댁이 그걸 믿지 않는다는 걸 압니다 — 하지만 너무 영리하게 굴지 마세요."], ["I know you don’t believe in it--but don’t be over-wise; fling yourself straight into life, without deliberation; don’t be afraid--the flood will bear you to the bank and set you safe on your feet again.", "머뭇거리지 말고 곧장 삶 속으로 몸을 던지세요. 두려워하지 마세요 — 홍수가 댁을 강기슭으로 실어다 다시 두 발로 안전하게 세워 줄 겁니다."], ["What bank? How can I tell?", "어떤 기슭이냐고요?"], ["I only believe that you have long life before you.", "내가 어찌 알겠습니까? 다만 나는 댁 앞에 긴 삶이 있다고 믿어요."], ["I know that you take all my words now for a set speech prepared beforehand, but maybe you will remember them after.", "댁이 지금 내 말을 죄다 미리 준비해 둔 연설쯤으로 여긴다는 걸 압니다, 하지만 어쩌면 나중에 이 말들을 기억하게 될 거예요."], ["They may be of use some time.", "언젠가 쓸모가 있을지도 모릅니다."], ["That’s why I speak.", "그래서 말하는 거예요."], ["It’s as well that you only killed the old woman.", "댁이 그 노파만 죽인 건 그나마 다행이지요."], ["If you’d invented another theory you might perhaps have done something a thousand times more hideous.", "다른 이론을 지어냈더라면 어쩌면 천 배는 더 끔찍한 짓을 저질렀을지도 몰라요."], ["You ought to thank God, perhaps.", "어쩌면 신께 감사해야 할지도 모릅니다."], ["How do you know?", "댁이 어떻게 알겠어요?"], ["Perhaps God is saving you for something.", "어쩌면 신께서 무언가를 위해 댁을 살려 두시는지도 모르지요."], ["But keep a good heart and have less fear!", "하지만 마음을 굳게 먹고, 두려움을 좀 덜어 내세요!"], ["Are you afraid of the great expiation before you?", "댁 앞에 놓인 그 큰 속죄가 두렵습니까?"], ["No, it would be shameful to be afraid of it.", "아니, 그걸 두려워하는 건 부끄러운 일일 겁니다."], ["Since you have taken such a step, you must harden your heart.", "그런 걸음을 내디뎠으니, 댁은 마음을 단단히 해야 해요."], ["There is justice in it.", "거기엔 정의가 있습니다."], ["You must fulfil the demands of justice.", "댁은 정의의 요구를 다해야 해요."], ["I know that you don’t believe it, but indeed, life will bring you through.", "댁이 그걸 믿지 않는다는 걸 압니다, 하지만 정말이지, 삶이 댁을 끝까지 이끌어 줄 겁니다."], ["You will live it down in time.", "시간이 지나면 댁은 그걸 견뎌 내고 극복할 거예요."], ["What you need now is fresh air, fresh air, fresh air!”", "댁에게 지금 필요한 건 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기, 신선한 공기입니다!”"]], [["Raskolnikov positively started.", "라스콜니코프는 정말이지 흠칫했다."]], [["“But who are you?", "“하지만 당신은 누구요?"], ["what prophet are you?", "무슨 예언자란 말이오?"], ["From the height of what majestic calm do you proclaim these words of wisdom?”", "도대체 어떤 위엄에 찬 평온의 높이에서 이런 지혜의 말을 선포하는 겁니까?”"]], [["“Who am I?", "“내가 누구냐고요?"], ["I am a man with nothing to hope for, that’s all.", "나는 아무것도 바랄 게 없는 사람이지요, 그게 다입니다."], ["A man perhaps of feeling and sympathy, maybe of some knowledge too, but my day is over.", "어쩌면 감정과 연민을 지닌 사람, 어쩌면 약간의 지식도 지닌 사람일지 모르지만, 내 시절은 끝났어요."], ["But you are a different matter, there is life waiting for you.", "하지만 댁은 다른 문제예요, 댁에겐 삶이 기다리고 있지요."], ["Though, who knows?", "하기야, 누가 알겠어요?"], ["maybe your life, too, will pass off in smoke and come to nothing.", "어쩌면 댁의 삶도 연기처럼 흩어져 아무것도 아닌 게 되어 버릴지도 모르지요."], ["Come, what does it matter, that you will pass into another class of men?", "자, 댁이 다른 부류의 인간들 틈으로 넘어간들 그게 무슨 대수겠습니까?"], ["It’s not comfort you regret, with your heart!", "댁이, 그런 마음을 지니고서, 아쉬워하는 게 안락은 아니잖아요!"], ["What of it that perhaps no one will see you for so long?", "어쩌면 오랫동안 아무도 댁을 보지 못한들 그게 무슨 상관이겠습니까?"], ["It’s not time, but yourself that will decide that.", "그건 시간이 아니라 댁 자신이 결정할 일이에요."], ["Be the sun and all will see you.", "태양이 되세요, 그러면 모두가 댁을 볼 겁니다."], ["The sun has before all to be the sun.", "태양은 무엇보다 먼저 태양이어야 하니까요."], ["Why are you smiling again?", "왜 또 미소 짓습니까?"], ["At my being such a Schiller?", "내가 이렇게 실러처럼 군다고요?"], ["I bet you’re imagining that I am trying to get round you by flattery.", "장담컨대 댁은 내가 아첨으로 댁을 구워삶으려 한다고 상상하고 있겠지요."], ["Well, perhaps I am, he-he-he!", "뭐, 어쩌면 그런지도 모르지요, 헤헤헤!"], ["Perhaps you’d better not believe my word, perhaps you’d better never believe it altogether--I’m made that way, I confess it.", "어쩌면 댁은 내 말을 안 믿는 게 나을지도, 어쩌면 영영 통째로는 믿지 않는 게 나을지도 몰라요 — 나는 그렇게 생겨먹었거든요, 인정합니다."], ["But let me add, you can judge for yourself, I think, how far I am a base sort of man and how far I am honest.”", "하지만 한마디 덧붙이지요, 댁 스스로 판단할 수 있을 거라고 봅니다, 내가 어느 정도 천박한 사람이고 어느 정도 정직한 사람인지를 말이지요.”"]], [["“When do you mean to arrest me?”", "“언제 나를 체포할 작정이오?”"]], [["“Well, I can let you walk about another day or two.", "“뭐, 하루 이틀쯤 더 돌아다니게 둘 수 있지요."], ["Think it over, my dear fellow, and pray to God.", "잘 생각해 보세요, 친애하는 양반, 그리고 신께 기도하세요."], ["It’s more in your interest, believe me.”", "그게 댁에게 더 이롭습니다, 믿으세요.”"]], [["“And what if I run away?”", "“그런데 내가 달아나면요?”"], ["asked Raskolnikov with a strange smile.", "라스콜니코프가 묘한 미소를 띠며 물었다."]], [["“No, you won’t run away.", "“아니, 댁은 달아나지 않을 겁니다."], ["A peasant would run away, a fashionable dissenter would run away, the flunkey of another man’s thought, for you’ve only to show him the end of your little finger and he’ll be ready to believe in anything for the rest of his life.", "농부라면 달아나겠지요, 유행을 좇는 분리파라면 달아날 거예요, 남의 생각의 하인이라면 말입니다, 그런 자에게는 새끼손가락 끝만 보여 줘도 평생 무엇이든 믿을 채비가 되거든요."], ["But you’ve ceased to believe in your theory already, what will you run away with?", "하지만 댁은 이미 자기 이론을 믿지 않게 됐는데, 무얼 들고 달아나겠습니까?"], ["And what would you do in hiding?", "그리고 숨어서 무얼 하겠어요?"], ["It would be hateful and difficult for you, and what you need more than anything in life is a definite position, an atmosphere to suit you.", "댁에겐 가증스럽고 괴로운 일일 거예요, 그리고 무엇보다 댁이 삶에서 필요로 하는 건 분명한 위치, 댁에게 어울리는 분위기지요. 한데 댁이 어떤 분위기를 얻겠습니까?"], ["And what sort of atmosphere would you have?", "달아난다면 댁은 제 발로 돌아올 거예요."], ["If you ran away, you’d come back of yourself.", "댁은 우리 없이는 살아갈 수 없으니까요."], ["_You can’t get on without us._ And if I put you in prison--say you’ve been there a month, or two, or three--remember my word, you’ll confess of yourself and perhaps to your own surprise.", "그리고 내가 댁을 감옥에 넣는다면 — 한 달, 두 달, 석 달쯤 거기 있었다고 칩시다 — 내 말을 기억하세요, 댁은 제 발로 자백할 겁니다, 어쩌면 댁 자신도 놀라면서 말이지요."], ["You won’t know an hour beforehand that you are coming with a confession.", "한 시간 전까지도 댁은 자기가 자백하러 가는 줄 모를 거예요."], ["I am convinced that you will decide, ‘to take your suffering.’ You don’t believe my words now, but you’ll come to it of yourself.", "나는 확신합니다, 댁이 ‘자기 수난을 받아들이기로’ 마음먹으리라는 걸요. 댁은 지금 내 말을 믿지 않지만, 제 발로 거기에 이르게 될 겁니다."], ["For suffering, Rodion Romanovitch, is a great thing.", "수난이란, 로디온 로마노비치, 위대한 것이거든요."], ["Never mind my having grown fat, I know all the same.", "내가 살이 쪘다고 마음 쓰지 마세요, 그래도 나는 다 압니다."], ["Don’t laugh at it, there’s an idea in suffering, Nikolay is right.", "비웃지 마세요, 수난 속엔 하나의 사상이 있어요, 니콜라이가 옳습니다."], ["No, you won’t run away, Rodion Romanovitch.”", "아니에요, 댁은 달아나지 않을 겁니다, 로디온 로마노비치.”"]], [["Raskolnikov got up and took his cap.", "라스콜니코프가 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["Porfiry Petrovitch also rose.", "포르피리 페트로비치도 일어섰다."]], [["“Are you going for a walk?", "“산책하러 가십니까?"], ["The evening will be fine, if only we don’t have a storm.", "폭풍만 안 친다면 저녁이 좋을 겁니다."], ["Though it would be a good thing to freshen the air.”", "공기를 좀 식히는 것도 좋은 일이겠지요.”"]], [["He, too, took his cap.", "그도 모자를 집어 들었다."]], [["“Porfiry Petrovitch, please don’t take up the notion that I have confessed to you to-day,”", "“포르피리 페트로비치, 제발 내가 오늘 당신에게 자백했다는 식으로 생각하지 마시오.”"], ["Raskolnikov pronounced with sullen insistence.", "라스콜니코프가 침울하게 다그치듯 잘라 말했다."], ["“You’re a strange man and I have listened to you from simple curiosity.", "“당신은 이상한 사람이고, 나는 그저 호기심에서 당신 말을 들었을 뿐이오."], ["But I have admitted nothing, remember that!”", "하지만 나는 아무것도 인정하지 않았소, 그걸 기억하시오!”"]], [["“Oh, I know that, I’ll remember.", "“오, 압니다, 기억하지요."], ["Look at him, he’s trembling!", "저 사람 좀 보게, 떨고 있군요!"], ["Don’t be uneasy, my dear fellow, have it your own way.", "불안해하지 마세요, 친애하는 양반, 댁 뜻대로 하세요."], ["Walk about a bit, you won’t be able to walk too far.", "좀 돌아다니세요, 너무 멀리 가지는 못하겠지만요."], ["If anything happens, I have one request to make of you,”", "만일 무슨 일이 생긴다면, 댁에게 한 가지 부탁이 있습니다.”"], ["he added, dropping his voice.", "그가 목소리를 낮추며 덧붙였다."], ["“It’s an awkward one, but important.", "“난처한 부탁이지만, 중요한 일이에요."], ["If anything were to happen (though indeed I don’t believe in it and think you quite incapable of it), yet in case you were taken during these forty or fifty hours with the notion of putting an end to the business in some other way, in some fantastic fashion--laying hands on yourself--(it’s an absurd proposition, but you must forgive me for it) do leave a brief but precise note, only two lines, and mention the stone.", "만일 무슨 일이 생긴다면(하기야 정말 나는 그걸 믿지도 않고, 댁이 그럴 위인이 전혀 못 된다고 여깁니다만), 그래도 이 사오십 시간 사이에 댁이 어떤 다른 방식으로, 어떤 환상적인 식으로 이 일을 끝장내려는 — 스스로 목숨을 끊으려는 — 생각에 사로잡힌다면(터무니없는 가정이지만, 그 점은 용서해 주셔야겠어요), 짧지만 분명한 쪽지를, 단 두 줄이라도 남겨 주세요, 그리고 그 돌을 언급해 주시고요."], ["It will be more generous.", "그게 더 너그러운 일일 겁니다."], ["Come, till we meet!", "자, 또 만날 때까지!"], ["Good thoughts and sound decisions to you!”", "좋은 생각과 건전한 결단이 함께하기를!”"]], [["Porfiry went out, stooping and avoiding looking at Raskolnikov.", "포르피리는 몸을 구부린 채 라스콜니코프를 쳐다보지 않으면서 나갔다."], ["The latter went to the window and waited with irritable impatience till he calculated that Porfiry had reached the street and moved away.", "라스콜니코프는 창가로 가서, 포르피리가 거리에 다다라 멀어졌으리라고 계산될 때까지 짜증스러운 초조함을 안고 기다렸다."], ["Then he too went hurriedly out of the room.", "그러고는 그도 서둘러 방을 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["He hurried to Svidrigaïlov’s.", "그는 스비드리가일로프에게 서둘러 갔다."], ["What he had to hope from that man he did not know.", "그 사내에게서 무엇을 기대해야 할지 그는 알지 못했다."], ["But that man had some hidden power over him.", "그러나 그 사내는 그에게 어떤 은밀한 힘을 행사하고 있었다."], ["Having once recognised this, he could not rest, and now the time had come.", "일단 이를 깨달은 이상, 그는 마음을 가라앉힐 수 없었고, 이제 그때가 온 것이다."]], [["On the way, one question particularly worried him: had Svidrigaïlov been to Porfiry’s?", "가는 길에 한 가지 의문이 유난히 그를 괴롭혔다. 스비드리가일로프가 포르피리에게 다녀왔을까?"]], [["As far as he could judge, he would swear to it, that he had not.", "그가 판단할 수 있는 한, 그는 결코 가지 않았다고 단언할 수 있었다."], ["He pondered again and again, went over Porfiry’s visit; no, he hadn’t been, of course he hadn’t.", "그는 거듭거듭 곰곰이 생각하며 포르피리의 방문을 되짚어 보았다. 아니, 그는 가지 않았어, 물론 가지 않았지."]], [["But if he had not been yet, would he go?", "그러나 아직 가지 않았다면, 앞으로 갈까?"], ["Meanwhile, for the present he fancied he couldn’t.", "한편으로 지금으로서는 그가 갈 수 없으리라 여겨졌다."], ["Why?", "왜?"], ["He could not have explained, but if he could, he would not have wasted much thought over it at the moment.", "그는 설명할 수 없었으나, 설령 설명할 수 있다 해도 지금 이 순간 그 일에 골몰하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["It all worried him and at the same time he could not attend to it.", "그 모든 것이 그를 괴롭혔지만 동시에 거기에 마음을 쏟을 수도 없었다."], ["Strange to say, none would have believed it perhaps, but he only felt a faint vague anxiety about his immediate future.", "이상한 일이지만, 어쩌면 아무도 믿지 못할 일이지만, 그는 자신의 임박한 미래에 대해서는 그저 희미하고 막연한 불안만을 느꼈을 뿐이다."], ["Another, much more important anxiety tormented him--it concerned himself, but in a different, more vital way.", "또 다른, 훨씬 더 중요한 불안이 그를 괴롭혔다 — 그것은 그 자신에 관한 것이었으나, 다른, 더 절박한 방식으로 그러했다."], ["Moreover, he was conscious of immense moral fatigue, though his mind was working better that morning than it had done of late.", "게다가 그는 엄청난 정신적 피로를 의식하고 있었다, 비록 그날 아침 그의 머리는 요사이보다 더 잘 돌아갔지만."]], [["And was it worth while, after all that had happened, to contend with these new trivial difficulties?", "그리고 그 모든 일이 일어난 마당에, 이런 새로운 하찮은 곤경들과 씨름할 가치가 과연 있었을까?"], ["Was it worth while, for instance, to manoeuvre that Svidrigaïlov should not go to Porfiry’s?", "가령 스비드리가일로프가 포르피리에게 가지 못하도록 책략을 꾸미는 일이 가치가 있었을까?"], ["Was it worth while to investigate, to ascertain the facts, to waste time over anyone like Svidrigaïlov?", "사실을 조사하고 확인하느라, 스비드리가일로프 따위의 인간에게 시간을 낭비하는 일이 가치가 있었을까?"]], [["Oh, how sick he was of it all!", "오, 그는 이 모든 것에 얼마나 진저리가 났던가!"]], [["And yet he was hastening to Svidrigaïlov; could he be expecting something _new_ from him, information, or means of escape?", "그러면서도 그는 스비드리가일로프에게 서둘러 가고 있었다. 그에게서 무언가 새로운 것을, 정보나 탈출구를 기대하고 있었던 것일까?"], ["Men will catch at straws!", "인간이란 지푸라기라도 붙잡으려 하는 법이지!"], ["Was it destiny or some instinct bringing them together?", "그들을 한데 끌어모은 것은 운명이었을까, 아니면 어떤 본능이었을까?"], ["Perhaps it was only fatigue, despair; perhaps it was not Svidrigaïlov but some other whom he needed, and Svidrigaïlov had simply presented himself by chance.", "어쩌면 그저 피로였을지도, 절망이었을지도. 어쩌면 그에게 필요한 사람은 스비드리가일로프가 아니라 다른 누군가였고, 스비드리가일로프는 그저 우연히 나타났을 뿐인지도 몰랐다."], ["Sonia?", "소냐?"], ["But what should he go to Sonia for now?", "하지만 이제 와서 그가 소냐에게 무엇하러 가겠는가?"], ["To beg her tears again?", "또다시 그녀의 눈물을 구걸하려고?"], ["He was afraid of Sonia, too.", "그는 소냐도 두려웠다."], ["Sonia stood before him as an irrevocable sentence.", "소냐는 그 앞에 돌이킬 수 없는 선고처럼 서 있었다."], ["He must go his own way or hers.", "그는 자신의 길을 가든가 그녀의 길을 가든가 해야 했다."], ["At that moment especially he did not feel equal to seeing her.", "특히 지금 이 순간 그는 그녀를 만날 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["No, would it not be better to try Svidrigaïlov?", "아니, 차라리 스비드리가일로프를 한번 시험해 보는 편이 낫지 않을까?"], ["And he could not help inwardly owning that he had long felt that he must see him for some reason.", "그리고 그는 어떤 이유에서인지 오래전부터 그를 꼭 만나야만 한다고 느껴 왔음을 속으로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["But what could they have in common?", "하지만 그들 사이에 무슨 공통점이 있을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Their very evil-doing could not be of the same kind.", "그들의 악행조차 같은 종류일 수가 없었다."], ["The man, moreover, was very unpleasant, evidently depraved, undoubtedly cunning and deceitful, possibly malignant.", "게다가 그 사내는 무척 불쾌했고, 분명 타락했으며, 의심할 여지 없이 교활하고 기만적이었으며, 어쩌면 악의에 차 있었다."], ["Such stories were told about him.", "그에 관해서는 그런 이야기들이 떠돌았다."], ["It is true he was befriending Katerina Ivanovna’s children, but who could tell with what motive and what it meant?", "사실 그는 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이들을 거두어 주고 있었지만, 무슨 동기로 그러는지, 또 그게 무엇을 뜻하는지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["The man always had some design, some project.", "그 사내에게는 늘 어떤 속셈이, 어떤 계획이 있었다."]], [["There was another thought which had been continually hovering of late about Raskolnikov’s mind, and causing him great uneasiness.", "요사이 라스콜니코프의 마음속에 끊임없이 맴돌며 그를 몹시 불안하게 하는 또 다른 생각이 있었다."], ["It was so painful that he made distinct efforts to get rid of it.", "그것이 어찌나 고통스러운지 그는 그것을 떨쳐 버리려 분명한 노력을 기울였다."], ["He sometimes thought that Svidrigaïlov was dogging his footsteps.", "그는 이따금 스비드리가일로프가 자신의 뒤를 밟고 있는 게 아닐까 생각했다."], ["Svidrigaïlov had found out his secret and had had designs on Dounia.", "스비드리가일로프는 그의 비밀을 알아냈고, 두냐에 대해 흑심을 품고 있었다."], ["What if he had them still?", "만일 그가 여전히 그 흑심을 품고 있다면?"], ["Wasn’t it practically certain that he had?", "그가 그러리라는 게 사실상 확실하지 않은가?"], ["And what if, having learnt his secret and so having gained power over him, he were to use it as a weapon against Dounia?", "그리고 만일 그가 그의 비밀을 알아내어 그에 대한 힘을 얻은 뒤, 그것을 두냐를 겨누는 무기로 사용한다면?"]], [["This idea sometimes even tormented his dreams, but it had never presented itself so vividly to him as on his way to Svidrigaïlov.", "이 생각은 때로 그의 꿈마저 괴롭혔지만, 스비드리가일로프에게 가는 길에서만큼 생생하게 떠오른 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["The very thought moved him to gloomy rage.", "그 생각만으로도 그는 음울한 분노에 휩싸였다."], ["To begin with, this would transform everything, even his own position; he would have at once to confess his secret to Dounia.", "우선 이것은 모든 것을, 그 자신의 처지마저 뒤바꿔 놓을 터였다. 그는 당장 두냐에게 자신의 비밀을 고백해야만 할 것이다."], ["Would he have to give himself up perhaps to prevent Dounia from taking some rash step?", "두냐가 어떤 무모한 짓을 저지르지 못하도록 어쩌면 자수까지 해야 할까?"], ["The letter?", "그 편지?"], ["This morning Dounia had received a letter.", "오늘 아침 두냐는 편지 한 통을 받았다."], ["From whom could she get letters in Petersburg?", "페테르부르크에서 누구한테 편지를 받을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Luzhin, perhaps?", "어쩌면 루진일까?"], ["It’s true Razumihin was there to protect her, but Razumihin knew nothing of the position.", "사실 라주미힌이 곁에서 그녀를 지켜 주고 있었지만, 라주미힌은 이 사정을 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["Perhaps it was his duty to tell Razumihin?", "어쩌면 라주미힌에게 알리는 것이 그의 의무였을까?"], ["He thought of it with repugnance.", "그는 혐오감을 느끼며 그 생각을 떠올렸다."]], [["In any case he must see Svidrigaïlov as soon as possible, he decided finally.", "어쨌든 그는 되도록 빨리 스비드리가일로프를 만나야 한다고, 마침내 결심했다."], ["Thank God, the details of the interview were of little consequence, if only he could get at the root of the matter; but if Svidrigaïlov were capable...", "다행히도 면담의 세세한 내용은 별로 중요하지 않았으니, 그저 문제의 핵심에 가닿을 수만 있다면 그만이었다. 그러나 만일 스비드리가일로프가 그럴 수 있는 자라면……"], ["if he were intriguing against Dounia--then...", "만일 그가 두냐를 겨누어 음모를 꾸미고 있다면 — 그렇다면……."]], [["Raskolnikov was so exhausted by what he had passed through that month that he could only decide such questions in one way; “then I shall kill him,”", "라스콜니코프는 그 한 달 동안 겪은 일들로 어찌나 지쳐 있었던지, 그런 문제들을 단 한 가지 방식으로밖에 결정할 수 없었다. '그렇다면 나는 그를 죽이겠어.'"], ["he thought in cold despair.", "그는 싸늘한 절망 속에서 생각했다."]], [["A sudden anguish oppressed his heart, he stood still in the middle of the street and began looking about to see where he was and which way he was going.", "별안간 고뇌가 그의 가슴을 짓눌렀고, 그는 거리 한복판에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 자신이 어디에 있는지, 어느 쪽으로 가고 있는지 둘러보기 시작했다."], ["He found himself in X. Prospect, thirty or forty paces from the Hay Market, through which he had come.", "그는 자신이 X 거리에, 자기가 지나온 건초 시장에서 서른 걸음인가 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["The whole second storey of the house on the left was used as a tavern.", "왼쪽 건물의 이 층 전체가 선술집으로 쓰이고 있었다."], ["All the windows were wide open; judging from the figures moving at the windows, the rooms were full to overflowing.", "창문은 모조리 활짝 열려 있었는데, 창가에서 움직이는 사람들의 모습으로 미루어 보아 방 안은 넘쳐 날 만큼 사람들로 가득 차 있었다."], ["There were sounds of singing, of clarionet and violin, and the boom of a Turkish drum.", "노랫소리며 클라리넷과 바이올린 소리, 터키 북의 둥둥거리는 울림이 들려왔다."], ["He could hear women shrieking.", "여자들이 꽥꽥 소리 지르는 것도 들렸다."], ["He was about to turn back wondering why he had come to the X. Prospect, when suddenly at one of the end windows he saw Svidrigaïlov, sitting at a tea-table right in the open window with a pipe in his mouth.", "그는 도대체 무엇 때문에 X 거리에 왔을까 의아해하며 막 돌아서려던 참이었는데, 갑자기 맨 끝 창문 하나에서 스비드리가일로프가 활짝 열린 창가의 찻상 앞에 앉아 입에 파이프를 문 채 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Raskolnikov was dreadfully taken aback, almost terrified.", "라스콜니코프는 끔찍하게 당황했고, 거의 겁에 질렸다."], ["Svidrigaïlov was silently watching and scrutinising him and, what struck Raskolnikov at once, seemed to be meaning to get up and slip away unobserved.", "스비드리가일로프는 말없이 그를 지켜보며 뜯어보고 있었는데, 라스콜니코프를 단번에 놀라게 한 것은, 그가 자리에서 일어나 들키지 않게 슬그머니 사라지려는 듯 보였다는 점이었다."], ["Raskolnikov at once pretended not to have seen him, but to be looking absent-mindedly away, while he watched him out of the corner of his eye.", "라스콜니코프는 곧장 그를 보지 못한 척, 멍하니 딴 데를 보는 척하면서 곁눈질로 그를 살폈다."], ["His heart was beating violently.", "그의 가슴은 격렬하게 뛰었다."], ["Yet, it was evident that Svidrigaïlov did not want to be seen.", "그러나 스비드리가일로프가 들키고 싶어 하지 않는다는 것은 분명했다."], ["He took the pipe out of his mouth and was on the point of concealing himself, but as he got up and moved back his chair, he seemed to have become suddenly aware that Raskolnikov had seen him, and was watching him.", "그는 입에서 파이프를 빼고 몸을 숨기려던 참이었으나, 자리에서 일어나 의자를 뒤로 밀치다가, 라스콜니코프가 자신을 보았고 또 지켜보고 있음을 갑자기 알아차린 듯했다."], ["What had passed between them was much the same as what happened at their first meeting in Raskolnikov’s room.", "두 사람 사이에 오간 것은 라스콜니코프의 방에서 처음 만났을 때 일어났던 일과 거의 같았다."], ["A sly smile came into Svidrigaïlov’s face and grew broader and broader.", "스비드리가일로프의 얼굴에 교활한 미소가 떠올라 점점 더 크게 번져 갔다."], ["Each knew that he was seen and watched by the other.", "두 사람은 저마다 자신이 상대에게 들켰고 또 지켜보이고 있음을 알았다."], ["At last Svidrigaïlov broke into a loud laugh.", "마침내 스비드리가일로프가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Well, well, come in if you want me; I am here!”", "\"이런, 이런, 내게 볼일이 있거든 들어오시구려."], ["he shouted from the window.", "나 여기 있소!\" 그가 창에서 소리쳤다."]], [["Raskolnikov went up into the tavern.", "라스콜니코프는 선술집으로 올라갔다."], ["He found Svidrigaïlov in a tiny back room, adjoining the saloon in which merchants, clerks and numbers of people of all sorts were drinking tea at twenty little tables to the desperate bawling of a chorus of singers.", "그는 스비드리가일로프를 어느 자그마한 안쪽 방에서 찾았는데, 그 방은 상인이며 점원이며 온갖 부류의 사람들이 합창단의 필사적인 고함 소리에 맞춰 스무 개쯤 되는 작은 탁자에 둘러앉아 차를 마시고 있는 큰 홀에 붙어 있었다."], ["The click of billiard balls could be heard in the distance.", "멀리서 당구공 부딪치는 소리가 들렸다."], ["On the table before Svidrigaïlov stood an open bottle and a glass half full of champagne.", "스비드리가일로프 앞 탁자에는 마개를 딴 병 하나와 샴페인이 반쯤 찬 잔 하나가 놓여 있었다."], ["In the room he found also a boy with a little hand organ, a healthy-looking red-cheeked girl of eighteen, wearing a tucked-up striped skirt, and a Tyrolese hat with ribbons.", "방 안에는 또 작은 손풍금을 든 소년 하나와, 걷어 올린 줄무늬 치마에 리본 달린 티롤식 모자를 쓴, 건강해 보이고 볼이 발그레한 열여덟 살가량의 처녀가 있었다."], ["In spite of the chorus in the other room, she was singing some servants’ hall song in a rather husky contralto, to the accompaniment of the organ.", "옆방의 합창에도 아랑곳없이, 그녀는 풍금 반주에 맞춰 다소 쉰 듯한 콘트랄토로 어떤 하인들이나 부를 법한 노래를 부르고 있었다."]], [["“Come, that’s enough,”", "\"자, 그만하면 됐다.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov stopped her at Raskolnikov’s entrance.", "라스콜니코프가 들어서자 스비드리가일로프가 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다."], ["The girl at once broke off and stood waiting respectfully.", "처녀는 곧바로 노래를 그치고 공손하게 서서 기다렸다."], ["She had sung her guttural rhymes, too, with a serious and respectful expression in her face.", "그녀는 그 목쉰 가락의 노래마저 진지하고 공손한 표정으로 부르고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Hey, Philip, a glass!”", "\"이봐, 필립, 잔 하나 가져와!\""], ["shouted Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프가 소리쳤다."]], [["“I won’t drink anything,”", "\"저는 아무것도 안 마십니다.\""], ["said Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 말했다."]], [["“As you like, I didn’t mean it for you.", "\"좋을 대로 하시오, 댁더러 마시라는 게 아니니까."], ["Drink, Katia!", "마셔라, 카탸!"], ["I don’t want anything more to-day, you can go.”", "오늘은 더 필요한 게 없으니 가도 좋다.\""], ["He poured her out a full glass, and laid down a yellow note.", "그는 그녀에게 잔을 가득 따라 주고는 노란 지폐 한 장을 내려놓았다."]], [["Katia drank off her glass of wine, as women do, without putting it down, in twenty gulps, took the note and kissed Svidrigaïlov’s hand, which he allowed quite seriously.", "카탸는 여자들이 으레 그러듯 잔을 내려놓지도 않은 채 스무 모금에 와인을 단숨에 비우고는, 지폐를 받아 들고 스비드리가일로프의 손에 입을 맞추었는데, 그는 자못 진지하게 그것을 받아들였다."], ["She went out of the room and the boy trailed after her with the organ.", "그녀가 방에서 나가자 소년도 풍금을 끌며 그 뒤를 따라갔다."], ["Both had been brought in from the street.", "둘 다 거리에서 불러들인 자들이었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov had not been a week in Petersburg, but everything about him was already, so to speak, on a patriarchal footing; the waiter, Philip, was by now an old friend and very obsequious.", "스비드리가일로프는 페테르부르크에 온 지 일주일도 채 안 되었지만, 그를 둘러싼 모든 것은 이미, 말하자면 가부장적인 기반 위에 놓여 있었다. 웨이터인 필립은 어느새 오랜 친구처럼 굴며 무척 비굴하게 알랑거렸다."]], [["The door leading to the saloon had a lock on it.", "홀로 통하는 문에는 자물쇠가 달려 있었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov was at home in this room and perhaps spent whole days in it.", "스비드리가일로프는 이 방에서 제집처럼 편안히 지냈고, 어쩌면 온종일 이 안에서 보내는지도 몰랐다."], ["The tavern was dirty and wretched, not even second-rate.", "그 선술집은 더럽고 누추했으며, 이류 축에도 못 끼었다."]], [["“I was going to see you and looking for you,”", "\"당신을 만나러 가서 찾던 참이었소.\""], ["Raskolnikov began, “but I don’t know what made me turn from the Hay Market into the X.", "라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다. \"그런데 무엇 때문인지 지금 막 건초 시장에서 X 거리 쪽으로 꺾어 들고 말았소."], ["Prospect just now. I never take this turning.", "나는 이 길로는 한 번도 꺾어 든 적이 없는데."], ["I turn to the right from the Hay Market.", "늘 건초 시장에서 오른쪽으로 도는데 말이오."], ["And this isn’t the way to you.", "게다가 이 길은 당신에게 가는 길도 아니고."], ["I simply turned and here you are.", "그저 무심코 꺾어 들었더니 당신이 여기 있었던 거요."], ["It is strange!”", "이상한 일이군!\""]], [["“Why don’t you say at once ‘it’s a miracle’?”", "\"왜 단번에 '기적이다'라고 말하지 않으시오?\""]], [["“Because it may be only chance.”", "\"그저 우연일 수도 있으니까.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s the way with all you folk,”", "\"오, 댁 같은 사람들은 다 그렇지.\""], ["laughed Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프가 웃었다."], ["“You won’t admit it, even if you do inwardly believe it a miracle!", "\"속으로는 기적이라고 믿으면서도 절대 인정하려 들지 않거든!"], ["Here you say that it may be only chance.", "여기서는 우연일 수도 있다고 말하고 말이오."], ["And what cowards they all are here, about having an opinion of their own, you can’t fancy, Rodion Romanovitch.", "한데 여기 사람들이 자기 나름의 의견을 갖는 일에 얼마나 겁쟁이인지, 댁은 상상도 못 할 거요, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["I don’t mean you, you have an opinion of your own and are not afraid to have it.", "댁 얘기가 아니오, 댁은 자기 나름의 의견을 갖고 있고 또 그걸 가지길 두려워하지 않으니까."], ["That’s how it was you attracted my curiosity.”", "바로 그 점이 내 호기심을 끌었던 거요.\""]], [["“Nothing else?”", "\"그것뿐이오?\""]], [["“Well, that’s enough, you know,”", "\"뭐, 그거면 충분하지 않소.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov was obviously exhilarated, but only slightly so, he had not had more than half a glass of wine.", "스비드리가일로프는 보아하니 들떠 있었지만, 그저 약간 그럴 뿐이었다. 그는 와인을 반 잔도 채 마시지 않았으니까."]], [["“I fancy you came to see me before you knew that I was capable of having what you call an opinion of my own,”", "\"내가 당신이 말하는 그 자기 나름의 의견이라는 걸 가질 수 있는 인간임을 당신이 알기도 전에, 당신은 나를 만나러 왔던 것 같은데.\""], ["observed Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 지적했다."]], [["“Oh, well, it was a different matter.", "\"오, 뭐, 그건 별개의 문제요."], ["Everyone has his own plans.", "누구나 제 나름의 속셈이 있는 법이니까."], ["And apropos of the miracle let me tell you that I think you have been asleep for the last two or three days.", "그리고 기적 얘기가 나온 김에 한마디 하자면, 내 생각에 댁은 지난 이삼 일을 잠에 취해 보낸 것 같소."], ["I told you of this tavern myself, there is no miracle in your coming straight here.", "이 선술집은 내가 직접 댁한테 일러 줬으니, 댁이 곧장 여기로 온 데에는 아무 기적도 없는 거요."], ["I explained the way myself, told you where it was, and the hours you could find me here.", "가는 길도 내가 직접 설명해 주었고, 여기가 어딘지, 또 몇 시에 나를 여기서 만날 수 있는지도 말해 줬소."], ["Do you remember?”", "기억나시오?\""]], [["“I don’t remember,”", "\"기억나지 않는데.\""], ["answered Raskolnikov with surprise.", "라스콜니코프가 놀라며 대답했다."]], [["“I believe you.", "\"댁 말을 믿겠소."], ["I told you twice.", "내가 두 번이나 말했는데."], ["The address has been stamped mechanically on your memory.", "그 주소가 댁의 기억 속에 기계적으로 새겨진 거요."], ["You turned this way mechanically and yet precisely according to the direction, though you are not aware of it.", "댁은 기계적으로 이 길로 꺾어 들었으면서도, 정작 자신은 알지 못한 채 정확히 그 방향대로 온 셈이지."], ["When I told you then, I hardly hoped you understood me.", "그때 내가 말했을 때, 댁이 내 말을 알아들었으리라곤 거의 기대하지 않았소."], ["You give yourself away too much, Rodion Romanovitch.", "댁은 자기 속을 너무 많이 드러낸단 말이오, 로디온 로마노비치."], ["And another thing, I’m convinced there are lots of people in Petersburg who talk to themselves as they walk.", "그리고 또 한 가지, 나는 페테르부르크에 길을 걸으면서 혼잣말을 하는 사람이 수두룩하다고 확신하오."], ["This is a town of crazy people.", "여기는 미친 사람들의 도시요."], ["If only we had scientific men, doctors, lawyers and philosophers might make most valuable investigations in Petersburg each in his own line.", "만일 우리에게 학자들만 있다면, 의사며 법률가며 철학자들이 페테르부르크에서 저마다 제 분야의 더없이 값진 연구를 해낼 수 있을 텐데."], ["There are few places where there are so many gloomy, strong and queer influences on the soul of man as in Petersburg.", "페테르부르크만큼 인간의 영혼에 그토록 음울하고 강렬하고 기이한 영향을 끼치는 곳도 드물지."], ["The mere influences of climate mean so much.", "기후의 영향만 해도 얼마나 큰지 모르오."], ["And it’s the administrative centre of all Russia and its character must be reflected on the whole country.", "게다가 여기는 온 러시아의 행정 중심지이니, 그 성격이 온 나라에 반영될 수밖에 없소."], ["But that is neither here nor there now.", "하지만 지금 그건 여기서 따질 문제가 아니지."], ["The point is that I have several times watched you.", "요점은 내가 여러 번 댁을 지켜봤다는 거요."], ["You walk out of your house--holding your head high--twenty paces from home you let it sink, and fold your hands behind your back.", "댁은 집을 나설 때면 — 고개를 빳빳이 쳐들고 — 집에서 스무 걸음쯤 가다가 고개를 떨구고 두 손을 등 뒤로 모으지."], ["You look and evidently see nothing before nor beside you.", "댁은 바라보면서도 분명 앞도 옆도 아무것도 못 보고 있소."], ["At last you begin moving your lips and talking to yourself, and sometimes you wave one hand and declaim, and at last stand still in the middle of the road.", "마침내 입술을 움직이며 혼잣말을 하기 시작하고, 때로는 한 손을 휘저으며 무언가를 읊조리다가, 끝내는 길 한복판에 우뚝 멈춰 서기도 하지."], ["That’s not at all the thing.", "그건 전혀 좋은 일이 아니오."], ["Someone may be watching you besides me, and it won’t do you any good.", "나 말고 또 누군가 댁을 지켜보고 있을지도 모르고, 그게 댁한테 이로울 게 없으니까."], ["It’s nothing really to do with me and I can’t cure you, but, of course, you understand me.”", "사실 나하곤 아무 상관도 없고, 나는 댁을 고쳐 줄 수도 없지만, 물론 댁은 내 말을 알아듣겠지.\""]], [["“Do you know that I am being followed?”", "\"내가 미행당하고 있는 걸 당신이 아시오?\""], ["asked Raskolnikov, looking inquisitively at him.", "라스콜니코프가 캐묻듯 그를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“No, I know nothing about it,”", "\"아니, 그런 건 전혀 모르오.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov, seeming surprised.", "스비드리가일로프가 놀란 듯 말했다."]], [["“Well, then, let us leave me alone,”", "\"그럼 좋소, 내 얘긴 그만둡시다.\""], ["Raskolnikov muttered, frowning.", "라스콜니코프가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Very good, let us leave you alone.”", "\"좋소, 댁 얘긴 그만두지.\""]], [["“You had better tell me, if you come here to drink, and directed me twice to come here to you, why did you hide, and try to get away just now when I looked at the window from the street?", "\"그보다 말해 보시오, 당신이 여기 술을 마시러 오는 거고, 내게 두 번이나 여기로 당신을 찾아오라고 일러 줬다면, 방금 내가 거리에서 창문을 올려다봤을 때 왜 몸을 숨기고 빠져나가려 했던 거요?"], ["I saw it.”", "내 눈으로 똑똑히 봤소.\""]], [["“He-he!", "\"헤헤!"], ["And why was it you lay on your sofa with closed eyes and pretended to be asleep, though you were wide awake while I stood in your doorway?", "그러는 댁은 왜 내가 댁의 방문간에 서 있는 동안, 멀쩡히 깨어 있으면서도 눈을 감고 소파에 누워 자는 척했던 거요?"], ["I saw it.”", "내 눈으로 똑똑히 봤소.\""]], [["“I may have had...", "\"내겐…… 그럴 만한 이유가 있었을 수도 있지."], ["reasons. You know that yourself.”", "그건 당신 자신이 알 거요.\""]], [["“And I may have had my reasons, though you don’t know them.”", "\"나 역시 내 나름의 이유가 있었을 수도 있지, 비록 댁은 모르겠지만.\""]], [["Raskolnikov dropped his right elbow on the table, leaned his chin in the fingers of his right hand, and stared intently at Svidrigaïlov.", "라스콜니코프는 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괴고 오른손 손가락에 턱을 받친 채, 스비드리가일로프를 뚫어지게 응시했다."], ["For a full minute he scrutinised his face, which had impressed him before.", "그는 꼬박 일 분 동안, 전에도 자신에게 인상 깊었던 그의 얼굴을 뜯어보았다."], ["It was a strange face, like a mask; white and red, with bright red lips, with a flaxen beard, and still thick flaxen hair.", "그것은 가면처럼 기이한 얼굴이었다. 희고도 붉었으며, 새빨간 입술에, 아마빛 턱수염과 아직도 숱 많은 아마빛 머리칼을 지니고 있었다."], ["His eyes were somehow too blue and their expression somehow too heavy and fixed.", "그의 눈은 어딘가 지나치게 푸르렀고, 그 표정은 어딘가 지나치게 무겁고 굳어 있었다."], ["There was something awfully unpleasant in that handsome face, which looked so wonderfully young for his age.", "나이에 비해 놀랍도록 젊어 보이는 그 잘생긴 얼굴에는 무언가 끔찍하게 불쾌한 데가 있었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov was smartly dressed in light summer clothes and was particularly dainty in his linen.", "스비드리가일로프는 가벼운 여름옷을 맵시 있게 차려입었고, 특히 셔츠가 유난히 깔끔했다."], ["He wore a huge ring with a precious stone in it.", "그는 보석이 박힌 커다란 반지를 끼고 있었다."]], [["“Have I got to bother myself about you, too, now?”", "\"이제 나는 당신 일까지 신경 써야 하는 거요?\""], ["said Raskolnikov suddenly, coming with nervous impatience straight to the point.", "라스콜니코프가 신경질적인 조급함으로 곧장 본론으로 들어가며 불쑥 말했다."], ["“Even though perhaps you are the most dangerous man if you care to injure me, I don’t want to put myself out any more.", "\"당신이 나를 해치려 들기만 하면 어쩌면 가장 위험한 인간일지도 모르지만, 나는 더 이상 나를 괴롭히고 싶지 않소."], ["I will show you at once that I don’t prize myself as you probably think I do.", "당신이 짐작하는 것만큼 내가 나 자신을 소중히 여기지 않는다는 걸 당장 보여 주겠소."], ["I’ve come to tell you at once that if you keep to your former intentions with regard to my sister and if you think to derive any benefit in that direction from what has been discovered of late, I will kill you before you get me locked up.", "내가 단도직입적으로 말하러 온 거요. 만일 당신이 내 누이에 관해 예전의 의도를 그대로 품고 있다면, 그리고 요사이 밝혀진 일에서 그쪽으로 무슨 이득을 끌어내려 한다면, 당신이 나를 쇠고랑 채우기 전에 내가 당신을 죽여 버리겠소."], ["You can reckon on my word.", "내 말을 믿어도 좋소."], ["You know that I can keep it.", "내가 그 말을 지킬 수 있는 인간임을 당신도 알 테니까."], ["And in the second place if you want to tell me anything--for I keep fancying all this time that you have something to tell me--make haste and tell it, for time is precious and very likely it will soon be too late.”", "그리고 둘째로, 만일 당신이 내게 무슨 할 말이 있다면 — 줄곧 당신이 내게 뭔가 할 말이 있는 것 같은 느낌이 들었으니 — 어서 말하시오, 시간이 귀하고, 자칫하면 곧 너무 늦어 버릴 테니까.\""]], [["“Why in such haste?”", "\"왜 그리 서두르시오?\""], ["asked Svidrigaïlov, looking at him curiously.", "스비드리가일로프가 호기심 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“Everyone has his plans,”", "\"누구나 제 나름의 속셈이 있는 법이오.\""], ["Raskolnikov answered gloomily and impatiently.", "라스콜니코프가 음울하고 조급하게 대답했다."]], [["“You urged me yourself to frankness just now, and at the first question you refuse to answer,”", "\"방금 댁이 직접 내게 솔직하라고 다그쳐 놓고, 첫 질문부터 대답을 거부하시는구려.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov observed with a smile.", "스비드리가일로프가 미소를 지으며 지적했다."], ["“You keep fancying that I have aims of my own and so you look at me with suspicion.", "\"댁은 내가 무슨 꿍꿍이를 품고 있다고 자꾸만 생각하니까 의심의 눈초리로 나를 바라보는 거요."], ["Of course it’s perfectly natural in your position.", "물론 댁의 처지에서는 지극히 자연스러운 일이지."], ["But though I should like to be friends with you, I shan’t trouble myself to convince you of the contrary.", "하지만 내가 댁과 친구가 되고 싶다 해도, 굳이 그 반대를 증명하느라 애쓰진 않겠소."], ["The game isn’t worth the candle and I wasn’t intending to talk to you about anything special.”", "그럴 만한 가치도 없거니와, 나는 댁한테 무슨 특별한 얘기를 하려던 것도 아니었으니까.\""]], [["“What did you want me for, then?", "\"그럼 도대체 무엇 때문에 나를 찾았던 거요?"], ["It was you who came hanging about me.”", "내 주위를 어슬렁거린 건 당신 쪽이었는데.\""]], [["“Why, simply as an interesting subject for observation.", "\"뭐, 그저 흥미로운 관찰 대상이라서."], ["I liked the fantastic nature of your position--that’s what it was!", "댁이 처한 처지의 그 환상적인 성격이 마음에 들었던 거요 — 바로 그거였소!"], ["Besides, you are the brother of a person who greatly interested me, and from that person I had in the past heard a very great deal about you, from which I gathered that you had a great influence over her; isn’t that enough?", "게다가 댁은 내 흥미를 무척 돋우었던 어떤 사람의 오라비이고, 그 사람한테서 나는 예전에 댁에 관해 아주 많은 얘기를 들었는데, 그걸로 미루어 댁이 그 사람한테 큰 영향력을 갖고 있다는 걸 알았소. 그만하면 충분하지 않소?"], ["Ha-ha-ha!", "하하하!"], ["Still I must admit that your question is rather complex, and is difficult for me to answer.", "그래도 인정하건대 댁의 질문은 꽤 복잡해서 내가 대답하기 어렵소."], ["Here, you, for instance, have come to me not only for a definite object, but for the sake of hearing something new.", "자, 가령 댁은 내게 뚜렷한 목적이 있어서만이 아니라, 무언가 새로운 얘기를 들으려고 온 거요. 안 그렇소?"], ["Isn’t that so?", "안 그렇소?\""], ["Isn’t that so?” persisted Svidrigaïlov with a sly smile.", "스비드리가일로프가 교활한 미소를 지으며 집요하게 물었다."], ["“Well, can’t you fancy then that I, too, on my way here in the train was reckoning on you, on your telling me something new, and on my making some profit out of you!", "\"그렇다면 댁은 나 역시 여기 오는 기차 안에서 댁을 셈에 넣고 있었다고는, 댁이 내게 뭔가 새로운 얘기를 해 주고, 내가 댁한테서 무슨 이득을 좀 보리라고 기대했다고는 상상도 못 하겠소?"], ["You see what rich men we are!”", "우리가 얼마나 부유한 인간들인지 알겠소!\""]], [["“What profit could you make?”", "\"당신이 무슨 이득을 볼 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“How can I tell you?", "\"내가 어찌 말하겠소?"], ["How do I know?", "내가 어찌 알겠소?"], ["You see in what a tavern I spend all my time and it’s my enjoyment, that’s to say it’s no great enjoyment, but one must sit somewhere; that poor Katia now--you saw her?...", "보다시피 나는 이런 선술집에서 온종일을 보내고, 그게 내 낙이오. 그러니까 대단한 낙은 아니지만, 어딘가에는 앉아 있어야 하니까."], ["If only I had been a glutton now, a club gourmand, but you see I can eat this.”", "저 가엾은 카탸만 해도 — 댁도 봤지?…… 차라리 내가 대식가라면, 미식가 클럽의 한량이라면 모를까, 보다시피 나는 이런 걸 먹을 수 있는 사람이오.\""]], [["He pointed to a little table in the corner where the remnants of a terrible-looking beef-steak and potatoes lay on a tin dish.", "그는 구석에 놓인 작은 탁자를 가리켰는데, 거기 양철 접시 위에는 끔찍해 보이는 비프스테이크와 감자 찌꺼기가 남아 있었다."]], [["“Have you dined, by the way?", "\"그건 그렇고, 식사는 했소?"], ["I’ve had something and want nothing more.", "나는 뭘 좀 먹어서 더는 아무것도 필요 없소."], ["I don’t drink, for instance, at all.", "가령 나는 술을 전혀 안 마시오."], ["Except for champagne I never touch anything, and not more than a glass of that all the evening, and even that is enough to make my head ache.", "샴페인 말고는 아무것도 입에 대지 않고, 그것도 저녁 내내 한 잔 이상은 안 마시는데, 그것마저 머리가 아파질 정도라오."], ["I ordered it just now to wind myself up, for I am just going off somewhere and you see me in a peculiar state of mind.", "방금 이걸 시킨 건 기운을 좀 돋우려는 거였소, 이제 막 어디로 갈 참인 데다 댁이 보다시피 내 심사가 야릇한 상태라서."], ["That was why I hid myself just now like a schoolboy, for I was afraid you would hinder me.", "그래서 아까 학생 녀석처럼 몸을 숨겼던 거요, 댁이 나를 방해할까 봐 겁이 났거든."], ["But I believe,”", "하지만 내 생각엔,\""], ["he pulled out his watch, “I can spend an hour with you.", "그는 회중시계를 꺼냈다, \"댁과 한 시간쯤은 보낼 수 있을 것 같소."], ["It’s half-past four now.", "지금 네 시 반이오."], ["If only I’d been something, a landowner, a father, a cavalry officer, a photographer, a journalist...", "만일 내가 뭐라도 됐더라면, 지주든, 한 집안의 가장이든, 기병 장교든, 사진사든, 신문기자든……"], ["I am nothing, no specialty, and sometimes I am positively bored.", "나는 아무것도 아니오, 아무 전문 분야도 없고, 그래서 때로는 정말이지 따분해 죽을 지경이오."], ["I really thought you would tell me something new.”", "정말이지 댁이 내게 무슨 새로운 얘기를 해 주리라 생각했는데.\""]], [["“But what are you, and why have you come here?”", "\"하지만 당신은 도대체 뭐 하는 사람이고, 무엇 때문에 여기 온 거요?\""]], [["“What am I?", "\"내가 뭐 하는 사람이냐고?"], ["You know, a gentleman, I served for two years in the cavalry, then I knocked about here in Petersburg, then I married Marfa Petrovna and lived in the country.", "알다시피 신사지, 기병대에서 이 년 복무했고, 그다음엔 여기 페테르부르크에서 빈둥거렸고, 그러다 마르파 페트로브나와 결혼해서 시골에서 살았소."], ["There you have my biography!”", "자, 이게 내 약력이오!\""]], [["“You are a gambler, I believe?”", "\"당신은 노름꾼이지, 아마?\""]], [["“No, a poor sort of gambler.", "\"아니, 변변찮은 노름꾼이오."], ["A card-sharper--not a gambler.”", "야바위꾼이지 — 노름꾼이 아니라.\""]], [["“You have been a card-sharper then?”", "\"그럼 야바위꾼 노릇을 했단 말이오?\""]], [["“Yes, I’ve been a card-sharper too.”", "\"그렇소, 야바위도 했었지.\""]], [["“Didn’t you get thrashed sometimes?”", "\"그러다 가끔 두들겨 맞기도 했소?\""]], [["“It did happen.", "\"그런 적도 있었지."], ["Why?”", "왜 그러시오?\""]], [["“Why, you might have challenged them...", "\"뭐, 그자들한테 결투를 신청할 수도 있었을 테고……"], ["altogether it must have been lively.”", "어쨌든 꽤나 활기찼겠군.\""]], [["“I won’t contradict you, and besides I am no hand at philosophy.", "\"댁 말을 반박하진 않겠소, 게다가 나는 철학엔 영 소질이 없으니."], ["I confess that I hastened here for the sake of the women.”", "고백하건대 나는 여자들 때문에 이곳으로 서둘러 왔소.\""]], [["“As soon as you buried Marfa Petrovna?”", "\"마르파 페트로브나를 땅에 묻자마자?\""]], [["“Quite so,”", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov smiled with engaging candour.", "스비드리가일로프가 사람을 끌어당기는 솔직함으로 미소 지었다."], ["“What of it?", "\"그게 어쨌단 말이오?"], ["You seem to find something wrong in my speaking like that about women?”", "내가 여자들에 대해 그렇게 말하는 데에서 댁은 뭔가 잘못된 점을 찾는 것 같은데?\""]], [["“You ask whether I find anything wrong in vice?”", "\"내가 악덕에서 뭔가 잘못된 점을 찾느냐고 묻는 거요?\""]], [["“Vice!", "\"악덕!"], ["Oh, that’s what you are after!", "오, 그게 댁의 관심사로군!"], ["But I’ll answer you in order, first about women in general; you know I am fond of talking.", "하지만 차근차근 대답해 주겠소, 우선 여자 일반에 관해서 말이지. 알다시피 나는 떠드는 걸 좋아하오."], ["Tell me, what should I restrain myself for?", "말해 보시오, 내가 무엇 때문에 자제해야 하오?"], ["Why should I give up women, since I have a passion for them?", "여자들에게 열정을 품고 있는데, 무엇하러 그들을 단념해야 하오?"], ["It’s an occupation, anyway.”", "어쨌든 그건 하나의 소일거리니까.\""]], [["“So you hope for nothing here but vice?”", "\"그럼 당신은 여기서 악덕 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않는단 말이오?\""]], [["“Oh, very well, for vice then.", "\"오, 좋소, 그럼 악덕이라 칩시다."], ["You insist on its being vice.", "댁이 그걸 악덕이라 우기니까."], ["But anyway I like a direct question.", "하지만 어쨌든 나는 단도직입적인 질문이 좋소."], ["In this vice at least there is something permanent, founded indeed upon nature and not dependent on fantasy, something present in the blood like an ever-burning ember, for ever setting one on fire and, maybe, not to be quickly extinguished, even with years.", "적어도 이 악덕에는 무언가 영속적인 것이, 실로 본성에 뿌리박혀 환상에 좌우되지 않는 무언가가 있단 말이오, 피 속에 영원히 타오르는 불씨처럼 깃들어, 언제까지나 사람을 불태우며, 어쩌면 세월이 흘러도 쉽사리 꺼지지 않을 무언가가."], ["You’ll agree it’s an occupation of a sort.”", "그것도 일종의 소일거리임은 댁도 인정할 거요.\""]], [["“That’s nothing to rejoice at, it’s a disease and a dangerous one.”", "\"기뻐할 일이 못 되오, 그건 병이고, 그것도 위험한 병이오.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s what you think, is it!", "\"오, 댁은 그렇게 생각하는군!"], ["I agree, that it is a disease like everything that exceeds moderation.", "인정하오, 그게 절도를 넘어서는 모든 것이 그러하듯 병이라는 걸."], ["And, of course, in this one must exceed moderation.", "그리고 물론 이 일에선 절도를 넘어설 수밖에 없지."], ["But in the first place, everybody does so in one way or another, and in the second place, of course, one ought to be moderate and prudent, however mean it may be, but what am I to do?", "하지만 첫째로, 누구나 이런저런 방식으로 그러는 법이고, 둘째로, 물론 아무리 비루하더라도 절제하고 신중해야 하겠지만, 그런데 나더러 어쩌란 말이오?"], ["If I hadn’t this, I might have to shoot myself.", "만일 내게 이게 없다면, 나는 차라리 총으로 자살이라도 해야 할 거요."], ["I am ready to admit that a decent man ought to put up with being bored, but yet...”", "점잖은 사람은 따분함을 견뎌야 한다는 걸 나도 기꺼이 인정하지만, 그래도…….\""]], [["“And could you shoot yourself?”", "\"그럼 당신은 자살할 수도 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“Oh, come!”", "\"오, 그만!\""], ["Svidrigaïlov parried with disgust.", "스비드리가일로프가 혐오스럽다는 듯 받아쳤다."], ["“Please don’t speak of it,”", "\"제발 그 얘긴 하지 마시오.\""], ["he added hurriedly and with none of the bragging tone he had shown in all the previous conversation.", "그는 서둘러, 앞서 대화 내내 보였던 그 허세 어린 어조 따위는 전혀 없이 덧붙였다."], ["His face quite changed.", "그의 얼굴빛이 완전히 변했다."], ["“I admit it’s an unpardonable weakness, but I can’t help it.", "\"용서받지 못할 약점임은 인정하지만, 나로서도 어쩔 수가 없소."], ["I am afraid of death and I dislike its being talked of.", "나는 죽음이 두렵고, 그게 입에 오르는 게 싫소."], ["Do you know that I am to a certain extent a mystic?”", "내가 어느 정도는 신비주의자라는 걸 아시오?\""]], [["“Ah, the apparitions of Marfa Petrovna!", "\"아, 마르파 페트로브나의 유령 말이오!"], ["Do they still go on visiting you?”", "그게 아직도 당신을 찾아오고 있소?\""]], [["“Oh, don’t talk of them; there have been no more in Petersburg, confound them!”", "\"오, 그것들 얘긴 하지 마시오, 페테르부르크에선 더 이상 나타나지 않았으니까, 빌어먹을 것들!\""], ["he cried with an air of irritation.", "그가 짜증스러운 기색으로 외쳤다."], ["“Let’s rather talk of that...", "\"차라리 그 얘기나……"], ["though...", "하지만……"], ["H’m!", "흠!"], ["I have not much time, and can’t stay long with you, it’s a pity!", "시간이 많지 않아 댁과 오래 있을 수 없소, 안타깝군!"], ["I should have found plenty to tell you.”", "댁한테 들려줄 얘기가 잔뜩 있었는데.\""]], [["“What’s your engagement, a woman?”", "\"무슨 약속이오, 여자요?\""]], [["“Yes, a woman, a casual incident....", "\"그렇소, 여자, 어쩌다 생긴 일이라……"], ["No, that’s not what I want to talk of.”", "아니, 내가 하려던 얘긴 그게 아니오.\""]], [["“And the hideousness, the filthiness of all your surroundings, doesn’t that affect you?", "\"그럼 당신을 둘러싼 이 모든 것의 추악함, 그 더러움이 당신을 괴롭히지 않소?"], ["Have you lost the strength to stop yourself?”", "당신은 스스로를 멈출 힘을 잃어버린 거요?\""]], [["“And do you pretend to strength, too?", "\"그러는 댁은 무슨 힘이라도 있는 척하시는 거요?"], ["He-he-he!", "헤헤헤!"], ["You surprised me just now, Rodion Romanovitch, though I knew beforehand it would be so.", "댁은 방금 나를 놀라게 했소, 로디온 로마노비치, 비록 그러리라고 미리 알고는 있었지만."], ["You preach to me about vice and æsthetics!", "댁이 내게 악덕이며 미학을 설교하다니!"], ["You--a Schiller, you--an idealist!", "댁이 — 실러라니, 댁이 — 이상주의자라니!"], ["Of course that’s all as it should be and it would be surprising if it were not so, yet it is strange in reality....", "물론 다 마땅히 그래야 할 일이고, 그렇지 않다면 오히려 놀라운 일이겠지만, 그래도 실제로 보니 기이하군……"], ["Ah, what a pity I have no time, for you’re a most interesting type!", "아, 시간이 없어 정말 안타깝소, 댁은 더없이 흥미로운 인간형이니까!"], ["And, by-the-way, are you fond of Schiller?", "그건 그렇고, 댁은 실러를 좋아하시오?"], ["I am awfully fond of him.”", "나는 그를 끔찍이도 좋아한다오.\""]], [["“But what a braggart you are,”", "\"당신은 정말 허풍쟁이로군.\""], ["Raskolnikov said with some disgust.", "라스콜니코프가 다소 혐오스럽다는 듯 말했다."]], [["“Upon my word, I am not,”", "\"맹세컨대 나는 그렇지 않소.\""], ["answered Svidrigaïlov laughing.", "스비드리가일로프가 웃으며 대답했다."], ["“However, I won’t dispute it, let me be a braggart, why not brag, if it hurts no one?", "\"하지만 굳이 다투진 않겠소, 그래, 내가 허풍쟁이라 칩시다, 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않는다면 허풍을 못 떨 이유가 어디 있겠소?"], ["I spent seven years in the country with Marfa Petrovna, so now when I come across an intelligent person like you--intelligent and highly interesting--I am simply glad to talk and, besides, I’ve drunk that half-glass of champagne and it’s gone to my head a little.", "나는 마르파 페트로브나와 함께 시골에서 칠 년을 보냈으니, 이제 댁처럼 영리한 사람을 — 영리하고도 더없이 흥미로운 사람을 — 만나고 보니, 그저 떠드는 게 즐거울 따름이오, 게다가 그 반 잔의 샴페인을 마셨더니 머리가 좀 핑 도는구려."], ["And besides, there’s a certain fact that has wound me up tremendously, but about that I...", "그리고 또, 나를 굉장히 흥분시킨 어떤 일이 하나 있는데, 그것에 관해서는 나……"], ["will keep quiet.", "입을 다물겠소."], ["Where are you off to?”", "어디 가시오?\""], ["he asked in alarm.", "그가 깜짝 놀라 물었다."]], [["Raskolnikov had begun getting up.", "라스콜니코프가 일어서기 시작했던 것이다."], ["He felt oppressed and stifled and, as it were, ill at ease at having come here.", "그는 답답하고 숨이 막혔으며, 어쩐지 여기 온 것이 영 불편하게 느껴졌다."], ["He felt convinced that Svidrigaïlov was the most worthless scoundrel on the face of the earth.", "그는 스비드리가일로프가 이 세상에서 가장 하찮은 악당이라고 확신했다."]], [["“A-ach!", "\"아아!"], ["Sit down, stay a little!”", "앉으시오, 좀 더 있다 가시오!\""], ["Svidrigaïlov begged.", "스비드리가일로프가 간청했다."], ["“Let them bring you some tea, anyway.", "\"어쨌든 차라도 좀 가져오게 하리다."], ["Stay a little, I won’t talk nonsense, about myself, I mean.", "좀 더 있어 보시오, 헛소리는 안 하리다, 그러니까 내 얘기 말이오."], ["I’ll tell you something.", "댁한테 들려줄 게 있소."], ["If you like I’ll tell you how a woman tried ‘to save’ me, as you would call it?", "원한다면, 댁이라면 어떻게 말할까, 한 여자가 어떻게 나를 '구원'하려 했는지 들려주겠소."], ["It will be an answer to your first question indeed, for the woman was your sister.", "그건 정말이지 댁의 첫 질문에 대한 답이 될 거요, 그 여자가 바로 댁의 누이였으니까."], ["May I tell you?", "들려줘도 되겠소?"], ["It will help to spend the time.”", "시간 보내기에 도움이 될 거요.\""]], [["“Tell me, but I trust that you...”", "\"말해 보시오, 하지만 부디 당신이…….\""]], [["“Oh, don’t be uneasy.", "\"오, 불안해 마시오."], ["Besides, even in a worthless low fellow like me, Avdotya Romanovna can only excite the deepest respect.”", "게다가 나처럼 하찮고 천한 인간한테도 아브도탸 로마노브나는 그저 가장 깊은 존경심만을 불러일으킬 뿐이오.\""]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["“You know perhaps--yes, I told you myself,”", "\"아마 당신도 알 거요 — 그래, 내가 직접 말했지.\""], ["began Svidrigaïlov, “that I was in the debtors’ prison here, for an immense sum, and had not any expectation of being able to pay it.", "스비드리가일로프가 입을 열었다. \"내가 여기 채무자 감옥에 갇혀 있었다는 거 말이오, 엄청난 액수의 빚 때문에, 그걸 갚을 가망이라곤 전혀 없었지."], ["There’s no need to go into particulars how Marfa Petrovna bought me out; do you know to what a point of insanity a woman can sometimes love?", "마르파 페트로브나가 어떻게 나를 빼내 줬는지 시시콜콜 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠고. 여자가 때로 얼마나 미친 지경에까지 이르도록 사랑할 수 있는지 아시오?"], ["She was an honest woman, and very sensible, although completely uneducated.", "그 여자는 정직한 여자였고, 아주 분별 있는 사람이었소, 비록 교육이라곤 전혀 못 받았지만."], ["Would you believe that this honest and jealous woman, after many scenes of hysterics and reproaches, condescended to enter into a kind of contract with me which she kept throughout our married life?", "이 정직하고 질투심 많은 여자가, 숱한 히스테리와 비난의 소동 끝에, 나와 일종의 계약을 맺는 데까지 굽히고 들어왔다면 믿겠소? 그러고는 결혼 생활 내내 그걸 지켰단 말이오."], ["She was considerably older than I, and besides, she always kept a clove or something in her mouth.", "그 여자는 나보다 한참 연상이었고, 게다가 늘 입에 정향이나 뭐 그런 걸 물고 있었지. 내 영혼 속에는 돼지 같은 추잡함이 가득했지만, 또 나름의 정직함도 있어서, 그 여자한테 당신한테 절대적으로 충실할 수는 없다고 대놓고 말해 버렸소."], ["There was so much swinishness in my soul and honesty too, of a sort, as to tell her straight out that I couldn’t be absolutely faithful to her.", "이 고백에 그 여자는 미칠 듯이 날뛰었지만, 그래도 어쩐 일인지 내 짐승 같은 솔직함을 마음에 들어 한 모양이오. 이렇게 미리 경고하는 걸 보니 자기를 속일 마음은 없는 거라고 여긴 거지."], ["This confession drove her to frenzy, but yet she seems in a way to have liked my brutal frankness.", "알다시피 질투심 많은 여자한테는 그게 무엇보다 중요한 거니까. 숱한 눈물 끝에 우리 사이에 명문화되지 않은 계약이 맺어졌소."], ["She thought it showed I was unwilling to deceive her if I warned her like this beforehand and for a jealous woman, you know, that’s the first consideration.", "첫째, 나는 절대로 마르파 페트로브나를 떠나지 않으며 늘 그녀의 남편으로 남는다. 둘째, 나는 그녀의 허락 없이는 절대로 집을 비우지 않는다."], ["After many tears an unwritten contract was drawn up between us: first, that I would never leave Marfa Petrovna and would always be her husband; secondly, that I would never absent myself without her permission; thirdly, that I would never set up a permanent mistress; fourthly, in return for this, Marfa Petrovna gave me a free hand with the maidservants, but only with her secret knowledge; fifthly, God forbid my falling in love with a woman of our class; sixthly, in case I--which God forbid--should be visited by a great serious passion I was bound to reveal it to Marfa Petrovna.", "셋째, 나는 절대로 고정된 정부를 두지 않는다. 넷째, 그 대가로 마르파 페트로브나는 하녀들에 한해서는 나에게 자유를 허락하되, 오직 그녀가 은밀히 아는 한에서만 그렇게 한다."], ["On this last score, however, Marfa Petrovna was fairly at ease.", "다섯째, 우리 계급의 여자와 사랑에 빠지는 일은 결단코 안 된다."], ["She was a sensible woman and so she could not help looking upon me as a dissolute profligate incapable of real love.", "여섯째, 만에 하나 내가 — 천만에 그럴 리야 없겠지만 — 어떤 크고 진지한 정념에 사로잡힌다면, 나는 그것을 마르파 페트로브나에게 반드시 털어놓아야 한다. 하지만 이 마지막 조항에 관해서라면 마르파 페트로브나는 꽤나 마음이 놓였던 모양이오."], ["But a sensible woman and a jealous woman are two very different things, and that’s where the trouble came in.", "그녀는 분별 있는 여자였으니, 나를 진정한 사랑이라곤 할 줄 모르는 방탕한 난봉꾼으로 볼 수밖에 없었던 거지."], ["But to judge some people impartially we must renounce certain preconceived opinions and our habitual attitude to the ordinary people about us.", "그러나 분별 있는 여자와 질투심 많은 여자는 완전히 다른 두 가지요, 바로 거기서 골치 아픈 일이 생긴 거지. 하지만 어떤 사람들을 공정하게 판단하려면 우리는 어떤 선입견들과, 우리 주변의 평범한 사람들을 대하는 습관적인 태도를 버려야만 하오."], ["I have reason to have faith in your judgment rather than in anyone’s.", "나는 다른 누구보다도 당신의 판단을 믿을 만한 이유가 있소."], ["Perhaps you have already heard a great deal that was ridiculous and absurd about Marfa Petrovna.", "아마 당신은 이미 마르파 페트로브나에 관해 우스꽝스럽고 터무니없는 얘기를 잔뜩 들었을 거요."], ["She certainly had some very ridiculous ways, but I tell you frankly that I feel really sorry for the innumerable woes of which I was the cause.", "그 여자한테 분명 아주 우스꽝스러운 면들이 있긴 했지. 하지만 솔직히 말해서, 내가 원인이 됐던 그 헤아릴 수 없는 슬픔들에 대해 나는 정말로 마음이 아프오."], ["Well, and that’s enough, I think, by way of a decorous _oraison funèbre_ for the most tender wife of a most tender husband.", "자, 이만하면 더없이 다정한 남편의 더없이 다정한 아내를 위한 점잖은 추도사로는 충분할 것 같군."], ["When we quarrelled, I usually held my tongue and did not irritate her and that gentlemanly conduct rarely failed to attain its object, it influenced her, it pleased her, indeed.", "우리가 다툴 때면, 나는 대개 입을 다물고 그녀를 자극하지 않았는데, 그 신사다운 처신은 좀처럼 목적을 이루지 못하는 법이 없었소. 그게 그녀를 감화시켰고, 사실 그녀를 흡족하게 했지."], ["These were times when she was positively proud of me.", "그럴 때면 그녀는 정말로 나를 자랑스러워했소."], ["But your sister she couldn’t put up with, anyway.", "하지만 당신 누이만큼은 도무지 견뎌 내지 못했다오."], ["And however she came to risk taking such a beautiful creature into her house as a governess.", "도대체 그렇게 아름다운 여자를 가정교사로 자기 집에 들이는 모험을 어쩌자고 한 건지. 내 설명은 이렇소."], ["My explanation is that Marfa Petrovna was an ardent and impressionable woman and simply fell in love herself--literally fell in love--with your sister.", "마르파 페트로브나는 열정적이고 감수성이 예민한 여자라서, 그저 자기가 직접 사랑에 빠진 거요 — 문자 그대로 사랑에 빠진 거지 — 당신 누이한테 말이오."], ["Well, little wonder--look at Avdotya Romanovna!", "뭐, 놀랄 일도 아니지 — 아브도티야 로마노브나를 한번 보시오!"], ["I saw the danger at the first glance and what do you think, I resolved not to look at her even.", "나는 첫눈에 위험을 알아챘고, 그래서 어떻게 했는지 아시오, 그녀를 쳐다보지조차 않기로 결심했소."], ["But Avdotya Romanovna herself made the first step, would you believe it?", "그런데 아브도티야 로마노브나가 먼저 첫걸음을 내디뎠다면, 믿겠소?"], ["Would you believe it too that Marfa Petrovna was positively angry with me at first for my persistent silence about your sister, for my careless reception of her continual adoring praises of Avdotya Romanovna.", "또 이건 믿겠소? 마르파 페트로브나가 처음엔 내가 당신 누이에 대해 끈질기게 침묵하는 것 때문에, 아브도티야 로마노브나를 향한 끝없는 찬미의 칭송을 내가 시큰둥하게 받아넘기는 것 때문에 나한테 정말로 화를 냈단 말이오."], ["I don’t know what it was she wanted!", "도대체 그 여자가 뭘 원했던 건지 모르겠소!"], ["Well, of course, Marfa Petrovna told Avdotya Romanovna every detail about me.", "뭐, 물론 마르파 페트로브나는 아브도티야 로마노브나한테 나에 관한 시시콜콜한 것까지 다 얘기했소."], ["She had the unfortunate habit of telling literally everyone all our family secrets and continually complaining of me; how could she fail to confide in such a delightful new friend?", "그 여자한텐 우리 집안의 비밀이란 비밀은 문자 그대로 모두에게 떠벌리고 끊임없이 나에 대해 불평하는 불행한 버릇이 있었으니, 그렇게 사랑스러운 새 친구한테야 어찌 털어놓지 않았겠소?"], ["I expect they talked of nothing else but me and no doubt Avdotya Romanovna heard all those dark mysterious rumours that were current about me....", "짐작건대 그들은 나 말고는 다른 얘기는 하지도 않았을 테고, 의심할 바 없이 아브도티야 로마노브나는 나에 대해 떠도는 그 온갖 어둡고 음산한 소문들을 다 들었을 거요……."], ["I don’t mind betting that you too have heard something of the sort already?”", "당신도 이미 그런 비슷한 걸 들었을 거라고 내기를 걸어도 좋소만?\""]], [["“I have.", "\"들었소."], ["Luzhin charged you with having caused the death of a child.", "루진이 당신더러 어떤 아이를 죽음에 이르게 했다고 비난하더군."], ["Is that true?”", "그게 사실이오?\""]], [["“Don’t refer to those vulgar tales, I beg,”", "\"제발, 그런 천박한 얘기는 끄집어내지 마시오.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov with disgust and annoyance.", "스비드리가일로프가 혐오와 짜증을 드러내며 말했다."], ["“If you insist on wanting to know about all that idiocy, I will tell you one day, but now...”", "\"그 모든 멍청한 일에 대해 기어이 알고 싶다면, 언젠가 말해 주겠소, 하지만 지금은…….\""]], [["“I was told too about some footman of yours in the country whom you treated badly.”", "\"당신이 시골에서 어떤 하인을 학대했다는 얘기도 들었소.\""]], [["“I beg you to drop the subject,”", "\"제발 그 얘긴 그만둡시다.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov interrupted again with obvious impatience.", "스비드리가일로프가 또다시 노골적인 짜증을 내며 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Was that the footman who came to you after death to fill your pipe?...", "\"그게 죽은 뒤에 당신한테 와서 당신 파이프에 담배를 채워 줬다던 그 하인이오?……"], ["you told me about it yourself.”", "당신이 직접 나한테 얘기했잖소.\""], ["Raskolnikov felt more and more irritated.", "라스콜니코프는 점점 더 짜증이 치밀었다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov looked at him attentively and Raskolnikov fancied he caught a flash of spiteful mockery in that look.", "스비드리가일로프는 그를 유심히 바라보았고, 라스콜니코프는 그 시선 속에서 심술궂은 조롱의 번득임을 언뜻 잡아챈 듯한 기분이 들었다."], ["But Svidrigaïlov restrained himself and answered very civilly:", "그러나 스비드리가일로프는 자제하며 아주 정중하게 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, it was.", "\"그렇소, 바로 그 하인이오."], ["I see that you, too, are extremely interested and shall feel it my duty to satisfy your curiosity at the first opportunity.", "보아하니 당신도 무척 흥미가 동하는 모양이니, 기회가 닿는 대로 당신의 호기심을 채워 주는 게 내 의무라고 여기겠소."], ["Upon my soul!", "맹세컨대!"], ["I see that I really might pass for a romantic figure with some people.", "보아하니 내가 정말 어떤 사람들한테는 낭만적인 인물로 통할 수도 있겠군."], ["Judge how grateful I must be to Marfa Petrovna for having repeated to Avdotya Romanovna such mysterious and interesting gossip about me.", "마르파 페트로브나가 아브도티야 로마노브나한테 나에 관한 그토록 신비롭고 흥미로운 소문을 그대로 옮겨 준 것에 대해 내가 얼마나 고마워해야 할지 한번 헤아려 보시오."], ["I dare not guess what impression it made on her, but in any case it worked in my interests.", "그게 그녀한테 어떤 인상을 남겼을지는 감히 짐작도 못 하겠지만, 어쨌든 그건 내 이익에 보탬이 됐소."], ["With all Avdotya Romanovna’s natural aversion and in spite of my invariably gloomy and repellent aspect--she did at least feel pity for me, pity for a lost soul.", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 천성적으로 나를 혐오하고, 또 내가 변함없이 음울하고 거부감을 주는 인상이었음에도 불구하고 — 그녀는 적어도 나를 가엾게는 여겼소, 길 잃은 영혼을 향한 연민 말이오."], ["And if once a girl’s heart is moved to _pity_, it’s more dangerous than anything.", "그리고 처녀의 마음이 일단 연민으로 움직이고 나면, 그건 무엇보다 위험한 거요."], ["She is bound to want to ‘save him,’ to bring him to his senses, and lift him up and draw him to nobler aims, and restore him to new life and usefulness--well, we all know how far such dreams can go.", "그녀는 반드시 '그를 구원하고', 그를 정신 차리게 하고, 그를 일으켜 세워 더 고귀한 목표로 이끌고, 그를 새로운 삶과 쓸모 있는 존재로 되돌려 놓고 싶어 하게 마련이거든 — 뭐, 그런 꿈이 어디까지 갈 수 있는지야 우리 모두 알지 않소."], ["I saw at once that the bird was flying into the cage of herself.", "나는 그 새가 제 발로 새장 안으로 날아드는 걸 단박에 알아챘소."], ["And I too made ready.", "그래서 나도 만반의 준비를 했지."], ["I think you are frowning, Rodion Romanovitch?", "당신, 얼굴을 찌푸리는 것 같은데, 로지온 로마노비치?"], ["There’s no need.", "그럴 필요 없소."], ["As you know, it all ended in smoke.", "알다시피, 결국 다 물거품이 되고 말았으니까."], ["(Hang it all, what a lot I am drinking!) Do you know, I always, from the very beginning, regretted that it wasn’t your sister’s fate to be born in the second or third century A.D., as the daughter of a reigning prince or some governor or pro-consul in Asia Minor.", "(제기랄, 내가 술을 어지간히 마시는군!) 한데 말이오, 나는 처음부터 줄곧, 당신 누이가 서기 이삼 세기에, 군림하던 어느 군주나 소아시아의 어느 총독이나 부집정관의 딸로 태어나지 못한 게 늘 안타까웠소."], ["She would undoubtedly have been one of those who would endure martyrdom and would have smiled when they branded her bosom with hot pincers.", "그녀는 틀림없이 순교를 견뎌 낼 사람들 중 하나였을 테고, 시뻘겋게 달군 집게로 가슴을 지질 때조차 미소를 지었을 거요."], ["And she would have gone to it of herself.", "그것도 제 발로 그리로 걸어갔겠지."], ["And in the fourth or fifth century she would have walked away into the Egyptian desert and would have stayed there thirty years living on roots and ecstasies and visions.", "그리고 사오 세기였다면 이집트 사막으로 걸어 들어가 거기서 삼십 년을 풀뿌리와 황홀경과 환영으로 연명하며 머물렀을 거요."], ["She is simply thirsting to face some torture for someone, and if she can’t get her torture, she’ll throw herself out of a window. I’ve heard something of a Mr.", "그녀는 그저 누군가를 위해 어떤 고난과 맞서기를 갈망하고 있는 거고, 만약 그 고난을 얻지 못하면 창밖으로 몸을 던질 거요."], ["Razumihin--he’s said to be a sensible fellow; his surname suggests it, indeed.", "라주미힌이라는 분에 대해 좀 들었는데 — 분별 있는 친구라더군."], ["He’s probably a divinity student.", "하긴 성씨부터가 그걸 암시하지 않소."], ["Well, he’d better look after your sister!", "아마 신학생일 거요."], ["I believe I understand her, and I am proud of it.", "뭐, 그 친구가 당신 누이를 잘 돌보는 게 좋을 거요!"], ["But at the beginning of an acquaintance, as you know, one is apt to be more heedless and stupid.", "나는 그녀를 이해한다고 믿고, 또 그걸 자랑스럽게 여기오. 하지만 알다시피 사귐의 처음에는 누구나 더 부주의하고 어리석어지게 마련이지."], ["One doesn’t see clearly.", "분명하게 보질 못하는 거요."], ["Hang it all, why is she so handsome?", "제기랄, 그녀는 왜 그렇게 아름다운 거요?"], ["It’s not my fault.", "내 잘못은 아니잖소."], ["In fact, it began on my side with a most irresistible physical desire.", "사실 내 쪽에서는 도저히 거역할 수 없는 육체적 욕망으로 시작됐소."], ["Avdotya Romanovna is awfully chaste, incredibly and phenomenally so.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 무서울 만큼 순결한 여자요, 믿기지 않을 정도로, 경이로울 정도로."], ["Take note, I tell you this about your sister as a fact.", "명심하시오, 당신 누이에 관해 이걸 사실로서 말하는 거요."], ["She is almost morbidly chaste, in spite of her broad intelligence, and it will stand in her way.", "그녀는 그 넓은 지성에도 불구하고 거의 병적일 만큼 순결한데, 그게 그녀의 앞길에 걸림돌이 될 거요."], ["There happened to be a girl in the house then, Parasha, a black-eyed wench, whom I had never seen before--she had just come from another village--very pretty, but incredibly stupid: she burst into tears, wailed so that she could be heard all over the place and caused scandal.", "그때 마침 집에 한 처녀가 있었소, 파라샤라고, 까만 눈의 계집애였는데, 나로선 처음 보는 얼굴이었지 — 막 다른 마을에서 온 참이었거든 — 아주 예뻤지만 믿기지 않을 만큼 멍청했소. 그애가 그만 울음을 터뜨리고는, 온 집 안에 들리도록 통곡을 해서 추문을 일으켰지."], ["One day after dinner Avdotya Romanovna followed me into an avenue in the garden and with flashing eyes _insisted_ on my leaving poor Parasha alone.", "어느 날 저녁 식사 후에 아브도티야 로마노브나가 정원 산책로로 나를 따라 나와서는, 눈을 번득이며 가엾은 파라샤를 가만 내버려 두라고 다그쳤소."], ["It was almost our first conversation by ourselves.", "그게 우리 단둘이 나눈 거의 첫 대화였지."], ["I, of course, was only too pleased to obey her wishes, tried to appear disconcerted, embarrassed, in fact played my part not badly.", "나야 물론 그녀의 뜻을 따르는 게 더없이 기뻤으니, 당황하고 난처한 척하면서, 사실 내 역할을 제법 그럴듯하게 해냈소."], ["Then came interviews, mysterious conversations, exhortations, entreaties, supplications, even tears--would you believe it, even tears?", "그러고 나서 만남이 이어졌소, 은밀한 대화들, 훈계, 간청, 애원, 심지어 눈물까지 — 믿겠소, 눈물까지 흘렸다오?"], ["Think what the passion for propaganda will bring some girls to!", "어떤 처녀들이 전도에 대한 열정 때문에 어디까지 가게 되는지 생각해 보시오!"], ["I, of course, threw it all on my destiny, posed as hungering and thirsting for light, and finally resorted to the most powerful weapon in the subjection of the female heart, a weapon which never fails one.", "나는 물론 모든 걸 내 운명 탓으로 돌렸고, 빛에 굶주리고 목말라하는 자인 양 행세하다가, 마침내 여자의 마음을 굴복시키는 가장 강력한 무기에 의지했는데, 결코 빗나가는 법이 없는 무기요."], ["It’s the well-known resource--flattery.", "그건 잘 알려진 수단이지 — 바로 아첨이오."], ["Nothing in the world is harder than speaking the truth and nothing easier than flattery.", "세상에서 진실을 말하는 것보다 어려운 일은 없고, 아첨보다 쉬운 일은 없소."], ["If there’s the hundredth part of a false note in speaking the truth, it leads to a discord, and that leads to trouble.", "진실을 말할 때 백분의 일이라도 거짓된 가락이 섞이면 불협화음이 생기고, 그러면 골치 아픈 일이 생기지."], ["But if all, to the last note, is false in flattery, it is just as agreeable, and is heard not without satisfaction.", "하지만 아첨이라면 마지막 음 하나까지 죄다 거짓이라 해도 한결같이 기분 좋게 들리고, 만족스럽게 받아들여지오."], ["It may be a coarse satisfaction, but still a satisfaction.", "천박한 만족일지언정, 그래도 만족이지."], ["And however coarse the flattery, at least half will be sure to seem true.", "그리고 아무리 천박한 아첨이라도 적어도 절반은 틀림없이 진실처럼 들리게 마련이오."], ["That’s so for all stages of development and classes of society.", "이건 발달의 모든 단계와 사회의 모든 계급에 해당하는 진리요."], ["A vestal virgin might be seduced by flattery.", "베스타의 처녀라도 아첨에 넘어갈 수 있소."], ["I can never remember without laughter how I once seduced a lady who was devoted to her husband, her children, and her principles.", "한번은 자기 남편과 자식들과 신조에 헌신하던 어떤 부인을 내가 유혹한 일이 있는데, 떠올릴 때마다 웃음이 나지 않을 수가 없소."], ["What fun it was and how little trouble!", "얼마나 재미났던지, 또 얼마나 수고가 안 들던지!"], ["And the lady really had principles--of her own, anyway.", "그 부인은 정말로 신조가 있었소 — 어쨌든 자기 나름의 신조가."], ["All my tactics lay in simply being utterly annihilated and prostrate before her purity.", "내 모든 전술이라곤 그저 그녀의 순결 앞에서 완전히 무너지고 납작 엎드리는 것이었소."], ["I flattered her shamelessly, and as soon as I succeeded in getting a pressure of the hand, even a glance from her, I would reproach myself for having snatched it by force, and would declare that she had resisted, so that I could never have gained anything but for my being so unprincipled.", "나는 뻔뻔하게 그녀에게 아첨을 했고, 그녀의 손을 한 번 잡거나 눈길 한 번 받는 데 성공하기만 하면, 그걸 억지로 빼앗았다고 자책하며, 그녀가 저항했노라고, 그러니 내가 이토록 신조 없는 인간만 아니었다면 결코 아무것도 얻지 못했을 거라고 선언하곤 했소."], ["I maintained that she was so innocent that she could not foresee my treachery, and yielded to me unconsciously, unawares, and so on.", "나는 그녀가 너무도 순진해서 내 배신을 미리 내다보지 못했고, 무의식적으로, 자기도 모르는 새에 나에게 굴복한 거라는 둥 그렇게 우겨 댔지."], ["In fact, I triumphed, while my lady remained firmly convinced that she was innocent, chaste, and faithful to all her duties and obligations and had succumbed quite by accident.", "결국 나는 승리를 거뒀고, 한편 내 부인께서는 자기가 결백하고 순결하며 자신의 모든 의무와 책무에 충실했는데 그저 순전히 우연으로 넘어가고 만 거라고 굳게 믿고 있었소."], ["And how angry she was with me when I explained to her at last that it was my sincere conviction that she was just as eager as I.", "그리고 내가 마침내 그녀에게 사실은 그녀도 나만큼이나 열을 올렸던 거라는 게 내 진심 어린 확신이라고 설명해 주었을 때, 그녀가 나한테 얼마나 화를 냈던지."], ["Poor Marfa Petrovna was awfully weak on the side of flattery, and if I had only cared to, I might have had all her property settled on me during her lifetime.", "가엾은 마르파 페트로브나도 아첨이라면 무섭게 약했으니, 내가 마음만 먹었다면 그녀 생전에 그녀의 전 재산을 내 앞으로 돌려놓을 수도 있었을 거요."], ["(I am drinking an awful lot of wine now and talking too much.) I hope you won’t be angry if I mention now that I was beginning to produce the same effect on Avdotya Romanovna.", "(지금 내가 술을 엄청나게 마셔 대고, 말도 너무 많이 하고 있군.) 이제 내가 아브도티야 로마노브나한테도 똑같은 효과를 내기 시작했다고 말하더라도 화내지 않기를 바라오."], ["But I was stupid and impatient and spoiled it all.", "하지만 나는 어리석고 성급해서 그걸 다 망쳐 버렸소."], ["Avdotya Romanovna had several times--and one time in particular--been greatly displeased by the expression of my eyes, would you believe it?", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 여러 번 — 특히 한 번은 — 내 눈빛 때문에 몹시 불쾌해했는데, 믿겠소?"], ["There was sometimes a light in them which frightened her and grew stronger and stronger and more unguarded till it was hateful to her.", "거기엔 이따금 그녀를 두렵게 하는 어떤 불빛이 어렸고, 그게 점점 더 강해지고 더 함부로 드러나더니 마침내 그녀한테 혐오스러운 것이 되고 만 거지."], ["No need to go into detail, but we parted.", "시시콜콜 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠고, 우리는 헤어졌소."], ["There I acted stupidly again.", "거기서 나는 또 어리석게 굴었지."], ["I fell to jeering in the coarsest way at all such propaganda and efforts to convert me; Parasha came on to the scene again, and not she alone; in fact there was a tremendous to-do.", "나는 그런 온갖 전도와 나를 개종시키려는 노력을 가장 천박한 방식으로 비웃기 시작했소. 파라샤가 다시 등장했고, 그것도 그애 혼자만이 아니었지."], ["Ah, Rodion Romanovitch, if you could only see how your sister’s eyes can flash sometimes!", "사실상 엄청난 소동이 벌어진 거요. 아, 로지온 로마노비치, 당신 누이의 눈이 이따금 얼마나 번득일 수 있는지 당신이 볼 수만 있다면!"], ["Never mind my being drunk at this moment and having had a whole glass of wine.", "지금 내가 취해 있고 포도주를 한 잔이나 들이켰다는 건 신경 쓰지 마시오."], ["I am speaking the truth.", "나는 진실을 말하고 있소."], ["I assure you that this glance has haunted my dreams; the very rustle of her dress was more than I could stand at last.", "단언컨대 그 눈길이 내 꿈속을 떠나지 않았고, 마침내는 그녀의 옷자락 스치는 소리조차 견딜 수 없을 지경이 됐소."], ["I really began to think that I might become epileptic.", "정말이지 나는 내가 간질에 걸리는 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했소."], ["I could never have believed that I could be moved to such a frenzy.", "내가 그런 광란의 지경까지 흔들릴 수 있으리라곤 결코 믿지 못했을 거요."], ["It was essential, indeed, to be reconciled, but by then it was impossible.", "사실 화해를 하는 게 꼭 필요했지만, 그때는 이미 불가능했소."], ["And imagine what I did then!", "그래서 내가 그때 무슨 짓을 했는지 상상해 보시오!"], ["To what a pitch of stupidity a man can be brought by frenzy!", "인간이 광란에 휩싸이면 얼마나 어리석은 지경까지 갈 수 있는지!"], ["Never undertake anything in a frenzy, Rodion Romanovitch.", "로지온 로마노비치, 절대로 광란 속에서 무슨 일을 벌이지 마시오."], ["I reflected that Avdotya Romanovna was after all a beggar (ach, excuse me, that’s not the word...", "나는 아브도티야 로마노브나가 어쨌든 거지나 다름없다는 데 생각이 미쳤소(아, 미안하오, 그건 적절한 말이 아니지만…… 뜻만 전해진다면 무슨 상관이겠소?)."], ["but does it matter if it expresses the meaning?), that she lived by her work, that she had her mother and you to keep (ach, hang it, you are frowning again), and I resolved to offer her all my money--thirty thousand roubles I could have realised then--if she would run away with me here, to Petersburg.", "그녀가 자기 노동으로 먹고살고, 어머니와 당신을 부양해야 한다는 것에 말이오(아, 제기랄, 또 얼굴을 찌푸리는군). 그래서 나는 그녀에게 내 돈을 다 주겠다고 — 그때 내가 마련할 수 있었던 삼만 루블을 — 그녀가 나와 함께 이리로, 페테르부르크로 달아나 준다면 그러겠다고 마음먹었소."], ["Of course I should have vowed eternal love, rapture, and so on.", "물론 영원한 사랑이며 황홀이며 그런 걸 맹세했겠지."], ["Do you know, I was so wild about her at that time that if she had told me to poison Marfa Petrovna or to cut her throat and to marry herself, it would have been done at once!", "한데 말이오, 그때 나는 그녀한테 어찌나 미쳐 있었던지, 만약 그녀가 나더러 마르파 페트로브나를 독살하든지 목을 따든지 해서 자기와 결혼하라고 했다면, 당장에 해치웠을 거요!"], ["But it ended in the catastrophe of which you know already.", "하지만 그 일은 당신이 이미 아는 그 파국으로 끝나고 말았소."], ["You can fancy how frantic I was when I heard that Marfa Petrovna had got hold of that scoundrelly attorney, Luzhin, and had almost made a match between them--which would really have been just the same thing as I was proposing.", "마르파 페트로브나가 그 비열한 변호사 나부랭이 루진을 손아귀에 넣고 그 둘을 거의 맺어 줄 뻔했다는 걸 들었을 때 내가 얼마나 미칠 듯했을지 짐작이 갈 거요 — 그건 사실상 내가 제안하던 것과 똑같은 거였으니 말이오."], ["Wouldn’t it?", "안 그렇소?"], ["Wouldn’t it?", "안 그렇소?"], ["I notice that you’ve begun to be very attentive...", "보아하니 당신이 아주 귀를 기울이기 시작했군……"], ["you interesting young man....”", "흥미로운 젊은이로구먼…….\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov struck the table with his fist impatiently. He was flushed.", "스비드리가일로프는 짜증스레 주먹으로 탁자를 쳤다."], ["Raskolnikov saw clearly that the glass or glass and a half of champagne that he had sipped almost unconsciously was affecting him--and he resolved to take advantage of the opportunity.", "그의 얼굴은 벌겋게 달아올라 있었다. 라스콜니코프는 그가 거의 무의식적으로 홀짝인 그 한 잔, 아니 한 잔 반쯤의 샴페인이 그에게 영향을 미치고 있음을 똑똑히 알아챘고 — 그래서 이 기회를 이용하기로 마음먹었다."], ["He felt very suspicious of Svidrigaïlov.", "그는 스비드리가일로프가 몹시 미심쩍었다."]], [["“Well, after what you have said, I am fully convinced that you have come to Petersburg with designs on my sister,”", "\"자, 당신이 한 얘기를 듣고 나니, 당신이 내 누이를 노리고 페테르부르크에 왔다는 걸 완전히 확신하게 됐소.\""], ["he said directly to Svidrigaïlov, in order to irritate him further.", "그는 그를 더 자극하려고 스비드리가일로프에게 대놓고 말했다."]], [["“Oh, nonsense,”", "\"오, 말도 안 되는 소리.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov, seeming to rouse himself.", "스비드리가일로프가 정신을 차리는 듯이 말했다."], ["“Why, I told you...", "\"아니, 내가 말했잖소……"], ["besides your sister can’t endure me.”", "게다가 당신 누이는 나를 견디지 못하는데.\""]], [["“Yes, I am certain that she can’t, but that’s not the point.”", "\"그래, 그녀가 견디지 못한다는 건 나도 확신하오, 하지만 그게 요점이 아니오.\""]], [["“Are you so sure that she can’t?”", "\"그녀가 견디지 못한다고 그렇게 확신하시오?\""], ["Svidrigaïlov screwed up his eyes and smiled mockingly.", "스비드리가일로프가 눈을 가늘게 뜨며 조롱하듯 미소 지었다."], ["“You are right, she doesn’t love me, but you can never be sure of what has passed between husband and wife or lover and mistress.", "\"당신 말이 맞소, 그녀는 나를 사랑하지 않지. 하지만 남편과 아내 사이, 혹은 연인과 정부 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지는 결코 확신할 수 없는 법이오."], ["There’s always a little corner which remains a secret to the world and is only known to those two.", "세상에는 늘 비밀로 남아 오직 그 두 사람만 아는 작은 구석이 있게 마련이지."], ["Will you answer for it that Avdotya Romanovna regarded me with aversion?”", "아브도티야 로마노브나가 나를 혐오의 눈으로 봤다고 당신이 장담할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“From some words you’ve dropped, I notice that you still have designs--and of course evil ones--on Dounia and mean to carry them out promptly.”", "\"당신이 흘린 몇 마디로 보아, 당신이 두냐에게 여전히 어떤 속셈을 — 물론 사악한 속셈을 — 품고 있고, 그걸 곧 실행에 옮길 작정이라는 걸 알겠소.\""]], [["“What, have I dropped words like that?”", "\"뭐, 내가 그런 말을 흘렸단 말이오?\""], ["Svidrigaïlov asked in naïve dismay, taking not the slightest notice of the epithet bestowed on his designs.", "스비드리가일로프가 순진한 당혹감을 띠며 물었는데, 자기 속셈에 붙은 그 형용사에는 조금도 신경 쓰지 않았다."]], [["“Why, you are dropping them even now.", "\"아니, 지금도 흘리고 있잖소."], ["Why are you so frightened?", "왜 그렇게 겁을 먹는 거요?"], ["What are you so afraid of now?”", "지금 뭘 그리 두려워하는 거요?\""]], [["“Me--afraid?", "\"내가 — 겁을 먹는다고?"], ["Afraid of you?", "당신을 두려워한다고?"], ["You have rather to be afraid of me, _cher ami_.", "오히려 당신이 나를 두려워해야 할 판인데, 친애하는 벗이여."], ["But what nonsense....", "하지만 무슨 헛소릴."], ["I’ve drunk too much though, I see that.", "그래도 내가 술을 너무 많이 마셨군, 그건 알겠소."], ["I was almost saying too much again.", "또 하마터면 말을 너무 많이 할 뻔했어."], ["Damn the wine!", "빌어먹을 술!"], ["Hi!", "이봐!"], ["there, water!”", "거기, 물 좀!\""]], [["He snatched up the champagne bottle and flung it without ceremony out of the window.", "그는 샴페인 병을 집어 들더니 거리낌 없이 창밖으로 내던졌다."], ["Philip brought the water.", "필립이 물을 가져왔다."]], [["“That’s all nonsense!”", "\"그건 다 헛소리요!\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov, wetting a towel and putting it to his head.", "스비드리가일로프가 수건을 적셔 머리에 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["“But I can answer you in one word and annihilate all your suspicions.", "\"하지만 한마디로 당신한테 대답해서 당신의 모든 의심을 없애 줄 수 있소."], ["Do you know that I am going to get married?”", "내가 결혼할 거란 걸 아시오?\""]], [["“You told me so before.”", "\"전에도 그렇게 말했잖소.\""]], [["“Did I?", "\"그랬소?"], ["I’ve forgotten.", "잊어버렸군."], ["But I couldn’t have told you so for certain for I had not even seen my betrothed; I only meant to.", "하지만 분명하게 말했을 리는 없는데, 그땐 약혼녀를 본 적조차 없었으니까. 그저 그러려고 작정만 했던 거지."], ["But now I really have a betrothed and it’s a settled thing, and if it weren’t that I have business that can’t be put off, I would have taken you to see them at once, for I should like to ask your advice.", "그런데 이제는 정말로 약혼녀가 생겼고, 다 정해진 일이오. 미룰 수 없는 볼일만 없었다면 지금 당장 당신을 데리고 그들을 보러 갔을 텐데, 당신 조언을 좀 구하고 싶거든."], ["Ach, hang it, only ten minutes left!", "아, 제기랄, 이제 십 분밖에 안 남았군!"], ["See, look at the watch.", "자, 시계 좀 보시오."], ["But I must tell you, for it’s an interesting story, my marriage, in its own way.", "하지만 당신한테 얘기해 줘야겠소, 내 결혼은 그 나름으로 흥미로운 이야기니까."], ["Where are you off to?", "어디 가는 거요?"], ["Going again?”", "또 가려는 거요?\""]], [["“No, I’m not going away now.”", "\"아니, 지금은 안 갈 거요.\""]], [["“Not at all?", "\"아예 안 간다고?"], ["We shall see.", "두고 봅시다."], ["I’ll take you there, I’ll show you my betrothed, only not now.", "내가 당신을 거기 데려가서 약혼녀를 보여 주겠소, 다만 지금은 아니오."], ["For you’ll soon have to be off.", "당신은 곧 가 봐야 할 테니까."], ["You have to go to the right and I to the left.", "당신은 오른쪽으로 가야 하고 나는 왼쪽으로 가야 하오."], ["Do you know that Madame Resslich, the woman I am lodging with now, eh?", "레슬리히 부인이라고, 지금 내가 세 들어 사는 그 여자 말인데, 응?"], ["I know what you’re thinking, that she’s the woman whose girl they say drowned herself in the winter.", "당신이 무슨 생각을 하는지 알겠소, 겨울에 그 집 처녀가 물에 빠져 죽었다는 그 여자 아니냐고 말이지."], ["Come, are you listening?", "자, 듣고 있소?"], ["She arranged it all for me.", "그 여자가 나를 위해 다 주선해 준 거요."], ["You’re bored, she said, you want something to fill up your time.", "당신은 따분해하니까, 시간을 채울 뭔가가 필요하다고 그러더군."], ["For, you know, I am a gloomy, depressed person.", "알다시피 나는 음울하고 우울한 사람이오."], ["Do you think I’m light-hearted? No, I’m gloomy.", "내가 명랑한 사람인 줄 아시오?"], ["I do no harm, but sit in a corner without speaking a word for three days at a time.", "아니, 나는 음울한 사람이오. 해코지는 안 하지만, 사흘씩 내리 한구석에 앉아 말 한마디 안 하지."], ["And that Resslich is a sly hussy, I tell you.", "그리고 그 레슬리히는 교활한 계집이오, 정말이지."], ["I know what she has got in her mind; she thinks I shall get sick of it, abandon my wife and depart, and she’ll get hold of her and make a profit out of her--in our class, of course, or higher.", "나는 그 여자가 무슨 꿍꿍이를 품고 있는지 알지. 그 여자 생각은, 내가 결국 진절머리를 내고 아내를 버리고 떠나 버리면, 그때 자기가 그 여자를 손에 넣어 한밑천 챙기겠다는 거요 — 물론 우리 계급에서, 아니면 더 높은 데서 말이오."], ["She told me the father was a broken-down retired official, who has been sitting in a chair for the last three years with his legs paralysed.", "그 여자 말로는 아버지란 사람이 영락한 퇴직 관리인데, 지난 삼 년 동안 다리가 마비된 채 의자에 앉아만 있다더군."], ["The mamma, she said, was a sensible woman.", "어머니는, 그 여자 말로는 분별 있는 여자라고 하오."], ["There is a son serving in the provinces, but he doesn’t help; there is a daughter, who is married, but she doesn’t visit them.", "아들 하나가 지방에서 근무하는데 도움은 안 주고, 시집간 딸이 하나 있지만 찾아오질 않는답디다."], ["And they’ve two little nephews on their hands, as though their own children were not enough, and they’ve taken from school their youngest daughter, a girl who’ll be sixteen in another month, so that then she can be married.", "게다가 자기 자식들로도 모자란지 어린 조카 둘까지 떠맡고 있어서, 막내딸을 학교에서 빼냈다는 거요, 한 달만 있으면 열여섯이 되는 계집애를, 그래야 시집을 보낼 수 있으니까."], ["She was for me. We went there.", "그애가 바로 내 상대요."], ["How funny it was!", "우리는 거기 갔소."], ["I present myself--a landowner, a widower, of a well-known name, with connections, with a fortune.", "얼마나 우스웠던지! 내가 나서니 — 지주에, 홀아비에, 이름난 가문에, 연줄도 있고, 재산도 있는 사람이지."], ["What if I am fifty and she is not sixteen? Who thinks of that?", "내가 쉰 살이고 그애가 열여섯도 안 됐다 한들 그게 무슨 대수겠소?"], ["But it’s fascinating, isn’t it?", "누가 그런 걸 따진단 말이오?"], ["It is fascinating, ha-ha!", "하지만 매혹적이지 않소?"], ["You should have seen how I talked to the papa and mamma.", "매혹적이오, 하하! 내가 그 아빠 엄마한테 어떻게 얘기했는지 당신이 봤어야 하는데."], ["It was worth paying to have seen me at that moment.", "그 순간의 나를 보는 데는 돈을 내도 아깝지 않았을 거요."], ["She comes in, curtseys, you can fancy, still in a short frock--an unopened bud!", "그애가 들어와서, 인사를 하는데, 상상해 보시오, 아직도 짧은 원피스 차림이라니 — 채 피지도 않은 봉오리지!"], ["Flushing like a sunset--she had been told, no doubt.", "노을처럼 발갛게 물든 얼굴로 — 분명 미리 무슨 말을 들었겠지."], ["I don’t know how you feel about female faces, but to my mind these sixteen years, these childish eyes, shyness and tears of bashfulness are better than beauty; and she is a perfect little picture, too.", "당신이 여자 얼굴을 어떻게 생각하는지는 모르겠소만, 내가 보기엔 그 열여섯이라는 나이, 그 앳된 눈, 수줍음과 부끄러움의 눈물이 미모보다 낫소. 게다가 그애는 정말 한 폭의 그림 같은 작은 미인이오."], ["Fair hair in little curls, like a lamb’s, full little rosy lips, tiny feet, a charmer!...", "양털처럼 작게 곱슬거리는 금발, 도톰하고 작은 장밋빛 입술, 앙증맞은 발 — 그야말로 매혹덩어리지!……"], ["Well, we made friends.", "자, 우리는 친해졌소."], ["I told them I was in a hurry owing to domestic circumstances, and the next day, that is the day before yesterday, we were betrothed.", "나는 그들한테 집안 사정 때문에 서두른다고 했고, 이튿날, 그러니까 그저께 우리는 약혼을 했소."], ["When I go now I take her on my knee at once and keep her there....", "이제 거기 가면 나는 곧장 그애를 무릎에 앉히고 거기 그대로 둔다오……."], ["Well, she flushes like a sunset and I kiss her every minute.", "그러면 그애는 노을처럼 발갛게 물들고, 나는 시시각각 입을 맞추지."], ["Her mamma of course impresses on her that this is her husband and that this must be so.", "물론 그애 엄마는 이 사람이 네 남편이고 마땅히 이래야 하는 거라고 그애한테 단단히 일러 두지."], ["It’s simply delicious!", "그야말로 황홀하오!"], ["The present betrothed condition is perhaps better than marriage.", "어쩌면 지금의 이 약혼 상태가 결혼보다 나을지도 모르오."], ["Here you have what is called _la nature et la vérité_, ha-ha!", "여기엔 이른바 '자연과 진실'이라는 게 있거든, 하하!"], ["I’ve talked to her twice, she is far from a fool.", "나는 그애와 두 번 얘기를 나눠 봤는데, 결코 바보가 아니오."], ["Sometimes she steals a look at me that positively scorches me.", "가끔 그애가 나를 흘끔 훔쳐보는데, 그 눈길이 정말이지 나를 불태워 버린다오."], ["Her face is like Raphael’s Madonna.", "그애 얼굴은 라파엘로의 성모 같소."], ["You know, the Sistine Madonna’s face has something fantastic in it, the face of mournful religious ecstasy.", "알잖소, 시스티나 성모의 얼굴엔 뭔가 환상적인 것이, 그 비통한 종교적 황홀의 얼굴이 있다는 걸."], ["Haven’t you noticed it?", "당신은 못 알아챘소?"], ["Well, she’s something in that line.", "뭐, 그애가 그런 부류요."], ["The day after we’d been betrothed, I bought her presents to the value of fifteen hundred roubles--a set of diamonds and another of pearls and a silver dressing-case as large as this, with all sorts of things in it, so that even my Madonna’s face glowed.", "약혼한 다음 날 나는 그애한테 천오백 루블어치 선물을 사 줬소 — 다이아몬드 세트 하나에 진주 세트 하나, 그리고 이만큼 큰 은제 화장 상자에 온갖 물건을 가득 채워서 말이오, 그래서 내 성모님의 얼굴까지 환히 빛나더군."], ["I sat her on my knee, yesterday, and I suppose rather too unceremoniously--she flushed crimson and the tears started, but she didn’t want to show it.", "어제 나는 그애를 무릎에 앉혔는데, 아마 좀 너무 허물없이 굴었던 모양인지 — 그애가 새빨갛게 물들며 눈물을 글썽였지만, 그걸 내보이려 하진 않았소."], ["We were left alone, she suddenly flung herself on my neck (for the first time of her own accord), put her little arms round me, kissed me, and vowed that she would be an obedient, faithful, and good wife, would make me happy, would devote all her life, every minute of her life, would sacrifice everything, everything, and that all she asks in return is my _respect_, and that she wants ‘nothing, nothing more from me, no presents.’ You’ll admit that to hear such a confession, alone, from an angel of sixteen in a muslin frock, with little curls, with a flush of maiden shyness in her cheeks and tears of enthusiasm in her eyes is rather fascinating!", "우리 단둘만 남게 되자, 그애가 갑자기 내 목에 매달려서는(제 발로 그런 건 처음이었지) 작은 두 팔로 나를 감싸 안고 입을 맞추더니, 순종하고 충실하고 착한 아내가 되겠노라, 나를 행복하게 해 주겠노라, 자기 평생을, 자기 삶의 매 순간을 바치겠노라, 모든 걸, 모든 걸 희생하겠노라 맹세하면서, 그 대신 바라는 건 오직 내 '존경'뿐이고, 나한테 '아무것도, 아무것도 더는 바라지 않는다, 선물도 필요 없다'고 하더군. 단둘이서, 모슬린 원피스를 입고 작은 곱슬머리에 두 뺨엔 처녀의 수줍은 홍조를 띠고 두 눈엔 감격의 눈물을 글썽이는 열여섯 살 천사한테서 그런 고백을 듣는 게 제법 매혹적이라는 건 당신도 인정할 거요!"], ["Isn’t it fascinating?", "매혹적이지 않소?"], ["It’s worth paying for, isn’t it?", "돈을 낼 만하지 않소?"], ["Well...", "자……"], ["listen, we’ll go to see my betrothed, only not just now!”", "들어 보시오, 우리 내 약혼녀를 보러 갑시다, 다만 지금 당장은 아니고!\""]], [["“The fact is this monstrous difference in age and development excites your sensuality!", "\"실은 그 끔찍한 나이 차와 성숙도의 차이가 당신의 음욕을 자극하는 거요!"], ["Will you really make such a marriage?”", "정말로 그런 결혼을 하겠단 말이오?\""]], [["“Why, of course.", "\"아니, 물론이지."], ["Everyone thinks of himself, and he lives most gaily who knows best how to deceive himself.", "누구나 자기 생각만 하는 법이고, 자기 자신을 가장 잘 속일 줄 아는 자가 가장 즐겁게 사는 거요."], ["Ha-ha!", "하하!"], ["But why are you so keen about virtue?", "한데 당신은 왜 그리 미덕에 열을 올리는 거요?"], ["Have mercy on me, my good friend.", "나를 좀 봐주시오, 나의 좋은 벗이여."], ["I am a sinful man.", "나는 죄 많은 인간이오."], ["Ha-ha-ha!”", "하하하!\""]], [["“But you have provided for the children of Katerina Ivanovna.", "\"하지만 당신은 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이들을 거둬 줬잖소."], ["Though...", "비록……"], ["though you had your own reasons....", "비록 당신 나름의 이유가 있긴 했겠지만……."], ["I understand it all now.”", "이제 다 알겠소.\""]], [["“I am always fond of children, very fond of them,”", "\"나는 늘 아이들을 좋아하오, 아주 좋아하지.\""], ["laughed Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프가 웃었다."], ["“I can tell you one curious instance of it.", "\"그것에 관해 흥미로운 일화를 하나 들려줄 수 있소."], ["The first day I came here I visited various haunts, after seven years I simply rushed at them.", "여기 온 첫날 나는 여러 소굴을 돌아다녔소, 칠 년 만이라 그저 거기로 내달았던 거지."], ["You probably notice that I am not in a hurry to renew acquaintance with my old friends.", "보아하니 당신도 알아챘겠지만, 나는 옛 친구들과 다시 사귀는 걸 서두르지 않소."], ["I shall do without them as long as I can.", "견딜 수 있는 한 그들 없이 지낼 거요."], ["Do you know, when I was with Marfa Petrovna in the country, I was haunted by the thought of these places where anyone who knows his way about can find a great deal.", "한데 말이오, 시골에서 마르파 페트로브나와 지낼 때, 길만 잘 아는 사람이라면 누구든 많은 걸 찾아낼 수 있는 그런 장소들 생각이 나를 사로잡았소."], ["Yes, upon my soul!", "그렇소, 맹세컨대!"], ["The peasants have vodka, the educated young people, shut out from activity, waste themselves in impossible dreams and visions and are crippled by theories; Jews have sprung up and are amassing money, and all the rest give themselves up to debauchery.", "농민들한테는 보드카가 있고, 활동의 길이 막힌 교육받은 젊은이들은 불가능한 꿈과 환상 속에서 자신을 허비하며 이론에 절름발이가 되어 버리지. 유대인들이 생겨나 돈을 긁어모으고, 나머지는 죄다 방탕에 몸을 맡기고."], ["From the first hour the town reeked of its familiar odours.", "첫 순간부터 이 도시는 그 익숙한 냄새를 풍기더군."], ["I chanced to be in a frightful den--I like my dens dirty--it was a dance, so called, and there was a _cancan_ such as I never saw in my day.", "나는 우연히 어느 끔찍한 소굴에 들어가게 됐는데 — 나는 더러운 소굴을 좋아하거든 — 이른바 무도회라는 거였소, 거기서 내 시절엔 한 번도 본 적 없는 그런 캉캉이 벌어지고 있었지."], ["Yes, there you have progress.", "그렇소, 거기에 진보라는 게 있는 거요."], ["All of a sudden I saw a little girl of thirteen, nicely dressed, dancing with a specialist in that line, with another one _vis-à-vis_.", "그런데 별안간 곱게 차려입은 열세 살짜리 계집애가, 그 방면의 전문가와, 맞은편엔 또 다른 자와 짝을 이뤄 춤추는 게 보이지 뭐요."], ["Her mother was sitting on a chair by the wall.", "그애 엄마는 벽 옆 의자에 앉아 있었소."], ["You can’t fancy what a _cancan_ that was!", "그게 어떤 캉캉이었는지는 당신은 상상도 못 할 거요!"], ["The girl was ashamed, blushed, at last felt insulted, and began to cry.", "그애는 부끄러워서 얼굴을 붉히다가, 마침내 모욕감을 느끼고 울기 시작했소."], ["Her partner seized her and began whirling her round and performing before her; everyone laughed and--I like your public, even the _cancan_ public--they laughed and shouted, ‘Serves her right--serves her right!", "그애의 짝은 그애를 붙들고 빙글빙글 돌리며 그애 앞에서 재주를 부려 댔지. 다들 웃어 댔고 — 나는 당신네 대중이 마음에 드오, 캉캉을 보러 온 대중조차도 — 그들은 웃고 소리치더군, '꼴좋다 — 꼴좋아!"], ["Shouldn’t bring children!’ Well, it’s not my business whether that consoling reflection was logical or not.", "애를 데려오면 안 되지!' 뭐, 그 위로가 되는 한마디가 논리적이었는지 아닌지는 내 알 바 아니오."], ["I at once fixed on my plan, sat down by the mother, and began by saying that I too was a stranger and that people here were ill-bred and that they couldn’t distinguish decent folks and treat them with respect, gave her to understand that I had plenty of money, offered to take them home in my carriage.", "나는 즉시 계획을 세우고 그 엄마 옆에 앉아, 나도 이곳에 처음 온 사람이라느니, 여기 사람들은 버르장머리가 없어서 점잖은 사람들을 알아보지도 못하고 존중할 줄도 모른다느니 하는 말로 운을 떼면서, 내게 돈이 많다는 걸 넌지시 알리고는 내 마차로 그들을 집까지 데려다주겠다고 했소."], ["I took them home and got to know them.", "나는 그들을 집까지 데려다주고 알게 됐지."], ["They were lodging in a miserable little hole and had only just arrived from the country.", "그들은 비참하고 작은 구멍 같은 데서 셋방살이를 하고 있었고, 막 시골에서 올라온 참이었소."], ["She told me that she and her daughter could only regard my acquaintance as an honour.", "그 여자는 자기와 딸이 나와 알게 된 걸 영광으로 여길 따름이라고 하더군."], ["I found out that they had nothing of their own and had come to town upon some legal business.", "알고 보니 그들은 가진 거라곤 아무것도 없었고, 무슨 소송 일로 도시에 올라온 거였소."], ["I proffered my services and money.", "나는 도움과 돈을 내밀었지."], ["I learnt that they had gone to the dancing saloon by mistake, believing that it was a genuine dancing class.", "그들이 진짜 무용 교습소인 줄 알고 그 무도장에 잘못 들어갔다는 것도 알게 됐소."], ["I offered to assist in the young girl’s education in French and dancing.", "나는 그 어린 처녀의 프랑스어와 무용 교육을 돕겠다고 제안했지."], ["My offer was accepted with enthusiasm as an honour--and we are still friendly....", "내 제안은 영광이라며 열렬히 받아들여졌고 — 우리는 지금도 사이좋게 지낸다오……."], ["If you like, we’ll go and see them, only not just now.”", "원한다면 우리 그들을 보러 갑시다, 다만 지금 당장은 아니고.\""]], [["“Stop!", "\"그만하시오!"], ["Enough of your vile, nasty anecdotes, depraved vile, sensual man!”", "그 비열하고 추잡한 일화는 이제 됐소, 타락하고 비열하고 음탕한 인간 같으니!\""]], [["“Schiller, you are a regular Schiller!", "\"실러로군, 당신은 영락없는 실러요!"], ["_O la vertu va-t-elle se nicher?_ But you know I shall tell you these things on purpose, for the pleasure of hearing your outcries!”", "'오 미덕은 도대체 어디에 둥지를 틀려는가?' 하지만 알아 두시오, 나는 일부러 당신한테 이런 얘기들을 들려주는 거요, 당신이 비명을 지르는 걸 듣는 즐거움 때문에 말이오!\""]], [["“I dare say.", "\"그렇겠지."], ["I can see I am ridiculous myself,”", "나 자신도 우스꽝스럽다는 걸 알겠소.\""], ["muttered Raskolnikov angrily.", "라스콜니코프가 성난 듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov laughed heartily; finally he called Philip, paid his bill, and began getting up.", "스비드리가일로프는 유쾌하게 웃었다. 마침내 그는 필립을 불러 계산을 치르고는 자리에서 일어서기 시작했다."]], [["“I say, but I am drunk, _assez causé_,” he said.", "\"이런, 내가 취했군, 이제 그만 떠들지.\""], ["“It’s been a pleasure.”", "그가 말했다. \"즐거웠소.\""]], [["“I should rather think it must be a pleasure!”", "\"즐거웠을 거라고 생각하오!\""], ["cried Raskolnikov, getting up.", "라스콜니코프가 일어서며 소리쳤다."], ["“No doubt it is a pleasure for a worn-out profligate to describe such adventures with a monstrous project of the same sort in his mind--especially under such circumstances and to such a man as me....", "\"지칠 대로 지친 난봉꾼이 마음속에 똑같은 종류의 끔찍한 계획을 품은 채 그런 모험담을 늘어놓는 게 즐거운 일이 아닐 리 없겠지 — 특히 이런 상황에서, 게다가 나 같은 인간한테 말이오……."], ["It’s stimulating!”", "그게 자극이 되는 거지!\""]], [["“Well, if you come to that,”", "\"뭐, 정 그렇게 말한다면,\""], ["Svidrigaïlov answered, scrutinising Raskolnikov with some surprise, “if you come to that, you are a thorough cynic yourself.", "스비드리가일로프가 다소 놀란 표정으로 라스콜니코프를 뜯어보며 대답했다. \"정 그렇게 말한다면, 당신이야말로 철두철미한 냉소가요."], ["You’ve plenty to make you so, anyway.", "어쨌든 당신한테는 그렇게 될 만한 게 잔뜩 있으니까."], ["You can understand a great deal...", "당신은 많은 걸 이해할 수 있고……"], ["and you can do a great deal too.", "또 많은 일을 해낼 수도 있지."], ["But enough.", "하지만 그만합시다."], ["I sincerely regret not having had more talk with you, but I shan’t lose sight of you....", "당신과 더 얘기를 나누지 못해 진심으로 유감이지만, 당신을 시야에서 놓치진 않을 거요……."], ["Only wait a bit.”", "그저 조금만 기다리시오.\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov walked out of the restaurant.", "스비드리가일로프는 음식점에서 걸어 나갔다."], ["Raskolnikov walked out after him.", "라스콜니코프도 그를 뒤따라 나갔다."], ["Svidrigaïlov was not however very drunk, the wine had affected him for a moment, but it was passing off every minute.", "그렇지만 스비드리가일로프는 그리 많이 취한 게 아니었으니, 술이 한순간 그에게 영향을 미쳤을 뿐, 그것도 시시각각 가시고 있었다."], ["He was preoccupied with something of importance and was frowning.", "그는 무언가 중요한 일에 골몰한 채 얼굴을 찌푸리고 있었다."], ["He was apparently excited and uneasy in anticipation of something.", "그는 분명 무언가를 기다리며 흥분하고 불안해하는 듯했다."], ["His manner to Raskolnikov had changed during the last few minutes, and he was ruder and more sneering every moment.", "지난 몇 분 사이 라스콜니코프를 대하는 그의 태도가 달라져서, 시시각각 더 무례하고 더 비웃는 투가 되어 갔다."], ["Raskolnikov noticed all this, and he too was uneasy.", "라스콜니코프는 이 모든 걸 알아챘고, 그 역시 불안했다."], ["He became very suspicious of Svidrigaïlov and resolved to follow him.", "그는 스비드리가일로프가 몹시 미심쩍어져서 그를 뒤따르기로 마음먹었다."]], [["They came out on to the pavement.", "그들은 보도로 나왔다."]], [["“You go to the right, and I to the left, or if you like, the other way.", "\"당신은 오른쪽으로 가시오, 나는 왼쪽으로, 아니 원한다면 그 반대로 가든지."], ["Only _adieu, mon plaisir_, may we meet again.”", "그저 잘 가시오, 나의 즐거움이여, 또 만날 수 있기를.\""]], [["And he walked to the right towards the Hay Market.", "그러고는 그는 건초 시장 쪽을 향해 오른쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Raskolnikov walked after him.", "라스콜니코프는 그를 뒤따라갔다."]], [["“What’s this?”", "\"이게 무슨 짓이오?\""], ["cried Svidrigaïlov turning round, “I thought I said...”", "스비드리가일로프가 뒤를 돌아보며 외쳤다. \"내 분명 말한 것 같은데…….\""]], [["“It means that I am not going to lose sight of you now.”", "\"이제부터 당신을 절대 놓치지 않겠다는 뜻이오.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""]], [["Both stood still and gazed at one another, as though measuring their strength.", "두 사람은 멈춰 서서, 마치 서로의 힘을 가늠하기라도 하듯 상대를 노려보았다."]], [["“From all your half tipsy stories,”", "\"당신이 반쯤 취해서 늘어놓은 그 모든 이야기로 미루어,\""], ["Raskolnikov observed harshly, “I am _positive_ that you have not given up your designs on my sister, but are pursuing them more actively than ever.", "라스콜니코프가 거칠게 내뱉었다. \"나는 확신하오, 당신이 내 누이에 대한 흉계를 포기하기는커녕, 그 어느 때보다 더 적극적으로 밀어붙이고 있다는 걸 말이오."], ["I have learnt that my sister received a letter this morning.", "오늘 아침 내 누이가 편지 한 통을 받았다는 걸 알고 있소."], ["You have hardly been able to sit still all this time....", "당신은 그동안 도무지 가만히 앉아 있질 못했지……."], ["You may have unearthed a wife on the way, but that means nothing.", "오는 길에 어디서 아내를 하나 파냈는지 모르겠지만, 그건 아무 의미도 없소."], ["I should like to make certain myself.”", "나는 직접 확인하고 싶소.\""]], [["Raskolnikov could hardly have said himself what he wanted and of what he wished to make certain.", "라스콜니코프 자신도 자기가 무엇을 원하는지, 무엇을 확인하고 싶은지조차 제대로 말할 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Upon my word!", "\"이거 참!"], ["I’ll call the police!”", "경찰을 부르겠소!\""]], [["“Call away!”", "\"부르시오!\""]], [["Again they stood for a minute facing each other.", "또다시 두 사람은 잠시 서로 마주 보고 서 있었다."], ["At last Svidrigaïlov’s face changed.", "마침내 스비드리가일로프의 얼굴빛이 달라졌다."], ["Having satisfied himself that Raskolnikov was not frightened at his threat, he assumed a mirthful and friendly air.", "라스콜니코프가 자기 협박에 겁먹지 않는다는 것을 확인하고는, 그는 명랑하고 다정한 태도를 꾸며 보였다."]], [["“What a fellow!", "\"이런 사람을 봤나!"], ["I purposely refrained from referring to your affair, though I am devoured by curiosity.", "사실 나는 호기심에 잡아먹힐 지경이면서도 일부러 당신 일을 입에 올리지 않았소."], ["It’s a fantastic affair.", "그건 환상적인 사건이거든."], ["I’ve put it off till another time, but you’re enough to rouse the dead....", "다음 기회로 미뤄 두려 했는데, 당신은 죽은 사람도 깨울 만한 양반이군……. 자, 그럼 갑시다."], ["Well, let us go, only I warn you beforehand I am only going home for a moment, to get some money; then I shall lock up the flat, take a cab and go to spend the evening at the Islands.", "다만 미리 일러두는데, 나는 잠깐 돈을 가지러 집에 들르려는 것뿐이오. 그러고 나서 셋집 문을 잠그고, 마차를 잡아 섬으로 저녁을 보내러 갈 거요."], ["Now, now are you going to follow me?”", "자, 그래도 나를 따라오겠소?\""]], [["“I’m coming to your lodgings, not to see you but Sofya Semyonovna, to say I’m sorry not to have been at the funeral.”", "\"당신을 보러 가는 게 아니라 소피야 세묘노브나를 만나러 당신 하숙집에 가는 거요, 장례식에 가지 못해 죄송하다고 말하려고.\""]], [["“That’s as you like, but Sofya Semyonovna is not at home.", "\"그건 당신 좋을 대로 하시오. 하지만 소피야 세묘노브나는 집에 없소."], ["She has taken the three children to an old lady of high rank, the patroness of some orphan asylums, whom I used to know years ago.", "그녀는 아이 셋을 데리고 지체 높은 어느 노부인에게 갔거든. 고아원 몇 곳의 후원자인데, 내가 옛날부터 알던 사람이오."], ["I charmed the old lady by depositing a sum of money with her to provide for the three children of Katerina Ivanovna and subscribing to the institution as well.", "나는 카테리나 이바노브나의 아이 셋을 위한 돈을 그 노부인에게 맡기고 그 시설에도 기부를 함으로써 그녀의 마음을 사로잡았소."], ["I told her too the story of Sofya Semyonovna in full detail, suppressing nothing.", "게다가 소피야 세묘노브나의 사연도 하나도 숨김없이 낱낱이 들려주었지."], ["It produced an indescribable effect on her.", "그게 그녀에게 이루 말할 수 없는 감동을 주었소."], ["That’s why Sofya Semyonovna has been invited to call to-day at the X. Hotel where the lady is staying for the time.”", "그래서 소피야 세묘노브나가 오늘 그 부인이 잠시 머무는 X 호텔로 와 달라는 초대를 받은 거요.\""]], [["“No matter, I’ll come all the same.”", "\"상관없소, 그래도 가 보겠소.\""]], [["“As you like, it’s nothing to me, but I won’t come with you; here we are at home.", "\"좋을 대로 하시오, 나야 아무래도 좋으니까. 하지만 나는 당신과 함께 가지는 않겠소."], ["By the way, I am convinced that you regard me with suspicion just because I have shown such delicacy and have not so far troubled you with questions...", "자, 다 왔소. 그건 그렇고, 당신이 나를 의심스러운 눈초리로 바라보는 건, 단지 내가 이렇게 섬세하게 굴면서 지금껏 당신을 질문으로 괴롭히지 않았기 때문이라는 걸 나는 확신하오……."], ["you understand?", "무슨 말인지 알겠소?"], ["It struck you as extraordinary; I don’t mind betting it’s that.", "그게 당신에겐 이상하게 여겨진 거지. 내기를 걸어도 좋소, 바로 그거요."], ["Well, it teaches one to show delicacy!” “And to listen at doors!”", "흠, 이래서 섬세하게 굴면 안 된다는 교훈을 얻는 거지!\""]], [["“Ah, that’s it, is it?”", "\"그리고 문에 귀를 갖다 대고 엿듣는 것도 말이오!\""], ["laughed Svidrigaïlov.", "\"아하, 그거였군, 응?\""], ["“Yes, I should have been surprised if you had let that pass after all that has happened.", "스비드리가일로프가 웃었다. \"그래, 그 모든 일이 있고 나서 당신이 그걸 그냥 넘겼다면 오히려 내가 놀랐을 거요."], ["Ha-ha!", "하하!"], ["Though I did understand something of the pranks you had been up to and were telling Sofya Semyonovna about, what was the meaning of it?", "하긴 나도 당신이 저질러 놓고 소피야 세묘노브나에게 들려주던 그 장난질을 어느 정도는 알아들었지만, 그게 다 무슨 뜻이었던 거요?"], ["Perhaps I am quite behind the times and can’t understand.", "어쩌면 내가 시대에 한참 뒤떨어져서 이해를 못 하는 건지도 모르지."], ["For goodness’ sake, explain it, my dear boy.", "제발이지 좀 설명해 주시오, 이보게 젊은이."], ["Expound the latest theories!”", "최신 이론을 한번 펼쳐 보시구려!\""]], [["“You couldn’t have heard anything.", "\"당신은 아무것도 듣지 못했을 거요."], ["You’re making it all up!”", "죄다 지어내는 거잖소!\""]], [["“But I’m not talking about that (though I did hear something).", "\"하지만 나는 그 얘기를 하는 게 아니오(하긴 뭔가 듣긴 했지만)."], ["No, I’m talking of the way you keep sighing and groaning now.", "아니, 내가 말하려는 건 당신이 지금 이렇게 끊임없이 한숨을 쉬고 신음하는 그 꼴이오."], ["The Schiller in you is in revolt every moment, and now you tell me not to listen at doors.", "당신 안의 실러가 시도 때도 없이 반란을 일으키고 있는데, 그러면서 이제 와 나더러 문에 귀를 대지 말라고 하는군."], ["If that’s how you feel, go and inform the police that you had this mischance: you made a little mistake in your theory.", "그렇게 느낀다면, 가서 경찰에 신고하시오, 이런 불운을 겪었노라고. 당신은 자기 이론에서 작은 실수를 저질렀다고 말이오."], ["But if you are convinced that one mustn’t listen at doors, but one may murder old women at one’s pleasure, you’d better be off to America and make haste.", "하지만 문에 귀를 대선 안 되지만 노파쯤은 제 마음껏 죽여도 된다고 확신한다면, 차라리 어서 서둘러 미국으로 떠나는 게 낫겠소. 달아나시오, 젊은이!"], ["Run, young man! There may still be time.", "어쩌면 아직 시간이 있을지도 모르오."], ["I’m speaking sincerely.", "나는 진심으로 하는 말이오."], ["Haven’t you the money?", "돈이 없소?"], ["I’ll give you the fare.”", "내가 차비를 주리다.\""]], [["“I’m not thinking of that at all,”", "\"나는 그런 건 전혀 생각하고 있지 않소.\""], ["Raskolnikov interrupted with disgust.", "라스콜니코프가 역겨운 듯 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“I understand (but don’t put yourself out, don’t discuss it if you don’t want to).", "\"이해하오(하지만 무리하지 마시오, 말하고 싶지 않으면 따지지 말고)."], ["I understand the questions you are worrying over--moral ones, aren’t they?", "당신이 골머리를 앓고 있는 그 문제들을 나도 이해한단 말이오. 도덕적인 문제들이지, 안 그렇소?"], ["Duties of citizen and man?", "시민으로서, 또 인간으로서의 의무 말이오?"], ["Lay them all aside.", "그딴 건 죄다 제쳐 두시오."], ["They are nothing to you now, ha-ha!", "이제 당신에게는 아무 의미도 없으니까, 하하!"], ["You’ll say you are still a man and a citizen.", "당신은 여전히 자기가 인간이고 시민이라고 말하겠지."], ["If so you ought not to have got into this coil.", "그렇다면 이런 곤경에 빠져들지 말았어야지."], ["It’s no use taking up a job you are not fit for.", "자기에게 맞지도 않는 일에 손을 대 봐야 소용없는 법이오."], ["Well, you’d better shoot yourself, or don’t you want to?”", "자, 차라리 권총으로 자살하는 게 낫겠소, 아니면 그러기도 싫소?\""]], [["“You seem trying to enrage me, to make me leave you.”", "\"당신은 나를 화나게 해서, 당신 곁을 떠나게 만들려는 모양이군.\""]], [["“What a queer fellow!", "\"이 별난 양반을 봤나!"], ["But here we are.", "하지만 다 왔소."], ["Welcome to the staircase.", "계단에 온 걸 환영하오."], ["You see, that’s the way to Sofya Semyonovna.", "보시오, 이쪽이 소피야 세묘노브나에게 가는 길이오."], ["Look, there is no one at home.", "봐요, 집에 아무도 없잖소. 못 믿겠소?"], ["Don’t you believe me? Ask Kapernaumov.", "카페르나우모프에게 물어보시오."], ["She leaves the key with him.", "그녀는 그에게 열쇠를 맡겨 두니까."], ["Here is Madame de Kapernaumov herself.", "자, 여기 카페르나우모프 부인이 직접 나오는군."], ["Hey, what?", "어이, 뭐요?"], ["She is rather deaf. Has she gone out?", "이 사람은 귀가 좀 먹었거든."], ["Where?", "외출했소?"], ["Did you hear?", "어디로? 들었소?"], ["She is not in and won’t be till late in the evening probably.", "그녀는 집에 없고, 아마 저녁 늦게나 돼야 돌아올 거요."], ["Well, come to my room; you wanted to come and see me, didn’t you?", "자, 내 방으로 오시오. 당신, 나를 보러 오고 싶다고 했잖소, 안 그랬소?"], ["Here we are.", "자, 다 왔소."], ["Madame Resslich’s not at home.", "레슬리히 부인은 집에 없군."], ["She is a woman who is always busy, an excellent woman I assure you....", "그 여자는 늘 바쁜 사람이오, 정말 훌륭한 여자지……."], ["She might have been of use to you if you had been a little more sensible.", "당신이 조금만 더 분별이 있었다면 당신에게도 쓸모가 있었을 텐데."], ["Now, see!", "자, 보시오!"], ["I take this five-per-cent bond out of the bureau--see what a lot I’ve got of them still--this one will be turned into cash to-day.", "내가 책상에서 이 오 푼 이자 채권을 꺼내는데, 아직 이렇게나 많이 가지고 있는 걸 보시오. 이건 오늘 현금으로 바꿀 거요."], ["I mustn’t waste any more time.", "더는 시간을 낭비할 수 없소."], ["The bureau is locked, the flat is locked, and here we are again on the stairs.", "책상도 잠그고, 셋집도 잠그고, 자, 우리는 다시 계단에 와 있소."], ["Shall we take a cab?", "마차를 탈까?"], ["I’m going to the Islands.", "나는 섬으로 가는 길이오."], ["Would you like a lift?", "좀 태워 드릴까?"], ["I’ll take this carriage.", "이 마차를 잡겠소."], ["Ah, you refuse?", "아, 거절하는 거요?"], ["You are tired of it!", "이젠 질렸군그래!"], ["Come for a drive!", "같이 드라이브나 갑시다!"], ["I believe it will come on to rain.", "곧 비가 올 것 같소."], ["Never mind, we’ll put down the hood....”", "상관없소, 포장을 내리면 되니까…….\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov was already in the carriage.", "스비드리가일로프는 어느새 마차에 올라타 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov decided that his suspicions were at least for that moment unjust.", "라스콜니코프는 적어도 이 순간만큼은 자기 의심이 부당한 것이라고 결론지었다."], ["Without answering a word he turned and walked back towards the Hay Market.", "그는 한마디 대꾸도 없이 몸을 돌려 건초 시장 쪽으로 되돌아 걸었다."], ["If he had only turned round on his way he might have seen Svidrigaïlov get out not a hundred paces off, dismiss the cab and walk along the pavement.", "만일 그가 가던 길에 한 번이라도 뒤를 돌아보았더라면, 스비드리가일로프가 백 걸음도 채 못 가서 마차에서 내려 마차를 돌려보내고 보도를 따라 걸어가는 모습을 보았을 것이다."], ["But he had turned the corner and could see nothing.", "그러나 그는 이미 모퉁이를 돌아 아무것도 볼 수 없었다."], ["Intense disgust drew him away from Svidrigaïlov.", "강렬한 혐오가 그를 스비드리가일로프에게서 멀찍이 떼어 놓았다."]], [["“To think that I could for one instant have looked for help from that coarse brute, that depraved sensualist and blackguard!”", "\"내가 단 한순간이라도 저런 천박한 짐승, 저런 타락한 호색한이자 악당에게 도움을 구하려 했다니!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["Raskolnikov’s judgment was uttered too lightly and hastily: there was something about Svidrigaïlov which gave him a certain original, even a mysterious character.", "라스콜니코프의 판단은 너무 가볍고 성급하게 내려진 것이었다. 스비드리가일로프에게는 어딘가 독특하고, 심지어 신비롭기까지 한 무언가가 있었던 것이다."], ["As concerned his sister, Raskolnikov was convinced that Svidrigaïlov would not leave her in peace.", "누이에 관한 한, 라스콜니코프는 스비드리가일로프가 그녀를 가만히 내버려 두지 않으리라 확신했다."], ["But it was too tiresome and unbearable to go on thinking and thinking about this.", "그러나 이 일을 두고 끝없이 생각하고 또 생각한다는 것은 너무도 지긋지긋하고 견딜 수 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["When he was alone, he had not gone twenty paces before he sank, as usual, into deep thought.", "혼자가 되자, 그는 스무 걸음도 못 가서, 늘 그렇듯 깊은 생각에 잠겨 들었다."], ["On the bridge he stood by the railing and began gazing at the water.", "다리 위에서 그는 난간 옆에 멈춰 서서 물을 바라보기 시작했다."], ["And his sister was standing close by him.", "그리고 그의 누이가 바로 그 가까이에 서 있었다."]], [["He met her at the entrance to the bridge, but passed by without seeing her.", "그는 다리 입구에서 그녀와 마주쳤으나, 보지도 못한 채 그냥 지나쳐 버렸다."], ["Dounia had never met him like this in the street before and was struck with dismay.", "두냐는 지금껏 거리에서 그가 이런 모습을 한 것을 본 적이 없었기에 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았다."], ["She stood still and did not know whether to call to him or not.", "그녀는 멈춰 선 채, 그를 불러야 할지 말아야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Suddenly she saw Svidrigaïlov coming quickly from the direction of the Hay Market.", "그때 갑자기 그녀는 스비드리가일로프가 건초 시장 쪽에서 빠르게 다가오는 것을 보았다."]], [["He seemed to be approaching cautiously.", "그는 조심스럽게 다가오는 듯했다."], ["He did not go on to the bridge, but stood aside on the pavement, doing all he could to avoid Raskolnikov’s seeing him.", "그는 다리 위로 올라서지 않고 보도 한쪽에 비켜선 채, 라스콜니코프의 눈에 띄지 않으려고 안간힘을 다했다."], ["He had observed Dounia for some time and had been making signs to her.", "그는 한동안 두냐를 지켜보면서 그녀에게 신호를 보내고 있었다."], ["She fancied he was signalling to beg her not to speak to her brother, but to come to him.", "그녀는 그 신호가 오빠에게 말을 걸지 말고 자기에게로 와 달라고 청하는 것이라고 짐작했다."]], [["That was what Dounia did.", "두냐는 그렇게 했다."], ["She stole by her brother and went up to Svidrigaïlov.", "그녀는 오빠 곁을 살그머니 지나쳐 스비드리가일로프에게로 다가갔다."]], [["“Let us make haste away,”", "\"어서 자리를 뜹시다.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov whispered to her, “I don’t want Rodion Romanovitch to know of our meeting.", "스비드리가일로프가 그녀에게 속삭였다. \"로디온 로마노비치가 우리가 만난 걸 알게 하고 싶지 않소."], ["I must tell you I’ve been sitting with him in the restaurant close by, where he looked me up and I had great difficulty in getting rid of him.", "말해 두지만, 나는 방금 바로 저 근처 식당에서 그와 함께 앉아 있었소. 그가 나를 찾아왔는데, 떼어 내느라 어찌나 애를 먹었는지."], ["He has somehow heard of my letter to you and suspects something.", "그가 어찌어찌 내가 당신에게 보낸 편지에 대해 듣고는 뭔가를 의심하고 있소."], ["It wasn’t you who told him, of course, but if not you, who then?”", "물론 당신이 그에게 말한 건 아니겠지만, 당신이 아니라면 대체 누구겠소?\""]], [["“Well, we’ve turned the corner now,”", "\"자, 이제 우리는 모퉁이를 돌았으니,\""], ["Dounia interrupted, “and my brother won’t see us.", "두냐가 말을 가로막았다. \"오빠는 우리를 보지 못할 거예요."], ["I have to tell you that I am going no further with you.", "말해 두는데, 나는 더 이상 당신과 함께 가지 않겠어요."], ["Speak to me here.", "여기서 말하세요."], ["You can tell it all in the street.”", "거리에서도 다 말할 수 있잖아요.\""]], [["“In the first place, I can’t say it in the street; secondly, you must hear Sofya Semyonovna too; and, thirdly, I will show you some papers....", "\"첫째, 거리에서는 말할 수가 없소. 둘째, 당신은 소피야 세묘노브나의 이야기도 들어야 하오."], ["Oh well, if you won’t agree to come with me, I shall refuse to give any explanation and go away at once.", "셋째, 당신에게 몇 가지 서류를 보여 주겠소……. 흠, 좋소, 나와 함께 가는 데 동의하지 않겠다면, 나는 아무 설명도 하지 않고 당장 떠나 버리겠소."], ["But I beg you not to forget that a very curious secret of your beloved brother’s is entirely in my keeping.”", "다만 한 가지, 당신의 사랑하는 오빠의 아주 묘한 비밀이 전적으로 내 손에 쥐여 있다는 사실을 잊지 마시오.\""]], [["Dounia stood still, hesitating, and looked at Svidrigaïlov with searching eyes.", "두냐는 망설이며 멈춰 서서, 캐묻는 눈으로 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다."]], [["“What are you afraid of?”", "\"무엇이 두렵소?\""], ["he observed quietly.", "그가 조용히 말했다."], ["“The town is not the country.", "\"도시는 시골과 다르오."], ["And even in the country you did me more harm than I did you.”", "게다가 시골에서도 당신이 내게 끼친 해가 내가 당신에게 끼친 것보다 더 컸소.\""]], [["“Have you prepared Sofya Semyonovna?”", "\"소피야 세묘노브나에게 미리 말해 두었나요?\""]], [["“No, I have not said a word to her and am not quite certain whether she is at home now.", "\"아니, 그녀에게는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 지금 그녀가 집에 있는지조차 확실치 않소."], ["But most likely she is.", "하지만 십중팔구는 있을 거요."], ["She has buried her stepmother to-day: she is not likely to go visiting on such a day.", "오늘 계모를 묻었으니, 그런 날 어디 방문하러 나갈 리는 없겠지."], ["For the time I don’t want to speak to anyone about it and I half regret having spoken to you.", "당분간은 이 일을 누구에게도 말하고 싶지 않고, 당신에게 말한 것조차 반쯤은 후회하고 있소."], ["The slightest indiscretion is as bad as betrayal in a thing like this.", "이런 일에서는 아주 사소한 경솔함도 배신만큼이나 나쁜 거요. 나는 저 집에 살고 있소, 이제 거의 다 왔군."], ["I live there in that house, we are coming to it.", "저게 우리 집 문지기인데, 나를 아주 잘 알지. 보시오, 인사를 하잖소."], ["That’s the porter of our house--he knows me very well; you see, he’s bowing; he sees I’m coming with a lady and no doubt he has noticed your face already and you will be glad of that if you are afraid of me and suspicious.", "그는 내가 어떤 부인과 함께 오는 걸 보았고, 분명 이미 당신 얼굴도 눈여겨봤을 거요. 당신이 나를 두려워하고 의심한다면 그 점이 다행스러울 거요."], ["Excuse my putting things so coarsely.", "이렇게 거칠게 말하는 걸 용서하시오."], ["I haven’t a flat to myself; Sofya Semyonovna’s room is next to mine--she lodges in the next flat.", "나는 셋집을 혼자 다 쓰지는 않소. 소피야 세묘노브나의 방이 내 방 옆에 붙어 있는데, 그녀는 옆 셋집에 세 들어 살고 있소."], ["The whole floor is let out in lodgings.", "이 층 전체가 셋방으로 나뉘어 있지."], ["Why are you frightened like a child?", "어째서 어린애처럼 겁을 먹는 거요?"], ["Am I really so terrible?”", "내가 정말 그렇게 무섭소?\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov’s lips were twisted in a condescending smile; but he was in no smiling mood.", "스비드리가일로프의 입술은 짐짓 너그러운 미소로 비틀려 있었다. 그러나 그는 웃을 기분이 전혀 아니었다."], ["His heart was throbbing and he could scarcely breathe.", "심장이 두근거려 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없었다."], ["He spoke rather loud to cover his growing excitement.", "그는 점점 커져 가는 흥분을 감추려고 일부러 좀 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["But Dounia did not notice this peculiar excitement, she was so irritated by his remark that she was frightened of him like a child and that he was so terrible to her.", "그러나 두냐는 이 묘한 흥분을 알아채지 못했으니, 자기가 어린애처럼 그를 두려워한다느니, 그가 그렇게 무서우냐느니 하는 그의 말에 잔뜩 화가 나 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Though I know that you are not a man...", "\"당신이 명예로운……"], ["of honour, I am not in the least afraid of you.", "인간이 아니라는 걸 알지만, 나는 당신이 조금도 두렵지 않아요."], ["Lead the way,”", "앞장서세요.\""], ["she said with apparent composure, but her face was very pale.", "그녀는 짐짓 침착한 척 말했지만, 얼굴은 몹시 창백했다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov stopped at Sonia’s room.", "스비드리가일로프는 소냐의 방 앞에서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Allow me to inquire whether she is at home....", "\"그녀가 집에 있는지 여쭤봐도 되겠소……. 없군."], ["She is not. How unfortunate!", "이런 운도 없을 데가!"], ["But I know she may come quite soon.", "하지만 곧 돌아올 수도 있다는 걸 나는 알고 있소."], ["If she’s gone out, it can only be to see a lady about the orphans.", "외출했다면 고아들 일로 그 부인을 만나러 간 것밖에 없을 거요."], ["Their mother is dead....", "아이들 어머니가 죽었거든……."], ["I’ve been meddling and making arrangements for them.", "내가 그 애들 일에 끼어들어 이런저런 조처를 해 두었소."], ["If Sofya Semyonovna does not come back in ten minutes, I will send her to you, to-day if you like.", "만일 소피야 세묘노브나가 십 분 안에 돌아오지 않으면, 그녀를 당신에게 보내겠소, 원한다면 오늘이라도."], ["This is my flat.", "이게 내 셋집이오."], ["These are my two rooms.", "이 두 개가 내 방이오."], ["Madame Resslich, my landlady, has the next room.", "내 집주인인 레슬리히 부인이 옆방을 쓰고 있소."], ["Now, look this way.", "자, 이쪽을 보시오."], ["I will show you my chief piece of evidence: this door from my bedroom leads into two perfectly empty rooms, which are to let.", "내 가장 중요한 증거를 보여 주겠소. 내 침실에서 난 이 문이 텅 빈 두 개의 방으로 통하는데, 그 방들은 세놓는 중이오."], ["Here they are...", "자, 여기요……."], ["You must look into them with some attention.”", "좀 주의 깊게 들여다봐야 하오.\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov occupied two fairly large furnished rooms.", "스비드리가일로프는 가구가 딸린 제법 넓은 방 두 개를 쓰고 있었다."], ["Dounia was looking about her mistrustfully, but saw nothing special in the furniture or position of the rooms.", "두냐는 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았으나, 가구나 방의 배치에서 특별한 점은 아무것도 발견하지 못했다."], ["Yet there was something to observe, for instance, that Svidrigaïlov’s flat was exactly between two sets of almost uninhabited apartments.", "그러나 눈여겨볼 만한 것이 있었으니, 예컨대 스비드리가일로프의 셋집이 거의 사람이 살지 않는 두 채의 셋방 사이에 정확히 끼여 있다는 점이었다."], ["His rooms were not entered directly from the passage, but through the landlady’s two almost empty rooms.", "그의 방들은 복도에서 곧장 들어오는 게 아니라, 집주인의 거의 텅 빈 방 두 개를 거쳐야 들어올 수 있었다."], ["Unlocking a door leading out of his bedroom, Svidrigaïlov showed Dounia the two empty rooms that were to let.", "스비드리가일로프는 침실에서 통하는 문을 열어 두냐에게 세놓는 중인 텅 빈 방 두 개를 보여 주었다."], ["Dounia stopped in the doorway, not knowing what she was called to look upon, but Svidrigaïlov hastened to explain.", "두냐는 자기가 무엇을 보라는 것인지 알 수 없어 문간에 멈춰 섰으나, 스비드리가일로프가 서둘러 설명했다."]], [["“Look here, at this second large room.", "\"여기, 이 두 번째 큰 방을 보시오."], ["Notice that door, it’s locked.", "저 문을 눈여겨봐요, 잠겨 있소."], ["By the door stands a chair, the only one in the two rooms.", "문 옆에 의자가 하나 놓여 있는데, 이 두 방을 통틀어 하나뿐인 의자요."], ["I brought it from my rooms so as to listen more conveniently.", "더 편하게 엿듣기 위해 내 방에서 가져온 거지."], ["Just the other side of the door is Sofya Semyonovna’s table; she sat there talking to Rodion Romanovitch.", "바로 저 문 너머에 소피야 세묘노브나의 탁자가 있소. 그녀가 거기 앉아 로디온 로마노비치와 이야기를 나누었지."], ["And I sat here listening on two successive evenings, for two hours each time--and of course I was able to learn something, what do you think?”", "그리고 나는 여기 앉아 이틀 밤을 잇따라, 매번 두 시간씩 귀를 기울였소. 그러니 당연히 뭔가를 알아낼 수 있었지, 어떻게 생각하오?\""]], [["“You listened?”", "\"엿들었군요?\""]], [["“Yes, I did.", "\"그렇소, 엿들었소."], ["Now come back to my room; we can’t sit down here.”", "자, 이제 내 방으로 돌아갑시다. 여기서는 앉아 있을 수가 없으니.\""]], [["He brought Avdotya Romanovna back into his sitting-room and offered her a chair.", "그는 아브도티야 로마노브나를 자기 거실로 데려와 의자를 권했다."], ["He sat down at the opposite side of the table, at least seven feet from her, but probably there was the same glow in his eyes which had once frightened Dounia so much.", "그는 탁자 맞은편, 적어도 그녀에게서 일곱 자쯤 떨어진 자리에 앉았으나, 아마도 그의 눈에는 언젠가 두냐를 그토록 겁에 질리게 했던 바로 그 불꽃이 타오르고 있었을 것이다."], ["She shuddered and once more looked about her distrustfully.", "그녀는 몸서리치며 다시 한번 미심쩍은 눈으로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["It was an involuntary gesture; she evidently did not wish to betray her uneasiness.", "그것은 자기도 모르게 나온 몸짓이었다. 그녀는 분명 자신의 불안을 드러내고 싶지 않았다."], ["But the secluded position of Svidrigaïlov’s lodging had suddenly struck her.", "그러나 스비드리가일로프의 하숙집이 외따로 떨어져 있다는 사실이 문득 그녀의 마음을 쳤다."], ["She wanted to ask whether his landlady at least were at home, but pride kept her from asking.", "그녀는 적어도 집주인 여자라도 집에 있는지 묻고 싶었으나, 자존심 때문에 차마 묻지 못했다."], ["Moreover, she had another trouble in her heart incomparably greater than fear for herself.", "게다가 그녀의 마음속에는 자기 자신에 대한 두려움 따위와는 비교도 안 될 만큼 큰 또 다른 근심이 자리하고 있었다."], ["She was in great distress.", "그녀는 깊은 고뇌에 빠져 있었다."]], [["“Here is your letter,”", "\"여기 당신 편지가 있어요.\""], ["she said, laying it on the table.", "그녀가 그것을 탁자 위에 내려놓으며 말했다."], ["“Can it be true what you write?", "\"여기 쓴 게 사실일 수 있나요?"], ["You hint at a crime committed, you say, by my brother.", "당신은 내 오빠가 저질렀다는 범죄를 암시하고 있어요."], ["You hint at it too clearly; you daren’t deny it now.", "너무 노골적으로 암시했으니, 이제 와서 부인할 수는 없겠죠."], ["I must tell you that I’d heard of this stupid story before you wrote and don’t believe a word of it.", "말해 두는데, 나는 당신이 이 편지를 쓰기 전에 이미 그 어처구니없는 이야기를 들었고, 한마디도 믿지 않아요."], ["It’s a disgusting and ridiculous suspicion.", "그건 역겹고 우스꽝스러운 의심일 뿐이에요."], ["I know the story and why and how it was invented.", "나는 그 이야기가 어떻게, 왜 꾸며진 것인지 알고 있어요."], ["You can have no proofs.", "당신에게는 아무런 증거도 있을 수 없어요."], ["You promised to prove it. Speak!", "당신은 그걸 증명하겠다고 약속했죠."], ["But let me warn you that I don’t believe you!", "말해 보세요! 하지만 미리 경고하는데, 나는 당신을 믿지 않아요!"], ["I don’t believe you!”", "나는 당신을 믿지 않는다고요!\""]], [["Dounia said this, speaking hurriedly, and for an instant the colour rushed to her face.", "두냐는 서둘러 이렇게 말했고, 한순간 그녀의 얼굴로 핏기가 솟구쳤다."]], [["“If you didn’t believe it, how could you risk coming alone to my rooms?", "\"믿지 않는다면, 어떻게 혼자서 위험을 무릅쓰고 내 방까지 왔단 말이오?"], ["Why have you come?", "왜 온 거요?"], ["Simply from curiosity?”", "그저 호기심에서?\""]], [["“Don’t torment me.", "\"나를 괴롭히지 마세요."], ["Speak, speak!”", "말해요, 말하라고요!\""]], [["“There’s no denying that you are a brave girl.", "\"당신이 용감한 처녀라는 건 부정할 수 없군."], ["Upon my word, I thought you would have asked Mr. Razumihin to escort you here.", "정말이지, 나는 당신이 라주미힌 씨더러 여기까지 데려다 달라고 청할 줄 알았소."], ["But he was not with you nor anywhere near.", "그런데 그는 당신과 함께 있지도, 근처 어디에도 있지 않았소."], ["I was on the look-out.", "나는 줄곧 살피고 있었거든."], ["It’s spirited of you, it proves you wanted to spare Rodion Romanovitch.", "당신의 그 기개라니, 그건 당신이 로디온 로마노비치를 감싸려 했다는 증거요."], ["But everything is divine in you....", "하지만 당신에게는 모든 게 거룩하기만 하군……."], ["About your brother, what am I to say to you?", "당신 오빠에 관해서라면, 내가 뭐라고 말해야 하겠소?"], ["You’ve just seen him yourself.", "당신도 방금 직접 그를 보았잖소."], ["What did you think of him?”", "그가 어떻게 보였소?\""]], [["“Surely that’s not the only thing you are building on?”", "\"설마 당신이 근거로 삼는 게 그것뿐인가요?\""]], [["“No, not on that, but on his own words.", "\"아니, 그것만은 아니오, 그 자신의 말에 근거한 거지."], ["He came here on two successive evenings to see Sofya Semyonovna.", "그는 이틀 밤을 잇따라 소피야 세묘노브나를 만나러 여기 왔소."], ["I’ve shown you where they sat.", "두 사람이 어디 앉아 있었는지는 당신에게 보여 주었고."], ["He made a full confession to her.", "그는 그녀에게 죄다 털어놓았소."], ["He is a murderer.", "그는 살인자요."], ["He killed an old woman, a pawnbroker, with whom he had pawned things himself.", "그는 노파를, 자기가 직접 물건을 저당 잡혔던 어느 전당포 노파를 죽였소."], ["He killed her sister too, a pedlar woman called Lizaveta, who happened to come in while he was murdering her sister.", "그리고 그녀의 동생도 죽였는데, 리자베타라는 행상 여자로, 그가 언니를 죽이고 있는 동안 마침 들어왔던 거요."], ["He killed them with an axe he brought with him.", "그는 그들을 자기가 가져간 도끼로 죽였소."], ["He murdered them to rob them and he did rob them.", "그는 그들을 죽이고 돈을 빼앗았고, 실제로 빼앗았소."], ["He took money and various things....", "돈과 여러 물건을 가져갔지……."], ["He told all this, word for word, to Sofya Semyonovna, the only person who knows his secret.", "그는 이 모든 걸 토씨 하나 틀리지 않고 소피야 세묘노브나에게 말했소, 그의 비밀을 아는 유일한 사람이지."], ["But she has had no share by word or deed in the murder; she was as horrified at it as you are now.", "하지만 그녀는 말로도 행동으로도 그 살인에 가담하지 않았소. 그녀도 지금의 당신만큼이나 그 일에 경악했으니까."], ["Don’t be anxious, she won’t betray him.”", "걱정 마시오, 그녀는 그를 배신하지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“It cannot be,”", "\"그럴 리 없어요.\""], ["muttered Dounia, with white lips.", "두냐가 새하얗게 질린 입술로 중얼거렸다."], ["She gasped for breath.", "그녀는 숨이 막혀 헐떡였다."], ["“It cannot be.", "\"그럴 리 없어요."], ["There was not the slightest cause, no sort of ground....", "손톱만큼의 이유도, 그 어떤 까닭도 없었어요……."], ["It’s a lie, a lie!”", "거짓말이에요, 거짓말!\""]], [["“He robbed her, that was the cause, he took money and things.", "\"그가 그녀의 물건을 훔쳤소, 그게 이유였지, 돈과 물건을 가져갔으니까."], ["It’s true that by his own admission he made no use of the money or things, but hid them under a stone, where they are now.", "하기야 그 자신의 고백으로는, 그 돈도 물건도 쓰지 않고 돌멩이 밑에 숨겨 두었는데, 지금도 거기 그대로 있다더군."], ["But that was because he dared not make use of them.”", "하지만 그건 감히 그것들을 쓸 엄두가 나지 않았기 때문이오.\""]], [["“But how could he steal, rob?", "\"하지만 어떻게 그가 도둑질을, 강도질을 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["How could he dream of it?”", "어떻게 그런 걸 꿈엔들 생각하겠어요?\""], ["cried Dounia, and she jumped up from the chair.", "두냐가 외치며 의자에서 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["“Why, you know him, and you’ve seen him, can he be a thief?”", "\"아니, 당신도 그를 알고, 그를 직접 봤잖아요, 그가 도둑일 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["She seemed to be imploring Svidrigaïlov; she had entirely forgotten her fear.", "그녀는 마치 스비드리가일로프에게 애원하는 듯했다. 그녀는 자신의 두려움을 완전히 잊고 있었다."]], [["“There are thousands and millions of combinations and possibilities, Avdotya Romanovna.", "\"수천수만 가지 조합과 가능성이 있는 거요, 아브도티야 로마노브나."], ["A thief steals and knows he is a scoundrel, but I’ve heard of a gentleman who broke open the mail.", "도둑은 훔치면서 자기가 악당임을 알지만, 우편 마차를 털어 부순 어느 신사 이야기를 나는 들은 적이 있소."], ["Who knows, very likely he thought he was doing a gentlemanly thing!", "누가 알겠소, 어쩌면 그는 자기가 신사다운 일을 하고 있다고 여겼을지도!"], ["Of course I should not have believed it myself if I’d been told of it as you have, but I believe my own ears.", "물론 나도, 당신처럼 누군가에게서 그 이야기를 전해 들었다면 믿지 않았겠지. 하지만 나는 내 두 귀를 믿소."], ["He explained all the causes of it to Sofya Semyonovna too, but she did not believe her ears at first, yet she believed her own eyes at last.”", "그는 소피야 세묘노브나에게도 그 모든 이유를 설명했소. 그녀도 처음에는 제 귀를 믿지 못했지만, 결국에는 제 눈을 믿게 되었지.\""]], [["“What...", "\"무슨……"], ["were the causes?”", "이유였나요?\""]], [["“It’s a long story, Avdotya Romanovna. Here’s...", "\"긴 이야기요, 아브도티야 로마노브나."], ["how shall I tell you?--A theory of a sort, the same one by which I for instance consider that a single misdeed is permissible if the principal aim is right, a solitary wrongdoing and hundreds of good deeds!", "자……, 어떻게 말해야 할까? 일종의 이론인데, 이를테면 나 자신도, 만일 주된 목적이 옳다면 단 한 번의 악행은 허용된다고 여기는 바로 그 이론이오."], ["It’s galling too, of course, for a young man of gifts and overweening pride to know that if he had, for instance, a paltry three thousand, his whole career, his whole future would be differently shaped and yet not to have that three thousand.", "하나의 악행에 수백 가지 선행이라! 물론, 재능 있고 자존심이 하늘을 찌르는 젊은이로서는, 예컨대 자기에게 하찮은 삼천 루블만 있었어도 자기 인생 전체, 미래 전체가 다르게 풀렸을 텐데 정작 그 삼천 루블이 없다는 걸 아는 게 분통 터지는 일이기도 하지."], ["Add to that, nervous irritability from hunger, from lodging in a hole, from rags, from a vivid sense of the charm of his social position and his sister’s and mother’s position too.", "거기에다 굶주림에서, 누추한 굴 같은 데서 사는 데서, 누더기에서, 그리고 자기 사회적 처지며 누이와 어머니의 처지를 생생히 느끼는 데서 오는 신경의 자극이 더해지는 거요. 무엇보다 허영심, 자존심과 허영심이지, 물론 그에게 좋은 자질도 있을지 모르지만 말이오……."], ["Above all, vanity, pride and vanity, though goodness knows he may have good qualities too....", "나는 그를 탓하는 게 아니오, 부디 그렇게 생각하지 마시오. 게다가 그건 내 알 바도 아니고."], ["I am not blaming him, please don’t think it; besides, it’s not my business.", "거기에 또 하나의 특별하고 자그마한 이론이 끼어들었소."], ["A special little theory came in too--a theory of a sort--dividing mankind, you see, into material and superior persons, that is persons to whom the law does not apply owing to their superiority, who make laws for the rest of mankind, the material, that is.", "일종의 이론인데, 알다시피 인류를 평범한 사람과 비범한 사람으로 나누는 거요. 즉 자신의 비범함 덕분에 법이 적용되지 않는 사람들, 나머지 인류, 그러니까 평범한 자들을 위해 법을 만드는 사람들 말이오."], ["It’s all right as a theory, _une théorie comme une autre_.", "이론으로서야 나쁘지 않지, 그저 다른 이론들과 다를 바 없는 하나의 이론이오."], ["Napoleon attracted him tremendously, that is, what affected him was that a great many men of genius have not hesitated at wrongdoing, but have overstepped the law without thinking about it.", "나폴레옹이 그를 엄청나게 사로잡았는데, 다시 말해 그를 사로잡은 건, 수많은 천재들이 악행 앞에서 망설이지 않고 별생각 없이 법을 뛰어넘었다는 사실이오."], ["He seems to have fancied that he was a genius too--that is, he was convinced of it for a time.", "그는 자기도 천재라고 여겼던 모양이오. 다시 말해 한동안은 그렇게 확신했던 거지."], ["He has suffered a great deal and is still suffering from the idea that he could make a theory, but was incapable of boldly overstepping the law, and so he is not a man of genius.", "그는 자기가 이론은 만들어 낼 수 있었으나 대담하게 법을 뛰어넘을 능력은 없었고, 그래서 자기가 천재가 아니라는 생각 때문에 무척이나 괴로워했고 지금도 괴로워하고 있소."], ["And that’s humiliating for a young man of any pride, in our day especially....”", "그건 자존심 있는 젊은이에게는 굴욕적인 일이지, 특히 요즘 같은 시대에는…….\""]], [["“But remorse?", "\"하지만 양심의 가책은요?"], ["You deny him any moral feeling then?", "그럼 당신은 그에게 도덕적 감정 따위는 전혀 없다는 건가요?"], ["Is he like that?”", "그가 그런 사람인가요?\""]], [["“Ah, Avdotya Romanovna, everything is in a muddle now; not that it was ever in very good order.", "\"아, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 요즘은 모든 게 뒤죽박죽이오. 하긴 한 번도 그리 질서정연한 적은 없었지만."], ["Russians in general are broad in their ideas, Avdotya Romanovna, broad like their land and exceedingly disposed to the fantastic, the chaotic.", "러시아인들은 대체로 생각이 넓소, 아브도티야 로마노브나, 자기네 땅처럼 넓고, 환상적인 것, 혼돈스러운 것에 더할 나위 없이 기우는 경향이 있지."], ["But it’s a misfortune to be broad without a special genius.", "하지만 특별한 천재성도 없이 넓기만 한 건 불행이오."], ["Do you remember what a lot of talk we had together on this subject, sitting in the evenings on the terrace after supper?", "우리가 이 문제를 놓고 저녁마다 저녁 식사 후 테라스에 앉아 얼마나 많은 이야기를 나누었는지 기억하시오?"], ["Why, you used to reproach me with breadth!", "아니, 당신은 그 넓이를 두고 나를 나무라곤 했잖소!"], ["Who knows, perhaps we were talking at the very time when he was lying here thinking over his plan.", "누가 알겠소, 어쩌면 바로 그 무렵에 그는 여기 누워 자기 계획을 곰곰이 짜고 있었을지도 모르지."], ["There are no sacred traditions amongst us, especially in the educated class, Avdotya Romanovna.", "우리에게는 신성한 전통이라는 게 없소, 특히 교육받은 계층에서는 말이오, 아브도티야 로마노브나."], ["At the best someone will make them up somehow for himself out of books or from some old chronicle.", "기껏해야 누군가가 책이나 어떤 낡은 연대기에서 끄집어내 그럭저럭 자기 나름대로 만들어 내는 정도지."], ["But those are for the most part the learned and all old fogeys, so that it would be almost ill-bred in a man of society.", "하지만 그런 건 대개 학자들이나 케케묵은 늙은이들이 하는 일이라, 사교계 사람에게는 거의 무례한 짓이 될 거요."], ["You know my opinions in general, though.", "하긴 당신도 대체로 내 견해를 알고 있잖소."], ["I never blame anyone.", "나는 결코 누구도 탓하지 않소."], ["I do nothing at all, I persevere in that.", "나는 아무것도 하지 않고, 그 점을 한결같이 지켜 나가지."], ["But we’ve talked of this more than once before.", "하지만 우리는 전에도 이 얘기를 여러 번 나눴소."], ["I was so happy indeed as to interest you in my opinions....", "사실 나는 당신이 내 견해에 흥미를 느끼게 할 만큼 행복하기도 했지……."], ["You are very pale, Avdotya Romanovna.”", "당신, 안색이 몹시 창백하군요, 아브도티야 로마노브나.\""]], [["“I know his theory.", "\"나는 그의 이론을 알아요."], ["I read that article of his about men to whom all is permitted.", "모든 게 허용되는 사람들에 관한 그의 논문을 읽었거든요."], ["Razumihin brought it to me.”", "라주미힌이 가져다줬어요.\""]], [["“Mr. Razumihin?", "\"라주미힌 씨가?"], ["Your brother’s article?", "당신 오빠의 논문을?"], ["In a magazine?", "잡지에?"], ["Is there such an article?", "그런 논문이 있단 말이오?"], ["I didn’t know.", "나는 몰랐군."], ["It must be interesting.", "흥미로울 게 틀림없소."], ["But where are you going, Avdotya Romanovna?”", "한데 어디로 가는 거요, 아브도티야 로마노브나?\""]], [["“I want to see Sofya Semyonovna,”", "\"소피야 세묘노브나를 만나고 싶어요.\""], ["Dounia articulated faintly.", "두냐가 가냘프게 말했다."], ["“How do I go to her?", "\"그녀에게 어떻게 가죠?"], ["She has come in, perhaps.", "어쩌면 돌아왔을지도 몰라요."], ["I must see her at once.", "당장 그녀를 봐야겠어요."], ["Perhaps she...”", "어쩌면 그녀가…….\""]], [["Avdotya Romanovna could not finish.", "아브도티야 로마노브나는 말을 맺지 못했다."], ["Her breath literally failed her.", "글자 그대로 숨이 막혔던 것이다."]], [["“Sofya Semyonovna will not be back till night, at least I believe not.", "\"소피야 세묘노브나는 밤이 돼야 돌아올 거요, 적어도 내 생각엔 그렇소."], ["She was to have been back at once, but if not, then she will not be in till quite late.”", "곧 돌아오기로 돼 있었지만, 그렇지 않다면 아주 늦게나 돌아올 거요.\""]], [["“Ah, then you are lying!", "\"아, 그럼 당신은 거짓말을 하고 있는 거예요!"], ["I see...", "알겠어요……."], ["you were lying... lying all the time....", "당신은 거짓말을 했어요……, 줄곧 거짓말을 했어요……."], ["I don’t believe you!", "나는 당신을 믿지 않아요!"], ["I don’t believe you!”", "당신을 믿지 않는다고요!\""], ["cried Dounia, completely losing her head.", "두냐가 완전히 정신을 놓은 채 외쳤다."]], [["Almost fainting, she sank on to a chair which Svidrigaïlov made haste to give her.", "거의 실신할 지경이 되어, 그녀는 스비드리가일로프가 황급히 받쳐 준 의자에 풀썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“Avdotya Romanovna, what is it?", "\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 왜 이러시오?"], ["Control yourself!", "정신을 차리시오!"], ["Here is some water.", "여기 물이 있소."], ["Drink a little....”", "조금만 마셔요…….\""]], [["He sprinkled some water over her.", "그는 그녀에게 물을 끼얹었다."], ["Dounia shuddered and came to herself.", "두냐는 몸을 떨며 정신을 차렸다."]], [["“It has acted violently,”", "\"독하게 들었군.\""], ["Svidrigaïlov muttered to himself, frowning.", "스비드리가일로프가 얼굴을 찌푸리며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["“Avdotya Romanovna, calm yourself!", "\"아브도티야 로마노브나, 진정하시오!"], ["Believe me, he has friends.", "내 말을 믿어요, 그에게는 친구들이 있소."], ["We will save him.", "우리가 그를 구할 거요."], ["Would you like me to take him abroad?", "내가 그를 외국으로 데려가길 바라오?"], ["I have money, I can get a ticket in three days.", "나에게는 돈이 있고, 사흘이면 표를 구할 수 있소."], ["And as for the murder, he will do all sorts of good deeds yet, to atone for it.", "살인이라면, 그도 앞으로 그걸 속죄하려고 온갖 선행을 베풀 거요."], ["Calm yourself.", "진정하시오."], ["He may become a great man yet.", "그는 아직 위대한 인물이 될 수도 있소."], ["Well, how are you?", "자, 좀 어떻소?"], ["How do you feel?”", "기분이 어떻소?\""]], [["“Cruel man!", "\"잔인한 사람!"], ["To be able to jeer at it!", "그런 일을 두고 비웃을 수 있다니!"], ["Let me go...”", "놓아줘요…….\""]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디로 가려는 거요?\""]], [["“To him.", "\"그에게로요."], ["Where is he?", "그가 어디 있죠?"], ["Do you know?", "알고 있나요?"], ["Why is this door locked?", "이 문은 왜 잠겨 있죠?"], ["We came in at that door and now it is locked.", "우리는 저 문으로 들어왔는데 지금은 잠겨 있어요."], ["When did you manage to lock it?”", "언제 문을 잠근 거예요?\""]], [["“We couldn’t be shouting all over the flat on such a subject.", "\"이런 일을 두고 셋집이 떠나가도록 소리를 질러 댈 수는 없잖소."], ["I am far from jeering; it’s simply that I’m sick of talking like this.", "나는 비웃는 게 결코 아니오, 그저 이런 식으로 이야기하는 데 진력이 났을 뿐이오."], ["But how can you go in such a state?", "한데 그런 상태로 어떻게 가겠다는 거요?"], ["Do you want to betray him?", "그를 배신하고 싶소?"], ["You will drive him to fury, and he will give himself up.", "당신은 그를 광분하게 만들 거고, 그러면 그는 자수해 버릴 거요."], ["Let me tell you, he is already being watched; they are already on his track.", "말해 두는데, 그는 이미 감시당하고 있소. 그들은 이미 그의 뒤를 쫓고 있단 말이오."], ["You will simply be giving him away.", "당신은 그저 그를 넘겨주는 셈이 될 뿐이오."], ["Wait a little: I saw him and was talking to him just now.", "조금만 기다리시오. 나는 방금 그를 만나 이야기를 나누고 왔소."], ["He can still be saved.", "그는 아직 구할 수 있소."], ["Wait a bit, sit down; let us think it over together.", "좀 기다려요, 앉으시오. 우리 함께 잘 궁리해 봅시다."], ["I asked you to come in order to discuss it alone with you and to consider it thoroughly.", "내가 당신더러 들어오라고 한 건 당신과 단둘이 이 일을 의논하고 철저히 따져 보기 위해서였소."], ["But do sit down!”", "자, 좀 앉아요!\""]], [["“How can you save him?", "\"당신이 어떻게 그를 구한다는 거죠?"], ["Can he really be saved?”", "그가 정말 구원받을 수 있나요?\""]], [["Dounia sat down.", "두냐는 자리에 앉았다."], ["Svidrigaïlov sat down beside her.", "스비드리가일로프도 그녀 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“It all depends on you, on you, on you alone,”", "\"그건 전적으로 당신에게, 당신에게, 오직 당신에게 달려 있소.\""], ["he began with glowing eyes, almost in a whisper and hardly able to utter the words for emotion.", "그는 이글거리는 눈으로, 거의 속삭이듯, 격정에 차 말조차 제대로 잇지 못하며 입을 열었다."]], [["Dounia drew back from him in alarm.", "두냐는 깜짝 놀라 그에게서 몸을 뒤로 뺐다."], ["He too was trembling all over.", "그 역시 온몸을 떨고 있었다."]], [["“You...", "\"당신의……"], ["one word from you, and he is saved.", "당신의 말 한마디면, 그는 구원받소. 내가……"], ["I... I’ll save him.", "내가 그를 구하겠소."], ["I have money and friends.", "내게는 돈도 친구도 있소."], ["I’ll send him away at once.", "당장 그를 떠나보내겠소."], ["I’ll get a passport, two passports, one for him and one for me.", "여권을 구하겠소, 두 개를, 하나는 그를 위해, 하나는 나를 위해."], ["I have friends... capable people....", "내게는 친구들이 있소……, 유능한 자들이지……."], ["If you like, I’ll take a passport for you... for your mother....", "원한다면 당신을 위한 여권도 구하겠소……, 당신 어머니를 위한 것도……."], ["What do you want with Razumihin?", "라주미힌이 당신에게 무슨 소용이오?"], ["I love you too....", "나도 당신을 사랑하오……."], ["I love you beyond everything....", "나는 그 무엇보다 당신을 사랑하오……."], ["Let me kiss the hem of your dress, let me, let me....", "당신 옷자락에 입 맞추게 해 주오, 제발, 제발……."], ["The very rustle of it is too much for me.", "그 옷자락 스치는 소리만으로도 나는 견딜 수가 없소."], ["Tell me, ‘do that,’ and I’ll do it.", "내게 '이걸 하라'고 말만 하시오, 그럼 하겠소."], ["I’ll do everything.", "무엇이든 하겠소."], ["I will do the impossible.", "불가능한 일이라도 해내겠소."], ["What you believe, I will believe.", "당신이 믿는 것을 나도 믿겠소."], ["I’ll do anything--anything!", "무엇이든, 무엇이든 하겠소!"], ["Don’t, don’t look at me like that.", "그러지 마오, 그런 눈으로 나를 보지 마오."], ["Do you know that you are killing me?...”", "당신이 나를 죽이고 있다는 걸 알기나 하오?……\""]], [["He was almost beginning to rave....", "그는 거의 헛소리를 늘어놓기 시작했다……."], ["Something seemed suddenly to go to his head.", "무언가가 갑자기 그의 머리로 치솟는 것 같았다."], ["Dounia jumped up and rushed to the door.", "두냐는 벌떡 일어나 문으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Open it!", "\"열어요!"], ["Open it!”", "열어 줘요!\""], ["she called, shaking the door.", "그녀가 문을 흔들며 외쳤다."], ["“Open it!", "\"열어 줘요!"], ["Is there no one there?”", "거기 아무도 없어요?\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov got up and came to himself.", "스비드리가일로프는 일어서서 정신을 차렸다."], ["His still trembling lips slowly broke into an angry mocking smile.", "여전히 떨리는 그의 입술이 천천히 성난 조소의 미소로 비틀렸다."]], [["“There is no one at home,”", "\"집에는 아무도 없소.\""], ["he said quietly and emphatically.", "그가 조용히, 또박또박 힘주어 말했다."], ["“The landlady has gone out, and it’s waste of time to shout like that.", "\"집주인 여자는 외출했고, 그렇게 소리를 질러 봐야 시간 낭비요."], ["You are only exciting yourself uselessly.”", "당신은 공연히 자기만 흥분시키고 있을 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Where is the key?", "\"열쇠는 어디 있죠?"], ["Open the door at once, at once, base man!”", "당장 문을 열어요, 당장, 비열한 인간!\""]], [["“I have lost the key and cannot find it.”", "\"열쇠를 잃어버려 찾을 수가 없소.\""]], [["“This is an outrage,”", "\"이건 폭거예요.\""], ["cried Dounia, turning pale as death.", "두냐가 죽은 사람처럼 창백해지며 외쳤다."], ["She rushed to the furthest corner, where she made haste to barricade herself with a little table.", "그녀는 가장 먼 구석으로 달려가, 작은 탁자로 황급히 자기 앞을 가로막아 방벽을 쳤다."]], [["She did not scream, but she fixed her eyes on her tormentor and watched every movement he made.", "그녀는 비명을 지르지는 않았으나, 자기를 괴롭히는 자에게 두 눈을 박은 채 그의 일거수일투족을 지켜보았다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov remained standing at the other end of the room facing her.", "스비드리가일로프는 그녀를 마주 본 채 방의 반대편 끝에 선 채로 있었다."], ["He was positively composed, at least in appearance, but his face was pale as before.", "그는 적어도 겉보기엔 확실히 침착했으나, 얼굴은 여전히 창백했다."], ["The mocking smile did not leave his face.", "조소의 미소는 그의 얼굴에서 떠나지 않았다."]], [["“You spoke of outrage just now, Avdotya Romanovna.", "\"방금 폭거라고 했소, 아브도티야 로마노브나."], ["In that case you may be sure I’ve taken measures.", "그렇다면 내가 만반의 조처를 취해 두었다는 걸 확신해도 좋소."], ["Sofya Semyonovna is not at home.", "소피야 세묘노브나는 집에 없소."], ["The Kapernaumovs are far away--there are five locked rooms between.", "카페르나우모프 일가는 멀리 떨어져 있고, 그 사이에는 잠긴 방이 다섯 개나 있소."], ["I am at least twice as strong as you are and I have nothing to fear, besides.", "나는 적어도 당신보다 두 배는 힘이 세고, 게다가 두려울 게 아무것도 없소."], ["For you could not complain afterwards.", "당신은 나중에 하소연조차 할 수 없을 테니까."], ["You surely would not be willing actually to betray your brother?", "설마 당신이 정말로 제 오빠를 팔아넘기려 들지는 않겠지?"], ["Besides, no one would believe you.", "게다가 아무도 당신 말을 믿지 않을 거요."], ["How should a girl have come alone to visit a solitary man in his lodgings?", "처녀가 혼자서 외딴곳에 사는 사내의 하숙집을 찾아왔다니, 어디 말이나 되는 소리요?"], ["So that even if you do sacrifice your brother, you could prove nothing.", "그러니 설령 당신이 오빠를 희생시킨다 해도, 당신은 아무것도 증명할 수 없을 거요."], ["It is very difficult to prove an assault, Avdotya Romanovna.”", "폭행을 입증하기란 무척 어려운 일이오, 아브도티야 로마노브나.\""]], [["“Scoundrel!”", "\"악당!\""], ["whispered Dounia indignantly.", "두냐가 분개하여 속삭였다."]], [["“As you like, but observe I was only speaking by way of a general proposition.", "\"좋을 대로 하시오, 하지만 내가 그저 일반론으로 말한 것뿐임을 알아 두시오."], ["It’s my personal conviction that you are perfectly right--violence is hateful.", "내 개인적인 확신으로는 당신 말이 전적으로 옳소, 폭력은 가증스러운 거요."], ["I only spoke to show you that you need have no remorse even if...", "내가 그렇게 말한 건 다만, 설령 당신이……"], ["you were willing to save your brother of your own accord, as I suggest to you.", "내가 권하는 대로 제 발로 오빠를 구하려 마음먹는다 해도, 양심의 가책 따위는 가질 필요가 없다는 걸 보여 주기 위해서요."], ["You would be simply submitting to circumstances, to violence, in fact, if we must use that word.", "당신은 그저 정황에, 사실상 폭력에, 굳이 그 말을 써야 한다면 말이오, 굴복하는 것뿐일 테니까."], ["Think about it.", "잘 생각해 보시오."], ["Your brother’s and your mother’s fate are in your hands.", "당신 오빠와 어머니의 운명이 당신 손에 달려 있소."], ["I will be your slave... all my life...", "나는 당신의 노예가 되겠소……, 평생토록……."], ["I will wait here.”", "나는 여기서 기다리겠소.\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov sat down on the sofa about eight steps from Dounia.", "스비드리가일로프는 두냐에게서 여덟 걸음쯤 떨어진 소파에 앉았다."], ["She had not the slightest doubt now of his unbending determination.", "이제 그녀에게는 그의 굽힐 줄 모르는 결의에 대해 한 점의 의심도 없었다."], ["Besides, she knew him.", "게다가 그녀는 그를 알고 있었다."], ["Suddenly she pulled out of her pocket a revolver, cocked it and laid it in her hand on the table.", "그녀는 별안간 주머니에서 권총을 꺼내, 공이치기를 당기고는 손에 쥔 채 탁자 위에 올려놓았다."], ["Svidrigaïlov jumped up.", "스비드리가일로프가 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“Aha!", "\"아하!"], ["So that’s it, is it?”", "그러니까 그거였군, 응?\""], ["he cried, surprised but smiling maliciously.", "그가 놀라면서도 짓궂게 웃으며 외쳤다."], ["“Well, that completely alters the aspect of affairs.", "\"흠, 그러면 사정이 완전히 달라지지."], ["You’ve made things wonderfully easier for me, Avdotya Romanovna.", "당신이 일을 놀랍도록 쉽게 만들어 주는군, 아브도티야 로마노브나."], ["But where did you get the revolver?", "한데 그 권총은 어디서 났소?"], ["Was it Mr. Razumihin?", "라주미힌 씨한테서요?"], ["Why, it’s my revolver, an old friend!", "아니, 이건 내 권총이잖소, 오랜 친구로군!"], ["And how I’ve hunted for it!", "내가 이걸 얼마나 찾아 헤맸는데!"], ["The shooting lessons I’ve given you in the country have not been thrown away.”", "시골에서 내가 당신에게 가르쳐 준 사격 수업이 헛되지 않았군그래.\""]], [["“It’s not your revolver, it belonged to Marfa Petrovna, whom you killed, wretch!", "\"이건 당신 권총이 아니에요, 당신이 죽인 마르파 페트로브나의 것이었어요, 이 비열한 인간!"], ["There was nothing of yours in her house.", "그 집에 당신 물건이라곤 아무것도 없었어요."], ["I took it when I began to suspect what you were capable of.", "당신이 어떤 짓까지 저지를 수 있는지 의심하기 시작했을 때 내가 가져온 거예요."], ["If you dare to advance one step, I swear I’ll kill you.”", "감히 한 발짝이라도 다가오면, 맹세코 당신을 죽여 버리겠어요.\""], ["She was frantic.", "그녀는 제정신이 아니었다."]], [["“But your brother?", "\"하지만 당신 오빠는 어쩌고?"], ["I ask from curiosity,”", "호기심에서 묻는 거요.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov, still standing where he was.", "스비드리가일로프가 여전히 제자리에 선 채 말했다."]], [["“Inform, if you want to!", "\"신고하고 싶으면 하세요!"], ["Don’t stir!", "꼼짝 마요!"], ["Don’t come nearer!", "더 다가오지 마요!"], ["I’ll shoot!", "쏘겠어요!"], ["You poisoned your wife, I know; you are a murderer yourself!”", "당신은 아내를 독살했어요, 나는 알아요. 당신 자신이 살인자라고요!\""], ["She held the revolver ready.", "그녀는 권총을 겨눈 채 들고 있었다."]], [["“Are you so positive I poisoned Marfa Petrovna?”", "\"내가 마르파 페트로브나를 독살했다고 그렇게 확신하오?\""]], [["“You did!", "\"당신이 했어요!"], ["You hinted it yourself; you talked to me of poison....", "당신 스스로 그걸 암시했잖아요. 나에게 독약 이야기를 했어요……."], ["I know you went to get it...", "당신이 그걸 구하러 갔다는 걸 알아요……."], ["you had it in readiness....", "미리 준비해 두었던 거예요……."], ["It was your doing....", "그건 당신 짓이었어요……."], ["It must have been your doing....", "당신 짓이 틀림없어요……."], ["Scoundrel!”", "악당!\""]], [["“Even if that were true, it would have been for your sake...", "\"설령 그게 사실이라 해도, 그건 당신 때문이었을 거요……."], ["you would have been the cause.”", "당신이 그 원인이었을 테니까.\""]], [["“You are lying!", "\"거짓말!"], ["I hated you always, always....”", "나는 늘 당신을 증오했어요, 늘, 언제나…….\""]], [["“Oho, Avdotya Romanovna!", "\"오호, 아브도티야 로마노브나!"], ["You seem to have forgotten how you softened to me in the heat of propaganda.", "당신은 선동의 열기 속에서 나에게 마음이 누그러졌던 걸 잊은 모양이군."], ["I saw it in your eyes.", "나는 당신 눈에서 그걸 봤소."], ["Do you remember that moonlight night, when the nightingale was singing?”", "그 달밤을 기억하오? 나이팅게일이 노래하던 그날 밤 말이오.\""]], [["“That’s a lie,”", "\"거짓말이에요.\""], ["there was a flash of fury in Dounia’s eyes, “that’s a lie and a libel!”", "두냐의 두 눈에서 분노의 불꽃이 번뜩였다. \"거짓말이고 모함이에요!\""]], [["“A lie?", "\"거짓말이라?"], ["Well, if you like, it’s a lie.", "흠, 좋소, 당신이 원한다면 거짓말이라고 해 둡시다."], ["I made it up.", "내가 지어낸 거요."], ["Women ought not to be reminded of such things,”", "여자에게 그런 일을 상기시켜선 안 되는 거지.\""], ["he smiled.", "그가 미소 지었다."], ["“I know you will shoot, you pretty wild creature.", "\"당신이 쏘리라는 걸 나는 알고 있소, 이 어여쁜 들짐승 같으니."], ["Well, shoot away!”", "자, 쏘시오!\""]], [["Dounia raised the revolver, and deadly pale, gazed at him, measuring the distance and awaiting the first movement on his part.", "두냐는 권총을 들어 올린 채, 죽은 사람처럼 창백해져 거리를 가늠하며 그가 처음 움직이기를 기다리며 그를 노려보았다."], ["Her lower lip was white and quivering and her big black eyes flashed like fire.", "그녀의 아랫입술은 새하얗게 질려 떨리고 있었고, 큼직한 검은 두 눈은 불꽃처럼 번뜩였다."], ["He had never seen her so handsome.", "그는 그녀가 그토록 아름다워 보인 적이 없었다."], ["The fire glowing in her eyes at the moment she raised the revolver seemed to kindle him and there was a pang of anguish in his heart.", "그녀가 권총을 들어 올리는 순간 그녀의 두 눈에서 이글거리던 불꽃은 그를 불사르는 듯했고, 그의 가슴에는 고통스러운 통증이 일었다."], ["He took a step forward and a shot rang out.", "그가 한 걸음 앞으로 내디뎠고, 총성이 울렸다."], ["The bullet grazed his hair and flew into the wall behind.", "총알은 그의 머리카락을 스치고 뒤쪽 벽으로 날아가 박혔다."], ["He stood still and laughed softly.", "그는 멈춰 선 채 나직이 웃었다."]], [["“The wasp has stung me.", "\"말벌이 나를 쏘았군."], ["She aimed straight at my head.", "곧장 내 머리를 겨냥했어."], ["What’s this?", "이게 뭐지?"], ["Blood?”", "피인가?\""], ["he pulled out his handkerchief to wipe the blood, which flowed in a thin stream down his right temple.", "그는 손수건을 꺼내 오른쪽 관자놀이를 따라 가느다란 줄기로 흘러내리는 피를 닦았다."], ["The bullet seemed to have just grazed the skin.", "총알은 그저 살갗을 스치고 지나간 듯했다."]], [["Dounia lowered the revolver and looked at Svidrigaïlov not so much in terror as in a sort of wild amazement.", "두냐는 권총을 내리고는, 공포라기보다는 일종의 격렬한 경악에 차 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다."], ["She seemed not to understand what she was doing and what was going on.", "그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지, 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지조차 이해하지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["“Well, you missed!", "\"흠, 빗나갔군!"], ["Fire again, I’ll wait,”", "다시 쏘시오, 기다리겠소.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov softly, still smiling, but gloomily.", "스비드리가일로프가 여전히 미소를 지으면서도 음울하게 나직이 말했다."], ["“If you go on like that, I shall have time to seize you before you cock again.”", "\"그렇게 꾸물대다간, 당신이 다시 공이치기를 당기기 전에 내가 당신을 붙잡을 시간이 충분하겠소.\""]], [["Dounia started, quickly cocked the pistol and again raised it.", "두냐는 흠칫하며 재빨리 권총의 공이치기를 당기고는 다시 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Let me be,”", "\"날 내버려 둬요.\""], ["she cried in despair.", "그녀가 절망에 차 외쳤다."], ["“I swear I’ll shoot again.", "\"맹세코 다시 쏘겠어요. 내가……"], ["I... I’ll kill you.”", "내가 당신을 죽이겠어요.\""]], [["“Well... at three paces you can hardly help it.", "\"흠……, 세 걸음 거리에서야 못 맞히기도 어렵지."], ["But if you don’t... then.”", "하지만 만약 못 맞힌다면……, 그땐.\""], ["His eyes flashed and he took two steps forward.", "그의 눈이 번뜩였고, 그는 두 걸음 앞으로 내디뎠다."], ["Dounia shot again: it missed fire.", "두냐가 다시 쏘았으나, 불발이었다."]], [["“You haven’t loaded it properly.", "\"제대로 장전하지 않았군."], ["Never mind, you have another charge there.", "괜찮소, 거기 한 발이 더 남아 있으니."], ["Get it ready, I’ll wait.”", "준비하시오, 기다리겠소.\""]], [["He stood facing her, two paces away, waiting and gazing at her with wild determination, with feverishly passionate, stubborn, set eyes.", "그는 두 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 그녀를 마주 본 채, 기다리며, 열에 들떠 격정에 찬, 고집스럽고 단호한 눈으로 그녀를 응시했다."], ["Dounia saw that he would sooner die than let her go.", "두냐는 그가 자기를 놓아주느니 차라리 죽음을 택하리라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["“And...", "'그리고……"], ["now, of course she would kill him, at two paces!”", "이제 당연히, 두 걸음 거리에서라면 그녀는 그를 죽일 터였다!'"], ["Suddenly she flung away the revolver.", "별안간 그녀는 권총을 내던져 버렸다."]], [["“She’s dropped it!”", "\"떨어뜨렸군!\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov with surprise, and he drew a deep breath.", "스비드리가일로프가 놀라며 말하고는 깊은숨을 내쉬었다. 가슴에서 무거운 짐덩이가 굴러떨어진 듯했다."], ["A weight seemed to have rolled from his heart--perhaps not only the fear of death; indeed he may scarcely have felt it at that moment.", "어쩌면 그것은 단지 죽음의 공포만은 아니었으리라. 실은 그 순간 그는 죽음의 공포를 거의 느끼지도 못했을 것이다."], ["It was the deliverance from another feeling, darker and more bitter, which he could not himself have defined.", "그것은 그 자신도 무어라 규정할 수 없는, 더 어둡고 더 쓰라린 또 다른 감정으로부터의 해방이었다."]], [["He went to Dounia and gently put his arm round her waist.", "그는 두냐에게 다가가 그녀의 허리를 살며시 감싸 안았다."], ["She did not resist, but, trembling like a leaf, looked at him with suppliant eyes.", "그녀는 저항하지 않은 채, 그저 나뭇잎처럼 떨면서 애원하는 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["He tried to say something, but his lips moved without being able to utter a sound.", "그는 뭔가 말하려 했으나, 입술만 움직일 뿐 소리를 내지 못했다."]], [["“Let me go,”", "\"놓아줘요.\""], ["Dounia implored.", "두냐가 간청했다."], ["Svidrigaïlov shuddered.", "스비드리가일로프가 몸서리쳤다."], ["Her voice now was quite different.", "이제 그녀의 목소리는 완전히 달라져 있었다."]], [["“Then you don’t love me?”", "\"그럼 당신은 나를 사랑하지 않는 거요?\""], ["he asked softly.", "그가 나직이 물었다."], ["Dounia shook her head.", "두냐는 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“And...", "\"그리고……"], ["and you can’t? Never?”", "앞으로도 사랑할 수 없는 거요?"], ["he whispered in despair.", "결코?\" 그가 절망에 차 속삭였다."]], [["“Never!”", "\"결코!\""]], [["There followed a moment of terrible, dumb struggle in the heart of Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프의 가슴속에 끔찍하고도 말 없는 투쟁의 한순간이 이어졌다."], ["He looked at her with an indescribable gaze.", "그는 형언할 수 없는 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Suddenly he withdrew his arm, turned quickly to the window and stood facing it.", "별안간 그는 팔을 거두고 재빨리 창가로 돌아서서 그것을 마주 보고 섰다."], ["Another moment passed.", "또 한순간이 흘렀다."]], [["“Here’s the key.”", "\"여기 열쇠가 있소.\""]], [["He took it out of the left pocket of his coat and laid it on the table behind him, without turning or looking at Dounia.", "그는 외투 왼쪽 주머니에서 그것을 꺼내, 돌아서지도 두냐를 쳐다보지도 않은 채, 등 뒤 탁자 위에 내려놓았다."]], [["“Take it!", "\"가져가시오!"], ["Make haste!”", "어서!\""]], [["He looked stubbornly out of the window.", "그는 고집스레 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["Dounia went up to the table to take the key.", "두냐는 열쇠를 집으러 탁자로 다가갔다."]], [["“Make haste!", "\"어서! 어서!\""], ["Make haste!” repeated Svidrigaïlov, still without turning or moving.", "스비드리가일로프가 여전히 돌아서지도 움직이지도 않은 채 거듭 말했다."], ["But there seemed a terrible significance in the tone of that “make haste.”", "그러나 그 \"어서\"라는 말의 어조에는 뭔가 끔찍한 의미가 담겨 있는 듯했다."]], [["Dounia understood it, snatched up the key, flew to the door, unlocked it quickly and rushed out of the room.", "두냐는 그것을 알아채고는 열쇠를 낚아채 문으로 날아가, 재빨리 잠금을 풀고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["A minute later, beside herself, she ran out on to the canal bank in the direction of X. Bridge.", "잠시 후, 그녀는 정신없이 X 다리 쪽으로 운하 강둑을 따라 달려 나갔다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov remained three minutes standing at the window.", "스비드리가일로프는 삼 분 동안 창가에 선 채로 있었다."], ["At last he slowly turned, looked about him and passed his hand over his forehead.", "마침내 그는 천천히 돌아서서 주위를 둘러보고는 한 손으로 이마를 쓸었다."], ["A strange smile contorted his face, a pitiful, sad, weak smile, a smile of despair.", "기묘한 미소가 그의 얼굴을 일그러뜨렸으니, 가련하고 슬프고 힘없는 미소, 절망의 미소였다."], ["The blood, which was already getting dry, smeared his hand.", "이미 굳어 가던 피가 그의 손에 묻어났다."], ["He looked angrily at it, then wetted a towel and washed his temple.", "그는 성난 눈으로 그것을 바라보다가, 수건을 적셔 관자놀이를 닦았다."], ["The revolver which Dounia had flung away lay near the door and suddenly caught his eye.", "두냐가 내던진 권총이 문가에 떨어져 있었는데, 문득 그의 눈에 띄었다."], ["He picked it up and examined it.", "그는 그것을 집어 들어 살펴보았다."], ["It was a little pocket three-barrel revolver of old-fashioned construction.", "구식으로 만들어진 작은 휴대용 삼연발 권총이었다."], ["There were still two charges and one capsule left in it.", "그 안에는 아직 두 발의 탄환과 한 개의 뇌관이 남아 있었다."], ["It could be fired again.", "한 번 더 쏠 수 있었다."], ["He thought a little, put the revolver in his pocket, took his hat and went out.", "그는 잠시 생각하다가, 권총을 주머니에 넣고 모자를 집어 들고는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["He spent that evening till ten o’clock going from one low haunt to another.", "그날 저녁 그는 열 시까지 이 술집 저 술집을 전전하며 보냈다."], ["Katia too turned up and sang another gutter song, how a certain “villain and tyrant”", "카탸도 나타나 또 다른 저속한 노래를 불렀는데, 어떤 \"악당이자 폭군\"이\n\"카탸에게 입을 맞추기 시작했네\" 하는 노래였다."]], [["“began kissing Katia.” Svidrigaïlov treated Katia and the organ-grinder and some singers and the waiters and two little clerks.", "스비드리가일로프는 카탸와 손풍금쟁이, 그리고 몇몇 가수들과 종업원들, 거기에 두 명의 말단 서기에게도 술을 샀다."], ["He was particularly drawn to these clerks by the fact that they both had crooked noses, one bent to the left and the other to the right.", "그가 이 서기들에게 유독 끌린 까닭은, 두 사람 다 코가 비뚤어져 있었기 때문이다 — 한 사람은 왼쪽으로, 다른 한 사람은 오른쪽으로."], ["They took him finally to a pleasure garden, where he paid for their entrance.", "결국 그들은 그를 어느 유원지로 데려갔고, 그가 입장료를 치렀다."], ["There was one lanky three-year-old pine-tree and three bushes in the garden, besides a “Vauxhall,” which was in reality a drinking-bar where tea too was served, and there were a few green tables and chairs standing round it.", "그 정원에는 비쩍 마른 삼 년생 소나무 한 그루와 관목 세 그루, 그 밖에 \"복스홀\"이라는 것이 있었는데, 실은 차도 파는 술집에 불과했고 그 주위에 초록색 탁자와 의자가 몇 개 놓여 있었다."], ["A chorus of wretched singers and a drunken but exceedingly depressed German clown from Munich with a red nose entertained the public.", "형편없는 합창단과, 술에 취했으나 지독히도 의기소침한, 코가 빨간 뮌헨 출신의 독일인 광대 하나가 손님들을 즐겁게 했다."], ["The clerks quarrelled with some other clerks and a fight seemed imminent.", "서기들은 다른 서기들과 시비가 붙어 곧 싸움이라도 벌어질 듯했다."], ["Svidrigaïlov was chosen to decide the dispute.", "스비드리가일로프가 그 분쟁의 심판으로 뽑혔다."], ["He listened to them for a quarter of an hour, but they shouted so loud that there was no possibility of understanding them.", "그는 십오 분 동안이나 그들의 말을 들었지만, 어찌나 큰 소리로 떠들어 대는지 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다."], ["The only fact that seemed certain was that one of them had stolen something and had even succeeded in selling it on the spot to a Jew, but would not share the spoil with his companion.", "다만 한 가지 분명해 보이는 사실은, 그들 가운데 하나가 무언가를 훔쳐서는 그 자리에서 어느 유대인에게 팔아넘기기까지 했으면서도 동료와 그 몫을 나누려 하지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["Finally it appeared that the stolen object was a teaspoon belonging to the Vauxhall.", "마침내 밝혀진 바로는, 그 훔친 물건이란 복스홀에 속한 찻숟가락 하나였다."], ["It was missed and the affair began to seem troublesome.", "그것이 없어진 것이 드러나 일이 성가시게 되어 가기 시작했다."], ["Svidrigaïlov paid for the spoon, got up, and walked out of the garden.", "스비드리가일로프는 숟가락 값을 치르고 일어나 정원을 나섰다."], ["It was about six o’clock.", "여섯 시쯤이었다."], ["He had not drunk a drop of wine all this time and had ordered tea more for the sake of appearances than anything.", "그동안 그는 포도주를 단 한 방울도 마시지 않았고, 무엇보다 겉모양을 차리느라 차를 시켰을 뿐이었다."]], [["It was a dark and stifling evening.", "어둡고 숨 막히는 저녁이었다."], ["Threatening storm-clouds came over the sky about ten o’clock.", "열 시쯤 위협적인 먹구름이 하늘을 덮었다."], ["There was a clap of thunder, and the rain came down like a waterfall.", "천둥이 한 차례 치더니, 폭포처럼 비가 쏟아졌다."], ["The water fell not in drops, but beat on the earth in streams.", "빗물은 방울방울 떨어지는 것이 아니라 줄기를 이루어 대지를 두드렸다."], ["There were flashes of lightning every minute and each flash lasted while one could count five.", "일 분마다 번개가 번쩍였고, 그 번쩍임은 다섯을 셀 만큼 길게 이어졌다."]], [["Drenched to the skin, he went home, locked himself in, opened the bureau, took out all his money and tore up two or three papers.", "물에 흠뻑 젖은 채 그는 집으로 돌아와 문을 잠그고, 책상을 열어 돈을 모조리 꺼내고는 서류 두세 장을 찢어 버렸다."], ["Then, putting the money in his pocket, he was about to change his clothes, but, looking out of the window and listening to the thunder and the rain, he gave up the idea, took up his hat and went out of the room without locking the door.", "그러고는 돈을 주머니에 넣고 옷을 갈아입으려 했으나, 창밖을 내다보며 천둥과 빗소리에 귀를 기울이다가 그 생각을 접고, 모자를 집어 들고는 문도 잠그지 않은 채 방을 나섰다."], ["He went straight to Sonia.", "그는 곧장 소냐에게 향했다."], ["She was at home.", "그녀는 집에 있었다."]], [["She was not alone: the four Kapernaumov children were with her.", "그녀는 혼자가 아니었다. 카페르나우모프네 네 아이들이 그녀와 함께 있었다."], ["She was giving them tea.", "그녀는 아이들에게 차를 따라 주고 있었다."], ["She received Svidrigaïlov in respectful silence, looking wonderingly at his soaking clothes.", "그녀는 흠뻑 젖은 그의 옷을 의아하게 바라보며 공손한 침묵으로 스비드리가일로프를 맞았다."], ["The children all ran away at once in indescribable terror.", "아이들은 모두 형언할 수 없는 공포에 휩싸여 일제히 달아나 버렸다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov sat down at the table and asked Sonia to sit beside him.", "스비드리가일로프는 탁자에 앉아 소냐에게 옆에 앉으라고 청했다."], ["She timidly prepared to listen.", "그녀는 머뭇거리며 들을 채비를 했다."]], [["“I may be going to America, Sofya Semyonovna,”", "\"저는 어쩌면 아메리카로 가게 될지도 모릅니다, 소피야 세묘노브나.\""], ["said Svidrigaïlov, “and as I am probably seeing you for the last time, I have come to make some arrangements.", "스비드리가일로프가 말했다. \"그리고 아마 당신을 보는 게 이번이 마지막일 테니, 몇 가지 정리해 둘 것이 있어 왔습니다."], ["Well, did you see the lady to-day?", "그래, 오늘 그 부인을 만났습니까?"], ["I know what she said to you, you need not tell me.”", "그녀가 당신에게 뭐라고 했는지 압니다, 말하지 않아도 돼요.\""], ["(Sonia made a movement and blushed.) “Those people have their own way of doing things.", "(소냐가 움찔하며 얼굴을 붉혔다.) \"그런 사람들은 일을 처리하는 자기들만의 방식이 있지요."], ["As to your sisters and your brother, they are really provided for and the money assigned to them I’ve put into safe keeping and have received acknowledgments.", "당신의 동생들과 남동생으로 말하자면, 그들은 정말로 살길이 마련되었고, 그들에게 배정된 돈은 내가 안전한 곳에 맡겨 두고 보관증도 받아 두었습니다."], ["You had better take charge of the receipts, in case anything happens.", "무슨 일이 생길 때를 대비해, 당신이 그 영수증을 맡아 두는 편이 좋겠어요."], ["Here, take them!", "자, 받으세요!"], ["Well now, that’s settled.", "그럼 이제 그건 됐고."], ["Here are three 5-per-cent bonds to the value of three thousand roubles.", "여기 삼천 루블어치의 오 퍼센트 채권 세 장이 있습니다."], ["Take those for yourself, entirely for yourself, and let that be strictly between ourselves, so that no one knows of it, whatever you hear.", "이것은 당신 몫이니, 오롯이 당신을 위해 받아 두세요. 그리고 무슨 소릴 듣더라도, 아무도 알지 못하도록 이건 우리 둘만의 비밀로 해 둡시다."], ["You will need the money, for to go on living in the old way, Sofya Semyonovna, is bad, and besides there is no need for it now.”", "당신에게는 그 돈이 필요할 겁니다. 예전처럼 사는 것은, 소피야 세묘노브나, 좋지 못하고, 게다가 이제는 그럴 필요도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I am so much indebted to you, and so are the children and my stepmother,”", "\"저는 당신께 너무도 큰 신세를 졌어요, 아이들도, 새어머니도요.\" 소냐가 황급히 말했다."], ["said Sonia hurriedly, “and if I’ve said so little...", "\"그리고 제가 그동안 말씀을 너무 적게 드렸다면……"], ["please don’t consider...”", "부디 그렇게 여기지 말아 주세요…….\""]], [["“That’s enough!", "\"됐습니다!"], ["that’s enough!”", "됐어요!\""]], [["“But as for the money, Arkady Ivanovitch, I am very grateful to you, but I don’t need it now.", "\"하지만 그 돈으로 말하자면, 아르카디 이바노비치, 정말 감사하지만, 저는 이제 그게 필요 없어요."], ["I can always earn my own living.", "저는 언제든 제 힘으로 먹고살 수 있어요."], ["Don’t think me ungrateful.", "제가 은혜를 모른다고 생각하지 마세요."], ["If you are so charitable, that money....”", "그렇게 자비를 베푸실 거라면, 그 돈은…….\""]], [["“It’s for you, for you, Sofya Semyonovna, and please don’t waste words over it.", "\"이건 당신 것입니다, 당신 것이에요, 소피야 세묘노브나. 부디 그 일로 말을 낭비하지 마세요."], ["I haven’t time for it.", "제겐 그럴 시간이 없습니다."], ["You will want it.", "당신에게 필요할 거예요."], ["Rodion Romanovitch has two alternatives: a bullet in the brain or Siberia.”", "로디온 로마노비치에게는 두 갈래 길이 있습니다. 머리에 총알을 박든가, 아니면 시베리아든가.\""], ["(Sonia looked wildly at him, and started.) “Don’t be uneasy, I know all about it from himself and I am not a gossip; I won’t tell anyone.", "(소냐가 사납게 그를 바라보며 흠칫했다.) \"불안해하지 마세요, 나는 그에게서 직접 다 들었고, 떠벌리는 사람이 아닙니다. 아무에게도 말하지 않아요."], ["It was good advice when you told him to give himself up and confess.", "당신이 그에게 자수해서 죄를 고백하라고 권한 것은 좋은 충고였습니다."], ["It would be much better for him.", "그에게는 훨씬 나을 거예요."], ["Well, if it turns out to be Siberia, he will go and you will follow him.", "자, 만일 시베리아로 결판이 난다면, 그는 갈 것이고 당신은 그를 따라가겠지요."], ["That’s so, isn’t it?", "그렇지 않습니까?"], ["And if so, you’ll need money.", "그렇다면 돈이 필요할 겁니다."], ["You’ll need it for him, do you understand?", "그를 위해 필요할 거예요, 아시겠습니까?"], ["Giving it to you is the same as my giving it to him.", "당신에게 주는 것은 그에게 주는 것과 같습니다."], ["Besides, you promised Amalia Ivanovna to pay what’s owing.", "게다가 당신은 아말리야 이바노브나에게 빚을 갚겠다고 약속했지요."], ["I heard you.", "내가 들었습니다."], ["How can you undertake such obligations so heedlessly, Sofya Semyonovna?", "어떻게 그토록 경솔하게 그런 의무를 떠맡을 수 있습니까, 소피야 세묘노브나?"], ["It was Katerina Ivanovna’s debt and not yours, so you ought not to have taken any notice of the German woman.", "그건 카테리나 이바노브나의 빚이지 당신 빚이 아니니, 그 독일 여자 따위는 신경 쓰지 말았어야 했어요."], ["You can’t get through the world like that.", "그래서야 세상을 헤쳐 나갈 수 없습니다."], ["If you are ever questioned about me--to-morrow or the day after you will be asked--don’t say anything about my coming to see you now and don’t show the money to anyone or say a word about it.", "혹시라도 누가 나에 대해 당신에게 묻거든 — 내일이나 모레쯤 물어볼 텐데 — 지금 내가 당신을 찾아온 일은 한마디도 말하지 말고, 그 돈도 누구에게도 보이거나 입에 올리지 마세요."], ["Well, now good-bye.”", "자, 이제 안녕히.\""], ["(He got up.) “My greetings to Rodion Romanovitch.", "(그가 일어섰다.) \"로디온 로마노비치에게 안부를 전해 주세요."], ["By the way, you’d better put the money for the present in Mr.", "그건 그렇고, 그 돈은 당분간 라주미힌 씨에게 맡겨 두는 게 좋겠습니다."], ["Razumihin’s keeping.", "라주미힌 씨를 알지요?"], ["You know Mr.", "물론 알겠지요."], ["Razumihin? Of course you do.", "나쁜 친구가 아니에요."], ["He’s not a bad fellow.", "내일이나……"], ["Take it to him to-morrow or... when the time comes.", "때가 되거든 그에게 가져다주세요."], ["And till then, hide it carefully.”", "그때까지는 잘 숨겨 두고요.\""]], [["Sonia too jumped up from her chair and looked in dismay at Svidrigaïlov.", "소냐도 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 어찌할 바를 모르고 스비드리가일로프를 바라보았다."], ["She longed to speak, to ask a question, but for the first moments she did not dare and did not know how to begin.", "그녀는 무언가 말하고 싶고, 묻고 싶었으나, 처음 얼마간은 감히 그러지 못했고 어떻게 시작해야 할지도 알지 못했다."]], [["“How can you...", "\"어떻게……"], ["how can you be going now, in such rain?”", "어떻게 지금, 이런 빗속에 가시려고요?\""]], [["“Why, be starting for America, and be stopped by rain!", "\"아니, 아메리카로 떠나려는데 비 따위에 발이 묶이겠습니까!"], ["Ha, ha!", "하하!"], ["Good-bye, Sofya Semyonovna, my dear!", "안녕히, 소피야 세묘노브나, 그대여!"], ["Live and live long, you will be of use to others.", "부디 오래오래 사세요, 당신은 다른 이들에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 테니."], ["By the way...", "그건 그렇고……"], ["tell Mr. Razumihin I send my greetings to him.", "라주미힌 씨에게 내 안부를 전한다고 일러 주세요."], ["Tell him Arkady Ivanovitch Svidrigaïlov sends his greetings.", "아르카디 이바노비치 스비드리가일로프가 안부를 전하더라고요."], ["Be sure to.”", "꼭 그렇게 해 주세요.\""]], [["He went out, leaving Sonia in a state of wondering anxiety and vague apprehension.", "그는 소냐를 의아한 불안과 막연한 두려움 속에 남겨 둔 채 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["It appeared afterwards that on the same evening, at twenty past eleven, he made another very eccentric and unexpected visit.", "나중에 밝혀진 바로는, 바로 그날 저녁 열한 시 이십 분에 그는 또 한 차례 매우 기이하고 뜻밖의 방문을 했다."], ["The rain still persisted.", "비는 여전히 그치지 않고 있었다."], ["Drenched to the skin, he walked into the little flat where the parents of his betrothed lived, in Third Street in Vassilyevsky Island.", "그는 물에 흠뻑 젖은 채, 바실리예프스키섬 삼번가에 있는, 자기 약혼녀의 부모가 사는 작은 셋집으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["He knocked some time before he was admitted, and his visit at first caused great perturbation; but Svidrigaïlov could be very fascinating when he liked, so that the first, and indeed very intelligent surmise of the sensible parents that Svidrigaïlov had probably had so much to drink that he did not know what he was doing vanished immediately.", "그는 한참을 두드리고 나서야 안으로 들어갈 수 있었고, 그의 방문은 처음에 큰 소동을 일으켰다. 그러나 스비드리가일로프는 마음만 먹으면 무척 매력적일 수 있었던지라, 이 분별 있는 부모가 처음에 떠올렸던, 사실 무척이나 영리한 추측 — 스비드리가일로프가 아마도 술을 너무 마셔 제정신이 아닌 모양이라는 생각 — 은 곧바로 사라져 버렸다."], ["The decrepit father was wheeled in to see Svidrigaïlov by the tender and sensible mother, who as usual began the conversation with various irrelevant questions.", "다정하고 분별 있는 어머니가 노쇠한 아버지를 휠체어에 태워 스비드리가일로프 앞으로 밀고 나왔고, 그녀는 여느 때처럼 이런저런 동떨어진 질문들로 대화를 시작했다."], ["She never asked a direct question, but began by smiling and rubbing her hands and then, if she were obliged to ascertain something--for instance, when Svidrigaïlov would like to have the wedding--she would begin by interested and almost eager questions about Paris and the court life there, and only by degrees brought the conversation round to Third Street.", "그녀는 결코 직접적으로 묻는 법이 없었고, 우선 미소를 지으며 두 손을 비비다가, 만일 무언가를 — 이를테면 스비드리가일로프가 언제쯤 결혼식을 올리고 싶어 하는지를 — 알아내야만 할 때면, 파리며 그곳 궁정 생활에 관한 흥미롭고 거의 들뜬 질문들로 운을 떼고는, 그저 차츰차츰 화제를 삼번가 쪽으로 돌려 가곤 했다."], ["On other occasions this had of course been very impressive, but this time Arkady Ivanovitch seemed particularly impatient, and insisted on seeing his betrothed at once, though he had been informed, to begin with, that she had already gone to bed.", "다른 때 같으면 이것이 물론 무척 인상적이었겠지만, 이번에는 아르카디 이바노비치가 유난히 조바심을 내며, 무엇보다 먼저 약혼녀가 이미 잠자리에 들었다는 말을 들었는데도 당장 그녀를 보겠다고 고집을 부렸다."], ["The girl of course appeared.", "처녀는 물론 나타났다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov informed her at once that he was obliged by very important affairs to leave Petersburg for a time, and therefore brought her fifteen thousand roubles and begged her accept them as a present from him, as he had long been intending to make her this trifling present before their wedding.", "스비드리가일로프는 그녀에게 곧바로, 매우 중대한 용무로 당분간 페테르부르크를 떠나야만 한다고 알리고는, 그래서 그녀에게 만 오천 루블을 가져왔으니 자기가 주는 선물로 받아 달라고 청했다. 결혼식 전에 이 사소한 선물을 그녀에게 하려고 진작부터 마음먹고 있었다는 것이었다."], ["The logical connection of the present with his immediate departure and the absolute necessity of visiting them for that purpose in pouring rain at midnight was not made clear.", "이 선물과 그의 임박한 출발 사이의 논리적 연관, 그리고 그 목적으로 한밤중 쏟아지는 빗속에 굳이 그들을 찾아와야만 했던 절대적인 필요성은 분명히 설명되지 않았다."], ["But it all went off very well; even the inevitable ejaculations of wonder and regret, the inevitable questions were extraordinarily few and restrained.", "그러나 모든 것이 무척 순조롭게 흘러갔다. 으레 따르기 마련인 놀라움과 아쉬움의 탄성, 으레 나오기 마련인 질문들마저 놀라울 만큼 적고 절제되어 있었다."], ["On the other hand, the gratitude expressed was most glowing and was reinforced by tears from the most sensible of mothers.", "반면 표현된 감사만은 더없이 뜨거웠고, 더없이 분별 있는 그 어머니의 눈물로 한층 힘을 받았다."], ["Svidrigaïlov got up, laughed, kissed his betrothed, patted her cheek, declared he would soon come back, and noticing in her eyes, together with childish curiosity, a sort of earnest dumb inquiry, reflected and kissed her again, though he felt sincere anger inwardly at the thought that his present would be immediately locked up in the keeping of the most sensible of mothers.", "스비드리가일로프는 일어나 웃으며 약혼녀에게 입을 맞추고 그녀의 뺨을 토닥이고는 곧 돌아오겠노라 말했는데, 그녀의 눈에서 어린아이 같은 호기심과 더불어 무언가 간절하고 말 없는 물음 같은 것을 알아채고는, 잠시 생각하다가 다시 한번 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 비록 자신의 선물이 더없이 분별 있는 그 어머니의 손에 곧장 자물쇠로 채워져 보관되리라는 생각에 속으로는 진심 어린 분노를 느꼈지만 말이다."], ["He went away, leaving them all in a state of extraordinary excitement, but the tender mamma, speaking quietly in a half whisper, settled some of the most important of their doubts, concluding that Svidrigaïlov was a great man, a man of great affairs and connections and of great wealth--there was no knowing what he had in his mind.", "그는 그들 모두를 엄청난 흥분 속에 남겨 둔 채 떠났는데, 다정한 어머니는 반쯤 속삭이듯 나직이 말하며 그들의 가장 중대한 의혹 몇 가지를 정리했으니, 스비드리가일로프는 위대한 인물이요, 큰 사업과 인맥을 지니고 막대한 부를 가진 사람이라 — 그가 속으로 무슨 생각을 품고 있는지 알 도리가 없다는 것이었다."], ["He would start off on a journey and give away money just as the fancy took him, so that there was nothing surprising about it.", "그는 기분 내키는 대로 여행을 떠나기도 하고 돈을 내주기도 하는 사람이니, 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니라고 했다."], ["Of course it was strange that he was wet through, but Englishmen, for instance, are even more eccentric, and all these people of high society didn’t think of what was said of them and didn’t stand on ceremony.", "물론 그가 흠뻑 젖어 있는 것은 이상한 일이지만, 이를테면 영국인들은 더더욱 기이하며, 그런 상류 사회 사람들은 남들이 자기에 대해 뭐라고 하든 개의치 않고 격식을 차리지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["Possibly, indeed, he came like that on purpose to show that he was not afraid of anyone.", "어쩌면 정말로 그는 자기가 누구도 두려워하지 않는다는 것을 보이려고 일부러 그런 꼴로 온 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["Above all, not a word should be said about it, for God knows what might come of it, and the money must be locked up, and it was most fortunate that Fedosya, the cook, had not left the kitchen.", "무엇보다도 이 일은 한마디도 입 밖에 내서는 안 되니, 무슨 일이 생길지 하느님만이 아실 일이고, 돈은 자물쇠로 채워 두어야 하며, 요리사 페도시야가 부엌을 떠나지 않은 것이 천만다행이라고 했다."], ["And above all not a word must be said to that old cat, Madame Resslich, and so on and so on.", "그리고 무엇보다도 저 늙은 암고양이 같은 레슬리히 부인에게는 한마디도 해서는 안 된다는 둥, 이런저런 이야기가 이어졌다."], ["They sat up whispering till two o’clock, but the girl went to bed much earlier, amazed and rather sorrowful.", "그들은 두 시까지 속닥거리며 앉아 있었지만, 처녀는 어리둥절하고 어딘지 서글픈 마음으로 훨씬 일찍 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov meanwhile, exactly at midnight, crossed the bridge on the way back to the mainland.", "한편 스비드리가일로프는 정각 자정에 다리를 건너 본토 쪽으로 돌아가는 길이었다."], ["The rain had ceased and there was a roaring wind.", "비는 그쳤고 바람이 윙윙 울부짖었다."], ["He began shivering, and for one moment he gazed at the black waters of the Little Neva with a look of special interest, even inquiry.", "그는 몸을 떨기 시작했고, 한순간 작은 네바강의 검은 물을 유달리 흥미로운, 아니 무언가를 캐묻는 듯한 눈길로 가만히 응시했다."], ["But he soon felt it very cold, standing by the water; he turned and went towards Y.", "그러나 곧 물가에 서 있으려니 몹시 추워졌다. 그는 돌아서서 Y 대로 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Prospect. He walked along that endless street for a long time, almost half an hour, more than once stumbling in the dark on the wooden pavement, but continually looking for something on the right side of the street.", "그는 그 끝없는 거리를 오래도록, 거의 반 시간 가까이 걸으며, 어둠 속 나무 포도에서 몇 번이고 발을 헛디뎠으나, 줄곧 거리 오른편에서 무언가를 찾고 있었다."], ["He had noticed passing through this street lately that there was a hotel somewhere towards the end, built of wood, but fairly large, and its name he remembered was something like Adrianople.", "그는 요즘 이 거리를 지나다니며, 끝자락 어디쯤에 나무로 지은, 그래도 제법 큰 호텔이 하나 있다는 것을 눈여겨봐 두었는데, 그 이름이 아드리아노플인가 뭔가 하는 것이었다고 기억하고 있었다."], ["He was not mistaken: the hotel was so conspicuous in that God-forsaken place that he could not fail to see it even in the dark.", "그의 기억은 틀리지 않았다. 그 호텔은 그 하느님께 버림받은 곳에서 워낙 눈에 띄어, 어둠 속에서도 못 보고 지나칠 리가 없었다."], ["It was a long, blackened wooden building, and in spite of the late hour there were lights in the windows and signs of life within.", "그것은 거뭇하게 변색된 길쭉한 목조 건물이었는데, 늦은 시각인데도 창마다 불이 켜져 있고 안에서 인기척이 났다."], ["He went in and asked a ragged fellow who met him in the corridor for a room.", "그는 안으로 들어가, 복도에서 마주친 누더기 차림의 사내에게 방 하나를 청했다."], ["The latter, scanning Svidrigaïlov, pulled himself together and led him at once to a close and tiny room in the distance, at the end of the corridor, under the stairs.", "사내는 스비드리가일로프를 훑어보더니 정신을 차리고는, 곧바로 그를 멀찍이 떨어진, 복도 끝 계단 밑의 비좁고 답답한 작은 방으로 안내했다."], ["There was no other, all were occupied.", "다른 방은 없었으니, 모두 차 있었던 것이다."], ["The ragged fellow looked inquiringly.", "누더기 차림의 사내가 캐묻듯 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Is there tea?”", "\"차는 있나?\""], ["asked Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 있습니다.\""]], [["“What else is there?”", "\"또 뭐가 있지?\""]], [["“Veal, vodka, savouries.”", "\"송아지 고기, 보드카, 안주가 있습니다.\""]], [["“Bring me tea and veal.”", "\"차와 송아지 고기를 가져오게.\""]], [["“And you want nothing else?”", "\"그것 말고는 아무것도 안 드십니까?\""], ["he asked with apparent surprise.", "사내가 적이 놀란 듯 물었다."]], [["“Nothing, nothing.”", "\"아무것도, 아무것도 필요 없네.\""]], [["The ragged man went away, completely disillusioned.", "누더기 차림의 사내는 완전히 김이 빠진 채 물러갔다."]], [["“It must be a nice place,”", "'괜찮은 곳임에 틀림없군.'"], ["thought Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프는 생각했다."], ["“How was it I didn’t know it?", "'어쩌다 내가 이런 곳을 몰랐을까?"], ["I expect I look as if I came from a café chantant and have had some adventure on the way.", "아마 나는 카페 샹탕에서 나와 오는 길에 무슨 모험이라도 겪은 사람처럼 보이겠지."], ["It would be interesting to know who stayed here?”", "누가 여기 묵었는지 알면 흥미로울 텐데.'"]], [["He lighted the candle and looked at the room more carefully.", "그는 촛불을 켜고 방을 좀 더 찬찬히 살펴보았다."], ["It was a room so low-pitched that Svidrigaïlov could only just stand up in it; it had one window; the bed, which was very dirty, and the plain-stained chair and table almost filled it up.", "그 방은 천장이 너무 낮아 스비드리가일로프가 간신히 똑바로 설 수 있을 정도였다. 창이 하나 있었고, 무척 더러운 침대와, 칠이 거칠게 된 의자와 탁자가 방을 거의 다 채우고 있었다."], ["The walls looked as though they were made of planks, covered with shabby paper, so torn and dusty that the pattern was indistinguishable, though the general colour--yellow--could still be made out.", "벽은 판자로 된 것처럼 보였고, 다 해진 벽지가 발려 있었는데, 어찌나 찢기고 먼지투성이인지 무늬는 분간할 수 없었으나, 전체적인 색깔 — 노란색 — 만큼은 그래도 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["One of the walls was cut short by the sloping ceiling, though the room was not an attic but just under the stairs.", "한쪽 벽은 비스듬한 천장에 가로막혀 짧게 잘려 있었으니, 다락방은 아니고 그저 계단 밑이었다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov set down the candle, sat down on the bed and sank into thought.", "스비드리가일로프는 촛불을 내려놓고 침대에 앉아 생각에 잠겼다."], ["But a strange persistent murmur which sometimes rose to a shout in the next room attracted his attention.", "그러나 옆방에서 때때로 고함으로 치솟곤 하는 기이하고도 집요한 웅얼거림이 그의 주의를 끌었다."], ["The murmur had not ceased from the moment he entered the room.", "그 웅얼거림은 그가 방에 들어선 순간부터 그친 적이 없었다."], ["He listened: someone was upbraiding and almost tearfully scolding, but he heard only one voice.", "그는 귀를 기울였다. 누군가가 거의 눈물이라도 흘릴 듯 책망하고 꾸짖고 있었으나, 들리는 것은 한 사람의 목소리뿐이었다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov got up, shaded the light with his hand and at once he saw light through a crack in the wall; he went up and peeped through.", "스비드리가일로프는 일어나 손으로 불빛을 가렸고, 곧 벽 틈으로 새어 나오는 빛을 보았다. 그는 다가가 틈으로 들여다보았다."], ["The room, which was somewhat larger than his, had two occupants.", "그의 방보다 다소 큰 그 방에는 두 사람이 있었다."], ["One of them, a very curly-headed man with a red inflamed face, was standing in the pose of an orator, without his coat, with his legs wide apart to preserve his balance, and smiting himself on the breast.", "그중 하나는 머리가 곱슬곱슬하고 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 사내로, 웃옷을 벗은 채 균형을 잡으려고 두 다리를 넓게 벌리고 서서 제 가슴을 치며 웅변가 같은 자세를 취하고 있었다."], ["He reproached the other with being a beggar, with having no standing whatever.", "그는 상대를 거지라느니, 아무런 지체도 없는 자라느니 하며 나무랐다."], ["He declared that he had taken the other out of the gutter and he could turn him out when he liked, and that only the finger of Providence sees it all.", "그는 자기가 저자를 시궁창에서 건져 냈으며, 마음만 먹으면 언제든 내쫓을 수 있고, 이 모든 것은 오직 신의 손가락만이 굽어본다고 떠들어 댔다."], ["The object of his reproaches was sitting in a chair, and had the air of a man who wants dreadfully to sneeze, but can’t.", "그 책망의 대상은 의자에 앉아 있었는데, 몹시 재채기를 하고 싶지만 못하는 사람 같은 꼴이었다."], ["He sometimes turned sheepish and befogged eyes on the speaker, but obviously had not the slightest idea what he was talking about and scarcely heard it.", "그는 이따금 멍하고 흐리멍덩한 눈을 그 말하는 자에게 돌리곤 했으나, 분명 그가 무슨 말을 하는지 도무지 알지 못했고 제대로 듣지도 못하는 것이 분명했다."], ["A candle was burning down on the table; there were wine-glasses, a nearly empty bottle of vodka, bread and cucumber, and glasses with the dregs of stale tea.", "탁자 위에서는 초 한 자루가 다 타들어 가고 있었고, 포도주 잔들과 거의 비어 가는 보드카 병 하나, 빵과 오이, 그리고 김빠진 차 찌꺼기가 남은 잔들이 놓여 있었다."], ["After gazing attentively at this, Svidrigaïlov turned away indifferently and sat down on the bed.", "스비드리가일로프는 이 광경을 유심히 들여다보다가 무심히 돌아서서 침대에 앉았다."]], [["The ragged attendant, returning with the tea, could not resist asking him again whether he didn’t want anything more, and again receiving a negative reply, finally withdrew.", "차를 들고 돌아온 누더기 차림의 종업원은 또다시 더 필요한 것이 없느냐고 묻지 않을 수 없었고, 또다시 없다는 대답을 듣고는 마침내 물러갔다."], ["Svidrigaïlov made haste to drink a glass of tea to warm himself, but could not eat anything.", "스비드리가일로프는 몸을 덥히려 서둘러 차 한 잔을 마셨으나, 아무것도 먹을 수가 없었다."], ["He began to feel feverish.", "그는 열이 오르는 것을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He took off his coat and, wrapping himself in the blanket, lay down on the bed.", "그는 웃옷을 벗고 담요를 두른 채 침대에 누웠다."], ["He was annoyed.", "그는 짜증이 났다."], ["“It would have been better to be well for the occasion,”", "'하필 이런 때에 건강했더라면 좋았을 것을.'"], ["he thought with a smile.", "그는 미소를 지으며 생각했다."], ["The room was close, the candle burnt dimly, the wind was roaring outside, he heard a mouse scratching in the corner and the room smelt of mice and of leather.", "방은 답답했고, 촛불은 희미하게 타올랐으며, 밖에서는 바람이 울부짖었고, 구석에서 생쥐가 무언가를 갉아 대는 소리가 들렸으며, 방에서는 쥐 냄새와 가죽 냄새가 났다."], ["He lay in a sort of reverie: one thought followed another.", "그는 일종의 몽상에 잠겨 누워 있었다. 한 생각이 또 다른 생각으로 이어졌다."], ["He felt a longing to fix his imagination on something.", "그는 무언가에 상상을 붙들어 매고 싶은 갈망을 느꼈다."], ["“It must be a garden under the window,”", "'창밖에 정원이 있나 보군.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“There’s a sound of trees.", "'나무 소리가 나는군."], ["How I dislike the sound of trees on a stormy night, in the dark!", "폭풍우 치는 밤, 어둠 속에서 나는 저 나무 소리가 어찌나 싫은지!"], ["They give one a horrid feeling.”", "사람을 끔찍한 기분에 빠뜨린단 말이야.'"], ["He remembered how he had disliked it when he passed Petrovsky Park just now.", "그는 방금 페트로프스키 공원을 지날 때 그 소리가 얼마나 싫었는지를 떠올렸다."], ["This reminded him of the bridge over the Little Neva and he felt cold again as he had when standing there.", "이것이 작은 네바강의 다리를 떠올리게 했고, 그는 거기 서 있을 때처럼 다시 한기를 느꼈다. '나는 물을 좋아한 적이 없어.'"], ["“I never have liked water,”", "그는 생각했다."], ["he thought, “even in a landscape,”", "'풍경 속의 물이라 해도 말이지.'"], ["and he suddenly smiled again at a strange idea: “Surely now all these questions of taste and comfort ought not to matter, but I’ve become more particular, like an animal that picks out a special place...", "그러고는 문득 어떤 기이한 생각에 또다시 미소를 지었다. '이 마당에 이런 취향이니 안락이니 하는 문제가 무슨 상관이람."], ["for such an occasion.", "그런데도 나는 더 까다로워졌어, 마치……"], ["I ought to have gone into the Petrovsky Park!", "그런 때를 위해 특별한 자리를 골라 두는 짐승처럼 말이야."], ["I suppose it seemed dark, cold, ha-ha!", "페트로프스키 공원으로 갔어야 했는데!"], ["As though I were seeking pleasant sensations!...", "어두컴컴하고 추워 보여서 그랬겠지, 하하! 마치 내가 유쾌한 기분이라도 찾는 것처럼!……"], ["By the way, why haven’t I put out the candle?”", "그건 그렇고, 왜 촛불을 끄지 않았지?'"], ["he blew it out.", "그는 촛불을 불어 껐다."], ["“They’ve gone to bed next door,”", "'옆방 사람들은 잠자리에 들었군.'"], ["he thought, not seeing the light at the crack.", "그는 틈에서 빛이 보이지 않는 것을 보고 생각했다."], ["“Well, now, Marfa Petrovna, now is the time for you to turn up; it’s dark, and the very time and place for you.", "'자, 마르파 페트로브나, 이제 당신이 나타날 때가 되었군. 어둡고, 당신에게 딱 알맞은 때와 장소이니 말이야."], ["But now you won’t come!”", "하지만 이제 당신은 오지 않겠지!'"]], [["He suddenly recalled how, an hour before carrying out his design on Dounia, he had recommended Raskolnikov to trust her to Razumihin’s keeping.", "그는 두냐에게 손을 대려던 한 시간 전에, 라스콜니코프에게 그녀를 라주미힌의 손에 맡기라고 권했던 일을 문득 떠올렸다."], ["“I suppose I really did say it, as Raskolnikov guessed, to tease myself.", "'라스콜니코프가 짐작한 대로, 나는 정말로 나 자신을 괴롭히려고 그런 말을 한 것 같군."], ["But what a rogue that Raskolnikov is!", "하지만 그 라스콜니코프란 자는 얼마나 교활한 악당인가!"], ["He’s gone through a good deal.", "그는 꽤나 많은 일을 겪었어."], ["He may be a successful rogue in time when he’s got over his nonsense.", "그 허튼소리를 다 떨쳐 내고 나면, 언젠가는 성공한 악당이 될지도 모르지."], ["But now he’s _too_ eager for life.", "하지만 지금 그는 삶에 너무도 안달이 나 있어."], ["These young men are contemptible on that point.", "이 젊은 녀석들은 그 점에서 경멸스럽단 말이야."], ["But, hang the fellow!", "하지만 그 녀석은 될 대로 되라지!"], ["Let him please himself, it’s nothing to do with me.”", "제 좋을 대로 하라고, 나와는 상관없는 일이니까.'"]], [["He could not get to sleep.", "그는 잠들 수가 없었다."], ["By degrees Dounia’s image rose before him, and a shudder ran over him.", "차츰차츰 두냐의 모습이 눈앞에 떠올랐고, 전율이 그의 온몸을 휩쓸었다."], ["“No, I must give up all that now,”", "'아니, 이제 그 모든 걸 단념해야 해.'"], ["he thought, rousing himself.", "그는 정신을 추스르며 생각했다."], ["“I must think of something else.", "'다른 걸 생각해야지."], ["It’s queer and funny.", "참 기묘하고도 우스운 일이야."], ["I never had a great hatred for anyone, I never particularly desired to avenge myself even, and that’s a bad sign, a bad sign, a bad sign.", "나는 누구도 크게 미워해 본 적이 없고, 심지어 복수하고 싶은 마음조차 유독 품어 본 적이 없어. 그건 나쁜 징조야, 나쁜 징조, 나쁜 징조라고."], ["I never liked quarrelling either, and never lost my temper--that’s a bad sign too.", "다투는 것도 좋아한 적이 없고, 성을 낸 적도 없지 — 그것도 나쁜 징조야."], ["And the promises I made her just now, too--Damnation!", "게다가 방금 그녀에게 했던 그 약속들도 — 빌어먹을!"], ["But--who knows?--perhaps she would have made a new man of me somehow....”", "하지만 — 누가 알겠어 — 어쩌면 그녀가 어떻게든 나를 새사람으로 만들어 주었을지도……'"]], [["He ground his teeth and sank into silence again.", "그는 이를 갈며 다시 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["Again Dounia’s image rose before him, just as she was when, after shooting the first time, she had lowered the revolver in terror and gazed blankly at him, so that he might have seized her twice over and she would not have lifted a hand to defend herself if he had not reminded her.", "또다시 두냐의 모습이 눈앞에 떠올랐는데, 그녀가 처음으로 총을 쏘고 나서 공포에 질려 권총을 내리고는 멍하니 그를 바라보던 바로 그 모습이었다. 그래서 그가 일깨워 주지만 않았더라면 그는 두 번이라도 그녀를 붙잡을 수 있었고, 그녀는 손 하나 들어 자신을 지키려 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["He recalled how at that instant he felt almost sorry for her, how he had felt a pang at his heart...", "그는 그 순간 그녀가 거의 가엾게 느껴졌던 것을, 가슴이 찌릿하게 아파 왔던 것을 떠올렸다……"]], [["“Aïe!", "\"아이!"], ["Damnation, these thoughts again!", "빌어먹을, 또 이 생각이군!"], ["I must put it away!”", "떨쳐 버려야 해!\""]], [["He was dozing off; the feverish shiver had ceased, when suddenly something seemed to run over his arm and leg under the bedclothes.", "그는 깜박 잠이 들고 있었다. 열에 들뜬 오한도 가라앉았는데, 갑자기 이불 속에서 무언가가 그의 팔과 다리를 스쳐 지나가는 것 같았다."], ["He started.", "그는 흠칫했다."], ["“Ugh!", "'윽!"], ["hang it!", "빌어먹을!"], ["I believe it’s a mouse,”", "생쥐인가 보군.'"], ["he thought, “that’s the veal I left on the table.”", "그는 생각했다. '탁자에 두고 온 송아지 고기 탓이야.'"], ["He felt fearfully disinclined to pull off the blanket, get up, get cold, but all at once something unpleasant ran over his leg again.", "담요를 걷어 내고 일어나 한기를 무릅쓰기가 끔찍이도 싫었으나, 별안간 또다시 무언가 불쾌한 것이 그의 다리를 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["He pulled off the blanket and lighted the candle.", "그는 담요를 걷어 내고 촛불을 켰다."], ["Shaking with feverish chill he bent down to examine the bed: there was nothing.", "열에 들뜬 한기로 몸을 떨며 그는 침대를 살피려 몸을 숙였다. 아무것도 없었다."], ["He shook the blanket and suddenly a mouse jumped out on the sheet.", "그가 담요를 흔들자 별안간 생쥐 한 마리가 시트 위로 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["He tried to catch it, but the mouse ran to and fro in zigzags without leaving the bed, slipped between his fingers, ran over his hand and suddenly darted under the pillow.", "그가 그것을 잡으려 했으나, 생쥐는 침대를 벗어나지 않은 채 지그재그로 이리저리 내달리며 그의 손가락 사이를 빠져나가고, 그의 손등을 타고 넘더니 별안간 베개 밑으로 쏜살같이 들어가 버렸다."], ["He threw down the pillow, but in one instant felt something leap on his chest and dart over his body and down his back under his shirt.", "그가 베개를 내던졌으나, 순식간에 무언가가 그의 가슴팍으로 뛰어올라 몸을 가로질러 등줄기를 타고 셔츠 속으로 내달리는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["He trembled nervously and woke up.", "그는 신경질적으로 부르르 떨다가 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["The room was dark.", "방은 어두웠다."], ["He was lying on the bed and wrapped up in the blanket as before.", "그는 아까처럼 담요를 두른 채 침대에 누워 있었다."], ["The wind was howling under the window.", "창 밑에서는 바람이 울부짖고 있었다."], ["“How disgusting,”", "'참 역겹기도 하지.'"], ["he thought with annoyance.", "그는 짜증스레 생각했다."]], [["He got up and sat on the edge of the bedstead with his back to the window.", "그는 일어나 창을 등진 채 침대 가장자리에 걸터앉았다."], ["“It’s better not to sleep at all,”", "'아예 안 자는 게 낫겠어.'"], ["he decided.", "그는 마음을 정했다."], ["There was a cold damp draught from the window, however; without getting up he drew the blanket over him and wrapped himself in it.", "그러나 창에서는 차고 축축한 외풍이 들어왔다. 그는 일어나지 않은 채 담요를 끌어당겨 몸을 감쌌다."], ["He was not thinking of anything and did not want to think.", "그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았고, 생각하고 싶지도 않았다."], ["But one image rose after another, incoherent scraps of thought without beginning or end passed through his mind.", "그러나 하나의 영상에 또 하나의 영상이 떠올랐고, 처음도 끝도 없는 두서없는 생각의 조각들이 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["He sank into drowsiness.", "그는 졸음에 빠져들었다."], ["Perhaps the cold, or the dampness, or the dark, or the wind that howled under the window and tossed the trees roused a sort of persistent craving for the fantastic.", "어쩌면 추위나, 습기, 혹은 어둠, 아니면 창 밑에서 울부짖으며 나무들을 뒤흔드는 바람이, 환상적인 것에 대한 일종의 집요한 갈망을 불러일으켰는지도 모른다."], ["He kept dwelling on images of flowers, he fancied a charming flower garden, a bright, warm, almost hot day, a holiday--Trinity day.", "그는 자꾸만 꽃의 영상에 마음을 머물렀다. 그는 매혹적인 화원을, 밝고 따스한, 거의 무더운 어느 날을, 어느 축제일을 — 삼위일체 축일을 떠올렸다."], ["A fine, sumptuous country cottage in the English taste overgrown with fragrant flowers, with flower beds going round the house; the porch, wreathed in climbers, was surrounded with beds of roses.", "향기로운 꽃들이 우거진, 영국식 취향의 아름답고 호화로운 시골 별장이 있었고, 집 둘레로 화단이 빙 둘러 있었다. 덩굴식물로 화환처럼 뒤덮인 현관 주위에는 장미 화단이 둘러져 있었다."], ["A light, cool staircase, carpeted with rich rugs, was decorated with rare plants in china pots.", "값진 양탄자가 깔린 가볍고 서늘한 계단은 도자기 화분에 담긴 진귀한 식물들로 장식되어 있었다."], ["He noticed particularly in the windows nosegays of tender, white, heavily fragrant narcissus bending over their bright, green, thick long stalks.", "그는 특히 창가에, 밝고 푸르고 굵고 긴 줄기 위로 고개를 숙인, 부드럽고 새하얗고 짙은 향기를 풍기는 수선화 다발들을 눈여겨보았다."], ["He was reluctant to move away from them, but he went up the stairs and came into a large, high drawing-room and again everywhere--at the windows, the doors on to the balcony, and on the balcony itself--were flowers.", "그는 그것들에게서 차마 떠나기 싫었으나, 계단을 올라 크고 천장이 높은 응접실로 들어갔는데, 거기에도 또다시 사방에 — 창가에, 발코니로 통하는 문에, 발코니 위에까지 — 꽃이 있었다."], ["The floors were strewn with freshly-cut fragrant hay, the windows were open, a fresh, cool, light air came into the room.", "바닥에는 갓 베어 낸 향기로운 건초가 흩뿌려져 있었고, 창문은 열려 있었으며, 신선하고 서늘하고 가벼운 공기가 방 안으로 들어왔다."], ["The birds were chirruping under the window, and in the middle of the room, on a table covered with a white satin shroud, stood a coffin.", "새들이 창 밑에서 지저귀고 있었고, 방 한가운데, 흰 새틴 천으로 덮인 탁자 위에는 관 하나가 놓여 있었다."], ["The coffin was covered with white silk and edged with a thick white frill; wreaths of flowers surrounded it on all sides.", "관은 흰 비단으로 덮이고 두툼한 흰 주름 장식으로 가장자리가 둘려 있었으며, 사방에서 꽃 화환이 그것을 둘러싸고 있었다."], ["Among the flowers lay a girl in a white muslin dress, with her arms crossed and pressed on her bosom, as though carved out of marble.", "그 꽃들 사이에, 흰 모슬린 드레스를 입은 한 소녀가, 두 팔을 가슴 위에 가지런히 포개고 누워 있었는데, 마치 대리석을 깎아 놓은 듯했다."], ["But her loose fair hair was wet; there was a wreath of roses on her head.", "그러나 풀어 헤쳐진 그녀의 금빛 머리칼은 젖어 있었고, 머리에는 장미 화환이 얹혀 있었다."], ["The stern and already rigid profile of her face looked as though chiselled of marble too, and the smile on her pale lips was full of an immense unchildish misery and sorrowful appeal.", "엄숙하고 이미 굳어 버린 그녀 얼굴의 옆모습 역시 대리석으로 새긴 것 같았으며, 창백한 입술에 어린 미소에는 어린아이답지 않은 엄청난 비탄과 슬픈 호소가 가득했다."], ["Svidrigaïlov knew that girl; there was no holy image, no burning candle beside the coffin; no sound of prayers: the girl had drowned herself.", "스비드리가일로프는 그 소녀를 알고 있었다. 관 곁에는 성상도, 타오르는 촛불도 없었고, 기도 소리도 없었다."], ["She was only fourteen, but her heart was broken.", "그 소녀는 스스로 물에 몸을 던진 것이었다."], ["And she had destroyed herself, crushed by an insult that had appalled and amazed that childish soul, had smirched that angel purity with unmerited disgrace and torn from her a last scream of despair, unheeded and brutally disregarded, on a dark night in the cold and wet while the wind howled....", "그녀는 겨우 열네 살이었으나, 그 마음은 부서져 버린 것이었다. 그리고 그녀는 스스로를 파멸시켰으니, 그 어린 영혼을 경악시키고 소스라치게 한 어떤 모욕에 짓눌려, 그 천사 같은 순결이 분에 넘치는 치욕으로 더럽혀지고, 차갑고 축축한 어둠의 밤, 바람이 울부짖는 가운데 누구의 귀에도 닿지 못한 채 잔혹하게 외면당한 마지막 절망의 비명이 그녀에게서 터져 나왔던 것이다……"]], [["Svidrigaïlov came to himself, got up from the bed and went to the window.", "스비드리가일로프는 정신을 차리고 침대에서 일어나 창가로 갔다."], ["He felt for the latch and opened it.", "그는 더듬어 걸쇠를 찾아 창을 열었다."], ["The wind lashed furiously into the little room and stung his face and his chest, only covered with his shirt, as though with frost.", "바람이 작은 방 안으로 사납게 휘몰아쳐, 셔츠만 걸친 그의 얼굴과 가슴을 서리처럼 찔러 댔다."], ["Under the window there must have been something like a garden, and apparently a pleasure garden.", "창 밑에는 정원 비슷한 것이, 보아하니 유원지 같은 것이 있는 듯했다."], ["There, too, probably there were tea-tables and singing in the daytime.", "거기에도 아마 낮이면 찻상이 놓이고 노랫소리가 울렸으리라."], ["Now drops of rain flew in at the window from the trees and bushes; it was dark as in a cellar, so that he could only just make out some dark blurs of objects.", "지금은 나무와 관목에서 빗방울이 창 안으로 흩날려 들어왔다. 마치 지하실 속처럼 캄캄해서, 그는 사물들의 거뭇한 윤곽 몇 개를 간신히 알아볼 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov, bending down with elbows on the window-sill, gazed for five minutes into the darkness; the boom of a cannon, followed by a second one, resounded in the darkness of the night.", "스비드리가일로프는 창턱에 팔꿈치를 괸 채 몸을 숙이고 오 분 동안 어둠 속을 응시했다. 대포 소리가, 뒤이어 두 번째 대포 소리가 밤의 어둠 속에 울려 퍼졌다."], ["“Ah, the signal!", "'아, 신호로군!"], ["The river is overflowing,”", "강물이 넘치고 있어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“By morning it will be swirling down the street in the lower parts, flooding the basements and cellars.", "'아침이 되면 낮은 지대의 거리로 물이 소용돌이치며 흘러내려, 지하실과 지하 창고를 침수시키겠지."], ["The cellar rats will swim out, and men will curse in the rain and wind as they drag their rubbish to their upper storeys.", "지하실 쥐들이 헤엄쳐 나올 테고, 사람들은 제 살림살이를 위층으로 끌어 올리며 비바람 속에서 욕을 퍼붓겠지."], ["What time is it now?”", "지금 몇 시쯤 됐을까?'"], ["And he had hardly thought it when, somewhere near, a clock on the wall, ticking away hurriedly, struck three.", "그가 채 그렇게 생각하기도 전에, 어디 가까운 곳에서 벽시계가 분주히 째깍거리다가 세 시를 쳤다."]], [["“Aha!", "'아하!"], ["It will be light in an hour!", "한 시간이면 날이 밝겠군!"], ["Why wait?", "뭘 더 기다린담?"], ["I’ll go out at once straight to the park.", "당장 나가서 곧장 공원으로 가야지."], ["I’ll choose a great bush there drenched with rain, so that as soon as one’s shoulder touches it, millions of drops drip on one’s head.”", "거기서 비에 흠뻑 젖은 커다란 관목을 하나 고를 거야, 어깨가 닿기만 해도 수백만 개의 물방울이 머리 위로 뚝뚝 떨어지도록 말이야.'"]], [["He moved away from the window, shut it, lighted the candle, put on his waistcoat, his overcoat and his hat and went out, carrying the candle, into the passage to look for the ragged attendant who would be asleep somewhere in the midst of candle-ends and all sorts of rubbish, to pay him for the room and leave the hotel.", "그는 창가에서 물러나 창을 닫고, 촛불을 켜고, 조끼와 외투를 입고 모자를 쓰고는, 촛불을 들고 복도로 나갔다. 초 토막들과 온갖 잡동사니 한가운데서 어디쯤 잠들어 있을 누더기 차림의 종업원을 찾아, 방값을 치르고 호텔을 떠나려는 것이었다."], ["“It’s the best minute; I couldn’t choose a better.”", "'지금이 가장 좋은 순간이야. 이보다 더 좋은 때를 고를 수는 없겠어.'"]], [["He walked for some time through a long narrow corridor without finding anyone and was just going to call out, when suddenly in a dark corner between an old cupboard and the door he caught sight of a strange object which seemed to be alive.", "그는 길고 좁은 복도를 한참 동안 걸었으나 아무도 찾지 못했고, 막 소리쳐 부르려던 참에, 별안간 낡은 찬장과 문 사이의 어두운 구석에서 살아 있는 것 같은 기이한 물체 하나가 눈에 들어왔다."], ["He bent down with the candle and saw a little girl, not more than five years old, shivering and crying, with her clothes as wet as a soaking house-flannel.", "그가 촛불을 들고 몸을 숙여 보니, 다섯 살이 채 안 된 어린 계집아이가, 물에 푹 젖은 걸레짝처럼 옷이 흠뻑 젖은 채 떨며 울고 있었다."], ["She did not seem afraid of Svidrigaïlov, but looked at him with blank amazement out of her big black eyes.", "아이는 스비드리가일로프를 무서워하는 것 같지는 않았고, 커다란 검은 두 눈으로 멍하니 놀란 듯 그를 바라보았다."], ["Now and then she sobbed as children do when they have been crying a long time, but are beginning to be comforted.", "이따금 아이는, 한참을 울고 나서 이제 막 진정되기 시작하는 아이들이 그러듯, 흐느꼈다."], ["The child’s face was pale and tired, she was numb with cold.", "아이의 얼굴은 창백하고 지쳐 있었고, 추위로 곱아 있었다."], ["“How can she have come here?", "'어쩌다 여기까지 왔을까?"], ["She must have hidden here and not slept all night.”", "여기 숨어서 밤새 한숨도 못 잔 모양이군.'"], ["He began questioning her.", "그는 아이에게 이것저것 묻기 시작했다."], ["The child suddenly becoming animated, chattered away in her baby language, something about “mammy”", "아이는 갑자기 생기를 띠더니, 어린아이 말투로 \"엄마\"가 어떻고, \"엄마가 때릴 거야\""], ["and that “mammy would beat her,”", "하는 둥, \"깨뜨린\""], ["and about some cup that she had “bwoken.”", "무슨 컵에 관해 재잘재잘 떠들어 댔다."], ["The child chattered on without stopping.", "아이는 쉬지 않고 종알거렸다."], ["He could only guess from what she said that she was a neglected child, whose mother, probably a drunken cook, in the service of the hotel, whipped and frightened her; that the child had broken a cup of her mother’s and was so frightened that she had run away the evening before, had hidden for a long while somewhere outside in the rain, at last had made her way in here, hidden behind the cupboard and spent the night there, crying and trembling from the damp, the darkness and the fear that she would be badly beaten for it.", "그는 아이의 말에서 그저 이렇게 짐작할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 아이는 보살핌을 받지 못하는 처지로, 그 어머니는 아마 이 호텔에서 일하는 술꾼 요리사일 텐데 아이를 때리고 겁주었던 것이고, 아이가 어머니의 컵 하나를 깨뜨리고는 너무 무서워 어제저녁 달아나, 한참을 밖에서 빗속 어딘가에 숨어 있다가, 마침내 이리로 기어 들어와 찬장 뒤에 숨어, 습기와 어둠, 그리고 그 일로 호되게 매를 맞으리라는 두려움에 울며 떨며 밤을 지새운 것이었다."], ["He took her in his arms, went back to his room, sat her on the bed, and began undressing her.", "그는 아이를 품에 안고 방으로 돌아와 침대에 앉히고는, 아이의 옷을 벗기기 시작했다."], ["The torn shoes which she had on her stockingless feet were as wet as if they had been standing in a puddle all night.", "양말도 신지 않은 맨발에 신은 다 떨어진 신은, 밤새 웅덩이에 담가 두기라도 한 것처럼 흠뻑 젖어 있었다."], ["When he had undressed her, he put her on the bed, covered her up and wrapped her in the blanket from her head downwards.", "옷을 벗긴 뒤 그는 아이를 침대에 눕히고, 이불을 덮고 머리부터 발끝까지 담요로 감싸 주었다."], ["She fell asleep at once.", "아이는 곧바로 잠이 들었다."], ["Then he sank into dreary musing again.", "그러고 나서 그는 다시 음울한 상념에 잠겼다."]], [["“What folly to trouble myself,”", "'괜한 짓으로 신경을 쓰다니 어리석기도 하지.'"], ["he decided suddenly with an oppressive feeling of annoyance. “What idiocy!”", "그는 갑자기 짓누르는 듯한 짜증을 느끼며 마음을 정했다."], ["In vexation he took up the candle to go and look for the ragged attendant again and make haste to go away.", "'이 무슨 멍청한 짓인가!' 그는 짜증스레 촛불을 집어 들고, 다시 누더기 차림의 종업원을 찾아 서둘러 떠나려 했다."], ["“Damn the child!”", "'빌어먹을 계집애 같으니!'"], ["he thought as he opened the door, but he turned again to see whether the child was asleep.", "그는 문을 열며 생각했으나, 아이가 잠들었는지 보려고 다시 돌아섰다."], ["He raised the blanket carefully.", "그는 조심스레 담요를 들어 올렸다."], ["The child was sleeping soundly, she had got warm under the blanket, and her pale cheeks were flushed.", "아이는 깊이 잠들어 담요 밑에서 몸이 따뜻해졌고, 창백하던 두 뺨이 발그레해져 있었다."], ["But strange to say that flush seemed brighter and coarser than the rosy cheeks of childhood.", "그러나 이상하게도 그 홍조는 어린아이의 발그레한 뺨보다 더 짙고 거칠어 보였다."], ["“It’s a flush of fever,”", "'열이 올라서 그런 거군.'"], ["thought Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프는 생각했다."], ["It was like the flush from drinking, as though she had been given a full glass to drink.", "그것은 마치 술기운으로 인한 홍조 같았으니, 마치 누가 가득 찬 잔을 들이켜게 하기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["Her crimson lips were hot and glowing; but what was this?", "아이의 진홍빛 입술은 뜨겁게 달아올라 있었다. 그런데 이게 무슨 일인가?"], ["He suddenly fancied that her long black eyelashes were quivering, as though the lids were opening and a sly crafty eye peeped out with an unchildlike wink, as though the little girl were not asleep, but pretending.", "그는 문득 아이의 길고 검은 속눈썹이 파르르 떨리는 것 같은 느낌이 들었으니, 마치 눈꺼풀이 열리며 그 속에서 교활하고 음흉한 눈 하나가, 어린아이답지 않게 눈을 찡긋하며 빠끔히 내다보는 것만 같았다. 마치 그 어린 계집아이가 잠든 게 아니라 자는 척하고 있는 것처럼."], ["Yes, it was so.", "그랬다, 정말 그러했다."], ["Her lips parted in a smile.", "아이의 입술이 미소를 띠며 벌어졌다."], ["The corners of her mouth quivered, as though she were trying to control them.", "입가가 부르르 떨렸으니, 마치 그것을 억누르려 애쓰는 듯했다."], ["But now she quite gave up all effort, now it was a grin, a broad grin; there was something shameless, provocative in that quite unchildish face; it was depravity, it was the face of a harlot, the shameless face of a French harlot.", "그러나 이제 아이는 모든 애를 완전히 포기했고, 이제 그것은 활짝 웃는 웃음, 한껏 벌어진 웃음이었다. 그 조금도 어린아이답지 않은 얼굴에는 무언가 뻔뻔스럽고 도발적인 것이 있었다."], ["Now both eyes opened wide; they turned a glowing, shameless glance upon him; they laughed, invited him....", "그것은 타락이었고, 창녀의 얼굴이었으며, 프랑스 창녀의 뻔뻔한 낯짝이었다. 이제 두 눈이 활짝 열렸다."], ["There was something infinitely hideous and shocking in that laugh, in those eyes, in such nastiness in the face of a child.", "그것은 달아오른, 뻔뻔한 눈길을 그에게 던졌고, 웃으며, 그를 꾀어 들였다……. 그 웃음에는, 그 두 눈에는, 어린아이의 얼굴에 어린 그 추잡함에는, 무한히 추악하고 충격적인 무언가가 있었다."], ["“What, at five years old?”", "\"뭐, 다섯 살짜리가?\""], ["Svidrigaïlov muttered in genuine horror.", "스비드리가일로프는 진정한 공포에 휩싸여 중얼거렸다."], ["“What does it mean?”", "\"이게 대체 무슨 일이야?\""], ["And now she turned to him, her little face all aglow, holding out her arms....", "그리고 이제 아이는 환하게 달아오른 작은 얼굴로 그를 향해 돌아누우며 두 팔을 뻗었다……."], ["“Accursed child!”", "\"이 저주받을 계집애!\""], ["Svidrigaïlov cried, raising his hand to strike her, but at that moment he woke up.", "스비드리가일로프는 아이를 내리치려 손을 치켜들며 소리쳤으나, 바로 그 순간 잠에서 깨어났다."]], [["He was in the same bed, still wrapped in the blanket.", "그는 여전히 담요를 두른 채 같은 침대에 있었다."], ["The candle had not been lighted, and daylight was streaming in at the windows.", "촛불은 켜져 있지 않았고, 창으로는 햇빛이 흘러 들어오고 있었다."]], [["“I’ve had nightmare all night!”", "\"밤새 악몽에 시달렸군!\""], ["He got up angrily, feeling utterly shattered; his bones ached.", "그는 화가 나서 일어났는데, 완전히 녹초가 된 느낌이었다."], ["There was a thick mist outside and he could see nothing.", "뼈마디가 쑤셨다. 밖에는 짙은 안개가 끼어 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["It was nearly five.", "거의 다섯 시였다."], ["He had overslept himself!", "그는 늦잠을 잔 것이다!"], ["He got up, put on his still damp jacket and overcoat.", "그는 일어나, 아직 축축한 재킷과 외투를 걸쳤다."], ["Feeling the revolver in his pocket, he took it out and then he sat down, took a notebook out of his pocket and in the most conspicuous place on the title page wrote a few lines in large letters.", "주머니 속의 권총이 만져지자 그것을 꺼냈다가 다시 자리에 앉아, 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼내 속표지의 가장 눈에 잘 띄는 자리에 큰 글씨로 몇 줄을 적었다."], ["Reading them over, he sank into thought with his elbows on the table.", "그것을 다시 읽어 본 뒤, 그는 탁자에 팔꿈치를 괴고 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The revolver and the notebook lay beside him.", "권총과 수첩이 그의 곁에 놓여 있었다."], ["Some flies woke up and settled on the untouched veal, which was still on the table.", "파리 몇 마리가 깨어나, 여전히 탁자 위에 손도 대지 않은 채 놓여 있는 송아지 고기에 내려앉았다."], ["He stared at them and at last with his free right hand began trying to catch one.", "그는 그것들을 물끄러미 바라보다가, 마침내 비어 있는 오른손으로 한 마리를 잡으려 들기 시작했다."], ["He tried till he was tired, but could not catch it.", "그는 지칠 때까지 시도했으나 잡을 수 없었다."], ["At last, realising that he was engaged in this interesting pursuit, he started, got up and walked resolutely out of the room.", "마침내 자기가 이런 흥미로운 일에 몰두하고 있다는 것을 깨닫고는 흠칫하여, 일어서서 단호하게 방을 걸어 나갔다."], ["A minute later he was in the street.", "일 분 뒤 그는 거리에 나와 있었다."]], [["A thick milky mist hung over the town.", "짙은 우윳빛 안개가 도시 위에 드리워 있었다."], ["Svidrigaïlov walked along the slippery dirty wooden pavement towards the Little Neva.", "스비드리가일로프는 미끄럽고 더러운 나무 포도를 따라 작은 네바강 쪽으로 걸었다."], ["He was picturing the waters of the Little Neva swollen in the night, Petrovsky Island, the wet paths, the wet grass, the wet trees and bushes and at last the bush....", "그는 밤사이 불어난 작은 네바강의 물과, 페트로프스키섬과, 젖은 오솔길과, 젖은 풀과, 젖은 나무와 관목, 그리고 마침내 그 관목을 머릿속에 그려 보고 있었다……."], ["He began ill-humouredly staring at the houses, trying to think of something else.", "그는 다른 것을 생각해 보려고 언짢은 기색으로 집들을 노려보기 시작했다."], ["There was not a cabman or a passer-by in the street.", "거리에는 마부도, 행인도 없었다."], ["The bright yellow, wooden, little houses looked dirty and dejected with their closed shutters.", "밝은 노란빛의 작은 목조 가옥들은 덧문을 닫은 채 더럽고 풀이 죽어 보였다."], ["The cold and damp penetrated his whole body and he began to shiver.", "추위와 습기가 그의 온몸에 스며들었고, 그는 몸을 떨기 시작했다."], ["From time to time he came across shop signs and read each carefully.", "이따금 그는 상점 간판을 마주치면 하나하나 꼼꼼히 읽었다."], ["At last he reached the end of the wooden pavement and came to a big stone house.", "마침내 그는 나무 포도의 끝에 다다라 커다란 석조 건물 앞에 이르렀다."], ["A dirty, shivering dog crossed his path with its tail between its legs.", "더럽고 떨고 있는 개 한 마리가 다리 사이에 꼬리를 말아 넣은 채 그의 앞을 가로질러 갔다."], ["A man in a greatcoat lay face downwards, dead drunk, across the pavement.", "외투를 입은 사내 하나가 곤드레만드레 취해 보도를 가로질러 엎드려 죽은 듯 쓰러져 있었다."], ["He looked at him and went on.", "그는 사내를 흘끗 보고는 계속 걸었다."], ["A high tower stood up on the left.", "왼편에 높다란 탑 하나가 솟아 있었다."], ["“Bah!”", "\"이런!\""], ["he shouted, “here is a place.", "그가 소리쳤다. \"여기야말로 제격이군."], ["Why should it be Petrovsky?", "왜 꼭 페트로프스키여야 하지?"], ["It will be in the presence of an official witness anyway....”", "어차피 공적인 증인이 지켜보는 가운데서 하게 될 테니까…….\""]], [["He almost smiled at this new thought and turned into the street where there was the big house with the tower.", "그는 이 새로운 생각에 거의 미소를 지으며, 탑이 있는 그 커다란 건물이 선 거리로 접어들었다."], ["At the great closed gates of the house, a little man stood with his shoulder leaning against them, wrapped in a grey soldier’s coat, with a copper Achilles helmet on his head.", "건물의 굳게 닫힌 커다란 대문에, 회색 군용 외투를 두르고 머리에 구리로 된 아킬레우스 투구를 쓴 자그마한 사내 하나가 어깨를 문에 기댄 채 서 있었다."], ["He cast a drowsy and indifferent glance at Svidrigaïlov.", "그는 졸린 듯 무심한 눈길을 스비드리가일로프에게 던졌다."], ["His face wore that perpetual look of peevish dejection, which is so sourly printed on all faces of Jewish race without exception.", "그의 얼굴에는 유대 인종의 모든 얼굴에 예외 없이 시큰둥하게 찍혀 있는 그 영원한 심술궂은 우수의 표정이 어려 있었다."], ["They both, Svidrigaïlov and Achilles, stared at each other for a few minutes without speaking.", "두 사람은, 스비드리가일로프와 아킬레우스는, 몇 분 동안 아무 말 없이 서로를 빤히 바라보았다."], ["At last it struck Achilles as irregular for a man not drunk to be standing three steps from him, staring and not saying a word.", "마침내 아킬레우스는, 취하지도 않은 사내가 자기에게서 세 걸음 떨어진 곳에 서서 빤히 쳐다보며 한마디도 하지 않는 것이 이상하게 여겨졌다."]], [["“What do you want here?”", "\"여기서 뭘 하시오?\""], ["he said, without moving or changing his position.", "그는 꼼짝도 않고 자세도 바꾸지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["“Nothing, brother, good morning,”", "\"아무것도 아닐세, 이보게, 좋은 아침이군.\""], ["answered Svidrigaïlov.", "스비드리가일로프가 대답했다."]], [["“This isn’t the place.”", "\"여긴 그럴 데가 아니오.\""]], [["“I am going to foreign parts, brother.”", "\"나는 외국으로 떠날 거라네, 이보게.\""]], [["“To foreign parts?”", "\"외국으로?\""]], [["“To America.”", "\"아메리카로.\""]], [["“America.”", "\"아메리카로.\""]], [["Svidrigaïlov took out the revolver and cocked it.", "스비드리가일로프는 권총을 꺼내 공이치기를 당겼다."], ["Achilles raised his eyebrows.", "아킬레우스가 눈썹을 치켜올렸다."]], [["“I say, this is not the place for such jokes!”", "\"이보시오, 여긴 그런 장난을 칠 데가 아니란 말이오!\""]], [["“Why shouldn’t it be the place?”", "\"여기가 왜 그럴 데가 아니란 말인가?\""]], [["“Because it isn’t.”", "\"그럴 데가 아니니까 그렇소.\""]], [["“Well, brother, I don’t mind that.", "\"뭐, 이보게, 나는 그런 건 상관없네."], ["It’s a good place.", "좋은 곳이야."], ["When you are asked, you just say he was going, he said, to America.”", "누가 묻거든, 그냥 그자가 아메리카로 간다고 하더라고 말하게.\""]], [["He put the revolver to his right temple.", "그는 권총을 자기 오른쪽 관자놀이에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“You can’t do it here, it’s not the place,”", "\"여기선 그러면 안 되오, 여긴 그럴 데가 아니란 말이오!\""], ["cried Achilles, rousing himself, his eyes growing bigger and bigger.", "아킬레우스가 정신이 번쩍 든 듯, 눈이 점점 더 휘둥그레지며 소리쳤다."]], [["Svidrigaïlov pulled the trigger.", "스비드리가일로프는 방아쇠를 당겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["The same day, about seven o’clock in the evening, Raskolnikov was on his way to his mother’s and sister’s lodging--the lodging in Bakaleyev’s house which Razumihin had found for them.", "같은 날, 저녁 일곱 시쯤, 라스콜니코프는 어머니와 누이가 묵고 있는 셋집으로 가는 길이었다. 라주미힌이 그들을 위해 찾아 준, 바칼레예프의 집에 있는 그 셋집이었다."], ["The stairs went up from the street.", "계단은 거리에서 위로 이어져 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov walked with lagging steps, as though still hesitating whether to go or not.", "라스콜니코프는 갈까 말까 여전히 망설이는 듯, 굼뜬 걸음으로 걸었다."], ["But nothing would have turned him back: his decision was taken.", "그러나 그 무엇도 그를 돌이키게 하지는 못했을 것이다. 그의 결심은 이미 서 있었다."]], [["“Besides, it doesn’t matter, they still know nothing,”", "'게다가, 아무래도 상관없어, 아직 그들은 아무것도 모르니까.'"], ["he thought, “and they are used to thinking of me as eccentric.”", "그는 생각했다. '그리고 그들은 나를 괴짜로 여기는 데 익숙하지.'"]], [["He was appallingly dressed: his clothes torn and dirty, soaked with a night’s rain.", "그의 옷차림은 끔찍하리만치 형편없었다. 옷은 찢기고 더러웠으며, 밤새 내린 비에 흠뻑 젖어 있었다."], ["His face was almost distorted from fatigue, exposure, the inward conflict that had lasted for twenty-four hours.", "그의 얼굴은 피로와, 비바람에 시달린 탓과, 스물네 시간 동안 이어진 내면의 갈등으로 거의 일그러져 있었다."], ["He had spent all the previous night alone, God knows where.", "그는 지난밤을 통째로 어딘지도 모를 곳에서 홀로 보냈던 것이다."], ["But anyway he had reached a decision.", "그러나 어쨌든 그는 결심에 다다랐다."]], [["He knocked at the door which was opened by his mother.", "그는 문을 두드렸고, 문을 연 것은 어머니였다."], ["Dounia was not at home.", "두냐는 집에 없었다."], ["Even the servant happened to be out.", "하녀마저 마침 외출 중이었다."], ["At first Pulcheria Alexandrovna was speechless with joy and surprise; then she took him by the hand and drew him into the room.", "처음에 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 기쁨과 놀라움에 말을 잇지 못했다. 그러다 그의 손을 잡고 방 안으로 끌어들였다."]], [["“Here you are!”", "\"네가 왔구나!\""], ["she began, faltering with joy.", "그녀는 기쁨에 떨리는 목소리로 입을 열었다."], ["“Don’t be angry with me, Rodya, for welcoming you so foolishly with tears: I am laughing, not crying.", "\"로쟈, 이렇게 바보같이 눈물로 너를 맞는다고 화내지 마라. 우는 게 아니라 웃고 있는 거란다."], ["Did you think I was crying?", "내가 운다고 생각했니?"], ["No, I am delighted, but I’ve got into such a stupid habit of shedding tears.", "아니야, 기뻐서 그래, 그저 이렇게 눈물을 흘리는 멍청한 버릇이 들어 버린 거야."], ["I’ve been like that ever since your father’s death.", "네 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 줄곧 이 모양이란다."], ["I cry for anything.", "무슨 일에든 눈물이 나."], ["Sit down, dear boy, you must be tired; I see you are.", "앉으렴, 얘야, 피곤하겠지. 피곤한 게 보이는구나."], ["Ah, how muddy you are.”", "아, 이렇게 흙투성이가 되다니.\""]], [["“I was in the rain yesterday, mother....”", "\"어제 비를 맞았어요, 어머니…….\""], ["Raskolnikov began.", "라스콜니코프가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\" 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 황급히 말을 가로막았다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna hurriedly interrupted, “you thought I was going to cross-question you in the womanish way I used to; don’t be anxious, I understand, I understand it all: now I’ve learned the ways here and truly I see for myself that they are better.", "\"내가 예전처럼 여자들이나 하는 식으로 꼬치꼬치 캐물을 거라고 생각했겠지. 걱정 마라, 다 안다, 다 이해해."], ["I’ve made up my mind once for all: how could I understand your plans and expect you to give an account of them?", "이제 나도 이곳 방식을 익혔고, 정말이지 내가 보기에도 그편이 더 낫더구나. 한번에 마음을 정했단다."], ["God knows what concerns and plans you may have, or what ideas you are hatching; so it’s not for me to keep nudging your elbow, asking you what you are thinking about?", "내가 어떻게 네 계획들을 이해하고, 또 너더러 그걸 일일이 설명하라고 할 수 있겠니? 네가 무슨 걱정거리며 계획을 품고 있는지, 무슨 생각을 키우고 있는지 하느님만 아실 일인데."], ["But, my goodness!", "그러니 내가 자꾸 네 팔꿈치를 쿡쿡 찌르며 무슨 생각을 하느냐고 물어볼 일이 아니지. 한데 어머나!"], ["why am I running to and fro as though I were crazy...?", "내가 왜 이렇게 미친 사람처럼 왔다 갔다 하는지……? 로쟈, 나는 잡지에 실린 네 글을 벌써 세 번째 읽고 있단다."], ["I am reading your article in the magazine for the third time, Rodya.", "드미트리 프로코피치가 가져다주었어. 그걸 보자마자 나는 속으로 외쳤단다."], ["Dmitri Prokofitch brought it to me.", "'저런, 어리석은 것아,' 하고 생각했지."], ["Directly I saw it I cried out to myself: ‘There, foolish one,’ I thought, ‘that’s what he is busy about; that’s the solution of the mystery!", "'저게 바로 그애가 몰두하고 있는 일이구나, 저게 수수께끼의 해답이야! 학식 있는 사람들은 다 저렇지."], ["Learned people are always like that.", "지금 그애 머릿속엔 새로운 생각들이 들어 있을 거야. 그걸 곰곰이 따져 보고 있는데, 내가 괜히 그애를 성가시게 하고 어지럽히고 있는 거야.'"], ["He may have some new ideas in his head just now; he is thinking them over and I worry him and upset him.’ I read it, my dear, and of course there was a great deal I did not understand; but that’s only natural--how should I?”", "읽었단다, 얘야, 물론 이해 못 한 부분이 많았지만, 그야 당연하지. 내가 어떻게 알겠니?\""]], [["“Show me, mother.”", "\"보여 주세요, 어머니.\""]], [["Raskolnikov took the magazine and glanced at his article.", "라스콜니코프는 잡지를 받아 들고 자기 글을 훑어보았다."], ["Incongruous as it was with his mood and his circumstances, he felt that strange and bitter sweet sensation that every author experiences the first time he sees himself in print; besides, he was only twenty-three.", "그의 기분이나 처지와는 도무지 어울리지 않았지만, 그는 모든 작가가 활자화된 자기 자신을 처음 볼 때 느끼는, 그 야릇하고 씁쓸하면서도 달콤한 감각을 맛보았다. 게다가 그는 이제 겨우 스물세 살이었다."], ["It lasted only a moment.", "그것은 한순간뿐이었다."], ["After reading a few lines he frowned and his heart throbbed with anguish.", "몇 줄을 읽고 나자 그는 이맛살을 찌푸렸고, 가슴은 고통으로 욱신거렸다."], ["He recalled all the inward conflict of the preceding months.", "지난 몇 달 동안의 그 모든 내면의 갈등이 떠올랐다."], ["He flung the article on the table with disgust and anger.", "그는 혐오와 분노에 차서 잡지를 탁자 위로 내던졌다."]], [["“But, however foolish I may be, Rodya, I can see for myself that you will very soon be one of the leading--if not the leading man--in the world of Russian thought.", "\"하지만, 내가 아무리 어리석어도, 로쟈, 네가 머지않아 러시아 사상계의 지도적인 인물들 가운데 하나가, 아니 어쩌면 가장 앞서는 인물이 되리라는 건 내 눈으로도 알 수 있단다."], ["And they dared to think you were mad!", "그런데 사람들이 감히 너를 미쳤다고 생각하다니!"], ["You don’t know, but they really thought that.", "너는 모르겠지만, 정말로 그렇게들 생각했어."], ["Ah, the despicable creatures, how could they understand genius!", "아, 그 비열한 인간들이, 천재를 어떻게 알아보겠니!"], ["And Dounia, Dounia was all but believing it--what do you say to that?", "두냐도, 두냐마저도 하마터면 그 말을 믿을 뻔했단다 — 너는 그걸 어떻게 생각하니?"], ["Your father sent twice to magazines--the first time poems (I’ve got the manuscript and will show you) and the second time a whole novel (I begged him to let me copy it out) and how we prayed that they should be taken--they weren’t!", "네 아버지도 잡지사에 두 번이나 원고를 보냈었지 — 처음엔 시였고(나한테 그 원고가 있으니 보여 주마), 두 번째엔 소설 한 편을 통째로 보냈단다(내가 베껴 쓰게 해 달라고 졸랐지). 그게 채택되게 해 달라고 우리가 얼마나 기도했는지 — 한데 안 됐어!"], ["I was breaking my heart, Rodya, six or seven days ago over your food and your clothes and the way you are living.", "로쟈, 엿새인가 이레 전만 해도 나는 네 끼니며 옷이며 네가 사는 꼴 때문에 가슴이 미어졌단다."], ["But now I see again how foolish I was, for you can attain any position you like by your intellect and talent.", "하지만 이제 보니 내가 또 어리석었던 거야. 너는 네 지성과 재능으로 어떤 지위든 얻을 수 있으니까."], ["No doubt you don’t care about that for the present and you are occupied with much more important matters....”", "물론 지금은 그런 데 마음을 쓰지 않고 훨씬 더 중요한 일들에 매달려 있겠지만…….\""]], [["“Dounia’s not at home, mother?”", "\"두냐는 집에 없나요, 어머니?\""]], [["“No, Rodya.", "\"없단다, 로쟈."], ["I often don’t see her; she leaves me alone.", "나도 그애를 자주 못 봐. 나를 혼자 두고 나가 버리거든."], ["Dmitri Prokofitch comes to see me, it’s so good of him, and he always talks about you.", "드미트리 프로코피치가 나를 보러 와 준단다, 참 고마운 일이지, 그리고 늘 네 얘기를 해."], ["He loves you and respects you, my dear.", "그 사람은 너를 사랑하고 존경한단다, 얘야."], ["I don’t say that Dounia is very wanting in consideration.", "두냐가 사려가 몹시 부족하다는 말은 아니야."], ["I am not complaining.", "불평하는 게 아니란다."], ["She has her ways and I have mine; she seems to have got some secrets of late and I never have any secrets from you two.", "그애한테는 그애 나름의 방식이 있고, 나한텐 내 방식이 있는 거지. 그애가 요즘 들어 무슨 비밀이 생긴 것 같은데, 나야 너희 둘한테 비밀이라곤 통 없잖니."], ["Of course, I am sure that Dounia has far too much sense, and besides she loves you and me... but I don’t know what it will all lead to.", "물론 두냐가 분별이 넘칠 만큼 많다는 건 확실하고, 게다가 그앤 너와 나를 사랑하지……, 하지만 이 모든 게 어디로 흘러갈지 모르겠구나."], ["You’ve made me so happy by coming now, Rodya, but she has missed you by going out; when she comes in I’ll tell her: ‘Your brother came in while you were out.", "네가 지금 와 줘서 나는 이리도 기쁜데, 그애는 나가는 바람에 너를 놓쳤구나. 그애가 들어오면 이렇게 말해 줘야지."], ["Where have you been all this time?’ You mustn’t spoil me, Rodya, you know; come when you can, but if you can’t, it doesn’t matter, I can wait.", "'네가 나간 사이에 오라비가 다녀갔단다. 그동안 어디 가 있었니?'"], ["I shall know, anyway, that you are fond of me, that will be enough for me.", "로쟈, 너 나를 너무 응석받이로 만들면 안 돼, 알지. 올 수 있을 때 오렴, 못 오면 괜찮아, 나는 기다릴 수 있으니까."], ["I shall read what you write, I shall hear about you from everyone, and sometimes you’ll come yourself to see me.", "어쨌든 네가 나를 아낀다는 걸 알 테니, 나한테는 그걸로 족하단다. 네가 쓰는 글을 읽고, 모두에게서 네 소식을 듣고, 가끔은 네가 직접 나를 보러 와 주고."], ["What could be better?", "이보다 더 좋은 게 어디 있겠니?"], ["Here you’ve come now to comfort your mother, I see that.”", "봐라, 지금도 어미를 위로하러 와 줬잖니, 나는 다 안단다.\""]], [["Here Pulcheria Alexandrovna began to cry.", "여기서 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 울기 시작했다."]], [["“Here I am again!", "\"또 이러는구나!"], ["Don’t mind my foolishness.", "내 어리석음을 마음에 두지 마라."], ["My goodness, why am I sitting here?”", "어머나, 내가 왜 여기 이러고 앉아 있지?\""], ["she cried, jumping up.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["“There is coffee and I don’t offer you any.", "\"커피가 있는데 너한테 권하지도 않았구나."], ["Ah, that’s the selfishness of old age.", "아, 이게 늙은이의 이기심이라는 거야."], ["I’ll get it at once!”", "당장 가져오마!\""]], [["“Mother, don’t trouble, I am going at once.", "\"어머니, 수고하지 마세요, 저는 곧 가야 해요."], ["I haven’t come for that.", "그것 때문에 온 게 아니거든요."], ["Please listen to me.”", "제발 제 말 좀 들어 주세요.\""]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna went up to him timidly.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 머뭇거리며 그에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Mother, whatever happens, whatever you hear about me, whatever you are told about me, will you always love me as you do now?”", "\"어머니, 무슨 일이 일어나든, 저에 대해 무슨 소문을 듣든, 사람들이 저에 대해 무슨 말을 하든, 지금처럼 늘 저를 사랑해 주시겠어요?\""], ["he asked suddenly from the fullness of his heart, as though not thinking of his words and not weighing them.", "그는 자기 말을 생각하지도, 가늠하지도 않은 채, 가슴 가득 차오르는 마음에서 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Rodya, Rodya, what is the matter?", "\"로쟈, 로쟈, 무슨 일이니?"], ["How can you ask me such a question?", "어떻게 나한테 그런 걸 묻니?"], ["Why, who will tell me anything about you?", "아니, 누가 너에 대해 나한테 뭐라고 말하겠니?"], ["Besides, I shouldn’t believe anyone, I should refuse to listen.”", "게다가 나는 누구 말도 믿지 않을 거고, 아예 들으려고도 하지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“I’ve come to assure you that I’ve always loved you and I am glad that we are alone, even glad Dounia is out,”", "\"제가 늘 어머니를 사랑해 왔다는 걸 확실히 말씀드리려고 왔어요. 그리고 우리 둘만 있어서 다행이에요, 두냐가 없는 것까지도 기뻐요.\""], ["he went on with the same impulse.", "그는 똑같은 충동에 휩싸여 말을 이었다."], ["“I have come to tell you that though you will be unhappy, you must believe that your son loves you now more than himself, and that all you thought about me, that I was cruel and didn’t care about you, was all a mistake.", "\"비록 어머니가 불행해지시겠지만, 어머니 아들이 지금 자기 자신보다 어머니를 더 사랑한다는 것을, 그리고 제가 잔인하고 어머니를 아끼지 않는다고 어머니가 생각하셨던 게 모두 잘못된 거였다는 걸 믿어 주십사 말씀드리러 온 거예요."], ["I shall never cease to love you....", "저는 결코 어머니를 사랑하기를 멈추지 않을 겁니다……."], ["Well, that’s enough: I thought I must do this and begin with this....”", "자, 이걸로 충분해요. 이렇게 해야 한다고, 이걸로 시작해야 한다고 생각했어요…….\""]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna embraced him in silence, pressing him to her bosom and weeping gently.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 말없이 그를 끌어안고 가슴에 꼭 품은 채 가만히 흐느꼈다."]], [["“I don’t know what is wrong with you, Rodya,”", "\"네가 뭐가 잘못된 건지 모르겠구나, 로쟈.\""], ["she said at last.", "그녀가 마침내 입을 열었다."], ["“I’ve been thinking all this time that we were simply boring you and now I see that there is a great sorrow in store for you, and that’s why you are miserable.", "\"그동안 나는 우리가 그저 너를 성가시게 하는 거라고만 생각했는데, 이제 보니 너한테 커다란 슬픔이 닥쳐오고 있고, 그래서 네가 괴로운 거였구나."], ["I’ve foreseen it a long time, Rodya.", "나는 오래전부터 그걸 예감하고 있었단다, 로쟈."], ["Forgive me for speaking about it.", "이런 말을 하는 걸 용서해라."], ["I keep thinking about it and lie awake at nights.", "자꾸 그 생각이 들어서 밤에 잠을 못 이룬단다."], ["Your sister lay talking in her sleep all last night, talking of nothing but you.", "네 누이가 어젯밤 내내 잠꼬대를 했는데, 온통 네 얘기뿐이었어."], ["I caught something, but I couldn’t make it out.", "몇 마디 알아들었지만, 무슨 뜻인지는 알 수가 없었지."], ["I felt all the morning as though I were going to be hanged, waiting for something, expecting something, and now it has come!", "오늘 아침 내내 마치 교수형에라도 처해질 것처럼, 무언가를 기다리며, 무언가를 예감하며 지냈는데, 이제 그게 닥쳐온 거야!"], ["Rodya, Rodya, where are you going?", "로쟈, 로쟈, 어디로 가려는 거니?"], ["You are going away somewhere?”", "어디론가 멀리 떠나는 거니?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“That’s what I thought!", "\"그럴 줄 알았다!"], ["I can come with you, you know, if you need me.", "나도 너랑 같이 갈 수 있어, 알지, 네가 나를 필요로 한다면."], ["And Dounia, too; she loves you, she loves you dearly--and Sofya Semyonovna may come with us if you like.", "두냐도 함께. 그앤 너를 사랑한단다, 너를 끔찍이 사랑해 — 그리고 네가 원하면 소피야 세묘노브나도 우리와 함께 가도 좋아."], ["You see, I am glad to look upon her as a daughter even...", "봐라, 나는 그애를 딸로 여기는 것까지도 기쁘니까……."], ["Dmitri Prokofitch will help us to go together. But...", "드미트리 프로코피치가 우리가 같이 갈 수 있도록 도와줄 거야."], ["where... are you going?”", "한데……, 어디로……, 가려는 거니?\""]], [["“Good-bye, mother.”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 어머니.\""]], [["“What, to-day?”", "\"뭐라고, 오늘?\""], ["she cried, as though losing him for ever.", "그녀는 그를 영영 잃기라도 하는 듯 외쳤다."]], [["“I can’t stay, I must go now....”", "\"머무를 수가 없어요, 지금 가야 해요…….\""]], [["“And can’t I come with you?”", "\"나도 너랑 같이 갈 순 없니?\""]], [["“No, but kneel down and pray to God for me.", "\"안 돼요, 그저 무릎 꿇고 저를 위해 하느님께 기도해 주세요."], ["Your prayer perhaps will reach Him.”", "어머니 기도라면 어쩌면 그분께 가닿을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Let me bless you and sign you with the cross.", "\"너를 축복하고 성호를 그어 주마. 됐다, 됐어."], ["That’s right, that’s right. Oh, God, what are we doing?”", "오, 하느님, 우리가 지금 무엇을 하고 있는 건지?\""]], [["Yes, he was glad, he was very glad that there was no one there, that he was alone with his mother.", "그렇다, 그는 기뻤다, 거기에 아무도 없다는 것이, 어머니와 단둘이라는 것이 그는 무척 기뻤다."], ["For the first time after all those awful months his heart was softened.", "그 끔찍한 몇 달이 지난 뒤 처음으로 그의 마음이 누그러졌다."], ["He fell down before her, he kissed her feet and both wept, embracing.", "그는 어머니 앞에 엎드려 그 발에 입을 맞추었고, 둘은 서로 끌어안은 채 울었다."], ["And she was not surprised and did not question him this time.", "그리고 이번에 그녀는 놀라지도, 그에게 캐묻지도 않았다."], ["For some days she had realised that something awful was happening to her son and that now some terrible minute had come for him.", "며칠 전부터 그녀는 자기 아들에게 무언가 끔찍한 일이 벌어지고 있으며, 이제 그에게 어떤 무서운 순간이 닥쳐왔음을 깨닫고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Rodya, my darling, my first born,”", "\"로쟈, 내 사랑, 내 맏이.\""], ["she said sobbing, “now you are just as when you were little.", "그녀가 흐느끼며 말했다. \"지금 너는 꼭 어릴 적 모습이로구나."], ["You would run like this to me and hug me and kiss me.", "그땐 이렇게 나한테 달려와 끌어안고 입을 맞추곤 했지."], ["When your father was living and we were poor, you comforted us simply by being with us and when I buried your father, how often we wept together at his grave and embraced, as now.", "네 아버지가 살아 계시고 우리가 가난했을 때, 너는 그저 우리 곁에 있어 주는 것만으로 우리를 위로했단다. 그리고 네 아버지를 묻었을 때, 우리가 그 무덤가에서 얼마나 자주 함께 울며 끌어안았는지, 지금처럼 말이야."], ["And if I’ve been crying lately, it’s that my mother’s heart had a foreboding of trouble.", "요즘 내가 울었다면, 그건 어미의 마음이 화가 닥칠 걸 예감했기 때문이야."], ["The first time I saw you, that evening, you remember, as soon as we arrived here, I guessed simply from your eyes.", "처음 너를 본 그날 저녁, 기억하니, 우리가 여기 도착하자마자, 나는 단지 네 눈빛만 보고도 짐작했단다."], ["My heart sank at once, and to-day when I opened the door and looked at you, I thought the fatal hour had come.", "그 순간 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았고, 오늘 문을 열고 너를 봤을 땐, 운명의 시각이 왔구나 싶었지."], ["Rodya, Rodya, you are not going away to-day?” “No!”", "로쟈, 로쟈, 오늘 떠나는 건 아니지?\""]], [["“You’ll come again?”", "\"아니에요!\" \"또 올 거지?\""]], [["“Yes... I’ll come.”", "\"네……, 올게요.\""]], [["“Rodya, don’t be angry, I don’t dare to question you.", "\"로쟈, 화내지 마라, 나는 감히 너한테 캐묻지 않으마."], ["I know I mustn’t.", "그래선 안 된다는 걸 안다."], ["Only say two words to me--is it far where you are going?”", "그저 두 마디만 해 다오 — 네가 가는 곳이 머니?\""]], [["“Very far.”", "\"아주 멀어요.\""]], [["“What is awaiting you there?", "\"거기서 너를 기다리는 게 무엇이니?"], ["Some post or career for you?”", "무슨 자리나 일자리라도 있는 거니?\""]], [["“What God sends... only pray for me.”", "\"하느님이 보내 주시는 것이지요……, 그저 저를 위해 기도해 주세요.\""], ["Raskolnikov went to the door, but she clutched him and gazed despairingly into his eyes.", "라스콜니코프는 문 쪽으로 갔지만, 그녀가 그를 붙들고 절망적인 눈길로 그의 눈을 들여다보았다."], ["Her face worked with terror.", "그녀의 얼굴은 공포로 일그러졌다."]], [["“Enough, mother,”", "\"이제 됐어요, 어머니.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, deeply regretting that he had come.", "라스콜니코프가, 여기 온 것을 깊이 후회하며 말했다."]], [["“Not for ever, it’s not yet for ever?", "\"영영은 아니지, 아직 영영은 아니지?"], ["You’ll come, you’ll come to-morrow?”", "올 거지, 내일 올 거지?\""]], [["“I will, I will, good-bye.”", "\"올게요, 올게요, 안녕히 계세요.\""], ["He tore himself away at last.", "그는 마침내 몸을 떼어 냈다."]], [["It was a warm, fresh, bright evening; it had cleared up in the morning.", "따뜻하고 상쾌하고 밝은 저녁이었다. 아침나절에 날이 개었던 것이다."], ["Raskolnikov went to his lodgings; he made haste.", "라스콜니코프는 자기 하숙집으로 향했다. 그는 서둘렀다."], ["He wanted to finish all before sunset.", "해가 지기 전에 모든 것을 끝내고 싶었다."], ["He did not want to meet anyone till then.", "그때까지는 아무와도 마주치고 싶지 않았다."], ["Going up the stairs he noticed that Nastasya rushed from the samovar to watch him intently.", "계단을 올라가다가 그는 나스타시야가 사모바르 곁에서 달려 나와 그를 뚫어지게 지켜보는 것을 알아챘다."], ["“Can anyone have come to see me?”", "'누가 나를 보러 온 건가?'"], ["he wondered.", "그는 의아해했다."], ["He had a disgusted vision of Porfiry.", "포르피리의 모습이 역겹게 떠올랐다."], ["But opening his door he saw Dounia.", "그러나 문을 열자 두냐가 보였다."], ["She was sitting alone, plunged in deep thought, and looked as though she had been waiting a long time.", "그녀는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 채 홀로 앉아 있었는데, 오래 기다린 사람 같았다."], ["He stopped short in the doorway.", "그는 문간에서 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["She rose from the sofa in dismay and stood up facing him.", "그녀는 당황하여 소파에서 일어나 그를 마주하고 섰다."], ["Her eyes, fixed upon him, betrayed horror and infinite grief.", "그를 응시하는 그녀의 눈에는 공포와 끝없는 비탄이 어려 있었다."], ["And from those eyes alone he saw at once that she knew.", "그리고 그 눈빛만으로 그는 그녀가 알고 있다는 것을 단번에 알아차렸다."]], [["“Am I to come in or go away?”", "\"들어가도 되니, 아니면 가야 하니?\""], ["he asked uncertainly.", "그가 머뭇머뭇 물었다."]], [["“I’ve been all day with Sofya Semyonovna.", "\"하루 종일 소피야 세묘노브나랑 있었어요."], ["We were both waiting for you.", "우리 둘 다 오빠를 기다렸어요."], ["We thought that you would be sure to come there.”", "오빠가 분명 거기로 올 거라고 생각했거든요.\""]], [["Raskolnikov went into the room and sank exhausted on a chair.", "라스콜니코프는 방 안으로 들어가 기진맥진한 채 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["“I feel weak, Dounia, I am very tired; and I should have liked at this moment to be able to control myself.”", "\"기운이 없구나, 두냐, 몹시 피곤해. 그리고 지금 이 순간만큼은 나 자신을 추스를 수 있었으면 했는데.\""]], [["He glanced at her mistrustfully.", "그는 미심쩍은 눈길로 그녀를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Where were you all night?”", "\"밤새 어디 계셨어요?\""]], [["“I don’t remember clearly.", "\"잘 기억이 안 나."], ["You see, sister, I wanted to make up my mind once for all, and several times I walked by the Neva, I remember that I wanted to end it all there, but... I couldn’t make up my mind,”", "그게 말이야, 누이야, 나는 한번에 마음을 정하고 싶었어, 그래서 몇 번이나 네바 강가를 걸었지, 거기서 모든 걸 끝내 버리고 싶었던 건 기억나는데, 하지만……, 마음을 정할 수가 없었어.\""], ["he whispered, looking at her mistrustfully again.", "그는 또다시 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 보며 속삭였다."]], [["“Thank God!", "\"다행이에요!"], ["That was just what we were afraid of, Sofya Semyonovna and I.", "그게 바로 우리가, 소피야 세묘노브나랑 제가 두려워했던 거예요."], ["Then you still have faith in life?", "그럼 오빠는 아직 삶에 대한 믿음이 있는 거네요?"], ["Thank God, thank God!”", "다행이에요, 정말 다행이에요!\""]], [["Raskolnikov smiled bitterly.", "라스콜니코프는 씁쓸하게 웃었다."]], [["“I haven’t faith, but I have just been weeping in mother’s arms; I haven’t faith, but I have just asked her to pray for me.", "\"나한텐 믿음이 없어, 하지만 방금 어머니 품에 안겨 울었지. 믿음이 없으면서도, 방금 어머니한테 나를 위해 기도해 달라고 했어."], ["I don’t know how it is, Dounia, I don’t understand it.”", "어떻게 그런 건지 모르겠다, 두냐, 나도 이해할 수가 없어.\""]], [["“Have you been at mother’s?", "\"어머니한테 다녀오셨어요?"], ["Have you told her?”", "어머니한테 말씀드렸어요?\""], ["cried Dounia, horror-stricken.", "두냐가 공포에 질려 외쳤다."], ["“Surely you haven’t done that?”", "\"설마 그러신 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“No, I didn’t tell her...", "\"아니, 말씀은……, 입으로는 안 드렸어."], ["in words; but she understood a great deal.", "하지만 어머니는 많은 걸 이해하셨어."], ["She heard you talking in your sleep.", "네 잠꼬대를 들으셨거든."], ["I am sure she half understands it already.", "어머니는 이미 절반쯤은 짐작하고 계셔, 분명히."], ["Perhaps I did wrong in going to see her.", "어쩌면 어머니를 보러 간 게 잘못이었는지도 몰라."], ["I don’t know why I did go.", "내가 왜 갔는지 모르겠어."], ["I am a contemptible person, Dounia.”", "나는 비열한 인간이야, 두냐.\""]], [["“A contemptible person, but ready to face suffering!", "\"비열한 인간이라도, 고통을 마주할 각오는 되어 있잖아요!"], ["You are, aren’t you?”", "그렇죠, 그렇잖아요?\""]], [["“Yes, I am going. At once.", "\"그래, 나는 갈 거야."], ["Yes, to escape the disgrace I thought of drowning myself, Dounia, but as I looked into the water, I thought that if I had considered myself strong till now I’d better not be afraid of disgrace,”", "당장. 그래, 치욕을 피하려고 물에 빠져 죽을 생각도 했어, 두냐, 하지만 물속을 들여다보면서 생각했지, 지금껏 나 자신을 강하다고 여겨 왔다면 치욕 따위 두려워하지 않는 편이 낫다고.\""], ["he said, hurrying on.", "그는 서둘러 말을 이었다."], ["“It’s pride, Dounia.”", "\"그건 자존심이야, 두냐.\""]], [["“Pride, Rodya.”", "\"자존심이죠, 로쟈.\""]], [["There was a gleam of fire in his lustreless eyes; he seemed to be glad to think that he was still proud.", "생기 없던 그의 눈에 불꽃이 번뜩였다. 자신이 아직 자존심을 지니고 있다는 생각이 기뻤던 모양이다."]], [["“You don’t think, sister, that I was simply afraid of the water?”", "\"누이야, 너는 내가 그저 물이 무서웠던 거라고 생각하진 않니?\""], ["he asked, looking into her face with a sinister smile.", "그가 음험한 미소를 띠며 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, Rodya, hush!”", "\"오, 로쟈, 그만해요!\""], ["cried Dounia bitterly.", "두냐가 쓰라리게 외쳤다."], ["Silence lasted for two minutes.", "이 분 동안 침묵이 이어졌다."], ["He sat with his eyes fixed on the floor; Dounia stood at the other end of the table and looked at him with anguish.", "그는 바닥에 눈을 박은 채 앉아 있었고, 두냐는 탁자 반대편 끝에 서서 비통한 얼굴로 그를 바라보았다."], ["Suddenly he got up.", "갑자기 그가 일어섰다."]], [["“It’s late, it’s time to go!", "\"늦었어, 갈 시간이야!"], ["I am going at once to give myself up.", "지금 당장 가서 자수할 거야."], ["But I don’t know why I am going to give myself up.”", "하지만 내가 왜 자수하러 가는 건지는 모르겠어.\""]], [["Big tears fell down her cheeks.", "굵은 눈물방울이 그녀의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."]], [["“You are crying, sister, but can you hold out your hand to me?”", "\"누이야, 너는 울고 있지만, 그래도 나한테 손을 내밀어 줄 수 있겠니?\""]], [["“You doubted it?”", "\"그걸 의심하셨어요?\""]], [["She threw her arms round him.", "그녀는 두 팔로 그를 끌어안았다."]], [["“Aren’t you half expiating your crime by facing the suffering?”", "\"고통을 마주하는 것만으로도 오빠는 죄를 절반쯤 속죄하는 거 아닌가요?\""], ["she cried, holding him close and kissing him.", "그녀는 그를 꼭 끌어안고 입을 맞추며 외쳤다."]], [["“Crime?", "\"죄?"], ["What crime?”", "무슨 죄?\""], ["he cried in sudden fury.", "그가 별안간 격분하여 외쳤다."], ["“That I killed a vile noxious insect, an old pawnbroker woman, of use to no one!...", "\"내가 비열하고 해로운 벌레 한 마리를, 아무에게도 쓸모없는 늙은 전당포 노파를 죽였다는 거?"], ["Killing her was atonement for forty sins.", "……그 여자를 죽인 건 마흔 가지 죄를 갚은 셈이야."], ["She was sucking the life out of poor people.", "그 여자는 가난한 사람들의 피를 빨아먹고 있었어."], ["Was that a crime?", "그게 죄야?"], ["I am not thinking of it and I am not thinking of expiating it, and why are you all rubbing it in on all sides?", "나는 그걸 죄라고 생각하지 않고, 그걸 속죄할 생각도 없어, 그런데 왜들 사방에서 그걸 나한테 들이미는 거지?"], ["‘A crime!", "'죄라니!"], ["a crime!’ Only now I see clearly the imbecility of my cowardice, now that I have decided to face this superfluous disgrace.", "죄라니!'"], ["It’s simply because I am contemptible and have nothing in me that I have decided to, perhaps too for my advantage, as that...", "이 불필요한 치욕을 마주하기로 결심한 지금에서야, 내 비겁함이 얼마나 천치 같은 짓이었는지 똑똑히 보이는구나."], ["Porfiry... suggested!”", "그저 내가 비열하고 내 안에 아무것도 없으니까 결심한 거야, 어쩌면 그……, 포르피리가……, 넌지시 일러 준 대로 내 이득을 위해서이기도 하고!\""]], [["“Brother, brother, what are you saying?", "\"오빠, 오빠, 무슨 말을 하는 거예요?"], ["Why, you have shed blood?”", "아니, 오빠는 피를 흘리게 했잖아요?\""], ["cried Dounia in despair.", "두냐가 절망에 차서 외쳤다."]], [["“Which all men shed,”", "\"모든 인간이 흘리게 하는 피지.\""], ["he put in almost frantically, “which flows and has always flowed in streams, which is spilt like champagne, and for which men are crowned in the Capitol and are called afterwards benefactors of mankind.", "그는 거의 미친 듯이 끼어들었다. \"강물처럼 흐르고 늘 흘러왔던, 샴페인처럼 쏟아지는 피, 그것 때문에 사람들이 카피톨리움에서 월계관을 쓰고, 나중에 인류의 은인이라 불리는 그런 피야."], ["Look into it more carefully and understand it!", "더 잘 들여다보고 이해해 봐!"], ["I too wanted to do good to men and would have done hundreds, thousands of good deeds to make up for that one piece of stupidity, not stupidity even, simply clumsiness, for the idea was by no means so stupid as it seems now that it has failed....", "나도 사람들에게 선을 베풀고 싶었고, 그 한 번의 어리석은 짓을 — 아니, 어리석은 짓도 아니야, 그저 서투른 짓이지, 왜냐하면 이제 와 실패하고 나니 어리석어 보일 뿐, 그 발상 자체는 결코 그렇게 어리석은 게 아니었거든……"], ["(Everything seems stupid when it fails.) By that stupidity I only wanted to put myself into an independent position, to take the first step, to obtain means, and then everything would have been smoothed over by benefits immeasurable in comparison....", "(실패하면 모든 게 어리석어 보이는 법이지.) — 만회하려고 수백, 수천 가지 선행을 베풀었을 거야. 그 어리석은 짓으로 나는 그저 독립된 처지에 서고, 첫걸음을 내딛고, 수단을 손에 넣고 싶었을 뿐이야, 그러고 나면 그에 비할 수 없이 막대한 선행으로 모든 게 무마됐겠지……."], ["But I... I couldn’t carry out even the first step, because I am contemptible, that’s what’s the matter!", "하지만 나는……, 나는 첫걸음조차 해내지 못했어, 내가 비열한 인간이니까, 문제는 바로 그거야!"], ["And yet I won’t look at it as you do.", "그래도 나는 너처럼 그걸 바라보진 않을 거야."], ["If I had succeeded I should have been crowned with glory, but now I’m trapped.”", "만약 내가 성공했다면 영광의 월계관을 썼겠지만, 지금은 덫에 걸린 거지.\""]], [["“But that’s not so, not so!", "\"하지만 그건 그렇지 않아요, 그렇지 않아요!"], ["Brother, what are you saying?”", "오빠, 무슨 말을 하는 거예요?\""]], [["“Ah, it’s not picturesque, not æsthetically attractive!", "\"아, 그게 그림처럼 멋지지가 않고, 미적으로 매혹적이지가 않다는 거지!"], ["I fail to understand why bombarding people by regular siege is more honourable.", "나는 왜 정식으로 포위해서 사람들을 포격하는 게 더 명예로운 일인지 이해할 수가 없어."], ["The fear of appearances is the first symptom of impotence.", "겉모습을 두려워하는 건 무능의 첫 번째 징후야."], ["I’ve never, never recognised this more clearly than now, and I am further than ever from seeing that what I did was a crime.", "지금처럼 이것을 또렷이 깨달은 적이 없고, 내가 한 짓이 죄였다고 보는 것과는 그 어느 때보다 멀어졌어."], ["I’ve never, never been stronger and more convinced than now.”", "지금처럼 강하고 확신에 찬 적이 없어.\""]], [["The colour had rushed into his pale exhausted face, but as he uttered his last explanation, he happened to meet Dounia’s eyes and he saw such anguish in them that he could not help being checked.", "그의 창백하고 지친 얼굴에 핏기가 확 올랐지만, 마지막 변론을 입에 올리던 순간, 그는 우연히 두냐의 눈과 마주쳤고, 거기서 어찌나 깊은 비탄을 보았던지 멈칫하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He felt that he had, anyway, made these two poor women miserable, that he was, anyway, the cause...", "그는 어쨌든 자신이 이 가엾은 두 여인을 비참하게 만들었음을, 어쨌든 자신이 그 원인임을 느꼈다……."]], [["“Dounia darling, if I am guilty forgive me (though I cannot be forgiven if I am guilty).", "\"두냐, 내 사랑, 내가 죄인이라면 나를 용서해 줘(하긴 내가 죄인이라면 용서받을 수 없겠지만)."], ["Good-bye!", "잘 있어!"], ["We won’t dispute.", "다투지 말자."], ["It’s time, high time to go.", "갈 시간이야, 이미 한참 늦었어."], ["Don’t follow me, I beseech you, I have somewhere else to go....", "나를 따라오지 마, 제발 부탁이야, 들를 데가 따로 있어……."], ["But you go at once and sit with mother.", "그러니 너는 당장 가서 어머니 곁에 있어 줘. 제발 그래 주렴!"], ["I entreat you to!", "너한테 하는 마지막 부탁이야."], ["It’s my last request of you.", "어머니 곁을 절대 떠나지 마."], ["Don’t leave her at all; I left her in a state of anxiety, that she is not fit to bear; she will die or go out of her mind.", "나는 어머니를 불안한 상태로 두고 왔는데, 어머니가 견뎌 내기 힘든 상태야. 어머니가 돌아가시거나 정신을 놓아 버릴지도 몰라."], ["Be with her!", "어머니 곁에 있어 줘!"], ["Razumihin will be with you.", "라주미힌이 너희와 함께 있을 거야."], ["I’ve been talking to him....", "내가 그 친구한테 얘기해 뒀어……."], ["Don’t cry about me: I’ll try to be honest and manly all my life, even if I am a murderer.", "나 때문에 울지 마. 비록 살인자라 해도, 나는 평생 정직하고 의연하게 살려고 애쓸 거야."], ["Perhaps I shall some day make a name.", "어쩌면 언젠가 이름을 떨칠지도 몰라."], ["I won’t disgrace you, you will see; I’ll still show....", "너희를 욕되게 하지 않을게, 두고 봐. 그래도 보여 줄 테니까……."], ["Now good-bye for the present,”", "자, 이제 당분간 안녕.\""], ["he concluded hurriedly, noticing again a strange expression in Dounia’s eyes at his last words and promises.", "그는 자신의 마지막 말과 약속에 두냐의 눈에 또다시 떠오른 야릇한 표정을 알아채고는 서둘러 말을 맺었다."], ["“Why are you crying?", "\"왜 우는 거야?"], ["Don’t cry, don’t cry: we are not parting for ever!", "울지 마, 울지 마. 우리가 영영 헤어지는 게 아니잖아!"], ["Ah, yes!", "아, 그래!"], ["Wait a minute, I’d forgotten!”", "잠깐만, 깜빡할 뻔했네!\""]], [["He went to the table, took up a thick dusty book, opened it and took from between the pages a little water-colour portrait on ivory.", "그는 탁자로 가서 먼지 앉은 두꺼운 책 한 권을 집어 펼치더니, 책장 사이에서 상아에 그린 작은 수채화 초상 하나를 꺼냈다."], ["It was the portrait of his landlady’s daughter, who had died of fever, that strange girl who had wanted to be a nun.", "그것은 열병으로 죽은 하숙집 여주인의 딸, 수녀가 되고 싶어 했던 그 기묘한 처녀의 초상이었다."], ["For a minute he gazed at the delicate expressive face of his betrothed, kissed the portrait and gave it to Dounia.", "그는 잠시 자기 약혼녀의 섬세하고 표정 어린 얼굴을 응시하다가, 초상에 입을 맞추고는 두냐에게 건넸다."]], [["“I used to talk a great deal about it to her, only to her,”", "\"나는 이 일을 그 여자한테 많이 얘기하곤 했어, 오직 그 여자한테만.\""], ["he said thoughtfully.", "그가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["“To her heart I confided much of what has since been so hideously realised.", "\"그 여자의 마음에다 나는, 그 뒤로 이토록 흉측하게 실현되어 버린 많은 것을 털어놓았지."], ["Don’t be uneasy,”", "마음 쓰지 마.\""], ["he returned to Dounia, “she was as much opposed to it as you, and I am glad that she is gone.", "그가 두냐에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"그 여자도 너만큼이나 반대했어, 그래서 나는 그 여자가 떠난 게 다행이야."], ["The great point is that everything now is going to be different, is going to be broken in two,”", "중요한 건, 이제 모든 게 달라지리라는 거, 둘로 쪼개지리라는 거야.\""], ["he cried, suddenly returning to his dejection.", "그가 갑자기 다시 침울함에 빠져들며 외쳤다."], ["“Everything, everything, and am I prepared for it?", "\"모든 게, 모든 게, 한데 나는 그것에 대비가 되어 있나?"], ["Do I want it myself?", "나 스스로 그걸 원하긴 하나?"], ["They say it is necessary for me to suffer!", "사람들은 내가 고통받아야 한다고 말하지!"], ["What’s the object of these senseless sufferings?", "이 무의미한 고통의 목적이 대체 뭐야?"], ["shall I know any better what they are for, when I am crushed by hardships and idiocy, and weak as an old man after twenty years’ penal servitude?", "이십 년 징역살이 끝에 고난과 천치 같은 짓에 짓눌려 늙은이처럼 쇠약해지고 나면, 그 고통이 무엇을 위한 것이었는지 내가 더 잘 알게 되기라도 하나?"], ["And what shall I have to live for then?", "그러고 나면 나는 무엇을 위해 살아야 하지?"], ["Why am I consenting to that life now?", "왜 나는 지금 그런 삶에 동의하고 있는 거지?"], ["Oh, I knew I was contemptible when I stood looking at the Neva at daybreak to-day!”", "오, 오늘 동틀 녘 네바 강을 바라보며 서 있을 때, 나는 내가 비열한 인간이라는 걸 알았어!\""]], [["At last they both went out.", "마침내 둘은 함께 밖으로 나왔다."], ["It was hard for Dounia, but she loved him.", "두냐에게는 힘겨운 일이었지만, 그녀는 그를 사랑했다."], ["She walked away, but after going fifty paces she turned round to look at him again.", "그녀는 걸어갔지만, 쉰 걸음쯤 가다가 다시 그를 보려고 돌아섰다."], ["He was still in sight.", "그는 아직 시야에 있었다."], ["At the corner he too turned and for the last time their eyes met; but noticing that she was looking at him, he motioned her away with impatience and even vexation, and turned the corner abruptly.", "모퉁이에서 그도 돌아섰고, 마지막으로 둘의 눈이 마주쳤다. 그러나 그녀가 자신을 바라보고 있는 것을 알아채자, 그는 짜증스럽게, 심지어 언짢은 듯 손짓으로 그녀를 물리치고는 휙 모퉁이를 돌아 사라졌다."]], [["“I am wicked, I see that,”", "'나는 못된 인간이야, 그건 알아.'"], ["he thought to himself, feeling ashamed a moment later of his angry gesture to Dounia.", "그는 잠시 후 두냐에게 짜증스러운 손짓을 한 것이 부끄러워지며 속으로 생각했다."], ["“But why are they so fond of me if I don’t deserve it?", "'하지만 내가 그럴 자격도 없는데 왜들 나를 그토록 아끼는 거지?"], ["Oh, if only I were alone and no one loved me and I too had never loved anyone!", "오, 차라리 내가 혼자였고 아무도 나를 사랑하지 않았고, 나 역시 그 누구도 사랑한 적이 없었더라면!"], ["_Nothing of all this would have happened._ But I wonder shall I in those fifteen or twenty years grow so meek that I shall humble myself before people and whimper at every word that I am a criminal?", "이 모든 일은 일어나지도 않았을 텐데. 그런데 과연 나는 그 십오 년이나 이십 년 동안 그토록 온순해져서, 사람들 앞에 굽실거리고 한마디 할 때마다 나는 죄인입니다 하고 훌쩍거리게 될까?"], ["Yes, that’s it, that’s it, that’s what they are sending me there for, that’s what they want.", "그래, 바로 그거야, 그거야, 사람들이 나를 거기로 보내는 이유가 바로 그거지, 그게 그들이 원하는 거야."], ["Look at them running to and fro about the streets, every one of them a scoundrel and a criminal at heart and, worse still, an idiot.", "저들이 거리를 이리저리 분주히 오가는 꼴을 봐, 하나같이 마음속은 악당이고 죄인이고, 더 나쁘게는 천치들이지."], ["But try to get me off and they’d be wild with righteous indignation.", "그런데 나를 풀어 주려고 해 봐라, 의로운 분노로 펄펄 날뛸걸."], ["Oh, how I hate them all!”", "오, 나는 저들 모두가 얼마나 미운지!'"]], [["He fell to musing by what process it could come to pass, that he could be humbled before all of them, indiscriminately--humbled by conviction.", "그는 어떤 과정을 거쳐, 자신이 저들 모두 앞에, 가리지 않고, 굴복하게 될 수 있는지를 — 확신에 의해 굴복하게 될 수 있는지를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다. 그런데 안 될 게 또 뭐람?"], ["And yet why not? It must be so.", "분명 그렇게 될 것이다."], ["Would not twenty years of continual bondage crush him utterly?", "이십 년간 끊임없이 이어지는 속박이 그를 완전히 으스러뜨리지 않겠는가?"], ["Water wears out a stone.", "물은 돌도 닳게 하는 법."], ["And why, why should he live after that?", "그러고 나서 그는 왜, 왜 살아야 하는가?"], ["Why should he go now when he knew that it would be so?", "그렇게 되리라는 걸 알면서 왜 지금 가야 하는가?"], ["It was the hundredth time perhaps that he had asked himself that question since the previous evening, but still he went.", "어제저녁부터 그가 자신에게 이 질문을 던진 것이 어쩌면 백 번째였지만, 그래도 그는 갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# CHAPTER VIII"]], [["When he went into Sonia’s room, it was already getting dark.", "그가 소냐의 방으로 들어섰을 때는 이미 어둑어둑해지고 있었다."], ["All day Sonia had been waiting for him in terrible anxiety.", "소냐는 하루 종일 끔찍한 불안 속에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["Dounia had been waiting with her.", "두냐도 그녀와 함께 기다렸다."], ["She had come to her that morning, remembering Svidrigaïlov’s words that Sonia knew.", "두냐는 그날 아침, 소냐가 알고 있다던 스비드리가일로프의 말을 떠올리고 그녀를 찾아온 것이었다."], ["We will not describe the conversation and tears of the two girls, and how friendly they became.", "두 처녀의 대화와 눈물을, 그리고 그들이 얼마나 다정해졌는지를 여기서 구구절절 묘사하지는 않겠다."], ["Dounia gained one comfort at least from that interview, that her brother would not be alone.", "두냐는 적어도 이 만남에서 한 가지 위안만큼은 얻었는데, 자기 오빠가 혼자가 아니리라는 것이었다. 오빠는 자신의 고백을 안고 가장 먼저 그녀, 소냐에게로 갔다."], ["He had gone to her, Sonia, first with his confession; he had gone to her for human fellowship when he needed it; she would go with him wherever fate might send him.", "인간의 정이 필요했을 때 그녀에게로 갔던 것이다. 그녀는 운명이 그를 어디로 보내든 그와 함께 갈 터였다."], ["Dounia did not ask, but she knew it was so.", "두냐는 묻지 않았지만, 그것이 그러하다는 걸 알았다."], ["She looked at Sonia almost with reverence and at first almost embarrassed her by it.", "그녀는 거의 경외에 가까운 눈길로 소냐를 바라보았고, 처음엔 그 시선에 소냐가 거의 당황할 지경이었다."], ["Sonia was almost on the point of tears.", "소냐는 금방이라도 눈물을 쏟을 것만 같았다."], ["She felt herself, on the contrary, hardly worthy to look at Dounia.", "오히려 그녀는 자신이 두냐를 마주 볼 자격조차 없다고 느꼈다."], ["Dounia’s gracious image when she had bowed to her so attentively and respectfully at their first meeting in Raskolnikov’s room had remained in her mind as one of the fairest visions of her life.", "라스콜니코프의 방에서 처음 만났을 때 두냐가 그토록 정중하고 공손하게 고개 숙여 인사하던 그 우아한 모습은, 소냐의 마음속에 평생 가장 아름다운 환영 중 하나로 남아 있었다."]], [["Dounia at last became impatient and, leaving Sonia, went to her brother’s room to await him there; she kept thinking that he would come there first.", "두냐는 마침내 더는 참지 못하고 소냐를 남겨 둔 채 오빠를 기다리려 그의 방으로 갔다. 오빠가 그곳으로 먼저 오리라는 생각이 자꾸만 들었던 것이다."], ["When she had gone, Sonia began to be tortured by the dread of his committing suicide, and Dounia too feared it.", "그녀가 떠난 뒤, 소냐는 그가 자살하지나 않을까 하는 공포에 시달리기 시작했고, 두냐 역시 그것을 두려워했다."], ["But they had spent the day trying to persuade each other that that could not be, and both were less anxious while they were together.", "그러나 그들은 그럴 리 없다고 서로를 설득하며 하루를 보냈고, 함께 있는 동안에는 둘 다 덜 불안했다."], ["As soon as they parted, each thought of nothing else.", "하지만 헤어지자마자 각자 다른 것은 아무것도 생각할 수 없었다."], ["Sonia remembered how Svidrigaïlov had said to her the day before that Raskolnikov had two alternatives--Siberia or...", "소냐는 전날 스비드리가일로프가 자기에게 라스콜니코프에게는 두 가지 길밖에 없다고 — 시베리아 아니면…… 하고 말했던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Besides she knew his vanity, his pride and his lack of faith.", "게다가 그녀는 그의 허영심, 그의 자존심, 그리고 그의 신앙 없음을 알고 있었다."]], [["“Is it possible that he has nothing but cowardice and fear of death to make him live?”", "'그가 그저 비겁함과 죽음에 대한 두려움 때문에 살아간다는 게 가능한 일일까?'"], ["she thought at last in despair.", "그녀는 마침내 절망 속에서 생각했다."]], [["Meanwhile the sun was setting.", "그러는 사이 해가 지고 있었다."], ["Sonia was standing in dejection, looking intently out of the window, but from it she could see nothing but the unwhitewashed blank wall of the next house.", "소냐는 의기소침하게 서서 창밖을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었지만, 그 창으로는 옆집의 회칠도 안 된 텅 빈 벽 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["At last when she began to feel sure of his death--he walked into the room.", "마침내 그녀가 그의 죽음을 확신하기 시작했을 때 — 그가 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["She gave a cry of joy, but looking carefully into his face she turned pale.", "그녀는 기쁨의 비명을 질렀으나, 그의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보고는 새파랗게 질렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Raskolnikov, smiling.", "라스콜니코프가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I have come for your cross, Sonia.", "\"당신의 십자가를 받으러 왔어, 소냐."], ["It was you told me to go to the cross-roads; why is it you are frightened now it’s come to that?”", "사거리로 가라고 한 건 당신이었잖아. 그런데 정작 그렇게 됐는데 왜 지금 겁을 먹는 거지?\""]], [["Sonia gazed at him astonished.", "소냐는 놀란 눈으로 그를 응시했다."], ["His tone seemed strange to her; a cold shiver ran over her, but in a moment she guessed that the tone and the words were a mask.", "그의 어조가 그녀에게는 이상하게 들렸다. 싸늘한 전율이 그녀의 온몸을 훑고 지나갔지만, 곧 그녀는 그 어조와 말들이 가면이라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He spoke to her looking away, as though to avoid meeting her eyes.", "그는 그녀의 눈길과 마주치기를 피하려는 듯 딴 곳을 보며 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“You see, Sonia, I’ve decided that it will be better so.", "\"있잖아, 소냐, 나는 이렇게 하는 게 더 낫겠다고 결심했어."], ["There is one fact....", "한 가지 사실이 있는데……"], ["But it’s a long story and there’s no need to discuss it.", "하지만 긴 얘기라 굳이 따질 필요는 없지."], ["But do you know what angers me?", "그런데 내가 뭣 때문에 화가 나는지 알아?"], ["It annoys me that all those stupid brutish faces will be gaping at me directly, pestering me with their stupid questions, which I shall have to answer--they’ll point their fingers at me....", "그 멍청하고 짐승 같은 낯짝들이 곧장 나를 빤히 쳐다보면서, 멍청한 질문들로 나를 들볶고, 나는 거기에 일일이 대답해야 한다는 거야 — 손가락질을 하겠지……."], ["Tfoo!", "퉤!"], ["You know I am not going to Porfiry, I am sick of him.", "알다시피 나는 포르피리한테는 안 갈 거야, 그자한테는 신물이 나니까."], ["I’d rather go to my friend, the Explosive Lieutenant; how I shall surprise him, what a sensation I shall make!", "차라리 내 친구, 그 화약 같은 중위한테 가겠어. 내가 그자를 얼마나 놀라게 할지, 얼마나 큰 소동을 일으킬지!"], ["But I must be cooler; I’ve become too irritable of late.", "하지만 좀 더 냉정해져야 해. 요즘 나는 너무 신경질적이 됐어."], ["You know I was nearly shaking my fist at my sister just now, because she turned to take a last look at me.", "알다시피 방금도 내 누이동생한테 주먹질을 할 뻔했다고, 그애가 나를 마지막으로 한 번 더 보겠다고 돌아섰다는 이유로."], ["It’s a brutal state to be in!", "이건 짐승 같은 상태야!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["what am I coming to!", "내가 도대체 어떻게 되어 가는 거지!"], ["Well, where are the crosses?”", "자, 십자가들은 어디 있지?\""]], [["He seemed hardly to know what he was doing.", "그는 자신이 무얼 하고 있는지조차 잘 모르는 듯했다."], ["He could not stay still or concentrate his attention on anything; his ideas seemed to gallop after one another, he talked incoherently, his hands trembled slightly.", "그는 가만히 있지도, 무언가에 정신을 집중하지도 못했다. 그의 생각들은 서로의 뒤를 쫓아 질주하는 것 같았고, 두서없이 지껄였으며, 손은 가볍게 떨렸다."]], [["Without a word Sonia took out of the drawer two crosses, one of cypress wood and one of copper.", "소냐는 말없이 서랍에서 십자가 두 개를 꺼냈다. 하나는 노송나무로 된 것이고 다른 하나는 구리로 된 것이었다."], ["She made the sign of the cross over herself and over him, and put the wooden cross on his neck.", "그녀는 자기 몸 위에, 그리고 그의 몸 위에 성호를 긋고는, 나무 십자가를 그의 목에 걸어 주었다."]], [["“It’s the symbol of my taking up the cross,”", "\"이건 내가 십자가를 짊어진다는 상징이로군.\""], ["he laughed.", "그가 웃었다."], ["“As though I had not suffered much till now!", "\"마치 지금까지 내가 별로 고통받지 않았다는 듯이!"], ["The wooden cross, that is the peasant one; the copper one, that is Lizaveta’s--you will wear yourself, show me!", "나무 십자가, 그건 농민의 것이고, 구리 십자가는 리자베타의 것이지 — 당신이 직접 걸어, 어디 보여 봐!"], ["So she had it on... at that moment?", "그래, 그여자가 그걸 걸고 있었지……"], ["I remember two things like these too, a silver one and a little ikon.", "그 순간에? 나도 이것들과 비슷한 걸 두 개 기억해, 은으로 된 거랑 작은 성상 하나."], ["I threw them back on the old woman’s neck.", "나는 그것들을 노파의 목에다 도로 던져 버렸어."], ["Those would be appropriate now, really, those are what I ought to put on now....", "그것들이야말로 지금 어울릴 텐데, 정말이지, 그게 바로 지금 내가 걸어야 할 것들인데……."], ["But I am talking nonsense and forgetting what matters; I’m somehow forgetful....", "하지만 내가 헛소리를 하고 있군, 정작 중요한 건 잊어버리고. 어쩐지 나는 자꾸 잊어버려……."], ["You see I have come to warn you, Sonia, so that you might know...", "봐, 나는 당신한테 알려 주려고 왔어, 소냐, 당신이 알 수 있도록……"], ["that’s all--that’s all I came for.", "그게 다야 — 그것 때문에 온 거야."], ["But I thought I had more to say.", "하지만 할 말이 더 있었던 것 같은데."], ["You wanted me to go yourself.", "당신이 직접 나더러 가라고 했잖아."], ["Well, now I am going to prison and you’ll have your wish.", "그래, 이제 나는 감옥으로 가고, 당신은 소원을 이루는 거야."], ["Well, what are you crying for?", "자, 뭣 때문에 우는 거지? 당신마저?"], ["You too?", "그러지 마."], ["Don’t.", "그만둬!"], ["Leave off! Oh, how I hate it all!”", "오, 이 모든 게 얼마나 싫은지!\""]], [["But his feeling was stirred; his heart ached, as he looked at her.", "그러나 그의 감정은 흔들렸다. 그녀를 바라보자 그의 가슴이 아려 왔다."], ["“Why is she grieving too?”", "'왜 이 여자도 슬퍼하는 거지?'"], ["he thought to himself.", "그는 속으로 생각했다."], ["“What am I to her? Why does she weep?", "'내가 이 여자한테 무엇이라고?"], ["Why is she looking after me, like my mother or Dounia?", "왜 우는 거지? 왜 어머니나 두냐처럼 나를 보살피려는 거지?"], ["She’ll be my nurse.”", "이 여자는 내 간병인이 되겠군.'"]], [["“Cross yourself, say at least one prayer,”", "\"성호를 그어요, 적어도 기도 하나만이라도 올려요.\""], ["Sonia begged in a timid broken voice.", "소냐가 겁먹은 듯 떨리는 목소리로 애원했다."]], [["“Oh certainly, as much as you like!", "\"오 물론이지, 당신이 원하는 만큼!"], ["And sincerely, Sonia, sincerely....”", "그리고 진심으로, 소냐, 진심으로…….\""], ["But he wanted to say something quite different.", "그러나 그가 하고 싶었던 말은 전혀 다른 것이었다."]], [["He crossed himself several times.", "그는 여러 번 성호를 그었다."], ["Sonia took up her shawl and put it over her head.", "소냐는 숄을 집어 들어 머리에 둘렀다."], ["It was the green _drap de dames_ shawl of which Marmeladov had spoken, “the family shawl.”", "그것은 마르멜라도프가 말했던 그 초록색 드 담 숄, \"집안 공용 숄\"이었다."], ["Raskolnikov thought of that looking at it, but he did not ask.", "라스콜니코프는 그것을 보며 그 일을 떠올렸지만, 묻지는 않았다."], ["He began to feel himself that he was certainly forgetting things and was disgustingly agitated.", "그는 자기가 정말로 무언가를 자꾸 잊고 있으며, 역겨울 만큼 동요하고 있다는 것을 스스로 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He was frightened at this.", "그는 이에 겁이 났다."], ["He was suddenly struck too by the thought that Sonia meant to go with him.", "게다가 소냐가 자기와 함께 가려 한다는 생각이 불현듯 그를 강타했다."]], [["“What are you doing?", "\"당신 뭐 하는 거야?"], ["Where are you going?", "어디 가려고?"], ["Stay here, stay!", "여기 있어, 여기!"], ["I’ll go alone,”", "나 혼자 갈 거야.\""], ["he cried in cowardly vexation, and almost resentful, he moved towards the door.", "그가 비겁한 짜증을 담아 소리쳤고, 거의 분개한 듯 문 쪽으로 움직였다."], ["“What’s the use of going in procession?”", "\"행렬을 지어 갈 필요가 뭐 있어?\""], ["he muttered going out.", "그는 나가면서 중얼거렸다."]], [["Sonia remained standing in the middle of the room.", "소냐는 방 한가운데 선 채 남겨졌다."], ["He had not even said good-bye to her; he had forgotten her.", "그는 그녀에게 작별 인사조차 하지 않았다. 그녀를 잊어버린 것이다."], ["A poignant and rebellious doubt surged in his heart.", "가슴을 후벼 파는 반항적인 의혹이 그의 마음속에서 솟구쳤다."]], [["“Was it right, was it right, all this?”", "'이게 옳은 일이었나, 옳은 일이었나, 이 모든 게?'"], ["he thought again as he went down the stairs.", "그는 계단을 내려가며 다시 생각했다. '멈춰 서서 이 모든 걸 철회하고……"], ["“Couldn’t he stop and retract it all... and not go?”", "가지 않을 수는 없는 걸까?'"]], [["But still he went.", "그러나 그는 그래도 갔다."], ["He felt suddenly once for all that he mustn’t ask himself questions.", "그는 문득, 단번에, 자신에게 질문 따위를 던져서는 안 된다고 느꼈다."], ["As he turned into the street he remembered that he had not said good-bye to Sonia, that he had left her in the middle of the room in her green shawl, not daring to stir after he had shouted at her, and he stopped short for a moment.", "거리로 접어들면서 그는 소냐에게 작별 인사를 하지 않았다는 것을, 자기가 그녀에게 소리를 지른 뒤 그녀가 감히 꼼짝도 못 한 채 초록색 숄을 두르고 방 한가운데 서 있던 것을 떠올렸고, 잠시 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["At the same instant, another thought dawned upon him, as though it had been lying in wait to strike him then.", "바로 그 순간, 또 다른 생각이, 마치 그때 그를 칠 기회를 노리고 숨어 있었다는 듯, 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다."]], [["“Why, with what object did I go to her just now?", "'아니, 도대체 무슨 목적으로 방금 그 여자한테 갔던 거지?"], ["I told her--on business; on what business?", "나는 그여자한테 — 볼일이 있다고 말했어. 무슨 볼일?"], ["I had no sort of business!", "나한테는 그 어떤 볼일도 없었어!"], ["To tell her I was _going_; but where was the need?", "내가 간다고 말하려고? 하지만 그럴 필요가 어디 있었나?"], ["Do I love her?", "내가 그 여자를 사랑하나?"], ["No, no, I drove her away just now like a dog.", "아니, 아니야, 방금도 개처럼 그 여자를 쫓아냈잖아."], ["Did I want her crosses?", "내가 그 여자의 십자가를 원했던가?"], ["Oh, how low I’ve sunk!", "오, 내가 얼마나 비천하게 떨어졌는지!"], ["No, I wanted her tears, I wanted to see her terror, to see how her heart ached!", "아니야, 나는 그 여자의 눈물을 원했어, 그 여자의 공포를 보고 싶었어, 그 여자의 가슴이 어떻게 미어지는지 보고 싶었던 거야!"], ["I had to have something to cling to, something to delay me, some friendly face to see!", "나는 매달릴 무언가가, 나를 붙들어 둘 무언가가, 바라볼 다정한 얼굴 하나가 필요했던 거야!"], ["And I dared to believe in myself, to dream of what I would do!", "그러고도 나는 감히 나 자신을 믿고, 내가 무슨 일을 할지를 꿈꾸었다니!"], ["I am a beggarly contemptible wretch, contemptible!”", "나는 거지같이 비열한 놈이야, 비열한 놈!'"]], [["He walked along the canal bank, and he had not much further to go.", "그는 운하 둑을 따라 걸었고, 이제 갈 길은 그리 멀지 않았다."], ["But on reaching the bridge he stopped and turning out of his way along it went to the Hay Market.", "그러나 다리에 다다르자 그는 멈춰 서더니, 가던 길에서 벗어나 다리를 따라 건초 시장 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["He looked eagerly to right and left, gazed intently at every object and could not fix his attention on anything; everything slipped away.", "그는 오른쪽 왼쪽을 간절히 둘러보며 모든 사물을 뚫어지게 응시했지만 무엇에도 정신을 붙들어 둘 수 없었다. 모든 것이 미끄러져 빠져나갔다."], ["“In another week, another month I shall be driven in a prison van over this bridge, how shall I look at the canal then?", "'일주일 뒤, 한 달 뒤면 나는 죄수 호송차에 실려 이 다리를 건너가겠지, 그때 나는 이 운하를 어떤 눈으로 바라볼까?"], ["I should like to remember this!”", "이걸 기억해 두고 싶군!'"], ["slipped into his mind.", "하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["“Look at this sign!", "'이 간판을 봐!"], ["How shall I read those letters then?", "그때 나는 저 글자들을 어떻게 읽을까?"], ["It’s written here ‘Campany,’ that’s a thing to remember, that letter _a_, and to look at it again in a month--how shall I look at it then?", "여기 「캄파니」라고 쓰여 있군, 기억해 둘 만한 거야, 저 a라는 글자를, 그리고 한 달 뒤에 다시 보는 거지 — 그때 나는 저걸 어떻게 바라볼까?"], ["What shall I be feeling and thinking then?...", "그때 나는 무엇을 느끼고 무엇을 생각하고 있을까?……"], ["How trivial it all must be, what I am fretting about now!", "지금 내가 안달복달하고 있는 이 모든 게 얼마나 하찮은 것일까!"], ["Of course it must all be interesting...", "물론 이 모든 게 흥미롭긴 하겠지……"], ["in its way...", "나름대로……"], ["(Ha-ha-ha!", "(하-하-하!"], ["What am I thinking about?) I am becoming a baby, I am showing off to myself; why am I ashamed?", "내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거지?) 나는 어린애가 되어 가는군, 나 자신한테 으스대고 있어. 내가 왜 부끄러워하는 거지?"], ["Foo!", "퉤!"], ["how people shove!", "사람들이 어찌 이리 밀쳐 대는지!"], ["that fat man--a German he must be--who pushed against me, does he know whom he pushed?", "저 뚱뚱한 사내 — 분명 독일 놈일 텐데 — 나한테 부딪힌 저자는, 자기가 누구한테 부딪혔는지 알기나 할까?"], ["There’s a peasant woman with a baby, begging.", "저기 아기를 안고 구걸하는 농가 아낙이 있군."], ["It’s curious that she thinks me happier than she is.", "저 여자가 나를 자기보다 더 행복하다고 여긴다는 게 묘하구나."], ["I might give her something, for the incongruity of it.", "그 부조리함 때문에라도 저 여자한테 뭔가 줄 수도 있겠지."], ["Here’s a five copeck piece left in my pocket, where did I get it?", "여기 주머니에 오 코페이카짜리 동전 하나가 남아 있군, 이걸 어디서 났더라?"], ["Here, here...", "여기, 여기……"], ["take it, my good woman!”", "받아요, 아주머니!'"]], [["“God bless you,”", "\"하느님의 축복이 있으시길.\""], ["the beggar chanted in a lachrymose voice.", "거지 여인이 눈물 어린 목소리로 읊조렸다."]], [["He went into the Hay Market.", "그는 건초 시장으로 들어갔다."], ["It was distasteful, very distasteful to be in a crowd, but he walked just where he saw most people.", "군중 속에 있는 것은 불쾌하기 짝이 없는, 정말이지 불쾌하기 짝이 없는 일이었지만, 그는 사람이 가장 많이 보이는 바로 그곳으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["He would have given anything in the world to be alone; but he knew himself that he would not have remained alone for a moment.", "혼자 있을 수만 있다면 세상의 무엇이라도 내주었으련만, 그는 자신이 단 한순간도 혼자 있지 못하리라는 것을 스스로 알고 있었다."], ["There was a man drunk and disorderly in the crowd; he kept trying to dance and falling down.", "군중 속에 술에 취해 행패를 부리는 사내가 하나 있었는데, 그는 자꾸만 춤을 추려다가 넘어지곤 했다."], ["There was a ring round him.", "그 주위로 사람들이 둥글게 모여 있었다."], ["Raskolnikov squeezed his way through the crowd, stared for some minutes at the drunken man and suddenly gave a short jerky laugh.", "라스콜니코프는 군중을 비집고 들어가 몇 분 동안 그 취한 사내를 빤히 바라보다가, 갑자기 짧고 발작적인 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["A minute later he had forgotten him and did not see him, though he still stared.", "잠시 후 그는 그 사내를 잊어버렸고, 여전히 빤히 보면서도 그를 보고 있지 않았다."], ["He moved away at last, not remembering where he was; but when he got into the middle of the square an emotion suddenly came over him, overwhelming him body and mind.", "마침내 그는 자기가 어디 있는지도 기억하지 못한 채 자리를 떴다. 그러나 광장 한복판에 이르렀을 때, 한 감정이 불현듯 그를 덮쳐 와 몸과 마음을 송두리째 휩쓸어 버렸다."]], [["He suddenly recalled Sonia’s words, “Go to the cross-roads, bow down to the people, kiss the earth, for you have sinned against it too, and say aloud to the whole world, ‘I am a murderer.’”", "그는 갑자기 소냐의 말을 떠올렸다. \"사거리로 가서 사람들에게 절을 하고, 대지에 입을 맞춰요, 당신은 대지에도 죄를 지었으니까요, 그리고 온 세상을 향해 큰 소리로 외쳐요, '나는 살인자다.'라고.\""], ["He trembled, remembering that.", "그것을 떠올리며 그는 부르르 떨었다."], ["And the hopeless misery and anxiety of all that time, especially of the last hours, had weighed so heavily upon him that he positively clutched at the chance of this new unmixed, complete sensation.", "그리고 그 모든 시간의, 특히 지난 몇 시간의 절망적인 비참함과 불안이 그를 너무도 무겁게 짓눌렀던 터라, 그는 이 새롭고 순수하고 온전한 감정의 기회를 그야말로 덥석 움켜쥐었다. 그것은 발작처럼 그를 덮쳐 왔다."], ["It came over him like a fit; it was like a single spark kindled in his soul and spreading fire through him.", "그것은 그의 영혼 속에 점화된 단 하나의 불꽃이 그의 온몸으로 불길을 번져 가는 것과도 같았다."], ["Everything in him softened at once and the tears started into his eyes.", "그의 안에 있던 모든 것이 단번에 누그러지면서 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."], ["He fell to the earth on the spot....", "그는 그 자리에서 대지에 엎드렸다……."]], [["He knelt down in the middle of the square, bowed down to the earth, and kissed that filthy earth with bliss and rapture.", "그는 광장 한복판에 무릎을 꿇고 대지에 절을 하고는, 그 더러운 대지에 황홀과 환희에 차서 입을 맞추었다."], ["He got up and bowed down a second time.", "그는 일어서서 두 번째로 절을 했다."]], [["“He’s boozed,”", "\"술에 절었구먼.\""], ["a youth near him observed.", "그의 곁에 있던 젊은이 하나가 한마디 했다."]], [["There was a roar of laughter.", "웃음소리가 와르르 터졌다."]], [["“He’s going to Jerusalem, brothers, and saying good-bye to his children and his country.", "\"저 사람 예루살렘으로 가는 거야, 형제들, 자기 자식들이랑 고국에 작별을 고하는 거라고."], ["He’s bowing down to all the world and kissing the great city of St. Petersburg and its pavement,”", "온 세상에 절을 하고 위대한 도시 페테르부르크와 그 보도에 입을 맞추는 거지.\""], ["added a workman who was a little drunk.", "약간 취한 노동자 하나가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Quite a young man, too!”", "\"게다가 아주 젊은 사람이군!\""], ["observed a third.", "세 번째 사람이 한마디 했다."]], [["“And a gentleman,”", "\"그리고 신사야.\""], ["someone observed soberly.", "누군가가 진지하게 한마디 보탰다."]], [["“There’s no knowing who’s a gentleman and who isn’t nowadays.”", "\"요즘 세상엔 누가 신사고 누가 아닌지 알 수가 없지.\""]], [["These exclamations and remarks checked Raskolnikov, and the words, “I am a murderer,” which were perhaps on the point of dropping from his lips, died away.", "이런 외침들과 말들이 라스콜니코프를 멈칫하게 했고, 어쩌면 막 그의 입술에서 떨어지려던 \"나는 살인자다.\"라는 말은 사그라들고 말았다."], ["He bore these remarks quietly, however, and, without looking round, he turned down a street leading to the police office.", "그러나 그는 이 말들을 조용히 견뎌 내고는, 주위를 둘러보지도 않은 채, 경찰서로 통하는 골목으로 접어들었다."], ["He had a glimpse of something on the way which did not surprise him; he had felt that it must be so.", "도중에 그는 어떤 광경을 언뜻 보았는데, 그것은 그를 놀라게 하지 않았다. 그는 분명 그러리라고 느끼고 있었던 것이다."], ["The second time he bowed down in the Hay Market he saw, standing fifty paces from him on the left, Sonia.", "건초 시장에서 두 번째로 절을 할 때, 그는 자기에게서 쉰 걸음쯤 떨어진 왼쪽에 소냐가 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She was hiding from him behind one of the wooden shanties in the market-place.", "그녀는 시장 광장의 목조 가건물 뒤에 숨어 그에게서 몸을 가리고 있었다."], ["She had followed him then on his painful way!", "그러니까 그녀는 그의 고통스러운 길을 줄곧 뒤따라온 것이었다!"], ["Raskolnikov at that moment felt and knew once for all that Sonia was with him for ever and would follow him to the ends of the earth, wherever fate might take him.", "그 순간 라스콜니코프는, 소냐가 영원히 자기와 함께 있으리라는 것을, 운명이 그를 어디로 데려가든 땅끝까지 그를 따라오리라는 것을, 단번에 느끼고 또 알았다."], ["It wrung his heart...", "그것이 그의 가슴을 쥐어뜯었지만……"], ["but he was just reaching the fatal place.", "그는 이미 그 숙명의 장소에 다다르고 있었다."]], [["He went into the yard fairly resolutely.", "그는 제법 단호하게 마당 안으로 들어섰다."], ["He had to mount to the third storey.", "그는 사 층까지 올라가야 했다."], ["“I shall be some time going up,”", "'한참 걸리겠군.' 그는 생각했다."], ["he thought. He felt as though the fateful moment was still far off, as though he had plenty of time left for consideration.", "그는 마치 그 숙명의 순간이 아직 멀리 있는 듯, 마치 숙고할 시간이 아직 많이 남아 있는 듯 느꼈다."]], [["Again the same rubbish, the same eggshells lying about on the spiral stairs, again the open doors of the flats, again the same kitchens and the same fumes and stench coming from them.", "또다시 그 똑같은 쓰레기더미, 나선형 계단에 여기저기 흩어진 그 똑같은 달걀 껍데기들, 또다시 열려 있는 셋집들의 문, 또다시 그 똑같은 부엌들과 거기서 풍겨 나오는 그 똑같은 김과 악취."], ["Raskolnikov had not been here since that day.", "라스콜니코프는 그날 이후로 이곳에 와 본 적이 없었다."], ["His legs were numb and gave way under him, but still they moved forward.", "그의 다리는 감각이 없고 풀려 버렸지만, 그래도 발은 앞으로 움직였다."], ["He stopped for a moment to take breath, to collect himself, so as to enter _like a man_.", "그는 숨을 고르고, 정신을 가다듬어 사내답게 들어가기 위해 잠시 멈춰 섰다. '하지만 무엇 때문에?"], ["“But why? what for?”", "무엇을 위해서?'"], ["he wondered, reflecting.", "그는 곰곰이 생각하며 의아해했다."], ["“If I must drink the cup what difference does it make?", "'어차피 이 잔을 마셔야 한다면 그게 무슨 차이가 있겠나?"], ["The more revolting the better.”", "역겨우면 역겨울수록 더 나은 거지.'"], ["He imagined for an instant the figure of the “explosive lieutenant,” Ilya Petrovitch.", "그는 한순간 그 \"화약 같은 중위\", 일리야 페트로비치의 모습을 떠올렸다."], ["Was he actually going to him?", "정말 그자에게 가려는 걸까?"], ["Couldn’t he go to someone else?", "다른 누군가에게 갈 수는 없을까?"], ["To Nikodim Fomitch?", "니코딤 포미치에게?"], ["Couldn’t he turn back and go straight to Nikodim Fomitch’s lodgings?", "발길을 돌려 곧장 니코딤 포미치의 거처로 갈 수는 없을까?"], ["At least then it would be done privately.... No, no!", "적어도 그러면 은밀하게 처리될 텐데……."], ["To the “explosive lieutenant”!", "아니, 아니야! \"화약 같은 중위\"에게로!"], ["If he must drink it, drink it off at once.", "마셔야 한다면, 단숨에 들이켜는 거다."]], [["Turning cold and hardly conscious, he opened the door of the office.", "싸늘하게 식어 거의 정신이 나간 채로, 그는 사무실 문을 열었다."], ["There were very few people in it this time--only a house porter and a peasant.", "이번에는 안에 사람이 거의 없었다 — 문지기 하나와 농부 하나뿐이었다."], ["The doorkeeper did not even peep out from behind his screen.", "수위는 칸막이 뒤에서 고개조차 내밀지 않았다."], ["Raskolnikov walked into the next room.", "라스콜니코프는 옆방으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["“Perhaps I still need not speak,”", "'어쩌면 아직은 말하지 않아도 될지 몰라.'"], ["passed through his mind.", "하는 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["Some sort of clerk not wearing a uniform was settling himself at a bureau to write.", "제복을 입지 않은 어떤 서기 하나가 책상 앞에 자리를 잡고 무언가를 쓰려 하고 있었다."], ["In a corner another clerk was seating himself.", "한쪽 구석에서는 또 다른 서기가 막 자리에 앉고 있었다."], ["Zametov was not there, nor, of course, Nikodim Fomitch.", "자메토프는 거기 없었고, 물론 니코딤 포미치도 없었다."]], [["“No one in?”", "\"아무도 안 계십니까?\""], ["Raskolnikov asked, addressing the person at the bureau.", "라스콜니코프가 책상 앞의 사람에게 물었다."]], [["“Whom do you want?”", "\"누굴 찾으시오?\""]], [["“A-ah!", "\"아-아!"], ["Not a sound was heard, not a sight was seen, but I scent the Russian...", "소리도 들리지 않고, 모습도 보이지 않건만, 러시아 사람의 냄새가 풍기는구나……"], ["how does it go on in the fairy tale...", "그 옛이야기에서 다음이 어떻게 되더라……"], ["I’ve forgotten!", "잊어버렸네!"], ["‘At your service!’”", "'대령했습니다!'\""], ["a familiar voice cried suddenly.", "익숙한 목소리가 갑자기 외쳤다."]], [["Raskolnikov shuddered.", "라스콜니코프는 흠칫했다."], ["The Explosive Lieutenant stood before him.", "그 화약 같은 중위가 그의 앞에 서 있었다."], ["He had just come in from the third room.", "그는 방금 세 번째 방에서 나온 참이었다."], ["“It is the hand of fate,”", "'이건 운명의 손길이야.'"], ["thought Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프는 생각했다."], ["“Why is he here?”", "'왜 그가 여기 있는 거지?'"]], [["“You’ve come to see us?", "\"우리를 보러 오셨소?"], ["What about?”", "무슨 일로?\""], ["cried Ilya Petrovitch.", "일리야 페트로비치가 외쳤다."], ["He was obviously in an exceedingly good humour and perhaps a trifle exhilarated.", "그는 분명 기분이 몹시 좋았고, 어쩌면 약간 들떠 있는 듯도 했다."], ["“If it’s on business you are rather early.[*] It’s only a chance that I am here...", "\"볼일이 있어서 오신 거라면 좀 이르오.[*] 내가 여기 있는 것도 그저 우연이긴 한데……"], ["however I’ll do what I can.", "어쨌든 내가 할 수 있는 건 해 드리리다."], ["I must admit, I...", "솔직히 나는……"], ["what is it, what is it?", "무슨 일이오, 무슨 일이오?"], ["Excuse me....”", "실례지만…….\""]], [["[*] Dostoevsky appears to have forgotten that it is after sunset, and that the last time Raskolnikov visited the police office at two in the afternoon he was reproached for coming too late.--TRANSLATOR.", "[*] 도스토옙스키는 지금이 해가 진 뒤이며, 라스콜니코프가 지난번 오후 두 시에 경찰서를 찾았을 때 너무 늦게 왔다고 핀잔을 들었던 것을 잊은 듯하다. — 옮긴이."]], [["“Raskolnikov.”", "\"라스콜니코프입니다.\""]], [["“Of course, Raskolnikov.", "\"물론, 라스콜니코프지."], ["You didn’t imagine I’d forgotten?", "내가 잊어버렸으리라 생각했소?"], ["Don’t think I am like that...", "내가 그런 사람이라고 여기진 마시오……"], ["Rodion Ro--Ro--Rodionovitch, that’s it, isn’t it?”", "로디온 로 — 로 — 로디오노비치, 맞지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["“Rodion Romanovitch.”", "\"로디온 로마노비치입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, of course, Rodion Romanovitch!", "\"그래, 그래, 물론이지, 로디온 로마노비치!"], ["I was just getting at it.", "막 떠올리던 참이었소."], ["I made many inquiries about you.", "당신에 대해 여러 가지를 알아봤다오."], ["I assure you I’ve been genuinely grieved since that...", "단언컨대 나는 그 일 이후로…… 그렇게 처신한 이후로……"], ["since I behaved like that...", "진심으로 마음이 아팠소……"], ["it was explained to me afterwards that you were a literary man...", "나중에 듣자 하니 당신이 문필가였다더군……"], ["and a learned one too...", "게다가 학식 있는 사람이고……"], ["and so to say the first steps...", "말하자면 첫발을 내딛는……"], ["Mercy on us!", "맙소사!"], ["What literary or scientific man does not begin by some originality of conduct!", "어떤 문필가나 학자가 행동거지에 좀 별난 데 없이 시작하겠소!"], ["My wife and I have the greatest respect for literature, in my wife it’s a genuine passion!", "나도 내 아내도 문학에 더없는 경의를 품고 있소, 내 아내로 말하자면 그건 진정한 열정이라오!"], ["Literature and art!", "문학과 예술!"], ["If only a man is a gentleman, all the rest can be gained by talents, learning, good sense, genius.", "사람이 신사이기만 하다면, 그 나머지는 재능이며 학식이며 양식이며 천재성으로 다 얻을 수 있는 거요."], ["As for a hat--well, what does a hat matter?", "모자로 말할 것 같으면 — 뭐, 모자가 무슨 대수겠소?"], ["I can buy a hat as easily as I can a bun; but what’s under the hat, what the hat covers, I can’t buy that!", "나는 모자쯤이야 빵 한 덩이 사듯 쉽게 살 수 있소. 하지만 모자 아래에 있는 것, 모자가 가리고 있는 것, 그건 살 수 없는 거요!"], ["I was even meaning to come and apologise to you, but thought maybe you’d...", "나는 심지어 당신을 찾아가 사과할까도 생각했지만, 어쩌면 당신이……"], ["But I am forgetting to ask you, is there anything you want really?", "아 참, 묻는 걸 깜빡했는데, 정말로 무슨 원하는 게 있으시오?"], ["I hear your family have come?”", "듣자 하니 가족이 오셨다던데?\""]], [["“Yes, my mother and sister.”", "\"네, 어머니와 누이동생이요.\""]], [["“I’ve even had the honour and happiness of meeting your sister--a highly cultivated and charming person.", "\"실은 나도 당신 누이동생을 뵙는 영광과 기쁨을 누렸소 — 대단히 교양 있고 매력적인 분이더군."], ["I confess I was sorry I got so hot with you.", "솔직히 당신한테 그렇게 발끈했던 게 후회스러웠소."], ["There it is!", "바로 그렇소!"], ["But as for my looking suspiciously at your fainting fit--that affair has been cleared up splendidly!", "하지만 당신의 그 졸도 발작을 의심스럽게 본 것으로 말할 것 같으면 — 그 일은 멋지게 해명됐소!"], ["Bigotry and fanaticism!", "광신과 맹신이었지!"], ["I understand your indignation.", "당신의 분노도 이해하오."], ["Perhaps you are changing your lodging on account of your family’s arriving?”", "가족이 오셔서 거처를 옮기시려는 거요?\""]], [["“No, I only looked in...", "\"아닙니다, 그냥 잠깐 들른 겁니다……"], ["I came to ask...", "물어볼 게 있어서 왔어요……"], ["I thought that I should find Zametov here.”", "여기서 자메토프를 만날 수 있을 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes!", "\"아, 그렇지!"], ["Of course, you’ve made friends, I heard.", "물론, 두 분이 친구가 됐다고 들었소."], ["Well, no, Zametov is not here. Yes, we’ve lost Zametov.", "그런데 안됐지만, 자메토프는 여기 없소."], ["He’s not been here since yesterday...", "그래요, 우리는 자메토프를 잃었다오."], ["he quarrelled with everyone on leaving...", "그자는 어제부터 여기 없소…… 떠나면서 모두와 다투었는데……"], ["in the rudest way.", "아주 무례하게 굴었지."], ["He is a feather-headed youngster, that’s all; one might have expected something from him, but there, you know what they are, our brilliant young men.", "그저 머리가 텅 빈 풋내기일 뿐이오. 그자한테 뭔가 기대해 볼 만도 했지만, 뭐, 당신도 그런 자들을 알지 않소, 우리네 그 잘난 젊은이들 말이오."], ["He wanted to go in for some examination, but it’s only to talk and boast about it, it will go no further than that.", "무슨 시험인가를 보겠다고 했지만, 그저 떠벌리고 자랑이나 할 뿐, 그 이상은 안 갈 거요."], ["Of course it’s a very different matter with you or Mr. Razumihin there, your friend.", "물론 당신이나 저기 당신 친구 라주미힌 씨라면 전혀 다른 얘기지."], ["Your career is an intellectual one and you won’t be deterred by failure.", "당신들의 길은 지적인 길이고, 실패에 좌절하지도 않을 테니."], ["For you, one may say, all the attractions of life _nihil est_--you are an ascetic, a monk, a hermit!...", "당신으로 말하자면, 인생의 온갖 즐거움이 니힐 에스트라고 할 만하지 — 당신은 금욕가요, 수도승이요, 은둔자란 말이오!……"], ["A book, a pen behind your ear, a learned research--that’s where your spirit soars!", "책 한 권, 귀에 꽂은 펜 한 자루, 학문적 탐구 — 거기에 당신의 정신이 날아오르는 거요!"], ["I am the same way myself....", "나도 똑같은 사람이라오……."], ["Have you read Livingstone’s Travels?”", "리빙스턴의 여행기를 읽어 보셨소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“Oh, I have.", "\"오, 나는 읽었소."], ["There are a great many Nihilists about nowadays, you know, and indeed it is not to be wondered at.", "요즘 세상엔 니힐리스트가 아주 많지 않소, 그런데 사실 놀랄 일도 아니지. 대체 어떤 시대인지, 내 당신께 묻겠소."], ["What sort of days are they?", "그런데 우리는 생각했소만……"], ["I ask you. But we thought...", "당신은 물론 니힐리스트는 아니겠지?"], ["you are not a Nihilist of course?", "솔직히, 솔직히 대답해 보시오!\""], ["Answer me openly, openly!”", "\"네…… 아니요…….\""]], [["“N-no...” “Believe me, you can speak openly to me as you would to yourself!", "\"내 말을 믿으시오, 당신은 나한테 자기 자신한테 하듯 솔직히 말해도 되오!"], ["Official duty is one thing but...", "공무란 별개의 것이지만……"], ["you are thinking I meant to say _friendship_ is quite another?", "당신은 내가 우정을 말하려 한다고 생각하지요?"], ["No, you’re wrong!", "아니, 틀렸소!"], ["It’s not friendship, but the feeling of a man and a citizen, the feeling of humanity and of love for the Almighty.", "우정이 아니라, 한 인간으로서, 한 시민으로서의 감정, 인류애와 전능하신 분에 대한 사랑의 감정이오."], ["I may be an official, but I am always bound to feel myself a man and a citizen....", "내가 관리이긴 하나, 나는 언제나 나 자신을 한 인간이자 한 시민으로 느낄 의무가 있소……."], ["You were asking about Zametov.", "당신은 자메토프에 대해 물었지."], ["Zametov will make a scandal in the French style in a house of bad reputation, over a glass of champagne...", "자메토프 그자야 평판 나쁜 집에서 샴페인 한 잔 걸치고 프랑스식 추문이나 일으킬 자라오……"], ["that’s all your Zametov is good for!", "당신네 그 자메토프가 쓸모 있는 거라곤 그게 다요!"], ["While I’m perhaps, so to speak, burning with devotion and lofty feelings, and besides I have rank, consequence, a post!", "그에 비해 나는, 말하자면 헌신과 고결한 감정으로 불타고 있고, 게다가 나한테는 지위와 위신과 직책이 있단 말이오!"], ["I am married and have children, I fulfil the duties of a man and a citizen, but who is he, may I ask?", "나는 처자식이 있고, 한 인간으로서, 한 시민으로서의 의무를 다하고 있소, 한데 그자는 뭐란 말이오, 내 묻겠소?"], ["I appeal to you as a man ennobled by education...", "나는 교양으로 고결해진 한 인간으로서 당신께 호소하는 거요……"], ["Then these midwives, too, have become extraordinarily numerous.”", "그리고 또 그 산파라는 것들도 엄청나게 늘어났단 말이오.\""]], [["Raskolnikov raised his eyebrows inquiringly.", "라스콜니코프는 묻는 듯 눈썹을 치켜올렸다."], ["The words of Ilya Petrovitch, who had obviously been dining, were for the most part a stream of empty sounds for him.", "분명 식사를 거나하게 한 일리야 페트로비치의 말은 그에게 대부분 공허한 소리의 흐름일 뿐이었다."], ["But some of them he understood.", "그래도 그중 일부는 알아들었다."], ["He looked at him inquiringly, not knowing how it would end.", "그는 이것이 어떻게 끝날지 알지 못한 채 묻는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I mean those crop-headed wenches,”", "\"내 말은 그 머리를 짧게 친 계집애들 얘기요.\""], ["the talkative Ilya Petrovitch continued.", "수다스러운 일리야 페트로비치가 말을 이었다."], ["“Midwives is my name for them.", "\"산파라는 게 내가 그들한테 붙인 이름이오."], ["I think it a very satisfactory one, ha-ha!", "아주 흡족한 이름이라고 생각하오, 하-하!"], ["They go to the Academy, study anatomy.", "그것들이 학교에 다니면서 해부학을 공부한단 말이오."], ["If I fall ill, am I to send for a young lady to treat me?", "내가 병이라도 나면, 새파란 처녀를 불러다 치료를 받아야 하오?"], ["What do you say?", "어떻게 생각하시오?"], ["Ha-ha!”", "하-하!\""], ["Ilya Petrovitch laughed, quite pleased with his own wit.", "일리야 페트로비치는 자기 재담에 흡족해하며 웃었다."], ["“It’s an immoderate zeal for education, but once you’re educated, that’s enough.", "\"교육에 대한 도를 넘은 열의지, 하지만 교육을 받았으면 그것으로 족한 거요. 왜 그걸 남용하느냐 말이오?"], ["Why abuse it? Why insult honourable people, as that scoundrel Zametov does?", "왜 그 악당 자메토프처럼 점잖은 사람들을 모욕하느냐 말이오?"], ["Why did he insult me, I ask you?", "그자가 왜 나를 모욕했소, 내 묻겠소?"], ["Look at these suicides, too, how common they are, you can’t fancy!", "또 이 자살이라는 것들 좀 보시오, 얼마나 흔한지, 당신은 상상도 못 할 거요!"], ["People spend their last halfpenny and kill themselves, boys and girls and old people.", "사람들이 마지막 한 푼까지 다 써 버리고는 스스로 목숨을 끊는단 말이오, 사내애도 계집애도 노인네도."], ["Only this morning we heard about a gentleman who had just come to town.", "바로 오늘 아침에도 막 시내에 온 어떤 신사 얘기를 들었소."], ["Nil Pavlitch, I say, what was the name of that gentleman who shot himself?”", "닐 파블리치, 내가 묻겠는데, 스스로 총을 쏜 그 신사 이름이 뭐였더라?\""]], [["“Svidrigaïlov,”", "\"스비드리가일로프요.\""], ["someone answered from the other room with drowsy listlessness.", "누군가 옆방에서 졸린 듯 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["Raskolnikov started.", "라스콜니코프는 움찔했다."]], [["“Svidrigaïlov!", "\"스비드리가일로프!"], ["Svidrigaïlov has shot himself!”", "스비드리가일로프가 총으로 자살을 했다고요!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."]], [["“What, do you know Svidrigaïlov?”", "\"아니, 스비드리가일로프를 아시오?\""]], [["“Yes...", "\"네……"], ["I knew him....", "알았습니다……."], ["He hadn’t been here long.”", "그가 이곳에 온 지 얼마 안 됐지요.\""]], [["“Yes, that’s so.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소."], ["He had lost his wife, was a man of reckless habits and all of a sudden shot himself, and in such a shocking way....", "그자는 아내를 잃었고, 무절제한 생활을 하던 자였는데, 별안간 스스로 총을 쏘았다오, 그것도 아주 충격적인 방식으로……."], ["He left in his notebook a few words: that he dies in full possession of his faculties and that no one is to blame for his death.", "수첩에 몇 마디 남겼더군, 자기는 온전한 정신으로 죽는 것이며 누구도 자기 죽음에 책임이 없다고."], ["He had money, they say.", "돈도 있었다던데."], ["How did you come to know him?”", "어떻게 그자를 알게 됐소?\""]], [["“I...", "\"저는……"], ["was acquainted...", "아는 사이였습니다……"], ["my sister was governess in his family.”", "제 누이동생이 그의 집에서 가정교사를 했지요.\""]], [["“Bah-bah-bah!", "\"바-바-바!"], ["Then no doubt you can tell us something about him.", "그렇다면 분명 그자에 대해 우리한테 뭔가 얘기해 줄 수 있겠군."], ["You had no suspicion?”", "의심스러운 점은 없었소?\""]], [["“I saw him yesterday...", "\"어제 그를 봤습니다……"], ["he...", "그는……"], ["was drinking wine; I knew nothing.”", "술을 마시고 있었어요. 저는 아무것도 몰랐습니다.\""], ["Raskolnikov felt as though something had fallen on him and was stifling him.", "라스콜니코프는 무언가가 자기 위로 떨어져 숨통을 조여 오는 것만 같았다."]], [["“You’ve turned pale again.", "\"또 안색이 창백해지셨군."], ["It’s so stuffy here...”", "여기가 워낙 숨이 막혀서…….\""]], [["“Yes, I must go,”", "\"네, 가 봐야겠습니다.\""], ["muttered Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Excuse my troubling you....”", "\"폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다…….\""]], [["“Oh, not at all, as often as you like.", "\"오, 천만에요, 언제든 오시오."], ["It’s a pleasure to see you and I am glad to say so.”", "당신을 보니 반갑소, 정말 그렇게 말할 수 있어 기쁘오.\""]], [["Ilya Petrovitch held out his hand.", "일리야 페트로비치가 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“I only wanted...", "\"그저……"], ["I came to see Zametov.”", "자메토프를 보러 온 거였습니다.\""]], [["“I understand, I understand, and it’s a pleasure to see you.”", "\"알겠소, 알겠소, 그래도 당신을 보니 반갑소.\""]], [["“I...", "\"저는……"], ["am very glad...", "정말 기쁩니다……"], ["good-bye,”", "안녕히 계십시오.\""], ["Raskolnikov smiled.", "라스콜니코프가 미소 지었다."]], [["He went out; he reeled, he was overtaken with giddiness and did not know what he was doing.", "그는 밖으로 나왔다. 그는 비틀거렸고, 현기증에 사로잡혀 자기가 무얼 하는지도 몰랐다."], ["He began going down the stairs, supporting himself with his right hand against the wall.", "그는 오른손으로 벽을 짚으며 계단을 내려가기 시작했다."], ["He fancied that a porter pushed past him on his way upstairs to the police office, that a dog in the lower storey kept up a shrill barking and that a woman flung a rolling-pin at it and shouted.", "어떤 문지기가 경찰서로 올라가는 길에 그를 밀치고 지나간 것 같았고, 아래층에서는 개 한 마리가 날카롭게 짖어 대고, 어떤 여자가 그 개한테 밀방망이를 집어 던지며 소리를 지른 것만 같았다."], ["He went down and out into the yard.", "그는 계단을 다 내려가 마당으로 나왔다."], ["There, not far from the entrance, stood Sonia, pale and horror-stricken.", "거기, 출입구에서 멀지 않은 곳에 소냐가 서 있었다, 새파랗게 질려 공포에 사로잡힌 채로."], ["She looked wildly at him.", "그녀는 넋이 나간 듯 그를 바라보았다."], ["He stood still before her.", "그는 그녀 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["There was a look of poignant agony, of despair, in her face.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 가슴을 후벼 파는 고뇌와 절망의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["She clasped her hands.", "그녀는 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["His lips worked in an ugly, meaningless smile.", "그의 입술이 추하고 의미 없는 미소로 일그러졌다."], ["He stood still a minute, grinned and went back to the police office.", "그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 이를 드러내고 씩 웃더니, 다시 경찰서로 돌아갔다."]], [["Ilya Petrovitch had sat down and was rummaging among some papers.", "일리야 페트로비치는 자리에 앉아 어떤 서류들을 뒤적이고 있었다."], ["Before him stood the same peasant who had pushed by on the stairs.", "그의 앞에는 아까 계단에서 그를 밀치고 지나갔던 바로 그 농부가 서 있었다."]], [["“Hulloa!", "\"이런!"], ["Back again!", "또 오셨군!"], ["have you left something behind?", "뭘 두고 가셨소?"], ["What’s the matter?”", "무슨 일이오?\""]], [["Raskolnikov, with white lips and staring eyes, came slowly nearer.", "라스콜니코프는 입술이 새하얗게 질리고 눈을 부릅뜬 채 천천히 다가왔다."], ["He walked right to the table, leaned his hand on it, tried to say something, but could not; only incoherent sounds were audible.", "그는 곧장 책상으로 걸어가 손을 짚고 무언가 말하려 했으나 할 수 없었다. 그저 두서없는 소리만 들릴 뿐이었다."]], [["“You are feeling ill, a chair!", "\"몸이 안 좋으시군, 의자를!"], ["Here, sit down!", "여기, 앉으시오!"], ["Some water!”", "물 좀 가져와!\""]], [["Raskolnikov dropped on to a chair, but he kept his eyes fixed on the face of Ilya Petrovitch, which expressed unpleasant surprise.", "라스콜니코프는 의자에 털썩 주저앉았지만, 불쾌한 놀라움이 어린 일리야 페트로비치의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["Both looked at one another for a minute and waited.", "둘은 잠시 서로를 바라보며 기다렸다."], ["Water was brought.", "물이 왔다."]], [["“It was I...”", "\"제가…….\""], ["began Raskolnikov.", "라스콜니코프가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Drink some water.”", "\"물을 좀 드시오.\""]], [["Raskolnikov refused the water with his hand, and softly and brokenly, but distinctly said:", "라스콜니코프는 손으로 물을 거절하고는, 나직하고 띄엄띄엄, 그러나 또렷하게 말했다."]], [["“_It was I killed the old pawnbroker woman and her sister Lizaveta with an axe and robbed them._”", "\"노파 전당포 주인과 그 동생 리자베타를 도끼로 죽이고 물건을 훔친 건 바로 저였습니다.\""]], [["Ilya Petrovitch opened his mouth.", "일리야 페트로비치는 입을 떡 벌렸다."], ["People ran up on all sides.", "사방에서 사람들이 달려왔다."], ["Raskolnikov repeated his statement.", "라스콜니코프는 자기 진술을 되풀이했다."]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["Siberia.", "시베리아."], ["On the banks of a broad solitary river stands a town, one of the administrative centres of Russia; in the town there is a fortress, in the fortress there is a prison.", "넓고 적막한 강가에 한 도시가 서 있는데, 러시아 행정 중심지의 하나다. 그 도시에는 요새가 있고, 그 요새 안에는 감옥이 있다."], ["In the prison the second-class convict Rodion Raskolnikov has been confined for nine months.", "그 감옥에 이등 죄수 로디온 라스콜니코프가 아홉 달째 갇혀 있다."], ["Almost a year and a half has passed since his crime.", "그의 범행 이후로 거의 일 년 반이 흘렀다."]], [["There had been little difficulty about his trial.", "그의 재판에는 별 어려움이 없었다."], ["The criminal adhered exactly, firmly, and clearly to his statement.", "죄인은 자신의 진술을 정확하고 단호하고 명료하게 고수했다."], ["He did not confuse nor misrepresent the facts, nor soften them in his own interest, nor omit the smallest detail.", "그는 사실을 혼동하거나 왜곡하지 않았고, 자기에게 유리하도록 누그러뜨리지도 않았으며, 아주 사소한 세부 하나도 빠뜨리지 않았다."], ["He explained every incident of the murder, the secret of _the pledge_ (the piece of wood with a strip of metal) which was found in the murdered woman’s hand.", "그는 살인의 모든 정황을, 살해당한 여인의 손에서 발견된 그 저당물(쇳조각을 댄 나뭇조각)의 비밀을 설명했다. 그는 어떻게 그녀의 열쇠들을 꺼냈는지, 그것들이 어떻게 생겼는지를, 또한 궤짝과 그 안의 내용물을 상세히 진술했다."], ["He described minutely how he had taken her keys, what they were like, as well as the chest and its contents; he explained the mystery of Lizaveta’s murder; described how Koch and, after him, the student knocked, and repeated all they had said to one another; how he afterwards had run downstairs and heard Nikolay and Dmitri shouting; how he had hidden in the empty flat and afterwards gone home.", "그는 리자베타 살인의 수수께끼를 설명했고, 코흐가, 그다음으로 대학생이 어떻게 문을 두드렸는지, 그리고 그들이 서로 주고받은 말을 모두 그대로 되풀이했다. 그가 어떻게 계단을 뛰어 내려가 니콜라이와 드미트리가 소리치는 것을 들었는지, 어떻게 빈 셋집에 숨었다가 그 후 집으로 돌아갔는지를."], ["He ended by indicating the stone in the yard off the Voznesensky Prospect under which the purse and the trinkets were found.", "그는 마지막으로 보즈네센스키 거리 옆 마당에 있는, 지갑과 장신구들이 발견된 그 돌을 가리키며 진술을 끝맺었다."], ["The whole thing, in fact, was perfectly clear.", "사실 모든 것이 완벽하게 명료했다."], ["The lawyers and the judges were very much struck, among other things, by the fact that he had hidden the trinkets and the purse under a stone, without making use of them, and that, what was more, he did not now remember what the trinkets were like, or even how many there were.", "변호사들과 판사들은 무엇보다도, 그가 장신구들과 지갑을 사용하지도 않고 돌 밑에 숨겨 두었다는 사실에, 게다가 그가 이제 와서는 그 장신구들이 어떻게 생겼는지, 심지어 몇 개나 됐는지조차 기억하지 못한다는 사실에 큰 충격을 받았다."], ["The fact that he had never opened the purse and did not even know how much was in it seemed incredible.", "그가 지갑을 한 번도 열어 보지 않았고 그 안에 얼마가 들었는지조차 몰랐다는 사실은 믿기 어려운 일로 보였다."], ["There turned out to be in the purse three hundred and seventeen roubles and sixty copecks.", "지갑 속에는 삼백십칠 루블 육십 코페이카가 들어 있던 것으로 드러났다."], ["From being so long under the stone, some of the most valuable notes lying uppermost had suffered from the damp.", "너무 오래 돌 밑에 있었던 탓에, 맨 위에 놓여 있던 가장 값나가는 지폐 몇 장은 습기로 상해 있었다."], ["They were a long while trying to discover why the accused man should tell a lie about this, when about everything else he had made a truthful and straightforward confession.", "그들은 다른 모든 것에 대해서는 진실하고 솔직하게 자백한 피고가, 어째서 이 점에 대해서만은 거짓말을 하는지 한참 동안 알아내려 애썼다."], ["Finally some of the lawyers more versed in psychology admitted that it was possible he had really not looked into the purse, and so didn’t know what was in it when he hid it under the stone.", "마침내 심리학에 좀 더 정통한 몇몇 변호사들은, 그가 정말로 지갑을 들여다보지 않았고, 그래서 그것을 돌 밑에 숨길 때 그 안에 무엇이 들었는지 몰랐을 가능성이 있다고 인정했다."], ["But they immediately drew the deduction that the crime could only have been committed through temporary mental derangement, through homicidal mania, without object or the pursuit of gain.", "그러나 그들은 곧장, 그 범행이 일시적인 정신 착란을 통해, 목적도 이득의 추구도 없는 살인광적 광기를 통해서만 저질러질 수 있었다는 결론을 끌어냈다."], ["This fell in with the most recent fashionable theory of temporary insanity, so often applied in our days in criminal cases.", "이는 요즘 형사 사건에 너무도 빈번히 적용되는, 일시적 정신 이상이라는 가장 최신 유행의 이론과 맞아떨어졌다."], ["Moreover Raskolnikov’s hypochondriacal condition was proved by many witnesses, by Dr. Zossimov, his former fellow students, his landlady and her servant.", "게다가 라스콜니코프의 우울증적 상태는 많은 증인들에 의해, 즉 조시모프 의사, 그의 옛 동창들, 그의 하숙집 주인 여자와 그 하녀에 의해 입증되었다."], ["All this pointed strongly to the conclusion that Raskolnikov was not quite like an ordinary murderer and robber, but that there was another element in the case.", "이 모든 것은 라스콜니코프가 여느 평범한 살인범이나 강도와는 사뭇 다르며, 이 사건에는 또 다른 요소가 있다는 결론을 강하게 가리켰다."]], [["To the intense annoyance of those who maintained this opinion, the criminal scarcely attempted to defend himself.", "이 견해를 주장하던 이들의 극심한 짜증에도 불구하고, 죄인은 자신을 변호하려는 시도를 거의 하지 않았다."], ["To the decisive question as to what motive impelled him to the murder and the robbery, he answered very clearly with the coarsest frankness that the cause was his miserable position, his poverty and helplessness, and his desire to provide for his first steps in life by the help of the three thousand roubles he had reckoned on finding.", "무엇이 그를 살인과 강도로 내몰았는가 하는 결정적 질문에, 그는 더없이 거친 솔직함으로 아주 명료하게 대답했다. 그 원인은 자신의 비참한 처지, 자신의 가난과 무력함이었으며, 손에 넣을 수 있으리라 계산했던 삼천 루블의 도움으로 인생의 첫발을 마련하려는 욕망이었다고."], ["He had been led to the murder through his shallow and cowardly nature, exasperated moreover by privation and failure.", "그는 자신의 천박하고 비겁한 본성 때문에, 게다가 궁핍과 실패로 더욱 격앙되어 살인으로 이끌렸다고 했다."], ["To the question what led him to confess, he answered that it was his heartfelt repentance.", "무엇이 자백하게 했는가 하는 질문에는, 마음에서 우러난 회개 때문이라고 대답했다."], ["All this was almost coarse....", "이 모든 것이 거의 거칠다 싶을 정도였다……."]], [["The sentence however was more merciful than could have been expected, perhaps partly because the criminal had not tried to justify himself, but had rather shown a desire to exaggerate his guilt.", "그러나 선고는 예상보다 자비로웠는데, 어쩌면 그건 부분적으로 죄인이 자신을 정당화하려 하기는커녕 오히려 자기 죄를 과장하려는 기색을 보였기 때문이었다."], ["All the strange and peculiar circumstances of the crime were taken into consideration.", "범행의 온갖 기이하고 특수한 정황이 참작되었다."], ["There could be no doubt of the abnormal and poverty-stricken condition of the criminal at the time.", "범행 당시 죄인이 비정상적이고 가난에 찌든 상태였다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["The fact that he had made no use of what he had stolen was put down partly to the effect of remorse, partly to his abnormal mental condition at the time of the crime.", "그가 훔친 것을 사용하지 않았다는 사실은 한편으로는 양심의 가책 탓으로, 한편으로는 범행 당시 그의 비정상적인 정신 상태 탓으로 돌려졌다."], ["Incidentally the murder of Lizaveta served indeed to confirm the last hypothesis: a man commits two murders and forgets that the door is open!", "더구나 리자베타 살인은 마지막 가설을 확증하는 데 한몫했다. 한 사내가 두 건의 살인을 저지르면서 문이 열려 있다는 것을 잊다니!"], ["Finally, the confession, at the very moment when the case was hopelessly muddled by the false evidence given by Nikolay through melancholy and fanaticism, and when, moreover, there were no proofs against the real criminal, no suspicions even (Porfiry Petrovitch fully kept his word)--all this did much to soften the sentence.", "마지막으로, 니콜라이가 우울증과 광신 탓에 거짓 증언을 함으로써 사건이 절망적으로 뒤엉켜 버린 바로 그 순간에, 게다가 진범에 대한 그 어떤 증거도, 심지어 혐의조차 없던 때에(포르피리 페트로비치는 자신의 약속을 온전히 지켰다) 자백이 이루어진 것 — 이 모든 것이 선고를 누그러뜨리는 데 크게 작용했다."], ["Other circumstances, too, in the prisoner’s favour came out quite unexpectedly.", "죄수에게 유리한 다른 정황들도 전혀 뜻밖에 드러났다."], ["Razumihin somehow discovered and proved that while Raskolnikov was at the university he had helped a poor consumptive fellow student and had spent his last penny on supporting him for six months, and when this student died, leaving a decrepit old father whom he had maintained almost from his thirteenth year, Raskolnikov had got the old man into a hospital and paid for his funeral when he died.", "라주미힌은 어떻게 알아냈는지, 라스콜니코프가 대학에 다닐 때 폐병을 앓던 가난한 동급생 하나를 도왔으며, 여섯 달 동안 마지막 한 푼까지 그를 부양하는 데 썼다는 것을, 그리고 그 학생이 죽으면서 거의 열세 살 때부터 자기가 부양해 온 노쇠한 늙은 아버지를 남기자, 라스콜니코프가 그 노인을 병원에 입원시키고 그가 죽었을 때 장례 비용까지 댔다는 것을 밝혀내 입증했다."], ["Raskolnikov’s landlady bore witness, too, that when they had lived in another house at Five Corners, Raskolnikov had rescued two little children from a house on fire and was burnt in doing so.", "라스콜니코프의 하숙집 주인 여자 역시 증언하기를, 그들이 '다섯 모퉁이'의 다른 집에 살 때 라스콜니코프가 불난 집에서 어린아이 둘을 구해 냈고 그 과정에서 화상을 입었다고 했다."], ["This was investigated and fairly well confirmed by many witnesses.", "이는 조사되어 여러 증인들에 의해 상당히 잘 확증되었다."], ["These facts made an impression in his favour.", "이런 사실들은 그에게 유리한 인상을 남겼다."]], [["And in the end the criminal was, in consideration of extenuating circumstances, condemned to penal servitude in the second class for a term of eight years only.", "그리하여 결국 죄인은 정상 참작을 고려하여 이등 징역형에 처해졌는데, 형기는 겨우 팔 년이었다."]], [["At the very beginning of the trial Raskolnikov’s mother fell ill.", "재판이 막 시작될 무렵 라스콜니코프의 어머니가 병이 났다."], ["Dounia and Razumihin found it possible to get her out of Petersburg during the trial.", "두냐와 라주미힌은 재판이 진행되는 동안 그녀를 페테르부르크에서 데리고 나가는 것이 가능하다고 판단했다."], ["Razumihin chose a town on the railway not far from Petersburg, so as to be able to follow every step of the trial and at the same time to see Avdotya Romanovna as often as possible.", "라주미힌은 페테르부르크에서 멀지 않은 철도변의 한 도시를 골랐는데, 재판의 모든 진행을 좇으면서 동시에 가능한 한 자주 아브도탸 로마노브나를 볼 수 있도록 하기 위함이었다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s illness was a strange nervous one and was accompanied by a partial derangement of her intellect.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 병은 기이한 신경성 질환이었고, 부분적인 지적 착란을 동반했다."]], [["When Dounia returned from her last interview with her brother, she had found her mother already ill, in feverish delirium.", "두냐는 오빠와의 마지막 면회에서 돌아왔을 때, 어머니가 이미 병이 나 열에 들떠 헛소리를 하고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["That evening Razumihin and she agreed what answers they must make to her mother’s questions about Raskolnikov and made up a complete story for her mother’s benefit of his having to go away to a distant part of Russia on a business commission, which would bring him in the end money and reputation.", "그날 저녁 라주미힌과 그녀는 어머니가 라스콜니코프에 대해 묻는 질문에 어떻게 대답해야 할지 합의를 보고, 어머니를 위해 완전한 이야기를 하나 지어냈다. 그가 사업상의 임무로 러시아의 먼 지방으로 떠나야 했으며, 그것이 결국엔 그에게 돈과 명성을 가져다주리라는 것이었다."]], [["But they were struck by the fact that Pulcheria Alexandrovna never asked them anything on the subject, neither then nor thereafter.", "그러나 그들은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 그 일에 대해 그때도 그 후로도 아무것도 묻지 않는다는 사실에 놀랐다."], ["On the contrary, she had her own version of her son’s sudden departure; she told them with tears how he had come to say good-bye to her, hinting that she alone knew many mysterious and important facts, and that Rodya had many very powerful enemies, so that it was necessary for him to be in hiding.", "오히려 그녀는 아들의 갑작스러운 출발에 대해 나름의 해석을 가지고 있었다. 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 그들에게 아들이 어떻게 자기에게 작별 인사를 하러 왔는지 이야기했고, 자기만이 많은 신비롭고 중요한 사실들을 알고 있다는 듯, 또 로댜에게는 매우 강력한 적들이 많아서 그가 몸을 숨길 수밖에 없다는 듯 암시했다."], ["As for his future career, she had no doubt that it would be brilliant when certain sinister influences could be removed.", "그의 앞날에 대해서는, 어떤 사악한 영향력들만 제거되면 그것이 찬란하리라는 것을 그녀는 조금도 의심하지 않았다."], ["She assured Razumihin that her son would be one day a great statesman, that his article and brilliant literary talent proved it.", "그녀는 라주미힌에게, 자기 아들이 언젠가 위대한 정치가가 될 것이며, 그의 논문과 빛나는 문학적 재능이 그것을 증명한다고 장담했다."], ["This article she was continually reading, she even read it aloud, almost took it to bed with her, but scarcely asked where Rodya was, though the subject was obviously avoided by the others, which might have been enough to awaken her suspicions.", "이 논문을 그녀는 끊임없이 읽었고, 심지어 소리 내어 읽기도 했으며, 거의 잠자리에까지 가지고 들어갈 정도였지만, 정작 로댜가 어디 있는지는 거의 묻지 않았는데, 다른 이들이 그 주제를 분명히 피하는 것만으로도 그녀의 의심을 불러일으키기에 충분했을 법한데도 그랬다."]], [["They began to be frightened at last at Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s strange silence on certain subjects.", "그들은 마침내 어떤 주제들에 대한 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 기이한 침묵에 겁이 나기 시작했다."], ["She did not, for instance, complain of getting no letters from him, though in previous years she had only lived on the hope of letters from her beloved Rodya.", "예컨대 그녀는 그에게서 편지가 오지 않는다고 한 번도 불평하지 않았는데, 지난 몇 해 동안에는 사랑하는 로댜의 편지에 대한 희망만으로 살아왔던 그녀였다."], ["This was the cause of great uneasiness to Dounia; the idea occurred to her that her mother suspected that there was something terrible in her son’s fate and was afraid to ask, for fear of hearing something still more awful.", "이것이 두냐에게는 큰 불안의 원인이었다. 어머니가 아들의 운명에 무언가 끔찍한 것이 있음을 의심하면서도, 더욱 무서운 무언가를 듣게 될까 두려워 차마 묻지 못하는 것이라는 생각이 그녀에게 떠올랐다."], ["In any case, Dounia saw clearly that her mother was not in full possession of her faculties.", "어쨌든 두냐는 어머니가 온전히 제정신이 아니라는 것을 분명히 알 수 있었다."]], [["It happened once or twice, however, that Pulcheria Alexandrovna gave such a turn to the conversation that it was impossible to answer her without mentioning where Rodya was, and on receiving unsatisfactory and suspicious answers she became at once gloomy and silent, and this mood lasted for a long time.", "그러나 한두 번은, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나가 대화를 그런 식으로 몰아가서, 로댜가 어디 있는지를 언급하지 않고는 도저히 대답할 수 없게 만든 적도 있었는데, 그럴 때 미흡하고 의심스러운 대답을 듣고 나면 그녀는 곧장 침울해져 입을 다물었고, 그런 기분이 한참 동안 이어졌다."], ["Dounia saw at last that it was hard to deceive her and came to the conclusion that it was better to be absolutely silent on certain points; but it became more and more evident that the poor mother suspected something terrible.", "두냐는 마침내 어머니를 속이기가 어렵다는 것을 깨닫고, 어떤 점들에 대해서는 아예 입을 다무는 편이 낫겠다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그러나 그 가엾은 어머니가 무언가 끔찍한 것을 의심하고 있다는 것이 점점 더 분명해졌다."], ["Dounia remembered her brother’s telling her that her mother had overheard her talking in her sleep on the night after her interview with Svidrigaïlov and before the fatal day of the confession: had not she made out something from that?", "두냐는 오빠가 자기에게 했던 말을 떠올렸다. 그녀가 스비드리가일로프와 면회한 다음 날 밤, 자백이라는 그 숙명적인 날을 앞두고, 어머니가 자기가 잠결에 하는 말을 엿들었다던 이야기를."], ["Sometimes days and even weeks of gloomy silence and tears would be succeeded by a period of hysterical animation, and the invalid would begin to talk almost incessantly of her son, of her hopes of his future....", "어머니가 거기서 무언가를 알아챈 것은 아닐까? 때로는 며칠씩, 심지어 몇 주씩 침울한 침묵과 눈물이 이어지다가, 발작적으로 활기를 띠는 시기가 뒤따랐고, 그러면 환자는 거의 쉬지 않고 자기 아들에 대해, 그의 앞날에 대한 자기 희망에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다……."], ["Her fancies were sometimes very strange.", "그녀의 공상은 때때로 무척 기이했다."], ["They humoured her, pretended to agree with her (she saw perhaps that they were pretending), but she still went on talking.", "그들은 그녀를 달래며 그녀의 말에 동의하는 척했고(어쩌면 그녀는 그들이 척하고 있다는 것을 알았을지도 모른다), 그래도 그녀는 계속 이야기했다."]], [["Five months after Raskolnikov’s confession, he was sentenced.", "라스콜니코프가 자백한 지 다섯 달 만에 그에게 선고가 내려졌다."], ["Razumihin and Sonia saw him in prison as often as it was possible.", "라주미힌과 소냐는 가능한 한 자주 감옥으로 그를 면회하러 갔다."], ["At last the moment of separation came.", "마침내 이별의 순간이 왔다."], ["Dounia swore to her brother that the separation should not be for ever, Razumihin did the same.", "두냐는 오빠에게 이 이별이 영원하지는 않으리라고 맹세했고, 라주미힌도 똑같이 했다."], ["Razumihin, in his youthful ardour, had firmly resolved to lay the foundations at least of a secure livelihood during the next three or four years, and saving up a certain sum, to emigrate to Siberia, a country rich in every natural resource and in need of workers, active men and capital.", "라주미힌은 젊은이다운 열정으로, 앞으로 삼사 년 안에 적어도 안정된 생계의 기반은 닦아 놓고, 일정한 액수를 모은 뒤, 온갖 천연자원이 풍부하고 일꾼과 활동적인 사람들과 자본을 필요로 하는 나라, 시베리아로 이주하기로 굳게 결심했다."], ["There they would settle in the town where Rodya was and all together would begin a new life.", "거기서 그들은 로댜가 있는 도시에 정착하여 다 함께 새 삶을 시작할 터였다."], ["They all wept at parting.", "그들은 모두 헤어지면서 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["Raskolnikov had been very dreamy for a few days before.", "라스콜니코프는 며칠 전부터 무척 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He asked a great deal about his mother and was constantly anxious about her.", "그는 어머니에 대해 많은 것을 물었고 끊임없이 그녀를 걱정했다."], ["He worried so much about her that it alarmed Dounia.", "그가 어찌나 어머니를 염려하는지 두냐가 다 불안할 정도였다."], ["When he heard about his mother’s illness he became very gloomy.", "어머니의 병에 대해 들었을 때 그는 매우 침울해졌다."], ["With Sonia he was particularly reserved all the time.", "그는 줄곧 소냐에게 특히 말이 없었다."], ["With the help of the money left to her by Svidrigaïlov, Sonia had long ago made her preparations to follow the party of convicts in which he was despatched to Siberia.", "스비드리가일로프가 그녀에게 남긴 돈의 도움으로, 소냐는 오래전부터 그가 시베리아로 호송되는 죄수 무리를 따라갈 채비를 해 두었다."], ["Not a word passed between Raskolnikov and her on the subject, but both knew it would be so.", "라스콜니코프와 그녀 사이에 이 일에 대해 단 한 마디도 오가지 않았지만, 둘 다 그러리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["At the final leave-taking he smiled strangely at his sister’s and Razumihin’s fervent anticipations of their happy future together when he should come out of prison.", "마지막 작별 인사를 나눌 때, 그는 자기가 출옥하면 함께 누릴 행복한 미래에 대한 누이동생과 라주미힌의 뜨거운 기대를 두고 기이하게 미소 지었다."], ["He predicted that their mother’s illness would soon have a fatal ending.", "그는 어머니의 병이 곧 죽음으로 끝나리라고 예언했다."], ["Sonia and he at last set off.", "소냐와 그는 마침내 길을 떠났다."]], [["Two months later Dounia was married to Razumihin.", "두 달 뒤 두냐는 라주미힌과 결혼했다."], ["It was a quiet and sorrowful wedding; Porfiry Petrovitch and Zossimov were invited however.", "그것은 조용하고 슬픈 결혼식이었다. 그래도 포르피리 페트로비치와 조시모프가 초대되었다."], ["During all this period Razumihin wore an air of resolute determination.", "이 모든 기간 동안 라주미힌은 단호한 결의에 찬 모습이었다."], ["Dounia put implicit faith in his carrying out his plans and indeed she could not but believe in him.", "두냐는 그가 자기 계획을 실행에 옮기리라는 것을 굳게 믿었고, 사실 그를 믿지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He displayed a rare strength of will.", "그는 보기 드문 강한 의지를 보여 주었다."], ["Among other things he began attending university lectures again in order to take his degree.", "무엇보다도 그는 학위를 따기 위해 다시 대학 강의에 출석하기 시작했다."], ["They were continually making plans for the future; both counted on settling in Siberia within five years at least.", "그들은 끊임없이 미래에 대한 계획을 세웠다. 둘 다 적어도 오 년 안에는 시베리아에 정착할 작정이었다."], ["Till then they rested their hopes on Sonia.", "그때까지 그들은 소냐에게 희망을 걸었다."]], [["Pulcheria Alexandrovna was delighted to give her blessing to Dounia’s marriage with Razumihin; but after the marriage she became even more melancholy and anxious.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 두냐와 라주미힌의 결혼에 기꺼이 축복을 내려 주었다. 그러나 결혼 후 그녀는 한층 더 우울하고 불안해졌다."], ["To give her pleasure Razumihin told her how Raskolnikov had looked after the poor student and his decrepit father and how a year ago he had been burnt and injured in rescuing two little children from a fire.", "그녀를 기쁘게 해 주려고 라주미힌은 라스콜니코프가 어떻게 그 가난한 학생과 그의 노쇠한 아버지를 보살폈는지, 또 일 년 전 어떻게 불난 집에서 어린아이 둘을 구해 내다 화상을 입었는지를 이야기해 주었다."], ["These two pieces of news excited Pulcheria Alexandrovna’s disordered imagination almost to ecstasy.", "이 두 가지 소식은 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나의 어지러운 상상력을 거의 황홀경에 이를 만큼 흥분시켰다."], ["She was continually talking about them, even entering into conversation with strangers in the street, though Dounia always accompanied her.", "그녀는 끊임없이 그 이야기를 했고, 두냐가 늘 동행하는데도 길거리에서 낯선 사람들과 대화를 나누기까지 했다."], ["In public conveyances and shops, wherever she could capture a listener, she would begin the discourse about her son, his article, how he had helped the student, how he had been burnt at the fire, and so on!", "대중교통이나 가게에서, 들어 줄 사람만 붙잡을 수 있으면 어디서든 그녀는 자기 아들 이야기를, 그의 논문 이야기를, 그가 어떻게 그 학생을 도왔는지, 어떻게 불 속에서 화상을 입었는지 따위를 늘어놓기 시작했다!"], ["Dounia did not know how to restrain her.", "두냐는 어머니를 어떻게 말려야 할지 몰랐다."], ["Apart from the danger of her morbid excitement, there was the risk of someone’s recalling Raskolnikov’s name and speaking of the recent trial.", "그녀의 병적인 흥분이 위험할 뿐 아니라, 누군가가 라스콜니코프의 이름을 떠올리고 최근의 재판에 대해 입에 올릴 위험도 있었다."], ["Pulcheria Alexandrovna found out the address of the mother of the two children her son had saved and insisted on going to see her.", "풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 아들이 구해 준 두 아이의 어머니 주소를 알아내서는 기어이 그녀를 찾아가겠다고 고집했다."]], [["At last her restlessness reached an extreme point.", "마침내 그녀의 안절부절못함은 극에 달했다."], ["She would sometimes begin to cry suddenly and was often ill and feverishly delirious.", "그녀는 때때로 갑자기 울음을 터뜨리곤 했고, 자주 병이 나 열에 들떠 헛소리를 했다."], ["One morning she declared that by her reckoning Rodya ought soon to be home, that she remembered when he said good-bye to her he said that they must expect him back in nine months.", "어느 날 아침 그녀는, 자기 계산으로는 로댜가 곧 집에 돌아올 때가 되었다고, 작별 인사를 할 때 그가 아홉 달 뒤에는 돌아올 테니 기다리라고 했던 것이 기억난다고 선언했다."], ["She began to prepare for his coming, began to do up her room for him, to clean the furniture, to wash and put up new hangings and so on.", "그녀는 그가 올 것에 대비하기 시작했고, 그를 위해 방을 단장하고, 가구를 닦고, 새 휘장을 빨아서 다는 등 부산을 떨었다."], ["Dounia was anxious, but said nothing and helped her to arrange the room.", "두냐는 불안했지만 아무 말도 하지 않고 어머니가 방을 꾸미는 것을 도왔다."], ["After a fatiguing day spent in continual fancies, in joyful day-dreams and tears, Pulcheria Alexandrovna was taken ill in the night and by morning she was feverish and delirious.", "끊임없는 공상과 기쁨에 찬 백일몽과 눈물로 진을 빼는 하루를 보낸 뒤, 풀헤리야 알렉산드로브나는 밤사이 병이 났고 아침이 되자 열에 들떠 헛소리를 했다. 뇌막염이었다."], ["It was brain fever. She died within a fortnight.", "그녀는 보름도 못 되어 세상을 떠났다."], ["In her delirium she dropped words which showed that she knew a great deal more about her son’s terrible fate than they had supposed.", "헛소리를 하는 가운데 그녀는, 그들이 짐작했던 것보다 아들의 끔찍한 운명에 대해 훨씬 더 많이 알고 있었음을 드러내는 말들을 흘렸다."]], [["For a long time Raskolnikov did not know of his mother’s death, though a regular correspondence had been maintained from the time he reached Siberia.", "라스콜니코프는 오랫동안 어머니의 죽음을 알지 못했는데, 그가 시베리아에 도착한 때부터 정기적인 서신 왕래가 유지되고 있었는데도 그랬다."], ["It was carried on by means of Sonia, who wrote every month to the Razumihins and received an answer with unfailing regularity.", "그것은 소냐를 통해 이루어졌으니, 그녀는 매달 라주미힌 부부에게 편지를 썼고 어김없이 규칙적으로 답장을 받았다."], ["At first they found Sonia’s letters dry and unsatisfactory, but later on they came to the conclusion that the letters could not be better, for from these letters they received a complete picture of their unfortunate brother’s life.", "처음에 그들은 소냐의 편지가 무미건조하고 미흡하다고 여겼지만, 나중에는 그 편지들이 더할 나위 없다는 결론에 이르렀는데, 그 편지들로부터 그들은 불행한 오빠의 삶의 완전한 모습을 전해 받았기 때문이다."], ["Sonia’s letters were full of the most matter-of-fact detail, the simplest and clearest description of all Raskolnikov’s surroundings as a convict.", "소냐의 편지는 더없이 사실적인 세부로, 죄수로서의 라스콜니코프를 둘러싼 모든 것에 대한 가장 단순하고 명료한 묘사로 가득 차 있었다."], ["There was no word of her own hopes, no conjecture as to the future, no description of her feelings.", "자기 자신의 희망에 대한 말도, 미래에 대한 추측도, 자기 감정에 대한 묘사도 없었다."], ["Instead of any attempt to interpret his state of mind and inner life, she gave the simple facts--that is, his own words, an exact account of his health, what he asked for at their interviews, what commission he gave her and so on.", "그의 정신 상태나 내면의 삶을 해석하려는 시도 대신, 그녀는 단순한 사실들을 — 즉 그가 직접 한 말, 그의 건강에 대한 정확한 보고, 면회 때 그가 청한 것, 그가 그녀에게 맡긴 심부름 따위를 전했다."], ["All these facts she gave with extraordinary minuteness.", "이 모든 사실을 그녀는 놀라울 만큼 세밀하게 전했다."], ["The picture of their unhappy brother stood out at last with great clearness and precision.", "불행한 오빠의 모습이 마침내 매우 또렷하고 정확하게 드러났다."], ["There could be no mistake, because nothing was given but facts.", "오류가 있을 수 없었으니, 사실 외에는 아무것도 전하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["But Dounia and her husband could get little comfort out of the news, especially at first.", "그러나 두냐와 그녀의 남편은 그 소식에서 위안을 거의 얻을 수 없었는데, 특히 처음에는 그랬다."], ["Sonia wrote that he was constantly sullen and not ready to talk, that he scarcely seemed interested in the news she gave him from their letters, that he sometimes asked after his mother and that when, seeing that he had guessed the truth, she told him at last of her death, she was surprised to find that he did not seem greatly affected by it, not externally at any rate.", "소냐의 편지에 따르면, 그는 늘 침울하고 말할 마음이 없으며, 그녀가 그들의 편지에서 전해 주는 소식에도 거의 관심이 없는 듯하고, 때때로 어머니의 안부를 물으며, 그가 진실을 짐작했음을 알아챈 그녀가 마침내 어머니의 죽음을 알렸을 때, 그가 적어도 겉으로는 그 일에 크게 동요하지 않는 것처럼 보여 그녀가 놀랐다고 했다."], ["She told them that, although he seemed so wrapped up in himself and, as it were, shut himself off from everyone--he took a very direct and simple view of his new life; that he understood his position, expected nothing better for the time, had no ill-founded hopes (as is so common in his position) and scarcely seemed surprised at anything in his surroundings, so unlike anything he had known before.", "그녀가 그들에게 전하기를, 그는 그토록 자기 안에 틀어박혀, 말하자면 모두에게서 자신을 차단한 듯 보였지만 — 그래도 자신의 새 삶을 매우 직접적이고 단순하게 받아들였으며, 자기 처지를 이해했고, 당분간 더 나은 것을 기대하지 않았으며, (그런 처지에서 흔히 그러하듯) 근거 없는 희망도 품지 않았고, 예전에 알던 그 어떤 것과도 너무나 다른 자기 주변의 어떤 것에도 거의 놀라지 않는 듯하다고 했다."], ["She wrote that his health was satisfactory; he did his work without shirking or seeking to do more; he was almost indifferent about food, but except on Sundays and holidays the food was so bad that at last he had been glad to accept some money from her, Sonia, to have his own tea every day.", "그녀는 그의 건강이 양호하며, 그가 일을 게을리하지도 더 하려 들지도 않고 해낸다고 썼다. 그는 음식에 거의 무관심했지만, 일요일과 축일을 빼면 음식이 너무 형편없어서 마침내 매일 자기 차를 마실 수 있도록 그녀, 소냐에게서 약간의 돈을 기꺼이 받았다고 했다."], ["He begged her not to trouble about anything else, declaring that all this fuss about him only annoyed him.", "그는 다른 어떤 것에도 신경 쓰지 말라고 그녀에게 부탁하며, 자기에 대한 이 모든 부산스러움이 그저 짜증스러울 뿐이라고 단언했다."], ["Sonia wrote further that in prison he shared the same room with the rest, that she had not seen the inside of their barracks, but concluded that they were crowded, miserable and unhealthy; that he slept on a plank bed with a rug under him and was unwilling to make any other arrangement.", "소냐는 또한, 감옥에서 그가 나머지 사람들과 한 방을 쓰며, 그들의 막사 안은 자기가 본 적이 없지만, 그곳이 비좁고 비참하고 비위생적이리라 짐작한다고 썼다. 그가 밑에 깔개를 깐 널빤지 침상에서 잔다고, 그리고 다른 어떤 조치도 마련하기를 꺼린다고."], ["But that he lived so poorly and roughly, not from any plan or design, but simply from inattention and indifference.", "그러나 그가 그렇게 가난하고 거칠게 사는 것은 무슨 계획이나 의도에서가 아니라, 그저 무관심과 무신경에서일 뿐이라고 했다."]], [["Sonia wrote simply that he had at first shown no interest in her visits, had almost been vexed with her indeed for coming, unwilling to talk and rude to her.", "소냐는 단순히, 그가 처음에는 자기 면회에 아무런 관심도 보이지 않았으며, 사실 자기가 찾아오는 것에 거의 짜증을 냈고, 말하기를 꺼리며 자기에게 무례하게 굴었다고 썼다."], ["But that in the end these visits had become a habit and almost a necessity for him, so that he was positively distressed when she was ill for some days and could not visit him.", "그러나 결국 이 면회가 그에게 하나의 습관이, 거의 필수가 되어서, 그녀가 며칠 아파서 면회를 갈 수 없었을 때 그가 정말로 괴로워했다고 했다."], ["She used to see him on holidays at the prison gates or in the guard-room, to which he was brought for a few minutes to see her.", "그녀는 축일이면 감옥 정문에서, 혹은 그가 그녀를 만나러 몇 분 동안 끌려 나오는 위병소에서 그를 보곤 했다."], ["On working days she would go to see him at work either at the workshops or at the brick kilns, or at the sheds on the banks of the Irtish.", "평일이면 그녀는 그가 일하는 곳으로, 작업장이나 벽돌 가마, 혹은 이르티시 강가의 헛간으로 그를 보러 가곤 했다."]], [["About herself, Sonia wrote that she had succeeded in making some acquaintances in the town, that she did sewing, and, as there was scarcely a dressmaker in the town, she was looked upon as an indispensable person in many houses.", "자기 자신에 대해 소냐는, 그 도시에서 몇몇 사람들과 알고 지내게 되었고, 바느질을 하는데, 그 도시에 양재사가 거의 없는 터라 많은 집에서 없어서는 안 될 사람으로 여겨진다고 썼다."], ["But she did not mention that the authorities were, through her, interested in Raskolnikov; that his task was lightened and so on.", "그러나 그녀는 당국이 자기를 통해 라스콜니코프에게 관심을 두고 있다는 것, 그래서 그의 일이 가벼워졌다는 것 따위는 언급하지 않았다."]], [["At last the news came (Dounia had indeed noticed signs of alarm and uneasiness in the preceding letters) that he held aloof from everyone, that his fellow prisoners did not like him, that he kept silent for days at a time and was becoming very pale.", "마침내 소식이 왔는데(사실 두냐는 그에 앞선 편지들에서 불안과 동요의 기미를 알아챘다), 그가 모두에게서 거리를 두고 있으며, 동료 죄수들이 그를 좋아하지 않고, 며칠씩 입을 다물고 있으며, 몹시 창백해지고 있다는 것이었다."], ["In the last letter Sonia wrote that he had been taken very seriously ill and was in the convict ward of the hospital.", "마지막 편지에서 소냐는, 그가 매우 위중하게 병이 나 병원의 죄수 병동에 누워 있다고 썼다."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["He was ill a long time.", "그는 오랫동안 앓았다."], ["But it was not the horrors of prison life, not the hard labour, the bad food, the shaven head, or the patched clothes that crushed him.", "그러나 그를 무너뜨린 것은 감옥살이의 참상도, 중노동도, 형편없는 음식도, 빡빡 깎인 머리도, 누덕누덕 기운 옷도 아니었다."], ["What did he care for all those trials and hardships!", "그까짓 시련과 고난쯤이 그에게 무슨 상관이랴!"], ["he was even glad of the hard work.", "그는 중노동이 차라리 반갑기까지 했다."], ["Physically exhausted, he could at least reckon on a few hours of quiet sleep.", "육체적으로 진이 빠지면 적어도 몇 시간은 편히 잠들 수 있었으니까."], ["And what was the food to him--the thin cabbage soup with beetles floating in it?", "그리고 음식 따위가 그에게 무슨 대수랴 — 딱정벌레가 둥둥 떠다니는 멀건 양배추 수프가?"], ["In the past as a student he had often not had even that.", "학생 시절에 그는 그것조차 먹지 못한 적이 많았다."], ["His clothes were warm and suited to his manner of life.", "그의 옷은 따뜻했고 그의 생활 방식에 맞았다."], ["He did not even feel the fetters.", "그는 족쇄조차 느끼지 못했다."], ["Was he ashamed of his shaven head and parti-coloured coat?", "그가 빡빡 깎인 머리와 얼룩덜룩한 죄수복을 부끄러워했을까?"], ["Before whom?", "누구 앞에서?"], ["Before Sonia?", "소냐 앞에서?"], ["Sonia was afraid of him, how could he be ashamed before her?", "소냐가 그를 무서워하는데, 그녀 앞에서 그가 어찌 부끄러워할 수 있겠는가?"], ["And yet he was ashamed even before Sonia, whom he tortured because of it with his contemptuous rough manner.", "그런데도 그는 소냐 앞에서조차 부끄러워했으며, 그 때문에 자신의 경멸하는 듯한 거친 태도로 그녀를 괴롭혔다."], ["But it was not his shaven head and his fetters he was ashamed of: his pride had been stung to the quick.", "하지만 그가 부끄러워한 것은 빡빡 깎인 머리도 족쇄도 아니었다. 그의 자존심이 깊이 찔렸던 것이다."], ["It was wounded pride that made him ill.", "그를 병들게 한 것은 상처 입은 자존심이었다."], ["Oh, how happy he would have been if he could have blamed himself!", "오, 만일 자기 자신을 탓할 수 있었다면 그는 얼마나 행복했으랴!"], ["He could have borne anything then, even shame and disgrace.", "그랬다면 그는 그 무엇이라도, 수치와 치욕마저도 견뎌 냈으리라."], ["But he judged himself severely, and his exasperated conscience found no particularly terrible fault in his past, except a simple _blunder_ which might happen to anyone.", "그러나 그는 자신을 엄혹하게 심판했고, 그의 격앙된 양심은 자신의 과거에서 그 누구에게나 일어날 수 있는 단순한 실수 하나 말고는 딱히 끔찍한 잘못을 찾아내지 못했다."], ["He was ashamed just because he, Raskolnikov, had so hopelessly, stupidly come to grief through some decree of blind fate, and must humble himself and submit to “the idiocy” of a sentence, if he were anyhow to be at peace.", "그가 부끄러워한 것은 다름 아니라, 자기, 라스콜니코프가 눈먼 운명의 어떤 명령에 의해 그토록 절망적으로, 어리석게 파멸하고 말았다는 것, 그리고 조금이라도 마음의 평안을 얻으려면 그 선고의 \"어리석음\"에 머리를 숙이고 복종해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["Vague and objectless anxiety in the present, and in the future a continual sacrifice leading to nothing--that was all that lay before him.", "현재에는 막연하고 목적 없는 불안이, 미래에는 아무것에도 이르지 못할 끊임없는 희생이 — 그것이 그의 앞에 놓인 전부였다."], ["And what comfort was it to him that at the end of eight years he would only be thirty-two and able to begin a new life!", "팔 년의 끝에 가서 그가 겨우 서른두 살이 되어 새 삶을 시작할 수 있으리라는 것이 그에게 무슨 위안이 되겠는가!"], ["What had he to live for?", "무엇을 위해 살아야 한단 말인가?"], ["What had he to look forward to?", "무엇을 기대해야 한단 말인가?"], ["Why should he strive?", "무엇을 위해 애써야 한단 말인가?"], ["To live in order to exist?", "그저 존재하기 위해 살아야 하나?"], ["Why, he had been ready a thousand times before to give up existence for the sake of an idea, for a hope, even for a fancy.", "아니, 그는 이전에도 천 번이나, 하나의 사상을 위해, 하나의 희망을 위해, 심지어 하나의 공상을 위해 존재를 내던질 각오가 되어 있었다."], ["Mere existence had always been too little for him; he had always wanted more.", "단순한 존재는 그에게 늘 너무 보잘것없었다. 그는 늘 그 이상을 원했다."], ["Perhaps it was just because of the strength of his desires that he had thought himself a man to whom more was permissible than to others.", "어쩌면 그가 자신을, 남들보다 더 많은 것이 허용되는 인간이라고 여겼던 것도 바로 그 욕망의 강렬함 때문이었으리라."], ["And if only fate would have sent him repentance--burning repentance that would have torn his heart and robbed him of sleep, that repentance, the awful agony of which brings visions of hanging or drowning!", "그리고 운명이 그에게 회개만이라도 보내 주었더라면 — 가슴을 갈가리 찢고 잠을 앗아 가는 불타는 회개를, 그 끔찍한 고뇌가 목매다는 환영이나 물에 빠지는 환영을 불러오는 그런 회개를!"], ["Oh, he would have been glad of it!", "오, 그는 그것을 반겼으련만!"], ["Tears and agonies would at least have been life.", "눈물과 고뇌도 적어도 삶이었으련만."], ["But he did not repent of his crime.", "그러나 그는 자신의 범죄를 회개하지 않았다."]], [["At least he might have found relief in raging at his stupidity, as he had raged at the grotesque blunders that had brought him to prison.", "적어도 그는 자신의 어리석음을 향해 분노함으로써 위안을 얻을 수도 있었으련만, 자기를 감옥으로 몰아넣은 그 터무니없는 실수들을 향해 분노했던 것처럼."], ["But now in prison, _in freedom_, he thought over and criticised all his actions again and by no means found them so blundering and so grotesque as they had seemed at the fatal time.", "그러나 이제 감옥에서, 자유로운 가운데, 그는 자신의 모든 행동을 다시 곱씹어 보고 비판했고, 그것들이 그 숙명적인 때에 보였던 것만큼 그렇게 실수투성이도 터무니없는 것도 결코 아니라고 여기게 되었다."]], [["“In what way,”", "'대체 무슨 점에서,'"], ["he asked himself, “was my theory stupider than others that have swarmed and clashed from the beginning of the world?", "그는 자문했다. '내 이론이 세상이 시작된 이래로 우글거리며 충돌해 온 다른 이론들보다 더 어리석단 말인가?"], ["One has only to look at the thing quite independently, broadly, and uninfluenced by commonplace ideas, and my idea will by no means seem so...", "이 문제를 완전히 독립적으로, 폭넓게, 그리고 진부한 관념에 휘둘리지 않고 바라보기만 하면, 내 사상은 결코 그렇게…… 기이해 보이지 않을 텐데."], ["strange. Oh, sceptics and halfpenny philosophers, why do you halt half-way!", "오, 회의론자들, 한 푼짜리 철학자들이여, 어찌하여 그대들은 중도에 멈춰 서는가!"]], [["“Why does my action strike them as so horrible?”", "'왜 내 행위가 그들에게 그토록 끔찍하게 비치는 걸까?'"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“Is it because it was a crime?", "'그것이 범죄였기 때문에?"], ["What is meant by crime?", "범죄란 무엇을 뜻하는가?"], ["My conscience is at rest.", "내 양심은 평온하다."], ["Of course, it was a legal crime, of course, the letter of the law was broken and blood was shed.", "물론 그것은 법적인 범죄였고, 물론 법조문이 깨졌으며 피가 흘렀다."], ["Well, punish me for the letter of the law...", "그래, 법조문에 대해서라면 나를 벌하라……"], ["and that’s enough.", "그것으로 족하다."], ["Of course, in that case many of the benefactors of mankind who snatched power for themselves instead of inheriting it ought to have been punished at their first steps.", "물론 그렇게 따진다면, 권력을 물려받는 대신 스스로 움켜쥔 인류의 은인들 가운데 상당수도 그 첫걸음에서 벌을 받았어야 했다."], ["But those men succeeded and so _they were right_, and I didn’t, and so I had no right to have taken that step.”", "그러나 그자들은 성공했고 그래서 그들이 옳았으며, 나는 성공하지 못했으니 그 걸음을 내디딜 권리가 없었던 것이다.'"]], [["It was only in that that he recognised his criminality, only in the fact that he had been unsuccessful and had confessed it.", "그가 자신의 범죄성을 인정한 것은 오직 그 점에서뿐이었다. 오직 자기가 성공하지 못했고 그것을 자백했다는 사실에서뿐."]], [["He suffered too from the question: why had he not killed himself?", "그는 또 하나의 물음으로도 괴로워했다. 왜 나는 자살하지 않았을까?"], ["Why had he stood looking at the river and preferred to confess?", "왜 나는 강을 바라보며 서 있다가 차라리 자백하는 쪽을 택했을까?"], ["Was the desire to live so strong and was it so hard to overcome it?", "살고자 하는 욕망이 그토록 강하고, 그것을 이겨 내기가 그토록 어려운 일이었던가?"], ["Had not Svidrigaïlov overcome it, although he was afraid of death?", "스비드리가일로프는, 죽음을 두려워했으면서도, 그것을 이겨 내지 않았던가?"]], [["In misery he asked himself this question, and could not understand that, at the very time he had been standing looking into the river, he had perhaps been dimly conscious of the fundamental falsity in himself and his convictions.", "비참함 속에서 그는 이 물음을 스스로에게 던졌고, 강물을 들여다보며 서 있던 바로 그 순간에 어쩌면 자기 자신과 자기 신념 속의 근본적인 거짓을 어렴풋이 의식하고 있었을지도 모른다는 것을 이해하지 못했다."], ["He didn’t understand that that consciousness might be the promise of a future crisis, of a new view of life and of his future resurrection.", "그 의식이야말로 미래의 위기에 대한, 삶에 대한 새로운 관점에 대한, 자신의 미래의 부활에 대한 약속일 수 있다는 것을 그는 이해하지 못했다."]], [["He preferred to attribute it to the dead weight of instinct which he could not step over, again through weakness and meanness.", "그는 그것을, 자신이 넘어설 수 없는 본능의 죽은 무게 탓으로 돌리는 쪽을 택했다, 또다시 나약함과 비열함 탓으로."], ["He looked at his fellow prisoners and was amazed to see how they all loved life and prized it.", "그는 동료 죄수들을 바라보며, 그들 모두가 얼마나 삶을 사랑하고 그것을 소중히 여기는지를 보고 놀랐다."], ["It seemed to him that they loved and valued life more in prison than in freedom.", "그에게는 그들이 자유로울 때보다 감옥에서 삶을 더 사랑하고 더 소중히 여기는 것처럼 보였다."], ["What terrible agonies and privations some of them, the tramps for instance, had endured!", "그들 중 어떤 이들, 가령 부랑자들은 얼마나 끔찍한 고통과 궁핍을 견뎌 냈던가!"], ["Could they care so much for a ray of sunshine, for the primeval forest, the cold spring hidden away in some unseen spot, which the tramp had marked three years before, and longed to see again, as he might to see his sweetheart, dreaming of the green grass round it and the bird singing in the bush?", "그런데도 그들이 한 줄기 햇살을, 원시림을, 어딘가 보이지 않는 곳에 숨겨진, 부랑자가 삼 년 전에 표시해 두고 마치 연인을 보고 싶어 하듯 다시 보기를 갈망하는 그 차가운 샘을, 그 주위의 푸른 풀과 덤불에서 지저귀는 새를 꿈꾸며 그토록 소중히 여길 수 있단 말인가?"], ["As he went on he saw still more inexplicable examples.", "더 나아가면서 그는 한층 더 설명할 수 없는 예들을 보았다."]], [["In prison, of course, there was a great deal he did not see and did not want to see; he lived as it were with downcast eyes.", "물론 감옥에서 그가 보지 못한 것, 보고 싶어 하지 않은 것이 많았다. 그는 말하자면 눈을 내리깐 채 살았다."], ["It was loathsome and unbearable for him to look.", "바라보는 것이 그에게는 역겹고 견딜 수 없는 일이었다."], ["But in the end there was much that surprised him and he began, as it were involuntarily, to notice much that he had not suspected before.", "그러나 결국 그를 놀라게 하는 것이 많아졌고, 그는 말하자면 자기도 모르게, 전에는 짐작조차 못 했던 많은 것을 알아차리기 시작했다."], ["What surprised him most of all was the terrible impossible gulf that lay between him and all the rest.", "무엇보다 그를 놀라게 한 것은 그와 나머지 모두 사이에 가로놓인 그 끔찍하고 불가능한 심연이었다."], ["They seemed to be a different species, and he looked at them and they at him with distrust and hostility.", "그들은 다른 종족인 것 같았고, 그는 그들을, 그들은 그를 불신과 적의의 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["He felt and knew the reasons of his isolation, but he would never have admitted till then that those reasons were so deep and strong.", "그는 자신이 고립된 이유를 느끼고 또 알았지만, 그 이유가 그토록 깊고 강한 것이라고는 그때까지 결코 인정하려 들지 않았었다."], ["There were some Polish exiles, political prisoners, among them.", "그들 가운데는 폴란드 유형수, 정치범 몇 사람이 있었다."], ["They simply looked down upon all the rest as ignorant churls; but Raskolnikov could not look upon them like that.", "그들은 나머지 모두를 그저 무지한 촌놈들로 깔보았다. 그러나 라스콜니코프는 그들을 그렇게 볼 수 없었다."], ["He saw that these ignorant men were in many respects far wiser than the Poles.", "그는 이 무지한 사람들이 여러 면에서 폴란드인들보다 훨씬 더 현명하다는 것을 알았다."], ["There were some Russians who were just as contemptuous, a former officer and two seminarists.", "똑같이 경멸을 일삼는 러시아인 몇몇도 있었으니, 전직 장교 하나와 신학생 둘이었다."], ["Raskolnikov saw their mistake as clearly.", "라스콜니코프는 그들의 잘못 또한 똑똑히 보았다."], ["He was disliked and avoided by everyone; they even began to hate him at last--why, he could not tell.", "그는 모두에게서 미움을 받고 따돌림을 당했다. 그들은 마침내 그를 증오하기까지 했는데 — 왜인지 그는 알 수 없었다."], ["Men who had been far more guilty despised and laughed at his crime.", "그보다 훨씬 더 죄가 무거운 자들이 그의 범죄를 경멸하고 비웃었다."]], [["“You’re a gentleman,”", "\"넌 신사야.\""], ["they used to say.", "그들은 말하곤 했다."], ["“You shouldn’t hack about with an axe; that’s not a gentleman’s work.”", "\"넌 도끼를 휘둘러선 안 됐어. 그건 신사가 할 일이 아니거든.\""]], [["The second week in Lent, his turn came to take the sacrament with his gang.", "사순절 둘째 주에, 자기 작업조와 함께 성찬을 받을 차례가 그에게 돌아왔다."], ["He went to church and prayed with the others.", "그는 교회에 가서 다른 이들과 함께 기도했다."], ["A quarrel broke out one day, he did not know how.", "어느 날 어쩌다 그런지도 모르게 다툼이 벌어졌다."], ["All fell on him at once in a fury.", "모두가 한꺼번에 격분하여 그에게 달려들었다."]], [["“You’re an infidel!", "\"넌 무신론자야!"], ["You don’t believe in God,”", "넌 하느님을 안 믿잖아.\""], ["they shouted.", "그들이 소리쳤다."], ["“You ought to be killed.”", "\"넌 죽어 마땅해.\""]], [["He had never talked to them about God nor his belief, but they wanted to kill him as an infidel. He said nothing.", "그는 그들에게 하느님에 대해서도 자기 신앙에 대해서도 결코 이야기한 적이 없었지만, 그들은 그를 무신론자라며 죽이려 했다."], ["One of the prisoners rushed at him in a perfect frenzy.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 죄수 하나가 완전히 광란에 빠져 그에게 달려들었다."], ["Raskolnikov awaited him calmly and silently; his eyebrows did not quiver, his face did not flinch.", "라스콜니코프는 침착하고 말없이 그를 기다렸다. 그의 눈썹은 떨리지 않았고, 그의 얼굴은 움찔하지도 않았다."], ["The guard succeeded in intervening between him and his assailant, or there would have been bloodshed.", "경비병이 가까스로 그와 그 습격자 사이에 끼어들었기에 망정이지, 그러지 않았더라면 피를 보았을 것이다."]], [["There was another question he could not decide: why were they all so fond of Sonia?", "그가 풀 수 없는 또 하나의 물음이 있었다. 왜 그들 모두 그토록 소냐를 좋아하는 걸까?"], ["She did not try to win their favour; she rarely met them, sometimes only she came to see him at work for a moment.", "그녀는 그들의 호의를 얻으려 애쓰지 않았다. 그녀는 그들과 좀처럼 마주치지 않았고, 이따금 그가 일하는 곳으로 잠깐 그를 보러 올 뿐이었다."], ["And yet everybody knew her, they knew that she had come out to follow _him_, knew how and where she lived.", "그런데도 모두가 그녀를 알았고, 그녀가 그를 따라 나왔다는 것을, 그녀가 어떻게 어디서 사는지를 알았다."], ["She never gave them money, did them no particular services.", "그녀는 그들에게 돈을 준 적도, 딱히 무슨 도움을 준 적도 없었다."], ["Only once at Christmas she sent them all presents of pies and rolls.", "단 한 번, 크리스마스 때 그녀가 그들 모두에게 파이와 빵을 선물로 보낸 적은 있었다."], ["But by degrees closer relations sprang up between them and Sonia.", "그러나 차츰 그들과 소냐 사이에 더 가까운 관계가 생겨났다."], ["She would write and post letters for them to their relations.", "그녀는 그들을 위해 그들 친척에게 보내는 편지를 써서 부쳐 주곤 했다."], ["Relations of the prisoners who visited the town, at their instructions, left with Sonia presents and money for them.", "그 도시를 찾은 죄수들의 친척들은, 그들의 부탁에 따라, 소냐에게 그들에게 줄 선물과 돈을 맡기곤 했다."], ["Their wives and sweethearts knew her and used to visit her.", "그들의 아내와 연인들도 그녀를 알고 그녀를 찾아오곤 했다."], ["And when she visited Raskolnikov at work, or met a party of the prisoners on the road, they all took off their hats to her.", "그리고 그녀가 일하는 라스콜니코프를 면회하러 오거나 길에서 죄수 한 무리와 마주치면, 그들 모두 그녀에게 모자를 벗어 인사했다."], ["“Little mother Sofya Semyonovna, you are our dear, good little mother,”", "\"소피야 세묘노브나 어머니, 당신은 우리의 다정하고 착한 작은 어머니예요.\""], ["coarse branded criminals said to that frail little creature.", "거칠고 낙인찍힌 죄인들이 그 가냘프고 자그마한 존재에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["She would smile and bow to them and everyone was delighted when she smiled.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 그들에게 고개를 숙이면 모두가 기뻐했다."], ["They even admired her gait and turned round to watch her walking; they admired her too for being so little, and, in fact, did not know what to admire her most for.", "그들은 그녀의 걸음걸이마저 좋아해 그녀가 걸어가는 모습을 보려고 돌아보았고, 그녀가 그토록 자그마한 것도 좋아했으니, 사실 무엇 때문에 그녀를 가장 좋아하는지 자신들도 알지 못했다."], ["They even came to her for help in their illnesses.", "그들은 병이 나면 도움을 청하러 그녀에게 오기까지 했다."]], [["He was in the hospital from the middle of Lent till after Easter.", "그는 사순절 한가운데부터 부활절이 지난 뒤까지 병원에 있었다."], ["When he was better, he remembered the dreams he had had while he was feverish and delirious.", "병세가 나아졌을 때, 그는 열에 들떠 헛소리를 하는 동안 꾸었던 꿈들을 떠올렸다."], ["He dreamt that the whole world was condemned to a terrible new strange plague that had come to Europe from the depths of Asia.", "그는 온 세계가 아시아 깊은 곳에서 유럽으로 건너온 어떤 끔찍하고 새로운 기이한 역병에 의해 멸망하도록 선고받은 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["All were to be destroyed except a very few chosen.", "극소수의 선택된 자들을 제외하고는 모두가 죽게 되어 있었다."], ["Some new sorts of microbes were attacking the bodies of men, but these microbes were endowed with intelligence and will.", "어떤 새로운 종류의 미생물이 인간의 몸을 공격했는데, 그 미생물에는 지능과 의지가 부여되어 있었다."], ["Men attacked by them became at once mad and furious.", "그것들에게 공격당한 사람들은 당장 미쳐 날뛰게 되었다."], ["But never had men considered themselves so intellectual and so completely in possession of the truth as these sufferers, never had they considered their decisions, their scientific conclusions, their moral convictions so infallible.", "그러나 인간이 이 환자들만큼 스스로를 그토록 지적이며 그토록 완벽하게 진리를 소유하고 있다고 여긴 적은 일찍이 없었고, 자신들의 결정, 자신들의 과학적 결론, 자신들의 도덕적 신념을 그토록 오류 없는 것으로 여긴 적도 일찍이 없었다."], ["Whole villages, whole towns and peoples went mad from the infection.", "마을 전체가, 도시 전체가, 민족 전체가 그 감염으로 미쳐 갔다."], ["All were excited and did not understand one another.", "모두가 흥분했고 서로를 이해하지 못했다."], ["Each thought that he alone had the truth and was wretched looking at the others, beat himself on the breast, wept, and wrung his hands.", "저마다 자기 혼자만 진리를 가졌다고 여기며 남들을 바라보고는 비참해하고, 가슴을 치고, 울고, 두 손을 쥐어짰다."], ["They did not know how to judge and could not agree what to consider evil and what good; they did not know whom to blame, whom to justify.", "그들은 어떻게 판단해야 할지 몰랐고, 무엇을 악으로 무엇을 선으로 여길지 합의하지 못했다. 누구를 탓하고 누구를 옳다 할지 몰랐다."], ["Men killed each other in a sort of senseless spite.", "사람들은 일종의 무의미한 적의에 차서 서로를 죽였다."], ["They gathered together in armies against one another, but even on the march the armies would begin attacking each other, the ranks would be broken and the soldiers would fall on each other, stabbing and cutting, biting and devouring each other.", "그들은 서로에 맞서 군대를 이루어 모였지만, 행군하는 도중에도 그 군대는 자기들끼리 공격하기 시작했고, 대열이 무너지면 병사들이 서로에게 덤벼들어 찌르고 베고, 물어뜯고 잡아먹었다. 도시에서는 하루 종일 비상종이 울렸다."], ["The alarm bell was ringing all day long in the towns; men rushed together, but why they were summoned and who was summoning them no one knew.", "사람들이 우르르 모여들었지만, 무엇 때문에 소집된 것인지, 누가 그들을 소집한 것인지 아무도 몰랐다."], ["The most ordinary trades were abandoned, because everyone proposed his own ideas, his own improvements, and they could not agree.", "가장 평범한 생업도 버려졌으니, 저마다 자기 생각, 자기 식의 개선책을 내세워 합의를 볼 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["The land too was abandoned.", "농토 또한 버려졌다."], ["Men met in groups, agreed on something, swore to keep together, but at once began on something quite different from what they had proposed.", "사람들은 무리 지어 모여 무언가를 합의하고 함께하기로 맹세했지만, 곧장 자기들이 제안한 것과는 전혀 다른 일을 시작했다."], ["They accused one another, fought and killed each other.", "그들은 서로를 비난하고, 싸우고, 죽였다."], ["There were conflagrations and famine.", "큰불과 기근이 일었다."], ["All men and all things were involved in destruction.", "모든 인간과 모든 것이 파멸에 휩쓸렸다."], ["The plague spread and moved further and further.", "역병은 점점 더 멀리멀리 번져 갔다."], ["Only a few men could be saved in the whole world.", "온 세상에서 단지 몇 사람만이 구원받을 수 있었다."], ["They were a pure chosen people, destined to found a new race and a new life, to renew and purify the earth, but no one had seen these men, no one had heard their words and their voices.", "그들은 순결한 선택된 백성으로, 새로운 종족과 새로운 삶을 세우고, 대지를 새롭게 하고 정화하도록 운명 지어진 자들이었다. 그러나 아무도 이 사람들을 본 적이 없었고, 아무도 그들의 말과 그들의 목소리를 들은 적이 없었다."]], [["Raskolnikov was worried that this senseless dream haunted his memory so miserably, the impression of this feverish delirium persisted so long.", "라스콜니코프는 이 무의미한 꿈이 그토록 비참하게 기억에 들러붙어 있는 것이, 이 열병의 헛소리가 남긴 인상이 그토록 오래 가시지 않는 것이 마음에 걸렸다."], ["The second week after Easter had come.", "부활절이 지난 둘째 주가 왔다."], ["There were warm bright spring days; in the prison ward the grating windows under which the sentinel paced were opened.", "따뜻하고 화창한 봄날이었다. 죄수 병동에서는 보초가 그 아래로 오가는 창살 달린 창문들이 열렸다."], ["Sonia had only been able to visit him twice during his illness; each time she had to obtain permission, and it was difficult.", "소냐는 그가 앓는 동안 두 번밖에 그를 면회할 수 없었는데, 매번 허가를 받아야 했고 그것이 어려웠다."], ["But she often used to come to the hospital yard, especially in the evening, sometimes only to stand a minute and look up at the windows of the ward.", "그러나 그녀는 자주 병원 마당으로 오곤 했는데, 특히 저녁이면 그랬고, 때로는 그저 잠깐 서서 병동의 창문들을 올려다보기 위해서였다."]], [["One evening, when he was almost well again, Raskolnikov fell asleep.", "어느 저녁, 그가 거의 회복되었을 무렵, 라스콜니코프는 잠이 들었다."], ["On waking up he chanced to go to the window, and at once saw Sonia in the distance at the hospital gate.", "깨어나서 그는 우연히 창가로 갔다가, 곧장 저 멀리 병원 정문에 서 있는 소냐를 보았다."], ["She seemed to be waiting for someone.", "그녀는 누군가를 기다리는 듯했다."], ["Something stabbed him to the heart at that minute.", "그 순간 무언가가 그의 가슴을 찔렀다."], ["He shuddered and moved away from the window.", "그는 부르르 떨며 창가에서 물러났다."], ["Next day Sonia did not come, nor the day after; he noticed that he was expecting her uneasily.", "이튿날 소냐는 오지 않았고, 그 다음 날도 오지 않았다. 그는 자기가 불안한 마음으로 그녀를 기다리고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["At last he was discharged.", "마침내 그는 퇴원했다."], ["On reaching the prison he learnt from the convicts that Sofya Semyonovna was lying ill at home and was unable to go out.", "감옥에 돌아와 그는 죄수들에게서, 소피야 세묘노브나가 병이 나 집에 누워 있어 밖에 나오지 못한다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["He was very uneasy and sent to inquire after her; he soon learnt that her illness was not dangerous.", "그는 몹시 불안하여 사람을 보내 그녀의 안부를 물었다. 곧 그는 그녀의 병이 위험하지 않다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Hearing that he was anxious about her, Sonia sent him a pencilled note, telling him that she was much better, that she had a slight cold and that she would soon, very soon come and see him at his work.", "그가 자기를 걱정한다는 말을 듣고, 소냐는 연필로 쓴 쪽지를 보내, 자기는 훨씬 나아졌고, 가벼운 감기에 걸렸을 뿐이며, 곧, 아주 곧 그가 일하는 곳으로 그를 보러 가겠다고 전했다."], ["His heart throbbed painfully as he read it.", "그것을 읽으며 그의 심장은 고통스럽게 두근거렸다."]], [["Again it was a warm bright day.", "또다시 따뜻하고 화창한 날이었다."], ["Early in the morning, at six o’clock, he went off to work on the river bank, where they used to pound alabaster and where there was a kiln for baking it in a shed.", "이른 아침 여섯 시에 그는 강가로 일을 나갔는데, 그곳에서 그들은 설화석고를 빻았고 헛간 안에는 그것을 굽는 가마가 있었다."], ["There were only three of them sent.", "보내진 것은 세 사람뿐이었다."], ["One of the convicts went with the guard to the fortress to fetch a tool; the other began getting the wood ready and laying it in the kiln.", "죄수 하나는 연장을 가지러 경비병과 함께 요새로 갔고, 다른 하나는 장작을 준비해 가마에 넣기 시작했다."], ["Raskolnikov came out of the shed on to the river bank, sat down on a heap of logs by the shed and began gazing at the wide deserted river.", "라스콜니코프는 헛간에서 나와 강가로 가서, 헛간 옆 통나무 더미에 앉아 넓고 적막한 강을 바라보기 시작했다."], ["From the high bank a broad landscape opened before him, the sound of singing floated faintly audible from the other bank.", "높은 강둑에서 드넓은 풍경이 그의 앞에 펼쳐졌고, 노랫소리가 맞은편 강가에서 희미하게 들려왔다."], ["In the vast steppe, bathed in sunshine, he could just see, like black specks, the nomads’ tents.", "햇살에 잠긴 광막한 초원에는 유목민의 천막들이 검은 점들처럼 겨우 보였다."], ["There there was freedom, there other men were living, utterly unlike those here; there time itself seemed to stand still, as though the age of Abraham and his flocks had not passed.", "거기에는 자유가 있었고, 거기에는 이곳 사람들과는 전혀 다른 사람들이 살고 있었다. 거기서는 시간 자체가 멈춰 선 듯했으니, 마치 아브라함과 그의 양 떼의 시대가 아직 지나가지 않은 것만 같았다."], ["Raskolnikov sat gazing, his thoughts passed into day-dreams, into contemplation; he thought of nothing, but a vague restlessness excited and troubled him.", "라스콜니코프는 앉아 바라보았고, 그의 생각은 백일몽으로, 명상으로 흘러갔다. 그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았지만, 막연한 불안이 그를 흥분시키고 어지럽혔다."], ["Suddenly he found Sonia beside him; she had come up noiselessly and sat down at his side.", "갑자기 그는 소냐가 자기 곁에 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 소리 없이 다가와 그의 곁에 앉았던 것이다."], ["It was still quite early; the morning chill was still keen.", "아직 무척 일렀고, 아침 한기가 여전히 매서웠다."], ["She wore her poor old burnous and the green shawl; her face still showed signs of illness, it was thinner and paler.", "그녀는 낡고 초라한 외투와 초록색 숄을 두르고 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 아직 병색이 남아 있어, 한층 야위고 창백했다."], ["She gave him a joyful smile of welcome, but held out her hand with her usual timidity.", "그녀는 그에게 반가운 기쁨의 미소를 지어 보였지만, 손은 여느 때처럼 수줍게 내밀었다."], ["She was always timid of holding out her hand to him and sometimes did not offer it at all, as though afraid he would repel it.", "그녀는 늘 그에게 손을 내미는 것을 수줍어했고, 때로는 그가 뿌리칠까 두려운 듯 아예 내밀지 않기도 했다."], ["He always took her hand as though with repugnance, always seemed vexed to meet her and was sometimes obstinately silent throughout her visit.", "그는 늘 마지못한 듯 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 늘 그녀를 만나는 것이 짜증스러운 듯했으며, 때로는 그녀가 면회하는 내내 고집스레 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["Sometimes she trembled before him and went away deeply grieved.", "어떤 때는 그녀가 그 앞에서 부들부들 떨다가 깊은 슬픔에 잠겨 돌아가곤 했다."], ["But now their hands did not part.", "그러나 이번에는 그들의 손이 떨어지지 않았다."], ["He stole a rapid glance at her and dropped his eyes on the ground without speaking.", "그는 그녀를 재빨리 흘끗 보고는 아무 말 없이 땅바닥으로 눈을 떨구었다. 그들은 단둘이었고, 아무도 그들을 보지 못했다."], ["They were alone, no one had seen them.", "경비병은 잠시 자리를 비우고 있었다."], ["The guard had turned away for the time.", "어떻게 그런 일이 일어났는지 그는 알지 못했다. 그러나 별안간 무언가가"]], [["How it happened he did not know.", "그를 사로잡아 그녀의 발치에 내던졌다."], ["But all at once something seemed to seize him and fling him at her feet. He wept and threw his arms round her knees.", "그는 흐느끼며 두 팔로 그녀의 무릎을 끌어안았다."], ["For the first instant she was terribly frightened and she turned pale.", "처음 한순간 그녀는 끔찍하게 놀라 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["She jumped up and looked at him trembling.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 부들부들 떨며 그를 바라보았다."], ["But at the same moment she understood, and a light of infinite happiness came into her eyes.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 그녀는 깨달았고, 그녀의 두 눈에 무한한 행복의 빛이 떠올랐다."], ["She knew and had no doubt that he loved her beyond everything and that at last the moment had come....", "그녀는 그가 무엇보다도 자신을 사랑한다는 것을, 그리고 마침내 그 순간이 왔다는 것을 알았고, 또 의심하지 않았다……."]], [["They wanted to speak, but could not; tears stood in their eyes.", "그들은 말을 하려 했으나 할 수 없었다. 두 사람의 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었다."], ["They were both pale and thin; but those sick pale faces were bright with the dawn of a new future, of a full resurrection into a new life.", "둘 다 창백하고 야위었지만, 그 병들고 핼쑥한 얼굴은 새로운 미래의 여명으로, 새로운 삶으로의 완전한 부활로 환하게 빛났다."], ["They were renewed by love; the heart of each held infinite sources of life for the heart of the other.", "그들은 사랑으로 새로워졌다. 두 사람의 가슴은 서로의 가슴을 위해 무한한 생명의 샘을 품고 있었다."]], [["They resolved to wait and be patient.", "그들은 기다리며 인내하기로 마음먹었다."], ["They had another seven years to wait, and what terrible suffering and what infinite happiness before them!", "그들에게는 아직 칠 년이라는 세월이 남아 있었고, 그 앞에는 얼마나 끔찍한 고통이, 그리고 얼마나 무한한 행복이 놓여 있었던가!"], ["But he had risen again and he knew it and felt it in all his being, while she--she only lived in his life.", "그러나 그는 다시 일어섰고, 자신의 온 존재로 그것을 알고 또 느꼈다. 한편 그녀는 — 그녀는 오로지 그의 삶 속에서만 살았다."]], [["On the evening of the same day, when the barracks were locked, Raskolnikov lay on his plank bed and thought of her.", "같은 날 저녁, 막사가 잠겼을 때, 라스콜니코프는 판자 침상에 누워 그녀를 생각했다."], ["He had even fancied that day that all the convicts who had been his enemies looked at him differently; he had even entered into talk with them and they answered him in a friendly way.", "그날따라 그는 그동안 자신의 적이었던 죄수들조차 그를 다른 눈으로 바라보는 것만 같다는 생각마저 들었다. 그는 그들에게 말을 걸기까지 했고, 그들도 다정하게 대답해 주었다."], ["He remembered that now, and thought it was bound to be so.", "이제 그것을 떠올리며, 그는 그렇게 되는 것이 마땅하다고 생각했다."], ["Wasn’t everything now bound to be changed?", "이제 모든 것이 변하는 게 당연하지 않은가?"]], [["He thought of her.", "그는 그녀를 생각했다."], ["He remembered how continually he had tormented her and wounded her heart.", "그는 자신이 얼마나 끊임없이 그녀를 괴롭혔고 그녀의 마음에 상처를 입혔는지를 떠올렸다."], ["He remembered her pale and thin little face.", "그는 그녀의 창백하고 야윈 작은 얼굴을 떠올렸다."], ["But these recollections scarcely troubled him now; he knew with what infinite love he would now repay all her sufferings.", "그러나 이런 회상들은 이제 그를 거의 괴롭히지 못했다. 그는 자신이 이제 얼마나 무한한 사랑으로 그녀의 모든 고통을 갚아 줄 것인지 알고 있었다."], ["And what were all, _all_ the agonies of the past!", "그러니 과거의 그 모든, 그 모든 고뇌가 다 무엇이란 말인가!"], ["Everything, even his crime, his sentence and imprisonment, seemed to him now in the first rush of feeling an external, strange fact with which he had no concern.", "모든 것이, 심지어 그의 범죄도, 그의 선고와 유형마저도, 이제 솟구치는 감정의 첫 물결 속에서는 그와는 아무 상관도 없는, 외적이고 낯선 사실처럼 느껴졌다."], ["But he could not think for long together of anything that evening, and he could not have analysed anything consciously; he was simply feeling.", "그러나 그날 저녁 그는 무엇 하나에 대해서도 오래 생각을 이어 가지 못했고, 그 무엇도 의식적으로 분석할 수 없었다. 그는 그저 느낄 뿐이었다."], ["Life had stepped into the place of theory and something quite different would work itself out in his mind.", "이론의 자리에 삶이 들어섰고, 그의 마음속에서는 무언가 전혀 다른 것이 스스로 펼쳐지려 하고 있었다."]], [["Under his pillow lay the New Testament.", "그의 베개 밑에는 신약 성경이 놓여 있었다."], ["He took it up mechanically.", "그는 무심코 그것을 집어 들었다."], ["The book belonged to Sonia; it was the one from which she had read the raising of Lazarus to him.", "그 책은 소냐의 것으로, 그녀가 그에게 나사로의 부활을 읽어 주었던 바로 그 책이었다."], ["At first he was afraid that she would worry him about religion, would talk about the gospel and pester him with books.", "처음에 그는 그녀가 종교 문제로 자신을 들볶고, 복음에 대해 떠들며 책으로 귀찮게 굴까 봐 두려웠다."], ["But to his great surprise she had not once approached the subject and had not even offered him the Testament.", "그러나 놀랍게도 그녀는 단 한 번도 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았고, 성경을 권하지조차 않았다."], ["He had asked her for it himself not long before his illness and she brought him the book without a word.", "그는 병에 걸리기 얼마 전에 직접 그것을 그녀에게 청했고, 그녀는 말없이 그 책을 가져다주었다."], ["Till now he had not opened it.", "지금까지 그는 그것을 펼쳐 본 적이 없었다."]], [["He did not open it now, but one thought passed through his mind: “Can her convictions not be mine now?", "그는 지금도 그것을 펼치지 않았지만, 한 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. '이제 그녀의 신념이 나의 것이 될 수는 없을까?"], ["Her feelings, her aspirations at least....”", "적어도 그녀의 감정, 그녀의 갈망만이라도…….'"]], [["She too had been greatly agitated that day, and at night she was taken ill again.", "그날 그녀 역시 몹시 동요했고, 밤에는 다시 앓아누웠다."], ["But she was so happy--and so unexpectedly happy--that she was almost frightened of her happiness.", "그러나 그녀는 너무도 행복했고 — 너무도 뜻밖에 행복했기에 — 자신의 행복이 거의 두렵기까지 했다."], ["Seven years, _only_ seven years!", "칠 년, 겨우 칠 년이라니!"], ["At the beginning of their happiness at some moments they were both ready to look on those seven years as though they were seven days.", "행복이 시작된 무렵 어떤 순간에는, 두 사람 모두 그 칠 년을 마치 이레인 양 여길 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["He did not know that the new life would not be given him for nothing, that he would have to pay dearly for it, that it would cost him great striving, great suffering.", "그는 새로운 삶이 거저 주어지지 않으리라는 것을, 그것을 위해 값비싼 대가를 치러야 하리라는 것을, 그것이 그에게 엄청난 분투와 엄청난 고통을 요구하리라는 것을 알지 못했다."]], [["But that is the beginning of a new story--the story of the gradual renewal of a man, the story of his gradual regeneration, of his passing from one world into another, of his initiation into a new unknown life.", "그러나 여기서부터 새로운 이야기가 시작된다 — 한 인간이 점차 새로워져 가는 이야기, 그가 점차 거듭나는 이야기, 그가 한 세계에서 다른 세계로 건너가, 미지의 새로운 삶에 발을 들여놓는 이야기가."], ["That might be the subject of a new story, but our present story is ended.", "그것은 새로운 이야기의 주제가 될 수 있겠지만, 지금 우리의 이야기는 여기서 끝난다."]]];
